window.MATH_CATALOG = {"meta":{"schemaVersion":"1.0","generatedAt":"2026-10-17T21:12:49.460076+00:00","sourceRepository":"https://github.com/openai/math","sourceCommit":"1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a","activeManuscripts":719,"families":372,"subjects":17,"withdrawnManuscripts":3,"initialReleaseManuscripts":722,"officialFormalizationClaim":"README/history reports 300 / 719 (~42%) top-line results formalized. This build did not re-run Lean.","leanCatalog":{"sourceCount":173,"scope":"Partial progress.","review":{"status":"unchecked"},"mainResultDeclarationCount":200},"methodology":"All canonical manuscripts are machine-extracted from their TeX include trees or PDF full text; metadata and abstracts are matched to CONTENTS.md. This is source-grounded extraction, not a claim of 719 complete expert expositions, full translations, peer review, or independently checked proofs.","methodologyZh":"逐一解析官方目錄 719 篇的 TeX 主檔與引入檔，必要時擷取 PDF 全文；屬可追溯的自動抽取，並非已完成 719 篇人工逐句翻譯、專家導讀、同儕審查或獨立證明驗證。","sourceLicensePath":"LICENSE","extractionWarning":"LaTeX mathematics and custom macros are preserved; the upstream PDF remains authoritative for notation.","previewNote":"Statement, definition, section, and Lean scope previews are shortened. Fetch extraction.extractUrl for the complete source-based record."},"subjects":[{"id":"1","name":"Number theory","nameZh":"數論","familyIds":["001","002","003","004","005","006","007","008","009","010","011","012","013","014","015","016","017","018","019","020","021","022","023","024","025","026","027","028","029","030","031"],"paperCount":58},{"id":"3","name":"Algebraic and complex geometry","nameZh":"代數與複幾何","familyIds":["032","033","034","035","036","037","038","039","040","041","042","043","044","046","047","048","049","050","051","052","053","054","055","056","057","058","059","060","062","063","064","065","066","067","068","069"],"paperCount":86},{"id":"10","name":"Real and complex analysis","nameZh":"實分析與複分析","familyIds":["071","072","073","074","075","076","077","078","079","080","081","082","083","084","085","086"],"paperCount":26},{"id":"8","name":"Convex and metric geometry","nameZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","familyIds":["087","088","089","090","091","092","093","094","095","096","097","098","099","100","101"],"paperCount":30},{"id":"14","name":"Theoretical computer science","nameZh":"理論計算機科學","familyIds":["102","103","104","105","106","107","108","109","110","111","112","113","114","115","116","117","118","119","120","121","122","124","125","126","127","128","129","130","131","132","133","134","135","136","137","138","139","140","141","142"],"paperCount":73},{"id":"12","name":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","nameZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","familyIds":["143","144","145","146","147","148","149","150","151","152","153","154"],"paperCount":19},{"id":"13","name":"Combinatorics","nameZh":"組合數學","familyIds":["155","156","157","158","159","160","161","162","164","165","166","167","168","169","170","171","172","173","174","175","176","177","178","179","180","181","182","183","184","185","186","187","188","189","190","191","192"],"paperCount":50},{"id":"2","name":"Algebra","nameZh":"代數","familyIds":["193","194","195","196","197","198","199","200","201","202","203","204","205","206","207","208","209","210"],"paperCount":29},{"id":"15","name":"Probability and statistical mechanics","nameZh":"機率與統計力學","familyIds":["211","212","213","214","215","216","217","218","219","220","221","222","223","224","225","226","227","228","229","230","231","232","233","234","235","236","237","238","239"],"paperCount":105},{"id":"6","name":"Mathematical logic","nameZh":"數理邏輯","familyIds":["240","241","242","243","244","245"],"paperCount":8},{"id":"5","name":"Group theory","nameZh":"群論","familyIds":["246","247","248","249","250","251","252","253","254","255","256","257","258","259"],"paperCount":22},{"id":"17","name":"Mathematical physics","nameZh":"數學物理","familyIds":["260","261","262","263","264","265","266","267","268","269","270","271","272","273","274","275","276","277","278","279","280","281","282","283","284"],"paperCount":59},{"id":"16","name":"Operator algebras","nameZh":"算子代數","familyIds":["285","286","287","288","289","290","291","292","293","294","295","296","297","298","299","300","301","302","303"],"paperCount":31},{"id":"4","name":"Topology","nameZh":"拓撲學","familyIds":["304","305","306","307","308","309","310","311","312","313","314","315","316","317","318","319","320","321"],"paperCount":26},{"id":"11","name":"Functional analysis","nameZh":"泛函分析","familyIds":["322","323","324","325","326","327","328","329","330","331","332"],"paperCount":19},{"id":"7","name":"Differential geometry","nameZh":"微分幾何","familyIds":["333","334","335","336","337","338","339","340","341","342","343","344","345","346","347","348","349","350","351","352","353","354","355","356","357","358","359","360","361"],"paperCount":49},{"id":"9","name":"Partial differential equations","nameZh":"偏微分方程","familyIds":["362","363","364","365","366","367","368","369","370","371","372","373","374","375","376","377"],"paperCount":29}],"families":[{"id":"001","title":"Milne’s rationality conjecture and algebraic specialization","summary":"Proves Milne's rationality conjecture for abelian varieties over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$ with good reduction: specialized Hodge classes pair rationally with complementary divisor products, independently of cohomology theory. Together with result 032, every specialized Hodge class is represented by a single rational algebraic cycle simultaneously in all prime-to-p and crystalline realizations, for every residue characteristic p.","paperIds":["001-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"Milne's rationality conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves Milne's rationality conjecture for abelian varieties over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$ with good reduction: specialized Hodge classes pair rationally with complementary divisor products, independently of cohomology theory. Together with result 032, every specialized Hodge class is represented by a single rational algebraic cycle simultaneously in all prime-to-$p$ and crystalline realizations, for every residue characteristic $p$."},{"id":"002","title":"The full BSD formula from low Selmer corank","summary":"Proves the full Birch–Swinnerton-Dyer leading-term formula for every elliptic curve over ℚ whose full q-power Selmer group has corank zero or one for some prime q, including finiteness of the Tate–Shafarevich group. With result 006, this gives full BSD for a density-one set of quadratic twists of every elliptic curve over ℚ.","paperIds":["002-01","002-02","002-03"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"The full Birch--Swinnerton-Dyer formula in Selmer coranks zero and one","overviewSummary":"Proves the full Birch--Swinnerton-Dyer leading-term formula for every elliptic curve over $\\mathbb Q$ whose full $q$-power Selmer group has corank zero or one for some prime $q$, including finiteness of the Tate--Shafarevich group. With result 006, this gives full BSD for a density-one set of quadratic twists of every elliptic curve over $\\mathbb Q$."},{"id":"003","title":"The quasi-Riemann hypothesis","summary":"Proves that every Dirichlet L-function, including $\\zeta(s)$, is zero-free in $\\Re s\\gt 7/8$, resolving the quasi-Riemann hypothesis. The same half-plane is zero-free for every finite-order Hecke L-function over $\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{-3})$. A companion gives a different proof of the zero-free half-plane $\\Re s\\gt 11/12$.","paperIds":["003-01","003-02","003-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/003.md","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"The quasi-Riemann hypothesis","overviewSummary":"Proves that every Dirichlet $L$-function, including $\\zeta(s)$, is zero-free in $\\Re s>7/8$, resolving the quasi-Riemann hypothesis. The same half-plane is zero-free for every finite-order Hecke $L$-function over $\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{-3})$. A companion gives a different proof of the zero-free half-plane $\\Re s>11/12$."},{"id":"004","title":"Hilbert’s tenth problem over ℚ","summary":"Proves that no algorithm decides whether an integer-coefficient polynomial in an arbitrary number of variables has a rational zero, resolving Hilbert's tenth problem over ℚ negatively.","paperIds":["004-01","004-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"Hilbert's tenth problem over $\\mathbb Q$","overviewSummary":"Proves that no algorithm decides whether an integer-coefficient polynomial in an arbitrary number of variables has a rational zero, resolving Hilbert's tenth problem over $\\mathbb Q$ negatively."},{"id":"005","title":"Irrationality of Catalan’s constant","summary":"Proves that Catalan's constant $G=\\sum_{j\\ge0}(-1)^j/(2j+1)^2$ is irrational.","paperIds":["005-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/005.md","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"Irrationality of Catalan's constant","overviewSummary":"Proves that Catalan's constant $G=\\sum_{j\\ge0}(-1)^j/(2j+1)^2$ is irrational."},{"id":"006","title":"Goldfeld’s conjecture: densities and mean analytic rank","summary":"Proves Goldfeld's conjecture for quadratic twists of every elliptic curve over ℚ: analytic ranks zero and one each have density 1/2, and the mean analytic rank tends to 1/2. Both statements order signed squarefree twist parameters by absolute value.","paperIds":["006-01","006-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"Goldfeld's conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves Goldfeld's conjecture for quadratic twists of every elliptic curve over $\\mathbb Q$: analytic ranks zero and one each have density $1/2$, and the mean analytic rank tends to $1/2$. Both statements order signed squarefree twist parameters by absolute value."},{"id":"007","title":"Ordinary two-point correlations and the corrected Elliott conjecture","summary":"Proves the ordinary two-point Chowla conjecture, with a bound $O(X/(\\log X)^c)$ for Liouville correlation sums along fixed nonproportional affine forms, where c > 0 is absolute. More generally, proves the binary corrected Elliott conjecture for complex multiplicative functions bounded by one when one factor is uniformly nonpretentious against each fixed Dirichlet character times $n^{it}$ for $|t|\\le X$.","paperIds":["007-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/007.md","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"Two-point Chowla and the corrected binary Elliott conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves the ordinary two-point Chowla conjecture, with a bound $O(X/(\\log X)^c)$ for Liouville correlation sums along fixed nonproportional affine forms, where $c>0$ is absolute. More generally, proves the binary corrected Elliott conjecture for complex multiplicative functions bounded by one when one factor is uniformly nonpretentious against each fixed Dirichlet character times $n^{it}$ for $|t|\\le X$."},{"id":"008","title":"The Deligne–Drinfeld conjecture","summary":"Proves that the rational Grothendieck–Teichmüller Lie algebra, with the Ihara bracket, is freely generated by one element in each odd weight 3, 5, 7, …, resolving the Deligne–Drinfeld conjecture.","paperIds":["008-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/008.md","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"The Deligne--Drinfeld conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves that the rational Grothendieck--Teichm\\\"uller Lie algebra, with the Ihara bracket, is freely generated by one element in each odd weight $3,5,7,\\ldots$, resolving the Deligne--Drinfeld conjecture."},{"id":"009","title":"Function-field reconstruction from Milnor K-theory and Galois data","summary":"Reconstructs function fields of transcendence degree at least two over algebraically closed constants from $K^{\\mathrm M}_1/\\ell$, $K^{\\mathrm M}_2/\\ell$, and their product. These data recover the perfect closure and constants when ℓ differs from the characteristic, and the original field and its named base in equal characteristic. Also proves Bogomolov–Pop reconstruction from abelian-by-central pro-ℓ Galois data away from the characteristic.","paperIds":["009-01","009-02","009-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/009.md","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"Bogomolov--Pop and Milnor $K$-theoretic reconstruction","overviewSummary":"Reconstructs function fields of transcendence degree at least two over algebraically closed constants from $K^{\\mathrm M}_1/\\ell$, $K^{\\mathrm M}_2/\\ell$, and their product. These data recover the perfect closure and constants when $\\ell$ differs from the characteristic, and the original field and its named base in equal characteristic. Also proves Bogomolov--Pop reconstruction from abelian-by-central pro-$\\ell$ Galois data away from the characteristic."},{"id":"010","title":"Unrestricted pro-modularity at the prime two","summary":"Every continuous odd absolutely irreducible two-dimensional 2-adic representation of $G_{\\mathbb Q}$ unramified outside finitely many primes occurs in a completed Hecke algebra at some odd tame level. Also proves classical modularity up to Tate twist for irreducible odd representations with these finiteness conditions that are de Rham at 2 with distinct Hodge–Tate weights, resolving the dyadic Fontaine–Mazur case without residual restrictions.","paperIds":["010-01","010-02","010-03"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"Fontaine--Mazur modularity at the prime $2$ and $2$-adic pro-modularity","overviewSummary":"Every continuous odd absolutely irreducible two-dimensional $2$-adic representation of $G_{\\mathbb Q}$ unramified outside finitely many primes occurs in a completed Hecke algebra at some odd tame level. Also proves classical modularity up to Tate twist for irreducible odd representations with these finiteness conditions that are de Rham at $2$ with distinct Hodge--Tate weights, resolving the dyadic Fontaine--Mazur case without residual restrictions."},{"id":"011","title":"Prime-factor statistics of $p-1$","summary":"Proves that the normalized ordered logarithms of the prime factors of $p-1$, counted with multiplicity, converge jointly to the Poisson–Dirichlet law $\\mathrm{PD}(1)$ as p ranges uniformly over primes up to x and $x\\to\\infty$. This resolves the Ford–Konyagin–Luca conjecture. It also proves that infinitely many integers n have more than $n^{1-\\varepsilon}$ totient preimages, for every ε > 0.","paperIds":["011-01","011-02","011-03"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"The Ford--Konyagin--Luca conjecture on prime predecessors","overviewSummary":"Proves that the normalized ordered logarithms of the prime factors of $p-1$, counted with multiplicity, converge jointly to the Poisson--Dirichlet law $\\mathrm{PD}(1)$ as $p$ ranges uniformly over primes up to $x$ and $x\\to\\infty$. This resolves the Ford--Konyagin--Luca conjecture. It also proves that infinitely many integers $n$ have more than $n^{1-\\varepsilon}$ totient preimages, for every $\\varepsilon>0$."},{"id":"012","title":"Independent largest prime factors of consecutive integers","summary":"Resolves the Erdős–Pomerance joint Dickman conjecture: the logarithmic sizes of the largest prime factors of n and $n+1$ are asymptotically independent in ordinary natural density. In particular, the integers satisfying $P^+(n)\\lt P^+(n+1)$ have density 1/2.","paperIds":["012-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/012.md","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"Independent largest prime factors of consecutive integers","overviewSummary":"Resolves the Erd\\H{o}s--Pomerance joint Dickman conjecture: the logarithmic sizes of the largest prime factors of $n$ and $n+1$ are asymptotically independent in ordinary natural density. In particular, the integers satisfying $P^+(n)<P^+(n+1)$ have density $1/2$."},{"id":"013","title":"Ostmann’s inverse Goldbach conjecture","summary":"Proves that no finite modification of the primes can be written as $A+B$ with $A,B\\subseteq\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}$ each containing at least two elements. This resolves Ostmann's inverse Goldbach conjecture on additive indecomposability.","paperIds":["013-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/013.md","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"Ostmann's inverse Goldbach conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves that no finite modification of the primes can be written as $A+B$ with $A,B\\subseteq\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}$ each containing at least two elements. This resolves Ostmann's inverse Goldbach conjecture on additive indecomposability."},{"id":"014","title":"Restricted geometric Langlands, global Arthur enhancements, and generic Ramanujan","summary":"Proves the restricted geometric Langlands equivalence for connected reductive groups on smooth projective connected curves over $\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q$ under the four stated Lie-theoretic characteristic hypotheses. Over arbitrary algebraically closed fields of characteristic p > 0, the same conclusion holds assuming additionally that p is very good for the group and $p\\nmid |W_G|$. Over global function fields, proves Ramanujan at every place for globally generic cuspidal representations of split adjoint absolutely simple exceptional groups, without characteristic or ramification-depth restrictions, and at every unramified place for cuspidal representations of split adjoint absolutely simple groups with a generic unramified component. Assuming the finite-level Ramanujan–Arthur decomposition, constructs global Arthur enhancements of occurring cuspidal excursion parameters for split connected semisimple groups at full finite level, recovering the given parameters by diagonal specialization on the entire Weil group, including inertia.","paperIds":["014-01","014-02","014-03","014-04","014-05","014-06","014-07","014-08"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"Restricted geometric Langlands, generic Ramanujan and Arthur parameters","overviewSummary":"Proves the restricted geometric Langlands equivalence for connected reductive groups on smooth projective connected curves over $\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q$ under the four stated Lie-theoretic characteristic hypotheses. Over arbitrary algebraically closed fields of characteristic $p>0$, the same conclusion holds assuming additionally that $p$ is very good for the group and $p\\nmid |W_G|$. Over global function fields, proves Ramanujan at every place for globally generic cuspidal representations of split adjoint absolutely simple exceptional groups, without characteristic or ramification-depth restrictions, and at every unramified place for cuspidal representations of split adjoint absolutely simple groups with a generic unramified component. Assuming the finite-level Ramanujan--Arthur decomposition, constructs global Arthur enhancements of occurring cuspidal excursion parameters for split connected semisimple groups at full finite level, recovering the given parameters by diagonal specialization on the entire Weil group, including inertia."},{"id":"015","title":"Torus-packet equidistribution in prime, quartic, and sextic degrees","summary":"Proves Haar equidistribution without escape of mass for complete volume-weighted torus packets from totally real fields: arbitrary lattices and prescribed local types in fixed prime degree at least five, arbitrary-order Picard packets in primitive quartic fields, and maximal-order ideal-class packets in primitive sextic fields. Here primitive means having no proper intermediate field; the relevant order or field discriminant tends to infinity.","paperIds":["015-01","015-02","015-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/015.md","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"Torus-packet equidistribution in prime, quartic, and sextic degrees","overviewSummary":"Proves Haar equidistribution without escape of mass for complete volume-weighted torus packets from totally real fields: arbitrary lattices and prescribed local types in fixed prime degree at least five, arbitrary-order Picard packets in primitive quartic fields, and maximal-order ideal-class packets in primitive sextic fields. Here primitive means having no proper intermediate field; the relevant order or field discriminant tends to infinity."},{"id":"016","title":"Zilber–Pink in abelian varieties and the Siegel threefold","summary":"Proves the abelian Zilber–Pink conjecture over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$: every irreducible subvariety has finitely many maximal atypical subvarieties relative to its smallest containing torsion coset. It also proves the full curve case in the Siegel threefold $\\mathcal A_2$ for Hodge-generic curves defined over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$, without boundary or reduction assumptions.","paperIds":["016-01","016-02","016-03","016-04"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"Zilber--Pink in abelian varieties and the Siegel threefold","overviewSummary":"Proves the abelian Zilber--Pink conjecture over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$: every irreducible subvariety has finitely many maximal atypical subvarieties relative to its smallest containing torsion coset. It also proves the full curve case in the Siegel threefold $\\mathcal A_2$ for Hodge-generic curves defined over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$, without boundary or reduction assumptions."},{"id":"017","title":"The irrationality exponent of π is 2","summary":"Proves that the irrationality exponent of π is exactly 2: for every ε > 0 and all sufficiently large denominators q, every rational $p/q$ satisfies $|\\pi-p/q|\\ge q^{-2-\\varepsilon}$. This also proves convergence of the Flint–Hills series $\\sum_{n\\ge1}1/(n^3\\sin^2 n)$, with angles in radians.","paperIds":["017-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/017.md","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"The irrationality exponent of $\\pi$ is $2$","overviewSummary":"Proves that the irrationality exponent of $\\pi$ is exactly $2$: for every $\\varepsilon>0$ and all sufficiently large denominators $q$, every rational $p/q$ satisfies $|\\pi-p/q|\\ge q^{-2-\\varepsilon}$. This also proves convergence of the Flint--Hills series $\\sum_{n\\ge1}1/(n^3\\sin^2 n)$, with angles in radians."},{"id":"018","title":"The Margulis–Platonov conjecture over global fields","summary":"Proves the Margulis–Platonov conjecture over every global field, including function fields of characteristic two. For an absolutely almost simple simply connected algebraic group G over k, every noncentral abstract normal subgroup of $G(k)$ is the inverse image of an open normal subgroup in the finite product of its anisotropic nonarchimedean local groups.","paperIds":["018-01","018-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"The Margulis--Platonov conjecture over global fields","overviewSummary":"Proves the Margulis--Platonov conjecture over every global field, including function fields of characteristic two. For an absolutely almost simple simply connected algebraic group $G$ over $k$, every noncentral abstract normal subgroup of $G(k)$ is the inverse image of an open normal subgroup in the finite product of its anisotropic nonarchimedean local groups."},{"id":"019","title":"The local p-adic section conjecture and global consequences","summary":"Proves that rational points on every smooth proper geometrically connected curve of genus at least two over a finite extension of ℚp correspond bijectively to conjugacy classes of sections of its full arithmetic étale fundamental group. It also proves Grothendieck's section conjecture over ℚ for the modular curves $X_0(N)$ and $X_1(N)$ of genus at least two.","paperIds":["019-01","019-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"The $p$-adic section conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves that rational points on every smooth proper geometrically connected curve of genus at least two over a finite extension of $\\mathbb Q_p$ correspond bijectively to conjugacy classes of sections of its full arithmetic \\'etale fundamental group. It also proves Grothendieck's section conjecture over $\\mathbb Q$ for the modular curves $X_0(N)$ and $X_1(N)$ of genus at least two."},{"id":"020","title":"Squarefree quartics and power-free polynomial values","summary":"Proves the squarefree-values conjecture for irreducible integer quartics with no fixed prime-square divisor: squarefree values on positive integers have the predicted positive Euler-product density. More generally, establishes the $(d-2)$-power-free density for irreducible integer polynomials of degrees four through eight under the necessary local condition; together with Browning's higher-degree theorem, this covers every d ≥ 4.","paperIds":["020-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/020.md","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"Squarefree quartics and power-free polynomial values","overviewSummary":"Proves the squarefree-values conjecture for irreducible integer quartics with no fixed prime-square divisor: squarefree values on positive integers have the predicted positive Euler-product density. More generally, establishes the $(d-2)$-power-free density for irreducible integer polynomials of degrees four through eight under the necessary local condition; together with Browning's higher-degree theorem, this covers every $d\\ge4$."},{"id":"021","title":"A quadratic bound for Jacobsthal’s function","summary":"Answers Jacobsthal's quadratic-bound question: every interval of $Ck^2$ consecutive integers contains an integer coprime to any prescribed positive integer with at most k distinct prime divisors, for an absolute constant C. The bound is uniform over prime sets and interval positions and removes the classical logarithmic loss.","paperIds":["021-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/021.md","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"A quadratic bound for Jacobsthal's function","overviewSummary":"Answers Jacobsthal's quadratic-bound question: every interval of $Ck^2$ consecutive integers contains an integer coprime to any prescribed positive integer with at most $k$ distinct prime divisors, for an absolute constant $C$. The bound is uniform over prime sets and interval positions and removes the classical logarithmic loss."},{"id":"022","title":"The weak inhomogeneous Duffin–Schaeffer conjecture","summary":"Proves that for every real shift γ and finite-valued $\\psi:\\mathbb N\\to[0,\\infty)$, divergence of $\\sum_q\\phi(q)\\psi(q)/q$ implies $\\|qx-\\gamma\\|\\lt \\psi(q)$ for infinitely many q, for almost every x. Here ϕ is Euler's totient and the norm is distance to the nearest integer. Numerators are unrestricted; no monotonicity or Diophantine condition on γ is needed.","paperIds":["022-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"The weak inhomogeneous Duffin--Schaeffer conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves that for every real shift $\\gamma$ and finite-valued $\\psi:\\mathbb N\\to[0,\\infty)$, divergence of $\\sum_q\\phi(q)\\psi(q)/q$ implies $\\|qx-\\gamma\\|<\\psi(q)$ for infinitely many $q$, for almost every $x$. Here $\\phi$ is Euler's totient and the norm is distance to the nearest integer. Numerators are unrestricted; no monotonicity or Diophantine condition on $\\gamma$ is needed."},{"id":"023","title":"Patterson's first moment for cubic Gauss sums","summary":"Proves unconditionally the all-primary-prime form of Patterson’s first-moment asymptotic: normalized cubic Gauss sums over primary Eisenstein primes of norm at most X, including both conjugates, have an explicit positive main term of order $X^{5/6}/\\log X$. Every fixed nonzero prime-angle Fourier mode has smaller order.","paperIds":["023-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/023.md","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"Patterson’s first moment for cubic Gauss sums","overviewSummary":"Proves unconditionally the all-primary-prime form of Patterson’s first-moment asymptotic: normalized cubic Gauss sums over primary Eisenstein primes of norm at most $X$, including both conjugates, have an explicit positive main term of order $X^{5/6}/\\log X$. Every fixed nonzero prime-angle Fourier mode has smaller order."},{"id":"024","title":"An asymptotic formula for the number of totients","summary":"Gives an asymptotic equivalent for the number $V(x)$ of distinct totient values up to x, with a positive bounded phase-dependent factor determined by convergent arithmetic approximations. In particular, $V(cx)/V(x)\\to c$ for every fixed c > 0, answering Erdős and Hall’s scaling question.","paperIds":["024-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/024.md","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"An asymptotic formula for the number of totients","overviewSummary":"Gives an asymptotic equivalent for the number $V(x)$ of distinct totient values up to $x$, with a positive bounded phase-dependent factor determined by convergent arithmetic approximations. In particular, $V(cx)/V(x)\\to c$ for every fixed $c>0$, answering Erd\\H{o}s and Hall’s scaling question."},{"id":"025","title":"Short Egyptian fractions","summary":"Every rational $a/b$ with $1\\le a\\lt b$ is a sum of $O(\\log\\log b)$ distinct positive unit fractions. The worst-case minimum number of terms has the same order, resolving Erdős’s conjecture on short Egyptian fractions.","paperIds":["025-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/025.md","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"Erd\\H{o}s’s short Egyptian-fraction conjecture","overviewSummary":"Every rational $a/b$ with $1\\le a<b$ is a sum of $O(\\log\\log b)$ distinct positive unit fractions. The worst-case minimum number of terms has the same order, resolving Erd\\H{o}s’s conjecture on short Egyptian fractions."},{"id":"026","title":"Positive lower density of large prime gaps","summary":"For every fixed C > 0, a positive proportion of consecutive prime gaps exceed $C\\log p_n$, throughout every sufficiently large initial segment of the primes. The proportion may depend on C. Consequently, the indices where $p_n/n$ increases have positive lower density, answering Erdős and Prachar.","paperIds":["026-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/026.md","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"Positive lower density of large prime gaps","overviewSummary":"For every fixed $C>0$, a positive proportion of consecutive prime gaps exceed $C\\log p_n$, throughout every sufficiently large initial segment of the primes. The proportion may depend on $C$. Consequently, the indices where $p_n/n$ increases have positive lower density, answering Erd\\H{o}s and Prachar."},{"id":"027","title":"Potential integral density on curve character varieties","summary":"Resolves the determinant-one curve case of Litt's integral-density question. For every smooth connected complex algebraic curve and every rank, integral points become Zariski dense in every component of its SLr character variety over the full ring of integers of one number field. Prescribed quasi-unipotent boundary conjugacy classes are allowed, including nonsemisimple classes.","paperIds":["027-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/027.md","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"Integral density on curve character varieties","overviewSummary":"Resolves the determinant-one curve case of Litt's integral-density question. For every smooth connected complex algebraic curve and every rank, integral points become Zariski dense in every component of its $\\mathrm{SL}_r$ character variety over the full ring of integers of one number field. Prescribed quasi-unipotent boundary conjugacy classes are allowed, including nonsemisimple classes."},{"id":"028","title":"Uniformly bounded components of Gaussian-prime graphs","summary":"Proves the Gaussian moat conjecture: no infinite walk through distinct Gaussian primes can have uniformly bounded steps. More strongly, for every distance bound D, the graph joining Gaussian primes at distance at most D has uniformly bounded finite component sizes, depending only on D, including primes on the coordinate axes.","paperIds":["028-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/028.md","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"The Gaussian moat conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves the Gaussian moat conjecture: no infinite walk through distinct Gaussian primes can have uniformly bounded steps. More strongly, for every distance bound $D$, the graph joining Gaussian primes at distance at most $D$ has uniformly bounded finite component sizes, depending only on $D$, including primes on the coordinate axes."},{"id":"029","title":"Primitive roots for every admissible integer base","summary":"Proves the infinitude assertion in Artin's primitive root conjecture for every integer a that is neither −1 nor a square. For each such base, at least $c_a x/(\\log x)^2$ primes in every sufficiently large interval $(x,2x)$ have primitive root a, with $c_a\\gt 0$.","paperIds":["029-01","029-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"Artin's primitive root conjecture: infinitude for every base","overviewSummary":"Proves the infinitude assertion in Artin's primitive root conjecture for every integer $a$ that is neither $-1$ nor a square. For each such base, at least $c_a x/(\\log x)^2$ primes in every sufficiently large interval $(x,2x)$ have primitive root $a$, with $c_a>0$."},{"id":"030","title":"Modularity of elliptic curves over imaginary quadratic fields","summary":"Proves the modularity conjecture for elliptic curves over imaginary quadratic fields: every elliptic curve over every imaginary quadratic field is modular, with matching local parameters at every place.","paperIds":["030-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"Modularity over imaginary quadratic fields","overviewSummary":"Proves the modularity conjecture for elliptic curves over imaginary quadratic fields: every elliptic curve over every imaginary quadratic field is modular, with matching local parameters at every place."},{"id":"031","title":"Uchida’s conjecture for open homomorphisms of Galois groups","summary":"Proves Uchida's conjecture: every continuous open homomorphism between Galois groups of possibly infinite solvably closed Galois extensions of number fields comes from a unique equivariant field embedding in the opposite direction. No restriction on the kernel or separate cyclotomic-compatibility assumption is needed.","paperIds":["031-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","overviewTitle":"Uchida's conjecture for open Galois homomorphisms","overviewSummary":"Proves Uchida's conjecture: every continuous open homomorphism between Galois groups of possibly infinite solvably closed Galois extensions of number fields comes from a unique equivariant field embedding in the opposite direction. No restriction on the kernel or separate cyclotomic-compatibility assumption is needed."},{"id":"032","title":"The rational Hodge conjecture for CM abelian varieties","summary":"Proves the rational Hodge conjecture for every complex CM abelian variety, in every dimension and codimension. Through Milne's theorems, this also gives the Tate conjecture for all abelian varieties over finite fields and the Hodge standard conjecture for abelian varieties in every characteristic.","paperIds":["032-01","032-02","032-03","032-04","032-05"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"The rational Hodge conjecture for CM abelian varieties","overviewSummary":"Proves the rational Hodge conjecture for every complex CM abelian variety, in every dimension and codimension. Through Milne's theorems, this also gives the Tate conjecture for all abelian varieties over finite fields and the Hodge standard conjecture for abelian varieties in every characteristic."},{"id":"033","title":"Iitaka subadditivity, variation, and logarithmic additivity","summary":"Proves Campana's orbifold Iitaka subadditivity conjecture for smooth Fujiki-class-$\\mathcal C$ manifolds with rational simple-normal-crossing boundaries. For projective fibrations $f:U\\to V$ of smooth complex quasi-projective varieties with connected fibers, general fiber F, and $\\bar\\kappa(V)\\ge0$, proves Popa's inequality $\\bar\\kappa(U)\\ge\\kappa(F)+\\max\\{\\bar\\kappa(V),\\mathop{\\mathrm{Var}}\\nolimits (f)\\}$, where variation measures the whole geometric generic fiber.","paperIds":["033-01","033-02","033-03","033-04","033-05"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/033.md","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Campana's orbifold Iitaka conjecture and logarithmic subadditivity","overviewSummary":"Proves Campana's orbifold Iitaka subadditivity conjecture for smooth Fujiki-class-$\\mathcal C$ manifolds with rational simple-normal-crossing boundaries. For projective fibrations $f:U\\to V$ of smooth complex quasi-projective varieties with connected fibers, general fiber $F$, and $\\bar\\kappa(V)\\ge0$, proves Popa's inequality $\\bar\\kappa(U)\\ge\\kappa(F)+\\max\\{\\bar\\kappa(V),\\operatorname{Var}(f)\\}$, where variation measures the whole geometric generic fiber."},{"id":"034","title":"Log abundance for compact Kähler spaces under logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity","summary":"Using logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity, proves log abundance in every dimension for normal compact Kähler log canonical pairs with effective rational boundary: an analytically nef ℚ-Cartier adjoint is semiample. It also proves projective log abundance over every algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and the effective Iitaka fibration conjecture for smooth projective varieties of nonnegative Kodaira dimension in that setting. A further result resolves the finite-rational-coefficient index conjecture for connected projective semi-log-canonical log Calabi–Yau pairs over such fields in each fixed dimension and for each fixed finite set of rational boundary coefficients, with a uniform index independent of the number of components.","paperIds":["034-01","034-02","034-03","034-04","034-05","034-06","034-07","034-08","034-09","034-10","034-11","034-12","034-13","034-14"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Log abundance and effective Iitaka fibrations","overviewSummary":"Using logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity, proves log abundance in every dimension for normal compact K\\\"ahler log canonical pairs with effective rational boundary: an analytically nef $\\mathbb Q$-Cartier adjoint is semiample. It also proves projective log abundance over every algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and the effective Iitaka fibration conjecture for smooth projective varieties of nonnegative Kodaira dimension in that setting. A further result resolves the finite-rational-coefficient index conjecture for connected projective semi-log-canonical log Calabi--Yau pairs over such fields in each fixed dimension and for each fixed finite set of rational boundary coefficients, with a uniform index independent of the number of components."},{"id":"035","title":"Log-canonical threefold abundance in numerical dimension one","summary":"Proves log abundance for projective log canonical threefold pairs over algebraically closed fields of characteristic p > 3 when the effective boundary is rational and the ℚ-Cartier adjoint is nef of numerical dimension one. The adjoint is semiample, without requiring the original variety to be terminal or ℚ-factorial.","paperIds":["035-01","035-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Threefold log abundance in numerical dimension one in characteristic $p>3$","overviewSummary":"Proves log abundance for projective log canonical threefold pairs over algebraically closed fields of characteristic $p>3$ when the effective boundary is rational and the $\\mathbb Q$-Cartier adjoint is nef of numerical dimension one. The adjoint is semiample, without requiring the original variety to be terminal or $\\mathbb Q$-factorial."},{"id":"036","title":"Numerical semiampleness and generalized minimal models","summary":"Proves numerical semiampleness for nef adjoints $K_X+B+M$ with $K_X+B$ pseudo-effective and M nef rational, for projective klt rational pairs over algebraically closed characteristic-zero fields and smooth compact Kähler rational klt simple-normal-crossing pairs, using Bott–Chern cohomology in the latter case. Separately, projective generalized log canonical rational pairs over such fields admit minimal models for pseudo-effective adjoints and Mori fiber spaces otherwise, with nef b-data fixed.","paperIds":["036-01","036-02","036-03","036-04"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Numerical semiampleness and generalized minimal models","overviewSummary":"Proves numerical semiampleness for nef adjoints $K_X+B+M$ with $K_X+B$ pseudo-effective and $M$ nef rational, for projective klt rational pairs over algebraically closed characteristic-zero fields and smooth compact K\\\"ahler rational klt simple-normal-crossing pairs, using Bott--Chern cohomology in the latter case. Separately, projective generalized log canonical rational pairs over such fields admit minimal models for pseudo-effective adjoints and Mori fiber spaces otherwise, with nef b-data fixed."},{"id":"037","title":"The ordinary-double-point volume gap","summary":"Proves the ordinary-double-point volume-gap conjecture: every singular complex algebraic klt germ of dimension n ≥ 2, with zero boundary, has normalized volume at most $2(n-1)^n$. Equality holds precisely for an analytic ordinary double point.","paperIds":["037-01","037-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"The sharp ordinary-double-point volume gap","overviewSummary":"Proves the ordinary-double-point volume-gap conjecture: every singular complex algebraic klt germ of dimension $n\\ge2$, with zero boundary, has normalized volume at most $2(n-1)^n$. Equality holds precisely for an analytic ordinary double point."},{"id":"038","title":"Fujita’s freeness conjecture","summary":"Proves Fujita's freeness conjecture at its sharp bound in every dimension: for a smooth projective complex variety X of dimension n and an ample line bundle L, the adjoint $K_X+mL$ is globally generated for every integer $m\\ge n+1$.","paperIds":["038-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Fujita's freeness conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves Fujita's freeness conjecture at its sharp bound in every dimension: for a smooth projective complex variety $X$ of dimension $n$ and an ample line bundle $L$, the adjoint $K_X+mL$ is globally generated for every integer $m\\ge n+1$."},{"id":"039","title":"Nagata’s conjecture and maximal Seshadri constants","summary":"Proves Nagata's strict inequality $\\sum_i m_i\\lt d\\sqrt r$ for every nonzero effective plane curve of degree d through r ≥ 10 very general complex points, with arbitrary multiplicities mi. It also proves maximal multipoint Seshadri constants $(L^n/r)^{1/n}$ for every smooth polarized projective variety of dimension n ≥ 2 and all sufficiently large r: at very general points over ℂ, and at the geometric generic tuple over any algebraically closed field of positive characteristic.","paperIds":["039-01","039-02","039-03","039-04"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/039.md","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Nagata's conjecture and maximal Seshadri constants","overviewSummary":"Proves Nagata's strict inequality $\\sum_i m_i<d\\sqrt r$ for every nonzero effective plane curve of degree $d$ through $r\\ge10$ very general complex points, with arbitrary multiplicities $m_i$. It also proves maximal multipoint Seshadri constants $(L^n/r)^{1/n}$ for every smooth polarized projective variety of dimension $n\\ge2$ and all sufficiently large $r$: at very general points over $\\mathbb C$, and at the geometric generic tuple over any algebraically closed field of positive characteristic."},{"id":"040","title":"Bloch’s conjecture for complex surfaces","summary":"Proves Bloch's conjecture: for every smooth connected projective complex surface S with $p_g(S)=0$, the Albanese map $\\mathrm{CH}_0(S)^0\\to\\mathrm{Alb}(S)(\\mathbb C)$ on integral degree-zero zero-cycles is an isomorphism. This combines the new $p_g=q=0$ theorem with the classical theorem of Bloch, Kas, and Lieberman.","paperIds":["040-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Bloch's conjecture for complex surfaces","overviewSummary":"Proves Bloch's conjecture: for every smooth connected projective complex surface $S$ with $p_g(S)=0$, the Albanese map $\\mathrm{CH}_0(S)^0\\to\\mathrm{Alb}(S)(\\mathbb C)$ on integral degree-zero zero-cycles is an isomorphism. This combines the new $p_g=q=0$ theorem with the classical theorem of Bloch, Kas, and Lieberman."},{"id":"041","title":"Hyperkähler SYZ and projective-space bases","summary":"Proves the strong hyperkähler SYZ conjecture: every holomorphic line bundle with nonzero nef isotropic first Chern class on a compact irreducible holomorphic symplectic Kähler manifold is semiample. It also proves that every projective Lagrangian fibration with normal projective base has projective space as its base, in every dimension and deformation type.","paperIds":["041-01","041-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Hyperk\\\"ahler SYZ and projective-space bases","overviewSummary":"Proves the strong hyperk\\\"ahler SYZ conjecture: every holomorphic line bundle with nonzero nef isotropic first Chern class on a compact irreducible holomorphic symplectic K\\\"ahler manifold is semiample. It also proves that every projective Lagrangian fibration with normal projective base has projective space as its base, in every dimension and deformation type."},{"id":"042","title":"Oka classification for minimal compact complex surfaces: Kodaira dimension zero and class VII","summary":"Proves that every complex K3 surface is Oka, including nonprojective surfaces. More generally, every connected minimal compact complex surface of Kodaira dimension zero is Oka; a connected minimal compact complex surface of class VII is Oka exactly when it is a Hopf or Enoki surface.","paperIds":["042-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Oka classification for K3 surfaces and other compact complex surfaces","overviewSummary":"Proves that every complex K3 surface is Oka, including nonprojective surfaces. More generally, every connected minimal compact complex surface of Kodaira dimension zero is Oka; a connected minimal compact complex surface of class VII is Oka exactly when it is a Hopf or Enoki surface."},{"id":"043","title":"P = W for fixed-determinant SLn moduli spaces","summary":"Proves $P_k=W_{2k}=W_{2k+1}$ on the full rational cohomology of smooth coprime fixed-determinant, trace-free Higgs moduli spaces and their character varieties for composite ranks over smooth projective complex curves of genus at least two. This includes variant cohomology and, together with the known prime-rank theorems, establishes the fixed-determinant P = W conjecture in every coprime rank.","paperIds":["043-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"$P=W$ for fixed-determinant moduli spaces","overviewSummary":"Proves $P_k=W_{2k}=W_{2k+1}$ on the full rational cohomology of smooth coprime fixed-determinant, trace-free Higgs moduli spaces and their character varieties for composite ranks over smooth projective complex curves of genus at least two. This includes variant cohomology and, together with the known prime-rank theorems, establishes the fixed-determinant $P=W$ conjecture in every coprime rank."},{"id":"044","title":"The equivariant cohomological Hikita conjecture","summary":"Proves the equivariant cohomological Hikita correspondence for every finite quiver, including loops and multiple arrows, with arbitrary dimension and framing vectors and commuting flavor torus. When every semistable point is stable and the gauge action is free, the equivariant cohomology of the Nakajima variety is canonically the coordinate ring of the scheme-theoretic cocharacter-fixed locus of its flavor-deformed Coulomb branch.","paperIds":["044-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"The equivariant cohomological Hikita conjecture for quivers","overviewSummary":"Proves the equivariant cohomological Hikita correspondence for every finite quiver, including loops and multiple arrows, with arbitrary dimension and framing vectors and commuting flavor torus. When every semistable point is stable and the gauge action is free, the equivariant cohomology of the Nakajima variety is canonically the coordinate ring of the scheme-theoretic cocharacter-fixed locus of its flavor-deformed Coulomb branch."},{"id":"046","title":"Shafarevich counterexamples in dimension two and with large fundamental group","summary":"Constructs a smooth projective complex fourfold with large fundamental group whose universal cover contains no positive-dimensional compact analytic subvariety but is neither Stein nor holomorphically convex. A separate smooth projective complex surface already disproves unrestricted Shafarevich holomorphic convexity in dimension two.","paperIds":["046-01","046-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Counterexamples to Shafarevich holomorphic convexity","overviewSummary":"Constructs a smooth projective complex fourfold with large fundamental group whose universal cover contains no positive-dimensional compact analytic subvariety but is neither Stein nor holomorphically convex. A separate smooth projective complex surface already disproves unrestricted Shafarevich holomorphic convexity in dimension two."},{"id":"047","title":"Zariski cancellation and affine fibrations over the complex numbers","summary":"Constructs an integral complex affine fourfold $X\\not\\cong\\mathbb A^4$ with $X\\times\\mathbb A^1\\cong\\mathbb A^5$, disproving affine-space cancellation over ℂ in dimension four. It also disproves the Dolgachev–Weisfeiler affine-fibration conjecture: smooth surjections $X\\to\\mathbb A^1$ and $\\mathbb A^5\\to\\mathbb A^2$ have every residue-field fiber isomorphic to affine three-space but are not Zariski-locally trivial.","paperIds":["047-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/047.md","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Complex counterexamples to cancellation and affine fibrations","overviewSummary":"Constructs an integral complex affine fourfold $X\\not\\cong\\mathbb A^4$ with $X\\times\\mathbb A^1\\cong\\mathbb A^5$, disproving affine-space cancellation over $\\mathbb C$ in dimension four. It also disproves the Dolgachev--Weisfeiler affine-fibration conjecture: smooth surjections $X\\to\\mathbb A^1$ and $\\mathbb A^5\\to\\mathbb A^2$ have every residue-field fiber isomorphic to affine three-space but are not Zariski-locally trivial."},{"id":"048","title":"A characteristic-zero counterexample to Lipman–Zariski","summary":"Constructs a singular normal affine complex surface with free rank-two tangent sheaf, disproving the characteristic-zero Lipman–Zariski conjecture.","paperIds":["048-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"A characteristic-zero counterexample to Lipman--Zariski","overviewSummary":"Constructs a singular normal affine complex surface with free rank-two tangent sheaf, disproving the characteristic-zero Lipman--Zariski conjecture."},{"id":"049","title":"A stable-coordinate counterexample in four variables","summary":"Constructs a polynomial in four complex variables that is not a coordinate but becomes one after adjoining a single variable, disproving the Stable Coordinate conjecture in four variables. Every fiber is affine three-space, yet none of its embeddings is rectifiable, also disproving the Abhyankar–Sathaye conjecture even when all fibers are affine spaces.","paperIds":["049-01","049-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/049.md","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"A stable-coordinate counterexample in four variables","overviewSummary":"Constructs a polynomial in four complex variables that is not a coordinate but becomes one after adjoining a single variable, disproving the Stable Coordinate conjecture in four variables. Every fiber is affine three-space, yet none of its embeddings is rectifiable, also disproving the Abhyankar--Sathaye conjecture even when all fibers are affine spaces."},{"id":"050","title":"A counterexample to Griffiths’ positivity conjecture","summary":"Constructs ample rank-two bundles on $\\mathbb P^1\\times\\mathbb P^1$ with no smooth Hermitian metric of strictly Griffiths-positive curvature, disproving Griffiths' positivity conjecture already on the quadric surface.","paperIds":["050-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/050.md","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"A counterexample to Griffiths' positivity conjecture","overviewSummary":"Constructs ample rank-two bundles on $\\mathbb P^1\\times\\mathbb P^1$ with no smooth Hermitian metric of strictly Griffiths-positive curvature, disproving Griffiths' positivity conjecture already on the quadric surface."},{"id":"051","title":"Kobayashi’s canonical-ampleness conjecture","summary":"Every compact connected Kähler manifold of positive complex dimension with no nonconstant entire curve has ample canonical bundle and is therefore projective. This proves Kobayashi's canonical-ampleness conjecture in the smooth compact Kähler setting.","paperIds":["051-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Kobayashi's canonical-ampleness conjecture","overviewSummary":"Every compact connected K\\\"ahler manifold of positive complex dimension with no nonconstant entire curve has ample canonical bundle and is therefore projective. This proves Kobayashi's canonical-ampleness conjecture in the smooth compact K\\\"ahler setting."},{"id":"052","title":"Tangent splittings and product decompositions","summary":"A splitting of the tangent bundle of a compact Kähler manifold into two integrable holomorphic subbundles induces a compatible product decomposition of its universal cover, proving the two-summand form of Beauville's splitting conjecture. On smooth rationally connected projective manifolds, both summands are automatically integrable, establishing Höring's conjecture and the corresponding product decomposition.","paperIds":["052-01","052-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/052.md","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Tangent-bundle splittings and universal covers","overviewSummary":"A splitting of the tangent bundle of a compact K\\\"ahler manifold into two integrable holomorphic subbundles induces a compatible product decomposition of its universal cover, proving the two-summand form of Beauville's splitting conjecture. On smooth rationally connected projective manifolds, both summands are automatically integrable, establishing H\\\"oring's conjecture and the corresponding product decomposition."},{"id":"053","title":"A counterexample to Pixton completeness in Chow","summary":"Constructs a tautological relation on a moduli space of stable pointed curves that vanishes in rational Chow, hence in rational cohomology, but lies outside Pixton's original relation span. This disproves the Chow and rational-cohomological forms of his original completeness conjecture.","paperIds":["053-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"A counterexample to Pixton's original completeness conjecture","overviewSummary":"Constructs a tautological relation on a moduli space of stable pointed curves that vanishes in rational Chow, hence in rational cohomology, but lies outside Pixton's original relation span. This disproves the Chow and rational-cohomological forms of his original completeness conjecture."},{"id":"054","title":"Irrational cubic fourfolds with Hodge-theoretic and categorical K3 associations","summary":"For every sufficiently large admissible Hassett discriminant, a very general smooth complex cubic fourfold is irrational despite having both an untwisted geometric K3 category and an integral Hodge-theoretic K3 association. This disproves Kuznetsov's rationality conjecture and the sufficiency of the associated-K3 criterion for rationality; the discriminant threshold is ineffective.","paperIds":["054-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Counterexamples to Kuznetsov's rationality conjecture","overviewSummary":"For every sufficiently large admissible Hassett discriminant, a very general smooth complex cubic fourfold is irrational despite having both an untwisted geometric K3 category and an integral Hodge-theoretic K3 association. This disproves Kuznetsov's rationality conjecture and the sufficiency of the associated-K3 criterion for rationality; the discriminant threshold is ineffective."},{"id":"055","title":"Gepner symmetry and large-volume stability on threefolds","summary":"Proves Toda's Gepner conjecture for every smooth complex quintic threefold, constructing a numerical Bridgeland stability condition with the prescribed phase shift 2/5. Also constructs numerical Bridgeland stability conditions at every sufficiently large volume on all smooth projective complex threefolds with trivial canonical bundle, with the exact ordinary and square-root-Todd central charges.","paperIds":["055-01","055-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Toda's Gepner conjecture and large-volume stability","overviewSummary":"Proves Toda's Gepner conjecture for every smooth complex quintic threefold, constructing a numerical Bridgeland stability condition with the prescribed phase shift $2/5$. Also constructs numerical Bridgeland stability conditions at every sufficiently large volume on all smooth projective complex threefolds with trivial canonical bundle, with the exact ordinary and square-root-Todd central charges."},{"id":"056","title":"Termination of projective and Kähler fourfold minimal model programs","summary":"Proves termination of every existing generalized log canonical flip sequence on globally Weil ℚ-factorial compact Kähler fourfolds, with rational boundary, fixed rational analytically nef b-data, and projective small flip diagrams with the prescribed ample signs. Also proves termination of arbitrary permitted minimal model programs for projective log canonical fourfolds with rational boundary in characteristic zero.","paperIds":["056-01","056-02","056-03","056-04","056-05"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Termination of fourfold minimal model programs","overviewSummary":"Proves termination of every existing generalized log canonical flip sequence on globally Weil $\\mathbb Q$-factorial compact K\\\"ahler fourfolds, with rational boundary, fixed rational analytically nef b-data, and projective small flip diagrams with the prescribed ample signs. Also proves termination of arbitrary permitted minimal model programs for projective log canonical fourfolds with rational boundary in characteristic zero."},{"id":"057","title":"Fundamental groups of special complex varieties and root orbifolds","summary":"Proves Campana's abelianity conjecture: special compact Kähler manifolds have virtually abelian fundamental groups. Using this theorem, establishes the same conclusion for order-two root orbifolds of smooth projective complex fourfolds along one nonempty smooth connected divisor, when special in the stated differential-line sense. For smooth special complex quasi-projective varieties, proves that every finite-dimensional complex linear representation of the fundamental group has virtually nilpotent image of class at most two.","paperIds":["057-01","057-02","057-03"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Campana's abelianity conjecture and special varieties","overviewSummary":"Proves Campana's abelianity conjecture: special compact K\\\"ahler manifolds have virtually abelian fundamental groups. Using this theorem, establishes the same conclusion for order-two root orbifolds of smooth projective complex fourfolds along one nonempty smooth connected divisor, when special in the stated differential-line sense. For smooth special complex quasi-projective varieties, proves that every finite-dimensional complex linear representation of the fundamental group has virtually nilpotent image of class at most two."},{"id":"058","title":"Semialgebraic universal covers and bounded domains","summary":"Proves the Kollár–Pardon conjecture: the semialgebraic universal covers of connected normal projective complex varieties are exactly products $D\\times\\mathbb C^m\\times F$, with D bounded symmetric and F simply connected, normal, and projective. A universal cover is quasi-projective exactly when the bounded symmetric factor is absent. In particular, a smooth projective variety covered by ℂn has a finite étale cover by an abelian variety.","paperIds":["058-01","058-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/058.md","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"The Koll\\'ar--Pardon universal-cover conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves the Koll\\'ar--Pardon conjecture: the semialgebraic universal covers of connected normal projective complex varieties are exactly products $D\\times\\mathbb C^m\\times F$, with $D$ bounded symmetric and $F$ simply connected, normal, and projective. A universal cover is quasi-projective exactly when the bounded symmetric factor is absent. In particular, a smooth projective variety covered by $\\mathbb C^n$ has a finite étale cover by an abelian variety."},{"id":"059","title":"Counterexamples to Zariski’s multiplicity conjecture","summary":"Disproves Zariski's multiplicity conjecture by constructing reduced holomorphic hypersurface germs that are ambiently homeomorphic but have different multiplicities. The examples include hypersurfaces in ℂ4 with isolated critical points and multiplicities four and five.","paperIds":["059-01","059-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Counterexamples to Zariski's multiplicity conjecture","overviewSummary":"Disproves Zariski's multiplicity conjecture by constructing reduced holomorphic hypersurface germs that are ambiently homeomorphic but have different multiplicities. The examples include hypersurfaces in $\\mathbb C^4$ with isolated critical points and multiplicities four and five."},{"id":"060","title":"The Global Spherical Shell conjecture","summary":"Every connected minimal compact complex surface of class VII with $b_2\\gt 0$ contains a global spherical shell, proving the positive-b2 Global Spherical Shell conjecture. Such a shell is a holomorphically embedded neighborhood of the standard three-sphere in $\\mathbb C^2\\setminus\\{0\\}$ whose complement is connected.","paperIds":["060-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"The Global Spherical Shell conjecture","overviewSummary":"Every connected minimal compact complex surface of class VII with $b_2>0$ contains a global spherical shell, proving the positive-$b_2$ Global Spherical Shell conjecture. Such a shell is a holomorphically embedded neighborhood of the standard three-sphere in $\\mathbb C^2\\setminus\\{0\\}$ whose complement is connected."},{"id":"062","title":"Projective contact classification and the LeBrun–Salamon conjecture","summary":"Proves the LeBrun–Salamon conjecture: every closed connected positive quaternionic-Kähler manifold of real dimension at least eight is homothetic to a compact symmetric Wolf space. It also proves contact-Fano homogeneity and classifies smooth connected complex projective contact manifolds of complex dimension at least three: those with $b_2=1$ are adjoint varieties with their canonical contact structures, while those with $b_2\\ge2$ have underlying manifold $\\mathbb P(T^*Z)$ for a smooth projective variety Z.","paperIds":["062-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"The LeBrun--Salamon conjecture and projective contact classification","overviewSummary":"Proves the LeBrun--Salamon conjecture: every closed connected positive quaternionic-K\\\"ahler manifold of real dimension at least eight is homothetic to a compact symmetric Wolf space. It also proves contact-Fano homogeneity and classifies smooth connected complex projective contact manifolds of complex dimension at least three: those with $b_2=1$ are adjoint varieties with their canonical contact structures, while those with $b_2\\ge2$ have underlying manifold $\\mathbb P(T^*Z)$ for a smooth projective variety $Z$."},{"id":"063","title":"The generalized Mukai conjecture","summary":"Proves the generalized Mukai conjecture: every positive-dimensional smooth complex projective Fano manifold of dimension n, Picard number ρ and pseudoindex ι satisfies $\\rho(\\iota-1)\\le n$, with equality exactly for $(\\mathbb P^{\\iota-1})^\\rho$. Here the pseudoindex is the least anticanonical degree of a rational curve.","paperIds":["063-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"The generalized Mukai conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves the generalized Mukai conjecture: every positive-dimensional smooth complex projective Fano manifold of dimension $n$, Picard number $\\rho$ and pseudoindex $\\iota$ satisfies $\\rho(\\iota-1)\\le n$, with equality exactly for $(\\mathbb P^{\\iota-1})^\\rho$. Here the pseudoindex is the least anticanonical degree of a rational curve."},{"id":"064","title":"Topological triviality of μ-constant surface singularities","summary":"Proves topological right-triviality for every holomorphic one-parameter family of isolated hypersurface singularities in ℂ3 with constant Milnor number, resolving the surface case of the μ-constant problem. After shrinking the parameter disk and representatives, ambient homeomorphisms vary jointly continuously, fix the origin section and preserve the defining functions.","paperIds":["064-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"The $\\mu$-constant problem for surface singularities","overviewSummary":"Proves topological right-triviality for every holomorphic one-parameter family of isolated hypersurface singularities in $\\mathbb C^3$ with constant Milnor number, resolving the surface case of the $\\mu$-constant problem. After shrinking the parameter disk and representatives, ambient homeomorphisms vary jointly continuously, fix the origin section and preserve the defining functions."},{"id":"065","title":"Virasoro constraints for complete intersections and projective-bundle towers","summary":"Proves the full ordinary unreduced descendant Virasoro conjecture for smooth complete intersections in complex projective space, in every genus and curve class with arbitrary cohomology insertions. The constraints also pass from any smooth projective complex base satisfying them to the projectivization of every algebraic vector bundle of rank at least two, and hence to projective-bundle towers.","paperIds":["065-01","065-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Virasoro constraints for complete intersections and projective bundles","overviewSummary":"Proves the full ordinary unreduced descendant Virasoro conjecture for smooth complete intersections in complex projective space, in every genus and curve class with arbitrary cohomology insertions. The constraints also pass from any smooth projective complex base satisfying them to the projectivization of every algebraic vector bundle of rank at least two, and hence to projective-bundle towers."},{"id":"066","title":"Bounded klt complements for Fano contractions","summary":"Proves the finite-rational-coefficient form of Shokurov’s bounded-klt-complement conjecture for ϵ-lc complex Fano-type pairs with nef anti-log-canonical divisor. For ϵ-lc Fano contractions over any algebraically closed characteristic-zero field, it gives klt complements near every base point, with index bounded only by dimension and positive rational ϵ.","paperIds":["066-01","066-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/066.md","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Bounded klt complements for Fano contractions","overviewSummary":"Proves the finite-rational-coefficient form of Shokurov’s bounded-klt-complement conjecture for $\\epsilon$-lc complex Fano-type pairs with nef anti-log-canonical divisor. For $\\epsilon$-lc Fano contractions over any algebraically closed characteristic-zero field, it gives klt complements near every base point, with index bounded only by dimension and positive rational $\\epsilon$."},{"id":"067","title":"The Campana–Peternell conjecture in dimension six","summary":"Proves the Campana–Peternell conjecture in complex dimension six: every smooth connected complex projective Fano sixfold with nef tangent bundle is rational homogeneous.","paperIds":["067-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"The Campana--Peternell conjecture in dimension six","overviewSummary":"Proves the Campana--Peternell conjecture in complex dimension six: every smooth connected complex projective Fano sixfold with nef tangent bundle is rational homogeneous."},{"id":"068","title":"Anticanonical nonvanishing in every dimension","summary":"If X is a smooth connected complex projective variety and $-K_X$ admits a smooth Hermitian metric with nonnegative curvature, then $H^0(X,-mK_X)\\ne0$ for some m > 0. Thus smooth semipositivity forces a nonzero section of a positive tensor power of the anticanonical bundle in every dimension.","paperIds":["068-01","068-02","068-03","068-04","068-05","068-06","068-07"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Anticanonical nonvanishing under smooth semipositivity","overviewSummary":"If $X$ is a smooth connected complex projective variety and $-K_X$ admits a smooth Hermitian metric with nonnegative curvature, then $H^0(X,-mK_X)\\ne0$ for some $m>0$. Thus smooth semipositivity forces a nonzero section of a positive tensor power of the anticanonical bundle in every dimension."},{"id":"069","title":"Global quantum geometric Langlands at irrational level","summary":"Proves the unramified de Rham quantum geometric Langlands equivalence for every connected simple complex algebraic group on every smooth projective connected complex curve, at every shifted level $c\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus\\mathbb Q$. It identifies the full derived categories of twisted D-modules for the group and its Langlands dual, retaining all global forms and connected components.","paperIds":["069-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","overviewTitle":"Quantum geometric Langlands at irrational level","overviewSummary":"Proves the unramified de Rham quantum geometric Langlands equivalence for every connected simple complex algebraic group on every smooth projective connected complex curve, at every shifted level $c\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus\\mathbb Q$. It identifies the full derived categories of twisted $D$-modules for the group and its Langlands dual, retaining all global forms and connected components."},{"id":"071","title":"Koebe’s circle-domain conjecture","summary":"Resolves the existence part of Koebe's circle-domain conjecture: every domain in the Riemann sphere is conformally equivalent to a domain whose complementary components are round disks or points. It also proves that circle domains with conformally removable boundary are rigid, meaning every conformal equivalence to another circle domain is Möbius, establishing this direction of the He–Schramm conjecture.","paperIds":["071-01","071-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/071.md","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","overviewTitle":"Koebe's circle-domain conjecture and circle-domain rigidity","overviewSummary":"Resolves the existence part of Koebe's circle-domain conjecture: every domain in the Riemann sphere is conformally equivalent to a domain whose complementary components are round disks or points. It also proves that circle domains with conformally removable boundary are rigid, meaning every conformal equivalence to another circle domain is M\\\"obius, establishing this direction of the He--Schramm conjecture."},{"id":"072","title":"Brennan's conjecture and the integral-means spectrum","summary":"Proves Brennan's conjecture: for every conformal bijection ϕ from a simply connected plane domain onto the disk, $|\\phi'|^s$ is area-integrable for $4/3\\lt s\\lt 4$. The sharp universal integral-means identity is $B_{\\mathcal S}(t)=|t|-1$ for t ≤ −2. A strict bound $B_b(-1)\\lt 1/4$ for bounded univalent functions disproves Kraetzer's prediction at that parameter.","paperIds":["072-01","072-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/072.md","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","overviewTitle":"Brennan's conjecture and a counterexample to Kraetzer's prediction","overviewSummary":"Proves Brennan's conjecture: for every conformal bijection $\\phi$ from a simply connected plane domain onto the disk, $|\\phi'|^s$ is area-integrable for $4/3<s<4$. The sharp universal integral-means identity is $B_{\\mathcal S}(t)=|t|-1$ for $t\\le-2$. A strict bound $B_b(-1)<1/4$ for bounded univalent functions disproves Kraetzer's prediction at that parameter."},{"id":"073","title":"The Falconer distance conjecture","summary":"Resolves the Falconer distance conjecture in every dimension d ≥ 2: every compact set $E\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ with Hausdorff dimension greater than $d/2$ determines a set of Euclidean distances of positive Lebesgue measure.","paperIds":["073-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/073.md","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","overviewTitle":"The Falconer distance conjecture","overviewSummary":"Resolves the Falconer distance conjecture in every dimension $d\\ge2$: every compact set $E\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ with Hausdorff dimension greater than $d/2$ determines a set of Euclidean distances of positive Lebesgue measure."},{"id":"074","title":"Kakeya in three and four dimensions","summary":"Resolves the Kakeya maximal conjecture in three dimensions and the Hausdorff-dimension conjecture in four. In three dimensions, the radius-δ tube maximal operator maps $L^3(\\mathbb R^3)$ to $L^3(S^2)$ with norm $O_\\varepsilon(\\delta^{-\\varepsilon})$ for every ε > 0. In four dimensions, every set containing a unit segment in every direction has Hausdorff dimension four.","paperIds":["074-01","074-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","overviewTitle":"Kakeya in three and four dimensions","overviewSummary":"Resolves the Kakeya maximal conjecture in three dimensions and the Hausdorff-dimension conjecture in four. In three dimensions, the radius-$\\delta$ tube maximal operator maps $L^3(\\mathbb R^3)$ to $L^3(S^2)$ with norm $O_\\varepsilon(\\delta^{-\\varepsilon})$ for every $\\varepsilon>0$. In four dimensions, every set containing a unit segment in every direction has Hausdorff dimension four."},{"id":"075","title":"The $L\\log L$ Fourier-convergence conjecture","summary":"Proves that the ordinary symmetric Fourier partial sums of every complex-valued function in $L\\log L(\\mathbb T)$ converge almost everywhere along the full sequence. This resolves the classical sufficiency conjecture at the $L\\log L$ scale.","paperIds":["075-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/075.md","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","overviewTitle":"The $L\\log L$ Fourier-convergence conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves that the ordinary symmetric Fourier partial sums of every complex-valued function in $L\\log L(\\mathbb T)$ converge almost everywhere along the full sequence. This resolves the classical sufficiency conjecture at the $L\\log L$ scale."},{"id":"076","title":"Real ultraflat Littlewood polynomials and unbounded binary merit factors","summary":"Constructs polynomials with N consecutive coefficients in $\\{-1,1\\}$ whose modulus is $(1+o(1))\\sqrt N$ uniformly on the entire unit circle, for every sufficiently large integer length N. Thus real Littlewood polynomials are ultraflat, including at the real endpoints. Their binary merit factors tend to infinity, disproving Turyn's bounded-merit-factor conjecture.","paperIds":["076-01","076-02","076-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/076.md","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","overviewTitle":"Ultraflat real Littlewood polynomials","overviewSummary":"Constructs polynomials with $N$ consecutive coefficients in $\\{-1,1\\}$ whose modulus is $(1+o(1))\\sqrt N$ uniformly on the entire unit circle, for every sufficiently large integer length $N$. Thus real Littlewood polynomials are ultraflat, including at the real endpoints. Their binary merit factors tend to infinity, disproving Turyn's bounded-merit-factor conjecture."},{"id":"077","title":"Fourier restriction for positively curved surfaces","summary":"Proves the diagonal Fourier extension conjecture for positively curved surfaces in three dimensions. For every compact smooth positively curved surface $\\Sigma\\subset\\mathbb R^3$, including surfaces with boundary, the extension operator is bounded from $L^p(\\Sigma)$ to $L^p(\\mathbb R^3)$ for every p > 3.","paperIds":["077-01","077-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","overviewTitle":"Fourier restriction for positively curved surfaces","overviewSummary":"Proves the diagonal Fourier extension conjecture for positively curved surfaces in three dimensions. For every compact smooth positively curved surface $\\Sigma\\subset\\mathbb R^3$, including surfaces with boundary, the extension operator is bounded from $L^p(\\Sigma)$ to $L^p(\\mathbb R^3)$ for every $p>3$."},{"id":"078","title":"The three-dimensional Bochner–Riesz conjecture","summary":"Resolves the three-dimensional Bochner–Riesz conjecture in its strict range: the Bochner–Riesz multipliers of order δ are bounded on $L^p(\\mathbb R^3)$ for every $1\\le p\\le\\infty$ whenever $\\delta\\gt \\max\\{3|1/p-1/2|-1/2,0\\}$.","paperIds":["078-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","overviewTitle":"The three-dimensional Bochner--Riesz conjecture","overviewSummary":"Resolves the three-dimensional Bochner--Riesz conjecture in its strict range: the Bochner--Riesz multipliers of order $\\delta$ are bounded on $L^p(\\mathbb R^3)$ for every $1\\le p\\le\\infty$ whenever $\\delta>\\max\\{3|1/p-1/2|-1/2,0\\}$."},{"id":"079","title":"Local smoothing in three dimensions","summary":"Resolves Sogge's local smoothing conjecture for the Euclidean wave equation in three spatial dimensions. The estimate holds throughout the full strict range $2\\lt p\\lt \\infty$, with Sobolev regularity above $\\max\\{0,1-3/p\\}$. In particular, the critical L3 estimate holds with every positive Sobolev loss.","paperIds":["079-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","overviewTitle":"Sogge's local smoothing conjecture in dimension three","overviewSummary":"Resolves Sogge's local smoothing conjecture for the Euclidean wave equation in three spatial dimensions. The estimate holds throughout the full strict range $2<p<\\infty$, with Sobolev regularity above $\\max\\{0,1-3/p\\}$. In particular, the critical $L^3$ estimate holds with every positive Sobolev loss."},{"id":"080","title":"The exact Sobolev endpoint for Schrödinger convergence","summary":"Proves almost-everywhere convergence $e^{it\\Delta}f\\to f$ as $t\\downarrow0$ for every $f\\in H^{n/(2(n+1))}(\\mathbb R^n)$ and every dimension n ≥ 2. This attains the sharp Sobolev equality case of Carleson's Schrödinger convergence problem, including the planar endpoint H1/3.","paperIds":["080-01","080-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","overviewTitle":"The Sobolev endpoint in Carleson's Schr\\\"odinger convergence problem","overviewSummary":"Proves almost-everywhere convergence $e^{it\\Delta}f\\to f$ as $t\\downarrow0$ for every $f\\in H^{n/(2(n+1))}(\\mathbb R^n)$ and every dimension $n\\ge2$. This attains the sharp Sobolev equality case of Carleson's Schr\\\"odinger convergence problem, including the planar endpoint $H^{1/3}$."},{"id":"081","title":"Riesz transforms and rectifiability in higher codimension","summary":"Resolves the remaining higher-codimension Riesz-transform rectifiability problem: for d ≥ 4 and $2\\le n\\le d-2$, an n-Ahlfors–David regular Radon measure on ℝd is uniformly n-rectifiable whenever its n-dimensional Riesz transform is uniformly L2-bounded over all positive hard truncations. The rectifiability bounds depend only on dimension, regularity and operator bounds.","paperIds":["081-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/081.md","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","overviewTitle":"The David--Semmes Riesz-transform problem in higher codimension","overviewSummary":"Resolves the remaining higher-codimension Riesz-transform rectifiability problem: for $d\\ge4$ and $2\\le n\\le d-2$, an $n$-Ahlfors--David regular Radon measure on $\\mathbb R^d$ is uniformly $n$-rectifiable whenever its $n$-dimensional Riesz transform is uniformly $L^2$-bounded over all positive hard truncations. The rectifiability bounds depend only on dimension, regularity and operator bounds."},{"id":"082","title":"Annular variation and dyadic absolute bounds for the triangular Hilbert transform","summary":"Proves maximal and annular r-variation bounds, for every r > 2, from complex $L^3(\\mathbb R^2)\\times L^3(\\mathbb R^2)$ to $L^{3/2}(\\mathbb R^2)$. The maximal estimate controls both hard truncation endpoints and gives almost-everywhere and norm convergence. Pairing with a third input settles the triangular Hilbert transform estimate at the symmetric $L^3\\times L^3\\times L^3$ point.","paperIds":["082-01","082-02","082-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/082.md","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","overviewTitle":"Maximal and variational bounds for the triangular Hilbert transform","overviewSummary":"Proves maximal and annular $r$-variation bounds, for every $r>2$, from complex $L^3(\\mathbb R^2)\\times L^3(\\mathbb R^2)$ to $L^{3/2}(\\mathbb R^2)$. The maximal estimate controls both hard truncation endpoints and gives almost-everywhere and norm convergence. Pairing with a third input settles the triangular Hilbert transform estimate at the symmetric $L^3\\times L^3\\times L^3$ point."},{"id":"083","title":"Hilbert transforms along Lipschitz directions","summary":"Proves a uniform strong L2 bound for the planar Hilbert transform along any Lipschitz unit vector field, at integration lengths bounded by an absolute multiple of its reciprocal Lipschitz constant. The estimate is uniform over inner truncations and yields an L2-bounded principal-value operator, establishing Stein's weak-type conjecture at this short scale.","paperIds":["083-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/083.md","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","overviewTitle":"Stein's conjecture for Hilbert transforms along Lipschitz directions","overviewSummary":"Proves a uniform strong $L^2$ bound for the planar Hilbert transform along any Lipschitz unit vector field, at integration lengths bounded by an absolute multiple of its reciprocal Lipschitz constant. The estimate is uniform over inner truncations and yields an $L^2$-bounded principal-value operator, establishing Stein's weak-type conjecture at this short scale."},{"id":"084","title":"The geometric case of the Erdős similarity conjecture","summary":"For every fixed $q\\in(0,1)$, constructs compact subsets of $[0,1]$ with measure arbitrarily close to one containing no translated and nontrivially dilated copy of $\\{q^n:n\\ge1\\}$, with dilations of either sign. This resolves the geometric-progression case of the Erdős similarity conjecture for every ratio.","paperIds":["084-01","084-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/084.md","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","overviewTitle":"The geometric case of the Erd\\H{o}s similarity conjecture","overviewSummary":"For every fixed $q\\in(0,1)$, constructs compact subsets of $[0,1]$ with measure arbitrarily close to one containing no translated and nontrivially dilated copy of $\\{q^n:n\\ge1\\}$, with dilations of either sign. This resolves the geometric-progression case of the Erd\\H{o}s similarity conjecture for every ratio."},{"id":"085","title":"Endpoint Sobolev regularity of centered disk averages","summary":"Resolves the planar centered-disk case of the Hajłasz–Onninen maximal-function regularity problem. For every real $f\\in W^{1,1}(\\mathbb R^2)$, the centered disk maximal function satisfies $\\|\\nabla Mf\\|_1\\le C\\|\\nabla f\\|_1$ with an absolute constant. It belongs locally to $W^{1,1}$ and has a globally integrable weak gradient.","paperIds":["085-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/085.md","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","overviewTitle":"Endpoint regularity of the planar centered maximal function","overviewSummary":"Resolves the planar centered-disk case of the Haj{\\l}asz--Onninen maximal-function regularity problem. For every real $f\\in W^{1,1}(\\mathbb R^2)$, the centered disk maximal function satisfies $\\|\\nabla Mf\\|_1\\le C\\|\\nabla f\\|_1$ with an absolute constant. It belongs locally to $W^{1,1}$ and has a globally integrable weak gradient."},{"id":"086","title":"An L3 bound for the trilinear Hilbert transform","summary":"Proves that the principal-value trilinear Hilbert transform with shifts $x-t$, $x-2t$, $x-3t$ is bounded from $L^3(\\mathbb R)^3$ to $L^1(\\mathbb R)$. This resolves the L3 exponent case of the standard conjecture for slopes 1, 2, 3.","paperIds":["086-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","overviewTitle":"An $L^3$ bound for the trilinear Hilbert transform","overviewSummary":"Proves that the principal-value trilinear Hilbert transform with shifts $x-t$, $x-2t$, $x-3t$ is bounded from $L^3(\\mathbb R)^3$ to $L^1(\\mathbb R)$. This resolves the $L^3$ exponent case of the standard conjecture for slopes $1,2,3$."},{"id":"087","title":"The Mahler conjectures, functional inequalities and polar-product symplectic width","summary":"Resolves the symmetric and nonsymmetric geometric Mahler conjectures in every dimension, with Hanner polytopes and simplices as the respective volume-product minimizers and all equality cases classified. The corresponding sharp functional Mahler inequalities also hold. For n ≥ 2, every symmetric polar product $K\\times K^\\circ$ in dimension $2n$ has Gromov width 4.","paperIds":["087-01","087-02","087-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/087.md","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","overviewTitle":"The Mahler conjectures and symplectic width","overviewSummary":"Resolves the symmetric and nonsymmetric geometric Mahler conjectures in every dimension, with Hanner polytopes and simplices as the respective volume-product minimizers and all equality cases classified. The corresponding sharp functional Mahler inequalities also hold. For $n\\ge2$, every symmetric polar product $K\\times K^\\circ$ in dimension $2n$ has Gromov width $4$."},{"id":"088","title":"Sharp projection-body inequalities and a counterexample to simplex maximization","summary":"Proves Petty's projection-volume conjecture in the remaining dimensions n ≥ 4: ellipsoids uniquely minimize projection-body volume at fixed body volume. Also establishes the full Lutwak–Petty projection inequalities. In contrast, products of simplices exceed Brannen's proposed simplex maximum for normalized projection-body volume by an exponential factor in every sufficiently large dimension.","paperIds":["088-01","088-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/088.md","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","overviewTitle":"Petty's projection-volume conjecture and simplex counterexamples","overviewSummary":"Proves Petty's projection-volume conjecture in the remaining dimensions $n\\ge4$: ellipsoids uniquely minimize projection-body volume at fixed body volume. Also establishes the full Lutwak--Petty projection inequalities. In contrast, products of simplices exceed Brannen's proposed simplex maximum for normalized projection-body volume by an exponential factor in every sufficiently large dimension."},{"id":"089","title":"Bounded-distortion L1 embeddings of planar and bounded-treewidth graphs","summary":"Resolves the planar and bounded-treewidth cases of the Gupta–Newman–Rabinovich–Sinclair conjecture. Shortest-path metrics of finite connected graphs with arbitrary positive edge lengths embed into real L1 with universal distortion for planar graphs, and distortion depending only on treewidth for bounded-treewidth graphs. The corresponding multicommodity flow–cut gaps are uniformly bounded.","paperIds":["089-01","089-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/089.md","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","overviewTitle":"Bounded-distortion $L_1$ embeddings of planar and bounded-treewidth graphs","overviewSummary":"Resolves the planar and bounded-treewidth cases of the Gupta--Newman--Rabinovich--Sinclair conjecture. Shortest-path metrics of finite connected graphs with arbitrary positive edge lengths embed into real $L_1$ with universal distortion for planar graphs, and distortion depending only on treewidth for bounded-treewidth graphs. The corresponding multicommodity flow--cut gaps are uniformly bounded."},{"id":"090","title":"Triangular-lattice optimality, long-range Riesz and Coulomb energies, and spherical logarithmic energy","summary":"Proves that the triangular lattice minimizes the lower limit of energy per particle for every nonnegative completely monotone potential of squared distance among locally finite planar configurations of centered-disk density one. It also minimizes unit-background renormalized Riesz energies for $0\\lt s\\lt 2$ and Coulomb energy, resolving Sandier–Serfaty and the two-dimensional Brauchart–Hardin–Saff conjecture on the linear term of optimal spherical logarithmic energy.","paperIds":["090-01","090-02","090-03","090-04"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/090.md","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","overviewTitle":"Universal optimality of the triangular lattice","overviewSummary":"Proves that the triangular lattice minimizes the lower limit of energy per particle for every nonnegative completely monotone potential of squared distance among locally finite planar configurations of centered-disk density one. It also minimizes unit-background renormalized Riesz energies for $0<s<2$ and Coulomb energy, resolving Sandier--Serfaty and the two-dimensional Brauchart--Hardin--Saff conjecture on the linear term of optimal spherical logarithmic energy."},{"id":"091","title":"Logarithmic and Lp Brunn–Minkowski inequalities and the B-conjecture","summary":"Proves the logarithmic Brunn–Minkowski inequality for origin-symmetric convex bodies in every dimension, and the scalar-dilation B-conjecture for all even log-concave Radon measures. For Lebesgue volume it also proves the additive Lp Brunn–Minkowski inequality for full-dimensional origin-symmetric convex bodies throughout $0\\lt p\\lt 1$.","paperIds":["091-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/091.md","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","overviewTitle":"The logarithmic Brunn--Minkowski conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves the logarithmic Brunn--Minkowski inequality for origin-symmetric convex bodies in every dimension, and the scalar-dilation B-conjecture for all even log-concave Radon measures. For Lebesgue volume it also proves the additive $L_p$ Brunn--Minkowski inequality for full-dimensional origin-symmetric convex bodies throughout $0<p<1$."},{"id":"092","title":"The optimal order of convex-body covering density","summary":"Determines the optimal worst-case covering density as $\\Theta(n\\log n)$, for both lattice and unrestricted translative coverings. Every convex body in ℝn, n ≥ 2, admits a lattice covering of density at most $Cn\\log n$; centrally symmetric examples in every sufficiently large dimension require at least $cn\\log n$ even without the lattice restriction, for absolute $c,C\\gt 0$.","paperIds":["092-01","092-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/092.md","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","overviewTitle":"The optimal order of convex-body covering density","overviewSummary":"Determines the optimal worst-case covering density as $\\Theta(n\\log n)$, for both lattice and unrestricted translative coverings. Every convex body in $\\mathbb R^n$, $n\\ge2$, admits a lattice covering of density at most $Cn\\log n$; centrally symmetric examples in every sufficiently large dimension require at least $cn\\log n$ even without the lattice restriction, for absolute $c,C>0$."},{"id":"093","title":"Dimension-free logarithmic Sobolev inequality for subgaussian log-concave measures","summary":"Proves a dimension-free logarithmic Sobolev inequality for centered log-concave densities with uniformly subgaussian linear marginals, with constant bounded by a universal multiple of the squared linear subgaussian parameter.","paperIds":["093-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","overviewTitle":"Dimension-free logarithmic Sobolev inequality for subgaussian log-concave measures","overviewSummary":"Proves a dimension-free logarithmic Sobolev inequality for centered log-concave densities with uniformly subgaussian linear marginals, with constant bounded by a universal multiple of the squared linear subgaussian parameter."},{"id":"094","title":"Subpolynomial dimension reduction in Lp","summary":"For every fixed $1\\lt p\\lt \\infty$ and distortion D > 1, every n-point subset of real Lp embeds into $\\ell_p^d$ with distortion at most D and dimension $d=n^{o(1)}$, answering Naor's sublinear-dimension question for p ≠ 2. In contrast, exact embeddings require worst-case dimension $\\Theta(n^2)$ when p ≠ 2.","paperIds":["094-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/094.md","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","overviewTitle":"Subpolynomial dimension reduction in $L_p$","overviewSummary":"For every fixed $1<p<\\infty$ and distortion $D>1$, every $n$-point subset of real $L_p$ embeds into $\\ell_p^d$ with distortion at most $D$ and dimension $d=n^{o(1)}$, answering Naor's sublinear-dimension question for $p\\ne2$. In contrast, exact embeddings require worst-case dimension $\\Theta(n^2)$ when $p\\ne2$."},{"id":"095","title":"Hyperbolicity cones without semidefinite lifts","summary":"Disproves the Projected Lax conjecture: some hyperbolicity cones are not spectrahedral shadows. The examples admit no exact finite affine semidefinite lift, regardless of the number of auxiliary variables or the real coefficients used. This also disproves the generalized Lax conjecture that every hyperbolicity cone is spectrahedral.","paperIds":["095-01","095-02","095-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/095.md","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","overviewTitle":"Hyperbolicity cones without semidefinite lifts","overviewSummary":"Disproves the Projected Lax conjecture: some hyperbolicity cones are not spectrahedral shadows. The examples admit no exact finite affine semidefinite lift, regardless of the number of auxiliary variables or the real coefficients used. This also disproves the generalized Lax conjecture that every hyperbolicity cone is spectrahedral."},{"id":"096","title":"The Gaussian propeller conjecture in every dimension","summary":"Proves that the sum of squared Gaussian first moments of any finite measurable partition is at most $9/(8\\pi)$. In dimension at least two, three planar sectors of angle $2\\pi/3$, extended orthogonally, attain the bound. Combined with the separate Unique Games theorem, this proves NP-hardness of improving the loss factor $(8\\pi/9)(1-1/k)$ for identity-target kernel clustering with fixed k ≥ 3 on rational centered positive semidefinite inputs.","paperIds":["096-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/096.md","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","overviewTitle":"The Gaussian propeller conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves that the sum of squared Gaussian first moments of any finite measurable partition is at most $9/(8\\pi)$. In dimension at least two, three planar sectors of angle $2\\pi/3$, extended orthogonally, attain the bound. Combined with the separate Unique Games theorem, this proves NP-hardness of improving the loss factor $(8\\pi/9)(1-1/k)$ for identity-target kernel clustering with fixed $k\\ge3$ on rational centered positive semidefinite inputs."},{"id":"097","title":"The Euclidean Steinitz–Bergström bound","summary":"Proves that any finite sequence in the Euclidean unit ball of ℝd admits signs keeping every partial sum within $C\\sqrt d$, independently of length. Consequently every zero-sum family can be reordered with the same bound on unsigned partial sums. A matching lower bound gives the optimal order $S_2(d)=\\Theta(\\sqrt d)$.","paperIds":["097-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/097.md","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","overviewTitle":"The Euclidean Steinitz--Bergstr\\\"om conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves that any finite sequence in the Euclidean unit ball of $\\mathbb R^d$ admits signs keeping every partial sum within $C\\sqrt d$, independently of length. Consequently every zero-sum family can be reordered with the same bound on unsigned partial sums. A matching lower bound gives the optimal order $S_2(d)=\\Theta(\\sqrt d)$."},{"id":"098","title":"Compact counterexamples to bi-Lipschitz dimension reduction","summary":"Every infinite-dimensional real Banach space contains a compact doubling set that admits no bi-Lipschitz embedding into any finite-dimensional normed space. The doubling constant is universal. This answers the Lang–Plaut problem negatively, even for compact subsets of Hilbert space.","paperIds":["098-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/098.md","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","overviewTitle":"A negative answer to the Lang--Plaut problem","overviewSummary":"Every infinite-dimensional real Banach space contains a compact doubling set that admits no bi-Lipschitz embedding into any finite-dimensional normed space. The doubling constant is universal. This answers the Lang--Plaut problem negatively, even for compact subsets of Hilbert space."},{"id":"099","title":"The sharp exponential scale of edit-distance distortion","summary":"Determines the least distortion of embedding edit distance on words of length at most d into real ℓ1: it is $\\exp(\\Theta(\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d}))$. Insertions, deletions and substitutions have unit cost. The constants are uniform over all finite alphabets with at least two symbols, even when the alphabet grows with d; binary words already force the lower bound.","paperIds":["099-01","099-02","099-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/099.md","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","overviewTitle":"The sharp distortion of edit distance into $\\ell_1$","overviewSummary":"Determines the least distortion of embedding edit distance on words of length at most $d$ into real $\\ell_1$: it is $\\exp(\\Theta(\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d}))$. Insertions, deletions and substitutions have unit cost. The constants are uniform over all finite alphabets with at least two symbols, even when the alphabet grows with $d$; binary words already force the lower bound."},{"id":"100","title":"Cylinder coverings below the half-area bound","summary":"Covers the entire closed regular tetrahedron by finitely many cylinders with compact triangular perpendicular bases whose total area is less than half its smallest orthogonal projection area. This disproves Bang's half-area cylinder-covering bound and the stronger directionwise normalized conjecture in dimension three.","paperIds":["100-01","100-02","100-03","100-04"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/100.md","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","overviewTitle":"A counterexample to Bang's cylinder-covering bound","overviewSummary":"Covers the entire closed regular tetrahedron by finitely many cylinders with compact triangular perpendicular bases whose total area is less than half its smallest orthogonal projection area. This disproves Bang's half-area cylinder-covering bound and the stronger directionwise normalized conjecture in dimension three."},{"id":"101","title":"The sharp simplex conjecture for isotropic constants","summary":"Proves that simplices uniquely maximize the isotropic constant among convex bodies in every dimension, resolving the strong isotropic constant conjecture. Also establishes the sharp entropy lower bound for log-concave probability densities, with equality precisely for invertible affine images of products of one-sided exponential laws.","paperIds":["101-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","overviewTitle":"The sharp simplex conjecture for isotropic constants","overviewSummary":"Proves that simplices uniquely maximize the isotropic constant among convex bodies in every dimension, resolving the strong isotropic constant conjecture. Also establishes the sharp entropy lower bound for log-concave probability densities, with equality precisely for invertible affine images of products of one-sided exponential laws."},{"id":"102","title":"The Unique Games Conjecture and optimal approximation thresholds","summary":"Proves Khot's Unique Games Conjecture. Independent direct reductions also establish NP-hardness, on unweighted graphs, of approximation beyond the Goemans–Williamson ratio for Max-Cut, below factor two for Vertex Cover, and within any fixed constant factor for Min-UnCut and directed feedback vertex set. These direct proofs use established PCP and Label Cover hardness results.","paperIds":["102-01","102-02","102-03","102-04","102-05"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/102.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"The Unique Games Conjecture and optimal approximation thresholds","overviewSummary":"Proves Khot's Unique Games Conjecture. Independent direct reductions also establish NP-hardness, on unweighted graphs, of approximation beyond the Goemans--Williamson ratio for Max-Cut, below factor two for Vertex Cover, and within any fixed constant factor for Min-UnCut and directed feedback vertex set. These direct proofs use established PCP and Label Cover hardness results."},{"id":"103","title":"Exact derandomization of logarithmic space: $\\mathsf L=\\mathsf{RL}=\\mathsf{BPL}$","summary":"Proves $\\mathsf L=\\mathsf{RL}=\\mathsf{BPL}$, resolving derandomization for bounded-error logarithmic-space computation. An effective compiler converts each randomized polynomial-time logarithmic-space machine deciding a language with one-sided or two-sided error into a deterministic logarithmic-space decider with explicit polynomial running-time bounds.","paperIds":["103-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/103.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Derandomization of logarithmic space: $\\mathsf L=\\mathsf{RL}=\\mathsf{BPL}$","overviewSummary":"Proves $\\mathsf L=\\mathsf{RL}=\\mathsf{BPL}$, resolving derandomization for bounded-error logarithmic-space computation. An effective compiler converts each randomized polynomial-time logarithmic-space machine deciding a language with one-sided or two-sided error into a deterministic logarithmic-space decider with explicit polynomial running-time bounds."},{"id":"104","title":"Quasipolynomial algorithms for mean-payoff, stochastic and parity games","summary":"Gives deterministic algorithms using $2^{O((\\log(L+2))^2)}$ bit operations, for complete binary input length L, for ordinary mean-payoff games and two separate extensions. They compute exact values and optimal positional strategies in ordinary games, the nonnegative expectation-of-liminf value set in turn-based stochastic games, and the winning set for nonnegative liminf mean payoff conjoined with parity. Signed rewards, rational chance probabilities, and parity priorities are unrestricted and binary-encoded.","paperIds":["104-01","104-02","104-03","104-04"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/104.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Quasipolynomial algorithms for mean-payoff, stochastic and parity games","overviewSummary":"Gives deterministic algorithms using $2^{O((\\log(L+2))^2)}$ bit operations, for complete binary input length $L$, for ordinary mean-payoff games and two separate extensions. They compute exact values and optimal positional strategies in ordinary games, the nonnegative expectation-of-liminf value set in turn-based stochastic games, and the winning set for nonnegative liminf mean payoff conjoined with parity. Signed rewards, rational chance probabilities, and parity priorities are unrestricted and binary-encoded."},{"id":"105","title":"Perfect completeness for 2-to-1 games","summary":"Proves Khot's 2-to-1 Games Conjecture with perfect completeness: for every fixed rational $\\delta\\in(0,1)$, it is NP-hard to distinguish satisfiable games from games whose optimum is at most δ, on explicit unweighted instances. The alphabet depends only on δ, and every right-hand label has exactly two preimages under each constraint map.","paperIds":["105-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/105.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"The 2-to-1 Games Conjecture with perfect completeness","overviewSummary":"Proves Khot's 2-to-1 Games Conjecture with perfect completeness: for every fixed rational $\\delta\\in(0,1)$, it is NP-hard to distinguish satisfiable games from games whose optimum is at most $\\delta$, on explicit unweighted instances. The alphabet depends only on $\\delta$, and every right-hand label has exactly two preimages under each constraint map."},{"id":"106","title":"Hardness of coloring three-colorable graphs","summary":"It is NP-hard to color a three-colorable graph using any fixed number c ≥ 3 of colors. More strongly, for every fixed $0\\lt \\delta\\lt 1/3$, a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from 3SAT produces simple unweighted graphs that are three-colorable in the satisfiable case and have no independent set of size $\\delta n$ otherwise, where n is the number of vertices.","paperIds":["106-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/106.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Hardness of coloring three-colorable graphs","overviewSummary":"It is NP-hard to color a three-colorable graph using any fixed number $c\\ge3$ of colors. More strongly, for every fixed $0<\\delta<1/3$, a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from $3$SAT produces simple unweighted graphs that are three-colorable in the satisfiable case and have no independent set of size $\\delta n$ otherwise, where $n$ is the number of vertices."},{"id":"107","title":"Matrix multiplication with exponent at most 9/4","summary":"Proves $\\omega\\le9/4$ over ℂ, giving $O_\\varepsilon(n^{9/4+\\varepsilon})$ arithmetic operations for square matrix multiplication. In characteristic zero, some inner dimension na with a > 0.465 permits $n^{2+o(1)}$ rectangular multiplication. Further square bounds give ω < 2.258 outside finitely many positive characteristics and ω < 2.371054886006746 over every fixed field.","paperIds":["107-01","107-02","107-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/107.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Matrix multiplication with exponent at most $9/4$","overviewSummary":"Proves $\\omega\\le9/4$ over $\\mathbb C$, giving $O_\\varepsilon(n^{9/4+\\varepsilon})$ arithmetic operations for square matrix multiplication. In characteristic zero, some inner dimension $n^a$ with $a>0.465$ permits $n^{2+o(1)}$ rectangular multiplication. Further square bounds give $\\omega<2.258$ outside finitely many positive characteristics and $\\omega<2.371054886006746$ over every fixed field."},{"id":"108","title":"A cubic permanent–determinant lower bound","summary":"Proves an $\\Omega(n^3)$ lower bound for the border determinantal complexity of the $n\\times n$ permanent over ℂ. Even coefficientwise limits of determinants of affine-linear matrices require matrix size at least $cn^3$, for an absolute c > 0 and all sufficiently large n; the same bound therefore holds for exact representations.","paperIds":["108-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/108.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"A cubic permanent--determinant lower bound","overviewSummary":"Proves an $\\Omega(n^3)$ lower bound for the border determinantal complexity of the $n\\times n$ permanent over $\\mathbb C$. Even coefficientwise limits of determinants of affine-linear matrices require matrix size at least $cn^3$, for an absolute $c>0$ and all sufficiently large $n$; the same bound therefore holds for exact representations."},{"id":"109","title":"Integer multiplication below $n\\log n$","summary":"Multiplies two n-bit integers exactly at every input length in deterministic worst-case time $O(n(\\log n)^{1-\\kappa})$, with $\\kappa=2^{-182}$, on one fixed finite-alphabet Turing machine with finitely many one-dimensional tapes. This disproves the Schönhage–Strassen $n\\log n$ optimality conjecture in the ordinary multitape bit model.","paperIds":["109-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Integer multiplication below $n\\log n$","overviewSummary":"Multiplies two $n$-bit integers exactly at every input length in deterministic worst-case time $O(n(\\log n)^{1-\\kappa})$, with $\\kappa=2^{-182}$, on one fixed finite-alphabet Turing machine with finitely many one-dimensional tapes. This disproves the Sch\\\"onhage--Strassen $n\\log n$ optimality conjecture in the ordinary multitape bit model."},{"id":"110","title":"Optimal-order randomized k-server on arbitrary metrics","summary":"Establishes a randomized competitive ratio $O(\\log^2(k+1))$ for k-server on every metric space, matching the worst-case lower-bound order. One policy serves every finite oblivious request sequence, including on infinite unbounded metrics. On finite rational metrics, a uniform implementation has polynomial preprocessing and per-request bit cost in the input length and $\\log(t+1)$ at request t, with a finite instance-dependent additive movement constant.","paperIds":["110-01","110-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/110.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Optimal-order randomized $k$-server on arbitrary metrics","overviewSummary":"Establishes a randomized competitive ratio $O(\\log^2(k+1))$ for $k$-server on every metric space, matching the worst-case lower-bound order. One policy serves every finite oblivious request sequence, including on infinite unbounded metrics. On finite rational metrics, a uniform implementation has polynomial preprocessing and per-request bit cost in the input length and $\\log(t+1)$ at request $t$, with a finite instance-dependent additive movement constant."},{"id":"111","title":"One-sample matroid prophet inequalities against an almighty adversary","summary":"For every finite matroid known in advance, gives a distribution-independent online rule using one independent sample per element and earning a universal constant fraction of the expected offline optimum. Values are independent and nonnegative, with finite expected optimum. The guarantee holds even when the arrival-order adversary sees all samples, values, and the rule's entire random seed; no polynomial-time implementation is asserted.","paperIds":["111-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/111.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"One-sample matroid prophet inequalities against an almighty adversary","overviewSummary":"For every finite matroid known in advance, gives a distribution-independent online rule using one independent sample per element and earning a universal constant fraction of the expected offline optimum. Values are independent and nonnegative, with finite expected optimum. The guarantee holds even when the arrival-order adversary sees all samples, values, and the rule's entire random seed; no polynomial-time implementation is asserted."},{"id":"112","title":"Beyond the square-root exponent for depth-three circuits","summary":"Constructs a single language in deterministic polynomial time whose n-bit membership function requires $2^{\\omega(\\sqrt n)}$ total gates in unbounded-fan-in OR–AND–OR circuits, at every sufficiently large input length. This crosses the square-root-exponent threshold for explicit depth-three Boolean circuit lower bounds.","paperIds":["112-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/112.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Beyond the square-root exponent for depth-three circuits","overviewSummary":"Constructs a single language in deterministic polynomial time whose $n$-bit membership function requires $2^{\\omega(\\sqrt n)}$ total gates in unbounded-fan-in OR--AND--OR circuits, at every sufficiently large input length. This crosses the square-root-exponent threshold for explicit depth-three Boolean circuit lower bounds."},{"id":"113","title":"Approximate counting and entropy of perfect matchings","summary":"Gives a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting perfect matchings in arbitrary finite simple graphs, with exact detection of zero counts. Also proves the perfect-matching entropy conjecture of Anari, Oveis Gharan, and Vinzant, bounding the maximum entropy of a matching law at every feasible edge-marginal vector in a loopless labelled multigraph, including boundary points.","paperIds":["113-01","113-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/113.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Approximate counting and the perfect-matching entropy conjecture","overviewSummary":"Gives a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting perfect matchings in arbitrary finite simple graphs, with exact detection of zero counts. Also proves the perfect-matching entropy conjecture of Anari, Oveis Gharan, and Vinzant, bounding the maximum entropy of a matching law at every feasible edge-marginal vector in a loopless labelled multigraph, including boundary points."},{"id":"114","title":"Approximate counting of common integer polymatroid bases","summary":"Gives a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting common integer bases of two integral polymatroids of equal total rank, supplied by exact rank-value oracles. Capacities are binary-encoded, each integer vector counts once, and oracle calls and bit operations outside the oracles are polynomial on every execution. For matroids presented by independence oracles, the results also cover common independent sets of prescribed, unrestricted, or maximum cardinality, even when the ranks differ.","paperIds":["114-01","114-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/114.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Approximate counting of common integer polymatroid bases","overviewSummary":"Gives a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting common integer bases of two integral polymatroids of equal total rank, supplied by exact rank-value oracles. Capacities are binary-encoded, each integer vector counts once, and oracle calls and bit operations outside the oracles are polynomial on every execution. For matroids presented by independence oracles, the results also cover common independent sets of prescribed, unrestricted, or maximum cardinality, even when the ranks differ."},{"id":"115","title":"Sampling and counting contingency tables with arbitrary margins","summary":"For nonnegative integer matrices with prescribed row and column sums, gives exact uniform sampling in expected polynomial bit time and almost-uniform sampling in worst-case polynomial bit time. The dimensions and binary-encoded margins are unrestricted. Also gives a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting such tables with arbitrary individual cell bounds, including structural zeros, with polynomial cost on every execution.","paperIds":["115-01","115-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/115.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Sampling and counting contingency tables with arbitrary margins","overviewSummary":"For nonnegative integer matrices with prescribed row and column sums, gives exact uniform sampling in expected polynomial bit time and almost-uniform sampling in worst-case polynomial bit time. The dimensions and binary-encoded margins are unrestricted. Also gives a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting such tables with arbitrary individual cell bounds, including structural zeros, with polynomial cost on every execution."},{"id":"116","title":"Uniform black-box noncommutative identity testing across characteristics","summary":"For each characteristic, constructs in deterministic polynomial bit time a polynomial-dimensional matrix tuple detecting every nonzero division-free noncommutative formula of bounded size over any field of that characteristic. Rational formulas over ℚ also admit polynomial-size hitting lists whenever they have a defined rational-matrix evaluation.","paperIds":["116-01","116-02","116-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/116.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Uniform identity testing for noncommutative formulas","overviewSummary":"For each characteristic, constructs in deterministic polynomial bit time a polynomial-dimensional matrix tuple detecting every nonzero division-free noncommutative formula of bounded size over any field of that characteristic. Rational formulas over $\\mathbb Q$ also admit polynomial-size hitting lists whenever they have a defined rational-matrix evaluation."},{"id":"117","title":"Uniform sparsest cut: hardness and semidefinite gaps","summary":"Proves that approximating Uniform Sparsest Cut within any fixed constant factor is NP-hard, even with nonnegative rational capacities and unit demands. The Goemans–Linial semidefinite relaxation also has integrality gaps of order at least $\\sqrt{\\log n}/(\\log\\log n)^3$, approaching the square-root-logarithmic upper bound.","paperIds":["117-01","117-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/117.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Uniform sparsest cut: hardness and semidefinite gaps","overviewSummary":"Proves that approximating Uniform Sparsest Cut within any fixed constant factor is NP-hard, even with nonnegative rational capacities and unit demands. The Goemans--Linial semidefinite relaxation also has integrality gaps of order at least $\\sqrt{\\log n}/(\\log\\log n)^3$, approaching the square-root-logarithmic upper bound."},{"id":"118","title":"Bin packing and unbounded configuration-LP gaps","summary":"Disproves the modified integer round-up conjecture of Scheithauer and Terno: the integral bin-packing optimum can exceed its configuration linear-programming value by an arbitrarily large additive constant. Approximating the optimum within any fixed additive constant is also NP-hard, even when every item exceeds 1/6 and each bin holds at most five items.","paperIds":["118-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/118.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Unbounded bin-packing gaps and the modified integer round-up conjecture","overviewSummary":"Disproves the modified integer round-up conjecture of Scheithauer and Terno: the integral bin-packing optimum can exceed its configuration linear-programming value by an arbitrarily large additive constant. Approximating the optimum within any fixed additive constant is also NP-hard, even when every item exceeds $1/6$ and each bin holds at most five items."},{"id":"119","title":"The Courtade–Kumar and Hellinger conjectures","summary":"Proves the Courtade–Kumar conjecture: among Boolean functions of independent uniform bits, a single coordinate retains the most mutual information after independent bit-flip noise. A stronger theorem treats randomized binary summaries at fixed initial information. The Hellinger conjecture is also proved for every Boolean output bias and noise correlation.","paperIds":["119-01","119-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/119.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"The Courtade--Kumar and Hellinger conjectures","overviewSummary":"Proves the Courtade--Kumar conjecture: among Boolean functions of independent uniform bits, a single coordinate retains the most mutual information after independent bit-flip noise. A stronger theorem treats randomized binary summaries at fixed initial information. The Hellinger conjecture is also proved for every Boolean output bias and noise correlation."},{"id":"120","title":"Almost-linear-time exact matching and prescribed-degree factors in general graphs","summary":"Gives a randomized algorithm finding an exact maximum-cardinality matching in any simple undirected graph in $(n+m)^{1+o(1)}$ word time, with success probability at least 2/3. The time bound holds on every computation path. The same guarantees apply to finding a spanning subgraph with prescribed admissible vertex degrees, or deciding that none exists.","paperIds":["120-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Almost-linear-time exact matching in general graphs","overviewSummary":"Gives a randomized algorithm finding an exact maximum-cardinality matching in any simple undirected graph in $(n+m)^{1+o(1)}$ word time, with success probability at least $2/3$. The time bound holds on every computation path. The same guarantees apply to finding a spanning subgraph with prescribed admissible vertex degrees, or deciding that none exists."},{"id":"121","title":"Almost-linear approximation of edit distance","summary":"For every fixed rational $\\varepsilon\\in(0,1)$, gives a randomized $(1+\\varepsilon)$ approximation to unit-cost edit distance in worst-case expected time $N^{1+o(1)}$, with success probability at least 2/3. The strings have total length N and polynomially bounded integer symbols. This is an asymptotic guarantee at fixed accuracy.","paperIds":["121-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/121.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Almost-linear expected-time approximation of edit distance","overviewSummary":"For every fixed rational $\\varepsilon\\in(0,1)$, gives a randomized $(1+\\varepsilon)$ approximation to unit-cost edit distance in worst-case expected time $N^{1+o(1)}$, with success probability at least $2/3$. The strings have total length $N$ and polynomially bounded integer symbols. This is an asymptotic guarantee at fixed accuracy."},{"id":"122","title":"Quantitative trace-reconstruction bounds with a uniform decoder","summary":"At every fixed deletion probability in $(0,1)$, reconstructing an arbitrary length-n binary string requires $n^{\\Omega(\\log\\log n)}$ independent traces, ruling out polynomial-sample reconstruction. A uniform decoder achieves quasipolynomial sample and running-time bounds for known fixed rational retention probabilities. When the deletion probability is at most $n^{-\\varepsilon}$ for fixed ε > 0, both bounds become polynomial in the input and parameter encoding.","paperIds":["122-01","122-02","122-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/122.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Superpolynomial lower bounds and quasipolynomial reconstruction from deletion traces","overviewSummary":"At every fixed deletion probability in $(0,1)$, reconstructing an arbitrary length-$n$ binary string requires $n^{\\Omega(\\log\\log n)}$ independent traces, ruling out polynomial-sample reconstruction. A uniform decoder achieves quasipolynomial sample and running-time bounds for known fixed rational retention probabilities. When the deletion probability is at most $n^{-\\varepsilon}$ for fixed $\\varepsilon>0$, both bounds become polynomial in the input and parameter encoding."},{"id":"124","title":"Polynomial-time scheduling on three identical machines","summary":"Resolves the three-processor unit-job scheduling problem of Garey and Johnson: a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm minimizes makespan for nonpreemptive unit-length jobs with arbitrary precedence constraints on three identical parallel machines. For an explicitly given precedence graph, it decides deadline feasibility exactly and constructs a feasible schedule.","paperIds":["124-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/124.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Polynomial-time scheduling on three identical machines","overviewSummary":"Resolves the three-processor unit-job scheduling problem of Garey and Johnson: a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm minimizes makespan for nonpreemptive unit-length jobs with arbitrary precedence constraints on three identical parallel machines. For an explicitly given precedence graph, it decides deadline feasibility exactly and constructs a feasible schedule."},{"id":"125","title":"The metric k-median approximation threshold and recovery","summary":"Gives a deterministic polynomial-time $(1+2/e+\\varepsilon)$-approximation for finite rational metric k-median with specified candidate facilities, for every fixed ε > 0. Assuming $P\\ne NP$, the optimal infimum approximation factor is $1+2/e$.","paperIds":["125-01","125-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/125.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"The approximation threshold for metric $k$-median","overviewSummary":"Gives a deterministic polynomial-time $(1+2/e+\\varepsilon)$-approximation for finite rational metric $k$-median with specified candidate facilities, for every fixed $\\varepsilon>0$. Assuming $P\\ne NP$, the optimal infimum approximation factor is $1+2/e$."},{"id":"126","title":"Exponential semidefinite complexity of perfect matching","summary":"Proves that every exact semidefinite lift of the perfect matching polytope has exponential size, answering Rothvoss's polynomial-size lift question negatively. The bound holds even for the positive semidefinite rank of its odd-cut slack matrix after any fixed shift $0\\lt \\rho\\lt 1$, allowing arbitrary real positive semidefinite factors.","paperIds":["126-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/126.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Exponential semidefinite complexity of perfect matching","overviewSummary":"Proves that every exact semidefinite lift of the perfect matching polytope has exponential size, answering Rothvoss's polynomial-size lift question negatively. The bound holds even for the positive semidefinite rank of its odd-cut slack matrix after any fixed shift $0<\\rho<1$, allowing arbitrary real positive semidefinite factors."},{"id":"127","title":"Average sensitivity of polynomial threshold functions","summary":"Proves that a degree-at-most-d polynomial threshold function on the uniform n-dimensional Boolean cube has average sensitivity at most $8d\\sqrt n$, uniformly for $1\\le d\\le n$. Average sensitivity counts expected output changes under single-bit flips. This establishes the asymptotic Gotsman–Linial conjecture, allowing polynomial zeros with $\\mathop{\\mathrm{sign}}\\nolimits (0)=1$.","paperIds":["127-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/127.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"The asymptotic Gotsman--Linial conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves that a degree-at-most-$d$ polynomial threshold function on the uniform $n$-dimensional Boolean cube has average sensitivity at most $8d\\sqrt n$, uniformly for $1\\le d\\le n$. Average sensitivity counts expected output changes under single-bit flips. This establishes the asymptotic Gotsman--Linial conjecture, allowing polynomial zeros with $\\operatorname{sign}(0)=1$."},{"id":"128","title":"A factor-two approximation for shortest common superstring","summary":"Gives a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm constructing a common superstring of length at most twice the optimum for every finite family of explicitly represented strings. The running time is polynomial in the full encoded input length, including symbol labels.","paperIds":["128-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/128.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"A factor-two approximation for shortest common superstring","overviewSummary":"Gives a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm constructing a common superstring of length at most twice the optimum for every finite family of explicitly represented strings. The running time is polynomial in the full encoded input length, including symbol labels."},{"id":"129","title":"Exponential state costs for two-way automata","summary":"Proves exponential lower bounds both for complementing two-way nondeterministic finite automata and for simulating one-way nondeterministic automata by two-way deterministic ones. The latter resolves the Sakoda–Sipser state-succinctness conjecture over growing finite alphabets; both results rule out polynomial state bounds independent of alphabet size.","paperIds":["129-01","129-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/129.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Exponential state costs for two-way automata","overviewSummary":"Proves exponential lower bounds both for complementing two-way nondeterministic finite automata and for simulating one-way nondeterministic automata by two-way deterministic ones. The latter resolves the Sakoda--Sipser state-succinctness conjecture over growing finite alphabets; both results rule out polynomial state bounds independent of alphabet size."},{"id":"130","title":"Exact Fourier transforms below $n\\log n$","summary":"Gives a deterministic length-n discrete Fourier transform algorithm using $O(n(\\log n)^{1-\\delta})$ operations for every n, with explicit $\\delta=10^{-13}$. The model uses exact complex arithmetic, unrestricted coefficients and a supplied root of unity, and counts scalar preparation and logarithmic-word indexing.","paperIds":["130-01","130-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/130.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Fourier transforms below $n\\log n$","overviewSummary":"Gives a deterministic length-$n$ discrete Fourier transform algorithm using $O(n(\\log n)^{1-\\delta})$ operations for every $n$, with explicit $\\delta=10^{-13}$. The model uses exact complex arithmetic, unrestricted coefficients and a supplied root of unity, and counts scalar preparation and logarithmic-word indexing."},{"id":"131","title":"Rapid mixing of graph switches for every degree sequence","summary":"Resolves the simple-undirected Kannan–Tetali–Vempala conjecture: the lazy edge-switch chain mixes in $O(n^8)$ time for every graphical labeled degree sequence. The same degree-constrained graphs can also be sampled exactly uniformly by an almost-surely terminating algorithm with expected polynomial bit running time.","paperIds":["131-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/131.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Polynomial mixing of graph switches with prescribed degrees","overviewSummary":"Resolves the simple-undirected Kannan--Tetali--Vempala conjecture: the lazy edge-switch chain mixes in $O(n^8)$ time for every graphical labeled degree sequence. The same degree-constrained graphs can also be sampled exactly uniformly by an almost-surely terminating algorithm with expected polynomial bit running time."},{"id":"132","title":"A superquadratic separation of sensitivity and block sensitivity","summary":"Constructs total Boolean functions with block sensitivity $\\mathop{\\mathrm{bs}}\\nolimits (f)\\ge s(f)^\\alpha$ for a fixed α > 2, disproving the quadratic strengthening of the Sensitivity Conjecture. Here $s(f)$ counts influential individual-bit flips, while block sensitivity allows disjoint groups of bits to change together.","paperIds":["132-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/132.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"A counterexample to the quadratic sensitivity conjecture","overviewSummary":"Constructs total Boolean functions with block sensitivity $\\operatorname{bs}(f)\\ge s(f)^\\alpha$ for a fixed $\\alpha>2$, disproving the quadratic strengthening of the Sensitivity Conjecture. Here $s(f)$ counts influential individual-bit flips, while block sensitivity allows disjoint groups of bits to change together."},{"id":"133","title":"The computational complexity of Weisfeiler–Leman refinement","summary":"Proves unconditional $n^{\\Omega(k)}$ deterministic time lower bounds for joint and separate k-dimensional Weisfeiler–Leman equivalence, for sufficiently large fixed k in the specified sequential adjacency-matrix models. With dimension as input, joint equivalence is EXPTIME-complete even on subcubic graphs; deciding whether refinement identifies a graph is also EXPTIME-complete.","paperIds":["133-01","133-02","133-03","133-04"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/133.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"The complexity of Weisfeiler--Leman refinement","overviewSummary":"Proves unconditional $n^{\\Omega(k)}$ deterministic time lower bounds for joint and separate $k$-dimensional Weisfeiler--Leman equivalence, for sufficiently large fixed $k$ in the specified sequential adjacency-matrix models. With dimension as input, joint equivalence is EXPTIME-complete even on subcubic graphs; deciding whether refinement identifies a graph is also EXPTIME-complete."},{"id":"134","title":"Generalized star height at most three","summary":"Every regular language over a finite alphabet has a generalized regular expression with at most three nested Kleene stars, allowing union, concatenation and complement over the same alphabet. This establishes an absolute bound independent of automaton size, resolving the uniform-boundedness version of the generalized star-height problem.","paperIds":["134-01","134-02","134-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/134.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Generalized star height at most three","overviewSummary":"Every regular language over a finite alphabet has a generalized regular expression with at most three nested Kleene stars, allowing union, concatenation and complement over the same alphabet. This establishes an absolute bound independent of automaton size, resolving the uniform-boundedness version of the generalized star-height problem."},{"id":"135","title":"Homogeneous depth-five lower bounds for iterated matrix multiplication","summary":"Over every characteristic-zero field, the $(1,1)$ entry of a product of n independent $n\\times n$ variable matrices requires $n^{\\Theta(\\sqrt n)}$ gates in homogeneous depth-five sum–product circuits. This sharp bound allows shared gates and bottom linear forms involving all variables.","paperIds":["135-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/135.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Sharp homogeneous depth-five complexity of matrix products","overviewSummary":"Over every characteristic-zero field, the $(1,1)$ entry of a product of $n$ independent $n\\times n$ variable matrices requires $n^{\\Theta(\\sqrt n)}$ gates in homogeneous depth-five sum--product circuits. This sharp bound allows shared gates and bottom linear forms involving all variables."},{"id":"136","title":"A quasilinear PCP theorem for PPAD","summary":"Resolves the quasilinear PCP-for-PPAD conjecture. An End-of-Line instance of length N reduces to numerical circuit constraints of total length $N(\\log N)^{O(1)}$ such that any polynomially encoded rational assignment satisfying all but a fixed fraction to fixed accuracy yields an endpoint solution. Such assignments always exist, giving robust local verification with only quasilinear size overhead.","paperIds":["136-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"The quasilinear PCP-for-PPAD conjecture","overviewSummary":"Resolves the quasilinear PCP-for-PPAD conjecture. An End-of-Line instance of length $N$ reduces to numerical circuit constraints of total length $N(\\log N)^{O(1)}$ such that any polynomially encoded rational assignment satisfying all but a fixed fraction to fixed accuracy yields an endpoint solution. Such assignments always exist, giving robust local verification with only quasilinear size overhead."},{"id":"137","title":"One-tape time simulation in two-fifths-power space","summary":"Determines the halting and finite-control outcome of a fixed deterministic one-writable-tape machine up to time T using $O(T^{2/5}\\log^C(T+2))$ space, improving the square-root exponent. Heads move at most one cell per step; finitely many read-only input heads are allowed. Initial contents are independent of T, and contents and input symbols have polylogarithmic-space access. Simulation time is unrestricted.","paperIds":["137-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/137.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"One-tape time simulation in two-fifths-power space","overviewSummary":"Determines the halting and finite-control outcome of a fixed deterministic one-writable-tape machine up to time $T$ using $O(T^{2/5}\\log^C(T+2))$ space, improving the square-root exponent. Heads move at most one cell per step; finitely many read-only input heads are allowed. Initial contents are independent of $T$, and contents and input symbols have polylogarithmic-space access. Simulation time is unrestricted."},{"id":"138","title":"Subset Sum in $O(2^{0.49n})$ time","summary":"Gives a uniform randomized classical algorithm for worst-case Subset Sum in ordinary $O(2^{0.49n})$ word-RAM time on polynomial-bit inputs, where n counts the integers. The time bound holds on every execution and success probability is at least 2/3 on every input. Inputs may repeat positive integers; words have $O(n+b)$ bits for maximum input bit length b.","paperIds":["138-01","138-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Subset Sum in $O(2^{0.49n})$ time","overviewSummary":"Gives a uniform randomized classical algorithm for worst-case Subset Sum in ordinary $O(2^{0.49n})$ word-RAM time on polynomial-bit inputs, where $n$ counts the integers. The time bound holds on every execution and success probability is at least $2/3$ on every input. Inputs may repeat positive integers; words have $O(n+b)$ bits for maximum input bit length $b$."},{"id":"139","title":"Subpolynomial query complexity for log-concave sampling","summary":"For C2 potentials with a supplied minimizer and $I\\preceq\\nabla^2V\\preceq2I$, proves that sampling within total variation 1/10 requires only $C_\\varepsilon d^\\varepsilon$ exact value-and-gradient queries for every fixed ε > 0. The bound holds on every run, with unrestricted computation between queries. A logarithmic lower bound also holds, so the optimal power-law exponent in this oracle model is zero.","paperIds":["139-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/139.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Subpolynomial queries for log-concave sampling","overviewSummary":"For $C^2$ potentials with a supplied minimizer and $I\\preceq\\nabla^2V\\preceq2I$, proves that sampling within total variation $1/10$ requires only $C_\\varepsilon d^\\varepsilon$ exact value-and-gradient queries for every fixed $\\varepsilon>0$. The bound holds on every run, with unrestricted computation between queries. A logarithmic lower bound also holds, so the optimal power-law exponent in this oracle model is zero."},{"id":"140","title":"Memory–sample lower bounds for noiseless Gaussian regression","summary":"For fixed A > 0, a one-pass learner with $Ad^2$ persistent bits needs $\\Omega_A(d\\log(1/\\epsilon))$ noiseless Gaussian samples to recover a unit vector to angular error $0\\lt \\epsilon\\le1/10$ with probability 2/3, uniformly in accuracy for large d. Computation and randomized updates are unrestricted, but output uses only the terminal state, stopping index and fresh randomness.","paperIds":["140-01","140-02","140-03","140-04","140-05","140-06"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/140.md","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Memory--sample lower bounds for noiseless Gaussian regression","overviewSummary":"For fixed $A>0$, a one-pass learner with $Ad^2$ persistent bits needs $\\Omega_A(d\\log(1/\\epsilon))$ noiseless Gaussian samples to recover a unit vector to angular error $0<\\epsilon\\le1/10$ with probability $2/3$, uniformly in accuracy for large $d$. Computation and randomized updates are unrestricted, but output uses only the terminal state, stopping index and fresh randomness."},{"id":"141","title":"Existential–universal real sentences in the counting hierarchy","summary":"Proves that the existential theory of the reals lies in the counting hierarchy. More generally, truth of existential–universal real sentences can be decided at one fixed level of that hierarchy, even when their integer polynomials are specified by arithmetic circuits.","paperIds":["141-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"The existential theory of the reals and existential--universal sentences in the counting hierarchy","overviewSummary":"Proves that the existential theory of the reals lies in the counting hierarchy. More generally, truth of existential--universal real sentences can be decided at one fixed level of that hierarchy, even when their integer polynomials are specified by arithmetic circuits."},{"id":"142","title":"Deterministic polynomial factorization over prime fields","summary":"Gives a uniform deterministic algorithm that completely factors every nonzero dense degree-n polynomial over a prime field 𝔽p, including multiplicities, in bit complexity polynomial in $(n+1)\\log p$. The prime is supplied in binary. No randomness, integer-factorization or primitive-root oracle, or GRH assumption is required.","paperIds":["142-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","overviewTitle":"Deterministic polynomial factorization over prime fields","overviewSummary":"Gives a uniform deterministic algorithm that completely factors every nonzero dense degree-$n$ polynomial over a prime field $\\mathbb F_p$, including multiplicities, in bit complexity polynomial in $(n+1)\\log p$. The prime is supplied in binary. No randomness, integer-factorization or primitive-root oracle, or GRH assumption is required."},{"id":"143","title":"Hilbert's sixteenth problem: uniform bounds for limit cycles","summary":"Resolves the uniform boundedness assertion in Hilbert's sixteenth problem: the number of isolated periodic orbits of a real planar polynomial vector field is bounded by a finite constant depending only on its degree. For classical quintic Liénard systems, the exact maximum is two limit cycles.","paperIds":["143-01","143-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/143.md","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","overviewTitle":"Uniform limit-cycle bounds in Hilbert's sixteenth problem","overviewSummary":"Resolves the uniform boundedness assertion in Hilbert's sixteenth problem: the number of isolated periodic orbits of a real planar polynomial vector field is bounded by a finite constant depending only on its degree. For classical quintic Li\\'enard systems, the exact maximum is two limit cycles."},{"id":"144","title":"Banach’s simple Lebesgue-spectrum problem","summary":"Resolves the probability-preserving form of Banach's simple Lebesgue-spectrum problem within smooth dynamics. A smooth volume-preserving diffeomorphism of the standard-volume three-torus has simple Lebesgue spectrum on its entire complex mean-zero L2 space: the bilateral iterates of one real observable form an orthonormal basis of that space.","paperIds":["144-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/144.md","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","overviewTitle":"Banach's simple Lebesgue-spectrum problem","overviewSummary":"Resolves the probability-preserving form of Banach's simple Lebesgue-spectrum problem within smooth dynamics. A smooth volume-preserving diffeomorphism of the standard-volume three-torus has simple Lebesgue spectrum on its entire complex mean-zero $L^2$ space: the bilateral iterates of one real observable form an orthonormal basis of that space."},{"id":"145","title":"Rokhlin’s multiple-mixing problem","summary":"Proves that every invertible mixing probability-preserving transformation is mixing of all finite orders, resolving Rokhlin's multiple-mixing problem for a single transformation. Correlations among any finite collection of measurable sets converge to the product of their measures whenever all pairwise time separations diverge.","paperIds":["145-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/145.md","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","overviewTitle":"Rokhlin's multiple-mixing problem","overviewSummary":"Proves that every invertible mixing probability-preserving transformation is mixing of all finite orders, resolving Rokhlin's multiple-mixing problem for a single transformation. Correlations among any finite collection of measurable sets converge to the product of their measures whenever all pairwise time separations diverge."},{"id":"146","title":"Positive metric entropy for the standard map","summary":"Proves that the standard sine map on the two-dimensional torus has positive metric entropy with respect to area for every sufficiently large positive parameter. This establishes Sinai's positive-parameter-measure conjecture for the original family, with the stronger conclusion of a full parameter tail.","paperIds":["146-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/146.md","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","overviewTitle":"Sinai's positive-entropy conjecture for the standard map","overviewSummary":"Proves that the standard sine map on the two-dimensional torus has positive metric entropy with respect to area for every sufficiently large positive parameter. This establishes Sinai's positive-parameter-measure conjecture for the original family, with the stronger conclusion of a full parameter tail."},{"id":"147","title":"The near-boundary Birkhoff conjecture","summary":"Resolves the near-boundary Birkhoff conjecture for smooth strictly convex planar billiards of positive curvature. Such a billiard is an ellipse whenever a full grazing annulus is continuously foliated by individually invariant essential curves. A continuous physical collar of smooth closed convex caustics also suffices.","paperIds":["147-01","147-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","overviewTitle":"The near-boundary Birkhoff conjecture","overviewSummary":"Resolves the near-boundary Birkhoff conjecture for smooth strictly convex planar billiards of positive curvature. Such a billiard is an ellipse whenever a full grazing annulus is continuously foliated by individually invariant essential curves. A continuous physical collar of smooth closed convex caustics also suffices."},{"id":"148","title":"The entropy-rate dimension formula for self-similar measures","summary":"For every self-similar measure on the line generated by finitely many contracting similarities, proves $\\dim_{\\mathrm H}\\mu=\\min\\{1,h_{\\mathrm{RW}}/\\chi\\}$, where $h_{\\mathrm{RW}}$ is the entropy rate of random composed maps and χ the average logarithmic contraction. This resolves the entropy-rate dimension conjecture without a separation assumption, allowing exact overlaps and unequal contraction ratios.","paperIds":["148-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/148.md","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","overviewTitle":"The dimension formula for self-similar measures on the line","overviewSummary":"For every self-similar measure on the line generated by finitely many contracting similarities, proves $\\dim_{\\mathrm H}\\mu=\\min\\{1,h_{\\mathrm{RW}}/\\chi\\}$, where $h_{\\mathrm{RW}}$ is the entropy rate of random composed maps and $\\chi$ the average logarithmic contraction. This resolves the entropy-rate dimension conjecture without a separation assumption, allowing exact overlaps and unequal contraction ratios."},{"id":"149","title":"Classwise permanence for weakly reversible mass-action systems","summary":"Proves the permanence conjecture for every finite weakly reversible mass-action system with fixed positive rate constants. Every positive stoichiometric compatibility class, even an unbounded one, has a common compact convex forward-invariant absorbing set. All positive trajectories in that class therefore eventually share positive lower and finite upper concentration bounds.","paperIds":["149-01","149-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/149.md","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","overviewTitle":"Permanence for weakly reversible reaction networks","overviewSummary":"Proves the permanence conjecture for every finite weakly reversible mass-action system with fixed positive rate constants. Every positive stoichiometric compatibility class, even an unbounded one, has a common compact convex forward-invariant absorbing set. All positive trajectories in that class therefore eventually share positive lower and finite upper concentration bounds."},{"id":"150","title":"Weak mixing of triangular billiards with an irrational angle","summary":"Proves that the billiard flow in every nondegenerate Euclidean triangle with at least one angle irrational relative to π is weakly mixing for normalized area times uniform direction. This strengthens ergodicity on the entire irrational-angle class, with no genericity or Diophantine restrictions.","paperIds":["150-01","150-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/150.md","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","overviewTitle":"Weak mixing of irrational triangular billiards","overviewSummary":"Proves that the billiard flow in every nondegenerate Euclidean triangle with at least one angle irrational relative to $\\pi$ is weakly mixing for normalized area times uniform direction. This strengthens ergodicity on the entire irrational-angle class, with no genericity or Diophantine restrictions."},{"id":"151","title":"A C1 counterexample to the entropy conjecture","summary":"Constructs a noninvertible C1 self-map of a compact smooth manifold with zero topological entropy but eigenvalue 2 on second homology. This disproves the homological entropy lower bound for general C1 self-maps: homological growth need not force positive orbit complexity.","paperIds":["151-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/151.md","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","overviewTitle":"A $C^1$ counterexample to Shub’s entropy conjecture","overviewSummary":"Constructs a noninvertible $C^1$ self-map of a compact smooth manifold with zero topological entropy but eigenvalue $2$ on second homology. This disproves the homological entropy lower bound for general $C^1$ self-maps: homological growth need not force positive orbit complexity."},{"id":"152","title":"Zero entropy does not guarantee a smooth positive-volume model","summary":"Constructs a zero-entropy ergodic invertible transformation of a standard nonatomic probability space that is not measurably conjugate to any C∞ diffeomorphism preserving a strictly positive smooth probability density on a compact finite-dimensional manifold. One example rules out every finite dimension.","paperIds":["152-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/152.md","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","overviewTitle":"Zero entropy does not guarantee a smooth positive-volume model","overviewSummary":"Constructs a zero-entropy ergodic invertible transformation of a standard nonatomic probability space that is not measurably conjugate to any $C^\\infty$ diffeomorphism preserving a strictly positive smooth probability density on a compact finite-dimensional manifold. One example rules out every finite dimension."},{"id":"153","title":"Arithmetic classification and non-Pisot singularity for Bernoulli convolutions","summary":"Classifies singular and absolutely continuous unbiased Bernoulli convolutions for every $\\lambda\\in(0,1)$ by an infinite, one-sided approximation condition using explicit finite sets of algebraic units. It also proves singularity at reciprocals of every quartic Salem number in $(1,2)$, giving examples beyond reciprocal Pisot parameters.","paperIds":["153-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","overviewTitle":"Arithmetic classification of Bernoulli convolutions","overviewSummary":"Classifies singular and absolutely continuous unbiased Bernoulli convolutions for every $\\lambda\\in(0,1)$ by an infinite, one-sided approximation condition using explicit finite sets of algebraic units. It also proves singularity at reciprocals of every quartic Salem number in $(1,2)$, giving examples beyond reciprocal Pisot parameters."},{"id":"154","title":"Pointwise multiple ergodic averages for mixing transformations","summary":"Proves almost-everywhere convergence of consecutive multiple ergodic averages of every finite length for invertible mixing probability-preserving transformations. For each fixed tuple of bounded functions, the limit is the product of their integrals, along all positive averaging lengths. No mixing rate or standardness assumption on the probability space is required.","paperIds":["154-01","154-02","154-03","154-04"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","overviewTitle":"Pointwise multiple ergodic averages for mixing transformations","overviewSummary":"Proves almost-everywhere convergence of consecutive multiple ergodic averages of every finite length for invertible mixing probability-preserving transformations. For each fixed tuple of bounded functions, the limit is the product of their integrals, along all positive averaging lengths. No mixing rate or standardness assumption on the probability space is required."},{"id":"155","title":"A counterexample to periodic tiling in dimension three","summary":"Constructs a finite translational tile in ℤ3 that tiles space but admits no fully periodic tiling, disproving the periodic tiling conjecture in the smallest possible lattice dimension. Its unit-cube thickening gives the same counterexample in ℝ3, even with arbitrary real translation vectors.","paperIds":["155-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/155.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"A counterexample to periodic tiling in dimension three","overviewSummary":"Constructs a finite translational tile in $\\mathbb Z^3$ that tiles space but admits no fully periodic tiling, disproving the periodic tiling conjecture in the smallest possible lattice dimension. Its unit-cube thickening gives the same counterexample in $\\mathbb R^3$, even with arbitrary real translation vectors."},{"id":"156","title":"Borsuk's conjecture fails in dimension nine","summary":"Constructs a compact subset of ℝ9 that cannot be covered by ten sets of strictly smaller diameter, disproving Borsuk's covering assertion already in dimension nine. The example consists of rank-one orthogonal projectors onto lines in ℝ4, with the Frobenius metric.","paperIds":["156-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/156.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Borsuk's conjecture fails in dimension nine","overviewSummary":"Constructs a compact subset of $\\mathbb R^9$ that cannot be covered by ten sets of strictly smaller diameter, disproving Borsuk's covering assertion already in dimension nine. The example consists of rank-one orthogonal projectors onto lines in $\\mathbb R^4$, with the Frobenius metric."},{"id":"157","title":"Graph coloring, clique minors, and Colin de Verdière invariants","summary":"Disproves Hadwiger's conjecture even for fractional coloring: arbitrarily large finite simple graphs with independence number at most two satisfy $\\chi_f(G)\\gt h(G)$, where $h(G)$ is the largest clique-minor order. Also disproves the fractional Colin de Verdière chromatic bound $\\chi_f(G)\\le\\mu(G)+1$. In the positive direction, every finite nonempty graph satisfies $\\chi_{\\mathrm{list}}(G)\\le C h(G)$ for a universal constant C.","paperIds":["157-01","157-02","157-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/157.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Counterexamples to the Hadwiger and Colin de Verdi\\`ere conjectures","overviewSummary":"Disproves Hadwiger's conjecture even for fractional coloring: arbitrarily large finite simple graphs with independence number at most two satisfy $\\chi_f(G)>h(G)$, where $h(G)$ is the largest clique-minor order. Also disproves the fractional Colin de Verdi\\`ere chromatic bound $\\chi_f(G)\\le\\mu(G)+1$. In the positive direction, every finite nonempty graph satisfies $\\chi_{\\mathrm{list}}(G)\\le C h(G)$ for a universal constant $C$."},{"id":"158","title":"The Euclidean plane cannot be colored with five colors","summary":"Proves that every five-coloring of the Euclidean plane has a monochromatic pair at distance one, with no restriction on the color classes. This advances the Hadwiger–Nelson problem: together with the classical seven-coloring, only six and seven remain possible chromatic numbers of the plane.","paperIds":["158-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/158.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"The Euclidean plane cannot be colored with five colors","overviewSummary":"Proves that every five-coloring of the Euclidean plane has a monochromatic pair at distance one, with no restriction on the color classes. This advances the Hadwiger--Nelson problem: together with the classical seven-coloring, only six and seven remain possible chromatic numbers of the plane."},{"id":"159","title":"Erdős’s reciprocal-sum conjecture and quasipolynomial Szemerédi bounds","summary":"Proves Erdős's conjecture that every set of positive integers with divergent reciprocal sum contains arithmetic progressions of every finite length. Quantitatively, for each fixed k ≥ 3, every subset of $\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ with no nonconstant k-term progression has size at most $C_kN\\exp[-c_k(\\log N)^{\\varepsilon_k}]$, with positive constants depending only on k.","paperIds":["159-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/159.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Erd\\H{o}s's reciprocal-sum conjecture and quasipolynomial Szemer\\'edi bounds","overviewSummary":"Proves Erd\\H{o}s's conjecture that every set of positive integers with divergent reciprocal sum contains arithmetic progressions of every finite length. Quantitatively, for each fixed $k\\ge3$, every subset of $\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ with no nonconstant $k$-term progression has size at most $C_kN\\exp[-c_k(\\log N)^{\\varepsilon_k}]$, with positive constants depending only on $k$."},{"id":"160","title":"Superexponential van der Waerden numbers","summary":"Resolves Erdős's superexponential-growth question for van der Waerden numbers. If $W_r(k)$ is the least interval length forcing a monochromatic k-term progression in every r-coloring, then $W_r(k)\\gt k^{ck\\lfloor\\log_2 r\\rfloor}$ for an absolute c > 0, all r ≥ 2 and sufficiently large k, uniformly in r. In particular, $W_r(k)^{1/k}\\to\\infty$ for each fixed r.","paperIds":["160-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/160.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Superexponential van der Waerden numbers","overviewSummary":"Resolves Erd\\H{o}s's superexponential-growth question for van der Waerden numbers. If $W_r(k)$ is the least interval length forcing a monochromatic $k$-term progression in every $r$-coloring, then $W_r(k)>k^{ck\\lfloor\\log_2 r\\rfloor}$ for an absolute $c>0$, all $r\\ge2$ and sufficiently large $k$, uniformly in $r$. In particular, $W_r(k)^{1/k}\\to\\infty$ for each fixed $r$."},{"id":"161","title":"Counterexamples to Sidorenko’s conjecture and the forcing conjecture","summary":"Disproves Sidorenko's conjecture with a connected bipartite pattern on 35 vertices and 66 edges that occurs less frequently than in a random graph of the same edge density. The same pattern disproves the forcing conjecture of Skokan and Thoma: matching its density and the edge density of a constant graphon need not force quasirandomness.","paperIds":["161-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/161.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Counterexamples to Sidorenko's conjecture and the forcing conjecture","overviewSummary":"Disproves Sidorenko's conjecture with a connected bipartite pattern on $35$ vertices and $66$ edges that occurs less frequently than in a random graph of the same edge density. The same pattern disproves the forcing conjecture of Skokan and Thoma: matching its density and the edge density of a constant graphon need not force quasirandomness."},{"id":"162","title":"Counterexamples to Ryser’s covering conjecture","summary":"Disproves Ryser's covering conjecture by constructing intersecting $(q+1)$-partite, $(q+1)$-uniform hypergraphs with covering number $q+1$, rather than the predicted bound q, for every sufficiently large prime q. A separate construction over extension fields also disproves Gyárfás's monochromatic tree-cover conjecture.","paperIds":["162-01","162-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/162.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Counterexamples to Ryser's covering and Gy\\'arf\\'as's tree-cover conjectures","overviewSummary":"Disproves Ryser's covering conjecture by constructing intersecting $(q+1)$-partite, $(q+1)$-uniform hypergraphs with covering number $q+1$, rather than the predicted bound $q$, for every sufficiently large prime $q$. A separate construction over extension fields also disproves Gy\\'arf\\'as's monochromatic tree-cover conjecture."},{"id":"164","title":"Hindman’s finite sums and products conjecture","summary":"Proves Hindman's finite sums and products conjecture: every finite coloring of the positive integers contains sets of any prescribed finite size whose nonempty subset sums and nonempty subset products all have one common color.","paperIds":["164-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Hindman's finite sums and products conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves Hindman's finite sums and products conjecture: every finite coloring of the positive integers contains sets of any prescribed finite size whose nonempty subset sums and nonempty subset products all have one common color."},{"id":"165","title":"The Harary–Hill and Zarankiewicz crossing-number formulas","summary":"Resolves the Harary–Hill conjecture and Turán's brickyard problem in the Zarankiewicz formulation, determining the crossing numbers of every complete and complete bipartite graph. The result proves the optimality of the classical drawings among all plane drawings with continuous edge arcs.","paperIds":["165-01","165-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/165.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Exact crossing numbers of complete and complete bipartite graphs","overviewSummary":"Resolves the Harary--Hill conjecture and Tur\\'an's brickyard problem in the Zarankiewicz formulation, determining the crossing numbers of every complete and complete bipartite graph. The result proves the optimality of the classical drawings among all plane drawings with continuous edge arcs."},{"id":"166","title":"The higher-dimensional Erdős distinct-distances conjecture","summary":"For every fixed d ≥ 3, any n ≥ 2 distinct points in ℝd determine at least $c_dn^{2/d}$ distinct distances, with $c_d\\gt 0$ depending only on dimension. This matches the integer-grid order and resolves the higher-dimensional Erdős distinct-distances conjecture with a constant-factor bound.","paperIds":["166-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"The higher-dimensional Erd\\H{o}s distinct-distances conjecture","overviewSummary":"For every fixed $d\\ge3$, any $n\\ge2$ distinct points in $\\mathbb R^d$ determine at least $c_dn^{2/d}$ distinct distances, with $c_d>0$ depending only on dimension. This matches the integer-grid order and resolves the higher-dimensional Erd\\H{o}s distinct-distances conjecture with a constant-factor bound."},{"id":"167","title":"Planar distinct distances and unit-distance bounds","summary":"Proves the weak pinned Erdős distance conjecture: for every fixed ε > 0, all but $o(n)$ points of any n-point planar set determine at least $n^{1-\\varepsilon}$ distinct nonzero distances. A complementary theorem bounds the number of unit-distance pairs by $O(n^{4/3-\\delta})$ for an absolute δ > 0.","paperIds":["167-01","167-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/167.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Pinned distances and a power saving for planar unit distances","overviewSummary":"Proves the weak pinned Erd\\H{o}s distance conjecture: for every fixed $\\varepsilon>0$, all but $o(n)$ points of any $n$-point planar set determine at least $n^{1-\\varepsilon}$ distinct nonzero distances. A complementary theorem bounds the number of unit-distance pairs by $O(n^{4/3-\\delta})$ for an absolute $\\delta>0$."},{"id":"168","title":"Combinatorial invariance of Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomials","summary":"Resolves the full combinatorial invariance conjecture: isomorphic Bruhat intervals in arbitrary Coxeter systems have identical equal-parameter Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomials. Thus the abstract order of the interval determines the polynomial, even across different Coxeter systems.","paperIds":["168-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/168.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Combinatorial invariance of Kazhdan--Lusztig polynomials","overviewSummary":"Resolves the full combinatorial invariance conjecture: isomorphic Bruhat intervals in arbitrary Coxeter systems have identical equal-parameter Kazhdan--Lusztig polynomials. Thus the abstract order of the interval determines the polynomial, even across different Coxeter systems."},{"id":"169","title":"Shareshian–Wachs elementary positivity","summary":"Resolves the elementary-positivity part of the Shareshian–Wachs conjecture: the chromatic quasisymmetric function of every natural unit interval graph has elementary-basis coefficients in $\\mathbb N[q]$. The coefficients count explicitly described permutations, giving a combinatorial explanation of positivity.","paperIds":["169-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/169.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"The Shareshian--Wachs $e$-positivity conjecture","overviewSummary":"Resolves the elementary-positivity part of the Shareshian--Wachs conjecture: the chromatic quasisymmetric function of every natural unit interval graph has elementary-basis coefficients in $\\mathbb N[q]$. The coefficients count explicitly described permutations, giving a combinatorial explanation of positivity."},{"id":"170","title":"Sharp logarithmic exponents for off-diagonal Ramsey numbers","summary":"For every fixed integer s ≥ 5, proves $r(s,t)=t^{s-1}/(\\log t)^{s-2+o(1)}$ as $t\\to\\infty$, determining the logarithmic exponent and matching the classical upper bound at that scale. Here $r(s,t)$ is the least number of vertices forcing an s-clique or a t-vertex independent set.","paperIds":["170-01","170-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/170.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Sharp logarithmic exponents for off-diagonal Ramsey numbers","overviewSummary":"For every fixed integer $s\\ge5$, proves $r(s,t)=t^{s-1}/(\\log t)^{s-2+o(1)}$ as $t\\to\\infty$, determining the logarithmic exponent and matching the classical upper bound at that scale. Here $r(s,t)$ is the least number of vertices forcing an $s$-clique or a $t$-vertex independent set."},{"id":"171","title":"The hypercube Ramsey conjecture","summary":"Resolves the Burr–Erdős hypercube Ramsey conjecture: the two-color Ramsey number of the n-dimensional cube is $\\Theta(2^n)$. Thus every red-blue coloring of a complete graph on a universal constant times the cube's number of vertices contains a monochromatic copy of the cube.","paperIds":["171-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"The hypercube Ramsey conjecture","overviewSummary":"Resolves the Burr--Erd\\H{o}s hypercube Ramsey conjecture: the two-color Ramsey number of the $n$-dimensional cube is $\\Theta(2^n)$. Thus every red-blue coloring of a complete graph on a universal constant times the cube's number of vertices contains a monochromatic copy of the cube."},{"id":"172","title":"Classification of finite Euclidean Ramsey configurations","summary":"Classifies finite point configurations that occur monochromatically, at their original scale, in every finite coloring of sufficiently high-dimensional Euclidean space. The characterization is an algebraic condition over the coordinate field. It also disproves the Leader–Russell–Walters conjecture that every such configuration is a subset of a finite transitive set.","paperIds":["172-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/172.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Classification of finite Euclidean Ramsey configurations","overviewSummary":"Classifies finite point configurations that occur monochromatically, at their original scale, in every finite coloring of sufficiently high-dimensional Euclidean space. The characterization is an algebraic condition over the coordinate field. It also disproves the Leader--Russell--Walters conjecture that every such configuration is a subset of a finite transitive set."},{"id":"173","title":"Seymour’s second-neighborhood conjecture","summary":"Proves Seymour's second-neighborhood conjecture: every nonempty finite oriented graph has a vertex with at least as many vertices at directed distance exactly two as at directed distance one. Oriented graphs may be arbitrary apart from the exclusion of loops and oppositely directed edge pairs.","paperIds":["173-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/173.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Seymour's second-neighborhood conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves Seymour's second-neighborhood conjecture: every nonempty finite oriented graph has a vertex with at least as many vertices at directed distance exactly two as at directed distance one. Oriented graphs may be arbitrary apart from the exclusion of loops and oppositely directed edge pairs."},{"id":"174","title":"Deterministic construction of strong thin spanning trees","summary":"Resolves the strong thin-tree conjecture constructively. Every finite loopless k-edge-connected multigraph on at least two vertices has a spanning tree containing at most a universal $C/k$ fraction of the edges of every cut. Such a tree can be found deterministically in polynomial time, even with binary-encoded parallel-edge multiplicities.","paperIds":["174-01","174-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/174.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Deterministic construction of strong thin spanning trees","overviewSummary":"Resolves the strong thin-tree conjecture constructively. Every finite loopless $k$-edge-connected multigraph on at least two vertices has a spanning tree containing at most a universal $C/k$ fraction of the edges of every cut. Such a tree can be found deterministically in polynomial time, even with binary-encoded parallel-edge multiplicities."},{"id":"175","title":"Talagrand’s expectation thresholds, discrete convexity, and graph decompositions","summary":"Proves that integral and fractional expectation thresholds differ by at most a universal factor, and resolves Talagrand's discrete-convexity conjecture. An application proves the Ascoli–He–Park–Talagrand graph-decomposition conjecture: every graph's edges split into a universally bounded number of fixed pieces, each with containment threshold at most a universal constant times the original graph's integral expectation threshold. The pieces' embeddings need not agree on shared vertices.","paperIds":["175-01","175-02","175-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/175.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Talagrand's conjectures and graph decompositions at expectation thresholds","overviewSummary":"Proves that integral and fractional expectation thresholds differ by at most a universal factor, and resolves Talagrand's discrete-convexity conjecture. An application proves the Ascoli--He--Park--Talagrand graph-decomposition conjecture: every graph's edges split into a universally bounded number of fixed pieces, each with containment threshold at most a universal constant times the original graph's integral expectation threshold. The pieces' embeddings need not agree on shared vertices."},{"id":"176","title":"The second Kahn–Kalai conjecture with an edge-count bound","summary":"Proves the second Kahn–Kalai conjecture: for every finite simple graph H with h ≥ 1 edges and at most n vertices, its appearance threshold in $G(n,p)$ is at most $C p_{\\mathrm E}(n,H)(1+\\log_2 h)$, with universal C. Here $p_{\\mathrm E}$ is the least density at which every subgraph of H has expected copy count at least 1/2.","paperIds":["176-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/176.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"The second Kahn--Kalai conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves the second Kahn--Kalai conjecture: for every finite simple graph $H$ with $h\\ge1$ edges and at most $n$ vertices, its appearance threshold in $G(n,p)$ is at most $C p_{\\mathrm E}(n,H)(1+\\log_2 h)$, with universal $C$. Here $p_{\\mathrm E}$ is the least density at which every subgraph of $H$ has expected copy count at least $1/2$."},{"id":"177","title":"Bounded-degree coboundary expanders","summary":"Constructs arbitrarily large finite d-dimensional simplicial complexes, for every d ≥ 3, with uniformly bounded vertex degrees and uniform 𝔽2 coboundary expansion in every degree below d. Together with the known graph and two-dimensional cases, this establishes the existence of such expanders in every positive dimension.","paperIds":["177-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/177.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Bounded-degree coboundary expanders in every dimension","overviewSummary":"Constructs arbitrarily large finite $d$-dimensional simplicial complexes, for every $d\\ge3$, with uniformly bounded vertex degrees and uniform $\\mathbb F_2$ coboundary expansion in every degree below $d$. Together with the known graph and two-dimensional cases, this establishes the existence of such expanders in every positive dimension."},{"id":"178","title":"Deterministic nonbipartite Ramanujan graphs in every fixed degree","summary":"For every fixed d ≥ 3, constructs a simple d-regular nonbipartite Ramanujan graph on every sufficiently large even number n of vertices, with every nonconstant adjacency eigenvalue strictly between $-2\\sqrt{d-1}$ and $2\\sqrt{d-1}$. A deterministic algorithm outputs the full adjacency list in polynomial bit time, with exponent depending on d.","paperIds":["178-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Deterministic nonbipartite Ramanujan graphs in every fixed degree","overviewSummary":"For every fixed $d\\ge3$, constructs a simple $d$-regular nonbipartite Ramanujan graph on every sufficiently large even number $n$ of vertices, with every nonconstant adjacency eigenvalue strictly between $-2\\sqrt{d-1}$ and $2\\sqrt{d-1}$. A deterministic algorithm outputs the full adjacency list in polynomial bit time, with exponent depending on $d$."},{"id":"179","title":"The circulant Hadamard and Barker-sequence conjectures","summary":"Proves that real circulant Hadamard matrices exist exactly in orders 1 and 4, resolving the circulant Hadamard conjecture. Together with classical Barker-sequence results, this shows that binary sequences whose nontrivial aperiodic autocorrelations have magnitude at most 1 exist at lengths n > 1 exactly when $n\\in\\{2,3,4,5,7,11,13\\}$.","paperIds":["179-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/179.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"The circulant Hadamard conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves that real circulant Hadamard matrices exist exactly in orders $1$ and $4$, resolving the circulant Hadamard conjecture. Together with classical Barker-sequence results, this shows that binary sequences whose nontrivial aperiodic autocorrelations have magnitude at most $1$ exist at lengths $n>1$ exactly when $n\\in\\{2,3,4,5,7,11,13\\}$."},{"id":"180","title":"Barnette’s Hamiltonian-cycle conjecture","summary":"Proves that every finite simple cubic bipartite planar 3-vertex-connected graph has a Hamiltonian cycle, resolving Barnette's conjecture. Equivalently, every three-edge path in a finite simple cubic 3-vertex-connected bipartite Pfaffian graph lies in a Hamiltonian cycle.","paperIds":["180-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/180.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Barnette's Hamiltonian-cycle conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves that every finite simple cubic bipartite planar $3$-vertex-connected graph has a Hamiltonian cycle, resolving Barnette's conjecture. Equivalently, every three-edge path in a finite simple cubic $3$-vertex-connected bipartite Pfaffian graph lies in a Hamiltonian cycle."},{"id":"181","title":"The Erdős–Gallai cycle-decomposition conjecture","summary":"Proves that the edges of every finite simple undirected graph on n vertices can be partitioned into at most $Cn$ simple cycles and single edges, for an absolute constant C. This resolves the Erdős–Gallai cycle-decomposition conjecture, bounding the number of pieces linearly even for dense graphs.","paperIds":["181-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/181.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"The Erd\\H{o}s--Gallai cycle-decomposition conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves that the edges of every finite simple undirected graph on $n$ vertices can be partitioned into at most $Cn$ simple cycles and single edges, for an absolute constant $C$. This resolves the Erd\\H{o}s--Gallai cycle-decomposition conjecture, bounding the number of pieces linearly even for dense graphs."},{"id":"182","title":"Power savings for intersective polynomial differences and prime arguments","summary":"For every fixed intersective integer polynomial h of degree k ≥ 2 with positive leading coefficient, proves that a subset of $\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ avoiding nonzero values $h(1),h(2),\\ldots$ as differences has size $O_h(N^{1-c_k})$, with $c_k\\gt 0$ depending only on degree. Here intersective means having a root modulo every modulus. For prime arguments, a power saving also holds when h has a unit root modulo every modulus, with exponent allowed to depend on h.","paperIds":["182-01","182-02","182-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/182.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Power savings for polynomial-difference-free sets","overviewSummary":"For every fixed intersective integer polynomial $h$ of degree $k\\ge2$ with positive leading coefficient, proves that a subset of $\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ avoiding nonzero values $h(1),h(2),\\ldots$ as differences has size $O_h(N^{1-c_k})$, with $c_k>0$ depending only on degree. Here intersective means having a root modulo every modulus. For prime arguments, a power saving also holds when $h$ has a unit root modulo every modulus, with exponent allowed to depend on $h$."},{"id":"183","title":"Power savings for planar halving lines and k-sets","summary":"Improves the planar halving-line bound to $O(n^{4/3-\\varepsilon})$ for sets with no three collinear and an absolute ε > 0. More generally, an n-point set with no three collinear has $O(n(k+1)^{1/3-\\varepsilon_0})$ strictly separable k-subsets for $1\\le k\\le n/2$, with an absolute $\\varepsilon_0\\gt 0$. The constants and positive exponents are nonquantitative.","paperIds":["183-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/183.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Power savings for planar halving lines and $k$-sets","overviewSummary":"Improves the planar halving-line bound to $O(n^{4/3-\\varepsilon})$ for sets with no three collinear and an absolute $\\varepsilon>0$. More generally, an $n$-point set with no three collinear has $O(n(k+1)^{1/3-\\varepsilon_0})$ strictly separable $k$-subsets for $1\\le k\\le n/2$, with an absolute $\\varepsilon_0>0$. The constants and positive exponents are nonquantitative."},{"id":"184","title":"Correspondence coloring with a fixed forbidden subgraph","summary":"Proves the Alon–Krivelevich–Sudakov coloring conjecture in correspondence-coloring form: graphs avoiding any fixed subgraph F need $O_F(\\Delta/\\log\\Delta)$ colors when their maximum degree Δ is sufficiently large. Also proves the Ajtai–Erdős–Komlós–Szemerédi independence conjecture: for fixed r ≥ 4, every n-vertex Kr-free graph of average degree d ≥ 2 has an independent set of size $\\Omega_r(n\\log d/d)$.","paperIds":["184-01","184-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/184.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Coloring and independence in graphs with forbidden subgraphs","overviewSummary":"Proves the Alon--Krivelevich--Sudakov coloring conjecture in correspondence-coloring form: graphs avoiding any fixed subgraph $F$ need $O_F(\\Delta/\\log\\Delta)$ colors when their maximum degree $\\Delta$ is sufficiently large. Also proves the Ajtai--Erd\\H{o}s--Koml\\'os--Szemer\\'edi independence conjecture: for fixed $r\\ge4$, every $n$-vertex $K_r$-free graph of average degree $d\\ge2$ has an independent set of size $\\Omega_r(n\\log d/d)$."},{"id":"185","title":"Counterexamples to infinite matroid intersection and packing/covering","summary":"Disproves the unrestricted infinite matroid intersection and packing/covering conjectures in ZFC, using two self-dual partitional matroids on a countably infinite ground set. The same examples answer Joó’s partitional-matroid question negatively. They are neither finitary nor cofinitary, so Nash-Williams’ original finitary conjecture remains outside the result.","paperIds":["185-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/185.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Counterexamples to infinite matroid intersection and packing/covering","overviewSummary":"Disproves the unrestricted infinite matroid intersection and packing/covering conjectures in ZFC, using two self-dual partitional matroids on a countably infinite ground set. The same examples answer Jo\\'o’s partitional-matroid question negatively. They are neither finitary nor cofinitary, so Nash-Williams’ original finitary conjecture remains outside the result."},{"id":"186","title":"Uniform influence and sharp thresholds for graph and hypergraph properties","summary":"Proves the Friedgut–Kalai threshold-width conjectures for graphs and fixed-uniformity hypergraphs. For fixed $0\\lt \\varepsilon\\lt 1/2$, every nontrivial increasing relabeling-invariant property crosses from probability ε to $1-\\varepsilon$ within width $O((\\log n)^{-2})$ for graphs and $O_r((\\log n)^{-r/(r-1)})$ for r-uniform hypergraphs, r ≥ 3. The hypergraph influence bound also applies to nonmonotone properties.","paperIds":["186-01","186-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/186.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"The Friedgut--Kalai graph and hypergraph threshold conjectures","overviewSummary":"Proves the Friedgut--Kalai threshold-width conjectures for graphs and fixed-uniformity hypergraphs. For fixed $0<\\varepsilon<1/2$, every nontrivial increasing relabeling-invariant property crosses from probability $\\varepsilon$ to $1-\\varepsilon$ within width $O((\\log n)^{-2})$ for graphs and $O_r((\\log n)^{-r/(r-1)})$ for $r$-uniform hypergraphs, $r\\ge3$. The hypergraph influence bound also applies to nonmonotone properties."},{"id":"187","title":"Snaky in 21 Maker moves","summary":"Settles the Snaky achievement problem: Maker can force the six-cell Snaky shape within 21 of its own moves on the initially empty infinite square board. Maker moves first, each player claims one free cell per turn, and translations, rotations and reflections count as wins.","paperIds":["187-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/187.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Snaky in 21 Maker moves","overviewSummary":"Settles the Snaky achievement problem: Maker can force the six-cell Snaky shape within 21 of its own moves on the initially empty infinite square board. Maker moves first, each player claims one free cell per turn, and translations, rotations and reflections count as wins."},{"id":"188","title":"The sharp terminal leave in random triangle removal","summary":"Starting from the complete graph on n vertices, repeatedly delete a uniformly chosen remaining triangle. The terminal edge count is asymptotic to $n^{3/2}/(2\\sqrt2)$, with mean-square convergence after normalization by n3/2. This proves the triangle case of the Joos–Kühn sharp-constant conjecture.","paperIds":["188-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/188.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"The sharp constant in random triangle removal","overviewSummary":"Starting from the complete graph on $n$ vertices, repeatedly delete a uniformly chosen remaining triangle. The terminal edge count is asymptotic to $n^{3/2}/(2\\sqrt2)$, with mean-square convergence after normalization by $n^{3/2}$. This proves the triangle case of the Joos--K\\\"uhn sharp-constant conjecture."},{"id":"189","title":"Cycle–clique Ramsey numbers","summary":"Proves the Erdős–Faudree–Rousseau–Schelp conjecture: $R(C_m,K_n)=(m-1)(n-1)+1$ for every $m\\ge n\\ge3$, except $R(C_3,K_3)=6$. This is the exact threshold forcing a red m-cycle or a blue n-clique in every red–blue coloring of a complete graph.","paperIds":["189-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/189.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Exact cycle--clique Ramsey numbers","overviewSummary":"Proves the Erd\\H{o}s--Faudree--Rousseau--Schelp conjecture: $R(C_m,K_n)=(m-1)(n-1)+1$ for every $m\\ge n\\ge3$, except $R(C_3,K_3)=6$. This is the exact threshold forcing a red $m$-cycle or a blue $n$-clique in every red--blue coloring of a complete graph."},{"id":"190","title":"Polynomial removal fails for ordered binary matrices","summary":"Disproves polynomial ordered binary matrix removal with one fixed $66\\times66$ zero–one pattern. Matrices can require many binary-entry changes to become pattern-free while their copy density is smaller than every proposed polynomial bound in that distance. Copies preserve row and column orders and match both zeros and ones.","paperIds":["190-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/190.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"Polynomial removal fails for ordered binary matrices","overviewSummary":"Disproves polynomial ordered binary matrix removal with one fixed $66\\times66$ zero--one pattern. Matrices can require many binary-entry changes to become pattern-free while their copy density is smaller than every proposed polynomial bound in that distance. Copies preserve row and column orders and match both zeros and ones."},{"id":"191","title":"A power improvement in the Heilbronn triangle lower bound","summary":"For every sufficiently large n, constructs n points in the unit square such that every triangle has area at least $n^{-2+c}$ for one absolute c > 0. This disproves the conjectured almost-n−2 upper bound in Heilbronn's triangle problem, which asks how large the smallest determined triangle can be.","paperIds":["191-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/191.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"A power improvement in the Heilbronn triangle problem","overviewSummary":"For every sufficiently large $n$, constructs $n$ points in the unit square such that every triangle has area at least $n^{-2+c}$ for one absolute $c>0$. This disproves the conjectured almost-$n^{-2}$ upper bound in Heilbronn's triangle problem, which asks how large the smallest determined triangle can be."},{"id":"192","title":"Boolean functions violate the square-root degree bound by arbitrary factors","summary":"Disproves the proposed square-root bound relating a Boolean function's linear Fourier coefficients to its polynomial degree. For every C > 0, there is a sign-valued Boolean function f with $\\sum_i\\widehat f(\\{i\\})\\gt C\\sqrt{\\deg(f)}$. Thus its total signed correlation with individual input bits can exceed the proposed bound by an arbitrary factor.","paperIds":["192-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/192.md","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","overviewTitle":"A counterexample to the Gopalan--Servedio conjecture","overviewSummary":"Disproves the proposed square-root bound relating a Boolean function's linear Fourier coefficients to its polynomial degree. For every $C>0$, there is a sign-valued Boolean function $f$ with $\\sum_i\\widehat f(\\{i\\})>C\\sqrt{\\deg(f)}$. Thus its total signed correlation with individual input bits can exceed the proposed bound by an arbitrary factor."},{"id":"193","title":"Serre’s intersection-multiplicity conjecture","summary":"Proves strict positivity of Serre's intersection multiplicity $\\chi^R(M,N)$ for nonzero finitely generated modules over any regular local ring, provided $M\\otimes_R N$ has finite length and $\\dim M+\\dim N=\\dim R$. This resolves the positivity conjecture, including ramified mixed characteristic.","paperIds":["193-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","overviewTitle":"Serre's intersection-multiplicity conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves strict positivity of Serre's intersection multiplicity $\\chi^R(M,N)$ for nonzero finitely generated modules over any regular local ring, provided $M\\otimes_R N$ has finite length and $\\dim M+\\dim N=\\dim R$. This resolves the positivity conjecture, including ramified mixed characteristic."},{"id":"194","title":"Lech’s multiplicity conjecture","summary":"Proves $e(R)\\le e(S)$ for every flat local homomorphism of nonzero Noetherian local rings, where e is Hilbert–Samuel multiplicity. This resolves Lech's conjecture in every dimension and characteristic.","paperIds":["194-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/194.md","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","overviewTitle":"Lech's multiplicity conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves $e(R)\\le e(S)$ for every flat local homomorphism of nonzero Noetherian local rings, where $e$ is Hilbert--Samuel multiplicity. This resolves Lech's conjecture in every dimension and characteristic."},{"id":"195","title":"A counterexample to the small Cohen–Macaulay module conjecture","summary":"Constructs a three-dimensional complete Noetherian normal local domain over ℂ with no nonzero finitely generated maximal Cohen–Macaulay module. A three-dimensional local domain essentially of finite type over ℂ has the same property, disproving the domain form of the small Cohen–Macaulay module conjecture.","paperIds":["195-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/195.md","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","overviewTitle":"A counterexample to the small Cohen--Macaulay module conjecture","overviewSummary":"Constructs a three-dimensional complete Noetherian normal local domain over $\\mathbb C$ with no nonzero finitely generated maximal Cohen--Macaulay module. A three-dimensional local domain essentially of finite type over $\\mathbb C$ has the same property, disproving the domain form of the small Cohen--Macaulay module conjecture."},{"id":"196","title":"A counterexample to Kaplansky’s zero-divisor conjecture","summary":"Constructs a finitely presented torsion-free group G whose group algebra $\\mathbb F_2[G]$ has nonzero zero divisors, disproving Kaplansky's zero-divisor conjecture. The group has a finite two-dimensional classifying space.","paperIds":["196-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/196.md","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","overviewTitle":"A counterexample to Kaplansky's zero-divisor conjecture","overviewSummary":"Constructs a finitely presented torsion-free group $G$ whose group algebra $\\mathbb F_2[G]$ has nonzero zero divisors, disproving Kaplansky's zero-divisor conjecture. The group has a finite two-dimensional classifying space."},{"id":"197","title":"A torsion-free group algebra that is not directly finite","summary":"Constructs a finitely presented torsion-free nonsofic group whose group algebra over 𝔽2 is not directly finite, disproving Kaplansky's conjecture even without torsion. Companion examples give injective nonsurjective cellular automata on all configurations, refuting Gottschalk's surjunctivity conjecture. Another counterexample is an integral group-ring matrix, invertible over the rational group ring, with Fuglede–Kadison determinant strictly between zero and one, disproving the unrestricted Determinant Conjecture.","paperIds":["197-01","197-02","197-03","197-04"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/197.md","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","overviewTitle":"Nonsofic groups and group-ring counterexamples","overviewSummary":"Constructs a finitely presented torsion-free nonsofic group whose group algebra over $\\mathbb F_2$ is not directly finite, disproving Kaplansky's conjecture even without torsion. Companion examples give injective nonsurjective cellular automata on all configurations, refuting Gottschalk's surjunctivity conjecture. Another counterexample is an integral group-ring matrix, invertible over the rational group ring, with Fuglede--Kadison determinant strictly between zero and one, disproving the unrestricted Determinant Conjecture."},{"id":"198","title":"A counterexample to finitistic-dimension finiteness","summary":"Constructs a finite-dimensional complex algebra whose finite-dimensional modules have unbounded finite projective dimensions. This disproves the little finitistic-dimension conjecture.","paperIds":["198-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/198.md","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","overviewTitle":"A counterexample to the little finitistic-dimension conjecture","overviewSummary":"Constructs a finite-dimensional complex algebra whose finite-dimensional modules have unbounded finite projective dimensions. This disproves the little finitistic-dimension conjecture."},{"id":"199","title":"Counterexamples to Auslander–Reiten, Tachikawa and related homological conjectures","summary":"Constructs finite-dimensional algebras over a characteristic-two rational-function field that disprove the Auslander–Reiten and Gorenstein-projective conjectures, and Tachikawa's second conjecture. An associated endomorphism algebra also disproves the classical, generalized and strong Nakayama conjectures, the Auslander–Gorenstein conjecture, and the Wakamatsu tilting conjecture. The counterexamples persist under every extension of the base field.","paperIds":["199-01","199-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/199.md","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","overviewTitle":"Counterexamples to conjectures of Auslander--Reiten, Tachikawa, and Nakayama","overviewSummary":"Constructs finite-dimensional algebras over a characteristic-two rational-function field that disprove the Auslander--Reiten and Gorenstein-projective conjectures, and Tachikawa's second conjecture. An associated endomorphism algebra also disproves the classical, generalized and strong Nakayama conjectures, the Auslander--Gorenstein conjecture, and the Wakamatsu tilting conjecture. The counterexamples persist under every extension of the base field."},{"id":"200","title":"Eisenbud–Green–Harris and lex-plus-powers","summary":"Proves the Eisenbud–Green–Harris and lex-plus-powers conjectures over every characteristic-zero field. Any homogeneous ideal containing a regular sequence, of arbitrary length and degrees at least two, admits a lex-plus-powers ideal with the same Hilbert function and no smaller graded Betti numbers.","paperIds":["200-01","200-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","overviewTitle":"Eisenbud--Green--Harris and lex-plus-powers in characteristic zero","overviewSummary":"Proves the Eisenbud--Green--Harris and lex-plus-powers conjectures over every characteristic-zero field. Any homogeneous ideal containing a regular sequence, of arbitrary length and degrees at least two, admits a lex-plus-powers ideal with the same Hilbert function and no smaller graded Betti numbers."},{"id":"201","title":"A counterexample to Kurosh’s division-ring problem","summary":"Constructs a countable characteristic-zero division ring that is algebraic over its center and generated by two elements over that center, but has infinite dimension over it. This answers Kurosh's division-ring problem on local finiteness negatively.","paperIds":["201-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","overviewTitle":"A counterexample to Kurosh's division-ring problem","overviewSummary":"Constructs a countable characteristic-zero division ring that is algebraic over its center and generated by two elements over that center, but has infinite dimension over it. This answers Kurosh's division-ring problem on local finiteness negatively."},{"id":"202","title":"The blockwise Alperin weight conjecture","summary":"Proves the numerical blockwise Alperin weight conjecture for every prime and every finite group: the number of irreducible Brauer characters in a block equals the number of conjugacy classes of its weights.","paperIds":["202-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","overviewTitle":"The blockwise Alperin weight conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves the numerical blockwise Alperin weight conjecture for every prime and every finite group: the number of irreducible Brauer characters in a block equals the number of conjugacy classes of its weights."},{"id":"203","title":"Donovan's conjecture over fields and complete mixed-characteristic DVRs","summary":"Proves Donovan's conjecture: over each fixed algebraically closed field of characteristic p, blocks of finite groups with bounded defect-group order have only finitely many Morita-equivalence classes, for every prime p. Also proves the integral form over each fixed complete mixed-characteristic discrete valuation ring with algebraically closed residue field.","paperIds":["203-01","203-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","overviewTitle":"Donovan's conjecture over fields and discrete valuation rings","overviewSummary":"Proves Donovan's conjecture: over each fixed algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$, blocks of finite groups with bounded defect-group order have only finitely many Morita-equivalence classes, for every prime $p$. Also proves the integral form over each fixed complete mixed-characteristic discrete valuation ring with algebraically closed residue field."},{"id":"204","title":"Tensor saturation for even spin groups","summary":"Proves saturation factor one for $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Spin}}\\nolimits (2n)$, n ≥ 2: for three dominant integral weights whose sum lies in the root lattice, an invariant at any common positive integral dilation already gives an invariant at the original weights. This resolves the type-D part of the simply-laced saturation conjecture.","paperIds":["204-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","overviewTitle":"Tensor saturation for even spin groups","overviewSummary":"Proves saturation factor one for $\\operatorname{Spin}(2n)$, $n\\ge2$: for three dominant integral weights whose sum lies in the root lattice, an invariant at any common positive integral dilation already gives an invariant at the original weights. This resolves the type-$D$ part of the simply-laced saturation conjecture."},{"id":"205","title":"Saxl’s conjecture and universal tensor squares","summary":"Proves Saxl's conjecture: the tensor square of every staircase representation contains every irreducible complex representation of the corresponding symmetric group. More generally, every Sn with $n\\notin\\{2,4,9\\}$ has an irreducible representation whose tensor square contains all irreducibles.","paperIds":["205-01","205-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/205.md","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","overviewTitle":"Saxl's conjecture and universal tensor squares","overviewSummary":"Proves Saxl's conjecture: the tensor square of every staircase representation contains every irreducible complex representation of the corresponding symmetric group. More generally, every $S_n$ with $n\\notin\\{2,4,9\\}$ has an irreducible representation whose tensor square contains all irreducibles."},{"id":"206","title":"Finite lattice representation and undecidability","summary":"Some finite lattices are not congruence lattices of any finite algebra, answering the finite lattice representation problem negatively. Moreover, no algorithm decides whether a finite lattice has such a representation, or whether it is a full subgroup interval of a finite group.","paperIds":["206-01","206-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/206.md","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","overviewTitle":"Finite lattice representation: counterexamples and undecidability","overviewSummary":"Some finite lattices are not congruence lattices of any finite algebra, answering the finite lattice representation problem negatively. Moreover, no algorithm decides whether a finite lattice has such a representation, or whether it is a full subgroup interval of a finite group."},{"id":"207","title":"The ℓ¹-Bass conjecture for all discrete groups","summary":"Proves the ℓ1-Bass conjecture for every discrete group: Hattori–Stallings traces of idempotent matrices over $\\ell^1(G)$ are supported on finitely many finite-order conjugacy classes. The algebraic companion proves the integral Bass trace conjecture and Kaplansky's idempotent conjecture for torsion-free groups over every commutative unital characteristic-zero domain.","paperIds":["207-01","207-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/207.md","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","overviewTitle":"The $\\ell^1$-Bass and complex Bass trace conjectures","overviewSummary":"Proves the $\\ell^1$-Bass conjecture for every discrete group: Hattori--Stallings traces of idempotent matrices over $\\ell^1(G)$ are supported on finitely many finite-order conjugacy classes. The algebraic companion proves the integral Bass trace conjecture and Kaplansky's idempotent conjecture for torsion-free groups over every commutative unital characteristic-zero domain."},{"id":"208","title":"Finite symmetric tensor categories and the Verlinde tower","summary":"Proves that every finite symmetric tensor category over an algebraically closed field k of characteristic p > 0 admits a k-linear exact faithful strong symmetric monoidal fiber functor to a higher Verlinde category $\\mathrm{Ver}_{p^n}$. The level may depend on the category, and the theorem includes characteristic two, resolving the finite case of the Benson–Etingof–Ostrik conjecture.","paperIds":["208-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","overviewTitle":"The finite Benson--Etingof--Ostrik conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves that every finite symmetric tensor category over an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $p>0$ admits a $k$-linear exact faithful strong symmetric monoidal fiber functor to a higher Verlinde category $\\mathrm{Ver}_{p^n}$. The level may depend on the category, and the theorem includes characteristic two, resolving the finite case of the Benson--Etingof--Ostrik conjecture."},{"id":"209","title":"Integral counterexamples to Gersten’s conjecture","summary":"Disproves unrestricted integral Gersten injectivity in degrees 3 and 5. Two explicit two-dimensional ramified regular local rings of mixed characteristic $(0,5)$ have nonzero integral K-theory classes that vanish over their fraction fields.","paperIds":["209-01","209-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","overviewTitle":"Integral counterexamples to Gersten’s conjecture","overviewSummary":"Disproves unrestricted integral Gersten injectivity in degrees $3$ and $5$. Two explicit two-dimensional ramified regular local rings of mixed characteristic $(0,5)$ have nonzero integral $K$-theory classes that vanish over their fraction fields."},{"id":"210","title":"Foulkes' conjecture for sixth powers and quadratic stabilization","summary":"Proves the sixth case of Foulkes’ conjecture: $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^6(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^bV)$ embeds equivariantly in $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^b(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^6V)$ for every b ≥ 6 and finite-dimensional complex V. More generally, the canonical multiplication map $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^b(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^aV)\\to\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^a(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^bV)$ is surjective for a ≥ 2 and $b\\ge a(a-1)$, giving dimension-independent quadratic stabilization.","paperIds":["210-01","210-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/210.md","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","overviewTitle":"Foulkes’ conjecture for sixth powers and quadratic stabilization","overviewSummary":"Proves the sixth case of Foulkes’ conjecture: $\\operatorname{Sym}^6(\\operatorname{Sym}^bV)$ embeds equivariantly in $\\operatorname{Sym}^b(\\operatorname{Sym}^6V)$ for every $b\\ge6$ and finite-dimensional complex $V$. More generally, the canonical multiplication map $\\operatorname{Sym}^b(\\operatorname{Sym}^aV)\\to\\operatorname{Sym}^a(\\operatorname{Sym}^bV)$ is surjective for $a\\ge2$ and $b\\ge a(a-1)$, giving dimension-independent quadratic stabilization."},{"id":"211","title":"The geometric phase diagram, diffusion, and spectra of random planar maps","summary":"Critical Fortuin–Kasteleyn planar maps converge to Liouville quantum gravity spheres for $0\\lt q\\le4$ and to the Brownian continuum random tree for q > 4, establishing the surface-to-tree geometric transition. For FK–Ising and spanning-tree-weighted maps, stationary random walks converge to Liouville Brownian motion on the limiting sphere. The FK–Ising spectral result also gives convergence of eigenvalues and heat traces, using the stated Brownian/LQG inputs.","paperIds":["211-01","211-02","211-03","211-04","211-05","211-06","211-07"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/211.md","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"Geometry, diffusion, and spectra of random planar maps","overviewSummary":"Critical Fortuin--Kasteleyn planar maps converge to Liouville quantum gravity spheres for $0<q\\le4$ and to the Brownian continuum random tree for $q>4$, establishing the surface-to-tree geometric transition. For FK--Ising and spanning-tree-weighted maps, stationary random walks converge to Liouville Brownian motion on the limiting sphere. The FK--Ising spectral result also gives convergence of eigenvalues and heat traces, using the stated Brownian/LQG inputs."},{"id":"212","title":"Planar first-passage geometry and the absence of bigeodesics","summary":"Proves that planar first-passage percolation has no doubly infinite geodesic for iid nonnegative nonatomic edge weights when the minimum of four weights has finite second moment. For exponential weights, the limit shape is strictly convex with C1 boundary. Differentiability also holds for every Gamma law with positive shape and rate.","paperIds":["212-01","212-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/212.md","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"No bigeodesics and smooth limit shapes in planar first-passage percolation","overviewSummary":"Proves that planar first-passage percolation has no doubly infinite geodesic for iid nonnegative nonatomic edge weights when the minimum of four weights has finite second moment. For exponential weights, the limit shape is strictly convex with $C^1$ boundary. Differentiability also holds for every Gamma law with positive shape and rate."},{"id":"213","title":"Critical percolation on every quasi-transitive graph","summary":"Resolves the Benjamini–Schramm criticality conjecture for bond percolation on every infinite connected locally finite quasi-transitive graph with $p_c\\lt 1$: at the critical probability, there is almost surely no infinite cluster. The family also establishes this conclusion for both nearest-neighbor bond and site percolation on ℤ3.","paperIds":["213-01","213-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/213.md","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"No infinite critical clusters on quasi-transitive graphs","overviewSummary":"Resolves the Benjamini--Schramm criticality conjecture for bond percolation on every infinite connected locally finite quasi-transitive graph with $p_c<1$: at the critical probability, there is almost surely no infinite cluster. The family also establishes this conclusion for both nearest-neighbor bond and site percolation on $\\mathbb Z^3$."},{"id":"214","title":"The Benjamini–Schramm nonuniqueness conjecture","summary":"Proves $p_c\\lt p_u$ for Bernoulli bond percolation on every infinite connected locally finite nonamenable quasi-transitive graph, resolving the Benjamini–Schramm nonuniqueness conjecture. Thus there is a nonempty range of probabilities with infinitely many infinite clusters. A stronger operator bound also establishes the critical triangle condition.","paperIds":["214-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/214.md","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"The Benjamini--Schramm nonuniqueness conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves $p_c<p_u$ for Bernoulli bond percolation on every infinite connected locally finite nonamenable quasi-transitive graph, resolving the Benjamini--Schramm nonuniqueness conjecture. Thus there is a nonempty range of probabilities with infinitely many infinite clusters. A stronger operator bound also establishes the critical triangle condition."},{"id":"215","title":"Canonical $O(3)$ continuum limit and exact $O(4)$ mass asymptotics","summary":"Constructs the canonical continuum limit of the two-dimensional nearest-neighbor $O(3)$ model: a non-Gaussian local relativistic theory with a unique vacuum and a positive mass gap. For the square-lattice $O(4)$ model, determines the exact leading asymptotic of the full transfer gap, $m_{\\mathrm{lat}}(\\beta)\\sim32e^{\\pi/4-1/2}\\sqrt\\beta\\,e^{-\\pi\\beta}$. The family also proves exponential spin-correlation decay for two-dimensional nearest-neighbor $O(n)$ models with n ≥ 3 at every positive temperature.","paperIds":["215-01","215-02","215-03","215-04","215-05"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/215.md","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"Massive continuum limits and exact mass asymptotics for planar $O(n)$ models","overviewSummary":"Constructs the canonical continuum limit of the two-dimensional nearest-neighbor $O(3)$ model: a non-Gaussian local relativistic theory with a unique vacuum and a positive mass gap. For the square-lattice $O(4)$ model, determines the exact leading asymptotic of the full transfer gap, $m_{\\mathrm{lat}}(\\beta)\\sim32e^{\\pi/4-1/2}\\sqrt\\beta\\,e^{-\\pi\\beta}$. The family also proves exponential spin-correlation decay for two-dimensional nearest-neighbor $O(n)$ models with $n\\ge3$ at every positive temperature."},{"id":"216","title":"Critical and near-critical XY scaling and BKT universality","summary":"For the square-lattice nearest-neighbor cosine XY model, proves critical axis correlations $C_{\\beta_c}(r)\\sim Ar^{-1/4}(\\log r)^{1/8}$ and the Berezinskii–Kosterlitz–Thouless essential singularity $\\sqrt{\\beta_c-\\beta}\\log\\xi(\\beta)\\to B$, with $A,B\\gt 0$ after the free-box thermodynamic limit. For finite square-symmetric interactions containing nearest neighbors, discrete Gaussian heights converge to Gaussian fields throughout the rough phase, including its threshold, along geometric torus sizes. Critical center-magnetization and spin-field conclusions retain their stated height, renormalization, and field-input assumptions.","paperIds":["216-01","216-02","216-03","216-04","216-05","216-06"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"Critical logarithmic corrections and BKT scaling for the planar XY model","overviewSummary":"For the square-lattice nearest-neighbor cosine XY model, proves critical axis correlations $C_{\\beta_c}(r)\\sim Ar^{-1/4}(\\log r)^{1/8}$ and the Berezinskii--Kosterlitz--Thouless essential singularity $\\sqrt{\\beta_c-\\beta}\\log\\xi(\\beta)\\to B$, with $A,B>0$ after the free-box thermodynamic limit. For finite square-symmetric interactions containing nearest neighbors, discrete Gaussian heights converge to Gaussian fields throughout the rough phase, including its threshold, along geometric torus sizes. Critical center-magnetization and spin-field conclusions retain their stated height, renormalization, and field-input assumptions."},{"id":"217","title":"The low-temperature Sherrington–Kirkpatrick fluctuation law","summary":"For every fixed inverse temperature β > 1, determines the fluctuation scale and limiting law of the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington–Kirkpatrick log partition function. Its variance is asymptotic to $c_\\beta n^{1/3}$, with $c_\\beta\\gt 0$, confirming the predicted n1/6 standard-deviation scale. Exact centering and standardization give full-sequence convergence to a uniquely characterized nondegenerate law.","paperIds":["217-01","217-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"The low-temperature Sherrington--Kirkpatrick fluctuation law","overviewSummary":"For every fixed inverse temperature $\\beta>1$, determines the fluctuation scale and limiting law of the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington--Kirkpatrick log partition function. Its variance is asymptotic to $c_\\beta n^{1/3}$, with $c_\\beta>0$, confirming the predicted $n^{1/6}$ standard-deviation scale. Exact centering and standardization give full-sequence convergence to a uniquely characterized nondegenerate law."},{"id":"218","title":"Conformal universality for weakly interacting and random-bond Ising models","summary":"Weak finite-range square-symmetric even multispin perturbations of the square-lattice Ising model preserve critical bulk spin and energy limits; weak square-symmetric contour interactions also yield chordal SLE3 interface limits. With sufficiently weak iid bond disorder of any fixed bounded nondegenerate mean-zero law, critical spin interfaces converge to the same law in probability over environments.","paperIds":["218-01","218-02","218-03","218-04","218-05","218-06"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/218.md","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"Conformal universality for weakly interacting and disordered Ising models","overviewSummary":"Weak finite-range square-symmetric even multispin perturbations of the square-lattice Ising model preserve critical bulk spin and energy limits; weak square-symmetric contour interactions also yield chordal $\\mathrm{SLE}_3$ interface limits. With sufficiently weak iid bond disorder of any fixed bounded nondegenerate mean-zero law, critical spin interfaces converge to the same law in probability over environments."},{"id":"219","title":"GOE bulk universality for regular graphs with weak Anderson disorder","summary":"For every fixed degree d ≥ 3, the bulk adjacency-eigenvalue point process of a uniform simple random d-regular graph converges to the Gaussian orthogonal ensemble law, including for cubic graphs. The same fixed-energy universality persists under sufficiently weak fixed iid uniform diagonal disorder, throughout compact bands strictly inside the clean spectral edges.","paperIds":["219-01","219-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"GOE universality for random regular graphs with weak disorder","overviewSummary":"For every fixed degree $d\\ge3$, the bulk adjacency-eigenvalue point process of a uniform simple random $d$-regular graph converges to the Gaussian orthogonal ensemble law, including for cubic graphs. The same fixed-energy universality persists under sufficiently weak fixed iid uniform diagonal disorder, throughout compact bands strictly inside the clean spectral edges."},{"id":"220","title":"Directional zero–one laws beyond iid environments and iid ballisticity","summary":"On ℤd, d ≥ 3, directional escape has probability zero or one for iid strictly elliptic nearest-neighbor environments, and for stationary ergodic finite-range-dependent environments under uniform ellipticity. In iid uniformly elliptic environments with d ≥ 2, almost-sure directional transience implies a deterministic limiting velocity with positive projection in that direction, resolving the ballisticity conjecture.","paperIds":["220-01","220-02","220-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/220.md","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"Directional zero--one laws and ballisticity in random environments","overviewSummary":"On $\\mathbb Z^d$, $d\\ge3$, directional escape has probability zero or one for iid strictly elliptic nearest-neighbor environments, and for stationary ergodic finite-range-dependent environments under uniform ellipticity. In iid uniformly elliptic environments with $d\\ge2$, almost-sure directional transience implies a deterministic limiting velocity with positive projection in that direction, resolving the ballisticity conjecture."},{"id":"221","title":"The Mézard–Parisi formula for diluted spin glasses","summary":"Proves the Mézard–Parisi hierarchical cavity formula for Poisson-diluted even-arity Ising models satisfying the Panchenko–Talagrand factorization and positivity assumptions, with only first-moment integrability. The limiting free energy equals the infimum over finite-depth hierarchical trial laws. This includes the Viana–Bray model, symmetric diluted even-spin models, and weighted soft even-K satisfiability.","paperIds":["221-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/221.md","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"The M\\'ezard--Parisi formula for diluted spin glasses","overviewSummary":"Proves the M\\'ezard--Parisi hierarchical cavity formula for Poisson-diluted even-arity Ising models satisfying the Panchenko--Talagrand factorization and positivity assumptions, with only first-moment integrability. The limiting free energy equals the infimum over finite-depth hierarchical trial laws. This includes the Viana--Bray model, symmetric diluted even-spin models, and weighted soft even-$K$ satisfiability."},{"id":"222","title":"Perceptron free energies and microscopic jamming exponents","summary":"Determines finite-temperature variational free energies for Gaussian Ising perceptrons with bounded Borel log-potentials and Gaussian spherical perceptrons with bounded continuous potentials, at every positive pattern density. A spherical extension treats bi-orthogonally invariant disorder with compact limiting singular-value distributions and no outliers. At margin −1, the quadratic-penalty spherical model has a sharp feasibility threshold and limiting gap and force laws, with system size, zero temperature, and critical density taken in that order.","paperIds":["222-01","222-02","222-03","222-04"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/222.md","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"Perceptron free energies and microscopic jamming","overviewSummary":"Determines finite-temperature variational free energies for Gaussian Ising perceptrons with bounded Borel log-potentials and Gaussian spherical perceptrons with bounded continuous potentials, at every positive pattern density. A spherical extension treats bi-orthogonally invariant disorder with compact limiting singular-value distributions and no outliers. At margin $-1$, the quadratic-penalty spherical model has a sharp feasibility threshold and limiting gap and force laws, with system size, zero temperature, and critical density taken in that order."},{"id":"223","title":"Random-cluster interfaces: critical, disordered, thermal, and natural-time scaling","summary":"Proves chordal SLEκ limits for critical square-lattice random-cluster interfaces for $0\\lt q\\le4$, with $\\kappa=4\\pi/\\arccos(-\\sqrt q/2)$: bounded Jordan domains are allowed for q ≥ 1, and smooth Jordan domains for q < 1, under the stated marked-boundary approximations. For $1\\le q\\le4$, complete nested plane loops converge to CLEκ.","paperIds":["223-01","223-02","223-03","223-04","223-05","223-06"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"Conformal limits of square-lattice random-cluster interfaces","overviewSummary":"Proves chordal $\\mathrm{SLE}_\\kappa$ limits for critical square-lattice random-cluster interfaces for $0<q\\le4$, with $\\kappa=4\\pi/\\arccos(-\\sqrt q/2)$: bounded Jordan domains are allowed for $q\\ge1$, and smooth Jordan domains for $q<1$, under the stated marked-boundary approximations. For $1\\le q\\le4$, complete nested plane loops converge to $\\mathrm{CLE}_\\kappa$."},{"id":"224","title":"Critical and quenched near-critical universality for Poisson–Voronoi percolation","summary":"Proves Cardy's formula for annealed critical Poisson–Voronoi crossing probabilities in every bounded Jordan quadrilateral. With each model normalized by its own expected unit-square pivotal count, the conditional joint near-critical crossing-threshold laws for rational polygonal quads converge in environment probability to the triangular-lattice reference law. This establishes quenched near-critical universality for crossing thresholds.","paperIds":["224-01","224-02","224-03"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"Critical and near-critical universality for Voronoi percolation","overviewSummary":"Proves Cardy's formula for annealed critical Poisson--Voronoi crossing probabilities in every bounded Jordan quadrilateral. With each model normalized by its own expected unit-square pivotal count, the conditional joint near-critical crossing-threshold laws for rational polygonal quads converge in environment probability to the triangular-lattice reference law. This establishes quenched near-critical universality for crossing thresholds."},{"id":"225","title":"Gaussian free field limits throughout the balanced six-vertex regime","summary":"The balanced square-lattice six-vertex height field with $a=b=1$ and $0\\lt c\\le2$ converges to a Gaussian free field, including at the endpoint c = 2. The plane state is defined by balanced-torus limits. For unit height increments and Green kernel $-(2\\pi)^{-1}\\log|x-y|$, the exact variance multiplier is $1/\\arcsin(c/2)$.","paperIds":["225-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"Gaussian free field limits for the balanced six-vertex model","overviewSummary":"The balanced square-lattice six-vertex height field with $a=b=1$ and $0<c\\le2$ converges to a Gaussian free field, including at the endpoint $c=2$. The plane state is defined by balanced-torus limits. For unit height increments and Green kernel $-(2\\pi)^{-1}\\log|x-y|$, the exact variance multiplier is $1/\\arcsin(c/2)$."},{"id":"226","title":"The double-dimer loop ensemble converges to CLE4","summary":"Resolves the half-plane Temperleyan form of the double-dimer scaling-limit conjecture: the complete loop ensemble formed by two independent dimer coverings of the Temperleyan square lattice converges to nested CLE4. Convergence matches every macroscopic loop as an unparametrized curve, upgrading convergence of loop observables to convergence of the loops themselves.","paperIds":["226-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"The double-dimer $\\mathrm{CLE}_4$ conjecture in the half-plane","overviewSummary":"Resolves the half-plane Temperleyan form of the double-dimer scaling-limit conjecture: the complete loop ensemble formed by two independent dimer coverings of the Temperleyan square lattice converges to nested $\\mathrm{CLE}_4$. Convergence matches every macroscopic loop as an unparametrized curve, upgrading convergence of loop observables to convergence of the loops themselves."},{"id":"227","title":"Critical SK autocorrelation processes and dynamics across the temperature transition","summary":"For zero-field Gaussian SK heat-bath dynamics with rate-one updates per spin, proves worst-start cutoff on the $\\log n$ scale for fixed $0\\le\\beta\\lt 1$, mixing time $n^{2/3+o(1)}$ at β = 1, and stretched-exponential mixing from a Gibbs-sampled fixed starting configuration for β > 1, in probability over disorder. At criticality, rescaled stationary and quench autocorrelation processes have universal random limits for Gaussian and Rademacher disorder; the quench limit relaxes to the stationary limit.","paperIds":["227-01","227-02","227-03","227-04","227-05","227-06","227-07","227-08"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/227.md","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"The dynamical phase transition in the Sherrington--Kirkpatrick model","overviewSummary":"For zero-field Gaussian SK heat-bath dynamics with rate-one updates per spin, proves worst-start cutoff on the $\\log n$ scale for fixed $0\\le\\beta<1$, mixing time $n^{2/3+o(1)}$ at $\\beta=1$, and stretched-exponential mixing from a Gibbs-sampled fixed starting configuration for $\\beta>1$, in probability over disorder. At criticality, rescaled stationary and quench autocorrelation processes have universal random limits for Gaussian and Rademacher disorder; the quench limit relaxes to the stationary limit."},{"id":"228","title":"Continuum phase transitions for radial pair potentials","summary":"Constructs stable distance-dependent pair interactions for three-dimensional classical particles with a first-order phase transition: the canonical free energy has a derivative jump at one inverse temperature throughout an open density interval. One potential has a divergent repulsive core; another is bounded and continuous with an integrable power-law tail, realizing the type of transition sought in Simon's continuum problem.","paperIds":["228-01","228-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/228.md","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"Continuum phase transitions for radial pair potentials","overviewSummary":"Constructs stable distance-dependent pair interactions for three-dimensional classical particles with a first-order phase transition: the canonical free energy has a derivative jump at one inverse temperature throughout an open density interval. One potential has a divergent repulsive core; another is bounded and continuous with an integrable power-law tail, realizing the type of transition sought in Simon's continuum problem."},{"id":"229","title":"Exact three- and four-state reconstruction thresholds and four-state tree capacity","summary":"Proves the exact reconstruction threshold $d\\lambda^2\\gt 1$, with nonreconstruction at equality, for three-state symmetric and four-state ferromagnetic broadcasting on regular trees (d ≥ 2) and observed Poisson trees (mean d > 1 and d > 0, respectively), with Poisson advantage averaged without conditioning on survival. The three-state theorem allows both signs of λ and gives the exact weak-recovery threshold for the symmetric three-community stochastic block model.","paperIds":["229-01","229-02","229-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/229.md","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"Sharp three- and four-state reconstruction thresholds","overviewSummary":"Proves the exact reconstruction threshold $d\\lambda^2>1$, with nonreconstruction at equality, for three-state symmetric and four-state ferromagnetic broadcasting on regular trees ($d\\ge2$) and observed Poisson trees (mean $d>1$ and $d>0$, respectively), with Poisson advantage averaged without conditioning on survival. The three-state theorem allows both signs of $\\lambda$ and gives the exact weak-recovery threshold for the symmetric three-community stochastic block model."},{"id":"230","title":"Exact Hausdorff gauges for SLE","summary":"Resolves Schramm’s Hausdorff-measure question for chordal SLEκ, $0\\lt \\kappa\\lt 8$. The explicit gauge $r^d(\\log\\log(1/r))^{(2-d)/2}$, $d=1+\\kappa/8$, gives almost surely positive finite measure to every trace segment $\\gamma([s,t])$ with $0\\lt s\\lt t\\lt \\infty$, and finite expected measure to the trace in every bounded disk.","paperIds":["230-01","230-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/230.md","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"Exact Hausdorff measure for SLE","overviewSummary":"Resolves Schramm’s Hausdorff-measure question for chordal $\\mathrm{SLE}_\\kappa$, $0<\\kappa<8$. The explicit gauge $r^d(\\log\\log(1/r))^{(2-d)/2}$, $d=1+\\kappa/8$, gives almost surely positive finite measure to every trace segment $\\gamma([s,t])$ with $0<s<t<\\infty$, and finite expected measure to the trace in every bounded disk."},{"id":"231","title":"The free uniform spanning forest is a factor of IID","summary":"On every infinite connected locally finite simple unweighted graph, the free uniform spanning forest is a factor of independent vertex labels, by one isomorphism-equivariant rule using no root. Translation-invariant strongly Rayleigh binary processes on every countable group, including invariant determinantal processes with Hermitian positive-contraction kernels, are also factors of IID.","paperIds":["231-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/231.md","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"The free uniform spanning forest is a factor of IID","overviewSummary":"On every infinite connected locally finite simple unweighted graph, the free uniform spanning forest is a factor of independent vertex labels, by one isomorphism-equivariant rule using no root. Translation-invariant strongly Rayleigh binary processes on every countable group, including invariant determinantal processes with Hermitian positive-contraction kernels, are also factors of IID."},{"id":"232","title":"Gaussian fields and interfaces for triangular-lattice Lipschitz heights","summary":"Proves Gaussian free field limits on bounded smooth simply connected domains for triangular-lattice height models: uniform odd heights with increments $0,\\pm2$ and two-arc boundary values $\\pm1$, and zero-boundary integer Lipschitz heights weighted by fixed $x\\in[1/\\sqrt2,1]$. Uniform real Lipschitz heights also converge to a Gaussian field; at a tuned opposite-boundary amplitude, their interface converges to chordal SLE4, establishing Schramm’s real-field/interface predictions.","paperIds":["232-01","232-02","232-03"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"Gaussian fields and SLE interfaces for Lipschitz heights","overviewSummary":"Proves Gaussian free field limits on bounded smooth simply connected domains for triangular-lattice height models: uniform odd heights with increments $0,\\pm2$ and two-arc boundary values $\\pm1$, and zero-boundary integer Lipschitz heights weighted by fixed $x\\in[1/\\sqrt2,1]$. Uniform real Lipschitz heights also converge to a Gaussian field; at a tuned opposite-boundary amplitude, their interface converges to chordal $\\mathrm{SLE}_4$, establishing Schramm’s real-field/interface predictions."},{"id":"233","title":"The joint critical Ashkin–Teller current limit","summary":"Identifies the joint scaling limit of Ashkin–Teller heights and both complete current-cluster collections throughout the critical line, including the four-state Potts endpoint. In bounded Jordan domains with admissible lattice approximations and wired primal/free dual boundaries, the height converges to the predicted Gaussian free field, and the clusters to canonical recursive sets of that same field, retaining every nesting depth.","paperIds":["233-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"The joint critical Ashkin--Teller scaling limit","overviewSummary":"Identifies the joint scaling limit of Ashkin--Teller heights and both complete current-cluster collections throughout the critical line, including the four-state Potts endpoint. In bounded Jordan domains with admissible lattice approximations and wired primal/free dual boundaries, the height converges to the predicted Gaussian free field, and the clusters to canonical recursive sets of that same field, retaining every nesting depth."},{"id":"234","title":"All-temperature pressure of orthogonally invariant Ising spin glasses","summary":"Gives an exact variational formula for the limiting pressure of orthogonally invariant Ising spin glasses at every fixed temperature, both almost surely and in expectation. The coupling matrix is a Haar-random rotation of a deterministic spectrum converging to a compactly supported law, with extreme eigenvalues converging to its support edges. The zero-field ground-state energy follows as temperature tends to zero.","paperIds":["234-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/234.md","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"All-temperature pressure for orthogonally invariant Ising spin glasses","overviewSummary":"Gives an exact variational formula for the limiting pressure of orthogonally invariant Ising spin glasses at every fixed temperature, both almost surely and in expectation. The coupling matrix is a Haar-random rotation of a deterministic spectrum converging to a compactly supported law, with extreme eigenvalues converging to its support edges. The zero-field ground-state energy follows as temperature tends to zero."},{"id":"235","title":"Limiting random SAT thresholds, sharp variance and computability","summary":"For random k-SAT with independent uniformly signed proper clauses sampled with replacement, proves finite positive limiting thresholds and hitting-time variance $\\Theta_k(n)$ for every fixed k ≥ 3, and computability of the 3-SAT threshold. We credit Gaia Carenini with priority for resolving the threshold-existence conjecture in her concurrent [ECCC TR26-229](https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2026/229/), made public October 5, 2026; this family supplies another proof and the sharper variance and computability results.","paperIds":["235-01","235-02","235-03","235-04"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/235.md","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"Random-SAT thresholds, sharp variance and computability","overviewSummary":"For random $k$-SAT with independent uniformly signed proper clauses sampled with replacement, proves finite positive limiting thresholds and hitting-time variance $\\Theta_k(n)$ for every fixed $k\\ge3$, and computability of the 3-SAT threshold. We credit Gaia Carenini with priority for resolving the threshold-existence conjecture in her concurrent \\href{https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2026/229/}{ECCC TR26-229}, made public October 5, 2026; this family supplies another proof and the sharper variance and computability results."},{"id":"236","title":"The exact factor-of-IID threshold for free Ising spins on trees","summary":"Determines when the free zero-field ferromagnetic Ising state on the infinite d-regular tree is a factor of independent vertex labels: exactly when $\\tanh\\beta\\le(d-1)^{-1/2}$, including equality, for d ≥ 3 and β ≥ 0. The construction uses no root and is almost surely equivariant for each fixed tree automorphism, resolving the ferromagnetic case of Lyons's question.","paperIds":["236-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/236.md","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"The factor-of-IID threshold for free Ising states on trees","overviewSummary":"Determines when the free zero-field ferromagnetic Ising state on the infinite $d$-regular tree is a factor of independent vertex labels: exactly when $\\tanh\\beta\\le(d-1)^{-1/2}$, including equality, for $d\\ge3$ and $\\beta\\ge0$. The construction uses no root and is almost surely equivariant for each fixed tree automorphism, resolving the ferromagnetic case of Lyons's question."},{"id":"237","title":"The three-quarter exponent for honeycomb self-avoiding walk","summary":"Proves the diameter form of Nienhuis's predicted three-quarter exponent: a uniformly chosen n-step self-avoiding walk on the honeycomb lattice has diameter $n^{3/4+o(1)}$. Its local mass and covering numbers have exponent 4/3. These estimates hold at every sufficiently large fixed length, simultaneously across scales, with arbitrarily high polynomial probability.","paperIds":["237-01","237-02","237-03","237-04","237-05","237-06","237-07","237-08","237-09","237-10","237-11","237-12","237-13"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/237.md","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"The three-quarter diameter exponent for honeycomb walks","overviewSummary":"Proves the diameter form of Nienhuis's predicted three-quarter exponent: a uniformly chosen $n$-step self-avoiding walk on the honeycomb lattice has diameter $n^{3/4+o(1)}$. Its local mass and covering numbers have exponent $4/3$. These estimates hold at every sufficiently large fixed length, simultaneously across scales, with arbitrarily high polynomial probability."},{"id":"238","title":"Optimal logarithmic mixing of the Thorp shuffle","summary":"Proves that the Thorp shuffle randomizes $N=2^d$ labeled cards in $\\Theta(\\log N)$ physical shuffles, settling its optimal mixing order for power-of-two deck sizes. Convergence is in total variation from the worst initial ordering and concerns the entire permutation, not just individual card positions.","paperIds":["238-01","238-02","238-03","238-04","238-05","238-06","238-07","238-08","238-09","238-10","238-11","238-12"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"Optimal logarithmic mixing of the Thorp shuffle","overviewSummary":"Proves that the Thorp shuffle randomizes $N=2^d$ labeled cards in $\\Theta(\\log N)$ physical shuffles, settling its optimal mixing order for power-of-two deck sizes. Convergence is in total variation from the worst initial ordering and concerns the entire permutation, not just individual card positions."},{"id":"239","title":"Sharp singularity rates for symmetric random sign matrices","summary":"Determines the sharp exponential singularity rate of symmetric random sign matrices with independent entries on and above the diagonal. Uniform signs give $\\Pr(\\det A_n=0)=(1/2+o(1))^n$; for fixed bias $p\\in(0,1)\\setminus\\{1/2\\}$, the rate is $(p^2+(1-p)^2+o(1))^n$. In the biased case, agreeing rows attain this rate.","paperIds":["239-01","239-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","overviewTitle":"Sharp singularity rates for symmetric sign matrices","overviewSummary":"Determines the sharp exponential singularity rate of symmetric random sign matrices with independent entries on and above the diagonal. Uniform signs give $\\Pr(\\det A_n=0)=(1/2+o(1))^n$; for fixed bias $p\\in(0,1)\\setminus\\{1/2\\}$, the rate is $(p^2+(1-p)^2+o(1))^n$. In the biased case, agreeing rows attain this rate."},{"id":"240","title":"Shelah's eventual categoricity and the prescribed-threshold obstruction","summary":"Proves Shelah's eventual categoricity conjecture in ZFC: for each bound on the Löwenheim–Skolem number, a uniform threshold makes categoricity of an abstract elementary class in one cardinal above that threshold imply categoricity throughout the same tail. Categoricity means uniqueness up to isomorphism at a given cardinality. Under the continuum hypothesis, a proposed specific Hanf threshold need not suffice.","paperIds":["240-01","240-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/240.md","subjectId":"6","subject":"Mathematical logic","subjectZh":"數理邏輯","overviewTitle":"Shelah's eventual categoricity conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves Shelah's eventual categoricity conjecture in ZFC: for each bound on the L\\\"owenheim--Skolem number, a uniform threshold makes categoricity of an abstract elementary class in one cardinal above that threshold imply categoricity throughout the same tail. Categoricity means uniqueness up to isomorphism at a given cardinality. Under the continuum hypothesis, a proposed specific Hanf threshold need not suffice."},{"id":"241","title":"Rigidity of the Turing degrees","summary":"Every order automorphism of the Turing degrees is the identity, resolving their rigidity problem. Thus no nontrivial relabeling of degrees preserves the ordering by relative computability.","paperIds":["241-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/241.md","subjectId":"6","subject":"Mathematical logic","subjectZh":"數理邏輯","overviewTitle":"Rigidity of the Turing degrees","overviewSummary":"Every order automorphism of the Turing degrees is the identity, resolving their rigidity problem. Thus no nontrivial relabeling of degrees preserves the ordering by relative computability."},{"id":"242","title":"Single-fold Diophantine representations and undecidability under an at-most-one-solution promise","summary":"Every recursively enumerable set of tuples of natural numbers has a Diophantine representation with exactly one auxiliary solution for each member and none for nonmembers. This proves the single-fold conjecture and hence the finite-fold conjecture. Diophantine solvability over the nonnegative integers remains undecidable even with an at-most-one-solution promise.","paperIds":["242-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/242.md","subjectId":"6","subject":"Mathematical logic","subjectZh":"數理邏輯","overviewTitle":"Single-fold Diophantine representations","overviewSummary":"Every recursively enumerable set of tuples of natural numbers has a Diophantine representation with exactly one auxiliary solution for each member and none for nonmembers. This proves the single-fold conjecture and hence the finite-fold conjecture. Diophantine solvability over the nonnegative integers remains undecidable even with an at-most-one-solution promise."},{"id":"243","title":"Separating choiceless counting from polynomial time and witnessed choice","summary":"Confirms the Blass–Gurevich–Shelah noncapture conjecture: consistency of a linear system over 𝔽3 defines a polynomial-time query on unordered finite structures that choiceless polynomial time with counting cannot express. A separate result shows that adding witnessed symmetric choice strictly increases expressive power. Both separations hold for the full counting formalism, allowing hereditarily finite sets of arbitrary finite rank.","paperIds":["243-01","243-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/243.md","subjectId":"6","subject":"Mathematical logic","subjectZh":"數理邏輯","overviewTitle":"Choiceless counting does not capture polynomial time","overviewSummary":"Confirms the Blass--Gurevich--Shelah noncapture conjecture: consistency of a linear system over $\\mathbb F_3$ defines a polynomial-time query on unordered finite structures that choiceless polynomial time with counting cannot express. A separate result shows that adding witnessed symmetric choice strictly increases expressive power. Both separations hold for the full counting formalism, allowing hereditarily finite sets of arbitrary finite rank."},{"id":"244","title":"The Partition Principle does not imply Choice","summary":"Assuming ZF is consistent, constructs a model in which every surjective image of a set injects into that set, yet the axiom of choice fails. Choice for ordinal-indexed families still holds. From any countable transitive model of ZFC, a separate construction gives a transitive symmetric extension with these properties and no new countable sequences of ground-model elements.","paperIds":["244-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/244.md","subjectId":"6","subject":"Mathematical logic","subjectZh":"數理邏輯","overviewTitle":"The partition principle does not imply choice","overviewSummary":"Assuming ZF is consistent, constructs a model in which every surjective image of a set injects into that set, yet the axiom of choice fails. Choice for ordinal-indexed families still holds. From any countable transitive model of ZFC, a separate construction gives a transitive symmetric extension with these properties and no new countable sequences of ground-model elements."},{"id":"245","title":"Weak normalization implies strong normalization in pure type systems","summary":"Proves that weak normalization implies strong normalization for every pure type system: if every legal expression in every valid context has a β-normal form, every β-reduction sequence terminates. This resolves the β-Barendregt–Geuvers–Klop conjecture, including nonfunctional rules and open contexts.","paperIds":["245-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/245.md","subjectId":"6","subject":"Mathematical logic","subjectZh":"數理邏輯","overviewTitle":"The $\\beta$-Barendregt--Geuvers--Klop conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves that weak normalization implies strong normalization for every pure type system: if every legal expression in every valid context has a $\\beta$-normal form, every $\\beta$-reduction sequence terminates. This resolves the $\\beta$-Barendregt--Geuvers--Klop conjecture, including nonfunctional rules and open contexts."},{"id":"246","title":"Cannon's conjecture","summary":"Every word-hyperbolic group with boundary homeomorphic to S2 admits a proper cocompact isometric action on hyperbolic three-space with finite kernel, proving Cannon's conjecture. Every torsion-free such group is therefore the fundamental group of a closed hyperbolic three-manifold.","paperIds":["246-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/246.md","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","overviewTitle":"Cannon's conjecture","overviewSummary":"Every word-hyperbolic group with boundary homeomorphic to $S^2$ admits a proper cocompact isometric action on hyperbolic three-space with finite kernel, proving Cannon's conjecture. Every torsion-free such group is therefore the fundamental group of a closed hyperbolic three-manifold."},{"id":"247","title":"An infinite finitely presented residually finite 2-group and a finitely presented nil algebra","summary":"Constructs an infinite finitely presented residually finite group whose elements all have finite 2-power order, answering the finitely presented Burnside problem negatively even in this class. The construction also yields an infinite-dimensional finitely presented nil associative 𝔽2-algebra and a finitely presented infinite-dimensional algebraic unitization, giving negative answers to the corresponding nilpotence and Kurosh finiteness questions.","paperIds":["247-01","247-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/247.md","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","overviewTitle":"An infinite finitely presented residually finite $2$-group","overviewSummary":"Constructs an infinite finitely presented residually finite group whose elements all have finite $2$-power order, answering the finitely presented Burnside problem negatively even in this class. The construction also yields an infinite-dimensional finitely presented nil associative $\\mathbb F_2$-algebra and a finitely presented infinite-dimensional algebraic unitization, giving negative answers to the corresponding nilpotence and Kurosh finiteness questions."},{"id":"248","title":"Thompson's group F is nonamenable","summary":"Proves that Thompson's group F, the group of dyadic piecewise linear homeomorphisms of the interval, is nonamenable, resolving its longstanding amenability problem.","paperIds":["248-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/248.md","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","overviewTitle":"Thompson's group $F$ is nonamenable","overviewSummary":"Proves that Thompson's group $F$, the group of dyadic piecewise linear homeomorphisms of the interval, is nonamenable, resolving its longstanding amenability problem."},{"id":"249","title":"A finitely generated Eilenberg–Ganea counterexample","summary":"Constructs a finitely generated residually finite group with integral cohomological dimension two and geometric dimension three, disproving the Eilenberg–Ganea conjecture. It has no two-dimensional classifying space, even with infinitely many cells.","paperIds":["249-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/249.md","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","overviewTitle":"A finitely generated counterexample to Eilenberg--Ganea","overviewSummary":"Constructs a finitely generated residually finite group with integral cohomological dimension two and geometric dimension three, disproving the Eilenberg--Ganea conjecture. It has no two-dimensional classifying space, even with infinitely many cells."},{"id":"250","title":"Boone–Higman embeddings with higher finiteness","summary":"A finitely generated group has decidable word problem exactly when it embeds in a finitely presented simple group, proving the Boone–Higman conjecture. The target can have type F∞: a classifying space with finitely many cells in each dimension. A single group of type F∞ can also contain every finitely presented group.","paperIds":["250-01","250-02","250-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/250.md","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","overviewTitle":"The Boone--Higman conjecture and higher finiteness","overviewSummary":"A finitely generated group has decidable word problem exactly when it embeds in a finitely presented simple group, proving the Boone--Higman conjecture. The target can have type $F_\\infty$: a classifying space with finitely many cells in each dimension. A single group of type $F_\\infty$ can also contain every finitely presented group."},{"id":"251","title":"Amenability, unitarizability, and strong Ulam stability","summary":"Resolves Dixmier's problem for all discrete groups: amenability is equivalent to every uniformly bounded Hilbert-space representation being similar to a unitary representation. For countable discrete groups, amenability is also equivalent to strong Ulam stability: sufficiently accurate unitary approximate representations are uniformly close in operator norm to genuine representations on the same, possibly infinite-dimensional, Hilbert space.","paperIds":["251-01","251-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/251.md","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","overviewTitle":"Amenability, unitarizability, and Ulam stability","overviewSummary":"Resolves Dixmier's problem for all discrete groups: amenability is equivalent to every uniformly bounded Hilbert-space representation being similar to a unitary representation. For countable discrete groups, amenability is also equivalent to strong Ulam stability: sufficiently accurate unitary approximate representations are uniformly close in operator norm to genuine representations on the same, possibly infinite-dimensional, Hilbert space."},{"id":"252","title":"A torsion-free hyperbolic group that is neither residually finite nor linear over any field","summary":"Constructs a torsion-free word-hyperbolic group that is not residually finite, answering the residual-finiteness question negatively. One fixed nonidentity element is killed by every finite-dimensional linear representation over every commutative field, so the group is not linear over any such field.","paperIds":["252-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/252.md","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","overviewTitle":"A non-residually-finite torsion-free hyperbolic group","overviewSummary":"Constructs a torsion-free word-hyperbolic group that is not residually finite, answering the residual-finiteness question negatively. One fixed nonidentity element is killed by every finite-dimensional linear representation over every commutative field, so the group is not linear over any such field."},{"id":"253","title":"An infinite finitely presented simple amenable group","summary":"Constructs an infinite finitely presented simple amenable group, answering the longstanding question of whether these properties can occur simultaneously.","paperIds":["253-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/253.md","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","overviewTitle":"An infinite finitely presented simple amenable group","overviewSummary":"Constructs an infinite finitely presented simple amenable group, answering the longstanding question of whether these properties can occur simultaneously."},{"id":"254","title":"Classifying spaces and geometric obstructions for Artin groups","summary":"The Salvetti complex of every finite-rank Artin group is aspherical, proving the Artin $K(\\pi,1)$ conjecture. Arbitrary intersections of its parabolic subgroups are parabolic, proving the Parabolic Intersection Conjecture. An explicit Artin group admits no proper cocompact isometric action on any nonempty proper CAT$(0)$ space.","paperIds":["254-01","254-02","254-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/254.md","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","overviewTitle":"Classifying spaces and geometric obstructions for Artin groups","overviewSummary":"The Salvetti complex of every finite-rank Artin group is aspherical, proving the Artin $K(\\pi,1)$ conjecture. Arbitrary intersections of its parabolic subgroups are parabolic, proving the Parabolic Intersection Conjecture. An explicit Artin group admits no proper cocompact isometric action on any nonempty proper CAT$(0)$ space."},{"id":"255","title":"Quasi-isometric recognition of virtually polycyclic groups","summary":"Proves that every finitely generated group quasi-isometric to a finitely generated virtually polycyclic group is virtually polycyclic, resolving the Eskin–Fisher–Whyte lattice-recognition conjecture. Equivalently, a group quasi-isometric to a lattice in a connected simply connected solvable Lie group is virtually a uniform lattice in some such Lie group, possibly a different one.","paperIds":["255-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/255.md","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","overviewTitle":"Quasi-isometric rigidity of virtually polycyclic groups","overviewSummary":"Proves that every finitely generated group quasi-isometric to a finitely generated virtually polycyclic group is virtually polycyclic, resolving the Eskin--Fisher--Whyte lattice-recognition conjecture. Equivalently, a group quasi-isometric to a lattice in a connected simply connected solvable Lie group is virtually a uniform lattice in some such Lie group, possibly a different one."},{"id":"256","title":"Nonsingular systems of equations over arbitrary groups","summary":"Proves that every finite system of equations over an arbitrary group whose exponent-sum matrix has full row rank over ℚ has a simultaneous solution in an overgroup, resolving Howie's conjecture. The coefficient group embeds in the presented quotient. A companion proves Kervaire's conjecture: adjoining one generator and one relation cannot trivialize a nontrivial group.","paperIds":["256-01","256-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/256.md","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","overviewTitle":"Howie's conjecture on equations over groups","overviewSummary":"Proves that every finite system of equations over an arbitrary group whose exponent-sum matrix has full row rank over $\\mathbb Q$ has a simultaneous solution in an overgroup, resolving Howie's conjecture. The coefficient group embeds in the presented quotient. A companion proves Kervaire's conjecture: adjoining one generator and one relation cannot trivialize a nontrivial group."},{"id":"257","title":"A hyperbolic group without a geometric CAT(0) action","summary":"Answers negatively whether every word-hyperbolic group is a CAT(0) group. Constructs one with a finite classifying space but no proper cocompact isometric action on any proper complete $\\mathop{\\mathrm{CAT}}\\nolimits (0)$ space, in any dimension.","paperIds":["257-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/257.md","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","overviewTitle":"A hyperbolic group with no geometric CAT(0) action","overviewSummary":"Answers negatively whether every word-hyperbolic group is a CAT(0) group. Constructs one with a finite classifying space but no proper cocompact isometric action on any proper complete $\\operatorname{CAT}(0)$ space, in any dimension."},{"id":"258","title":"Gersten’s conjecture and virtual compact specialness of one-relator groups","summary":"Proves Gersten's conjecture: every finitely generated one-relator group containing no Baumslag–Solitar subgroup $\\mathrm{BS}(m,n)$, with $m,n\\ne0$, is word-hyperbolic. It also proves that every word-hyperbolic one-relator group is virtually compact special.","paperIds":["258-01","258-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","overviewTitle":"Gersten's conjecture for one-relator groups","overviewSummary":"Proves Gersten's conjecture: every finitely generated one-relator group containing no Baumslag--Solitar subgroup $\\mathrm{BS}(m,n)$, with $m,n\\ne0$, is word-hyperbolic. It also proves that every word-hyperbolic one-relator group is virtually compact special."},{"id":"259","title":"A group without fixed price","summary":"Constructs a finitely generated group with two essentially free probability-measure-preserving actions of different costs, answering the general fixed-price problem negatively. Its Bernoulli action has cost bounded away from one, while a sequence of finite height extensions has costs tending to one.","paperIds":["259-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","overviewTitle":"A group without fixed price","overviewSummary":"Constructs a finitely generated group with two essentially free probability-measure-preserving actions of different costs, answering the general fixed-price problem negatively. Its Bernoulli action has cost bounded away from one, while a sequence of finite height extensions has costs tending to one."},{"id":"260","title":"Spacetime Penrose inequalities: enclosing area, charge, rotation, and anti-de Sitter extensions","summary":"Proves the sharp enclosing-area spacetime Penrose inequality for smooth one-ended asymptotically flat initial data in every spatial dimension n ≥ 3, under dominant energy, weak future trapping, positive enclosing area, and the stated decay assumptions. It bounds invariant ADM mass below using minimum enclosing area, with equality rigidity under additional horizon hypotheses. Charged upper-area bounds treat dyonic three-dimensional data; the higher-dimensional purely electric extension uses the matched neutral theorem.","paperIds":["260-01","260-02","260-03","260-04","260-05","260-06","260-07","260-08","260-09","260-10","260-11","260-12","260-13"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/260.md","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"Spacetime Penrose inequalities and rigidity","overviewSummary":"Proves the sharp enclosing-area spacetime Penrose inequality for smooth one-ended asymptotically flat initial data in every spatial dimension $n\\ge3$, under dominant energy, weak future trapping, positive enclosing area, and the stated decay assumptions. It bounds invariant ADM mass below using minimum enclosing area, with equality rigidity under additional horizon hypotheses. Charged upper-area bounds treat dyonic three-dimensional data; the higher-dimensional purely electric extension uses the matched neutral theorem."},{"id":"261","title":"Localization and delocalization in the Anderson model","summary":"Resolves the predicted spectral contrast for the lattice Anderson model with independent uniform site potentials. In dimension two, every positive disorder strength gives almost surely pure-point spectrum. In every fixed dimension d ≥ 3, sufficiently weak positive disorder gives purely absolutely continuous spectrum on a fixed open interval with nonzero spectral weight.","paperIds":["261-01","261-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/261.md","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"Localization and delocalization in the Anderson model","overviewSummary":"Resolves the predicted spectral contrast for the lattice Anderson model with independent uniform site potentials. In dimension two, every positive disorder strength gives almost surely pure-point spectrum. In every fixed dimension $d\\ge3$, sufficiently weak positive disorder gives purely absolutely continuous spectrum on a fixed open interval with nonzero spectral weight."},{"id":"262","title":"Sharp finite-matrix Lieb–Thirring inequalities and all equality cases","summary":"Proves the sharp one-dimensional Lieb–Thirring inequality for $1/2\\lt \\gamma\\lt 3/2$ and arbitrary finite-matrix potentials W ≥ 0 with $\\int\\mathop{\\mathrm{tr}}\\nolimits (W^{\\gamma+1/2})\\lt \\infty$: the optimal constant is the scalar one-bound-state value, independent of matrix size. All equality cases are direct sums, in one constant unitary basis, of scalar sech2 solitons with independent scales and centers, and zero channels.","paperIds":["262-01","262-02","262-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/262.md","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"Sharp one-dimensional Lieb--Thirring inequalities","overviewSummary":"Proves the sharp one-dimensional Lieb--Thirring inequality for $1/2<\\gamma<3/2$ and arbitrary finite-matrix potentials $W\\ge0$ with $\\int\\operatorname{tr}(W^{\\gamma+1/2})<\\infty$: the optimal constant is the scalar one-bound-state value, independent of matrix size. All equality cases are direct sums, in one constant unitary basis, of scalar $\\operatorname{sech}^2$ solitons with independent scales and centers, and zero channels."},{"id":"263","title":"The ionization and generalized ionization conjectures","summary":"For the full nonrelativistic Coulomb model with two electron spin states, proves that a molecule with M fixed nuclei of charges at least one and total charge Z strictly binds at most $Z+CM$ electrons. Neutral-atom first ionization energies and radii containing all but an expected half-electron have universal positive upper and lower bounds. The energy cost of removing m electrons has Thomas–Fermi asymptotics as $m\\to\\infty$ and $Z/m\\to\\infty$; neutral-atom outer radii have the corresponding iterated-limit asymptotics, taking $Z\\to\\infty$ first.","paperIds":["263-01","263-02","263-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/263.md","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"The ionization and generalized ionization conjectures","overviewSummary":"For the full nonrelativistic Coulomb model with two electron spin states, proves that a molecule with $M$ fixed nuclei of charges at least one and total charge $Z$ strictly binds at most $Z+CM$ electrons. Neutral-atom first ionization energies and radii containing all but an expected half-electron have universal positive upper and lower bounds. The energy cost of removing $m$ electrons has Thomas--Fermi asymptotics as $m\\to\\infty$ and $Z/m\\to\\infty$; neutral-atom outer radii have the corresponding iterated-limit asymptotics, taking $Z\\to\\infty$ first."},{"id":"264","title":"Strong cosmic censorship near two-ended Kerr data","summary":"Proves local strong cosmic censorship near each fixed rotating subextremal Kerr bridge. A dense Gδ subset of a weighted smooth neighborhood of smooth complete two-ended asymptotically flat vacuum data has full maximal globally hyperbolic developments with no future continuous nondegenerate extension whose weak connection is locally square-integrable. No symmetry is imposed; extensions need not satisfy the vacuum equations.","paperIds":["264-01","264-02","264-03"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"Strong cosmic censorship near two-ended Kerr data","overviewSummary":"Proves local strong cosmic censorship near each fixed rotating subextremal Kerr bridge. A dense $G_\\delta$ subset of a weighted smooth neighborhood of smooth complete two-ended asymptotically flat vacuum data has full maximal globally hyperbolic developments with no future continuous nondegenerate extension whose weak connection is locally square-integrable. No symmetry is imposed; extensions need not satisfy the vacuum equations."},{"id":"265","title":"Area laws and tensor networks for two-dimensional gapped systems","summary":"Proves an entropy area law for unique ground states of finite-range Hamiltonians on arbitrary finite induced square-lattice domains, using only a uniform full-system spectral gap and bounds on the local interactions. On open $L\\times L$ squares, uniformly gapped nearest-neighbor ground states also admit projected entangled-pair state approximations with polynomial bond dimension and global vector error at most L−1.","paperIds":["265-01","265-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"The two-dimensional gapped area law","overviewSummary":"Proves an entropy area law for unique ground states of finite-range Hamiltonians on arbitrary finite induced square-lattice domains, using only a uniform full-system spectral gap and bounds on the local interactions. On open $L\\times L$ squares, uniformly gapped nearest-neighbor ground states also admit projected entangled-pair state approximations with polynomial bond dimension and global vector error at most $L^{-1}$."},{"id":"266","title":"Exactly three mutually unbiased bases in dimension six","summary":"Proves $N(6)=3$, resolving Zauner's dimension-six mutually unbiased bases conjecture: three such bases exist in ℂ6, but four cannot. The exclusion is a complete certified computation under the stated binary64 arithmetic and compiler conditions. An independent companion proves the Matolcsi–Ruzsa–Weiner Fourier-vanishing conjecture for order-six complex Hadamard matrices outside Tao's cubic equivalence class.","paperIds":["266-01","266-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/266.md","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"Exactly three mutually unbiased bases in dimension six","overviewSummary":"Proves $N(6)=3$, resolving Zauner\\textquotesingle s dimension-six mutually unbiased bases conjecture: three such bases exist in $\\mathbb C^6$, but four cannot. The exclusion is a complete certified computation under the stated binary64 arithmetic and compiler conditions. An independent companion proves the Matolcsi--Ruzsa--Weiner Fourier-vanishing conjecture for order-six complex Hadamard matrices outside Tao\\textquotesingle s cubic equivalence class."},{"id":"267","title":"Positive-temperature Bose–Einstein condensation and exact quantum depletion","summary":"Proves Bose–Einstein condensation for the exact canonical Gibbs state of the three-dimensional hard-sphere gas: each fixed exclusion distance and sufficiently small fixed density admit a strictly positive temperature, independent of volume, with positive condensate fraction in the thermodynamic limit. At zero temperature, proves the Bogoliubov leading quantum-depletion law for hard spheres and fixed bounded nonnegative radial finite-range potentials of positive scattering length, taking the thermodynamic limit before the dilute limit.","paperIds":["267-01","267-02","267-03","267-04","267-05"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/267.md","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"Positive-temperature Bose--Einstein condensation and quantum depletion","overviewSummary":"Proves Bose--Einstein condensation for the exact canonical Gibbs state of the three-dimensional hard-sphere gas: each fixed exclusion distance and sufficiently small fixed density admit a strictly positive temperature, independent of volume, with positive condensate fraction in the thermodynamic limit. At zero temperature, proves the Bogoliubov leading quantum-depletion law for hard spheres and fixed bounded nonnegative radial finite-range potentials of positive scattering length, taking the thermodynamic limit before the dilute limit."},{"id":"268","title":"The spin-one Haldane gap","summary":"Proves the spin-one Haldane gap conjecture for the pure antiferromagnetic Heisenberg chain on even periodic rings: the spectral gap stays uniformly positive as the chain grows. A companion establishes a gap for odd open chains with endpoint field $h=3/5$ and gives boundary-selected infinite-volume states with topological index −1.","paperIds":["268-01","268-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"The spin-one Haldane gap","overviewSummary":"Proves the spin-one Haldane gap conjecture for the pure antiferromagnetic Heisenberg chain on even periodic rings: the spectral gap stays uniformly positive as the chain grows. A companion establishes a gap for odd open chains with endpoint field $h=3/5$ and gives boundary-selected infinite-volume states with topological index $-1$."},{"id":"269","title":"Uniform Laughlin gap and stability under bounded scalar disorder","summary":"Proves the fermionic Laughlin spectral-gap conjecture for the full V1 interaction at filling 1/3 on the round sphere. The unique ground state remains uniformly gapped under sufficiently weak bounded real scalar one-body potentials projected to the lowest Landau level. Both the gap and disorder threshold are uniform over all sufficiently large particle numbers and all normalized potential profiles.","paperIds":["269-01","269-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/269.md","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"The Laughlin gap and stability under scalar disorder","overviewSummary":"Proves the fermionic Laughlin spectral-gap conjecture for the full $V_1$ interaction at filling $1/3$ on the round sphere. The unique ground state remains uniformly gapped under sufficiently weak bounded real scalar one-body potentials projected to the lowest Landau level. Both the gap and disorder threshold are uniform over all sufficiently large particle numbers and all normalized potential profiles."},{"id":"270","title":"Threshold and positive-energy bound states of the BFSS matrix model","summary":"Proves that the undeformed relative $\\mathrm{SU}(N)$ BFSS model has exactly one normalizable zero-energy state for every finite N ≥ 2, resolving the threshold-bound-state conjecture. For $\\mathrm{SU}(2)$, a companion proves infinitely many normalizable positive-energy eigenstates with unbounded energies, contradicting the original BFSS paper's exclusion of additional bound states at N = 2.","paperIds":["270-01","270-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"Threshold and positive-energy bound states of the BFSS model","overviewSummary":"Proves that the undeformed relative $\\mathrm{SU}(N)$ BFSS model has exactly one normalizable zero-energy state for every finite $N\\ge2$, resolving the threshold-bound-state conjecture. For $\\mathrm{SU}(2)$, a companion proves infinitely many normalizable positive-energy eigenstates with unbounded energies, contradicting the original BFSS paper\\textquotesingle s exclusion of additional bound states at $N=2$."},{"id":"271","title":"Bloch's law, its lattice correction, and the spherical magnetization law","summary":"Proves Bloch's T3/2 law with its exact coefficient for three-dimensional quantum Heisenberg ferromagnets at every positive quantum spin, allowing nonnegative symmetric finite-range couplings whose support generates ℤ3. The thermodynamic limit precedes the zero-field derivative and low-temperature limit. The family also proves spontaneous magnetization for nearest-neighbor models in every dimension d ≥ 3 and determines the first lattice correction for three-dimensional nearest-neighbor couplings.","paperIds":["271-01","271-02","271-03","271-04"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/271.md","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"Bloch's law and spontaneous ferromagnetic order","overviewSummary":"Proves Bloch's $T^{3/2}$ law with its exact coefficient for three-dimensional quantum Heisenberg ferromagnets at every positive quantum spin, allowing nonnegative symmetric finite-range couplings whose support generates $\\mathbb Z^3$. The thermodynamic limit precedes the zero-field derivative and low-temperature limit. The family also proves spontaneous magnetization for nearest-neighbor models in every dimension $d\\ge3$ and determines the first lattice correction for three-dimensional nearest-neighbor couplings."},{"id":"272","title":"Entanglement without distillable secret key","summary":"Constructs an entangled state on $\\mathbb C^{10}\\otimes\\mathbb C^{10}$ with zero distillable secret key for the specified local-instrument protocols that complete almost surely. These allow joint local processing and authenticated two-way public communication, with no other shared private resource and an eavesdropper holding the input purification and public record. A trace-preserving PPT channel on $M_{21}(\\mathbb C)$ whose square is not entanglement breaking disproves Christandl's PPT-square conjecture.","paperIds":["272-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/272.md","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"Entanglement without secret key and the PPT-square conjecture","overviewSummary":"Constructs an entangled state on $\\mathbb C^{10}\\otimes\\mathbb C^{10}$ with zero distillable secret key for the specified local-instrument protocols that complete almost surely. These allow joint local processing and authenticated two-way public communication, with no other shared private resource and an eavesdropper holding the input purification and public record. A trace-preserving PPT channel on $M_{21}(\\mathbb C)$ whose square is not entanglement breaking disproves Christandl\\textquotesingle s PPT-square conjecture."},{"id":"273","title":"The entropy photon-number inequality","summary":"Proves the entropy photon-number inequality for beam-splitter mixing of two independent finite-energy bosonic inputs in any finite number of modes: the output's entropy photon number is at least the transmissivity-weighted average of the inputs'. Arbitrary entanglement within each input is allowed, and product thermal inputs attain equality even when their entropies differ.","paperIds":["273-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/273.md","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"The entropy photon-number inequality","overviewSummary":"Proves the entropy photon-number inequality for beam-splitter mixing of two independent finite-energy bosonic inputs in any finite number of modes: the output\\textquotesingle s entropy photon number is at least the transmissivity-weighted average of the inputs\\textquotesingle. Arbitrary entanglement within each input is allowed, and product thermal inputs attain equality even when their entropies differ."},{"id":"274","title":"Parity is not in QAC0","summary":"Resolves Moore's parity conjecture in the measured-output model: constant-depth quantum circuits with arbitrary one-qubit gates, unbounded-arity Toffoli gates and polynomially many total qubits cannot compute parity with any fixed positive worst-case advantage. Ancillas start in zero, one output qubit is measured, and all other registers may be discarded. Xu–Li's reductions give the same bounded-error obstruction for strict majority.","paperIds":["274-01","274-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/274.md","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"Moore's parity conjecture for $\\mathrm{QAC}^0$","overviewSummary":"Resolves Moore's parity conjecture in the measured-output model: constant-depth quantum circuits with arbitrary one-qubit gates, unbounded-arity Toffoli gates and polynomially many total qubits cannot compute parity with any fixed positive worst-case advantage. Ancillas start in zero, one output qubit is measured, and all other registers may be discarded. Xu--Li's reductions give the same bounded-error obstruction for strict majority."},{"id":"275","title":"QMA-hardness of continuum Coulomb energy","summary":"Proves QMA-hardness of approximating the electronic Coulomb energy infimum in three dimensions, minimizing over the full spinful fermionic continuum space. Deterministic polynomial-time reductions work even with only unit-charge nuclei at distinct rational positions, polynomially many electrons and an energy-threshold separation of at least one.","paperIds":["275-01","275-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/275.md","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"QMA-hardness of continuum Coulomb energy","overviewSummary":"Proves QMA-hardness of approximating the electronic Coulomb energy infimum in three dimensions, minimizing over the full spinful fermionic continuum space. Deterministic polynomial-time reductions work even with only unit-charge nuclei at distinct rational positions, polynomially many electrons and an energy-threshold separation of at least one."},{"id":"276","title":"Classical capacity of generalized amplitude damping","summary":"Determines the unassisted classical capacity of every qubit generalized amplitude-damping channel, including all damping and thermal parameters. An explicit one-variable optimization gives the capacity, attained by independent two-state signal ensembles with collective decoding. Holevo capacity, minimum output entropy and regularized classical capacity are additive when tensoring with any finite-dimensional quantum channel.","paperIds":["276-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/276.md","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"The classical capacity of generalized amplitude damping","overviewSummary":"Determines the unassisted classical capacity of every qubit generalized amplitude-damping channel, including all damping and thermal parameters. An explicit one-variable optimization gives the capacity, attained by independent two-state signal ensembles with collective decoding. Holevo capacity, minimum output entropy and regularized classical capacity are additive when tensoring with any finite-dimensional quantum channel."},{"id":"277","title":"Threshold repetition for entangled games","summary":"Proves exponential threshold repetition for every finite two-player one-round game: if its entangled value is v < 1, the probability of winning at least a fraction $v+\\delta$ of k independent repetitions decays exponentially in k, for $0\\lt \\delta\\lt 1-v$. Arbitrary joint finite-dimensional entangled strategies and correlated question distributions are allowed.","paperIds":["277-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/277.md","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"Threshold repetition for entangled games","overviewSummary":"Proves exponential threshold repetition for every finite two-player one-round game: if its entangled value is $v<1$, the probability of winning at least a fraction $v+\\delta$ of $k$ independent repetitions decays exponentially in $k$, for $0<\\delta<1-v$. Arbitrary joint finite-dimensional entangled strategies and correlated question distributions are allowed."},{"id":"278","title":"Failure of Kohn–Sham ensemble representation","summary":"Constructs a three-electron Coulomb molecule with two equal positive-integer-charge nuclei whose absolute ground-state density has no noninteracting ground-state ensemble representation by a single real spin-independent local potential in $L^{3/2}(\\mathbb R^3)+L^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$. This disproves Kohn–Sham ensemble representability for that potential class; the required nuclear charge is specified nonnumerically.","paperIds":["278-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"Failure of Kohn--Sham ensemble representation","overviewSummary":"Constructs a three-electron Coulomb molecule with two equal positive-integer-charge nuclei whose absolute ground-state density has no noninteracting ground-state ensemble representation by a single real spin-independent local potential in $L^{3/2}(\\mathbb R^3)+L^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$. This disproves Kohn--Sham ensemble representability for that potential class; the required nuclear charge is specified nonnumerically."},{"id":"279","title":"Exact quantum factoring over a fixed finite gate set","summary":"Gives a polynomial-time uniform quantum circuit family that outputs the complete prime factorization of every integer with probability one. Both gate count and qubit count are polynomial in the input length, and one fixed finite gate set suffices.","paperIds":["279-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/279.md","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"Exact quantum factoring over a fixed finite gate set","overviewSummary":"Gives a polynomial-time uniform quantum circuit family that outputs the complete prime factorization of every integer with probability one. Both gate count and qubit count are polynomial in the input length, and one fixed finite gate set suffices."},{"id":"280","title":"Unitary vertex operator algebras and conformal nets","summary":"Proves the strongly rational case of the strong-locality conjecture: every simple unitary strongly rational complex vertex operator algebra generates a completely rational conformal net. Its simple modules are unitarizable, and its representation category agrees with the net’s finite-index sectors as a braided unitary tensor category.","paperIds":["280-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/280.md","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"Strong locality for strongly rational unitary vertex operator algebras","overviewSummary":"Proves the strongly rational case of the strong-locality conjecture: every simple unitary strongly rational complex vertex operator algebra generates a completely rational conformal net. Its simple modules are unitarizable, and its representation category agrees with the net’s finite-index sectors as a braided unitary tensor category."},{"id":"281","title":"QAOA attains the SK optimum in the thermodynamic-first limit","summary":"Proves that QAOA approaches the ground-state energy of the Gaussian zero-field Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model when system size tends to infinity before circuit depth. For every accuracy, finite depth and deterministic angles independent of size and disorder achieve the required limiting expected energy per spin. This also yields leading-order optimal expected MaxCut values on large-degree random regular graphs, with size tending to infinity before degree.","paperIds":["281-01","281-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/281.md","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"QAOA optimality for the SK model","overviewSummary":"Proves that QAOA approaches the ground-state energy of the Gaussian zero-field Sherrington--Kirkpatrick model when system size tends to infinity before circuit depth. For every accuracy, finite depth and deterministic angles independent of size and disorder achieve the required limiting expected energy per spin. This also yields leading-order optimal expected MaxCut values on large-degree random regular graphs, with size tending to infinity before degree."},{"id":"282","title":"From scale symmetry to local conformal symmetry in four-dimensional QFT","summary":"Under the stated bounded-local-net and field-reconstruction hypotheses, proves that scale symmetry implies local conformal symmetry for four-dimensional unitary positive-energy theories with a discrete bounded-below scaling spectrum of finite multiplicity, finite scaling support and a physical local scale current. The stress tensor has a traceless improvement with unchanged spacetime charges. The conclusion concerns local Ward identities, not a global conformal action on the whole net.","paperIds":["282-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"Scale-to-conformal enhancement in four-dimensional QFT","overviewSummary":"Under the stated bounded-local-net and field-reconstruction hypotheses, proves that scale symmetry implies local conformal symmetry for four-dimensional unitary positive-energy theories with a discrete bounded-below scaling spectrum of finite multiplicity, finite scaling support and a physical local scale current. The stress tensor has a traceless improvement with unchanged spacetime charges. The conclusion concerns local Ward identities, not a global conformal action on the whole net."},{"id":"283","title":"Polynomial-time unitary synthesis from a Boolean oracle","summary":"Solves the constant-error Aaronson–Kuperberg unitary synthesis problem: a uniform polynomial-size quantum oracle circuit approximates every n-qubit unitary channel within diamond-norm error 1/2, after a suitable Boolean oracle is chosen. Gates, qubits, oracle calls and query length are polynomially bounded. The target-dependent oracle may have an unrestricted truth table; its efficient classical construction is not asserted.","paperIds":["283-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"Polynomial-time, constant-error unitary synthesis from a Boolean oracle","overviewSummary":"Solves the constant-error Aaronson--Kuperberg unitary synthesis problem: a uniform polynomial-size quantum oracle circuit approximates every $n$-qubit unitary channel within diamond-norm error $1/2$, after a suitable Boolean oracle is chosen. Gates, qubits, oracle calls and query length are polynomially bounded. The target-dependent oracle may have an unrestricted truth table; its efficient classical construction is not asserted."},{"id":"284","title":"The optimal quartic separation between randomized and quantum queries","summary":"Shows that the universal bound $R(f)=O((1+Q(f))^4)$ for total Boolean functions is sharp in its exponent, ruling out every smaller power and disproving the conjectured cubic relation. Here R and Q are randomized and quantum worst-case bit-query complexities with error at most 1/3; computation between queries is unrestricted.","paperIds":["284-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","overviewTitle":"The optimal randomized--quantum query exponent","overviewSummary":"Shows that the universal bound $R(f)=O((1+Q(f))^4)$ for total Boolean functions is sharp in its exponent, ruling out every smaller power and disproving the conjectured cubic relation. Here $R$ and $Q$ are randomized and quantum worst-case bit-query complexities with error at most $1/3$; computation between queries is unrestricted."},{"id":"285","title":"Counterexamples to Baum–Connes and Kadison–Kaplansky","summary":"Disproves the coefficient-free reduced Baum–Connes conjecture through a failure of rational injectivity and a separate failure of surjectivity of assembly. A finitely generated torsion-free example witnesses the injectivity failure. Separately, a torsion-free group has a nontrivial projection in its reduced group C∗-algebra, disproving the Kadison–Kaplansky conjecture.","paperIds":["285-01","285-02","285-03"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","overviewTitle":"Counterexamples to Baum--Connes and Kadison--Kaplansky","overviewSummary":"Disproves the coefficient-free reduced Baum--Connes conjecture through a failure of rational injectivity and a separate failure of surjectivity of assembly. A finitely generated torsion-free example witnesses the injectivity failure. Separately, a torsion-free group has a nontrivial projection in its reduced group $C^*$-algebra, disproving the Kadison--Kaplansky conjecture."},{"id":"286","title":"Rigidity and arithmetic of lattice von Neumann algebras","summary":"Classifies finite-index bimodules between scalar-twisted group factors of ICC groups commensurable with property-$(T)$ lattices over characteristic-zero local fields and arbitrary ICC group factors. Every such bimodule is a summand of finite sums of models arising from finite-index subgroup isomorphisms and finite-dimensional projective representations. The classification also recovers the group, scalar cocycle and amplification scale up to the stated stable equivalence.","paperIds":["286-01","286-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","overviewTitle":"Arithmetic rigidity of lattice von Neumann algebras","overviewSummary":"Classifies finite-index bimodules between scalar-twisted group factors of ICC groups commensurable with property-$(T)$ lattices over characteristic-zero local fields and arbitrary ICC group factors. Every such bimodule is a summand of finite sums of models arising from finite-index subgroup isomorphisms and finite-dimensional projective representations. The classification also recovers the group, scalar cocycle and amplification scale up to the stated stable equivalence."},{"id":"287","title":"Isomorphism of the free group factors","summary":"Resolves the free group factor isomorphism problem: $L(\\mathbb F_2)\\cong L(\\mathbb F_3)$, and hence all interpolated free group factors, including $L(\\mathbb F_\\infty)$, are isomorphic. Their common factor has fundamental group $\\mathbb R_{\\gt 0}$.","paperIds":["287-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/287.md","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","overviewTitle":"All nonabelian free group factors are isomorphic","overviewSummary":"Resolves the free group factor isomorphism problem: $L(\\mathbb F_2)\\cong L(\\mathbb F_3)$, and hence all interpolated free group factors, including $L(\\mathbb F_\\infty)$, are isomorphic. Their common factor has fundamental group $\\mathbb R_{>0}$."},{"id":"288","title":"Kadison's similarity conjecture","summary":"Proves Kadison's similarity conjecture: every bounded complex-linear unital algebra homomorphism from a unital complex C∗-algebra to operators on a Hilbert space becomes a $*$-homomorphism after conjugation by a bounded invertible operator.","paperIds":["288-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/288.md","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","overviewTitle":"Kadison's similarity conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves Kadison's similarity conjecture: every bounded complex-linear unital algebra homomorphism from a unital complex $C^*$-algebra to operators on a Hilbert space becomes a $*$-homomorphism after conjugation by a bounded invertible operator."},{"id":"289","title":"Strong Kadison–Kastler stability and its spatial boundaries","summary":"Proves that sufficiently close unital von Neumann algebras on the same Hilbert space are conjugate by a unitary arbitrarily close to the identity, with a universal tolerance in the operator-norm distance between unit balls. Counterexamples show that near-identity conjugacy fails for one-sided near inclusions, and that arbitrarily close norm-separable C∗-algebras need not be ambiently unitarily conjugate.","paperIds":["289-01","289-02","289-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/289.md","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","overviewTitle":"The strong Kadison--Kastler conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves that sufficiently close unital von Neumann algebras on the same Hilbert space are conjugate by a unitary arbitrarily close to the identity, with a universal tolerance in the operator-norm distance between unit balls. Counterexamples show that near-identity conjugacy fails for one-sided near inclusions, and that arbitrarily close norm-separable $C^*$-algebras need not be ambiently unitarily conjugate."},{"id":"290","title":"Relative bicentralizers and modular spectral recovery","summary":"Proves Connes' bicentralizer conjecture for every type III1 factor with separable predual and every faithful normal state. More generally, for every inclusion $N\\subset M$ of von Neumann algebras with separable preduals admitting a faithful normal conditional expectation, constructs an amenable expected subalgebra $P\\subset N$ with $P'\\cap c(M)=N'\\cap c(M)$, resolving the relative bicentralizer conjecture.","paperIds":["290-01","290-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/290.md","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","overviewTitle":"Connes' bicentralizer conjecture and relative bicentralizers","overviewSummary":"Proves Connes' bicentralizer conjecture for every type $\\mathrm{III}_1$ factor with separable predual and every faithful normal state. More generally, for every inclusion $N\\subset M$ of von Neumann algebras with separable preduals admitting a faithful normal conditional expectation, constructs an amenable expected subalgebra $P\\subset N$ with $P'\\cap c(M)=N'\\cap c(M)$, resolving the relative bicentralizer conjecture."},{"id":"291","title":"Cuntz comparison, nuclear dimension, and equivariant Jiang–Su stability","summary":"Proves equivariant Jiang–Su stability for every countable discrete amenable group action on a simple separable unital infinite-dimensional nuclear stably finite Jiang–Su-stable C∗-algebra, resolving this case of Szabó's conjecture without restrictions on trace dynamics. The family also proves the unital Toms–Winter conjecture, equating strict comparison, finite nuclear dimension and Jiang–Su stability in the simple separable unital infinite-dimensional nuclear setting.","paperIds":["291-01","291-02","291-03","291-04"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/291.md","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","overviewTitle":"Toms--Winter and equivariant Jiang--Su stability","overviewSummary":"Proves equivariant Jiang--Su stability for every countable discrete amenable group action on a simple separable unital infinite-dimensional nuclear stably finite Jiang--Su-stable $C^*$-algebra, resolving this case of Szab\\'o's conjecture without restrictions on trace dynamics. The family also proves the unital Toms--Winter conjecture, equating strict comparison, finite nuclear dimension and Jiang--Su stability in the simple separable unital infinite-dimensional nuclear setting."},{"id":"292","title":"Kirchberg's $\\mathcal O_2$ norm-ultrapower embedding problem","summary":"Constructs an explicit separable unital full group C∗-algebra that cannot embed unitally into the norm ultrapower of any fixed nonzero unital nuclear C∗-algebra, for any free ultrafilter on the natural numbers. Taking the target to be $\\mathcal O_2$ answers Kirchberg's norm-ultrapower embedding problem negatively.","paperIds":["292-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/292.md","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","overviewTitle":"A counterexample to Kirchberg's norm-ultrapower embedding problem","overviewSummary":"Constructs an explicit separable unital full group $C^*$-algebra that cannot embed unitally into the norm ultrapower of any fixed nonzero unital nuclear $C^*$-algebra, for any free ultrafilter on the natural numbers. Taking the target to be $\\mathcal O_2$ answers Kirchberg's norm-ultrapower embedding problem negatively."},{"id":"293","title":"Invariant projections, hyperinvariant subspaces, and transitive algebras","summary":"Constructs a nonzero norm-quasinilpotent operator on every infinite-dimensional separable complex Hilbert space with no nonzero proper closed subspace invariant under every commuting operator. The construction also gives operators with no nontrivial invariant projection in the hyperfinite type II1 factor.","paperIds":["293-01","293-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/293.md","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","overviewTitle":"A counterexample to the hyperinvariant-subspace problem","overviewSummary":"Constructs a nonzero norm-quasinilpotent operator on every infinite-dimensional separable complex Hilbert space with no nonzero proper closed subspace invariant under every commuting operator. The construction also gives operators with no nontrivial invariant projection in the hyperfinite type $\\mathrm{II}_1$ factor."},{"id":"294","title":"Kaplansky's quasitrace conjecture and failure of tensor-product stable finiteness","summary":"Disproves Kaplansky's quasitrace conjecture by constructing a separable unital complex C∗-algebra admitting normalized 2-quasitraces, all of which are nonadditive. As a consequence, two unital simple stably finite C∗-algebras can have a properly infinite minimal tensor product, with one factor $C_r^*(\\mathbb F_2)$.","paperIds":["294-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/294.md","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","overviewTitle":"A counterexample to Kaplansky's quasitrace conjecture","overviewSummary":"Disproves Kaplansky's quasitrace conjecture by constructing a separable unital complex $C^*$-algebra admitting normalized $2$-quasitraces, all of which are nonadditive. As a consequence, two unital simple stably finite $C^*$-algebras can have a properly infinite minimal tensor product, with one factor $C_r^*(\\mathbb F_2)$."},{"id":"295","title":"The Kadison–Ringrose cohomology conjecture","summary":"Proves that every bounded Hochschild cocycle of degree at least two on a complex von Neumann algebra, with coefficients in the algebra itself, has a bounded primitive. Equivalently, all higher bounded Hochschild cohomology groups vanish, resolving the Kadison–Ringrose conjecture.","paperIds":["295-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/295.md","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","overviewTitle":"The Kadison--Ringrose cohomology conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves that every bounded Hochschild cocycle of degree at least two on a complex von Neumann algebra, with coefficients in the algebra itself, has a bounded primitive. Equivalently, all higher bounded Hochschild cohomology groups vanish, resolving the Kadison--Ringrose conjecture."},{"id":"296","title":"The generator problem for finite factors","summary":"Proves that every type II1 factor with separable predual is generated by a single operator, equivalently by two self-adjoint operators, resolving the generator problem. More strongly, for every irreducible inclusion $P\\subset M$ of such factors, the unitaries u with $M=W^*(P,u)$ form a dense Gδ subset in the trace 2-norm topology.","paperIds":["296-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/296.md","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","overviewTitle":"The generator problem for finite factors","overviewSummary":"Proves that every type $\\mathrm{II}_1$ factor with separable predual is generated by a single operator, equivalently by two self-adjoint operators, resolving the generator problem. More strongly, for every irreducible inclusion $P\\subset M$ of such factors, the unitaries $u$ with $M=W^*(P,u)$ form a dense $G_\\delta$ subset in the trace $2$-norm topology."},{"id":"297","title":"A ZFC counterexample to Naimark's problem","summary":"Gives an alternative to [Tanaka's ZFC construction](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26930v1) of a unital infinite-dimensional simple complex C∗-algebra with a faithful tracial state and exactly one nonzero irreducible representation up to unitary equivalence. Thus the unrestricted compact-operator characterization fails without additional set-theoretic assumptions; the counterexample is nonseparable.","paperIds":["297-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/297.md","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","overviewTitle":"A ZFC counterexample to Naimark's problem","overviewSummary":"Gives an alternative to \\href{https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26930v1}{Tanaka's ZFC construction} of a unital infinite-dimensional simple complex $C^*$-algebra with a faithful tracial state and exactly one nonzero irreducible representation up to unitary equivalence. Thus the unrestricted compact-operator characterization fails without additional set-theoretic assumptions; the counterexample is nonseparable."},{"id":"298","title":"Two notions of free entropy differ even when both are finite","summary":"Constructs a bounded self-adjoint tuple in a tracial von Neumann algebra whose microstates and nonmicrostates free entropies satisfy $-\\infty\\lt \\chi\\lt \\chi^*\\lt \\infty$. This answers Voiculescu’s finite-entropy equality question negatively: the matrix-approximation and free-Fisher-information definitions differ even when both are finite.","paperIds":["298-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/298.md","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","overviewTitle":"A counterexample to Voiculescu’s free-entropy equality conjecture","overviewSummary":"Constructs a bounded self-adjoint tuple in a tracial von Neumann algebra whose microstates and nonmicrostates free entropies satisfy $-\\infty<\\chi<\\chi^*<\\infty$. This answers Voiculescu’s finite-entropy equality question negatively: the matrix-approximation and free-Fisher-information definitions differ even when both are finite."},{"id":"299","title":"The Kirchberg–Rørdam character criterion and infinite tensor-power Jiang–Su stability","summary":"A nonzero unital separable complex C∗-algebra is Jiang–Su stable exactly when its norm central-sequence algebra has no characters, for every free ultrafilter. This answers the Kirchberg–Rørdam character question. Also, the infinite minimal tensor power of every such algebra without characters is Jiang–Su stable, answering the Dadarlat–Toms question.","paperIds":["299-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/299.md","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","overviewTitle":"The Kirchberg--R\\o rdam character criterion","overviewSummary":"A nonzero unital separable complex $C^*$-algebra is Jiang--Su stable exactly when its norm central-sequence algebra has no characters, for every free ultrafilter. This answers the Kirchberg--R\\o rdam character question. Also, the infinite minimal tensor power of every such algebra without characters is Jiang--Su stable, answering the Dadarlat--Toms question."},{"id":"300","title":"Approximation and quadratic strong-operator paving","summary":"Proves that every self-adjoint element of a complex von Neumann algebra admits strong-operator paving relative to any maximal abelian subalgebra with $O(\\varepsilon^{-2})$ blocks. The norm bound holds after compression by a projection arbitrarily close to the identity in the strong topology, resolving the Popa–Vaes quadratic paving conjecture.","paperIds":["300-01","300-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","overviewTitle":"The Popa--Vaes quadratic strong-operator paving conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves that every self-adjoint element of a complex von Neumann algebra admits strong-operator paving relative to any maximal abelian subalgebra with $O(\\varepsilon^{-2})$ blocks. The norm bound holds after compression by a projection arbitrarily close to the identity in the strong topology, resolving the Popa--Vaes quadratic paving conjecture."},{"id":"301","title":"Trace cones and Razak–Jacelon stabilization","summary":"Classifies separable nuclear complex C∗-algebras after tensoring with the Razak–Jacelon algebra and the compact operators, using their full topological cones of extended lower-semicontinuous tracial weights. This answers Robert’s trace-cone question, including algebras with arbitrary ideal structure and both finite and infinite subquotients.","paperIds":["301-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/301.md","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","overviewTitle":"Classification by trace cones after Razak--Jacelon stabilization","overviewSummary":"Classifies separable nuclear complex $C^*$-algebras after tensoring with the Razak--Jacelon algebra and the compact operators, using their full topological cones of extended lower-semicontinuous tracial weights. This answers Robert’s trace-cone question, including algebras with arbitrary ideal structure and both finite and infinite subquotients."},{"id":"302","title":"Radius of comparison equals half the mean dimension","summary":"For every minimal homeomorphism h of an infinite compact metrizable space X, the radius of comparison of $C(X)\\rtimes_h\\mathbb Z$ equals $\\tfrac12\\mathrm{mdim}(X,h)$, including infinite values. Zero mean dimension is equivalent to the small boundary property, Jiang–Su stability and finite nuclear dimension; in this case nuclear dimension is at most one.","paperIds":["302-01","302-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","overviewTitle":"The Phillips--Toms formula for minimal integer actions","overviewSummary":"For every minimal homeomorphism $h$ of an infinite compact metrizable space $X$, the radius of comparison of $C(X)\\rtimes_h\\mathbb Z$ equals $\\tfrac12\\mathrm{mdim}(X,h)$, including infinite values. Zero mean dimension is equivalent to the small boundary property, Jiang--Su stability and finite nuclear dimension; in this case nuclear dimension is at most one."},{"id":"303","title":"Weak pure infiniteness and Cuntz-algebra absorption","summary":"Resolves the ordinary-to-strong pure-infiniteness question of Kirchberg and Rørdam for complex C∗-algebras. For exact algebras, proper infiniteness of one fixed finite amplification of every positive element also suffices. Consequently, every separable nuclear algebra with this property absorbs $\\mathcal O_\\infty$, without unitality or simplicity assumptions.","paperIds":["303-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/303.md","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","overviewTitle":"From ordinary to strong pure infiniteness","overviewSummary":"Resolves the ordinary-to-strong pure-infiniteness question of Kirchberg and R{\\o}rdam for complex $C^*$-algebras. For exact algebras, proper infiniteness of one fixed finite amplification of every positive element also suffices. Consequently, every separable nuclear algebra with this property absorbs $\\mathcal O_\\infty$, without unitality or simplicity assumptions."},{"id":"304","title":"The Hilbert–Smith conjecture in every dimension","summary":"Every locally compact second-countable Hausdorff group acting faithfully and jointly continuously on a connected finite-dimensional topological manifold is a Lie group. This proves the Hilbert–Smith conjecture in all finite dimensions, for Hausdorff second-countable manifolds without boundary.","paperIds":["304-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","overviewTitle":"The Hilbert--Smith conjecture in every dimension","overviewSummary":"Every locally compact second-countable Hausdorff group acting faithfully and jointly continuously on a connected finite-dimensional topological manifold is a Lie group. This proves the Hilbert--Smith conjecture in all finite dimensions, for Hausdorff second-countable manifolds without boundary."},{"id":"305","title":"Four-dimensional disk embedding and Wall's conjecture","summary":"The unrestricted four-dimensional disk-embedding conjecture fails: framed algebraic dual spheres do not suffice to obtain disjoint locally flat spanning disks. In particular, the free group F2 is not good in the sense of Freedman–Quinn. Also constructs a finitely presented integral Poincaré duality group of dimension four with a finite classifying space but no realization as the fundamental group of a closed aspherical topological four-manifold, disproving Wall's conjecture.","paperIds":["305-01","305-02","305-03"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","overviewTitle":"Counterexamples to disk embedding and Wall's manifold conjecture","overviewSummary":"The unrestricted four-dimensional disk-embedding conjecture fails: framed algebraic dual spheres do not suffice to obtain disjoint locally flat spanning disks. In particular, the free group $F_2$ is not good in the sense of Freedman--Quinn. Also constructs a finitely presented integral Poincaré duality group of dimension four with a finite classifying space but no realization as the fundamental group of a closed aspherical topological four-manifold, disproving Wall's conjecture."},{"id":"306","title":"The purely cosmetic surgery conjecture","summary":"Distinct Dehn surgery slopes on a nontrivial smooth knot in S3 never produce orientation-preservingly homeomorphic manifolds, proving the purely cosmetic surgery conjecture. The statement includes the meridional slope.","paperIds":["306-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","overviewTitle":"The purely cosmetic surgery conjecture for knots in $S^3$","overviewSummary":"Distinct Dehn surgery slopes on a nontrivial smooth knot in $S^3$ never produce orientation-preservingly homeomorphic manifolds, proving the purely cosmetic surgery conjecture. The statement includes the meridional slope."},{"id":"307","title":"Failure of rational injectivity for maximal coarse assembly","summary":"Constructs a uniformly discrete bounded-geometry space whose maximal coarse assembly map is not rationally injective. The example is a coarse disjoint union of finite connected graphs of uniformly bounded degree, with an infinite-order kernel class. A companion gives the analogous failure for reduced coarse assembly, disproving the rational coarse Novikov conjecture.","paperIds":["307-01","307-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/307.md","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","overviewTitle":"Failure of rational injectivity for maximal coarse assembly","overviewSummary":"Constructs a uniformly discrete bounded-geometry space whose maximal coarse assembly map is not rationally injective. The example is a coarse disjoint union of finite connected graphs of uniformly bounded degree, with an infinite-order kernel class. A companion gives the analogous failure for reduced coarse assembly, disproving the rational coarse Novikov conjecture."},{"id":"308","title":"Finite Smith–Toda complexes at every height","summary":"For every n ≥ 0, constructs a finite Smith–Toda spectrum at a prime p depending on n, with Brown–Peterson homology $BP_*/(p,v_1,\\ldots,v_n)$ and the canonical comodule structure. Thus Smith–Toda complexes exist at every height when the prime may vary; an explicit example realizes $V(4)$ at p = 1009.","paperIds":["308-01","308-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","overviewTitle":"Smith--Toda complexes at every height with varying primes","overviewSummary":"For every $n\\ge0$, constructs a finite Smith--Toda spectrum at a prime $p$ depending on $n$, with Brown--Peterson homology $BP_*/(p,v_1,\\ldots,v_n)$ and the canonical comodule structure. Thus Smith--Toda complexes exist at every height when the prime may vary; an explicit example realizes $V(4)$ at $p=1009$."},{"id":"309","title":"The Kervaire invariant problem at the prime three","summary":"Resolves the odd-primary Kervaire invariant problem at the prime three: exactly the standard classes with indices 0, 2, and 3 survive in the mod-three Adams spectral sequence, in stems 10, 106, and 322. Each surviving detection coset contains an element of exact additive order three.","paperIds":["309-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","overviewTitle":"The Kervaire invariant problem at the prime three","overviewSummary":"Resolves the odd-primary Kervaire invariant problem at the prime three: exactly the standard classes with indices $0$, $2$, and $3$ survive in the mod-three Adams spectral sequence, in stems $10$, $106$, and $322$. Each surviving detection coset contains an element of exact additive order three."},{"id":"310","title":"Quillen's conjecture in rational homology","summary":"Proves the rational-homology form of Quillen's conjecture for every finite group and every prime. If the largest normal p-subgroup of G is trivial, the poset of nontrivial elementary abelian p-subgroups has nonzero augmented reduced rational homology and is therefore not contractible.","paperIds":["310-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","overviewTitle":"Quillen's conjecture in rational homology","overviewSummary":"Proves the rational-homology form of Quillen's conjecture for every finite group and every prime. If the largest normal $p$-subgroup of $G$ is trivial, the poset of nontrivial elementary abelian $p$-subgroups has nonzero augmented reduced rational homology and is therefore not contractible."},{"id":"311","title":"The Hovey–Strickland and Chai conjectures","summary":"Proves Chai's invariant-ideal conjecture for Lubin–Tate deformation rings over finite residue fields, at every prime and positive height n. Through the implication of Barthel–Heard–Naumann, this proves the Hovey–Strickland conjecture: dualizable $K(n)$-local spectra have exactly $n+2$ thick tensor ideals, and their Balmer spectrum is a chain of $n+1$ points.","paperIds":["311-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","overviewTitle":"Chai's invariant-ideal conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves Chai's invariant-ideal conjecture for Lubin--Tate deformation rings over finite residue fields, at every prime and positive height $n$. Through the implication of Barthel--Heard--Naumann, this proves the Hovey--Strickland conjecture: dualizable $K(n)$-local spectra have exactly $n+2$ thick tensor ideals, and their Balmer spectrum is a chain of $n+1$ points."},{"id":"312","title":"The Grothendieck homotopy hypothesis","summary":"Proves the Grothendieck homotopy hypothesis for ∞-groupoids associated with every Grothendieck coherator in the Ara–Henry convention: these algebraic objects recover the homotopy theory of spaces.","paperIds":["312-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/312.md","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","overviewTitle":"The Grothendieck homotopy hypothesis","overviewSummary":"Proves the Grothendieck homotopy hypothesis for $\\infty$-groupoids associated with every Grothendieck coherator in the Ara--Henry convention: these algebraic objects recover the homotopy theory of spaces."},{"id":"313","title":"Finite generation for the $K(n)$-local sphere","summary":"Answers the degreewise finiteness question of Hovey and Hovey–Strickland: every homotopy group of the $K(n)$-local sphere is a finitely generated ℤp-module, for every prime, positive height and integer degree. The same conclusion holds after $K(n)$-localizing any finite p-local spectrum.","paperIds":["313-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","overviewTitle":"Finite generation for the $K(n)$-local sphere","overviewSummary":"Answers the degreewise finiteness question of Hovey and Hovey--Strickland: every homotopy group of the $K(n)$-local sphere is a finitely generated $\\mathbb Z_p$-module, for every prime, positive height and integer degree. The same conclusion holds after $K(n)$-localizing any finite $p$-local spectrum."},{"id":"314","title":"Cyclic length and chromatic fixed-point loss","summary":"Determines the optimal chromatic loss from geometric H-fixed points to geometric G-fixed points for every subgroup H of a finite p-group G. At every nonnegative height, the loss equals the shortest subnormal-chain length from H to G with cyclic quotients. Each quotient counts once regardless of order, and finite spectra witness sharpness.","paperIds":["314-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","overviewTitle":"The chromatic Smith fixed-point problem for finite $p$-groups","overviewSummary":"Determines the optimal chromatic loss from geometric $H$-fixed points to geometric $G$-fixed points for every subgroup $H$ of a finite $p$-group $G$. At every nonnegative height, the loss equals the shortest subnormal-chain length from $H$ to $G$ with cyclic quotients. Each quotient counts once regardless of order, and finite spectra witness sharpness."},{"id":"315","title":"The four-dimensional Singer conjecture","summary":"Proves that the L2-Betti numbers of the universal cover of every closed connected aspherical topological four-manifold vanish outside degree two. More generally, the same conclusion holds for every finite connected aspherical integral Poincaré complex of formal dimension four, proving the four-dimensional Singer conjecture in this wider class.","paperIds":["315-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","overviewTitle":"The four-dimensional Singer conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves that the $L^2$-Betti numbers of the universal cover of every closed connected aspherical topological four-manifold vanish outside degree two. More generally, the same conclusion holds for every finite connected aspherical integral Poincar\\'e complex of formal dimension four, proving the four-dimensional Singer conjecture in this wider class."},{"id":"316","title":"Curtis’s conjecture","summary":"Proves Curtis’s conjecture: the positive-degree mod-two stable Hurewicz image of the sphere is spanned by the images of the Hopf-invariant-one classes η, ν, σ and the Kervaire-invariant-one classes that exist.","paperIds":["316-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","overviewTitle":"Curtis’s conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves Curtis’s conjecture: the positive-degree mod-two stable Hurewicz image of the sphere is spanned by the images of the Hopf-invariant-one classes $\\eta,\\nu,\\sigma$ and the Kervaire-invariant-one classes that exist."},{"id":"317","title":"Thomason model structures in all strict higher dimensions","summary":"Resolves the Ara–Maltsiniotis conjecture: for every n ≥ 1 and n = ω, small strict globular n-categories admit proper combinatorial Thomason model structures Quillen equivalent to simplicial sets. Thus strict higher categories model the homotopy theory of spaces in every stated dimension.","paperIds":["317-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/317.md","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","overviewTitle":"Thomason model structures in every strict higher dimension","overviewSummary":"Resolves the Ara--Maltsiniotis conjecture: for every $n\\ge1$ and $n=\\omega$, small strict globular $n$-categories admit proper combinatorial Thomason model structures Quillen equivalent to simplicial sets. Thus strict higher categories model the homotopy theory of spaces in every stated dimension."},{"id":"318","title":"Chromatic splitting: filtrations and counterexamples","summary":"Disproves strong chromatic splitting at height three for primes p ≥ 5, and weak splitting for the derived p-completed sphere at heights p (p ≥ 5) and $p+1$ (p ≥ 7). Nevertheless, for n ≥ 1 and $p\\gt n+1$, the overlap $L_{n-1}L_{K(n)}S_p^\\wedge$ admits a $2^n$-stage filtration by the predicted localized-sphere pieces. At height three and prime three, even finite assembly from such pieces fails in the category of $E(2)$-local modules over the derived completed sphere.","paperIds":["318-01","318-02","318-03","318-04","318-05"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","overviewTitle":"Chromatic splitting: counterexamples and filtrations","overviewSummary":"Disproves strong chromatic splitting at height three for primes $p\\ge5$, and weak splitting for the derived $p$-completed sphere at heights $p$ ($p\\ge5$) and $p+1$ ($p\\ge7$). Nevertheless, for $n\\ge1$ and $p>n+1$, the overlap $L_{n-1}L_{K(n)}S_p^\\wedge$ admits a $2^n$-stage filtration by the predicted localized-sphere pieces. At height three and prime three, even finite assembly from such pieces fails in the category of $E(2)$-local modules over the derived completed sphere."},{"id":"319","title":"Counterexamples to finite generation at chromatic height two","summary":"Refutes the Hahn–Wilson conjecture at chromatic height two. For every sufficiently large prime p, constructs a connective p-complete spectrum of exact fp-type two that cannot be built from completed $\\mathrm{BP}\\langle2\\rangle$ by finitely many sums, shifts, cones and retracts. The examples nevertheless satisfy the finite and telescopic localization comparisons.","paperIds":["319-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","overviewTitle":"Counterexamples to the Hahn--Wilson conjecture at height two","overviewSummary":"Refutes the Hahn--Wilson conjecture at chromatic height two. For every sufficiently large prime $p$, constructs a connective $p$-complete spectrum of exact fp-type two that cannot be built from completed $\\mathrm{BP}\\langle2\\rangle$ by finitely many sums, shifts, cones and retracts. The examples nevertheless satisfy the finite and telescopic localization comparisons."},{"id":"320","title":"Nonhomeomorphic closed aspherical four-manifolds","summary":"Constructs closed connected aspherical topological four-manifolds that are homotopy equivalent but not homeomorphic, with a common word-hyperbolic fundamental group. This disproves even the homeomorphism-existence formulation of the Borel conjecture in dimension four.","paperIds":["320-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","overviewTitle":"A four-dimensional counterexample to Borel rigidity","overviewSummary":"Constructs closed connected aspherical topological four-manifolds that are homotopy equivalent but not homeomorphic, with a common word-hyperbolic fundamental group. This disproves even the homeomorphism-existence formulation of the Borel conjecture in dimension four."},{"id":"321","title":"A counterexample to Wall's finite D(2) problem","summary":"Constructs a finite connected three-dimensional CW complex whose universal cover has no integral homology above degree two and whose third cohomology vanishes for every local coefficient module, but which has no finite two-dimensional homotopy model. This disproves Wall's finite D(2) conjecture; the example has infinite fundamental group.","paperIds":["321-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","overviewTitle":"A counterexample to Wall's finite D(2) conjecture","overviewSummary":"Constructs a finite connected three-dimensional CW complex whose universal cover has no integral homology above degree two and whose third cohomology vanishes for every local coefficient module, but which has no finite two-dimensional homotopy model. This disproves Wall's finite D(2) conjecture; the example has infinite fundamental group."},{"id":"322","title":"Tingley’s sphere-isometry problem","summary":"Resolves Tingley's problem: every surjective isometry between the unit spheres of nonzero real Banach spaces extends uniquely to a surjective real-linear isometry of the whole spaces. No dimension restriction is imposed, so the metric geometry of the unit sphere determines the Banach space up to linear isometry.","paperIds":["322-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/322.md","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","overviewTitle":"Tingley's sphere-isometry problem","overviewSummary":"Resolves Tingley's problem: every surjective isometry between the unit spheres of nonzero real Banach spaces extends uniquely to a surjective real-linear isometry of the whole spaces. No dimension restriction is imposed, so the metric geometry of the unit sphere determines the Banach space up to linear isometry."},{"id":"323","title":"Independence of the separable quotient problem","summary":"Establishes, relative to the consistency of a measurable cardinal, that the separable quotient problem is independent of ZFC. The assertion that every infinite-dimensional Banach space has a separable infinite-dimensional quotient can hold for all real and complex Banach spaces, whereas the continuum hypothesis yields counterexamples over both fields.","paperIds":["323-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/323.md","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","overviewTitle":"Relative independence of the separable quotient problem","overviewSummary":"Establishes, relative to the consistency of a measurable cardinal, that the separable quotient problem is independent of ZFC. The assertion that every infinite-dimensional Banach space has a separable infinite-dimensional quotient can hold for all real and complex Banach spaces, whereas the continuum hypothesis yields counterexamples over both fields."},{"id":"324","title":"Lipschitz equivalent Banach spaces need not be linearly isomorphic","summary":"Constructs separable real Banach spaces that are globally bi-Lipschitz equivalent but not linearly isomorphic, resolving the separable Lipschitz-isomorphism problem negatively. Thus even the complete metric structure up to bi-Lipschitz equivalence does not determine a separable Banach space's linear isomorphism class.","paperIds":["324-01","324-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/324.md","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","overviewTitle":"Lipschitz equivalence without linear isomorphism","overviewSummary":"Constructs separable real Banach spaces that are globally bi-Lipschitz equivalent but not linearly isomorphic, resolving the separable Lipschitz-isomorphism problem negatively. Thus even the complete metric structure up to bi-Lipschitz equivalence does not determine a separable Banach space's linear isomorphism class."},{"id":"325","title":"The complete Crouzeix conjecture","summary":"Resolves the complete Crouzeix conjecture: for every bounded operator A on a complex Hilbert space and every finite matrix-valued polynomial P, one has $\\lVert P[A]\\rVert\\le2\\sup_{z\\in W(A)}\\lVert P(z)\\rVert$, where $W(A)$ is the numerical range. The constant 2 is sharp, independent of the matrix size, and valid in infinite dimensions.","paperIds":["325-01","325-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/325.md","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","overviewTitle":"The complete Crouzeix conjecture","overviewSummary":"Resolves the complete Crouzeix conjecture: for every bounded operator $A$ on a complex Hilbert space and every finite matrix-valued polynomial $P$, one has $\\lVert P[A]\\rVert\\le2\\sup_{z\\in W(A)}\\lVert P(z)\\rVert$, where $W(A)$ is the numerical range. The constant $2$ is sharp, independent of the matrix size, and valid in infinite dimensions."},{"id":"326","title":"The cotype–cotype conjecture under the approximation property","summary":"Resolves the cotype–cotype conjecture for real Banach spaces with the approximation property. Such a nonzero space is K-convex if and only if both it and its dual have finite Rademacher cotype, with possibly different exponents. Equivalently, these cotype assumptions force nontrivial Rademacher type.","paperIds":["326-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/326.md","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","overviewTitle":"The cotype--cotype conjecture under the approximation property","overviewSummary":"Resolves the cotype--cotype conjecture for real Banach spaces with the approximation property. Such a nonzero space is $K$-convex if and only if both it and its dual have finite Rademacher cotype, with possibly different exponents. Equivalently, these cotype assumptions force nontrivial Rademacher type."},{"id":"327","title":"Markov type characterizes superreflexivity","summary":"Proves that every real Banach space with Markov type p for some p > 1 admits an equivalent uniformly convex norm, answering Naor's renorming question. Together with the known converse, this characterizes superreflexivity by nontrivial Markov type.","paperIds":["327-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/327.md","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","overviewTitle":"Markov type characterizes superreflexivity","overviewSummary":"Proves that every real Banach space with Markov type $p$ for some $p>1$ admits an equivalent uniformly convex norm, answering Naor's renorming question. Together with the known converse, this characterizes superreflexivity by nontrivial Markov type."},{"id":"328","title":"Nonexpansive fixed points in reflexive Banach spaces","summary":"Resolves Kirk's reflexive-space fixed-point problem: every nonexpansive selfmap of a nonempty closed bounded convex subset of a real reflexive Banach space has a fixed point. The result uses the original norm, without assuming uniform convexity.","paperIds":["328-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/328.md","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","overviewTitle":"Fixed points of nonexpansive maps in reflexive Banach spaces","overviewSummary":"Resolves Kirk's reflexive-space fixed-point problem: every nonexpansive selfmap of a nonempty closed bounded convex subset of a real reflexive Banach space has a fixed point. The result uses the original norm, without assuming uniform convexity."},{"id":"329","title":"A counterexample to metric-entropy duality","summary":"Disproves Pietsch's dimension-free duality conjecture for metric entropy. Origin-symmetric convex bodies violate every proposed choice of universal constants in the conjectured comparison between covering numbers and those of the polar bodies, even when the covering body is a cube.","paperIds":["329-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/329.md","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","overviewTitle":"A counterexample to Pietsch's metric-entropy duality conjecture","overviewSummary":"Disproves Pietsch's dimension-free duality conjecture for metric entropy. Origin-symmetric convex bodies violate every proposed choice of universal constants in the conjectured comparison between covering numbers and those of the polar bodies, even when the covering body is a cube."},{"id":"330","title":"A uniformly discrete counterexample to bounded approximation in Lipschitz-free spaces","summary":"Constructs a countable uniformly discrete metric space whose real Lipschitz-free Banach space has the approximation property but not the bounded approximation property, answering Kalton's question negatively. Finite-rank operators approximate the identity on every compact set, but their norms cannot share a finite bound.","paperIds":["330-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/330.md","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","overviewTitle":"A negative answer to Kalton's Lipschitz-free approximation question","overviewSummary":"Constructs a countable uniformly discrete metric space whose real Lipschitz-free Banach space has the approximation property but not the bounded approximation property, answering Kalton's question negatively. Finite-rank operators approximate the identity on every compact set, but their norms cannot share a finite bound."},{"id":"331","title":"Reflexive midpoint convexity and diamond distortion","summary":"Constructs a real reflexive Banach space with an asymptotically midpoint uniformly convex norm but no equivalent asymptotically uniformly convex norm, extending Baudier's separation to reflexive spaces. In the same space, depth-k countably branching diamonds require distortion at least $\\sqrt{1+k/12}$, so midpoint uniform convexity does not force uniformly bounded diamond distortion even under reflexivity.","paperIds":["331-01","331-02","331-03","331-04","331-05","331-06","331-07"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/331.md","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","overviewTitle":"Asymptotic midpoint uniform convexity without asymptotically uniformly convex renorming","overviewSummary":"Constructs a real reflexive Banach space with an asymptotically midpoint uniformly convex norm but no equivalent asymptotically uniformly convex norm, extending Baudier's separation to reflexive spaces. In the same space, depth-$k$ countably branching diamonds require distortion at least $\\sqrt{1+k/12}$, so midpoint uniform convexity does not force uniformly bounded diamond distortion even under reflexivity."},{"id":"332","title":"Metric Markov cotype of ℓ1 and Hilbert-space Lipschitz extension","summary":"Proves that real ℓ1 has metric Markov cotype two, answering Mendel and Naor's question. Consequently, every Lipschitz map from an arbitrary subset of a real Hilbert space into ℓ1 extends to the whole space with a universal multiplicative loss in its Lipschitz constant, resolving Ball's extension problem for this target.","paperIds":["332-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","overviewTitle":"Metric Markov cotype two for $\\ell_1$","overviewSummary":"Proves that real $\\ell_1$ has metric Markov cotype two, answering Mendel and Naor's question. Consequently, every Lipschitz map from an arbitrary subset of a real Hilbert space into $\\ell_1$ extends to the whole space with a universal multiplicative loss in its Lipschitz constant, resolving Ball's extension problem for this target."},{"id":"333","title":"Smooth isometric immersions of surfaces into ℝ4","summary":"Every closed smooth Riemannian surface admits a smooth isometric immersion into ℝ4, resolving the closed-surface form of the four-dimensional isometric-immersion problem. This includes nonorientable surfaces and metrics of arbitrary Gaussian curvature.","paperIds":["333-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/333.md","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Smooth isometric immersions of surfaces into $\\mathbb R^4$","overviewSummary":"Every closed smooth Riemannian surface admits a smooth isometric immersion into $\\mathbb R^4$, resolving the closed-surface form of the four-dimensional isometric-immersion problem. This includes nonorientable surfaces and metrics of arbitrary Gaussian curvature."},{"id":"334","title":"A smooth surface metric with no local isometric immersion in ℝ3","summary":"Constructs a smooth positive-definite metric on $(-1,1)^2$ for which no neighborhood of the origin admits a smooth isometric immersion into ℝ3. This answers the unrestricted smooth local isometric realization problem for surfaces negatively, even after shrinking the neighborhood.","paperIds":["334-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/334.md","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"A smooth surface metric with no local immersion in $\\mathbb R^3$","overviewSummary":"Constructs a smooth positive-definite metric on $(-1,1)^2$ for which no neighborhood of the origin admits a smooth isometric immersion into $\\mathbb R^3$. This answers the unrestricted smooth local isometric realization problem for surfaces negatively, even after shrinking the neighborhood."},{"id":"335","title":"Gromov’s integral scalar-curvature bound for simplicial volume","summary":"Proves $\\int_M(\\mathrm{Scal}_g^-)^{n/2}\\,dV_g\\ge a_n\\lVert M\\rVert$ for every closed connected oriented smooth n-manifold, n ≥ 3, and every smooth metric, with $a_n\\gt 0$ depending only on dimension. Here $\\mathrm{Scal}_g^-=\\max\\{0,-\\mathrm{Scal}_g\\}$ and $\\lVert M\\rVert$ is real simplicial volume. Also proves rational inessentiality under positive scalar curvature, resolving the Gromov–Lawson conjecture; every nonnegative-scalar-curvature metric on a closed aspherical manifold is flat.","paperIds":["335-01","335-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Gromov's integral scalar-curvature inequality","overviewSummary":"Proves $\\int_M(\\mathrm{Scal}_g^-)^{n/2}\\,dV_g\\ge a_n\\lVert M\\rVert$ for every closed connected oriented smooth $n$-manifold, $n\\ge3$, and every smooth metric, with $a_n>0$ depending only on dimension. Here $\\mathrm{Scal}_g^-=\\max\\{0,-\\mathrm{Scal}_g\\}$ and $\\lVert M\\rVert$ is real simplicial volume. Also proves rational inessentiality under positive scalar curvature, resolving the Gromov--Lawson conjecture; every nonnegative-scalar-curvature metric on a closed aspherical manifold is flat."},{"id":"336","title":"Spectral scalar curvature, Urysohn width, and macroscopic dimension","summary":"Every complete connected smooth boundaryless n-manifold, n ≥ 3, satisfying $-4\\Delta+\\mathrm{Scal}\\ge1$ as a quadratic-form inequality admits a continuous map to a simplicial complex of dimension at most $n-2$ whose entire fibers have diameter bounded only by n in the original metric. This strengthens Gromov's width conclusion to spectral scalar curvature. Universal covers of closed positive-scalar-curvature manifolds also have continuous macroscopic dimension at most $n-2$ for every n ≥ 2.","paperIds":["336-01","336-02","336-03"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Spectral scalar curvature and codimension-two width","overviewSummary":"Every complete connected smooth boundaryless $n$-manifold, $n\\ge3$, satisfying $-4\\Delta+\\mathrm{Scal}\\ge1$ as a quadratic-form inequality admits a continuous map to a simplicial complex of dimension at most $n-2$ whose entire fibers have diameter bounded only by $n$ in the original metric. This strengthens Gromov's width conclusion to spectral scalar curvature. Universal covers of closed positive-scalar-curvature manifolds also have continuous macroscopic dimension at most $n-2$ for every $n\\ge2$."},{"id":"337","title":"Sharp Cartan–Hadamard isoperimetry and rigidity","summary":"Proves generalized Cartan–Hadamard isoperimetry in every dimension: in a complete simply connected manifold with sectional curvature at most κ ≤ 0, every finite-volume finite-perimeter set satisfies the sharp comparison with the equal-volume model ball. Bounded positive-volume equality regions for κ = 0 are Euclidean balls. Also proves sharp Euclidean filling bounds for compactly supported integral n-cycles, n ≥ 2, in arbitrary proper CAT$(0)$ spaces.","paperIds":["337-01","337-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/337.md","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Cartan--Hadamard isoperimetry and CAT$(0)$ fillings","overviewSummary":"Proves generalized Cartan--Hadamard isoperimetry in every dimension: in a complete simply connected manifold with sectional curvature at most $\\kappa\\le0$, every finite-volume finite-perimeter set satisfies the sharp comparison with the equal-volume model ball. Bounded positive-volume equality regions for $\\kappa=0$ are Euclidean balls. Also proves sharp Euclidean filling bounds for compactly supported integral $n$-cycles, $n\\ge2$, in arbitrary proper CAT$(0)$ spaces."},{"id":"338","title":"Yau's uniformization conjecture","summary":"Proves Yau's uniformization conjecture: every complete connected noncompact Kähler manifold with strictly positive holomorphic bisectional curvature is biholomorphic to ℂn.","paperIds":["338-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Yau's uniformization conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves Yau's uniformization conjecture: every complete connected noncompact K\\\"ahler manifold with strictly positive holomorphic bisectional curvature is biholomorphic to $\\mathbb C^n$."},{"id":"339","title":"Katok's entropy rigidity conjecture","summary":"Proves Katok's entropy rigidity conjecture for closed connected Riemannian manifolds of dimension at least three with strictly negative sectional curvature: normalized Liouville measure maximizes entropy for the geodesic flow if and only if the metric is locally symmetric.","paperIds":["339-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Katok's entropy rigidity conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves Katok's entropy rigidity conjecture for closed connected Riemannian manifolds of dimension at least three with strictly negative sectional curvature: normalized Liouville measure maximizes entropy for the geodesic flow if and only if the metric is locally symmetric."},{"id":"340","title":"A counterexample to the nearby Lagrangian conjecture","summary":"Disproves the unrestricted nearby Lagrangian conjecture. For some sufficiently large even N, constructs a closed exact embedded Lagrangian in $T^*(S^9\\times S^{N-1})$ that is diffeomorphic to the base but not Hamiltonian isotopic to its zero section.","paperIds":["340-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"A counterexample to the nearby Lagrangian conjecture","overviewSummary":"Disproves the unrestricted nearby Lagrangian conjecture. For some sufficiently large even $N$, constructs a closed exact embedded Lagrangian in $T^*(S^9\\times S^{N-1})$ that is diffeomorphic to the base but not Hamiltonian isotopic to its zero section."},{"id":"341","title":"Donaldson's hypersymplectic deformation conjecture","summary":"Proves Donaldson's hypersymplectic deformation conjecture in a cohomology-preserving form. Every positive triple of smooth closed two-forms on a closed connected oriented four-manifold, normalized by $\\int\\omega_i\\wedge\\omega_j=\\delta_{ij}$, deforms through positive closed triples to a hyperkähler triple while preserving all three cohomology classes. Any positive triple can first be normalized by a constant linear change.","paperIds":["341-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Donaldson's hypersymplectic deformation conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves Donaldson's hypersymplectic deformation conjecture in a cohomology-preserving form. Every positive triple of smooth closed two-forms on a closed connected oriented four-manifold, normalized by $\\int\\omega_i\\wedge\\omega_j=\\delta_{ij}$, deforms through positive closed triples to a hyperk\\\"ahler triple while preserving all three cohomology classes. Any positive triple can first be normalized by a constant linear change."},{"id":"342","title":"Donaldson's tamed-to-compatible conjecture","summary":"Proves Donaldson's tamed-to-compatible conjecture: every smooth almost complex structure on a closed four-manifold that is tamed by a symplectic form admits a compatible symplectic form. The almost complex structure stays fixed; the form's cohomology class may change.","paperIds":["342-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/342.md","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Donaldson's tamed-to-compatible conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves Donaldson's tamed-to-compatible conjecture: every smooth almost complex structure on a closed four-manifold that is tamed by a symplectic form admits a compatible symplectic form. The almost complex structure stays fixed; the form's cohomology class may change."},{"id":"343","title":"Symplectic ball packing in higher dimensions","summary":"Resolves the Siegel–Yao conjecture for arbitrary capacities in every dimension $2n\\ge6$. Finitely many closed symplectic balls of capacities $R_1,\\ldots,R_k$ embed disjointly into an open ball of capacity R exactly when $\\sum_iR_i^n\\lt R^n$ and $R_i+R_j\\lt R$ for every distinct pair i, j.","paperIds":["343-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/343.md","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Sharp symplectic ball-packing criteria in higher dimensions","overviewSummary":"Resolves the Siegel--Yao conjecture for arbitrary capacities in every dimension $2n\\ge6$. Finitely many closed symplectic balls of capacities $R_1,\\ldots,R_k$ embed disjointly into an open ball of capacity $R$ exactly when $\\sum_iR_i^n<R^n$ and $R_i+R_j<R$ for every distinct pair $i,j$."},{"id":"344","title":"The metric Blaschke conjecture","summary":"Proves the metric Blaschke conjecture: every closed connected Riemannian manifold of positive dimension whose injectivity radius equals its diameter is, up to scaling, a standard compact rank-one symmetric space.","paperIds":["344-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"The metric Blaschke conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves the metric Blaschke conjecture: every closed connected Riemannian manifold of positive dimension whose injectivity radius equals its diameter is, up to scaling, a standard compact rank-one symmetric space."},{"id":"345","title":"Infinitely many closed geodesics on Riemannian spheres and closed three-manifolds","summary":"Proves that every smooth Riemannian metric on Sn, n ≥ 2, has infinitely many prime closed geodesics with pairwise distinct images. The same conclusion holds on every closed manifold admitting a finite smooth spherical cover and on every closed three-manifold, without orientability or nondegeneracy restrictions.","paperIds":["345-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Infinitely many closed geodesics on spheres and three-manifolds","overviewSummary":"Proves that every smooth Riemannian metric on $S^n$, $n\\ge2$, has infinitely many prime closed geodesics with pairwise distinct images. The same conclusion holds on every closed manifold admitting a finite smooth spherical cover and on every closed three-manifold, without orientability or nondegeneracy restrictions."},{"id":"346","title":"Sharp singular-set bounds for stationary integral varifolds","summary":"Proves that every stationary integral m-varifold in a Euclidean open set has singular set of Hausdorff dimension at most $m-1$, a sharp bound in every positive dimension and codimension. On round spheres, the family also proves almost-everywhere regularity: the singular set has zero m-dimensional Hausdorff measure.","paperIds":["346-01","346-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Sharp singular-set bounds for stationary integral varifolds","overviewSummary":"Proves that every stationary integral $m$-varifold in a Euclidean open set has singular set of Hausdorff dimension at most $m-1$, a sharp bound in every positive dimension and codimension. On round spheres, the family also proves almost-everywhere regularity: the singular set has zero $m$-dimensional Hausdorff measure."},{"id":"347","title":"Counterexamples to stable-Morse and strong Arnold fixed-point bounds","summary":"Disproves stable-Morse lower bounds for nondegenerate Hamiltonian fixed points: on simply connected closed Kähler manifolds of real dimension 22, the deficit below the stable Morse number is unbounded. A separate Hamiltonian diffeomorphism of the complex quadric threefold has exactly three fixed points, fewer than the four critical points required of every smooth function.","paperIds":["347-01","347-02","347-03","347-04","347-05"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/347.md","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Counterexamples to strong forms of Arnold's fixed-point conjecture","overviewSummary":"Disproves stable-Morse lower bounds for nondegenerate Hamiltonian fixed points: on simply connected closed K\\\"ahler manifolds of real dimension $22$, the deficit below the stable Morse number is unbounded. A separate Hamiltonian diffeomorphism of the complex quadric threefold has exactly three fixed points, fewer than the four critical points required of every smooth function."},{"id":"348","title":"Nonnegative-curvature Einstein classification and an L2 topological gap","summary":"Classifies closed connected Einstein four-manifolds with positive Einstein constant and nonnegative sectional curvature: up to scaling, their universal Riemannian covers are the round S4, Fubini–Study $\\mathbb{CP}^2$, or a product of equal round two-spheres. A closed simply connected nonnegatively curved four-manifold is diffeomorphic to one of these whenever the scale-invariant L2 norm of its trace-free Ricci curvature lies below a universal positive constant.","paperIds":["348-01","348-02","348-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/348.md","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Nonnegatively curved Einstein four-manifolds and a topological gap","overviewSummary":"Classifies closed connected Einstein four-manifolds with positive Einstein constant and nonnegative sectional curvature: up to scaling, their universal Riemannian covers are the round $S^4$, Fubini--Study $\\mathbb{CP}^2$, or a product of equal round two-spheres. A closed simply connected nonnegatively curved four-manifold is diffeomorphic to one of these whenever the scale-invariant $L^2$ norm of its trace-free Ricci curvature lies below a universal positive constant."},{"id":"349","title":"The Solomon–Yau least-volume conjecture","summary":"Proves the Solomon–Yau least-volume conjecture for minimal hypersurfaces of round spheres. For every m ≥ 2, a closed connected minimal immersion into the unit sphere $S^{m+1}$ with non-totally-geodesic image has volume at least that of the smallest minimal Clifford product, counting covering multiplicity.","paperIds":["349-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"The Solomon--Yau least-volume conjecture","overviewSummary":"Proves the Solomon--Yau least-volume conjecture for minimal hypersurfaces of round spheres. For every $m\\ge2$, a closed connected minimal immersion into the unit sphere $S^{m+1}$ with non-totally-geodesic image has volume at least that of the smallest minimal Clifford product, counting covering multiplicity."},{"id":"350","title":"Yau’s nodal bounds: surfaces and higher dimensions","summary":"Proves the sharp $C\\sqrt\\lambda$ upper bound for nodal length on every fixed smooth closed surface, completing Yau's conjecture there. The upper bound fails for fixed smooth metrics in dimensions three and four, including metrics on S3 arbitrarily close to round. In dimension five, nodal measure can grow faster than $\\lambda^{1/2+\\varepsilon_0}$ for some fixed $\\varepsilon_0\\gt 0$, ruling out even arbitrarily small power losses.","paperIds":["350-01","350-02","350-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/350.md","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Yau's nodal conjecture: surfaces and counterexamples","overviewSummary":"Proves the sharp $C\\sqrt\\lambda$ upper bound for nodal length on every fixed smooth closed surface, completing Yau's conjecture there. The upper bound fails for fixed smooth metrics in dimensions three and four, including metrics on $S^3$ arbitrarily close to round. In dimension five, nodal measure can grow faster than $\\lambda^{1/2+\\varepsilon_0}$ for some fixed $\\varepsilon_0>0$, ruling out even arbitrarily small power losses."},{"id":"351","title":"Scalar curvature and finite-time Ricci-flow singularities","summary":"Proves that a smooth Ricci flow on a closed four-manifold extends past any finite time at which scalar curvature remains uniformly bounded. A higher-dimensional counterexample has bounded scalar curvature but unbounded full curvature at its finite maximal time, disproving the unrestricted scalar-curvature extension conjecture.","paperIds":["351-01","351-02","351-03"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Scalar curvature detects four-dimensional Ricci-flow singularities","overviewSummary":"Proves that a smooth Ricci flow on a closed four-manifold extends past any finite time at which scalar curvature remains uniformly bounded. A higher-dimensional counterexample has bounded scalar curvature but unbounded full curvature at its finite maximal time, disproving the unrestricted scalar-curvature extension conjecture."},{"id":"352","title":"A finite-time singularity of Calabi flow","summary":"Disproves Chen's smooth long-time existence conjecture for Calabi flow by constructing a smooth $U(10)$-invariant Kähler metric on $\\mathbb{CP}^{10}$ whose flow develops a finite-time singularity. The metric lies in the Fubini–Study class, so the failure occurs even in a class containing a constant-scalar-curvature metric.","paperIds":["352-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Finite-time singularity of Calabi flow","overviewSummary":"Disproves Chen's smooth long-time existence conjecture for Calabi flow by constructing a smooth $U(10)$-invariant K\\\"ahler metric on $\\mathbb{CP}^{10}$ whose flow develops a finite-time singularity. The metric lies in the Fubini--Study class, so the failure occurs even in a class containing a constant-scalar-curvature metric."},{"id":"353","title":"Affine Bernstein rigidity through dimension nine and a smooth dimension-ten counterexample","summary":"Proves that every smooth locally uniformly convex affine-maximal graph of dimension three through nine, complete for its induced Euclidean metric, is an elliptic paraboloid. A smooth entire nonquadratic example in dimension ten makes this range sharp. In dimensions three through nine, the paraboloid classification also holds for connected open locally uniformly convex affine-maximal hypersurfaces complete for the affine Berwald–Blaschke metric.","paperIds":["353-01","353-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/353.md","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"The sharp dimension threshold for affine Bernstein rigidity","overviewSummary":"Proves that every smooth locally uniformly convex affine-maximal graph of dimension three through nine, complete for its induced Euclidean metric, is an elliptic paraboloid. A smooth entire nonquadratic example in dimension ten makes this range sharp. In dimensions three through nine, the paraboloid classification also holds for connected open locally uniformly convex affine-maximal hypersurfaces complete for the affine Berwald--Blaschke metric."},{"id":"354","title":"The isoperimetric profile of the cubic three-torus","summary":"Determines the isoperimetric profile of the unit cubic flat three-torus and classifies every finite-perimeter minimizer: balls, circular tubes around shortest closed geodesics, coordinate slabs, and their complements. The transition volumes are $4\\pi/81$ and $1/\\pi$, with exactly the adjacent two types minimizing at each transition.","paperIds":["354-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/354.md","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Isoperimetric regions in the cubic three-torus","overviewSummary":"Determines the isoperimetric profile of the unit cubic flat three-torus and classifies every finite-perimeter minimizer: balls, circular tubes around shortest closed geodesics, coordinate slabs, and their complements. The transition volumes are $4\\pi/81$ and $1/\\pi$, with exactly the adjacent two types minimizing at each transition."},{"id":"355","title":"Unique tangent flows at the first surface singularity","summary":"Proves the first-singular-time case of tangent-flow uniqueness for smooth compact connected embedded surfaces without boundary in ℝ3. At every singular point, all fixed-center backward tangent flows agree as area measures at every negative time in the original ambient coordinates, without mean-convexity or a prescribed tangent model.","paperIds":["355-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Unique tangent flows at the first surface singularity","overviewSummary":"Proves the first-singular-time case of tangent-flow uniqueness for smooth compact connected embedded surfaces without boundary in $\\mathbb R^3$. At every singular point, all fixed-center backward tangent flows agree as area measures at every negative time in the original ambient coordinates, without mean-convexity or a prescribed tangent model."},{"id":"356","title":"Gigli’s characterization of Alexandrov curvature","summary":"Proves Gigli's conjecture: in every integer dimension n ≥ 2, Alexandrov curvature at least κ is characterized by the full-support $\\mathop{\\mathrm{RCD}}\\nolimits ((n-1)\\kappa,n)$ condition with reference measure $\\mathcal H^n$ and distributional sectional curvature at least κ in the original global test classes. The RCD condition is unreduced.","paperIds":["356-01","356-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/356.md","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Gigli's characterization of Alexandrov curvature","overviewSummary":"Proves Gigli's conjecture: in every integer dimension $n\\ge2$, Alexandrov curvature at least $\\kappa$ is characterized by the full-support $\\operatorname{RCD}((n-1)\\kappa,n)$ condition with reference measure $\\mathcal H^n$ and distributional sectional curvature at least $\\kappa$ in the original global test classes. The RCD condition is unreduced."},{"id":"357","title":"Bi-Lipschitz coordinates at every regular RCD point","summary":"Proves that every regular point of a noncollapsed $\\mathop{\\mathrm{RCD}}\\nolimits (K,n)$ space, for K ∈ ℝ and integer n ≥ 2, has an open neighborhood bi-Lipschitz to an open subset of ℝn. Regularity requires all pointed tangents to be Euclidean, the reference measure is exactly $\\mathcal H^n$, and the chart compares ambient distances with a point-dependent finite constant.","paperIds":["357-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Bi-Lipschitz coordinates at every regular RCD point","overviewSummary":"Proves that every regular point of a noncollapsed $\\operatorname{RCD}(K,n)$ space, for $K\\in\\mathbb R$ and integer $n\\ge2$, has an open neighborhood bi-Lipschitz to an open subset of $\\mathbb R^n$. Regularity requires all pointed tangents to be Euclidean, the reference measure is exactly $\\mathcal H^n$, and the chart compares ambient distances with a point-dependent finite constant."},{"id":"358","title":"A three-manifold without conjugate points or nonpositive curvature","summary":"Constructs a closed connected orientable smooth three-manifold that admits a metric without conjugate points but no metric of nonpositive sectional curvature. This answers negatively, already in dimension three, whether the first metric-existence property implies the second.","paperIds":["358-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/358.md","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"A three-manifold with no conjugate points and no nonpositively curved metric","overviewSummary":"Constructs a closed connected orientable smooth three-manifold that admits a metric without conjugate points but no metric of nonpositive sectional curvature. This answers negatively, already in dimension three, whether the first metric-existence property implies the second."},{"id":"359","title":"Negative Kähler curvature without bounded holomorphic coordinates","summary":"Constructs a contractible domain in ℂ3 with a complete negatively pinched Kähler metric but no bounded holomorphic coordinates, disproving bounded-domain uniformization in this setting. A higher-dimensional example has sectional curvature at most −1 and only constant bounded holomorphic functions; its curvature is not bounded below.","paperIds":["359-01","359-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/359.md","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Negative K\\\"ahler curvature without bounded holomorphic coordinates","overviewSummary":"Constructs a contractible domain in $\\mathbb C^3$ with a complete negatively pinched K\\\"ahler metric but no bounded holomorphic coordinates, disproving bounded-domain uniformization in this setting. A higher-dimensional example has sectional curvature at most $-1$ and only constant bounded holomorphic functions; its curvature is not bounded below."},{"id":"360","title":"Weak MTW curvature gives convexity and regular optimal transport","summary":"On every closed connected Riemannian manifold of dimension at least two satisfying weak Ma–Trudinger–Wang curvature, all tangent injectivity domains are convex, resolving Villani’s conjecture in this setting. For squared-distance transport between measurable probability densities bounded above and away from zero, the optimal map and its inverse are Hölder continuous.","paperIds":["360-01","360-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/360.md","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Villani’s convexity conjecture and regular optimal transport","overviewSummary":"On every closed connected Riemannian manifold of dimension at least two satisfying weak Ma--Trudinger--Wang curvature, all tangent injectivity domains are convex, resolving Villani’s conjecture in this setting. For squared-distance transport between measurable probability densities bounded above and away from zero, the optimal map and its inverse are H\\\"older continuous."},{"id":"361","title":"Failure of integer-degree harmonic dimension comparison","summary":"Disproves Yau's proposed Euclidean dimension bound for harmonic functions of integer growth on manifolds with nonnegative Ricci curvature. For every sufficiently large integer k, a complete smooth metric on ℝ3 has at least $(k+2)^2$ independent harmonic functions of growth at most k, exceeding the Euclidean count $(k+1)^2$. The metric may depend on k.","paperIds":["361-01","361-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/361.md","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","overviewTitle":"Counterexamples to Yau's harmonic dimension bound","overviewSummary":"Disproves Yau's proposed Euclidean dimension bound for harmonic functions of integer growth on manifolds with nonnegative Ricci curvature. For every sufficiently large integer $k$, a complete smooth metric on $\\mathbb R^3$ has at least $(k+2)^2$ independent harmonic functions of growth at most $k$, exceeding the Euclidean count $(k+1)^2$. The metric may depend on $k$."},{"id":"362","title":"Global smoothness for relativistic Vlasov–Maxwell","summary":"Proves large-data global existence and uniqueness for the three-dimensional, one-species relativistic Vlasov–Maxwell system. Smooth admissible initial data may be arbitrary provided the particle density is compactly supported and the electromagnetic fields have finite energy and bounded derivatives of every order; the solution remains smooth on every finite time interval.","paperIds":["362-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/362.md","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","overviewTitle":"Global smoothness for relativistic Vlasov--Maxwell","overviewSummary":"Proves large-data global existence and uniqueness for the three-dimensional, one-species relativistic Vlasov--Maxwell system. Smooth admissible initial data may be arbitrary provided the particle density is compactly supported and the electromagnetic fields have finite energy and bounded derivatives of every order; the solution remains smooth on every finite time interval."},{"id":"363","title":"Nonuniqueness with local conservation for the hard-sphere Boltzmann equation","summary":"Constructs two distinct global entropy solutions of the three-dimensional periodic hard-sphere Boltzmann equation from the same nonnegative initial density, with bounded velocity support and finite mass, energy and absolute entropy. Both are strongly continuous in L1 and satisfy exact local conservation of mass, momentum and kinetic energy.","paperIds":["363-01","363-02"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/363.md","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","overviewTitle":"Boltzmann nonuniqueness with exact local conservation","overviewSummary":"Constructs two distinct global entropy solutions of the three-dimensional periodic hard-sphere Boltzmann equation from the same nonnegative initial density, with bounded velocity support and finite mass, energy and absolute entropy. Both are strongly continuous in $L^1$ and satisfy exact local conservation of mass, momentum and kinetic energy."},{"id":"364","title":"Kinetic limits and fluctuations over the Boltzmann lifespan","summary":"Derives the nonlinear Boltzmann equation from three-dimensional grand-canonical Newtonian gases throughout every regular kinetic interval with uniform Gaussian decay. Stable finite-range radial potentials may have attractive wells and a singular repulsive core; initial pair exclusion and spatially summable Gaussian density and gradient bounds are assumed. A companion gives finite-dimensional hard-sphere Gaussian fluctuations, centered at the exact microscopic expectation and governed by the linear fluctuating Boltzmann equation.","paperIds":["364-01","364-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","overviewTitle":"Kinetic limits and fluctuations over the Boltzmann lifespan","overviewSummary":"Derives the nonlinear Boltzmann equation from three-dimensional grand-canonical Newtonian gases throughout every regular kinetic interval with uniform Gaussian decay. Stable finite-range radial potentials may have attractive wells and a singular repulsive core; initial pair exclusion and spatially summable Gaussian density and gradient bounds are assumed. A companion gives finite-dimensional hard-sphere Gaussian fluctuations, centered at the exact microscopic expectation and governed by the linear fluctuating Boltzmann equation."},{"id":"365","title":"Joint metric and connection recovery from one boundary patch","summary":"Zero-frequency measurements on any nonempty open boundary patch determine a smooth metric and smooth unitary connection on a trivial Hermitian rank-two bundle over a compact connected manifold of dimension at least three, up to diffeomorphism and gauge fixed on that patch. Inputs and observations use the same patch. In contrast, distinct uniformly positive bounded measurable scalar conductivities on a three-dimensional ball can have identical full-boundary data.","paperIds":["365-01","365-02","365-03"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/365.md","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","overviewTitle":"Joint metric and connection recovery from one boundary patch","overviewSummary":"Zero-frequency measurements on any nonempty open boundary patch determine a smooth metric and smooth unitary connection on a trivial Hermitian rank-two bundle over a compact connected manifold of dimension at least three, up to diffeomorphism and gauge fixed on that patch. Inputs and observations use the same patch. In contrast, distinct uniformly positive bounded measurable scalar conductivities on a three-dimensional ball can have identical full-boundary data."},{"id":"366","title":"The planar Mumford–Shah regularity conjecture and local weak-L4 gradient bounds","summary":"Resolves the interior regularity conjecture for reduced absolute planar Mumford–Shah minimizers with bounded fidelity data. Locally, the closed discontinuity set is a $C^{1,\\alpha}$ arc, a regular crack tip, or three arcs meeting at $120^\\circ$; only finitely many global connected components meet any compact interior region.","paperIds":["366-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","overviewTitle":"The planar Mumford--Shah regularity conjecture","overviewSummary":"Resolves the interior regularity conjecture for reduced absolute planar Mumford--Shah minimizers with bounded fidelity data. Locally, the closed discontinuity set is a $C^{1,\\alpha}$ arc, a regular crack tip, or three arcs meeting at $120^\\circ$; only finitely many global connected components meet any compact interior region."},{"id":"367","title":"The critical dimension for the one-phase Bernoulli problem","summary":"Establishes seven as the first dimension admitting a nonflat, one-homogeneous global minimizer of the one-phase Bernoulli energy. Consequently, minimizing free boundaries are smooth through dimension six, and their singular sets have dimension at most $n-7$ in higher dimensions.","paperIds":["367-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/367.md","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","overviewTitle":"The critical dimension for the one-phase Bernoulli problem","overviewSummary":"Establishes seven as the first dimension admitting a nonflat, one-homogeneous global minimizer of the one-phase Bernoulli energy. Consequently, minimizing free boundaries are smooth through dimension six, and their singular sets have dimension at most $n-7$ in higher dimensions."},{"id":"368","title":"The three-dimensional Ball–Evans approximation problem","summary":"Resolves the three-dimensional Ball–Evans approximation problem: every $W^{1,p}$ homeomorphism between arbitrary bounded domains in ℝ3, for $1\\le p\\lt \\infty$, is a strong $W^{1,p}$ limit of smooth diffeomorphisms onto the same target.","paperIds":["368-01","368-02"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","overviewTitle":"The three-dimensional Ball--Evans approximation problem","overviewSummary":"Resolves the three-dimensional Ball--Evans approximation problem: every $W^{1,p}$ homeomorphism between arbitrary bounded domains in $\\mathbb R^3$, for $1\\le p<\\infty$, is a strong $W^{1,p}$ limit of smooth diffeomorphisms onto the same target."},{"id":"369","title":"The hot spots conjecture for simply connected planar domains","summary":"Proves a strict form of Burdzy's simply connected hot spots conjecture. On every smooth bounded simply connected planar domain, each nonzero eigenfunction for the first positive Neumann eigenvalue has no interior critical point, so all global extrema lie on the boundary. Eigenvalue multiplicity is allowed.","paperIds":["369-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/369.md","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","overviewTitle":"The hot spots conjecture for simply connected planar domains","overviewSummary":"Proves a strict form of Burdzy's simply connected hot spots conjecture. On every smooth bounded simply connected planar domain, each nonzero eigenfunction for the first positive Neumann eigenvalue has no interior critical point, so all global extrema lie on the boundary. Eigenvalue multiplicity is allowed."},{"id":"370","title":"The Lane–Emden and Hénon–Lane–Emden conjectures","summary":"Resolves the subcritical Lane–Emden conjecture and its weighted Hénon extension. For n ≥ 2, $p,q\\gt 0$ and real A, B, the system $-\\Delta u=|x|^A v^p$, $-\\Delta v=|x|^B u^q$ has no positive entire solution when $(n+A)/(p+1)+(n+B)/(q+1)\\gt n-2$, with solutions continuous at the origin and classical elsewhere. No symmetry or growth assumption is needed. Known radial existence gives the exact existence criterion for n ≥ 3 and $A,B\\gt -2$.","paperIds":["370-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/370.md","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","overviewTitle":"The subcritical Lane--Emden and H\\'enon--Lane--Emden conjectures","overviewSummary":"Resolves the subcritical Lane--Emden conjecture and its weighted H\\'enon extension. For $n\\ge2$, $p,q>0$ and real $A,B$, the system $-\\Delta u=|x|^A v^p$, $-\\Delta v=|x|^B u^q$ has no positive entire solution when $(n+A)/(p+1)+(n+B)/(q+1)>n-2$, with solutions continuous at the origin and classical elsewhere. No symmetry or growth assumption is needed. Known radial existence gives the exact existence criterion for $n\\ge3$ and $A,B>-2$."},{"id":"371","title":"Stable blowup for the defocusing Schrödinger equation","summary":"For a sufficiently large odd nonlinearity power, constructs a nonempty open set of initial data in $H^k(\\mathbb T^{12})$, with k > 8, whose solutions of the scalar defocusing nonlinear Schrödinger equation blow up in finite time. Thus finite-time blowup is stable under Sobolev perturbations in this supercritical regime.","paperIds":["371-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/371.md","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","overviewTitle":"Stable blowup for a defocusing Schr\\\"odinger equation","overviewSummary":"For a sufficiently large odd nonlinearity power, constructs a nonempty open set of initial data in $H^k(\\mathbb T^{12})$, with $k>8$, whose solutions of the scalar defocusing nonlinear Schr\\\"odinger equation blow up in finite time. Thus finite-time blowup is stable under Sobolev perturbations in this supercritical regime."},{"id":"372","title":"Global uniqueness in smooth isotropic elasticity","summary":"Proves that full static boundary displacement-to-traction data determine both smooth real Lamé moduli on every bounded connected smooth domain in ℝ3, provided μ > 0 and $3\\lambda+2\\mu\\gt 0$ on the closure. Neither analyticity, proximity to constant coefficients nor prior knowledge near the boundary is required.","paperIds":["372-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/372.md","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","overviewTitle":"Global uniqueness in smooth isotropic elasticity","overviewSummary":"Proves that full static boundary displacement-to-traction data determine both smooth real Lam\\'e moduli on every bounded connected smooth domain in $\\mathbb R^3$, provided $\\mu>0$ and $3\\lambda+2\\mu>0$ on the closure. Neither analyticity, proximity to constant coefficients nor prior knowledge near the boundary is required."},{"id":"373","title":"Nonattainment of the three-marginal Coulomb Monge problem","summary":"An optimal three-particle Coulomb configuration need not be a deterministic function of the first particle, even for smooth identical spatial densities. The Monge and Kantorovich infima agree, but the Monge infimum is not attained. The same phenomenon occurs for every inverse-power Riesz exponent in each dimension at least two, with a suitable density in each case.","paperIds":["373-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/373.md","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","overviewTitle":"Nonattainment in three-marginal Coulomb transport","overviewSummary":"An optimal three-particle Coulomb configuration need not be a deterministic function of the first particle, even for smooth identical spatial densities. The Monge and Kantorovich infima agree, but the Monge infimum is not attained. The same phenomenon occurs for every inverse-power Riesz exponent in each dimension at least two, with a suitable density in each case."},{"id":"374","title":"Sharp one-third stability of Brenier maps","summary":"For uniform source measure ρ on a compact convex body with interior in dimension at least two, quadratic optimal transport maps satisfy $\\|T_\\mu-T_\\nu\\|_{L^2(\\rho)}\\le C W_2(\\mu,\\nu)^{1/3}$ uniformly over targets in a fixed compact set. The exponent is sharp, even for three-atom targets, disproving Letrouit's conjectured square-root bound.","paperIds":["374-01"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/374.md","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","overviewTitle":"Sharp one-third stability of optimal transport maps","overviewSummary":"For uniform source measure $\\rho$ on a compact convex body with interior in dimension at least two, quadratic optimal transport maps satisfy $\\|T_\\mu-T_\\nu\\|_{L^2(\\rho)}\\le C W_2(\\mu,\\nu)^{1/3}$ uniformly over targets in a fixed compact set. The exponent is sharp, even for three-atom targets, disproving Letrouit's conjectured square-root bound."},{"id":"375","title":"De Giorgi's conjecture in dimension eight","summary":"Proves De Giorgi's conjecture at its sharp dimension-eight endpoint: every entire C2 solution $u:\\mathbb R^8\\to(-1,1)$ of $\\Delta u=u^3-u$ that is strictly increasing in one direction depends on only one linear coordinate. A stronger theorem classifies all stable entire solutions $v:\\mathbb R^7\\to[-1,1]$ as constant wells or planar transitions, without an energy-growth assumption.","paperIds":["375-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","overviewTitle":"De Giorgi's conjecture in dimension eight","overviewSummary":"Proves De Giorgi's conjecture at its sharp dimension-eight endpoint: every entire $C^2$ solution $u:\\mathbb R^8\\to(-1,1)$ of $\\Delta u=u^3-u$ that is strictly increasing in one direction depends on only one linear coordinate. A stronger theorem classifies all stable entire solutions $v:\\mathbb R^7\\to[-1,1]$ as constant wells or planar transitions, without an energy-growth assumption."},{"id":"376","title":"Universal computation in forced Navier–Stokes flows","summary":"Constructs viscous incompressible flows starting from rest on a fixed flat three-dimensional domain that perform universal computation under smooth external forcing. A terminating compiler turns a Turing machine and input into a finite program for the force, so a designated particle reaches a fixed region exactly when the machine halts. The viscosity is fixed, positive and computable.","paperIds":["376-01","376-02","376-03","376-04","376-05","376-06","376-07","376-08","376-09"],"leanDocUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/376.md","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","overviewTitle":"Universal computation in forced Navier--Stokes flows","overviewSummary":"Constructs viscous incompressible flows starting from rest on a fixed flat three-dimensional domain that perform universal computation under smooth external forcing. A terminating compiler turns a Turing machine and input into a finite program for the force, so a designated particle reaches a fixed region exactly when the machine halts. The viscosity is fixed, positive and computable."},{"id":"377","title":"Interior $C^{1,\\alpha}$ regularity for infinity-harmonic functions","summary":"Proves uniform interior $C^{1,\\alpha_d}$ regularity for bounded infinity-harmonic functions in every dimension d ≥ 3, for a positive exponent depending only on dimension. The gradient's supremum norm and Hölder seminorm on the half unit ball are bounded by a dimension-dependent constant times the oscillation on the unit ball. The exponent is not explicit.","paperIds":["377-01"],"leanDocUrl":null,"subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","overviewTitle":"Interior $C^{1,\\alpha}$ regularity for infinity-harmonic functions","overviewSummary":"Proves uniform interior $C^{1,\\alpha_d}$ regularity for bounded infinity-harmonic functions in every dimension $d\\ge3$, for a positive exponent depending only on dimension. The gradient's supremum norm and H\\\"older seminorm on the half unit ball are bounded by a dimension-dependent constant times the oscillation on the unit ball. The exponent is not explicit."}],"papers":[{"id":"001-01","slug":"Milnes-rationality-conjecture-for-abelian-varieties-October-7-2026","familyId":"001","title":"Milne's rationality conjecture for abelian varieties","abstract":"We prove Milne's rationality conjecture for abelian varieties, including residue characteristic 2. After good reduction, the pairing of a rational Hodge class with any complementary product of divisor classes on the reduction is the same rational number in every prime-to-p realization and in crystalline cohomology. Using the Hodge theorem for CM abelian varieties, we also show that every such specialized Hodge class is represented by a single rational algebraic cycle in all these realizations.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Milne's rationality conjecture for abelian varieties over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$ with good reduction: specialized Hodge classes pair rationally with complementary divisor products, independently of cohomology theory. Together with result 032, every specialized Hodge class is represented by a single rational algebraic cycle simultaneously in all prime-to-p and crystalline realizations, for every residue characteristic p.","pdfPath":"preprints/Milnes-rationality-conjecture-for-abelian-varieties-October-7-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Milnes-rationality-conjecture-for-abelian-varieties-October-7-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Milnes-rationality-conjecture-for-abelian-varieties-October-7-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Milnes-rationality-conjecture-for-abelian-varieties-October-7-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Milnes-rationality-conjecture-for-abelian-varieties-October-7-2026/build/sections/00_intro.tex","preprints/Milnes-rationality-conjecture-for-abelian-varieties-October-7-2026/build/figures/two-transports.tex","preprints/Milnes-rationality-conjecture-for-abelian-varieties-October-7-2026/build/sections/01_preliminaries.tex","preprints/Milnes-rationality-conjecture-for-abelian-varieties-October-7-2026/build/sections/02_transport.tex","preprints/Milnes-rationality-conjecture-for-abelian-varieties-October-7-2026/build/sections/03_filtration.tex","preprints/Milnes-rationality-conjecture-for-abelian-varieties-October-7-2026/build/sections/04_weil.tex","preprints/Milnes-rationality-conjecture-for-abelian-varieties-October-7-2026/build/sections/05_reduction.tex","preprints/Milnes-rationality-conjecture-for-abelian-varieties-October-7-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Milnes-rationality-conjecture-for-abelian-varieties-October-7-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Milne's rationality conjecture for abelian varieties, including residue characteristic \\(2\\). After good reduction, the pairing of a rational Hodge class with any complementary product of divisor classes on the reduction is the same rational number in every prime-to-\\(p\\) realization and in crystalline cohomology. Using the Hodge theorem for CM abelian varieties, we also show that every such specialized Hodge class is represented by a single rational algebraic cycle in all these realizations.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":566},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Origins and consequences","sourceOffset":3165},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The method and its antecedents","sourceOffset":6036},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":10027},{"kind":"section","title":"Comparisons and rational Lefschetz classes","sourceOffset":16313},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison of Hodge tensors","sourceOffset":18631},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The common rational Lefschetz space","sourceOffset":20476},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Lifting a prescribed rational homomorphism","sourceOffset":23160},{"kind":"section","title":"Transport through Hodge-type integral models","sourceOffset":27385},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An auxiliary strongly admissible datum","sourceOffset":32111},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Realizing a prescribed admissible filtration","sourceOffset":42341},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof of the transport proposition","sourceOffset":54198}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(A/\\Qbar\\) be an abelian variety of dimension \\(d\\) with good reduction \\(A_0/\\F\\) at \\(w\\). Let \\(0\\leq r\\leq d\\), and let \\[ \\gamma\\in H^{2r}(A(\\C),\\Q(r)) \\] be a rational Hodge class. For every list of Cartier divisors \\(D_1,\\ldots,D_{d-r}\\) on \\(A_0\\), there is a number \\(q\\in\\Q\\) such that \\begin{align*} \\Tr_\\ell\\bigl(\\gamma_{0,\\ell} c_{1,\\ell}(D_1)\\cdots c_{1,\\ell}(D_{d-r})\\bigr)&=q &&\\text{in }\\Q_\\ell\\quad(\\ell\\ne p),\\\\ \\Tr_p\\bigl(\\gamma_{0,p} c_{1,\\mathrm{cris}}(D_1)\\cdots c_{1,\\mathrm{cris}}(D_{d-r})\\bigr)&=q &&\\text{in }\\Cw. \\end{align*} Here \\(\\gamma_{0,\\ell}\\) is the smooth proper specialization of the \\(\\ell\\)-adic realization, and \\(\\gamma_{0,p}\\) is the de Rham--crystalline specialization in \\[ H^{2r}_{\\mathrm{cris}}(A_0/W(\\F))[1/p](r) \\otimes_{\\Kzero}\\Cw. \\] The traces use the top Tate twist and send the class of a zero-cycle to its degree. If \\(r=d\\), the divisor product is \\(1\\).","sourceOffset":1854,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Transport","label":"prop:transport","text":"Let $A/\\Qbar$ be an abelian variety of positive dimension with good reduction at $w$, and put $V=H_1(A(\\C),\\Q)$. Suppose \\[ G\\subset\\GSp(V,\\psi) \\] is connected reductive, its Hodge homomorphism $h$ gives the Hodge structure of $A$, and its conjugacy class $X$ makes this inclusion a Hodge embedding. Let $\\gamma$ be a rational Hodge class of codimension $r$, represented on $V$ by an alternating form satisfying \\eqref{eq:transport-multiplier}. Then there exist a positive integer $n$, an auxiliary good-reduction abelian variety $B$, a quasi-isogeny $a:A_0^n\\to B_0$, and a rational Hodge class $\\kappa$ of codimension $r$ on $B$ such that, for the first-factor inclusion $j:A_0\\to A_0^n$, \\begin{equation} (aj)^*\\kappa_0=\\gamma_0. \\end{equation} The equality holds simultaneously in every $\\ell$-adic realization with $\\ell\\ne p$ and in the crystalline realization after extension to $\\Cw$. Either of the following two further specifications can be imposed. \\begin{enumerate} \\item The variety $B$ is of CM type and is defined over $\\Qbar$, with its indicated good reduction at $w$. \\item Put $D_A=H^1_{\\mathrm{cris}}(A_0/W(\\F))[1/p]$. Suppose $\\mathcal F_A$ is a filtration in degrees $0,1$ on $D_A$, over a finite extension of $\\Kzero$, which is weakly admissible and has finite local-field descent together with the specialized tensors specifying the $G$-structure. Suppose also that, after a geometric tensor-preserving identification, it lies in the $G$-orbit of the original Hodge filtration","sourceOffset":29171,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":null,"text":"A tuple \\(\\alpha=(\\alpha_v)_v\\), with \\(\\alpha_v\\in H_v^{2r}(X)(r)\\), has the Lefschetz pairing property if, for every \\(\\delta\\in\\Lef^{\\dim X-r}(X)\\), the numbers \\(\\Tr_v(\\alpha_v\\delta_v)\\) are the images of one number in \\(\\Q\\).","sourceOffset":21554,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:weil-space","text":"The rational space of Weil classes associated with $E$ is the subspace \\[ W_E^s(A)\\subset \\bigwedge\\nolimits_{\\Q}^{2s} V^\\vee\\otimes\\Q(s) =H^{2s}(A(\\C),\\Q(s)) \\] whose scalar extension to a splitting field $\\Omega$ of $E$ is \\begin{equation} W_E^s(A)\\otimes_\\Q\\Omega =\\bigoplus_{\\tau:E\\hookrightarrow\\Omega} \\bigwedge\\nolimits_\\Omega^{2s}V_\\tau^\\vee \\otimes\\Q(s)_\\Omega. \\end{equation} Here each summand is extended by zero on the other eigenspaces. The right side is Galois stable, so it defines the indicated rational subspace.","sourceOffset":86243,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":137367,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Milnes-rationality-conjecture-for-abelian-varieties-October-7-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"002-01","slug":"Exact-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-Formula-from-Low-Selmer-Corank-October-7-2026","familyId":"002","title":"Exact Birch–Swinnerton-Dyer Formula from Low Selmer Corank","abstract":"We prove the full Birch–Swinnerton-Dyer leading-term formula for every elliptic curve over ℚ whose full q-power Selmer group has corank zero or one at some prime q. The analytic and Mordell–Weil ranks equal that corank, and the Tate–Shafarevich group is finite. The formula includes all prime factors and requires no additional hypotheses on reduction, rational torsion, isogenies, complex multiplication, or residual Galois representations.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the full Birch–Swinnerton-Dyer leading-term formula for every elliptic curve over ℚ whose full q-power Selmer group has corank zero or one for some prime q, including finiteness of the Tate–Shafarevich group. With result 006, this gives full BSD for a density-one set of quadratic twists of every elliptic curve over ℚ.","pdfPath":"preprints/Exact-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-Formula-from-Low-Selmer-Corank-October-7-2026/exact-bsd-low-selmer-corank.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Exact-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-Formula-from-Low-Selmer-Corank-October-7-2026/exact-bsd-low-selmer-corank.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Exact-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-Formula-from-Low-Selmer-Corank-October-7-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Exact-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-Formula-from-Low-Selmer-Corank-October-7-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Exact-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-Formula-from-Low-Selmer-Corank-October-7-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Exact-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-Formula-from-Low-Selmer-Corank-October-7-2026/build/sections/02-classical.tex","preprints/Exact-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-Formula-from-Low-Selmer-Corank-October-7-2026/build/sections/03-models.tex","preprints/Exact-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-Formula-from-Low-Selmer-Corank-October-7-2026/build/sections/04-vertical.tex","preprints/Exact-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-Formula-from-Low-Selmer-Corank-October-7-2026/build/sections/05-single.tex","preprints/Exact-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-Formula-from-Low-Selmer-Corank-October-7-2026/build/sections/06-pair.tex","preprints/Exact-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-Formula-from-Low-Selmer-Corank-October-7-2026/build/sections/07-theta.tex","preprints/Exact-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-Formula-from-Low-Selmer-Corank-October-7-2026/build/sections/08-extraction.tex","preprints/Exact-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-Formula-from-Low-Selmer-Corank-October-7-2026/build/sections/09-unit.tex","preprints/Exact-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-Formula-from-Low-Selmer-Corank-October-7-2026/build/sections/10-center.tex","preprints/Exact-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-Formula-from-Low-Selmer-Corank-October-7-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Exact-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-Formula-from-Low-Selmer-Corank-October-7-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the full Birch--Swinnerton-Dyer leading-term formula for every elliptic curve over $\\Q$ whose full $q$-power Selmer group has corank zero or one at some prime $q$. The analytic and Mordell--Weil ranks equal that corank, and the Tate--Shafarevich group is finite. The formula includes all prime factors and requires no additional hypotheses on reduction, rational torsion, isogenies, complex multiplication, or residual Galois representations.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":514},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Statement and normalization","sourceOffset":983},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and the integral problem","sourceOffset":3470},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The arithmetic discrepancy","sourceOffset":7184},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Integral comparisons and the role of theta series","sourceOffset":8526},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":13608},{"kind":"section","title":"Arithmetic inputs and exact normalizations","sourceOffset":14304},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two low-corank inputs","sourceOffset":14737},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Auxiliary quadratic fields","sourceOffset":18274},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Heegner points and the height formula","sourceOffset":22868},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The real arithmetic comparison","sourceOffset":25444},{"kind":"section","title":"Integral cohomology and determinant comparisons","sourceOffset":28435}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $E/\\Q$ be an elliptic curve and let $q$ be any prime. If $s_q(E)\\in\\{0,1\\}$, then \\[ r(E)=a(E)=s_q(E),\\qquad \\#\\Sha(E/\\Q)<\\infty, \\] and, with $r=r(E)$ and the preceding normalizations, \\begin{equation} \\frac{L^{(r)}(E,1)}{r!} =\\frac{\\Omega_E\\Reg_E\\,\\#\\Sha(E/\\Q) \\prod_{\\ell\\text{ finite}}c_\\ell(E)} {\\#E(\\Q)_{\\rm tors}^{2}}. \\end{equation} There are no additional hypotheses on reduction, rational torsion, isogenies, complex multiplication, or residual Galois representations.","sourceOffset":2382,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Unrestricted low-corank converse","label":"classical:low-corank","text":"For every elliptic curve $A/\\Q$, every prime $q$, and $u\\in\\{0,1\\}$, the equality $s_q(A)=u$ implies \\[ a(A)=r(A)=u,\\qquad \\#\\Sha(A/\\Q)<\\infty. \\]","sourceOffset":15393,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":358673,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":28,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Exact-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-Formula-from-Low-Selmer-Corank-October-7-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"002-02","slug":"The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026","familyId":"002","title":"The Selmer converse for elliptic curves at every prime","abstract":"We prove the Selmer converse in coranks zero and one for every elliptic curve over ℚ and every prime p: if the full p-power Selmer group has ℤp-corank $r\\in\\{0,1\\}$, then the analytic and Mordell–Weil ranks both equal r, and the entire Tate–Shafarevich group is finite. As an application at the additive prime 3, we prove that for every prime $\\ell\\equiv4,7,8\\pmod9$, the cubic $X^3+Y^3=\\ell Z^3$ has analytic and Mordell–Weil rank one and finite Tate–Shafarevich group. In particular, every such ℓ is a sum of two rational cubes.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the full Birch–Swinnerton-Dyer leading-term formula for every elliptic curve over ℚ whose full q-power Selmer group has corank zero or one for some prime q, including finiteness of the Tate–Shafarevich group. With result 006, this gives full BSD for a density-one set of quadratic twists of every elliptic curve over ℚ.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026/build/figures/proof-roadmap.tex","preprints/The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026/build/sections/02-reduction.tex","preprints/The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026/build/sections/03-tame-deformation.tex","preprints/The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026/build/sections/04-theta-construction.tex","preprints/The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026/build/sections/05-constant-terms.tex","preprints/The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026/build/sections/06-nonconstant-coefficient.tex","preprints/The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026/build/sections/07-cusp-lifting.tex","preprints/The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026/build/sections/08-galois-parameters.tex","preprints/The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026/build/sections/09-extension-extraction.tex","preprints/The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026/build/sections/10-sylvester-cube-sums.tex","preprints/The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026/build/sections/10-nonstandard-transfer.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Selmer converse in coranks zero and one for every elliptic curve over $\\Q$ and every prime $p$: if the full $p$-power Selmer group has $\\Z_p$-corank $r\\in\\{0,1\\}$, then the analytic and Mordell--Weil ranks both equal $r$, and the entire Tate--Shafarevich group is finite. As an application at the additive prime $3$, we prove that for every prime $\\ell\\equiv4,7,8\\pmod9$, the cubic $X^3+Y^3=\\ell Z^3$ has analytic and Mordell--Weil rank one and finite Tate--Shafarevich group. In particular, every such $\\ell$ is a sum of two rational cubes.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":617},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The converse problem and its development","sourceOffset":3106},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy: two incompatible lengths","sourceOffset":7346},{"kind":"section","title":"Arithmetic reduction and conventions","sourceOffset":15576},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Selmer groups and established inputs","sourceOffset":15935},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two auxiliary discriminants","sourceOffset":18478},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coefficient, character, and measure conventions","sourceOffset":23570},{"kind":"section","title":"A tame deformation and its Selmer length","sourceOffset":26769},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The undeformed Selmer spaces at the two places above \\texorpdfstring{$p$}{p}","sourceOffset":28333},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Primes with a controlled tame logarithm","sourceOffset":32696},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Admissible cochains and division by the parameter","sourceOffset":38498},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The first-order obstruction","sourceOffset":44761}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"The unrestricted low-corank Selmer converse","label":"thm:main","text":"For every elliptic curve $A_0/\\Q$, every prime $p$, and $r\\in\\{0,1\\}$, \\[ s_p(A_0)=r \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a(A_0)=\\rank_{\\Z}A_0(\\Q)=r, \\qquad \\#\\Sha(A_0/\\Q)<\\infty. \\] There is no restriction on reduction type, complex multiplication, the residual representation, rational torsion, or rational isogenies.","sourceOffset":1544,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Established converse at $2$","label":"thm:two-converse-input","text":"If $E/\\Q$ is any elliptic curve and $\\corank_{\\Z_2}\\Sel_{2^\\infty}(E/\\Q)=r\\in\\{0,1\\}$, then \\[ \\ord_{s=1}L(E,s)=\\rank E(\\Q)=r, \\qquad \\Sha(E/\\Q)\\text{ is finite}. \\]","sourceOffset":16958,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:admissible","text":"An admissible cochain with coefficients in $M_b^{\\eta}$ is a function on sequences of Galois elements which is the coefficientwise ultralimit of continuous finite-stage cochains, in the fixed basis of $V\\otimes R_b$, with one denominator $p^C$ independent of $i$. Finite-stage cochain identities are required modulo precisions $A_i\\longrightarrow\\infty$ along $\\cU$, with $A_i\\le n_i-c_b$. Equivalently, the limiting identity holds on every sequence of arguments. Cohomology is the cohomology of this cochain complex, so coboundaries are also required to be admissible. Local complexes are defined by","sourceOffset":39885,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":290493,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":19,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Selmer-converse-for-elliptic-curves-at-every-prime-October-7-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"002-03","slug":"The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026","familyId":"002","title":"The two-primary Birch–Swinnerton-Dyer formula in Selmer corank at most one","abstract":"We prove the two-primary Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer leading-term formula for every elliptic curve over the rationals whose two-power Selmer group has corank at most one. In this range, the algebraic rank, analytic rank, and Selmer corank are equal, and the Tate–Shafarevich group is finite. Combined with the quadratic-twist Selmer distribution, this gives the exact two-primary formula for a density-one set of signed squarefree twists of each fixed curve, ordered by absolute value; the common rank is zero or one, with each value having density one half.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the full Birch–Swinnerton-Dyer leading-term formula for every elliptic curve over ℚ whose full q-power Selmer group has corank zero or one for some prime q, including finiteness of the Tate–Shafarevich group. With result 006, this gives full BSD for a density-one set of quadratic twists of every elliptic curve over ℚ.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026/build/sections/02-classical.tex","preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026/build/sections/03-foundations.tex","preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026/build/sections/04-siegel.tex","preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026/build/sections/05-positive-determinant.tex","preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026/build/sections/06-tame.tex","preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026/build/sections/07-positive-completion.tex","preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026/build/sections/08-split-measures.tex","preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026/build/sections/09-split-local.tex","preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026/build/sections/10-reducible.tex","preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026/build/sections/11-cm.tex","preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026/build/sections/12-alternation-imaginary.tex","preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026/build/sections/13-real.tex","preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026/build/sections/14-cyclic.tex","preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026/build/sections/15-conclusion.tex","preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the two-primary Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer leading-term formula for every elliptic curve over the rationals whose two-power Selmer group has corank at most one. In this range, the algebraic rank, analytic rank, and Selmer corank are equal, and the Tate--Shafarevich group is finite. Combined with the quadratic-twist Selmer distribution, this gives the exact two-primary formula for a density-one set of signed squarefree twists of each fixed curve, ordered by absolute value; the common rank is zero or one, with each value having density one half.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":620},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Statement and normalizations","sourceOffset":1708},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the integral difficulty","sourceOffset":4840},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The three comparisons","sourceOffset":10473},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof structure and reusable constructions","sourceOffset":12355},{"kind":"section","title":"Classical facts and normalizations","sourceOffset":16704},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Analytic low-rank inputs and parity","sourceOffset":16771},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Gross--Zagier with absolute heights","sourceOffset":20220},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The split discrepancy identity","sourceOffset":23178},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite cochain models and determinant comparisons","sourceOffset":25580},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite models with marked arithmetic maps","sourceOffset":28236},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Localization and compatible duality","sourceOffset":35087}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(E/\\mathbb Q\\) be any elliptic curve with \\(s_2(E)\\leq1\\). Then \\[ r:=\\operatorname{rank}E(\\mathbb Q) =\\operatorname{ord}_{s=1}L(E,s)=s_2(E), \\qquad \\#\\Sha(E/\\mathbb Q)<\\infty. \\] Moreover, \\[ Q_E=\\frac{L^{(r)}(E,1)\\,(\\#T_E)^2} {r!\\,\\Omega_E\\Reg_E\\prod_{\\ell\\ \\mathrm{finite}}c_\\ell(E)} \\in\\mathbb Q_{>0}, \\qquad v_2(Q_E)=v_2\\bigl(\\#\\Sha(E/\\mathbb Q)\\bigr). \\] There is no restriction on \\(E[2]\\) or on the reduction of \\(E\\) at two.","sourceOffset":2989,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"An any-prime input criterion","label":"cor:any-prime-bsd","text":"Let \\(E/\\mathbb Q\\) be an elliptic curve. Suppose that, for some prime \\(p\\), its full \\(p\\)-power Selmer group with the usual local Kummer conditions at every place satisfies \\[ s_p(E):=\\corank_{\\mathbb Z_p}\\Sel_{p^\\infty}(E/\\mathbb Q)\\le1. \\] Then all conclusions of Theorem \\ref{thm:main} hold, with common rank \\(r=s_p(E)\\).","sourceOffset":3485,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Horizontal data","label":"tame:data","text":"A horizontal sequence consists of primes \\(r_i\\), integers \\(m_i\\to\\infty\\), and even surjective residue exponents \\[ \\lambda_i:(\\Z/r_i)^\\times\\longrightarrow\\Z/2^{m_i}, \\] with the following properties. The primes avoid the fixed data, \\(r_i\\equiv1\\pmod{ND}\\), \\(r_i\\to1\\) dyadically, and \\(r_i\\) splits in the Hilbert class field of \\(K\\) and in any fixed twisting field in use. The characters kill every prime of \\(S_f\\) and of the fixed smoothing integers \\(c,d\\), including \\(2\\). We may require them also to kill any specified finite list, in particular \\(3\\), the primes of \\(D\\), the tested i","sourceOffset":168133,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":633032,"sourceFileCount":17,"statementCount":52,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-two-primary-Birch-Swinnerton-Dyer-formula-in-Selmer-corank-at-most-one-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"003-01","slug":"The-Quasi-Riemann-Hypothesis-September-30-2026","familyId":"003","title":"The Quasi-Riemann Hypothesis: A Zero-Free Half-Plane $\\Re s\\gt 7/8$","abstract":"We prove that all finite-order Hecke L-functions over $\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{-3})$ and all Dirichlet L-functions are zero-free in the half-plane $\\Re s\\gt 7/8$, with the principal pole at s = 1 allowed. In particular, the Riemann zeta function is zero-free in this half-plane, proving the quasi-Riemann hypothesis.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every Dirichlet L-function, including $\\zeta(s)$, is zero-free in $\\Re s\\gt 7/8$, resolving the quasi-Riemann hypothesis. The same half-plane is zero-free for every finite-order Hecke L-function over $\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{-3})$. A companion gives a different proof of the zero-free half-plane $\\Re s\\gt 11/12$.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Quasi-Riemann-Hypothesis-September-30-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Quasi-Riemann-Hypothesis-September-30-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Quasi-Riemann-Hypothesis-September-30-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/The-Quasi-Riemann-Hypothesis-September-30-2026/build/paper.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Quasi-Riemann-Hypothesis-September-30-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that all finite-order Hecke $L$-functions over $\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{-3})$ and all Dirichlet $L$-functions are zero-free in the half-plane $\\Re s>7/8$, with the principal pole at $s=1$ allowed. In particular, the Riemann zeta function is zero-free in this half-plane, proving the quasi-Riemann hypothesis.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":385},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":7053},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Least nonresidues and square roots over prime fields","sourceOffset":15651},{"kind":"section","title":"From a common signal to a zero-free half-plane","sourceOffset":17660},{"kind":"part","title":"The quasi-Riemann hypothesis","sourceOffset":23883},{"kind":"section","title":"A first zero-free half-plane","sourceOffset":23949},{"kind":"section","title":"Arithmetic and analytic preliminaries","sourceOffset":25434},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Arithmetic notation and coefficient classes","sourceOffset":25954},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Gauss sums and the finite reciprocity phase","sourceOffset":36209},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Smooth norm profiles","sourceOffset":50460},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Growth and logarithmic control for Hecke functions","sourceOffset":65456},{"kind":"section","title":"Completed cubic reflection and unmarked row energy","sourceOffset":76162}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Every finite-order Hecke $L$-function over $F=\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{-3})$ has no zero in $\\Re s>7/8$. The same holds for every Dirichlet $L$-function, including $\\zeta(s)$. A pole at $s=1$ for a principal character is allowed.","sourceOffset":3362,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:nonresidues-square-roots","text":"There are absolute constants $A,C>0$ such that, for every odd prime $p$, the least positive quadratic nonresidue $n(p)$ satisfies \\[ n(p)\\le C(\\log p)^A. \\] In particular, $n(p)\\ll_\\delta p^\\delta$ for every $\\delta>0$, proving Vinogradov's least quadratic nonresidue conjecture \\cite[Conjecture 1.1]{TaoNonresidues}. Given an odd prime $p$ and $a\\in\\mathbb F_p$ in binary representation, there is a deterministic algorithm, with running time polynomial in $\\log p$, that returns a square root of $a$ or reports that none exists.","sourceOffset":15824,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"An unmarked reflected block","label":"def:unmarked-reflected-block","text":"Fix $Z\\ge2$, $X=Z^N$, one of the fixed characters $\\Psi_0$ and its common modulus $L$ from Proposition \\ref{prop:completed-reflection}, and put $M_{\\rm ref}=\\lambda^{12}L^4$. For the current norm over $R$, freeze a finite set $\\mathcal F$ of good primes with exponents $j_p\\in\\{0,\\ldots,5\\}$. Let $R$ range over squarefree primary good ideals coprime to every prime of $\\mathcal F$, in one fixed class modulo $M_{\\rm ref}^2$ and with any further fixed row restrictions. These restrictions are independent of the dual variables. On primary elements prime to $S$ set \\[ \\Psi_R(A)=\\Psi_0(A)\\prod_{p\\in\\m","sourceOffset":134074,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Data for an exact high representation","label":"def:stage-high-data","text":"Fix real numbers $l_x,l_y>0$ and $\\ell\\ge0$ such that \\begin{equation} X=Z^{l_x},\\qquad Y=Z^{l_y},\\qquad h=1-l_x+\\ell>0, \\qquad C(s)=s+\\frac{l_x}{2}-1+\\frac h6 =s-\\frac56+\\frac{l_x}{3}+\\frac\\ell6. \\end{equation} These real numbers are fixed before the target character. For each primitive finite-order target $\\eta$, fix the data $S,\\xi,W_0,W_1$ of Section \\ref{sec:probe}, independently of $Z$. Data for an exact high representation consist of the following objects and assertions. First, $\\mathscr I_\\eta(Z)$ is an independently specified complex-valued quantity for every sufficiently large real $","sourceOffset":253842,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/003.md","scopeText":"The quasi-Riemann hypothesis asks for a fixed zero-free half-plane $\\Re s>\\theta$ with $\\theta<1$. The formalization gives $\\theta=7/8$ for the Riemann zeta function and every Dirichlet $L$-function, uniformly over all positive moduli and all characters. It also establishes the same bound for finite-order Hecke $L$-functions over $\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{-3})$.\n\nThe principal-character poles at $s=1$ are excluded in the Dirichlet and Hecke statements. The paper's later applications are not included.\n\nThe formalized result gives a uniform logarithmic exclusion region for Landau–Siegel zeros. There is one constant $c>0$ such that every primitive nonprincipal real Dirichlet character of conductor $q\\ge3$ and every real zero $0<\\beta<1$ of its $L$-function satisfy $1-\\beta\\ge c/\\log q$.\n\nBoth characte","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/003.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":765096,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":17,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Quasi-Riemann-Hypothesis-September-30-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"003-02","slug":"The-Quasi-Riemann-Hypothesis-October-5-2026","familyId":"003","title":"The Quasi-Riemann Hypothesis (alternate 11/12 proof)","abstract":"We establish the quasi-Riemann hypothesis by proving that every Dirichlet L-function, including Riemann's zeta function, has no zeros in the half-plane $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Re}}\\nolimits s\\gt 11/12$. More generally, we prove the same zero-free half-plane for every finite-order Hecke L-function over $K=\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{-3})$. In particular, this rules out the existence of Landau–Siegel zeros.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every Dirichlet L-function, including $\\zeta(s)$, is zero-free in $\\Re s\\gt 7/8$, resolving the quasi-Riemann hypothesis. The same half-plane is zero-free for every finite-order Hecke L-function over $\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{-3})$. A companion gives a different proof of the zero-free half-plane $\\Re s\\gt 11/12$.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Quasi-Riemann-Hypothesis-October-5-2026/paper2.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Quasi-Riemann-Hypothesis-October-5-2026/paper2.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Quasi-Riemann-Hypothesis-October-5-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/The-Quasi-Riemann-Hypothesis-October-5-2026/build/paper2.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Quasi-Riemann-Hypothesis-October-5-2026/build/paper2.tex","sourceAbstract":"We establish the quasi-Riemann hypothesis by proving that every Dirichlet $L$-function, including Riemann's zeta function, has no zeros in the half-plane $\\Rea s>11/12$. More generally, we prove the same zero-free half-plane for every finite-order Hecke $L$-function over $K=\\Q(\\sqrt{-3})$. In particular, this rules out the existence of Landau--Siegel zeros.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":423},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work","sourceOffset":4808},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":7041},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Notation","sourceOffset":7867},{"kind":"section","title":"Outline of the argument","sourceOffset":9378},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Step 1: Reduction to power-saving estimates for twisted M\\\"obius sums","sourceOffset":9671},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Step 2: Embedding the sum in a family","sourceOffset":11295},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Step 3: Poisson summation","sourceOffset":14739},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Step 4: \\texorpdfstring{Relation}{Relation} to cubic Gauss sums and Kubota's cubic theta function","sourceOffset":18739},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Step 5: Removing the cube factors","sourceOffset":24930},{"kind":"section","title":"From the mean-square estimate to the zero-free region","sourceOffset":35642},{"kind":"section","title":"Poisson summation and the dual mean square","sourceOffset":40526}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Every finite-order Hecke $L$-function over $K$ has no zeros in the half-plane $\\Rea s>11/12$. Thus every Dirichlet $L$-function, including $\\zeta(s)$, has no zeros for $\\Rea s>11/12$.","sourceOffset":1669,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:primes-ap","text":"Let $\\pi(x;q,a)$ count the primes $p\\le x$ with $p\\equiv a\\pmod q$. Write $\\varphi$ for Euler's totient function. For $x\\ge2$, \\[ \\sup_{\\substack{1\\le q\\le x\\\\(a,q)=1}} \\biggl|\\pi(x;q,a)-\\frac1{\\varphi(q)} \\int_2^x\\frac{\\dd t}{\\log t}\\biggr| \\ll x^{11/12}\\log x, \\] where the implied constant is absolute and effective.","sourceOffset":2461,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"eq:energy","text":"The parameters $\\mathcal H$, $X$, and $F$ are the norm scales of $k$, $n$, and $f$, respectively. Here $k$ ranges over elements of $\\OO$, and a star restricts a sum to squarefree ideals prime to $S$, represented by their primary generators. For a smooth compactly supported weight $W$ on $(0,\\infty)$, define the dual mean square by \\begin{equation} \\begin{aligned} &\\E(\\mathcal{H},X,F;\\xi,W)\\\\ &\\qquad=\\frac1{XF} \\sum_{\\substack{F\\le\\NK(f)<2F\\\\(f,S)=1}}^*\\sum_{0<\\NK(k)\\le \\mathcal{H}} \\Bigl|\\sum_{(n,S)=1}^*a_\\xi(n)\\chi_n(k)\\chi_n(f)^4 W(\\NK(n)/X)\\Bigr|^2. \\end{aligned} \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":47877,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/003.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":167473,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Quasi-Riemann-Hypothesis-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"003-03","slug":"Uniform-exclusion-of-Landau-Siegel-zeros-October-1-2026","familyId":"003","title":"Uniform exclusion of Landau–Siegel zeros","abstract":"We prove the uniform exclusion of Landau–Siegel zeros. There is an absolute constant c > 0 such that every real zero $\\beta\\in(0,1)$ of every primitive nonprincipal real Dirichlet L-function of conductor q ≥ 3 satisfies $(1-\\beta)\\log q\\ge c$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every Dirichlet L-function, including $\\zeta(s)$, is zero-free in $\\Re s\\gt 7/8$, resolving the quasi-Riemann hypothesis. The same half-plane is zero-free for every finite-order Hecke L-function over $\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{-3})$. A companion gives a different proof of the zero-free half-plane $\\Re s\\gt 11/12$.","pdfPath":"preprints/Uniform-exclusion-of-Landau-Siegel-zeros-October-1-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-exclusion-of-Landau-Siegel-zeros-October-1-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-exclusion-of-Landau-Siegel-zeros-October-1-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Uniform-exclusion-of-Landau-Siegel-zeros-October-1-2026/build/paper.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Uniform-exclusion-of-Landau-Siegel-zeros-October-1-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the uniform exclusion of Landau--Siegel zeros. There is an absolute constant $c>0$ such that every real zero $\\beta\\in(0,1)$ of every primitive nonprincipal real Dirichlet $L$-function of conductor $q\\ge3$ satisfies $(1-\\beta)\\log q\\ge c$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":294},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The argument","sourceOffset":2281},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Interpolation and arithmetic context","sourceOffset":4542},{"kind":"section","title":"The prime bias forced by a real zero","sourceOffset":6402},{"kind":"section","title":"Interpolation on a projected integer box","sourceOffset":8969},{"kind":"section","title":"A weighted determinant of conjugates","sourceOffset":16442},{"kind":"section","title":"Two bounds for the determinant","sourceOffset":20499},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Archimedean bound","sourceOffset":20928},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The prime divisibility","sourceOffset":21471},{"kind":"section","title":"Completion of the proof","sourceOffset":24775}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is an absolute constant $c>0$ such that every real zero $\\beta\\in(0,1)$ of every primitive nonprincipal real Dirichlet $L$-function of conductor $q\\ge3$ satisfies \\begin{equation} (1-\\beta)\\log q\\ge c. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":877,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:rectangle","text":"Let $A$ satisfy the hypotheses of Lemma \\ref{lem:interpolation}. For integers $1\\le H\\le N$, set \\[ U=N^{4/3},\\qquad T_1=32H^{2/3}U, \\qquad T_2=T_3=32H^{-1/3}U. \\] The rows $((An)_1^{\\alpha_1}(An)_2^{\\alpha_2}(An)_3^{\\alpha_3})_{n\\in E_N}$, with $\\alpha\\in\\N^3$ and $\\alpha_j\\le T_j$, span $\\C^{E_N}$. The constant $32$ is independent of $A$ and $H$.","sourceOffset":15198,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/003.md","scopeText":"The quasi-Riemann hypothesis asks for a fixed zero-free half-plane $\\Re s>\\theta$ with $\\theta<1$. The formalization gives $\\theta=7/8$ for the Riemann zeta function and every Dirichlet $L$-function, uniformly over all positive moduli and all characters. It also establishes the same bound for finite-order Hecke $L$-functions over $\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{-3})$.\n\nThe principal-character poles at $s=1$ are excluded in the Dirichlet and Hecke statements. The paper's later applications are not included.\n\nThe formalized result gives a uniform logarithmic exclusion region for Landau–Siegel zeros. There is one constant $c>0$ such that every primitive nonprincipal real Dirichlet character of conductor $q\\ge3$ and every real zero $0<\\beta<1$ of its $L$-function satisfy $1-\\beta\\ge c/\\log q$.\n\nBoth characte","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/003.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":29805,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Uniform-exclusion-of-Landau-Siegel-zeros-October-1-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"004-01","slug":"Hilberts-tenth-problem-over-the-rational-numbers-October-6-2026","familyId":"004","title":"Hilbert’s tenth problem over the rational numbers","abstract":"We give a negative answer to Hilbert's tenth problem over the rational numbers: no algorithm decides whether a polynomial with integer coefficients has a rational zero. The number of variables is part of the input.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that no algorithm decides whether an integer-coefficient polynomial in an arbitrary number of variables has a rational zero, resolving Hilbert's tenth problem over ℚ negatively.","pdfPath":"preprints/Hilberts-tenth-problem-over-the-rational-numbers-October-6-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Hilberts-tenth-problem-over-the-rational-numbers-October-6-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Hilberts-tenth-problem-over-the-rational-numbers-October-6-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Hilberts-tenth-problem-over-the-rational-numbers-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Hilberts-tenth-problem-over-the-rational-numbers-October-6-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Hilberts-tenth-problem-over-the-rational-numbers-October-6-2026/build/sections/02-reduction.tex","preprints/Hilberts-tenth-problem-over-the-rational-numbers-October-6-2026/build/figures/reduction-overview.tex","preprints/Hilberts-tenth-problem-over-the-rational-numbers-October-6-2026/build/sections/03-parity.tex","preprints/Hilberts-tenth-problem-over-the-rational-numbers-October-6-2026/build/sections/04-height.tex","preprints/Hilberts-tenth-problem-over-the-rational-numbers-October-6-2026/build/sections/05-elliptic.tex","preprints/Hilberts-tenth-problem-over-the-rational-numbers-October-6-2026/build/sections/06-primes.tex","preprints/Hilberts-tenth-problem-over-the-rational-numbers-October-6-2026/build/sections/07-consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Hilberts-tenth-problem-over-the-rational-numbers-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a negative answer to Hilbert's tenth problem over the rational numbers: no algorithm decides whether a polynomial with integer coefficients has a rational zero. The number of variables is part of the input.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":332},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical setting","sourceOffset":759},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":9484},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite rational tests for integer solvability","sourceOffset":16308},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Elliptic indices and the local comparison","sourceOffset":17003},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Contact primes and ordinary integer witnesses","sourceOffset":25788},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A recursive theory with an ordinary integer model","sourceOffset":31048},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite rational tests and compactness","sourceOffset":37403},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Odd valuations select a tested branch","sourceOffset":43604},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Index congruences at every contact prime","sourceOffset":49525},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The height contradiction","sourceOffset":51047},{"kind":"section","title":"An existential condition excluding odd poles","sourceOffset":59472}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is no algorithm which, given a polynomial $f\\in\\Z[X_1,\\ldots,X_n]$, with $n$ part of the input, decides whether $f$ has a zero in $\\Q^n$.","sourceOffset":568,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"ell:rank","text":"The group $E(F)$ has rank one. Consequently there exist a positive integer $m$ and a nontorsion point $P\\in E(F)$ such that \\[ mE(F)=\\Z P. \\] We fix such $m$ and $P$ once and for all.","sourceOffset":17409,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":204289,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Hilberts-tenth-problem-over-the-rational-numbers-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"004-02","slug":"A-pointwise-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-with-rational-two-torsion-October-7-2026","familyId":"004","title":"A pointwise 2-converse for elliptic curves with rational two-torsion","abstract":"We prove a pointwise 2-converse for elliptic curves over $\\mathbf Q$ with nonzero rational two-torsion: if the $2^\\infty$-Selmer corank is zero or one, then the analytic rank and Mordell–Weil rank equal that corank, and the Shafarevich–Tate group is finite. The result allows arbitrary reduction at 2.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that no algorithm decides whether an integer-coefficient polynomial in an arbitrary number of variables has a rational zero, resolving Hilbert's tenth problem over ℚ negatively.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-pointwise-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-with-rational-two-torsion-October-7-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-pointwise-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-with-rational-two-torsion-October-7-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-pointwise-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-with-rational-two-torsion-October-7-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/A-pointwise-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-with-rational-two-torsion-October-7-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-pointwise-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-with-rational-two-torsion-October-7-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-pointwise-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-with-rational-two-torsion-October-7-2026/build/sections/proof-roadmap-figure.tex","preprints/A-pointwise-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-with-rational-two-torsion-October-7-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/A-pointwise-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-with-rational-two-torsion-October-7-2026/build/sections/cyclotomic.tex","preprints/A-pointwise-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-with-rational-two-torsion-October-7-2026/build/sections/ring-limits.tex","preprints/A-pointwise-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-with-rational-two-torsion-October-7-2026/build/sections/ring-determinants.tex","preprints/A-pointwise-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-with-rational-two-torsion-October-7-2026/build/sections/nonvanishing.tex","preprints/A-pointwise-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-with-rational-two-torsion-October-7-2026/build/sections/coefficients.tex","preprints/A-pointwise-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-with-rational-two-torsion-October-7-2026/build/sections/graphs.tex","preprints/A-pointwise-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-with-rational-two-torsion-October-7-2026/build/sections/pointwise.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-pointwise-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-with-rational-two-torsion-October-7-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a pointwise $2$-converse for elliptic curves over $\\mathbf Q$ with nonzero rational two-torsion: if the $2^\\infty$-Selmer corank is zero or one, then the analytic rank and Mordell--Weil rank equal that corank, and the Shafarevich--Tate group is finite. The result allows arbitrary reduction at $2$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":406},{"kind":"section","title":"Selmer coranks and fixed local data","sourceOffset":11632},{"kind":"section","title":"Cyclotomic interpolation for extensions of trivial residual modules","sourceOffset":17983},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The integral scalar family and two algebraic steps","sourceOffset":20621},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The positive global complex","sourceOffset":25134},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fixed-form zeta classes and the integral coefficient map","sourceOffset":33209},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rational divisibility and the local Euler corrections","sourceOffset":38770},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The determinant and its central specialization","sourceOffset":47604},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proofs of the interpolation theorems","sourceOffset":55086},{"kind":"section","title":"Ring-class interpolation and integral deformations","sourceOffset":56696},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Free models with maps to the original cochains","sourceOffset":61675},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Kummer conditions, including their derived degree zero","sourceOffset":70536}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"The rational-two-torsion $2$-converse","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $E/\\Q$ be an elliptic curve with $E(\\Q)[2]\\ne0$ and $s_2(E)\\in\\{0,1\\}$. Then \\[ \\ord_{s=1}L(E,s)=\\rank_{\\Z}E(\\Q)=s_2(E), \\qquad \\Sha(E/\\Q)\\text{ is finite}. \\]","sourceOffset":1347,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Cyclotomic lower bound","label":"cy:lower-bound","text":"There is a constant $C_E$ such that, for every allowed $h$ with $L(E^{(h)},1)\\ne0$, \\[ v_2(\\mathcal L(h))\\ \\geq\\ 2w(h)+s(h)-C_E. \\] The constant is independent of the number and sizes of the primes dividing $h$.","sourceOffset":19568,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Witnesses and permitted partitions","label":"gr:partitionable","text":"A witness is a finite graph on which the symbol is $1$. A graph is partitionable for the symbol if its vertices can be partitioned into nonempty clusters, each with at most one simple vertex, with a choice of oriented contraction trees whose contracted graph is a witness. This definition includes a specified contraction certificate; it makes no assertion about a Selmer group.","sourceOffset":263038,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":340587,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":25,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-pointwise-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-with-rational-two-torsion-October-7-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-pointwise-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-with-rational-two-torsion-October-7-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/A-pointwise-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-with-rational-two-torsion-October-7-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"005-01","slug":"Catalans-constant-is-irrational-September-24-2026","familyId":"005","title":"Catalan's constant is irrational","abstract":"We prove that Catalan's constant $G=\\sum_{j\\geq0}(-1)^j/(2j+1)^2$ is irrational.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that Catalan's constant $G=\\sum_{j\\ge0}(-1)^j/(2j+1)^2$ is irrational.","pdfPath":"preprints/Catalans-constant-is-irrational-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Catalans-constant-is-irrational-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Catalans-constant-is-irrational-September-24-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Catalans-constant-is-irrational-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Catalans-constant-is-irrational-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Catalans-constant-is-irrational-September-24-2026/build/sections/foundations.tex","preprints/Catalans-constant-is-irrational-September-24-2026/build/sections/odd-primes.tex","preprints/Catalans-constant-is-irrational-September-24-2026/build/sections/nonvanishing.tex","preprints/Catalans-constant-is-irrational-September-24-2026/build/sections/real-place.tex","preprints/Catalans-constant-is-irrational-September-24-2026/build/sections/energy.tex","preprints/Catalans-constant-is-irrational-September-24-2026/build/sections/certificate.tex","preprints/Catalans-constant-is-irrational-September-24-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Catalans-constant-is-irrational-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that Catalan's constant $G=\\sum_{j\\geq0}(-1)^j/(2j+1)^2$ is irrational.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":127},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier results and the approximation problem","sourceOffset":826},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The determinant strategy","sourceOffset":5447},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and conventions","sourceOffset":10171},{"kind":"section","title":"Polynomial rows and mixed moments","sourceOffset":11301},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Moment evaluation and cancellation","sourceOffset":14804},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A uniform estimate at the prime two","sourceOffset":21296},{"kind":"section","title":"Odd primes and the finite-place lower bound","sourceOffset":28339},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Digit reduction of the rational moments","sourceOffset":29398},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The leading layer and paired raw columns","sourceOffset":37383},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The central layer and integral column elimination","sourceOffset":40651},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Summing the prime losses","sourceOffset":48078}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Catalan's constant is irrational.","sourceOffset":745,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:rationality","text":"Each raw entry $F_j(r)$ lies in $\\mathbb Q+\\mathbb QG$. Consequently, if $G\\in\\mathbb Q$, then $\\Delta_N\\in\\mathbb Q$ for every positive integer $N$.","sourceOffset":20561,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/005.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves that Catalan's constant $G=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}(-1)^j/(2j+1)^2$ is irrational. This is the mathematical assertion in the paper's title.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/005.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":121833,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Catalans-constant-is-irrational-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"006-01","slug":"Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026","familyId":"006","title":"Goldfeld's analytic density conjecture and the 2-converse for elliptic curves","abstract":"We prove Goldfeld's analytic density conjecture: for every elliptic curve E over ℚ, the quadratic twists of E with analytic rank zero and one each have density 1/2 among signed squarefree twist parameters ordered by absolute value. We also prove the low-corank 2-converse: if the $2^\\infty$-Selmer corank of E is zero or one, then it equals the analytic and Mordell–Weil ranks, and the Tate–Shafarevich group is finite.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Goldfeld's conjecture for quadratic twists of every elliptic curve over ℚ: analytic ranks zero and one each have density 1/2, and the mean analytic rank tends to 1/2. Both statements order signed squarefree twist parameters by absolute value.","pdfPath":"preprints/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026/build/sections/01-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026/build/sections/01-arithmetic-transfer.tex","preprints/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026/build/sections/05-coefficients.tex","preprints/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026/build/sections/06-traces.tex","preprints/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026/build/sections/07-prime-graphs.tex","preprints/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026/build/sections/08-rational-converse.tex","preprints/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026/build/sections/09-local-matrices.tex","preprints/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026/build/sections/10-irreducible-converse.tex","preprints/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026/build/sections/01-nonvanishing.tex","preprints/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026/build/sections/02-binary-algebra.tex","preprints/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026/build/sections/04-cyclotomic.tex","preprints/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026/build/sections/03-heegner.tex","preprints/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026/build/sections/11-conclusions.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Goldfeld's analytic density conjecture: for every elliptic curve $E$ over $\\Q$, the quadratic twists of $E$ with analytic rank zero and one each have density $1/2$ among signed squarefree twist parameters ordered by absolute value. We also prove the low-corank $2$-converse: if the $2^\\infty$-Selmer corank of $E$ is zero or one, then it equals the analytic and Mordell--Weil ranks, and the Tate--Shafarevich group is finite.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":508},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Consequences and companion results","sourceOffset":2624},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Goldfeld's conjecture and analytic nonvanishing","sourceOffset":4692},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Selmer converse problem","sourceOffset":8033},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":11885},{"kind":"section","title":"Selmer ranks and local conventions","sourceOffset":17619},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite lengths and twisting data","sourceOffset":21320},{"kind":"section","title":"Arithmetic estimates for finite twist families","sourceOffset":26164},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Transfer of a central value","sourceOffset":28756},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Transfer of a Heegner point","sourceOffset":30935},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Lower bounds and the coefficient constructions","sourceOffset":36803},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Auxiliary twists in prescribed local classes","sourceOffset":41245}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"The $2$-converse","label":"thm:main-converse","text":"For every elliptic curve $E/\\Q$ with $c_2(E)\\in\\{0,1\\}$, \\[ a(E)=r(E)=c_2(E),\\qquad \\Sha(E/\\Q)\\text{ is finite}. \\]","sourceOffset":1025,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Goldfeld's analytic density conjecture","label":"thm:main-density","text":"For every elliptic curve $E/\\Q$ and each $j\\in\\{0,1\\}$, \\[ \\lim_{X\\to\\infty} \\frac{\\#\\{d\\in\\mathcal D(X):a(E^{(d)})=j\\}}{\\#\\mathcal D(X)} =\\frac12. \\] Consequently the twists of analytic rank at least two have density zero. For a density-one set of signed squarefree parameters $d$, the analytic and Mordell--Weil ranks of $E^{(d)}$ agree and its whole Tate--Shafarevich group is finite.","sourceOffset":1819,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Witnesses and permitted partitions","label":"gr:partitionable","text":"A witness is a finite graph with symbol $1$. A graph is partitionable if it admits a partition into nonempty clusters, each containing at most one simple vertex, and oriented contraction trees whose contracted graph is a witness. The contraction trees are part of the data; this definition makes no assertion about a Selmer group.","sourceOffset":139985,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:h:determinant-coordinate","text":"Choose an integral basis of $\\det^{-1}_{\\Lambda}C$. In generic rank one, the element formed by $P$ in $H^1$ and the functional $\\langle P,\\cdot\\rangle$ on $H^2$ determines a scalar $u(C,P)\\in\\operatorname{Frac}(\\Lambda)$ in that basis. Put $u=0$ if $P=0$ or the generic rank is zero. Integral changes of determinant basis multiply $u$ by a unit.","sourceOffset":381738,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":461005,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":31,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"006-02","slug":"The-mean-analytic-rank-of-quadratic-twists-of-elliptic-curves-October-6-2026","familyId":"006","title":"The mean analytic rank of quadratic twists of elliptic curves","abstract":"For every elliptic curve over ℚ, we prove that the average analytic rank of its quadratic twists tends to 1/2 when signed squarefree twist parameters are ordered by absolute value. This resolves Goldfeld's mean analytic-rank conjecture in this counting convention.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Goldfeld's conjecture for quadratic twists of every elliptic curve over ℚ: analytic ranks zero and one each have density 1/2, and the mean analytic rank tends to 1/2. Both statements order signed squarefree twist parameters by absolute value.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-mean-analytic-rank-of-quadratic-twists-of-elliptic-curves-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-mean-analytic-rank-of-quadratic-twists-of-elliptic-curves-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-mean-analytic-rank-of-quadratic-twists-of-elliptic-curves-October-6-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/The-mean-analytic-rank-of-quadratic-twists-of-elliptic-curves-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-mean-analytic-rank-of-quadratic-twists-of-elliptic-curves-October-6-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-mean-analytic-rank-of-quadratic-twists-of-elliptic-curves-October-6-2026/build/sections/setup.tex","preprints/The-mean-analytic-rank-of-quadratic-twists-of-elliptic-curves-October-6-2026/build/sections/analytic.tex","preprints/The-mean-analytic-rank-of-quadratic-twists-of-elliptic-curves-October-6-2026/build/sections/weights.tex","preprints/The-mean-analytic-rank-of-quadratic-twists-of-elliptic-curves-October-6-2026/build/sections/model.tex","preprints/The-mean-analytic-rank-of-quadratic-twists-of-elliptic-curves-October-6-2026/build/sections/tail.tex","preprints/The-mean-analytic-rank-of-quadratic-twists-of-elliptic-curves-October-6-2026/build/sections/algebraic.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-mean-analytic-rank-of-quadratic-twists-of-elliptic-curves-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every elliptic curve over \\(\\Q\\), we prove that the average analytic rank of its quadratic twists tends to \\(1/2\\) when signed squarefree twist parameters are ordered by absolute value. This resolves Goldfeld's mean analytic-rank conjecture in this counting convention.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":320},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Algebraic-rank moments","sourceOffset":3126},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":4274},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The analytic argument","sourceOffset":9916},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":13631},{"kind":"section","title":"Twist families and the integer model","sourceOffset":14160},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite family closed under reduction","sourceOffset":17187},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Complete integer averages","sourceOffset":21034},{"kind":"section","title":"Poisson summation and logarithmic second moments","sourceOffset":22067},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The normalized Gauss sums","sourceOffset":25558},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A weighted Poisson estimate","sourceOffset":32332},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Closing a logarithmic second-moment induction","sourceOffset":37457}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"proposition","name":"Independent-model estimates for the weight pieces","label":"model:main-model-estimates","text":"With the weights and sums $S_{j,Z}(x)$ defined in Section \\ref{sec:weights}, there is a fixed exponent $C_5$ such that \\begin{align} \\|H_0(Z)S_{0,Z}(x)-A_x\\|_2&\\ll k^{-1}, \\\\ \\|H_j(Z)S_{j,Z}(x)\\|_2&\\ll(k2^j)^{C_5}\\delta_j^k \\qquad(1\\le j\\le J). \\end{align} These bounds are uniform in $x\\in\\mathcal X$ and over the entire range $k_0\\le k\\le\\ell^{3/5}$, with one fixed $k_0$ and one sufficiently large $X$ threshold.","sourceOffset":108006,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:mean","text":"For every elliptic curve \\(E/\\Q\\), \\[ \\lim_{Y\\to\\infty}\\frac{1}{\\#\\mathcal D(Y)} \\sum_{d\\in\\mathcal D(Y)}a(E^{(d)})=\\frac12. \\]","sourceOffset":1228,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":124133,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-mean-analytic-rank-of-quadratic-twists-of-elliptic-curves-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"007-01","slug":"Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026","familyId":"007","title":"Ordinary two-point correlations of multiplicative functions","abstract":"We prove the ordinary two-point Chowla conjecture. For every fixed pair of nonproportional affine forms, the Liouville correlation has a power-of-logarithm saving at every cutoff, with an absolute exponent. We also prove the binary corrected Elliott conjecture for ordinary averages of complex multiplicative functions of modulus at most one, under uniform nonpretentiousness of at least one original factor. This qualitative conclusion holds in fixed residue classes and for fixed nonproportional affine forms.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the ordinary two-point Chowla conjecture, with a bound $O(X/(\\log X)^c)$ for Liouville correlation sums along fixed nonproportional affine forms, where c > 0 is absolute. More generally, proves the binary corrected Elliott conjecture for complex multiplicative functions bounded by one when one factor is uniformly nonpretentious against each fixed Dirichlet character times $n^{it}$ for $|t|\\le X$.","pdfPath":"preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/final.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/final.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/build/quantitative/01-setup.tex","preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/build/quantitative/02-finite-law.tex","preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/build/quantitative/03-analytic.tex","preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/build/quantitative/04-paths-trace.tex","preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/build/quantitative/05-rank-witness.tex","preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/build/quantitative/06-forest.tex","preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/build/quantitative/07-spectral.tex","preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/build/quantitative/08-affine.tex","preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/build/qualitative/graph-setup.tex","preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/build/qualitative/analytic-transfer.tex","preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/build/qualitative/paths.tex","preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/build/qualitative/thinning.tex","preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/build/qualitative/cylinder-sieve.tex","preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/build/qualitative/trace-count.tex","preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/build/qualitative/localization.tex","preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/build/qualitative/affine-corollaries.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the ordinary two-point Chowla conjecture. For every fixed pair of nonproportional affine forms, the Liouville correlation has a power-of-logarithm saving at every cutoff, with an absolute exponent. We also prove the binary corrected Elliott conjecture for ordinary averages of complex multiplicative functions of modulus at most one, under uniform nonpretentiousness of at least one original factor. This qualitative conclusion holds in fixed residue classes and for fixed nonproportional affine forms.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":574},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and prior work","sourceOffset":4837},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Main ideas","sourceOffset":9321},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":13443},{"kind":"part","title":"A logarithmic saving for Liouville correlations","sourceOffset":14640},{"kind":"section","title":"Prime supplies and the quantitative target","sourceOffset":14696},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Centered primes and padding primes","sourceOffset":16428},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Short multiplicative intervals","sourceOffset":18862},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Centered divisor sums","sourceOffset":20219},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The independent residue space","sourceOffset":21423},{"kind":"section","title":"Comparing finite integer averages with independent residues","sourceOffset":22406},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Low-degree residue states and truncated weights","sourceOffset":30424}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"A logarithmic saving for affine Liouville correlations","label":"thm:q-affine","text":"There is an absolute constant \\(c>0\\) such that, for every fixed choice of integers \\(a_1,a_2\\ge1\\) and \\(b_1,b_2\\ge0\\) with \\(a_1b_2-a_2b_1\\ne0\\), there is a constant \\(C_{a_1,a_2,b_1,b_2}\\) for which \\[ \\left|\\sum_{1\\le n\\le X} \\lambda(a_1n+b_1)\\lambda(a_2n+b_2)\\right| \\le C_{a_1,a_2,b_1,b_2}\\frac{X}{(\\log X)^c} \\qquad(X\\ge3). \\] The cutoff \\(X\\) may be any real number. No coprimality condition is imposed on the coefficients.","sourceOffset":1399,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Binary corrected Elliott","label":"thm:elliott","text":"Let \\(f_1,f_2:\\N\\to\\mathbb D\\) be multiplicative, and suppose that at least one satisfies \\eqref{eq:unp}. For every fixed pair of distinct nonnegative integers \\(h_1,h_2\\), \\[ \\frac1N\\sum_{n=1}^N f_1(n+h_1)f_2(n+h_2)\\longrightarrow0 \\qquad(N\\longrightarrow\\infty,\\ N\\in\\N). \\]","sourceOffset":3277,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"q:prohibited","text":"A numerical word is forward prohibited if it has length $3\\le m\\le s$ and satisfies three conditions: \\begin{enumerate} \\item Consecutive whole tuples $d_i,d_{i+1}$ are unequal. \\item For every prime in $P$, its indices of appearance among $d_1,\\ldots,d_m$ form an interval, if nonempty. \\item Some prime $p\\mid d_1$, absent from $d_m$, satisfies \\begin{equation} p\\mid\\sum_{i=a}^m D_i\\qquad\\text{for some }1<a<m. \\end{equation} \\end{enumerate} A forward prohibited word is minimal if no shorter contiguous subword is forward prohibited in either orientation. A witness is a positive path realizing a","sourceOffset":58672,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Path specifications","label":"def:primitive-specification","text":"A specification consists of a path with distinct integer offsets $z_0=0,z_1,\\ldots,z_m$, where $1\\le m\\le L$ and \\[ z_i-z_{i-1}=\\varepsilon_i h e_i, \\qquad e_i\\in\\mathcal D,\\quad \\varepsilon_i\\in\\{-1,1\\}, \\] an extra prime $p\\in\\mathcal S$ dividing none of the $e_i$, and an index $i_0\\in\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$ such that $p\\mid z_m-z_{i_0-1}$. It qualifies at $x$ when \\[ p\\mid x,\\qquad e_i\\mid x+z_{i-1}\\quad(1\\le i\\le m). \\] Its prime support $\\Pi$ is the union of $\\{p\\}$ and the prime factors of all its edge labels. Its cylinder is the set of starting integers $x$ satisfying these qualification condit","sourceOffset":165916,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/007.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves ordinary two-point cancellation for multiplicative functions. For the Liouville function on every fixed pair of nonproportional affine forms, the correlation sum up to $X$ is $O(X/(\\log X)^c)$ for an absolute $c>0$, with the implied constant depending on the forms.\n\nFor two one-bounded multiplicative functions, if at least one is uniformly nonpretentious against all Dirichlet-character twists with frequency $|t|\\le N$, then their shifted and nonproportional affine correlation sums divided by $N$ tend to zero. These are ordinary averages, with no logarithmic averaging.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/007.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":272006,"sourceFileCount":18,"statementCount":17,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Ordinary-two-point-correlations-of-multiplicative-functions-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"008-01","slug":"The-Deligne-Drinfeld-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"008","title":"The Deligne-Drinfeld conjecture","abstract":"We prove the Deligne–Drinfeld conjecture: the rational Grothendieck–Teichmüller Lie algebra is freely generated by one element in every odd weight at least three, with the corresponding isomorphism after weight completion.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the rational Grothendieck–Teichmüller Lie algebra, with the Ihara bracket, is freely generated by one element in each odd weight 3, 5, 7, …, resolving the Deligne–Drinfeld conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Deligne-Drinfeld-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Deligne-Drinfeld-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Deligne-Drinfeld-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/The-Deligne-Drinfeld-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Deligne-Drinfeld-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-Deligne-Drinfeld-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-Deligne-Drinfeld-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-algebra.tex","preprints/The-Deligne-Drinfeld-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-degeneration.tex","preprints/The-Deligne-Drinfeld-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-deletion.tex","preprints/The-Deligne-Drinfeld-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/deletion-block.tex","preprints/The-Deligne-Drinfeld-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-image.tex","preprints/The-Deligne-Drinfeld-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-category.tex","preprints/The-Deligne-Drinfeld-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/reassociation.tex","preprints/The-Deligne-Drinfeld-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-holonomy.tex","preprints/The-Deligne-Drinfeld-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-induction.tex","preprints/The-Deligne-Drinfeld-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Deligne-Drinfeld-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Deligne--Drinfeld conjecture: the rational Grothendieck--Teichm\\\"uller Lie algebra is freely generated by one element in every odd weight at least three, with the corresponding isomorphism after weight completion.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":289},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The statement","sourceOffset":838},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and the main estimate","sourceOffset":3088},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":6211},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and conventions","sourceOffset":9106},{"kind":"section","title":"Integral Lie algebras and the special identity","sourceOffset":9833},{"kind":"section","title":"A filtered degeneration of the pentagon","sourceOffset":27861},{"kind":"section","title":"Deletion operators and injectivity of the leading projection","sourceOffset":46736},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The integral deletion representation","sourceOffset":47181},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Detecting mixed terms","sourceOffset":55183},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Excluding the residual kernel","sourceOffset":63106},{"kind":"section","title":"The image of the leading projection","sourceOffset":68525}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are homogeneous elements \\(\\sigma_{2k+1}\\in W_{2k+1}\\), one for each \\(k\\ge1\\), for which \\[ \\Lie_{\\Q}\\langle e_3,e_5,e_7,\\ldots\\rangle \\longrightarrow (W,\\{\\, ,\\,\\}),\\qquad e_{2k+1}\\longmapsto\\sigma_{2k+1}, \\] is an isomorphism of graded Lie algebras. In particular, \\(W\\) is closed under the bracket \\eqref{eq:ihara}. The induced map on completions by weight is a continuous graded isomorphism.","sourceOffset":2404,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Saturated reduction","label":"prop:saturated-reduction","text":"Let \\[ K_n=\\Lie_R\\langle x,y\\rangle_n, \\qquad L_n=W_n\\cap K_n. \\] Then \\(L_n\\) is a saturated finite free \\(R\\)-submodule of \\(K_n\\), and its reduction embeds in \\(K_n/2K_n\\). Writing its image as \\(\\overline L_n\\), one has \\begin{equation} \\dim_{\\F}\\overline L_n=\\dim_{\\Q}W_n. \\end{equation} More generally, for any rational subspace \\(V_n\\subset W_n\\), the lattice \\(M_n=V_n\\cap K_n\\) has an embedded reduction \\(\\overline M_n\\subset\\overline L_n\\) of dimension \\(\\dim_{\\Q}V_n\\). Every element of \\(\\overline L_n\\) satisfies the reductions of the three defining identities. If \\(n>2\\), it also satisfies \\eqref{eq:special} and \\eqref{eq:special-linearization} after reduction, together with all formal coefficient consequences of these identities. In particular, putting \\(z=x+y\\), \\begin{equation} [x,\\overline\\psi(x,y)+\\overline\\psi(z,y)] +[y,\\overline\\psi(z,y)]=0 \\qquad(\\overline\\psi\\in\\overline L_n). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":24734,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/008.md","scopeText":"The Deligne–Drinfeld conjecture predicts that the rational Grothendieck–Teichmüller Lie algebra is freely generated by one element in each odd weight $3,5,7,\\ldots$. The formalization establishes this for the rational solution space of antisymmetry, the three-term equation, and the four-strand pentagon with the Ihara bracket. It also gives the corresponding continuous isomorphism after completion by weight.\n\nThe regularized-transport proposition and graph-complex consequences are not included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/008.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":143299,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Deligne-Drinfeld-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"009-01","slug":"Reconstruction-of-Function-Fields-from-Mod-ell-Milnor-K-Theory-October-5-2026","familyId":"009","title":"Reconstruction of Function Fields from Mod-ℓ Milnor K-Theory","abstract":"We prove a mod-ℓ Bogomolov–Pop reconstruction theorem for function fields of transcendence degree at least two over arbitrary algebraically closed fields of characteristic different from ℓ. The groups $K^{\\mathrm M}_1/\\ell$ and $K^{\\mathrm M}_2/\\ell$, together with their full bilinear product, determine the perfect closure and its constant field. Every compatible isomorphism of these data is induced by a field isomorphism up to a single scalar in $\\mathbb F_\\ell^\\times$, with only Frobenius ambiguity in the field isomorphism in positive characteristic.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Reconstructs function fields of transcendence degree at least two over algebraically closed constants from $K^{\\mathrm M}_1/\\ell$, $K^{\\mathrm M}_2/\\ell$, and their product. These data recover the perfect closure and constants when ℓ differs from the characteristic, and the original field and its named base in equal characteristic. Also proves Bogomolov–Pop reconstruction from abelian-by-central pro-ℓ Galois data away from the characteristic.","pdfPath":"preprints/Reconstruction-of-Function-Fields-from-Mod-ell-Milnor-K-Theory-October-5-2026/mod-ell-bogomolov-pop.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Reconstruction-of-Function-Fields-from-Mod-ell-Milnor-K-Theory-October-5-2026/mod-ell-bogomolov-pop.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Reconstruction-of-Function-Fields-from-Mod-ell-Milnor-K-Theory-October-5-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Reconstruction-of-Function-Fields-from-Mod-ell-Milnor-K-Theory-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Reconstruction-of-Function-Fields-from-Mod-ell-Milnor-K-Theory-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Reconstruction-of-Function-Fields-from-Mod-ell-Milnor-K-Theory-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-local.tex","preprints/Reconstruction-of-Function-Fields-from-Mod-ell-Milnor-K-Theory-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-residual-curves.tex","preprints/Reconstruction-of-Function-Fields-from-Mod-ell-Milnor-K-Theory-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-support.tex","preprints/Reconstruction-of-Function-Fields-from-Mod-ell-Milnor-K-Theory-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-curve-fields.tex","preprints/Reconstruction-of-Function-Fields-from-Mod-ell-Milnor-K-Theory-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-good-functions.tex","preprints/Reconstruction-of-Function-Fields-from-Mod-ell-Milnor-K-Theory-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-field.tex","preprints/Reconstruction-of-Function-Fields-from-Mod-ell-Milnor-K-Theory-October-5-2026/build/sections/08-compatibility.tex","preprints/Reconstruction-of-Function-Fields-from-Mod-ell-Milnor-K-Theory-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Reconstruction-of-Function-Fields-from-Mod-ell-Milnor-K-Theory-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a mod-$\\ell$ Bogomolov--Pop reconstruction theorem for function fields of transcendence degree at least two over arbitrary algebraically closed fields of characteristic different from $\\ell$. The groups $K^{\\mathrm M}_1/\\ell$ and $K^{\\mathrm M}_2/\\ell$, together with their full bilinear product, determine the perfect closure and its constant field. Every compatible isomorphism of these data is induced by a field isomorphism up to a single scalar in $\\F_\\ell^\\times$, with only Frobenius ambiguity in the field isomorphism in positive characteristic.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":661},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The reconstruction theorem","sourceOffset":1288},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":4237},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its main ingredients","sourceOffset":7073},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":11731},{"kind":"section","title":"Alternating characters and quasi-divisorial valuations","sourceOffset":12500},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The established local input","sourceOffset":14603},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Value groups and residue characters","sourceOffset":16182},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Recovering dimension and quasi-divisors","sourceOffset":23341},{"kind":"section","title":"Recognizing points on residual curves","sourceOffset":32797},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The density input and reduction of coefficients","sourceOffset":34469},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Valuative characters and the relation among point orders","sourceOffset":39447}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $\\ell$ be any prime. Let $K/k$ and $L/l$ be finitely generated extensions of arbitrary algebraically closed fields, with $\\charac k,\\charac l\\ne\\ell$ and $\\trdeg(K/k),\\trdeg(L/l)\\ge2$. Then \\eqref{eq:main-map} is bijective. In particular, a compatible isomorphism $V_K\\to V_L$ forces equality of the characteristics and of the relative transcendence degrees.","sourceOffset":3551,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Alternating-pair valuation theory","label":"thm:local-input","text":"Let \\(F\\) be a field and set \\(A_F^{\\pm}=\\{f\\in A_F:f(-1)=0\\}\\). \\begin{enumerate} \\item Every valuative subset \\(\\Sigma\\subset A_F\\) has a unique coarsest valuation \\(v_\\Sigma\\) with \\(\\Sigma\\subset I_{v_\\Sigma}\\). In particular, \\(v_\\Sigma\\) coarsens every valuation whose inertia contains \\(\\Sigma\\). For such a valuation \\(w\\), this coarsening is obtained by quotienting \\(wF\\) by its largest convex subgroup contained in \\(w(\\bigcap_{f\\in\\Sigma}\\ker f)\\). \\item If \\(S\\subset A_F^{\\pm}\\) is an alternating linear subspace, its valuative elements form a valuative subspace \\(S_0\\) of codimension at most one. Moreover \\(S\\subset D_{v_{S_0}}\\). \\item If \\(\\Sigma\\subset A_F^{\\pm}\\) is valuative and \\(g\\in A_F^{\\pm}\\) is alternating with every element of \\(\\Sigma\\), then \\(g\\in D_{v_\\Sigma}\\). \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":14901,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":null,"text":"A quasi-divisorial valuation, or quasi-prime divisor, of \\(F/\\kappa\\) is a valuation minimal under coarsening among those with \\[ vF/v\\kappa\\cong\\Z,\\qquad \\trdeg(Fv/\\kappa v)=s-1. \\] A quasi-prime divisor trivial on \\(\\kappa\\) is called a prime divisor, or a true prime divisor. A quasi-prime \\(r\\)-divisor is a composition of \\(r\\) successive quasi-prime divisors on the resulting residue fields.","sourceOffset":19599,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:good-subfield","text":"A curve subfield $E\\subset F$ is good if it is rational over the constants and every point line in $A_E$ is the nonzero image of the inertia of some true divisor of $F$. A good function is an element $t\\in F$ generating a good subfield $\\kappa(t)$.","sourceOffset":91053,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/009.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":131369,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Reconstruction-of-Function-Fields-from-Mod-ell-Milnor-K-Theory-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"009-02","slug":"Reconstruction-from-Milnor-K-theory-modulo-the-characteristic-October-5-2026","familyId":"009","title":"Reconstruction from Milnor K-theory modulo the characteristic","abstract":"Let K and L be finitely generated extensions of transcendence degree at least two over algebraically closed fields of characteristic p. We prove that every isomorphism of their first Milnor K-groups modulo p preserving the degree-two Steinberg relations is a nonzero scalar multiple of the map induced by a unique field isomorphism. The proof recovers projective lines over the subfields of pth powers by an elementary calculation with derivations.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Reconstructs function fields of transcendence degree at least two over algebraically closed constants from $K^{\\mathrm M}_1/\\ell$, $K^{\\mathrm M}_2/\\ell$, and their product. These data recover the perfect closure and constants when ℓ differs from the characteristic, and the original field and its named base in equal characteristic. Also proves Bogomolov–Pop reconstruction from abelian-by-central pro-ℓ Galois data away from the characteristic.","pdfPath":"preprints/Reconstruction-from-Milnor-K-theory-modulo-the-characteristic-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Reconstruction-from-Milnor-K-theory-modulo-the-characteristic-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Reconstruction-from-Milnor-K-theory-modulo-the-characteristic-October-5-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Reconstruction-from-Milnor-K-theory-modulo-the-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/build/main.tex","preprints/Reconstruction-from-Milnor-K-theory-modulo-the-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Reconstruction-from-Milnor-K-theory-modulo-the-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Reconstruction-from-Milnor-K-theory-modulo-the-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/build/sections/02-symbols.tex","preprints/Reconstruction-from-Milnor-K-theory-modulo-the-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/build/sections/03-intersection.tex","preprints/Reconstruction-from-Milnor-K-theory-modulo-the-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/build/sections/04-lines.tex","preprints/Reconstruction-from-Milnor-K-theory-modulo-the-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/build/sections/05-fields.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Reconstruction-from-Milnor-K-theory-modulo-the-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let $K$ and $L$ be finitely generated extensions of transcendence degree at least two over algebraically closed fields of characteristic $p$. We prove that every isomorphism of their first Milnor $K$-groups modulo $p$ preserving the degree-two Steinberg relations is a nonzero scalar multiple of the map induced by a unique field isomorphism. The proof recovers projective lines over the subfields of $p$th powers by an elementary calculation with derivations.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":506},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and method","sourceOffset":2748},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Outline of the proof","sourceOffset":4956},{"kind":"section","title":"Symbols and projective points over \\texorpdfstring{$p$}{p}th powers","sourceOffset":7254},{"kind":"section","title":"An intersection that determines an exponent","sourceOffset":12701},{"kind":"section","title":"One scalar recovers all projective lines","sourceOffset":18171},{"kind":"section","title":"From projective lines to the field","sourceOffset":26564}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $K/k$ and $L/l$ be finitely generated extensions of algebraically closed fields of characteristic $p$, with \\[ \\trdeg(K/k)\\ge2,\\qquad \\trdeg(L/l)\\ge2. \\] The canonical map \\[ \\Isom(K,k;L,l)\\longrightarrow \\Isom_{\\mathrm M}(V_K,V_L)/\\Lambda^\\times, \\qquad \\alpha\\longmapsto[\\alpha_*], \\] is bijective.","sourceOffset":2161,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Intersection and exponent","label":"prop:intersection","text":"Let $F$ be a field of characteristic $p$ with $[F:F^p]<\\infty$, and put $C=F^p$. Let $X,Y\\in F^\\times$ be $p$-independent. Suppose \\[ U\\in C(X)^\\times,\\qquad V\\in C(Y)^\\times, \\qquad [U]\\notin\\Fp[X],\\qquad [V]\\notin\\Fp[Y]. \\] If \\begin{equation} X\\,C(Y/X)^\\times\\ \\cap\\ U\\,C(V/U)^\\times\\ne\\varnothing, \\end{equation} then there is a unique integer $n$, $1\\le n<p$, such that \\begin{equation} U^n\\in C+CX^n,\\qquad V^n\\in C+CY^n. \\end{equation} In fact, either inclusion in \\eqref{eq:intersection-conclusion} determines $n$ uniquely. In each inclusion, both coefficients are nonzero.","sourceOffset":13521,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/009.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":33556,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Reconstruction-from-Milnor-K-theory-modulo-the-characteristic-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"009-03","slug":"The-Bogomolov-Pop-reconstruction-theorem-September-23-2026","familyId":"009","title":"The Bogomolov-Pop reconstruction theorem","abstract":"We prove the Bogomolov–Pop reconstruction conjecture for function fields of transcendence degree at least two over arbitrary algebraically closed fields of characteristic different from ℓ. The pro-ℓ abelian-by-central datum determines the perfect closure and its constant field, with precisely the Frobenius and ℓ-adic unit ambiguities.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Reconstructs function fields of transcendence degree at least two over algebraically closed constants from $K^{\\mathrm M}_1/\\ell$, $K^{\\mathrm M}_2/\\ell$, and their product. These data recover the perfect closure and constants when ℓ differs from the characteristic, and the original field and its named base in equal characteristic. Also proves Bogomolov–Pop reconstruction from abelian-by-central pro-ℓ Galois data away from the characteristic.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Bogomolov-Pop-reconstruction-theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Bogomolov-Pop-reconstruction-theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Bogomolov-Pop-reconstruction-theorem-September-23-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/The-Bogomolov-Pop-reconstruction-theorem-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Bogomolov-Pop-reconstruction-theorem-September-23-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/The-Bogomolov-Pop-reconstruction-theorem-September-23-2026/build/preliminaries.tex","preprints/The-Bogomolov-Pop-reconstruction-theorem-September-23-2026/build/local-theory.tex","preprints/The-Bogomolov-Pop-reconstruction-theorem-September-23-2026/build/residual-tests.tex","preprints/The-Bogomolov-Pop-reconstruction-theorem-September-23-2026/build/pencil-bound.tex","preprints/The-Bogomolov-Pop-reconstruction-theorem-September-23-2026/build/curve-recovery.tex","preprints/The-Bogomolov-Pop-reconstruction-theorem-September-23-2026/build/reconstruction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Bogomolov-Pop-reconstruction-theorem-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Bogomolov--Pop reconstruction conjecture for function fields of transcendence degree at least two over arbitrary algebraically closed fields of characteristic different from $\\ell$. The pro-$\\ell$ abelian-by-central datum determines the perfect closure and its constant field, with precisely the Frobenius and $\\ell$-adic unit ambiguities.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The reconstruction problem","sourceOffset":415},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exact statement","sourceOffset":906},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Previous work and the proof","sourceOffset":3577},{"kind":"section","title":"Kummer duality and the group input","sourceOffset":8767},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Orders on a curve","sourceOffset":14994},{"kind":"section","title":"Recovering the local data","sourceOffset":16720},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Commutators and alternating characters","sourceOffset":17345},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Character groups of valuations","sourceOffset":21326},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An intrinsic description of quasi-prime divisors","sourceOffset":26024},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Recognizing the finite-constant residual curves","sourceOffset":32723},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite tests on residual curves","sourceOffset":38201},{"kind":"section","title":"A uniform bound for pencils","sourceOffset":49793}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Bogomolov--Pop reconstruction","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $k$ and $l$ be arbitrary algebraically closed fields of characteristics different from $\\ell$, and let $K/k$ and $L/l$ be function fields with $\\trdeg(K/k),\\trdeg(L/l)\\ge2$. Then \\[ \\Phi_{K,L}:\\Isom_F^i(K,L)\\longrightarrow \\Isom^c(\\Pi_L^a,\\Pi_K^a)/\\Zell^\\times, \\qquad [\\alpha]\\longmapsto[\\alpha^*], \\] is a bijection.","sourceOffset":2824,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"local:transport","text":"Let $K/k$ and $L/l$ be function fields over algebraically closed fields of characteristic different from $\\ell$, each of transcendence degree at least two. Let $\\varphi:W_L\\to W_K$ be a continuous bracket-compatible $\\Zell$-module isomorphism. Then their transcendence degrees agree, say they are $d$. For every $1\\leq r<d$, the map $\\varphi$ carries exactly the pairs $I_w\\subset D_w$ of quasi-prime $r$-divisors of $L/l$ onto the corresponding pairs of $K/k$. For matched pairs it induces a continuous isomorphism \\[ D_w/I_w\\cong W_{Lw}\\ \\longrightarrow\\ D_v/I_v\\cong W_{Kv} \\] that preserves alternating pairs.","sourceOffset":31167,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/009.md","scopeText":"The Bogomolov–Pop reconstruction conjecture concerns recovering a function field and its constants from pro-$\\ell$ abelian-by-central Galois data. The linked formalization proves injectivity of the reconstruction correspondence for function fields of transcendence degree at least two over algebraically closed fields of characteristic different from the prime $\\ell$.\n\nIf two isomorphisms of perfect closures preserving the constant fields induce the same Galois-data isomorphism modulo $\\ell$-adic unit scaling, then they agree modulo Frobenius. The selected statement proves this uniqueness; existence of a field isomorphism for every admissible Galois-data isomorphism is outside it.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/009.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":87313,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Bogomolov-Pop-reconstruction-theorem-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"010-01","slug":"Unrestricted-pro-modularity-at-the-prime-two-October-6-2026","familyId":"010","title":"Unrestricted pro-modularity at the prime two","abstract":"Every continuous, odd, absolutely irreducible two-dimensional 2-adic representation of $G_{\\mathbb Q}$ that is unramified outside finitely many finite primes occurs in the full completed Hecke algebra at some odd tame level. The level may contain auxiliary tame primes, and scalar and reducible residual representations are included. This is a completed-Hecke occurrence result, with no de Rham hypothesis; it does not assert classical modularity.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Every continuous odd absolutely irreducible two-dimensional 2-adic representation of $G_{\\mathbb Q}$ unramified outside finitely many primes occurs in a completed Hecke algebra at some odd tame level. Also proves classical modularity up to Tate twist for irreducible odd representations with these finiteness conditions that are de Rham at 2 with distinct Hodge–Tate weights, resolving the dyadic Fontaine–Mazur case without residual restrictions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Unrestricted-pro-modularity-at-the-prime-two-October-6-2026/two-adic-promodularity.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Unrestricted-pro-modularity-at-the-prime-two-October-6-2026/two-adic-promodularity.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Unrestricted-pro-modularity-at-the-prime-two-October-6-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Unrestricted-pro-modularity-at-the-prime-two-October-6-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Unrestricted-pro-modularity-at-the-prime-two-October-6-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Unrestricted-pro-modularity-at-the-prime-two-October-6-2026/build/source/sections/family.tex","preprints/Unrestricted-pro-modularity-at-the-prime-two-October-6-2026/build/source/sections/diagonal.tex","preprints/Unrestricted-pro-modularity-at-the-prime-two-October-6-2026/build/source/sections/regular-point.tex","preprints/Unrestricted-pro-modularity-at-the-prime-two-October-6-2026/build/source/sections/hecke.tex","preprints/Unrestricted-pro-modularity-at-the-prime-two-October-6-2026/build/source/sections/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Unrestricted-pro-modularity-at-the-prime-two-October-6-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every continuous, odd, absolutely irreducible two-dimensional $2$-adic representation of $G_{\\Q}$ that is unramified outside finitely many finite primes occurs in the full completed Hecke algebra at some odd tame level. The level may contain auxiliary tame primes, and scalar and reducible residual representations are included. This is a completed-Hecke occurrence result, with no de Rham hypothesis; it does not assert classical modularity.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The full Hecke algebra and the theorem","sourceOffset":489},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and main ideas","sourceOffset":2850},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coefficient and deformation conventions","sourceOffset":7286},{"kind":"section","title":"A trace family with potential automorphic support","sourceOffset":8890},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Normalizing the determinant","sourceOffset":9585},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The dimension of the trace family","sourceOffset":14056},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Potential support with independently prescribed residue","sourceOffset":20567},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The diagonal local parameter and completed forms","sourceOffset":31094},{"kind":"section","title":"Admissibility from diagonal local parameters","sourceOffset":36389},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The local block input","sourceOffset":39208},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coinvariants and the noncharacter corner","sourceOffset":42583},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The characteristic-two curve test","sourceOffset":46171}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $E/\\Q_2$ be finite, with ring of integers $\\OO_E$, and let $r:G_{\\Q}\\longrightarrow\\GL_2(E)$ be continuous and absolutely irreducible. Suppose that $r$ is unramified outside finitely many finite primes and is odd: $\\det r(c)=-1$ for complex conjugation $c$. Then there exist an odd positive integer $N$, divisible by every odd prime where $r$ ramifies, and a continuous $\\Z_2$-algebra homomorphism \\[ \\lambda:\\mathbb T_2(N)\\longrightarrow\\OO_E \\] such that, for every prime $\\ell\\nmid2N$, \\[ \\lambda(T_\\ell)=\\tr r(\\operatorname{Frob}_\\ell),\\qquad \\lambda(\\ell S_\\ell)=\\det r(\\operatorname{Frob}_\\ell). \\]","sourceOffset":1910,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:potential-support","text":"Let $B$ be the family in Lemma \\ref{lem:large-family}, with the normalized determinant of Lemma \\ref{lem:normalization}. There is a solvable totally real Galois extension $F/\\Q$, of even degree and completely split at $2$, such that the closed trace image of $B|_{G_F}$ is pro-modular at one fixed tame level and central character $\\psi|_{G_F}$. This assertion includes scalar and reducible semisimple residual representations.","sourceOffset":21392,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":101272,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Unrestricted-pro-modularity-at-the-prime-two-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"010-02","slug":"The-Dimension-of-the-Two-Adic-Hecke-Algebra-at-Odd-Level-October-5-2026","familyId":"010","title":"The Dimension of the Two-Adic Hecke Algebra at Odd Level","abstract":"For every odd positive integer N, every irreducible component of the full two-adic Hecke algebra of level $\\Gamma_1(N)$ has Krull dimension four. This proves the p = 2 case of Emerton's dimension conjecture, including all residual components.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Every continuous odd absolutely irreducible two-dimensional 2-adic representation of $G_{\\mathbb Q}$ unramified outside finitely many primes occurs in a completed Hecke algebra at some odd tame level. Also proves classical modularity up to Tate twist for irreducible odd representations with these finiteness conditions that are de Rham at 2 with distinct Hodge–Tate weights, resolving the dyadic Fontaine–Mazur case without residual restrictions.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Dimension-of-the-Two-Adic-Hecke-Algebra-at-Odd-Level-October-5-2026/two-adic-hecke.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Dimension-of-the-Two-Adic-Hecke-Algebra-at-Odd-Level-October-5-2026/two-adic-hecke.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Dimension-of-the-Two-Adic-Hecke-Algebra-at-Odd-Level-October-5-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/The-Dimension-of-the-Two-Adic-Hecke-Algebra-at-Odd-Level-October-5-2026/build/main.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Dimension-of-the-Two-Adic-Hecke-Algebra-at-Odd-Level-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every odd positive integer $N$, every irreducible component of the full two-adic Hecke algebra of level $\\Gamma_1(N)$ has Krull dimension four. This proves the $p=2$ case of Emerton's dimension conjecture, including all residual components.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":291},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the main input","sourceOffset":2062},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Method and organization","sourceOffset":3908},{"kind":"section","title":"The integral algebra and its Galois determinant","sourceOffset":5635},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite stages and classical systems","sourceOffset":6119},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An integral determinant law","sourceOffset":8848},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Noetherianity at two","sourceOffset":10941},{"kind":"section","title":"Finding cuspidal points on every component","sourceOffset":14359},{"kind":"section","title":"A tangent bound in characteristic zero","sourceOffset":19593},{"kind":"section","title":"The adjoint cohomology bound","sourceOffset":25377},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Global Selmer vanishing","sourceOffset":26178},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The local quotients","sourceOffset":27814}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every odd positive integer $N$, every irreducible component of $\\Spec T_2(N)$ has dimension exactly $4$.","sourceOffset":1584,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:noetherian","text":"The ring $A$ is a finite product of complete Noetherian local rings with finite residue fields. Its given topology on each factor is the maximal-ideal-adic topology.","sourceOffset":10975,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":33252,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Dimension-of-the-Two-Adic-Hecke-Algebra-at-Odd-Level-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/The-Dimension-of-the-Two-Adic-Hecke-Algebra-at-Odd-Level-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/The-Dimension-of-the-Two-Adic-Hecke-Algebra-at-Odd-Level-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"010-03","slug":"Fontaine-Mazur-modularity-at-the-prime-2-October-6-2026","familyId":"010","title":"Fontaine–Mazur modularity at the prime 2","abstract":"We prove that every continuous, irreducible, odd two-dimensional 2-adic representation of $G_{\\mathbb Q}$, unramified outside finitely many primes and de Rham at 2 with distinct Hodge–Tate weights, is modular up to Tate twist. This resolves the odd, regular two-dimensional Fontaine–Mazur conjecture over ℚ at 2, including all residual representations.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Every continuous odd absolutely irreducible two-dimensional 2-adic representation of $G_{\\mathbb Q}$ unramified outside finitely many primes occurs in a completed Hecke algebra at some odd tame level. Also proves classical modularity up to Tate twist for irreducible odd representations with these finiteness conditions that are de Rham at 2 with distinct Hodge–Tate weights, resolving the dyadic Fontaine–Mazur case without residual restrictions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Fontaine-Mazur-modularity-at-the-prime-2-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Fontaine-Mazur-modularity-at-the-prime-2-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Fontaine-Mazur-modularity-at-the-prime-2-October-6-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Fontaine-Mazur-modularity-at-the-prime-2-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Fontaine-Mazur-modularity-at-the-prime-2-October-6-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Fontaine-Mazur-modularity-at-the-prime-2-October-6-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Fontaine-Mazur-modularity-at-the-prime-2-October-6-2026/build/sections/deformation.tex","preprints/Fontaine-Mazur-modularity-at-the-prime-2-October-6-2026/build/sections/completed_forms.tex","preprints/Fontaine-Mazur-modularity-at-the-prime-2-October-6-2026/build/sections/patching.tex","preprints/Fontaine-Mazur-modularity-at-the-prime-2-October-6-2026/build/sections/fields.tex","preprints/Fontaine-Mazur-modularity-at-the-prime-2-October-6-2026/build/sections/connectedness.tex","preprints/Fontaine-Mazur-modularity-at-the-prime-2-October-6-2026/build/sections/classicality.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Fontaine-Mazur-modularity-at-the-prime-2-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every continuous, irreducible, odd two-dimensional \\(2\\)-adic representation of \\(G_{\\mathbb Q}\\), unramified outside finitely many primes and de Rham at \\(2\\) with distinct Hodge--Tate weights, is modular up to Tate twist. This resolves the odd, regular two-dimensional Fontaine--Mazur conjecture over \\(\\mathbb Q\\) at \\(2\\), including all residual representations.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":427},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and the dyadic problem","sourceOffset":2578},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From occurrence to classicality and descent","sourceOffset":7098},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The constructions used in the proof","sourceOffset":13046},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and initial reductions","sourceOffset":15846},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coefficients and cyclotomic exponents","sourceOffset":18015},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The virtually solvable case","sourceOffset":21876},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fields, deformation spaces, and trace loci","sourceOffset":25223},{"kind":"section","title":"Deformations and local geometry","sourceOffset":28246},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Representation spaces and formal dimensions","sourceOffset":29158},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Continuous deformations at a curve","sourceOffset":38553},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Relative presentations","sourceOffset":47577}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\[ r:G_{\\mathbb Q}\\longrightarrow\\GL_2(\\overline{\\mathbb Q}_2) \\] be continuous and irreducible. Suppose that \\(r\\) is odd, is unramified outside finitely many primes, and is de Rham at \\(2\\) with distinct Hodge--Tate weights. Then \\(r\\) is isomorphic to a Tate twist of the \\(2\\)-adic Galois representation associated with a classical cuspidal eigenform.","sourceOffset":1369,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Representation spaces","label":"prop:rep-space","text":"Let $G$ satisfy $\\Phi_2$, let $\\overline D$ be a continuous two-dimensional determinant over the finite field $\\kk$, and fix a continuous lift $\\chi$ of its determinant character. The following statements hold. \\begin{enumerate} \\item The determinant deformation functor is represented by a complete Noetherian local $\\OO$-algebra $R^{\\mathrm{ps}}$. There is a finite $R^{\\mathrm{ps}}$-algebra $\\mathscr E$ carrying its universal Cayley--Hamilton representation. Matrix representations of $\\mathscr E$ compatible with the universal determinant form an affine scheme of finite type over $R^{\\mathrm{ps}}$. Its completion at a closed residual matrix representation is that representation's framed deformation space. \\item Invariant lines at finitely many closed subgroups can be imposed by closed incidence conditions in products of projective lines. On the absolutely irreducible global locus, existence of such a line with a specified character is closed in the trace and character parameters. \\item Suppose the subgroups' abelianizations have finitely generated maximal pro-$2$ quotients. Adjoining the characters on the specified lines gives a finite extension of the completed trace-and-determinant image. \\item At an absolutely irreducible representation over a coefficient field, determinants classify unframed deformations. After a splitting extension, the framed fiber is a conjugacy orbit of dimension three. \\end{enumerate} These assertions do not require the residual determinant to be mult","sourceOffset":29696,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":255849,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Fontaine-Mazur-modularity-at-the-prime-2-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"011-01","slug":"Weighted-Dilation-Graphs-Smooth-Shifted-Primes-and-Totient-Fibers-September-24-2026","familyId":"011","title":"Weighted dilation graphs, smooth shifted primes and totient fibers","abstract":"We prove Erdős's conjecture on the largest fibers of Euler's totient function: for every ε > 0, infinitely many positive integers n have more than $n^{1-\\varepsilon }$ preimages. We also show that, for every fixed δ > 0, there are at least $x^{1-o(1)}$ primes p in $2x\\lt p\\le5x$ whose predecessors have no prime factor exceeding xδ.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the normalized ordered logarithms of the prime factors of $p-1$, counted with multiplicity, converge jointly to the Poisson–Dirichlet law $\\mathrm{PD}(1)$ as p ranges uniformly over primes up to x and $x\\to\\infty$. This resolves the Ford–Konyagin–Luca conjecture. It also proves that infinitely many integers n have more than $n^{1-\\varepsilon}$ totient preimages, for every ε > 0.","pdfPath":"preprints/Weighted-Dilation-Graphs-Smooth-Shifted-Primes-and-Totient-Fibers-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Weighted-Dilation-Graphs-Smooth-Shifted-Primes-and-Totient-Fibers-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Weighted-Dilation-Graphs-Smooth-Shifted-Primes-and-Totient-Fibers-September-24-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Weighted-Dilation-Graphs-Smooth-Shifted-Primes-and-Totient-Fibers-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Weighted-Dilation-Graphs-Smooth-Shifted-Primes-and-Totient-Fibers-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Weighted-Dilation-Graphs-Smooth-Shifted-Primes-and-Totient-Fibers-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Weighted-Dilation-Graphs-Smooth-Shifted-Primes-and-Totient-Fibers-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Weighted-Dilation-Graphs-Smooth-Shifted-Primes-and-Totient-Fibers-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-transference.tex","preprints/Weighted-Dilation-Graphs-Smooth-Shifted-Primes-and-Totient-Fibers-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-ideal-kernels.tex","preprints/Weighted-Dilation-Graphs-Smooth-Shifted-Primes-and-Totient-Fibers-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-shifted-correlations.tex","preprints/Weighted-Dilation-Graphs-Smooth-Shifted-Primes-and-Totient-Fibers-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-type-ii.tex","preprints/Weighted-Dilation-Graphs-Smooth-Shifted-Primes-and-Totient-Fibers-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-prime-extraction.tex","preprints/Weighted-Dilation-Graphs-Smooth-Shifted-Primes-and-Totient-Fibers-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-totient-conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Weighted-Dilation-Graphs-Smooth-Shifted-Primes-and-Totient-Fibers-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Erd\\H{o}s's conjecture on the largest fibers of Euler's totient function: for every $\\eps>0$, infinitely many positive integers $n$ have more than $n^{1-\\eps}$ preimages. We also show that, for every fixed $\\delta>0$, there are at least $x^{1-o(1)}$ primes $p$ in $2x<p\\le5x$ whose predecessors have no prime factor exceeding $x^\\delta$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":392},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The analytic mechanism","sourceOffset":5087},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and dependencies","sourceOffset":10382},{"kind":"section","title":"Notation and preliminary estimates","sourceOffset":12738},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Classical prime estimates","sourceOffset":16496},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Divisors, Dirichlet polynomials, and smooth separation","sourceOffset":19348},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An elementary weighted sieve","sourceOffset":24155},{"kind":"section","title":"Transference for dilation graphs","sourceOffset":30384},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prime groups and the two operators","sourceOffset":31364},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Replacing the root by independent residues","sourceOffset":39960},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exact expansion for one big prime","sourceOffset":44531},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A Hilbert space which remembers lifespans","sourceOffset":47999}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:totient","text":"For every real $\\eps>0$, there are infinitely many positive integers $n$ such that \\[ g(n)>n^{1-\\eps}. \\]","sourceOffset":626,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:smooth","text":"For every fixed $0<\\delta<1/4$, as $x\\to\\infty$, \\begin{equation} \\#\\{p:\\ p\\text{ prime},\\ 2x<p\\le5x, \\ P^+(p-1)\\le x^\\delta\\} \\ge x^{1-o(1)}. \\end{equation} The quantity $o(1)$ may depend on $\\delta$.","sourceOffset":1891,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Prime groups","label":"def:prime-groups","text":"Fix constants \\[ 0<a<b<c<d<0.47,\\qquad c_0,C_0,v_-,v_+>0, \\qquad 0<q<1,\\qquad \\ell\\in\\N. \\] For each sufficiently large $x$, let the finite, pairwise disjoint sets of primes $\\mathcal P_g$ be indexed by the disjoint sets $\\mathcal S$ and $\\mathcal B$, where \\[ c_0T\\le |\\mathcal S|,|\\mathcal B|\\le C_0T. \\] The small and big groups, respectively, satisfy \\begin{align} \\exp(L^a)\\le p\\le\\exp(L^b)&\\quad(p\\in\\mathcal P_g, \\ g\\in\\mathcal S),\\notag\\\\ \\exp(L^c)\\le p\\le\\exp(L^d)&\\quad(p\\in\\mathcal P_g, \\ g\\in\\mathcal B),\\\\ v_-\\le V_g:=\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P_g}\\frac1p&\\le v_+, \\qquad \\mu_g(p):=\\frac1{V_gp}","sourceOffset":31413,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Ideal operator","label":"def:ideal-operator","text":"On the space \\[ \\mathcal H_{\\rm id} =L^2\\left(\\prod_{g\\in\\mathcal B}\\mathcal P_g^M, \\ \\bigotimes_{g\\in\\mathcal B}\\mu_g^{\\otimes M}\\right), \\] repetitions of prime values are allowed. For a real $\\zeta$, the unsymmetrized ideal row operation for $\\nu$ retains shared labels, samples all unshared target labels independently with laws $\\mu_g$, and samples the auxiliary labels of $D_{\\mathcal B}$ independently with the same laws. Its multiplier is \\[ K_\\nu D_{\\mathcal B}^{i\\zeta}\\ee{\\Theta D_{\\mathcal B}}. \\] The sum over $\\nu$, composed on both sides with big-group symmetrization, is denoted $\\mat","sourceOffset":33803,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":222415,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Weighted-Dilation-Graphs-Smooth-Shifted-Primes-and-Totient-Fibers-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"011-02","slug":"The-Poisson-Dirichlet-Law-for-Prime-Predecessors-September-24-2026","familyId":"011","title":"The Poisson-Dirichlet law for prime predecessors","abstract":"For a prime p chosen uniformly from $3\\le p\\le x$, list the prime factors of $p-1$ in decreasing order, with multiplicity. As $x\\to\\infty$, their logarithms, divided by $\\log(p-1)$, converge in every finite joint distribution to the Poisson–Dirichlet distribution with parameter one. This proves the conjecture of Ford, Konyagin and Luca.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the normalized ordered logarithms of the prime factors of $p-1$, counted with multiplicity, converge jointly to the Poisson–Dirichlet law $\\mathrm{PD}(1)$ as p ranges uniformly over primes up to x and $x\\to\\infty$. This resolves the Ford–Konyagin–Luca conjecture. It also proves that infinitely many integers n have more than $n^{1-\\varepsilon}$ totient preimages, for every ε > 0.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Poisson-Dirichlet-Law-for-Prime-Predecessors-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Poisson-Dirichlet-Law-for-Prime-Predecessors-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Poisson-Dirichlet-Law-for-Prime-Predecessors-September-24-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/The-Poisson-Dirichlet-Law-for-Prime-Predecessors-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Poisson-Dirichlet-Law-for-Prime-Predecessors-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-Poisson-Dirichlet-Law-for-Prime-Predecessors-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-Poisson-Dirichlet-Law-for-Prime-Predecessors-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-map.tex","preprints/The-Poisson-Dirichlet-Law-for-Prime-Predecessors-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-preliminaries.tex","preprints/The-Poisson-Dirichlet-Law-for-Prime-Predecessors-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-determinant.tex","preprints/The-Poisson-Dirichlet-Law-for-Prime-Predecessors-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-memory.tex","preprints/The-Poisson-Dirichlet-Law-for-Prime-Predecessors-September-24-2026/build/figures/lifespan.tex","preprints/The-Poisson-Dirichlet-Law-for-Prime-Predecessors-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-major-arcs.tex","preprints/The-Poisson-Dirichlet-Law-for-Prime-Predecessors-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-correlations.tex","preprints/The-Poisson-Dirichlet-Law-for-Prime-Predecessors-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-extraction.tex","preprints/The-Poisson-Dirichlet-Law-for-Prime-Predecessors-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-poisson-dirichlet.tex","preprints/The-Poisson-Dirichlet-Law-for-Prime-Predecessors-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-long-prime-appendix.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Poisson-Dirichlet-Law-for-Prime-Predecessors-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For a prime $p$ chosen uniformly from $3\\le p\\le x$, list the prime factors of $p-1$ in decreasing order, with multiplicity. As $x\\to\\infty$, their logarithms, divided by $\\log(p-1)$, converge in every finite joint distribution to the Poisson--Dirichlet distribution with parameter one. This proves the conjecture of Ford, Konyagin and Luca.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":404},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and analytic inputs","sourceOffset":13110},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prime estimates and mean squares","sourceOffset":14768},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prime polynomials and logarithmic phases","sourceOffset":18238},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Elementary rough counts","sourceOffset":20376},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A sieve for nonnegative weighted objects","sourceOffset":22873},{"kind":"section","title":"A determinant estimate with marks on one side","sourceOffset":24306},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cauchy's inequality and the normalization of the square","sourceOffset":27111},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Physical states, good positions, and endpoint vectors","sourceOffset":31357},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From the moment to endpoint pairings","sourceOffset":39151},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform residues at a primitive root","sourceOffset":41945},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Independent residue lines and the archimedean error budget","sourceOffset":49145}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed $k\\ge1$ and every bounded continuous function $F:[0,1]^k\\to\\R$, \\begin{equation} \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac1{\\pi(x)-1} \\sum_{\\substack{3\\le p\\le x\\\\p\\text{ prime}}} F\\bigl(V_1(p),\\ldots,V_k(p)\\bigr) =\\E F(L_1,\\ldots,L_k). \\end{equation} The limit holds through all real $x$ and uses ordinary equal weighting of the primes.","sourceOffset":1345,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"One-sided marked Type II estimate","label":"thm:type-II","text":"Fix $\\delta,C,D_*>0$ and $q\\in(0,1)$. Suppose $H_m,H_n\\geq x^\\delta$ and $X=H_mH_n\\asymp x$. Let $F$ satisfy $|F(h)|\\leq1$ and $F(ph)=F(h)$ for every prime in the groups \\eqref{det:bands}. Let $\\alpha_m$ be supported on an arbitrary interval in $[H_m,2H_m]$, where it has the value \\[ \\alpha_m=m^{iv}\\left(\\1_{m\\text{ prime}} -\\frac{\\1_{m\\text{ rough}}}{V(W)\\log m}\\right), \\qquad |v|\\leq L^C. \\] Let $\\beta_n$ be supported on rough integers in $[H_n,2H_n]$, with $|\\beta_n|\\leq L^C$. If $K$ is sufficiently large in terms of $\\delta,C,D_*,q$, then \\begin{equation} \\left|\\sum_{m,n}\\alpha_m\\beta_n F(mn-1)\\mathcal W(mn-1)\\right| \\ll X L^{-D_*}. \\end{equation} The required lower bound on $K$ is independent of the particular $a_i$. The implicit constant and the threshold for $x$ may depend on all the fixed parameters, including the $a_i$.","sourceOffset":25678,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":241952,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Poisson-Dirichlet-Law-for-Prime-Predecessors-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"011-03","slug":"Prime-Predecessors-with-an-Even-Number-of-Prime-Factors-September-17-2026","familyId":"011","title":"Prime Predecessors with an Even Number of Prime Factors","abstract":"We prove that there are infinitely many primes p for which $p-1$ is squarefree and has an even number of prime factors. Equivalently, there are infinitely many primes p with $\\mu(p-1)=1$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the normalized ordered logarithms of the prime factors of $p-1$, counted with multiplicity, converge jointly to the Poisson–Dirichlet law $\\mathrm{PD}(1)$ as p ranges uniformly over primes up to x and $x\\to\\infty$. This resolves the Ford–Konyagin–Luca conjecture. It also proves that infinitely many integers n have more than $n^{1-\\varepsilon}$ totient preimages, for every ε > 0.","pdfPath":"preprints/Prime-Predecessors-with-an-Even-Number-of-Prime-Factors-September-17-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Prime-Predecessors-with-an-Even-Number-of-Prime-Factors-September-17-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Prime-Predecessors-with-an-Even-Number-of-Prime-Factors-September-17-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Prime-Predecessors-with-an-Even-Number-of-Prime-Factors-September-17-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Prime-Predecessors-with-an-Even-Number-of-Prime-Factors-September-17-2026/build/sections/01-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Prime-Predecessors-with-an-Even-Number-of-Prime-Factors-September-17-2026/build/sections/02-long-prime.tex","preprints/Prime-Predecessors-with-an-Even-Number-of-Prime-Factors-September-17-2026/build/sections/03-shifted.tex","preprints/Prime-Predecessors-with-an-Even-Number-of-Prime-Factors-September-17-2026/build/sections/04-weights.tex","preprints/Prime-Predecessors-with-an-Even-Number-of-Prime-Factors-September-17-2026/build/sections/05-type-ii.tex","preprints/Prime-Predecessors-with-an-Even-Number-of-Prime-Factors-September-17-2026/build/sections/06-congruences.tex","preprints/Prime-Predecessors-with-an-Even-Number-of-Prime-Factors-September-17-2026/build/sections/07-extraction.tex","preprints/Prime-Predecessors-with-an-Even-Number-of-Prime-Factors-September-17-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Prime-Predecessors-with-an-Even-Number-of-Prime-Factors-September-17-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that there are infinitely many primes $p$ for which $p-1$ is squarefree and has an even number of prime factors. Equivalently, there are infinitely many primes $p$ with $\\mu(p-1)=1$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":238},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and classical estimates","sourceOffset":4715},{"kind":"section","title":"A long prime polynomial","sourceOffset":10745},{"kind":"section","title":"A prime-slot shifted correlation with a common parity twist","sourceOffset":33308},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Groups, marked weights, and the assertion","sourceOffset":33659},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The graph inputs","sourceOffset":38202},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Endpoint norms and removal of the shared dilation","sourceOffset":44550},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison multipliers and minor arcs","sourceOffset":51353},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The endpoint on a major arc","sourceOffset":55189},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local Fourier energy and Mellin polynomials","sourceOffset":57843},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exceptional times and completion of the proof","sourceOffset":62810},{"kind":"section","title":"Candidate weights and their mass","sourceOffset":68078}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are infinitely many primes $p$ such that $p-1$ is squarefree and $\\Omega(p-1)$ is even. In particular, there are infinitely many such primes under either convention for counting prime factors.","sourceOffset":516,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Prime estimates","label":"pre:primes","text":"The prime number theorem holds with an error smaller than every fixed negative logarithmic power. Mertens' estimates give \\[ \\sum_{p\\le y}\\frac1p=\\log\\log y+O(1),\\qquad \\prod_{p\\le y}\\left(1-\\frac1p\\right) \\sim\\frac{e^{-\\gamma_E}}{\\log y}, \\] where $\\gamma_E$ is Euler's constant. For fixed $A,C>0$, uniformly for $r\\le(\\log y)^C$ and $(a,r)=1$, the Siegel--Walfisz estimate is \\[ \\#\\{p\\le y:p\\equiv a\\pmod r\\} =\\frac{\\Li(y)}{\\varphi(r)} +O_{A,C}\\left(\\frac{y}{(\\log y)^A}\\right). \\] Its constants need not be effective.","sourceOffset":5800,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":140424,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Prime-Predecessors-with-an-Even-Number-of-Prime-Factors-September-17-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"012-01","slug":"The-joint-Dickman-law-for-consecutive-integers-September-24-2026","familyId":"012","title":"The joint Dickman law for consecutive integers","abstract":"Let $P^+(n)$ denote the largest prime factor of n. We prove that $\\log P^+(n)/\\log n$ and $\\log P^+(n+1)/\\log n$ are asymptotically independent in ordinary natural density, with Dickman marginals. This resolves the Erdős–Pomerance joint Dickman conjecture positively and implies that the ordering $P^+(n)\\lt P^+(n+1)$ has natural density 1/2.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the Erdős–Pomerance joint Dickman conjecture: the logarithmic sizes of the largest prime factors of n and $n+1$ are asymptotically independent in ordinary natural density. In particular, the integers satisfying $P^+(n)\\lt P^+(n+1)$ have density 1/2.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-joint-Dickman-law-for-consecutive-integers-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-joint-Dickman-law-for-consecutive-integers-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-joint-Dickman-law-for-consecutive-integers-September-24-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/The-joint-Dickman-law-for-consecutive-integers-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-joint-Dickman-law-for-consecutive-integers-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-joint-Dickman-law-for-consecutive-integers-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-joint-Dickman-law-for-consecutive-integers-September-24-2026/build/sections/labels.tex","preprints/The-joint-Dickman-law-for-consecutive-integers-September-24-2026/build/sections/arithmetic.tex","preprints/The-joint-Dickman-law-for-consecutive-integers-September-24-2026/build/sections/amplification.tex","preprints/The-joint-Dickman-law-for-consecutive-integers-September-24-2026/build/sections/graph.tex","preprints/The-joint-Dickman-law-for-consecutive-integers-September-24-2026/build/sections/moments.tex","preprints/The-joint-Dickman-law-for-consecutive-integers-September-24-2026/build/sections/channels.tex","preprints/The-joint-Dickman-law-for-consecutive-integers-September-24-2026/build/sections/kernel.tex","preprints/The-joint-Dickman-law-for-consecutive-integers-September-24-2026/build/sections/sampling.tex","preprints/The-joint-Dickman-law-for-consecutive-integers-September-24-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex","preprints/The-joint-Dickman-law-for-consecutive-integers-September-24-2026/build/sections/distribution.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-joint-Dickman-law-for-consecutive-integers-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let $P^+(n)$ denote the largest prime factor of $n$. We prove that $\\log P^+(n)/\\log n$ and $\\log P^+(n+1)/\\log n$ are asymptotically independent in ordinary natural density, with Dickman marginals. This resolves the Erd\\H{o}s--Pomerance joint Dickman conjecture positively and implies that the ordering $P^+(n)<P^+(n+1)$ has natural density $1/2$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":422},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Previous work","sourceOffset":2474},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Method and organization","sourceOffset":5175},{"kind":"section","title":"Large-prime labels and their short averages","sourceOffset":16207},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Labels and their one-variable distributions","sourceOffset":16795},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The mixed correlation and its short-average input","sourceOffset":25080},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The short-interval inputs","sourceOffset":28473},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A uniform estimate for the character twists","sourceOffset":30767},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof of the weighted short-average lemma","sourceOffset":34554},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A profile of a nonzero mixed correlation","sourceOffset":39939},{"kind":"section","title":"Arithmetic preliminaries at an auxiliary log scale","sourceOffset":43178},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform local asymptotics","sourceOffset":45331}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Joint Dickman law","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed $a,b\\in(0,1)$, \\[ \\lim_{X\\to\\infty}\\frac1X \\#\\{2\\leq n\\leq X:\\Pplus(n)\\leq n^a, \\ \\Pplus(n+1)\\leq n^b\\} =\\rho(1/a)\\rho(1/b). \\] Here the limit is taken over all real $X$, with ordinary, unweighted counting.","sourceOffset":1054,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Vanishing of the main energy","label":"prop:conclusion-main-vanishes","text":"With $C_*$, $\\eta$, $C_6$, and the coarse partition fixed as above, \\[ \\lim_{B\\to\\infty}\\ \\limsup_{x\\to\\infty} |\\mathcal Q^{\\mathrm{main}}_{B,x}|=0. \\]","sourceOffset":235431,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Regular prime sets","label":"def:arith-regularity","text":"For $S\\subset\\mathcal P$ put \\[ N_g(S)=\\begin{cases} |\\{p\\in S:\\log p\\le B^g\\}|,&g<1,\\\\ |S|,&g=1. \\end{cases} \\] Let $Y_i=2^i\\log P_0$, $0\\le i\\le i_{\\max}$, where $i_{\\max}$ is the first index with $Y_i\\ge4B$. The set $S$ is regular if both of the following conditions hold: \\[ (g/2-\\tau)\\ell\\le N_g(S)\\le(g/2+\\tau)\\ell \\quad\\hbox{for every grid point }g, \\] and \\[ |\\{p\\in S:\\log p>Y_i\\}| \\ge0.4\\log(B/Y_i)-C_* \\quad(0\\le i\\le i_{\\max}). \\] An integer or profinite integer is regular when its set of dividing primes from $\\mathcal P$ is regular. Multiplicities are not counted in these cutoffs.","sourceOffset":73137,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/012.md","scopeText":"Let $P^+(n)$ be the largest prime factor of $n$. The formalization proves the joint Dickman law in ordinary natural density: for every $0<a,b<1$, the density of integers satisfying $P^+(n)\\le n^a$ and $P^+(n+1)\\le n^b$ tends to $\\rho(1/a)\\rho(1/b)$, where $\\rho$ is the Dickman function.\n\nIt also proves that each of the orderings $P^+(n)<P^+(n+1)$ and $P^+(n+1)<P^+(n)$ has natural density $1/2$.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/012.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":257554,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-joint-Dickman-law-for-consecutive-integers-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/The-joint-Dickman-law-for-consecutive-integers-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"013-01","slug":"the-additive-indecomposability-of-the-primes-September-24-2026","familyId":"013","title":"The additive indecomposability of the primes","abstract":"We prove Ostmann's inverse Goldbach conjecture: no set differing from the primes by finitely many elements can be written as $A+B$, where A and B are sets of nonnegative integers with at least two elements each.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that no finite modification of the primes can be written as $A+B$ with $A,B\\subseteq\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}$ each containing at least two elements. This resolves Ostmann's inverse Goldbach conjecture on additive indecomposability.","pdfPath":"preprints/the-additive-indecomposability-of-the-primes-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/the-additive-indecomposability-of-the-primes-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/the-additive-indecomposability-of-the-primes-September-24-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/the-additive-indecomposability-of-the-primes-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/the-additive-indecomposability-of-the-primes-September-24-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","preprints/the-additive-indecomposability-of-the-primes-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/the-additive-indecomposability-of-the-primes-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-preliminaries.tex","preprints/the-additive-indecomposability-of-the-primes-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-quadratic.tex","preprints/the-additive-indecomposability-of-the-primes-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-characters.tex","preprints/the-additive-indecomposability-of-the-primes-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-supply.tex","preprints/the-additive-indecomposability-of-the-primes-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-tree.tex","preprints/the-additive-indecomposability-of-the-primes-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-construction.tex","preprints/the-additive-indecomposability-of-the-primes-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-arithmetic.tex","preprints/the-additive-indecomposability-of-the-primes-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/the-additive-indecomposability-of-the-primes-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Ostmann's inverse Goldbach conjecture: no set differing from the primes by finitely many elements can be written as $A+B$, where $A$ and $B$ are sets of nonnegative integers with at least two elements each.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":342},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite summands and residue supports","sourceOffset":8339},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A sieve for finitely many shifts","sourceOffset":9163},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two-infinite-set theorem","sourceOffset":12829},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Complementary residue supports","sourceOffset":13917},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sizes of the summands","sourceOffset":14449},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Collision stability","sourceOffset":17138},{"kind":"section","title":"Quadratic characters with arbitrary translating centres","sourceOffset":19782},{"kind":"section","title":"Translated characters of higher order","sourceOffset":65163},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Selection of scales and positive statistics","sourceOffset":67097},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Templates and the transfer identity","sourceOffset":79779},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Frequency histories and their square weights","sourceOffset":88319}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Two infinite summands","label":"thm:main","text":"There do not exist two infinite sets $A,B\\subseteq\\N_0$ such that $(A+B)\\mathbin{\\triangle}\\mathcal P$ is finite.","sourceOffset":12926,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Ostmann's conjecture","label":"cor:ostmann","text":"If $A,B\\subseteq\\N_0$ satisfy $|A|,|B|\\ge2$, then $(A+B)\\mathbin{\\triangle}\\mathcal P$ is infinite. Equivalently, every finite modification of $\\mathcal P$ is additively indecomposable into two sets with at least two elements each.","sourceOffset":1079,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/013.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves Ostmann's inverse Goldbach conjecture. For any two sets $A,B$ of nonnegative integers, each containing at least two elements, the symmetric difference between their sumset $A+B$ and the set of primes is infinite. Thus no set differing from the primes by only finitely many elements can be decomposed as such a sumset. The linked statements include the full result and its two-infinite-summands case.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/013.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":262737,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/the-additive-indecomposability-of-the-primes-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/the-additive-indecomposability-of-the-primes-September-24-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"014-01","slug":"Global-Arthur-Enhancements-of-Cuspidal-Excursion-Parameters-October-5-2026","familyId":"014","title":"Global Arthur Enhancements of Cuspidal Excursion Parameters","abstract":"Under the finite-level Ramanujan–Arthur decomposition stated in Theorem 1.1, we construct a global Arthur enhancement for every occurring cuspidal excursion parameter at a specified full finite level for a split connected semisimple group over a global function field. A single algebraic SL2 and a commuting Weil centralizer map recover the given parameter by diagonal specialization on the entire Weil group, including inertia. The result does not assert ellipticity, Arthur-packet classification, or a multiplicity formula.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the restricted geometric Langlands equivalence for connected reductive groups on smooth projective connected curves over $\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q$ under the four stated Lie-theoretic characteristic hypotheses. Over arbitrary algebraically closed fields of characteristic p > 0, the same conclusion holds assuming additionally that p is very good for the group and $p\\nmid |W_G|$. Over global function fields, proves Ramanujan at every place for globally generic cuspidal representations of split adjoint absolutely simple exceptional groups, without characteristic or ramification-depth restrictions, and at every unramified place for cuspidal representations of split adjoint absolutely simple groups with a generic unramified component. Assuming the finite-level Ramanujan–Arthur decomposition, constructs global Arthur enhancements of occurring cuspidal excursion parameters for split connected semisimple groups at full finite level, recovering the given parameters by diagonal specialization on the entire Weil group, including inertia.","pdfPath":"preprints/Global-Arthur-Enhancements-of-Cuspidal-Excursion-Parameters-October-5-2026/manuscript.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Global-Arthur-Enhancements-of-Cuspidal-Excursion-Parameters-October-5-2026/manuscript.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Global-Arthur-Enhancements-of-Cuspidal-Excursion-Parameters-October-5-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Global-Arthur-Enhancements-of-Cuspidal-Excursion-Parameters-October-5-2026/build/manuscript.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Global-Arthur-Enhancements-of-Cuspidal-Excursion-Parameters-October-5-2026/build/manuscript.tex","sourceAbstract":"Under the finite-level Ramanujan--Arthur decomposition stated in Theorem \\ref{thm:parent}, we construct a global Arthur enhancement for every occurring cuspidal excursion parameter at a specified full finite level for a split connected semisimple group over a global function field. A single algebraic $\\mathrm{SL}_2$ and a commuting Weil centralizer map recover the given parameter by diagonal specialization on the entire Weil group, including inertia. The result does not assert ellipticity, Arthur-packet classification, or a multiplicity formula.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":614},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Excursion parameters and the statement","sourceOffset":1266},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and related work","sourceOffset":6776},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof mechanism","sourceOffset":11534},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Technical contributions and organization","sourceOffset":14965},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":17062},{"kind":"section","title":"A global support from shtuka cohomology","sourceOffset":17840},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The cohomology and its Weil actions","sourceOffset":18904},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Matrix entries and a Lie-algebra coordinate","sourceOffset":24799},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison with fixed-place path traces","sourceOffset":33738},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite-type cyclic support","sourceOffset":36911},{"kind":"section","title":"An open-orbit criterion for enhancement","sourceOffset":40100}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Global Arthur enhancement","label":"thm:main","text":"Assume Theorem \\ref{thm:parent}. For every $X,G,D,\\ell,j$ and every occurring excursion character $\\nu$ as above, there are an algebraic homomorphism \\[ \\phi_\\nu:\\SL_{2,k}\\longrightarrow L \\] and a continuous homomorphism \\[ \\tau_\\nu:\\Gamma\\longrightarrow Z_L\\bigl(\\phi_\\nu(\\SL_2)\\bigr)(k), \\] both defined over a finite extension of $\\Q_\\ell$, with the following properties. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item The differential of $\\phi_\\nu$ sends $\\begin{psmallmatrix}0&1\\\\0&0\\end{psmallmatrix}$ to $\\mathcal O_\\nu$. \\item The Zariski closure of $\\tau_\\nu(\\Gamma)$ is reductive. For every finite-dimensional algebraic representation $r$ of the split rational form of $L$ and every closed point $x\\in U$, each eigenvalue of $r(\\tau_\\nu(\\Frob_x))$ lies in $j(\\overline\\Q)$ and its inverse image under $j$ has absolute value one under every embedding $\\overline\\Q\\hookrightarrow\\C$. \\item After one fixed $L(k)$-conjugation of $\\sigma_\\nu$, \\begin{equation} \\sigma_\\nu(w)=\\tau_\\nu(w)\\, \\phi_\\nu\\begin{pmatrix}a^{\\deg(w)}&0\\\\0&a^{-\\deg(w)}\\end{pmatrix} \\qquad(w\\in\\Gamma). \\end{equation} \\end{enumerate} The centralizer is the full algebraic centralizer; it may be disconnected.","sourceOffset":5008,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Finite-level Ramanujan--Arthur decomposition {\\cite[Theorem 1.1","label":"thm:parent","text":"{Parent}}] For every occurring excursion character $\\nu$, there is a unique nilpotent $L$-orbit $\\mathcal O_\\nu\\subset\\Lie L$ such that, at every closed point $x\\in U$, the semisimple class of $\\sigma_\\nu(\\Frob_x)$ has the form \\begin{equation} \\left[ \\phi_{\\mathcal O_\\nu} \\begin{pmatrix}a^{d_x}&0\\\\0&a^{-d_x}\\end{pmatrix}c_x \\right]. \\end{equation} Here $\\phi_{\\mathcal O_\\nu}:\\SL_2\\to L$ is a Jacobson--Morozov homomorphism for $\\mathcal O_\\nu$ and $c_x$ is semisimple, centralizes its image, and is algebraic and conjugate into a compact subgroup at every complex embedding. The same orbit occurs at every $x$.","sourceOffset":3964,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":263824,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":20,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Global-Arthur-Enhancements-of-Cuspidal-Excursion-Parameters-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"014-02","slug":"Rationality-of-the-Canonical-Unramified-Arthur-Filtration-September-24-2026","familyId":"014","title":"Rationality of the Canonical Unramified Arthur Filtration","abstract":"Under the characteristic hypotheses of restricted geometric Langlands theory, we prove that the canonical Arthur filtration on finitely supported unramified automorphic functions is defined over ℚ for split connected semisimple groups. The result includes the noncuspidal part and every closed invariant nilpotent support, establishing the rationality conjecture of Gaitsgory–Lafforgue–Raskin in this setting.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the restricted geometric Langlands equivalence for connected reductive groups on smooth projective connected curves over $\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q$ under the four stated Lie-theoretic characteristic hypotheses. Over arbitrary algebraically closed fields of characteristic p > 0, the same conclusion holds assuming additionally that p is very good for the group and $p\\nmid |W_G|$. Over global function fields, proves Ramanujan at every place for globally generic cuspidal representations of split adjoint absolutely simple exceptional groups, without characteristic or ramification-depth restrictions, and at every unramified place for cuspidal representations of split adjoint absolutely simple groups with a generic unramified component. Assuming the finite-level Ramanujan–Arthur decomposition, constructs global Arthur enhancements of occurring cuspidal excursion parameters for split connected semisimple groups at full finite level, recovering the given parameters by diagonal specialization on the entire Weil group, including inertia.","pdfPath":"preprints/Rationality-of-the-Canonical-Unramified-Arthur-Filtration-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Rationality-of-the-Canonical-Unramified-Arthur-Filtration-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Rationality-of-the-Canonical-Unramified-Arthur-Filtration-September-24-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Rationality-of-the-Canonical-Unramified-Arthur-Filtration-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Rationality-of-the-Canonical-Unramified-Arthur-Filtration-September-24-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/Rationality-of-the-Canonical-Unramified-Arthur-Filtration-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Rationality-of-the-Canonical-Unramified-Arthur-Filtration-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-inputs.tex","preprints/Rationality-of-the-Canonical-Unramified-Arthur-Filtration-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-components.tex","preprints/Rationality-of-the-Canonical-Unramified-Arthur-Filtration-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-orbits.tex","preprints/Rationality-of-the-Canonical-Unramified-Arthur-Filtration-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-traces.tex","preprints/Rationality-of-the-Canonical-Unramified-Arthur-Filtration-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-measures.tex","preprints/Rationality-of-the-Canonical-Unramified-Arthur-Filtration-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-descent.tex","preprints/Rationality-of-the-Canonical-Unramified-Arthur-Filtration-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Rationality-of-the-Canonical-Unramified-Arthur-Filtration-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"Under the characteristic hypotheses of restricted geometric Langlands theory, we prove that the canonical Arthur filtration on finitely supported unramified automorphic functions is defined over $\\Q$ for split connected semisimple groups. The result includes the noncuspidal part and every closed invariant nilpotent support, establishing the rationality conjecture of Gaitsgory--Lafforgue--Raskin in this setting.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":513},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Hypotheses and the filtration","sourceOffset":2385},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A bilinear detection criterion","sourceOffset":7086},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The local arguments","sourceOffset":9523},{"kind":"section","title":"Geometric inputs and Frobenius conventions","sourceOffset":14029},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Restricted Langlands and functions","sourceOffset":14574},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Duality and finite evaluations","sourceOffset":18617},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The geometry of a spectral component","sourceOffset":21099},{"kind":"section","title":"A quadratic model with its Frobenius action","sourceOffset":25008},{"kind":"subsection","title":"What is recovered from completion at the closed orbit","sourceOffset":27157},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Simultaneous linearization","sourceOffset":39038},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Which action contracts Hom complexes","sourceOffset":48656}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"intro:main","text":"Under Assumption \\ref{intro:characteristic}, for every prime $\\ell\\ne\\operatorname{char}(\\mathbb F_q)$ and every closed invariant $Y\\subset\\Nhat$, the natural map \\begin{equation} E\\otimes_\\Q\\bigl(\\cA_\\Q\\cap\\cF_Y\\cA_E\\bigr) \\xrightarrow{\\ \\sim\\ }\\cF_Y\\cA_E \\end{equation} is an isomorphism. The intersection is taken inside $\\cA_E$.","sourceOffset":6137,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Restricted geometric Langlands","label":"inp:langlands","text":"There is a Frobenius-invariant union $Z$ of connected components of $\\LocSys^{\\mathrm{restr}}_{\\Ghat}(\\overline X)$ and an equivalence \\[ \\mathbb L_G^{\\mathrm{restr}}:\\cC \\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\IndCoh_{\\Nhat}(Z). \\] It identifies $\\cC_Y$ with $\\IndCoh_Y(Z)$ for every closed $\\Ghat$-invariant $Y\\subset\\Nhat$ and intertwines Hecke functors with tensoring by the corresponding evaluation bundles. If $f:Z\\to Z$ sends a local system to its pullback along geometric Frobenius of $\\overline X$, the functor corresponding to $\\Phi=(\\operatorname{Frob}_{\\Bun})_*$ is $f^!$. The embedding $\\cC\\hookrightarrow\\Shv(\\Bun_G(\\overline X),\\E)$ preserves compact objects.","sourceOffset":14623,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"assumption","name":"","label":"intro:characteristic","text":"The following four conditions hold. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item The Lie algebra $\\mathfrak h$ has an $H$-invariant nondegenerate symmetric bilinear form whose restriction to the center of $\\Lie(M)$ is nondegenerate for every Levi subgroup $M$ of $H$. \\item For each Levi subgroup $M$ of $H$, including $H$ itself, and each maximal torus $T_M\\subset M$, restriction induces an isomorphism \\[ k_0[\\mathfrak m]^M\\xrightarrow{\\ \\sim\\ } k_0[\\mathfrak t_M]^{W_M},\\qquad \\mathfrak m=\\Lie(M),\\quad \\mathfrak t_M=\\Lie(T_M),\\quad W_M=N_M(T_M)/T_M. \\] \\item The scheme-theoretic centralize","sourceOffset":2644,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":162314,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":17,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Rationality-of-the-Canonical-Unramified-Arthur-Filtration-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"014-03","slug":"Ramanujan-Arthur-Decompositions-of-Cuspidal-Functions-at-Full-Finite-Level-September-24-2026","familyId":"014","title":"Ramanujan-Arthur Decompositions of Cuspidal Functions at Full Finite Level","abstract":"We prove rational and $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}_\\ell$ decompositions of cuspidal automorphic functions for split semisimple groups over global function fields into subspaces indexed by nilpotent orbits of the dual group. The decompositions hold at every full finite level, with arbitrary divisor multiplicities. Each summand is governed by the same orbit at every unramified place and every complex embedding. At trivial level this proves Conjectures 3.4.5 and 3.4.6 of Gaitsgory–Lafforgue–Raskin. For split connected adjoint absolutely simple groups, we also prove that a cuspidal automorphic representation with one generic unramified local component is tempered at every unramified place. Thus globally generic cuspidal representations in this scope satisfy the unramified part of the generalized Ramanujan conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the restricted geometric Langlands equivalence for connected reductive groups on smooth projective connected curves over $\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q$ under the four stated Lie-theoretic characteristic hypotheses. Over arbitrary algebraically closed fields of characteristic p > 0, the same conclusion holds assuming additionally that p is very good for the group and $p\\nmid |W_G|$. Over global function fields, proves Ramanujan at every place for globally generic cuspidal representations of split adjoint absolutely simple exceptional groups, without characteristic or ramification-depth restrictions, and at every unramified place for cuspidal representations of split adjoint absolutely simple groups with a generic unramified component. Assuming the finite-level Ramanujan–Arthur decomposition, constructs global Arthur enhancements of occurring cuspidal excursion parameters for split connected semisimple groups at full finite level, recovering the given parameters by diagonal specialization on the entire Weil group, including inertia.","pdfPath":"preprints/Ramanujan-Arthur-Decompositions-of-Cuspidal-Functions-at-Full-Finite-Level-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Ramanujan-Arthur-Decompositions-of-Cuspidal-Functions-at-Full-Finite-Level-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Ramanujan-Arthur-Decompositions-of-Cuspidal-Functions-at-Full-Finite-Level-September-24-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Ramanujan-Arthur-Decompositions-of-Cuspidal-Functions-at-Full-Finite-Level-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Ramanujan-Arthur-Decompositions-of-Cuspidal-Functions-at-Full-Finite-Level-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Ramanujan-Arthur-Decompositions-of-Cuspidal-Functions-at-Full-Finite-Level-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Ramanujan-Arthur-Decompositions-of-Cuspidal-Functions-at-Full-Finite-Level-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-categorical.tex","preprints/Ramanujan-Arthur-Decompositions-of-Cuspidal-Functions-at-Full-Finite-Level-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-amplitude.tex","preprints/Ramanujan-Arthur-Decompositions-of-Cuspidal-Functions-at-Full-Finite-Level-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-rotation.tex","preprints/Ramanujan-Arthur-Decompositions-of-Cuspidal-Functions-at-Full-Finite-Level-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-extraction.tex","preprints/Ramanujan-Arthur-Decompositions-of-Cuspidal-Functions-at-Full-Finite-Level-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-global.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Ramanujan-Arthur-Decompositions-of-Cuspidal-Functions-at-Full-Finite-Level-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove rational and $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}_\\ell$ decompositions of cuspidal automorphic functions for split semisimple groups over global function fields into subspaces indexed by nilpotent orbits of the dual group. The decompositions hold at every full finite level, with arbitrary divisor multiplicities. Each summand is governed by the same orbit at every unramified place and every complex embedding. At trivial level this proves Conjectures 3.4.5 and 3.4.6 of Gaitsgory--Lafforgue--Raskin. For split connected adjoint absolutely simple groups, we also prove that a cuspidal automorphic representation with one generic unramified local component is tempered at every unramified place. Thus globally generic cuspidal representations in this scope satisfy the unramified part of the generalized Ramanujan conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":882},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The statement","sourceOffset":1722},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Generic cuspidal representations","sourceOffset":6368},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context","sourceOffset":8024},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":11672},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and conventions","sourceOffset":16026},{"kind":"section","title":"Local kernels and their Frobenius traces","sourceOffset":17275},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and change of characteristic","sourceOffset":18588},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Central kernels and Springer tests","sourceOffset":30876},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The geometric cyclic functor","sourceOffset":43004},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Hecke characters and finite flag transfers","sourceOffset":47472},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The algebra of a twisted loop","sourceOffset":56771}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"main:decomposition","text":"For every \\(X,G,D,\\ell\\) as above, the natural addition maps \\begin{equation} \\bigoplus_{\\mathcal O}C_{D,\\mathcal O,\\mathbb Q} \\xrightarrow{\\ \\sim\\ }C_{D,\\mathbb Q}, \\qquad \\bigoplus_{\\mathcal O}C_{D,\\mathcal O,\\ell} \\xrightarrow{\\ \\sim\\ }C_{D,\\ell} \\end{equation} are isomorphisms. The sums range over the nilpotent orbits of \\(\\Gd\\).","sourceOffset":5548,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Unramified generalized Ramanujan","label":"main:generic-ramanujan","text":"Let \\(F\\) be the function field of a smooth projective geometrically connected curve over a finite field, and let \\(G/F\\) be split connected adjoint absolutely simple. Let \\(\\pi=\\bigotimes'_x\\pi_x\\) be a complex cuspidal automorphic representation of \\(G(\\mathbb A_F)\\). If \\(\\pi_v\\) is unramified and generic at one place \\(v\\), then \\(\\pi_x\\) is tempered at every place \\(x\\) at which it is unramified. In particular, every globally generic cuspidal automorphic representation of \\(G(\\mathbb A_F)\\) is tempered at every unramified place.","sourceOffset":7100,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":235933,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":23,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Ramanujan-Arthur-Decompositions-of-Cuspidal-Functions-at-Full-Finite-Level-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"014-04","slug":"Temperedness-at-ramified-places-for-globally-generic-exceptional-groups-October-5-2026","familyId":"014","title":"Temperedness at ramified places for globally generic exceptional groups","abstract":"We prove the generalized Ramanujan conjecture for globally generic cuspidal automorphic representations of split connected adjoint exceptional groups over global function fields. Every local component is tempered, without restrictions on characteristic or ramification depth. The proof extends the unramified Ramanujan theorem of a companion paper to all ramified places.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the restricted geometric Langlands equivalence for connected reductive groups on smooth projective connected curves over $\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q$ under the four stated Lie-theoretic characteristic hypotheses. Over arbitrary algebraically closed fields of characteristic p > 0, the same conclusion holds assuming additionally that p is very good for the group and $p\\nmid |W_G|$. Over global function fields, proves Ramanujan at every place for globally generic cuspidal representations of split adjoint absolutely simple exceptional groups, without characteristic or ramification-depth restrictions, and at every unramified place for cuspidal representations of split adjoint absolutely simple groups with a generic unramified component. Assuming the finite-level Ramanujan–Arthur decomposition, constructs global Arthur enhancements of occurring cuspidal excursion parameters for split connected semisimple groups at full finite level, recovering the given parameters by diagonal specialization on the entire Weil group, including inertia.","pdfPath":"preprints/Temperedness-at-ramified-places-for-globally-generic-exceptional-groups-October-5-2026/ramified-ramanujan.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Temperedness-at-ramified-places-for-globally-generic-exceptional-groups-October-5-2026/ramified-ramanujan.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Temperedness-at-ramified-places-for-globally-generic-exceptional-groups-October-5-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Temperedness-at-ramified-places-for-globally-generic-exceptional-groups-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Temperedness-at-ramified-places-for-globally-generic-exceptional-groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Temperedness-at-ramified-places-for-globally-generic-exceptional-groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/parameters.tex","preprints/Temperedness-at-ramified-places-for-globally-generic-exceptional-groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/monodromy.tex","preprints/Temperedness-at-ramified-places-for-globally-generic-exceptional-groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/local-coefficients.tex","preprints/Temperedness-at-ramified-places-for-globally-generic-exceptional-groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/local-criterion.tex","preprints/Temperedness-at-ramified-places-for-globally-generic-exceptional-groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/global.tex","preprints/Temperedness-at-ramified-places-for-globally-generic-exceptional-groups-October-5-2026/build/references/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Temperedness-at-ramified-places-for-globally-generic-exceptional-groups-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the generalized Ramanujan conjecture for globally generic cuspidal automorphic representations of split connected adjoint exceptional groups over global function fields. Every local component is tempered, without restrictions on characteristic or ramification depth. The proof extends the unramified Ramanujan theorem of a companion paper to all ramified places.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":417},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and the local problem","sourceOffset":2337},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its reusable local criterion","sourceOffset":5887},{"kind":"section","title":"Local conventions and parameter theorems","sourceOffset":9370},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Groups, induction, and Whittaker characters","sourceOffset":9741},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weil--Deligne parameters and local factors","sourceOffset":13605},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The parameter inputs","sourceOffset":15779},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Weil part of a global restriction","sourceOffset":19842},{"kind":"section","title":"Adjoint regularity and monodromy","sourceOffset":22393},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A ratio independent of monodromy","sourceOffset":23065},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Consequences of purity","sourceOffset":24813},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Adjoint regularity determines the orbit","sourceOffset":28042}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $G/F$ be split connected adjoint absolutely simple of type $G_2$, $F_4$, $E_6$, $E_7$, or $E_8$. If $\\pi=\\bigotimes'_v\\pi_v$ is a complex globally generic cuspidal automorphic representation of $G(\\A_F)$, then $\\pi_v$ is tempered for every place $v$. There is no restriction on the characteristic of $F$, the ramification depth, or the local representation type.","sourceOffset":1489,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Lafforgue; Genestier--Lafforgue","label":"thm:parameters","text":"For the split groups considered here the following hold. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item An irreducible smooth local representation $\\sigma$ has a semisimple Weil parameter $\\rho_\\sigma$, with finite inertia image. The parametrization is compatible with normalized parabolic induction, tori and local class field theory, group isomorphisms, central characters, and twists through algebraic characters of the group. \\item A cuspidal automorphic representation $\\Pi$ over a function field, with finite-order central character, occurs in an excursion summand with a semisimple global parameter $\\Sigma_\\Pi$. This parameter is defined over a finite extension of $\\Q_\\ell$ and matches normalized Satake parameters away from a finite set. \\item At every place $v$, the semisimplification of $\\Sigma_\\Pi|_{W_{F_v}}$ is conjugate to $\\rho_{\\Pi_v}$. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":16010,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":72630,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Temperedness-at-ramified-places-for-globally-generic-exceptional-groups-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"014-05","slug":"The-Restricted-Geometric-Langlands-Equivalence-in-Positive-Characteristic-September-24-2026","familyId":"014","title":"The Restricted Geometric Langlands Equivalence in Positive Characteristic","abstract":"We prove the $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}_\\ell$-linear restricted geometric Langlands equivalence for smooth projective connected curves and connected reductive groups in characteristic p > 0, with ℓ ≠ p, in two regimes. Over $\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q$, we assume the four characteristic conditions of the restricted theory stated below. Over arbitrary algebraically closed fields, we assume these conditions and additionally that p is very good and does not divide the Weyl-group order. This proves Gaitsgory–Raskin's full-support conjecture [[12, Conjecture 1.3.10]](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.02237v1) in these regimes.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the restricted geometric Langlands equivalence for connected reductive groups on smooth projective connected curves over $\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q$ under the four stated Lie-theoretic characteristic hypotheses. Over arbitrary algebraically closed fields of characteristic p > 0, the same conclusion holds assuming additionally that p is very good for the group and $p\\nmid |W_G|$. Over global function fields, proves Ramanujan at every place for globally generic cuspidal representations of split adjoint absolutely simple exceptional groups, without characteristic or ramification-depth restrictions, and at every unramified place for cuspidal representations of split adjoint absolutely simple groups with a generic unramified component. Assuming the finite-level Ramanujan–Arthur decomposition, constructs global Arthur enhancements of occurring cuspidal excursion parameters for split connected semisimple groups at full finite level, recovering the given parameters by diagonal specialization on the entire Weil group, including inertia.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Restricted-Geometric-Langlands-Equivalence-in-Positive-Characteristic-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Restricted-Geometric-Langlands-Equivalence-in-Positive-Characteristic-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Restricted-Geometric-Langlands-Equivalence-in-Positive-Characteristic-September-24-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/The-Restricted-Geometric-Langlands-Equivalence-in-Positive-Characteristic-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Restricted-Geometric-Langlands-Equivalence-in-Positive-Characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-Restricted-Geometric-Langlands-Equivalence-in-Positive-Characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-specialization.tex","preprints/The-Restricted-Geometric-Langlands-Equivalence-in-Positive-Characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-lie.tex","preprints/The-Restricted-Geometric-Langlands-Equivalence-in-Positive-Characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-hecke.tex","preprints/The-Restricted-Geometric-Langlands-Equivalence-in-Positive-Characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-two-hecke.tex","preprints/The-Restricted-Geometric-Langlands-Equivalence-in-Positive-Characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-slices.tex","preprints/The-Restricted-Geometric-Langlands-Equivalence-in-Positive-Characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-support.tex","preprints/The-Restricted-Geometric-Langlands-Equivalence-in-Positive-Characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-consequences.tex","preprints/The-Restricted-Geometric-Langlands-Equivalence-in-Positive-Characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-coefficients.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Restricted-Geometric-Langlands-Equivalence-in-Positive-Characteristic-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}_\\ell$-linear restricted geometric Langlands equivalence for smooth projective connected curves and connected reductive groups in characteristic $p>0$, with $\\ell\\ne p$, in two regimes. Over $\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q$, we assume the four characteristic conditions of the restricted theory stated below. Over arbitrary algebraically closed fields, we assume these conditions and additionally that $p$ is very good and does not divide the Weyl-group order. This proves Gaitsgory--Raskin's full-support conjecture \\cite[Conjecture 1.3.10]{GR} in these regimes.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":678},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The spectral stack and the two hypothesis regimes","sourceOffset":5089},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Full support","sourceOffset":9618},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rational coefficients","sourceOffset":11394},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The specialization and slicing argument","sourceOffset":14355},{"kind":"section","title":"Specialization and a missing spectral component","sourceOffset":17731},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A trait and geometric specialization","sourceOffset":18450},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The spectral summand and compactness","sourceOffset":23729},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Hecke vanishing with an arbitrary leg","sourceOffset":28121},{"kind":"section","title":"A Levi reduction for a formal Higgs field","sourceOffset":33222},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Root pairings and Jordan parts","sourceOffset":35076},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite-field regime","sourceOffset":41396}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Full support","label":"thm:full-support","text":"Let $k,\\ell,E,X,G$ be as above. Under either Hypothesis \\ref{hyp:A} or Hypothesis \\ref{hyp:B}, the inclusion $Y'\\subset Y$ is equality as spectral prestacks. Consequently the given functor is a $\\QCoh(Y)$-linear equivalence \\[ \\mathbb L_G^{\\mathrm{restr}}: \\Shv_{\\Nilp}(\\Bun_G(X))\\xrightarrow{\\ \\sim\\ } \\IndCoh_{\\Nilp}(Y). \\]","sourceOffset":10078,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Rational spectral support","label":"thm:rational-support","text":"For the rational datum just specified, every nonempty open-and-closed substack $U\\subset Y_0$ has \\[ \\QCoh(U)\\underset{\\QCoh(Y_0)}\\otimes\\mathcal C_0\\ne0. \\] Consequently, if $Y'_0\\subset Y_0$ is an open-and-closed support union whose complementary structure idempotent acts by zero on $\\mathcal C_0$, then $Y'_0=Y_0$ as spectral prestacks. In particular this applies to the support of any given $\\QCoh(Y_0)$-linear primed equivalence.","sourceOffset":12868,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"hypothesis","name":"Common characteristic assumptions","label":"hyp:common","text":"The following conditions hold for $G/k$. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item There is a nondegenerate $G$-invariant symmetric bilinear form $\\kappa$ on $\\mathfrak g$ whose restriction to the center of $\\Lie(M)$ is nondegenerate for every Levi subgroup $M$. \\item For every Levi subgroup $M$, including $G$, and a maximal torus $T_M\\subset M$, Chevalley restriction is an isomorphism \\[ k[\\Lie(M)]^M\\xrightarrow{\\ \\sim\\ } k[\\Lie(T_M)]^{W_M},\\qquad W_M=N_M(T_M)/T_M. \\] \\item The scheme-theoretic centralizer in $G$ of every semisimple element of $\\mathfrak g$ is a Levi subgroup. \\item F","sourceOffset":7183,"truncated":true},{"kind":"hypothesis","name":"Regime A: finite-field descent","label":"hyp:A","text":"There are $q=p^r$, a smooth projective geometrically connected curve $X_0/\\mathbb F_q$, and a split connected reductive group $G_0/\\mathbb F_q$ such that \\[ k=\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q,\\qquad X=X_0\\times_{\\mathbb F_q}k,\\qquad G=G_0\\times_{\\mathbb F_q}k. \\] Hypothesis \\ref{hyp:common} holds. No additional restriction on the order of the Weyl group is imposed in this regime.","sourceOffset":8401,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":142991,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":4,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Restricted-Geometric-Langlands-Equivalence-in-Positive-Characteristic-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"014-06","slug":"Constructible-tame-Hecke-eigensheaves-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026","familyId":"014","title":"Constructible tame Hecke eigensheaves in positive characteristic","abstract":"We construct nonzero locally constructible perverse Hecke eigensheaves with Borel level at one marked point on a smooth projective curve of genus at least two over an algebraic closure of a finite field. The parameter is a Zariski-dense geometric ℓ-adic local system with tame regular-unipotent monodromy. The group is simple and simply connected and satisfies four explicit Lie-theoretic characteristic hypotheses. The eigensheaves have parabolic nilpotent singular support, and their eigenisomorphisms are compatible with tensor products, permutations, and fusion.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the restricted geometric Langlands equivalence for connected reductive groups on smooth projective connected curves over $\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q$ under the four stated Lie-theoretic characteristic hypotheses. Over arbitrary algebraically closed fields of characteristic p > 0, the same conclusion holds assuming additionally that p is very good for the group and $p\\nmid |W_G|$. Over global function fields, proves Ramanujan at every place for globally generic cuspidal representations of split adjoint absolutely simple exceptional groups, without characteristic or ramification-depth restrictions, and at every unramified place for cuspidal representations of split adjoint absolutely simple groups with a generic unramified component. Assuming the finite-level Ramanujan–Arthur decomposition, constructs global Arthur enhancements of occurring cuspidal excursion parameters for split connected semisimple groups at full finite level, recovering the given parameters by diagonal specialization on the entire Weil group, including inertia.","pdfPath":"preprints/Constructible-tame-Hecke-eigensheaves-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/constructible-tame-hecke-eigensheaves-positive-characteristic.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Constructible-tame-Hecke-eigensheaves-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/constructible-tame-hecke-eigensheaves-positive-characteristic.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Constructible-tame-Hecke-eigensheaves-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Constructible-tame-Hecke-eigensheaves-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/main.tex","preprints/Constructible-tame-Hecke-eigensheaves-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/macros.tex","preprints/Constructible-tame-Hecke-eigensheaves-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/introduction.tex","preprints/Constructible-tame-Hecke-eigensheaves-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/foundations.tex","preprints/Constructible-tame-Hecke-eigensheaves-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/geometry.tex","preprints/Constructible-tame-Hecke-eigensheaves-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/detection.tex","preprints/Constructible-tame-Hecke-eigensheaves-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/specialization.tex","preprints/Constructible-tame-Hecke-eigensheaves-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/perverse.tex","preprints/Constructible-tame-Hecke-eigensheaves-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/descent.tex","preprints/Constructible-tame-Hecke-eigensheaves-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/construction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Constructible-tame-Hecke-eigensheaves-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct nonzero locally constructible perverse Hecke eigensheaves with Borel level at one marked point on a smooth projective curve of genus at least two over an algebraic closure of a finite field. The parameter is a Zariski-dense geometric $\\ell$-adic local system with tame regular-unipotent monodromy. The group is simple and simply connected and satisfies four explicit Lie-theoretic characteristic hypotheses. The eigensheaves have parabolic nilpotent singular support, and their eigenisomorphisms are compatible with tensor products, permutations, and fusion.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":634},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The setting and the main theorem","sourceOffset":1154},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":6271},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two specializations and the proof strategy","sourceOffset":9850},{"kind":"section","title":"Sheaf conventions and the relative Hecke system","sourceOffset":17737},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local constructibility and flag levels","sourceOffset":18151},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Geometric specialization","sourceOffset":20979},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Relative Satake normalization","sourceOffset":27775},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The use of Betti realization","sourceOffset":31038},{"kind":"section","title":"Nilpotent cotangent cones and transverse modifications","sourceOffset":32705},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cotangent vectors and the dimension bound","sourceOffset":33835},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A central cocharacter detecting a nonnilpotent value","sourceOffset":41665}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Assume \\ref{hyp:form}--\\ref{hyp:nilpotent}. For every Zariski-dense parameter \\eqref{eq:parameter} satisfying \\eqref{eq:regular-monodromy}, there is a nonzero locally constructible perverse geometric $E$-adic sheaf $M$ on $\\cA$ such that \\[ \\SS(M)\\subset\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{par}}. \\] For every finite set $I$ and every family $(V_i)_{i\\in I}$, it carries isomorphisms \\begin{equation} \\Hecke_{I,(V_i)}(M) \\simeq M\\boxtimes\\Bigl(\\mathop{\\boxtimes}_{i\\in I}(V_i)_\\sigma\\Bigr) \\quad\\text{on }\\cA\\times U^I, \\end{equation} natural in the representations and coherently compatible with the tensor unit, convolution, permutations of legs, and fusion along every collision diagonal.","sourceOffset":5526,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Geometric nearby-cycle formalism","label":"prop:nearby-foundations","text":"For the traits just specified, geometric nearby cycles preserve bounded constructibility and are exact for the perverse $t$-structures on the geometric fibers. They are unchanged by finite extension of the trait inside $\\overline K$. The maps \\eqref{eq:found-pull-exchange}, \\eqref{eq:found-push-exchange}, and \\eqref{eq:found-exterior-exchange} are isomorphisms, respectively, for smooth $f$, for proper $f$, and for exterior products of bounded constructible complexes. These assertions extend to locally constructible complexes on the smooth stacks used here by smooth descent; proper morphisms in this extension are representable. Let $L_{\\bar\\eta}$ be a lisse finite-rank $E$-sheaf on a model's geometric generic fiber. Suppose it has, after a finite extension of the coefficient field, a lisse lattice whose finite reductions extend to lisse sheaves after finite extensions of $S$. Suppose these extensions are compatible on further common extensions and have reductions of a lisse special-fiber sheaf $L_s$. The finite extension of $S$ may depend on the reduction. Then, for any map $g:Y\\to Z$ to that model and any $F\\in D^b_c(Y_{\\bar\\eta},E)$, the natural map is an isomorphism \\begin{equation} \\Psi_Y F\\otimes g_s^*L_s \\xrightarrow{\\ \\sim\\ } \\Psi_Y(F\\otimes g_{\\bar\\eta}^*L_{\\bar\\eta}). \\end{equation} The statement also holds locally on the stacks in question, and is compatible with tensor products and morphisms of the lisse systems.","sourceOffset":22909,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":191490,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Constructible-tame-Hecke-eigensheaves-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"014-07","slug":"Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-with-Several-Marked-Points-October-5-2026","familyId":"014","title":"Tame Hecke Eigensheaves with Several Marked Points","abstract":"For SLn in characteristic p > n, we construct nonzero locally constructible perverse Hecke eigensheaves with Borel level at two or more marked points on a smooth projective curve of genus at least two over an algebraic closure of a finite field. The parameter is a Zariski-dense geometric ℓ-adic PGLn-local system with unipotent tame monodromy; its nilpotent logarithms may have any Jordan type, including zero. The eigensheaves have parabolic nilpotent singular support, and their eigenisomorphisms retain the full tensor and fusion structure.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the restricted geometric Langlands equivalence for connected reductive groups on smooth projective connected curves over $\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q$ under the four stated Lie-theoretic characteristic hypotheses. Over arbitrary algebraically closed fields of characteristic p > 0, the same conclusion holds assuming additionally that p is very good for the group and $p\\nmid |W_G|$. Over global function fields, proves Ramanujan at every place for globally generic cuspidal representations of split adjoint absolutely simple exceptional groups, without characteristic or ramification-depth restrictions, and at every unramified place for cuspidal representations of split adjoint absolutely simple groups with a generic unramified component. Assuming the finite-level Ramanujan–Arthur decomposition, constructs global Arthur enhancements of occurring cuspidal excursion parameters for split connected semisimple groups at full finite level, recovering the given parameters by diagonal specialization on the entire Weil group, including inertia.","pdfPath":"preprints/Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-with-Several-Marked-Points-October-5-2026/Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-with-Several-Marked-Points.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-with-Several-Marked-Points-October-5-2026/Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-with-Several-Marked-Points.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-with-Several-Marked-Points-October-5-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-with-Several-Marked-Points-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-with-Several-Marked-Points-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-with-Several-Marked-Points-October-5-2026/build/sections/history.tex","preprints/Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-with-Several-Marked-Points-October-5-2026/build/sections/foundations.tex","preprints/Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-with-Several-Marked-Points-October-5-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-with-Several-Marked-Points-October-5-2026/build/sections/detection.tex","preprints/Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-with-Several-Marked-Points-October-5-2026/build/sections/perverse.tex","preprints/Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-with-Several-Marked-Points-October-5-2026/build/sections/descent.tex","preprints/Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-with-Several-Marked-Points-October-5-2026/build/sections/construction.tex","preprints/Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-with-Several-Marked-Points-October-5-2026/build/sections/lifting.tex","preprints/Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-with-Several-Marked-Points-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-with-Several-Marked-Points-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For $\\SL_n$ in characteristic $p>n$, we construct nonzero locally constructible perverse Hecke eigensheaves with Borel level at two or more marked points on a smooth projective curve of genus at least two over an algebraic closure of a finite field. The parameter is a Zariski-dense geometric $\\ell$-adic $\\PGL_n$-local system with unipotent tame monodromy; its nilpotent logarithms may have any Jordan type, including zero. The eigensheaves have parabolic nilpotent singular support, and their eigenisomorphisms retain the full tensor and fusion structure.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":656},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The theorem","sourceOffset":1174},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and antecedents","sourceOffset":4606},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and the several-point extensions","sourceOffset":9489},{"kind":"section","title":"Level structures, sheaves, and specialization","sourceOffset":14476},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The level stacks","sourceOffset":14983},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local constructibility and relative Satake","sourceOffset":18036},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Geometric nearby cycles","sourceOffset":21703},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Betti comparison","sourceOffset":27462},{"kind":"section","title":"Nilpotent covectors and transverse Hecke modifications","sourceOffset":28533},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The matrix hypotheses and the cotangent cone","sourceOffset":29164},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A detecting cocharacter","sourceOffset":36410}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"intro:main","text":"For every parameter \\eqref{intro:parameter} satisfying the preceding hypotheses, there is a nonzero locally constructible perverse geometric $E$-adic sheaf $M$ on $\\cA$ with \\[ \\SS(M)\\subset\\Lambda_D. \\] For every finite set $I$ and every family $(V_i)_{i\\in I}$, it has isomorphisms \\begin{equation} \\Hecke_{I,(V_i)}(M) \\simeq M\\boxtimes\\Bigl(\\mathop{\\boxtimes}_{i\\in I}(V_i)_\\rho\\Bigr) \\qquad\\text{on }\\cA\\times U^I, \\end{equation} natural in the representations and coherent for the tensor unit, convolution, permutations, and fusion on every collision diagonal. These isomorphisms use the relative normalization above.","sourceOffset":3721,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Nearby-cycle input","label":"found:nearby","text":"For finite-type $S$-schemes, geometric nearby cycles preserve bounded constructibility, are perverse exact, commute with smooth pullback and proper pushforward, and satisfy exterior-product comparison. They are unchanged by finite extensions of the trait inside the fixed geometric generic field. The same statements hold locally on the smooth stacks used here, with representable proper maps. Let $Z$ be a finite-type $S$-scheme. Suppose a lisse finite-rank sheaf $L_{\\bar\\eta}$ on $Z_{\\bar\\eta}$ has a lattice over the integers of a finite extension of $\\mathbb Q_\\ell$. Assume that each finite reduction extends lisse over $Z$ after a finite extension of $S$, compatibly on further common extensions, with special reductions belonging to a fixed lisse sheaf $L_0$ on $Z_0$. The trait extension may depend on the reduction. Then for any map $g:Y\\to Z$ of finite-type $S$-schemes and any bounded constructible $F$ on $Y_{\\bar\\eta}$, \\begin{equation} \\Psi_YF\\otimes g_0^*L_0 \\xrightarrow{\\sim} \\Psi_Y(F\\otimes g_{\\bar\\eta}^*L_{\\bar\\eta}). \\end{equation} This is natural in maps and tensor products of the lisse systems, and holds locally on the smooth stacks in use.","sourceOffset":22167,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"found:coherence","text":"A coherent eigenstructure for a tensor local system $V\\mapsto L_V$ on $U$ consists of natural isomorphisms \\[ \\Hecke_{I,\\boldsymbol V}(F) \\simeq F\\boxtimes\\Bigl(\\mathop{\\boxtimes}_{i\\in I}L_{V_i}\\Bigr) \\] for all finite sets and representations, compatible with the unit, successive modifications and convolution, permutations, and fusion. Explicitly, if $a:I\\twoheadrightarrow J$ and $\\delta_a:U^J\\to U^I$ is its collision diagonal, the fusion comparison uses ordinary pullback and identifies \\[ (1\\times\\delta_a)^*\\Hecke_{I,\\boldsymbol V}(F) \\simeq\\Hecke_{J,\\boldsymbol W}(F),\\qquad W_j=\\bigotimes_","sourceOffset":20706,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"geom:cone","text":"For any of these stacks $\\cB$, let $\\Lambda\\subset T^*\\cB$ be the cone of Higgs fields generically nilpotent on each component curve in its presentation. For a smooth chart $f:S\\to\\cB$, write $\\Lambda_S=f^\\circ\\Lambda$.","sourceOffset":32067,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":153350,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-with-Several-Marked-Points-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"014-08","slug":"Frobenius-Structures-on-Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-October-5-2026","familyId":"014","title":"Frobenius Structures on Tame Hecke Eigensheaves","abstract":"We construct nonzero perverse Weil Hecke eigensheaves for SLn with Borel level at one marked point, after a finite extension of the field of constants. The parameter is a geometrically dense arithmetic PGLn-local system with tame regular-unipotent monodromy on a once-punctured curve of genus at least two over a finite field of characteristic p > n. The full multi-leg eigenstructure is Frobenius compatible with the prescribed arithmetic eigenvalue, and the geometric sheaf has parabolic nilpotent singular support.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the restricted geometric Langlands equivalence for connected reductive groups on smooth projective connected curves over $\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q$ under the four stated Lie-theoretic characteristic hypotheses. Over arbitrary algebraically closed fields of characteristic p > 0, the same conclusion holds assuming additionally that p is very good for the group and $p\\nmid |W_G|$. Over global function fields, proves Ramanujan at every place for globally generic cuspidal representations of split adjoint absolutely simple exceptional groups, without characteristic or ramification-depth restrictions, and at every unramified place for cuspidal representations of split adjoint absolutely simple groups with a generic unramified component. Assuming the finite-level Ramanujan–Arthur decomposition, constructs global Arthur enhancements of occurring cuspidal excursion parameters for split connected semisimple groups at full finite level, recovering the given parameters by diagonal specialization on the entire Weil group, including inertia.","pdfPath":"preprints/Frobenius-Structures-on-Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-October-5-2026/tame-hecke-frobenius.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Frobenius-Structures-on-Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-October-5-2026/tame-hecke-frobenius.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Frobenius-Structures-on-Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-October-5-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Frobenius-Structures-on-Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Frobenius-Structures-on-Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Frobenius-Structures-on-Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-October-5-2026/build/sections/conventions.tex","preprints/Frobenius-Structures-on-Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-October-5-2026/build/sections/unramified.tex","preprints/Frobenius-Structures-on-Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-October-5-2026/build/sections/parameters.tex","preprints/Frobenius-Structures-on-Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-October-5-2026/build/figures/node.tex","preprints/Frobenius-Structures-on-Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-October-5-2026/build/sections/nodal.tex","preprints/Frobenius-Structures-on-Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-October-5-2026/build/sections/final.tex","preprints/Frobenius-Structures-on-Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Frobenius-Structures-on-Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct nonzero perverse Weil Hecke eigensheaves for $\\SL_n$ with Borel level at one marked point, after a finite extension of the field of constants. The parameter is a geometrically dense arithmetic $\\PGL_n$-local system with tame regular-unipotent monodromy on a once-punctured curve of genus at least two over a finite field of characteristic $p>n$. The full multi-leg eigenstructure is Frobenius compatible with the prescribed arithmetic eigenvalue, and the geometric sheaf has parabolic nilpotent singular support.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":628},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The result","sourceOffset":1162},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and the geometric input","sourceOffset":4419},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The arithmetic constructions and the proof","sourceOffset":7687},{"kind":"section","title":"Equivariant Hecke systems and specialization","sourceOffset":10345},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sheaves, level structures, and moving legs","sourceOffset":10641},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Satake transport and its Weil normalization","sourceOffset":15138},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The specialization input with an action","sourceOffset":19758},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The characteristic restriction for \\texorpdfstring{$\\SL_n$}{SLn}","sourceOffset":23580},{"kind":"section","title":"An equivariant unramified eigencomplex","sourceOffset":25870},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The spectral skyscraper and transport of the action","sourceOffset":28052},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Recognizing the transported object","sourceOffset":36323}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $n\\geq2$, let $\\F_q$ have characteristic $p>n$, let $\\ell\\ne p$, and put $E=\\E$. Let $X_0/\\F_q$ be a smooth projective geometrically connected curve of genus at least two, with $x_0\\in X_0(\\F_q)$, and put $U_0=X_0\\setminus\\{x_0\\}$. Suppose \\[ \\rho:\\pi_1^{\\mathrm{et}}(U_0)\\longrightarrow\\PGL_n(L) \\] is continuous for a finite extension $L/\\Q_\\ell$, its geometric restriction is Zariski dense, and its inertia at $x_0$ kills wild inertia and has the form \\begin{equation} \\rho(\\gamma)=\\exp\\bigl(t_\\ell(\\gamma)N\\bigr) \\end{equation} for a regular nilpotent $N$. Here a choice of compatible $\\ell$-power roots of unity identifies the tame quotient $\\Z_\\ell(1)$ with $\\Z_\\ell$ and defines $t_\\ell$. There are an integer $m\\geq1$ and a nonzero $E$-adic Weil sheaf $M_0$ on \\[ \\Bun_{\\SL_n,B,x_0}(X_0)_{\\F_{q^m}} \\] whose geometric pullback $M$ is locally constructible perverse and has singular support in $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{par}}$. If $\\rho_m$ denotes the restriction of $\\rho$ to $\\pi_1^{\\mathrm{et}}(U_{0,\\F_{q^m}})$, then for every finite set $I$ and every family $V_i\\in\\Rep_E(\\PGL_n)$ there are isomorphisms of Weil sheaves \\begin{equation} \\Hecke_{I,\\boldsymbol V}(M_0) \\simeq M_0\\boxtimes\\bigboxtimes_{i\\in I}(V_i)_{\\rho_m}. \\end{equation} They are natural in the representations and coherent for the unit, convolution, permutations, and all fusion diagonals.","sourceOffset":2462,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Geometric specialization and transport","label":"prop:equivariant-specialization","text":"In this setup, geometric nearby cycles preserve local bounded constructibility and are perverse exact. They admit natural isomorphisms \\begin{equation} \\Hecke^s_{I,\\boldsymbol V}(\\Psi F) \\xrightarrow{\\ \\sim\\ } \\Psi\\bigl(\\Hecke^{\\bar\\eta}_{I,\\boldsymbol V}(F)\\bigr) \\end{equation} compatible with the unit, convolution, permutations, and every fusion diagonal. Suppose $F$ has a coherent eigenstructure with parameter $\\vartheta_{\\bar\\eta}$. For each representation, suppose its evaluation sheaf has a lisse lattice whose finite reductions extend lisse after finite trait extensions, compatibly on further extensions and with the reductions of a special-fiber tensor system $\\vartheta_s$. Then $\\Psi F$ has a coherent eigenstructure with eigenvalue $\\vartheta_s$. If the models, $F$ with its given coherent eigenstructure, and these tensor-specialization data carry a semilinear generator, with a compatible lift to $\\bar\\eta$, all the comparisons and the resulting eigenstructure are equivariant. For the finite-stage lattice models one may use compatible invariant trait extensions. No single finite extension is required for all reductions.","sourceOffset":20648,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:eigenstructure","text":"For a $\\Gd$-local system $\\vartheta$ on $U$, a coherent Hecke eigenstructure on $F$ consists of isomorphisms \\begin{equation} \\Hecke_{I,\\boldsymbol V}(F) \\simeq F\\boxtimes\\bigboxtimes_{i\\in I}(V_i)_\\vartheta \\end{equation} for all finite $I$, natural in the representations, and compatible with the unit, successive modifications and convolution, permutations, and all collision maps \\eqref{eq:fusion-convention}, including their iterated compatibilities. The notation $(V)_\\vartheta$ means the lisse sheaf obtained by evaluating the representation $V$ on $\\vartheta$.","sourceOffset":13764,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":105938,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Frobenius-Structures-on-Tame-Hecke-Eigensheaves-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"015-01","slug":"Equidistribution-of-Prime-Degree-Torus-Packets-with-Arbitrary-Local-Type-September-24-2026","familyId":"015","title":"Equidistribution of Prime-Degree Torus Packets with Arbitrary Local Type","abstract":"We prove the packet form of the higher-dimensional Duke equidistribution problem for totally real fields of any fixed prime degree at least five, allowing arbitrary local homothety types of full lattices. As the multiplier-order discriminant tends to infinity, the volume-weighted packet measures converge to Haar probability measure, with no escape of mass.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Haar equidistribution without escape of mass for complete volume-weighted torus packets from totally real fields: arbitrary lattices and prescribed local types in fixed prime degree at least five, arbitrary-order Picard packets in primitive quartic fields, and maximal-order ideal-class packets in primitive sextic fields. Here primitive means having no proper intermediate field; the relevant order or field discriminant tends to infinity.","pdfPath":"preprints/Equidistribution-of-Prime-Degree-Torus-Packets-with-Arbitrary-Local-Type-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Equidistribution-of-Prime-Degree-Torus-Packets-with-Arbitrary-Local-Type-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Equidistribution-of-Prime-Degree-Torus-Packets-with-Arbitrary-Local-Type-September-24-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Equidistribution-of-Prime-Degree-Torus-Packets-with-Arbitrary-Local-Type-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Prime-Degree-Torus-Packets-with-Arbitrary-Local-Type-September-24-2026/build/packets.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Prime-Degree-Torus-Packets-with-Arbitrary-Local-Type-September-24-2026/build/local.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Prime-Degree-Torus-Packets-with-Arbitrary-Local-Type-September-24-2026/build/analytic.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Prime-Degree-Torus-Packets-with-Arbitrary-Local-Type-September-24-2026/build/geometry.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Prime-Degree-Torus-Packets-with-Arbitrary-Local-Type-September-24-2026/build/entropy.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Prime-Degree-Torus-Packets-with-Arbitrary-Local-Type-September-24-2026/build/adelic.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Equidistribution-of-Prime-Degree-Torus-Packets-with-Arbitrary-Local-Type-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the packet form of the higher-dimensional Duke equidistribution problem for totally real fields of any fixed prime degree at least five, allowing arbitrary local homothety types of full lattices. As the multiplier-order discriminant tends to infinity, the volume-weighted packet measures converge to Haar probability measure, with no escape of mass.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":404},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The maximal-order specialization","sourceOffset":3445},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and prior work","sourceOffset":4610},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The arithmetic ingredient","sourceOffset":6317},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof outline","sourceOffset":8365},{"kind":"section","title":"The packet as a weighted ideal sum","sourceOffset":9643},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Saturation and local choices","sourceOffset":10394},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Real parameters and measure","sourceOffset":12883},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Containment probabilities and unfolding","sourceOffset":16092},{"kind":"section","title":"Uniform control of the local weights","sourceOffset":18910},{"kind":"section","title":"Residue-relative weighted ideal counts","sourceOffset":22711},{"kind":"section","title":"Nonescape and ordinary ball bounds","sourceOffset":30207}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed prime $n\\ge5$ and every sequence $(K_i,M_i,\\sigma_i)$ as above with $D(M_i)\\to\\infty$, \\[ \\mu_{K_i,M_i,\\sigma_i}\\longrightarrow m_n. \\] The convergence is weak convergence of probability measures. In particular, it holds against every continuous compactly supported function, and no mass escapes into the cusp.","sourceOffset":2729,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:unfolding","text":"Writing $\\mu_*=\\mu_{K,M,\\sigma}$, one has \\begin{equation} \\int_{X_n}E_f\\dd\\mu_* =\\frac1{\\sqrt D\\,\\kappaK} \\sum_{\\mathfrak a\\subset R,\\ \\mathfrak a\\ne0} P(\\mathfrak a)\\cV_f(\\N\\mathfrak a/Q). \\end{equation} The identity holds for nonnegative extended integrals.","sourceOffset":17620,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/015.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves equidistribution of volume-weighted torus packets for totally real number fields of every fixed prime degree at least five. For any sequence of full lattices whose multiplier-order discriminants tend to infinity, the packet measures converge weakly to Haar probability measure and form a tight family, so no mass escapes. Arbitrary local homothety types are allowed, and the fields may vary along the sequence.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/015.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":50372,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Equidistribution-of-Prime-Degree-Torus-Packets-with-Arbitrary-Local-Type-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"015-02","slug":"Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Quartic-Torus-Packets-for-Arbitrary-Orders-October-5-2026","familyId":"015","title":"Equidistribution of primitive quartic torus packets for arbitrary orders","abstract":"Let K range over totally real quartic fields with no proper intermediate field, and let $\\mathcal O$ be any order in K. We prove that the packets of periodic diagonal orbits attached to invertible $\\mathcal O$-ideal classes, weighted by orbit volume, equidistribute with no escape of mass as $|\\mathop{\\mathrm{Disc}}\\nolimits (\\mathcal O)|$ tends to infinity. The proof combines measure rigidity with a cubic-resolvent estimate that remains uniform at primes dividing the order index.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Haar equidistribution without escape of mass for complete volume-weighted torus packets from totally real fields: arbitrary lattices and prescribed local types in fixed prime degree at least five, arbitrary-order Picard packets in primitive quartic fields, and maximal-order ideal-class packets in primitive sextic fields. Here primitive means having no proper intermediate field; the relevant order or field discriminant tends to infinity.","pdfPath":"preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Quartic-Torus-Packets-for-Arbitrary-Orders-October-5-2026/quartic-torus-packets.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Quartic-Torus-Packets-for-Arbitrary-Orders-October-5-2026/quartic-torus-packets.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Quartic-Torus-Packets-for-Arbitrary-Orders-October-5-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Quartic-Torus-Packets-for-Arbitrary-Orders-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/main.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Quartic-Torus-Packets-for-Arbitrary-Orders-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/macros.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Quartic-Torus-Packets-for-Arbitrary-Orders-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/introduction.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Quartic-Torus-Packets-for-Arbitrary-Orders-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/packets.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Quartic-Torus-Packets-for-Arbitrary-Orders-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/ordinary.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Quartic-Torus-Packets-for-Arbitrary-Orders-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/rigidity.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Quartic-Torus-Packets-for-Arbitrary-Orders-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/exterior.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Quartic-Torus-Packets-for-Arbitrary-Orders-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/local-fourier.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Quartic-Torus-Packets-for-Arbitrary-Orders-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/additive.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Quartic-Torus-Packets-for-Arbitrary-Orders-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/sieve.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Quartic-Torus-Packets-for-Arbitrary-Orders-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Quartic-Torus-Packets-for-Arbitrary-Orders-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let $K$ range over totally real quartic fields with no proper intermediate field, and let $\\OO$ be any order in $K$. We prove that the packets of periodic diagonal orbits attached to invertible $\\OO$-ideal classes, weighted by orbit volume, equidistribute with no escape of mass as $|\\Disc(\\OO)|$ tends to infinity. The proof combines measure rigidity with a cubic-resolvent estimate that remains uniform at primes dividing the order index.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":503},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and context","sourceOffset":4400},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two counts and the conductor estimate","sourceOffset":8056},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":13060},{"kind":"section","title":"Packets from arbitrary orders","sourceOffset":14259},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite local choices","sourceOffset":15937},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Real parameters, identifications, and volume","sourceOffset":21522},{"kind":"section","title":"Ordinary vectors and probability limits","sourceOffset":28145},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Containment probabilities and unfolding","sourceOffset":28819},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The mass and tail of the local weights","sourceOffset":33360},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ideal counts relative to the residue","sourceOffset":38783},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Short vectors and small neighborhoods","sourceOffset":49276}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $(K_i,\\OO_i,\\sigma_i)$ be any sequence in which $K_i$ is a primitive totally real quartic field, $\\OO_i$ is an order in $K_i$, and $\\sigma_i$ is an ordering of its real embeddings. If $|\\Disc(\\OO_i)|\\to\\infty$, then \\[ \\int_{X_4} f\\,d\\mu_{\\OO_i,\\sigma_i} \\longrightarrow \\int_{X_4} f\\,dm_4 \\qquad\\text{for every }f\\in C_c(X_4). \\] There is no escape of mass: for every $\\varepsilon>0$ there is a compact $\\mathcal K\\subset X_4$ such that $\\mu_{\\OO_i,\\sigma_i}(\\mathcal K)\\ge1-\\varepsilon$ for all sufficiently large $i$.","sourceOffset":3509,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Packet model and volume","label":"prop:pkt-model","text":"The maps \\[ (L,v)\\longmapsto c_I\\sigma(L)v \\qquad (L\\in\\mathscr L_{\\OO}(I),\\ v\\in T_\\infty) \\] give a bijection from the disjoint union over the chosen \\(I\\) of the quotients by \\begin{equation} \\epsilon:(L,v)\\longmapsto (\\epsilon L,\\sigma(\\epsilon)^{-1}v),\\qquad \\epsilon\\in U, \\end{equation} onto \\(\\bigcup_{\\mathcal Y\\in\\mathcal P_{\\OO,\\sigma}}\\mathcal Y\\subset X_4\\). Counting measure on the \\(L\\)'s times \\(dv\\) descends to the sum of the Haar measures of those orbits. In particular, \\begin{equation} \\sum_{\\mathcal Y\\in\\mathcal P_{\\OO,\\sigma}}\\vol(\\mathcal Y) =N_{\\OO}\\sqrt D\\,\\kappa . \\end{equation} Writing \\(\\mu_*=\\mu_{\\OO,\\sigma}\\), for every nonnegative Borel function \\(\\Phi\\) on \\(X_4\\) one has \\begin{equation} \\int_{X_4}\\Phi\\,d\\mu_* =\\frac{1}{h_KN_{\\OO}\\vol(\\mathscr F_U)} \\sum_I\\sum_{L\\in\\mathscr L_{\\OO}(I)} \\int_{\\mathscr F_U}\\Phi(c_I\\sigma(L)v)\\,dv . \\end{equation} The identity permits infinite values on both sides.","sourceOffset":23288,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/015.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":236445,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Quartic-Torus-Packets-for-Arbitrary-Orders-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"015-03","slug":"Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Sextic-Torus-Packets-October-5-2026","familyId":"015","title":"Equidistribution of Primitive Sextic Torus Packets","abstract":"We prove that the complete ideal-class packets of maximal orders in totally real sextic fields with no proper intermediate fields become equidistributed, with no escape of mass, in the space of unimodular lattices as their field discriminants tend to infinity. The limit is Haar probability measure. Each packet includes all coordinate-sign translates and is weighted by diagonal-orbit volume.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Haar equidistribution without escape of mass for complete volume-weighted torus packets from totally real fields: arbitrary lattices and prescribed local types in fixed prime degree at least five, arbitrary-order Picard packets in primitive quartic fields, and maximal-order ideal-class packets in primitive sextic fields. Here primitive means having no proper intermediate field; the relevant order or field discriminant tends to infinity.","pdfPath":"preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Sextic-Torus-Packets-October-5-2026/primitive-sextic-torus-packets.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Sextic-Torus-Packets-October-5-2026/primitive-sextic-torus-packets.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Sextic-Torus-Packets-October-5-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Sextic-Torus-Packets-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Sextic-Torus-Packets-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Sextic-Torus-Packets-October-5-2026/build/sections/arithmetic.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Sextic-Torus-Packets-October-5-2026/build/sections/homogeneous.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Sextic-Torus-Packets-October-5-2026/build/sections/conditioning.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Sextic-Torus-Packets-October-5-2026/build/sections/product.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Sextic-Torus-Packets-October-5-2026/build/figures/product-mechanism.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Sextic-Torus-Packets-October-5-2026/build/sections/activity.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Sextic-Torus-Packets-October-5-2026/build/sections/entropy.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Sextic-Torus-Packets-October-5-2026/build/sections/combination.tex","preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Sextic-Torus-Packets-October-5-2026/build/sections/assembly.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Sextic-Torus-Packets-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the complete ideal-class packets of maximal orders in totally real sextic fields with no proper intermediate fields become equidistributed, with no escape of mass, in the space of unimodular lattices as their field discriminants tend to infinity. The limit is Haar probability measure. Each packet includes all coordinate-sign translates and is weighted by diagonal-orbit volume.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":456},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Packets and the main theorem","sourceOffset":1053},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the composite-degree obstruction","sourceOffset":3707},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The intermediate-resolution argument","sourceOffset":7045},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":11993},{"kind":"section","title":"Arithmetic estimates for the packets","sourceOffset":12846},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Volume and unfolding","sourceOffset":14344},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform counts of ideals","sourceOffset":19262},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The cusp and balls in the space of lattices","sourceOffset":24249},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Trace slices of the codifferent","sourceOffset":29805},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exponential decay on diagonal tubes","sourceOffset":34112},{"kind":"section","title":"Homogeneous limits and their fixed points","sourceOffset":40958}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $K_i$ be totally real primitive fields of degree six, with $|\\Disc(K_i)|\\to\\infty$, and choose any ordering $\\sigma_i$ of the embeddings of each field. Then \\[ \\int_{X_6} f\\,\\dd\\mu_{K_i,\\sigma_i} \\longrightarrow\\int_{X_6} f\\,\\dd m_6 \\qquad(f\\in C_c(X_6)). \\] There is no escape of mass: for every $\\epsilon>0$ there is a compact $C\\subset X_6$ such that $\\mu_{K_i,\\sigma_i}(C)\\ge1-\\epsilon$ for all sufficiently large $i$.","sourceOffset":2872,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Packet volume and vector unfolding","label":"prop:packet-volume","text":"Every orbit in $\\mathcal P_{K,\\sigma}$ has $A$-volume $(s/2)R_K$, and there are $h_K2^6/s$ distinct orbits. In particular, the total packet volume is $Q\\kappa$. For every nonnegative Borel function $f$, \\begin{equation} \\int_X\\widehat f\\,\\dd\\mu_{K,\\sigma} =\\frac1{Q\\kappa}\\sum_{l\\ge1}d_K(l)V_f(l/Q), \\end{equation} where both sides are allowed to be infinite.","sourceOffset":15766,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/015.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":187082,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Equidistribution-of-Primitive-Sextic-Torus-Packets-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"016-01","slug":"The-Abelian-Zilber-Pink-Conjecture-September-24-2026","familyId":"016","title":"The abelian Zilber–Pink conjecture","abstract":"We prove the abelian Zilber–Pink conjecture over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$. Every irreducible subvariety of an abelian variety has only finitely many maximal atypical subvarieties, where atypicality is measured inside its smallest containing torsion coset.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the abelian Zilber–Pink conjecture over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$: every irreducible subvariety has finitely many maximal atypical subvarieties relative to its smallest containing torsion coset. It also proves the full curve case in the Siegel threefold $\\mathcal A_2$ for Hodge-generic curves defined over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$, without boundary or reduction assumptions.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Abelian-Zilber-Pink-Conjecture-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Abelian-Zilber-Pink-Conjecture-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Abelian-Zilber-Pink-Conjecture-September-24-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/The-Abelian-Zilber-Pink-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Abelian-Zilber-Pink-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/The-Abelian-Zilber-Pink-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-Abelian-Zilber-Pink-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-sequences.tex","preprints/The-Abelian-Zilber-Pink-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-tangent.tex","preprints/The-Abelian-Zilber-Pink-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-high.tex","preprints/The-Abelian-Zilber-Pink-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-minimal.tex","preprints/The-Abelian-Zilber-Pink-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-arithmetic.tex","preprints/The-Abelian-Zilber-Pink-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/figures/low-measures.tex","preprints/The-Abelian-Zilber-Pink-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-index.tex","preprints/The-Abelian-Zilber-Pink-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-cut.tex","preprints/The-Abelian-Zilber-Pink-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-finiteness.tex","preprints/The-Abelian-Zilber-Pink-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-finite-rank.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Abelian-Zilber-Pink-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the abelian Zilber--Pink conjecture over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$. Every irreducible subvariety of an abelian variety has only finitely many maximal atypical subvarieties, where atypicality is measured inside its smallest containing torsion coset.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":375},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finiteness statement","sourceOffset":758},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":5203},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and organization","sourceOffset":10338},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":12932},{"kind":"section","title":"Height scales and an auxiliary sequence theorem","sourceOffset":13557},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Real homomorphisms and height norms","sourceOffset":13941},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The sequence theorem","sourceOffset":17579},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Decomposing a sequence into height scales","sourceOffset":21409},{"kind":"section","title":"Projection and tangent positivity","sourceOffset":28359},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Quotients of minimal counterexamples","sourceOffset":28950},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A product tangent obstruction","sourceOffset":34634}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every number field $K$, every abelian variety $A/K$, and every irreducible subvariety $X\\subseteq A_{\\bar K}$, there are only finitely many maximal atypical subvarieties of $X$ in $S=\\langle X\\rangle_{\\mathrm{sp}}$. Equivalently, there are finitely many proper atypical subvarieties $Y_1,\\ldots,Y_m\\subsetneq X$ such that every atypical subvariety is contained in one of the $Y_j$.","sourceOffset":2466,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:nondensity","text":"Let $A$ be an abelian variety over $\\Qbar$, and let $X\\subseteq A$ be an irreducible subvariety contained in no proper torsion coset. Then $X\\cap A^{[\\dim X+1]}$ is not Zariski dense in $X$.","sourceOffset":3527,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Sequence data","label":"def:sequence-data","text":"Let $I$ be the ordered list $1,\\ldots,s$, possibly preceded by $0$. The list is nonempty; if $0$ occurs, $s$ may be zero. For each $\\ell\\in I$, fix an abelian variety $A_\\ell$ over $\\Qbar$, a nonzero abelian subvariety $W_\\ell\\subseteq A_\\ell$, an irreducible subvariety $Y_\\ell\\subseteq A_\\ell$, and an integer $m_\\ell\\ge0$. Put \\[ \\cA=\\prod_{\\ell\\in I}A_\\ell, \\qquad W=\\prod_{\\ell\\in I}W_\\ell, \\qquad D_\\ell=A_\\ell/W_\\ell. \\] For positive integers $i$, let $y_{i\\ell}\\in Y_\\ell(\\Qbar)$ and $H_{i\\ell}\\ge1$ satisfy \\begin{equation} \\dim_{y_{i\\ell}}\\bigl(Y_\\ell\\cap(y_{i\\ell}+W_\\ell)\\bigr) \\le m_\\ell","sourceOffset":18947,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":149715,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Abelian-Zilber-Pink-Conjecture-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"016-02","slug":"The-E-times-CM-Component-of-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026","familyId":"016","title":"The E×CM component of Zilber–Pink for curves in A2","abstract":"We prove the $E\\times\\mathrm{CM}$ component of Zilber–Pink for Hodge-generic algebraic curves in $\\mathcal A_2$ over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$. Each such curve contains only finitely many points whose abelian surface is isogenous to a product of elliptic curves with at least one factor having complex multiplication.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the abelian Zilber–Pink conjecture over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$: every irreducible subvariety has finitely many maximal atypical subvarieties relative to its smallest containing torsion coset. It also proves the full curve case in the Siegel threefold $\\mathcal A_2$ for Hodge-generic curves defined over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$, without boundary or reduction assumptions.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-E-times-CM-Component-of-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-E-times-CM-Component-of-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-E-times-CM-Component-of-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/The-E-times-CM-Component-of-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-E-times-CM-Component-of-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the \\(E\\times\\CM\\) component of Zilber--Pink for Hodge-generic algebraic curves in \\(\\A_2\\) over \\(\\Qbar\\). Each such curve contains only finitely many points whose abelian surface is isogenous to a product of elliptic curves with at least one factor having complex multiplication.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":337},{"kind":"section","title":"Heights, periods, and CM elliptic curves","sourceOffset":7391},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":7460},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two established height and period estimates","sourceOffset":9022},{"kind":"subsection","title":"CM estimates","sourceOffset":12260},{"kind":"section","title":"Canonical gluing and boundary lengths","sourceOffset":15158},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The canonical product isogeny","sourceOffset":15225},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The degeneration input","sourceOffset":17709},{"kind":"section","title":"A uniform height estimate on a curve","sourceOffset":25680},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform local height comparisons","sourceOffset":27008},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An archimedean boundary estimate","sourceOffset":29630},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof of Proposition \\ref{prop:height}","sourceOffset":33301}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(C\\subset\\A_2\\) be a reduced, irreducible, closed algebraic curve defined over \\(\\Qbar\\). Suppose that \\(C\\) is Hodge generic, meaning that it is contained in no proper special subvariety of \\(\\A_2\\). Then the set \\[ \\bigl\\{s\\in C(\\Qbar): A_s\\text{ is isogenous to }E_1\\times E_2 \\text{ with at least one }E_i\\text{ having CM}\\bigr\\} \\] is finite.","sourceOffset":1551,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:orbits","text":"Let \\(C\\) be as in Theorem \\ref{thm:main}, and let \\(K\\) be a number field over which it is defined. There is a constant \\(c>0\\), depending on \\(C\\) and \\(K\\), such that every mixed point \\(s\\in C(\\Qbar)\\) satisfies \\[ \\#\\bigl(\\Gal(\\Qbar/K)\\cdot s\\bigr) \\ge c\\,\\cplx(s)^{1/42}. \\]","sourceOffset":6135,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":51433,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-E-times-CM-Component-of-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"016-03","slug":"Quaternionic-Division-Points-on-Curves-in-the-Siegel-Threefold-September-24-2026","familyId":"016","title":"Quaternionic division points on curves in the Siegel threefold","abstract":"We prove the quaternionic-division component of Zilber–Pink for curves in $\\mathcal A_2$ over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$. A Hodge-generic algebraic curve contains only finitely many points whose full geometric rational endomorphism algebra is an indefinite quaternion division algebra over ℚ. No boundary or reduction hypothesis is required.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the abelian Zilber–Pink conjecture over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$: every irreducible subvariety has finitely many maximal atypical subvarieties relative to its smallest containing torsion coset. It also proves the full curve case in the Siegel threefold $\\mathcal A_2$ for Hodge-generic curves defined over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$, without boundary or reduction assumptions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Quaternionic-Division-Points-on-Curves-in-the-Siegel-Threefold-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quaternionic-Division-Points-on-Curves-in-the-Siegel-Threefold-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quaternionic-Division-Points-on-Curves-in-the-Siegel-Threefold-September-24-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Quaternionic-Division-Points-on-Curves-in-the-Siegel-Threefold-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Quaternionic-Division-Points-on-Curves-in-the-Siegel-Threefold-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Quaternionic-Division-Points-on-Curves-in-the-Siegel-Threefold-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Quaternionic-Division-Points-on-Curves-in-the-Siegel-Threefold-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-geometry.tex","preprints/Quaternionic-Division-Points-on-Curves-in-the-Siegel-Threefold-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-interpolation.tex","preprints/Quaternionic-Division-Points-on-Curves-in-the-Siegel-Threefold-September-24-2026/build/figures/graph-projection.tex","preprints/Quaternionic-Division-Points-on-Curves-in-the-Siegel-Threefold-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-arithmetic.tex","preprints/Quaternionic-Division-Points-on-Curves-in-the-Siegel-Threefold-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-height.tex","preprints/Quaternionic-Division-Points-on-Curves-in-the-Siegel-Threefold-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-counting.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Quaternionic-Division-Points-on-Curves-in-the-Siegel-Threefold-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the quaternionic-division component of Zilber--Pink for curves in \\(\\Acal_2\\) over \\(\\Qbar\\). A Hodge-generic algebraic curve contains only finitely many points whose full geometric rational endomorphism algebra is an indefinite quaternion division algebra over \\(\\Q\\). No boundary or reduction hypothesis is required.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":430},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier results","sourceOffset":2273},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The height argument","sourceOffset":4218},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and organization","sourceOffset":10084},{"kind":"section","title":"The quadratic variation and its isogeny exceptions","sourceOffset":11118},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fixed curves and finite covers","sourceOffset":11704},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A rank-five operator model","sourceOffset":15845},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Monodromy and rational isogenies","sourceOffset":22401},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Only finitely many dominating correspondences","sourceOffset":27616},{"kind":"section","title":"Interpolation with two arguments","sourceOffset":36886},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weighted absolute heights","sourceOffset":37484},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The interpolation statement","sourceOffset":39017}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(C\\subset\\Acal_{2,\\Qbar}\\) be a reduced irreducible closed Hodge-generic algebraic curve. Then \\[ \\Sigma_{\\mathrm{QM}}(C)= \\bigl\\{s\\in C(\\Qbar): \\End^0(A_s)\\text{ is an indefinite quaternion division algebra over }\\Q \\bigr\\} \\] is finite.","sourceOffset":1307,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Single and product monodromy","label":"prop:monodromy","text":"For the family over any fixed smooth finite cover of a Hodge-generic curve in $\\Acal_2$, the connected algebraic monodromy on $\\mathbb H$ is $\\Sp_4$, and that on $\\mathbb V$ is $\\SO(V,q)$. The same holds after pullback by a dominant morphism from an irreducible complex algebraic variety, on a smooth open of a resolution. Let $S_1,S_2$ be two such curves, and let an irreducible complex algebraic variety $W$ map dominantly to both. On the sum of the two pulled-back weight-one systems, either the connected monodromy is $\\Sp_4\\times\\Sp_4$, or the image of $W$ in the product of the two moduli curves is contained in a dominating component of a polarized isogeny correspondence. In the latter case the corresponding local period relation is given by a fixed element of $\\GSp_4(\\Q)$ with positive multiplier.","sourceOffset":22448,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":162604,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Quaternionic-Division-Points-on-Curves-in-the-Siegel-Threefold-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"016-04","slug":"Elliptic-Squares-and-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026","familyId":"016","title":"Elliptic squares and Zilber–Pink for curves in A2","abstract":"We prove that every Hodge-generic algebraic curve in $\\mathcal A_2$ over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$ contains only finitely many points whose abelian surface is isogenous to the square of an elliptic curve without complex multiplication (CM). Combining this result with the companion $E\\times\\mathrm{CM}$ and quaternionic-division finiteness theorems, we prove the curve case of Zilber–Pink in $\\mathcal A_2$ over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$, without a boundary hypothesis.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the abelian Zilber–Pink conjecture over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$: every irreducible subvariety has finitely many maximal atypical subvarieties relative to its smallest containing torsion coset. It also proves the full curve case in the Siegel threefold $\\mathcal A_2$ for Hodge-generic curves defined over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$, without boundary or reduction assumptions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Elliptic-Squares-and-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Elliptic-Squares-and-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Elliptic-Squares-and-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Elliptic-Squares-and-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Elliptic-Squares-and-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Elliptic-Squares-and-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/build/unicode-mappings.tex","preprints/Elliptic-Squares-and-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Elliptic-Squares-and-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/build/figures/two-incidences.tex","preprints/Elliptic-Squares-and-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-interpolation.tex","preprints/Elliptic-Squares-and-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-monodromy.tex","preprints/Elliptic-Squares-and-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-incidences.tex","preprints/Elliptic-Squares-and-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-heights.tex","preprints/Elliptic-Squares-and-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-local-equations.tex","preprints/Elliptic-Squares-and-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-nonidentities.tex","preprints/Elliptic-Squares-and-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-finiteness.tex","preprints/Elliptic-Squares-and-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-interpolation-proof.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Elliptic-Squares-and-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every Hodge-generic algebraic curve in $\\mathcal A_2$ over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$ contains only finitely many points whose abelian surface is isogenous to the square of an elliptic curve without complex multiplication (CM). Combining this result with the companion $E\\times\\mathrm{CM}$ and quaternionic-division finiteness theorems, we prove the curve case of Zilber--Pink in $\\mathcal A_2$ over $\\overline{\\mathbb Q}$, without a boundary hypothesis.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":527},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The companion branches and the full theorem","sourceOffset":2068},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the arithmetic step","sourceOffset":4813},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof mechanism","sourceOffset":10949},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and organization","sourceOffset":17918},{"kind":"section","title":"Interpolation with a common small parameter","sourceOffset":19424},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The quantitative statement","sourceOffset":19912},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why the graph gives a height bound","sourceOffset":24565},{"kind":"section","title":"The geometric systems and a finite list of correspondences","sourceOffset":29304},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fixed covers and the center","sourceOffset":29876},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The five-dimensional orthogonal system","sourceOffset":33659},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Full and joint monodromy","sourceOffset":39789}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"The elliptic-square case","label":"intro:squares","text":"Let $C\\subset\\A_2$ be a nonempty reduced irreducible closed algebraic curve over $\\Qbar$. If $C$ is Hodge generic, then $\\Sigma_{E^2}(C)$ is finite.","sourceOffset":1618,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"The two companion branches","label":"intro:branches","text":"For every nonempty reduced irreducible closed Hodge-generic curve $C\\subset\\A_2$ over $\\Qbar$, the following two subsets of $C(\\Qbar)$ are finite: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\Alph*)}] \\item the points for which $A_s$ is isogenous over $\\Qbar$ to $E_1\\times E_2$, with at least one elliptic factor having CM; \\item the points for which the full algebra $\\End_{\\Qbar}(A_s)\\otimes_{\\Z}\\Q$ is an indefinite quaternion division algebra over $\\Q$. \\end{enumerate} These statements allow all CM fields, endomorphism orders, quaternion algebras, isogeny degrees and principal polarizations. They impose no boundary, reduction, integrality, height or field-degree condition.","sourceOffset":2300,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":226294,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":22,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Elliptic-Squares-and-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Elliptic-Squares-and-Zilber-Pink-for-Curves-in-A2-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"017-01","slug":"The-irrationality-exponent-of-pi-is-2-September-24-2026","familyId":"017","title":"The irrationality exponent of pi is 2","abstract":"We prove the conjecture that the irrationality exponent of π is 2. As a consequence, the classical Flint–Hills series $\\sum_{n\\ge1}1/(n^3\\sin^2 n)$ converges, with angles in radians.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the irrationality exponent of π is exactly 2: for every ε > 0 and all sufficiently large denominators q, every rational $p/q$ satisfies $|\\pi-p/q|\\ge q^{-2-\\varepsilon}$. This also proves convergence of the Flint–Hills series $\\sum_{n\\ge1}1/(n^3\\sin^2 n)$, with angles in radians.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-irrationality-exponent-of-pi-is-2-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-irrationality-exponent-of-pi-is-2-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-irrationality-exponent-of-pi-is-2-September-24-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/The-irrationality-exponent-of-pi-is-2-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-irrationality-exponent-of-pi-is-2-September-24-2026/build/sections/interpolation.tex","preprints/The-irrationality-exponent-of-pi-is-2-September-24-2026/build/sections/determinant.tex","preprints/The-irrationality-exponent-of-pi-is-2-September-24-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex","preprints/The-irrationality-exponent-of-pi-is-2-September-24-2026/build/sections/series.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-irrationality-exponent-of-pi-is-2-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the conjecture that the irrationality exponent of \\(\\pi\\) is \\(2\\). As a consequence, the classical Flint--Hills series \\(\\sum_{n\\ge1}1/(n^3\\sin^2 n)\\) converges, with angles in radians.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":242},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Quantitative approximation and related work","sourceOffset":2770},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof in outline","sourceOffset":5429},{"kind":"section","title":"Interpolation with separated weights","sourceOffset":11034},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A local multiplicity comparison","sourceOffset":13551},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The curve inequality","sourceOffset":19055},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From the curve inequality to all coefficient packets","sourceOffset":30527},{"kind":"section","title":"The arithmetic and analytic determinant estimates","sourceOffset":37692},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A full-row-rank minor and its arithmetic size","sourceOffset":39375},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Translation to entire functions","sourceOffset":43129},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Repeated Taylor degrees force a quadratic saving","sourceOffset":46430},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A comparison with two alternatives","sourceOffset":49004}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"The irrationality exponent of \\(\\pi\\) is \\(2\\). More precisely, for every real \\(\\nu>2\\), there is an integer \\(Q(\\nu)\\) such that \\[ \\left|\\pi-\\frac pq\\right|\\ge q^{-\\nu} \\qquad(p\\in\\ZZ,\\ q\\in\\ZZ,\\ q\\ge Q(\\nu)). \\]","sourceOffset":939,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"thm:flinthills","text":"The classical Flint--Hills series converges.","sourceOffset":1952,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/017.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves that the irrationality exponent of $\\pi$ is exactly two. For every $\\nu>2$, all sufficiently large positive denominators $q$ satisfy $|\\pi-p/q|\\ge q^{-\\nu}$ for every integer numerator $p$. It also states the exact supremum characterization using infinitely many rational approximations.\n\nThe paper's convergence consequence for the Flint–Hills series is outside this selected statement.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/017.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":64286,"sourceFileCount":5,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-irrationality-exponent-of-pi-is-2-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"018-01","slug":"The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026","familyId":"018","title":"The Margulis–Platonov conjecture over global function fields","abstract":"We prove the Margulis–Platonov conjecture over every global function field, including characteristic two. For an absolutely almost simple simply connected algebraic group, every noncentral abstract normal subgroup of its rational points is the inverse image of an open normal subgroup of the finite product of its anisotropic local groups.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the Margulis–Platonov conjecture over every global field, including function fields of characteristic two. For an absolutely almost simple simply connected algebraic group G over k, every noncentral abstract normal subgroup of $G(k)$ is the inverse image of an open normal subgroup in the finite product of its anisotropic nonarchimedean local groups.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026/margulis-platonov-global-function-fields.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026/margulis-platonov-global-function-fields.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026/build/paper/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026/build/paper/02-arithmetic.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026/build/paper/03-approximation.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026/build/paper/04-norm-tori.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026/build/paper/05-quotients.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026/build/paper/06-palettes.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026/build/paper/finite-input.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026/build/paper/07-recurrence.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026/build/paper/08-characters.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026/build/paper/09-induction.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026/build/paper/figures/even-degree-norms.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026/build/paper/10-conclusion.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026/build/paper/A-finite-groups.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Margulis--Platonov conjecture over every global function field, including characteristic two. For an absolutely almost simple simply connected algebraic group, every noncentral abstract normal subgroup of its rational points is the inverse image of an open normal subgroup of the finite product of its anisotropic local groups.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":403},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":2130},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Relation to the number-field proof","sourceOffset":5246},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The argument","sourceOffset":8336},{"kind":"section","title":"Arithmetic reduction and exact cosets","sourceOffset":12288},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The remaining unitary groups","sourceOffset":12819},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite index and the power quotient","sourceOffset":18037},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite quotients of groups already understood","sourceOffset":26406},{"kind":"section","title":"Approximation in a prescribed power coset","sourceOffset":28633},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Avoidance in affine space","sourceOffset":29709},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Products of torus powers","sourceOffset":34503},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Boundary conditions and the approximation theorem","sourceOffset":41852}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $k$ be a global function field and let $G$ be an absolutely almost simple simply connected $k$-group. If $N\\triangleleft G(k)$ and $N\\not\\subseteq Z(G(k))$, then \\[ N=\\delta_A^{-1}(W) \\] for an open normal subgroup $W\\triangleleft H_A$. This holds in every positive characteristic, including when $G$ is anisotropic over $k$.","sourceOffset":1188,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"ind:main","text":"Let $k$ be a global function field, let $L/k$ be a separable quadratic extension, and let $(D,*)$ be a central simple $L$-algebra of degree $n\\geq 3$ with unitary involution. Suppose that $S=\\SU(D,*)$ is $k$-anisotropic. For every integer $e\\geq 1$, put \\[ R=S(k)^e,\\qquad P_0=\\overline{\\delta_A(R)},\\qquad V_0=\\delta_A^{-1}(P_0). \\] Here $S(k)^e$ denotes the subgroup generated by the $e$-th powers, and $A$ is the set of places where $S$ is anisotropic. Then $V_0=R$.","sourceOffset":182652,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":280421,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":19,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-global-function-fields-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"018-02","slug":"The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-number-fields-September-23-2026","familyId":"018","title":"The Margulis–Platonov conjecture over number fields","abstract":"We prove the Margulis–Platonov conjecture over number fields. For an absolutely almost simple simply connected group, every noncentral abstract normal subgroup of its rational points is the inverse image of an open normal subgroup of the product of its anisotropic nonarchimedean local groups.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the Margulis–Platonov conjecture over every global field, including function fields of characteristic two. For an absolutely almost simple simply connected algebraic group G over k, every noncentral abstract normal subgroup of $G(k)$ is the inverse image of an open normal subgroup in the finite product of its anisotropic nonarchimedean local groups.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-number-fields-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-number-fields-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-number-fields-September-23-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-number-fields-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-number-fields-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-number-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-number-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/foundations.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-number-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/colors.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-number-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/characters.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-number-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/palettes.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-number-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/finite-groups.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-number-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/unitary-induction.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-number-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/exceptional-types.tex","preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-number-fields-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-number-fields-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Margulis--Platonov conjecture over number fields. For an absolutely almost simple simply connected group, every noncentral abstract normal subgroup of its rational points is the inverse image of an open normal subgroup of the product of its anisotropic nonarchimedean local groups.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":368},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and significance","sourceOffset":1791},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A positive-density congruence consequence","sourceOffset":4989},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite obstruction","sourceOffset":6985},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Odd-degree division algebras","sourceOffset":8243},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Completion of the proof","sourceOffset":11019},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite quotients and approximation","sourceOffset":12117},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and established inputs","sourceOffset":12661},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite quotient not detected by closure","sourceOffset":17791},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An affine avoidance lemma","sourceOffset":25234},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Approximation by abstract power factors","sourceOffset":29371},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cohomology detected on cyclic subgroups","sourceOffset":42510}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $k$ be a number field and $G$ an absolutely almost simple simply connected algebraic $k$-group. If $N\\triangleleft G(k)$ is not contained in $Z(G(k))$, then there is an open normal subgroup $W\\triangleleft H_A$ such that \\[ N=\\delta_A^{-1}(W). \\]","sourceOffset":1211,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Positive-density congruence subgroup property","label":"cor:positive-density-csp","text":"Let $k$ be a number field and $G$ an absolutely almost simple simply connected algebraic $k$-group. Let $M/k$ be the minimal Galois extension over which $G$ becomes an inner form of the split group, and let $\\operatorname{Spl}(M/k)$ be the set of nonarchimedean places of $k$ that split completely in $M$. Suppose that $S$ is a set of places of $k$ which contains all archimedean places, contains no nonarchimedean place $v$ with $\\rank_{k_v}G=0$, and satisfies \\[ d_k\\bigl(S\\cap\\operatorname{Spl}(M/k)\\bigr)>0, \\] where $d_k$ denotes Dirichlet density and the indicated density is assumed to exist. Then the $S$-congruence kernel is trivial: \\[ C^S(G)=\\{1\\}. \\] Equivalently, for any fixed faithful $k$-defined representation of $G$, the associated group $G(\\mathcal O(S))$ over the ring of $S$-integers has the classical congruence subgroup property: every finite-index normal subgroup contains a principal congruence subgroup of some nonzero ideal level.","sourceOffset":5187,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":283591,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":17,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Margulis-Platonov-conjecture-over-number-fields-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"019-01","slug":"Etale-covers-with-a-prescribed-exterior-sheet-September-24-2026","familyId":"019","title":"Étale covers with a prescribed exterior sheet","abstract":"Let X be a smooth proper hyperbolic curve over an algebraic closure of a p-adic local field. Given finitely many disjoint small open disks, we construct one connected finite étale cover with a sheet isomorphic to their entire exterior and with degree divisible by p on every connected component above each disk.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that rational points on every smooth proper geometrically connected curve of genus at least two over a finite extension of ℚp correspond bijectively to conjugacy classes of sections of its full arithmetic étale fundamental group. It also proves Grothendieck's section conjecture over ℚ for the modular curves $X_0(N)$ and $X_1(N)$ of genus at least two.","pdfPath":"preprints/Etale-covers-with-a-prescribed-exterior-sheet-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Etale-covers-with-a-prescribed-exterior-sheet-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Etale-covers-with-a-prescribed-exterior-sheet-September-24-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Etale-covers-with-a-prescribed-exterior-sheet-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Etale-covers-with-a-prescribed-exterior-sheet-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-statement.tex","preprints/Etale-covers-with-a-prescribed-exterior-sheet-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-cycles.tex","preprints/Etale-covers-with-a-prescribed-exterior-sheet-September-24-2026/build/figures/path-to-cycle.tex","preprints/Etale-covers-with-a-prescribed-exterior-sheet-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-characters.tex","preprints/Etale-covers-with-a-prescribed-exterior-sheet-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-quotient.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Etale-covers-with-a-prescribed-exterior-sheet-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let $X$ be a smooth proper hyperbolic curve over an algebraic closure of a $p$-adic local field. Given finitely many disjoint small open disks, we construct one connected finite \\'etale cover with a sheet isomorphic to their entire exterior and with degree divisible by $p$ on every connected component above each disk.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The simultaneous covering statement","sourceOffset":384},{"kind":"section","title":"Models, components, and cycles","sourceOffset":4539},{"kind":"section","title":"Graph characters supported above the disks","sourceOffset":15416},{"kind":"section","title":"The stabilizer quotient","sourceOffset":18622}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:exterior","text":"For the fixed disks and exterior above, there is a smooth proper connected curve $Y/\\bar k$ and a finite \\'etale surjective morphism $g:Y\\to X$ such that: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item a connected component of $g^{-1}(U)$ maps isomorphically to $U$; \\item for every $i$, every connected component of $g^{-1}(V_i)$ maps to $V_i$ with degree divisible by $p$. \\end{enumerate} Here the inverse images and the degrees of their components refer to the induced finite \\'etale analytic maps. The disks are the prescribed $V_i$; they are not shrunk in the conclusion.","sourceOffset":1558,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:cycles","text":"There is a connected finite \\'etale Galois cover $Z\\to X$ whose stable skeleton contains, for each $i$, an embedded circle lying entirely above $V_i$.","sourceOffset":7672,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":22092,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Etale-covers-with-a-prescribed-exterior-sheet-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"019-02","slug":"The-p-adic-section-conjecture-October-6-2026","familyId":"019","title":"The p-adic section conjecture","abstract":"We prove the local p-adic section conjecture for smooth proper geometrically connected curves of genus at least two, over every finite extension of ℚp. Combined with established finite-descent theorems, this also yields the global section conjecture for smooth proper geometrically connected curves of genus at least two over number fields when their finite-cover descent locus equals their rational points; this includes $X_0(N)$ and $X_1(N)$ of genus at least two over ℚ.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that rational points on every smooth proper geometrically connected curve of genus at least two over a finite extension of ℚp correspond bijectively to conjugacy classes of sections of its full arithmetic étale fundamental group. It also proves Grothendieck's section conjecture over ℚ for the modular curves $X_0(N)$ and $X_1(N)$ of genus at least two.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-p-adic-section-conjecture-October-6-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-p-adic-section-conjecture-October-6-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-p-adic-section-conjecture-October-6-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/The-p-adic-section-conjecture-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-p-adic-section-conjecture-October-6-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-p-adic-section-conjecture-October-6-2026/build/sections/02-preliminaries.tex","preprints/The-p-adic-section-conjecture-October-6-2026/build/sections/03-patching.tex","preprints/The-p-adic-section-conjecture-October-6-2026/build/sections/04-tame-roots.tex","preprints/The-p-adic-section-conjecture-October-6-2026/build/sections/05-arbitrary-roots.tex","preprints/The-p-adic-section-conjecture-October-6-2026/build/figures/parallel-edges.tex","preprints/The-p-adic-section-conjecture-October-6-2026/build/sections/06-realization.tex","preprints/The-p-adic-section-conjecture-October-6-2026/build/sections/07-sections.tex","preprints/The-p-adic-section-conjecture-October-6-2026/build/sections/08-global-sections.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-p-adic-section-conjecture-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the local $p$-adic section conjecture for smooth proper geometrically connected curves of genus at least two, over every finite extension of $\\mathbb Q_p$. Combined with established finite-descent theorems, this also yields the global section conjecture for smooth proper geometrically connected curves of genus at least two over number fields when their finite-cover descent locus equals their rational points; this includes $X_0(N)$ and $X_1(N)$ of genus at least two over $\\mathbb Q$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":559},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier results and the lifting problem","sourceOffset":2598},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The completion statement","sourceOffset":5429},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two stages of collar roots","sourceOffset":8309},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Global consequences","sourceOffset":12450},{"kind":"section","title":"Fields, analytic points, and horizontal ramification","sourceOffset":15226},{"kind":"section","title":"Replacing branch disks by trivial covers","sourceOffset":18669},{"kind":"section","title":"Prime-to-\\texorpdfstring{$p$}{p} roots from stable reduction","sourceOffset":29715},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Units and roots on node annuli","sourceOffset":30255},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Moving disk collars into the stable skeleton","sourceOffset":39870},{"kind":"section","title":"Arbitrary roots on a collar","sourceOffset":45975},{"kind":"section","title":"From collar roots to the completed universal tower","sourceOffset":57976}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:section","text":"For every prime $p$, every finite extension $k/\\mathbb Q_p$, and every smooth proper geometrically connected curve $X/k$ of genus at least two, the map \\[ X(k)\\longrightarrow \\left\\{\\begin{gathered} s:G_k\\to\\pi_1(X)\\text{ a continuous group homomorphism}\\\\ \\text{such that }(\\pi_1(X)\\to G_k)\\circ s=\\mathrm{id}_{G_k} \\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\big/\\pi_1(X_{\\bar k}) \\] is bijective. The quotient is by conjugation by the full geometric \\'etale fundamental group.","sourceOffset":2084,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:completion","text":"Let $p$ be a prime, let $k/\\mathbb Q_p$ be finite, and let $X/k$ be a smooth proper geometrically connected curve of genus at least two. Put $K=k(X)$, choose a separable closure $K^{\\mathrm{sep}}$, and let $\\widetilde K\\subset K^{\\mathrm{sep}}$ be the compositum of the function fields of all connected finite \\'etale covers of $X$, with all their $K$-embeddings. For every real-valued rank-one norm on $K$ extending the normalized $p$-adic norm on $k$, and every prolongation to $K^{\\mathrm{sep}}$, set \\[ E=\\widehat{K^{\\mathrm{sep}}},\\qquad H=\\widehat{\\widetilde K}\\subset E, \\] where the completions use that fixed prolongation. Then the specified subfield $K^{\\mathrm{sep}}$ of $E$ is contained in $H$.","sourceOffset":6028,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":85579,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-p-adic-section-conjecture-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"020-01","slug":"Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026","familyId":"020","title":"Squarefree values of quartics and power-free values of polynomials","abstract":"We prove that every irreducible integer quartic with no fixed prime-square divisor takes squarefree values with the predicted positive Euler-product density. More generally, we obtain the corresponding $(d-2)$-power-free density in degrees $4\\le d\\le8$. The proof combines number-field factorization, determinant estimates with adaptive auxiliary primes, and explicit low-degree geometry. Together with Browning's theorem for higher degrees, this gives the $(d-2)$-power-free density for every d ≥ 4.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the squarefree-values conjecture for irreducible integer quartics with no fixed prime-square divisor: squarefree values on positive integers have the predicted positive Euler-product density. More generally, establishes the $(d-2)$-power-free density for irreducible integer polynomials of degrees four through eight under the necessary local condition; together with Browning's higher-degree theorem, this covers every d ≥ 4.","pdfPath":"preprints/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026/manuscript.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026/manuscript.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-flow.tex","preprints/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-sieve.tex","preprints/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-affine-counting.tex","preprints/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-arithmetic-boxes.tex","preprints/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-determinant-cuts.tex","preprints/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-lattice-boxes.tex","preprints/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-hilbert-bounds.tex","preprints/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-quartic-geometry.tex","preprints/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-curves.tex","preprints/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-parameters.tex","preprints/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026/build/sections/11-tail-proof.tex","preprints/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026/build/sections/13-cyclotomic.tex","preprints/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026/build/sections/12-certificate.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every irreducible integer quartic with no fixed prime-square divisor takes squarefree values with the predicted positive Euler-product density. More generally, we obtain the corresponding $(d-2)$-power-free density in degrees $4\\le d\\le8$. The proof combines number-field factorization, determinant estimates with adaptive auxiliary primes, and explicit low-degree geometry. Together with Browning's theorem for higher degrees, this gives the $(d-2)$-power-free density for every $d\\ge4$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":565},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier results and the higher-degree consequence","sourceOffset":2283},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The large-prime problem","sourceOffset":9028},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":10266},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":13424},{"kind":"section","title":"From a large-prime tail to the exact density","sourceOffset":15899},{"kind":"section","title":"Affine counting with adaptive auxiliary primes","sourceOffset":27110},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Smooth patches with controlled equations","sourceOffset":30541},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A determinant cut in a simultaneous residue class","sourceOffset":35175},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Adaptive paths and a deletion estimate","sourceOffset":38647},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Good cumulative products and the counting tree","sourceOffset":43884},{"kind":"section","title":"Arithmetic factorization and paired boxes","sourceOffset":50279}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:density","text":"Let $f\\in\\Z[x]$ be irreducible over $\\Q$, of degree $d$ with $4\\le d\\le8$, and put $k=d-2$. If \\eqref{eq:local-admissibility} holds, then \\[ S_{f,k}(X)=c_{f,k}X+o_f(X),\\qquad c_{f,k}=\\prod_p\\left(1-\\frac{\\rho_f(p^k)}{p^k}\\right)>0. \\] No primitivity or sign condition on the leading coefficient is required.","sourceOffset":1253,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:all-degrees","text":"Let $f\\in\\Z[x]$ be irreducible over $\\Q$ of degree $d\\ge4$, and put $k=d-2$. If $f$ has no fixed prime $k$th-power divisor, then \\[ S_{f,k}(X)=c_{f,k}X+o_f(X),\\qquad c_{f,k}=\\prod_p\\left(1-\\frac{\\rho_f(p^k)}{p^k}\\right)>0. \\]","sourceOffset":5906,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/020.md","scopeText":"The formalized result proves positive-density power-free values for every integer polynomial $f$ irreducible over $\\mathbb Q$ of degree $d\\ge4$. Put $k=d-2$ and assume no prime $k$th power divides every value of $f$. Then the number of positive integers $n\\le X$ for which $f(n)$ is $k$-free is $c_fX+o_f(X)$, where $c_f>0$ is the convergent product of local factors. Negative values are allowed and zero is excluded. No monicity, primitivity, or coefficient-height restriction is imposed.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/020.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":169873,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":15,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Squarefree-values-of-quartics-and-power-free-values-of-polynomials-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"021-01","slug":"A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026","familyId":"021","title":"A quadratic bound for Jacobsthal's function","abstract":"Let $h(k)$ be the least integer such that every interval of $h(k)$ consecutive integers contains an integer coprime to any prescribed positive integer having at most k distinct prime divisors. We prove $h(k)\\ll k^2/(\\log\\log(3k))^2$, giving an affirmative answer to Jacobsthal's quadratic-bound question.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Answers Jacobsthal's quadratic-bound question: every interval of $Ck^2$ consecutive integers contains an integer coprime to any prescribed positive integer with at most k distinct prime divisors, for an absolute constant C. The bound is uniform over prime sets and interval positions and removes the classical logarithmic loss.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026/build/sections/inputs.tex","preprints/A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026/build/sections/tree.tex","preprints/A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026/build/figures/proof-route.tex","preprints/A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026/build/sections/functions.tex","preprints/A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026/build/sections/prime-paths.tex","preprints/A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026/build/sections/reference.tex","preprints/A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026/build/sections/boxes.tex","preprints/A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026/build/sections/inverse.tex","preprints/A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026/build/sections/variance.tex","preprints/A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026/build/sections/stops.tex","preprints/A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026/build/sections/assembly.tex","preprints/A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026/build/sections/hyperbola.tex","preprints/A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026/build/sections/elementary-inputs.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let $h(k)$ be the least integer such that every interval of $h(k)$ consecutive integers contains an integer coprime to any prescribed positive integer having at most $k$ distinct prime divisors. We prove $h(k)\\ll k^2/(\\log\\log(3k))^2$, giving an affirmative answer to Jacobsthal's quadratic-bound question.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":369},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and related work","sourceOffset":1870},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A quantitative covering estimate","sourceOffset":5712},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Outline of the argument","sourceOffset":8743},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":12821},{"kind":"section","title":"Sieve and prime-distribution inputs","sourceOffset":13503},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Small-prime counts in a progression","sourceOffset":19598},{"kind":"section","title":"The decreasing-prime tree","sourceOffset":22266},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The reference tree","sourceOffset":28026},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison after stopping","sourceOffset":29586},{"kind":"section","title":"Delay functions and the continuous path process","sourceOffset":30561},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Future integrals for the sieve functions","sourceOffset":31079}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is an absolute constant $C>0$ such that \\[ h(k)\\le C\\frac{k^2}{(\\log\\log(3k))^2} \\qquad\\text{for every integer }k\\ge1. \\]","sourceOffset":1499,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:survivors","text":"There are absolute constants $c>0$ and $z_0$ with the following property. For every real $z\\ge z_0$ and every choice of a residue class $a_p\\pmod p$ for each prime $p\\le z$, \\begin{equation} \\#\\{1\\le n\\le Y:n\\not\\equiv a_p\\pmod p\\text{ for every }p\\le z\\} \\ge c\\frac{YV_0}{B^2}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":6892,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":null,"text":"A prime \\(p\\) aligns with a reduced rational \\(A/D\\), where \\(A\\in\\Z\\), \\(D\\in\\N\\), and \\(\\gcd(A,D)=1\\), if \\[ D a_p\\equiv A\\pmod p. \\] This condition implies \\(p\\nmid D\\). For a squarefree product \\(q\\) of primes carrying prescribed classes, write \\[ q_E(A/D)= \\prod_{\\substack{\\ell\\mid q\\\\ \\ell\\text{ does not align with }A/D}}\\ell . \\]","sourceOffset":137249,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:stops-owner","text":"A reduced rational $A/D$, with $D>0$, is eligible if \\begin{equation} D\\le w^{C_s},\\qquad |A|\\le Yw^{C_s+2}. \\end{equation} Fix a constant $\\eta>0$, to be bounded by an absolute constant below. A search bin has an owner if some eligible rational aligns to at least a $1-\\eta$ fraction of its primes, by prime count. The owner is that rational. Along a descending-prime path, its tag prime is the first search-bin factor that aligns to the owner of its bin, if such a factor occurs. Its tag rational is this owner and is retained for the rest of the path.","sourceOffset":199499,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/021.md","scopeText":"Let $h(k)$ be the least interval length that guarantees an integer coprime to any prescribed positive modulus with at most $k$ distinct prime factors. The formalization proves the paper's strengthened Jacobsthal bound $h(k)\\le Ck^2/(\\log\\log(3k))^2$ for one absolute $C>0$ and every $k\\ge1$. Intervals may begin at any signed integer. The earlier quadratic bound is also retained as a separate statement; the optimal order of growth is not determined.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/021.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":267838,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-quadratic-bound-for-Jacobsthals-function-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"022-01","slug":"The-weak-inhomogeneous-Duffin-Schaeffer-conjecture-September-25-2026","familyId":"022","title":"The Weak Inhomogeneous Duffin–Schaeffer Conjecture","abstract":"We prove the weak inhomogeneous Duffin–Schaeffer conjecture. For every fixed γ ∈ ℝ and finite-valued $\\psi:\\mathbb N\\to[0,\\infty)$, divergence of $\\sum_{q\\ge1}\\phi(q)\\psi(q)/q$ implies $\\|qx-\\gamma\\|\\lt \\psi(q)$ for infinitely many q, for almost every x. Here ϕ is Euler's totient and $\\|\\cdot\\|$ is distance to the nearest integer. Numerators need not be reduced, and the shift satisfies no Diophantine restriction.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that for every real shift γ and finite-valued $\\psi:\\mathbb N\\to[0,\\infty)$, divergence of $\\sum_q\\phi(q)\\psi(q)/q$ implies $\\|qx-\\gamma\\|\\lt \\psi(q)$ for infinitely many q, for almost every x. Here ϕ is Euler's totient and the norm is distance to the nearest integer. Numerators are unrestricted; no monotonicity or Diophantine condition on γ is needed.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-weak-inhomogeneous-Duffin-Schaeffer-conjecture-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-weak-inhomogeneous-Duffin-Schaeffer-conjecture-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-weak-inhomogeneous-Duffin-Schaeffer-conjecture-September-25-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/The-weak-inhomogeneous-Duffin-Schaeffer-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-weak-inhomogeneous-Duffin-Schaeffer-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-weak-inhomogeneous-Duffin-Schaeffer-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/The-weak-inhomogeneous-Duffin-Schaeffer-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/sections/centres.tex","preprints/The-weak-inhomogeneous-Duffin-Schaeffer-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/sections/prescriptions.tex","preprints/The-weak-inhomogeneous-Duffin-Schaeffer-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/sections/tables.tex","preprints/The-weak-inhomogeneous-Duffin-Schaeffer-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/figures/chronology.tex","preprints/The-weak-inhomogeneous-Duffin-Schaeffer-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/sections/arithmetic.tex","preprints/The-weak-inhomogeneous-Duffin-Schaeffer-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/sections/direct.tex","preprints/The-weak-inhomogeneous-Duffin-Schaeffer-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/sections/sparse.tex","preprints/The-weak-inhomogeneous-Duffin-Schaeffer-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/sections/partitions.tex","preprints/The-weak-inhomogeneous-Duffin-Schaeffer-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/figures/inherited-hull.tex","preprints/The-weak-inhomogeneous-Duffin-Schaeffer-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/sections/joins.tex","preprints/The-weak-inhomogeneous-Duffin-Schaeffer-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-weak-inhomogeneous-Duffin-Schaeffer-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the weak inhomogeneous Duffin--Schaeffer conjecture. For every fixed $\\gamma\\in\\mathbb R$ and finite-valued $\\psi:\\mathbb N\\to[0,\\infty)$, divergence of $\\sum_{q\\ge1}\\phi(q)\\psi(q)/q$ implies $\\|qx-\\gamma\\|<\\psi(q)$ for infinitely many $q$, for almost every $x$. Here $\\phi$ is Euler's totient and $\\|\\cdot\\|$ is distance to the nearest integer. Numerators need not be reduced, and the shift satisfies no Diophantine restriction.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":513},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and prior work","sourceOffset":2676},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ancestry of the proof methods","sourceOffset":6755},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and reusable estimates","sourceOffset":8561},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":12455},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite blocks and elementary conventions","sourceOffset":13404},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reduction to small fixed-mass blocks","sourceOffset":13999},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Arithmetic notation and divisor laws","sourceOffset":16905},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A tensor kernel for integer families","sourceOffset":20292},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Residue averages for rational grids","sourceOffset":24641},{"kind":"section","title":"Centres, density, and prime heights","sourceOffset":26529},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Popular multiples and the density bound","sourceOffset":27147}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Weak inhomogeneous Duffin--Schaeffer","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $\\gamma\\in\\mathbb R$ be fixed, and let $\\psi:\\mathbb N\\to[0,\\infty)$ be finite-valued. If \\begin{equation} \\sum_{q=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{\\phi(q)}q\\,\\psi(q)=\\infty, \\end{equation} then, for Lebesgue-almost every $x\\in\\mathbb R$, the inequality \\[ \\|qx-\\gamma\\|<\\psi(q) \\] holds for infinitely many positive integers $q$.","sourceOffset":869,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Remaining first-join comparisons","label":"join:remaining-first-joins","text":"Consider a finite block and the retained centres, masks, and table operations of Sections \\ref{sec:centres}--\\ref{sec:partitions}, with the stated parameter hierarchy. At each first sharing of a component, omit the simultaneous-birth comparisons already treated in Section \\ref{sec:direct}. For the remaining comparisons, the following estimates hold uniformly over the permitted prior table history. \\begin{enumerate} \\item The sum of broad pair counts in $\\lambda\\otimes\\lambda$, at distance at most $C r_{\\max}$ and with width weight $r_{\\min}$, is $O(1)$. Here $C$ is any fixed positive constant. \\item The same-width, same-remaining-mask counts in common new cells, weighted by the single factor $r_i\\pi_I(a)$ and the product of the two raw weights, have total \\[ O(W)+O_{k_0}(W^{1+c}) \\] for some fixed $c>0$. \\end{enumerate} The constants are independent of the slot cap $N_0$ and of the finite block. Thus, together with the direct comparisons, these estimates establish Proposition \\ref{tab:first-join-input}.","sourceOffset":256717,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"pre:deficit-law","text":"A mask on an integer $L\\ge1$ is a divisor $d\\mid L$. Its deficit law is \\begin{equation} \\pi_L(d)=\\frac{\\phi(L/d)}L. \\end{equation} An exponent in $d$ records the corresponding deficit from $L$ in $L/d$.","sourceOffset":17478,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Popular multiples and original hosts","label":"cen:popular","text":"An integer $D\\ge1$ is popular at width $r$ if $Dr\\le k_0$ and \\[ \\sum_{\\substack{v\\mid D\\\\r(v)=r}}\\frac{\\phi(v)}D \\ge (Dr)^{H_0}. \\] A row $v$ is raw if it divides no popular multiple at its width. Otherwise its original host is the largest popular multiple at that width divisible by $v$.","sourceOffset":27366,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":299564,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":15,"definitionCount":4,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-weak-inhomogeneous-Duffin-Schaeffer-conjecture-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"023-01","slug":"An-unconditional-first-moment-for-cubic-Gauss-sums-September-25-2026","familyId":"023","title":"An unconditional first moment for cubic Gauss sums","abstract":"We prove Patterson's first-moment conjecture for normalized cubic Gauss sums over all primary Eisenstein primes, unconditionally. The sharp-cutoff main term is $(6/5)c_*X^{5/6}/\\log X$, where $c_*=(2\\pi)^{2/3}/(3\\Gamma(2/3))$. For every fixed nonzero angular Fourier mode of the prime argument, we also prove cancellation at the first-moment scale.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves unconditionally the all-primary-prime form of Patterson’s first-moment asymptotic: normalized cubic Gauss sums over primary Eisenstein primes of norm at most X, including both conjugates, have an explicit positive main term of order $X^{5/6}/\\log X$. Every fixed nonzero prime-angle Fourier mode has smaller order.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-unconditional-first-moment-for-cubic-Gauss-sums-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-unconditional-first-moment-for-cubic-Gauss-sums-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-unconditional-first-moment-for-cubic-Gauss-sums-September-25-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/An-unconditional-first-moment-for-cubic-Gauss-sums-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/An-unconditional-first-moment-for-cubic-Gauss-sums-September-25-2026/build/figures/proof-map.tex","preprints/An-unconditional-first-moment-for-cubic-Gauss-sums-September-25-2026/build/sections/background-type1.tex","preprints/An-unconditional-first-moment-for-cubic-Gauss-sums-September-25-2026/build/sections/dual.tex","preprints/An-unconditional-first-moment-for-cubic-Gauss-sums-September-25-2026/build/sections/dispersion.tex","preprints/An-unconditional-first-moment-for-cubic-Gauss-sums-September-25-2026/build/sections/decomposition.tex","preprints/An-unconditional-first-moment-for-cubic-Gauss-sums-September-25-2026/build/sections/perron.tex","preprints/An-unconditional-first-moment-for-cubic-Gauss-sums-September-25-2026/build/sections/angular.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-unconditional-first-moment-for-cubic-Gauss-sums-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Patterson's first-moment conjecture for normalized cubic Gauss sums over all primary Eisenstein primes, unconditionally. The sharp-cutoff main term is $(6/5)c_*X^{5/6}/\\log X$, where $c_*=(2\\pi)^{2/3}/(3\\Gamma(2/3))$. For every fixed nonzero angular Fourier mode of the prime argument, we also prove cancellation at the first-moment scale.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":394},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":3219},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The rational-prime convention","sourceOffset":5586},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The argument and its inputs","sourceOffset":7592},{"kind":"section","title":"Arithmetic and analytic preliminaries","sourceOffset":11938},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform conventions and classical inputs","sourceOffset":15003},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two large sieves","sourceOffset":19451},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The unconditional level input","sourceOffset":25091},{"kind":"section","title":"Type I estimates","sourceOffset":32122},{"kind":"section","title":"Character moments at the two exceptional configurations","sourceOffset":39157},{"kind":"section","title":"Corrected dispersion","sourceOffset":60614},{"kind":"section","title":"An exact decomposition of the prime sum","sourceOffset":89252}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"First moment over primary primes","label":"thm:main","text":"As $X\\longrightarrow\\infty$, \\begin{equation} \\sum_{\\substack{N\\pi\\le X\\\\\\pi\\equiv1\\pmod3\\ \\mathrm{prime}}}G(\\pi) =\\frac65c_*\\frac{X^{5/6}}{\\log X} +o\\!\\left(\\frac{X^{5/6}}{\\log X}\\right). \\end{equation} The sum is over all primary primes in $\\mathcal O$.","sourceOffset":1102,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Heath-Brown's cubic large sieve","label":"thm:cls","text":"Let $A,B\\ge1$, and let $(u_b)$ be supported on primary squarefree elements with $Nb\\asymp B$. Then, for every $\\epsilon>0$, \\[ \\sum_{Na\\asymp A}\\mu^2(a) \\left|\\sum_b u_b\\chi_a(b)\\right|^2 \\ll_\\epsilon (AB)^\\epsilon \\bigl(A+B+(AB)^{2/3}\\bigr)\\sum_b|u_b|^2. \\] The same assertion holds with upper cutoffs in place of dyads.","sourceOffset":19482,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/023.md","scopeText":"Patterson's first-moment conjecture concerns the average of normalized cubic Gauss sums over primary Eisenstein primes. The formalization proves the sharp-cutoff asymptotic with main term $\\frac65c_*X^{5/6}/\\log X$, where $c_*=(2\\pi)^{2/3}/(3\\Gamma(2/3))$. It also proves the corresponding angular comparison for every integer Fourier mode and cancellation of order $o(X^{5/6}/\\log X)$ for each fixed nonzero mode. All primary Eisenstein primes are included, without an extra conjectural premise.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/023.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":175898,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":15,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-unconditional-first-moment-for-cubic-Gauss-sums-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"024-01","slug":"An-asymptotic-formula-for-the-number-of-totients-September-25-2026","familyId":"024","title":"An asymptotic formula for the number of totients","abstract":"Let $V(x)$ count the distinct values of Euler's totient function up to x. We give an explicit asymptotic equivalent for $V(x)$. Its coefficient is a uniform limit of functions defined from finite arithmetic data. We also prove that $V(cx)/V(x)\\to c$ as $x\\to\\infty$ for every fixed c > 0, answering a question of Erdős and Hall.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives an asymptotic equivalent for the number $V(x)$ of distinct totient values up to x, with a positive bounded phase-dependent factor determined by convergent arithmetic approximations. In particular, $V(cx)/V(x)\\to c$ for every fixed c > 0, answering Erdős and Hall’s scaling question.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-asymptotic-formula-for-the-number-of-totients-September-25-2026/An-asymptotic-formula-for-the-number-of-totients-September-25-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-asymptotic-formula-for-the-number-of-totients-September-25-2026/An-asymptotic-formula-for-the-number-of-totients-September-25-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-asymptotic-formula-for-the-number-of-totients-September-25-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/An-asymptotic-formula-for-the-number-of-totients-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/An-asymptotic-formula-for-the-number-of-totients-September-25-2026/build/source/introduction.tex","preprints/An-asymptotic-formula-for-the-number-of-totients-September-25-2026/build/source/statements.tex","preprints/An-asymptotic-formula-for-the-number-of-totients-September-25-2026/build/source/extraction.tex","preprints/An-asymptotic-formula-for-the-number-of-totients-September-25-2026/build/source/layers.tex","preprints/An-asymptotic-formula-for-the-number-of-totients-September-25-2026/build/source/collisions.tex","preprints/An-asymptotic-formula-for-the-number-of-totients-September-25-2026/build/source/limits.tex","preprints/An-asymptotic-formula-for-the-number-of-totients-September-25-2026/build/source/companion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-asymptotic-formula-for-the-number-of-totients-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let $V(x)$ count the distinct values of Euler's totient function up to $x$. We give an explicit asymptotic equivalent for $V(x)$. Its coefficient is a uniform limit of functions defined from finite arithmetic data. We also prove that $V(cx)/V(x)\\to c$ as $x\\to\\infty$ for every fixed $c>0$, answering a question of Erd\\H{o}s and Hall.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":471},{"kind":"section","title":"The explicit main term","sourceOffset":6347},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The scale","sourceOffset":6569},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The arithmetic coefficient","sourceOffset":8912},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Least-preimage intervals","sourceOffset":13318},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":14515},{"kind":"section","title":"Extracting a long prime prefix","sourceOffset":16052},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Structure estimates from Ford","sourceOffset":18070},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Basic witnesses and the coverage target","sourceOffset":21239},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Simplex volume and concentration","sourceOffset":24657},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prime boxes and smooth cofactors","sourceOffset":28403},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coverage of the values","sourceOffset":34188}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For sufficiently large positive integers $H$, there are nonnegative functions $A_H(1;\\cdot):[0,1)\\to\\mathbb R$, given by the finite arithmetic formula \\eqref{eq:AH} below, which converge uniformly as $H\\to\\infty$ to a function $A(1;\\cdot)$ satisfying \\[ 0<\\inf_{0\\le s<1}A(1;s) \\le\\sup_{0\\le s<1}A(1;s)<\\infty. \\] With the parameters \\eqref{eq:renewal}--\\eqref{eq:Gj}, \\[ V(x)\\sim\\frac{x}{\\log x}\\,G_m A(1;\\theta). \\] Moreover, for every fixed real $c>0$, \\[ \\frac{V(cx)}{V(x)}\\longrightarrow c. \\]","sourceOffset":8045,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:companion","text":"For each fixed positive integer $k$, the functions $A_H(f_k;s)$ converge uniformly for $0\\le s<1$, and \\[ N_k(x)=\\frac{x}{\\log x}\\,G_m\\bigl(A(f_k;\\theta)+o(1)\\bigr). \\] There are two possibilities. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item If some $d\\in\\mathcal V$ satisfies $\\ell(d)>kd$, then \\[ \\inf_{0\\le s<1}A(f_k;s)>0,\\qquad N_k(x)\\sim\\frac{x}{\\log x}\\,G_m A(f_k;\\theta) \\asymp_k V(x). \\] \\item If no such $d$ exists, then $N_k(x)=0$ for every $x>0$, and $A(f_k;s)=0$ for every $s\\in[0,1)$. \\end{enumerate} The first alternative holds for $k=1,2$.","sourceOffset":13627,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:normal","text":"For \\(S\\ge\\e^\\e\\), a prime \\(p\\) is \\(S\\)-normal if \\[ \\Omega(p-1,1,S)\\le2\\log_2S \\] and, for every \\(S\\le U<T\\le p-1\\), \\[ \\left|\\Omega(p-1,U,T)-(\\log_2T-\\log_2U)\\right| <\\sqrt{\\log_2S\\,\\log_2T}. \\]","sourceOffset":20634,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:basic","text":"A basic witness is a choice of primes \\(p_0,\\ldots,p_L\\) and an integer \\(a\\ge1\\) satisfying \\begin{equation} \\begin{gathered} p_0\\ge x^{0.9},\\qquad 0.9\\widetilde b_i\\le\\log_2p_i\\le1.1\\widetilde b_i \\quad(1\\le i\\le L),\\\\ (u_1,\\ldots,u_L)\\in S_L(\\boldsymbol\\xi),\\qquad P^+(a)\\le p_L,\\qquad \\log a\\le\\exp(2\\widetilde b_L). \\end{gathered} \\end{equation} Here \\(u_0=B\\), and \\(u_i=\\log_2p_i\\) for \\(i\\ge1\\).","sourceOffset":21905,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/024.md","scopeText":"Let $V(x)$ count the distinct values of Euler's totient function up to $x$. The formalization constructs the paper's explicit positive main term from finite arithmetic approximants and proves that their ratio tends to one. In particular, $V(cx)/V(x)\\to c$ for every fixed $c>0$, answering the Erdős–Hall regular-variation question.\n\nThe formalization also gives asymptotics for totients $v\\le x$ whose least preimage $\\ell(v)=\\min\\{n\\ge1:\\varphi(n)=v\\}$ lies between $kx$ and $(k+1)x$. The associated coefficient is positive under the stated existence condition; when no totient $d$ satisfies $kd<\\ell(d)$, the count and coefficient are identically zero. The cases $k=1,2$ have positive coefficients.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/024.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":109726,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-asymptotic-formula-for-the-number-of-totients-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"025-01","slug":"Short-Egyptian-fractions-September-25-2026","familyId":"025","title":"Short Egyptian fractions","abstract":"We prove a conjecture of Erdős: for every sufficiently large integer b, every rational number $a/b$ with $1\\le a\\lt b$ is a sum of $O(\\log\\log b)$ distinct positive unit fractions, with an absolute implied constant. This order is best possible when the numerator varies. We also show that both the number of expansions of 1 with exactly k distinct terms and the least integer at least 2 that never occurs as a denominator in such an expansion grow doubly exponentially in k: their double logarithms have order k.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Every rational $a/b$ with $1\\le a\\lt b$ is a sum of $O(\\log\\log b)$ distinct positive unit fractions. The worst-case minimum number of terms has the same order, resolving Erdős’s conjecture on short Egyptian fractions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Short-Egyptian-fractions-September-25-2026/Short-Egyptian-fractions-September-25-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Short-Egyptian-fractions-September-25-2026/Short-Egyptian-fractions-September-25-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Short-Egyptian-fractions-September-25-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Short-Egyptian-fractions-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Short-Egyptian-fractions-September-25-2026/build/unicode-mappings.tex","preprints/Short-Egyptian-fractions-September-25-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/Short-Egyptian-fractions-September-25-2026/build/elementary.tex","preprints/Short-Egyptian-fractions-September-25-2026/build/divisors.tex","preprints/Short-Egyptian-fractions-September-25-2026/build/residues.tex","preprints/Short-Egyptian-fractions-September-25-2026/build/random.tex","preprints/Short-Egyptian-fractions-September-25-2026/build/descent.tex","preprints/Short-Egyptian-fractions-September-25-2026/build/counting.tex","preprints/Short-Egyptian-fractions-September-25-2026/build/prescribed.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Short-Egyptian-fractions-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a conjecture of Erd\\H{o}s: for every sufficiently large integer $b$, every rational number $a/b$ with $1\\le a<b$ is a sum of $O(\\log\\log b)$ distinct positive unit fractions, with an absolute implied constant. This order is best possible when the numerator varies. We also show that both the number of expansions of $1$ with exactly $k$ distinct terms and the least integer at least $2$ that never occurs as a denominator in such an expansion grow doubly exponentially in $k$: their double logarithms have order $k$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":588},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof in outline","sourceOffset":6762},{"kind":"section","title":"From a dense set to every numerator","sourceOffset":12564},{"kind":"section","title":"A uniform divisor moment","sourceOffset":19163},{"kind":"section","title":"Divisors with small residues","sourceOffset":28765},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The denominator and the residue statement","sourceOffset":29950},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Discrepancy and the deterministic list","sourceOffset":35345},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Random products","sourceOffset":43385},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A simultaneous choice of divisors","sourceOffset":48717},{"kind":"section","title":"Propagating the exceptional sets","sourceOffset":52367},{"kind":"section","title":"Counting representations of one","sourceOffset":62391},{"kind":"section","title":"Preserving a prescribed denominator","sourceOffset":68106}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are absolute constants $c_1,c_2>0$ and $b_0$ such that, for every integer $b\\ge b_0$, \\[ c_1\\log\\log b\\le N(b)\\le c_2\\log\\log b. \\] In particular, the upper bound holds for every integer numerator $1\\le a<b$.","sourceOffset":1274,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:counting","text":"There are absolute constants $c,C>0$ and $k_0$ such that, for every integer $k\\ge k_0$, \\[ ck\\le\\log\\log F(k)\\le Ck. \\]","sourceOffset":3370,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/025.md","scopeText":"An Egyptian-fraction expansion writes a rational number as a sum of distinct unit fractions. The formalization proves that every $a/b$ with $1\\le a<b$ has such an expansion and that the largest minimum length at denominator $b$ is $\\Theta(\\log\\log b)$. If $F(k)$ counts exact $k$-term expansions of $1$, it also proves $\\log\\log F(k)=\\Theta(k)$ and bounds every denominator in such an expansion.\n\nEvery integer $m\\ge2$ occurs as a denominator in an expansion of $1$, with length eventually at most $(257/\\log2+\\varepsilon)\\log\\log m$ for every $\\varepsilon>0$; padding can preserve that denominator. If $v(k)$ is the least integer at least $2$ absent from all exact $k$-term expansions of $1$, then eventually $e^{e^{k/600}}\\le v(k)\\le1+k^{2^{k-1}}$, and $\\liminf_{k\\to\\infty}\\log\\log v(k)/k\\ge\\log2/","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/025.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":90971,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Short-Egyptian-fractions-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"026-01","slug":"Positive-lower-density-of-large-prime-gaps-September-25-2026","familyId":"026","title":"Positive lower density of large prime gaps","abstract":"For every fixed C > 0, we prove that a positive proportion of consecutive prime gaps exceed $C\\log p$, where p is the smaller prime. The proportion is bounded below for every sufficiently large initial segment of the prime sequence, with a constant depending on C. It follows that the indices at which $p_n/n$ increases have positive lower asymptotic density, answering a question of Erdős and Prachar.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every fixed C > 0, a positive proportion of consecutive prime gaps exceed $C\\log p_n$, throughout every sufficiently large initial segment of the primes. The proportion may depend on C. Consequently, the indices where $p_n/n$ increases have positive lower density, answering Erdős and Prachar.","pdfPath":"preprints/Positive-lower-density-of-large-prime-gaps-September-25-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Positive-lower-density-of-large-prime-gaps-September-25-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Positive-lower-density-of-large-prime-gaps-September-25-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Positive-lower-density-of-large-prime-gaps-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Positive-lower-density-of-large-prime-gaps-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Positive-lower-density-of-large-prime-gaps-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-counting.tex","preprints/Positive-lower-density-of-large-prime-gaps-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-weights.tex","preprints/Positive-lower-density-of-large-prime-gaps-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-cancellation.tex","preprints/Positive-lower-density-of-large-prime-gaps-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-moments.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Positive-lower-density-of-large-prime-gaps-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed $C>0$, we prove that a positive proportion of consecutive prime gaps exceed $C\\log p$, where $p$ is the smaller prime. The proportion is bounded below for every sufficiently large initial segment of the prime sequence, with a constant depending on $C$. It follows that the indices at which $p_n/n$ increases have positive lower asymptotic density, answering a question of Erd\\H{o}s and Prachar.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":473},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Gap frequency and empty intervals","sourceOffset":2548},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The adjacent-interval construction","sourceOffset":3874},{"kind":"section","title":"From two adjacent intervals to gap counts","sourceOffset":6971},{"kind":"section","title":"A square with a small marked moment","sourceOffset":12696},{"kind":"section","title":"Cancellation between adjacent dimensions","sourceOffset":18128},{"kind":"section","title":"Analytic estimates for the square","sourceOffset":25252},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Residue classes and the prime mark","sourceOffset":30514},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fourier evaluation of the density sum","sourceOffset":39290},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Averaging the singular series","sourceOffset":45581},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Evaluation of the square","sourceOffset":49445}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed real $C>0$, there are constants $c(C)>0$ and $N_0(C)$ such that, for every integer $N\\ge N_0(C)$, \\[ \\#\\{1\\le n\\le N:p_{n+1}-p_n>C\\log p_n\\}\\ge c(C)N. \\]","sourceOffset":1488,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:ratios","text":"The set \\[ \\left\\{n\\ge1:\\frac{p_n}{n}<\\frac{p_{n+1}}{n+1}\\right\\} \\] has positive lower asymptotic density.","sourceOffset":2061,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/026.md","scopeText":"The formalized supplement proves that the indices $n$ for which $p_n/n<p_{n+1}/(n+1)$ have positive lower asymptotic density, where $p_n$ is the $n$th prime. Thus the normalized prime sequence has a positive-density set of increases. This is the prime-ratio corollary associated with the paper's theorem on a positive lower density of large prime gaps.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/026.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":54337,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Positive-lower-density-of-large-prime-gaps-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"027-01","slug":"Integral-points-on-character-varieties-of-curves-September-25-2026","familyId":"027","title":"Integral points on character varieties of curves","abstract":"We prove potential Zariski density of integral points on SLr-character varieties of smooth complex curves in every rank. The result allows prescribed quasi-unipotent boundary monodromy, including exact nonsemisimple conjugacy classes, and holds on every component over the full ring of integers of one finite extension. Here integrality is measured in the ambient character variety, while the exact boundary conditions are imposed on the complex representation.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the determinant-one curve case of Litt's integral-density question. For every smooth connected complex algebraic curve and every rank, integral points become Zariski dense in every component of its SLr character variety over the full ring of integers of one number field. Prescribed quasi-unipotent boundary conjugacy classes are allowed, including nonsemisimple classes.","pdfPath":"preprints/Integral-points-on-character-varieties-of-curves-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Integral-points-on-character-varieties-of-curves-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Integral-points-on-character-varieties-of-curves-September-25-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Integral-points-on-character-varieties-of-curves-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Integral-points-on-character-varieties-of-curves-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Integral-points-on-character-varieties-of-curves-September-25-2026/build/sections/model.tex","preprints/Integral-points-on-character-varieties-of-curves-September-25-2026/build/sections/arithmetic.tex","preprints/Integral-points-on-character-varieties-of-curves-September-25-2026/build/sections/diagrams.tex","preprints/Integral-points-on-character-varieties-of-curves-September-25-2026/build/sections/reduction.tex","preprints/Integral-points-on-character-varieties-of-curves-September-25-2026/build/figures/diagram-band.tex","preprints/Integral-points-on-character-varieties-of-curves-September-25-2026/build/figures/diagram-swap.tex","preprints/Integral-points-on-character-varieties-of-curves-September-25-2026/build/sections/surfaces.tex","preprints/Integral-points-on-character-varieties-of-curves-September-25-2026/build/sections/dynamics.tex","preprints/Integral-points-on-character-varieties-of-curves-September-25-2026/build/sections/descent.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Integral-points-on-character-varieties-of-curves-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove potential Zariski density of integral points on $\\SL_r$-character varieties of smooth complex curves in every rank. The result allows prescribed quasi-unipotent boundary monodromy, including exact nonsemisimple conjugacy classes, and holds on every component over the full ring of integers of one finite extension. Here integrality is measured in the ambient character variety, while the exact boundary conditions are imposed on the complex representation.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":528},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and relation to previous work","sourceOffset":4075},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof","sourceOffset":8125},{"kind":"section","title":"The integral model and exact boundary conditions","sourceOffset":11258},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Representation schemes","sourceOffset":11562},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rank conditions on the quotient","sourceOffset":13801},{"kind":"section","title":"Arithmetic parameters and integral splittings","sourceOffset":16713},{"kind":"section","title":"Filtered local systems on surfaces","sourceOffset":21725},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Surfaces, seams, and their solutions","sourceOffset":22344},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Transferring density through choices","sourceOffset":26474},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparing two flags","sourceOffset":28257},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Lifting a filtered exact sequence","sourceOffset":32242}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $X$ be a smooth connected complex algebraic curve and let $r\\geq1$. For prescribed quasi-unipotent conjugacy classes $C_1,\\ldots,C_s$ as above, let $K\\subset\\C$ be a number field containing their eigenvalues. There is a finite extension $L/K$ such that \\[ M_r(X)(\\cO_L)\\cap Y(\\C) \\] is Zariski dense in $Y$, and hence in every irreducible component of $Y$. The same conclusion holds when only the characteristic polynomial of each boundary monodromy is prescribed, with all its roots roots of unity. It also holds for the entire character variety without boundary conditions; in that case one may take $K=\\Q$. For the relative statements and for projective $X$, one may take distinct primes $p,q>r$ and \\begin{equation} F=K(\\sqrt2,\\zeta_{pq}),\\qquad L=F\\bigl(\\zeta_r,\\{u^{1/r}:u\\in\\cO_F^\\times\\}\\bigr), \\end{equation} where $\\zeta_m$ denotes a primitive $m$th root of unity. In the projective case there are no boundary conditions and $K=\\Q$.","sourceOffset":2373,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Diagram density","label":"thm:diagram-density","text":"For every surface diagram $D$ with ranks at most $r$ and prescribed scalar puncture monodromies in $F$, its integral solutions over $R$ are Zariski dense in $\\mathcal S(D)$.","sourceOffset":25723,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/027.md","scopeText":"The integral-density question asks whether integral points become Zariski dense in every component of a curve character variety after one finite extension of the number field. The linked formalization proves a supporting compatibility result for its marked-surface construction. Given a proper diagram with finitely many vertices, a chosen seam with maximal adjacent facet rank and a positive-genus parent, puncture data, an existing solution over a field, and a chosen boundary occurrence, the produced marked values satisfy every seam equation.\n\nThis is conditional compatibility of the produced solution. Potential integral density, including the prescribed-boundary and all-component conclusions, remains outside the selected statement.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/027.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":89722,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Integral-points-on-character-varieties-of-curves-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Integral-points-on-character-varieties-of-curves-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"028-01","slug":"Bounded-Step-Walks-on-Gaussian-Primes-September-26-2026","familyId":"028","title":"Bounded-Step Walks on Gaussian Primes","abstract":"We prove the Gaussian moat conjecture: no infinite walk through distinct Gaussian primes can have bounded steps. More strongly, for each fixed finite step bound, the connected components of the Gaussian-prime graph have uniformly bounded size. This bound applies to every starting prime, including primes on the coordinate axes, and is nonexplicit. The proof constructs a finite periodic sieve obstruction using geometric sampling and information-theoretic estimates.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the Gaussian moat conjecture: no infinite walk through distinct Gaussian primes can have uniformly bounded steps. More strongly, for every distance bound D, the graph joining Gaussian primes at distance at most D has uniformly bounded finite component sizes, depending only on D, including primes on the coordinate axes.","pdfPath":"preprints/Bounded-Step-Walks-on-Gaussian-Primes-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Bounded-Step-Walks-on-Gaussian-Primes-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Bounded-Step-Walks-on-Gaussian-Primes-September-26-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Bounded-Step-Walks-on-Gaussian-Primes-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Bounded-Step-Walks-on-Gaussian-Primes-September-26-2026/build/periodicity.tex","preprints/Bounded-Step-Walks-on-Gaussian-Primes-September-26-2026/build/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Bounded-Step-Walks-on-Gaussian-Primes-September-26-2026/build/geometry.tex","preprints/Bounded-Step-Walks-on-Gaussian-Primes-September-26-2026/build/coverage-transfer.tex","preprints/Bounded-Step-Walks-on-Gaussian-Primes-September-26-2026/build/schedule.tex","preprints/Bounded-Step-Walks-on-Gaussian-Primes-September-26-2026/build/information.tex","preprints/Bounded-Step-Walks-on-Gaussian-Primes-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Bounded-Step-Walks-on-Gaussian-Primes-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Gaussian moat conjecture: no infinite walk through distinct Gaussian primes can have bounded steps. More strongly, for each fixed finite step bound, the connected components of the Gaussian-prime graph have uniformly bounded size. This bound applies to every starting prime, including primes on the coordinate axes, and is nonexplicit. The proof constructs a finite periodic sieve obstruction using geometric sampling and information-theoretic estimates.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The Gaussian moat problem","sourceOffset":513},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite obstruction and the route to its proof","sourceOffset":3539},{"kind":"section","title":"Periodicity and the uniform bound","sourceOffset":7602},{"kind":"section","title":"Arithmetic, walks, and entropy","sourceOffset":10340},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Split primes","sourceOffset":10830},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Entropy conventions","sourceOffset":12857},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sampling a deterministic walk","sourceOffset":14735},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Continuity and short lists","sourceOffset":16070},{"kind":"section","title":"Entropy enrichment from a forward segment","sourceOffset":17582},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How geometry produces many differences","sourceOffset":18290},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Separating differences with randomly chosen factors","sourceOffset":20863},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From differences to endpoint entropy","sourceOffset":26884}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform component bound","label":"thm:main","text":"For every finite real $D$ there is a finite $B_D$ such that every connected component of $G_D$ has at most $B_D$ vertices. The bound is independent of the starting prime and includes primes on the coordinate axes. Consequently every sequence of distinct Gaussian primes whose successive distances are at most $D$ has at most $B_D$ terms.","sourceOffset":1080,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Finite sieve obstruction","label":"thm:sieve","text":"For every $D\\ge1$ there is a finite set $\\mathcal P_D$ of rational primes congruent to $1$ modulo $4$ such that $\\mathcal A(\\mathcal P_D)$ contains no infinite sequence of distinct points with successive distances at most $D$. The set $\\mathcal P_D$ depends only on $D$.","sourceOffset":4056,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/028.md","scopeText":"The Gaussian moat problem asks whether an infinite walk through distinct Gaussian primes can have bounded step lengths. The formalized result gives a negative answer for every real step bound $D$. More strongly, one finite bound depending only on $D$ limits the size of every connected component of the bounded-step graph and the length of every injective bounded-step walk. Axis primes and all associates are included. No explicit function of $D$ is supplied.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/028.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":89912,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Bounded-Step-Walks-on-Gaussian-Primes-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"029-01","slug":"Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026","familyId":"029","title":"Primitive roots for every admissible integer base","abstract":"We prove the infinitude assertion in Artin's primitive root conjecture: for every integer a that is neither −1 nor a square, there are at least $c_a x/(\\log x)^2$ primes in $(x,2x)$ with primitive root a, for some $c_a\\gt 0$ and every sufficiently large x.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the infinitude assertion in Artin's primitive root conjecture for every integer a that is neither −1 nor a square. For each such base, at least $c_a x/(\\log x)^2$ primes in every sufficiently large interval $(x,2x)$ have primitive root a, with $c_a\\gt 0$.","pdfPath":"preprints/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026/primitive-roots-all-integer-bases.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026/primitive-roots-all-integer-bases.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026/build/sections/00-abstract.tex","preprints/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026/build/sections/01a-reduction.tex","preprints/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026/build/sections/02-arithmetic.tex","preprints/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026/build/sections/02a-coefficient-family.tex","preprints/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026/build/sections/03-theta.tex","preprints/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026/build/sections/04-reflection.tex","preprints/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026/build/sections/05-poisson.tex","preprints/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026/build/sections/06-zero-free.tex","preprints/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026/build/sections/07-splitting.tex","preprints/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026/build/sections/08-type-ii.tex","preprints/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026/build/sections/11-sieve.tex","preprints/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026/build/sections/12-construction.tex","preprints/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026/build/sections/10-whittaker-calculation.tex","preprints/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the infinitude assertion in Artin's primitive root conjecture: for every integer $a$ that is neither $-1$ nor a square, there are at least $c_a x/(\\log x)^2$ primes in $(x,2x)$ with primitive root $a$, for some $c_a>0$ and every sufficiently large $x$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":327},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and the two obstructions","sourceOffset":2757},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Hecke zero-free argument","sourceOffset":4981},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Controlled predecessors in a fixed progression","sourceOffset":7785},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and notation","sourceOffset":10303},{"kind":"section","title":"Reduction to controlled predecessors and complete splitting","sourceOffset":11736},{"kind":"section","title":"S-integers, residue symbols, and Gauss sums","sourceOffset":20612},{"kind":"section","title":"The coefficient family and analytic comparison","sourceOffset":48932},{"kind":"section","title":"The normalized cubic theta input and its radial profiles","sourceOffset":57121},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The automorphic input and its exterior coefficients","sourceOffset":58666},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bounds and a prescribed finite Whittaker function","sourceOffset":68469},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Integration on norm sheets","sourceOffset":78494}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:primitive-root","text":"For every admissible integer $a$, there are constants $c_a>0$ and $x_a\\ge2$ such that, for every real $x\\ge x_a$, \\[ \\#\\{p\\text{ prime}:x<p<2x,\\ p\\nmid a,\\ \\ord_p(a)=p-1\\} \\ge c_a\\frac{x}{(\\log x)^2}. \\]","sourceOffset":1165,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:hecke-zero-free","text":"Let $F$ be a cyclotomic number field containing $\\mu_{12}$. For every finite-order Hecke character $\\eta$ of $F$, the meromorphic continuation of $L_F(s,\\eta)$ has no zeros in \\[ \\re s>1-10^{-6}. \\]","sourceOffset":2023,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":323123,"sourceFileCount":16,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Primitive-roots-for-every-admissible-integer-base-October-4-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"029-02","slug":"Simultaneous-primitive-roots-a-conditional-lower-bound-for-prime-bases-October-4-2026","familyId":"029","title":"Simultaneous primitive roots: a conditional lower bound for prime bases","abstract":"For every fixed finite set of distinct positive primes, we prove that at least $cx/(\\log x)^2$ primes in $(x,2x)$ have every member of the set as a primitive root, for some c > 0 and all sufficiently large x. The result assumes four explicitly stated analytic and sieve inputs from the companion paper on primitive roots for admissible integer bases.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the infinitude assertion in Artin's primitive root conjecture for every integer a that is neither −1 nor a square. For each such base, at least $c_a x/(\\log x)^2$ primes in every sufficiently large interval $(x,2x)$ have primitive root a, with $c_a\\gt 0$.","pdfPath":"preprints/Simultaneous-primitive-roots-a-conditional-lower-bound-for-prime-bases-October-4-2026/simultaneous-primitive-roots-conditional-lower-bound-prime-bases.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Simultaneous-primitive-roots-a-conditional-lower-bound-for-prime-bases-October-4-2026/simultaneous-primitive-roots-conditional-lower-bound-prime-bases.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Simultaneous-primitive-roots-a-conditional-lower-bound-for-prime-bases-October-4-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Simultaneous-primitive-roots-a-conditional-lower-bound-for-prime-bases-October-4-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Simultaneous-primitive-roots-a-conditional-lower-bound-for-prime-bases-October-4-2026/build/sections/00-abstract.tex","preprints/Simultaneous-primitive-roots-a-conditional-lower-bound-for-prime-bases-October-4-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Simultaneous-primitive-roots-a-conditional-lower-bound-for-prime-bases-October-4-2026/build/sections/02-reduction.tex","preprints/Simultaneous-primitive-roots-a-conditional-lower-bound-for-prime-bases-October-4-2026/build/sections/03-splitting.tex","preprints/Simultaneous-primitive-roots-a-conditional-lower-bound-for-prime-bases-October-4-2026/build/sections/04-marked-family.tex","preprints/Simultaneous-primitive-roots-a-conditional-lower-bound-for-prime-bases-October-4-2026/build/sections/05-prime-reservoir.tex","preprints/Simultaneous-primitive-roots-a-conditional-lower-bound-for-prime-bases-October-4-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Simultaneous-primitive-roots-a-conditional-lower-bound-for-prime-bases-October-4-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed finite set of distinct positive primes, we prove that at least $cx/(\\log x)^2$ primes in $(x,2x)$ have every member of the set as a primitive root, for some $c>0$ and all sufficiently large $x$. The result assumes four explicitly stated analytic and sieve inputs from the companion paper on primitive roots for admissible integer bases.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":401},{"kind":"section","title":"Reduction to two estimates","sourceOffset":3351},{"kind":"section","title":"A uniform splitting estimate","sourceOffset":8234},{"kind":"section","title":"A marked family in a fixed progression","sourceOffset":17932},{"kind":"section","title":"Extracting primes from the marked family","sourceOffset":40268},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The initial rough counts","sourceOffset":44478},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removing the least prime factor","sourceOffset":50048},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Balanced composites and the parameter choices","sourceOffset":56902}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Assume Inputs \\ref{input:hecke}, \\ref{input:marked}, \\ref{input:block}, and \\ref{input:density}. Let \\(k\\ge1\\) and let \\(q_1,\\ldots,q_k\\) be distinct positive primes. There are constants \\(c_{\\mathbf q}>0\\) and \\(x_{\\mathbf q}>1\\) such that, for every real \\(x\\ge x_{\\mathbf q}\\), at least \\[ c_{\\mathbf q}\\frac{x}{(\\log x)^2} \\] primes \\(p\\in(x,2x)\\) satisfy \\[ p\\nmid q_1\\cdots q_k, \\qquad \\ord_p(q_i)=p-1\\quad(1\\le i\\le k). \\]","sourceOffset":2135,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"A prime reservoir in a fixed progression","label":"prop:reservoir","text":"Assume Inputs \\ref{input:marked}, \\ref{input:block}, and \\ref{input:density}. Let \\(M\\ge1\\) be odd and squarefree, and let \\(a\\) be a residue class modulo \\(M\\) with \\(\\gcd(a(a-1),M)=1\\). There are constants \\(c_{M,a}>0\\) and \\(x_{M,a}>1\\) such that, for every real \\(x\\ge x_{M,a}\\), at least \\(c_{M,a}x/L^2\\) primes \\(p\\) satisfy \\[ x<p<2x,\\qquad p\\equiv a\\pmod M,\\qquad p=4rQ+1, \\] where \\(r\\) is a positive integer, \\(Q>x^{0.9}\\) is prime, and \\[ \\exp(L^{0.1})<\\ell<\\exp(L^{0.3}) \\qquad\\text{for every prime }\\ell\\mid r. \\] The case \\(M=1\\) is included.","sourceOffset":3703,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"hypothesis","name":"Uniform Hecke zero-free assertion","label":"input:hecke","text":"Let \\(F\\) be a cyclotomic number field containing \\(\\mu_{12}\\). For every finite-order Hecke character \\(\\chi\\) of \\(F\\), the meromorphic continuation of \\(L_F(s,\\chi)\\) has no zeros in \\[ \\Re s>1-10^{-6}. \\]","sourceOffset":8524,"truncated":false},{"kind":"hypothesis","name":"Marked Type II estimate","label":"input:marked","text":"(i) Coefficient criterion. Fix \\(\\delta,C,D_*>0\\). Suppose \\(H_m,H_n\\ge x^\\delta\\) and \\(X=H_mH_n\\asymp x\\), with fixed comparison constants. Let \\(F:\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\to\\mathbb C\\) satisfy \\(|F(h)|\\le1\\) and \\(F(ph)=F(h)\\) for every group prime \\(p\\) and every \\(h\\ge1\\). Let \\((\\alpha_m)\\) and \\((\\beta_n)\\) be scalar sequences supported on \\(W\\)-rough integers in \\([H_m,2H_m]\\) and \\([H_n,2H_n]\\), respectively, with \\(\\alpha\\) supported on an arbitrary interval \\(I\\subseteq[H_m,2H_m]\\), and with \\(|\\alpha_m|,|\\beta_n|\\le L^C\\). Suppose that for every fixed \\(A_0,B,A>0\\), every Dirichlet characte","sourceOffset":22450,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":69671,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":4,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Simultaneous-primitive-roots-a-conditional-lower-bound-for-prime-bases-October-4-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"030-01","slug":"Modularity-of-elliptic-curves-over-imaginary-quadratic-fields-October-4-2026","familyId":"030","title":"Modularity of elliptic curves over imaginary quadratic fields","abstract":"We prove the modularity conjecture for elliptic curves over imaginary quadratic fields: every such curve is modular, with matching local parameters at every place.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the modularity conjecture for elliptic curves over imaginary quadratic fields: every elliptic curve over every imaginary quadratic field is modular, with matching local parameters at every place.","pdfPath":"preprints/Modularity-of-elliptic-curves-over-imaginary-quadratic-fields-October-4-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Modularity-of-elliptic-curves-over-imaginary-quadratic-fields-October-4-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Modularity-of-elliptic-curves-over-imaginary-quadratic-fields-October-4-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Modularity-of-elliptic-curves-over-imaginary-quadratic-fields-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Modularity-of-elliptic-curves-over-imaginary-quadratic-fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Modularity-of-elliptic-curves-over-imaginary-quadratic-fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/conventions.tex","preprints/Modularity-of-elliptic-curves-over-imaginary-quadratic-fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Modularity-of-elliptic-curves-over-imaginary-quadratic-fields-October-4-2026/build/figures/degeneration-graphs.tex","preprints/Modularity-of-elliptic-curves-over-imaginary-quadratic-fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/monodromy.tex","preprints/Modularity-of-elliptic-curves-over-imaginary-quadratic-fields-October-4-2026/build/figures/slit-vanishing.tex","preprints/Modularity-of-elliptic-curves-over-imaginary-quadratic-fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/systems.tex","preprints/Modularity-of-elliptic-curves-over-imaginary-quadratic-fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/automorphy-inputs.tex","preprints/Modularity-of-elliptic-curves-over-imaginary-quadratic-fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/local.tex","preprints/Modularity-of-elliptic-curves-over-imaginary-quadratic-fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/residual.tex","preprints/Modularity-of-elliptic-curves-over-imaginary-quadratic-fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/automorphy.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Modularity-of-elliptic-curves-over-imaginary-quadratic-fields-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the modularity conjecture for elliptic curves over imaginary quadratic fields: every such curve is modular, with matching local parameters at every place.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":209},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":2121},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The geometric prime switch","sourceOffset":5657},{"kind":"section","title":"Galois and automorphic conventions","sourceOffset":10649},{"kind":"section","title":"Cyclic covers and two degenerations","sourceOffset":12973},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The family and its dihedral action","sourceOffset":13466},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Separating colliding sections","sourceOffset":17157},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The second fiber and its nodes","sourceOffset":22440},{"kind":"section","title":"Integral homology and explicit monodromy","sourceOffset":26710},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A basis obtained by cutting the torus","sourceOffset":27366},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Vanishing cycles and a common monodromy basis","sourceOffset":31271},{"kind":"section","title":"Rank-two systems and integral congruences","sourceOffset":39191}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $K$ be an imaginary quadratic number field and $E/K$ an elliptic curve. There is an isobaric automorphic representation $\\pi_E$ of $\\GL_2(\\A_K)$ with trivial central character and weight zero such that, for every prime $l$ and every isomorphism $\\iota:\\overline\\Q_l\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\C$, \\[ r_\\iota(\\pi_E)\\simeq H^1_{\\et}(E_{\\overline K},\\overline\\Q_l). \\] At every finite place $v\\nmid l$ one has \\[ \\iota\\WD\\bigl(H^1_{\\et}(E_{\\overline K},\\overline\\Q_l) |_{G_{K_v}}\\bigr)^{\\Fss} \\simeq \\rec_{K_v}\\bigl(\\pi_{E,v}\\otimes|\\det|_v^{-1/2}\\bigr). \\] The unitary parameter at the complex place is $z\\mapsto\\diag(z/|z|,\\overline z/|z|)$. In particular every local factor agrees, and \\[ L(E/K,s)=L(\\pi_E,s-1/2). \\] If $E$ has no complex multiplication, $\\pi_E$ is cuspidal. In the CM case it is given by automorphic induction of a Hecke character, or by an isobaric sum when the CM field is already $K$.","sourceOffset":1017,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"geo:compact-type","text":"Suppose that $k$ contains $\\mu_m$. At $R=O$, the special fiber of the model in Lemma \\ref{geo:semistable-models} is a rational curve joined at distinct points to $m$ copies of $E$, all defined over $k$. Rotations cyclically permute the elliptic components. The Jacobian family of $C_{m,R}$ therefore extends to an abelian scheme on a Zariski neighborhood of $O$ in the parameter curve, with fiber $E^m$ at $O$.","sourceOffset":19700,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Decomposed genericity","label":"def:decomposed-generic","text":"Let $L$ be a number field and let $\\overline r:G_L\\to\\GL_2(\\overline\\F_l)$ be continuous. The representation is decomposed generic if there is a rational prime $q\\ne l$ splitting completely in $L$ such that, for every $v\\mid q$, it is unramified at $v$ and its Frobenius eigenvalues $\\alpha_v,\\beta_v$ satisfy \\[ \\alpha_v/\\beta_v\\ne q,\\qquad \\beta_v/\\alpha_v\\ne q \\quad\\text{in }\\overline\\F_l. \\]","sourceOffset":51850,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":104976,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":15,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Modularity-of-elliptic-curves-over-imaginary-quadratic-fields-October-4-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"031-01","slug":"Open-Homomorphisms-of-Global-Solvably-Closed-Galois-Groups-October-5-2026","familyId":"031","title":"Open Homomorphisms of Global Solvably Closed Galois Groups","abstract":"We prove Uchida's conjecture on open homomorphisms of Galois groups. Every continuous open homomorphism between Galois groups of solvably closed Galois extensions of number fields is induced by a unique equivariant field embedding in the opposite direction. No restriction on the kernel or additional compatibility hypothesis is required.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Uchida's conjecture: every continuous open homomorphism between Galois groups of possibly infinite solvably closed Galois extensions of number fields comes from a unique equivariant field embedding in the opposite direction. No restriction on the kernel or separate cyclotomic-compatibility assumption is needed.","pdfPath":"preprints/Open-Homomorphisms-of-Global-Solvably-Closed-Galois-Groups-October-5-2026/open-homomorphisms-solvably-closed-galois-groups.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Open-Homomorphisms-of-Global-Solvably-Closed-Galois-Groups-October-5-2026/open-homomorphisms-solvably-closed-galois-groups.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Open-Homomorphisms-of-Global-Solvably-Closed-Galois-Groups-October-5-2026","subjectId":"1","subject":"Number theory","subjectZh":"數論","texPaths":["preprints/Open-Homomorphisms-of-Global-Solvably-Closed-Galois-Groups-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Open-Homomorphisms-of-Global-Solvably-Closed-Galois-Groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Open-Homomorphisms-of-Global-Solvably-Closed-Galois-Groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Open-Homomorphisms-of-Global-Solvably-Closed-Galois-Groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/characters.tex","preprints/Open-Homomorphisms-of-Global-Solvably-Closed-Galois-Groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/generation.tex","preprints/Open-Homomorphisms-of-Global-Solvably-Closed-Galois-Groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/matching.tex","preprints/Open-Homomorphisms-of-Global-Solvably-Closed-Galois-Groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/identification.tex","preprints/Open-Homomorphisms-of-Global-Solvably-Closed-Galois-Groups-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Open-Homomorphisms-of-Global-Solvably-Closed-Galois-Groups-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Uchida's conjecture on open homomorphisms of Galois groups. Every continuous open homomorphism between Galois groups of solvably closed Galois extensions of number fields is induced by a unique equivariant field embedding in the opposite direction. No restriction on the kernel or additional compatibility hypothesis is required.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":384},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":1776},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The main ideas","sourceOffset":3738},{"kind":"section","title":"Galois groups and partial local correspondences","sourceOffset":7667},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and solvably closed fields","sourceOffset":8058},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The anabelian inputs","sourceOffset":10926},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Tame inertia and norms","sourceOffset":14407},{"kind":"section","title":"Additive characters and Frobenius detection","sourceOffset":17150},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A logarithm-rank lemma","sourceOffset":17726},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Detection, averaging, and corestriction","sourceOffset":26450},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A signed representation in the character space","sourceOffset":32843},{"kind":"section","title":"Normalizer extension and Frobenius generation","sourceOffset":35617}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $F_1,F_2$ be number fields, and let $E_i/F_i$ be possibly infinite solvably closed Galois extensions. Every continuous open homomorphism \\[ \\alpha:\\Gal(E_1/F_1)\\longrightarrow\\Gal(E_2/F_2) \\] is induced by a unique field embedding $j:E_2\\hookrightarrow E_1$.","sourceOffset":1353,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uchida","label":"thm:uchida-isomorphism","text":"Let $E_i/F_i$ be solvably closed Galois extensions of number fields. Every isomorphism $\\theta:\\Gal(E_1/F_1)\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\Gal(E_2/F_2)$ is induced by a unique field isomorphism $j:E_2\\xrightarrow{\\sim}E_1$, with $g j=j\\theta(g)$ for all $g$.","sourceOffset":11070,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":79677,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Open-Homomorphisms-of-Global-Solvably-Closed-Galois-Groups-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"032-01","slug":"The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-CM-abelian-varieties-October-6-2026","familyId":"032","title":"The rational Hodge conjecture for CM abelian varieties","abstract":"We prove the rational Hodge conjecture for complex abelian varieties with complex multiplication: every rational Hodge class on such a variety is a rational linear combination of algebraic cycle classes. As consequences, we obtain the generalized Hodge conjecture for CM abelian varieties, the Tate conjecture for abelian varieties over finite fields, and the Hodge standard conjecture for abelian varieties in arbitrary characteristic.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the rational Hodge conjecture for every complex CM abelian variety, in every dimension and codimension. Through Milne's theorems, this also gives the Tate conjecture for all abelian varieties over finite fields and the Hodge standard conjecture for abelian varieties in every characteristic.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-CM-abelian-varieties-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-CM-abelian-varieties-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-CM-abelian-varieties-October-6-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-CM-abelian-varieties-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-CM-abelian-varieties-October-6-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-CM-abelian-varieties-October-6-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-CM-abelian-varieties-October-6-2026/build/sections/02-tensors.tex","preprints/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-CM-abelian-varieties-October-6-2026/build/sections/03-surface.tex","preprints/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-CM-abelian-varieties-October-6-2026/build/sections/04-theta.tex","preprints/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-CM-abelian-varieties-October-6-2026/build/sections/05a-moduli.tex","preprints/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-CM-abelian-varieties-October-6-2026/build/sections/05b-frobenius.tex","preprints/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-CM-abelian-varieties-October-6-2026/build/sections/05b-satake-frobenius.tex","preprints/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-CM-abelian-varieties-October-6-2026/build/sections/05c-cm-types.tex","preprints/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-CM-abelian-varieties-October-6-2026/build/sections/06-assembly.tex","preprints/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-CM-abelian-varieties-October-6-2026/build/sections/07-consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-CM-abelian-varieties-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the rational Hodge conjecture for complex abelian varieties with complex multiplication: every rational Hodge class on such a variety is a rational linear combination of algebraic cycle classes. As consequences, we obtain the generalized Hodge conjecture for CM abelian varieties, the Tate conjecture for abelian varieties over finite fields, and the Hodge standard conjecture for abelian varieties in arbitrary characteristic.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":484},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Hodge classes on CM abelian varieties","sourceOffset":2622},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From balanced tensors to surface periods","sourceOffset":6686},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Theta forms and their CM sources","sourceOffset":8810},{"kind":"section","title":"CM tensors and four-factor relations","sourceOffset":12391},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Scalar coefficients and degree-one maps","sourceOffset":13054},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rank-one CM structures","sourceOffset":17031},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reduction to four factors","sourceOffset":25956},{"kind":"section","title":"A surface criterion for the switch","sourceOffset":36251},{"kind":"section","title":"Theta differentials and a nonzero mixed period","sourceOffset":42695},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The ball and the sign data","sourceOffset":44586},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positive Lagrangians and differential bundles","sourceOffset":47863}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:cm","text":"Let \\(A\\) be a complex CM abelian variety. For every integer \\(p\\geq0\\), the Betti cycle-class map \\[ \\operatorname{cl}_B:\\CH^p(A)_{\\Q} \\longrightarrow H^{2p}(A,\\Q)\\cap H^{p,p}(A) \\] is surjective, where \\(\\CH^p(A)_{\\Q}=\\CH^p(A)\\otimes_{\\Z}\\Q\\) and the intersection is taken in \\(H^{2p}(A,\\C)\\). In particular, the rational Hodge conjecture holds in every codimension on every finite product of complex CM abelian varieties and on every power of such a product.","sourceOffset":1175,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Four-factor switch","label":"thm:switch","text":"Let \\(E\\) be a Galois CM field and let \\(v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4:G_E\\to\\{1,-1\\}\\) be odd sign functions satisfying \\(v_1+v_2=v_3+v_4\\). Under the Künneth inclusion, the following line is algebraic: \\begin{equation} U(v_1)_1\\otimes U(v_2)_1\\otimes U(v_3)_c\\otimes U(v_4)_c \\ \\subset\\ H^4\\!\\left(\\prod_{i=1}^4B(v_i),\\Qbar\\right) \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":26067,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":188863,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-CM-abelian-varieties-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"032-02","slug":"Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-correspondences-and-Hodge-conjectures-on-a-K3-quadratic-locus-September-30-2026","familyId":"032","title":"Algebraic Kuga–Satake correspondences and Hodge conjectures on a K3 quadratic locus","abstract":"We prove the rational Hodge and generalized Hodge conjectures in every cohomological degree of every self-power of a projective complex K3 surface whose transcendental quadratic space, with its cup-product form, admits a rational isometric embedding in $\\mathbb U_{\\mathbb Q}^{\\oplus2}\\perp\\langle-1\\rangle^4$. This includes every ample P-polarized K3 surface, including all Picard jumps, for $P=\\mathbb U\\oplus D_8(-1)\\oplus D_4(-1)$. On this locus we construct algebraic correspondences inducing every prescribed standard even-Clifford Kuga–Satake tensor. More generally, for any projective complex K3 surface, algebraicity of one exact standard even-Clifford Kuga–Satake tensor implies both conjectures for every self-power.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the rational Hodge conjecture for every complex CM abelian variety, in every dimension and codimension. Through Milne's theorems, this also gives the Tate conjecture for all abelian varieties over finite fields and the Hodge standard conjecture for abelian varieties in every characteristic.","pdfPath":"preprints/Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-correspondences-and-Hodge-conjectures-on-a-K3-quadratic-locus-September-30-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-correspondences-and-Hodge-conjectures-on-a-K3-quadratic-locus-September-30-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-correspondences-and-Hodge-conjectures-on-a-K3-quadratic-locus-September-30-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-correspondences-and-Hodge-conjectures-on-a-K3-quadratic-locus-September-30-2026/build/paper.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-correspondences-and-Hodge-conjectures-on-a-K3-quadratic-locus-September-30-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the rational Hodge and generalized Hodge conjectures in every cohomological degree of every self-power of a projective complex K3 surface whose transcendental quadratic space, with its cup-product form, admits a rational isometric embedding in $\\mathbb U_{\\Q}^{\\oplus2}\\perp\\langle-1\\rangle^4$. This includes every ample $P$-polarized K3 surface, including all Picard jumps, for $P=\\mathbb U\\oplus D_8(-1)\\oplus D_4(-1)$. On this locus we construct algebraic correspondences inducing every prescribed standard even-Clifford Kuga--Satake tensor. More generally, for any projective complex K3 surface, algebraicity of one exact standard even-Clifford Kuga--Satake tensor implies both conjectures for every self-power.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":788},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One exact Kuga--Satake tensor","sourceOffset":2602},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The quadratic locus","sourceOffset":4613},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and proof strategy","sourceOffset":6503},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and the lattice","sourceOffset":12387},{"kind":"section","title":"Hodge classes and geometric supports from one exact tensor","sourceOffset":14960},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Algebraic summands and coefficient extensions","sourceOffset":15264},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Algebraic returns from exterior powers","sourceOffset":18096},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Geometric supports for exterior powers","sourceOffset":24949},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The endomorphism field at an individual surface","sourceOffset":27983},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The ordinary Hodge assertion","sourceOffset":30201},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Generalized Hodge conjecture for K3 powers","sourceOffset":36112}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"main:theorem","text":"Let $S$ be a smooth projective complex K3 surface. Suppose that there is a rational isometric embedding \\[ (T_S,q)\\hookrightarrow V_P. \\] For any full even-Clifford realization $H^1(A,\\Q)=C^+(T_S,q)$, any rational nonisotropic $w\\in T_S$, and any rational polarization $E$ of this weight-one Hodge structure, there is a cycle \\[ \\Gamma_S\\in\\CH^2(S\\times A\\times A)_{\\Q} \\] whose action on every $x\\in T_S$ is exactly $\\iota_{w,E}(x)$ in $H^2(A\\times A,\\Q)$. The same assertion holds on a transported isogenous model with its specified tensor. Moreover, the rational Hodge conjecture and the rational generalized Hodge conjecture hold in every cohomological degree of every self-power $S^m$, for every integer $m\\ge1$.","sourceOffset":5321,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"ext:criterion","text":"Let $S$ be a projective K3 surface. Suppose that, for one rational nonisotropic $w\\in T_S$, one rational polarization, and one full even-Clifford realization, the tensor of $j_w(v)(a)=vaw$ is induced by a cycle in $\\CH^2(S\\times A^2)_{\\Q}$. Then rational HC and rational GHC hold in every degree of every self-power of $S$.","sourceOffset":4128,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":258844,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-correspondences-and-Hodge-conjectures-on-a-K3-quadratic-locus-September-30-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"032-03","slug":"Weil-classes-and-Hodge-classes-on-abelian-powers-October-6-2026","familyId":"032","title":"Weil classes and Hodge classes on abelian powers","abstract":"We prove the rational Hodge conjecture in every codimension on every self-power of a complex abelian sixfold with an imaginary-quadratic action and a compatible polarization whose rational homological Hermitian form is hyperbolic of signature $(3,3)$. We also prove it in every codimension on every self-power of a complex abelian variety of dimension at most five admitting an imaginary-quadratic action. Both results include nonsimple varieties and special periods with additional endomorphisms. The proof uses the companion theorem on the rational Hodge conjecture for CM abelian varieties.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the rational Hodge conjecture for every complex CM abelian variety, in every dimension and codimension. Through Milne's theorems, this also gives the Tate conjecture for all abelian varieties over finite fields and the Hodge standard conjecture for abelian varieties in every characteristic.","pdfPath":"preprints/Weil-classes-and-Hodge-classes-on-abelian-powers-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Weil-classes-and-Hodge-classes-on-abelian-powers-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Weil-classes-and-Hodge-classes-on-abelian-powers-October-6-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Weil-classes-and-Hodge-classes-on-abelian-powers-October-6-2026/build/paper.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Weil-classes-and-Hodge-classes-on-abelian-powers-October-6-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the rational Hodge conjecture in every codimension on every self-power of a complex abelian sixfold with an imaginary-quadratic action and a compatible polarization whose rational homological Hermitian form is hyperbolic of signature \\((3,3)\\). We also prove it in every codimension on every self-power of a complex abelian variety of dimension at most five admitting an imaginary-quadratic action. Both results include nonsimple varieties and special periods with additional endomorphisms. The proof uses the companion theorem on the rational Hodge conjecture for CM abelian varieties.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":796},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weil classes and earlier results","sourceOffset":3169},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":7636},{"kind":"section","title":"Algebraic supply and invariant tensors","sourceOffset":11321},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Correspondences from CM abelian varieties","sourceOffset":12421},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Degree-one summands and pairings","sourceOffset":15463},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The tensor criterion","sourceOffset":21387},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Passing from supplied cohomology to powers","sourceOffset":35048},{"kind":"section","title":"Quadratic actions at every period","sourceOffset":38911},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Weil-plane input","sourceOffset":39361},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Determinants supplied by a split sixfold","sourceOffset":44166},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Columns and their pairings at the given period","sourceOffset":50515}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Split Weil sixfolds","label":"p3ab:split-sixfolds","text":"Let \\(E\\) be an imaginary quadratic field and let \\(A\\) be a complex abelian sixfold with an \\(E\\)-action. Suppose a compatible polarization has hyperbolic rational Hermitian form of signature \\((3,3)\\). Then the rational Hodge conjecture holds in every codimension on \\(A^m\\), for every \\(m\\geq1\\).","sourceOffset":2107,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Quadratic actions in dimension at most five","label":"p3ab:quadratic-five","text":"Let \\(A\\) be a complex abelian variety of dimension at most five admitting an action of an imaginary quadratic field. The rational Hodge conjecture holds in every codimension on \\(A^m\\), for every \\(m\\geq1\\).","sourceOffset":2489,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":367202,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":23,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Weil-classes-and-Hodge-classes-on-abelian-powers-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"032-04","slug":"Abelian-covers-Gale-correspondences-and-the-Hodge-conjecture-for-powers-October-6-2026","familyId":"032","title":"Abelian covers, Gale correspondences, and the Hodge conjecture for powers","abstract":"We prove the rational Hodge conjecture on every self-power of the Jacobian at each tensor Hodge-generic point of the full marked variation of a connected abelian cover of curves. This holds in every base genus and for every compatible branching pattern. For any CM abelian variety, the conclusion also holds for every self-power of its product with the Jacobian, on the same Hodge-generic locus. We also prove the conjecture on every self-power of a very general member of the full smooth labelled family of diagonal complete intersections cut out by at most two equations of a common degree, in every dimension and every degree at least two.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the rational Hodge conjecture for every complex CM abelian variety, in every dimension and codimension. Through Milne's theorems, this also gives the Tate conjecture for all abelian varieties over finite fields and the Hodge standard conjecture for abelian varieties in every characteristic.","pdfPath":"preprints/Abelian-covers-Gale-correspondences-and-the-Hodge-conjecture-for-powers-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Abelian-covers-Gale-correspondences-and-the-Hodge-conjecture-for-powers-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Abelian-covers-Gale-correspondences-and-the-Hodge-conjecture-for-powers-October-6-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Abelian-covers-Gale-correspondences-and-the-Hodge-conjecture-for-powers-October-6-2026/build/paper.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Abelian-covers-Gale-correspondences-and-the-Hodge-conjecture-for-powers-October-6-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the rational Hodge conjecture on every self-power of the Jacobian at each tensor Hodge-generic point of the full marked variation of a connected abelian cover of curves. This holds in every base genus and for every compatible branching pattern. For any CM abelian variety, the conclusion also holds for every self-power of its product with the Jacobian, on the same Hodge-generic locus. We also prove the conjecture on every self-power of a very general member of the full smooth labelled family of diagonal complete intersections cut out by at most two equations of a common degree, in every dimension and every degree at least two.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":709},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Abelian covers","sourceOffset":1361},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Diagonal complete intersections","sourceOffset":3719},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier work","sourceOffset":5210},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof in outline","sourceOffset":9072},{"kind":"section","title":"Shared algebraic and tensor tools","sourceOffset":13199},{"kind":"section","title":"CM sources for character determinants","sourceOffset":20935},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fermat cohomology from CM abelian varieties","sourceOffset":21607},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Character determinants on curves","sourceOffset":29411},{"kind":"section","title":"Abelian covers with full marked variation","sourceOffset":52590},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The marked family and its Hodge-generic locus","sourceOffset":53138},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A rank-one criterion for standard actions","sourceOffset":60095}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Powers of diagonal complete intersections","label":"thm:main","text":"Fix \\(n\\geq2\\), \\(d\\geq0\\), \\(c\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\), and put \\(m=d+c+1\\). Outside a countable union of proper closed algebraic subsets of \\(\\D_{c,m}\\), the rational Hodge conjecture holds in every codimension on \\(X_A^u\\) for every \\(u\\geq1\\). For \\(c\\leq1\\), or for \\(d=0\\), no exceptional subset is needed.","sourceOffset":4480,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Powers of abelian-cover Jacobians","label":"p4:abelian-covers","text":"For each fixed connected topological abelian cover as above, every \\(s\\in\\mathcal T_\\phi^{\\mathrm{Hg}}\\), every complex CM abelian variety \\(M\\), and every integer \\(N\\geq1\\), the rational Hodge conjecture holds in every codimension of \\[ \\bigl(\\Jac(C_s)\\times M\\bigr)^N. \\] It also holds for \\(C_s^a\\times M^b\\) for all integers \\(a,b\\geq0\\), where a zeroth power means a point. The point \\(s\\) is chosen before \\(M\\): the same Hodge-generic locus works for every CM companion.","sourceOffset":2733,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:root","text":"Let \\(m,n\\geq2\\) and \\(0\\leq d\\leq m-2\\). A subspace \\(P\\subset\\C^m\\) of dimension \\(d+1\\) is coordinate-general if every \\(d+1\\) coordinate functionals restrict to a basis of \\(P^*\\). Let \\(\\U_{d,m}\\subset\\Gr(d+1,m)\\) be this open locus. For \\(P\\in\\U_{d,m}\\), define \\[ X_P=\\{[x_i]\\in\\PP^{m-1}:(x_1^n,\\ldots,x_m^n)\\in P\\},\\qquad G_{m,n}=(\\mu_n)^m/\\mu_n, \\] where the denominator is diagonal. The phase action preserves \\(X_P\\), and \\(q_P:X_P\\to\\PP(P)\\), \\([x_i]\\mapsto[x_i^n]\\), is its coordinate-power map.","sourceOffset":121167,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":176250,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":15,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Abelian-covers-Gale-correspondences-and-the-Hodge-conjecture-for-powers-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"032-05","slug":"A-Conditional-Reduction-for-Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-September-10-2026","familyId":"032","title":"A Conditional Reduction for Algebraic Kuga–Satake Correspondences","abstract":"For a polarized K3 surface whose primitive cohomology has full orthogonal Hodge group, one algebraic correspondence inducing a nonzero map from that cohomology to the second cohomology of an abelian variety suffices to recover the prescribed full Kuga–Satake correspondence. We give an equivalent condition using holomorphic one-forms on a generically finite surface cover. If this input holds very generally in a polarized component, the prescribed correspondence is algebraic throughout that component, for every choice of standard data on the transcendental part. The existence of the initial correspondence remains a hypothesis.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the rational Hodge conjecture for every complex CM abelian variety, in every dimension and codimension. Through Milne's theorems, this also gives the Tate conjecture for all abelian varieties over finite fields and the Hodge standard conjecture for abelian varieties in every characteristic.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Conditional-Reduction-for-Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-September-10-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Conditional-Reduction-for-Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-September-10-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Conditional-Reduction-for-Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-September-10-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-Conditional-Reduction-for-Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-September-10-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-Conditional-Reduction-for-Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-September-10-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-Conditional-Reduction-for-Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-September-10-2026/build/sections/cycles.tex","preprints/A-Conditional-Reduction-for-Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-September-10-2026/build/sections/clifford.tex","preprints/A-Conditional-Reduction-for-Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-September-10-2026/build/sections/generic.tex","preprints/A-Conditional-Reduction-for-Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-September-10-2026/build/sections/specialization.tex","preprints/A-Conditional-Reduction-for-Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-September-10-2026/build/sections/special_data.tex","preprints/A-Conditional-Reduction-for-Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-September-10-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex","preprints/A-Conditional-Reduction-for-Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-September-10-2026/build/references/dependency-table.tex","preprints/A-Conditional-Reduction-for-Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-September-10-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Conditional-Reduction-for-Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-September-10-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For a polarized K3 surface whose primitive cohomology has full orthogonal Hodge group, one algebraic correspondence inducing a nonzero map from that cohomology to the second cohomology of an abelian variety suffices to recover the prescribed full Kuga--Satake correspondence. We give an equivalent condition using holomorphic one-forms on a generically finite surface cover. If this input holds very generally in a polarized component, the prescribed correspondence is algebraic throughout that component, for every choice of standard data on the transcendental part. The existence of the initial correspondence remains a hypothesis.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction and statements","sourceOffset":705},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and the exact target","sourceOffset":2556},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The conditional theorems","sourceOffset":5083},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof outline","sourceOffset":8171},{"kind":"section","title":"Cycle operations and the two inputs","sourceOffset":8868},{"kind":"section","title":"Full Clifford structures and their tensor maps","sourceOffset":17269},{"kind":"section","title":"Recovering the full Clifford correspondence at a general point","sourceOffset":25710},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The generic Hodge group","sourceOffset":26231},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The spin representation forced by weight one","sourceOffset":28982},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Independent target operations and rational descent","sourceOffset":37604},{"kind":"section","title":"Specialization of an actual algebraic class","sourceOffset":42787},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Hilbert parameters over an analytic base","sourceOffset":43212}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Recovery at a point with full Hodge group","label":"main:generic","text":"Suppose $\\operatorname{Hg}(V)=\\operatorname{SO}(V,q)$. The following conditions are equivalent. \\begin{enumerate} \\item There are an abelian variety $B$ and a cycle $\\alpha\\in\\operatorname{CH}^2(S\\times B)_{\\mathbb Q}$ such that \\[ \\alpha_*:V\\longrightarrow H^2(B,\\mathbb Q) \\] is nonzero. \\item There are a smooth connected projective surface $Y$ and a dominant generically finite morphism $f:Y\\to S$ such that \\begin{equation} f_*\\!\\left(\\operatorname{im}\\!\\left[ \\bigwedge\\nolimits^2 H^0(Y,\\Omega_Y^1) \\longrightarrow H^0(Y,\\Omega_Y^2)\\right]\\right)\\ne0. \\end{equation} \\item The prescribed full Kuga--Satake map is realized as in \\eqref{eq:cycle-target}, for any stipulated standard data on $T$. \\end{enumerate} In this situation $T=V$. The same input realizes the standard map for the full primitive Clifford construction with the cup form.","sourceOffset":5122,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Specialization in a polarized component","label":"main:specialization","text":"Fix a component of the moduli space of primitively amply polarized K3 surfaces of degree $2d$. Assume that condition \\textup{(i)} or condition \\textup{(ii)} of Theorem \\ref{main:generic} holds very generally in that component. Then every $(S,h)$ in the component satisfies \\eqref{eq:cycle-target}, with all the stipulated standard data on $T(S)$. Here it suffices that, about each desired point, the input holds outside a countable union of proper closed analytic subsets of a full polarized period chart. In particular an input outside countably many proper algebraic subsets of the moduli component suffices. The witness cycles, abelian varieties, or surface covers need not vary in a family.","sourceOffset":6446,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":86428,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Conditional-Reduction-for-Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-September-10-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"033-01","slug":"Orbifold-and-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-26-2026","familyId":"033","title":"Orbifold and logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity","abstract":"We prove Campana's orbifold Iitaka subadditivity conjecture for rational simple normal crossing boundaries on compact manifolds in Fujiki class $\\mathcal C$, including coefficient one. Ordinary and logarithmic subadditivity follow.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Campana's orbifold Iitaka subadditivity conjecture for smooth Fujiki-class-$\\mathcal C$ manifolds with rational simple-normal-crossing boundaries. For projective fibrations $f:U\\to V$ of smooth complex quasi-projective varieties with connected fibers, general fiber F, and $\\bar\\kappa(V)\\ge0$, proves Popa's inequality $\\bar\\kappa(U)\\ge\\kappa(F)+\\max\\{\\bar\\kappa(V),\\mathop{\\mathrm{Var}}\\nolimits (f)\\}$, where variation measures the whole geometric generic fiber.","pdfPath":"preprints/Orbifold-and-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Orbifold-and-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Orbifold-and-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-26-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Orbifold-and-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Orbifold-and-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-26-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Orbifold-and-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-26-2026/build/figures/two-maps.tex","preprints/Orbifold-and-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-26-2026/build/sections/models.tex","preprints/Orbifold-and-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-26-2026/build/sections/root-comparison.tex","preprints/Orbifold-and-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-26-2026/build/sections/adjoint-addition.tex","preprints/Orbifold-and-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-26-2026/build/sections/period-positivity.tex","preprints/Orbifold-and-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-26-2026/build/sections/general-type-addition.tex","preprints/Orbifold-and-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-26-2026/build/sections/applications.tex","preprints/Orbifold-and-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-26-2026/build/sections/reduced-root.tex","preprints/Orbifold-and-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-26-2026/build/sections/normalization.tex","preprints/Orbifold-and-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Orbifold-and-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Campana's orbifold Iitaka subadditivity conjecture for rational simple normal crossing boundaries on compact manifolds in Fujiki class $\\mathcal C$, including coefficient one. Ordinary and logarithmic subadditivity follow.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":294},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The orbifold base and the main theorem","sourceOffset":3490},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The reduction and the two positivity inputs","sourceOffset":8403},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The reduced-boundary refinement","sourceOffset":14403},{"kind":"section","title":"The relative Iitaka reduction and its Hodge line","sourceOffset":16181},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The fixed smooth models","sourceOffset":17055},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reduced exceptional boundaries","sourceOffset":17945},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Flattening relative to a fixed reference pair","sourceOffset":23721},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Keeping the original base fixed","sourceOffset":27512},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The relative Iitaka map","sourceOffset":29917},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exact comparison for Kodaira-zero fibers","sourceOffset":33142},{"kind":"subsubsection","title":"The root form and its eigenspace","sourceOffset":36757}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Orbifold subadditivity","label":"main:orbifold","text":"Let $X$ be a smooth compact connected complex manifold in Fujiki class $\\mathcal C$, let $\\Delta$ be a rational SNC boundary, and let $f:X\\to Y$ be a surjective holomorphic map with connected fibers onto a normal compact irreducible complex space. For a very general smooth fiber $F$, put $\\Delta_F=\\Delta|_F$. Then \\begin{equation} \\kappa(X,K_X+\\Delta) \\geq\\kappa(F,K_F+\\Delta_F)+\\kappa(f\\mid\\Delta). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":6581,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Descent across exceptional centers","label":"setup:exceptional-descent","text":"In the situation of \\Cref{setup:log-modification}, a meromorphic $m$-pluricanonical form on $Z'$ satisfying the boundary bounds of $m\\Gamma'$ at every nonexceptional prime descends to a section of $m(K_Z+\\Gamma)$ whenever $m\\Gamma$ and $m\\Gamma'$ are integral. The assertion also holds after tensoring by the pullback of a line bundle on $Z$.","sourceOffset":20648,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/033.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":232756,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Orbifold-and-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"033-02","slug":"Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026","familyId":"033","title":"Logarithmic Kodaira dimension and whole-fiber variation","abstract":"We prove the logarithmic Iitaka–Viehweg inequality for projective surjective morphisms with connected fibers between smooth complex quasi-projective varieties whose base has nonnegative logarithmic Kodaira dimension. The variation measures the birational field of definition of the whole geometric generic fiber. This resolves Popa's logarithmic variation conjecture positively.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Campana's orbifold Iitaka subadditivity conjecture for smooth Fujiki-class-$\\mathcal C$ manifolds with rational simple-normal-crossing boundaries. For projective fibrations $f:U\\to V$ of smooth complex quasi-projective varieties with connected fibers, general fiber F, and $\\bar\\kappa(V)\\ge0$, proves Popa's inequality $\\bar\\kappa(U)\\ge\\kappa(F)+\\max\\{\\bar\\kappa(V),\\mathop{\\mathrm{Var}}\\nolimits (f)\\}$, where variation measures the whole geometric generic fiber.","pdfPath":"preprints/Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026/build/companion-locators.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026/build/figures/whole-fiber-route.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026/build/sections/02-parameter.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026/build/figures/combined-base.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026/build/sections/03-root-hodge.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026/build/sections/04-constancy.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026/build/sections/04-constancy-estimates.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026/build/sections/05-model-comparison.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026/build/sections/06-marking.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026/build/sections/07-descent.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026/build/sections/08-numerical-extension.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026/build/sections/09-ordinary-reduction.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the logarithmic Iitaka--Viehweg inequality for projective surjective morphisms with connected fibers between smooth complex quasi-projective varieties whose base has nonnegative logarithmic Kodaira dimension. The variation measures the birational field of definition of the whole geometric generic fiber. This resolves Popa's logarithmic variation conjecture positively.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":442},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the two constancy problems","sourceOffset":4530},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its parameter field","sourceOffset":8917},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Smooth families","sourceOffset":14010},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and an additional numerical result","sourceOffset":17080},{"kind":"section","title":"The parameter field detected by logarithmic forms","sourceOffset":18695},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The lower bound and section conventions","sourceOffset":19116},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Models, sections, and fields","sourceOffset":20272},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compactification and the first Iitaka base","sourceOffset":28929},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Logarithmic restrictions to the Iitaka fibers","sourceOffset":37652},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The image family and the second base","sourceOffset":42715},{"kind":"section","title":"The root cover and its Hodge line","sourceOffset":51145}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Logarithmic variation","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $f:U\\to V$ be a projective surjective morphism with connected fibers between smooth connected quasi-projective complex varieties. Assume $\\kbar(V)\\geq0$, and let $F$ be its geometric generic fiber. Then \\begin{equation} \\kbar(U)\\geq\\kappa(F)+\\max\\{\\kbar(V),\\Var(f)\\}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":2296,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"The dimension retained by interpolation","label":"num:main","text":"Let $g:W\\to Y$ and $h:W\\to Z$ be surjective morphisms with connected fibers between smooth projective complex varieties. Let $B$ be an effective rational SNC boundary, and let $D_Y$ be a reduced SNC divisor, with the zero divisor allowed. Assume \\[ B\\geq(g^*D_Y)_{\\red},\\qquad \\kappa(Y,K_Y+D_Y)\\geq0. \\] Let $A$ be a nef rational divisor on $Z$ such that $K_Z+aA$ is big for all sufficiently large rational $a$. Put \\[ M=h^*A,\\qquad L(c)=K_W+B+cM,\\qquad d=\\dim(W/Y), \\] and suppose $L(1)$ is big over $Y$. On smooth birational models, with the boundary rule of Lemma \\ref{setup:log-modification}, there are morphisms \\[ W\\xrightarrow{q}U\\xrightarrow{j}Z, \\qquad U\\xrightarrow{s}T_0,\\qquad S\\xrightarrow{p}T_0, \\qquad \\rho:S\\to Y \\] with the following properties: \\begin{enumerate} \\item $h=jq$, and $q$ is the relative Iitaka fibration of $K_W+B$ over $Z$. Its geometric general fiber $G$ is integral and satisfies $\\kappa(G,K_G+B_G)=0$. \\item The map $g$ factors through $S$; $\\rho$ is birational, and $W\\to(S\\times_{T_0}U)_{\\mathrm{main}}$ is dominant and generically finite. The generic fibers of $S\\to T_0$ and $U\\to T_0$ are geometrically integral. With $D_S=(\\rho^*D_Y)_{\\red}$ made SNC, one has $\\kappa(S_t,K_{S_t}+D_S|_{S_t})=0$ for very general $t$. \\item One has \\begin{equation} \\kappa(W,L(1))=\\dim U=d+\\dim T_0, \\qquad \\kappa(Y,K_Y+D_Y)\\leq\\dim T_0. \\end{equation} \\end{enumerate} Rational linear equivalence in the definition of $M$ is sufficient. All section comparisons are with the fi","sourceOffset":230797,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"mk:moving-definition","text":"In the notation \\eqref{mk:fields}, let $E/b(I)$ be a finite extension. A moving test is a prime divisor $P$ of $V'_E$ whose image in $V'$ is dense. Its valuation is trivial both on $k=b(V')$ and on $E$.","sourceOffset":197307,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/033.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":303019,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":31,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Logarithmic-Kodaira-dimension-and-whole-fiber-variation-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"033-03","slug":"The-reverse-logarithmic-Kodaira-inequality-and-additivity-September-26-2026","familyId":"033","title":"The reverse logarithmic Kodaira inequality and additivity","abstract":"We prove the reverse logarithmic Kodaira inequality for a surjective connected-fiber morphism $f:(X,E)\\to(Y,D)$ of smooth projective reduced simple-normal-crossing pairs, with $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Supp}}\\nolimits (f^*D)\\subseteq\\mathop{\\mathrm{Supp}}\\nolimits E$, such that X and every boundary stratum are smooth over $Y\\setminus\\mathop{\\mathrm{Supp}}\\nolimits D$. Together with logarithmic subadditivity, the inequality gives additivity, including both negative-infinity cases. This resolves Popa's logarithmic additivity conjecture positively in the projective reduced-SNC, stratum-smooth setting.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Campana's orbifold Iitaka subadditivity conjecture for smooth Fujiki-class-$\\mathcal C$ manifolds with rational simple-normal-crossing boundaries. For projective fibrations $f:U\\to V$ of smooth complex quasi-projective varieties with connected fibers, general fiber F, and $\\bar\\kappa(V)\\ge0$, proves Popa's inequality $\\bar\\kappa(U)\\ge\\kappa(F)+\\max\\{\\bar\\kappa(V),\\mathop{\\mathrm{Var}}\\nolimits (f)\\}$, where variation measures the whole geometric generic fiber.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-reverse-logarithmic-Kodaira-inequality-and-additivity-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-reverse-logarithmic-Kodaira-inequality-and-additivity-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-reverse-logarithmic-Kodaira-inequality-and-additivity-September-26-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/The-reverse-logarithmic-Kodaira-inequality-and-additivity-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-reverse-logarithmic-Kodaira-inequality-and-additivity-September-26-2026/build/companion-locators.tex","preprints/The-reverse-logarithmic-Kodaira-inequality-and-additivity-September-26-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-reverse-logarithmic-Kodaira-inequality-and-additivity-September-26-2026/build/sections/reduction.tex","preprints/The-reverse-logarithmic-Kodaira-inequality-and-additivity-September-26-2026/build/sections/geometric-vector.tex","preprints/The-reverse-logarithmic-Kodaira-inequality-and-additivity-September-26-2026/build/sections/marked-periods.tex","preprints/The-reverse-logarithmic-Kodaira-inequality-and-additivity-September-26-2026/build/sections/boundary-rank.tex","preprints/The-reverse-logarithmic-Kodaira-inequality-and-additivity-September-26-2026/build/sections/relative-iitaka.tex","preprints/The-reverse-logarithmic-Kodaira-inequality-and-additivity-September-26-2026/build/figures/intermediate-base.tex","preprints/The-reverse-logarithmic-Kodaira-inequality-and-additivity-September-26-2026/build/sections/nonvanishing.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-reverse-logarithmic-Kodaira-inequality-and-additivity-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the reverse logarithmic Kodaira inequality for a surjective connected-fiber morphism $f:(X,E)\\to(Y,D)$ of smooth projective reduced simple-normal-crossing pairs, with $\\Supp(f^*D)\\subseteq\\Supp E$, such that $X$ and every boundary stratum are smooth over $Y\\setminus\\Supp D$. Together with logarithmic subadditivity, the inequality gives additivity, including both negative-infinity cases. This resolves Popa's logarithmic additivity conjecture positively in the projective reduced-SNC, stratum-smooth setting.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":583},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The section-construction result","sourceOffset":3431},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two outcomes of the Hodge construction","sourceOffset":5018},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier results and the proof route","sourceOffset":7379},{"kind":"section","title":"Reduction to the section construction","sourceOffset":12162},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and birational changes","sourceOffset":12505},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two negative-infinity cases","sourceOffset":15124},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A base of Kodaira dimension zero","sourceOffset":15962},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positive and intermediate base dimensions","sourceOffset":17506},{"kind":"section","title":"A logarithmic Hodge vector and its coefficient lattice","sourceOffset":21319},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Variations and extension conventions","sourceOffset":22168},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The vector and the coefficient statement","sourceOffset":27017}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Reverse logarithmic Kodaira inequality","label":"thm:upper-bound","text":"Under these hypotheses, \\begin{equation} \\kappa(X,L_X)\\leq\\kappa(Y,L_Y)+\\kappa(F,L_F). \\end{equation} If either term on the right is $-\\infty$, then $H^0(X,mL_X)=0$ for every $m>0$.","sourceOffset":1899,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Logarithmic additivity","label":"cor:additivity","text":"For the pairs in Theorem \\ref{thm:upper-bound}, \\begin{equation} \\kappa(X,K_X+E)=\\kappa(Y,K_Y+D)+\\kappa(F,K_F+E_F). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":2866,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/033.md","scopeText":"The paper studies logarithmic Kodaira additivity for connected-fiber morphisms of smooth projective reduced simple-normal-crossing pairs that are smooth on all boundary strata away from the base boundary. The linked formalization proves the negative-fiber branch: for a very general base point, if the logarithmic Kodaira dimension of the fiber is $-\\infty$, then the total logarithmic Kodaira dimension equals the sum of the base and fiber dimensions and is $-\\infty$. Every positive-degree logarithmic pluriform section on the total space then vanishes.\n\nThe finite-dimension and negative-base branches of the paper's additivity theorem are outside this selected statement.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/033.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":202105,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-reverse-logarithmic-Kodaira-inequality-and-additivity-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"033-04","slug":"Projective-Hodge-lines-and-ordinary-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-27-2026","familyId":"033","title":"Projective Hodge lines and ordinary Iitaka subadditivity","abstract":"We prove the ordinary Iitaka subadditivity conjecture for surjective projective morphisms with connected fibers between smooth connected projective varieties over algebraically closed fields of characteristic zero. If F is the geometric generic fiber of $f:X\\to Z$, then $\\kappa(X)\\geq\\kappa(F)+\\kappa(Z)$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Campana's orbifold Iitaka subadditivity conjecture for smooth Fujiki-class-$\\mathcal C$ manifolds with rational simple-normal-crossing boundaries. For projective fibrations $f:U\\to V$ of smooth complex quasi-projective varieties with connected fibers, general fiber F, and $\\bar\\kappa(V)\\ge0$, proves Popa's inequality $\\bar\\kappa(U)\\ge\\kappa(F)+\\max\\{\\bar\\kappa(V),\\mathop{\\mathrm{Var}}\\nolimits (f)\\}$, where variation measures the whole geometric generic fiber.","pdfPath":"preprints/Projective-Hodge-lines-and-ordinary-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Projective-Hodge-lines-and-ordinary-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Projective-Hodge-lines-and-ordinary-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-27-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Projective-Hodge-lines-and-ordinary-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Projective-Hodge-lines-and-ordinary-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-27-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Projective-Hodge-lines-and-ordinary-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-27-2026/build/figures/period-and-original-base.tex","preprints/Projective-Hodge-lines-and-ordinary-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-27-2026/build/sections/02-rational.tex","preprints/Projective-Hodge-lines-and-ordinary-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-27-2026/build/sections/03-limits.tex","preprints/Projective-Hodge-lines-and-ordinary-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-27-2026/build/sections/04-rankloss.tex","preprints/Projective-Hodge-lines-and-ordinary-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-27-2026/build/sections/05-quotient.tex","preprints/Projective-Hodge-lines-and-ordinary-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-27-2026/build/sections/06-canonical.tex","preprints/Projective-Hodge-lines-and-ordinary-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-27-2026/build/sections/07-ordinary.tex","preprints/Projective-Hodge-lines-and-ordinary-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-27-2026/build/sections/08-integral.tex","preprints/Projective-Hodge-lines-and-ordinary-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-27-2026/build/sections/09-thom.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Projective-Hodge-lines-and-ordinary-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the ordinary Iitaka subadditivity conjecture for surjective projective morphisms with connected fibers between smooth connected projective varieties over algebraically closed fields of characteristic zero. If $F$ is the geometric generic fiber of $f:X\\to Z$, then $\\kappa(X)\\geq\\kappa(F)+\\kappa(Z)$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":371},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The boundary obstruction and its resolution","sourceOffset":6173},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From the period theorem to ordinary subadditivity","sourceOffset":9148},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":13189},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":14153},{"kind":"section","title":"Rational minimality and fixed periods","sourceOffset":14824},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The smallest rational subvariation","sourceOffset":15994},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From a fixed highest line to a fixed full period","sourceOffset":18793},{"kind":"section","title":"Numerical rank and tangential limits","sourceOffset":23742},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The limiting line along a divisor","sourceOffset":27115},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A local rank principle","sourceOffset":30832},{"kind":"section","title":"Rank loss on the boundary and the ramification divisor","sourceOffset":35949}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Ordinary Iitaka subadditivity","label":"thm:ordinary","text":"Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, and let $f:X\\to Z$ be a surjective projective morphism with connected fibres between smooth connected projective $k$-varieties. If $F$ is its geometric generic fibre, then \\[ \\kappa(X)\\geq\\kappa(F)+\\kappa(Z). \\]","sourceOffset":2897,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Projective adjoint positivity","label":"thm:adjoint","text":"Let $Y$ be a smooth connected projective complex variety, let $U\\subset Y$ be a dense Zariski open subset, and let $\\mathbb V$ be a rationally polarized integral pure variation of Hodge structure on $U$ whose entire highest nonzero Hodge filtration step is a line. Let $M\\in\\operatorname{Pic}(Y)\\otimes\\Q$. Assume that, after a smooth projective birational modification of $Y$ if necessary, there are a smooth projective alteration $\\tau:\\widehat Y\\to Y$ and a rational number $c>0$ such that the pulled-back variation has unipotent monodromy along a simple-normal-crossing boundary and \\[ \\tau^*M\\sim_{\\Q}cJ_{\\widehat Y}, \\] where $J_{\\widehat Y}$ is its extended highest line. After further smooth projective birational modification, there are a surjective morphism $p:Y\\to S$ with connected fibres onto a smooth projective variety and a nef rational line $L$ on $S$ such that \\[ M\\sim_{\\Q}p^*L,\\qquad K_S+jL\\text{ is big for all sufficiently large integers }j. \\] If $S$ is a point, the conclusion is $M\\sim_{\\Q}0$.","sourceOffset":4651,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/033.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":150912,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Projective-Hodge-lines-and-ordinary-Iitaka-subadditivity-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"033-05","slug":"B-semiampleness-for-compact-log-smooth-Kahler-fibrations-September-10-2026","familyId":"033","title":"B-semiampleness for compact log-smooth Kähler fibrations","abstract":"We prove the compact log-smooth Kähler case of b-semiampleness. Let $f:Y\\to X$ be a surjective holomorphic map with connected fibers between smooth compact connected Kähler manifolds, and let Δ be an effective rational divisor with simple normal crossing support and coefficients in $[0,1]$, with $K_Y+\\Delta\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}f^*L$ for $L\\in\\mathop{\\mathrm{Pic}}\\nolimits (X)_{\\mathbb Q}$. There is a smooth compact Kähler modification $S\\to X$ for which the threshold-moduli line satisfies $M_{S_1}=\\nu^*M_S$ in $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Pic}}\\nolimits (S_1)_{\\mathbb Q}$ for every smooth compact Kähler modification $\\nu:S_1\\to S$, and some positive multiple of MS is represented by a holomorphic line bundle generated by global sections. Horizontal components of coefficient one are allowed; neither projectivity nor a Campana orbifold Iitaka hypothesis is assumed.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Campana's orbifold Iitaka subadditivity conjecture for smooth Fujiki-class-$\\mathcal C$ manifolds with rational simple-normal-crossing boundaries. For projective fibrations $f:U\\to V$ of smooth complex quasi-projective varieties with connected fibers, general fiber F, and $\\bar\\kappa(V)\\ge0$, proves Popa's inequality $\\bar\\kappa(U)\\ge\\kappa(F)+\\max\\{\\bar\\kappa(V),\\mathop{\\mathrm{Var}}\\nolimits (f)\\}$, where variation measures the whole geometric generic fiber.","pdfPath":"preprints/B-semiampleness-for-compact-log-smooth-Kahler-fibrations-September-10-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/B-semiampleness-for-compact-log-smooth-Kahler-fibrations-September-10-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/B-semiampleness-for-compact-log-smooth-Kahler-fibrations-September-10-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/B-semiampleness-for-compact-log-smooth-Kahler-fibrations-September-10-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/B-semiampleness-for-compact-log-smooth-Kahler-fibrations-September-10-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/B-semiampleness-for-compact-log-smooth-Kahler-fibrations-September-10-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/B-semiampleness-for-compact-log-smooth-Kahler-fibrations-September-10-2026/build/sections/hodge.tex","preprints/B-semiampleness-for-compact-log-smooth-Kahler-fibrations-September-10-2026/build/sections/orders.tex","preprints/B-semiampleness-for-compact-log-smooth-Kahler-fibrations-September-10-2026/build/sections/spreading.tex","preprints/B-semiampleness-for-compact-log-smooth-Kahler-fibrations-September-10-2026/build/sections/comparison.tex","preprints/B-semiampleness-for-compact-log-smooth-Kahler-fibrations-September-10-2026/build/sections/periods.tex","preprints/B-semiampleness-for-compact-log-smooth-Kahler-fibrations-September-10-2026/build/sections/descent.tex","preprints/B-semiampleness-for-compact-log-smooth-Kahler-fibrations-September-10-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/B-semiampleness-for-compact-log-smooth-Kahler-fibrations-September-10-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the compact log-smooth K\\\"ahler case of b-semiampleness. Let $f:Y\\to X$ be a surjective holomorphic map with connected fibers between smooth compact connected K\\\"ahler manifolds, and let $\\Delta$ be an effective rational divisor with simple normal crossing support and coefficients in $[0,1]$, with $K_Y+\\Delta\\sim_{\\Q}f^*L$ for $L\\in\\Pic(X)_{\\Q}$. There is a smooth compact K\\\"ahler modification $S\\to X$ for which the threshold-moduli line satisfies $M_{S_1}=\\nu^*M_S$ in $\\Pic(S_1)_{\\Q}$ for every smooth compact K\\\"ahler modification $\\nu:S_1\\to S$, and some positive multiple of $M_S$ is represented by a holomorphic line bundle generated by global sections. Horizontal components of coefficient one are allowed; neither projectivity nor a Campana orbifold Iitaka hypothesis is assumed.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":888},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Statement","sourceOffset":2190},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof and technical contributions","sourceOffset":5449},{"kind":"section","title":"Analytic models and plurivolumes","sourceOffset":8260},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Discrepancies and the rank condition","sourceOffset":8322},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model constructions","sourceOffset":11100},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The prepared family","sourceOffset":13652},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Degenerate dimensions","sourceOffset":15909},{"kind":"section","title":"The root eigenline and its deepest residue","sourceOffset":16928},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A local root construction","sourceOffset":17373},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Extension facts and the mixed top step","sourceOffset":21973},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The pure line detected by residues","sourceOffset":29399}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Under the assumptions above, \\(\\mathbf M\\) is b-semiample. More explicitly, there is a smooth compact K\\\"ahler modification \\(\\mu_0:S\\to X\\) such that: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\alph*)}] \\item for every smooth compact K\\\"ahler modification \\(\\nu:S_1\\to S\\), \\[ M_{S_1}=\\nu^*M_S\\quad\\text{in }\\Pic(S_1)_{\\Q}; \\] \\item for some positive integer \\(m\\), the actual line bundle representing \\(mM_S\\) is generated by its global holomorphic sections. \\end{enumerate} The theorem includes a point base and relative dimension zero. No projectivity of \\(f\\), \\(X\\), or \\(Y\\) is assumed.","sourceOffset":4480,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Root eigenline and deepest residue","label":"hodge:residue","text":"Let \\(k\\) be the maximal number of horizontal coefficient-one components meeting in a good fiber, constant after shrinking \\(U\\). For the local root family above: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item \\[ F^d\\mathbb V_\\chi=\\cO_{V^\\circ}\\tau. \\] This line lies in the single pure weight grade \\(\\Gr^W_{d+k}\\mathbb V_\\chi\\). \\item Choose a depth-\\(k\\) intersection component downstairs dominating the good open. After a good-open base change selecting one connected component \\(A_x^*\\) of its fibers, take every root-cover sheet above that component. Iterated residue identifies the line in \\textup{(i)}, through its pure grade and with the Tate twist removed, with the highest eigenline of the resolved root cover of the induced klt volume on \\(A_x^*\\). In degree \\(m\\) this is the specified plurivolume identification from SNC adjunction. \\item These highest-line identifications preserve generators in canonical extensions on unipotent disks. In a disk model satisfying the hypotheses of \\eqref{hodge:FF}, its extended mixed top line projects isomorphically to the same pure line. \\end{enumerate} If \\(k=0\\), the intersection is the whole original fiber, there is no removed divisor, and the pure root-cover cohomology itself provides the line.","sourceOffset":30270,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/033.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":135091,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/B-semiampleness-for-compact-log-smooth-Kahler-fibrations-September-10-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"034-01","slug":"Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026","familyId":"034","title":"Log abundance for compact Kähler spaces under logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity","abstract":"Assume logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity for surjective morphisms with connected fibers between smooth projective complex varieties with compatible reduced simple normal crossing boundaries. We prove log abundance for normal irreducible compact Kähler spaces in every dimension: for a log canonical pair $(X,\\Delta)$ with effective rational boundary and $K_X+\\Delta$ ℚ-Cartier, analytic nefness of $K_X+\\Delta$ implies semiampleness.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Using logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity, proves log abundance in every dimension for normal compact Kähler log canonical pairs with effective rational boundary: an analytically nef ℚ-Cartier adjoint is semiample. It also proves projective log abundance over every algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and the effective Iitaka fibration conjecture for smooth projective varieties of nonnegative Kodaira dimension in that setting. A further result resolves the finite-rational-coefficient index conjecture for connected projective semi-log-canonical log Calabi–Yau pairs over such fields in each fixed dimension and for each fixed finite set of rational boundary coefficients, with a uniform index independent of the number of components.","pdfPath":"preprints/Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026/build/sections/02-inductive-reduction.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026/build/sections/03-programs.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026/build/sections/03a-program-foundations.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026/build/sections/03b-projective-good-model.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026/build/sections/03c-restricted-induction.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026/build/sections/03c-base-presentations.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026/build/sections/04-boundary.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026/build/sections/05-rank-one.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026/build/sections/05-projective-program-input.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026/build/sections/06-simple-nonvanishing.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026/build/figures/two-diagonals.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026/build/sections/07-signed-rigidity.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Assume logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity for surjective morphisms with connected fibers between smooth projective complex varieties with compatible reduced simple normal crossing boundaries. We prove log abundance for normal irreducible compact Kähler spaces in every dimension: for a log canonical pair \\((X,\\Delta)\\) with effective rational boundary and \\(K_X+\\Delta\\) \\(\\Q\\)-Cartier, analytic nefness of \\(K_X+\\Delta\\) implies semiampleness.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":509},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The assumption and the theorem","sourceOffset":1466},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and relation to earlier work","sourceOffset":3961},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its main constructions","sourceOffset":7752},{"kind":"section","title":"Birational decompositions and the geometric reduction","sourceOffset":13756},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The inductive decomposition and its negative part","sourceOffset":14138},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Birational transfer of the decomposition","sourceOffset":25651},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The projective anchor and the two geometric cases","sourceOffset":33703},{"kind":"section","title":"Programs with a fixed nef part and special termination","sourceOffset":40632},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Program inputs and descent of actual lines","sourceOffset":41873},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Relative klt programs and small models","sourceOffset":55520},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Scaling toward zero and keeping a nef rational line","sourceOffset":68852}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Log abundance for compact Kähler spaces","label":"thm:main","text":"Assume Assumption \\ref{asm:log-iitaka}. Let \\(X\\) be a normal irreducible compact Kähler complex analytic space, and let \\(\\Delta\\geq0\\) be a rational divisor such that \\((X,\\Delta)\\) is log canonical and \\(K_X+\\Delta\\) is rational Cartier. If \\(K_X+\\Delta\\) is analytically nef, then it is semiample. Explicitly, there is an integer \\(m>0\\) such that \\(m(K_X+\\Delta)\\) is Cartier and \\[ H^0\\bigl(X,\\OO_X(m(K_X+\\Delta))\\bigr)\\otimes_{\\C}\\OO_X \\longrightarrow \\OO_X(m(K_X+\\Delta)) \\] is surjective at every point of \\(X\\).","sourceOffset":3026,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Resolution and descent","label":"bir:resolution-transfer","text":"Let \\((X,\\Delta)\\) be a normal compact Kähler lc pair with rational boundary and rational Cartier adjoint \\(J\\). Let \\(p:Y\\to X\\) be a log resolution, and put \\[ B_Y=p_*^{-1}\\Delta+\\sum_{E\\text{ exceptional}}E. \\] If \\(p^*J\\) is pseudo-effective and the conclusion of Definition \\ref{def:good-decomposition} holds for \\((Y,B_Y)\\), then there is a smooth compact Kähler modification \\(r:V\\to X\\) with an actual rational line identity \\[ r^*J\\sim_{\\Q}P+R,\\qquad P\\text{ semiample},\\qquad R=N(r^*J)\\geq0, \\] where \\(R\\) is a rational divisor. If \\(J\\) is analytically nef, it is semiample on \\(X\\).","sourceOffset":26376,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"assumption","name":"Logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity","label":"asm:log-iitaka","text":"Let \\(f:X\\to Y\\) be a surjective morphism with connected fibers between smooth connected projective complex varieties. Let \\(D_X\\) and \\(D_Y\\) be reduced effective simple normal crossing divisors, allowing zero divisors, such that \\[ \\Supp(f^*D_Y)\\subseteq\\Supp(D_X). \\] For a very general smooth fiber \\(F\\), put \\(D_F=D_X|_F\\). Then \\[ \\kappa(X,K_X+D_X)\\geq \\kappa(F,K_F+D_F)+\\kappa(Y,K_Y+D_Y). \\] Here \\(D_F\\) is reduced SNC and \\(K_F+D_F\\sim (K_X+D_X)|_F\\). The Iitaka dimension is \\(-\\infty\\) when all positive integral systems are empty, with \\((-\\infty)+b=-\\infty\\) also for \\(b=-\\infty\\); the","sourceOffset":2277,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:good-decomposition","text":"For \\(n\\geq0\\), let \\(\\Ggood_n\\) be the following assertion. If \\(X\\) is a connected smooth compact Kähler manifold of dimension \\(n\\), \\(B\\) is a rational simple normal crossing boundary with coefficients in \\([0,1]\\), and \\(J=K_X+B\\) is pseudo-effective, then there is a smooth compact Kähler modification \\(\\mu:Y\\to X\\) and an actual rational line identity \\begin{equation} \\mu^*J\\sim_{\\Q}P+R, \\qquad P\\text{ semiample},\\qquad R=N(\\mu^*J)\\geq0, \\end{equation} where \\(R\\) is a rational divisor.","sourceOffset":15868,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":573305,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":40,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Log-abundance-for-compact-Kahler-spaces-under-logarithmic-Iitaka-subadditivity-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"034-02","slug":"Uniform-indices-for-semi-log-canonical-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-October-5-2026","familyId":"034","title":"Uniform indices for semi-log-canonical log Calabi–Yau pairs","abstract":"We prove a uniform index theorem for connected projective semi-log-canonical log Calabi–Yau pairs in every fixed dimension at least four over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero. For boundary coefficients in a fixed finite rational set, a single multiple of the log canonical divisor is Cartier and linearly trivial. The multiple depends only on the dimension and coefficient set, not on the number of irreducible components. Together with the established theorem in dimensions at most three, this resolves the finite-rational-coefficient semi-log-canonical index conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Using logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity, proves log abundance in every dimension for normal compact Kähler log canonical pairs with effective rational boundary: an analytically nef ℚ-Cartier adjoint is semiample. It also proves projective log abundance over every algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and the effective Iitaka fibration conjecture for smooth projective varieties of nonnegative Kodaira dimension in that setting. A further result resolves the finite-rational-coefficient index conjecture for connected projective semi-log-canonical log Calabi–Yau pairs over such fields in each fixed dimension and for each fixed finite set of rational boundary coefficients, with a uniform index independent of the number of components.","pdfPath":"preprints/Uniform-indices-for-semi-log-canonical-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-October-5-2026/uniform-slc-index.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-indices-for-semi-log-canonical-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-October-5-2026/uniform-slc-index.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-indices-for-semi-log-canonical-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-October-5-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Uniform-indices-for-semi-log-canonical-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Uniform-indices-for-semi-log-canonical-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Uniform-indices-for-semi-log-canonical-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-October-5-2026/build/research/history.tex","preprints/Uniform-indices-for-semi-log-canonical-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-October-5-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Uniform-indices-for-semi-log-canonical-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-October-5-2026/build/sections/hodge.tex","preprints/Uniform-indices-for-semi-log-canonical-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-October-5-2026/build/sections/diagonal.tex","preprints/Uniform-indices-for-semi-log-canonical-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-October-5-2026/build/sections/rank.tex","preprints/Uniform-indices-for-semi-log-canonical-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-October-5-2026/build/sections/rank-calibration.tex","preprints/Uniform-indices-for-semi-log-canonical-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-October-5-2026/build/sections/rank-count.tex","preprints/Uniform-indices-for-semi-log-canonical-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-October-5-2026/build/sections/characters.tex","preprints/Uniform-indices-for-semi-log-canonical-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-October-5-2026/build/sections/residues.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Uniform-indices-for-semi-log-canonical-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a uniform index theorem for connected projective semi-log-canonical log Calabi--Yau pairs in every fixed dimension at least four over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero. For boundary coefficients in a fixed finite rational set, a single multiple of the log canonical divisor is Cartier and linearly trivial. The multiple depends only on the dimension and coefficient set, not on the number of irreducible components. Together with the established theorem in dimensions at most three, this resolves the finite-rational-coefficient semi-log-canonical index conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":692},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and relation to earlier work","sourceOffset":3081},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The character obstruction and the proof","sourceOffset":8391},{"kind":"section","title":"Forms, birational maps, and uniform inputs","sourceOffset":12452},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Trivializations as rational forms","sourceOffset":13094},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The normal index and good-model inputs","sourceOffset":15391},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bounded comparison models for rationally connected bases","sourceOffset":17141},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Generic data and section orders","sourceOffset":19577},{"kind":"section","title":"The Hodge structure generated by the top forms","sourceOffset":20828},{"kind":"section","title":"A diagonal estimate for the Hodge rank","sourceOffset":27494},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Diagonal conditions and the number of sections","sourceOffset":29775},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removing base components that dominate both factors","sourceOffset":36470}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $d\\ge4$ and every finite set $\\Phi\\subset[0,1]\\cap\\Q$, there is an integer $a(d,\\Phi)>0$ with the following property. Let $(X,B)$ be a connected projective equidimensional semi-log-canonical log Calabi--Yau pair of dimension $d$ over $k$, with every nonzero coefficient of $B$ in $\\Phi$. Then \\[ a(d,\\Phi)(K_X+B)\\text{ is Cartier}, \\qquad \\OO_X\\bigl(a(d,\\Phi)(K_X+B)\\bigr)\\simeq\\OO_X. \\]","sourceOffset":2143,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Normal index theorem {\\citep[Theorem 1.2","label":"input:normal-index","text":"{UPI}}] For every integer $d\\ge0$ and every rational set $I\\subset[0,1]$ satisfying the descending chain condition, there is an integer $a_{\\mathrm{lc}}(d,I)>0$ such that every normal projective integral lc pair $(X,\\Delta)$ of dimension $d$, with coefficients in $I$ and $K_X+\\Delta\\sim_{\\Q}0$, satisfies \\[ a_{\\mathrm{lc}}(d,I)(K_X+\\Delta)\\sim0. \\]","sourceOffset":15625,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":138190,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Uniform-indices-for-semi-log-canonical-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Uniform-indices-for-semi-log-canonical-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Uniform-indices-for-semi-log-canonical-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"034-03","slug":"Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026","familyId":"034","title":"Conditional good minimal models for compact Kähler fourfolds","abstract":"Assuming orbifold Iitaka subadditivity and abundance for nef fourfold adjoints of nonnegative Kodaira dimension, we prove the existence of good minimal models for globally strongly ℚ-factorial compact Kähler klt fourfold pairs with effective rational boundary and analytically pseudo-effective actual ℚ-Cartier adjoint. The ordinary fourfold minimal model program supplies the nef endpoint. We prove nonvanishing by fibration arguments and, in algebraic dimension zero, by singular metrics, holomorphic foliations, and extension from a reduced boundary. The projective abundance argument used in the proof is included in full.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Using logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity, proves log abundance in every dimension for normal compact Kähler log canonical pairs with effective rational boundary: an analytically nef ℚ-Cartier adjoint is semiample. It also proves projective log abundance over every algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and the effective Iitaka fibration conjecture for smooth projective varieties of nonnegative Kodaira dimension in that setting. A further result resolves the finite-rational-coefficient index conjecture for connected projective semi-log-canonical log Calabi–Yau pairs over such fields in each fixed dimension and for each fixed finite set of rational boundary coefficients, with a uniform index independent of the number of components.","pdfPath":"preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/figures/proof_map.tex","preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/premises.tex","preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/reductions.tex","preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/positive_dimension.tex","preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/minimal_metric.tex","preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/empty_divisor.tex","preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/birational.tex","preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/floor.tex","preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/completion.tex","preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/appendices/projective_introduction.tex","preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/appendices/projective_induction.tex","preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/appendices/projective_signed_geometry.tex","preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/appendices/projective_hodge.tex","preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/appendices/projective_signed_descent.tex","preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/appendices/projective_exclusions.tex","preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/appendices/projective_jets.tex","preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/appendices/projective_frobenius.tex","preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/appendices/projective_completion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Assuming orbifold Iitaka subadditivity and abundance for nef fourfold adjoints of nonnegative Kodaira dimension, we prove the existence of good minimal models for globally strongly \\(\\Q\\)-factorial compact K\\\"ahler klt fourfold pairs with effective rational boundary and analytically pseudo-effective actual \\(\\Q\\)-Cartier adjoint. The ordinary fourfold minimal model program supplies the nef endpoint. We prove nonvanishing by fibration arguments and, in algebraic dimension zero, by singular metrics, holomorphic foliations, and extension from a reduced boundary. The projective abundance argument used in the proof is included in full.","sections":[{"kind":"part","title":"Compact K\\\"ahler fourfolds","sourceOffset":709},{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":744},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Structure of the proof","sourceOffset":4758},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and the three inputs","sourceOffset":11013},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Actual adjoints and positivity","sourceOffset":11646},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Orbifold subadditivity","sourceOffset":14729},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The pseudo-effective fourfold program","sourceOffset":18974},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Abundance after nonvanishing","sourceOffset":22676},{"kind":"section","title":"Reductions to canonical nonvanishing","sourceOffset":23836},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The projective case","sourceOffset":24279},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Lower-dimensional inputs and restriction to fibers","sourceOffset":29928},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The uniruled and irregular cases","sourceOffset":32664}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Conditional good minimal models","label":"thm:main","text":"Assume Assumptions \\ref{hyp:campana}, \\ref{hyp:mmp}, and \\ref{hyp:effective-abundance}. Let \\(X\\) be a normal connected compact K\\\"ahler fourfold, and let \\(B\\) be an effective rational Weil divisor. Suppose that \\((X,B)\\) is klt, that its actual adjoint \\(D=K_X+B\\) is \\(\\Q\\)-Cartier and analytically pseudo-effective, and that \\(X\\) is globally strongly \\(\\Q\\)-factorial in the sense of Definition \\ref{def:strong}. Then there are a normal connected globally strongly \\(\\Q\\)-factorial compact K\\\"ahler fourfold \\(Y\\) and a bimeromorphic map \\(\\phi:X\\dashrightarrow Y\\) such that: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item \\(\\phi\\) extracts no prime divisor, and \\(B_Y=\\phi_*B\\); \\item \\((Y,B_Y)\\) is klt and its actual adjoint \\(D_Y=K_Y+B_Y\\) is \\(\\Q\\)-Cartier and analytically nef; \\item \\(a(E;Y,B_Y)\\ge a(E;X,B)\\) for every prime divisor over the models, with strict inequality for each prime on \\(X\\) contracted by \\(\\phi\\); \\item for some \\(m>0\\), the Cartier line bundle \\(\\OO_Y(mD_Y)\\) is globally generated. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":2566,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Conditional log abundance","label":"proj:thm:main","text":"Assume Assumption \\ref{proj:asm:iitaka}. Let \\(k\\) be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, and let \\((X,B)\\) be a normal projective log canonical pair over \\(k\\), where \\(B\\) is an effective \\(\\Q\\)-divisor and \\(K_X+B\\) is \\(\\Q\\)-Cartier. If \\(K_X+B\\) is nef, then it is semiample: for some integer \\(m>0\\), the divisor \\(m(K_X+B)\\) is Cartier and \\(\\OO_X(m(K_X+B))\\) is generated by its global sections.","sourceOffset":315559,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Global strong \\(\\Q\\)-factoriality","label":"def:strong","text":"A normal compact space \\(X\\) is globally strongly \\(\\Q\\)-factorial if every coherent rank-one reflexive sheaf \\(\\cF\\) on the whole \\(X\\) has an invertible positive reflexive power \\(\\cF^{[m]}=(\\cF^{\\otimes m})^{**}\\).","sourceOffset":14113,"truncated":false},{"kind":"assumption","name":"Full orbifold Iitaka subadditivity","label":"hyp:campana","text":"Let \\(X\\) be a smooth compact connected manifold in Fujiki class \\(\\mathcal C\\), let \\(\\Delta\\) be an effective rational SNC boundary with coefficients in \\([0,1]\\), and let \\(f:X\\to Y\\) be a surjective holomorphic map with connected fibers onto a normal compact irreducible complex space. In arbitrary dimensions, \\begin{equation} \\kappa(X,K_X+\\Delta)\\ge \\kappa(F,K_F+\\Delta|_F)+\\kappa(f\\mid\\Delta), \\end{equation} where \\(F\\) is a very general smooth fiber with SNC boundary restriction, and the invariant base is defined by \\eqref{eq:orbifold-base}--\\eqref{eq:neat-formula}.","sourceOffset":17634,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":492229,"sourceFileCount":21,"statementCount":34,"definitionCount":7,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Conditional-good-minimal-models-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"034-04","slug":"Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026","familyId":"034","title":"Log abundance in characteristic zero","abstract":"We prove the rational-boundary log abundance conjecture in every dimension over algebraically closed fields of characteristic zero: every nef ℚ-Cartier log canonical divisor on a projective log canonical pair with effective rational boundary is semiample. Over ℂ, the proof also establishes canonical nonvanishing for smooth projective varieties in every dimension.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Using logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity, proves log abundance in every dimension for normal compact Kähler log canonical pairs with effective rational boundary: an analytically nef ℚ-Cartier adjoint is semiample. It also proves projective log abundance over every algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and the effective Iitaka fibration conjecture for smooth projective varieties of nonnegative Kodaira dimension in that setting. A further result resolves the finite-rational-coefficient index conjecture for connected projective semi-log-canonical log Calabi–Yau pairs over such fields in each fixed dimension and for each fixed finite set of rational boundary coefficients, with a uniform index independent of the number of components.","pdfPath":"preprints/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026/build/sections/induction.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026/build/sections/signed_geometry.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026/build/sections/hodge.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026/build/sections/signed_descent.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026/build/sections/exclusions.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026/build/sections/common-tools.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026/build/sections/jets.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026/build/sections/frobenius.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026/build/sections/completion.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026/build/sections/canonical-rings.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026/build/sections/classification.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026/build/sections/analytic-abundance.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the rational-boundary log abundance conjecture in every dimension over algebraically closed fields of characteristic zero: every nef \\(\\Q\\)-Cartier log canonical divisor on a projective log canonical pair with effective rational boundary is semiample. Over \\(\\C\\), the proof also establishes canonical nonvanishing for smooth projective varieties in every dimension.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":438},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical development","sourceOffset":3586},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two tasks in the induction","sourceOffset":6730},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The subadditivity input","sourceOffset":15453},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":19039},{"kind":"section","title":"The inductive framework","sourceOffset":20134},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison and boundary restrictions","sourceOffset":23206},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A uniform trivial-perturbation argument","sourceOffset":27159},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The good-model implication","sourceOffset":29558},{"kind":"section","title":"Geometry of a signed boundary representative","sourceOffset":39454},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The positive boundary and its small intersections","sourceOffset":41192},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The divisor and adjunction data for lifting","sourceOffset":45236}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Log abundance","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $(X,B)$ be a normal projective log canonical pair over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero. Assume that $B$ is an effective rational divisor and $K_X+B$ is $\\Q$-Cartier. If $K_X+B$ is nef, then some positive Cartier multiple of it is generated by global sections.","sourceOffset":1074,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity, {\\cite[Corollary 6.2","label":"asm:iitaka","text":"{OpenAIIitaka2026}}] Let \\(f:X\\to Y\\) be a surjective morphism with connected fibers between smooth connected projective complex varieties. Let \\(D_X,D_Y\\) be reduced effective simple normal crossing divisors, allowing zero boundaries, such that \\[ \\Supp(f^*D_Y)\\subseteq\\Supp(D_X). \\] For a very general smooth fiber \\(F\\), put \\(D_F=D_X|_F\\). Then \\[ \\kappa(X,K_X+D_X)\\ge \\kappa(F,K_F+D_F)+\\kappa(Y,K_Y+D_Y). \\] Here \\(D_F\\) is reduced with simple normal crossings and \\(K_F+D_F\\sim(K_X+D_X)|_F\\). The convention \\((-\\infty)+b=-\\infty\\) applies also when \\(b=-\\infty\\), and the zero divisor on a point has Iitaka dimension zero.","sourceOffset":15628,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"assumption","name":"Lower-dimensional good models","label":"mmp:lower-models","text":"Every projective log canonical pair of dimension less than \\(n\\), with real boundary and pseudo-effective adjoint divisor, has a good log minimal model.","sourceOffset":21123,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":300928,"sourceFileCount":16,"statementCount":32,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Log-abundance-in-characteristic-zero-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"034-05","slug":"Minimal-metrics-and-interior-injectivity-for-nef-adjoints-September-27-2026","familyId":"034","title":"Minimal metrics and interior injectivity for nef adjoints","abstract":"Let $(H,\\Theta)$ be a projective complex klt pair with effective rational boundary and nef ℚ-Cartier adjoint. On any projective log resolution, minimal semipositive metrics on the pulled-back adjoint exist and have zero Lelong numbers everywhere. On smooth projective complex varieties, we also prove an H1-injectivity theorem for rational interior boundaries with simple-normal-crossing support when both endpoint bundles carry zero-Lelong semipositive metrics.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Using logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity, proves log abundance in every dimension for normal compact Kähler log canonical pairs with effective rational boundary: an analytically nef ℚ-Cartier adjoint is semiample. It also proves projective log abundance over every algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and the effective Iitaka fibration conjecture for smooth projective varieties of nonnegative Kodaira dimension in that setting. A further result resolves the finite-rational-coefficient index conjecture for connected projective semi-log-canonical log Calabi–Yau pairs over such fields in each fixed dimension and for each fixed finite set of rational boundary coefficients, with a uniform index independent of the number of components.","pdfPath":"preprints/Minimal-metrics-and-interior-injectivity-for-nef-adjoints-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Minimal-metrics-and-interior-injectivity-for-nef-adjoints-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Minimal-metrics-and-interior-injectivity-for-nef-adjoints-September-27-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Minimal-metrics-and-interior-injectivity-for-nef-adjoints-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Minimal-metrics-and-interior-injectivity-for-nef-adjoints-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Minimal-metrics-and-interior-injectivity-for-nef-adjoints-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Minimal-metrics-and-interior-injectivity-for-nef-adjoints-September-27-2026/build/sections/adjoint-metrics.tex","preprints/Minimal-metrics-and-interior-injectivity-for-nef-adjoints-September-27-2026/build/sections/interior-injectivity.tex","preprints/Minimal-metrics-and-interior-injectivity-for-nef-adjoints-September-27-2026/build/sections/euler-route.tex","preprints/Minimal-metrics-and-interior-injectivity-for-nef-adjoints-September-27-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Minimal-metrics-and-interior-injectivity-for-nef-adjoints-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let \\((H,\\Theta)\\) be a projective complex klt pair with effective rational boundary and nef \\(\\Q\\)-Cartier adjoint. On any projective log resolution, minimal semipositive metrics on the pulled-back adjoint exist and have zero Lelong numbers everywhere. On smooth projective complex varieties, we also prove an \\(H^1\\)-injectivity theorem for rational interior boundaries with simple-normal-crossing support when both endpoint bundles carry zero-Lelong semipositive metrics.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":562},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Interior boundaries and restriction of sections","sourceOffset":4035},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two analytic arguments","sourceOffset":6787},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A fourfold application and organization","sourceOffset":10608},{"kind":"section","title":"Zero Lelong numbers for nef klt adjoints","sourceOffset":12487},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weights and the complete \\texorpdfstring{$L^2$}{L2} estimate","sourceOffset":13029},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The fixed twist and normalization","sourceOffset":16478},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A singular weighted equation with a fixed constant","sourceOffset":22280},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Extension, evaluation, and the extremal contradiction","sourceOffset":29519},{"kind":"section","title":"An interior injectivity theorem","sourceOffset":35013},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Endpoint regularization and the comparison weights","sourceOffset":36590},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A complete metric with bounded local potentials","sourceOffset":42469}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:adjoint-metric","text":"Let $(H,\\Theta)$ be a normal connected projective complex klt pair, with $\\Theta$ an effective rational divisor. Suppose that $D_H=K_H+\\Theta$ is a nef $\\Q$-Cartier divisor. For every projective log resolution $\\pi:V\\to H$, the rational line bundle $N=\\pi^*D_H$ has the following property: every semipositive singular Hermitian metric with minimal singularities on $N$ has Lelong number zero at every point of $V$. Such a metric exists. Neither $K_H$ nor $\\Theta$ is required to be separately $\\Q$-Cartier.","sourceOffset":2051,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:interior-injectivity","text":"Let $V$ be a smooth projective complex variety, let $L$ be an integral divisor, and let $0\\le C_0\\le C_2$ be effective rational divisors whose combined support is simple normal crossing. Suppose that both rational line bundles $L-C_0$ and $L-C_2$ admit singular Hermitian metrics of semipositive curvature with zero Lelong numbers at every point. For a rational number $0<\\lambda<1$, set \\[ C_1=(1-\\lambda)C_0+\\lambda C_2,\\qquad L_i=L-\\floor{C_i}\\quad(i=0,1). \\] Then the natural inclusion of line bundles induces an injection \\[ H^1\\bigl(V,\\cO_V(K_V+L_1)\\bigr) \\longrightarrow H^1\\bigl(V,\\cO_V(K_V+L_0)\\bigr). \\]","sourceOffset":4412,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":73764,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Minimal-metrics-and-interior-injectivity-for-nef-adjoints-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"034-06","slug":"Fourfold-nonvanishing-by-minimal-metrics-and-moving-jets-September-27-2026","familyId":"034","title":"Fourfold nonvanishing by minimal metrics and moving jets","abstract":"We prove canonical nonvanishing for smooth connected projective complex fourfolds: if KX is pseudo-effective, then $H^0(X,mK_X)\\ne0$ for some positive integer m.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Using logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity, proves log abundance in every dimension for normal compact Kähler log canonical pairs with effective rational boundary: an analytically nef ℚ-Cartier adjoint is semiample. It also proves projective log abundance over every algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and the effective Iitaka fibration conjecture for smooth projective varieties of nonnegative Kodaira dimension in that setting. A further result resolves the finite-rational-coefficient index conjecture for connected projective semi-log-canonical log Calabi–Yau pairs over such fields in each fixed dimension and for each fixed finite set of rational boundary coefficients, with a uniform index independent of the number of components.","pdfPath":"preprints/Fourfold-nonvanishing-by-minimal-metrics-and-moving-jets-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Fourfold-nonvanishing-by-minimal-metrics-and-moving-jets-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Fourfold-nonvanishing-by-minimal-metrics-and-moving-jets-September-27-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Fourfold-nonvanishing-by-minimal-metrics-and-moving-jets-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Fourfold-nonvanishing-by-minimal-metrics-and-moving-jets-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Fourfold-nonvanishing-by-minimal-metrics-and-moving-jets-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Fourfold-nonvanishing-by-minimal-metrics-and-moving-jets-September-27-2026/build/sections/reduction.tex","preprints/Fourfold-nonvanishing-by-minimal-metrics-and-moving-jets-September-27-2026/build/sections/moving-centers.tex","preprints/Fourfold-nonvanishing-by-minimal-metrics-and-moving-jets-September-27-2026/build/sections/analytic-inputs.tex","preprints/Fourfold-nonvanishing-by-minimal-metrics-and-moving-jets-September-27-2026/build/sections/signed.tex","preprints/Fourfold-nonvanishing-by-minimal-metrics-and-moving-jets-September-27-2026/build/sections/two-slot.tex","preprints/Fourfold-nonvanishing-by-minimal-metrics-and-moving-jets-September-27-2026/build/sections/finite-inputs.tex","preprints/Fourfold-nonvanishing-by-minimal-metrics-and-moving-jets-September-27-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex","preprints/Fourfold-nonvanishing-by-minimal-metrics-and-moving-jets-September-27-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Fourfold-nonvanishing-by-minimal-metrics-and-moving-jets-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove canonical nonvanishing for smooth connected projective complex fourfolds: if \\(K_X\\) is pseudo-effective, then \\(H^0(X,mK_X)\\ne0\\) for some positive integer \\(m\\).","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":297},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Geometry of a possible counterexample","sourceOffset":5448},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From jets to a rank contradiction","sourceOffset":10784},{"kind":"section","title":"A fixed minimal model of full nef dimension","sourceOffset":14756},{"kind":"section","title":"Moving centers and scalar vanishing","sourceOffset":22566},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Numerical subadjunction at a generic lc center","sourceOffset":24709},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Degree and canonical bounds for a base component","sourceOffset":26467},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Differentiating the full moving jet kernel","sourceOffset":28240},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A uniform obstruction from logarithmic general type","sourceOffset":34688},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The scalar order bound","sourceOffset":38551},{"kind":"section","title":"The analytic inputs and the lifting problem","sourceOffset":42498},{"kind":"section","title":"Signed representatives and logarithmic general type","sourceOffset":46212}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:nonvanishing","text":"Let $X$ be a smooth connected projective complex fourfold. If $K_X$ is pseudo-effective, then $H^0(X,mK_X)\\ne0$ for some positive integer $m$.","sourceOffset":1294,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":null,"text":"\\label[corollary]{cor:lc-nonvanishing} Let $(X,\\Delta)$ be a connected normal projective log canonical complex pair of dimension at most four. Suppose that $\\Delta\\geq0$ is rational and $D=K_X+\\Delta$ is $\\mathbb Q$-Cartier and nef. For every positive integer $r$ such that $rD$ is Cartier, there is a positive integer $m$ with $H^0(X,\\mathcal O_X(mrD))\\ne0$.","sourceOffset":4097,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":131377,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Fourfold-nonvanishing-by-minimal-metrics-and-moving-jets-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"034-07","slug":"Lifting-sections-from-the-reduced-support-of-an-adjoint-September-27-2026","familyId":"034","title":"Lifting sections from the reduced support of an adjoint","abstract":"For a projective ℚ-factorial dlt pair with effective rational boundary over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, we prove that the adjoint has positive Iitaka dimension whenever a nonzero effective Cartier multiple is supported on the coefficient-one boundary and restricts to a semiample line bundle on its whole reduced support. This gives log abundance after nonvanishing in dimension at most four over ℂ.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Using logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity, proves log abundance in every dimension for normal compact Kähler log canonical pairs with effective rational boundary: an analytically nef ℚ-Cartier adjoint is semiample. It also proves projective log abundance over every algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and the effective Iitaka fibration conjecture for smooth projective varieties of nonnegative Kodaira dimension in that setting. A further result resolves the finite-rational-coefficient index conjecture for connected projective semi-log-canonical log Calabi–Yau pairs over such fields in each fixed dimension and for each fixed finite set of rational boundary coefficients, with a uniform index independent of the number of components.","pdfPath":"preprints/Lifting-sections-from-the-reduced-support-of-an-adjoint-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Lifting-sections-from-the-reduced-support-of-an-adjoint-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Lifting-sections-from-the-reduced-support-of-an-adjoint-September-27-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Lifting-sections-from-the-reduced-support-of-an-adjoint-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Lifting-sections-from-the-reduced-support-of-an-adjoint-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Lifting-sections-from-the-reduced-support-of-an-adjoint-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Lifting-sections-from-the-reduced-support-of-an-adjoint-September-27-2026/build/sections/covers.tex","preprints/Lifting-sections-from-the-reduced-support-of-an-adjoint-September-27-2026/build/sections/hodge.tex","preprints/Lifting-sections-from-the-reduced-support-of-an-adjoint-September-27-2026/build/sections/frames.tex","preprints/Lifting-sections-from-the-reduced-support-of-an-adjoint-September-27-2026/build/sections/lifting.tex","preprints/Lifting-sections-from-the-reduced-support-of-an-adjoint-September-27-2026/build/sections/abundance.tex","preprints/Lifting-sections-from-the-reduced-support-of-an-adjoint-September-27-2026/build/sections/fields.tex","preprints/Lifting-sections-from-the-reduced-support-of-an-adjoint-September-27-2026/build/sections/curve-base.tex","preprints/Lifting-sections-from-the-reduced-support-of-an-adjoint-September-27-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Lifting-sections-from-the-reduced-support-of-an-adjoint-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For a projective \\(\\Q\\)-factorial dlt pair with effective rational boundary over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, we prove that the adjoint has positive Iitaka dimension whenever a nonzero effective Cartier multiple is supported on the coefficient-one boundary and restricts to a semiample line bundle on its whole reduced support. This gives log abundance after nonvanishing in dimension at most four over \\(\\C\\).","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":560},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Boundary deformation and extension","sourceOffset":4694},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The obstruction on a finite neighborhood","sourceOffset":7321},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From lifting to abundance and other boundary methods","sourceOffset":11664},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reading the proof","sourceOffset":13317},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and the complex formulation","sourceOffset":13985},{"kind":"section","title":"Moving frames, cyclic covers, and residues","sourceOffset":15784},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The moving frame and the first cover","sourceOffset":17662},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The root of the adjoint tensor","sourceOffset":22546},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The residue summand","sourceOffset":29561},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The obstruction to be detected","sourceOffset":35187},{"kind":"section","title":"A filtered module on the boundary graph","sourceOffset":36632}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Supported boundary lifting","label":"bl:thm:supported","text":"Let $(V,C)$ be a normal projective $\\mathbb Q$-factorial dlt pair over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, with $C$ an effective rational boundary, and put $A=K_V+C$. Suppose that \\[ 0\\ne G\\geq0,\\qquad G\\sim qA,\\qquad \\operatorname{Supp}G\\subseteq\\operatorname{Supp}\\lfloor C\\rfloor, \\] where $G$ is Cartier and $q>0$ is a sufficiently divisible integer. If $\\mathcal O_V(G)|_{G_{\\mathrm{red}}}$ is semiample, then $\\kappa(V,A)>0$.","sourceOffset":1012,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Abundance after nonvanishing","label":"thm:abundance-after-nonvanishing","text":"Let $(X,\\Delta)$ be a projective log canonical pair of dimension at most four over $\\C$, with effective rational boundary. If $D=K_X+\\Delta$ is $\\mathbb Q$-Cartier and nef and $\\kappa(X,D)\\geq0$, then $D$ is semiample. If $\\kappa(X,D)=0$, then $D\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}0$.","sourceOffset":2193,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":152253,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Lifting-sections-from-the-reduced-support-of-an-adjoint-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"034-08","slug":"Schnell-fiber-spaces-and-good-canonical-models-September-24-2026","familyId":"034","title":"Schnell fiber spaces and good canonical models","abstract":"We prove the Campana–Peternell inequality $\\kappa(X)\\geq\\kappa(D)$ for a smooth connected projective complex variety X and an effective Cartier divisor D whenever $m_0K_X-D$ is pseudo-effective for some positive integer m0. For an algebraic fiber space $f\\colon X\\to Y$ between smooth connected projective complex varieties, the hypothesis that $m_0K_X-f^*H$ is pseudo-effective with H ample Cartier gives $\\kappa(X)=\\kappa(F)+\\dim Y$ for a very general smooth fiber F, as well as nonzero sections of $mK_X-f^*H$ for all sufficiently large divisible m.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Using logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity, proves log abundance in every dimension for normal compact Kähler log canonical pairs with effective rational boundary: an analytically nef ℚ-Cartier adjoint is semiample. It also proves projective log abundance over every algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and the effective Iitaka fibration conjecture for smooth projective varieties of nonnegative Kodaira dimension in that setting. A further result resolves the finite-rational-coefficient index conjecture for connected projective semi-log-canonical log Calabi–Yau pairs over such fields in each fixed dimension and for each fixed finite set of rational boundary coefficients, with a uniform index independent of the number of components.","pdfPath":"preprints/Schnell-fiber-spaces-and-good-canonical-models-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Schnell-fiber-spaces-and-good-canonical-models-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Schnell-fiber-spaces-and-good-canonical-models-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Schnell-fiber-spaces-and-good-canonical-models-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Schnell-fiber-spaces-and-good-canonical-models-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Schnell-fiber-spaces-and-good-canonical-models-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-geometric-argument.tex","preprints/Schnell-fiber-spaces-and-good-canonical-models-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-good-model-interface.tex","preprints/Schnell-fiber-spaces-and-good-canonical-models-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-general-consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Schnell-fiber-spaces-and-good-canonical-models-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Campana--Peternell inequality \\(\\kappa(X)\\geq\\kappa(D)\\) for a smooth connected projective complex variety \\(X\\) and an effective Cartier divisor \\(D\\) whenever \\(m_0K_X-D\\) is pseudo-effective for some positive integer \\(m_0\\). For an algebraic fiber space \\(f\\colon X\\to Y\\) between smooth connected projective complex varieties, the hypothesis that \\(m_0K_X-f^*H\\) is pseudo-effective with \\(H\\) ample Cartier gives \\(\\kappa(X)=\\kappa(F)+\\dim Y\\) for a very general smooth fiber \\(F\\), as well as nonzero sections of \\(mK_X-f^*H\\) for all sufficiently large divisible \\(m\\).","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":636},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The problem and previous approaches","sourceOffset":5180},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the numerical condition produces the lower bound","sourceOffset":8448},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":10789},{"kind":"section","title":"The numerical argument on a good minimal model","sourceOffset":11705},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The upper bound from the generic fiber","sourceOffset":12824},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A curve class that detects the base","sourceOffset":14995},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sections and the numerical contradiction","sourceOffset":20123},{"kind":"section","title":"The canonical good model and the main theorem","sourceOffset":23775},{"kind":"section","title":"Campana--Peternell and general Schnell fiber spaces","sourceOffset":25788},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Nonvanishing and very general fibers","sourceOffset":26075},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Campana--Peternell inequality","sourceOffset":28802}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Schnell's zero-Kodaira fiber-space conclusion","label":"thm:schnell","text":"Let $f\\colon X\\to Y$ be a surjective morphism with connected fibers between smooth connected projective complex varieties. Let $F$ be its smooth geometric generic fiber and assume $\\kappa(F)=0$. Suppose that there are an ample Cartier divisor $H$ on $Y$ and a positive integer $m_0$ such that $m_0K_X-f^*H$ is pseudo-effective. Then \\[ \\kappa(X)=\\dim Y. \\]","sourceOffset":2089,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Campana--Peternell","label":"cor:campana-peternell","text":"Let $X$ be a smooth connected projective complex variety, let $D$ be an effective Cartier divisor on $X$, and let $m_0$ be a positive integer. If $m_0K_X-D$ is pseudo-effective, then \\[ \\kappa(X)\\geq\\kappa(D). \\]","sourceOffset":3660,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":34252,"sourceFileCount":5,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Schnell-fiber-spaces-and-good-canonical-models-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"034-09","slug":"Uniform-log-Iitaka-fibrations-and-bounded-moduli-denominators-October-4-2026","familyId":"034","title":"Uniform log Iitaka fibrations and bounded moduli denominators","abstract":"For normal projective log canonical pairs over algebraically closed fields of characteristic zero, of fixed dimension d ≥ 5 and with effective boundary coefficients in a fixed finite rational set, we prove that one complete rounded pluricanonical system generates the full Iitaka field whenever the ℚ-Cartier log canonical divisor has nonnegative Kodaira dimension. Its degree depends only on the dimension and coefficient set. For the paper's normalized canonical bundle formulae over ℂ, we also bound the Cartier denominators of the moduli divisors on smooth projective determining models in terms of the dimension and coefficient set.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Using logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity, proves log abundance in every dimension for normal compact Kähler log canonical pairs with effective rational boundary: an analytically nef ℚ-Cartier adjoint is semiample. It also proves projective log abundance over every algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and the effective Iitaka fibration conjecture for smooth projective varieties of nonnegative Kodaira dimension in that setting. A further result resolves the finite-rational-coefficient index conjecture for connected projective semi-log-canonical log Calabi–Yau pairs over such fields in each fixed dimension and for each fixed finite set of rational boundary coefficients, with a uniform index independent of the number of components.","pdfPath":"preprints/Uniform-log-Iitaka-fibrations-and-bounded-moduli-denominators-October-4-2026/uniform-log-iitaka.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-log-Iitaka-fibrations-and-bounded-moduli-denominators-October-4-2026/uniform-log-iitaka.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-log-Iitaka-fibrations-and-bounded-moduli-denominators-October-4-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Uniform-log-Iitaka-fibrations-and-bounded-moduli-denominators-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Uniform-log-Iitaka-fibrations-and-bounded-moduli-denominators-October-4-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Uniform-log-Iitaka-fibrations-and-bounded-moduli-denominators-October-4-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Uniform-log-Iitaka-fibrations-and-bounded-moduli-denominators-October-4-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Uniform-log-Iitaka-fibrations-and-bounded-moduli-denominators-October-4-2026/build/sections/mmp.tex","preprints/Uniform-log-Iitaka-fibrations-and-bounded-moduli-denominators-October-4-2026/build/sections/weights.tex","preprints/Uniform-log-Iitaka-fibrations-and-bounded-moduli-denominators-October-4-2026/build/sections/blocks.tex","preprints/Uniform-log-Iitaka-fibrations-and-bounded-moduli-denominators-October-4-2026/build/sections/degeneration.tex","preprints/Uniform-log-Iitaka-fibrations-and-bounded-moduli-denominators-October-4-2026/build/sections/fibration.tex","preprints/Uniform-log-Iitaka-fibrations-and-bounded-moduli-denominators-October-4-2026/build/sections/completion.tex","preprints/Uniform-log-Iitaka-fibrations-and-bounded-moduli-denominators-October-4-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Uniform-log-Iitaka-fibrations-and-bounded-moduli-denominators-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For normal projective log canonical pairs over algebraically closed fields of characteristic zero, of fixed dimension $d\\geq5$ and with effective boundary coefficients in a fixed finite rational set, we prove that one complete rounded pluricanonical system generates the full Iitaka field whenever the $\\Q$-Cartier log canonical divisor has nonnegative Kodaira dimension. Its degree depends only on the dimension and coefficient set. For the paper's normalized canonical bundle formulae over $\\C$, we also bound the Cartier denominators of the moduli divisors on smooth projective determining models in terms of the dimension and coefficient set.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":749},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the denominator problem","sourceOffset":3232},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The main intermediate result and the proof","sourceOffset":6783},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and companion theorems","sourceOffset":11532},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The three companion inputs","sourceOffset":13566},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Trivializations and constants","sourceOffset":15971},{"kind":"section","title":"Minimal models over a curve","sourceOffset":17690},{"kind":"section","title":"Weights of logarithmic pluriforms","sourceOffset":20622},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Changes of fields and forms","sourceOffset":23372},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A model on which the weight is exact","sourceOffset":25752},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Residue and the reduction to klt fibres","sourceOffset":31541},{"kind":"section","title":"Product covers and semilinear factor actions","sourceOffset":36528}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform log Iitaka degree","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $d\\geq5$ and every finite set $\\Phi\\subset[0,1]\\cap\\Q$, there is an integer $m=m(d,\\Phi)>0$ with the following property. Let $k$ be any algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, and let $(X,B)$ be a normal integral projective lc $d$-fold pair over $k$. Assume that $B\\geq0$, every nonzero coefficient of $B$ belongs to $\\Phi$, $D=K_X+B$ is rational Cartier, and $\\kappa(X,D)\\geq0$. Then \\[ V_m(D)\\neq0,\\qquad F_m(D)=K(D) \\quad\\text{inside }k(X). \\]","sourceOffset":2008,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Arithmetic Stein degrees {\\cite[Main Theorem","label":"thm:stein-degree","text":"{SD}}] For every integer $n\\geq1$ and every real number $t>0$, there is an integer $N(n,t)$ with the following property. Let $F$ be a field of characteristic zero, and let $(V,\\Delta)$ be a normal integral projective lc rational pair over $F$ with \\[ \\dim V=n,\\qquad H^0(V,\\OO_V)=F,\\qquad \\Delta\\geq0,\\qquad K_V+\\Delta\\simq0. \\] If $S$ is a prime component of $\\Delta$ of coefficient at least $t$, the algebraic closure of $F$ in $F(S)$ has degree at most $N(n,t)$ over $F$.","sourceOffset":15138,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:weight","text":"For a rational relative $m$-canonical form $\\phi$, set \\begin{equation} w_z(\\phi)=\\inf_E \\frac{\\ord_E\\bigl(\\phi\\wedge(dz/z)^{\\otimes m}\\bigr)+m} {m\\ord_E z}. \\end{equation} Here $E$ runs through the normalized divisorial valuations of $F/\\C$ whose restriction to $K$ is a positive multiple of $\\ord_c$.","sourceOffset":21781,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":114623,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Uniform-log-Iitaka-fibrations-and-bounded-moduli-denominators-October-4-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"034-10","slug":"Uniform-Pluricanonical-Iitaka-Fibrations-October-3-2026","familyId":"034","title":"Uniform Pluricanonical Iitaka Fibrations","abstract":"We prove the effective Iitaka fibration conjecture in characteristic zero. For each dimension, one pluricanonical degree defines the Iitaka fibration of every smooth integral projective variety of that dimension and nonnegative Kodaira dimension over an algebraically closed field. The associated sections generate the full Iitaka function field.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Using logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity, proves log abundance in every dimension for normal compact Kähler log canonical pairs with effective rational boundary: an analytically nef ℚ-Cartier adjoint is semiample. It also proves projective log abundance over every algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and the effective Iitaka fibration conjecture for smooth projective varieties of nonnegative Kodaira dimension in that setting. A further result resolves the finite-rational-coefficient index conjecture for connected projective semi-log-canonical log Calabi–Yau pairs over such fields in each fixed dimension and for each fixed finite set of rational boundary coefficients, with a uniform index independent of the number of components.","pdfPath":"preprints/Uniform-Pluricanonical-Iitaka-Fibrations-October-3-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-Pluricanonical-Iitaka-Fibrations-October-3-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-Pluricanonical-Iitaka-Fibrations-October-3-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Uniform-Pluricanonical-Iitaka-Fibrations-October-3-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Uniform-Pluricanonical-Iitaka-Fibrations-October-3-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Uniform-Pluricanonical-Iitaka-Fibrations-October-3-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Uniform-Pluricanonical-Iitaka-Fibrations-October-3-2026/build/figures/induction.tex","preprints/Uniform-Pluricanonical-Iitaka-Fibrations-October-3-2026/build/sections/index.tex","preprints/Uniform-Pluricanonical-Iitaka-Fibrations-October-3-2026/build/sections/denominators.tex","preprints/Uniform-Pluricanonical-Iitaka-Fibrations-October-3-2026/build/sections/scalar.tex","preprints/Uniform-Pluricanonical-Iitaka-Fibrations-October-3-2026/build/sections/diagonal.tex","preprints/Uniform-Pluricanonical-Iitaka-Fibrations-October-3-2026/build/sections/completion.tex","preprints/Uniform-Pluricanonical-Iitaka-Fibrations-October-3-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Uniform-Pluricanonical-Iitaka-Fibrations-October-3-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the effective Iitaka fibration conjecture in characteristic zero. For each dimension, one pluricanonical degree defines the Iitaka fibration of every smooth integral projective variety of that dimension and nonnegative Kodaira dimension over an algebraically closed field. The associated sections generate the full Iitaka function field.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":409},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and preceding work","sourceOffset":3042},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Inputs and contributions","sourceOffset":5319},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The induction","sourceOffset":7302},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and the induction hypotheses","sourceOffset":10487},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Geometric generic fibres and fields of definition","sourceOffset":15553},{"kind":"section","title":"Normal log canonical indices and finite covers","sourceOffset":17265},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Descent from a normalized boundary component","sourceOffset":17764},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Quasi-\\'etale covers and factor actions","sourceOffset":27632},{"kind":"section","title":"Uniform denominators and the base of a fibration","sourceOffset":37776},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exact canonical bundle formula","sourceOffset":38736},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weights of relative forms","sourceOffset":41603}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform pluricanonical degree","label":"thm:main","text":"For each integer $d\\geq1$ there is an integer $m(d)>0$ such that, over every algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, the complete system $|m(d)K_X|$ defines the Iitaka fibration of every smooth integral projective $d$-fold $X$ with $\\kappa(X)\\geq0$.","sourceOffset":1409,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"The remaining index sequence","label":"prop:index-reduction","text":"If the canonical indices of projective klt $n$-folds with $K_X\\sim_{\\Q}0$ are unbounded, there is a sequence of projective $\\Q$-factorial terminal $n$-folds $V_i$, of canonical indices $r_i\\to\\infty$, satisfying the following properties. \\begin{enumerate} \\item A smooth projective resolution of $V_i$ has irregularity zero, and $\\Bir(V_i)$ is countable. \\item Every positive-dimensional rational image of $V_i$ of dimension less than $n$ has rationally connected smooth projective resolution. \\item Every effective Weil divisor on $V_i$ with positive Iitaka dimension is big. \\item Let $\\pi_i:Y_i\\to V_i$ be the cyclic index cover, with its deck group $G_i$. Then $Y_i$ is terminal and $K_{Y_i}\\sim0$. Every $G_i$-equivariant rational fibration of $Y_i$ having positive-dimensional base and fibre has general-type smooth geometric generic fibre. The same assertion holds for rational fibrations of $V_i$, with trivial group action. \\end{enumerate} Here a rational fibration has geometrically integral generic fibre, or equivalently has a relatively algebraically closed base function field.","sourceOffset":73552,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"notation","name":"Induction statements","label":"not:induction","text":"Let $I_n$ denote Theorem \\ref{thm:main} in dimension $n$, and let $L_n$ denote Theorem \\ref{thm:lc-index} in dimension $n$, for every rational DCC coefficient set. Let $K_n$ denote its zero-boundary klt case: there is a common integer $a_n>0$ with $a_nK_X\\sim0$ for all projective klt $n$-folds satisfying $K_X\\sim_\\Q0$.","sourceOffset":13079,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":166372,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Uniform-Pluricanonical-Iitaka-Fibrations-October-3-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"034-11","slug":"Relative-denominators-and-effective-systems-for-log-Calabi-Yau-fibrations-September-27-2026","familyId":"034","title":"Relative denominators and effective systems for log Calabi-Yau fibrations","abstract":"We prove uniform denominator and effective-system bounds for log Calabi-Yau fibrations from projective log canonical complex pairs of dimension at most four onto positive-dimensional bases, with boundary coefficients in a fixed finite rational set. The bounds give a uniform trivializing degree and a uniform b-Cartier multiple of the moduli b-divisor. When the base divisor is big, a uniform complete rounded adjoint system has section ratios generating the full base function field.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Using logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity, proves log abundance in every dimension for normal compact Kähler log canonical pairs with effective rational boundary: an analytically nef ℚ-Cartier adjoint is semiample. It also proves projective log abundance over every algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and the effective Iitaka fibration conjecture for smooth projective varieties of nonnegative Kodaira dimension in that setting. A further result resolves the finite-rational-coefficient index conjecture for connected projective semi-log-canonical log Calabi–Yau pairs over such fields in each fixed dimension and for each fixed finite set of rational boundary coefficients, with a uniform index independent of the number of components.","pdfPath":"preprints/Relative-denominators-and-effective-systems-for-log-Calabi-Yau-fibrations-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Relative-denominators-and-effective-systems-for-log-Calabi-Yau-fibrations-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Relative-denominators-and-effective-systems-for-log-Calabi-Yau-fibrations-September-27-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Relative-denominators-and-effective-systems-for-log-Calabi-Yau-fibrations-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Relative-denominators-and-effective-systems-for-log-Calabi-Yau-fibrations-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Relative-denominators-and-effective-systems-for-log-Calabi-Yau-fibrations-September-27-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Relative-denominators-and-effective-systems-for-log-Calabi-Yau-fibrations-September-27-2026/build/sections/generic.tex","preprints/Relative-denominators-and-effective-systems-for-log-Calabi-Yau-fibrations-September-27-2026/build/sections/curve.tex","preprints/Relative-denominators-and-effective-systems-for-log-Calabi-Yau-fibrations-September-27-2026/build/sections/residue.tex","preprints/Relative-denominators-and-effective-systems-for-log-Calabi-Yau-fibrations-September-27-2026/build/sections/effective.tex","preprints/Relative-denominators-and-effective-systems-for-log-Calabi-Yau-fibrations-September-27-2026/build/sections/torsion.tex","preprints/Relative-denominators-and-effective-systems-for-log-Calabi-Yau-fibrations-September-27-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Relative-denominators-and-effective-systems-for-log-Calabi-Yau-fibrations-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove uniform denominator and effective-system bounds for log Calabi-Yau fibrations from projective log canonical complex pairs of dimension at most four onto positive-dimensional bases, with boundary coefficients in a fixed finite rational set. The bounds give a uniform trivializing degree and a uniform b-Cartier multiple of the moduli b-divisor. When the base divisor is big, a uniform complete rounded adjoint system has section ratios generating the full base function field.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":530},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Denominators, positivity, and earlier work","sourceOffset":4307},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The route to the relative bound","sourceOffset":7606},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Effective systems and torsion","sourceOffset":9793},{"kind":"section","title":"Divisors and the lower-dimensional index input","sourceOffset":13039},{"kind":"section","title":"An exact pullback and the coefficient to be controlled","sourceOffset":17813},{"kind":"section","title":"Reduction to a dlt fibre over a curve","sourceOffset":23353},{"kind":"section","title":"The denominator detected by residues on the whole fibre","sourceOffset":34842},{"kind":"section","title":"Effective systems recovering the entire base field","sourceOffset":54580},{"kind":"section","title":"Integral principal multiples over rationally connected bases","sourceOffset":60563}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform relative denominators","label":"thm:relative-denominators","text":"Fix a finite set $\\Phi\\subset[0,1]\\cap\\Q$ and an integer $1\\leq d\\leq4$. There are positive integers $p_0\\mid p$, with $p_0$ clearing $\\Phi$, and a rational DCC set $\\mathcal B\\subset[0,1]$, depending only on $d$ and $\\Phi$, with the following property. Suppose $(X,B)$ is a projective lc pair of dimension $d$ with coefficients in $\\Phi$, and \\[ f:X\\longrightarrow Z,\\qquad \\dim Z>0,\\qquad K_X+B\\sim_\\Q f^*D, \\] where $f$ is a contraction and $D$ is a rational Cartier divisor. There exist $\\psi\\in\\C(X)^*$ and $D_Z\\sim_\\Q D$ such that \\begin{equation} K_X+B+\\frac{1}{p_0}\\Div(\\psi)=f^*D_Z, \\qquad D_Z=K_Z+B_Z+M_Z. \\end{equation} Here $B_Z$ is effective with coefficients in $\\mathcal B$, the pair $(Z,B_Z+M_Z)$ is generalized lc, and $\\mathbf M$ is b-nef with $p\\mathbf M$ b-Cartier. If $(X,B)$ is klt, the generalized pair is generalized klt.","sourceOffset":2781,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:effective-base","text":"Under the hypotheses of Theorem \\ref{thm:relative-denominators}, suppose in addition that $D$ is big. There is a positive integer $m=m(d,\\Phi)$ such that, for every positive multiple $l$ of $m$, the complete divisorial system \\[ \\left|\\left\\lfloor l(K_X+B)\\right\\rfloor\\right| \\] is nonempty and generates precisely the subfield $\\C(Z)\\subset\\C(X)$. Its rational map is therefore birationally equivalent to $f$ and to the Iitaka fibration of $K_X+B$.","sourceOffset":54860,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":82808,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Relative-denominators-and-effective-systems-for-log-Calabi-Yau-fibrations-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"034-12","slug":"Arithmetic-Stein-degree-bounds-for-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-September-25-2026","familyId":"034","title":"Arithmetic Stein-degree bounds for log Calabi–Yau pairs","abstract":"Fix d ≥ 1 and t > 0. Let $(X,B)$ be an ordinary projective log canonical ℚ-pair of dimension d over a characteristic-zero field k, with X normal and integral, $H^0(X,\\mathcal O_X)=k$, B effective, and $K_X+B\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}0$. We prove that every prime component S of B with coefficient at least t satisfies $[k_S:k]\\leq N(d,t)$, where kS is the relative algebraic closure of k in $k(S)$. This also bounds the Stein degree of S over k, proving the contraction-to-a-point formulation of Birkar's Stein-degree conjecture for ordinary ℚ-pairs.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Using logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity, proves log abundance in every dimension for normal compact Kähler log canonical pairs with effective rational boundary: an analytically nef ℚ-Cartier adjoint is semiample. It also proves projective log abundance over every algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and the effective Iitaka fibration conjecture for smooth projective varieties of nonnegative Kodaira dimension in that setting. A further result resolves the finite-rational-coefficient index conjecture for connected projective semi-log-canonical log Calabi–Yau pairs over such fields in each fixed dimension and for each fixed finite set of rational boundary coefficients, with a uniform index independent of the number of components.","pdfPath":"preprints/Arithmetic-Stein-degree-bounds-for-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Arithmetic-Stein-degree-bounds-for-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Arithmetic-Stein-degree-bounds-for-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-September-25-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Arithmetic-Stein-degree-bounds-for-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Arithmetic-Stein-degree-bounds-for-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"Fix $d\\geq1$ and $t>0$. Let $(X,B)$ be an ordinary projective log canonical $\\Q$-pair of dimension $d$ over a characteristic-zero field $k$, with $X$ normal and integral, $H^0(X,\\OO_X)=k$, $B$ effective, and $K_X+B\\simq0$. We prove that every prime component $S$ of $B$ with coefficient at least $t$ satisfies $[k_S:k]\\leq N(d,t)$, where $k_S$ is the relative algebraic closure of $k$ in $k(S)$. This also bounds the Stein degree of $S$ over $k$, proving the contraction-to-a-point formulation of Birkar's Stein-degree conjecture for ordinary $\\Q$-pairs.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":616},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and foundational inputs","sourceOffset":3531},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Constants and generic fibres","sourceOffset":4807},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Established theorems used in the proof","sourceOffset":7264},{"kind":"section","title":"Arithmetic consequences of geometric boundedness","sourceOffset":11004},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Descending a complement","sourceOffset":11086},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Log canonical places of a simple normal crossings pair","sourceOffset":13400},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bounded resolutions and Galois orbits","sourceOffset":16939},{"kind":"section","title":"Birational preparation and adjunction","sourceOffset":25922},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Making the distinguished component positive","sourceOffset":25994},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Making a vertical prime a pullback","sourceOffset":29349},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Adjunction with a specified index","sourceOffset":32902}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $d\\geq 1$ and real number $t>0$, there is an integer $N(d,t)\\geq1$ with the following property. Let $k$ be any field of characteristic zero, and let $(X,B)$ be a projective log canonical $\\Q$-pair such that \\[ \\dim X=d,\\qquad H^0(X,\\OO_X)=k,\\qquad K_X+B\\simq0. \\] Assume that $X$ is normal and integral and that $B$ is effective. If $S$ is a prime component of $B$ with coefficient at least $t$, then \\[ \\cdeg{S}{k}\\leq N(d,t). \\] Consequently \\[ \\sdeg(S/\\Spec k)\\leq \\dim_k H^0(S^\\nu,\\OO_{S^\\nu}) =\\cdeg{S}{k}\\leq N(d,t), \\] where $S^\\nu$ is the normalization of $S$.","sourceOffset":1729,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:finite-low","text":"A klt rational pair has only finitely many divisorial valuations of log discrepancy less than one.","sourceOffset":15857,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":51758,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Arithmetic-Stein-degree-bounds-for-log-Calabi-Yau-pairs-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"034-13","slug":"Uniform-effective-log-Iitaka-fibrations-for-fourfolds-September-26-2026","familyId":"034","title":"Uniform effective log Iitaka fibrations for fourfolds","abstract":"We prove the finite-rational-coefficient case of the effective log Iitaka conjecture in dimension four. For normal projective log canonical complex fourfolds with boundary coefficients in a fixed finite rational set and pseudo-effective rational Cartier adjoint, one uniform degree makes the complete rounded reflexive system nonempty and its section ratios generate the full Iitaka field. We also prove a uniform canonical index bound for projective klt complex fourfolds with rationally trivial canonical divisor.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Using logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity, proves log abundance in every dimension for normal compact Kähler log canonical pairs with effective rational boundary: an analytically nef ℚ-Cartier adjoint is semiample. It also proves projective log abundance over every algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and the effective Iitaka fibration conjecture for smooth projective varieties of nonnegative Kodaira dimension in that setting. A further result resolves the finite-rational-coefficient index conjecture for connected projective semi-log-canonical log Calabi–Yau pairs over such fields in each fixed dimension and for each fixed finite set of rational boundary coefficients, with a uniform index independent of the number of components.","pdfPath":"preprints/Uniform-effective-log-Iitaka-fibrations-for-fourfolds-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-effective-log-Iitaka-fibrations-for-fourfolds-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-effective-log-Iitaka-fibrations-for-fourfolds-September-26-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Uniform-effective-log-Iitaka-fibrations-for-fourfolds-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Uniform-effective-log-Iitaka-fibrations-for-fourfolds-September-26-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Uniform-effective-log-Iitaka-fibrations-for-fourfolds-September-26-2026/build/sections/reductions.tex","preprints/Uniform-effective-log-Iitaka-fibrations-for-fourfolds-September-26-2026/build/sections/relative-inputs.tex","preprints/Uniform-effective-log-Iitaka-fibrations-for-fourfolds-September-26-2026/build/sections/index-reductions.tex","preprints/Uniform-effective-log-Iitaka-fibrations-for-fourfolds-September-26-2026/build/sections/chains.tex","preprints/Uniform-effective-log-Iitaka-fibrations-for-fourfolds-September-26-2026/build/sections/scalar.tex","preprints/Uniform-effective-log-Iitaka-fibrations-for-fourfolds-September-26-2026/build/sections/frobenius.tex","preprints/Uniform-effective-log-Iitaka-fibrations-for-fourfolds-September-26-2026/build/sections/transports.tex","preprints/Uniform-effective-log-Iitaka-fibrations-for-fourfolds-September-26-2026/build/sections/index-completion.tex","preprints/Uniform-effective-log-Iitaka-fibrations-for-fourfolds-September-26-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex","preprints/Uniform-effective-log-Iitaka-fibrations-for-fourfolds-September-26-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Uniform-effective-log-Iitaka-fibrations-for-fourfolds-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the finite-rational-coefficient case of the effective log Iitaka conjecture in dimension four. For normal projective log canonical complex fourfolds with boundary coefficients in a fixed finite rational set and pseudo-effective rational Cartier adjoint, one uniform degree makes the complete rounded reflexive system nonempty and its section ratios generate the full Iitaka field. We also prove a uniform canonical index bound for projective klt complex fourfolds with rationally trivial canonical divisor.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":562},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model comparison and the index reductions","sourceOffset":6564},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Curves on the canonical cover and its products","sourceOffset":9297},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From chains of curves to birational maps","sourceOffset":12845},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and conventions","sourceOffset":16012},{"kind":"section","title":"Semiample models and rounded section spaces","sourceOffset":18462},{"kind":"section","title":"Relative adjunction inputs","sourceOffset":26601},{"kind":"section","title":"Structural reductions for the canonical index","sourceOffset":31200},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite covers and canonical characters","sourceOffset":32163},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rational images of a single nonflat factor","sourceOffset":44952},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Terminal models and intermediate fibrations","sourceOffset":55514},{"kind":"section","title":"Generic chains and orders of sections","sourceOffset":65835}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform effective log Iitaka fibrations","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $\\Phi\\subset[0,1]\\cap\\Q$ be finite. There is a positive integer $m=m(\\Phi)$ such that the following holds. Suppose $X$ is a normal integral projective complex fourfold, $\\Delta\\geq0$ is a rational Weil divisor whose nonzero coefficients lie in $\\Phi$, $(X,\\Delta)$ is lc, and $D=K_X+\\Delta$ is rational Cartier and pseudo-effective. Then the complete system $|\\lfloor mD\\rfloor|$ is nonempty and its section ratios generate $K(D)$ inside $\\C(X)$. The same integer works for every choice of canonical divisor on $X$.","sourceOffset":3326,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform canonical index","label":"thm:fourfold-index-zero","text":"There is a positive integer $N_4$ such that $N_4K_X\\sim0$ for every normal projective klt complex fourfold $X$ with $K_X\\sim_\\Q0$.","sourceOffset":1974,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:orders","text":"Let $V$ be an integral projective variety of positive dimension, and let $P$ be a big, nef, semiample rational Cartier divisor on $V$. For a smooth projective birational resolution $\\pi:\\widetilde V\\to V$, set \\[ \\gamma(P;V)= \\sup_{\\substack{k>0\\text{ sufficiently divisible}\\\\ 0\\ne s\\in H^0(\\widetilde V,k\\pi^*P)}} \\frac{\\ord_x(s)}{k}, \\qquad \\rho(P;V)=(P^{\\dim V})^{1/\\dim V}, \\] where $x$ is very general in the smooth isomorphism locus, identified with its lift to $\\widetilde V$. If a specified smooth point $x$ is used instead, write $\\gamma(P;V,x)$, with the resolution chosen to be an isomorp","sourceOffset":78610,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":195791,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Uniform-effective-log-Iitaka-fibrations-for-fourfolds-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"034-14","slug":"Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026","familyId":"034","title":"Abundance after nonvanishing for compact Kähler fourfolds","abstract":"We prove semiampleness of the actual ℚ-Cartier adjoint $K_X+\\Delta$ of a normal connected compact Kähler klt fourfold whenever it is analytically nef and some positive Cartier multiple has a nonzero section. The boundary is effective and rational; the fourfold need not be projective or ℚ-factorial. In Iitaka dimension zero, a positive Cartier multiple is the trivial holomorphic line bundle.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Using logarithmic Iitaka subadditivity, proves log abundance in every dimension for normal compact Kähler log canonical pairs with effective rational boundary: an analytically nef ℚ-Cartier adjoint is semiample. It also proves projective log abundance over every algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and the effective Iitaka fibration conjecture for smooth projective varieties of nonnegative Kodaira dimension in that setting. A further result resolves the finite-rational-coefficient index conjecture for connected projective semi-log-canonical log Calabi–Yau pairs over such fields in each fixed dimension and for each fixed finite set of rational boundary coefficients, with a uniform index independent of the number of components.","pdfPath":"preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026/build/sections/conventions.tex","preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026/build/sections/finite-null.tex","preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026/build/sections/contraction-inputs.tex","preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026/build/sections/analytic-extension.tex","preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026/build/sections/reduction.tex","preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026/build/sections/outer-positivity.tex","preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026/build/sections/adjunction.tex","preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026/build/sections/links.tex","preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026/build/sections/scalars.tex","preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026/build/sections/gluing.tex","preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026/build/sections/covers.tex","preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026/build/sections/hodge.tex","preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026/build/sections/lifting.tex","preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026/build/sections/completion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove semiampleness of the actual \\(\\Q\\)-Cartier adjoint \\(K_X+\\Delta\\) of a normal connected compact K\\\"ahler klt fourfold whenever it is analytically nef and some positive Cartier multiple has a nonzero section. The boundary is effective and rational; the fourfold need not be projective or \\(\\Q\\)-factorial. In Iitaka dimension zero, a positive Cartier multiple is the trivial holomorphic line bundle.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":470},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From a plurisection to a supported model","sourceOffset":4730},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compatible sections on the whole floor","sourceOffset":7703},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Lifting from the supported boundary","sourceOffset":11012},{"kind":"section","title":"Actual adjoints and analytic conventions","sourceOffset":16150},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Adjoints, residues, and singularities","sourceOffset":16569},{"kind":"subsection","title":"K\\\"ahler positivity and section spaces","sourceOffset":20512},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A connectedness and rationality lemma","sourceOffset":23196},{"kind":"section","title":"Threefold contractions for the boundary argument","sourceOffset":26798},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Contraction of the full finite null locus","sourceOffset":29136},{"kind":"subsubsection","title":"An exceptional divisor and one actual positive line","sourceOffset":30569},{"kind":"subsubsection","title":"Numerical ampleness on the projective inverse image","sourceOffset":37105}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(X\\) be a normal connected compact K\\\"ahler complex space of dimension four. Let \\(\\Delta\\) be an effective rational Weil divisor such that \\((X,\\Delta)\\) is Kawamata log terminal and the actual adjoint \\(D=K_X+\\Delta\\) is \\(\\Q\\)-Cartier. If \\(D\\) is analytically nef and \\(\\kappa(X,D)\\geq0\\), then there is an integer \\(m>0\\) for which \\(mD\\) is Cartier and \\[ H^0(X,\\OO_X(mD))\\otimes_\\C\\OO_X\\longrightarrow\\OO_X(mD) \\] is surjective at every point. If \\(\\kappa(X,D)=0\\), then one may choose \\(m\\) so that \\(\\OO_X(mD)\\simeq\\OO_X\\).","sourceOffset":1755,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Supported lifting","label":"thm:supported-lifting","text":"Let \\((V,B)\\) be a normal irreducible compact K\\\"ahler dlt pair of positive dimension, with effective rational boundary and actual \\(\\Q\\)-Cartier adjoint \\(A=K_V+B\\). Suppose that an effective nonzero rational \\(\\Q\\)-Cartier divisor \\(P\\) satisfies \\[ P\\sim_\\Q A,\\qquad \\Supp P=\\Supp\\floor B. \\] If the actual restriction \\(A|_S\\) to the whole reduced subspace \\(S=\\floor B\\) is semiample, then \\(\\kappa(V,A)\\geq1\\).","sourceOffset":11256,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":343731,"sourceFileCount":17,"statementCount":18,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Abundance-after-nonvanishing-for-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"035-01","slug":"Log-abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026","familyId":"035","title":"Log abundance in numerical dimension one for threefolds in positive characteristic","abstract":"We prove the numerical-dimension-one case of log abundance for threefolds over algebraically closed fields of characteristic p > 3. If $(X,B)$ is a projective log canonical threefold pair with effective rational boundary, and $K_X+B$ is ℚ-Cartier, nef, and of numerical dimension one, then $K_X+B$ is semiample. Neither terminality nor ℚ-factoriality of X is required.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves log abundance for projective log canonical threefold pairs over algebraically closed fields of characteristic p > 3 when the effective boundary is rational and the ℚ-Cartier adjoint is nef of numerical dimension one. The adjoint is semiample, without requiring the original variety to be terminal or ℚ-factorial.","pdfPath":"preprints/Log-abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Log-abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Log-abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Log-abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/sections/foundations.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/sections/boundary.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/sections/isolation.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/sections/torsion.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/sections/cycle.tex","preprints/Log-abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Log-abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the numerical-dimension-one case of log abundance for threefolds over algebraically closed fields of characteristic $p>3$. If $(X,B)$ is a projective log canonical threefold pair with effective rational boundary, and $K_X+B$ is $\\Q$-Cartier, nef, and of numerical dimension one, then $K_X+B$ is semiample. Neither terminality nor $\\Q$-factoriality of $X$ is required.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":422},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The boundary and the proof strategy","sourceOffset":3855},{"kind":"section","title":"The cycle obstruction and the initial reductions","sourceOffset":6425},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The obstruction supplied by the companion","sourceOffset":7103},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Numerical observations","sourceOffset":10207},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reduction to a terminal underlying variety","sourceOffset":11505},{"kind":"section","title":"Separating the boundary from the log canonical cycle","sourceOffset":16078},{"kind":"section","title":"Isolating a log canonical component","sourceOffset":20206},{"kind":"section","title":"Torsion on the full boundary cycle","sourceOffset":33607},{"kind":"section","title":"Construction of the prepared cycle","sourceOffset":41457}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p>3$. Let $(X,B)$ be a projective log canonical threefold pair over $k$, where $X$ is normal, $B$ is an effective $\\Q$-divisor, and $K_X+B$ is $\\Q$-Cartier. If $K_X+B$ is nef and has numerical dimension one, then $K_X+B$ is semiample.","sourceOffset":987,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Cycle obstruction from the companion","label":"thm:obstruction","text":"Let $k$ be an uncountable algebraically closed field of characteristic $p>3$. There do not exist a smooth projective threefold $V$, a very ample Cartier divisor $H_V$, and a nonzero effective Cartier divisor $D=\\sum_\\alpha m_\\alpha D_\\alpha$ satisfying all the following conditions: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item The $D_\\alpha$ are distinct prime divisors, $m_\\alpha\\in\\Z_{>0}$, the support of $D$ is connected and has simple normal crossings, and $\\gcd_\\alpha m_\\alpha=1$. \\item $D$ is nef, its restriction to every $D_\\alpha$ is numerically trivial, and \\[ DH_V^2>0,\\qquad D^2H_V=0,\\qquad n(D)=3. \\] \\item The line bundle $\\mathcal O_D(D)$ on the possibly nonreduced Cartier scheme $D$ is torsion. \\item For some integer $b\\geq0$ and integers $b_\\alpha$, there is an isomorphism on a neighborhood of $\\Supp D$, \\begin{equation} \\omega_V^{\\otimes p^b}\\simeq \\mathcal O_V\\Bigl(\\sum_\\alpha b_\\alpha D_\\alpha\\Bigr). \\end{equation} \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":7713,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":59800,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Log-abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"035-02","slug":"Abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-terminal-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026","familyId":"035","title":"Abundance in numerical dimension one for terminal threefolds in positive characteristic","abstract":"Let X be a projective ℚ-factorial terminal threefold over an algebraically closed field of characteristic p > 3. We prove the numerical-dimension-one case of abundance: if KX is nef with $\\nu(K_X)=1$, then KX is semiample and $\\kappa(X,K_X)=1$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves log abundance for projective log canonical threefold pairs over algebraically closed fields of characteristic p > 3 when the effective boundary is rational and the ℚ-Cartier adjoint is nef of numerical dimension one. The adjoint is semiample, without requiring the original variety to be terminal or ℚ-factorial.","pdfPath":"preprints/Abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-terminal-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-terminal-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-terminal-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-terminal-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-terminal-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-terminal-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-terminal-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/preparation.tex","preprints/Abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-terminal-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/slopes.tex","preprints/Abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-terminal-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/jets.tex","preprints/Abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-terminal-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/laurent.tex","preprints/Abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-terminal-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/cartier.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-terminal-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let $X$ be a projective $\\Q$-factorial terminal threefold over an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p>3$. We prove the numerical-dimension-one case of abundance: if $K_X$ is nef with $\\nu(K_X)=1$, then $K_X$ is semiample and $\\kappa(X,K_X)=1$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":343},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context","sourceOffset":2020},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The argument","sourceOffset":6072},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":9966},{"kind":"section","title":"Birational preparation","sourceOffset":10993},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Resolution and field extension","sourceOffset":13600},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The maximal nef-dimension branch","sourceOffset":17435},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Isolating a boundary component","sourceOffset":18772},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local surface intersections and terminal regularity","sourceOffset":26311},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Adjunction on the reduced boundary","sourceOffset":33705},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Torsion on the full Cartier cycle","sourceOffset":40356},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A tame cover and the primitive cycle","sourceOffset":44796}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $X$ be a normal projective $\\Q$-factorial threefold over an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $p>3$. Suppose that $X$ has terminal singularities and that $K_X$ is nef with $\\nu(K_X)=1$. Then some positive Cartier multiple of $K_X$ has at least two independent global sections. In particular, $\\kappa(X,K_X)=1$ and $K_X$ is semiample.","sourceOffset":955,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"The prepared cycle","label":"prop:prepared-cycle","text":"Suppose that \\(k\\) is uncountable and algebraically closed of characteristic \\(p>3\\), and that \\(X\\) is a projective terminal threefold with \\(K_X\\) nef, \\(\\nu(K_X)=1\\), and \\(n(X,K_X)=3\\). There exist a smooth projective threefold \\(V\\), a generically finite separable morphism \\(\\rho:V\\to X\\), a very ample divisor \\(H\\) on \\(V\\), and a nonzero effective Cartier divisor \\[ D=\\sum_{\\alpha}m_\\alpha D_\\alpha,\\qquad m_\\alpha\\in\\Z_{>0},\\qquad \\gcd_\\alpha m_\\alpha=1, \\] with the following properties. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item The support of \\(D\\) is connected and has simple normal crossings. The divisor \\(D\\) is nef, and \\(D|_{D_\\alpha}\\) is numerically trivial for every \\(\\alpha\\). \\item There is no \\(D\\)-trivial curve through a very general point of \\(V\\). Moreover \\(D\\cdot H^2>0\\) and \\(D^2\\cdot H=0\\). \\item The invertible sheaf \\(\\cO_D(D)\\) is torsion. Its order will be denoted by \\(r\\). \\item For an integer \\(b\\geq0\\), an open neighborhood \\(U\\) of \\(|D|\\), and integers \\(b_\\alpha\\), there is an isomorphism \\begin{equation} \\omega_V^{\\otimes p^b}|_U \\simeq \\cO_U(B_*),\\qquad B_*=\\sum_\\alpha b_\\alpha D_\\alpha . \\end{equation} Consequently \\(K_V\\cdot D\\cdot A=0\\) for every Cartier divisor \\(A\\) on \\(V\\). \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":11286,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":155270,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-terminal-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Abundance-in-numerical-dimension-one-for-terminal-threefolds-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"036-01","slug":"Numerical-semiampleness-of-nef-adjoint-classes-on-compact-Kahler-manifolds-October-6-2026","familyId":"036","title":"Numerical semiampleness of nef adjoint classes on compact Kähler manifolds","abstract":"Let X be a smooth connected compact Kähler manifold, let B be an effective rational simple normal crossing divisor with coefficients less than one, and let M be a nef rational holomorphic line bundle on X. If $K_X+B$ is pseudo-effective and $K_X+B+M$ is nef, we prove that its first Chern class in real Bott–Chern cohomology is represented by a semiample rational line bundle. This numerical statement allows a flat change of line bundle; it does not assert semiampleness of the original adjoint.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves numerical semiampleness for nef adjoints $K_X+B+M$ with $K_X+B$ pseudo-effective and M nef rational, for projective klt rational pairs over algebraically closed characteristic-zero fields and smooth compact Kähler rational klt simple-normal-crossing pairs, using Bott–Chern cohomology in the latter case. Separately, projective generalized log canonical rational pairs over such fields admit minimal models for pseudo-effective adjoints and Mori fiber spaces otherwise, with nef b-data fixed.","pdfPath":"preprints/Numerical-semiampleness-of-nef-adjoint-classes-on-compact-Kahler-manifolds-October-6-2026/numerical-generalized-abundance.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Numerical-semiampleness-of-nef-adjoint-classes-on-compact-Kahler-manifolds-October-6-2026/numerical-generalized-abundance.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Numerical-semiampleness-of-nef-adjoint-classes-on-compact-Kahler-manifolds-October-6-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Numerical-semiampleness-of-nef-adjoint-classes-on-compact-Kahler-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Numerical-semiampleness-of-nef-adjoint-classes-on-compact-Kahler-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Numerical-semiampleness-of-nef-adjoint-classes-on-compact-Kahler-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Numerical-semiampleness-of-nef-adjoint-classes-on-compact-Kahler-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/02-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Numerical-semiampleness-of-nef-adjoint-classes-on-compact-Kahler-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/03-descent.tex","preprints/Numerical-semiampleness-of-nef-adjoint-classes-on-compact-Kahler-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/04-adjoint.tex","preprints/Numerical-semiampleness-of-nef-adjoint-classes-on-compact-Kahler-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/05-zero.tex","preprints/Numerical-semiampleness-of-nef-adjoint-classes-on-compact-Kahler-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/figures/zero-two-diagonals.tex","preprints/Numerical-semiampleness-of-nef-adjoint-classes-on-compact-Kahler-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/06-cycles.tex","preprints/Numerical-semiampleness-of-nef-adjoint-classes-on-compact-Kahler-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/07-completion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Numerical-semiampleness-of-nef-adjoint-classes-on-compact-Kahler-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let \\(X\\) be a smooth connected compact K\\\"ahler manifold, let \\(B\\) be an effective rational simple normal crossing divisor with coefficients less than one, and let \\(M\\) be a nef rational holomorphic line bundle on \\(X\\). If \\(K_X+B\\) is pseudo-effective and \\(K_X+B+M\\) is nef, we prove that its first Chern class in real Bott--Chern cohomology is represented by a semiample rational line bundle. This numerical statement allows a flat change of line bundle; it does not assert semiampleness of the original adjoint.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":582},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and the inputs to the proof","sourceOffset":2382},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The stronger statement and the proof","sourceOffset":4780},{"kind":"section","title":"Rational lines and divisorial decompositions","sourceOffset":8845},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and changes of model","sourceOffset":9153},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The analytic negative part","sourceOffset":12955},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The ordinary K\\\"ahler input","sourceOffset":15690},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The projective input","sourceOffset":19390},{"kind":"section","title":"Descent of nef and flat lines","sourceOffset":21870},{"kind":"section","title":"An ordinary adjoint on the projective base","sourceOffset":33832},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The period input and the discriminant","sourceOffset":35327},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The root cover and its highest Hodge line","sourceOffset":39906}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(X\\) be a smooth connected compact K\\\"ahler manifold, let \\(B\\geq0\\) be a rational simple normal crossing divisor whose coefficients are strictly less than one, and let \\(M\\in\\Pic(X)\\otimes_{\\Z}\\Q\\). Assume that \\(K_X+B\\) is pseudo-effective, \\(M\\) is nef, and \\(D=K_X+B+M\\) is nef. Then there is a semiample rational line bundle \\(L\\) on \\(X\\) such that \\[ c_1(L)=c_1(D)\\quad\\text{in }H^{1,1}_{\\mathrm{BC}}(X,\\R). \\]","sourceOffset":1507,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:decomposition","text":"Let \\(T\\) be a smooth connected compact K\\\"ahler manifold, \\(B\\geq0\\) a rational simple normal crossing divisor with coefficients less than one, and \\(M\\) a nef rational line bundle. Suppose \\(J=K_T+B\\) is pseudo-effective. There are a smooth compact K\\\"ahler modification \\(\\mu:U\\to T\\), a semiample rational line \\(P\\) on \\(U\\), and an effective rational divisor \\(R\\) on \\(U\\), such that \\[ \\mu^*(J+M)\\num P+R,\\qquad R=N\\bigl(c_1(\\mu^*(J+M))\\bigr). \\] Here \\(\\num\\) denotes equality of real Chern classes. Moreover, if \\(a(T)=0\\), then \\(c_1(M)=0\\).","sourceOffset":5524,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":172478,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":15,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Numerical-semiampleness-of-nef-adjoint-classes-on-compact-Kahler-manifolds-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"036-02","slug":"Numerical-Semiampleness-of-Nef-Adjoint-Divisors-October-3-2026","familyId":"036","title":"Numerical Semiampleness of Nef Adjoint Divisors","abstract":"We prove the Generalised Abundance Conjecture: if $(X,B)$ is a projective klt ℚ-pair over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, $K_X+B$ is pseudo-effective, M is a nef ℚ-Cartier divisor on X, and $K_X+B+M$ is nef, then $K_X+B+M$ is numerically equivalent to a semiample ℚ-Cartier divisor on X.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves numerical semiampleness for nef adjoints $K_X+B+M$ with $K_X+B$ pseudo-effective and M nef rational, for projective klt rational pairs over algebraically closed characteristic-zero fields and smooth compact Kähler rational klt simple-normal-crossing pairs, using Bott–Chern cohomology in the latter case. Separately, projective generalized log canonical rational pairs over such fields admit minimal models for pseudo-effective adjoints and Mori fiber spaces otherwise, with nef b-data fixed.","pdfPath":"preprints/Numerical-Semiampleness-of-Nef-Adjoint-Divisors-October-3-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Numerical-Semiampleness-of-Nef-Adjoint-Divisors-October-3-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Numerical-Semiampleness-of-Nef-Adjoint-Divisors-October-3-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Numerical-Semiampleness-of-Nef-Adjoint-Divisors-October-3-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Numerical-Semiampleness-of-Nef-Adjoint-Divisors-October-3-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Numerical-Semiampleness-of-Nef-Adjoint-Divisors-October-3-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Numerical-Semiampleness-of-Nef-Adjoint-Divisors-October-3-2026/build/sections/reduction.tex","preprints/Numerical-Semiampleness-of-Nef-Adjoint-Divisors-October-3-2026/build/sections/jet-tools.tex","preprints/Numerical-Semiampleness-of-Nef-Adjoint-Divisors-October-3-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Numerical-Semiampleness-of-Nef-Adjoint-Divisors-October-3-2026/build/sections/frobenius.tex","preprints/Numerical-Semiampleness-of-Nef-Adjoint-Divisors-October-3-2026/build/sections/completion.tex","preprints/Numerical-Semiampleness-of-Nef-Adjoint-Divisors-October-3-2026/build/sections/positive.tex","preprints/Numerical-Semiampleness-of-Nef-Adjoint-Divisors-October-3-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Numerical-Semiampleness-of-Nef-Adjoint-Divisors-October-3-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Generalised Abundance Conjecture: if $(X,B)$ is a projective klt $\\Q$-pair over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, $K_X+B$ is pseudo-effective, $M$ is a nef $\\Q$-Cartier divisor on $X$, and $K_X+B+M$ is nef, then $K_X+B+M$ is numerically equivalent to a semiample $\\Q$-Cartier divisor on $X$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":412},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and the role of the present argument","sourceOffset":3015},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Structure of the proof","sourceOffset":6663},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and birational inputs","sourceOffset":8659},{"kind":"section","title":"Reduction of full nef dimension to Calabi--Yau pairs","sourceOffset":13309},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The induction statements","sourceOffset":13851},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Keeping a semiample adjoint fixed","sourceOffset":16350},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positive Iitaka dimension and the remaining case","sourceOffset":26080},{"kind":"section","title":"Bounded-degree curves and moving jet kernels","sourceOffset":30181},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The bounded-degree obstruction","sourceOffset":30602},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Subadjunction and high-multiplicity components","sourceOffset":35957},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Full jet kernels and restriction to a fiber","sourceOffset":39345}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $(X,B)$ be a projective klt $\\Q$-pair over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero. Suppose that $K_X+B$ is pseudo-effective, and let $M$ be a nef $\\Q$-Cartier divisor on $X$. If \\[ D=K_X+B+M \\] is nef, then there is a semiample $\\Q$-Cartier divisor $L$ on $X$ such that $D\\equiv L$.","sourceOffset":1220,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:cy","text":"Let $(X,B)$ be a projective klt $\\Q$-pair in characteristic zero with $K_X+B\\equiv0$. Every nef $\\Q$-Cartier divisor on $X$ is numerically semiample.","sourceOffset":2673,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":141153,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Numerical-Semiampleness-of-Nef-Adjoint-Divisors-October-3-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"036-03","slug":"Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026","familyId":"036","title":"Minimal models and Mori fibre spaces for generalized log canonical Q-pairs","abstract":"We resolve the existence form of the minimal-model conjecture for projective generalized log canonical ℚ-pairs over algebraically closed fields of characteristic zero. Such a pair admits a minimal model when its adjoint divisor is pseudo-effective, and a Mori fibre space otherwise, while keeping the nef ℚ-Cartier b-divisor fixed. Taking the nef part to be zero gives the corresponding result for ordinary log canonical ℚ-pairs.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves numerical semiampleness for nef adjoints $K_X+B+M$ with $K_X+B$ pseudo-effective and M nef rational, for projective klt rational pairs over algebraically closed characteristic-zero fields and smooth compact Kähler rational klt simple-normal-crossing pairs, using Bott–Chern cohomology in the latter case. Separately, projective generalized log canonical rational pairs over such fields admit minimal models for pseudo-effective adjoints and Mori fiber spaces otherwise, with nef b-data fixed.","pdfPath":"preprints/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-conventions.tex","preprints/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-global.tex","preprints/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-cones.tex","preprints/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-product.tex","preprints/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-valuative.tex","preprints/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-cartier.tex","preprints/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-rounding.tex","preprints/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-integral-jump.tex","preprints/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-tower.tex","preprints/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-bounded-presentations.tex","preprints/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026/build/sections/11-traits.tex","preprints/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026/build/sections/12-incidence.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We resolve the existence form of the minimal-model conjecture for projective generalized log canonical $\\Q$-pairs over algebraically closed fields of characteristic zero. Such a pair admits a minimal model when its adjoint divisor is pseudo-effective, and a Mori fibre space otherwise, while keeping the nef $\\Q$-Cartier b-divisor fixed. Taking the nef part to be zero gives the corresponding result for ordinary log canonical $\\Q$-pairs.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":537},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and significance","sourceOffset":4914},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The local input and the global contradiction","sourceOffset":8898},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform estimates and the structure of the proof","sourceOffset":10218},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and preliminary notation","sourceOffset":14781},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pairs, nef data and discrepancies","sourceOffset":14852},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Homogeneous valuations and volume forms","sourceOffset":16095},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positivity, bounds and limits","sourceOffset":20398},{"kind":"section","title":"From local phases to minimal models","sourceOffset":21405},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Termination of smooth canonical programs","sourceOffset":23588},{"kind":"subsubsection","title":"A uniform multiplier-ideal estimate","sourceOffset":29248},{"kind":"subsubsection","title":"Countability of finite limiting changes","sourceOffset":31433}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, and let $(X,B+M)$ be a projective generalized log canonical $\\Q$-pair. Thus $X$ is normal and integral, $B$ is an effective rational boundary, and $M$ is the trace of a nef rational Cartier divisor on a higher projective birational model, with $K_X+B+M$ $\\Q$-Cartier. There are a normal integral projective variety $Y$ and a birational map $\\phi\\colon X\\dashrightarrow Y$ whose inverse contracts no divisors such that, writing $B_Y=\\phi_*B$ and taking $M_Y$ to be the trace of the same nef b-divisor, the generalized pair $(Y,B_Y+M_Y)$ is log canonical and \\[ A_{X,B+M}(E)\\le A_{Y,B_Y+M_Y}(E) \\] for every prime divisor $E$ over their common function field. The inequality is strict for every divisor on $X$ contracted by $\\phi$. More precisely, the following alternatives are determined by $D=K_X+B+M$: \\begin{enumerate} \\item if $D$ is pseudo-effective, then $K_Y+B_Y+M_Y$ is nef; \\item if $D$ is not pseudo-effective, there is a projective contraction $h\\colon Y\\to T$ to a normal projective variety with connected fibres such that \\[ \\dim T<\\dim Y,\\qquad \\rho(Y/T)=1, \\qquad -(K_Y+B_Y+M_Y)\\text{ is ample over }T. \\] \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":1332,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Ordinary log canonical pairs","label":"cor:ordinary","text":"Let $(X,B)$ be a projective log canonical $\\Q$-pair over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero. There is a birational map $\\phi:X\\dashrightarrow Y$ extracting no divisors, with $B_Y=\\phi_*B$, such that $(Y,B_Y)$ is log canonical and \\[ A_{X,B}(E)\\leq A_{Y,B_Y}(E) \\] for every prime divisor over the common function field, strictly for prime divisors on $X$ contracted by $\\phi$. If $K_X+B$ is pseudo-effective, $K_Y+B_Y$ is nef; otherwise $Y$ admits a Mori fibre contraction with relative Picard number one and relatively ample $-(K_Y+B_Y)$.","sourceOffset":3128,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":313562,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Minimal-models-and-Mori-fibre-spaces-for-generalized-log-canonical-Q-pairs-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"036-04","slug":"Minimal-models-in-numerical-dimension-one-September-24-2026","familyId":"036","title":"Minimal models in numerical dimension one","abstract":"We resolve the numerical-dimension-one case of the minimal-model conjecture for smooth connected complex projective varieties of dimension at least three. If KX is pseudo-effective and $\\kappa_\\sigma(X,K_X)=1$, with κσ defined by section growth with a fixed ample twist, then X admits a projective ℚ-factorial terminal minimal model.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves numerical semiampleness for nef adjoints $K_X+B+M$ with $K_X+B$ pseudo-effective and M nef rational, for projective klt rational pairs over algebraically closed characteristic-zero fields and smooth compact Kähler rational klt simple-normal-crossing pairs, using Bott–Chern cohomology in the latter case. Separately, projective generalized log canonical rational pairs over such fields admit minimal models for pseudo-effective adjoints and Mori fiber spaces otherwise, with nef b-data fixed.","pdfPath":"preprints/Minimal-models-in-numerical-dimension-one-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Minimal-models-in-numerical-dimension-one-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Minimal-models-in-numerical-dimension-one-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Minimal-models-in-numerical-dimension-one-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Minimal-models-in-numerical-dimension-one-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Minimal-models-in-numerical-dimension-one-September-24-2026/build/sections/mmp.tex","preprints/Minimal-models-in-numerical-dimension-one-September-24-2026/build/sections/numerics.tex","preprints/Minimal-models-in-numerical-dimension-one-September-24-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Minimal-models-in-numerical-dimension-one-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We resolve the numerical-dimension-one case of the minimal-model conjecture for smooth connected complex projective varieties of dimension at least three. If $K_X$ is pseudo-effective and $\\kappa_\\sigma(X,K_X)=1$, with $\\kappa_\\sigma$ defined by section growth with a fixed ample twist, then $X$ admits a projective $\\mathbb Q$-factorial terminal minimal model.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":407},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The invariant and the main theorem","sourceOffset":1513},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The problem and the methods in the literature","sourceOffset":4863},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The route through the proof","sourceOffset":9116},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":11843},{"kind":"section","title":"Positive perturbations on a fixed model","sourceOffset":12900},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Discrepancies and codimension two","sourceOffset":14580},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Construction of the perturbations","sourceOffset":22669},{"kind":"section","title":"Two surface tests for nefness","sourceOffset":28722},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A fixed twist detects positive surface square","sourceOffset":30192},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Negative degree forces ambient vanishing","sourceOffset":35175},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A fixed exceptional curve forces a positive square","sourceOffset":38505}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"main:theorem","text":"Let \\(X\\) be a smooth connected complex projective variety of dimension \\(n\\geq3\\). Suppose that \\(K_X\\) is pseudo-effective and \\(\\kappa_\\sigma(X,K_X)=1\\), with the convention \\eqref{main:sigma}. Then there are a normal projective \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-factorial terminal variety \\(Y\\) with \\(K_Y\\) nef and a finite sequence \\(\\phi:X\\dashrightarrow Y\\) of \\(K\\)-negative divisorial contractions and flips. The inverse of \\(\\phi\\) contracts no prime divisor. On a common smooth projective resolution \\(p:W\\to X\\), \\(q:W\\to Y\\), compatible canonical divisors satisfy \\begin{equation} p^*K_X=q^*K_Y+E, \\end{equation} where \\(E\\) is an effective \\(q\\)-exceptional \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-divisor whose support contains the strict transform of every prime divisor of \\(X\\) contracted by \\(\\phi\\).","sourceOffset":2784,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Good minimal model","label":"cor:good-model","text":"Under the hypotheses of Theorem \\ref{main:theorem}, the same \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-factorial terminal endpoint \\(Y\\) has semiample \\(K_Y\\); hence \\(Y\\) is a good minimal model of \\(X\\).","sourceOffset":4397,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":50116,"sourceFileCount":5,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Minimal-models-in-numerical-dimension-one-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"037-01","slug":"The-ordinary-double-point-gap-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026","familyId":"037","title":"The ordinary-double-point gap in every dimension","abstract":"We prove the ordinary-double-point gap conjecture for boundary-zero complex algebraic klt germs in every dimension: a singular n-dimensional germ has normalized volume at most $2(n-1)^n$, with equality precisely at an analytic ordinary double point.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the ordinary-double-point volume-gap conjecture: every singular complex algebraic klt germ of dimension n ≥ 2, with zero boundary, has normalized volume at most $2(n-1)^n$. Equality holds precisely for an analytic ordinary double point.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-ordinary-double-point-gap-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-ordinary-double-point-gap-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-ordinary-double-point-gap-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/The-ordinary-double-point-gap-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-ordinary-double-point-gap-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-ordinary-double-point-gap-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-ordinary-double-point-gap-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-route.tex","preprints/The-ordinary-double-point-gap-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-cone-reduction.tex","preprints/The-ordinary-double-point-gap-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-small-stabilizers.tex","preprints/The-ordinary-double-point-gap-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-jet-interiority.tex","preprints/The-ordinary-double-point-gap-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-adjoint-semigroup.tex","preprints/The-ordinary-double-point-gap-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-filtrations.tex","preprints/The-ordinary-double-point-gap-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-bootstrap.tex","preprints/The-ordinary-double-point-gap-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-ordinary-double-point-gap-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the ordinary-double-point gap conjecture for boundary-zero complex algebraic klt germs in every dimension: a singular \\(n\\)-dimensional germ has normalized volume at most \\(2(n-1)^n\\), with equality precisely at an analytic ordinary double point.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":318},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier results","sourceOffset":3921},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The argument and its additional statements","sourceOffset":10254},{"kind":"section","title":"Normalized volume and the cone reduction","sourceOffset":16831},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and public inputs","sourceOffset":16900},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Products and embedding dimension","sourceOffset":20480},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The minimum at the ordinary double point","sourceOffset":22952},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The induction setup","sourceOffset":25816},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Integral gradings and orbifold quotients","sourceOffset":31834},{"kind":"section","title":"Small stabilizers","sourceOffset":37806},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A minimal orbifold rational curve","sourceOffset":39921},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite evaluation and its symmetric tensor","sourceOffset":45141}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"The ordinary double point gap","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(n\\ge2\\), and let \\(x\\) be a singular closed point of a normal complex algebraic \\(n\\)-fold \\(X\\). Suppose that \\(K_X\\) is \\(\\QQ\\)-Cartier and that \\(X\\) is klt near \\(x\\), with zero boundary. Then \\[ \\nvol(x,X)\\le2(n-1)^n. \\] Equality holds if and only if \\[ (X,x)_{\\mathrm{an}}\\cong \\left(\\{z_0^2+\\cdots+z_n^2=0\\}\\subset\\CC^{n+1},0\\right). \\]","sourceOffset":1890,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Global volume bounds","label":"cor:global","text":"Let \\(V\\) be an \\(n\\)-dimensional K-semistable \\(\\QQ\\)-Fano variety, where \\(n\\ge2\\). If \\(V\\) is singular, then \\[ (-K_V)^n\\le 2\\left(\\frac{n^2-1}{n}\\right)^n<2n^n. \\] If \\(V\\not\\cong\\PP^n\\), then \\((-K_V)^n\\le2n^n\\), with equality precisely when \\(V\\) is a smooth quadric \\(Q^n\\subset\\PP^{n+1}\\) or \\(\\PP^1\\times\\PP^{n-1}\\). These two descriptions coincide for \\(n=2\\).","sourceOffset":3202,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":178123,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":15,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-ordinary-double-point-gap-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"037-02","slug":"The-normalized-volume-gap-in-dimension-four-September-24-2026","familyId":"037","title":"The normalized-volume gap in dimension four","abstract":"We prove that every singular complex algebraic klt fourfold germ with zero boundary has normalized volume at most 162, with equality precisely for an analytic ordinary double point. This resolves the ordinary-double-point volume-gap conjecture in dimension four.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the ordinary-double-point volume-gap conjecture: every singular complex algebraic klt germ of dimension n ≥ 2, with zero boundary, has normalized volume at most $2(n-1)^n$. Equality holds precisely for an analytic ordinary double point.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-normalized-volume-gap-in-dimension-four-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-normalized-volume-gap-in-dimension-four-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-normalized-volume-gap-in-dimension-four-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/The-normalized-volume-gap-in-dimension-four-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-normalized-volume-gap-in-dimension-four-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every singular complex algebraic klt fourfold germ with zero boundary has normalized volume at most \\(162\\), with equality precisely for an analytic ordinary double point. This resolves the ordinary-double-point volume-gap conjecture in dimension four.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":312},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier results and the remaining obstacle","sourceOffset":2805},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Main ideas and proof structure","sourceOffset":6457},{"kind":"section","title":"Volume conventions and the cone reduction","sourceOffset":11327},{"kind":"section","title":"Stabilizers and discrepancies","sourceOffset":19674},{"kind":"section","title":"Hilbert series and short weights","sourceOffset":29441},{"kind":"section","title":"The log canonical threshold","sourceOffset":43592},{"kind":"section","title":"A terminal threefold section and the main theorem","sourceOffset":51885}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(x\\in X\\) be a singular closed point of a normal complex algebraic variety of dimension four. Suppose that \\(K_X\\) is \\(\\Q\\)-Cartier and that \\(X\\) is klt near \\(x\\), with boundary zero. Then \\[ \\nvol(x,X)\\le 162. \\] Equality holds if and only if the analytic germ \\((X,x)\\) is isomorphic to \\[ \\left(\\left\\{z_0^2+z_1^2+z_2^2+z_3^2+z_4^2=0\\right\\}\\subset\\C^5,0\\right). \\]","sourceOffset":2040,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:cone","text":"Suppose that \\(x\\in X\\) satisfies the hypotheses of Theorem \\ref{thm:main} and that \\[ V:=\\nvol(x,X)\\ge162. \\] There is a positively graded affine fourfold \\(C=\\Spec S\\), with vertex \\(o\\), such that: \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\alph*)] \\item \\(C\\) is Gorenstein and klt, and \\(C\\setminus\\{o\\}\\) is smooth; \\item \\(\\nvol(o,C)=V\\), and \\(\\edim(x,X)\\le\\edim(o,C)\\); \\item an effective torus acts on \\(C\\), with a minimizing Reeb vector \\(\\xi_*\\). Primitive positive integral gradings along rational rays approaching \\(\\xi_*\\) have degree valuations of discrepancies \\(r\\in\\Z_{>0}\\) and volumes \\(h>0\\), with \\[ r^4h\\ge V,\\qquad r^4h\\longrightarrow V. \\] For each such grading, the canonical module has a homogeneous generator of degree \\(r\\). \\end{enumerate} Moreover, \\(o\\) is singular.","sourceOffset":16024,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":62849,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-normalized-volume-gap-in-dimension-four-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"038-01","slug":"Fujitas-freeness-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"038","title":"Fujita's freeness conjecture","abstract":"We prove Fujita's freeness conjecture. If X is a smooth complex projective variety of dimension n and L is an ample line bundle, then $K_X+mL$ is globally generated for every integer $m\\geq n+1$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Fujita's freeness conjecture at its sharp bound in every dimension: for a smooth projective complex variety X of dimension n and an ample line bundle L, the adjoint $K_X+mL$ is globally generated for every integer $m\\ge n+1$.","pdfPath":"preprints/Fujitas-freeness-conjecture-September-23-2026/Fujitas-freeness-conjecture-September-23-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Fujitas-freeness-conjecture-September-23-2026/Fujitas-freeness-conjecture-September-23-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Fujitas-freeness-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Fujitas-freeness-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Fujitas-freeness-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/proof-route.tex","preprints/Fujitas-freeness-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Fujitas-freeness-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/minimization.tex","preprints/Fujitas-freeness-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/centers.tex","preprints/Fujitas-freeness-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/isolation.tex","preprints/Fujitas-freeness-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Fujitas-freeness-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Fujita's freeness conjecture. If $X$ is a smooth complex projective variety of dimension $n$ and $L$ is an ample line bundle, then $K_X+mL$ is globally generated for every integer $m\\geq n+1$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":247},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and methods","sourceOffset":1543},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof","sourceOffset":4504},{"kind":"section","title":"Orders, thresholds, and vanishing","sourceOffset":8404},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weighted orders","sourceOffset":8913},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Thresholds in families","sourceOffset":13962},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Vanishing and descent","sourceOffset":16751},{"kind":"section","title":"The ordinary-order alternative","sourceOffset":18600},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite-degree exponential minimization","sourceOffset":29985},{"kind":"section","title":"Eliminating positive-dimensional centers","sourceOffset":43830},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A uniform estimate on restriction spaces","sourceOffset":44845},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A minimizing test and its tangent directions","sourceOffset":48576}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $X$ be a smooth connected projective variety over $\\C$ of dimension $n\\geq 1$, and let $L$ be an ample line bundle on $X$. Then \\[ K_X+(n+1)L \\] is generated by its global sections.","sourceOffset":618,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Kawamata--Viehweg vanishing","label":"thm:kv","text":"Let $T$ be smooth and projective over $\\C$. Let $\\Delta$ be an effective rational SNC divisor with coefficients in $[0,1)$, and let $D$ be an integral divisor. If $D-(K_T+\\Delta)$ is nef and big, then $H^i(T,\\cO_T(D))=0$ for every $i>0$.","sourceOffset":16943,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":88220,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Fujitas-freeness-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"039-01","slug":"Nagatas-Conjecture-for-Plane-Curves-September-23-2026","familyId":"039","title":"Nagata's conjecture for plane curves","abstract":"We prove that a nonzero effective plane curve of degree d at r ≥ 10 very general complex points has total multiplicity strictly less than $d\\sqrt r$. The inequality holds simultaneously for all curves, including reducible and nonreduced curves, and establishes Nagata's conjecture in its strict, nonhomogeneous form.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Nagata's strict inequality $\\sum_i m_i\\lt d\\sqrt r$ for every nonzero effective plane curve of degree d through r ≥ 10 very general complex points, with arbitrary multiplicities mi. It also proves maximal multipoint Seshadri constants $(L^n/r)^{1/n}$ for every smooth polarized projective variety of dimension n ≥ 2 and all sufficiently large r: at very general points over ℂ, and at the geometric generic tuple over any algebraically closed field of positive characteristic.","pdfPath":"preprints/Nagatas-Conjecture-for-Plane-Curves-September-23-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Nagatas-Conjecture-for-Plane-Curves-September-23-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Nagatas-Conjecture-for-Plane-Curves-September-23-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Nagatas-Conjecture-for-Plane-Curves-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Nagatas-Conjecture-for-Plane-Curves-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Nagatas-Conjecture-for-Plane-Curves-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-reductions.tex","preprints/Nagatas-Conjecture-for-Plane-Curves-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-elliptic.tex","preprints/Nagatas-Conjecture-for-Plane-Curves-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-limits.tex","preprints/Nagatas-Conjecture-for-Plane-Curves-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-interpolation.tex","preprints/Nagatas-Conjecture-for-Plane-Curves-September-23-2026/build/figures/slice-profile.tex","preprints/Nagatas-Conjecture-for-Plane-Curves-September-23-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Nagatas-Conjecture-for-Plane-Curves-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that a nonzero effective plane curve of degree $d$ at $r\\ge10$ very general complex points has total multiplicity strictly less than $d\\sqrt r$. The inequality holds simultaneously for all curves, including reducible and nonreduced curves, and establishes Nagata's conjecture in its strict, nonhomogeneous form.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":366},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work and the interpolation approach","sourceOffset":2984},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":5855},{"kind":"section","title":"From plane curves to normal polynomials","sourceOffset":8218},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A universal equal-multiplicity system","sourceOffset":8991},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Specialization to a normal bundle","sourceOffset":12593},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The square case","sourceOffset":17951},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Nine zero displacements on a cubic","sourceOffset":21702},{"kind":"section","title":"Polynomial sections on an elliptic curve","sourceOffset":24120},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removing the nine prescribed coefficient zeros","sourceOffset":24886},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Theta functions and plane cubics","sourceOffset":28473},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Choosing the cubic and marked points","sourceOffset":32907}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $r\\ge10$, there is a countable union $E_r$ of proper Zariski-closed subsets of $U_r$, with nonempty complement, such that the following holds for every tuple $(p_1,\\ldots,p_r)\\notin E_r$. If $C$ is a nonzero effective plane curve of degree $d\\ge1$ and $m_1,\\ldots,m_r\\in\\Z_{\\ge0}$ satisfy $\\mult_{p_i}C\\ge m_i$, then \\[ d\\sqrt r>\\sum_{i=1}^r m_i. \\]","sourceOffset":1997,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"A necessary normal polynomial","label":"prop:normal-polynomial","text":"Suppose that $\\mathsf U_r(d,m)$ holds. For every choice of distinct points $a_1,\\ldots,a_r\\in B$ and arbitrary vectors $c_i\\in M_{a_i}$, there is a nonzero polynomial \\begin{equation} F(w)=\\sum_{j=0}^{\\lfloor d/k\\rfloor}f_jw^j, \\qquad f_j\\in H^0(B,L^{d-kj}), \\end{equation} having multiplicity at least $m$ at each $(a_i,c_i)\\in\\Tot(M)$. Its coefficients may be taken to be algebraic sections.","sourceOffset":13320,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/039.md","scopeText":"Nagata's conjecture asserts that a nonzero effective plane curve of degree $d$, with multiplicities at least $m_i$ at $r\\ge10$ very general points, satisfies $\\sum_i m_i<d\\sqrt r$.\n\nThe formalization establishes this inequality simultaneously for all curves and multiplicity vectors outside one countable union of proper Zariski-closed exceptional sets with nonempty complement. Reducible curves, repeated components, and unequal multiplicities are included, in both effective-curve and homogeneous-polynomial formulations. The formalization also includes the passage from homogeneous effective cycles to equations.\n\nThe maximality question asks whether the multipoint Seshadri constant reaches the upper bound $\\sqrt{L^2/r}$ at very general points once $r$ is sufficiently large. The formalized resu","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/039.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":70218,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Nagatas-Conjecture-for-Plane-Curves-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"039-02","slug":"Maximal-Seshadri-Constants-on-Arbitrary-Polarized-Surfaces-September-23-2026","familyId":"039","title":"Maximal Seshadri constants on arbitrary polarized surfaces","abstract":"We prove that, for every smooth integral complex projective surface S and every ample line bundle L, the multipoint Seshadri constant at r very general points equals $\\sqrt{L^2/r}$ for every sufficiently large integer r. This resolves positively the qualitative Nagata–Biran conjecture for surfaces.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Nagata's strict inequality $\\sum_i m_i\\lt d\\sqrt r$ for every nonzero effective plane curve of degree d through r ≥ 10 very general complex points, with arbitrary multiplicities mi. It also proves maximal multipoint Seshadri constants $(L^n/r)^{1/n}$ for every smooth polarized projective variety of dimension n ≥ 2 and all sufficiently large r: at very general points over ℂ, and at the geometric generic tuple over any algebraically closed field of positive characteristic.","pdfPath":"preprints/Maximal-Seshadri-Constants-on-Arbitrary-Polarized-Surfaces-September-23-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Maximal-Seshadri-Constants-on-Arbitrary-Polarized-Surfaces-September-23-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Maximal-Seshadri-Constants-on-Arbitrary-Polarized-Surfaces-September-23-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Maximal-Seshadri-Constants-on-Arbitrary-Polarized-Surfaces-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Maximal-Seshadri-Constants-on-Arbitrary-Polarized-Surfaces-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Maximal-Seshadri-Constants-on-Arbitrary-Polarized-Surfaces-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Maximal-Seshadri-Constants-on-Arbitrary-Polarized-Surfaces-September-23-2026/build/sections/jets.tex","preprints/Maximal-Seshadri-Constants-on-Arbitrary-Polarized-Surfaces-September-23-2026/build/sections/node.tex","preprints/Maximal-Seshadri-Constants-on-Arbitrary-Polarized-Surfaces-September-23-2026/build/sections/compression.tex","preprints/Maximal-Seshadri-Constants-on-Arbitrary-Polarized-Surfaces-September-23-2026/build/figures/compression.tex","preprints/Maximal-Seshadri-Constants-on-Arbitrary-Polarized-Surfaces-September-23-2026/build/sections/lattice.tex","preprints/Maximal-Seshadri-Constants-on-Arbitrary-Polarized-Surfaces-September-23-2026/build/figures/triangle.tex","preprints/Maximal-Seshadri-Constants-on-Arbitrary-Polarized-Surfaces-September-23-2026/build/sections/nef.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Maximal-Seshadri-Constants-on-Arbitrary-Polarized-Surfaces-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that, for every smooth integral complex projective surface $S$ and every ample line bundle $L$, the multipoint Seshadri constant at $r$ very general points equals $\\sqrt{L^2/r}$ for every sufficiently large integer $r$. This resolves positively the qualitative Nagata--Biran conjecture for surfaces.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":371},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background","sourceOffset":2983},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":6089},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite jet tests and initial monomials","sourceOffset":9395},{"kind":"section","title":"A nodal divisor and its exponent diagram","sourceOffset":18570},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compressing diagonal slices","sourceOffset":26764},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite subgroups as jet tests","sourceOffset":35501},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A multiplicity estimate for triangles","sourceOffset":35945},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The subgroup test","sourceOffset":41035},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A direction for every sufficiently large integer","sourceOffset":44539},{"kind":"section","title":"From homogeneous jets to nefness","sourceOffset":47490},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Extension of the ground field","sourceOffset":56891}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every smooth integral complex projective surface $S$ and every ample line bundle $L$ on $S$, there is an integer $r_0=r_0(S,L)$ with the following property. For each integer $r\\ge r_0$, there is a countable union $Z_r$ of proper Zariski-closed subsets of $U_r$, with nonempty complement, such that for every $\\mathbf p\\in U_r\\setminus Z_r$ the class \\[ \\pi^*L-\\sqrt{\\frac{L^2}{r}}\\sum_{i=1}^r E_i \\] is nef on the blow-up at $\\mathbf p$. Consequently, \\[ \\varepsilon(S,L;\\mathbf p)=\\sqrt{\\frac{L^2}{r}}. \\]","sourceOffset":1647,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Transfer from initial monomials","label":"prop:initial-transfer","text":"Let $F\\subset\\C\\{x,z\\}$ be a finite-dimensional complex vector space of convergent holomorphic germs, and let $t>0$. The set $E_t(F)$ has exactly $\\dim F$ elements, and $F$ admits a basis whose initial exponents are these distinct elements. Let $r,m$ be positive integers. If the monomial space \\[ \\Span_{\\C}\\{X^\\alpha Z^\\beta:(\\alpha,\\beta)\\in E_t(F)\\} \\] has an injective order-$m$ jet test at some distinct points $q_1,\\ldots,q_r\\in\\T$, then representatives of $F$ on a common neighborhood of the origin have an injective order-$m$ jet test at distinct points arbitrarily close to the origin.","sourceOffset":13122,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/039.md","scopeText":"Nagata's conjecture asserts that a nonzero effective plane curve of degree $d$, with multiplicities at least $m_i$ at $r\\ge10$ very general points, satisfies $\\sum_i m_i<d\\sqrt r$.\n\nThe formalization establishes this inequality simultaneously for all curves and multiplicity vectors outside one countable union of proper Zariski-closed exceptional sets with nonempty complement. Reducible curves, repeated components, and unequal multiplicities are included, in both effective-curve and homogeneous-polynomial formulations. The formalization also includes the passage from homogeneous effective cycles to equations.\n\nThe maximality question asks whether the multipoint Seshadri constant reaches the upper bound $\\sqrt{L^2/r}$ at very general points once $r$ is sufficiently large. The formalized resu","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/039.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":60029,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Maximal-Seshadri-Constants-on-Arbitrary-Polarized-Surfaces-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"039-03","slug":"Maximal-Multipoint-Seshadri-Constants-in-Higher-Dimensions-October-5-2026","familyId":"039","title":"Maximal Multipoint Seshadri Constants in Higher Dimensions","abstract":"Let L be an ample line bundle on a smooth integral complex projective variety X of dimension n ≥ 3. We prove that there is a threshold $r_0=r_0(X,L)$ such that for every integer $r\\ge r_0$, the ordinary multipoint Seshadri constant at r very general points equals the volume bound $(L^n/r)^{1/n}$. This establishes the qualitative Nagata–Biran–Szemberg assertion in these dimensions.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Nagata's strict inequality $\\sum_i m_i\\lt d\\sqrt r$ for every nonzero effective plane curve of degree d through r ≥ 10 very general complex points, with arbitrary multiplicities mi. It also proves maximal multipoint Seshadri constants $(L^n/r)^{1/n}$ for every smooth polarized projective variety of dimension n ≥ 2 and all sufficiently large r: at very general points over ℂ, and at the geometric generic tuple over any algebraically closed field of positive characteristic.","pdfPath":"preprints/Maximal-Multipoint-Seshadri-Constants-in-Higher-Dimensions-October-5-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Maximal-Multipoint-Seshadri-Constants-in-Higher-Dimensions-October-5-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Maximal-Multipoint-Seshadri-Constants-in-Higher-Dimensions-October-5-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Maximal-Multipoint-Seshadri-Constants-in-Higher-Dimensions-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Maximal-Multipoint-Seshadri-Constants-in-Higher-Dimensions-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Maximal-Multipoint-Seshadri-Constants-in-Higher-Dimensions-October-5-2026/build/sections/transfers.tex","preprints/Maximal-Multipoint-Seshadri-Constants-in-Higher-Dimensions-October-5-2026/build/sections/flag.tex","preprints/Maximal-Multipoint-Seshadri-Constants-in-Higher-Dimensions-October-5-2026/build/sections/reshaping.tex","preprints/Maximal-Multipoint-Seshadri-Constants-in-Higher-Dimensions-October-5-2026/build/figures/reshaping.tex","preprints/Maximal-Multipoint-Seshadri-Constants-in-Higher-Dimensions-October-5-2026/build/sections/saturation.tex","preprints/Maximal-Multipoint-Seshadri-Constants-in-Higher-Dimensions-October-5-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Maximal-Multipoint-Seshadri-Constants-in-Higher-Dimensions-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let $L$ be an ample line bundle on a smooth integral complex projective variety $X$ of dimension $n\\ge3$. We prove that there is a threshold $r_0=r_0(X,L)$ such that for every integer $r\\ge r_0$, the ordinary multipoint Seshadri constant at $r$ very general points equals the volume bound $(L^n/r)^{1/n}$. This establishes the qualitative Nagata--Biran--Szemberg assertion in these dimensions.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":439},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and relation to earlier work","sourceOffset":2544},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and the two-coordinate construction","sourceOffset":5741},{"kind":"section","title":"Monomial spaces and finite jets","sourceOffset":9428},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Jets and graded exponent sets","sourceOffset":9953},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Least terms and analytic limits","sourceOffset":11782},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Lattice changes and coordinate compression","sourceOffset":17997},{"kind":"section","title":"A simplex from a complete-intersection flag","sourceOffset":22084},{"kind":"section","title":"Reshaping two intercepts","sourceOffset":27864},{"kind":"section","title":"Interior lattice points and interpolation","sourceOffset":45187},{"kind":"section","title":"Proof of the main theorem","sourceOffset":51190},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exponent sets for every sufficiently large point count","sourceOffset":51585}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $X$ be a smooth integral complex projective variety of dimension $n\\ge3$, and let $L$ be an ample line bundle on $X$. There is an integer $r_0=r_0(X,L)\\ge1$ such that for every integer $r\\ge r_0$ there is a countable union $Z_r$ of proper Zariski-closed subsets of $U_r(X)$, with nonempty complement, for which \\[ \\eps(X,L;\\mathbf p)=\\left(\\frac{L^n}{r}\\right)^{1/n} \\qquad\\text{for every }\\mathbf p\\in U_r(X)\\setminus Z_r. \\]","sourceOffset":1500,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"The initial simplex","label":"prop:flag-simplex","text":"Let $X$ be a smooth integral complex projective variety of dimension $n\\geq3$, let $L$ be ample, and put $V=L^n$. Choose a positive integer $D$ such that $DL$ is very ample. There is a graded support family $S_k\\subset\\Z_{\\geq0}^n$ such that \\begin{align} S_k&\\subset kP_0, &P_0&=\\Delta(1/D,\\ldots,1/D,D^{n-1}V), \\\\ \\#S_k&=h^0(X,kL) =\\frac{V}{n!}k^n+O(k^{n-1}), &\\vol(P_0)&=\\frac{V}{n!}. \\end{align} For all integers $k\\geq1$, $m\\geq0$, and $r\\geq1$, if $\\M(S_k)$ separates jets through degree $m$ at some distinct $r$-tuple in $\\Torus^n$, then $H^0(X,kL)$ does so at some distinct $r$-tuple in $X$.","sourceOffset":23084,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:graded-support","text":"A graded support family in $\\Z^n$ is a sequence of finite sets $(S_k)_{k\\geq1}$ such that \\begin{equation} S_k+S_l\\subset S_{k+l}\\qquad(k,l\\geq1). \\end{equation} Empty sets are allowed. Equivalently, $\\M(S_k)\\M(S_l)\\subset\\M(S_{k+l})$, where a product of spaces denotes the span of their pairwise products.","sourceOffset":11006,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/039.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":60388,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Maximal-Multipoint-Seshadri-Constants-in-Higher-Dimensions-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"039-04","slug":"Maximal-multipoint-Seshadri-constants-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026","familyId":"039","title":"Maximal multipoint Seshadri constants in positive characteristic","abstract":"Let L be an ample line bundle on a smooth integral projective variety X over an algebraically closed field of positive characteristic, with $\\dim X=n\\ge3$. We prove that there is a threshold $r_0=r_0(X,L)$ such that for every integer $r\\ge r_0$, the ordinary multipoint Seshadri constant at the geometric generic tuple of r points equals the volume bound $(L^n/r)^{1/n}$. The same conclusion holds in dimension two. This establishes the positive-characteristic form of the qualitative Nagata–Biran–Szemberg assertion at geometric generic tuples.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Nagata's strict inequality $\\sum_i m_i\\lt d\\sqrt r$ for every nonzero effective plane curve of degree d through r ≥ 10 very general complex points, with arbitrary multiplicities mi. It also proves maximal multipoint Seshadri constants $(L^n/r)^{1/n}$ for every smooth polarized projective variety of dimension n ≥ 2 and all sufficiently large r: at very general points over ℂ, and at the geometric generic tuple over any algebraically closed field of positive characteristic.","pdfPath":"preprints/Maximal-multipoint-Seshadri-constants-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/seshadri-positive-characteristic.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Maximal-multipoint-Seshadri-constants-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/seshadri-positive-characteristic.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Maximal-multipoint-Seshadri-constants-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Maximal-multipoint-Seshadri-constants-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/build/main.tex","preprints/Maximal-multipoint-Seshadri-constants-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Maximal-multipoint-Seshadri-constants-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Maximal-multipoint-Seshadri-constants-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/build/sections/02-transfer.tex","preprints/Maximal-multipoint-Seshadri-constants-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/build/sections/03-flag.tex","preprints/Maximal-multipoint-Seshadri-constants-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/build/sections/04-reshape.tex","preprints/Maximal-multipoint-Seshadri-constants-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/build/sections/05-interpolation.tex","preprints/Maximal-multipoint-Seshadri-constants-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/build/sections/06-positivity.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Maximal-multipoint-Seshadri-constants-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026/build/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let $L$ be an ample line bundle on a smooth integral projective variety $X$ over an algebraically closed field of positive characteristic, with $\\dim X=n\\ge3$. We prove that there is a threshold $r_0=r_0(X,L)$ such that for every integer $r\\ge r_0$, the ordinary multipoint Seshadri constant at the geometric generic tuple of $r$ points equals the volume bound $(L^n/r)^{1/n}$. The same conclusion holds in dimension two. This establishes the positive-characteristic form of the qualitative Nagata--Biran--Szemberg assertion at geometric generic tuples.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":599},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the characteristic issue","sourceOffset":2722},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":5879},{"kind":"section","title":"Leading terms and transfer of jets","sourceOffset":8870},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Least exponents","sourceOffset":11167},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Formal specialization of jet matrices","sourceOffset":14401},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two elementary transformations","sourceOffset":18715},{"kind":"section","title":"An initial simplex from a flag","sourceOffset":21058},{"kind":"section","title":"Reshaping two intercepts","sourceOffset":25486},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two branches of a node","sourceOffset":27123},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A lattice direction of prescribed height","sourceOffset":30482},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Completion of the transformation","sourceOffset":34113}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $X/k$ be a smooth integral projective variety over an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p>0$, with $\\dim X=n\\ge3$, and let $L$ be ample. There is an integer $r_0=r_0(X,L)$ such that \\[ \\varepsilon_r(X,L)=\\left(\\frac{L^n}{r}\\right)^{1/n} \\qquad\\text{for every integer }r\\ge r_0. \\]","sourceOffset":1867,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:initial-simplex","text":"Let $X$ be a smooth integral projective $n$-dimensional variety over an algebraically closed field, with $n\\geq2$, and let $L$ be ample. Put $V=L^n$, and choose an integer $D\\geq1$ such that $DL$ is very ample. There is a graded family of finite sets $S_N\\subset\\Z_{\\geq0}^n$ such that \\begin{align} S_N&\\subset NP_0, &P_0&=\\Delta(1/D,\\ldots,1/D,D^{n-1}V), \\\\ \\#S_N&=h^0(X,NL) =\\frac{V}{n!}N^n+o(N^n). \\end{align} For every $N,r\\geq1$ and $m\\geq0$, if $M(S_N)$ separates jets through order $m$ at some $r$ distinct torus points, then $H^0(X,NL)$ separates the same jets at some $r$ distinct points of $X$.","sourceOffset":21687,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:graded","text":"A family of finite sets $(S_N)_{N\\geq1}$ in $\\Z^d$ is graded if \\[ S_N+S_{N'}\\subset S_{N+N'}\\qquad(N,N'\\geq1). \\] A transformation $(S_N)\\mapsto(T_N)$ has backward jet transfer if, for every $N,r\\geq1$ and $m\\geq0$, separation of jets through order $m$ by $M(T_N)$ at some $r$ distinct torus points implies the same assertion for $M(S_N)$, possibly at a different tuple.","sourceOffset":10725,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/039.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":53540,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Maximal-multipoint-Seshadri-constants-in-positive-characteristic-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"040-01","slug":"Blochs-Conjecture-for-Surfaces-with-pg-equals-q-equals-0-September-24-2026","familyId":"040","title":"Bloch’s conjecture for surfaces with p_g=0","abstract":"We prove Bloch's conjecture for smooth connected projective complex surfaces with $p_g=0$: the Albanese homomorphism on integral degree-zero zero-cycles is an isomorphism.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Bloch's conjecture: for every smooth connected projective complex surface S with $p_g(S)=0$, the Albanese map $\\mathrm{CH}_0(S)^0\\to\\mathrm{Alb}(S)(\\mathbb C)$ on integral degree-zero zero-cycles is an isomorphism. This combines the new $p_g=q=0$ theorem with the classical theorem of Bloch, Kas, and Lieberman.","pdfPath":"preprints/Blochs-Conjecture-for-Surfaces-with-pg-equals-q-equals-0-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Blochs-Conjecture-for-Surfaces-with-pg-equals-q-equals-0-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Blochs-Conjecture-for-Surfaces-with-pg-equals-q-equals-0-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Blochs-Conjecture-for-Surfaces-with-pg-equals-q-equals-0-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Blochs-Conjecture-for-Surfaces-with-pg-equals-q-equals-0-September-24-2026/build/unicode-map.tex","preprints/Blochs-Conjecture-for-Surfaces-with-pg-equals-q-equals-0-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Blochs-Conjecture-for-Surfaces-with-pg-equals-q-equals-0-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-flow.tex","preprints/Blochs-Conjecture-for-Surfaces-with-pg-equals-q-equals-0-September-24-2026/build/sections/reductions.tex","preprints/Blochs-Conjecture-for-Surfaces-with-pg-equals-q-equals-0-September-24-2026/build/sections/derived.tex","preprints/Blochs-Conjecture-for-Surfaces-with-pg-equals-q-equals-0-September-24-2026/build/sections/fixed.tex","preprints/Blochs-Conjecture-for-Surfaces-with-pg-equals-q-equals-0-September-24-2026/build/sections/relation.tex","preprints/Blochs-Conjecture-for-Surfaces-with-pg-equals-q-equals-0-September-24-2026/build/sections/nonrank.tex","preprints/Blochs-Conjecture-for-Surfaces-with-pg-equals-q-equals-0-September-24-2026/build/sections/rankone.tex","preprints/Blochs-Conjecture-for-Surfaces-with-pg-equals-q-equals-0-September-24-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex","preprints/Blochs-Conjecture-for-Surfaces-with-pg-equals-q-equals-0-September-24-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Blochs-Conjecture-for-Surfaces-with-pg-equals-q-equals-0-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Bloch's conjecture for smooth connected projective complex surfaces with \\(p_g=0\\): the Albanese homomorphism on integral degree-zero zero-cycles is an isomorphism.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":219},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and antecedents","sourceOffset":2365},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The mechanism of the proof","sourceOffset":6458},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Motivic consequences","sourceOffset":13550},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":16314},{"kind":"section","title":"Birational reductions","sourceOffset":17322},{"kind":"section","title":"The determinant-fixed Quot obstruction theory","sourceOffset":20155},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Derived arrows and classical families","sourceOffset":22857},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The based determinant and its amplitude","sourceOffset":31883},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Equivariant resolutions and the fixed theory","sourceOffset":40234},{"kind":"section","title":"Fixed virtual classes and the divisor cosection","sourceOffset":48558},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rank-one quotients in families","sourceOffset":49960}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:bloch","text":"Let \\(S\\) be a smooth connected projective surface over \\(\\mathbb C\\) with \\(p_g(S)=q(S)=0\\). Then \\[ \\deg:\\CH_0(S)\\longrightarrow\\mathbb Z \\] is an isomorphism, where \\(\\CH_0(S)\\) has integral coefficients.","sourceOffset":1017,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Bloch's conjecture for surfaces with \\(p_g=0\\)","label":"cor:bloch-pg-zero","text":"Let \\(S\\) be a smooth connected projective surface over \\(\\mathbb C\\) with \\(p_g(S)=0\\). Then the Albanese homomorphism \\[ \\operatorname{alb}_S:\\CH_0(S)^0\\longrightarrow\\Alb(S)(\\mathbb C) \\] is an isomorphism of abelian groups, where \\(\\CH_0(S)\\) has integral coefficients.","sourceOffset":1456,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":159180,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Blochs-Conjecture-for-Surfaces-with-pg-equals-q-equals-0-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Blochs-Conjecture-for-Surfaces-with-pg-equals-q-equals-0-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"041-01","slug":"Projective-Space-Bases-of-Lagrangian-Fibrations-September-23-2026","familyId":"041","title":"Projective-space bases of Lagrangian fibrations","abstract":"We prove that the normal projective base of a projective Lagrangian fibration from a compact irreducible holomorphic symplectic Kähler manifold is projective space. This resolves the projective-space base conjecture for such fibrations in every dimension and deformation type.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the strong hyperkähler SYZ conjecture: every holomorphic line bundle with nonzero nef isotropic first Chern class on a compact irreducible holomorphic symplectic Kähler manifold is semiample. It also proves that every projective Lagrangian fibration with normal projective base has projective space as its base, in every dimension and deformation type.","pdfPath":"preprints/Projective-Space-Bases-of-Lagrangian-Fibrations-September-23-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Projective-Space-Bases-of-Lagrangian-Fibrations-September-23-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Projective-Space-Bases-of-Lagrangian-Fibrations-September-23-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Projective-Space-Bases-of-Lagrangian-Fibrations-September-23-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Projective-Space-Bases-of-Lagrangian-Fibrations-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Projective-Space-Bases-of-Lagrangian-Fibrations-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/02-setup.tex","preprints/Projective-Space-Bases-of-Lagrangian-Fibrations-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/03-local-tensor.tex","preprints/Projective-Space-Bases-of-Lagrangian-Fibrations-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/04-stratum.tex","preprints/Projective-Space-Bases-of-Lagrangian-Fibrations-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/05-base-change.tex","preprints/Projective-Space-Bases-of-Lagrangian-Fibrations-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/06-euler.tex","preprints/Projective-Space-Bases-of-Lagrangian-Fibrations-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/07-deformation.tex","preprints/Projective-Space-Bases-of-Lagrangian-Fibrations-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/08-isotrivial.tex","preprints/Projective-Space-Bases-of-Lagrangian-Fibrations-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/09-second-system.tex","preprints/Projective-Space-Bases-of-Lagrangian-Fibrations-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/10-variation.tex","preprints/Projective-Space-Bases-of-Lagrangian-Fibrations-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/11-quotient.tex","preprints/Projective-Space-Bases-of-Lagrangian-Fibrations-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/12-conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Projective-Space-Bases-of-Lagrangian-Fibrations-September-23-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the normal projective base of a projective Lagrangian fibration from a compact irreducible holomorphic symplectic Kähler manifold is projective space. This resolves the projective-space base conjecture for such fibrations in every dimension and deformation type.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":339},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":6484},{"kind":"section","title":"Notation and global inputs","sourceOffset":14811},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite local covers and cotangent tensors","sourceOffset":22007},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The trace quotient","sourceOffset":35336},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Connections before and after the trace quotient","sourceOffset":43132},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The prolongation forced by a nontrivial cotangent block","sourceOffset":51698},{"kind":"section","title":"A closed stratum and compatible algebraic products","sourceOffset":72704},{"kind":"section","title":"Good reductions and ordinary base change","sourceOffset":113118},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite smooth covers as coefficient modules","sourceOffset":117056},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One twist bound for all coefficient modules","sourceOffset":125288},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The differential identity attached to a clearing factor","sourceOffset":134636}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(n\\geq1\\), and let \\(X\\) be a compact irreducible holomorphic symplectic Kähler manifold of dimension \\(2n\\), with symplectic form \\(\\sigma\\). Suppose that \\[ f:X\\longrightarrow B \\] is a projective surjective holomorphic morphism with connected fibers, where \\(B\\) is a normal projective variety of dimension \\(n\\). Assume that every irreducible component of every fiber has dimension \\(n\\) and that the restriction of \\(\\sigma\\) to its smooth locus is zero. Then \\(B\\) is isomorphic to \\(\\PP^n_{\\C}\\).","sourceOffset":3858,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Background for the given fibration","label":"prop:background","text":"In the setting of Theorem \\ref{thm:main}, the following hold. \\begin{enumerate} \\item $X$ is projective, $e\\neq0$ is nef and $q(e)=0$. The base is $\\Q$-factorial and klt, has Picard number one, and $-K_B$ is ample as a $\\Q$-Cartier divisor. A smooth fiber is an abelian variety after choosing an origin. \\item There are reflexive identifications \\[ R^j f_*\\cO_X\\simeq\\Omega_B^{[j]}. \\] For every integral Weil divisor $A$ ample as a $\\Q$-divisor, \\[ H^i\\!\\left(B, (\\Omega_B^{[j]}\\otimes\\cO_B(A))^{**}\\right)=0 \\quad(i>0,\\ j>0). \\] The reflexivity assertion also applies locally to the projective equidimensional fibrations with connected fibers, smooth source \\(Z\\) satisfying \\(\\omega_Z\\simeq\\cO_Z\\), and normal Cohen--Macaulay \\(\\Q\\)-Gorenstein target that occur below. \\item The rational intersection cohomology of $B$ has the Betti numbers of $\\PP^n$, and the restriction of $H^2(X,\\Q)$ to a smooth fiber has one-dimensional image. \\item If $B$ is smooth, then $B\\simeq\\PP^n$. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":15677,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":374544,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":23,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Projective-Space-Bases-of-Lagrangian-Fibrations-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"041-02","slug":"The-Strong-Hyperkahler-SYZ-Conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"041","title":"The strong hyperkähler SYZ conjecture","abstract":"We prove the strong hyperkähler SYZ conjecture: every holomorphic line bundle with nonzero nef isotropic first Chern class on a compact irreducible holomorphic symplectic Kähler manifold is semiample.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the strong hyperkähler SYZ conjecture: every holomorphic line bundle with nonzero nef isotropic first Chern class on a compact irreducible holomorphic symplectic Kähler manifold is semiample. It also proves that every projective Lagrangian fibration with normal projective base has projective space as its base, in every dimension and deformation type.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Strong-Hyperkahler-SYZ-Conjecture-September-23-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Strong-Hyperkahler-SYZ-Conjecture-September-23-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Strong-Hyperkahler-SYZ-Conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/The-Strong-Hyperkahler-SYZ-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Strong-Hyperkahler-SYZ-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-Strong-Hyperkahler-SYZ-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-Strong-Hyperkahler-SYZ-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/metric_estimates.tex","preprints/The-Strong-Hyperkahler-SYZ-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/completion.tex","preprints/The-Strong-Hyperkahler-SYZ-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/polarized_family.tex","preprints/The-Strong-Hyperkahler-SYZ-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/rotation.tex","preprints/The-Strong-Hyperkahler-SYZ-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/rotation.tex","preprints/The-Strong-Hyperkahler-SYZ-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/finiteness.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Strong-Hyperkahler-SYZ-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the strong hyperk\\\"ahler SYZ conjecture: every holomorphic line bundle with nonzero nef isotropic first Chern class on a compact irreducible holomorphic symplectic K\\\"ahler manifold is semiample.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":267},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context","sourceOffset":3941},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof in four steps","sourceOffset":9019},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and conventions","sourceOffset":12169},{"kind":"section","title":"Metric estimates at the torus scale","sourceOffset":13291},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coordinate volume and mixed intersections","sourceOffset":16339},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Global energies and many noncollapsed centers","sourceOffset":22065},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One center with estimates at every radius","sourceOffset":27142},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The regular part of the pointed limit","sourceOffset":31339},{"kind":"section","title":"A flat cylinder and a special Lagrangian torus","sourceOffset":38795},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The coordinate map and its derivative bounds","sourceOffset":39444},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The metric completion is the full cylinder","sourceOffset":46743}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $X$ be a compact irreducible holomorphic symplectic K\\\"ahler manifold of complex dimension $2n$, where $n\\ge1$. If a holomorphic line bundle $L$ satisfies \\[ c_1(L)\\ne0,\\qquad c_1(L)\\in\\overline{\\mathcal K_X}, \\qquad q_X(c_1(L))=0, \\] then $L$ is semiample. More precisely, there exist a positive integer $m$, a normal projective variety $B$, an ample line bundle $A$ on $B$, and a surjective holomorphic map with connected fibers \\[ f:X\\longrightarrow B,\\qquad L^m\\simeq f^*A, \\] such that $\\dim_{\\C}B=n$. Every irreducible component of every fiber has complex dimension $n$, and $\\sigma$ vanishes on its smooth locus.","sourceOffset":1498,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:finiteness","text":"For each integer $n\\ge1$, there are only finitely many smooth deformation types of compact irreducible holomorphic symplectic K\\\"ahler manifolds of complex dimension $2n$ with $b_2\\ge5$.","sourceOffset":2714,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":116049,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Strong-Hyperkahler-SYZ-Conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"042-01","slug":"Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026","familyId":"042","title":"Every complex K3 surface is Oka","abstract":"We prove that every complex K3 surface X is Oka, resolving the K3 Oka conjecture. Equivalently, for every m ≥ 1, every holomorphic map to X from a neighborhood of a compact convex set in ℂm can be approximated uniformly on that set by entire maps $\\mathbb C^m\\to X$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every complex K3 surface is Oka, including nonprojective surfaces. More generally, every connected minimal compact complex surface of Kodaira dimension zero is Oka; a connected minimal compact complex surface of class VII is Oka exactly when it is a Hopf or Enoki surface.","pdfPath":"preprints/Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026/build/sections/local.tex","preprints/Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026/build/figures/planar-pieces.tex","preprints/Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026/build/sections/gluing.tex","preprints/Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026/build/sections/globalization.tex","preprints/Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026/build/figures/real-interface.tex","preprints/Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026/build/sections/projective.tex","preprints/Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026/build/sections/sewing.tex","preprints/Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026/build/sections/degeneration.tex","preprints/Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026/build/sections/rational.tex","preprints/Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026/build/sections/dynamics.tex","preprints/Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026/build/sections/dense.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every complex K3 surface $X$ is Oka, resolving the K3 Oka conjecture. Equivalently, for every $m\\geq1$, every holomorphic map to $X$ from a neighborhood of a compact convex set in $\\C^m$ can be approximated uniformly on that set by entire maps $\\C^m\\to X$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":342},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier results and the remaining obstacles","sourceOffset":3861},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its organization","sourceOffset":7908},{"kind":"section","title":"Local approximation from complete directions","sourceOffset":12725},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Planar splitting and two strip maps","sourceOffset":14226},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sprays, deformation, and planar gluing","sourceOffset":26496},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One planar enlargement with passive parameters","sourceOffset":31564},{"kind":"section","title":"Approximation on polydiscs","sourceOffset":39737},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Gluing on a buffered open overlap","sourceOffset":40989},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A change of coordinates with a thin parameter range","sourceOffset":46129},{"kind":"section","title":"From polydiscs to convex approximation","sourceOffset":60252},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Holomorphic approximation with a real parameter","sourceOffset":61832}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $X$ be a complex K3 surface, with any smooth Hermitian distance $d_X$. For every integer $m\\geq1$, every nonempty compact convex set $K\\subset\\C^m$, every open neighborhood $U$ of $K$, every holomorphic map $f:U\\to X$, and every $\\epsilon>0$, there exists a holomorphic map $F:\\C^m\\to X$ such that \\[ \\sup_{z\\in K}d_X(F(z),f(z))<\\epsilon. \\]","sourceOffset":1427,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:dense","text":"Let $S$ be a complex K3 surface. For every $x\\in S$ and every nonzero vector $v\\in T_xS$, there is a holomorphic immersion $f:\\C\\to S$ such that \\[ f(0)=x,\\qquad f'(0)=v,\\qquad \\overline{f(\\C)}=S, \\] where closure is taken in the ordinary complex topology. If $S$ is projective, then $f(\\C)$ is Zariski dense.","sourceOffset":2405,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"loc:property","text":"A complex surface $X$ has property $L$ at $x$ if there is an open neighborhood $V$ of $x$ with the following property. For every special pair $D\\subset D'$, every pair of closed polydiscs $P_0\\Subset\\operatorname{int}P\\subset\\C^q$, and every holomorphic map $f$ from a neighborhood of $D\\times P$ into $V$, the restriction $f|_{D\\times P_0}$ is a uniform limit of maps holomorphic near $D'\\times P_0$ with values in $X$. The case $q=0$ is included. We call $V$ a witness neighborhood.","sourceOffset":13587,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":191094,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"043-01","slug":"P-equals-W-in-composite-rank-for-fixed-determinant-September-24-2026","familyId":"043","title":"P=W in composite rank for fixed determinant","abstract":"We prove the P = W conjecture on the full rational cohomology of fixed-determinant, trace-free Higgs moduli spaces in composite rank and coprime degree, for smooth projective complex curves of genus at least two. Together with the established prime-rank cases, this gives the equality in every coprime rank.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves $P_k=W_{2k}=W_{2k+1}$ on the full rational cohomology of smooth coprime fixed-determinant, trace-free Higgs moduli spaces and their character varieties for composite ranks over smooth projective complex curves of genus at least two. This includes variant cohomology and, together with the known prime-rank theorems, establishes the fixed-determinant P = W conjecture in every coprime rank.","pdfPath":"preprints/P-equals-W-in-composite-rank-for-fixed-determinant-September-24-2026/P-equals-W-in-composite-rank-for-fixed-determinant-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/P-equals-W-in-composite-rank-for-fixed-determinant-September-24-2026/P-equals-W-in-composite-rank-for-fixed-determinant-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/P-equals-W-in-composite-rank-for-fixed-determinant-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/P-equals-W-in-composite-rank-for-fixed-determinant-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/P-equals-W-in-composite-rank-for-fixed-determinant-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/P-equals-W-in-composite-rank-for-fixed-determinant-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/P-equals-W-in-composite-rank-for-fixed-determinant-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-lefschetz.tex","preprints/P-equals-W-in-composite-rank-for-fixed-determinant-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-betti.tex","preprints/P-equals-W-in-composite-rank-for-fixed-determinant-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-diagram.tex","preprints/P-equals-W-in-composite-rank-for-fixed-determinant-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-logarithmic.tex","preprints/P-equals-W-in-composite-rank-for-fixed-determinant-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-translation.tex","preprints/P-equals-W-in-composite-rank-for-fixed-determinant-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-specialization.tex","preprints/P-equals-W-in-composite-rank-for-fixed-determinant-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-commutator.tex","preprints/P-equals-W-in-composite-rank-for-fixed-determinant-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/P-equals-W-in-composite-rank-for-fixed-determinant-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the $P=W$ conjecture on the full rational cohomology of fixed-determinant, trace-free Higgs moduli spaces in composite rank and coprime degree, for smooth projective complex curves of genus at least two. Together with the established prime-rank cases, this gives the equality in every coprime rank.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":370},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the fixed-determinant problem","sourceOffset":3942},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The operator behind the comparison","sourceOffset":7765},{"kind":"section","title":"Lefschetz operators and the comparison criterion","sourceOffset":11664},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The normalized perverse filtration","sourceOffset":12121},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The degree-two class","sourceOffset":15694},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A common lowering operator determines the filtration","sourceOffset":19233},{"kind":"section","title":"Curious hard Lefschetz on the full character variety","sourceOffset":24913},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The ordered family and its scalar fiber","sourceOffset":26362},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Lifting the toric stratifications","sourceOffset":29959},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The scalar summand and the determinant factor","sourceOffset":37704},{"kind":"section","title":"Weights for flat bundles of algebraic families","sourceOffset":41933}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every smooth projective complex curve $C$ of genus $g\\ge2$, every composite integer $n\\ge4$, every integer $d$ with $\\gcd(n,d)=1$, and every $L\\in\\Pic^d(C)$, one has \\[ P_kH^m(X^B,\\Q)=W_{2k}H^m(X^B,\\Q) =W_{2k+1}H^m(X^B,\\Q) \\] for all $m,k\\ge0$.","sourceOffset":3021,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"norm:relative-lefschetz","text":"Cup product by $e$ sends $P_kH^m(X)$ into $P_{k+2}H^{m+2}(X)$. For every $i\\ge0$ and every $m$, it induces an isomorphism \\begin{equation} e^i:\\operatorname{Gr}^P_{R-i}H^m(X) \\xrightarrow{\\ \\sim\\ } \\operatorname{Gr}^P_{R+i}H^{m+2i}(X). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":18074,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":127526,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/P-equals-W-in-composite-rank-for-fixed-determinant-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"044-01","slug":"The-equivariant-cohomological-Hikita-conjecture-for-arbitrary-quivers-September-24-2026","familyId":"044","title":"The equivariant cohomological Hikita conjecture for arbitrary quivers","abstract":"We prove the equivariant cohomological Hikita conjecture for arbitrary finite quivers, including loops and multiple arrows. For any dimension and framing vectors, any commuting flavor torus, and a stability character whose semistable locus is stable and has free gauge action, the equivariant cohomology of the Nakajima variety is canonically isomorphic to the coordinate ring of the scheme-theoretic fixed locus of the stability cocharacter on the flavor-deformed Coulomb branch. This is an isomorphism of graded algebras over the common coefficient ring, retaining nilpotents and including the empty case.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the equivariant cohomological Hikita correspondence for every finite quiver, including loops and multiple arrows, with arbitrary dimension and framing vectors and commuting flavor torus. When every semistable point is stable and the gauge action is free, the equivariant cohomology of the Nakajima variety is canonically the coordinate ring of the scheme-theoretic cocharacter-fixed locus of its flavor-deformed Coulomb branch.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-equivariant-cohomological-Hikita-conjecture-for-arbitrary-quivers-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-equivariant-cohomological-Hikita-conjecture-for-arbitrary-quivers-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-equivariant-cohomological-Hikita-conjecture-for-arbitrary-quivers-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/The-equivariant-cohomological-Hikita-conjecture-for-arbitrary-quivers-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-equivariant-cohomological-Hikita-conjecture-for-arbitrary-quivers-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-equivariant-cohomological-Hikita-conjecture-for-arbitrary-quivers-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-route.tex","preprints/The-equivariant-cohomological-Hikita-conjecture-for-arbitrary-quivers-September-24-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/The-equivariant-cohomological-Hikita-conjecture-for-arbitrary-quivers-September-24-2026/build/sections/correspondences.tex","preprints/The-equivariant-cohomological-Hikita-conjecture-for-arbitrary-quivers-September-24-2026/build/sections/stable-pairs.tex","preprints/The-equivariant-cohomological-Hikita-conjecture-for-arbitrary-quivers-September-24-2026/build/sections/laurent.tex","preprints/The-equivariant-cohomological-Hikita-conjecture-for-arbitrary-quivers-September-24-2026/build/sections/counting.tex","preprints/The-equivariant-cohomological-Hikita-conjecture-for-arbitrary-quivers-September-24-2026/build/sections/deformations.tex","preprints/The-equivariant-cohomological-Hikita-conjecture-for-arbitrary-quivers-September-24-2026/build/sections/cocharacters.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-equivariant-cohomological-Hikita-conjecture-for-arbitrary-quivers-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the equivariant cohomological Hikita conjecture for arbitrary finite quivers, including loops and multiple arrows. For any dimension and framing vectors, any commuting flavor torus, and a stability character whose semistable locus is stable and has free gauge action, the equivariant cohomology of the Nakajima variety is canonically isomorphic to the coordinate ring of the scheme-theoretic fixed locus of the stability cocharacter on the flavor-deformed Coulomb branch. This is an isomorphism of graded algebras over the common coefficient ring, retaining nilpotents and including the empty case.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":653},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The statement and its coefficient maps","sourceOffset":1685},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the comparison problem","sourceOffset":5216},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and organization","sourceOffset":10493},{"kind":"section","title":"Coefficient rings, minuscule classes, and flavor","sourceOffset":19125},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite correspondence test for Coulomb relations","sourceOffset":32739},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bounded lattice families","sourceOffset":33191},{"kind":"section","title":"Testing coefficient relations by stable pairs","sourceOffset":47159},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A degree constraint on global stable pairs","sourceOffset":47879},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite vanishing-cycle test","sourceOffset":51602},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The neutral component and its quadratic normal form","sourceOffset":56410},{"kind":"section","title":"A Laurent model and a filtration by multipartitions","sourceOffset":63877}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every datum \\eqref{eq:gauge-data}, every commuting flavor torus $F$, and every regular stability character $\\nu$, both coefficient maps are surjective and have the same kernel. They therefore induce a canonical isomorphism of graded $R$-algebras \\[ H_F^*(X;\\C)\\ \\cong\\ \\C[Y_{\\mathfrak f}^{\\nu}]. \\] The gradings are ordinary cohomological degree and BFN homological degree, respectively. The assertion includes the empty case, whose ring is zero.","sourceOffset":4430,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Abelianization and minuscule generation","label":"prop:minuscule","text":"There is an injective coefficient-preserving algebra map from $A_F$ into \\[ \\bigl(\\operatorname{Frac}(P_F)[X_*(T)]\\bigr)^{\\mathcal W},\\qquad e^\\lambda e^\\gamma=e^{\\lambda+\\gamma}. \\] Besides $R$, the algebra is generated by the dressed classes associated with all nonzero positive and negative minuscule coweights. For $p\\in P_F^{\\mathcal W_\\lambda}$, the class has expression \\begin{equation} \\sum_{\\lambda'\\in\\mathcal W\\lambda} e^{\\lambda'}p_{\\lambda'} \\frac{\\prod_{\\beta\\in\\wt(N)} \\beta^{(-\\langle\\beta,\\lambda'\\rangle)_+}} {\\eu_T(T_{t^{\\lambda'}}\\Gr_G^\\lambda)}. \\end{equation} Each distinct translate occurs once, and the dressing is translated with the coweight. Denote this class by $M_{\\lambda,p}$. Weights $\\beta$ include their flavor components, but pairings use only the gauge components; factors with exponent zero are omitted. The class has charge $[\\lambda]$. On charge zero, the BFN grading is twice the ordinary polynomial degree of the rational coefficients in this description.","sourceOffset":22686,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":145227,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-equivariant-cohomological-Hikita-conjecture-for-arbitrary-quivers-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"046-01","slug":"A-projective-fourfold-with-large-fundamental-group-and-non-Stein-universal-cover-October-5-2026","familyId":"046","title":"A projective fourfold with large fundamental group and non-Stein universal cover","abstract":"We construct a smooth projective complex fourfold with large fundamental group whose universal cover is not Stein. The universal cover contains no positive-dimensional compact complex-analytic subvariety, so this disproves Shafarevich's holomorphic-convexity conjecture even under the large-fundamental-group hypothesis.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a smooth projective complex fourfold with large fundamental group whose universal cover contains no positive-dimensional compact analytic subvariety but is neither Stein nor holomorphically convex. A separate smooth projective complex surface already disproves unrestricted Shafarevich holomorphic convexity in dimension two.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-projective-fourfold-with-large-fundamental-group-and-non-Stein-universal-cover-October-5-2026/large-fundamental-group-non-stein.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-projective-fourfold-with-large-fundamental-group-and-non-Stein-universal-cover-October-5-2026/large-fundamental-group-non-stein.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-projective-fourfold-with-large-fundamental-group-and-non-Stein-universal-cover-October-5-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-projective-fourfold-with-large-fundamental-group-and-non-Stein-universal-cover-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-projective-fourfold-with-large-fundamental-group-and-non-Stein-universal-cover-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/A-projective-fourfold-with-large-fundamental-group-and-non-Stein-universal-cover-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-projective-fourfold-with-large-fundamental-group-and-non-Stein-universal-cover-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-reduction.tex","preprints/A-projective-fourfold-with-large-fundamental-group-and-non-Stein-universal-cover-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-paths.tex","preprints/A-projective-fourfold-with-large-fundamental-group-and-non-Stein-universal-cover-October-5-2026/build/figures/path-ladder.tex","preprints/A-projective-fourfold-with-large-fundamental-group-and-non-Stein-universal-cover-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-geometry.tex","preprints/A-projective-fourfold-with-large-fundamental-group-and-non-Stein-universal-cover-October-5-2026/build/figures/arrangement.tex","preprints/A-projective-fourfold-with-large-fundamental-group-and-non-Stein-universal-cover-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-models.tex","preprints/A-projective-fourfold-with-large-fundamental-group-and-non-Stein-universal-cover-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-image.tex","preprints/A-projective-fourfold-with-large-fundamental-group-and-non-Stein-universal-cover-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-projective.tex","preprints/A-projective-fourfold-with-large-fundamental-group-and-non-Stein-universal-cover-October-5-2026/build/figures/blowdown.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-projective-fourfold-with-large-fundamental-group-and-non-Stein-universal-cover-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a smooth projective complex fourfold with large fundamental group whose universal cover is not Stein. The universal cover contains no positive-dimensional compact complex-analytic subvariety, so this disproves Shafarevich's holomorphic-convexity conjecture even under the large-fundamental-group hypothesis.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":383},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":2276},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The central construction","sourceOffset":5922},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Structure of the proof","sourceOffset":9585},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":10477},{"kind":"section","title":"Reduction to an abelian surface with cyclic image","sourceOffset":11123},{"kind":"section","title":"A local criterion for nontrivial loops","sourceOffset":20289},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Paths and their readings","sourceOffset":21184},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Calculations within one chart","sourceOffset":23747},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Narrowing comparisons between guides","sourceOffset":29558},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Continuing a guide by one edge","sourceOffset":36567},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The invariant of a path","sourceOffset":43592}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist a smooth connected projective complex fourfold $X$ and a simple abelian surface $A\\subset X$ such that \\begin{enumerate} \\item $X$ has large fundamental group; \\item $\\im\\bigl(\\pi_1(A)\\to\\pi_1(X)\\bigr)\\simeq\\Z$. \\end{enumerate} The universal cover $\\widetilde X$ contains no positive-dimensional compact complex analytic subvariety and is not Stein.","sourceOffset":1256,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:large-reduction","text":"Let $A$ be a simple abelian surface and let $A\\hookrightarrow S'$ be a closed embedding in a smooth connected projective complex variety. Suppose that \\[ \\im\\bigl(\\pi_1(A)\\longrightarrow\\pi_1(S')\\bigr)\\simeq\\Z. \\] There exist a smooth connected projective fourfold $X$ with large fundamental group and a closed embedding $A\\hookrightarrow X$ with infinite cyclic image on fundamental groups, such that the universal cover of $X$ is not Stein.","sourceOffset":11524,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":162794,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-projective-fourfold-with-large-fundamental-group-and-non-Stein-universal-cover-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"046-02","slug":"A-surface-counterexample-to-Shafarevich-holomorphic-convexity-September-23-2026","familyId":"046","title":"A surface counterexample to Shafarevich holomorphic convexity","abstract":"We construct a smooth connected projective complex surface whose universal cover is not holomorphically convex. This disproves the Shafarevich conjecture on holomorphic convexity in complex dimension two.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a smooth projective complex fourfold with large fundamental group whose universal cover contains no positive-dimensional compact analytic subvariety but is neither Stein nor holomorphically convex. A separate smooth projective complex surface already disproves unrestricted Shafarevich holomorphic convexity in dimension two.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-surface-counterexample-to-Shafarevich-holomorphic-convexity-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-surface-counterexample-to-Shafarevich-holomorphic-convexity-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-surface-counterexample-to-Shafarevich-holomorphic-convexity-September-23-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-surface-counterexample-to-Shafarevich-holomorphic-convexity-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-surface-counterexample-to-Shafarevich-holomorphic-convexity-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-surface-counterexample-to-Shafarevich-holomorphic-convexity-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-weighted.tex","preprints/A-surface-counterexample-to-Shafarevich-holomorphic-convexity-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-family.tex","preprints/A-surface-counterexample-to-Shafarevich-holomorphic-convexity-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-involution.tex","preprints/A-surface-counterexample-to-Shafarevich-holomorphic-convexity-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-quotient.tex","preprints/A-surface-counterexample-to-Shafarevich-holomorphic-convexity-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-surface.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-surface-counterexample-to-Shafarevich-holomorphic-convexity-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a smooth connected projective complex surface whose universal cover is not holomorphically convex. This disproves the Shafarevich conjecture on holomorphic convexity in complex dimension two.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":278},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier approaches and the obstruction","sourceOffset":1715},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Consequences and comparisons","sourceOffset":6215},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction in outline","sourceOffset":7628},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why the rational curve is enough","sourceOffset":10803},{"kind":"section","title":"Weighted free pro-2 groups","sourceOffset":13113},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Magnus degrees and automorphisms","sourceOffset":13439},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The infinitude inequality","sourceOffset":17024},{"kind":"section","title":"A family of paired punctures","sourceOffset":20526},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The family and its torus cover","sourceOffset":20944},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Actual based monodromy","sourceOffset":25356},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Three invariant double covers","sourceOffset":28424}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a smooth connected projective complex surface $X$ containing a connected nodal curve $Z_0$ with smooth rational irreducible components such that \\[ \\im\\bigl(\\pi_1(Z_0)\\longrightarrow\\pi_1(X)\\bigr) \\quad\\text{is infinite}. \\] In the simply connected universal cover of $X$, a connected component above $Z_0$ is a closed, connected, noncompact, locally finite union of compact rational curves. The holomorphic hull of any one of its points is noncompact. In particular, this universal cover is not holomorphically convex.","sourceOffset":1137,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:based-monodromy","text":"The actions $\\varphi_m$ form a homomorphism $\\Gamma\\to\\Aut(F)$, are pure, and commute with $\\iota_*$. They preserve $K$. If $\\beta_m\\in\\Aut(\\Pi)$ is the actual based monodromy of the punctured sphere family, then \\begin{equation} p\\circ\\varphi_m|_K=\\beta_m\\circ p. \\end{equation} Moreover, $p:K\\to\\Pi$ is surjective.","sourceOffset":26322,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":81872,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-surface-counterexample-to-Shafarevich-holomorphic-convexity-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"047-01","slug":"An-explicit-failure-of-complex-affine-space-cancellation-September-23-2026","familyId":"047","title":"An explicit failure of complex affine-space cancellation","abstract":"We construct an explicit integral complex affine fourfold X with $X\\times\\mathbb A^1\\cong\\mathbb A^5$ but $X\\not\\cong\\mathbb A^4$. This gives a negative answer to Zariski's affine-space cancellation problem over ℂ in dimension four. The same construction disproves the Stable Coordinate Conjecture in ambient dimension five and yields smooth 𝔸3-fibrations over 𝔸1 and 𝔸2 that are not Zariski-locally trivial. These fibrations disprove the Dolgachev–Weisfeiler affine-fibration conjecture over these bases.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs an integral complex affine fourfold $X\\not\\cong\\mathbb A^4$ with $X\\times\\mathbb A^1\\cong\\mathbb A^5$, disproving affine-space cancellation over ℂ in dimension four. It also disproves the Dolgachev–Weisfeiler affine-fibration conjecture: smooth surjections $X\\to\\mathbb A^1$ and $\\mathbb A^5\\to\\mathbb A^2$ have every residue-field fiber isomorphic to affine three-space but are not Zariski-locally trivial.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-explicit-failure-of-complex-affine-space-cancellation-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-explicit-failure-of-complex-affine-space-cancellation-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-explicit-failure-of-complex-affine-space-cancellation-September-23-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/An-explicit-failure-of-complex-affine-space-cancellation-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/An-explicit-failure-of-complex-affine-space-cancellation-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/An-explicit-failure-of-complex-affine-space-cancellation-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/An-explicit-failure-of-complex-affine-space-cancellation-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-construction.tex","preprints/An-explicit-failure-of-complex-affine-space-cancellation-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-degeneration.tex","preprints/An-explicit-failure-of-complex-affine-space-cancellation-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-bundle.tex","preprints/An-explicit-failure-of-complex-affine-space-cancellation-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-reduction.tex","preprints/An-explicit-failure-of-complex-affine-space-cancellation-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-rigidity.tex","preprints/An-explicit-failure-of-complex-affine-space-cancellation-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-affine-fibrations.tex","preprints/An-explicit-failure-of-complex-affine-space-cancellation-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-explicit-failure-of-complex-affine-space-cancellation-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct an explicit integral complex affine fourfold $X$ with $X\\times\\mathbb A^1\\cong\\mathbb A^5$ but $X\\not\\cong\\mathbb A^4$. This gives a negative answer to Zariski's affine-space cancellation problem over $\\mathbb C$ in dimension four. The same construction disproves the Stable Coordinate Conjecture in ambient dimension five and yields smooth $\\mathbb A^3$-fibrations over $\\mathbb A^1$ and $\\mathbb A^2$ that are not Zariski-locally trivial. These fibrations disprove the Dolgachev--Weisfeiler affine-fibration conjecture over these bases.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":597},{"kind":"section","title":"The equation and its polynomial cylinder","sourceOffset":13322},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Localization and integrality","sourceOffset":14616},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An explicit stabilization","sourceOffset":16997},{"kind":"section","title":"A smooth degeneration and its derivations","sourceOffset":23052},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The valuation and its coefficients","sourceOffset":23424},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exact associated graded algebra","sourceOffset":27599},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Inducing locally nilpotent derivations","sourceOffset":33641},{"kind":"section","title":"Principalization and lifted additive actions","sourceOffset":38664},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A line bundle that principalizes the center","sourceOffset":39634},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A normalized lift on a line bundle","sourceOffset":45103},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compatibility with valuation degree","sourceOffset":51365}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"The algebra $A$ in \\eqref{eq:example} is an integral complex algebra of dimension four. For an independent variable $w$, \\[ A[w]\\simeq\\kk\\poly5, \\qquad A\\not\\simeq\\kk\\poly4. \\] Consequently the Zariski cancellation problem over the complex numbers has a negative answer in dimension four.","sourceOffset":3382,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:principalization","text":"The morphism $\\Spec\\Rt\\to\\Spec R$ is the complement of the zero section in a line bundle, with fiber scaling specified by the weights \\[ \\wt(a)=\\wt(d)=1,\\qquad \\wt(b)=\\wt(c)=-1,\\qquad \\wt(u)=0. \\] The homomorphism $R\\to\\Rt$ is faithfully flat, and $I\\Rt=(v)$. Moreover, base change gives a faithfully flat inclusion $G\\hookrightarrow\\Gt$, where \\begin{equation} \\Gt:=G\\otimes_R\\Rt =\\Rt[\\tau,v/\\tau^2]\\subset\\Rt[\\tau,\\tau^{-1}],\\qquad \\Gt\\cong\\Rt[\\tau,V]/(\\tau^2V-v). \\end{equation} Thus $V$ denotes the element $v/\\tau^2$ in the displayed subalgebra. The ring $\\Gt$ is a domain and has the following commuting gradings: \\begin{center} \\begin{tabular}{c@{\\qquad}rrrrr} \\toprule & $a,d$ & $b,c$ & $u$ & $\\tau$ & $V$\\\\ \\midrule Valuation degree & $0$ & $0$ & $0$ & $-1$ & $2$\\\\ Fiber weight & $1$ & $-1$ & $0$ & $0$ & $2$\\\\ \\bottomrule \\end{tabular} \\end{center}","sourceOffset":40333,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/047.md","scopeText":"Affine-space cancellation asks whether $X\\times\\mathbb A^1\\cong\\mathbb A^{n+1}$ forces $X\\cong\\mathbb A^n$. The formalized result gives an explicit finite-type complex domain $A$ of Krull dimension four with $A[w]\\cong\\mathbb C[x_1,\\ldots,x_5]$ but $A\\not\\cong\\mathbb C[x_1,\\ldots,x_4]$. Thus adjoining one variable erases a genuine algebraic distinction.\n\nThe separate stable-coordinate and general line-bundle lifting consequences are not included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/047.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":89641,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-explicit-failure-of-complex-affine-space-cancellation-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"048-01","slug":"A-singular-normal-affine-surface-with-free-tangent-sheaf-September-23-2026","familyId":"048","title":"A singular normal affine surface with free tangent sheaf","abstract":"We construct a singular normal affine complex surface whose tangent sheaf is free of rank two. This disproves the Lipman–Zariski conjecture in characteristic zero.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a singular normal affine complex surface with free rank-two tangent sheaf, disproving the characteristic-zero Lipman–Zariski conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-singular-normal-affine-surface-with-free-tangent-sheaf-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-singular-normal-affine-surface-with-free-tangent-sheaf-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-singular-normal-affine-surface-with-free-tangent-sheaf-September-23-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-singular-normal-affine-surface-with-free-tangent-sheaf-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-singular-normal-affine-surface-with-free-tangent-sheaf-September-23-2026/build/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-singular-normal-affine-surface-with-free-tangent-sheaf-September-23-2026/build/02-curve.tex","preprints/A-singular-normal-affine-surface-with-free-tangent-sheaf-September-23-2026/build/03-trace.tex","preprints/A-singular-normal-affine-surface-with-free-tangent-sheaf-September-23-2026/build/04-jets.tex","preprints/A-singular-normal-affine-surface-with-free-tangent-sheaf-September-23-2026/build/05-analytic.tex","preprints/A-singular-normal-affine-surface-with-free-tangent-sheaf-September-23-2026/build/06-algebraization.tex","preprints/A-singular-normal-affine-surface-with-free-tangent-sheaf-September-23-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-singular-normal-affine-surface-with-free-tangent-sheaf-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a singular normal affine complex surface whose tangent sheaf is free of rank two. This disproves the Lipman--Zariski conjecture in characteristic zero.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":211},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and significance","sourceOffset":1306},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Main ideas and proof structure","sourceOffset":6529},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":12150},{"kind":"section","title":"A curve with prescribed residue data","sourceOffset":12978},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The elliptic model and its quadratic form","sourceOffset":13644},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fourth-order contact on the quadric","sourceOffset":20345},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Section counts and evaluation maps","sourceOffset":23335},{"kind":"section","title":"The trace form and the residue data","sourceOffset":31112},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A two-dimensional quotient of a perfect trace form","sourceOffset":32172},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Identification by full principal parts","sourceOffset":37417},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Meromorphic sections with the required residues","sourceOffset":45644}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist a finitely generated normal integral $\\CC$-algebra $A$ of Krull dimension two and a maximal ideal $\\m\\subset A$ such that \\[ \\Der_{\\CC}(A)\\simeq A^{\\oplus2}, \\qquad A_{\\m}\\text{ is not regular}. \\]","sourceOffset":763,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:curve-data","text":"The construction above produces a smooth connected projective curve $C$, a reduced divisor $Z=q_1+\\cdots+q_6$, line bundles $H,L,\\theta$, and a basis $u=(u_1,u_2)$ of $H^0(C,H)$ with the following properties. \\begin{enumerate} \\item The numerical and canonical data are \\[ \\begin{gathered} g(C)=21,\\qquad \\deg H=4,\\qquad\\deg L=10,\\\\ L=H+Z,\\qquad\\theta=2L,\\qquad K_C=4L, \\end{gathered} \\] where the last isomorphism is normalized by \\eqref{curve:adjunction-normalization} and $\\xi^4\\mapsto\\gamma|_C$. The curve $C$ has exactly fourth-order contact with the auxiliary elliptic curve $\\Gamma$ at every $q_j$. \\item The pencil $u$ defines a finite degree-four morphism $f:C\\to\\PP^1$ unramified at every $q_j$, and the values $f(q_j)$ are not all equal. The zero divisor of $u_1$ can be chosen to be a reduced fiber disjoint from $Z$. \\item The section dimensions are \\begin{equation} \\begin{gathered} h^0(C,jH)=j+1\\quad(0\\le j\\le3),\\\\ h^0(C,L)=h^0(C,H+2Z)=2,\\qquad h^0(C,\\theta)=3,\\qquad h^0(C,\\OO_C(Z))=1,\\\\ h^0(C,\\theta+H)=6. \\end{gathered} \\end{equation} More precisely, \\begin{equation} \\begin{split} H^0(C,L)&=\\xi H^0(C,H),\\\\ H^0(C,H+2Z)&=\\xi^2H^0(C,H),\\\\ H^0(C,\\theta)&=\\xi^2H^0(C,2H). \\end{split} \\end{equation} In particular $r=\\xi u$ is a basis of $H^0(C,L)$ with simple common zero divisor exactly $Z$, and every section of $\\theta$ vanishes twice along $Z$. \\item For every $k\\ge2$, the evaluation map \\begin{equation} H^0(C,(k+1)L)\\longrightarrow H^0(Z,((k+1)L)|_Z) \\end{equation} is surjecti","sourceOffset":25725,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":127240,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-singular-normal-affine-surface-with-free-tangent-sheaf-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"049-01","slug":"A-stable-coordinate-that-is-not-a-coordinate-in-four-variables-October-5-2026","familyId":"049","title":"A stable coordinate that is not a coordinate in four variables","abstract":"We construct an explicit degree-five polynomial over $\\mathbf C$ that is not a coordinate in four variables but becomes one after adjoining a single variable. This gives a counterexample to the stable coordinate conjecture in four variables. Every fiber is isomorphic to affine three-space, yet its embedding in affine four-space is not rectifiable. Thus the example also disproves the Abhyankar–Sathaye embedding conjecture in ambient dimension four.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a polynomial in four complex variables that is not a coordinate but becomes one after adjoining a single variable, disproving the Stable Coordinate conjecture in four variables. Every fiber is affine three-space, yet none of its embeddings is rectifiable, also disproving the Abhyankar–Sathaye conjecture even when all fibers are affine spaces.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-stable-coordinate-that-is-not-a-coordinate-in-four-variables-October-5-2026/stable-coordinate-four-variables.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-stable-coordinate-that-is-not-a-coordinate-in-four-variables-October-5-2026/stable-coordinate-four-variables.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-stable-coordinate-that-is-not-a-coordinate-in-four-variables-October-5-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-stable-coordinate-that-is-not-a-coordinate-in-four-variables-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/A-stable-coordinate-that-is-not-a-coordinate-in-four-variables-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-stable-coordinate-that-is-not-a-coordinate-in-four-variables-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/02-construction.tex","preprints/A-stable-coordinate-that-is-not-a-coordinate-in-four-variables-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/03-filtration.tex","preprints/A-stable-coordinate-that-is-not-a-coordinate-in-four-variables-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/04-bundle.tex","preprints/A-stable-coordinate-that-is-not-a-coordinate-in-four-variables-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/05-rigidity.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-stable-coordinate-that-is-not-a-coordinate-in-four-variables-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct an explicit degree-five polynomial over $\\C$ that is not a coordinate in four variables but becomes one after adjoining a single variable. This gives a counterexample to the stable coordinate conjecture in four variables. Every fiber is isomorphic to affine three-space, yet its embedding in affine four-space is not rectifiable. Thus the example also disproves the Abhyankar--Sathaye embedding conjecture in ambient dimension four.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":491},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and related methods","sourceOffset":3110},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and the obstruction","sourceOffset":4895},{"kind":"section","title":"Polynomial presentation and stabilization","sourceOffset":7782},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A polynomial change of coordinates","sourceOffset":8531},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One-variable stabilization","sourceOffset":10428},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The fibers","sourceOffset":13781},{"kind":"section","title":"A graded obstruction to coordinateness","sourceOffset":14713},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The associated graded ring","sourceOffset":15367},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A quadric and its distinguished ideal","sourceOffset":18998},{"kind":"subsection","title":"What a coordinate would force","sourceOffset":21292},{"kind":"section","title":"Lifting the obstruction to a line bundle","sourceOffset":25155}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"In $R=\\C[x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4]$, put \\begin{equation} Q=x_2^2-x_4^2+x_1x_3, \\qquad f=x_1-2Q\\bigl(Q(x_2+x_4)+x_1x_4\\bigr). \\end{equation} There is a $\\C$-algebra automorphism of $R[w]$ sending $f$ to $x_1$, but there is no such automorphism of $R$.","sourceOffset":1481,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:fibers","text":"For every $\\lambda\\in\\C$, the hypersurface $f^{-1}(\\lambda)\\subset\\A^4$ is isomorphic to $\\A^3$ and its embedding in $\\A^4$ is not rectifiable.","sourceOffset":2464,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/049.md","scopeText":"The stable coordinate conjecture predicts that a polynomial that becomes a coordinate after adjoining variables was already a coordinate. The formalization gives an explicit degree-five polynomial in four complex variables that becomes a coordinate after adjoining one variable but is not a coordinate in four variables. Every fiber is isomorphic to affine three-space, yet no fiber can be carried to a coordinate hyperplane by an ambient polynomial automorphism.\n\nThe Abhyankar–Sathaye conjecture predicts that a polynomial defining an affine-space quotient must be an ambient coordinate. For every $n\\ge4$, the formalized counterexample gives $F\\in\\mathbb C[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]$ with quotient $\\mathbb C[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]/(F)\\cong\\mathbb C[y_1,\\ldots,y_{n-1}]$, although $F$ is not a coordinate.\n\nA compa","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/049.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":40576,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-stable-coordinate-that-is-not-a-coordinate-in-four-variables-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"049-02","slug":"An-explicit-noncoordinate-polynomial-with-affine-three-space-zero-fibre-September-24-2026","familyId":"049","title":"An explicit noncoordinate polynomial with affine three-space zero fibre","abstract":"We construct an explicit counterexample to the Abhyankar–Sathaye conjecture: a noncoordinate polynomial in four complex variables whose zero fibre is affine three-space. Adjoining variables gives counterexamples in every ambient dimension at least four.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a polynomial in four complex variables that is not a coordinate but becomes one after adjoining a single variable, disproving the Stable Coordinate conjecture in four variables. Every fiber is affine three-space, yet none of its embeddings is rectifiable, also disproving the Abhyankar–Sathaye conjecture even when all fibers are affine spaces.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-explicit-noncoordinate-polynomial-with-affine-three-space-zero-fibre-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-explicit-noncoordinate-polynomial-with-affine-three-space-zero-fibre-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-explicit-noncoordinate-polynomial-with-affine-three-space-zero-fibre-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/An-explicit-noncoordinate-polynomial-with-affine-three-space-zero-fibre-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-explicit-noncoordinate-polynomial-with-affine-three-space-zero-fibre-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct an explicit counterexample to the Abhyankar--Sathaye conjecture: a noncoordinate polynomial in four complex variables whose zero fibre is affine three-space. Adjoining variables gives counterexamples in every ambient dimension at least four.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction and statement","sourceOffset":301},{"kind":"section","title":"An elementary lifting lemma","sourceOffset":6445},{"kind":"section","title":"The affine-space fibre and the critical point","sourceOffset":9425},{"kind":"section","title":"Explicit coordinates on the zero fibre","sourceOffset":13158}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"The polynomial $F$ defined in \\eqref{eq:xyz}--\\eqref{eq:F} satisfies \\[ R/(F)\\cong\\CC^{[3]}, \\qquad \\grad F(2,0,-1/2,1/2)=0. \\] Consequently $F$ is not a coordinate of $R$.","sourceOffset":3866,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:dimension","text":"For every integer $n\\ge4$, there is a polynomial $f\\in\\CC[z_1,\\ldots,z_n]$ such that $\\CC[z_1,\\ldots,z_n]/(f)\\cong\\CC^{[n-1]}$ and $f$ is not a coordinate.","sourceOffset":4229,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/049.md","scopeText":"The stable coordinate conjecture predicts that a polynomial that becomes a coordinate after adjoining variables was already a coordinate. The formalization gives an explicit degree-five polynomial in four complex variables that becomes a coordinate after adjoining one variable but is not a coordinate in four variables. Every fiber is isomorphic to affine three-space, yet no fiber can be carried to a coordinate hyperplane by an ambient polynomial automorphism.\n\nThe Abhyankar–Sathaye conjecture predicts that a polynomial defining an affine-space quotient must be an ambient coordinate. For every $n\\ge4$, the formalized counterexample gives $F\\in\\mathbb C[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]$ with quotient $\\mathbb C[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]/(F)\\cong\\mathbb C[y_1,\\ldots,y_{n-1}]$, although $F$ is not a coordinate.\n\nA compa","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/049.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":18001,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-explicit-noncoordinate-polynomial-with-affine-three-space-zero-fibre-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"050-01","slug":"ample-rank-two-bundles-on-the-quadric-surface-without-griffiths-positive-metrics-September-24-2026","familyId":"050","title":"Ample rank-two bundles on the quadric surface without Griffiths-positive metrics","abstract":"We give counterexamples to Griffiths' conjecture in rank two on the quadric surface $\\mathbb P^1\\times\\mathbb P^1$. We construct an explicit bundle G whose coordinatewise power pullbacks, tensored with $\\mathcal O(1,1)$, are ample for every positive power, but admit no smooth strictly Griffiths-positive Hermitian metric for all sufficiently large powers.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs ample rank-two bundles on $\\mathbb P^1\\times\\mathbb P^1$ with no smooth Hermitian metric of strictly Griffiths-positive curvature, disproving Griffiths' positivity conjecture already on the quadric surface.","pdfPath":"preprints/ample-rank-two-bundles-on-the-quadric-surface-without-griffiths-positive-metrics-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/ample-rank-two-bundles-on-the-quadric-surface-without-griffiths-positive-metrics-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/ample-rank-two-bundles-on-the-quadric-surface-without-griffiths-positive-metrics-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/ample-rank-two-bundles-on-the-quadric-surface-without-griffiths-positive-metrics-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/ample-rank-two-bundles-on-the-quadric-surface-without-griffiths-positive-metrics-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/ample-rank-two-bundles-on-the-quadric-surface-without-griffiths-positive-metrics-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-construction.tex","preprints/ample-rank-two-bundles-on-the-quadric-surface-without-griffiths-positive-metrics-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-ampleness.tex","preprints/ample-rank-two-bundles-on-the-quadric-surface-without-griffiths-positive-metrics-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-limit.tex","preprints/ample-rank-two-bundles-on-the-quadric-surface-without-griffiths-positive-metrics-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-obstruction.tex","preprints/ample-rank-two-bundles-on-the-quadric-surface-without-griffiths-positive-metrics-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/ample-rank-two-bundles-on-the-quadric-surface-without-griffiths-positive-metrics-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give counterexamples to Griffiths' conjecture in rank two on the quadric surface $\\PP^1\\times\\PP^1$. We construct an explicit bundle $G$ whose coordinatewise power pullbacks, tensored with $\\OO(1,1)$, are ample for every positive power, but admit no smooth strictly Griffiths-positive Hermitian metric for all sufficiently large powers.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":402},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and related results","sourceOffset":2001},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":6904},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and notation","sourceOffset":10981},{"kind":"section","title":"A bundle defined by three graphs","sourceOffset":12114},{"kind":"section","title":"Ampleness of every member of the family","sourceOffset":20118},{"kind":"section","title":"A necessary quadratic limit","sourceOffset":26636},{"kind":"section","title":"The invariant measure obstruction","sourceOffset":40796},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From the two rulings to one invariant seminorm","sourceOffset":41268},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Logarithmic weights and their measure","sourceOffset":43568},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The incompatible invariances","sourceOffset":50764},{"kind":"section","title":"Proof of the main theorem","sourceOffset":53347}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is an algebraic vector bundle $G$ of rank two on $S$ such that, for every positive integer $m$, the bundle \\[ E_m=f_m^*G\\otimes P \\] is ample. There is an integer $m_0$ such that $E_m$ admits no smooth Hermitian metric with strictly Griffiths-positive Chern curvature for every $m\\ge m_0$.","sourceOffset":1278,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:construction","text":"These three isomorphisms glue to an algebraic isomorphism \\begin{equation} \\alpha:L_1^{-1}|_D\\xrightarrow{\\sim}L_2^{-1}|_D, \\qquad \\alpha|_{\\Gamma_M}=M^{\\otimes b}. \\end{equation} Let $\\beta=(\\alpha^\\vee)^{-1}:L_1|_D\\to L_2|_D$ and define \\begin{equation} G=\\bigl\\{(u_1,u_2)\\in L_1\\oplus L_2: u_2|_D=\\beta(u_1|_D)\\bigr\\}. \\end{equation} Then $G$ is an algebraic vector bundle of rank two. Both projections $\\rho_i:G\\to L_i$ are surjective, with exact sequences \\begin{equation} 0\\longrightarrow L_i(-D)\\longrightarrow G \\xrightarrow{\\rho_j}L_j\\longrightarrow0, \\qquad \\{i,j\\}=\\{1,2\\}. \\end{equation} For $F=G^\\vee$, the dual inclusions $\\iota_i=\\rho_i^\\vee:L_i^{-1}\\hookrightarrow F$ span each fiber over $S\\setminus D$ and satisfy \\begin{equation} \\iota_2\\circ\\alpha=\\iota_1\\qquad\\text{on }D. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":16289,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/050.md","scopeText":"Griffiths' positivity conjecture predicts that every ample holomorphic vector bundle on a smooth complex projective variety admits a smooth Hermitian metric with strictly Griffiths-positive curvature. The formalized counterexample starts with a rank-two algebraic bundle $G$ on $\\mathbb P^1\\times\\mathbb P^1$. Its coordinatewise power pullbacks, tensored with $\\mathcal O(1,1)$, are ample for every positive power but admit no such metric for all sufficiently large powers.\n\nThe separate very-ampleness result for all admissible even exponents is not included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/050.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":54749,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/ample-rank-two-bundles-on-the-quadric-surface-without-griffiths-positive-metrics-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"051-01","slug":"Canonical-ampleness-of-compact-hyperbolic-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026","familyId":"051","title":"Canonical ampleness of compact hyperbolic Kähler manifolds","abstract":"We prove that every compact connected Kähler manifold of positive complex dimension containing no nonconstant entire curve has ample canonical bundle and is projective. This resolves positively Kobayashi's canonical-ampleness conjecture in the smooth compact Kähler category.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Every compact connected Kähler manifold of positive complex dimension with no nonconstant entire curve has ample canonical bundle and is therefore projective. This proves Kobayashi's canonical-ampleness conjecture in the smooth compact Kähler setting.","pdfPath":"preprints/Canonical-ampleness-of-compact-hyperbolic-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/canonical-ampleness.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Canonical-ampleness-of-compact-hyperbolic-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/canonical-ampleness.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Canonical-ampleness-of-compact-hyperbolic-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Canonical-ampleness-of-compact-hyperbolic-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Canonical-ampleness-of-compact-hyperbolic-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Canonical-ampleness-of-compact-hyperbolic-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Canonical-ampleness-of-compact-hyperbolic-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/extremal-discs.tex","preprints/Canonical-ampleness-of-compact-hyperbolic-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/frames-variations.tex","preprints/Canonical-ampleness-of-compact-hyperbolic-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/hessian.tex","preprints/Canonical-ampleness-of-compact-hyperbolic-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/nefness.tex","preprints/Canonical-ampleness-of-compact-hyperbolic-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/volume-ampleness.tex","preprints/Canonical-ampleness-of-compact-hyperbolic-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex","preprints/Canonical-ampleness-of-compact-hyperbolic-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/smoothing.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Canonical-ampleness-of-compact-hyperbolic-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every compact connected K\\\"ahler manifold of positive complex dimension containing no nonconstant entire curve has ample canonical bundle and is projective. This resolves positively Kobayashi's canonical-ampleness conjecture in the smooth compact K\\\"ahler category.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":326},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and the main difficulty","sourceOffset":1679},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The analytic argument","sourceOffset":5242},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From the estimates to ampleness","sourceOffset":8238},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":9421},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and disc functionals","sourceOffset":10435},{"kind":"section","title":"Extremal discs and boundary regularity","sourceOffset":14997},{"kind":"section","title":"Boundary frames and centered holomorphic variations","sourceOffset":28275},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A holomorphic frame regular at the boundary","sourceOffset":29075},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Integrating the infinitesimal variations","sourceOffset":38594},{"kind":"section","title":"The trace of the complex Hessian","sourceOffset":50874},{"kind":"section","title":"Bounded canonical potentials and nefness","sourceOffset":59283}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $X$ be a compact connected K\\\"ahler manifold of positive complex dimension. If every holomorphic map $\\C\\to X$ is constant, then $K_X$ is ample. In particular, a positive tensor power of $K_X$ defines a holomorphic embedding of $X$ into a complex projective space.","sourceOffset":1363,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Existence of a maximizing disc","label":"ex:maximizer","text":"The functional in Equation \\eqref{eq:disc-functional} has finite supremum, attained by a finite-area holomorphic disc centered at $x$.","sourceOffset":18594,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":93084,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":17,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Canonical-ampleness-of-compact-hyperbolic-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"052-01","slug":"Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026","familyId":"052","title":"Universal-cover splitting for compact Kähler manifolds","abstract":"We prove the two-summand form of Beauville's compatible splitting conjecture. If the tangent bundle of a compact connected Kähler manifold decomposes into two integrable holomorphic subbundles of positive rank, then its ordinary universal cover admits a product decomposition whose factor tangent bundles are the lifted specified summands.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"A splitting of the tangent bundle of a compact Kähler manifold into two integrable holomorphic subbundles induces a compatible product decomposition of its universal cover, proving the two-summand form of Beauville's splitting conjecture. On smooth rationally connected projective manifolds, both summands are automatically integrable, establishing Höring's conjecture and the corresponding product decomposition.","pdfPath":"preprints/Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/intro.tex","preprints/Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/prelim.tex","preprints/Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/hartogs.tex","preprints/Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/boundary.tex","preprints/Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/figures/boundary-scales.tex","preprints/Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/boxes.tex","preprints/Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/global.tex","preprints/Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/figures/grid.tex","preprints/Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/canonical.tex","preprints/Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/appendices/01-collapsed-metric.tex","preprints/Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/appendices/02-rational-boxes.tex","preprints/Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/appendices/03-neighborhood-crossing.tex","preprints/Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/appendices/04-metric-globalization.tex","preprints/Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the two-summand form of Beauville's compatible splitting conjecture. If the tangent bundle of a compact connected K\\\"ahler manifold decomposes into two integrable holomorphic subbundles of positive rank, then its ordinary universal cover admits a product decomposition whose factor tangent bundles are the lifted specified summands.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":415},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The compatible splitting problem","sourceOffset":1735},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The nef-and-big application","sourceOffset":7550},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The analytic continuation argument","sourceOffset":9634},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An alternative through collapsed fibres","sourceOffset":12406},{"kind":"section","title":"Product charts and boxes","sourceOffset":14286},{"kind":"section","title":"Hartogs extension for boxes","sourceOffset":17987},{"kind":"section","title":"Extension across a mixed boundary","sourceOffset":25944},{"kind":"section","title":"Boxes with prescribed axes","sourceOffset":36575},{"kind":"section","title":"Path rectangles and the universal cover","sourceOffset":44470},{"kind":"section","title":"Nef and big canonical bundle: parallelism and integrability","sourceOffset":59883},{"kind":"section","title":"The canonical distance and its fibres","sourceOffset":67772}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $X$ be a compact connected K\\\"ahler manifold of complex dimension $n\\geq2$, and let \\[ T_X=E_1\\oplus E_2 \\] be a specified decomposition into integrable holomorphic subbundles of positive ranks $r_1,r_2$. If $\\pi:\\widetilde X\\to X$ is the ordinary universal covering map, there are connected simply connected complex manifolds $Y_1,Y_2$ and a biholomorphism $\\Phi:\\widetilde X\\to Y_1\\times Y_2$ such that \\[ \\dd\\Phi(\\pi^*E_i)=\\pr_i^*T_{Y_i}\\qquad(i=1,2), \\] where $\\dd\\pi$ identifies $T_{\\widetilde X}$ with $\\pi^*T_X$. In particular, $\\dim_\\C Y_i=r_i$.","sourceOffset":5870,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Non-uniruled projective manifolds","label":"cor:non-uniruled","text":"Let $X$ be a smooth connected non-uniruled projective complex manifold, and let $T_X=E_1\\oplus E_2$ be a specified holomorphic splitting into subbundles of positive ranks. Then both summands are integrable and the ordinary universal cover has a compatible product decomposition as in Theorem \\ref{thm:main}.","sourceOffset":6945,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:box","text":"Let $U\\subset\\C^a\\times\\C^b$ be open, with parameter blocks $(s,t)$. A holomorphic map $f:U\\to X$ is a holomorphic box if \\[ \\dd f(T\\C^a\\oplus0)\\subset E_1,\\qquad \\dd f(0\\oplus T\\C^b)\\subset E_2. \\] The conditions are imposed at every point of $U$. They do not require $f$ to be an immersion.","sourceOffset":16921,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"rat:definition","text":"Let $A,B$ be locally path connected spaces. A continuous map $R:A\\times B\\to M$ is a box if, near every parameter pair, there are a product neighborhood $A'\\times B'$ and a product chart on its image in which \\[ z\\bigl(R(a,b)\\bigr)=\\bigl(r_1(a),r_2(b)\\bigr). \\] Thus first-parameter slices locally follow $\\mathcal F_1$ plaques and second-parameter slices locally follow $\\mathcal F_2$ plaques. In particular these slices are continuous into their leaves with the leaf topology. A rational tree is a connected nodal curve whose irreducible components are copies of $\\Pp^1$ and whose dual graph is a f","sourceOffset":73508,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/052.md","scopeText":"The splitting question asks whether a holomorphic decomposition of the tangent bundle comes from a product decomposition of the universal cover. The formalized result gives an affirmative answer for a compact connected Kähler manifold whose tangent bundle splits into two positive-rank, integrable holomorphic subbundles. The universal cover is a product of connected simply connected complex manifolds of the prescribed dimensions, and the differential identifies the two tangent factors with the original summands.\n\nBoth integrability assumptions are required. Automatic integrability and the paper's additional corollaries are not included.\n\nThe formalization proves integrability of both summands in a holomorphic splitting of the tangent bundle of a rationally connected projective manifold. Mor","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/052.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":118945,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":15,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Universal-cover-splitting-for-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"052-02","slug":"Integrability-of-split-tangent-bundles-on-rationally-connected-manifolds-September-23-2026","familyId":"052","title":"Integrability of split tangent bundles on rationally connected manifolds","abstract":"We prove that both summands of every specified holomorphic splitting of the tangent bundle of a smooth rationally connected projective complex manifold into two positive-rank subbundles are integrable. This proves Höring's conjecture on rationally connected projective manifolds. Höring's product theorem then gives a product decomposition compatible with the specified splitting.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"A splitting of the tangent bundle of a compact Kähler manifold into two integrable holomorphic subbundles induces a compatible product decomposition of its universal cover, proving the two-summand form of Beauville's splitting conjecture. On smooth rationally connected projective manifolds, both summands are automatically integrable, establishing Höring's conjecture and the corresponding product decomposition.","pdfPath":"preprints/Integrability-of-split-tangent-bundles-on-rationally-connected-manifolds-September-23-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Integrability-of-split-tangent-bundles-on-rationally-connected-manifolds-September-23-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Integrability-of-split-tangent-bundles-on-rationally-connected-manifolds-September-23-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Integrability-of-split-tangent-bundles-on-rationally-connected-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Integrability-of-split-tangent-bundles-on-rationally-connected-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Integrability-of-split-tangent-bundles-on-rationally-connected-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/boxes.tex","preprints/Integrability-of-split-tangent-bundles-on-rationally-connected-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/transport.tex","preprints/Integrability-of-split-tangent-bundles-on-rationally-connected-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/vanishing.tex","preprints/Integrability-of-split-tangent-bundles-on-rationally-connected-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/figures/tangent-box.tex","preprints/Integrability-of-split-tangent-bundles-on-rationally-connected-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex","preprints/Integrability-of-split-tangent-bundles-on-rationally-connected-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/surface.tex","preprints/Integrability-of-split-tangent-bundles-on-rationally-connected-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/variation.tex","preprints/Integrability-of-split-tangent-bundles-on-rationally-connected-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Integrability-of-split-tangent-bundles-on-rationally-connected-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that both summands of every specified holomorphic splitting of the tangent bundle of a smooth rationally connected projective complex manifold into two positive-rank subbundles are integrable. This proves H\\\"oring's conjecture on rationally connected projective manifolds. H\\\"oring's product theorem then gives a product decomposition compatible with the specified splitting.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":432},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and scope","sourceOffset":2859},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":4901},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":7572},{"kind":"section","title":"Tangent boxes","sourceOffset":8101},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The formal Cauchy problem","sourceOffset":9129},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rational reconstruction along the diagonal","sourceOffset":14859},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Complementary tangency removes indeterminacy","sourceOffset":23579},{"kind":"section","title":"Bundles on a tangent box","sourceOffset":29198},{"kind":"section","title":"Vanishing of the bracket tensors","sourceOffset":33437},{"kind":"section","title":"Consequences for the rationally connected quotient","sourceOffset":37716},{"kind":"section","title":"Elementary elimination of surface base points","sourceOffset":40600}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Automatic integrability","label":null,"text":"\\label[theorem]{thm:main} Let \\(X\\) be a smooth connected projective complex manifold of dimension at least two. Suppose that two general points of \\(X\\) lie on the image of a morphism \\(\\PP^1\\to X\\). For every specified holomorphic decomposition \\[ T_X=E_1\\oplus E_2 \\] into subbundles of positive rank, both \\(E_1\\) and \\(E_2\\) are integrable.","sourceOffset":1300,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Compatible product","label":null,"text":"\\label[corollary]{cor:product} Under the hypotheses of \\cref{thm:main}, there are smooth projective manifolds \\(X_1,X_2\\) and an isomorphism \\(X\\simeq X_1\\times X_2\\) under which \\[ E_i=\\pr_i^*T_{X_i}\\qquad (i=1,2). \\]","sourceOffset":1985,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/052.md","scopeText":"The splitting question asks whether a holomorphic decomposition of the tangent bundle comes from a product decomposition of the universal cover. The formalized result gives an affirmative answer for a compact connected Kähler manifold whose tangent bundle splits into two positive-rank, integrable holomorphic subbundles. The universal cover is a product of connected simply connected complex manifolds of the prescribed dimensions, and the differential identifies the two tangent factors with the original summands.\n\nBoth integrability assumptions are required. Automatic integrability and the paper's additional corollaries are not included.\n\nThe formalization proves integrability of both summands in a holomorphic splitting of the tangent bundle of a rationally connected projective manifold. Mor","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/052.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":53186,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Integrability-of-split-tangent-bundles-on-rationally-connected-manifolds-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"053-01","slug":"A-high-arity-counterexample-to-Pixton-completeness-in-Chow-September-24-2026","familyId":"053","title":"A high-arity counterexample to Pixton completeness in Chow","abstract":"We disprove the Chow and rational-cohomological forms of Pixton's completeness conjecture for his original relation system. We construct a formal tautological class outside the original Pixton relation span whose image is zero in the Chow ring and in rational cohomology. The example has genus $10^{60}$ and $3\\binom{10^{60}}3$ markings.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a tautological relation on a moduli space of stable pointed curves that vanishes in rational Chow, hence in rational cohomology, but lies outside Pixton's original relation span. This disproves the Chow and rational-cohomological forms of his original completeness conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-high-arity-counterexample-to-Pixton-completeness-in-Chow-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-high-arity-counterexample-to-Pixton-completeness-in-Chow-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-high-arity-counterexample-to-Pixton-completeness-in-Chow-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-high-arity-counterexample-to-Pixton-completeness-in-Chow-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-high-arity-counterexample-to-Pixton-completeness-in-Chow-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-high-arity-counterexample-to-Pixton-completeness-in-Chow-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-map.tex","preprints/A-high-arity-counterexample-to-Pixton-completeness-in-Chow-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-strata.tex","preprints/A-high-arity-counterexample-to-Pixton-completeness-in-Chow-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-detector.tex","preprints/A-high-arity-counterexample-to-Pixton-completeness-in-Chow-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-tensors.tex","preprints/A-high-arity-counterexample-to-Pixton-completeness-in-Chow-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-quot.tex","preprints/A-high-arity-counterexample-to-Pixton-completeness-in-Chow-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-coefficient.tex","preprints/A-high-arity-counterexample-to-Pixton-completeness-in-Chow-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-compactification.tex","preprints/A-high-arity-counterexample-to-Pixton-completeness-in-Chow-September-24-2026/build/figures/bridge-cuts.tex","preprints/A-high-arity-counterexample-to-Pixton-completeness-in-Chow-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-support.tex","preprints/A-high-arity-counterexample-to-Pixton-completeness-in-Chow-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-high-arity-counterexample-to-Pixton-completeness-in-Chow-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove the Chow and rational-cohomological forms of Pixton's completeness conjecture for his original relation system. We construct a formal tautological class outside the original Pixton relation span whose image is zero in the Chow ring and in rational cohomology. The example has genus \\(10^{60}\\) and \\(3\\binom{10^{60}}3\\) markings.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":404},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The relation problem","sourceOffset":1015},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The counterexample","sourceOffset":3897},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof and methods","sourceOffset":8013},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":15809},{"kind":"section","title":"The strata algebra and the explicit class","sourceOffset":18306},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Decorated graphs","sourceOffset":18554},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exact Pixton span","sourceOffset":21157},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The corrected cubic and its alternation","sourceOffset":25491},{"kind":"section","title":"A formal detector","sourceOffset":27624},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An isotropic cubic Frobenius algebra","sourceOffset":28437},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compression of rational tails","sourceOffset":32439}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Set \\[ g=10^{60},\\qquad D_*=\\binom g3,\\qquad n=3D_*. \\] The explicitly defined class \\(Y\\in \\Salg_{g,n}^{2D_*}\\) of Definition \\ref{def:candidate} satisfies \\[ q_{g,n}(Y)=0,\\qquad Y\\notin\\Pspan_{g,n}. \\] Consequently Pixton's original relation span does not exhaust the tautological Chow relations: the equality \\(\\ker q_{g,n}=\\Pspan_{g,n}\\) fails for this stable pair.","sourceOffset":4322,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Rational-cohomological incompleteness","label":"cor:cohomological-incompleteness","text":"For the stable pair and class \\(Y\\) of Theorem \\ref{thm:main}, \\[ q^H_{g,n}(Y)=0 \\quad\\text{in }H^{4D_*}\\!\\left(\\Mbar_{g,n}(\\C);\\Q\\right), \\qquad Y\\notin\\Pspan_{g,n}. \\] In particular, \\(\\Pspan_{g,n}\\subsetneq\\ker q^H_{g,n}\\).","sourceOffset":5361,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Pixton's original span","label":"def:pixton-span","text":"Let \\(\\sigma=(\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_\\ell)\\) be a partition with no part congruent to \\(2\\) modulo \\(3\\), possibly empty, and let \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_n\\geq0\\) also avoid that residue class. For \\(0\\leq r\\leq3g-3+n\\), require \\begin{equation} 3r\\geq g+1+|\\sigma|+\\sum_i a_i,\\qquad 3r\\equiv g+1+|\\sigma|+\\sum_i a_i\\pmod2. \\end{equation} Set \\begin{align} P_\\Gamma={}& \\left[ 2^{-h^1(\\Gamma)} \\widehat\\kappa_\\Gamma \\left( \\exp\\{1-\\widehat C_0\\} \\prod_{j=1}^{\\ell}\\{\\widehat C_{\\sigma_j}\\} \\right)\\right.\\notag\\\\ &\\left.\\hspace{12mm}\\cdot \\prod_{i=1}^n\\widehat C_{a_i}(\\psi_iT,\\zeta_{v(i)}) \\prod_{e\\in E(\\Gam","sourceOffset":22512,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"The explicit counterexample","label":"def:candidate","text":"On three labeled slots \\(J=(a,b,c)\\), take every one-edge unmarked stable graph of genus \\(g\\), subdivide its edge by a genus-zero vertex, and put all three labels at that new vertex. Sum the resulting undecorated graphs, dividing each by its automorphism order. Pull this sum back by forgetting all labels outside \\(J\\); call the resulting class \\(\\beta_J^{\\mathrm{st}}\\). Define \\begin{equation} F_J^{\\mathrm{st}}= \\bar D_{ab}\\bar D_{ac}-\\frac12\\beta_J^{\\mathrm{st}}, \\qquad Y=\\sum_{\\substack{\\tau\\in\\mathfrak S_n\\\\\\tau\\mathbf i=\\mathbf i}} \\operatorname{sgn}(\\tau)\\, \\tau\\left(\\prod_J F_J^{\\mathrm","sourceOffset":26204,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":215149,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-high-arity-counterexample-to-Pixton-completeness-in-Chow-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"054-01","slug":"Irrational-cubic-fourfolds-with-geometric-K3-categories-September-24-2026","familyId":"054","title":"Irrational cubic fourfolds with geometric K3 categories","abstract":"For every sufficiently large admissible Hassett discriminant, we prove that a very general cubic fourfold of that discriminant is irrational, although its Kuznetsov component is equivalent to the ordinary derived category of a projective K3 surface. The discriminant threshold is ineffective. This disproves Kuznetsov's rationality conjecture. The same cubics have associated untwisted polarized K3 surfaces in the Hodge-theoretic sense, so they also disprove the sufficiency direction of the associated-K3 rationality prediction.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every sufficiently large admissible Hassett discriminant, a very general smooth complex cubic fourfold is irrational despite having both an untwisted geometric K3 category and an integral Hodge-theoretic K3 association. This disproves Kuznetsov's rationality conjecture and the sufficiency of the associated-K3 criterion for rationality; the discriminant threshold is ineffective.","pdfPath":"preprints/Irrational-cubic-fourfolds-with-geometric-K3-categories-September-24-2026/Irrational-cubic-fourfolds-with-geometric-K3-categories-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Irrational-cubic-fourfolds-with-geometric-K3-categories-September-24-2026/Irrational-cubic-fourfolds-with-geometric-K3-categories-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Irrational-cubic-fourfolds-with-geometric-K3-categories-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Irrational-cubic-fourfolds-with-geometric-K3-categories-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Irrational-cubic-fourfolds-with-geometric-K3-categories-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Irrational-cubic-fourfolds-with-geometric-K3-categories-September-24-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Irrational-cubic-fourfolds-with-geometric-K3-categories-September-24-2026/build/sections/gw.tex","preprints/Irrational-cubic-fourfolds-with-geometric-K3-categories-September-24-2026/build/sections/bounds.tex","preprints/Irrational-cubic-fourfolds-with-geometric-K3-categories-September-24-2026/build/sections/surface-links.tex","preprints/Irrational-cubic-fourfolds-with-geometric-K3-categories-September-24-2026/build/sections/subfields.tex","preprints/Irrational-cubic-fourfolds-with-geometric-K3-categories-September-24-2026/build/sections/cubics.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Irrational-cubic-fourfolds-with-geometric-K3-categories-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every sufficiently large admissible Hassett discriminant, we prove that a very general cubic fourfold of that discriminant is irrational, although its Kuznetsov component is equivalent to the ordinary derived category of a projective K3 surface. The discriminant threshold is ineffective. This disproves Kuznetsov's rationality conjecture. The same cubics have associated untwisted polarized K3 surfaces in the Hodge-theoretic sense, so they also disprove the sufficiency direction of the associated-K3 rationality prediction.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":618},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two evaluations of one divisorial invariant","sourceOffset":5569},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Consequences for K3 geometry and zero-cycles","sourceOffset":11790},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Relation to birational invariants and quantum methods","sourceOffset":15411},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Notation and organization","sourceOffset":19042},{"kind":"section","title":"Birational fields and a divisorial cocycle","sourceOffset":21008},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The K3 test","sourceOffset":22253},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prime divisors as valuations","sourceOffset":27627},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The smooth factorization used below","sourceOffset":31571},{"kind":"section","title":"Whole transcendental lattices under factorization","sourceOffset":32456},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Hodge structures and the operator algebra","sourceOffset":33167},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Classical degeneration and local path tensors","sourceOffset":40695}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Irrationality on large admissible Hassett divisors","label":"thm:main","text":"There is an integer \\(d_0\\) such that, for every admissible \\(d>d_0\\), a very general cubic fourfold \\(X\\) in \\(\\mathcal C_d\\) is irrational and admits an exact \\(\\C\\)-linear equivalence \\[ \\Ku(X)\\simeq D^b(\\operatorname{Coh}S) \\] for a smooth projective K3 surface \\(S\\).","sourceOffset":4521,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Failure of associated-K3 sufficiency","label":"cor:associated-k3","text":"Let \\(d_0\\) be the threshold in Theorem \\ref{thm:main}. For every admissible \\(d>d_0\\), a very general \\(X\\in\\mathcal C_d\\) is irrational and admits a labelling \\(K\\) of discriminant \\(d\\) and a primitively polarized smooth projective K3 surface \\((S_{\\mathrm{Hdg}},L)\\), with \\(L^2=d\\), for which there is an integral Hodge isometry \\[ K^\\perp \\simeq L^\\perp(-1). \\] Here \\(L^\\perp\\subset H^2(S_{\\mathrm{Hdg}},\\Z)\\), and the surface cup pairing is reversed. Thus an associated untwisted polarized K3 surface in this Hodge-theoretic sense is not sufficient for rationality.","sourceOffset":12273,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"The test function","label":"pre:test","text":"For a finitely generated field $F/\\C$, choose a smooth projective model $V$ and set \\[ u(F)= \\begin{cases} 1,&\\text{if }R(V)\\text{ is birational to some }S\\in\\mathcal S,\\\\ 0,&\\text{otherwise}. \\end{cases} \\] For an integral variety $V$, put $u(V)=u(\\C(V))$.","sourceOffset":23432,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"gw:algebra","text":"For every invariant \\[ B(u,v)= \\left\\langle\\tau_a(u),\\tau_b(v), \\tau_{a_1}(\\gamma_1),\\ldots,\\tau_{a_n}(\\gamma_n) \\right\\rangle^M_{0,\\beta},\\qquad u,v\\in T_{M,\\Q}, \\] let $A_B\\in\\operatorname{End}_{\\Q}(T_{M,\\Q})$ be determined by $B(u,v)=q_M(A_Bu,v)$. Let $G_M$ be the unital rational algebra generated by all such operators. On the zero space we take the zero algebra.","sourceOffset":36804,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":225385,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":19,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Irrational-cubic-fourfolds-with-geometric-K3-categories-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"055-01","slug":"A-Gepner-stability-condition-on-every-smooth-quintic-threefold-September-24-2026","familyId":"055","title":"A Gepner stability condition on every smooth quintic threefold","abstract":"We prove Toda's normalized quintic Gepner conjecture. On every smooth complex quintic threefold, we construct a numerical Bridgeland stability condition for which tensoring by the hyperplane bundle, followed by the spherical twist at the structure sheaf, increases phase by 2/5.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Toda's Gepner conjecture for every smooth complex quintic threefold, constructing a numerical Bridgeland stability condition with the prescribed phase shift 2/5. Also constructs numerical Bridgeland stability conditions at every sufficiently large volume on all smooth projective complex threefolds with trivial canonical bundle, with the exact ordinary and square-root-Todd central charges.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Gepner-stability-condition-on-every-smooth-quintic-threefold-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Gepner-stability-condition-on-every-smooth-quintic-threefold-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Gepner-stability-condition-on-every-smooth-quintic-threefold-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-Gepner-stability-condition-on-every-smooth-quintic-threefold-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-Gepner-stability-condition-on-every-smooth-quintic-threefold-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-Gepner-stability-condition-on-every-smooth-quintic-threefold-September-24-2026/build/sections/numerical.tex","preprints/A-Gepner-stability-condition-on-every-smooth-quintic-threefold-September-24-2026/build/sections/aisles.tex","preprints/A-Gepner-stability-condition-on-every-smooth-quintic-threefold-September-24-2026/build/sections/grid.tex","preprints/A-Gepner-stability-condition-on-every-smooth-quintic-threefold-September-24-2026/build/figures/fifth-phase-grid.tex","preprints/A-Gepner-stability-condition-on-every-smooth-quintic-threefold-September-24-2026/build/sections/periodicity.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Gepner-stability-condition-on-every-smooth-quintic-threefold-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Toda's normalized quintic Gepner conjecture. On every smooth complex quintic threefold, we construct a numerical Bridgeland stability condition for which tensoring by the hyperplane bundle, followed by the spherical twist at the structure sheaf, increases phase by $2/5$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":326},{"kind":"section","title":"Periodicity and the numerical central charge","sourceOffset":8307},{"kind":"section","title":"A uniform family of double-tilt aisles","sourceOffset":15720},{"kind":"section","title":"From uniform aisle inclusions to a slicing","sourceOffset":37456},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finer grid","sourceOffset":38874},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Full numerical support on the blocks","sourceOffset":47436},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite refinement inside a block","sourceOffset":50438},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The slicing and its phase cuts","sourceOffset":55873},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Quasi-abelian intervals and local finite length","sourceOffset":62131},{"kind":"section","title":"A relative-kernel proof of periodicity","sourceOffset":67892}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Every smooth complex quintic threefold admits a numerical Bridgeland stability condition $\\sigma=(Z,\\mathcal P)$ with the support property on $\\Knum(X)_{\\R}$ such that, for every $E\\in\\D$ and $\\varphi\\in\\R$, \\[ Z(\\Phi E)=e^{2\\pi i/5}Z(E),\\qquad \\Phi\\bigl(\\mathcal P(\\varphi)\\bigr)=\\mathcal P(\\varphi+2/5),\\qquad Z(\\OO_x)=-1 \\] for every closed point $x\\in X$.","sourceOffset":3073,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:central-charge","text":"There is a unique numerical homomorphism $Z\\colon\\Knum(X)\\to\\C$ such that $Z\\circ\\Phi=\\lambda Z$ and $Z(\\OO_x)=-1$ for closed points $x\\in X$. It is \\begin{equation} \\frac{Z(r,c,d,e)}5 =\\frac{\\lambda-1}{5}r +\\left(t_0^2-\\frac56+\\frac{i t_0}{2}\\right)c +\\left(-\\frac12+i t_0\\right)d-e. \\end{equation} In particular, \\begin{equation} \\frac{\\Im Z(r,c,d,e)}{5t_0}=d+\\frac12c+ur. \\end{equation} For any fixed norm on $\\Knum(X)_{\\R}$, there is a constant $C_0>0$ such that every real numerical class $v=(r,c,d,e)$ satisfies \\begin{equation} \\norm{v}\\le C_0\\bigl(|r|+|c|+|Z(v)|\\bigr). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":13857,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":73856,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Gepner-stability-condition-on-every-smooth-quintic-threefold-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"055-02","slug":"Prescribed-large-volume-charges-on-threefolds-with-trivial-canonical-bundle-September-24-2026","familyId":"055","title":"Prescribed large-volume charges on threefolds with trivial canonical bundle","abstract":"Let X be a smooth projective complex threefold with trivial canonical bundle. We construct numerical Bridgeland stability conditions with the exact ordinary and square-root-Todd central charges at every sufficiently large volume. One volume threshold works on an open set of real twists and ample directions. The resulting stability conditions have the support property on the full numerical Grothendieck group and stable point sheaves. Separately, on every smooth projective complex threefold we prove a strong tilt inequality above a volume threshold uniform in the object and twist along a fixed polarization.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Toda's Gepner conjecture for every smooth complex quintic threefold, constructing a numerical Bridgeland stability condition with the prescribed phase shift 2/5. Also constructs numerical Bridgeland stability conditions at every sufficiently large volume on all smooth projective complex threefolds with trivial canonical bundle, with the exact ordinary and square-root-Todd central charges.","pdfPath":"preprints/Prescribed-large-volume-charges-on-threefolds-with-trivial-canonical-bundle-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Prescribed-large-volume-charges-on-threefolds-with-trivial-canonical-bundle-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Prescribed-large-volume-charges-on-threefolds-with-trivial-canonical-bundle-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Prescribed-large-volume-charges-on-threefolds-with-trivial-canonical-bundle-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Prescribed-large-volume-charges-on-threefolds-with-trivial-canonical-bundle-September-24-2026/build/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Prescribed-large-volume-charges-on-threefolds-with-trivial-canonical-bundle-September-24-2026/build/06-ambient.tex","preprints/Prescribed-large-volume-charges-on-threefolds-with-trivial-canonical-bundle-September-24-2026/build/07-heart.tex","preprints/Prescribed-large-volume-charges-on-threefolds-with-trivial-canonical-bundle-September-24-2026/build/02-tilt.tex","preprints/Prescribed-large-volume-charges-on-threefolds-with-trivial-canonical-bundle-September-24-2026/build/03-analysis.tex","preprints/Prescribed-large-volume-charges-on-threefolds-with-trivial-canonical-bundle-September-24-2026/build/04-apex.tex","preprints/Prescribed-large-volume-charges-on-threefolds-with-trivial-canonical-bundle-September-24-2026/build/05-wall.tex","preprints/Prescribed-large-volume-charges-on-threefolds-with-trivial-canonical-bundle-September-24-2026/build/08-counterexamples.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Prescribed-large-volume-charges-on-threefolds-with-trivial-canonical-bundle-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let $X$ be a smooth projective complex threefold with trivial canonical bundle. We construct numerical Bridgeland stability conditions with the exact ordinary and square-root-Todd central charges at every sufficiently large volume. One volume threshold works on an open set of real twists and ample directions. The resulting stability conditions have the support property on the full numerical Grothendieck group and stable point sheaves. Separately, on every smooth projective complex threefold we prove a strong tilt inequality above a volume threshold uniform in the object and twist along a fixed polarization.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":677},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prescribed charges and the full numerical group","sourceOffset":2581},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Independent uniform tilt inequalities","sourceOffset":7516},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Previous constructions","sourceOffset":12356},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two independent proof strategies","sourceOffset":15080},{"kind":"section","title":"Prescribed charges on the full numerical lattice","sourceOffset":20975},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An embedding which retains every numerical class","sourceOffset":22653},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Scaled support on elliptic products","sourceOffset":25734},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Transfer to projective blocks","sourceOffset":31475},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Geometric slicings and the restriction criterion","sourceOffset":38954},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Correcting the charge before restriction","sourceOffset":44601},{"kind":"section","title":"The ordinary double tilt throughout the real sector","sourceOffset":54206}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Exact large-volume charges on a real sector","label":"thm:geometric-sector","text":"Let $X$ be a smooth projective complex threefold with $K_X\\simeq\\OO_X$. For any $B_*\\in N^1(X)_{\\RR}$ and $H_*\\in\\operatorname{Amp}(X)$ there are open neighborhoods $V$ of $B_*$ and $W$ of $H_*$ and one $T>0$ such that, for all $B\\in V$, $H\\in W$ and $t\\geq T$, the following hold. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item $(Z_{B,tH},\\mathcal A_{B,tH})$ is a geometric numerical stability condition with support on $\\Knum(X)_{\\RR}$. \\item There is a geometric numerical stability condition, with support on the same full numerical space, whose charge is exactly \\[ Z^{\\mathrm{LV}}_{B,tH}(E) =-\\int_Xe^{-B-itH}\\ch(E)\\sqrt{\\td(X)}, \\qquad \\sqrt{\\td(X)}=1+\\frac{c_2(X)}{24}. \\] Its heart is the one constructed in Section \\ref{sec:ambient}. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":5632,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform strong inequality","label":"thm:uniform-inequality","text":"Let $X$ be a smooth projective complex threefold, $H$ an ample integral divisor, and $B_0\\in N^1(X)_{\\QQ}$. There are constants $k\\in\\QQ_{\\geq0}$ and $R_*>0$, depending only on $(X,H,B_0)$, such that every finite-slope $\\nu_{a,b}$-semistable $E\\in\\mathcal B_{H,B_0+bH}$ with $\\nu_{a,b}(E)=0$ satisfies \\begin{equation} \\ch_3^{B_0+bH}(E) \\leq \\left(\\frac{a^2}{6}+k\\right)H^2\\ch_1^{B_0+bH}(E) \\qquad(a>0,\\ b\\in\\RR). \\end{equation} For the same fixed data, the stronger bound \\begin{equation} \\ch_3^{B_0+bH}(E) \\leq \\frac{a^2}{6}H^2\\ch_1^{B_0+bH}(E) \\end{equation} holds whenever $a>R_*$, for every $b\\in\\RR$.","sourceOffset":8698,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":171381,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Prescribed-large-volume-charges-on-threefolds-with-trivial-canonical-bundle-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Prescribed-large-volume-charges-on-threefolds-with-trivial-canonical-bundle-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"056-01","slug":"Termination-of-generalized-log-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-7-2026","familyId":"056","title":"Termination of generalized log canonical flips on compact Kähler fourfolds","abstract":"Every sequence of generalized log canonical flips on a normal irreducible globally Weil ℚ-factorial compact Kähler fourfold terminates, provided the flips are projective small diagrams with the stated opposite ample signs. The boundary is rational, and the nef b-divisor is fixed and represented by an analytically nef ℚ-Cartier divisor on a projective modification. No scaling rule or pseudo-effectivity assumption is required. The theorem concerns existing flip sequences; it does not assert the existence of all contractions or flips.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves termination of every existing generalized log canonical flip sequence on globally Weil ℚ-factorial compact Kähler fourfolds, with rational boundary, fixed rational analytically nef b-data, and projective small flip diagrams with the prescribed ample signs. Also proves termination of arbitrary permitted minimal model programs for projective log canonical fourfolds with rational boundary in characteristic zero.","pdfPath":"preprints/Termination-of-generalized-log-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-7-2026/termination-generalized-lc-kahler-fourfolds.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Termination-of-generalized-log-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-7-2026/termination-generalized-lc-kahler-fourfolds.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Termination-of-generalized-log-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-7-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Termination-of-generalized-log-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-7-2026/build/termination-generalized-lc-kahler-fourfolds.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Termination-of-generalized-log-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-7-2026/build/termination-generalized-lc-kahler-fourfolds.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every sequence of generalized log canonical flips on a normal irreducible globally Weil \\(\\Q\\)-factorial compact K\\\"ahler fourfold terminates, provided the flips are projective small diagrams with the stated opposite ample signs. The boundary is rational, and the nef b-divisor is fixed and represented by an analytically nef \\(\\Q\\)-Cartier divisor on a projective modification. No scaling rule or pseudo-effectivity assumption is required. The theorem concerns existing flip sequences; it does not assert the existence of all contractions or flips.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":613},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":7304},{"kind":"section","title":"Generalized pairs and auxiliary programs","sourceOffset":11642},{"kind":"section","title":"Birational comparisons","sourceOffset":25419},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Common models and the nef part","sourceOffset":27149},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Small diagrams and finite programs","sourceOffset":35043},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A nef end and a prescribed ample model","sourceOffset":45331},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ranks of analytic cycle classes","sourceOffset":47925},{"kind":"section","title":"Terminal junctions and surface centers","sourceOffset":51915},{"kind":"section","title":"The branch inequality","sourceOffset":77086},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Projectivity over the center and normalization relations","sourceOffset":81452},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A smooth resolution and purity over the center","sourceOffset":90278}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(X_0\\) be a normal irreducible globally Weil \\(\\Q\\)-factorial compact K\\\"ahler fourfold. Let \\(B_0\\) be an effective rational Weil divisor of finite support with coefficients in \\([0,1]\\). Let \\(\\M\\) be represented by an analytically nef \\(\\Q\\)-Cartier divisor on a projective bimeromorphic normal compact K\\\"ahler model of \\(X_0\\). Fix compatible actual canonical divisor representatives on all birational models under consideration. Suppose that \\(D_0=K_{X_0}+B_0+M_{X_0}\\) is \\(\\Q\\)-Cartier and that \\((X_0,B_0+\\M)\\) is generalized log canonical. Consider a sequence of normal irreducible globally Weil \\(\\Q\\)-factorial compact K\\\"ahler fourfolds \\[ X_0\\dashrightarrow X_1\\dashrightarrow X_2\\dashrightarrow\\cdots. \\] On \\(X_i\\), let \\(B_i\\) be the strict transform of \\(B_0\\), let \\(M_i=M_{X_i}\\) be the trace of the same b-divisor, and assume that \\(D_i=K_{X_i}+B_i+M_i\\) is \\(\\Q\\)-Cartier. Transport the canonical representatives under the induced identifications of prime divisors. Suppose that every step is given by a diagram \\begin{equation} \\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large] X_i \\arrow[r,\"f_i\"] & Z_i & X_{i+1}\\arrow[l,\"f_i^+\"'] \\end{tikzcd} \\end{equation} where \\(Z_i\\) is normal compact K\\\"ahler, both maps are projective small bimeromorphic morphisms with connected fibers, neither is an isomorphism, and \\[ -D_i\\text{ is }f_i\\text{-ample},\\qquad D_{i+1}\\text{ is }f_i^+\\text{-ample}. \\] Here small means that the exceptional locus contains no prime divisor. Then the sequence is fin","sourceOffset":5309,"truncated":true},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Low places and projective realization","label":"prop:low-places","text":"Let \\((T,B+\\M)\\) be gklt rational generalized data on a normal compact K\\\"ahler space, with effective boundary of finite support and an absolutely nef rational datum carried by a projective modification. Fix a projective log resolution carrying the datum. There is \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) such that every global place satisfies \\[ a(E;T,B+\\M)\\geq\\varepsilon, \\] and only finitely many places exceptional over \\(T\\) satisfy \\[ a(E;T,B+\\M)<1+\\varepsilon. \\] This finite set can be represented simultaneously by prime divisors on a smooth model projective over \\(T\\). In particular the exceptional places of discrepancy at most one form a finite set with such a realization.","sourceOffset":19027,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Global strong factoriality","label":"def:strong-factoriality","text":"A normal compact complex space \\(T\\) is globally strongly \\(\\Q\\)-factorial if, for every coherent rank-one reflexive sheaf \\(\\mathcal F\\) on all of \\(T\\), some positive reflexive power \\[ \\mathcal F^{[m]}=(\\mathcal F^{\\otimes m})^{**},\\qquad m>0, \\] is an invertible sheaf.","sourceOffset":12193,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":190837,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":15,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Termination-of-generalized-log-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-7-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"056-02","slug":"Termination-of-generalized-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026","familyId":"056","title":"Termination of generalized-canonical flips on compact Kähler fourfolds","abstract":"Every sequence of generalized-canonical flips on compact Kähler fourfolds with rational boundary and fixed rational analytically nef b-divisor terminates when the small morphisms are projective. We prove this for normal globally Weil ℚ-factorial models with boundary coefficients less than one, allowing exceptional log discrepancies equal to one. No scaling rule or pseudo-effectivity assumption is required. The theorem applies to existing projective small diagrams with the specified opposite ample signs.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves termination of every existing generalized log canonical flip sequence on globally Weil ℚ-factorial compact Kähler fourfolds, with rational boundary, fixed rational analytically nef b-data, and projective small flip diagrams with the prescribed ample signs. Also proves termination of arbitrary permitted minimal model programs for projective log canonical fourfolds with rational boundary in characteristic zero.","pdfPath":"preprints/Termination-of-generalized-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/termination-generalized-terminal-flips-compact-kahler-fourfolds.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Termination-of-generalized-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/termination-generalized-terminal-flips-compact-kahler-fourfolds.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Termination-of-generalized-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Termination-of-generalized-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/build/paper.tex","preprints/Termination-of-generalized-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Termination-of-generalized-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/build/sections/02-foundations.tex","preprints/Termination-of-generalized-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/build/sections/03-comparison.tex","preprints/Termination-of-generalized-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/build/sections/04-low-places.tex","preprints/Termination-of-generalized-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/build/sections/05-witnesses.tex","preprints/Termination-of-generalized-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/build/sections/06-cycles.tex","preprints/Termination-of-generalized-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/build/sections/07-termination.tex","preprints/Termination-of-generalized-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Termination-of-generalized-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every sequence of generalized-canonical flips on compact K\\\"ahler fourfolds with rational boundary and fixed rational analytically nef b-divisor terminates when the small morphisms are projective. We prove this for normal globally Weil $\\Q$-factorial models with boundary coefficients less than one, allowing exceptional log discrepancies equal to one. No scaling rule or pseudo-effectivity assumption is required. The theorem applies to existing projective small diagrams with the specified opposite ample signs.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction and the main theorem","sourceOffset":560},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The data and the statement","sourceOffset":1721},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Preceding results and the present argument","sourceOffset":6651},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":10562},{"kind":"section","title":"Analytic foundations and common models","sourceOffset":14313},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Places and projective resolutions","sourceOffset":14667},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Negativity for actual divisors","sourceOffset":19519},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ordinary terminal singularities in codimension two","sourceOffset":20748},{"kind":"section","title":"Discrepancies across one flip","sourceOffset":24380},{"kind":"section","title":"Places of small discrepancy","sourceOffset":34634},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The nef trace and local estimates","sourceOffset":35196},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite exceptional set of places","sourceOffset":39183}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:termination","text":"Let $X_0$ be a normal irreducible globally Weil $\\Q$-factorial compact K\\\"ahler fourfold, and let $B_0\\geq0$ be a rational divisor with coefficients in $[0,1)$. Fix compatible actual canonical divisors on the birational models. Let $\\bM$ be the rational b-divisor represented by an analytically nef $\\Q$-Cartier divisor $M'$ on a projective birational morphism $X'\\to X_0$ of normal compact K\\\"ahler spaces. Assume \\[ \\begin{aligned} a(E;X_0,B_0+\\bM)&>0&&\\text{for every prime over $X_0$},\\\\ a(E;X_0,B_0+\\bM)&\\geq1&&\\text{for every exceptional prime}. \\end{aligned} \\] Consider a sequence of normal globally Weil $\\Q$-factorial compact K\\\"ahler fourfolds \\[ X_0\\dashrightarrow X_1\\dashrightarrow X_2\\dashrightarrow\\cdots . \\] On $X_i$, let $B_i$ be the strict transform of $B_0$, let $M_i$ be the trace of this same $\\bM$, and suppose $D_i=K_{X_i}+B_i+M_i$ is $\\Q$-Cartier. Suppose that every step is given by a diagram \\begin{equation} X_i\\xrightarrow{\\ f_i\\ }Z_i \\xleftarrow{\\ f_i^+\\ }X_{i+1} \\end{equation} of projective small bimeromorphic morphisms with connected fibres, where $Z_i$ is normal compact K\\\"ahler, both morphisms are nonisomorphisms, $-D_i$ is $f_i$-ample, and $D_{i+1}$ is $f_i^+$-ample. Then the sequence is finite.","sourceOffset":4988,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Common models","label":"prop:common-models","text":"Any finite portion of the sequence in Theorem \\ref{thm:termination} has a smooth common model dominating its members and $X'$, projective over each member. It may also dominate any prescribed projective modification of one of those members. For a chosen member $T$, it can be arranged that the exceptional locus over $T$ is divisorial and that its union with the strict transform of $\\Supp B_T$ has simple normal crossings. The model carries $\\bM$ as the pullback of $M'$.","sourceOffset":17295,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":80513,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Termination-of-generalized-canonical-flips-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"056-03","slug":"Termination-for-projective-log-canonical-fourfolds-with-rational-boundary-September-24-2026","familyId":"056","title":"Termination for projective log canonical fourfolds with rational boundary","abstract":"We prove that every permitted minimal model program for a projective log canonical fourfold with rational boundary over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero terminates. The result allows arbitrary negative extremal rays and mixed birational steps on the given models, without pseudo-effectivity or initial ℚ-factoriality.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves termination of every existing generalized log canonical flip sequence on globally Weil ℚ-factorial compact Kähler fourfolds, with rational boundary, fixed rational analytically nef b-data, and projective small flip diagrams with the prescribed ample signs. Also proves termination of arbitrary permitted minimal model programs for projective log canonical fourfolds with rational boundary in characteristic zero.","pdfPath":"preprints/Termination-for-projective-log-canonical-fourfolds-with-rational-boundary-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Termination-for-projective-log-canonical-fourfolds-with-rational-boundary-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Termination-for-projective-log-canonical-fourfolds-with-rational-boundary-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Termination-for-projective-log-canonical-fourfolds-with-rational-boundary-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Termination-for-projective-log-canonical-fourfolds-with-rational-boundary-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Termination-for-projective-log-canonical-fourfolds-with-rational-boundary-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-comparisons.tex","preprints/Termination-for-projective-log-canonical-fourfolds-with-rational-boundary-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-terminal-models.tex","preprints/Termination-for-projective-log-canonical-fourfolds-with-rational-boundary-September-24-2026/build/sections/02a-difficulty-setup.tex","preprints/Termination-for-projective-log-canonical-fourfolds-with-rational-boundary-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-branch-topology.tex","preprints/Termination-for-projective-log-canonical-fourfolds-with-rational-boundary-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-local-valuations.tex","preprints/Termination-for-projective-log-canonical-fourfolds-with-rational-boundary-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-difficulty.tex","preprints/Termination-for-projective-log-canonical-fourfolds-with-rational-boundary-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-lc-and-existence.tex","preprints/Termination-for-projective-log-canonical-fourfolds-with-rational-boundary-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-field-transfer.tex","preprints/Termination-for-projective-log-canonical-fourfolds-with-rational-boundary-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Termination-for-projective-log-canonical-fourfolds-with-rational-boundary-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every permitted minimal model program for a projective log canonical fourfold with rational boundary over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero terminates. The result allows arbitrary negative extremal rays and mixed birational steps on the given models, without pseudo-effectivity or initial $\\mathbb Q$-factoriality.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction and statement","sourceOffset":410},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The difficulty and the branch estimate","sourceOffset":8116},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Foundational inputs","sourceOffset":11788},{"kind":"section","title":"Graph comparisons and cycle ranks","sourceOffset":12803},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The full bad loci","sourceOffset":13761},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ranks of cycle classes","sourceOffset":18900},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Identifying a prescribed ample end","sourceOffset":22164},{"kind":"section","title":"Terminal junctions and the coefficients that can vary","sourceOffset":24161},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A log-smooth calculation","sourceOffset":25001},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Crepant terminalizations and the finite bridges","sourceOffset":32041},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Surface mld ACC and the centers of varying labels","sourceOffset":38449},{"kind":"section","title":"The difficulty and its local deficit","sourceOffset":43959}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, and let $(X,B)$ be a projective lc pair of dimension four with rational boundary. No $\\mathbb Q$-factoriality or pseudo-effectivity is assumed. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item Every negative extremal ray at every stage has the contraction and, when birational, a positive model required by Definition \\ref{def:program}. \\item Every program of Definition \\ref{def:program} starting from $(X,B)$ has finitely many birational steps, for every sequence of permitted choices. \\item Every maximal program ends either at a projective lc pair with nef adjoint, or at a Mori fibre space: a projective contraction with connected fibres, relative Picard number one, lower-dimensional normal base, and relatively ample negative adjoint. \\end{enumerate} An already nef input has a zero-step completed program.","sourceOffset":6683,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:singularities-preserved","text":"Under the hypotheses of Lemma \\ref{lem:graph}, lc and klt are preserved. For a small step, an effective terminal pair remains terminal. At a divisor-losing step the discrepancy of every lost prime divisor increases strictly.","sourceOffset":18427,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Programs and mixed steps","label":"def:program","text":"Let $(X,B)$ be a projective lc pair. Set $(X_0,B_0)=(X,B)$ and $D_i=K_{X_i}+B_i$. At a non-nef stage choose any $D_i$-negative extremal ray of $\\overline{\\operatorname{NE}}(X_i)$ and its projective contraction \\[ f_i:X_i\\longrightarrow Z_i, \\qquad (f_i)_*\\mathcal O_{X_i}=\\mathcal O_{Z_i}, \\qquad \\rho(X_i/Z_i)=1. \\] The target is normal, and $-D_i$ is relatively ample. Stop if $f_i$ is of fibre type. If it is birational, a permitted step is \\[ X_i\\xrightarrow{\\ f_i\\ }Z_i \\xleftarrow{\\ f_i^+\\ }X_{i+1}, \\] where $f_i^+$ is small and projective birational, possibly an isomorphism, $(X_{i+1},B_{i+1","sourceOffset":5302,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:difficulty","text":"For a terminal pair \\((Y,\\Theta)\\) on the prepared chain, define \\begin{align} \\mathcal D(Y,\\Theta) ={}&\\sum_{\\operatorname{codim}_Y c_Y(v)\\geq3}w_v \\\\ &+\\sum_{\\substack{V\\subset Y\\text{ irreducible}\\\\\\dim V=2}} \\left( \\sum_{c_Y(v)=V}w_v-\\sum_h r_{V,h}d_h \\right) +\\sum_h d_h\\rho_*(S_h^\\nu). \\notag \\end{align} The correction in parentheses is performed at each individual surface before summing over surfaces.","sourceOffset":47892,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":152388,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":15,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Termination-for-projective-log-canonical-fourfolds-with-rational-boundary-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Termination-for-projective-log-canonical-fourfolds-with-rational-boundary-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"056-04","slug":"Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026","familyId":"056","title":"Finite ordinary minimal model programs on compact Kähler fourfolds","abstract":"We prove that every maximal ordinary negative-ray program starting from a compact Kähler klt fourfold pair with effective rational boundary in the global Weil-divisor ℚ-factorial category terminates. It ends at a nef model when the adjoint is pseudo-effective and at a projective Mori fibre space otherwise.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves termination of every existing generalized log canonical flip sequence on globally Weil ℚ-factorial compact Kähler fourfolds, with rational boundary, fixed rational analytically nef b-data, and projective small flip diagrams with the prescribed ample signs. Also proves termination of arbitrary permitted minimal model programs for projective log canonical fourfolds with rational boundary in characteristic zero.","pdfPath":"preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/figures/dependencies.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/02-analytic.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/03-steps.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/04-terminal.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/05-scaling.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/06-perturbation.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/07-endpoints.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/08-resolutions.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/08-relative.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/09-threefold.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/10-special.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/11-threshold.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/12-supply.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/13-local-convention.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every maximal ordinary negative-ray program starting from a compact Kähler klt fourfold pair with effective rational boundary in the global Weil-divisor $\\Q$-factorial category terminates. It ends at a nef model when the adjoint is pseudo-effective and at a projective Mori fibre space otherwise.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":373},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Category and main theorem","sourceOffset":1010},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work and proof ingredients","sourceOffset":6332},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof outline","sourceOffset":8431},{"kind":"section","title":"Classes, currents, and exceptional divisors","sourceOffset":11661},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pullback of classes","sourceOffset":12136},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positive currents on a normal model","sourceOffset":13401},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Generalized pairs and negativity","sourceOffset":16444},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A fixed nef b-part and its traces","sourceOffset":26549},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison with the same nef datum","sourceOffset":32043},{"kind":"section","title":"Ordinary steps on the given space","sourceOffset":34415},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Integral descent","sourceOffset":34900}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Termination of ordinary programs","label":"thm:finite","text":"Let $X$ be a normal globally Weil $\\Q$-factorial compact Kähler fourfold, with a canonical Weil divisor $K_X$, and let $\\Delta\\geq0$ be a rational divisor such that $(X,\\Delta)$ is klt. Then every maximal ordinary negative-ray program starting on $(X,\\Delta)$ terminates. Its birational steps form a finite sequence \\[ (X,\\Delta)=(X_0,\\Delta_0)\\dashrightarrow\\cdots \\dashrightarrow(X_n,\\Delta_n). \\] Every $X_i$ is normal, compact Kähler and globally Weil $\\Q$-factorial; every $(X_i,\\Delta_i)$ is klt, and $D_i=K_{X_i}+\\Delta_i$ is $\\Q$-Cartier. The canonical divisors are the transforms of the specified datum. The following alternatives hold. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item If $D=K_X+\\Delta$ is pseudo-effective, then $D_n$ is nef. The composite $\\phi:X\\dashrightarrow X_n$ extracts no prime divisor, and \\[ a(E;X,\\Delta)\\leq a(E;X_n,\\Delta_n) \\] for every prime divisor over the two models, with strict inequality for every prime on $X$ contracted by $\\phi$. \\item If $D$ is not pseudo-effective, there is a projective surjective morphism $g:X_n\\to S$ with connected fibres, where $S$ is normal compact Kähler, $\\dim S<4$, $\\rho(X_n/S)=1$, and $-D_n$ is $g$-ample. \\end{enumerate} For each negative contraction, including $g$, the relative Bott--Chern dimension is one. If $X$ is globally strongly $\\Q$-factorial, so is every $X_i$. The same conclusions hold in the intrinsic formulation with the canonical rational line bundle in place of a chosen canonical Weil divisor. In e","sourceOffset":4597,"truncated":true},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Positivity of the trace","label":"cor:big-trace","text":"Under the hypotheses of Lemma \\ref{lem:class-trace}, the actual trace $\\mathbf H_T$ has local plurisubharmonic potentials and represents $H_T$. The class $H_T$ is big.","sourceOffset":30358,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:global-factoriality","text":"A normal compact space $X$ is globally Weil $\\Q$-factorial if every prime Weil divisor defined on the whole of $X$ is $\\Q$-Cartier and some positive reflexive power of its canonical sheaf $\\omega_X$ is a line bundle. It is globally strongly $\\Q$-factorial if every coherent rank-one reflexive sheaf $\\cF$ on $X$ has an invertible positive reflexive power $\\cF^{[m]}=(\\cF^{\\otimes m})^{**}$.","sourceOffset":1332,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:generalized-data","text":"Rational generalized b-line data on a normal compact space $T$ consist of an effective rational divisor $B$, a projective modification $p\\colon W\\to T$, and a rational line bundle $M_W$ on $W$. The bundle is pulled back on every higher model; this b-object is denoted by $\\bM$. We require $M_W$ to be nef, or to be nef over a specified base in a relative argument. Its trace on $T$ is obtained by taking a proper direct image of a cleared power and then its double dual. The adjoint \\[ D_T=K_T+B+M_T \\] is required to be rationally invertible. We may increase $W$ to a smooth projective log resolutio","sourceOffset":223809,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":357363,"sourceFileCount":16,"statementCount":25,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"056-05","slug":"Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-24-2026","familyId":"056","title":"Finite ordinary minimal model programs on compact Kähler fourfolds","abstract":"We prove that a globally Weil-ℚ-factorial compact Kähler klt fourfold pair with effective rational boundary and canonical rational line bundle admits a finite ordinary minimal model program starting on the given pair. It ends at a nef model when the adjoint class is pseudo-effective and at a Mori fibre space otherwise.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves termination of every existing generalized log canonical flip sequence on globally Weil ℚ-factorial compact Kähler fourfolds, with rational boundary, fixed rational analytically nef b-data, and projective small flip diagrams with the prescribed ample signs. Also proves termination of arbitrary permitted minimal model programs for projective log canonical fourfolds with rational boundary in characteristic zero.","pdfPath":"preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-24-2026/build/figures/dependencies.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-analytic.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-steps.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-terminal.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-scaling.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-perturbation.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-endpoints.tex","preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-24-2026/build/sections/13-local-convention.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that a globally Weil-$\\Q$-factorial compact Kähler klt fourfold pair with effective rational boundary and canonical rational line bundle admits a finite ordinary minimal model program starting on the given pair. It ends at a nef model when the adjoint class is pseudo-effective and at a Mori fibre space otherwise.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":386},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Category and the finite-program theorem","sourceOffset":1581},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the additional argument","sourceOffset":7356},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and organization","sourceOffset":11219},{"kind":"section","title":"Classes, currents, and exceptional divisors","sourceOffset":14956},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pullback of classes","sourceOffset":15431},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positive currents on a normal model","sourceOffset":16696},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Generalized pairs and negativity","sourceOffset":20060},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A fixed nef b-part and its traces","sourceOffset":30165},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison with the same nef datum","sourceOffset":35659},{"kind":"section","title":"Ordinary steps on the given space","sourceOffset":38084},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Integral descent","sourceOffset":38569}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Finite ordinary programs","label":"thm:finite","text":"Let $X$ be a normal globally Weil $\\Q$-factorial compact Kähler fourfold, with a canonical Weil divisor $K_X$, and let $\\Delta\\geq0$ be a rational divisor such that $(X,\\Delta)$ is klt. Then there is a finite sequence of ordinary negative steps \\[ (X,\\Delta)=(X_0,\\Delta_0)\\dashrightarrow\\cdots \\dashrightarrow(X_n,\\Delta_n). \\] Every $X_i$ is normal, compact Kähler and globally Weil $\\Q$-factorial; every $(X_i,\\Delta_i)$ is klt, and $D_i=K_{X_i}+\\Delta_i$ is $\\Q$-Cartier. The canonical divisors are the transforms of the specified datum. The following alternatives hold. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item If $D=K_X+\\Delta$ is pseudo-effective, then $D_n$ is nef. The composite $\\phi:X\\dashrightarrow X_n$ extracts no prime divisor, and \\[ a(E;X,\\Delta)\\leq a(E;X_n,\\Delta_n) \\] for every prime divisor over the two models, with strict inequality for every prime on $X$ contracted by $\\phi$. \\item If $D$ is not pseudo-effective, there is a projective surjective morphism $g:X_n\\to S$ with connected fibres, where $S$ is normal compact Kähler, $\\dim S<4$, $\\rho(X_n/S)=1$, and $-D_n$ is $g$-ample. \\end{enumerate} For each negative contraction, including $g$, the relative Bott--Chern dimension is one. If $X$ is globally strongly $\\Q$-factorial, so is every $X_i$. The same conclusions hold in the intrinsic formulation with the canonical rational line bundle in place of a chosen canonical Weil divisor. In either formulation the first model is exactly $X$.","sourceOffset":5376,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Positivity of the trace","label":"cor:big-trace","text":"Under the hypotheses of Lemma \\ref{lem:class-trace}, the actual trace $\\mathbf H_T$ has local plurisubharmonic potentials and represents $H_T$. The class $H_T$ is big.","sourceOffset":33974,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:global-factoriality","text":"A normal compact space $X$ is globally Weil $\\Q$-factorial if every prime Weil divisor defined on the whole of $X$ is $\\Q$-Cartier and some positive reflexive power of its canonical sheaf $\\omega_X$ is a line bundle. It is globally strongly $\\Q$-factorial if every coherent rank-one reflexive sheaf $\\cF$ on $X$ has an invertible positive reflexive power $\\cF^{[m]}=(\\cF^{\\otimes m})^{**}$.","sourceOffset":1917,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":136935,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Finite-ordinary-minimal-model-programs-on-compact-Kahler-fourfolds-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode.tex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"057-01","slug":"The-abelianity-conjecture-for-special-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026","familyId":"057","title":"The abelianity conjecture for special compact Kähler manifolds","abstract":"We prove that the fundamental group of every special compact Kähler manifold is virtually abelian, resolving Campana's abelianity conjecture in all dimensions. In particular, every smooth compact connected Kähler manifold of Kodaira dimension zero has virtually abelian ordinary fundamental group.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Campana's abelianity conjecture: special compact Kähler manifolds have virtually abelian fundamental groups. Using this theorem, establishes the same conclusion for order-two root orbifolds of smooth projective complex fourfolds along one nonempty smooth connected divisor, when special in the stated differential-line sense. For smooth special complex quasi-projective varieties, proves that every finite-dimensional complex linear representation of the fundamental group has virtually nilpotent image of class at most two.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-abelianity-conjecture-for-special-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-abelianity-conjecture-for-special-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-abelianity-conjecture-for-special-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/The-abelianity-conjecture-for-special-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-abelianity-conjecture-for-special-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/The-abelianity-conjecture-for-special-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-abelianity-conjecture-for-special-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/figures/proof-overview.tex","preprints/The-abelianity-conjecture-for-special-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/The-abelianity-conjecture-for-special-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/spectral.tex","preprints/The-abelianity-conjecture-for-special-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/hilbert.tex","preprints/The-abelianity-conjecture-for-special-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/descent.tex","preprints/The-abelianity-conjecture-for-special-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/completion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-abelianity-conjecture-for-special-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the fundamental group of every special compact K\\\"ahler manifold is virtually abelian, resolving Campana's abelianity conjecture in all dimensions. In particular, every smooth compact connected K\\\"ahler manifold of Kodaira dimension zero has virtually abelian ordinary fundamental group.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":441},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Kodaira dimension zero","sourceOffset":2277},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conjecture S and affine-space uniformization","sourceOffset":3401},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Holomorphic convexity of the universal cover","sourceOffset":6458},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compactifiable covers","sourceOffset":8703},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work and the remaining group-theoretic obstacle","sourceOffset":11796},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Overview of the proof","sourceOffset":16204},{"kind":"section","title":"Geometric and group-theoretic preliminaries","sourceOffset":22741},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Specialness and quotient reductions","sourceOffset":23746},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Root orbifolds and adjoint positivity","sourceOffset":30394},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite kernels and finite covers","sourceOffset":32840},{"kind":"section","title":"Zero in the Dolbeault spectrum","sourceOffset":35512}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:abelianity","text":"Let \\(X\\) be a connected smooth compact K\\\"ahler manifold of arbitrary complex dimension. If \\(X\\) is special, then \\(\\pi_1(X)\\) has an abelian subgroup of finite index. Equivalently, \\(X\\) has a connected finite unramified holomorphic cover with abelian fundamental group.","sourceOffset":1621,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Fundamental groups in Kodaira dimension zero","label":"cor:kappa-zero-abelianity","text":"Let \\(X\\) be a smooth compact connected K\\\"ahler manifold with \\(\\kappa(X)=\\kappa(X,K_X)=0\\). Then its ordinary topological fundamental group \\(\\pi_1(X)\\) is virtually abelian.","sourceOffset":2619,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"de:inertial-boundary","text":"The inertial boundary associated with \\(\\rho\\) on this model of \\(Z\\) is \\[ \\Delta_Z=\\sum_D(1-m_D^{-1})D. \\] A prepared model is a smooth compact K\\\"ahler base model, together with a resolved source, on which the divisorial deleted locus has simple normal crossings. If a map to a fixed torus is present, the model is required to map holomorphically to that torus.","sourceOffset":111173,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":164996,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-abelianity-conjecture-for-special-compact-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"057-02","slug":"Two-step-monodromy-of-special-quasi-projective-varieties-September-24-2026","familyId":"057","title":"Two-step monodromy of special quasi-projective varieties","abstract":"We give an independent proof that every complex linear representation of the ordinary fundamental group of a connected smooth special complex quasi-projective variety has virtually nilpotent image of class at most two. This conclusion was previously announced by Cao–Deng–Hacon–Păun. We also construct special open surfaces whose general quasi-Albanese fibres are not special.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Campana's abelianity conjecture: special compact Kähler manifolds have virtually abelian fundamental groups. Using this theorem, establishes the same conclusion for order-two root orbifolds of smooth projective complex fourfolds along one nonempty smooth connected divisor, when special in the stated differential-line sense. For smooth special complex quasi-projective varieties, proves that every finite-dimensional complex linear representation of the fundamental group has virtually nilpotent image of class at most two.","pdfPath":"preprints/Two-step-monodromy-of-special-quasi-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Two-step-monodromy-of-special-quasi-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Two-step-monodromy-of-special-quasi-projective-varieties-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Two-step-monodromy-of-special-quasi-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Two-step-monodromy-of-special-quasi-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Two-step-monodromy-of-special-quasi-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/sections/mhs.tex","preprints/Two-step-monodromy-of-special-quasi-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/sections/linear.tex","preprints/Two-step-monodromy-of-special-quasi-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/sections/definitions.tex","preprints/Two-step-monodromy-of-special-quasi-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/sections/specialness.tex","preprints/Two-step-monodromy-of-special-quasi-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/sections/albanese-topology.tex","preprints/Two-step-monodromy-of-special-quasi-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/sections/composition.tex","preprints/Two-step-monodromy-of-special-quasi-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/figures/retained-exceptional.tex","preprints/Two-step-monodromy-of-special-quasi-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/sections/ordinary.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Two-step-monodromy-of-special-quasi-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give an independent proof that every complex linear representation of the ordinary fundamental group of a connected smooth special complex quasi-projective variety has virtually nilpotent image of class at most two. This conclusion was previously announced by Cao--Deng--Hacon--P\\u aun. We also construct special open surfaces whose general quasi-Albanese fibres are not special.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":429},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The structural completion theorem","sourceOffset":3837},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A special surface with nonspecial quasi-Albanese fibre","sourceOffset":9554},{"kind":"section","title":"Exactness and unipotent completion","sourceOffset":13527},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The canonical quotients and their weights","sourceOffset":13937},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The fibre image is the commutator at every unipotent stage","sourceOffset":17548},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two weight bounds force centrality","sourceOffset":19689},{"kind":"section","title":"From completion to complex linear monodromy","sourceOffset":24161},{"kind":"section","title":"The surface and its orbifold multiplicities","sourceOffset":29449},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The surface and its two projections","sourceOffset":29887},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The infimum convention","sourceOffset":32556},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Upper models and specialness","sourceOffset":34128}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Two-step linear monodromy","label":"thm:linear","text":"Let $V$ be a connected smooth special complex quasi-projective variety. For every positive integer $d$ and every representation $\\rho:\\pi_1(V)\\to\\GL_d(\\C)$, the image $\\rho(\\pi_1(V))$ has a subgroup of finite index and nilpotency class at most two.","sourceOffset":2381,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Completion under quasi-Albanese exactness","label":"thm:completion","text":"Let $V$ be a connected smooth complex quasi-projective variety and let $a:V\\to A=\\Alb(V)$ be its algebraic quasi-Albanese map. Suppose $a$ is dominant, choose a smooth connected general fibre $F$ and a point $v\\in F$, and assume that the sequence of ordinary fundamental groups \\begin{equation} \\pi_1(F,v)\\longrightarrow\\Gamma:=\\pi_1(V,v) \\xrightarrow{a_*}\\Lambda:=\\pi_1(A,a(v))\\longrightarrow1 \\end{equation} is exact. Then every canonical rational unipotent quotient of $\\Gamma$ has nilpotency class at most two. The full rational unipotent completion is a finite-dimensional unipotent algebraic group of class at most two. Every finite-dimensional complex unipotent representation of $\\Gamma$ also has image of class at most two.","sourceOffset":5263,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:special","text":"A smooth projective orbifold pair $(Y,D)$ is special if for every elementary birational orbifold morphism $u:(Y',D')\\to(Y,D)$ with $(Y',D')$ smooth projective and every neat fibration $g:(Y',D')\\to Z$ with $\\dim Z>0$, \\[ \\kappa\\bigl(Z,K_Z+\\Delta(g,D')\\bigr)<\\dim Z. \\] A smooth open variety $X=Y\\setminus D$ with reduced boundary is logarithmically special when its smooth projective compactification pair is special in this sense.","sourceOffset":35932,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":61597,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Two-step-monodromy-of-special-quasi-projective-varieties-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"057-03","slug":"A-conditional-abelianity-theorem-for-special-fourfold-pairs-with-a-half-weight-divisor-October-5-2026","familyId":"057","title":"A conditional abelianity theorem for special fourfold pairs with a half-weight divisor","abstract":"Using the abelianity theorem for special compact Kähler manifolds in every dimension, we prove virtual abelianity of the entire orbifold fundamental group of the special order-two root orbifold associated with a pair $(X,\\tfrac12D)$, where X is a smooth projective fourfold and D is a nonempty smooth connected divisor. We construct a special smooth projective eightfold whose fundamental group surjects onto the required group.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Campana's abelianity conjecture: special compact Kähler manifolds have virtually abelian fundamental groups. Using this theorem, establishes the same conclusion for order-two root orbifolds of smooth projective complex fourfolds along one nonempty smooth connected divisor, when special in the stated differential-line sense. For smooth special complex quasi-projective varieties, proves that every finite-dimensional complex linear representation of the fundamental group has virtually nilpotent image of class at most two.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-conditional-abelianity-theorem-for-special-fourfold-pairs-with-a-half-weight-divisor-October-5-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-conditional-abelianity-theorem-for-special-fourfold-pairs-with-a-half-weight-divisor-October-5-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-conditional-abelianity-theorem-for-special-fourfold-pairs-with-a-half-weight-divisor-October-5-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-conditional-abelianity-theorem-for-special-fourfold-pairs-with-a-half-weight-divisor-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/A-conditional-abelianity-theorem-for-special-fourfold-pairs-with-a-half-weight-divisor-October-5-2026/build/source/preamble.tex","preprints/A-conditional-abelianity-theorem-for-special-fourfold-pairs-with-a-half-weight-divisor-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-conditional-abelianity-theorem-for-special-fourfold-pairs-with-a-half-weight-divisor-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/differentials.tex","preprints/A-conditional-abelianity-theorem-for-special-fourfold-pairs-with-a-half-weight-divisor-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/transfer.tex","preprints/A-conditional-abelianity-theorem-for-special-fourfold-pairs-with-a-half-weight-divisor-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/quadrics.tex","preprints/A-conditional-abelianity-theorem-for-special-fourfold-pairs-with-a-half-weight-divisor-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/branched.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-conditional-abelianity-theorem-for-special-fourfold-pairs-with-a-half-weight-divisor-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Using the abelianity theorem for special compact K\\\"ahler manifolds in every dimension, we prove virtual abelianity of the entire orbifold fundamental group of the special order-two root orbifold associated with a pair $(X,\\tfrac12D)$, where $X$ is a smooth projective fourfold and $D$ is a nonempty smooth connected divisor. We construct a special smooth projective eightfold whose fundamental group surjects onto the required group.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Statement and context","sourceOffset":480},{"kind":"section","title":"Differential lines and section ratios","sourceOffset":6394},{"kind":"section","title":"A manifold criterion for specialness and fundamental groups","sourceOffset":12030},{"kind":"section","title":"Four genus-one factors over the root orbifold","sourceOffset":18911},{"kind":"section","title":"An alternative construction using auxiliary double covers","sourceOffset":30932}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Assume Assumption \\ref{ass:manifold}. Let $X$ be a connected smooth projective complex fourfold and let $D\\subset X$ be a nonempty smooth connected reduced divisor. If $\\sqrt[2]{(X,D)}$ is special, then \\begin{equation} G=\\pi_1(X\\setminus D,x)\\big/\\langle\\!\\langle\\gamma^2\\rangle\\!\\rangle \\end{equation} contains an abelian subgroup of finite index. Here $\\gamma$ is a positively oriented meridian about $D$, transported to $x\\in X\\setminus D$.","sourceOffset":3691,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Transfer criterion","label":"prop:transfer","text":"Let $g:Y\\to X$ be a surjective morphism with $Y$ connected smooth projective. Assume: \\begin{enumerate} \\item $g^*D=2E$ for an effective integral divisor $E$ on $Y$; \\item over a nonempty Zariski open $B\\subset X\\setminus D$, the map $g$ is smooth with connected fibres $F$, and $\\Omega_F^1$ is trivial; \\item above a general point of each prime divisor $P\\ne D$ of $X$, there is a point at which $g$ is a submersion; \\item above a general point of $D$, there is a point at which a local lift to the root chart is a submersion. \\end{enumerate} If $\\cX$ is special, then $Y$ is special. There is also a surjection \\begin{equation} \\pi_1(Y)\\twoheadrightarrow \\pi_1(X\\setminus D)/\\langle\\!\\langle\\gamma^2\\rangle\\!\\rangle. \\end{equation} Consequently Assumption \\ref{ass:manifold} implies virtual abelianity of the group on the right.","sourceOffset":12756,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:special","text":"A smooth compact complex manifold or a smooth root orbifold $V$ is special if there is no integer $1\\le p\\le\\dim V$ and no holomorphic line bundle $L$ admitting a nonzero sheaf map \\[ L\\longrightarrow\\Omega_V^p,\\qquad \\kappa(L)=p. \\]","sourceOffset":2638,"truncated":false},{"kind":"assumption","name":"Manifold abelianity","label":"ass:manifold","text":"Every connected smooth compact K\\\"ahler manifold which is special in the sense of Definition \\ref{def:special} has virtually abelian entire discrete topological fundamental group, in every complex dimension.","sourceOffset":3205,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":43691,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-conditional-abelianity-theorem-for-special-fourfold-pairs-with-a-half-weight-divisor-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"058-01","slug":"Semialgebraic-universal-covers-of-normal-projective-varieties-September-24-2026","familyId":"058","title":"Semialgebraic universal covers of normal projective varieties","abstract":"We prove the Kollár–Pardon conjecture: the universal cover of a connected normal projective complex variety is biholomorphic to a semialgebraic open subset of a projective variety if and only if it is a product of a bounded symmetric domain, a complex affine space, and a simply connected normal projective variety.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the Kollár–Pardon conjecture: the semialgebraic universal covers of connected normal projective complex varieties are exactly products $D\\times\\mathbb C^m\\times F$, with D bounded symmetric and F simply connected, normal, and projective. A universal cover is quasi-projective exactly when the bounded symmetric factor is absent. In particular, a smooth projective variety covered by ℂn has a finite étale cover by an abelian variety.","pdfPath":"preprints/Semialgebraic-universal-covers-of-normal-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Semialgebraic-universal-covers-of-normal-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Semialgebraic-universal-covers-of-normal-projective-varieties-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Semialgebraic-universal-covers-of-normal-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Semialgebraic-universal-covers-of-normal-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/sections/history.tex","preprints/Semialgebraic-universal-covers-of-normal-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-overview.tex","preprints/Semialgebraic-universal-covers-of-normal-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Semialgebraic-universal-covers-of-normal-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/sections/localization.tex","preprints/Semialgebraic-universal-covers-of-normal-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/sections/windows.tex","preprints/Semialgebraic-universal-covers-of-normal-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/sections/quotient.tex","preprints/Semialgebraic-universal-covers-of-normal-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/sections/groups.tex","preprints/Semialgebraic-universal-covers-of-normal-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/sections/compact.tex","preprints/Semialgebraic-universal-covers-of-normal-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/sections/quasiprojective.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Semialgebraic-universal-covers-of-normal-projective-varieties-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Koll\\'ar--Pardon conjecture: the universal cover of a connected normal projective complex variety is biholomorphic to a semialgebraic open subset of a projective variety if and only if it is a product of a bounded symmetric domain, a complex affine space, and a simply connected normal projective variety.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":365},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and related results","sourceOffset":3709},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its main obstacles","sourceOffset":9027},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":16611},{"kind":"section","title":"Preparation, kernels, and projective holes","sourceOffset":17310},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The preparation conventions","sourceOffset":19032},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Open kernels and finite jets","sourceOffset":25971},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Polynomial tests for compact holes","sourceOffset":38906},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Thin holes and propagation in a projective family","sourceOffset":46757},{"kind":"section","title":"Localization with triangular recenterings","sourceOffset":60042},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Normalized domains and recenterings","sourceOffset":61372},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Controlled paths from ordinary uniformization","sourceOffset":64046}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"The Koll\\'ar--Pardon classification","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(X\\) be a connected normal projective variety over \\(\\C\\), and let \\(\\widetilde X\\) be its universal cover. The following conditions are equivalent: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item \\(\\widetilde X\\) is biholomorphic to a semialgebraic open subset of a projective variety. \\item There are an integer \\(m\\geq0\\), a bounded symmetric domain \\(D\\), and a simply connected normal projective variety \\(F\\) such that \\[ \\widetilde X\\simeq D\\times\\C^m\\times F. \\] \\end{enumerate} A point is allowed as either \\(D\\) or \\(F\\). No algebraicity of the deck transformations is assumed.","sourceOffset":1895,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"A section detects the projection domain","label":"holes:section-test","text":"Suppose a full-box container $D$ as in Corollary \\ref{holes:container-propagation} is available. A local holomorphic section $\\sigma$ of $q$ avoiding $D$ lies in $G$ at every parameter of $S_0$. Near a boundary point of $S_0$ that lies in the parameter box, $S_0$ is locally pseudoconvex wherever such a section exists.","sourceOffset":58676,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Generic real parameters","label":"prep:generic","text":"An assertion at generic parameters in an open real box means that it holds outside a locally finite union of subanalytic subsets of smaller dimension. When countably many fixed compact boxes or positive accuracies are tested, a countable union of such exceptional sets is permitted. Each application involving finitely many data is subsequently restricted to a neighborhood of the chosen parameter on which those data have the asserted regularity. No uniform neighborhood for all members of a countable list is implicit.","sourceOffset":19074,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Open kernel","label":"prep:kernel","text":"Let $U_t$ be open subsets of a fixed manifold $M$, $0<t<t_0$. Their open kernel is \\[ \\mathcal K(U_t)=\\{z\\in M: \\text{some neighborhood }W\\ni z \\text{ satisfies }W\\subset U_t \\text{ for every sufficiently small }t\\}. \\] Equivalently, if $F_t=M\\setminus U_t$, then \\[ \\mathcal K(U_t)=M\\setminus\\limsup_{t\\downarrow0}F_t, \\] where the upper limit allows both $t$ and the point of $F_t$ to vary. In a varying family these tests are relative to the total space; smooth local trivializations identify the limiting fibre. A marked kernel is the connected component containing a specified point of the open ","sourceOffset":26014,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/058.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":238512,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":22,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Semialgebraic-universal-covers-of-normal-projective-varieties-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"058-02","slug":"Symmetry-of-semialgebraic-bounded-domains-with-compact-quotient-September-24-2026","familyId":"058","title":"Symmetry of semialgebraic bounded domains with compact quotient","abstract":"Every nonempty connected semialgebraic bounded open subset of a complex affine variety admitting a properly discontinuous cocompact group of biholomorphisms is smooth and biholomorphic to a bounded symmetric domain. This answers the bounded-domain question of Kollár and Pardon affirmatively.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the Kollár–Pardon conjecture: the semialgebraic universal covers of connected normal projective complex varieties are exactly products $D\\times\\mathbb C^m\\times F$, with D bounded symmetric and F simply connected, normal, and projective. A universal cover is quasi-projective exactly when the bounded symmetric factor is absent. In particular, a smooth projective variety covered by ℂn has a finite étale cover by an abelian variety.","pdfPath":"preprints/Symmetry-of-semialgebraic-bounded-domains-with-compact-quotient-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Symmetry-of-semialgebraic-bounded-domains-with-compact-quotient-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Symmetry-of-semialgebraic-bounded-domains-with-compact-quotient-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Symmetry-of-semialgebraic-bounded-domains-with-compact-quotient-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Symmetry-of-semialgebraic-bounded-domains-with-compact-quotient-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-overview.tex","preprints/Symmetry-of-semialgebraic-bounded-domains-with-compact-quotient-September-24-2026/build/01_geometry.tex","preprints/Symmetry-of-semialgebraic-bounded-domains-with-compact-quotient-September-24-2026/build/02_preparation.tex","preprints/Symmetry-of-semialgebraic-bounded-domains-with-compact-quotient-September-24-2026/build/03_noncollapse.tex","preprints/Symmetry-of-semialgebraic-bounded-domains-with-compact-quotient-September-24-2026/build/04_scaling.tex","preprints/Symmetry-of-semialgebraic-bounded-domains-with-compact-quotient-September-24-2026/build/05_symmetry.tex","preprints/Symmetry-of-semialgebraic-bounded-domains-with-compact-quotient-September-24-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Symmetry-of-semialgebraic-bounded-domains-with-compact-quotient-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every nonempty connected semialgebraic bounded open subset of a complex affine variety admitting a properly discontinuous cocompact group of biholomorphisms is smooth and biholomorphic to a bounded symmetric domain. This answers the bounded-domain question of Koll\\'ar and Pardon affirmatively.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":340},{"kind":"section","title":"Smoothness and a proper intrinsic distance","sourceOffset":11417},{"kind":"section","title":"Boundary preparation and independent supports","sourceOffset":22947},{"kind":"section","title":"An open cone of limiting interior","sourceOffset":39930},{"kind":"section","title":"Weighted scaling and the quadratic model","sourceOffset":53700},{"kind":"section","title":"Symmetry of the quadratic model","sourceOffset":69646}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(U\\) be a nonempty connected semialgebraic bounded open subset of a complex affine variety. Suppose that a group \\(\\Gamma\\) of biholomorphisms acts properly discontinuously on \\(U\\) and that \\(U/\\Gamma\\) is compact. Then \\(U\\) is smooth and is biholomorphic to a bounded symmetric domain.","sourceOffset":1286,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:smoothness","text":"If $U$ is not a point, then it is a complex manifold of positive dimension and lies in the smooth locus of a single irreducible component $V$ of the ambient variety.","sourceOffset":16392,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/058.md","scopeText":"The formalized result answers the paper's symmetry question affirmatively. A nonempty connected bounded semialgebraic subset, relatively open in a complex affine algebraic set, is smooth and biholomorphic to a bounded symmetric domain whenever a discrete group acts properly and holomorphically with compact quotient.\n\nThe action need not be free, and the ambient algebraic set may be singular.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/058.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":77176,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Symmetry-of-semialgebraic-bounded-domains-with-compact-quotient-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"059-01","slug":"Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurfaces-of-multiplicities-two-and-three-September-24-2026","familyId":"059","title":"Ambiently homeomorphic isolated hypersurfaces of multiplicities two and three","abstract":"We give a negative answer to the embedded Zariski multiplicity conjecture. We construct two reduced hypersurface germs that are ambiently homeomorphic but have multiplicities two and three. Both have isolated singularities and lie in a common complex affine space of dimension divisible by eight. Their defining function germs are also topologically right equivalent, and the same examples answer Arnold's corank problem negatively for ambient topological equivalence.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves Zariski's multiplicity conjecture by constructing reduced holomorphic hypersurface germs that are ambiently homeomorphic but have different multiplicities. The examples include hypersurfaces in ℂ4 with isolated critical points and multiplicities four and five.","pdfPath":"preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurfaces-of-multiplicities-two-and-three-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurfaces-of-multiplicities-two-and-three-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurfaces-of-multiplicities-two-and-three-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurfaces-of-multiplicities-two-and-three-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurfaces-of-multiplicities-two-and-three-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurfaces-of-multiplicities-two-and-three-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurfaces-of-multiplicities-two-and-three-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-route.tex","preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurfaces-of-multiplicities-two-and-three-September-24-2026/build/sections/background.tex","preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurfaces-of-multiplicities-two-and-three-September-24-2026/build/sections/seeds.tex","preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurfaces-of-multiplicities-two-and-three-September-24-2026/build/sections/local.tex","preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurfaces-of-multiplicities-two-and-three-September-24-2026/build/sections/global.tex","preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurfaces-of-multiplicities-two-and-three-September-24-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex","preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurfaces-of-multiplicities-two-and-three-September-24-2026/build/sections/certificate.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurfaces-of-multiplicities-two-and-three-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a negative answer to the embedded Zariski multiplicity conjecture. We construct two reduced hypersurface germs that are ambiently homeomorphic but have multiplicities two and three. Both have isolated singularities and lie in a common complex affine space of dimension divisible by eight. Their defining function germs are also topologically right equivalent, and the same examples answer Arnold's corank problem negatively for ambient topological equivalence.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The multiplicity problem","sourceOffset":514},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Families, metric equivalence, and the present question","sourceOffset":6074},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The main ideas","sourceOffset":8831},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":15347},{"kind":"section","title":"Classical inputs and conventions","sourceOffset":15733},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Milnor lattices, spectra, and integral monodromy","sourceOffset":15927},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Arithmetic inputs","sourceOffset":23469},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Embedded high-dimensional topology","sourceOffset":25100},{"kind":"section","title":"Two seeds with different orders","sourceOffset":26365},{"kind":"section","title":"Local and rational isometries of tensor squares","sourceOffset":39715},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Odd primes","sourceOffset":42404},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The prime two","sourceOffset":47719}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a finite integer $N>3$, divisible by eight, and there are reduced polynomials $f_1,f_2\\in\\R[z_1,\\ldots,z_N]$, weighted homogeneous for positive rational weights, such that: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item each $f_j$ has an isolated critical point at the origin; \\item $\\ord_0f_1=2$ and $\\ord_0f_2=3$; \\item there is a homeomorphism germ of ambient pairs \\[ (\\C^N,V(f_1),0)\\ \\cong\\ (\\C^N,V(f_2),0). \\] \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":2671,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Milnor","label":"bg:milnor","text":"Let $s\\geq2$, and let $f:(\\mathbb C^s,0)\\to(\\mathbb C,0)$ be a holomorphic germ with an isolated critical point at $0$, with $f(0)=0$. For sufficiently small $\\varepsilon>0$, the link $K_f=f^{-1}(0)\\cap S^{2s-1}_\\varepsilon$ is a smooth closed manifold of dimension $2s-3$, and \\[ f/|f|:S^{2s-1}_\\varepsilon\\setminus K_f\\longrightarrow S^1 \\] is a locally trivial fibration. Each fiber is the interior of its closure $F_f$, a compact manifold with boundary $K_f$. Both $F_f$ and its interior are homotopy equivalent to a finite bouquet of $(s-1)$-spheres. The link is $(s-3)$-connected.","sourceOffset":15990,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":93559,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":18,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurfaces-of-multiplicities-two-and-three-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"059-02","slug":"Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurface-germs-in-C4-with-multiplicities-four-and-five-September-27-2026","familyId":"059","title":"Ambiently homeomorphic isolated hypersurface germs in ℂ⁴ with multiplicities four and five","abstract":"We give a negative answer to Zariski's multiplicity question in four complex variables. We construct two reduced convergent holomorphic function germs with isolated critical points and multiplicities four and five whose zero-set germs are ambiently homeomorphic.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves Zariski's multiplicity conjecture by constructing reduced holomorphic hypersurface germs that are ambiently homeomorphic but have different multiplicities. The examples include hypersurfaces in ℂ4 with isolated critical points and multiplicities four and five.","pdfPath":"preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurface-germs-in-C4-with-multiplicities-four-and-five-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurface-germs-in-C4-with-multiplicities-four-and-five-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurface-germs-in-C4-with-multiplicities-four-and-five-September-27-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurface-germs-in-C4-with-multiplicities-four-and-five-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurface-germs-in-C4-with-multiplicities-four-and-five-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurface-germs-in-C4-with-multiplicities-four-and-five-September-27-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurface-germs-in-C4-with-multiplicities-four-and-five-September-27-2026/build/sections/02-plane.tex","preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurface-germs-in-C4-with-multiplicities-four-and-five-September-27-2026/build/sections/03-ambient.tex","preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurface-germs-in-C4-with-multiplicities-four-and-five-September-27-2026/build/sections/04-tube.tex","preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurface-germs-in-C4-with-multiplicities-four-and-five-September-27-2026/build/sections/05-boundary.tex","preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurface-germs-in-C4-with-multiplicities-four-and-five-September-27-2026/build/sections/06-collapse.tex","preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurface-germs-in-C4-with-multiplicities-four-and-five-September-27-2026/build/figures/exterior-cut.tex","preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurface-germs-in-C4-with-multiplicities-four-and-five-September-27-2026/build/sections/07-lattice.tex","preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurface-germs-in-C4-with-multiplicities-four-and-five-September-27-2026/build/sections/08-fillings.tex","preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurface-germs-in-C4-with-multiplicities-four-and-five-September-27-2026/build/sections/09-arithmetic.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurface-germs-in-C4-with-multiplicities-four-and-five-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a negative answer to Zariski's multiplicity question in four complex variables. We construct two reduced convergent holomorphic function germs with isolated critical points and multiplicities four and five whose zero-set germs are ambiently homeomorphic.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":308},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and predecessors","sourceOffset":2762},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":5910},{"kind":"section","title":"Plane curves and the finite maps","sourceOffset":9136},{"kind":"section","title":"Smooth ambient germs and their ordinary orders","sourceOffset":22051},{"kind":"section","title":"The cone tube and integral gluing","sourceOffset":28490},{"kind":"section","title":"Boundary geometry and the Milnor representative","sourceOffset":32931},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Regularity on the outer boundary","sourceOffset":34896},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compatible continuations and the lens circle","sourceOffset":39441},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Isolation in the chosen tube","sourceOffset":45848},{"kind":"section","title":"Circle collapse and integral homology","sourceOffset":52206},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The circle collapse","sourceOffset":52674}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are reduced convergent holomorphic function germs \\(f_4,f_5\\colon(\\mathbb C^4,0)\\to(\\mathbb C,0)\\), each with an isolated critical point at the origin, such that \\[ \\operatorname{ord}_0f_4=4,\\qquad \\operatorname{ord}_0f_5=5, \\] and a homeomorphism germ of ambient pairs \\[ (\\mathbb C^4,V(f_4),0) \\;\\cong\\; (\\mathbb C^4,V(f_5),0). \\]","sourceOffset":1860,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Analytic germs and their orders","label":"prop:ambient-orders","text":"For the parameters of Lemma \\ref{lem:simultaneous-parameters} and every sufficiently small \\(s>0\\), each \\(C_{i,s}\\) is a smooth complex fourfold near the origin, and \\(f_{i,s}\\) has an isolated critical point there, as proved in Lemma \\ref{lem:isolation}. After solving the smooth ambient equations in analytic local coordinates, \\(f_{0,s}\\) has order five and \\(f_{1,s}\\) has order four. Both are reduced convergent germs.","sourceOffset":24886,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":126874,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Ambiently-homeomorphic-isolated-hypersurface-germs-in-C4-with-multiplicities-four-and-five-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"060-01","slug":"Global-Spherical-Shells-on-Minimal-Surfaces-of-Class-VII-September-24-2026","familyId":"060","title":"Global Spherical Shells on Minimal Surfaces of Class VII","abstract":"We prove the Global Spherical Shell conjecture: every connected minimal compact complex surface of class VII with positive second Betti number contains a global spherical shell. This is a holomorphically embedded neighborhood of the standard three-sphere in $\\mathbb C^2\\setminus\\{0\\}$ whose complement is connected.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Every connected minimal compact complex surface of class VII with $b_2\\gt 0$ contains a global spherical shell, proving the positive-b2 Global Spherical Shell conjecture. Such a shell is a holomorphically embedded neighborhood of the standard three-sphere in $\\mathbb C^2\\setminus\\{0\\}$ whose complement is connected.","pdfPath":"preprints/Global-Spherical-Shells-on-Minimal-Surfaces-of-Class-VII-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Global-Spherical-Shells-on-Minimal-Surfaces-of-Class-VII-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Global-Spherical-Shells-on-Minimal-Surfaces-of-Class-VII-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Global-Spherical-Shells-on-Minimal-Surfaces-of-Class-VII-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Global-Spherical-Shells-on-Minimal-Surfaces-of-Class-VII-September-24-2026/build/sections/surfaces.tex","preprints/Global-Spherical-Shells-on-Minimal-Surfaces-of-Class-VII-September-24-2026/build/sections/compact-data.tex","preprints/Global-Spherical-Shells-on-Minimal-Surfaces-of-Class-VII-September-24-2026/build/sections/weighted.tex","preprints/Global-Spherical-Shells-on-Minimal-Surfaces-of-Class-VII-September-24-2026/build/sections/curves.tex","preprints/Global-Spherical-Shells-on-Minimal-Surfaces-of-Class-VII-September-24-2026/build/sections/cylinder.tex","preprints/Global-Spherical-Shells-on-Minimal-Surfaces-of-Class-VII-September-24-2026/build/figures/flat-chain.tex","preprints/Global-Spherical-Shells-on-Minimal-Surfaces-of-Class-VII-September-24-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex","preprints/Global-Spherical-Shells-on-Minimal-Surfaces-of-Class-VII-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Global-Spherical-Shells-on-Minimal-Surfaces-of-Class-VII-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Global Spherical Shell conjecture: every connected minimal compact complex surface of class VII with positive second Betti number contains a global spherical shell. This is a holomorphically embedded neighborhood of the standard three-sphere in $\\CC^2\\setminus\\{0\\}$ whose complement is connected.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":357},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Curves, shells, and earlier approaches","sourceOffset":3714},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The argument","sourceOffset":6920},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and conventions","sourceOffset":13042},{"kind":"section","title":"Surface invariants and the cyclic cover","sourceOffset":14018},{"kind":"section","title":"Compact Chern classes","sourceOffset":27336},{"kind":"section","title":"Weighted Dolbeault theory on the cyclic cover","sourceOffset":33202},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Spaces, adjoints, and Fredholm complexes","sourceOffset":34783},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Continuation in the multiplier and positive-tail uniqueness","sourceOffset":46142},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rapid scalar solutions and holomorphic compact data","sourceOffset":59657},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The index and compact excision","sourceOffset":63329},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sections with prescribed end limits","sourceOffset":69552}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Global Spherical Shell conjecture","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $X$ be a connected compact complex surface with \\[ b_1(X)=1,\\qquad b_2(X)>0,\\qquad \\kappa(X)=-\\infty. \\] Assume that $X$ is minimal: it contains no smooth rational curve of self-intersection $-1$. Then there are an open neighborhood $U$ of \\[ S^3=\\{z\\in\\CC^2:|z|=1\\} \\quad\\text{in }\\CC^2\\setminus\\{0\\}, \\] an open subset $\\Sigma\\subset X$, and a biholomorphism $\\phi:U\\longrightarrow\\Sigma$ such that $X\\setminus\\Sigma$ is connected.","sourceOffset":1355,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Deformation and smooth topology","label":"cor:topology","text":"Let $X$ satisfy the hypotheses of Theorem \\ref{thm:main}, and put $b=b_2(X)$. There is a small one-parameter deformation $\\mathcal X\\to\\Delta$ over a disk about $0$, with $\\mathcal X_0\\simeq X$, such that every nonzero fiber is obtained from a primary Hopf surface by exactly $b$ successive point blowups. The centers may be infinitely near. Moreover, \\[ \\pi_1(X)\\cong\\ZZ,\\qquad X\\cong_{\\mathrm{or}}(S^1\\times S^3)\\#\\,b\\,\\overline{\\mathbb{CP}}^{\\,2}, \\] where the second is an orientation-preserving diffeomorphism for the complex orientation of $X$.","sourceOffset":2138,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":145641,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Global-Spherical-Shells-on-Minimal-Surfaces-of-Class-VII-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Global-Spherical-Shells-on-Minimal-Surfaces-of-Class-VII-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"062-01","slug":"Contact-Fano-manifolds-and-the-LeBrun-Salamon-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"062","title":"Contact Fano manifolds and the LeBrun–Salamon conjecture","abstract":"We resolve the contact-Fano homogeneity conjecture and the Riemannian LeBrun–Salamon conjecture positively. Every smooth connected complex projective contact Fano manifold of complex dimension at least three, with its given contact distribution, is contact-isomorphic to the adjoint variety of a simple complex Lie algebra. Consequently, every closed connected smooth positive quaternionic-Kähler manifold of real dimension $4m\\geq8$ is homothetic to a compact symmetric Wolf space.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the LeBrun–Salamon conjecture: every closed connected positive quaternionic-Kähler manifold of real dimension at least eight is homothetic to a compact symmetric Wolf space. It also proves contact-Fano homogeneity and classifies smooth connected complex projective contact manifolds of complex dimension at least three: those with $b_2=1$ are adjoint varieties with their canonical contact structures, while those with $b_2\\ge2$ have underlying manifold $\\mathbb P(T^*Z)$ for a smooth projective variety Z.","pdfPath":"preprints/Contact-Fano-manifolds-and-the-LeBrun-Salamon-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Contact-Fano-manifolds-and-the-LeBrun-Salamon-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Contact-Fano-manifolds-and-the-LeBrun-Salamon-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Contact-Fano-manifolds-and-the-LeBrun-Salamon-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Contact-Fano-manifolds-and-the-LeBrun-Salamon-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Contact-Fano-manifolds-and-the-LeBrun-Salamon-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/reduction.tex","preprints/Contact-Fano-manifolds-and-the-LeBrun-Salamon-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/lines.tex","preprints/Contact-Fano-manifolds-and-the-LeBrun-Salamon-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/smoothing.tex","preprints/Contact-Fano-manifolds-and-the-LeBrun-Salamon-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/extension.tex","preprints/Contact-Fano-manifolds-and-the-LeBrun-Salamon-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/unmarked-boundary.tex","preprints/Contact-Fano-manifolds-and-the-LeBrun-Salamon-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/descent.tex","preprints/Contact-Fano-manifolds-and-the-LeBrun-Salamon-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/jets.tex","preprints/Contact-Fano-manifolds-and-the-LeBrun-Salamon-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/homogeneous.tex","preprints/Contact-Fano-manifolds-and-the-LeBrun-Salamon-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/twistor.tex","preprints/Contact-Fano-manifolds-and-the-LeBrun-Salamon-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Contact-Fano-manifolds-and-the-LeBrun-Salamon-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We resolve the contact-Fano homogeneity conjecture and the Riemannian LeBrun--Salamon conjecture positively. Every smooth connected complex projective contact Fano manifold of complex dimension at least three, with its given contact distribution, is contact-isomorphic to the adjoint variety of a simple complex Lie algebra. Consequently, every closed connected smooth positive quaternionic-K\\\"ahler manifold of real dimension $4m\\geq8$ is homothetic to a compact symmetric Wolf space.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":531},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and prior work","sourceOffset":5935},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The mechanism of the proof","sourceOffset":11750},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and organization","sourceOffset":16444},{"kind":"section","title":"The contact line and the Picard alternatives","sourceOffset":17466},{"kind":"section","title":"Contact lines and a suitable pair","sourceOffset":25242},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The dimension-three endpoint","sourceOffset":32371},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Addition of two Legendrian cones","sourceOffset":33655},{"kind":"section","title":"Smoothing two contact lines","sourceOffset":40298},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The source family and the evaluation cokernel","sourceOffset":41069},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The obstruction with both ends fixed","sourceOffset":45729},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A very free conic and its full neighborhood","sourceOffset":49621}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $(X,F)$ be a smooth connected complex projective contact Fano manifold of dimension $2n+1$, with $n\\geq1$, and let $L=T_X/F$. There exist a finite-dimensional simple complex Lie algebra $\\mathfrak g$ and an algebraic isomorphism \\[ \\varphi:X\\xrightarrow{\\ \\sim\\ }Y_{\\mathfrak g} \\] whose differential carries the given distribution $F$ to the canonical contact distribution on $Y_{\\mathfrak g}$. Moreover, \\[ L\\simeq\\varphi^*\\bigl(\\OO_{\\PP(\\mathfrak g)}(1)|_{Y_{\\mathfrak g}}\\bigr). \\]","sourceOffset":1676,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"K\\\"ahler--Einstein existence","label":"thm:contact-fano-ke","text":"Let $(X,F)$ be a smooth connected complex projective contact Fano manifold of complex dimension $2n+1$, where $n\\geq1$. Then $X$ admits a K\\\"ahler--Einstein metric of positive scalar curvature for its given complex structure.","sourceOffset":2918,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":144336,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":17,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Contact-Fano-manifolds-and-the-LeBrun-Salamon-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Contact-Fano-manifolds-and-the-LeBrun-Salamon-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"063-01","slug":"The-Generalized-Mukai-Conjecture-September-24-2026","familyId":"063","title":"The generalized Mukai conjecture","abstract":"We prove the generalized Mukai conjecture: every positive-dimensional smooth complex Fano manifold of dimension n, Picard number ρ, and pseudoindex ι satisfies $\\rho(\\iota-1)\\le n$. Equality holds precisely for the product of ρ copies of $\\mathbb P^{\\iota-1}$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the generalized Mukai conjecture: every positive-dimensional smooth complex projective Fano manifold of dimension n, Picard number ρ and pseudoindex ι satisfies $\\rho(\\iota-1)\\le n$, with equality exactly for $(\\mathbb P^{\\iota-1})^\\rho$. Here the pseudoindex is the least anticanonical degree of a rational curve.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Generalized-Mukai-Conjecture-September-24-2026/article.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Generalized-Mukai-Conjecture-September-24-2026/article.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Generalized-Mukai-Conjecture-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/The-Generalized-Mukai-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Generalized-Mukai-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-Generalized-Mukai-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/setup.tex","preprints/The-Generalized-Mukai-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/descendants.tex","preprints/The-Generalized-Mukai-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/spectral.tex","preprints/The-Generalized-Mukai-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/trace.tex","preprints/The-Generalized-Mukai-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/equality.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Generalized-Mukai-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the generalized Mukai conjecture: every positive-dimensional smooth complex Fano manifold of dimension $n$, Picard number $\\rho$, and pseudoindex $\\iota$ satisfies $\\rho(\\iota-1)\\le n$. Equality holds precisely for the product of $\\rho$ copies of $\\mathbb P^{\\iota-1}$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":352},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The argument","sourceOffset":3691},{"kind":"section","title":"Quantum divisors and point descendants","sourceOffset":7141},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The descendant recurrence","sourceOffset":10557},{"kind":"section","title":"A lower bound for point descendants","sourceOffset":13271},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A free evaluation fiber","sourceOffset":15365},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Contracting away from the marking","sourceOffset":17471},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The weighted projective fiber","sourceOffset":21841},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The boundary does not contribute","sourceOffset":24483},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Degrees in a weighted projective stack","sourceOffset":26478},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Enough free degrees","sourceOffset":28940},{"kind":"section","title":"An independent joint eigenvalue tuple","sourceOffset":32235}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $X$ be a smooth connected complex projective Fano manifold of positive dimension $n$, Picard number $\\rho_X$ and pseudoindex $\\iota_X$. Then \\[ \\rho_X(\\iota_X-1)\\le n. \\] Equality holds if and only if \\[ X\\cong(\\mathbb P^{\\iota_X-1})^{\\rho_X}. \\]","sourceOffset":1326,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Point-descendant lower bound","label":"prop:descendant-bound","text":"Suppose that \\(\\beta\\) is represented by a nonconstant free map \\(\\mathbb P^1\\to X\\). Put \\(d=d_\\beta\\) and \\(b=ad\\). Then \\[ \\left\\langle\\tau_{d-2}(\\mathrm{pt})\\right\\rangle_\\beta \\ge \\frac{1}{b^{d-1}}. \\]","sourceOffset":14612,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":55579,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Generalized-Mukai-Conjecture-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"064-01","slug":"Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026","familyId":"064","title":"Topological triviality of mu-constant families of surface singularities","abstract":"We prove that every holomorphic one-parameter family of isolated hypersurface singularities in ℂ3 with constant Milnor number is topologically right-trivial. This gives a positive answer to the surface case of the μ-constant problem. The trivialization fixes the parameter and the origin section, and preserves the defining functions.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves topological right-triviality for every holomorphic one-parameter family of isolated hypersurface singularities in ℂ3 with constant Milnor number, resolving the surface case of the μ-constant problem. After shrinking the parameter disk and representatives, ambient homeomorphisms vary jointly continuously, fix the origin section and preserve the defining functions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-local-families.tex","preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-invariant.tex","preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-reduction.tex","preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-compactification.tex","preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-model.tex","preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-boundary.tex","preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-nearby.tex","preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-surface-estimates.tex","preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-square.tex","preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/build/sections/11-numerical-assembly.tex","preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/build/sections/12-resolution.tex","preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/build/sections/13-lifting.tex","preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/build/figures/transport-seam.tex","preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/build/sections/14-low-cases.tex","preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/build/sections/15-local-numerical.tex","preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/build/sections/16-continuous-gradient.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every holomorphic one-parameter family of isolated hypersurface singularities in $\\mathbb C^3$ with constant Milnor number is topologically right-trivial. This gives a positive answer to the surface case of the $\\mu$-constant problem. The trivialization fixes the parameter and the origin section, and preserves the defining functions.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":412},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The problem and its antecedents","sourceOffset":2874},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The resolution invariant and the numerical comparison","sourceOffset":7749},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From resolution to a homeomorphism of the functions","sourceOffset":12523},{"kind":"section","title":"Local families and fixed representatives","sourceOffset":14445},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Birational conventions","sourceOffset":21590},{"kind":"section","title":"The local logarithmic square","sourceOffset":22845},{"kind":"section","title":"Reduction of the numerical assertion to algebraic curves","sourceOffset":27802},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A projective family with the prescribed singularities","sourceOffset":35751},{"kind":"section","title":"A semistable log model","sourceOffset":40966},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The full exceptional boundary","sourceOffset":48941},{"kind":"section","title":"The nearby Euler characteristic along the boundary","sourceOffset":55923}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $F:(\\mathbb C^3\\times\\Delta,\\{0\\}\\times\\Delta)\\to(\\mathbb C,0)$ be holomorphic. Suppose that, for every $t\\in\\Delta$, $f_t(0)=0$, $f_t$ has an isolated critical point at $0$, and $\\mu(f_t,0)=\\mu(f_0,0)$. After shrinking the disk and the germ representatives, there is a homeomorphism germ \\[ \\Phi(x,t)=(\\phi_t(x),t),\\qquad \\phi_t(0)=0,\\quad \\phi_0=\\mathrm{id},\\qquad f_t\\bigl(\\phi_t(x)\\bigr)=f_0(x). \\] Both $\\Phi$ and $\\Phi^{-1}$ are jointly continuous on neighborhoods of the origin section over the original disk.","sourceOffset":1847,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Scalar curve reduction","label":"prop:scalar-curve-reduction","text":"Assume the following algebraic assertion: for every polynomial family in three variables with regular coefficients on a smooth complex algebraic curve $C$, with zero constant and linear terms and constant positive Milnor number at the origin, the Wahl invariant at a marked non-log-canonical fiber equals its value on the nearby general fibers. Then $\\beta(X_t,0)$ is constant near $t=0$ in every holomorphic $\\mu$-constant family whose central surface germ is non-log-canonical.","sourceOffset":32914,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":130419,"sourceFileCount":18,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Topological-triviality-of-mu-constant-families-of-surface-singularities-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"065-01","slug":"Virasoro-Constraints-under-Projectivization-October-5-2026","familyId":"065","title":"Virasoro Constraints under Projectivization","abstract":"We prove that full ordinary descendant Virasoro constraints pass from a smooth projective complex base to the projectivization of any algebraic vector bundle of rank at least two. The bundle need not split and satisfies no positivity requirement. Assuming the full constraints on the base, the conclusion includes every genus, each individual integral curve class, and all cohomology insertions, including primitive and odd classes. The result also applies successively to towers of projective bundles.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the full ordinary unreduced descendant Virasoro conjecture for smooth complete intersections in complex projective space, in every genus and curve class with arbitrary cohomology insertions. The constraints also pass from any smooth projective complex base satisfying them to the projectivization of every algebraic vector bundle of rank at least two, and hence to projective-bundle towers.","pdfPath":"preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-under-Projectivization-October-5-2026/virasoro-constraints-under-projectivization.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-under-Projectivization-October-5-2026/virasoro-constraints-under-projectivization.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-under-Projectivization-October-5-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-under-Projectivization-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-under-Projectivization-October-5-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-under-Projectivization-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-under-Projectivization-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-conventions.tex","preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-under-Projectivization-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-master-space.tex","preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-under-Projectivization-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-localization.tex","preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-under-Projectivization-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-shift.tex","preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-under-Projectivization-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-spectral.tex","preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-under-Projectivization-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-fixed-block.tex","preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-under-Projectivization-October-5-2026/build/sections/08-continuation.tex","preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-under-Projectivization-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-under-Projectivization-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that full ordinary descendant Virasoro constraints pass from a smooth projective complex base to the projectivization of any algebraic vector bundle of rank at least two. The bundle need not split and satisfies no positivity requirement. Assuming the full constraints on the base, the conclusion includes every genus, each individual integral curve class, and all cohomology insertions, including primitive and odd classes. The result also applies successively to towers of projective bundles.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":565},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and related results","sourceOffset":2765},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof mechanism","sourceOffset":6242},{"kind":"section","title":"Descendant potentials and quantization","sourceOffset":10197},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cohomology, curve classes, and descendants","sourceOffset":10637},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The ordinary Virasoro operators","sourceOffset":13104},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Projective constraints and changes of frame","sourceOffset":15670},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coefficientwise finiteness near zero curve variables","sourceOffset":17817},{"kind":"section","title":"The master space and its fixed theories","sourceOffset":21161},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Geometry and integral curve labels","sourceOffset":21579},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Equivariant pairings and inverse-Euler twists","sourceOffset":25616},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ancestors and the fundamental solution","sourceOffset":27832}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $B$ be a smooth connected projective variety over $\\C$, and let $E$ be an algebraic vector bundle of rank $r\\geq2$ on $B$. Let $X=\\mathbb P_B(E)$ parametrize one-dimensional subspaces of the fibers of $E$. Then \\[ \\V(B)\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ \\V(X). \\]","sourceOffset":2028,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Ancestor localization","label":"loc:factorization","text":"There are classes $u_F\\in H_F$ with coefficients of positive Novikov filtration, for $F=B,X$, and a symplectic power series $R(z)\\in\\End(H^{\\mathrm{fix}})[[z]]$, with $R-\\id$ of positive Novikov filtration, such that, writing \\[ S_f(z)=S_{F,\\mathrm{tw}}(u_F,z),\\qquad S_{\\mathrm{bl}}(z)=\\bigoplus_{F=B,X}S_f(z), \\] one has \\begin{align} S_W(z)&=R(z)S_{\\mathrm{bl}}(z),\\\\ \\mathcal A_W(0)&=c\\,U_R \\prod_{F=B,X}\\mathcal A_{F,\\mathrm{tw}}(u_F), \\end{align} where $c$ is an invertible scalar independent of ancestor variables. For every curve coefficient, $S_W(z)$ is the expansion at infinity of a rational function of $z$. In \\eqref{loc:S-factorization} that rational function is expanded at $z=0$; the equality is in Laurent series with a finite lower bound at each curve coefficient. Both $R$ and its inverse admit quantized actions in the coefficientwise completion used here.","sourceOffset":32998,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"conv:projective","text":"An even infinitesimal symplectic operator $A$ is satisfied projectively by a potential $Z$ if \\[ Z^{-1}\\op_\\eta(A)Z \\] is independent of the descendant or ancestor variables. In this expression $\\op_\\eta(A)$ acts on $Z$. Such a scalar may depend on the Novikov variables, equivariant parameters, and $\\hbar$.","sourceOffset":15728,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"geo:fundamental","text":"The fundamental solution $S(u,z)=\\id+O(z^{-1})$ is defined by \\begin{equation} \\eta(b,S(u,z)a) =\\eta(b,a)+\\left\\langle b,\\frac{a}{z-\\psi}\\right\\rangle_{0,u}, \\qquad \\frac1{z-\\psi}=\\sum_{j\\geq0}\\psi^jz^{-j-1}. \\end{equation} The pairing term supplies the unstable two-point contribution.","sourceOffset":29896,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":139725,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Virasoro-Constraints-under-Projectivization-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"065-02","slug":"Virasoro-Constraints-for-Projective-Complete-Intersections-September-24-2026","familyId":"065","title":"Virasoro Constraints for Projective Complete Intersections","abstract":"We prove the Virasoro conjecture for the ordinary descendant Gromov–Witten theory of smooth complete intersections in projective space, in every genus and curve class and with arbitrary cohomology insertions. This includes primitive and odd cohomology classes, with no semisimplicity assumption.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the full ordinary unreduced descendant Virasoro conjecture for smooth complete intersections in complex projective space, in every genus and curve class with arbitrary cohomology insertions. The constraints also pass from any smooth projective complex base satisfying them to the projectivization of every algebraic vector bundle of rank at least two, and hence to projective-bundle towers.","pdfPath":"preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-for-Projective-Complete-Intersections-September-24-2026/article.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-for-Projective-Complete-Intersections-September-24-2026/article.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-for-Projective-Complete-Intersections-September-24-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-for-Projective-Complete-Intersections-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-for-Projective-Complete-Intersections-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-for-Projective-Complete-Intersections-September-24-2026/build/figures/sewing.tex","preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-for-Projective-Complete-Intersections-September-24-2026/build/sections/conventions.tex","preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-for-Projective-Complete-Intersections-September-24-2026/build/sections/firstpage.tex","preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-for-Projective-Complete-Intersections-September-24-2026/build/sections/evaluation.tex","preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-for-Projective-Complete-Intersections-September-24-2026/build/sections/caps.tex","preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-for-Projective-Complete-Intersections-September-24-2026/build/sections/sewing.tex","preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-for-Projective-Complete-Intersections-September-24-2026/build/sections/detection.tex","preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-for-Projective-Complete-Intersections-September-24-2026/build/sections/induction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Virasoro-Constraints-for-Projective-Complete-Intersections-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Virasoro conjecture for the ordinary descendant Gromov--Witten theory of smooth complete intersections in projective space, in every genus and curve class and with arbitrary cohomology insertions. This includes primitive and odd cohomology classes, with no semisimplicity assumption.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":370},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Degeneration and primitive cohomology","sourceOffset":3793},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The transport argument","sourceOffset":6044},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and scope of the inputs","sourceOffset":11703},{"kind":"section","title":"Operators, tensor tests, and conventions","sourceOffset":12766},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The descendant theory","sourceOffset":12841},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The positive Virasoro operators","sourceOffset":15282},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Admissible scalar tests","sourceOffset":19121},{"kind":"section","title":"First-page contractions and locality","sourceOffset":22843},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The three-column complex","sourceOffset":23997},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Filtered multiplicative models and ordered pullbacks","sourceOffset":29636},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Trace and specialization of correspondences","sourceOffset":37400}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(X\\subset\\PP^N_\\C\\) be a smooth complete intersection. Its ordinary, unreduced total descendant potential satisfies the Virasoro constraints in every genus and curve class, with arbitrary insertions from \\(H^*(X;\\C)\\).","sourceOffset":1991,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Positive coefficient rule","label":"conv:positive-rule","text":"A labelled coefficient of \\(L_k^XZ_X\\), for fixed \\(k>0\\), is a finite linear combination of the following operations on the disconnected Gromov--Witten tensors: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item add one special unit marking, with prescribed descendant and \\(\\mathcal R_X\\)-powers; \\item act on one existing marking, changing its descendant exponent and applying an \\(\\mathcal R_X\\)-power, with a coefficient polynomial in its first Hodge degree; \\item add two markings joined by the coevaluation of \\((\\, ,\\,)_X\\), with descendant powers, first-Hodge-degree polynomials, and \\(\\mathcal R_X\\)-powers on its legs; \\item remove two primary markings, leaving their two cohomology inputs in the classical pairing with \\(\\mathcal R_X^{k+1}\\), multiplied by the remaining disconnected Gromov--Witten tensor. \\end{enumerate} The numerical coefficients and genus powers are universal for targets of fixed dimension. At most two existing labels are used nonordinarily in one summand.","sourceOffset":16601,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Coherent data","label":"conv:coherent","text":"Admissible singles and numerical divisor-degree tests on several degenerations are coherent if their restrictions agree on the common local component and stratum diagrams used in the comparison. For source-supplied singles this agreement is required for the specified extending algebraic correspondences with their cohomology slots exposed, before applying the fixed inputs. A class supported at a specified unaffected end is taken to have zero restriction on the other ends and replacement caps.","sourceOffset":21719,"truncated":false},{"kind":"notation","name":"","label":"sew:configurations","text":"All targets in a chain have complex dimension $n$. Let $D$ be a smooth projective variety, possibly disconnected, and let $N$ be a line bundle on $D$. We use the convention that $\\PP(E)$ parametrizes lines in $E$. Set \\[ C=\\PP_D(\\cO_D\\oplus N),\\qquad D_l=\\PP_D(N),\\qquad D_r=\\PP_D(\\cO_D). \\] The normals of $D_l,D_r$ in $C$ are $N^{-1},N$, respectively. Thus a chain, written in its geometric order, is \\begin{equation} U\\mid C_1\\mid\\cdots\\mid C_m\\mid V, \\qquad C_i\\cong C. \\end{equation} Every displayed junction identifies a right section with a left section, or identifies the corresponding diviso","sourceOffset":115928,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":179048,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Virasoro-Constraints-for-Projective-Complete-Intersections-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"066-01","slug":"Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026","familyId":"066","title":"Bounded klt complements for Fano contractions","abstract":"For fixed dimension d and positive rational ϵ, we prove that every ϵ-log-canonical Fano contraction over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero admits, near each closed base point, a klt complement of index bounded only by d and ϵ. Over ℂ, we also obtain monotone klt complements for Fano type pairs with nef anti-log-canonical divisor and coefficients in a fixed finite rational set. This proves the finite-rational-coefficient form of Shokurov's bounded-klt-complement conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the finite-rational-coefficient form of Shokurov’s bounded-klt-complement conjecture for ϵ-lc complex Fano-type pairs with nef anti-log-canonical divisor. For ϵ-lc Fano contractions over any algebraically closed characteristic-zero field, it gives klt complements near every base point, with index bounded only by dimension and positive rational ϵ.","pdfPath":"preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/reduction.tex","preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/initial-cone.tex","preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/constrained-minimum.tex","preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/compatible-filtrations.tex","preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/essential-system.tex","preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/discrepancy-push.tex","preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/scale-tower.tex","preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/width-estimates.tex","preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/bounded-axes.tex","preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/valuation-optimization.tex","preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/trace-lifting.tex","preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026/build/figures/trace-survival.tex","preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/cheap-collapse.tex","preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/rank-growth.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For fixed dimension $d$ and positive rational $\\epsilon$, we prove that every $\\epsilon$-log-canonical Fano contraction over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero admits, near each closed base point, a klt complement of index bounded only by $d$ and $\\epsilon$. Over $\\mathbb C$, we also obtain monotone klt complements for Fano type pairs with nef anti-log-canonical divisor and coefficients in a fixed finite rational set. This proves the finite-rational-coefficient form of Shokurov's bounded-klt-complement conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":622},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier results and the finite-coefficient conjecture","sourceOffset":3660},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Methods and related work","sourceOffset":6398},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Outline of the proof","sourceOffset":8617},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":14870},{"kind":"section","title":"The birational threshold reduction","sourceOffset":16908},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The contradiction sequence","sourceOffset":23278},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite-coefficient consequence","sourceOffset":26380},{"kind":"section","title":"The anticanonical cone and a distinguished lc place","sourceOffset":27763},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Valuations and forms","sourceOffset":28192},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The cone and its canonical generator","sourceOffset":31465},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An lc place at the closed vertex","sourceOffset":37232}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every positive integer $d$ and positive rational number $\\epsilon$ there is a positive integer $N=N(d,\\epsilon)$ with the following property. Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, and let $f\\colon X\\to Z$ be a projective surjective morphism of normal integral $k$-varieties such that $f_*\\OO_X=\\OO_Z$, $\\dim X=d$, $K_X$ is $\\Q$-Cartier, $X$ is $\\epsilon$-lc, and $-K_X$ is ample over $Z$. For every closed point $z\\in Z$ there are an effective $\\Q$-divisor $B$ on $X$ and a Zariski open neighbourhood $U$ of $z$ such that $(X,B)$ is klt over $U$ and \\[ N(K_X+B)|_{f^{-1}(U)}\\sim 0 \\] is an integral Cartier divisor.","sourceOffset":1782,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"b:principal-denominators","text":"In Lemma \\ref{b:primary-minimization}, with the fixed positive integer \\(r_{\\mathrm{ind}}\\) as there, assume in addition that \\(r_{\\mathrm{ind}}\\mathbf M_A\\) is principal. Then every value \\((\\pi_\\#A)(P)\\) has bounded denominator, in terms only of the dimension and \\(r_{\\mathrm{ind}}\\).","sourceOffset":123740,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"b:discrepancy-presentation","text":"A discrepancy presentation on a normal projective model \\(Y\\) is \\[ a=a_{K,Y}+\\mathbf M-\\overline D. \\] Here \\(a_{K,Y}\\) is the form discrepancy, \\(\\mathbf M\\) is b-nef and b-\\(\\Q\\)-Cartier, and \\(D\\) is a rational Cartier divisor on \\(Y\\). The boundary trace \\(B=\\sum_P(1-a(P))P\\), summed over strict primes on \\(Y\\), satisfies \\[ K_Y+B+M_Y=D \\] as divisors. The presentation is Calabi--Yau if \\(D=0\\). Sub-generalized lc and sub-generalized klt mean, respectively, \\(a(P)\\ge0\\) and \\(a(P)>0\\) for every relevant prime divisor on every higher model. Effectivity means effectivity of the boundary tra","sourceOffset":108772,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"b:valuative-push","text":"For a projective contraction \\(\\pi:Y\\to V\\), put \\begin{equation} (\\pi_\\#a)(P)= \\min_{\\substack{F|_{\\C(V)}=mP\\\\m>0}}\\frac{a(F)}m. \\end{equation} We use this operation for data sub-generalized lc over the generic point of \\(V\\), with total divisor pulled back from \\(V\\), and for positive scalings of such data. A prime valuation upstairs is horizontal when its restriction to \\(\\C(V)\\) is trivial.","sourceOffset":109908,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/066.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":381137,"sourceFileCount":17,"statementCount":25,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Bounded-klt-complements-for-Fano-contractions-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"066-02","slug":"Uniform-Cartier-sections-for-Fano-type-contractions-September-25-2026","familyId":"066","title":"Uniform Cartier sections for Fano type contractions","abstract":"We prove the Cartier-divisor conjecture of Birkar and Shokurov for rational boundaries in characteristic zero and for real boundaries over ℂ. For an ϵ-lc Fano type contraction with positive-dimensional base and nef negative log canonical class, a Cartier divisor through any prescribed base point can be chosen with pullback log canonical threshold bounded below in terms of the dimension and ϵ alone.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the finite-rational-coefficient form of Shokurov’s bounded-klt-complement conjecture for ϵ-lc complex Fano-type pairs with nef anti-log-canonical divisor. For ϵ-lc Fano contractions over any algebraically closed characteristic-zero field, it gives klt complements near every base point, with index bounded only by dimension and positive rational ϵ.","pdfPath":"preprints/Uniform-Cartier-sections-for-Fano-type-contractions-September-25-2026/Uniform-Cartier-sections-for-Fano-type-contractions-September-25-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-Cartier-sections-for-Fano-type-contractions-September-25-2026/Uniform-Cartier-sections-for-Fano-type-contractions-September-25-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-Cartier-sections-for-Fano-type-contractions-September-25-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Uniform-Cartier-sections-for-Fano-type-contractions-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Uniform-Cartier-sections-for-Fano-type-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Uniform-Cartier-sections-for-Fano-type-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/A-discriminants.tex","preprints/Uniform-Cartier-sections-for-Fano-type-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/B-phase-nonvanishing.tex","preprints/Uniform-Cartier-sections-for-Fano-type-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/C-cutoff-fields.tex","preprints/Uniform-Cartier-sections-for-Fano-type-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/D-cone-characters.tex","preprints/Uniform-Cartier-sections-for-Fano-type-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/E-lc-slices.tex","preprints/Uniform-Cartier-sections-for-Fano-type-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/F-degeneration.tex","preprints/Uniform-Cartier-sections-for-Fano-type-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/G-pinning.tex","preprints/Uniform-Cartier-sections-for-Fano-type-contractions-September-25-2026/build/sections/H-reduction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Uniform-Cartier-sections-for-Fano-type-contractions-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Cartier-divisor conjecture of Birkar and Shokurov for rational boundaries in characteristic zero and for real boundaries over $\\C$. For an $\\epsilon$-lc Fano type contraction with positive-dimensional base and nef negative log canonical class, a Cartier divisor through any prescribed base point can be chosen with pullback log canonical threshold bounded below in terms of the dimension and $\\epsilon$ alone.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":468},{"kind":"section","title":"Discriminant comparison and uniform chambers","sourceOffset":16087},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and the canonical bundle formula","sourceOffset":16533},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The comparison statements","sourceOffset":22275},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Extraction and the Mori step","sourceOffset":27824},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Tailored complements and connected minimizing places","sourceOffset":31255},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform charts and discrepancy estimates","sourceOffset":39774},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Integral approximation on the cones","sourceOffset":68665},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proofs of the comparison statements","sourceOffset":74488},{"kind":"section","title":"Nonvanishing from signed phases","sourceOffset":93726},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Seifert bundles and two consequences of comparison","sourceOffset":97744},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof of phase nonvanishing","sourceOffset":110163}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"maintheorem","name":"Uniform Cartier sections","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $d>0$ and every real number $\\epsilon>0$, there is a real number $\\tau=\\tau(d,\\epsilon)>0$ with the following property. Let $(X,B)$ be an $\\epsilon$-lc rational pair of dimension $d$, and let $f:X\\to Z$ be a Fano type contraction of normal integral quasi-projective varieties with $\\dim Z>0$. Suppose that $-(K_X+B)$ is nef over $Z$. For every closed point $z\\in Z$, there are an open neighbourhood $U$ of $z$ and a nonzero effective Cartier divisor $D$ on $U$, with $z\\in\\Supp D$, such that \\[ \\bigl(f^{-1}(U),B|_{f^{-1}(U)}+\\tau f^*D\\bigr) \\quad\\text{is log canonical.} \\]","sourceOffset":2428,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Lc equality and principal boundaries","label":"cor:F-lc-degeneration","text":"If $w$ is $h$-lc, then $(S^0,\\divisor(\\bar h))$ is lc and $\\omega^0$ has the same fine torus weight and $G$-character as $\\bar h$. Consequently $\\omega^0/\\bar h$ is invariant, and the quotient cone has the log-form data of Section \\ref{sec:D}. If $\\Delta$ is any effective rational sum of principal divisors near $x$, with $(S,\\Delta)$ lc and $A_S(w)=w(\\Delta)$, its initial boundary $\\Delta^0$ is lc on $S^0$. All these assertions also hold with specified additional equivariant gradings.","sourceOffset":284382,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/066.md","scopeText":"The paper develops uniform Cartier sections for Fano-type contractions as a step toward bounded klt complements. The linked formalization proves a local compactness result: for positive numbers $a_i$, the chart-field retractions indexed by nonnegative weights satisfying $\\sum_i a_iw_i=1$ form a compact set in the pointwise topology on the fraction field.\n\nThe chart is built over an algebraically closed field from the localization of a polynomial ring at its origin. Its local domain is Noetherian, essentially of finite type and formally unramified over that localization, and formally étale over the polynomial ring. Uniform Cartier sections, bounded klt complements, and the paper's real-boundary and companion conclusions are outside this supporting statement.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/066.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":394124,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":25,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Uniform-Cartier-sections-for-Fano-type-contractions-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Uniform-Cartier-sections-for-Fano-type-contractions-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"067-01","slug":"The-Campana-Peternell-conjecture-in-dimension-six-September-25-2026","familyId":"067","title":"The Campana–Peternell conjecture in dimension six","abstract":"We prove that every smooth connected complex projective Fano sixfold with nef tangent bundle is rational homogeneous, resolving the Campana–Peternell conjecture in complex dimension six. As a consequence, a connected compact Kähler manifold X with nef holomorphic tangent bundle and $\\dim_{\\mathbb C}X-\\widetilde q(X)\\leq6$ has ordinary universal cover $F\\times\\mathbb C^{\\widetilde q(X)}$, where F is a rational homogeneous manifold and $\\widetilde q(X)$ is the maximal irregularity of a connected finite étale cover.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the Campana–Peternell conjecture in complex dimension six: every smooth connected complex projective Fano sixfold with nef tangent bundle is rational homogeneous.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Campana-Peternell-conjecture-in-dimension-six-September-25-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Campana-Peternell-conjecture-in-dimension-six-September-25-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Campana-Peternell-conjecture-in-dimension-six-September-25-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/The-Campana-Peternell-conjecture-in-dimension-six-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Campana-Peternell-conjecture-in-dimension-six-September-25-2026/build/glyph-unicode.tex","preprints/The-Campana-Peternell-conjecture-in-dimension-six-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-Campana-Peternell-conjecture-in-dimension-six-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-minimal-curves.tex","preprints/The-Campana-Peternell-conjecture-in-dimension-six-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-p1-cohomology.tex","preprints/The-Campana-Peternell-conjecture-in-dimension-six-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-pseudoindex-four.tex","preprints/The-Campana-Peternell-conjecture-in-dimension-six-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-characteristic-classes.tex","preprints/The-Campana-Peternell-conjecture-in-dimension-six-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-arithmetic.tex","preprints/The-Campana-Peternell-conjecture-in-dimension-six-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-grassmannian.tex","preprints/The-Campana-Peternell-conjecture-in-dimension-six-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-conclusion.tex","preprints/The-Campana-Peternell-conjecture-in-dimension-six-September-25-2026/build/sections/08-certificate.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Campana-Peternell-conjecture-in-dimension-six-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every smooth connected complex projective Fano sixfold with nef tangent bundle is rational homogeneous, resolving the Campana--Peternell conjecture in complex dimension six. As a consequence, a connected compact K\\\"ahler manifold $X$ with nef holomorphic tangent bundle and $\\dim_{\\mathbb C}X-\\widetilde q(X)\\leq6$ has ordinary universal cover $F\\times\\mathbb C^{\\widetilde q(X)}$, where $F$ is a rational homogeneous manifold and $\\widetilde q(X)$ is the maximal irregularity of a connected finite \\'{e}tale cover.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":603},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The argument","sourceOffset":5292},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Consequences of the classification","sourceOffset":7989},{"kind":"section","title":"Minimal rational curves","sourceOffset":12115},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cohomology of a rational-curve fibration","sourceOffset":18826},{"kind":"section","title":"Pseudoindex four","sourceOffset":21315},{"kind":"section","title":"Characteristic-class constraints in pseudoindex five","sourceOffset":29115},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The polarized evaluation fibre","sourceOffset":30033},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Classes descending to the parameter space","sourceOffset":31938},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two direct-image identities","sourceOffset":33709},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Explicit polynomial representatives","sourceOffset":36475},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The linear constraints and their geometric meaning","sourceOffset":40225}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Every smooth connected complex projective Fano variety of dimension six with nef tangent bundle is rational homogeneous.","sourceOffset":1725,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Albanese fibres and uniformization","label":"cor:nef-tangent-uniformization","text":"Let $X$ be a connected compact K\\\"ahler manifold whose holomorphic tangent bundle is nef in the analytic sense. Suppose \\[ 0\\leq d=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}X-\\widetilde q(X)\\leq6. \\] Then there is a connected finite \\'{e}tale cover $X'\\to X$ with the following properties. \\begin{enumerate} \\item The Albanese map $\\alpha:X'\\to A=\\operatorname{Alb}(X')$ is a smooth proper surjection with connected fibres, where $A$ is a complex torus of dimension $\\widetilde q(X)$. Every fibre is rational homogeneous of dimension $d$, with a point allowed when $d=0$. \\item There is one such fibre $F$ for which $\\alpha$ is a holomorphically locally trivial $F$-bundle. The ordinary universal cover of $X$ is biholomorphic to \\[ \\widetilde X\\simeq F\\times\\mathbb C^{\\widetilde q(X)}. \\] It is therefore biholomorphic to a quasi-projective variety and is holomorphically convex. Its Remmert reduction is the projection \\[ F\\times\\mathbb C^{\\widetilde q(X)} \\longrightarrow\\mathbb C^{\\widetilde q(X)}. \\] \\end{enumerate} In particular, these conclusions hold whenever $\\dim_{\\mathbb C}X\\leq6$.","sourceOffset":9425,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":70191,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Campana-Peternell-conjecture-in-dimension-six-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/The-Campana-Peternell-conjecture-in-dimension-six-September-25-2026/build/glyph-unicode.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"068-01","slug":"Anticanonical-nonvanishing-from-smooth-semipositivity-September-26-2026","familyId":"068","title":"Anticanonical nonvanishing from smooth semipositivity","abstract":"Every smooth connected projective complex variety with smoothly semipositive anticanonical bundle has a nonzero section of some positive anticanonical power.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"If X is a smooth connected complex projective variety and $-K_X$ admits a smooth Hermitian metric with nonnegative curvature, then $H^0(X,-mK_X)\\ne0$ for some m > 0. Thus smooth semipositivity forces a nonzero section of a positive tensor power of the anticanonical bundle in every dimension.","pdfPath":"preprints/Anticanonical-nonvanishing-from-smooth-semipositivity-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Anticanonical-nonvanishing-from-smooth-semipositivity-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Anticanonical-nonvanishing-from-smooth-semipositivity-September-26-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Anticanonical-nonvanishing-from-smooth-semipositivity-September-26-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Anticanonical-nonvanishing-from-smooth-semipositivity-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Anticanonical-nonvanishing-from-smooth-semipositivity-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/cover.tex","preprints/Anticanonical-nonvanishing-from-smooth-semipositivity-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/models.tex","preprints/Anticanonical-nonvanishing-from-smooth-semipositivity-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/slope.tex","preprints/Anticanonical-nonvanishing-from-smooth-semipositivity-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/transfer.tex","preprints/Anticanonical-nonvanishing-from-smooth-semipositivity-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/induction.tex","preprints/Anticanonical-nonvanishing-from-smooth-semipositivity-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/torus.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Anticanonical-nonvanishing-from-smooth-semipositivity-September-26-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every smooth connected projective complex variety with smoothly semipositive anticanonical bundle has a nonzero section of some positive anticanonical power.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":220},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Geometry, monodromy, and earlier methods","sourceOffset":1846},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite-volume theorem and the proof strategy","sourceOffset":6324},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite covers and residual monodromy","sourceOffset":12231},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The norm of a section","sourceOffset":25465},{"kind":"section","title":"Measures and models for rational fibrations","sourceOffset":27378},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Adjoint measures and extension of weights","sourceOffset":27751},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A normal model and a smooth resolution","sourceOffset":29955},{"kind":"section","title":"Cotangent tensors and the permitted boundary","sourceOffset":34041},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removing the ramification from the Bergman metric","sourceOffset":35420},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The slope bound and the face used by induction","sourceOffset":42073},{"kind":"section","title":"Transferring a finite measure and a bounded metric","sourceOffset":49057}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Anticanonical nonvanishing","label":"thm:headline","text":"Let \\(X\\) be a smooth connected projective variety over \\(\\C\\). If \\(-K_X\\) admits a smooth Hermitian metric of semipositive curvature, then \\(H^0(X,-mK_X)\\ne0\\) for some integer \\(m>0\\).","sourceOffset":771,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Finite volume and sections with prescribed poles","label":"thm:finite-volume","text":"Let $Z$ be a smooth connected projective complex variety of dimension $d$ with $H^0(Z,\\Omega_Z^q)=0$ for every $1\\le q\\le d$. Let $D\\ge0$ be an integral divisor. Suppose that $J=-K_Z+D$ has a semipositive singular Hermitian metric $h_J$ satisfying \\[ \\int_Z |s_D|_{h_J}^2<\\infty. \\] If a line bundle $L$ on $Z$ has a semipositive singular Hermitian metric with locally bounded weights, then there exist integers $a>0$ and $b\\ge0$ such that \\[ H^0(Z,aL+bD)\\ne0. \\]","sourceOffset":6965,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":89395,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Anticanonical-nonvanishing-from-smooth-semipositivity-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"068-02","slug":"Invariant-anticanonical-indices-and-conversion-of-twisted-differentials-September-26-2026","familyId":"068","title":"Invariant anticanonical indices and conversion of twisted differentials","abstract":"For a holomorphic action of a compact torus on a compact complex manifold, we prove that the invariant Euler characteristic of naturally linearized anticanonical powers is polynomial on a divisible progression, with its actual value at exponent zero. Independently, on a smooth projective variety with smoothly semipositive anticanonical bundle, we remove a fixed pseudoeffective error from an unbounded sequence of effective twists. These results convert invariant cohomology into twisted differential forms and prove anticanonical nonvanishing on smooth projective varieties with smoothly semipositive anticanonical bundle, by descending the forms before conversion.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"If X is a smooth connected complex projective variety and $-K_X$ admits a smooth Hermitian metric with nonnegative curvature, then $H^0(X,-mK_X)\\ne0$ for some m > 0. Thus smooth semipositivity forces a nonzero section of a positive tensor power of the anticanonical bundle in every dimension.","pdfPath":"preprints/Invariant-anticanonical-indices-and-conversion-of-twisted-differentials-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Invariant-anticanonical-indices-and-conversion-of-twisted-differentials-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Invariant-anticanonical-indices-and-conversion-of-twisted-differentials-September-26-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Invariant-anticanonical-indices-and-conversion-of-twisted-differentials-September-26-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Invariant-anticanonical-indices-and-conversion-of-twisted-differentials-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Invariant-anticanonical-indices-and-conversion-of-twisted-differentials-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/index.tex","preprints/Invariant-anticanonical-indices-and-conversion-of-twisted-differentials-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/conversion.tex","preprints/Invariant-anticanonical-indices-and-conversion-of-twisted-differentials-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/differentials.tex","preprints/Invariant-anticanonical-indices-and-conversion-of-twisted-differentials-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/descent.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Invariant-anticanonical-indices-and-conversion-of-twisted-differentials-September-26-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For a holomorphic action of a compact torus on a compact complex manifold, we prove that the invariant Euler characteristic of naturally linearized anticanonical powers is polynomial on a divisible progression, with its actual value at exponent zero. Independently, on a smooth projective variety with smoothly semipositive anticanonical bundle, we remove a fixed pseudoeffective error from an unbounded sequence of effective twists. These results convert invariant cohomology into twisted differential forms and prove anticanonical nonvanishing on smooth projective varieties with smoothly semipositive anticanonical bundle, by descending the forms before conversion.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":714},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The invariant index and its value at zero","sourceOffset":1430},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removing a fixed error","sourceOffset":5241},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Application through monodromy","sourceOffset":8431},{"kind":"section","title":"The invariant index, including exponent zero","sourceOffset":11432},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The deleted boundary","sourceOffset":16002},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Localization and the anticanonical character","sourceOffset":20191},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Invariant twisted forms","sourceOffset":26032},{"kind":"section","title":"Removing a pseudoeffective error","sourceOffset":28327},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The base and the relative section","sourceOffset":29410},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The bounded base metric","sourceOffset":37844},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Vanishing at every nonnegative exponent","sourceOffset":44100}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Invariant anticanonical index","label":"thm:index","text":"Let a compact real torus \\(T\\) act holomorphically on a compact complex manifold \\(F\\), and give \\(-K_F\\) its natural linearization. There are an integer \\(a>0\\) and a polynomial \\(P\\in\\Q[t]\\) such that \\[ I_T(m)=P(m)\\quad\\text{for every positive multiple \\(m\\) of \\(a\\)}, \\qquad P(0)=I_T(0). \\]","sourceOffset":2029,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Pseudoeffective-error conversion","label":"thm:conversion","text":"Let \\(S\\) be a smooth connected projective complex variety, and suppose that \\(L=-K_S\\) has a smooth Hermitian metric with semipositive Chern curvature. Let \\(D\\) be a pseudoeffective Cartier divisor. If \\[ H^0(S,\\OO_S(m_jL-D))\\ne0 \\] for a strictly increasing sequence of positive integers \\(m_j\\), then \\(H^0(S,\\OO_S(kL))\\ne0\\) for some integer \\(k>0\\).","sourceOffset":5641,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":57300,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Invariant-anticanonical-indices-and-conversion-of-twisted-differentials-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"068-03","slug":"Bounded-anticanonical-metrics-on-klt-pairs-and-torus-quotients-September-26-2026","familyId":"068","title":"Bounded anticanonical metrics on klt pairs and torus quotients","abstract":"Let $(W,D)$ be a projective ℚ-factorial klt pair with effective rational boundary. We prove that a positive Cartier multiple of $-(K_W+D)$ has a nonzero section if this divisor is nef, its pullback to a resolution admits a semipositive metric with locally bounded weights, and the resolution has nonzero structure-sheaf Euler characteristic. Consequently a smooth rationally connected projective variety with smoothly semipositive anticanonical bundle has a nonzero naturally invariant anticanonical plurisection for every algebraic torus action.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"If X is a smooth connected complex projective variety and $-K_X$ admits a smooth Hermitian metric with nonnegative curvature, then $H^0(X,-mK_X)\\ne0$ for some m > 0. Thus smooth semipositivity forces a nonzero section of a positive tensor power of the anticanonical bundle in every dimension.","pdfPath":"preprints/Bounded-anticanonical-metrics-on-klt-pairs-and-torus-quotients-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Bounded-anticanonical-metrics-on-klt-pairs-and-torus-quotients-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Bounded-anticanonical-metrics-on-klt-pairs-and-torus-quotients-September-26-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Bounded-anticanonical-metrics-on-klt-pairs-and-torus-quotients-September-26-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Bounded-anticanonical-metrics-on-klt-pairs-and-torus-quotients-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Bounded-anticanonical-metrics-on-klt-pairs-and-torus-quotients-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/criterion.tex","preprints/Bounded-anticanonical-metrics-on-klt-pairs-and-torus-quotients-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/quotient.tex","preprints/Bounded-anticanonical-metrics-on-klt-pairs-and-torus-quotients-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/descent.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Bounded-anticanonical-metrics-on-klt-pairs-and-torus-quotients-September-26-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let $(W,D)$ be a projective $\\QQ$-factorial klt pair with effective rational boundary. We prove that a positive Cartier multiple of $-(K_W+D)$ has a nonzero section if this divisor is nef, its pullback to a resolution admits a semipositive metric with locally bounded weights, and the resolution has nonzero structure-sheaf Euler characteristic. Consequently a smooth rationally connected projective variety with smoothly semipositive anticanonical bundle has a nonzero naturally invariant anticanonical plurisection for every algebraic torus action.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":613},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and predecessors","sourceOffset":5050},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the proofs fit together","sourceOffset":10507},{"kind":"section","title":"A bounded-metric criterion for klt pairs","sourceOffset":14340},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Analytic inputs and numerical comparisons","sourceOffset":14822},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Twisted differential forms and determinants","sourceOffset":17732},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A maximal dominated fibration","sourceOffset":21421},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Horizontal affineness and vertical minima","sourceOffset":26315},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Discrepancy rounding and rank one for every twist","sourceOffset":29549},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A bounded metric from normalized fiber integrals","sourceOffset":31878},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One small mixture and Euler interpolation","sourceOffset":38343},{"kind":"section","title":"Stable torus quotients and invariant sections","sourceOffset":42814}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Bounded-metric nonvanishing","label":"thm:klt","text":"Let $(W,D)$ be a projective $\\QQ$-factorial klt pair with effective rational boundary, and put $P=-(K_W+D)$. Suppose that $P$ is nef. Suppose that on a smooth projective resolution $\\rho:\\widetilde W\\to W$, the pullback $\\rho^*P$ has a semipositive metric with locally bounded weights and \\[ \\chi(\\widetilde W,\\OO_{\\widetilde W})\\ne0. \\] Then $H^0(W,\\OO_W(mP))\\ne0$ for some integer $m>0$ such that $mP$ is Cartier.","sourceOffset":2487,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Invariant sections from a stable quotient","label":"thm:equivariant","text":"Let $Z$ be a smooth rationally connected projective variety whose anticanonical bundle has a smooth semipositive Hermitian metric. For every algebraic torus $T$ acting on $Z$, some integer $m>0$ satisfies \\[ H^0(Z,-mK_Z)^T\\ne0, \\] with respect to the natural anticanonical linearization.","sourceOffset":3640,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":69828,"sourceFileCount":5,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Bounded-anticanonical-metrics-on-klt-pairs-and-torus-quotients-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"068-04","slug":"Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026","familyId":"068","title":"Cohomological transfer and equivariant anticanonical sections","abstract":"Let X be a smooth projective complex variety with smoothly semipositive anticanonical bundle. For any torus linearization of that bundle, we show that invariant sections in unbounded degrees with one fixed negative pseudoeffective error yield an invariant section in a positive untwisted degree. Combining the natural invariant index with compact-monodromy structure, we deduce that every such X has a nonzero section of some positive anticanonical power.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"If X is a smooth connected complex projective variety and $-K_X$ admits a smooth Hermitian metric with nonnegative curvature, then $H^0(X,-mK_X)\\ne0$ for some m > 0. Thus smooth semipositivity forces a nonzero section of a positive tensor power of the anticanonical bundle in every dimension.","pdfPath":"preprints/Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/models.tex","preprints/Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/transfer.tex","preprints/Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/cohomological-input.tex","preprints/Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/extremal-transfer.tex","preprints/Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/ordinary.tex","preprints/Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/harmonic-restriction.tex","preprints/Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/harmonic-iitaka.tex","preprints/Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/harmonic-fields.tex","preprints/Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/harmonic-extremal.tex","preprints/Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/alteration.tex","preprints/Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/local-ideal.tex","preprints/Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/stabilized.tex","preprints/Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/descent.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let $X$ be a smooth projective complex variety with smoothly semipositive anticanonical bundle. For any torus linearization of that bundle, we show that invariant sections in unbounded degrees with one fixed negative pseudoeffective error yield an invariant section in a positive untwisted degree. Combining the natural invariant index with compact-monodromy structure, we deduce that every such $X$ has a nonzero section of some positive anticanonical power.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":522},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why a fibration removes the error","sourceOffset":4033},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A separate harmonic mechanism","sourceOffset":6247},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and analytic predecessors","sourceOffset":8072},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and reading path","sourceOffset":12994},{"kind":"section","title":"Preparing a fibration and measuring its relative section","sourceOffset":16092},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rank-one adjoints and a continuous maximum metric","sourceOffset":19087},{"kind":"section","title":"Character-sensitive transfer with a fixed multiplier ideal","sourceOffset":26260},{"kind":"section","title":"Natural linearization and cohomological input","sourceOffset":37592},{"kind":"section","title":"Exact source characters from the transfer theorem","sourceOffset":45791},{"kind":"section","title":"Singular injectivity and an Iitaka conversion","sourceOffset":51954},{"kind":"section","title":"Harmonic restriction with a continuous base metric","sourceOffset":63848}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"corollary","name":"Natural invariant nonvanishing","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $X$ be smooth connected projective over $\\C$, with smoothly semipositive $L=-K_X$. If an algebraic torus $T$ acts on $X$ and $\\chi(X,\\mathcal O_X)\\ne0$, then $H^0(X,mL)^T\\ne0$ for some integer $m>0$, with the natural linearization. The torus may be trivial.","sourceOffset":41810,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Invariant transfer","label":"inj:transfer","text":"Let $X$ be smooth connected projective, let $L=-K_X$ carry a smooth semipositive metric, and let an algebraic torus $T$ act on $X$. Let $P,M$ be linearized line bundles such that $P=L$ as ordinary line bundles and $-M$ is pseudoeffective. The linearization of $P$ may differ from the natural one on $L$. Suppose that for an unbounded $I\\subset\\mathbb Z_{>0}$ there are nonzero invariant sections \\[ s_i\\in H^0(X,M+iP)^T\\qquad(i\\in I). \\] Then $H^0(X,mP)^T\\ne0$ for some integer $m>0$.","sourceOffset":1815,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":167449,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":19,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Cohomological-transfer-and-equivariant-anticanonical-sections-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"068-05","slug":"Metric-descent-and-rank-preserving-contractions-September-26-2026","familyId":"068","title":"Metric descent and rank-preserving contractions","abstract":"From a smooth semipositive anticanonical metric on a smooth projective complex variety, we construct an unweighted integrable semipositive metric on a corrected anticanonical line of a smooth base, using a normal equidimensional toroidal model and full generic adjoint rank one at exponent zero. The explicit rational boundary correction pulls back to an exceptional divisor. On varieties without positive-degree holomorphic forms, two-metric transfer gives sections with prescribed boundary poles from a finite-volume log-anticanonical metric and a target line with bounded semipositive weights. In particular, a smooth projective complex variety with a smoothly semipositive anticanonical bundle and no such forms has a nonzero invariant section of a positive anticanonical multiple for every torus action with its natural linearization. On this class of varieties, generic section and adjoint-rank hypotheses give contractions of invariant fibrations that preserve both conditions unless an invariant global section already exists.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"If X is a smooth connected complex projective variety and $-K_X$ admits a smooth Hermitian metric with nonnegative curvature, then $H^0(X,-mK_X)\\ne0$ for some m > 0. Thus smooth semipositivity forces a nonzero section of a positive tensor power of the anticanonical bundle in every dimension.","pdfPath":"preprints/Metric-descent-and-rank-preserving-contractions-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Metric-descent-and-rank-preserving-contractions-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Metric-descent-and-rank-preserving-contractions-September-26-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Metric-descent-and-rank-preserving-contractions-September-26-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Metric-descent-and-rank-preserving-contractions-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Metric-descent-and-rank-preserving-contractions-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/toroidal-volume.tex","preprints/Metric-descent-and-rank-preserving-contractions-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/analytic-input.tex","preprints/Metric-descent-and-rank-preserving-contractions-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/contraction.tex","preprints/Metric-descent-and-rank-preserving-contractions-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/boundary-transfer.tex","preprints/Metric-descent-and-rank-preserving-contractions-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/reductions.tex","preprints/Metric-descent-and-rank-preserving-contractions-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/orbit-integrals.tex","preprints/Metric-descent-and-rank-preserving-contractions-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/fields.tex","preprints/Metric-descent-and-rank-preserving-contractions-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/boundary-applications.tex","preprints/Metric-descent-and-rank-preserving-contractions-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/multiplicative-systems.tex","preprints/Metric-descent-and-rank-preserving-contractions-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/reduced-flat.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Metric-descent-and-rank-preserving-contractions-September-26-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"From a smooth semipositive anticanonical metric on a smooth projective complex variety, we construct an unweighted integrable semipositive metric on a corrected anticanonical line of a smooth base, using a normal equidimensional toroidal model and full generic adjoint rank one at exponent zero. The explicit rational boundary correction pulls back to an exceptional divisor. On varieties without positive-degree holomorphic forms, two-metric transfer gives sections with prescribed boundary poles from a finite-volume log-anticanonical metric and a target line with bounded semipositive weights. In particular, a smooth projective complex variety with a smoothly semipositive anticanonical bundle and no such forms has a nonzero invariant section of a positive anticanonical multiple for every torus action with its natural linearization. On this class of varieties, generic section and adjoint-rank hypotheses give contractions of invariant fibrations that preserve both conditions unless an invariant global section already exists.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":1097},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From a volume on the total space to a metric on the base","sourceOffset":3533},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A contraction that keeps its generic section","sourceOffset":5972},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Methods, predecessors, and other metric hypotheses","sourceOffset":8180},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reading the proofs","sourceOffset":13827},{"kind":"section","title":"An integrable anticanonical metric on a toroidal base","sourceOffset":15166},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The correction and its discrepancies","sourceOffset":19628},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positivity of the integrated density","sourceOffset":24805},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Extension and unweighted integrability","sourceOffset":29098},{"kind":"section","title":"Two metrics and controlled pole support","sourceOffset":34275},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Models and adjoint measures","sourceOffset":35522},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Transferring the two metrics","sourceOffset":36952}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Toroidal volume metric","label":"g:toroidal-volume","text":"Let $X$ and $Y$ be smooth connected projective complex varieties, with $n=\\dim X$ and $d=\\dim Y>0$, and let $L=-K_X$ carry a smooth Hermitian metric $h$ of semipositive curvature $\\alpha$. Suppose \\[ p:W\\longrightarrow X,\\qquad f:W\\longrightarrow Y \\] are projective morphisms, where $W$ is normal and integral, $p$ is birational, and $f$ is surjective, equidimensional, and toroidal for strict toroidal embeddings. Choose a smooth projective resolution $\\rho:Z\\to W$, and put $\\pi=p\\rho$, $g=f\\rho$, and $E_Z=K_{Z/X}$. Assume \\begin{equation} \\operatorname{rk}g_*\\mathcal O_Z(E_Z)=1. \\end{equation} For each prime divisor $Q\\subset Y$ and each prime $E\\subset W$ dominating $Q$, set \\[ a_E=\\operatorname{ord}_E K_{W/X},\\qquad e_E=\\operatorname{ord}_E f^*Q, \\] where the Jacobian divisor $K_{W/X}=K_W-p^*K_X$ is defined in codimension one. Define \\begin{equation} a_Q=\\max\\left\\{0,\\min_{f(E)=Q} \\left(\\frac{1+a_E}{e_E}-1\\right)\\right\\}, \\qquad A_0=\\sum_Q a_Q Q. \\end{equation} Then $A_0$ is an effective rational divisor with finite support, and $f^*A_0$ is $p$-exceptional. The rational line bundle $-K_Y+A_0$ has a singular Hermitian metric of semipositive curvature whose squared frame norms are locally integrable without a divisor weight. This metric is smooth on a nonempty Zariski open subset of $Y$. Moreover, let $U\\subset Y$ be a nonempty Zariski open set where $g$ is smooth and formation of the adjoint direct image $g_*(K_{Z/Y}+\\pi^*L)$ commutes with base change. If there are a nonempty","sourceOffset":16753,"truncated":true},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"g:correction-iitaka-zero","text":"For the correction in Theorem \\ref{g:toroidal-volume}, $\\kappa(Y,A_0)=0$. More precisely, for every positive integer $m$ for which $mA_0$ is integral, \\[ H^0(Y,\\mathcal O_Y(mA_0)) =\\mathbb C\\,s_{mA_0}. \\]","sourceOffset":33389,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":256301,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":24,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Metric-descent-and-rank-preserving-contractions-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"068-06","slug":"Integrable-metrics-and-effectivity-with-controlled-boundary-September-26-2026","familyId":"068","title":"Integrable metrics and effectivity with controlled boundary","abstract":"Let Y be smooth projective and let C be an effective integral divisor. If $-K_Y+C$ admits a metric with a global strict curvature lower bound for which the canonical section of C is locally square integrable, every pseudoeffective rational divisor becomes rationally effective after an effective correction supported on C. For a smooth projective X with smoothly semipositive $L=-K_X$, sections of $m_jL-P$ at unbounded positive exponents, with any fixed pseudoeffective Cartier error P, yield a section of a positive multiple of L.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"If X is a smooth connected complex projective variety and $-K_X$ admits a smooth Hermitian metric with nonnegative curvature, then $H^0(X,-mK_X)\\ne0$ for some m > 0. Thus smooth semipositivity forces a nonzero section of a positive tensor power of the anticanonical bundle in every dimension.","pdfPath":"preprints/Integrable-metrics-and-effectivity-with-controlled-boundary-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Integrable-metrics-and-effectivity-with-controlled-boundary-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Integrable-metrics-and-effectivity-with-controlled-boundary-September-26-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Integrable-metrics-and-effectivity-with-controlled-boundary-September-26-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Integrable-metrics-and-effectivity-with-controlled-boundary-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Integrable-metrics-and-effectivity-with-controlled-boundary-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/approximation.tex","preprints/Integrable-metrics-and-effectivity-with-controlled-boundary-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/effectivity.tex","preprints/Integrable-metrics-and-effectivity-with-controlled-boundary-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/correction-ring.tex","preprints/Integrable-metrics-and-effectivity-with-controlled-boundary-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/conversion.tex","preprints/Integrable-metrics-and-effectivity-with-controlled-boundary-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/smooth-coarea.tex","preprints/Integrable-metrics-and-effectivity-with-controlled-boundary-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/jet-bigness.tex","preprints/Integrable-metrics-and-effectivity-with-controlled-boundary-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/fano-auxiliary.tex","preprints/Integrable-metrics-and-effectivity-with-controlled-boundary-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/invariant-applications.tex","preprints/Integrable-metrics-and-effectivity-with-controlled-boundary-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/extremal-tensors.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Integrable-metrics-and-effectivity-with-controlled-boundary-September-26-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let $Y$ be smooth projective and let $C$ be an effective integral divisor. If $-K_Y+C$ admits a metric with a global strict curvature lower bound for which the canonical section of $C$ is locally square integrable, every pseudoeffective rational divisor becomes rationally effective after an effective correction supported on $C$. For a smooth projective $X$ with smoothly semipositive $L=-K_X$, sections of $m_jL-P$ at unbounded positive exponents, with any fixed pseudoeffective Cartier error $P$, yield a section of a positive multiple of $L$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":623},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The boundary construction and its antecedents","sourceOffset":2838},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Anticanonical nonvanishing and fixed-error conversion","sourceOffset":5946},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Volume metrics and the distinct geometric refinements","sourceOffset":8666},{"kind":"section","title":"From an integrable section to a rational boundary","sourceOffset":14173},{"kind":"section","title":"Effectivity with a prescribed correction support","sourceOffset":22119},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Preserving integrability under a pseudoeffective perturbation","sourceOffset":26732},{"kind":"section","title":"Unweighted integrability and nearby adjoint rings","sourceOffset":29041},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Direct selection of an integrable divisor","sourceOffset":34507},{"kind":"section","title":"Removing a fixed pseudoeffective error","sourceOffset":38521},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Vertical minima and return of sections","sourceOffset":39722},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Iitaka base and its strict metric","sourceOffset":43003}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Effectivity modulo a controlled boundary","label":"thm:controlled-boundary","text":"Let $Y$ be a smooth projective complex variety of positive dimension and let $C\\ge0$ be an integral divisor. Suppose $-K_Y+C$ has a singular Hermitian metric $h$ whose curvature dominates a K\\\"ahler form and for which \\[ \\mathcal J(h)\\supset\\mathcal O_Y(-C). \\] For every pseudoeffective rational divisor $M$ on $Y$, there is an effective rational divisor $C_0$ supported on $C$ such that $M+C_0$ is rationally effective.","sourceOffset":1923,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Weighted anticanonical approximation","label":"thm:weighted-approximation","text":"Let $Y$ be smooth projective and let $J\\ge0$ be integral. Suppose $-K_Y+J$ has a singular metric with a global strict curvature lower bound and multiplier ideal containing $\\mathcal O_Y(-J)$. Choose a sufficiently small ample rational divisor $A$. For a sufficiently large divisible integer $r$, resolve the complete system $|r(-K_Y+J-A)|$ and $J$ by $\\sigma:Y'\\to Y$, and write \\[ \\sigma^*|r(-K_Y+J-A)|=G+|\\mathrm{Mov}|. \\] Then $\\Psi=G/r-\\sigma^*J-K_{Y'/Y}$ has coefficients less than one, $\\Psi^+$ is an effective klt boundary, and $D_0=\\Psi^-$ is effective and supported on $\\sigma^*J$ and exceptional primes. Moreover \\begin{equation} D_0\\sim_{\\mathbb Q} K_{Y'}+\\Psi^++\\mathrm{Mov}/r+\\sigma^*A. \\end{equation} The rational class $\\mathrm{Mov}/r+\\sigma^*A$ is nef and big. If an algebraic torus acts on $Y$, $J$ is invariant, and the indicated full systems are torus-stable, the resolution may be chosen equivariantly. Then $G$, $K_{Y'/Y}$, $\\Psi^\\pm$ and $D_0$ are invariant, and the free system $|\\mathrm{Mov}|$ is torus-stable. Identity \\eqref{eq:weighted-adjoint-identity} is an identity of ordinary rational line bundles; it does not assert that a chosen member of either the free system or the ample class is invariant.","sourceOffset":19658,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":170978,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Integrable-metrics-and-effectivity-with-controlled-boundary-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"068-07","slug":"Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026","familyId":"068","title":"Exact orders and invariant anticanonical linear systems","abstract":"On a smooth connected projective complex variety with smoothly semipositive anticanonical bundle, we prove that asymptotically zero valuation on the curvature-null face is attained by an effective rational anticanonical divisor. The fixed auxiliary line bundle is arbitrary.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"If X is a smooth connected complex projective variety and $-K_X$ admits a smooth Hermitian metric with nonnegative curvature, then $H^0(X,-mK_X)\\ne0$ for some m > 0. Thus smooth semipositivity forces a nonzero section of a positive tensor power of the anticanonical bundle in every dimension.","pdfPath":"preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026/build/source/glyph-unicode.tex","preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/endpoint.tex","preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/null-field.tex","preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/exact-proof.tex","preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/arc-corollary.tex","preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/order-weight.tex","preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/exact-weights.tex","preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/nef-tools.tex","preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/approximate-arc.tex","preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/approximate-weights.tex","preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/invariant-field.tex","preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/controlled-cone.tex","preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/isometric-descent.tex","preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026/build/source/sections/mrc-descent.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"On a smooth connected projective complex variety with smoothly semipositive anticanonical bundle, we prove that asymptotically zero valuation on the curvature-null face is attained by an effective rational anticanonical divisor. The fixed auxiliary line bundle is arbitrary.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":351},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why finite generation attains the limit","sourceOffset":4186},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From orders to invariant sections","sourceOffset":7292},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Independent methods and their additional outputs","sourceOffset":10938},{"kind":"section","title":"Selecting a generator on the boundary ray","sourceOffset":14640},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removing a birational correction","sourceOffset":18068},{"kind":"section","title":"The field selected by the curvature intersection","sourceOffset":24809},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An equidimensional model without a field extension","sourceOffset":28695},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The horizontal curvature patch","sourceOffset":30886},{"kind":"section","title":"Proof of exact attainment","sourceOffset":34062},{"kind":"section","title":"Exact zero order along a formal arc","sourceOffset":36971},{"kind":"section","title":"Orbit orders and characters","sourceOffset":40613}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"V:invariant-main","text":"Let $X$ be smooth connected projective over $\\C$, with $-K_X$ smoothly semipositive and $H^i(X,\\OO_X)=0$ for every $i>0$. For every algebraic torus $T$ acting on $X$, some positive anticanonical multiple has a nonzero $T$-invariant section for its natural linearization.","sourceOffset":8137,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Exact attainment of zero order","label":"V:exact","text":"With $X,L,\\alpha,\\nu,G_0,\\ell$ as above, let $v$ be either a discrete valuation of $\\C(X)$ centered on $X$ or the zero valuation. Let $M$ be any fixed line bundle. Suppose that effective integral divisors $N_j$ and positive integers $m_j\\to\\infty$ satisfy \\[ N_j\\sim m_jL+M,\\qquad \\ell(N_j)=0,\\qquad \\frac{v(N_j)}{m_j}\\longrightarrow0. \\] There is an effective rational divisor $N\\sim_{\\Q}L$ such that $v(N)=0$.","sourceOffset":2741,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":150003,"sourceFileCount":16,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Exact-orders-and-invariant-anticanonical-linear-systems-September-26-2026/build/source/glyph-unicode.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"069-01","slug":"Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026","familyId":"069","title":"Global quantum geometric Langlands at irrational level","abstract":"We prove the unramified quantum geometric Langlands equivalence for every connected simple complex algebraic group G and every level $c\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus\\mathbb Q$, including non-real levels. For every smooth projective connected complex curve X, it identifies the full twisted D-module categories on $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Bun}}\\nolimits _G(X)$ and $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Bun}}\\nolimits _{G^\\vee}(X)$ at the dual shifted levels c and $-1/(rc)$, where r is the lacing number. The equivalence uses the given global forms and includes all connected components.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the unramified de Rham quantum geometric Langlands equivalence for every connected simple complex algebraic group on every smooth projective connected complex curve, at every shifted level $c\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus\\mathbb Q$. It identifies the full derived categories of twisted D-modules for the group and its Langlands dual, retaining all global forms and connected components.","pdfPath":"preprints/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026/quantum-langlands.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026/quantum-langlands.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026","subjectId":"3","subject":"Algebraic and complex geometry","subjectZh":"代數與複幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026/build/sections/02-foundations.tex","preprints/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026/build/sections/03-pseudoidentity.tex","preprints/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026/build/sections/04-whittaker.tex","preprints/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026/build/sections/05-deformation.tex","preprints/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026/build/sections/06-plancherel.tex","preprints/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026/build/sections/07-loop-categories.tex","preprints/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026/build/sections/08-w-algebras.tex","preprints/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026/build/sections/09-local-comparison.tex","preprints/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026/build/sections/10-localization.tex","preprints/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026/build/sections/11-unipotent-period.tex","preprints/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026/build/sections/12-ran-period.tex","preprints/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026/build/sections/13-equivalence.tex","preprints/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the unramified quantum geometric Langlands equivalence for every connected simple complex algebraic group $G$ and every level $c\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus\\mathbb Q$, including non-real levels. For every smooth projective connected complex curve $X$, it identifies the full twisted $D$-module categories on $\\operatorname{Bun}_G(X)$ and $\\operatorname{Bun}_{G^\\vee}(X)$ at the dual shifted levels $c$ and $-1/(rc)$, where $r$ is the lacing number. The equivalence uses the given global forms and includes all connected components.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":596},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and prior work","sourceOffset":2991},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two main comparisons","sourceOffset":6069},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and the global period comparison","sourceOffset":9800},{"kind":"section","title":"Twists, boundedness, and categorical duality","sourceOffset":11039},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Root data, determinant lines, and level conventions","sourceOffset":11543},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An irrational twist cuts off the unstable directions","sourceOffset":16241},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Operations with compatible twists","sourceOffset":23605},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compact generation and opposite-twist duality","sourceOffset":25447},{"kind":"section","title":"Twisted pseudo-identity","sourceOffset":36400},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Parabolic correspondences and their compactifications","sourceOffset":38610},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Boundary smoothness and cleanness","sourceOffset":44055}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $c\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus\\mathbb Q$ there is an equivalence of presentable $\\mathbb C$-linear DG categories \\begin{equation} D_c(\\operatorname{Bun}_G(X)) \\simeq D_{-1/(rc)}(\\operatorname{Bun}_{G^\\vee}(X)). \\end{equation} The construction uses the given global forms and all connected components. It is characterized by the localization--Whittaker comparison of Section \\ref{sec:equivalence}, including its compatibility with families of marked points and their collisions. It also intertwines central torsor actions with the corresponding transgression local systems.","sourceOffset":1847,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Bounded support in the categorical sense","label":"foundations:cutoff","text":"For $s\\notin\\mathbb Q$, the category $D_{Y_H}(L(V)^s)$ vanishes on every component of nontorsion degree. There is a quasi-compact open $j:U\\hookrightarrow Y_H$, contained in the union of the torsion-degree components, such that \\begin{equation} j^!:D_{Y_H}(L(V)^s)\\xrightarrow{\\ \\sim\\ }D_U(L(V)^s) \\end{equation} with inverse $j_*$. The same open works for $-s$, and the equivalence persists after taking a product with any spectator stack and any external twist there.","sourceOffset":18258,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":460351,"sourceFileCount":16,"statementCount":41,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Global-quantum-geometric-Langlands-at-irrational-level-October-4-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"071-01","slug":"Removable-Boundaries-and-Rigidity-of-Circle-Domains-September-23-2026","familyId":"071","title":"Removable Boundaries and Rigidity of Circle Domains","abstract":"We prove that a circle domain with conformally removable boundary is conformally rigid, with no restriction on the number of complementary components. This establishes the removability-to-rigidity direction of the He–Schramm Conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the existence part of Koebe's circle-domain conjecture: every domain in the Riemann sphere is conformally equivalent to a domain whose complementary components are round disks or points. It also proves that circle domains with conformally removable boundary are rigid, meaning every conformal equivalence to another circle domain is Möbius, establishing this direction of the He–Schramm conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/Removable-Boundaries-and-Rigidity-of-Circle-Domains-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Removable-Boundaries-and-Rigidity-of-Circle-Domains-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Removable-Boundaries-and-Rigidity-of-Circle-Domains-September-23-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/Removable-Boundaries-and-Rigidity-of-Circle-Domains-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Removable-Boundaries-and-Rigidity-of-Circle-Domains-September-23-2026/build/companion-references.tex","preprints/Removable-Boundaries-and-Rigidity-of-Circle-Domains-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Removable-Boundaries-and-Rigidity-of-Circle-Domains-September-23-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Removable-Boundaries-and-Rigidity-of-Circle-Domains-September-23-2026/build/sections/models.tex","preprints/Removable-Boundaries-and-Rigidity-of-Circle-Domains-September-23-2026/build/sections/conductivity.tex","preprints/Removable-Boundaries-and-Rigidity-of-Circle-Domains-September-23-2026/build/sections/sobolev.tex","preprints/Removable-Boundaries-and-Rigidity-of-Circle-Domains-September-23-2026/build/figures/line-payment.tex","preprints/Removable-Boundaries-and-Rigidity-of-Circle-Domains-September-23-2026/build/sections/traces.tex","preprints/Removable-Boundaries-and-Rigidity-of-Circle-Domains-September-23-2026/build/sections/rigidity.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Removable-Boundaries-and-Rigidity-of-Circle-Domains-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that a circle domain with conformally removable boundary is conformally rigid, with no restriction on the number of complementary components. This establishes the removability-to-rigidity direction of the He--Schramm Conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":283},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rigidity and removability","sourceOffset":1924},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its quantitative input","sourceOffset":4470},{"kind":"section","title":"Contours, ends, and normalization","sourceOffset":8591},{"kind":"section","title":"Models retaining finitely many scalar tests","sourceOffset":16908},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The precise retained-test input","sourceOffset":18810},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Filling holes and identifying the limiting tests","sourceOffset":22092},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact traces under smoothing","sourceOffset":27683},{"kind":"section","title":"Almost straight coordinates and null trace pairs","sourceOffset":30001},{"kind":"section","title":"Sobolev extension across removable dust","sourceOffset":43090},{"kind":"section","title":"Null projections of the common point traces","sourceOffset":61028},{"kind":"section","title":"The length--area argument","sourceOffset":76073}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Every circle domain with conformally removable boundary is conformally rigid. More explicitly, if $\\Omega,\\Omega'\\subset\\Chat$ are circle domains, $\\partial\\Omega$ is conformally removable, and $f\\colon\\Omega\\to\\Omega'$ is a conformal equivalence, then there is a M\\\"obius transformation $M$ such that $f=M|_\\Omega$.","sourceOffset":1361,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:models","text":"Every finite list in Definition \\ref{def:model} satisfying \\eqref{eq:pair-null} has a model. A model can, in fact, be chosen with circle-domain image.","sourceOffset":18370,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:model","text":"Let $u_1,\\ldots,u_m$ be bounded continuous real functions on $\\Chat$ whose restrictions to $G$ belong to $W^{1,2}(G)$. A model for this list is a univalent conformal map \\[ h(z)=z+\\frac{b(h)}z+O(z^{-2})\\qquad(z\\to\\infty) \\] such that its nonpoint complementary components form a finite or countable null family of closed Jordan disks, and \\[ U_i=u_i\\circ\\pi_h\\in W^{1,2}(\\Chat),\\qquad \\nabla U_i=0\\quad\\text{a.e. on }\\Chat\\setminus h(G). \\] A family is null if only finitely many members have diameter greater than any prescribed positive number.","sourceOffset":17287,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/071.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves the removability-to-rigidity direction of the He–Schramm conjecture. If a circle domain has conformally removable boundary, then every conformal equivalence from it to another circle domain agrees on the source with a Möbius transformation. There is no bound on the number of complementary components.\n\nThe same Comparator file also states Koebe's circle-domain theorem, which gives a circle-domain representative for every domain in the Riemann sphere.\n\nKoebe's circle-domain conjecture asks whether every domain in the Riemann sphere is conformally equivalent to a circle domain, whose complementary components are round disks or points. The formalization proves this for every domain. It also proves rigidity under a separate hypothesis: a conformal equivalence between ci","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/071.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":85409,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Removable-Boundaries-and-Rigidity-of-Circle-Domains-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"071-02","slug":"Koebes-Circle-Domain-Conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"071","title":"Koebe's Circle-Domain Conjecture","abstract":"We prove that every domain in the Riemann sphere is conformally equivalent to a circle domain, resolving Koebe's circle-domain conjecture positively.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the existence part of Koebe's circle-domain conjecture: every domain in the Riemann sphere is conformally equivalent to a domain whose complementary components are round disks or points. It also proves that circle domains with conformally removable boundary are rigid, meaning every conformal equivalence to another circle domain is Möbius, establishing this direction of the He–Schramm conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/Koebes-Circle-Domain-Conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Koebes-Circle-Domain-Conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Koebes-Circle-Domain-Conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/Koebes-Circle-Domain-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Koebes-Circle-Domain-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Koebes-Circle-Domain-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/protrusion.tex","preprints/Koebes-Circle-Domain-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-topology.tex","preprints/Koebes-Circle-Domain-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-transfer.tex","preprints/Koebes-Circle-Domain-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-barriers.tex","preprints/Koebes-Circle-Domain-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-laws.tex","preprints/Koebes-Circle-Domain-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-selection.tex","preprints/Koebes-Circle-Domain-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/crossing.tex","preprints/Koebes-Circle-Domain-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-streams.tex","preprints/Koebes-Circle-Domain-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-hyperbolic-surfaces.tex","preprints/Koebes-Circle-Domain-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Koebes-Circle-Domain-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every domain in the Riemann sphere is conformally equivalent to a circle domain, resolving Koebe's circle-domain conjecture positively.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":254},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From finite uniformization to boundary control","sourceOffset":3196},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":6849},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and classical inputs","sourceOffset":10900},{"kind":"section","title":"The quotient sphere and compatible coordinates","sourceOffset":11643},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Collapsing complementary components","sourceOffset":12646},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coordinates, bands, and compatibility","sourceOffset":22565},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The geometry of generic level unions","sourceOffset":27644},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite transfer and normalized limits","sourceOffset":34958},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The transfer theorem","sourceOffset":35784},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Circle-domain completions and compactness","sourceOffset":53512},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Identification and trapping of the ends","sourceOffset":58910}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Circle-domain uniformization","label":"thm:main","text":"Every nonempty connected open set $G\\subset\\Chat$ is conformally equivalent to a circle domain.","sourceOffset":623,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Finite transfer","label":"thm:transfer","text":"Let $u_1,\\ldots,u_m$ be a finite ordered list satisfying Definition \\ref{def:coordinates} and compatibility (P), with a common countable flag set $\\Sigma$. The same assertion holds for a finite ordered sublist of a countable compatible list. Prescribe a finite set $F\\subset B$ containing all singular points of the list, a compact core $K_0\\subset G$, a dust-disk cover from the fixed exhaustion, and $\\eps>0$. There exist a smooth compact core $K\\subset G$, finitely many terminal disks $T_s$ with pairwise disjoint closures, indexed by a finite set $S\\subset B$ containing $F$, and a smooth metric $g$ on the physical sphere, with the following properties. Every terminal disk lies in a member of the prescribed cover, $s\\in T_s$, and $K$ contains $K_0$ and all mask boundaries in its interior. The metric $g$ agrees with the spherical metric on a neighborhood of $K$. Write \\[ Q=\\Chat\\setminus\\bigcup_{s\\in S}T_s, \\qquad Q^\\circ=\\Chat\\setminus\\bigcup_{s\\in S}\\overline{T_s}. \\] Then $K\\subset Q^\\circ$, and $Q$, equipped with $g$, is a smooth bordered surface of genus zero. Each $u_i$ has a transferred coordinate $u_i^*$ on $Q\\cap M_i$, smooth up to its boundary in local real branches, with the following properties. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item $u_i^*=u_i$ on a neighborhood of $K\\cap M_i$; \\item The total energy outside $K$ satisfies \\begin{equation} \\sum_{i=1}^m \\|\\dd u_i^*\\|_{2,(Q\\setminus K)\\cap M_i,g}^2<\\eps^2; \\end{equation} \\item For each nonsingular terminal i","sourceOffset":36283,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Coordinates","label":"def:coordinates","text":"A coordinate $u_i$ comes with a closed set $M_i\\subset X$, called its mask, and has one of the following forms. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item A bounded smooth real function on $G$, with mask $M_i=X$. \\item A coordinate with additive period one, with mask $M_i=\\overline U$ for a dust disk $U$ and singular point $p_i\\in B\\cap U$. On the cyclic universal cover of $\\overline U\\setminus\\{p_i\\}$, it is a smooth real function over $G\\cap M_i$, smooth up to the boundary curve, satisfying \\[ u_i(Tx)=u_i(x)+1 \\] for the positive deck transformation $T$. \\end{enumerate} In both cases ","sourceOffset":23141,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Compatibility","label":"def:compatibility","text":"A well-ordered list with its flag set satisfies \\textup{(P)} if, for each label $j$, each nonempty finite list of lower labels $i_1,\\ldots,i_d<j$ with repetitions permitted, and almost every $(t,t_1,\\ldots,t_d)\\in\\T^{d+1}$, no free point of $H_j(t)$ belongs to a nondegenerate component of \\[ H_{i_1}(t_1)\\cup\\cdots\\cup H_{i_d}(t_d). \\tag{P} \\]","sourceOffset":26052,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/071.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves the removability-to-rigidity direction of the He–Schramm conjecture. If a circle domain has conformally removable boundary, then every conformal equivalence from it to another circle domain agrees on the source with a Möbius transformation. There is no bound on the number of complementary components.\n\nThe same Comparator file also states Koebe's circle-domain theorem, which gives a circle-domain representative for every domain in the Riemann sphere.\n\nKoebe's circle-domain conjecture asks whether every domain in the Riemann sphere is conformally equivalent to a circle domain, whose complementary components are round disks or points. The formalization proves this for every domain. It also proves rigidity under a separate hypothesis: a conformal equivalence between ci","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/071.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":225785,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":4,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Koebes-Circle-Domain-Conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"072-01","slug":"Brennans-conjecture-and-sharp-inverse-square-integral-means-September-24-2026","familyId":"072","title":"Brennan's conjecture and sharp inverse-square integral means","abstract":"We prove Brennan's conjecture: if a simply connected plane domain admits a conformal bijection φ onto the unit disk, then $|\\varphi'|^s$ is area-integrable for every $4/3\\lt s\\lt 4$. We also prove the sharp inverse-square integral-means exponent $B_{\\mathcal S}(-2)=1$ for the normalized schlicht class $\\mathcal S$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Brennan's conjecture: for every conformal bijection ϕ from a simply connected plane domain onto the disk, $|\\phi'|^s$ is area-integrable for $4/3\\lt s\\lt 4$. The sharp universal integral-means identity is $B_{\\mathcal S}(t)=|t|-1$ for t ≤ −2. A strict bound $B_b(-1)\\lt 1/4$ for bounded univalent functions disproves Kraetzer's prediction at that parameter.","pdfPath":"preprints/Brennans-conjecture-and-sharp-inverse-square-integral-means-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Brennans-conjecture-and-sharp-inverse-square-integral-means-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Brennans-conjecture-and-sharp-inverse-square-integral-means-September-24-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/Brennans-conjecture-and-sharp-inverse-square-integral-means-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Brennans-conjecture-and-sharp-inverse-square-integral-means-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Brennans-conjecture-and-sharp-inverse-square-integral-means-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-history.tex","preprints/Brennans-conjecture-and-sharp-inverse-square-integral-means-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Brennans-conjecture-and-sharp-inverse-square-integral-means-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-weighted-law.tex","preprints/Brennans-conjecture-and-sharp-inverse-square-integral-means-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-escape.tex","preprints/Brennans-conjecture-and-sharp-inverse-square-integral-means-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-critical-pairing.tex","preprints/Brennans-conjecture-and-sharp-inverse-square-integral-means-September-24-2026/build/figures/rank-pairing.tex","preprints/Brennans-conjecture-and-sharp-inverse-square-integral-means-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-transport.tex","preprints/Brennans-conjecture-and-sharp-inverse-square-integral-means-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-integrability.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Brennans-conjecture-and-sharp-inverse-square-integral-means-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Brennan's conjecture: if a simply connected plane domain admits a conformal bijection $\\varphi$ onto the unit disk, then $|\\varphi'|^s$ is area-integrable for every $4/3<s<4$. We also prove the sharp inverse-square integral-means exponent $B_{\\SSS}(-2)=1$ for the normalized schlicht class $\\SSS$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":369},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The problem and its development","sourceOffset":3879},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Analytic and geometric ingredients","sourceOffset":7320},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The obstacle and the proof strategy","sourceOffset":9761},{"kind":"section","title":"Univalent maps and a dyadic transfer operator","sourceOffset":13988},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Classical estimates and the normalized compact class","sourceOffset":14449},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Affine rescaling and dyadic growth","sourceOffset":18668},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From dyadic samples to disk integral means","sourceOffset":22490},{"kind":"section","title":"A probability law from transfer growth","sourceOffset":25989},{"kind":"section","title":"Escape estimates and compact superlevels","sourceOffset":35898},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A weighted area integral","sourceOffset":37373},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Escape and horizontal preimage length","sourceOffset":39446}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Brennan's conjecture and inverse-square means","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $\\varepsilon>0$ there is $C_\\varepsilon<\\infty$ such that \\begin{equation} M_{-2}[f'](r)\\le C_\\varepsilon(1-r)^{-1-\\varepsilon} \\qquad\\left(f\\in\\SSS,\\ \\frac12\\le r<1\\right). \\end{equation} Moreover, $B_{\\SSS}(-2)=1$. Let $W\\subset\\CC$ be any simply connected domain whose boundary in the Riemann sphere contains at least two points. Every conformal bijection $\\varphi:W\\to\\DD$ satisfies \\[ \\int_W|\\varphi'(z)|^s\\dd A(z)<\\infty \\qquad\\left(\\frac43<s<4\\right). \\] Equivalently, every univalent map $f:\\DD\\to\\CC$ satisfies \\[ \\int_{\\DD}|f'(w)|^t\\dd A(w)<\\infty \\qquad\\left(-2<t<\\frac23\\right). \\]","sourceOffset":2428,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Sharpness of the open range","label":"cor:sharp-endpoints","text":"The Koebe function $f(z)=z/(1-z)^2$ belongs to $\\SSS$ and satisfies \\[ \\int_{\\DD}|f'|^{-2}\\dd A=\\infty, \\qquad \\int_{\\DD}|f'|^{2/3}\\dd A=\\infty. \\] Its inverse therefore fails area integrability at both $s=4$ and $s=4/3$.","sourceOffset":3562,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:good-pair","text":"A pair $(F,\\xi)\\in\\KK\\times\\CC$ is good if every closed positive superlevel of $V^F_\\xi$ is compact in $\\HH$, and $\\xi$ is a regular value of $G_F$. Thus every solution $G_F(z)=\\xi$ must satisfy $J_F(z)\\ne0$; a target with no such solution is regular. Write $\\mathcal G$ for the set of good pairs.","sourceOffset":51666,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/072.md","scopeText":"Brennan's conjecture asserts that $|\\phi'|^s$ is area-integrable for $4/3<s<4$ when $\\phi$ conformally maps a simply connected plane domain with nontrivial spherical boundary onto the disk. The formalization establishes this interval, along with area integrability of $|f'|^t$ for univalent disk maps when $-2<t<2/3$.\n\nIt also gives the uniform inverse-square radial-mean bound with exponent $-1-\\varepsilon$ for every $\\varepsilon>0$ and the spectrum value $B_{\\mathcal S}(-2)=1$. The Koebe map and its inverse give divergence at all four boundary exponents.\n\nKraetzer's proposed integral-means spectrum predicts $B_b(-1)=1/4$ for bounded univalent maps. The formalized result proves $B_b(-1)<1/4$, contradicting that prediction.\n\nThe underlying estimate gives constants $0<\\varepsilon<1/4$ and $C<\\","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/072.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":89806,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Brennans-conjecture-and-sharp-inverse-square-integral-means-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Brennans-conjecture-and-sharp-inverse-square-integral-means-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Brennans-conjecture-and-sharp-inverse-square-integral-means-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"072-02","slug":"A-strict-inverse-first-power-bound-for-univalent-functions-September-24-2026","familyId":"072","title":"A strict inverse-first-power bound for univalent functions","abstract":"We prove a uniform upper bound for inverse-first-power integral means of normalized univalent disk maps with exponent strictly below 1/4. Consequently, the bounded universal integral-means spectrum satisfies $B_b(-1)\\lt 1/4$, disproving Kraetzer's conjectured spectrum at p = −1.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Brennan's conjecture: for every conformal bijection ϕ from a simply connected plane domain onto the disk, $|\\phi'|^s$ is area-integrable for $4/3\\lt s\\lt 4$. The sharp universal integral-means identity is $B_{\\mathcal S}(t)=|t|-1$ for t ≤ −2. A strict bound $B_b(-1)\\lt 1/4$ for bounded univalent functions disproves Kraetzer's prediction at that parameter.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-strict-inverse-first-power-bound-for-univalent-functions-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-strict-inverse-first-power-bound-for-univalent-functions-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-strict-inverse-first-power-bound-for-univalent-functions-September-24-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/A-strict-inverse-first-power-bound-for-univalent-functions-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-strict-inverse-first-power-bound-for-univalent-functions-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/A-strict-inverse-first-power-bound-for-univalent-functions-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-compact-model.tex","preprints/A-strict-inverse-first-power-bound-for-univalent-functions-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-weighted-law.tex","preprints/A-strict-inverse-first-power-bound-for-univalent-functions-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-escape.tex","preprints/A-strict-inverse-first-power-bound-for-univalent-functions-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-strict-pairing.tex","preprints/A-strict-inverse-first-power-bound-for-univalent-functions-September-24-2026/build/figures/rank-pairing.tex","preprints/A-strict-inverse-first-power-bound-for-univalent-functions-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-strict-transport.tex","preprints/A-strict-inverse-first-power-bound-for-univalent-functions-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-equality-barrier.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-strict-inverse-first-power-bound-for-univalent-functions-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a uniform upper bound for inverse-first-power integral means of normalized univalent disk maps with exponent strictly below \\(1/4\\). Consequently, the bounded universal integral-means spectrum satisfies \\(B_b(-1)<1/4\\), disproving Kraetzer's conjectured spectrum at \\(p=-1\\).","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":331},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The conjecture and the uniform bound","sourceOffset":1617},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and the role of strictness","sourceOffset":2839},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof outline","sourceOffset":5766},{"kind":"section","title":"A compact model and its growth operator","sourceOffset":7750},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Univalence estimates","sourceOffset":7952},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Affine rebasings and dyadic samples","sourceOffset":10347},{"kind":"section","title":"A weighted affine law","sourceOffset":13164},{"kind":"section","title":"Compact superlevels for almost every target","sourceOffset":17732},{"kind":"section","title":"Critical points and strict pairing","sourceOffset":24009},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The critical map","sourceOffset":24326},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Good targets","sourceOffset":25883}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform inverse-first-power bound","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist constants \\(0<\\varepsilon<1/4\\) and \\(C<\\infty\\) such that \\begin{equation} M_{-1}[f'](r)\\le C(1-r)^{-1/4+\\varepsilon} \\qquad\\text{for every }f\\in\\SSS\\text{ and every }1/2\\le r<1. \\end{equation} Consequently \\(B_b(-1)<1/4\\), and Kraetzer's conjectured identity \\eqref{eq:kraetzer} is false.","sourceOffset":2183,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"From dyadic samples to disk means","label":"prop:disk","text":"There is a universal constant \\(C_0\\) such that, for \\(f\\in\\SSS\\), \\[ M_{-1}[f'](r)\\le C_0\\|L^n\\| \\quad\\text{if}\\quad 1/2\\le r<1,\\quad v=2^{-n}\\le1-r<2v. \\] Consequently \\(B_b(-1)\\le\\beta\\).","sourceOffset":11891,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/072.md","scopeText":"Brennan's conjecture asserts that $|\\phi'|^s$ is area-integrable for $4/3<s<4$ when $\\phi$ conformally maps a simply connected plane domain with nontrivial spherical boundary onto the disk. The formalization establishes this interval, along with area integrability of $|f'|^t$ for univalent disk maps when $-2<t<2/3$.\n\nIt also gives the uniform inverse-square radial-mean bound with exponent $-1-\\varepsilon$ for every $\\varepsilon>0$ and the spectrum value $B_{\\mathcal S}(-2)=1$. The Koebe map and its inverse give divergence at all four boundary exponents.\n\nKraetzer's proposed integral-means spectrum predicts $B_b(-1)=1/4$ for bounded univalent maps. The formalized result proves $B_b(-1)<1/4$, contradicting that prediction.\n\nThe underlying estimate gives constants $0<\\varepsilon<1/4$ and $C<\\","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/072.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":43207,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-strict-inverse-first-power-bound-for-univalent-functions-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-strict-inverse-first-power-bound-for-univalent-functions-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/A-strict-inverse-first-power-bound-for-univalent-functions-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"073-01","slug":"The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026","familyId":"073","title":"The Falconer distance conjecture in all dimensions","abstract":"We resolve the Falconer distance conjecture in every dimension. For every integer d ≥ 2, a compact subset of ℝd with Hausdorff dimension greater than $d/2$ determines a set of Euclidean distances of positive Lebesgue measure.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the Falconer distance conjecture in every dimension d ≥ 2: every compact set $E\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ with Hausdorff dimension greater than $d/2$ determines a set of Euclidean distances of positive Lebesgue measure.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/glyphtounicode.tex","preprints/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/00_introduction.tex","preprints/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/01_conventions.tex","preprints/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/02_regularization.tex","preprints/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/03_angular_gain.tex","preprints/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/04_preparation.tex","preprints/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/05_profiles_tests.tex","preprints/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/06_oscillatory.tex","preprints/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/07_graph.tex","preprints/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/08_recursion.tex","preprints/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/09_ledger.tex","preprints/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/10_assembly.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We resolve the Falconer distance conjecture in every dimension. For every integer \\(d\\ge2\\), a compact subset of \\(\\R^d\\) with Hausdorff dimension greater than \\(d/2\\) determines a set of Euclidean distances of positive Lebesgue measure.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":351},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and scope","sourceOffset":1284},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Methodological antecedents","sourceOffset":4885},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two main issues","sourceOffset":7249},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From profiles to a distance measure","sourceOffset":9115},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and preliminary facts","sourceOffset":12924},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Measures, scales and directions","sourceOffset":12992},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite energy and projection","sourceOffset":14726},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Subpower losses and fixed gains","sourceOffset":17457},{"kind":"section","title":"Regularization and multiplication of fibre bounds","sourceOffset":20011},{"kind":"section","title":"An angular projection gain","sourceOffset":29533},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The gain and its two slicing regimes","sourceOffset":30562}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(d\\ge2\\) be an integer and let \\(E\\subset\\R^d\\) be compact. If \\(\\dim_H E>d/2\\), then \\[ \\cL^1(\\Delta(E))>0. \\]","sourceOffset":691,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Linear projection gain","label":"ang:linear-gain","text":"Fix $k\\ge2$ and \\[ k-1\\le q\\le k-\\tfrac12<t<k. \\] Fix a tube-width constant $C$. In the case $q=k-1$, also fix $\\zeta>0$. There exist $\\epsilon,c>0$ such that, for all sufficiently small $\\delta$, the following holds. Let $\\mu$ be a bounded point subprobability in $\\R^{k+1}$ and $\\nu$ a direction subprobability on $S^k$ with respective $t$- and $q$-ball bounds of loss $\\delta^{-\\epsilon}$ down to $\\delta$. At $q=k-1$ assume in addition \\begin{equation} \\nu\\{w:\\dist(w,H)\\le r\\}\\le\\delta^{-\\epsilon}r^\\zeta \\qquad(\\delta\\le r\\le1) \\end{equation} for every linear hyperplane $H$. Let $E\\subset\\R^{k+1}\\times S^k$ be a measurable incidence relation, and write $E_w=\\{x:(x,w)\\in E\\}$. If each direction fiber $E_w$ is covered by at most $\\delta^{-q-\\epsilon}$ tubes parallel to $w$ of width $C\\delta$, then \\[ (\\mu\\otimes\\nu)(E)\\le\\delta^c. \\] All constants are uniform over the measures and the incidence relations with these fixed parameters.","sourceOffset":30613,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"prep:availability","text":"Let $\\rho,\\nu$ be fixed separated probabilities in $\\R^{k+1}$, and let $G_N$ be Borel pair sets. An exponent $q$ is available for this family if, for every $e>0$, there are $c_e>0$, $C_e<\\infty$, and Borel $B_N\\subset G_N$, for all sufficiently large $N$, such that \\[ (\\nu\\otimes\\rho)(B_N)\\le C_e2^{-c_eN}, \\] and both directional measures of $G_N\\setminus B_N$ give every spherical cap of radius $u\\in[2^{-N},1]$ mass at most $2^{eN}u^q$. The estimates hold at every pin in the retained supports, after removal of null fibers if necessary.","sourceOffset":72552,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Good directions","label":"test:good-directions","text":"Fix a class, a descriptor $\\mathfrak t$ with anchor $p$, and an occupied depth-$p$ cube $P$, of centre $c_P$. Its level-$i$ good set $G_{i,\\mathfrak t}(P)\\subset S^{d-1}$ is defined as follows, with one sufficiently large dimensional constant $C_d$ for all tests. \\begin{enumerate} \\item For a parallel descriptor $[g,p]$, a direction $w$ is good if the number of occupied depth-$p$ cube centres in $B(c_P,10(d+1)2^{-g})$ whose distance to $c_P+\\R w$ is at most $W_i(p)$ is at most \\begin{equation} R^{k(g,p)+C_d\\sigma}. \\end{equation} \\item For a projection descriptor $[p,t]$, let $\\rho_P=\\rho|_P/\\","sourceOffset":115986,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/073.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves the Falconer distance conjecture in every dimension. For every integer $d\\ge2$ and compact set $E\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ with Hausdorff dimension greater than $d/2$, the set $\\{\\lVert x-y\\rVert:x,y\\in E\\}$ has positive Lebesgue measure. The linked statements include both this all-dimensional result and the earlier planar case.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/073.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":240396,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":7,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/The-Falconer-distance-conjecture-in-all-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"074-01","slug":"The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026","familyId":"074","title":"The Kakeya maximal conjecture in three dimensions","abstract":"We prove the Kakeya maximal conjecture in three dimensions. For every ε > 0, the maximal average over unit tubes of radius δ maps $L^3(\\mathbb R^3)$ to $L^3(S^2)$ with norm at most $C_\\varepsilon\\delta^{-\\varepsilon}$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the Kakeya maximal conjecture in three dimensions and the Hausdorff-dimension conjecture in four. In three dimensions, the radius-δ tube maximal operator maps $L^3(\\mathbb R^3)$ to $L^3(S^2)$ with norm $O_\\varepsilon(\\delta^{-\\varepsilon})$ for every ε > 0. In four dimensions, every set containing a unit segment in every direction has Hausdorff dimension four.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/10-critical.tex","preprints/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-inputs.tex","preprints/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/20-local.tex","preprints/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/30-stationary.tex","preprints/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/figures/angle-packing.tex","preprints/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/figures/canonical-profile.tex","preprints/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/40-estimates.tex","preprints/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/50-entropy.tex","preprints/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/60-projections.tex","preprints/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/figures/entropy-layers.tex","preprints/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/65-projection-proofs.tex","preprints/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/70-closure.tex","preprints/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/80-consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Kakeya maximal conjecture in three dimensions. For every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), the maximal average over unit tubes of radius \\(\\delta\\) maps \\(L^3(\\R^3)\\) to \\(L^3(S^2)\\) with norm at most \\(C_\\varepsilon\\delta^{-\\varepsilon}\\).","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":301},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The set problem and the maximal problem","sourceOffset":1296},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Geometry of the proof","sourceOffset":5818},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Consequences and organization","sourceOffset":11475},{"kind":"section","title":"Weighted configurations and critical exponents","sourceOffset":13323},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The indexed model and logarithmic comparisons","sourceOffset":13949},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The density bound and its deformation","sourceOffset":19123},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite cleaning and profile regularization","sourceOffset":25600},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Temporal cost under restriction and changes of scale","sourceOffset":39956},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform bounds for ensembles","sourceOffset":43014},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A differentiability point forced by failure","sourceOffset":44624},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Hybrid equalities and compactness","sourceOffset":49530}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) there exists a finite constant \\(C_\\varepsilon\\) such that, for every \\(0<\\delta<1\\) and every \\(f\\in L^3(\\R^3)\\), \\[ \\|K_\\delta f\\|_{L^3(S^2)} \\le C_\\varepsilon\\delta^{-\\varepsilon} \\|f\\|_{L^3(\\R^3)}. \\]","sourceOffset":743,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Active weight after cleaning","label":"cor:active-weight-cleaning","text":"Let $\\mathcal A$ be a finite collection of pairs $(i,I)$, where $I$ is a dyadic temporal block and the blocks assigned to each index $i$ are disjoint. Let $\\mathcal C$ be a partition of $\\mathcal A$. For a pair $(i,I)$, its events are $(i,J)$ with $J\\in S_i\\cap I$ and have inherited weight $\\omega_i$. Fix $a\\in\\R$, $\\varepsilon\\ge0$, and $0<\\vartheta_0,\\sigma_N\\le1$. Suppose each pair has between $N^{a-\\varepsilon}$ and $N^{a+\\varepsilon}$ events. For $C\\in\\mathcal C$ put \\[ M_C=\\sum_{(i,I)\\in C}\\omega_i. \\] If a refinement retains at least a fraction $\\vartheta_0$ of the event mass assigned to $C$, then its active weight in that cell satisfies \\[ \\sum_{\\substack{(i,I)\\in C\\\\S_i'\\cap I\\ne\\varnothing}}\\omega_i \\ge\\vartheta_0N^{-2\\varepsilon}M_C. \\] Suppose further that each cell's assigned events are partitioned into at most $L_N$ pieces, where $L_N\\ge1$ is an integer. Discard the pieces of mass less than $\\sigma_N/L_N$ times their original cell's event mass. This discards at most a fraction $\\sigma_N$ of the total event mass. If subsequent cleaning retains at least a fraction $\\vartheta_0$ of each surviving piece's original event mass, then the active weight on every occupied piece is at least \\[ \\frac{\\vartheta_0\\sigma_N}{L_N}N^{-2\\varepsilon}M_C. \\] Here active weight counts each pair $(i,I)$ meeting the piece once.","sourceOffset":36019,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Uniform temporal profile","label":"def:uniform-profile","text":"An admissible profile is a nondecreasing $1$-Lipschitz function $F:[0,1]\\to[0,1]$ with $F(0)=0$. A sequence of indexed configurations has uniform profile $F$ if, uniformly over every retained index and every occupied dyadic time block containing $K$ fine bins, \\begin{equation} |S_i\\cap I|=N^{\\kappa-F(\\kappa)+o(1)}, \\qquad \\kappa=\\log_N K. \\end{equation} The uniform error also applies over the scale range $0\\le\\kappa\\le1$. In this case $k\\eqexp N^{1-\\alpha}$, where $\\alpha=F(1)$.","sourceOffset":17103,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Critical exponent","label":"def:critical","text":"For fixed $p,z,\\eta$, let $h(p,z)$ be the least $A\\in[0,d-1]$ such that for every sequence with a uniform profile $F$, \\begin{equation} m\\leexp N^{A+\\Pi_F(1)}\\Delta_A. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":22199,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":348858,"sourceFileCount":16,"statementCount":17,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Kakeya-maximal-conjecture-in-three-dimensions-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"074-02","slug":"Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026","familyId":"074","title":"Every four-dimensional Kakeya set has full Hausdorff dimension","abstract":"We prove the four-dimensional Hausdorff-dimension Kakeya conjecture: every subset of ℝ4 containing a unit line segment in every direction has Hausdorff dimension four. No compactness or regularity assumption is imposed on the set or its witnessing line family.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the Kakeya maximal conjecture in three dimensions and the Hausdorff-dimension conjecture in four. In three dimensions, the radius-δ tube maximal operator maps $L^3(\\mathbb R^3)$ to $L^3(S^2)$ with norm $O_\\varepsilon(\\delta^{-\\varepsilon})$ for every ε > 0. In four dimensions, every set containing a unit segment in every direction has Hausdorff dimension four.","pdfPath":"preprints/Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-map.tex","preprints/Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-framework.tex","preprints/Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-comparison.tex","preprints/Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-critical-paths.tex","preprints/Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-scalar-tools.tex","preprints/Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-isotropic.tex","preprints/Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/06a-retained-edge.tex","preprints/Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-finite-narrowness.tex","preprints/Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-critical-rates.tex","preprints/Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-unbounded-narrowness.tex","preprints/Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the four-dimensional Hausdorff-dimension Kakeya conjecture: every subset of \\(\\R^4\\) containing a unit line segment in every direction has Hausdorff dimension four. No compactness or regularity assumption is imposed on the set or its witnessing line family.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":329},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and input estimates","sourceOffset":2182},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Mechanisms of the proof","sourceOffset":7809},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and scale conventions","sourceOffset":17141},{"kind":"section","title":"Weighted models and the reduction from arbitrary Kakeya sets","sourceOffset":18704},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact problems and observations","sourceOffset":19747},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Density selection and homogeneous profiles","sourceOffset":25302},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Time charts and prior-mass budgets","sourceOffset":36541},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Normalization, composition, and horizon","sourceOffset":47799},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The weighted plank input and the initial horizon bound","sourceOffset":55613},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From an arbitrary Kakeya counterexample to positive horizon","sourceOffset":66450},{"kind":"section","title":"Comparison of algebraic sheets and extension of terminal time","sourceOffset":84147}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(K\\subset\\R^4\\). Suppose that for every \\(e\\in S^3\\) there is a point \\(a_e\\in\\R^4\\) such that \\[ \\{a_e+te:0\\leq t\\leq1\\}\\subset K. \\] Then \\(\\dim_H K=4\\).","sourceOffset":607,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Initial horizon bound","label":"a01:horizon-bound","text":"Every homogeneous exact problem satisfies \\(H(E)\\le L\\).","sourceOffset":59133,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Exact problems","label":"def:exact-problem","text":"An exact problem is a sequence indexed by a real parameter \\(N_0\\to\\infty\\), with a fixed length \\(0<L<\\infty\\). For \\(0\\le r\\le L\\), write \\(a_r=N_0^{-r}\\). A subpower factor \\(K_0\\ge1\\) satisfies \\(\\log K_0/\\log N_0\\to0\\); its value may be increased from one occurrence to the next. We write \\(A\\sim_{\\log}B\\) when \\(K_0^{-1}B\\le A\\le K_0B\\) for such a factor. Subsequences are permitted. There are finitely many auxiliary worlds \\(\\gamma\\), of weights \\(\\omega_\\gamma\\ge0\\) satisfying \\(\\sum_\\gamma\\omega_\\gamma=1\\). In world \\(\\gamma\\), a trajectory has prior probability law \\(\\nu_\\gamma\\), inde","sourceOffset":20071,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Tempered data","label":"a01:tempered","text":"The numbers of worlds, specified parameter ranges and coefficients, and \\(B,B^{-1}\\), are polynomially bounded. In the full trajectory coefficient coordinates, each prior has piecewise constant Lebesgue density, polynomially bounded on polynomially many semialgebraic pieces. Trajectory and incidence predicates use polynomially many polynomial conditions of bounded individual degree, with arbitrary Boolean combinations. The number of variables in each geometric test is bounded. Real constants in these conditions have no height restriction. Exact-level selections, labels, cells, and frames obey ","sourceOffset":22617,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":642230,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":31,"definitionCount":9,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Every-four-dimensional-Kakeya-set-has-full-Hausdorff-dimension-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"075-01","slug":"Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026","familyId":"075","title":"Almost-everywhere Fourier convergence in L log L","abstract":"We prove that the ordinary symmetric Fourier partial sums of every complex-valued function in $L\\log L$ on the circle converge almost everywhere to the function along the full sequence. This establishes the classical $L\\log L$ sufficiency conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the ordinary symmetric Fourier partial sums of every complex-valued function in $L\\log L(\\mathbb T)$ converge almost everywhere along the full sequence. This resolves the classical sufficiency conjecture at the $L\\log L$ scale.","pdfPath":"preprints/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-entropy-foundations.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-entropy-refinement.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026/build/figures/entropy-skip.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-thermal.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-procedure.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-capture.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026/build/figures/snapshot-timing.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-paths.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026/build/sections/09-assembly.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026/build/sections/11-fourier.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the ordinary symmetric Fourier partial sums of every complex-valued function in $L\\log L$ on the circle converge almost everywhere to the function along the full sequence. This establishes the classical $L\\log L$ sufficiency conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":313},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The estimate behind the convergence theorem","sourceOffset":2951},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The structural argument","sourceOffset":5402},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":9796},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and probability notation","sourceOffset":10415},{"kind":"section","title":"Entropy foundations for compression","sourceOffset":13851},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A variational construction of correlated letters","sourceOffset":17105},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rules for keeping the same error","sourceOffset":22228},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An exchangeable law with nearly independent short blocks","sourceOffset":26119},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Contexts and bounded exact-sum costs","sourceOffset":30520},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Noisy block costs and their full-vector interpretation","sourceOffset":38689},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Thinning correlated blocks to exact iid marginals","sourceOffset":42556}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Almost-everywhere convergence in $L\\log L$","label":"thm:main","text":"For every complex-valued $f\\in L\\log L(\\mathbb T)$, there is a measurable set $N_f$ of measure zero such that \\[ \\lim_{N\\to\\infty}S_Nf(x)=f(x) \\qquad (x\\in\\mathbb T\\setminus N_f). \\] The limit is along the full sequence $N=0,1,2,\\ldots$.","sourceOffset":775,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Finite refinement search","label":"entropy-refinement:search","text":"Under the hypotheses of \\Cref{lemma:compression}, there is a finite sequence of context refinements from empty context to $W$, and an index $j$, such that \\begin{equation} \\begin{split} G_1(\\varnothing)-G_1(W)&\\le\\frac{CD_*^2}{Eh^2},\\\\ K_{m_{j,1}}(W;r_j)&\\le K_{m_{j,0}}(W;r_j)+E. \\end{split} \\end{equation} The constant $C$ is numerical, and every context produced is finite-valued.","sourceOffset":55207,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Thermal forms","label":"eq:thermal-definitions","text":"A thermal form on the unit circle is a finite sum \\[ A(y)=\\sum_s\\lambda_s(1-\\operatorname{Re}\\chi_s(y)), \\qquad \\lambda_s\\ge0,\\quad s\\in\\mathbb Z. \\] We write $A\\preceq A'$ if $A'=A+D$ for a thermal form $D$. Define \\begin{equation} \\begin{gathered} q_A(u)=\\int_{\\mathbb T}e^{-A(y)}\\overline{\\chi_u(y)}\\,dy, \\qquad F(A)=-\\log q_A(0),\\qquad K_A=e^{F(A)-A},\\\\ \\Delta_A(u)=\\left(\\int_{\\mathbb T}|1-\\chi_u(y)|^2K_A(y)\\,dy\\right)^{1/2}, \\qquad B_A(r)=\\{u\\in\\mathbb Z:\\Delta_A(u)\\le r\\}. \\end{gathered} \\end{equation} Thus $K_A$ is a probability density. All circle integrals in this section use normalized","sourceOffset":75298,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/075.md","scopeText":"The $L\\log L$ Fourier-convergence conjecture asks whether ordinary symmetric Fourier partial sums converge almost everywhere at this integrability scale. The formalization proves this for every measurable complex-valued function on $\\mathbb T=\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)$ satisfying $\\int_{\\mathbb T}|f|\\log(2+|f|)<\\infty$. Outside one measurable null set,\n$\\sum_{k=-N}^{N}\\widehat f(k)e^{ikx}\\to f(x)$ along the full sequence $N\\to\\infty$.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/075.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":246294,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Almost-everywhere-Fourier-convergence-in-L-log-L-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"076-01","slug":"Ultraflat-real-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026","familyId":"076","title":"Ultraflat real Littlewood polynomials","abstract":"For every $\\varepsilon\\in(0,1)$ and every sufficiently large integer N, there is a polynomial of length N with coefficients in $\\{-1,1\\}$ whose modulus lies between $(1-\\varepsilon)\\sqrt N$ and $(1+\\varepsilon)\\sqrt N$ everywhere on the unit circle. Thus real Littlewood polynomials can be ultraflat through every sufficiently large integer length. The signs may be chosen separately at each length.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs polynomials with N consecutive coefficients in $\\{-1,1\\}$ whose modulus is $(1+o(1))\\sqrt N$ uniformly on the entire unit circle, for every sufficiently large integer length N. Thus real Littlewood polynomials are ultraflat, including at the real endpoints. Their binary merit factors tend to infinity, disproving Turyn's bounded-merit-factor conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/Ultraflat-real-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/ultraflat-real-littlewood-polynomials.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Ultraflat-real-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/ultraflat-real-littlewood-polynomials.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Ultraflat-real-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/Ultraflat-real-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Ultraflat-real-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Ultraflat-real-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/build/sections/oscillation.tex","preprints/Ultraflat-real-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/build/sections/rounding.tex","preprints/Ultraflat-real-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/build/sections/balanced.tex","preprints/Ultraflat-real-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/build/sections/waves.tex","preprints/Ultraflat-real-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/build/sections/completion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Ultraflat-real-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every $\\varepsilon\\in(0,1)$ and every sufficiently large integer $N$, there is a polynomial of length $N$ with coefficients in $\\{-1,1\\}$ whose modulus lies between $(1-\\varepsilon)\\sqrt N$ and $(1+\\varepsilon)\\sqrt N$ everywhere on the unit circle. Thus real Littlewood polynomials can be ultraflat through every sufficiently large integer length. The signs may be chosen separately at each length.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":449},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The flatness problem","sourceOffset":1536},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Construction and relation to the earlier version","sourceOffset":3797},{"kind":"section","title":"Oscillatory integral estimates","sourceOffset":7317},{"kind":"section","title":"Rounding with a small defect","sourceOffset":12129},{"kind":"section","title":"A real auxiliary function with balanced coefficients","sourceOffset":16716},{"kind":"section","title":"Waves of nearly constant modulus","sourceOffset":27593},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Packed intervals and inverse curvature","sourceOffset":30592},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coherent stationary contributions","sourceOffset":33656},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Joining the waves through the gaps","sourceOffset":35879},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fourier cost of the gaps","sourceOffset":40883},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cancellation at the joins and the exterior tail","sourceOffset":42652}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $\\varepsilon\\in(0,1)$ there is an integer $N_0$ such that, for every integer $N\\ge N_0$, there are signs $\\varepsilon_0,\\ldots,\\varepsilon_{N-1}$ for which \\[ (1-\\varepsilon)\\sqrt N \\le\\left|\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\\varepsilon_k z^k\\right| \\le(1+\\varepsilon)\\sqrt N \\qquad\\text{for every }|z|=1. \\]","sourceOffset":1031,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:balanced","text":"For every $0<\\delta<1/100$, there are an integer $m\\ge2$, a real trigonometric polynomial $F$ on $\\T^m$, and $v\\in\\R^m$ with the following properties. Its Fourier support $\\mathcal A\\subset\\Z^m\\setminus\\{0\\}$ consists of at least two pairs $\\{a,-a\\}$, with distinct pairs nonparallel. If \\[ c_a=\\widehat F(a),\\qquad \\lambda_a=a\\cdot v,\\qquad w_a=|\\lambda_a|, \\] then, for an absolute constant $C$, \\begin{equation} \\begin{gathered} \\norm F_\\infty\\le1+\\delta,\\qquad w_a>0,\\\\ 1-C\\delta\\le\\sum_{a\\in\\mathcal A}w_a<1,\\qquad 1\\le\\frac{|c_a|}{\\sqrt{w_a}}\\le1+C\\delta \\quad(a\\in\\mathcal A). \\end{gathered} \\end{equation} The coefficient ratios in \\eqref{eq:balanced} are also at most $2$.","sourceOffset":17255,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/076.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":47928,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Ultraflat-real-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"076-02","slug":"Nearly-minimal-maxima-and-positive-minima-of-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026","familyId":"076","title":"Nearly minimal maxima and positive minima of Littlewood polynomials","abstract":"For every η > 0 and every sufficiently large integer N, there is a polynomial with N consecutive coefficients in $\\{-1,1\\}$ whose modulus lies between $\\sqrt N/16$ and $(1+\\eta)\\sqrt N$ everywhere on the unit circle.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs polynomials with N consecutive coefficients in $\\{-1,1\\}$ whose modulus is $(1+o(1))\\sqrt N$ uniformly on the entire unit circle, for every sufficiently large integer length N. Thus real Littlewood polynomials are ultraflat, including at the real endpoints. Their binary merit factors tend to infinity, disproving Turyn's bounded-merit-factor conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/Nearly-minimal-maxima-and-positive-minima-of-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/littlewood-lower-envelope.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Nearly-minimal-maxima-and-positive-minima-of-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/littlewood-lower-envelope.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Nearly-minimal-maxima-and-positive-minima-of-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/Nearly-minimal-maxima-and-positive-minima-of-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Nearly-minimal-maxima-and-positive-minima-of-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Nearly-minimal-maxima-and-positive-minima-of-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/build/sections/spreading.tex","preprints/Nearly-minimal-maxima-and-positive-minima-of-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/build/sections/packing.tex","preprints/Nearly-minimal-maxima-and-positive-minima-of-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/build/sections/sampling.tex","preprints/Nearly-minimal-maxima-and-positive-minima-of-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/build/sections/correction.tex","preprints/Nearly-minimal-maxima-and-positive-minima-of-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/build/figures/gap-slots.tex","preprints/Nearly-minimal-maxima-and-positive-minima-of-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/build/sections/rounding.tex","preprints/Nearly-minimal-maxima-and-positive-minima-of-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/build/sections/completion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Nearly-minimal-maxima-and-positive-minima-of-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every $\\eta>0$ and every sufficiently large integer $N$, there is a polynomial with $N$ consecutive coefficients in $\\{-1,1\\}$ whose modulus lies between $\\sqrt N/16$ and $(1+\\eta)\\sqrt N$ everywhere on the unit circle.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":286},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and antecedents","sourceOffset":1373},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and notation","sourceOffset":4728},{"kind":"section","title":"Spreading Fourier coefficients","sourceOffset":7298},{"kind":"section","title":"Packing signed intervals","sourceOffset":22912},{"kind":"section","title":"Sampling and the geometry of the small values","sourceOffset":36772},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A uniform formula for quadratic sums","sourceOffset":39241},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Disjoint waves and their mass","sourceOffset":42482},{"kind":"section","title":"A correction with small Fourier coefficients","sourceOffset":49533},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Assigning the phase derivatives","sourceOffset":52736},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Matching phases while turning on the correction","sourceOffset":55646},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A bound for each Fourier coefficient","sourceOffset":63862}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $\\eta>0$ there is an integer $N_0=N_0(\\eta)$ such that, for every integer $N\\ge N_0$, there exist real signs $\\varepsilon_0,\\ldots,\\varepsilon_{N-1}$ for which \\[ \\frac1{16}\\sqrt N \\le\\left|\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\\varepsilon_kz^k\\right| \\le(1+\\eta)\\sqrt N \\qquad (|z|=1). \\]","sourceOffset":830,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"An auxiliary polynomial with controlled widths","label":"prop:auxiliary","text":"For every $0<\\delta<1/20$, there exist integers $m\\ge1$ and $r\\ge2$, nonzero vectors $a_1,\\ldots,a_r\\in\\Z^m$, a real-valued trigonometric polynomial $F$ on $\\T^m$, and a vector $v\\in\\R^m$ with the following properties. No two of the vectors $a_i$ are parallel, and \\[ \\mathcal A=\\{a_1,-a_1,\\ldots,a_r,-a_r\\} \\] is a containing Fourier support of $F$. Moreover, \\begin{equation} \\norm{F}_\\infty\\le1, \\qquad \\norm{F}_2\\ge1-3\\delta. \\end{equation} Writing $\\lambda_a=a\\cdot v$ for $a\\in\\mathcal A$, we have \\begin{equation} \\lambda_a\\ne0, \\qquad \\sum_{a\\in\\mathcal A}|\\lambda_a|<1, \\qquad \\frac{|\\widehat F(a)|}{\\sqrt{|\\lambda_a|}} \\le K_\\delta, \\qquad K_\\delta=\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\delta)^3}{1-\\delta}}. \\end{equation} All these objects depend on $\\delta$ alone.","sourceOffset":8330,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/076.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":83251,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Nearly-minimal-maxima-and-positive-minima-of-Littlewood-polynomials-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"076-03","slug":"Asymptotically-minimal-maxima-of-real-Littlewood-polynomials-September-23-2026","familyId":"076","title":"Asymptotically minimal maxima of real Littlewood polynomials","abstract":"We prove that the minimum possible maximum modulus on the unit circle of a polynomial with N consecutive real coefficients in $\\{-1,1\\}$ is $(1+o(1))\\sqrt N$, as N tends to infinity through all integers. This disproves the real-sign analogue of Erdős's fixed relative-gap conjecture. As a consequence, the largest binary merit factor at length N tends to infinity through all integer lengths, disproving Turyn's conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs polynomials with N consecutive coefficients in $\\{-1,1\\}$ whose modulus is $(1+o(1))\\sqrt N$ uniformly on the entire unit circle, for every sufficiently large integer length N. Thus real Littlewood polynomials are ultraflat, including at the real endpoints. Their binary merit factors tend to infinity, disproving Turyn's bounded-merit-factor conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/Asymptotically-minimal-maxima-of-real-Littlewood-polynomials-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Asymptotically-minimal-maxima-of-real-Littlewood-polynomials-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Asymptotically-minimal-maxima-of-real-Littlewood-polynomials-September-23-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/Asymptotically-minimal-maxima-of-real-Littlewood-polynomials-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Asymptotically-minimal-maxima-of-real-Littlewood-polynomials-September-23-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/Asymptotically-minimal-maxima-of-real-Littlewood-polynomials-September-23-2026/build/reduction.tex","preprints/Asymptotically-minimal-maxima-of-real-Littlewood-polynomials-September-23-2026/build/packing.tex","preprints/Asymptotically-minimal-maxima-of-real-Littlewood-polynomials-September-23-2026/build/spreading.tex","preprints/Asymptotically-minimal-maxima-of-real-Littlewood-polynomials-September-23-2026/build/sampling.tex","preprints/Asymptotically-minimal-maxima-of-real-Littlewood-polynomials-September-23-2026/build/rounding.tex","preprints/Asymptotically-minimal-maxima-of-real-Littlewood-polynomials-September-23-2026/build/flatness.tex","preprints/Asymptotically-minimal-maxima-of-real-Littlewood-polynomials-September-23-2026/build/merit-morse.tex","preprints/Asymptotically-minimal-maxima-of-real-Littlewood-polynomials-September-23-2026/build/concentration.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Asymptotically-minimal-maxima-of-real-Littlewood-polynomials-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the minimum possible maximum modulus on the unit circle of a polynomial with $N$ consecutive real coefficients in $\\{-1,1\\}$ is $(1+o(1))\\sqrt N$, as $N$ tends to infinity through all integers. This disproves the real-sign analogue of Erd\\H{o}s's fixed relative-gap conjecture. As a consequence, the largest binary merit factor at length $N$ tends to infinity through all integer lengths, disproving Turyn's conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":496},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and comparison","sourceOffset":2115},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Merit factors and a symbolic spectral consequence","sourceOffset":4661},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":6353},{"kind":"section","title":"Reduction to approximation and rounding","sourceOffset":8742},{"kind":"section","title":"Packing signed intervals","sourceOffset":11138},{"kind":"section","title":"Spreading Fourier coefficients","sourceOffset":25868},{"kind":"section","title":"Sampling the auxiliary polynomial","sourceOffset":40801},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A uniform estimate for quadratic sums","sourceOffset":41335},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fixed blocks and separated stationary contributions","sourceOffset":44630},{"kind":"section","title":"Rounding with a small defect mass","sourceOffset":52324},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A discrepancy bound for real matrices","sourceOffset":53258}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For Littlewood polynomials with real coefficients, \\[ \\lim_{N\\to\\infty}m_N=1. \\] Equivalently, for every $\\eta>0$ there is $N_0$ such that for every integer $N\\ge N_0$ there are signs $\\varepsilon_0,\\ldots,\\varepsilon_{N-1}$ with \\[ \\max_{|z|=1}\\left|\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\\varepsilon_kz^k\\right| \\le(1+\\eta)\\sqrt N. \\]","sourceOffset":1452,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Almost-sign approximation","label":"prop:almost-signs","text":"For every $0<\\delta<1/20$, there are vectors $X_N=(X_{N,0},\\ldots,X_{N,N-1})\\in[-1,1]^N$, one for each integer $N\\ge1$, such that \\begin{align} \\limsup_{N\\to\\infty}\\frac{\\norm{Q_{X_N}}_\\infty}{\\sqrt N} &\\le K_\\delta, &K_\\delta&=\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\delta)^3}{1-\\delta}}, \\\\ \\liminf_{N\\to\\infty}\\frac1N\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}X_{N,k}^2 &\\ge1-7\\delta. \\end{align}","sourceOffset":9121,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/076.md","scopeText":"The formalization gives real Littlewood polynomials of every sufficiently large length whose maximum modulus on the unit circle is at most $(1+\\eta)\\sqrt N$ for any fixed $\\eta>0$. Thus the smallest possible maximum is asymptotically minimal through all integer lengths.\n\nA further statement chooses one family of real sign polynomials for all lengths such that, for every fixed finite $p>0$, the $L^p$ mean of $\\bigl||P_N(z)|/\\sqrt N-1\\bigr|$ on the unit circle tends to zero. The results are existential and do not give an effective convergence rate or a signing algorithm.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/076.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":71720,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Asymptotically-minimal-maxima-of-real-Littlewood-polynomials-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"077-01","slug":"Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026","familyId":"077","title":"Elliptic capacity propagation and Fourier restriction to the sphere","abstract":"We prove the bounded-data Fourier restriction conjecture for the sphere in three dimensions: the Fourier extension operator maps $L^\\infty(S^2)$ boundedly into $L^p(\\mathbb R^3)$ for every p > 3. This is the full conjectured open range, and the threshold p = 3 is sharp.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the diagonal Fourier extension conjecture for positively curved surfaces in three dimensions. For every compact smooth positively curved surface $\\Sigma\\subset\\mathbb R^3$, including surfaces with boundary, the extension operator is bounded from $L^p(\\Sigma)$ to $L^p(\\mathbb R^3)$ for every p > 3.","pdfPath":"preprints/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-geometric.tex","preprints/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026/build/figures/capacity-tests.tex","preprints/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-entropy.tex","preprints/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-projection.tex","preprints/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-aspect.tex","preprints/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-packets.tex","preprints/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-extremal.tex","preprints/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026/build/figures/packet-proof-map.tex","preprints/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026/build/figures/adjacency-radii.tex","preprints/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-global.tex","preprints/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the bounded-data Fourier restriction conjecture for the sphere in three dimensions: the Fourier extension operator maps $L^\\infty(S^2)$ boundedly into $L^p(\\R^3)$ for every $p>3$. This is the full conjectured open range, and the threshold $p=3$ is sharp.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":310},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The restriction problem and earlier methods","sourceOffset":1382},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Elliptic capacity and the geometric obstacle","sourceOffset":5415},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite packet theorem","sourceOffset":10100},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Consequences and related estimates","sourceOffset":15376},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The argument","sourceOffset":18874},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and conventions","sourceOffset":23591},{"kind":"section","title":"Geometric propagation and a calibrated path","sourceOffset":24897},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ellipse capacity and the propagation exponent","sourceOffset":24967},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sequence conventions and conditional time profiles","sourceOffset":32859},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A hairbrush bound and angular uniqueness","sourceOffset":42339},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reduction to bounded spatial complexity","sourceOffset":51768}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every real \\(p>3\\), there is a finite constant \\(C_p\\), depending only on \\(p\\), such that \\[ \\|Eg\\|_{L^p(\\R^3)} \\le C_p\\|g\\|_{L^\\infty(S^2,\\sigma)} \\] for every bounded measurable complex-valued function \\(g\\) on \\(S^2\\).","sourceOffset":794,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Geometric propagation","label":"geo:main","text":"For \\(0<s\\le1\\) and \\(0<\\kappa<1/10\\), \\begin{equation} q_*\\ge1+2s-10\\kappa. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":30821,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"geo:large-sublaw","text":"A sublaw of a probability measure \\(\\mu\\) is a finite measure \\(\\nu\\le\\mu\\). Along a sequence with base \\(M\\to\\infty\\), a sublaw has large mass if \\[ \\nu(\\text{whole space})=M^{-o(1)}. \\] Normalizing such a sublaw changes every upper mass bound by at most a factor \\(M^{o(1)}\\). We use either its unnormalized masses or its normalized probabilities, recording this factor when necessary.","sourceOffset":26104,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Admissible families","label":"pkt:families","text":"An admissible family has $m\\to\\infty$, with $m,N$ dyadic and $m\\le N\\le m^B$ for a fixed finite $B$. Its arrays have finite supports, all their position labels have size at most $m^B$, and all frequency labels lie in a fixed bounded set. Enlarging $B$ is allowed; it must remain fixed along each family. Constants controlling the phase and windows are fixed as well. The same convention applies to diagrams with a fixed finite number of scales.","sourceOffset":245356,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":353084,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":21,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Elliptic-capacity-propagation-and-Fourier-restriction-to-the-sphere-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"077-02","slug":"Diagonal-Fourier-extension-for-positively-curved-surfaces-in-three-dimensions-September-24-2026","familyId":"077","title":"Diagonal Fourier extension for positively curved surfaces in three dimensions","abstract":"We prove the diagonal Fourier extension conjecture for compact smooth positively curved surfaces $\\Sigma\\subset\\mathbb R^3$, including surfaces with smooth boundary. For every $3\\lt p\\lt \\infty$, the extension operator maps $L^p(\\Sigma)$ boundedly into $L^p(\\mathbb R^3)$. The compact paraboloid case also yields free Schrödinger local smoothing in two spatial dimensions for every $3\\lt p\\lt \\infty$ and Sobolev order $s\\gt 2-6/p$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the diagonal Fourier extension conjecture for positively curved surfaces in three dimensions. For every compact smooth positively curved surface $\\Sigma\\subset\\mathbb R^3$, including surfaces with boundary, the extension operator is bounded from $L^p(\\Sigma)$ to $L^p(\\mathbb R^3)$ for every p > 3.","pdfPath":"preprints/Diagonal-Fourier-extension-for-positively-curved-surfaces-in-three-dimensions-September-24-2026/Diagonal-Fourier-extension-for-positively-curved-surfaces-in-three-dimensions-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Diagonal-Fourier-extension-for-positively-curved-surfaces-in-three-dimensions-September-24-2026/Diagonal-Fourier-extension-for-positively-curved-surfaces-in-three-dimensions-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Diagonal-Fourier-extension-for-positively-curved-surfaces-in-three-dimensions-September-24-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/Diagonal-Fourier-extension-for-positively-curved-surfaces-in-three-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Diagonal-Fourier-extension-for-positively-curved-surfaces-in-three-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Diagonal-Fourier-extension-for-positively-curved-surfaces-in-three-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/figures/argument-map.tex","preprints/Diagonal-Fourier-extension-for-positively-curved-surfaces-in-three-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-setup.tex","preprints/Diagonal-Fourier-extension-for-positively-curved-surfaces-in-three-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-packets.tex","preprints/Diagonal-Fourier-extension-for-positively-curved-surfaces-in-three-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-density.tex","preprints/Diagonal-Fourier-extension-for-positively-curved-surfaces-in-three-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-sparse.tex","preprints/Diagonal-Fourier-extension-for-positively-curved-surfaces-in-three-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-global.tex","preprints/Diagonal-Fourier-extension-for-positively-curved-surfaces-in-three-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-surfaces.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Diagonal-Fourier-extension-for-positively-curved-surfaces-in-three-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the diagonal Fourier extension conjecture for compact smooth positively curved surfaces \\(\\Sigma\\subset\\R^3\\), including surfaces with smooth boundary. For every \\(3<p<\\infty\\), the extension operator maps \\(L^p(\\Sigma)\\) boundedly into \\(L^p(\\R^3)\\). The compact paraboloid case also yields free Schr\\\"odinger local smoothing in two spatial dimensions for every \\(3<p<\\infty\\) and Sobolev order \\(s>2-6/p\\).","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":678},{"kind":"section","title":"Local graphs and fixed parameters","sourceOffset":11489},{"kind":"section","title":"The packet estimate and its phase class","sourceOffset":14653},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Continued phases and local changes of coordinates","sourceOffset":15082},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Frames and the capacity norm","sourceOffset":20023},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The cited theorem and its specialization","sourceOffset":25007},{"kind":"section","title":"From packet capacity to weighted tubes","sourceOffset":28833},{"kind":"section","title":"Joint estimates on sparse balls","sourceOffset":40912},{"kind":"section","title":"Global bounds and exponent dependence","sourceOffset":54294},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Elementary bounds and the number of occupied cubes","sourceOffset":55060},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A multiscale cover without a diameter bound","sourceOffset":59267},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Integration of the distribution bound","sourceOffset":63236}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(\\Sigma\\subset\\R^3\\) be a compact smooth positively curved surface, possibly with smooth boundary. For every \\(3<p<\\infty\\), there is a finite constant \\(C_{\\Sigma,p}\\) such that \\begin{equation} \\|E_\\Sigma f\\|_{L^p(\\R^3)} \\le C_{\\Sigma,p}\\|f\\|_{L^p(\\Sigma)} \\end{equation} for every complex-valued \\(f\\in L^p(\\Sigma)\\).","sourceOffset":1121,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Free Schr\\\"odinger local smoothing","label":"cor:schrodinger-smoothing","text":"For every \\(3<p<\\infty\\) and \\(s>2-6/p\\), the free Schr\\\"odinger propagator, initially defined on Schwartz functions, extends boundedly from \\(W^{s,p}(\\R^2)\\) to \\(L^p(\\R^2\\times[0,1])\\): \\begin{equation} \\|e^{it\\Delta}f\\|_{L^p(\\R^2\\times[0,1])} \\le C_{p,s}\\|f\\|_{W^{s,p}(\\R^2)}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":3837,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":78020,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Diagonal-Fourier-extension-for-positively-curved-surfaces-in-three-dimensions-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"078-01","slug":"Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026","familyId":"078","title":"Bochner–Riesz multipliers in three dimensions","abstract":"We prove the three-dimensional Bochner–Riesz conjecture in its strict-order formulation. The main result is boundedness of the Bochner–Riesz multipliers on $L^3(\\mathbb R^3)$ for every positive order. Interpolation and duality then give the full conjectured strict range.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the three-dimensional Bochner–Riesz conjecture in its strict range: the Bochner–Riesz multipliers of order δ are bounded on $L^p(\\mathbb R^3)$ for every $1\\le p\\le\\infty$ whenever $\\delta\\gt \\max\\{3|1/p-1/2|-1/2,0\\}$.","pdfPath":"preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-entropy.tex","preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-projection.tex","preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-classical-extremality.tex","preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/build/figures/aperture-geometry.tex","preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-isotropic.tex","preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-characteristic-plane.tex","preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/build/figures/characteristic-plane.tex","preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-rate-transfer.tex","preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-classical-closure.tex","preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-packet-structure.tex","preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-packet-composition.tex","preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/11-packet-repayment.tex","preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/build/figures/packet-cells.tex","preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/12-multiplier.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the three-dimensional Bochner--Riesz conjecture in its strict-order formulation. The main result is boundedness of the Bochner--Riesz multipliers on $L^3(\\RR^3)$ for every positive order. Interpolation and duality then give the full conjectured strict range.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":341},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The problem and its development","sourceOffset":2069},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From geometric growth to oscillatory cancellation","sourceOffset":7340},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite laws and order of limits","sourceOffset":12458},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite entropy, conditioning, and passage to profiles","sourceOffset":14454},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Entropy estimates with unrestricted parent alphabets","sourceOffset":15279},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From small information to explicit product comparisons","sourceOffset":27780},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Countable profiles and the order of the limits","sourceOffset":41576},{"kind":"section","title":"The planar projection estimate","sourceOffset":49467},{"kind":"section","title":"Extremal positive transport and its aspect","sourceOffset":59772},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Apertures and the universal growth rate","sourceOffset":60619},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Splitting and cap bounds","sourceOffset":69079}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:bochner-riesz","text":"For every $\\delta>0$, there is a finite constant $C_\\delta$ such that \\begin{equation} \\norm{T_\\delta f}_{L^3(\\RR^3)} \\le C_\\delta\\norm f_{L^3(\\RR^3)} \\qquad(f\\in\\mathcal S(\\RR^3)). \\end{equation} Consequently $T_\\delta$ has a unique bounded extension to $L^3(\\RR^3)$.","sourceOffset":973,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Finite stars and conditioning with named comparison laws","label":"thm:finite-conditioning","text":"Let $\\pi(p,b)$ be a probability law with $\\mu=\\pi_P$, $\\lambda=\\pi_B$. Let $E$ be an allowed set with $\\pi(E)\\ge1-\\zeta$ and suppose \\begin{equation} \\boldsymbol1_E(p,b)\\pi(p,b)\\le K\\mu(p)\\lambda(b),\\qquad K\\ge1. \\end{equation} Choose $0<\\alpha<1$ with $\\zeta<1-\\alpha$, retain only point fibers with $r(p):=\\pi(E\\mid P=p)\\ge\\alpha$, and normalize the pairs in these fibers and in $E$ to a law $q$. Its retained mass $M_0$ satisfies \\begin{equation} M_0\\ge1-\\frac{\\zeta}{1-\\alpha},\\qquad q(p,b)\\le M_0^{-1}\\pi(p,b),\\qquad q(b\\mid p)\\le c\\lambda(b),\\quad c:=K/\\alpha. \\end{equation} For a fixed integer $n\\ge1$, sample $B_1,\\ldots,B_n$ independently given $P$ with kernel $q(b\\mid p)$, and let \\[ S(p,b_1,\\ldots,b_n)=q_P(p)\\prod_{i=1}^nq(b_i\\mid p). \\] The following are comparison probability laws on the whole star: \\begin{align} \\Lambda_0&=q_P\\otimes\\lambda^{\\otimes n}, &S&\\le c^n\\Lambda_0,\\\\ \\Lambda_i&=q_{P,B_i}\\otimes\\bigotimes_{j\\ne i}\\lambda_{B_j}, &S&\\le c^{n-1}\\Lambda_i\\quad(1\\le i\\le n). \\end{align} Here $q_{P,B_i}$ is a copy of the retained pair law $q$. It may be replaced by the original $\\pi$ in \\eqref{eq:star-pair} at the additional cost $M_0^{-1}$. Let $R=wS/m$, where $0\\le w\\le1$ and $m=\\mathbb E_Sw>0$. Fix an index set $J\\subset\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$ of fixed labels; write $F=\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\setminus J$ for the free labels. Choose any observation \\[ C=(B_J,Z),\\qquad Z=f(P,B_J). \\] Thus the function defining $Z$ involves the point and the fixed labels only. For every $0<\\eta<1/(n+","sourceOffset":31194,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Standard aperture observation","label":"def:classical-aperture","text":"At duration $t$ and horizon $L$, a standard observation $G$ records \\[ (t,b,a,C_t,R_{a-b},[V]_{b,a;R}, [X(C_t)]_{b+t,a+t;R}), \\qquad 0\\le b\\le a,\\quad a+t\\le L. \\] Here the bracket denotes the rectangular grid cell, in the recorded frame, with side lengths $\\eps^b,\\eps^a$, or respectively $\\eps^{b+t},\\eps^{a+t}$. Its aperture weight is $w(G)=w(b,a)$. The shape and orientation may be chosen by a stochastic kernel; extra finite labels are permitted. Equivalently, a label may locate the data in bounded enlargements of the indicated rectangles. Adding the actual grid cells then costs $O(1/\\log(1/\\","sourceOffset":61379,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Successive saturation arrays","label":"def:packet-saturation-array","text":"After normalizing duration to one, a saturation array consists of rows indexed by $j$, with small parameters $\\eps_{j,n}\\downarrow0$ within each row. Row $j$ obeys one fixed collection of analytic, domain, and depth bounds $\\mathcal B_j$, and, for numbers $\\Delta_j\\downarrow0$, \\[ \\limsup_{n\\to\\infty} \\bigl(K_{0,j,n}+\\betaq-P_{1,j,n}\\bigr)\\le\\Delta_j. \\] The corresponding lower limit is nonnegative by the universal bound for that row. Bounds $\\mathcal B_j$ may grow with $j$; the outer array is not asserted to be one admissible small-parameter sequence. All uses of a universal modulus are made ","sourceOffset":304763,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":404955,"sourceFileCount":17,"statementCount":22,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Bochner-Riesz-Multipliers-in-Three-Dimensions-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"079-01","slug":"Critical-local-smoothing-for-the-three-dimensional-wave-equation-September-24-2026","familyId":"079","title":"Critical local smoothing for the three-dimensional wave equation","abstract":"We prove the critical L3 local smoothing estimate for the wave equation in three spatial dimensions, with every positive Sobolev loss. This resolves Sogge's Euclidean local smoothing conjecture in dimension three.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves Sogge's local smoothing conjecture for the Euclidean wave equation in three spatial dimensions. The estimate holds throughout the full strict range $2\\lt p\\lt \\infty$, with Sobolev regularity above $\\max\\{0,1-3/p\\}$. In particular, the critical L3 estimate holds with every positive Sobolev loss.","pdfPath":"preprints/Critical-local-smoothing-for-the-three-dimensional-wave-equation-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Critical-local-smoothing-for-the-three-dimensional-wave-equation-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Critical-local-smoothing-for-the-three-dimensional-wave-equation-September-24-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/Critical-local-smoothing-for-the-three-dimensional-wave-equation-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Critical-local-smoothing-for-the-three-dimensional-wave-equation-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Critical-local-smoothing-for-the-three-dimensional-wave-equation-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-framework.tex","preprints/Critical-local-smoothing-for-the-three-dimensional-wave-equation-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-cylinder.tex","preprints/Critical-local-smoothing-for-the-three-dimensional-wave-equation-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-lower-dimensional.tex","preprints/Critical-local-smoothing-for-the-three-dimensional-wave-equation-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-canonical-names.tex","preprints/Critical-local-smoothing-for-the-three-dimensional-wave-equation-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-interior.tex","preprints/Critical-local-smoothing-for-the-three-dimensional-wave-equation-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-cylinder-actions.tex","preprints/Critical-local-smoothing-for-the-three-dimensional-wave-equation-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-round-transfer.tex","preprints/Critical-local-smoothing-for-the-three-dimensional-wave-equation-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-round-entropy.tex","preprints/Critical-local-smoothing-for-the-three-dimensional-wave-equation-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-physical-wave.tex","preprints/Critical-local-smoothing-for-the-three-dimensional-wave-equation-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-bochner-riesz.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Critical-local-smoothing-for-the-three-dimensional-wave-equation-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the critical $L^3$ local smoothing estimate for the wave equation in three spatial dimensions, with every positive Sobolev loss. This resolves Sogge's Euclidean local smoothing conjecture in dimension three.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":263},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":3944},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Structure of the argument","sourceOffset":10880},{"kind":"section","title":"Scales, energies, and limiting arguments","sourceOffset":18553},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fixed setups and exponent bounds","sourceOffset":18960},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Energy and finite extraction","sourceOffset":23316},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Closure of exponent data","sourceOffset":26755},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ordered limits and changes of scale","sourceOffset":30508},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Elementary probability comparisons","sourceOffset":32576},{"kind":"section","title":"Cylinder experiments and extremal configurations","sourceOffset":38833},{"kind":"subsection","title":"States, tests, and assignments","sourceOffset":39258},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cutoffs and localization","sourceOffset":43853}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Critical local smoothing","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $\\eps>0$ there exists $C_\\eps<\\infty$ such that \\begin{equation} \\norm{Uf}_{L^3(\\R^3\\times[1,2])} \\le C_\\eps\\norm{J^\\eps f}_{L^3(\\R^3)} \\qquad (f\\in\\mathcal S(\\R^3)). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":960,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Cylinder exponent bound","label":"thm:cylinder","text":"For the classical and hybrid cylinder experiments with the regularity weight parameter $0<\\eta<1$, \\[ \\Gamma_{\\mathrm{cl}}\\le\\eta/2, \\qquad \\Gamma_{\\mathrm{hyb}}\\le\\eta/2. \\]","sourceOffset":53360,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Fixed polynomial setup","label":"def:fw-setup","text":"A fixed polynomial setup specifies all structural constants, the finite upper bounds on powers of $s^{-1}$ occurring in its hypotheses, and a finite bound on the number of operations along each branch. These data are fixed before $s\\to0$. A sequence in this setup may have parameters, operators, measures, and Hilbert spaces depending on $s$, provided the specified bounds hold uniformly along the sequence. An error $r_s$ is negligible relative to a positive reference quantity $a_s$ if, for every $N>0$, \\[ |r_s|\\le C_N s^N a_s \\] for all sufficiently small scales, with $C_N$ independent of scale.","sourceOffset":19509,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Cylinder states","label":"cyl:def-states","text":"In the classical cylinder, $(N,D)$ is another exact index, $D_T=D+TN$, and free evolution leaves the state unchanged in these moving coordinates. In the hybrid cylinder, $H>0$ is fixed, there is an exact index $\\sigma\\in[1,2]$, and a fiber state belongs to $L^2(\\R_D;\\mathcal H)$. Its free propagator $U_t^{H,\\sigma}$ is \\[ \\widehat{U_t^{H,\\sigma}g}(p) =e^{-itHp^2/(2\\sigma)}\\widehat g(p). \\] At length $\\ell$, set \\[ d_\\ell=(H/\\ell)^{1/2},\\qquad q_\\ell=(H\\ell)^{1/2}, \\qquad \\phi_{D,N}^{\\ell,\\sigma}(z) =(\\pi q_\\ell^2)^{-1/4} e^{-(z-D)^2/(2q_\\ell^2)+i\\sigma Nz/H}. \\] The analysis operator is \\[ (V_","sourceOffset":40235,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":583100,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":45,"definitionCount":10,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Critical-local-smoothing-for-the-three-dimensional-wave-equation-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"080-01","slug":"Endpoint-convergence-for-the-planar-Schrodinger-equation-September-24-2026","familyId":"080","title":"Endpoint convergence for the planar Schrodinger equation","abstract":"We resolve the planar Sobolev endpoint of Carleson's convergence problem: for initial data in $H^{1/3}(\\mathbb R^2)$, the Schrödinger evolution converges almost everywhere to the initial data as $t\\downarrow0$. The evolution is defined by taking the Gaussian regularization limit first; on one full-measure spatial set, this limit exists for every $0\\lt t\\lt 1$, and the resulting evolution is continuous at t = 0.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves almost-everywhere convergence $e^{it\\Delta}f\\to f$ as $t\\downarrow0$ for every $f\\in H^{n/(2(n+1))}(\\mathbb R^n)$ and every dimension n ≥ 2. This attains the sharp Sobolev equality case of Carleson's Schrödinger convergence problem, including the planar endpoint H1/3.","pdfPath":"preprints/Endpoint-convergence-for-the-planar-Schrodinger-equation-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Endpoint-convergence-for-the-planar-Schrodinger-equation-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Endpoint-convergence-for-the-planar-Schrodinger-equation-September-24-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/Endpoint-convergence-for-the-planar-Schrodinger-equation-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Endpoint-convergence-for-the-planar-Schrodinger-equation-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Endpoint-convergence-for-the-planar-Schrodinger-equation-September-24-2026/build/figures/dependencies.tex","preprints/Endpoint-convergence-for-the-planar-Schrodinger-equation-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Endpoint-convergence-for-the-planar-Schrodinger-equation-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-transverse.tex","preprints/Endpoint-convergence-for-the-planar-Schrodinger-equation-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-sparse.tex","preprints/Endpoint-convergence-for-the-planar-Schrodinger-equation-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-frame.tex","preprints/Endpoint-convergence-for-the-planar-Schrodinger-equation-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-flat.tex","preprints/Endpoint-convergence-for-the-planar-Schrodinger-equation-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-geometry.tex","preprints/Endpoint-convergence-for-the-planar-Schrodinger-equation-September-24-2026/build/figures/geometry.tex","preprints/Endpoint-convergence-for-the-planar-Schrodinger-equation-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-dyadic.tex","preprints/Endpoint-convergence-for-the-planar-Schrodinger-equation-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-endpoint.tex","preprints/Endpoint-convergence-for-the-planar-Schrodinger-equation-September-24-2026/build/figures/endpoint-tree.tex","preprints/Endpoint-convergence-for-the-planar-Schrodinger-equation-September-24-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Endpoint-convergence-for-the-planar-Schrodinger-equation-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We resolve the planar Sobolev endpoint of Carleson's convergence problem: for initial data in $H^{1/3}(\\R^2)$, the Schr\\\"odinger evolution converges almost everywhere to the initial data as $t\\downarrow0$. The evolution is defined by taking the Gaussian regularization limit first; on one full-measure spatial set, this limit exists for every $0<t<1$, and the resulting evolution is continuous at $t=0$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":466},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":1712},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Three ingredients of the proof","sourceOffset":3992},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and dependencies","sourceOffset":7181},{"kind":"section","title":"Packets, localization, and fractal estimates","sourceOffset":9299},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and the compact atom class","sourceOffset":9815},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact packets and spatial localization","sourceOffset":15457},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The refined fractal estimate and unit-cube suprema","sourceOffset":20842},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weights and cap rescaling","sourceOffset":25523},{"kind":"section","title":"A power saving for transverse inputs","sourceOffset":33509},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform multilinear estimates and the tube model","sourceOffset":35314},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Information at spatial scales","sourceOffset":46622}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Planar endpoint convergence","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $f\\in H^{1/3}(\\R^2)$ there is a set $E_f\\subset\\R^2$ of full Lebesgue measure such that, for each $x\\in E_f$, the finite limit \\[ u_f(x,t)=\\lim_{a\\downarrow0}U_af(x,t) \\] exists for every real $0<t<1$, and \\[ \\lim_{t\\downarrow0}u_f(x,t)=f^*(x). \\] In fact $u_f(x,\\cdot)$ extends continuously to $[0,1)$ with value $f^*(x)$ at zero, and the Gaussian limit is uniform over $0<t<1$.","sourceOffset":1244,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Transverse power saving","label":"prop:transverse","text":"Fix a bounded frequency region. There are $c>0$ and $L_0<\\infty$, depending only on that region, for which the following configuration cannot exist when $L\\ge L_0$. Let $\\mathcal Q$ be unit lattice cubes inside a ball of radius $L^2$ in $\\R^3$, with \\[ \\#\\mathcal Q\\ge L^{4-c},\\qquad \\#\\{Q\\in\\mathcal Q:Q\\cap B(z,r)\\ne\\varnothing\\} \\le L^c r^2\\quad(r\\ge1), \\] where cube counts may equivalently use their centers, up to fixed constants. Suppose $v_1,v_2,v_3$ have frequency support in the fixed region, $\\|v_i\\|_2\\le1$, and \\[ \\sup_Q|Ev_i|\\ge L^{-4/3-c} \\qquad(Q\\in\\mathcal Q,\\ i=1,2,3). \\] Then it is impossible that every triple of unit frequency directions, one from each support, has absolute determinant at least $L^{-c}$.","sourceOffset":34023,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Compact atoms","label":"def:prelim-atoms","text":"Let $L\\geq1$, and set $R=L^2$. An atom with label $\\alpha$ is the field \\begin{equation} A_\\alpha(x,t)=L^{-1} e^{i(x\\cdot\\omega_\\alpha-t|\\omega_\\alpha|^2)} H_\\alpha\\left(\\frac{x-b_\\alpha-2\\omega_\\alpha t}{L}, \\frac{t}{L^2}\\right). \\end{equation} The frequencies $\\omega_\\alpha$ lie in a fixed square. The labels obey a joint phase-space bound: every unit ball in $\\R^2\\times\\R^2$ contains at most a fixed number of the pairs \\[ \\left(\\frac{b_\\alpha}{L},L\\omega_\\alpha\\right). \\] The profiles $H_\\alpha(y,\\tau)$ are supported in a fixed bounded box, and have uniform bounds for sufficiently many deriv","sourceOffset":11650,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":298410,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Endpoint-convergence-for-the-planar-Schrodinger-equation-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"080-02","slug":"Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026","familyId":"080","title":"Endpoint pointwise convergence for the Schrodinger equation in higher dimensions","abstract":"We resolve the Sobolev endpoint of Carleson's pointwise convergence problem in every dimension n ≥ 3. For initial data in $H^{n/(2(n+1))}(\\mathbb R^n)$, the free Schrödinger evolution converges almost everywhere to the initial data as $t\\downarrow0$. The evolution is defined by removing Gaussian regularization on one full-measure spatial set, uniformly over the interval $0\\lt t\\lt 1$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves almost-everywhere convergence $e^{it\\Delta}f\\to f$ as $t\\downarrow0$ for every $f\\in H^{n/(2(n+1))}(\\mathbb R^n)$ and every dimension n ≥ 2. This attains the sharp Sobolev equality case of Carleson's Schrödinger convergence problem, including the planar endpoint H1/3.","pdfPath":"preprints/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/packets.tex","preprints/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/frame.tex","preprints/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/figures/forced_profiles.tex","preprints/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/fractal.tex","preprints/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/transverse.tex","preprints/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/sparse.tex","preprints/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/weak.tex","preprints/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/angular.tex","preprints/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/wall.tex","preprints/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/flattening.tex","preprints/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/sections/endpoint.tex","preprints/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/figures/time_tree.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We resolve the Sobolev endpoint of Carleson's pointwise convergence problem in every dimension $n\\geq3$. For initial data in $H^{n/(2(n+1))}(\\R^n)$, the free Schr\\\"odinger evolution converges almost everywhere to the initial data as $t\\downarrow0$. The evolution is defined by removing Gaussian regularization on one full-measure spatial set, uniformly over the interval $0<t<1$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":442},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":2390},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The new estimates and the proof","sourceOffset":6154},{"kind":"section","title":"Packet estimates and a near-Cauchy bound","sourceOffset":9428},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and external estimates","sourceOffset":9820},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Localization with fixed original regularity","sourceOffset":17605},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weighted decoupling","sourceOffset":20900},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local estimates without a scale loss","sourceOffset":43393},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Hierarchies, masks, and positive weights","sourceOffset":48607},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A near-Cauchy estimate","sourceOffset":56298},{"kind":"section","title":"The transverse frame estimate","sourceOffset":74610},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact packets and a normalized countersequence","sourceOffset":77468}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Endpoint convergence","label":"m:main","text":"For every fixed integer $n\\geq3$ and every complex-valued $f\\in H^{s_n}(\\R^n)$, there is a full-measure set $E_f$ of Lebesgue points of $f$ and a function $v(x,t)$, $x\\in E_f$, $0\\leq t<1$, such that $v(x,\\cdot)$ is continuous, $v(x,0)=f^*(x)$, and \\begin{equation} \\lim_{a\\downarrow0}\\sup_{0<t<1}|U_af(x,t)-v(x,t)|=0 \\qquad(x\\in E_f). \\end{equation} In particular the finite Gaussian limit \\[ u_f(x,t)=\\lim_{a\\downarrow0}U_af(x,t) \\] exists for every real $0<t<1$ on this one set, and $\\lim_{t\\downarrow0}u_f(x,t)=f^*(x)$.","sourceOffset":1544,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Weighted endpoint multilinear Kakeya","label":"p:kakeya","text":"Let $d\\geq2$. For $j=1,\\ldots,d$, let $\\mathcal T_j$ be a family of infinite cylinders of radius $h$ in $\\R^d$, with unit direction vectors $e(T)$. Suppose \\[ |\\det(e(T_1),\\ldots,e(T_d))|\\geq\\vartheta>0 \\quad(T_j\\in\\mathcal T_j). \\] For nonnegative weights $c_j(T)$, \\begin{equation} \\int_{\\R^d}\\prod_{j=1}^d \\left(\\sum_{T\\in\\mathcal T_j}c_j(T)\\mathbf1_T\\right)^{1/(d-1)} \\leq C_d\\vartheta^{-1/(d-1)}h^d \\prod_{j=1}^d\\left(\\sum_{T\\in\\mathcal T_j}c_j(T)\\right)^{1/(d-1)}. \\end{equation} For lattice cubes of side $a\\le h$, the same right-hand side bounds $a^d$ times the corresponding sum of cube suprema, after a fixed dimensional enlargement of every cylinder.","sourceOffset":13372,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Normalized compact arrays and admissible measures","label":"d:measure","text":"A compact array of scale $L\\ge1$ is a finite sum \\begin{equation} U(x,t)=\\sum_\\alpha c_\\alpha L^{-n/2} e^{i(x\\cdot\\omega_\\alpha-t|\\omega_\\alpha|^2)} \\phi_\\alpha\\!\\left( \\frac{x-y_\\alpha-2t\\omega_\\alpha}{L},\\frac t{L^2}\\right). \\end{equation} The frequencies $\\omega_\\alpha$ belong to a fixed bounded box. The native profiles $\\phi_\\alpha$ are supported in a fixed box and have uniform bounds for derivatives through a prescribed sufficiently large finite order. The number of labels $(L\\omega_\\alpha,y_\\alpha/L)$ in every unit phase-space ball is bounded by a fixed constant. Its coefficient energy i","sourceOffset":161540,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":334450,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":20,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Endpoint-pointwise-convergence-for-the-Schrodinger-equation-in-higher-dimensions-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"081-01","slug":"Riesz-transforms-and-uniform-rectifiability-in-higher-codimension-September-24-2026","familyId":"081","title":"Riesz transforms and uniform rectifiability in higher codimension","abstract":"We prove that an n-Ahlfors–David regular Radon measure on ℝd is uniformly n-rectifiable if its n-dimensional Riesz transform is uniformly bounded from scalar $L^2(\\mu)$ to vector-valued $L^2(\\mu)$ over all positive hard truncations, for integers d ≥ 4 and $2\\le n\\le d-2$. This gives a positive answer to the David–Semmes Riesz-transform question in its remaining higher-codimension range. The conclusion gives uniform big pieces of Lipschitz images of Euclidean balls.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the remaining higher-codimension Riesz-transform rectifiability problem: for d ≥ 4 and $2\\le n\\le d-2$, an n-Ahlfors–David regular Radon measure on ℝd is uniformly n-rectifiable whenever its n-dimensional Riesz transform is uniformly L2-bounded over all positive hard truncations. The rectifiability bounds depend only on dimension, regularity and operator bounds.","pdfPath":"preprints/Riesz-transforms-and-uniform-rectifiability-in-higher-codimension-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Riesz-transforms-and-uniform-rectifiability-in-higher-codimension-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Riesz-transforms-and-uniform-rectifiability-in-higher-codimension-September-24-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/Riesz-transforms-and-uniform-rectifiability-in-higher-codimension-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Riesz-transforms-and-uniform-rectifiability-in-higher-codimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Riesz-transforms-and-uniform-rectifiability-in-higher-codimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-background.tex","preprints/Riesz-transforms-and-uniform-rectifiability-in-higher-codimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-flat-balls.tex","preprints/Riesz-transforms-and-uniform-rectifiability-in-higher-codimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-planar-limits.tex","preprints/Riesz-transforms-and-uniform-rectifiability-in-higher-codimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-height-compactness.tex","preprints/Riesz-transforms-and-uniform-rectifiability-in-higher-codimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-fractional-rigidity.tex","preprints/Riesz-transforms-and-uniform-rectifiability-in-higher-codimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-excess-propagation.tex","preprints/Riesz-transforms-and-uniform-rectifiability-in-higher-codimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-packing-endpoint.tex","preprints/Riesz-transforms-and-uniform-rectifiability-in-higher-codimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-consequences.tex","preprints/Riesz-transforms-and-uniform-rectifiability-in-higher-codimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-geometric-conventions.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Riesz-transforms-and-uniform-rectifiability-in-higher-codimension-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that an $n$-Ahlfors--David regular Radon measure on $\\R^d$ is uniformly $n$-rectifiable if its $n$-dimensional Riesz transform is uniformly bounded from scalar $L^2(\\mu)$ to vector-valued $L^2(\\mu)$ over all positive hard truncations, for integers $d\\ge4$ and $2\\le n\\le d-2$. This gives a positive answer to the David--Semmes Riesz-transform question in its remaining higher-codimension range. The conclusion gives uniform big pieces of Lipschitz images of Euclidean balls.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The quantitative theorem and normalized pairings","sourceOffset":546},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the analytic question","sourceOffset":3420},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The main mechanism","sourceOffset":7090},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Global growth and hard truncations","sourceOffset":11270},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Mean-zero Riesz pairings","sourceOffset":13416},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weak limits and supports","sourceOffset":17314},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Flatness and regular dyadic cells","sourceOffset":22122},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof map","sourceOffset":26661},{"kind":"section","title":"Analytic oscillation and flat seeds","sourceOffset":29934},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Packing the oscillation tests","sourceOffset":30910},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Flat descendants outside a Carleson family","sourceOffset":38358},{"kind":"subsubsection","title":"The local annular alternative","sourceOffset":39570}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $d\\ge4$ and $2\\le n\\le d-2$ be integers. Let $\\mu$ be an $n$-Ahlfors--David regular Radon measure on $\\R^d$ satisfying \\eqref{eq:riesz-bound}. Then $\\mu$ is uniformly $n$-rectifiable in the sense of Definition \\ref{def:ur}. Its constants $\\theta$ and $M$ can be chosen in terms of $d,n,C_{\\rm AD}$ and $C_{\\rm R}$ alone.","sourceOffset":3048,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Regular dyadic cells","label":"prop:dyadic-lattice","text":"If $D_E>0$, there are a common $\\mu$-null set $N\\subset E$ and families $\\mathcal D_k$ of Borel cells in $E\\setminus N$ such that: \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\roman*)] \\item each $\\mathcal D_k$ partitions $E\\setminus N$, and cells of different generations are disjoint or nested; \\item writing $\\ell(Q)=2^{-k}$ for $Q\\in\\mathcal D_k$, there are centers $z_Q\\in Q$ and constants $0<c_0\\le1/2$, $C_0\\ge2$ for which \\[ (E\\setminus N)\\cap B(z_Q,c_0\\ell(Q))\\subset Q \\subset E\\cap B(z_Q,C_0\\ell(Q)),\\qquad c_1\\ell(Q)^n\\le\\mu(Q)\\le C_1\\ell(Q)^n; \\] \\item every non-top cell has one parent, and every cell has finitely many children. When $D_E=\\infty$, all integer generations are available. When $D_E<\\infty$, there is a largest scale $\\ell_0$ with $D_E\\le\\ell_0<2D_E$, and finitely many cells at that scale. \\end{enumerate} All constants depend only on $d,n,C_{\\rm AD}$.","sourceOffset":23275,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Ahlfors--David regularity","label":"def:ad","text":"A Radon measure $\\mu$ on $\\R^d$ is $n$-Ahlfors--David regular with constant $C_{\\rm AD}\\ge1$ if \\[ C_{\\rm AD}^{-1}r^n\\le\\mu(B(x,r))\\le C_{\\rm AD}r^n \\qquad(x\\in E,\\quad 0<r\\le D_E). \\] We call these positive radii admissible. The inequalities have no instances when $D_E=0$.","sourceOffset":1372,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Uniform rectifiability","label":"def:ur","text":"An $n$-Ahlfors--David regular measure $\\mu$ on $\\R^d$ is uniformly $n$-rectifiable if there are $\\theta>0$ and $M<\\infty$ such that, for every $x\\in E$ and $0<r\\le D_E$, there is an $M$-Lipschitz map \\[ g:B_{\\R^n}(0,r)\\longrightarrow\\R^d, \\qquad \\mu\\bigl(B(x,r)\\cap g(B_{\\R^n}(0,r))\\bigr)\\ge\\theta r^n. \\] The constants are chosen before $x$ and $r$.","sourceOffset":2612,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/081.md","scopeText":"The formalized result proves that bounded Riesz transforms force quantitative uniform rectifiability in higher codimension. For $d\\ge4$ and $2\\le n\\le d-2$, an $n$-Ahlfors–David regular measure whose positive hard truncations have one uniform $L^2$ operator bound has big pieces of Lipschitz images. The mass fraction and Lipschitz constant depend only on the dimensions and the stated regularity and operator bounds, and work at every support point and admissible radius, including unbounded support. The variation and principal-value corollary is not included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/081.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":180553,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Riesz-transforms-and-uniform-rectifiability-in-higher-codimension-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"082-01","slug":"Annular-variation-of-the-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-October-5-2026","familyId":"082","title":"Annular variation of the triangular Hilbert transform at the symmetric point","abstract":"We prove the annular r-variation estimate for the triangular Hilbert transform from complex $L^3\\times L^3$ to L3/2 for every r > 2. The partitions may depend on the output point and range over all positive scales. The estimate yields the two-endpoint maximal bound and joint almost-everywhere and norm principal values, and resolves the symmetric scalar triangular Hilbert transform problem.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves maximal and annular r-variation bounds, for every r > 2, from complex $L^3(\\mathbb R^2)\\times L^3(\\mathbb R^2)$ to $L^{3/2}(\\mathbb R^2)$. The maximal estimate controls both hard truncation endpoints and gives almost-everywhere and norm convergence. Pairing with a third input settles the triangular Hilbert transform estimate at the symmetric $L^3\\times L^3\\times L^3$ point.","pdfPath":"preprints/Annular-variation-of-the-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-October-5-2026/annular-variation.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Annular-variation-of-the-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-October-5-2026/annular-variation.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Annular-variation-of-the-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-October-5-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/Annular-variation-of-the-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Annular-variation-of-the-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Annular-variation-of-the-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-October-5-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Annular-variation-of-the-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-October-5-2026/build/sections/matrix.tex","preprints/Annular-variation-of-the-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-October-5-2026/build/sections/heat.tex","preprints/Annular-variation-of-the-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-October-5-2026/build/sections/smooth.tex","preprints/Annular-variation-of-the-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-October-5-2026/build/sections/frequency.tex","preprints/Annular-variation-of-the-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-October-5-2026/build/sections/rough.tex","preprints/Annular-variation-of-the-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-October-5-2026/build/sections/completion.tex","preprints/Annular-variation-of-the-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-October-5-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Annular-variation-of-the-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the annular $r$-variation estimate for the triangular Hilbert transform from complex $L^3\\times L^3$ to $L^{3/2}$ for every $r>2$. The partitions may depend on the output point and range over all positive scales. The estimate yields the two-endpoint maximal bound and joint almost-everywhere and norm principal values, and resolves the symmetric scalar triangular Hilbert transform problem.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":462},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and related work","sourceOffset":3187},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The count estimate and the new ingredients","sourceOffset":6167},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":9805},{"kind":"section","title":"Truncations, coordinates, and maximal averages","sourceOffset":10486},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The triangular coordinates","sourceOffset":12540},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Maximal averages on a line","sourceOffset":14507},{"kind":"section","title":"Dimension-free matrix inequalities","sourceOffset":17153},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The energy and its quadratic costs","sourceOffset":19327},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Changes that alter the rank","sourceOffset":26545},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The cyclic product","sourceOffset":28273},{"kind":"section","title":"Gaussian heat dissipation","sourceOffset":33644}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"corollary","name":"Joint bilinear principal values","label":"cor:bilinear-pv","text":"For every complex $F,G\\in L^3(\\R^2)$, the joint limit \\[ B(F,G)=\\lim_{\\substack{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\\\R\\uparrow\\infty}} B_{\\varepsilon,R}(F,G) \\] exists almost everywhere and in $L^p(\\R^2)$, and \\[ \\|B(F,G)\\|_p\\le C_M\\|F\\|_3\\|G\\|_3. \\] More precisely, the entire tail converges in the maximal sense \\begin{equation} \\left\\|\\sup_{\\substack{0<\\varepsilon<1/n\\\\R>n}} |B_{\\varepsilon,R}(F,G)-B(F,G)|\\right\\|_p \\longrightarrow0. \\end{equation} The operator $B$ is the unique bounded complex bilinear extension of the principal-value operator on compactly supported smooth inputs.","sourceOffset":102844,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Flat scalar principal values","label":"cor:scalar-pv","text":"For every complex $L^3$ triple, each finite flat truncation is absolutely integrable, and \\[ \\sup_{0<\\varepsilon<R<\\infty} |\\mathcal L_{\\varepsilon,R}(F_0,F_1,F_2)| \\le C_M\\prod_{v=0}^2\\|F_v\\|_3. \\] Its joint scalar principal value exists and equals $\\int_{\\R^2}F_0 B(F_1,F_2)$. This limiting form is the unique bounded complex trilinear extension of its compactly supported smooth definition.","sourceOffset":105930,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/082.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":109520,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Annular-variation-of-the-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Annular-variation-of-the-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode.tex","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Annular-variation-of-the-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex.tex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"082-02","slug":"An-L3-bound-for-the-dyadic-triangular-Hilbert-form-October-5-2026","familyId":"082","title":"An L³ bound for the dyadic triangular Hilbert form","abstract":"We prove a uniform $L^3\\times L^3\\times L^3$ estimate for the dyadic triangular Hilbert form with unrestricted real inputs. The sum of the absolute local contributions over any finite set of scales is bounded by forty times the product of the input norms. In particular, the bound allows coefficients of modulus at most one to vary independently among admissible interval triples.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves maximal and annular r-variation bounds, for every r > 2, from complex $L^3(\\mathbb R^2)\\times L^3(\\mathbb R^2)$ to $L^{3/2}(\\mathbb R^2)$. The maximal estimate controls both hard truncation endpoints and gives almost-everywhere and norm convergence. Pairing with a third input settles the triangular Hilbert transform estimate at the symmetric $L^3\\times L^3\\times L^3$ point.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-dyadic-triangular-Hilbert-form-October-5-2026/dyadic-triangular-hilbert.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-dyadic-triangular-Hilbert-form-October-5-2026/dyadic-triangular-hilbert.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-dyadic-triangular-Hilbert-form-October-5-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-dyadic-triangular-Hilbert-form-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-dyadic-triangular-Hilbert-form-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-dyadic-triangular-Hilbert-form-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-dyadic-triangular-Hilbert-form-October-5-2026/build/sections/history.tex","preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-dyadic-triangular-Hilbert-form-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-matrix.tex","preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-dyadic-triangular-Hilbert-form-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-trace.tex","preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-dyadic-triangular-Hilbert-form-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-dyadic.tex","preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-dyadic-triangular-Hilbert-form-October-5-2026/build/figures/children.tex","preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-dyadic-triangular-Hilbert-form-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-summation.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-dyadic-triangular-Hilbert-form-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a uniform \\(L^3\\times L^3\\times L^3\\) estimate for the dyadic triangular Hilbert form with unrestricted real inputs. The sum of the absolute local contributions over any finite set of scales is bounded by forty times the product of the input norms. In particular, the bound allows coefficients of modulus at most one to vary independently among admissible interval triples.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":428},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and relation to earlier work","sourceOffset":2478},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The energy argument","sourceOffset":5798},{"kind":"section","title":"The matrix energy and its curvature","sourceOffset":7825},{"kind":"section","title":"A triangular trace estimate","sourceOffset":12973},{"kind":"section","title":"Energy increase under a dyadic split","sourceOffset":18038},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Matrices for a local integral","sourceOffset":18363},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The four children","sourceOffset":19582},{"kind":"section","title":"Summation and passage to measurable inputs","sourceOffset":23146}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every finite \\(S\\subset\\Z\\) and all real bounded compactly supported measurable functions \\(F_0,F_1,F_2\\) on \\(\\R_+^2\\), \\begin{equation} \\sum_{k\\in S}\\sum_{\\mathbf I\\in\\cA_k} \\abs{L_{\\mathbf I}(F_0,F_1,F_2)} \\le 40\\prod_{v=0}^2\\norm{F_v}_{L^3(\\R_+^2)}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":1791,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"A midpoint gap","label":"cor:gap","text":"If \\(T\\ge0\\) and \\(U=U^*\\) satisfy \\(-T\\le U\\le T\\), then \\(U\\) is supported on \\(T\\) and \\begin{equation} \\frac{\\Phi(T+U)+\\Phi(T-U)}2-\\Phi(T) \\ge\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt2}b_T(U). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":12128,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/082.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":28950,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-L3-bound-for-the-dyadic-triangular-Hilbert-form-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"082-03","slug":"The-maximal-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-September-24-2026","familyId":"082","title":"The maximal triangular Hilbert transform at the symmetric point","abstract":"For arbitrary complex inputs in $L^3(\\mathbb R^2)$, we prove the pointwise maximal $L^3\\times L^3\\to L^{3/2}$ estimate for the triangular Hilbert transform, with the supremum over both hard truncation endpoints. The estimate yields joint almost-everywhere and L3/2 convergence as the lower endpoint tends to zero and the upper endpoint tends to infinity. This also proves the conjectured scalar estimate at the symmetric point.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves maximal and annular r-variation bounds, for every r > 2, from complex $L^3(\\mathbb R^2)\\times L^3(\\mathbb R^2)$ to $L^{3/2}(\\mathbb R^2)$. The maximal estimate controls both hard truncation endpoints and gives almost-everywhere and norm convergence. Pairing with a third input settles the triangular Hilbert transform estimate at the symmetric $L^3\\times L^3\\times L^3$ point.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-maximal-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-maximal-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-maximal-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-September-24-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/The-maximal-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-maximal-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-maximal-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-maximal-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-September-24-2026/build/sections/matrix.tex","preprints/The-maximal-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-September-24-2026/build/sections/trace.tex","preprints/The-maximal-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-September-24-2026/build/figures/triangle.tex","preprints/The-maximal-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-September-24-2026/build/sections/heat.tex","preprints/The-maximal-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-September-24-2026/build/sections/jumps.tex","preprints/The-maximal-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-September-24-2026/build/figures/masks.tex","preprints/The-maximal-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-September-24-2026/build/sections/maximal.tex","preprints/The-maximal-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-September-24-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-maximal-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For arbitrary complex inputs in $L^3(\\R^2)$, we prove the pointwise maximal $L^3\\times L^3\\to L^{3/2}$ estimate for the triangular Hilbert transform, with the supremum over both hard truncation endpoints. The estimate yields joint almost-everywhere and $L^{3/2}$ convergence as the lower endpoint tends to zero and the upper endpoint tends to infinity. This also proves the conjectured scalar estimate at the symmetric point.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":488},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Previous work","sourceOffset":2684},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":5482},{"kind":"section","title":"A matrix energy and its Hessian","sourceOffset":7660},{"kind":"section","title":"A mixed trace inequality","sourceOffset":14251},{"kind":"section","title":"Gaussian heat dissipation","sourceOffset":22323},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Differentiating the star energies","sourceOffset":25596},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparing neighbors and integrating centers","sourceOffset":28615},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The initial energy","sourceOffset":34228},{"kind":"section","title":"Output-dependent truncations and energy jumps","sourceOffset":36723},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Maximal averages on a line","sourceOffset":37404},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Changing a matrix entry","sourceOffset":41256}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is an absolute constant $C$ such that, for every pair of complex functions $F,G\\in L^3(\\R^2)$, \\[ \\|B_*(F,G)\\|_{L^{3/2}(\\R^2)} \\le C\\|F\\|_{L^3(\\R^2)}\\|G\\|_{L^3(\\R^2)}. \\] The supremum is over both finite hard truncation endpoints.","sourceOffset":1061,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Joint bilinear principal values","label":"thm:bilinear-pv","text":"For all complex $F,G\\in L^3(\\R^2)$, the joint limit \\[ B(F,G)=\\lim_{\\substack{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\\\R\\uparrow\\infty}} B_{\\varepsilon,R}(F,G) \\] exists almost everywhere and in $L^{3/2}(\\R^2)$, with $\\|B(F,G)\\|_{3/2}\\le C_M\\|F\\|_3\\|G\\|_3$. More precisely, \\begin{equation} \\left\\|\\sup_{\\substack{0<\\varepsilon<1/n\\\\R>n}} |B_{\\varepsilon,R}(F,G)-B(F,G)|\\right\\|_{3/2} \\longrightarrow0. \\end{equation} The limit is the unique bounded bilinear extension of the principal-value operator on compactly supported smooth inputs.","sourceOffset":71689,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/082.md","scopeText":"The formalized result proves the symmetric maximal estimate for the triangular Hilbert transform: for arbitrary complex $F,G\\in L^3(\\mathbb R^2)$, the $L^{3/2}$ norm of the supremum over all finite hard-truncation intervals is at most $C\\|F\\|_3\\|G\\|_3$ for one absolute constant $C$. It also establishes a common full-measure set on which the truncated integrals are defined and almost-everywhere measurability of the maximal output.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/082.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":77876,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-maximal-triangular-Hilbert-transform-at-the-symmetric-point-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"083-01","slug":"A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026","familyId":"083","title":"A uniform Hilbert transform estimate for Lipschitz directions","abstract":"We prove a uniform L2 bound for the Hilbert transform along a Lipschitz unit vector field in the plane, with integration restricted to a fixed absolute multiple of the reciprocal Lipschitz seminorm. The bound is uniform in the inner truncation and holds for fields depending on both coordinates. This gives an affirmative answer to Stein's weak-$(2,2)$ conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves a uniform strong L2 bound for the planar Hilbert transform along any Lipschitz unit vector field, at integration lengths bounded by an absolute multiple of its reciprocal Lipschitz constant. The estimate is uniform over inner truncations and yields an L2-bounded principal-value operator, establishing Stein's weak-type conjecture at this short scale.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-reductions.tex","preprints/A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-ordinary-tiles.tex","preprints/A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-popular-forest.tex","preprints/A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-concentration.tex","preprints/A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-separated-count.tex","preprints/A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-finite-phase.tex","preprints/A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026/build/sections/08-hierarchy.tex","preprints/A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026/build/figures/shifted-blocks.tex","preprints/A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026/build/sections/09-phase-prediction.tex","preprints/A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026/build/figures/phase-scales.tex","preprints/A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026/build/sections/10-residuals.tex","preprints/A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026/build/sections/11-assembly.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a uniform \\(L^2\\) bound for the Hilbert transform along a Lipschitz unit vector field in the plane, with integration restricted to a fixed absolute multiple of the reciprocal Lipschitz seminorm. The bound is uniform in the inner truncation and holds for fields depending on both coordinates. This gives an affirmative answer to Stein's weak-\\((2,2)\\) conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Short transforms and band reduction","sourceOffset":445},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The problem and its predecessors","sourceOffset":2573},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The analytic and geometric reductions","sourceOffset":7751},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The phase gain and its iteration","sourceOffset":10010},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization, inputs and uniformity","sourceOffset":13520},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fourier conventions and the local reduction","sourceOffset":15313},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A maximal theorem and elementary analytic tools","sourceOffset":15944},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The local slope operator and its endpoints","sourceOffset":18551},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The band theorem and summation of amplitude levels","sourceOffset":22531},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A Lipschitz commutator","sourceOffset":25842},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Square-function reassembly","sourceOffset":35519},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removing long and very short times","sourceOffset":38876}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are absolute constants \\(a_*\\in(0,1/2)\\) and \\(C_*<\\infty\\) such that every Lipschitz field \\(v:\\R^2\\to S^1\\) with \\(\\Lip(v)\\le1\\) satisfies \\[ \\sup_{0<\\epsilon<a_*} \\norm{H_{v,a_*}^{\\epsilon}f}_{L^2(\\R^2)} \\le C_*\\norm{f}_{L^2(\\R^2)} \\qquad(f\\in\\mathcal S(\\R^2)). \\] Its symmetric principal value therefore satisfies \\[ \\norm{H_{v,a_*}f}_2\\le C_*\\norm f_2, \\qquad \\big|\\{x:|H_{v,a_*}f(x)|>\\lambda\\}\\big| \\le C_*^2\\lambda^{-2}\\norm f_2^2 \\quad(\\lambda>0). \\]","sourceOffset":1055,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"red:band-implies-main","text":"The uniform restricted estimate in \\Cref{thm:band-restricted} implies \\Cref{thm:main}.","sourceOffset":37759,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"red:packets","text":"A tile \\(s\\) has a length \\(\\ell_s\\in\\mathcal L\\), a bounded slope \\(\\theta_s\\), a center \\(z_s=(x_s,y_s)\\), and precision \\(d_s=w/\\ell_s\\). Its box is \\[ R_s=\\left\\{(x,y): |x-x_s|\\le\\frac{\\ell_s}{2},\\quad |(y-y_s)-\\theta_s(x-x_s)|\\le\\frac W2\\right\\}, \\qquad |R_s|=\\ell_s W. \\] A \\(t\\)-box is defined by the same formula with slope \\(t\\). The nominal horizontal length need not be the Euclidean long side when \\(W>\\ell_s\\). At a fixed scale, slope labels have bounded multiplicity on every interval of length \\(d_s\\). For each scale and label, the centers lie in one translated box lattice \\[ z_0+\\{(","sourceOffset":52095,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/083.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves uniform $L^2$ bounds for short Hilbert transforms along Lipschitz unit vector fields in the plane, including fields depending on both coordinates. For Lipschitz constant $K>0$, every hard truncation with $0<\\varepsilon\\le R\\le1/(10^6K)$ has one universal $L^2$ bound on Schwartz functions, independent of the inner cutoff.\n\nAt a fixed short scale for $1$-Lipschitz fields, the formalization also gives principal-value and weak-$(2,2)$ estimates and bounded extensions to all of $L^2$. These results establish the stated short-scale form of Stein's question.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/083.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":308906,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":19,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-uniform-Hilbert-transform-estimate-for-Lipschitz-directions-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"084-01","slug":"The-geometric-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-October-5-2026","familyId":"084","title":"The geometric case of the Erdős similarity conjecture","abstract":"We prove the geometric-progression case of the Erdős similarity conjecture. For every fixed $q\\in(0,1)$ and every $\\eta\\in(0,1)$, we construct a compact set $E_{q,\\eta}\\subseteq[0,1]$ of measure greater than $1-\\eta$ containing no nontrivial affine copy of $\\{q^n:n\\ge1\\}$, for any translation and either sign of nonzero dilation. The set may depend on q; the result makes no simultaneous assertion for different ratios.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every fixed $q\\in(0,1)$, constructs compact subsets of $[0,1]$ with measure arbitrarily close to one containing no translated and nontrivially dilated copy of $\\{q^n:n\\ge1\\}$, with dilations of either sign. This resolves the geometric-progression case of the Erdős similarity conjecture for every ratio.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-geometric-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-October-5-2026/geometric-erdos-similarity.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-geometric-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-October-5-2026/geometric-erdos-similarity.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-geometric-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-October-5-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/The-geometric-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-geometric-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-geometric-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-history.tex","preprints/The-geometric-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-periodic.tex","preprints/The-geometric-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-windows.tex","preprints/The-geometric-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-October-5-2026/build/figures/window-block.tex","preprints/The-geometric-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-routing.tex","preprints/The-geometric-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-scales.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-geometric-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the geometric-progression case of the Erd\\H{o}s similarity conjecture. For every fixed $q\\in(0,1)$ and every $\\eta\\in(0,1)$, we construct a compact set $E_{q,\\eta}\\subseteq[0,1]$ of measure greater than $1-\\eta$ containing no nontrivial affine copy of $\\{q^n:n\\ge1\\}$, for any translation and either sign of nonzero dilation. The set may depend on $q$; the result makes no simultaneous assertion for different ratios.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":472},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and related work","sourceOffset":1599},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof mechanism","sourceOffset":5341},{"kind":"section","title":"A periodic hitting set and the global deduction","sourceOffset":8659},{"kind":"section","title":"Grids, preorder windows, and stable centers","sourceOffset":11229},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Assigning the windows","sourceOffset":12538},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Preserving earlier keys","sourceOffset":16737},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Separating the test points","sourceOffset":18537},{"kind":"section","title":"Random routing and independent tests","sourceOffset":20134},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The random tables","sourceOffset":20883},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exposing the center and routing local tests","sourceOffset":22732},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exact failure probability at a fixed scale","sourceOffset":26152}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $q\\in(0,1)$ and $\\eta\\in(0,1)$, there is a compact set $E_{q,\\eta}\\subseteq[0,1]$ with $m(E_{q,\\eta})>1-\\eta$ such that \\[ \\text{for every }x\\in\\R\\text{ and }s\\in\\R\\setminus\\{0\\}, \\quad x+sq^n\\notin E_{q,\\eta}\\text{ for some integer }n\\ge1. \\]","sourceOffset":996,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:periodic","text":"For every $p\\in(0,1)$, there is an open $1$-periodic set $H\\subseteq\\R$ with $\\rho(H)\\le6p$ such that \\begin{equation} \\text{for every }x\\in\\R\\text{ and }t\\in[1,2], \\quad x+tq^n\\in H\\text{ for some integer }n\\ge1. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":8994,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/084.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":37248,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-geometric-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"084-02","slug":"The-dyadic-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-September-25-2026","familyId":"084","title":"The dyadic case of the Erdős similarity conjecture","abstract":"We construct a compact subset of the unit interval, of measure arbitrarily close to one, that contains no affine copy of the dyadic sequence $\\{2^{-n}:n\\ge1\\}$. The conclusion holds for every translation and every nonzero real dilation, of either sign, proving the dyadic case of the Erdős similarity conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every fixed $q\\in(0,1)$, constructs compact subsets of $[0,1]$ with measure arbitrarily close to one containing no translated and nontrivially dilated copy of $\\{q^n:n\\ge1\\}$, with dilations of either sign. This resolves the geometric-progression case of the Erdős similarity conjecture for every ratio.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-dyadic-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-dyadic-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-dyadic-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-September-25-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/The-dyadic-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-dyadic-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-dyadic-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/sections/periodic.tex","preprints/The-dyadic-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/sections/windows.tex","preprints/The-dyadic-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/figures/windows.tex","preprints/The-dyadic-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/sections/routing.tex","preprints/The-dyadic-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/figures/routing.tex","preprints/The-dyadic-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/sections/tests.tex","preprints/The-dyadic-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/sections/scales.tex","preprints/The-dyadic-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/sections/repair.tex","preprints/The-dyadic-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/sections/global.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-dyadic-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a compact subset of the unit interval, of measure arbitrarily close to one, that contains no affine copy of the dyadic sequence $\\{2^{-n}:n\\ge1\\}$. The conclusion holds for every translation and every nonzero real dilation, of either sign, proving the dyadic case of the Erd\\H{o}s similarity conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":379},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Related results","sourceOffset":2022},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction","sourceOffset":5349},{"kind":"section","title":"The periodic hitting problem","sourceOffset":9278},{"kind":"section","title":"Separated index windows","sourceOffset":10995},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The tree and the window lengths","sourceOffset":11383},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier grid cells remain unchanged","sourceOffset":17510},{"kind":"section","title":"Random routing and independent tests","sourceOffset":20833},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The tables and the selected set","sourceOffset":21269},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conditioning at a center","sourceOffset":23389},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Routing trials through the first default","sourceOffset":25648},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Independent terminal tests","sourceOffset":30087}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $\\eta\\in(0,1)$, there is a compact set $E_\\eta\\subseteq[0,1]$ with $m(E_\\eta)>1-\\eta$ such that \\[ \\text{for every }x\\in\\R\\text{ and every }s\\in\\R\\setminus\\{0\\}, \\quad x+sD\\nsubseteq E_\\eta. \\] Equivalently, for every such $x,s$ there is an integer $n\\ge1$ with $x+s2^{-n}\\notin E_\\eta$.","sourceOffset":1373,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:raw-failure","text":"For every fixed stable center $x$, \\begin{equation} \\Pbb\\bigl(\\exists t\\in[1,2]\\ \\forall n\\in\\mathcal N: x+t2^{-n}\\notin B\\bigr)\\le2p. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":38920,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/084.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves the dyadic case of the Erdős similarity conjecture. For every $0<\\eta<1$, it constructs a compact set $E\\subset[0,1]$ of measure greater than $1-\\eta$ that contains no affine copy of $\\{2^{-n}:n\\ge1\\}$. Explicitly, for every translation $x$ and every nonzero real dilation $s$, some point $x+s2^{-n}$ lies outside $E$. Both signs of $s$ are included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/084.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":48607,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-dyadic-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"085-01","slug":"An-Endpoint-Gradient-Bound-for-the-Centered-Disk-Maximal-Operator-September-26-2026","familyId":"085","title":"An Endpoint Gradient Bound for the Centered Disk Maximal Operator","abstract":"We prove the endpoint gradient bound $\\|\\nabla Mf\\|_1\\le C\\|\\nabla f\\|_1$ for every real-valued $f\\in W^{1,1}(\\mathbb R^2)$, where $Mf(x)$ is the supremum of the averages of $|f|$ over disks centered at x, and C is an absolute constant. The maximal function belongs to $W^{1,1}_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\mathbb R^2)$ and has a globally integrable weak gradient. This gives a positive resolution of the planar centered-disk case of the endpoint question of Hajłasz and Onninen.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the planar centered-disk case of the Hajłasz–Onninen maximal-function regularity problem. For every real $f\\in W^{1,1}(\\mathbb R^2)$, the centered disk maximal function satisfies $\\|\\nabla Mf\\|_1\\le C\\|\\nabla f\\|_1$ with an absolute constant. It belongs locally to $W^{1,1}$ and has a globally integrable weak gradient.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-Endpoint-Gradient-Bound-for-the-Centered-Disk-Maximal-Operator-September-26-2026/article.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-Endpoint-Gradient-Bound-for-the-Centered-Disk-Maximal-Operator-September-26-2026/article.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-Endpoint-Gradient-Bound-for-the-Centered-Disk-Maximal-Operator-September-26-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/An-Endpoint-Gradient-Bound-for-the-Centered-Disk-Maximal-Operator-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/An-Endpoint-Gradient-Bound-for-the-Centered-Disk-Maximal-Operator-September-26-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/An-Endpoint-Gradient-Bound-for-the-Centered-Disk-Maximal-Operator-September-26-2026/build/sections/local.tex","preprints/An-Endpoint-Gradient-Bound-for-the-Centered-Disk-Maximal-Operator-September-26-2026/build/figures/tangent-disks.tex","preprints/An-Endpoint-Gradient-Bound-for-the-Centered-Disk-Maximal-Operator-September-26-2026/build/sections/prices.tex","preprints/An-Endpoint-Gradient-Bound-for-the-Centered-Disk-Maximal-Operator-September-26-2026/build/sections/labels.tex","preprints/An-Endpoint-Gradient-Bound-for-the-Centered-Disk-Maximal-Operator-September-26-2026/build/sections/assembly.tex","preprints/An-Endpoint-Gradient-Bound-for-the-Centered-Disk-Maximal-Operator-September-26-2026/build/sections/extension.tex","preprints/An-Endpoint-Gradient-Bound-for-the-Centered-Disk-Maximal-Operator-September-26-2026/build/appendices/analytic-tools.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-Endpoint-Gradient-Bound-for-the-Centered-Disk-Maximal-Operator-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the endpoint gradient bound $\\|\\nabla Mf\\|_1\\le C\\|\\nabla f\\|_1$ for every real-valued $f\\in W^{1,1}(\\R^2)$, where $Mf(x)$ is the supremum of the averages of $|f|$ over disks centered at $x$, and $C$ is an absolute constant. The maximal function belongs to $W^{1,1}_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\R^2)$ and has a globally integrable weak gradient. This gives a positive resolution of the planar centered-disk case of the endpoint question of Haj{\\l}asz and Onninen.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":505},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The signed estimate behind the theorem","sourceOffset":2264},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier endpoint results","sourceOffset":4005},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the finite-band estimate is proved","sourceOffset":6823},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From the finite-band estimate to a weak gradient","sourceOffset":13764},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":15456},{"kind":"section","title":"Cancellation at disks and three local bounds","sourceOffset":16276},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Disk moments and an affine cancellation","sourceOffset":20547},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One exceptional set for all radii","sourceOffset":25900},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cancellation of small error pieces","sourceOffset":33750},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The directional estimate","sourceOffset":36790},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The size estimate, including band endpoints","sourceOffset":45433}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is an absolute constant $C<\\infty$ such that, for every real-valued $f\\in W^{1,1}(\\R^2)$, the function $Mf$ is locally integrable, belongs to $W^{1,1}_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\R^2)$, and satisfies \\begin{equation} \\int_{\\R^2}|\\nabla Mf|\\,\\dd x \\le C\\int_{\\R^2}|\\nabla f|\\,\\dd x. \\end{equation} In particular, its distributional derivatives are represented by globally integrable functions.","sourceOffset":1325,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Signed finite-band estimate","label":"prop:finite-band","text":"There is an absolute constant $C$ such that, for every real $g\\in C_c^\\infty(\\R^2)$ and $0<a\\le b$ with $b/a$ a power of two, including one, \\begin{equation} \\int_{\\R^2}|\\nabla S_{a,b}g|\\,\\dd x \\le C\\int_{\\R^2}|\\nabla g|\\,\\dd x. \\end{equation} The constant is independent of $a$, $b$, and the number of dyadic radius scales between them.","sourceOffset":2671,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/085.md","scopeText":"The centered disk maximal operator takes the supremum of averages of $|f|$ over disks centered at each point. The formalization proves that every real $f\\in W^{1,1}(\\mathbb R^2)$ has a maximal function that is finite almost everywhere, belongs to $W^{1,1}_{\\mathrm{loc}}$, and has a globally integrable weak gradient satisfying $\\|\\nabla Mf\\|_1\\le C\\|\\nabla f\\|_1$ for one absolute constant $C$.\n\nThe earlier signed finite-band estimate for smooth compactly supported functions is also retained. The general endpoint statement covers the Sobolev setting of the paper; a BV extension is outside these statements.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/085.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":141942,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-Endpoint-Gradient-Bound-for-the-Centered-Disk-Maximal-Operator-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"086-01","slug":"An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026","familyId":"086","title":"An L3 bound for the trilinear Hilbert transform","abstract":"We prove that the trilinear Hilbert transform with fixed slopes 1, 2, 3 maps $L^3(\\mathbb R)\\times L^3(\\mathbb R)\\times L^3(\\mathbb R)$ to $L^1(\\mathbb R)$, resolving this case of the trilinear Hilbert transform conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the principal-value trilinear Hilbert transform with shifts $x-t$, $x-2t$, $x-3t$ is bounded from $L^3(\\mathbb R)^3$ to $L^1(\\mathbb R)$. This resolves the L3 exponent case of the standard conjecture for slopes 1, 2, 3.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026","subjectId":"10","subject":"Real and complex analysis","subjectZh":"實分析與複分析","texPaths":["preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026/build/sections/outer.tex","preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026/build/sections/compression.tex","preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026/build/sections/charts.tex","preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026/build/sections/base.tex","preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026/build/sections/localization.tex","preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026/build/sections/sides.tex","preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026/build/sections/linearization.tex","preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026/build/sections/counting.tex","preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026/build/sections/completion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the trilinear Hilbert transform with fixed slopes $1,2,3$ maps $L^3(\\R)\\times L^3(\\R)\\times L^3(\\R)$ to $L^1(\\R)$, resolving this case of the trilinear Hilbert transform conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":258},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and notation","sourceOffset":3941},{"kind":"section","title":"Local forms and the outer reduction","sourceOffset":5374},{"kind":"section","title":"Budgets, private coordinates, and compression","sourceOffset":13619},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Quantitative conventions and stopping attempts","sourceOffset":13888},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Columns and inherited projections","sourceOffset":17396},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A binary-prefix estimate","sourceOffset":21600},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compression of local projection products","sourceOffset":24752},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rational heights and occupied scale ranges","sourceOffset":36154},{"kind":"section","title":"Quadratic charts and local detection","sourceOffset":41758},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Atoms and quantitative conventions","sourceOffset":42599},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A quantitative Weyl alternative","sourceOffset":47013}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a finite constant $C$ such that \\[ \\norm{T(f_1,f_2,f_3)}_{L^1(\\R)} \\le C\\prod_{j=1}^3\\norm{f_j}_{L^3(\\R)} \\qquad(f_1,f_2,f_3\\in\\mathcal S(\\R)). \\] Consequently $T$ has a unique bounded trilinear extension from $L^3(\\R)\\times L^3(\\R)\\times L^3(\\R)$ to $L^1(\\R)$.","sourceOffset":564,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform local estimate","label":"thm:local","text":"For some $q\\in(2,3)$, the constants $C_N(q)$ are bounded independently of $N$.","sourceOffset":7824,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Quadratic chart atom","label":"def:quadratic-atom","text":"A quadratic chart atom is a function \\begin{equation*} \\varphi(y)=e(\\lambda y^2+\\beta y) \\sum_{n\\in\\Z^d}\\chi(\\theta y-n) e\\bigl((\\theta y)^T C(\\theta y-n)\\bigr). \\tag{1} \\end{equation*} Here \\(\\theta\\in\\R^d\\), \\(\\lambda,\\beta\\in\\R\\), \\(C\\) is a rational \\(d\\)-by-\\(d\\) matrix, and \\(\\chi\\) is smooth and compactly supported. The case \\(d=0\\) includes ordinary quadratic phases. An overall unimodular constant factor is allowed. Every non-unimodular scalar multiplying an atom is either absorbed into \\(\\chi\\), and is then subject to its stated complexity bounds, or is written as an external coeffici","sourceOffset":43172,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":346449,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/An-L3-bound-for-the-trilinear-Hilbert-transform-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"087-01","slug":"The-symmetric-Mahler-conjecture-and-its-equality-cases-September-22-2026","familyId":"087","title":"The symmetric Mahler conjecture and its equality cases","abstract":"We resolve the symmetric Mahler conjecture positively, including its equality classification. Every origin-symmetric convex body in ℝn has volume product at least $4^n/n!$, with equality exactly for invertible linear images of Hanner polytopes.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the symmetric and nonsymmetric geometric Mahler conjectures in every dimension, with Hanner polytopes and simplices as the respective volume-product minimizers and all equality cases classified. The corresponding sharp functional Mahler inequalities also hold. For n ≥ 2, every symmetric polar product $K\\times K^\\circ$ in dimension $2n$ has Gromov width 4.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-symmetric-Mahler-conjecture-and-its-equality-cases-September-22-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-symmetric-Mahler-conjecture-and-its-equality-cases-September-22-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-symmetric-Mahler-conjecture-and-its-equality-cases-September-22-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/The-symmetric-Mahler-conjecture-and-its-equality-cases-September-22-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-symmetric-Mahler-conjecture-and-its-equality-cases-September-22-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-symmetric-Mahler-conjecture-and-its-equality-cases-September-22-2026/build/sections/convex-preliminaries.tex","preprints/The-symmetric-Mahler-conjecture-and-its-equality-cases-September-22-2026/build/sections/lens.tex","preprints/The-symmetric-Mahler-conjecture-and-its-equality-cases-September-22-2026/build/sections/holomorphic-mass.tex","preprints/The-symmetric-Mahler-conjecture-and-its-equality-cases-September-22-2026/build/sections/feasibility.tex","preprints/The-symmetric-Mahler-conjecture-and-its-equality-cases-September-22-2026/build/sections/simplex.tex","preprints/The-symmetric-Mahler-conjecture-and-its-equality-cases-September-22-2026/build/sections/zero-deficit.tex","preprints/The-symmetric-Mahler-conjecture-and-its-equality-cases-September-22-2026/build/sections/median.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-symmetric-Mahler-conjecture-and-its-equality-cases-September-22-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We resolve the symmetric Mahler conjecture positively, including its equality classification. Every origin-symmetric convex body in $\\R^n$ has volume product at least $4^n/n!$, with equality exactly for invertible linear images of Hanner polytopes.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":311},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and context","sourceOffset":2054},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":7150},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Functional Mahler inequality","sourceOffset":10251},{"kind":"section","title":"Polarity, norm sums, and Hanner polytopes","sourceOffset":14637},{"kind":"section","title":"A conformal lens with a uniform boundary coordinate","sourceOffset":18802},{"kind":"section","title":"Mass at an isolated holomorphic zero","sourceOffset":26384},{"kind":"section","title":"Feasible bases","sourceOffset":33691},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Boundary equalities for the lens","sourceOffset":39403},{"kind":"section","title":"The real simplex identity","sourceOffset":42066},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Approximation of an arbitrary convex body","sourceOffset":49906},{"kind":"section","title":"Equality and a common feasibility witness","sourceOffset":51800}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $n\\ge1$ and every origin-symmetric convex body $K\\subset\\R^n$, \\begin{equation} |K|\\,|K^\\circ|\\ge\\frac{4^n}{n!}. \\end{equation} Equality holds if and only if $K$ is a linear Hanner body.","sourceOffset":1689,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Even functional Mahler inequality","label":"cor:functional-mahler","text":"For every integer $n\\ge1$ and every even proper lower-semicontinuous convex function $\\varphi:\\R^n\\to\\R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$ such that $0<\\int_{\\R^n}e^{-\\varphi(x)}\\,dx<\\infty$, \\[ \\left(\\int_{\\R^n}e^{-\\varphi(x)}\\,dx\\right) \\left(\\int_{\\R^n}e^{-\\varphi^*(y)}\\,dy\\right)\\ge4^n. \\] The second integral is allowed to be infinite, in which case the inequality is immediate.","sourceOffset":10674,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/087.md","scopeText":"The symmetric Mahler conjecture predicts $|K||K^\\circ|\\ge4^n/n!$ for every origin-symmetric convex body $K\\subset\\mathbb R^n$. The formalization establishes this for every $n\\ge1$ and characterizes equality exactly by invertible linear images of Hanner bodies, built from intervals using Cartesian products and convex-hull joins.\n\nThe nonsymmetric Mahler conjecture and the functional inequalities are not included.\n\nThe general Mahler conjecture gives a sharp lower bound for the volume product of a convex body and its polar. For every $n\\ge1$ and compact convex body $K\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ with nonempty interior, the formalization proves\n$\\inf_{z\\in\\mathrm{int}\\,K}|K|\\,|(K-z)^\\circ|\\ge (n+1)^{n+1}/(n!)^2$.\nEquality holds exactly when $K$ is a simplex. The paper's functional inequality is outsid","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/087.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":70149,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-symmetric-Mahler-conjecture-and-its-equality-cases-September-22-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/The-symmetric-Mahler-conjecture-and-its-equality-cases-September-22-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/The-symmetric-Mahler-conjecture-and-its-equality-cases-September-22-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"087-02","slug":"The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026","familyId":"087","title":"The Mahler Conjecture for General Convex Bodies","abstract":"We resolve the Mahler conjecture for general convex bodies positively. For every convex body $K\\subset\\mathbb R^n$, n ≥ 1, with Santaló point $s(K)$, $|K|\\,|(K-s(K))^\\circ|\\ge (n+1)^{n+1}/(n!)^2$, with equality exactly for simplices.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the symmetric and nonsymmetric geometric Mahler conjectures in every dimension, with Hanner polytopes and simplices as the respective volume-product minimizers and all equality cases classified. The corresponding sharp functional Mahler inequalities also hold. For n ≥ 2, every symmetric polar product $K\\times K^\\circ$ in dimension $2n$ has Gromov width 4.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026/build/abstract.tex","preprints/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026/build/figures/proof-map.tex","preprints/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026/build/sections/01-cones.tex","preprints/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026/build/sections/02-projections.tex","preprints/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026/build/sections/03-entropy.tex","preprints/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026/build/sections/04-quadratic.tex","preprints/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026/build/sections/05-layers.tex","preprints/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026/build/sections/06-linear-equality.tex","preprints/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026/build/sections/09-consequences.tex","preprints/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026/build/sections/07-scalar.tex","preprints/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026/build/sections/08-segments.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We resolve the Mahler conjecture for general convex bodies positively. For every convex body $K\\subset\\mathbb R^n$, $n\\ge1$, with Santal\\'o point $s(K)$, $|K|\\,|(K-s(K))^\\circ|\\ge (n+1)^{n+1}/(n!)^2$, with equality exactly for simplices.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":286},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and context","sourceOffset":2069},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":4886},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Functional Mahler inequality","sourceOffset":10651},{"kind":"section","title":"Polarity, cone reduction, and the scalar input","sourceOffset":12758},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The center of polarity","sourceOffset":13241},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The cone formulation","sourceOffset":14848},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Scalar profiles and their identities","sourceOffset":17748},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The quantitative scalar inequalities","sourceOffset":19079},{"kind":"section","title":"Biased projections and simultaneous normalization","sourceOffset":24391},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The biased projection field","sourceOffset":24791},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Tails and layer integrals","sourceOffset":29732}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"General Mahler conjecture","label":"thm:mahler","text":"For every integer $n\\ge1$ and every convex body $K\\subset\\mathbb R^n$, \\[ P(K)\\ge\\frac{(n+1)^{n+1}}{(n!)^2}. \\] Equality holds if and only if $K$ is a simplex.","sourceOffset":1336,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Functional Mahler inequality","label":"cor:functional-mahler","text":"For every integer $n\\ge1$ and every proper lower-semicontinuous convex function $\\varphi:\\R^n\\to\\R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$ such that $0<\\int_{\\R^n}e^{-\\varphi(x)}\\,dx<\\infty$, \\[ \\left(\\int_{\\R^n}e^{-\\varphi(x)}\\,dx\\right) \\left(\\int_{\\R^n}e^{-\\varphi^*(y)}\\,dy\\right)\\ge e^n. \\] The second integral is allowed to be infinite, in which case the inequality is immediate.","sourceOffset":11059,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/087.md","scopeText":"The symmetric Mahler conjecture predicts $|K||K^\\circ|\\ge4^n/n!$ for every origin-symmetric convex body $K\\subset\\mathbb R^n$. The formalization establishes this for every $n\\ge1$ and characterizes equality exactly by invertible linear images of Hanner bodies, built from intervals using Cartesian products and convex-hull joins.\n\nThe nonsymmetric Mahler conjecture and the functional inequalities are not included.\n\nThe general Mahler conjecture gives a sharp lower bound for the volume product of a convex body and its polar. For every $n\\ge1$ and compact convex body $K\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ with nonempty interior, the formalization proves\n$\\inf_{z\\in\\mathrm{int}\\,K}|K|\\,|(K-z)^\\circ|\\ge (n+1)^{n+1}/(n!)^2$.\nEquality holds exactly when $K$ is a simplex. The paper's functional inequality is outsid","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/087.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":219346,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"087-03","slug":"Symplectic-Balls-in-Symmetric-Polar-Products-September-22-2026","familyId":"087","title":"Symplectic Balls in Symmetric Polar Products","abstract":"For every integer n ≥ 2 and every origin-symmetric convex body $K\\subset\\mathbb R^n$, we prove that the Gromov width of $\\mathop{\\mathrm{int}}\\nolimits K\\times\\mathop{\\mathrm{int}}\\nolimits K^\\circ$ is 4. We construct smooth symplectic embeddings of standard balls of every capacity $0\\lt c\\lt 4$ into this polar product. Volume preservation then resolves the symmetric Mahler conjecture positively in every dimension.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the symmetric and nonsymmetric geometric Mahler conjectures in every dimension, with Hanner polytopes and simplices as the respective volume-product minimizers and all equality cases classified. The corresponding sharp functional Mahler inequalities also hold. For n ≥ 2, every symmetric polar product $K\\times K^\\circ$ in dimension $2n$ has Gromov width 4.","pdfPath":"preprints/Symplectic-Balls-in-Symmetric-Polar-Products-September-22-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Symplectic-Balls-in-Symmetric-Polar-Products-September-22-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Symplectic-Balls-in-Symmetric-Polar-Products-September-22-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Symplectic-Balls-in-Symmetric-Polar-Products-September-22-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Symplectic-Balls-in-Symmetric-Polar-Products-September-22-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Symplectic-Balls-in-Symmetric-Polar-Products-September-22-2026/build/sections/history.tex","preprints/Symplectic-Balls-in-Symmetric-Polar-Products-September-22-2026/build/sections/ball.tex","preprints/Symplectic-Balls-in-Symmetric-Polar-Products-September-22-2026/build/sections/planar.tex","preprints/Symplectic-Balls-in-Symmetric-Polar-Products-September-22-2026/build/figures/lens-slices.tex","preprints/Symplectic-Balls-in-Symmetric-Polar-Products-September-22-2026/build/sections/realization.tex","preprints/Symplectic-Balls-in-Symmetric-Polar-Products-September-22-2026/build/sections/approximation.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Symplectic-Balls-in-Symmetric-Polar-Products-September-22-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every integer $n\\ge2$ and every origin-symmetric convex body $K\\subset\\R^n$, we prove that the Gromov width of $\\interior K\\times\\interior K^\\circ$ is $4$. We construct smooth symplectic embeddings of standard balls of every capacity $0<c<4$ into this polar product. Volume preservation then resolves the symmetric Mahler conjecture positively in every dimension.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":413},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and prior constructions","sourceOffset":2164},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction","sourceOffset":6578},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":9529},{"kind":"section","title":"Balls from holomorphic vanishing","sourceOffset":10006},{"kind":"section","title":"A planar coordinate and a uniform slice estimate","sourceOffset":20287},{"kind":"section","title":"Realization in a polar product","sourceOffset":35455},{"kind":"section","title":"Arbitrary convex bodies and consequences","sourceOffset":42568}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $n\\ge2$ and every origin-symmetric convex body $K\\subset\\R^n$, \\[ c_G(U_K)=4. \\] More precisely, for every $0<c<4$ there is a smooth symplectic embedding $B^{2n}(c)\\hookrightarrow U_K$.","sourceOffset":1135,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"A ball from a holomorphic zero","label":"thm:holomorphic-ball","text":"Let $n,m\\ge1$, let $\\Omega\\subset\\C^n$ be an open set containing $0$, and let $f=(f_1,\\ldots,f_m):\\Omega\\to\\C^m$ be holomorphic. Suppose that, for some integer $k\\ge1$, \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item $f^{-1}(0)=\\{0\\}$ and $|f(z)|=O(|z|^k)$ as $z\\to0$; \\item for every $0<s<1$, the set $\\{z\\in\\Omega:|f(z)|^2\\le s\\}$ is a compact subset of $\\Omega$. \\end{enumerate} Set \\[ \\tau=|f|^2,\\qquad V=\\{z\\in\\Omega:\\tau(z)<1\\}, \\qquad \\omega=\\ddc(|z|^2+\\tau). \\] For every $0<c<\\pi k$, there is a smooth symplectic embedding \\[ \\bigl(B^{2n}(c),\\omega_0\\bigr)\\longrightarrow(V,\\omega). \\]","sourceOffset":11576,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/087.md","scopeText":"The symmetric Mahler conjecture predicts $|K||K^\\circ|\\ge4^n/n!$ for every origin-symmetric convex body $K\\subset\\mathbb R^n$. The formalization establishes this for every $n\\ge1$ and characterizes equality exactly by invertible linear images of Hanner bodies, built from intervals using Cartesian products and convex-hull joins.\n\nThe nonsymmetric Mahler conjecture and the functional inequalities are not included.\n\nThe general Mahler conjecture gives a sharp lower bound for the volume product of a convex body and its polar. For every $n\\ge1$ and compact convex body $K\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ with nonempty interior, the formalization proves\n$\\inf_{z\\in\\mathrm{int}\\,K}|K|\\,|(K-z)^\\circ|\\ge (n+1)^{n+1}/(n!)^2$.\nEquality holds exactly when $K$ is a simplex. The paper's functional inequality is outsid","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/087.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":51867,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Symplectic-Balls-in-Symmetric-Polar-Products-September-22-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"088-01","slug":"Pettys-projection-volume-conjecture-in-dimensions-at-least-four-September-24-2026","familyId":"088","title":"Petty’s projection-volume conjecture in dimensions at least four","abstract":"We prove that ellipsoids uniquely minimize the volume of the projection body among convex bodies of fixed volume in every dimension at least four. This proves Petty's projection-volume conjecture in these dimensions.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Petty's projection-volume conjecture in the remaining dimensions n ≥ 4: ellipsoids uniquely minimize projection-body volume at fixed body volume. Also establishes the full Lutwak–Petty projection inequalities. In contrast, products of simplices exceed Brannen's proposed simplex maximum for normalized projection-body volume by an exponential factor in every sufficiently large dimension.","pdfPath":"preprints/Pettys-projection-volume-conjecture-in-dimensions-at-least-four-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Pettys-projection-volume-conjecture-in-dimensions-at-least-four-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Pettys-projection-volume-conjecture-in-dimensions-at-least-four-September-24-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Pettys-projection-volume-conjecture-in-dimensions-at-least-four-September-24-2026/build/latex/main.tex","preprints/Pettys-projection-volume-conjecture-in-dimensions-at-least-four-September-24-2026/build/latex/sections/01_introduction.tex","preprints/Pettys-projection-volume-conjecture-in-dimensions-at-least-four-September-24-2026/build/latex/sections/02_gauges.tex","preprints/Pettys-projection-volume-conjecture-in-dimensions-at-least-four-September-24-2026/build/latex/sections/03_spherical.tex","preprints/Pettys-projection-volume-conjecture-in-dimensions-at-least-four-September-24-2026/build/latex/sections/04_norm.tex","preprints/Pettys-projection-volume-conjecture-in-dimensions-at-least-four-September-24-2026/build/latex/sections/05_position.tex","preprints/Pettys-projection-volume-conjecture-in-dimensions-at-least-four-September-24-2026/build/latex/sections/06_conclusion.tex","preprints/Pettys-projection-volume-conjecture-in-dimensions-at-least-four-September-24-2026/build/latex/sections/07_consequences.tex","preprints/Pettys-projection-volume-conjecture-in-dimensions-at-least-four-September-24-2026/build/latex/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Pettys-projection-volume-conjecture-in-dimensions-at-least-four-September-24-2026/build/latex/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that ellipsoids uniquely minimize the volume of the projection body among convex bodies of fixed volume in every dimension at least four. This proves Petty's projection-volume conjecture in these dimensions.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":262},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Previous results and methods","sourceOffset":2265},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The argument","sourceOffset":4970},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Notation","sourceOffset":8702},{"kind":"section","title":"Gauges and projection measures","sourceOffset":9315},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Differentiating a gauge","sourceOffset":9597},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A volume inequality with its equality case","sourceOffset":14516},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The measure associated with a convex body","sourceOffset":16824},{"kind":"section","title":"A spherical sign estimate","sourceOffset":20616},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Harmonics and the spectral gap","sourceOffset":21015},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The sign-test operator","sourceOffset":24504},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Multipliers and passage to norms","sourceOffset":28025}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $n\\ge4$ and every convex body $K\\subset\\R^n$, \\begin{equation} \\frac{|\\Pi K|}{|K|^{n-1}} \\ge \\kappa_{n-1}^{\\,n}\\kappa_n^{\\,2-n}. \\end{equation} Equality holds if and only if $K=a+TB_2^n$ for some $a\\in\\R^n$ and some invertible linear map $T$.","sourceOffset":1795,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Brunn--Minkowski inequality","label":"thm:brunn-minkowski","text":"For every integer $d\\ge1$ and convex bodies $A,B\\subset\\R^d$, write $|\\cdot|$ for $d$-dimensional volume. For $0\\le t\\le1$, \\begin{equation} |(1-t)A+tB|^{1/d} \\ge (1-t)|A|^{1/d}+t|B|^{1/d}. \\end{equation} If $0<t<1$, equality holds if and only if $B=a+cA$ for some $a\\in\\R^d$ and $c>0$.","sourceOffset":14843,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:admissible-measure","text":"We call $\\eta$ admissible if its support spans $\\R^n$ and \\begin{equation} \\tau_\\eta(M)\\ge\\left(\\frac{|M|}{\\kappa_n}\\right)^{1/n} \\quad\\text{for every origin-symmetric convex body }M\\subset\\R^n. \\end{equation} The measure itself is not required to be symmetric.","sourceOffset":17325,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/088.md","scopeText":"Petty's projection-volume conjecture predicts\n\n$\\displaystyle \\frac{|\\Pi K|}{|K|^{n-1}}\\ge \\kappa_{n-1}^{n}\\kappa_n^{2-n},$\n\nwith equality exactly for ellipsoids; here $\\kappa_j$ is the volume of the Euclidean unit ball in dimension $j$. The formalization establishes this for every convex body $K\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ and every $n\\ge4$.\n\nOther inequalities in the projection-body family are not included.\n\nBrannen's simplex-maximization conjecture predicts that a simplex maximizes normalized projection-body volume $|\\Pi K|/|K|^{n-1}$ in dimension $n$. The formalized counterexample is the product of two ten-dimensional simplices. Its normalized projection volume, divided by that of a twenty-dimensional simplex, is\n\n$\\displaystyle \\frac{22{,}355{,}476}{22{,}020{,}096}>1.$\n\nThus the proposed maxim","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/088.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":90070,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Pettys-projection-volume-conjecture-in-dimensions-at-least-four-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Pettys-projection-volume-conjecture-in-dimensions-at-least-four-September-24-2026/build/latex/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"088-02","slug":"A-product-counterexample-to-the-simplex-maximum-for-projection-body-volume-September-24-2026","familyId":"088","title":"A product counterexample to the simplex maximum for projection-body volume","abstract":"The product of two ten-dimensional simplices has larger normalized projection-body volume than a twenty-dimensional simplex. This gives a counterexample to Brannen's proposed simplex maximum.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Petty's projection-volume conjecture in the remaining dimensions n ≥ 4: ellipsoids uniquely minimize projection-body volume at fixed body volume. Also establishes the full Lutwak–Petty projection inequalities. In contrast, products of simplices exceed Brannen's proposed simplex maximum for normalized projection-body volume by an exponential factor in every sufficiently large dimension.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-product-counterexample-to-the-simplex-maximum-for-projection-body-volume-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-product-counterexample-to-the-simplex-maximum-for-projection-body-volume-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-product-counterexample-to-the-simplex-maximum-for-projection-body-volume-September-24-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-product-counterexample-to-the-simplex-maximum-for-projection-body-volume-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-product-counterexample-to-the-simplex-maximum-for-projection-body-volume-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-product-counterexample-to-the-simplex-maximum-for-projection-body-volume-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"The product of two ten-dimensional simplices has larger normalized projection-body volume than a twenty-dimensional simplex. This gives a counterexample to Brannen's proposed simplex maximum.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":238},{"kind":"section","title":"Facet vectors and Cartesian products","sourceOffset":4811},{"kind":"section","title":"The simplex value","sourceOffset":11562},{"kind":"section","title":"The counterexample and exponential excess","sourceOffset":14388}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:counterexample","text":"Let \\(T_{10}=\\conv(0,e_1,\\ldots,e_{10})\\subset\\R^{10}\\), where the \\(e_i\\) are the coordinate unit vectors, and let \\(K=T_{10}\\times T_{10}\\subset\\R^{20}\\). Then \\[ \\frac{R_{20}(K)}{c_{20}} =\\frac{121\\binom{20}{10}}{21\\cdot2^{20}} =\\frac{22\\,355\\,476}{22\\,020\\,096}>1. \\] Thus \\(K\\) violates \\eqref{eq:proposed-bound} in dimension twenty.","sourceOffset":2693,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:product","text":"Let \\(A\\subset\\R^r\\) and \\(B\\subset\\R^s\\) be full-dimensional convex polytopes, where \\(r,s\\ge2\\). In the orthogonal product space \\(\\R^{r+s}=\\R^r\\times\\R^s\\), \\begin{equation} \\Pi(A\\times B)=(|B|\\Pi A)\\times(|A|\\Pi B). \\end{equation} In particular, \\begin{equation} R_{r+s}(A\\times B)=R_r(A)R_s(B). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":7384,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/088.md","scopeText":"Petty's projection-volume conjecture predicts\n\n$\\displaystyle \\frac{|\\Pi K|}{|K|^{n-1}}\\ge \\kappa_{n-1}^{n}\\kappa_n^{2-n},$\n\nwith equality exactly for ellipsoids; here $\\kappa_j$ is the volume of the Euclidean unit ball in dimension $j$. The formalization establishes this for every convex body $K\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ and every $n\\ge4$.\n\nOther inequalities in the projection-body family are not included.\n\nBrannen's simplex-maximization conjecture predicts that a simplex maximizes normalized projection-body volume $|\\Pi K|/|K|^{n-1}$ in dimension $n$. The formalized counterexample is the product of two ten-dimensional simplices. Its normalized projection volume, divided by that of a twenty-dimensional simplex, is\n\n$\\displaystyle \\frac{22{,}355{,}476}{22{,}020{,}096}>1.$\n\nThus the proposed maxim","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/088.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":19734,"sourceFileCount":2,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-product-counterexample-to-the-simplex-maximum-for-projection-body-volume-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"089-01","slug":"Planar-Graph-Metrics-Embed-into-L1-with-Constant-Distortion-September-23-2026","familyId":"089","title":"Planar Graph Metrics Embed into L1 with Constant Distortion","abstract":"We prove that every finite connected planar graph with arbitrary positive real edge lengths embeds into real L1 with a universal distortion bound. This resolves the planar embedding conjecture positively.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the planar and bounded-treewidth cases of the Gupta–Newman–Rabinovich–Sinclair conjecture. Shortest-path metrics of finite connected graphs with arbitrary positive edge lengths embed into real L1 with universal distortion for planar graphs, and distortion depending only on treewidth for bounded-treewidth graphs. The corresponding multicommodity flow–cut gaps are uniformly bounded.","pdfPath":"preprints/Planar-Graph-Metrics-Embed-into-L1-with-Constant-Distortion-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Planar-Graph-Metrics-Embed-into-L1-with-Constant-Distortion-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Planar-Graph-Metrics-Embed-into-L1-with-Constant-Distortion-September-23-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Planar-Graph-Metrics-Embed-into-L1-with-Constant-Distortion-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Planar-Graph-Metrics-Embed-into-L1-with-Constant-Distortion-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Planar-Graph-Metrics-Embed-into-L1-with-Constant-Distortion-September-23-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Planar-Graph-Metrics-Embed-into-L1-with-Constant-Distortion-September-23-2026/build/figures/column-realization.tex","preprints/Planar-Graph-Metrics-Embed-into-L1-with-Constant-Distortion-September-23-2026/build/figures/two-portals.tex","preprints/Planar-Graph-Metrics-Embed-into-L1-with-Constant-Distortion-September-23-2026/build/sections/sets.tex","preprints/Planar-Graph-Metrics-Embed-into-L1-with-Constant-Distortion-September-23-2026/build/sections/interpolation.tex","preprints/Planar-Graph-Metrics-Embed-into-L1-with-Constant-Distortion-September-23-2026/build/sections/scale-setup.tex","preprints/Planar-Graph-Metrics-Embed-into-L1-with-Constant-Distortion-September-23-2026/build/sections/main-packing.tex","preprints/Planar-Graph-Metrics-Embed-into-L1-with-Constant-Distortion-September-23-2026/build/figures/surviving-seeds.tex","preprints/Planar-Graph-Metrics-Embed-into-L1-with-Constant-Distortion-September-23-2026/build/sections/ordered.tex","preprints/Planar-Graph-Metrics-Embed-into-L1-with-Constant-Distortion-September-23-2026/build/sections/secondary.tex","preprints/Planar-Graph-Metrics-Embed-into-L1-with-Constant-Distortion-September-23-2026/build/sections/assembly.tex","preprints/Planar-Graph-Metrics-Embed-into-L1-with-Constant-Distortion-September-23-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Planar-Graph-Metrics-Embed-into-L1-with-Constant-Distortion-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every finite connected planar graph with arbitrary positive real edge lengths embeds into real $L_1$ with a universal distortion bound. This resolves the planar embedding conjecture positively.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":271},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cuts, multicommodity flow, and the embedding problem","sourceOffset":1503},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier results and the remaining obstacle","sourceOffset":3312},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and its main ideas","sourceOffset":6909},{"kind":"section","title":"Columns and thin bands","sourceOffset":13047},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Consequences of the circular order","sourceOffset":23064},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Portals and radial cells","sourceOffset":27292},{"kind":"section","title":"Threshold sets and local coordinates","sourceOffset":39343},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Charts on a fixed label space","sourceOffset":40285},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact gluing and component refinement","sourceOffset":44860},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The estimates required of a local update","sourceOffset":49832},{"kind":"section","title":"Interpolation by single-source distance functions","sourceOffset":52207}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a finite universal constant $C\\ge1$ with the following property. For every finite connected planar graph $G=(V,E)$ with strictly positive real edge lengths, there exist a measure space $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mu)$ and a map $f:V\\to L_1(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mu;\\R)$ such that \\[ d_G(x,y)\\le\\|f(x)-f(y)\\|_1\\le C d_G(x,y) \\qquad(x,y\\in V). \\]","sourceOffset":824,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Separation supplied by the main test","label":"main:pair-separation","text":"Let $(x,y)$ be an eligible pair satisfying the interior requirements, $1\\le d(x,y)\\le2$, and $|s(x)-s(y)|\\le E_A$. If either endpoint has no descending path, then, conditional on main-update mode, the independent cell coins give probability at least $1/4$ of system-A symmetric-difference separation at least $\\kappa_A E_A$ at this step.","sourceOffset":89112,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"geo:column-model","text":"A column model consists of a finite cyclically ordered set $V_0$ of indices, a copy $\\{i\\}\\times\\R$ of the height line for each $i\\in V_0$, and a finite collection of links. A link $ij$ has a prescribed closed bounded set $S_{ij}\\subset\\R$ of switch heights. At $H\\in S_{ij}$ we identify $(i,H)$ with $(j,H)$. The resulting quotient is denoted by $X$, and its height function by $h$. Paths consist of finitely many vertical segments and permitted switches; their length is the sum of the lengths of the vertical segments. The distance $d$ is the infimum of these lengths. The links are noncrossing if","sourceOffset":13697,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Radial cells","label":"geo:cell-definition","text":"Fix $D>0$ and an offset $\\theta\\in[0,D)$. Partition the indices into layers by assigning $i$ to the unique half-open interval \\[ [\\theta+nD,\\theta+(n+1)D),\\qquad n\\in\\mathbb Z, \\] containing $b_i$. Split each layer into the connected components of its induced subgraph of $G_J$. A cell is the image in $X_J$ of all intervals $\\{i\\}\\times J$ belonging to one such component. Its frontier $\\partial C$ is the union of their images for indices adjacent in $G_J$ to an index outside the cell.","sourceOffset":32371,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/089.md","scopeText":"The planar case of the Gupta–Newman–Rabinovich–Sinclair embedding problem asks for a universal distortion bound in $L_1$. The formalized result gives one constant $C$ for every finite connected planar graph with arbitrary positive real edge lengths: its weighted shortest-path metric embeds into real $L_1$ with distortion at most $C$. The constant is independent of the number of vertices and the ratios between edge lengths; singleton graphs are included.\n\nThe formalized result gives a uniform $L_1$ embedding bound for each bounded-treewidth class. For every bag-size bound $k\\ge2$, there is a constant $C(k)$ such that the weighted shortest-path metric of every nonempty finite connected graph with a tree decomposition of bag size at most $k$ embeds into finite-dimensional real $L_1$ with dist","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/089.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":153264,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":5,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Planar-Graph-Metrics-Embed-into-L1-with-Constant-Distortion-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"089-02","slug":"L1-Embeddings-of-Graphs-of-Bounded-Treewidth-September-23-2026","familyId":"089","title":"L1 Embeddings of Graphs of Bounded Treewidth","abstract":"For every fixed treewidth bound, the shortest-path metrics of finite connected graphs with arbitrary positive real edge lengths embed into real L1 with uniformly bounded distortion. This resolves the bounded-treewidth case of the Gupta–Newman–Rabinovich–Sinclair conjecture positively.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the planar and bounded-treewidth cases of the Gupta–Newman–Rabinovich–Sinclair conjecture. Shortest-path metrics of finite connected graphs with arbitrary positive edge lengths embed into real L1 with universal distortion for planar graphs, and distortion depending only on treewidth for bounded-treewidth graphs. The corresponding multicommodity flow–cut gaps are uniformly bounded.","pdfPath":"preprints/L1-Embeddings-of-Graphs-of-Bounded-Treewidth-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/L1-Embeddings-of-Graphs-of-Bounded-Treewidth-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/L1-Embeddings-of-Graphs-of-Bounded-Treewidth-September-23-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/L1-Embeddings-of-Graphs-of-Bounded-Treewidth-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/L1-Embeddings-of-Graphs-of-Bounded-Treewidth-September-23-2026/build/01-introduction.tex","preprints/L1-Embeddings-of-Graphs-of-Bounded-Treewidth-September-23-2026/build/02-cut-laws.tex","preprints/L1-Embeddings-of-Graphs-of-Bounded-Treewidth-September-23-2026/build/03-particles.tex","preprints/L1-Embeddings-of-Graphs-of-Bounded-Treewidth-September-23-2026/build/04-perturbations.tex","preprints/L1-Embeddings-of-Graphs-of-Bounded-Treewidth-September-23-2026/build/05-anchors.tex","preprints/L1-Embeddings-of-Graphs-of-Bounded-Treewidth-September-23-2026/build/06-lower-comparison.tex","preprints/L1-Embeddings-of-Graphs-of-Bounded-Treewidth-September-23-2026/build/figures/anchor-reservations.tex","preprints/L1-Embeddings-of-Graphs-of-Bounded-Treewidth-September-23-2026/build/07-conclusion.tex","preprints/L1-Embeddings-of-Graphs-of-Bounded-Treewidth-September-23-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/L1-Embeddings-of-Graphs-of-Bounded-Treewidth-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed treewidth bound, the shortest-path metrics of finite connected graphs with arbitrary positive real edge lengths embed into real $L_1$ with uniformly bounded distortion. This resolves the bounded-treewidth case of the Gupta--Newman--Rabinovich--Sinclair conjecture positively.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":366},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and prior work","sourceOffset":2448},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof: preserving local laws and transporting separation","sourceOffset":8576},{"kind":"section","title":"Cut measures and transport along a decomposition","sourceOffset":14743},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Separators and consistent bag laws","sourceOffset":16427},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A transport inequality for a common submeasure","sourceOffset":22595},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Threshold sweeps and height tests","sourceOffset":29774},{"kind":"section","title":"Particles and reservations","sourceOffset":34344},{"kind":"section","title":"Separating pairs in a common bag","sourceOffset":46386},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Retaining a scale for pairs in a common bag","sourceOffset":49753},{"kind":"section","title":"The anchor construction","sourceOffset":55954},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two independent sources of launch noise","sourceOffset":57766}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $k\\ge2$ there is a finite constant $C_k\\ge1$ with the following property. If a finite connected graph $G=(V,E)$ admits a tree decomposition with bags of size at most $k$, then, for every edge-length function $\\ell:E\\to(0,\\infty)$, there is a map $F$ from $V$ into a real $L_1$ space such that \\begin{equation} d_{G,\\ell}(u,v) \\le \\|F(u)-F(v)\\|_1 \\le C_k\\,d_{G,\\ell}(u,v) \\qquad(u,v\\in V). \\end{equation} The constant is independent of the number of vertices, the depth of the decomposition, and all ratios of edge lengths.","sourceOffset":1272,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Symmetric Markov flow bound { \\cite[Theorem 4.4","label":"thm:markov-flow","text":"{CKR}}] For every integer $w\\geq2$ there is a finite constant $C(w)\\geq1$ with the following property. Suppose a symmetric Markov flow graph has at most $w$ states per layer, first layer $\\{s_0,s_1\\}$, and last layer $\\{t_0,t_1\\}$, and its involutions interchange the two states in each endpoint layer. Every capacity-respecting flow from $s_0$ to $t_1$ has value at most \\[ C(w)\\Pr[X_0=s_0,\\ X_r=t_1]. \\] The constant is independent of the number of layers and of all transition probabilities.","sourceOffset":23546,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/089.md","scopeText":"The planar case of the Gupta–Newman–Rabinovich–Sinclair embedding problem asks for a universal distortion bound in $L_1$. The formalized result gives one constant $C$ for every finite connected planar graph with arbitrary positive real edge lengths: its weighted shortest-path metric embeds into real $L_1$ with distortion at most $C$. The constant is independent of the number of vertices and the ratios between edge lengths; singleton graphs are included.\n\nThe formalized result gives a uniform $L_1$ embedding bound for each bounded-treewidth class. For every bag-size bound $k\\ge2$, there is a constant $C(k)$ such that the weighted shortest-path metric of every nonempty finite connected graph with a tree decomposition of bag size at most $k$ embeds into finite-dimensional real $L_1$ with dist","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/089.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":99751,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/L1-Embeddings-of-Graphs-of-Bounded-Treewidth-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"090-01","slug":"An-atomic-certificate-for-triangular-lattice-universal-optimality-September-26-2026","familyId":"090","title":"An atomic certificate for triangular-lattice universal optimality","abstract":"We prove that the density-one triangular lattice minimizes the lower energy per particle for every nonnegative completely monotone function of squared distance, among all locally finite planar configurations of centered disk density one. The comparison includes infinite energies. The proof constructs sharp Gaussian Fourier minorants using an atomic interpolation certificate.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the triangular lattice minimizes the lower limit of energy per particle for every nonnegative completely monotone potential of squared distance among locally finite planar configurations of centered-disk density one. It also minimizes unit-background renormalized Riesz energies for $0\\lt s\\lt 2$ and Coulomb energy, resolving Sandier–Serfaty and the two-dimensional Brauchart–Hardin–Saff conjecture on the linear term of optimal spherical logarithmic energy.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-atomic-certificate-for-triangular-lattice-universal-optimality-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-atomic-certificate-for-triangular-lattice-universal-optimality-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-atomic-certificate-for-triangular-lattice-universal-optimality-September-26-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/An-atomic-certificate-for-triangular-lattice-universal-optimality-September-26-2026/build/main.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-atomic-certificate-for-triangular-lattice-universal-optimality-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the density-one triangular lattice minimizes the lower energy per particle for every nonnegative completely monotone function of squared distance, among all locally finite planar configurations of centered disk density one. The comparison includes infinite energies. The proof constructs sharp Gaussian Fourier minorants using an atomic interpolation certificate.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":423},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":3950},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":9575},{"kind":"section","title":"Energy from Gaussian minorants","sourceOffset":14696},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Duality and the lattice normalization","sourceOffset":16004},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A Fourier comparison using only centered density","sourceOffset":18406},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positive mixtures without a boundedness assumption at zero","sourceOffset":22001},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Lower limits, singular interactions, and attainment","sourceOffset":25498},{"kind":"section","title":"The Gaussian interpolation construction","sourceOffset":28049},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Spectral measures for the interpolation columns","sourceOffset":32562},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Schwartz convergence and the Fourier waves","sourceOffset":36893},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Jets and repeated nodes","sourceOffset":41445}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Universal energy minimum","label":"thm:universal","text":"For every smooth nonnegative completely monotone function $g$ on $(0,\\infty)$ and every locally finite planar set $\\mathcal C$ of centered disk density one, \\[ E_g(\\mathcal C)\\ge \\sum_{a\\in A\\setminus\\{0\\}}g(|a|^2)=E_g(A). \\] The comparison and the equality are in the extended nonnegative reals.","sourceOffset":1821,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Sharp Gaussian minorants","label":"thm:gaussian","text":"For every $\\alpha>0$ there is a real radial Schwartz function $f_\\alpha:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$ such that \\[ f_\\alpha(x)\\le k_\\alpha(x),\\qquad \\widehat f_\\alpha(\\xi)\\ge0 \\quad(x,\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^2), \\] and \\[ f_\\alpha(a)=k_\\alpha(a)\\quad(a\\in A\\setminus\\{0\\}),\\qquad \\widehat f_\\alpha(w)=0\\quad(w\\in A^*\\setminus\\{0\\}). \\] For $\\alpha\\ge1$, the construction uses two finite $20$-by-$20$ interpolation blocks and an absolutely summable infinite correction.","sourceOffset":2872,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/090.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves that the density-one triangular lattice minimizes lower energy per particle among all locally finite planar configurations of centered-disk density one, for every nonnegative completely monotone function of squared distance. Infinite energies are allowed in the comparison.\n\nIt also constructs sharp radial Schwartz minorants for every Gaussian potential: each minorant lies below the Gaussian, has nonnegative real Fourier transform, agrees with the Gaussian at nonzero triangular-lattice points, and vanishes on nonzero dual-lattice points after Fourier transformation. For the stated parameter range, the formalization includes the explicit atomic interpolation construction.\n\nThe planar Cohn–Elkies sharpness conjecture asks whether the two-point Fourier method attains t","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/090.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":102341,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-atomic-certificate-for-triangular-lattice-universal-optimality-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/An-atomic-certificate-for-triangular-lattice-universal-optimality-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"090-02","slug":"Universal-optimality-of-the-triangular-lattice-September-23-2026","familyId":"090","title":"Universal optimality of the triangular lattice","abstract":"We prove universal energy minimality for the triangular lattice in the plane. Among locally finite configurations of centered density one, it minimizes the lower limit of centered-ball energy averages for every nonnegative completely monotone function of squared distance, including when the energy is infinite. We also prove triangular minimality for planar logarithmic and Riesz renormalized energies, with $0\\lt s\\lt 2$ in the Riesz case, and the corresponding jellium minima. The proof uses sharp Gaussian Fourier bounds, positive mixtures, and heat-kernel comparison.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the triangular lattice minimizes the lower limit of energy per particle for every nonnegative completely monotone potential of squared distance among locally finite planar configurations of centered-disk density one. It also minimizes unit-background renormalized Riesz energies for $0\\lt s\\lt 2$ and Coulomb energy, resolving Sandier–Serfaty and the two-dimensional Brauchart–Hardin–Saff conjecture on the linear term of optimal spherical logarithmic energy.","pdfPath":"preprints/Universal-optimality-of-the-triangular-lattice-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Universal-optimality-of-the-triangular-lattice-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Universal-optimality-of-the-triangular-lattice-September-23-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Universal-optimality-of-the-triangular-lattice-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Universal-optimality-of-the-triangular-lattice-September-23-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/Universal-optimality-of-the-triangular-lattice-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-uniform-gaussian-theorem.tex","preprints/Universal-optimality-of-the-triangular-lattice-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-cardinal-fourier.tex","preprints/Universal-optimality-of-the-triangular-lattice-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-quadrature-finite-data.tex","preprints/Universal-optimality-of-the-triangular-lattice-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-exact-interpolation.tex","preprints/Universal-optimality-of-the-triangular-lattice-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-global-signs.tex","preprints/Universal-optimality-of-the-triangular-lattice-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-gaussian-energy-transfer.tex","preprints/Universal-optimality-of-the-triangular-lattice-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-shifted-mixtures.tex","preprints/Universal-optimality-of-the-triangular-lattice-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-renormalized-jellium.tex","preprints/Universal-optimality-of-the-triangular-lattice-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-arithmetic-verification.tex","preprints/Universal-optimality-of-the-triangular-lattice-September-23-2026/build/sections/09-conditional-floor-verification.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Universal-optimality-of-the-triangular-lattice-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove universal energy minimality for the triangular lattice in the plane. Among locally finite configurations of centered density one, it minimizes the lower limit of centered-ball energy averages for every nonnegative completely monotone function of squared distance, including when the energy is infinite. We also prove triangular minimality for planar logarithmic and Riesz renormalized energies, with $0<s<2$ in the Riesz case, and the corresponding jellium minima. The proof uses sharp Gaussian Fourier bounds, positive mixtures, and heat-kernel comparison.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":612},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The planar problem and its predecessors","sourceOffset":2892},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Gaussian route and the interpolation problem","sourceOffset":5933},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Antecedents of the method","sourceOffset":11507},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Renormalized and jellium consequences","sourceOffset":14440},{"kind":"section","title":"Cardinal interpolation and Fourier transformation","sourceOffset":17559},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Shells and interpolation nodes","sourceOffset":18368},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The normalized Gaussian theorem","sourceOffset":20071},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The sine product and its node coordinates","sourceOffset":22110},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A spectral realization of summable cardinal data","sourceOffset":24590},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Gaussian damping and the transformed kernel","sourceOffset":29642},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The jet operator and its distant outputs","sourceOffset":33727}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Universal energy minimality","label":"thm:universal","text":"Let $g:(0,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty)$ be smooth and completely monotone, meaning that $(-1)^r g^{(r)}(t)\\ge0$ for every integer $r\\ge0$ and every $t>0$. For every locally finite configuration $\\mathcal C\\subset\\R^2$ of centered disk density one, the following equality and inequality hold in the extended nonnegative reals: \\begin{equation} E_g(\\mathcal C)\\ge \\sum_{a\\in A\\setminus\\{0\\}}g(\\abs a^2)=E_g(A). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":1926,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Normalized Gaussian Fourier pair","label":"thm:normalized-pair","text":"For every finite real $k\\ge2.36$, put $T_k(s)=k^{-1}e^{-k(s-1)}$. There are entire functions $H_1,H_2$, real on the real axis, and a real radial Schwartz function $f$ on $\\R^2$ such that \\[ f(x)=e^{-\\pi h b\\abs x^2}H_1(b\\abs x^2),\\qquad \\widehat f(\\xi)=e^{-\\pi h b\\abs\\xi^2}H_2(b\\abs\\xi^2). \\] For every real $s\\ge0$, \\begin{equation} H_1(s)\\le T_k(s),\\qquad H_2(s)\\ge0. \\end{equation} At every $n\\in\\mathcal N$, the values and first derivatives satisfy \\begin{equation} \\begin{gathered} H_1(n)=T_k(n),\\qquad H_1'(n)=T_k'(n)=-e^{-k(n-1)},\\\\ H_2(n)=H_2'(n)=0. \\end{gathered} \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":20395,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/090.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":223759,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Universal-optimality-of-the-triangular-lattice-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"090-03","slug":"A-sharp-Fourier-certificate-for-planar-circle-packing-September-23-2026","familyId":"090","title":"A sharp Fourier certificate for planar circle packing","abstract":"We resolve the planar Cohn–Elkies sharpness conjecture: the two-point Fourier bound attains the optimal circle-packing density $\\pi/(2\\sqrt3)$. We construct a radial Schwartz certificate and prove its global sign conditions using rigorous interval arithmetic and analytic estimates. The same certificate recovers the classical uniqueness of the triangular packing among periodic equality cases.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the triangular lattice minimizes the lower limit of energy per particle for every nonnegative completely monotone potential of squared distance among locally finite planar configurations of centered-disk density one. It also minimizes unit-background renormalized Riesz energies for $0\\lt s\\lt 2$ and Coulomb energy, resolving Sandier–Serfaty and the two-dimensional Brauchart–Hardin–Saff conjecture on the linear term of optimal spherical logarithmic energy.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-sharp-Fourier-certificate-for-planar-circle-packing-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-sharp-Fourier-certificate-for-planar-circle-packing-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-sharp-Fourier-certificate-for-planar-circle-packing-September-23-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-sharp-Fourier-certificate-for-planar-circle-packing-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-sharp-Fourier-certificate-for-planar-circle-packing-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-sharp-Fourier-certificate-for-planar-circle-packing-September-23-2026/build/sections/fourier.tex","preprints/A-sharp-Fourier-certificate-for-planar-circle-packing-September-23-2026/build/sections/interpolation.tex","preprints/A-sharp-Fourier-certificate-for-planar-circle-packing-September-23-2026/build/sections/signs.tex","preprints/A-sharp-Fourier-certificate-for-planar-circle-packing-September-23-2026/build/figures/half-gaps.tex","preprints/A-sharp-Fourier-certificate-for-planar-circle-packing-September-23-2026/build/sections/normalization.tex","preprints/A-sharp-Fourier-certificate-for-planar-circle-packing-September-23-2026/build/sections/certificates.tex","preprints/A-sharp-Fourier-certificate-for-planar-circle-packing-September-23-2026/build/data/coefficients.tex","preprints/A-sharp-Fourier-certificate-for-planar-circle-packing-September-23-2026/build/data/W_plus.tex","preprints/A-sharp-Fourier-certificate-for-planar-circle-packing-September-23-2026/build/data/W_minus.tex","preprints/A-sharp-Fourier-certificate-for-planar-circle-packing-September-23-2026/build/sections/independent-certification.tex","preprints/A-sharp-Fourier-certificate-for-planar-circle-packing-September-23-2026/build/sections/rational-check.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-sharp-Fourier-certificate-for-planar-circle-packing-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We resolve the planar Cohn--Elkies sharpness conjecture: the two-point Fourier bound attains the optimal circle-packing density $\\pi/(2\\sqrt3)$. We construct a radial Schwartz certificate and prove its global sign conditions using rigorous interval arithmetic and analytic estimates. The same certificate recovers the classical uniqueness of the triangular packing among periodic equality cases.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":499},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fourier certificates and interpolation","sourceOffset":4015},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction","sourceOffset":6784},{"kind":"section","title":"Gaussian cardinal functions","sourceOffset":10474},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The cardinal family","sourceOffset":12875},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fourier transformation","sourceOffset":20380},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The coupled interpolation conditions","sourceOffset":24646},{"kind":"section","title":"Solving the interpolation equations","sourceOffset":26972},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform envelopes for the Fourier terms","sourceOffset":28841},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite certificates","sourceOffset":35543},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Inversion on the whole coefficient space","sourceOffset":37220},{"kind":"section","title":"Global signs","sourceOffset":42809}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Sharp Fourier certificate","label":"intro:main","text":"There is a real radial Schwartz function $f:\\RR^2\\to\\RR$ such that \\begin{equation} \\widehat f(0)=1,\\qquad f(0)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt3},\\qquad \\widehat f(\\xi)\\ge0\\quad(\\xi\\in\\RR^2),\\qquad f(x)\\le0\\quad(|x|\\ge1). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":1933,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Gaussian Fourier pairing","label":"fourier:transform","text":"The function \\(q_p\\) is real, radial, and Schwartz on \\(\\RR^2\\), and \\[ \\widehat{q_p}(\\xi)=e^{-\\pi hs}K_p(s),\\qquad s=b\\abs{\\xi}^2. \\] The function \\(K_p\\) is entire and real on the real axis. Moreover, \\begin{equation} \\operatorname{Im}z(t) =h\\left(\\frac{B}{t^2+h^2}-1\\right) \\ge\\frac{26}{435}>0\\qquad(-1\\le t\\le1). \\end{equation} Consequently every derivative of \\(K_p(s)\\) decays exponentially as \\(s\\to+\\infty\\).","sourceOffset":20839,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/090.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves that the density-one triangular lattice minimizes lower energy per particle among all locally finite planar configurations of centered-disk density one, for every nonnegative completely monotone function of squared distance. Infinite energies are allowed in the comparison.\n\nIt also constructs sharp radial Schwartz minorants for every Gaussian potential: each minorant lies below the Gaussian, has nonnegative real Fourier transform, agrees with the Gaussian at nonzero triangular-lattice points, and vanishes on nonzero dual-lattice points after Fourier transformation. For the stated parameter range, the formalization includes the explicit atomic interpolation construction.\n\nThe planar Cohn–Elkies sharpness conjecture asks whether the two-point Fourier method attains t","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/090.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":148539,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-sharp-Fourier-certificate-for-planar-circle-packing-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-sharp-Fourier-certificate-for-planar-circle-packing-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"090-04","slug":"Triangular-minimality-for-planar-Coulomb-renormalized-energy-September-23-2026","familyId":"090","title":"Triangular minimality for planar Coulomb renormalized energy","abstract":"We prove the Sandier–Serfaty conjecture: the triangular lattice of covolume one minimizes planar Coulomb renormalized energy over all admissible curl-free fields with a unit uniform background. Combined with Bétermin and Sandier's asymptotic formula, this also proves the Brauchart–Hardin–Saff conjecture for the linear term of optimal ordered-pair logarithmic energy on the unit two-sphere. The proof uses a direct Voronoi-cell comparison with rigorous interval arithmetic.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the triangular lattice minimizes the lower limit of energy per particle for every nonnegative completely monotone potential of squared distance among locally finite planar configurations of centered-disk density one. It also minimizes unit-background renormalized Riesz energies for $0\\lt s\\lt 2$ and Coulomb energy, resolving Sandier–Serfaty and the two-dimensional Brauchart–Hardin–Saff conjecture on the linear term of optimal spherical logarithmic energy.","pdfPath":"preprints/Triangular-minimality-for-planar-Coulomb-renormalized-energy-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Triangular-minimality-for-planar-Coulomb-renormalized-energy-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Triangular-minimality-for-planar-Coulomb-renormalized-energy-September-23-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Triangular-minimality-for-planar-Coulomb-renormalized-energy-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Triangular-minimality-for-planar-Coulomb-renormalized-energy-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Triangular-minimality-for-planar-Coulomb-renormalized-energy-September-23-2026/build/sections/history.tex","preprints/Triangular-minimality-for-planar-Coulomb-renormalized-energy-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-reduction.tex","preprints/Triangular-minimality-for-planar-Coulomb-renormalized-energy-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-geometry.tex","preprints/Triangular-minimality-for-planar-Coulomb-renormalized-energy-September-23-2026/build/figures/signed-sectors.tex","preprints/Triangular-minimality-for-planar-Coulomb-renormalized-energy-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-calibration.tex","preprints/Triangular-minimality-for-planar-Coulomb-renormalized-energy-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-tails.tex","preprints/Triangular-minimality-for-planar-Coulomb-renormalized-energy-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-computation.tex","preprints/Triangular-minimality-for-planar-Coulomb-renormalized-energy-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-conclusion.tex","preprints/Triangular-minimality-for-planar-Coulomb-renormalized-energy-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-scientific-support.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Triangular-minimality-for-planar-Coulomb-renormalized-energy-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Sandier--Serfaty conjecture: the triangular lattice of covolume one minimizes planar Coulomb renormalized energy over all admissible curl-free fields with a unit uniform background. Combined with B\\'etermin and Sandier's asymptotic formula, this also proves the Brauchart--Hardin--Saff conjecture for the linear term of optimal ordered-pair logarithmic energy on the unit two-sphere. The proof uses a direct Voronoi-cell comparison with rigorous interval arithmetic.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":553},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The energy and the main results","sourceOffset":1021},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and prior work","sourceOffset":6341},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the comparison works","sourceOffset":10258},{"kind":"section","title":"From square tori to the full plane","sourceOffset":15029},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local regularity and the puncture limit","sourceOffset":15459},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Approximation and local energy estimates","sourceOffset":17763},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Scaling and comparison of fixed-width cutoffs","sourceOffset":22494},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Periodic averaging and the harmonic component","sourceOffset":26506},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite tori and separated minimizing configurations","sourceOffset":32084},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Green representation and existence","sourceOffset":32843},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Separation of minimizers","sourceOffset":35111}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every cutoff family satisfying \\eqref{eq:cutoffs} and every field $E\\in\\mathcal A_1$, \\[ W(E)\\geq W(E_\\triangle). \\] The triangular value is finite, and $\\inf_{E\\in\\mathcal A_1}W(E)=W(E_\\triangle)$.","sourceOffset":3262,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:torus","text":"For every integer $n\\geq2$ and every configuration of $n$ distinct points on $T_n$, the potential in \\eqref{eq:torus-energy} satisfies \\[ \\mathcal W_n(h)\\geq nW(E_\\triangle). \\]","sourceOffset":4209,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/090.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":145936,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Triangular-minimality-for-planar-Coulomb-renormalized-energy-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Triangular-minimality-for-planar-Coulomb-renormalized-energy-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"091-01","slug":"The-logarithmic-Brunn-Minkowski-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"091","title":"The logarithmic Brunn–Minkowski conjecture","abstract":"We prove the logarithmic Brunn–Minkowski conjecture for arbitrary origin-symmetric convex bodies in every dimension. The theorem also gives the symmetric Lp Brunn–Minkowski inequality for every $0\\lt p\\lt 1$. Combined with Saroglou's transfer theorem and a support-subspace reduction, it yields the logarithmic inequality for every even log-concave Radon measure and the scalar-dilation $(B)$-conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the logarithmic Brunn–Minkowski inequality for origin-symmetric convex bodies in every dimension, and the scalar-dilation B-conjecture for all even log-concave Radon measures. For Lebesgue volume it also proves the additive Lp Brunn–Minkowski inequality for full-dimensional origin-symmetric convex bodies throughout $0\\lt p\\lt 1$.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-logarithmic-Brunn-Minkowski-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-logarithmic-Brunn-Minkowski-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-logarithmic-Brunn-Minkowski-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/The-logarithmic-Brunn-Minkowski-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-logarithmic-Brunn-Minkowski-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/The-logarithmic-Brunn-Minkowski-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/reduction.tex","preprints/The-logarithmic-Brunn-Minkowski-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figure-slabs.tex","preprints/The-logarithmic-Brunn-Minkowski-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/moment.tex","preprints/The-logarithmic-Brunn-Minkowski-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/identities.tex","preprints/The-logarithmic-Brunn-Minkowski-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/density.tex","preprints/The-logarithmic-Brunn-Minkowski-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/tensor.tex","preprints/The-logarithmic-Brunn-Minkowski-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/variance.tex","preprints/The-logarithmic-Brunn-Minkowski-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/measure.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-logarithmic-Brunn-Minkowski-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the logarithmic Brunn--Minkowski conjecture for arbitrary origin-symmetric convex bodies in every dimension. The theorem also gives the symmetric $L_p$ Brunn--Minkowski inequality for every $0<p<1$. Combined with Saroglou's transfer theorem and a support-subspace reduction, it yields the logarithmic inequality for every even log-concave Radon measure and the scalar-dilation $(B)$-conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":467},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and prior work","sourceOffset":3858},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":8474},{"kind":"section","title":"From a variance bound to volume","sourceOffset":12371},{"kind":"section","title":"Moment coordinates on the whole space","sourceOffset":18730},{"kind":"section","title":"Adjoints in moment coordinates","sourceOffset":26986},{"kind":"section","title":"Density of the even tests","sourceOffset":31492},{"kind":"section","title":"A tensor estimate for the divergence tests","sourceOffset":36958},{"kind":"section","title":"The variance inequality","sourceOffset":44526},{"kind":"section","title":"Volume and measure consequences","sourceOffset":48007},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The symmetric \\texorpdfstring{$L_p$}{Lp} volume inequality","sourceOffset":48252},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Even log-concave measures and the \\texorpdfstring{$(B)$}{(B)}-conjecture","sourceOffset":49769}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Even logarithmic Brunn--Minkowski inequality","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $n\\ge1$ and let $K,L\\subset\\R^n$ be convex bodies satisfying $K=-K$ and $L=-L$. For every $0\\le\\lambda\\le1$, \\begin{equation} \\left|\\mathcal W[h_K^{1-\\lambda}h_L^\\lambda]\\right| \\ge |K|^{1-\\lambda}|L|^\\lambda. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":1153,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Symmetric $L_p$ Brunn--Minkowski inequality","label":"cor:lp","text":"Let $n\\ge1$, let $K,L\\subset\\R^n$ be full-dimensional origin-symmetric convex bodies, and let $0<p<1$. For every $0\\le\\lambda\\le1$, \\begin{equation} \\left|\\mathcal W\\!\\left[ \\bigl((1-\\lambda)h_K^p+\\lambda h_L^p\\bigr)^{1/p} \\right]\\right|^{p/n} \\ge (1-\\lambda)|K|^{p/n}+\\lambda|L|^{p/n}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":2566,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/091.md","scopeText":"The logarithmic Brunn–Minkowski conjecture asserts that logarithmic interpolation of origin-symmetric convex bodies preserves the geometric-mean lower bound for volume. The formalized result proves, for every dimension $n\\ge1$, such bodies $K,L\\subset\\mathbb R^n$, and $0\\le t\\le1$, that their logarithmic Wulff combination has volume at least $|K|^{1-t}|L|^t$. It assumes neither boundary smoothness nor coordinatewise unconditionality.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/091.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":55438,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-logarithmic-Brunn-Minkowski-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"092-01","slug":"A-single-lattice-covering-bound-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026","familyId":"092","title":"A single-lattice covering bound of order n log n","abstract":"Every convex body in ℝn, n ≥ 2, admits a covering by translates along one full-rank lattice with density at most $Cn\\log n$, for an absolute constant C. No symmetry or boundary regularity is assumed.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Determines the optimal worst-case covering density as $\\Theta(n\\log n)$, for both lattice and unrestricted translative coverings. Every convex body in ℝn, n ≥ 2, admits a lattice covering of density at most $Cn\\log n$; centrally symmetric examples in every sufficiently large dimension require at least $cn\\log n$ even without the lattice restriction, for absolute $c,C\\gt 0$.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-single-lattice-covering-bound-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-single-lattice-covering-bound-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-single-lattice-covering-bound-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-single-lattice-covering-bound-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-single-lattice-covering-bound-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/overview.tex","preprints/A-single-lattice-covering-bound-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/preliminaries.tex","preprints/A-single-lattice-covering-bound-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sampler.tex","preprints/A-single-lattice-covering-bound-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/blocks.tex","preprints/A-single-lattice-covering-bound-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/hierarchy.tex","preprints/A-single-lattice-covering-bound-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/final.tex","preprints/A-single-lattice-covering-bound-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/figures/suffix-tree.tex","preprints/A-single-lattice-covering-bound-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-single-lattice-covering-bound-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every convex body in $\\R^n$, $n\\ge2$, admits a covering by translates along one full-rank lattice with density at most $Cn\\log n$, for an absolute constant $C$. No symmetry or boundary regularity is assumed.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":253},{"kind":"section","title":"Geometric and horizontal preparations","sourceOffset":6747},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite list of horizontal sections","sourceOffset":7344},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One lattice for all labels","sourceOffset":12300},{"kind":"section","title":"A uniform Boolean sampler","sourceOffset":14831},{"kind":"section","title":"Folded Gaussian blocks","sourceOffset":21664},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The folded distribution and its grid","sourceOffset":22841},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Usable anchors and sparse line maxima","sourceOffset":30747},{"kind":"section","title":"A hierarchy of branching lattice points","sourceOffset":40298},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The block sizes and the lattice","sourceOffset":41058},{"kind":"section","title":"One shear and exact coverage","sourceOffset":54720},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A simultaneous shear for finitely many patterns","sourceOffset":55121}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is an absolute constant $C>0$ such that, for every integer $n\\ge2$ and every convex body $K\\subset\\R^n$, one full-rank lattice $\\Lambda$ satisfies \\[ K+\\Lambda=\\R^n,\\qquad \\frac{\\vol_n(K)}{\\det\\Lambda}\\le Cn\\log n. \\] No symmetry or boundary regularity is assumed.","sourceOffset":882,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Weighted vertical patterns","label":"prop:vertical","text":"There are absolute constants $B,C,c,\\kappa>0$ such that, for every integer $b_1\\ge B$, the construction above has \\begin{equation} D\\le C(b_1+1),\\qquad R^2:=\\sum_{i=1}^{r+1}b_i h_i^2 \\le C\\bigl(b_1\\log(b_1+1)+1\\bigr). \\end{equation} For every $y\\in\\R^D$ there is a finite nonempty pattern $\\mathcal P_y\\subset L$ such that \\begin{align} \\|(y-M\\ell)_i\\|_\\infty&\\le h_i \\qquad(\\ell\\in\\mathcal P_y,\\ 1\\le i\\le r+1), \\\\ \\sum_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal P_y}D_v\\gamma_D(y-M\\ell)&\\ge\\kappa, \\\\ \\max_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal P_y}D_v\\gamma_D(y-M\\ell) &\\le \\exp(-c b_1^{0.70}). \\end{align} The terminal block is constant on the pattern. For each ordinary block $i$ and each fixed suffix $(\\ell_{i+1},\\ldots,\\ell_{r+1})$, its retained values belong to $\\lambda_i+\\{0,1\\}^{b_i}$ for one $\\lambda_i\\in L_i$ depending on that suffix. In particular $|\\mathcal P_y|\\le 2^D$. Order the scalar coordinates by increasing block index and denote them by $\\ell[1],\\ldots,\\ell[D]$. Conditional on any fixed scalar suffix $\\ell[j+1],\\ldots,\\ell[D]$, the possible next values $\\ell[j]$ are at most two; if there are two, they differ by $1$. This property is preserved upon deleting any subset of the pattern. If $y\\in M[0,1)^D$, all raw coordinates can in addition be restricted to the fixed finite alphabets \\begin{equation} \\begin{aligned} \\ell_i&\\in\\bigl(p_i^{-1}\\Z\\cap[-Q_{\\rm raw},Q_{\\rm raw}]\\bigr)^{b_i} &&(i\\le r),\\\\ \\ell_{r+1}&\\in\\bigl(\\Z\\cap[-Q_{\\rm raw},Q_{\\rm raw}]\\bigr)^T, &&Q_{\\rm raw}=1+(D+2)^D. \\end{aligned} \\end{equation} Fo","sourceOffset":45012,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/092.md","scopeText":"The formalized result gives an absolute constant $C>0$ such that every convex body $K\\subset\\mathbb R^n$, $n\\ge2$, admits a covering by translates along one full-rank lattice $L$ with density $|K|/\\mathrm{covol}(L)\\le Cn\\log n$. The covering is exact, and no symmetry, boundary regularity, or volume normalization is assumed.\n\nThe formalization determines the order of the largest covering density in dimension $n$. For all sufficiently large $n$, the suprema of translative covering density and lattice covering density are each bounded above and below by absolute positive multiples of $n\\log n$, both over all convex bodies and over centrally symmetric convex bodies.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/092.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":70402,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-single-lattice-covering-bound-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"092-02","slug":"Translative-covering-densities-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026","familyId":"092","title":"Translative covering densities of order n log n","abstract":"For every sufficiently large dimension n, we construct a centrally symmetric convex body whose translative covering density exceeds $c n\\log n$, where c > 0 is absolute. This disproves the existence of a universal linear upper bound and matches the order of Rogers' upper bound.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Determines the optimal worst-case covering density as $\\Theta(n\\log n)$, for both lattice and unrestricted translative coverings. Every convex body in ℝn, n ≥ 2, admits a lattice covering of density at most $Cn\\log n$; centrally symmetric examples in every sufficiently large dimension require at least $cn\\log n$ even without the lattice restriction, for absolute $c,C\\gt 0$.","pdfPath":"preprints/Translative-covering-densities-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Translative-covering-densities-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Translative-covering-densities-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Translative-covering-densities-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Translative-covering-densities-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Translative-covering-densities-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-construction.tex","preprints/Translative-covering-densities-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-localization.tex","preprints/Translative-covering-densities-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-caps.tex","preprints/Translative-covering-densities-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/figures/residual-cap.tex","preprints/Translative-covering-densities-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-blocks.tex","preprints/Translative-covering-densities-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-poisson.tex","preprints/Translative-covering-densities-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/figures/self-matching.tex","preprints/Translative-covering-densities-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-targets.tex","preprints/Translative-covering-densities-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-conclusion.tex","preprints/Translative-covering-densities-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/09-encoding.tex","preprints/Translative-covering-densities-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/10-periodization.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Translative-covering-densities-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every sufficiently large dimension $n$, we construct a centrally symmetric convex body whose translative covering density exceeds $c n\\log n$, where $c>0$ is absolute. This disproves the existence of a universal linear upper bound and matches the order of Rogers' upper bound.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":354},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Antecedents and the additional uniformity","sourceOffset":3782},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof outline","sourceOffset":6240},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Notation","sourceOffset":9226},{"kind":"section","title":"The random body","sourceOffset":9982},{"kind":"section","title":"Reducing all periodic coverings to finitely many patterns","sourceOffset":12684},{"kind":"section","title":"Intersections of spherical caps","sourceOffset":23336},{"kind":"section","title":"Grouping cap conditions","sourceOffset":35414},{"kind":"section","title":"Poisson witnesses and their lower tail","sourceOffset":44709},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite inequality","sourceOffset":45469},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Measurable slots and matching diagrams","sourceOffset":48896},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Self and cross diagrams","sourceOffset":56362}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are absolute constants $c>0$ and $n_0$ such that, for every integer $n\\ge n_0$, some centrally symmetric convex body $K_n\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ satisfies \\[ \\theta_T(K_n)>c n\\log n. \\]","sourceOffset":2614,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Deterministic localization","label":"prop:localization","text":"For all sufficiently large $n$, uniformly for $1\\le R\\le n^2$, the family $\\mathcal F_n$ has the following properties. With $\\lambda_0=\\log(1+D/a)$, \\begin{equation} N_n\\le n^{C_1}\\exp(\\lambda_0n),\\qquad \\log|\\mathcal F_n| \\le\\exp\\bigl(\\lambda_0n+O(\\log n)\\bigr), \\end{equation} where $C_1$ and the implicit constant depend only on the fixed parameters. Let $\\mathcal U\\subset S^{n-1}$ be any finite set, and put \\[ K=bB\\cap\\bigcap_{u\\in\\mathcal U}\\{x:|u\\cdot x|\\le1\\},\\qquad K^+=(b+3\\eta)B\\cap \\bigcap_{u\\in\\mathcal U}\\{x:|u\\cdot x|\\le1+3\\eta\\}. \\] Suppose that $\\Lambda$ is any full-rank lattice, $T\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ is finite, and \\[ \\frac{|T|}{\\det\\Lambda}\\le \\rho_0:=\\frac{R}{2v\\omega_na^n}. \\] If $K+\\Lambda+T$ covers $\\mathbb R^n$, there is a list $P=(p_1,\\ldots,p_m)\\in\\mathcal F_n$ such that \\begin{equation} B_D\\subseteq\\bigcup_{j=1}^{m}(p_j+K^+). \\end{equation} The constructed list in fact satisfies $|G_P|\\ge|B_D|/2$. For every $P\\in\\mathcal F_n$ and $y\\in G_P$, list all residual vectors $x_i=y-p_j$ with $|y-p_j|\\le b+3\\eta$, retaining their indices, and denote their number by $m_y$. Then \\begin{equation} \\begin{gathered} r_--2\\eta\\le |x_i|\\le b+3\\eta,\\qquad m_y\\le2C_{\\mathrm{loc}}R,\\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{m_y}w_a(|x_i|)\\le2C_{\\mathrm{loc}}R,\\\\ m_y+\\sum_{i=1}^{m_y}w_a(|x_i|)\\le4C_{\\mathrm{loc}}R. \\end{gathered} \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":14734,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/092.md","scopeText":"The formalized result gives an absolute constant $C>0$ such that every convex body $K\\subset\\mathbb R^n$, $n\\ge2$, admits a covering by translates along one full-rank lattice $L$ with density $|K|/\\mathrm{covol}(L)\\le Cn\\log n$. The covering is exact, and no symmetry, boundary regularity, or volume normalization is assumed.\n\nThe formalization determines the order of the largest covering density in dimension $n$. For all sufficiently large $n$, the suprema of translative covering density and lattice covering density are each bounded above and below by absolute positive multiples of $n\\log n$, both over all convex bodies and over centrally symmetric convex bodies.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/092.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":89800,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Translative-covering-densities-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"093-01","slug":"A-dimension-free-logarithmic-Sobolev-inequality-for-subgaussian-log-concave-measures-September-23-2026","familyId":"093","title":"A dimension-free logarithmic Sobolev inequality for subgaussian log-concave measures","abstract":"We prove that every centered log-concave probability measure with a Lebesgue density on ℝn and linear subgaussian parameter a satisfies $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Ent}}\\nolimits _\\mu(f^2)\\le Ca^2\\int|Df|^2\\,d\\mu$ for compactly supported smooth f, with one universal constant C. This resolves positively the dimension-free logarithmic Sobolev conjecture for subgaussian log-concave measures.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves a dimension-free logarithmic Sobolev inequality for centered log-concave densities with uniformly subgaussian linear marginals, with constant bounded by a universal multiple of the squared linear subgaussian parameter.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-dimension-free-logarithmic-Sobolev-inequality-for-subgaussian-log-concave-measures-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-dimension-free-logarithmic-Sobolev-inequality-for-subgaussian-log-concave-measures-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-dimension-free-logarithmic-Sobolev-inequality-for-subgaussian-log-concave-measures-September-23-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-dimension-free-logarithmic-Sobolev-inequality-for-subgaussian-log-concave-measures-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-dimension-free-logarithmic-Sobolev-inequality-for-subgaussian-log-concave-measures-September-23-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","preprints/A-dimension-free-logarithmic-Sobolev-inequality-for-subgaussian-log-concave-measures-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-dimension-free-logarithmic-Sobolev-inequality-for-subgaussian-log-concave-measures-September-23-2026/build/figures/proof-dependencies.tex","preprints/A-dimension-free-logarithmic-Sobolev-inequality-for-subgaussian-log-concave-measures-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-calculus.tex","preprints/A-dimension-free-logarithmic-Sobolev-inequality-for-subgaussian-log-concave-measures-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-critical.tex","preprints/A-dimension-free-logarithmic-Sobolev-inequality-for-subgaussian-log-concave-measures-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-prediction.tex","preprints/A-dimension-free-logarithmic-Sobolev-inequality-for-subgaussian-log-concave-measures-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-hierarchy.tex","preprints/A-dimension-free-logarithmic-Sobolev-inequality-for-subgaussian-log-concave-measures-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-matrices.tex","preprints/A-dimension-free-logarithmic-Sobolev-inequality-for-subgaussian-log-concave-measures-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-observations.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-dimension-free-logarithmic-Sobolev-inequality-for-subgaussian-log-concave-measures-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every centered log-concave probability measure with a Lebesgue density on $\\mathbb R^n$ and linear subgaussian parameter $a$ satisfies $\\operatorname{Ent}_\\mu(f^2)\\le Ca^2\\int|Df|^2\\,d\\mu$ for compactly supported smooth $f$, with one universal constant $C$. This resolves positively the dimension-free logarithmic Sobolev conjecture for subgaussian log-concave measures.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":430},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":3307},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":6896},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Components of the argument","sourceOffset":14075},{"kind":"section","title":"Curvature, diffusion, and entropy calculus","sourceOffset":15181},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The transport--entropy consequence","sourceOffset":30197},{"kind":"section","title":"Critical laws after Gaussian regularization","sourceOffset":32159},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Normalization and Gaussian regularization","sourceOffset":32734},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Attainment above the Gaussian and Poincar\\'e thresholds","sourceOffset":37644},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform estimates in the two alternatives","sourceOffset":51856},{"kind":"section","title":"Efficient probes and the prediction curve","sourceOffset":65569},{"kind":"section","title":"A symmetric hierarchy of nearly first eigenfunctions","sourceOffset":98488}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is an absolute constant \\(C<\\infty\\) with the following property. For every \\(n\\ge1\\), every centered log-concave probability measure \\(\\mu\\) with a Lebesgue density on \\(\\R^n\\), and every \\(a>0\\) satisfying \\eqref{eq:intro-subgaussian} for \\(X\\sim\\mu\\), \\[ \\Ent_\\mu(f^2)\\le C a^2\\int_{\\R^n}|Df|^2\\,d\\mu \\qquad\\text{for every }f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\R^n). \\] The same constant works in every dimension.","sourceOffset":1530,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Quadratic transport--entropy inequality","label":"cor:intro-transport","text":"Under the assumptions of Theorem \\ref{thm:main}, every $\\nu\\in\\mathcal P_2(\\R^n)$ satisfies \\[ W_2(\\nu,\\mu)^2\\le Ca^2 H(\\nu\\mid\\mu), \\] with the same absolute constant $C$ as in that theorem.","sourceOffset":2886,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":212718,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-dimension-free-logarithmic-Sobolev-inequality-for-subgaussian-log-concave-measures-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-dimension-free-logarithmic-Sobolev-inequality-for-subgaussian-log-concave-measures-September-23-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"094-01","slug":"Subpolynomial-dimension-reduction-in-Lp-September-23-2026","familyId":"094","title":"Subpolynomial dimension reduction in Lp","abstract":"For every fixed $1\\lt p\\lt \\infty$ and D > 1, every n-point subset of a real Lp space embeds into $\\ell_p^d$ with distortion at most D and subpolynomial dimension $d=n^{o(1)}$. The target has the same exponent p, and the embedding need not be linear.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every fixed $1\\lt p\\lt \\infty$ and distortion D > 1, every n-point subset of real Lp embeds into $\\ell_p^d$ with distortion at most D and dimension $d=n^{o(1)}$, answering Naor's sublinear-dimension question for p ≠ 2. In contrast, exact embeddings require worst-case dimension $\\Theta(n^2)$ when p ≠ 2.","pdfPath":"preprints/Subpolynomial-dimension-reduction-in-Lp-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Subpolynomial-dimension-reduction-in-Lp-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Subpolynomial-dimension-reduction-in-Lp-September-23-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Subpolynomial-dimension-reduction-in-Lp-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Subpolynomial-dimension-reduction-in-Lp-September-23-2026/build/figures/truncation-ramp.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Subpolynomial-dimension-reduction-in-Lp-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed $1<p<\\infty$ and $D>1$, every $n$-point subset of a real $L_p$ space embeds into $\\ell_p^d$ with distortion at most $D$ and subpolynomial dimension $d=n^{o(1)}$. The target has the same exponent $p$, and the embedding need not be linear.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":299},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier results and the exact-distortion contrast","sourceOffset":2813},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Method and organization","sourceOffset":5486},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite coordinates and normalized increments","sourceOffset":8575},{"kind":"section","title":"From weighted moments to an embedding","sourceOffset":11183},{"kind":"section","title":"A projection with controlled row sums","sourceOffset":14858},{"kind":"section","title":"Homogeneous increments and signed Poisson sampling","sourceOffset":18494},{"kind":"section","title":"The range \\texorpdfstring{$1<p<2$}{1 < p < 2}","sourceOffset":21134},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A common heavy-tailed marginal","sourceOffset":21352},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Clipping and projection","sourceOffset":24044},{"kind":"section","title":"The range \\texorpdfstring{$2<p<\\infty$}{2 < p < infinity}","sourceOffset":26665},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A ramp of truncations","sourceOffset":27129}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Fix $1<p<\\infty$, $p\\ne2$, and put \\[ \\gamma(p)= \\begin{cases} 2-p,&1<p<2,\\\\ 1-2/p,&2<p<\\infty. \\end{cases} \\] For every $D>1$ there is a constant $C_{p,D}<\\infty$ such that, for every integer $n\\ge2$, \\begin{equation} \\frac{\\log n}{\\log(1+2D)} \\le d_p(n,D) \\le \\exp\\!\\bigl(C_{p,D}(\\log n)^{\\gamma(p)}\\bigr). \\end{equation} For exact embeddings, \\begin{equation} \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{4}\\right\\rfloor^2 \\le d_p(n,1)\\le\\binom n2\\qquad(n\\ge9). \\end{equation} Consequently, \\[ \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac{\\log d_p(n,D)}{\\log n} =\\begin{cases}0,&D>1,\\\\2,&D=1.\\end{cases} \\]","sourceOffset":1798,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/094.md","scopeText":"For $p>1$, $p\\ne2$, the formalization bounds the least dimension needed to embed every $n$-point subset of real $L_p$ with distortion $D$. For each fixed $D>1$, there is a constant $C=C(p,D)$ such that this dimension lies between $\\log n/\\log(1+2D)$ and $\\exp(C(\\log n)^{\\gamma(p)})$ for every $n\\ge2$, where $\\gamma(p)=2-p$ for $p<2$ and $1-2/p$ for $p>2$. At distortion $1$ and $n\\ge9$, it lies between $\\lfloor(n-1)/4\\rfloor^2$ and $\\binom n2$. The least dimension is attained, and its logarithm divided by $\\log n$ tends to $0$ for $D>1$ and to $2$ for $D=1$.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/094.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":55830,"sourceFileCount":2,"statementCount":1,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Subpolynomial-dimension-reduction-in-Lp-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Subpolynomial-dimension-reduction-in-Lp-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"095-01","slug":"Hyperbolicity-Cones-Without-Semidefinite-Lifts-October-5-2026","familyId":"095","title":"Hyperbolicity Cones Without Semidefinite Lifts","abstract":"We prove that not every hyperbolicity cone is a spectrahedral shadow: some closed hyperbolicity cones admit no finite affine semidefinite lift, even with arbitrary real coefficients and any finite number of auxiliary variables. This disproves the Projected Lax Conjecture and hence the generalized Lax conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves the Projected Lax conjecture: some hyperbolicity cones are not spectrahedral shadows. The examples admit no exact finite affine semidefinite lift, regardless of the number of auxiliary variables or the real coefficients used. This also disproves the generalized Lax conjecture that every hyperbolicity cone is spectrahedral.","pdfPath":"preprints/Hyperbolicity-Cones-Without-Semidefinite-Lifts-October-5-2026/nonliftable-hyperbolicity.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Hyperbolicity-Cones-Without-Semidefinite-Lifts-October-5-2026/nonliftable-hyperbolicity.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Hyperbolicity-Cones-Without-Semidefinite-Lifts-October-5-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Hyperbolicity-Cones-Without-Semidefinite-Lifts-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Hyperbolicity-Cones-Without-Semidefinite-Lifts-October-5-2026/build/source/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Hyperbolicity-Cones-Without-Semidefinite-Lifts-October-5-2026/build/source/02-cone.tex","preprints/Hyperbolicity-Cones-Without-Semidefinite-Lifts-October-5-2026/build/source/03-hankel.tex","preprints/Hyperbolicity-Cones-Without-Semidefinite-Lifts-October-5-2026/build/source/04-separation.tex","preprints/Hyperbolicity-Cones-Without-Semidefinite-Lifts-October-5-2026/build/source/05-obstruction.tex","preprints/Hyperbolicity-Cones-Without-Semidefinite-Lifts-October-5-2026/build/source/appendix-certificate.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Hyperbolicity-Cones-Without-Semidefinite-Lifts-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that not every hyperbolicity cone is a spectrahedral shadow: some closed hyperbolicity cones admit no finite affine semidefinite lift, even with arbitrary real coefficients and any finite number of auxiliary variables. This disproves the Projected Lax Conjecture and hence the generalized Lax conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":359},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and earlier obstructions","sourceOffset":2398},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and proof strategy","sourceOffset":5271},{"kind":"section","title":"A determinant construction and its affine section","sourceOffset":8881},{"kind":"section","title":"Hankel matrices and finitely many inverse limits","sourceOffset":13909},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The rank stratum and the seed matrix","sourceOffset":14543},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removing translations and dilations","sourceOffset":18584},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Selecting a finite fiber","sourceOffset":24307},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The inverse-limit property","sourceOffset":29101},{"kind":"section","title":"Separation from Hankel images","sourceOffset":38111},{"kind":"section","title":"A local obstruction to a semidefinite lift","sourceOffset":48724},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A polynomial patch and its moment deformation","sourceOffset":50673}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exists a real homogeneous hyperbolic polynomial whose closed hyperbolicity cone has no finite semidefinite lift.","sourceOffset":1912,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:hyperbolic","text":"The polynomial $p_Q$ is homogeneous of degree $n(n+1)$ and hyperbolic with respect to $e=(I_n,I_n,0)$, with $p_Q(e)=1$. Fix $y$ and choose vectors $b_1,\\ldots,b_n\\in\\R^r$ with $Q(y)=\\sum_{j=1}^n b_jb_j^\\top$. Define \\[ B_j=\\begin{pmatrix}0&b_j^\\top\\\\-b_j&0\\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad \\mathcal B=\\begin{pmatrix}B_1\\\\\\vdots\\\\B_n\\end{pmatrix}. \\] Then the closed hyperbolicity cone $K_Q$ of $p_Q$ is characterized, at this value of $y$, by \\begin{equation} (X,Z,y)\\in K_Q \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\begin{pmatrix}I_n\\otimes X&\\mathcal B\\\\ \\mathcal B^\\top&Z\\end{pmatrix}\\succeq0. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":9803,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/095.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":94507,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Hyperbolicity-Cones-Without-Semidefinite-Lifts-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Hyperbolicity-Cones-Without-Semidefinite-Lifts-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Hyperbolicity-Cones-Without-Semidefinite-Lifts-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"095-02","slug":"A-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-September-24-2026","familyId":"095","title":"A nonspectrahedral hyperbolicity cone","abstract":"We construct a homogeneous polynomial of degree 16 in 23 real variables whose hyperbolicity cone has no representation by a finite homogeneous real symmetric linear matrix inequality. This disproves the geometric Generalized Lax conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves the Projected Lax conjecture: some hyperbolicity cones are not spectrahedral shadows. The examples admit no exact finite affine semidefinite lift, regardless of the number of auxiliary variables or the real coefficients used. This also disproves the generalized Lax conjecture that every hyperbolicity cone is spectrahedral.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-September-24-2026/nonspectrahedral-hyperbolicity-cone.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-September-24-2026/nonspectrahedral-hyperbolicity-cone.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-September-24-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-history.tex","preprints/A-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-form.tex","preprints/A-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-hyperbolic.tex","preprints/A-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-pencil.tex","preprints/A-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-norm.tex","preprints/A-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-tangent.tex","preprints/A-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-September-24-2026/build/figures/tangent-variation.tex","preprints/A-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-September-24-2026/build/sections/reduced-polynomial.tex","preprints/A-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-comparison.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a homogeneous polynomial of degree $16$ in $23$ real variables whose hyperbolicity cone has no representation by a finite homogeneous real symmetric linear matrix inequality. This disproves the geometric Generalized Lax conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":307},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The explicit polynomial","sourceOffset":1256},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":3663},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":7788},{"kind":"section","title":"A biquadratic form with no dominated square","sourceOffset":9551},{"kind":"section","title":"Hyperbolicity and two cone slices","sourceOffset":12196},{"kind":"section","title":"What a semidefinite representation would imply","sourceOffset":17650},{"kind":"section","title":"Rank-one limits and a norm identity","sourceOffset":23429},{"kind":"section","title":"The tangent obstruction","sourceOffset":27365},{"kind":"section","title":"Removing a determinant factor","sourceOffset":32759},{"kind":"section","title":"A path-containment assertion","sourceOffset":35208}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"The polynomial \\eqref{eq:polynomial} is homogeneous of degree $20$ and hyperbolic with respect to $e=(I_4,I_4,0)$, with $p(e)=1$. Its closed hyperbolicity cone $K=\\Lambda_+(p,e)$ is not spectrahedral: for every finite $N\\geq1$ and every real linear map $L:\\Sym^4\\times\\Sym^4\\times\\R^3\\to\\Sym^N$, \\[ K\\ne\\{(X,Z,y):L(X,Z,y)\\succeq0\\}. \\]","sourceOffset":2385,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:reduced","text":"The quotient $q=p/\\det X$ extends to a homogeneous polynomial of degree $16$ in the same $23$ variables. It is hyperbolic with respect to $e$, with $q(e)=1$, and $\\Lambda_+(q,e)=K$. In particular, its closed hyperbolicity cone is not spectrahedral.","sourceOffset":2985,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/095.md","scopeText":"The generalized Lax conjecture predicts that every hyperbolicity cone is spectrahedral. The formalized counterexample is an explicit homogeneous polynomial of degree $20$ in $23$ real variables. It is hyperbolic, but its closed hyperbolicity cone cannot be represented as the positive-semidefinite region of any finite real symmetric linear matrix pencil, in any positive matrix size.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/095.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":37728,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"095-03","slug":"An-Exact-Semidefinite-Lift-of-a-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-October-5-2026","familyId":"095","title":"An Exact Semidefinite Lift of a Nonspectrahedral Hyperbolicity Cone","abstract":"We construct an exact semidefinite lift of the explicit nonspectrahedral hyperbolicity cone in twenty-three variables defined in the companion paper. The lift is a homogeneous real symmetric pencil of size 100 with 307 auxiliary variables and represents the entire closed cone, including every point with singular X. Thus, although this cone has no semidefinite representation in its original coordinates, it admits one when auxiliary variables are allowed. The stated sizes are not claimed to be minimal.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves the Projected Lax conjecture: some hyperbolicity cones are not spectrahedral shadows. The examples admit no exact finite affine semidefinite lift, regardless of the number of auxiliary variables or the real coefficients used. This also disproves the generalized Lax conjecture that every hyperbolicity cone is spectrahedral.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-Exact-Semidefinite-Lift-of-a-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-October-5-2026/Exact-Semidefinite-Lift-of-a-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-Exact-Semidefinite-Lift-of-a-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-October-5-2026/Exact-Semidefinite-Lift-of-a-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-Exact-Semidefinite-Lift-of-a-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-October-5-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/An-Exact-Semidefinite-Lift-of-a-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/An-Exact-Semidefinite-Lift-of-a-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/An-Exact-Semidefinite-Lift-of-a-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-October-5-2026/build/sections/slices.tex","preprints/An-Exact-Semidefinite-Lift-of-a-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-October-5-2026/build/sections/angular.tex","preprints/An-Exact-Semidefinite-Lift-of-a-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-October-5-2026/build/sections/matching.tex","preprints/An-Exact-Semidefinite-Lift-of-a-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-October-5-2026/build/sections/lift.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-Exact-Semidefinite-Lift-of-a-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct an exact semidefinite lift of the explicit nonspectrahedral hyperbolicity cone in twenty-three variables defined in the companion paper. The lift is a homogeneous real symmetric pencil of size $100$ with $307$ auxiliary variables and represents the entire closed cone, including every point with singular $X$. Thus, although this cone has no semidefinite representation in its original coordinates, it admits one when auxiliary variables are allowed. The stated sizes are not claimed to be minimal.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":557},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The cone and the main result","sourceOffset":2087},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":4898},{"kind":"section","title":"Exact semidefinite representations on angular slices","sourceOffset":6888},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The slice pencil","sourceOffset":9153},{"kind":"section","title":"A degree-two matching identity","sourceOffset":12388},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite semidefinite lift","sourceOffset":20032},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The matrix condition","sourceOffset":20437},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The size of the lift","sourceOffset":27482}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist fixed matrices $A_1,\\ldots,A_{23},B_1,\\ldots,B_{307} \\in\\Sym^{100}$ such that, for every $x=(X,Z,y)\\in\\mathcal V$, \\[ x\\in K\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\text{there exists }v\\in\\R^{307}\\text{ with } \\sum_{\\nu=1}^{23}x_\\nu A_\\nu+ \\sum_{j=1}^{307}v_jB_j\\succeq0. \\] The equivalence holds on the entire closed cone, including points with singular $X$.","sourceOffset":3409,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:lift","text":"For $(X,Z,y)\\in\\mathbb S^4\\times\\mathbb S^4\\times\\mathbb R^3$, the following are equivalent: \\begin{enumerate} \\item $(X,Z,y)\\in K$; \\item there is a real linear functional $\\Lambda:\\mathcal E\\to\\mathbb R$ satisfying \\eqref{eq:output} and $H(\\Lambda)\\succeq0$. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":22438,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/095.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":28665,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-Exact-Semidefinite-Lift-of-a-Nonspectrahedral-Hyperbolicity-Cone-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"096-01","slug":"The-Gaussian-Propeller-Bound-in-Every-Dimension-September-24-2026","familyId":"096","title":"The Gaussian propeller bound in every dimension","abstract":"We prove the Gaussian propeller conjecture: for every finite measurable partition of a Euclidean space, the sum of the squared lengths of its Gaussian first moments is at most $9/(8\\pi)$. For dimension at least two and at least three cells, three planar sectors of angle $2\\pi/3$, extended by an orthogonal Euclidean factor, attain the bound.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the sum of squared Gaussian first moments of any finite measurable partition is at most $9/(8\\pi)$. In dimension at least two, three planar sectors of angle $2\\pi/3$, extended orthogonally, attain the bound. Combined with the separate Unique Games theorem, this proves NP-hardness of improving the loss factor $(8\\pi/9)(1-1/k)$ for identity-target kernel clustering with fixed k ≥ 3 on rational centered positive semidefinite inputs.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Gaussian-Propeller-Bound-in-Every-Dimension-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Gaussian-Propeller-Bound-in-Every-Dimension-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Gaussian-Propeller-Bound-in-Every-Dimension-September-24-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/The-Gaussian-Propeller-Bound-in-Every-Dimension-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-Propeller-Bound-in-Every-Dimension-September-24-2026/build/figures/propeller.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-Propeller-Bound-in-Every-Dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/extremizers.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-Propeller-Bound-in-Every-Dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/scalars.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-Propeller-Bound-in-Every-Dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-Propeller-Bound-in-Every-Dimension-September-24-2026/build/figures/residual-triangle.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-Propeller-Bound-in-Every-Dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/elimination.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-Propeller-Bound-in-Every-Dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/kernel-application.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-Propeller-Bound-in-Every-Dimension-September-24-2026/build/sections/certificates.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-Propeller-Bound-in-Every-Dimension-September-24-2026/build/sources/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Gaussian-Propeller-Bound-in-Every-Dimension-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Gaussian propeller conjecture: for every finite measurable partition of a Euclidean space, the sum of the squared lengths of its Gaussian first moments is at most $9/(8\\pi)$. For dimension at least two and at least three cells, three planar sectors of angle $2\\pi/3$, extended by an orthogonal Euclidean factor, attain the bound.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":388},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The attaining propeller","sourceOffset":2726},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Analytic background and related partition problems","sourceOffset":5250},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The kernel-clustering consequence","sourceOffset":7032},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":8350},{"kind":"section","title":"Extremal partitions and their centroids","sourceOffset":11852},{"kind":"section","title":"Probability bounds for the cells","sourceOffset":18947},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rearrangement bounds","sourceOffset":19963},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The loss on removing one score","sourceOffset":22866},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A linear--quadratic probability bound","sourceOffset":25788},{"kind":"section","title":"Competition with two other cells","sourceOffset":28891},{"kind":"section","title":"Excluding five or more active cells","sourceOffset":37589}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For all positive integers $d$ and $k$, every measurable partition $(A_1,\\ldots,A_k)$ of $\\mathbb R^d$ satisfies \\[ \\sum_{i=1}^k\\left\\|\\int_{A_i}x\\,d\\gamma_d(x)\\right\\|^2 \\le\\frac9{8\\pi}. \\] Empty cells are allowed. The constant is sharp whenever $d\\ge2$ and $k\\ge3$: three planar sectors of angle $2\\pi/3$, multiplied by the orthogonal complement of their plane, attain equality, with any further cells empty.","sourceOffset":2267,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Kernel-clustering hardness","label":"kc:main","text":"For each fixed \\(k\\ge3\\) and every fixed \\(1\\le\\alpha<\\alpha_k\\), it is NP-hard, given a rational symmetric matrix \\(A\\succeq0\\) with \\(A\\one=0\\) and the identity-target objective \\eqref{kc:value}, to find \\(\\sigma:[N]\\to[k]\\) such that \\[ \\val_A(\\sigma)\\ge\\Clust(A\\mid I_k)/\\alpha. \\] In particular, such a deterministic polynomial-time approximation would imply \\(\\mathrm P=\\mathrm{NP}\\).","sourceOffset":50536,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/096.md","scopeText":"The Gaussian propeller problem asks how large the sum of squared Gaussian first moments can be over a partition. The formalized result proves the sharp bound $9/(8\\pi)$ for every positive dimension and every positive number of labelled cells, allowing empty cells and arbitrary masses. In dimension at least two with at least three cells, three planar sectors of angle $120^\\circ$, extended in orthogonal directions, attain equality. The separate Gaussian-maxima and kernel-clustering consequences are not included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/096.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":74835,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Gaussian-Propeller-Bound-in-Every-Dimension-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"097-01","slug":"The-Euclidean-Steinitz-Bergstrom-theorem-September-24-2026","familyId":"097","title":"The Euclidean Steinitz–Bergström theorem","abstract":"Every prescribed-order finite sequence of vectors in the Euclidean unit ball of ℝd has one signing for which every signed prefix has norm at most $C\\sqrt d$, with C absolute and independent of the sequence length. Consequently, every indexed zero-sum family of unit-ball vectors admits an ordering with the same bound for its unsigned partial sums. This determines the Euclidean Steinitz constant up to absolute factors, $S_2(d)=\\Theta(\\sqrt d)$, and resolves the Euclidean Steinitz–Bergström conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that any finite sequence in the Euclidean unit ball of ℝd admits signs keeping every partial sum within $C\\sqrt d$, independently of length. Consequently every zero-sum family can be reordered with the same bound on unsigned partial sums. A matching lower bound gives the optimal order $S_2(d)=\\Theta(\\sqrt d)$.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Euclidean-Steinitz-Bergstrom-theorem-September-24-2026/The-Euclidean-Steinitz-Bergstrom-theorem-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Euclidean-Steinitz-Bergstrom-theorem-September-24-2026/The-Euclidean-Steinitz-Bergstrom-theorem-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Euclidean-Steinitz-Bergstrom-theorem-September-24-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/The-Euclidean-Steinitz-Bergstrom-theorem-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Euclidean-Steinitz-Bergstrom-theorem-September-24-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/The-Euclidean-Steinitz-Bergstrom-theorem-September-24-2026/build/assembly.tex","preprints/The-Euclidean-Steinitz-Bergstrom-theorem-September-24-2026/build/brownian.tex","preprints/The-Euclidean-Steinitz-Bergstrom-theorem-September-24-2026/build/signing.tex","preprints/The-Euclidean-Steinitz-Bergstrom-theorem-September-24-2026/build/matrix.tex","preprints/The-Euclidean-Steinitz-Bergstrom-theorem-September-24-2026/build/survival.tex","preprints/The-Euclidean-Steinitz-Bergstrom-theorem-September-24-2026/build/consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Euclidean-Steinitz-Bergstrom-theorem-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every prescribed-order finite sequence of vectors in the Euclidean unit ball of $\\R^d$ has one signing for which every signed prefix has norm at most $C\\sqrt d$, with $C$ absolute and independent of the sequence length. Consequently, every indexed zero-sum family of unit-ball vectors admits an ordering with the same bound for its unsigned partial sums. This determines the Euclidean Steinitz constant up to absolute factors, $S_2(d)=\\Theta(\\sqrt d)$, and resolves the Euclidean Steinitz--Bergstr\\\"om conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":595},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From signs to an ordering","sourceOffset":3570},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":5312},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":7635},{"kind":"section","title":"Constructing the signed walk","sourceOffset":10992},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two analytic inputs","sourceOffset":11419},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The filter, the shifts, and their cancellation","sourceOffset":15201},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Brownian proofs of the slab bound","sourceOffset":21405},{"kind":"section","title":"From low energy to shifted signs","sourceOffset":27648},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One function for all coordinate energies","sourceOffset":28689},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Convexity under Minkowski averages","sourceOffset":31626},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A section that permits shifted signed steps","sourceOffset":33900}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Prescribed-order signed prefixes","label":"thm:signing","text":"There is an absolute constant $C$ such that, for all integers $d,N\\ge1$ and every sequence $v_1,\\ldots,v_N\\in\\R^d$ with $\\lVert v_i\\rVert_2\\le1$, there are signs $\\varepsilon_1,\\ldots,\\varepsilon_N\\in\\{-1,1\\}$ for which \\[ \\max_{0\\le k\\le N} \\left\\lVert\\sum_{i=1}^k\\varepsilon_i v_i\\right\\rVert_2\\le C\\sqrt d. \\] The same signs control all prefixes, and $C$ is independent of $d$, $N$ and the sequence.","sourceOffset":1050,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Euclidean Steinitz--Bergstr\\\"om bound","label":"thm:steinitz","text":"There is an absolute constant $C$ such that, for all integers $d,N\\ge1$ and every indexed family $v_1,\\ldots,v_N\\in\\R^d$ satisfying $\\lVert v_i\\rVert_2\\le1$ and $\\sum_{i=1}^N v_i=0$, some permutation $\\pi\\in\\mathfrak S_N$ satisfies \\[ \\max_{0\\le k\\le N}\\left\\lVert\\sum_{i=1}^k v_{\\pi(i)}\\right\\rVert_2 \\le C\\sqrt d. \\] The constant in Theorem \\ref{thm:signing} also suffices here.","sourceOffset":2167,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/097.md","scopeText":"The formalized result gives the Euclidean Steinitz–Bergström bound with one absolute constant $C$. For every finite family of vectors in the unit ball of $\\mathbb R^d$, signs can be chosen so that every prefix in the prescribed order has norm at most $C\\sqrt d$. When the vector sum is zero, a permutation makes every unsigned prefix satisfy the same bound. Repeated and zero vectors are included. The result is existential and does not supply an online algorithm.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/097.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":81445,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Euclidean-Steinitz-Bergstrom-theorem-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"098-01","slug":"A-doubling-Hilbert-subset-with-no-finite-dimensional-bi-Lipschitz-embedding-September-25-2026","familyId":"098","title":"A doubling Hilbert subset with no finite-dimensional bi-Lipschitz embedding","abstract":"Every infinite-dimensional real Banach space contains a compact doubling subset that admits no bi-Lipschitz embedding into any finite-dimensional real normed space. The doubling constant has a universal bound, independent of the ambient Banach space. This answers the Lang–Plaut problem negatively, even for compact subsets of Hilbert space.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Every infinite-dimensional real Banach space contains a compact doubling set that admits no bi-Lipschitz embedding into any finite-dimensional normed space. The doubling constant is universal. This answers the Lang–Plaut problem negatively, even for compact subsets of Hilbert space.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-doubling-Hilbert-subset-with-no-finite-dimensional-bi-Lipschitz-embedding-September-25-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-doubling-Hilbert-subset-with-no-finite-dimensional-bi-Lipschitz-embedding-September-25-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-doubling-Hilbert-subset-with-no-finite-dimensional-bi-Lipschitz-embedding-September-25-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-doubling-Hilbert-subset-with-no-finite-dimensional-bi-Lipschitz-embedding-September-25-2026/build/main.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-doubling-Hilbert-subset-with-no-finite-dimensional-bi-Lipschitz-embedding-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every infinite-dimensional real Banach space contains a compact doubling subset that admits no bi-Lipschitz embedding into any finite-dimensional real normed space. The doubling constant has a universal bound, independent of the ambient Banach space. This answers the Lang--Plaut problem negatively, even for compact subsets of Hilbert space.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":388},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and proof strategy","sourceOffset":4068},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":8744},{"kind":"section","title":"The subset and its doubling bound","sourceOffset":9215},{"kind":"section","title":"Sheets and extremal derivatives","sourceOffset":12467},{"kind":"section","title":"Near-extremal rectangles and two energy estimates","sourceOffset":16870},{"kind":"section","title":"Crossings force a packing contradiction","sourceOffset":23384},{"kind":"section","title":"A consequence for finite-\\texorpdfstring{$p$}{p} function spaces","sourceOffset":27680},{"kind":"section","title":"Compact obstructions in infinite-dimensional Banach spaces","sourceOffset":30497}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a fixed subset $S$ of real $\\ell_2$, equipped with its induced Hilbert distance, whose doubling constant is at most $76800$ and which admits no bi-Lipschitz embedding into $\\R^k$ for any positive integer $k$, at any finite distortion.","sourceOffset":3172,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:doubling","text":"The doubling constant of $S$ is at most $300\\cdot16^2=76800$.","sourceOffset":10780,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/098.md","scopeText":"The formalized result gives a doubling subset of real $\\ell_2$, with doubling constant at most $76800$, that admits no bi-Lipschitz embedding into any finite-dimensional Euclidean space at any finite distortion. A companion gives one universal doubling constant such that every infinite-dimensional real Banach space contains a compact doubling subset with no bi-Lipschitz embedding into any finite-dimensional normed space.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/098.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":40925,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-doubling-Hilbert-subset-with-no-finite-dimensional-bi-Lipschitz-embedding-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"099-01","slug":"Edit-Distance-in-l1-Matching-Bounds-up-to-Constants-in-the-Exponent-September-27-2026","familyId":"099","title":"Edit Distance in l1: Matching Bounds up to Constants in the Exponent","abstract":"We determine the exponential scale of the least ℓ1 distortion of unit-cost edit distance on all strings of length at most d. For every sufficiently large d, uniformly over finite alphabets of size at least two, the distortion lies between $\\exp(c\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d})$ and $\\exp(C\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d})$ for absolute constants $c,C\\gt 0$. The lower bound already holds on binary strings of one common length. Thus the order of logarithmic distortion is sharp up to absolute constants.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Determines the least distortion of embedding edit distance on words of length at most d into real ℓ1: it is $\\exp(\\Theta(\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d}))$. Insertions, deletions and substitutions have unit cost. The constants are uniform over all finite alphabets with at least two symbols, even when the alphabet grows with d; binary words already force the lower bound.","pdfPath":"preprints/Edit-Distance-in-l1-Matching-Bounds-up-to-Constants-in-the-Exponent-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Edit-Distance-in-l1-Matching-Bounds-up-to-Constants-in-the-Exponent-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Edit-Distance-in-l1-Matching-Bounds-up-to-Constants-in-the-Exponent-September-27-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Edit-Distance-in-l1-Matching-Bounds-up-to-Constants-in-the-Exponent-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Edit-Distance-in-l1-Matching-Bounds-up-to-Constants-in-the-Exponent-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Edit-Distance-in-l1-Matching-Bounds-up-to-Constants-in-the-Exponent-September-27-2026/build/sections/displacement.tex","preprints/Edit-Distance-in-l1-Matching-Bounds-up-to-Constants-in-the-Exponent-September-27-2026/build/sections/construction.tex","preprints/Edit-Distance-in-l1-Matching-Bounds-up-to-Constants-in-the-Exponent-September-27-2026/build/sections/binary.tex","preprints/Edit-Distance-in-l1-Matching-Bounds-up-to-Constants-in-the-Exponent-September-27-2026/build/sections/upper.tex","preprints/Edit-Distance-in-l1-Matching-Bounds-up-to-Constants-in-the-Exponent-September-27-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Edit-Distance-in-l1-Matching-Bounds-up-to-Constants-in-the-Exponent-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We determine the exponential scale of the least $\\ell_1$ distortion of unit-cost edit distance on all strings of length at most $d$. For every sufficiently large $d$, uniformly over finite alphabets of size at least two, the distortion lies between $\\exp(c\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d})$ and $\\exp(C\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d})$ for absolute constants $c,C>0$. The lower bound already holds on binary strings of one common length. Thus the order of logarithmic distortion is sharp up to absolute constants.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":547},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The earlier bounds","sourceOffset":2702},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the lower bound works","sourceOffset":4232},{"kind":"section","title":"Alignments and a displacement inequality","sourceOffset":7471},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Distances from increasing matchings","sourceOffset":7822},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The comparison to be proved","sourceOffset":9250},{"kind":"section","title":"Recursive words with incompatible displacements","sourceOffset":16407},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The recursive family and its cheap steps","sourceOffset":17433},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Separation across row boundaries","sourceOffset":21788},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The averaged analytic recurrence","sourceOffset":26035},{"kind":"section","title":"Binary encoding and the lower bound","sourceOffset":28457},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The quantitative obstruction","sourceOffset":32198}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are absolute constants $c,C>0$ and an integer $d_0$ such that, for every integer $d\\ge d_0$ and every finite alphabet $\\Sigma$ with $|\\Sigma|\\ge2$, \\begin{equation} \\exp\\!\\bigl(c\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d}\\bigr) \\le E_\\Sigma(d)\\le \\exp\\!\\bigl(C\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d}\\bigr). \\end{equation} The lower bound is witnessed by a subset of binary strings of one common length at most $d$. The same bounds hold for $\\sup_{\\Sigma:\\,2\\le|\\Sigma|<\\infty}E_\\Sigma(d)$.","sourceOffset":1896,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Prime-product displacement inequality","label":"prop:displacement","text":"Let $b,h\\ge1$ be integers, let $P\\ge1$, and let $G_1,\\ldots,G_b$ be finite abelian groups. Suppose $v_j\\in G_j$ has order $p_j$, where $p_1,\\ldots,p_b$ are distinct primes in $[P,2P]$. In $G=\\prod_{j=1}^bG_j$, use the same notation $v_j$ for the element acting only in coordinate $j$, and put $\\tau=\\sum_{j=1}^b v_j$. Then every $H:G\\to\\ell_1$ and every integer $u$ satisfy \\begin{equation} V_H(uv_1) \\le (2P)^{2h}V_H(\\tau) +\\frac2h\\sum_{j=1}^b\\E_{a\\in\\Z/p_j\\Z}V_H(av_j). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":9805,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/099.md","scopeText":"The formalization determines the exponential scale of the least $\\ell_1$ distortion of unit-cost edit distance on strings of length at most $d$. For every sufficiently large $d$, uniformly over finite alphabets with at least two symbols, the distortion lies between $\\exp(c\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d})$ and $\\exp(C\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d})$ for absolute constants $c,C>0$. The lower bound has a witness consisting of binary strings of one common length, and the same scale controls the supremum over finite alphabets.\n\nThe formalization gives two finite-circle constructions of binary strings whose least $\\ell_1$ distortion is at least $\\exp(c\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d})$ for all sufficiently large length caps $d$. The witnesses use strings of one common length and include the binary coding transfer","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/099.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":45100,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Edit-Distance-in-l1-Matching-Bounds-up-to-Constants-in-the-Exponent-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"099-02","slug":"Finite-Circle-Obstructions-Binary-Codes-and-Histogram-Embeddings-for-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026","familyId":"099","title":"Finite-Circle Obstructions, Binary Codes, and Histogram Embeddings for Edit Distance","abstract":"We give two finite-circle constructions of binary strings whose least ℓ1 distortion is $\\exp(\\Omega(\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d}))$, where d bounds their length. Both constructions supply words of one common length for every sufficiently large cap. Two direct binary coding arguments transfer the constructions with absolute distortion and logarithmic block width. We also develop the overlapping-substring method of Ostrovsky and Rabani into a complete finite histogram embedding at the same exponential scale, uniformly over all finite alphabets and all words of length at most d, including the empty word.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Determines the least distortion of embedding edit distance on words of length at most d into real ℓ1: it is $\\exp(\\Theta(\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d}))$. Insertions, deletions and substitutions have unit cost. The constants are uniform over all finite alphabets with at least two symbols, even when the alphabet grows with d; binary words already force the lower bound.","pdfPath":"preprints/Finite-Circle-Obstructions-Binary-Codes-and-Histogram-Embeddings-for-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-Circle-Obstructions-Binary-Codes-and-Histogram-Embeddings-for-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-Circle-Obstructions-Binary-Codes-and-Histogram-Embeddings-for-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Finite-Circle-Obstructions-Binary-Codes-and-Histogram-Embeddings-for-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Finite-Circle-Obstructions-Binary-Codes-and-Histogram-Embeddings-for-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Finite-Circle-Obstructions-Binary-Codes-and-Histogram-Embeddings-for-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Finite-Circle-Obstructions-Binary-Codes-and-Histogram-Embeddings-for-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/figures/split-row.tex","preprints/Finite-Circle-Obstructions-Binary-Codes-and-Histogram-Embeddings-for-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/sections/masks.tex","preprints/Finite-Circle-Obstructions-Binary-Codes-and-Histogram-Embeddings-for-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/sections/circle-fourier.tex","preprints/Finite-Circle-Obstructions-Binary-Codes-and-Histogram-Embeddings-for-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/sections/coding.tex","preprints/Finite-Circle-Obstructions-Binary-Codes-and-Histogram-Embeddings-for-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/sections/mask-binary.tex","preprints/Finite-Circle-Obstructions-Binary-Codes-and-Histogram-Embeddings-for-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/sections/excluded.tex","preprints/Finite-Circle-Obstructions-Binary-Codes-and-Histogram-Embeddings-for-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/sections/assembly.tex","preprints/Finite-Circle-Obstructions-Binary-Codes-and-Histogram-Embeddings-for-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/sections/upper.tex","preprints/Finite-Circle-Obstructions-Binary-Codes-and-Histogram-Embeddings-for-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Finite-Circle-Obstructions-Binary-Codes-and-Histogram-Embeddings-for-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give two finite-circle constructions of binary strings whose least $\\ell_1$ distortion is $\\exp(\\Omega(\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d}))$, where $d$ bounds their length. Both constructions supply words of one common length for every sufficiently large cap. Two direct binary coding arguments transfer the constructions with absolute distortion and logarithmic block width. We also develop the overlapping-substring method of Ostrovsky and Rabani into a complete finite histogram embedding at the same exponential scale, uniformly over all finite alphabets and all words of length at most $d$, including the empty word.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":662},{"kind":"section","title":"Alignments and tagged rows","sourceOffset":10759},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Subsequence deficit and tagged rows","sourceOffset":11076},{"kind":"section","title":"A circle construction with denominator resets","sourceOffset":15775},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Translated rows on a finite circle","sourceOffset":16235},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A scalar cost and separation under arbitrary alignments","sourceOffset":20316},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Keeping a nonzero phase through the descent","sourceOffset":23060},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A common analytic comparison for circle shifts","sourceOffset":26746},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prime groups force leaf frequency","sourceOffset":31846},{"kind":"section","title":"Two logarithmic-width binary substitutions","sourceOffset":34344},{"kind":"subsection","title":"What an arbitrary bit matching tells us","sourceOffset":36125},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Different-label intervals and a common offset","sourceOffset":37715}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are absolute constants $c,C>0$ and an integer $d_0$ such that, for every integer $d\\geq d_0$ and every finite alphabet $\\Sigma$ with $|\\Sigma|\\geq2$, \\begin{equation} \\exp\\!\\bigl(c\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d}\\bigr) \\leq E_\\Sigma(d)\\leq \\exp\\!\\bigl(C\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d}\\bigr). \\end{equation} Each of our two lower constructions supplies a binary subset whose words have one common length at most $d$ and whose least $\\ell_1$ distortion satisfies the lower bound. The upper bound is realized by a deterministic finite-dimensional map.","sourceOffset":2045,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Two binary substitutions","label":"code:two-proofs","text":"Let $\\mathcal A$ be a finite alphabet with $A\\geq2$ letters. Each of the two constructions below gives an integer width $w\\geq1$ with $w=O(\\log(2A))$, an absolute constant $a>0$, and a map $c:\\mathcal A\\to\\{0,1\\}^w$ such that, simultaneously for every integer $L\\geq0$ and all $x,y\\in\\mathcal A^L$, \\begin{equation} a w r_L(x,y)\\leq r_{wL}(c(x),c(y))\\leq w r_L(x,y). \\end{equation} Here $c$ extends to words by concatenation. For arbitrary finite words, \\begin{equation} \\ED(c(x),c(y))\\leq w\\ED(x,y). \\end{equation} The code and its width depend only on $\\mathcal A$, not on $L$.","sourceOffset":34780,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/099.md","scopeText":"The formalization determines the exponential scale of the least $\\ell_1$ distortion of unit-cost edit distance on strings of length at most $d$. For every sufficiently large $d$, uniformly over finite alphabets with at least two symbols, the distortion lies between $\\exp(c\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d})$ and $\\exp(C\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d})$ for absolute constants $c,C>0$. The lower bound has a witness consisting of binary strings of one common length, and the same scale controls the supremum over finite alphabets.\n\nThe formalization gives two finite-circle constructions of binary strings whose least $\\ell_1$ distortion is at least $\\exp(c\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d})$ for all sufficiently large length caps $d$. The witnesses use strings of one common length and include the binary coding transfer","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/099.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":109459,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Finite-Circle-Obstructions-Binary-Codes-and-Histogram-Embeddings-for-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Finite-Circle-Obstructions-Binary-Codes-and-Histogram-Embeddings-for-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmr","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"099-03","slug":"Tree-Constructions-for-the-l1-Distortion-of-Binary-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026","familyId":"099","title":"Tree Constructions for the l1 Distortion of Binary Edit Distance","abstract":"We give two independent constructions of binary words of one length at most d whose ordinary edit-distance metrics require ℓ1 distortion $\\exp(\\Omega(\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d}))$ for every sufficiently large d. We also prove a constant-distortion binary conversion for one prescribed input length. Together with the companion upper embedding theorem, these lower bounds determine the order of logarithmic distortion uniformly over finite alphabets with at least two symbols.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Determines the least distortion of embedding edit distance on words of length at most d into real ℓ1: it is $\\exp(\\Theta(\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d}))$. Insertions, deletions and substitutions have unit cost. The constants are uniform over all finite alphabets with at least two symbols, even when the alphabet grows with d; binary words already force the lower bound.","pdfPath":"preprints/Tree-Constructions-for-the-l1-Distortion-of-Binary-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Tree-Constructions-for-the-l1-Distortion-of-Binary-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Tree-Constructions-for-the-l1-Distortion-of-Binary-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Tree-Constructions-for-the-l1-Distortion-of-Binary-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Tree-Constructions-for-the-l1-Distortion-of-Binary-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Tree-Constructions-for-the-l1-Distortion-of-Binary-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Tree-Constructions-for-the-l1-Distortion-of-Binary-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/figures/tagged-row-gap.tex","preprints/Tree-Constructions-for-the-l1-Distortion-of-Binary-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/sections/finite-tree.tex","preprints/Tree-Constructions-for-the-l1-Distortion-of-Binary-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/sections/torus-construction.tex","preprints/Tree-Constructions-for-the-l1-Distortion-of-Binary-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/sections/torus-obstruction.tex","preprints/Tree-Constructions-for-the-l1-Distortion-of-Binary-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex","preprints/Tree-Constructions-for-the-l1-Distortion-of-Binary-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/sections/polynomial-code.tex","preprints/Tree-Constructions-for-the-l1-Distortion-of-Binary-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Tree-Constructions-for-the-l1-Distortion-of-Binary-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give two independent constructions of binary words of one length at most $d$ whose ordinary edit-distance metrics require $\\ell_1$ distortion $\\exp(\\Omega(\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d}))$ for every sufficiently large $d$. We also prove a constant-distortion binary conversion for one prescribed input length. Together with the companion upper embedding theorem, these lower bounds determine the order of logarithmic distortion uniformly over finite alphabets with at least two symbols.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":572},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the embedding question","sourceOffset":3390},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two independent tree proofs","sourceOffset":7360},{"kind":"section","title":"Edit deficits, ordered rows and binary transfer","sourceOffset":10372},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Subsequence matchings","sourceOffset":10854},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The common alignment lemma","sourceOffset":11865},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cuts and prime counts","sourceOffset":17040},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A short binary code","sourceOffset":19843},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite tree with nested periods","sourceOffset":22579},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Periods and word construction","sourceOffset":23125},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cheap translations and uniform separation","sourceOffset":25372},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fourier detection of sparse activity","sourceOffset":28654}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Binary lower bound","label":"thm:binary","text":"There are an absolute constant $c>0$ and an integer $d_0$ such that, for every integer $d\\geq d_0$, there exist an integer $1\\leq n\\leq d$ and a finite set $\\mathcal W\\subseteq\\{0,1\\}^n$ with $|\\mathcal W|\\geq2$ satisfying \\[ c_1(\\mathcal W,\\ED) \\geq \\exp\\!\\left(c\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d}\\right). \\]","sourceOffset":2229,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Separation in the finite tree","label":"prop:finite-separation","text":"For every node $v$ of height $h$, every $x\\in A$, and every integer $t$ with $Q_v\\nmid t$, \\begin{equation} r_{L_v}\\bigl(G_v(x),G_v(x+t w_v)\\bigr)\\geq4^{-h}L_v. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":26836,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Good shift","label":"def:good","text":"At a leaf, $t\\in\\T$ is good if $|t|_{\\T}\\geq\\epsilon$. At an internal node $v$, it is good if, for every integer $a$ modulo $P_v$, at least $\\delta m$ children $vi$ have $t+a c_{vi}/P_v$ good at $vi$.","sourceOffset":38985,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/099.md","scopeText":"The formalization determines the exponential scale of the least $\\ell_1$ distortion of unit-cost edit distance on strings of length at most $d$. For every sufficiently large $d$, uniformly over finite alphabets with at least two symbols, the distortion lies between $\\exp(c\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d})$ and $\\exp(C\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d})$ for absolute constants $c,C>0$. The lower bound has a witness consisting of binary strings of one common length, and the same scale controls the supremum over finite alphabets.\n\nThe formalization gives two finite-circle constructions of binary strings whose least $\\ell_1$ distortion is at least $\\exp(c\\sqrt{\\log d\\,\\log\\log d})$ for all sufficiently large length caps $d$. The witnesses use strings of one common length and include the binary coding transfer","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/099.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":73131,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Tree-Constructions-for-the-l1-Distortion-of-Binary-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Tree-Constructions-for-the-l1-Distortion-of-Binary-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Tree-Constructions-for-the-l1-Distortion-of-Binary-Edit-Distance-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"100-01","slug":"Finite-angular-cylinder-covers-below-the-half-area-bound-September-27-2026","familyId":"100","title":"Finite angular cylinder covers below the half-area bound","abstract":"A regular tetrahedron admits a finite cylinder covering with compact triangular perpendicular bases whose total area is less than half its minimum orthogonal projection area. This disproves the half-area cylinder-covering conjecture. By affine invariance, the same construction gives a counterexample to the directionwise normalized half-bound for every nondegenerate tetrahedron.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Covers the entire closed regular tetrahedron by finitely many cylinders with compact triangular perpendicular bases whose total area is less than half its smallest orthogonal projection area. This disproves Bang's half-area cylinder-covering bound and the stronger directionwise normalized conjecture in dimension three.","pdfPath":"preprints/Finite-angular-cylinder-covers-below-the-half-area-bound-September-27-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-angular-cylinder-covers-below-the-half-area-bound-September-27-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-angular-cylinder-covers-below-the-half-area-bound-September-27-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Finite-angular-cylinder-covers-below-the-half-area-bound-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Finite-angular-cylinder-covers-below-the-half-area-bound-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Finite-angular-cylinder-covers-below-the-half-area-bound-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Finite-angular-cylinder-covers-below-the-half-area-bound-September-27-2026/build/sections/history.tex","preprints/Finite-angular-cylinder-covers-below-the-half-area-bound-September-27-2026/build/sections/construction.tex","preprints/Finite-angular-cylinder-covers-below-the-half-area-bound-September-27-2026/build/figures/construction.tex","preprints/Finite-angular-cylinder-covers-below-the-half-area-bound-September-27-2026/build/figures/shared-boundary-panel.tex","preprints/Finite-angular-cylinder-covers-below-the-half-area-bound-September-27-2026/build/sections/coverage.tex","preprints/Finite-angular-cylinder-covers-below-the-half-area-bound-September-27-2026/build/sections/area.tex","preprints/Finite-angular-cylinder-covers-below-the-half-area-bound-September-27-2026/build/sections/angular-extensions.tex","preprints/Finite-angular-cylinder-covers-below-the-half-area-bound-September-27-2026/build/sections/cubic-buffer.tex","preprints/Finite-angular-cylinder-covers-below-the-half-area-bound-September-27-2026/build/sections/alternative-estimates.tex","preprints/Finite-angular-cylinder-covers-below-the-half-area-bound-September-27-2026/build/sections/scaling-specializations.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Finite-angular-cylinder-covers-below-the-half-area-bound-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"A regular tetrahedron admits a finite cylinder covering with compact triangular perpendicular bases whose total area is less than half its minimum orthogonal projection area. This disproves the half-area cylinder-covering conjecture. By affine invariance, the same construction gives a counterexample to the directionwise normalized half-bound for every nondegenerate tetrahedron.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":426},{"kind":"section","title":"Geometry and the finite construction","sourceOffset":9423},{"kind":"section","title":"Coverage of the entire tetrahedron","sourceOffset":19477},{"kind":"section","title":"The strict area decrease","sourceOffset":25926},{"kind":"section","title":"Other caps and angular partitions","sourceOffset":30764},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A fixed positive margin","sourceOffset":31419},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Triangular and curved apertures","sourceOffset":36334},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sampling at the endpoints","sourceOffset":39817},{"kind":"section","title":"A vanishing radial margin","sourceOffset":41336},{"kind":"section","title":"Alternative coverage and area calculations","sourceOffset":45136},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Squared radii","sourceOffset":45733},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A Cartesian discriminant","sourceOffset":47971}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:counterexample","text":"The tetrahedron \\eqref{eq:tetrahedron} has $A_{\\min}(K)=\\sqrt2$. For every $0<\\eps\\le1/2000$, it admits a covering by $m=2\\lceil2/\\eps^2\\rceil$ cylinders with compact triangular perpendicular bases such that \\begin{equation} \\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\sum_{i=1}^m|B_i| =\\frac12-\\frac{13}{6000}\\eps^2+O(\\eps^4). \\end{equation} The remainder has absolute value at most $2\\eps^4$, and the total base area is strictly less than $A_{\\min}(K)/2$.","sourceOffset":4354,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:normalized-covering","text":"Every nondegenerate tetrahedron $T\\subset\\R^3$ admits a finite cylinder covering with compact triangular perpendicular bases for which \\[ \\sum_{i=1}^m\\frac{|B_i|}{|\\pi_{u_i^\\perp}T|}<\\frac12. \\] Thus the 1-Codimensional Cylinder Covering Conjecture is false.","sourceOffset":5107,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/100.md","scopeText":"The half-area cylinder-covering conjecture predicts that a cylinder cover of a convex body has total perpendicular-base area at least half its smallest projection area. The formalization constructs finite covers of a regular tetrahedron by cylinders with compact triangular bases whose total area is strictly below that bound. For the explicit small parameter $\\varepsilon$, the normalized area is $1/2-(13/6000)\\varepsilon^2+O(\\varepsilon^4)$, giving the strict saving.\n\nIt also gives a counterexample to the directionwise normalized half-bound. The broader Comparator file includes the companion ruled-set and radial-sweep approximation results.\n\nThe formalization approximates compact ruled families of line segments by finite cylinder covers with perpendicular-base area at most the integral proj","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/100.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":59066,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Finite-angular-cylinder-covers-below-the-half-area-bound-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"100-02","slug":"Finite-cylinder-approximation-of-ruled-sets-September-27-2026","familyId":"100","title":"Finite cylinder approximation of ruled sets","abstract":"We approximate compact ruled families of segments by finitely many cylinders with square intercept tiles and perpendicular-base area at most their integral projection cost plus any positive error. The velocity field is C1, and its differential has opposite real eigenvalues whose magnitudes are strictly below the inverse segment half-length; both eigenvalues may vanish. The result includes square-zero differentials with unrestricted shear and fields that pass between the two regimes. It holds for arbitrary compact label sets.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Covers the entire closed regular tetrahedron by finitely many cylinders with compact triangular perpendicular bases whose total area is less than half its smallest orthogonal projection area. This disproves Bang's half-area cylinder-covering bound and the stronger directionwise normalized conjecture in dimension three.","pdfPath":"preprints/Finite-cylinder-approximation-of-ruled-sets-September-27-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-cylinder-approximation-of-ruled-sets-September-27-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-cylinder-approximation-of-ruled-sets-September-27-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Finite-cylinder-approximation-of-ruled-sets-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Finite-cylinder-approximation-of-ruled-sets-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Finite-cylinder-approximation-of-ruled-sets-September-27-2026/build/sections/linear-coverings.tex","preprints/Finite-cylinder-approximation-of-ruled-sets-September-27-2026/build/figures/lattice.tex","preprints/Finite-cylinder-approximation-of-ruled-sets-September-27-2026/build/figures/sliding-rows.tex","preprints/Finite-cylinder-approximation-of-ruled-sets-September-27-2026/build/sections/finite-approximation.tex","preprints/Finite-cylinder-approximation-of-ruled-sets-September-27-2026/build/sections/product-methods.tex","preprints/Finite-cylinder-approximation-of-ruled-sets-September-27-2026/build/sections/comparison.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Finite-cylinder-approximation-of-ruled-sets-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We approximate compact ruled families of segments by finitely many cylinders with square intercept tiles and perpendicular-base area at most their integral projection cost plus any positive error. The velocity field is $C^1$, and its differential has opposite real eigenvalues whose magnitudes are strictly below the inverse segment half-length; both eigenvalues may vanish. The result includes square-zero differentials with unrestricted shear and fields that pass between the two regimes. It holds for arbitrary compact label sets.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The finite approximation problem","sourceOffset":579},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Segments, tiles, and their Euclidean cost","sourceOffset":2079},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why fixed squares suffice","sourceOffset":6306},{"kind":"section","title":"Square coverings for affine motion","sourceOffset":9070},{"kind":"section","title":"From affine coverings to a finite family","sourceOffset":16583},{"kind":"section","title":"Product cells and logarithmic packing","sourceOffset":22623},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Logarithmic cells and finite stopping","sourceOffset":32036},{"kind":"section","title":"Scope, physical coordinates, and applications","sourceOffset":35640}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"proposition","name":"Perpendicular base area","label":"b:projection","text":"Let $h>0$, let $T\\subset\\R^2$ be bounded and measurable, and let $g\\in\\R^2$. The image $F_h(\\Cyl(T,g))$ is a cylinder whose perpendicular base has area $h|T|J_h(g)$. A compact nondegenerate parallelogram tile has a compact nondegenerate parallelogram base. An open tile gives an open base. The formula is unchanged after a Euclidean isometry.","sourceOffset":3034,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Finite approximation by square tiles","label":"b:approximation","text":"Let $D\\subset\\R^2$ be compact, let $V$ be $C^1$ on an open neighborhood of $D$, and let $L,h>0$. Suppose that \\begin{equation} \\tr DV(p)=0,\\qquad -L^{-2}<\\det DV(p)\\le0 \\quad(p\\in D). \\end{equation} For every $\\varepsilon>0$, there are finitely many compact nondegenerate square tiles $T_i\\subset\\R^2$, with possibly different orientations, and velocities $g_i\\in\\R^2$ such that \\begin{equation} E(D,V,L)\\subset\\bigcup_i\\Cyl(T_i,g_i),\\qquad \\sum_i |T_i|J_h(g_i) \\le\\int_D J_h(V(p))\\,dp+\\varepsilon. \\end{equation} Under $F_h$, the perpendicular bases are compact nondegenerate parallelograms and have total area $h\\sum_i|T_i|J_h(g_i)$. No condition on the area of $\\partial D$ or smallness of $\\|DV\\|$ is required.","sourceOffset":4406,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/100.md","scopeText":"The half-area cylinder-covering conjecture predicts that a cylinder cover of a convex body has total perpendicular-base area at least half its smallest projection area. The formalization constructs finite covers of a regular tetrahedron by cylinders with compact triangular bases whose total area is strictly below that bound. For the explicit small parameter $\\varepsilon$, the normalized area is $1/2-(13/6000)\\varepsilon^2+O(\\varepsilon^4)$, giving the strict saving.\n\nIt also gives a counterexample to the directionwise normalized half-bound. The broader Comparator file includes the companion ruled-set and radial-sweep approximation results.\n\nThe formalization approximates compact ruled families of line segments by finite cylinder covers with perpendicular-base area at most the integral proj","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/100.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":38091,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Finite-cylinder-approximation-of-ruled-sets-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"100-03","slug":"Slope-field-perturbations-of-the-two-cylinder-covering-September-27-2026","familyId":"100","title":"Slope-field perturbations of the two-cylinder covering","abstract":"The two-cylinder covering of a regular tetrahedron can be perturbed to give finite covers with total perpendicular base area strictly below half its minimum projection area. These covers give negative answers to both the half-area question and the directionwise normalized half-bound conjecture. By affine invariance, the directionwise conclusion holds for every nondegenerate tetrahedron.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Covers the entire closed regular tetrahedron by finitely many cylinders with compact triangular perpendicular bases whose total area is less than half its smallest orthogonal projection area. This disproves Bang's half-area cylinder-covering bound and the stronger directionwise normalized conjecture in dimension three.","pdfPath":"preprints/Slope-field-perturbations-of-the-two-cylinder-covering-September-27-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Slope-field-perturbations-of-the-two-cylinder-covering-September-27-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Slope-field-perturbations-of-the-two-cylinder-covering-September-27-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Slope-field-perturbations-of-the-two-cylinder-covering-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Slope-field-perturbations-of-the-two-cylinder-covering-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Slope-field-perturbations-of-the-two-cylinder-covering-September-27-2026/build/sections/rational.tex","preprints/Slope-field-perturbations-of-the-two-cylinder-covering-September-27-2026/build/figures/equality.tex","preprints/Slope-field-perturbations-of-the-two-cylinder-covering-September-27-2026/build/sections/threshold.tex","preprints/Slope-field-perturbations-of-the-two-cylinder-covering-September-27-2026/build/sections/dictionary.tex","preprints/Slope-field-perturbations-of-the-two-cylinder-covering-September-27-2026/build/sections/singular.tex","preprints/Slope-field-perturbations-of-the-two-cylinder-covering-September-27-2026/build/sections/nilpotent.tex","preprints/Slope-field-perturbations-of-the-two-cylinder-covering-September-27-2026/build/sections/projection.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Slope-field-perturbations-of-the-two-cylinder-covering-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"The two-cylinder covering of a regular tetrahedron can be perturbed to give finite covers with total perpendicular base area strictly below half its minimum projection area. These covers give negative answers to both the half-area question and the directionwise normalized half-bound conjecture. By affine invariance, the directionwise conclusion holds for every nondegenerate tetrahedron.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":435},{"kind":"section","title":"A rational field and two choices of padding","sourceOffset":8414},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Inverting the tilted maps","sourceOffset":15818},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why the two families leave no gap","sourceOffset":17315},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The area comparison and finite choices","sourceOffset":19162},{"kind":"section","title":"A threshold proof for fixed quadratic padding","sourceOffset":22501},{"kind":"section","title":"Two coordinate forms and two finite realization methods","sourceOffset":26469},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Logarithmic-cell realization of the fixed-padding cover","sourceOffset":30418},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Product-cell realization of the centered cover","sourceOffset":33034},{"kind":"section","title":"A singular power-law field","sourceOffset":34321},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The field and the cutoff","sourceOffset":36261},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Inversion, including the cone boundary","sourceOffset":40465}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"c:main","text":"Every regular tetrahedron $K\\subset\\R^3$ of positive edge length admits a finite cylinder cover with compact parallelogram perpendicular bases such that \\[ \\sum_i|B_i|<\\tfrac12 A_{\\min}(K), \\qquad \\sum_i\\frac{|B_i|}{|\\pi_{u_i^\\perp}K|}<\\tfrac12. \\] It also admits finite covers with bounded open measurable bases satisfying both inequalities.","sourceOffset":2782,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"c:affine-tetrahedra","text":"Every nondegenerate tetrahedron $T\\subset\\R^3$ admits a finite cylinder cover $\\mathcal C$ with compact parallelogram perpendicular bases and $\\mathcal R(\\mathcal C;T)<1/2$. The same conclusion holds with bounded open measurable bases.","sourceOffset":3346,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/100.md","scopeText":"The half-area cylinder-covering conjecture predicts that a cylinder cover of a convex body has total perpendicular-base area at least half its smallest projection area. The formalization constructs finite covers of a regular tetrahedron by cylinders with compact triangular bases whose total area is strictly below that bound. For the explicit small parameter $\\varepsilon$, the normalized area is $1/2-(13/6000)\\varepsilon^2+O(\\varepsilon^4)$, giving the strict saving.\n\nIt also gives a counterexample to the directionwise normalized half-bound. The broader Comparator file includes the companion ruled-set and radial-sweep approximation results.\n\nThe formalization approximates compact ruled families of line segments by finite cylinder covers with perpendicular-base area at most the integral proj","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/100.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":68837,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Slope-field-perturbations-of-the-two-cylinder-covering-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"100-04","slug":"Finite-triangular-approximation-of-radial-sweeps-September-27-2026","familyId":"100","title":"Finite triangular approximation of radial sweeps","abstract":"We prove that radially aligned segment sweeps admit finite cylinder covers with one triangular base for each interval of any tagged partition. As the mesh tends to zero, the total perpendicular base area converges to a weighted parameter area. An explicit application covers every regular tetrahedron with total base area below half its minimum projection area, giving negative answers to the half-area question and the directionwise normalized 1-Codimensional Cylinder Covering Conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Covers the entire closed regular tetrahedron by finitely many cylinders with compact triangular perpendicular bases whose total area is less than half its smallest orthogonal projection area. This disproves Bang's half-area cylinder-covering bound and the stronger directionwise normalized conjecture in dimension three.","pdfPath":"preprints/Finite-triangular-approximation-of-radial-sweeps-September-27-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-triangular-approximation-of-radial-sweeps-September-27-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-triangular-approximation-of-radial-sweeps-September-27-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Finite-triangular-approximation-of-radial-sweeps-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Finite-triangular-approximation-of-radial-sweeps-September-27-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/Finite-triangular-approximation-of-radial-sweeps-September-27-2026/build/freezing.tex","preprints/Finite-triangular-approximation-of-radial-sweeps-September-27-2026/build/figures/triangle.tex","preprints/Finite-triangular-approximation-of-radial-sweeps-September-27-2026/build/tetrahedron.tex","preprints/Finite-triangular-approximation-of-radial-sweeps-September-27-2026/build/beams.tex","preprints/Finite-triangular-approximation-of-radial-sweeps-September-27-2026/build/projections.tex","preprints/Finite-triangular-approximation-of-radial-sweeps-September-27-2026/build/caps.tex","preprints/Finite-triangular-approximation-of-radial-sweeps-September-27-2026/build/footprints.tex","preprints/Finite-triangular-approximation-of-radial-sweeps-September-27-2026/build/coordinates.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Finite-triangular-approximation-of-radial-sweeps-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that radially aligned segment sweeps admit finite cylinder covers with one triangular base for each interval of any tagged partition. As the mesh tends to zero, the total perpendicular base area converges to a weighted parameter area. An explicit application covers every regular tetrahedron with total base area below half its minimum projection area, giving negative answers to the half-area question and the directionwise normalized 1-Codimensional Cylinder Covering Conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Radial sweeps and finite cylinders","sourceOffset":536},{"kind":"section","title":"A triangle around each frozen intercept","sourceOffset":7732},{"kind":"section","title":"Two overlapping sweeps in a tetrahedron","sourceOffset":13874},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The reference area","sourceOffset":14367},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The perturbed segments cover the whole tetrahedron","sourceOffset":15693},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite bases and the weighted cost","sourceOffset":19734},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The saving and the final finite choice","sourceOffset":20760},{"kind":"section","title":"Shorter beams and the strip between them","sourceOffset":22536},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Euclidean coordinates and the cost normalization","sourceOffset":23101},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compact beams and their finite coverings","sourceOffset":25148},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The points left between the beams","sourceOffset":28856},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite horizontal cylinders for the strip","sourceOffset":32452}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:radial","text":"For the data in \\eqref{eq:radial-sweep}, suppose that \\[ w'(t)=\\lambda(t)(a+tb)\\qquad(t\\in J) \\] for a continuous real function $\\lambda$. For any finite partition \\(\\alpha=t_0<t_1<\\cdots<t_N=\\beta\\), put \\(I_i=[t_{i-1},t_i]\\) and choose arbitrary tags \\(c_i\\in I_i\\). There are \\(N\\) cylinders covering \\(\\mathcal S\\), with axes parallel to \\(e+w(c_i)\\) and nondegenerate compact triangular perpendicular bases. These covers can be chosen so that their total base area tends to \\(\\mathcal A\\) as the mesh \\(\\delta=\\max_i|I_i|\\) tends to zero, uniformly over the partitions and tags. Alternatively, take each base to be the perpendicular projection of the actual compact sweep with \\(t\\in I_i\\), in the same frozen direction \\(e+w(c_i)\\). These \\(N\\) cylinders cover \\(\\mathcal S\\), and their total base area also tends to \\(\\mathcal A\\), uniformly over the partitions and tags.","sourceOffset":3602,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:tetrahedron","text":"Every regular tetrahedron $K\\subset\\R^3$ of positive edge length has a finite cylinder cover $B_i+\\R u_i$, with unit axes $u_i$ and nondegenerate compact triangular bases $B_i\\subset u_i^\\perp$, such that \\[ \\sum_i\\area(B_i)<\\frac{A_{\\min}(K)}2, \\qquad \\sum_i\\frac{\\area(B_i)}{\\area(\\pi_{u_i^\\perp}K)}<\\frac12. \\]","sourceOffset":5040,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/100.md","scopeText":"The half-area cylinder-covering conjecture predicts that a cylinder cover of a convex body has total perpendicular-base area at least half its smallest projection area. The formalization constructs finite covers of a regular tetrahedron by cylinders with compact triangular bases whose total area is strictly below that bound. For the explicit small parameter $\\varepsilon$, the normalized area is $1/2-(13/6000)\\varepsilon^2+O(\\varepsilon^4)$, giving the strict saving.\n\nIt also gives a counterexample to the directionwise normalized half-bound. The broader Comparator file includes the companion ruled-set and radial-sweep approximation results.\n\nThe formalization approximates compact ruled families of line segments by finite cylinder covers with perpendicular-base area at most the integral proj","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/100.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":85123,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Finite-triangular-approximation-of-radial-sweeps-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"101-01","slug":"A-sharp-entropy-bound-and-the-simplex-inequality-for-isotropic-constants-October-5-2026","familyId":"101","title":"A sharp entropy bound and the simplex inequality for isotropic constants","abstract":"We prove the strong isotropic constant conjecture: in each dimension, simplices are the unique maximizers of the isotropic constant among convex bodies. We also prove the sharp entropy bound $h(f)\\ge m+\\tfrac12\\log\\det\\mathop{\\mathrm{Cov}}\\nolimits (f)$ for every log-concave probability density on ℝm, with equality precisely for invertible affine images of products of one-sided exponential laws.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that simplices uniquely maximize the isotropic constant among convex bodies in every dimension, resolving the strong isotropic constant conjecture. Also establishes the sharp entropy lower bound for log-concave probability densities, with equality precisely for invertible affine images of products of one-sided exponential laws.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-sharp-entropy-bound-and-the-simplex-inequality-for-isotropic-constants-October-5-2026/isotropic-simplex.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-sharp-entropy-bound-and-the-simplex-inequality-for-isotropic-constants-October-5-2026/isotropic-simplex.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-sharp-entropy-bound-and-the-simplex-inequality-for-isotropic-constants-October-5-2026","subjectId":"8","subject":"Convex and metric geometry","subjectZh":"凸幾何與度量幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-sharp-entropy-bound-and-the-simplex-inequality-for-isotropic-constants-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/main.tex","preprints/A-sharp-entropy-bound-and-the-simplex-inequality-for-isotropic-constants-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/introduction.tex","preprints/A-sharp-entropy-bound-and-the-simplex-inequality-for-isotropic-constants-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/transport.tex","preprints/A-sharp-entropy-bound-and-the-simplex-inequality-for-isotropic-constants-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/matrix.tex","preprints/A-sharp-entropy-bound-and-the-simplex-inequality-for-isotropic-constants-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/scalar.tex","preprints/A-sharp-entropy-bound-and-the-simplex-inequality-for-isotropic-constants-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/approximation.tex","preprints/A-sharp-entropy-bound-and-the-simplex-inequality-for-isotropic-constants-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/rigidity.tex","preprints/A-sharp-entropy-bound-and-the-simplex-inequality-for-isotropic-constants-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/geometry.tex","preprints/A-sharp-entropy-bound-and-the-simplex-inequality-for-isotropic-constants-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/certificate.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-sharp-entropy-bound-and-the-simplex-inequality-for-isotropic-constants-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the strong isotropic constant conjecture: in each dimension, simplices are the unique maximizers of the isotropic constant among convex bodies. We also prove the sharp entropy bound $h(f)\\ge m+\\tfrac12\\log\\det\\Cov(f)$ for every log-concave probability density on $\\R^m$, with equality precisely for invertible affine images of products of one-sided exponential laws.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":422},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ideas of the proof","sourceOffset":5665},{"kind":"section","title":"Gaussian transport and a matrix surplus","sourceOffset":10341},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Gaussian parametrization","sourceOffset":11240},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Transport and the choice of coordinates","sourceOffset":15149},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The transport equation and its energy","sourceOffset":21770},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Keeping the eigenvalue separation","sourceOffset":25110},{"kind":"section","title":"Gaussian decomposition of the entropy deficit","sourceOffset":33017},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Covariance and the first Gaussian chaos","sourceOffset":36001},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An exact expansion with noncommuting factors","sourceOffset":40993},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The commutator and the weighted squares","sourceOffset":45654},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two scalar estimates and the final chaos bound","sourceOffset":49736}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"The smooth entropy bound with a remainder","label":"mat:entropy-smooth","text":"Let $m\\ge1$ be an integer and let $\\mu=e^{-V}\\,\\dd y$ be a probability measure on $\\R^m$, where $V\\in C^\\infty(\\R^m)$ and $c_-\\Id\\le D^2V\\le c_+\\Id$ for constants $0<c_-\\le c_+<\\infty$. Choose the affine normalization of Proposition \\ref{tr:normalization}, and let $A=q^{-1}(J)$ be the resulting Gaussian transport matrix field, so $\\E A=0$. Set \\[ X_r=\\E(x_rA),\\qquad Y=A-\\sum_{r=1}^m x_rX_r,\\qquad B=\\sum_{r=1}^mX_r^2. \\] If $\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_m$ are the eigenvalues of $B$, then \\begin{equation} 2h(\\mu)-2m-\\log\\det\\Cov(\\mu) \\ge \\frac1{500}\\E\\tr(Y^2) +\\frac1{1000}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\omega(\\lambda_i). \\end{equation} In particular, \\[ h(\\mu)\\ge m+\\frac12\\log\\det\\Cov(\\mu). \\]","sourceOffset":34083,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Absorption with a first-chaos remainder","label":"mat:scalar-absorption","text":"Let $A\\in L^2(\\gamma_m;\\operatorname{Sym}_m)$ satisfy $\\E A=0$, and define $X_r,Y,B$ by \\eqref{mat:chaos} and $S,T,H$ by \\eqref{mat:dual-matrices}. With the constants \\eqref{mat:dual-constants}, \\eqref{mat:constants}, and \\eqref{mat:completion-constants}, the functions \\eqref{mat:scalar-functions}, and $\\rho=\\epsilon\\Id+\\kappa S/s$, one has \\begin{equation} \\begin{split} &\\tr(T-\\log S)-\\tau(H\\rho p) +s\\tau\\left[Y^2\\left((b-\\beta)^2+ \\frac{\\alpha h_0^2}{4(1-\\epsilon-\\kappa)p}\\right)\\right]\\\\ &\\hspace{15mm}\\le\\frac{69}{500}\\|Y\\|^2 -\\frac1{1000}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\omega(\\lambda_i), \\end{split} \\end{equation} where $\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_m$ are the eigenvalues of $B$. All scalar factors in the expected traces are evaluated at $A$.","sourceOffset":50274,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":123993,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-sharp-entropy-bound-and-the-simplex-inequality-for-isotropic-constants-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"102-01","slug":"The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026","familyId":"102","title":"The Unique Games Theorem","abstract":"We prove the Unique Games Conjecture. For every fixed $\\varepsilon,\\delta\\in(0,1/2)$, we give a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from 3SAT to Unique Games over a fixed finite alphabet, with completeness at least $1-\\varepsilon$ and soundness at most δ.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Khot's Unique Games Conjecture. Independent direct reductions also establish NP-hardness, on unweighted graphs, of approximation beyond the Goemans–Williamson ratio for Max-Cut, below factor two for Vertex Cover, and within any fixed constant factor for Min-UnCut and directed feedback vertex set. These direct proofs use established PCP and Label Cover hardness results.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/figures/overview.tex","preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-preliminaries.tex","preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-latent.tex","preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/figures/latent-recursion.tex","preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-game.tex","preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-matrix.tex","preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/figures/advice.tex","preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-clean.tex","preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-finish.tex","preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-consequences.tex","preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/appendices/finite-consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Unique Games Conjecture. For every fixed \\(\\varepsilon,\\delta\\in(0,1/2)\\), we give a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from \\(\\mathrm{3SAT}\\) to Unique Games over a fixed finite alphabet, with completeness at least \\(1-\\varepsilon\\) and soundness at most \\(\\delta\\).","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":458},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The theorem","sourceOffset":854},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proofs, codes, and the quantifiers","sourceOffset":3163},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Grassmann expansion and the completeness obstacle","sourceOffset":6508},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and proof","sourceOffset":9019},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":21035},{"kind":"section","title":"A latent alphabet gadget","sourceOffset":24040},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The quadratic block","sourceOffset":26821},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Generic spaces of characters","sourceOffset":32810},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Concatenation and its noise","sourceOffset":40126},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Linear lifts and loss of rank","sourceOffset":46701},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Quotients and enlargement","sourceOffset":53131}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Unique Games Theorem","label":"thm:ugc","text":"For every fixed \\(\\varepsilon,\\delta\\in(0,1/2)\\), there are an integer \\(s\\ge1\\) and a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from \\(\\mathrm{3SAT}\\) to explicit unweighted Unique Games instances \\(G_\\varphi\\) over \\(K=\\F_2^s\\) such that \\[ \\begin{aligned} \\varphi\\text{ satisfiable} &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\val(G_\\varphi)\\ge1-\\varepsilon,\\\\ \\varphi\\text{ unsatisfiable} &\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\val(G_\\varphi)\\le\\delta. \\end{aligned} \\] The graph is simple and bipartite, and every constraint is a translation \\(a(v_e)=a(u_e)+c_e\\) of \\(K\\). The alphabet, the degree of the running-time polynomial, and its constants depend only on \\(\\varepsilon,\\delta\\).","sourceOffset":1508,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:latent-full-lift-detection","text":"Set \\begin{equation} s=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{H_{r_0}}{8\\theta}\\right\\rfloor. \\end{equation} Every full lift on $P_s$ detects $\\xi_s$ with probability at least $1/4$: \\begin{equation} \\Pr[\\text{there exists }z\\in B^*\\text{ with }\\psi_z(\\xi_s)\\ne0] \\ge\\frac14. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":49865,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"eq:latent-alignment","text":"A binary subspace $S\\le F^3$ of dimension $r\\ge1$ is generic if no two distinct nonzero elements of $S$ are proportional over $F$, and if, for every binary linear map $g:S\\to F^3$, at most $3r$ nonzero $z\\in S$ satisfy \\begin{equation} z\\cdot g(z)=\\sqrt{z_1z_2z_3}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":33186,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Projected advice experiment","label":"def:projected-advice","text":"Sample $U,O,\\pi$, then an independent uniform map $A:K\\to\\F_2^{r_{\\rm s}}$ and independent uniform maps \\[ T_O:L_O\\to W,\\qquad M_O:L_O\\to K. \\] Put $S_O=AM_O$ and send the following inputs to the two provers: \\begin{equation} \\begin{aligned} &\\text{$U$-prover:}&& (U,A,T_O\\bar\\pi,S_O\\bar\\pi),\\\\ &\\text{$O$-prover:}&& (O,A,T_O,S_O). \\end{aligned} \\end{equation} The map $M_O$ beyond the stated advice is hidden. Each prover returns an actual answer, that is, a vector of first bit one in its homogeneous answer space. They succeed when their answers $a_U,a_O$ satisfy $\\bar\\pi a_U=a_O$.","sourceOffset":83091,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/102.md","scopeText":"The Unique Games conjecture asks for hardness of distinguishing nearly satisfiable unique games from games of very small value. The formalized result gives, for every fixed $0<\\varepsilon,\\delta<1/2$, a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from binary 3SAT to nonempty unweighted simple bipartite unique games. Satisfiable inputs have value at least $1-\\varepsilon$ and unsatisfiable inputs have value at most $\\delta$. The alphabet is a fixed $\\mathbb F_2^s$ depending only on the errors, and every constraint is a translation.\n\nThe formalized result establishes hardness of approximating Max-Cut beyond the Goemans–Williamson constant $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{GW}}$. For every fixed $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{GW}}<\\alpha\\le1$, it gives a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from binary 3SAT to a strictly sep","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/102.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":194506,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"102-02","slug":"A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026","familyId":"102","title":"A Direct Proof of Optimal Max-Cut Hardness","abstract":"We prove that approximating Max-Cut on simple unweighted graphs within any fixed factor greater than the Goemans–Williamson constant is NP-hard.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Khot's Unique Games Conjecture. Independent direct reductions also establish NP-hardness, on unweighted graphs, of approximation beyond the Goemans–Williamson ratio for Max-Cut, below factor two for Vertex Cover, and within any fixed constant factor for Min-UnCut and directed feedback vertex set. These direct proofs use established PCP and Label Cover hardness results.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026/build/sections/tree.tex","preprints/A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026/build/figures/complementary-traversals.tex","preprints/A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026/build/sections/reduction.tex","preprints/A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026/build/sections/tree_fourier.tex","preprints/A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026/build/sections/hashing.tex","preprints/A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026/build/sections/extraction.tex","preprints/A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026/build/sections/decoding.tex","preprints/A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex","preprints/A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026/build/sections/affine_games.tex","preprints/A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026/build/sections/unweighted.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that approximating Max-Cut on simple unweighted graphs within any fixed factor greater than the Goemans--Williamson constant is NP-hard.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":192},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work and the direct route","sourceOffset":3155},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its main ingredients","sourceOffset":7162},{"kind":"section","title":"Preliminaries","sourceOffset":11822},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Affine rows and labels","sourceOffset":12514},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Boolean Fourier analysis","sourceOffset":14028},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Affine projection games","sourceOffset":16502},{"kind":"section","title":"A tree code with complementary traversals","sourceOffset":18548},{"kind":"section","title":"The Max-Cut reduction","sourceOffset":26455},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Vertices and edge distribution","sourceOffset":26944},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A large cut produces excess stability","sourceOffset":30121},{"kind":"section","title":"Fourier structure of the tree code","sourceOffset":33814}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed $\\alpha$ with $\\GW<\\alpha\\le1$, it is NP-hard to approximate Max-Cut within a factor of $\\alpha$. This holds for simple unweighted graphs.","sourceOffset":2264,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:gap","text":"Fix a rational $t\\in(0,1)$ and a constant $0<\\eps<(t-B(t))/4$. For graphs equipped with a rational probability distribution on edges, it is NP-hard to distinguish \\begin{align*} YES:\\quad&\\Val\\ge\\frac{1+t}{2}-\\eps,\\\\ NO:\\quad&\\Val\\le\\frac{1+B(t)}{2}+\\eps, \\end{align*} where $\\Val$ is the maximum cut weight.","sourceOffset":2772,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Tree code","label":"def:tree-code","text":"Fix integers $s\\ge2$ and $m\\ge1$, and put \\[ k=(2s)^m,\\qquad d=s^m. \\] Let $\\mathcal T$ be the complete rooted $2s$-ary tree of depth $m$. The children of each internal node $v$ are indexed by $(g,b)\\in[s]\\times\\{0,1\\}$. Its leaves are indexed by $[k]$. To each internal node assign a table $F_v:\\F^s\\to\\{-1,1\\}$, and to each leaf assign a row $R_i\\in H$. The word $W(R,F)\\in\\{-1,1\\}^{\\mathcal A}$ is defined pointwise as follows. At $a\\in\\mathcal A$, leaf $i$ has sign $(-1)^{R_i(a)}$. An internal node with child signs $(Z_{g,b})$ outputs \\begin{equation} \\left(\\prod_{g=1}^s Z_{g,0}\\right)F_v(z), ","sourceOffset":18931,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/102.md","scopeText":"The Unique Games conjecture asks for hardness of distinguishing nearly satisfiable unique games from games of very small value. The formalized result gives, for every fixed $0<\\varepsilon,\\delta<1/2$, a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from binary 3SAT to nonempty unweighted simple bipartite unique games. Satisfiable inputs have value at least $1-\\varepsilon$ and unsatisfiable inputs have value at most $\\delta$. The alphabet is a fixed $\\mathbb F_2^s$ depending only on the errors, and every constraint is a translation.\n\nThe formalized result establishes hardness of approximating Max-Cut beyond the Goemans–Williamson constant $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{GW}}$. For every fixed $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{GW}}<\\alpha\\le1$, it gives a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from binary 3SAT to a strictly sep","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/102.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":116815,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Direct-Proof-of-Optimal-Max-Cut-Hardness-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"102-03","slug":"The-Factor-Two-Hardness-Threshold-for-Vertex-Cover-September-23-2026","familyId":"102","title":"The Factor-Two Hardness Threshold for Vertex Cover","abstract":"We prove that minimum Vertex Cover is NP-hard to approximate within every fixed factor below two, even on simple unweighted graphs.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Khot's Unique Games Conjecture. Independent direct reductions also establish NP-hardness, on unweighted graphs, of approximation beyond the Goemans–Williamson ratio for Max-Cut, below factor two for Vertex Cover, and within any fixed constant factor for Min-UnCut and directed feedback vertex set. These direct proofs use established PCP and Label Cover hardness results.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Factor-Two-Hardness-Threshold-for-Vertex-Cover-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Factor-Two-Hardness-Threshold-for-Vertex-Cover-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Factor-Two-Hardness-Threshold-for-Vertex-Cover-September-23-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/The-Factor-Two-Hardness-Threshold-for-Vertex-Cover-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Factor-Two-Hardness-Threshold-for-Vertex-Cover-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-Factor-Two-Hardness-Threshold-for-Vertex-Cover-September-23-2026/build/sections/history.tex","preprints/The-Factor-Two-Hardness-Threshold-for-Vertex-Cover-September-23-2026/build/sections/source.tex","preprints/The-Factor-Two-Hardness-Threshold-for-Vertex-Cover-September-23-2026/build/sections/graph.tex","preprints/The-Factor-Two-Hardness-Threshold-for-Vertex-Cover-September-23-2026/build/sections/analysis.tex","preprints/The-Factor-Two-Hardness-Threshold-for-Vertex-Cover-September-23-2026/build/sections/restriction.tex","preprints/The-Factor-Two-Hardness-Threshold-for-Vertex-Cover-September-23-2026/build/figures/star.tex","preprints/The-Factor-Two-Hardness-Threshold-for-Vertex-Cover-September-23-2026/build/sections/lists.tex","preprints/The-Factor-Two-Hardness-Threshold-for-Vertex-Cover-September-23-2026/build/sections/decoding.tex","preprints/The-Factor-Two-Hardness-Threshold-for-Vertex-Cover-September-23-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Factor-Two-Hardness-Threshold-for-Vertex-Cover-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that minimum Vertex Cover is NP-hard to approximate within every fixed factor below two, even on simple unweighted graphs.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":178},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and the role of the method","sourceOffset":2019},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and the soundness mechanism","sourceOffset":5951},{"kind":"section","title":"The source problem and construction parameters","sourceOffset":12071},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Parameters for the construction","sourceOffset":13959},{"kind":"section","title":"The graph and completeness","sourceOffset":15239},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Queries and compatible labels","sourceOffset":15721},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Vertices and edges","sourceOffset":19454},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Completeness","sourceOffset":25645},{"kind":"section","title":"A fixed function from an independent set","sourceOffset":30013},{"kind":"section","title":"A restriction preserving own-question variance","sourceOffset":35474},{"kind":"section","title":"Lists from own-question conditional means","sourceOffset":52707}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Explicit cover gap","label":"thm:gap","text":"For every fixed integer \\(m\\geq4\\), there is a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from \\(\\mathrm{3SAT}\\) to finite simple undirected unweighted graphs with the following guarantees: \\[ \\begin{array}{ll} \\text{the formula is satisfiable} &\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\tau(G)<\\left(\\dfrac12+\\dfrac1m\\right)|V(G)|,\\\\[6pt] \\text{the formula is unsatisfiable} &\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\tau(G)>\\left(1-\\dfrac1m\\right)|V(G)|. \\end{array} \\] The output lists all vertices and edges explicitly.","sourceOffset":822,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"thm:hardness","text":"For every fixed real \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(1\\leq\\alpha<2\\), approximating Vertex Cover within factor \\(\\alpha\\) is NP-hard. Thus, unless \\(\\mathrm P=\\mathrm{NP}\\), no deterministic polynomial-time algorithm achieves factor \\(2-\\varepsilon\\) for any fixed \\(\\varepsilon\\in(0,1]\\).","sourceOffset":1374,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/102.md","scopeText":"The Unique Games conjecture asks for hardness of distinguishing nearly satisfiable unique games from games of very small value. The formalized result gives, for every fixed $0<\\varepsilon,\\delta<1/2$, a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from binary 3SAT to nonempty unweighted simple bipartite unique games. Satisfiable inputs have value at least $1-\\varepsilon$ and unsatisfiable inputs have value at most $\\delta$. The alphabet is a fixed $\\mathbb F_2^s$ depending only on the errors, and every constraint is a translation.\n\nThe formalized result establishes hardness of approximating Max-Cut beyond the Goemans–Williamson constant $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{GW}}$. For every fixed $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{GW}}<\\alpha\\le1$, it gives a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from binary 3SAT to a strictly sep","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/102.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":80819,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Factor-Two-Hardness-Threshold-for-Vertex-Cover-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"102-04","slug":"Constant-factor-hardness-of-Min-UnCut-September-23-2026","familyId":"102","title":"Constant-factor hardness of Min-UnCut","abstract":"For every fixed C > 1, approximating Min-UnCut within factor C is NP-hard, even on simple undirected unweighted graphs.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Khot's Unique Games Conjecture. Independent direct reductions also establish NP-hardness, on unweighted graphs, of approximation beyond the Goemans–Williamson ratio for Max-Cut, below factor two for Vertex Cover, and within any fixed constant factor for Min-UnCut and directed feedback vertex set. These direct proofs use established PCP and Label Cover hardness results.","pdfPath":"preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-Min-UnCut-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-Min-UnCut-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-Min-UnCut-September-23-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-Min-UnCut-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-Min-UnCut-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-Min-UnCut-September-23-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-Min-UnCut-September-23-2026/build/sections/inner-smoothing.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-Min-UnCut-September-23-2026/build/sections/inner-box.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-Min-UnCut-September-23-2026/build/figures/independent-square.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-Min-UnCut-September-23-2026/build/sections/outer.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-Min-UnCut-September-23-2026/build/sections/composition.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-Min-UnCut-September-23-2026/build/sections/finite.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-Min-UnCut-September-23-2026/build/figures/path-demands.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-Min-UnCut-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed \\(C>1\\), approximating Min-UnCut within factor \\(C\\) is NP-hard, even on simple undirected unweighted graphs.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":188},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":5222},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Relation to earlier methods","sourceOffset":10758},{"kind":"section","title":"Preliminaries and unconditional starting theorems","sourceOffset":12734},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Affine alphabets and odd characters","sourceOffset":13114},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Gaussian identities","sourceOffset":14934},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The starting gap and repetition","sourceOffset":17601},{"kind":"section","title":"The bit test and its smoothed correlation","sourceOffset":20796},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Folded proofs, face arrays, and queries","sourceOffset":21126},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The decoding statement","sourceOffset":26971},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Quantitative smoothing and degree projections","sourceOffset":29064},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Truth-table differentiation and a product-of-faces correlation","sourceOffset":35989}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed integer $K\\ge2$, there is a deterministic polynomial-time many-one reduction mapping a $3$SAT formula $\\varphi$ to a pair $(G,k)$, where $G$ is an explicit finite simple undirected unweighted graph and $k\\ge1$ is a binary-encoded integer, such that \\[ \\begin{aligned} \\varphi\\text{ satisfiable}&\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\OPT_{\\rm uncut}(G)\\le k,\\\\ \\varphi\\text{ unsatisfiable}&\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\OPT_{\\rm uncut}(G)>Kk. \\end{aligned} \\] For each fixed $K$, the graph size, full construction work, and bit complexity are polynomial in the bit length of $\\varphi$. All constants are effective; no advice or oracle is supplied to the reduction.","sourceOffset":2964,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:min-2cnf-deletion","text":"For every fixed real $C>1$, minimum $2$CNF clause deletion is NP-hard to approximate within factor $C$. In particular, a deterministic polynomial-time $C$-approximation would imply $\\mathsf P=\\NP$.","sourceOffset":4828,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/102.md","scopeText":"The Unique Games conjecture asks for hardness of distinguishing nearly satisfiable unique games from games of very small value. The formalized result gives, for every fixed $0<\\varepsilon,\\delta<1/2$, a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from binary 3SAT to nonempty unweighted simple bipartite unique games. Satisfiable inputs have value at least $1-\\varepsilon$ and unsatisfiable inputs have value at most $\\delta$. The alphabet is a fixed $\\mathbb F_2^s$ depending only on the errors, and every constraint is a translation.\n\nThe formalized result establishes hardness of approximating Max-Cut beyond the Goemans–Williamson constant $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{GW}}$. For every fixed $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{GW}}<\\alpha\\le1$, it gives a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from binary 3SAT to a strictly sep","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/102.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":119246,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Constant-factor-hardness-of-Min-UnCut-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"102-05","slug":"Constant-factor-hardness-of-directed-feedback-vertex-set-September-23-2026","familyId":"102","title":"Constant-factor hardness of directed feedback vertex set","abstract":"Approximating minimum directed feedback vertex set within any fixed constant factor is NP-hard, even on unweighted digraphs.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Khot's Unique Games Conjecture. Independent direct reductions also establish NP-hardness, on unweighted graphs, of approximation beyond the Goemans–Williamson ratio for Max-Cut, below factor two for Vertex Cover, and within any fixed constant factor for Min-UnCut and directed feedback vertex set. These direct proofs use established PCP and Label Cover hardness results.","pdfPath":"preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-directed-feedback-vertex-set-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-directed-feedback-vertex-set-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-directed-feedback-vertex-set-September-23-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-directed-feedback-vertex-set-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-directed-feedback-vertex-set-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-directed-feedback-vertex-set-September-23-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-directed-feedback-vertex-set-September-23-2026/build/sections/construction.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-directed-feedback-vertex-set-September-23-2026/build/sections/ranks.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-directed-feedback-vertex-set-September-23-2026/build/sections/prefix.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-directed-feedback-vertex-set-September-23-2026/build/figures/prefix-refinement.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-directed-feedback-vertex-set-September-23-2026/build/sections/pivotality.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-directed-feedback-vertex-set-September-23-2026/build/sections/soundness.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-directed-feedback-vertex-set-September-23-2026/build/sections/unweighting.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-directed-feedback-vertex-set-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Approximating minimum directed feedback vertex set within any fixed constant factor is NP-hard, even on unweighted digraphs.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":187},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The approximation question","sourceOffset":855},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Methodological background","sourceOffset":4111},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The reduction and its new ingredients","sourceOffset":5780},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":11152},{"kind":"section","title":"Games and Boolean functions","sourceOffset":11787},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The game used in the reduction","sourceOffset":12195},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Junta approximation at fixed dyadic biases","sourceOffset":14653},{"kind":"section","title":"The weighted reduction","sourceOffset":19023},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Choice of parameters","sourceOffset":19575},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rank vertices and their identifications","sourceOffset":22568},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Wildcard vertices and prefix data","sourceOffset":26026}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed real constant $A\\ge1$, it is NP-hard to approximate minimum directed feedback vertex set within factor $A$ on unweighted digraphs. More precisely, there is a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from an NP-hard promise problem to instances $(G,k)$ with $k\\in\\N_{>0}$. The polynomial and its constants may depend on $A$. The reduction has the following alternatives: \\[ YES: \\DFVS(G)\\le k, \\qquad NO: \\DFVS(G)>Ak. \\]","sourceOffset":2646,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Two-to-one games with imperfect completeness","label":"thm:games","text":"For every constant \\(\\theta\\in(0,1/2)\\), there are constant label sets \\(X,Y\\) such that it is \\(\\mathrm{NP}\\)-hard, by a deterministic polynomial-time reduction, to distinguish two-to-one games of the following kinds: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item there is a labeling of value at least \\(1-\\theta\\); \\item every labeling has value at most \\(\\theta\\). \\end{enumerate} Every constraint is a total map \\(X\\to Y\\) with exactly two preimages of each label in \\(Y\\). The label sets may depend on \\(\\theta\\).","sourceOffset":12871,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Rank graph","label":"def:rank-graph","text":"For $w\\in U\\cup V$, let $\\Lambda_w=X$ if $w\\in U$ and $\\Lambda_w=Y$ if $w\\in V$, and put $m_w=\\abs{\\Lambda_w}$. The allowed vertex tuples are \\[ \\bigcup_{t=0}^{T}U^t \\quad\\cup\\quad \\bigcup_{t=0}^{T-1}(U^t\\times V). \\] Tuples are ordered sequences and may contain repeated vertices. For an allowed tuple $\\mathbf d=(w_1,\\ldots,w_t)$, let $\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}=\\prod_{j=1}^t\\Lambda_{w_j}$; the product for the empty tuple is a singleton. In every case \\begin{equation} \\abs{\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}}\\le m^T. \\end{equation} For every function $x:\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}\\to[N]$, introduce a formal rank vertex $b_{","sourceOffset":22622,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Wildcard vertex","label":"def:wildcard","text":"Fix an allowed tuple $\\mathbf d$, an increasing map $\\psi$, an ordered partition $C_1,\\ldots,C_k$ of $\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}$ with $1\\le k\\le k_{\\max}$, and a set $Z\\subseteq\\Lambda_{\\mathbf d}$, called the star set. Empty cells in the partition are permitted. Put $z_\\ell=\\psi(10\\ell+10)$ for $\\ell\\in[k]$. The corresponding wildcard vertex has an incoming arc from every rank vertex $b_{\\mathbf d}(x)$ for which \\begin{equation} x(\\lambda)<z_\\ell \\quad\\text{for all }\\ell\\in[k] \\text{ and }\\lambda\\in C_\\ell\\setminus Z, \\end{equation} and an outgoing arc to every $b_{\\mathbf d}(x)$ satisfying the str","sourceOffset":26074,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/102.md","scopeText":"The Unique Games conjecture asks for hardness of distinguishing nearly satisfiable unique games from games of very small value. The formalized result gives, for every fixed $0<\\varepsilon,\\delta<1/2$, a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from binary 3SAT to nonempty unweighted simple bipartite unique games. Satisfiable inputs have value at least $1-\\varepsilon$ and unsatisfiable inputs have value at most $\\delta$. The alphabet is a fixed $\\mathbb F_2^s$ depending only on the errors, and every constraint is a translation.\n\nThe formalized result establishes hardness of approximating Max-Cut beyond the Goemans–Williamson constant $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{GW}}$. For every fixed $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{GW}}<\\alpha\\le1$, it gives a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from binary 3SAT to a strictly sep","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/102.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":95353,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":7,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Constant-factor-hardness-of-directed-feedback-vertex-set-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"103-01","slug":"Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026","familyId":"103","title":"Exact derandomization of logarithmic space: L = RL = BPL","abstract":"We prove $\\mathsf L=\\mathsf{RL}=\\mathsf{BPL}$, resolving the derandomization problem for polynomial-time randomized logarithmic space.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves $\\mathsf L=\\mathsf{RL}=\\mathsf{BPL}$, resolving derandomization for bounded-error logarithmic-space computation. An effective compiler converts each randomized polynomial-time logarithmic-space machine deciding a language with one-sided or two-sided error into a deterministic logarithmic-space decider with explicit polynomial running-time bounds.","pdfPath":"preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/build/sections/model.tex","preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/build/sections/environment.tex","preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/build/sections/hierarchy.tex","preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/build/sections/atoms.tex","preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/build/sections/averaging.tex","preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/build/sections/estimation.tex","preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/build/sections/compression.tex","preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/build/sections/fingerprints.tex","preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/build/sections/arithmetic.tex","preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/build/sections/implementation.tex","preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/build/figures/controller-space.tex","preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex","preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/build/sections/compiler.tex","preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/build/figures/compiler-padding.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove $\\Lclass=\\RL=\\BPL$, resolving the derandomization problem for polynomial-time randomized logarithmic space.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":191},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":3687},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":10817},{"kind":"section","title":"The transition model and resource parameters","sourceOffset":19976},{"kind":"section","title":"Rank channels for retained vertex sets","sourceOffset":27635},{"kind":"section","title":"The exact correction and copy hierarchy","sourceOffset":32195},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Correction with a fixed column multiplier","sourceOffset":33804},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite pilot chooses the column multiplier","sourceOffset":39870},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite residual","sourceOffset":48166},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Copies reduce density while controlling active vertices","sourceOffset":51448},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reward transport and the final deterministic target","sourceOffset":56699},{"kind":"section","title":"Stochastic atoms and their interfaces","sourceOffset":61187}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Exact logarithmic-space derandomization","label":"thm:main","text":"\\[ \\Lclass=\\RL=\\BPL. \\]","sourceOffset":1604,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Acceptance-probability approximation","label":"thm:probability","text":"Fix a randomized machine $\\mathcal M$ with polynomial worst-case running time and $O(\\log(n+2))$ work space. Let $p_{\\mathcal M}(x)$ be its acceptance probability on an input $x$ of length $n$. There is a uniform deterministic algorithm which, on input $(x,1^q)$ with $q\\ge1$, outputs an integer $0\\le a\\le2^{q+2}$ satisfying \\[ \\left|a2^{-(q+2)}-p_{\\mathcal M}(x)\\right|\\le2^{-q}. \\] It uses $O(\\log(n+2)+q)$ work space and $(n+2)^{O(1)}2^{O(q)}$ time. The constants depend only on $\\mathcal M$.","sourceOffset":2039,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Regenerable record","label":"def:ctrl-record","text":"A local record is a delimited binary word with a tag from a fixed finite alphabet. It may contain immediate descriptor choices, bounded port or copy symbols, offsets, Boolean evaluation paths and pending bits, and the early numerical words permitted by Theorem \\ref{thm:arith-interface}. Head positions in such words are marked locally. A root tuple, a root operation and its numerical clock, together with the active records, determine every descendant tuple and clock by a scratch computation. This computation does not call a graph procedure and does not use a cursor that has been left behind. We","sourceOffset":275731,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Raw allowance certificate","label":"def:ctrl-allowance","text":"A raw controller is the caller's own selection, arithmetic, scoring or terminal-path record, excluding the working states of a query it calls. In a terminal program graph an explicit chain contains only its terminal-path controllers and, in a scoring program, its score suffix or completed score word. The interiors of cap traversals, inverse score ranks, numerical digit queries and paired motions are opaque queries; they are never explicitly pushed working graphs in this chain. A raw controller has an integer allowance $w\\ge1$ and a numerical clock $t\\ge0$. Fixed raw constants $a_0,b_0,a'>0$ gi","sourceOffset":277450,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/103.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves $\\mathsf L=\\mathsf{RL}=\\mathsf{BPL}$ for binary languages in an explicit Turing-machine model with logarithmic workspace. The randomized classes use polynomial time: $\\mathsf{RL}$ never accepts a no-instance and accepts a yes-instance with probability at least $1/2$, while $\\mathsf{BPL}$ has acceptance probability at least $2/3$ on yes-instances and at most $1/3$ on no-instances. Thus bounded-error randomness does not enlarge deterministic logarithmic space in this model.\n\nThe selected theorem asserts equality of the language classes. The paper's explicit compiler and numerical running-time bounds are not separately stated in this Comparator target.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/103.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":395456,"sourceFileCount":17,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Exact-Derandomization-of-Logarithmic-Space-L-equals-RL-equals-BPL-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"104-01","slug":"Turn-Based-Stochastic-Mean-Payoff-Games-in-Deterministic-Quasipolynomial-Time-October-5-2026","familyId":"104","title":"Turn-Based Stochastic Mean-Payoff Games in Deterministic Quasipolynomial Time","abstract":"We give a uniform deterministic quasipolynomial-time algorithm for finite turn-based stochastic mean-payoff games with signed integer rewards and rational chance-transition probabilities encoded in binary. It computes exactly the vertices of nonnegative value, including value zero, for the expectation of the pathwise liminf mean payoff. The algorithm uses exact rational arithmetic and $2^{O((\\log(L+2))^2)}$ bit operations, where L is the complete binary input length.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives deterministic algorithms using $2^{O((\\log(L+2))^2)}$ bit operations, for complete binary input length L, for ordinary mean-payoff games and two separate extensions. They compute exact values and optimal positional strategies in ordinary games, the nonnegative expectation-of-liminf value set in turn-based stochastic games, and the winning set for nonnegative liminf mean payoff conjoined with parity. Signed rewards, rational chance probabilities, and parity priorities are unrestricted and binary-encoded.","pdfPath":"preprints/Turn-Based-Stochastic-Mean-Payoff-Games-in-Deterministic-Quasipolynomial-Time-October-5-2026/stochastic-mean-payoff-games.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Turn-Based-Stochastic-Mean-Payoff-Games-in-Deterministic-Quasipolynomial-Time-October-5-2026/stochastic-mean-payoff-games.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Turn-Based-Stochastic-Mean-Payoff-Games-in-Deterministic-Quasipolynomial-Time-October-5-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Turn-Based-Stochastic-Mean-Payoff-Games-in-Deterministic-Quasipolynomial-Time-October-5-2026/build/build/main.tex","preprints/Turn-Based-Stochastic-Mean-Payoff-Games-in-Deterministic-Quasipolynomial-Time-October-5-2026/build/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Turn-Based-Stochastic-Mean-Payoff-Games-in-Deterministic-Quasipolynomial-Time-October-5-2026/build/build/sections/discount.tex","preprints/Turn-Based-Stochastic-Mean-Payoff-Games-in-Deterministic-Quasipolynomial-Time-October-5-2026/build/build/sections/labeling.tex","preprints/Turn-Based-Stochastic-Mean-Payoff-Games-in-Deterministic-Quasipolynomial-Time-October-5-2026/build/build/figures/labeling.tex","preprints/Turn-Based-Stochastic-Mean-Payoff-Games-in-Deterministic-Quasipolynomial-Time-October-5-2026/build/build/sections/application.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Turn-Based-Stochastic-Mean-Payoff-Games-in-Deterministic-Quasipolynomial-Time-October-5-2026/build/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a uniform deterministic quasipolynomial-time algorithm for finite turn-based stochastic mean-payoff games with signed integer rewards and rational chance-transition probabilities encoded in binary. It computes exactly the vertices of nonnegative value, including value zero, for the expectation of the pathwise liminf mean payoff. The algorithm uses exact rational arithmetic and $2^{O((\\log(L+2))^2)}$ bit operations, where $L$ is the complete binary input length.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":519},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The problem and the result","sourceOffset":1058},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":3064},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its main ideas","sourceOffset":7086},{"kind":"section","title":"A quantitative discount reduction","sourceOffset":10650},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A positional pair valid for all small discounts","sourceOffset":14730},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The rational gain and its approximation error","sourceOffset":17958},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From one-step inequalities to pathwise payoffs","sourceOffset":20372},{"kind":"section","title":"Simultaneous labeling for discounted monotone maps","sourceOffset":26596},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Witnesses and the labeling guarantee","sourceOffset":27731},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The recursive procedure","sourceOffset":31707},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Translating a witness into a centered box","sourceOffset":38600}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are a uniform deterministic Turing machine and an absolute constant $C$ such that, on every valid input described above, the machine outputs exactly $U$ in at most \\[ 2^{C(\\log_2(L+2))^2} \\] bit operations. The bound includes input reading, exact rational arithmetic, and output. Vertices of value zero are included.","sourceOffset":2670,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Quantitative discount reduction","label":"thm:discount","text":"For every game in the input class, there are vectors $G,\\psi\\in\\R^V$ and a pure positional strategy pair $(\\sigma^*,\\tau^*)$ whose selected edges attain the extrema defining $f_u(v(u))$ for every $0<u\\le1/(2H)$. Moreover, \\begin{equation} v_i(u)=G_i+u\\psi_i+O(u^2) \\qquad(u\\downarrow0). \\end{equation} For every start vertex $i$ and every behavioral opposing strategy, \\begin{equation} \\E_{i,\\sigma^*,\\tau}[\\operatorname{MP}_w]\\ge G_i, \\qquad \\E_{i,\\sigma,\\tau^*}[\\operatorname{MP}_w]\\le G_i. \\end{equation} Consequently $G_i=\\val(i)$. Each $G_i$ is rational with a representation whose denominator has absolute value at most $A_0$, and \\begin{equation} \\begin{gathered} |G_i|\\le W, \\qquad G_i\\ne0\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ |G_i|\\ge\\frac1H,\\\\ |v_i(\\lambda_0)-G_i|\\le\\frac1{16H}. \\end{gathered} \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":13400,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Witnesses and claims","label":"def:label-witness","text":"An integer vector $y\\in[A,u]$ is a subsolution in the box if \\[ F_i(y)\\ge y_i\\quad\\text{for every }i\\in S_y, \\qquad S_y=\\{i:y_i>A_i\\}. \\] It is a plus witness if also $a(S_y)\\le1$; its claims are the indices $i$ for which $y_i\\ge u_i-P$. An integer vector $z\\in[A,u]$ is a supersolution in the box if \\[ F_i(z)\\le z_i\\quad\\text{for every }i\\in S_z, \\qquad S_z=\\{i:z_i<u_i\\}. \\] It is a minus witness if also $b(S_z)\\le1$; its claims are the indices $i$ for which $z_i\\le A_i+P$. The sets $S_y,S_z$ are the respective supports. A witness is relevant if it has at least one claim.","sourceOffset":28265,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/104.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":62836,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Turn-Based-Stochastic-Mean-Payoff-Games-in-Deterministic-Quasipolynomial-Time-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"104-02","slug":"Mean-payoff-parity-games-in-quasipolynomial-time-October-5-2026","familyId":"104","title":"Mean-payoff parity games in quasipolynomial time","abstract":"We give a uniform deterministic quasipolynomial-time algorithm for mean-payoff parity games. It computes all vertices from which a player can enforce both nonnegative liminf mean payoff and the parity condition, with arbitrary signed binary rewards and unrestricted binary priorities. The running time is $2^{O((\\log(L+2))^2)}$ bit operations, where L is the complete input length.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives deterministic algorithms using $2^{O((\\log(L+2))^2)}$ bit operations, for complete binary input length L, for ordinary mean-payoff games and two separate extensions. They compute exact values and optimal positional strategies in ordinary games, the nonnegative expectation-of-liminf value set in turn-based stochastic games, and the winning set for nonnegative liminf mean payoff conjoined with parity. Signed rewards, rational chance probabilities, and parity priorities are unrestricted and binary-encoded.","pdfPath":"preprints/Mean-payoff-parity-games-in-quasipolynomial-time-October-5-2026/mean-payoff-parity.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Mean-payoff-parity-games-in-quasipolynomial-time-October-5-2026/mean-payoff-parity.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Mean-payoff-parity-games-in-quasipolynomial-time-October-5-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Mean-payoff-parity-games-in-quasipolynomial-time-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Mean-payoff-parity-games-in-quasipolynomial-time-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Mean-payoff-parity-games-in-quasipolynomial-time-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Mean-payoff-parity-games-in-quasipolynomial-time-October-5-2026/build/sections/history.tex","preprints/Mean-payoff-parity-games-in-quasipolynomial-time-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-arenas.tex","preprints/Mean-payoff-parity-games-in-quasipolynomial-time-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-strategies.tex","preprints/Mean-payoff-parity-games-in-quasipolynomial-time-October-5-2026/build/figures/round.tex","preprints/Mean-payoff-parity-games-in-quasipolynomial-time-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-algorithm.tex","preprints/Mean-payoff-parity-games-in-quasipolynomial-time-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-complexity.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Mean-payoff-parity-games-in-quasipolynomial-time-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a uniform deterministic quasipolynomial-time algorithm for mean-payoff parity games. It computes all vertices from which a player can enforce both nonnegative liminf mean payoff and the parity condition, with arbitrary signed binary rewards and unrestricted binary priorities. The running time is $2^{O((\\log(L+2))^2)}$ bit operations, where $L$ is the complete input length.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":429},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and the role of this reduction","sourceOffset":4851},{"kind":"section","title":"Mean-payoff input and arena restrictions","sourceOffset":8985},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The ordinary mean-payoff interface","sourceOffset":9955},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Attractors and dominions","sourceOffset":12241},{"kind":"section","title":"Combining strategies and extracting dominions","sourceOffset":15794},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Combination across an attractor","sourceOffset":17163},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Determinacy with uniform prefix bounds","sourceOffset":24069},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A dominion outside the least-priority attractor","sourceOffset":28006},{"kind":"section","title":"Recursion with bounded-dominion guarantees","sourceOffset":30440},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The procedure","sourceOffset":31663},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Termination and correctness","sourceOffset":34916}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"A uniform deterministic algorithm returns exactly all vertices from which Max can force \\eqref{eq:objective} against every Min strategy. For an absolute constant $C>0$, it uses at most \\[ 2^{C(\\log_2(L+2))^2} \\] bit operations. The number of distinct priorities and the magnitudes of the rewards are unrestricted.","sourceOffset":1988,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Mean-payoff input","label":"prop:mp-interface","text":"For a finite nonempty total arena $H$ with signed binary integer weights, its ordinary mean-payoff winning set \\[ U_{\\mathrm{mp}}(H)= \\{v:\\text{Max can force liminf mean payoff at least }0\\} \\] is computable deterministically in $2^{O((\\log_2(L_H+2))^2)}$ bit operations, where $L_H$ is its full binary encoding length. There is a single positional Max strategy winning from every vertex of $U_{\\mathrm{mp}}(H)$, and a single positional Min strategy forcing strictly negative liminf mean payoff from every vertex outside it. Both guarantees hold against every history-dependent opposing strategy.","sourceOffset":10004,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/104.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":45365,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Mean-payoff-parity-games-in-quasipolynomial-time-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"104-03","slug":"Deterministic-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026","familyId":"104","title":"Deterministic quasipolynomial-time mean-payoff games","abstract":"We give a deterministic algorithm that computes the complete zero-threshold winning set of a finite mean-payoff game with arbitrary signed integer edge weights encoded in binary. For total explicit input length L, it uses $2^{O((\\log(L+2))^2)}$ bit operations. A reduction also computes the exact rational value at every vertex and globally optimal positional strategies for both players within the same quasipolynomial bound.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives deterministic algorithms using $2^{O((\\log(L+2))^2)}$ bit operations, for complete binary input length L, for ordinary mean-payoff games and two separate extensions. They compute exact values and optimal positional strategies in ordinary games, the nonnegative expectation-of-liminf value set in turn-based stochastic games, and the winning set for nonnegative liminf mean payoff conjoined with parity. Signed rewards, rational chance probabilities, and parity priorities are unrestricted and binary-encoded.","pdfPath":"preprints/Deterministic-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Deterministic-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Deterministic-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Deterministic-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Deterministic-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/preamble.tex","preprints/Deterministic-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Deterministic-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/02-boxes.tex","preprints/Deterministic-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/03-translations.tex","preprints/Deterministic-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/04-algorithm.tex","preprints/Deterministic-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/figures/recursion.tex","preprints/Deterministic-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/05-correctness.tex","preprints/Deterministic-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/06-complexity.tex","preprints/Deterministic-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/07-values-and-strategies.tex","preprints/Deterministic-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/08-energy-credits.tex","preprints/Deterministic-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/09-tropical.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Deterministic-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a deterministic algorithm that computes the complete zero-threshold winning set of a finite mean-payoff game with arbitrary signed integer edge weights encoded in binary. For total explicit input length $L$, it uses $2^{O((\\log(L+2))^2)}$ bit operations. A reduction also computes the exact rational value at every vertex and globally optimal positional strategies for both players within the same quasipolynomial bound.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":557},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The problem and the main theorem","sourceOffset":1189},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier algorithms and binary weights","sourceOffset":3363},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the proof works","sourceOffset":6280},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Potential methods and recursive precision","sourceOffset":10723},{"kind":"section","title":"From potential comparisons to a labelling problem","sourceOffset":14001},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The common finite-box interface","sourceOffset":14412},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two bands of a wide box","sourceOffset":17798},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Simultaneous boundary obligations","sourceOffset":21675},{"kind":"section","title":"Moving witnesses to a narrower box","sourceOffset":23995},{"kind":"section","title":"A deterministic recursion on width and mass","sourceOffset":29103},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Integral nested boxes and bounded passes","sourceOffset":32602}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"A uniform deterministic algorithm returns exactly the set \\[ U=\\bigl\\{v\\in V:\\exists\\text{ Max strategy }\\sigma\\ \\forall\\text{ Min strategies }\\tau, \\ \\MP_w(\\pi_{v,\\sigma,\\tau})\\ge0\\bigr\\} \\] for every game specified above. For an absolute constant $C>0$, the complete computation uses at most \\[ 2^{C(\\log_2(L+2))^2} \\] bit operations in a standard model polynomially equivalent to a uniform deterministic Turing machine. The bound includes preprocessing, exact arithmetic, recursion, and output of the entire set $U$.","sourceOffset":2086,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Winning partition from the wide box","label":"prop:game-reduction","text":"Let $h$ be the box solution in $[0,D_0]^V$. Then \\[ V_{\\lo}=\\{i:h_i\\le nW\\},\\qquad V_{\\hi}=\\{i:h_i\\ge D_0-nW\\} \\] partition $V$. There is a positional Max strategy under which every play starting in $V_{\\hi}$ has original mean payoff at least zero. There is a positional Min strategy under which every play starting in $V_{\\lo}$ has original upper limiting average at most $-1/n$. Both assertions allow arbitrary history-dependent opposition.","sourceOffset":18162,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Witnesses and their claims","label":"def:witnesses","text":"A Max witness for $(A,D,a,b)$ is a subsolution $x\\in[A,u]$ with support $S_x=\\{i:x_i>A_i\\}$ and $a(S_x)\\le1$. It claims each $i$ with $x_i\\ge u_i-P$. A Min witness is a supersolution $y\\in[A,u]$ with support $S_y=\\{i:y_i<u_i\\}$ and $b(S_y)\\le1$. It claims each $i$ with $y_i\\le A_i+P$. A witness is relevant when it claims at least one index.","sourceOffset":22084,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/104.md","scopeText":"The formalization checks that a specified two-step execution of Truffet's elimination procedure terminates with a feasible but nonoptimal output. It is a finite counterexample to that proposed optimization step.\n\nThe formalized result gives one randomized algorithm for the complete zero-threshold winning set of every finite mean-payoff game with signed binary weights. It succeeds with probability at least $7/8$ and runs in quasipolynomial bit time on every random tape. Self-loops, parallel edges, and history-dependent strategies are allowed, and winning means a nonnegative liminf average payoff.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/104.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":81001,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Deterministic-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"104-04","slug":"Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026","familyId":"104","title":"Randomized quasipolynomial-time mean-payoff games","abstract":"We give a randomized algorithm that computes the complete zero-threshold winning set of a finite mean-payoff game with arbitrary signed integer edge weights encoded in binary. For total explicit input length L, it uses $2^{O((\\log(L+2))^2)}$ bit operations on every random tape and is correct with probability at least 7/8. A polynomial-time check certifies the winning regions and positional strategies for both players or reports failure. Independent repetition therefore gives an always-correct algorithm with the same expected quasipolynomial bit bound.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives deterministic algorithms using $2^{O((\\log(L+2))^2)}$ bit operations, for complete binary input length L, for ordinary mean-payoff games and two separate extensions. They compute exact values and optimal positional strategies in ordinary games, the nonnegative expectation-of-liminf value set in turn-based stochastic games, and the winning set for nonnegative liminf mean payoff conjoined with parity. Signed rewards, rational chance probabilities, and parity priorities are unrestricted and binary-encoded.","pdfPath":"preprints/Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/preamble.tex","preprints/Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/02-boxes.tex","preprints/Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/03-budgets.tex","preprints/Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/04-algorithm.tex","preprints/Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/04a-termination.tex","preprints/Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/05-orientation.tex","preprints/Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/06-solving.tex","preprints/Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/07-complexity.tex","preprints/Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/figures/solver-recursion.tex","preprints/Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/08-conclusion.tex","preprints/Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/09-comparison.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a randomized algorithm that computes the complete zero-threshold winning set of a finite mean-payoff game with arbitrary signed integer edge weights encoded in binary. For total explicit input length $L$, it uses $2^{O((\\log(L+2))^2)}$ bit operations on every random tape and is correct with probability at least $7/8$. A polynomial-time check certifies the winning regions and positional strategies for both players or reports failure. Independent repetition therefore gives an always-correct algorithm with the same expected quasipolynomial bit bound.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":690},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Games, encoding, and the main theorem","sourceOffset":1631},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and related algorithms","sourceOffset":5167},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the solver finds the potential","sourceOffset":11161},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":14422},{"kind":"section","title":"Integer boxes and the winning-set reduction","sourceOffset":15141},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The transformed operator","sourceOffset":15924},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison and exact iteration","sourceOffset":17977},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two potential bands","sourceOffset":22293},{"kind":"section","title":"Weighted supports and transport of comparisons","sourceOffset":25750},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A rank that decreases with the support parameters","sourceOffset":27892},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Moving boundaries and recentering","sourceOffset":31033}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a uniform randomized algorithm that, for every such game, returns the entire set \\[ U=\\{v\\in V:\\text{there is a Max strategy }\\sigma \\text{ such that for every Min strategy }\\tau,\\ \\operatorname{MP}_w(\\pi_{v,\\sigma,\\tau})\\ge0\\} \\] correctly with probability at least $7/8$. For an absolute constant $C>0$, on every random tape its total cost is at most \\[ 2^{C(\\log_2(L+2))^2} \\] bit operations in a standard model polynomially equivalent to a uniform probabilistic Turing machine. The bound includes preprocessing, recursive calls, intermediate arithmetic, and random-bit generation.","sourceOffset":2392,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Reduction to a box solution","label":"prop:game-reduction","text":"Let $x$ solve the constant box $[0,B_*]$, with $B_*=8nW$ as in Equation \\eqref{eq:transformed}. Then the original game's exact zero-threshold winning set is \\[ U=\\{i\\in V:x_i>B_*/2\\}. \\] A positional Max strategy guarantees original mean payoff at least zero from every vertex of $U$. Outside $U$, a positional Min strategy ensures that every consistent play has original upper limiting average at most $-1/n$, including against arbitrary history-dependent Max strategies.","sourceOffset":22331,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Box comparisons","label":"def:box-comparisons","text":"For a finite integer box $[a,b]$, a subsolution is a full vector $y\\in[a,b]$ satisfying \\[ y_i\\le F_i(y)\\quad(i\\in S_y),\\qquad S_y=\\{i\\in V:y_i>a_i\\}. \\] A supersolution is a full vector $z\\in[a,b]$ satisfying \\[ z_i\\ge F_i(z)\\quad(i\\in S_z),\\qquad S_z=\\{i\\in V:z_i<b_i\\}. \\] We call $S_y$ and $S_z$ their supports, always relative to the specified box. At a collapsed coordinate $a_i=b_i$, neither support contains $i$ and neither inequality is required.","sourceOffset":18022,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Admissible comparisons","label":"def:admissible-comparison","text":"A subsolution $y$ of $[a,b]$ is admissible if $\\alpha(S_y)\\le k$. A supersolution $z$ of that box is admissible if $\\beta(S_z)\\le m$. The supports are relative to $[a,b]$ and have the boundary conventions of Definition \\ref{def:box-comparisons}.","sourceOffset":26866,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/104.md","scopeText":"The formalization checks that a specified two-step execution of Truffet's elimination procedure terminates with a feasible but nonoptimal output. It is a finite counterexample to that proposed optimization step.\n\nThe formalized result gives one randomized algorithm for the complete zero-threshold winning set of every finite mean-payoff game with signed binary weights. It succeeds with probability at least $7/8$ and runs in quasipolynomial bit time on every random tape. Self-loops, parallel edges, and history-dependent strategies are allowed, and winning means a nonnegative liminf average payoff.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/104.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":120722,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Randomized-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"105-01","slug":"Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026","familyId":"105","title":"Perfect completeness for 2-to-1 games","abstract":"We prove the 2-to-1 Games Conjecture with perfect completeness. For every fixed rational $\\delta\\in(0,1)$, it is NP-hard to distinguish satisfiable 2-to-1 games from games of value at most δ, with a fixed alphabet and an explicitly listed unweighted multiset of constraints.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Khot's 2-to-1 Games Conjecture with perfect completeness: for every fixed rational $\\delta\\in(0,1)$, it is NP-hard to distinguish satisfiable games from games whose optimum is at most δ, on explicit unweighted instances. The alphabet depends only on δ, and every right-hand label has exactly two preimages under each constraint map.","pdfPath":"preprints/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026/build/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026/build/01-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026/build/02-construction.tex","preprints/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026/build/03-sampling.tex","preprints/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026/build/04-upper-decoding.tex","preprints/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026/build/figures/tree-cut.tex","preprints/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026/build/05-lower-decoding.tex","preprints/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026/build/06-clean-repetition.tex","preprints/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026/build/07-parameters-output.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the 2-to-1 Games Conjecture with perfect completeness. For every fixed rational $\\delta\\in(0,1)$, it is NP-hard to distinguish satisfiable 2-to-1 games from games of value at most $\\delta$, with a fixed alphabet and an explicitly listed unweighted multiset of constraints.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":327},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and ingredients","sourceOffset":2953},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":5500},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Applications","sourceOffset":10955},{"kind":"section","title":"Source games and inverse matrix structure","sourceOffset":14450},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite projection games and the source gap","sourceOffset":15392},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Matrix slices and the Grassmann input","sourceOffset":24955},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Partial tables and many row fibers","sourceOffset":33497},{"kind":"section","title":"The tree of function spaces and the one-bit test","sourceOffset":39472},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Formal slots and pointwise spaces","sourceOffset":40385},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Joint evaluations and canonical questions","sourceOffset":45181},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One-bit constraints and perfect completeness","sourceOffset":53435}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed rational $\\delta\\in(0,1)$ there are an integer $q=q(\\delta)\\ge2$ and a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from $3$-SAT to 2-to-1 games with alphabets $[2q]$ and $[q]$ such that: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item a satisfiable formula produces a game of value $1$; \\item an unsatisfiable formula produces a game of value at most $\\delta$. \\end{enumerate} The output contains a nonempty explicit list of unweighted edge occurrences and an explicit table for every projection map. Every table has exactly two preimages for each right answer. The polynomial running-time bound is in the ordinary binary input length; its degree and constants may depend on the fixed $\\delta$.","sourceOffset":1965,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Maximum $k$-colorable subgraph","label":"cor:max-k-colorable","text":"There are absolute constants $C>0$ and an integer $k_0\\ge3$ such that, for every fixed integer $k\\ge k_0$, it is NP-hard to distinguish the following cases for a finite unweighted graph $G=(W,F)$ with $F\\ne\\varnothing$: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item $G$ is $k$-colorable; \\item every coloring $\\chi:W\\to[k]$ satisfies \\[ \\frac{|\\{\\{u,v\\}\\in F:\\chi(u)\\ne\\chi(v)\\}|}{|F|} \\le 1-\\frac1k+C\\frac{\\log k}{k^2}. \\] \\end{enumerate} The fraction on the left is the proportion of edges properly colored by $\\chi$, or equivalently cut by its partition into $k$ color classes.","sourceOffset":10982,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Pointwise function spaces","label":"def:spaces","text":"For any such tuple $Q$ of clause and variable questions, define finite vector spaces over $\\F$ recursively. At a leaf $C$, set $H_C(Q)=\\F^{\\Omega_C(Q)}$, the space of all functions from its answer domain to $\\F$. For every node $C$, set \\[ H_C(Q)^2=\\Span\\{gh:g,h\\in H_C(Q)\\}, \\qquad (gh)(x)=g(x)h(x). \\] At a nonleaf node $D$, put \\begin{equation} H_D(Q)=\\sum_{C\\text{ child of }D} H_C(Q)^2 \\ \\subseteq\\ \\F^{\\Omega_D(Q)}. \\end{equation} Here a function on a descendant domain is extended by ignoring every other argument. All sums, products and equalities are equalities of actual functions on these ","sourceOffset":42861,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/105.md","scopeText":"The formalized result establishes perfect-completeness hardness for 2-to-1 games. For every fixed rational $0<\\delta<1$, a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from binary 3SAT produces a nonempty unweighted game of value exactly $1$ on satisfiable inputs and at most $\\delta$ on unsatisfiable inputs. Each constraint projection has exactly two preimages for each output label. The alphabet depends only on $\\delta$, and the runtime is measured in the original input bit length.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/105.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":190245,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Perfect-completeness-for-2-to-1-games-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"106-01","slug":"Hardness-of-finding-large-independent-sets-in-three-colorable-graphs-September-24-2026","familyId":"106","title":"Hardness of finding large independent sets in three-colorable graphs","abstract":"We prove that, for every fixed $0\\lt \\delta\\lt 1/3$, it is NP-hard to distinguish three-colorable graphs from graphs in which every independent set has fewer than δ times the number of vertices. Consequently, for every fixed integer c ≥ 3, finding a proper c-coloring of a three-colorable graph is NP-hard.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"It is NP-hard to color a three-colorable graph using any fixed number c ≥ 3 of colors. More strongly, for every fixed $0\\lt \\delta\\lt 1/3$, a deterministic polynomial-time reduction from 3SAT produces simple unweighted graphs that are three-colorable in the satisfiable case and have no independent set of size $\\delta n$ otherwise, where n is the number of vertices.","pdfPath":"preprints/Hardness-of-finding-large-independent-sets-in-three-colorable-graphs-September-24-2026/Hardness-of-finding-large-independent-sets-in-three-colorable-graphs-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Hardness-of-finding-large-independent-sets-in-three-colorable-graphs-September-24-2026/Hardness-of-finding-large-independent-sets-in-three-colorable-graphs-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Hardness-of-finding-large-independent-sets-in-three-colorable-graphs-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Hardness-of-finding-large-independent-sets-in-three-colorable-graphs-September-24-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Hardness-of-finding-large-independent-sets-in-three-colorable-graphs-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Hardness-of-finding-large-independent-sets-in-three-colorable-graphs-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Hardness-of-finding-large-independent-sets-in-three-colorable-graphs-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/construction.tex","preprints/Hardness-of-finding-large-independent-sets-in-three-colorable-graphs-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/lists.tex","preprints/Hardness-of-finding-large-independent-sets-in-three-colorable-graphs-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/alignment.tex","preprints/Hardness-of-finding-large-independent-sets-in-three-colorable-graphs-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/coefficient.tex","preprints/Hardness-of-finding-large-independent-sets-in-three-colorable-graphs-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/parameters.tex","preprints/Hardness-of-finding-large-independent-sets-in-three-colorable-graphs-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/alignment-proof.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Hardness-of-finding-large-independent-sets-in-three-colorable-graphs-September-24-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that, for every fixed $0<\\delta<1/3$, it is NP-hard to distinguish three-colorable graphs from graphs in which every independent set has fewer than $\\delta$ times the number of vertices. Consequently, for every fixed integer $c\\ge3$, finding a proper $c$-coloring of a three-colorable graph is NP-hard.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":357},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and comparison with prior work","sourceOffset":2943},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview and method ancestry","sourceOffset":7398},{"kind":"section","title":"Two standard inputs and an analytic consequence","sourceOffset":11862},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A projection constraint problem","sourceOffset":12514},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Juntas for the sup metric","sourceOffset":14251},{"kind":"section","title":"The finite graph reduction","sourceOffset":17778},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Tuples, grids, and zero-length links","sourceOffset":19453},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Vertices and edges","sourceOffset":21494},{"kind":"section","title":"From an independent set to bounded subchain lists","sourceOffset":28979},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Odd functions and compatibility","sourceOffset":29563},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bounded lists for a subchain","sourceOffset":32074}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed real $0<\\delta<1/3$, there is a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm $R_\\delta$ that maps each explicitly encoded Boolean $3$-CNF formula $\\phi$ to a nonempty finite simple undirected unweighted graph $G_\\phi$ such that \\[ \\begin{array}{ll} \\phi\\text{ is satisfiable} &\\Longrightarrow G_\\phi\\text{ is three-colorable},\\\\[2pt] \\phi\\text{ is unsatisfiable} &\\Longrightarrow \\alpha(G_\\phi)<\\delta\\,|V(G_\\phi)|. \\end{array} \\] The running time and explicit output size are polynomial in the ordinary binary encoding length of $\\phi$. Their constants and polynomial degree may depend on the fixed $\\delta$, but not on $\\phi$. The coloring in the first implication covers every vertex and is not supplied with the graph.","sourceOffset":878,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:coloring","text":"For every fixed pair of integers $3\\le k\\le c$, it is NP-hard to distinguish $k$-colorable graphs from graphs that are not $c$-colorable.","sourceOffset":2497,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":null,"text":"A pair consisting of a chain and a set $B\\in\\binom{[r]}s$ is aligned if there is a map $a:M_r\\to B$ such that $a(A_I)\\subseteq I$ for every nonempty $I\\subseteq B$.","sourceOffset":41155,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/106.md","scopeText":"The formalized result shows hardness of finding large independent sets even under a three-colorability promise. For every fixed $0<\\delta<1/3$, a deterministic polynomial-time reduction maps binary 3SAT formulas to nonempty finite simple graphs. Satisfiable formulas produce three-colorable graphs; unsatisfiable formulas produce graphs whose largest independent set has fewer than $\\delta$ times the number of vertices. The complete adjacency-matrix output is included in the runtime bound.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/106.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":67933,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Hardness-of-finding-large-independent-sets-in-three-colorable-graphs-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"107-01","slug":"Matrix-Multiplication-Nine-Fourths-October-2-2026","familyId":"107","title":"An Upper Bound of 9/4 for the Matrix Multiplication Exponent","abstract":"We prove that the exponent of matrix multiplication over the complex numbers is at most 9/4.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves $\\omega\\le9/4$ over ℂ, giving $O_\\varepsilon(n^{9/4+\\varepsilon})$ arithmetic operations for square matrix multiplication. In characteristic zero, some inner dimension na with a > 0.465 permits $n^{2+o(1)}$ rectangular multiplication. Further square bounds give ω < 2.258 outside finitely many positive characteristics and ω < 2.371054886006746 over every fixed field.","pdfPath":"preprints/Matrix-Multiplication-Nine-Fourths-October-2-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Matrix-Multiplication-Nine-Fourths-October-2-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Matrix-Multiplication-Nine-Fourths-October-2-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Matrix-Multiplication-Nine-Fourths-October-2-2026/build/paper.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Matrix-Multiplication-Nine-Fourths-October-2-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the exponent of matrix multiplication over the complex numbers is at most $9/4$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":141},{"kind":"section","title":"Tensors and their characters","sourceOffset":5924},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Dot-product exponents","sourceOffset":8800},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Degenerations and interpolation","sourceOffset":10035},{"kind":"section","title":"Separating a shared tensor leg","sourceOffset":11561},{"kind":"section","title":"Two inequalities for polynomial multiplication","sourceOffset":16359},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A determinant filtration","sourceOffset":18260},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Three sectors","sourceOffset":22014},{"kind":"section","title":"Discrete growth and the arithmetic exponent","sourceOffset":25013},{"kind":"section","title":"Existence of detecting characters","sourceOffset":28597},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Obtaining multiplicativity","sourceOffset":29799},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Constructing the detecting state space","sourceOffset":31560}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $\\eps>0$, two $n\\times n$ complex matrices can be multiplied using $O_\\eps(n^{9/4+\\eps})$ arithmetic operations. In particular, $\\omega\\leq9/4$.","sourceOffset":534,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Finite separation","label":"prop:separation","text":"Let $M\\geq1$ and \\[ A=\\sum_{h=1}^M A_h,\\qquad A_h\\in X\\otimes Y_h\\otimes Z_h, \\] where the $Y_h$ are disjoint coordinate spaces and so are the $Z_h$. There are no terms between unmatched second- and third-leg sectors. Then \\begin{equation} A^{\\oplus5M}\\degen \\bigoplus_{h=1}^M\\bigl(A_h\\otimes B_X(M)\\bigr). \\end{equation} Each $A_h$ on the right has its own copy of $X$.","sourceOffset":11874,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:character","text":"A tensor character is an additive, multiplicative, restriction-monotone map $\\lambda:S\\to\\R_{\\geq0}$ with $\\lambda(1)=1$.","sourceOffset":7982,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/107.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":42015,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Matrix-Multiplication-Nine-Fourths-October-2-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"107-02","slug":"Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026","familyId":"107","title":"Complex Matrix Multiplication Below 2.258 and Rectangular Bounds","abstract":"Over every field of characteristic zero, we prove that the square matrix-multiplication exponent satisfies ω < 2.258, the dual exponent satisfies α > 0.465, and $\\omega(1,0.709,1)\\lt 2.092$. The strict square and k = 0.709 rectangular bounds also hold over every field except possibly in one finite set of positive characteristics, in the arithmetic-operation model.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves $\\omega\\le9/4$ over ℂ, giving $O_\\varepsilon(n^{9/4+\\varepsilon})$ arithmetic operations for square matrix multiplication. In characteristic zero, some inner dimension na with a > 0.465 permits $n^{2+o(1)}$ rectangular multiplication. Further square bounds give ω < 2.258 outside finitely many positive characteristics and ω < 2.371054886006746 over every fixed field.","pdfPath":"preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/build/rectangular/02-tensor-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/build/tex/algebra.tex","preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/build/figures/group_geometry.tex","preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/build/tex/values.tex","preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/build/tex/ap.tex","preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/build/tex/certificate-w.tex","preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/build/tex/certificate.tex","preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/build/tex/conclusion.tex","preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/build/rectangular/04-label-trees.tex","preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/build/rectangular/05-completion.tex","preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/build/rectangular/06-optimization.tex","preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/build/rectangular/07-certificates.tex","preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/build/rectangular/08-comparison.tex","preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/build/tex/barrier-coefficients.tex","preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/build/tex/reproducibility.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Over every field of characteristic zero, we prove that the square matrix-multiplication exponent satisfies \\(\\omega<2.258\\), the dual exponent satisfies \\(\\alpha>0.465\\), and \\(\\omega(1,0.709,1)<2.092\\). The strict square and \\(k=0.709\\) rectangular bounds also hold over every field except possibly in one finite set of positive characteristics, in the arithmetic-operation model.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":444},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and main result","sourceOffset":1658},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Separating labels with an auxiliary tensor","sourceOffset":5981},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The square bound: finite constructions and comparison","sourceOffset":8514},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rectangular bounds and finite-family optimization","sourceOffset":12502},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":15848},{"kind":"section","title":"Tensors and arithmetic exponents","sourceOffset":16942},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite tensor constructions","sourceOffset":25991},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pairing conventions and the input rank","sourceOffset":26698},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Supplying a label to its missing party","sourceOffset":30042},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact types and a finite hierarchy of labels","sourceOffset":38903},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An explicit four-component construction","sourceOffset":44198}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Over \\(\\C\\), the arithmetic matrix-multiplication exponents satisfy \\[ \\omega<\\frac{1129}{500}=2.258,\\qquad \\alpha>0.465,\\qquad w(0.709)<2.092. \\]","sourceOffset":4027,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Asymptotic sum inequality, \\citet{schonhage}","label":"thm:asi","text":"If a tensor \\(U\\) degenerates to \\(\\bigoplus_{\\ell=1}^{L}\\langle a_\\ell,b_\\ell,c_\\ell\\rangle\\), with all dimensions positive integers, then \\[ \\sum_{\\ell=1}^{L}(a_\\ell b_\\ell c_\\ell)^{\\omega/3} \\leq\\brank(U). \\] In particular, if \\(T^{\\otimes n}\\otimes\\mathcal A\\unrhd \\bigoplus_{\\ell=1}^{L}\\langle a,b,c\\rangle\\) and \\(\\brank(\\mathcal A)\\leq R\\), then \\begin{equation} L(abc)^{\\omega/3}\\leq3^nR. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":29088,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Strong banked realization","label":"def:strong","text":"A strong realization consists of a finite auxiliary tensor $\\mathcal A$, a finite degeneration by three local maps, a set $\\Lambda$ of direct labels, and positive integers $a,b,c$, with the following properties. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item Every $\\lambda\\in\\Lambda$ designates an original word $w(\\lambda)$, and $\\lambda\\mapsto w(\\lambda)$ is injective. All these words have the same exact canonical law in each orientation bank. \\item For every family of unresolved tensors $P=(P_w)$ the same maps give \\begin{equation} \\mathcal T(P)\\otimes\\mathcal A \\unrhd \\bigoplus_{\\lambda\\","sourceOffset":72638,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Attainable-value functions","label":"val:def-value","text":"For each finite realization let $\\alpha$ be its canonical bank law. Take all pairs $(\\alpha,t)$ with $t$ no greater than its score, and take their ordinary Euclidean closure in $\\Delta(\\mathcal B)\\times\\R$. The upper boundaries of these closed sets are denoted by $F_\\beta(\\alpha)$ for the six-state class and $U^W_\\gamma(q)$ for the $\\mathsf W$ class.","sourceOffset":80826,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/107.md","scopeText":"The formalized results bound the complex matrix-multiplication exponent by $\\omega(\\mathbb C)\\le9/4$, the dual exponent by $\\alpha>0.465$, and the rectangular exponent at aspect ratio $0.709$ by $\\omega(\\mathbb C;1,0.709,1)<2.092$. The dual exponent is the supremum of rectangular aspect ratios attainable with exponent $2$. The arithmetic model counts additions, subtractions, and multiplications in finite division-free programs, with arbitrary positive exponent slack. The square bound implies the paper's weaker $2.258$ headline bound.\n\nThe formalized result gives the unconditional bound $\\omega(F)<2.371054886006746$ for every field $F$, including finite fields and fields of positive characteristic. The exponent counts additions, subtractions, and multiplications in finite division-free arit","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/107.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":271401,"sourceFileCount":17,"statementCount":20,"definitionCount":6,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true,"editorialLabel":"secondary writeup"},{"id":"107-03","slug":"Staggered-extraction-for-exact-matrix-multiplication-over-every-field-September-24-2026","familyId":"107","title":"Staggered extraction for exact matrix multiplication over every field","abstract":"We prove that the arithmetic exponent of square matrix multiplication over every fixed field satisfies ω < 2.371054886006746. This includes every positive characteristic.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves $\\omega\\le9/4$ over ℂ, giving $O_\\varepsilon(n^{9/4+\\varepsilon})$ arithmetic operations for square matrix multiplication. In characteristic zero, some inner dimension na with a > 0.465 permits $n^{2+o(1)}$ rectangular multiplication. Further square bounds give ω < 2.258 outside finitely many positive characteristics and ω < 2.371054886006746 over every fixed field.","pdfPath":"preprints/Staggered-extraction-for-exact-matrix-multiplication-over-every-field-September-24-2026/Staggered-extraction-for-exact-matrix-multiplication-over-every-field-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Staggered-extraction-for-exact-matrix-multiplication-over-every-field-September-24-2026/Staggered-extraction-for-exact-matrix-multiplication-over-every-field-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Staggered-extraction-for-exact-matrix-multiplication-over-every-field-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Staggered-extraction-for-exact-matrix-multiplication-over-every-field-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Staggered-extraction-for-exact-matrix-multiplication-over-every-field-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Staggered-extraction-for-exact-matrix-multiplication-over-every-field-September-24-2026/build/sections/foundations.tex","preprints/Staggered-extraction-for-exact-matrix-multiplication-over-every-field-September-24-2026/build/sections/joint-extraction.tex","preprints/Staggered-extraction-for-exact-matrix-multiplication-over-every-field-September-24-2026/build/sections/recovery.tex","preprints/Staggered-extraction-for-exact-matrix-multiplication-over-every-field-September-24-2026/build/sections/staggering.tex","preprints/Staggered-extraction-for-exact-matrix-multiplication-over-every-field-September-24-2026/build/figures/pipeline.tex","preprints/Staggered-extraction-for-exact-matrix-multiplication-over-every-field-September-24-2026/build/sections/leaf-volumes.tex","preprints/Staggered-extraction-for-exact-matrix-multiplication-over-every-field-September-24-2026/build/sections/parameters.tex","preprints/Staggered-extraction-for-exact-matrix-multiplication-over-every-field-September-24-2026/build/sections/parameter-tables.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Staggered-extraction-for-exact-matrix-multiplication-over-every-field-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the arithmetic exponent of square matrix multiplication over every fixed field satisfies $\\omega<2.371054886006746$. This includes every positive characteristic.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":221},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":3886},{"kind":"section","title":"Rank and the basic tensor","sourceOffset":9132},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact rank and the arithmetic exponent","sourceOffset":10386},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An integer coefficient construction","sourceOffset":15062},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Strands and shapes","sourceOffset":18200},{"kind":"section","title":"A joint extraction at several depths","sourceOffset":19798},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The data of a joint step","sourceOffset":20917},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Entropy and a common modular hash","sourceOffset":30180},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite compatibility count","sourceOffset":35230},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sequential assignment by variable deletion","sourceOffset":40219},{"kind":"section","title":"Recovering the ideal outputs and composing extractions","sourceOffset":45366}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Over every fixed field $\\F$, the arithmetic exponent of square matrix multiplication satisfies \\[ \\omega_\\F<2.371054886006746<2.371054887. \\]","sourceOffset":3391,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Heterogeneous joint step","label":"thm:joint-step","text":"Let $B_N$ be the restriction of $\\bigotimes_r F_r^{\\otimes m_r}$ by the inherited masks just specified, with the population data above. Set \\begin{equation} A_{\\rm amb}=\\sum_{r\\in\\mathcal R}c_rH(p_r),\\qquad C_W=\\sum_{r\\in\\mathcal R}c_rC_{r,W},\\qquad E=\\min\\{C_X,C_Y,C_Z\\}. \\end{equation} If $E>0$, then for every $\\varepsilon>0$ one can choose positive output widths, as small as desired, such that for all sufficiently large $N$ divisible by the fixed type denominators, a direct sum of $\\exp(o(N))$ independent copies of $B_N$ has a linear restriction to at least $\\lfloor\\exp(N(E-\\varepsilon))\\rfloor$ independent copies of $Q_N$. The choices of populations, rational laws, positive widths, and divisibility requirements precede the limit $N\\to\\infty$. In particular, the assertion does not require common strand lengths.","sourceOffset":28895,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/107.md","scopeText":"The formalized results bound the complex matrix-multiplication exponent by $\\omega(\\mathbb C)\\le9/4$, the dual exponent by $\\alpha>0.465$, and the rectangular exponent at aspect ratio $0.709$ by $\\omega(\\mathbb C;1,0.709,1)<2.092$. The dual exponent is the supremum of rectangular aspect ratios attainable with exponent $2$. The arithmetic model counts additions, subtractions, and multiplications in finite division-free programs, with arbitrary positive exponent slack. The square bound implies the paper's weaker $2.258$ headline bound.\n\nThe formalized result gives the unconditional bound $\\omega(F)<2.371054886006746$ for every field $F$, including finite fields and fields of positive characteristic. The exponent counts additions, subtractions, and multiplications in finite division-free arit","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/107.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":116045,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Staggered-extraction-for-exact-matrix-multiplication-over-every-field-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"108-01","slug":"A-cubic-lower-bound-for-border-determinantal-complexity-of-the-permanent-September-24-2026","familyId":"108","title":"A cubic lower bound for border determinantal complexity of the permanent","abstract":"We prove that the complex border determinantal complexity of the $m\\times m$ permanent is $\\Omega(m^3)$, allowing arbitrary affine-linear determinant representations and coefficientwise limits. It also gives cubic lower bounds for exact determinantal complexity and for the numbers of vertices and edges in affine-linear algebraic branching programs, including coefficientwise limits with a fixed vertex or edge budget.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves an $\\Omega(n^3)$ lower bound for the border determinantal complexity of the $n\\times n$ permanent over ℂ. Even coefficientwise limits of determinants of affine-linear matrices require matrix size at least $cn^3$, for an absolute c > 0 and all sufficiently large n; the same bound therefore holds for exact representations.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-cubic-lower-bound-for-border-determinantal-complexity-of-the-permanent-September-24-2026/A-cubic-lower-bound-for-border-determinantal-complexity-of-the-permanent-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-cubic-lower-bound-for-border-determinantal-complexity-of-the-permanent-September-24-2026/A-cubic-lower-bound-for-border-determinantal-complexity-of-the-permanent-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-cubic-lower-bound-for-border-determinantal-complexity-of-the-permanent-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/A-cubic-lower-bound-for-border-determinantal-complexity-of-the-permanent-September-24-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/A-cubic-lower-bound-for-border-determinantal-complexity-of-the-permanent-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-cubic-lower-bound-for-border-determinantal-complexity-of-the-permanent-September-24-2026/build/source/figures/proof-flow.tex","preprints/A-cubic-lower-bound-for-border-determinantal-complexity-of-the-permanent-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/models.tex","preprints/A-cubic-lower-bound-for-border-determinantal-complexity-of-the-permanent-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/selector.tex","preprints/A-cubic-lower-bound-for-border-determinantal-complexity-of-the-permanent-September-24-2026/build/source/figures/selector-wiring.tex","preprints/A-cubic-lower-bound-for-border-determinantal-complexity-of-the-permanent-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/adjugate.tex","preprints/A-cubic-lower-bound-for-border-determinantal-complexity-of-the-permanent-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/conductor.tex","preprints/A-cubic-lower-bound-for-border-determinantal-complexity-of-the-permanent-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/dimension.tex","preprints/A-cubic-lower-bound-for-border-determinantal-complexity-of-the-permanent-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/transfer.tex","preprints/A-cubic-lower-bound-for-border-determinantal-complexity-of-the-permanent-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/polar.tex","preprints/A-cubic-lower-bound-for-border-determinantal-complexity-of-the-permanent-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-cubic-lower-bound-for-border-determinantal-complexity-of-the-permanent-September-24-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the complex border determinantal complexity of the $m\\times m$ permanent is $\\Omega(m^3)$, allowing arbitrary affine-linear determinant representations and coefficientwise limits. It also gives cubic lower bounds for exact determinantal complexity and for the numbers of vertices and edges in affine-linear algebraic branching programs, including coefficientwise limits with a fixed vertex or edge budget.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":482},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier bounds and geometric methods","sourceOffset":3501},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The coefficient construction and the proof","sourceOffset":8426},{"kind":"section","title":"Border models and smooth homogeneous forms","sourceOffset":16056},{"kind":"section","title":"Selecting a coefficient by a permanent projection","sourceOffset":22547},{"kind":"section","title":"The adjugate scheme in characteristic two","sourceOffset":37889},{"kind":"section","title":"The conductor and the normalization defect","sourceOffset":50072},{"kind":"section","title":"From curve defects to matrix codimension","sourceOffset":59851},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Curves with large normalization defect","sourceOffset":61610},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A row subscheme determines a matrix","sourceOffset":64023},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite subschemes of a plane curve","sourceOffset":68471},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The matrix fibers and the gradient locus","sourceOffset":72107}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist absolute constants $c>0$ and $m_0$ such that, for every integer $m\\ge m_0$, \\[ \\bdc(\\per_m)\\ge c m^3. \\] One may take $c=1/(5\\,529\\,600e)$ and $m_0=1408$.","sourceOffset":2375,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Equivalence of the two border models","label":"prop:model-equivalence","text":"For every $n\\ge m\\ge1$, \\[ \\bdc(\\per_m)\\le n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad [P_{n,m}]\\in\\mathcal D_n. \\]","sourceOffset":18095,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/108.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves a cubic lower bound for both exact and border determinantal representations of the complex $m\\times m$ permanent. For $m\\ge1408$, every affine-linear determinant representation of size $n$, including coefficientwise limits, satisfies $n\\ge m^3/(5529600e)$.\n\nA general supporting theorem applies to a polynomial in $d\\ge2$ variables whose first nonzero homogeneous Taylor term has degree $r\\ge2$ and no nonzero singular zero. Its exact and border determinantal sizes are at least $(r-1)(d-1)/(4e)$. The paper's algebraic-branching-program consequences are outside these statements.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/108.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":111846,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":15,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-cubic-lower-bound-for-border-determinantal-complexity-of-the-permanent-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"109-01","slug":"Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026","familyId":"109","title":"Integer multiplication below n log n","abstract":"We give a deterministic algorithm that multiplies two n-bit integers in $O(n(\\lg n)^{1-\\kappa})$ worst-case time, with $\\kappa=2^{-182}$, on one fixed finite-alphabet Turing machine with a fixed finite number of one-dimensional tapes. The algorithm is exact for every input length and disproves the $n\\log n$ optimality conjecture of Schönhage and Strassen in this model.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Multiplies two n-bit integers exactly at every input length in deterministic worst-case time $O(n(\\log n)^{1-\\kappa})$, with $\\kappa=2^{-182}$, on one fixed finite-alphabet Turing machine with finitely many one-dimensional tapes. This disproves the Schönhage–Strassen $n\\log n$ optimality conjecture in the ordinary multitape bit model.","pdfPath":"preprints/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/figures/multiplication-flow.tex","preprints/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-history.tex","preprints/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-streams.tex","preprints/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-motifs.tex","preprints/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/figures/proof-map.tex","preprints/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-swap.tex","preprints/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-layers.tex","preprints/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-transforms.tex","preprints/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-resampling.tex","preprints/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-assembly.tex","preprints/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/09-exact-arithmetic.tex","preprints/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/10-transposition.tex","preprints/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/sections/11-grouped-rectangles.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a deterministic algorithm that multiplies two $n$-bit integers in $O(n(\\lg n)^{1-\\kappa})$ worst-case time, with $\\kappa=2^{-182}$, on one fixed finite-alphabet Turing machine with a fixed finite number of one-dimensional tapes. The algorithm is exact for every input length and disproves the $n\\log n$ optimality conjecture of Sch\\\"onhage and Strassen in this model.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":449},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two tape operations","sourceOffset":2229},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Linear networks and the recursive saving","sourceOffset":4615},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From transforms to the exact product","sourceOffset":8224},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Other arithmetic operations","sourceOffset":11558},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and inherited methods","sourceOffset":12744},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite-network ingredients","sourceOffset":16463},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Lower-bound scope and matrix transposition","sourceOffset":19253},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Companion application to exact Fourier transforms","sourceOffset":21002},{"kind":"section","title":"The machine model and elementary streams","sourceOffset":22437},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Array order and elementary scans","sourceOffset":23181},{"kind":"section","title":"Two finite networks with a rank saving","sourceOffset":33751}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is one deterministic Turing machine $A$, with a fixed finite alphabet and a fixed finite number of one-dimensional tapes, that computes this exact product for every $n\\ge1$ and every pair of $n$-bit inputs. Its worst-case running time satisfies \\[ T_A(n)=O\\!\\left(n(\\lg n)^{1-\\kappa}\\right), \\qquad \\kappa=2^{-182}. \\]","sourceOffset":1554,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Exact division and integer square root","label":"cor:exact-arithmetic","text":"There are deterministic machines with fixed finite alphabets and fixed finite numbers of one-dimensional tapes performing the following tasks in $O(n(\\lg n)^{1-\\kappa})$ worst-case time, for every $n\\ge1$. For $x,y\\in\\{0,1\\}^n$, put $a=\\operatorname{val}(x)$ and $b=\\operatorname{val}(y)$. If $b>0$, the division machine takes $x\\#y$ and returns $\\operatorname{bin}_n(q)\\#\\operatorname{bin}_n(r)$, where $q=\\lfloor a/b\\rfloor$ and $r=a-bq$. The square-root machine takes $x$ and returns $\\operatorname{bin}_n(\\lfloor\\sqrt a\\rfloor)$. Leading zeroes are allowed, and $\\kappa$ is as in Theorem \\ref{thm:main}.","sourceOffset":11600,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Grouped packed stream","label":"def:grouped-packed-stream","text":"Let $M,R\\ge1$, $K\\ge6$, $f\\ge1$, $L=fK$, and $0\\le\\rho<K$. Fix an ordered template of $t\\ge3$ two-bit tags: $\\mathtt{00}$ denotes a spectator field, and $\\mathtt{01},\\mathtt{10},\\mathtt{11}$ denote $x,y,z$, respectively. Each chunk tag occurs exactly once. The template is common to all $J\\ge1$ groups of this input, but its length may vary between inputs. Group $\\nu$ has a positive row count $n_\\nu$ and a positive length $a_{\\nu,h}$ at each spectator tag $h$; at a chunk tag put $a_{\\nu,h}=2^L$. Define \\[ Q_\\nu=\\prod_{h=1}^t a_{\\nu,h},\\qquad M_\\nu=n_\\nu Q_\\nu,\\qquad C_\\nu=\\sum_{\\mu<\\nu}M_\\mu,\\qq","sourceOffset":245127,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":252009,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"110-01","slug":"Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026","familyId":"110","title":"Squared-logarithmic randomized k-server on arbitrary metrics","abstract":"We prove that randomized k-server has competitive ratio $O((\\log(k+1))^2)$ on every metric space against oblivious request sequences, matching the known worst-case lower bound. For each metric and initial configuration, one policy works for all finite request sequences, including on infinite and unbounded spaces. When the initial server positions are distinct, no additive term is needed.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Establishes a randomized competitive ratio $O(\\log^2(k+1))$ for k-server on every metric space, matching the worst-case lower-bound order. One policy serves every finite oblivious request sequence, including on infinite unbounded metrics. On finite rational metrics, a uniform implementation has polynomial preprocessing and per-request bit cost in the input length and $\\log(t+1)$ at request t, with a finite instance-dependent additive movement constant.","pdfPath":"preprints/Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026/Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026/Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026/build/source/latex/common.tex","preprints/Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026/build/source/latex/sections/01_introduction.tex","preprints/Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026/build/source/latex/sections/02_setup.tex","preprints/Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026/build/source/latex/sections/03_allocation_data.tex","preprints/Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026/build/source/latex/sections/04_allocation_trackers.tex","preprints/Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026/build/source/latex/sections/05_allocation_finish.tex","preprints/Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026/build/source/latex/sections/06_pilot.tex","preprints/Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026/build/source/latex/sections/07_partitions.tex","preprints/Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026/build/source/latex/sections/08_travel.tex","preprints/Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026/build/source/latex/sections/09_rounding.tex","preprints/Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026/build/source/latex/sections/10_transfer.tex","preprints/Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026/build/source/latex/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that randomized \\(k\\)-server has competitive ratio \\(O((\\log(k+1))^2)\\) on every metric space against oblivious request sequences, matching the known worst-case lower bound. For each metric and initial configuration, one policy works for all finite request sequences, including on infinite and unbounded spaces. When the initial server positions are distinct, no additive term is needed.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":459},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and previous work","sourceOffset":2412},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Main ingredients","sourceOffset":7121},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":10922},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite laws, posterior motion, and the proof architecture","sourceOffset":11701},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Matching and lazy simulation","sourceOffset":12177},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Hidden comparators and the two parts of a posterior update","sourceOffset":15905},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The true-metric measure update","sourceOffset":19231},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Scales and a logarithmic telescope","sourceOffset":20319},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Where the two logarithms occur","sourceOffset":21929},{"kind":"section","title":"Allocation on a separated tree: rank data","sourceOffset":24776},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Explicit rank functions and compatible lower bounds","sourceOffset":29521}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is an absolute constant \\(C<\\infty\\) such that, for every integer \\(k\\ge2\\), every metric space \\((X,d)\\) with at least \\(k+1\\) points, and every \\(s\\in X^k\\), there exist a randomized online policy \\(A\\) and a finite \\(B\\ge0\\) satisfying \\begin{equation} \\E\\,\\cost_{A,s}(\\sigma) \\le C\\bigl(\\log(k+1)\\bigr)^2\\OPT_{X,d,k,s}(\\sigma)+B \\end{equation} for every finite request sequence \\(\\sigma\\). The constant \\(C\\) is independent of every property of the metric. The number \\(B\\) may depend on \\((X,d),k,s\\), but not on the sequence or its length. The same policy serves all finite sequences and horizons. If the initial positions are distinct, one may take \\(B=0\\).","sourceOffset":1512,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Finite distributional estimate","label":"prop:finite-law","text":"There is an absolute \\(a<\\infty\\) with the following property. For every finite metric \\(Y\\) of positive diameter, every \\(k\\ge2\\), and every start \\(s\\in Y^k\\), there is a finite \\(D_0=D_0(Y,k,s)\\) such that, for every finite horizon \\(H\\) and every probability law on \\(Y^H\\), there is a causal strongly lazy policy satisfying \\begin{equation} \\E\\,\\cost\\le a\\ell^2\\E\\,\\OPT+D_0 . \\end{equation} The policy may depend on the law and horizon. The number \\(D_0\\) does not.","sourceOffset":14939,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/110.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves an $O((\\log(k+1))^2)$ competitive ratio for randomized $k$-server against oblivious finite request sequences. For every $k\\ge2$, every metric space containing at least $k+1$ points, and every initial configuration, one policy works for all request sequences, including in infinite and unbounded spaces.\n\nThe bound permits a configuration-dependent additive constant, which is zero when the initial server positions are distinct. The universal multiplicative constant is independent of the metric and $k$.\n\nThe formalization constructs one uniform randomized bit algorithm for $k$-server on finite rational metrics, for $2\\le k<n$. Its expected movement on every oblivious finite request sequence is at most an absolute multiple of $(\\log(k+1))^2$ times the offline optimum, p","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/110.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":243469,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Squared-logarithmic-randomized-k-server-on-arbitrary-metrics-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"110-02","slug":"Uniform-computation-of-the-squared-logarithmic-k-server-bound-September-24-2026","familyId":"110","title":"Uniform computation of the squared-logarithmic k-server bound","abstract":"We construct a uniform randomized k-server algorithm on finite rational metrics with competitive ratio $O(\\log^2(k+1))$ against oblivious request sequences. Preprocessing is polynomial in the input length, and per-request bit complexity is polynomial in that length and the binary request-counter length. The additive movement constant is finite and instance-dependent, but may be enormous. The construction uses the companion squared-logarithmic existence theorem.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Establishes a randomized competitive ratio $O(\\log^2(k+1))$ for k-server on every metric space, matching the worst-case lower-bound order. One policy serves every finite oblivious request sequence, including on infinite unbounded metrics. On finite rational metrics, a uniform implementation has polynomial preprocessing and per-request bit cost in the input length and $\\log(t+1)$ at request t, with a finite instance-dependent additive movement constant.","pdfPath":"preprints/Uniform-computation-of-the-squared-logarithmic-k-server-bound-September-24-2026/Uniform-computation-of-the-squared-logarithmic-k-server-bound-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-computation-of-the-squared-logarithmic-k-server-bound-September-24-2026/Uniform-computation-of-the-squared-logarithmic-k-server-bound-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-computation-of-the-squared-logarithmic-k-server-bound-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Uniform-computation-of-the-squared-logarithmic-k-server-bound-September-24-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Uniform-computation-of-the-squared-logarithmic-k-server-bound-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Uniform-computation-of-the-squared-logarithmic-k-server-bound-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/02-finite-tables.tex","preprints/Uniform-computation-of-the-squared-logarithmic-k-server-bound-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/03-bounded-suffix.tex","preprints/Uniform-computation-of-the-squared-logarithmic-k-server-bound-September-24-2026/build/source/figures/epoch-shadow.tex","preprints/Uniform-computation-of-the-squared-logarithmic-k-server-bound-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/04-uniform-computation.tex","preprints/Uniform-computation-of-the-squared-logarithmic-k-server-bound-September-24-2026/build/source/figures/activation.tex","preprints/Uniform-computation-of-the-squared-logarithmic-k-server-bound-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/05-conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Uniform-computation-of-the-squared-logarithmic-k-server-bound-September-24-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a uniform randomized \\(k\\)-server algorithm on finite rational metrics with competitive ratio \\(O(\\log^2(k+1))\\) against oblivious request sequences. Preprocessing is polynomial in the input length, and per-request bit complexity is polynomial in that length and the binary request-counter length. The additive movement constant is finite and instance-dependent, but may be enormous. The construction uses the companion squared-logarithmic existence theorem.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":517},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and computational scope","sourceOffset":3541},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof ideas","sourceOffset":7211},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite rational decision tables","sourceOffset":9838},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The existence input and strong laziness","sourceOffset":11078},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite flow system","sourceOffset":14418},{"kind":"section","title":"A bounded table for an unbounded suffix","sourceOffset":23658},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A universal capped filter","sourceOffset":26003},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Virtual service and fallback","sourceOffset":29117},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparisons and amortization","sourceOffset":33595},{"kind":"section","title":"A uniform schedule in the bit model","sourceOffset":41666},{"kind":"section","title":"Scope of the construction","sourceOffset":56662}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are an absolute constant \\(C\\) and a polynomial \\(p\\) with the following property. Let \\(n\\ge3\\), \\(2\\le k<n\\), let \\(d\\) be a rational metric on \\(X=\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\), and let \\(s\\in X^k\\) be an initial labeled tuple, with repetitions allowed. Write \\(L\\) for the total binary encoding length of \\(n,k,d,s\\). A single uniform randomized online algorithm preprocesses these data in at most \\(p(L)\\) bit operations and serves request \\(r_t\\), for every reachable history and internal state, in at most \\[ p\\!\\left(L+\\left\\lceil\\log_2(t+1)\\right\\rceil\\right) \\] additional bit operations. It uses only unbiased random bits and moves exactly one chosen server to each request, permitting a zero-length move. For every fixed finite request sequence \\(\\sigma\\) independent of those bits, \\begin{equation} \\E\\cost_{A,s}(\\sigma) \\le C\\bigl(\\log(k+1)\\bigr)^2\\OPT_s(\\sigma)+B_{d,k,s}, \\end{equation} where \\(B_{d,k,s}\\) is finite and independent of \\(\\sigma\\) and its length. The algorithm does not need to know that length in advance.","sourceOffset":1652,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Existence from distinct initial positions, {\\cite[Theorem 1.1","label":"thm:existence","text":"{SquaredLog2026}}] There is an absolute constant $C_0<\\infty$ such that, for every integer $k\\ge2$, every finite metric space with at least $k+1$ points, and every distinct initial labeled tuple $u$, there exists a randomized online policy $A$ satisfying \\begin{equation} \\E\\bigl[\\operatorname{cost}_{A,u}(w)\\bigr] \\le C_0\\bigl(\\log(k+1)\\bigr)^2\\operatorname{OPT}_u(w) \\end{equation} for every fixed finite request word $w$ chosen independently of its random choices. The same policy serves all words and all horizons. At each request it chooses one labeled server and moves that server to the request, leaving the others fixed.","sourceOffset":11309,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/110.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves an $O((\\log(k+1))^2)$ competitive ratio for randomized $k$-server against oblivious finite request sequences. For every $k\\ge2$, every metric space containing at least $k+1$ points, and every initial configuration, one policy works for all request sequences, including in infinite and unbounded spaces.\n\nThe bound permits a configuration-dependent additive constant, which is zero when the initial server positions are distinct. The universal multiplicative constant is independent of the metric and $k$.\n\nThe formalization constructs one uniform randomized bit algorithm for $k$-server on finite rational metrics, for $2\\le k<n$. Its expected movement on every oblivious finite request sequence is at most an absolute multiple of $(\\log(k+1))^2$ times the offline optimum, p","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/110.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":57771,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Uniform-computation-of-the-squared-logarithmic-k-server-bound-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"111-01","slug":"One-Sample-Suffices-for-Matroid-Prophet-Inequalities-against-an-Almighty-Adversary-September-23-2026","familyId":"111","title":"One Sample Suffices for Matroid Prophet Inequalities against an Almighty Adversary","abstract":"We prove that one independent sample per element suffices for a constant-competitive prophet inequality on every finite matroid. The guarantee holds even when the arrival-order adversary observes all samples, all online values, and the algorithm's entire random seed. The rule needs no description of the value distributions and achieves the absolute competitive ratio $2^{-310}$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every finite matroid known in advance, gives a distribution-independent online rule using one independent sample per element and earning a universal constant fraction of the expected offline optimum. Values are independent and nonnegative, with finite expected optimum. The guarantee holds even when the arrival-order adversary sees all samples, values, and the rule's entire random seed; no polynomial-time implementation is asserted.","pdfPath":"preprints/One-Sample-Suffices-for-Matroid-Prophet-Inequalities-against-an-Almighty-Adversary-September-23-2026/final.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/One-Sample-Suffices-for-Matroid-Prophet-Inequalities-against-an-Almighty-Adversary-September-23-2026/final.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/One-Sample-Suffices-for-Matroid-Prophet-Inequalities-against-an-Almighty-Adversary-September-23-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/One-Sample-Suffices-for-Matroid-Prophet-Inequalities-against-an-Almighty-Adversary-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/One-Sample-Suffices-for-Matroid-Prophet-Inequalities-against-an-Almighty-Adversary-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/One-Sample-Suffices-for-Matroid-Prophet-Inequalities-against-an-Almighty-Adversary-September-23-2026/build/sections/setup.tex","preprints/One-Sample-Suffices-for-Matroid-Prophet-Inequalities-against-an-Almighty-Adversary-September-23-2026/build/sections/algorithm.tex","preprints/One-Sample-Suffices-for-Matroid-Prophet-Inequalities-against-an-Almighty-Adversary-September-23-2026/build/sections/safety.tex","preprints/One-Sample-Suffices-for-Matroid-Prophet-Inequalities-against-an-Almighty-Adversary-September-23-2026/build/sections/transfer.tex","preprints/One-Sample-Suffices-for-Matroid-Prophet-Inequalities-against-an-Almighty-Adversary-September-23-2026/build/sections/accounting.tex","preprints/One-Sample-Suffices-for-Matroid-Prophet-Inequalities-against-an-Almighty-Adversary-September-23-2026/build/sections/secretary.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/One-Sample-Suffices-for-Matroid-Prophet-Inequalities-against-an-Almighty-Adversary-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that one independent sample per element suffices for a constant-competitive prophet inequality on every finite matroid. The guarantee holds even when the arrival-order adversary observes all samples, all online values, and the algorithm's entire random seed. The rule needs no description of the value distributions and achieves the absolute competitive ratio $2^{-310}$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":426},{"kind":"section","title":"A fixed-vector formulation","sourceOffset":11814},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Elementary matroid facts","sourceOffset":15796},{"kind":"section","title":"Constructing the fixed-vector rule","sourceOffset":17045},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rounding and a maximum branch","sourceOffset":17622},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Candidates and weight groups","sourceOffset":19881},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Density expansions","sourceOffset":22466},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Nominal paths and guards","sourceOffset":24681},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The online decisions","sourceOffset":26794},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A generator budget for the entire path","sourceOffset":31343},{"kind":"section","title":"Reverse exposure and safe samples","sourceOffset":33826},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The reverse computation and its filtration","sourceOffset":35677}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a measurable online selection rule, independent of the value distributions, with the following property. Let $M=(E,\\mathcal I)$ be any finite known labeled matroid, and let $(S_e,V_e:e\\in E)$ be mutually independent nonnegative real random variables, with $S_e$ and $V_e$ having the same arbitrary law $D_e$. Assume $\\Ex[\\OPT_M(V)]<\\infty$. The rule receives exactly the sample vector $S$ before arrivals and uses a seed $R$ independent of $S,V$. For every measurable permutation rule $\\pi$ depending on $M$, the laws $(D_e)$, $S$, $V$, and $R$, the rule accepts an independent set $A$, irrevocably deciding at each arrival using only the available history, and \\begin{equation} \\Ex\\left[\\sum_{e\\in A}V_e\\right] \\ge 2^{-310}\\Ex[\\OPT_M(V)]. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":1573,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:hidden","text":"There is a measurable rule in the fixed-vector formulation that is feasible for every realization and every permutation, and for every $w\\in[0,\\infty)^E$ satisfies \\begin{equation} \\Ex_R\\left[\\min_{\\pi}\\sum_{e\\in A(w,R,\\pi)}w_e\\right] \\ge 2^{-293}\\OPT_M(w). \\end{equation} Its revealed mask is selected before any weights are seen.","sourceOffset":12951,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/111.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves a one-sample matroid prophet inequality with expected reward at least $2^{-310}$ times the expected offline optimum. Each element has one independent sample paired with an identically distributed nonnegative online value, and all coordinates are independent. The rule and finite seed law depend only on the labeled matroid; choices are irrevocable and feasible after every prefix. The arrival order may depend measurably on all samples, values, and the seed, and only the offline optimum must be integrable.\n\nA supporting hidden-vector selection result for fixed nonnegative matroid weights gives expected worst-order reward at least $2^{-293}$ times the optimum, again with a finite seed law and prefix feasibility.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/111.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":88503,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/One-Sample-Suffices-for-Matroid-Prophet-Inequalities-against-an-Almighty-Adversary-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"112-01","slug":"Beyond-the-Square-Root-Exponent-for-Depth-Three-Boolean-Circuits-September-23-2026","familyId":"112","title":"Beyond the Square-Root Exponent for Depth-Three Boolean Circuits","abstract":"We construct a language in deterministic polynomial time whose n-bit membership function requires $2^{\\omega(\\sqrt n)}$ gates in an unbounded-fan-in OR–AND–OR circuit. The bound holds at every sufficiently large input length and counts all gates, including the bottom layer.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a single language in deterministic polynomial time whose n-bit membership function requires $2^{\\omega(\\sqrt n)}$ total gates in unbounded-fan-in OR–AND–OR circuits, at every sufficiently large input length. This crosses the square-root-exponent threshold for explicit depth-three Boolean circuit lower bounds.","pdfPath":"preprints/Beyond-the-Square-Root-Exponent-for-Depth-Three-Boolean-Circuits-September-23-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Beyond-the-Square-Root-Exponent-for-Depth-Three-Boolean-Circuits-September-23-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Beyond-the-Square-Root-Exponent-for-Depth-Three-Boolean-Circuits-September-23-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Beyond-the-Square-Root-Exponent-for-Depth-Three-Boolean-Circuits-September-23-2026/build/latex/main.tex","preprints/Beyond-the-Square-Root-Exponent-for-Depth-Three-Boolean-Circuits-September-23-2026/build/latex/common.tex","preprints/Beyond-the-Square-Root-Exponent-for-Depth-Three-Boolean-Circuits-September-23-2026/build/latex/sections/00_introduction.tex","preprints/Beyond-the-Square-Root-Exponent-for-Depth-Three-Boolean-Circuits-September-23-2026/build/latex/sections/01_preliminaries.tex","preprints/Beyond-the-Square-Root-Exponent-for-Depth-Three-Boolean-Circuits-September-23-2026/build/latex/sections/02_restriction.tex","preprints/Beyond-the-Square-Root-Exponent-for-Depth-Three-Boolean-Circuits-September-23-2026/build/latex/sections/03_sparse_partition.tex","preprints/Beyond-the-Square-Root-Exponent-for-Depth-Three-Boolean-Circuits-September-23-2026/build/latex/sections/04_construction.tex","preprints/Beyond-the-Square-Root-Exponent-for-Depth-Three-Boolean-Circuits-September-23-2026/build/latex/sections/05_lower_bound.tex","preprints/Beyond-the-Square-Root-Exponent-for-Depth-Three-Boolean-Circuits-September-23-2026/build/latex/appendices/finite_fields.tex","preprints/Beyond-the-Square-Root-Exponent-for-Depth-Three-Boolean-Circuits-September-23-2026/build/latex/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Beyond-the-Square-Root-Exponent-for-Depth-Three-Boolean-Circuits-September-23-2026/build/latex/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a language in deterministic polynomial time whose \\(n\\)-bit membership function requires \\(2^{\\omega(\\sqrt n)}\\) gates in an unbounded-fan-in OR--AND--OR circuit. The bound holds at every sufficiently large input length and counts all gates, including the bottom layer.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":330},{"kind":"section","title":"Clauses, correlation, and restrictions","sourceOffset":8247},{"kind":"section","title":"A restriction bound independent of clause count","sourceOffset":10552},{"kind":"section","title":"A disjoint sparse partition of bounded-width CNFs","sourceOffset":25821},{"kind":"section","title":"One explicit language and its hard slices","sourceOffset":38534},{"kind":"section","title":"The depth-three circuit lower bound","sourceOffset":52634},{"kind":"section","title":"Irreducible polynomials over \\texorpdfstring{$\\F_2$}{F2}","sourceOffset":58290}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a language \\(L\\subseteq\\bits^*\\), a deterministic Turing machine deciding \\(L\\), and fixed positive integers \\(C,a\\) such that the machine runs in time at most \\(C(n+1)^a\\) on every input of length \\(n\\). If \\(f_n\\) is the membership function of \\(L\\) on \\(\\bits^n\\), then \\[ \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac{\\log_2 S_3(f_n)}{\\sqrt n}=\\infty. \\] Equivalently, for every real \\(A>0\\), there is an integer \\(N_A\\) such that \\[ S_3(f_n)>2^{A\\sqrt n}\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge N_A. \\] No uniformity or bottom fan-in restriction is imposed on the circuits.","sourceOffset":1379,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:polytime","text":"The language $L$ is decided on every input of length $n$ by one deterministic Turing machine in time $C(n+1)^a$, for some fixed positive integers $C,a$.","sourceOffset":41606,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"The language","label":"def:language","text":"For an input of length $n$, put $d=\\floor{n/5}$ and reject if $d<64$. Otherwise define \\[ r=\\ceil{d^{2/3}},\\qquad t=\\max\\{j\\in\\N\\cup\\{0\\}: j\\text{ is even and }j^6\\le d\\}. \\] Reject if fewer than $2d+(t+2)r-1$ input bits are available. Otherwise parse the input into the following consecutive blocks: \\begin{enumerate} \\item $d$ data bits $x=(x_1,\\ldots,x_d)$; \\item $d+r-1$ hash bits $u_1,\\ldots,u_{d+r-1}$; \\item $r$ bits $p_0,\\ldots,p_{r-1}$, encoding the monic polynomial \\[ P(Z)=Z^r+\\sum_{i=0}^{r-1}p_iZ^i\\in\\F_2[Z]; \\] \\item $t$ blocks of $r$ bits, encoding elements $\\beta_0,\\ldots,\\beta_{t-1}","sourceOffset":39323,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/112.md","scopeText":"The formalized result gives one polynomial-time Boolean language whose exact depth-three OR–AND–OR circuit size eventually exceeds $2^{A\\sqrt n}$ for every fixed $A>0$. The language and its polynomial-time algorithm are fixed before $A$ is chosen, while circuits may vary with the input length. Gates have unrestricted finite fan-in and sharing, with free input negations and constants. The result does not assert a fixed exponent $n^{1/2+\\varepsilon}$ or a lower bound for unrestricted depth.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/112.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":65314,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Beyond-the-Square-Root-Exponent-for-Depth-Three-Boolean-Circuits-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"113-01","slug":"A-Fully-Polynomial-Randomized-Approximation-Scheme-for-Perfect-Matchings-in-General-Graphs-September-23-2026","familyId":"113","title":"A Fully Polynomial Randomized Approximation Scheme for Perfect Matchings in General Graphs","abstract":"We give a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme (FPRAS) for counting perfect matchings in arbitrary finite simple undirected graphs, resolving the general-graph perfect-matching approximation problem. The algorithm returns zero with certainty when no perfect matching exists. Otherwise, it achieves relative error ε with failure probability at most δ in worst-case bit time polynomial in the input length, $\\varepsilon ^{-1}$, and $\\log\\delta^{-1}$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting perfect matchings in arbitrary finite simple graphs, with exact detection of zero counts. Also proves the perfect-matching entropy conjecture of Anari, Oveis Gharan, and Vinzant, bounding the maximum entropy of a matching law at every feasible edge-marginal vector in a loopless labelled multigraph, including boundary points.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Fully-Polynomial-Randomized-Approximation-Scheme-for-Perfect-Matchings-in-General-Graphs-September-23-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Fully-Polynomial-Randomized-Approximation-Scheme-for-Perfect-Matchings-in-General-Graphs-September-23-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Fully-Polynomial-Randomized-Approximation-Scheme-for-Perfect-Matchings-in-General-Graphs-September-23-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/A-Fully-Polynomial-Randomized-Approximation-Scheme-for-Perfect-Matchings-in-General-Graphs-September-23-2026/build/main.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Fully-Polynomial-Randomized-Approximation-Scheme-for-Perfect-Matchings-in-General-Graphs-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme (FPRAS) for counting perfect matchings in arbitrary finite simple undirected graphs, resolving the general-graph perfect-matching approximation problem. The algorithm returns zero with certainty when no perfect matching exists. Otherwise, it achieves relative error $\\eps$ with failure probability at most $\\delta$ in worst-case bit time polynomial in the input length, $\\eps^{-1}$, and $\\log\\delta^{-1}$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":526},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":1882},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof architecture","sourceOffset":7237},{"kind":"section","title":"Preliminaries","sourceOffset":12033},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weighted matchings and holes","sourceOffset":12136},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bottleneck similarities","sourceOffset":13940},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Tree bags","sourceOffset":14959},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Dirichlet energy and approximation","sourceOffset":16219},{"kind":"section","title":"Controlling the cost of unmatched vertices","sourceOffset":17649},{"kind":"section","title":"A subdivision with controlled holes","sourceOffset":27936},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The path profile","sourceOffset":28973},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The enlarged matching law","sourceOffset":32446}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a uniform classical randomized algorithm which, given $G$ and rational parameters $0<\\eps<1$ and $0<\\delta<1/2$, returns a nonnegative rational number $\\widehat Z$ such that \\[ \\Prb\\bigl[(1-\\eps)Z(G)\\le \\widehat Z\\le(1+\\eps)Z(G)\\bigr] \\ge 1-\\delta. \\] If $Z(G)=0$, the output is zero with certainty. The worst-case bit running time is polynomial in the input encoding length, $\\eps^{-1}$, and $\\log\\delta^{-1}$.","sourceOffset":1124,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Logical hole bound","label":"thm:logical-hole-bound","text":"Let $\\lambda$ be balanced, let $B$ be its bottleneck similarity, and set \\[ D_0=10^8(n+1)^4. \\] For every even set $U\\subseteq V$, \\begin{equation} g_\\lambda(U)\\Phi_B(U)\\le 2D_0^{|U|/2}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":18584,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:tree-bags","text":"A tree-bag graph consists of a finite rooted tree of labels and a finite even vertex set. A label $l$ at level $k$ has height $h_l=2^k$; the root has level zero and a child has level one greater than its parent. Each vertex belongs to either one label or two adjacent labels. The bag at $l$ is the set of vertices belonging to $l$. For each unordered pair of distinct vertices and each common label $l$, there is one edge of color $l$. There are no other edges. For an integer parameter $D\\ge1$, its reference activities obey \\begin{equation} \\frac{h_l}{D}\\le W_{xy,l}\\le3h_l. \\end{equation} We also ","sourceOffset":14982,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/113.md","scopeText":"The formalization gives a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting perfect matchings in every finite simple undirected graph. For rational $0<\\varepsilon<1$ and $0<\\delta<1/2$, it returns a nonnegative rational estimate with relative error at most $\\varepsilon$ with probability at least $1-\\delta$. If the graph has no perfect matching, every execution returns zero.\n\nThe algorithm is a fixed finite-alphabet randomized machine. Its worst-case running time is polynomial in the binary input length, $\\varepsilon^{-1}$, and $\\log(\\delta^{-1})$, including on unsuccessful random tapes.\n\nFor feasible edge marginals $x$ of perfect matchings in a loopless multigraph on $2m$ vertices, let $F(x)=-\\sum_e x_e\\log x_e$, $B(x)=-\\sum_e(1-x_e)\\log(1-x_e)$, and let $H(x)$ be the maximum e","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/113.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":148444,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Fully-Polynomial-Randomized-Approximation-Scheme-for-Perfect-Matchings-in-General-Graphs-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"113-02","slug":"Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026","familyId":"113","title":"Entropy and Face Dimension of the Perfect-Matching Polytope","abstract":"We prove the perfect-matching entropy conjecture of Anari, Oveis Gharan, and Vinzant. For every feasible vector x of perfect-matching edge marginals in a loopless labelled multigraph on $2m\\ge2$ vertices, the maximum entropy $H(x)$ of a matching law with marginals x satisfies $\\displaystyle F(x)-(2-2/m)B(x)\\le H(x)\\le F(x),$ where $F(x)=-\\sum_e x_e\\log x_e$ and $B(x)=-\\sum_e(1-x_e)\\log(1-x_e)$. This bound holds throughout the polytope, including its boundary. We also prove the sharp bound $|\\mathop{\\mathrm{supp}}\\nolimits x|-\\dim F_x\\le3m-2$, where Fx is the minimal face of the perfect-matching polytope containing x.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting perfect matchings in arbitrary finite simple graphs, with exact detection of zero counts. Also proves the perfect-matching entropy conjecture of Anari, Oveis Gharan, and Vinzant, bounding the maximum entropy of a matching law at every feasible edge-marginal vector in a loopless labelled multigraph, including boundary points.","pdfPath":"preprints/Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-polytope.tex","preprints/Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-entropy.tex","preprints/Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-consequences.tex","preprints/Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026/build/sections/10-examples.tex","preprints/Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-weighted.tex","preprints/Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-objective.tex","preprints/Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-optimization.tex","preprints/Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-odd-cuts.tex","preprints/Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026/build/figures/odd-cut-recursion.tex","preprints/Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026/build/sections/09-algorithm.tex","preprints/Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026/build/appendices/appendix-a.tex","preprints/Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026/build/appendices/appendix-b.tex","preprints/Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026/build/appendices/appendix-c.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the perfect-matching entropy conjecture of Anari, Oveis Gharan, and Vinzant. For every feasible vector $x$ of perfect-matching edge marginals in a loopless labelled multigraph on $2m\\ge2$ vertices, the maximum entropy $H(x)$ of a matching law with marginals $x$ satisfies \\[ F(x)-(2-2/m)B(x)\\le H(x)\\le F(x), \\] where $F(x)=-\\sum_e x_e\\log x_e$ and $B(x)=-\\sum_e(1-x_e)\\log(1-x_e)$. This bound holds throughout the polytope, including its boundary. We also prove the sharp bound $|\\supp x|-\\dim F_x\\le3m-2$, where $F_x$ is the minimal face of the perfect-matching polytope containing $x$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":643},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Entropy and structural consequences","sourceOffset":4001},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Face dimension and the entropy argument","sourceOffset":7032},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Deterministic counting with binary multiplicities","sourceOffset":10708},{"kind":"section","title":"Faces and tight cuts of the perfect matching polytope","sourceOffset":16401},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Contraction and the odd-cut description","sourceOffset":17748},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Directions in the minimal face","sourceOffset":23491},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sharp codimension from compatible directions","sourceOffset":26889},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removing fixed coordinates","sourceOffset":30559},{"kind":"section","title":"Entropy and the covariance trace","sourceOffset":34320},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Continuity and maximum entropy on a face","sourceOffset":34830},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The trace obstruction","sourceOffset":39920}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Pointwise entropy bounds","label":"ent:main","text":"Let $G$ be a loopless multigraph on $2m\\ge2$ vertices with at least one perfect matching. For every $x\\in P(G)$, \\begin{equation} F(x)-\\left(2-\\frac2m\\right)B(x) \\le H(x)\\le F(x). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":2154,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Deterministic approximate counting","label":"thm:main","text":"There is one uniform deterministic algorithm which, on a graph on $n$ vertices with nonnegative binary integer pair multiplicities, returns an integer $A\\ge0$ satisfying \\[ N(G)/512^n\\le A\\le N(G). \\] Its running time and output length are polynomial in the input bit length. In particular, $A=0$ exactly when $N(G)=0$. The guarantee includes a compact input consisting of binary $n$ and a list of positive pair records.","sourceOffset":11222,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/113.md","scopeText":"The formalization gives a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting perfect matchings in every finite simple undirected graph. For rational $0<\\varepsilon<1$ and $0<\\delta<1/2$, it returns a nonnegative rational estimate with relative error at most $\\varepsilon$ with probability at least $1-\\delta$. If the graph has no perfect matching, every execution returns zero.\n\nThe algorithm is a fixed finite-alphabet randomized machine. Its worst-case running time is polynomial in the binary input length, $\\varepsilon^{-1}$, and $\\log(\\delta^{-1})$, including on unsuccessful random tapes.\n\nFor feasible edge marginals $x$ of perfect matchings in a loopless multigraph on $2m$ vertices, let $F(x)=-\\sum_e x_e\\log x_e$, $B(x)=-\\sum_e(1-x_e)\\log(1-x_e)$, and let $H(x)$ be the maximum e","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/113.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":144028,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":22,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Entropy-and-Face-Dimension-of-the-Perfect-Matching-Polytope-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"114-01","slug":"An-FPRAS-for-Common-Integer-Polymatroid-Bases-with-Binary-Capacities-October-5-2026","familyId":"114","title":"An FPRAS for Common Integer Polymatroid Bases with Binary Capacities","abstract":"We give a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting common integer bases of two polymatroids with the same total rank, supplied by exact rank-value oracles. The total rank and capacities are encoded in binary, and each integer vector is counted once. On every execution, the number of oracle calls and the bit work outside the oracles are bounded by a fixed polynomial in the ground-set size, the binary input length, the inverse relative-error tolerance, and the logarithm of the inverse failure probability. The algorithm handles binary capacities directly, without expanding them into labelled copies.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting common integer bases of two integral polymatroids of equal total rank, supplied by exact rank-value oracles. Capacities are binary-encoded, each integer vector counts once, and oracle calls and bit operations outside the oracles are polynomial on every execution. For matroids presented by independence oracles, the results also cover common independent sets of prescribed, unrestricted, or maximum cardinality, even when the ranks differ.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Common-Integer-Polymatroid-Bases-with-Binary-Capacities-October-5-2026/polymatroid-fpras.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Common-Integer-Polymatroid-Bases-with-Binary-Capacities-October-5-2026/polymatroid-fpras.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Common-Integer-Polymatroid-Bases-with-Binary-Capacities-October-5-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Common-Integer-Polymatroid-Bases-with-Binary-Capacities-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Common-Integer-Polymatroid-Bases-with-Binary-Capacities-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Common-Integer-Polymatroid-Bases-with-Binary-Capacities-October-5-2026/build/sections/polyhedra.tex","preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Common-Integer-Polymatroid-Bases-with-Binary-Capacities-October-5-2026/build/sections/signatures.tex","preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Common-Integer-Polymatroid-Bases-with-Binary-Capacities-October-5-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Common-Integer-Polymatroid-Bases-with-Binary-Capacities-October-5-2026/build/sections/ports.tex","preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Common-Integer-Polymatroid-Bases-with-Binary-Capacities-October-5-2026/build/sections/evaluation.tex","preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Common-Integer-Polymatroid-Bases-with-Binary-Capacities-October-5-2026/build/sections/counting.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Common-Integer-Polymatroid-Bases-with-Binary-Capacities-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting common integer bases of two polymatroids with the same total rank, supplied by exact rank-value oracles. The total rank and capacities are encoded in binary, and each integer vector is counted once. On every execution, the number of oracle calls and the bit work outside the oracles are bounded by a fixed polynomial in the ground-set size, the binary input length, the inverse relative-error tolerance, and the logarithm of the inverse failure probability. The algorithm handles binary capacities directly, without expanding them into labelled copies.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":671},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The problem and the result","sourceOffset":1838},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and prior work","sourceOffset":4174},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":9526},{"kind":"section","title":"Polyhedral tools and a feasible center","sourceOffset":12748},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact oracle operations","sourceOffset":14046},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Base-polytope calculus","sourceOffset":17691},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Integrality and quantitative stability","sourceOffset":22901},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Constructing a feasible center","sourceOffset":29277},{"kind":"section","title":"Integer signatures and difference counts","sourceOffset":32818},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two tests and balanced summation","sourceOffset":33621},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rank penalties, grouped coordinates, and reflection","sourceOffset":40852}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a single uniform randomized algorithm which, for every input described above, outputs a nonnegative rational $\\widehat Z$ satisfying \\[ \\PP\\bigl((1-\\eps)Z\\le\\widehat Z\\le(1+\\eps)Z\\bigr) \\ge1-\\delta. \\] If $Z=0$, the algorithm outputs zero on every execution. On every execution, both its number of oracle calls and its number of other bit operations are bounded by one fixed polynomial in \\[ n,\\qquad \\ell,\\qquad \\eps^{-1},\\qquad \\log(\\delta^{-1}). \\] The bit-operation bound includes rational arithmetic, generation of independent unbiased random bits, and the encoding of the output.","sourceOffset":3160,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Oracle algorithms","label":"prop:oracle-tools","text":"The following operations have deterministic oracle and bit complexity bounded by fixed polynomials in the indicated input sizes. \\begin{enumerate} \\item An integer-valued submodular function on a finite ground set can be minimized, with a minimizing set returned, from an exact value oracle and a bound on the binary lengths of its values. The size parameters are the ground-set cardinality and that value-length bound. \\item Let $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ be a bounded rational polytope, supplied by strong separation, a coordinate bound, and a bound on the binary encoding length of each individual defining inequality. The separator returns inequalities of length polynomial in the query length and the supplied description bounds. Exact rational linear optimization, including an emptiness decision and the production of an optimal vertex when $Q$ is nonempty, is polynomial in $d$, these bounds, and the objective length. This assertion includes lower-dimensional polytopes. All separation queries have polynomial encoding length. \\end{enumerate} The first operation also applies to an integer submodular function with a rational modular term added, and with prescribed elements required to belong or not to belong to the minimizing set.","sourceOffset":14223,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Integer quadratic signatures","label":"def:integer-signatures","text":"A strictly positive weight $F$ on all of $\\mathcal B(m,r)$ has the removal signature if, for every box-valid display $z$ of total $r+2$, the matrix \\begin{equation} \\bigl(F(z-e_i-e_j)\\bigr)_{i,j\\in I} \\quad\\text{has at most one positive eigenvalue}. \\end{equation} It has the addition signature if, for every box-valid display $z$ of total $r-2$, the same conclusion holds for \\begin{equation} \\bigl(F(z+e_i+e_j)\\bigr)_{i,j\\in I}. \\end{equation} Diagonal entries represent two changes in the same coordinate. All invalid changes have weight zero. A test with no box-valid display is vacuous.","sourceOffset":34002,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/114.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":145640,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-FPRAS-for-Common-Integer-Polymatroid-Bases-with-Binary-Capacities-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Common-Integer-Polymatroid-Bases-with-Binary-Capacities-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Common-Integer-Polymatroid-Bases-with-Binary-Capacities-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"114-02","slug":"Approximate-counting-of-common-bases-of-two-matroids-September-23-2026","familyId":"114","title":"Approximate counting of common bases of two matroids","abstract":"We give a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting the common bases of two arbitrary matroids of the same rank, supplied by independence oracles. The algorithm requires no explicit representation of either matroid and has polynomial bounds on both oracle calls and bit operations on every execution.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting common integer bases of two integral polymatroids of equal total rank, supplied by exact rank-value oracles. Capacities are binary-encoded, each integer vector counts once, and oracle calls and bit operations outside the oracles are polynomial on every execution. For matroids presented by independence oracles, the results also cover common independent sets of prescribed, unrestricted, or maximum cardinality, even when the ranks differ.","pdfPath":"preprints/Approximate-counting-of-common-bases-of-two-matroids-September-23-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Approximate-counting-of-common-bases-of-two-matroids-September-23-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Approximate-counting-of-common-bases-of-two-matroids-September-23-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Approximate-counting-of-common-bases-of-two-matroids-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Approximate-counting-of-common-bases-of-two-matroids-September-23-2026/build/figures/transport.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Approximate-counting-of-common-bases-of-two-matroids-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting the common bases of two arbitrary matroids of the same rank, supplied by independence oracles. The algorithm requires no explicit representation of either matroid and has polynomial bounds on both oracle calls and bit operations on every execution.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":366},{"kind":"section","title":"The paired-ground-set reduction","sourceOffset":10837},{"kind":"section","title":"Quadratic signature inequalities","sourceOffset":14248},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Relations between defect totals","sourceOffset":16830},{"kind":"section","title":"A transport inequality","sourceOffset":19908},{"kind":"section","title":"A chain and its observable Poincar\\'e bound","sourceOffset":33359},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Variation on transversals","sourceOffset":35657},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Means of defect classes","sourceOffset":38861},{"kind":"section","title":"Two consequences for simulation","sourceOffset":43325},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Time averages from a warm start","sourceOffset":43932},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The trace on transversals","sourceOffset":46016},{"kind":"section","title":"Annealing and learning the multipliers","sourceOffset":49067}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a single randomized oracle algorithm with the following guarantee. Given independence oracles for arbitrary rank-$r$ matroids $M_1,M_2$ on $[n]$, and rational $\\epsilon,\\delta\\in(0,1)$, it outputs a nonnegative rational $\\widehat Z$ such that \\[ \\Prob\\bigl[(1-\\epsilon)Z(M_1,M_2)\\le\\widehat Z \\le(1+\\epsilon)Z(M_1,M_2)\\bigr]\\ge1-\\delta. \\] If $Z(M_1,M_2)=0$, its output is always zero. On every execution, the number of oracle calls and the number of other bit operations are bounded by a fixed polynomial in \\[ n,\\qquad \\ell,\\qquad \\epsilon^{-1},\\qquad \\log\\delta^{-1}, \\] where $\\ell$ is the binary encoding length of $n,r,\\epsilon,\\delta$. The algorithm uses only independent unbiased random bits and does not require a representation of either matroid.","sourceOffset":1833,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Br\\\"and\\'en--Huh","label":"thm:BH","text":"For a matroid $N$ on a ground set $E$ and $0<q\\le1$, the polynomial \\[ T_{q,N}(z_0,z)=\\sum_{A\\subseteq E} q^{-\\rank_N(A)}z_0^{|E|-|A|}\\prod_{a\\in A}z_a \\] is Lorentzian. Lorentzian polynomials are closed under partial derivatives and nonnegative linear substitutions, and a quadratic Lorentzian polynomial has a Hessian with at most one positive eigenvalue. In particular, $T_{q,N}$ has the quadratic signature property.","sourceOffset":15057,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/114.md","scopeText":"The formalized result gives a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for the number of common bases of two equal-rank matroids on an enumerated finite ground set, using independence oracles. For rational $0<\\varepsilon,\\delta<1$, the nonnegative rational output has relative error at most $\\varepsilon$ with probability at least $1-\\delta$. A zero count produces zero on every execution. Oracle calls and bit operations have polynomial bounds on every random tape in the input size, $\\varepsilon^{-1}$, and $\\log(\\delta^{-1})$.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/114.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":81892,"sourceFileCount":2,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Approximate-counting-of-common-bases-of-two-matroids-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"115-01","slug":"Exact-Uniform-Sampling-of-Contingency-Tables-with-Arbitrary-Margins-September-24-2026","familyId":"115","title":"Exact Uniform Sampling of Contingency Tables with Arbitrary Margins","abstract":"We give an exact uniform sampler for nonnegative integer contingency tables with arbitrary prescribed margins. It terminates almost surely and has expected bit complexity polynomial in both dimensions and the binary length of the margins. No positivity, balance, sparsity, or fixed-dimension assumption is required.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For nonnegative integer matrices with prescribed row and column sums, gives exact uniform sampling in expected polynomial bit time and almost-uniform sampling in worst-case polynomial bit time. The dimensions and binary-encoded margins are unrestricted. Also gives a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting such tables with arbitrary individual cell bounds, including structural zeros, with polynomial cost on every execution.","pdfPath":"preprints/Exact-Uniform-Sampling-of-Contingency-Tables-with-Arbitrary-Margins-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Exact-Uniform-Sampling-of-Contingency-Tables-with-Arbitrary-Margins-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Exact-Uniform-Sampling-of-Contingency-Tables-with-Arbitrary-Margins-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Exact-Uniform-Sampling-of-Contingency-Tables-with-Arbitrary-Margins-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Exact-Uniform-Sampling-of-Contingency-Tables-with-Arbitrary-Margins-September-24-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/Exact-Uniform-Sampling-of-Contingency-Tables-with-Arbitrary-Margins-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Exact-Uniform-Sampling-of-Contingency-Tables-with-Arbitrary-Margins-September-24-2026/build/sections/model-and-graph.tex","preprints/Exact-Uniform-Sampling-of-Contingency-Tables-with-Arbitrary-Margins-September-24-2026/build/figures/repair.tex","preprints/Exact-Uniform-Sampling-of-Contingency-Tables-with-Arbitrary-Margins-September-24-2026/build/sections/signatures.tex","preprints/Exact-Uniform-Sampling-of-Contingency-Tables-with-Arbitrary-Margins-September-24-2026/build/sections/transport.tex","preprints/Exact-Uniform-Sampling-of-Contingency-Tables-with-Arbitrary-Margins-September-24-2026/build/sections/dense.tex","preprints/Exact-Uniform-Sampling-of-Contingency-Tables-with-Arbitrary-Margins-September-24-2026/build/sections/algorithms.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Exact-Uniform-Sampling-of-Contingency-Tables-with-Arbitrary-Margins-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give an exact uniform sampler for nonnegative integer contingency tables with arbitrary prescribed margins. It terminates almost surely and has expected bit complexity polynomial in both dimensions and the binary length of the margins. No positivity, balance, sparsity, or fixed-dimension assumption is required.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":361},{"kind":"section","title":"The binary model and the padded graph","sourceOffset":9411},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Small states and large completions","sourceOffset":11058},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Repairs and graph edges","sourceOffset":14108},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Stationary mass and unpadding","sourceOffset":20198},{"kind":"section","title":"Quadratic signatures and balanced summation","sourceOffset":24241},{"kind":"section","title":"Transport and the graph variance bound","sourceOffset":38197},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Child weights and adjacent means","sourceOffset":39822},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From conditional transport to variance","sourceOffset":51576},{"kind":"section","title":"Sampling a dense completion block","sourceOffset":54576},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Scaled coordinates and rejection from bins","sourceOffset":56218},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An integral interpolation inequality","sourceOffset":64277}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For arbitrary nonnegative integer margins of equal total, the following algorithms exist in the model of unbiased random bits and bit operations. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item Given an integer $k\\ge1$, a bounded-time algorithm outputs a table in $\\Omega(r,c)$ whose law is within $2^{-k}$ in total variation of the uniform law. Its running time is polynomial in $m,n,\\log(N+1),k$. \\item An algorithm outputs an exactly uniform table in $\\Omega(r,c)$, terminates almost surely, and has expected running time polynomial in $m,n,\\log(N+1)$. \\end{enumerate} The polynomials are uniform over all margin vectors. No positivity, sparsity, balance, or fixed-dimension hypothesis is imposed.","sourceOffset":1138,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Stationary mass and unpadding","label":"prop:stationary-success","text":"The masses satisfy \\begin{equation} 0<Z_0\\le\\Lambda\\le(1+d^2)Z_0, \\qquad \\pi_{\\min}\\ge(C+1)^{-2d}. \\end{equation} Draw $X$ with law $\\pi$ and then a uniform $A\\in\\mathcal F_X$. Declare success if $X$ is a transversal and every large entry of $A$ is at least $L$. Subtracting $L$ from these entries gives a uniform original table conditional on success, and \\begin{equation} \\Pp\\{\\text{success}\\}\\ge\\frac{1}{2(1+d^2)}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":21872,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Softened weights","label":"def:soft-weight","text":"Let $\\mathcal B$ and $\\mathcal S$ be the large and small slots from Section \\ref{sec:graph}, and put \\[ T=\\sum_s B_s,\\qquad \\mathcal Q=\\prod_s\\{0,\\ldots,B_s\\}^2. \\] For a full coordinate vector $\\xi=(x_s,y_s)_s$, write $|\\xi|=\\sum_s(x_s+y_s)$ and $q_s=B_s-y_s$. For $a\\in\\R$, let $a_+=\\max(a,0)$. Define \\begin{align*} D(\\xi)={}&\\sum_i\\left|\\sum_jx_{ij}-\\bar r_i\\right| +\\sum_j\\left|\\sum_iq_{ij}-\\bar c_j\\right|\\\\ &+\\sum_i\\left(\\sum_{j:(i,j)\\in\\mathcal S}x_{ij}-r_i\\right)_+ +\\sum_j\\left(\\sum_{i:(i,j)\\in\\mathcal S}q_{ij}-c_j\\right)_+. \\end{align*} For $0<\\eta<1$, set \\[ F_\\eta(\\xi)= \\begin{cases} \\","sourceOffset":25076,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/115.md","scopeText":"The formalization gives an exact uniform sampler for nonnegative integer contingency tables with arbitrary prescribed row and column sums having equal totals. It terminates almost surely, every halted output is feasible, and each table has exactly the uniform limiting probability. Expected bit complexity is polynomial in the dimensions and the binary length of the margins.\n\nIt also gives a sampler with a fixed polynomial time bound on every execution whose total-variation error is at most $2^{-k}$ for requested precision $k\\ge1$. The same Comparator file includes the companion approximation scheme for counting tables with individual cell bounds.\n\nThe formalization gives a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting nonnegative integer matrices with prescribed row sums, co","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/115.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":100990,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Exact-Uniform-Sampling-of-Contingency-Tables-with-Arbitrary-Margins-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"115-02","slug":"An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026","familyId":"115","title":"An FPRAS for Cell-Bounded Contingency Tables","abstract":"We give a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting nonnegative integer matrices with prescribed row sums, column sums, and individual entry bounds. Both dimensions vary, all numerical data are encoded in binary, and zero bounds are allowed. The algorithm uses only unbiased random bits and has a polynomial bound on its bit operations on every execution.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For nonnegative integer matrices with prescribed row and column sums, gives exact uniform sampling in expected polynomial bit time and almost-uniform sampling in worst-case polynomial bit time. The dimensions and binary-encoded margins are unrestricted. Also gives a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting such tables with arbitrary individual cell bounds, including structural zeros, with polynomial cost on every execution.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026/build/sections/history.tex","preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026/build/sections/scales.tex","preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026/build/sections/integer-signatures.tex","preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026/build/sections/signatures.tex","preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026/build/figures/paired-states.tex","preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026/build/sections/transport.tex","preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026/build/sections/evaluation.tex","preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026/build/sections/algorithm.tex","preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026/build/sections/flows.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting nonnegative integer matrices with prescribed row sums, column sums, and individual entry bounds. Both dimensions vary, all numerical data are encoded in binary, and zero bounds are allowed. The algorithm uses only unbiased random bits and has a polynomial bound on its bit operations on every execution.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":421},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":2506},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From tables to weighted transversals","sourceOffset":7945},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and conventions","sourceOffset":14093},{"kind":"section","title":"Tight capacities and two scales","sourceOffset":15445},{"kind":"section","title":"Integer signatures and the softened count","sourceOffset":28443},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A projection fact for quadratic forms","sourceOffset":29384},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Integer coordinates and balanced summation","sourceOffset":30769},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Application to the softened table weights","sourceOffset":39931},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Tree marginals and contamination","sourceOffset":42896},{"kind":"section","title":"Paired binary states and two factorial lifts","sourceOffset":48853},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The paired encoding and its weights","sourceOffset":49444}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is one randomized algorithm with the following guarantee. Given $(r,c,b)$ as above and rational $\\varepsilon,\\delta\\in(0,1)$, it outputs a nonnegative rational $\\widehat Z$ such that \\[ \\P\\bigl((1-\\varepsilon)Z(r,c,b)\\le \\widehat Z \\le(1+\\varepsilon)Z(r,c,b)\\bigr)\\ge1-\\delta. \\] If $\\Omega(r,c,b)$ is empty, the output is zero on every execution. The algorithm uses independent unbiased bits, and one fixed polynomial in $L$, $\\varepsilon^{-1}$, and $\\log(\\delta^{-1})$ bounds its total bit operations on every execution, where $L$ is the total binary input length, including the accuracy parameters. No counting or sampling oracle is required.","sourceOffset":1315,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Contamination bound","label":"prop:contamination","text":"For the feasible tightened instance, \\[ Z\\le C(H)\\le(1+\\epsilon/32)Z. \\]","sourceOffset":27921,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/115.md","scopeText":"The formalization gives an exact uniform sampler for nonnegative integer contingency tables with arbitrary prescribed row and column sums having equal totals. It terminates almost surely, every halted output is feasible, and each table has exactly the uniform limiting probability. Expected bit complexity is polynomial in the dimensions and the binary length of the margins.\n\nIt also gives a sampler with a fixed polynomial time bound on every execution whose total-variation error is at most $2^{-k}$ for requested precision $k\\ge1$. The same Comparator file includes the companion approximation scheme for counting tables with individual cell bounds.\n\nThe formalization gives a fully polynomial randomized approximation scheme for counting nonnegative integer matrices with prescribed row sums, co","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/115.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":160007,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"116-01","slug":"Uniform-Matrix-Hitting-Points-in-Every-Positive-Characteristic-October-4-2026","familyId":"116","title":"Uniform Matrix Hitting Points in Every Positive Characteristic","abstract":"We construct a single matrix substitution that detects every nonzero size-s division-free noncommutative formula in n variables over every field of a given positive characteristic. One deterministic machine, given a promised prime p in binary and n, s in unary, outputs matrices over 𝔽p of dimension $O(n^3s^6)$ in polynomial bit time. The same tuple works with arbitrary extension-field coefficients, including in characteristic two. The construction also applies to the stated acyclic algebraic path programs.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For each characteristic, constructs in deterministic polynomial bit time a polynomial-dimensional matrix tuple detecting every nonzero division-free noncommutative formula of bounded size over any field of that characteristic. Rational formulas over ℚ also admit polynomial-size hitting lists whenever they have a defined rational-matrix evaluation.","pdfPath":"preprints/Uniform-Matrix-Hitting-Points-in-Every-Positive-Characteristic-October-4-2026/uniform-matrix-hitting-points-positive-characteristic.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-Matrix-Hitting-Points-in-Every-Positive-Characteristic-October-4-2026/uniform-matrix-hitting-points-positive-characteristic.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-Matrix-Hitting-Points-in-Every-Positive-Characteristic-October-4-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Uniform-Matrix-Hitting-Points-in-Every-Positive-Characteristic-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Uniform-Matrix-Hitting-Points-in-Every-Positive-Characteristic-October-4-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Uniform-Matrix-Hitting-Points-in-Every-Positive-Characteristic-October-4-2026/build/sections/words.tex","preprints/Uniform-Matrix-Hitting-Points-in-Every-Positive-Characteristic-October-4-2026/build/sections/systems.tex","preprints/Uniform-Matrix-Hitting-Points-in-Every-Positive-Characteristic-October-4-2026/build/sections/order.tex","preprints/Uniform-Matrix-Hitting-Points-in-Every-Positive-Characteristic-October-4-2026/build/sections/construction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Uniform-Matrix-Hitting-Points-in-Every-Positive-Characteristic-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a single matrix substitution that detects every nonzero size-$s$ division-free noncommutative formula in $n$ variables over every field of a given positive characteristic. One deterministic machine, given a promised prime $p$ in binary and $n,s$ in unary, outputs matrices over $\\mathbb F_p$ of dimension $O(n^3s^6)$ in polynomial bit time. The same tuple works with arbitrary extension-field coefficients, including in characteristic two. The construction also applies to the stated acyclic algebraic path programs.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":575},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The result","sourceOffset":1265},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and the characteristic obstruction","sourceOffset":3609},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":6339},{"kind":"section","title":"Independent word series from a multiplicative shift","sourceOffset":8876},{"kind":"section","title":"From formulas to finite shift systems","sourceOffset":16216},{"kind":"section","title":"An order bound for multiplicative-shift systems","sourceOffset":23918},{"kind":"section","title":"Truncation and construction over the prime field","sourceOffset":30461},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Truncating the word operators","sourceOffset":30987},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Retaining finitely many coefficients of \\texorpdfstring{$t$}{t}","sourceOffset":33053},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The uniform algorithm and the main theorem","sourceOffset":36864}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a deterministic Turing machine and absolute positive integers $C,k$ with the following property. Given a prime $p$ in binary and positive integers $n,s$ in unary, the machine outputs a positive integer $d=O(n^3s^6)$ and a tuple \\[ (T_1,\\ldots,T_n)\\in\\Mat_d(\\F_p)^n. \\] For every field $F$ of characteristic $p$ and every nonzero $f\\in F\\langle x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\rangle$ computed by a formula of size at most $s$, the same tuple, viewed over $F$, satisfies \\[ f(T_1,\\ldots,T_n)\\ne0. \\] Writing $L=\\lceil\\log_2p\\rceil$, the total running time and output length are at most $C(n+s+L+1)^k$. All matrix entries are output densely as ordinary binary residues in $\\{0,\\ldots,p-1\\}$, with dimensions and separators included. Primality of $p$ is promised.","sourceOffset":1933,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Word-series independence","label":"thm:words","text":"For every field $F$ and every positive integer $n$, the series $(h_w)_{w\\in\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}^*}$ in \\eqref{eq:word-series} are linearly independent over $F(t)(z)$.","sourceOffset":14096,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/116.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":41613,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Uniform-Matrix-Hitting-Points-in-Every-Positive-Characteristic-October-4-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"116-02","slug":"One-Rational-Matrix-Hitting-Point-for-Noncommutative-Formulas-September-24-2026","familyId":"116","title":"One Rational Matrix Hitting Point for Noncommutative Formulas","abstract":"We construct, in deterministic polynomial bit time, one tuple of rational matrices that detects every nonzero polynomial computed by a noncommutative division-free formula of a prescribed size. The matrices have dimension $O(ns^2)$ for n variables and formula size s, and the same tuple works over every field of characteristic zero.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For each characteristic, constructs in deterministic polynomial bit time a polynomial-dimensional matrix tuple detecting every nonzero division-free noncommutative formula of bounded size over any field of that characteristic. Rational formulas over ℚ also admit polynomial-size hitting lists whenever they have a defined rational-matrix evaluation.","pdfPath":"preprints/One-Rational-Matrix-Hitting-Point-for-Noncommutative-Formulas-September-24-2026/One-Rational-Matrix-Hitting-Point-for-Noncommutative-Formulas-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/One-Rational-Matrix-Hitting-Point-for-Noncommutative-Formulas-September-24-2026/One-Rational-Matrix-Hitting-Point-for-Noncommutative-Formulas-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/One-Rational-Matrix-Hitting-Point-for-Noncommutative-Formulas-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/One-Rational-Matrix-Hitting-Point-for-Noncommutative-Formulas-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/One-Rational-Matrix-Hitting-Point-for-Noncommutative-Formulas-September-24-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/One-Rational-Matrix-Hitting-Point-for-Noncommutative-Formulas-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-route.tex","preprints/One-Rational-Matrix-Hitting-Point-for-Noncommutative-Formulas-September-24-2026/build/iterated-integrals.tex","preprints/One-Rational-Matrix-Hitting-Point-for-Noncommutative-Formulas-September-24-2026/build/formula-systems.tex","preprints/One-Rational-Matrix-Hitting-Point-for-Noncommutative-Formulas-September-24-2026/build/multiplicity.tex","preprints/One-Rational-Matrix-Hitting-Point-for-Noncommutative-Formulas-September-24-2026/build/construction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/One-Rational-Matrix-Hitting-Point-for-Noncommutative-Formulas-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct, in deterministic polynomial bit time, one tuple of rational matrices that detects every nonzero polynomial computed by a noncommutative division-free formula of a prescribed size. The matrices have dimension $O(ns^2)$ for $n$ variables and formula size $s$, and the same tuple works over every field of characteristic zero.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":400},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and scope","sourceOffset":2791},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof idea","sourceOffset":6732},{"kind":"section","title":"A faithful encoding by iterated integrals","sourceOffset":11441},{"kind":"section","title":"From formulas to differential systems","sourceOffset":19136},{"kind":"section","title":"A bound on the order of vanishing","sourceOffset":24956},{"kind":"section","title":"The rational hitting point","sourceOffset":31773},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Taylor coefficients as a matrix space","sourceOffset":32060},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Detection and construction cost","sourceOffset":33748},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Acyclic path programs","sourceOffset":35635}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a deterministic algorithm which, given integers $n,s\\ge1$, constructs a tuple \\[ \\mathbf T_{n,s}\\in\\Mat_d(\\Q)^n, \\qquad d\\le 2ns^2, \\] in time polynomial in $n,s$, measured in bit operations. The numerators and denominators of its entries have bit length $O(\\log(ns+1)+ns^2\\log(n+1))$. For every field $F$ of characteristic zero and every $0\\ne f\\in\\mathsf{Form}_{n,s}(F)$, \\[ f(\\mathbf T_{n,s})\\ne0, \\] where the tuple is viewed over $F$ through the canonical embedding $\\Q\\hookrightarrow F$. The construction uses $d=2ns^2$ when $ns\\ge2$ and $d=1$ when $n=s=1$.","sourceOffset":1645,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"The system associated to a formula","label":"prop:formula-system","text":"Let $F$ have characteristic zero, and let $0\\ne f\\in F\\langle x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\rangle$ have a formula of size at most $s$. There are $m\\le2s$, a nonzero constant row $u$, a constant column $v$, and an invertible matrix $G\\in F[[z]]^{m\\times m}$ such that \\begin{equation} G'=AG,\\qquad G(0)=I_m,\\qquad A(z)=\\frac{P(z)}{q(z)},\\qquad q(z)=\\prod_{i=1}^n(z+i),\\quad \\deg P\\le n-1, \\end{equation} where $P$ is a polynomial matrix. Moreover, \\begin{equation} h_f(z)=\\sum_w f_wh_w(z)=uG(z)v\\ne0. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":23035,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/116.md","scopeText":"The formalization gives one explicit tuple of rational matrices that simultaneously detects every nonzero division-free noncommutative formula with at most $s$ gates in $n$ variables, for $n,s\\ge1$. Evaluation at that tuple is a nonzero matrix over every characteristic-zero field.\n\nThe selected theorem states this universal hitting property. The paper's deterministic polynomial bit-construction bound and matrix-dimension bound $O(ns^2)$ are not separately asserted in it.\n\nThe formalized result constructs polynomial-size hitting lists for noncommutative rational formulas with rational constants, addition, multiplication, and inverse gates. Given the number of variables and a formula-size bound, one deterministic polynomial-time machine outputs rational matrix tuples of a common positive dim","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/116.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":38298,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/One-Rational-Matrix-Hitting-Point-for-Noncommutative-Formulas-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"116-03","slug":"Polynomial-Hitting-Lists-for-Noncommutative-Rational-Formulas-September-24-2026","familyId":"116","title":"Polynomial Hitting Lists for Noncommutative Rational Formulas","abstract":"We construct, in deterministic polynomial bit time, a polynomial-size list of rational matrix tuples for noncommutative rational formulas over ℚ of bounded tree size. Every nonzero admissible formula has a defined, invertible value at one tuple, with no separate bounds on inverse nesting or rational constant heights. Matrix dimensions, entry bit lengths, and total output length are polynomially bounded.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For each characteristic, constructs in deterministic polynomial bit time a polynomial-dimensional matrix tuple detecting every nonzero division-free noncommutative formula of bounded size over any field of that characteristic. Rational formulas over ℚ also admit polynomial-size hitting lists whenever they have a defined rational-matrix evaluation.","pdfPath":"preprints/Polynomial-Hitting-Lists-for-Noncommutative-Rational-Formulas-September-24-2026/Polynomial-Hitting-Lists-for-Noncommutative-Rational-Formulas-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Polynomial-Hitting-Lists-for-Noncommutative-Rational-Formulas-September-24-2026/Polynomial-Hitting-Lists-for-Noncommutative-Rational-Formulas-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Polynomial-Hitting-Lists-for-Noncommutative-Rational-Formulas-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Polynomial-Hitting-Lists-for-Noncommutative-Rational-Formulas-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Hitting-Lists-for-Noncommutative-Rational-Formulas-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Hitting-Lists-for-Noncommutative-Rational-Formulas-September-24-2026/build/sections/expansion.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Hitting-Lists-for-Noncommutative-Rational-Formulas-September-24-2026/build/figures/taylor-reversal.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Hitting-Lists-for-Noncommutative-Rational-Formulas-September-24-2026/build/sections/division.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Hitting-Lists-for-Noncommutative-Rational-Formulas-September-24-2026/build/sections/encoding.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Hitting-Lists-for-Noncommutative-Rational-Formulas-September-24-2026/build/sections/generator.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Hitting-Lists-for-Noncommutative-Rational-Formulas-September-24-2026/build/sections/rank.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Polynomial-Hitting-Lists-for-Noncommutative-Rational-Formulas-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct, in deterministic polynomial bit time, a polynomial-size list of rational matrix tuples for noncommutative rational formulas over $\\Q$ of bounded tree size. Every nonzero admissible formula has a defined, invertible value at one tuple, with no separate bounds on inverse nesting or rational constant heights. Matrix dimensions, entry bit lengths, and total output length are polynomially bounded.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":455},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context","sourceOffset":3059},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and its logic","sourceOffset":7435},{"kind":"section","title":"Rectangular tests from subspace expansion","sourceOffset":11671},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Vanishing orders and integral reduction","sourceOffset":15152},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Expansion of every small subspace","sourceOffset":19769},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From expansion to the rank bound","sourceOffset":25341},{"kind":"section","title":"Deformation inside a division algebra","sourceOffset":28575},{"kind":"section","title":"Encoding a formula and its evaluation domain","sourceOffset":40916},{"kind":"section","title":"A rational hitting list and its bit complexity","sourceOffset":51310},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The rational representation and the generator","sourceOffset":52487},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The hitting property","sourceOffset":56118}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are a deterministic Turing machine $G$ and fixed positive integers $C,k$ with the following property. Given $1^n,1^s$, where $n,s\\geq1$, the machine outputs a finite list $\\mathcal H_{n,s}$ of $n$-tuples of rational square matrices, in at most $C(n+s+1)^k$ bit operations. The entire output, with signed binary numerators and positive binary denominators, has at most $C(n+s+1)^k$ bits. For every nonzero admissible rational formula $\\Phi$ in $n$ variables of size at most $s$, some tuple in $\\mathcal H_{n,s}$ is a defined evaluation of $\\Phi$ with invertible value. The same list works for all such formulas, regardless of the bit lengths of their rational constants or their inverse depth. The generator receives no formula, oracle, randomness, or advice.","sourceOffset":2083,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Rectangular rank bound","label":"prop:rectangular","text":"Let $1\\leq q\\leq B$ and $A_0,\\ldots,A_n\\in\\Mat_q(\\Q)$. Suppose that, for some extension field $E/\\Q$, some integer $e\\geq1$, and some $X_1,\\ldots,X_n\\in\\Mat_e(E)$, the matrix \\[ A_0\\otimes I_e+\\sum_{i=1}^n A_i\\otimes X_i \\] is invertible. Then $\\sum_{i=0}^n A_i\\otimes T_i$ is injective over $F$, and \\begin{equation} \\rank_{\\Q(t)}\\left(\\sum_{i=0}^n A_i\\otimes R_i(t)\\right) \\geq qN>(q-1)M. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":13617,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/116.md","scopeText":"The formalization gives one explicit tuple of rational matrices that simultaneously detects every nonzero division-free noncommutative formula with at most $s$ gates in $n$ variables, for $n,s\\ge1$. Evaluation at that tuple is a nonzero matrix over every characteristic-zero field.\n\nThe selected theorem states this universal hitting property. The paper's deterministic polynomial bit-construction bound and matrix-dimension bound $O(ns^2)$ are not separately asserted in it.\n\nThe formalized result constructs polynomial-size hitting lists for noncommutative rational formulas with rational constants, addition, multiplication, and inverse gates. Given the number of variables and a formula-size bound, one deterministic polynomial-time machine outputs rational matrix tuples of a common positive dim","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/116.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":76933,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Polynomial-Hitting-Lists-for-Noncommutative-Rational-Formulas-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"117-01","slug":"Constant-factor-hardness-of-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026","familyId":"117","title":"Constant-factor hardness of uniform sparsest cut","abstract":"We prove that, for every fixed C > 1, approximating Uniform Sparsest Cut within factor C is NP-hard. The output graphs have nonnegative rational capacities and unit demand between every pair of distinct vertices.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that approximating Uniform Sparsest Cut within any fixed constant factor is NP-hard, even with nonnegative rational capacities and unit demands. The Goemans–Linial semidefinite relaxation also has integrality gaps of order at least $\\sqrt{\\log n}/(\\log\\log n)^3$, approaching the square-root-logarithmic upper bound.","pdfPath":"preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/Constant-factor-hardness-of-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/Constant-factor-hardness-of-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/01-pencil.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/02-bit-tests.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/03-events.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/04-entropy.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/05-scores.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/06-paths.tex","preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/07-finite-graph.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Constant-factor-hardness-of-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that, for every fixed $C>1$, approximating Uniform Sparsest Cut within factor $C$ is NP-hard. The output graphs have nonnegative rational capacities and unit demand between every pair of distinct vertices.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":277},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and significance","sourceOffset":1985},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof structure and main constructions","sourceOffset":6727},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and parameter dependence","sourceOffset":11140},{"kind":"section","title":"An explicit pencil of directions","sourceOffset":12325},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two local extensions","sourceOffset":15313},{"kind":"subsection","title":"All branches of the tower","sourceOffset":19462},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Integral lattices and their determinants","sourceOffset":22959},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A large space of global functions","sourceOffset":28775},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Evaluation and the quadratic zero bound","sourceOffset":31382},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite algorithm and its precision bounds","sourceOffset":34111},{"kind":"section","title":"A bit-test system with mixing parity queries","sourceOffset":42038}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed real constant $C>1$, there is a polynomial-time reduction from $3$CNF satisfiability to finite undirected graphs with nonnegative rational capacities and exactly unit demands, together with a positive rational threshold $a$, such that \\[ \\begin{array}{ll} \\text{the formula is satisfiable}&\\Longrightarrow\\ \\Phi(G)\\le a,\\\\[2pt] \\text{the formula is unsatisfiable}&\\Longrightarrow\\ \\Phi(G)>Ca. \\end{array} \\] The graph size and the bit lengths of all output numbers are polynomial in the formula size, with constants and exponents allowed to depend on $C$. In particular, approximating Uniform Sparsest Cut within any fixed factor $C>1$ is NP-hard.","sourceOffset":971,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:bit-test-system","text":"For every fixed $\\lambda>0$ there are constants $\\eta>0$, $B<\\infty$, and a fixed finite list of bounded-arity test laws with the following properties. From a Boolean $3$CNF formula one can construct, in polynomial time, a finite uniform space $\\cS$ of bit positions and these test laws on $\\cS$. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item Every individual question marginal is at most $B$ times uniform measure on $\\cS$. All samplers are polynomially enumerable finite weighted lists with rational probabilities; every positive seed probability is at least inverse-polynomial in the input size. \\item If the formula is satisfiable, there is an assignment $y:\\cS\\to\\F_2$ accepted by every test in every support. \\item If the formula is unsatisfiable, every assignment fails one of the test laws with probability greater than $\\eta$. \\item There is also an exchangeable law of $(s_1,s_2,s_3,s_4)\\in\\cS^4$, with uniform marginals, such that the conditional expectation operator of any coordinate given any other has norm at most $\\lambda$ on mean-zero $L^2(\\cS)$. The assignment in \\textup{(ii)} satisfies \\[ y_{s_1}+y_{s_2}+y_{s_3}+y_{s_4}=0 \\qquad\\text{in }\\F_2 \\] throughout the support of this law. \\end{enumerate} All constants, including the degrees of the polynomial size and time bounds, depend only on $\\lambda$. Two independent copies of the law in \\textup{(iv)}, paired coordinatewise, have the same pair-operator bound on $\\cS^2$ and give the corresponding parity identity for the ex","sourceOffset":42772,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"eq:primitive-calibration-order","text":"A Boolean valuation $H$ on the actual predicates is $\\zeta$-calibrated and ordered if, for every primitive type and every specified comparison, \\begin{equation} \\abs{\\E_{R\\sim P_s}H(R)-p_s}\\leq\\zeta, \\qquad \\Prob[H(R)>H(R')]\\leq\\zeta. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":75794,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/117.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":183161,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Constant-factor-hardness-of-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"117-02","slug":"Near-square-root-logarithmic-integrality-gaps-for-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026","familyId":"117","title":"Near-square-root logarithmic integrality gaps for uniform sparsest cut","abstract":"We construct uniform sparsest-cut instances whose Goemans–Linial semidefinite integrality gap is at least $c\\sqrt{\\log n}/(\\log\\log n)^3$ along a sequence $n\\to\\infty$. The demand is one between every pair of distinct vertices, and the capacities are nonnegative real numbers. This matches the Arora–Rao–Vazirani upper bound up to a power of $\\log\\log n$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that approximating Uniform Sparsest Cut within any fixed constant factor is NP-hard, even with nonnegative rational capacities and unit demands. The Goemans–Linial semidefinite relaxation also has integrality gaps of order at least $\\sqrt{\\log n}/(\\log\\log n)^3$, approaching the square-root-logarithmic upper bound.","pdfPath":"preprints/Near-square-root-logarithmic-integrality-gaps-for-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/Near-square-root-logarithmic-integrality-gaps-for-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Near-square-root-logarithmic-integrality-gaps-for-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/Near-square-root-logarithmic-integrality-gaps-for-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Near-square-root-logarithmic-integrality-gaps-for-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Near-square-root-logarithmic-integrality-gaps-for-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Near-square-root-logarithmic-integrality-gaps-for-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/build/source/figures/construction.tex","preprints/Near-square-root-logarithmic-integrality-gaps-for-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/build/source/directions.tex","preprints/Near-square-root-logarithmic-integrality-gaps-for-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/build/source/kernel.tex","preprints/Near-square-root-logarithmic-integrality-gaps-for-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/build/source/repair.tex","preprints/Near-square-root-logarithmic-integrality-gaps-for-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/build/source/metric.tex","preprints/Near-square-root-logarithmic-integrality-gaps-for-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/build/source/contraction.tex","preprints/Near-square-root-logarithmic-integrality-gaps-for-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/build/source/reduction.tex","preprints/Near-square-root-logarithmic-integrality-gaps-for-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/build/source/directions-proof.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Near-square-root-logarithmic-integrality-gaps-for-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct uniform sparsest-cut instances whose Goemans--Linial semidefinite integrality gap is at least $c\\sqrt{\\log n}/(\\log\\log n)^3$ along a sequence $n\\to\\infty$. The demand is one between every pair of distinct vertices, and the capacities are nonnegative real numbers. This matches the Arora--Rao--Vazirani upper bound up to a power of $\\log\\log n$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":404},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the uniform-demand obstacle","sourceOffset":2393},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":5062},{"kind":"section","title":"Families of directions","sourceOffset":14456},{"kind":"section","title":"A Hilbert kernel with chart extensions","sourceOffset":17374},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The common kernel","sourceOffset":18420},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Extensions with small coordinate profiles","sourceOffset":20689},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rounded charts and their angles","sourceOffset":25686},{"kind":"section","title":"The finite negative-type semimetric","sourceOffset":30101},{"kind":"section","title":"Contractions into \\texorpdfstring{$\\ell_1$}{ell1}","sourceOffset":37448},{"kind":"section","title":"Exactly uniform demands","sourceOffset":48978},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Replacing the parameter distribution by multiplicities","sourceOffset":53149}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are an absolute constant $c>0$ and capacity instances $C^{(j)}$ on $n_j\\to\\infty$ vertices, with $\\GL(C^{(j)})>0$, such that \\[ \\frac{\\OPT(C^{(j)})}{\\GL(C^{(j)})} \\ge c\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{\\log n_j}}{(\\log\\log n_j)^3} \\] for all sufficiently large $j$. The demand is one between every pair of distinct vertices.","sourceOffset":2035,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:metric","text":"For every sufficiently large integer $m$, the vertex set $V$ in \\eqref{ker:vertices} admits a negative-type semimetric $d$ with the following properties. Its chart maps $v_s(\\theta)$, defined almost everywhere on $U=(-2,2)^m$, satisfy \\begin{equation} \\left|d(v_s(\\theta),v_t(\\eta)) -c_*\\sqrt m\\,|\\theta-\\eta|\\right|\\le Cr \\qquad(\\theta,\\eta\\in U) \\end{equation} whenever the labels are defined, where $r=m^{-8}$. For labels in the same chart, \\begin{equation} \\|x-x'\\|_1=\\tau \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad d((s,x),(s,x'))\\le C\\tau\\frac mN(\\log m)^2. \\end{equation} Moreover, \\begin{equation} |V|\\le\\exp(Cm\\log m),\\qquad \\operatorname{diam}(V,d)\\le Cm, \\end{equation} and, for independent uniform $\\theta,\\eta\\in T=(-1,1)^m$, \\begin{equation} \\E d(v_1(\\theta),v_1(\\eta))\\ge cm. \\end{equation} Here $N=m^6$, $\\tau=m^{-2000}$, and all constants are absolute.","sourceOffset":33932,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/117.md","scopeText":"The formalized result gives integrality gaps for the Goemans–Linial relaxation of uniform sparsest cut. Along a sequence of instance sizes $n\\to\\infty$, the ratio of the integral optimum to the positive relaxation optimum is at least $c\\sqrt{\\log n}/(\\log\\log n)^3$ for one absolute $c>0$. Every unordered pair has unit demand, and the relaxation uses squared Euclidean distances satisfying the triangle inequalities. The result is an existential sequence, not a bound for every size or a computational hardness theorem.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/117.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":63527,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Near-square-root-logarithmic-integrality-gaps-for-uniform-sparsest-cut-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"118-01","slug":"Additive-hardness-and-unbounded-configuration-gaps-in-bin-packing-September-24-2026","familyId":"118","title":"Additive hardness and unbounded configuration gaps in bin packing","abstract":"We disprove the Modified Integer Round-Up Conjecture for bin packing by constructing instances with arbitrarily large additive gaps between the configuration-LP value and the integral optimum. We also prove that, for every fixed nonnegative integer c, distinguishing instances that fit in B bins from those requiring more than $B+c$ bins is NP-hard. Both results hold with rational item sizes greater than 1/6, so each bin contains at most five items.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves the modified integer round-up conjecture of Scheithauer and Terno: the integral bin-packing optimum can exceed its configuration linear-programming value by an arbitrarily large additive constant. Approximating the optimum within any fixed additive constant is also NP-hard, even when every item exceeds 1/6 and each bin holds at most five items.","pdfPath":"preprints/Additive-hardness-and-unbounded-configuration-gaps-in-bin-packing-September-24-2026/Additive-hardness-and-unbounded-configuration-gaps-in-bin-packing-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Additive-hardness-and-unbounded-configuration-gaps-in-bin-packing-September-24-2026/Additive-hardness-and-unbounded-configuration-gaps-in-bin-packing-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Additive-hardness-and-unbounded-configuration-gaps-in-bin-packing-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Additive-hardness-and-unbounded-configuration-gaps-in-bin-packing-September-24-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Additive-hardness-and-unbounded-configuration-gaps-in-bin-packing-September-24-2026/build/source/preamble.tex","preprints/Additive-hardness-and-unbounded-configuration-gaps-in-bin-packing-September-24-2026/build/source/introduction.tex","preprints/Additive-hardness-and-unbounded-configuration-gaps-in-bin-packing-September-24-2026/build/source/figures/interval-mechanism.tex","preprints/Additive-hardness-and-unbounded-configuration-gaps-in-bin-packing-September-24-2026/build/source/trees.tex","preprints/Additive-hardness-and-unbounded-configuration-gaps-in-bin-packing-September-24-2026/build/source/instance.tex","preprints/Additive-hardness-and-unbounded-configuration-gaps-in-bin-packing-September-24-2026/build/source/completeness.tex","preprints/Additive-hardness-and-unbounded-configuration-gaps-in-bin-packing-September-24-2026/build/source/soundness.tex","preprints/Additive-hardness-and-unbounded-configuration-gaps-in-bin-packing-September-24-2026/build/source/consequences.tex","preprints/Additive-hardness-and-unbounded-configuration-gaps-in-bin-packing-September-24-2026/build/source/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Additive-hardness-and-unbounded-configuration-gaps-in-bin-packing-September-24-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove the Modified Integer Round-Up Conjecture for bin packing by constructing instances with arbitrarily large additive gaps between the configuration-LP value and the integral optimum. We also prove that, for every fixed nonnegative integer $c$, distinguishing instances that fit in $B$ bins from those requiring more than $B+c$ bins is NP-hard. Both results hold with rational item sizes greater than $1/6$, so each bin contains at most five items.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":503},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Configuration relaxations and integer rounding","sourceOffset":1789},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Results","sourceOffset":4429},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Approximation bounds and related constructions","sourceOffset":6282},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The graph-to-packing construction","sourceOffset":9255},{"kind":"section","title":"Two competing trees","sourceOffset":19591},{"kind":"section","title":"The reduction instance","sourceOffset":28736},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Parameters and interval geometry","sourceOffset":29894},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Patterns and the complete item inventory","sourceOffset":35433},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Encoding the patterns and labels in rational sizes","sourceOffset":40975},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Feasibility of good tuples","sourceOffset":49756},{"kind":"section","title":"Packing from a vertex cover","sourceOffset":53791}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Unbounded configuration gap","label":"thm:unbounded-gap","text":"For every integer $c\\ge0$, there is a finite rational bin-packing instance $I$ and an integer $B$ such that every item size exceeds $1/6$ and \\begin{equation} \\LP(I)=B, \\qquad \\OPT(I)>B+c. \\end{equation} The equality for the fractional optimum also holds for the configuration LP whose patterns are subsets of the individual item copies.","sourceOffset":4540,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Constant-additive hardness","label":"thm:additive-hardness","text":"For every fixed integer $c\\ge0$, it is NP-hard to distinguish pairs $(I,B)$ such that $I$ packs into $B$ bins from pairs such that $I$ cannot be packed into $B+c$ bins. Here $B$ is an integer supplied with the instance, the item sizes are rational, and every item size is strictly greater than $1/6$.","sourceOffset":5348,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Configuration relaxations","label":"def:configuration-lp","text":"For the ordinary type formulation, let $\\mathcal S$ be the set of distinct numerical sizes and let $n_\\sigma$ be the multiplicity of $\\sigma\\in\\mathcal S$. Its configurations are all integer vectors \\[ \\mathcal C=\\left\\{(c_\\sigma)_{\\sigma\\in\\mathcal S} \\in\\Z_{\\ge0}^{\\mathcal S}: \\sum_{\\sigma\\in\\mathcal S}\\sigma c_\\sigma\\le1\\right\\}. \\] The Gilmore--Gomory relaxation is \\begin{equation} \\LP= \\min\\left\\{\\sum_{\\mathbf c\\in\\mathcal C}z_{\\mathbf c}: z_{\\mathbf c}\\ge0,\\quad \\sum_{\\mathbf c\\in\\mathcal C}c_\\sigma z_{\\mathbf c} \\ge n_\\sigma\\quad(\\sigma\\in\\mathcal S)\\right\\}. \\end{equation} In particula","sourceOffset":1850,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/118.md","scopeText":"The formalized results rule out a universal additive bound for the configuration linear program in bin packing. For every integer $c\\ge0$, there are an integer $B$ and a rational instance with $5B$ items whose individual-copy and size-type configuration-LP values both equal $B$, but whose integral optimum is greater than $B+c$. Distinguishing a packing in $B$ bins from the absence of one in $B+c$ bins is NP-hard for each fixed $c$. A deterministic polynomial-time algorithm with a fixed absolute additive allowance exists exactly when $P=NP$; under that equality, an optimal algorithm is constructed.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/118.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":93524,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Additive-hardness-and-unbounded-configuration-gaps-in-bin-packing-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Additive-hardness-and-unbounded-configuration-gaps-in-bin-packing-September-24-2026/build/source/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"119-01","slug":"Sharp-binary-information-contraction-on-the-discrete-cube-September-24-2026","familyId":"119","title":"Sharp binary-information contraction on the discrete cube","abstract":"We prove sharp contraction of the information carried by a binary channel under independent symmetric noise on a uniform discrete cube. At fixed initial information, a noisy coordinate retains the most information. The Boolean specialization resolves the Courtade–Kumar conjecture and gives an output-entropy refinement. We also establish a stronger mean-dependent entropy-production bound. The proof combines an explicit three-point optimizer for the local joining problem, two entropy capacities, and a common-output thinning inequality, followed by dimension induction and integration along the noise semigroup.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the Courtade–Kumar conjecture: among Boolean functions of independent uniform bits, a single coordinate retains the most mutual information after independent bit-flip noise. A stronger theorem treats randomized binary summaries at fixed initial information. The Hellinger conjecture is also proved for every Boolean output bias and noise correlation.","pdfPath":"preprints/Sharp-binary-information-contraction-on-the-discrete-cube-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Sharp-binary-information-contraction-on-the-discrete-cube-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Sharp-binary-information-contraction-on-the-discrete-cube-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Sharp-binary-information-contraction-on-the-discrete-cube-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Sharp-binary-information-contraction-on-the-discrete-cube-September-24-2026/build/sections/scalar.tex","preprints/Sharp-binary-information-contraction-on-the-discrete-cube-September-24-2026/build/sections/canonical.tex","preprints/Sharp-binary-information-contraction-on-the-discrete-cube-September-24-2026/build/figures/reflected-core.tex","preprints/Sharp-binary-information-contraction-on-the-discrete-cube-September-24-2026/build/sections/capacity.tex","preprints/Sharp-binary-information-contraction-on-the-discrete-cube-September-24-2026/build/sections/reserve.tex","preprints/Sharp-binary-information-contraction-on-the-discrete-cube-September-24-2026/build/sections/thinning.tex","preprints/Sharp-binary-information-contraction-on-the-discrete-cube-September-24-2026/build/sections/upgrade.tex","preprints/Sharp-binary-information-contraction-on-the-discrete-cube-September-24-2026/build/sections/flow.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Sharp-binary-information-contraction-on-the-discrete-cube-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove sharp contraction of the information carried by a binary channel under independent symmetric noise on a uniform discrete cube. At fixed initial information, a noisy coordinate retains the most information. The Boolean specialization resolves the Courtade--Kumar conjecture and gives an output-entropy refinement. We also establish a stronger mean-dependent entropy-production bound. The proof combines an explicit three-point optimizer for the local joining problem, two entropy capacities, and a common-output thinning inequality, followed by dimension induction and integration along the noise semigroup.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The contraction theorem and its mechanism","sourceOffset":661},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work and related results","sourceOffset":3652},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From information loss to a local joining inequality","sourceOffset":6974},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two profiles and the geometry of the joining cost","sourceOffset":8866},{"kind":"section","title":"The scalar rate and its curvature","sourceOffset":13343},{"kind":"section","title":"A joining cost with one entropy budget","sourceOffset":20154},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Constructing and certifying the priced core","sourceOffset":22240},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two canonical geometries","sourceOffset":28965},{"kind":"section","title":"The deterministic capacity comparison","sourceOffset":32754},{"kind":"section","title":"A reserve that survives adding either common output","sourceOffset":36287},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A logarithmic bound for the corner geometry","sourceOffset":38916},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The common-output contraction","sourceOffset":40303}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Sharp soft-channel contraction","label":"main:soft","text":"For every finite $n\\ge0$, every $u:\\Omega_n\\to[-1,1]$, and $-1\\le\\rho\\le1$, \\begin{equation} I(T_\\rho u) \\le\\psi\\!\\left(|\\rho|\\,\\psi^{-1}(I(u))\\right). \\end{equation} The inverse is the nonnegative increasing branch on $[0,L]$. For $n\\ge1$, every soft coordinate $u(x)=a x_i$, $|a|\\le1$, attains equality.","sourceOffset":2037,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"The Boolean bound with output bias","label":"main:ck","text":"For every finite $n\\ge0$, $\\rho\\in[-1,1]$, and $f:\\Omega_n\\to\\{-1,1\\}$, put $m=\\E f$ and $r_m=\\psi^{-1}(H(m))$. Then \\begin{equation} I(f(X);Y)\\le\\psi(|\\rho|r_m)\\le\\psi(|\\rho|). \\end{equation} For $n\\ge1$, signed coordinate functions attain the bound at $m=0$. If $0<|m|<1$ and $0<|\\rho|\\le1$, the refined upper curve is strictly below the universal curve $\\psi(|\\rho|)$.","sourceOffset":2796,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/119.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves sharp contraction of the information carried by a binary channel under independent symmetric noise on a uniform discrete cube. At each fixed initial information level, a noisy coordinate channel attains the maximum retained information. It also proves the refined Boolean bound that accounts for output bias, with strict improvement for nonconstant biased outputs at nonzero noise correlation, and the selected mean-dependent entropy-production inequality.\n\nFor Boolean functions this includes the Courtade–Kumar inequality $I(f(X);Y)\\le1-h_2(\\varepsilon)$ in bits at crossover probability $0\\le\\varepsilon\\le1/2$, where $h_2$ is binary entropy. A coordinate and its complement attain equality.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/119.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":76299,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Sharp-binary-information-contraction-on-the-discrete-cube-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"119-02","slug":"Hellinger-contraction-with-arbitrary-Boolean-output-bias-September-24-2026","familyId":"119","title":"Hellinger contraction with arbitrary Boolean output bias","abstract":"We prove the Hellinger conjecture for Boolean functions on the uniform discrete cube, with arbitrary output bias. For a Boolean function of mean m and every $\\rho\\in[-1,1]$, the loss $\\sqrt{1-m^2}-\\mathbb E\\sqrt{1-(T_\\rho f)^2}$ is at most $1-\\sqrt{1-\\rho^2}$, with equality for signed coordinates. The proof combines asymmetric dimension induction, a calibrated noise-semigroup energy estimate, and finite exact arithmetic certificates. The Hellinger inequality also yields the Courtade–Kumar information bound.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the Courtade–Kumar conjecture: among Boolean functions of independent uniform bits, a single coordinate retains the most mutual information after independent bit-flip noise. A stronger theorem treats randomized binary summaries at fixed initial information. The Hellinger conjecture is also proved for every Boolean output bias and noise correlation.","pdfPath":"preprints/Hellinger-contraction-with-arbitrary-Boolean-output-bias-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Hellinger-contraction-with-arbitrary-Boolean-output-bias-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Hellinger-contraction-with-arbitrary-Boolean-output-bias-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Hellinger-contraction-with-arbitrary-Boolean-output-bias-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Hellinger-contraction-with-arbitrary-Boolean-output-bias-September-24-2026/build/supporting/typography-mappings.tex","preprints/Hellinger-contraction-with-arbitrary-Boolean-output-bias-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-regions.tex","preprints/Hellinger-contraction-with-arbitrary-Boolean-output-bias-September-24-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Hellinger-contraction-with-arbitrary-Boolean-output-bias-September-24-2026/build/sections/induction.tex","preprints/Hellinger-contraction-with-arbitrary-Boolean-output-bias-September-24-2026/build/sections/continuation.tex","preprints/Hellinger-contraction-with-arbitrary-Boolean-output-bias-September-24-2026/build/sections/central.tex","preprints/Hellinger-contraction-with-arbitrary-Boolean-output-bias-September-24-2026/build/sections/intermediate.tex","preprints/Hellinger-contraction-with-arbitrary-Boolean-output-bias-September-24-2026/build/sections/large-bias.tex","preprints/Hellinger-contraction-with-arbitrary-Boolean-output-bias-September-24-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex","preprints/Hellinger-contraction-with-arbitrary-Boolean-output-bias-September-24-2026/build/sections/arithmetic.tex","preprints/Hellinger-contraction-with-arbitrary-Boolean-output-bias-September-24-2026/build/sections/scalar.tex","preprints/Hellinger-contraction-with-arbitrary-Boolean-output-bias-September-24-2026/build/sections/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Hellinger-contraction-with-arbitrary-Boolean-output-bias-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Hellinger conjecture for Boolean functions on the uniform discrete cube, with arbitrary output bias. For a Boolean function of mean $m$ and every $\\rho\\in[-1,1]$, the loss $\\sqrt{1-m^2}-\\E\\sqrt{1-(T_\\rho f)^2}$ is at most $1-\\sqrt{1-\\rho^2}$, with equality for signed coordinates. The proof combines asymmetric dimension induction, a calibrated noise-semigroup energy estimate, and finite exact arithmetic certificates. The Hellinger inequality also yields the Courtade--Kumar information bound.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":554},{"kind":"section","title":"Cube conventions and elementary reductions","sourceOffset":10299},{"kind":"section","title":"Dimension induction","sourceOffset":12181},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compression and a small coordinate","sourceOffset":13042},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The scalar interface","sourceOffset":16634},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Averaging and dimension induction","sourceOffset":21104},{"kind":"section","title":"A continuation criterion","sourceOffset":25780},{"kind":"section","title":"The central band","sourceOffset":30916},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The calibrated edge cost","sourceOffset":32473},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reduction to variance and first-level estimates","sourceOffset":38243},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A variance gain around the calibration","sourceOffset":39873},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Concentration of the first Fourier level","sourceOffset":49302}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Hellinger conjecture","label":"thm:main","text":"For every \\(n\\ge1\\), every Boolean function \\(f:\\{-1,1\\}^n\\to\\{-1,1\\}\\), and every \\(\\rho\\in[-1,1]\\), writing \\(m=\\E f\\), one has \\begin{equation} H(m)-\\E H(T_\\rho f)\\le1-H(\\rho). \\end{equation} Both expectations are with respect to the uniform probability measure.","sourceOffset":1486,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:induction","text":"Let $f:\\{-1,1\\}^n\\to\\{-1,1\\}$, let $m=\\E f$, and let $0<\\rho<1$. If $s=\\sqrt{1-\\rho^2}\\le .84$, then \\[ \\E H(T_\\rho f)\\ge H(m)-1+s. \\]","sourceOffset":12383,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/119.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":144310,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Hellinger-contraction-with-arbitrary-Boolean-output-bias-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"120-01","slug":"Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026","familyId":"120","title":"Almost-Linear-Time Maximum-Cardinality Matching in General Graphs","abstract":"We prove that maximum-cardinality matching in a simple undirected graph with n vertices and m edges can be found by one uniform randomized algorithm in $(n+m)^{1+o(1)}$ time. The bound holds on every computation path in a logarithmic-word model, and the algorithm returns an explicit maximum matching with probability at least 2/3. An explicit reduction gives the same time and probability guarantees for deciding whether a simple host graph has a spanning subgraph with prescribed valid vertex degrees, and for finding one when it exists.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives a randomized algorithm finding an exact maximum-cardinality matching in any simple undirected graph in $(n+m)^{1+o(1)}$ word time, with success probability at least 2/3. The time bound holds on every computation path. The same guarantees apply to finding a spanning subgraph with prescribed admissible vertex degrees, or deciding that none exists.","pdfPath":"preprints/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-matching-state.tex","preprints/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-projection-analysis.tex","preprints/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026/build/figures/local-projection.tex","preprints/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-engine.tex","preprints/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-routing-helper.tex","preprints/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-backend-quality.tex","preprints/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026/build/figures/frozen-compression.tex","preprints/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-backend-updates.tex","preprints/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026/build/figures/arm-slots.tex","preprints/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-uniformization.tex","preprints/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-f-factors.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that maximum-cardinality matching in a simple undirected graph with $n$ vertices and $m$ edges can be found by one uniform randomized algorithm in $(n+m)^{1+o(1)}$ time. The bound holds on every computation path in a logarithmic-word model, and the algorithm returns an explicit maximum matching with probability at least $2/3$. An explicit reduction gives the same time and probability guarantees for deciding whether a simple host graph has a spanning subgraph with prescribed valid vertex degrees, and for finding one when it exists.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":619},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Statement and computational model","sourceOffset":1118},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and significance","sourceOffset":4377},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Main ideas and organization","sourceOffset":10929},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and static primitives","sourceOffset":17540},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Representations and fixed-parameter bounds","sourceOffset":17610},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact integral flow","sourceOffset":20135},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A clocked fallback","sourceOffset":25087},{"kind":"section","title":"Matching states and structural operations","sourceOffset":26719},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The phase state and its ledgers","sourceOffset":27722},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The top objective and the pricing primitive","sourceOffset":33305},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The main actions and exact ledger edits","sourceOffset":36360}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Exact matching in almost-linear input-size time","label":"thm:main","text":"There are a single uniform randomized word-RAM algorithm, fixed constants $c_0\\ge2$ and $C\\ge1$, and a nonincreasing function $\\eta:\\mathbb N_{\\ge1}\\to\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}$ with $\\eta(q)\\to0$ as $q\\to\\infty$ such that the following holds. For every input graph $G=([n],E)$ with $m=|E|$ and $p=n+m$, the algorithm halts on every computation path in at most \\[ C p^{1+\\eta(p)} \\] instructions. With probability at least $2/3$ it outputs an explicit list of edges of a matching of size $\\nu(G)$.","sourceOffset":2375,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Fixed-parameter subcubic perfect-matching search","label":"thm:subcubic","text":"For the integers \\(a\\ge1000\\) and fixed reliability parameters \\(0\\le\\sigma\\le a+1\\), the construction defines a uniform randomized algorithm on loopless subcubic graphs with identified edge copies and \\(N\\le S\\) vertices. Every computation halts with a checked perfect matching, a negative answer, or a suspension. It has no false positive. For each fixed input with a perfect matching, the probability of a negative answer is at most \\begin{equation} 2^{-\\sigma}S^{-5}. \\end{equation} This statement bounds erroneous answers, not suspensions, and holds conditionally for adaptive invocations with fresh randomness and valid prerequisites. For every sufficiently large fixed \\(a\\) and fixed admissible \\(\\sigma\\), there is no suspension on any path at all sufficiently large \\(S\\). The total time on every path is then \\begin{equation} S^{1+O(1/a)+o_a(1)}, \\end{equation} where the exponent coefficients in \\(O(1/a)\\) are absolute. All initialization, physical data movement, arithmetic, random bits, and explicit witness writing are included.","sourceOffset":110523,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Ledger invariant","label":"match:invariant","text":"There are ledgers \\(j=0,\\ldots,a\\), with \\(c_j=C-j\\). Ledger \\(j\\) has nonnegative incidence allocations \\(x^j_e\\), with \\[ \\sum_{e\\text{ at row }r}x^j_e=L\\qquad(r\\in\\mathcal A). \\] Its column residual and nonnegative debt are \\[ y^j_d=L-\\operatorname{load}^j_d,\\qquad \\operatorname{load}^j_d=\\sum_{e\\text{ at bin }d}x^j_e,\\qquad p^j_d\\ge0, \\] and they satisfy \\begin{equation} y^j_d+p^j_d\\ge c_jw_d. \\end{equation} At the top level, \\begin{equation} x_e=x^0_e>0,\\qquad s_d=y^0_d+p^0_d-Cw_d>0 \\end{equation} are \\(\\varepsilon\\)-grid coordinates. The other ledgers are integral. At each settled main-l","sourceOffset":31283,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":313244,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Almost-Linear-Time-Maximum-Cardinality-Matching-in-Sparse-General-Graphs-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"121-01","slug":"An-Almost-Linear-Approximation-Scheme-for-Edit-Distance-September-24-2026","familyId":"121","title":"An Almost-Linear Approximation Scheme for Edit Distance","abstract":"We give a uniform randomized approximation scheme for unit-cost edit distance. For every fixed rational $\\varepsilon\\in(0,1)$, it estimates the distance between arbitrary explicitly stored strings of total length N within a factor $1+\\varepsilon$ with probability at least 2/3, in worst-case expected time $N^{1+o(1)}$ on a logarithmic-word RAM. The algorithm supports polynomially bounded integer alphabets and returns zero deterministically on equal strings.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every fixed rational $\\varepsilon\\in(0,1)$, gives a randomized $(1+\\varepsilon)$ approximation to unit-cost edit distance in worst-case expected time $N^{1+o(1)}$, with success probability at least 2/3. The strings have total length N and polynomially bounded integer symbols. This is an asymptotic guarantee at fixed accuracy.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-Almost-Linear-Approximation-Scheme-for-Edit-Distance-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-Almost-Linear-Approximation-Scheme-for-Edit-Distance-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-Almost-Linear-Approximation-Scheme-for-Edit-Distance-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/An-Almost-Linear-Approximation-Scheme-for-Edit-Distance-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/An-Almost-Linear-Approximation-Scheme-for-Edit-Distance-September-24-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/An-Almost-Linear-Approximation-Scheme-for-Edit-Distance-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/An-Almost-Linear-Approximation-Scheme-for-Edit-Distance-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-geometry.tex","preprints/An-Almost-Linear-Approximation-Scheme-for-Edit-Distance-September-24-2026/build/figures/connection-cost.tex","preprints/An-Almost-Linear-Approximation-Scheme-for-Edit-Distance-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-seed.tex","preprints/An-Almost-Linear-Approximation-Scheme-for-Edit-Distance-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-cones.tex","preprints/An-Almost-Linear-Approximation-Scheme-for-Edit-Distance-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-bands.tex","preprints/An-Almost-Linear-Approximation-Scheme-for-Edit-Distance-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-comparison.tex","preprints/An-Almost-Linear-Approximation-Scheme-for-Edit-Distance-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-regret.tex","preprints/An-Almost-Linear-Approximation-Scheme-for-Edit-Distance-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-accuracy.tex","preprints/An-Almost-Linear-Approximation-Scheme-for-Edit-Distance-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-numerics.tex","preprints/An-Almost-Linear-Approximation-Scheme-for-Edit-Distance-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-work.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-Almost-Linear-Approximation-Scheme-for-Edit-Distance-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a uniform randomized approximation scheme for unit-cost edit distance. For every fixed rational $\\varepsilon\\in(0,1)$, it estimates the distance between arbitrary explicitly stored strings of total length $N$ within a factor $1+\\varepsilon$ with probability at least $2/3$, in worst-case expected time $N^{1+o(1)}$ on a logarithmic-word RAM. The algorithm supports polynomially bounded integer alphabets and returns zero deterministically on equal strings.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":546},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and approach","sourceOffset":2554},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":7821},{"kind":"section","title":"Alignment geometry and computational parameters","sourceOffset":12334},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Paths, states and connection costs","sourceOffset":12641},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform access to the accuracy input","sourceOffset":18125},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Parameters and the fixed interval tree","sourceOffset":19960},{"kind":"section","title":"Simultaneous coarse estimates","sourceOffset":23972},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A deterministic shift tree","sourceOffset":25186},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pruning and precision sampling","sourceOffset":29220},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Converting the estimate to an integer seed","sourceOffset":35568},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The conditional cost of an adaptive request","sourceOffset":36188}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is one uniform randomized algorithm that, given $x,y$ and a rational $\\varepsilon\\in(0,1)$, returns a nonnegative integer $\\widehat D$ such that \\[ \\Pr\\bigl[\\ED(x,y)\\le \\widehat D\\le(1+\\varepsilon)\\ED(x,y)\\bigr] \\ge \\frac23. \\] For every fixed rational $\\varepsilon$, its worst-case expected running time on strings of total length $N$ is $N^{1+o(1)}$ as $N\\to\\infty$ on the logarithmic-word RAM. The expectation is over internal randomness only, and the bound includes input processing, preprocessing, numerical work, random sampling and all data-structure operations. Equal strings return zero deterministically.","sourceOffset":1235,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Initial estimates","label":"thm:seed","text":"With the parameters and labeled interval tree of Section \\ref{sec:geometry}, there is a lazily evaluated random table $U^{(0)}(I,q)$ of integers in $[0,N]$ such that, with probability at least $0.99$, simultaneously at every state, \\begin{equation} c(I,q)\\le U^{(0)}(I,q)\\le A_{\\mathrm{init}}c(I,q). \\end{equation} Every zero-cost state has value zero on every execution. Conditional on any first request for an entry and the previously exposed randomness, its expected evaluation cost is at most $(1+|I|)2^{o(H)}$, uniformly in the state. This cost bound includes arithmetic and random sampling and does not require \\eqref{eq:seed-guarantee} to hold.","sourceOffset":24440,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/121.md","scopeText":"The formalization gives a randomized $(1+\\varepsilon)$-approximation for unit-cost edit distance between explicitly stored integer strings, for every fixed rational $0<\\varepsilon<1$. Its estimate is at least the true distance and at most $(1+\\varepsilon)$ times it with probability at least $2/3$; equal strings return zero on every execution.\n\nFor total length at most $N$ and integer symbols bounded by a fixed polynomial in $N$, expected work is at most $(N+2)^{1+\\eta}$ for every fixed $\\eta>0$ and all sufficiently large $N$. Space is polynomially bounded on every execution.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/121.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":139076,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-Almost-Linear-Approximation-Scheme-for-Edit-Distance-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"122-01","slug":"Uniform-quasipolynomial-time-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026","familyId":"122","title":"Uniform quasipolynomial-time trace reconstruction","abstract":"We give a uniform algorithm that reconstructs every binary string from independent deletion traces when its length and rational retention probability are known. For each fixed retention probability, both the number of traces and the bit complexity are quasipolynomial in the string length. More generally, we give an explicit sample bound uniform over all rational retention probabilities, with running time polynomial in the sample budget and the binary input length. If the deletion probability is at most $n^{-\\varepsilon}$ for fixed ε > 0, the sample and running-time bounds are polynomial. Reconstruction succeeds with probability at least 2/3 for each input string.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"At every fixed deletion probability in $(0,1)$, reconstructing an arbitrary length-n binary string requires $n^{\\Omega(\\log\\log n)}$ independent traces, ruling out polynomial-sample reconstruction. A uniform decoder achieves quasipolynomial sample and running-time bounds for known fixed rational retention probabilities. When the deletion probability is at most $n^{-\\varepsilon}$ for fixed ε > 0, both bounds become polynomial in the input and parameter encoding.","pdfPath":"preprints/Uniform-quasipolynomial-time-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026/uniform-trace-reconstruction.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-quasipolynomial-time-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026/uniform-trace-reconstruction.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-quasipolynomial-time-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Uniform-quasipolynomial-time-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/main.tex","preprints/Uniform-quasipolynomial-time-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/history.tex","preprints/Uniform-quasipolynomial-time-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/setup.tex","preprints/Uniform-quasipolynomial-time-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/multiscale.tex","preprints/Uniform-quasipolynomial-time-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/contour.tex","preprints/Uniform-quasipolynomial-time-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/separation.tex","preprints/Uniform-quasipolynomial-time-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/decoding.tex","preprints/Uniform-quasipolynomial-time-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026/build/references/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Uniform-quasipolynomial-time-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a uniform algorithm that reconstructs every binary string from independent deletion traces when its length and rational retention probability are known. For each fixed retention probability, both the number of traces and the bit complexity are quasipolynomial in the string length. More generally, we give an explicit sample bound uniform over all rational retention probabilities, with running time polynomial in the sample budget and the binary input length. If the deletion probability is at most $n^{-\\varepsilon}$ for fixed $\\varepsilon>0$, the sample and running-time bounds are polynomial. Reconstruction succeeds with probability at least $2/3$ for each input string.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":729},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The uniform guarantee","sourceOffset":1576},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and relation to earlier work","sourceOffset":3736},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and additional ingredients","sourceOffset":7521},{"kind":"section","title":"Moment relaxations and prefix separation","sourceOffset":11079},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Observable trace moments","sourceOffset":11445},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positive moment arrays","sourceOffset":12375},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exponentially weighted tuple statistics","sourceOffset":16901},{"kind":"section","title":"Moving a signal to a larger scale","sourceOffset":18053},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A Gaussian window and its logarithmic bound","sourceOffset":20796},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Frequencies at prescribed differences","sourceOffset":25220},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A product with small total frequency","sourceOffset":29866}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform trace reconstruction","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist absolute constants $C,c\\ge1$ and one randomized multitape Turing machine $\\mathcal A$ with the following property. For every $n\\ge2$, every rational $p\\in(0,1]$, and every $x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n$, given $n,p$ in binary and access to independent ordinary deletion traces of $x$, the machine requests at most \\[ M_C(n,p)=\\left\\lceil\\exp\\bigl(CB(n,p)\\bigr)\\right\\rceil \\] traces, outputs an element of $\\{0,1\\}^n$, and returns $x$ with probability at least $2/3$. For every internal random-bit sequence and every well-formed trace stream of strings of length at most $n$, it halts within \\[ \\bigl(n+L+M_C(n,p)\\bigr)^c \\] bit operations. This bound includes preprocessing, all parameter and finite-precision calculations, every received trace bit and delimiter, and the output. The only oracle operation is the external generation of the independent deletion traces.","sourceOffset":2136,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Separation from a wrong prefix","label":"thm:separation","text":"There exist absolute constants $C_{\\mathrm{sep}}\\ge1$ and an integer $H\\ge1$ with the following property. For $n\\ge2$ and $0<p<1$, define \\[ D_*=4^{\\lceil\\log_2 Y\\rceil+H}\\le4^{H+1}Y^2, \\] using \\eqref{eq:aux-parameters}. For every $x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n$, every $d\\in[n]$, and every positive moment functional of order $2D$ fixing $x_1,\\ldots,x_{d-1},1-x_d$, where $D\\ge D_*$, some nonempty $I\\subseteq[n]$ with $|I|\\le D_*$ satisfies \\[ |\\EE[T_I]-T_I(x)|> \\exp\\bigl(-C_{\\mathrm{sep}}B(n,p)\\bigr). \\]","sourceOffset":15972,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/122.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":94539,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Uniform-quasipolynomial-time-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"122-02","slug":"A-latest-anchor-induction-with-spectrally-compact-masks-for-worst-case-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026","familyId":"122","title":"A latest-anchor induction with spectrally compact masks for worst-case trace reconstruction","abstract":"We give an improved worst-case sample bound for reconstructing a string from independent deletion traces, with its length and retention probability known. For each fixed retention probability, the number of traces is quasipolynomial: the logarithm of the sample budget is $O((\\log n)^3(1+\\log\\log(2n))^6)$. If the deletion probability is at most $n^{-\\varepsilon}$ for fixed ε > 0, polynomially many traces suffice. These bounds apply to binary strings and to strings of general symbols observed exactly. They concern sample complexity and do not assert an efficient reconstruction algorithm or matching optimality.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"At every fixed deletion probability in $(0,1)$, reconstructing an arbitrary length-n binary string requires $n^{\\Omega(\\log\\log n)}$ independent traces, ruling out polynomial-sample reconstruction. A uniform decoder achieves quasipolynomial sample and running-time bounds for known fixed rational retention probabilities. When the deletion probability is at most $n^{-\\varepsilon}$ for fixed ε > 0, both bounds become polynomial in the input and parameter encoding.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-latest-anchor-induction-with-spectrally-compact-masks-for-worst-case-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-latest-anchor-induction-with-spectrally-compact-masks-for-worst-case-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-latest-anchor-induction-with-spectrally-compact-masks-for-worst-case-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/A-latest-anchor-induction-with-spectrally-compact-masks-for-worst-case-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026/build/main.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-latest-anchor-induction-with-spectrally-compact-masks-for-worst-case-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"","sections":[{"kind":"detected-heading","title":"1 + log n/(1 + log(1/q)), the logarithm of the required sample budget is at most"},{"kind":"detected-heading","title":"16                               h"},{"kind":"detected-heading","title":"2        16   b"},{"kind":"detected-heading","title":"2           2               −10                     −y 2 /2"},{"kind":"detected-heading","title":"0 7→ 1). Here S is a real interval radius parameter and M, J∗ are conservative lower bounds:"},{"kind":"detected-heading","title":"1   1.5   .419   .524"},{"kind":"detected-heading","title":"2   2.1   .357   .453"},{"kind":"detected-heading","title":"3   2.8   .297   .380"},{"kind":"detected-heading","title":"4   3.6   .246   .335"},{"kind":"detected-heading","title":"5   4.4   .208   .292"},{"kind":"detected-heading","title":"1    .4217        .5262       4.96"},{"kind":"detected-heading","title":"2    .3594        .4553       5.79"}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"pdf-detected-statement","name":"Theorem 1. For integer n ≥ 2 and 0 < p ≤ 1, worst-case trace reconstruction of binary strings is","text":"Theorem 1. For integer n ≥ 2 and 0 < p ≤ 1, worst-case trace reconstruction of binary strings is\npossible in this channel with at most\n                                                                     \u0001\u0001\n                               exp O p−1 (log n)3 (1 + log log(2n))6\n\ntraces, with an absolute constant. Write q = 1 − p. More finely, when q > 0, setting X =\n1 + log n/(1 + log(1/q)), the logarithm of the required sample budget is at most\n                                                                   \u0001\n                                    O p−1 (log n)X 2 (1 + log X )6\n\nwith an absolute constant. In particular for fixed ε > 0 and 0 ≤ q ≤ n−ε the number of traces can be\npolynomial in n (with parameters of the polynomial allowed to depend on ε). The same asymptotic\nbounds also suffice for strings with general symbols (assuming exact observation of retained symbols).\nThis concerns sample complexity, not computational efficiency.\n\n    This improves the order of the upper estimate with the parameter prescriptions of the\nquasipolynomial-bound proof of Burudgunte, Valiant and Wang [1] (BVW). For clarity, their\nTheorem 43 statement uses an unspecified log-power c and dependence p−7/3 in the exponent.\nThe proof in Section 8 uses bold = ⌈log1.1 log2 n⌉ as its depth bound, pbase = 3−bold min(p, 1/4), and\n                     −2C bold                                                                 1/3 −1/3\na pair-test bound 4τbase      (some C ≥ 1), with log(1/τbase ) given by a c","caution":"Automatic PDF excerpt; may include following proof text.","truncated":true},{"kind":"pdf-detected-statement","name":"Theorem 43 statement uses an unspecified log-power c and dependence p−7/3 in the exponent.","text":"Theorem 43 statement uses an unspecified log-power c and dependence p−7/3 in the exponent.\nThe proof in Section 8 uses bold = ⌈log1.1 log2 n⌉ as its depth bound, pbase = 3−bold min(p, 1/4), and\n                     −2C bold                                                                 1/3 −1/3\na pair-test bound 4τbase      (some C ≥ 1), with log(1/τbase ) given by a constant times dbase pbase\n                  −6\nfor dbase = max(pbase , O(1)). Thus even ignoring the C bold multiplier, the order from the inverse\n\n\n                                                     1\n\fretention term of that estimate uses a log-power at least (7/3) log1.1 (3) > 26 for fixed p. Our\nimprovement is relative to the proved quantitative estimate with those prescriptions, not a claim\nabout all possible optimizations of BVW’s approach. In particular our bound does not go below\nquasipolynomial order for fixed 0 < p < 1. The method here is inspired by BVW’s idea of\ntesting phase relations via Fourier products; we give a different induction entirely on source-string\ngenerating functions, including a weighted, up-to-four-factor phase argument.\n    We keep the period-avoiding-word idea from Chase’s analysis and its relatives (see [2, 6]), using\nan exact contiguous pattern at the base. A new ingredient in the bound is to propagate additional\nlow-dimensional factors with spectral compactification, enabling partial angle-budget relaxation\nduring the induction before expanding at the last pivot. The polynomi","caution":"Automatic PDF excerpt; may include following proof text.","truncated":true}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/122.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full PDF text extracted"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"pdf-pdftotext","chars":54561,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-latest-anchor-induction-with-spectrally-compact-masks-for-worst-case-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":["Short TeX body; PDF fallback"]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"122-03","slug":"quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026","familyId":"122","title":"Quantitative lower bounds for trace reconstruction","abstract":"Exact worst-case reconstruction of a binary word from independent deletion traces requires $n^{\\Omega(\\log\\log n)}$ samples for every fixed deletion probability $q\\in(0,1)$, even with unrestricted computation and any fixed positive success probability. This gives a negative answer to the polynomial-sample question for binary trace reconstruction. More generally, when $q^3\\log n\\to\\infty$, we prove a lower bound of $n^{c\\log(q^3\\log n)}$ samples for every fixed $0\\lt c\\lt 1/(4\\log2)$. Here q is the known deletion probability, and all logarithms are natural.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"At every fixed deletion probability in $(0,1)$, reconstructing an arbitrary length-n binary string requires $n^{\\Omega(\\log\\log n)}$ independent traces, ruling out polynomial-sample reconstruction. A uniform decoder achieves quasipolynomial sample and running-time bounds for known fixed rational retention probabilities. When the deletion probability is at most $n^{-\\varepsilon}$ for fixed ε > 0, both bounds become polynomial in the input and parameter encoding.","pdfPath":"preprints/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026/build/sections/scales.tex","preprints/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026/build/sections/tools.tex","preprints/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026/build/figures/paired-hiding.tex","preprints/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026/build/sections/initial.tex","preprints/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026/build/sections/block.tex","preprints/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026/build/sections/layout.tex","preprints/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026/build/sections/coefficients.tex","preprints/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026/build/figures/forced-subtree-jitter.tex","preprints/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026/build/sections/transfer.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Exact worst-case reconstruction of a binary word from independent deletion traces requires $n^{\\Omega(\\log\\log n)}$ samples for every fixed deletion probability $q\\in(0,1)$, even with unrestricted computation and any fixed positive success probability. This gives a negative answer to the polynomial-sample question for binary trace reconstruction. More generally, when $q^3\\log n\\to\\infty$, we prove a lower bound of $n^{c\\log(q^3\\log n)}$ samples for every fixed $0<c<1/(4\\log2)$. Here $q$ is the known deletion probability, and all logarithms are natural.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":604},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and comparison with earlier results","sourceOffset":3130},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Mechanism and proof structure","sourceOffset":7387},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Distances and channel reduction","sourceOffset":11475},{"kind":"section","title":"The induction and global scales","sourceOffset":12818},{"kind":"section","title":"Common mass, smoothing, and score moments","sourceOffset":20452},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Paired hiding and the reported information","sourceOffset":25556},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conditioning to obtain a small likelihood score","sourceOffset":30094},{"kind":"section","title":"An initial pair at the working deletion rate","sourceOffset":32987},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Words and the reported mark lists","sourceOffset":33998},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conditional densities and the rank kernel","sourceOffset":38235},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Common mass for consecutive pure runs","sourceOffset":47195}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Quantitative sample lower bound","label":"thm:main","text":"Fix $s\\in(0,1]$ and $0<c<1/(4\\log2)$. Along every sequence of instances $(n,q)$ with $n\\to\\infty$, $q\\in(0,1)$, and $q^3\\log n\\to\\infty$, \\begin{equation} T_{q,s}(n)\\ge n^{c\\log(q^3\\log n)} \\end{equation} eventually. All logarithms are natural. In particular, for each fixed deletion probability $q\\in(0,1)$ and fixed positive success probability $s$, the required sample number is $n^{\\Omega(\\log\\log n)}$.","sourceOffset":1476,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Superpolynomial one-trace indistinguishability","label":"thm:tv","text":"For every fixed deletion probability $q\\in(0,1)$, \\begin{equation} \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}n^A \\min_{\\substack{x,y\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\\\x\\ne y}} \\TV\\bigl(\\Dcal_q(x),\\Dcal_q(y)\\bigr)=0 \\qquad\\text{for every }A>0. \\end{equation} At this same channel $T_{q,s}(n)/n^A\\to\\infty$ for every fixed $A>0$ and $s\\in(0,1]$.","sourceOffset":2471,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"The level invariant","label":"def:invariant","text":"Choose $C>2$ and $0<\\omega<1/8$ depending only on $\\delta$, with \\[ \\log C=O(\\Lambda),\\qquad \\log(\\omega^{-1})=O(\\Lambda), \\] and a constant $M_B\\ge0$ depending only on $B$. The same choices are used at every level. For $0\\le i\\le D$, a level-$i$ pair consists of two different strings $x_i,y_i$ of the same length $\\ell_i\\in[N_i/2,N_i]$, and probability laws $\\mu_i,\\nu_i$ on a common auxiliary space, which may depend on $n$ and $\\delta$, satisfying the following conditions. \\begin{itemize} \\item There are a Markov kernel $\\mathcal K_i$ from the auxiliary space to finite binary words and a numbe","sourceOffset":17983,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/122.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves superpolynomial sample lower bounds for exact worst-case reconstruction of binary words from independent deletion traces, with unrestricted computation and any fixed positive success probability. For every fixed deletion probability $q\\in(0,1)$, sample complexity grows faster than every fixed power of the word length; the minimum total-variation distance between two one-trace laws decays faster than every inverse power.\n\nMore quantitatively, along sequences with $q^3\\log n\\to\\infty$, the required number of samples is at least $n^{c\\log(q^3\\log n)}$ eventually for every fixed $0<c<1/(4\\log2)$. All logarithms are natural.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/122.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":134162,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"124-01","slug":"A-polynomial-time-algorithm-for-three-machine-unit-job-scheduling-September-24-2026","familyId":"124","title":"A Polynomial-Time Algorithm for Three-Machine Unit-Job Scheduling","abstract":"We give a uniform deterministic polynomial-time algorithm for scheduling unit-length jobs with arbitrary precedence constraints on three identical parallel machines. The algorithm constructs a schedule of minimum makespan and decides exactly whether all jobs can finish by a specified deadline. The proof reorganizes feasible schedules into intervals whose job sets have descriptions of bounded size. A dynamic program searches a family containing polynomially many such descriptions. Global boundary conditions and simplification of inherited information keep the descriptions bounded throughout the decomposition.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the three-processor unit-job scheduling problem of Garey and Johnson: a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm minimizes makespan for nonpreemptive unit-length jobs with arbitrary precedence constraints on three identical parallel machines. For an explicitly given precedence graph, it decides deadline feasibility exactly and constructs a feasible schedule.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-polynomial-time-algorithm-for-three-machine-unit-job-scheduling-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-polynomial-time-algorithm-for-three-machine-unit-job-scheduling-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-polynomial-time-algorithm-for-three-machine-unit-job-scheduling-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/A-polynomial-time-algorithm-for-three-machine-unit-job-scheduling-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-algorithm-for-three-machine-unit-job-scheduling-September-24-2026/build/separators.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-algorithm-for-three-machine-unit-job-scheduling-September-24-2026/build/construction.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-algorithm-for-three-machine-unit-job-scheduling-September-24-2026/build/figures/shared-cutoff.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-algorithm-for-three-machine-unit-job-scheduling-September-24-2026/build/lists.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-algorithm-for-three-machine-unit-job-scheduling-September-24-2026/build/figures/capacity-three.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-algorithm-for-three-machine-unit-job-scheduling-September-24-2026/build/figures/switch-localization.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-algorithm-for-three-machine-unit-job-scheduling-September-24-2026/build/boundary.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-algorithm-for-three-machine-unit-job-scheduling-September-24-2026/build/descriptions.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-algorithm-for-three-machine-unit-job-scheduling-September-24-2026/build/figures/global-gap-filters.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-algorithm-for-three-machine-unit-job-scheduling-September-24-2026/build/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-polynomial-time-algorithm-for-three-machine-unit-job-scheduling-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a uniform deterministic polynomial-time algorithm for scheduling unit-length jobs with arbitrary precedence constraints on three identical parallel machines. The algorithm constructs a schedule of minimum makespan and decides exactly whether all jobs can finish by a specified deadline. The proof reorganizes feasible schedules into intervals whose job sets have descriptions of bounded size. A dynamic program searches a family containing polynomially many such descriptions. Global boundary conditions and simplification of inherited information keep the descriptions bounded throughout the decomposition.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":661},{"kind":"section","title":"Full schedules, frames, and descriptions","sourceOffset":9422},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Padding and interval reorderings","sourceOffset":9827},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two frames","sourceOffset":11407},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A fixed finite family","sourceOffset":12670},{"kind":"section","title":"The algorithm and its structural certificate","sourceOffset":14072},{"kind":"section","title":"Separators with a shared cutoff","sourceOffset":20062},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Lexicographic normalization","sourceOffset":22316},{"kind":"section","title":"The interval construction and its invariants","sourceOffset":28155},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Global lists and boundary conditions","sourceOffset":28590},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A center and two open sides","sourceOffset":31635},{"kind":"section","title":"Short global lists and changes of direction","sourceOffset":39068}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let an explicitly listed finite directed acyclic graph specify the precedence constraints on \\(n\\ge1\\) nonpreemptive unit-length jobs on three identical machines. There is a uniform deterministic algorithm that constructs a feasible schedule of minimum makespan. Given also an integer deadline \\(1\\le T\\le n\\), it decides feasibility exactly and returns a schedule whenever the answer is affirmative. Both tasks can be performed in \\(O((L+2)^{150020})\\) steps on a deterministic multitape Turing machine, where \\(L\\) is the total binary input length.","sourceOffset":2707,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:algorithm","text":"This is a uniform deterministic polynomial-time algorithm. Every set it accepts has a full schedule, which the algorithm can explicitly return. With \\(K=10000\\), its deadline decision and schedule construction take \\(O((L+2)^{150020})\\) deterministic multitape steps.","sourceOffset":15269,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:hierarchy","text":"A bounded-description hierarchy for a feasible \\(J\\) has the whole schedule as its root interval. At a nonempty node \\(W\\), one may first replace its schedule by a full schedule of the same job set. One then marks at least one actual slot; the nonempty open gaps between successive marks and the two boundaries become its children. Different children may be developed with separate copies of their witnessing exterior schedules. The following descriptions are required: \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\roman*)] \\item every node job set belongs to \\(\\F\\); \\item for every marked triple, its earlier jobs wit","sourceOffset":17523,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:separator","text":"Given an ideal \\(S\\) of low jobs in a domain, a slot \\(z\\) is a separator at cutoff \\(S\\) if \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\alph*)] \\item every domain job before \\(z\\) that is outside \\(S\\) belongs to \\(P_z\\); \\item every domain job after \\(z\\) that belongs to \\(S\\) belongs to \\(D_z\\). \\end{enumerate} The other domain jobs are called high. A left boundary outside the domain is also a separator when all low domain jobs descend from it; there are then no domain jobs before that boundary.","sourceOffset":20863,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/124.md","scopeText":"The formalization gives a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm for scheduling nonempty collections of unit-length jobs with arbitrary acyclic precedence constraints on three identical parallel machines. It constructs a schedule of minimum makespan and decides exactly whether a valid specified deadline can be met. The algorithm is one fixed finite machine, and its running time is bounded by a polynomial in the binary input length.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/124.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":79025,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-polynomial-time-algorithm-for-three-machine-unit-job-scheduling-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"125-01","slug":"Single-Exponential-Recovery-and-Bounded-Price-Strictness-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026","familyId":"125","title":"Single-exponential recovery and bounded-price strictness for metric k-median","abstract":"We give an exact-budget recovery algorithm for metric k-median with single-exponential dependence on the number of comparison clusters without accurate, distinct proxies in a supplied anchor solution. On positive integral metrics of polynomially bounded diameter, a sufficiently small total proxy error and logarithmically many such clusters yield a $(1+2/e+\\varepsilon)$ approximation in polynomial time with arbitrarily high success probability. We also prove bounded-price strictness for one compatible execution of the logarithmic-surplus construction. Together the recovery and payment arguments give a randomized $(2-\\sigma)$ approximation, for an absolute σ > 0, on arbitrary finite rational metrics, both with high probability and in expectation, while opening at most k facilities on every output.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives a deterministic polynomial-time $(1+2/e+\\varepsilon)$-approximation for finite rational metric k-median with specified candidate facilities, for every fixed ε > 0. Assuming $P\\ne NP$, the optimal infimum approximation factor is $1+2/e$.","pdfPath":"preprints/Single-Exponential-Recovery-and-Bounded-Price-Strictness-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Single-Exponential-Recovery-and-Bounded-Price-Strictness-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Single-Exponential-Recovery-and-Bounded-Price-Strictness-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Single-Exponential-Recovery-and-Bounded-Price-Strictness-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Single-Exponential-Recovery-and-Bounded-Price-Strictness-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Single-Exponential-Recovery-and-Bounded-Price-Strictness-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Single-Exponential-Recovery-and-Bounded-Price-Strictness-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Single-Exponential-Recovery-and-Bounded-Price-Strictness-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/sections/local.tex","preprints/Single-Exponential-Recovery-and-Bounded-Price-Strictness-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/sections/sampling.tex","preprints/Single-Exponential-Recovery-and-Bounded-Price-Strictness-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/sections/completion.tex","preprints/Single-Exponential-Recovery-and-Bounded-Price-Strictness-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/figures/budget.tex","preprints/Single-Exponential-Recovery-and-Bounded-Price-Strictness-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/sections/refinement.tex","preprints/Single-Exponential-Recovery-and-Bounded-Price-Strictness-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/sections/imports.tex","preprints/Single-Exponential-Recovery-and-Bounded-Price-Strictness-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/sections/strictness.tex","preprints/Single-Exponential-Recovery-and-Bounded-Price-Strictness-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/sections/surplus.tex","preprints/Single-Exponential-Recovery-and-Bounded-Price-Strictness-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/sections/assembly.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Single-Exponential-Recovery-and-Bounded-Price-Strictness-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give an exact-budget recovery algorithm for metric $k$-median with single-exponential dependence on the number of comparison clusters without accurate, distinct proxies in a supplied anchor solution. On positive integral metrics of polynomially bounded diameter, a sufficiently small total proxy error and logarithmically many such clusters yield a $(1+2/e+\\varepsilon)$ approximation in polynomial time with arbitrarily high success probability. We also prove bounded-price strictness for one compatible execution of the logarithmic-surplus construction. Together the recovery and payment arguments give a randomized $(2-\\sigma)$ approximation, for an absolute $\\sigma>0$, on arbitrary finite rational metrics, both with high probability and in expectation, while opening at most $k$ facilities on every output.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":889},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Recovery from an anchor","sourceOffset":2113},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A compatible execution and its global application","sourceOffset":5287},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work and the relation to these methods","sourceOffset":8554},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":11675},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and reduction to bounded integral distances","sourceOffset":15709},{"kind":"section","title":"Local search and anchored comparison clusters","sourceOffset":21729},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local optimality and weighted exchanges","sourceOffset":22591},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Anchoring at any fixed accuracy","sourceOffset":29934},{"kind":"section","title":"Joint sampling of leaders and removals","sourceOffset":37859},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Computable radii and reference metrics","sourceOffset":40397},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite radius experiment","sourceOffset":45691}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Global application","label":"main:theorem","text":"There is an absolute constant $\\sigma>0$ with the following property. For every fixed $a>0$, a randomized polynomial-time algorithm for metric $k$-median always returns a set $S\\subseteq F$ with $|S|\\le k$ and satisfies \\[ \\Pr\\!\\left\\{\\operatorname{cost}(S) \\le(2-\\sigma)\\operatorname{opt}_k\\right\\} \\ge 1-(|D|+|F|+2)^{-a}. \\] The same algorithm can be chosen to satisfy $\\mathbb E\\operatorname{cost}(S)\\le(2-\\sigma)\\operatorname{opt}_k$. The running time is polynomial in the bit length of the finite rational metric input. Its degree may depend on the fixed constants.","sourceOffset":7903,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Refined recovery","label":"ref:recovery","text":"Let $D$ and $F$ be disjoint indexed sets in a metric whose distances between distinct indices are positive integers bounded by a fixed polynomial in $N=|D|+|F|$. Fix $\\varepsilon>0$ and $0<L_0<\\infty$, and choose a fixed rational parameter \\[ 0<\\mu\\le \\min\\{1/4,\\varepsilon/12\\}. \\] Let $S\\subseteq F$ have cardinality $h\\ge1$. Suppose there exists a comparison solution $O\\subseteq F$ of cardinality $h$ and cost $P>0$, with a partition into good and bad centers and distinct proxies $\\phi_i\\in S$ for the good centers, such that \\begin{equation} \\sum_{i\\ \\mathrm{good}}\\sum_{p\\in C_i}d(p,\\phi_i) \\le P_g+\\mu P, \\qquad m:=|\\{i:i\\ \\mathrm{bad}\\}|\\le L_0\\log N. \\end{equation} Given $S$, $\\mu$, $L_0$, and a failure parameter $\\zeta\\in(0,1)$, a randomized algorithm returns $\\widehat S\\subseteq F$ with \\[ |\\widehat S|\\le h \\quad\\text{always},\\qquad \\operatorname{cost}(\\widehat S) \\le (1+2/e+\\varepsilon)P \\quad\\text{with probability at least }1-\\zeta. \\] Its running time is polynomial in $N$ and $\\log(1/\\zeta)$ for the fixed parameters. The algorithm is not given $O$, $P$, the partition, or its proxies.","sourceOffset":3226,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/125.md","scopeText":"The formalization gives an exact-budget recovery algorithm for metric $k$-median on the stated polynomially bounded integral metrics. Suppose a supplied anchor represents all but logarithmically many comparison clusters by distinct proxies, with total proxy cost at most the comparison cost plus a sufficiently small relative error. The algorithm always opens at most $k$ facilities and, with any requested confidence, attains a $(1+2/e+\\varepsilon)$ factor relative to those comparison centers in polynomial time.\n\nThe linked final approximation result applies to arbitrary finite rational metrics. For one absolute $\\sigma>0$, a polynomial-time fair-bit algorithm always returns a feasible solution, has expected cost at most $(2-\\sigma)\\mathrm{OPT}$, and attains the same factor with arbitrarily h","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/125.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":169768,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Single-Exponential-Recovery-and-Bounded-Price-Strictness-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"125-02","slug":"The-Approximation-Threshold-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026","familyId":"125","title":"The approximation threshold for metric k-median","abstract":"For every fixed ε > 0, we give a deterministic polynomial-time $(1+2/e+\\varepsilon)$-approximation for finite rational metric k-median with specified candidate facilities, opening at most k facilities. Under $P\\ne NP$, the infimum approximation factor in this model is therefore $1+2/e$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives a deterministic polynomial-time $(1+2/e+\\varepsilon)$-approximation for finite rational metric k-median with specified candidate facilities, for every fixed ε > 0. Assuming $P\\ne NP$, the optimal infimum approximation factor is $1+2/e$.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Approximation-Threshold-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Approximation-Threshold-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Approximation-Threshold-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/The-Approximation-Threshold-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Approximation-Threshold-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-Approximation-Threshold-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/The-Approximation-Threshold-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/sections/preparation.tex","preprints/The-Approximation-Threshold-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/sections/inequality.tex","preprints/The-Approximation-Threshold-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/sections/rounding.tex","preprints/The-Approximation-Threshold-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/figures/survivors.tex","preprints/The-Approximation-Threshold-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/sections/derandomization.tex","preprints/The-Approximation-Threshold-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/sections/certificates.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Approximation-Threshold-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), we give a deterministic polynomial-time \\((1+2/e+\\varepsilon)\\)-approximation for finite rational metric \\(k\\)-median with specified candidate facilities, opening at most \\(k\\) facilities. Under \\(P\\ne NP\\), the infimum approximation factor in this model is therefore \\(1+2/e\\).","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":361},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and method","sourceOffset":3554},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":6837},{"kind":"section","title":"Preliminaries and the reduction","sourceOffset":11148},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The fractional solution","sourceOffset":11611},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Constant-additive pseudo-approximation","sourceOffset":13246},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Probability and bookkeeping","sourceOffset":15047},{"kind":"section","title":"Preparing the openings","sourceOffset":16212},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite inequality for the drift","sourceOffset":40063},{"kind":"section","title":"Tapered iterative rounding","sourceOffset":52558},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The graph and the physical process","sourceOffset":54544},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The opening-count bound","sourceOffset":60194}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), there is a deterministic algorithm which, given a finite rational metric \\(k\\)-median instance with \\(1\\le k\\le |F|\\), returns \\(S\\subseteq F\\), \\(|S|\\le k\\), such that \\[ \\sum_{j\\in J}d(j,S)\\le \\left(1+\\frac2e+\\varepsilon\\right)\\OPT_k. \\] Its running time is polynomial in the binary encoding length of the instance. The polynomial may depend on \\(\\varepsilon\\).","sourceOffset":1453,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:threshold","text":"If \\(P\\ne NP\\), the infimum of deterministic polynomial-time approximation factors for metric \\(k\\)-median with specified candidate facilities is \\(1+2/e\\).","sourceOffset":3047,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":null,"text":"A \\(c\\)-additive \\(\\alpha\\)-approximation for metric \\(k\\)-median returns at most \\(k+c\\) facilities of cost at most \\(\\alpha\\OPT_k\\). The additive allowance \\(c\\) is independent of the input instance.","sourceOffset":13299,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/125.md","scopeText":"The formalization gives an exact-budget recovery algorithm for metric $k$-median on the stated polynomially bounded integral metrics. Suppose a supplied anchor represents all but logarithmically many comparison clusters by distinct proxies, with total proxy cost at most the comparison cost plus a sufficiently small relative error. The algorithm always opens at most $k$ facilities and, with any requested confidence, attains a $(1+2/e+\\varepsilon)$ factor relative to those comparison centers in polynomial time.\n\nThe linked final approximation result applies to arbitrary finite rational metrics. For one absolute $\\sigma>0$, a polynomial-time fair-bit algorithm always returns a feasible solution, has expected cost at most $(2-\\sigma)\\mathrm{OPT}$, and attains the same factor with arbitrarily h","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/125.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":142640,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Approximation-Threshold-for-Metric-k-Median-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"126-01","slug":"Exponential-PSD-rank-of-positively-shifted-matching-matrices-October-5-2026","familyId":"126","title":"Exponential PSD rank of positively shifted matching matrices","abstract":"For every fixed $0\\lt \\rho\\lt 1$, the matrix indexed by odd vertex sets U and perfect matchings M of Kn, with entries $|M\\cap\\delta(U)|-1+\\rho$, has real positive semidefinite rank $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ as even n tends to infinity. Here $\\delta(U)$ is the edge cut of U. Consequently, every exact semidefinite lift of the perfect matching polytope has exponential size.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every exact semidefinite lift of the perfect matching polytope has exponential size, answering Rothvoss's polynomial-size lift question negatively. The bound holds even for the positive semidefinite rank of its odd-cut slack matrix after any fixed shift $0\\lt \\rho\\lt 1$, allowing arbitrary real positive semidefinite factors.","pdfPath":"preprints/Exponential-PSD-rank-of-positively-shifted-matching-matrices-October-5-2026/shifted-matching-psd.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Exponential-PSD-rank-of-positively-shifted-matching-matrices-October-5-2026/shifted-matching-psd.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Exponential-PSD-rank-of-positively-shifted-matching-matrices-October-5-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Exponential-PSD-rank-of-positively-shifted-matching-matrices-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Exponential-PSD-rank-of-positively-shifted-matching-matrices-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Exponential-PSD-rank-of-positively-shifted-matching-matrices-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/local-averaging.tex","preprints/Exponential-PSD-rank-of-positively-shifted-matching-matrices-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/splitting.tex","preprints/Exponential-PSD-rank-of-positively-shifted-matching-matrices-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/fourier-smoothing.tex","preprints/Exponential-PSD-rank-of-positively-shifted-matching-matrices-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/parity.tex","preprints/Exponential-PSD-rank-of-positively-shifted-matching-matrices-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/matching.tex","preprints/Exponential-PSD-rank-of-positively-shifted-matching-matrices-October-5-2026/build/source/figures/matching-cells.tex","preprints/Exponential-PSD-rank-of-positively-shifted-matching-matrices-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/matching-smoothing.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Exponential-PSD-rank-of-positively-shifted-matching-matrices-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed $0<\\rho<1$, the matrix indexed by odd vertex sets $U$ and perfect matchings $M$ of $K_n$, with entries $|M\\cap\\delta(U)|-1+\\rho$, has real positive semidefinite rank $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ as even $n$ tends to infinity. Here $\\delta(U)$ is the edge cut of $U$. Consequently, every exact semidefinite lift of the perfect matching polytope has exponential size.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":416},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact semidefinite lifts","sourceOffset":2639},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the role of the shift","sourceOffset":5062},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and reusable ingredients","sourceOffset":6884},{"kind":"section","title":"Averaging within a cell","sourceOffset":11222},{"kind":"section","title":"Orthogonal splitting of positive semidefinite matrices","sourceOffset":21335},{"kind":"section","title":"Fourier concentration of squares under product averages","sourceOffset":24993},{"kind":"section","title":"An expanding parity system","sourceOffset":38338},{"kind":"section","title":"From matching factors to low-degree squares","sourceOffset":49830},{"kind":"section","title":"Uniform smoothing of matching averages","sourceOffset":59957},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The fourth moment","sourceOffset":62997},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Dimensions outside the edge-product span","sourceOffset":67661}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a constant $c>0$ such that, for every fixed $0<\\rho<1$, there is $n_0=n_0(\\rho)$ for which \\[ \\rankpsd A_n(\\rho)\\ge2^{cn} \\] for every even $n\\ge n_0$.","sourceOffset":1996,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Unshifted rank and exact lifts","label":"cor:exact-lift","text":"There are constants $c_0>0$ and $n_1$ such that, for every even $n\\ge n_1$, both $A_n(0)$ and $T_n(0)$ have real PSD rank at least $2^{c_0n}$, and every exact semidefinite lift of $P_n$ has size at least $2^{c_0n}$.","sourceOffset":3250,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/126.md","scopeText":"The paper proves exponential PSD-rank lower bounds for positively shifted matching matrices. The linked formalization records the related superpolynomial lower bounds: for every fixed $C>0$, the PSD rank of the selected perfect-matching slack matrix exceeds $n^C$ for all sufficiently large even $n$. Every exact affine semidefinite lift of the perfect-matching polytope likewise requires matrix size greater than $n^C$.\n\nThese selected statements give superpolynomial growth. They do not state the paper's exponential bound for every fixed positive shift.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/126.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":75484,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Exponential-PSD-rank-of-positively-shifted-matching-matrices-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"127-01","slug":"Average-Sensitivity-of-Polynomial-Threshold-Functions-September-25-2026","familyId":"127","title":"Average sensitivity of polynomial threshold functions","abstract":"For every n ≥ 1 and $1\\le d\\le n$, we prove that a polynomial threshold function of degree at most d on the uniform Boolean cube has average sensitivity at most $8d\\sqrt n$. This proves the asymptotic form of the Gotsman–Linial conjecture. The bound is uniform in both parameters and uses the convention $\\mathop{\\mathrm{sgn}}\\nolimits (0)=1$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that a degree-at-most-d polynomial threshold function on the uniform n-dimensional Boolean cube has average sensitivity at most $8d\\sqrt n$, uniformly for $1\\le d\\le n$. Average sensitivity counts expected output changes under single-bit flips. This establishes the asymptotic Gotsman–Linial conjecture, allowing polynomial zeros with $\\mathop{\\mathrm{sign}}\\nolimits (0)=1$.","pdfPath":"preprints/Average-Sensitivity-of-Polynomial-Threshold-Functions-September-25-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Average-Sensitivity-of-Polynomial-Threshold-Functions-September-25-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Average-Sensitivity-of-Polynomial-Threshold-Functions-September-25-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Average-Sensitivity-of-Polynomial-Threshold-Functions-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Average-Sensitivity-of-Polynomial-Threshold-Functions-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Average-Sensitivity-of-Polynomial-Threshold-Functions-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-coupling.tex","preprints/Average-Sensitivity-of-Polynomial-Threshold-Functions-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-weighted-grading.tex","preprints/Average-Sensitivity-of-Polynomial-Threshold-Functions-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-edge-recovery.tex","preprints/Average-Sensitivity-of-Polynomial-Threshold-Functions-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-parity-coupling.tex","preprints/Average-Sensitivity-of-Polynomial-Threshold-Functions-September-25-2026/build/figures/grade-coupling.tex","preprints/Average-Sensitivity-of-Polynomial-Threshold-Functions-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-consequences.tex","preprints/Average-Sensitivity-of-Polynomial-Threshold-Functions-September-25-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Average-Sensitivity-of-Polynomial-Threshold-Functions-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every $n\\ge1$ and $1\\le d\\le n$, we prove that a polynomial threshold function of degree at most $d$ on the uniform Boolean cube has average sensitivity at most $8d\\sqrt n$. This proves the asymptotic form of the Gotsman--Linial conjecture. The bound is uniform in both parameters and uses the convention $\\sgn(0)=1$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":368},{"kind":"section","title":"A one-sided coupling estimate","sourceOffset":9178},{"kind":"section","title":"Weighted Fourier grading","sourceOffset":11745},{"kind":"section","title":"Recovering edges from the anticommutator","sourceOffset":16151},{"kind":"section","title":"Parity and the binomial coupling","sourceOffset":20707},{"kind":"section","title":"Noise sensitivity and agnostic learning","sourceOffset":27194},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Noise sensitivity","sourceOffset":27593},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Agnostic learning","sourceOffset":30427}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $n\\ge1$ and $1\\le d\\le n$ be integers. Let $p$ be a real multilinear polynomial of degree at most $d$, and define $f:\\{-1,1\\}^n\\to\\{-1,1\\}$ by $f(x)=\\sgn(p(x))$, with $\\sgn(0)=1$. Then, for average sensitivity under the uniform law, \\[ I(f)\\le 8d\\sqrt n. \\]","sourceOffset":1215,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Boolean noise sensitivity","label":"cor:noise-sensitivity","text":"Let $n\\ge1$ and $d\\ge0$ be integers. If $f=\\sgn(p)$ on $\\{-1,1\\}^n$ for a real polynomial $p$ of degree at most $d$, with $\\sgn(0)=1$, then \\[ \\operatorname{NS}_{\\eta}(f)\\le C d\\sqrt\\eta \\qquad(0<\\eta\\le1/2), \\] where $C$ is an absolute constant.","sourceOffset":27856,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/127.md","scopeText":"The formalized result proves the average-sensitivity bound $8d\\sqrt n$ for every Boolean threshold function defined by a real multilinear polynomial of degree at most $d$ on the $n$-dimensional cube. The sign convention assigns value $1$ at zero. This establishes the stated asymptotic bound, rather than the separate conjecture identifying exact extremizers.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/127.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":38811,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Average-Sensitivity-of-Polynomial-Threshold-Functions-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"128-01","slug":"A-Polynomial-Time-2-Approximation-for-Shortest-Common-Superstring-September-24-2026","familyId":"128","title":"A Polynomial-Time 2-Approximation for Shortest Common Superstring","abstract":"We give a deterministic algorithm that, for every finite family of explicitly represented ordinary strings, outputs a common superstring of length at most twice the optimum in time polynomial in the total encoded input size, including symbol labels. The guarantee applies to the algorithm constructed here, not the classical maximum-overlap Greedy procedure.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm constructing a common superstring of length at most twice the optimum for every finite family of explicitly represented strings. The running time is polynomial in the full encoded input length, including symbol labels.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Polynomial-Time-2-Approximation-for-Shortest-Common-Superstring-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Polynomial-Time-2-Approximation-for-Shortest-Common-Superstring-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Polynomial-Time-2-Approximation-for-Shortest-Common-Superstring-September-24-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/A-Polynomial-Time-2-Approximation-for-Shortest-Common-Superstring-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-Polynomial-Time-2-Approximation-for-Shortest-Common-Superstring-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-Polynomial-Time-2-Approximation-for-Shortest-Common-Superstring-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-counts.tex","preprints/A-Polynomial-Time-2-Approximation-for-Shortest-Common-Superstring-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-layers.tex","preprints/A-Polynomial-Time-2-Approximation-for-Shortest-Common-Superstring-September-24-2026/build/figures/layers.tex","preprints/A-Polynomial-Time-2-Approximation-for-Shortest-Common-Superstring-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-connections.tex","preprints/A-Polynomial-Time-2-Approximation-for-Shortest-Common-Superstring-September-24-2026/build/figures/collective.tex","preprints/A-Polynomial-Time-2-Approximation-for-Shortest-Common-Superstring-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-processing.tex","preprints/A-Polynomial-Time-2-Approximation-for-Shortest-Common-Superstring-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-cycles.tex","preprints/A-Polynomial-Time-2-Approximation-for-Shortest-Common-Superstring-September-24-2026/build/figures/cycles.tex","preprints/A-Polynomial-Time-2-Approximation-for-Shortest-Common-Superstring-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-algorithm.tex","preprints/A-Polynomial-Time-2-Approximation-for-Shortest-Common-Superstring-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Polynomial-Time-2-Approximation-for-Shortest-Common-Superstring-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a deterministic algorithm that, for every finite family of explicitly represented ordinary strings, outputs a common superstring of length at most twice the optimum in time polynomial in the total encoded input size, including symbol labels. The guarantee applies to the algorithm constructed here, not the classical maximum-overlap Greedy procedure.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":404},{"kind":"section","title":"Forced occurrence counts","sourceOffset":7247},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Blocking an occurrence","sourceOffset":8060},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The rules","sourceOffset":9451},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The balanced base graph","sourceOffset":12610},{"kind":"section","title":"Periodic layers","sourceOffset":15058},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Lifting closed walks to windows","sourceOffset":15327},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sorting primitive passages","sourceOffset":17241},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Unblocked occurrences on layers","sourceOffset":22425},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two periodicity facts","sourceOffset":24559},{"kind":"section","title":"Connection operations","sourceOffset":27061},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Following ordered windows","sourceOffset":27876}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a deterministic algorithm, polynomial in the total encoded input size, that on every finite collection $\\mathcal S$ of explicitly represented ordinary strings outputs a common superstring $T$ satisfying \\[ \\len{T}\\le 2\\OPT(\\mathcal S). \\]","sourceOffset":1078,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:partition","text":"After all groups have been processed, every layer is either marked rooted or belongs to exactly one outgoing block. These two classes are disjoint. Each block consists of layers from one primitive-text group and targets a single actual layer. Its total budget remains reserved for its planned link. A collective block of $k$ layers and period $p$ also satisfies \\eqref{eq:sharp-band} at its distinguished position; an individual block consists of one layer.","sourceOffset":48826,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/128.md","scopeText":"The shortest common superstring problem asks for the shortest string containing each input string as a contiguous substring. The formalized result gives one deterministic polynomial-time algorithm whose output is a common superstring of length at most twice the unrestricted optimum. It applies to every finite list of explicitly encoded strings, with running time measured in input bits and the approximation ratio measured in symbol length.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/128.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":67212,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Polynomial-Time-2-Approximation-for-Shortest-Common-Superstring-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"129-01","slug":"An-exponential-state-lower-bound-for-two-way-nondeterministic-complementation-September-25-2026","familyId":"129","title":"An exponential state lower bound for two-way nondeterministic complementation","abstract":"We prove that two-way nondeterministic finite automata cannot be complemented with a polynomial number of states independent of the alphabet. For each n ≥ 4 we construct an n-state automaton over a finite alphabet whose complement requires at least $\\tfrac12 2^{\\lfloor(n-4)/127\\rfloor}-1$ states.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves exponential lower bounds both for complementing two-way nondeterministic finite automata and for simulating one-way nondeterministic automata by two-way deterministic ones. The latter resolves the Sakoda–Sipser state-succinctness conjecture over growing finite alphabets; both results rule out polynomial state bounds independent of alphabet size.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-exponential-state-lower-bound-for-two-way-nondeterministic-complementation-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-exponential-state-lower-bound-for-two-way-nondeterministic-complementation-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-exponential-state-lower-bound-for-two-way-nondeterministic-complementation-September-25-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/An-exponential-state-lower-bound-for-two-way-nondeterministic-complementation-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/An-exponential-state-lower-bound-for-two-way-nondeterministic-complementation-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/An-exponential-state-lower-bound-for-two-way-nondeterministic-complementation-September-25-2026/build/sections/diagrams.tex","preprints/An-exponential-state-lower-bound-for-two-way-nondeterministic-complementation-September-25-2026/build/sections/nesting.tex","preprints/An-exponential-state-lower-bound-for-two-way-nondeterministic-complementation-September-25-2026/build/figures/nesting-budget.tex","preprints/An-exponential-state-lower-bound-for-two-way-nondeterministic-complementation-September-25-2026/build/sections/amplification.tex","preprints/An-exponential-state-lower-bound-for-two-way-nondeterministic-complementation-September-25-2026/build/figures/hub.tex","preprints/An-exponential-state-lower-bound-for-two-way-nondeterministic-complementation-September-25-2026/build/sections/automata.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-exponential-state-lower-bound-for-two-way-nondeterministic-complementation-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that two-way nondeterministic finite automata cannot be complemented with a polynomial number of states independent of the alphabet. For each $n\\ge4$ we construct an $n$-state automaton over a finite alphabet whose complement requires at least $\\tfrac12 2^{\\lfloor(n-4)/127\\rfloor}-1$ states.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":347},{"kind":"section","title":"Path diagrams and missing recurrent classes","sourceOffset":8431},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The diagram monoid","sourceOffset":9266},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From complementation to an order-reversing image","sourceOffset":12835},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Recurrent classes in a corner","sourceOffset":17698},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Transport through a fixed context","sourceOffset":22318},{"kind":"section","title":"A loss budget for nested idempotents","sourceOffset":25466},{"kind":"section","title":"An exponential bound for order-reversing images","sourceOffset":34371},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Corners and unit lifts","sourceOffset":35372},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Amplifying a single added pair","sourceOffset":38370},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite computations and the main lower bound","sourceOffset":48345},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The source automaton","sourceOffset":48907}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"main:theorem","text":"For every integer $n\\ge4$, set $H=\\{1,\\ldots,n-2\\}$ and $\\Sigma_n=\\mathcal R_H$. There is an $n$-state 2NFA $A_n$ recognizing $L_H$ such that every 2NFA recognizing $\\Sigma_n^*\\setminus L(A_n)$ has at least \\[ \\frac12\\,2^{\\lfloor(n-4)/127\\rfloor}-1 \\] states. In particular, there is no polynomial state bound for complementation that is independent of the finite alphabet.","sourceOffset":2249,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Order-reversing image bound","label":"amp:image","text":"Let $m\\geq1$, let $H$ be a finite set of cardinality $h\\geq2$, and let $S_0$ be a submonoid of $\\mathcal T_m$. Suppose that a surjective multiplicative map $\\phi_0:S_0\\to\\mathcal R_H$ satisfies \\begin{equation} z\\sqsubseteq w\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\phi_0(w)\\subseteq\\phi_0(z) \\qquad(z,w\\in S_0). \\end{equation} Then \\begin{equation} 2m\\geq 2^{\\lfloor(h-2)/127\\rfloor}. \\end{equation} The map need not initially be assumed to preserve identities.","sourceOffset":13077,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/129.md","scopeText":"The paper asks how many states are needed to complement or determinize two-way nondeterministic finite automata. The formalization gives, for every $n\\ge4$, an explicit $n$-state automaton whose complement requires at least $\\tfrac12 2^{\\lfloor(n-4)/127\\rfloor}-1$ states. For the same family of relational languages and every $n\\ge131$, every equivalent deterministic two-way automaton requires at least $\\tfrac12 2^{\\lfloor(n-4)/127\\rfloor}$ states.\n\nThe alphabet grows with $n$, so neither complementation nor determinization has a polynomial state bound uniform over alphabets. The model has two endmarkers, left, right, and stay moves, and acceptance by a finite run. The separate results about one-way liveness and the Sakoda–Sipser problem are outside this scope.\n\nThe formalization gives a fa","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/129.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":59159,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-exponential-state-lower-bound-for-two-way-nondeterministic-complementation-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"129-02","slug":"An-exponential-two-way-deterministic-state-lower-bound-for-one-way-liveness-September-25-2026","familyId":"129","title":"An exponential two-way deterministic state lower bound for one-way liveness","abstract":"One-way liveness on h points accepts a word of binary relations when their ordered product is nonempty. For every h ≥ 2, it has a nondeterministic automaton with $h+3$ states and no left moves, whereas every equivalent s-state two-way deterministic automaton satisfies $4(s+2)^2\\ge2^{\\lfloor(h-2)/31\\rfloor}$. Partial transition rules, stay moves, and nonaccepting infinite computations are allowed. The alphabets are finite and grow with h, so the result rules out an alphabet-independent polynomial state bound for deterministic two-way simulation, already for one-way nondeterministic sources.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves exponential lower bounds both for complementing two-way nondeterministic finite automata and for simulating one-way nondeterministic automata by two-way deterministic ones. The latter resolves the Sakoda–Sipser state-succinctness conjecture over growing finite alphabets; both results rule out polynomial state bounds independent of alphabet size.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-exponential-two-way-deterministic-state-lower-bound-for-one-way-liveness-September-25-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-exponential-two-way-deterministic-state-lower-bound-for-one-way-liveness-September-25-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-exponential-two-way-deterministic-state-lower-bound-for-one-way-liveness-September-25-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/An-exponential-two-way-deterministic-state-lower-bound-for-one-way-liveness-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/An-exponential-two-way-deterministic-state-lower-bound-for-one-way-liveness-September-25-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/An-exponential-two-way-deterministic-state-lower-bound-for-one-way-liveness-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/An-exponential-two-way-deterministic-state-lower-bound-for-one-way-liveness-September-25-2026/build/sections/matching.tex","preprints/An-exponential-two-way-deterministic-state-lower-bound-for-one-way-liveness-September-25-2026/build/figures/corner-reduction.tex","preprints/An-exponential-two-way-deterministic-state-lower-bound-for-one-way-liveness-September-25-2026/build/sections/rank.tex","preprints/An-exponential-two-way-deterministic-state-lower-bound-for-one-way-liveness-September-25-2026/build/figures/amplification.tex","preprints/An-exponential-two-way-deterministic-state-lower-bound-for-one-way-liveness-September-25-2026/build/sections/machines.tex","preprints/An-exponential-two-way-deterministic-state-lower-bound-for-one-way-liveness-September-25-2026/build/figures/candidate-slot.tex","preprints/An-exponential-two-way-deterministic-state-lower-bound-for-one-way-liveness-September-25-2026/build/figures/tour-test-ports.tex","preprints/An-exponential-two-way-deterministic-state-lower-bound-for-one-way-liveness-September-25-2026/build/sections/separation.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-exponential-two-way-deterministic-state-lower-bound-for-one-way-liveness-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"One-way liveness on $h$ points accepts a word of binary relations when their ordered product is nonempty. For every $h\\ge2$, it has a nondeterministic automaton with $h+3$ states and no left moves, whereas every equivalent $s$-state two-way deterministic automaton satisfies $4(s+2)^2\\ge2^{\\lfloor(h-2)/31\\rfloor}$. Partial transition rules, stay moves, and nonaccepting infinite computations are allowed. The alphabets are finite and grow with $h$, so the result rules out an alphabet-independent polynomial state bound for deterministic two-way simulation, already for one-way nondeterministic sources.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":650},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model and statement","sourceOffset":4295},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof architecture","sourceOffset":7164},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Related results","sourceOffset":9662},{"kind":"section","title":"Matching and relation corners","sourceOffset":10664},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Matching diagrams, rank, and support","sourceOffset":11010},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reduction at an idempotent","sourceOffset":14529},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Homomorphic images and permutation lifts","sourceOffset":23941},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Corners of the relation monoid","sourceOffset":26262},{"kind":"section","title":"An exponential rank-loss bound","sourceOffset":28001},{"kind":"section","title":"Local matching representation of deterministic machines","sourceOffset":42492},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A sink and its tree","sourceOffset":44888}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $h\\ge2$, the language $\\OWL_h$ is recognized by a nondeterministic automaton with $h+3$ states using no left moves. Every equivalent deterministic two-way automaton with $s$ states satisfies \\begin{equation} 4(s+2)^2\\ge 2^{\\lfloor(h-2)/31\\rfloor}. \\end{equation} If an initially accepting configuration counts as acceptance, $s+2$ in \\eqref{eq:main-bound} may be replaced by $s+1$.","sourceOffset":5994,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:no-polynomial","text":"There are no absolute constants $C,c>0$ such that every $n$-state two-way nondeterministic automaton over every finite alphabet has an equivalent two-way deterministic automaton with at most $Cn^c$ states.","sourceOffset":6465,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/129.md","scopeText":"The paper asks how many states are needed to complement or determinize two-way nondeterministic finite automata. The formalization gives, for every $n\\ge4$, an explicit $n$-state automaton whose complement requires at least $\\tfrac12 2^{\\lfloor(n-4)/127\\rfloor}-1$ states. For the same family of relational languages and every $n\\ge131$, every equivalent deterministic two-way automaton requires at least $\\tfrac12 2^{\\lfloor(n-4)/127\\rfloor}$ states.\n\nThe alphabet grows with $n$, so neither complementation nor determinization has a polynomial state bound uniform over alphabets. The model has two endmarkers, left, right, and stay moves, and acceptance by a finite run. The separate results about one-way liveness and the Sakoda–Sipser problem are outside this scope.\n\nThe formalization gives a fa","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/129.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":66698,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-exponential-two-way-deterministic-state-lower-bound-for-one-way-liveness-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"130-01","slug":"An-explicit-power-saving-for-the-exact-discrete-Fourier-transform-September-25-2026","familyId":"130","title":"An explicit power saving for the exact discrete Fourier transform","abstract":"We give a deterministic algorithm that computes the discrete Fourier transform at every length n in $O(n(\\log n)^{1-10^{-13}})$ operations. The model uses exact complex arithmetic, unrestricted coefficients, specified Fourier roots, and unit-cost logarithmic-size indexing; scalar preparation and array organization are included.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives a deterministic length-n discrete Fourier transform algorithm using $O(n(\\log n)^{1-\\delta})$ operations for every n, with explicit $\\delta=10^{-13}$. The model uses exact complex arithmetic, unrestricted coefficients and a supplied root of unity, and counts scalar preparation and logarithmic-word indexing.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-explicit-power-saving-for-the-exact-discrete-Fourier-transform-September-25-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-explicit-power-saving-for-the-exact-discrete-Fourier-transform-September-25-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-explicit-power-saving-for-the-exact-discrete-Fourier-transform-September-25-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/An-explicit-power-saving-for-the-exact-discrete-Fourier-transform-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/An-explicit-power-saving-for-the-exact-discrete-Fourier-transform-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/An-explicit-power-saving-for-the-exact-discrete-Fourier-transform-September-25-2026/build/figures/proof-map.tex","preprints/An-explicit-power-saving-for-the-exact-discrete-Fourier-transform-September-25-2026/build/sections/network.tex","preprints/An-explicit-power-saving-for-the-exact-discrete-Fourier-transform-September-25-2026/build/sections/local.tex","preprints/An-explicit-power-saving-for-the-exact-discrete-Fourier-transform-September-25-2026/build/sections/synchronization.tex","preprints/An-explicit-power-saving-for-the-exact-discrete-Fourier-transform-September-25-2026/build/sections/all-lengths.tex","preprints/An-explicit-power-saving-for-the-exact-discrete-Fourier-transform-September-25-2026/build/sections/synthesis.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-explicit-power-saving-for-the-exact-discrete-Fourier-transform-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a deterministic algorithm that computes the discrete Fourier transform at every length $n$ in $O(n(\\log n)^{1-10^{-13}})$ operations. The model uses exact complex arithmetic, unrestricted coefficients, specified Fourier roots, and unit-cost logarithmic-size indexing; scalar preparation and array organization are included.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":377},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Arithmetic model and main result","sourceOffset":3867},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and its reusable parts","sourceOffset":8175},{"kind":"section","title":"An explicit saving for a fixed two-coordinate transform","sourceOffset":12882},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A scalar network with arbitrary auxiliary values","sourceOffset":13799},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Frames turn binary dimensions into directional operations","sourceOffset":17098},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Binary labels and the dimension budget","sourceOffset":21313},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Transforming every physical role","sourceOffset":28930},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Linear-time address changes","sourceOffset":31038},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact batching and the critical exponent","sourceOffset":33512},{"kind":"section","title":"Compiling a Fourier transform on its own coordinates","sourceOffset":38269},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reducing Fourier matrices to triangular Toeplitz matrices","sourceOffset":40645}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a single deterministic algorithm which, for every positive integer $n$ and every $x\\in\\C^n$, computes $F_nx$ exactly. In the model above, including scalar preparation, schedule construction, and index work, its running time is \\begin{equation} O\\!\\left(n(\\log n)^\\theta(\\log\\log n)^{4-\\theta}\\right) \\end{equation} as $n\\to\\infty$. It suffices to supply the specified root $\\zeta_{D_*}$ for an explicitly computable integer $D_*<1024n^3$.","sourceOffset":5738,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:decimal","text":"In the same model, the transform is computable at every length in \\[ O\\!\\left(n(\\log n)^{1-10^{-13}}\\right)=o(n\\log n) \\] time.","sourceOffset":6255,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/130.md","scopeText":"The formalized result gives exact discrete Fourier transforms with arbitrarily small normalized circuit cost along an unbounded sequence of lengths. For every $c>0$ and every cutoff $N_0\\ge2$, some $n\\ge N_0$ has a circuit computing the unnormalized DFT with fewer than $cn\\log_2 n$ gates. Addition, subtraction, and multiplication by a predetermined complex scalar each cost one gate; diagonal scalings are charged. The result is subsequential, with no all-length, bounded-coefficient, conditioning, or bit-complexity claim.\n\nA separate uniform formalization gives fixed deterministic programs for every positive length $n$, computing the canonical discrete Fourier transform and the full convolution of two length-$n$ complex vectors exactly. Their work is $O(n(\\log n)^{1-10^{-13}})=o(n\\log n)$, i","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/130.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":100131,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-explicit-power-saving-for-the-exact-discrete-Fourier-transform-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"130-02","slug":"Finite-tensor-savings-and-exact-Fourier-circuits-September-25-2026","familyId":"130","title":"Finite tensor savings and exact Fourier circuits","abstract":"We construct exact nonuniform Fourier circuits of size $o(n\\log n)$ along an unbounded sequence of lengths, counting every addition, subtraction, and scalar multiplication. This refutes the $\\Omega(n\\log n)$ lower bound in the unrestricted complex linear-circuit model. The construction uses a finite tensor saving: a tensor power of some invertible nonmonomial complex matrix can be computed with fewer matrix calls than the standard tensor-axis algorithm on the same coordinates, when invertible monomial maps are allowed freely between calls.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives a deterministic length-n discrete Fourier transform algorithm using $O(n(\\log n)^{1-\\delta})$ operations for every n, with explicit $\\delta=10^{-13}$. The model uses exact complex arithmetic, unrestricted coefficients and a supplied root of unity, and counts scalar preparation and logarithmic-word indexing.","pdfPath":"preprints/Finite-tensor-savings-and-exact-Fourier-circuits-September-25-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-tensor-savings-and-exact-Fourier-circuits-September-25-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-tensor-savings-and-exact-Fourier-circuits-September-25-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Finite-tensor-savings-and-exact-Fourier-circuits-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Finite-tensor-savings-and-exact-Fourier-circuits-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Finite-tensor-savings-and-exact-Fourier-circuits-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-generation.tex","preprints/Finite-tensor-savings-and-exact-Fourier-circuits-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-fourier-transfer.tex","preprints/Finite-tensor-savings-and-exact-Fourier-circuits-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-packing-prices.tex","preprints/Finite-tensor-savings-and-exact-Fourier-circuits-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-corners.tex","preprints/Finite-tensor-savings-and-exact-Fourier-circuits-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-feedback.tex","preprints/Finite-tensor-savings-and-exact-Fourier-circuits-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-pivots.tex","preprints/Finite-tensor-savings-and-exact-Fourier-circuits-September-25-2026/build/sections/08-reflection.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Finite-tensor-savings-and-exact-Fourier-circuits-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct exact nonuniform Fourier circuits of size $o(n\\log n)$ along an unbounded sequence of lengths, counting every addition, subtraction, and scalar multiplication. This refutes the $\\Omega(n\\log n)$ lower bound in the unrestricted complex linear-circuit model. The construction uses a finite tensor saving: a tensor power of some invertible nonmonomial complex matrix can be computed with fewer matrix calls than the standard tensor-axis algorithm on the same coordinates, when invertible monomial maps are allowed freely between calls.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":608},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and the result","sourceOffset":1877},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":4037},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The mechanism of the proof","sourceOffset":8847},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and order of the argument","sourceOffset":13094},{"kind":"section","title":"Positive words and tensor amplification","sourceOffset":14937},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One fixed positive pattern generates every target","sourceOffset":16513},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Amplifying a finite saving","sourceOffset":25070},{"kind":"section","title":"From a finite win to exact Fourier circuits","sourceOffset":29092},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Convolutions and borrowed coordinates","sourceOffset":29862},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An exact-width factorization","sourceOffset":35438},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prime factors and synchronized generation","sourceOffset":43006}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"In this model, \\[ \\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac{L(n)}{n\\log_2 n}=0. \\] Equivalently, for every $c>0$ and every integer $N_0\\ge2$, there is an integer $n\\ge N_0$ and an exact circuit for $F_n$ with fewer than $c n\\log_2 n$ gates.","sourceOffset":2938,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Transfer of a finite win","label":"prop:win-to-fourier","text":"If a finite win exists, there are integers $n_m\\to\\infty$ and exact linear circuits for $F_{n_m}$ whose scalar gate counts satisfy \\[ L(n_m)=o(n_m\\log_2n_m). \\] In particular the conclusion is in the model charging additions, subtractions, and multiplication by every prechosen complex scalar.","sourceOffset":43366,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Finite win","label":"def:finite-win","text":"A finite win consists of an invertible nonmonomial matrix $A\\in\\GL_q(\\C)$, an integer $b\\geq2$, and a word on exactly $q^b$ coordinates implementing $A^{\\otimes b}$ with $g$ calls to $A$, where \\begin{equation} g<bq^{b-1}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":15753,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/130.md","scopeText":"The formalized result gives exact discrete Fourier transforms with arbitrarily small normalized circuit cost along an unbounded sequence of lengths. For every $c>0$ and every cutoff $N_0\\ge2$, some $n\\ge N_0$ has a circuit computing the unnormalized DFT with fewer than $cn\\log_2 n$ gates. Addition, subtraction, and multiplication by a predetermined complex scalar each cost one gate; diagonal scalings are charged. The result is subsequential, with no all-length, bounded-coefficient, conditioning, or bit-complexity claim.\n\nA separate uniform formalization gives fixed deterministic programs for every positive length $n$, computing the canonical discrete Fourier transform and the full convolution of two length-$n$ complex vectors exactly. Their work is $O(n(\\log n)^{1-10^{-13}})=o(n\\log n)$, i","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/130.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":150088,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Finite-tensor-savings-and-exact-Fourier-circuits-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"131-01","slug":"Polynomial-Mixing-of-the-Switch-Chain-for-Every-Graphical-Degree-Sequence-September-25-2026","familyId":"131","title":"Polynomial mixing of the switch chain for every graphical degree sequence","abstract":"We prove the simple-undirected form of the Kannan–Tetali–Vempala conjecture: the switch chain on simple undirected graphs mixes in polynomial time for every graphical degree sequence. For a lazy chain that proposes switches uniformly on four vertices, the total-variation mixing time at distance 1/4 is at most $2n^8$. We also give an exactly uniform sampler for every graphical labeled degree vector. It uses unbiased random bits, terminates almost surely, and has expected polynomial bit running time.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the simple-undirected Kannan–Tetali–Vempala conjecture: the lazy edge-switch chain mixes in $O(n^8)$ time for every graphical labeled degree sequence. The same degree-constrained graphs can also be sampled exactly uniformly by an almost-surely terminating algorithm with expected polynomial bit running time.","pdfPath":"preprints/Polynomial-Mixing-of-the-Switch-Chain-for-Every-Graphical-Degree-Sequence-September-25-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Polynomial-Mixing-of-the-Switch-Chain-for-Every-Graphical-Degree-Sequence-September-25-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Polynomial-Mixing-of-the-Switch-Chain-for-Every-Graphical-Degree-Sequence-September-25-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Polynomial-Mixing-of-the-Switch-Chain-for-Every-Graphical-Degree-Sequence-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Mixing-of-the-Switch-Chain-for-Every-Graphical-Degree-Sequence-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Mixing-of-the-Switch-Chain-for-Every-Graphical-Degree-Sequence-September-25-2026/build/sections/setup.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Mixing-of-the-Switch-Chain-for-Every-Graphical-Degree-Sequence-September-25-2026/build/sections/rows.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Mixing-of-the-Switch-Chain-for-Every-Graphical-Degree-Sequence-September-25-2026/build/sections/triples.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Mixing-of-the-Switch-Chain-for-Every-Graphical-Degree-Sequence-September-25-2026/build/sections/disjoint.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Mixing-of-the-Switch-Chain-for-Every-Graphical-Degree-Sequence-September-25-2026/build/sections/slice.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Mixing-of-the-Switch-Chain-for-Every-Graphical-Degree-Sequence-September-25-2026/build/sections/mixing.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Mixing-of-the-Switch-Chain-for-Every-Graphical-Degree-Sequence-September-25-2026/build/sections/exact-sampling.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Mixing-of-the-Switch-Chain-for-Every-Graphical-Degree-Sequence-September-25-2026/build/sources/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Polynomial-Mixing-of-the-Switch-Chain-for-Every-Graphical-Degree-Sequence-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the simple-undirected form of the Kannan--Tetali--Vempala conjecture: the switch chain on simple undirected graphs mixes in polynomial time for every graphical degree sequence. For a lazy chain that proposes switches uniformly on four vertices, the total-variation mixing time at distance $1/4$ is at most $2n^8$. We also give an exactly uniform sampler for every graphical labeled degree vector. It uses unbiased random bits, terminates almost surely, and has expected polynomial bit running time.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":553},{"kind":"section","title":"Pair resamplings","sourceOffset":10189},{"kind":"section","title":"A variance inequality for three rows","sourceOffset":12123},{"kind":"section","title":"Resamplings on a vertex triple","sourceOffset":21149},{"kind":"section","title":"Disjoint pair resamplings","sourceOffset":25648},{"kind":"section","title":"Controlling the equality projections","sourceOffset":37758},{"kind":"section","title":"From pair resampling to the switch chain","sourceOffset":46531},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The global operator inequality","sourceOffset":46744},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Connectivity and the kernel","sourceOffset":47822},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison with single switches","sourceOffset":51404},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The prescribed chain and its mixing time","sourceOffset":54857},{"kind":"section","title":"Exact uniform sampling","sourceOffset":56960}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $n\\ge4$ and every graphical degree vector $d$, \\[ t_{\\mathrm{mix}}(d)\\le 2n^8. \\] If $|\\Omega_d|>1$, the spectral gap of $P_d$ is at least $[24n^2\\binom n4]^{-1}$.","sourceOffset":1814,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Exact uniform sampling","label":"cor:exact-sampling","text":"There is a uniform randomized algorithm that, given any finite graphical labeled degree vector $d=(d_1,\\ldots,d_n)$, uses unbiased random bits, terminates almost surely, and outputs a graph chosen exactly uniformly from the simple undirected graphs on $\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$ with labeled degree vector $d$. Its expected bit running time is bounded by a fixed polynomial in the binary encoding length of $d$.","sourceOffset":2053,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/131.md","scopeText":"The Kannan–Tetali–Vempala conjecture asks for polynomial mixing of the switch chain for every graphical degree sequence. The formalization proves the simple undirected case: for $n\\ge4$, the lazy chain that proposes switches on four vertices has total-variation mixing time at distance $1/4$ at most $2n^8$. It also proves switch connectivity, the stated exponential total-variation bound, and a spectral gap of at least $1/(24n^2\\binom n4)$ when more than one graph realizes the degree sequence.\n\nThe paper's exactly uniform sampling algorithm is outside these selected chain estimates.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/131.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":72491,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Polynomial-Mixing-of-the-Switch-Chain-for-Every-Graphical-Degree-Sequence-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"132-01","slug":"A-superquadratic-separation-between-sensitivity-and-block-sensitivity-September-25-2026","familyId":"132","title":"A superquadratic separation between sensitivity and block sensitivity","abstract":"We disprove the quadratic strengthening of the Sensitivity Conjecture by constructing nonconstant total Boolean functions whose block sensitivity grows faster than any constant multiple of sensitivity squared. In fact, for some fixed α > 2, our examples have unbounded block sensitivity and satisfy $\\mathop{\\mathrm{bs}}\\nolimits (f)\\ge s(f)^\\alpha$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs total Boolean functions with block sensitivity $\\mathop{\\mathrm{bs}}\\nolimits (f)\\ge s(f)^\\alpha$ for a fixed α > 2, disproving the quadratic strengthening of the Sensitivity Conjecture. Here $s(f)$ counts influential individual-bit flips, while block sensitivity allows disjoint groups of bits to change together.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-superquadratic-separation-between-sensitivity-and-block-sensitivity-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-superquadratic-separation-between-sensitivity-and-block-sensitivity-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-superquadratic-separation-between-sensitivity-and-block-sensitivity-September-25-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/A-superquadratic-separation-between-sensitivity-and-block-sensitivity-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-superquadratic-separation-between-sensitivity-and-block-sensitivity-September-25-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/A-superquadratic-separation-between-sensitivity-and-block-sensitivity-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-construction.tex","preprints/A-superquadratic-separation-between-sensitivity-and-block-sensitivity-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-recurrences.tex","preprints/A-superquadratic-separation-between-sensitivity-and-block-sensitivity-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-separation.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-superquadratic-separation-between-sensitivity-and-block-sensitivity-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove the quadratic strengthening of the Sensitivity Conjecture by constructing nonconstant total Boolean functions whose block sensitivity grows faster than any constant multiple of sensitivity squared. In fact, for some fixed \\(\\alpha>2\\), our examples have unbounded block sensitivity and satisfy \\(\\bs(f)\\ge s(f)^\\alpha\\).","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":378},{"kind":"section","title":"The labelled tournament and nested predicates","sourceOffset":7715},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Initial predicates","sourceOffset":10008},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Recursive clauses","sourceOffset":10474},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sensitivity profiles and the initial bounds","sourceOffset":12935},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Disjoint blocks at zero","sourceOffset":14761},{"kind":"section","title":"Sensitivity recurrences","sourceOffset":16125},{"kind":"section","title":"Quantitative bounds and separation","sourceOffset":23433},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A fixed exponent by composition","sourceOffset":28802}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every real \\(C>0\\), there are a positive integer \\(n\\) and a nonconstant total Boolean function \\(f:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}\\) such that \\[ \\bs(f)>C\\,s(f)^2. \\] More precisely, for every integer \\(d\\ge1\\) the construction below gives a nonconstant total Boolean function \\(f\\) with \\[ \\frac{\\bs(f)}{s(f)^2}\\ge \\frac{2^d}{4(d+2)^2}. \\]","sourceOffset":2772,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:recurrences","text":"For $1\\leq \\ell\\leq d$, write $S'_b=S_{\\ell-1,b}$ and $J'_b=J_{\\ell-1,b}$. Then \\begin{equation} \\begin{aligned} S_{\\ell,1}&\\leq M^2S'_0+rS'_1, &\\qquad J_{\\ell,1}&\\leq M^2J'_0+rS'_1,\\\\ S_{\\ell,0}&\\leq tS'_0+S'_1+3J'_1, &\\qquad J_{\\ell,0}&\\leq tS'_0+3J'_1. \\end{aligned} \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":16395,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/132.md","scopeText":"The formalized result disproves a universal quadratic bound of block sensitivity by sensitivity for total Boolean functions. For every integer $d\\ge1$, it constructs a nonconstant function with $\\mathrm{bs}(f)/s(f)^2\\ge2^d/(4(d+2)^2)$, making the ratio unbounded. The formalization also gives a fixed exponent $\\alpha>2$ and a sequence with $s(f)^\\alpha\\le\\mathrm{bs}(f,0)$ while the latter tends to infinity; here $\\mathrm{bs}(f,0)$ is block sensitivity at the all-zero input.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/132.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":32586,"sourceFileCount":5,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-superquadratic-separation-between-sensitivity-and-block-sensitivity-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"133-01","slug":"Parity-lifts-and-bounded-treewidth-witnesses-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026","familyId":"133","title":"Parity lifts and bounded-treewidth witnesses for Weisfeiler–Leman equivalence","abstract":"For k ≥ 4, we construct two uncolored graphs that are k-dimensional Weisfeiler–Leman equivalent exactly when a prescribed finite-domain choice system has no compatible choice. The system has $k+1$ domains for joint refinement and k for separate-coordinate refinement. A successful choice is detected after two joint rounds or one separate round. Applied to sparse satisfiability, the reduction gives fixed-dimension $n^{\\Omega(k)}$ time exclusions under positive-rate ETH, even for deciding equality of these early histograms.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves unconditional $n^{\\Omega(k)}$ deterministic time lower bounds for joint and separate k-dimensional Weisfeiler–Leman equivalence, for sufficiently large fixed k in the specified sequential adjacency-matrix models. With dimension as input, joint equivalence is EXPTIME-complete even on subcubic graphs; deciding whether refinement identifies a graph is also EXPTIME-complete.","pdfPath":"preprints/Parity-lifts-and-bounded-treewidth-witnesses-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Parity-lifts-and-bounded-treewidth-witnesses-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Parity-lifts-and-bounded-treewidth-witnesses-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Parity-lifts-and-bounded-treewidth-witnesses-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Parity-lifts-and-bounded-treewidth-witnesses-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Parity-lifts-and-bounded-treewidth-witnesses-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-wl-tests.tex","preprints/Parity-lifts-and-bounded-treewidth-witnesses-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-parity-lifts.tex","preprints/Parity-lifts-and-bounded-treewidth-witnesses-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026/build/figures/helper-incidence.tex","preprints/Parity-lifts-and-bounded-treewidth-witnesses-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-separator.tex","preprints/Parity-lifts-and-bounded-treewidth-witnesses-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-complexity.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Parity-lifts-and-bounded-treewidth-witnesses-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For \\(k\\ge4\\), we construct two uncolored graphs that are \\(k\\)-dimensional Weisfeiler--Leman equivalent exactly when a prescribed finite-domain choice system has no compatible choice. The system has \\(k+1\\) domains for joint refinement and \\(k\\) for separate-coordinate refinement. A successful choice is detected after two joint rounds or one separate round. Applied to sparse satisfiability, the reduction gives fixed-dimension \\(n^{\\Omega(k)}\\) time exclusions under positive-rate ETH, even for deciding equality of these early histograms.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":590},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Refinement and simultaneous choices","sourceOffset":1460},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and the main ideas","sourceOffset":4945},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conditional time bounds and their scope","sourceOffset":9652},{"kind":"section","title":"WL refinement and homomorphism tests","sourceOffset":13599},{"kind":"section","title":"Choices and parity lifts","sourceOffset":23129},{"kind":"section","title":"Extracting a choice from a bounded-bag witness","sourceOffset":31672},{"kind":"section","title":"The ETH lower bound","sourceOffset":40337},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The uniform subexponential formulation","sourceOffset":47097}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Parity reduction","label":"thm:choice-reduction","text":"Fix \\(k\\ge4\\), either refinement convention, and the corresponding \\(t\\) in \\eqref{eq:bag-parameter}. From every choice system on \\(t\\) domains one can explicitly construct two uncolored simple graphs \\(X_0,X_\\star\\) of equal order such that \\[ X_0\\equiv_k X_\\star \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\text{the choice system has no successful choice}. \\] The assertion holds both with common color names on separate graphs and with pure-tuple comparison in their disjoint union. If every domain and label set has size at most \\(A\\), where \\(A\\ge1\\), their common order is at most \\(A M_t\\), where \\[ M_t=t\\,2^{\\,t-2+3\\binom{t-1}{2}}+12\\binom t3. \\] When a successful choice exists, the two graphs already have different histograms after at most two joint rounds, or one separate round.","sourceOffset":3940,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Fixed-dimension ETH consequence","label":"thm:eth","text":"Assume positive exponential-rate ETH. There are constants \\(c>0\\) and \\(K\\ge4\\) such that, for every fixed integer \\(k\\ge K\\), no deterministic algorithm decides \\(k\\)-WL equivalence of two \\(n\\)-vertex graphs in time \\(O(n^{ck})\\). This holds for both joint and separate refinement. The inputs are explicit adjacency matrices of uncolored simple undirected graphs, and time is measured on a multitape Turing machine.","sourceOffset":10248,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Simultaneous choices","label":"def:choice","text":"A choice system consists of finite domains \\(D_1,\\ldots,D_t\\) and, for every \\(i<j\\), a finite label set \\(L_{ij}\\) and maps \\[ \\lambda_{ij}^i:D_i\\longrightarrow L_{ij}, \\qquad \\lambda_{ij}^j:D_j\\longrightarrow L_{ij}. \\] A successful choice is a tuple \\((d_1,\\ldots,d_t)\\in\\prod_iD_i\\) satisfying \\(\\lambda_{ij}^i(d_i)=\\lambda_{ij}^j(d_j)\\) for every \\(i<j\\). Empty domains are allowed.","sourceOffset":2881,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:template","text":"The template \\(T=T_t\\) has main vertices \\([t]\\) forming a clique. For each \\(i<j\\) and \\(\\ell\\in[t]\\setminus\\{i,j\\}\\), it has a helper \\(h_{ij;\\ell}\\) adjacent precisely to \\(i,j,\\ell\\).","sourceOffset":14455,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/133.md","scopeText":"The formalization gives the paper's parity-lift graph construction for every Weisfeiler–Leman dimension $k\\ge4$, under both joint and separate conventions. From a finite choice system it constructs two equal-size uncolored graphs that are Weisfeiler–Leman equivalent exactly when the system has no successful compatible choice. The same equivalence holds for the pure variant.\n\nA successful choice is detected by the specified early round: round two in the joint convention and round one in the separate convention. The graph-size bound is linear in the choice-system size with a factor depending on $k$. The paper's conditional running-time exclusions are outside these selected construction statements.\n\nThe formalization proves that deciding whether Weisfeiler–Leman refinement of a supplied dimen","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/133.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":51860,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Parity-lifts-and-bounded-treewidth-witnesses-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"133-02","slug":"The-complexity-of-identifying-a-graph-by-Weisfeiler-Leman-refinement-September-25-2026","familyId":"133","title":"The complexity of identifying a graph by Weisfeiler–Leman refinement","abstract":"We prove that deciding whether Weisfeiler–Leman refinement of an input dimension identifies a given graph is EXPTIME-complete. The input is a nonempty finite simple uncolored graph in adjacency-matrix form and a positive binary-encoded dimension. Identification quantifies over every comparison graph.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves unconditional $n^{\\Omega(k)}$ deterministic time lower bounds for joint and separate k-dimensional Weisfeiler–Leman equivalence, for sufficiently large fixed k in the specified sequential adjacency-matrix models. With dimension as input, joint equivalence is EXPTIME-complete even on subcubic graphs; deciding whether refinement identifies a graph is also EXPTIME-complete.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-complexity-of-identifying-a-graph-by-Weisfeiler-Leman-refinement-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-complexity-of-identifying-a-graph-by-Weisfeiler-Leman-refinement-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-complexity-of-identifying-a-graph-by-Weisfeiler-Leman-refinement-September-25-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/The-complexity-of-identifying-a-graph-by-Weisfeiler-Leman-refinement-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-complexity-of-identifying-a-graph-by-Weisfeiler-Leman-refinement-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-complexity-of-identifying-a-graph-by-Weisfeiler-Leman-refinement-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-wl.tex","preprints/The-complexity-of-identifying-a-graph-by-Weisfeiler-Leman-refinement-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-encoding.tex","preprints/The-complexity-of-identifying-a-graph-by-Weisfeiler-Leman-refinement-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-offsets.tex","preprints/The-complexity-of-identifying-a-graph-by-Weisfeiler-Leman-refinement-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-projection.tex","preprints/The-complexity-of-identifying-a-graph-by-Weisfeiler-Leman-refinement-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-computation.tex","preprints/The-complexity-of-identifying-a-graph-by-Weisfeiler-Leman-refinement-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-identification.tex","preprints/The-complexity-of-identifying-a-graph-by-Weisfeiler-Leman-refinement-September-25-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-complexity-of-identifying-a-graph-by-Weisfeiler-Leman-refinement-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that deciding whether Weisfeiler--Leman refinement of an input dimension identifies a given graph is EXPTIME-complete. The input is a nonempty finite simple uncolored graph in adjacency-matrix form and a positive binary-encoded dimension. Identification quantifies over every comparison graph.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":401},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and contribution","sourceOffset":2976},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof outline","sourceOffset":6232},{"kind":"section","title":"Weisfeiler--Leman refinement and the bijective game","sourceOffset":9116},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The game characterization","sourceOffset":10839},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An exponential-time decision procedure","sourceOffset":17491},{"kind":"section","title":"Encoding comparisons by locally reconstructible graphs","sourceOffset":19549},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparisons and subdivided wires","sourceOffset":20299},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Readouts and blocks","sourceOffset":22497},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The uncolored graph","sourceOffset":26525},{"kind":"section","title":"Wire comparisons and global offsets","sourceOffset":33593},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Wire shifts and image sets","sourceOffset":34059}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"The following problem is EXPTIME-complete under deterministic polynomial-time many-one reductions: given a nonempty finite simple undirected uncolored graph $G$ by its adjacency matrix and a positive integer $k$ in binary, decide whether $\\WLdim(G)\\le k$. Here dimension one uses ordinary neighbor color refinement, and dimensions at least two use the joint-update rule described above.","sourceOffset":2333,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:upper","text":"For a nonempty graph given by its binary adjacency matrix and a positive integer $k$ in binary, deciding whether its Weisfeiler--Leman dimension is at most $k$ belongs to EXPTIME.","sourceOffset":17544,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"eq:row-sum","text":"For a nonempty product $A=\\prod_{i=1}^r A_i$ and a vector space $V$ over $\\F$, a function $h:A\\to V$ has row-sum form if there are functions $h_i:A_i\\times A_{i+1}\\to V$ such that \\begin{equation} h(a)=\\sum_{i=1}^r h_i(a_i,a_{i+1}). \\end{equation} Indices are cyclic, so $A_{r+1}=A_1$ and $a_{r+1}=a_1$.","sourceOffset":39307,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/133.md","scopeText":"The formalization gives the paper's parity-lift graph construction for every Weisfeiler–Leman dimension $k\\ge4$, under both joint and separate conventions. From a finite choice system it constructs two equal-size uncolored graphs that are Weisfeiler–Leman equivalent exactly when the system has no successful compatible choice. The same equivalence holds for the pure variant.\n\nA successful choice is detected by the specified early round: round two in the joint convention and round one in the separate convention. The graph-size bound is linear in the choice-system size with a factor depending on $k$. The paper's conditional running-time exclusions are outside these selected construction statements.\n\nThe formalization proves that deciding whether Weisfeiler–Leman refinement of a supplied dimen","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/133.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":92080,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-complexity-of-identifying-a-graph-by-Weisfeiler-Leman-refinement-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"133-03","slug":"Unconditional-time-lower-bounds-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026","familyId":"133","title":"Unconditional time lower bounds for Weisfeiler–Leman equivalence","abstract":"For every sufficiently large fixed k, deciding whether two n-vertex graphs are k-Weisfeiler–Leman equivalent requires $n^{\\Omega(k)}$ deterministic sequential time in the worst case. The bound holds at every sufficiently large graph order, even for simple connected uncolored graphs of diameter at most two, without a complexity assumption. Inputs are explicit adjacency matrices; the models are multitape Turing machines and sequential logarithmic-word RAMs with fixed polynomial-bit-time instructions. Both joint and separate replacement conventions are covered.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves unconditional $n^{\\Omega(k)}$ deterministic time lower bounds for joint and separate k-dimensional Weisfeiler–Leman equivalence, for sufficiently large fixed k in the specified sequential adjacency-matrix models. With dimension as input, joint equivalence is EXPTIME-complete even on subcubic graphs; deciding whether refinement identifies a graph is also EXPTIME-complete.","pdfPath":"preprints/Unconditional-time-lower-bounds-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Unconditional-time-lower-bounds-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Unconditional-time-lower-bounds-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Unconditional-time-lower-bounds-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Unconditional-time-lower-bounds-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-game.tex","preprints/Unconditional-time-lower-bounds-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-compression.tex","preprints/Unconditional-time-lower-bounds-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-circuits.tex","preprints/Unconditional-time-lower-bounds-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-diagonal.tex","preprints/Unconditional-time-lower-bounds-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-transfer.tex","preprints/Unconditional-time-lower-bounds-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Unconditional-time-lower-bounds-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every sufficiently large fixed $k$, deciding whether two $n$-vertex graphs are $k$-Weisfeiler--Leman equivalent requires $n^{\\Omega(k)}$ deterministic sequential time in the worst case. The bound holds at every sufficiently large graph order, even for simple connected uncolored graphs of diameter at most two, without a complexity assumption. Inputs are explicit adjacency matrices; the models are multitape Turing machines and sequential logarithmic-word RAMs with fixed polynomial-bit-time instructions. Both joint and separate replacement conventions are covered.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":618},{"kind":"section","title":"Weisfeiler--Leman conventions and the bijective game","sourceOffset":12098},{"kind":"section","title":"A compressed graph representation of arc consistency","sourceOffset":21567},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The consistency instance","sourceOffset":22464},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Subdivided types and local blocks","sourceOffset":25800},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reading a comparison","sourceOffset":30916},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Corner graphs and wrapping","sourceOffset":33088},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compatible families of offsets","sourceOffset":36835},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uncolored graphs, padding, and size","sourceOffset":48009},{"kind":"section","title":"Encoding computations by short consistency instances","sourceOffset":51228},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A directed circuit simulation","sourceOffset":51618},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Size and effective construction","sourceOffset":57730}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are absolute constants $c>0$ and $k_0$ with the following property. For every fixed integer $k\\ge k_0$ and every correct deterministic decider $A$ for $k$-Weisfeiler--Leman equivalence, there is an integer $n_0=n_0(A,k)$ such that \\[ T_A(n)\\ge n^{ck} \\qquad\\text{for every integer }n\\ge n_0. \\] This holds for multitape Turing machines and for sequential random-access machines with $O(\\log(n+2))$-bit addresses and words, boundedly many memory accesses per instruction, and fixed instructions computable in time polynomial in the word length. The same bound holds if $A$ is required to decide correctly only on pairs of simple connected uncolored graphs of diameter at most two, with $T_A(n)$ maximized over that class. It applies both to the joint replacement-multiset convention for $k$-dimensional refinement and to the separate-multiset convention defined below.","sourceOffset":2332,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Compressed consistency reduction","label":"comp:reduction","text":"For fixed $r$ and fixed description shape, an instance as above determines two equally large simple uncolored connected graphs $G^0,G^1$, of diameter at most two and order \\[ O_{r,\\mathrm{shape}}(m^6), \\] such that Duplicator wins the $K$-pair bijective game on $G^0,G^1$ if and only if the instance has individual-edge arc consistency. The construction uses the bounded scalar-form data together with the one-coordinate tables, and enumerates $O_{r,\\mathrm{shape}}(m^2)$ blocks without enumerating all full addresses. An arbitrary equal number of additional vertices can be added to the two graphs while preserving the equivalence, connectedness, and diameter bound.","sourceOffset":24801,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"The two refinement conventions","label":"game:conventions","text":"Let $k\\geq 2$. The initial color $\\chi_0^X(\\bar v)$ records the atomic type of $\\bar v$ in $X$: all coordinate equalities, adjacencies, and vertex colors. The joint convention updates by \\[ \\chi_{t+1}^X(\\bar v)= \\left(\\chi_t^X(\\bar v), \\left\\{\\!\\left\\{ \\bigl(\\chi_t^X(\\bar v[i\\leftarrow z])\\bigr)_{i=1}^k :z\\in V(X) \\right\\}\\!\\right\\}\\right). \\] The separate convention instead records the ordered list \\[ \\left( \\left\\{\\!\\left\\{ \\chi_t^X(\\bar v[i\\leftarrow z]):z\\in V(X) \\right\\}\\!\\right\\}\\right)_{i=1}^k \\] along with the old color. In either convention, the same recursive color names are used for","sourceOffset":12788,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Bijective game","label":"game:definition","text":"The bijective game with $K$ pairs is played on $G,H$ as follows. Spoiler may remove any marked pairs and choose a vacant pair. Duplicator then announces a bijection $f:V(G)\\to V(H)$, after which Spoiler chooses $z\\in V(G)$ and marks $z,f(z)$ with that pair. Duplicator loses as soon as the marked correspondence fails to preserve equality, adjacency, or vertex colors. A winning strategy for Duplicator must survive every finite continuation, and hence every infinite play. If the graph orders differ, Duplicator cannot announce a bijection and loses.","sourceOffset":14586,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/133.md","scopeText":"The formalization gives the paper's parity-lift graph construction for every Weisfeiler–Leman dimension $k\\ge4$, under both joint and separate conventions. From a finite choice system it constructs two equal-size uncolored graphs that are Weisfeiler–Leman equivalent exactly when the system has no successful compatible choice. The same equivalence holds for the pure variant.\n\nA successful choice is detected by the specified early round: round two in the joint convention and round one in the separate convention. The graph-size bound is linear in the choice-system size with a factor depending on $k$. The paper's conditional running-time exclusions are outside these selected construction statements.\n\nThe formalization proves that deciding whether Weisfeiler–Leman refinement of a supplied dimen","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/133.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":101219,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Unconditional-time-lower-bounds-for-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"133-04","slug":"Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026","familyId":"133","title":"Variable-dimension Weisfeiler–Leman equivalence on general and subcubic graphs","abstract":"Deciding joint-update k-dimensional Weisfeiler–Leman equivalence is $\\mathsf{EXPTIME}$-complete when the two graphs are given by explicit adjacency matrices and k ≥ 2 is encoded in binary. The result holds even for connected simple uncolored graphs of equal positive order and maximum degree at most three.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves unconditional $n^{\\Omega(k)}$ deterministic time lower bounds for joint and separate k-dimensional Weisfeiler–Leman equivalence, for sufficiently large fixed k in the specified sequential adjacency-matrix models. With dimension as input, joint equivalence is EXPTIME-complete even on subcubic graphs; deciding whether refinement identifies a graph is also EXPTIME-complete.","pdfPath":"preprints/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","preprints/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-refinement-game.tex","preprints/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-computation-images.tex","preprints/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-blocks-projection.tex","preprints/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026/build/figures/affine-square.tex","preprints/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026/build/figures/potential-adjustment.tex","preprints/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-general-realization.tex","preprints/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-game-reduction.tex","preprints/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026/build/figures/ring-transfer.tex","preprints/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-subcubic-realization.tex","preprints/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026/build/figures/readout-route.tex","preprints/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026/build/figures/sort-gadget.tex","preprints/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026/build/sections/08-uniformity.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Deciding joint-update \\(k\\)-dimensional Weisfeiler--Leman equivalence is \\(\\EXPTIME\\)-complete when the two graphs are given by explicit adjacency matrices and \\(k\\ge2\\) is encoded in binary. The result holds even for connected simple uncolored graphs of equal positive order and maximum degree at most three.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":387},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the role of the degree restriction","sourceOffset":2694},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the reduction uses the bijective game","sourceOffset":5390},{"kind":"section","title":"Joint refinement and its bijective game","sourceOffset":13194},{"kind":"section","title":"Uniform computation and scalar-image constraints","sourceOffset":25104},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Nonempty sets and individual scalar images","sourceOffset":36636},{"kind":"section","title":"Affine blocks and exact projection","sourceOffset":47342},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Subdivided comparison wires","sourceOffset":48447},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Readouts, blocks, and affine fibers","sourceOffset":50704},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The extension target and a site-ring model","sourceOffset":55098},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Small supports and recovered addresses","sourceOffset":59577},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Admissible shifts and corner potentials","sourceOffset":65716}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Both \\(\\WL\\) and \\(\\SubWL\\) are \\(\\EXPTIME\\)-complete under deterministic polynomial-time many-one reductions.","sourceOffset":1869,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Uniform upper bound","label":"prop:membership","text":"Both \\(\\WL\\) and \\(\\SubWL\\) belong to \\(\\EXPTIME\\). More precisely, after checking their respective input conditions, an input of common graph order \\(n\\ge2\\) and total bit length \\(L\\) can be decided in time $2^{O(n\\log n)}L^{O(1)}$, with absolute constants in the exponents.","sourceOffset":16019,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Joint-update Weisfeiler--Leman refinement","label":"def:joint-wl","text":"Let $X$ be a finite simple graph and let $k\\geq 2$. For $\\mathbf v=(v_1,\\ldots,v_k)\\in V(X)^k$, write $\\mathbf v[i\\leftarrow z]$ for the tuple obtained by replacing its $i$th coordinate by $z$. The initial color $\\chi_0^X(\\mathbf v)$ records, for every ordered pair of coordinates, whether their entries are equal and whether they are adjacent. Recursively, set \\begin{equation} \\chi_{s+1}^X(\\mathbf v)= \\left(\\chi_s^X(\\mathbf v), \\left\\{\\!\\!\\left\\{ \\bigl(\\chi_s^X(\\mathbf v[1\\leftarrow z]),\\ldots, \\chi_s^X(\\mathbf v[k\\leftarrow z])\\bigr):z\\in V(X) \\right\\}\\!\\!\\right\\}\\right). \\end{equation} The br","sourceOffset":13601,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Product description","label":"def:computation-product","text":"For an address length $r$, every gate type has one gate at each address in $\\{0,1\\}^r$. A connection schema specifies source and destination types, a destination port, nonempty coordinate domains $A_i\\subseteq\\{0,1\\}$, and injections $\\sigma_i:A_i\\longrightarrow\\{0,1\\}$, for $1\\le i\\le r$. It describes the connections \\[ (\\text{source type},\\mathbf a) \\longrightarrow (\\text{destination type},\\boldsymbol\\sigma(\\mathbf a)), \\qquad \\mathbf a\\in\\prod_{i=1}^r A_i, \\] at the specified port, where $\\boldsymbol\\sigma(\\mathbf a)=(\\sigma_i(a_i))_{i=1}^r$. A seed or test request specifies a scalar type a","sourceOffset":26264,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/133.md","scopeText":"The formalization gives the paper's parity-lift graph construction for every Weisfeiler–Leman dimension $k\\ge4$, under both joint and separate conventions. From a finite choice system it constructs two equal-size uncolored graphs that are Weisfeiler–Leman equivalent exactly when the system has no successful compatible choice. The same equivalence holds for the pure variant.\n\nA successful choice is detected by the specified early round: round two in the joint convention and round one in the separate convention. The graph-size bound is linear in the choice-system size with a factor depending on $k$. The paper's conditional running-time exclusions are outside these selected construction statements.\n\nThe formalization proves that deciding whether Weisfeiler–Leman refinement of a supplied dimen","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/133.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":143585,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Variable-dimension-Weisfeiler-Leman-equivalence-on-general-and-subcubic-graphs-September-25-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"134-01","slug":"Finite-Monoid-Computations-and-a-Uniform-Generalized-Star-Height-Bound-September-25-2026","familyId":"134","title":"Finite Monoid Computations and a Uniform Generalized Star-Height Bound","abstract":"Every regular language over a finite alphabet has a generalized regular expression of star height at most thirteen over that same alphabet. We prove this uniform bound by representing finite monoid computations as affine updates and recovering them through twelve successive split constructions.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Every regular language over a finite alphabet has a generalized regular expression with at most three nested Kleene stars, allowing union, concatenation and complement over the same alphabet. This establishes an absolute bound independent of automaton size, resolving the uniform-boundedness version of the generalized star-height problem.","pdfPath":"preprints/Finite-Monoid-Computations-and-a-Uniform-Generalized-Star-Height-Bound-September-25-2026/Finite-Monoid-Computations-and-a-Uniform-Generalized-Star-Height-Bound-September-25-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-Monoid-Computations-and-a-Uniform-Generalized-Star-Height-Bound-September-25-2026/Finite-Monoid-Computations-and-a-Uniform-Generalized-Star-Height-Bound-September-25-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-Monoid-Computations-and-a-Uniform-Generalized-Star-Height-Bound-September-25-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Finite-Monoid-Computations-and-a-Uniform-Generalized-Star-Height-Bound-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Finite-Monoid-Computations-and-a-Uniform-Generalized-Star-Height-Bound-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Finite-Monoid-Computations-and-a-Uniform-Generalized-Star-Height-Bound-September-25-2026/build/sections/algebra.tex","preprints/Finite-Monoid-Computations-and-a-Uniform-Generalized-Star-Height-Bound-September-25-2026/build/sections/clock.tex","preprints/Finite-Monoid-Computations-and-a-Uniform-Generalized-Star-Height-Bound-September-25-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Finite-Monoid-Computations-and-a-Uniform-Generalized-Star-Height-Bound-September-25-2026/build/sections/outer.tex","preprints/Finite-Monoid-Computations-and-a-Uniform-Generalized-Star-Height-Bound-September-25-2026/build/sections/aligned.tex","preprints/Finite-Monoid-Computations-and-a-Uniform-Generalized-Star-Height-Bound-September-25-2026/build/sections/parameters.tex","preprints/Finite-Monoid-Computations-and-a-Uniform-Generalized-Star-Height-Bound-September-25-2026/build/sections/compilation.tex","preprints/Finite-Monoid-Computations-and-a-Uniform-Generalized-Star-Height-Bound-September-25-2026/build/references/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Finite-Monoid-Computations-and-a-Uniform-Generalized-Star-Height-Bound-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every regular language over a finite alphabet has a generalized regular expression of star height at most thirteen over that same alphabet. We prove this uniform bound by representing finite monoid computations as affine updates and recovering them through twelve successive split constructions.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":341},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical and algebraic context","sourceOffset":3207},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The mechanism of the proof","sourceOffset":6346},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite computations and split expressions","sourceOffset":10805},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Words, monoids, and graph tests","sourceOffset":11448},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The split identity","sourceOffset":14110},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Affine corrections and linear recovery","sourceOffset":16861},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prediction trials","sourceOffset":18107},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite tag clock","sourceOffset":20942},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Interpretation at a cut","sourceOffset":38242},{"kind":"section","title":"Local markers and episode ends","sourceOffset":40770},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An elementary local marker rule","sourceOffset":41669}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every finite alphabet \\(\\Sigma\\) and every regular language \\(L\\subseteq\\Sigma^*\\), \\[ h_\\Sigma(L)\\le 13. \\]","sourceOffset":1988,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:parameters","text":"For every fixed nonempty finite alphabet, finite monoid \\(M\\), and finite representation space \\(V\\), all parameters in the two outer splits and the five aligned stages can be chosen to satisfy their stated hypotheses. More precisely, the stencils are nonempty, have more than \\(C_i\\) copies of each required kind, and satisfy \\[ \\#\\{(p,p')\\in\\mathcal P_i^2:p\\ne p',\\ A_i^{\\Gamma_i}(t+p-p',q)\\}\\le C_i \\qquad(0\\le i\\le4) \\] on every complete episode. Write \\(N_i=|\\mathcal P_i|\\). In order, these bounds concern first-clock or prediction failure, inner-search instability, second-clock failure, a right-margin marker, and end-search instability. The constants can be taken to be \\begin{align} C_0&=K+c_{\\rm cl}\\log(2m_1),\\\\ C_1&=10|\\mathcal Z|N_0^4(2R_1+3),\\\\ C_2&=N_0^4N_1^4c_{\\rm cl}\\log(2m_2),\\\\ C_3&=10|\\mathcal Z|(2R_2+3)\\prod_{j<3}N_j^4, \\\\ C_4&=10(2w_E+3)\\prod_{j<4}N_j^4. \\end{align} Here \\(K=|M|^{|V|^2+1}\\), \\(m_1,m_2\\) are the respective full tag alphabet sizes, and \\(c_{\\rm cl}\\) is the absolute constant of Lemma \\ref{lem:clock}; logarithms are natural. Also, \\(\\operatorname{diam}\\mathcal P_i<w_E\\) for \\(i<4\\). All measurements, trials, and inspections have the chronological and reading-side access required in Lemmas \\ref{lem:main-split}, \\ref{lem:periodic-split}, and \\ref{lem:aligned-stage}. Every bounded control, including every control at a false candidate origin, lies inside the true first episode. The same choices satisfy the end margins of Section \\ref{sec:geometry}.","sourceOffset":103814,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:eligibility","text":"Block \\(j\\) is ideally eligible if \\[ x_jT_*=y_j,\\qquad T_{z_{i-1},z_i}=p_i\\ (1\\le i<j),\\qquad T_{z_j,C}=p_{j+1}\\cdots p_{J+1}. \\] A trial is perfectly predicted if every interval product is its corresponding \\(p_i\\).","sourceOffset":18589,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Complete episodes","label":"def:episodes","text":"Fix a positive integer $B_{\\rm tail}$. With all required bounded data present, the anchor at origin $t$ is defined as follows: \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\roman*)] \\item if $\\eta(t)\\ne\\bot$, the anchor is $q=\\eta(t)$; \\item if $\\eta(t)=\\bot$, the anchor is the first letter position $q\\ge z_\\bullet(t)+K_\\bullet$ whose letter differs from $\\pi_t(q)$. If no such letter is present, no anchor has yet been found. \\end{enumerate} An episode beginning at $s$ ends exactly at $q+B_{\\rm tail}$. The language $E$ consists of the nonempty words which, with $s=0$, contain the required search and seed data, hav","sourceOffset":51038,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/134.md","scopeText":"Generalized star height measures the nesting of Kleene stars in regular expressions that also allow Boolean operations. The formalization proves that every regular language over a finite alphabet has a generalized expression over the same alphabet of star height at most three. This is stronger than the bound of thirteen in the accompanying finite-monoid paper. The selected statement asserts the uniform expression bound, without separately encoding every construction step of that paper.\n\nThe formalization proves that every regular language over a finite alphabet has a generalized regular expression over that same alphabet with star height at most three. Generalized expressions allow Boolean operations as well as concatenation and Kleene star. The bound of three implies the accompanying pape","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/134.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":153461,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Finite-Monoid-Computations-and-a-Uniform-Generalized-Star-Height-Bound-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"134-02","slug":"Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Four-September-25-2026","familyId":"134","title":"Generalized Star Height at Most Four","abstract":"Every regular language over a finite alphabet has generalized star height at most four over that same alphabet. We give a complete construction using an affine correction that hides one interval product, a finite clock, and several scales for moving boundaries through periodic words.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Every regular language over a finite alphabet has a generalized regular expression with at most three nested Kleene stars, allowing union, concatenation and complement over the same alphabet. This establishes an absolute bound independent of automaton size, resolving the uniform-boundedness version of the generalized star-height problem.","pdfPath":"preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Four-September-25-2026/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Four-September-25-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Four-September-25-2026/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Four-September-25-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Four-September-25-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Four-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Four-September-25-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Four-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Four-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-algebra.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Four-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-clock.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Four-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-scales.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Four-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-episodes.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Four-September-25-2026/build/figures/episode-schedule.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Four-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-splits.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Four-September-25-2026/build/figures/proof-stages.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Four-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-compilation.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Four-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every regular language over a finite alphabet has generalized star height at most four over that same alphabet. We give a complete construction using an affine correction that hides one interval product, a finite clock, and several scales for moving boundaries through periodic words.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":330},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The problem and the three constructions","sourceOffset":1794},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From products to three splits","sourceOffset":5353},{"kind":"section","title":"Algebra and split computations","sourceOffset":9509},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Splitting a sequence of episodes","sourceOffset":11421},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Recovering linear parts","sourceOffset":15176},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An affine correction that hides one interval product","sourceOffset":16616},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite tag clock","sourceOffset":22237},{"kind":"section","title":"Markers and several scales of cuts","sourceOffset":33883},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local markers and periodic extensions","sourceOffset":35394},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Algebraic data and the order of the buffers","sourceOffset":38698},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The clock tags and a polynomial roster","sourceOffset":41546}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every finite alphabet $\\Sigma$ and every regular language $L\\subseteq\\Sigma^*$, one has $h_\\Sigma(L)\\le4$.","sourceOffset":1346,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Main split","label":"prop:main-split","text":"The languages $P_x^{\\rm main},Q_y^{\\rm main}$ satisfy the two split hypotheses of Lemma \\ref{lem:split} for the episode update $g_0$, with $D=E$ and $D'=D_0$.","sourceOffset":85347,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:usable","text":"A scale $r$ is usable at the origin $t$ if, at each of its $Z$ centers $z_j$, the following conditions hold: \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\roman*)] \\item The value $u(z_j+h)$ equals $u(z_j)$ for every integer $h$ with $|h|\\leq R_r$, including agreement on absence. \\item If $u(z_j)=\\bot$, then either $\\sigma\\leq S_r$, or the inclusive interval $[z_j-R_r,z_j+R_r]$ contains no point of $x+\\sigma\\mathbb Z$. \\end{enumerate} Here $d,\\sigma,x$ refer to the periodic extension at the indicated center. All windows needed by these checks are mandatory.","sourceOffset":50964,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":null,"text":"Let $E$ consist of the nonempty words starting at $s$ for which the required data are available, the anchor rule defines $q$, and the word ends exactly at $q+b_{\\rm tail}$. The rule is interpreted using the placement just fixed. Since all tests translate with the letters, it does not depend on an ambient coordinate origin.","sourceOffset":73778,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/134.md","scopeText":"Generalized star height measures the nesting of Kleene stars in regular expressions that also allow Boolean operations. The formalization proves that every regular language over a finite alphabet has a generalized expression over the same alphabet of star height at most three. This is stronger than the bound of thirteen in the accompanying finite-monoid paper. The selected statement asserts the uniform expression bound, without separately encoding every construction step of that paper.\n\nThe formalization proves that every regular language over a finite alphabet has a generalized regular expression over that same alphabet with star height at most three. Generalized expressions allow Boolean operations as well as concatenation and Kleene star. The bound of three implies the accompanying pape","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/134.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":116241,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Four-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"134-03","slug":"Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026","familyId":"134","title":"Generalized Star Height at Most Three","abstract":"Every regular language over a finite alphabet has generalized star height at most three, with complement taken in the same free monoid. We express finite-monoid computations using a prefix code of word pieces.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Every regular language over a finite alphabet has a generalized regular expression with at most three nested Kleene stars, allowing union, concatenation and complement over the same alphabet. This establishes an absolute bound independent of automaton size, resolving the uniform-boundedness version of the generalized star-height problem.","pdfPath":"preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026/article.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026/article.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026/build/sections/algebra.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026/build/sections/clock.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026/build/sections/markers.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026/build/sections/schedule.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026/build/sections/episodes.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026/build/figures/episode-schedule.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026/build/sections/outer-split.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026/build/sections/decoder.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026/build/figures/suffix-decoder.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026/build/sections/inner-split.tex","preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026/build/sections/compilation.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every regular language over a finite alphabet has generalized star height at most three, with complement taken in the same free monoid. We express finite-monoid computations using a prefix code of word pieces.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":272},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The problem and its mathematical context","sourceOffset":1949},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Computing a word value with independent tests","sourceOffset":5291},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why two splits suffice","sourceOffset":7087},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From finite tests to height-three expressions","sourceOffset":10903},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite computations and split expressions","sourceOffset":12809},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Monoid values and deterministic updates","sourceOffset":13425},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The split identity","sourceOffset":16239},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removing affine corrections","sourceOffset":20307},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A shift table that hides one entry","sourceOffset":22989},{"kind":"section","title":"A robust tag clock","sourceOffset":28839},{"kind":"section","title":"Local markers and periodic continuations","sourceOffset":44234}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every finite alphabet $\\Sigma$ and every regular language $L\\subseteq\\Sigma^*$, \\[ h_\\Sigma(L)\\le3. \\]","sourceOffset":1527,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"First split","label":"outer:split","text":"For the total updates $F_e$ above and $D\\subseteq E$ from \\eqref{outer:exceptional-domain}, the languages $P_x,Q_y$ have the following properties. \\begin{enumerate} \\item On any word in $E^*$, a match of $P_xQ_y$ beginning at the beginning of the word consumes exactly its first episode $e$ and satisfies $F_e(x)=y$. \\item For every $e\\in E\\setminus D$ and every $x\\in V$, some cut of $e$ gives a match of $P_xQ_{F_e(x)}$. \\end{enumerate} There is no match on the empty word.","sourceOffset":88119,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/134.md","scopeText":"Generalized star height measures the nesting of Kleene stars in regular expressions that also allow Boolean operations. The formalization proves that every regular language over a finite alphabet has a generalized expression over the same alphabet of star height at most three. This is stronger than the bound of thirteen in the accompanying finite-monoid paper. The selected statement asserts the uniform expression bound, without separately encoding every construction step of that paper.\n\nThe formalization proves that every regular language over a finite alphabet has a generalized regular expression over that same alphabet with star height at most three. Generalized expressions allow Boolean operations as well as concatenation and Kleene star. The bound of three implies the accompanying pape","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/134.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":135097,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Generalized-Star-Height-at-Most-Three-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"135-01","slug":"Homogeneous-depth-five-lower-bounds-for-iterated-matrix-multiplication-September-25-2026","familyId":"135","title":"Homogeneous depth-five lower bounds for iterated matrix multiplication","abstract":"Let $\\mathop{\\mathrm{IMM}}\\nolimits _{n,n}$ be the $(1,1)$ entry of a product of n independent $n\\times n$ matrices of variables. Over every field of characteristic zero, every syntactically homogeneous $\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma$ circuit computing $\\mathop{\\mathrm{IMM}}\\nolimits _{n,n}$ has at least $n^{\\sqrt n/400}$ gates for all sufficiently large n, with an absolute threshold independent of the field. Bottom linear forms may have arbitrary support, and arbitrary finite fan-in, fan-out, and gate sharing are allowed. Over every field, a block expansion gives such circuits with at most $n^{\\sqrt n+4}$ gates for n ≥ 2. Thus the gate complexity over characteristic-zero fields is $n^{\\Theta(\\sqrt n)}$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Over every characteristic-zero field, the $(1,1)$ entry of a product of n independent $n\\times n$ variable matrices requires $n^{\\Theta(\\sqrt n)}$ gates in homogeneous depth-five sum–product circuits. This sharp bound allows shared gates and bottom linear forms involving all variables.","pdfPath":"preprints/Homogeneous-depth-five-lower-bounds-for-iterated-matrix-multiplication-September-25-2026/Homogeneous-depth-five-lower-bounds-for-iterated-matrix-multiplication-September-25-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Homogeneous-depth-five-lower-bounds-for-iterated-matrix-multiplication-September-25-2026/Homogeneous-depth-five-lower-bounds-for-iterated-matrix-multiplication-September-25-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Homogeneous-depth-five-lower-bounds-for-iterated-matrix-multiplication-September-25-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Homogeneous-depth-five-lower-bounds-for-iterated-matrix-multiplication-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Homogeneous-depth-five-lower-bounds-for-iterated-matrix-multiplication-September-25-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/Homogeneous-depth-five-lower-bounds-for-iterated-matrix-multiplication-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Homogeneous-depth-five-lower-bounds-for-iterated-matrix-multiplication-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-rank.tex","preprints/Homogeneous-depth-five-lower-bounds-for-iterated-matrix-multiplication-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-circuits.tex","preprints/Homogeneous-depth-five-lower-bounds-for-iterated-matrix-multiplication-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-moments.tex","preprints/Homogeneous-depth-five-lower-bounds-for-iterated-matrix-multiplication-September-25-2026/build/figures/pairings.tex","preprints/Homogeneous-depth-five-lower-bounds-for-iterated-matrix-multiplication-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-paths.tex","preprints/Homogeneous-depth-five-lower-bounds-for-iterated-matrix-multiplication-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-completion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Homogeneous-depth-five-lower-bounds-for-iterated-matrix-multiplication-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let $\\IMM_{n,n}$ be the $(1,1)$ entry of a product of $n$ independent $n\\times n$ matrices of variables. Over every field of characteristic zero, every syntactically homogeneous $\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma$ circuit computing $\\IMM_{n,n}$ has at least $n^{\\sqrt n/400}$ gates for all sufficiently large $n$, with an absolute threshold independent of the field. Bottom linear forms may have arbitrary support, and arbitrary finite fan-in, fan-out, and gate sharing are allowed. Over every field, a block expansion gives such circuits with at most $n^{\\sqrt n+4}$ gates for $n\\geq2$. Thus the gate complexity over characteristic-zero fields is $n^{\\Theta(\\sqrt n)}$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":724},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Circuit model and main result","sourceOffset":1538},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and previous bounds","sourceOffset":4272},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":9814},{"kind":"section","title":"A rank measure from differentiation and multiplication","sourceOffset":13738},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The operator and its homogeneous spaces","sourceOffset":14147},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Choosing the degrees","sourceOffset":15712},{"kind":"section","title":"The rank of a homogeneous depth-five circuit","sourceOffset":19478},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Products and intermediate dimensions","sourceOffset":20601},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The cost of integer degrees","sourceOffset":23573},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From gates to products","sourceOffset":26544},{"kind":"section","title":"Trace moments for iterated matrix multiplication","sourceOffset":30571}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist absolute constants $c>0$ and $n_0$ such that, for every integer $n\\geq n_0$, every syntactically homogeneous $\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma$ circuit over $\\C$ computing $\\IMM_{n,n}$ has size at least \\[ n^{c\\sqrt n}. \\] One may take $c=1/400$.","sourceOffset":3363,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:circuit","text":"There are absolute constants $C>0$ and $n_0$ with the following property. For $n\\ge n_0$, use the parameters in \\eqref{eq:parameters} and any partition of the matrix layers with $k$ layers in $V$ and $m$ layers in $U$. Let $K$ be a field, and let $g\\in K[V,U]$ be a homogeneous degree-$n$ polynomial computed by a syntactically homogeneous $\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma$ circuit of size $S$. Then \\[ R(g)\\le 2S^2D\\exp(C\\sqrt n)\\,n^{-\\sqrt n/100}. \\]","sourceOffset":19886,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/135.md","scopeText":"Let $\\mathrm{IMM}_{n,n}$ be the $(1,1)$ entry of the product of $n$ independent $n\\times n$ variable matrices. The formalization proves that, over every characteristic-zero field, syntactically homogeneous depth-five $\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma\\Pi\\Sigma$ circuits computing this polynomial require at least $n^{\\sqrt n/400}$ gates for all sufficiently large $n$, with a threshold independent of the field. Arbitrary bottom support, finite fan-in and fan-out, and sharing are allowed.\n\nIt also gives, over every field and for $n\\ge2$, circuits with at most $n^{\\sqrt n+4}$ gates. These are the lower and upper bounds selected from the paper.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/135.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":59961,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Homogeneous-depth-five-lower-bounds-for-iterated-matrix-multiplication-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"136-01","slug":"The-PCP-for-PPAD-conjecture-a-quasilinear-reduction-September-25-2026","familyId":"136","title":"The PCP-for-PPAD conjecture: a quasilinear reduction","abstract":"We prove the quasilinear-size PCP-for-PPAD conjecture of Babichenko, Papadimitriou, and Rubinstein. There are fixed positive rational constants ε and δ and a deterministic polynomial-time reduction that transforms an End-of-Line instance of binary length N into a generalized circuit of total binary length $N(\\log N)^{O(1)}$. From any rational assignment of polynomial encoding length that ε-satisfies all but a δ fraction of the gates, a solution to the original End-of-Line instance can be recovered in polynomial time, regardless of which gates fail. Such assignments always exist, with one fixed polynomial bound on their encoding length.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the quasilinear PCP-for-PPAD conjecture. An End-of-Line instance of length N reduces to numerical circuit constraints of total length $N(\\log N)^{O(1)}$ such that any polynomially encoded rational assignment satisfying all but a fixed fraction to fixed accuracy yields an endpoint solution. Such assignments always exist, giving robust local verification with only quasilinear size overhead.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-PCP-for-PPAD-conjecture-a-quasilinear-reduction-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-PCP-for-PPAD-conjecture-a-quasilinear-reduction-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-PCP-for-PPAD-conjecture-a-quasilinear-reduction-September-25-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/The-PCP-for-PPAD-conjecture-a-quasilinear-reduction-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-PCP-for-PPAD-conjecture-a-quasilinear-reduction-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-PCP-for-PPAD-conjecture-a-quasilinear-reduction-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-macrograph.tex","preprints/The-PCP-for-PPAD-conjecture-a-quasilinear-reduction-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-outer-codes.tex","preprints/The-PCP-for-PPAD-conjecture-a-quasilinear-reduction-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-outer-programs.tex","preprints/The-PCP-for-PPAD-conjecture-a-quasilinear-reduction-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-outer-representation.tex","preprints/The-PCP-for-PPAD-conjecture-a-quasilinear-reduction-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-outer-composition.tex","preprints/The-PCP-for-PPAD-conjecture-a-quasilinear-reduction-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-inner-algebra.tex","preprints/The-PCP-for-PPAD-conjecture-a-quasilinear-reduction-September-25-2026/build/sections/08-inner-binary.tex","preprints/The-PCP-for-PPAD-conjecture-a-quasilinear-reduction-September-25-2026/build/sections/09-inner-gadget.tex","preprints/The-PCP-for-PPAD-conjecture-a-quasilinear-reduction-September-25-2026/build/sections/10-geometry.tex","preprints/The-PCP-for-PPAD-conjecture-a-quasilinear-reduction-September-25-2026/build/sections/11-weak-nash.tex","preprints/The-PCP-for-PPAD-conjecture-a-quasilinear-reduction-September-25-2026/build/sections/12-extensions.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-PCP-for-PPAD-conjecture-a-quasilinear-reduction-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the quasilinear-size PCP-for-PPAD conjecture of Babichenko, Papadimitriou, and Rubinstein. There are fixed positive rational constants $\\eps$ and $\\delta$ and a deterministic polynomial-time reduction that transforms an End-of-Line instance of binary length $N$ into a generalized circuit of total binary length $N(\\log N)^{O(1)}$. From any rational assignment of polynomial encoding length that $\\eps$-satisfies all but a $\\delta$ fraction of the gates, a solution to the original End-of-Line instance can be recovered in polynomial time, regardless of which gates fail. Such assignments always exist, with one fixed polynomial bound on their encoding length.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction and the exact search relation","sourceOffset":732},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The source and target problems","sourceOffset":4740},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Main theorem","sourceOffset":8489},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy: encoded paths and robust local computation","sourceOffset":10119},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Consequences for games and markets","sourceOffset":21643},{"kind":"section","title":"A sparse macrograph for End-of-Line","sourceOffset":22534},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sparse transcripts and their two orders","sourceOffset":23633},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The cyclic schedule and reciprocal proposals","sourceOffset":29375},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Endpoints, sparse edits, and uniform size","sourceOffset":35754},{"kind":"section","title":"Outer index groups and systematic square codes","sourceOffset":44735},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A group of quasilinear order","sourceOffset":45921},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Common scalar local codes","sourceOffset":54183}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Quasilinear PCP for PPAD","label":"thm:main","text":"There are fixed rational constants \\[ 0<\\eps<\\tfrac1{10},\\qquad 0<\\delta<1, \\] a fixed positive integer $A$, a fixed nonnegative integer $b$, a fixed polynomial $p$ with nonnegative integer coefficients, and deterministic algorithms $R,D$ with the following properties. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item Every generalized circuit in Definition \\ref{def:gcircuit} has an acceptable rational assignment of encoding length at most $p(M)$. \\item For every End-of-Line instance $I$ of length $N$, the output $R(I)$ is a generalized circuit whose complete encoding length satisfies \\[ M\\leq A N\\bigl\\lceil\\log_2(N+2)\\bigr\\rceil^b. \\] \\item The construction $R$ runs in time polynomial in $N$, with one fixed polynomial bound. \\item For every acceptable assignment $x$ of $R(I)$, the algorithm $D(I,x)$ returns a solution of $I$ in time polynomial in $N$ plus the encoding length of $x$, with one fixed polynomial bound. \\end{enumerate} All constants, polynomials, and algorithms are chosen once. They use no advice depending on the input length and no randomized construction.","sourceOffset":8533,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Outer-code interface","label":"thm:outer-codes","text":"There exist fixed constants $d$, $\\delta_1>0$, $C_0<\\infty$, $\\lambda>0$, $\\theta_{\\mathrm{out}}>0$, and $K_{\\mathrm{out}}<\\infty$, with $\\lambda\\le\\min\\{(8C_0)^{-1},\\delta_1/16\\}$, having the following uniform properties for every input length $N$ and its padded tape length $h=O(N)$, after the fixed padding in \\eqref{oc:outer-size}. \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\roman*)] \\item The group $\\Gamma=\\F_2^r\\rtimes H$ has $|\\Gamma|\\le M_or^9=O(N\\operatorname{polylog} N)$, where $M_o=2^r=O(N)$. Its A and B label lists are symmetric, of degree $d$, and have mean-zero norm at most $\\lambda$. There is a field $J=\\F_{p^4}$ with $p>M_o>|H|$, $p\\equiv1\\pmod{M_o}$, and a primitive $M_o$-th root $\\zeta\\in\\F_p$. All public group and field data and the common scalar check matrices are deterministically constructible in polynomial time in $N$, and the data needed at an index have $\\operatorname{polylog}(N)$ size and Boolean computation cost. \\item Each of the scalar and three matrix-representation families, with either axis independently twisted or untwisted as specified in \\eqref{oc:matrix-twists}, has the simultaneous systematic encoder \\eqref{oc:encoder}. Arbitrary systematic rectangle data extend uniquely; the A and B extensions commute. Duplicated payload encoding is injective, linear, and has rate $1/(4d^2)$ in field coordinates. A full slot has $\\operatorname{polylog}(N)$ bits per packed site. Arbitrary finite tuples reuse the same checks and inverses. \\item The local distance and tensor-ag","sourceOffset":87247,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"End-of-Line","label":"def:eol","text":"An instance $I$ consists of two explicitly listed, acyclic, bounded-fanin Boolean circuits \\[ S,P:\\{0,1\\}^n\\longrightarrow\\{0,1\\}^n,\\qquad n\\geq1, \\] using AND, OR, and NOT gates, with unrestricted fan-out, and satisfying \\[ P(0^n)=0^n,\\qquad S(0^n)\\ne0^n,\\qquad P(S(0^n))=0^n. \\] A solution is a string $v\\ne0^n$ such that \\begin{equation} P(S(v))\\ne v\\quad\\text{or}\\quad S(P(v))\\ne v. \\end{equation} The input length $N\\geq2$ includes the complete descriptions of both circuits and their input and output lists.","sourceOffset":5119,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Generalized circuit","label":"def:gcircuit","text":"A generalized circuit $G=(V,T)$ has an explicitly listed finite set of nodes $V$ and a nonempty set of gates $T$. Each gate has one output and at most two inputs. A node is the output of at most one gate. Directed cycles and unrestricted fan-out are permitted. Every gate parameter $\\alpha$ is a binary-encoded rational in $[0,1]$. For an assignment $x:V\\to[0,1]$, write $x_a,x_b$ for input values and $x_z$ for the output. The five arithmetic gate types are $\\eps$-satisfied when the corresponding condition holds: \\begin{align*} \\mathrm{Constant}(\\alpha;z)&:&\\quad \\abs{x_z-\\alpha}&\\leq\\eps,\\\\ \\mat","sourceOffset":5732,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":557966,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":21,"definitionCount":6,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-PCP-for-PPAD-conjecture-a-quasilinear-reduction-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"137-01","slug":"Simulating-One-Tape-Time-in-Two-Fifths-Power-Space-September-25-2026","familyId":"137","title":"Simulating One-Tape Time in Two-Fifths-Power Space","abstract":"We show that a fixed deterministic Turing machine with one writable tape and head can be simulated in $O(T^{2/5}\\mathop{\\mathrm{polylog}}\\nolimits (T+2))$ work-space bits when a binary time cap T ≥ 2 is supplied. The simulator computes the finite-control and halting outcome by time T; its running time is unrestricted. The result allows a fixed number of read-only input heads and requires a fixed accessor that supplies every initial writable and read-only symbol within distance T of the relevant head origin in polylogarithmic space. This improves the square-root space exponent for one-tape machines, answering Williams's question for this model.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Determines the halting and finite-control outcome of a fixed deterministic one-writable-tape machine up to time T using $O(T^{2/5}\\log^C(T+2))$ space, improving the square-root exponent. Heads move at most one cell per step; finitely many read-only input heads are allowed. Initial contents are independent of T, and contents and input symbols have polylogarithmic-space access. Simulation time is unrestricted.","pdfPath":"preprints/Simulating-One-Tape-Time-in-Two-Fifths-Power-Space-September-25-2026/article.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Simulating-One-Tape-Time-in-Two-Fifths-Power-Space-September-25-2026/article.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Simulating-One-Tape-Time-in-Two-Fifths-Power-Space-September-25-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Simulating-One-Tape-Time-in-Two-Fifths-Power-Space-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Simulating-One-Tape-Time-in-Two-Fifths-Power-Space-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Simulating-One-Tape-Time-in-Two-Fifths-Power-Space-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/evaluation.tex","preprints/Simulating-One-Tape-Time-in-Two-Fifths-Power-Space-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/simulation.tex","preprints/Simulating-One-Tape-Time-in-Two-Fifths-Power-Space-September-25-2026/build/source/figures/epoch-path.tex","preprints/Simulating-One-Tape-Time-in-Two-Fifths-Power-Space-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/multitape.tex","preprints/Simulating-One-Tape-Time-in-Two-Fifths-Power-Space-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/hierarchy.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Simulating-One-Tape-Time-in-Two-Fifths-Power-Space-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We show that a fixed deterministic Turing machine with one writable tape and head can be simulated in \\(O(T^{2/5}\\polylog(T+2))\\) work-space bits when a binary time cap \\(T\\ge2\\) is supplied. The simulator computes the finite-control and halting outcome by time \\(T\\); its running time is unrestricted. The result allows a fixed number of read-only input heads and requires a fixed accessor that supplies every initial writable and read-only symbol within distance \\(T\\) of the relevant head origin in polylogarithmic space. This improves the square-root space exponent for one-tape machines, answering Williams's question for this model.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":684},{"kind":"section","title":"An evaluator with weighted dependency paths","sourceOffset":8094},{"kind":"section","title":"Block visits and epoch transcripts","sourceOffset":28286},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A visit accesses one block","sourceOffset":29137},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Histories and checkpoints","sourceOffset":37120},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The weighted dependency path","sourceOffset":41271},{"kind":"subsection","title":"What varying the three scales achieves","sourceOffset":47213},{"kind":"section","title":"A fixed-multitape comparison","sourceOffset":48269},{"kind":"section","title":"Time lower bounds from the space hierarchy","sourceOffset":53826}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Fix a deterministic machine with one writable tape and head, a fixed number of read-only input heads, unit movement and fixed origins for all heads, cap-independent symbol functions, and a fixed accessor as above. For every public input \\(x\\) and supplied binary cap \\(T\\ge2\\) for which the access condition holds, its finite-control and halting outcome by time \\(T\\) can be computed deterministically in \\(\\softO(T^{2/5})\\) work-space bits, excluding storage for the read-only input. Simulation time is unrestricted, and the final writable tape need not be materialized. If the access condition holds for \\((x,T)\\) at every \\(T\\ge2\\), and the machine halts on \\(x\\) after \\(t\\) steps without a supplied running time, its outcome can be computed in \\(\\softO((t+2)^{2/5})\\) work-space bits.","sourceOffset":4262,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:multitape","text":"Fix a deterministic machine with a fixed number of ordinary writable tapes and read-only input heads, unit movement and fixed origins for all heads, cap-independent symbol functions, and a fixed accessor. For every public input \\(x\\) and supplied binary cap \\(T\\ge2\\) satisfying the access condition, its finite-control and halting outcome admits a deterministic simulation in \\(\\softO(\\sqrt T)\\) work-space bits, excluding read-only input storage. Simulation time is unrestricted.","sourceOffset":49159,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/137.md","scopeText":"The formalization gives two-fifths-power space simulation of a fixed deterministic machine with one writable tape and finitely many read-only input heads. Heads move at most one cell per step, initial contents are independent of the time bound, and contents and input symbols must be accessible in polylogarithmic space. Given a binary time cap $T\\ge2$, the simulator returns the source's finite-control outcome and halting flag using $O(T^{2/5}(\\log(T+2))^C)$ space.\n\nA second simulator needs no time cap: on an input that halts at time $t$, it returns the halted outcome using $O((t+2)^{2/5}(\\log(t+2))^C)$ space. Simulation time is unrestricted. General multitape simulation, hierarchy consequences, and bounded-space-halting results are outside these selected statements.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/137.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":61451,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Simulating-One-Tape-Time-in-Two-Fifths-Power-Space-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"138-01","slug":"Subset-Sum-in-Time-2-power-0-49n-October-4-2026","familyId":"138","title":"Subset Sum in Time $O(2^{0.49n})$","abstract":"We give a uniform randomized classical algorithm for Subset Sum with bounded error and worst-case running time $O(2^{0.49n})$ on polynomial-bit inputs in a word-RAM model, where n is the number of input integers. The time bound holds on every random execution.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives a uniform randomized classical algorithm for worst-case Subset Sum in ordinary $O(2^{0.49n})$ word-RAM time on polynomial-bit inputs, where n counts the integers. The time bound holds on every execution and success probability is at least 2/3 on every input. Inputs may repeat positive integers; words have $O(n+b)$ bits for maximum input bit length b.","pdfPath":"preprints/Subset-Sum-in-Time-2-power-0-49n-October-4-2026/subset-sum.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Subset-Sum-in-Time-2-power-0-49n-October-4-2026/subset-sum.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Subset-Sum-in-Time-2-power-0-49n-October-4-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Subset-Sum-in-Time-2-power-0-49n-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Subset-Sum-in-Time-2-power-0-49n-October-4-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Subset-Sum-in-Time-2-power-0-49n-October-4-2026/build/sections/setup.tex","preprints/Subset-Sum-in-Time-2-power-0-49n-October-4-2026/build/sections/filtering.tex","preprints/Subset-Sum-in-Time-2-power-0-49n-October-4-2026/build/sections/checksum.tex","preprints/Subset-Sum-in-Time-2-power-0-49n-October-4-2026/build/sections/aliases.tex","preprints/Subset-Sum-in-Time-2-power-0-49n-October-4-2026/build/sections/implementation.tex","preprints/Subset-Sum-in-Time-2-power-0-49n-October-4-2026/build/sections/numerical.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Subset-Sum-in-Time-2-power-0-49n-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a uniform randomized classical algorithm for Subset Sum with bounded error and worst-case running time $O(2^{0.49n})$ on polynomial-bit inputs in a word-RAM model, where $n$ is the number of input integers. The time bound holds on every random execution.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":309},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model and main result","sourceOffset":1803},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Related approaches","sourceOffset":4249},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":6437},{"kind":"section","title":"Isolation, distinct sums, and prime collisions","sourceOffset":12687},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Isolating one exact target","sourceOffset":13458},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Deficiency and the preliminary search","sourceOffset":15537},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A collision bound for random primes","sourceOffset":22184},{"kind":"section","title":"A fixed filtering construction","sourceOffset":23426},{"kind":"section","title":"Estimating a modular checksum","sourceOffset":31424},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Frequency patterns and block tables","sourceOffset":33551},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coverage and list sizes","sourceOffset":38595}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a uniform randomized classical algorithm $\\mathcal A$ for Subset Sum with the following properties in the word-RAM model \\eqref{eq:word-length}. For every input with $n\\ge2$, the algorithm halts on every execution and outputs the correct answer with probability at least $2/3$. For every fixed positive integer $c$, there are constants $C_c>0$ and $N_c\\ge2$ such that every input with $n\\ge N_c$ and $b\\le n^c$ is processed, on every outcome of the random choices, in at most \\[ C_c\\,2^{0.49n} \\] word operations. The program is not given $c$ and does not depend on $c$.","sourceOffset":3167,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Capped preliminary search","label":"prop:preliminary","text":"Given fixed transformed weights and targets from \\eqref{eq:transformed}, there is a randomized procedure that either returns the correct decision for the original instance or returns \\textup{CONTINUE}. On every execution its running time is at most \\[ 2^{0.489985n}\\operatorname{poly}(n+b+2). \\] If $V$ is uniform among the $\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor$-element subsets and $\\mathbb E d(V)>0.01004n$, then the probability of \\textup{CONTINUE} is $O(1/n)$, uniformly in the fixed weights.","sourceOffset":18758,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":107924,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Subset-Sum-in-Time-2-power-0-49n-October-4-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Subset-Sum-in-Time-2-power-0-49n-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Subset-Sum-in-Time-2-power-0-49n-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"138-02","slug":"A-Low-Space-Algorithm-for-Worst-Case-Subset-Sum-September-26-2026","familyId":"138","title":"A Low-Space Algorithm for Worst-Case Subset Sum","abstract":"We give a uniform classical randomized decision algorithm for worst-case Subset Sum. Under every fixed polynomial bound on input-integer bit length, it uses $\\mathop{\\mathrm{poly}}\\nolimits (n)2^{n/2}$ time and ordinary $O(2^{n/5})$ writable words of $O(n+b)$ bits, where b is the largest input bit length. Both resource bounds hold on every execution. The error is one-sided: the algorithm always rejects unsolvable instances and accepts each solvable instance with probability at least 2/3.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives a uniform randomized classical algorithm for worst-case Subset Sum in ordinary $O(2^{0.49n})$ word-RAM time on polynomial-bit inputs, where n counts the integers. The time bound holds on every execution and success probability is at least 2/3 on every input. Inputs may repeat positive integers; words have $O(n+b)$ bits for maximum input bit length b.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Low-Space-Algorithm-for-Worst-Case-Subset-Sum-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Low-Space-Algorithm-for-Worst-Case-Subset-Sum-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Low-Space-Algorithm-for-Worst-Case-Subset-Sum-September-26-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/A-Low-Space-Algorithm-for-Worst-Case-Subset-Sum-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-Low-Space-Algorithm-for-Worst-Case-Subset-Sum-September-26-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/A-Low-Space-Algorithm-for-Worst-Case-Subset-Sum-September-26-2026/build/preliminaries.tex","preprints/A-Low-Space-Algorithm-for-Worst-Case-Subset-Sum-September-26-2026/build/streams.tex","preprints/A-Low-Space-Algorithm-for-Worst-Case-Subset-Sum-September-26-2026/build/compression.tex","preprints/A-Low-Space-Algorithm-for-Worst-Case-Subset-Sum-September-26-2026/build/representation.tex","preprints/A-Low-Space-Algorithm-for-Worst-Case-Subset-Sum-September-26-2026/build/separators.tex","preprints/A-Low-Space-Algorithm-for-Worst-Case-Subset-Sum-September-26-2026/build/survival.tex","preprints/A-Low-Space-Algorithm-for-Worst-Case-Subset-Sum-September-26-2026/build/resources.tex","preprints/A-Low-Space-Algorithm-for-Worst-Case-Subset-Sum-September-26-2026/build/implementation.tex","preprints/A-Low-Space-Algorithm-for-Worst-Case-Subset-Sum-September-26-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Low-Space-Algorithm-for-Worst-Case-Subset-Sum-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a uniform classical randomized decision algorithm for worst-case Subset Sum. Under every fixed polynomial bound on input-integer bit length, it uses $\\poly(n)2^{n/2}$ time and ordinary $O(2^{n/5})$ writable words of $O(n+b)$ bits, where $b$ is the largest input bit length. Both resource bounds hold on every execution. The error is one-sided: the algorithm always rejects unsolvable instances and accepts each solvable instance with probability at least $2/3$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":517},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model and main result","sourceOffset":2241},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Representations and their predecessors","sourceOffset":5297},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two trials and the proof strategy","sourceOffset":7064},{"kind":"section","title":"Counting and enumeration tools","sourceOffset":12427},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How many distinct sums does a block have?","sourceOffset":14217},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact entropy estimates","sourceOffset":17171},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Random primes and the sampling interface","sourceOffset":18791},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sorted modular pair streams","sourceOffset":21742},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bounded dictionaries","sourceOffset":28206},{"kind":"section","title":"Compressing repeated sums","sourceOffset":29867},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Seven compacted blocks and four groups","sourceOffset":30877}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a single uniform classical randomized algorithm in this model that never answers YES incorrectly and answers each YES instance correctly with probability at least $2/3$. For every fixed positive integer $c$, there are a constant $A_c>0$ and positive integers $k_c,N_c$ such that, whenever $n\\ge N_c$ and $b\\le n^c$, every outcome of the algorithm's randomness uses at most \\[ A_c n^{k_c}2^{n/2}\\quad\\text{operations} \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad A_c(n+b+2)^{15}2^{0.199n}\\quad\\text{writable words}. \\] The algorithm does not depend on $c$.","sourceOffset":4029,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:target","text":"For every fixed $\\rho>0.199$, the same algorithm has ordinary $O_{c,\\rho}(2^{\\rho n})$ word space under each fixed polynomial-bit convention, with the time and correctness guarantees of Theorem \\ref{thm:main}. In particular, it has $O_c(2^{n/5})$ word space.","sourceOffset":4623,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":126867,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Low-Space-Algorithm-for-Worst-Case-Subset-Sum-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"139-01","slug":"Subpolynomial-query-complexity-for-well-conditioned-log-concave-sampling-September-26-2026","familyId":"139","title":"Subpolynomial query complexity for well-conditioned log-concave sampling","abstract":"For every fixed ε > 0, we give a sampling algorithm using at most $C_\\varepsilon d^\\varepsilon$ exact first-order queries on every execution for C2 potentials on ℝd with a known minimizer and Hessian between Id and $2I_d$. The output has total-variation distance at most 1/10 from the target Gibbs law. Computation between queries is unrestricted. We also prove an $\\Omega(\\log d)$ query lower bound for arbitrary randomized adaptive algorithms, determining the optimal dimension exponent to be zero.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For C2 potentials with a supplied minimizer and $I\\preceq\\nabla^2V\\preceq2I$, proves that sampling within total variation 1/10 requires only $C_\\varepsilon d^\\varepsilon$ exact value-and-gradient queries for every fixed ε > 0. The bound holds on every run, with unrestricted computation between queries. A logarithmic lower bound also holds, so the optimal power-law exponent in this oracle model is zero.","pdfPath":"preprints/Subpolynomial-query-complexity-for-well-conditioned-log-concave-sampling-September-26-2026/article.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Subpolynomial-query-complexity-for-well-conditioned-log-concave-sampling-September-26-2026/article.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Subpolynomial-query-complexity-for-well-conditioned-log-concave-sampling-September-26-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Subpolynomial-query-complexity-for-well-conditioned-log-concave-sampling-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Subpolynomial-query-complexity-for-well-conditioned-log-concave-sampling-September-26-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Subpolynomial-query-complexity-for-well-conditioned-log-concave-sampling-September-26-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Subpolynomial-query-complexity-for-well-conditioned-log-concave-sampling-September-26-2026/build/sections/jets.tex","preprints/Subpolynomial-query-complexity-for-well-conditioned-log-concave-sampling-September-26-2026/build/sections/divergence.tex","preprints/Subpolynomial-query-complexity-for-well-conditioned-log-concave-sampling-September-26-2026/build/figures/divergence-trace-opening.tex","preprints/Subpolynomial-query-complexity-for-well-conditioned-log-concave-sampling-September-26-2026/build/sections/centering.tex","preprints/Subpolynomial-query-complexity-for-well-conditioned-log-concave-sampling-September-26-2026/build/sections/numerics.tex","preprints/Subpolynomial-query-complexity-for-well-conditioned-log-concave-sampling-September-26-2026/build/sections/implementation.tex","preprints/Subpolynomial-query-complexity-for-well-conditioned-log-concave-sampling-September-26-2026/build/figures/implementation-two-depths.tex","preprints/Subpolynomial-query-complexity-for-well-conditioned-log-concave-sampling-September-26-2026/build/sections/sampling.tex","preprints/Subpolynomial-query-complexity-for-well-conditioned-log-concave-sampling-September-26-2026/build/sections/lower.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Subpolynomial-query-complexity-for-well-conditioned-log-concave-sampling-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed $\\varepsilon>0$, we give a sampling algorithm using at most $C_\\varepsilon d^\\varepsilon$ exact first-order queries on every execution for $C^2$ potentials on $\\R^d$ with a known minimizer and Hessian between $I_d$ and $2I_d$. The output has total-variation distance at most $1/10$ from the target Gibbs law. Computation between queries is unrestricted. We also prove an $\\Omega(\\log d)$ query lower bound for arbitrary randomized adaptive algorithms, determining the optimal dimension exponent to be zero.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":568},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model and result","sourceOffset":1351},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":3443},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and principal ingredients","sourceOffset":8284},{"kind":"section","title":"The near-quadratic sampling problem","sourceOffset":13540},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Notation and the auxiliary theorem","sourceOffset":14064},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Convexity, moments, and comparisons","sourceOffset":17982},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coupling and smoothing conventions","sourceOffset":22556},{"kind":"section","title":"Gaussian transport and tensor derivatives","sourceOffset":25180},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cumulants from the Poincar\\'e inequality","sourceOffset":27094},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The transport equation","sourceOffset":36987},{"kind":"section","title":"Dimension bounds for material derivatives","sourceOffset":38708}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"In the oracle model above, for every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) there is \\(C_\\varepsilon<\\infty\\) such that \\[ Q(d)\\le C_\\varepsilon d^\\varepsilon \\qquad(d\\ge2). \\] There are also absolute constants \\(c>0\\) and \\(d_0\\) such that \\[ Q(d)\\ge c\\log d\\qquad(d\\ge d_0). \\] The lower bound applies to arbitrary randomized adaptive first-order algorithms. In particular, \\(\\gamma_*=0\\).","sourceOffset":2556,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Noisy near-quadratic sampling","label":"thm:primitive","text":"Fix \\(0<e<1\\), an integer \\(K_0\\ge2\\), and \\(t>0\\) satisfying \\begin{equation} 40(K_0+2)t<\\frac14. \\end{equation} There are constants and a dimension threshold depending only on \\(e,K_0,t\\) with the following property. For every integer \\(d\\) above that threshold, put \\(\\delta=d^{-e}\\). One measurable randomized oracle procedure works for every \\(F\\in C^2(\\R^d)\\) with \\(\\Lip(\\nabla F)\\le\\delta\\), every \\(x\\in\\R^d\\), and every \\(0<r\\le1\\). Given evaluation access to \\(g=\\nabla F\\), it uses at most \\(\\polylog(d)\\delta^{-t}\\) evaluations of \\(g\\) on every execution and returns \\[ X=X_{\\mathrm{pre}}+\\tfrac12G, \\] where \\(G\\sim N(0,I_d)\\) is independent of \\(X_{\\mathrm{pre}}\\) and of all queries, and \\begin{equation} \\Wtwo\\!\\left(\\mathcal L(X_{\\mathrm{pre}}), \\nu_{x,r}*N(0,\\tfrac34I_d)\\right) \\le \\polylog(d)\\delta^{K_0}D(x). \\end{equation} Consequently both \\[ \\Wtwo\\!\\left(\\mathcal L(X),\\nu_{x,r}*N(0,I_d)\\right) \\quad\\hbox{and}\\quad \\norm{\\mathcal L(X)-\\nu_{x,r}*N(0,I_d)}_{\\TV} \\] are at most \\(\\polylog(d)\\delta^{K_0}D(x)\\). All constants in these bounds are uniform in \\(F,x,r\\). The procedure's scalar parameters, shapes of its computations, and query cap are independent of \\(x\\) and of the primitive. It uses no evaluations of \\(F\\) itself.","sourceOffset":16040,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Tensor expressions and their weights","label":"def:divergence-expressions","text":"A tensor expression is a finite formula built by the following rules. \\begin{enumerate} \\item For each integer $q\\ge1$, $U_q/L$ is an expression with $q+1$ free slots and weight $q-1$. \\item A permutation of the free slots preserves the weight. \\item Two expressions may be contracted on one chosen slot each, provided each factor retains at least one free slot. The resulting weight is the sum of their weights. \\item An adjoint divergence on a chosen free slot is allowed when at least two free slots remain. It increases the weight by one. \\end{enumerate} The formula specifies its leaves, their o","sourceOffset":45190,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/139.md","scopeText":"The formalized result determines the dimension exponent of exact value-and-gradient query complexity for well-conditioned log-concave sampling. For potentials with $V(0)=0$, $\\nabla V(0)=0$, and $I\\le\\nabla^2V\\le2I$, the least worst-case query budget achieving total-variation error at most $1/10$ is at most $C_\\varepsilon d^\\varepsilon$ for every $\\varepsilon>0$, and at least $c\\log d$ eventually. Hence the infimal polynomial exponent is zero. Algorithms may be measurable, randomized, and adaptive; arithmetic and bit costs are not bounded.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/139.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":186513,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Subpolynomial-query-complexity-for-well-conditioned-log-concave-sampling-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"140-01","slug":"Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026","familyId":"140","title":"Memory and precision in noiseless Gaussian regression","abstract":"For every fixed A > 0, a learner that retains at most $Ad^2$ bits between fresh exact Gaussian linear measurements needs $\\Omega_A(d\\log(1/\\epsilon))$ measurements to estimate a uniformly random unit vector to angular error at most ϵ, for any $0\\lt \\epsilon\\le 1/10$, with probability at least 2/3. The constant is absolute for $o(d^2)$ memory.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For fixed A > 0, a one-pass learner with $Ad^2$ persistent bits needs $\\Omega_A(d\\log(1/\\epsilon))$ noiseless Gaussian samples to recover a unit vector to angular error $0\\lt \\epsilon\\le1/10$ with probability 2/3, uniformly in accuracy for large d. Computation and randomized updates are unrestricted, but output uses only the terminal state, stopping index and fresh randomness.","pdfPath":"preprints/Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/local-mass.tex","preprints/Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/projection.tex","preprints/Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/blocks.tex","preprints/Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/figures/block-scaling.tex","preprints/Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/streaming.tex","preprints/Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/endpoint.tex","preprints/Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/alternative-selection.tex","preprints/Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/cube-application.tex","preprints/Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/density-variants.tex","preprints/Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/ellipsoid-variants.tex","preprints/Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/density-applications.tex","preprints/Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/full-data-tail.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed \\(A>0\\), a learner that retains at most \\(Ad^2\\) bits between fresh exact Gaussian linear measurements needs \\(\\Omega_A(d\\log(1/\\epsilon))\\) measurements to estimate a uniformly random unit vector to angular error at most \\(\\epsilon\\), for any \\(0<\\epsilon\\le 1/10\\), with probability at least \\(2/3\\). The constant is absolute for \\(o(d^2)\\) memory.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Exact observations and finite memory","sourceOffset":429},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The precision problem and its predecessors","sourceOffset":5965},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why the precision scale is natural","sourceOffset":10573},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A backward proof under the original prior","sourceOffset":11760},{"kind":"section","title":"Local mass of terminal success","sourceOffset":15288},{"kind":"section","title":"Exact Gaussian projection","sourceOffset":18549},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Affine distances and the Gram determinant","sourceOffset":21740},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Smoothing and exact labels","sourceOffset":25449},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The common projected support","sourceOffset":32997},{"kind":"section","title":"One block of arbitrary computation","sourceOffset":35265},{"kind":"section","title":"From one block to a finite stream","sourceOffset":40799},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fresh continuations and local destinations","sourceOffset":41660}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Memory and precision","label":"h:main","text":"For every fixed finite \\(A>0\\), there are constants \\(c_A>0\\) and \\(d_A\\) such that the following holds. Let \\(d\\ge d_A\\), let \\(0\\le M\\le Ad^2\\) be an integer, and let \\(0<\\epsilon\\le1/10\\). For any learner in Definition \\ref{h:model}, draw \\(S\\sim\\sigma_d\\) independently of the rows and the learner's data-independent randomness. If \\[ \\Prob\\{\\arccos\\ip{\\widehat s}{S}\\le\\epsilon\\}\\ge\\frac23, \\] then its prescribed horizon satisfies \\[ T\\ge c_A\\,d\\log(1/\\epsilon). \\]","sourceOffset":3386,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Subquadratic memory","label":"h:subquadratic","text":"Let \\(M(d)\\ge0\\) be integer-valued with \\(M(d)=o(d^2)\\), and let \\(0<\\epsilon(d)\\le1/10\\) be any accuracy sequence. A family of learners in Definition \\ref{h:model} with uniform-sphere average success at least \\(2/3\\) satisfies \\[ T(d)\\ge c\\,d\\log(1/\\epsilon(d)) \\] for all sufficiently large \\(d\\), where \\(c>0\\) is absolute. The dimension threshold may depend on the memory sequence.","sourceOffset":4208,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Finite-state Gaussian regression","label":"h:model","text":"Fix integers \\(d\\ge2\\), \\(M\\ge0\\), and \\(T\\ge0\\). A data-independent shared seed may choose the learner's rules; the descriptions of its transitions and output below are conditional on that seed. A signal \\(s\\in S^{d-1}\\) is chosen before sampling. At sample index \\(t\\in\\{1,\\ldots,T\\}\\), the observation is \\[ x_t\\sim N(0,I_d),\\qquad y_t=\\ip{x_t}{s}, \\] where the rows \\(x_1,\\ldots,x_T\\) are independent. A learner reads the pairs once, in order. After each number of samples its persistent state belongs to a prescribed set of at most \\(2^M\\) elements. The initial state and shared seed are jointly","sourceOffset":909,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/140.md","scopeText":"In noiseless Gaussian regression, a learner observes inner products of an unknown unit vector in $\\mathbb R^d$ with independent standard Gaussian vectors. The formalization proves that, for every fixed $A>0$, there is $c_A>0$ such that, in sufficiently large dimension, a learner retaining at most $Ad^2$ bits between observations needs at least $c_A d\\log(1/\\varepsilon)$ observations to attain angular error at most $\\varepsilon$ with probability at least $2/3$ for a uniformly random unit signal, for $0<\\varepsilon\\le1/10$.\n\nFor memory $o(d^2)$, the constant can be universal, and the selected statement also permits success at least $2/3$ separately for every signal. The linked supporting results include inverse Gram-matrix moments, exact-projection densities, regularization bounds, and spher","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/140.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":135698,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Memory-and-precision-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"140-02","slug":"Posterior-replicas-and-conditional-information-in-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026","familyId":"140","title":"Posterior replicas and conditional information in Gaussian regression","abstract":"For a signal with density bounded by L relative to uniform probability on $S^{d-1}$, we bound the information in a finite message W formed from exact Gaussian measurements, conditional on an independent projection revealed only to the analyst. For explicit row counts proportional to d, the bound is $O(H(W)/d+d+\\log(2+\\log L))$. Consequently, a finite-state learner with $o(d^2)$ persistent bits and a deterministic sample horizon needs $\\Omega(d\\log(1/\\epsilon))$ fresh noiseless Gaussian measurements for constant-probability angular accuracy $0\\lt \\epsilon\\le1/10$ under the uniform spherical prior.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For fixed A > 0, a one-pass learner with $Ad^2$ persistent bits needs $\\Omega_A(d\\log(1/\\epsilon))$ noiseless Gaussian samples to recover a unit vector to angular error $0\\lt \\epsilon\\le1/10$ with probability 2/3, uniformly in accuracy for large d. Computation and randomized updates are unrestricted, but output uses only the terminal state, stopping index and fresh randomness.","pdfPath":"preprints/Posterior-replicas-and-conditional-information-in-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Posterior-replicas-and-conditional-information-in-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Posterior-replicas-and-conditional-information-in-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Posterior-replicas-and-conditional-information-in-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Posterior-replicas-and-conditional-information-in-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Posterior-replicas-and-conditional-information-in-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Posterior-replicas-and-conditional-information-in-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/01-model-endpoint.tex","preprints/Posterior-replicas-and-conditional-information-in-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/02-replicas.tex","preprints/Posterior-replicas-and-conditional-information-in-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/03-gaussian.tex","preprints/Posterior-replicas-and-conditional-information-in-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/04-distance-bins.tex","preprints/Posterior-replicas-and-conditional-information-in-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/08-streaming.tex","preprints/Posterior-replicas-and-conditional-information-in-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/05-haar.tex","preprints/Posterior-replicas-and-conditional-information-in-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/06-synthetic.tex","preprints/Posterior-replicas-and-conditional-information-in-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/07-incidence.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Posterior-replicas-and-conditional-information-in-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For a signal with density bounded by $L$ relative to uniform probability on $S^{d-1}$, we bound the information in a finite message $W$ formed from exact Gaussian measurements, conditional on an independent projection revealed only to the analyst. For explicit row counts proportional to $d$, the bound is $O(H(W)/d+d+\\log(2+\\log L))$. Consequently, a finite-state learner with $o(d^2)$ persistent bits and a deterministic sample horizon needs $\\Omega(d\\log(1/\\epsilon))$ fresh noiseless Gaussian measurements for constant-probability angular accuracy $0<\\epsilon\\le1/10$ under the uniform spherical prior.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":669},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Related memory--sample bounds","sourceOffset":1733},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conditional information from one block","sourceOffset":4579},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A streaming consequence","sourceOffset":7602},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Information and geometric comparisons","sourceOffset":9773},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite states and the information required at the endpoint","sourceOffset":13514},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite-state learners","sourceOffset":14041},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Spherical caps and residual fibers","sourceOffset":16296},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Borel rules, randomness, and bounded stopping","sourceOffset":24836},{"kind":"section","title":"Posterior replicas and exact equal-label measures","sourceOffset":34810},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Information in a message and loss of correlation","sourceOffset":35682},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Projection fibers and inverse affine volumes","sourceOffset":40207}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Gaussian replica block bound","label":"thm:gaussian-block","text":"For all sufficiently large $d$, set \\[ k=2\\lfloor d/16\\rfloor,\\qquad m=k,\\qquad t=m+1,\\qquad \\ell=4k. \\] Suppose $p=f\\sigma_d$ is a probability with $0\\le f\\le L<\\infty$. In the block experiment above, let $A$ have $k$ rows and let $B$ have $\\ell$ rows. There is an absolute constant $C$ such that \\begin{equation} I(S;W\\mid B,BS) \\le \\frac{H(W)}{t}+Cd+C\\log(2+\\log L). \\end{equation} The bound is uniform over the prior, the density bound $L$, and the message kernel. The labels used to produce $W$ are the exact values $AS$.","sourceOffset":5192,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform-prior sample lower bound","label":"thm:streaming","text":"Let $M(d)=o(d^2)$ be nonnegative integer-valued, and let $0<\\epsilon(d)\\le1/10$. Suppose a family of learners in Definition \\ref{def:streaming-model} satisfies \\[ \\Prob\\{\\arccos\\langle\\widehat S,S\\rangle\\le\\epsilon(d)\\}\\ge\\frac23 \\qquad\\text{when }S\\sim\\sigma_d, \\] where the probability includes the signal, samples, and learner randomness. Then there is an absolute $c>0$ such that \\[ T(d)\\ge c\\,d\\log\\frac1{\\epsilon(d)} \\] for all sufficiently large $d$. The eventual threshold may depend on the memory sequence.","sourceOffset":8616,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Finite-state noiseless regression","label":"def:streaming-model","text":"An unknown unit vector $s\\in S^{d-1}$ is fixed before sampling. At index $j\\ge1$ the learner receives \\[ X_j\\sim N(0,I_d),\\qquad Y_j=\\langle X_j,s\\rangle, \\] where the $X_j$ are independent. It cannot choose a row or revisit an earlier one. At each index its persistent state has at most $2^M$ values. Its transition and stopping rule may be any measurable randomized kernel of the current state and the entire current pair $(X_j,Y_j)$. Computation within a transition is unrestricted, and every piece of information retained for later indices must be in the finite state. The learner stops at an ind","sourceOffset":14077,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/140.md","scopeText":"In noiseless Gaussian regression, a learner observes inner products of an unknown unit vector in $\\mathbb R^d$ with independent standard Gaussian vectors. The formalization proves that, for every fixed $A>0$, there is $c_A>0$ such that, in sufficiently large dimension, a learner retaining at most $Ad^2$ bits between observations needs at least $c_A d\\log(1/\\varepsilon)$ observations to attain angular error at most $\\varepsilon$ with probability at least $2/3$ for a uniformly random unit signal, for $0<\\varepsilon\\le1/10$.\n\nFor memory $o(d^2)$, the constant can be universal, and the selected statement also permits success at least $2/3$ separately for every signal. The linked supporting results include inverse Gram-matrix moments, exact-projection densities, regularization bounds, and spher","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/140.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":201135,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Posterior-replicas-and-conditional-information-in-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"140-03","slug":"Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026","familyId":"140","title":"Localization costs and information growth for exact Gaussian observations","abstract":"For the image of a uniform cube under a spherical coordinate map, we prove that finite messages from t blocks of $\\Theta(d)$ exact Gaussian measurements reveal only $O_A(dt)$ information when each message has at most $\\exp(Ad^2)$ values, for fixed A. The same bound holds when each message is supplemented with a nested cell that restores the required geometric spread.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For fixed A > 0, a one-pass learner with $Ad^2$ persistent bits needs $\\Omega_A(d\\log(1/\\epsilon))$ noiseless Gaussian samples to recover a unit vector to angular error $0\\lt \\epsilon\\le1/10$ with probability 2/3, uniformly in accuracy for large d. Computation and randomized updates are unrestricted, but output uses only the terminal state, stopping index and fresh randomness.","pdfPath":"preprints/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026/build/sections/01-model-information.tex","preprints/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026/build/sections/02-dyadic.tex","preprints/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026/build/sections/drafts/dyadic-entropy/maximum-score.tex","preprints/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026/build/sections/drafts/dyadic-entropy/finite-entropy.tex","preprints/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026/build/sections/drafts/dyadic-entropy/bounded-density-splitting.tex","preprints/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026/build/sections/drafts/likelihood-truncation/likelihood-truncation.tex","preprints/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026/build/sections/drafts/grid-side-projection/grid-entropy.tex","preprints/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026/build/sections/drafts/grid-side-projection/side-projection.tex","preprints/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026/build/sections/drafts/replica-localization/separated-tuples.tex","preprints/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026/build/sections/drafts/replica-localization/predecessor-span.tex","preprints/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026/build/sections/13-kernel-potential.tex","preprints/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026/build/sections/14-cell-correction.tex","preprints/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For the image of a uniform cube under a spherical coordinate map, we prove that finite messages from \\(t\\) blocks of \\(\\Theta(d)\\) exact Gaussian measurements reveal only \\(O_A(dt)\\) information when each message has at most \\(\\exp(Ad^2)\\) values, for fixed \\(A\\). The same bound holds when each message is supplemented with a nested cell that restores the required geometric spread.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":446},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why localization can pay for itself","sourceOffset":2853},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and companion inputs","sourceOffset":8689},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reading the arguments","sourceOffset":12218},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite memory, priors, and information","sourceOffset":13238},{"kind":"section","title":"Dyadic localization and its information cost","sourceOffset":26665},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A weighted density for exact projections","sourceOffset":30846},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The information in one message","sourceOffset":36911},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Selecting a cell and paying for its entropy","sourceOffset":40250},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Closing the information bound","sourceOffset":44011},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Application to finite-state Gaussian regression","sourceOffset":46982},{"kind":"section","title":"Maximum-score localization","sourceOffset":51083}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Repeated localization","label":"ld:thm:main","text":"There is an absolute constant $c>0$ with the following property. For any fixed $C_0>0$, consider the experiment above with $t\\ge0$ blocks and $N\\ge1$ possible message values, and suppose \\begin{equation} \\bigl(2/m+e^{-ck}\\bigr)\\log N\\le C_0d. \\end{equation} One can reveal nested dyadic cells $Q_0\\supset Q_1\\supset\\cdots\\supset Q_t$ containing $Z$, where $Q_i$ is a finite-valued measurable function of $Z$, the preceding revelations, and $W_1,\\ldots,W_i$, so that the augmented transcript \\[ \\Pi_t=(W_1,Q_1,\\ldots,W_t,Q_t) \\] satisfies \\begin{equation} I(Z;\\Pi_t)\\le Cdt. \\end{equation} Here $C$ is a constant depending only on $C_0$. If $J_t$ is the level of $Q_t$, the construction also gives $\\E J_t\\le Ct$.","sourceOffset":29002,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"ld:cor:streaming","text":"Suppose \\(M(d)=o(d^2)\\) and \\(0<\\epsilon(d)\\le1/10\\). If a family of learners in the model of Section \\ref{l:model} satisfies \\[ \\Prob\\{\\arccos\\langle\\widehat s,s\\rangle\\le\\epsilon(d)\\} \\ge2/3\\qquad\\text{for every }s\\in S^{d-1}, \\] then \\(T(d)\\ge c d\\log(1/\\epsilon(d))\\) for an absolute \\(c>0\\) and all sufficiently large \\(d\\). The dimension threshold may depend on the memory sequence.","sourceOffset":47256,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"l:finite-entropy:regular","text":"Put \\(\\alpha=1/2\\). A probability \\(P\\) supported on a cell \\(C\\) is regular on \\(C\\) if every descendant \\(E\\) of relative depth \\(h\\ge1\\) satisfies \\[ P(E)\\le2\\,2^{-\\alpha nh}. \\]","sourceOffset":74600,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/140.md","scopeText":"In noiseless Gaussian regression, a learner observes inner products of an unknown unit vector in $\\mathbb R^d$ with independent standard Gaussian vectors. The formalization proves that, for every fixed $A>0$, there is $c_A>0$ such that, in sufficiently large dimension, a learner retaining at most $Ad^2$ bits between observations needs at least $c_A d\\log(1/\\varepsilon)$ observations to attain angular error at most $\\varepsilon$ with probability at least $2/3$ for a uniformly random unit signal, for $0<\\varepsilon\\le1/10$.\n\nFor memory $o(d^2)$, the constant can be universal, and the selected statement also permits success at least $2/3$ separately for every signal. The linked supporting results include inverse Gram-matrix moments, exact-projection densities, regularization bounds, and spher","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/140.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":314941,"sourceFileCount":16,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Localization-costs-and-information-growth-for-exact-Gaussian-observations-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"140-04","slug":"Projection-moments-positive-cap-domination-and-Riesz-estimates-on-the-sphere-September-27-2026","familyId":"140","title":"Projection moments, positive cap domination, and Riesz estimates on the sphere","abstract":"We prove moment estimates for exact random projections of finite measures whose mass is controlled on Euclidean balls, and derive positive domination by countable sums of spherical cap measures. For learners with $M=o(d^2)$ bits of memory, these estimates give three proofs that uniform-sphere average success at least 2/3 at angular accuracy $0\\lt \\epsilon\\le1/10$ requires $\\Omega(d\\log(1/\\epsilon))$ noiseless Gaussian observations. The three proofs keep their different stopping and accuracy costs explicit.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For fixed A > 0, a one-pass learner with $Ad^2$ persistent bits needs $\\Omega_A(d\\log(1/\\epsilon))$ noiseless Gaussian samples to recover a unit vector to angular error $0\\lt \\epsilon\\le1/10$ with probability 2/3, uniformly in accuracy for large d. Computation and randomized updates are unrestricted, but output uses only the terminal state, stopping index and fresh randomness.","pdfPath":"preprints/Projection-moments-positive-cap-domination-and-Riesz-estimates-on-the-sphere-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Projection-moments-positive-cap-domination-and-Riesz-estimates-on-the-sphere-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Projection-moments-positive-cap-domination-and-Riesz-estimates-on-the-sphere-September-27-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Projection-moments-positive-cap-domination-and-Riesz-estimates-on-the-sphere-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Projection-moments-positive-cap-domination-and-Riesz-estimates-on-the-sphere-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Projection-moments-positive-cap-domination-and-Riesz-estimates-on-the-sphere-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Projection-moments-positive-cap-domination-and-Riesz-estimates-on-the-sphere-September-27-2026/build/sections/geometry-model.tex","preprints/Projection-moments-positive-cap-domination-and-Riesz-estimates-on-the-sphere-September-27-2026/build/sections/projection.tex","preprints/Projection-moments-positive-cap-domination-and-Riesz-estimates-on-the-sphere-September-27-2026/build/sections/positive-caps.tex","preprints/Projection-moments-positive-cap-domination-and-Riesz-estimates-on-the-sphere-September-27-2026/build/sections/riesz.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Projection-moments-positive-cap-domination-and-Riesz-estimates-on-the-sphere-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove moment estimates for exact random projections of finite measures whose mass is controlled on Euclidean balls, and derive positive domination by countable sums of spherical cap measures. For learners with \\(M=o(d^2)\\) bits of memory, these estimates give three proofs that uniform-sphere average success at least \\(2/3\\) at angular accuracy \\(0<\\epsilon\\le1/10\\) requires \\(\\Omega(d\\log(1/\\epsilon))\\) noiseless Gaussian observations. The three proofs keep their different stopping and accuracy costs explicit.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":621},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Projection geometry and earlier methods","sourceOffset":4381},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the three arguments work","sourceOffset":7836},{"kind":"section","title":"Spherical probabilities and exact observations","sourceOffset":12444},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coordinates and caps","sourceOffset":12896},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite-state regression experiment","sourceOffset":18070},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Measurable continuations and shared randomness","sourceOffset":22254},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A common full-data endpoint","sourceOffset":26887},{"kind":"section","title":"Integrated moments of orthonormal projections","sourceOffset":28655},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The projected frame and its determinant","sourceOffset":30313},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From local mass to an exact density norm","sourceOffset":34264},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Radial averaging and dimension-preserving propagation","sourceOffset":43150}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Finite-memory precision bound","label":"p:regression","text":"Let \\(M(d)=o(d^2)\\) be a sequence of nonnegative integers and let \\(0<\\epsilon(d)\\le1/10\\). There is an absolute \\(c>0\\) such that, for all sufficiently large \\(d\\), every learner in the experiment above whose uniform-sphere average success satisfies \\[ \\Prob\\{\\arccos\\inner{\\widehat S}{S}\\le\\epsilon(d)\\}\\ge\\frac23 \\] must use \\[ T(d)\\ge c\\,d\\log\\frac1{\\epsilon(d)}. \\] The dimension threshold may depend on the memory sequence. In particular, the conclusion holds when the same success probability is required separately for every \\(s\\in S^{d-1}\\).","sourceOffset":20758,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Finite-dimensional memory dependence","label":"p:finite-memory-tradeoff","text":"There are absolute constants \\(c>0\\) and \\(d_0\\) such that, for every integer \\(d\\ge d_0\\), every nonnegative integer \\(M\\), and every \\(0<\\epsilon\\le1/10\\), a learner in the experiment above with uniform-sphere average success at least \\(2/3\\) and deterministic finite horizon \\(T\\) satisfies \\[ T\\ge \\frac{c\\,d\\log(1/\\epsilon)}{1+M/d^2}. \\]","sourceOffset":21607,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/140.md","scopeText":"In noiseless Gaussian regression, a learner observes inner products of an unknown unit vector in $\\mathbb R^d$ with independent standard Gaussian vectors. The formalization proves that, for every fixed $A>0$, there is $c_A>0$ such that, in sufficiently large dimension, a learner retaining at most $Ad^2$ bits between observations needs at least $c_A d\\log(1/\\varepsilon)$ observations to attain angular error at most $\\varepsilon$ with probability at least $2/3$ for a uniformly random unit signal, for $0<\\varepsilon\\le1/10$.\n\nFor memory $o(d^2)$, the constant can be universal, and the selected statement also permits success at least $2/3$ separately for every signal. The linked supporting results include inverse Gram-matrix moments, exact-projection densities, regularization bounds, and spher","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/140.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":94819,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Projection-moments-positive-cap-domination-and-Riesz-estimates-on-the-sphere-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"140-05","slug":"Replacing-Gaussian-observations-in-memory-constrained-inference-September-27-2026","familyId":"140","title":"Replacing Gaussian observations in memory-constrained inference","abstract":"Replacing the Gaussian rows used to select a finite message by independent rows increases the remaining conditional information by at most $Cd$, for a uniform spherical signal, message entropy at most d2, and the specified row dimensions proportional to d. As an application, we prove that learners with $M=o(d^2)$ persistent bits need $T=\\Omega(d\\log(1/\\epsilon))$ exact observations to attain uniform-sphere angular success at least 3/5, for $0\\lt \\epsilon\\le1/10$ and a deterministic finite horizon.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For fixed A > 0, a one-pass learner with $Ad^2$ persistent bits needs $\\Omega_A(d\\log(1/\\epsilon))$ noiseless Gaussian samples to recover a unit vector to angular error $0\\lt \\epsilon\\le1/10$ with probability 2/3, uniformly in accuracy for large d. Computation and randomized updates are unrestricted, but output uses only the terminal state, stopping index and fresh randomness.","pdfPath":"preprints/Replacing-Gaussian-observations-in-memory-constrained-inference-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Replacing-Gaussian-observations-in-memory-constrained-inference-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Replacing-Gaussian-observations-in-memory-constrained-inference-September-27-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Replacing-Gaussian-observations-in-memory-constrained-inference-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Replacing-Gaussian-observations-in-memory-constrained-inference-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Replacing-Gaussian-observations-in-memory-constrained-inference-September-27-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Replacing-Gaussian-observations-in-memory-constrained-inference-September-27-2026/build/sections/01-information-and-densities.tex","preprints/Replacing-Gaussian-observations-in-memory-constrained-inference-September-27-2026/build/sections/02-mixed-moments.tex","preprints/Replacing-Gaussian-observations-in-memory-constrained-inference-September-27-2026/build/sections/03-critical-radius.tex","preprints/Replacing-Gaussian-observations-in-memory-constrained-inference-September-27-2026/build/sections/04-density-levels.tex","preprints/Replacing-Gaussian-observations-in-memory-constrained-inference-September-27-2026/build/sections/05-hybrids.tex","preprints/Replacing-Gaussian-observations-in-memory-constrained-inference-September-27-2026/build/sections/06-fiber.tex","preprints/Replacing-Gaussian-observations-in-memory-constrained-inference-September-27-2026/build/sections/07-streaming-applications.tex","preprints/Replacing-Gaussian-observations-in-memory-constrained-inference-September-27-2026/build/sections/07a-density-endpoints.tex","preprints/Replacing-Gaussian-observations-in-memory-constrained-inference-September-27-2026/build/sections/07b-hybrid-endpoint.tex","preprints/Replacing-Gaussian-observations-in-memory-constrained-inference-September-27-2026/build/sections/07c-fiber-endpoint.tex","preprints/Replacing-Gaussian-observations-in-memory-constrained-inference-September-27-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Replacing-Gaussian-observations-in-memory-constrained-inference-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Replacing the Gaussian rows used to select a finite message by independent rows increases the remaining conditional information by at most \\(Cd\\), for a uniform spherical signal, message entropy at most \\(d^2\\), and the specified row dimensions proportional to \\(d\\). As an application, we prove that learners with \\(M=o(d^2)\\) persistent bits need \\(T=\\Omega(d\\log(1/\\epsilon))\\) exact observations to attain uniform-sphere angular success at least \\(3/5\\), for \\(0<\\epsilon\\le1/10\\) and a deterministic finite horizon.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":622},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Memory, exact equations, and prior methods","sourceOffset":2777},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The streaming model and the application","sourceOffset":5821},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the comparisons work","sourceOffset":11033},{"kind":"section","title":"The two experiments and their information difference","sourceOffset":14433},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Density versions for exact labels","sourceOffset":19755},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A message selected using actual rows","sourceOffset":26229},{"kind":"section","title":"A projection moment with shared and fresh rows","sourceOffset":29498},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Changing the actual row law before taking the density limit","sourceOffset":37032},{"kind":"section","title":"A critical radius makes the density terms cancel","sourceOffset":42881},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Selecting a radius and a mass level","sourceOffset":44825},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The independent projection loses the mass level","sourceOffset":47276}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"proposition","name":"The information identity and its density bound","label":"prop:r-information-comparison","text":"In the two experiments just defined, all quantities below are finite, and \\begin{align} J_0(W)-J_1(W) &=h_0(Y\\mid G,W)-h_1(Y\\mid G,W)-I_1(S;G\\mid W) \\\\ &\\le h_0(Y\\mid G,W)+\\E_1\\log p_{\\nu_W}(G,Y). \\end{align} If \\(E=E(S,W)\\) is countable and \\(H(E\\mid W)<\\infty\\), and the same deterministic refinement \\(W'=(W,E)\\) is used in both laws, then \\begin{equation} J_0(W)-J_1(W) \\le J_0(W')-J_1(W')+H(E\\mid W). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":27252,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Mixed projection moment and exact density version","label":"thm:r-mixed-moment","text":"Let \\(n=d-1\\), let \\(m,r,q\\ge1\\) be integers, and put \\(\\ell=m+r\\). Suppose \\begin{equation} \\ell<n,\\qquad \\ell-m-(q-1)\\ge1. \\end{equation} Let \\(\\rho\\) be a finite nonnegative Borel measure on \\(S^{d-1}\\). Suppose that \\(B,D>0\\) and, for every \\(z\\in\\R^d\\) and \\(t>0\\), \\begin{equation} \\rho(B(z,t))\\le Bt^\\ell,\\qquad \\rho(B(z,t))\\le Dt^n. \\end{equation} Let \\(X\\sim\\gamma_m\\) and \\(Z\\sim\\gamma_r\\) be independent, and set \\(G=(X;Z)\\). For \\(\\delta>0\\), define the cube average \\[ p_{\\rho,\\delta}(g,y) =(2\\delta)^{-\\ell}\\int \\ind_{\\{\\norm{gu-y}_\\infty\\le\\delta\\}}\\,d\\rho(u). \\] There is an absolute \\(C\\) such that, for every fixed \\(s\\in S^{d-1}\\) and every \\(\\delta>0\\), \\begin{equation} \\left[\\E_X \\left(\\E_Z p_{\\rho,\\delta}((X;Z),(X;Z)s)\\right)^q \\right]^{1/q} \\le e^{Cd}B\\bigl(1+\\log^+(D/B)\\bigr). \\end{equation} If in addition \\(\\rho\\ll\\sigma\\), use the jointly Borel density \\(p_\\rho\\) in \\eqref{eq:r-density-version}. There is a measurable set \\(E_\\rho\\subset S^{d-1}\\), with \\(\\rho(E_\\rho)=0\\), such that for every \\(s\\notin E_\\rho\\) the cube averages converge to \\(p_\\rho(G,Gs)\\) for \\(\\gamma_\\ell\\)-almost every \\(G\\), and \\begin{equation} \\left[\\E_X \\left(\\E_Zp_\\rho((X;Z),(X;Z)s)\\right)^q \\right]^{1/q} \\le e^{Cd}B\\bigl(1+\\log^+(D/B)\\bigr). \\end{equation} When \\(\\rho\\ne0\\), \\(E_\\rho\\) may also be chosen so that \\(0<p_\\rho(G,Gs)<\\infty\\) for \\(\\gamma_\\ell\\)-almost every \\(G\\) and every \\(s\\notin E_\\rho\\).","sourceOffset":30499,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Finite persistent-state learner","label":"def:r-streaming-model","text":"Fix \\(d\\ge2\\), an integer memory bound \\(M\\ge0\\), an angular accuracy \\(0<\\epsilon\\le1/10\\), and a deterministic finite integer horizon \\(T\\ge0\\). The signal is a unit vector. It may be fixed before sampling, or sampled from a specified prior; the average-case statements in this paper use uniform probability on \\(S^{d-1}\\). Pre-generate independent \\[ X_t\\sim N(0,I_d),\\qquad Y_t=\\langle X_t,S\\rangle,\\qquad t\\ge1. \\] The row sequence is independent of \\(S\\). Let \\(\\Xi\\) be a shared rule seed, constant if no shared seed is used, and let \\(U_0\\) be the initial finite state. The pair \\((\\Xi,U_0)\\)","sourceOffset":6042,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/140.md","scopeText":"In noiseless Gaussian regression, a learner observes inner products of an unknown unit vector in $\\mathbb R^d$ with independent standard Gaussian vectors. The formalization proves that, for every fixed $A>0$, there is $c_A>0$ such that, in sufficiently large dimension, a learner retaining at most $Ad^2$ bits between observations needs at least $c_A d\\log(1/\\varepsilon)$ observations to attain angular error at most $\\varepsilon$ with probability at least $2/3$ for a uniformly random unit signal, for $0<\\varepsilon\\le1/10$.\n\nFor memory $o(d^2)$, the constant can be universal, and the selected statement also permits success at least $2/3$ separately for every signal. The linked supporting results include inverse Gram-matrix moments, exact-projection densities, regularization bounds, and spher","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/140.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":176096,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Replacing-Gaussian-observations-in-memory-constrained-inference-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"140-06","slug":"Subsphere-methods-for-memory-sample-lower-bounds-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026","familyId":"140","title":"Subsphere methods for memory-sample lower bounds in noiseless Gaussian regression","abstract":"Let a finite-state streaming learner estimate a uniformly random unit vector from independent exact Gaussian linear measurements. We prove that $o(d^2)$ bits of persistent memory and angular success probability at least 2/3 require at least $2^{-16}d\\log_2(1/\\epsilon)$ samples for all sufficiently large d, uniformly for $0\\lt \\epsilon\\le1/10$. The proof conditions each batch on its observed projection and controls the resulting random residual subsphere.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For fixed A > 0, a one-pass learner with $Ad^2$ persistent bits needs $\\Omega_A(d\\log(1/\\epsilon))$ noiseless Gaussian samples to recover a unit vector to angular error $0\\lt \\epsilon\\le1/10$ with probability 2/3, uniformly in accuracy for large d. Computation and randomized updates are unrestricted, but output uses only the terminal state, stopping index and fresh randomness.","pdfPath":"preprints/Subsphere-methods-for-memory-sample-lower-bounds-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Subsphere-methods-for-memory-sample-lower-bounds-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Subsphere-methods-for-memory-sample-lower-bounds-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Subsphere-methods-for-memory-sample-lower-bounds-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Subsphere-methods-for-memory-sample-lower-bounds-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Subsphere-methods-for-memory-sample-lower-bounds-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Subsphere-methods-for-memory-sample-lower-bounds-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/01-residual-spheres.tex","preprints/Subsphere-methods-for-memory-sample-lower-bounds-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/figures/residual-sphere.tex","preprints/Subsphere-methods-for-memory-sample-lower-bounds-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/02-subspace-moments.tex","preprints/Subsphere-methods-for-memory-sample-lower-bounds-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/03-stopping.tex","preprints/Subsphere-methods-for-memory-sample-lower-bounds-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/07-beta-mixture.tex","preprints/Subsphere-methods-for-memory-sample-lower-bounds-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/04-weighted.tex","preprints/Subsphere-methods-for-memory-sample-lower-bounds-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/05-affine.tex","preprints/Subsphere-methods-for-memory-sample-lower-bounds-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/sections/06-fixed-length.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Subsphere-methods-for-memory-sample-lower-bounds-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let a finite-state streaming learner estimate a uniformly random unit vector from independent exact Gaussian linear measurements. We prove that \\(o(d^2)\\) bits of persistent memory and angular success probability at least \\(2/3\\) require at least \\(2^{-16}d\\log_2(1/\\epsilon)\\) samples for all sufficiently large \\(d\\), uniformly for \\(0<\\epsilon\\le1/10\\). The proof conditions each batch on its observed projection and controls the resulting random residual subsphere.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":532},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and ancestry","sourceOffset":4578},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact observations and residual subspheres","sourceOffset":7235},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof of the precision bound","sourceOffset":9528},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Further analytic formulations","sourceOffset":11650},{"kind":"section","title":"Continuations and exact residual spheres","sourceOffset":13139},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Borel rules and counterfactual continuations","sourceOffset":13484},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Spherical coordinates and beta fractions","sourceOffset":17711},{"kind":"subsection","title":"What an exact block leaves","sourceOffset":21774},{"kind":"section","title":"Caps and directions in a random subspace","sourceOffset":28459},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Caps and terminal outputs","sourceOffset":28882},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Directions with a common random subspace","sourceOffset":31069}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Explicit noiseless precision bound","label":"s:main","text":"Let \\(M:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\) satisfy \\(M(d)=o(d^2)\\). There is a dimension threshold depending only on this memory sequence such that, for every larger \\(d\\), every \\(0<\\epsilon\\le1/10\\), and every learner in Definition \\ref{s:model}, the following implication holds. If \\(S\\) is uniform on \\(S^{d-1}\\), independently of the Gaussian rows and learner randomness, and \\[ \\Prob\\{\\arccos\\ip{\\widehat s}{S}\\le\\epsilon\\}\\ge\\frac23, \\] then \\begin{equation} T\\ge 2^{-16}d\\log_2(1/\\epsilon). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":3231,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Guarantees for every signal","label":"s:worst-signal","text":"Under the memory assumption of Theorem \\ref{s:main}, suppose a learner satisfies \\[ \\Prob_s\\{\\arccos\\ip{\\widehat s}{s}\\le\\epsilon\\}\\ge\\frac23 \\qquad\\text{for every }s\\in S^{d-1}, \\] where \\(\\Prob_s\\) averages its observations and randomness at the fixed signal \\(s\\). Then \\eqref{s:main-bound} holds at the same dimension threshold.","sourceOffset":3844,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Finite-state Gaussian stream","label":"s:model","text":"Fix integers \\(d\\ge2\\), \\(M\\ge0\\), and \\(T\\ge0\\), and an accuracy \\(0<\\epsilon\\le1/10\\). For a signal \\(s\\in S^{d-1}\\), the observations are \\[ X_t\\sim N(0,I_d),\\qquad Y_t=\\ip{X_t}{s},\\qquad 1\\le t\\le T, \\] with the \\(X_t\\) independent. The learner reads these pairs in order. At every sample index its persistent state has at most \\(2^M\\) possible values. Its transition and stopping rules may depend on the index, \\(d,M,T,\\epsilon\\), the current state, the entire current pair, and fresh randomness. Computation within a transition is unrestricted. All information retained for a later sample must ","sourceOffset":1058,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/140.md","scopeText":"In noiseless Gaussian regression, a learner observes inner products of an unknown unit vector in $\\mathbb R^d$ with independent standard Gaussian vectors. The formalization proves that, for every fixed $A>0$, there is $c_A>0$ such that, in sufficiently large dimension, a learner retaining at most $Ad^2$ bits between observations needs at least $c_A d\\log(1/\\varepsilon)$ observations to attain angular error at most $\\varepsilon$ with probability at least $2/3$ for a uniformly random unit signal, for $0<\\varepsilon\\le1/10$.\n\nFor memory $o(d^2)$, the constant can be universal, and the selected statement also permits success at least $2/3$ separately for every signal. The linked supporting results include inverse Gram-matrix moments, exact-projection densities, regularization bounds, and spher","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/140.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":80188,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Subsphere-methods-for-memory-sample-lower-bounds-in-noiseless-Gaussian-regression-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"141-01","slug":"Existential-universal-real-sentences-in-the-counting-hierarchy-October-4-2026","familyId":"141","title":"Existential–universal real sentences in the counting hierarchy","abstract":"We prove that the existential theory of the reals lies in the counting hierarchy. More generally, we show that the truth of existential–universal sentences over the reals can be decided in a fixed level of the counting hierarchy, even when the integer polynomials are given by arithmetic circuits.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the existential theory of the reals lies in the counting hierarchy. More generally, truth of existential–universal real sentences can be decided at one fixed level of that hierarchy, even when their integer polynomials are specified by arithmetic circuits.","pdfPath":"preprints/Existential-universal-real-sentences-in-the-counting-hierarchy-October-4-2026/etr-counting-hierarchy.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Existential-universal-real-sentences-in-the-counting-hierarchy-October-4-2026/etr-counting-hierarchy.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Existential-universal-real-sentences-in-the-counting-hierarchy-October-4-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Existential-universal-real-sentences-in-the-counting-hierarchy-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Existential-universal-real-sentences-in-the-counting-hierarchy-October-4-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Existential-universal-real-sentences-in-the-counting-hierarchy-October-4-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Existential-universal-real-sentences-in-the-counting-hierarchy-October-4-2026/build/sections/02-counting.tex","preprints/Existential-universal-real-sentences-in-the-counting-hierarchy-October-4-2026/build/sections/03-algebra.tex","preprints/Existential-universal-real-sentences-in-the-counting-hierarchy-October-4-2026/build/sections/04-labels.tex","preprints/Existential-universal-real-sentences-in-the-counting-hierarchy-October-4-2026/build/sections/05-existential.tex","preprints/Existential-universal-real-sentences-in-the-counting-hierarchy-October-4-2026/build/sections/06-two-blocks.tex","preprints/Existential-universal-real-sentences-in-the-counting-hierarchy-October-4-2026/build/sections/08-existential-bound.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Existential-universal-real-sentences-in-the-counting-hierarchy-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the existential theory of the reals lies in the counting hierarchy. More generally, we show that the truth of existential--universal sentences over the reals can be decided in a fixed level of the counting hierarchy, even when the integer polynomials are given by arithmetic circuits.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":453},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":2644},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From quantified fibers to short algebraic witnesses","sourceOffset":5740},{"kind":"section","title":"Counting and controlled polynomial families","sourceOffset":10169},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniformity and counting","sourceOffset":10928},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Polynomial families accessible by evaluation","sourceOffset":16964},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Recovering an integer sign","sourceOffset":25553},{"kind":"section","title":"Multiplication determinants without large matrices","sourceOffset":31942},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A basis and a coefficient functional","sourceOffset":32542},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A trace formula and its spectral consequences","sourceOffset":35918},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Characteristic coefficients from finite-field evaluations","sourceOffset":40915},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The determinant rule for controlled families","sourceOffset":46074}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is an absolute integer $j$ such that \\[ \\EATR\\in\\Clevel j. \\] The level $j$ is independent of the input length, the numbers of variables, the degrees of the circuit polynomials, and their coefficient magnitudes.","sourceOffset":1912,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Closure of controlled families","label":"prop:controlled-closure","text":"Controlled families are preserved by each of the following operations, applied a fixed number of times: \\begin{enumerate} \\item sums and products over short index sets, with uniform bounds on the summands or factors; \\item powers with short nonnegative exponents computable in polynomial time, and substitution of controlled polynomials for indeterminates; \\item extraction of indexed coefficients in any subset of the indeterminates, and differentiation in one variable to a short indexed nonnegative order. \\end{enumerate} In the first operation the index domain is specified in polynomial time; more generally, an available predicate $A(w,i)$ may restrict the indices when polynomial-time uniform bounds for the unrestricted domain are retained. This predicate depends only on the primary word and the family index, not on an evaluation prime or evaluation point. All resulting bounds and prime cutoffs are computable in polynomial bit time from the primary family data. In particular, they are independent of evaluation primes.","sourceOffset":20968,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Controlled polynomial family","label":"def:controlled","text":"A family $V_w\\in\\Z[X_1,\\ldots,X_{n(w)}]$ is controlled if the following conditions hold. \\begin{enumerate} \\item The number $n(w)$ is polynomially bounded and computable in polynomial time. \\item Nonnegative short integers $d(w),b(w),\\lambda(w)$ are computable in polynomial time and satisfy \\[ \\deg V_w\\le d(w),\\qquad \\norm{V_w}\\le2^{b(w)}. \\] The zero polynomial is permitted and satisfies every nonnegative degree bound. \\item For some fixed $a$, evaluation \\[ (w,p,z)\\longmapsto V_w(z)\\bmod p, \\qquad z\\in\\F_p^{n(w)}, \\] is in $\\Flevel{a}$ on prime inputs $p>\\lambda(w)$. Field elements are given","sourceOffset":17418,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":110615,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Existential-universal-real-sentences-in-the-counting-hierarchy-October-4-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"142-01","slug":"Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026","familyId":"142","title":"Deterministic Polynomial Factorization over Prime Fields","abstract":"We give a uniform deterministic polynomial-time algorithm for complete factorization over prime fields. For a prime p in binary and a nonzero polynomial $f\\in\\mathbf F_p[x]$ given by its dense coefficient list, the algorithm computes the irreducible factors and their multiplicities using a number of bit operations polynomial in $(\\deg f+1)\\log p$. The proof uses the uniform Hecke zero-free theorem from the companion paper *Primitive roots for every admissible integer base*.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives a uniform deterministic algorithm that completely factors every nonzero dense degree-n polynomial over a prime field 𝔽p, including multiplicities, in bit complexity polynomial in $(n+1)\\log p$. The prime is supplied in binary. No randomness, integer-factorization or primitive-root oracle, or GRH assumption is required.","pdfPath":"preprints/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026","subjectId":"14","subject":"Theoretical computer science","subjectZh":"理論計算機科學","texPaths":["preprints/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/02-finite-fields.tex","preprints/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/01-table.tex","preprints/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/05-geometry.tex","preprints/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/04-divisors.tex","preprints/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/03-norms.tex","preprints/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/06-division.tex","preprints/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/07-odd-split.tex","preprints/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/08-driver.tex","preprints/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/09-complexity.tex","preprints/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026/build/sections/10-analytic.tex","preprints/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a uniform deterministic polynomial-time algorithm for complete factorization over prime fields. For a prime $p$ in binary and a nonzero polynomial $f\\in\\Fp[x]$ given by its dense coefficient list, the algorithm computes the irreducible factors and their multiplicities using a number of bit operations polynomial in $(\\deg f+1)\\log p$. The proof uses the uniform Hecke zero-free theorem from the companion paper Primitive roots for every admissible integer base.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":540},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The auxiliary-prime reduction","sourceOffset":2692},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the role of the construction","sourceOffset":4440},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":10384},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite-field computations without known roots","sourceOffset":15562},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Simultaneous execution and zero tests","sourceOffset":16739},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Logarithms in primary subgroups","sourceOffset":19168},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Root extraction in a finite reduced algebra","sourceOffset":20726},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Splitting an even number of roots","sourceOffset":25995},{"kind":"section","title":"Auxiliary primes and primary generators","sourceOffset":28972},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A degree-\\texorpdfstring{$q$}{q} field without factoring a cyclotomic polynomial","sourceOffset":31060},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Extracting the primary generators","sourceOffset":33422}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:factorization","text":"There is a uniform deterministic algorithm that, given a prime $p$ in binary and a nonzero dense polynomial $f\\in\\Fp[x]$ of degree $n$, outputs its leading coefficient and its distinct monic irreducible factors with their multiplicities in \\[ O\\bigl(((n+1)\\lceil\\log_2p\\rceil)^{10^{12}}\\bigr) \\] bit operations, with absolute implied constant. A constant polynomial returns an empty factor list. The algorithm uses no randomness, integer-factorization oracle, primitive-root oracle, or GRH assumption.","sourceOffset":1919,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:reduction","text":"There is a uniform deterministic algorithm with the following guarantee. Its input is a prime $p$ and a nonzero dense polynomial $f\\in\\Fp[x]$. If $p>B^{200000}$ and $n\\ge2$, its input also includes one auxiliary prime $\\ell_q$ satisfying \\eqref{eq:auxiliary} for each prime $q\\le n$. It outputs the complete factorization of $f$, including multiplicities. Let $E=\\max(2,\\max_q\\ell_q)$, with $E=2$ when no auxiliary primes are required. For an absolute constant $C$, the algorithm uses $O((B+E)^C)$ bit operations. It uses neither randomness nor an integer factorization or primitive-root oracle.","sourceOffset":3273,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":163921,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Deterministic-Polynomial-Factorization-over-Prime-Fields-October-4-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"143-01","slug":"uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026","familyId":"143","title":"Uniform bounds for planar polynomial limit cycles","abstract":"For every degree, we prove that the number of isolated periodic orbits of a real planar polynomial vector field is bounded by a finite constant depending only on that degree. This establishes the uniform boundedness assertion in the second part of Hilbert's sixteenth problem. The proof uses separation of asymptotic expansions on nested complex domains and a finite-dimensional counting argument.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the uniform boundedness assertion in Hilbert's sixteenth problem: the number of isolated periodic orbits of a real planar polynomial vector field is bounded by a finite constant depending only on its degree. For classical quintic Liénard systems, the exact maximum is two limit cycles.","pdfPath":"preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","texPaths":["preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-roadmap.tex","preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-flags.tex","preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-separation.tex","preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/build/figures/coupled-bands.tex","preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-calculus.tex","preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-elimination.tex","preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-additive.tex","preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/build/figures/retained-charges.tex","preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-regular.tex","preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-mixed.tex","preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-subdivision.tex","preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-terminal.tex","preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/build/sections/11-counting.tex","preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/build/figures/matching-arguments.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every degree, we prove that the number of isolated periodic orbits of a real planar polynomial vector field is bounded by a finite constant depending only on that degree. This establishes the uniform boundedness assertion in the second part of Hilbert's sixteenth problem. The proof uses separation of asymptotic expansions on nested complex domains and a finite-dimensional counting argument.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":485},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":1433},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":5182},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Analytic ingredients","sourceOffset":13219},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Notation and conventions","sourceOffset":15270},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite logarithmic flags and angular paths","sourceOffset":17547},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sequence conventions and exact logarithmic relations","sourceOffset":18266},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Paths through far anchors","sourceOffset":30462},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Nested contours and background variation","sourceOffset":44673},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exceptional growth and safe columns","sourceOffset":53814},{"kind":"section","title":"Separation of packets","sourceOffset":59066},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Data and quantifiers","sourceOffset":60024}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform boundedness","label":"thm:uniform","text":"For each integer \\(d\\geq1\\), there is a finite nonnegative integer \\(B(d)\\) such that every real planar polynomial vector field of degree at most \\(d\\) has at most \\(B(d)\\) limit cycles in the whole plane.","sourceOffset":864,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Separation","label":"thm:separation","text":"Suppose the data of Definition \\ref{def:packet} satisfy its requirements on a saturated flag. If all paired terminal coefficients vanish, the actual function is identically zero on the real flag locus sufficiently far in the regime, locally in ordinary inputs. Otherwise, let $\\lambda=(\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_m)$ be the first nonzero paired exponent. Its coefficients coincide as ordinary analytic germs, say $c^+_\\lambda=c^-_\\lambda=c_\\lambda$, and \\begin{equation} \\exp\\!\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^m\\lambda_i Z_i\\right)S^1 \\longrightarrow c_\\lambda(t) \\end{equation} locally uniformly on the real locus. The same assertion holds for each selected leading prefix, with all earlier whole subtrees zero. In band $j$, use its raw packet and normalize by the remaining selected exponents, evaluating all these rates with $b_j$. The coupled normalized determinations have the same leading limit inside their selected contours and through the used bands. With \\eqref{eq:separation-background-fringe}, their leading estimates extend through the owning outer fringe for any fixed required normalization. An entire zero subtree in band $p+1$, passed at such a prefix, has size \\begin{equation} O(e^{-cV_p}) \\end{equation} on more vertical level-$p$ collars and on the subsequent portion of that fringe, where its outer determination is in use. All constants and contour choices concern fixed finite labels and requests; none is asserted uniform over the whole packet tree.","sourceOffset":69574,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Logarithmic flag","label":"def:flags-flag","text":"A logarithmic flag consists of positive scales \\[ Z_1\\gg Z_2\\gg\\cdots\\gg Z_m\\gg1, \\qquad f_i=\\log Z_i,\\qquad h_i=e^{-Z_i}, \\] each declared either free or dependent. The tuple of free scales is denoted by $S$. A dependent scale has the exact relation \\begin{equation} f_i=\\sum_{j>i}a_{ij}Z_j+b_i, \\qquad \\sigma(i)=\\min\\{j:a_{ij}\\ne0\\},\\qquad a_{i,\\sigma(i)}>0. \\end{equation} The coefficients $a_{ij}$ are fixed real numbers, and the backgrounds $b_i$ are bounded ordinary coordinates, or their current values on an ordinary chart. The free scales are independent arguments; dependent scales are alwa","sourceOffset":19260,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Packet data","label":"def:packet","text":"Let $t_c$ be a real ordinary germ point. A packet consists of the following data and estimates, separately for signs $+$ and $-$. Tree and actual function. For every prefix $\\alpha=(a_1,\\ldots,a_{j-1})$ there is a raw function $S^{j,\\pm}_{\\alpha}$ in band $j$, and a set $E^{j,\\pm}_{\\alpha}\\subset\\RR$ of child exponents. Each such set is finite below every finite ceiling. At level $m+1$ the functions $c^{\\pm}_\\alpha=S^{m+1,\\pm}_\\alpha$ are analytic ordinary germs at $t_c$. The two functions at level $1$ are restrictions of one actual holomorphic function across the real ray. Absent exponents ca","sourceOffset":61867,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/143.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":607664,"sourceFileCount":17,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":7,"extractUrl":"data/papers/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"143-02","slug":"two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026","familyId":"143","title":"Two limit cycles for quintic Liénard systems","abstract":"Every classical Liénard system $\\dot x=y-F(x)$, $\\dot y=-x$, with F an arbitrary real polynomial of degree at most five, has at most two geometrically distinct isolated periodic orbits, and the bound is attained. This proves the degree-five case of the Lins Neto–de Melo–Pugh conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the uniform boundedness assertion in Hilbert's sixteenth problem: the number of isolated periodic orbits of a real planar polynomial vector field is bounded by a finite constant depending only on its degree. For classical quintic Liénard systems, the exact maximum is two limit cycles.","pdfPath":"preprints/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","texPaths":["preprints/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026/build/sections/arcs.tex","preprints/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026/build/figures/half-arcs.tex","preprints/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026/build/sections/fitting.tex","preprints/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026/build/sections/model.tex","preprints/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026/build/sections/transport.tex","preprints/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026/build/sections/application.tex","preprints/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026/build/sections/sharpness.tex","preprints/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026/build/sections/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every classical Li\\'enard system $\\dot x=y-F(x)$, $\\dot y=-x$, with $F$ an arbitrary real polynomial of degree at most five, has at most two geometrically distinct isolated periodic orbits, and the bound is attained. This proves the degree-five case of the Lins Neto--de Melo--Pugh conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":339},{"kind":"section","title":"Arc coordinates and periodic-orbit matching","sourceOffset":7105},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Global endpoint data and their transport","sourceOffset":7939},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Profile variation and smooth small-width data","sourceOffset":15469},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Continuation to a nonsmooth boundary height","sourceOffset":23054},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Matching half-arcs and isolated periodic orbits","sourceOffset":27808},{"kind":"section","title":"Quadratic fitting and its transport law","sourceOffset":34279},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reference characteristics and curvature comparison","sourceOffset":36142},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Midpoint fitting and the positive conditional derivative","sourceOffset":45298},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The full range of the conditional fit","sourceOffset":50325},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The remaining derivative signs and the global inverse","sourceOffset":53158},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Transport of an actual profile in fitted coordinates","sourceOffset":57587}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"If $\\deg F\\le5$, the system \\eqref{eq:original-system} has at most two limit cycles in $\\R^2$. Some members of this class have two limit cycles. Thus its exact maximum is two.","sourceOffset":823,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Periodic-orbit matching","label":"prop:cycle-matching","text":"For the system \\begin{equation} \\dot x=y-F(x),\\qquad \\dot y=-x, \\end{equation} the images of nonconstant periodic solutions are in one-to-one correspondence with the zeros of \\begin{equation} \\Delta(r)=M_+(0,r)-M_-(0,r) \\end{equation} on the single open interval \\(\\mathcal I=\\mathcal I_+\\cap\\mathcal I_-\\). If this interval is empty, there are no such periodic solutions. Each periodic orbit has exactly one positive and one negative intersection with the \\(y\\)-axis. Under the correspondence, isolated periodic orbits are exactly isolated zeros of \\(\\Delta\\).","sourceOffset":28308,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/143.md","scopeText":"For the quintic Liénard system $x'=y-F(x)$, $y'=-x$, the formalized result proves that every real polynomial $F$ of degree at most five yields at most two limit cycles, and that some such $F$ yields exactly two. Limit cycles are isolated images of nonconstant periodic solutions. No sign, parity, hyperbolicity, or amplitude restriction is imposed.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/143.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":125259,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"144-01","slug":"A-smooth-three-torus-diffeomorphism-with-simple-Lebesgue-spectrum-September-23-2026","familyId":"144","title":"A smooth three-torus diffeomorphism with simple Lebesgue spectrum","abstract":"We solve the probability-preserving form of Banach's simple Lebesgue-spectrum problem in smooth dynamics. We construct a C∞ diffeomorphism of the three-torus that preserves standard volume and whose Koopman operator has simple Lebesgue spectrum on the entire mean-zero L2 space.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the probability-preserving form of Banach's simple Lebesgue-spectrum problem within smooth dynamics. A smooth volume-preserving diffeomorphism of the standard-volume three-torus has simple Lebesgue spectrum on its entire complex mean-zero L2 space: the bilateral iterates of one real observable form an orthonormal basis of that space.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-smooth-three-torus-diffeomorphism-with-simple-Lebesgue-spectrum-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-smooth-three-torus-diffeomorphism-with-simple-Lebesgue-spectrum-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-smooth-three-torus-diffeomorphism-with-simple-Lebesgue-spectrum-September-23-2026","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","texPaths":["preprints/A-smooth-three-torus-diffeomorphism-with-simple-Lebesgue-spectrum-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-smooth-three-torus-diffeomorphism-with-simple-Lebesgue-spectrum-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-smooth-three-torus-diffeomorphism-with-simple-Lebesgue-spectrum-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-passages.tex","preprints/A-smooth-three-torus-diffeomorphism-with-simple-Lebesgue-spectrum-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-signals.tex","preprints/A-smooth-three-torus-diffeomorphism-with-simple-Lebesgue-spectrum-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-blocks.tex","preprints/A-smooth-three-torus-diffeomorphism-with-simple-Lebesgue-spectrum-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-completion.tex","preprints/A-smooth-three-torus-diffeomorphism-with-simple-Lebesgue-spectrum-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-smooth-three-torus-diffeomorphism-with-simple-Lebesgue-spectrum-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We solve the probability-preserving form of Banach's simple Lebesgue-spectrum problem in smooth dynamics. We construct a $C^\\infty$ diffeomorphism of the three-torus that preserves standard volume and whose Koopman operator has simple Lebesgue spectrum on the entire mean-zero $L^2$ space.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":352},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction","sourceOffset":4937},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Order of the proof","sourceOffset":7605},{"kind":"section","title":"Smooth passages and spectral densities","sourceOffset":8366},{"kind":"section","title":"Small Fourier signals","sourceOffset":31674},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite blocks and prediction","sourceOffset":50646},{"kind":"section","title":"Preparation, whitening, and the smooth limit","sourceOffset":71268},{"kind":"section","title":"Dynamical consequences","sourceOffset":91332}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist a $C^\\infty$ volume-preserving diffeomorphism $T\\colon\\T^3\\to\\T^3$ and a real function $f\\in L^2_0(\\T^3,\\mu)$ such that \\[ \\{f\\circ T^n:n\\in\\Z\\} \\] is an orthonormal basis of $L^2_0(\\T^3,\\mu)$. Consequently $T$ is ergodic, and $U_T$ on this space is unitarily equivalent to multiplication by the coordinate $w$ on $L^2(S^1,m)$.","sourceOffset":1248,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Prediction of a tagged packet sum","label":"prop:prediction","text":"Let $S$ be an intermediate map, expressed in a fixed physical volume-preserving coordinate chart. Let \\[ F_r=\\sum_{m\\in\\mathcal M_r} A_{r,m}(u,a)\\ee(m\\theta), \\qquad F=\\sum_r F_r, \\] be a finite list of colors of smooth packets supported compactly in the good arc. Suppose that the finite sets $\\mathcal M_r\\subset\\Z\\setminus\\{0\\}$ are pairwise disjoint, that $|A_{r,m}(u,a)|$ is independent of $a$, and that the list is invariant under complex conjugation: $F_{r^*}=\\overline{F_r}$. In particular, $F$ is real. Let $p_r$ be the corresponding spectral densities, put $p=\\sum_r p_r$, and suppose that \\[ \\int_\\T p(z)\\,\\dd z\\le \\frac32. \\] Assign constants $b_r\\in\\C$ satisfying $b_{r^*}=\\overline{b_r}$. Given $\\eps,\\tau,\\kappa>0$ and any neighborhood of the physical map and its inverse in the smooth topology, there are a finite sequence of passages, a final intermediate map $S'$, final packets $F'_r$, and a smooth function $\\Psi:\\T\\to\\C$, such that \\begin{align} \\sum_r\\|F'_r-F_r\\|_2&<\\eps, \\\\ \\int_\\T\\sqrt{p'(z)}\\,\\dd z &\\ge \\int_\\T\\sqrt{p(z)}\\,\\dd z-\\tau, \\qquad p'=\\sum_r p'_r, \\\\ \\left\\|\\sum_r b_rF'_r- \\Psi(U_{S'})\\sum_r F'_r\\right\\|_2&<\\eps. \\end{align} Here the differences in \\eqref{eq:prediction-transport} are measured in the original physical $L^2$ space. All the physical maps and their inverses can be kept in the prescribed neighborhood. The final good arc $I'$ satisfies $|\\T\\setminus I'|<\\kappa$. The output colors again have disjoint nonzero angular-index sets, moduli independen","sourceOffset":51197,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/144.md","scopeText":"Banach's simple Lebesgue-spectrum problem asks for a probability-preserving transformation with simple Lebesgue spectrum on its mean-zero $L^2$ space. The formalization constructs a smooth volume-preserving diffeomorphism of the three-torus with this property. It gives a real-valued cyclic vector whose integer iterates form an orthonormal basis of the entire mean-zero space, and an isometric identification under which the Koopman operator becomes multiplication by the coordinate function on the circle. The transformation is also ergodic.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/144.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":94292,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-smooth-three-torus-diffeomorphism-with-simple-Lebesgue-spectrum-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"145-01","slug":"Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026","familyId":"145","title":"Rokhlin's multiple-mixing problem for one transformation","abstract":"Every invertible mixing probability-preserving transformation is mixing of every finite order. Thus Rokhlin's multiple-mixing problem for a single transformation has an affirmative answer.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every invertible mixing probability-preserving transformation is mixing of all finite orders, resolving Rokhlin's multiple-mixing problem for a single transformation. Correlations among any finite collection of measurable sets converge to the product of their measures whenever all pairwise time separations diverge.","pdfPath":"preprints/Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","texPaths":["preprints/Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-reduction.tex","preprints/Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-arrays.tex","preprints/Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-operators.tex","preprints/Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026/build/figures/operator-square.tex","preprints/Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-labels-blocks.tex","preprints/Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-cancellation.tex","preprints/Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-main-proof.tex","preprints/Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every invertible mixing probability-preserving transformation is mixing of every finite order. Thus Rokhlin's multiple-mixing problem for a single transformation has an affirmative answer.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":286},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The structural argument","sourceOffset":5176},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From lines to the contradiction","sourceOffset":8020},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":10139},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite-alphabet witness of zero entropy","sourceOffset":11068},{"kind":"section","title":"Ordered arrays and conditional locality","sourceOffset":17793},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The initial array laws","sourceOffset":18124},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The class of extensions","sourceOffset":20308},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conditioning on parent planes","sourceOffset":27829},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The square and its grouped kernels","sourceOffset":34249},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The geometry of the parameters","sourceOffset":41932},{"kind":"section","title":"The operator square","sourceOffset":45306}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Every invertible mixing probability-preserving transformation is mixing of every finite order. More explicitly, under the assumptions above, for every $k\\ge3$ and every $A_1,\\ldots,A_k\\in\\mathcal F$, \\[ \\mu\\bigl(A_1\\cap T^{-n_1}A_2\\cap\\cdots\\cap T^{-(n_1+\\cdots+n_{k-1})}A_k\\bigr) \\longrightarrow\\prod_{i=1}^k\\mu(A_i) \\] as $\\min(n_1,\\ldots,n_{k-1})\\to\\infty$ through positive integers. No standardness or countable-generation assumption is imposed on the original probability space.","sourceOffset":953,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Zero-entropy witness","label":"prop:zero-entropy-witness","text":"Suppose an invertible mixing transformation fails to be mixing of every order. There then exist an integer $k\\geq3$, a finite alphabet $\\mathcal A$, a shift-invariant probability measure $\\mu_X$ on $X=\\mathcal A^{\\Z}$, and real one-site functions $f_1,\\ldots,f_k$ such that the shift $T_X$ is mixing of every order less than $k$, \\[ \\int f_i\\dd\\mu_X=0,\\qquad \\norm{f_i}_{\\infty}\\leq1, \\qquad \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac1nH_{\\mu_X}(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)=0. \\] Moreover, there are $\\delta>0$ and integer layouts \\[ 0=d_1(b)<d_2(b)<\\cdots<d_k(b),\\qquad \\min_{i<k}\\bigl(d_{i+1}(b)-d_i(b)\\bigr)\\longrightarrow\\infty, \\] for which \\begin{equation} \\int_X\\prod_{i=1}^k f_i(T_X^{d_i(b)}x)\\dd\\mu_X(x)\\geq\\delta \\quad\\text{for every }b. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":11400,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"An array object","label":"def:array-object","text":"An array object consists of standard probability systems $(Y,m_0,T_Y)$ and $(U,\\nu,T_U)$, factor maps \\[ \\kappa:Y\\longrightarrow X,\\qquad u:Y\\longrightarrow U, \\] and probability laws $m_n$ on $Y^{I^n}$ for $n\\geq0$. All transformations are invertible and the factor maps commute with time. The laws satisfy the following requirements. \\begin{enumerate} \\item Each $m_n$ is invariant under simultaneous $T_Y$ and projects coordinatewise to $\\rho_n$. For $n\\geq1$, fixing any axis at a slot gives $m_{n-1}$, and every proper family of last-axis planes is independent under $m_n$. \\item For $n\\geq1$, c","sourceOffset":21253,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:active-space","text":"Let $\\lambda$ be a probability law on a finite product $S_1\\times\\cdots\\times S_m$ of standard spaces, with coordinate variables $Y_1,\\ldots,Y_m$ and marginals $\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_m$. The active space at coordinate $i$ is the closed subspace \\[ \\mathcal A_i(\\lambda) =\\overline{\\Span}\\bigl\\{ \\E_\\lambda[F(Y_j:j\\ne i)\\mid Y_i]:F\\text{ bounded and measurable} \\bigr\\}\\subseteq L^2(\\mu_i). \\] An exact tuple is a tuple of measurable functions $\\chi_i:S_i\\to\\T$ for which \\begin{equation} \\prod_{i=1}^m\\chi_i(Y_i)=c\\qquad\\lambda\\text{-almost surely}, \\qquad c\\in\\T. \\end{equation} An exact line at coordina","sourceOffset":46168,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/145.md","scopeText":"Rokhlin's multiple-mixing problem asks whether ordinary mixing of one invertible probability-preserving transformation implies mixing of every finite order. The formalization proves this implication. For each $k\\ge3$ and every $k$ measurable sets, the measure of their translated intersection tends to the product of their measures as all successive time gaps tend to infinity.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/145.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":134304,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"146-01","slug":"Positive-Metric-Entropy-for-the-Standard-Map-at-Large-Parameters-September-23-2026","familyId":"146","title":"Positive Metric Entropy for the Standard Map at Large Parameters","abstract":"We prove that the standard sine map of the two-dimensional torus has positive metric entropy with respect to normalized area for every sufficiently large positive parameter. This gives a full parameter tail, and hence answers Sinai's positive-parameter-measure conjecture affirmatively.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the standard sine map on the two-dimensional torus has positive metric entropy with respect to area for every sufficiently large positive parameter. This establishes Sinai's positive-parameter-measure conjecture for the original family, with the stronger conclusion of a full parameter tail.","pdfPath":"preprints/Positive-Metric-Entropy-for-the-Standard-Map-at-Large-Parameters-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Positive-Metric-Entropy-for-the-Standard-Map-at-Large-Parameters-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Positive-Metric-Entropy-for-the-Standard-Map-at-Large-Parameters-September-23-2026","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","texPaths":["preprints/Positive-Metric-Entropy-for-the-Standard-Map-at-Large-Parameters-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Positive-Metric-Entropy-for-the-Standard-Map-at-Large-Parameters-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Positive-Metric-Entropy-for-the-Standard-Map-at-Large-Parameters-September-23-2026/build/sections/setup.tex","preprints/Positive-Metric-Entropy-for-the-Standard-Map-at-Large-Parameters-September-23-2026/build/sections/cancellation.tex","preprints/Positive-Metric-Entropy-for-the-Standard-Map-at-Large-Parameters-September-23-2026/build/sections/local-scarcity.tex","preprints/Positive-Metric-Entropy-for-the-Standard-Map-at-Large-Parameters-September-23-2026/build/sections/bridge.tex","preprints/Positive-Metric-Entropy-for-the-Standard-Map-at-Large-Parameters-September-23-2026/build/figures/bridge-sweep.tex","preprints/Positive-Metric-Entropy-for-the-Standard-Map-at-Large-Parameters-September-23-2026/build/sections/scales.tex","preprints/Positive-Metric-Entropy-for-the-Standard-Map-at-Large-Parameters-September-23-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Positive-Metric-Entropy-for-the-Standard-Map-at-Large-Parameters-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Positive-Metric-Entropy-for-the-Standard-Map-at-Large-Parameters-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the standard sine map of the two-dimensional torus has positive metric entropy with respect to normalized area for every sufficiently large positive parameter. This gives a full parameter tail, and hence answers Sinai's positive-parameter-measure conjecture affirmatively.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":349},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context","sourceOffset":1938},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof mechanism","sourceOffset":6100},{"kind":"section","title":"Transfer matrices and growth shortfalls","sourceOffset":10771},{"kind":"section","title":"Nearly full-rate cancellations","sourceOffset":16602},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A solution small at both endpoints","sourceOffset":17847},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Outward paths and good indices","sourceOffset":19392},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Matching the two expansion scales","sourceOffset":24886},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The local input","sourceOffset":29158},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From local scarcity to exponential decay","sourceOffset":30426},{"kind":"section","title":"Local scarcity at matched scales","sourceOffset":35779},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Control on a single rescaled path","sourceOffset":36814}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"main:entropy","text":"There exists $k_0>0$ such that \\[ h_m(f_k)>0\\qquad\\text{for every }k\\ge k_0. \\] Equivalently, for every such $k$ the largest Lyapunov exponent of $f_k$ is positive on a set of positive $m$-measure.","sourceOffset":925,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Nonuniformly hyperbolic ergodic component","label":"main:components","text":"For each $k\\ge k_0$ there is a measurable $f_k$-invariant set $E=E(k)$ with $m(E)>0$ such that $(E,m(E)^{-1}m|_E,f_k)$ is ergodic and $f_k$ has one positive and one negative Lyapunov exponent almost everywhere on $E$. For some integer $N=N(k,E)\\ge1$, there is a measurable partition $E=E_0\\sqcup\\cdots\\sqcup E_{N-1}$ such that $f_k(E_j)=E_{j+1\\bmod N}$ and $(E_j,m(E_j)^{-1}m|_{E_j},f_k^N)$ is Bernoulli for every $j$. All invariance and partition statements are modulo $m$-null sets; each $E_j$ has area $m(E)/N>0$.","sourceOffset":4706,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"can:good-definition","text":"An index $2\\le j\\le N-2$ is good for the selected normalized solution if \\begin{equation} \\begin{aligned} \\tau_j-\\tau_l&\\ge c(j-l)&& (1\\le l<j),\\\\ \\tau_{j+1}-\\tau_j&\\ge c,\\\\ \\upsilon_j-\\upsilon_{j+1}&\\ge c, &\\upsilon_{j+1}-\\upsilon_{j+2}&\\ge c. \\end{aligned} \\end{equation} For a path extending beyond a selected index, the same inequalities define goodness without reference to a particular terminal length $N$.","sourceOffset":21313,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/146.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves Sinai's positive-entropy conjecture for the standard sine map on the two-dimensional torus in the stronger form of a full positive parameter tail. For every sufficiently large parameter, normalized area has positive metric entropy, and a positive-area set has positive largest Lyapunov exponent with the stated derivative-growth limit.\n\nIt also constructs a positive-area invariant ergodic hyperbolic component. That component splits into finitely many cyclic pieces on each of which the corresponding iterate is Bernoulli. No genericity assumption on the parameter is used.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/146.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":142950,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Positive-Metric-Entropy-for-the-Standard-Map-at-Large-Parameters-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"147-01","slug":"Continuous-Phase-Foliations-Create-Analytic-Caustic-Collars-September-24-2026","familyId":"147","title":"Continuous Phase Foliations Create Analytic Caustic Collars","abstract":"For a smooth strictly convex planar billiard of positive curvature, a continuous foliation of a full grazing annulus by individually invariant essential curves forces an analytic boundary and a jointly analytic collar of smooth strictly convex caustics. The physical-collar rigidity theorem proved in the companion article then implies that the table is an ellipse.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the near-boundary Birkhoff conjecture for smooth strictly convex planar billiards of positive curvature. Such a billiard is an ellipse whenever a full grazing annulus is continuously foliated by individually invariant essential curves. A continuous physical collar of smooth closed convex caustics also suffices.","pdfPath":"preprints/Continuous-Phase-Foliations-Create-Analytic-Caustic-Collars-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Continuous-Phase-Foliations-Create-Analytic-Caustic-Collars-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Continuous-Phase-Foliations-Create-Analytic-Caustic-Collars-September-24-2026","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","texPaths":["preprints/Continuous-Phase-Foliations-Create-Analytic-Caustic-Collars-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Continuous-Phase-Foliations-Create-Analytic-Caustic-Collars-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Continuous-Phase-Foliations-Create-Analytic-Caustic-Collars-September-24-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Continuous-Phase-Foliations-Create-Analytic-Caustic-Collars-September-24-2026/build/sections/analytic.tex","preprints/Continuous-Phase-Foliations-Create-Analytic-Caustic-Collars-September-24-2026/build/sections/joint.tex","preprints/Continuous-Phase-Foliations-Create-Analytic-Caustic-Collars-September-24-2026/build/sections/caustics.tex","preprints/Continuous-Phase-Foliations-Create-Analytic-Caustic-Collars-September-24-2026/build/figures/phase-to-caustic.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Continuous-Phase-Foliations-Create-Analytic-Caustic-Collars-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For a smooth strictly convex planar billiard of positive curvature, a continuous foliation of a full grazing annulus by individually invariant essential curves forces an analytic boundary and a jointly analytic collar of smooth strictly convex caustics. The physical-collar rigidity theorem proved in the companion article then implies that the table is an ellipse.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":413},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and significance","sourceOffset":2802},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The regularity reduction","sourceOffset":5866},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Structure of the argument","sourceOffset":7797},{"kind":"section","title":"From continuous leaves to periodic invariant graphs","sourceOffset":10953},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Oriented lines and the two twists","sourceOffset":11528},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A separation barrier for continuous leaves","sourceOffset":14741},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Graphs, recurrence, and rational rotation","sourceOffset":21134},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Closing stationary chains","sourceOffset":28319},{"kind":"section","title":"Analyticity of the boundary","sourceOffset":31143},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Normalization and the two analytic inputs","sourceOffset":32005},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The equations satisfied by the actual boundary","sourceOffset":37933}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Phase-foliation rigidity","label":"thm:main","text":"Suppose a \\(T\\)-invariant open annulus \\(U\\subset M\\) contains \\((\\R/L\\mathbb Z)\\times(0,\\eta)\\) for some \\(\\eta>0\\). Suppose there is a homeomorphism \\[ H:\\mathbb S^1\\times(0,1)\\longrightarrow U \\] whose leaves are essential simple closed curves, each individually invariant under \\(T\\). Then \\(\\Omega\\) is an ellipse: there are \\(c\\in\\R^2\\) and a real symmetric positive-definite matrix \\(Q\\) such that \\[ \\Omega=\\{x\\in\\R^2:(x-c)^TQ(x-c)<1\\}. \\] Circles are included.","sourceOffset":1141,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Physical-collar rigidity, {\\citep[Definition 1.1 and Theorem 1.2","label":"thm:nb-rigidity","text":"{NB}}] A bounded strictly convex planar domain with \\(C^\\infty\\) regular boundary of positive curvature and a continuous physical caustic collar is an ellipse.","sourceOffset":6486,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":91131,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Continuous-Phase-Foliations-Create-Analytic-Caustic-Collars-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"147-02","slug":"Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026","familyId":"147","title":"Rigidity of Smooth Billiards with a Continuous Caustic Collar","abstract":"We prove that a smooth strictly convex planar billiard with positive curvature is an ellipse whenever a full neighborhood of its boundary is continuously foliated by smooth closed convex caustics. No differentiability across the leaves is assumed. This resolves the near-boundary Birkhoff conjecture in the stated smooth class.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the near-boundary Birkhoff conjecture for smooth strictly convex planar billiards of positive curvature. Such a billiard is an ellipse whenever a full grazing annulus is continuously foliated by individually invariant essential curves. A continuous physical collar of smooth closed convex caustics also suffices.","pdfPath":"preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","texPaths":["preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026/build/figures/caustic.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-analytic.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-joint.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-continuation.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026/build/figures/monodromy.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-shapes.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-cones.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026/build/figures/cone.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-profiles.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-critical.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-model.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026/build/figures/mesh.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-odd-slice.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026/build/sections/11-sign-test.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026/build/figures/last-site.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that a smooth strictly convex planar billiard with positive curvature is an ellipse whenever a full neighborhood of its boundary is continuously foliated by smooth closed convex caustics. No differentiability across the leaves is assumed. This resolves the near-boundary Birkhoff conjecture in the stated smooth class.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":401},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and scope","sourceOffset":4449},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The main ideas","sourceOffset":7439},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and dependencies","sourceOffset":9845},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":11482},{"kind":"section","title":"Rational caustics and analytic regularity","sourceOffset":12387},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The invariant graphs and their rational leaves","sourceOffset":13508},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The normalized action","sourceOffset":20057},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform stationary meshes","sourceOffset":23413},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The high-frequency equations","sourceOffset":31781},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A Wiener-space inverse without loss of width","sourceOffset":40878},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Analytic approximation and uniqueness","sourceOffset":43890}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"If \\(\\Omega\\) has a continuous caustic collar, then \\(\\Omega\\) is an ellipse. Equivalently, there are \\(c\\in\\R^2\\) and a real symmetric positive-definite matrix \\(Q\\) such that \\[ \\Omega=\\{x\\in\\R^2:(x-c)^TQ(x-c)<1\\}. \\] Circles are included.","sourceOffset":1791,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Analytic regularity","label":"thm:analytic","text":"Under the hypotheses of \\Cref{thm:main}, the support function of the table and its boundary are real analytic.","sourceOffset":13325,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"A continuous caustic collar","label":"def:caustic-collar","text":"Let \\(\\Omega\\subset\\R^2\\) be a bounded strictly convex domain whose boundary is a \\(C^\\infty\\) simple closed regular curve with positive curvature. A smooth closed convex caustic is a \\(C^\\infty\\) simple closed regular curve in \\(\\Omega\\), bounding a strictly convex set, such that a billiard segment tangent to the curve remains tangent after reflection at either endpoint. Here reflection of an incoming unit velocity \\(v\\) at a boundary point with outward unit normal \\(n\\) is \\[ v\\longmapsto v-2(v\\cdot n)n. \\] A continuous caustic collar consists of a relatively open set \\(A\\subset\\overline\\Ome","sourceOffset":831,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"cont:width-definition","text":"For \\(F=L\\) or \\(F=X\\), let \\(h_F(t)\\) be the supremum of the heights \\(b>s_*\\) for which the primary branch of \\(F\\) has a single-valued meromorphic extension to \\[ \\{-s_*<\\Im y<b\\}\\times V \\] for some open neighborhood \\(V\\subset B\\) of \\(t\\), agreeing with the initial branch on the basic strip. The neighborhood \\(V\\) may depend on \\(b\\). The value \\(+\\infty\\) is allowed.","sourceOffset":87779,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":489376,"sourceFileCount":17,"statementCount":39,"definitionCount":4,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Rigidity-of-Smooth-Billiards-with-a-Continuous-Caustic-Collar-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"148-01","slug":"The-entropy-rate-dimension-formula-for-self-similar-measures-on-the-line-September-24-2026","familyId":"148","title":"The entropy-rate dimension formula for self-similar measures on the line","abstract":"We prove that the Hausdorff dimension of every finite real self-similar measure equals the minimum of one and its random-walk entropy rate divided by its Lyapunov exponent. Exact overlaps are allowed, and the contraction ratios may be unequal and negative. This resolves the entropy-rate dimension conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every self-similar measure on the line generated by finitely many contracting similarities, proves $\\dim_{\\mathrm H}\\mu=\\min\\{1,h_{\\mathrm{RW}}/\\chi\\}$, where $h_{\\mathrm{RW}}$ is the entropy rate of random composed maps and χ the average logarithmic contraction. This resolves the entropy-rate dimension conjecture without a separation assumption, allowing exact overlaps and unequal contraction ratios.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-entropy-rate-dimension-formula-for-self-similar-measures-on-the-line-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-entropy-rate-dimension-formula-for-self-similar-measures-on-the-line-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-entropy-rate-dimension-formula-for-self-similar-measures-on-the-line-September-24-2026","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","texPaths":["preprints/The-entropy-rate-dimension-formula-for-self-similar-measures-on-the-line-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-entropy-rate-dimension-formula-for-self-similar-measures-on-the-line-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-entropy-rate-dimension-formula-for-self-similar-measures-on-the-line-September-24-2026/build/sections/entropy.tex","preprints/The-entropy-rate-dimension-formula-for-self-similar-measures-on-the-line-September-24-2026/build/sections/pairs.tex","preprints/The-entropy-rate-dimension-formula-for-self-similar-measures-on-the-line-September-24-2026/build/sections/types.tex","preprints/The-entropy-rate-dimension-formula-for-self-similar-measures-on-the-line-September-24-2026/build/sections/windows.tex","preprints/The-entropy-rate-dimension-formula-for-self-similar-measures-on-the-line-September-24-2026/build/sections/corollaries.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-entropy-rate-dimension-formula-for-self-similar-measures-on-the-line-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the Hausdorff dimension of every finite real self-similar measure equals the minimum of one and its random-walk entropy rate divided by its Lyapunov exponent. Exact overlaps are allowed, and the contraction ratios may be unequal and negative. This resolves the entropy-rate dimension conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":372},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and proof strategy","sourceOffset":4791},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The entropy-rate upper bound","sourceOffset":11735},{"kind":"section","title":"Entropy between two scales","sourceOffset":13639},{"kind":"section","title":"Pair mass below a fixed scale","sourceOffset":25879},{"kind":"section","title":"Conditioning on block types","sourceOffset":35647},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Parameters and conditional coding laws","sourceOffset":37462},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From the deficit to bands of pair mass","sourceOffset":44727},{"kind":"section","title":"Disjoint windows of entropy gain","sourceOffset":48074},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A gain after averaging the unobserved future","sourceOffset":50321},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Choosing disjoint block windows","sourceOffset":53296},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Summing over target depths","sourceOffset":57471}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every finite nonempty indexed family $\\varphi_i(x)=r_i x+t_i$ on $\\R$ with $0<|r_i|<1$, and every strictly positive probability vector $p$, its self-similar measure satisfies \\[ \\dim_H\\mu_{\\Phi,p} =\\min\\left\\{1,\\frac{h_{\\mathrm{RW}}(\\Phi,p)}{\\chi(\\Phi,p)}\\right\\}. \\] No separation assumption is required; exact overlaps and repeated generators are allowed.","sourceOffset":3826,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Pair mass in finite scale bands","label":"prop:pair-bands","text":"Under the assumptions and notation of Lemma \\ref{lem:type-deficit}, there exist $c>0$ and an integer $n_0$ such that, for each $n\\ge n_0$, there is a deterministic finite integer $B_n\\ge a_n$ satisfying \\begin{equation} \\E\\!\\left[\\mathbf1_{E_n} \\sum_{\\ell=a_n}^{B_n}c_\\ell(\\nu_{\\omega,n})\\right]\\ge cn. \\end{equation} Here $c_\\ell(\\nu)$ is the maximum total weight of fair two-point laws with separations in $[2^{-\\ell},2^{1-\\ell})$ whose weighted sum is a submeasure of $\\nu$, as in Lemma \\ref{lem:pair-bound}. The constants $b,A,A',c,n_0$ are independent of $n$ and $\\omega$; no growth bound on $B_n$ is asserted.","sourceOffset":45057,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Pair capacity","label":"def:pair-capacity","text":"Let $\\nu$ be a probability law with finite support $F\\subset\\R$. For $\\ell\\in\\mathbb Z$, let $\\mathcal E_\\ell(F)$ be the set of unordered pairs $\\{x,y\\}\\subset F$ satisfying $2^{-\\ell}\\le |x-y|<2^{1-\\ell}$. Define $c_\\ell(\\nu)$ as the maximum of \\[ \\sum_{e\\in\\mathcal E_\\ell(F)}w_e \\quad\\text{subject to}\\quad w_e\\ge0,\\qquad \\frac12\\sum_{e\\ni x}w_e\\le\\nu(\\{x\\})\\quad(x\\in F). \\] When $\\mathcal E_\\ell(F)$ is empty, this maximum is zero.","sourceOffset":26596,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/148.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves $\\dim_H\\mu=\\min\\{1,h_{\\mathrm{RW}}/\\chi\\}$ for the lower Hausdorff dimension of every finite real self-similar probability measure with positive weights and nonzero contraction ratios of absolute value below one. Here $h_{\\mathrm{RW}}$ is the entropy rate of random affine compositions and $\\chi$ is the corresponding Lyapunov exponent in the same logarithmic base. Ratios may be signed and unequal, and exact overlaps are allowed.\n\nIt also gives two consequences without exact overlaps: the usual entropy-over-Lyapunov formula for a common positive contraction ratio, and the attractor formula $\\dim_H K=\\min\\{1,s\\}$, where $s\\ge0$ is the unique solution of $\\sum_i|r_i|^s=1$. Absolute continuity is outside these statements.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/148.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":64266,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-entropy-rate-dimension-formula-for-self-similar-measures-on-the-line-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/The-entropy-rate-dimension-formula-for-self-similar-measures-on-the-line-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/The-entropy-rate-dimension-formula-for-self-similar-measures-on-the-line-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"149-01","slug":"Uniform-Permanence-in-Weakly-Reversible-Mass-Action-Systems-October-5-2026","familyId":"149","title":"Uniform Permanence in Weakly Reversible Mass-Action Systems","abstract":"We prove the permanence conjecture for finite weakly reversible mass-action systems with fixed positive reaction rates. Every positive stoichiometric compatibility class admits one compact convex forward-invariant set that every positive trajectory in that class enters in finite time, even when the class is unbounded. The set depends only on the network, the rates, and the class; the entry time may depend on the initial state. Thus, after its entry time, every concentration satisfies the same positive lower and finite upper bounds throughout that class.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the permanence conjecture for every finite weakly reversible mass-action system with fixed positive rate constants. Every positive stoichiometric compatibility class, even an unbounded one, has a common compact convex forward-invariant absorbing set. All positive trajectories in that class therefore eventually share positive lower and finite upper concentration bounds.","pdfPath":"preprints/Uniform-Permanence-in-Weakly-Reversible-Mass-Action-Systems-October-5-2026/permanence.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-Permanence-in-Weakly-Reversible-Mass-Action-Systems-October-5-2026/permanence.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-Permanence-in-Weakly-Reversible-Mass-Action-Systems-October-5-2026","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","texPaths":["preprints/Uniform-Permanence-in-Weakly-Reversible-Mass-Action-Systems-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Uniform-Permanence-in-Weakly-Reversible-Mass-Action-Systems-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Uniform-Permanence-in-Weakly-Reversible-Mass-Action-Systems-October-5-2026/build/sections/affine.tex","preprints/Uniform-Permanence-in-Weakly-Reversible-Mass-Action-Systems-October-5-2026/build/figures/intervals.tex","preprints/Uniform-Permanence-in-Weakly-Reversible-Mass-Action-Systems-October-5-2026/build/sections/trapping.tex","preprints/Uniform-Permanence-in-Weakly-Reversible-Mass-Action-Systems-October-5-2026/build/sections/permanence.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Uniform-Permanence-in-Weakly-Reversible-Mass-Action-Systems-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the permanence conjecture for finite weakly reversible mass-action systems with fixed positive reaction rates. Every positive stoichiometric compatibility class admits one compact convex forward-invariant set that every positive trajectory in that class enters in finite time, even when the class is unbounded. The set depends only on the network, the rates, and the class; the entry time may depend on the initial state. Thus, after its entry time, every concentration satisfies the same positive lower and finite upper bounds throughout that class.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":605},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The problem and main result","sourceOffset":1146},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and the contribution","sourceOffset":3706},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":7368},{"kind":"section","title":"Affine minima with arbitrary tolerance","sourceOffset":10488},{"kind":"section","title":"Compact plateaux and strict increase","sourceOffset":25015},{"kind":"section","title":"A common absorbing set for each class","sourceOffset":38462}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $C\\subset\\Z_{\\geq0}^d$ be finite, let $R\\subset C\\times C$ be weakly reversible, and fix positive rate constants in \\eqref{eq:mass-action}. For every positive stoichiometric class $P$ there is a nonempty compact convex set $K_P\\subset P$ with the following properties. Every initial point $x^0\\in P$ gives a unique global positive solution, and there is a finite $T(x^0)\\geq0$ such that \\[ x(t)\\in K_P\\qquad(t\\geq T(x^0)). \\] Moreover, $K_P$ is forward invariant. In particular, there is $\\eps_P\\in(0,1)$, depending only on the network, the fixed rates, and $P$, such that every such solution satisfies \\begin{equation} \\eps_P\\leq x_i(t)\\leq\\eps_P^{-1} \\qquad(1\\leq i\\leq d,\\ t\\geq T(x^0)). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":2651,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Uniform activity bound","label":"cor:activity","text":"Let $m\\geq1$, $a<b$, and $E:[a,b]^m\\to(0,\\infty)$ be as in Lemma \\ref{lem:affine}. Fix a family $\\Lambda$ supplied by that lemma. There is $h_0\\in(0,1)$ such that, for every $0<h<h_0$ and every $p\\in[a,b]^m$, each label $\\lambda\\in\\Lambda$ active at $X_h(p)$ satisfies \\[ \\norm{p-r_\\lambda}_\\infty<E(r_\\lambda). \\]","sourceOffset":24136,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/149.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":41684,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Uniform-Permanence-in-Weakly-Reversible-Mass-Action-Systems-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Uniform-Permanence-in-Weakly-Reversible-Mass-Action-Systems-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Uniform-Permanence-in-Weakly-Reversible-Mass-Action-Systems-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"149-02","slug":"Boundedness-and-persistence-of-weakly-reversible-mass-action-systems-September-25-2026","familyId":"149","title":"Boundedness and persistence of weakly reversible mass-action systems","abstract":"We prove the boundedness and persistence conjectures for finite weakly reversible mass-action systems with positive constant reaction rates. For every positive initial condition, the solution exists for all forward time, and every concentration remains bounded above and bounded away from zero. The bounds may depend on the initial condition, and no boundedness assumption is imposed on its stoichiometric compatibility class.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the permanence conjecture for every finite weakly reversible mass-action system with fixed positive rate constants. Every positive stoichiometric compatibility class, even an unbounded one, has a common compact convex forward-invariant absorbing set. All positive trajectories in that class therefore eventually share positive lower and finite upper concentration bounds.","pdfPath":"preprints/Boundedness-and-persistence-of-weakly-reversible-mass-action-systems-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Boundedness-and-persistence-of-weakly-reversible-mass-action-systems-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Boundedness-and-persistence-of-weakly-reversible-mass-action-systems-September-25-2026","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","texPaths":["preprints/Boundedness-and-persistence-of-weakly-reversible-mass-action-systems-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Boundedness-and-persistence-of-weakly-reversible-mass-action-systems-September-25-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","preprints/Boundedness-and-persistence-of-weakly-reversible-mass-action-systems-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Boundedness-and-persistence-of-weakly-reversible-mass-action-systems-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-affine.tex","preprints/Boundedness-and-persistence-of-weakly-reversible-mass-action-systems-September-25-2026/build/figures/interval-gluing.tex","preprints/Boundedness-and-persistence-of-weakly-reversible-mass-action-systems-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-dynamics.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Boundedness-and-persistence-of-weakly-reversible-mass-action-systems-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the boundedness and persistence conjectures for finite weakly reversible mass-action systems with positive constant reaction rates. For every positive initial condition, the solution exists for all forward time, and every concentration remains bounded above and bounded away from zero. The bounds may depend on the initial condition, and no boundedness assumption is imposed on its stoichiometric compatibility class.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":472},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The problem and main result","sourceOffset":1010},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the role of the construction","sourceOffset":4473},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":9666},{"kind":"section","title":"Affine labels with a prescribed tolerance","sourceOffset":13813},{"kind":"section","title":"From affine labels to persistent trajectories","sourceOffset":31807},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform approximation and sign constraints","sourceOffset":32310},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Flux across the ordered complex levels","sourceOffset":37655},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Derivatives of finite affine minima","sourceOffset":42029},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The invariant polytope and trajectory bounds","sourceOffset":44262},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Equilibria and complex-balanced convergence","sourceOffset":48219}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $d\\ge1$, let $\\mathcal C\\subset\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}^d$ be finite, let $\\mathcal R$ be weakly reversible, and let all reaction rates be positive constants. For every $x^0\\in\\R_{>0}^d$, the solution of \\eqref{eq:mass-action} with $x(0)=x^0$ exists for every $t\\ge0$. There is a number $\\varepsilon\\in(0,1)$, depending on the network, the rates, and $x^0$, such that \\begin{equation} \\varepsilon\\le x_i(t)\\le\\varepsilon^{-1} \\qquad(t\\ge0,\\ 1\\le i\\le d). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":2145,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Uniform activity bound","label":"cor:uniform-affine","text":"Let $m\\ge1$, $a<b$, and $E:[a,b]^m\\to(0,\\infty)$ be as in Lemma \\ref{lem:affine}. Fix a finite nonempty family $\\{(r_\\lambda,c_\\lambda):\\lambda\\in\\Lambda\\}$ supplied by that Lemma. There is $h_0>0$ such that, for every $0<h<h_0$ and every $p\\in[a,b]^m$, each label active at $X_h(p)$ satisfies \\[ \\norm{p-r_\\lambda}_\\infty<E(r_\\lambda). \\] The family is fixed before $h_0$ is chosen.","sourceOffset":30475,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/149.md","scopeText":"The boundedness and persistence conjectures for mass-action systems ask whether positive concentrations remain finite and separated from zero. The formalization proves this for every finite weakly reversible reaction network with positive constant reaction rates and every strictly positive initial state. A global forward solution exists, and one $\\varepsilon\\in(0,1)$ bounds every concentration of every global forward solution between $\\varepsilon$ and $\\varepsilon^{-1}$ for all nonnegative times. The bound may depend on the network, rates, and initial state.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/149.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":50892,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Boundedness-and-persistence-of-weakly-reversible-mass-action-systems-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Boundedness-and-persistence-of-weakly-reversible-mass-action-systems-September-25-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"150-01","slug":"Weak-mixing-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-October-5-2026","familyId":"150","title":"Weak mixing of triangular billiards with an irrational angle","abstract":"We prove that the unit-speed billiard flow in every nondegenerate Euclidean triangle with at least one angle irrational relative to π is weakly mixing for normalized Liouville measure. Equivalently, the product of the flow with itself is ergodic.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the billiard flow in every nondegenerate Euclidean triangle with at least one angle irrational relative to π is weakly mixing for normalized area times uniform direction. This strengthens ergodicity on the entire irrational-angle class, with no genericity or Diophantine restrictions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Weak-mixing-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-October-5-2026/weak-mixing-triangular-billiards.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Weak-mixing-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-October-5-2026/weak-mixing-triangular-billiards.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Weak-mixing-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-October-5-2026","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","texPaths":["preprints/Weak-mixing-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Weak-mixing-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Weak-mixing-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-October-5-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Weak-mixing-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-October-5-2026/build/sections/energy.tex","preprints/Weak-mixing-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-October-5-2026/build/sections/rigidity.tex","preprints/Weak-mixing-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-October-5-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Weak-mixing-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the unit-speed billiard flow in every nondegenerate Euclidean triangle with at least one angle irrational relative to \\(\\pi\\) is weakly mixing for normalized Liouville measure. Equivalently, the product of the flow with itself is ergodic.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":298},{"kind":"section","title":"The double and its angular coefficients","sourceOffset":7615},{"kind":"section","title":"Localized energy and individual coefficient bounds","sourceOffset":15178},{"kind":"section","title":"Transverse rigidity of bounded eigenfunctions","sourceOffset":24661},{"kind":"section","title":"Side reflections and weak mixing","sourceOffset":35741}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"The unit-speed billiard flow in every nondegenerate Euclidean triangle with at least one angle irrational relative to \\(\\pi\\) is weakly mixing for normalized Liouville measure.","sourceOffset":996,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Transverse rigidity","label":"prop:rigidity","text":"Let \\(M\\) be the regular oriented double of a fixed nondegenerate Euclidean triangle, and let \\(\\Psi_t\\) be its geodesic flow on \\(SM\\) with normalized Liouville measure. Suppose \\(\\lambda\\in\\R\\) and \\(f\\in L^\\infty(SM)\\) satisfy \\[ f\\circ\\Psi_t=e^{i\\lambda t}f \\quad\\text{in }L^2(SM)\\quad\\text{for every }t\\in\\R. \\] Then \\(f\\) has square-integrable horizontal weak derivatives and \\[ Yf=0,\\qquad Xf=i\\lambda f,\\qquad \\norm{\\nabla f}_2^2=\\lambda^2\\norm{f}_2^2. \\]","sourceOffset":14413,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/150.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":41673,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Weak-mixing-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"150-02","slug":"Ergodicity-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-September-25-2026","familyId":"150","title":"Ergodicity of triangular billiards with an irrational angle","abstract":"We prove that the unit-speed billiard flow in every nondegenerate Euclidean triangle with at least one angle irrational relative to π is ergodic for normalized area times uniform angular measure. No genericity or Diophantine condition is required. The flow is considered outside the null set of trajectories that hit a vertex.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the billiard flow in every nondegenerate Euclidean triangle with at least one angle irrational relative to π is weakly mixing for normalized area times uniform direction. This strengthens ergodicity on the entire irrational-angle class, with no genericity or Diophantine restrictions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Ergodicity-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-September-25-2026/Ergodicity-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-September-25-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Ergodicity-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-September-25-2026/Ergodicity-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-September-25-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Ergodicity-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-September-25-2026","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","texPaths":["preprints/Ergodicity-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Ergodicity-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the unit-speed billiard flow in every nondegenerate Euclidean triangle with at least one angle irrational relative to \\(\\pi\\) is ergodic for normalized area times uniform angular measure. No genericity or Diophantine condition is required. The flow is considered outside the null set of trajectories that hit a vertex.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":379},{"kind":"section","title":"The double and angular coefficients","sourceOffset":7369},{"kind":"section","title":"Smoothing away from vertices","sourceOffset":12154},{"kind":"section","title":"A localized coefficient estimate","sourceOffset":16444},{"kind":"section","title":"From flow invariance to parallel coefficients","sourceOffset":20628},{"kind":"section","title":"Holonomy and the conclusion","sourceOffset":29178},{"kind":"section","title":"Quantum ergodicity and nodal domains","sourceOffset":30884}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(Q\\) be the interior of a nondegenerate Euclidean triangle with angles \\(\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma\\). Suppose that at least one of \\[ \\alpha/\\pi,\\qquad \\beta/\\pi,\\qquad \\gamma/\\pi \\] is irrational. The unit-speed billiard flow \\(\\Phi_t\\), with specular reflection at the open sides, is ergodic on \\(Q\\times S^1\\) for the probability measure \\[ d\\mu=\\frac{dA\\,d\\theta}{2\\pi\\operatorname{Area}(Q)}. \\] Here trajectories hitting a vertex in either time direction are discarded. In particular, if a measurable set \\(A\\) satisfies \\(\\mu(\\Phi_t^{-1}A\\mathbin\\triangle A)=0\\) for every \\(t\\in\\R\\), then \\(\\mu(A)\\in\\{0,1\\}\\).","sourceOffset":2604,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:rigidity","text":"If \\(f\\in L^\\infty(SM)\\) is invariant almost everywhere under \\(\\Psi_t\\) for each \\(t\\in\\R\\), then \\[ \\pa f_j=\\pb f_j=0\\qquad(j\\in\\Z) \\] as distributions on the regular surface.","sourceOffset":21002,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/150.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves that the unit-speed billiard flow in every nondegenerate Euclidean triangle with at least one angle irrational relative to $\\pi$ is ergodic for normalized area times uniform angular measure. It constructs the flow outside the null set of exceptional trajectories, proves uniqueness of the flight chain there, and establishes measure preservation and the flow law almost everywhere. No genericity or Diophantine condition on the irrational angle is assumed.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/150.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":35949,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Ergodicity-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Ergodicity-of-triangular-billiards-with-an-irrational-angle-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"151-01","slug":"A-C1-Counterexample-to-the-Entropy-Conjecture-September-25-2026","familyId":"151","title":"A C^1 Counterexample to the Entropy Conjecture","abstract":"We disprove the general C1 self-map formulation of Shub's entropy conjecture. We construct a noninvertible C1 self-map of a compact smooth manifold without boundary whose topological entropy is zero, while its action on second real homology has eigenvalue 2. Thus the topological entropy is strictly smaller than the logarithm of the homological spectral radius.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a noninvertible C1 self-map of a compact smooth manifold with zero topological entropy but eigenvalue 2 on second homology. This disproves the homological entropy lower bound for general C1 self-maps: homological growth need not force positive orbit complexity.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-C1-Counterexample-to-the-Entropy-Conjecture-September-25-2026/article.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-C1-Counterexample-to-the-Entropy-Conjecture-September-25-2026/article.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-C1-Counterexample-to-the-Entropy-Conjecture-September-25-2026","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","texPaths":["preprints/A-C1-Counterexample-to-the-Entropy-Conjecture-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/A-C1-Counterexample-to-the-Entropy-Conjecture-September-25-2026/build/source/construction.tex","preprints/A-C1-Counterexample-to-the-Entropy-Conjecture-September-25-2026/build/source/clock-figure.tex","preprints/A-C1-Counterexample-to-the-Entropy-Conjecture-September-25-2026/build/source/orbits.tex","preprints/A-C1-Counterexample-to-the-Entropy-Conjecture-September-25-2026/build/source/entropy.tex","preprints/A-C1-Counterexample-to-the-Entropy-Conjecture-September-25-2026/build/source/homology.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-C1-Counterexample-to-the-Entropy-Conjecture-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove the general \\(C^1\\) self-map formulation of Shub's entropy conjecture. We construct a noninvertible \\(C^1\\) self-map of a compact smooth manifold without boundary whose topological entropy is zero, while its action on second real homology has eigenvalue \\(2\\). Thus the topological entropy is strictly smaller than the logarithm of the homological spectral radius.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":423},{"kind":"section","title":"The construction","sourceOffset":5928},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The clock and its intervals","sourceOffset":6907},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prediction inputs and the update rule","sourceOffset":9725},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Continuous differentiability","sourceOffset":15361},{"kind":"section","title":"Attraction of every orbit","sourceOffset":20727},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bounded clocks and complete passages","sourceOffset":22635},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prediction and linearity up to a register's readout","sourceOffset":26273},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Phase alignment and escape","sourceOffset":31057},{"kind":"section","title":"Vanishing entropy","sourceOffset":36738},{"kind":"section","title":"The homological eigenvalue","sourceOffset":46644}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are a positive integer \\(q\\) and a \\(C^1\\) self-map \\(f\\) of the compact smooth manifold \\[ M=(\\R/100\\Z)\\times(S^2)^{q+1} \\] such that \\(\\htop(f)=0\\) and \\(f_*:H_2(M;\\R)\\to H_2(M;\\R)\\) has eigenvalue \\(2\\). In particular, \\[ \\htop(f)=0<\\log 2\\le \\log\\rho(f_*). \\]","sourceOffset":2766,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:regularity","text":"For the choices above, with $q$ finite and $L>\\max\\{B,2\\}$, the series \\eqref{eq:input} defines $C^1$ maps $U_\\ell\\colon(\\mathbb R/100\\mathbb Z)\\times\\Sigma\\to\\mathbb C$. Consequently, \\eqref{eq:map} defines a $C^1$ self-map of the nonempty compact smooth manifold $M$.","sourceOffset":15404,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/151.md","scopeText":"Shub's entropy conjecture predicts that a smooth self-map's topological entropy is at least the logarithm of the spectral radius of its action on real homology. The formalization gives a counterexample to the general $C^1$ self-map version: a noninvertible $C^1$ map on a compact smooth manifold without boundary has topological entropy zero, while its action on second real homology has a nonzero eigenvector with eigenvalue $2$. Thus the homological lower bound is strictly positive and fails for this map.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/151.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":49426,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-C1-Counterexample-to-the-Entropy-Conjecture-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"152-01","slug":"A-finite-entropy-system-without-a-smooth-positive-volume-model-September-25-2026","familyId":"152","title":"A zero-entropy system without a smooth positive-volume model","abstract":"We construct an ergodic invertible transformation of a standard nonatomic probability space with zero Kolmogorov–Sinai entropy that has no smooth positive-volume model. More precisely, it is not measurably conjugate to any C∞ diffeomorphism preserving a strictly positive smooth probability density on a compact finite-dimensional manifold. A single example excludes every finite dimension, including models on nonorientable manifolds and manifolds with smooth boundary.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a zero-entropy ergodic invertible transformation of a standard nonatomic probability space that is not measurably conjugate to any C∞ diffeomorphism preserving a strictly positive smooth probability density on a compact finite-dimensional manifold. One example rules out every finite dimension.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-finite-entropy-system-without-a-smooth-positive-volume-model-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-finite-entropy-system-without-a-smooth-positive-volume-model-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-finite-entropy-system-without-a-smooth-positive-volume-model-September-25-2026","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","texPaths":["preprints/A-finite-entropy-system-without-a-smooth-positive-volume-model-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-finite-entropy-system-without-a-smooth-positive-volume-model-September-25-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/A-finite-entropy-system-without-a-smooth-positive-volume-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-finite-entropy-system-without-a-smooth-positive-volume-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-stationary.tex","preprints/A-finite-entropy-system-without-a-smooth-positive-volume-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-linear.tex","preprints/A-finite-entropy-system-without-a-smooth-positive-volume-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-switching.tex","preprints/A-finite-entropy-system-without-a-smooth-positive-volume-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-words.tex","preprints/A-finite-entropy-system-without-a-smooth-positive-volume-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-process.tex","preprints/A-finite-entropy-system-without-a-smooth-positive-volume-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-obstruction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-finite-entropy-system-without-a-smooth-positive-volume-model-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct an ergodic invertible transformation of a standard nonatomic probability space with zero Kolmogorov--Sinai entropy that has no smooth positive-volume model. More precisely, it is not measurably conjugate to any $C^\\infty$ diffeomorphism preserving a strictly positive smooth probability density on a compact finite-dimensional manifold. A single example excludes every finite dimension, including models on nonorientable manifolds and manifolds with smooth boundary.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":553},{"kind":"section","title":"Stationary estimates for derivative growth","sourceOffset":9803},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Deviation from a dyadic chord","sourceOffset":10303},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A stationary triangular derivative representation","sourceOffset":13551},{"kind":"section","title":"Linear estimates on finite orbit segments","sourceOffset":20667},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Triangular products and separation of rates","sourceOffset":21192},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Spaces that can be chosen uniformly in a spatial bin","sourceOffset":32072},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Relative stability of exterior products","sourceOffset":41525},{"kind":"section","title":"Switching a past and a future with controlled volume","sourceOffset":44955},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Grids, retained pairs, and fixed endpoint projections","sourceOffset":47561},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A boundary inverse and a nonlinear orbit","sourceOffset":57873},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Relative minors and the joint determinant","sourceOffset":66297}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exists an ergodic invertible measure-preserving transformation $(X,\\mu,T)$ of a standard nonatomic probability space such that $h_\\mu(T)=0$ and $(X,\\mu,T)$ is not measurably conjugate, on invariant conull sets, to any $C^\\infty$ diffeomorphism of a compact finite-dimensional manifold preserving a strictly positive smooth probability density. The manifold may have smooth boundary and need not be orientable.","sourceOffset":5284,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Good finite orbit segments","label":"prop:good-frames","text":"For each $\\eta>0$ there is a positive sequence $(b_k)_{k\\geq1}$ with \\begin{equation} b_k\\geq 2^{-k/10}, \\qquad \\sum_{k\\geq1}b_k^2<\\infty, \\end{equation} and compact sets $\\widetilde{\\mathcal G}_k\\subset\\mathcal F M$ and $\\mathcal G_k=\\pi(\\widetilde{\\mathcal G}_k)\\subset M$ such that \\[ \\widetilde\\nu(\\widetilde{\\mathcal G}_k)\\geq1-\\eta/100, \\qquad \\nu(\\mathcal G_k)\\geq1-\\eta/100. \\] For $n=2^k$, $b=b_k$, and $e\\in\\widetilde{\\mathcal G}_k$, define \\[ A_t=R(\\widetilde S^t e)\\quad(-n\\leq t<n), \\qquad \\lambda_j=\\frac1{2n}\\sum_{t=-n}^{n-1}\\ln(A_t)_{jj}. \\] Then for every $j$ and every integer $-n\\leq u\\leq n$, \\begin{equation} \\left|\\sum_{t=-n}^{u-1}\\ln(A_t)_{jj} -(u+n)\\lambda_j\\right|\\leq bn. \\end{equation} Consequently, on every subinterval $-n\\leq u\\leq v\\leq n$, \\begin{equation} \\left|\\sum_{t=u}^{v-1}\\ln(A_t)_{jj} -(v-u)\\lambda_j\\right|\\leq2bn. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":16655,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/152.md","scopeText":"The paper asks whether a measure-preserving system can be represented by a smooth diffeomorphism preserving positive smooth volume. The formalized supporting result constructs an ergodic invertible transformation of a standard nonatomic probability space with finite Kolmogorov–Sinai entropy that has no such model on any compact finite-dimensional manifold, including manifolds with smooth boundary. The comparison is measurable conjugacy after discarding null sets.\n\nThe linked statement gives finite entropy; the paper's stronger zero-entropy conclusion is outside this statement.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/152.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":130828,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-finite-entropy-system-without-a-smooth-positive-volume-model-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"153-01","slug":"Arithmetic-classification-and-non-Pisot-singularity-for-Bernoulli-convolutions-October-3-2026","familyId":"153","title":"Arithmetic classification and non-Pisot singularity for Bernoulli convolutions","abstract":"We give an arithmetic classification of singular and absolutely continuous unbiased Bernoulli convolutions for every parameter $\\lambda\\in(0,1)$. The criterion is expressed through one-sided approximation by explicitly defined finite sets of algebraic units; it is an infinite approximation condition, not a finite membership algorithm. We also establish singular examples beyond reciprocals of Pisot numbers: singularity holds at the reciprocal of every quartic Salem number in $(1,2)$ and at the reciprocal of a specified non-Pisot root of an explicit degree-31 polynomial.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Classifies singular and absolutely continuous unbiased Bernoulli convolutions for every $\\lambda\\in(0,1)$ by an infinite, one-sided approximation condition using explicit finite sets of algebraic units. It also proves singularity at reciprocals of every quartic Salem number in $(1,2)$, giving examples beyond reciprocal Pisot parameters.","pdfPath":"preprints/Arithmetic-classification-and-non-Pisot-singularity-for-Bernoulli-convolutions-October-3-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Arithmetic-classification-and-non-Pisot-singularity-for-Bernoulli-convolutions-October-3-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Arithmetic-classification-and-non-Pisot-singularity-for-Bernoulli-convolutions-October-3-2026","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","texPaths":["preprints/Arithmetic-classification-and-non-Pisot-singularity-for-Bernoulli-convolutions-October-3-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Arithmetic-classification-and-non-Pisot-singularity-for-Bernoulli-convolutions-October-3-2026/build/classification.tex","preprints/Arithmetic-classification-and-non-Pisot-singularity-for-Bernoulli-convolutions-October-3-2026/build/salem.tex","preprints/Arithmetic-classification-and-non-Pisot-singularity-for-Bernoulli-convolutions-October-3-2026/build/nonpisot.tex","preprints/Arithmetic-classification-and-non-Pisot-singularity-for-Bernoulli-convolutions-October-3-2026/build/certificate.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Arithmetic-classification-and-non-Pisot-singularity-for-Bernoulli-convolutions-October-3-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give an arithmetic classification of singular and absolutely continuous unbiased Bernoulli convolutions for every parameter $\\lambda\\in(0,1)$. The criterion is expressed through one-sided approximation by explicitly defined finite sets of algebraic units; it is an infinite approximation condition, not a finite membership algorithm. We also establish singular examples beyond reciprocals of Pisot numbers: singularity holds at the reciprocal of every quartic Salem number in $(1,2)$ and at the reciprocal of a specified non-Pisot root of an explicit degree-$31$ polynomial.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":623},{"kind":"section","title":"An arithmetic criterion for the measure type","sourceOffset":7630},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Measure-theoretic preliminaries","sourceOffset":10719},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Algebraic clusters imply singularity","sourceOffset":13475},{"kind":"section","title":"Constructing the algebraic approximants","sourceOffset":15805},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Overlap of the first-bit measures","sourceOffset":16317},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Completion to irreducible polynomials","sourceOffset":21767},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof of the converse and the quantifiers","sourceOffset":29966},{"kind":"section","title":"Recovery of classical parameter classes","sourceOffset":31740},{"kind":"section","title":"Degree-four Salem parameters","sourceOffset":35461},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Multipliers with small unit-circle conjugates","sourceOffset":36805},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The loss in the cosine products","sourceOffset":43767}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"sal:main","text":"Let $\\beta\\in(1,2)$ be a Salem number of degree four. Then the unbiased Bernoulli convolution $\\nu_{1/\\beta}$ is singular with respect to Lebesgue measure.","sourceOffset":35961,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"np:main","text":"The polynomial \\(p\\) in \\eqref{np:polynomial} has a unique real root \\(\\beta\\in(1.8392,1.8393)\\). This root is not a Pisot number, and \\(\\nu_{1/\\beta}\\) is singular with respect to Lebesgue measure.","sourceOffset":53918,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":107768,"sourceFileCount":5,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Arithmetic-classification-and-non-Pisot-singularity-for-Bernoulli-convolutions-October-3-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"154-01","slug":"Pointwise-Multiple-Ergodic-Averages-for-Mixing-Transformations-October-4-2026","familyId":"154","title":"Pointwise Multiple Ergodic Averages for Mixing Transformations","abstract":"Let T be an invertible mixing probability-preserving transformation. For every integer n ≥ 2 and every fixed tuple of bounded measurable functions, we prove that the consecutive multiple ergodic averages of length n converge almost everywhere to the product of the integrals, as the averaging length tends to infinity through all positive integers. The probability space need not be standard, and no rate of mixing is required.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves almost-everywhere convergence of consecutive multiple ergodic averages of every finite length for invertible mixing probability-preserving transformations. For each fixed tuple of bounded functions, the limit is the product of their integrals, along all positive averaging lengths. No mixing rate or standardness assumption on the probability space is required.","pdfPath":"preprints/Pointwise-Multiple-Ergodic-Averages-for-Mixing-Transformations-October-4-2026/multiple-ergodic-averages.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Pointwise-Multiple-Ergodic-Averages-for-Mixing-Transformations-October-4-2026/multiple-ergodic-averages.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Pointwise-Multiple-Ergodic-Averages-for-Mixing-Transformations-October-4-2026","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","texPaths":["preprints/Pointwise-Multiple-Ergodic-Averages-for-Mixing-Transformations-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Pointwise-Multiple-Ergodic-Averages-for-Mixing-Transformations-October-4-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Pointwise-Multiple-Ergodic-Averages-for-Mixing-Transformations-October-4-2026/build/sections/inputs.tex","preprints/Pointwise-Multiple-Ergodic-Averages-for-Mixing-Transformations-October-4-2026/build/sections/selected.tex","preprints/Pointwise-Multiple-Ergodic-Averages-for-Mixing-Transformations-October-4-2026/build/sections/channels.tex","preprints/Pointwise-Multiple-Ergodic-Averages-for-Mixing-Transformations-October-4-2026/build/sections/correlations.tex","preprints/Pointwise-Multiple-Ergodic-Averages-for-Mixing-Transformations-October-4-2026/build/sections/square.tex","preprints/Pointwise-Multiple-Ergodic-Averages-for-Mixing-Transformations-October-4-2026/build/figures/square.tex","preprints/Pointwise-Multiple-Ergodic-Averages-for-Mixing-Transformations-October-4-2026/build/sections/packing.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Pointwise-Multiple-Ergodic-Averages-for-Mixing-Transformations-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let $T$ be an invertible mixing probability-preserving transformation. For every integer $n\\ge2$ and every fixed tuple of bounded measurable functions, we prove that the consecutive multiple ergodic averages of length $n$ converge almost everywhere to the product of the integrals, as the averaging length tends to infinity through all positive integers. The probability space need not be standard, and no rate of mixing is required.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":479},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and prior work","sourceOffset":1749},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The arbitrary-length step","sourceOffset":3784},{"kind":"section","title":"Dynamical inputs and standard models","sourceOffset":6390},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Standard models and the two companion inputs","sourceOffset":6776},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Relative weak mixing and distal factors","sourceOffset":10060},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Triple convergence along all lengths","sourceOffset":15192},{"kind":"section","title":"Selected lengths and their joining laws","sourceOffset":16964},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A positive witness at selected lengths","sourceOffset":17578},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Selected laws on all extensions","sourceOffset":21512},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Distal parameters and small marginals","sourceOffset":26618},{"kind":"section","title":"Channels and closure under selected laws","sourceOffset":33104}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Suppose that $T$ satisfies \\eqref{eq:mixing}. For every integer $n\\ge2$ and every fixed $f_1,\\ldots,f_n\\in L^\\infty(\\mu)$, \\[ A_N(f_1,\\ldots,f_n)(x) \\longrightarrow\\prod_{j=1}^n\\int_X f_j\\dd\\mu \\qquad\\text{for $\\mu$-almost every }x, \\] as $N$ tends to infinity through all positive integers. The probability space need not be standard, and no rate of mixing is required.","sourceOffset":1135,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Higher-order mixing, companion input","label":"thm:multiple-mixing","text":"Let $T$ be an invertible mixing probability-preserving transformation. For any fixed $s\\ge2$, $g_1,\\ldots,g_s\\in L^\\infty(\\mu)$, and integer times $t_1,\\ldots,t_s$, \\[ \\int_X\\prod_{j=1}^s g_j(T^{t_j}x)\\dd\\mu(x) \\longrightarrow\\prod_{j=1}^s\\int_Xg_j\\dd\\mu \\quad\\text{as}\\quad \\min_{i\\ne j}|t_i-t_j|\\longrightarrow\\infty. \\] For $s=1$ the equality holds for every time.","sourceOffset":8122,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Channel","label":"def:channel","text":"A channel is a flow $Y$ over the suspension $C$, with factor map $\\pi_Y:Y\\to C$, together with a finite list of probability-preserving maps $D_\\gamma:Y\\to C$, called outputs, and positive rational numbers $q_\\gamma$, such that \\[ D_\\gamma(S_Y^t y)=S_C^{q_\\gamma t}D_\\gamma(y) \\] for each fixed $t$, modulo null sets. Partition the output indices into three groups, numbered $1,2,3$; empty groups are allowed. Set \\[ x_0=\\xi\\circ\\pi_Y,\\qquad x_\\gamma=\\xi\\circ D_\\gamma, \\qquad d_Y=(x_\\gamma)_\\gamma, \\] and let $d_{Y,H}$ be the sublist from groups in $H\\subseteq\\{1,2,3\\}$. Let $V$ collect the phase c","sourceOffset":33701,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":79860,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Pointwise-Multiple-Ergodic-Averages-for-Mixing-Transformations-October-4-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Pointwise-Multiple-Ergodic-Averages-for-Mixing-Transformations-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Pointwise-Multiple-Ergodic-Averages-for-Mixing-Transformations-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"154-02","slug":"Pointwise-convergence-of-fourfold-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026","familyId":"154","title":"Pointwise convergence of fourfold ergodic averages for mixing transformations","abstract":"We prove that fourfold ergodic averages along the times $n,2n,3n,4n$ converge almost everywhere to the product of the integrals for every invertible, bimeasurable, mixing probability-preserving transformation and every fixed choice of bounded measurable inputs. Convergence holds along all positive integer averaging lengths. No quantitative mixing rate is assumed, and the probability space need not be standard.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves almost-everywhere convergence of consecutive multiple ergodic averages of every finite length for invertible mixing probability-preserving transformations. For each fixed tuple of bounded functions, the limit is the product of their integrals, along all positive averaging lengths. No mixing rate or standardness assumption on the probability space is required.","pdfPath":"preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-fourfold-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/fourfold-ergodic-averages.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-fourfold-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/fourfold-ergodic-averages.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-fourfold-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","texPaths":["preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-fourfold-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-fourfold-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-fourfold-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/sections/02-structure.tex","preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-fourfold-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/sections/03-selected.tex","preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-fourfold-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/sections/04-packing.tex","preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-fourfold-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/sections/05-channels.tex","preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-fourfold-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/sections/06-activation.tex","preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-fourfold-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/sections/07-lower-length.tex","preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-fourfold-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-fourfold-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that fourfold ergodic averages along the times $n,2n,3n,4n$ converge almost everywhere to the product of the integrals for every invertible, bimeasurable, mixing probability-preserving transformation and every fixed choice of bounded measurable inputs. Convergence holds along all positive integer averaging lengths. No quantitative mixing rate is assumed, and the probability space need not be standard.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":459},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and inputs","sourceOffset":1745},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The difficulty caused by selected lengths","sourceOffset":4839},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From a correlation to a contradiction","sourceOffset":6973},{"kind":"section","title":"Convergence inputs and relative structure","sourceOffset":9041},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Standard systems and the pointwise inputs","sourceOffset":9455},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Distal factors and relatively weakly mixing extensions","sourceOffset":11420},{"kind":"section","title":"Selected laws and their distal parameters","sourceOffset":22586},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From failure to a sequence of selectors","sourceOffset":23002},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A functorial law on four outputs","sourceOffset":27043},{"kind":"section","title":"Packing tensors into a subspace of small dimension","sourceOffset":34990},{"kind":"section","title":"Channels and preservation of independence","sourceOffset":45108}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $(X,\\mathcal F,\\mu)$ be a probability space and let $T:X\\to X$ be invertible, bimeasurable, measure preserving, and mixing in the sense of \\eqref{eq:mixing}. For every $f_1,f_2,f_3,f_4\\in L^\\infty(\\mu)$, \\[ \\frac1N\\sum_{n=1}^N f_1(T^nx)f_2(T^{2n}x)f_3(T^{3n}x)f_4(T^{4n}x) \\longrightarrow \\prod_{j=1}^4\\int_X f_j\\dd\\mu \\] for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, along all positive integers $N$. The original probability space need not be standard.","sourceOffset":1255,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Relative structure","label":"prop:structure","text":"The following statements hold for standard probability-preserving systems. \\begin{enumerate} \\item There is a maximal measure-distal factor $W_Y$. It contains the invariant factor, and $Y\\to W_Y$ is relatively weakly mixing. A distal factor pulls back into the maximal distal factor of any extension. Every measure-preserving automorphism commuting with $R$ preserves $W_Y$. \\item Factors and joinings of distal systems are distal. A distal system is relatively distal over each of its factors. For every nonzero integer $q$, $R$ is distal if and only if $R^q$ is distal. \\item Relative weak mixing passes to nonzero integer powers and to conditionally independent base change. More generally, the product of relatively weakly mixing fibre laws over any invariant joining of their base laws is relatively weakly mixing over that joining. \\item A relatively weakly mixing extension and a relatively distal extension of the same base are relatively disjoint: the only invariant joining which identifies their base coordinates is the relatively independent joining. \\item If $R$ is distal, then, for every $d\\ge1$ and bounded $g_1,\\ldots,g_d$, the averages \\[ \\frac1N\\sum_{n=1}^N\\prod_{i=1}^d g_i(R^{in}y) \\] converge almost surely. \\end{enumerate} If $(S_Y^t)_{t\\in\\R}$ is a probability-preserving flow, the maximal distal factor $W_Y$ of $S_Y^1$ is a flow factor. Its action at every nonzero rational time is distal.","sourceOffset":12697,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:channel","text":"A channel is a flow extension $\\pi_Y:(Y,m_Y,S_Y^t)\\to C$ together with a finite list of output factors $D_\\alpha:Y\\to C$, of positive rational speeds $q_\\alpha$, partitioned into three groups $\\mathcal D_1,\\mathcal D_2,\\mathcal D_3$. Empty groups are allowed. Let $x_0$ be the spatial coordinate of $\\pi_Y$, let $x_\\alpha$ be that of $D_\\alpha$, and let $V$ collect the phases of $\\pi_Y$ and of all the $D_\\alpha$. The following conditional laws are required: \\begin{align} \\mathcal L((x_\\alpha)_\\alpha\\mid V) &=\\bigotimes_\\alpha\\mu, \\\\ \\mathcal L\\bigl(x_0,(x_\\alpha)_{\\alpha\\in\\cup_{j\\in J}\\mathcal ","sourceOffset":46003,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":127073,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Pointwise-convergence-of-fourfold-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"154-03","slug":"Triple-ergodic-averages-with-distinct-integer-slopes-October-4-2026","familyId":"154","title":"Triple ergodic averages with distinct integer slopes","abstract":"For every invertible mixing probability-preserving transformation, triple ergodic averages of bounded measurable functions along any three pairwise distinct nonzero integer slopes converge almost everywhere to the product of their integrals. The slopes may be positive or negative.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves almost-everywhere convergence of consecutive multiple ergodic averages of every finite length for invertible mixing probability-preserving transformations. For each fixed tuple of bounded functions, the limit is the product of their integrals, along all positive averaging lengths. No mixing rate or standardness assumption on the probability space is required.","pdfPath":"preprints/Triple-ergodic-averages-with-distinct-integer-slopes-October-4-2026/triple-ergodic-distinct-slopes.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Triple-ergodic-averages-with-distinct-integer-slopes-October-4-2026/triple-ergodic-distinct-slopes.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Triple-ergodic-averages-with-distinct-integer-slopes-October-4-2026","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","texPaths":["preprints/Triple-ergodic-averages-with-distinct-integer-slopes-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Triple-ergodic-averages-with-distinct-integer-slopes-October-4-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Triple-ergodic-averages-with-distinct-integer-slopes-October-4-2026/build/sections/local.tex","preprints/Triple-ergodic-averages-with-distinct-integer-slopes-October-4-2026/build/sections/transfer.tex","preprints/Triple-ergodic-averages-with-distinct-integer-slopes-October-4-2026/build/sections/analytic.tex","preprints/Triple-ergodic-averages-with-distinct-integer-slopes-October-4-2026/build/sections/arithmetic.tex","preprints/Triple-ergodic-averages-with-distinct-integer-slopes-October-4-2026/build/sections/completion.tex","preprints/Triple-ergodic-averages-with-distinct-integer-slopes-October-4-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Triple-ergodic-averages-with-distinct-integer-slopes-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every invertible mixing probability-preserving transformation, triple ergodic averages of bounded measurable functions along any three pairwise distinct nonzero integer slopes converge almost everywhere to the product of their integrals. The slopes may be positive or negative.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":327},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and predecessors","sourceOffset":1801},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Analytic input and the coefficient extension","sourceOffset":4269},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof structure","sourceOffset":6573},{"kind":"section","title":"The local estimate and the coefficient algebra","sourceOffset":7571},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Normalization and local forms","sourceOffset":7921},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Integer reconstruction and quadratic cancellation","sourceOffset":11260},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The fixed-input constant","sourceOffset":13572},{"kind":"section","title":"From the local estimate to pointwise convergence","sourceOffset":14601},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Smooth profiles and cancellation kernels","sourceOffset":15438},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Integer sequences and finite orbit segments","sourceOffset":22221},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Identifying the limit by mixing","sourceOffset":30165}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every system satisfying \\eqref{eq:mixing}, every triple of pairwise distinct nonzero integers $\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2,\\lambda_3$, and every bounded measurable $f_1,f_2,f_3:X\\to\\C$, the averages in \\eqref{eq:averages} satisfy \\[ A_N(x)\\longrightarrow\\prod_{j=1}^3\\int_X f_j\\,d\\mu \\quad\\text{for almost every }x, \\] as $N$ tends to infinity through all positive integers. The exceptional null set may depend on the slopes and the functions.","sourceOffset":1050,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Local estimate with variation in depth","label":"thm:local","text":"Fix the primitive distinct integers $b_0=0,b_1,b_2,b_3$ and the parameters $\\delta_0,C_0$. Let $\\mathcal T$ be a finite dyadic tree with root $J$ and largest depth $d$, and let $\\mathcal I\\subseteq\\mathcal T$. Assign an admissible kernel to every $I\\in\\mathcal I$. For each $0\\le j\\le3$, let $z_{j,s}:J\\to\\C$, $0\\le s\\le d$, be measurable, and partition $\\{0,\\ldots,d\\}$ into $W_j\\ge1$ deterministic nonempty consecutive blocks. Suppose that \\[ \\mathcal V_B(z_j)\\le W_j^{-1/2}\\quad\\text{almost everywhere on }J \\] for each block in the $j$th partition. Extend every input by zero outside $J$. Then \\begin{equation} \\sum_{I\\in\\mathcal I}\\abs{I}\\, \\abs{H_I(z_{0,s(I)},z_{1,s(I)},z_{2,s(I)},z_{3,s(I)})} \\le C_{b,\\delta_0,C_0}\\abs{J}. \\end{equation} The constant is independent of the lattice, tree, depth range, subcollection, kernels, and the four partitions and their sizes.","sourceOffset":9797,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Local kernels","label":"def:kernels","text":"Fix $\\delta_0>0$ and $C_0\\ge1$. For an interval $I$ of length $r$, a kernel $w_I\\in C_c^\\infty(\\R^2;\\C)$ is admissible if \\begin{enumerate} \\item $\\dist(x+b_jt,\\partial I)\\ge\\delta_0r$ and $x+b_jt\\in I$ for every $(x,t)\\in\\supp w_I$ and $0\\le j\\le3$; \\item for every $v\\in\\R$ and $0\\le j\\le3$, \\begin{equation} \\int_\\R w_I(v-b_jt,t)\\,dt=0; \\end{equation} \\item for nonnegative integers $a,d$ and $n=a+d$, \\begin{equation} \\norm{\\partial_x^a\\partial_t^d w_I}_\\infty \\le r^{-n}\\bigl(C_0(n+2)\\bigr)^{C_0(n+2)}. \\end{equation} \\end{enumerate} For scalar measurable inputs define \\begin{equation} H_I(z_0,","sourceOffset":8613,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Averaging profile","label":"def:tr-profile","text":"An averaging profile is a real \\(\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\R)\\) with \\begin{equation} \\int_\\R\\varphi=1, \\qquad \\int_\\R t^j\\varphi(t)\\,dt=0\\quad(1\\leq j\\leq3), \\end{equation} and, for some \\(G\\geq1\\), \\begin{equation} \\|\\varphi^{(n)}\\|_\\infty \\leq \\bigl(G(n+2)\\bigr)^{G(n+2)}\\qquad(n\\geq0). \\end{equation} Write \\(\\varphi_a(t)=a^{-1}\\varphi(t/a)\\) for \\(a>0\\). Profiles are necessarily signed: a nonnegative smooth function of mass one cannot have zero second moment.","sourceOffset":15493,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":159350,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Triple-ergodic-averages-with-distinct-integer-slopes-October-4-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"154-04","slug":"Pointwise-convergence-of-triple-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026","familyId":"154","title":"Pointwise convergence of triple ergodic averages for mixing transformations","abstract":"We prove pointwise convergence of triple ergodic averages for every invertible mixing probability preserving transformation T of an arbitrary probability space $(X,\\mathcal F,\\mu)$, with measurable inverse. For every triple of bounded measurable functions $f_1,f_2,f_3:X\\to\\mathbb C$, $\\displaystyle \\frac1N\\sum_{n=1}^N f_1(T^nx)f_2(T^{2n}x)f_3(T^{3n}x) \\longrightarrow \\prod_{j=1}^3\\int_X f_j\\,d\\mu$ for μ-almost every x as $N\\to\\infty$ through all positive integers.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves almost-everywhere convergence of consecutive multiple ergodic averages of every finite length for invertible mixing probability-preserving transformations. For each fixed tuple of bounded functions, the limit is the product of their integrals, along all positive averaging lengths. No mixing rate or standardness assumption on the probability space is required.","pdfPath":"preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-triple-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/pointwise-triple-ergodic-averages-mixing-transformations.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-triple-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/pointwise-triple-ergodic-averages-mixing-transformations.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-triple-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026","subjectId":"12","subject":"Dynamical systems and ergodic theory","subjectZh":"動力系統與遍歷理論","texPaths":["preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-triple-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/paper/main.tex","preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-triple-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/paper/preamble.tex","preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-triple-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/paper/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-triple-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/paper/sections/ergodic.tex","preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-triple-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/paper/sections/transfer.tex","preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-triple-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/paper/sections/rows.tex","preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-triple-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/paper/figures/row-chronology.tex","preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-triple-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/paper/sections/base.tex","preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-triple-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/paper/sections/localization.tex","preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-triple-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/paper/figures/frozen-depths.tex","preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-triple-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/paper/sections/completion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-triple-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/paper/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove pointwise convergence of triple ergodic averages for every invertible mixing probability preserving transformation \\(T\\) of an arbitrary probability space \\((X,\\mathcal F,\\mu)\\), with measurable inverse. For every triple of bounded measurable functions \\(f_1,f_2,f_3:X\\to\\mathbb C\\), \\[ \\frac1N\\sum_{n=1}^N f_1(T^nx)f_2(T^{2n}x)f_3(T^{3n}x) \\longrightarrow \\prod_{j=1}^3\\int_X f_j\\,d\\mu \\] for \\(\\mu\\)-almost every \\(x\\) as \\(N\\to\\infty\\) through all positive integers.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":543},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":5562},{"kind":"section","title":"Smooth orbit averages and the pointwise theorem","sourceOffset":10180},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The product limit in norm","sourceOffset":15286},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Moment approximation and all lengths","sourceOffset":23864},{"kind":"section","title":"Local forms and the orbit oscillation estimate","sourceOffset":31582},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Admissible local forms","sourceOffset":32011},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Kernels and the continuous test","sourceOffset":35486},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Planting integer sequences and transferring to orbits","sourceOffset":43941},{"kind":"section","title":"Bessel rows with inputs varying in depth","sourceOffset":51870},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The input size and variation bounds","sourceOffset":53251},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A persistent catalog and the maximal estimate","sourceOffset":57410}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $(X,\\mathcal F,\\mu)$ be an arbitrary probability space and let $T:X\\to X$ be an invertible mixing probability preserving transformation with measurable inverse. For every triple of bounded measurable functions $f_1,f_2,f_3:X\\to\\C$, \\[ A_N(f_1,f_2,f_3)(x) \\longrightarrow \\prod_{j=1}^3\\int_X f_j\\,d\\mu \\quad\\text{for $\\mu$-almost every }x \\] as $N\\to\\infty$ through all positive integers. The exceptional null set may depend on the triple of functions.","sourceOffset":1327,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Finite oscillation of smooth orbit averages","label":"prop:orbit-oscillation","text":"For every averaging profile \\(\\varphi\\), there is a constant \\(C_\\varphi<\\infty\\) with the following property. On every invertible probability preserving system, let \\(f_1,f_2,f_3:X\\to\\mathbb C\\) be measurable with \\(|f_j(x)|\\leq1\\) for all \\(x\\), and define \\[ V_k^\\varphi(x)=\\sum_{n\\in\\mathbb Z}\\varphi_{2^k}(n) \\prod_{j=1}^3f_j(T^{jn}x), \\qquad k=0,1,2,\\ldots . \\] For every integer \\(w\\geq1\\) and every deterministic list of integers \\(0\\leq k_1<\\cdots<k_{w+1}\\), \\begin{equation} \\sum_{i=1}^w\\int_X \\max_{k_i\\leq\\ell\\leq k_{i+1}} |V_\\ell^\\varphi-V_{k_i}^\\varphi|\\,d\\mu \\leq C_\\varphi\\sqrt w. \\end{equation} The constant is independent of the system, the inputs, \\(w\\), and the endpoints.","sourceOffset":11836,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Averaging profiles","label":"def:averaging-profile","text":"An averaging profile is a real function \\(\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb R)\\) such that, for some \\(G\\geq1\\), \\begin{equation} \\|\\varphi^{(n)}\\|_\\infty \\leq\\bigl(G(n+2)\\bigr)^{G(n+2)} \\qquad(n=0,1,2,\\ldots), \\end{equation} and \\begin{equation} \\int_{\\mathbb R}\\varphi(t)\\,dt=1,\\qquad \\int_{\\mathbb R}t^j\\varphi(t)\\,dt=0\\quad(1\\leq j\\leq3). \\end{equation} The function may have either sign. For \\(a>0\\), write \\(\\varphi_a(t)=a^{-1}\\varphi(t/a)\\).","sourceOffset":10899,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Admissible local kernels","label":"def:transfer-local-kernels","text":"Fix \\(c_0>0\\) and \\(C_0\\geq1\\). For an interval \\(I\\) of length \\(r=|I|\\), a kernel \\(w_I\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2;\\mathbb C)\\) is admissible if the following hold. \\begin{enumerate} \\item For every \\((x,t)\\in\\operatorname{supp}w_I\\) and \\(j\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\), \\(x+jt\\in I\\) and \\(\\operatorname{dist}(x+jt,\\partial I)\\geq c_0r\\). \\item For every \\(v\\in\\mathbb R\\) and \\(j=0,1,2,3\\), \\[ \\int_{\\mathbb R}w_I(v-jt,t)\\,dt=0. \\] \\item For all nonnegative integers \\(p,q\\), with \\(n=p+q\\), \\[ \\|\\partial_x^p\\partial_t^q w_I\\|_\\infty \\leq r^{-n}\\bigl(C_0(n+2)\\bigr)^{C_0(n+2)}. \\] \\end{enumerate} For measura","sourceOffset":32360,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":232238,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Pointwise-convergence-of-triple-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Pointwise-convergence-of-triple-ergodic-averages-for-mixing-transformations-October-4-2026/build/paper/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"155-01","slug":"A-translational-tile-with-no-fully-periodic-tiling-in-dimension-three-September-23-2026","familyId":"155","title":"A translational tile with no fully periodic tiling in dimension three","abstract":"We construct a finite translational tile in ℤ3 that admits tilings but no fully periodic tiling. Its unit-cube thickening has the same property in ℝ3, even when arbitrary real translations are allowed. This gives a negative resolution of the periodic tiling conjecture in dimension three.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a finite translational tile in ℤ3 that tiles space but admits no fully periodic tiling, disproving the periodic tiling conjecture in the smallest possible lattice dimension. Its unit-cube thickening gives the same counterexample in ℝ3, even with arbitrary real translation vectors.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-translational-tile-with-no-fully-periodic-tiling-in-dimension-three-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-translational-tile-with-no-fully-periodic-tiling-in-dimension-three-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-translational-tile-with-no-fully-periodic-tiling-in-dimension-three-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-translational-tile-with-no-fully-periodic-tiling-in-dimension-three-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-translational-tile-with-no-fully-periodic-tiling-in-dimension-three-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-translational-tile-with-no-fully-periodic-tiling-in-dimension-three-September-23-2026/build/figures/proof-route.tex","preprints/A-translational-tile-with-no-fully-periodic-tiling-in-dimension-three-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-word-rule.tex","preprints/A-translational-tile-with-no-fully-periodic-tiling-in-dimension-three-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-cyclic-encoding.tex","preprints/A-translational-tile-with-no-fully-periodic-tiling-in-dimension-three-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-activation.tex","preprints/A-translational-tile-with-no-fully-periodic-tiling-in-dimension-three-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-common-solution.tex","preprints/A-translational-tile-with-no-fully-periodic-tiling-in-dimension-three-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-stacking.tex","preprints/A-translational-tile-with-no-fully-periodic-tiling-in-dimension-three-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-three-dimensions.tex","preprints/A-translational-tile-with-no-fully-periodic-tiling-in-dimension-three-September-23-2026/build/figures/rigidity.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-translational-tile-with-no-fully-periodic-tiling-in-dimension-three-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a finite translational tile in $\\Z^3$ that admits tilings but no fully periodic tiling. Its unit-cube thickening has the same property in $\\R^3$, even when arbitrary real translations are allowed. This gives a negative resolution of the periodic tiling conjecture in dimension three.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":342},{"kind":"section","title":"A word rule with no vertical period","sourceOffset":9504},{"kind":"section","title":"Encoding symbols in a cyclic finite coordinate","sourceOffset":21211},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Channels and the ambient group","sourceOffset":21944},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One graph and freely chosen high coordinates","sourceOffset":24484},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Dependence constraints on the useful outputs","sourceOffset":26304},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A cycle test for simultaneous activity","sourceOffset":29513},{"kind":"section","title":"Forcing activity and excluding full periodicity","sourceOffset":34593},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A shift list with two fibre orderings","sourceOffset":35158},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The activation tiles","sourceOffset":37217},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact coverage in a fibre","sourceOffset":40173},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Every required channel is active","sourceOffset":42887}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a finite nonempty set $T\\subset\\Z^3$ such that: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item $T$ tiles $\\Z^3$ by translations, but no translation set $A$ with $A\\oplus T=\\Z^3$ is invariant under a subgroup of finite index in $\\Z^3$; \\item the set $\\Omega=T+[0,1]^3$ tiles $\\R^3$ by translations up to null sets, but no such tiling has a translation set invariant under a lattice of full rank in $\\R^3$. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":1218,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"The vertical-period obstruction","label":"word:obstruction","text":"There is no array $W\\colon I\\times\\Z\\to\\Sigma$ satisfying the line rule, having every column nonconstant, and having a positive vertical period.","sourceOffset":16470,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Allowed words and the line rule","label":"word:definition","text":"A word $w\\colon I\\to\\Sigma$ is allowed if there are integers $a,b$, not both divisible by $p$, such that, for every $n\\in I$, \\[ w(n)= \\begin{cases} an+b\\pmod p, & p\\nmid an+b,\\\\[2pt] (an+b)/p\\pmod p, & p\\mid an+b\\text{ and }p^2\\nmid an+b. \\end{cases} \\] At indices with $p^2\\mid an+b$, the value may be any member of $\\Sigma$. An array $W\\colon I\\times\\Z\\to\\Sigma$ satisfies the line rule if \\[ n\\longmapsto W(n,dn+e) \\] is an allowed word for every $d,e\\in\\Z$. A positive vertical period of $W$ is an integer $M>0$ such that $W(n,m+M)=W(n,m)$ for all $(n,m)\\in I\\times\\Z$.","sourceOffset":10209,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/155.md","scopeText":"The periodic-tiling question asks whether a finite tile that tiles a lattice must admit a periodic tiling. The formalized counterexample is a finite tile in $\\mathbb Z^3$ that tiles but has no complement invariant under a finite-index subgroup. Its unit-cube thickening also tiles $\\mathbb R^3$ almost everywhere but admits no fully periodic tiling, even with arbitrary real translation vectors. The result also establishes that dimension three is the least lattice dimension where this failure occurs.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/155.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":85723,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-translational-tile-with-no-fully-periodic-tiling-in-dimension-three-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"156-01","slug":"A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026","familyId":"156","title":"A nine-dimensional counterexample to Borsuk's covering assertion","abstract":"The compact set of rank-one orthogonal projectors on ℝ4, with the Frobenius metric, cannot be covered by ten sets of strictly smaller diameter. It therefore gives a counterexample to Borsuk's conjecture in dimension nine.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a compact subset of ℝ9 that cannot be covered by ten sets of strictly smaller diameter, disproving Borsuk's covering assertion already in dimension nine. The example consists of rank-one orthogonal projectors onto lines in ℝ4, with the Frobenius metric.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026/build/sections/extension.tex","preprints/A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026/build/sections/topology.tex","preprints/A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026/build/sections/six-labels.tex","preprints/A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026/build/figures/six-label-paths.tex","preprints/A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026/build/sections/ten-labels.tex","preprints/A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026/build/figures/partner-links.tex","preprints/A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026/build/figures/partner-blocks.tex","preprints/A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"The compact set of rank-one orthogonal projectors on $\\mathbb R^4$, with the Frobenius metric, cannot be covered by ten sets of strictly smaller diameter. It therefore gives a counterexample to Borsuk's conjecture in dimension nine.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":331},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the method","sourceOffset":1818},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":4310},{"kind":"section","title":"From diameter covers to projective maps","sourceOffset":6919},{"kind":"section","title":"An extension to symmetric matrices","sourceOffset":12804},{"kind":"section","title":"The equality case gives a simplicial cycle","sourceOffset":20945},{"kind":"section","title":"Triangle systems on six labels","sourceOffset":34674},{"kind":"section","title":"The obstruction on ten labels","sourceOffset":41270},{"kind":"section","title":"The diameter obstruction","sourceOffset":53405}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"The compact set \\[ X=\\{uu^{\\mathsf T}:u\\in\\mathbb R^4,\\ \\|u\\|=1\\} \\subset\\{A\\in\\operatorname{Sym}_4(\\mathbb R):\\operatorname{tr}A=1\\}, \\] with the Frobenius metric, has diameter $\\sqrt2$ and cannot be covered by ten subsets of diameter strictly less than $\\sqrt2$.","sourceOffset":1153,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"The number of labels","label":"thm:label-bound","text":"If $k\\ge2$, every smooth admissible map $f:\\mathbb P(\\mathbb R^k)\\to\\Delta^{m-1}$ satisfies $m\\ge N_k$. If $m=N_k$, its extension $F$ has mod-two degree one and is surjective. In this equality case, every pair of labels is an edge of the support complex of $f$.","sourceOffset":22121,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:admissible","text":"A smooth map $f=(f_1,\\ldots,f_m):\\mathbb P(V)\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is admissible if each $f_i$ is nonnegative, $\\sum_i f_i=1$, and, writing $S(x)=\\{i:f_i(x)>0\\}$, \\begin{equation} x\\perp y\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad S(x)\\cap S(y)=\\varnothing. \\end{equation} Here smoothness means smoothness of the real coordinate functions on the usual smooth manifold $\\mathbb P(V)$.","sourceOffset":10159,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:six-system","text":"A six-label triangle system on a set $C$ of cardinality six is a collection $\\mathcal T\\subseteq\\binom{C}{3}$ such that \\begin{enumerate} \\item exactly one of $T$ and $C\\setminus T$ belongs to $\\mathcal T$ for each $T\\in\\binom{C}{3}$; \\item every pair of elements of $C$ belongs to exactly two members of $\\mathcal T$. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":35136,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/156.md","scopeText":"Borsuk's conjecture predicts that every bounded subset of $\\mathbb R^d$ can be covered by $d+1$ sets of strictly smaller diameter. The formalized counterexample is the compact set of rank-one orthogonal projectors onto lines in $\\mathbb R^4$, with the Frobenius metric. It lies in the nine-dimensional affine space of trace-one symmetric matrices, has diameter $\\sqrt2$, and cannot be covered by ten arbitrary sets of smaller diameter.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/156.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":55812,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"157-01","slug":"A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"157","title":"A counterexample to Hadwiger's conjecture","abstract":"We disprove Hadwiger's conjecture by constructing arbitrarily large graphs whose chromatic number exceeds their Hadwiger number. The examples have independence number at most two, and even their ordinary fractional chromatic number exceeds their Hadwiger number. Thus they also disprove the fractional-coloring weakening discussed by Reed and Seymour.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves Hadwiger's conjecture even for fractional coloring: arbitrarily large finite simple graphs with independence number at most two satisfy $\\chi_f(G)\\gt h(G)$, where $h(G)$ is the largest clique-minor order. Also disproves the fractional Colin de Verdière chromatic bound $\\chi_f(G)\\le\\mu(G)+1$. In the positive direction, every finite nonempty graph satisfies $\\chi_{\\mathrm{list}}(G)\\le C h(G)$ for a universal constant C.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-history.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-geometry.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-distributions.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-frame-laws.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-moments.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-realization.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-collision.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-phases.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-preparation.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/09-histograms.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/10-product-tests.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/11-common-limit.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/12-status-span.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/13-rare-overlap.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/14-parameters.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove Hadwiger's conjecture by constructing arbitrarily large graphs whose chromatic number exceeds their Hadwiger number. The examples have independence number at most two, and even their ordinary fractional chromatic number exceeds their Hadwiger number. Thus they also disprove the fractional-coloring weakening discussed by Reed and Seymour.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":480},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":3677},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and its proof","sourceOffset":9310},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and conventions","sourceOffset":15664},{"kind":"section","title":"The hole relation and its tensor realization","sourceOffset":17205},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A triangle-free relation","sourceOffset":17676},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Construction parameters","sourceOffset":20556},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Moment matrices and cut profiles","sourceOffset":22272},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Raw vertices","sourceOffset":28110},{"kind":"section","title":"From supersaturation to a finite graph","sourceOffset":31048},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Entropy minimizers and terminal cuts","sourceOffset":32339},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Containers and the random sample","sourceOffset":36860}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are arbitrarily large integers \\(m\\) for which an \\(m\\)-vertex finite simple graph \\(G\\) satisfies \\[ \\alpha(G)\\le2 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\cm(G)<\\frac{m}{100}. \\]","sourceOffset":1272,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:hadwiger","text":"There are finite nonempty simple graphs \\(G\\) of arbitrarily large order \\(m\\) for which \\[ h(G)<\\frac{26m}{75}+\\frac23 <\\frac m2\\le\\chi_f(G)\\le\\chi(G). \\] Thus Hadwiger's conjecture and its fractional-coloring weakening \\(\\chi_f(G)\\le h(G)\\) are false.","sourceOffset":2440,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:hole","text":"Two elements \\(i,j\\in\\Omega\\) have a hole between them if there exist \\(\\lambda_i,\\lambda_j\\in\\cX\\) such that \\begin{equation} \\begin{gathered} U_i\\lambda_i=U_j\\lambda_j,\\qquad u_jU_i=r(\\lambda_i),\\qquad u_iU_j=r(\\lambda_j),\\\\ a(\\lambda_i)+a(\\lambda_j)=1. \\end{gathered} \\end{equation} The middle two identities are identities of linear functionals on the whole space \\(\\cX\\).","sourceOffset":18075,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Atoms and flavors","label":"def:atom","text":"For a tag $l$, selector $s$, and base point $z$ allowed at $l$, let $\\mathfrak a_l(s,z)\\in\\cX$ be the cut profile represented by $w_l=v(s,z)v(s,z)\\trans$ and $w_t=0$ for $t\\ne l$. Thus its component is $v(s,z)v(s,z)\\trans$ at every component incident with $l$, and zero elsewhere. Its diagonal bit is $q=\\eta(w_l)$ and its role bit is $a(\\mathfrak a_l(s,z))$. A generic flavor retains all base coordinates allowed at $l$. A shared-only flavor sets all $O$ blocks to zero. A pure flavor sets $S$ to zero and retains a specified subset of the allowed $O$ blocks, setting the others to zero. Every flavo","sourceOffset":81940,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/157.md","scopeText":"Hadwiger's conjecture predicts $\\chi(G)\\le h(G)$ for every finite graph, where $h(G)$ is the largest clique-minor order. The formalization constructs arbitrarily large finite simple counterexamples with independence number at most two. For a graph on $m$ vertices it proves\n$h(G)<26m/75+2/3<m/2\\le\\chi(G)$.\nThis disproves the ordinary chromatic form. The paper's fractional-chromatic strengthening and the Colin de Verdière consequence are outside this selected statement.\n\nThe Linear List Hadwiger conjecture asks for a universal linear bound on list chromatic number in terms of clique-minor size. The formalization proves that there is one integer $C\\ge1$ such that every finite nonempty simple graph $G$ satisfies $\\chi_{\\mathrm{list}}(G)\\le C h(G)$, where $h(G)$ is the largest order of a clique","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/157.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":357039,"sourceFileCount":18,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":7,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"157-02","slug":"A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"157","title":"A counterexample to the Colin de Verdière chromatic conjecture","abstract":"We disprove the Colin de Verdière chromatic conjecture by constructing graphs whose chromatic number exceeds their Colin de Verdière invariant by more than one. The examples have independence number at most two. In fact, their ordinary fractional chromatic number also exceeds their Colin de Verdière invariant by more than one.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves Hadwiger's conjecture even for fractional coloring: arbitrarily large finite simple graphs with independence number at most two satisfy $\\chi_f(G)\\gt h(G)$, where $h(G)$ is the largest clique-minor order. Also disproves the fractional Colin de Verdière chromatic bound $\\chi_f(G)\\le\\mu(G)+1$. In the positive direction, every finite nonempty graph satisfies $\\chi_{\\mathrm{list}}(G)\\le C h(G)$ for a universal constant C.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/matrix-trees.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/matrix-rank.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-geometry.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-distributions.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-moments.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-realization.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-collision.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-phases.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-preparation.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/09-histograms.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/10-product-tests.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/11-common-limit.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/12-status-span.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/12b-asymmetric.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/13-graph-transfer.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/14-parameters.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/15-measures.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove the Colin de Verdière chromatic conjecture by constructing graphs whose chromatic number exceeds their Colin de Verdière invariant by more than one. The examples have independence number at most two. In fact, their ordinary fractional chromatic number also exceeds their Colin de Verdière invariant by more than one.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":391},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The invariant and the main result","sourceOffset":639},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History, significance, and the construction","sourceOffset":2785},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The base graphs and the proof structure","sourceOffset":7020},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From distribution tests to finite graphs","sourceOffset":13323},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite trees for Lorentz blocks","sourceOffset":16085},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Hyperbolic flats and block normalization","sourceOffset":16623},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite configuration and its segment points","sourceOffset":20384},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weighted extraction from the conflict properties","sourceOffset":31730},{"kind":"section","title":"The matrix obstruction","sourceOffset":42847},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positive blocks and bounded spectral limits","sourceOffset":44725},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The first normalization and the candidate groups","sourceOffset":55940}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are finite graphs \\(G\\) of arbitrarily large order for which \\[ \\alpha(G)\\le2,\\qquad \\mu(G)+1<\\frac{|V(G)|}{2}\\le\\chi(G). \\] In particular, the Colin de Verdière chromatic conjecture is false.","sourceOffset":1928,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Base graphs","label":"thm:base-graphs","text":"There is a constant \\(a>0\\) such that, for every \\(\\beta>0\\), there are arbitrarily large integers \\(m\\) and graphs \\(H\\) on \\(m\\) vertices with \\(\\alpha(H)\\le2\\) and the following properties. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(G\\arabic*)},ref=G\\arabic*] \\item Every matching of size at least \\(m/20\\), partitioned into classes of size at most \\(am\\), contains two conflicting edges in different classes. \\item Every matching of size at least \\(\\beta m\\) and every vertex set of size at least \\(\\beta m\\) contain an edge and a vertex in conflict. \\item Every two vertex sets of size at least \\(\\beta m\\) contain a cross hole. \\end{enumerate} The two vertex sets in \\ref{item:base-holes} may overlap.","sourceOffset":7553,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:hole","text":"Two raw vertices \\(i,j\\) have a hole between them if there exist \\(\\lambda_i,\\lambda_j\\in\\cX\\) such that \\begin{equation} \\begin{gathered} U_i\\lambda_i=U_j\\lambda_j,\\qquad u_jU_i=r(\\lambda_i),\\qquad u_iU_j=r(\\lambda_j),\\\\ a(\\lambda_i)+a(\\lambda_j)=1. \\end{gathered} \\end{equation} The middle two identities are identities of linear functionals on the whole cut-profile space.","sourceOffset":86633,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Atoms and flavors","label":"def:atom","text":"For a tag $l$, selector $s$, and base point $z$ allowed at $l$, let $\\mathfrak a_l(s,z)\\in\\cX$ be the cut profile represented by $w_l=v(s,z)v(s,z)\\trans$ and $w_t=0$ for $t\\ne l$. Its diagonal bit is $q=\\eta(w_l)$ and its role bit is $a(\\mathfrak a_l(s,z))$. A generic flavor retains all base coordinates allowed at $l$. A shared-only flavor sets all $O$ blocks to zero. A pure flavor sets $S$ to zero and retains a specified subset of the allowed $O$ blocks, setting the others to zero. Every flavor retains both $\\#$ and $Z$ in their entirety. At a fixed label, a parameter test samples all retaine","sourceOffset":135618,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/157.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":453362,"sourceFileCount":20,"statementCount":22,"definitionCount":7,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-counterexample-to-the-Colin-de-Verdiere-chromatic-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"157-03","slug":"A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026","familyId":"157","title":"A linear list-coloring bound in terms of the Hadwiger number","abstract":"We prove that every finite nonempty graph G satisfies $\\chi_{\\mathrm{list}}(G)\\le C h(G)$ for an absolute integer C, where $h(G)$ is the largest order of a clique minor. This resolves the Linear List Hadwiger conjecture affirmatively.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves Hadwiger's conjecture even for fractional coloring: arbitrarily large finite simple graphs with independence number at most two satisfy $\\chi_f(G)\\gt h(G)$, where $h(G)$ is the largest clique-minor order. Also disproves the fractional Colin de Verdière chromatic bound $\\chi_f(G)\\le\\mu(G)+1$. In the positive direction, every finite nonempty graph satisfies $\\chi_{\\mathrm{list}}(G)\\le C h(G)$ for a universal constant C.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","preprints/A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-preliminaries.tex","preprints/A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-small-graphs.tex","preprints/A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-list-transfer.tex","preprints/A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-density.tex","preprints/A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-routing.tex","preprints/A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-separability.tex","preprints/A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026/build/figures/separability.tex","preprints/A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-recursion.tex","preprints/A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026/build/figures/three-districts.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every finite nonempty graph $G$ satisfies $\\chi_{\\mathrm{list}}(G)\\le C h(G)$ for an absolute integer $C$, where $h(G)$ is the largest order of a clique minor. This resolves the Linear List Hadwiger conjecture affirmatively.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":284},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work and the two obstacles","sourceOffset":2417},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":6035},{"kind":"section","title":"Preliminaries and probabilistic tools","sourceOffset":10216},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fractional coloring and established structural results","sourceOffset":11632},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bounded sampling and concentration","sourceOffset":14166},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A small family of connectivity tests","sourceOffset":16124},{"kind":"section","title":"List coloring small graphs","sourceOffset":18602},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Eligible samples and uniform concentration","sourceOffset":20923},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Localizing a failure of thinning","sourceOffset":25895},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Private models and a connected complement","sourceOffset":30970},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Assigning the colors to bins","sourceOffset":37168}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is an absolute integer $C\\ge1$ such that every finite nonempty simple graph $G$ satisfies \\[ \\ell(G)\\le C h(G). \\]","sourceOffset":1681,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:ordinary","text":"Every finite nonempty simple graph $G$ satisfies $\\chi(G)\\le C h(G)$, with the constant $C$ in Theorem \\ref{thm:main}.","sourceOffset":2130,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:woven","text":"For integers $a\\ge1$ and $b\\ge0$, a graph $J$ is $(a,b)$-woven if the following holds. Prescribe $a$ distinct roots $R=\\{r_1,\\ldots,r_a\\}$ and at most $b$ pairs $(x_1,y_1),\\ldots,(x_p,y_p)$ of vertices of $J$. The endpoint sets $\\{x_i,y_i\\}$ must be disjoint for different $i$, but $x_i=y_i$ is allowed, and endpoints may belong to $R$. Then there are a $K_a$ model with bags $M_1,\\ldots,M_a$ satisfying $r_j\\in M_j$, and pairwise vertex-disjoint $x_i$--$y_i$ paths $Q_i$, such that \\[ V(Q_i)\\cap\\bigcup_{j=1}^a M_j \\ \\subseteq\\ R\\cap\\{x_i,y_i\\}\\qquad(1\\le i\\le p). \\] For a coincident pair the path ","sourceOffset":82581,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/157.md","scopeText":"Hadwiger's conjecture predicts $\\chi(G)\\le h(G)$ for every finite graph, where $h(G)$ is the largest clique-minor order. The formalization constructs arbitrarily large finite simple counterexamples with independence number at most two. For a graph on $m$ vertices it proves\n$h(G)<26m/75+2/3<m/2\\le\\chi(G)$.\nThis disproves the ordinary chromatic form. The paper's fractional-chromatic strengthening and the Colin de Verdière consequence are outside this selected statement.\n\nThe Linear List Hadwiger conjecture asks for a universal linear bound on list chromatic number in terms of clique-minor size. The formalization proves that there is one integer $C\\ge1$ such that every finite nonempty simple graph $G$ satisfies $\\chi_{\\mathrm{list}}(G)\\le C h(G)$, where $h(G)$ is the largest order of a clique","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/157.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":138633,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-linear-list-coloring-bound-in-terms-of-the-Hadwiger-number-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"158-01","slug":"The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026","familyId":"158","title":"The Euclidean plane is not five-colorable","abstract":"We prove that every coloring of the Euclidean plane with five colors has a monochromatic unit-distance pair, with no regularity assumption on the color classes. Consequently, the chromatic number of the plane is either six or seven.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every five-coloring of the Euclidean plane has a monochromatic pair at distance one, with no restriction on the color classes. This advances the Hadwiger–Nelson problem: together with the classical seven-coloring, only six and seven remain possible chromatic numbers of the plane.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026/build/figures/proof-flow.tex","preprints/The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026/build/sections/spectral.tex","preprints/The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026/build/sections/rigidity.tex","preprints/The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026/build/sections/transfer.tex","preprints/The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026/build/sections/palettes.tex","preprints/The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026/build/sections/transitions.tex","preprints/The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026/build/sections/interfaces.tex","preprints/The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026/build/figures/exclusion-continuum.tex","preprints/The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026/build/sections/angular.tex","preprints/The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026/build/figures/moser-placement.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every coloring of the Euclidean plane with five colors has a monochromatic unit-distance pair, with no regularity assumption on the color classes. Consequently, the chromatic number of the plane is either six or seven.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":278},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview and the role of earlier methods","sourceOffset":7116},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A probabilistic consequence","sourceOffset":16349},{"kind":"section","title":"Spectral preliminaries and relative singularity","sourceOffset":18354},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Algebraic rotations and continuous characters","sourceOffset":18935},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A line average without a measurability assumption","sourceOffset":24607},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A measurable equivariant center","sourceOffset":28742},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compact towers and relative singularity","sourceOffset":31031},{"kind":"section","title":"Compact factors, multiple averages, and Haar rigidity","sourceOffset":46048},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A compact-factor tower","sourceOffset":47030},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conditional multiple averages","sourceOffset":53049},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The radial difference inequality","sourceOffset":61592}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"The Euclidean plane has no proper five-coloring, even when arbitrary color classes are allowed. Consequently, \\[ 6\\le \\chi(\\R^2)\\le 7. \\]","sourceOffset":3797,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Transfer of colorability","label":"thm:transfer","text":"For every positive integer $k$, in ZFC, \\begin{equation} \\begin{gathered} \\text{a proper $k$-coloring of $\\R^2$ exists} \\\\ \\Longleftrightarrow \\\\ \\text{a weak measurable $k$-coloring of $\\R^2$ exists}. \\end{gathered} \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":5671,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Weak measurable coloring","label":"def:weak-coloring","text":"Let $k$ be a positive integer, let $S^1\\subset\\R^2$ be the unit circle, and let $\\sigma$ be its normalized arc-length measure. A weak measurable $k$-coloring is a Lebesgue measurable function $c:\\R^2\\to\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}$, with color classes $A_i=c^{-1}(\\{i\\})$, such that, for every $R>0$, \\[ \\sum_{i=1}^k\\int_{B(0,R)}\\int_{S^1} \\ind_{A_i}(x)\\ind_{A_i}(x+u)\\,d\\sigma(u)\\,dx=0. \\] The integrals use the completed product measure; equivalently, one may first choose Borel representatives of the color classes forming a partition of the plane.","sourceOffset":4737,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:spectral-groups","text":"Identify the plane with $\\C$, and set \\[ F=\\overline{\\Q}\\cap\\R,\\qquad E=F(i),\\qquad K=\\{u\\in E:u\\bar u=1\\}. \\] We regard $E$ as an additive discrete group and $K$ as a multiplicative discrete group. Let \\[ D=\\widehat{E_{\\mathrm{disc}}} \\] be the compact character group of $E$. A point $d\\in D$ is a homomorphism from the additive group $E$ to the unit complex group $\\T$. We use additive notation on $D$, so $(d+d')(z)=d(z)d'(z)$. For $a,z\\in E$, put \\[ (ad)(z)=d(az),\\qquad \\psi_z(d)=d(z). \\] The subgroup of ordinary continuous characters is \\begin{equation} C=j(\\R^2),\\qquad j(\\xi)(z)=\\exp(i\\xi\\c","sourceOffset":18995,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/158.md","scopeText":"The Hadwiger–Nelson problem asks for the fewest colors needed to color the plane so that points at distance one have different colors. The formalized results prove that five colors do not suffice and that seven colors do suffice. The lower bound applies to arbitrary colorings, with no measurability or continuity assumption; the upper bound includes every boundary point of the coloring regions.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/158.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":211906,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":5,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"159-01","slug":"Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026","familyId":"159","title":"Quasipolynomial Bounds for Arithmetic Progressions","abstract":"We prove Erdős's conjecture that every set of positive integers with divergent reciprocal sum contains arithmetic progressions of every finite length. More quantitatively, for every fixed k ≥ 3, we show $\\displaystyle r_k(N)\\le C_kN\\exp\\bigl(-c_k(\\log N)^{\\varepsilon_k}\\bigr)$ with $C_k,c_k,\\varepsilon_k\\gt 0$, where $r_k(N)$ is the largest size of a subset of $\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ with no nonconstant k-term arithmetic progression.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Erdős's conjecture that every set of positive integers with divergent reciprocal sum contains arithmetic progressions of every finite length. Quantitatively, for each fixed k ≥ 3, every subset of $\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ with no nonconstant k-term progression has size at most $C_kN\\exp[-c_k(\\log N)^{\\varepsilon_k}]$, with positive constants depending only on k.","pdfPath":"preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-setup.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/foundations/figures/patch-coordinates.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/figures/proof-route.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-paths.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-major.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-relative-detection.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-scalar.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-return.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-ascent.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-active.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-extraction.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-iteration.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/sections/09-consequences.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/foundations/foundations.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/foundations/00-preface.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/foundations/02-symbols.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/foundations/03-shift.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/foundations/04-counting.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/foundations/05-sampling.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/foundations/05-constrained.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/foundations/06-cube.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/foundations/06-normalized-cube.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/foundations/06-transfer.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/foundations/07-lifting.tex","preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Erd\\H{o}s's conjecture that every set of positive integers with divergent reciprocal sum contains arithmetic progressions of every finite length. More quantitatively, for every fixed $k\\ge3$, we show \\[ r_k(N)\\le C_kN\\exp\\bigl(-c_k(\\log N)^{\\varepsilon_k}\\bigr) \\] with $C_k,c_k,\\varepsilon_k>0$, where $r_k(N)$ is the largest size of a subset of $\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ with no nonconstant $k$-term arithmetic progression.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":489},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Density thresholds and summability","sourceOffset":2733},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior density bounds","sourceOffset":4022},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Preserving structure through density increments","sourceOffset":7950},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coloring thresholds","sourceOffset":14738},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How to read the proof","sourceOffset":15626},{"kind":"section","title":"Polynomial cells and the increment theorem","sourceOffset":17164},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Precision conventions","sourceOffset":18318},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cells and certificates","sourceOffset":19289},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The triangular increment","sourceOffset":21415},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Polynomial patches","sourceOffset":24610},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The absolute starting rule","sourceOffset":27514}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"ap:main","text":"For each fixed integer $k\\ge3$ there are constants $C_k,c_k,\\varepsilon_k>0$ such that, for every $N\\ge2$, \\begin{equation} r_k(N)\\le C_kN\\exp\\bigl(-c_k(\\log N)^{\\varepsilon_k}\\bigr). \\end{equation} Equivalently, there is $A_k\\ge1$ such that an $\\alpha$-dense subset of $[N]$ contains a nonconstant $k$-term progression whenever \\begin{equation} \\log N\\ge A_k\\bigl(2+\\log(1/\\alpha)\\bigr)^{A_k}, \\qquad 0<\\alpha\\le1. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":1024,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Divergent reciprocal sums","label":"ap:reciprocal","text":"Every set $A\\subseteq\\mathbb N$ with $\\sum_{a\\in A}a^{-1}=\\infty$ contains nonconstant arithmetic progressions of every finite length.","sourceOffset":1736,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Descending budgets","label":"ap:budgets","text":"At layer $j$, the warm data consist of $p$, forecasts for all dimensions and structural variable counts, gap parameters depending only on these data, and the widths and numerical budgets already chosen at layers strictly above $j$. A warm logarithmic bound is polynomial in these data and does not use any $Q_i$ with $i\\le j$. The cold preliminary data may additionally use the current width log $Q_j$, including its forecast preparation losses, but not the eventual rank threshold, long path scale, height of the prepared value space, or parent-side cutoff at layer $j$. The late data are the pertur","sourceOffset":31632,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Ascent format","label":"ap:ascent:format","text":"At entry to layer $j$, the following data and properties constitute the ascent format. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item A warm-cost slice of the parameter box carries a full-mass score at least $\\delta$, with $\\log(1/\\delta)$ warm. Its positive payoff contains the fixed pulled-back input $f_0(\\psi(t))$, where $0\\leq f_0\\leq1$. When $j\\leq s$, it also contains the current factor $1_{\\mathrm{no\\ bad}}(t)$. Its negative payoff has multiplier $-\\lambda$. \\item Both payoffs use, respectively, the small and large cutoffs of the absolute slots and of all determining slots $b_i$ with ","sourceOffset":159079,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/159.md","scopeText":"Erdős's reciprocal-sum conjecture asks whether every set of positive integers with divergent reciprocal sum contains arithmetic progressions of every finite length. The formalization proves this statement: for every requested length, such a set contains a progression with positive common difference.\n\nThe selected theorem is the reciprocal-sum consequence. The paper's quantitative upper bound for the largest progression-free subset of $\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ is outside this statement.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/159.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":753221,"sourceFileCount":28,"statementCount":38,"definitionCount":7,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Quasipolynomial-Bounds-for-Arithmetic-Progressions-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"160-01","slug":"Quantitative-Superexponential-Bounds-for-van-der-Waerden-Numbers-September-23-2026","familyId":"160","title":"Quantitative Superexponential Bounds for van der Waerden Numbers","abstract":"We prove that there are absolute constants c > 0 and K0 such that $W_r(k)\\gt k^{ck\\lfloor\\log_2 r\\rfloor}$ for every $k\\ge K_0$ and r ≥ 2. Consequently $W_r(k)^{1/k}\\to\\infty$ for each fixed r ≥ 2, giving a quantitative positive resolution of Erdős's superexponential-growth question, including the two-color case.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves Erdős's superexponential-growth question for van der Waerden numbers. If $W_r(k)$ is the least interval length forcing a monochromatic k-term progression in every r-coloring, then $W_r(k)\\gt k^{ck\\lfloor\\log_2 r\\rfloor}$ for an absolute c > 0, all r ≥ 2 and sufficiently large k, uniformly in r. In particular, $W_r(k)^{1/k}\\to\\infty$ for each fixed r.","pdfPath":"preprints/Quantitative-Superexponential-Bounds-for-van-der-Waerden-Numbers-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quantitative-Superexponential-Bounds-for-van-der-Waerden-Numbers-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quantitative-Superexponential-Bounds-for-van-der-Waerden-Numbers-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/Quantitative-Superexponential-Bounds-for-van-der-Waerden-Numbers-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Superexponential-Bounds-for-van-der-Waerden-Numbers-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Superexponential-Bounds-for-van-der-Waerden-Numbers-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Superexponential-Bounds-for-van-der-Waerden-Numbers-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-setup.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Superexponential-Bounds-for-van-der-Waerden-Numbers-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-counting.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Superexponential-Bounds-for-van-der-Waerden-Numbers-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-outer.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Superexponential-Bounds-for-van-der-Waerden-Numbers-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-dichotomy.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Superexponential-Bounds-for-van-der-Waerden-Numbers-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-perturbation.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Superexponential-Bounds-for-van-der-Waerden-Numbers-September-23-2026/build/figures/norm-band.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Superexponential-Bounds-for-van-der-Waerden-Numbers-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-transfers.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Superexponential-Bounds-for-van-der-Waerden-Numbers-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-upper.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Quantitative-Superexponential-Bounds-for-van-der-Waerden-Numbers-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that there are absolute constants $c>0$ and $K_0$ such that $W_r(k)>k^{ck\\lfloor\\log_2 r\\rfloor}$ for every $k\\ge K_0$ and $r\\ge2$. Consequently $W_r(k)^{1/k}\\to\\infty$ for each fixed $r\\ge2$, giving a quantitative positive resolution of Erd\\H{o}s's superexponential-growth question, including the two-color case.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":368},{"kind":"section","title":"The cyclic model and its coordinate meshes","sourceOffset":9667},{"kind":"section","title":"Counting global words and anchored local patterns","sourceOffset":20142},{"kind":"section","title":"An outer coloring of the labels","sourceOffset":30275},{"kind":"section","title":"Repeated labels and the outer-color dichotomy","sourceOffset":38081},{"kind":"section","title":"A sparse perturbation of the outer coloring","sourceOffset":50693},{"kind":"section","title":"Integer intervals and the two growth regimes","sourceOffset":64117},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Growth in the number of colors","sourceOffset":66958},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform growth statements","sourceOffset":70200},{"kind":"section","title":"An elementary finite upper bound","sourceOffset":71616}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"main:intro","text":"There is an absolute integer $K_0$ such that, for every integer $k\\ge K_0$ and every integer $r\\ge2$, \\[ W_r(k)>k^{c k\\lfloor\\log_2r\\rfloor}, \\qquad c=10^{-5}. \\]","sourceOffset":1015,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Outer-color dichotomy","label":"dichotomy:main","text":"For every sufficiently large $k$, the coloring $c_0$ from \\Cref{outer:pullback} has the following property. Every cyclic progression $n_j=n_0+jd$, $0\\le j<k$, with $d\\ne0$ in $G$, satisfies at least one of these alternatives: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item each bit occurs among $c_0(n_0),\\ldots,c_0(n_{k-1})$ at least $\\gamma k$ times; \\item there are $A,B\\in\\R^D$ such that $\\tilde y(n_j)=A+jB$ for all $0\\le j<k$. \\end{enumerate} The threshold on $k$ is absolute.","sourceOffset":44665,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Eligible local patterns","label":"count:eligible","text":"Choose $x,y\\in\\T^D$ and $u,v\\in\\R^D$, and form \\begin{equation} X_z=x+\\frac z h(t+u),\\qquad Y_z=y+\\frac z h(\\lambda t+v)\\qquad(0\\le z<h) \\quad\\text{in }\\T^D. \\end{equation} A position $z$ is regular if $\\rho(Y_{z,i})\\ge\\eta$ in every rotating coordinate; if there are no rotating coordinates, every position is regular. Record $L(X_z,Y_z)$ at a regular position and a dummy symbol $*$ otherwise. The resulting word in $(\\cL\\cup\\{*\\})^h$ is eligible for $h,t$ if it admits a realization satisfying all of the following: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item At least $h/2$ positions are re","sourceOffset":24977,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/160.md","scopeText":"Let $W(r,k)$ be the least interval length forcing a monochromatic $k$-term arithmetic progression in every coloring with at most $r$ colors. The formalized result gives an absolute threshold $K$ such that $W(r,k)>k^{k\\lfloor\\log_2 r\\rfloor/100000}$ for all $k\\ge K$ and $r\\ge2$. It also establishes the associated growth limits, finiteness, and boundary values. The sharper intermediate estimates used in the paper are not part of the described formalization.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/160.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":76887,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Quantitative-Superexponential-Bounds-for-van-der-Waerden-Numbers-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Quantitative-Superexponential-Bounds-for-van-der-Waerden-Numbers-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode.tex","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Quantitative-Superexponential-Bounds-for-van-der-Waerden-Numbers-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex.tex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"161-01","slug":"A-counterexample-to-Sidorenkos-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"161","title":"A counterexample to Sidorenko's conjecture","abstract":"We disprove Sidorenko's conjecture with a bipartite graph on 35 vertices and 66 edges: its homomorphism density in some finite simple graph is smaller than the conjectured lower bound. The same connected graph also disproves the forcing conjecture: at one fixed density, asymptotically matching the edge and pattern densities does not imply quasirandomness.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves Sidorenko's conjecture with a connected bipartite pattern on 35 vertices and 66 edges that occurs less frequently than in a random graph of the same edge density. The same pattern disproves the forcing conjecture of Skokan and Thoma: matching its density and the edge density of a constant graphon need not force quasirandomness.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-counterexample-to-Sidorenkos-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-counterexample-to-Sidorenkos-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-counterexample-to-Sidorenkos-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-counterexample-to-Sidorenkos-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Sidorenkos-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Sidorenkos-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Sidorenkos-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/proof-overview.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Sidorenkos-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/complex.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Sidorenkos-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/kernel.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Sidorenkos-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/corner-activation.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Sidorenkos-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/transverse.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Sidorenkos-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/orbits.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Sidorenkos-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/planted.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Sidorenkos-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/tail.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Sidorenkos-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-counterexample-to-Sidorenkos-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove Sidorenko's conjecture with a bipartite graph on \\(35\\) vertices and \\(66\\) edges: its homomorphism density in some finite simple graph is smaller than the conjectured lower bound. The same connected graph also disproves the forcing conjecture: at one fixed density, asymptotically matching the edge and pattern densities does not imply quasirandomness.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":429},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The negative correlation","sourceOffset":2674},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and relation to positive results","sourceOffset":9375},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Amplification and injective copies","sourceOffset":16542},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A consequence for the forcing conjecture","sourceOffset":17991},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and conventions","sourceOffset":20042},{"kind":"section","title":"The finite complex","sourceOffset":21173},{"kind":"section","title":"Constructing a negative kernel correlation","sourceOffset":28412},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The rank-layer model","sourceOffset":28770},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Activation laws and the coordinate expansion","sourceOffset":32843},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A single negative term","sourceOffset":38765},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pinning the types by color refinement","sourceOffset":45956}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(H\\) be the incidence graph of the \\(22\\) triples on \\(13\\) points in Table \\ref{tab:complex}. This bipartite graph has \\(35\\) vertices and \\(66\\) edges. There is a finite simple undirected graph \\(G\\) with at least one edge such that \\[ t(H,G)<p(G)^{66}. \\]","sourceOffset":922,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Amplification and injective supersaturation","label":"cor:amplification","text":"For the same graph \\(H\\) as in Theorem \\ref{thm:main}, the following hold. \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\roman*)] \\item For every \\(c>0\\), there is a finite simple undirected graph \\(F\\) with \\(p(F)>0\\) such that \\[ t(H,F)<c\\,p(F)^{66}. \\] \\item There are \\(p_*\\in(0,1)\\), \\(\\delta_*>0\\), and finite simple undirected graphs \\(F_s\\) of orders \\(n_s\\to\\infty\\) for which \\(d_s=|E(F_s)|/\\binom{n_s}{2}\\to p_*\\) and, for all sufficiently large \\(s\\), \\[ \\operatorname{Inj}(H,F_s) \\le(1-\\delta_*)(n_s)_{35}d_s^{66}. \\] \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":16931,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/161.md","scopeText":"Sidorenko's conjecture predicts that every bipartite graph $H$ has homomorphism density at least the host graph's edge density raised to $|E(H)|$. The formalization disproves this for the paper's fixed bipartite graph with $35$ vertices and $66$ edges: it constructs a nonempty finite simple host graph with $t(H,G)<t(K_2,G)^{66}$. The separate forcing-conjecture consequence in the paper is outside this statement.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/161.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":167547,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-counterexample-to-Sidorenkos-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"162-01","slug":"Balanced-Counterexamples-to-Rysers-Conjecture-at-Prime-Orders-September-27-2026","familyId":"162","title":"Balanced counterexamples to Ryser's conjecture at prime orders","abstract":"For every sufficiently large prime q, we construct a finite intersecting $(q+1)$-partite $(q+1)$-uniform hypergraph with covering number $q+1$ and exactly $q+1$ nonisolated vertices in each part. This disproves Ryser's covering conjecture, even for intersecting hypergraphs with equally sized parts.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves Ryser's covering conjecture by constructing intersecting $(q+1)$-partite, $(q+1)$-uniform hypergraphs with covering number $q+1$, rather than the predicted bound q, for every sufficiently large prime q. A separate construction over extension fields also disproves Gyárfás's monochromatic tree-cover conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/Balanced-Counterexamples-to-Rysers-Conjecture-at-Prime-Orders-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Balanced-Counterexamples-to-Rysers-Conjecture-at-Prime-Orders-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Balanced-Counterexamples-to-Rysers-Conjecture-at-Prime-Orders-September-27-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/Balanced-Counterexamples-to-Rysers-Conjecture-at-Prime-Orders-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Balanced-Counterexamples-to-Rysers-Conjecture-at-Prime-Orders-September-27-2026/build/construction.tex","preprints/Balanced-Counterexamples-to-Rysers-Conjecture-at-Prime-Orders-September-27-2026/build/figures/merge-split.tex","preprints/Balanced-Counterexamples-to-Rysers-Conjecture-at-Prime-Orders-September-27-2026/build/deletion.tex","preprints/Balanced-Counterexamples-to-Rysers-Conjecture-at-Prime-Orders-September-27-2026/build/candidates.tex","preprints/Balanced-Counterexamples-to-Rysers-Conjecture-at-Prime-Orders-September-27-2026/build/selection.tex","preprints/Balanced-Counterexamples-to-Rysers-Conjecture-at-Prime-Orders-September-27-2026/build/local.tex","preprints/Balanced-Counterexamples-to-Rysers-Conjecture-at-Prime-Orders-September-27-2026/build/elementary.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Balanced-Counterexamples-to-Rysers-Conjecture-at-Prime-Orders-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every sufficiently large prime $q$, we construct a finite intersecting $(q+1)$-partite $(q+1)$-uniform hypergraph with covering number $q+1$ and exactly $q+1$ nonisolated vertices in each part. This disproves Ryser's covering conjecture, even for intersecting hypergraphs with equally sized parts.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":347},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Monochromatic tree covers","sourceOffset":5686},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Balance, intersection, and the cover obstruction","sourceOffset":7436},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Producing the geometric data","sourceOffset":10010},{"kind":"section","title":"From modified line partitions to a hypergraph","sourceOffset":13455},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Merged pairs and four-point split lines","sourceOffset":14451},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Partitions and intersecting edges","sourceOffset":22094},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Forcing the nonsplit members of a pencil","sourceOffset":24655},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The remaining designated points","sourceOffset":28712},{"kind":"section","title":"Deletion lines and concentration of core covers","sourceOffset":31635},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Profiles and incidence identities","sourceOffset":35175},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The three deletion estimates","sourceOffset":47932}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exists $q_0$ such that, for every prime $q\\ge q_0$, there is a finite intersecting $(q+1)$-partite $(q+1)$-uniform hypergraph $H$ with \\[ |V_1|=\\cdots=|V_{q+1}|=q+1, \\qquad \\tau(H)=q+1. \\] Every vertex belongs to an edge.","sourceOffset":4518,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:tree-cover","text":"For every sufficiently large prime $q$, there is a finite complete graph with a $(q+1)$-edge-coloring whose vertices require exactly $q+1$ monochromatic trees to cover. The same minimum holds for connected monochromatic subgraphs.","sourceOffset":6359,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/162.md","scopeText":"Ryser's covering conjecture predicts that an intersecting $r$-partite hypergraph has a vertex cover of size at most $r-1$. The formalization proves that every sufficiently large prime $q$ has a finite intersecting $(q+1)$-partite, $(q+1)$-uniform hypergraph with covering number $q+1$ and exactly $q+1$ nonisolated vertices in each part. Thus the conjecture fails even with equal part sizes. The prime threshold is existential.\n\nRyser's covering conjecture predicts $\\tau\\le(r-1)\\nu$ for an $r$-partite hypergraph, where $\\tau$ and $\\nu$ are its covering and matching numbers. The formalized constructions give finite intersecting $r$-partite $r$-uniform hypergraphs with $\\nu=1$ and $\\tau=r$, contradicting the bound. The ranks have the form $r=p^n+1$: one fixed prime $p\\equiv2\\pmod3$ works for all","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/162.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":143415,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Balanced-Counterexamples-to-Rysers-Conjecture-at-Prime-Orders-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Balanced-Counterexamples-to-Rysers-Conjecture-at-Prime-Orders-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Balanced-Counterexamples-to-Rysers-Conjecture-at-Prime-Orders-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"162-02","slug":"A-Counterexample-to-Rysers-Covering-Conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"162","title":"A counterexample to Ryser's covering conjecture","abstract":"For every sufficiently large prime $s\\equiv2\\pmod3$ and every sufficiently large odd integer n, with the threshold depending on s, we construct an intersecting $(s^n+1)$-partite $(s^n+1)$-uniform hypergraph with covering number $s^n+1$. This disproves Ryser's covering conjecture in its intersecting case.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves Ryser's covering conjecture by constructing intersecting $(q+1)$-partite, $(q+1)$-uniform hypergraphs with covering number $q+1$, rather than the predicted bound q, for every sufficiently large prime q. A separate construction over extension fields also disproves Gyárfás's monochromatic tree-cover conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Rysers-Covering-Conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Rysers-Covering-Conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Rysers-Covering-Conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Rysers-Covering-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Rysers-Covering-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/incidence.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Rysers-Covering-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/linecover.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Rysers-Covering-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/selection.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Rysers-Covering-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/construction.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Rysers-Covering-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/two-coordinates.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Rysers-Covering-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every sufficiently large prime $s\\equiv2\\pmod3$ and every sufficiently large odd integer $n$, with the threshold depending on $s$, we construct an intersecting $(s^n+1)$-partite $(s^n+1)$-uniform hypergraph with covering number $s^n+1$. This disproves Ryser's covering conjecture in its intersecting case.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":355},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Monochromatic tree covers","sourceOffset":6031},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":9039},{"kind":"section","title":"A uniform incidence bound","sourceOffset":13845},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite-set estimates","sourceOffset":15825},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bounded scalar expressions and subfields","sourceOffset":23687},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof of the incidence theorem","sourceOffset":33197},{"kind":"section","title":"Line covers after random deletions","sourceOffset":41816},{"kind":"section","title":"Selecting compatible translates","sourceOffset":61849},{"kind":"section","title":"A configuration with two coordinatizations","sourceOffset":85165},{"kind":"section","title":"Ambient placement","sourceOffset":92069},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fields, copies, and direction sets","sourceOffset":92705}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exists $s_0$ such that, for every prime $s\\ge s_0$ with $s\\equiv2\\pmod3$, there is an integer $n_0(s)\\ge3$ with the following property. For every odd integer $n\\ge n_0(s)$, put $q=s^n$. There is a finite intersecting $(q+1)$-partite $(q+1)$-uniform hypergraph $H$ satisfying \\[ \\tau(H)=q+1. \\] In particular, the intersecting case of \\eqref{eq:ryser} is false.","sourceOffset":3885,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:tree-cover","text":"Let $s_0$ and $n_0(s)$ be as in Theorem \\ref{thm:main}. For every prime $s\\ge s_0$ with $s\\equiv2\\pmod3$ and every odd integer $n\\ge n_0(s)$, put $r=s^n+1$. There is a finite simple complete graph with an $r$-edge-coloring for which the minimum number of monochromatic trees needed to cover its vertices is exactly $r$. The same minimum holds with connected monochromatic subgraphs or maximal monochromatic components in place of trees.","sourceOffset":6636,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/162.md","scopeText":"Ryser's covering conjecture predicts that an intersecting $r$-partite hypergraph has a vertex cover of size at most $r-1$. The formalization proves that every sufficiently large prime $q$ has a finite intersecting $(q+1)$-partite, $(q+1)$-uniform hypergraph with covering number $q+1$ and exactly $q+1$ nonisolated vertices in each part. Thus the conjecture fails even with equal part sizes. The prime threshold is existential.\n\nRyser's covering conjecture predicts $\\tau\\le(r-1)\\nu$ for an $r$-partite hypergraph, where $\\tau$ and $\\nu$ are its covering and matching numbers. The formalized constructions give finite intersecting $r$-partite $r$-uniform hypergraphs with $\\nu=1$ and $\\tau=r$, contradicting the bound. The ranks have the form $r=p^n+1$: one fixed prime $p\\equiv2\\pmod3$ works for all","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/162.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":122909,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Counterexample-to-Rysers-Covering-Conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Rysers-Covering-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Rysers-Covering-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"164-01","slug":"Monochromatic-finite-sums-and-products-in-the-positive-integers-September-23-2026","familyId":"164","title":"Monochromatic finite sums and products in the positive integers","abstract":"We prove Hindman's finite sums and products conjecture: for every finite coloring of the positive integers and every positive integer k, there is a k-element set whose nonempty subset sums and nonempty subset products all have the same color.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Hindman's finite sums and products conjecture: every finite coloring of the positive integers contains sets of any prescribed finite size whose nonempty subset sums and nonempty subset products all have one common color.","pdfPath":"preprints/Monochromatic-finite-sums-and-products-in-the-positive-integers-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Monochromatic-finite-sums-and-products-in-the-positive-integers-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Monochromatic-finite-sums-and-products-in-the-positive-integers-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/Monochromatic-finite-sums-and-products-in-the-positive-integers-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Monochromatic-finite-sums-and-products-in-the-positive-integers-September-23-2026/build/sections/01_introduction.tex","preprints/Monochromatic-finite-sums-and-products-in-the-positive-integers-September-23-2026/build/sections/02_framework.tex","preprints/Monochromatic-finite-sums-and-products-in-the-positive-integers-September-23-2026/build/sections/03_arithmetic.tex","preprints/Monochromatic-finite-sums-and-products-in-the-positive-integers-September-23-2026/build/sections/04_correlation.tex","preprints/Monochromatic-finite-sums-and-products-in-the-positive-integers-September-23-2026/build/sections/05_prediction.tex","preprints/Monochromatic-finite-sums-and-products-in-the-positive-integers-September-23-2026/build/sections/06_rough_progressions.tex","preprints/Monochromatic-finite-sums-and-products-in-the-positive-integers-September-23-2026/build/sections/07_cube_limits.tex","preprints/Monochromatic-finite-sums-and-products-in-the-positive-integers-September-23-2026/build/sections/08_alignment.tex","preprints/Monochromatic-finite-sums-and-products-in-the-positive-integers-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Monochromatic-finite-sums-and-products-in-the-positive-integers-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Hindman's finite sums and products conjecture: for every finite coloring of the positive integers and every positive integer $k$, there is a $k$-element set whose nonempty subset sums and nonempty subset products all have the same color.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":309},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":2294},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":5629},{"kind":"section","title":"Two principles and the combinatorial deduction","sourceOffset":11548},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ordered blocks and admissible scales","sourceOffset":12143},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Piecewise nilsequence models","sourceOffset":15606},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prediction and alignment","sourceOffset":18351},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite combinatorial selection","sourceOffset":21890},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Deduction of the main theorem","sourceOffset":24350},{"kind":"section","title":"Arithmetic scales and divisor weights","sourceOffset":31284},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Harmonic sampling identities","sourceOffset":32564},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Recovering the product sampling law","sourceOffset":37895}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $r,m\\ge1$ be integers, and fix real numbers $R\\ge2$ and $D\\ge1$. For every coloring $\\chi:\\N\\to[r]$, there are distinct positive integers $a_1<\\cdots<a_m$ and a color $c\\in[r]$ such that \\[ \\chi\\left(\\sum_{j\\in J}a_j\\right) =\\chi\\left(\\prod_{j\\in J}a_j\\right)=c \\quad\\text{for every }\\varnothing\\ne J\\subseteq[m]. \\] Equivalently, $\\FS(A)\\cup\\FP(A)$ is monochromatic for a set $A\\subset\\N$ of cardinality $m$. The elements can additionally be chosen to satisfy \\begin{equation} a_1>R,\\qquad a_d>R\\left(\\sum_{k<d}a_k+\\prod_{k<d}a_k\\right)^D \\quad(2\\le d\\le m). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":755,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:distinct-expressions","text":"For every finite coloring of $\\N$ and every $m\\ge1$, there is an $m$-element set $A\\subset\\N$ for which $\\FS(A)\\cup\\FP(A)$ is monochromatic and \\[ |\\FS(A)|=|\\FP(A)|=2^m-1,\\qquad \\FS(A)\\cap\\FP(A)=A. \\] In particular, the union has $2(2^m-1)-m$ distinct elements.","sourceOffset":1735,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Admissible parameters","label":"def:admissible","text":"For a fixed $n$, an admissible family consists of positive integers $M,h_1,\\ldots,h_n,H_1,\\ldots,H_n$, positive powers of two $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$, and independent random variables $t_1,\\ldots,t_n$, all depending on $w$, with the following properties. \\begin{enumerate} \\item $M$ and each $h_i$ are $w$-smooth multiples of $W^w$. For every block $B$, and also for every singleton $\\{i\\}$, $h_B\\le M$. For every $A\\in\\mathcal E_B$, the ratio $h_A/h_B$ is an integer multiple of $W^w$. \\item Each $H_i$ is a multiple of $M$ and dominates powers of $2+M+\\prod_{\\ell<i}X_\\ell$. The quantity $\\log X_i$ domina","sourceOffset":14092,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Piecewise models","label":"def:piecewise-model","text":"Fix finite sets $\\mathcal A\\subset\\Q_{>0}$ and $[r]$, and an admissible family. A system of step-at-most-$s$ models consists of functions $S_{B,a,c}:\\Z\\to[0,1]$, one for each block $B$, $a\\in\\mathcal A$, and $c\\in[r]$. If $i=\\max B$, then on each interval $[kH_i,(k+1)H_i)$ and each residue class modulo $M$, the function is a nilsequence in the progression index. The complexity is bounded uniformly over $w$, all pieces, and all model indices. The representing observables may be, and are, chosen $[0,1]$-valued on their entire nilmanifolds.","sourceOffset":16478,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":258401,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Monochromatic-finite-sums-and-products-in-the-positive-integers-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Monochromatic-finite-sums-and-products-in-the-positive-integers-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"165-01","slug":"The-crossing-number-of-complete-graphs-September-23-2026","familyId":"165","title":"The crossing number of complete graphs","abstract":"We prove the Harary–Hill conjecture: for every positive integer n, the ordinary crossing number of the complete graph Kn is $\\displaystyle \\frac14\\left\\lfloor\\frac n2\\right\\rfloor \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rfloor \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\right\\rfloor \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-3}{2}\\right\\rfloor.$","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the Harary–Hill conjecture and Turán's brickyard problem in the Zarankiewicz formulation, determining the crossing numbers of every complete and complete bipartite graph. The result proves the optimality of the classical drawings among all plane drawings with continuous edge arcs.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-graphs-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-graphs-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-graphs-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-graphs-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-graphs-September-23-2026/build/figures/local-pairing.tex","preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-graphs-September-23-2026/build/figures/interpolation.tex","preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-graphs-September-23-2026/build/figures/two-page.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-graphs-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Harary--Hill conjecture: for every positive integer $n$, the ordinary crossing number of the complete graph $K_n$ is \\[ \\frac14\\left\\lfloor\\frac n2\\right\\rfloor \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rfloor \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\right\\rfloor \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-3}{2}\\right\\rfloor. \\]","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":433},{"kind":"section","title":"Signed intersections of cycles","sourceOffset":7599},{"kind":"section","title":"An even-node order detector","sourceOffset":17410},{"kind":"section","title":"The lower bound","sourceOffset":24136},{"kind":"section","title":"A matching two-page drawing","sourceOffset":32230},{"kind":"section","title":"Normalization of continuous-arc drawings","sourceOffset":39643}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\), \\[ \\crn(K_n)=Z(n):= \\frac14\\left\\lfloor\\frac n2\\right\\rfloor \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}2\\right\\rfloor \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}2\\right\\rfloor \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-3}2\\right\\rfloor. \\]","sourceOffset":4408,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Odd lower bound","label":"prop:odd","text":"Every normalized minimum drawing of \\(K_{2s+1}\\), \\(s\\ge2\\), has at least \\(\\binom{s}{2}^{2}\\) crossings.","sourceOffset":29679,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/165.md","scopeText":"The formalized result proves Hill's proposed formula for the crossing number of the complete graph: for every $n\\ge3$, $\\mathrm{cr}(K_n)=\\frac14\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\lfloor(n-1)/2\\rfloor\\lfloor(n-2)/2\\rfloor\\lfloor(n-3)/2\\rfloor$. It proves the lower bound and constructs a matching two-page drawing. Crossings are spatial points, so repeated crossings of one edge pair count separately.\n\nZarankiewicz's crossing-number conjecture predicts $\\mathrm{cr}(K_{m,n})=\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor\\lfloor(m-1)/2\\rfloor\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\lfloor(n-1)/2\\rfloor$. The formalized result proves this equality for every positive $m,n$ and constructs a drawing attaining it. Crossings are counted as spatial points for continuous simple edge paths, including repeated crossings between the same pair of edges.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/165.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":44709,"sourceFileCount":4,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-crossing-number-of-complete-graphs-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"165-02","slug":"The-crossing-number-of-complete-bipartite-graphs-September-23-2026","familyId":"165","title":"The crossing number of complete bipartite graphs","abstract":"We prove the Zarankiewicz crossing-number conjecture, resolving Turán's brickyard problem. For all positive integers m, n, the ordinary crossing number of the complete bipartite graph $K_{m,n}$ is $\\displaystyle \\left\\lfloor\\frac m2\\right\\rfloor \\left\\lfloor\\frac{m-1}{2}\\right\\rfloor \\left\\lfloor\\frac n2\\right\\rfloor \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rfloor.$","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the Harary–Hill conjecture and Turán's brickyard problem in the Zarankiewicz formulation, determining the crossing numbers of every complete and complete bipartite graph. The result proves the optimality of the classical drawings among all plane drawings with continuous edge arcs.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-bipartite-graphs-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-bipartite-graphs-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-bipartite-graphs-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-bipartite-graphs-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-bipartite-graphs-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-bipartite-graphs-September-23-2026/build/sections/subspaces.tex","preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-bipartite-graphs-September-23-2026/build/sections/intersections.tex","preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-bipartite-graphs-September-23-2026/build/figures/chord-pairing.tex","preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-bipartite-graphs-September-23-2026/build/sections/detector.tex","preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-bipartite-graphs-September-23-2026/build/figures/triangular-grid.tex","preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-bipartite-graphs-September-23-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex","preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-bipartite-graphs-September-23-2026/build/sections/normalization.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-crossing-number-of-complete-bipartite-graphs-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Zarankiewicz crossing-number conjecture, resolving Tur\\'an's brickyard problem. For all positive integers $m,n$, the ordinary crossing number of the complete bipartite graph $K_{m,n}$ is \\[ \\left\\lfloor\\frac m2\\right\\rfloor \\left\\lfloor\\frac{m-1}{2}\\right\\rfloor \\left\\lfloor\\frac n2\\right\\rfloor \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rfloor. \\]","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":403},{"kind":"section","title":"A subspace intersection inequality","sourceOffset":6804},{"kind":"section","title":"Signed intersections and cycle relations","sourceOffset":14669},{"kind":"section","title":"Detecting spoke orders by matrices of full rank","sourceOffset":26098},{"kind":"section","title":"The crossing-number formula","sourceOffset":34799},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The upper bound","sourceOffset":34929},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The lower bound for odd \\texorpdfstring{$n$}{n}","sourceOffset":36468},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The even case","sourceOffset":38731},{"kind":"section","title":"Normalization of continuous-arc drawings","sourceOffset":39585}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For all positive integers $m,n$, \\[ \\crn(K_{m,n})=d_m d_n. \\]","sourceOffset":1538,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Rank sum for differences","label":"cor:rank-sum","text":"Let $X_1,\\ldots,X_m,Y_1,\\ldots,Y_m$ be real $d\\times d$ matrices. If $X_i-Y_i$ is invertible for every $i$, then \\begin{equation} \\sum_{\\substack{1\\leq i,j\\leq m\\\\i\\ne j}} \\rank(X_i-Y_j)\\geq2d_m d, \\qquad d_m=\\left\\lfloor\\frac m2\\right\\rfloor \\left\\lfloor\\frac{m-1}{2}\\right\\rfloor. \\end{equation} The sum is over ordered pairs of distinct indices.","sourceOffset":13544,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/165.md","scopeText":"The formalized result proves Hill's proposed formula for the crossing number of the complete graph: for every $n\\ge3$, $\\mathrm{cr}(K_n)=\\frac14\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\lfloor(n-1)/2\\rfloor\\lfloor(n-2)/2\\rfloor\\lfloor(n-3)/2\\rfloor$. It proves the lower bound and constructs a matching two-page drawing. Crossings are spatial points, so repeated crossings of one edge pair count separately.\n\nZarankiewicz's crossing-number conjecture predicts $\\mathrm{cr}(K_{m,n})=\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor\\lfloor(m-1)/2\\rfloor\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\lfloor(n-1)/2\\rfloor$. The formalized result proves this equality for every positive $m,n$ and constructs a drawing attaining it. Crossings are counted as spatial points for continuous simple edge paths, including repeated crossings between the same pair of edges.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/165.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":47764,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-crossing-number-of-complete-bipartite-graphs-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"166-01","slug":"The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"166","title":"The higher-dimensional Erdős distinct-distances conjecture","abstract":"For every fixed integer d ≥ 3, we prove that every set of n ≥ 2 distinct points in ℝd determines at least $c_d n^{2/d}$ distinct distances, where $c_d\\gt 0$ depends only on d. This resolves the higher-dimensional Erdős distinct-distances conjecture positively.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every fixed d ≥ 3, any n ≥ 2 distinct points in ℝd determine at least $c_dn^{2/d}$ distinct distances, with $c_d\\gt 0$ depending only on dimension. This matches the integer-grid order and resolves the higher-dimensional Erdős distinct-distances conjecture with a constant-factor bound.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/proof-map.tex","preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/reduction.tex","preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/polynomial.tex","preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/secants.tex","preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/profiles.tex","preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/scale-profile.tex","preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/sparse-cones.tex","preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/depth.tex","preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/motions.tex","preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/motion-partition.tex","preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/concentration.tex","preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/approximation.tex","preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/classical-incidence.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed integer $d\\ge3$, we prove that every set of $n\\ge2$ distinct points in $\\mathbb R^d$ determines at least $c_d n^{2/d}$ distinct distances, where $c_d>0$ depends only on $d$. This resolves the higher-dimensional Erd\\H{o}s distinct-distances conjecture positively.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":324},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and the methods of the proof","sourceOffset":1839},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why the constant factor matters","sourceOffset":6651},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Overview of the proof","sourceOffset":7569},{"kind":"section","title":"The reduction to sparse cones and concentration","sourceOffset":18592},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The contradiction regime","sourceOffset":18973},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A symmetric selection of directions","sourceOffset":22636},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The equal-distance graph and its pair flats","sourceOffset":29738},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Global and local concentration","sourceOffset":33477},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One random sample completes the proof","sourceOffset":37880},{"kind":"section","title":"Polynomial interpolation, regularity, and multiplicity","sourceOffset":40759},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Separators supplied by the distance set","sourceOffset":44112}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer \\(d\\ge3\\), there is a constant \\(c_d>0\\) such that every finite set \\(P\\subset\\mathbb R^d\\) of \\(n\\ge2\\) distinct points satisfies \\[ |\\Delta(P)|\\ge c_d n^{2/d}. \\]","sourceOffset":1111,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"red:main-contradiction","text":"The graph \\(\\mathcal H\\) cannot satisfy both bounds in Lemmas \\ref{red:global-concentration} and \\ref{red:local-concentration}.","sourceOffset":37932,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":353742,"sourceFileCount":16,"statementCount":24,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-higher-dimensional-Erdos-distinct-distances-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"167-01","slug":"The-weak-pinned-planar-distance-theorem-September-23-2026","familyId":"167","title":"The weak pinned planar distance theorem","abstract":"We prove the weak pinned Erdős distinct-distance conjecture. For every fixed ε > 0, all but $o(n)$ points of any n-point planar set determine at least $n^{1-\\varepsilon}$ distinct nonzero distances.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the weak pinned Erdős distance conjecture: for every fixed ε > 0, all but $o(n)$ points of any n-point planar set determine at least $n^{1-\\varepsilon}$ distinct nonzero distances. A complementary theorem bounds the number of unit-distance pairs by $O(n^{4/3-\\delta})$ for an absolute δ > 0.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-weak-pinned-planar-distance-theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-weak-pinned-planar-distance-theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-weak-pinned-planar-distance-theorem-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/The-weak-pinned-planar-distance-theorem-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-weak-pinned-planar-distance-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-weak-pinned-planar-distance-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/extremal.tex","preprints/The-weak-pinned-planar-distance-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/hierarchies.tex","preprints/The-weak-pinned-planar-distance-theorem-September-23-2026/build/figures/giant.tex","preprints/The-weak-pinned-planar-distance-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/smallcells.tex","preprints/The-weak-pinned-planar-distance-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/variance.tex","preprints/The-weak-pinned-planar-distance-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/unbounded.tex","preprints/The-weak-pinned-planar-distance-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/bounded.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-weak-pinned-planar-distance-theorem-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the weak pinned Erd\\H{o}s distinct-distance conjecture. For every fixed $\\varepsilon>0$, all but $o(n)$ points of any $n$-point planar set determine at least $n^{1-\\varepsilon}$ distinct nonzero distances.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":277},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and significance","sourceOffset":2773},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof in outline","sourceOffset":4534},{"kind":"section","title":"A graph of large distance classes","sourceOffset":11380},{"kind":"section","title":"Arithmetic and nested partitions","sourceOffset":22564},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two coordinates at every place","sourceOffset":22948},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Nested grids and their error","sourceOffset":25140},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removing the giant cell","sourceOffset":28615},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exact overlap identity","sourceOffset":32620},{"kind":"section","title":"Small cells and fiber mass","sourceOffset":34484},{"kind":"section","title":"Variance from off-diagonal sampling","sourceOffset":42131},{"kind":"section","title":"The case of unbounded overlap scale","sourceOffset":49765}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed $s>0$, one has $F_n(s)\\longrightarrow0$ as $n\\longrightarrow\\infty$.","sourceOffset":1468,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:pins","text":"For every fixed $\\varepsilon>0$, \\[ \\sup_{\\substack{P\\subset\\R^2\\\\|P|=n}} \\frac{\\bigl|\\{x\\in P:|D_x(P)|<n^{1-\\varepsilon}\\}\\bigr|}{n} \\longrightarrow0. \\] In particular, every sufficiently large $n$-point planar set has a pin determining at least $n^{1-\\varepsilon}$ distinct nonzero distances.","sourceOffset":1806,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/167.md","scopeText":"The formalized result shows that large repeated distance fibers are rare in arbitrary finite planar point sets. For each fixed $s>0$, the largest possible fraction of ordered distinct pairs $(x,y)$ whose distance from the pin $x$ occurs at least $n^s$ times tends to zero as the set size $n$ grows. Consequently, for every $\\varepsilon>0$, the fraction of pins determining fewer than $n^{1-\\varepsilon}$ distances tends uniformly to zero. The separate unit-distance power-saving theorem is not included.\n\nThe planar unit-distance problem asks how many pairs at distance one can occur among $n$ points. The formalization proves that there are absolute constants $C>0$ and $1\\le\\beta<4/3$ such that every finite planar point set of size $n$ determines at most $Cn^\\beta$ unordered unit-distance pairs. ","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/167.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":80126,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-weak-pinned-planar-distance-theorem-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"167-02","slug":"A-power-saving-for-planar-unit-distances-September-23-2026","familyId":"167","title":"A power saving for planar unit distances","abstract":"We prove a power saving for the planar unit-distance problem: for some absolute $\\beta\\lt 4/3$, every set of n points in the Euclidean plane determines $O(n^\\beta)$ unordered pairs at unit distance.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the weak pinned Erdős distance conjecture: for every fixed ε > 0, all but $o(n)$ points of any n-point planar set determine at least $n^{1-\\varepsilon}$ distinct nonzero distances. A complementary theorem bounds the number of unit-distance pairs by $O(n^{4/3-\\delta})$ for an absolute δ > 0.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-unit-distances-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-unit-distances-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-unit-distances-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-unit-distances-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-unit-distances-September-23-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-unit-distances-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-incidence.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-unit-distances-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-prediction.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-unit-distances-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-heights.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-unit-distances-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-budgets.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-unit-distances-September-23-2026/build/figures/interval-budgets.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-unit-distances-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-common-levels.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-unit-distances-September-23-2026/build/figures/strict-crosses.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-unit-distances-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-extraction.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-unit-distances-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-algebra.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-unit-distances-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a power saving for the planar unit-distance problem: for some absolute \\(\\beta<4/3\\), every set of \\(n\\) points in the Euclidean plane determines \\(O(n^\\beta)\\) unordered pairs at unit distance.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":249},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The unit-distance problem and earlier methods","sourceOffset":999},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof in outline","sourceOffset":4843},{"kind":"section","title":"An incidence piece and its probability laws","sourceOffset":11646},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Preserving incidences under algebraic specialization","sourceOffset":12710},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cuttings and the incidence bound","sourceOffset":14329},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Degree control and a maximizing rectangle","sourceOffset":20105},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Matching labels and uniformly rare sets","sourceOffset":27897},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The information carried by a wedge","sourceOffset":32907},{"kind":"section","title":"Prediction from short histories","sourceOffset":35455},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The probability laws and the entropy comparison","sourceOffset":36471},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Acceptance tests and the prediction coupling","sourceOffset":43306}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are absolute constants \\(0<C<\\infty\\) and \\(1\\le\\beta<4/3\\) such that \\(u(n)\\le Cn^\\beta\\) for every nonnegative integer \\(n\\).","sourceOffset":576,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"inc:incidence","text":"For \\(m\\) distinct points and \\(l\\) distinct unit circles in the plane, \\begin{equation} I\\le C\\log^2(2+m+l) \\bigl(m^{2/3}l^{2/3}+m+l\\bigr), \\end{equation} where \\(C\\) is an absolute constant. In particular, when \\(m,l\\) are bounded by a fixed power of \\(t\\), the logarithmic factor is \\((\\log t)^{O(1)}\\).","sourceOffset":19113,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/167.md","scopeText":"The formalized result shows that large repeated distance fibers are rare in arbitrary finite planar point sets. For each fixed $s>0$, the largest possible fraction of ordered distinct pairs $(x,y)$ whose distance from the pin $x$ occurs at least $n^s$ times tends to zero as the set size $n$ grows. Consequently, for every $\\varepsilon>0$, the fraction of pins determining fewer than $n^{1-\\varepsilon}$ distances tends uniformly to zero. The separate unit-distance power-saving theorem is not included.\n\nThe planar unit-distance problem asks how many pairs at distance one can occur among $n$ points. The formalization proves that there are absolute constants $C>0$ and $1\\le\\beta<4/3$ such that every finite planar point set of size $n$ determines at most $Cn^\\beta$ unordered unit-distance pairs. ","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/167.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":169751,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-power-saving-for-planar-unit-distances-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"168-01","slug":"Combinatorial-Invariance-of-Kazhdan-Lusztig-Polynomials-September-24-2026","familyId":"168","title":"Combinatorial invariance of Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomials","abstract":"We prove that an isomorphism of Bruhat intervals in arbitrary Coxeter systems preserves their equal-parameter Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomials. This resolves the full combinatorial invariance conjecture positively.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the full combinatorial invariance conjecture: isomorphic Bruhat intervals in arbitrary Coxeter systems have identical equal-parameter Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomials. Thus the abstract order of the interval determines the polynomial, even across different Coxeter systems.","pdfPath":"preprints/Combinatorial-Invariance-of-Kazhdan-Lusztig-Polynomials-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Combinatorial-Invariance-of-Kazhdan-Lusztig-Polynomials-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Combinatorial-Invariance-of-Kazhdan-Lusztig-Polynomials-September-24-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/Combinatorial-Invariance-of-Kazhdan-Lusztig-Polynomials-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Combinatorial-Invariance-of-Kazhdan-Lusztig-Polynomials-September-24-2026/build/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Combinatorial-Invariance-of-Kazhdan-Lusztig-Polynomials-September-24-2026/build/02-background.tex","preprints/Combinatorial-Invariance-of-Kazhdan-Lusztig-Polynomials-September-24-2026/build/03-dihedral.tex","preprints/Combinatorial-Invariance-of-Kazhdan-Lusztig-Polynomials-September-24-2026/build/figures/dihedral-transport.tex","preprints/Combinatorial-Invariance-of-Kazhdan-Lusztig-Polynomials-September-24-2026/build/04-localization.tex","preprints/Combinatorial-Invariance-of-Kazhdan-Lusztig-Polynomials-September-24-2026/build/05-comparison.tex","preprints/Combinatorial-Invariance-of-Kazhdan-Lusztig-Polynomials-September-24-2026/build/figures/comparison-dependencies.tex","preprints/Combinatorial-Invariance-of-Kazhdan-Lusztig-Polynomials-September-24-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Combinatorial-Invariance-of-Kazhdan-Lusztig-Polynomials-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that an isomorphism of Bruhat intervals in arbitrary Coxeter systems preserves their equal-parameter Kazhdan--Lusztig polynomials. This resolves the full combinatorial invariance conjecture positively.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":273},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the missing label data","sourceOffset":1877},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof mechanism","sourceOffset":7909},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":11270},{"kind":"section","title":"Real realizations and moment-graph sheaves","sourceOffset":12317},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reduction to finite rank","sourceOffset":12912},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A reflection-faithful real realization","sourceOffset":13891},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reflection orders and their path formula","sourceOffset":20030},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The sheaf and its full upward kernels","sourceOffset":22041},{"kind":"section","title":"Dihedral intervals and transported reflection orders","sourceOffset":29786},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Root planes and relative orders","sourceOffset":30365},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Graph reconstruction from interval order","sourceOffset":37264}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Combinatorial invariance","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $(W,S)$ and $(W',S')$ be arbitrary Coxeter systems, and let $u\\le b$ in $W$ and $u'\\le b'$ in $W'$. If \\[ \\iota:[u,b]\\longrightarrow[u',b'] \\] is an isomorphism of abstract Bruhat interval posets, then their equal-parameter Kazhdan--Lusztig polynomials satisfy \\[ P^W_{u,b}(q)=P^{W'}_{u',b'}(q). \\]","sourceOffset":1249,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Dyer's reflection-order path formula","label":"bg:paths","text":"Fix a reflection order and use left reflection labels on Bruhat edges. Let $\\widetilde R_{x,y}(T)$ be the sum of $T^r$ over directed paths of $r$ edges from $x$ to $y$ having strictly increasing labels. The empty path contributes $1$ when $x=y$. Then \\begin{equation} q^{-d(x,y)/2}R_{x,y}(q) =\\widetilde R_{x,y}\\bigl(q^{1/2}-q^{-1/2}\\bigr). \\end{equation} The same polynomial counts paths with strictly decreasing labels.","sourceOffset":20993,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/168.md","scopeText":"The combinatorial invariance conjecture asks whether a Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomial depends only on its Bruhat interval as an ordered set. The formalization proves that every order isomorphism between Bruhat intervals in arbitrary Coxeter systems preserves the corresponding equal-parameter Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomial. The two intervals may come from different Coxeter systems.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/168.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":107474,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Combinatorial-Invariance-of-Kazhdan-Lusztig-Polynomials-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"169-01","slug":"Elementary-Positivity-of-Chromatic-Quasisymmetric-Functions-September-24-2026","familyId":"169","title":"Elementary positivity of chromatic quasisymmetric functions","abstract":"We prove that the chromatic quasisymmetric function of every natural unit interval graph is elementary-positive over $\\mathbb N[q]$. This resolves the elementary-positivity part of the Shareshian–Wachs conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the elementary-positivity part of the Shareshian–Wachs conjecture: the chromatic quasisymmetric function of every natural unit interval graph has elementary-basis coefficients in $\\mathbb N[q]$. The coefficients count explicitly described permutations, giving a combinatorial explanation of positivity.","pdfPath":"preprints/Elementary-Positivity-of-Chromatic-Quasisymmetric-Functions-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Elementary-Positivity-of-Chromatic-Quasisymmetric-Functions-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Elementary-Positivity-of-Chromatic-Quasisymmetric-Functions-September-24-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/Elementary-Positivity-of-Chromatic-Quasisymmetric-Functions-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Elementary-Positivity-of-Chromatic-Quasisymmetric-Functions-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Elementary-Positivity-of-Chromatic-Quasisymmetric-Functions-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-graphs.tex","preprints/Elementary-Positivity-of-Chromatic-Quasisymmetric-Functions-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-reordering.tex","preprints/Elementary-Positivity-of-Chromatic-Quasisymmetric-Functions-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-walls.tex","preprints/Elementary-Positivity-of-Chromatic-Quasisymmetric-Functions-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-triangle.tex","preprints/Elementary-Positivity-of-Chromatic-Quasisymmetric-Functions-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-witness.tex","preprints/Elementary-Positivity-of-Chromatic-Quasisymmetric-Functions-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-completion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Elementary-Positivity-of-Chromatic-Quasisymmetric-Functions-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the chromatic quasisymmetric function of every natural unit interval graph is elementary-positive over $\\N[q]$. This resolves the elementary-positivity part of the Shareshian--Wachs conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":270},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":4172},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof structure","sourceOffset":12473},{"kind":"section","title":"Graph alphabets and the coloring reduction","sourceOffset":16214},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Admissible words and insertion digits","sourceOffset":16492},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The quantum torus of an ordered graph","sourceOffset":18538},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The elementary coefficient identity","sourceOffset":22612},{"kind":"section","title":"Positive reordering in a quantum torus","sourceOffset":27089},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Shuffle spaces and destabilizing quotients","sourceOffset":30620},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Separated centers and ordered decomposition","sourceOffset":34174},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Hilbert-series target","sourceOffset":41147},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A filtration and polynomial restriction symbols","sourceOffset":44131}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Elementary positivity and a permutation witness","label":"main:theorem","text":"There is a deterministic terminating procedure which, given a natural unit interval graph $G$ on $[n]$ and $\\sigma\\in D_G^0$, returns a partition $\\theta_G(\\sigma)\\vdash n$ such that \\begin{equation} \\chi_G(X;q)=\\sum_{\\sigma\\in D_G^0} q^{\\ginv_G(\\sigma)}e_{\\theta_G(\\sigma)}(X). \\end{equation} In particular every elementary coefficient of $\\chi_G(X;q)$ belongs to $\\N[q]$. The assertion holds as a formal symmetric-function identity, uniformly in $n$ and $G$.","sourceOffset":1703,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Young permutation modules in Hessenberg cohomology","label":"main:hessenberg","text":"Let $n\\ge1$, let $h$ be as above with graph $G=G(h)$, and let $S\\in M_n(\\C)$ be regular semisimple. Write \\[ \\operatorname{Hess}(S,h)= \\{F_\\bullet\\in\\operatorname{Flag}(\\C^n): SF_i\\subseteq F_{h(i)}\\text{ for all }i\\}. \\] For $\\lambda=(\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_\\ell)\\vdash n$, let $M^\\lambda=\\operatorname{Ind}_{S_\\lambda}^{S_n}\\mathbf{1}_{\\C}$ be the Young permutation module, where $S_\\lambda=S_{\\lambda_1}\\times\\cdots\\times S_{\\lambda_\\ell}$ is the standard Young subgroup, and put \\[ m_{\\lambda,d}(h)= \\#\\{\\sigma\\in D_G^0:\\theta_G(\\sigma)=\\lambda,\\ \\ginv_G(\\sigma)=d\\}. \\] Then for every integer $d\\ge0$, under Tymoczko's dot action on ordinary cohomology there is an abstract $\\C[S_n]$-module isomorphism \\[ H^{2d}(\\operatorname{Hess}(S,h);\\C) \\cong\\bigoplus_{\\lambda\\vdash n}(M^\\lambda)^{\\oplus m_{\\lambda,d}(h)}. \\]","sourceOffset":8229,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/169.md","scopeText":"The elementary-positivity part of the Shareshian–Wachs conjecture asks whether the chromatic quasisymmetric function of every natural unit interval graph has nonnegative coefficients in the elementary basis. The formalization proves this over $\\mathbb N[q]$. It constructs an explicit elementary-basis expansion indexed by the graph's permitted nondescent permutations, with each term weighted by a nonnegative power of $q$ determined by its graph inversions. The expansion holds for every finite number of color variables.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/169.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":161887,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":15,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Elementary-Positivity-of-Chromatic-Quasisymmetric-Functions-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"170-01","slug":"The-Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponent-of-r-5-t-September-24-2026","familyId":"170","title":"The sharp logarithmic exponent of r(5,t)","abstract":"We determine the sharp logarithmic exponent of the off-diagonal Ramsey number $r(5,t)$: $\\displaystyle r(5,t)=\\frac{t^4}{(\\log t)^{3+o(1)}} \\qquad (t\\longrightarrow\\infty).$","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every fixed integer s ≥ 5, proves $r(s,t)=t^{s-1}/(\\log t)^{s-2+o(1)}$ as $t\\to\\infty$, determining the logarithmic exponent and matching the classical upper bound at that scale. Here $r(s,t)$ is the least number of vertices forcing an s-clique or a t-vertex independent set.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponent-of-r-5-t-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponent-of-r-5-t-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponent-of-r-5-t-September-24-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/The-Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponent-of-r-5-t-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponent-of-r-5-t-September-24-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/The-Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponent-of-r-5-t-September-24-2026/build/upper-bound.tex","preprints/The-Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponent-of-r-5-t-September-24-2026/build/flags-entropy.tex","preprints/The-Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponent-of-r-5-t-September-24-2026/build/predictor-statement.tex","preprints/The-Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponent-of-r-5-t-September-24-2026/build/compression-local.tex","preprints/The-Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponent-of-r-5-t-September-24-2026/build/compression-tree.tex","preprints/The-Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponent-of-r-5-t-September-24-2026/build/predictor-setup.tex","preprints/The-Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponent-of-r-5-t-September-24-2026/build/predictor-geometry.tex","preprints/The-Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponent-of-r-5-t-September-24-2026/build/predictor-score.tex","preprints/The-Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponent-of-r-5-t-September-24-2026/build/predictor-completion.tex","preprints/The-Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponent-of-r-5-t-September-24-2026/build/final-conversion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponent-of-r-5-t-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We determine the sharp logarithmic exponent of the off-diagonal Ramsey number \\(r(5,t)\\): \\[ r(5,t)=\\frac{t^4}{(\\log t)^{3+o(1)}} \\qquad (t\\longrightarrow\\infty). \\]","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":230},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and contribution","sourceOffset":1209},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":4462},{"kind":"section","title":"The classical upper bound","sourceOffset":6867},{"kind":"section","title":"Projective flags and selected-stream entropy","sourceOffset":13257},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Incidence estimates","sourceOffset":13531},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The ordered graph","sourceOffset":15569},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Laws of selected tuples","sourceOffset":18408},{"kind":"section","title":"Describing a sparse incidence pair","sourceOffset":22494},{"kind":"section","title":"Preparing a consistent tuple for compression","sourceOffset":25082},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two marking scans","sourceOffset":27349},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Choosing a round before exposing its values","sourceOffset":34255}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is an absolute constant \\(C>0\\) such that, for every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), all sufficiently large integers \\(t\\) satisfy \\[ \\frac{t^4}{(\\log t)^{3+\\varepsilon}} \\ \\le\\ r(5,t)\\ \\le\\ C\\frac{t^4}{(\\log t)^3}. \\] The threshold for \\(t\\) may depend on \\(\\varepsilon\\). Consequently, \\[ \\lim_{t\\to\\infty} \\frac{4\\log t-\\log r(5,t)}{\\log\\log t}=3. \\]","sourceOffset":663,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:upper","text":"There is an absolute constant $C$ such that, for every integer $t\\ge2$, \\[ r(5,t)\\le C\\frac{t^4}{(\\log t)^3}. \\]","sourceOffset":7299,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/170.md","scopeText":"The formalization determines the sharp logarithmic exponent of the off-diagonal Ramsey number $r(5,t)$. It proves\n$r(5,t)=t^4/(\\log t)^{3+o(1)}$ as $t\\to\\infty$.\nFor every $\\varepsilon>0$, the lower bound $t^4/(\\log t)^{3+\\varepsilon}$ holds eventually, while the upper bound is $Ct^4/(\\log t)^3$ for an absolute $C>0$. The corresponding logarithmic exponent converges to three along all natural values of $t$.\n\nThe formalization determines the sharp logarithmic exponent of the off-diagonal Ramsey number for every fixed integer $s\\ge6$. It proves\n$r(s,t)=t^{s-1}/(\\log t)^{s-2+o(1)}$ as $t\\to\\infty$.\nMore explicitly, for each $\\varepsilon>0$ the lower bound $t^{s-1}/(\\log t)^{s-2+\\varepsilon}$ holds eventually, while the upper bound is $C_s t^{s-1}/(\\log t)^{s-2}$. The logarithmic exponent limi","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/170.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":133815,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponent-of-r-5-t-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"170-02","slug":"Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponents-for-Fixed-Off-Diagonal-Ramsey-Numbers-September-24-2026","familyId":"170","title":"Sharp logarithmic exponents for fixed off-diagonal Ramsey numbers","abstract":"For every fixed integer s ≥ 6, we determine the sharp logarithmic exponent of the off-diagonal Ramsey number: $\\displaystyle r(s,t)=\\frac{t^{s-1}}{(\\log t)^{s-2+o(1)}} \\qquad (t\\longrightarrow\\infty).$","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every fixed integer s ≥ 5, proves $r(s,t)=t^{s-1}/(\\log t)^{s-2+o(1)}$ as $t\\to\\infty$, determining the logarithmic exponent and matching the classical upper bound at that scale. Here $r(s,t)$ is the least number of vertices forcing an s-clique or a t-vertex independent set.","pdfPath":"preprints/Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponents-for-Fixed-Off-Diagonal-Ramsey-Numbers-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponents-for-Fixed-Off-Diagonal-Ramsey-Numbers-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponents-for-Fixed-Off-Diagonal-Ramsey-Numbers-September-24-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponents-for-Fixed-Off-Diagonal-Ramsey-Numbers-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponents-for-Fixed-Off-Diagonal-Ramsey-Numbers-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponents-for-Fixed-Off-Diagonal-Ramsey-Numbers-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-foundations.tex","preprints/Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponents-for-Fixed-Off-Diagonal-Ramsey-Numbers-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-geometry.tex","preprints/Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponents-for-Fixed-Off-Diagonal-Ramsey-Numbers-September-24-2026/build/figures/validation.tex","preprints/Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponents-for-Fixed-Off-Diagonal-Ramsey-Numbers-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-learning.tex","preprints/Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponents-for-Fixed-Off-Diagonal-Ramsey-Numbers-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-marking.tex","preprints/Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponents-for-Fixed-Off-Diagonal-Ramsey-Numbers-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-compression.tex","preprints/Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponents-for-Fixed-Off-Diagonal-Ramsey-Numbers-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-conclusion.tex","preprints/Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponents-for-Fixed-Off-Diagonal-Ramsey-Numbers-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponents-for-Fixed-Off-Diagonal-Ramsey-Numbers-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed integer $s\\ge6$, we determine the sharp logarithmic exponent of the off-diagonal Ramsey number: \\[ r(s,t)=\\frac{t^{s-1}}{(\\log t)^{s-2+o(1)}} \\qquad (t\\longrightarrow\\infty). \\]","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":269},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and contribution","sourceOffset":1176},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and its entropy obstruction","sourceOffset":5118},{"kind":"section","title":"Flags, incidence, and selected-tuple entropy","sourceOffset":11096},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Projective incidence","sourceOffset":11891},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The flag graph and rectangles","sourceOffset":14219},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Entropy after arbitrary selection","sourceOffset":17317},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The scales of a description","sourceOffset":21329},{"kind":"section","title":"Describing sparse pairs: validation and geometry","sourceOffset":22782},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The description and its validation","sourceOffset":24020},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reduction from higher projective dimensions","sourceOffset":33025},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Preparing dimensions two and three","sourceOffset":38364}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"intro:main","text":"For every fixed integer $s\\ge6$ there is a constant $C_s>0$ such that, for every $\\varepsilon>0$ and every sufficiently large integer $t$, \\[ \\frac{t^{s-1}}{(\\log t)^{s-2+\\varepsilon}} \\le r(s,t) \\le C_s\\frac{t^{s-1}}{(\\log t)^{s-2}}. \\] In particular, \\[ \\lim_{t\\to\\infty} \\frac{(s-1)\\log t-\\log r(s,t)}{\\log\\log t}=s-2. \\]","sourceOffset":642,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"intro:prime-construction","text":"Fix an integer $d\\ge5$ and a real number $0<\\eta<1/10$. For every sufficiently large prime $q$, with $\\sigma=\\log q$, there is a graph $G$ such that \\[ |V(G)|=\\lfloor q^d\\sigma\\rfloor,\\qquad K_{d+1}\\not\\subseteq G,\\qquad \\alpha(G)<\\lfloor q\\sigma^{1+\\eta}\\rfloor. \\]","sourceOffset":5301,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/170.md","scopeText":"The formalization determines the sharp logarithmic exponent of the off-diagonal Ramsey number $r(5,t)$. It proves\n$r(5,t)=t^4/(\\log t)^{3+o(1)}$ as $t\\to\\infty$.\nFor every $\\varepsilon>0$, the lower bound $t^4/(\\log t)^{3+\\varepsilon}$ holds eventually, while the upper bound is $Ct^4/(\\log t)^3$ for an absolute $C>0$. The corresponding logarithmic exponent converges to three along all natural values of $t$.\n\nThe formalization determines the sharp logarithmic exponent of the off-diagonal Ramsey number for every fixed integer $s\\ge6$. It proves\n$r(s,t)=t^{s-1}/(\\log t)^{s-2+o(1)}$ as $t\\to\\infty$.\nMore explicitly, for each $\\varepsilon>0$ the lower bound $t^{s-1}/(\\log t)^{s-2+\\varepsilon}$ holds eventually, while the upper bound is $C_s t^{s-1}/(\\log t)^{s-2}$. The logarithmic exponent limi","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/170.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":175342,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Sharp-Logarithmic-Exponents-for-Fixed-Off-Diagonal-Ramsey-Numbers-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"171-01","slug":"The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026","familyId":"171","title":"The hypercube Ramsey number has linear order","abstract":"We prove that the two-color Ramsey number of the n-dimensional binary cube is at most $C2^n$, where C is an absolute constant. This resolves positively the hypercube Ramsey conjecture of Burr and Erdős.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the Burr–Erdős hypercube Ramsey conjecture: the two-color Ramsey number of the n-dimensional cube is $\\Theta(2^n)$. Thus every red-blue coloring of a complete graph on a universal constant times the cube's number of vertices contains a monochromatic copy of the cube.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-regime-guide.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/figures/overview.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/proof-inputs.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-reduction-and-notation.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-general-conventions-and-probabilistic-tools.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-bias-versus-purity-at-power-widths.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-a-constant-broad-high-neighborhood-case.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-very-small-polynomial-width-on-the-broad-side-polynomial-defect.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-a-small-power-key-grid.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-asymmetric-pure-patches-under-2.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/09-intermediate-powers-of-bias.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/10-full-dimensional-cluster-patches.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/11-jump-to-large-bias-only-at-linear-budget.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/12-the-two-deep-orientations-some-estimates.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/13-patch-tiling-under-9.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/14-internal-solver-and-profiles-for-these-tilings.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/15-high-modes-of-the-tiling.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/16-low-modes-geometry-and-cell-states.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/17-initial-list-gates-and-resampling-estimates.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/18-late-dynamics-and-its-transfer-problem.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/figures/endpoint-comparisons.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/sections/regime-table.tex","preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the two-color Ramsey number of the $n$-dimensional binary cube is at most $C2^n$, where $C$ is an absolute constant. This resolves positively the hypercube Ramsey conjecture of Burr and Erd\\H{o}s.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":256},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The problem and its historical development","sourceOffset":806},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background on embedding and sampling","sourceOffset":6806},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The four phases of the proof","sourceOffset":10455},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Terminology","sourceOffset":22127},{"kind":"section","title":"Reduction and notation","sourceOffset":23377},{"kind":"section","title":"General conventions and probabilistic tools","sourceOffset":26990},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Geometric height device","sourceOffset":39880},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Near-product injections","sourceOffset":56604},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Clock lemma","sourceOffset":65494},{"kind":"section","title":"Bias versus purity at power widths","sourceOffset":84085},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Hidden tuples and height choices","sourceOffset":92331}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is an absolute constant $C>0$ such that \\[ R(Q_n)\\le C2^n \\qquad\\text{for every integer }n\\ge0. \\]","sourceOffset":654,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"The remaining deep regime","label":"cor:remaining-deep-regime","text":"The possibility remaining in this analysis has both \\(H_L^\\dagger\\ge1\\), i.e., for each fixed error exponent slack \\(\\epsilon>0\\) one has deep discrepancy \\(\\le n^{-1+\\epsilon}\\) on retained labels at some budgets \\(n^x,\\alpha n\\) with constants \\(x,\\alpha>0\\), in both orientations.","sourceOffset":378182,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Availability","label":null,"text":"A witness has specified supports on the current host sides. After a pair of label removals, it remains a witness if both supports are wholly retained. A family is available along a sequence if some constant \\(\\kappa>0\\) has the following property: eventually, after every removal of at most \\(\\kappa N\\) labels from each side, at least one witness remains. The witness may be chosen anew for each pair of removals.","sourceOffset":27494,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Raw experiments, gates, and stage histories","label":null,"text":"A raw experiment is the specified sampling law before the avoidance constraints currently under discussion are imposed. A local gate is an event determined by the declared local inputs; retaining the gate means multiplying by its indicator, rather than conditioning the reference law on it. A successful prehistory is an entering stage history on which that stage's stated hypotheses hold. Comparisons are made separately at each such history.","sourceOffset":32697,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":618626,"sourceFileCount":25,"statementCount":20,"definitionCount":4,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-hypercube-Ramsey-number-has-linear-order-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"172-01","slug":"A-classification-of-finite-Euclidean-Ramsey-configurations-September-23-2026","familyId":"172","title":"A classification of finite Euclidean Ramsey configurations","abstract":"We classify finite Euclidean Ramsey configurations by a necessary and sufficient tensor condition over their coordinate fields. The Ramsey property here concerns monochromatic congruent copies at the original scale under arbitrary finite colorings. The criterion shows that every nonempty subtransitive set and every nonempty set of at most five points on a circle is Ramsey. In particular, some Ramsey cyclic quadrilaterals are not subtransitive, disproving the necessity direction of the Leader–Russell–Walters conjectured characterization.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Classifies finite point configurations that occur monochromatically, at their original scale, in every finite coloring of sufficiently high-dimensional Euclidean space. The characterization is an algebraic condition over the coordinate field. It also disproves the Leader–Russell–Walters conjecture that every such configuration is a subset of a finite transitive set.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-classification-of-finite-Euclidean-Ramsey-configurations-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-classification-of-finite-Euclidean-Ramsey-configurations-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-classification-of-finite-Euclidean-Ramsey-configurations-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-classification-of-finite-Euclidean-Ramsey-configurations-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-classification-of-finite-Euclidean-Ramsey-configurations-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-classification-of-finite-Euclidean-Ramsey-configurations-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-tensors.tex","preprints/A-classification-of-finite-Euclidean-Ramsey-configurations-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-stencil.tex","preprints/A-classification-of-finite-Euclidean-Ramsey-configurations-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-pairs.tex","preprints/A-classification-of-finite-Euclidean-Ramsey-configurations-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-paths.tex","preprints/A-classification-of-finite-Euclidean-Ramsey-configurations-September-23-2026/build/figures/synchronization.tex","preprints/A-classification-of-finite-Euclidean-Ramsey-configurations-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-groups.tex","preprints/A-classification-of-finite-Euclidean-Ramsey-configurations-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-classification-of-finite-Euclidean-Ramsey-configurations-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We classify finite Euclidean Ramsey configurations by a necessary and sufficient tensor condition over their coordinate fields. The Ramsey property here concerns monochromatic congruent copies at the original scale under arbitrary finite colorings. The criterion shows that every nonempty subtransitive set and every nonempty set of at most five points on a circle is Ramsey. In particular, some Ramsey cyclic quadrilaterals are not subtransitive, disproving the necessity direction of the Leader--Russell--Walters conjectured characterization.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The classification problem","sourceOffset":607},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exact tensor criterion","sourceOffset":3611},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Geometric consequences","sourceOffset":5465},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the algebra forces a monochromatic copy","sourceOffset":7180},{"kind":"section","title":"Rational tensors and the coloring obstruction","sourceOffset":10591},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite lattice identity","sourceOffset":20349},{"kind":"section","title":"Exact coordinate permutations at two scales","sourceOffset":26887},{"kind":"section","title":"From paths to monochromatic copies","sourceOffset":38927},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite dimensions and synchronized weights","sourceOffset":40807},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Realizing ultrafilter products by strings","sourceOffset":43274},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Hales--Jewett step","sourceOffset":49487},{"kind":"section","title":"From coordinate permutations to equal path endpoints","sourceOffset":53895}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Classification","label":"thm:classification","text":"Let $A=\\{a_1,\\ldots,a_s\\}\\subset\\R^d$ be a finite set of at least two points with affine span $\\R^d$, and define $p_i,F,B,m_F$ as above. Then $A$ is Ramsey if and only if there is a matrix $P\\in\\operatorname{Mat}_{d+1}(B)$ such that \\begin{align} (p_i\\otimes1)^T P(1\\otimes p_i)&=0 &&(1\\le i\\le s),\\\\ m_F(P_{\\alpha\\beta})&=\\delta_{\\alpha\\beta} &&(1\\le\\alpha,\\beta\\le d). \\end{align} Here the entries of $p_i\\otimes1$ and $1\\otimes p_i$ are formed componentwise. There are no separate conditions on the multiplied constant row or constant column of $P$.","sourceOffset":4454,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"alg:equivalence","text":"The matrix condition in Theorem \\ref{thm:classification} is equivalent to \\eqref{alg:tensor}.","sourceOffset":12058,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/172.md","scopeText":"A finite configuration is Euclidean Ramsey if every finite coloring of some sufficiently high-dimensional Euclidean space contains a monochromatic congruent copy at the original scale. The formalization gives the tensor-field classification for full-affine-span configurations, covers singleton and affine-span reductions, and proves that every Ramsey configuration is cospherical. It also proves that every nonempty subset of a finite transitive configuration and every nonempty set of at most five points on a circle is Ramsey. A further sufficient condition uses linear independence of the quadratic evaluation rows of a spherical configuration.\n\nThe formalization also gives spherical non-Ramsey examples: the specified twelve-point set on the unit circle has a fifty-color obstruction in every p","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/172.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":81512,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-classification-of-finite-Euclidean-Ramsey-configurations-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"173-01","slug":"A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"173","title":"A proof of Seymour’s second-neighborhood conjecture","abstract":"We prove that every nonempty finite oriented graph has a vertex with at least as many vertices at directed distance two as at directed distance one. This resolves Seymour's second neighborhood conjecture positively.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Seymour's second-neighborhood conjecture: every nonempty finite oriented graph has a vertex with at least as many vertices at directed distance exactly two as at directed distance one. Oriented graphs may be arbitrary apart from the exclusion of loops and oppositely directed edge pairs.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/00-introduction.tex","preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/01-reduction.tex","preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/02-pruning.tex","preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/figures/pruning.tex","preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/03-incompatibility.tex","preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/04-weighted-consequences.tex","preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/05-directed-cycle-consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every nonempty finite oriented graph has a vertex with at least as many vertices at directed distance two as at directed distance one. This resolves Seymour's second neighborhood conjecture positively.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":261},{"kind":"section","title":"From a counterexample to a strict subset inequality","sourceOffset":7520},{"kind":"section","title":"Pruning two families of pairs","sourceOffset":17714},{"kind":"section","title":"The extremal contradiction","sourceOffset":34787},{"kind":"section","title":"Weighted consequences","sourceOffset":41064},{"kind":"section","title":"Directed-cycle consequences","sourceOffset":43864}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Every nonempty finite oriented graph has a vertex \\(v\\) such that \\[ \\abs{N_1^+(v)}\\leq \\abs{N_2^+(v)}. \\]","sourceOffset":947,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Counterexample reduction","label":"prop:counterexample-reduction","text":"If Theorem \\ref{thm:main} has a counterexample, then there is a finite nonempty oriented graph $G$ in which every vertex has positive indegree and \\begin{equation} \\abs{U}+\\abs{F_G^2(U)}<2\\abs{F_G(U)} \\qquad\\text{for every }\\varnothing\\ne U\\subsetneq V(G). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":7912,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/173.md","scopeText":"Seymour's second-neighborhood conjecture asserts that every nonempty finite oriented graph has a vertex with at least as many second out-neighbors as first out-neighbors. The formalized result proves this assertion, where the second neighborhood consists of vertices at directed distance exactly two. The initial vertex and first neighbors are excluded from that count, and sinks are included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/173.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":46699,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"174-01","slug":"The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"174","title":"The strong thin tree conjecture","abstract":"We prove that every finite loopless k-edge-connected multigraph on at least two vertices, with k ≥ 1, has a spanning tree meeting each cut in at most $C/k$ times the size of the cut, where C is a universal constant. This resolves the strong thin tree conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the strong thin-tree conjecture constructively. Every finite loopless k-edge-connected multigraph on at least two vertices has a spanning tree containing at most a universal $C/k$ fraction of the edges of every cut. Such a tree can be found deterministically in polynomial time, even with binary-encoded parallel-edge multiplicities.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/hierarchy.tex","preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/extraction.tex","preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/figures/packing_hierarchy.tex","preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/sparsification.tex","preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/iteration.tex","preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/mss.tex","preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/hierarchy_proof.tex","preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/hierarchy_dual.tex","preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/hierarchy_bags.tex","preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/hierarchy_bags_spectral.tex","preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/hierarchy_compact.tex","preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/hierarchy_assigned.tex","preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/hierarchy_assigned_preprocess.tex","preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/hierarchy_assigned_order.tex","preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/hierarchy_assigned_construction.tex","preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/hierarchy_assigned_postprocess.tex","preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/fixedpoint.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every finite loopless \\(k\\)-edge-connected multigraph on at least two vertices, with \\(k\\ge1\\), has a spanning tree meeting each cut in at most \\(C/k\\) times the size of the cut, where \\(C\\) is a universal constant. This resolves the strong thin tree conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":364},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The conjecture and its history","sourceOffset":1480},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two main deductions","sourceOffset":4124},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and inputs","sourceOffset":8151},{"kind":"section","title":"Laplacians and tree packings","sourceOffset":9331},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Matrix conventions","sourceOffset":9959},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The partition criterion","sourceOffset":12231},{"kind":"section","title":"Shortcuts for a locally connected hierarchy","sourceOffset":16435},{"kind":"section","title":"Extracting a tree packing with small resistance","sourceOffset":19526},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A compact set of matrices","sourceOffset":20815},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Packing partitions and a simultaneous response","sourceOffset":22368},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A consistent matrix from a fixed-point limit","sourceOffset":29338}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a universal constant \\(C>0\\) such that, for every integer \\(k\\ge1\\), every finite \\(k\\)-edge-connected undirected multigraph without loops and with at least two vertices has a spanning tree \\(T\\) satisfying \\[ |\\delta_T(S)|\\le\\frac Ck\\,|\\delta_G(S)| \\qquad\\text{for every }\\varnothing\\ne S\\subsetneq V. \\]","sourceOffset":1116,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Nash--Williams--Tutte","label":"thm:packing","text":"For a positive integer \\(r\\), a graph \\(H\\) contains \\(r\\) edge-disjoint spanning trees if and only if \\[ |E_H(\\mathcal P)|\\ge r(|\\mathcal P|-1) \\qquad\\text{for every partition }\\mathcal P\\text{ of }V(H). \\]","sourceOffset":12269,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Hierarchy and its boundaries","label":"def:hierarchy","text":"Let $H=(V,E)$ be a finite loopless multigraph. A hierarchy on $V$ is a finite rooted tree of distinct nonempty vertex sets. Its root is $V$, its leaves are the singleton sets, and the children of every nonleaf form a partition of that node into at least two sets. For a nonroot node $A$, write $A^*$ for its parent and define \\[ P(A)=\\delta_H(A),\\qquad O(A)=E_H(A,A^*\\setminus A). \\] All edge sets here retain multiplicities. Given an integer $h\\ge2$, the hierarchy is locally $h$-connected if $H[A]$ is $h$-edge-connected for every node $A$. For singleton nodes this condition is vacuous.","sourceOffset":16804,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Balls, bags, and geometric sequences","label":"hb:definitions","text":"Set $Q=[0,1]^d$ and \\[ B_Q(x,r)=\\{y\\in Q:\\|y-x\\|_1<r\\}\\qquad(r>0). \\] A bag is a nonempty collection of pairwise disjoint such balls, all of the same radius and with centers among the $X_v$. Its type is $(\\delta)$ when that radius is $\\delta$, and its type is $(\\delta,\\Delta)$ when, in addition, all distances between its centers are at most $\\Delta$. Its size $|\\mathrm{Bag}|$ is its number of balls. For $\\beta>1$, a bag of type $(\\delta,\\Delta)$ is $\\beta$-compact if \\[ |\\mathrm{Bag}|\\ge2,\\qquad \\beta\\Delta\\le\\delta|\\mathrm{Bag}|. \\] The midpoint of two centers belongs to $Q$, so disjoint radi","sourceOffset":82562,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/174.md","scopeText":"The strong thin-tree conjecture asks for spanning trees that cross every cut sparsely relative to the original graph. The formalized result gives one absolute constant $C>0$ such that every finite loopless $k$-edge-connected multigraph with at least two vertices has a spanning tree crossing each nontrivial cut at most $(C/k)$ times the original cut size. Parallel edges remain distinct. No numerical value of $C$ or construction algorithm is asserted.\n\nThe strong thin-tree problem asks for a spanning tree that crosses every cut sparsely relative to the original graph. The formalization gives one deterministic polynomial-time algorithm and an absolute constant $C>0$ such that a finite $k$-edge-connected loopless multigraph yields a spanning tree using at most a $C/k$ fraction of the edges of ","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/174.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":177798,"sourceFileCount":20,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":4,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-strong-thin-tree-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"174-02","slug":"A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026","familyId":"174","title":"A polynomial-time construction of strong thin trees","abstract":"We give a deterministic polynomial-time construction of strong thin trees. Given a finite k-edge-connected loopless multigraph on at least one vertex, the algorithm constructs a spanning tree meeting every cut in at most a $C/k$ fraction of its edges, for a universal constant C. The running time is polynomial in the binary input length, including when parallel-edge multiplicities are encoded in binary.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the strong thin-tree conjecture constructively. Every finite loopless k-edge-connected multigraph on at least two vertices has a spanning tree containing at most a universal $C/k$ fraction of the edges of every cut. Such a tree can be found deterministically in polynomial time, even with binary-encoded parallel-edge multiplicities.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/build/source/macros.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/main-theorem.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/tree_geometry.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/build/source/figures/metric_geometry.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/extraction.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/normalization.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/signing.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/iteration.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/fractional_rounding.tex","preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/signing_interface.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a deterministic polynomial-time construction of strong thin trees. Given a finite $k$-edge-connected loopless multigraph on at least one vertex, the algorithm constructs a spanning tree meeting every cut in at most a $C/k$ fraction of its edges, for a universal constant $C$. The running time is polynomial in the binary input length, including when parallel-edge multiplicities are encoded in binary.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":472},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and prior work","sourceOffset":3039},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From the geometric existence proof to an algorithm","sourceOffset":5179},{"kind":"section","title":"Metric-cone shortcuts for thin trees","sourceOffset":10300},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The conic dual","sourceOffset":13699},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A convex realization with exact radial motion","sourceOffset":18033},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Transferring the bag and charging estimates","sourceOffset":23307},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Choosing parameters and responding to a partition chain","sourceOffset":48796},{"kind":"section","title":"Constructive shortcut extraction","sourceOffset":54736},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A ramp of packing capacities","sourceOffset":55987},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact separation and a uniform interior ball","sourceOffset":65374},{"kind":"section","title":"A rank-preserving normalized frame","sourceOffset":77318}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Algorithmic strong thin trees","label":"thm:tt-algorithm","text":"There is an absolute constant $C$ with the following property. Given a finite $k$-edge-connected multigraph $G$ on at least one vertex and an integer $k\\ge1$, a deterministic algorithm returns a spanning tree $T$ satisfying \\begin{equation} |\\delta_T(S)|\\le \\frac Ck\\,|\\delta_G(S)| \\qquad(\\varnothing\\ne S\\subsetneq V(G)) \\end{equation} in time polynomial in the total binary input length, whether edges are listed explicitly or multiplicities are encoded in binary. For a one-vertex graph the answer is the empty tree.","sourceOffset":1509,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Hierarchy bound for the metric test cone","label":"thm:tg-hierarchy","text":"There is an absolute integer $h_0$ such that, for every integer $h\\ge h_0$, every locally $h$-connected hierarchy of $G$, and every collection $\\mathcal M$ of nonroot nodes satisfying $|O(A)|\\ge |P(A)|/h$, there is a matrix $D\\succ0$ on $\\mathsf E$ with \\begin{align} \\ip{D}{Z}&\\le\\ip{L_G}{Z} &&(Z\\in\\mathcal N),\\\\ \\frac1{|O(A)|}\\sum_{e\\in O(A)}b_e^TD^{-1}b_e &\\le h^{-31/40} &&(A\\in\\mathcal M). \\end{align} The cutoff is independent of the graph and the hierarchy depth.","sourceOffset":12793,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/174.md","scopeText":"The strong thin-tree conjecture asks for spanning trees that cross every cut sparsely relative to the original graph. The formalized result gives one absolute constant $C>0$ such that every finite loopless $k$-edge-connected multigraph with at least two vertices has a spanning tree crossing each nontrivial cut at most $(C/k)$ times the original cut size. Parallel edges remain distinct. No numerical value of $C$ or construction algorithm is asserted.\n\nThe strong thin-tree problem asks for a spanning tree that crosses every cut sparsely relative to the original graph. The formalization gives one deterministic polynomial-time algorithm and an absolute constant $C>0$ such that a finite $k$-edge-connected loopless multigraph yields a spanning tree using at most a $C/k$ fraction of the edges of ","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/174.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":129757,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"175-01","slug":"Graph-Decompositions-at-the-Integral-Expectation-Threshold-October-5-2026","familyId":"175","title":"Graph Decompositions at the Integral Expectation Threshold","abstract":"We prove the graph-decomposition conjecture of Ascoli, He, Park, and Talagrand. Every graph admits a partition into a universally bounded number of fixed edge pieces, each having ordinary containment threshold at most a universal constant times the original graph's integral expectation threshold. The partition is chosen before sampling the random host, and the separate embeddings of the pieces need not agree on shared vertices.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that integral and fractional expectation thresholds differ by at most a universal factor, and resolves Talagrand's discrete-convexity conjecture. An application proves the Ascoli–He–Park–Talagrand graph-decomposition conjecture: every graph's edges split into a universally bounded number of fixed pieces, each with containment threshold at most a universal constant times the original graph's integral expectation threshold. The pieces' embeddings need not agree on shared vertices.","pdfPath":"preprints/Graph-Decompositions-at-the-Integral-Expectation-Threshold-October-5-2026/graph-threshold-decompositions.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Graph-Decompositions-at-the-Integral-Expectation-Threshold-October-5-2026/graph-threshold-decompositions.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Graph-Decompositions-at-the-Integral-Expectation-Threshold-October-5-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/Graph-Decompositions-at-the-Integral-Expectation-Threshold-October-5-2026/build/main.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Graph-Decompositions-at-the-Integral-Expectation-Threshold-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the graph-decomposition conjecture of Ascoli, He, Park, and Talagrand. Every graph admits a partition into a universally bounded number of fixed edge pieces, each having ordinary containment threshold at most a universal constant times the original graph's integral expectation threshold. The partition is chosen before sampling the random host, and the separate embeddings of the pieces need not agree on shared vertices.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":478},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":2432},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Method","sourceOffset":3994},{"kind":"section","title":"Covering input and elementary estimates","sourceOffset":6069},{"kind":"section","title":"Uniform neighborhood transfer","sourceOffset":9877},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compressing requirements","sourceOffset":10538},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The transfer lemma","sourceOffset":12348},{"kind":"section","title":"Direct embedding lemmas","sourceOffset":18199},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A maximum-degree bound","sourceOffset":18516},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Small star forests","sourceOffset":21976},{"kind":"section","title":"Constructing the fixed partition","sourceOffset":26112}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist an absolute integer $k\\geq2$ and an absolute constant $L\\geq1$ such that, for every $n\\geq2$ and every graph $H$ on at most $n$ vertices, the edges of $H$ admit a partition \\[ E(H)=E(H_1)\\,\\dot\\cup\\,\\cdots\\,\\dot\\cup\\,E(H_k) \\quad\\text{with}\\quad p_c(H_i;n)\\leq Lq(H;n)\\qquad(1\\leq i\\leq k). \\] The pieces may be empty and may share vertices. The partition depends only on $H,n$ and is fixed before the random host is sampled.","sourceOffset":1656,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Discrete convexity, {\\cite[Theorem 1.1","label":"thm:discrete","text":"{OpenAI26DC}}] Set $K_0=2^{75}$. For every nonempty finite set $X$, every $r\\in(0,1)$, and every family $\\calD\\subseteq2^X$ with $\\mu_r(\\calD)\\geq1-1/K_0$, the family \\[ \\mathcal E_{K_0}(\\calD)= \\{S\\subseteq X:S\\not\\subseteq D_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup D_{K_0} \\text{ for every }D_1,\\ldots,D_{K_0}\\in\\calD\\} \\] has a cover of cost at most $1/2$ at the same parameter $r$. The family $\\calD$ need not be monotone, and members of a tuple may repeat.","sourceOffset":6538,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/175.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":32290,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Graph-Decompositions-at-the-Integral-Expectation-Threshold-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Graph-Decompositions-at-the-Integral-Expectation-Threshold-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Graph-Decompositions-at-the-Integral-Expectation-Threshold-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"175-02","slug":"Integral-and-fractional-expectation-thresholds-are-equivalent-September-23-2026","familyId":"175","title":"Integral and fractional expectation thresholds are equivalent","abstract":"We prove Talagrand's conjecture that integral and fractional expectation thresholds are within a universal constant factor, with the same covering budget.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that integral and fractional expectation thresholds differ by at most a universal factor, and resolves Talagrand's discrete-convexity conjecture. An application proves the Ascoli–He–Park–Talagrand graph-decomposition conjecture: every graph's edges split into a universally bounded number of fixed pieces, each with containment threshold at most a universal constant times the original graph's integral expectation threshold. The pieces' embeddings need not agree on shared vertices.","pdfPath":"preprints/Integral-and-fractional-expectation-thresholds-are-equivalent-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Integral-and-fractional-expectation-thresholds-are-equivalent-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Integral-and-fractional-expectation-thresholds-are-equivalent-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/Integral-and-fractional-expectation-thresholds-are-equivalent-September-23-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Integral-and-fractional-expectation-thresholds-are-equivalent-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Integral-and-fractional-expectation-thresholds-are-equivalent-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/selector.tex","preprints/Integral-and-fractional-expectation-thresholds-are-equivalent-September-23-2026/build/source/figures/one-level-delay.tex","preprints/Integral-and-fractional-expectation-thresholds-are-equivalent-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/rounding.tex","preprints/Integral-and-fractional-expectation-thresholds-are-equivalent-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/consequences.tex","preprints/Integral-and-fractional-expectation-thresholds-are-equivalent-September-23-2026/build/source/references/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Integral-and-fractional-expectation-thresholds-are-equivalent-September-23-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Talagrand's conjecture that integral and fractional expectation thresholds are within a universal constant factor, with the same covering budget.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":200},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Thresholds, selectors, and earlier comparisons","sourceOffset":2648},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The selector estimate and the rounding mechanism","sourceOffset":6815},{"kind":"section","title":"A multiscale selector estimate","sourceOffset":9927},{"kind":"section","title":"Rounding a fractional cover","sourceOffset":24376},{"kind":"section","title":"A consequence for unions of two sets","sourceOffset":29065}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every finite nonempty $X$ and every nonempty proper increasing family $\\F\\subseteq2^X$, \\[ q_f(\\F)\\le25\\cdot512^4\\,q(\\F). \\] Both thresholds use the budget $1/2$. The constant is independent of $|X|$, the sizes of the minimal members of $\\F$, and the support of the fractional cover.","sourceOffset":2131,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Multiscale selector estimate","label":"thm:multiscale-selector","text":"Let $X$ be a finite set, let $\\F$ be a nonempty family of nonempty subsets of $X$, and suppose that $\\F$ is not $p$-small, where $0<p\\le 1$. For each $H\\in\\F$, let $\\lambda_H$ be a probability mass function on $X$ supported on $H$. Set $D=256$, and let $s\\ge 0$ be an integer such that \\[ p\\sum_{i=1}^s D^i\\le \\frac12. \\] Independently color the elements of $X$ by a map $a:X\\to\\{1,\\ldots,s+1\\}$, with color probabilities \\begin{equation} \\pi_i=D^ip\\quad(1\\le i\\le s), \\qquad \\pi_{s+1}=1-p\\sum_{i=1}^sD^i. \\end{equation} For a coloring $v$, write $A_i(v)=\\{x\\in X:v(x)\\le i\\}$. With probability at least $9/10$, there is an $H\\in\\F$ such that \\begin{equation} \\lambda_H(A_i(a))\\ge 1-2^{-i} \\qquad(1\\le i\\le s). \\end{equation} In particular, with probability at least $9/10$, there is an $H\\in\\F$ for which \\begin{equation} \\sum_{x\\in X}a(x)\\lambda_H(x)\\le 2. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":10679,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/175.md","scopeText":"Talagrand's expectation-threshold conjecture asks whether the fractional and integral expectation thresholds are comparable by an absolute constant. The formalized result proves $q_f(\\mathcal F)\\le25\\cdot512^4 q(\\mathcal F)$ for every nonempty proper increasing family on a finite nonempty ground set. Both thresholds use cover budget $1/2$, and fractional covers may assign weights to every subset, including the empty set.\n\nThe formalized result proves Talagrand's discrete-convexity assertion with $k=2^{75}$. If a family of subsets of a finite nonempty ground set has Bernoulli-$p$ measure at least $1-1/k$, then the sets not contained in a union of $k$ members form a $p$-small family: they have a containment cover of total $p$-cost at most $1/2$. This holds for every $0<p<1$, with repeated me","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/175.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":36355,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Integral-and-fractional-expectation-thresholds-are-equivalent-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"175-03","slug":"Talagrands-discrete-convexity-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"175","title":"Talagrand’s discrete-convexity conjecture","abstract":"We prove Talagrand's discrete-convexity conjecture. There is a universal integer k such that, whenever an arbitrary family has Bernoulli product measure at least $1-1/k$, the sets not contained in a union of k members admit a cover of total cost at most 1/2 at the same density.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that integral and fractional expectation thresholds differ by at most a universal factor, and resolves Talagrand's discrete-convexity conjecture. An application proves the Ascoli–He–Park–Talagrand graph-decomposition conjecture: every graph's edges split into a universally bounded number of fixed pieces, each with containment threshold at most a universal constant times the original graph's integral expectation threshold. The pieces' embeddings need not agree on shared vertices.","pdfPath":"preprints/Talagrands-discrete-convexity-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Talagrands-discrete-convexity-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Talagrands-discrete-convexity-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/Talagrands-discrete-convexity-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Talagrands-discrete-convexity-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/00_intro.tex","preprints/Talagrands-discrete-convexity-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/01_covering.tex","preprints/Talagrands-discrete-convexity-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/02_weights.tex","preprints/Talagrands-discrete-convexity-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/sections/03_density.tex","preprints/Talagrands-discrete-convexity-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/figures/coupling.tex","preprints/Talagrands-discrete-convexity-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Talagrands-discrete-convexity-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Talagrand's discrete-convexity conjecture. There is a universal integer $k$ such that, whenever an arbitrary family has Bernoulli product measure at least $1-1/k$, the sets not contained in a union of $k$ members admit a cover of total cost at most $1/2$ at the same density.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":331},{"kind":"section","title":"An elementary covering lemma","sourceOffset":8256},{"kind":"section","title":"Weights for exceptional sets","sourceOffset":14070},{"kind":"section","title":"Returning to the original density","sourceOffset":21824}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Set $k=2^{75}$. For every integer $N\\ge1$, every $p\\in(0,1)$, and every family $\\mathcal D\\subseteq2^{[N]}$, \\[ \\mu_p(\\mathcal D)\\ge1-\\frac1k \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad E_k(\\mathcal D)\\text{ is }p\\text{-small}. \\]","sourceOffset":2204,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Two unions at constant density loss","label":"cor:two-union","text":"There is a universal constant $C\\ge1$ such that, for every integer $N\\ge1$, every $p\\in(0,1)$, and every family $\\mathcal D\\subseteq2^{[N]}$, \\[ \\mu_p(\\mathcal D)>\\frac12 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad E_2(\\mathcal D)\\text{ is }(p/C)\\text{-small}. \\]","sourceOffset":4875,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/175.md","scopeText":"Talagrand's expectation-threshold conjecture asks whether the fractional and integral expectation thresholds are comparable by an absolute constant. The formalized result proves $q_f(\\mathcal F)\\le25\\cdot512^4 q(\\mathcal F)$ for every nonempty proper increasing family on a finite nonempty ground set. Both thresholds use cover budget $1/2$, and fractional covers may assign weights to every subset, including the empty set.\n\nThe formalized result proves Talagrand's discrete-convexity assertion with $k=2^{75}$. If a family of subsets of a finite nonempty ground set has Bernoulli-$p$ measure at least $1-1/k$, then the sets not contained in a union of $k$ members form a $p$-small family: they have a containment cover of total $p$-cost at most $1/2$. This holds for every $0<p<1$, with repeated me","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/175.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":28939,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Talagrands-discrete-convexity-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"176-01","slug":"The-second-Kahn-Kalai-conjecture-September-24-2026","familyId":"176","title":"The second Kahn–Kalai conjecture","abstract":"We prove the second Kahn–Kalai conjecture. For every finite simple graph H with h ≥ 1 edges and at most n vertices, the threshold for $G(n,p)$ to contain an ordinary copy of H is at most $C p_{\\mathrm E}(n,H)(1+\\log_2 h)$, where C is universal. Here $p_{\\mathrm E}(n,H)$ is the least density at which every subgraph of H has expected copy count at least one half.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the second Kahn–Kalai conjecture: for every finite simple graph H with h ≥ 1 edges and at most n vertices, its appearance threshold in $G(n,p)$ is at most $C p_{\\mathrm E}(n,H)(1+\\log_2 h)$, with universal C. Here $p_{\\mathrm E}$ is the least density at which every subgraph of H has expected copy count at least 1/2.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-second-Kahn-Kalai-conjecture-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-second-Kahn-Kalai-conjecture-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-second-Kahn-Kalai-conjecture-September-24-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/The-second-Kahn-Kalai-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/The-second-Kahn-Kalai-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-second-Kahn-Kalai-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/resampling.tex","preprints/The-second-Kahn-Kalai-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/trees.tex","preprints/The-second-Kahn-Kalai-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/source/figures/outside-label.tex","preprints/The-second-Kahn-Kalai-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/graphs.tex","preprints/The-second-Kahn-Kalai-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/source/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-second-Kahn-Kalai-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the second Kahn--Kalai conjecture. For every finite simple graph $H$ with $h\\ge1$ edges and at most $n$ vertices, the threshold for $G(n,p)$ to contain an ordinary copy of $H$ is at most $C p_{\\mathrm E}(n,H)(1+\\log_2 h)$, where $C$ is universal. Here $p_{\\mathrm E}(n,H)$ is the least density at which every subgraph of $H$ has expected copy count at least one half.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Graph thresholds and the main result","sourceOffset":423},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the obstruction","sourceOffset":2770},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and method ancestry","sourceOffset":7718},{"kind":"section","title":"Resampling a spread family","sourceOffset":10254},{"kind":"section","title":"Covering a tree","sourceOffset":15796},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One simultaneous reduction","sourceOffset":17866},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Iteration","sourceOffset":25131},{"kind":"section","title":"Application to graph containment","sourceOffset":28262}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $n\\ge2$ and every finite simple graph $H$ with $h=e(H)\\ge1$ edges and at most $n$ vertices, \\begin{equation} \\pc(n,H)\\le \\min\\{1,\\;2048\\ee^{50}\\pE(n,H)(1+\\log_2 h)\\}. \\end{equation} In particular, \\[ \\pc(n,H)\\le6144\\ee^{50}\\pE(n,H)\\log_2 n. \\]","sourceOffset":1981,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Tree covering","label":"thm:tree","text":"Let $\\TT$ be a disjoint probability tree on a finite set $X$, with $k\\ge1$ levels. Suppose that every level is $\\sigma$-spread, where $\\sigma>0$, and that its positive integer capacities satisfy \\[ \\ell_{i+1}\\ge16\\ell_i\\qquad(1\\le i<k). \\] Set \\[ r=\\min\\{1,\\;16\\ee^{50}\\sigma(1+\\log_2\\ell_k)\\}. \\] Then \\[ \\PP_{W\\sim\\mu_r} \\bigl(\\UU(P)\\subseteq W\\text{ for some path }P\\text{ of }\\TT\\bigr) \\ge\\tfrac23. \\]","sourceOffset":17104,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/176.md","scopeText":"The second Kahn–Kalai conjecture compares the threshold for a random graph to contain a fixed graph with its expectation threshold. The formalized result proves the comparison up to a universal factor times $1+\\log_2|E(H)|$, and hence up to a universal factor times $\\log_2 n$, for every graph $H$ with at least one edge and at most $n$ vertices, $n\\ge2$. The bounds use the actual containment and expectation thresholds.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/176.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":37364,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-second-Kahn-Kalai-conjecture-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"177-01","slug":"Bounded-degree-coboundary-expanders-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026","familyId":"177","title":"Bounded-degree coboundary expanders in every dimension","abstract":"For every integer d ≥ 3, we construct arbitrarily large finite d-dimensional simplicial complexes with uniformly bounded vertex degrees and uniform 𝔽2 coboundary expansion in every degree below d. Together with the known graph and two-dimensional cases, this establishes the existence of bounded-degree 𝔽2 coboundary expanders in every positive dimension.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs arbitrarily large finite d-dimensional simplicial complexes, for every d ≥ 3, with uniformly bounded vertex degrees and uniform 𝔽2 coboundary expansion in every degree below d. Together with the known graph and two-dimensional cases, this establishes the existence of such expanders in every positive dimension.","pdfPath":"preprints/Bounded-degree-coboundary-expanders-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Bounded-degree-coboundary-expanders-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Bounded-degree-coboundary-expanders-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/Bounded-degree-coboundary-expanders-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Bounded-degree-coboundary-expanders-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Bounded-degree-coboundary-expanders-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/construction.tex","preprints/Bounded-degree-coboundary-expanders-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Bounded-degree-coboundary-expanders-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/source/figures/attachments.tex","preprints/Bounded-degree-coboundary-expanders-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/homology.tex","preprints/Bounded-degree-coboundary-expanders-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/expansion.tex","preprints/Bounded-degree-coboundary-expanders-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/source/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Bounded-degree-coboundary-expanders-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every integer $d\\ge3$, we construct arbitrarily large finite $d$-dimensional simplicial complexes with uniformly bounded vertex degrees and uniform $\\mathbb F_2$ coboundary expansion in every degree below $d$. Together with the known graph and two-dimensional cases, this establishes the existence of bounded-degree $\\mathbb F_2$ coboundary expanders in every positive dimension.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":429},{"kind":"section","title":"The congruence complexes","sourceOffset":9586},{"kind":"section","title":"The infinite coset complex","sourceOffset":10941},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite local groups","sourceOffset":11424},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The twin-building model","sourceOffset":13538},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A filtration by codistance","sourceOffset":17238},{"kind":"section","title":"Homology and the finite quotients","sourceOffset":24223},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An acyclic complex as a partial resolution","sourceOffset":24670},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From pro-stability to ordinary surjectivity","sourceOffset":28043},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Trivial conjugation and vanishing cohomology","sourceOffset":33294},{"kind":"section","title":"Coboundary expansion","sourceOffset":35986},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The quantitative input and its hypotheses","sourceOffset":36272}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $d\\ge3$, there exist constants $D<\\infty$, $\\eps>0$, and finite connected pure $d$-dimensional simplicial complexes $X_m$, with $|X_m(0)|\\longrightarrow\\infty$, such that every vertex belongs to at most $D$ faces of dimension $d$ and, for every $f\\in C^i(X_m;\\F)$, \\begin{equation} \\norm{\\delta_i f}_{X_m,i+1} \\ge \\eps\\,\\dist_{X_m,i}(f,B^i(X_m)) \\qquad(0\\le i<d). \\end{equation} The constants are independent of $m$, $i$, and $f$.","sourceOffset":2073,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:L-acyclic","text":"The complex $L$ satisfies \\eqref{eq:L-acyclic}.","sourceOffset":18348,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/177.md","scopeText":"The formalization constructs bounded-degree $\\mathbb F_2$ coboundary expanders in every dimension $d\\ge3$. For each such $d$, it gives finite pure connected $d$-dimensional simplicial complexes with vertex counts tending to infinity, one uniform bound on top-dimensional degree at each vertex, and one positive coboundary-expansion constant in every degree below $d$. The constants may depend on $d$. The graph and two-dimensional cases used for the paper's all-positive-dimensions conclusion are separate.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/177.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":49539,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Bounded-degree-coboundary-expanders-in-every-dimension-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"178-01","slug":"Deterministic-nonbipartite-Ramanujan-graphs-in-every-fixed-degree-September-23-2026","familyId":"178","title":"Deterministic nonbipartite Ramanujan graphs in every fixed degree","abstract":"For every fixed integer d ≥ 3, we give a deterministic algorithm that constructs a simple nonbipartite d-regular Ramanujan graph on every sufficiently large even number n of vertices. It outputs the full adjacency list in polynomially many bit operations, with an exponent that may depend on d. Every nonconstant adjacency eigenvalue lies strictly between $-2\\sqrt{d-1}$ and $2\\sqrt{d-1}$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every fixed d ≥ 3, constructs a simple d-regular nonbipartite Ramanujan graph on every sufficiently large even number n of vertices, with every nonconstant adjacency eigenvalue strictly between $-2\\sqrt{d-1}$ and $2\\sqrt{d-1}$. A deterministic algorithm outputs the full adjacency list in polynomial bit time, with exponent depending on d.","pdfPath":"preprints/Deterministic-nonbipartite-Ramanujan-graphs-in-every-fixed-degree-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Deterministic-nonbipartite-Ramanujan-graphs-in-every-fixed-degree-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Deterministic-nonbipartite-Ramanujan-graphs-in-every-fixed-degree-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/Deterministic-nonbipartite-Ramanujan-graphs-in-every-fixed-degree-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Deterministic-nonbipartite-Ramanujan-graphs-in-every-fixed-degree-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Deterministic-nonbipartite-Ramanujan-graphs-in-every-fixed-degree-September-23-2026/build/figures/construction.tex","preprints/Deterministic-nonbipartite-Ramanujan-graphs-in-every-fixed-degree-September-23-2026/build/sections/setup.tex","preprints/Deterministic-nonbipartite-Ramanujan-graphs-in-every-fixed-degree-September-23-2026/build/sections/accounts.tex","preprints/Deterministic-nonbipartite-Ramanujan-graphs-in-every-fixed-degree-September-23-2026/build/sections/foundations.tex","preprints/Deterministic-nonbipartite-Ramanujan-graphs-in-every-fixed-degree-September-23-2026/build/sections/early.tex","preprints/Deterministic-nonbipartite-Ramanujan-graphs-in-every-fixed-degree-September-23-2026/build/sections/cleanup.tex","preprints/Deterministic-nonbipartite-Ramanujan-graphs-in-every-fixed-degree-September-23-2026/build/sections/boundary.tex","preprints/Deterministic-nonbipartite-Ramanujan-graphs-in-every-fixed-degree-September-23-2026/build/sections/repair.tex","preprints/Deterministic-nonbipartite-Ramanujan-graphs-in-every-fixed-degree-September-23-2026/build/figures/switch.tex","preprints/Deterministic-nonbipartite-Ramanujan-graphs-in-every-fixed-degree-September-23-2026/build/sections/algorithm.tex","preprints/Deterministic-nonbipartite-Ramanujan-graphs-in-every-fixed-degree-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Deterministic-nonbipartite-Ramanujan-graphs-in-every-fixed-degree-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed integer $d\\ge3$, we give a deterministic algorithm that constructs a simple nonbipartite $d$-regular Ramanujan graph on every sufficiently large even number $n$ of vertices. It outputs the full adjacency list in polynomially many bit operations, with an exponent that may depend on $d$. Every nonconstant adjacency eigenvalue lies strictly between $-2\\sqrt{d-1}$ and $2\\sqrt{d-1}$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":511},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier constructions and the exact threshold","sourceOffset":2786},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the construction reaches both spectral edges","sourceOffset":7268},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The four construction stages","sourceOffset":10126},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and parameters","sourceOffset":17041},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Matrices used during the construction","sourceOffset":18069},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Stopping sizes and spectral parameters","sourceOffset":19760},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Order of the constant choices","sourceOffset":22069},{"kind":"section","title":"Simultaneous deterministic choice","sourceOffset":25395},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The induction and its accumulated error bounds","sourceOffset":30816},{"kind":"section","title":"Partial configurations and their deterministic estimates","sourceOffset":32371},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The configuration pencil and a single exposure","sourceOffset":33361}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $d\\ge3$ there are constants $n_0(d)$ and $k_d$ and a deterministic algorithm with the following property. On input an even integer $n\\ge n_0(d)$, the algorithm outputs the adjacency list of a simple $d$-regular graph $G$ on $n$ vertices in $O_d(n^{k_d})$ bit operations, and \\[ -2\\sqrt{d-1}<\\lambda<2\\sqrt{d-1} \\quad\\text{for every eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of }A_G|_{\\mathbf1^\\perp}. \\] In particular, $G$ is connected and nonbipartite.","sourceOffset":1690,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:target-size","text":"For every fixed $d\\ge3$, a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm, on every sufficiently large integer input $N$, produces a simple connected nonbipartite $d$-regular Ramanujan graph $G$ with $N\\le |V(G)|\\le N+1\\le2N$.","sourceOffset":2417,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":298004,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Deterministic-nonbipartite-Ramanujan-graphs-in-every-fixed-degree-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"179-01","slug":"The-circulant-Hadamard-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"179","title":"The circulant Hadamard conjecture","abstract":"We prove the circulant Hadamard conjecture: a real circulant Hadamard matrix has order 1 or 4. As a consequence, Barker sequences of length greater than one exist exactly at lengths 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 11, 13, proving the Barker-sequence conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that real circulant Hadamard matrices exist exactly in orders 1 and 4, resolving the circulant Hadamard conjecture. Together with classical Barker-sequence results, this shows that binary sequences whose nontrivial aperiodic autocorrelations have magnitude at most 1 exist at lengths n > 1 exactly when $n\\in\\{2,3,4,5,7,11,13\\}$.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-circulant-Hadamard-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-circulant-Hadamard-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-circulant-Hadamard-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/The-circulant-Hadamard-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-circulant-Hadamard-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/The-circulant-Hadamard-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-circulant-Hadamard-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/local.tex","preprints/The-circulant-Hadamard-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/reduction.tex","preprints/The-circulant-Hadamard-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/products.tex","preprints/The-circulant-Hadamard-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/contradiction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-circulant-Hadamard-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the circulant Hadamard conjecture: a real circulant Hadamard matrix has order $1$ or $4$. As a consequence, Barker sequences of length greater than one exist exactly at lengths $2,3,4,5,7,11,13$, proving the Barker-sequence conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":290},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and prior work","sourceOffset":2310},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Group-ring notation","sourceOffset":6146},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":7300},{"kind":"section","title":"The order restriction","sourceOffset":10730},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cyclotomic divisibility","sourceOffset":12008},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Descent at the prime two","sourceOffset":17249},{"kind":"section","title":"Alternating character products","sourceOffset":19960},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison of two character values","sourceOffset":21841},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From local units to roots of unity","sourceOffset":25092},{"kind":"section","title":"The binary coefficient identity","sourceOffset":32630},{"kind":"section","title":"Barker sequences","sourceOffset":40361}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For a positive integer \\(n\\), a real circulant Hadamard matrix of order \\(n\\) exists if and only if \\(n\\in\\{1,4\\}\\).","sourceOffset":1028,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Barker-sequence lengths","label":"cor:barker","text":"For an integer \\(n>1\\), a Barker sequence of length \\(n\\) exists if and only if \\[ n\\in\\{2,3,4,5,7,11,13\\}. \\]","sourceOffset":1999,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/179.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves the circulant Hadamard conjecture in exact form: a real circulant Hadamard matrix of positive order $n$ exists exactly when $n=1$ or $n=4$. Explicit witnesses are supplied for both orders, with no restriction on prime factors.\n\nIt also proves the even-length part of the Barker-sequence consequence. A positive even-length sign sequence whose nonzero aperiodic autocorrelations have absolute value at most one must have length $2$ or $4$. The paper's classification of odd Barker lengths is outside this selected additional statement.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/179.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":42633,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-circulant-Hadamard-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"180-01","slug":"Paired-states-and-Hamiltonian-cycles-in-cubic-bipartite-planar-graphs-September-24-2026","familyId":"180","title":"Paired states and Hamiltonian cycles in cubic bipartite planar graphs","abstract":"We prove Barnette's conjecture: every finite simple cubic bipartite planar 3-vertex-connected graph has a Hamiltonian cycle.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every finite simple cubic bipartite planar 3-vertex-connected graph has a Hamiltonian cycle, resolving Barnette's conjecture. Equivalently, every three-edge path in a finite simple cubic 3-vertex-connected bipartite Pfaffian graph lies in a Hamiltonian cycle.","pdfPath":"preprints/Paired-states-and-Hamiltonian-cycles-in-cubic-bipartite-planar-graphs-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Paired-states-and-Hamiltonian-cycles-in-cubic-bipartite-planar-graphs-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Paired-states-and-Hamiltonian-cycles-in-cubic-bipartite-planar-graphs-September-24-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/Paired-states-and-Hamiltonian-cycles-in-cubic-bipartite-planar-graphs-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Paired-states-and-Hamiltonian-cycles-in-cubic-bipartite-planar-graphs-September-24-2026/build/triangle-figure.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Paired-states-and-Hamiltonian-cycles-in-cubic-bipartite-planar-graphs-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Barnette's conjecture: every finite simple cubic bipartite planar \\(3\\)-vertex-connected graph has a Hamiltonian cycle.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":174},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The dual problem and the proof strategy","sourceOffset":2458},{"kind":"section","title":"Plane trees and the triangulation statement","sourceOffset":7319},{"kind":"section","title":"Two disjoint states","sourceOffset":10509},{"kind":"section","title":"The disk identity","sourceOffset":15045},{"kind":"section","title":"A weighted cancellation argument","sourceOffset":17362},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cancellation","sourceOffset":18834},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positive spoke circulations","sourceOffset":20262},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Nonvanishing","sourceOffset":21934},{"kind":"section","title":"Recovering the two induced trees","sourceOffset":27243},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A tree on the white faces","sourceOffset":28331},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Acyclicity and inducedness","sourceOffset":30306}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Every finite simple undirected graph that is cubic, bipartite, planar, and \\(3\\)-vertex-connected has a Hamiltonian cycle.","sourceOffset":1891,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:partition","text":"Let \\(T\\) be a sphere triangulation whose faces are colored black and white so that each edge has one incident face of each color. For any facial triangle \\(t_0\\) and any edge \\(e_0\\) of \\(t_0\\), the vertex set admits a partition into two sets, each inducing a tree, such that the endpoints of \\(e_0\\) are in one set and the third vertex of \\(t_0\\) is in the other.","sourceOffset":9017,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/180.md","scopeText":"Barnette's conjecture states that every finite simple cubic bipartite planar graph that is 3-vertex-connected has a Hamiltonian cycle. The formalization proves this statement for every such graph. The selected result is the existence of a cycle visiting every vertex exactly once.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/180.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":37382,"sourceFileCount":2,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Paired-states-and-Hamiltonian-cycles-in-cubic-bipartite-planar-graphs-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"181-01","slug":"A-linear-cycle-and-edge-decomposition-of-every-graph-September-24-2026","familyId":"181","title":"A linear cycle-and-edge decomposition of every graph","abstract":"We prove that every finite simple undirected graph on n vertices has an edge partition into at most $Cn$ simple cycles and single edges, for an absolute constant C. This resolves the Erdős–Gallai cycle decomposition conjecture positively.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the edges of every finite simple undirected graph on n vertices can be partitioned into at most $Cn$ simple cycles and single edges, for an absolute constant C. This resolves the Erdős–Gallai cycle-decomposition conjecture, bounding the number of pieces linearly even for dense graphs.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-linear-cycle-and-edge-decomposition-of-every-graph-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-linear-cycle-and-edge-decomposition-of-every-graph-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-linear-cycle-and-edge-decomposition-of-every-graph-September-24-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-linear-cycle-and-edge-decomposition-of-every-graph-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-linear-cycle-and-edge-decomposition-of-every-graph-September-24-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/A-linear-cycle-and-edge-decomposition-of-every-graph-September-24-2026/build/sections/routing.tex","preprints/A-linear-cycle-and-edge-decomposition-of-every-graph-September-24-2026/build/sections/residue.tex","preprints/A-linear-cycle-and-edge-decomposition-of-every-graph-September-24-2026/build/sections/splitting.tex","preprints/A-linear-cycle-and-edge-decomposition-of-every-graph-September-24-2026/build/sections/folding.tex","preprints/A-linear-cycle-and-edge-decomposition-of-every-graph-September-24-2026/build/figures/lifting.tex","preprints/A-linear-cycle-and-edge-decomposition-of-every-graph-September-24-2026/build/sections/induction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-linear-cycle-and-edge-decomposition-of-every-graph-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every finite simple undirected graph on \\(n\\) vertices has an edge partition into at most \\(Cn\\) simple cycles and single edges, for an absolute constant \\(C\\). This resolves the Erd\\H{o}s--Gallai cycle decomposition conjecture positively.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":300},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Outline of the proof","sourceOffset":6261},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and preliminary results","sourceOffset":10561},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Graphs, partitions and scales","sourceOffset":10633},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A path decomposition with controlled endpoints","sourceOffset":12666},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Disjoint representatives for hypergraphs","sourceOffset":14686},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Elementary probabilistic estimates","sourceOffset":15933},{"kind":"section","title":"Routing with team constraints","sourceOffset":16671},{"kind":"section","title":"Expansion in small reservoirs","sourceOffset":27088},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Splitting the expanding edges","sourceOffset":27851},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Expansion after vertex sampling","sourceOffset":29082},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Closing paths through three reservoirs","sourceOffset":37779}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is an absolute constant \\(C>0\\) such that, for every finite simple undirected graph \\(G\\) with \\(n\\) vertices, \\(E(G)\\) has a partition into at most \\(Cn\\) parts, each of which is the edge set of a cycle or consists of a single edge.","sourceOffset":681,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:eulerian-cycles","text":"Every finite simple undirected Eulerian graph on \\(n\\) vertices has an edge partition into at most \\(Cn\\) cycles, with \\(C\\) from \\Cref{thm:main}. For every fixed \\(\\delta,p>0\\), there exist \\(\\eps>0\\) and an integer \\(n_0\\ge1\\) such that every finite simple undirected Eulerian graph \\(G\\) on \\(n\\ge n_0\\) vertices has an edge partition into at most \\(\\Delta(G)/2+\\delta n\\) cycles, provided that every partition \\(V(G)=A\\mathbin{\\dot\\cup}B\\) with \\(|A|,|B|\\ge\\eps n\\) satisfies \\[ e_G(A,B)\\ge p|A||B|. \\] Here \\(\\Delta(G)\\) denotes the maximum degree, and \\(e_G(A,B)\\) is the number of edges joining \\(A\\) to \\(B\\).","sourceOffset":3535,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/181.md","scopeText":"The Erdős–Gallai cycle-decomposition conjecture asks for a linear bound on the number of cycles and single edges needed to partition a graph's edges. The formalized result gives one absolute constant $C>0$ such that every finite simple graph on $n$ vertices has an edge-disjoint decomposition into at most $Cn$ cycles or singleton edges. Edgeless and small graphs are included. The optimal value of $C$ is not determined.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/181.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":104769,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-linear-cycle-and-edge-decomposition-of-every-graph-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"182-01","slug":"A-power-saving-for-intersective-polynomial-differences-with-an-exponent-depending-only-on-the-degree-October-5-2026","familyId":"182","title":"A power saving for intersective polynomial differences with an exponent depending only on the degree","abstract":"An integer polynomial is intersective if it has a root modulo every positive integer. For each degree k ≥ 2, we prove that there is an exponent $c_k\\gt 0$ such that every set $A\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ whose differences avoid all nonzero values $h(1),h(2),\\ldots$, where h is an intersective polynomial of degree k with positive leading coefficient, satisfies $|A|=O_h(N^{1-c_k})$. The implied constant may depend on h, but the power-saving exponent depends only on its degree.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every fixed intersective integer polynomial h of degree k ≥ 2 with positive leading coefficient, proves that a subset of $\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ avoiding nonzero values $h(1),h(2),\\ldots$ as differences has size $O_h(N^{1-c_k})$, with $c_k\\gt 0$ depending only on degree. Here intersective means having a root modulo every modulus. For prime arguments, a power saving also holds when h has a unit root modulo every modulus, with exponent allowed to depend on h.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-power-saving-for-intersective-polynomial-differences-with-an-exponent-depending-only-on-the-degree-October-5-2026/power-saving-intersective-polynomial-differences.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-power-saving-for-intersective-polynomial-differences-with-an-exponent-depending-only-on-the-degree-October-5-2026/power-saving-intersective-polynomial-differences.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-power-saving-for-intersective-polynomial-differences-with-an-exponent-depending-only-on-the-degree-October-5-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-power-saving-for-intersective-polynomial-differences-with-an-exponent-depending-only-on-the-degree-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/main.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-intersective-polynomial-differences-with-an-exponent-depending-only-on-the-degree-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-intersective-polynomial-differences-with-an-exponent-depending-only-on-the-degree-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/sections/local.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-intersective-polynomial-differences-with-an-exponent-depending-only-on-the-degree-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/sections/tuples.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-intersective-polynomial-differences-with-an-exponent-depending-only-on-the-degree-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/figures/list-regrouping.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-intersective-polynomial-differences-with-an-exponent-depending-only-on-the-degree-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/sections/lifts.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-intersective-polynomial-differences-with-an-exponent-depending-only-on-the-degree-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/sections/kernel.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-intersective-polynomial-differences-with-an-exponent-depending-only-on-the-degree-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/sections/transfer.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-intersective-polynomial-differences-with-an-exponent-depending-only-on-the-degree-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/sections/induction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-power-saving-for-intersective-polynomial-differences-with-an-exponent-depending-only-on-the-degree-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"An integer polynomial is intersective if it has a root modulo every positive integer. For each degree $k\\ge2$, we prove that there is an exponent $c_k>0$ such that every set $A\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ whose differences avoid all nonzero values $h(1),h(2),\\ldots$, where $h$ is an intersective polynomial of degree $k$ with positive leading coefficient, satisfies $|A|=O_h(N^{1-c_k})$. The implied constant may depend on $h$, but the power-saving exponent depends only on its degree.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":547},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and relation to earlier work","sourceOffset":1930},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The argument and its principal constructions","sourceOffset":4626},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Notation and uniformity","sourceOffset":11342},{"kind":"section","title":"Auxiliary polynomials and local estimates","sourceOffset":12773},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A family compatible with progressions","sourceOffset":13720},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exponential sums uniformly over the family","sourceOffset":22800},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Admissible residues and uniform lifting","sourceOffset":27872},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Admissible cycles of a fixed length","sourceOffset":32987},{"kind":"section","title":"Weighted reflection-positive tuple laws","sourceOffset":35004},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Slots, reflections, and mixing of edge weights","sourceOffset":36187},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Measures indexed by marked blocks","sourceOffset":41733}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $k\\ge2$, there is a constant $c_k\\in(0,1)$ with the following property. For every intersective polynomial $h\\in\\Z[x]$ of degree $k$ with positive leading coefficient, there are a constant $C_h\\ge1$ and an integer $N_h\\ge1$ such that, whenever $N\\ge N_h$ and $A\\subseteq[N]$ satisfy \\[ (A-A)\\cap h(\\N_+)\\subseteq\\{0\\}, \\] one has \\[ |A|\\le C_h N^{1-c_k}. \\]","sourceOffset":1244,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Reflection-positive laws with polynomial cycle support","label":"tuple:laws","text":"For all sufficiently large primes $p$, uniformly in $l\\ge1$, define \\begin{align*} M_{l,p}&=\\sum_{\\substack{S\\subseteq[w]\\\\|S|\\le t+1}} p^{-|S|\\delta/2}\\nu_{l,p,S},\\\\ \\mu_{l,p}&=\\frac{M_{l,p}}{M_{l,p}(1)},\\\\ \\eps_{l,p}&=\\frac{1}{M_{l,p}(1)} \\sum_{|S|=t+1}p^{-(t+1)\\delta/2}\\nu_{l,p,S}. \\end{align*} Then $\\mu_{l,p}$ is a reflection-positive probability law and $0\\le\\eps_{l,p}\\le\\mu_{l,p}$. Its exceptional mass satisfies \\[ \\eta_{l,p}:=\\eps_{l,p}(1)\\le p^{-4}. \\] The probability law \\[ \\mu_{l,p}^{\\circ} =\\frac{\\mu_{l,p}-\\eps_{l,p}}{1-\\eta_{l,p}} \\] has only admissible increments $z_{v_{j+1}}-z_{v_j}$ along the designated cycle. Every component and both measures $\\mu_{l,p}$, $\\eps_{l,p}$ are invariant under simultaneous translation of all labels. Every positive-mass component or subsum therefore has uniform single-slot marginals after normalization. In either $\\mu_{l,p}$ or $\\mu_{l,p}^{\\circ}$, the total probability of components with $S\\ne\\varnothing$ is $O(p^{-\\delta/2})$.","sourceOffset":54444,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/182.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":149024,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-power-saving-for-intersective-polynomial-differences-with-an-exponent-depending-only-on-the-degree-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-power-saving-for-intersective-polynomial-differences-with-an-exponent-depending-only-on-the-degree-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"182-02","slug":"A-Power-Saving-for-Polynomial-Differences-at-Prime-Arguments-October-5-2026","familyId":"182","title":"A Power Saving for Polynomial Differences at Prime Arguments","abstract":"Let h be a fixed integer polynomial of degree at least two with positive leading coefficient, having a unit root modulo every positive integer. We prove that any set $A\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ whose differences avoid all nonzero values $h(p)$ at primes satisfies $|A|\\le C_hN^{1-c_h}$, where $c_h\\gt 0$ and $C_h\\ge1$ depend only on h. Thus the local unit-root condition gives a fixed power saving even when polynomial arguments are restricted to primes. The proof uses the companion zero-free half-plane theorem for Dirichlet L-functions to obtain the required prime-distribution estimates.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every fixed intersective integer polynomial h of degree k ≥ 2 with positive leading coefficient, proves that a subset of $\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ avoiding nonzero values $h(1),h(2),\\ldots$ as differences has size $O_h(N^{1-c_k})$, with $c_k\\gt 0$ depending only on degree. Here intersective means having a root modulo every modulus. For prime arguments, a power saving also holds when h has a unit root modulo every modulus, with exponent allowed to depend on h.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Power-Saving-for-Polynomial-Differences-at-Prime-Arguments-October-5-2026/prime-argument-polynomial-differences.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Power-Saving-for-Polynomial-Differences-at-Prime-Arguments-October-5-2026/prime-argument-polynomial-differences.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Power-Saving-for-Polynomial-Differences-at-Prime-Arguments-October-5-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-Power-Saving-for-Polynomial-Differences-at-Prime-Arguments-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-Power-Saving-for-Polynomial-Differences-at-Prime-Arguments-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-Power-Saving-for-Polynomial-Differences-at-Prime-Arguments-October-5-2026/build/sections/local.tex","preprints/A-Power-Saving-for-Polynomial-Differences-at-Prime-Arguments-October-5-2026/build/sections/tuples.tex","preprints/A-Power-Saving-for-Polynomial-Differences-at-Prime-Arguments-October-5-2026/build/figures/list-concatenation.tex","preprints/A-Power-Saving-for-Polynomial-Differences-at-Prime-Arguments-October-5-2026/build/sections/lifts.tex","preprints/A-Power-Saving-for-Polynomial-Differences-at-Prime-Arguments-October-5-2026/build/sections/primes.tex","preprints/A-Power-Saving-for-Polynomial-Differences-at-Prime-Arguments-October-5-2026/build/sections/kernel.tex","preprints/A-Power-Saving-for-Polynomial-Differences-at-Prime-Arguments-October-5-2026/build/sections/descent.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Power-Saving-for-Polynomial-Differences-at-Prime-Arguments-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let $h$ be a fixed integer polynomial of degree at least two with positive leading coefficient, having a unit root modulo every positive integer. We prove that any set $A\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ whose differences avoid all nonzero values $h(p)$ at primes satisfies $|A|\\le C_hN^{1-c_h}$, where $c_h>0$ and $C_h\\ge1$ depend only on $h$. Thus the local unit-root condition gives a fixed power saving even when polynomial arguments are restricted to primes. The proof uses the companion zero-free half-plane theorem for Dirichlet $L$-functions to obtain the required prime-distribution estimates.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":639},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and main ideas","sourceOffset":1980},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Notation and organization","sourceOffset":7648},{"kind":"section","title":"Auxiliary polynomials and local admissibility","sourceOffset":9086},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A family compatible with progression restriction","sourceOffset":9671},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform cancellation at large primes","sourceOffset":13944},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Small primes and uniform lifting","sourceOffset":18792},{"kind":"section","title":"Reflection-positive laws on residue tuples","sourceOffset":24652},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Slots, reflections, and the required properties","sourceOffset":25338},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two estimates for weighted graphs","sourceOffset":29188},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Components obtained by forgetting blocks","sourceOffset":32241},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positive contributions and their comparison","sourceOffset":35112}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $h\\in\\Z[x]$ be prime-intersective, of degree at least two and with positive leading coefficient. There exist constants $c_h>0$ and $C_h\\ge1$ such that, for every positive integer $N$ and every $A\\subseteq[N]$, \\[ (A-A)\\cap\\{h(p):p\\in\\mathbb P\\}\\subseteq\\{0\\} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad |A|\\le C_h N^{1-c_h}. \\]","sourceOffset":1382,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Tuple laws","label":"prop:tuple-laws","text":"There is a threshold $P>P_1$, depending only on $h$, with the following property. For every $l\\ge1$ and prime $p\\ge P$, there are a probability measure $\\mu_{l,p}$ on $\\F_p^V$ and a submeasure $0\\le\\varepsilon_{l,p}\\le\\mu_{l,p}$ such that: \\begin{enumerate} \\item $\\mu_{l,p}$ is reflection positive. Both $\\mu_{l,p}$ and $\\varepsilon_{l,p}$ are invariant under simultaneous translation of all labels. In particular, any nonzero one of these measures, or their difference, has uniform single-slot marginals after normalization. \\item Writing \\[ \\eta_{l,p}=\\varepsilon_{l,p}(1),\\qquad \\mu_{l,p}^{\\circ}=\\frac{\\mu_{l,p}-\\varepsilon_{l,p}}{1-\\eta_{l,p}}, \\] we have $\\eta_{l,p}\\le p^{-4}$. Under $\\mu_{l,p}^{\\circ}$ every increment $z_{v_{j+1}}-z_{v_j}$, $0\\le j<L$, is admissible at $l$. \\item For any integer $D\\ge1$ with $p\\nmid D$ and any $a'\\in\\F_p$, let $A_{D,a'}$ act on all labels by $z_v\\mapsto\\lambda(D)^{-1}(z_v-a')$. Then \\begin{equation} (A_{D,a'})_*\\mu_{l,p}=\\mu_{lD,p},\\qquad (A_{D,a'})_*\\varepsilon_{l,p}=\\varepsilon_{lD,p}. \\end{equation} \\item For $\\rho_p=\\mu_{l,p}$ or $\\mu_{l,p}^{\\circ}$, every $v\\in V$, and every $f:\\F_p\\to\\C$ of uniform mean zero, \\begin{equation} \\left\\|\\mathbb E_{\\rho_p} \\bigl[f(z_v)\\mid(z_u)_{u\\ne v}\\bigr]\\right\\|_{L^2(\\rho_p)} \\le p^{-\\gamma}\\|f\\|_{L^2(\\F_p)}. \\end{equation} \\end{enumerate} All thresholds and constants are independent of $l$.","sourceOffset":27008,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/182.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":136318,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Power-Saving-for-Polynomial-Differences-at-Prime-Arguments-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"182-03","slug":"A-power-saving-for-square-difference-free-sets-September-24-2026","familyId":"182","title":"A power saving for square-difference-free sets","abstract":"We prove that there are absolute constants c > 0 and C < ∞ such that every set $A\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ with no nonzero square difference satisfies $|A|\\le C N^{1-c}$. This answers the fixed-power question posed by Green and Sawhney.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every fixed intersective integer polynomial h of degree k ≥ 2 with positive leading coefficient, proves that a subset of $\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ avoiding nonzero values $h(1),h(2),\\ldots$ as differences has size $O_h(N^{1-c_k})$, with $c_k\\gt 0$ depending only on degree. Here intersective means having a root modulo every modulus. For prime arguments, a power saving also holds when h has a unit root modulo every modulus, with exponent allowed to depend on h.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-power-saving-for-square-difference-free-sets-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-power-saving-for-square-difference-free-sets-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-power-saving-for-square-difference-free-sets-September-24-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-power-saving-for-square-difference-free-sets-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-square-difference-free-sets-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-square-difference-free-sets-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-map.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-square-difference-free-sets-September-24-2026/build/sections/graph.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-square-difference-free-sets-September-24-2026/build/sections/tuples.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-square-difference-free-sets-September-24-2026/build/figures/list-regrouping.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-square-difference-free-sets-September-24-2026/build/sections/functional.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-square-difference-free-sets-September-24-2026/build/sections/lifts.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-square-difference-free-sets-September-24-2026/build/sections/kernel.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-square-difference-free-sets-September-24-2026/build/sections/transfer.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-square-difference-free-sets-September-24-2026/build/sections/contraction.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-square-difference-free-sets-September-24-2026/build/sections/induction.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-square-difference-free-sets-September-24-2026/build/figures/parameter-roles.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-power-saving-for-square-difference-free-sets-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that there are absolute constants $c>0$ and $C<\\infty$ such that every set $A\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ with no nonzero square difference satisfies $|A|\\le C N^{1-c}$. This answers the fixed-power question posed by Green and Sawhney.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":303},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Previous work","sourceOffset":2146},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Outline of the proof","sourceOffset":6208},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":14004},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite-field square constraints","sourceOffset":14993},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite reflection-positive probability laws","sourceOffset":20487},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The index set and the required properties","sourceOffset":21031},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Measures with marked blocks","sourceOffset":24498},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Strict positivity at a marked cut","sourceOffset":30488},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The error at an unmarked cut","sourceOffset":35470},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Domination and the reflection-positive mixture","sourceOffset":37707},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Normalization, exceptional mass, and cycle support","sourceOffset":43017}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are absolute constants $c>0$ and $C<\\infty$ such that, for every integer $N\\ge1$ and every square-difference-free set $A\\subseteq[N]$, \\[ |A|\\le C N^{1-c}. \\] Equivalently, every subset of $[N]$ larger than $C N^{1-c}$ contains distinct elements whose absolute difference is the square of a positive integer.","sourceOffset":723,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Tuple laws","label":"prop:tuple-laws","text":"There is an absolute integer \\(P\\) such that, for every prime \\(p\\ge P\\), there are a probability measure \\(\\mu_p\\) on \\(\\mathbb F_p^V\\) and a nonnegative submeasure \\(\\varepsilon_p\\le\\mu_p\\) whose mass satisfies \\[ \\eta_p=\\varepsilon_p(\\mathbb F_p^V)\\le p^{-4}. \\] Writing \\[ \\mu_p^\\circ=\\frac{\\mu_p-\\varepsilon_p}{1-\\eta_p}, \\] the following properties hold. \\begin{enumerate} \\item The full law \\(\\mu_p\\) is reflection positive. Both \\(\\mu_p\\) and \\(\\varepsilon_p\\) are invariant under simultaneous changes \\(z_v\\mapsto qz_v+a\\), where \\(a\\in\\mathbb F_p\\) and \\(q\\in Q_p^*\\). Every single-label marginal of \\(\\mu_p\\) and \\(\\mu_p^\\circ\\) is uniform on \\(\\mathbb F_p\\). \\item On the support of \\(\\mu_p^\\circ\\), \\[ z_{v_{j+1}}-z_{v_j}\\in Q_p^*\\qquad(0\\le j<h). \\] The marginal of each ordered pair \\((z_{v_j},z_{v_{j+1}})\\) under \\(\\mu_p^\\circ\\) is uniform on \\(\\{(x,y):y-x\\in Q_p^*\\}\\). \\item Put \\(\\gamma=1/64\\). For either \\(\\rho_p=\\mu_p\\) or \\(\\rho_p=\\mu_p^\\circ\\), every \\(v\\in V\\), and every \\(f:\\mathbb F_p\\to\\mathbb C\\) with uniform mean zero, \\begin{equation} \\left\\|\\mathbb E_{\\rho_p} \\bigl[f(z_v)\\mid(z_w)_{w\\ne v}\\bigr]\\right\\|_2 \\le p^{-\\gamma}\\|f\\|_2. \\end{equation} The norm on the left uses the other-label marginal of \\(\\rho_p\\); the norm on the right uses uniform measure on \\(\\mathbb F_p\\). \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":22691,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/182.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves a fixed power saving for sets with no nonzero square difference. There are absolute constants $c>0$ and $C$ such that every $A\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ satisfying $a-b\\ne m^2$ for all $a,b\\in A$ and integers $m\\ge1$ has $|A|\\le C N^{1-c}$. The bound is uniform for every integer $N\\ge1$.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/182.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":151316,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-power-saving-for-square-difference-free-sets-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"183-01","slug":"A-power-saving-for-planar-halving-lines-September-25-2026","familyId":"183","title":"A power saving for planar halving lines","abstract":"There are absolute constants ε > 0 and C such that every sufficiently large even n-point set in the plane with no three collinear has at most $Cn^{4/3-\\varepsilon}$ unordered halving pairs. This gives a power saving over the classical $O(n^{4/3})$ bound for planar halving lines. The proof is nonquantitative and does not supply explicit constants.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Improves the planar halving-line bound to $O(n^{4/3-\\varepsilon})$ for sets with no three collinear and an absolute ε > 0. More generally, an n-point set with no three collinear has $O(n(k+1)^{1/3-\\varepsilon_0})$ strictly separable k-subsets for $1\\le k\\le n/2$, with an absolute $\\varepsilon_0\\gt 0$. The constants and positive exponents are nonquantitative.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-halving-lines-September-25-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-halving-lines-September-25-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-halving-lines-September-25-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-halving-lines-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-halving-lines-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-halving-lines-September-25-2026/build/sections/finite.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-halving-lines-September-25-2026/build/sections/rank-limits.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-halving-lines-September-25-2026/build/sections/differentiability.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-halving-lines-September-25-2026/build/sections/zoom.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-halving-lines-September-25-2026/build/sections/spatial.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-halving-lines-September-25-2026/build/sections/boundary.tex","preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-halving-lines-September-25-2026/build/sections/recurrence.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-power-saving-for-planar-halving-lines-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"There are absolute constants $\\varepsilon>0$ and $C$ such that every sufficiently large even $n$-point set in the plane with no three collinear has at most $Cn^{4/3-\\varepsilon}$ unordered halving pairs. This gives a power saving over the classical $O(n^{4/3})$ bound for planar halving lines. The proof is nonquantitative and does not supply explicit constants.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":436},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The halving-line problem and earlier bounds","sourceOffset":1352},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rank sweeps and the stronger conclusion","sourceOffset":3445},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":6489},{"kind":"section","title":"Sweeps, activity, and exchange graphs","sourceOffset":10776},{"kind":"section","title":"Rank coordinates and limiting measures","sourceOffset":23629},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A pair of rank coordinates parametrizes the plane","sourceOffset":26015},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Crossing density and variation","sourceOffset":29557},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The limiting measures","sourceOffset":31823},{"kind":"section","title":"Differentiable coordinates and transverse directions","sourceOffset":40379},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Calculus for the limiting coordinates","sourceOffset":41460},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A positive part with independent gradients","sourceOffset":45999}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:halving-power","text":"There exist absolute constants $\\varepsilon>0$, $C<\\infty$, and $n_0$ such that, for every even $n\\ge n_0$ and every $n$-point set $P\\subset\\mathbb R^2$ with no three collinear, \\[ h(P)\\le C n^{4/3-\\varepsilon}. \\]","sourceOffset":880,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform bound over all ranks","label":"thm:all-ranks","text":"There are absolute constants $\\varepsilon>0$ and $C<\\infty$ such that for every integer $N\\ge1$, every generic $N$-point planar configuration $P$, and every integer $0\\le k\\le N$, \\[ S_k(P)\\le C N^{4/3-\\varepsilon}. \\]","sourceOffset":4392,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/183.md","scopeText":"A halving pair in an even planar point set is a pair whose line leaves equally many remaining points on each side. The formalization proves that some absolute $\\varepsilon>0$ and $C$ bound the number of halving pairs by $Cn^{4/3-\\varepsilon}$ for every sufficiently large even $n$ and every $n$-point set with no three collinear. It also proves a bound of the same form for all level-switch counts under the additional generic-position assumptions in the statement. The constants are existential.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/183.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":102397,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-power-saving-for-planar-halving-lines-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"184-01","slug":"Correspondence-Coloring-Graphs-with-a-Forbidden-Clique-October-5-2026","familyId":"184","title":"Correspondence coloring graphs with a forbidden clique","abstract":"For every fixed integer r ≥ 4, we prove that every Kr-free graph of sufficiently large maximum degree Δ has correspondence chromatic number $O_r(\\Delta/\\log\\Delta)$. This resolves the Alon–Krivelevich–Sudakov coloring conjecture in the stronger correspondence-coloring form. The same bound, with a constant depending on F, holds when any fixed graph F is excluded as an ordinary subgraph. Ordinary and list coloring satisfy the same bounds.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the Alon–Krivelevich–Sudakov coloring conjecture in correspondence-coloring form: graphs avoiding any fixed subgraph F need $O_F(\\Delta/\\log\\Delta)$ colors when their maximum degree Δ is sufficiently large. Also proves the Ajtai–Erdős–Komlós–Szemerédi independence conjecture: for fixed r ≥ 4, every n-vertex Kr-free graph of average degree d ≥ 2 has an independent set of size $\\Omega_r(n\\log d/d)$.","pdfPath":"preprints/Correspondence-Coloring-Graphs-with-a-Forbidden-Clique-October-5-2026/correspondence-coloring-forbidden-clique.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Correspondence-Coloring-Graphs-with-a-Forbidden-Clique-October-5-2026/correspondence-coloring-forbidden-clique.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Correspondence-Coloring-Graphs-with-a-Forbidden-Clique-October-5-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/Correspondence-Coloring-Graphs-with-a-Forbidden-Clique-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Correspondence-Coloring-Graphs-with-a-Forbidden-Clique-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Correspondence-Coloring-Graphs-with-a-Forbidden-Clique-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Correspondence-Coloring-Graphs-with-a-Forbidden-Clique-October-5-2026/build/sections/history.tex","preprints/Correspondence-Coloring-Graphs-with-a-Forbidden-Clique-October-5-2026/build/sections/adjustment.tex","preprints/Correspondence-Coloring-Graphs-with-a-Forbidden-Clique-October-5-2026/build/sections/triangles.tex","preprints/Correspondence-Coloring-Graphs-with-a-Forbidden-Clique-October-5-2026/build/figures/vertex-splitting.tex","preprints/Correspondence-Coloring-Graphs-with-a-Forbidden-Clique-October-5-2026/build/sections/rounds.tex","preprints/Correspondence-Coloring-Graphs-with-a-Forbidden-Clique-October-5-2026/build/sections/completion.tex","preprints/Correspondence-Coloring-Graphs-with-a-Forbidden-Clique-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Correspondence-Coloring-Graphs-with-a-Forbidden-Clique-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed integer $r\\ge4$, we prove that every $K_r$-free graph of sufficiently large maximum degree $\\Delta$ has correspondence chromatic number $O_r(\\Delta/\\log\\Delta)$. This resolves the Alon--Krivelevich--Sudakov coloring conjecture in the stronger correspondence-coloring form. The same bound, with a constant depending on $F$, holds when any fixed graph $F$ is excluded as an ordinary subgraph. Ordinary and list coloring satisfy the same bounds.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":532},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":3328},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its main ingredients","sourceOffset":8846},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":14902},{"kind":"section","title":"Local weight adjustment with bounded participation","sourceOffset":15494},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Dual prices and dyadic levels","sourceOffset":20006},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sparse multipliers and local edge mass","sourceOffset":26990},{"kind":"section","title":"Controlling triangles by splitting vertices","sourceOffset":36139},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removing triangles of small total mass","sourceOffset":37428},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Entropy bounds for walks in neighborhoods","sourceOffset":42253},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coupled labels and the split graph","sourceOffset":48711},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Iteration and completion of the adjustment lemma","sourceOffset":57290}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $r\\ge4$ there are constants $C_r>0$ and $\\Delta_r\\ge3$ such that every $K_r$-free graph $G$ of maximum degree $\\Delta\\ge\\Delta_r$ satisfies \\[ \\chi_{\\mathrm{DP}}(G) \\le \\left\\lceil C_r\\frac{\\Delta}{\\log\\Delta}\\right\\rceil. \\] The constants depend only on $r$.","sourceOffset":1682,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:forbidden","text":"For every finite graph $F$ there are constants $C_F>0$ and $\\Delta_F\\ge3$ such that every $F$-free graph $G$ of maximum degree $\\Delta\\ge\\Delta_F$ satisfies \\[ \\chi_{\\mathrm{DP}}(G) \\le \\left\\lceil C_F\\frac{\\Delta}{\\log\\Delta}\\right\\rceil. \\]","sourceOffset":2367,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/184.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":97502,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Correspondence-Coloring-Graphs-with-a-Forbidden-Clique-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"184-02","slug":"A-Logarithmic-Independence-Bound-for-Clique-Free-Graphs-September-25-2026","familyId":"184","title":"A logarithmic independence bound for clique-free graphs","abstract":"For every fixed integer r ≥ 4, every Kr-free graph on n vertices with average degree d ≥ 2 has an independent set of size at least $c_r n\\log d/d$, where $c_r\\gt 0$ depends only on r. This proves the fixed-clique-size independence conjecture of Ajtai, Erdős, Komlós and Szemerédi.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the Alon–Krivelevich–Sudakov coloring conjecture in correspondence-coloring form: graphs avoiding any fixed subgraph F need $O_F(\\Delta/\\log\\Delta)$ colors when their maximum degree Δ is sufficiently large. Also proves the Ajtai–Erdős–Komlós–Szemerédi independence conjecture: for fixed r ≥ 4, every n-vertex Kr-free graph of average degree d ≥ 2 has an independent set of size $\\Omega_r(n\\log d/d)$.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Logarithmic-Independence-Bound-for-Clique-Free-Graphs-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Logarithmic-Independence-Bound-for-Clique-Free-Graphs-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Logarithmic-Independence-Bound-for-Clique-Free-Graphs-September-25-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-Logarithmic-Independence-Bound-for-Clique-Free-Graphs-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-Logarithmic-Independence-Bound-for-Clique-Free-Graphs-September-25-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/A-Logarithmic-Independence-Bound-for-Clique-Free-Graphs-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-Logarithmic-Independence-Bound-for-Clique-Free-Graphs-September-25-2026/build/sections/optimizer.tex","preprints/A-Logarithmic-Independence-Bound-for-Clique-Free-Graphs-September-25-2026/build/sections/invariant.tex","preprints/A-Logarithmic-Independence-Bound-for-Clique-Free-Graphs-September-25-2026/build/sections/entropy.tex","preprints/A-Logarithmic-Independence-Bound-for-Clique-Free-Graphs-September-25-2026/build/sections/coupling.tex","preprints/A-Logarithmic-Independence-Bound-for-Clique-Free-Graphs-September-25-2026/build/figures/splitting.tex","preprints/A-Logarithmic-Independence-Bound-for-Clique-Free-Graphs-September-25-2026/build/sections/closure.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Logarithmic-Independence-Bound-for-Clique-Free-Graphs-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed integer $r\\ge4$, every $K_r$-free graph on $n$ vertices with average degree $d\\ge2$ has an independent set of size at least $c_r n\\log d/d$, where $c_r>0$ depends only on $r$. This proves the fixed-clique-size independence conjecture of Ajtai, Erd\\H{o}s, Koml\\'os and Szemer\\'edi.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":395},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and related results","sourceOffset":1789},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof in outline","sourceOffset":5261},{"kind":"section","title":"An extremal weight and its cross-mass bound","sourceOffset":9314},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The extremal weight","sourceOffset":10109},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Random multipliers with small expected edge mass","sourceOffset":12020},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The cross-mass estimate","sourceOffset":15462},{"kind":"section","title":"From cross mass to triangle mass","sourceOffset":19035},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Preservation and triangle counting","sourceOffset":20652},{"kind":"section","title":"Local walks and entropy","sourceOffset":25814},{"kind":"section","title":"Splitting vertices and bounding triangle mass","sourceOffset":36764},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sampling all rows together","sourceOffset":37233}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $r\\ge4$, there is a constant $c_r>0$ such that every finite simple $K_r$-free graph $G$ with $n$ vertices and average degree $d\\ge2$ satisfies \\[ \\alpha(G)\\ge c_r\\frac{n\\log d}{d}. \\]","sourceOffset":1071,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Triangle mass from cross mass","label":"thm:weighted-triangles","text":"For every $C\\ge1$ there is a constant $C_{\\triangle}(C)$ with the following property. If $G$ is a finite simple graph with positive vertex weights $w$ satisfying \\eqref{eq:cross-mass}, then \\[ T_G(w)\\le C_{\\triangle}(C)M_G(w). \\]","sourceOffset":19892,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/184.md","scopeText":"The formalized result gives a logarithmic improvement in the independence number of clique-free graphs. For every fixed integer $r\\ge4$, there is $c_r>0$ such that every finite $K_r$-free simple graph on $n$ vertices with average degree $d\\ge2$ has an independent set of size at least $c_r n\\log d/d$. The constant depends only on $r$.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/184.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":57171,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Logarithmic-Independence-Bound-for-Clique-Free-Graphs-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"185-01","slug":"A-Counterexample-to-the-Infinite-Matroid-Packing-Covering-Conjecture-September-24-2026","familyId":"185","title":"A Counterexample to the Infinite Matroid Packing/Covering Conjecture","abstract":"We construct in ZFC two self-dual partitional matroids on a countably infinite common ground set that admit neither a packing/covering partition nor an intersection witness. This disproves the unrestricted infinite matroid packing/covering and intersection conjectures and answers Joó's question for two partitional matroids negatively. The examples are neither finitary nor cofinitary.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves the unrestricted infinite matroid intersection and packing/covering conjectures in ZFC, using two self-dual partitional matroids on a countably infinite ground set. The same examples answer Joó’s partitional-matroid question negatively. They are neither finitary nor cofinitary, so Nash-Williams’ original finitary conjecture remains outside the result.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-the-Infinite-Matroid-Packing-Covering-Conjecture-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-the-Infinite-Matroid-Packing-Covering-Conjecture-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-the-Infinite-Matroid-Packing-Covering-Conjecture-September-24-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-Counterexample-to-the-Infinite-Matroid-Packing-Covering-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-the-Infinite-Matroid-Packing-Covering-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-the-Infinite-Matroid-Packing-Covering-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-matroids.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-the-Infinite-Matroid-Packing-Covering-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-filters.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-the-Infinite-Matroid-Packing-Covering-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-local-bases.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-the-Infinite-Matroid-Packing-Covering-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-assembly.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-the-Infinite-Matroid-Packing-Covering-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-counterexample.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-the-Infinite-Matroid-Packing-Covering-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/figures/double-ray.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-the-Infinite-Matroid-Packing-Covering-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-the-Infinite-Matroid-Packing-Covering-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct in ZFC two self-dual partitional matroids on a countably infinite common ground set that admit neither a packing/covering partition nor an intersection witness. This disproves the unrestricted infinite matroid packing/covering and intersection conjectures and answers Jo\\'o's question for two partitional matroids negatively. The examples are neither finitary nor cofinitary.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":491},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and the local construction","sourceOffset":2840},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":8434},{"kind":"section","title":"Matroids and the self-dual reduction","sourceOffset":11237},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Independence, closure, and duality","sourceOffset":11585},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From a mixed partition to an independent covering","sourceOffset":14584},{"kind":"section","title":"An ultrafilter rank and simultaneous reservations","sourceOffset":15894},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Blocks, columns, and two ideals","sourceOffset":16417},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The ultrafilter and its ordinal rank","sourceOffset":20298},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two simultaneous reservations","sourceOffset":24783},{"kind":"section","title":"Bases within a density-zero class","sourceOffset":27416},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Probe estimates and continuity","sourceOffset":29399}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"In ZFC, there exist a countably infinite set $E$ and two infinite matroids $M_0,M_1$ on $E$ such that $M_i^*=M_i$ on the same labelled ground set for $i=0,1$, and \\[ I_0\\cup I_1\\ne E \\quad\\text{for every independent }I_0\\text{ in }M_0 \\text{ and independent }I_1\\text{ in }M_1. \\] The pair $(M_0,M_1)$ has no packing/covering partition. Consequently, the infinite matroid packing/covering conjecture is false.","sourceOffset":1971,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:intersection","text":"The same pair $(M_0,M_1)$ constructed in Theorem \\ref{thm:main} consists of partitional matroids and does not satisfy intersection. More explicitly, for every set $J$ independent in both $M_0$ and $M_1$ and every partition $J=J_0\\dot\\cup J_1$, \\[ \\cl_{M_0}(J_0)\\cup\\cl_{M_1}(J_1)\\ne E. \\] Consequently, the unrestricted infinite Matroid Intersection Conjecture is false in ZFC. The pair also gives a negative ZFC answer to Jo\\'o's Question 1.5 for two partitional matroids on a common countable ground set.","sourceOffset":5757,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/185.md","scopeText":"The infinite matroid packing/covering conjecture predicts a partition of a common ground set into parts admitting the corresponding packing and covering. The formalization constructs two self-dual partitional matroids on one countably infinite ground set that admit neither an independent covering nor a packing/covering partition. The same pair has no intersection witness, so it also refutes the unrestricted infinite matroid intersection conjecture.\n\nSeparate formalized consequences give counterexamples to the individual covering and packing conjectures. The construction is in ordinary set theory with Choice and assumes no finitary restriction on the matroids.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/185.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":66266,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Counterexample-to-the-Infinite-Matroid-Packing-Covering-Conjecture-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"186-01","slug":"A-uniform-influence-bound-for-hypergraph-properties-October-5-2026","familyId":"186","title":"A uniform influence bound for hypergraph properties","abstract":"For every fixed integer r ≥ 3, we prove that every relabeling-invariant Boolean property of simple r-uniform hypergraphs on n vertices satisfies $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Var}}\\nolimits _p(f)\\le C_r I_p(f)/(\\log n)^{r/(r-1)}$. The constant depends only on r, and the bound holds uniformly for all $0\\lt p\\lt 1$ without a monotonicity assumption. For increasing properties, it gives the corresponding threshold-width bound with exponent $r/(r-1)$, proving the hypergraph threshold-width conjecture of Friedgut and Kalai.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the Friedgut–Kalai threshold-width conjectures for graphs and fixed-uniformity hypergraphs. For fixed $0\\lt \\varepsilon\\lt 1/2$, every nontrivial increasing relabeling-invariant property crosses from probability ε to $1-\\varepsilon$ within width $O((\\log n)^{-2})$ for graphs and $O_r((\\log n)^{-r/(r-1)})$ for r-uniform hypergraphs, r ≥ 3. The hypergraph influence bound also applies to nonmonotone properties.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-uniform-influence-bound-for-hypergraph-properties-October-5-2026/hypergraph-influences.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-uniform-influence-bound-for-hypergraph-properties-October-5-2026/hypergraph-influences.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-uniform-influence-bound-for-hypergraph-properties-October-5-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-uniform-influence-bound-for-hypergraph-properties-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-uniform-influence-bound-for-hypergraph-properties-October-5-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/A-uniform-influence-bound-for-hypergraph-properties-October-5-2026/build/fourier.tex","preprints/A-uniform-influence-bound-for-hypergraph-properties-October-5-2026/build/restriction.tex","preprints/A-uniform-influence-bound-for-hypergraph-properties-October-5-2026/build/completion.tex","preprints/A-uniform-influence-bound-for-hypergraph-properties-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-uniform-influence-bound-for-hypergraph-properties-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed integer $r\\ge3$, we prove that every relabeling-invariant Boolean property of simple $r$-uniform hypergraphs on $n$ vertices satisfies $\\Var_p(f)\\le C_r I_p(f)/(\\log n)^{r/(r-1)}$. The constant depends only on $r$, and the bound holds uniformly for all $0<p<1$ without a monotonicity assumption. For increasing properties, it gives the corresponding threshold-width bound with exponent $r/(r-1)$, proving the hypergraph threshold-width conjecture of Friedgut and Kalai.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":531},{"kind":"section","title":"A Fourier estimate for small individual influences","sourceOffset":6822},{"kind":"section","title":"Restriction to a vertex block","sourceOffset":11400},{"kind":"section","title":"The uniform bound and its threshold consequence","sourceOffset":16073}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $r\\ge3$ there is a finite constant $C_r$ such that, for every integer $n\\ge r$, every $0<p<1$, and every hypergraph property $f:\\{0,1\\}^{E_{n,r}}\\to\\{0,1\\}$, \\begin{equation} \\Var_p(f)\\le \\frac{C_r}{(\\log n)^{r/(r-1)}}I_p(f). \\end{equation} No monotonicity assumption is required.","sourceOffset":1508,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:threshold","text":"For a nontrivial increasing property of $r$-uniform hypergraphs on $n\\ge r$ vertices, where $r\\ge3$, and every $0<\\varepsilon<1/2$, \\[ p_{1-\\varepsilon}-p_\\varepsilon \\le\\frac{2C_r}{(\\log n)^{r/(r-1)}} \\log\\frac{1-\\varepsilon}{\\varepsilon}. \\]","sourceOffset":18522,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/186.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":21906,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-uniform-influence-bound-for-hypergraph-properties-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"186-02","slug":"A-Sharp-Threshold-Bound-for-Monotone-Graph-Properties-September-25-2026","familyId":"186","title":"A Sharp Threshold Bound for Monotone Graph Properties","abstract":"We prove the Friedgut–Kalai sharp-threshold conjecture. For every integer n ≥ 2, every nontrivial increasing family of graphs on n vertices invariant under all vertex permutations, and every $0\\lt \\varepsilon\\lt 1/2$, the edge probabilities at which its probability equals ε and $1-\\varepsilon$ differ by at most $C\\log(1/(2\\varepsilon))/(\\log n)^2$, for a universal constant C.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the Friedgut–Kalai threshold-width conjectures for graphs and fixed-uniformity hypergraphs. For fixed $0\\lt \\varepsilon\\lt 1/2$, every nontrivial increasing relabeling-invariant property crosses from probability ε to $1-\\varepsilon$ within width $O((\\log n)^{-2})$ for graphs and $O_r((\\log n)^{-r/(r-1)})$ for r-uniform hypergraphs, r ≥ 3. The hypergraph influence bound also applies to nonmonotone properties.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Sharp-Threshold-Bound-for-Monotone-Graph-Properties-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Sharp-Threshold-Bound-for-Monotone-Graph-Properties-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Sharp-Threshold-Bound-for-Monotone-Graph-Properties-September-25-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-Sharp-Threshold-Bound-for-Monotone-Graph-Properties-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-Sharp-Threshold-Bound-for-Monotone-Graph-Properties-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-Sharp-Threshold-Bound-for-Monotone-Graph-Properties-September-25-2026/build/sections/fourier.tex","preprints/A-Sharp-Threshold-Bound-for-Monotone-Graph-Properties-September-25-2026/build/sections/graph.tex","preprints/A-Sharp-Threshold-Bound-for-Monotone-Graph-Properties-September-25-2026/build/figures/block.tex","preprints/A-Sharp-Threshold-Bound-for-Monotone-Graph-Properties-September-25-2026/build/sections/threshold.tex","preprints/A-Sharp-Threshold-Bound-for-Monotone-Graph-Properties-September-25-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Sharp-Threshold-Bound-for-Monotone-Graph-Properties-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Friedgut--Kalai sharp-threshold conjecture. For every integer $n\\ge2$, every nontrivial increasing family of graphs on $n$ vertices invariant under all vertex permutations, and every $0<\\varepsilon<1/2$, the edge probabilities at which its probability equals $\\varepsilon$ and $1-\\varepsilon$ differ by at most $C\\log(1/(2\\varepsilon))/(\\log n)^2$, for a universal constant $C$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":437},{"kind":"section","title":"A low-degree estimate on biased products","sourceOffset":6652},{"kind":"section","title":"Restricting to the edges incident with a vertex block","sourceOffset":12802},{"kind":"section","title":"From influence to threshold width","sourceOffset":23918}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Friedgut--Kalai sharp-threshold conjecture","label":"thm:threshold","text":"For every integer $n\\ge2$, every nontrivial increasing family $\\mathcal P$ of graphs on $n$ vertices invariant under all vertex permutations, and every $0<\\varepsilon<1/2$, \\begin{equation} p_{1-\\varepsilon}(\\mathcal P)-p_\\varepsilon(\\mathcal P) \\le \\frac{2^{19}}{(\\log n)^2}\\log\\frac1{2\\varepsilon}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":1283,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Variance and influence","label":"thm:variance","text":"For every integer $n\\ge2$, every Boolean function $f$ on $E_n$ invariant under vertex permutations, and every $0<p<1$, \\begin{equation} \\Var_p(f)\\le\\frac{2^{17}}{(\\log n)^2}I_p(f). \\end{equation} No monotonicity assumption is needed.","sourceOffset":4365,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/186.md","scopeText":"The Friedgut–Kalai sharp-threshold conjecture concerns how quickly a nontrivial increasing graph property appears in the independent-edge random graph. For every $n\\ge2$, every such property invariant under vertex relabeling, and $0<\\varepsilon<1/2$, the formalization proves that the edge-probability interval between probabilities $\\varepsilon$ and $1-\\varepsilon$ has width at most $2^{19}\\log(1/(2\\varepsilon))/(\\log n)^2$. The constant is uniform over the graph property, $n$, and $\\varepsilon$.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/186.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":30272,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Sharp-Threshold-Bound-for-Monotone-Graph-Properties-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"187-01","slug":"Snaky-in-21-Maker-moves-September-25-2026","familyId":"187","title":"Snaky in 21 Maker moves","abstract":"We prove that Maker can achieve the Snaky hexomino within 21 actual Maker moves against arbitrary legal Breaker play on the initially empty infinite square board. The same bound holds on a $17\\times17$ square; in fact, Maker can confine its claims to a fixed 251-cell board.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Settles the Snaky achievement problem: Maker can force the six-cell Snaky shape within 21 of its own moves on the initially empty infinite square board. Maker moves first, each player claims one free cell per turn, and translations, rotations and reflections count as wins.","pdfPath":"preprints/Snaky-in-21-Maker-moves-September-25-2026/article.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Snaky-in-21-Maker-moves-September-25-2026/article.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Snaky-in-21-Maker-moves-September-25-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/Snaky-in-21-Maker-moves-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Snaky-in-21-Maker-moves-September-25-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/Snaky-in-21-Maker-moves-September-25-2026/build/templates.tex","preprints/Snaky-in-21-Maker-moves-September-25-2026/build/certificate-proof.tex","preprints/Snaky-in-21-Maker-moves-September-25-2026/build/strategy.tex","preprints/Snaky-in-21-Maker-moves-September-25-2026/build/geometry.tex","preprints/Snaky-in-21-Maker-moves-September-25-2026/build/verification.tex","preprints/Snaky-in-21-Maker-moves-September-25-2026/build/route25.tex","preprints/Snaky-in-21-Maker-moves-September-25-2026/build/route35.tex","preprints/Snaky-in-21-Maker-moves-September-25-2026/build/formalization.tex","preprints/Snaky-in-21-Maker-moves-September-25-2026/build/certificate-appendix.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Snaky-in-21-Maker-moves-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that Maker can achieve the Snaky hexomino within 21 actual Maker moves against arbitrary legal Breaker play on the initially empty infinite square board. The same bound holds on a $17\\times17$ square; in fact, Maker can confine its claims to a fixed $251$-cell board.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":322},{"kind":"section","title":"Conditional winning positions","sourceOffset":7725},{"kind":"section","title":"The certificate and its final condition","sourceOffset":12806},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coordinates, placements, and expressions","sourceOffset":13378},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two elementary cards","sourceOffset":15886},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The final card","sourceOffset":18095},{"kind":"section","title":"One strategy against every continuation","sourceOffset":21900},{"kind":"section","title":"Four stones in a row","sourceOffset":26808},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A five-cell fork","sourceOffset":28489},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The perpendicular endpoint attack","sourceOffset":29682},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof of the four-in-a-row result","sourceOffset":34058},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite verification and its scope","sourceOffset":38276}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"In this game, there is one globally legal Maker policy that completes an allowed copy of $S$ within at most $21$ actual Maker claims against every legal continuation of Breaker. Equivalently, extend the policy by fresh claims after an earlier win: its Maker set after claim $21$ contains a target whenever the first $20$ Breaker replies are legal.","sourceOffset":1524,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:certificate","text":"The data in Appendix \\ref{app:data} have exactly $722$ numbered lines, with indices $6,\\ldots,727$. Every line has a nonempty child list, every reference uses an allowed placement of an earlier card, and every parenthesized expression is a finite nonempty combination. Together with the six bases, they produce $728$ valid claims. Their heights are at most $21$, and the last card has \\begin{equation} p_{727}=(8,8),\\qquad A_{727}=\\varnothing, \\qquad |T_{727}|=251,\\qquad h_{727}=21. \\end{equation} Moreover, $T_{727}\\subseteq\\{0,\\ldots,16\\}^2$.","sourceOffset":18124,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:claim","text":"Let $A\\subseteq T\\subset\\Z^2$ be finite and let $h\\ge1$ be an integer. The triple $(A,T,h)$ is a valid claim if, at every position with Maker to move and with \\[ A\\subseteq M,\\qquad B\\cap T=\\varnothing, \\] Maker has already completed a target or has a strategy that completes one within $h$ further actual Maker moves. We call $A$ the required set, $T$ the envelope, and $h$ the height.","sourceOffset":8107,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/187.md","scopeText":"In the Snaky Maker–Breaker game, the players alternately claim cells of $\\mathbb Z^2$, and Maker seeks a translated, rotated, or reflected copy of the six-cell Snaky shape. The formalization gives a legal strategy that wins within $21$ actual Maker moves against every legal Breaker play from the empty infinite board. The selected theorem checks disjointness and the move counts throughout play; it does not impose the paper's smaller finite-board restriction.\n\nThe linked supplements reconstruct the older $35$-move appendix's finite recursive certificate and prove four conditional winning templates from partial positions, assuming the required Maker cells are present and Breaker avoids the corresponding finite envelope.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/187.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":100367,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Snaky-in-21-Maker-moves-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"188-01","slug":"The-Sharp-Terminal-Leave-in-Random-Triangle-Removal-September-25-2026","familyId":"188","title":"The sharp terminal leave in random triangle removal","abstract":"Starting from the complete graph on n vertices, repeatedly remove the three edges of a uniformly chosen remaining triangle. We prove that the number of edges left at termination, divided by n3/2, converges in L2 to $1/(2\\sqrt2)$. This proves the triangle case of the sharp-constant conjecture of Joos and Kühn. In particular, the same limit holds in probability and for the normalized expectation.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Starting from the complete graph on n vertices, repeatedly delete a uniformly chosen remaining triangle. The terminal edge count is asymptotic to $n^{3/2}/(2\\sqrt2)$, with mean-square convergence after normalization by n3/2. This proves the triangle case of the Joos–Kühn sharp-constant conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Sharp-Terminal-Leave-in-Random-Triangle-Removal-September-25-2026/The-Sharp-Terminal-Leave-in-Random-Triangle-Removal-September-25-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Sharp-Terminal-Leave-in-Random-Triangle-Removal-September-25-2026/The-Sharp-Terminal-Leave-in-Random-Triangle-Removal-September-25-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Sharp-Terminal-Leave-in-Random-Triangle-Removal-September-25-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/The-Sharp-Terminal-Leave-in-Random-Triangle-Removal-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Sharp-Terminal-Leave-in-Random-Triangle-Removal-September-25-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-Sharp-Terminal-Leave-in-Random-Triangle-Removal-September-25-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/The-Sharp-Terminal-Leave-in-Random-Triangle-Removal-September-25-2026/build/prefix.tex","preprints/The-Sharp-Terminal-Leave-in-Random-Triangle-Removal-September-25-2026/build/queries.tex","preprints/The-Sharp-Terminal-Leave-in-Random-Triangle-Removal-September-25-2026/build/stability.tex","preprints/The-Sharp-Terminal-Leave-in-Random-Triangle-Removal-September-25-2026/build/coupling.tex","preprints/The-Sharp-Terminal-Leave-in-Random-Triangle-Removal-September-25-2026/build/witnesses.tex","preprints/The-Sharp-Terminal-Leave-in-Random-Triangle-Removal-September-25-2026/build/moments.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Sharp-Terminal-Leave-in-Random-Triangle-Removal-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"Starting from the complete graph on $n$ vertices, repeatedly remove the three edges of a uniformly chosen remaining triangle. We prove that the number of edges left at termination, divided by $n^{3/2}$, converges in $L^2$ to $1/(2\\sqrt2)$. This proves the triangle case of the sharp-constant conjecture of Joos and K\\\"uhn. In particular, the same limit holds in probability and for the normalized expectation.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":483},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The continuation argument","sourceOffset":3081},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":7003},{"kind":"section","title":"A uniform early prefix","sourceOffset":7659},{"kind":"section","title":"Exact queries and independent unfoldings","sourceOffset":17208},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Priorities and the finite test","sourceOffset":17661},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fresh randomness at each occurrence","sourceOffset":23074},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact probability identities","sourceOffset":26809},{"kind":"section","title":"Stable unfolded probabilities","sourceOffset":30074},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exact equation and its linearization","sourceOffset":30898},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From link spectra to a uniform semigroup bound","sourceOffset":34374},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Closing the probability estimate","sourceOffset":42847}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For the uniform triangle-removal process starting from $K_n$, \\[ \\E\\left[\\left(\\frac{F_n}{n^{3/2}}- \\frac1{2\\sqrt2}\\right)^2\\right]\\longrightarrow0. \\] In particular, \\[ \\frac{F_n}{n^{3/2}}\\xrightarrow{\\Prb}\\frac1{2\\sqrt2}, \\qquad \\frac{\\E F_n}{n^{3/2}}\\longrightarrow\\frac1{2\\sqrt2}. \\]","sourceOffset":2382,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Good prefix","label":"prop:prefix","text":"There are constants $c>0$ and $C_0<\\infty$ and an event $\\mathcal G_n$ measurable from the process through step $i_0$ such that \\[ \\Prb(\\mathcal G_n^c)\\le \\exp\\bigl(- (\\log n)^{4/3}\\bigr)=n^{-\\omega(1)} \\] for all sufficiently large $n$. On this event, $i_0$ is reached and $G=G_{i_0}$ has $m$ edges and the following simultaneous properties. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item Every vertex has degree $(1\\pm n^{-c})np$, and every two distinct vertices have $(1\\pm n^{-c})D$ common neighbors. \\item For every vertex $u$ and $3\\le j\\le r_0$, the number of embeddings of a fixed labeled cycle $C_j$ into the link $G[N_G(u)]$ is $(1\\pm n^{-c})D^j$. \\item For every template $(H,I)$ with $v(H)\\le H_0$ and every injection $\\psi:I\\to[n]$, the following two bounds hold. If $S(H',I)\\ge1$ for every same-root subtemplate $H'$, then \\begin{equation} X_{H,I,\\psi}(G)\\le(1+\\log n)^{C_0}S(H,I). \\end{equation} If $n^{v(H)-|J|}p^{e(H)-e(H[J])}\\le1$ for every $I\\subseteq J\\subseteq V(H)$, then \\begin{equation} X_{H,I,\\psi}(G)\\le(1+\\log n)^{C_0}. \\end{equation} \\end{enumerate} All constants and bounds are uniform over the graph and the prescribed root images.","sourceOffset":9671,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Rooted templates","label":"def:template","text":"A template $(H,I)$ consists of a graph $H$ and an independent set $I\\subseteq V(H)$ of distinguished vertices. For an injection $\\psi:I\\to[n]$, let $X_{H,I,\\psi}(G)$ count its edge-preserving injective extensions to $V(H)$ in $G$. Its scaling at density $p$ is \\[ S(H,I)=n^{v(H)-|I|}p^{e(H)}. \\] A subtemplate with the same distinguished set is any subgraph $H'\\subseteq H$ whose vertex set contains $I$, distinguished by $I$. When $v(H)>|I|$, the rooted density is $e(H)/(v(H)-|I|)$; it is zero when there are no free vertices. The template is balanced if every same-root subtemplate has rooted dens","sourceOffset":8847,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/188.md","scopeText":"Start with the complete graph on $n$ vertices and repeatedly delete the three edges of a uniformly chosen remaining triangle. The formalization proves that the number of edges at termination, divided by $n^{3/2}$, converges in $L^2$ to $1/(2\\sqrt2)$. It also states convergence in probability and convergence of the normalized expectation to the same constant. This is the triangle case of the sharp terminal-leave conjecture of Joos and Kühn.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/188.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":78980,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Sharp-Terminal-Leave-in-Random-Triangle-Removal-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"189-01","slug":"Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026","familyId":"189","title":"Cycle--clique Ramsey numbers","abstract":"We prove that $R(C_m,K_n)=(m-1)(n-1)+1$ for every pair of integers $m\\ge n\\ge3$ other than $(m,n)=(3,3)$, for which $R(C_3,K_3)=6$. This establishes the cycle–clique conjecture of Erdős, Faudree, Rousseau and Schelp. The proof combines expansion in a minimal counterexample with a large-clique lemma and an optimization of paths joining clique vertices. These arguments reduce the remaining cases to $3{,}099$ finite parameter-pattern instances, which are excluded by two exact implementations of proved inference rules. Complete programs and deduction traces accompany the paper.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the Erdős–Faudree–Rousseau–Schelp conjecture: $R(C_m,K_n)=(m-1)(n-1)+1$ for every $m\\ge n\\ge3$, except $R(C_3,K_3)=6$. This is the exact threshold forcing a red m-cycle or a blue n-clique in every red–blue coloring of a complete graph.","pdfPath":"preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-reduction.tex","preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-clique-layers.tex","preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-clique-rotations.tex","preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026/build/sections/clique-figure.tex","preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-small.tex","preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-size.tex","preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-paths.tex","preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-growth.tex","preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026/build/sections/representative-figure.tex","preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026/build/sections/08-terminal.tex","preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026/build/sections/09-finite-patterns.tex","preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026/build/sections/09-finite-rules.tex","preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026/build/sections/09-finite-packing.tex","preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026/build/sections/09-finite-results.tex","preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026/build/sections/10-implementation.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that $R(C_m,K_n)=(m-1)(n-1)+1$ for every pair of integers $m\\ge n\\ge3$ other than $(m,n)=(3,3)$, for which $R(C_3,K_3)=6$. This establishes the cycle--clique conjecture of Erd\\H{o}s, Faudree, Rousseau and Schelp. The proof combines expansion in a minimal counterexample with a large-clique lemma and an optimization of paths joining clique vertices. These arguments reduce the remaining cases to $3{,}099$ finite parameter-pattern instances, which are excluded by two exact implementations of proved inference rules. Complete programs and deduction traces accompany the paper.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":648},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Previous results and the parameter range","sourceOffset":1687},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof","sourceOffset":3285},{"kind":"section","title":"A minimal counterexample and neighborhood expansion","sourceOffset":6003},{"kind":"section","title":"A large clique","sourceOffset":10166},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Paths of a prescribed length","sourceOffset":18377},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Closing a path through the breadth-first tree","sourceOffset":29320},{"kind":"section","title":"The cases of four- and five-cycles","sourceOffset":32986},{"kind":"section","title":"From size to independence","sourceOffset":43358},{"kind":"section","title":"Optimal path systems","sourceOffset":48474},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Paths joining clique vertices","sourceOffset":49173},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Outside paths and separated balls","sourceOffset":53924}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For all integers $m\\ge n\\ge3$ with $(m,n)\\ne(3,3)$, \\[ R(C_m,K_n)=(m-1)(n-1)+1. \\] For the excluded pair, $R(C_3,K_3)=6$.","sourceOffset":1053,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"clq:bound","text":"Let \\(k\\ge3\\) and \\(2\\le a\\le k\\) be integers, and let \\(G\\) be a finite simple graph such that \\[ |V(G)|=ka+1,\\qquad \\alpha(G)\\le a,\\qquad C_{k+1}\\not\\subseteq G. \\] Suppose also that \\[ |N_G[I]|\\ge k|I|+1 \\quad\\text{for every nonempty independent set }I\\subseteq V(G). \\] Then its maximum clique size \\(t\\) satisfies \\begin{equation} \\max\\{3,\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor\\}\\le t\\le k. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":10417,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/189.md","scopeText":"The cycle–clique Ramsey conjecture predicts the exact number of vertices forcing either a cycle $C_m$ or a clique $K_n$ in complementary colors. The formalization proves $R(C_m,K_n)=(m-1)(n-1)+1$ for all integers $m\\ge n\\ge3$ except $(m,n)=(3,3)$, where it proves $R(C_3,K_3)=6$. This is the complete parameter range of the paper's main theorem.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/189.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":125291,"sourceFileCount":17,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Cycle-clique-Ramsey-numbers-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"190-01","slug":"Polynomial-removal-fails-for-ordered-binary-matrices-September-25-2026","familyId":"190","title":"Polynomial removal fails for ordered binary matrices","abstract":"We construct a fixed $66\\times66$ binary matrix for which ordered matrix removal has no polynomial bound. This disproves the polynomial ordered binary matrix-removal conjecture. Ordered copies preserve the separate row and column orders and match both zeros and ones; removal permits changing entries in either direction.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves polynomial ordered binary matrix removal with one fixed $66\\times66$ zero–one pattern. Matrices can require many binary-entry changes to become pattern-free while their copy density is smaller than every proposed polynomial bound in that distance. Copies preserve row and column orders and match both zeros and ones.","pdfPath":"preprints/Polynomial-removal-fails-for-ordered-binary-matrices-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Polynomial-removal-fails-for-ordered-binary-matrices-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Polynomial-removal-fails-for-ordered-binary-matrices-September-25-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/Polynomial-removal-fails-for-ordered-binary-matrices-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Polynomial-removal-fails-for-ordered-binary-matrices-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Polynomial-removal-fails-for-ordered-binary-matrices-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-construction.tex","preprints/Polynomial-removal-fails-for-ordered-binary-matrices-September-25-2026/build/figures/tree-orders.tex","preprints/Polynomial-removal-fails-for-ordered-binary-matrices-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-distance.tex","preprints/Polynomial-removal-fails-for-ordered-binary-matrices-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-pinning.tex","preprints/Polynomial-removal-fails-for-ordered-binary-matrices-September-25-2026/build/sections/03b-count.tex","preprints/Polynomial-removal-fails-for-ordered-binary-matrices-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Polynomial-removal-fails-for-ordered-binary-matrices-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a fixed $66\\times66$ binary matrix for which ordered matrix removal has no polynomial bound. This disproves the polynomial ordered binary matrix-removal conjecture. Ordered copies preserve the separate row and column orders and match both zeros and ones; removal permits changing entries in either direction.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":367},{"kind":"section","title":"A fixed pattern and its tree matrices","sourceOffset":7183},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The pattern","sourceOffset":7533},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The ordered positions","sourceOffset":8346},{"kind":"subsection","title":"All binary entries","sourceOffset":14860},{"kind":"section","title":"Distance under arbitrary binary changes","sourceOffset":16681},{"kind":"section","title":"Copies are confined to the leaf diagonal","sourceOffset":22290},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The anchor and the non-anchor traces","sourceOffset":22685},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Every anchor copy uses one mode","sourceOffset":27027},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Every copy uses a diagonal leaf cell","sourceOffset":31031},{"kind":"section","title":"Proof of the main theorem","sourceOffset":34841}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a fixed binary $66\\times66$ matrix $H$ with the following property. For every integer $h\\ge1$, put \\[ n_h=(386h+2)2^h,\\qquad \\epsilon_h=(386h+2)^{-2}. \\] There is a binary $n_h\\times n_h$ matrix $A_h$ such that \\[ \\operatorname{dist}_H(A_h)\\ge\\epsilon_h, \\qquad \\frac{N_H(A_h)}{n_h^{132}}\\le\\epsilon_h2^{-h}. \\] Consequently, no positive constants $c,C$ give a polynomial removal bound for this fixed $H$.","sourceOffset":1661,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:distance","text":"Let $h\\geq 1$, let $m=2^h$, and let $A=A_h$ be the matrix constructed above, of order $n=d_hm$, where $d_h=386h+2$. Every $H$-free binary $n\\times n$ matrix differs from $A$ in at least $m^2$ entries. Consequently $A$ is $d_h^{-2}$-far from being $H$-free.","sourceOffset":17181,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/190.md","scopeText":"The formalized result disproves a polynomial removal bound for one explicit $66\\times66$ binary pattern. For every $c,C>0$, there is an $n\\times n$ binary matrix at normalized edit distance at least $\\varepsilon>0$ from being pattern-free but with fewer than $c\\varepsilon^C n^{132}$ induced ordered copies. Row and column indices are independently increasing, and every zero and one entry must match. The construction gives an explicit sequence of such matrices, allowing edits in both directions.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/190.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":37342,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Polynomial-removal-fails-for-ordered-binary-matrices-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"191-01","slug":"A-power-improvement-in-the-Heilbronn-triangle-lower-bound-September-25-2026","familyId":"191","title":"A power improvement in the Heilbronn triangle lower bound","abstract":"There are absolute constants $\\eta,c_1\\gt 0$ such that, for every sufficiently large integer n, one can choose n points in the unit square so that every triangle they determine has area at least $c_1n^{-2+\\eta}$. Thus the almost n−2 upper-bound formulation of Heilbronn's triangle problem is false. The exponent η is fixed but extremely small.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every sufficiently large n, constructs n points in the unit square such that every triangle has area at least $n^{-2+c}$ for one absolute c > 0. This disproves the conjectured almost-n−2 upper bound in Heilbronn's triangle problem, which asks how large the smallest determined triangle can be.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-power-improvement-in-the-Heilbronn-triangle-lower-bound-September-25-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-power-improvement-in-the-Heilbronn-triangle-lower-bound-September-25-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-power-improvement-in-the-Heilbronn-triangle-lower-bound-September-25-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/A-power-improvement-in-the-Heilbronn-triangle-lower-bound-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-power-improvement-in-the-Heilbronn-triangle-lower-bound-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-power-improvement-in-the-Heilbronn-triangle-lower-bound-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-lattices.tex","preprints/A-power-improvement-in-the-Heilbronn-triangle-lower-bound-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-norm-digits.tex","preprints/A-power-improvement-in-the-Heilbronn-triangle-lower-bound-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-orbits.tex","preprints/A-power-improvement-in-the-Heilbronn-triangle-lower-bound-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-auxiliary-cap.tex","preprints/A-power-improvement-in-the-Heilbronn-triangle-lower-bound-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-sampling.tex","preprints/A-power-improvement-in-the-Heilbronn-triangle-lower-bound-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-zero-determinants.tex","preprints/A-power-improvement-in-the-Heilbronn-triangle-lower-bound-September-25-2026/build/sections/08-alteration.tex","preprints/A-power-improvement-in-the-Heilbronn-triangle-lower-bound-September-25-2026/build/sections/09-prime-interval.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-power-improvement-in-the-Heilbronn-triangle-lower-bound-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"There are absolute constants $\\eta,c_1>0$ such that, for every sufficiently large integer $n$, one can choose $n$ points in the unit square so that every triangle they determine has area at least $c_1n^{-2+\\eta}$. Thus the almost $n^{-2}$ upper-bound formulation of Heilbronn's triangle problem is false. The exponent $\\eta$ is fixed but extremely small.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":428},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two congruence conditions","sourceOffset":4287},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and conventions","sourceOffset":7608},{"kind":"section","title":"Lattice counts with a prescribed determinant","sourceOffset":8605},{"kind":"section","title":"A determinant obstruction from field norms","sourceOffset":17477},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The norm polynomial","sourceOffset":17893},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Encoding the polynomial in a base expansion","sourceOffset":22178},{"kind":"section","title":"Residue orbits and a conditional divisor estimate","sourceOffset":27059},{"kind":"section","title":"An auxiliary cap and its inclusion probabilities","sourceOffset":36209},{"kind":"section","title":"Sampling integral columns","sourceOffset":45842},{"kind":"section","title":"Counting determinant-zero triples","sourceOffset":53569},{"kind":"section","title":"Deletion and projective normalization","sourceOffset":63732}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"intro:main","text":"There are absolute constants $\\eta,c_1>0$ and an integer $n_0\\ge3$ such that \\[ \\Delta(n)\\ge c_1n^{-2+\\eta} \\qquad\\text{for every integer }n\\ge n_0. \\]","sourceOffset":2322,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"A fixed nonzero determinant","label":"lat:fixed-det","text":"Let $\\Lambda\\subseteq\\mathbb Z^3$ have index $I$, let $X\\ge2$, and suppose its third successive length satisfies $\\lambda_3\\le X$. For every $t\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}$, the number of $3$ by $3$ matrices with rows in $\\Lambda$, row lengths at most $X$, and determinant $t$ is at most \\[ C\\bigl(\\log(2X)\\bigr)^2\\frac{X^6}{I^2}. \\] The constant is absolute.","sourceOffset":15767,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/191.md","scopeText":"Heilbronn's triangle problem asks how large the smallest triangle determined by $n$ points in the unit square can be. The formalization constructs an unbounded sequence of sizes $n$ and point sets for which every triangle has area at least $n^{-2+\\eta}$, for one fixed $\\eta>0$. It consequently refutes the proposed upper bound of order $n^{-2+\\varepsilon}$ for every $\\varepsilon>0$. The linked construction is for an unbounded sequence of sizes; the paper's statement for every sufficiently large $n$ is broader.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/191.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":71159,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-power-improvement-in-the-Heilbronn-triangle-lower-bound-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-power-improvement-in-the-Heilbronn-triangle-lower-bound-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"192-01","slug":"Unbounded-Violations-of-the-Square-Root-Degree-Bound-September-26-2026","familyId":"192","title":"Unbounded Violations of the Square-Root Degree Bound","abstract":"We disprove the Gopalan–Servedio square-root conjecture, even up to an arbitrary constant factor. For every real C > 0, there is a nonconstant Boolean function $f:\\{-1,1\\}^n\\to\\{-1,1\\}$ on a finite sign cube such that $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n \\widehat f(\\{i\\})\\gt C\\sqrt{\\deg(f)}.$ Here $\\widehat f(\\{i\\})$ is the linear Fourier coefficient associated with the ith input, and $\\deg(f)$ is the degree of the real multilinear polynomial representing f.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves the proposed square-root bound relating a Boolean function's linear Fourier coefficients to its polynomial degree. For every C > 0, there is a sign-valued Boolean function f with $\\sum_i\\widehat f(\\{i\\})\\gt C\\sqrt{\\deg(f)}$. Thus its total signed correlation with individual input bits can exceed the proposed bound by an arbitrary factor.","pdfPath":"preprints/Unbounded-Violations-of-the-Square-Root-Degree-Bound-September-26-2026/Unbounded-Violations-of-the-Square-Root-Degree-Bound-September-26-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Unbounded-Violations-of-the-Square-Root-Degree-Bound-September-26-2026/Unbounded-Violations-of-the-Square-Root-Degree-Bound-September-26-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Unbounded-Violations-of-the-Square-Root-Degree-Bound-September-26-2026","subjectId":"13","subject":"Combinatorics","subjectZh":"組合數學","texPaths":["preprints/Unbounded-Violations-of-the-Square-Root-Degree-Bound-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Unbounded-Violations-of-the-Square-Root-Degree-Bound-September-26-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Unbounded-Violations-of-the-Square-Root-Degree-Bound-September-26-2026/build/sections/01-observations.tex","preprints/Unbounded-Violations-of-the-Square-Root-Degree-Bound-September-26-2026/build/sections/02-reporting.tex","preprints/Unbounded-Violations-of-the-Square-Root-Degree-Bound-September-26-2026/build/sections/03-alternatives.tex","preprints/Unbounded-Violations-of-the-Square-Root-Degree-Bound-September-26-2026/build/sections/04-mean-cost.tex","preprints/Unbounded-Violations-of-the-Square-Root-Degree-Bound-September-26-2026/build/sections/05-weighted.tex","preprints/Unbounded-Violations-of-the-Square-Root-Degree-Bound-September-26-2026/build/sections/06-small-width.tex","preprints/Unbounded-Violations-of-the-Square-Root-Degree-Bound-September-26-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Unbounded-Violations-of-the-Square-Root-Degree-Bound-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove the Gopalan--Servedio square-root conjecture, even up to an arbitrary constant factor. For every real \\(C>0\\), there is a nonconstant Boolean function \\(f:\\{-1,1\\}^n\\to\\{-1,1\\}\\) on a finite sign cube such that \\[ \\sum_{i=1}^n \\widehat f(\\{i\\})>C\\sqrt{\\deg(f)}. \\] Here \\(\\widehat f(\\{i\\})\\) is the linear Fourier coefficient associated with the \\(i\\)th input, and \\(\\deg(f)\\) is the degree of the real multilinear polynomial representing \\(f\\).","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":505},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The square-root and majority bounds","sourceOffset":2172},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Retaining variance with low-degree observations","sourceOffset":4270},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite observations and amplification","sourceOffset":8497},{"kind":"section","title":"Retaining more variance than the degree cost","sourceOffset":14507},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A reporting rule and its prediction error","sourceOffset":15459},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Choosing the intervals in the Gaussian model","sourceOffset":19281},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Transfer to finite inputs and amplification","sourceOffset":23684},{"kind":"section","title":"Two other reporting rules","sourceOffset":25302},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Merging the all-leaves-pass reports","sourceOffset":25961},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Switching to an independent score","sourceOffset":30693},{"kind":"section","title":"Amplifying an average degree saving","sourceOffset":34281}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every real \\(C>0\\), there are a positive integer \\(n\\) and a nonconstant Boolean function \\(f:\\{-1,1\\}^n\\to\\{-1,1\\}\\) such that \\[ \\sum_{i=1}^n\\widehat f(\\{i\\})>C\\sqrt{\\deg(f)}. \\] Consequently, \\[ \\sup_{\\substack{n\\ge1,\\ f:\\{-1,1\\}^n\\to\\{-1,1\\}\\\\\\deg(f)>0}} \\frac{\\sum_i|\\widehat f(\\{i\\})|}{\\sqrt{\\deg(f)}}=\\infty. \\]","sourceOffset":1374,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Amplification criterion","label":"prop:amplification","text":"Let \\(1\\le d<r\\) be integers, and let \\(\\mathcal W\\) be a measurable observation rule on \\(r\\) real inputs. Suppose: \\begin{enumerate} \\item On any product of finite input alphabets, the indicator of each output value is a finite linear combination of functions each depending on at most \\(d\\) input coordinates. \\item There is an \\(\\eta>0\\) such that, whenever centered variance-one laws \\(Z_j\\) converge weakly to a standard normal with uniformly bounded fourth moments, independent copies satisfy, for all sufficiently large \\(j\\), \\[ \\Var\\!\\left(\\E\\!\\left[\\sum_{i=1}^r Z_{j,i}\\,\\middle|\\, \\mathcal W(\\mathbf Z_j)\\right]\\right)\\ge d+\\eta. \\] \\end{enumerate} Then finite observations have arbitrarily large retained variance-to-cell-degree ratios, and Theorem \\ref{thm:main} follows.","sourceOffset":12712,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/192.md","scopeText":"The formalization disproves the Gopalan–Servedio square-root degree conjecture by an unbounded factor. For every $C>0$, it gives a nonconstant Boolean function on a finite sign cube for which the sum of its linear Fourier coefficients exceeds $C\\sqrt{\\deg f}$, where $\\deg f$ is its real multilinear degree.\n\nIt also proves that the ratio of the sum of the absolute values of those coefficients to $\\sqrt{\\deg f}$ is unbounded among positive-degree Boolean functions.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/192.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":70802,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Unbounded-Violations-of-the-Square-Root-Degree-Bound-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"193-01","slug":"Positivity-of-Serres-Intersection-Multiplicity-September-23-2026","familyId":"193","title":"Positivity of Serre's Intersection Multiplicity","abstract":"We prove Serre's positivity conjecture, including ramified mixed characteristic. If two nonzero finitely generated modules over a regular local ring have tensor product of finite length and complementary dimensions, then their intersection multiplicity is strictly positive.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves strict positivity of Serre's intersection multiplicity $\\chi^R(M,N)$ for nonzero finitely generated modules over any regular local ring, provided $M\\otimes_R N$ has finite length and $\\dim M+\\dim N=\\dim R$. This resolves the positivity conjecture, including ramified mixed characteristic.","pdfPath":"preprints/Positivity-of-Serres-Intersection-Multiplicity-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Positivity-of-Serres-Intersection-Multiplicity-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Positivity-of-Serres-Intersection-Multiplicity-September-23-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/Positivity-of-Serres-Intersection-Multiplicity-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Positivity-of-Serres-Intersection-Multiplicity-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Positivity-of-Serres-Intersection-Multiplicity-September-23-2026/build/sections/lengths.tex","preprints/Positivity-of-Serres-Intersection-Multiplicity-September-23-2026/build/sections/descent.tex","preprints/Positivity-of-Serres-Intersection-Multiplicity-September-23-2026/build/sections/asymptotic.tex","preprints/Positivity-of-Serres-Intersection-Multiplicity-September-23-2026/build/sections/positivity.tex","preprints/Positivity-of-Serres-Intersection-Multiplicity-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Positivity-of-Serres-Intersection-Multiplicity-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Serre's positivity conjecture, including ramified mixed characteristic. If two nonzero finitely generated modules over a regular local ring have tensor product of finite length and complementary dimensions, then their intersection multiplicity is strictly positive.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":321},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and significance","sourceOffset":1894},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":4971},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Consequences","sourceOffset":9551},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":12715},{"kind":"section","title":"Residue fields and normalized length","sourceOffset":13679},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Extending the residue field","sourceOffset":14424},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Regular bases and the tower length","sourceOffset":17822},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Length algebras and parameter homology","sourceOffset":23807},{"kind":"section","title":"Rational descent with closed supports","sourceOffset":33348},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The truncating invariant and its supports","sourceOffset":35977},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Descent for finite surjections","sourceOffset":40261}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $(R,\\m)$ be a commutative Noetherian regular local ring of dimension $d$, and let $M,N$ be nonzero finitely generated $R$-modules. If $\\length_R(M\\otimes_R N)<\\infty$ and $\\dim_R M+\\dim_R N=d$, then \\[ \\chi^R(M,N)>0. \\]","sourceOffset":966,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Finite strict-transform intersection","label":"cor:tangent-cone","text":"Let $(A,\\m)$ be a commutative Noetherian regular local ring with residue field $k$, and let $P,Q$ be prime ideals such that $A/(P+Q)$ has finite length and $\\dim(A/P)+\\dim(A/Q)=\\dim A$. Write $e_\\m$ for Hilbert--Samuel multiplicity and put \\[ T=\\operatorname{gr}(A/P)\\otimes_{\\operatorname{gr}A}\\operatorname{gr}(A/Q), \\] where all associated graded rings use the maximal-ideal filtrations. If $\\dim T\\leq1$, then \\[ \\chi^A(A/P,A/Q)\\geq e_\\m(A/P)e_\\m(A/Q), \\] with equality if and only if $\\dim T=0$.","sourceOffset":10503,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":114891,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Positivity-of-Serres-Intersection-Multiplicity-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"194-01","slug":"Lechs-multiplicity-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"194","title":"Lech's multiplicity conjecture","abstract":"We prove Lech's multiplicity conjecture: Hilbert–Samuel multiplicity cannot decrease under a flat local homomorphism of nonzero Noetherian local rings. The result holds in arbitrary dimension, with no restrictions on the residue fields or characteristics.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves $e(R)\\le e(S)$ for every flat local homomorphism of nonzero Noetherian local rings, where e is Hilbert–Samuel multiplicity. This resolves Lech's conjecture in every dimension and characteristic.","pdfPath":"preprints/Lechs-multiplicity-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Lechs-multiplicity-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Lechs-multiplicity-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/Lechs-multiplicity-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Lechs-multiplicity-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/complex.tex","preprints/Lechs-multiplicity-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/lengths.tex","preprints/Lechs-multiplicity-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/reductions.tex","preprints/Lechs-multiplicity-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/charp.tex","preprints/Lechs-multiplicity-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/mixed.tex","preprints/Lechs-multiplicity-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/charzero.tex","preprints/Lechs-multiplicity-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Lechs-multiplicity-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Lech's multiplicity conjecture: Hilbert--Samuel multiplicity cannot decrease under a flat local homomorphism of nonzero Noetherian local rings. The result holds in arbitrary dimension, with no restrictions on the residue fields or characteristics.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":302},{"kind":"section","title":"A complex estimate with an additive length","sourceOffset":14493},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Length hypotheses and the estimate","sourceOffset":14960},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Koszul acyclicity modulo length zero","sourceOffset":18160},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The blowup and its exceptional divisor","sourceOffset":20182},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Euler characteristics on the blowup","sourceOffset":24376},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bundles with prescribed cohomological roots","sourceOffset":35109},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The sign and the leading coefficient","sourceOffset":38199},{"kind":"section","title":"Normalized length algebras","sourceOffset":42907},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The normalized length formalism","sourceOffset":45408},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Characteristic \\texorpdfstring{$p$}{p}","sourceOffset":49220},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Mixed characteristic","sourceOffset":59704}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Lech's conjecture","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $(R,\\mathfrak m)\\longrightarrow(S,\\mathfrak n)$ be a flat local homomorphism of nonzero Noetherian local rings. Then \\[ e(R)\\leq e(S). \\] There is no restriction on the dimensions, the residue fields, or the characteristics of the rings.","sourceOffset":807,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Dutta multiplicity over positive-characteristic domains","label":"cor:dutta-domain","text":"Let $(R,\\mathfrak m)$ be a complete Noetherian local domain of characteristic $p>0$, and let $F$ be a short complex over $R$. Then \\[ \\chi_\\infty^R(F)\\geq e(R). \\]","sourceOffset":86514,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:short-complex","text":"Let $(R,\\mathfrak m)$ be a Noetherian local ring of characteristic $p>0$ and dimension $d$. A short complex over $R$ is a complex of finite free modules \\[ F=(0\\longrightarrow F_d\\longrightarrow\\cdots \\longrightarrow F_0\\longrightarrow0) \\] whose homology modules have finite length and for which $H_0(F)\\ne0$. For any bounded complex $G$ with finite-length homology, write \\[ \\chi_R(G)=\\sum_i(-1)^i\\length_R H_i(G). \\] When $G$ is finite free, let $\\Phi_R^nG$ denote scalar extension along the $n$th Frobenius endomorphism. Its Dutta multiplicity is \\[ \\chi_\\infty^R(G)= \\lim_{n\\longrightarrow\\infty","sourceOffset":85001,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/194.md","scopeText":"Lech's multiplicity conjecture compares Hilbert–Samuel multiplicities across flat local maps. The linked formalization covers a supporting characteristic-$p$ comparison over a complete Noetherian local domain $D$: for a complex satisfying the stated short-complex and finite-length homology conditions, its Frobenius multiplicity sequence converges to its Dutta multiplicity, and the Hilbert–Samuel multiplicity of $D$ is at most that Dutta multiplicity.\n\nThis selected statement is the complete-domain Dutta comparison. The paper's full flat-local result in arbitrary characteristic is outside it.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/194.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":136526,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Lechs-multiplicity-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"195-01","slug":"A-Complete-Local-Domain-Without-a-Small-Cohen-Macaulay-Module-September-23-2026","familyId":"195","title":"A Complete Local Domain without a Small Cohen–Macaulay Module","abstract":"We construct a three-dimensional complete Noetherian normal local domain containing ℂ, with residue field ℂ, that has no nonzero finitely generated maximal Cohen–Macaulay module. This disproves the domain form of the small Cohen–Macaulay module conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a three-dimensional complete Noetherian normal local domain over ℂ with no nonzero finitely generated maximal Cohen–Macaulay module. A three-dimensional local domain essentially of finite type over ℂ has the same property, disproving the domain form of the small Cohen–Macaulay module conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Complete-Local-Domain-Without-a-Small-Cohen-Macaulay-Module-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Complete-Local-Domain-Without-a-Small-Cohen-Macaulay-Module-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Complete-Local-Domain-Without-a-Small-Cohen-Macaulay-Module-September-23-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/A-Complete-Local-Domain-Without-a-Small-Cohen-Macaulay-Module-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-Complete-Local-Domain-Without-a-Small-Cohen-Macaulay-Module-September-23-2026/build/surface.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Complete-Local-Domain-Without-a-Small-Cohen-Macaulay-Module-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a three-dimensional complete Noetherian normal local domain containing $\\C$, with residue field $\\C$, that has no nonzero finitely generated maximal Cohen--Macaulay module. This disproves the domain form of the small Cohen--Macaulay module conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":338},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":7306},{"kind":"section","title":"From a completed cone to its exceptional surface","sourceOffset":9328},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite map and the completed ring","sourceOffset":9669},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The regular model and normality","sourceOffset":12046},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Extending an arbitrary module","sourceOffset":14970},{"kind":"section","title":"Balancing slopes by modifications","sourceOffset":17139},{"kind":"section","title":"A Chern-character inequality from a divisor","sourceOffset":20943},{"kind":"section","title":"The numerical obstruction","sourceOffset":24499},{"kind":"section","title":"An explicit surface and its polarization","sourceOffset":28196},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The branch divisor","sourceOffset":29294},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The cover and its local coordinates","sourceOffset":32496}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exists a three-dimensional complete Noetherian normal local domain $R$ containing $\\C$, with residue field $\\C$, such that no nonzero finitely generated $R$-module has depth three.","sourceOffset":752,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:obstruction","text":"Let $X$ be a smooth integral projective surface over $\\C$, and let $H$ be an ample globally generated divisor. If $R(X,H)$ admits a small Cohen--Macaulay module, then \\begin{equation} K_X^2-2c_2(X)\\leq 6H^2. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":4604,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/195.md","scopeText":"The small Cohen–Macaulay module conjecture asks whether every complete local domain has a nonzero finitely generated maximal Cohen–Macaulay module. The linked formalization verifies only the ring-theoretic candidate: there exists a three-dimensional complete Noetherian integrally closed local domain over $\\mathbb C$ whose residue field is $\\mathbb C$.\n\nIt does not prove that this ring lacks a nonzero finitely generated maximal Cohen–Macaulay module, or the paper's counterexample essentially of finite type.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/195.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":45336,"sourceFileCount":2,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Complete-Local-Domain-Without-a-Small-Cohen-Macaulay-Module-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"196-01","slug":"A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-with-Zero-Divisors-September-23-2026","familyId":"196","title":"A Torsion-Free Group Algebra with Zero Divisors","abstract":"We disprove Kaplansky's zero-divisor conjecture by constructing a finitely presented torsion-free group G for which $\\mathbb F_2[G]$ has nonzero zero divisors. The group admits a finite two-dimensional classifying space.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a finitely presented torsion-free group G whose group algebra $\\mathbb F_2[G]$ has nonzero zero divisors, disproving Kaplansky's zero-divisor conjecture. The group has a finite two-dimensional classifying space.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-with-Zero-Divisors-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-with-Zero-Divisors-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-with-Zero-Divisors-September-23-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-with-Zero-Divisors-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-with-Zero-Divisors-September-23-2026/build/sections/types.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-with-Zero-Divisors-September-23-2026/build/sections/entropy.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-with-Zero-Divisors-September-23-2026/build/figures/block-alignment.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-with-Zero-Divisors-September-23-2026/build/sections/planar.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-with-Zero-Divisors-September-23-2026/build/sections/topology.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-with-Zero-Divisors-September-23-2026/build/figures/band-surgery.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-with-Zero-Divisors-September-23-2026/build/sections/factors.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-with-Zero-Divisors-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove Kaplansky's zero-divisor conjecture by constructing a finitely presented torsion-free group \\(G\\) for which \\(\\mathbb F_2[G]\\) has nonzero zero divisors. The group admits a finite two-dimensional classifying space.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":272},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and related constructions","sourceOffset":894},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The cancellation mechanism and the two safeguards","sourceOffset":4274},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From random graphs to topological control","sourceOffset":6457},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and proof structure","sourceOffset":8537},{"kind":"section","title":"Prescribed types and random matchings","sourceOffset":9897},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite geometry","sourceOffset":10379},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Turn weights","sourceOffset":13424},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Girth conditioning and switching","sourceOffset":15782},{"kind":"section","title":"A bounded-pattern estimate","sourceOffset":21501},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The image graph and multiplicity stages","sourceOffset":24523},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Word constraints on aligned blocks","sourceOffset":30934}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist a finitely presented torsion-free group \\(G\\) and nonzero elements \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb F_2[G]\\) such that \\(\\alpha\\beta=0\\). Moreover, \\(G\\) admits a finite two-dimensional classifying space.","sourceOffset":638,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Bounded-pattern estimate","label":"prop:bounded-pattern","text":"There is a constant \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), depending only on the fixed types and turn weights, with the following property. For every fixed positive integer \\(K\\), fixed real \\(C\\ge1\\), and fixed nonnegative integer \\(I\\), the conditional probability tends to zero that there exists a system as above having at most \\(K\\) paths, total length \\(H\\) with \\[ L\\le H\\le CL, \\] at most \\(I\\) interval pairs, and \\(b\\) unpaired occurrences, where \\(b\\le\\varepsilon H\\). In particular, \\(\\varepsilon\\) is chosen before \\(K,C,I\\), and these three bounds are held fixed as \\(n\\) tends to infinity through its admissible values.","sourceOffset":22805,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Reduced spherical arrangement","label":"def:arrangement","text":"A reduced spherical arrangement over $\\Gamma$ consists of the following data on an oriented sphere. \\begin{enumerate} \\item There are finitely many pairwise disjoint piecewise linear closed disks. Each disk boundary carries a positive-length cyclically immersed closed path in $\\Gamma$, with the possible exception of one boundary. That exceptional boundary carries a positive-length immersed interval path starting at $x_A$ or $x_B$: its two endpoints are represented at one marked boundary point, called the break. No immersion condition is imposed across that break. At least one ordinary cyclical","sourceOffset":48758,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/196.md","scopeText":"Kaplansky's zero-divisor conjecture asserts that the group algebra of a torsion-free group over a field has no zero divisors. The formalized result constructs a finitely presented torsion-free group $G$ and nonzero elements $\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb F_2[G]$ with $\\alpha\\beta=0$, giving a counterexample.\n\nThe same group admits a finite two-dimensional classifying space $K(G,1)$.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/196.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":86143,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-with-Zero-Divisors-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"197-01","slug":"A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-That-Is-Not-Directly-Finite-October-4-2026","familyId":"197","title":"A Torsion-Free Group Algebra That Is Not Directly Finite","abstract":"We construct a finitely presented torsion-free counterexample to Kaplansky's direct finiteness conjecture over the field of two elements. The group admits a finite two-dimensional classifying complex.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a finitely presented torsion-free nonsofic group whose group algebra over 𝔽2 is not directly finite, disproving Kaplansky's conjecture even without torsion. Companion examples give injective nonsurjective cellular automata on all configurations, refuting Gottschalk's surjunctivity conjecture. Another counterexample is an integral group-ring matrix, invertible over the rational group ring, with Fuglede–Kadison determinant strictly between zero and one, disproving the unrestricted Determinant Conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-That-Is-Not-Directly-Finite-October-4-2026/direct-finiteness.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-That-Is-Not-Directly-Finite-October-4-2026/direct-finiteness.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-That-Is-Not-Directly-Finite-October-4-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-That-Is-Not-Directly-Finite-October-4-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-That-Is-Not-Directly-Finite-October-4-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-That-Is-Not-Directly-Finite-October-4-2026/build/sections/algebra.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-That-Is-Not-Directly-Finite-October-4-2026/build/sections/random.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-That-Is-Not-Directly-Finite-October-4-2026/build/sections/patterns.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-That-Is-Not-Directly-Finite-October-4-2026/build/figures/blocks.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-That-Is-Not-Directly-Finite-October-4-2026/build/sections/planar.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-That-Is-Not-Directly-Finite-October-4-2026/build/figures/digon.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-That-Is-Not-Directly-Finite-October-4-2026/build/sections/topology.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-That-Is-Not-Directly-Finite-October-4-2026/build/figures/exterior-surgery.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-That-Is-Not-Directly-Finite-October-4-2026/build/figures/inner-surgery.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-That-Is-Not-Directly-Finite-October-4-2026/build/sections/assembly.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-That-Is-Not-Directly-Finite-October-4-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a finitely presented torsion-free counterexample to Kaplansky's direct finiteness conjecture over the field of two elements. The group admits a finite two-dimensional classifying complex.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":247},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and related work","sourceOffset":1298},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and its main estimates","sourceOffset":3774},{"kind":"section","title":"Labeled graphs and the parity criterion","sourceOffset":6636},{"kind":"section","title":"Types and random matchings","sourceOffset":12686},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prescribing the outgoing labels","sourceOffset":13330},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Turn frequencies and decay of word weights","sourceOffset":18753},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conditioning on large girth","sourceOffset":22207},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Expansion and component diameter","sourceOffset":28680},{"kind":"section","title":"A bounded path estimate","sourceOffset":32435},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The image graph and its multiplicity levels","sourceOffset":35326},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One grid for all comparisons","sourceOffset":41940}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist a finitely presented torsion-free group $G$ and elements $a,b,c\\in\\Ftwo[G]$ such that \\[ ab=1,\\qquad ac=0,\\qquad c\\ne0. \\] Consequently $ba\\ne1$. The group $G$ admits a finite two-dimensional classifying complex.","sourceOffset":920,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Parity criterion","label":"prop:parity","text":"Let $\\Gamma_A,\\Gamma_B$ be finite loopless graphs immersed in the same rose, with roots and cone group as above. Suppose that \\begin{equation} \\begin{aligned} |S_x\\cap S_{x'}|&\\equiv1\\pmod2 &&\\bigl(x,x'\\in A',\\ (x,x')\\ne(x_A,x_A)\\bigr),\\\\ |S_{x_A}|&\\equiv0\\pmod2,\\\\ |S_x\\cap S_y|&\\equiv1\\pmod2 &&\\bigl(x\\in A',\\ y\\in B'\\bigr). \\end{aligned} \\end{equation} Suppose also that the roots are protected in the following sense: \\begin{equation} \\begin{gathered} \\text{if a path in $\\Gamma$ starts at $x_A$ or $x_B$ and ends}\\\\[-2pt] \\text{at a different vertex, its label is nontrivial in $G$.} \\end{gathered} \\end{equation} Then the finite scalar sums \\begin{equation} a=\\sum_{x\\in A'}g_x,\\qquad b=\\sum_{x\\in A'}g_x^{-1},\\qquad c=\\sum_{y\\in B'}h_y^{-1} \\quad\\text{in }\\Ftwo[G] \\end{equation} satisfy $ab=1$, $ac=0$, and $c\\ne0$. Consequently $ba\\ne1$.","sourceOffset":9550,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Reduced spherical arrangement","label":"def:reduced-arrangement","text":"A reduced spherical arrangement over $\\Gamma$ consists of finitely many pairwise disjoint piecewise linear closed disks in an oriented sphere, together with the following data. Each boundary reads a positive-length cyclically immersed closed path in $\\Gamma$, except possibly one boundary. That boundary reads a positive-length immersed interval path starting at $x_A$ or $x_B$, with its two endpoints represented at a single marked boundary point, called the break. No immersion condition is imposed across the break. At least one ordinary, cyclically immersed boundary is required. The paths are re","sourceOffset":63747,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/197.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":109720,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Torsion-Free-Group-Algebra-That-Is-Not-Directly-Finite-October-4-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"197-02","slug":"A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Characteristic-Two-September-23-2026","familyId":"197","title":"A Counterexample to Kaplansky's Direct-Finiteness Conjecture in Characteristic Two","abstract":"We disprove Kaplansky's direct-finiteness conjecture by constructing a finite field K of characteristic two, a finitely presented group G, and finite sums $a,b\\in K[G]$ with $ab=1$ but $ba\\ne1$. The group G is nonsofic. The same elements define a cellular automaton on KG that is injective but not surjective, disproving Gottschalk's surjunctivity conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a finitely presented torsion-free nonsofic group whose group algebra over 𝔽2 is not directly finite, disproving Kaplansky's conjecture even without torsion. Companion examples give injective nonsurjective cellular automata on all configurations, refuting Gottschalk's surjunctivity conjecture. Another counterexample is an integral group-ring matrix, invertible over the rational group ring, with Fuglede–Kadison determinant strictly between zero and one, disproving the unrestricted Determinant Conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Characteristic-Two-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Characteristic-Two-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Characteristic-Two-September-23-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Characteristic-Two-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Characteristic-Two-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Characteristic-Two-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-pairing.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Characteristic-Two-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-criterion.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Characteristic-Two-September-23-2026/build/sections/02a-embedding.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Characteristic-Two-September-23-2026/build/figures/diagram-merge.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Characteristic-Two-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-hypergraph.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Characteristic-Two-September-23-2026/build/figures/trade.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Characteristic-Two-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-prescription.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Characteristic-Two-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-cellular-automata.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Characteristic-Two-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove Kaplansky's direct-finiteness conjecture by constructing a finite field $K$ of characteristic two, a finitely presented group $G$, and finite sums $a,b\\in K[G]$ with $ab=1$ but $ba\\ne1$. The group $G$ is nonsofic. The same elements define a cellular automaton on $K^G$ that is injective but not surjective, disproving Gottschalk's surjunctivity conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":414},{"kind":"section","title":"Polynomial pairings on a punctured affine space","sourceOffset":12994},{"kind":"section","title":"An incidence criterion and its embedded local groups","sourceOffset":18654},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The glued group","sourceOffset":20701},{"kind":"section","title":"From subgroup averages to the scalar defect","sourceOffset":33485},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Subgroup averages and a rectangular identity","sourceOffset":33868},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A group extension implementing character twists","sourceOffset":35781},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Packing into square matrices","sourceOffset":40354},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Detecting the reverse defect","sourceOffset":41791},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From matrices to two group-algebra elements","sourceOffset":43749},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite presentation and the retained odd torsion","sourceOffset":47605},{"kind":"section","title":"A regular hypergraph of affine lines","sourceOffset":49820}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist a finite field $K$ of characteristic two, a finitely presented group $G$ containing an element of odd prime order, and elements $a_{\\mathrm{out}},b_{\\mathrm{out}}\\in K[G]$ such that \\[ a_{\\mathrm{out}}b_{\\mathrm{out}}=1, \\qquad b_{\\mathrm{out}}a_{\\mathrm{out}}\\ne1. \\] The field, group, and finite sums are specified by a terminating prescription involving finite sets and finite-field arithmetic.","sourceOffset":1389,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"The pairing supplied by holes","label":"prop:pairing","text":"Let $q\\ge4$ be a power of two, let $Q$ be a field of order $q$, fix an enumeration of $Q$, and let $n\\ge1$. Put $d_0=(q-2)/2$, $S=S_{d_0}\\subset\\mathcal A=Q^n$, and $N_{\\rm pt}=q^n$. Let $\\ell$ be an odd prime and $D=\\ell+1$. Suppose that $O\\subset S$ and that $\\mathcal F$ is a family of full affine $Q$-lines contained in $V=\\mathcal A\\setminus O$, with \\begin{equation} \\frac{2N_{\\rm pt}}{D^2}\\le |O|\\le\\frac{7N_{\\rm pt}}{D^2}. \\end{equation} Suppose also that every point of $V$ lies on $D$ members of $\\mathcal F$ and that the incidence graph has girth at least $12$. If $K$ is a finite extension of $Q$ containing a primitive $\\ell$th root of unity, then, with $m=|O|$ and $t=|V|$, there are columns $x_v\\in K^m$, $v\\in V$, such that \\[ 0<m<t\\le m\\ell^2,\\qquad \\sum_{v\\in V}x_vx_v^{\\mathsf T}=-I_m,\\qquad \\sum_{v\\in f}x_vx_v^{\\mathsf T}=0\\quad(f\\in\\mathcal F). \\]","sourceOffset":16159,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/197.md","scopeText":"Kaplansky's direct-finiteness conjecture asserts that $ab=1$ implies $ba=1$ for $a,b\\in K[G]$, for every field $K$ and group $G$. The formalized results construct a finite field of characteristic two and a group algebra violating this implication. One statement records the counterexample for a finitely generated group; the more detailed construction gives a finitely presented group with an element of odd prime order.\n\nThe detailed construction also gives a precise recipe for choosing the data and proves that the required search terminates. The further conclusion that the group is nonsofic is outside these statements.\n\nA nonsingular integer matrix has absolute determinant at least $1$. The group-ring Determinant Conjecture extends this bound to matrices over $\\mathbb Z[G]$ for every discret","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/197.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":94988,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Characteristic-Two-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Characteristic-Two-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex.tex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"197-03","slug":"A-Counterexample-to-the-Group-Ring-Determinant-Conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"197","title":"A Counterexample to the Group-Ring Determinant Conjecture","abstract":"We disprove the unrestricted group-ring Determinant Conjecture. We construct a finitely generated group G and a square matrix over $\\mathbb Z[G]$ that is invertible over $\\mathbb Q[G]$ and has Fuglede–Kadison determinant strictly between zero and one. The logarithmic integral defining the determinant is finite.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a finitely presented torsion-free nonsofic group whose group algebra over 𝔽2 is not directly finite, disproving Kaplansky's conjecture even without torsion. Companion examples give injective nonsurjective cellular automata on all configurations, refuting Gottschalk's surjunctivity conjecture. Another counterexample is an integral group-ring matrix, invertible over the rational group ring, with Fuglede–Kadison determinant strictly between zero and one, disproving the unrestricted Determinant Conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-the-Group-Ring-Determinant-Conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-the-Group-Ring-Determinant-Conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-the-Group-Ring-Determinant-Conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/A-Counterexample-to-the-Group-Ring-Determinant-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-the-Group-Ring-Determinant-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove the unrestricted group-ring Determinant Conjecture. We construct a finitely generated group $G$ and a square matrix over $\\mathbb Z[G]$ that is invertible over $\\mathbb Q[G]$ and has Fuglede--Kadison determinant strictly between zero and one. The logarithmic integral defining the determinant is finite.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction and proof overview","sourceOffset":361},{"kind":"section","title":"From the scalar defect to a projection","sourceOffset":7518},{"kind":"section","title":"Lifting the factorization and preserving integrality","sourceOffset":12558},{"kind":"section","title":"The trace calculation","sourceOffset":17522},{"kind":"section","title":"Assembly and exact determinant","sourceOffset":21765}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are a finitely generated discrete group $G_D$, an integer $n\\ge1$, and $A\\in\\Mat_n(\\Z[G_D])$ invertible over $\\Q[G_D]$ such that \\[ 0<\\det_{\\N(G_D)}(T_A)<1. \\] The integral in Equation \\eqref{eq:conjecture} is finite and strictly negative. Thus the unrestricted group-ring Determinant Conjecture has a negative resolution.","sourceOffset":2473,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"{\\cite[Theorem 1.1","label":"thm:input","text":"{CharacteristicTwo}}] There exist a finite field $K_2$ of characteristic two, a finitely generated group $H_2$, and finite sums $a_2,b_2\\in K_2[H_2]$ with $a_2b_2=1$ and $b_2a_2\\ne1$.","sourceOffset":3054,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/197.md","scopeText":"Kaplansky's direct-finiteness conjecture asserts that $ab=1$ implies $ba=1$ for $a,b\\in K[G]$, for every field $K$ and group $G$. The formalized results construct a finite field of characteristic two and a group algebra violating this implication. One statement records the counterexample for a finitely generated group; the more detailed construction gives a finitely presented group with an element of odd prime order.\n\nThe detailed construction also gives a precise recipe for choosing the data and proves that the required search terminates. The further conclusion that the group is nonsofic is outside these statements.\n\nA nonsingular integer matrix has absolute determinant at least $1$. The group-ring Determinant Conjecture extends this bound to matrices over $\\mathbb Z[G]$ for every discret","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/197.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":23678,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Counterexample-to-the-Group-Ring-Determinant-Conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-the-Group-Ring-Determinant-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex.tex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"197-04","slug":"A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Odd-Characteristic-September-26-2026","familyId":"197","title":"A Counterexample to Kaplansky's Direct-Finiteness Conjecture in Odd Characteristic","abstract":"We construct a counterexample to Kaplansky's direct-finiteness conjecture in odd characteristic. For one specified odd prime p, we obtain a field K of order p4, a finitely generated group G containing torsion, and finite sums $a,b\\in K[G]$ with $ab=1$ but $ba\\ne1$. The same elements define a cellular automaton on KG that is injective but not surjective.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a finitely presented torsion-free nonsofic group whose group algebra over 𝔽2 is not directly finite, disproving Kaplansky's conjecture even without torsion. Companion examples give injective nonsurjective cellular automata on all configurations, refuting Gottschalk's surjunctivity conjecture. Another counterexample is an integral group-ring matrix, invertible over the rational group ring, with Fuglede–Kadison determinant strictly between zero and one, disproving the unrestricted Determinant Conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Odd-Characteristic-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Odd-Characteristic-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Odd-Characteristic-September-26-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Odd-Characteristic-September-26-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Odd-Characteristic-September-26-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Odd-Characteristic-September-26-2026/build/sections/02-finite-data.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Odd-Characteristic-September-26-2026/build/sections/03-modular.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Odd-Characteristic-September-26-2026/build/sections/04-gluing.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Odd-Characteristic-September-26-2026/build/sections/04a-sector.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Odd-Characteristic-September-26-2026/build/sections/05-compression.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Odd-Characteristic-September-26-2026/build/sections/06-scalar-cellular.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Odd-Characteristic-September-26-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a counterexample to Kaplansky's direct-finiteness conjecture in odd characteristic. For one specified odd prime $p$, we obtain a field $K$ of order $p^4$, a finitely generated group $G$ containing torsion, and finite sums $a,b\\in K[G]$ with $ab=1$ but $ba\\ne1$. The same elements define a cellular automaton on $K^G$ that is injective but not surjective.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":413},{"kind":"section","title":"The field, the graph, and the local group","sourceOffset":9499},{"kind":"section","title":"Large idempotents with ordered annihilations","sourceOffset":13828},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The annihilated column space","sourceOffset":14685},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Many nonsingular diagonal pairings","sourceOffset":16678},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Realizing the projections in the group algebra","sourceOffset":20586},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From opposite annihilations to an ordered zero product","sourceOffset":23359},{"kind":"section","title":"Gluing the finite groups without collapsing them","sourceOffset":24969},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An injectivity criterion","sourceOffset":25580},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Application to the modular construction","sourceOffset":43131},{"kind":"section","title":"Realizing the local ranks in a common central sector","sourceOffset":45435},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Attaching the finite groups","sourceOffset":47753}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"op:main","text":"Put \\[ m=\\binom{1200}{600},\\qquad p=\\min\\{h\\in\\mathbb Z:h>1,\\ h\\mid(m!)^2+1\\}. \\] Then $p$ is an odd prime. There are a specified field $K$ of order $p^4$, a specified finitely generated group $G$ containing torsion, and specified finite sums $a,b\\in K[G]$ satisfying $ab=1$ and $ba\\ne1$.","sourceOffset":972,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"op:graph","text":"The graph $\\Gamma$ is triangle-free and has a specified fitting factorization $F_{ij}=\\sum_{T\\in\\mathcal T}\\alpha_i(T)\\beta_j(T)$ over $\\F_p$ with $t/m<1/100$.","sourceOffset":11765,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/197.md","scopeText":"Kaplansky's direct-finiteness conjecture asserts that $ab=1$ implies $ba=1$ for $a,b\\in K[G]$, for every field $K$ and group $G$. The formalized results construct a finite field of characteristic two and a group algebra violating this implication. One statement records the counterexample for a finitely generated group; the more detailed construction gives a finitely presented group with an element of odd prime order.\n\nThe detailed construction also gives a precise recipe for choosing the data and proves that the required search terminates. The further conclusion that the group is nonsofic is outside these statements.\n\nA nonsingular integer matrix has absolute determinant at least $1$. The group-ring Determinant Conjecture extends this bound to matrices over $\\mathbb Z[G]$ for every discret","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/197.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":77201,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Odd-Characteristic-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Odd-Characteristic-September-26-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex.tex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"198-01","slug":"An-algebra-of-infinite-little-finitistic-dimension-September-23-2026","familyId":"198","title":"An algebra of infinite little finitistic dimension","abstract":"We construct a finite-dimensional complex algebra whose finite-dimensional modules have unbounded finite projective dimensions. This disproves the little finitistic-dimension conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a finite-dimensional complex algebra whose finite-dimensional modules have unbounded finite projective dimensions. This disproves the little finitistic-dimension conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-algebra-of-infinite-little-finitistic-dimension-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-algebra-of-infinite-little-finitistic-dimension-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-algebra-of-infinite-little-finitistic-dimension-September-23-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/An-algebra-of-infinite-little-finitistic-dimension-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/An-algebra-of-infinite-little-finitistic-dimension-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/An-algebra-of-infinite-little-finitistic-dimension-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/An-algebra-of-infinite-little-finitistic-dimension-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-selection-process.tex","preprints/An-algebra-of-infinite-little-finitistic-dimension-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-localization-and-lifting.tex","preprints/An-algebra-of-infinite-little-finitistic-dimension-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-tensor-realization.tex","preprints/An-algebra-of-infinite-little-finitistic-dimension-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-ordinary-simulation.tex","preprints/An-algebra-of-infinite-little-finitistic-dimension-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-square-zero-and-conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-algebra-of-infinite-little-finitistic-dimension-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a finite-dimensional complex algebra whose finite-dimensional modules have unbounded finite projective dimensions. This disproves the little finitistic-dimension conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":232},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and consequences","sourceOffset":2214},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and its principal mechanisms","sourceOffset":5623},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":10556},{"kind":"section","title":"A selection process with unbounded extinction","sourceOffset":11366},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The group and its finite presentation","sourceOffset":13205},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Central involutions and an automorphism","sourceOffset":17667},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite quotients detecting independent involutions","sourceOffset":20385},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The algebra and its test modules","sourceOffset":23041},{"kind":"section","title":"Localization and a fixed lifted diagram","sourceOffset":25904},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A quadratic presentation and its directed encoding","sourceOffset":26757},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The quotient action and evaluation","sourceOffset":30541}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a finite-dimensional unital complex algebra \\(A\\) such that, for every integer \\(m\\ge1\\), there is a finite-dimensional left \\(A\\)-module \\(N_m\\) with \\[ 2m-2\\le\\pd_A N_m<\\infty. \\] In particular, \\(\\findim A=\\infty\\).","sourceOffset":1106,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Extreme left--right asymmetry","label":"cor:left-right-asymmetry","text":"There is a finite-dimensional unital complex algebra \\(\\Lambda\\) such that \\[ \\findim \\Lambda=\\Findim \\Lambda=\\infty, \\qquad \\findim(\\Lambda^{\\mathrm{op}})=\\Findim(\\Lambda^{\\mathrm{op}})=0. \\] All four dimensions here use left modules. Thus the little and big finitistic dimensions of \\(\\Lambda\\) are infinite on the left and zero on the right. Injective left \\(\\Lambda\\)-modules do not generate the unbounded derived category of all left \\(\\Lambda\\)-modules, whereas injective right \\(\\Lambda\\)-modules generate the unbounded derived category of all right \\(\\Lambda\\)-modules.","sourceOffset":4600,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/198.md","scopeText":"The little finitistic-dimension conjecture predicts that finitely generated modules of finite projective dimension over a fixed finite-dimensional algebra have bounded projective dimensions. The formalized counterexample is a finite-dimensional complex algebra with a finitely generated module of projective dimension at least $2m-2$ and still finite for every $m\\ge1$.\n\nA separate formalized result gives a finite-dimensional complex algebra whose little and big finitistic dimensions are infinite on the left and zero on the right. Injectives fail to generate its unbounded derived category on the left and generate it on the right.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/198.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":74911,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-algebra-of-infinite-little-finitistic-dimension-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"199-01","slug":"An-explicit-counterexample-to-the-Auslander-Reiten-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"199","title":"An explicit counterexample to the Auslander-Reiten conjecture","abstract":"We disprove the Auslander–Reiten conjecture for Artin algebras. We construct a finite-dimensional algebra Λ over $k=\\mathbb F_2(q,H_1,H_2)$ and a finite-dimensional nonprojective left module Z such that $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Ext}}\\nolimits ^i_\\Lambda(Z,Z)=\\mathop{\\mathrm{Ext}}\\nolimits ^i_\\Lambda(Z,\\Lambda)=0$ for every i > 0. The module is Gorenstein-projective, so the same example also disproves the Gorenstein-projective conjecture. Both counterexamples persist after every extension of k.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs finite-dimensional algebras over a characteristic-two rational-function field that disprove the Auslander–Reiten and Gorenstein-projective conjectures, and Tachikawa's second conjecture. An associated endomorphism algebra also disproves the classical, generalized and strong Nakayama conjectures, the Auslander–Gorenstein conjecture, and the Wakamatsu tilting conjecture. The counterexamples persist under every extension of the base field.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-explicit-counterexample-to-the-Auslander-Reiten-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-explicit-counterexample-to-the-Auslander-Reiten-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-explicit-counterexample-to-the-Auslander-Reiten-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/An-explicit-counterexample-to-the-Auslander-Reiten-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/An-explicit-counterexample-to-the-Auslander-Reiten-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/01-introduction.tex","preprints/An-explicit-counterexample-to-the-Auslander-Reiten-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/01-stable.tex","preprints/An-explicit-counterexample-to-the-Auslander-Reiten-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/02-conversion.tex","preprints/An-explicit-counterexample-to-the-Auslander-Reiten-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/03-algebra.tex","preprints/An-explicit-counterexample-to-the-Auslander-Reiten-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/04-resolution.tex","preprints/An-explicit-counterexample-to-the-Auslander-Reiten-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/05-cones.tex","preprints/An-explicit-counterexample-to-the-Auslander-Reiten-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/06-lift.tex","preprints/An-explicit-counterexample-to-the-Auslander-Reiten-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/07-branches.tex","preprints/An-explicit-counterexample-to-the-Auslander-Reiten-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/08-consequences.tex","preprints/An-explicit-counterexample-to-the-Auslander-Reiten-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/09-cochain.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-explicit-counterexample-to-the-Auslander-Reiten-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove the Auslander--Reiten conjecture for Artin algebras. We construct a finite-dimensional algebra $\\Lambda$ over $k=\\mathbb F_2(q,H_1,H_2)$ and a finite-dimensional nonprojective left module $Z$ such that $\\Ext^i_\\Lambda(Z,Z)=\\Ext^i_\\Lambda(Z,\\Lambda)=0$ for every $i>0$. The module is Gorenstein-projective, so the same example also disproves the Gorenstein-projective conjecture. Both counterexamples persist after every extension of $k$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":512},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier constructions and restricted results","sourceOffset":3816},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the construction removes self-extensions","sourceOffset":7655},{"kind":"section","title":"Stable maps and complete resolutions","sourceOffset":14560},{"kind":"section","title":"From two stable actions to a finite module","sourceOffset":18250},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The conversion principle","sourceOffset":18707},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison of complete resolutions","sourceOffset":20711},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof of the conversion principle","sourceOffset":25339},{"kind":"section","title":"A symmetric algebra and a degree-three class","sourceOffset":31765},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The ten-dimensional algebra and its trivial extension","sourceOffset":32142},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The bar class and the finite identities","sourceOffset":36759},{"kind":"section","title":"A simple module with polynomial self-extensions","sourceOffset":41000}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $\\kk=\\mathbb F_2(q,H_1,H_2)$, where $q,H_1,H_2$ are algebraically independent. There exist a finite-dimensional $\\kk$-algebra $\\Lambda$ and a finite-dimensional nonprojective left $\\Lambda$-module $Z$ such that \\[ \\Ext^i_\\Lambda(Z,Z)=0=\\Ext^i_\\Lambda(Z,\\Lambda) \\qquad\\text{for every integer }i\\geq1. \\] The module $Z$ is Gorenstein-projective. The semisimple quotient is $\\Lambda/\\rad\\Lambda\\simeq\\kk^8$, so the algebra is split with eight isomorphism classes of simple modules; also $\\rad(\\Lambda)^4\\ne0$. These conclusions persist after every field extension of $\\kk$.","sourceOffset":1769,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"lift:main","text":"For each $H\\in\\kk^\\times$, there is a stable bimodule map \\[ f_H:U_H\\longrightarrow\\mathcal C[3] \\] whose composite with the top-cell projection $\\mathcal C[3]\\to A[-1]$ is represented by the bimodule map $\\beta_H:U_H\\to\\Omega A\\subset P_0\\otimes P_0$ determined by \\[ \\beta_H(1)=\\left(\\sum_w h(w)\\otimes_I w^*\\right) \\otimes\\left(\\sum_w h(w)\\otimes_I w^*\\right), \\] where each sum is over the letter basis of $T$. Under the canonical identification $U_H\\otimes_A X\\simeq X$, its projection after evaluation sends \\[ 1\\longmapsto H^2(f^*\\otimes f^*) \\quad\\text{in}\\quad \\Omega X=\\rad(A)(f\\otimes f)\\subset A(f\\otimes f). \\] This evaluated map is nonzero modulo maps factoring through projectives. In particular, $f_HX$ is nonzero in $\\stHom{A}{X}{\\mathcal C X[3]}$.","sourceOffset":65090,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/199.md","scopeText":"The Auslander–Reiten conjecture predicts that a finitely generated module $M$ over an Artin algebra $A$ is projective if $\\mathrm{Ext}^i_A(M,M\\oplus A)=0$ for every $i>0$. The formalized counterexample is a finite-dimensional algebra over $k=\\mathbb F_2(q,H_1,H_2)$ and a finite-dimensional nonprojective Gorenstein-projective module with this vanishing.\n\nIt also establishes $A/\\mathrm{rad}\\,A\\cong k^8$, $(\\mathrm{rad}\\,A)^4\\ne0$, and preservation of the listed properties after every field extension. The Tachikawa companion is separate.\n\nTachikawa's second conjecture predicts that a finite-dimensional module over a finite-dimensional self-injective algebra is projective if all its positive-degree self-Ext groups vanish. The formalized counterexample gives a finite-dimensional symmetric algeb","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/199.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":93743,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-explicit-counterexample-to-the-Auslander-Reiten-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"199-02","slug":"A-counterexample-to-Tachikawas-second-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"199","title":"A counterexample to Tachikawa's second conjecture","abstract":"We disprove Tachikawa's second conjecture by constructing a finite-dimensional symmetric algebra over $k=\\mathbb F_2(q,H_1,H_2)$ with a finite-dimensional nonprojective module whose self-extension groups vanish in every positive degree. The associated endomorphism algebra also gives counterexamples to the classical, generalized, and strong Nakayama conjectures, the Auslander–Gorenstein conjecture, and the Wakamatsu tilting conjecture. These conclusions persist after every extension of k.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs finite-dimensional algebras over a characteristic-two rational-function field that disprove the Auslander–Reiten and Gorenstein-projective conjectures, and Tachikawa's second conjecture. An associated endomorphism algebra also disproves the classical, generalized and strong Nakayama conjectures, the Auslander–Gorenstein conjecture, and the Wakamatsu tilting conjecture. The counterexamples persist under every extension of the base field.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-counterexample-to-Tachikawas-second-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-counterexample-to-Tachikawas-second-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-counterexample-to-Tachikawas-second-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/A-counterexample-to-Tachikawas-second-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Tachikawas-second-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Tachikawas-second-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/homological.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Tachikawas-second-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/transfer.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Tachikawas-second-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/algebra.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Tachikawas-second-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/stable.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Tachikawas-second-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/bimodule.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Tachikawas-second-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/triangular.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Tachikawas-second-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-counterexample-to-Tachikawas-second-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove Tachikawa's second conjecture by constructing a finite-dimensional symmetric algebra over $k=\\mathbb F_2(q,H_1,H_2)$ with a finite-dimensional nonprojective module whose self-extension groups vanish in every positive degree. The associated endomorphism algebra also gives counterexamples to the classical, generalized, and strong Nakayama conjectures, the Auslander--Gorenstein conjecture, and the Wakamatsu tilting conjecture. These conclusions persist after every extension of $k$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":541},{"kind":"section","title":"Homological preliminaries","sourceOffset":18296},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Complete resolutions and stable duality","sourceOffset":19861},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Projective models and their stable images","sourceOffset":26886},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bimodule evaluation","sourceOffset":36239},{"kind":"section","title":"Passing to a symmetric algebra","sourceOffset":40889},{"kind":"section","title":"The ten-dimensional algebra","sourceOffset":46038},{"kind":"section","title":"Polynomial stable endomorphisms and twists","sourceOffset":57210},{"kind":"section","title":"Derived induction and the trace obstruction","sourceOffset":69576},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite representative of derived induction","sourceOffset":71049},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The stable Hom profile in every degree","sourceOffset":75193},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From perfect factorizations to an ordinary trace","sourceOffset":82424}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\[ k=\\mathbb F_2(q,H_1,H_2), \\] where $q,H_1,H_2$ are algebraically independent. There exist a finite-dimensional associative unital symmetric $k$-algebra $A$ and a finite-dimensional unital left $A$-module $M$ such that $M$ is nonprojective and \\[ \\Ext_A^i(M,M)=0\\qquad\\text{for every integer }i>0. \\] In particular, Tachikawa's second conjecture is false.","sourceOffset":1699,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Homological conjectures","label":"cor:homological","text":"Let $A,M$ be as in Theorem \\ref{thm:main}. For every field extension $K/k$, put $A_K=K\\otimes_k A$, $M_K=K\\otimes_k M$, and \\[ \\Gamma_K=\\End_{A_K}(A_K\\oplus M_K)^{\\mathrm{op}}. \\] Then $\\Gamma_K$ is a finite-dimensional $K$-algebra with the following properties, in the category of finite left $\\Gamma_K$-modules. \\begin{enumerate} \\item Every term of the minimal injective resolution of $\\Gamma_K$ is projective, but $\\idim_{\\Gamma_K}\\Gamma_K=\\infty$. \\item There is a nonzero simple module $S$ such that $\\Ext_{\\Gamma_K}^i(S,\\Gamma_K)=0$ for every $i\\geq0$. \\item There is a projective-injective Wakamatsu tilting module $W$ which is not tilting. \\end{enumerate} Consequently the classical, generalized, and strong Nakayama conjectures, the Auslander--Gorenstein conjecture, and the Wakamatsu tilting conjecture are false for finite-dimensional algebras over $K$.","sourceOffset":13570,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/199.md","scopeText":"The Auslander–Reiten conjecture predicts that a finitely generated module $M$ over an Artin algebra $A$ is projective if $\\mathrm{Ext}^i_A(M,M\\oplus A)=0$ for every $i>0$. The formalized counterexample is a finite-dimensional algebra over $k=\\mathbb F_2(q,H_1,H_2)$ and a finite-dimensional nonprojective Gorenstein-projective module with this vanishing.\n\nIt also establishes $A/\\mathrm{rad}\\,A\\cong k^8$, $(\\mathrm{rad}\\,A)^4\\ne0$, and preservation of the listed properties after every field extension. The Tachikawa companion is separate.\n\nTachikawa's second conjecture predicts that a finite-dimensional module over a finite-dimensional self-injective algebra is projective if all its positive-degree self-Ext groups vanish. The formalized counterexample gives a finite-dimensional symmetric algeb","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/199.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":123802,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-counterexample-to-Tachikawas-second-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"200-01","slug":"The-Artinian-Lex-Plus-Powers-Betti-Theorem-September-23-2026","familyId":"200","title":"The Artinian Lex-Plus-Powers Betti Theorem","abstract":"We prove the Eisenbud–Green–Harris and lex-plus-powers conjectures over every characteristic-zero field for homogeneous regular sequences of any positive length, with degrees at least two. For every homogeneous ideal containing such a sequence, the corresponding lex-plus-powers ideal has the same Hilbert function and at least as large a graded Betti number in every homological and internal degree.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the Eisenbud–Green–Harris and lex-plus-powers conjectures over every characteristic-zero field. Any homogeneous ideal containing a regular sequence, of arbitrary length and degrees at least two, admits a lex-plus-powers ideal with the same Hilbert function and no smaller graded Betti numbers.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Artinian-Lex-Plus-Powers-Betti-Theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Artinian-Lex-Plus-Powers-Betti-Theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Artinian-Lex-Plus-Powers-Betti-Theorem-September-23-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/The-Artinian-Lex-Plus-Powers-Betti-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Artinian-Lex-Plus-Powers-Betti-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-Artinian-Lex-Plus-Powers-Betti-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-forms.tex","preprints/The-Artinian-Lex-Plus-Powers-Betti-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-filtered.tex","preprints/The-Artinian-Lex-Plus-Powers-Betti-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-monomial.tex","preprints/The-Artinian-Lex-Plus-Powers-Betti-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-stabilization.tex","preprints/The-Artinian-Lex-Plus-Powers-Betti-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-boxes.tex","preprints/The-Artinian-Lex-Plus-Powers-Betti-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-tor.tex","preprints/The-Artinian-Lex-Plus-Powers-Betti-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/figures/endpoint-slices.tex","preprints/The-Artinian-Lex-Plus-Powers-Betti-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-assembly.tex","preprints/The-Artinian-Lex-Plus-Powers-Betti-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-characteristic-zero.tex","preprints/The-Artinian-Lex-Plus-Powers-Betti-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Artinian-Lex-Plus-Powers-Betti-Theorem-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Eisenbud--Green--Harris and lex-plus-powers conjectures over every characteristic-zero field for homogeneous regular sequences of any positive length, with degrees at least two. For every homogeneous ideal containing such a sequence, the corresponding lex-plus-powers ideal has the same Hilbert function and at least as large a graded Betti number in every homological and internal degree.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":465},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and previous results","sourceOffset":4853},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The reduction that carries the Betti numbers","sourceOffset":8029},{"kind":"section","title":"Commuting forms with division coefficients","sourceOffset":11739},{"kind":"section","title":"Graded resolutions and leading coefficient spaces","sourceOffset":13901},{"kind":"section","title":"A monomial bound containing the prescribed powers","sourceOffset":23710},{"kind":"section","title":"Stabilization inside the exponent box","sourceOffset":32533},{"kind":"section","title":"Shadows and end slices in an exponent box","sourceOffset":40757},{"kind":"section","title":"Betti bounds for stable ideals","sourceOffset":51272},{"kind":"section","title":"The prescribed lex-plus-powers ideal","sourceOffset":62321},{"kind":"section","title":"Arbitrary regular-sequence length and characteristic-zero fields","sourceOffset":64491}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every \\(n\\ge1\\), every ordered degree sequence \\(2\\le a_1\\le\\cdots\\le a_n\\), and every homogeneous ideal \\(I\\subseteq\\C[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]\\) containing a regular sequence of these degrees, the spaces in \\eqref{eq:prescription} exist and \\(J=\\bigoplus_{d\\ge0}J_d\\) is a homogeneous ideal. For this single ideal, \\[ \\beta^S_{p,j}(S/I)\\le\\beta^S_{p,j}(S/J) \\qquad\\text{for every \\(p\\ge0\\) and \\(j\\in\\Z\\)}. \\] There is no restriction on the degrees or number of the additional generators of \\(I\\).","sourceOffset":2173,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"stab:main","text":"Let $S=\\C[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]$, let $2\\le a_1\\le\\cdots\\le a_n$, and put $P=(x_1^{a_1},\\ldots,x_n^{a_n})$. For every monomial ideal $K\\supseteq P$, there is a monomial ideal $H\\supseteq P$ stable in the box such that \\[ \\dim_\\C(S/H)_d=\\dim_\\C(S/K)_d\\quad(d\\in\\Z) \\] and \\[ \\beta^S_{p,j}(S/K)\\le\\beta^S_{p,j}(S/H) \\qquad(p\\ge0,\\ j\\in\\Z). \\]","sourceOffset":37936,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"stab:definition","text":"A subset $X\\subseteq\\mathcal B_r(d)$ is stable if \\[ u\\in X,\\quad h<\\ell,\\quad u+e_h-e_\\ell\\in\\mathcal B_r(d) \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u+e_h-e_\\ell\\in X. \\] A monomial ideal containing $(x_1^{a_1},\\ldots,x_r^{a_r})$ is stable in the box if its included exponent vectors in $\\mathcal B_r(d)$ form a stable set for every $d$. This definition imposes no transfer condition on monomials outside the box.","sourceOffset":33270,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":73727,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Artinian-Lex-Plus-Powers-Betti-Theorem-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"200-02","slug":"Commuting-Division-Coefficient-Forms-and-the-Artinian-Eisenbud-Green-Harris-Conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"200","title":"Commuting Division-Coefficient Forms and the Artinian Eisenbud--Green--Harris Conjecture","abstract":"We prove the Eisenbud–Green–Harris conjecture over every characteristic-zero field for homogeneous regular sequences of any positive length, with degrees at least two. Thus every homogeneous ideal containing such a sequence has the Hilbert function of a monomial ideal containing the corresponding pure powers.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the Eisenbud–Green–Harris and lex-plus-powers conjectures over every characteristic-zero field. Any homogeneous ideal containing a regular sequence, of arbitrary length and degrees at least two, admits a lex-plus-powers ideal with the same Hilbert function and no smaller graded Betti numbers.","pdfPath":"preprints/Commuting-Division-Coefficient-Forms-and-the-Artinian-Eisenbud-Green-Harris-Conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Commuting-Division-Coefficient-Forms-and-the-Artinian-Eisenbud-Green-Harris-Conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Commuting-Division-Coefficient-Forms-and-the-Artinian-Eisenbud-Green-Harris-Conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/Commuting-Division-Coefficient-Forms-and-the-Artinian-Eisenbud-Green-Harris-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Commuting-Division-Coefficient-Forms-and-the-Artinian-Eisenbud-Green-Harris-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Commuting-Division-Coefficient-Forms-and-the-Artinian-Eisenbud-Green-Harris-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-reduction.tex","preprints/Commuting-Division-Coefficient-Forms-and-the-Artinian-Eisenbud-Green-Harris-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-low-relations.tex","preprints/Commuting-Division-Coefficient-Forms-and-the-Artinian-Eisenbud-Green-Harris-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-parameters.tex","preprints/Commuting-Division-Coefficient-Forms-and-the-Artinian-Eisenbud-Green-Harris-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-curves.tex","preprints/Commuting-Division-Coefficient-Forms-and-the-Artinian-Eisenbud-Green-Harris-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-descent.tex","preprints/Commuting-Division-Coefficient-Forms-and-the-Artinian-Eisenbud-Green-Harris-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-index.tex","preprints/Commuting-Division-Coefficient-Forms-and-the-Artinian-Eisenbud-Green-Harris-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-assembly.tex","preprints/Commuting-Division-Coefficient-Forms-and-the-Artinian-Eisenbud-Green-Harris-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/09-consequences.tex","preprints/Commuting-Division-Coefficient-Forms-and-the-Artinian-Eisenbud-Green-Harris-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Commuting-Division-Coefficient-Forms-and-the-Artinian-Eisenbud-Green-Harris-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Eisenbud--Green--Harris conjecture over every characteristic-zero field for homogeneous regular sequences of any positive length, with degrees at least two. Thus every homogeneous ideal containing such a sequence has the Hilbert function of a monomial ideal containing the corresponding pure powers.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":426},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier results on EGH","sourceOffset":6680},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and its methods","sourceOffset":8364},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and standard tools","sourceOffset":15507},{"kind":"section","title":"Division coefficients and monomial ideals","sourceOffset":17903},{"kind":"section","title":"Low relations and simple factors","sourceOffset":24827},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The induction data","sourceOffset":25775},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The equations for the next row","sourceOffset":27403},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Starting with generic square roots","sourceOffset":34599},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Preserving the geometric condition","sourceOffset":42756},{"kind":"section","title":"The parameter complete intersections","sourceOffset":44970},{"kind":"section","title":"Curves realizing binary power addition","sourceOffset":59340}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"corollary","name":"Artinian Eisenbud--Green--Harris","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $n\\ge1$ and $2\\le a_1\\le\\cdots\\le a_n$. Suppose that a homogeneous ideal $I\\subseteq\\C[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]$ contains a regular sequence $f_1,\\ldots,f_n$ with $\\deg f_i=a_i$. There is a monomial ideal $J\\subseteq\\C[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]$ such that \\[ x_i^{a_i}\\in J\\quad(1\\le i\\le n), \\qquad H_{S/J}(d)=H_{S/I}(d)\\quad\\text{for every }d\\ge0. \\]","sourceOffset":4009,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Commuting-form construction","label":"thm:construction","text":"Let $n\\ge1$, let $2\\le a_1\\le\\cdots\\le a_n$, and let $f_1,\\ldots,f_n$ be a homogeneous regular sequence in $\\C[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]$ with $\\deg f_i=a_i$. There exist a finitely generated field extension $k/\\C$, a finite-dimensional central division $k$-algebra $\\Delta$, and a commutative graded $k$-subalgebra \\[ k[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]\\subseteq A\\subseteq\\Delta[x_1,\\ldots,x_n] \\] generated by finitely many pairwise commuting linear forms, with distinguished $t_1,\\ldots,t_n\\in A_1$, such that \\begin{equation} t_i^{a_i}=c_i f_i+\\sum_{l<i}B_{il}f_l+\\sum_{d>i}C_{id}t_d \\qquad(1\\le i\\le n). \\end{equation} Here $c_i\\in k^\\times$, $B_{il}\\in A_{a_i-a_l}$, and $C_{id}\\in A_{a_i-1}$. Moreover, $A$ is finite over $k[x]$, and \\[ \\{t_1^{\\alpha_1}\\cdots t_n^{\\alpha_n}: \\alpha\\in\\Z_{\\ge0}^n\\} \\] is a basis of the full polynomial algebra $\\Delta[x]$ as a left $\\Delta$-vector space.","sourceOffset":2652,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":149008,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Commuting-Division-Coefficient-Forms-and-the-Artinian-Eisenbud-Green-Harris-Conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"201-01","slug":"A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026","familyId":"201","title":"A Counterexample to Kurosh’s Division-Ring Problem","abstract":"We construct a countable division ring of characteristic zero that is algebraic over its center, generated by two elements as an algebra over that center, and infinite-dimensional over it. This gives a negative answer to the Kurosh problem for division rings.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a countable characteristic-zero division ring that is algebraic over its center and generated by two elements over that center, but has infinite dimension over it. This answers Kurosh's division-ring problem on local finiteness negatively.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/lifting.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/build/figures/response-interfaces.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/application.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/formal-generic.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/responses.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/transfer.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/linkage.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/global-defect.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/cyclic-obstruction.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/build/figures/repeated-leaves-hubs.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/correction.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/build/figures/eleven-block-offsets.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/division-realization.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/generation.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a countable division ring of characteristic zero that is algebraic over its center, generated by two elements as an algebra over that center, and infinite-dimensional over it. This gives a negative answer to the Kurosh problem for division rings.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":358},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":1785},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction","sourceOffset":4488},{"kind":"section","title":"The lifting theorem","sourceOffset":7992},{"kind":"section","title":"The countable construction and the actual center","sourceOffset":13967},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Scheduling all polynomial expressions","sourceOffset":14890},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A two-generated algebraic division ring","sourceOffset":16818},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Infinite dimension and passage to the center","sourceOffset":18886},{"kind":"section","title":"Formal evaluations and generic spectra","sourceOffset":21854},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Convergence and specialization","sourceOffset":24548},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Generic division evaluations","sourceOffset":31364},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Nonconstant roots and stable multiplicity","sourceOffset":35065}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a countable division ring $D$ of characteristic zero, with center $F=Z(D)$, and elements $x,y\\in D$ such that \\[ D=F[x,y]=F(x,y),\\qquad [D:F]=\\infty, \\] while every element of $D$ is algebraic over $F$.","sourceOffset":1165,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Algebraic lifting","label":"thm:lifting","text":"Let $k$ be a countable field of characteristic zero containing every root of unity, and let $A$ be a finite-dimensional central division algebra over $k$. Suppose $X\\in A\\langle\\!\\langle z_1,z_2\\rangle\\!\\rangle^u$ satisfies: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item $X_0$ generates $A$ as a $k$-algebra; \\item $X_1,X_2$ have linearly independent classes in $A^u/J_A(X_0)$. \\end{enumerate} For every $w\\in A\\langle\\!\\langle z_1,z_2\\rangle\\!\\rangle$, there exist a countable field extension $k'/k$, a central division algebra $A'/k'$ of strictly larger degree, and series \\[ X'\\in A'\\langle\\!\\langle s_1,s_2\\rangle\\!\\rangle^u, \\qquad w'\\in A'\\langle\\!\\langle s_1,s_2\\rangle\\!\\rangle \\] with the following properties: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\alph*)}] \\item Every noncommutative polynomial identity over $k$ satisfied by the tuple $(X,w)$ is satisfied by $(X',w')$ as an identity of formal series. \\item The constant tuple $X'_0$ generates $A'$ over $k'$, and the classes of $X'_1,X'_2$ in $(A')^u/J_{A'}(X'_0)$ are linearly independent. \\item There is a monic nonzero polynomial $q\\in k'[t]$ such that $q(w')=0$. \\end{enumerate} Here the degree of a central division algebra $B/F$ is $\\sqrt{[B:F]}$.","sourceOffset":9168,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":151448,"sourceFileCount":17,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kuroshs-Division-Ring-Problem-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex.tex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"202-01","slug":"The-Blockwise-Alperin-Weight-Conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"202","title":"The Blockwise Alperin Weight Conjecture","abstract":"We prove the numerical blockwise Alperin weight conjecture for every finite group and every prime p. For each p-block B, the number of irreducible Brauer characters in B equals the number of conjugacy classes of B-weights.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the numerical blockwise Alperin weight conjecture for every prime and every finite group: the number of irreducible Brauer characters in a block equals the number of conjugacy classes of its weights.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Blockwise-Alperin-Weight-Conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Blockwise-Alperin-Weight-Conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Blockwise-Alperin-Weight-Conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/The-Blockwise-Alperin-Weight-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Blockwise-Alperin-Weight-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/intro.tex","preprints/The-Blockwise-Alperin-Weight-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/proof-interfaces.tex","preprints/The-Blockwise-Alperin-Weight-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/foundations.tex","preprints/The-Blockwise-Alperin-Weight-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/group.tex","preprints/The-Blockwise-Alperin-Weight-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/twist.tex","preprints/The-Blockwise-Alperin-Weight-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/differentials.tex","preprints/The-Blockwise-Alperin-Weight-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/valuations.tex","preprints/The-Blockwise-Alperin-Weight-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/field-tower.tex","preprints/The-Blockwise-Alperin-Weight-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/specialization.tex","preprints/The-Blockwise-Alperin-Weight-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Blockwise-Alperin-Weight-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the numerical blockwise Alperin weight conjecture for every finite group and every prime $p$. For each $p$-block $B$, the number of irreducible Brauer characters in $B$ equals the number of conjugacy classes of $B$-weights.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":338},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local--global consequences","sourceOffset":3554},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work and the proof's ingredients","sourceOffset":5299},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The mechanism of the proof","sourceOffset":9594},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":20016},{"kind":"section","title":"Classical foundations used","sourceOffset":20717},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coefficient fields and scalar extension","sourceOffset":32642},{"kind":"section","title":"From block counts to generating tuples","sourceOffset":35498},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coefficient projection and the weight transform","sourceOffset":37140},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cocenters and subsections","sourceOffset":45778},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two cancellations on chain orbits","sourceOffset":52012},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Detecting defect zero by central elements","sourceOffset":55340}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every prime $p$, every finite group $G$, and every $p$-block $B$ of $G$, \\[ l(B)=|W_p(B)|. \\]","sourceOffset":3052,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Local--global and principal-block bounds","label":"cor:block-consequences","text":"Let $p$ be a prime, $G$ a finite group, $B$ a $p$-block with defect group $D$, and $b$ its Brauer correspondent in $N_G(D)$. Then \\[ l(B)\\geq l(b),\\qquad D\\text{ abelian}\\Longrightarrow l(B)=l(b). \\] For $P\\in\\operatorname{Syl}_p(G)$, one also has \\[ |\\IBr_p(G)|\\geq|\\IBr_p(N_G(P))|. \\] Let $B_0$ be the principal $p$-block of $G$, and let $k_p(G)$ count the conjugacy classes of $p$-power-order elements, including the identity. Then \\[ \\begin{aligned} k_p(G)=3&\\Longrightarrow l(B_0)\\geq (p-1)/2,\\\\ p\\mid |G|&\\Longrightarrow l(B_0)\\geq 2\\sqrt{p-1}+1-k_p(G). \\end{aligned} \\]","sourceOffset":3786,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Chains","label":"def:p-chains","text":"A chain in $X$ is a strictly increasing sequence of $p$-subgroups \\[ \\mathcal C=(1=Q_0<Q_1<\\cdots<Q_r), \\qquad |\\mathcal C|=r. \\] The length-zero chain $(1)$ is included. Set \\[ H_{\\mathcal C}=\\bigcap_{i=0}^rN_X(Q_i), \\qquad E_{\\mathcal C}=\\operatorname{Br}_{Q_r}(E). \\] Call $\\mathcal C$ normal if every $Q_i$ is normal in $Q_r$.","sourceOffset":42990,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":212275,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":15,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Blockwise-Alperin-Weight-Conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/The-Blockwise-Alperin-Weight-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"203-01","slug":"Donovans-Conjecture-over-Algebraic-Closures-of-Prime-Fields-September-24-2026","familyId":"203","title":"Donovan's Conjecture over Algebraically Closed Fields","abstract":"We prove Donovan's conjecture over every algebraically closed field K of characteristic p > 0. For each fixed K and bound on defect-group order, blocks of finite groups represent only finitely many K-linear Morita equivalence classes. The defect groups need not be abelian, and the result includes p = 2.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Donovan's conjecture: over each fixed algebraically closed field of characteristic p, blocks of finite groups with bounded defect-group order have only finitely many Morita-equivalence classes, for every prime p. Also proves the integral form over each fixed complete mixed-characteristic discrete valuation ring with algebraically closed residue field.","pdfPath":"preprints/Donovans-Conjecture-over-Algebraic-Closures-of-Prime-Fields-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Donovans-Conjecture-over-Algebraic-Closures-of-Prime-Fields-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Donovans-Conjecture-over-Algebraic-Closures-of-Prime-Fields-September-24-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/Donovans-Conjecture-over-Algebraic-Closures-of-Prime-Fields-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Donovans-Conjecture-over-Algebraic-Closures-of-Prime-Fields-September-24-2026/build/common.tex","preprints/Donovans-Conjecture-over-Algebraic-Closures-of-Prime-Fields-September-24-2026/build/sections/00_introduction.tex","preprints/Donovans-Conjecture-over-Algebraic-Closures-of-Prime-Fields-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-interfaces.tex","preprints/Donovans-Conjecture-over-Algebraic-Closures-of-Prime-Fields-September-24-2026/build/sections/01_preliminaries.tex","preprints/Donovans-Conjecture-over-Algebraic-Closures-of-Prime-Fields-September-24-2026/build/sections/02_geometry.tex","preprints/Donovans-Conjecture-over-Algebraic-Closures-of-Prime-Fields-September-24-2026/build/sections/03_families.tex","preprints/Donovans-Conjecture-over-Algebraic-Closures-of-Prime-Fields-September-24-2026/build/sections/04_labels.tex","preprints/Donovans-Conjecture-over-Algebraic-Closures-of-Prime-Fields-September-24-2026/build/sections/05_runners.tex","preprints/Donovans-Conjecture-over-Algebraic-Closures-of-Prime-Fields-September-24-2026/build/sections/06_endpoint.tex","preprints/Donovans-Conjecture-over-Algebraic-Closures-of-Prime-Fields-September-24-2026/build/sections/07_extensions.tex","preprints/Donovans-Conjecture-over-Algebraic-Closures-of-Prime-Fields-September-24-2026/build/sections/08_periodicity.tex","preprints/Donovans-Conjecture-over-Algebraic-Closures-of-Prime-Fields-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Donovans-Conjecture-over-Algebraic-Closures-of-Prime-Fields-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Donovan's conjecture over every algebraically closed field \\(K\\) of characteristic \\(p>0\\). For each fixed \\(K\\) and bound on defect-group order, blocks of finite groups represent only finitely many \\(K\\)-linear Morita equivalence classes. The defect groups need not be abelian, and the result includes \\(p=2\\).","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":367},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Previous work","sourceOffset":2747},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two uniformity problems","sourceOffset":5255},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its new ingredients","sourceOffset":7482},{"kind":"section","title":"Block-theoretic bounds and transfer","sourceOffset":15127},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Central quotients and ordinary coordinates","sourceOffset":18142},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Crossed algebras and upward Cartan transfer","sourceOffset":22777},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Restriction and decomposition rows","sourceOffset":28668},{"kind":"section","title":"A Cartan bound for fixed root data","sourceOffset":30966},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Strata and perfect extraordinary restrictions","sourceOffset":34317},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniformity without a bound on the finite torus","sourceOffset":42864},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Gluing with a bounded number of standard factors","sourceOffset":56857}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Donovan's conjecture","label":"thm:donovan","text":"Fix a prime \\(p\\), an algebraically closed field \\(K\\) of characteristic \\(p\\), and a positive integer \\(M\\). As \\(G\\) ranges over finite groups and \\(b\\) over block idempotents of \\(KG\\) with defect groups of order at most \\(M\\), only finitely many \\(K\\)-linear Morita equivalence classes of \\(KGb\\) occur. In particular, over this field \\(K\\), Donovan's conjecture holds for every finite \\(p\\)-group, including nonabelian groups.","sourceOffset":1276,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Fixed-root Cartan bound","label":"thm:geometric-cartan","text":"Fix a finite collection \\(\\mathcal R\\) of root data and a finite collection of the usual pinned diagram automorphisms and exceptional diagram isogenies on these data. There is a constant \\(C=C(\\mathcal R,p,M)\\), also depending on this fixed collection of maps, with the following property. Let \\(\\mathbf G\\) be a connected reductive group over \\(\\overline{\\F}_r\\), with root datum in \\(\\mathcal R\\), and let \\(F\\) be a Steinberg endomorphism which, after an isomorphism of rational structures, has the form \\[ F=\\theta\\operatorname{Fr}_Q. \\] Here \\(Q\\) is a power of \\(r\\), \\(\\operatorname{Fr}_Q\\) is split Frobenius, and \\(\\theta\\) belongs to the specified finite collection; exceptional isogenies are allowed only in their prescribed defining characteristics. Put \\(\\Gamma=\\mathbf G^F\\). For every subgroup \\(Z\\le Z(\\Gamma)\\) and every block \\(b\\) of \\(k[\\Gamma/Z]\\) with defect groups of order at most \\(M\\), all entries of the Cartan matrix of \\(b\\) are at most \\(C\\).","sourceOffset":31301,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"The comparison property","label":"def:ext:comparison-data","text":"For the data of Lemmas \\ref{lem:ext:partial-extension} and \\ref{lem:ext:dual-recovery}, let $S\\leq Y_0$ be a $p$-subgroup contained in the pure subgroup $X\\leq\\Xi\\rtimes X$. The comparison property is the existence of a positive integer $m$, bounded in terms of $p,M$, and an integral $(\\sigma^m\\bar B,\\bar B)$-Morita bimodule $\\mathcal M$ with the following additional structure on $M_0=k\\otimes_{\\cO}\\mathcal M$. There are invertible $k$-linear maps $J_x:M_0\\to M_0$, $x\\in S$, such that, writing $\\alpha'_x=\\sigma^m(\\alpha_x)$ and $a'_{xy}=\\sigma^m(a_{xy})$, one has \\begin{align} J_x(avb)&=\\alpha","sourceOffset":280138,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":358341,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":19,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Donovans-Conjecture-over-Algebraic-Closures-of-Prime-Fields-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Donovans-Conjecture-over-Algebraic-Closures-of-Prime-Fields-September-24-2026/build/common.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"203-02","slug":"Integral-Donovan-Finiteness-over-Witt-Vectors-September-25-2026","familyId":"203","title":"Integral Donovan Finiteness over Witt Vectors","abstract":"We prove integral Donovan finiteness: for every prime p and positive integer M, the blocks of all finite groups with defect groups of order at most M have only finitely many Morita equivalence classes over $W(\\overline{\\mathbb F}_p)$. The defect groups need not be abelian, and the result includes p = 2. The same bounded-defect finiteness holds over each fixed complete discrete valuation ring of characteristic zero with algebraically closed residue field of characteristic p, including ramified rings.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Donovan's conjecture: over each fixed algebraically closed field of characteristic p, blocks of finite groups with bounded defect-group order have only finitely many Morita-equivalence classes, for every prime p. Also proves the integral form over each fixed complete mixed-characteristic discrete valuation ring with algebraically closed residue field.","pdfPath":"preprints/Integral-Donovan-Finiteness-over-Witt-Vectors-September-25-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Integral-Donovan-Finiteness-over-Witt-Vectors-September-25-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Integral-Donovan-Finiteness-over-Witt-Vectors-September-25-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/Integral-Donovan-Finiteness-over-Witt-Vectors-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Integral-Donovan-Finiteness-over-Witt-Vectors-September-25-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Integral-Donovan-Finiteness-over-Witt-Vectors-September-25-2026/build/sections/01_introduction.tex","preprints/Integral-Donovan-Finiteness-over-Witt-Vectors-September-25-2026/build/sections/02_inputs.tex","preprints/Integral-Donovan-Finiteness-over-Witt-Vectors-September-25-2026/build/sections/03_rigidity.tex","preprints/Integral-Donovan-Finiteness-over-Witt-Vectors-September-25-2026/build/sections/04_comparison.tex","preprints/Integral-Donovan-Finiteness-over-Witt-Vectors-September-25-2026/build/sections/05_sylow.tex","preprints/Integral-Donovan-Finiteness-over-Witt-Vectors-September-25-2026/build/sections/06_kernel.tex","preprints/Integral-Donovan-Finiteness-over-Witt-Vectors-September-25-2026/build/sections/07_assembly.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Integral-Donovan-Finiteness-over-Witt-Vectors-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove integral Donovan finiteness: for every prime $p$ and positive integer $M$, the blocks of all finite groups with defect groups of order at most $M$ have only finitely many Morita equivalence classes over $W(\\overline{\\mathbb F}_p)$. The defect groups need not be abelian, and the result includes $p=2$. The same bounded-defect finiteness holds over each fixed complete discrete valuation ring of characteristic zero with algebraically closed residue field of characteristic $p$, including ramified rings.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":707},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finiteness criteria and extension methods","sourceOffset":3447},{"kind":"subsection","title":"What must be retained over the Witt ring","sourceOffset":6856},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and dependencies","sourceOffset":10244},{"kind":"section","title":"Integral orders and the imported block constructions","sourceOffset":11236},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Orders, Frobenius and the numerical criterion","sourceOffset":11648},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Multiplication factors and identity markings","sourceOffset":14267},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reduction and dual recovery","sourceOffset":15686},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The companion's comparison data","sourceOffset":20449},{"kind":"section","title":"Integral orbits and gradings of a fixed order","sourceOffset":21671},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Integral orbit finiteness","sourceOffset":22298},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Block rigidity and labelled gradings","sourceOffset":27036}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $p$ be a prime, let $\\cO=W(\\overline{\\F}_p)$, and let $M\\geq1$ be an integer. As $G$ ranges over all finite groups and $b$ ranges over all blocks of $\\cO G$ whose defect groups have order at most $M$, the algebras $\\cO G b$ belong to only finitely many $\\cO$-linear Morita equivalence classes.","sourceOffset":1607,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Fixed complete coefficient rings","label":"cor:complete-dvr","text":"Let $p$ be a prime, let $\\mathcal R$ be a fixed complete discrete valuation ring of characteristic zero whose residue field $\\ell$ is algebraically closed of characteristic $p$, and let $M\\geq1$ be an integer. As $G$ ranges over all finite groups and $b$ ranges over primitive central idempotents of $\\mathcal R G$ for which the residue block $\\ell G\\bar b$ has a defect group of order at most $M$, the algebras $\\mathcal R G b$ belong to only finitely many $\\mathcal R$-linear Morita equivalence classes.","sourceOffset":2533,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Crossed orders and based isomorphisms","label":"eq:action-law","text":"Let $R$ be an $\\cO$-order and $Q$ a finite group. A normalized crossed system consists of $\\alpha_q\\in\\Aut_{\\cO}(R)$ and $a_{q,t}\\in R^\\times$ such that \\begin{align} \\alpha_q\\alpha_t&=\\ad(a_{q,t})\\alpha_{qt}, \\\\ a_{q,t}a_{qt,v}&=\\alpha_q(a_{t,v})a_{q,tv}, \\end{align} with $\\alpha_1=\\id$ and $a_{1,q}=a_{q,1}=1$. Its crossed order is $A=\\bigoplus_{q\\in Q}Ru_q$, with \\[ u_qr=\\alpha_q(r)u_q,\\qquad u_qu_t=a_{q,t}u_{qt},\\qquad u_1=1. \\] A $Q$-labelled graded isomorphism preserves each homogeneous component with its label. When the identity component is identified with a fixed $R$, such an isomorphi","sourceOffset":14326,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":74588,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Integral-Donovan-Finiteness-over-Witt-Vectors-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Integral-Donovan-Finiteness-over-Witt-Vectors-September-25-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"204-01","slug":"Tensor-Saturation-for-Even-Spin-Groups-September-24-2026","familyId":"204","title":"Tensor saturation for even spin groups","abstract":"We prove the saturation conjecture for $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Spin}}\\nolimits (2n)$, n ≥ 2. If three dominant integral weights sum to an element of the root lattice, then the existence of a nonzero tensor invariant after a positive integral dilation implies the existence of one at the original weights.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves saturation factor one for $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Spin}}\\nolimits (2n)$, n ≥ 2: for three dominant integral weights whose sum lies in the root lattice, an invariant at any common positive integral dilation already gives an invariant at the original weights. This resolves the type-D part of the simply-laced saturation conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/Tensor-Saturation-for-Even-Spin-Groups-September-24-2026/Tensor-Saturation-for-Even-Spin-Groups-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Tensor-Saturation-for-Even-Spin-Groups-September-24-2026/Tensor-Saturation-for-Even-Spin-Groups-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Tensor-Saturation-for-Even-Spin-Groups-September-24-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/Tensor-Saturation-for-Even-Spin-Groups-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Tensor-Saturation-for-Even-Spin-Groups-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Tensor-Saturation-for-Even-Spin-Groups-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-real-schedules.tex","preprints/Tensor-Saturation-for-Even-Spin-Groups-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-lattice-continuation.tex","preprints/Tensor-Saturation-for-Even-Spin-Groups-September-24-2026/build/figures/half-relation.tex","preprints/Tensor-Saturation-for-Even-Spin-Groups-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-moment-knots.tex","preprints/Tensor-Saturation-for-Even-Spin-Groups-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-columns.tex","preprints/Tensor-Saturation-for-Even-Spin-Groups-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-zero-tail-completion.tex","preprints/Tensor-Saturation-for-Even-Spin-Groups-September-24-2026/build/figures/zero-tail-scale.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Tensor-Saturation-for-Even-Spin-Groups-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the saturation conjecture for $\\Spin(2n)$, $n\\ge2$. If three dominant integral weights sum to an element of the root lattice, then the existence of a nonzero tensor invariant after a positive integral dilation implies the existence of one at the original weights.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":318},{"kind":"section","title":"Real moments and schedules","sourceOffset":8275},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and the path problem","sourceOffset":8835},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coefficients and flags","sourceOffset":14224},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From moments to decreasing real paths","sourceOffset":23831},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Area maximization and the basis of switches","sourceOffset":34482},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From a basis to cover reflections","sourceOffset":42787},{"kind":"section","title":"Lattices and continuation of schedules","sourceOffset":52875},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Blocks, parity, and the exceptional graph","sourceOffset":54794},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The half-relation deformation","sourceOffset":63560},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sorting collisions and continuing a link","sourceOffset":71477},{"kind":"section","title":"A strict comparison count and simultaneous switches","sourceOffset":83533}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Type-$D$ saturation","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $G=\\Spin(2n)$, $n\\ge2$, and let $\\lambda,\\mu,\\nu$ be dominant integral weights such that $\\lambda+\\mu+\\nu\\in Q(D_n)$. Then, for every positive integer $N$, \\[ \\bigl(V(\\lambda)\\otimes V(\\mu)\\otimes V(\\nu)\\bigr)^G\\ne0 \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\bigl(V(N\\lambda)\\otimes V(N\\mu)\\otimes V(N\\nu)\\bigr)^G\\ne0. \\]","sourceOffset":2088,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Basic real schedule","label":"prop:basic-schedule","text":"Let the root system be of type $D$ or a permutation system, possibly with an orthogonal central space. If $(\\lambda,\\mu,\\xi)$ has a real moment solution, then every shape $\\eta$ ending at $\\mu$ admits a schedule whose switch roots form a real basis of the semisimple space. Weak signs, endpoint times, and coincident times are allowed. More precisely, it is a limit of schedules for nearby regular data whose switches are covers in their local chamber frames. Simplifying the limiting schedule may leave fewer than rank-many switches.","sourceOffset":13419,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Schedule","label":"def:schedule","text":"A schedule for $(\\lambda,\\eta,\\xi)$ starts at $\\lambda$, ends at a point of $W\\xi$, and, between finitely many ordered switch times, satisfies $y'=w\\eta'$ for a fixed $w\\in W$. At a switch at time $t$, the frame changes from $w$ to $s_\\beta w$, where $\\beta$ is a signed root satisfying \\begin{equation} G=y(t)\\cdot\\beta\\ge0, \\qquad -\\beta\\cdot w\\mu\\ge0. \\end{equation} The number $G$ is the position gap, and $-\\beta\\cdot w\\mu$ is the total moving-weight pairing. Several switches may occur at the same time, with their order part of the data. The switch coefficient is $m=-\\beta\\cdot w\\eta(t)$. The","sourceOffset":10063,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":159697,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Tensor-Saturation-for-Even-Spin-Groups-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"205-01","slug":"Universal-Tensor-Squares-for-Symmetric-Groups-September-24-2026","familyId":"205","title":"Universal Tensor Squares for Symmetric Groups","abstract":"For every positive integer n other than 2, 4, and 9, we prove that some irreducible complex representation of Sn has a tensor square containing every irreducible representation. This resolves the tensor square conjecture for symmetric groups affirmatively.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Saxl's conjecture: the tensor square of every staircase representation contains every irreducible complex representation of the corresponding symmetric group. More generally, every Sn with $n\\notin\\{2,4,9\\}$ has an irreducible representation whose tensor square contains all irreducibles.","pdfPath":"preprints/Universal-Tensor-Squares-for-Symmetric-Groups-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Universal-Tensor-Squares-for-Symmetric-Groups-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Universal-Tensor-Squares-for-Symmetric-Groups-September-24-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/Universal-Tensor-Squares-for-Symmetric-Groups-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Universal-Tensor-Squares-for-Symmetric-Groups-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Universal-Tensor-Squares-for-Symmetric-Groups-September-24-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Universal-Tensor-Squares-for-Symmetric-Groups-September-24-2026/build/sections/candidate.tex","preprints/Universal-Tensor-Squares-for-Symmetric-Groups-September-24-2026/build/sections/band.tex","preprints/Universal-Tensor-Squares-for-Symmetric-Groups-September-24-2026/build/sections/balance.tex","preprints/Universal-Tensor-Squares-for-Symmetric-Groups-September-24-2026/build/sections/capacity.tex","preprints/Universal-Tensor-Squares-for-Symmetric-Groups-September-24-2026/build/sections/finite.tex","preprints/Universal-Tensor-Squares-for-Symmetric-Groups-September-24-2026/build/sections/completion.tex","preprints/Universal-Tensor-Squares-for-Symmetric-Groups-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Universal-Tensor-Squares-for-Symmetric-Groups-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every positive integer $n$ other than $2$, $4$, and $9$, we prove that some irreducible complex representation of $S_n$ has a tensor square containing every irreducible representation. This resolves the tensor square conjecture for symmetric groups affirmatively.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":341},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":2641},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its reusable ingredients","sourceOffset":6465},{"kind":"section","title":"Representation-theoretic tools","sourceOffset":9863},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Alternating blocks and dual tests","sourceOffset":10805},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Support under induction","sourceOffset":13861},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The cyclic staircase input","sourceOffset":17423},{"kind":"section","title":"A fixed self-conjugate candidate","sourceOffset":18766},{"kind":"section","title":"The band criterion","sourceOffset":23169},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A forced triangle and a full induced quotient","sourceOffset":24485},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The path and its two attachments","sourceOffset":30039},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Contractions on an odd path","sourceOffset":34794}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every positive integer $n\\notin\\{2,4,9\\}$ there is a partition $\\lambda\\vdash n$ such that \\[ g(\\lambda,\\lambda,\\nu)>0\\qquad\\text{for every }\\nu\\vdash n. \\] Thus one irreducible representation has a tensor square containing every irreducible representation of $S_n$.","sourceOffset":724,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Unipotent tensor squares","label":"cor:unipotent-squares","text":"For each positive integer $n\\notin\\{2,4,9\\}$, choose a partition $\\lambda_n$ supplied by Theorem \\ref{thm:main}, including its finite-range witnesses. For every prime power $q$, let $U_q^\\lambda$ denote the unipotent irreducible complex character of $\\GL_n(\\mathbb F_q)$ indexed by $\\lambda\\vdash n$. Then $U_q^{\\lambda_n}\\otimes U_q^{\\lambda_n}$ contains $U_q^\\nu$ for every $\\nu\\vdash n$.","sourceOffset":1475,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/205.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves the universal tensor-square conjecture for symmetric groups in the stated range. For every positive integer $n\\notin\\{2,4,9\\}$, it constructs an irreducible complex representation of $S_n$ whose tensor square contains every irreducible complex representation of $S_n$. Equivalently, all corresponding Kronecker coefficients are positive. The statement also supplies injective intertwining maps for arbitrary finite-dimensional irreducible representations.\n\nSaxl's conjecture asserts that the tensor square of each staircase Specht module contains every irreducible representation of the corresponding symmetric group. The formalization establishes this for every $m\\ge1$: if $\\rho_m=(m,m-1,\\ldots,1)$, then $g(\\rho_m,\\rho_m,\\mu)>0$ for every partition $\\mu$ of $m(m+1)/2$.\n\nT","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/205.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":122298,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Universal-Tensor-Squares-for-Symmetric-Groups-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Universal-Tensor-Squares-for-Symmetric-Groups-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"205-02","slug":"A-Cyclic-Polytabloid-Proof-of-Saxls-Conjecture-September-24-2026","familyId":"205","title":"A Cyclic Polytabloid Proof of Saxl's Conjecture","abstract":"For every staircase partition, we prove that the tensor square of the corresponding irreducible complex representation of the symmetric group contains every irreducible representation of that group. This proves Saxl's conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Saxl's conjecture: the tensor square of every staircase representation contains every irreducible complex representation of the corresponding symmetric group. More generally, every Sn with $n\\notin\\{2,4,9\\}$ has an irreducible representation whose tensor square contains all irreducibles.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Cyclic-Polytabloid-Proof-of-Saxls-Conjecture-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Cyclic-Polytabloid-Proof-of-Saxls-Conjecture-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Cyclic-Polytabloid-Proof-of-Saxls-Conjecture-September-24-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/A-Cyclic-Polytabloid-Proof-of-Saxls-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-Cyclic-Polytabloid-Proof-of-Saxls-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-Cyclic-Polytabloid-Proof-of-Saxls-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/specht-tools.tex","preprints/A-Cyclic-Polytabloid-Proof-of-Saxls-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/dominance.tex","preprints/A-Cyclic-Polytabloid-Proof-of-Saxls-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/band-quotient.tex","preprints/A-Cyclic-Polytabloid-Proof-of-Saxls-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/path-band.tex","preprints/A-Cyclic-Polytabloid-Proof-of-Saxls-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/completion.tex","preprints/A-Cyclic-Polytabloid-Proof-of-Saxls-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Cyclic-Polytabloid-Proof-of-Saxls-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every staircase partition, we prove that the tensor square of the corresponding irreducible complex representation of the symmetric group contains every irreducible representation of that group. This proves Saxl's conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":275},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":1659},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and its reusable steps","sourceOffset":5653},{"kind":"section","title":"Specht modules and horizontal strips","sourceOffset":10188},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Position tensors and polytabloids","sourceOffset":11514},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pieri and the needed part of Young's rule","sourceOffset":15433},{"kind":"section","title":"The triangular cyclic module and dominance","sourceOffset":19831},{"kind":"section","title":"A quotient obtained by cutting off a band","sourceOffset":28014},{"kind":"section","title":"Cyclic support of the bands","sourceOffset":36283},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The indexed path contraction","sourceOffset":37080},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Alternating consecutive segments","sourceOffset":42164},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Detection by a dual polytabloid","sourceOffset":45404}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Saxl's conjecture","label":"thm:saxl","text":"For every integer $m\\ge1$ and every partition $\\lambda\\vdash N_m$, \\[ g(\\rho_m,\\rho_m,\\lambda)>0. \\] Equivalently, $S^{\\rho_m}\\otimes S^{\\rho_m}$ contains every irreducible complex representation of $S_{N_m}$.","sourceOffset":803,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Cyclic staircase support","label":"thm:cyclic-saxl","text":"For every $m\\ge1$ and every partition $\\lambda\\vdash N_m$, the irreducible $S^\\lambda$ occurs in $W_m$.","sourceOffset":21399,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/205.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves the universal tensor-square conjecture for symmetric groups in the stated range. For every positive integer $n\\notin\\{2,4,9\\}$, it constructs an irreducible complex representation of $S_n$ whose tensor square contains every irreducible complex representation of $S_n$. Equivalently, all corresponding Kronecker coefficients are positive. The statement also supplies injective intertwining maps for arbitrary finite-dimensional irreducible representations.\n\nSaxl's conjecture asserts that the tensor square of each staircase Specht module contains every irreducible representation of the corresponding symmetric group. The formalization establishes this for every $m\\ge1$: if $\\rho_m=(m,m-1,\\ldots,1)$, then $g(\\rho_m,\\rho_m,\\mu)>0$ for every partition $\\mu$ of $m(m+1)/2$.\n\nT","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/205.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":60330,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Cyclic-Polytabloid-Proof-of-Saxls-Conjecture-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-Cyclic-Polytabloid-Proof-of-Saxls-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"206-01","slug":"Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026","familyId":"206","title":"Finite congruence lattices: characterization and undecidability","abstract":"We give an explicit colored-graph characterization of the finite nonempty lattices that occur as full congruence lattices of finite algebras, and prove that deciding this representation property is undecidable. In particular, the finite lattice representation problem has a negative answer. We also prove that recognition of full subgroup intervals in finite groups is undecidable.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Some finite lattices are not congruence lattices of any finite algebra, answering the finite lattice representation problem negatively. Moreover, no algorithm decides whether a finite lattice has such a representation, or whether it is a full subgroup interval of a finite group.","pdfPath":"preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof_overview.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/sections/graph.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/sections/small.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/figures/l7.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/sections/affine.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/sections/patterns.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/figures/patterns.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/sections/classical.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/sections/alternating.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/sections/bounded.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/sections/weights.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/sections/reverse.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/sections/marker_setup.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/sections/forward_a.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/sections/forward_b.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/sections/weighted.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/figures/weighted_square.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/sections/counting.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/sections/diophantine.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/sections/quantitative.tex","preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/sections/minimum.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give an explicit colored-graph characterization of the finite nonempty lattices that occur as full congruence lattices of finite algebras, and prove that deciding this representation property is undecidable. In particular, the finite lattice representation problem has a negative answer. We also prove that recognition of full subgroup intervals in finite groups is undecidable.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":444},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Statements","sourceOffset":2949},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Main ideas and proof organization","sourceOffset":5577},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and dependencies","sourceOffset":14584},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite colored-graph characterization","sourceOffset":16193},{"kind":"section","title":"Representations through seven elements","sourceOffset":22528},{"kind":"section","title":"Affine test lattices and almost simple intervals","sourceOffset":33570},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Structural inputs and conventions","sourceOffset":34973},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Charts, branches, and prepared links","sourceOffset":39582},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From algebras to ordinary group intervals","sourceOffset":48946},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The monolith and extension description","sourceOffset":54840},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pure extensions and their kernels","sourceOffset":69164}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"m:main","text":"The following statements hold. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item A finite nonempty lattice is representable if and only if it satisfies the finite colored-graph criterion in \\Cref{f:criterion}. For each candidate graph size the criterion is decidable by a finite test. \\item There is no deterministic Turing machine that, given the order table of a finite nonempty lattice, always halts and decides \\(\\mathcal R(L)\\). \\item There is a nonrepresentable finite lattice \\(L_0\\) of minimum cardinality, specified by the finite formulas in \\Cref{sec:minimum}. Every finite lattice of smaller cardinality is representable, and \\(\\lvert L_0\\rvert>7\\). \\item Given the order table of a finite nonempty lattice \\(L\\), it is undecidable whether \\(L\\cong[H,G]\\) for some finite group \\(G\\) and subgroup \\(H\\le G\\), where \\([H,G]\\) is the full subgroup interval. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":3629,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Completion of the algebra exclusions","label":"c:remaining-factors","text":"After \\Cref{c:isometry-fields}, the full stabilizers of unordered proper idempotent pairs \\(e,e^*=1-e\\) are excluded. The two remaining retained-plane types in \\Cref{c:partial-isometry-factors} are then impossible, completing the ordered-factor exclusions.","sourceOffset":171836,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Admissible placements","label":"f:def-placement","text":"An $L$-colored complete graph consists of a finite nonempty vertex set $A$ and a symmetric function $d:A\\times A\\to L$, with $d(p,p)=0$. Loops are included among its edges. A placement into another such graph $(A',d')$ is a map $f:A\\to A'$; it is admissible if \\[ d'(f(p),f(q))\\le d(p,q)\\qquad(p,q\\in A). \\] For a nonempty set $S$ of edges of $(A,d)$, let $\\Gamma_S$ be the undirected graph on $A$ whose marked adjacencies are the pairs $\\{f(p),f(q)\\}$, where $\\{p,q\\}\\in S$ and $f:A\\to A$ is admissible. Collapsed pairs are allowed and do not affect connectivity. Write $a_S=\\bigvee_{\\{p,q\\}\\in S}d(","sourceOffset":16996,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"f:def-l7","text":"Let $L_7$ have elements $0,1,a,b,c,d,x$, with $0$ least, $1$ greatest, and with the comparabilities among the five interior elements exactly \\[ a<c,\\qquad b<c,\\qquad b<d. \\] In DeMeo's group-interval labeling these elements are $J_1,J_2,M_2,M_1,K$, respectively.","sourceOffset":23172,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/206.md","scopeText":"The finite lattice representation problem asks which finite lattices occur as the full congruence lattice of a finite algebra. The formalization proves the paper's colored-graph characterization: a finite nonempty lattice is representable exactly when it admits the specified finite nonempty graph witness, whose edge colors encode the required congruence relations. This selected theorem is the equivalence with the graph criterion; the paper's undecidability and subgroup-interval conclusions are outside it.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/206.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":719400,"sourceFileCount":23,"statementCount":72,"definitionCount":21,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Finite-Congruence-Lattices-Characterization-and-Undecidability-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"206-02","slug":"A-Negative-Solution-to-the-Finite-Lattice-Representation-Problem-September-24-2026","familyId":"206","title":"A negative solution to the finite lattice representation problem","abstract":"We give a negative solution to the finite lattice representation problem. We prove that there is a finite nonempty lattice that is not the full congruence lattice of any finite nonempty algebra of any finite signature.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Some finite lattices are not congruence lattices of any finite algebra, answering the finite lattice representation problem negatively. Moreover, no algorithm decides whether a finite lattice has such a representation, or whether it is a full subgroup interval of a finite group.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Negative-Solution-to-the-Finite-Lattice-Representation-Problem-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Negative-Solution-to-the-Finite-Lattice-Representation-Problem-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Negative-Solution-to-the-Finite-Lattice-Representation-Problem-September-24-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/A-Negative-Solution-to-the-Finite-Lattice-Representation-Problem-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-Negative-Solution-to-the-Finite-Lattice-Representation-Problem-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/A-Negative-Solution-to-the-Finite-Lattice-Representation-Problem-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-Negative-Solution-to-the-Finite-Lattice-Representation-Problem-September-24-2026/build/sections/framework.tex","preprints/A-Negative-Solution-to-the-Finite-Lattice-Representation-Problem-September-24-2026/build/sections/classification.tex","preprints/A-Negative-Solution-to-the-Finite-Lattice-Representation-Problem-September-24-2026/build/figures/tested-chain.tex","preprints/A-Negative-Solution-to-the-Finite-Lattice-Representation-Problem-September-24-2026/build/sections/growth.tex","preprints/A-Negative-Solution-to-the-Finite-Lattice-Representation-Problem-September-24-2026/build/sections/lifts.tex","preprints/A-Negative-Solution-to-the-Finite-Lattice-Representation-Problem-September-24-2026/build/sections/packets.tex","preprints/A-Negative-Solution-to-the-Finite-Lattice-Representation-Problem-September-24-2026/build/sections/rigidity.tex","preprints/A-Negative-Solution-to-the-Finite-Lattice-Representation-Problem-September-24-2026/build/sections/lattices.tex","preprints/A-Negative-Solution-to-the-Finite-Lattice-Representation-Problem-September-24-2026/build/figures/boolean-faces.tex","preprints/A-Negative-Solution-to-the-Finite-Lattice-Representation-Problem-September-24-2026/build/sections/extensions.tex","preprints/A-Negative-Solution-to-the-Finite-Lattice-Representation-Problem-September-24-2026/build/sections/gluing.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Negative-Solution-to-the-Finite-Lattice-Representation-Problem-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a negative solution to the finite lattice representation problem. We prove that there is a finite nonempty lattice that is not the full congruence lattice of any finite nonempty algebra of any finite signature.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":282},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the subgroup-interval approach","sourceOffset":3302},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof in two stages","sourceOffset":6048},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Structure and scope of the proof","sourceOffset":12006},{"kind":"section","title":"Fences, socles, and coordinate transport","sourceOffset":13607},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The normal-subgroup test","sourceOffset":14119},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Subdirect powers and unchanged simple types","sourceOffset":19856},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Products and one-coordinate intervals","sourceOffset":24857},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The active chief factor and its core","sourceOffset":30942},{"kind":"section","title":"Tested chains and the classification reduction","sourceOffset":38035},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Literature inputs and maximal-step factor actions","sourceOffset":42694},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A uniform lemma for natural giant actions","sourceOffset":50253}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"main:congruences","text":"There exists a finite nonempty lattice $L$ such that \\[ L\\not\\cong\\Con(A) \\] for every finite nonempty algebra $A$ of every finite signature.","sourceOffset":1467,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"main:group","text":"There exists a finite lattice that is not isomorphic to $[D,G]$ for any subgroup $D$ of any finite group $G$.","sourceOffset":2245,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":null,"text":"A bounded lattice interval $[a,b]$ is fenced if it has an interior element and, for each $a<c<b$, there are $a<u<v<b$ such that \\[ c\\wedge u=c\\wedge v=a, \\qquad c\\vee u=c\\vee v=b. \\] Thus the two comparable elements $u,v$ are both complements of $c$ in the interval. The requirement of an interior element excludes vacuous fences on covering intervals. Length always counts edges.","sourceOffset":14158,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"A tested chain","label":"cls:tested-chain","text":"Let $D<a<b$ belong to a finite group interval, and let \\[ a=x_0<x_1<\\cdots <x_N=b. \\] A tested chain consists of these vertices and vertices $v_{ij}$ for $0\\leq i<j\\leq N$, with the following properties. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(T\\arabic*)},ref=T\\arabic*] \\item Every $x_i$ and $v_{ij}$ is labelled: its interval above $D$ is fenced. Moreover, $x_i\\prec v_{ij}\\prec x_j$, where both relations are covers in the entire interval. \\item Every $[x_i,x_j]$, $i<j$, is fenced. \\item Every $x_i$ with $i<N$, and every $v_{ij}$ with $j<N$, is an intersection of coatoms of $[a,b]$. The $v_{iN}$ are c","sourceOffset":38594,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/206.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":313232,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":27,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Negative-Solution-to-the-Finite-Lattice-Representation-Problem-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"207-01","slug":"The-l1-Bass-Conjecture-for-Discrete-Groups-October-5-2026","familyId":"207","title":"The ℓ¹-Bass Conjecture for Discrete Groups","abstract":"We prove the ℓ1-Bass conjecture for every discrete group. The Hattori–Stallings trace of every idempotent matrix over the complex ℓ1 group algebra is supported on finitely many conjugacy classes of finite-order elements.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the ℓ1-Bass conjecture for every discrete group: Hattori–Stallings traces of idempotent matrices over $\\ell^1(G)$ are supported on finitely many finite-order conjugacy classes. The algebraic companion proves the integral Bass trace conjecture and Kaplansky's idempotent conjecture for torsion-free groups over every commutative unital characteristic-zero domain.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-l1-Bass-Conjecture-for-Discrete-Groups-October-5-2026/l1-bass-conjecture.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-l1-Bass-Conjecture-for-Discrete-Groups-October-5-2026/l1-bass-conjecture.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-l1-Bass-Conjecture-for-Discrete-Groups-October-5-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/The-l1-Bass-Conjecture-for-Discrete-Groups-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-l1-Bass-Conjecture-for-Discrete-Groups-October-5-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/The-l1-Bass-Conjecture-for-Discrete-Groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-l1-Bass-Conjecture-for-Discrete-Groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-reduction.tex","preprints/The-l1-Bass-Conjecture-for-Discrete-Groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-cyclic.tex","preprints/The-l1-Bass-Conjecture-for-Discrete-Groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-channels.tex","preprints/The-l1-Bass-Conjecture-for-Discrete-Groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-connection.tex","preprints/The-l1-Bass-Conjecture-for-Discrete-Groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-curvature.tex","preprints/The-l1-Bass-Conjecture-for-Discrete-Groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-l1-Bass-Conjecture-for-Discrete-Groups-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the $\\ell^1$-Bass conjecture for every discrete group. The Hattori--Stallings trace of every idempotent matrix over the complex $\\ell^1$ group algebra is supported on finitely many conjugacy classes of finite-order elements.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":279},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and relation to earlier methods","sourceOffset":2295},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":5488},{"kind":"section","title":"Reduction to exponential decay","sourceOffset":8245},{"kind":"section","title":"Cyclic simplex formulas for the trace","sourceOffset":12995},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Tuples and their weights","sourceOffset":13486},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compatible forms","sourceOffset":16040},{"kind":"section","title":"Smooth summaries at a spatial scale","sourceOffset":21408},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Smooth selection of centers","sourceOffset":28748},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Predictions with an enlarged radius","sourceOffset":37157},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Accurate finite sample strings","sourceOffset":38982},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Smooth weights on strings","sourceOffset":42858}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $G$ be a discrete group, let $q\\ge1$, and let $e\\in M_q(\\ell^1(G))$ be idempotent. There is a finite set $F\\subset\\mathcal C_{\\rm fin}(G)$ such that $\\tau_C(e)=0$ for every $C\\notin F$. Equivalently, \\[ \\operatorname{HS}^1\\bigl(K_0(\\ell^1(G))\\bigr) \\subseteq \\bigoplus_{C\\in\\mathcal C_{\\rm fin}(G)}\\C[C]. \\]","sourceOffset":1653,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Exponentially decaying representative","label":"red:reduction","text":"Let $e\\in M_q(\\ell^1(G))$ satisfy $e^2=e$. There are a finitely generated subgroup $\\Gamma\\le G$, a word length $|\\cdot|$ on $\\Gamma$, a number $c>0$, and an idempotent $p\\in M_q(\\ell^1(\\Gamma))$ such that \\begin{equation} \\sum_{h\\in\\Gamma}|p(h)|_1e^{c|h|}<\\infty, \\end{equation} and $p$ is similar to $e$ in $M_q(\\ell^1(G))$. If the trace of every such $p$ has finite support on finite-order conjugacy classes of $\\Gamma$, the same conclusion holds for every $e$ over every discrete group.","sourceOffset":10100,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"cy:connection","text":"A compatible connection family through dimension $n$ assigns a real one-form $\\alpha_h$ to each $h\\in\\mathcal T_k(g)$, $0\\le k\\le n$, on $\\widetilde\\Delta_k$, with the following properties: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item It is invariant under time translation, and $\\alpha_h(V)=1$. \\item It is invariant under simultaneous left multiplication by $H$ and under twisted rotation of both labels and times. \\item Its tangential restriction to $x_i=0$ agrees with the form of the shortened tuple obtained by deleting $(h_i,t_i)$. \\item The form is smooth up to every face, by restrictio","sourceOffset":16778,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"ch:channel-definition","text":"A channel with label set $\\Lambda$, on which $G$ acts, assigns to each $P\\in\\mathcal P_f(G)$ a finitely supported subprobability $Y(P)=(Y(P)_\\omega)_{\\omega\\in\\Lambda}$. Thus $Y(P)_\\omega\\ge0$ and $\\sum_\\omega Y(P)_\\omega\\le1$. Translation of weights means $(sP)_h=P_{s^{-1}h}$ and $(sY)_\\omega=Y_{s^{-1}\\omega}$. We require left equivariance, $Y(sP)=sY(P)$ for $s\\in G$, and the following smoothness property. For each finite $S\\subset G$, the restriction to probabilities supported in $S$ has its labels in a fixed finite subset of $\\Lambda$, and its coordinate functions extend smoothly to a neigh","sourceOffset":23307,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/207.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":104818,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-l1-Bass-Conjecture-for-Discrete-Groups-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"207-02","slug":"The-Bass-trace-conjecture-for-complex-group-rings-September-24-2026","familyId":"207","title":"The Bass trace conjecture and the characteristic-zero Kaplansky idempotent conjecture","abstract":"We prove the complex group-ring Bass trace conjecture for every discrete group: the Hattori–Stallings trace of a finitely generated projective module over its complex group ring is supported on conjugacy classes of finite-order elements. As a consequence, for every torsion-free group G and every commutative unital domain R of characteristic zero, the only idempotents in $RG$ are 0 and 1. This proves Kaplansky's idempotent conjecture in characteristic zero.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the ℓ1-Bass conjecture for every discrete group: Hattori–Stallings traces of idempotent matrices over $\\ell^1(G)$ are supported on finitely many finite-order conjugacy classes. The algebraic companion proves the integral Bass trace conjecture and Kaplansky's idempotent conjecture for torsion-free groups over every commutative unital characteristic-zero domain.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Bass-trace-conjecture-for-complex-group-rings-September-24-2026/The-Bass-trace-conjecture-for-complex-group-rings-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Bass-trace-conjecture-for-complex-group-rings-September-24-2026/The-Bass-trace-conjecture-for-complex-group-rings-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Bass-trace-conjecture-for-complex-group-rings-September-24-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/The-Bass-trace-conjecture-for-complex-group-rings-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Bass-trace-conjecture-for-complex-group-rings-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-Bass-trace-conjecture-for-complex-group-rings-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-framework.tex","preprints/The-Bass-trace-conjecture-for-complex-group-rings-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-metric.tex","preprints/The-Bass-trace-conjecture-for-complex-group-rings-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-filtrations.tex","preprints/The-Bass-trace-conjecture-for-complex-group-rings-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-surgery.tex","preprints/The-Bass-trace-conjecture-for-complex-group-rings-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-chains.tex","preprints/The-Bass-trace-conjecture-for-complex-group-rings-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-idempotents.tex","preprints/The-Bass-trace-conjecture-for-complex-group-rings-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-detector.tex","preprints/The-Bass-trace-conjecture-for-complex-group-rings-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-completion.tex","preprints/The-Bass-trace-conjecture-for-complex-group-rings-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-torsion-free.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Bass-trace-conjecture-for-complex-group-rings-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the complex group-ring Bass trace conjecture for every discrete group: the Hattori--Stallings trace of a finitely generated projective module over its complex group ring is supported on conjugacy classes of finite-order elements. As a consequence, for every torsion-free group $G$ and every commutative unital domain $R$ of characteristic zero, the only idempotents in $RG$ are $0$ and $1$. This proves Kaplansky's idempotent conjecture in characteristic zero.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":532},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Trace rank and the idempotent conjecture","sourceOffset":3316},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two parts of the proof","sourceOffset":6203},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Relation to previous work","sourceOffset":10315},{"kind":"section","title":"A vanishing theorem for sparse simplicial cycles","sourceOffset":15276},{"kind":"section","title":"A metric and volume bound for connected supports","sourceOffset":18958},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The metric and its finite local geometry","sourceOffset":19785},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Simplex areas and distance gradients","sourceOffset":25522},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The volume margin in high dimension","sourceOffset":27900},{"kind":"section","title":"Equivariant separating filtrations","sourceOffset":29978},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A free probability parameter","sourceOffset":30477},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The allowed filtrations","sourceOffset":32510}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:bass","text":"Let $G$ be any discrete group. For every $x\\in K_0(\\C G)$ and every infinite-order element $g\\in G$, \\[ \\HS_G(x)([g]_G)=0. \\] Equivalently, if $\\con_{\\mathrm{fin}}(G)$ denotes the conjugacy classes of finite-order elements, then \\[ \\HS_G\\bigl(K_0(\\C G)\\bigr) \\subseteq\\bigoplus_{C\\in\\con_{\\mathrm{fin}}(G)}\\C C. \\]","sourceOffset":2442,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:torsion-free","text":"Let $G$ be any torsion-free discrete group. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item For every $q\\geq1$ and every idempotent $e\\in M_q(\\C G)$, \\[ \\tau_{G,q}(e)=\\operatorname{rank}_{\\C}\\bigl(\\epsilon_{G,q}(e)\\bigr). \\] Consequently $\\tau_{G,*}=\\operatorname{rk}_{\\epsilon}$, with $\\Z$ viewed as a subgroup of $\\C$, and \\[ \\tau_{G,*}\\bigl(K_0(\\C G)\\bigr)=\\Z. \\] \\item For every commutative unital domain $R$ of characteristic zero, every $e\\in RG$ satisfying $e^2=e$ is either $0$ or $1$. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":4504,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/207.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves the complex Bass trace conjecture for every group: the Hattori–Stallings trace of each virtual class of finitely generated projective right $\\mathbb C[G]$-modules vanishes on conjugacy classes of infinite-order elements. No finiteness, countability, or geometric hypothesis on $G$ is imposed.\n\nFor torsion-free $G$, it identifies the trace on $K_0(\\mathbb C[G])$ with the integral augmentation rank and proves the characteristic-zero Kaplansky idempotent conjecture: for every commutative unital domain $R$ of characteristic zero, an idempotent in $R[G]$ is $0$ or $1$.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/207.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":98488,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Bass-trace-conjecture-for-complex-group-rings-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"208-01","slug":"Fiber-functors-for-finite-symmetric-tensor-categories-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026","familyId":"208","title":"Fiber functors for finite symmetric tensor categories in positive characteristic","abstract":"We prove the finite case of the Benson–Etingof–Ostrik conjecture: every finite symmetric tensor category over an algebraically closed field k of characteristic p > 0 admits a k-linear exact faithful strong symmetric monoidal functor to a finite higher Verlinde category $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Ver}}\\nolimits _{p^n}(k)$. The result includes characteristic two, and the level n may depend on the category.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every finite symmetric tensor category over an algebraically closed field k of characteristic p > 0 admits a k-linear exact faithful strong symmetric monoidal fiber functor to a higher Verlinde category $\\mathrm{Ver}_{p^n}$. The level may depend on the category, and the theorem includes characteristic two, resolving the finite case of the Benson–Etingof–Ostrik conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/Fiber-functors-for-finite-symmetric-tensor-categories-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Fiber-functors-for-finite-symmetric-tensor-categories-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Fiber-functors-for-finite-symmetric-tensor-categories-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/Fiber-functors-for-finite-symmetric-tensor-categories-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Fiber-functors-for-finite-symmetric-tensor-categories-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Fiber-functors-for-finite-symmetric-tensor-categories-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Fiber-functors-for-finite-symmetric-tensor-categories-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Fiber-functors-for-finite-symmetric-tensor-categories-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/restricted.tex","preprints/Fiber-functors-for-finite-symmetric-tensor-categories-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/higher.tex","preprints/Fiber-functors-for-finite-symmetric-tensor-categories-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/projectivity.tex","preprints/Fiber-functors-for-finite-symmetric-tensor-categories-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/support.tex","preprints/Fiber-functors-for-finite-symmetric-tensor-categories-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/descent.tex","preprints/Fiber-functors-for-finite-symmetric-tensor-categories-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/figures/descent-graph.tex","preprints/Fiber-functors-for-finite-symmetric-tensor-categories-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Fiber-functors-for-finite-symmetric-tensor-categories-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the finite case of the Benson--Etingof--Ostrik conjecture: every finite symmetric tensor category over an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $p>0$ admits a $k$-linear exact faithful strong symmetric monoidal functor to a finite higher Verlinde category $\\operatorname{Ver}_{p^n}(k)$. The result includes characteristic two, and the level $n$ may depend on the category.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":491},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and contribution","sourceOffset":3873},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":7131},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite tensor categories and products","sourceOffset":9301},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite-category conventions","sourceOffset":9775},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Deligne products","sourceOffset":12354},{"kind":"section","title":"Restricted tilting modules","sourceOffset":13996},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A vector bundle with bounded origin weights","sourceOffset":15645},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The origin fibre after tensoring with a Steinberg module","sourceOffset":21198},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparing the origin and the unipotent fibre","sourceOffset":23625},{"kind":"section","title":"From restricted tiltings to a higher Frobenius functor","sourceOffset":30933},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The functor on group representations","sourceOffset":31944}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every prime $p$, every algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $p$, and every finite symmetric tensor category $\\mathcal C$ over $k$, there are an integer $n\\geq1$ and a fiber functor \\[ \\mathcal C\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Ver}_{p^n}(k). \\]","sourceOffset":2123,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:restricted-tilting","text":"Let $r\\geq1$, let $E_r=(\\mathbb Z/p)^r$, and let $\\iota:E_r\\hookrightarrow U(k)$ be any embedding. Write $R_\\iota$ for restriction along $\\iota$. For every finite-dimensional $kE_r$-module $D$, there is a tilting $G$-module $T$ such that \\[ R_\\iota(\\operatorname{St}_{r-1})\\otimes D\\simeq R_\\iota(T). \\]","sourceOffset":14801,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":85332,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Fiber-functors-for-finite-symmetric-tensor-categories-in-positive-characteristic-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"209-01","slug":"An-Integral-Counterexample-to-Gerstens-Conjecture-September-25-2026","familyId":"209","title":"An integral counterexample to Gersten's conjecture","abstract":"We construct a two-dimensional ramified regular local ring A of mixed characteristic $(0,5)$ for which $K_5(A)\\to K_5(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Frac}}\\nolimits A)$ has a nonzero kernel. This disproves Gersten's conjecture in its unrestricted integral form.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves unrestricted integral Gersten injectivity in degrees 3 and 5. Two explicit two-dimensional ramified regular local rings of mixed characteristic $(0,5)$ have nonzero integral K-theory classes that vanish over their fraction fields.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-Integral-Counterexample-to-Gerstens-Conjecture-September-25-2026/An-Integral-Counterexample-to-Gerstens-Conjecture-September-25-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-Integral-Counterexample-to-Gerstens-Conjecture-September-25-2026/An-Integral-Counterexample-to-Gerstens-Conjecture-September-25-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-Integral-Counterexample-to-Gerstens-Conjecture-September-25-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/An-Integral-Counterexample-to-Gerstens-Conjecture-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/An-Integral-Counterexample-to-Gerstens-Conjecture-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/An-Integral-Counterexample-to-Gerstens-Conjecture-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/coefficients.tex","preprints/An-Integral-Counterexample-to-Gerstens-Conjecture-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/arithmetic.tex","preprints/An-Integral-Counterexample-to-Gerstens-Conjecture-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/specialization.tex","preprints/An-Integral-Counterexample-to-Gerstens-Conjecture-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/An-Integral-Counterexample-to-Gerstens-Conjecture-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/main-proof.tex","preprints/An-Integral-Counterexample-to-Gerstens-Conjecture-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/claimed-vanishing.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-Integral-Counterexample-to-Gerstens-Conjecture-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a two-dimensional ramified regular local ring $A$ of mixed characteristic $(0,5)$ for which $K_5(A)\\to K_5(\\Frac A)$ has a nonzero kernel. This disproves Gersten's conjecture in its unrestricted integral form.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":381},{"kind":"section","title":"Coefficients and Bott multiplication","sourceOffset":8661},{"kind":"section","title":"An integral cyclotomic class","sourceOffset":21896},{"kind":"section","title":"A specialization criterion for supported classes","sourceOffset":37304},{"kind":"section","title":"An elliptic quotient of the Fermat quartic","sourceOffset":48548},{"kind":"section","title":"The regular local ring and its split divisor","sourceOffset":55573},{"kind":"section","title":"The nonzero integral kernel class","sourceOffset":63022},{"kind":"section","title":"A claimed vanishing theorem for principal divisors","sourceOffset":68856},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The complexes and the proposed operation","sourceOffset":69910}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $V$ be the ring of integers in the unramified extension of $\\Q_5$ of degree eight, and put \\[ A=\\bigl(V[x,y]/(5+x^4+y^4)\\bigr)_{(5,x,y)}. \\] Then $A$ is a Noetherian regular local domain of dimension two and \\[ \\ker\\bigl(K_5(A)\\longrightarrow K_5(\\Frac A)\\bigr)\\ne0. \\] Consequently the unrestricted integral Gersten conjecture has a negative answer.","sourceOffset":1099,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Integral lift and conjugates","label":"prop:integral-lift","text":"Let $u=\\zeta-1\\in S^\\times$, and let $b$ be multiplication by the class $\\beta$ in \\eqref{eq:equivariant-bott}. There exists $c\\in K_5(S)$ such that \\begin{equation} c\\bmod5=b^2[u]_5\\quad\\text{in }K_5(S;5). \\end{equation} For every $\\delta\\in\\Delta$, \\begin{equation} (\\delta c)\\bmod5=\\chi(\\delta)^2b^2[\\delta u]_5 \\quad\\text{in }K_5(S;5). \\end{equation} For any fixed homomorphism $\\iota:S\\to H$ to a field, let $\\operatorname{res}_\\iota$ denote extension of scalars. Restriction of these equalities gives the corresponding identities in $K_5(H;5)$. In the second identity this means \\[ \\operatorname{res}_\\iota(\\delta c)\\bmod5 =\\chi(\\delta)^2 b_\\iota^2[\\iota(\\delta u)]_5, \\] where $b_\\iota$ is multiplication by $\\operatorname{res}_\\iota(\\beta)$; no action of $\\delta$ on $H$ is required.","sourceOffset":35586,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":76291,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-Integral-Counterexample-to-Gerstens-Conjecture-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"209-02","slug":"An-Integral-Degree-Three-Gersten-Counterexample-September-26-2026","familyId":"209","title":"An integral degree-three Gersten counterexample","abstract":"We construct a two-dimensional ramified regular local ring A in mixed characteristic $(0,5)$ for which the integral map $K_3(A)\\to K_3(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Frac}}\\nolimits A)$ has nonzero kernel. This gives a negative answer to the unrestricted integral Gersten conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves unrestricted integral Gersten injectivity in degrees 3 and 5. Two explicit two-dimensional ramified regular local rings of mixed characteristic $(0,5)$ have nonzero integral K-theory classes that vanish over their fraction fields.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-Integral-Degree-Three-Gersten-Counterexample-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-Integral-Degree-Three-Gersten-Counterexample-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-Integral-Degree-Three-Gersten-Counterexample-September-26-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/An-Integral-Degree-Three-Gersten-Counterexample-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/An-Integral-Degree-Three-Gersten-Counterexample-September-26-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/An-Integral-Degree-Three-Gersten-Counterexample-September-26-2026/build/sections/model.tex","preprints/An-Integral-Degree-Three-Gersten-Counterexample-September-26-2026/build/sections/integral-class.tex","preprints/An-Integral-Degree-Three-Gersten-Counterexample-September-26-2026/build/sections/field.tex","preprints/An-Integral-Degree-Three-Gersten-Counterexample-September-26-2026/build/sections/detection.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-Integral-Degree-Three-Gersten-Counterexample-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a two-dimensional ramified regular local ring $A$ in mixed characteristic $(0,5)$ for which the integral map $K_3(A)\\to K_3(\\Frac A)$ has nonzero kernel. This gives a negative answer to the unrestricted integral Gersten conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":290},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and earlier results","sourceOffset":2018},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The route through the proof","sourceOffset":5405},{"kind":"section","title":"The model and its distinguished divisor","sourceOffset":8425},{"kind":"section","title":"Lifting on the divisor","sourceOffset":13291},{"kind":"section","title":"The field comparison and norm detector","sourceOffset":17803},{"kind":"section","title":"Detection on the projective curve","sourceOffset":24658}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"The ring $A$ is a two-dimensional Noetherian regular local domain of mixed characteristic $(0,5)$, with $\\pi\\in\\mathfrak m_A^2$. There is an integral class $c\\in K_3(A)$ such that \\[ c|_{\\Frac A}=0, \\qquad c|_{A[1/\\pi]}\\bmod5\\ne0 \\quad\\text{in }G_3(A[1/\\pi];\\Z/5). \\] In particular, $K_3(A)\\to K_3(\\Frac A)$ is not injective.","sourceOffset":1313,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:integral","text":"There exists $b\\in K_3(S)$ whose restriction to $D$, followed by coefficient reduction, is $a_D$. If $j:\\Spec S\\hookrightarrow\\Spec A$ and $c=j_*b\\in G_3(A)=K_3(A)$, then $c|_{\\Frac A}=0$. Moreover, for $T=\\Spec A[1/\\pi]$ and $i:\\Spec D\\hookrightarrow T$, \\[ c|_T\\bmod5=i_*a_D\\quad\\text{in }G_3(T;\\Z/5). \\]","sourceOffset":14822,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":30634,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-Integral-Degree-Three-Gersten-Counterexample-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"210-01","slug":"Foulkes-Conjecture-for-the-Sixth-Symmetric-Power-September-25-2026","familyId":"210","title":"Foulkes' conjecture for the sixth symmetric power","abstract":"We prove the sixth-symmetric-power case of Foulkes' conjecture. For every integer b ≥ 6 and every finite-dimensional complex vector space V, there is a $\\mathop{\\mathrm{GL}}\\nolimits (V)$-equivariant injection $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^6(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^b V)\\hookrightarrow\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^b(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^6 V)$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sixth case of Foulkes’ conjecture: $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^6(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^bV)$ embeds equivariantly in $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^b(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^6V)$ for every b ≥ 6 and finite-dimensional complex V. More generally, the canonical multiplication map $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^b(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^aV)\\to\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^a(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^bV)$ is surjective for a ≥ 2 and $b\\ge a(a-1)$, giving dimension-independent quadratic stabilization.","pdfPath":"preprints/Foulkes-Conjecture-for-the-Sixth-Symmetric-Power-September-25-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Foulkes-Conjecture-for-the-Sixth-Symmetric-Power-September-25-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Foulkes-Conjecture-for-the-Sixth-Symmetric-Power-September-25-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/Foulkes-Conjecture-for-the-Sixth-Symmetric-Power-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Foulkes-Conjecture-for-the-Sixth-Symmetric-Power-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Foulkes-Conjecture-for-the-Sixth-Symmetric-Power-September-25-2026/build/sections/reduction.tex","preprints/Foulkes-Conjecture-for-the-Sixth-Symmetric-Power-September-25-2026/build/sections/upper.tex","preprints/Foulkes-Conjecture-for-the-Sixth-Symmetric-Power-September-25-2026/build/sections/lower.tex","preprints/Foulkes-Conjecture-for-the-Sixth-Symmetric-Power-September-25-2026/build/sections/certificates.tex","preprints/Foulkes-Conjecture-for-the-Sixth-Symmetric-Power-September-25-2026/build/sections/band.tex","preprints/Foulkes-Conjecture-for-the-Sixth-Symmetric-Power-September-25-2026/build/sections/verification.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Foulkes-Conjecture-for-the-Sixth-Symmetric-Power-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the sixth-symmetric-power case of Foulkes' conjecture. For every integer \\(b\\ge6\\) and every finite-dimensional complex vector space \\(V\\), there is a \\(\\GL(V)\\)-equivariant injection \\(\\Sym^6(\\Sym^b V)\\hookrightarrow\\Sym^b(\\Sym^6 V)\\).","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The result and its context","sourceOffset":291},{"kind":"section","title":"The large-degree range and finite reductions","sourceOffset":8506},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The multiplication map","sourceOffset":8980},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Six variables and rectangular shifts","sourceOffset":11036},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A chart certificate for the first row","sourceOffset":14330},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An independent large-degree bound","sourceOffset":18424},{"kind":"section","title":"An upper bound and two exact recurrences","sourceOffset":22071},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The signed strip rule","sourceOffset":22499},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A monotone upper certificate","sourceOffset":25664},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact coefficients","sourceOffset":28690},{"kind":"section","title":"A power bound from leading monomials","sourceOffset":31600},{"kind":"section","title":"Certificates in degrees below 150","sourceOffset":36815}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer \\(b\\ge6\\) and every finite-dimensional complex vector space \\(V\\), there is a \\(\\GL(V)\\)-equivariant injection \\[ \\Sym^6(\\Sym^b V)\\ \\hookrightarrow\\ \\Sym^b(\\Sym^6 V). \\] Equivalently, \\(h_b[h_6]-h_6[h_b]\\) is Schur-positive for every \\(b\\ge6\\).","sourceOffset":1174,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"The main large-degree range","label":"cor:quadratic-range","text":"For every finite-dimensional complex \\(V\\) and every \\(b\\ge30\\), there is a \\(\\GL(V)\\)-equivariant injection \\(\\Sym^6(\\Sym^b V)\\hookrightarrow\\Sym^b(\\Sym^6 V)\\).","sourceOffset":10167,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/210.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":70120,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Foulkes-Conjecture-for-the-Sixth-Symmetric-Power-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Foulkes-Conjecture-for-the-Sixth-Symmetric-Power-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"210-02","slug":"Quadratic-Stabilization-of-the-Canonical-Foulkes-Howe-Map-September-25-2026","familyId":"210","title":"Quadratic stabilization of the canonical Foulkes--Howe map","abstract":"For every finite-dimensional complex vector space V, we prove that the canonical Foulkes–Howe map $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^b(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^a V)\\longrightarrow\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^a(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^b V)$ is surjective whenever a ≥ 2 and $b\\ge a(a-1)$. This gives a quadratic stabilization bound independent of $\\dim V$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sixth case of Foulkes’ conjecture: $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^6(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^bV)$ embeds equivariantly in $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^b(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^6V)$ for every b ≥ 6 and finite-dimensional complex V. More generally, the canonical multiplication map $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^b(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^aV)\\to\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^a(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Sym}}\\nolimits ^bV)$ is surjective for a ≥ 2 and $b\\ge a(a-1)$, giving dimension-independent quadratic stabilization.","pdfPath":"preprints/Quadratic-Stabilization-of-the-Canonical-Foulkes-Howe-Map-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quadratic-Stabilization-of-the-Canonical-Foulkes-Howe-Map-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quadratic-Stabilization-of-the-Canonical-Foulkes-Howe-Map-September-25-2026","subjectId":"2","subject":"Algebra","subjectZh":"代數","texPaths":["preprints/Quadratic-Stabilization-of-the-Canonical-Foulkes-Howe-Map-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Quadratic-Stabilization-of-the-Canonical-Foulkes-Howe-Map-September-25-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Quadratic-Stabilization-of-the-Canonical-Foulkes-Howe-Map-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every finite-dimensional complex vector space $V$, we prove that the canonical Foulkes--Howe map $\\Sym^b(\\Sym^a V)\\longrightarrow\\Sym^a(\\Sym^b V)$ is surjective whenever $a\\ge2$ and $b\\ge a(a-1)$. This gives a quadratic stabilization bound independent of $\\dim V$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":314},{"kind":"section","title":"The canonical image and diagonal vanishing","sourceOffset":6987},{"kind":"section","title":"Differential injectivity","sourceOffset":9662},{"kind":"section","title":"Vanishing on products","sourceOffset":12046},{"kind":"section","title":"Representation comparison and scope","sourceOffset":17958}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $a\\ge2$, every integer $b\\ge a(a-1)$, and every finite-dimensional complex vector space $V$, the map $\\mu_{a,b,V}$ in \\eqref{eq:map} is surjective. Equivalently, $A_b=R_b$.","sourceOffset":2301,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:vanishing","text":"Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional complex vector space, and let $a\\ge1$ and $b\\ge1$ be integers satisfying $b\\ge a(a-1)$. If a symmetric $a$-linear form $T$ on $\\Sym^b V$ satisfies \\begin{equation} T(P,\\ldots,P)=0 \\qquad\\text{for every product $P$ of $b$ vectors}, \\end{equation} then $T=0$.","sourceOffset":12261,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/210.md","scopeText":"The formalized result proves surjectivity of the canonical averaged Foulkes–Howe map $\\mathrm{Sym}^b(\\mathrm{Sym}^a\\,V)\\to\\mathrm{Sym}^a(\\mathrm{Sym}^b\\,V)$ for every finite-dimensional complex vector space, $a\\ge2$, and $b\\ge a(a-1)$. It also covers the bijective $a=1$ case, the vanishing-on-products consequence, and the corresponding equivariant embedding in the reverse direction. The sixth-power specialization is covered for $b\\ge30$; the companion's full range $b\\ge6$ is not included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/210.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":22530,"sourceFileCount":2,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Quadratic-Stabilization-of-the-Canonical-Foulkes-Howe-Map-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Quadratic-Stabilization-of-the-Canonical-Foulkes-Howe-Map-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"211-01","slug":"Random-Walks-on-Critical-FK-Ising-Maps-and-Liouville-Brownian-Motion-October-5-2026","familyId":"211","title":"Random Walks on Critical FK–Ising Maps and Liouville Brownian Motion","abstract":"We prove that stationary random walks on critical spherical FK–Ising planar maps converge to Liouville Brownian motion on the ordinary unit-area $\\sqrt3$-quantum sphere, using the geometric and electrical results of the spectral companion. The walk chooses uniformly among all incident half-edges, retaining loops and multiple edges. With the corner measure as the stationary law, the deterministic time acceleration is exactly the number of map edges. For any deterministic metric scale giving the metric-measure limit, convergence retains that same surface and any fixed finite number of conditionally independent walks with their time parameters.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Critical Fortuin–Kasteleyn planar maps converge to Liouville quantum gravity spheres for $0\\lt q\\le4$ and to the Brownian continuum random tree for q > 4, establishing the surface-to-tree geometric transition. For FK–Ising and spanning-tree-weighted maps, stationary random walks converge to Liouville Brownian motion on the limiting sphere. The FK–Ising spectral result also gives convergence of eigenvalues and heat traces, using the stated Brownian/LQG inputs.","pdfPath":"preprints/Random-Walks-on-Critical-FK-Ising-Maps-and-Liouville-Brownian-Motion-October-5-2026/fk-ising-walk-limit.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Random-Walks-on-Critical-FK-Ising-Maps-and-Liouville-Brownian-Motion-October-5-2026/fk-ising-walk-limit.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Random-Walks-on-Critical-FK-Ising-Maps-and-Liouville-Brownian-Motion-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Random-Walks-on-Critical-FK-Ising-Maps-and-Liouville-Brownian-Motion-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Random-Walks-on-Critical-FK-Ising-Maps-and-Liouville-Brownian-Motion-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Random-Walks-on-Critical-FK-Ising-Maps-and-Liouville-Brownian-Motion-October-5-2026/build/sections/inputs.tex","preprints/Random-Walks-on-Critical-FK-Ising-Maps-and-Liouville-Brownian-Motion-October-5-2026/build/sections/operators.tex","preprints/Random-Walks-on-Critical-FK-Ising-Maps-and-Liouville-Brownian-Motion-October-5-2026/build/sections/tightness.tex","preprints/Random-Walks-on-Critical-FK-Ising-Maps-and-Liouville-Brownian-Motion-October-5-2026/build/sections/paths.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Random-Walks-on-Critical-FK-Ising-Maps-and-Liouville-Brownian-Motion-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that stationary random walks on critical spherical FK--Ising planar maps converge to Liouville Brownian motion on the ordinary unit-area $\\sqrt3$-quantum sphere, using the geometric and electrical results of the spectral companion. The walk chooses uniformly among all incident half-edges, retaining loops and multiple edges. With the corner measure as the stationary law, the deterministic time acceleration is exactly the number of map edges. For any deterministic metric scale giving the metric-measure limit, convergence retains that same surface and any fixed finite number of conditionally independent walks with their time parameters.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":696},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and the result","sourceOffset":1139},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and related work","sourceOffset":5266},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From operators to paths","sourceOffset":7055},{"kind":"section","title":"Energy normalization and the common coupling","sourceOffset":9130},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The graph energy and the spatial scale","sourceOffset":9540},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The geometric and analytic inputs","sourceOffset":13357},{"kind":"section","title":"Operators and finite-dimensional laws","sourceOffset":21943},{"kind":"section","title":"From energy recovery to path tightness","sourceOffset":26158},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform recovery and a stationary maximal inequality","sourceOffset":26673},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Resolvent approximation along the whole path","sourceOffset":30582},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Stopping-time increments","sourceOffset":33378}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $(M_n,A_n)$ have law \\eqref{eq:fk-law}, and let $a_n>0$ be any deterministic sequence with $a_n\\to0$ such that \\begin{equation} (V_n,a_nd_n,\\mu_n)\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ (S,D_h,\\mu_h) \\end{equation} in Gromov--Hausdorff--Prokhorov law. Given the map, start $r$ independent simple random walks $X^{n,1},\\ldots,X^{n,r}$ independently from $\\mu_n$. Given $h$, start $r$ independent Liouville Brownian motions $B^{h,1},\\ldots,B^{h,r}$ independently from $\\mu_h$, with the convention \\eqref{eq:continuum-form}. For every fixed integer $r\\ge1$, \\begin{equation} \\left(\\overline M_n,a_nd_n,\\mu_n; (\\overline X^{n,j}_{nt})_{t\\ge0,\\,1\\le j\\le r}\\right) \\ \\Longrightarrow\\ \\left(S,D_h,\\mu_h; (B^{h,j}_t)_{t\\ge0,\\,1\\le j\\le r}\\right) \\end{equation} in the curve-decorated Gromov--Hausdorff--Prokhorov topology.","sourceOffset":3648,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Inputs from the spectral companion","label":"prop:inputs","text":"From every subsequence of sizes tending to infinity there is a further subsequence and a representation coupling on which the following properties hold almost surely, simultaneously. The limiting sphere in these coordinates has the ordinary unit-area \\(\\sqrt3\\)-LQG law, with area \\(\\mu_h\\) and metric \\(D_h\\); the latter induces the round topology. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item Geometry in one coordinate. The vertex measures satisfy \\((z_n)_*\\mu_n\\to\\mu_h\\) weakly, and \\begin{align} \\delta_n&:=\\sup_{u,v\\in V_n} \\abs{a_nd_n(u,v)-D_h(z_n(u),z_n(v))}\\longrightarrow0, \\\\ \\varepsilon_n&:=d_{\\mathrm H}^{D_h}(z_n(V_n),\\Sph) \\longrightarrow0. \\end{align} The maximum round diameter of the embedded flags tends to zero. Consequently the maximum round diameter of an embedded primal edge tends to zero as well. \\item One Hilbert space. There is an order \\(v_n(i)\\), \\(0\\le i<2n\\), of the primal corner occurrences and a continuous tour \\(\\eta:[0,1]\\to\\Sph\\) such that \\begin{equation} \\eta_*\\dd t=\\mu_h,\\qquad \\sup_{0\\le t<1} \\rho\\bigl(z_n(v_n(\\lfloor2nt\\rfloor)),\\eta(t)\\bigr) \\longrightarrow0. \\end{equation} Each vertex \\(v\\) occurs \\(\\deg(v)\\) times in the corner list. For \\(\\mu_h\\)-almost every \\(z\\), the fiber \\(\\eta^{-1}(\\{z\\})\\) contains exactly one time. With \\(H=L^2([0,1],\\dd t)\\), the maps \\begin{align*} J_n:L^2(\\mu_n)&\\longrightarrow H,& (J_nu)(t)&=u(v_n(\\lfloor2nt\\rfloor)),\\quad 0\\le t<1,\\\\ J:L^2(\\mu_h)&\\longrightarrow H,& Jf&=f\\circ\\eta \\end{align*} are isometrie","sourceOffset":14174,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/211.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":49301,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Random-Walks-on-Critical-FK-Ising-Maps-and-Liouville-Brownian-Motion-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Random-Walks-on-Critical-FK-Ising-Maps-and-Liouville-Brownian-Motion-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Random-Walks-on-Critical-FK-Ising-Maps-and-Liouville-Brownian-Motion-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"211-02","slug":"Spectral-convergence-for-critical-FK-Ising-planar-maps-October-5-2026","familyId":"211","title":"Spectral convergence for critical FK–Ising planar maps","abstract":"Using the conformal and metric-measure companion results and the stated Brownian/Liouville quantum gravity inputs, we prove spectral convergence for critical spherical FK–Ising maps to Liouville Brownian motion on the ordinary unit-area $\\sqrt3$-quantum sphere. The discrete walk has total attempt rate one, uses every map edge including loops and multiplicities, and has the corner measure as its stationary law. Accelerating time by the number of map edges gives joint convergence of the metric-measure space, all ordered eigenvalues with multiplicities and padding, and the heat trace locally uniformly at strictly positive times. The conductivity and clock constant are one in these conventions.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Critical Fortuin–Kasteleyn planar maps converge to Liouville quantum gravity spheres for $0\\lt q\\le4$ and to the Brownian continuum random tree for q > 4, establishing the surface-to-tree geometric transition. For FK–Ising and spanning-tree-weighted maps, stationary random walks converge to Liouville Brownian motion on the limiting sphere. The FK–Ising spectral result also gives convergence of eigenvalues and heat traces, using the stated Brownian/LQG inputs.","pdfPath":"preprints/Spectral-convergence-for-critical-FK-Ising-planar-maps-October-5-2026/spectral-convergence-critical-fk-ising-planar-maps.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Spectral-convergence-for-critical-FK-Ising-planar-maps-October-5-2026/spectral-convergence-critical-fk-ising-planar-maps.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Spectral-convergence-for-critical-FK-Ising-planar-maps-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Spectral-convergence-for-critical-FK-Ising-planar-maps-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Spectral-convergence-for-critical-FK-Ising-planar-maps-October-5-2026/build/tex/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Spectral-convergence-for-critical-FK-Ising-planar-maps-October-5-2026/build/tex/02-model-geometry.tex","preprints/Spectral-convergence-for-critical-FK-Ising-planar-maps-October-5-2026/build/tex/03-locality.tex","preprints/Spectral-convergence-for-critical-FK-Ising-planar-maps-October-5-2026/build/tex/04-network.tex","preprints/Spectral-convergence-for-critical-FK-Ising-planar-maps-October-5-2026/build/tex/06-conductivity.tex","preprints/Spectral-convergence-for-critical-FK-Ising-planar-maps-October-5-2026/build/figures/mixed-boundary-duality.tex","preprints/Spectral-convergence-for-critical-FK-Ising-planar-maps-October-5-2026/build/tex/07-green.tex","preprints/Spectral-convergence-for-critical-FK-Ising-planar-maps-October-5-2026/build/tex/05-compactness.tex","preprints/Spectral-convergence-for-critical-FK-Ising-planar-maps-October-5-2026/build/tex/08-continuum.tex","preprints/Spectral-convergence-for-critical-FK-Ising-planar-maps-October-5-2026/build/tex/09-spectrum.tex","preprints/Spectral-convergence-for-critical-FK-Ising-planar-maps-October-5-2026/build/tex/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Spectral-convergence-for-critical-FK-Ising-planar-maps-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"Using the conformal and metric-measure companion results and the stated Brownian/Liouville quantum gravity inputs, we prove spectral convergence for critical spherical FK--Ising maps to Liouville Brownian motion on the ordinary unit-area $\\sqrt3$-quantum sphere. The discrete walk has total attempt rate one, uses every map edge including loops and multiplicities, and has the corner measure as its stationary law. Accelerating time by the number of map edges gives joint convergence of the metric-measure space, all ordered eigenvalues with multiplicities and padding, and the heat trace locally uniformly at strictly positive times. The conductivity and clock constant are one in these conventions.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":747},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and the theorem","sourceOffset":5350},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":11333},{"kind":"section","title":"The discrete energy and the common geometric limit","sourceOffset":16545},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The raw electrical energy","sourceOffset":17006},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Flags, corner occurrences, and the exploration","sourceOffset":18661},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One coordinate for area, distance, and local tests","sourceOffset":23091},{"kind":"section","title":"Local observations of the electrical network","sourceOffset":33724},{"kind":"section","title":"Electrical control in fixed regions","sourceOffset":73491},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Edge extremal length and traffic","sourceOffset":74403},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Annular barriers at power scales","sourceOffset":84475},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rectifiable limits of inexpensive crossings","sourceOffset":100835}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Joint Liouville spectral limit","label":"thm:main","text":"The form \\eqref{eq:continuum-energy} is closable on \\(L^2(\\mu_h)\\). Its closure has compact resolvent, has the constants as its one-dimensional kernel, and satisfies \\(H_h(t)<\\infty\\) for every \\(t>0\\). As \\(n\\to\\infty\\) through all positive integers, \\begin{equation} \\left( (V_n,a_nd_n,\\mu_n),\\ (n\\lambda_{n,j})_{j\\ge0},\\ H_n \\right) \\ \\Longrightarrow\\ \\left( (\\Sph,D_h,\\mu_h),\\ (\\Lambda_j(h))_{j\\ge0},\\ H_h \\right). \\end{equation} The first coordinate has the Gromov--Hausdorff--Prokhorov topology on isometry classes of compact metric probability spaces. The second has the product topology on \\([0,\\infty]^{\\N_0}\\). The third has \\(C_{\\mathrm{loc}}((0,\\infty))\\), the topology of uniform convergence on each \\([\\delta,T]\\) with \\(0<\\delta<T<\\infty\\). All three limiting coordinates are constructed from the same quantum sphere \\(h\\).","sourceOffset":9514,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Common geometric extraction","label":"prop:geometry","text":"From every sequence \\(n\\to\\infty\\), one may pass to a further subsequence and a representation coupling with the following almost-sure properties. The limiting coordinate sphere carries an ordinary unit-area \\(\\sqrt3\\)-LQG sphere with area \\(\\mu_h\\) and metric \\(D_h\\). \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item The pushed corner measures converge weakly, \\[ (z_n)_*\\mu_n\\longrightarrow\\mu_h, \\] and, in the product of the round topology with the ordinary topology of \\([0,\\infty)\\), \\begin{equation} \\bigl\\{(z_n(u),z_n(v),a_nd_n(u,v)):u,v\\in V_n\\bigr\\} \\longrightarrow \\bigl\\{(z,w,D_h(z,w)):z,w\\in\\Sph\\bigr\\} \\end{equation} in Hausdorff distance. Also \\(\\delta_n:=\\max_T\\diam_{\\mathrm{round}}\\phi_n(T)\\to0\\), where \\(T\\) ranges over flags. Every complete primal or dual incidence star consequently has diameter at most \\(2\\delta_n\\). \\item Finitely many conditionally uniform rerootings may be retained using the same map and the same \\(\\phi_n\\). For each rerooting \\(r\\), there is a continuous tour \\(\\eta_r:[0,1]\\to\\Sph\\) with \\((\\eta_r)_*(\\dd t)=\\mu_h\\) and \\begin{equation} \\sup_{0\\le t\\le1} d_{\\mathrm{round}}\\!\\left( z_n\\bigl(v_n^{(r)}(\\lfloor 2nt\\rfloor)\\bigr),\\eta_r(t)\\right) \\longrightarrow0. \\end{equation} For \\(\\mu_h\\)-almost every \\(z\\), the fiber \\(\\eta_r^{-1}(\\{z\\})\\) consists of one time among all of \\([0,1]\\). For two conditionally independent uniform rerootings, their limiting root positions, jointly with \\(\\mu_h\\), have the law of two independent samples from \\(\\mu_h","sourceOffset":25675,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/211.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":334619,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Spectral-convergence-for-critical-FK-Ising-planar-maps-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"211-03","slug":"A-Linear-Clock-for-Random-Walk-on-Tree-Weighted-Planar-Maps-October-5-2026","familyId":"211","title":"A Linear Clock for Random Walk on Tree-Weighted Planar Maps","abstract":"We prove that stationary random walk on a planar map sampled with weight equal to its number of spanning trees converges to Liouville Brownian motion on the unit-area $\\sqrt2$-Liouville quantum sphere. The convergence retains the conditional path law jointly with the measured metric space. The walk chooses uniformly among all incident half-edges and starts from the stationary degree measure. For total attempt rate one and the continuum Dirichlet form with factor 1/2, the time acceleration is exactly the number of map edges. The result uses the companion contour and metric limits for this same ensemble.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Critical Fortuin–Kasteleyn planar maps converge to Liouville quantum gravity spheres for $0\\lt q\\le4$ and to the Brownian continuum random tree for q > 4, establishing the surface-to-tree geometric transition. For FK–Ising and spanning-tree-weighted maps, stationary random walks converge to Liouville Brownian motion on the limiting sphere. The FK–Ising spectral result also gives convergence of eigenvalues and heat traces, using the stated Brownian/LQG inputs.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Linear-Clock-for-Random-Walk-on-Tree-Weighted-Planar-Maps-October-5-2026/linear-clock-random-walk-tree-weighted-planar-maps.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Linear-Clock-for-Random-Walk-on-Tree-Weighted-Planar-Maps-October-5-2026/linear-clock-random-walk-tree-weighted-planar-maps.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Linear-Clock-for-Random-Walk-on-Tree-Weighted-Planar-Maps-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/A-Linear-Clock-for-Random-Walk-on-Tree-Weighted-Planar-Maps-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-Linear-Clock-for-Random-Walk-on-Tree-Weighted-Planar-Maps-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-Linear-Clock-for-Random-Walk-on-Tree-Weighted-Planar-Maps-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-history.tex","preprints/A-Linear-Clock-for-Random-Walk-on-Tree-Weighted-Planar-Maps-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-inputs.tex","preprints/A-Linear-Clock-for-Random-Walk-on-Tree-Weighted-Planar-Maps-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-nondegeneration.tex","preprints/A-Linear-Clock-for-Random-Walk-on-Tree-Weighted-Planar-Maps-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-energy.tex","preprints/A-Linear-Clock-for-Random-Walk-on-Tree-Weighted-Planar-Maps-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-sphere.tex","preprints/A-Linear-Clock-for-Random-Walk-on-Tree-Weighted-Planar-Maps-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-green.tex","preprints/A-Linear-Clock-for-Random-Walk-on-Tree-Weighted-Planar-Maps-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-dynamics.tex","preprints/A-Linear-Clock-for-Random-Walk-on-Tree-Weighted-Planar-Maps-October-5-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Linear-Clock-for-Random-Walk-on-Tree-Weighted-Planar-Maps-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that stationary random walk on a planar map sampled with weight equal to its number of spanning trees converges to Liouville Brownian motion on the unit-area $\\sqrt2$-Liouville quantum sphere. The convergence retains the conditional path law jointly with the measured metric space. The walk chooses uniformly among all incident half-edges and starts from the stationary degree measure. For total attempt rate one and the continuum Dirichlet form with factor $1/2$, the time acceleration is exactly the number of map edges. The result uses the companion contour and metric limits for this same ensemble.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":675},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and the continuum process","sourceOffset":1597},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Retaining the conditional path law","sourceOffset":4791},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and related work","sourceOffset":6708},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and reusable estimates","sourceOffset":9967},{"kind":"section","title":"Sewing and local electrical observations","sourceOffset":13588},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The discrete graph and its continuum background","sourceOffset":14804},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Projections and planar topology","sourceOffset":20507},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite instruction graphs and numerical tests","sourceOffset":25277},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact cores and the selector sandwich","sourceOffset":30718},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The conditional law of enlarged outputs","sourceOffset":46734},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Spatial locality and changes of quantum units","sourceOffset":54695}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Linear clock and conditional laws","label":"thm:main","text":"For every finite $T>0$, define \\[ Q_n^T=\\Law\\bigl((X^n_{nt})_{0\\le t\\le T}\\mid M_n,T_n\\bigr), \\qquad Q_h^T=\\Law\\bigl((B^h_t)_{0\\le t\\le T}\\mid h\\bigr). \\] With the spatial normalization \\eqref{eq:spatial-normalization}, \\[ (V_n,a_nd_n,\\mu_n,Q_n^T) \\xrightarrow{\\ d\\ }(S,D_h,\\mu_h,Q_h^T) \\qquad\\text{for }\\delta_T, \\] through all positive integers $n$. Thus the deterministic clock multiplier for \\eqref{eq:walk-generator} and \\eqref{eq:continuum-form} is $c=1$.","sourceOffset":5710,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Sewing and continuum background","label":"inp:sewing","text":"The following couplings and normalizations will be used. \\begin{enumerate} \\item A uniform rooted map--spanning-tree pair with $n$ edges is encoded by the axial walk conditioned to remain in $\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}^2$ and return to zero after $2n$ steps. The preceding rules recover every primal and dual edge with its incidences. \\item Give the bilateral walk an independent Poisson clock of rate $m$ and multiply its heights by $m^{-1/2}$. As $m\\to\\infty$, its interpolated contour converges locally uniformly in distribution to $Z=(L,R)$, whose independent Brownian coordinates have variance $1/2$ per unit time. The limiting contour has the peanosphere coupling with a $\\sqrt2$-quantum cone $h$ and an independent whole-plane space-filling $\\mathrm{SLE}_8$, subsequently parameterized by $\\mu_h$. Write this curve as $\\eta$ and normalize $\\eta(0)=0$. \\item The imaginary field $\\widehat h$ is independent of $h$ and is a whole-plane GFF modulo $2\\pi\\chi\\mathbb Z$, with uniform phase in one period. It determines the oriented curve before area parameterization. The fields, curve, and contour are retained with their joint peanosphere law. \\item A finite interval of contour increments determines its abstract decorated quantum surface, its restricted curve, and the quantum lengths of its incoming and outgoing frontiers, modulo conformal coordinates. The analogous statement holds for every symmetric interior interval of the Brownian-excursion encoding of the ordinary unit-area sphere. \\end{enumera","sourceOffset":16335,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Finite packages","label":"inp:packages","text":"A master block consists of two increment strings on a time interval $[0,\\ell_b]$, starting at zero, together with a finite list of internal cuts. A finite package contains finitely many master blocks. For each coordinate $a\\in\\{L,R\\}$ choose a forest on these blocks and record its height differences $o^a_{bd}=s^a_d-s^a_b$. The values $s^a_b$ are thereby determined within each forest component up to one additive constant. A test chooses finitely many active windows, each between recorded cuts of a master block. Each active window initially has its own copy of the contour vertices. Its starting ","sourceOffset":26482,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/211.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":206113,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":17,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Linear-Clock-for-Random-Walk-on-Tree-Weighted-Planar-Maps-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"211-04","slug":"Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026","familyId":"211","title":"Canonical conformal limits of subcritical FK planar maps","abstract":"For every fixed $0\\lt q\\lt 4$, we prove joint convergence of spherical Fortuin–Kasteleyn planar maps in their flag-triangle uniformization to the corresponding unit-area Liouville quantum gravity sphere decorated by an independent conformal loop ensemble. The convergence includes the area measure, deterministically rescaled graph distances between all vertex pairs, and the full nested interface collection, with interfaces converging uniformly up to reparameterization.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Critical Fortuin–Kasteleyn planar maps converge to Liouville quantum gravity spheres for $0\\lt q\\le4$ and to the Brownian continuum random tree for q > 4, establishing the surface-to-tree geometric transition. For FK–Ising and spanning-tree-weighted maps, stationary random walks converge to Liouville Brownian motion on the limiting sphere. The FK–Ising spectral result also gives convergence of eigenvalues and heat traces, using the stated Brownian/LQG inputs.","pdfPath":"preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-model.tex","preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-encoding.tex","preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/figures/flag-quadrangle.tex","preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-topology.tex","preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-local-data.tex","preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-kernels.tex","preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/figures/kernel-word-arrangement.tex","preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-annuli.tex","preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-conformal.tex","preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/figures/conformal-transverse.tex","preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-loops.tex","preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/figures/loop-surgery-sides.tex","preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-metric.tex","preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/figures/metric-gate.tex","preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-completion.tex","preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/11-joint.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed $0<q<4$, we prove joint convergence of spherical Fortuin--Kasteleyn planar maps in their flag-triangle uniformization to the corresponding unit-area Liouville quantum gravity sphere decorated by an independent conformal loop ensemble. The convergence includes the area measure, deterministically rescaled graph distances between all vertex pairs, and the full nested interface collection, with interfaces converging uniformly up to reparameterization.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":530},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The result","sourceOffset":4476},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The intrinsic input","sourceOffset":7298},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":9213},{"kind":"section","title":"The model, normalization and topologies","sourceOffset":16182},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite FK law","sourceOffset":16260},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Flag triangles and the fixed conformal coordinate","sourceOffset":17778},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The continuum object","sourceOffset":21303},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Distance graphs and loop matching","sourceOffset":22302},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Extraction conventions","sourceOffset":24034},{"kind":"section","title":"The inventory encoding and its reference surfaces","sourceOffset":25087},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exact inventory law","sourceOffset":25825}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Joint canonical limit","label":"intro:main","text":"There is a deterministic sequence $a_n=a_n(q)>0$ with $a_n\\to0$ such that, through all positive integers, \\begin{equation} \\big((\\phi_n)_*m_n,K_n(a_n),\\Gamma_n\\big) \\ \\Longrightarrow\\ \\big(\\mu_h,\\{(z,w,D_h(z,w)):z,w\\in\\Chat\\},\\Gamma\\big). \\end{equation} The three topologies are weak convergence of probability measures, Hausdorff convergence of the full distance graphs, and uniform reparameterization matching of loop collections as defined in \\cref{intro:loop-topology}. Moreover, \\begin{equation} \\max_{T\\text{ a flag triangle of }X_n} \\diam_{\\rho}\\phi_n(T)\\ \\longrightarrow\\ 0 \\qquad\\text{in probability}, \\end{equation} where $\\rho$ is spherical distance. The conditional law of $\\Gamma$ is independent of the embedded marked quantum surface.","sourceOffset":5906,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Intrinsic spherical scaling; companion consequence","label":"intro:intrinsic-input","text":"For the finite law of \\cref{intro:finite-law} and $\\gamma$ in \\eqref{intro:parameters}, there is a deterministic sequence $a_n>0$ with $a_n\\to0$ such that \\[ (V(M_n),a_n d_n)\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ (S,D_h) \\] in the Gromov--Hausdorff topology on compact metric spaces up to isometry, through all positive integers. The limit is the ordinary unit-area $\\gamma$-LQG sphere of \\citet[Definition 4.21(ii)]{DMSMating}, disintegrated at total quantum area one, with its marked points forgotten and with the fixed intrinsic metric normalization. The root and FK decoration are forgotten in this convergence.","sourceOffset":7332,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Finite ensemble","label":"intro:finite-law","text":"Let $\\mathcal F_n$ consist of pairs $(M,A)$, where $M$ is a connected planar multigraph with $n$ edges embedded in the oriented sphere and rooted at a distinguished oriented edge, and $A\\subseteq E(M)$. Pairs are identified under orientation-preserving homeomorphisms preserving the root and $A$. Loops and multiple edges are allowed. Let $M^*$ be the dual map, and let $A^*$ consist of the dual edges crossing $E(M)\\setminus A$. Write $k(A)$ and $k(A^*)$ for the numbers of connected components of the respective spanning subgraphs, including isolated vertices. Set \\[ \\ell(M,A)=k(A)+k(A^*)-1 =2k(A)","sourceOffset":16292,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Loop and ensemble convergence","label":"intro:loop-topology","text":"Our curve distance and locally finite multiset topology follow \\citet[Section 2.2]{GwynneMillerQianCLE}, with loop orientation additionally forgotten. They retain the order within each loop and its multiplicity. For continuous loops $\\alpha,\\beta:\\mathbb S^1\\to\\Chat$, let \\[ d_{\\mathrm{loop}}(\\alpha,\\beta) =\\inf_{\\sigma}\\sup_{t\\in\\mathbb S^1} \\rho(\\alpha(t),\\beta(\\sigma(t))), \\] where $\\sigma$ ranges over circle homeomorphisms of either orientation. Identify loops at zero distance. A loop collection is locally finite in diameter if it has finitely many loops of diameter greater than $\\varepsil","sourceOffset":22690,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/211.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":472705,"sourceFileCount":19,"statementCount":32,"definitionCount":5,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Canonical-conformal-limits-of-subcritical-FK-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"211-05","slug":"The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026","familyId":"211","title":"The critical Liouville quantum sphere and geometric limits of FK maps at q=4","abstract":"We construct the field and area law of the unit-area critical Liouville quantum sphere as a limit of ordinary subcritical quantum spheres, and equip it with its critical intrinsic metric. We then prove that spherical Fortuin–Kasteleyn planar maps at q = 4, embedded by their equilateral flag uniformizations, converge jointly to this sphere decorated by an independent nested conformal loop ensemble CLE4. With deterministic distance normalization, the convergence includes the area measure, the full embedded distance function, and every macroscopic interface through all positive integer edge counts.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Critical Fortuin–Kasteleyn planar maps converge to Liouville quantum gravity spheres for $0\\lt q\\le4$ and to the Brownian continuum random tree for q > 4, establishing the surface-to-tree geometric transition. For FK–Ising and spanning-tree-weighted maps, stationary random walks converge to Liouville Brownian motion on the limiting sphere. The FK–Ising spectral result also gives convergence of eigenvalues and heat traces, using the stated Brownian/LQG inputs.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/build/figures/flags.tex","preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-dependencies.tex","preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-continuum.tex","preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-discrete.tex","preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-proxy.tex","preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/build/figures/stack-labels.tex","preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-cutting.tex","preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-walls.tex","preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-kernels.tex","preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-conformal.tex","preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-metric.tex","preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/build/figures/protected-shortcut.tex","preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-endpoints.tex","preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-address.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct the field and area law of the unit-area critical Liouville quantum sphere as a limit of ordinary subcritical quantum spheres, and equip it with its critical intrinsic metric. We then prove that spherical Fortuin--Kasteleyn planar maps at $q=4$, embedded by their equilateral flag uniformizations, converge jointly to this sphere decorated by an independent nested conformal loop ensemble $\\CLE_4$. With deterministic distance normalization, the convergence includes the area measure, the full embedded distance function, and every macroscopic interface through all positive integer edge counts.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction and statements","sourceOffset":654},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite model and its canonical embedding","sourceOffset":6067},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The independently specified continuum law","sourceOffset":10449},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Joint convergence","sourceOffset":13732},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof structure and reusable arguments","sourceOffset":19127},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":26249},{"kind":"section","title":"Construction of the critical quantum sphere","sourceOffset":27016},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local Gaussian inputs and conventions","sourceOffset":27547},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The maximum-centered subcritical law","sourceOffset":37845},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform area control on the whole cylinder","sourceOffset":41934},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Norm convergence and the three area marks","sourceOffset":47456},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local laws and derivative area in sampled coordinates","sourceOffset":50712}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Joint critical universality","label":"thm:main","text":"For the finite law \\eqref{intro:fk-weight}, there is a deterministic sequence $a_n>0$, with $a_n\\to0$, such that through all positive integers $n$, \\begin{equation} \\bigl((\\phi_n)_*m_n,K_n(a_n),\\Gamma_n\\bigr) \\ \\xrightarrow{\\ d\\ }\\ \\bigl(\\mu_h^{\\rm crit}, \\{(z,w,D_h(z,w)):z,w\\in S^2\\},\\Gamma\\bigr). \\end{equation} Here $(H,\\mu_h^{\\rm crit})\\sim\\mathsf P_{\\rm crit}$, the area topology is weak convergence, the distance-graph topology is the Hausdorff topology just defined, and the loop topology is convergence by $\\eps$-matchings. All nesting and multiplicities are retained. In the same joint convergence, \\begin{equation} \\max_{T\\ \\text{flag of }X_n}\\diam_\\rho\\phi_n(T) \\ \\xrightarrow{\\ \\Prob\\ }0. \\end{equation} The metric normalization is the one fixed in Theorem \\ref{thm:continuum}; changing it by a deterministic factor changes $a_n$ by that factor.","sourceOffset":14624,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"The critical area-marked sphere","label":"thm:continuum","text":"As $\\gamma\\uparrow2$, the laws of $(H_\\gamma,\\nu_\\gamma)$ converge to a unique nondegenerate probability law $\\mathsf P_{\\rm crit}$ in \\[ H^{-1}(S^2,g_{\\rm round})\\times\\mathcal P(S^2), \\] with the norm topology in the first factor and the weak topology in the second. Let $(H,\\nu)$ have this law and set $h=H+\\log g_{\\rm round}$ in local conformal coordinates. Then $\\nu=\\mu_h^{\\rm crit}$, where on compact sets avoiding $0,1,\\infty$, \\begin{equation} \\mu_h^{\\rm crit}(\\dd z) =\\lim_{\\eps\\downarrow0} \\bigl(2\\log(1/\\eps)-h_\\eps(z)\\bigr) \\eps^2e^{2h_\\eps(z)}\\,\\dd^2z \\end{equation} locally weakly in probability. This positive measure extends across the three marks without atoms, has full support, and has total mass one. After forgetting the three marks, sampling three new conditionally independent area points and normalizing them to $0,1,\\infty$ recovers $\\mathsf P_{\\rm crit}$. If $f$ maps the new coordinate to the old one, the field transforms as \\[ h_{\\rm new}=h_{\\rm old}\\circ f+2\\log|f'|. \\] With any one fixed deterministic normalization of the Ding--Gwynne critical local metric, the compatible chart metrics define an intrinsic length metric on the sphere punctured at the three marks. Its completion adds exactly those marks and is a finite continuous metric $D_h$ on $S^2$, inducing the usual topology. The area and completed metric are measurable functions of the same field and obey the corresponding charge-$2$ coordinate rules.","sourceOffset":11815,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"cont:local-ac-definition","text":"A field on an open planar set is locally absolutely continuous modulo constants if, in a neighborhood of each deterministic point, its restriction modulo constant functions is absolutely continuous with respect to the corresponding restriction of a whole-plane GFF modulo constants. Statements on an open set use a countable cover. When several neighborhoods are involved, we specify joint absolute continuity of their restrictions.","sourceOffset":34634,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Successful annulus","label":"cont:successful-annulus","text":"Fix $\\delta_0>0$. An annulus at $(p,r)$ is successful with constant $C$ if the following three conclusions hold in the original coordinates. \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\roman*)] \\item For $x,y$ on the old circles of radii $2r,4r$, respectively, $\\widetilde d(x,y)$ is at most $1+\\delta_0$ times the $d$-length of every path joining them within that closed band. \\item The $d$-distance between any two points of the old $2r$-circle is at most $C$ times the infimum of the $d$-lengths crossing the band $[2r,4r]$. \\item There is a $\\widetilde d$-rectifiable loop of winding one about $p$, lying in the ol","sourceOffset":65847,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/211.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":842022,"sourceFileCount":18,"statementCount":47,"definitionCount":13,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-critical-Liouville-quantum-sphere-and-geometric-limits-of-FK-maps-at-q-equals-4-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"211-06","slug":"Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026","familyId":"211","title":"Metric-measure limits of subcritical FK and spanning-tree planar maps","abstract":"We resolve the finite spherical cases of Gwynne and Miller's graph-metric conjecture for critical Fortuin–Kasteleyn maps at each fixed $q\\in(0,4)$ and for uniform spanning-tree-decorated maps. After deterministic rescaling of graph distances, these maps, equipped with the vertex probability measure proportional to degree, converge in Gromov–Hausdorff–Prokhorov law to their ordinary unit-area Liouville quantum gravity spheres. Distances use every primal edge, and convergence holds through all positive integer edge counts.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Critical Fortuin–Kasteleyn planar maps converge to Liouville quantum gravity spheres for $0\\lt q\\le4$ and to the Brownian continuum random tree for q > 4, establishing the surface-to-tree geometric transition. For FK–Ising and spanning-tree-weighted maps, stationary random walks converge to Liouville Brownian motion on the limiting sphere. The FK–Ising spectral result also gives convergence of eigenvalues and heat traces, using the stated Brownian/LQG inputs.","pdfPath":"preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-measured-convergence.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-framework.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-brownian-inputs.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-raw-word-fragment.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-generic-chords-fragment.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-finite-diagram-fragment.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-protected-charts.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-topology.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-local-kernels.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/figures/local-resampling.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-comparison-rigidity.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/common-passage.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/common-annuli.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/common-rigidity.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/figures/forcing-corridors.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/common-relative-units.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/fk-normalization.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-arms-entropy.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-sorted-tree-fragment.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-address-fragment.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/figures/regular-slots.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-finite-views.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/figures/seed-coarsening.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-endpoint-modulus.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/tree/background.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/tree/moments.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/tree/certificates.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/figures/tree/triple-contact.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/tree/coherent-background.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/tree/comparison.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/tree/bootstrap.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-finite-sphere.tex","preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/sections/tree/sphere.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We resolve the finite spherical cases of Gwynne and Miller's graph-metric conjecture for critical Fortuin--Kasteleyn maps at each fixed \\(q\\in(0,4)\\) and for uniform spanning-tree-decorated maps. After deterministic rescaling of graph distances, these maps, equipped with the vertex probability measure proportional to degree, converge in Gromov--Hausdorff--Prokhorov law to their ordinary unit-area Liouville quantum gravity spheres. Distances use every primal edge, and convergence holds through all positive integer edge counts.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":594},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The ensembles and the limit","sourceOffset":1552},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":5292},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":8904},{"kind":"section","title":"Sampling and deterministic normalization","sourceOffset":12986},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From sampled distances to measured convergence","sourceOffset":13365},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removing a deterministic subsequential multiplier","sourceOffset":21849},{"kind":"part","title":"Critical FK maps","sourceOffset":25081},{"kind":"section","title":"Encoding and continuum geometry","sourceOffset":25106},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The discrete and continuum encodings","sourceOffset":26200},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ordinary patches of the sphere","sourceOffset":33637},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The reference intrinsic metric","sourceOffset":40315}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Metric and measure convergence","label":"thm:main","text":"For each fixed \\(q\\in(0,4)\\) in the FK ensemble, and for the spanning-tree ensemble, there are deterministic positive numbers \\(a_n\\to0\\) such that \\[ (V(M_n),a_nd_n,\\mu_n) \\xrightarrow[n\\to\\infty]{\\mathrm{law}} (S,D_h,\\mu_h) \\] in Gromov--Hausdorff--Prokhorov topology on compact metric probability spaces modulo measure-preserving isometry. The limit has the parameter specified above. Convergence holds through all positive integers \\(n\\); the root and decoration are forgotten.","sourceOffset":4030,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Contour and surface inputs","label":"bg:continuum-inputs","text":"After fixed deterministic changes of time and height units: \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\roman*)] \\item The bilateral resolved contour, viewed at time scale \\(m\\) and height scale \\(\\sqrt m\\), converges locally uniformly in law to a two-dimensional Brownian motion with equal coordinate variances and correlation \\(\\rho\\). The empty-word contour at total time \\(2n\\), with total rescaled time one, converges to the corresponding quadrant excursion from zero to zero. \\item The bilateral Brownian contour determines a curve-decorated \\(\\gamma\\)-quantum cone. Its curve \\(\\eta:\\R\\to\\C\\) is continuous, transient, space filling, and parameterized by quantum area. The excursion determines the ordinary unit-area DMS quantum sphere with its area-parameterized space-filling curve. The latter sphere is the quantum-sphere measure disintegrated at area one. \\item The spatial equivalence relation of the sewing is generated by finite chains of scalar horizontal chords. For a scalar coordinate \\(X\\), a horizontal chord joins \\(s<t\\) if \\[ X(s)=X(t)=\\inf_{[s,t]}X. \\] At the deterministic or independently sampled cuts used below, the two boundary strands are contour-tree lineages parameterized by quantum boundary length. In the cone coupling, the intrinsic decorated surface of each fixed bounded time interval is determined by its contour increments. This assertion may be used simultaneously for a countable collection of deterministic cuts or cuts sampled independently of the surface, and for later sel","sourceOffset":28602,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Universal endpoint observations","label":"bg:universal-tests","text":"For each finite chart graph, retain in addition the following countable family of variables. Given two finite unions \\(J_1,J_2\\) of rational time intervals, select vertices having at least one representative in the respective unions. A rational chart confinement specifies the allowed vertex and step indices by a finite union of such intervals; the confined graph consists of the specified vertices and those specified edges whose endpoints are both allowed. Take the supremum over the selected endpoint pairs of their minimal distance in this confined graph, divided by \\(s_m\\). A disconnected pair","sourceOffset":92096,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Families of local passage laws","label":"common:admissible","text":"A family of local passage laws consists of laws of the reference continuum input together with countable closed collections of path certificates and their local restrictions. It has the following properties. \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\roman*)] \\item Certificates retain endpoints, compact ranges, every finite number of ordered subpaths, and nonnegative additive cost bounds. Finite certificates have actual path realizations with arbitrary open loss; bounded-cost realizing paths have subsequential certificates. Individual edge costs tend to zero, and ordered marking retains the subdivision of any ","sourceOffset":236761,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/211.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":888672,"sourceFileCount":36,"statementCount":64,"definitionCount":6,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Metric-measure-limits-of-subcritical-FK-and-spanning-tree-planar-maps-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"211-07","slug":"Brownian-continuum-random-tree-limits-of-finite-Fortuin-Kasteleyn-maps-above-four-September-24-2026","familyId":"211","title":"Brownian continuum random tree limits of finite Fortuin–Kasteleyn maps above four","abstract":"We prove the finite-volume continuum-random-tree prediction for critical Fortuin–Kasteleyn planar maps at every fixed q > 4. After rescaling graph distances by a constant times n−1/2, an n-edge map with normalized degree measure converges to the Brownian continuum random tree in the Gromov–Hausdorff–Prokhorov topology. The convergence holds through all positive integer sizes.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Critical Fortuin–Kasteleyn planar maps converge to Liouville quantum gravity spheres for $0\\lt q\\le4$ and to the Brownian continuum random tree for q > 4, establishing the surface-to-tree geometric transition. For FK–Ising and spanning-tree-weighted maps, stationary random walks converge to Liouville Brownian motion on the limiting sphere. The FK–Ising spectral result also gives convergence of eigenvalues and heat traces, using the stated Brownian/LQG inputs.","pdfPath":"preprints/Brownian-continuum-random-tree-limits-of-finite-Fortuin-Kasteleyn-maps-above-four-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Brownian-continuum-random-tree-limits-of-finite-Fortuin-Kasteleyn-maps-above-four-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Brownian-continuum-random-tree-limits-of-finite-Fortuin-Kasteleyn-maps-above-four-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Brownian-continuum-random-tree-limits-of-finite-Fortuin-Kasteleyn-maps-above-four-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Brownian-continuum-random-tree-limits-of-finite-Fortuin-Kasteleyn-maps-above-four-September-24-2026/build/sections/history.tex","preprints/Brownian-continuum-random-tree-limits-of-finite-Fortuin-Kasteleyn-maps-above-four-September-24-2026/build/sections/words.tex","preprints/Brownian-continuum-random-tree-limits-of-finite-Fortuin-Kasteleyn-maps-above-four-September-24-2026/build/sections/ladders.tex","preprints/Brownian-continuum-random-tree-limits-of-finite-Fortuin-Kasteleyn-maps-above-four-September-24-2026/build/sections/blocks.tex","preprints/Brownian-continuum-random-tree-limits-of-finite-Fortuin-Kasteleyn-maps-above-four-September-24-2026/build/figures/corner-substitution.tex","preprints/Brownian-continuum-random-tree-limits-of-finite-Fortuin-Kasteleyn-maps-above-four-September-24-2026/build/sections/trees.tex","preprints/Brownian-continuum-random-tree-limits-of-finite-Fortuin-Kasteleyn-maps-above-four-September-24-2026/build/sections/metric.tex","preprints/Brownian-continuum-random-tree-limits-of-finite-Fortuin-Kasteleyn-maps-above-four-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Brownian-continuum-random-tree-limits-of-finite-Fortuin-Kasteleyn-maps-above-four-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the finite-volume continuum-random-tree prediction for critical Fortuin--Kasteleyn planar maps at every fixed $q>4$. After rescaling graph distances by a constant times $n^{-1/2}$, an $n$-edge map with normalized degree measure converges to the Brownian continuum random tree in the Gromov--Hausdorff--Prokhorov topology. The convergence holds through all positive integer sizes.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":435},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite model and the main result","sourceOffset":1226},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the ingredients of the proof","sourceOffset":3139},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof roadmap","sourceOffset":9178},{"kind":"section","title":"Words and strict drift","sourceOffset":11380},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reduction of words","sourceOffset":12286},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An exact counting identity","sourceOffset":14584},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The first backward survivor","sourceOffset":22870},{"kind":"section","title":"Ladder cuts and a coefficient lower bound","sourceOffset":28238},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Stationary cuts","sourceOffset":30332},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Stationary outputs and matching","sourceOffset":39791},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Matching at a seam","sourceOffset":45101}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed real $q>4$, there is a deterministic constant $c(q)\\in(0,\\infty)$ such that, as $n\\to\\infty$ through all positive integers, \\[ \\big(V(M_n),c(q)n^{-1/2}d_n,\\mu_n\\big) \\ \\Longrightarrow\\ (\\mathcal T_e,d_e,\\mu_e) \\] in the Gromov--Hausdorff--Prokhorov topology on compact metric probability spaces modulo measure-preserving isometry.","sourceOffset":2536,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Finite word identity","label":"word:identity","text":"For every integer $n\\ge0$, an independent word of length $2n$ satisfies \\begin{equation} \\P(\\text{the word is empty-reducing})=a_nu^n. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":15196,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":null,"text":"For a two-sided iid sequence of $L$-pieces, a boundary $a$ is an $L$-cut if, for every $b>a$, the word between $a$ and $b$ has no unfilled orders when evaluated from an empty supply. For a two-sided iid sequence of $R$-pieces, a boundary $b$ is an $R$-cut if, for every $a<b$, the word between $a$ and $b$ leaves no surviving burgers.","sourceOffset":32937,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/211.md","scopeText":"For every fixed $q>4$, the formalization proves the Brownian continuum-random-tree limit for critical finite Fortuin–Kasteleyn planar maps. After rescaling graph distances in an $n$-edge map by a constant depending on $q$ times $n^{-1/2}$ and using normalized degree measure, the metric-measure space converges in distribution to the Brownian continuum random tree in the Gromov–Hausdorff–Prokhorov topology. The convergence holds through all positive integer sizes.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/211.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":87940,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Brownian-continuum-random-tree-limits-of-finite-Fortuin-Kasteleyn-maps-above-four-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"212-01","slug":"No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026","familyId":"212","title":"No bigeodesics in planar first-passage percolation","abstract":"We prove that planar first-passage percolation with independent identically distributed nonnegative nonatomic edge weights has almost surely no doubly infinite geodesic, provided the minimum of four independent weights has finite second moment. This resolves the planar no-bigeodesics conjecture under that moment assumption. The conclusion rules out all bigeodesics simultaneously, without any regularity assumption on the limit shape.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that planar first-passage percolation has no doubly infinite geodesic for iid nonnegative nonatomic edge weights when the minimum of four weights has finite second moment. For exponential weights, the limit shape is strictly convex with C1 boundary. Differentiability also holds for every Gamma law with positive shape and rate.","pdfPath":"preprints/No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-preliminaries.tex","preprints/No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-sectors.tex","preprints/No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026/build/figures/01-trapping.tex","preprints/No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-exceptional.tex","preprints/No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-budget.tex","preprints/No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-forks.tex","preprints/No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-tilts.tex","preprints/No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026/build/figures/02-union.tex","preprints/No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-conclusion.tex","preprints/No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-boundary-comparison.tex","preprints/No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that planar first-passage percolation with independent identically distributed nonnegative nonatomic edge weights has almost surely no doubly infinite geodesic, provided the minimum of four independent weights has finite second moment. This resolves the planar no-bigeodesics conjecture under that moment assumption. The conclusion rules out all bigeodesics simultaneously, without any regularity assumption on the limit shape.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":482},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background","sourceOffset":2571},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The argument","sourceOffset":5982},{"kind":"section","title":"Geodesics, Busemann functions, and structural input","sourceOffset":10611},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The metric and its rays","sourceOffset":10700},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Structural input","sourceOffset":14420},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform estimates and finite changes","sourceOffset":18843},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Measurability conventions","sourceOffset":21250},{"kind":"section","title":"Sectors, order, and confinement","sourceOffset":24006},{"kind":"section","title":"Exceptional labels and opposite coalescence","sourceOffset":47923},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Calibrated rays and countability","sourceOffset":48656},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Independent environments and exceptional parameters","sourceOffset":55833}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Suppose that \\(G\\) has no atoms and that, for independent \\(X_1,X_2,X_3,X_4\\) with law \\(G\\), \\begin{equation} \\EE\\!\\left[\\min\\{X_1,X_2,X_3,X_4\\}^{\\,2}\\right]<\\infty. \\end{equation} Then, with probability one, there is no sequence \\((v_i)_{i\\in\\Z}\\) of pairwise distinct vertices with consecutive vertices adjacent and \\[ \\sum_{k=i}^{j-1}t_{\\{v_k,v_{k+1}\\}}=T(v_i,v_j) \\qquad\\text{for every }i<j. \\]","sourceOffset":1612,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Ahlberg--Hoffman and the shape theorem","label":"thm:structure","text":"There are a deterministic norm \\(m\\) on \\(\\R^2\\) and a deterministic closed subset \\(C\\subseteq\\mathcal S\\) with the following properties. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item Almost surely, \\[ T(0,x)=m(x)+o(|x|)\\qquad (|x|\\to\\infty). \\] \\item Almost surely, simultaneously for all rays \\(p\\) from all lattice vertices, there is a unique \\(\\rho_p\\in C\\) such that \\begin{equation} B_p(0,x)=\\rho_p(x)+o(|x|)\\qquad (|x|\\to\\infty). \\end{equation} The limiting directions of \\(p\\) lie in \\(A(\\rho_p)\\). At each vertex the set of realized functionals is \\(C\\). \\item For each fixed \\(\\rho\\in C\\), almost surely there is exactly one ray \\(g_\\rho(x)\\) with functional \\(\\rho\\) from each \\(x\\in\\Z^2\\). These rays coalesce. Their family is backwards finite: for each vertex \\(z\\), the set of \\(x\\) for which \\(g_\\rho(x)\\) contains \\(z\\) is finite. \\end{enumerate} The norm \\(m\\) and the set \\(C\\) have the rotation and reflection symmetries of the square lattice.","sourceOffset":15276,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/212.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":151892,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/No-bigeodesics-in-planar-first-passage-percolation-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"212-02","slug":"Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026","familyId":"212","title":"Strict convexity and differentiability of the planar exponential first-passage limit shape","abstract":"We prove that the limit shape of undirected nearest-neighbor first-passage percolation on ℤ2 with independent exponential edge weights is strictly convex and has a C1 boundary. This resolves the strict convexity and differentiability conjectures for the planar exponential model. More generally, we prove differentiability of the time-constant norm for every Gamma edge-weight law with positive shape and rate.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that planar first-passage percolation has no doubly infinite geodesic for iid nonnegative nonatomic edge weights when the minimum of four weights has finite second moment. For exponential weights, the limit shape is strictly convex with C1 boundary. Differentiability also holds for every Gamma law with positive shape and rate.","pdfPath":"preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026/build/sections/differentiability/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026/build/sections/differentiability/crossings.tex","preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026/build/sections/differentiability/scales.tex","preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026/build/sections/differentiability/endpoints.tex","preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026/build/sections/differentiability/witnesses.tex","preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026/build/sections/differentiability/conclusion.tex","preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026/build/sections/strict/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026/build/sections/strict/foundations.tex","preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026/build/sections/strict/phases.tex","preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026/build/sections/strict/probability.tex","preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026/build/sections/strict/access.tex","preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026/build/sections/strict/links.tex","preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026/build/sections/strict/improvement.tex","preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026/build/sections/strict/conclusion.tex","preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the limit shape of undirected nearest-neighbor first-passage percolation on $\\Z^2$ with independent exponential edge weights is strictly convex and has a $C^1$ boundary. This resolves the strict convexity and differentiability conjectures for the planar exponential model. More generally, we prove differentiability of the time-constant norm for every Gamma edge-weight law with positive shape and rate.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":502},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":3979},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":6739},{"kind":"part","title":"Differentiability for Gamma weights","sourceOffset":11288},{"kind":"section","title":"Shape estimates and supporting functionals","sourceOffset":11363},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform shape and local sums","sourceOffset":11817},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two extreme supports","sourceOffset":14408},{"kind":"section","title":"Localized crossings","sourceOffset":16839},{"kind":"section","title":"Characteristic lengths","sourceOffset":35215},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A first crossing threshold","sourceOffset":35906},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Scales compatible with endpoint extensions","sourceOffset":42093},{"kind":"section","title":"Endpoint extensions","sourceOffset":43126}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"The exponential limit shape","label":"thm:exponential-shape","text":"For independent exponential edge weights of any rate $\\lambda>0$, the norm $\\mu$ is Fr\\'echet differentiable on $\\R^2\\setminus\\{0\\}$, and its unit ball is strictly convex. In particular, \\[ \\mu((1-t)x+ty)<1 \\quad\\text{if }x,y\\in\\partial\\Bcal,\\ x\\ne y,\\ 0<t<1, \\] and $\\partial\\Bcal$ is a $C^1$ curve with a unique supporting line at every point.","sourceOffset":1293,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Differentiability for Gamma weights","label":"thm:gamma-differentiability","text":"For independent Gamma edge weights with any fixed shape $\\kappa>0$ and rate $\\lambda>0$, the norm $\\mu$ is Fr\\'echet differentiable at every nonzero vector, and $\\partial\\Bcal$ is a $C^1$ curve.","sourceOffset":2033,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Admissible road","label":"def:fb-road","text":"For $d\\in\\{A,B\\}$, an admissible $d$-road is a measurable oriented two-sided lattice walk together with a continuous nondecreasing nominal parameter on its edge occurrences. The nominal parameter tends to $-\\infty$ and $+\\infty$ at the two ends. Every compact parameter interval contains finitely many edge occurrences. A point with nominal parameter $t$ satisfies $\\|x-td\\|_*\\le r$. The construction uses the edge environment and a private product field $U=(U_\\iota)_{\\iota\\in\\mathcal I}$ independent of it. Each coordinate has a spatial location and, when needed, a road-position or auxiliary type ","sourceOffset":88909,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/212.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves differentiability of the planar first-passage time-constant norm for independent Gamma edge weights of every positive shape and rate. The norm is differentiable away from the origin, and its unit sphere has a $C^1$ boundary. This includes the exponential model, for which the linked statement also gives a unique supporting line at every boundary point.\n\nThe paper also claims strict convexity for the exponential limit shape. Strict convexity is outside these selected differentiability statements.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/212.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":332542,"sourceFileCount":17,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Strict-convexity-and-differentiability-of-the-planar-exponential-first-passage-limit-shape-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"213-01","slug":"Critical-bond-and-site-percolation-on-the-cubic-lattice-September-24-2026","familyId":"213","title":"Critical bond and site percolation on the cubic lattice","abstract":"We prove that nearest-neighbor Bernoulli bond and site percolation on ℤ3 have no infinite cluster at their respective critical parameters. The proof combines a finite connection inequality for independent hyperedges with a finite-scale extension estimate and an adaptive exploration.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the Benjamini–Schramm criticality conjecture for bond percolation on every infinite connected locally finite quasi-transitive graph with $p_c\\lt 1$: at the critical probability, there is almost surely no infinite cluster. The family also establishes this conclusion for both nearest-neighbor bond and site percolation on ℤ3.","pdfPath":"preprints/Critical-bond-and-site-percolation-on-the-cubic-lattice-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Critical-bond-and-site-percolation-on-the-cubic-lattice-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Critical-bond-and-site-percolation-on-the-cubic-lattice-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Critical-bond-and-site-percolation-on-the-cubic-lattice-September-24-2026/build/critical-percolation-z3.tex","preprints/Critical-bond-and-site-percolation-on-the-cubic-lattice-September-24-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/Critical-bond-and-site-percolation-on-the-cubic-lattice-September-24-2026/build/comparison.tex","preprints/Critical-bond-and-site-percolation-on-the-cubic-lattice-September-24-2026/build/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Critical-bond-and-site-percolation-on-the-cubic-lattice-September-24-2026/build/figures/quarterfaces.tex","preprints/Critical-bond-and-site-percolation-on-the-cubic-lattice-September-24-2026/build/extension.tex","preprints/Critical-bond-and-site-percolation-on-the-cubic-lattice-September-24-2026/build/geometry.tex","preprints/Critical-bond-and-site-percolation-on-the-cubic-lattice-September-24-2026/build/exploration.tex","preprints/Critical-bond-and-site-percolation-on-the-cubic-lattice-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Critical-bond-and-site-percolation-on-the-cubic-lattice-September-24-2026/build/critical-percolation-z3.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that nearest-neighbor Bernoulli bond and site percolation on $\\mathbb Z^3$ have no infinite cluster at their respective critical parameters. The proof combines a finite connection inequality for independent hyperedges with a finite-scale extension estimate and an adaptive exploration.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":340},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite connection comparison","sourceOffset":9332},{"kind":"section","title":"Planar boundaries and finite scales","sourceOffset":24098},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A deterministic boundary count","sourceOffset":24441},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Seed boxes and quarter-faces","sourceOffset":27838},{"kind":"section","title":"Extending a connection through a fresh region","sourceOffset":36314},{"kind":"section","title":"Relaying between neighboring cubes","sourceOffset":45413},{"kind":"section","title":"Adaptive exploration and the final contradiction","sourceOffset":55045},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exploration and its conditional promise","sourceOffset":55618},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A uniform bound on bad vertices","sourceOffset":61356},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Contours and completion of the proof","sourceOffset":65315}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For nearest-neighbor Bernoulli bond percolation on $\\mathbb Z^3$, there is almost surely no infinite open cluster at the bond critical parameter. The same assertion holds for nearest-neighbor Bernoulli site percolation at the site critical parameter.","sourceOffset":1073,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Joint connection comparison","label":"cmp:joint","text":"In independent percolation on a finite hypergraph, with arbitrary hyperedge probabilities in $[0,1]$, let $o$ be a node and let $A,T$ be node sets with $A\\ne\\varnothing$. Then \\begin{equation} \\mathbb P(o\\leftrightarrow A,\\ o\\leftrightarrow T) \\ge \\mathbb P(o\\leftrightarrow A) \\min_{a\\in A}\\mathbb P(a\\leftrightarrow T). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":10117,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/213.md","scopeText":"The critical-percolation question asks whether an infinite cluster can remain at the threshold. The formalized result proves that, at their respective critical probabilities, nearest-neighbor bond and site percolation on $\\mathbb Z^3$ almost surely have no infinite cluster. Equivalently, almost surely every vertex belongs to a finite cluster in each model.\n\nThe formalization proves absence of an infinite cluster at criticality for Bernoulli bond percolation on every infinite connected locally finite quasi-transitive graph with critical probability $p_c<1$. At $p=p_c$, the probability that any infinite cluster exists is zero. The graph model permits bond multiplicities.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/213.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":72304,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Critical-bond-and-site-percolation-on-the-cubic-lattice-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"213-02","slug":"No-percolation-at-criticality-on-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026","familyId":"213","title":"No percolation at criticality on quasi-transitive graphs","abstract":"We prove that critical Bernoulli bond percolation has no infinite cluster on any infinite connected locally finite quasi-transitive graph with critical probability less than one. This resolves the bond form of the criticality conjecture of Benjamini and Schramm.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the Benjamini–Schramm criticality conjecture for bond percolation on every infinite connected locally finite quasi-transitive graph with $p_c\\lt 1$: at the critical probability, there is almost surely no infinite cluster. The family also establishes this conclusion for both nearest-neighbor bond and site percolation on ℤ3.","pdfPath":"preprints/No-percolation-at-criticality-on-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/No-percolation-at-criticality-on-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/No-percolation-at-criticality-on-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/No-percolation-at-criticality-on-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/No-percolation-at-criticality-on-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/No-percolation-at-criticality-on-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/No-percolation-at-criticality-on-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/two-cluster-dfs-entropy.tex","preprints/No-percolation-at-criticality-on-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/interpolation.tex","preprints/No-percolation-at-criticality-on-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/figures/interpolation.tex","preprints/No-percolation-at-criticality-on-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/superpolynomial.tex","preprints/No-percolation-at-criticality-on-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/gluing.tex","preprints/No-percolation-at-criticality-on-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/structure.tex","preprints/No-percolation-at-criticality-on-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/moves.tex","preprints/No-percolation-at-criticality-on-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/figures/moves.tex","preprints/No-percolation-at-criticality-on-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/corridor-exploration.tex","preprints/No-percolation-at-criticality-on-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/figures/corridors.tex","preprints/No-percolation-at-criticality-on-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/assembly.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/No-percolation-at-criticality-on-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that critical Bernoulli bond percolation has no infinite cluster on any infinite connected locally finite quasi-transitive graph with critical probability less than one. This resolves the bond form of the criticality conjecture of Benjamini and Schramm.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":325},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two subexponential arguments","sourceOffset":2124},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and related methods","sourceOffset":5528},{"kind":"section","title":"Uniformity and the growth alternatives","sourceOffset":10622},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Graph conventions and the threshold","sourceOffset":10942},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ball comparisons","sourceOffset":14019},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact clusters and finite witnesses","sourceOffset":16154},{"kind":"section","title":"Two clusters, depth-first search, and entropy","sourceOffset":17083},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Adjacent large distinct clusters","sourceOffset":17478},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Clusters conditioned to avoid one another","sourceOffset":22180},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A depth-first pivotal second moment","sourceOffset":25501},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Entropy of overlapping transcripts","sourceOffset":33894}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $G$ be an infinite connected locally finite quasi-transitive undirected graph. If $\\pc(G)<1$, then \\[ \\PP_{\\pc(G)}(\\text{there is an infinite open cluster})=0. \\] Equivalently, $\\PP_{\\pc(G)}(|C_x|=\\infty)=0$ for every vertex $x$.","sourceOffset":1426,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Hutchcroft {\\cite[Theorem 1.2","label":"thm:exponential","text":"{Hutchcroft2016}}] If a connected locally finite quasi-transitive graph satisfies $\\liminf_{r\\to\\infty}|B(x,r)|^{1/r}>1$, then it has almost surely no infinite Bernoulli bond cluster at its critical probability.","sourceOffset":15379,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/213.md","scopeText":"The critical-percolation question asks whether an infinite cluster can remain at the threshold. The formalized result proves that, at their respective critical probabilities, nearest-neighbor bond and site percolation on $\\mathbb Z^3$ almost surely have no infinite cluster. Equivalently, almost surely every vertex belongs to a finite cluster in each model.\n\nThe formalization proves absence of an infinite cluster at criticality for Bernoulli bond percolation on every infinite connected locally finite quasi-transitive graph with critical probability $p_c<1$. At $p=p_c$, the probability that any infinite cluster exists is zero. The graph model permits bond multiplicities.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/213.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":139318,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/No-percolation-at-criticality-on-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"214-01","slug":"Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026","familyId":"214","title":"Nonuniqueness of percolation on nonamenable quasi-transitive graphs","abstract":"We prove the bond-percolation nonuniqueness conjecture of Benjamini and Schramm: every infinite connected, locally finite, nonamenable quasi-transitive graph has a nonempty interval of parameters for which Bernoulli bond percolation almost surely has infinitely many infinite clusters. We also prove Hutchcroft's stronger operator-threshold conjecture, establishing $p_c\\lt p_{2\\to2}\\le p_u$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves $p_c\\lt p_u$ for Bernoulli bond percolation on every infinite connected locally finite nonamenable quasi-transitive graph, resolving the Benjamini–Schramm nonuniqueness conjecture. Thus there is a nonempty range of probabilities with infinitely many infinite clusters. A stronger operator bound also establishes the critical triangle condition.","pdfPath":"preprints/Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/goodness.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/amplification.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/figures/splicing.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/figures/joint-inverse.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/moments.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/trace.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/deformation.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/figures/corridor.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/paths.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/figures/escape-templates.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the bond-percolation nonuniqueness conjecture of Benjamini and Schramm: every infinite connected, locally finite, nonamenable quasi-transitive graph has a nonempty interval of parameters for which Bernoulli bond percolation almost surely has infinitely many infinite clusters. We also prove Hutchcroft's stronger operator-threshold conjecture, establishing $p_c<p_{2\\to2}\\le p_u$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":503},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The graph, the thresholds, and the main result","sourceOffset":1359},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":5229},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Critical behavior and exponential connectivity","sourceOffset":10857},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and the methods used here","sourceOffset":14381},{"kind":"section","title":"Preliminaries and the unimodular reduction","sourceOffset":23306},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Graph reductions and the two established inputs","sourceOffset":24022},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Product inequalities and operator bounds","sourceOffset":27692},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Averaging over types and a finite trace","sourceOffset":33032},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A symmetric walk with a spectral gap","sourceOffset":36692},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Subcritical susceptibility, differentiation, and sprinkling","sourceOffset":38656},{"kind":"section","title":"Critical clusters remain controlled after deletions","sourceOffset":46007}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $G$ be an infinite connected locally finite quasi-transitive graph with $h_V(G)>0$. For Bernoulli bond percolation on $G$, \\[ \\sup_{0\\le p<\\pc}\\norm{T_p}_{2\\to2} =\\norm{T_{\\pc}}_{2\\to2}<\\infty, \\qquad \\pc(G)<\\ptwo(G)\\le\\pu(G). \\] There exist $\\pc<p_1<p_2<1$ such that, in the coupling obtained from independent uniform edge labels, almost surely there are infinitely many infinite open clusters simultaneously for every $p\\in[p_1,p_2]$.","sourceOffset":3229,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Arbitrary Cayley graphs","label":"cor:cayley","text":"Let $\\Lambda$ be a nonamenable finitely generated group, and let $S$ be any finite symmetric generating set with the identity removed. For Bernoulli bond percolation on the simple undirected Cayley graph with edges $\\{x,xs\\}$, $x\\in\\Lambda$, $s\\in S$, the critical connection operator is bounded on counting-measure $\\ell^2(\\Lambda)$ and \\[ \\pc<\\ptwo\\le\\pu. \\] The simultaneous nonuniqueness interval in \\cref{thm:main} exists for every such fixed generating set. At every fixed $p\\in(\\pc,\\pu)$, there are almost surely infinitely many infinite open clusters.","sourceOffset":4234,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Pivotal distance and good clusters","label":"good:definition","text":"A bridge of a connected open graph is an open edge whose deletion disconnects that graph. The pivotal distance between two vertices is the number of bridges separating them. Equivalently, delete all bridges, contract the resulting connected pieces, and measure distance in the resulting tree. Write $\\operatorname{diam}_{\\mathrm{piv}}$ for the maximum pivotal distance and $\\operatorname{rad}_{\\mathrm{piv}}(H,x)$ for its maximum from $x$. Fix $0<\\epsilon<1/2$ and set $s=1/2+\\epsilon$. A finite open cluster $C$ with $|C|\\le M$ is good for cap $M$ if the following holds for every set $A\\subseteq C$","sourceOffset":46508,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Local endpoint map","label":"amp:map","text":"A local endpoint map on $O$ is a randomized rule at each $x\\in O$ that reads percolation bits in a deterministic finite ball about $x$ and uses auxiliary randomness independent of those bits. Its conditional rules are covariant under automorphisms. The rule either fails or outputs $y\\in C_x\\cap O$. A map with integer cap $M$ is required, on every successful input pattern, to have $s_x\\le M$ and $C_x$ good for cap $M$. Write $u>0$ for its success probability and \\[ \\begin{gathered} K(x,y)=\\Prob(\\text{output }y\\mid\\text{success at }x), \\qquad r=\\norm K,\\\\ U=\\log(1/u),\\quad m=\\log M,\\quad H=\\log(","sourceOffset":60747,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/214.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves the nonuniqueness phase conjecture for Bernoulli bond percolation on infinite connected locally finite nonamenable quasi-transitive graphs. It establishes $p_c<p_{2\\to2}\\le p_u$ and a common nonempty coupled interval with infinitely many infinite clusters almost surely. For Cayley graphs, the result holds for every finite symmetric generating set of a nonamenable finitely generated group and every $p\\in(p_c,p_u)$.\n\nThe selected critical estimates include a finite triangle diagram, susceptibility of order $(p_c-p)^{-1}$, percolation probability of order $p-p_c$, cluster-volume tail of order $n^{-1/2}$, and intrinsic and extrinsic radius tails of order $n^{-1}$. Connection probabilities decay exponentially below $p_{2\\to2}$.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/214.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":171117,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Nonuniqueness-of-percolation-on-nonamenable-quasi-transitive-graphs-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"215-01","slug":"The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026","familyId":"215","title":"The canonical massive continuum limit of the two-dimensional O(3) model","abstract":"We construct a canonical interacting massive continuum limit of the two-dimensional nearest-neighbor $O(3)$ model with unit-length spins, no external field, and no topological term. Normalized by susceptibility and second-moment correlation length, the limit exists as the bare coupling tends to infinity through all positive real values, without selecting subsequences. The limiting fields satisfy the Osterwalder–Schrader axioms and have a nonzero connected four-point correlation on separated time supports. Their reconstructed theory has a unique vacuum, a nonzero vacuum complement, and a positive Hamiltonian gap on that entire complement.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs the canonical continuum limit of the two-dimensional nearest-neighbor $O(3)$ model: a non-Gaussian local relativistic theory with a unique vacuum and a positive mass gap. For the square-lattice $O(4)$ model, determines the exact leading asymptotic of the full transfer gap, $m_{\\mathrm{lat}}(\\beta)\\sim32e^{\\pi/4-1/2}\\sqrt\\beta\\,e^{-\\pi\\beta}$. The family also proves exponential spin-correlation decay for two-dimensional nearest-neighbor $O(n)$ models with n ≥ 3 at every positive temperature.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/massive-continuum-o3.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/massive-continuum-o3.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/reference-locators.tex","preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/sections/history.tex","preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/sections/method-history.tex","preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/sections/preliminary.tex","preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/sections/setup.tex","preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/sections/free.tex","preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/sections/rg.tex","preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/sections/shooting.tex","preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/sections/comparison.tex","preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/figures/editable.tex","preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/sections/trace.tex","preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/sections/sources.tex","preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/sections/continuum.tex","preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/figures/orientations.tex","preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/sections/os.tex","preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/sections/trajectories.tex","preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/sections/volume.tex","preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/sections/fields.tex","preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/sections/uniqueness.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a canonical interacting massive continuum limit of the two-dimensional nearest-neighbor $O(3)$ model with unit-length spins, no external field, and no topological term. Normalized by susceptibility and second-moment correlation length, the limit exists as the bare coupling tends to infinity through all positive real values, without selecting subsequences. The limiting fields satisfy the Osterwalder--Schrader axioms and have a nonzero connected four-point correlation on separated time supports. Their reconstructed theory has a unique vacuum, a nonzero vacuum complement, and a positive Hamiltonian gap on that entire complement.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":718},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":1823},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Canonical normalization and the main result","sourceOffset":7580},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Construction and physical properties","sourceOffset":10803},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and new estimates","sourceOffset":13778},{"kind":"part","title":"Construction at a fixed physical scale","sourceOffset":21449},{"kind":"section","title":"A preliminary correlation length","sourceOffset":21496},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pinned squares, currents, and rotation identities","sourceOffset":24031},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A uniform decrease of stiffness","sourceOffset":29030},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A strict saving on the first square","sourceOffset":47003},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From small stiffness to local decorrelation","sourceOffset":50773},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Neutral observables and completion of the proposition","sourceOffset":57295}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are a dyadic integer $L>1$, a constant $H>0$, bare couplings $\\beta_N\\to\\infty$, and field normalizations $B_N>0$, such that \\begin{equation} a_N=L^{-N},\\qquad \\beta_N=H+\\frac{\\log L}{2\\pi}N+O(\\log(N+1)), \\end{equation} and the following assertions hold for the model \\eqref{eq:lattice-model}. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item Every finite list of the variables $\\phi_N(f)=\\phi_{a_N,B_N}(f)$, under $\\mu_{\\beta_N}$, converges jointly in law and in all moments. The component Schwinger distributions converge in $\\mathcal S'((\\R^2)^n)$ at every order. The same limits are obtained with suitable periodic physical tori tending to the plane as $N\\to\\infty$. \\item The limiting Schwinger distributions are Euclidean invariant, internally $O(3)$ invariant, reflection positive, symmetric and clustering. For every component distribution and $F\\in\\mathcal S((\\R^2)^n)$, \\[ |S_n(F)|\\le C^n n!\\sup_{x\\in(\\R^2)^n} (1+|x|^2)^{2n}|F(x)|, \\] with a constant $C$ independent of $n$. They reconstruct a local, unitary, relativistic theory in $1+1$ dimensions. \\item The reconstructed theory has a unique vacuum $\\Omega$, a nonzero vacuum complement, and a Hamiltonian $H_{\\mathrm{phys}}$ satisfying \\[ H_{\\mathrm{phys}}\\big|_{\\Omega^\\perp}\\ge m\\Id \\quad\\text{for some }m>0. \\] \\item A connected four-point distribution is nonzero on smooth tests with strictly separated time supports. In particular, the limiting field correlations do not satisfy Wick factorization. \\end{enumerate} The traj","sourceOffset":11065,"truncated":true},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Canonical continuum limit","label":"thm:canonical","text":"For the nearest-neighbor probability \\eqref{eq:lattice-model}, the periodic thermodynamic limit $\\mu_\\beta$ exists for all sufficiently large $\\beta$. Both sums in \\eqref{eq:canonical-lattice-scales} are finite, $\\chi_\\beta,\\xi_\\beta>0$, and $\\xi_\\beta\\to\\infty$ as $\\beta\\to\\infty$. The following limits hold through all real values of $\\beta$ tending to infinity. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item For every finite list of vector tests in $C_c^\\infty(\\R^2;\\R^3)$, the fields \\eqref{eq:canonical-field} converge jointly in law and in all joint moments to one common limiting hierarchy. \\item For every order and every choice of spin components, the associated Schwinger distributions converge in the strong topology of $\\mathcal S'((\\R^2)^n)$, that is, uniformly on bounded sets of Schwartz tests. \\item The limiting hierarchy has susceptibility and second-moment length equal to one. It is the hierarchy constructed in Theorem \\ref{thm:main}, after the unique positive constant length and field rescalings imposing these two normalizations. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":9355,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/215.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":436131,"sourceFileCount":21,"statementCount":36,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"215-02","slug":"An-Isolated-Particle-Pole-for-the-Two-Dimensional-O3-Spin-Field-October-4-2026","familyId":"215","title":"An Isolated Particle Pole for the Two-Dimensional O(3) Spin Field","abstract":"We consider the continuum spin field constructed from the nearest-neighbor two-dimensional $O(3)$ model by the fixed prescription of the companion paper. We prove that its vector two-point spectral measure has a positive atom corresponding to the lowest mass, separated by a positive gap from all remaining mass support.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs the canonical continuum limit of the two-dimensional nearest-neighbor $O(3)$ model: a non-Gaussian local relativistic theory with a unique vacuum and a positive mass gap. For the square-lattice $O(4)$ model, determines the exact leading asymptotic of the full transfer gap, $m_{\\mathrm{lat}}(\\beta)\\sim32e^{\\pi/4-1/2}\\sqrt\\beta\\,e^{-\\pi\\beta}$. The family also proves exponential spin-correlation decay for two-dimensional nearest-neighbor $O(n)$ models with n ≥ 3 at every positive temperature.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-Isolated-Particle-Pole-for-the-Two-Dimensional-O3-Spin-Field-October-4-2026/o3-particle-pole.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-Isolated-Particle-Pole-for-the-Two-Dimensional-O3-Spin-Field-October-4-2026/o3-particle-pole.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-Isolated-Particle-Pole-for-the-Two-Dimensional-O3-Spin-Field-October-4-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/An-Isolated-Particle-Pole-for-the-Two-Dimensional-O3-Spin-Field-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/An-Isolated-Particle-Pole-for-the-Two-Dimensional-O3-Spin-Field-October-4-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/An-Isolated-Particle-Pole-for-the-Two-Dimensional-O3-Spin-Field-October-4-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/An-Isolated-Particle-Pole-for-the-Two-Dimensional-O3-Spin-Field-October-4-2026/build/sections/02-inputs.tex","preprints/An-Isolated-Particle-Pole-for-the-Two-Dimensional-O3-Spin-Field-October-4-2026/build/sections/03-transfer.tex","preprints/An-Isolated-Particle-Pole-for-the-Two-Dimensional-O3-Spin-Field-October-4-2026/build/sections/04-parity.tex","preprints/An-Isolated-Particle-Pole-for-the-Two-Dimensional-O3-Spin-Field-October-4-2026/build/sections/05-visibility.tex","preprints/An-Isolated-Particle-Pole-for-the-Two-Dimensional-O3-Spin-Field-October-4-2026/build/figures/circuits.tex","preprints/An-Isolated-Particle-Pole-for-the-Two-Dimensional-O3-Spin-Field-October-4-2026/build/sections/06-counting.tex","preprints/An-Isolated-Particle-Pole-for-the-Two-Dimensional-O3-Spin-Field-October-4-2026/build/sections/07-mass-support.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-Isolated-Particle-Pole-for-the-Two-Dimensional-O3-Spin-Field-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We consider the continuum spin field constructed from the nearest-neighbor two-dimensional $O(3)$ model by the fixed prescription of the companion paper. We prove that its vector two-point spectral measure has a positive atom corresponding to the lowest mass, separated by a positive gap from all remaining mass support.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":419},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The field and the result","sourceOffset":899},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and methods","sourceOffset":2701},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":5778},{"kind":"section","title":"The continuum field and the uniform lattice inputs","sourceOffset":8281},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The bottom of the two-point mass support","sourceOffset":12403},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform tails for smeared spins","sourceOffset":15035},{"kind":"section","title":"Transfer spectra and volume comparison","sourceOffset":16838},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positive transfer and its energies","sourceOffset":17119},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From square defects to arbitrary rectangles","sourceOffset":19355},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Slab insertions and comparison with the plane","sourceOffset":22141},{"kind":"section","title":"The even transfer sector","sourceOffset":26980}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"An isolated lowest mass","label":"intro:main","text":"For the field $\\phi$ just specified, there exist $m_1,Z,\\delta>0$ and a positive measure $\\rho_{\\mathrm{rest}}$ such that \\begin{equation} \\rho=Z\\delta_{m_1^2}+\\rho_{\\mathrm{rest}}, \\qquad \\supp\\rho_{\\mathrm{rest}}\\subset[(m_1+\\delta)^2,\\infty). \\end{equation} In particular, $m_1$ is the smallest mass in the support of the spin-field two-point function.","sourceOffset":1769,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Inputs from the continuum construction","label":"inp:contracts","text":"There are integers $K_0,M_0>0$ and constants $c,C>0$ with the following properties. Write $w_k=M_0 2^k$, and let $Z_N(t,w)$ be the partition function on a torus of time length $t$ and spatial circumference $w$. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item In the cutoff plane, all joint moments of the variables \\eqref{inp:field} converge to those of the Euclidean field $\\phi$. The limiting Schwinger distributions are tempered, Euclidean invariant, and $O(3)$ covariant. Their Osterwalder--Schrader reconstruction is the Wightman field of Theorem \\ref{intro:main}, with unique vacuum $\\Omega$ and \\[ H_{\\mathrm{phys}}|_{\\Omega^\\perp}\\ge m>0. \\] For some real test $f$ supported strictly in positive time, the one-field reflection norm $\\E[\\phi^1(\\theta f)\\phi^1(f)]$ is strictly positive. \\item For all $N\\ge K_0$ and $k\\ge0$, \\begin{equation} 0\\le \\Delta_N(w_k) :=4\\log Z_N(w_k,w_k)-\\log Z_N(2w_k,2w_k) \\le C e^{-c w_k}. \\end{equation} \\item On these square tori and on the cutoff plane, the component moment measures \\[ S_{r,N}^{\\boldsymbol i} =(B_Na^2)^r\\sum_{x_1,\\ldots,x_r} \\E\\!\\left[\\prod_{j=1}^r q_{x_j}^{i_j}\\right] \\delta_{(x_1,\\ldots,x_r)} \\] are nonnegative. For arbitrary translated unit boxes $Q_1,\\ldots,Q_r$, \\begin{equation} S_{r,N}^{\\boldsymbol i}(Q_1\\times\\cdots\\times Q_r) \\le C^r r!, \\end{equation} uniformly in $N\\ge K_0$, the square torus, the box positions, and the component indices. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":9903,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/215.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":80187,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-Isolated-Particle-Pole-for-the-Two-Dimensional-O3-Spin-Field-October-4-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"215-03","slug":"Exact-mass-asymptotics-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-lattice-model-October-5-2026","familyId":"215","title":"Exact mass asymptotics for the two-dimensional O(4) lattice model","abstract":"For the nearest-neighbor $O(4)$ model on the square lattice at inverse temperature β, we prove the exact low-temperature asymptotic $\\displaystyle m_{\\mathrm{lat}}(\\beta)\\sim 32\\exp(\\pi/4-1/2)\\sqrt\\beta\\,\\exp(-\\pi\\beta) \\qquad (\\beta\\to\\infty).$ Here the mass is the gap of the full Osterwalder–Schrader transfer operator, including rotation-invariant local-observable sectors, in units of one original lattice time step.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs the canonical continuum limit of the two-dimensional nearest-neighbor $O(3)$ model: a non-Gaussian local relativistic theory with a unique vacuum and a positive mass gap. For the square-lattice $O(4)$ model, determines the exact leading asymptotic of the full transfer gap, $m_{\\mathrm{lat}}(\\beta)\\sim32e^{\\pi/4-1/2}\\sqrt\\beta\\,e^{-\\pi\\beta}$. The family also proves exponential spin-correlation decay for two-dimensional nearest-neighbor $O(n)$ models with n ≥ 3 at every positive temperature.","pdfPath":"preprints/Exact-mass-asymptotics-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-lattice-model-October-5-2026/exact-mass-o4.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Exact-mass-asymptotics-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-lattice-model-October-5-2026/exact-mass-o4.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Exact-mass-asymptotics-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-lattice-model-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Exact-mass-asymptotics-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-lattice-model-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Exact-mass-asymptotics-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-lattice-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Exact-mass-asymptotics-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-lattice-model-October-5-2026/build/figures/comparison.tex","preprints/Exact-mass-asymptotics-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-lattice-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-wilson.tex","preprints/Exact-mass-asymptotics-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-lattice-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-brownian.tex","preprints/Exact-mass-asymptotics-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-lattice-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-bethe.tex","preprints/Exact-mass-asymptotics-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-lattice-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-bethe-line.tex","preprints/Exact-mass-asymptotics-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-lattice-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-bethe-transfer.tex","preprints/Exact-mass-asymptotics-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-lattice-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-rg.tex","preprints/Exact-mass-asymptotics-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-lattice-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-comparison.tex","preprints/Exact-mass-asymptotics-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-lattice-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-matching.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Exact-mass-asymptotics-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-lattice-model-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For the nearest-neighbor \\(O(4)\\) model on the square lattice at inverse temperature \\(\\beta\\), we prove the exact low-temperature asymptotic \\[ m_{\\mathrm{lat}}(\\beta)\\sim 32\\exp(\\pi/4-1/2)\\sqrt\\beta\\,\\exp(-\\pi\\beta) \\qquad (\\beta\\to\\infty). \\] Here the mass is the gap of the full Osterwalder--Schrader transfer operator, including rotation-invariant local-observable sectors, in units of one original lattice time step.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":488},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and the result","sourceOffset":1045},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":3925},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":6374},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and conventions","sourceOffset":11625},{"kind":"section","title":"Spin correlations and the full transfer spectrum","sourceOffset":12952},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conditional rotators and correlation signs","sourceOffset":13780},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The covariance comparison","sourceOffset":19285},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Consequences for the plane and finite cylinders","sourceOffset":27462},{"kind":"section","title":"Cylinder masses and a partition-function rate","sourceOffset":36634},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The imported Wilson trajectory bounds","sourceOffset":37027},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Trace tails and rectangle identities","sourceOffset":41175}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For the measures \\eqref{eq:gibbs}, the full transfer gap \\eqref{eq:mass-definition} satisfies \\begin{equation} \\lim_{\\beta\\to\\infty} \\frac{\\exp(\\pi\\beta)}{\\sqrt\\beta}\\,m_{\\mathrm{lat}}(\\beta) =32\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac\\pi4-\\frac12\\right). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":2969,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Finite-graph sector comparison","label":"prop:wilson-covariance","text":"For the law \\eqref{eq:wilson-graph-law}, let $P,Q$ be spin monomials of positive degrees $r,s$ with site lists $\\mathcal X,\\mathcal Y$, and put \\[ G=\\max_{x\\in\\mathcal X,\\ y\\in\\mathcal Y}\\mathbb E_J(q_x^1q_y^1). \\] Then $G\\ge0$, and a constant $C_{r,s}$ depending only on the degrees satisfies \\begin{equation} |\\operatorname{Cov}_J(P,Q)|\\le C_{r,s} \\begin{cases} G^2,&r,s\\ \\text{even},\\\\ G,&r,s\\ \\text{odd},\\\\ 0,&r+s\\ \\text{odd}. \\end{cases} \\end{equation} If either monomial is constant, its covariance is zero. The estimate is independent of the finite graph and its nonnegative couplings.","sourceOffset":19325,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Retained density","label":"def:rg-retained-class","text":"A retained density is an expression \\begin{equation} \\exp\\!\\left\\{i\\theta\\sum_{e_t}u_e\\right\\} \\exp\\{V|\\Lambda|-B\\mathcal E-X_{\\rm can}-X_{\\rm err}\\}\\,\\Xi, \\end{equation} with the four kinetic coefficients in the bands above and the numerical energy $\\mathcal E$ formed from the direct terms \\eqref{eq:R4} and the enabled second-stack squares. If $\\mathcal P_a$ denotes a canonical label, including its displayed prefactor in \\eqref{eq:R5}, write \\[ X_{\\rm can}=\\sum_a1_{X_a}\\mathcal P_a, \\qquad X_{\\rm err}=\\sum_X1_Xf_X. \\] Here $X_a$ is the declared graph of the complete polynomial label, includin","sourceOffset":207406,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/215.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":388689,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Exact-mass-asymptotics-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-lattice-model-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"215-04","slug":"Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026","familyId":"215","title":"Sharp mass bounds for the two-dimensional O(4) model","abstract":"We prove sharp mass bounds for the two-dimensional nearest-neighbor $O(4)$ model. For all sufficiently large inverse couplings β, the full transfer gap, including rotation-invariant local-observable sectors, is bounded above and below by positive multiples of $\\sqrt\\beta e^{-\\pi\\beta}$. We also prove that the model has a unique periodic local limit and a positive full gap at every finite β > 0, resolving the all-temperature lattice mass-generation conjecture for this periodic state. Under the stated cutoff scaling, the mass bounds are uniform in independently fixed physical units. A continuum spectral interpretation requires separate convergence hypotheses for the transfer semigroup and the block observables.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs the canonical continuum limit of the two-dimensional nearest-neighbor $O(3)$ model: a non-Gaussian local relativistic theory with a unique vacuum and a positive mass gap. For the square-lattice $O(4)$ model, determines the exact leading asymptotic of the full transfer gap, $m_{\\mathrm{lat}}(\\beta)\\sim32e^{\\pi/4-1/2}\\sqrt\\beta\\,e^{-\\pi\\beta}$. The family also proves exponential spin-correlation decay for two-dimensional nearest-neighbor $O(n)$ models with n ≥ 3 at every positive temperature.","pdfPath":"preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/sections/finite-volume.tex","preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/figures/rectangle-doubling.tex","preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/sections/preliminary.tex","preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/sections/linear.tex","preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/sections/renormalization.tex","preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/sections/calibration.tex","preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/sections/trajectories.tex","preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/figures/matched-trajectories.tex","preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/sections/comparison.tex","preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/sections/assembly.tex","preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/figures/fixed-reference-box.tex","preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/sections/upper.tex","preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/figures/block-supports.tex","preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/sections/physical.tex","preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/supplements/analytic-rg.tex","preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/supplements/canonical-norm.tex","preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/supplements/geometry-rg.tex","preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/supplements/joint-majorants.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove sharp mass bounds for the two-dimensional nearest-neighbor $O(4)$ model. For all sufficiently large inverse couplings $\\beta$, the full transfer gap, including rotation-invariant local-observable sectors, is bounded above and below by positive multiples of $\\sqrt\\beta e^{-\\pi\\beta}$. We also prove that the model has a unique periodic local limit and a positive full gap at every finite $\\beta>0$, resolving the all-temperature lattice mass-generation conjecture for this periodic state. Under the stated cutoff scaling, the mass bounds are uniform in independently fixed physical units. A continuum spectral interpretation requires separate convergence hypotheses for the transfer semigroup and the block observables.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":816},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Methods and their relation to earlier work","sourceOffset":8688},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":12118},{"kind":"subsubsection","title":"A finite box controls the full spectrum","sourceOffset":12550},{"kind":"subsubsection","title":"The preliminary estimate: currents, stiffness, and mixing","sourceOffset":14801},{"kind":"subsubsection","title":"Comparing cutoffs at a fixed coarse coupling","sourceOffset":18243},{"kind":"subsubsection","title":"Choose the reference box once, then refine the cutoff","sourceOffset":22996},{"kind":"subsubsection","title":"A block correlation bounds the mass above","sourceOffset":24708},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite-volume criterion for the full gap","sourceOffset":28395},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Transfer eigenvalues and a measure of excited-state weight","sourceOffset":28770},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Increasing the spatial volume","sourceOffset":31256},{"kind":"section","title":"Preliminary mixing","sourceOffset":37853}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are finite constants $\\beta_0,c,C$, with $0<c<C$, such that for every $\\beta\\ge\\beta_0$ the periodic square measures have a unique local limit, and its full transfer gap satisfies \\begin{equation} c\\sqrt\\beta\\,e^{-\\pi\\beta}\\le\\gap(\\beta) \\le C\\sqrt\\beta\\,e^{-\\pi\\beta}. \\end{equation} There are also a fixed dyadic blocking factor $L$, a fixed large terminal coupling $H$, and finite integers $N_0,M_0$ with the following property. For every $N\\ge N_0$ one can choose a bare coupling $\\beta_N$ whose exact $N$-step block transformation has terminal kinetic coupling $H$, and \\begin{equation} \\frac{\\log2}{M_0L^N}\\le\\gap(\\beta_N) \\le\\frac{\\log8}{L^N}. \\end{equation} The same bounds hold uniformly for terminal couplings in a fixed small interval about $H$, after increasing $M_0$ and $N_0$ if necessary.","sourceOffset":6103,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Full gap at every positive temperature","label":"cor:all-temperature","text":"For every finite $\\beta>0$, the measures \\eqref{eq:measure} on periodic squares have a unique local limit. There are absolute constants $c,C>0$ such that its full transfer gap obeys \\[ \\gap(\\beta)\\ge c\\exp\\{-\\pi\\beta- C\\sqrt{(1+\\beta)\\log(2+\\beta)}\\}>0. \\]","sourceOffset":7035,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"A mandatory covering sum","label":"cmp:cover-definition","text":"A label \\(\\lambda\\) consists of a finite site support \\(P_\\lambda\\), a load \\(s_\\lambda\\ge1\\), and a fixed nonempty inventory \\(J_\\lambda\\) of bonds whose endpoints belong to \\(P_\\lambda\\). Its weight \\(k_\\lambda(q)\\) is a bounded measurable function of \\(q|_{P_\\lambda}\\); every eligibility indicator is included in this function. In particular \\(k_\\lambda(q)=0\\) unless \\(J_\\lambda\\subset D(q)\\). The support contains every spin read by the function or by an eligibility test, including a test asserting the absence of a flag. A family is compatible when its supports are pairwise disjoint. The man","sourceOffset":227645,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/215.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":349222,"sourceFileCount":20,"statementCount":15,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"215-05","slug":"Exponential-decay-in-two-dimensional-classical-On-models-September-23-2026","familyId":"215","title":"Exponential decay in two-dimensional classical O(n) models","abstract":"We prove exponential decay of two-point correlations for the classical nearest-neighbor $O(n)$ model on the square lattice, for every n ≥ 3 and every finite positive inverse temperature. The estimate is uniform over finite free-boundary subgraphs and bounded nonnegative edge strengths. This resolves positively the all-temperature exponential spin-decay conjecture for these models.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs the canonical continuum limit of the two-dimensional nearest-neighbor $O(3)$ model: a non-Gaussian local relativistic theory with a unique vacuum and a positive mass gap. For the square-lattice $O(4)$ model, determines the exact leading asymptotic of the full transfer gap, $m_{\\mathrm{lat}}(\\beta)\\sim32e^{\\pi/4-1/2}\\sqrt\\beta\\,e^{-\\pi\\beta}$. The family also proves exponential spin-correlation decay for two-dimensional nearest-neighbor $O(n)$ models with n ≥ 3 at every positive temperature.","pdfPath":"preprints/Exponential-decay-in-two-dimensional-classical-On-models-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Exponential-decay-in-two-dimensional-classical-On-models-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Exponential-decay-in-two-dimensional-classical-On-models-September-23-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Exponential-decay-in-two-dimensional-classical-On-models-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Exponential-decay-in-two-dimensional-classical-On-models-September-23-2026/build/sections/rotation.tex","preprints/Exponential-decay-in-two-dimensional-classical-On-models-September-23-2026/build/sections/annulus.tex","preprints/Exponential-decay-in-two-dimensional-classical-On-models-September-23-2026/build/sections/clusters.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Exponential-decay-in-two-dimensional-classical-On-models-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove exponential decay of two-point correlations for the classical nearest-neighbor $O(n)$ model on the square lattice, for every $n\\ge3$ and every finite positive inverse temperature. The estimate is uniform over finite free-boundary subgraphs and bounded nonnegative edge strengths. This resolves positively the all-temperature exponential spin-decay conjecture for these models.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":431},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and the problem","sourceOffset":3162},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Methods and their ancestry","sourceOffset":6117},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and organization","sourceOffset":8040},{"kind":"section","title":"A fourth-order rotation estimate","sourceOffset":10361},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Gauge invariance and second variations","sourceOffset":10833},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The family inequality","sourceOffset":15199},{"kind":"section","title":"A uniform estimate for an annular twist","sourceOffset":25408},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Boundary gauge and multiscale tests","sourceOffset":27224},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From the test form to the boundary twist","sourceOffset":37026},{"kind":"section","title":"Sign clusters and exponential decay","sourceOffset":39915},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An associated amplitude and bond law","sourceOffset":40503}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $n\\ge3$ and every finite $\\beta>0$, there are constants $A=A(n,\\beta)<\\infty$ and $m=m(n,\\beta)>0$ such that, for every finite nearest-neighbor square-lattice subgraph $G$, every strength array $b\\in[0,\\beta]^E$, and all $x,y\\in V$, \\begin{equation} 0\\le \\langle\\sigma_x\\cdot\\sigma_y\\rangle_{G,b}^{(n)} \\le A\\exp(-m\\|x-y\\|_2). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":1574,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Free-boundary limits","label":"cor:limits","text":"Let $\\Lambda_L=[-L,L]^2\\cap\\Z^2$, with all its nearest-neighbor edges, free boundary conditions, and constant strength $\\beta>0$. For every $n\\ge3$ and $x\\in\\Z^2$, \\begin{equation} \\limsup_{L\\to\\infty} \\left|\\langle\\sigma_0\\cdot\\sigma_x\\rangle_{\\Lambda_L,\\beta}^{(n)}\\right| \\le A e^{-m\\|x\\|_2}, \\end{equation} with the constants of Theorem \\ref{thm:main}. Every subsequential local weak limit of these laws satisfies the same two-point bound.","sourceOffset":2007,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/215.md","scopeText":"The formalized result proves exponential decay of spin correlations for the two-dimensional classical $O(n)$ model at every temperature when $n\\ge3$. For each interaction bound $\\beta>0$, constants $A$ and $m>0$ work for every finite square-lattice subgraph with free boundary and nonnegative edge strengths at most $\\beta$: the correlation between sites $x,y$ is at most $Ae^{-m|x-y|}$. The infinite-volume limit and the separate $O(4)$ spectral-gap claim are not included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/215.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":62686,"sourceFileCount":4,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Exponential-decay-in-two-dimensional-classical-On-models-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"216-01","slug":"The-critical-logarithmic-correction-for-the-planar-XY-model-October-5-2026","familyId":"216","title":"The critical logarithmic correction for the planar XY model","abstract":"We prove the critical logarithmic correction for the nearest-neighbor cosine XY model on the square lattice. Let $C_{b_c}(r)$ be the two-point correlation at critical inverse temperature, obtained by taking the free-box thermodynamic limit while the two sites remain r lattice steps apart along a coordinate axis. Then $\\displaystyle C_{b_c}(r)=B_{\\mathrm{XY}}r^{-1/4}(\\log r)^{1/8}(1+o(1)), \\qquad B_{\\mathrm{XY}}\\in(0,\\infty),$ as $r\\to\\infty$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For the square-lattice nearest-neighbor cosine XY model, proves critical axis correlations $C_{\\beta_c}(r)\\sim Ar^{-1/4}(\\log r)^{1/8}$ and the Berezinskii–Kosterlitz–Thouless essential singularity $\\sqrt{\\beta_c-\\beta}\\log\\xi(\\beta)\\to B$, with $A,B\\gt 0$ after the free-box thermodynamic limit. For finite square-symmetric interactions containing nearest neighbors, discrete Gaussian heights converge to Gaussian fields throughout the rough phase, including its threshold, along geometric torus sizes. Critical center-magnetization and spin-field conclusions retain their stated height, renormalization, and field-input assumptions.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-critical-logarithmic-correction-for-the-planar-XY-model-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-critical-logarithmic-correction-for-the-planar-XY-model-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-critical-logarithmic-correction-for-the-planar-XY-model-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-critical-logarithmic-correction-for-the-planar-XY-model-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-critical-logarithmic-correction-for-the-planar-XY-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-critical-logarithmic-correction-for-the-planar-XY-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/heights.tex","preprints/The-critical-logarithmic-correction-for-the-planar-XY-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/The-critical-logarithmic-correction-for-the-planar-XY-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/cutoff-entry.tex","preprints/The-critical-logarithmic-correction-for-the-planar-XY-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/trajectory.tex","preprints/The-critical-logarithmic-correction-for-the-planar-XY-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/observable.tex","preprints/The-critical-logarithmic-correction-for-the-planar-XY-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex","preprints/The-critical-logarithmic-correction-for-the-planar-XY-model-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-critical-logarithmic-correction-for-the-planar-XY-model-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the critical logarithmic correction for the nearest-neighbor cosine XY model on the square lattice. Let \\(C_{b_c}(r)\\) be the two-point correlation at critical inverse temperature, obtained by taking the free-box thermodynamic limit while the two sites remain \\(r\\) lattice steps apart along a coordinate axis. Then \\[ C_{b_c}(r)=B_{\\mathrm{XY}}r^{-1/4}(\\log r)^{1/8}(1+o(1)), \\qquad B_{\\mathrm{XY}}\\in(0,\\infty), \\] as \\(r\\to\\infty\\).","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":491},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and relation to earlier work","sourceOffset":2140},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The route to the amplitude","sourceOffset":6036},{"kind":"section","title":"Height representation and critical inputs","sourceOffset":11113},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Dual partition functions","sourceOffset":11465},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The critical height inputs","sourceOffset":16398},{"kind":"section","title":"Fixed-geometry comparison","sourceOffset":23675},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Separating the insertion from the exterior","sourceOffset":24969},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A pinned comparison for the thermodynamic correlation","sourceOffset":35932},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The energies outside the nests","sourceOffset":58217},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cancellation of the microscopic costs","sourceOffset":66538},{"kind":"section","title":"A scale-dependent cutoff and entry","sourceOffset":72655}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a constant \\(B_{\\rm XY}\\in(0,\\infty)\\) such that \\[ C_{b_c}(r) =B_{\\rm XY}\\,r^{-1/4}(\\log r)^{1/8}(1+o(1)) \\qquad(r\\to\\infty,\\ r\\in\\Z). \\]","sourceOffset":1604,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Finite affine comparisons","label":"prop:comparisons","text":"On every fixed primary graph, the Bessel height law has the following properties. \\begin{enumerate} \\item A shifted-to-centered partition ratio is at most one and decreases when the same nonnegative quadratic precision is added to the affine and centered coordinates. Affine data may include an integral connection, observation centers, and compatible nonzero equality values. The monotonicity remains valid with a fixed real linear statistic in the numerator exponent, although the tilted ratio need not be at most one. \\item For zero affine data, centered covariances and centered real moment generating functions decrease under added centered precision. Centered characteristic coefficients allowed by the constant convention in both laws are nonnegative and increase under added precision. They are strictly positive for the finite-graph phases used below, whose coefficient total on every unconfined component is an integer. \\item If \\(X=\\sum_e q_e\\nabla_eh\\) in a centered law, then \\[ \\Var X\\le C_b\\sum_eq_e^2,\\qquad \\E|X|^k\\le C_{k,b}\\left(\\sum_eq_e^2\\right)^{k/2}. \\] More generally, after releasing endpoint, curl, and period constraints while retaining independent primary increments, a tilt with \\(\\max_e|q_e|\\le K\\) costs at most \\(\\exp(C_{b,K}\\sum_eq_e^2)\\). \\end{enumerate} The statements include independent Gaussian observation noises. Released constants are taken modulo translations or confined; a real statistic used in releasing a constant must annihilate it.","sourceOffset":16439,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":325351,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-critical-logarithmic-correction-for-the-planar-XY-model-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"216-02","slug":"Critical-Center-Magnetization-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026","familyId":"216","title":"Critical Center Magnetization in the Planar XY Model","abstract":"Assuming the stated critical-height, local-renormalization, and spin-field inputs from the companion papers, we determine the center magnetization of the planar XY model in a square with aligned boundary spins at its mass-defined critical threshold. As $n\\to\\infty$, the magnetization is $A_{\\mathrm{XY}}n^{-1/8}(\\log n)^{1/16}(1+o(1))$, where $A_{\\mathrm{XY}}$ is a finite, strictly positive model-specific constant.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For the square-lattice nearest-neighbor cosine XY model, proves critical axis correlations $C_{\\beta_c}(r)\\sim Ar^{-1/4}(\\log r)^{1/8}$ and the Berezinskii–Kosterlitz–Thouless essential singularity $\\sqrt{\\beta_c-\\beta}\\log\\xi(\\beta)\\to B$, with $A,B\\gt 0$ after the free-box thermodynamic limit. For finite square-symmetric interactions containing nearest neighbors, discrete Gaussian heights converge to Gaussian fields throughout the rough phase, including its threshold, along geometric torus sizes. Critical center-magnetization and spin-field conclusions retain their stated height, renormalization, and field-input assumptions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Critical-Center-Magnetization-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Critical-Center-Magnetization-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Critical-Center-Magnetization-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Critical-Center-Magnetization-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Critical-Center-Magnetization-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Critical-Center-Magnetization-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/inputs.tex","preprints/Critical-Center-Magnetization-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/preparation.tex","preprints/Critical-Center-Magnetization-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/entry.tex","preprints/Critical-Center-Magnetization-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/bulk.tex","preprints/Critical-Center-Magnetization-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/figures/histories.tex","preprints/Critical-Center-Magnetization-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/trajectory.tex","preprints/Critical-Center-Magnetization-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/magnetic.tex","preprints/Critical-Center-Magnetization-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/figures/common-core.tex","preprints/Critical-Center-Magnetization-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/terminal.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Critical-Center-Magnetization-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Assuming the stated critical-height, local-renormalization, and spin-field inputs from the companion papers, we determine the center magnetization of the planar XY model in a square with aligned boundary spins at its mass-defined critical threshold. As \\(n\\to\\infty\\), the magnetization is \\(A_{\\mathrm{XY}}n^{-1/8}(\\log n)^{1/16}(1+o(1))\\), where \\(A_{\\mathrm{XY}}\\) is a finite, strictly positive model-specific constant.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":469},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":2854},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":6600},{"kind":"section","title":"Height conventions and local map inputs","sourceOffset":9995},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Dual heights and finite comparisons","sourceOffset":10501},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The critical Gaussian coefficient","sourceOffset":15827},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparing center observables at different sizes","sourceOffset":20077},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Covariances, blocks, and analytic norms","sourceOffset":23085},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exact partition map and its balanced coordinates","sourceOffset":30949},{"kind":"section","title":"Observations, affine Gaussian shapes, and isolation","sourceOffset":39369},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Observation cells and the cutoff","sourceOffset":40009},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The affine observation identity and local lifts","sourceOffset":46590}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Using the height, renormalization, and spin-field inputs from \\cite{B,X,S} stated in Section \\ref{sec:inputs}, there is a constant \\(A_{\\mathrm{XY}}\\in(0,\\infty)\\) such that \\begin{equation} a_n=A_{\\mathrm{XY}}\\,n^{-1/8}(\\log n)^{1/16}(1+o(1)) \\qquad(n\\to\\infty). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":1965,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Finite comparison inputs","label":"inp:comparisons","text":"The comparisons in \\cite[Lemmas 2.2 and 2.3]{X} hold on every fixed primary graph, also with independent Gaussian observation noises. \\begin{enumerate} \\item Adding centered precision decreases centered covariance and centered real moment generating functions. Covariance in an affine sector, including after a real tilt, dominates centered covariance. In particular, for a centered inversion-symmetric law, \\(\\log\\E e^X\\ge\\frac12\\operatorname{Var}(X)\\). \\item A shifted-to-centered partition ratio is at most one and decreases when the same positive precision is added in the two sectors. The shifts may be integral connections, prescribed equality values, or observation centers. Monotonicity also holds with a fixed real linear statistic in the numerator exponent; that tilted ratio need not be at most one. Infinite penalties may impose nonempty affine constraints. \\item Invariant centered characteristic coefficients are nonnegative and increase with precision. In the finite-graph situations used here they are positive, and the Hessian of their logarithm at any phase is at least its Hessian at zero. A phase on a free component is invariant whenever its coefficient total is an integer. \\item With any extra centered precision, for each fixed integer \\(r\\ge1\\) and real \\(t\\), \\begin{equation} \\operatorname{Var}(X)\\le C_b\\mathcal R(w),\\qquad \\E|X|^r\\le C_{r,b}\\mathcal R(w)^{r/2},\\qquad \\E e^{tX/\\sqrt{\\mathcal R(w)}}\\le C_{t,b}. \\end{equation} The last assertion is used for \\(\\mathcal R(w","sourceOffset":13002,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":221328,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Critical-Center-Magnetization-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"216-03","slug":"The-Critical-Spin-Field-of-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026","familyId":"216","title":"The Critical Spin Field of the Planar XY Model","abstract":"Using the companion critical Bessel-height and pin-limit theorems, we prove that at the mass-defined critical inverse temperature of the nearest-neighbor XY model on the square lattice, the spin field on a square with boundary angles fixed to zero converges along the full sequence to the full-variance imaginary exponential of a zero-Dirichlet Gaussian free field with stiffness $2/\\pi$. The normalization uses the exact center magnetization and the lattice Green-function factor. Convergence holds in law in $H^{-3}_{\\mathrm{loc}}((-1,1)^2)$; all mixed moments and joint laws of fields smeared against smooth compactly supported test functions in $(-1,1)^2$ converge as well.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For the square-lattice nearest-neighbor cosine XY model, proves critical axis correlations $C_{\\beta_c}(r)\\sim Ar^{-1/4}(\\log r)^{1/8}$ and the Berezinskii–Kosterlitz–Thouless essential singularity $\\sqrt{\\beta_c-\\beta}\\log\\xi(\\beta)\\to B$, with $A,B\\gt 0$ after the free-box thermodynamic limit. For finite square-symmetric interactions containing nearest neighbors, discrete Gaussian heights converge to Gaussian fields throughout the rough phase, including its threshold, along geometric torus sizes. Critical center-magnetization and spin-field conclusions retain their stated height, renormalization, and field-input assumptions.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Critical-Spin-Field-of-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Critical-Spin-Field-of-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Critical-Spin-Field-of-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-Critical-Spin-Field-of-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Critical-Spin-Field-of-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-Critical-Spin-Field-of-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-heights.tex","preprints/The-Critical-Spin-Field-of-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-sectors.tex","preprints/The-Critical-Spin-Field-of-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/figures/slit-patch.tex","preprints/The-Critical-Spin-Field-of-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-annuli.tex","preprints/The-Critical-Spin-Field-of-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/figures/annular-bands.tex","preprints/The-Critical-Spin-Field-of-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-kernels.tex","preprints/The-Critical-Spin-Field-of-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-correlations.tex","preprints/The-Critical-Spin-Field-of-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-fields.tex","preprints/The-Critical-Spin-Field-of-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Critical-Spin-Field-of-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Using the companion critical Bessel-height and pin-limit theorems, we prove that at the mass-defined critical inverse temperature of the nearest-neighbor XY model on the square lattice, the spin field on a square with boundary angles fixed to zero converges along the full sequence to the full-variance imaginary exponential of a zero-Dirichlet Gaussian free field with stiffness \\(2/\\pi\\). The normalization uses the exact center magnetization and the lattice Green-function factor. Convergence holds in law in \\(H^{-3}_{\\mathrm{loc}}((-1,1)^2)\\); all mixed moments and joint laws of fields smeared against smooth compactly supported test functions in \\((-1,1)^2\\) converge as well.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":746},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The physical critical parameter","sourceOffset":1631},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The normalized spin field","sourceOffset":2880},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and related work","sourceOffset":7303},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":10186},{"kind":"section","title":"Free heights and finite comparisons","sourceOffset":13441},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Partition sums and fixed-spin duality","sourceOffset":13904},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The cited height limits","sourceOffset":19574},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Affine comparisons and edge restoration","sourceOffset":26178},{"kind":"section","title":"Magnetic sectors with holes of positive radius","sourceOffset":36352},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The sector theorem and its upper bound","sourceOffset":36780},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Confinement suppresses separated pin interactions","sourceOffset":42807}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For the fixed-boundary critical XY law \\eqref{eq:pinned-spin-law}, the distributions \\(S_n\\) in \\eqref{eq:spin-field} converge in law along the full sequence \\(n\\to\\infty\\) to \\(V_*\\) in \\(H^{-3}_{\\mathrm{loc}}(D)\\). Moreover, for every \\(k\\ge1\\), every \\(f_1,\\ldots,f_k\\in C_c^\\infty(D;\\C)\\), and every collection of nonnegative integers \\(p_1,q_1,\\ldots,p_k,q_k\\), \\begin{equation} \\E_n^0\\prod_{j=1}^k S_n(f_j)^{p_j}\\overline{S_n(f_j)}^{\\,q_j} \\longrightarrow \\E\\prod_{j=1}^k V_*(f_j)^{p_j}\\overline{V_*(f_j)}^{\\,q_j}. \\end{equation} The joint laws of these complex smeared fields converge as well.","sourceOffset":5588,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Critical height coefficient {\\cite[Section 6","label":"heights:critical-input","text":"{C}}] For the free-box spin mass threshold \\(b_c\\) of Section \\ref{sec:intro}, the Bessel height coefficient satisfies \\(a(b_c)=8\\pi\\).","sourceOffset":20113,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Fixed mixed geometry","label":"heights:geometry","text":"A fixed mixed geometry is a finite union of bounded axis-parallel rectangular pieces with nondegenerate widths and faces. Pieces belonging to one component meet along full interfaces, which may be subdivided into segments. Components are polygonal Lipschitz domains, and no two parts are joined solely through a point. A pin region is an aligned finite union of positive-area boxes; pinning it means setting every primary lattice height there to zero. Discretizations use the nearest-neighbor graph at mesh \\(n^{-1}\\). Boundaries may be rounded by a bounded number of lattice steps, preserving the fa","sourceOffset":22550,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":141865,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":17,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Critical-Spin-Field-of-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"216-04","slug":"Essential-Singularity-of-the-Correlation-Length-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026","familyId":"216","title":"Essential Singularity of the Correlation Length in the Planar XY Model","abstract":"We prove the Berezinskii–Kosterlitz–Thouless essential singularity for the correlation length of the nearest-neighbor cosine XY model on the square lattice. Let $m(b)$ be the mass obtained by taking first the free-box thermodynamic limit and then the separation limit along a coordinate axis. As the inverse temperature b approaches bc from the massive side, $\\displaystyle \\sqrt{b_c-b}\\log\\frac{1}{m(b)} \\longrightarrow A_{\\mathrm{XY}},$ where $A_{\\mathrm{XY}}$ is a finite, strictly positive model-specific constant.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For the square-lattice nearest-neighbor cosine XY model, proves critical axis correlations $C_{\\beta_c}(r)\\sim Ar^{-1/4}(\\log r)^{1/8}$ and the Berezinskii–Kosterlitz–Thouless essential singularity $\\sqrt{\\beta_c-\\beta}\\log\\xi(\\beta)\\to B$, with $A,B\\gt 0$ after the free-box thermodynamic limit. For finite square-symmetric interactions containing nearest neighbors, discrete Gaussian heights converge to Gaussian fields throughout the rough phase, including its threshold, along geometric torus sizes. Critical center-magnetization and spin-field conclusions retain their stated height, renormalization, and field-input assumptions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Essential-Singularity-of-the-Correlation-Length-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Essential-Singularity-of-the-Correlation-Length-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Essential-Singularity-of-the-Correlation-Length-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Essential-Singularity-of-the-Correlation-Length-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Essential-Singularity-of-the-Correlation-Length-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Essential-Singularity-of-the-Correlation-Length-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/figures/flow-profile.tex","preprints/Essential-Singularity-of-the-Correlation-Length-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/finite.tex","preprints/Essential-Singularity-of-the-Correlation-Length-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/entry.tex","preprints/Essential-Singularity-of-the-Correlation-Length-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/trajectory.tex","preprints/Essential-Singularity-of-the-Correlation-Length-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/profile.tex","preprints/Essential-Singularity-of-the-Correlation-Length-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/mass.tex","preprints/Essential-Singularity-of-the-Correlation-Length-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/figures/mass-geometry.tex","preprints/Essential-Singularity-of-the-Correlation-Length-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Essential-Singularity-of-the-Correlation-Length-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Berezinskii--Kosterlitz--Thouless essential singularity for the correlation length of the nearest-neighbor cosine XY model on the square lattice. Let \\(m(b)\\) be the mass obtained by taking first the free-box thermodynamic limit and then the separation limit along a coordinate axis. As the inverse temperature \\(b\\) approaches \\(b_c\\) from the massive side, \\[ \\sqrt{b_c-b}\\log\\frac{1}{m(b)} \\longrightarrow A_{\\mathrm{XY}}, \\] where \\(A_{\\mathrm{XY}}\\) is a finite, strictly positive model-specific constant.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":569},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and preceding results","sourceOffset":2527},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and new estimates","sourceOffset":5487},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite height laws and their temperature response","sourceOffset":11997},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and finite comparisons","sourceOffset":12415},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Poisson insertions and a finite-volume continuity estimate","sourceOffset":19287},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A strict response bound","sourceOffset":22861},{"kind":"section","title":"Analytic entry and the activity coordinates","sourceOffset":25624},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The reference field and the initialization norm","sourceOffset":26244},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A parameter disk for initialization","sourceOffset":33359},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The map at fixed reference","sourceOffset":50021},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite torus observables and comparison of observation grids","sourceOffset":55479}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For the model \\eqref{eq:spin-law}, there is a constant $A_{\\mathrm{XY}}\\in(0,\\infty)$ such that \\begin{equation} \\lim_{b\\uparrow b_c}\\sqrt{b_c-b}\\,\\log\\xi(b)=A_{\\mathrm{XY}}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":2093,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Analytic initialization","label":"prop:entry","text":"For every sufficiently small $\\rho>0$, the fixed choices above can be made so that there exist $c_{R_0}>0$ and $s_0<\\infty$ with the following property. For every admissible $s\\ge s_0$, the ordinary plane and compatible-torus entry activities $K_s(b)$ are Banach analytic on \\begin{equation} |b-\\bc|<c_{R_0}s^{-2}, \\end{equation} and take values in $\\mathscr A^{\\mathrm{bulk}}_{L^{j_0},s}$ or its compatible-torus counterpart, with norm at most $\\rho$. They are real on real fields when $b$ is real. Their support, symmetry, and local copying identities agree with those at criticality throughout the complex parameter disk. For real parameters in this disk the cutoff partition representation is exact. On a smaller concentric disk, for each fixed $r\\ge1$, \\begin{equation} \\|\\partial_b^rK_s(b)\\|_{L^{j_0},s} \\le C_{R_0,r}s^{2r}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":34116,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":200522,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Essential-Singularity-of-the-Correlation-Length-in-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"216-05","slug":"The-critical-correlation-exponent-of-the-planar-XY-model-September-24-2026","familyId":"216","title":"The critical correlation exponent of the planar XY model","abstract":"For the ordinary nearest-neighbor XY model on the square lattice, we prove the Berezinskii–Kosterlitz–Thouless prediction that the critical spin-correlation exponent is 1/4. More precisely, at the mass-defined critical inverse temperature, the infinite-volume correlation is $n^{-1/4+o(1)}$. The infinite-volume limit through free square boxes is taken before the separation limit.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For the square-lattice nearest-neighbor cosine XY model, proves critical axis correlations $C_{\\beta_c}(r)\\sim Ar^{-1/4}(\\log r)^{1/8}$ and the Berezinskii–Kosterlitz–Thouless essential singularity $\\sqrt{\\beta_c-\\beta}\\log\\xi(\\beta)\\to B$, with $A,B\\gt 0$ after the free-box thermodynamic limit. For finite square-symmetric interactions containing nearest neighbors, discrete Gaussian heights converge to Gaussian fields throughout the rough phase, including its threshold, along geometric torus sizes. Critical center-magnetization and spin-field conclusions retain their stated height, renormalization, and field-input assumptions.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-critical-correlation-exponent-of-the-planar-XY-model-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-critical-correlation-exponent-of-the-planar-XY-model-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-critical-correlation-exponent-of-the-planar-XY-model-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-critical-correlation-exponent-of-the-planar-XY-model-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-critical-correlation-exponent-of-the-planar-XY-model-September-24-2026/build/source-refs.tex","preprints/The-critical-correlation-exponent-of-the-planar-XY-model-September-24-2026/build/sections/20-heights.tex","preprints/The-critical-correlation-exponent-of-the-planar-XY-model-September-24-2026/build/sections/30-pins.tex","preprints/The-critical-correlation-exponent-of-the-planar-XY-model-September-24-2026/build/figures/band.tex","preprints/The-critical-correlation-exponent-of-the-planar-XY-model-September-24-2026/build/sections/40-magnetic.tex","preprints/The-critical-correlation-exponent-of-the-planar-XY-model-September-24-2026/build/figures/annuli.tex","preprints/The-critical-correlation-exponent-of-the-planar-XY-model-September-24-2026/build/sections/50-openness.tex","preprints/The-critical-correlation-exponent-of-the-planar-XY-model-September-24-2026/build/sections/60-critical.tex","preprints/The-critical-correlation-exponent-of-the-planar-XY-model-September-24-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-critical-correlation-exponent-of-the-planar-XY-model-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For the ordinary nearest-neighbor XY model on the square lattice, we prove the Berezinskii--Kosterlitz--Thouless prediction that the critical spin-correlation exponent is \\(1/4\\). More precisely, at the mass-defined critical inverse temperature, the infinite-volume correlation is \\(n^{-1/4+o(1)}\\). The infinite-volume limit through free square boxes is taken before the separation limit.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":435},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model and statement","sourceOffset":3792},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Structure of the proof","sourceOffset":6546},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Companion inputs and Bessel transfers","sourceOffset":8930},{"kind":"section","title":"Dual heights and the structural coefficient","sourceOffset":11114},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Duality and finite comparisons","sourceOffset":12077},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Free Gaussian limits","sourceOffset":23112},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Soft joining and the coefficient gap","sourceOffset":31455},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An upper bound for spin correlations","sourceOffset":44507},{"kind":"section","title":"Bands, primary hard pins, and conditional data","sourceOffset":47802},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Geometry and the required limits","sourceOffset":48410},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A confining band and uniform primary estimates","sourceOffset":53450}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For the model \\eqref{eq:xy-law} and the threshold \\eqref{eq:critical-definition}, \\[ \\lim_{\\substack{n\\to\\infty\\\\n\\in\\N,\\ n\\ge2}} \\frac{\\log C_{b_c}(n)}{\\log n}=-\\frac14. \\] Equivalently, for every \\(\\eps>0\\) there is \\(n_0<\\infty\\) such that \\[ n^{-1/4-\\eps}\\le C_{b_c}(n)\\le n^{-1/4+\\eps} \\qquad(n\\ge n_0). \\]","sourceOffset":5247,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Critical polynomial lower bound","label":"prop:critical-lower","text":"For every \\(n\\ge1\\), \\[ C_{b_c}(n)\\ge\\frac1{8n}. \\]","sourceOffset":5675,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":186163,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-critical-correlation-exponent-of-the-planar-XY-model-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"216-06","slug":"BKT-universality-for-height-and-planar-spin-fields-September-24-2026","familyId":"216","title":"BKT universality for height and planar spin fields","abstract":"We prove Gaussian scaling limits for the two-dimensional discrete Gaussian height model throughout its rough phase, including the physical roughening threshold, for every finite square-symmetric interaction set containing the nearest neighbors. After the natural lattice normalization, the critical effective temperature has the universal value $8\\pi$. For the ordinary nearest-neighbor Villain and XY models at sufficiently low fixed temperatures, we also prove that their Green-function-normalized spin fields converge to the imaginary exponential of a Dirichlet Gaussian free field.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For the square-lattice nearest-neighbor cosine XY model, proves critical axis correlations $C_{\\beta_c}(r)\\sim Ar^{-1/4}(\\log r)^{1/8}$ and the Berezinskii–Kosterlitz–Thouless essential singularity $\\sqrt{\\beta_c-\\beta}\\log\\xi(\\beta)\\to B$, with $A,B\\gt 0$ after the free-box thermodynamic limit. For finite square-symmetric interactions containing nearest neighbors, discrete Gaussian heights converge to Gaussian fields throughout the rough phase, including its threshold, along geometric torus sizes. Critical center-magnetization and spin-field conclusions retain their stated height, renormalization, and field-input assumptions.","pdfPath":"preprints/BKT-universality-for-height-and-planar-spin-fields-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/BKT-universality-for-height-and-planar-spin-fields-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/BKT-universality-for-height-and-planar-spin-fields-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/BKT-universality-for-height-and-planar-spin-fields-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/BKT-universality-for-height-and-planar-spin-fields-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/BKT-universality-for-height-and-planar-spin-fields-September-24-2026/build/sections/structural.tex","preprints/BKT-universality-for-height-and-planar-spin-fields-September-24-2026/build/sections/annular.tex","preprints/BKT-universality-for-height-and-planar-spin-fields-September-24-2026/build/sections/toolkit.tex","preprints/BKT-universality-for-height-and-planar-spin-fields-September-24-2026/build/sections/villain.tex","preprints/BKT-universality-for-height-and-planar-spin-fields-September-24-2026/build/sections/xy-histories.tex","preprints/BKT-universality-for-height-and-planar-spin-fields-September-24-2026/build/sections/xy-flow.tex","preprints/BKT-universality-for-height-and-planar-spin-fields-September-24-2026/build/sections/weak-height.tex","preprints/BKT-universality-for-height-and-planar-spin-fields-September-24-2026/build/sections/entry.tex","preprints/BKT-universality-for-height-and-planar-spin-fields-September-24-2026/build/sections/endpoint.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/BKT-universality-for-height-and-planar-spin-fields-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Gaussian scaling limits for the two-dimensional discrete Gaussian height model throughout its rough phase, including the physical roughening threshold, for every finite square-symmetric interaction set containing the nearest neighbors. After the natural lattice normalization, the critical effective temperature has the universal value \\(8\\pi\\). For the ordinary nearest-neighbor Villain and XY models at sufficiently low fixed temperatures, we also prove that their Green-function-normalized spin fields converge to the imaginary exponential of a Dirichlet Gaussian free field.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction and statements","sourceOffset":634},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Height model and its physical threshold","sourceOffset":1567},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ordinary planar spin models and the Wick convention","sourceOffset":6348},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Normalizations and order of limits","sourceOffset":12322},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and earlier work","sourceOffset":15105},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof organization and the new estimates","sourceOffset":21852},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite comparisons and the structural height coefficient","sourceOffset":27518},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite lattice Gaussian inequalities","sourceOffset":29532},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Saturation on free rectangles","sourceOffset":37406},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Neutral charges and the fractional mean","sourceOffset":53420},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The charge gap and a uniform critical-scale ratio","sourceOffset":61094},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Localization when the structural coefficient vanishes","sourceOffset":69574}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Height universality through the roughening threshold","label":"main:heights","text":"For every admissible $J$, the threshold in Equation \\eqref{main:physical-threshold} satisfies $0<\\bc(J)<\\infty$. The supremum there is finite when $0<\\beta<\\bc(J)$ and infinite when $\\beta\\ge\\bc(J)$, including equality. There are an integer $L=L(J)\\ge2$ and a unique positive function $\\beff(J,\\beta)$, defined for $\\beta\\ge\\bc(J)$, such that along the full sequence $N\\to\\infty$ \\begin{equation} H_N\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{\\beff(J,\\beta)}{v_J^2}}\\,\\Phi_{\\T} \\quad\\hbox{in }H^{-3}(\\T^2). \\end{equation} For every smooth real mean-zero $f$, \\begin{equation} \\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\log\\E e^{H_N(f)} =\\frac{\\beff(J,\\beta)}{2v_J^2} (f,(-\\Delta_{\\T})^{-1}f). \\end{equation} Furthermore, \\begin{align} \\beff(J,\\bc(J))&=8\\pi v_J^2,\\\\ \\lim_{\\beta\\downarrow\\bc(J)} \\frac{\\beff(J,\\beta)-\\beff(J,\\bc(J))}{\\beta-\\bc(J)} &=+\\infty. \\end{align} The function $\\beff(J,\\cdot)$ is differentiable on $(\\bc(J),\\infty)$, and for some $c_J>0$, \\begin{equation} \\beff(J,\\beta)=\\beta+O_J(e^{-c_J\\beta})\\qquad(\\beta\\to\\infty). \\end{equation} For $J_\\rho=\\{x\\in\\Z^2\\setminus\\{0\\}:|x|_\\infty\\le\\rho\\}$, \\begin{equation} \\frac{\\bc(J_\\rho)}{8\\pi v_{J_\\rho}^2}\\longrightarrow1 \\qquad(\\rho\\to\\infty). \\end{equation} In particular $v_{J_{\\nn}}^2=1/4$ and the nearest-neighbor effective temperature at roughening is $2\\pi$.","sourceOffset":3363,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Full low-temperature spin-field limits","label":"main:spins","text":"For each $M\\in\\{\\mathrm{Villain},\\mathrm{XY}\\}$ there are a finite $b_0(M)>0$ and functions $K_M(b)>1/(4\\pi)$ and $A_M(b)>0$, defined for all $b\\ge b_0(M)$, with $K_M(b)/b\\to1$ as $b\\to\\infty$, such that \\begin{equation} S_{M,b,n}:=n^{-2}\\sum_{x\\in D_n^\\circ} A_M(b)e^{G_n(x,x)/(2K_M(b))}e^{i\\theta_x}\\delta_x \\ \\Longrightarrow\\ V_{K_M(b),D} \\end{equation} along the full sequence of integers $n\\to\\infty$, with tightness and convergence in $H^{-3}_{\\mathrm{loc}}(D)$. For every finite collection of complex smooth compactly supported test functions, their smeared fields converge jointly, including real and imaginary parts. The coefficients $K_M(b)$ and $A_M(b)$ are independent of the test functions, the insertion locations, and $n$.","sourceOffset":8318,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Permitted mixed geometry","label":"an:geometry","text":"Start with axis-parallel squares of a fixed dyadic side $R$. Remove from each corner the part of the square of side $R/2$ centered at that grid vertex, and declare the latter squares to be separate tiles. Thus no tile vertex involves more than three tiles, and every pair of tiles meeting there also shares a nondegenerate face. Take finite unions of these tiles, possibly clipped along the $R/4$ grid by boxes. Tiles may be isolated by removing every bond between them and other tiles. All exterior boundaries are free. Components are formed by face connections; boundary point contacts do not join ","sourceOffset":131307,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"A selected history","label":"xyh:history-definition","text":"First truncate the microscopic charge indices and use strict compensation as in Proposition \\ref{xyh:compensation}. Expand every finite sum, Taylor integral, Fourier direction choice, vertex/orientation average, and Cauchy contour, and fix one term. Cut its dependency graph at every scalar coefficient evaluation. The resulting record is a rooted forest of separately completed Gaussian roots and at most one live Gaussian root. The forest is understood by occurrences: if the same evaluated coefficient is used twice, its completed calculation has two separate copies when displayed. A live root is","sourceOffset":339595,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":622746,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":46,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/BKT-universality-for-height-and-planar-spin-fields-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"217-01","slug":"The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026","familyId":"217","title":"The low-temperature Sherrington–Kirkpatrick free-energy limiting law","abstract":"For every fixed inverse temperature β > 1, we prove that the zero-field Gaussian Ising Sherrington–Kirkpatrick free energy, centered by its expectation and divided by its standard deviation, converges in distribution to a nondegenerate law as the system size tends to infinity through all integers. We also prove that its variance divided by n1/3 converges to a finite positive constant.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every fixed inverse temperature β > 1, determines the fluctuation scale and limiting law of the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington–Kirkpatrick log partition function. Its variance is asymptotic to $c_\\beta n^{1/3}$, with $c_\\beta\\gt 0$, confirming the predicted n1/6 standard-deviation scale. Exact centering and standardization give full-sequence convergence to a uniquely characterized nondegenerate law.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026/build/figures/comparison-path.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-foundations.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-paths.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-reduction.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-inverse.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-global.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-budgets.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-statistics.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026/build/figures/fixed-marginal.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-expansion.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-parameters.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026/build/sections/11-companion-interface.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026/build/sections/12-notation.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed inverse temperature $\\beta>1$, we prove that the zero-field Gaussian Ising Sherrington--Kirkpatrick free energy, centered by its expectation and divided by its standard deviation, converges in distribution to a nondegenerate law as the system size tends to infinity through all integers. We also prove that its variance divided by $n^{1/3}$ converges to a finite positive constant.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":496},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The fluctuation problem and its history","sourceOffset":1867},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The limiting law","sourceOffset":5306},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite-system characterization of the law","sourceOffset":8108},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The size comparison and its main ingredients","sourceOffset":10147},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Consequences","sourceOffset":17089},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and order of limits","sourceOffset":21183},{"kind":"section","title":"Hierarchical Gaussian calculus","sourceOffset":22604},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Normalization and positive tree laws","sourceOffset":23020},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pinned moments and projection","sourceOffset":27940},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Signed allocation of replica labels","sourceOffset":34364},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Scalar references and Laplace tests","sourceOffset":37883}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Limiting law","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed real $\\beta>1$, there is a nondegenerate Borel probability measure $\\nu_\\beta$ such that \\[ X_n(\\beta)\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ \\nu_\\beta \\qquad\\text{as }n\\to\\infty\\text{ through all integers}. \\] The measure $\\nu_\\beta$ has mean zero, variance one, and a finite exponential moment in a neighborhood of the origin. It is the unique probability measure with the moments specified in Proposition \\ref{def:law} below.","sourceOffset":5337,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Moment convergence at the fluctuation scale","label":"thm:moments","text":"For each fixed $\\beta>1$, there are real numbers $a_k=a_k(\\beta)$, $k\\ge1$, with the following property. For every integer $k\\ge1$, there are constants $c_k>0$ and $C_k<\\infty$ such that \\begin{equation} \\big|\\mathbb E Y_n^k-a_k\\big|\\le C_k n^{-c_k},\\qquad n\\ge2. \\end{equation} There are also constants $c>0$ and $C<\\infty$, depending on $\\beta$, for which \\begin{equation} \\sup_{n\\ge2}\\mathbb E e^{c|Y_n|}\\le C. \\end{equation} The limits satisfy $a_1=0$ and $0<a_2<\\infty$.","sourceOffset":6775,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":461529,"sourceFileCount":16,"statementCount":22,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-free-energy-limiting-law-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"217-02","slug":"The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-fluctuation-scale-September-24-2026","familyId":"217","title":"The low-temperature Sherrington–Kirkpatrick fluctuation scale","abstract":"For the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model at every fixed inverse temperature β > 1, we prove that the standard deviation of the log partition function is $n^{1/6+o(1)}$. The same exponent describes its typical centered absolute fluctuations, establishing the predicted one-sixth exponent in this regime.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every fixed inverse temperature β > 1, determines the fluctuation scale and limiting law of the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington–Kirkpatrick log partition function. Its variance is asymptotic to $c_\\beta n^{1/3}$, with $c_\\beta\\gt 0$, confirming the predicted n1/6 standard-deviation scale. Exact centering and standardization give full-sequence convergence to a uniquely characterized nondegenerate law.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-fluctuation-scale-September-24-2026/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-fluctuation-scale-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-fluctuation-scale-September-24-2026/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-fluctuation-scale-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-fluctuation-scale-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-fluctuation-scale-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-fluctuation-scale-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-fluctuation-scale-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-order.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-fluctuation-scale-September-24-2026/build/sections/foundations.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-fluctuation-scale-September-24-2026/build/sections/comparisons.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-fluctuation-scale-September-24-2026/build/sections/cavity.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-fluctuation-scale-September-24-2026/build/sections/profile.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-fluctuation-scale-September-24-2026/build/sections/propagation.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-fluctuation-scale-September-24-2026/build/sections/fluctuations.tex","preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-fluctuation-scale-September-24-2026/build/sections/companion-interface.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-fluctuation-scale-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington--Kirkpatrick model at every fixed inverse temperature $\\beta>1$, we prove that the standard deviation of the log partition function is $n^{1/6+o(1)}$. The same exponent describes its typical centered absolute fluctuations, establishing the predicted one-sixth exponent in this regime.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":370},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":1741},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and method ancestry","sourceOffset":5424},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":12812},{"kind":"section","title":"Gaussian hierarchies and their calculus","sourceOffset":13412},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite hierarchies and conditional covariances","sourceOffset":14212},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pressure derivatives and the pinned moment estimate","sourceOffset":18927},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Signed replica sums at real masses","sourceOffset":27609},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cut truncations and conditional couplings","sourceOffset":40950},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Passage to bounded monotone paths","sourceOffset":49090},{"kind":"section","title":"Optimized short comparisons","sourceOffset":56161},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Parameters and the constrained comparison","sourceOffset":56820}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed $1<\\beta<\\infty$, \\[ \\sd(F_n)=n^{1/6+o(1)},\\qquad \\Var(F_n)=n^{1/3+o(1)}. \\] More explicitly, for every $\\eta>0$ there is $n_0(\\beta,\\eta)$ such that \\[ n^{1/6-\\eta}\\le\\sd(F_n)\\le n^{1/6+\\eta} \\qquad(n\\ge n_0(\\beta,\\eta)). \\] Moreover, for every $\\eta>0$, \\[ \\mathbb P\\!\\left(n^{1/6-\\eta}\\le|F_n-\\mathbb EF_n| \\le n^{1/6+\\eta}\\right)\\longrightarrow1. \\]","sourceOffset":847,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Covariance differentiation","label":"prop:found-replica","text":"Let $K_\\theta(u;\\sigma,\\tau)$ be an affine family of cumulative Gaussian covariance kernels of a finite hierarchy. Assume that the independent increment covariances are positive semidefinite throughout the parameter interval. Let $V=\\partial_\\theta K_\\theta$ and let $P$ be a fixed bounded spin test on a fixed observed split tree. Then \\begin{equation} \\frac{d}{d\\theta}\\nu_\\theta[P] =\\frac12\\sum_{c,d}\\nu_\\theta[P V_{cd}]. \\tag{2} \\end{equation} The signed sum includes coincidences with old labels and between $c,d$. For distinct labels, $V_{cd}$ is evaluated at their common revelation; on the diagonal it is evaluated at the terminal covariance. The exact weights are the real-power weights described in the proof. A constant diagonal contribution, independent of the summation label, sums to zero. Conditional means in $P$ can be represented by independent additional replicas at their specified prefixes before this formula is applied.","sourceOffset":28216,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Outer regularity","label":"comp:outer-regularity","text":"A minimizer has a regular outer region above $x$ if, for fixed positive constants $c,C,C_0$, one has $cu\\le r(u)\\le Cu$ whenever $u\\ge C_0x$. Enlarging the cutoff by a fixed factor gives the equivalent comparison $\\alpha(r)\\asymp r$ in radius coordinates. The constants are independent of $n$. No bound on a pointwise path derivative is part of this definition.","sourceOffset":64173,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Admissible tests","label":"def:cavity-tests","text":"A test of degree at most \\(N\\) is a finite linear combination of products of centered boundary overlaps and their conditional projections, with these specifications. Increase \\(N\\), if necessary, so that it also bounds the number of leaves after the fresh-replica representation of the projections. All low splits are \\(*\\), except for at most one initial edge at a cut \\(i\\). The distinguished target \\(a\\) is unique in its block among the observed test leaves. Its dependence is a power of \\[ Y_a=X_*\\cdot y_a-S_* \\] times at most one incoming boundary edge from an outside leaf. Other factors do n","sourceOffset":119325,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":272033,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-low-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-fluctuation-scale-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"218-01","slug":"Quenched-SLE3-limits-for-general-weak-random-bond-Ising-models-October-5-2026","familyId":"218","title":"Quenched SLE₃ limits for general weak random-bond Ising models","abstract":"For the planar Ising model with bonds $J_e=1+\\varepsilon\\xi_e$, where the ξe have any fixed bounded, nondegenerate, mean-zero iid law, we prove quenched chordal SLE3 convergence for sufficiently small ε > 0. The temperature is the spontaneous-magnetization threshold. Convergence holds in probability over environments for the full oriented curve law in deterministic Jordan-domain approximations.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Weak finite-range square-symmetric even multispin perturbations of the square-lattice Ising model preserve critical bulk spin and energy limits; weak square-symmetric contour interactions also yield chordal SLE3 interface limits. With sufficiently weak iid bond disorder of any fixed bounded nondegenerate mean-zero law, critical spin interfaces converge to the same law in probability over environments.","pdfPath":"preprints/Quenched-SLE3-limits-for-general-weak-random-bond-Ising-models-October-5-2026/general-weak-random-bond-ising.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quenched-SLE3-limits-for-general-weak-random-bond-Ising-models-October-5-2026/general-weak-random-bond-ising.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quenched-SLE3-limits-for-general-weak-random-bond-Ising-models-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Quenched-SLE3-limits-for-general-weak-random-bond-Ising-models-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-limits-for-general-weak-random-bond-Ising-models-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-limits-for-general-weak-random-bond-Ising-models-October-5-2026/build/sections/history.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-limits-for-general-weak-random-bond-Ising-models-October-5-2026/build/sections/scale.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-limits-for-general-weak-random-bond-Ising-models-October-5-2026/build/sections/tuning.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-limits-for-general-weak-random-bond-Ising-models-October-5-2026/build/sections/matching.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-limits-for-general-weak-random-bond-Ising-models-October-5-2026/build/sections/crossings.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-limits-for-general-weak-random-bond-Ising-models-October-5-2026/build/figures/pivotal-ranges.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-limits-for-general-weak-random-bond-Ising-models-October-5-2026/build/sections/criticality.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-limits-for-general-weak-random-bond-Ising-models-October-5-2026/build/sections/interface.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-limits-for-general-weak-random-bond-Ising-models-October-5-2026/build/figures/strip-squeeze.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-limits-for-general-weak-random-bond-Ising-models-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Quenched-SLE3-limits-for-general-weak-random-bond-Ising-models-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For the planar Ising model with bonds $J_e=1+\\varepsilon\\xi_e$, where the $\\xi_e$ have any fixed bounded, nondegenerate, mean-zero iid law, we prove quenched chordal $\\mathrm{SLE}_3$ convergence for sufficiently small $\\varepsilon>0$. The temperature is the spontaneous-magnetization threshold. Convergence holds in probability over environments for the full oriented curve law in deterministic Jordan-domain approximations.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":487},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model and statement","sourceOffset":1141},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and relation to earlier work","sourceOffset":5581},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The extension and the proof","sourceOffset":10785},{"kind":"section","title":"Scale estimates for general local arrays","sourceOffset":13051},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The reference field and its energy charge","sourceOffset":13682},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Arrays and the exact scale transformation","sourceOffset":15307},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Moment and variance coordinates","sourceOffset":19448},{"kind":"section","title":"Two temperature tunings","sourceOffset":24569},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Primal and dual initial arrays","sourceOffset":25030},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Keeping the expanding coordinate between two walls","sourceOffset":30363},{"kind":"section","title":"Matching the primal and dual temperatures","sourceOffset":39069}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every law $\\rho$ satisfying \\eqref{eq:law-assumptions}, there exists $\\varepsilon_0(\\rho)>0$ with the following property. Fix $0<\\varepsilon<\\min\\{1,\\varepsilon_0(\\rho)\\}$. For every bounded Jordan domain $D$, distinct $a,b\\in\\partial D$, and deterministic square-lattice approximations satisfying \\eqref{eq:boundary-approximation} with $\\delta_n\\downarrow0$, the interface law at $\\beta=\\beta_c^\\rho(\\varepsilon)$ satisfies \\begin{equation} d_{\\rm BL}(Q_{n,\\omega},\\mathsf S_{D;a,b}) \\xrightarrow[n\\to\\infty]{\\Prob_\\rho}0. \\end{equation} The disorder threshold depends on $\\rho$ and not on $D$, its marked points, or the approximation sequence. Moreover, $\\beta_c^\\rho(\\varepsilon)=\\tfrac12\\log(1+\\sqrt2)+O_\\rho(\\varepsilon^2)$.","sourceOffset":4767,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Imported scale estimates","label":"prop:scale-estimates","text":"For the local arrays just specified, the scale-step parameters can be chosen in the order \\(L\\) sufficiently large, then \\(b>0\\) sufficiently small, then \\(x_*>0\\). There are constants \\(0<\\lambda<1\\) and \\(C_1<\\infty\\), independent of the spacing, such that every input with \\(X\\le x_*\\) satisfies \\begin{equation} \\begin{split} |m'-Lm|&\\le C_1X^2,\\\\ Y'&\\le\\lambda Y+C_1(|m|+X^2),\\\\ X'&\\le\\lambda X+C_1(\\sqrt W+Y). \\end{split} \\end{equation} Primes denote the output coordinates, with charge normalization \\(q_{Ls}\\). For every fixed \\(H>R_0\\), \\begin{equation} |v_H-W|\\le C_HX^3\\qquad (X\\le1). \\end{equation} Furthermore, fix finite constants \\(A,B\\). For every sufficiently large fixed \\(H\\), whenever \\(0<z\\le1\\), \\(X\\le Az\\), and \\(Y\\le Bz^2\\), one has \\begin{equation} v'_H-v_H=-2\\kappa W^2 \\sum_{y\\in\\Z^2:\\,H<|y|\\le LH}|y|^{-2} +e_H z^4+O_H(z^5). \\end{equation} Here \\(|e_H|\\) is bounded by a deterministic quantity tending to zero as \\(H\\to\\infty\\). The constants may depend on \\(A,B\\) and the fixed step parameters, but are uniform in the lattice spacing.","sourceOffset":22465,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/218.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":148545,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Quenched-SLE3-limits-for-general-weak-random-bond-Ising-models-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"218-02","slug":"Quenched-SLE3-Universality-for-the-Weak-Random-Bond-Ising-Model-October-5-2026","familyId":"218","title":"Quenched SLE₃ Universality for the Weak Random-Bond Ising Model","abstract":"We prove quenched chordal SLE3 convergence for the planar Ising model with sufficiently weak independent symmetric two-valued ferromagnetic bonds. The temperature is the critical point defined by spontaneous magnetization. Convergence holds in probability over environments for the full oriented curve law in deterministic Jordan-domain approximations.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Weak finite-range square-symmetric even multispin perturbations of the square-lattice Ising model preserve critical bulk spin and energy limits; weak square-symmetric contour interactions also yield chordal SLE3 interface limits. With sufficiently weak iid bond disorder of any fixed bounded nondegenerate mean-zero law, critical spin interfaces converge to the same law in probability over environments.","pdfPath":"preprints/Quenched-SLE3-Universality-for-the-Weak-Random-Bond-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/quenched-sle3-weak-random-bond-ising.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quenched-SLE3-Universality-for-the-Weak-Random-Bond-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/quenched-sle3-weak-random-bond-ising.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quenched-SLE3-Universality-for-the-Weak-Random-Bond-Ising-Model-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Quenched-SLE3-Universality-for-the-Weak-Random-Bond-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-Universality-for-the-Weak-Random-Bond-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-Universality-for-the-Weak-Random-Bond-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/pure.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-Universality-for-the-Weak-Random-Bond-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/local-map.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-Universality-for-the-Weak-Random-Bond-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/moments.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-Universality-for-the-Weak-Random-Bond-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/variance.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-Universality-for-the-Weak-Random-Bond-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/trajectory.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-Universality-for-the-Weak-Random-Bond-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/crossings.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-Universality-for-the-Weak-Random-Bond-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/criticality.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-Universality-for-the-Weak-Random-Bond-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/curves.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE3-Universality-for-the-Weak-Random-Bond-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Quenched-SLE3-Universality-for-the-Weak-Random-Bond-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove quenched chordal $\\mathrm{SLE}_3$ convergence for the planar Ising model with sufficiently weak independent symmetric two-valued ferromagnetic bonds. The temperature is the critical point defined by spontaneous magnetization. Convergence holds in probability over environments for the full oriented curve law in deterministic Jordan-domain approximations.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":427},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The random ferromagnet and its critical point","sourceOffset":1156},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Domains and interfaces","sourceOffset":2677},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The limiting law","sourceOffset":6297},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and related work","sourceOffset":8825},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and the new estimates","sourceOffset":13334},{"kind":"section","title":"An exactly normalized pure energy coefficient","sourceOffset":17100},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The companion inputs and their nonsymmetric consequence","sourceOffset":19565},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact normalization by the energy covariance","sourceOffset":26166},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Separated products and positive local weights","sourceOffset":38527},{"kind":"section","title":"An exact local change of scale","sourceOffset":47011},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fields, environments, and reference variables","sourceOffset":47618}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Quenched interface universality","label":"thm:main","text":"There is $\\epsilon_0>0$ such that the following holds for every fixed $\\epsilon\\in(0,\\epsilon_0)$. Let $D$ be a bounded Jordan domain and $a,b\\in\\partial D$ distinct. Take any deterministic sequence of admissible lattice data with meshes $\\delta_n\\downarrow0$ and boundary homeomorphisms \\[ \\phi_n:\\partial D\\longrightarrow\\partial(\\delta_n D_{V_n}), \\qquad \\sup_{z\\in\\partial D}|\\phi_n(z)-z|\\longrightarrow0, \\] such that $\\phi_n(a)=\\delta_n f_{{\\rm in},n}$ and $\\phi_n(b)=\\delta_n f_{{\\rm out},n}$. Let $Q_{n,\\omega}$ be the law of $\\delta_n\\gamma(P)$ under $\\mu_{V_n,\\beta_c(\\epsilon),\\epsilon,\\omega}$. Then for every $\\eta>0$, \\begin{equation} \\Prob_{\\rm env}\\!\\left[ d_{\\rm BL}\\bigl(Q_{n,\\omega},\\SLE_3(D;a,b)\\bigr)>\\eta\\right] \\longrightarrow0. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":7385,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Exact pure energy charge","label":"pure:charge","text":"For every bounded even local observable $f$, the limit \\begin{equation} Q(f)=\\kappa^{-1}\\lim_{|x|\\to\\infty}|x|^2\\E_0[fB_x] \\end{equation} exists. It defines a linear functional that kills constants, is invariant under lattice translations and square symmetries, and satisfies $Q(b_e)=1$. Pure transfer preserves $Q$ exactly. If $f$ is supported in $B_s(x)$, then \\begin{equation} |Q(f)|\\leq Cs\\|f\\|_\\infty. \\end{equation} For centered $f$ and $R\\geq4s$, \\begin{equation} \\|P_{R,x}f\\|_\\infty\\leq C(s/R)\\|f\\|_\\infty. \\end{equation} If $Q(f)=0$, the factor $s/R$ improves to $(s/R)^u$; it improves to $(s/R)^w$ if, after auxiliary integration, $f$ is also invariant under quarter turns about $x$. More generally, with $v=u$ or $v=w$ according to these two symmetry classes, \\begin{equation} \\|P_{R,x}f-Q(f)P_{R,x}B_x\\|_\\infty \\leq C(s/R)^v\\|f\\|_\\infty. \\end{equation} All constants are independent of $s,R$, and $f$.","sourceOffset":34216,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Variance coordinate","label":"var:coordinate","text":"For $H>R_0$, define \\begin{equation} v_H=\\sum_{d=2}^4\\frac1{d!} \\sum_{i_2,\\ldots,i_d\\in\\Z^2} \\1_{\\{\\diam(0,i_2,\\ldots,i_d)\\le H\\}} C_d(0,i_2,\\ldots,i_d). \\end{equation} The diameter is Euclidean diameter in cell units. The coordinate is real and need not be nonnegative for an arbitrary field.","sourceOffset":87801,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/218.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":202018,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":18,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Quenched-SLE3-Universality-for-the-Weak-Random-Bond-Ising-Model-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"218-03","slug":"Logarithmic-Relative-Fluctuations-in-the-Weakly-Disordered-Planar-Ising-Model-October-5-2026","familyId":"218","title":"Logarithmic Relative Fluctuations in the Weakly Disordered Planar Ising Model","abstract":"Assuming the stated deterministic critical-reference estimates, we prove that the relative second moment of the quenched critical spin correlation in the square-lattice Ising model with independent fair bonds $1\\pm\\varepsilon$ grows as $(\\log r)^{1/4+o(1)}$ for each sufficiently small fixed ε > 0. The correlation is evaluated at the physical critical temperature, with the thermodynamic limit taken before the disorder moments and the large-distance limit.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Weak finite-range square-symmetric even multispin perturbations of the square-lattice Ising model preserve critical bulk spin and energy limits; weak square-symmetric contour interactions also yield chordal SLE3 interface limits. With sufficiently weak iid bond disorder of any fixed bounded nondegenerate mean-zero law, critical spin interfaces converge to the same law in probability over environments.","pdfPath":"preprints/Logarithmic-Relative-Fluctuations-in-the-Weakly-Disordered-Planar-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/critical-relative-second-moment-weak-random-bond-ising.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Logarithmic-Relative-Fluctuations-in-the-Weakly-Disordered-Planar-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/critical-relative-second-moment-weak-random-bond-ising.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Logarithmic-Relative-Fluctuations-in-the-Weakly-Disordered-Planar-Ising-Model-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Logarithmic-Relative-Fluctuations-in-the-Weakly-Disordered-Planar-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Relative-Fluctuations-in-the-Weakly-Disordered-Planar-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Relative-Fluctuations-in-the-Weakly-Disordered-Planar-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/reference.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Relative-Fluctuations-in-the-Weakly-Disordered-Planar-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/blocking.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Relative-Fluctuations-in-the-Weakly-Disordered-Planar-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/norms.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Relative-Fluctuations-in-the-Weakly-Disordered-Planar-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/figures/bad-island.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Relative-Fluctuations-in-the-Weakly-Disordered-Planar-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/linear.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Relative-Fluctuations-in-the-Weakly-Disordered-Planar-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/coefficients.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Relative-Fluctuations-in-the-Weakly-Disordered-Planar-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/flow.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Relative-Fluctuations-in-the-Weakly-Disordered-Planar-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/marked.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Relative-Fluctuations-in-the-Weakly-Disordered-Planar-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/readout.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Relative-Fluctuations-in-the-Weakly-Disordered-Planar-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/figures/terminal-readout.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Relative-Fluctuations-in-the-Weakly-Disordered-Planar-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/transition.tex","preprints/Logarithmic-Relative-Fluctuations-in-the-Weakly-Disordered-Planar-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Logarithmic-Relative-Fluctuations-in-the-Weakly-Disordered-Planar-Ising-Model-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Assuming the stated deterministic critical-reference estimates, we prove that the relative second moment of the quenched critical spin correlation in the square-lattice Ising model with independent fair bonds $1\\pm\\eps$ grows as $(\\log r)^{1/4+o(1)}$ for each sufficiently small fixed $\\eps>0$. The correlation is evaluated at the physical critical temperature, with the thermodynamic limit taken before the disorder moments and the large-distance limit.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":553},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model and result","sourceOffset":1241},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":4832},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and the role of locality","sourceOffset":7473},{"kind":"section","title":"Reference averaging, charges, and field products","sourceOffset":11588},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The reference measure and its accepted inputs","sourceOffset":12170},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Invariant energy and spin coefficients","sourceOffset":16260},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Extracting product coefficients from separated limits","sourceOffset":23588},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positive local tilts and finite boundary tests","sourceOffset":28546},{"kind":"section","title":"An exact local change of scale","sourceOffset":34844},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Lists and the two kinds of support","sourceOffset":35621},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Indexed norms and the order of the parameters","sourceOffset":40590}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Assume the deterministic reference-model estimates of \\cite{ReferenceIsing}, at the scopes stated in Section \\ref{sec:reference}. There is $\\eps_0>0$ such that for every fixed $\\eps\\in(0,\\eps_0)$, \\begin{equation} \\lim_{r\\to\\infty,\\,r\\in\\N} \\frac{\\log R_\\eps(r)}{\\log\\log r}=\\frac14. \\end{equation} The critical inverse temperature in \\eqref{eq:quenched-correlation} is the physical value \\eqref{eq:critical-definition}; it is the unique positive solution of \\begin{equation} \\sinh\\bigl(2\\beta(1-\\eps)\\bigr) \\sinh\\bigl(2\\beta(1+\\eps)\\bigr)=1. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":3381,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Averaging and charge cancellation","label":"ref:operator","text":"Fix $\\nu>0$ smaller than $p-b$ in the applicable row of \\eqref{ref:exponents}. For all sufficiently large $w$ and $R\\ge Cw$, a function $F$ of that parity, supported in $B_w(z)$, satisfies \\begin{align} \\norm{P_{R,z}F^\\circ}_\\infty &\\le C(w/R)^b\\norm{F}_\\infty,\\\\ \\norm{P_{R,z}F^\\circ-\\ell_b(F)P_{R,z}\\mathcal O_b(z)}_\\infty &\\le C(w/R)^{p-\\nu}\\norm{F}_\\infty. \\end{align} In particular, the second bound is the improved decay bound for a function of charge zero. We shall use $\\nu=1/4$. The exponent $3-\\nu$ requires the individual even function to be invariant under right-angle rotations; invariance of its probability law alone does not suffice. For a site at fixed positive relative distance from the cutting contour, \\begin{equation} \\norm{P_{R,z}e(y)}_\\infty\\le CR^{-1},\\qquad \\norm{P_{R,z}\\sigma_y}_\\infty\\le CR^{-d}. \\end{equation} The first bound also holds for a single plane-centered bond product.","sourceOffset":17413,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/218.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":230650,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":23,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Logarithmic-Relative-Fluctuations-in-the-Weakly-Disordered-Planar-Ising-Model-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"218-04","slug":"Conformal-universality-of-bulk-Ising-correlations-under-weak-interactions-September-23-2026","familyId":"218","title":"Conformal universality of bulk Ising correlations under weak interactions","abstract":"We prove conformal universality of mixed bulk spin and energy correlations for the square-lattice Ising model with sufficiently small, square-symmetric, finite-range even multispin perturbations of either sign. One critical-temperature branch and two field normalizations apply in every bounded simply connected C2 Jordan domain with free, plus, or minus boundary conditions, and in the periodic thermodynamic plane state. Energies are centered in their actual states.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Weak finite-range square-symmetric even multispin perturbations of the square-lattice Ising model preserve critical bulk spin and energy limits; weak square-symmetric contour interactions also yield chordal SLE3 interface limits. With sufficiently weak iid bond disorder of any fixed bounded nondegenerate mean-zero law, critical spin interfaces converge to the same law in probability over environments.","pdfPath":"preprints/Conformal-universality-of-bulk-Ising-correlations-under-weak-interactions-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Conformal-universality-of-bulk-Ising-correlations-under-weak-interactions-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Conformal-universality-of-bulk-Ising-correlations-under-weak-interactions-September-23-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Conformal-universality-of-bulk-Ising-correlations-under-weak-interactions-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Conformal-universality-of-bulk-Ising-correlations-under-weak-interactions-September-23-2026/build/math-glyph-unicode.tex","preprints/Conformal-universality-of-bulk-Ising-correlations-under-weak-interactions-September-23-2026/build/sections/model.tex","preprints/Conformal-universality-of-bulk-Ising-correlations-under-weak-interactions-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Conformal-universality-of-bulk-Ising-correlations-under-weak-interactions-September-23-2026/build/sections/inputs.tex","preprints/Conformal-universality-of-bulk-Ising-correlations-under-weak-interactions-September-23-2026/build/sections/buffers.tex","preprints/Conformal-universality-of-bulk-Ising-correlations-under-weak-interactions-September-23-2026/build/sections/radial.tex","preprints/Conformal-universality-of-bulk-Ising-correlations-under-weak-interactions-September-23-2026/build/sections/renormalization.tex","preprints/Conformal-universality-of-bulk-Ising-correlations-under-weak-interactions-September-23-2026/build/sections/critical.tex","preprints/Conformal-universality-of-bulk-Ising-correlations-under-weak-interactions-September-23-2026/build/sections/correlations.tex","preprints/Conformal-universality-of-bulk-Ising-correlations-under-weak-interactions-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Conformal-universality-of-bulk-Ising-correlations-under-weak-interactions-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove conformal universality of mixed bulk spin and energy correlations for the square-lattice Ising model with sufficiently small, square-symmetric, finite-range even multispin perturbations of either sign. One critical-temperature branch and two field normalizations apply in every bounded simply connected $C^2$ Jordan domain with free, plus, or minus boundary conditions, and in the periodic thermodynamic plane state. Energies are centered in their actual states.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction and main result","sourceOffset":543},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and the universality prediction","sourceOffset":5453},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Obstacles and main ideas","sourceOffset":9693},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof structure","sourceOffset":16542},{"kind":"section","title":"Reference model and equilibrium inputs","sourceOffset":17710},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reference conventions","sourceOffset":17778},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Equilibrium inputs","sourceOffset":20040},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite soft fields for arbitrary bounded tests","sourceOffset":36760},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reference charts and changes of continuation","sourceOffset":37601},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Deterministic barriers and soft fields","sourceOffset":44317},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform replacement of bounded observables","sourceOffset":52117},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Moments and the order of the two replacements","sourceOffset":61781}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Conformal universality","label":"model:main","text":"For each $J$ and potential $V$ as above, there are $\\lambda_0>0$ and real functions $\\beta_c,Z_\\sigma,Z_\\epsilon$ on $(-\\lambda_0,\\lambda_0)$, continuous at zero, such that \\[ \\beta_c(\\lambda)>0,\\quad \\beta_c(0)=\\beta_0,\\qquad Z_\\sigma(0)=Z_\\epsilon(0)=1,\\quad Z_\\sigma(\\lambda)Z_\\epsilon(\\lambda)\\ne0. \\] For every fixed $|\\lambda|<\\lambda_0$, every allowed domain $D$, every $b\\in\\{\\mathrm{free},+,-\\}$, all integers $n,m\\ge0$ with $n+m\\ge1$, all pairwise distinct interior points $x_1,\\ldots,x_n,y_1,\\ldots,y_m$, and all $j_1,\\ldots,j_m\\in\\{1,2\\}$, the full-sequence limit is \\begin{equation} \\lim_{a\\downarrow0} \\left\\langle\\prod_{i=1}^n S_a(x_i) \\prod_{k=1}^m E_{a,j_k}(y_k)\\right\\rangle_{a,D}^{\\lambda,b} =Z_\\sigma(\\lambda)^nZ_\\epsilon(\\lambda)^m \\mathcal C_D^b(\\boldsymbol x;\\boldsymbol y). \\end{equation} The same functions and coupling interval apply to every such choice. At each fixed mesh, the infinite-volume limit along periodic square boxes exists for all local observables. Using that state first, and then taking $a\\downarrow0$, gives \\eqref{model:limit} with $D$ replaced by $\\C$. In particular its two-spin limit is a nonzero constant times $|x-y|^{-1/4}$. If $\\phi:D\\to D'$ is a conformal bijection between allowed domains, the limiting correlation in $D$ is its counterpart at the image points in $D'$, multiplied by \\begin{equation} \\prod_{i=1}^n|\\phi'(x_i)|^{1/8} \\prod_{k=1}^m|\\phi'(y_k)|. \\end{equation} The boundary type is transported unchanged.","sourceOffset":3462,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Buffered and shrinking-patch comparison","label":"in:patch","text":"Fix $c>0$. If $I\\subset B_R(z)$ and $J\\subset B_{(1+c)R}(z)^c$, their conditional density ratios in Lemma \\ref{in:ratio} lie in $[C_c^{-1},C_c]$, uniformly in the mesh, common pins, and fields. There are $C<\\infty$ and $\\theta>0$ such that, if $I\\subset B_w(z)$, $J\\subset B_R(z)^c$, and $R\\geq8w\\geq8$ in lattice units, the absolute log density ratio is at most \\begin{equation} C(w/R)^\\theta. \\end{equation} No unpinned neighborhood of $I$ is required. The buffered bound holds for fixed polygonal separating collars with positive relative width, and for their intersections with a free or fixed physical boundary. It also compares different continuations or volumes when the reference interaction agrees in the separating chart. The same assertions hold for mixtures and after a common conditional randomization of the local spins. Finitely many shrinking patches can be changed successively.","sourceOffset":22097,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/218.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":197525,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Conformal-universality-of-bulk-Ising-correlations-under-weak-interactions-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"218-05","slug":"SLE3-universality-for-weak-finite-range-Ising-interactions-September-23-2026","familyId":"218","title":"SLE3 universality for weak finite-range Ising interactions","abstract":"We prove that every sufficiently small, square-symmetric finite-range perturbation of the planar Ising contour energy has a domain-independent inverse temperature at which its spin interface converges to chordal SLE3. The result allows interactions of either sign, arbitrary admissible exterior contours, and uniformly approximated Jordan domains. Convergence holds for the full oriented curve law in uniform distance modulo increasing reparametrization.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Weak finite-range square-symmetric even multispin perturbations of the square-lattice Ising model preserve critical bulk spin and energy limits; weak square-symmetric contour interactions also yield chordal SLE3 interface limits. With sufficiently weak iid bond disorder of any fixed bounded nondegenerate mean-zero law, critical spin interfaces converge to the same law in probability over environments.","pdfPath":"preprints/SLE3-universality-for-weak-finite-range-Ising-interactions-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/SLE3-universality-for-weak-finite-range-Ising-interactions-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/SLE3-universality-for-weak-finite-range-Ising-interactions-September-23-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/SLE3-universality-for-weak-finite-range-Ising-interactions-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/SLE3-universality-for-weak-finite-range-Ising-interactions-September-23-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","preprints/SLE3-universality-for-weak-finite-range-Ising-interactions-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/SLE3-universality-for-weak-finite-range-Ising-interactions-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-pure-inputs.tex","preprints/SLE3-universality-for-weak-finite-range-Ising-interactions-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-transfer.tex","preprints/SLE3-universality-for-weak-finite-range-Ising-interactions-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-scale-flow.tex","preprints/SLE3-universality-for-weak-finite-range-Ising-interactions-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-bulk-stability.tex","preprints/SLE3-universality-for-weak-finite-range-Ising-interactions-September-23-2026/build/figures/pivotal-walls.tex","preprints/SLE3-universality-for-weak-finite-range-Ising-interactions-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-incidence-comparison.tex","preprints/SLE3-universality-for-weak-finite-range-Ising-interactions-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-boundary-and-curves.tex","preprints/SLE3-universality-for-weak-finite-range-Ising-interactions-September-23-2026/build/figures/boundary-squeeze.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/SLE3-universality-for-weak-finite-range-Ising-interactions-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every sufficiently small, square-symmetric finite-range perturbation of the planar Ising contour energy has a domain-independent inverse temperature at which its spin interface converges to chordal \\(\\mathrm{SLE}_3\\). The result allows interactions of either sign, arbitrary admissible exterior contours, and uniformly approximated Jordan domains. Convergence holds for the full oriented curve law in uniform distance modulo increasing reparametrization.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":542},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and its boundary data","sourceOffset":1186},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The main theorem","sourceOffset":4483},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":6927},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":10767},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact spin formulation","sourceOffset":16757},{"kind":"section","title":"Pure critical Ising inputs and contour conventions","sourceOffset":21924},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pointwise comparisons through collars","sourceOffset":23591},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Signed sources and line magnetizations","sourceOffset":28077},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Spin correlations and the thermal direction","sourceOffset":35026},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pure interfaces, complete resolutions, and four passages","sourceOffset":44541},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Buffered crossings, circuits, and one passage","sourceOffset":53478}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(J>0\\), and let \\(U\\) satisfy the finite-range and symmetry assumptions above. There is \\(\\lambda_0=\\lambda_0(J,U)>0\\) such that for every \\(\\abs\\lambda<\\lambda_0\\) there is a positive inverse temperature \\(\\beta_c=\\beta_c(J,U,\\lambda)\\), independent of domains and boundary data, with \\[ \\beta_c(J,U,0)=\\beta_0:=\\frac{\\log(1+\\sqrt2)}{2J}, \\] for which the following holds. Let \\(D\\) be any bounded Jordan domain, let \\(a,b\\) be distinct boundary points, and let \\(\\delta_n\\downarrow0\\). For each \\(n\\), choose a domain \\(\\Omega_n\\), marks and stubs \\(\\xi_n\\), and an admissible exterior contour \\(P_{o,n}\\) as above. Suppose there are boundary homeomorphisms \\[ \\phi_n:\\partial D\\longrightarrow \\partial(\\delta_n D_{\\Omega_n}) \\] such that \\[ \\sup_{z\\in\\partial D}\\abs{\\phi_n(z)-z}\\longrightarrow0,\\qquad \\phi_n(a)=\\delta_n f_{{\\rm in},n},\\qquad \\phi_n(b)=\\delta_n f_{{\\rm out},n}. \\] If \\(P_n\\) has law \\(\\mu_{\\Omega_n,\\beta_c,\\lambda}^{\\xi_n,P_{o,n}}\\), then \\(\\delta_n\\gamma(P_n)\\) converges in distribution, in the metric \\eqref{eq:curve-metric}, to chordal \\(\\SLE_3\\) in \\(D\\) from \\(a\\) to \\(b\\).","sourceOffset":5451,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Domain-independent decaying trajectory","label":"thm:critical-trajectory","text":"For every \\(J>0\\) and finite-range real potential \\(U\\) satisfying the symmetries of Theorem \\ref{thm:main}, the preceding scale parameters can be fixed independently of all domains and boundary data. The initial scale \\(s_0\\) may be enlarged by any prescribed finite amount before the following construction. For each sufficiently small \\(\\epsilon>0\\) there is \\(\\lambda_*>0\\) and a real analytic function \\(\\beta_c:(-\\lambda_*,\\lambda_*)\\to(0,\\infty)\\), with \\(\\beta_c(0)=\\beta_0\\), for which the initial potential \\eqref{eq:flow-physical-input} has a trajectory \\(V_{k+1}=\\Phi_{s_k}(V_k)\\) satisfying \\begin{equation} \\|V_k\\|_{s_k,d}\\le\\epsilon2^{-k},\\qquad k\\ge0. \\end{equation} Its intermediate one-step trajectories obey \\begin{equation} \\sup_{0\\le t\\le1} \\sum_\\ell |\\ell|e^{(d/2)(|\\ell|-1)} \\|v_{k,\\ell}(t)\\|_\\infty\\le C\\epsilon2^{-k}. \\end{equation} The same construction applies to both signs of \\(\\lambda\\).","sourceOffset":136560,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Order-positive source","label":"def:pure-order-positive","text":"For a probability measure \\(\\mu_S\\) on spins in a finite set \\(S\\), a real function \\(q\\) is an order-positive centered density if \\[ \\E_{\\mu_S}q=0,\\qquad \\E_{\\mu_S}[qg]\\ge0 \\quad\\hbox{for every increasing }g. \\] Here density means the density of a signed measure.","sourceOffset":28341,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/218.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":288164,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":23,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/SLE3-universality-for-weak-finite-range-Ising-interactions-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/SLE3-universality-for-weak-finite-range-Ising-interactions-September-23-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"218-06","slug":"Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026","familyId":"218","title":"Buffered comparison and stopping-band resolution in critical Ising","abstract":"We prove pointwise comparison and finite stopping-band approximation theorems for the critical nearest-neighbor Ising model on the square lattice. Arbitrary common pinned spins are allowed in the comparison: the likelihood ratio is controlled by a zero-field FK connection probability on the graph with those vertices deleted. For an interface exploration across a band of fixed positive width, finitely many regular signed barriers approximate every good conditional target law in total variation, uniformly over bounded observables and sufficiently fine meshes.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Weak finite-range square-symmetric even multispin perturbations of the square-lattice Ising model preserve critical bulk spin and energy limits; weak square-symmetric contour interactions also yield chordal SLE3 interface limits. With sufficiently weak iid bond disorder of any fixed bounded nondegenerate mean-zero law, critical spin interfaces converge to the same law in probability over environments.","pdfPath":"preprints/Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026/build/sections/ratios.tex","preprints/Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026/build/sections/moments.tex","preprints/Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026/build/sections/transmission.tex","preprints/Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026/build/sections/lines.tex","preprints/Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026/build/sections/resolutions.tex","preprints/Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026/build/sections/stopping.tex","preprints/Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026/build/sections/regularization.tex","preprints/Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026/build/figures/ordered-slit.tex","preprints/Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026/build/sections/descriptors.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove pointwise comparison and finite stopping-band approximation theorems for the critical nearest-neighbor Ising model on the square lattice. Arbitrary common pinned spins are allowed in the comparison: the likelihood ratio is controlled by a zero-field FK connection probability on the graph with those vertices deleted. For an interface exploration across a band of fixed positive width, finitely many regular signed barriers approximate every good conditional target law in total variation, uniformly over bounded observables and sufficiently fine meshes.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":609},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite graphs and pointwise comparison","sourceOffset":1204},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exploration across a band","sourceOffset":3404},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Inputs and proof mechanism","sourceOffset":6824},{"kind":"section","title":"Pointwise comparison through buffers","sourceOffset":10995},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite-graph bound from extreme log odds","sourceOffset":11455},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Critical collars, small patches, and local charts","sourceOffset":17802},{"kind":"section","title":"Spin correlations and magnetic moments","sourceOffset":27134},{"kind":"section","title":"Ordered transmission across a cut","sourceOffset":37177},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The cut geometry and the transmission estimate","sourceOffset":37593},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An auxiliary FK model for ordered disagreements","sourceOffset":42535},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Mass carried by a disagreement connection","sourceOffset":56538}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"corollary","name":"Ordered changes of domains and total variation","label":"tr:ordered-tv","text":"Suppose two ordered Ising laws, possibly defined by different sets of pins or different volumes, have a common free source contour $S_0$ such that their conditional continuation from $S_0$ toward a target $T$ is the same. Let an admissible cut $J$ lie between $S_0$ and $T$. Then \\eqref{tr:transmission-eq} holds for their expectation differences. In particular, with total variation defined as the supremum of event probability differences, \\begin{equation} \\|\\mu_T^+-\\mu_T^-\\|_{\\rm TV} \\leq C(w/s)^{-7/8+\\delta} \\bigl(\\mathbb E_{\\mu^+}M_J-\\mathbb E_{\\mu^-}M_J\\bigr). \\end{equation} At fixed positive $w/s$, convergence of the magnetic difference to zero therefore implies convergence in total variation on the buffered target.","sourceOffset":69243,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Finite-graph comparison","label":"in:comparison","text":"For every two configurations $b,b'$ on $J$ and every configuration $a$ on $I$, \\[ e^{-4\\operatorname{arctanh}q_0} \\leq \\frac{\\mu(\\sigma_I=a\\mid\\sigma_J=b,\\sigma_F)} {\\mu(\\sigma_I=a\\mid\\sigma_J=b',\\sigma_F)} \\leq e^{4\\operatorname{arctanh}q_0}. \\] Either conditional law may be replaced by an arbitrary mixture of these laws. The bound is symmetric in the two terminal sets.","sourceOffset":2180,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"An admissible cut at scales $w,s$","label":"tr:admissible","text":"Fix constants $A,K\\geq1$ and $a\\in(0,1/(16A))$. For $0<w\\leq s$, a separating cut $J$ is admissible if it has a partition into patches $(b_i)$ and associated straight counting segments $L_i\\subseteq J$, with centers $z_i$ and scales $\\ell_i\\in[aw,Aw]$, satisfying: \\begin{enumerate} \\item $b_i\\cup L_i\\subseteq z_i+B_{\\ell_i}$ and $|L_i|\\in[a\\ell_i,2\\ell_i]$; \\item every $z_i+B_{16\\ell_i}$ is a pin-free planar lattice chart, including freedom from source pins; \\item each site of $J$ belongs to at most $K$ counting segments; \\item $T$ is at distance at least $s/A$ from every $z_i$, $16\\ell_i\\leq ","sourceOffset":38689,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Order-positive signed source","label":"tr:order-positive","text":"For a probability law $\\mu$ on the source spins, a real function $q$ is an order-positive centered density if $\\mathbb E_\\mu q=0$ and $\\mathbb E_\\mu[qf]\\geq0$ for every increasing source function $f$. The word density here refers to a signed measure; $q$ need not be nonnegative or itself increasing.","sourceOffset":66843,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/218.md","scopeText":"The formalized result compares conditional Ising probabilities on finite graphs when two boundary mixtures differ but a common set of spins is pinned. If $q_0$ is the associated zero-field Fortuin–Kasteleyn connection probability after deleting the pinned vertices, the likelihood ratio lies between $\\exp(-4\\mathrm{artanh}\\,q_0)$ and $\\exp(4\\mathrm{artanh}\\,q_0)$. The statement allows arbitrary external fields and arbitrary probability mixtures satisfying the stated disjointness conditions. The paper's finite stopping-band approximation theorem is outside this selected statement.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/218.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":168067,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Buffered-comparison-and-stopping-band-resolution-in-critical-Ising-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"219-01","slug":"Fixed-energy-universality-for-weak-Anderson-disorder-on-random-regular-graphs-October-5-2026","familyId":"219","title":"Fixed-energy universality for weak Anderson disorder on random regular graphs","abstract":"For every fixed degree d ≥ 3, we prove fixed-energy GOE universality for the adjacency matrix of a uniformly random simple labelled d-regular graph with independent uniform diagonal disorder. The disorder strength is positive, sufficiently small, and fixed as the graph grows. At each fixed energy in a compact subinterval of the clean spectral band, the full microscopic eigenvalue point process converges to the GOE bulk process after rescaling by the positive density of states of the corresponding infinite-tree operator. The permitted disorder strength depends on the degree and the distance from the clean spectral edges.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every fixed degree d ≥ 3, the bulk adjacency-eigenvalue point process of a uniform simple random d-regular graph converges to the Gaussian orthogonal ensemble law, including for cubic graphs. The same fixed-energy universality persists under sufficiently weak fixed iid uniform diagonal disorder, throughout compact bands strictly inside the clean spectral edges.","pdfPath":"preprints/Fixed-energy-universality-for-weak-Anderson-disorder-on-random-regular-graphs-October-5-2026/fixed-energy-universality-weak-anderson-disorder-random-regular-graphs.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Fixed-energy-universality-for-weak-Anderson-disorder-on-random-regular-graphs-October-5-2026/fixed-energy-universality-weak-anderson-disorder-random-regular-graphs.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Fixed-energy-universality-for-weak-Anderson-disorder-on-random-regular-graphs-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Fixed-energy-universality-for-weak-Anderson-disorder-on-random-regular-graphs-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Fixed-energy-universality-for-weak-Anderson-disorder-on-random-regular-graphs-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Fixed-energy-universality-for-weak-Anderson-disorder-on-random-regular-graphs-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-tree.tex","preprints/Fixed-energy-universality-for-weak-Anderson-disorder-on-random-regular-graphs-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-local-law.tex","preprints/Fixed-energy-universality-for-weak-Anderson-disorder-on-random-regular-graphs-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-marked-entropy.tex","preprints/Fixed-energy-universality-for-weak-Anderson-disorder-on-random-regular-graphs-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-rigidity.tex","preprints/Fixed-energy-universality-for-weak-Anderson-disorder-on-random-regular-graphs-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-resolvents.tex","preprints/Fixed-energy-universality-for-weak-Anderson-disorder-on-random-regular-graphs-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-insertion.tex","preprints/Fixed-energy-universality-for-weak-Anderson-disorder-on-random-regular-graphs-October-5-2026/build/sections/08-dbm.tex","preprints/Fixed-energy-universality-for-weak-Anderson-disorder-on-random-regular-graphs-October-5-2026/build/sections/09-goe-counts.tex","preprints/Fixed-energy-universality-for-weak-Anderson-disorder-on-random-regular-graphs-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Fixed-energy-universality-for-weak-Anderson-disorder-on-random-regular-graphs-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed degree $d\\ge3$, we prove fixed-energy GOE universality for the adjacency matrix of a uniformly random simple labelled $d$-regular graph with independent uniform diagonal disorder. The disorder strength is positive, sufficiently small, and fixed as the graph grows. At each fixed energy in a compact subinterval of the clean spectral band, the full microscopic eigenvalue point process converges to the GOE bulk process after rescaling by the positive density of states of the corresponding infinite-tree operator. The permitted disorder strength depends on the degree and the distance from the clean spectral edges.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":677},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model and main result","sourceOffset":5620},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and the role of the environment","sourceOffset":8141},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":12361},{"kind":"section","title":"Boundary Green functions and stability on the tree","sourceOffset":13413},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Deterministic estimates for the recursion","sourceOffset":17718},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Mean and fluctuation contraction","sourceOffset":22914},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Smallness, inverse margins, and boundary continuity","sourceOffset":26869},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Spatial covariances and the density of states","sourceOffset":32478},{"kind":"section","title":"A distributional local law on the finite graph","sourceOffset":37556},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Averaging potentials and sampling resolvent entries","sourceOffset":39788},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Switching the boundary of a finite tree","sourceOffset":44779}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Fixed-energy universality","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $d\\ge3$ and every $0<\\kappa<2\\sqrt{d-1}$, there is $w_0=w_0(d,\\kappa)>0$ with the following property. For each fixed $0<w<w_0$, the restriction of $\\nu_{d,w}$ to an open neighborhood of \\[ I_{d,\\kappa}=[-2\\sqrt{d-1}+\\kappa,\\,2\\sqrt{d-1}-\\kappa] \\] has a continuous density $\\rho_{d,w}$, strictly positive on $I_{d,\\kappa}$. For every fixed $E\\in I_{d,\\kappa}$, set \\[ \\Xi_{n,E}=\\sum_{i=1}^n \\delta_{n\\rho_{d,w}(E)(\\lambda_i-E)}. \\] Then, for every nonnegative $f\\in C_c(\\RR)$, \\begin{equation} \\left| \\EE\\exp\\left(-\\int f\\,d\\Xi_{n,E}\\right) -\\EE\\exp\\left(-\\int f\\,d\\Xi_n^{\\GOE}\\right) \\right|\\longrightarrow0 \\end{equation} as $n\\to\\infty$ through all sizes $n>d$ with $nd$ even.","sourceOffset":7030,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Tree stability","label":"prop:tree-stability","text":"There is an integer $\\ell\\ge1$, depending only on $b,J$, with the following properties. Given any finite order $p_*>0$ and a sufficiently small prescribed number $\\delta_{\\rm tree}>0$, one can choose $h,w_0>0$ so that, uniformly for $e\\in J$, $0<w\\le w_0$, and $0<\\eta\\le h$, \\begin{equation} \\|Y(e,\\eta)\\|_2\\le\\delta_{\\rm tree},\\qquad \\EE D(Y(e,\\eta))^p\\le K_p\\quad(0<p\\le p_*). \\end{equation} The choices also include the additional fixed finite moment orders required by the stability assertion that follows. There are constants $\\delta_*>0$ and $C<\\infty$ such that, if a probability law $P$ on $\\DD$ satisfies $\\int|x|^2\\,dP(x)\\le\\delta_*^2$ and \\[ W_2\\bigl(P,\\Law(F_j(X_1,\\ldots,X_{b^j}))\\bigr)\\le u, \\qquad j=1,\\ell, \\] where the $X_i$ are independent with law $P$, then \\begin{equation} W_2(P,\\Law(Y(e,\\eta)))\\le Cu. \\end{equation} The constants $K_p,\\delta_*,C$ can be fixed before decreasing the parameters further to make $\\delta_{\\rm tree}$ much smaller than $\\delta_*$. On a fixed tree with its fixed potential variables, $(e,\\eta)\\mapsto Y(e,\\eta)$ extends continuously in $L^2$ to $J\\times[0,h]$. The boundary values belong to $\\DD$ almost surely, obey \\eqref{eq:tree-small-moments}, and are measurable functions of the potentials.","sourceOffset":15920,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":207153,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Fixed-energy-universality-for-weak-Anderson-disorder-on-random-regular-graphs-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"219-02","slug":"GOE-bulk-universality-for-fixed-degree-random-regular-graphs-September-23-2026","familyId":"219","title":"GOE bulk universality for fixed-degree random regular graphs","abstract":"We prove the fixed-degree bulk-universality conjecture for random regular graphs. For every fixed integer d ≥ 3 and every fixed energy in the open Kesten–McKay bulk, the unfolded eigenvalue point process of the adjacency matrix of a uniform simple labelled d-regular graph converges to the GOE bulk process. The convergence holds along all admissible graph sizes, without additional conditioning.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every fixed degree d ≥ 3, the bulk adjacency-eigenvalue point process of a uniform simple random d-regular graph converges to the Gaussian orthogonal ensemble law, including for cubic graphs. The same fixed-energy universality persists under sufficiently weak fixed iid uniform diagonal disorder, throughout compact bands strictly inside the clean spectral edges.","pdfPath":"preprints/GOE-bulk-universality-for-fixed-degree-random-regular-graphs-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/GOE-bulk-universality-for-fixed-degree-random-regular-graphs-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/GOE-bulk-universality-for-fixed-degree-random-regular-graphs-September-23-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/GOE-bulk-universality-for-fixed-degree-random-regular-graphs-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/GOE-bulk-universality-for-fixed-degree-random-regular-graphs-September-23-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","preprints/GOE-bulk-universality-for-fixed-degree-random-regular-graphs-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/GOE-bulk-universality-for-fixed-degree-random-regular-graphs-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-preliminaries.tex","preprints/GOE-bulk-universality-for-fixed-degree-random-regular-graphs-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-entropy.tex","preprints/GOE-bulk-universality-for-fixed-degree-random-regular-graphs-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-limits.tex","preprints/GOE-bulk-universality-for-fixed-degree-random-regular-graphs-September-23-2026/build/figures/two-edge-switch.tex","preprints/GOE-bulk-universality-for-fixed-degree-random-regular-graphs-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-switching.tex","preprints/GOE-bulk-universality-for-fixed-degree-random-regular-graphs-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-dbm.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/GOE-bulk-universality-for-fixed-degree-random-regular-graphs-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the fixed-degree bulk-universality conjecture for random regular graphs. For every fixed integer \\(d\\ge3\\) and every fixed energy in the open Kesten--McKay bulk, the unfolded eigenvalue point process of the adjacency matrix of a uniform simple labelled \\(d\\)-regular graph converges to the GOE bulk process. The convergence holds along all admissible graph sizes, without additional conditioning.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":451},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Main result","sourceOffset":879},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and significance","sourceOffset":2700},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":7187},{"kind":"section","title":"Spectral input and tree identities","sourceOffset":11107},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and microscopic coordinates","sourceOffset":11556},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The simple regular-graph model","sourceOffset":13627},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The imported spectral estimates","sourceOffset":16446},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Covariance and logarithmic potential on the tree","sourceOffset":20025},{"kind":"section","title":"Gaussian moments at sampled edges","sourceOffset":23002},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Counting colored stars","sourceOffset":25381},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A quantitative differential-entropy inequality","sourceOffset":28036}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed integer \\(d\\ge3\\), every fixed \\(E\\in(-2\\sqrt{d-1},2\\sqrt{d-1})\\), and every nonnegative continuous compactly supported \\(h:\\R\\to\\R\\), \\[ \\left| \\EE\\exp\\!\\left(-\\int h\\dd\\Xi_{n,d,E}\\right) - \\EE\\exp\\!\\left(-\\int h\\dd\\Xi_n^{\\mathrm{GOE}}\\right) \\right|\\longrightarrow0 \\] as \\(n\\to\\infty\\) through all admissible sizes.","sourceOffset":1927,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Spectral input","label":"prop:spectral-input","text":"There are constants \\(c_0,c_1>0\\), \\(B<\\infty\\), and a fixed open interval \\(I_E\\) containing \\(E\\), with closure contained in \\((-2\\sqrt{d-1},2\\sqrt{d-1})\\), such that the following hold with probability tending to one. \\begin{enumerate} \\item After omitting the eigenvalue associated with the constant vector, the ordered eigenvalues are within \\(n^{-c_0}\\) of their Kesten--McKay classical locations. In the normalization above, every eigenvector entry is bounded by a deterministic \\(n^{o(1)}\\). \\item Uniformly for \\(\\Re\\zeta\\in I_E\\) and \\(S/n\\le\\Im\\zeta\\le1\\), \\[ \\left|\\frac1n\\Tr(A-\\zeta)^{-1}-m_d(\\zeta)\\right| \\le(\\log n)^B\\bigl(n^{-c_1}+(n\\Im\\zeta)^{-c_1}\\bigr). \\] Except at at most \\(n^{1-c_1}\\) centers, the resolvent entries on a center and its \\(d\\) neighbors satisfy the same error bound relative to the corresponding infinite \\(d\\)-regular tree entries. \\end{enumerate} These conclusions hold along all admissible \\(n\\). In particular, \\[ \\#\\{i:|x_i|\\le S^2\\}=O(S^2) \\] on an event of probability tending to one.","sourceOffset":16603,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":133535,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/GOE-bulk-universality-for-fixed-degree-random-regular-graphs-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/GOE-bulk-universality-for-fixed-degree-random-regular-graphs-September-23-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"220-01","slug":"A-directional-zero-one-law-for-finite-range-dependent-random-environments-October-5-2026","familyId":"220","title":"A directional zero–one law for finite-range-dependent random environments","abstract":"We prove a directional zero–one law for uniformly elliptic nearest-neighbor random walks in stationary, ergodic, finite-range-dependent environments on ℤd, d ≥ 3. For every fixed nonzero real direction, the probability of escape in that direction, averaged over the environment, is either zero or one. Finite-range dependence is imposed on the full transition rows: collections of rows at distance greater than a fixed range are independent, including collections indexed by infinite deterministic sets.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"On ℤd, d ≥ 3, directional escape has probability zero or one for iid strictly elliptic nearest-neighbor environments, and for stationary ergodic finite-range-dependent environments under uniform ellipticity. In iid uniformly elliptic environments with d ≥ 2, almost-sure directional transience implies a deterministic limiting velocity with positive projection in that direction, resolving the ballisticity conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-for-finite-range-dependent-random-environments-October-5-2026/directional-zero-one-finite-range.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-for-finite-range-dependent-random-environments-October-5-2026/directional-zero-one-finite-range.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-for-finite-range-dependent-random-environments-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-for-finite-range-dependent-random-environments-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/main.tex","preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-for-finite-range-dependent-random-environments-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/history.tex","preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-for-finite-range-dependent-random-environments-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/cuts.tex","preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-for-finite-range-dependent-random-environments-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/radius.tex","preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-for-finite-range-dependent-random-environments-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/bridges.tex","preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-for-finite-range-dependent-random-environments-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/entropy.tex","preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-for-finite-range-dependent-random-environments-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/opposed-chunks-figure.tex","preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-for-finite-range-dependent-random-environments-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/posteriors.tex","preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-for-finite-range-dependent-random-environments-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/conclusion.tex","preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-for-finite-range-dependent-random-environments-October-5-2026/build/references/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-for-finite-range-dependent-random-environments-October-5-2026/build/manuscript/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a directional zero--one law for uniformly elliptic nearest-neighbor random walks in stationary, ergodic, finite-range-dependent environments on $\\ZZ^d$, $d\\ge3$. For every fixed nonzero real direction, the probability of escape in that direction, averaged over the environment, is either zero or one. Finite-range dependence is imposed on the full transition rows: collections of rows at distance greater than a fixed range are independent, including collections indexed by infinite deterministic sets.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":557},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model and main theorem","sourceOffset":1107},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and relation to earlier work","sourceOffset":3400},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and additional ingredients","sourceOffset":6928},{"kind":"section","title":"Separated rows and regeneration words","sourceOffset":10833},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Marks and the rows a path uses","sourceOffset":11628},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cuts with independent continuations","sourceOffset":16437},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Escape signs and finite mean width","sourceOffset":26984},{"kind":"section","title":"Spatial moments and reduction to a coordinate direction","sourceOffset":32211},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An integrable spatial radius","sourceOffset":33117},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Opposite limiting rays","sourceOffset":46676},{"kind":"section","title":"Coordinate bridges and opposed paths","sourceOffset":50570}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Directional zero--one law","label":"thm:main","text":"Under assumptions \\textup{(i)--(iii)}, for every fixed $\\ell\\in\\RR^d\\setminus\\{0\\}$, \\[ P_0(A_\\ell)\\in\\{0,1\\}. \\]","sourceOffset":2803,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Regeneration law","label":"prop:cut-law","text":"Fix a normalized real direction \\(v\\) and suppose \\(p_v>0\\). \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\roman*),leftmargin=*] \\item Under the raw law \\(P_0\\), on \\(\\{\\sup_n h(X_n)=\\infty\\}\\) there is a cut almost surely. \\item Under \\(\\widehat P_v\\), there are infinitely many cuts. With time zero counted as an initial boundary, the relative marked words between successive boundaries are independent and identically distributed. Their common law \\(\\nu_v\\) assigns mass \\begin{equation} \\nu_v(\\{\\gamma\\})=W(\\gamma) \\end{equation} to each regeneration word \\(\\gamma\\). These finite words concatenate to the entire conditioned walk. \\item Under the raw law, conditional on any realized prefix through the first cut, the translated suffix has law \\(\\widehat P_v\\). This assertion is restricted to the event that a cut exists. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":22599,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/220.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":122679,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-directional-zero-one-law-for-finite-range-dependent-random-environments-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"220-02","slug":"A-directional-zero-one-law-under-strict-ellipticity-September-23-2026","familyId":"220","title":"A directional zero–one law under strict ellipticity","abstract":"We prove the directional zero–one conjecture for nearest-neighbor random walks in independent and identically distributed strictly elliptic environments on ℤd, d ≥ 3: the probability of escape in each fixed nonzero real direction is zero or one. Only strict positivity of the transition probabilities is required; no uniform lower bound or moment assumption is imposed.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"On ℤd, d ≥ 3, directional escape has probability zero or one for iid strictly elliptic nearest-neighbor environments, and for stationary ergodic finite-range-dependent environments under uniform ellipticity. In iid uniformly elliptic environments with d ≥ 2, almost-sure directional transience implies a deterministic limiting velocity with positive projection in that direction, resolving the ballisticity conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-under-strict-ellipticity-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-under-strict-ellipticity-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-under-strict-ellipticity-September-23-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-under-strict-ellipticity-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-under-strict-ellipticity-September-23-2026/build/math-glyph-unicode.tex","preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-under-strict-ellipticity-September-23-2026/build/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-under-strict-ellipticity-September-23-2026/build/02-regeneration.tex","preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-under-strict-ellipticity-September-23-2026/build/03-radius.tex","preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-under-strict-ellipticity-September-23-2026/build/04-arrangement.tex","preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-under-strict-ellipticity-September-23-2026/build/figures/opposed-arrangement.tex","preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-under-strict-ellipticity-September-23-2026/build/05-entropy.tex","preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-under-strict-ellipticity-September-23-2026/build/06-posterior-contact.tex","preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-under-strict-ellipticity-September-23-2026/build/figures/posterior-fresh-strip.tex","preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-under-strict-ellipticity-September-23-2026/build/07-contradiction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-directional-zero-one-law-under-strict-ellipticity-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the directional zero--one conjecture for nearest-neighbor random walks in independent and identically distributed strictly elliptic environments on $\\ZZ^d$, $d\\ge3$: the probability of escape in each fixed nonzero real direction is zero or one. Only strict positivity of the transition probabilities is required; no uniform lower bound or moment assumption is imposed.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":454},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and the theorem","sourceOffset":1089},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work","sourceOffset":4021},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":7595},{"kind":"section","title":"Regeneration in a real direction","sourceOffset":12363},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Departure rows and path weights","sourceOffset":12940},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fresh records and true cuts","sourceOffset":15550},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Mean width and the two signs","sourceOffset":25898},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite spatial radii under coexistence","sourceOffset":29315},{"kind":"section","title":"Rays and a common renewal arrangement","sourceOffset":45094},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reduction to a coordinate direction","sourceOffset":45419},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact-cut bridges and renewal tails","sourceOffset":49656}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $d\\ge3$, let $\\PP$ be an independent and identically distributed law of nearest-neighbor transition rows on $\\ZZ^d$ satisfying \\eqref{eq:ellipticity}, and let $\\ell\\in\\RR^d\\setminus\\{0\\}$. Then \\[ P_0(A_\\ell)\\in\\{0,1\\}. \\]","sourceOffset":3190,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Spatial radius under coexistence","label":"prop:radius","text":"Let the environment consist of iid strictly positive transition rows, and let \\(v\\in\\RR^d\\setminus\\{0\\}\\). Suppose \\(P_0(A_v)>0\\) and \\(P_0(A_{-v})>0\\). For each sign \\(\\sigma\\in\\{+,-\\}\\), let \\(\\nu_{\\sigma v}\\) be the slab law from Lemma \\ref{lem:slabs}. If \\(R\\) is the maximum Euclidean distance from a slab's initial site, including its terminal site, then \\[ \\int R\\,d\\nu_v+\\int R\\,d\\nu_{-v}<\\infty. \\]","sourceOffset":29950,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/220.md","scopeText":"The directional zero–one conjecture asks whether a random walk's probability of escape in a fixed direction must be zero or one. The formalization proves this for nearest-neighbor walks in independent identically distributed strictly elliptic environments on $\\mathbb Z^d$, for every $d\\ge3$ and every nonzero real direction. Strict ellipticity means that every allowed transition probability is positive almost surely; no uniform positive lower bound or moment condition is assumed. The probability is the annealed law from the origin.\n\nFor an i.i.d. uniformly elliptic nearest-neighbor random environment on $\\mathbb Z^d$, the formalization proves that almost-sure directional transience implies convergence of $X_n/n$ to a deterministic velocity with positive projection in that direction for ever","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/220.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":110783,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-directional-zero-one-law-under-strict-ellipticity-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"220-03","slug":"Directional-transience-implies-ballisticity-September-23-2026","familyId":"220","title":"Directional transience implies ballisticity","abstract":"We prove that almost-sure transience in a fixed direction implies a deterministic limiting velocity with positive projection in that direction for nearest-neighbor random walks in independent and identically distributed uniformly elliptic environments on ℤd, d ≥ 2. This resolves the ballisticity conjecture positively.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"On ℤd, d ≥ 3, directional escape has probability zero or one for iid strictly elliptic nearest-neighbor environments, and for stationary ergodic finite-range-dependent environments under uniform ellipticity. In iid uniformly elliptic environments with d ≥ 2, almost-sure directional transience implies a deterministic limiting velocity with positive projection in that direction, resolving the ballisticity conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/Directional-transience-implies-ballisticity-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Directional-transience-implies-ballisticity-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Directional-transience-implies-ballisticity-September-23-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Directional-transience-implies-ballisticity-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Directional-transience-implies-ballisticity-September-23-2026/build/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Directional-transience-implies-ballisticity-September-23-2026/build/02-geometry.tex","preprints/Directional-transience-implies-ballisticity-September-23-2026/build/figures/forward-template.tex","preprints/Directional-transience-implies-ballisticity-September-23-2026/build/03-fluctuations.tex","preprints/Directional-transience-implies-ballisticity-September-23-2026/build/04-gaussian.tex","preprints/Directional-transience-implies-ballisticity-September-23-2026/build/05-clipping.tex","preprints/Directional-transience-implies-ballisticity-September-23-2026/build/06-kernels.tex","preprints/Directional-transience-implies-ballisticity-September-23-2026/build/07-stages.tex","preprints/Directional-transience-implies-ballisticity-September-23-2026/build/figures/nested-opportunities.tex","preprints/Directional-transience-implies-ballisticity-September-23-2026/build/08-stationary.tex","preprints/Directional-transience-implies-ballisticity-September-23-2026/build/figures/tail-decorrelation.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Directional-transience-implies-ballisticity-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that almost-sure transience in a fixed direction implies a deterministic limiting velocity with positive projection in that direction for nearest-neighbor random walks in independent and identically distributed uniformly elliptic environments on $\\mathbb Z^d$, $d\\ge2$. This resolves the ballisticity conjecture positively.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":395},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model and main result","sourceOffset":855},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and significance","sourceOffset":3135},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":7620},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positive-probability transience","sourceOffset":10506},{"kind":"section","title":"Geometric and speed reductions","sourceOffset":13194},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Strict records and regeneration words","sourceOffset":13256},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite spatial radius and the limiting ray","sourceOffset":18642},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Layer notation; a small inverse moment","sourceOffset":30334},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Probability estimate sufficient for speed","sourceOffset":34328},{"kind":"section","title":"Two-walk scales and preliminary comparisons","sourceOffset":40921},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Common true cuts","sourceOffset":41812}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Directional transience implies ballisticity","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $d\\ge2$ and let $P=\\nu^{\\otimes\\mathbb Z^d}$ be an iid law of nearest-neighbor transition rows satisfying uniform ellipticity. If $\\ell\\in S^{d-1}$ and $P_0(A_\\ell)=1$, then there exists a deterministic vector $v=v(\\nu)\\in\\mathbb R^d$ such that \\[ v\\cdot\\ell>0, \\qquad P_0\\!\\left(\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac{X_n}{n}=v\\right)=1. \\] In particular, the walk is ballistic in the same direction $\\ell$.","sourceOffset":2267,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Positive-probability transience and the velocity hemisphere","label":"cor:positive-transience","text":"Let $d\\ge3$ and let $P=\\nu^{\\otimes\\mathbb Z^d}$ be an iid law of nearest-neighbor transition rows satisfying uniform ellipticity. For a fixed $\\ell\\in\\mathbb R^d\\setminus\\{0\\}$, set $A_\\ell=\\{X_n\\cdot\\ell\\longrightarrow+\\infty\\}$. If $P_0(A_\\ell)>0$, then there is a deterministic nonzero vector $v=v(\\nu)$ such that \\[ v\\cdot\\ell>0, \\qquad P_0\\!\\left(\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac{X_n}{n}=v\\right)=1. \\] If such a direction exists, then the deterministic sets of directions satisfy \\[ \\{u\\in S^{d-1}:P_0(A_u)=1\\} =\\{u\\in S^{d-1}:P_0(A_u)>0\\} =\\{u\\in S^{d-1}:v\\cdot u>0\\}. \\] In particular, $P_0(A_u)=0$ for each fixed $u\\in S^{d-1}$ with $v\\cdot u\\le0$, including every equator direction. The equality concerns annealed probabilities for each fixed deterministic direction, not a simultaneous pathwise assertion over all directions.","sourceOffset":10894,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Gaussian sequence","label":"gauss-sequence","text":"A sequence \\(r\\to\\infty\\) is Gaussian if \\[ n(r)\\Pr(|S|>a r)\\longrightarrow0\\qquad\\text{for every fixed }a>0. \\tag{14} \\]","sourceOffset":71882,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/220.md","scopeText":"The directional zero–one conjecture asks whether a random walk's probability of escape in a fixed direction must be zero or one. The formalization proves this for nearest-neighbor walks in independent identically distributed strictly elliptic environments on $\\mathbb Z^d$, for every $d\\ge3$ and every nonzero real direction. Strict ellipticity means that every allowed transition probability is positive almost surely; no uniform positive lower bound or moment condition is assumed. The probability is the annealed law from the origin.\n\nFor an i.i.d. uniformly elliptic nearest-neighbor random environment on $\\mathbb Z^d$, the formalization proves that almost-sure directional transience implies convergence of $X_n/n$ to a deterministic velocity with positive projection in that direction for ever","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/220.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":199082,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Directional-transience-implies-ballisticity-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"221-01","slug":"The-Mezard-Parisi-formula-for-diluted-spin-glasses-September-23-2026","familyId":"221","title":"The Mézard–Parisi formula for diluted spin glasses","abstract":"We prove the Mézard–Parisi hierarchical cavity formula for diluted even-arity Ising models in the Panchenko–Talagrand class. This resolves the variational equality conjecture for that class: the limiting pressure equals the infimum of the trial functional over all finite hierarchy depths and trial laws. Only first moments of the interaction and external field are required.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the Mézard–Parisi hierarchical cavity formula for Poisson-diluted even-arity Ising models satisfying the Panchenko–Talagrand factorization and positivity assumptions, with only first-moment integrability. The limiting free energy equals the infimum over finite-depth hierarchical trial laws. This includes the Viana–Bray model, symmetric diluted even-spin models, and weighted soft even-K satisfiability.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Mezard-Parisi-formula-for-diluted-spin-glasses-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Mezard-Parisi-formula-for-diluted-spin-glasses-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Mezard-Parisi-formula-for-diluted-spin-glasses-September-23-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-Mezard-Parisi-formula-for-diluted-spin-glasses-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Mezard-Parisi-formula-for-diluted-spin-glasses-September-23-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","preprints/The-Mezard-Parisi-formula-for-diluted-spin-glasses-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-Mezard-Parisi-formula-for-diluted-spin-glasses-September-23-2026/build/sections/model.tex","preprints/The-Mezard-Parisi-formula-for-diluted-spin-glasses-September-23-2026/build/sections/overview.tex","preprints/The-Mezard-Parisi-formula-for-diluted-spin-glasses-September-23-2026/build/sections/trees.tex","preprints/The-Mezard-Parisi-formula-for-diluted-spin-glasses-September-23-2026/build/sections/upper.tex","preprints/The-Mezard-Parisi-formula-for-diluted-spin-glasses-September-23-2026/build/sections/cavity.tex","preprints/The-Mezard-Parisi-formula-for-diluted-spin-glasses-September-23-2026/build/sections/perturbations.tex","preprints/The-Mezard-Parisi-formula-for-diluted-spin-glasses-September-23-2026/build/sections/decorrelation.tex","preprints/The-Mezard-Parisi-formula-for-diluted-spin-glasses-September-23-2026/build/sections/comparison.tex","preprints/The-Mezard-Parisi-formula-for-diluted-spin-glasses-September-23-2026/build/sections/reduction.tex","preprints/The-Mezard-Parisi-formula-for-diluted-spin-glasses-September-23-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Mezard-Parisi-formula-for-diluted-spin-glasses-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the M\\'ezard--Parisi hierarchical cavity formula for diluted even-arity Ising models in the Panchenko--Talagrand class. This resolves the variational equality conjecture for that class: the limiting pressure equals the infimum of the trial functional over all finite hierarchy depths and trial laws. Only first moments of the interaction and external field are required.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":425},{"kind":"section","title":"The model and the variational principle","sourceOffset":5434},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The hierarchical cavity functional","sourceOffset":7466},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":11528},{"kind":"section","title":"Calculus on finite trees","sourceOffset":14308},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Path kernels and insertions","sourceOffset":15053},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two expansion rules","sourceOffset":22455},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sums of extension coefficients","sourceOffset":28124},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The distribution of branching depths","sourceOffset":35177},{"kind":"section","title":"The interpolation upper bound","sourceOffset":38686},{"kind":"section","title":"A perturbed cavity reservoir","sourceOffset":44644},{"kind":"section","title":"Perturbations and identities on prescribed trees","sourceOffset":50498}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"main:theorem","text":"Under \\eqref{model:integrability}--\\eqref{model:positivity}, the thermodynamic limit exists and \\begin{equation} \\lim_{N\\to\\infty}F_N=\\inf_{r\\ge0}\\Phi_r. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":11106,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":null,"text":"\\label[proposition]{upper:bound} Under the hypotheses of \\cref{main:theorem}, $F_N\\le\\cB_r(\\zeta,\\mathbf m)$ for every $N$ and every admissible finite-level trial law. Consequently $F_N\\le\\inf_{r\\ge0}\\Phi_r$.","sourceOffset":39176,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/221.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves the Mézard–Parisi hierarchical cavity formula for diluted even-arity Ising models in the Panchenko–Talagrand class. Under the class's factorization, independence, integrability, and positivity assumptions, the finite-system pressure converges to the infimum of the trial functional over all finite hierarchy depths and trial laws. The arity is any even integer at least two and the interaction density is positive.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/221.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":122449,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Mezard-Parisi-formula-for-diluted-spin-glasses-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/The-Mezard-Parisi-formula-for-diluted-spin-glasses-September-23-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"222-01","slug":"The-free-energy-of-the-Ising-random-perceptron-September-24-2026","familyId":"222","title":"The free energy of the Ising random perceptron","abstract":"We determine the limiting free energy of the Ising perceptron with independent Gaussian patterns for every bounded Borel log-potential, at every fixed positive temperature and pattern density. We give an explicit variational formula for the limit and prove convergence in expectation and probability.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Determines finite-temperature variational free energies for Gaussian Ising perceptrons with bounded Borel log-potentials and Gaussian spherical perceptrons with bounded continuous potentials, at every positive pattern density. A spherical extension treats bi-orthogonally invariant disorder with compact limiting singular-value distributions and no outliers. At margin −1, the quadratic-penalty spherical model has a sharp feasibility threshold and limiting gap and force laws, with system size, zero temperature, and critical density taken in that order.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-free-energy-of-the-Ising-random-perceptron-September-24-2026/The-free-energy-of-the-Ising-random-perceptron-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-free-energy-of-the-Ising-random-perceptron-September-24-2026/The-free-energy-of-the-Ising-random-perceptron-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-free-energy-of-the-Ising-random-perceptron-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-free-energy-of-the-Ising-random-perceptron-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-free-energy-of-the-Ising-random-perceptron-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-free-energy-of-the-Ising-random-perceptron-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-free-energy-of-the-Ising-random-perceptron-September-24-2026/build/sections/model.tex","preprints/The-free-energy-of-the-Ising-random-perceptron-September-24-2026/build/sections/variational.tex","preprints/The-free-energy-of-the-Ising-random-perceptron-September-24-2026/build/sections/arrays.tex","preprints/The-free-energy-of-the-Ising-random-perceptron-September-24-2026/build/sections/upper.tex","preprints/The-free-energy-of-the-Ising-random-perceptron-September-24-2026/build/sections/bulk.tex","preprints/The-free-energy-of-the-Ising-random-perceptron-September-24-2026/build/sections/cavity.tex","preprints/The-free-energy-of-the-Ising-random-perceptron-September-24-2026/build/sections/extensions.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-free-energy-of-the-Ising-random-perceptron-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We determine the limiting free energy of the Ising perceptron with independent Gaussian patterns for every bounded Borel log-potential, at every fixed positive temperature and pattern density. We give an explicit variational formula for the limit and prove convergence in expectation and probability.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":363},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Storage models and rigorous predecessors","sourceOffset":1643},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The formula and its proof mechanisms","sourceOffset":4620},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization of the proof","sourceOffset":8711},{"kind":"section","title":"The model and the variational formula","sourceOffset":11608},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two finite Gaussian recursions","sourceOffset":12437},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Probabilistic conventions","sourceOffset":16947},{"kind":"section","title":"Variations, order, and the Ising entropy","sourceOffset":17839},{"kind":"subsection","title":"First variations and the pattern tail order","sourceOffset":18838},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Concavity of the field recursion","sourceOffset":27601},{"kind":"section","title":"Overlap arrays and probability cascades","sourceOffset":36423},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conditional identities, positivity, and duplication","sourceOffset":38064}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Gaussian Ising perceptron","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $\\alpha>0$ and every bounded Borel $f:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$, \\begin{equation} \\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\mathbb E p_N(f) =\\mathcal P_f(\\alpha) :=\\inf_{q\\in\\mathcal Q}\\{\\alpha V_f(q)+S_{\\mathrm I}(q)\\}. \\end{equation} Moreover, $p_N(f)$ converges to $\\mathcal P_f(\\alpha)$ in probability. The variational value is finite. In particular, the conclusion holds for $f=\\beta\\phi$ at every $\\beta>0$ and every bounded continuous activation $\\phi$, without symmetry, concavity, or a smallness restriction.","sourceOffset":15658,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Continuity and tail order","label":"var:continuity-order","text":"For step paths on arbitrary partitions, \\begin{align} |V_f(p)-V_f(q)|&\\le\\tfrac12\\|f'\\|_\\infty^2\\|p-q\\|_{L^1},\\\\ |\\ell(h')-\\ell(h)|&\\le\\tfrac12\\|h'-h\\|_{L^1}. \\end{align} Thus $V_f$ extends uniquely and continuously to all nondecreasing $[0,1]$-valued paths, and $\\ell$ extends to all bounded nonnegative nondecreasing paths. Moreover, \\begin{equation} \\int_s^1p(u)\\,du\\ge\\int_s^1q(u)\\,du \\quad\\hbox{for every }s\\in[0,1] \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad V_f(p)\\le V_f(q). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":23949,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Pattern and field recursions","label":"def:recursions","text":"For a step overlap path $q$, append $q_{-1}=0$ and $q_k=1$. Starting at $U_k=f$, set \\begin{equation} U_{i-1}=\\mathcal T_{q_i-q_{i-1},\\zeta_i}U_i \\quad(i=k,k-1,\\ldots,0),\\qquad V_f(q)=U_{-1}(0). \\end{equation} For a step field path $h$, append $h_{-1}=0$. Starting instead at $U_{k-1}(x)=\\log\\cosh x-h_{k-1}/2$, set \\begin{equation} U_{i-1}=\\mathcal T_{h_i-h_{i-1},\\zeta_i}U_i \\quad(i=k-1,k-2,\\ldots,0),\\qquad \\ell(h)=U_{-1}(0). \\end{equation} The letter $U$ refers to the recursion under discussion.","sourceOffset":13408,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Ising entropy","label":"def:entropy","text":"For $q\\in\\mathcal Q$, define the extended-valued functional \\begin{equation} S_{\\mathrm I}(q)=\\sup_{h\\in\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{step}}} \\left\\{\\ell(h)+\\frac12\\int_0^1h(u)q(u)\\,du\\right\\}. \\end{equation} The field partition is independent of the overlap partition. In calculations with two step paths, we always pass to a common refinement.","sourceOffset":15222,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/222.md","scopeText":"The linked formalization proves finiteness of the variational value used for the Ising random perceptron. For every nonnegative pattern density and every bounded continuous log-potential, the infimum over admissible overlap paths is a finite real number.\n\nThis is a supporting well-definedness result. It does not assert convergence of the finite-system pressure or the paper's extension to all bounded Borel log-potentials.\n\nThe formalization proves the variational formula for the spherical random perceptron's limiting pressure for every positive pattern density and inverse temperature and every bounded continuous single-pattern potential. It shows that the variational value is finite and that the pressure converges to it both in expectation and in probability.\n\nThe linked spherical-field res","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/222.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":151512,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-free-energy-of-the-Ising-random-perceptron-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"222-02","slug":"Microscopic-jamming-in-the-negative-spherical-perceptron-September-24-2026","familyId":"222","title":"Microscopic jamming in the negative spherical perceptron","abstract":"We prove a sharp feasibility threshold and limiting gap and force laws for the spherical perceptron with margin −1 and quadratic penalty. The limits are taken successively in system size, inverse temperature, and density approaching the threshold from above. They agree for Gaussian coordinates and for the equal mixture of centered Gaussian coordinates with variances $1-\\varepsilon$ and $1+\\varepsilon$, for every sufficiently small fixed ε. The contact-removed gap cumulative law and the mean-one force cumulative law satisfy $\\displaystyle G_J(u)=u^{1-\\gamma+o(1)},\\qquad F_J(s)=s^{1+\\theta+o(1)},$ as $u\\downarrow0$ and $s\\downarrow0$, with $\\gamma=(2+\\theta)^{-1}$, $0.4126930\\lt \\gamma\\lt 0.4126934$, and $0.4231063\\lt \\theta\\lt 0.4231088$. A finite numerical certificate for these exponent intervals, together with its mathematical error bounds, is included.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Determines finite-temperature variational free energies for Gaussian Ising perceptrons with bounded Borel log-potentials and Gaussian spherical perceptrons with bounded continuous potentials, at every positive pattern density. A spherical extension treats bi-orthogonally invariant disorder with compact limiting singular-value distributions and no outliers. At margin −1, the quadratic-penalty spherical model has a sharp feasibility threshold and limiting gap and force laws, with system size, zero temperature, and critical density taken in that order.","pdfPath":"preprints/Microscopic-jamming-in-the-negative-spherical-perceptron-September-24-2026/Microscopic-jamming-in-the-negative-spherical-perceptron-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Microscopic-jamming-in-the-negative-spherical-perceptron-September-24-2026/Microscopic-jamming-in-the-negative-spherical-perceptron-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Microscopic-jamming-in-the-negative-spherical-perceptron-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Microscopic-jamming-in-the-negative-spherical-perceptron-September-24-2026/build/main.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Microscopic-jamming-in-the-negative-spherical-perceptron-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a sharp feasibility threshold and limiting gap and force laws for the spherical perceptron with margin $-1$ and quadratic penalty. The limits are taken successively in system size, inverse temperature, and density approaching the threshold from above. They agree for Gaussian coordinates and for the equal mixture of centered Gaussian coordinates with variances $1-\\eps$ and $1+\\eps$, for every sufficiently small fixed $\\eps$. The contact-removed gap cumulative law and the mean-one force cumulative law satisfy \\[ G_J(u)=u^{1-\\gamma+o(1)},\\qquad F_J(s)=s^{1+\\theta+o(1)}, \\] as $u\\downarrow0$ and $s\\downarrow0$, with $\\gamma=(2+\\theta)^{-1}$, $0.4126930<\\gamma<0.4126934$, and $0.4231063<\\theta<0.4231088$. A finite numerical certificate for these exponent intervals, together with its mathematical error bounds, is included.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The problem and the proof architecture","sourceOffset":884},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and contribution","sourceOffset":958},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model and observables","sourceOffset":7476},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Main result","sourceOffset":10478},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The endpoint mechanism and the spectral characterization","sourceOffset":11832},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Architecture and conventions","sourceOffset":14637},{"kind":"section","title":"Hierarchical Gaussian functionals and spherical entropy","sourceOffset":20421},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite cascades and their change of measure","sourceOffset":21758},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The row functional and tilted derivatives","sourceOffset":32770},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The spherical functional","sourceOffset":43161},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Concavity and the entropy conjugate","sourceOffset":52597},{"kind":"section","title":"Concavity and stability of the row functional","sourceOffset":62842}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Microscopic criticality and mixture universality","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist $\\alpha_c\\in(0,\\infty)$ and $\\eps_0\\in(0,1)$ with the following properties for every fixed $\\eps\\in[0,\\eps_0)$ and coordinate law $\\nu_\\eps$. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item The SAT probability tends to one at every fixed $\\alpha<\\alpha_c$ and to zero at every fixed $\\alpha>\\alpha_c$. \\item All the ordered limits in \\eqref{eq:intro-GJ}--\\eqref{eq:intro-FJ} exist for their indicated positive arguments. They are independent of $\\eps$. \\item $F_J$ is the cumulative distribution function on $(0,\\infty)$ of a probability law of mean one. It has no atom at zero. There are exponents $\\gamma,\\theta$ such that \\[ G_J(u)=u^{1-\\gamma+o(1)},\\qquad F_J(s)=s^{1+\\theta+o(1)}, \\] as the corresponding argument decreases to zero, and \\[ \\gamma=\\frac1{2+\\theta},\\qquad 0.4126930<\\gamma<0.4126934,\\qquad 0.4231063<\\theta<0.4231088. \\] \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":10503,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Transfer of the remaining ordered limits","label":"thresh:ordered-limits","text":"Fix $0\\leq\\eps<\\eps_0(B_0)$. On a full interval above the common threshold, the thermodynamic gap and normalized-force observables at every fixed positive temperature and fixed argument agree with their Gaussian counterparts. Consequently each further ordered limit in the problem exists for the mixture law if and only if it exists for the Gaussian law, and their values agree whenever it exists.","sourceOffset":196266,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/222.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":471599,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":32,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Microscopic-jamming-in-the-negative-spherical-perceptron-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"222-03","slug":"The-spherical-perceptron-with-bi-orthogonally-invariant-disorder-September-24-2026","familyId":"222","title":"The spherical perceptron with bi-orthogonally invariant disorder","abstract":"We determine the limiting free energy of a spherical perceptron with a bounded continuous activation and a bi-orthogonally invariant disorder matrix. The singular values may have any compact limiting distribution, provided there are no outliers. We give an explicit variational formula for this limit.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Determines finite-temperature variational free energies for Gaussian Ising perceptrons with bounded Borel log-potentials and Gaussian spherical perceptrons with bounded continuous potentials, at every positive pattern density. A spherical extension treats bi-orthogonally invariant disorder with compact limiting singular-value distributions and no outliers. At margin −1, the quadratic-penalty spherical model has a sharp feasibility threshold and limiting gap and force laws, with system size, zero temperature, and critical density taken in that order.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-spherical-perceptron-with-bi-orthogonally-invariant-disorder-September-24-2026/The-spherical-perceptron-with-bi-orthogonally-invariant-disorder-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-spherical-perceptron-with-bi-orthogonally-invariant-disorder-September-24-2026/The-spherical-perceptron-with-bi-orthogonally-invariant-disorder-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-spherical-perceptron-with-bi-orthogonally-invariant-disorder-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-spherical-perceptron-with-bi-orthogonally-invariant-disorder-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-spherical-perceptron-with-bi-orthogonally-invariant-disorder-September-24-2026/build/pdf-glyph-mappings.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-spherical-perceptron-with-bi-orthogonally-invariant-disorder-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We determine the limiting free energy of a spherical perceptron with a bounded continuous activation and a bi-orthogonally invariant disorder matrix. The singular values may have any compact limiting distribution, provided there are no outliers. We give an explicit variational formula for this limit.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":347},{"kind":"section","title":"The variational formula","sourceOffset":7966},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Angular pressure and the spherical theorem","sourceOffset":8512},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A Gaussian model with outer and inner columns","sourceOffset":12154},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Paths with a shared coordinate","sourceOffset":13820},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Explicit evaluation of the angular pressure","sourceOffset":16382},{"kind":"section","title":"The Gaussian theorem and its probabilistic tools","sourceOffset":20253},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Marked cascades, zero restrictions, and visit variables","sourceOffset":21710},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Joint overlap identities and ordered matrix paths","sourceOffset":26977},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fresh fields and the one-pattern variation","sourceOffset":34051},{"kind":"section","title":"The Gaussian canonical calculation","sourceOffset":36411},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A canonical multiplier","sourceOffset":37658}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Fix $\\alpha>0$, $M=\\lfloor\\alpha N\\rfloor$, and $f\\in C_b(\\R)$. Suppose the law of $A_N$ is invariant under left and right orthogonal multiplication and, in probability, \\begin{equation} \\frac1{r_N}\\sum_{r=1}^{r_N}\\delta_{s_{N,r}}\\Longrightarrow\\nu, \\qquad \\max_{r\\le r_N}\\operatorname{dist}(s_{N,r},\\supp\\nu)\\longrightarrow0, \\end{equation} where $\\nu$ is a deterministic compactly supported probability measure on $[0,\\infty)$. Then $F_N(A_N,f)$ converges in probability and in mean to a deterministic value $F_{\\alpha,\\nu}(f)$. \\pagebreak[3] Here is its formula. Approximate the singular slots in operator norm by finitely many positive values $s_1,\\ldots,s_h$ whose multiplicities satisfy $n_j/M\\to p_j>0$. Thus $\\sum_jp_j=\\min(1,1/\\alpha)$. Put $v_j=\\alpha p_j$, and, if $\\alpha<1$, add an input null block with $v_0=1-\\alpha$. Let $\\mathcal I=\\{1,\\ldots,h\\}$ when $\\alpha\\ge1$ and $\\mathcal I=\\{0,1,\\ldots,h\\}$ otherwise. The variable $\\rho_i$ is the fraction of the input squared norm assigned to block $i$. The value for this finite approximation is \\begin{equation} \\sup_{\\substack{\\rho_i>0\\\\\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal I}\\rho_i=1}} \\left\\{ \\frac12\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal I}v_i\\log\\frac{\\rho_i}{v_i} +\\alpha P_p^f\\left(\\left(\\frac{s_j^2\\rho_j}{\\alpha}\\right)_{j=1}^h\\right) \\right\\}. \\end{equation} The limit of \\eqref{eq:radialformula} as the spectral mesh tends to zero is $F_{\\alpha,\\nu}(f)$. Blocks with zero limiting proportion are not used in \\eqref{eq:radialformula}; the no-outlier assumption ensu","sourceOffset":10087,"truncated":true},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Angular formula","label":"thm:angular","text":"For $\\sum_jp_j\\le1$ and $f\\in C_b(\\R)$, $P_M^f(a)$ converges in probability, uniformly on compact sets of $a\\in[0,\\infty)^h$, to a deterministic continuous function $P_p^f(a)$. Its explicit evaluation is given by \\eqref{eq:angularformula} and \\eqref{eq:pone} below. For $p<1$ and positive $a$, \\eqref{eq:angularformula} holds for every sufficiently large fixed coefficient radius $\\kappa$.","sourceOffset":9252,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/222.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":144432,"sourceFileCount":2,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-spherical-perceptron-with-bi-orthogonally-invariant-disorder-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/The-spherical-perceptron-with-bi-orthogonally-invariant-disorder-September-24-2026/build/pdf-glyph-mappings.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"222-04","slug":"The-free-energy-of-the-spherical-random-perceptron-September-24-2026","familyId":"222","title":"The free energy of the spherical random perceptron","abstract":"We prove an exact variational formula for the limiting pressure of the spherical random perceptron with an arbitrary bounded continuous single-pattern potential. The formula holds at every fixed positive density and inverse temperature, with convergence in expectation and in probability.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Determines finite-temperature variational free energies for Gaussian Ising perceptrons with bounded Borel log-potentials and Gaussian spherical perceptrons with bounded continuous potentials, at every positive pattern density. A spherical extension treats bi-orthogonally invariant disorder with compact limiting singular-value distributions and no outliers. At margin −1, the quadratic-penalty spherical model has a sharp feasibility threshold and limiting gap and force laws, with system size, zero temperature, and critical density taken in that order.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-free-energy-of-the-spherical-random-perceptron-September-24-2026/The-free-energy-of-the-spherical-random-perceptron-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-free-energy-of-the-spherical-random-perceptron-September-24-2026/The-free-energy-of-the-spherical-random-perceptron-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-free-energy-of-the-spherical-random-perceptron-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-free-energy-of-the-spherical-random-perceptron-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-free-energy-of-the-spherical-random-perceptron-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-map.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-free-energy-of-the-spherical-random-perceptron-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove an exact variational formula for the limiting pressure of the spherical random perceptron with an arbitrary bounded continuous single-pattern potential. The formula holds at every fixed positive density and inverse temperature, with convergence in expectation and in probability.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction and statement","sourceOffset":334},{"kind":"section","title":"Quantiles and cascade preliminaries","sourceOffset":11041},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Overlap identities and finite cascades","sourceOffset":13313},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Marked cascade transformations","sourceOffset":20584},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Passing fresh fields through overlap limits","sourceOffset":26739},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Gaussian differentiation at fixed size","sourceOffset":29814},{"kind":"section","title":"The single-pattern value and its order property","sourceOffset":32853},{"kind":"section","title":"The spherical linear-field formula","sourceOffset":41140},{"kind":"section","title":"The upper bound","sourceOffset":52044},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Enrichment, concentration, and derivatives","sourceOffset":52507},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A contact minimum forces the joint GG identities","sourceOffset":57034},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Monotone coupling and the contradiction","sourceOffset":62566}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $\\alpha,\\beta>0$ and every $\\phi\\in C_b(\\R;\\R)$, \\[ \\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\E p_N =\\inf_{m\\in\\U}\\{\\alpha V_{\\beta\\phi}(m)+S(m)\\} =:\\mathcal P(\\alpha,\\beta,\\phi), \\qquad p_N\\xrightarrow{\\mathbb P}\\mathcal P(\\alpha,\\beta,\\phi). \\] Trials of infinite entropy have value $+\\infty$. No condition that $m=1$ near $1$ is imposed.","sourceOffset":1974,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Spherical linear-field formula","label":"prop:spherical","text":"For fixed interval weights, uniformly on compact sets of nonnegative nondecreasing step field values, \\begin{equation} l_n(h)\\ \\longrightarrow\\ \\min_{q_0\\le\\cdots\\le q_{k-1}\\,;\\ 0\\le q_i<1} \\left\\{S(q)-\\sum_i w_i h_i q_i\\right\\} \\end{equation} as $n\\to\\infty$. Furthermore, for each fixed $0\\le B<1$, if $0\\le q(u)\\le B$ a.e. is nondecreasing and \\begin{equation} 2h(u)=A_q(q(u)),\\qquad A_q(r):=\\int_0^r\\frac{dt}{D_q(t)^2}\\quad(0\\le r<1), \\end{equation} then \\begin{equation} l_n(h)=S(q)-\\int_0^1 hq+o(1), \\end{equation} where the error tends to zero uniformly over these $q$.","sourceOffset":42896,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/222.md","scopeText":"The linked formalization proves finiteness of the variational value used for the Ising random perceptron. For every nonnegative pattern density and every bounded continuous log-potential, the infimum over admissible overlap paths is a finite real number.\n\nThis is a supporting well-definedness result. It does not assert convergence of the finite-system pressure or the paper's extension to all bounded Borel log-potentials.\n\nThe formalization proves the variational formula for the spherical random perceptron's limiting pressure for every positive pattern density and inverse temperature and every bounded continuous single-pattern potential. It shows that the variational value is finite and that the pressure converges to it both in expectation and in probability.\n\nThe linked spherical-field res","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/222.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":96026,"sourceFileCount":2,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-free-energy-of-the-spherical-random-perceptron-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"223-01","slug":"Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026","familyId":"223","title":"Square-lattice FK interfaces and nested loops for 1 <= q < 4","abstract":"For every fixed $1\\le q\\lt 4$, critical square-lattice random-cluster Dobrushin interfaces converge as ordered curves to chordal $\\mathop{\\mathrm{SLE}}\\nolimits _{\\kappa(q)}$, where $\\kappa(q)=4\\pi/\\arccos(-\\sqrt q/2)$, confirming the Rohde–Schramm prediction in this parameter range. This holds in every bounded Jordan domain under uniform marked boundary approximation. The complete nested plane loop collections converge to whole-plane $\\mathop{\\mathrm{CLE}}\\nolimits _{\\kappa(q)}$ in a spherical matching topology retaining multiplicities and traversals. At q = 1 we obtain Cardy's formula for square-lattice bond percolation with free boundary edges.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves chordal SLEκ limits for critical square-lattice random-cluster interfaces for $0\\lt q\\le4$, with $\\kappa=4\\pi/\\arccos(-\\sqrt q/2)$: bounded Jordan domains are allowed for q ≥ 1, and smooth Jordan domains for q < 1, under the stated marked-boundary approximations. For $1\\le q\\le4$, complete nested plane loops converge to CLEκ.","pdfPath":"preprints/Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026/build/figures/switch-pairings.tex","preprints/Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026/build/sections/angular.tex","preprints/Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026/build/sections/observable.tex","preprints/Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026/build/figures/four-change-pairings.tex","preprints/Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026/build/figures/paired-wall.tex","preprints/Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026/build/sections/chordal.tex","preprints/Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026/build/figures/jordan-sandwich.tex","preprints/Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026/build/figures/moving-comparison.tex","preprints/Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026/build/sections/peeling.tex","preprints/Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026/build/figures/radial-tree.tex","preprints/Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026/build/sections/percolation.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed $1\\le q<4$, critical square-lattice random-cluster Dobrushin interfaces converge as ordered curves to chordal $\\SLE_{\\kappa(q)}$, where $\\kappa(q)=4\\pi/\\arccos(-\\sqrt q/2)$, confirming the Rohde--Schramm prediction in this parameter range. This holds in every bounded Jordan domain under uniform marked boundary approximation. The complete nested plane loop collections converge to whole-plane $\\CLE_{\\kappa(q)}$ in a spherical matching topology retaining multiplicities and traversals. At $q=1$ we obtain Cardy's formula for square-lattice bond percolation with free boundary edges.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction and precise statements","sourceOffset":662},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and its boundary conventions","sourceOffset":6051},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Complete loop collections","sourceOffset":9878},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Square-lattice bond crossings","sourceOffset":12677},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Square-lattice bond critical exponents","sourceOffset":14330},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof structure and technical contributions","sourceOffset":16305},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions on constants and limits","sourceOffset":21220},{"kind":"section","title":"Tiles, positive laws, and localization","sourceOffset":22226},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Tiles and boundary caps","sourceOffset":22925},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Crossing estimates and finite plane loops","sourceOffset":28280},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison through a collar","sourceOffset":32768},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Passages and positive local tilts","sourceOffset":39616}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Interfaces and all nested loops","label":"intro:main","text":"For every fixed real $1\\le q<4$, the following assertions hold. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item For every bounded Jordan domain $D$ with distinct boundary marks $a,b$, and every approximation satisfying \\eqref{intro:marked-jordan}, the Dobrushin strand $\\eta_n$ converges in law, in \\eqref{intro:curve-metric}, to chordal $\\SLE_{\\kappa(q)}$ from $a$ to $b$ in $D$. \\item Under the specified free infinite-volume law, $\\Gamma_\\delta$ converges in law as $\\delta\\downarrow0$ to the nested whole-plane $\\CLE_{\\kappa(q)}$, in the spherical collection topology just defined. The limit is M\\\"obius invariant. \\end{enumerate} Both conclusions hold along every mesh sequence. No uniformity in $q$ is asserted.","sourceOffset":11233,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Cardy's formula for square-lattice bond percolation","label":"intro:cardy","text":"Let $\\psi:D\\to\\H$ send $a,c,d$ to $0,1,\\infty$, and put $x=\\psi(b)\\in(0,1)$. Under the boundary and approximation conventions above, \\begin{equation} \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\mathbb P(C_n) =\\frac{\\displaystyle\\int_0^x[t(1-t)]^{-2/3}\\,\\dd t} {\\displaystyle\\int_0^1[t(1-t)]^{-2/3}\\,\\dd t}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":13248,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":408187,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":23,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Square-lattice-FK-interfaces-and-nested-loops-for-1-leq-q-lt-4-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"223-02","slug":"Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026","familyId":"223","title":"Self-dual random-cluster interfaces below one","abstract":"For every fixed $0\\lt q\\lt 1$, we prove that the Dobrushin interface of the square-lattice random-cluster model at its self-dual parameter converges to chordal SLEκ, where $\\kappa=4\\pi/\\arccos(-\\sqrt q/2)\\in(6,8)$. The approximating domains are simple closed nearest-neighbor lattice polygons with distinct marked vertices, whose marked boundary parametrizations converge uniformly to those of a bounded smooth Jordan domain. Convergence holds along the full mesh sequence in the uniform metric on oriented curves modulo increasing reparametrization. The proof combines finite connection comparisons below one, localization in irregular tiled disks, and a boundary observable that determines the limiting Loewner driver.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves chordal SLEκ limits for critical square-lattice random-cluster interfaces for $0\\lt q\\le4$, with $\\kappa=4\\pi/\\arccos(-\\sqrt q/2)$: bounded Jordan domains are allowed for q ≥ 1, and smooth Jordan domains for q < 1, under the stated marked-boundary approximations. For $1\\le q\\le4$, complete nested plane loops converge to CLEκ.","pdfPath":"preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/figures/dependencies.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/sections/00-conventions.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/figures/local-switch.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-pressure.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/figures/interlacing.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-rectangles.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/figures/slab-pockets.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-sectors.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/sections/03a-radial.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/sections/03b-blank.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/figures/path-defect.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-disks.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/figures/disk-localization.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/figures/actual-network.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-angular.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-physical.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/figures/charged-cylinder.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-observable.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/figures/paired-walls.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-chordal.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/figures/short-seam.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/appendices/finite-calculus.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/appendices/reflection-templates.tex","preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/figures/reflection-template-projections.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed $0<q<1$, we prove that the Dobrushin interface of the square-lattice random-cluster model at its self-dual parameter converges to chordal $\\SLE_\\kappa$, where $\\kappa=4\\pi/\\arccos(-\\sqrt q/2)\\in(6,8)$. The approximating domains are simple closed nearest-neighbor lattice polygons with distinct marked vertices, whose marked boundary parametrizations converge uniformly to those of a bounded smooth Jordan domain. Convergence holds along the full mesh sequence in the uniform metric on oriented curves modulo increasing reparametrization. The proof combines finite connection comparisons below one, localization in irregular tiled disks, and a boundary observable that determines the limiting Loewner driver.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":797},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite law and the main theorem","sourceOffset":1842},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The problem and earlier results","sourceOffset":5733},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":9109},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ancestry of the methods","sourceOffset":16265},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A common-domain consequence","sourceOffset":22368},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and dependencies","sourceOffset":24720},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite laws and geometric conventions","sourceOffset":27756},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Odds, identifications, and conditional laws","sourceOffset":28183},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Tiles, caps, and Euler's identity","sourceOffset":31000},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cut surfaces and the order of limits","sourceOffset":36780},{"kind":"section","title":"Site pressure and boundary gluing","sourceOffset":39265}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Below-one interface convergence","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed $q\\in(0,1)$ and every marked domain approximation just specified, the laws of $\\eta_\\delta$ converge as $\\delta\\to0$ in the metric \\eqref{eq:curve-metric} to chordal $\\SLE_\\kappa(D;a,b)$, where \\[ \\kappa=\\frac{4\\pi}{\\arccos(-\\sqrt q/2)}. \\] The convergence holds along the full mesh sequence.","sourceOffset":4958,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Combined finite-law parameter range","label":"cor:full-fk-range","text":"For this statement, fix $q\\in(0,4)$ and put \\[ p(q)=\\frac{\\sqrt q}{1+\\sqrt q}, \\qquad \\kappa(q)=\\frac{4\\pi}{\\arccos(-\\sqrt q/2)}. \\] Let $D$ be a bounded Jordan domain with smooth simple boundary and distinct boundary marks $a,b$. Take simple nearest-neighbor lattice polygon approximations $D_{\\delta_n}$, with $\\delta_n\\downarrow0$ and the full closed-polygon graphs specified above, and take $a_{\\delta_n},b_{\\delta_n}$ to be distinct polygon vertices. Require counterclockwise parametrizations of $\\partial D_{\\delta_n}$ converging uniformly on $[0,1]$ to one of $\\partial D$, all injective on $[0,1)$, with the marks $a_{\\delta_n},b_{\\delta_n}$ and $a,b$ at parameters $0,1/2$ respectively. Wire all vertices of the counterclockwise arc from $b_{\\delta_n}$ to $a_{\\delta_n}$, including its endpoints, leaving the other boundary vertices singleton. Sample \\eqref{eq:finite-target-law} with this $q$ and $p=p(q)$, and declare wired-arc boundary edges open for drawing. Then the prescribed medial strand, oriented from $a_{\\delta_n}$ to $b_{\\delta_n}$, converges in law along the full mesh sequence to chordal $\\SLE_{\\kappa(q)}(D;a,b)$ in \\eqref{eq:curve-metric}. No uniformity in $q$ is asserted.","sourceOffset":22611,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Network and actual connections","label":"def:actual-network","text":"A network connection is an open connection in the graph after the prescribed vertex identifications. An actual connection uses open physical edges and does not jump between distinct vertices in a boundary class. A measured actual path may subsequently be contracted to a terminal when its opposite side remains uninspected.","sourceOffset":30275,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Corridors and level transversals","label":"rect:corridor-definition","text":"In a fixed rectangle $[0,W]\\times[0,H]$ with positive integer dimensions, a vertical Jordan corridor is the closed region between two disjoint simple dual-lattice paths from top to bottom, together with the top and bottom intervals between them. The paths' interior portions lie strictly inside the rectangle; their end links run from half-integer cell centers to boundary-edge midpoints. Its primal graph consists of all vertices and edges contained in the closed region. Two nonempty subsets of the top and bottom intervals are wired separately, with no other ties. A level transversal is a straigh","sourceOffset":93405,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":814149,"sourceFileCount":27,"statementCount":39,"definitionCount":4,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Self-dual-random-cluster-interfaces-below-one-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"223-03","slug":"Quenched-SLE-Universality-for-Weakly-Disordered-FK-Ising-Interfaces-October-5-2026","familyId":"223","title":"Quenched SLE Universality for Weakly Disordered FK–Ising Interfaces","abstract":"We prove that critical FK–Ising interfaces with sufficiently weak, symmetric, independent two-valued bond disorder converge to chordal SLE16/3. The disorder strength is fixed as the mesh tends to zero, and convergence of the conditional curve laws holds in probability over the environment.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves chordal SLEκ limits for critical square-lattice random-cluster interfaces for $0\\lt q\\le4$, with $\\kappa=4\\pi/\\arccos(-\\sqrt q/2)$: bounded Jordan domains are allowed for q ≥ 1, and smooth Jordan domains for q < 1, under the stated marked-boundary approximations. For $1\\le q\\le4$, complete nested plane loops converge to CLEκ.","pdfPath":"preprints/Quenched-SLE-Universality-for-Weakly-Disordered-FK-Ising-Interfaces-October-5-2026/quenched-fk-ising.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quenched-SLE-Universality-for-Weakly-Disordered-FK-Ising-Interfaces-October-5-2026/quenched-fk-ising.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quenched-SLE-Universality-for-Weakly-Disordered-FK-Ising-Interfaces-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Quenched-SLE-Universality-for-Weakly-Disordered-FK-Ising-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE-Universality-for-Weakly-Disordered-FK-Ising-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE-Universality-for-Weakly-Disordered-FK-Ising-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/inputs.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE-Universality-for-Weakly-Disordered-FK-Ising-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/boundary.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE-Universality-for-Weakly-Disordered-FK-Ising-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/polygon.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE-Universality-for-Weakly-Disordered-FK-Ising-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/pure.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE-Universality-for-Weakly-Disordered-FK-Ising-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/tightness.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE-Universality-for-Weakly-Disordered-FK-Ising-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/figures/gate.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE-Universality-for-Weakly-Disordered-FK-Ising-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/slits.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE-Universality-for-Weakly-Disordered-FK-Ising-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/identification.tex","preprints/Quenched-SLE-Universality-for-Weakly-Disordered-FK-Ising-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Quenched-SLE-Universality-for-Weakly-Disordered-FK-Ising-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that critical FK--Ising interfaces with sufficiently weak, symmetric, independent two-valued bond disorder converge to chordal $\\SLE_{16/3}$. The disorder strength is fixed as the mesh tends to zero, and convergence of the conditional curve laws holds in probability over the environment.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":360},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and the result","sourceOffset":836},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and the source of the difficulty","sourceOffset":4948},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its reusable ingredients","sourceOffset":7416},{"kind":"section","title":"Random-cluster conventions and imported estimates","sourceOffset":11048},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two probability spaces and planar duality","sourceOffset":11561},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The physical critical point and local barriers","sourceOffset":14716},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Vanishing bulk perturbations","sourceOffset":17789},{"kind":"section","title":"Pure transfer estimates at the boundary","sourceOffset":22579},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Shell kernels and their projective diameter","sourceOffset":23591},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conditional expectations and positive weights","sourceOffset":44200},{"kind":"section","title":"Partition functions up to a polygon boundary","sourceOffset":48307}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exists $\\eps_0>0$ such that for every fixed $0<\\eps<\\eps_0$, every bounded marked Jordan domain $(D;a,b)$, and every deterministic filled square-lattice approximation satisfying \\eqref{eq:jordan-approximation}, \\[ d_{\\mathrm{BL}}\\bigl(\\mu_{n,\\xi}, \\operatorname{Law}(\\SLE_{16/3}(D;a,b))\\bigr) \\xrightarrow[n\\to\\infty]{\\P_{\\mathrm{env}}}0. \\] The constant $\\eps_0$ is independent of the domain and its approximation.","sourceOffset":4295,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Four-mark test in a remaining domain","label":"prop:moving-test","text":"Consider the four-change open-cap law and any sequence of switch-revealing stopping rules before completion of its strand. Let $A_n$ be events determined by the stopped history. Suppose that, on $A_n$, there are basepoints $z_n$ and constants $r_0,\\alpha_0>0$, independent of $n$ and of the history, such that \\begin{enumerate} \\item $B(z_n,r_0)\\subset U_n$; \\item in the conformal disk chart normalized at $z_n$, the four current prime-end marks, including the tip, have pairwise distance at least $\\alpha_0$ on the unit circle. \\end{enumerate} Assume also that the stopped prefixes form a tight family in the oriented-curve topology, as in Proposition \\ref{prop:curve-tightness}. Write $\\mathcal F_n$ for the stopped information, $E_{\\mathrm P}$ for the primal pairing, and $\\chi_{U_n}$ for the ordered cross-ratio of the four current marks. Then, for every $t>0$, \\begin{equation} \\P_{\\mathrm{open}}\\left( A_n\\cap\\left\\{ \\left|\\P_{\\mathrm{open}}(E_{\\mathrm P}\\mid\\mathcal F_n) -f(\\chi_{U_n})\\right|>t\\right\\}\\right)\\longrightarrow0. \\end{equation} The basepoints may be selected from the history. The original domains lie in a fixed bounded set, so every deterministic sequence of these basepoints has a convergent subsequence.","sourceOffset":125142,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"eq:projective-diameter","text":"For a strictly positive kernel $T$ on two finite state spaces, its projective diameter is \\begin{equation} \\Delta(T)=\\sup_{u,u',v,v'} \\left|\\log\\frac{T(u,v)T(u',v')}{T(u,v')T(u',v)}\\right|. \\end{equation} If both boundary rays are free, write $[u]=\\{u,-u\\}$ and define the kernel on cap states modulo sign by \\begin{equation} \\overline T([u],[v]) =\\sum_{\\varepsilon,\\varepsilon'\\in\\{-1,1\\}} T(\\varepsilon u,\\varepsilon'v). \\end{equation} Thus the two absolute cap signs are summed independently.","sourceOffset":24740,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Polygons on sheets","label":"def:sheet-polygon","text":"Let $\\mathcal S$ be an oriented flat surface with a map $\\pi:\\mathcal S\\to\\R^2$ whose restriction to each sufficiently small neighborhood is an orientation-preserving isometry. A collared orthogonal polygon is a compact polygonal disk $\\mathcal P\\subset \\mathcal S$ such that $\\pi$ is defined on a neighborhood of $\\mathcal P$, and each side of $\\mathcal P$ is horizontal or vertical in these local coordinates. The polygon has finitely many sides; the map $\\pi$ need not be injective on $\\mathcal P$. Its lattice approximation is obtained by rounding its sides to the lifted square grid of mesh $\\de","sourceOffset":48996,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":188511,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Quenched-SLE-Universality-for-Weakly-Disordered-FK-Ising-Interfaces-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"223-04","slug":"Thermal-FK-Ising-interfaces-and-massive-SLE-October-5-2026","familyId":"223","title":"Thermal FK–Ising interfaces and massive SLE","abstract":"We prove convergence of thermal FK–Ising interfaces, for every fixed nonzero mass of either sign, on uniformly angle-bounded isoradial lattices in bounded simply connected domains. The limit is independent of the lattice and the admissible domain approximation. For positive mass it is the unique massive SLE$_{16/3}$ law with locally finite-energy drift prescribed by a massive boundary value problem; negative mass follows by duality and reversal. The theorem also allows Carathéodory approximations whose diameters diverge.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves chordal SLEκ limits for critical square-lattice random-cluster interfaces for $0\\lt q\\le4$, with $\\kappa=4\\pi/\\arccos(-\\sqrt q/2)$: bounded Jordan domains are allowed for q ≥ 1, and smooth Jordan domains for q < 1, under the stated marked-boundary approximations. For $1\\le q\\le4$, complete nested plane loops converge to CLEκ.","pdfPath":"preprints/Thermal-FK-Ising-interfaces-and-massive-SLE-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Thermal-FK-Ising-interfaces-and-massive-SLE-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Thermal-FK-Ising-interfaces-and-massive-SLE-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Thermal-FK-Ising-interfaces-and-massive-SLE-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Thermal-FK-Ising-interfaces-and-massive-SLE-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Thermal-FK-Ising-interfaces-and-massive-SLE-October-5-2026/build/sections/model.tex","preprints/Thermal-FK-Ising-interfaces-and-massive-SLE-October-5-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Thermal-FK-Ising-interfaces-and-massive-SLE-October-5-2026/build/sections/energy.tex","preprints/Thermal-FK-Ising-interfaces-and-massive-SLE-October-5-2026/build/sections/calculus.tex","preprints/Thermal-FK-Ising-interfaces-and-massive-SLE-October-5-2026/build/sections/uniqueness.tex","preprints/Thermal-FK-Ising-interfaces-and-massive-SLE-October-5-2026/build/sections/localization.tex","preprints/Thermal-FK-Ising-interfaces-and-massive-SLE-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Thermal-FK-Ising-interfaces-and-massive-SLE-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove convergence of thermal FK--Ising interfaces, for every fixed nonzero mass of either sign, on uniformly angle-bounded isoradial lattices in bounded simply connected domains. The limit is independent of the lattice and the admissible domain approximation. For positive mass it is the unique massive SLE$_{16/3}$ law with locally finite-energy drift prescribed by a massive boundary value problem; negative mass follows by duality and reversal. The theorem also allows Carath\\'eodory approximations whose diameters diverge.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":592},{"kind":"section","title":"The model and the limiting law","sourceOffset":6333},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Domains, weights and curves","sourceOffset":6411},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The scalar boundary value problem","sourceOffset":11277},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A continuum definition","sourceOffset":15189},{"kind":"section","title":"Geometry, compactness, and the martingale observable","sourceOffset":18213},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two established inputs","sourceOffset":18800},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exploration domains and a simultaneous arm comparison","sourceOffset":23929},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conformal control of cell changes","sourceOffset":34527},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The signed martingale field","sourceOffset":47774},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite-energy estimate at the moving marked point","sourceOffset":54864},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The point clock and the two geometric inputs","sourceOffset":56698}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $(\\Omega,a,b,m)$ as above, there is a curve law $\\mu_{\\Omega,a,b,m}$ such that, for every sequence with mesh tending to zero and satisfying all the preceding domain, marked Carath\\'eodory, angle and nome assumptions, \\[ \\mathcal L(\\chi_\\delta\\circ\\gamma_\\delta) \\ \\Longrightarrow\\ \\chi_*\\mu_{\\Omega,a,b,m} \\] in the metric \\eqref{mod:curve-metric}, along the full sequence. The physical prime-end curve law $\\mu_{\\Omega,a,b,m}$ is independent of the lattices, admissible domain approximations and nome sequence. For $m>0$, $\\mu_{\\Omega,a,b,m}$ is the unique law in Definition \\ref{def:law}. For $m<0$, it is the reversal of the positive-mass law in $(\\Omega,b,a)$ with mass $|m|$, with primal and dual boundary labels interchanged. In either case we call it thermal massive $\\mathrm{SLE}_{16/3}$.","sourceOffset":10325,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"mod:bvp-unique","text":"Equation \\eqref{mod:bvp} has exactly one solution in this class. It satisfies $0\\le h\\le1$. On interior compact sets, $h$ and its first derivatives depend continuously on the marked domain in the Carath\\'eodory topology, when the domains lie in a fixed bounded physical region.","sourceOffset":12167,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:law","text":"A positive-mass interface law in $(\\Omega,a,b)$ is a law of an oriented continuous curve in the prime-end compactification, from $a$ to $b$, with the following properties. In the chart $\\phi$ it is the Loewner trace for the entire evolution \\eqref{mod:loewner}, parametrized by half-plane capacity $2t$, $0\\le t<\\infty$, and completed continuously at $b$ in disc coordinates. Its driving function is a weak solution of \\begin{equation} \\dd W_t=\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{3}}\\,\\dd B_t +\\frac{2\\pi}{3}C_t\\,\\dd t, \\qquad W_0=0, \\end{equation} where $B$ is Brownian motion in a filtration to which the driver is ada","sourceOffset":16411,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":172556,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Thermal-FK-Ising-interfaces-and-massive-SLE-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"223-05","slug":"Natural-Occupation-Measures-for-Critical-Square-Lattice-FK-Interfaces-October-5-2026","familyId":"223","title":"Natural Occupation Measures for Critical Square-Lattice FK Interfaces","abstract":"We prove that the rescaled counting measure of a critical square-lattice Fortuin–Kasteleyn Dobrushin interface in the unit square converges to the Minkowski-content measure of its Schramm–Loewner limit, for every fixed cluster weight $1\\le q\\lt 4$. A single deterministic constant times the predicted power of the mesh gives the normalization. Convergence is joint with the ordered curve and includes the total mass, giving the scaling limit of the interface's total number of steps.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves chordal SLEκ limits for critical square-lattice random-cluster interfaces for $0\\lt q\\le4$, with $\\kappa=4\\pi/\\arccos(-\\sqrt q/2)$: bounded Jordan domains are allowed for q ≥ 1, and smooth Jordan domains for q < 1, under the stated marked-boundary approximations. For $1\\le q\\le4$, complete nested plane loops converge to CLEκ.","pdfPath":"preprints/Natural-Occupation-Measures-for-Critical-Square-Lattice-FK-Interfaces-October-5-2026/natural-occupation-measures-critical-square-lattice-fk-interfaces.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Natural-Occupation-Measures-for-Critical-Square-Lattice-FK-Interfaces-October-5-2026/natural-occupation-measures-critical-square-lattice-fk-interfaces.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Natural-Occupation-Measures-for-Critical-Square-Lattice-FK-Interfaces-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Natural-Occupation-Measures-for-Critical-Square-Lattice-FK-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Natural-Occupation-Measures-for-Critical-Square-Lattice-FK-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Natural-Occupation-Measures-for-Critical-Square-Lattice-FK-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-inputs.tex","preprints/Natural-Occupation-Measures-for-Critical-Square-Lattice-FK-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-radial.tex","preprints/Natural-Occupation-Measures-for-Critical-Square-Lattice-FK-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-spectral.tex","preprints/Natural-Occupation-Measures-for-Critical-Square-Lattice-FK-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-electric.tex","preprints/Natural-Occupation-Measures-for-Critical-Square-Lattice-FK-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-coupling.tex","preprints/Natural-Occupation-Measures-for-Critical-Square-Lattice-FK-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/figures/barrier.tex","preprints/Natural-Occupation-Measures-for-Critical-Square-Lattice-FK-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-calibration.tex","preprints/Natural-Occupation-Measures-for-Critical-Square-Lattice-FK-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/08-occupation.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Natural-Occupation-Measures-for-Critical-Square-Lattice-FK-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the rescaled counting measure of a critical square-lattice Fortuin--Kasteleyn Dobrushin interface in the unit square converges to the Minkowski-content measure of its Schramm--Loewner limit, for every fixed cluster weight $1\\le q<4$. A single deterministic constant times the predicted power of the mesh gives the normalization. Convergence is joint with the ordered curve and includes the total mass, giving the scaling limit of the interface's total number of steps.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":529},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The lattice model and the main result","sourceOffset":1433},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and inputs","sourceOffset":6175},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the normalization is determined","sourceOffset":9672},{"kind":"section","title":"Model conventions and inputs","sourceOffset":12697},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Trace and local FK estimates","sourceOffset":14748},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Plane loops and angular rows","sourceOffset":20479},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The covariance normalization","sourceOffset":24234},{"kind":"section","title":"Radial regeneration and normalized correlations","sourceOffset":25616},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Passage bounds at a circuit and under a tilt","sourceOffset":29181},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Stopping circuits and exponential waiting times","sourceOffset":34356},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coupling states at different levels","sourceOffset":41652}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For each fixed $q\\in[1,4)$ there is a deterministic constant $c(q)\\in(0,\\infty)$ such that, along all positive integers $n$, \\[ \\bigl([\\eta_n],\\nu_{n,c(q)}\\bigr) \\ \\Longrightarrow\\ \\bigl([\\eta],\\mu_\\eta\\bigr). \\] The first coordinate has the ordered curve topology \\eqref{eq:curve-metric}, and the second has the weak topology of finite measures on $\\overline D$. The two limiting coordinates belong to the same SLE sample. In particular, \\[ c(q)n^{-d}N_n\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ \\mu_\\eta(\\overline D) \\] jointly with the curve and all continuous spatial tests of its measure.","sourceOffset":5351,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Radial estimates","label":"prop:radial","text":"Fix $1\\le q<4$, fixed radius-comparison constants, and a compact interval $V\\subset(0,\\infty)$. The following bounds are uniform over the above circuit and plane states and over $v\\in V$. For states $\\gamma,\\gamma'$ of sizes $R,R'\\ge r_0$, \\begin{equation} \\left|\\log\\frac{F(\\gamma;v)}{F(\\gamma';v)}\\right| \\le C\\bigl(1+|\\log(R/R')|\\bigr). \\end{equation} There is $\\delta>0$ such that every $F(\\gamma;ve^w)$ is nonzero for $|w|<\\delta$. Its logarithm, continued from its real value at $w=0$, has absolute value at most $C\\log(2+R)$. Moreover \\begin{equation} \\left|\\log\\frac{F(\\gamma;ve^w)}{F(\\gamma;v)} -\\log\\frac{F(\\gamma';ve^w)}{F(\\gamma';v)}\\right| \\le C|w|\\bigl(1+|\\log(R/R')|\\bigr). \\end{equation} For two states of comparable size $R$, their positively tilted laws can be coupled so that their configurations agree inside a common surrounding primal-open circuit containing $B(u,s)$, except with probability $C(s/R)^c$, for $r_0\\le s\\le R/C$. Conditional on the common circuit, the common continuation has its circuit law tilted by $v$. Finally, let $F_{\\ge h}(\\gamma;v)$ count only the eligible loops with $R_u(\\ell)\\ge h$. For comparable starts of size $R$, \\begin{equation} \\frac{F_{\\ge\\epsilon R}(\\gamma;v)/ F_{\\ge\\epsilon R}(\\gamma';v)} {F(\\gamma;v)/F(\\gamma';v)}=1+o(1) \\end{equation} uniformly as $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$ and $\\epsilon R\\to\\infty$. The same real comparison and regeneration bounds hold with any such lower cutoff on the counted radii.","sourceOffset":27515,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":205218,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Natural-Occupation-Measures-for-Critical-Square-Lattice-FK-Interfaces-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"223-06","slug":"Conformal-Limits-of-Critical-Square-Lattice-Random-Cluster-Interfaces-October-5-2026","familyId":"223","title":"Conformal Limits of Critical Square-Lattice Random-Cluster Interfaces","abstract":"For every fixed $1\\le q\\le4$, critical square-lattice random-cluster Dobrushin interfaces converge to chordal $\\mathop{\\mathrm{SLE}}\\nolimits _{\\kappa(q)}$, where $\\kappa(q)=4\\pi/\\arccos(-\\sqrt q/2)$, and the complete nested plane loop collections converge to whole-plane $\\mathop{\\mathrm{CLE}}\\nolimits _{\\kappa(q)}$. The results hold under uniform marked Jordan boundary approximation and retain loop multiplicities and traversals. At q = 1 we obtain Cardy's formula for square-lattice bond percolation with free boundary edges.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves chordal SLEκ limits for critical square-lattice random-cluster interfaces for $0\\lt q\\le4$, with $\\kappa=4\\pi/\\arccos(-\\sqrt q/2)$: bounded Jordan domains are allowed for q ≥ 1, and smooth Jordan domains for q < 1, under the stated marked-boundary approximations. For $1\\le q\\le4$, complete nested plane loops converge to CLEκ.","pdfPath":"preprints/Conformal-Limits-of-Critical-Square-Lattice-Random-Cluster-Interfaces-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Conformal-Limits-of-Critical-Square-Lattice-Random-Cluster-Interfaces-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Conformal-Limits-of-Critical-Square-Lattice-Random-Cluster-Interfaces-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Conformal-Limits-of-Critical-Square-Lattice-Random-Cluster-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Conformal-Limits-of-Critical-Square-Lattice-Random-Cluster-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Conformal-Limits-of-Critical-Square-Lattice-Random-Cluster-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Conformal-Limits-of-Critical-Square-Lattice-Random-Cluster-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/angular.tex","preprints/Conformal-Limits-of-Critical-Square-Lattice-Random-Cluster-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/observable.tex","preprints/Conformal-Limits-of-Critical-Square-Lattice-Random-Cluster-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/chordal.tex","preprints/Conformal-Limits-of-Critical-Square-Lattice-Random-Cluster-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/peeling.tex","preprints/Conformal-Limits-of-Critical-Square-Lattice-Random-Cluster-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/rotation-repair.tex","preprints/Conformal-Limits-of-Critical-Square-Lattice-Random-Cluster-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/endpoint-field.tex","preprints/Conformal-Limits-of-Critical-Square-Lattice-Random-Cluster-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/sections/endpoint-loops.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Conformal-Limits-of-Critical-Square-Lattice-Random-Cluster-Interfaces-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed $1\\le q\\le4$, critical square-lattice random-cluster Dobrushin interfaces converge to chordal $\\SLE_{\\kappa(q)}$, where $\\kappa(q)=4\\pi/\\arccos(-\\sqrt q/2)$, and the complete nested plane loop collections converge to whole-plane $\\CLE_{\\kappa(q)}$. The results hold under uniform marked Jordan boundary approximation and retain loop multiplicities and traversals. At $q=1$ we obtain Cardy's formula for square-lattice bond percolation with free boundary edges.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction and precise statements","sourceOffset":539},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and its boundary conventions","sourceOffset":1880},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Complete loop collections","sourceOffset":5685},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Square-lattice bond crossings","sourceOffset":8661},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof structure and technical contributions","sourceOffset":10093},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions on constants and limits","sourceOffset":15508},{"kind":"section","title":"Tiles, positive laws, and localization","sourceOffset":16514},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Tiles and boundary caps","sourceOffset":16989},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Crossing estimates and finite plane loops","sourceOffset":21964},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison through a collar","sourceOffset":26057},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Passages and positive local tilts","sourceOffset":32722},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparing slit partition functions","sourceOffset":40400}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Interfaces and all nested loops","label":"intro:main","text":"For every fixed real $1\\le q\\le4$, the following assertions hold. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item For every bounded Jordan domain $D$ with distinct boundary marks $a,b$, and every approximation satisfying \\eqref{intro:marked-jordan}, the Dobrushin strand $\\eta_n$ converges in law, in \\eqref{intro:curve-metric}, to chordal $\\SLE_{\\kappa(q)}$ from $a$ to $b$ in $D$. \\item Under the specified free infinite-volume law, $\\Gamma_\\delta$ converges in law as $\\delta\\downarrow0$ to the nested whole-plane $\\CLE_{\\kappa(q)}$, in the spherical collection topology just defined. The limit is M\\\"obius invariant. \\end{enumerate} Both conclusions hold along every mesh sequence. No uniformity in $q$ is asserted.","sourceOffset":7055,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"The regular-domain field","label":"q4field:regular-domain","text":"Let $V$ be a fixed polygon and let its actual wall approximations be as above. Then \\begin{equation} h_\\delta\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ u+\\sigma\\Phi_V \\qquad\\text{as distributions in }V, \\end{equation} where $u=0$ for a homogeneous wall and $u$ is the harmonic extension of the boundary values $0,1$ for a mixed wall. All joint integrated moments converge. At every tuple of distinct fixed interior positions, the moment kernels converge to those of the same field.","sourceOffset":417219,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":482126,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":34,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Conformal-Limits-of-Critical-Square-Lattice-Random-Cluster-Interfaces-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"224-01","slug":"From-critical-crossings-to-quenched-near-critical-universality-in-Voronoi-percolation-October-5-2026","familyId":"224","title":"From critical crossings to quenched near-critical universality in Voronoi percolation","abstract":"Taking Cardy's crossing formula for critical Poisson–Voronoi percolation as an input, we prove a universal joint limit for the near-critical crossing thresholds of rational polygonal quadrilaterals under the monotone coupling. Conditional on the Poisson tessellation, the threshold law converges in probability over tessellations to the same law as on the triangular lattice. Each model is normalized by its own expected number of color-pivotal sites for a unit-square crossing.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Cardy's formula for annealed critical Poisson–Voronoi crossing probabilities in every bounded Jordan quadrilateral. With each model normalized by its own expected unit-square pivotal count, the conditional joint near-critical crossing-threshold laws for rational polygonal quads converge in environment probability to the triangular-lattice reference law. This establishes quenched near-critical universality for crossing thresholds.","pdfPath":"preprints/From-critical-crossings-to-quenched-near-critical-universality-in-Voronoi-percolation-October-5-2026/critical-crossings-quenched-near-critical-universality-voronoi-percolation.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/From-critical-crossings-to-quenched-near-critical-universality-in-Voronoi-percolation-October-5-2026/critical-crossings-quenched-near-critical-universality-voronoi-percolation.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/From-critical-crossings-to-quenched-near-critical-universality-in-Voronoi-percolation-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/From-critical-crossings-to-quenched-near-critical-universality-in-Voronoi-percolation-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/From-critical-crossings-to-quenched-near-critical-universality-in-Voronoi-percolation-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/From-critical-crossings-to-quenched-near-critical-universality-in-Voronoi-percolation-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-inputs.tex","preprints/From-critical-crossings-to-quenched-near-critical-universality-in-Voronoi-percolation-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-exploration.tex","preprints/From-critical-crossings-to-quenched-near-critical-universality-in-Voronoi-percolation-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-tests.tex","preprints/From-critical-crossings-to-quenched-near-critical-universality-in-Voronoi-percolation-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-expansion.tex","preprints/From-critical-crossings-to-quenched-near-critical-universality-in-Voronoi-percolation-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-sewing.tex","preprints/From-critical-crossings-to-quenched-near-critical-universality-in-Voronoi-percolation-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-identification.tex","preprints/From-critical-crossings-to-quenched-near-critical-universality-in-Voronoi-percolation-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/From-critical-crossings-to-quenched-near-critical-universality-in-Voronoi-percolation-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"Taking Cardy's crossing formula for critical Poisson--Voronoi percolation as an input, we prove a universal joint limit for the near-critical crossing thresholds of rational polygonal quadrilaterals under the monotone coupling. Conditional on the Poisson tessellation, the threshold law converges in probability over tessellations to the same law as on the triangular lattice. Each model is normalized by its own expected number of color-pivotal sites for a unit-square crossing.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":525},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Models, crossings, and thresholds","sourceOffset":4429},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The critical input and the main result","sourceOffset":7662},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and transferable ingredients","sourceOffset":9863},{"kind":"section","title":"Critical estimates and their geometric consequences","sourceOffset":12977},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Arm estimates, planar conventions, and localization","sourceOffset":13600},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Quenched crossing limits and deterministic sequences","sourceOffset":26091},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Color order and bulk estimates at fixed scales","sourceOffset":33971},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Boundary coordinates, trimming, and extension","sourceOffset":40860},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pivotal normalization","sourceOffset":60023},{"kind":"section","title":"Critical explorations and oriented loops","sourceOffset":64808},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exploration on a fixed triangulation","sourceOffset":66256}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Quenched near-critical universality","label":"thm:main","text":"Assume Hypothesis \\ref{hyp:cardy}. The expectations in \\eqref{eq:requested-scales} are finite and strictly positive for every mesh, and both scales tend to zero as their mesh tends to zero. There is a probability measure $\\nu_\\triangle$ on $\\mathcal K$ such that \\begin{equation} \\Law(\\tau_\\delta^\\triangle)\\Longrightarrow\\nu_\\triangle \\qquad(\\delta\\downarrow0). \\end{equation} For the independently normalized Voronoi model, \\begin{equation} d_{\\mathrm{BL}}\\bigl(\\Law(\\tau_\\varepsilon^V\\mid\\eta_\\varepsilon), \\nu_\\triangle\\bigr) \\xrightarrow[\\varepsilon\\downarrow0]{\\PP}0. \\end{equation} The conditional law averages all color marks and no geometry.","sourceOffset":8527,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Quenched scalar Cardy convergence","label":"prop:quenched-cardy","text":"For every fixed polygon quad $Q$, \\[ \\PP^{\\eta_e}(C_Q)\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Cardy}(Q) \\quad\\text{in probability over the Poisson geometry}. \\] The assertion holds simultaneously for every member of any fixed finite collection of polygon quads.","sourceOffset":26509,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"hypothesis","name":"Scalar critical Cardy formula","label":"hyp:cardy","text":"For every fixed bounded Jordan quad $Q=(D;a,b,c,d)$, the annealed probability of $C_Q$ in fair-colored full-plane Poisson--Voronoi percolation converges, as $\\varepsilon\\downarrow0$, to \\begin{equation} F(x)=\\frac{\\displaystyle\\int_0^x [u(1-u)]^{-2/3}\\,du} {\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 [u(1-u)]^{-2/3}\\,du}, \\end{equation} where a conformal map from $D$ to the upper half-plane sends $(a,b,c,d)$ to $(0,x,1,\\infty)$.","sourceOffset":7780,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":243040,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/From-critical-crossings-to-quenched-near-critical-universality-in-Voronoi-percolation-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"224-02","slug":"A-Pivotal-Amplitude-for-Voronoi-Percolation-from-Cardys-Formula-October-5-2026","familyId":"224","title":"A Pivotal Amplitude for Voronoi Percolation from Cardy's Formula","abstract":"Taking Cardy's conformal crossing formula for critical planar Poisson–Voronoi percolation in every bounded Jordan quadrilateral as an input, we prove that the expected number of color-pivotal cells for a unit-square crossing is asymptotic to a positive constant times $\\varepsilon ^{-3/4}$, where the point intensity is $\\varepsilon ^{-2}$. No rate of convergence in Cardy's formula is required.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Cardy's formula for annealed critical Poisson–Voronoi crossing probabilities in every bounded Jordan quadrilateral. With each model normalized by its own expected unit-square pivotal count, the conditional joint near-critical crossing-threshold laws for rational polygonal quads converge in environment probability to the triangular-lattice reference law. This establishes quenched near-critical universality for crossing thresholds.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Pivotal-Amplitude-for-Voronoi-Percolation-from-Cardys-Formula-October-5-2026/voronoi-pivotal-amplitude.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Pivotal-Amplitude-for-Voronoi-Percolation-from-Cardys-Formula-October-5-2026/voronoi-pivotal-amplitude.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Pivotal-Amplitude-for-Voronoi-Percolation-from-Cardys-Formula-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/A-Pivotal-Amplitude-for-Voronoi-Percolation-from-Cardys-Formula-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-Pivotal-Amplitude-for-Voronoi-Percolation-from-Cardys-Formula-October-5-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/A-Pivotal-Amplitude-for-Voronoi-Percolation-from-Cardys-Formula-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-inputs.tex","preprints/A-Pivotal-Amplitude-for-Voronoi-Percolation-from-Cardys-Formula-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-annuli.tex","preprints/A-Pivotal-Amplitude-for-Voronoi-Percolation-from-Cardys-Formula-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-spectrum.tex","preprints/A-Pivotal-Amplitude-for-Voronoi-Percolation-from-Cardys-Formula-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-shape.tex","preprints/A-Pivotal-Amplitude-for-Voronoi-Percolation-from-Cardys-Formula-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-transfer.tex","preprints/A-Pivotal-Amplitude-for-Voronoi-Percolation-from-Cardys-Formula-October-5-2026/build/figures/lobes.tex","preprints/A-Pivotal-Amplitude-for-Voronoi-Percolation-from-Cardys-Formula-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-amplitude.tex","preprints/A-Pivotal-Amplitude-for-Voronoi-Percolation-from-Cardys-Formula-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Pivotal-Amplitude-for-Voronoi-Percolation-from-Cardys-Formula-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"Taking Cardy's conformal crossing formula for critical planar Poisson--Voronoi percolation in every bounded Jordan quadrilateral as an input, we prove that the expected number of color-pivotal cells for a unit-square crossing is asymptotic to a positive constant times $\\eps^{-3/4}$, where the point intensity is $\\eps^{-2}$. No rate of convergence in Cardy's formula is required.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":479},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":2698},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The mechanism of the proof","sourceOffset":5273},{"kind":"section","title":"Critical inputs and normalization","sourceOffset":7981},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Arms, localization, and local changes","sourceOffset":8870},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fixed-scale convergence and exploration domains","sourceOffset":12508},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Calibration and the preliminary exponent","sourceOffset":16628},{"kind":"section","title":"Four-interface transmission in an annulus","sourceOffset":20013},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Connection states and two caps","sourceOffset":20576},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The boundary convergence used below","sourceOffset":24926},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Boundary motion and annular crossing laws","sourceOffset":31238},{"kind":"section","title":"The spectrum of four-interface transmission","sourceOffset":47550}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Under Assumption \\ref{ass:cardy}, there exists a constant $c_V\\in(0,\\infty)$ such that \\[ \\lim_{\\eps\\downarrow0}\\eps^{3/4}\\E N_\\eps=c_V. \\] Consequently, the inverse expected pivotal count is asymptotic to $c_V^{-1}\\eps^{3/4}$.","sourceOffset":2198,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Critical arm and localization inputs","label":"in:arms","text":"There are $g\\in(1,2)$, $\\alpha,\\beta>0$, and constants $C,c>0$ such that, for $b_*\\le r<s<t$, \\begin{align} c(r/s)^g&\\le A(r,s)\\le C(r/s)^{1+\\alpha},\\\\ C^{-1}A(r,s)A(s,t)&\\le A(r,t)\\le C A(r,s)A(s,t). \\end{align} The one-arm probability is at most $C(r/s)^\\beta$. The probability of three alternating arms in a straight half-plane is at most $C(r/s)^2$. For a collar of diameter comparable to $s$, an event determined by the Poisson points in a slightly enlarged collar makes the graph and all vertex stars in the smaller collar local to those points, with mesh smaller than any prescribed fixed fraction of $s$. Its failure probability is at most $C\\exp(-cs^2)$. The widths and constants can depend on that fraction. Such events may be chosen rotation equivariant about the center. Events with disjoint enlarged supports use independent Poisson variables.","sourceOffset":9185,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"assumption","name":"Scalar Cardy limit","label":"ass:cardy","text":"For every bounded Jordan quadrilateral $Q$ with four distinct marked boundary points, the critical crossing probability converges, as $\\eps\\downarrow0$, to Cardy's conformally invariant value.","sourceOffset":1356,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"an:admissible","text":"A sequence of marked graph annuli $D_n$ has admissible boundary convergence if it lies in a fixed bounded set and satisfies the following conditions. \\begin{enumerate} \\item Each boundary is a simple site cycle. With rotations normalized, its uniformization $\\phi_n:\\mathbb A_{p_n}\\longrightarrow D_n$ extends continuously to the closed annulus, where $p_n\\to p\\in(0,\\infty)$. After linear identification in logarithmic radial coordinates, these maps converge uniformly on the closed annulus to $\\phi$. The map $\\phi$ is conformal and one-to-one in $\\mathbb A_p$, and its boundary traversal specifies","sourceOffset":25938,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":157067,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Pivotal-Amplitude-for-Voronoi-Percolation-from-Cardys-Formula-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"224-03","slug":"Cardys-formula-for-critical-Poisson-Voronoi-percolation-September-23-2026","familyId":"224","title":"Cardy’s formula for critical Poisson–Voronoi percolation","abstract":"We prove Cardy's formula for annealed crossing probabilities in critical planar Poisson–Voronoi percolation in every bounded Jordan quadrilateral. This proves the annealed crossing-probability form of the conformal-invariance conjecture for this model.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Cardy's formula for annealed critical Poisson–Voronoi crossing probabilities in every bounded Jordan quadrilateral. With each model normalized by its own expected unit-square pivotal count, the conditional joint near-critical crossing-threshold laws for rational polygonal quads converge in environment probability to the triangular-lattice reference law. This establishes quenched near-critical universality for crossing thresholds.","pdfPath":"preprints/Cardys-formula-for-critical-Poisson-Voronoi-percolation-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Cardys-formula-for-critical-Poisson-Voronoi-percolation-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Cardys-formula-for-critical-Poisson-Voronoi-percolation-September-23-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Cardys-formula-for-critical-Poisson-Voronoi-percolation-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Cardys-formula-for-critical-Poisson-Voronoi-percolation-September-23-2026/build/math-glyph-unicode.tex","preprints/Cardys-formula-for-critical-Poisson-Voronoi-percolation-September-23-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Cardys-formula-for-critical-Poisson-Voronoi-percolation-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-fields.tex","preprints/Cardys-formula-for-critical-Poisson-Voronoi-percolation-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-probability.tex","preprints/Cardys-formula-for-critical-Poisson-Voronoi-percolation-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-plugs.tex","preprints/Cardys-formula-for-critical-Poisson-Voronoi-percolation-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-affine.tex","preprints/Cardys-formula-for-critical-Poisson-Voronoi-percolation-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-pins.tex","preprints/Cardys-formula-for-critical-Poisson-Voronoi-percolation-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-actual-arcs.tex","preprints/Cardys-formula-for-critical-Poisson-Voronoi-percolation-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-nondegeneracy.tex","preprints/Cardys-formula-for-critical-Poisson-Voronoi-percolation-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-observable.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Cardys-formula-for-critical-Poisson-Voronoi-percolation-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Cardy's formula for annealed crossing probabilities in critical planar Poisson--Voronoi percolation in every bounded Jordan quadrilateral. This proves the annealed crossing-probability form of the conformal-invariance conjecture for this model.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":316},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The crossing law","sourceOffset":910},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work","sourceOffset":3603},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From circuit identities to the crossing law","sourceOffset":6642},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A quenched consequence for rectangles","sourceOffset":11540},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Geometry, bulk patches and notation","sourceOffset":13909},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reading the proof and the order of limits","sourceOffset":17108},{"kind":"section","title":"Circuit components and orthogonal fields","sourceOffset":18366},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An exact identity on a triangulated sphere","sourceOffset":18871},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two displacement fields","sourceOffset":27340},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rims and their extremality","sourceOffset":29757},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Infinite volume and closed sums","sourceOffset":36011}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Annealed Voronoi crossing law","label":"thm:cardy","text":"For every bounded Jordan domain $D$ and every four distinct counterclockwise boundary marks $a,b,c,d$, one has \\begin{equation} \\lim_{\\eps\\downarrow0}\\PP\\bigl(\\mathcal C_\\eps(D;a,b,c,d)\\bigr) =F(x) :=\\frac{\\displaystyle\\int_0^x[u(1-u)]^{-2/3}\\,\\dd u} {\\displaystyle\\int_0^1[u(1-u)]^{-2/3}\\,\\dd u} =\\frac{\\Gamma(2/3)x^{1/3}} {\\Gamma(1/3)\\Gamma(4/3)} {}_2F_1\\!\\left(\\frac13,\\frac23;\\frac43;x\\right). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":2283,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Quenched Cardy limit for fixed rectangles","label":"cor:quenched-rectangles","text":"Let $D$ be a fixed bounded nondegenerate open rectangle and let $a,b,c,d$ be its corners in counterclockwise order, with $ab$ and $cd$ the chosen opposite sides. Write $x_D$ for its conformal parameter above and set \\[ Q_\\eps(\\eta)=\\Peta\\bigl(\\mathcal C_\\eps(D;a,b,c,d)\\bigr), \\qquad \\eta=X_\\eps. \\] If $\\PP_{\\mathrm{geom},\\eps}$ and $\\EE_{\\mathrm{geom},\\eps}$ denote probability and expectation for the Poisson geometry at mesh $\\eps$, then \\[ \\lim_{\\eps\\downarrow0} \\EE_{\\mathrm{geom},\\eps}\\bigl[|Q_\\eps-F(x_D)|^2\\bigr]=0. \\] In particular, for every $t>0$, $\\PP_{\\mathrm{geom},\\eps}(|Q_\\eps-F(x_D)|>t)\\to0$. These limits use the geometry law at each mesh, without a coupling across meshes.","sourceOffset":11592,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Bulk setting","label":"def:bulk-setting","text":"A bulk setting is either the full-plane sample or a finite simple triangulation of the sphere carrying independent fair site colors conditional on its graph and geometry. In the latter case an oriented open planar patch is coupled to a full-plane sample so that, on every fixed compact subset of the patch, the graphs and their colors agree with probability tending to one as $\\eps\\downarrow0$. The graph and chart agreement event is independent of color sampling conditional on the geometry. Spatial weights are used only on triangles in the agreement patch. Compactly supported tests may be set to ","sourceOffset":15346,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"A plug type","label":"def:plug-type","text":"A type consists of the following deterministic data: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item two concentric gate annuli $[a_1,b_1]$, $[a_2,b_2]$ and a cutoff radius $m$, with \\[ 10<a_1<b_1<m/10,\\qquad 10m<a_2<b_2; \\] \\item closed Jordan disks $O\\subset\\operatorname{int}H$, with $\\overline B(0,b_2)\\subset\\operatorname{int}O$, and an orientation-preserving band chart \\[ \\Phi:[-12,12]\\times[-3,3]\\longrightarrow\\R^2. \\] Writing its coordinates as $(u,v)$, membership in $O$ and $H$ within the chart is exactly $|u|\\le3$ and $|u|\\le8$, respectively; membership in the interiors is given by t","sourceOffset":71647,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":267134,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":15,"definitionCount":6,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Cardys-formula-for-critical-Poisson-Voronoi-percolation-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Cardys-formula-for-critical-Poisson-Voronoi-percolation-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"225-01","slug":"The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-the-balanced-six-vertex-model-with-variance-multiplier-1-over-arcsin-c-over-2-September-23-2026","familyId":"225","title":"The Gaussian free field limit of the balanced six-vertex model with variance multiplier 1/arcsin(c/2)","abstract":"We prove that the height function of the square-lattice six-vertex model with weights $a=b=1$ and $0\\lt c\\le2$, in the plane state obtained from balanced tori, converges to a multiple of the Gaussian free field. For unit height jumps and Green kernel $-(2\\pi)^{-1}\\log|x-y|$, the squared multiplier is $1/\\arcsin(c/2)$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"The balanced square-lattice six-vertex height field with $a=b=1$ and $0\\lt c\\le2$ converges to a Gaussian free field, including at the endpoint c = 2. The plane state is defined by balanced-torus limits. For unit height increments and Green kernel $-(2\\pi)^{-1}\\log|x-y|$, the exact variance multiplier is $1/\\arcsin(c/2)$.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-the-balanced-six-vertex-model-with-variance-multiplier-1-over-arcsin-c-over-2-September-23-2026/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-the-balanced-six-vertex-model-with-variance-multiplier-1-over-arcsin-c-over-2-September-23-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-the-balanced-six-vertex-model-with-variance-multiplier-1-over-arcsin-c-over-2-September-23-2026/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-the-balanced-six-vertex-model-with-variance-multiplier-1-over-arcsin-c-over-2-September-23-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-the-balanced-six-vertex-model-with-variance-multiplier-1-over-arcsin-c-over-2-September-23-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-the-balanced-six-vertex-model-with-variance-multiplier-1-over-arcsin-c-over-2-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-the-balanced-six-vertex-model-with-variance-multiplier-1-over-arcsin-c-over-2-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-the-balanced-six-vertex-model-with-variance-multiplier-1-over-arcsin-c-over-2-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-the-balanced-six-vertex-model-with-variance-multiplier-1-over-arcsin-c-over-2-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-finite-rows.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-the-balanced-six-vertex-model-with-variance-multiplier-1-over-arcsin-c-over-2-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-thermodynamic-kernels.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-the-balanced-six-vertex-model-with-variance-multiplier-1-over-arcsin-c-over-2-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-angular-plane.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-the-balanced-six-vertex-model-with-variance-multiplier-1-over-arcsin-c-over-2-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-normalization.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-the-balanced-six-vertex-model-with-variance-multiplier-1-over-arcsin-c-over-2-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-chiral-gaussianity.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-the-balanced-six-vertex-model-with-variance-multiplier-1-over-arcsin-c-over-2-September-23-2026/build/figures/chiral-windows.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-the-balanced-six-vertex-model-with-variance-multiplier-1-over-arcsin-c-over-2-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-probabilistic-endgame.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-the-balanced-six-vertex-model-with-variance-multiplier-1-over-arcsin-c-over-2-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-main-proof.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-the-balanced-six-vertex-model-with-variance-multiplier-1-over-arcsin-c-over-2-September-23-2026/build/sections/09-endpoint-rotation.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-the-balanced-six-vertex-model-with-variance-multiplier-1-over-arcsin-c-over-2-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the height function of the square-lattice six-vertex model with weights \\(a=b=1\\) and \\(0<c\\le2\\), in the plane state obtained from balanced tori, converges to a multiple of the Gaussian free field. For unit height jumps and Green kernel \\(-(2\\pi)^{-1}\\log|x-y|\\), the squared multiplier is \\(1/\\arcsin(c/2)\\).","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":387},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The plane law and the height convention","sourceOffset":782},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work and the new ingredients","sourceOffset":6552},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":11948},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite rows and their scalar products","sourceOffset":14064},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The physical row and the standard monodromy","sourceOffset":14530},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bethe vectors, counting functions, and finite regularity","sourceOffset":20067},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact actions on products of creation entries","sourceOffset":26472},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Slavnov's identity with its normalization factors","sourceOffset":30202},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Hilbert duality and the finite-word formula","sourceOffset":36280},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two-twist overlaps","sourceOffset":44269},{"kind":"section","title":"Thermodynamic row kernels","sourceOffset":46314}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed \\(c\\in(0,2]\\), the prescribed iterated balanced-torus limit \\(\\P_c\\) exists. Define \\begin{equation} \\Delta=1-\\frac{c^2}{2},\\qquad \\sigma(c)^2=\\frac2{\\arccos\\Delta}=\\frac1{\\arcsin(c/2)}. \\end{equation} As \\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\) through arbitrary positive real values, the fields \\(h_\\delta\\) converge to \\(\\sigma(c)\\Gamma\\) in each of the following senses. \\begin{enumerate} \\item For every finite family \\(\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_m\\) of compactly supported finite signed measures of total mass zero and finite energy, \\[ \\bigl(\\langle h_\\delta,\\mu_j\\rangle\\bigr)_{j=1}^m \\ \\Longrightarrow\\ \\mathcal N\\!\\left(0, \\bigl(\\sigma(c)^2\\mathcal E(\\mu_i,\\mu_j)\\bigr)_{i,j=1}^m\\right). \\] \\item For every bounded open \\(U\\subset\\R^2\\) and \\(-1<\\alpha<0\\), there is convergence in law, with tightness, in the ordinary restriction spaces \\[ \\mathcal C^\\alpha_{\\mathrm{res}}(U):=B^\\alpha_{\\infty,\\infty}(U), \\qquad B^\\alpha_{p,q}(U),\\qquad W^{\\alpha,p}(U), \\quad 1\\le p<\\infty,\\quad 1\\le q\\le\\infty, \\] modulo constants. Convergence also holds in the outward completion \\eqref{eq:source-holder} defined below. No regularity of \\(\\partial U\\) is required. One may fix a smooth compactly supported \\(\\rho\\) of integral one and replace each field \\(X\\) by \\(X-\\langle X,\\rho\\rangle\\), using the same pinning for the limit. \\item For every \\(k\\ge1\\) and every tuple \\((u_i,u_i')_{i=1}^k\\) with \\(\\{u_i,u_i'\\}\\cap\\{u_j,u_j'\\}=\\varnothing\\) for \\(i\\ne j\\), \\begin{equation} \\E_c\\prod_{i=1}^k \\bigl(h_\\delta(u_i","sourceOffset":2813,"truncated":true},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Exact finite-word expansion","label":"cor:finite-word-expansion","text":"Expand each auxiliary mark as \\[ T^{D_j}=\\sum_{\\alpha,\\beta}(D_j)_{\\beta\\alpha} M_{{\\rm ph},\\alpha\\beta}. \\] For each resulting entry word, let $n_j$ be the number of minus spins after its $j$th action, with $n_0=n$. Entry words with $n_m\\ne n$ have zero expectation in $\\Omega_Y$. For a path of a word with $n_m=n$, let $H\\subset Y$ be the roots absent at the end and let $V$ be its surviving added arguments, ordered in the same replacement slots. Then $|H|=|V|=:k\\le m$, and its contribution to the normalized physical expectation is \\begin{equation} \\left(\\prod_{j=1}^m(D_j)_{\\beta_j\\alpha_j}\\right) (-1)^{\\sum_{j=1}^m(n_{j-1}-n)} (-s_\\lambda)^{-k} \\frac{\\prod_{j=1}^m\\gamma_j} {\\prod_{j=1}^m(1+q_Y(v'_j))} \\frac{\\det(W_{v'}(p))_{H,V}} {\\det(1/[v'-p])_{H,V}} \\prod_{p\\in H}\\ell_p. \\end{equation} The expectation is the sum of these contributions. In particular, the determinant size is bounded by the word length. For diagonal entry words the parity sign in \\eqref{eq:exact-path-determinant} is one. Among distinct removed original roots the correction product contains only reciprocal $f$ factors, with one for each pair. Each removed original root also contributes one factor $\\beta$ joining it to the row argument at the step that removes it.","sourceOffset":40065,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":327622,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":19,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-the-balanced-six-vertex-model-with-variance-multiplier-1-over-arcsin-c-over-2-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"226-01","slug":"The-curve-scaling-limit-of-half-plane-double-dimers-September-23-2026","familyId":"226","title":"The curve scaling limit of half-plane double dimers","abstract":"We prove that the complete double-dimer loop ensemble for the Temperleyan square lattice in the upper half-plane converges to nested CLE4. The convergence holds along the full mesh limit and matches every macroscopic loop as an unparametrized curve. This resolves the half-plane Temperleyan form of the double-dimer CLE4 scaling-limit conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the half-plane Temperleyan form of the double-dimer scaling-limit conjecture: the complete loop ensemble formed by two independent dimer coverings of the Temperleyan square lattice converges to nested CLE4. Convergence matches every macroscopic loop as an unparametrized curve, upgrading convergence of loop observables to convergence of the loops themselves.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-curve-scaling-limit-of-half-plane-double-dimers-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-curve-scaling-limit-of-half-plane-double-dimers-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-curve-scaling-limit-of-half-plane-double-dimers-September-23-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-curve-scaling-limit-of-half-plane-double-dimers-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-curve-scaling-limit-of-half-plane-double-dimers-September-23-2026/build/sections/10-introduction.tex","preprints/The-curve-scaling-limit-of-half-plane-double-dimers-September-23-2026/build/sections/20-transfer.tex","preprints/The-curve-scaling-limit-of-half-plane-double-dimers-September-23-2026/build/sections/30-short-segment.tex","preprints/The-curve-scaling-limit-of-half-plane-double-dimers-September-23-2026/build/figures/two-particle-insertion.tex","preprints/The-curve-scaling-limit-of-half-plane-double-dimers-September-23-2026/build/sections/40-localization.tex","preprints/The-curve-scaling-limit-of-half-plane-double-dimers-September-23-2026/build/sections/50-identification.tex","preprints/The-curve-scaling-limit-of-half-plane-double-dimers-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-curve-scaling-limit-of-half-plane-double-dimers-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the complete double-dimer loop ensemble for the Temperleyan square lattice in the upper half-plane converges to nested \\(\\CLE_4\\). The convergence holds along the full mesh limit and matches every macroscopic loop as an unparametrized curve. This resolves the half-plane Temperleyan form of the double-dimer \\(\\CLE_4\\) scaling-limit conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":405},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The law and the topology","sourceOffset":1028},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the remaining geometric question","sourceOffset":5145},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The geometric estimates and the proof","sourceOffset":7851},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Established inputs","sourceOffset":11185},{"kind":"section","title":"Transfer through a rectangle and the number of crossings","sourceOffset":14847},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rectangles and traversals","sourceOffset":15418},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Particles and column transfer","sourceOffset":18247},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Singular modes and the cost of changing particle number","sourceOffset":20987},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The boundary caps","sourceOffset":25172},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cap diagrams and modified endpoint signs","sourceOffset":28514},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof of traversal tightness","sourceOffset":34035}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For the half-plane Temperleyan law specified above, the family \\(F(\\mathcal L_\\delta)\\) is tight as \\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\) in the loop-collection topology just defined, and \\[ F(\\mathcal L_\\delta)\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ F(\\mathcal L), \\qquad \\delta\\downarrow0, \\] where \\(\\mathcal L\\) is standard nested \\(\\CLE_4\\) in \\(\\HH\\). The convergence holds along the full mesh limit and matches all macroscopic loops as curves.","sourceOffset":4682,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Cylindrical convergence {\\cite[Theorem 4.2(2)","label":"thm:cylindrical","text":"{BI}}] Let \\(z_1,\\ldots,z_r\\) be distinct interior points of \\(\\HH\\), and choose face punctures \\(z_j^\\delta\\to z_j\\). For the two independent matchings used in Theorem \\ref{thm:main}, the reduced lamination relative to \\((z_j^\\delta)_{j=1}^r\\) converges in law to the corresponding reduced lamination of nested \\(\\CLE_4\\). In particular the multisets of enclosed puncture subsets, with multiplicities and with subsets of size at most one deleted, converge in law.","sourceOffset":11575,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":126420,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-curve-scaling-limit-of-half-plane-double-dimers-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"227-01","slug":"Universality-of-critical-quench-autocorrelations-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-October-5-2026","familyId":"227","title":"Universality of critical quench autocorrelations in the Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model","abstract":"We prove joint functional convergence of the stationary and quench autocorrelations of zero-field Sherrington–Kirkpatrick heat-bath dynamics at inverse temperature β = 1, with the same random limit for Gaussian and Rademacher couplings. Each site has a rate-one clock, mean spin autocorrelations are multiplied by n1/3, and waiting times and lags are measured in units n2/3. The quench starts from independent fair spins, and convergence is uniform on compact sets of positive waiting times and lags. The quench limit is selected by these initial states and relaxes to the stationary limiting autocorrelation as the waiting time tends to infinity.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For zero-field Gaussian SK heat-bath dynamics with rate-one updates per spin, proves worst-start cutoff on the $\\log n$ scale for fixed $0\\le\\beta\\lt 1$, mixing time $n^{2/3+o(1)}$ at β = 1, and stretched-exponential mixing from a Gibbs-sampled fixed starting configuration for β > 1, in probability over disorder. At criticality, rescaled stationary and quench autocorrelation processes have universal random limits for Gaussian and Rademacher disorder; the quench limit relaxes to the stationary limit.","pdfPath":"preprints/Universality-of-critical-quench-autocorrelations-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-October-5-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Universality-of-critical-quench-autocorrelations-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-October-5-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Universality-of-critical-quench-autocorrelations-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Universality-of-critical-quench-autocorrelations-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Universality-of-critical-quench-autocorrelations-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Universality-of-critical-quench-autocorrelations-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-background.tex","preprints/Universality-of-critical-quench-autocorrelations-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-edge-estimates.tex","preprints/Universality-of-critical-quench-autocorrelations-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-warming.tex","preprints/Universality-of-critical-quench-autocorrelations-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-gaussian-limits.tex","preprints/Universality-of-critical-quench-autocorrelations-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-entrance-selection.tex","preprints/Universality-of-critical-quench-autocorrelations-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-history-cutoffs.tex","preprints/Universality-of-critical-quench-autocorrelations-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/08-clock-scores.tex","preprints/Universality-of-critical-quench-autocorrelations-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/09-replacement.tex","preprints/Universality-of-critical-quench-autocorrelations-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-October-5-2026/build/sections/10-completion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Universality-of-critical-quench-autocorrelations-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove joint functional convergence of the stationary and quench autocorrelations of zero-field Sherrington--Kirkpatrick heat-bath dynamics at inverse temperature $\\beta=1$, with the same random limit for Gaussian and Rademacher couplings. Each site has a rate-one clock, mean spin autocorrelations are multiplied by $n^{1/3}$, and waiting times and lags are measured in units $n^{2/3}$. The quench starts from independent fair spins, and convergence is uniform on compact sets of positive waiting times and lags. The quench limit is selected by these initial states and relaxes to the stationary limiting autocorrelation as the waiting time tends to infinity.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":725},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and the result","sourceOffset":1260},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":7103},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The argument","sourceOffset":10164},{"kind":"section","title":"The stationary edge input","sourceOffset":14768},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite form and spectral coordinates","sourceOffset":15136},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conditioned measure and relaxed energy","sourceOffset":17170},{"kind":"subsection","title":"High-probability estimates and subsequences","sourceOffset":23084},{"kind":"section","title":"Gaussian estimates at the bottom edge scales","sourceOffset":25381},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cap observations and the estimates","sourceOffset":26026},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Spectral counts, saddles, and spherical tails","sourceOffset":37628},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison with the cube","sourceOffset":54930}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Critical quench universality","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a measurable family \\((h_s^g)_{s>0}\\), with \\(h_s^g\\in L^2(\\Pi_g)\\), such that for almost every \\(g\\) the following properties hold. \\begin{enumerate} \\item For every \\(s>0\\), \\(h_s^g\\ge0\\) and \\(\\Pi_g[h_s^g]=1\\). For \\(s,t>0\\), \\[ h_{s+t}^g=S_t^g h_s^g. \\] Moreover, \\[ h_s^g\\Pi_g\\ \\weakto\\ \\delta_0 \\quad\\text{in the product topology on }\\R^\\N \\quad(s\\downarrow0), \\qquad \\|h_s^g-1\\|_{L^1(\\Pi_g)}\\longrightarrow0 \\quad(s\\to\\infty). \\] \\item The series \\begin{equation} B_g(s,t)=\\sum_{a\\ge1} \\int h_s^g(x)\\,x_a\\,(S_t^g x_a)(x)\\,\\dd\\Pi_g(x) \\end{equation} converges locally uniformly on \\((0,\\infty)^2\\) and defines a continuous function. \\item For both choices of \\(D\\), \\[ \\Law_D(A_{n,J},B_{n,J}) \\ \\weakto\\ \\Law_g(A_g,B_g) \\] in \\(C_{\\mathrm{loc}}((0,\\infty)) \\times C_{\\mathrm{loc}}((0,\\infty)^2)\\). The two coupling laws have the same \\(g\\), the same family \\(h^g\\), and the same coefficient \\(c_*\\). \\item For every \\(0<a<b<\\infty\\), \\[ \\sup_{a\\le t\\le b}|B_g(s,t)-A_g(t)| \\longrightarrow0 \\quad\\text{in probability over }g \\quad(s\\to\\infty). \\] \\end{enumerate} The positive-time density limits of the Gaussian dynamics with initial law \\(\\nu_n\\) select this family, as made precise in Theorem \\ref{thm:entrance-selection}. The normalization is exactly \\eqref{eq:finite-A}--\\eqref{eq:finite-B}.","sourceOffset":5112,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Stationary inputs","label":"prop:stationary-inputs","text":"The following hold for the edge law and the Gaussian finite models. \\begin{enumerate} \\item Let \\(b+g_1>0\\), \\(\\ell_a=(g_a+b)^{-1}\\). Almost surely \\(\\sum_a\\ell_a=\\infty\\) and \\(\\sum_a\\ell_a^2<\\infty\\). If \\(Z_a\\) are independent \\(N(0,\\ell_a)\\), the densities \\(q\\) of \\(\\sum_a(Z_a^2-\\ell_a)\\) and \\(q_m\\) of \\(\\sum_{a>m}(Z_a^2-\\ell_a)\\) are continuous, bounded, and strictly positive. The first \\(m\\) coordinates of \\(\\Pi_g\\) have density \\begin{equation} \\frac{q_m\\!\\left(D_g(b)-\\sum_{a\\le m}(x_a^2-\\ell_a)\\right)} {q(D_g(b))} \\prod_{a\\le m} \\frac{e^{-x_a^2/(2\\ell_a)}}{\\sqrt{2\\pi\\ell_a}}. \\end{equation} These consistent densities are measurable in \\(g\\) and independent of \\(b\\). For each fixed \\(0\\le j<\\infty\\), \\(\\Pi_g[|x_a|^j]\\le C_{j,g,b}\\ell_a^{j/2}\\), with a finite constant uniform in \\(a\\). For sufficiently large fixed \\(m\\), the tail beyond \\(m\\) has a density bounded by a finite constant relative to \\(\\bigotimes_{a>m}N(0,\\ell_a)\\). Conditional on that tail, the first \\(m\\) coordinates have the Gaussian angular law on the sphere of squared radius \\[ D_g(b)+\\sum_{a\\le m}\\ell_a-\\sum_{a>m}(x_a^2-\\ell_a). \\] In particular the renormalized constraint holds \\(\\Pi_g\\)-almost surely. These are the statements of \\cite[Proposition 2.3]{FUA}. \\item With \\(b_n=1+(-g_{1,n})_+\\) and \\(b=1+(-g_1)_+\\), the spherical and cube edge marginals converge jointly with the edge points to \\(\\Pi_g\\) on the represented subsequences described below. Cube integrals converge for every continuous cylin","sourceOffset":17511,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Tested path law","label":"hist:tested-law","text":"A checked history is one of the following subprobabilities, with initial probability either $\\mu$ or the uniform cube probability: \\begin{enumerate} \\item[(i)] an entire query path with every survival factor in its legs; \\item[(ii)] the prefix through a specified first failing check $F$, with all preceding survival factors and the factor $1-w_F$ at that check. \\end{enumerate} The check order is the one following \\eqref{hist:kernel-recursion}; the choice of $F$ and all tree shapes is fixed independently of the path and of $e$. In case (ii), the maximal checked intervals of the prefix form a for","sourceOffset":289702,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/227.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":396047,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Universality-of-critical-quench-autocorrelations-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"227-02","slug":"Functional-universality-of-critical-SK-autocorrelations-October-5-2026","familyId":"227","title":"Functional universality of critical SK autocorrelations","abstract":"We prove that the stationary spin autocorrelation of the zero-field Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model at inverse temperature β = 1 has a common functional scaling limit for Gaussian and Rademacher couplings. With rate-one heat-bath clocks at every site, time is scaled by n2/3 and the mean spin autocorrelation is multiplied by n1/3. The functions converge in law uniformly on compact positive-time intervals. Their limiting law is not a point mass: it retains sample-to-sample randomness, and its functions decay to zero at large times.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For zero-field Gaussian SK heat-bath dynamics with rate-one updates per spin, proves worst-start cutoff on the $\\log n$ scale for fixed $0\\le\\beta\\lt 1$, mixing time $n^{2/3+o(1)}$ at β = 1, and stretched-exponential mixing from a Gibbs-sampled fixed starting configuration for β > 1, in probability over disorder. At criticality, rescaled stationary and quench autocorrelation processes have universal random limits for Gaussian and Rademacher disorder; the quench limit relaxes to the stationary limit.","pdfPath":"preprints/Functional-universality-of-critical-SK-autocorrelations-October-5-2026/critical-sk-autocorrelations.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Functional-universality-of-critical-SK-autocorrelations-October-5-2026/critical-sk-autocorrelations.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Functional-universality-of-critical-SK-autocorrelations-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Functional-universality-of-critical-SK-autocorrelations-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Functional-universality-of-critical-SK-autocorrelations-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Functional-universality-of-critical-SK-autocorrelations-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Functional-universality-of-critical-SK-autocorrelations-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-edge-law.tex","preprints/Functional-universality-of-critical-SK-autocorrelations-October-5-2026/build/figures/spectral-cap.tex","preprints/Functional-universality-of-critical-SK-autocorrelations-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-observations.tex","preprints/Functional-universality-of-critical-SK-autocorrelations-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-variance.tex","preprints/Functional-universality-of-critical-SK-autocorrelations-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-gradient.tex","preprints/Functional-universality-of-critical-SK-autocorrelations-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-microscopic.tex","preprints/Functional-universality-of-critical-SK-autocorrelations-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-trace.tex","preprints/Functional-universality-of-critical-SK-autocorrelations-October-5-2026/build/sections/08-comparison.tex","preprints/Functional-universality-of-critical-SK-autocorrelations-October-5-2026/build/sections/09-functional.tex","preprints/Functional-universality-of-critical-SK-autocorrelations-October-5-2026/build/sections/10-support.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Functional-universality-of-critical-SK-autocorrelations-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the stationary spin autocorrelation of the zero-field Sherrington--Kirkpatrick model at inverse temperature $\\beta=1$ has a common functional scaling limit for Gaussian and Rademacher couplings. With rate-one heat-bath clocks at every site, time is scaled by $n^{2/3}$ and the mean spin autocorrelation is multiplied by $n^{1/3}$. The functions converge in law uniformly on compact positive-time intervals. Their limiting law is not a point mass: it retains sample-to-sample randomness, and its functions decay to zero at large times.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":611},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model and result","sourceOffset":1080},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and relation to earlier work","sourceOffset":3869},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its main ingredients","sourceOffset":8397},{"kind":"section","title":"The conditioned edge measure and the Gaussian inputs","sourceOffset":12243},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The heat-bath form and the edge coordinates","sourceOffset":12928},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conditioning the infinite Gaussian family","sourceOffset":19137},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The limiting autocorrelation and its time constant","sourceOffset":27427},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Haar bias and Gaussian cap observations","sourceOffset":28631},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Saddles and cap estimates","sourceOffset":34403},{"kind":"section","title":"Quantitative estimates for the observations","sourceOffset":42984},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Location of the path and coordinate moments","sourceOffset":44732}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Functional universality","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a Borel probability law $Q_{\\mathrm{crit}}$ on $C_{\\mathrm{loc}}((0,\\infty))$ such that, for each of the two choices of $D$, \\[ \\Law_D(A_{n,J})\\Longrightarrow Q_{\\mathrm{crit}} \\qquad(n\\to\\infty). \\] The law $Q_{\\mathrm{crit}}$ is not a point mass. It assigns probability one to nonzero, nonnegative, nonincreasing functions $f$ satisfying $\\lim_{s\\to\\infty}f(s)=0$. The Gaussian and Rademacher models use exactly the normalization in \\eqref{eq:intro-rescaling}.","sourceOffset":2579,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Conditional variance","label":"prop:variance-main","text":"There is a fixed $p_*>0$ with the following property. For every $\\varepsilon>0$ there are finite constants $C$, $\\xi>0$ and disorder events $\\mathcal V_n$ with $\\liminf_n\\mathbb P(\\mathcal V_n)\\ge1-\\varepsilon$ such that, on $\\mathcal V_n$, simultaneously for every real function $f$ on the cube and every $r\\le p\\le p_*$, \\begin{equation} V_p(f):=Q_\\mu[\\Var(f\\mid h_p)] \\le Cp^{-2}\\cE(f)+Ce^{-n^\\xi}\\|f\\|_\\infty^2. \\end{equation} The constants and the disorder event are independent of $f$. The inequality also holds componentwise and may be summed for vector-valued functions.","sourceOffset":67098,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/227.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":229036,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":18,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Functional-universality-of-critical-SK-autocorrelations-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"227-03","slug":"A-spectral-gap-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026","familyId":"227","title":"A spectral gap throughout the high-temperature Sherrington–Kirkpatrick phase","abstract":"For every fixed inverse temperature $0\\lt \\beta\\lt 1$, we prove that the unscaled spectral gap of single-site heat-bath dynamics for the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model is bounded away from zero with probability tending to one over the disorder. Equivalently, the Gibbs law satisfies a dimension-free Poincaré inequality for all functions.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For zero-field Gaussian SK heat-bath dynamics with rate-one updates per spin, proves worst-start cutoff on the $\\log n$ scale for fixed $0\\le\\beta\\lt 1$, mixing time $n^{2/3+o(1)}$ at β = 1, and stretched-exponential mixing from a Gibbs-sampled fixed starting configuration for β > 1, in probability over disorder. At criticality, rescaled stationary and quench autocorrelation processes have universal random limits for Gaussian and Rademacher disorder; the quench limit relaxes to the stationary limit.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-spectral-gap-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-spectral-gap-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-spectral-gap-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/A-spectral-gap-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-spectral-gap-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/style.tex","preprints/A-spectral-gap-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-spectral-gap-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/sections/observation.tex","preprints/A-spectral-gap-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/figures/observation-flow.tex","preprints/A-spectral-gap-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/sections/matrices.tex","preprints/A-spectral-gap-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/sections/entropy.tex","preprints/A-spectral-gap-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/sections/stability.tex","preprints/A-spectral-gap-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/sections/residuals.tex","preprints/A-spectral-gap-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex","preprints/A-spectral-gap-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/sections/terminal.tex","preprints/A-spectral-gap-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-spectral-gap-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed inverse temperature $0<\\beta<1$, we prove that the unscaled spectral gap of single-site heat-bath dynamics for the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington--Kirkpatrick model is bounded away from zero with probability tending to one over the disorder. Equivalently, the Gibbs law satisfies a dimension-free Poincar\\'e inequality for all functions.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The theorem and the structure of the proof","sourceOffset":436},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier results and methods","sourceOffset":3053},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Notation and elementary identities","sourceOffset":7784},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof mechanism","sourceOffset":9463},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The three inputs","sourceOffset":10913},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":13189},{"kind":"section","title":"From posterior estimates to a spectral gap","sourceOffset":13763},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Posterior martingales and Dirichlet forms","sourceOffset":14321},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Stopping before stability fails","sourceOffset":15931},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Changing the law of the observations","sourceOffset":18044},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Completion of the theorem","sourceOffset":23296},{"kind":"section","title":"Uniform random-matrix estimates","sourceOffset":25518}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a finite constant $C_\\beta$, depending only on $\\beta$, such that \\[ \\Prob_J\\left\\{\\Var_{\\mu_0}(f)\\le C_\\beta\\D_0(f) \\text{ for every }f:\\{-1,1\\}^n\\longrightarrow\\R\\right\\} \\longrightarrow1. \\] Consequently, the discrete-time heat-bath chain that chooses a uniform site has spectral gap at least $1/(C_\\beta n)$ with probability tending to one.","sourceOffset":2178,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Stability along observations","label":"prop:stable-fields","text":"Fix $j\\in(0,1)$ and $0<T<\\infty$. There are constants $\\eta,c,\\epsilon_A,\\rho_0,a>0$, depending only on $j,T$, and events $\\mathcal J_n$ for the zero-diagonal Gaussian matrix $J$, such that $\\Prob(J\\in\\mathcal J_n)\\longrightarrow1$ and the following assertion holds for every $J\\in\\mathcal J_n$. Let $X\\sim\\mu_0$, let $B_0$ be an independent standard $n$-dimensional Brownian motion, and put $Y(t)=tX+B_0(t)$. With conditional probability at least $1-e^{-an}$, simultaneously for $0\\le t\\le T$, $y\\in\\R^n$, and all diagonal $A$, \\begin{equation} \\left. \\begin{gathered} \\norm{F_{J,Y(t)}(y)}\\le2\\rho_0\\sqrt n,\\\\ 0\\le A\\le(1+\\eta)I,\\qquad \\norm{A-V_y}_{\\Frob}\\le\\epsilon_A\\sqrt n \\end{gathered} \\right\\} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\mathsf H_J(y,A)\\succ cI. \\end{equation} We may require $j(1+\\eta)^2<1$ and $\\norm J\\le2\\sqrt j+\\theta$ on $\\mathcal J_n$, for any sufficiently small fixed $\\theta>0$ chosen in the proof.","sourceOffset":77055,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Ordinary admissible recipe","label":"def:small-recipe","text":"Choose finitely many deterministic seeds $s\\in\\R^n$ of bounded Euclidean norm. Seeds may depend on $J$ and $h_0$, but are fixed as functions of $x$. Choose a finite tuple of scalar functions $\\theta$ satisfying \\[ \\sup_x\\bigl(|\\theta_\\alpha(x)|+\\norm{d\\theta_\\alpha(x)}\\bigr)\\leq C \\] for each component. Such scalar functions will be called parameters; they may include the averages $b_l$. Given auxiliary fields $y^1,\\ldots,y^{a-1}$ already constructed, a source has the form \\begin{equation} w_i^a=\\sum_{s}\\phi_{a,s,i}\\bigl((h_i^l)_{l\\in I_a},\\theta\\bigr)s_i +\\sum_{b<a}\\phi_{a,b,i}\\bigl((h_i^l)_{","sourceOffset":135508,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/227.md","scopeText":"For every fixed inverse temperature $0<\\beta<1$, the formalization proves a dimension-independent Poincaré inequality for the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model with probability tending to one over the disorder. Equivalently, the unscaled single-site heat-bath spectral gap stays bounded away from zero. For dynamics that choose one site uniformly at each discrete step, the spectral gap is at least $1/(Cn)$ with probability tending to one, for a constant $C$ depending on $\\beta$.\n\nThe formalized supporting result proves ratio cutoff for discrete single-site heat-bath dynamics in the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model for $0\\le\\beta<1/2$. For every $0<\\varepsilon<1/2$ and $\\eta>0$, the probability over the disorder that $t_{\\mathrm{mix}}(\\varepsilon)/t_{\\mathrm{m","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/227.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":210425,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-spectral-gap-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"227-04","slug":"Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026","familyId":"227","title":"Cutoff throughout the high-temperature Sherrington–Kirkpatrick phase","abstract":"We prove worst-case total-variation cutoff for the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington–Kirkpatrick heat-bath dynamics at every fixed inverse temperature $0\\leq\\beta\\lt 1$. With rate-one refresh at each spin, the cutoff location is $\\log n/(2\\lambda(\\beta))$ for a positive deterministic rate $\\lambda(\\beta)$. The location for uniformly chosen single-site update attempts is n times as large. Convergence is in probability over the disorder.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For zero-field Gaussian SK heat-bath dynamics with rate-one updates per spin, proves worst-start cutoff on the $\\log n$ scale for fixed $0\\le\\beta\\lt 1$, mixing time $n^{2/3+o(1)}$ at β = 1, and stretched-exponential mixing from a Gibbs-sampled fixed starting configuration for β > 1, in probability over disorder. At criticality, rescaled stationary and quench autocorrelation processes have universal random limits for Gaussian and Rademacher disorder; the quench limit relaxes to the stationary limit.","pdfPath":"preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-dependencies.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-model-inputs.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-static.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-gradient-path.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-gaussian-calculus.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/figures/network-pinching.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-row-spin.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-hidden.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/figures/marked-continuation.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-exact.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-cutoff.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/appendices/introduction.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/appendices/entropy.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/appendices/gradient.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/appendices/comparison.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/appendices/scalar.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/appendices/dynamics.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/appendices/cutoff.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/appendices/scalar-certificate.tex","preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/appendices/clock-comparison.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove worst-case total-variation cutoff for the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington--Kirkpatrick heat-bath dynamics at every fixed inverse temperature \\(0\\leq\\beta<1\\). With rate-one refresh at each spin, the cutoff location is \\(\\log n/(2\\lambda(\\beta))\\) for a positive deterministic rate \\(\\lambda(\\beta)\\). The location for uniformly chosen single-site update attempts is \\(n\\) times as large. Convergence is in probability over the disorder.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":508},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The result","sourceOffset":3504},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The rate and the gradient mechanism","sourceOffset":5671},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Main ideas","sourceOffset":9243},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and dependencies","sourceOffset":13416},{"kind":"section","title":"The model, normalization, and imported estimates","sourceOffset":16389},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two clocks and the Dirichlet form","sourceOffset":16946},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The spectral-gap input","sourceOffset":20486},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two reusable consequences for finite chains","sourceOffset":22406},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The independent case","sourceOffset":25632},{"kind":"section","title":"Static estimates and Gaussian observation","sourceOffset":26763},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A stable field controls means under an arbitrary law","sourceOffset":28921}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed \\(0\\leq\\beta<1\\), there is a deterministic \\(\\lambda(\\beta)>0\\) such that, with \\[ t_n=\\frac{\\log n}{2\\lambda(\\beta)}, \\] for every \\(\\epsilon\\in(0,1)\\), \\begin{align*} d_n((1-\\epsilon)t_n)&\\longrightarrow1, & d_n((1+\\epsilon)t_n)&\\longrightarrow0,\\\\ d_n^{\\rm disc}(\\lfloor(1-\\epsilon)nt_n\\rfloor)&\\longrightarrow1, & d_n^{\\rm disc}(\\lceil(1+\\epsilon)nt_n\\rceil)&\\longrightarrow0. \\end{align*} All convergences are in probability over the disorder. For \\(\\beta=0\\), \\(\\lambda(0)=1\\). For \\(0<\\beta<1\\), \\(\\lambda(\\beta)\\) is the equilibrium-operator constant defined in \\eqref{eq:exact-lambda}; it satisfies \\[ 0<\\lambda(\\beta)\\leq(1-\\beta)^2. \\]","sourceOffset":4692,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"rcut:thm:main","text":"For every fixed $\\beta\\in[0,1/2)$, $\\varepsilon\\in(0,1/2)$, and $\\eta>0$, let \\[ t_{n,\\beta,J}(\\varepsilon)=\\min\\{k\\ge0:d_d(k)\\le\\varepsilon\\}. \\] Then \\[ \\P_J\\left\\{ \\frac{t_{n,\\beta,J}(\\varepsilon)} {t_{n,\\beta,J}(1-\\varepsilon)}>1+\\eta\\right\\}\\longrightarrow0. \\] The denominator is positive for sufficiently large $n$.","sourceOffset":394703,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Finite tree tests","label":"def:path-tree","text":"A stationary path tree has finitely many edges of fixed finite lengths. Choose a root state with law $\\mu$ and run independent ordinary heat-bath paths outwards along the edges. Reversibility permits an edge to be read in either direction. A nonstationary tree is obtained from a forward path whose initial density $h$ satisfies $\\log\\norm h_\\infty=o(n)$, and by making finitely many conditional copies of portions at a shared single endpoint, using their actual conditional laws. No new branch is conditioned on both of its endpoints. A test has a fixed finite list of times and a fixed polynomial b","sourceOffset":98503,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:exact-spaces","text":"Let $\\mathcal X$ be the closure in $\\mathcal H$ of the linear span of \\[ K_tdf_{B,\\delta},\\qquad t\\ge0,\\quad\\delta>0, \\quad B\\text{ a smooth bounded scaled recipe}. \\] Let $\\mathcal X_v$ be the closure of the same span in $\\mathcal H_v$. Equivalently, countable dense recipe, time, and accuracy sets may be used.","sourceOffset":300674,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/227.md","scopeText":"For every fixed inverse temperature $0<\\beta<1$, the formalization proves a dimension-independent Poincaré inequality for the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model with probability tending to one over the disorder. Equivalently, the unscaled single-site heat-bath spectral gap stays bounded away from zero. For dynamics that choose one site uniformly at each discrete step, the spectral gap is at least $1/(Cn)$ with probability tending to one, for a constant $C$ depending on $\\beta$.\n\nThe formalized supporting result proves ratio cutoff for discrete single-site heat-bath dynamics in the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model for $0\\le\\beta<1/2$. For every $0<\\varepsilon<1/2$ and $\\eta>0$, the probability over the disorder that $t_{\\mathrm{mix}}(\\varepsilon)/t_{\\mathrm{m","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/227.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":555681,"sourceFileCount":23,"statementCount":37,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode.tex","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Cutoff-throughout-the-high-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-phase-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex.tex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"227-05","slug":"Critical-slowing-down-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-24-2026","familyId":"227","title":"Critical slowing down in the Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model","abstract":"At the critical inverse temperature β = 1, we prove slow mixing for zero-field Gaussian Sherrington–Kirkpatrick heat-bath dynamics from typical equilibrium configurations held fixed as initial states. For every deterministic sequence $t_n=o(n^{2/3})$ in rate-one-per-site time, the Gibbs mass of initial states whose time-tn total-variation distance from equilibrium exceeds 1/4 tends to one in probability over the disorder. The same statement holds for every deterministic integer sequence $k_n=o(n^{5/3})$ of uniform-site update attempts.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For zero-field Gaussian SK heat-bath dynamics with rate-one updates per spin, proves worst-start cutoff on the $\\log n$ scale for fixed $0\\le\\beta\\lt 1$, mixing time $n^{2/3+o(1)}$ at β = 1, and stretched-exponential mixing from a Gibbs-sampled fixed starting configuration for β > 1, in probability over disorder. At criticality, rescaled stationary and quench autocorrelation processes have universal random limits for Gaussian and Rademacher disorder; the quench limit relaxes to the stationary limit.","pdfPath":"preprints/Critical-slowing-down-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Critical-slowing-down-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Critical-slowing-down-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Critical-slowing-down-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Critical-slowing-down-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Critical-slowing-down-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-structure.tex","preprints/Critical-slowing-down-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-24-2026/build/sections/model.tex","preprints/Critical-slowing-down-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-24-2026/build/sections/spectral.tex","preprints/Critical-slowing-down-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-24-2026/build/sections/spherical.tex","preprints/Critical-slowing-down-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-24-2026/build/sections/orientation.tex","preprints/Critical-slowing-down-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-24-2026/build/sections/dynamics.tex","preprints/Critical-slowing-down-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-24-2026/build/sections/specification.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Critical-slowing-down-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"At the critical inverse temperature $\\beta=1$, we prove slow mixing for zero-field Gaussian Sherrington--Kirkpatrick heat-bath dynamics from typical equilibrium configurations held fixed as initial states. For every deterministic sequence $t_n=o(n^{2/3})$ in rate-one-per-site time, the Gibbs mass of initial states whose time-$t_n$ total-variation distance from equilibrium exceeds $1/4$ tends to one in probability over the disorder. The same statement holds for every deterministic integer sequence $k_n=o(n^{5/3})$ of uniform-site update attempts.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":597},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and main results","sourceOffset":1044},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical and mathematical context","sourceOffset":5078},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the proof works","sourceOffset":10911},{"kind":"section","title":"Model, clocks, and conventions","sourceOffset":16958},{"kind":"section","title":"Spectral estimates at a slowly enlarged edge scale","sourceOffset":21935},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Tridiagonal reduction and concentration of its entries","sourceOffset":24310},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A simultaneous quadratic-form estimate","sourceOffset":27989},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The deterministic resolvent","sourceOffset":36407},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Eigenvalue comparison and transfer of the traces","sourceOffset":39713},{"kind":"section","title":"Gaussian conditioning on the sphere","sourceOffset":44499},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Radial representation and partition estimates","sourceOffset":45109}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Realized equilibrium initial states","label":"thm:gibbs-start","text":"For every deterministic sequence $t_n\\ge0$ with $t_n=o(n^{2/3})$, \\[ \\mu_W\\{x:d_{t_n}^{\\rm ct}(W,x)>1/4\\} \\xrightarrow{\\Prob}1. \\] For every deterministic integer sequence $k_n\\ge0$ with $k_n=o(n^{5/3})$, \\[ \\mu_W\\{x:d_{k_n}^{\\rm dt}(W,x)>1/4\\} \\xrightarrow{\\Prob}1. \\] The convergence is in probability over the disorder.","sourceOffset":2587,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Covariance and linear tests","label":"thm:linear-scale","text":"For every positive deterministic sequence $M_n\\to\\infty$, with probability tending to one both $\\|\\Sigma\\|$ and $\\mathcal R_{\\rm lin}(\\mu_W)$ belong to \\[ \\left[\\frac{n^{2/3}}{M_n},\\ M_n n^{2/3}\\right]. \\]","sourceOffset":3775,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/227.md","scopeText":"For every fixed inverse temperature $0<\\beta<1$, the formalization proves a dimension-independent Poincaré inequality for the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model with probability tending to one over the disorder. Equivalently, the unscaled single-site heat-bath spectral gap stays bounded away from zero. For dynamics that choose one site uniformly at each discrete step, the spectral gap is at least $1/(Cn)$ with probability tending to one, for a constant $C$ depending on $\\beta$.\n\nThe formalized supporting result proves ratio cutoff for discrete single-site heat-bath dynamics in the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model for $0\\le\\beta<1/2$. For every $0<\\varepsilon<1/2$ and $\\eta>0$, the probability over the disorder that $t_{\\mathrm{mix}}(\\varepsilon)/t_{\\mathrm{m","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/227.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":114638,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Critical-slowing-down-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"227-06","slug":"Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026","familyId":"227","title":"Stretched-exponential barriers for typical SK initial states","abstract":"At every fixed inverse temperature β > 1, we prove a stretched-exponential obstruction to mixing for the zero-field Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model from typical equilibrium configurations held fixed as initial states. The Gibbs mass of states whose total-variation distance from equilibrium at time $\\exp(n^{1/10000})$ exceeds 1/4 tends to one in probability over the disorder. This holds for rate-one-per-site heat-bath dynamics and after the same stated number of discrete update attempts.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For zero-field Gaussian SK heat-bath dynamics with rate-one updates per spin, proves worst-start cutoff on the $\\log n$ scale for fixed $0\\le\\beta\\lt 1$, mixing time $n^{2/3+o(1)}$ at β = 1, and stretched-exponential mixing from a Gibbs-sampled fixed starting configuration for β > 1, in probability over disorder. At criticality, rescaled stationary and quench autocorrelation processes have universal random limits for Gaussian and Rademacher disorder; the quench limit relaxes to the stationary limit.","pdfPath":"preprints/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026/build/math-glyph-unicode.tex","preprints/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-gaussian.tex","preprints/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-scalar.tex","preprints/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-locking.tex","preprints/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-energy.tex","preprints/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-coverage.tex","preprints/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-narrow-locking.tex","preprints/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026/build/figures/narrow-increments.tex","preprints/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-dynamics.tex","preprints/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026/build/figures/protected-block.tex","preprints/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026/build/appendices/polynomial-coverage.tex","preprints/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026/build/appendices/fixed-depth-crossing.tex","preprints/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"At every fixed inverse temperature $\\beta>1$, we prove a stretched-exponential obstruction to mixing for the zero-field Sherrington--Kirkpatrick model from typical equilibrium configurations held fixed as initial states. The Gibbs mass of states whose total-variation distance from equilibrium at time $\\exp(n^{1/10000})$ exceeds $1/4$ tends to one in probability over the disorder. This holds for rate-one-per-site heat-bath dynamics and after the same stated number of discrete update attempts.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":559},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and relation to earlier work","sourceOffset":5160},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":9447},{"kind":"section","title":"Gaussian hierarchies and quantitative free energy comparison","sourceOffset":15112},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two Gaussian tools and a hierarchy identity","sourceOffset":17021},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A matrix interpolation bound","sourceOffset":25171},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The scalar functional","sourceOffset":29382},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Concentration along a hierarchy","sourceOffset":32356},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Adaptive interpolation and the finite-size error","sourceOffset":42974},{"kind":"section","title":"Scalar regularity and properties of a minimizer","sourceOffset":50260},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Regularity and first variations","sourceOffset":52269},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Minimizers and the energy gap","sourceOffset":67266}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Set $\\kap=1/10000$. For every fixed $\\beta>1$, \\begin{align*} \\PP_{J,\\,x_0\\sim\\pi^J} \\left\\{d^J\\bigl(x_0,\\exp(n^\\kap)\\bigr)>\\frac14\\right\\} &\\longrightarrow1,\\\\ \\PP_{J,\\,x_0\\sim\\pi^J} \\left\\{d_{\\mathrm{disc}}^J \\bigl(x_0,\\lfloor\\exp(n^\\kap)\\rfloor\\bigr)>\\frac14\\right\\} &\\longrightarrow1. \\end{align*}","sourceOffset":3025,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:superpolynomial","text":"For every fixed $\\beta>1$ and every fixed $a>0$, \\[ \\PP_{J,\\,x_0\\sim\\pi^J}\\{T^J(x_0)>n^a\\}\\longrightarrow1, \\qquad \\PP_{J,\\,x_0\\sim\\pi^J} \\{T_{\\mathrm{disc}}^J(x_0)>n^a\\}\\longrightarrow1. \\] Equivalently, each corresponding Gibbs mass tends to one in probability over the disorder.","sourceOffset":4573,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/227.md","scopeText":"For every fixed inverse temperature $0<\\beta<1$, the formalization proves a dimension-independent Poincaré inequality for the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model with probability tending to one over the disorder. Equivalently, the unscaled single-site heat-bath spectral gap stays bounded away from zero. For dynamics that choose one site uniformly at each discrete step, the spectral gap is at least $1/(Cn)$ with probability tending to one, for a constant $C$ depending on $\\beta$.\n\nThe formalized supporting result proves ratio cutoff for discrete single-site heat-bath dynamics in the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model for $0\\le\\beta<1/2$. For every $0<\\varepsilon<1/2$ and $\\eta>0$, the probability over the disorder that $t_{\\mathrm{mix}}(\\varepsilon)/t_{\\mathrm{m","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/227.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":210739,"sourceFileCount":16,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Stretched-exponential-barriers-for-typical-SK-initial-states-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"227-07","slug":"A-typical-start-upper-bound-for-low-temperature-SK-Glauber-dynamics-September-24-2026","familyId":"227","title":"A typical-start upper bound for low-temperature SK Glauber dynamics","abstract":"For every fixed inverse temperature β > 1, we prove subexponential mixing for rate-one-per-site heat-bath dynamics in the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model from a typical equilibrium configuration. If one Gibbs-sampled configuration is held fixed as the initial state, its total-variation distance from equilibrium is at most 1/4 by time $\\exp(n^{1-1/40000000})$, with joint probability tending to one over the disorder and the sampled state.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For zero-field Gaussian SK heat-bath dynamics with rate-one updates per spin, proves worst-start cutoff on the $\\log n$ scale for fixed $0\\le\\beta\\lt 1$, mixing time $n^{2/3+o(1)}$ at β = 1, and stretched-exponential mixing from a Gibbs-sampled fixed starting configuration for β > 1, in probability over disorder. At criticality, rescaled stationary and quench autocorrelation processes have universal random limits for Gaussian and Rademacher disorder; the quench limit relaxes to the stationary limit.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-typical-start-upper-bound-for-low-temperature-SK-Glauber-dynamics-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-typical-start-upper-bound-for-low-temperature-SK-Glauber-dynamics-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-typical-start-upper-bound-for-low-temperature-SK-Glauber-dynamics-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/A-typical-start-upper-bound-for-low-temperature-SK-Glauber-dynamics-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-typical-start-upper-bound-for-low-temperature-SK-Glauber-dynamics-September-24-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/A-typical-start-upper-bound-for-low-temperature-SK-Glauber-dynamics-September-24-2026/build/sections/setup.tex","preprints/A-typical-start-upper-bound-for-low-temperature-SK-Glauber-dynamics-September-24-2026/build/sections/scalar.tex","preprints/A-typical-start-upper-bound-for-low-temperature-SK-Glauber-dynamics-September-24-2026/build/sections/proposal.tex","preprints/A-typical-start-upper-bound-for-low-temperature-SK-Glauber-dynamics-September-24-2026/build/sections/reverse.tex","preprints/A-typical-start-upper-bound-for-low-temperature-SK-Glauber-dynamics-September-24-2026/build/sections/connections.tex","preprints/A-typical-start-upper-bound-for-low-temperature-SK-Glauber-dynamics-September-24-2026/build/connection-figure.tex","preprints/A-typical-start-upper-bound-for-low-temperature-SK-Glauber-dynamics-September-24-2026/build/sections/mixing.tex","preprints/A-typical-start-upper-bound-for-low-temperature-SK-Glauber-dynamics-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-typical-start-upper-bound-for-low-temperature-SK-Glauber-dynamics-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed inverse temperature $\\beta>1$, we prove subexponential mixing for rate-one-per-site heat-bath dynamics in the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington--Kirkpatrick model from a typical equilibrium configuration. If one Gibbs-sampled configuration is held fixed as the initial state, its total-variation distance from equilibrium is at most $1/4$ by time $\\exp(n^{1-1/40000000})$, with joint probability tending to one over the disorder and the sampled state.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":511},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model and main theorem","sourceOffset":1584},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier mixing results and proof ingredients","sourceOffset":4818},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the proof reaches a typical fixed state","sourceOffset":8318},{"kind":"section","title":"Notation and Gaussian completion","sourceOffset":10985},{"kind":"section","title":"Scalar inputs and the entropy--energy identity","sourceOffset":12869},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact equilibrium inputs and normalization","sourceOffset":13323},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Tail estimates for the extended diffusion","sourceOffset":17578},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Balancing scalar entropy and energy","sourceOffset":20888},{"kind":"section","title":"A Gaussian-query proposal","sourceOffset":23136},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Construction and conditional Gaussian laws","sourceOffset":23582},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Entropy and likelihood estimates","sourceOffset":27580}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Mixing from a typical Gibbs state","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed $\\beta>1$, there is $c_\\beta\\in(0,1)$ such that \\[ \\mathbb P_{J,\\,\\sigma_0\\sim\\pi_{n,\\beta}^J} \\left\\{T_{n,\\beta}^J(\\sigma_0) \\le\\exp\\bigl(n^{1-c_\\beta}\\bigr)\\right\\} \\longrightarrow1 \\qquad\\text{as }n\\longrightarrow\\infty. \\] The exponent is independent of $n$ and of the realized disorder. The proof permits $c_\\beta=1/40000000$ for every fixed $\\beta>1$.","sourceOffset":2684,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Two-sided typical-start bounds","label":"cor:typical-two-sided","text":"For every fixed $\\beta>1$, in the model and continuous-time convention above, \\[ \\mathbb P_{J,\\,\\sigma_0\\sim\\pi_{n,\\beta}^J} \\left\\{ \\begin{aligned} \\exp\\bigl(n^{1/10000}\\bigr)&<T_{n,\\beta}^J(\\sigma_0)\\\\ &\\le\\exp\\bigl(n^{1-1/40000000}\\bigr) \\end{aligned} \\right\\}\\longrightarrow1 \\qquad\\text{as }n\\longrightarrow\\infty. \\] The Gibbs state $\\sigma_0$ is held fixed before the total variation in \\eqref{eq:intro-state-mixing-time} is evaluated.","sourceOffset":3695,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/227.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":75033,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-typical-start-upper-bound-for-low-temperature-SK-Glauber-dynamics-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"227-08","slug":"Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026","familyId":"227","title":"Critical mixing in the Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model","abstract":"At the critical inverse temperature β = 1, we determine the worst-start total-variation mixing exponent for single-site heat-bath dynamics in the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model. The mixing time is $n^{2/3+o(1)}$ when every site has rate one, or $n^{5/3+o(1)}$ attempted uniform-site updates, in probability over the disorder.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For zero-field Gaussian SK heat-bath dynamics with rate-one updates per spin, proves worst-start cutoff on the $\\log n$ scale for fixed $0\\le\\beta\\lt 1$, mixing time $n^{2/3+o(1)}$ at β = 1, and stretched-exponential mixing from a Gibbs-sampled fixed starting configuration for β > 1, in probability over disorder. At criticality, rescaled stationary and quench autocorrelation processes have universal random limits for Gaussian and Rademacher disorder; the quench limit relaxes to the stationary limit.","pdfPath":"preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/model.tex","preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/accepted-inputs.tex","preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/proof-overview.tex","preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/profile-spectral.tex","preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/profile-roots.tex","preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/profile-matrices.tex","preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/profile-orbits.tex","preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/profile-local.tex","preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/profile-amplification.tex","preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/profile-positive.tex","preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/profile-entropy.tex","preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/cap-static.tex","preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/cap-multiscale.tex","preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/cap-classical.tex","preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/cap-initialization.tex","preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/cap-entropy.tex","preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/sections/dynamics.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"At the critical inverse temperature $\\beta=1$, we determine the worst-start total-variation mixing exponent for single-site heat-bath dynamics in the zero-field Gaussian Sherrington--Kirkpatrick model. The mixing time is $n^{2/3+o(1)}$ when every site has rate one, or $n^{5/3+o(1)}$ attempted uniform-site updates, in probability over the disorder.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":412},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and Gaussian observations","sourceOffset":12792},{"kind":"section","title":"Companion inputs and the critical endpoint","sourceOffset":17010},{"kind":"section","title":"Two upper-bound mechanisms","sourceOffset":22214},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Isotropic observations: from covariance to a support profile","sourceOffset":23434},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Spectral caps: from a radial deficit to log-Sobolev","sourceOffset":29757},{"kind":"section","title":"Spectral and spherical estimates for the support-profile argument","sourceOffset":35840},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and the observation law","sourceOffset":36391},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Resolvents at the upper edge","sourceOffset":39403},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conditioning a Gaussian on the sphere","sourceOffset":51546},{"kind":"section","title":"Comparison on unrestricted rotation orbits","sourceOffset":64616},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Replica integrals and their tails","sourceOffset":67159}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Critical mixing and functional inequalities","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed $\\varepsilon\\in(0,1/2)$, in probability over the Gaussian disorder, \\begin{align*} t_{\\mathrm{mix},J}(\\varepsilon)&=n^{2/3+o(1)},& k_{\\mathrm{mix},J}(\\varepsilon)&=n^{5/3+o(1)},\\\\ R_J&=n^{2/3+o(1)},& C_{\\mathrm{LS},J}&=n^{2/3+o(1)}. \\end{align*} In each expression, $X_n=n^{a+o(1)}$ means that for every fixed $\\delta>0$, $\\Pprob\\{n^{a-\\delta}\\le X_n\\le n^{a+\\delta}\\}\\to1$.","sourceOffset":2969,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Realized equilibrium starts and linear observables","label":"thm:accepted-critical","text":"For every deterministic sequence $t_n\\ge0$ with $t_n=o(n^{2/3})$, \\[ \\mu_J\\!\\left\\{x: \\norm{e^{t_n\\mathcal L_J}(x,\\cdot)-\\mu_J}_{\\TV}>\\frac14\\right\\} \\longrightarrow1 \\] in probability over disorder. For every deterministic nonnegative integer sequence $k_n=o(n^{5/3})$, the same assertion holds with $P_J^{k_n}$. Let $\\Sigma_J=\\Cov_{\\mu_J}(X)$ and \\[ R_{\\mathrm{lin},J}=\\sup_{a\\in\\R^n\\setminus\\{0\\}} \\frac{\\Var_{\\mu_J}(a^{\\mathsf T}X)}{\\D_J(a^{\\mathsf T}X)}. \\] For every positive deterministic $M_n\\to\\infty$, with probability tending to one both $\\norm{\\Sigma_J}$ and $R_{\\mathrm{lin},J}$ belong to $[n^{2/3}/M_n,M_n n^{2/3}]$.","sourceOffset":4589,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/227.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":691776,"sourceFileCount":20,"statementCount":25,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Critical-mixing-in-the-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-model-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"228-01","slug":"A-continuum-temperature-singularity-for-a-radial-pair-potential-September-24-2026","familyId":"228","title":"A continuum temperature singularity for a radial pair potential","abstract":"We construct a stable radial pair potential in three dimensions whose canonical free energy has a strict downward derivative jump at one common finite positive inverse temperature throughout an open interval of positive densities. The potential has a divergent repulsive core, a nontrivial attractive interval, and an integrable tail satisfying $\\phi(r)=o(r^{-3})$. Its free-cube canonical free energy is finite at every positive inverse temperature and density.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs stable distance-dependent pair interactions for three-dimensional classical particles with a first-order phase transition: the canonical free energy has a derivative jump at one inverse temperature throughout an open density interval. One potential has a divergent repulsive core; another is bounded and continuous with an integrable power-law tail, realizing the type of transition sought in Simon's continuum problem.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-continuum-temperature-singularity-for-a-radial-pair-potential-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-continuum-temperature-singularity-for-a-radial-pair-potential-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-continuum-temperature-singularity-for-a-radial-pair-potential-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/A-continuum-temperature-singularity-for-a-radial-pair-potential-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-continuum-temperature-singularity-for-a-radial-pair-potential-September-24-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/A-continuum-temperature-singularity-for-a-radial-pair-potential-September-24-2026/build/model.tex","preprints/A-continuum-temperature-singularity-for-a-radial-pair-potential-September-24-2026/build/kac.tex","preprints/A-continuum-temperature-singularity-for-a-radial-pair-potential-September-24-2026/build/convex.tex","preprints/A-continuum-temperature-singularity-for-a-radial-pair-potential-September-24-2026/build/construction.tex","preprints/A-continuum-temperature-singularity-for-a-radial-pair-potential-September-24-2026/build/convex-facts.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-continuum-temperature-singularity-for-a-radial-pair-potential-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a stable radial pair potential in three dimensions whose canonical free energy has a strict downward derivative jump at one common finite positive inverse temperature throughout an open interval of positive densities. The potential has a divergent repulsive core, a nontrivial attractive interval, and an integrable tail satisfying $\\phi(r)=o(r^{-3})$. Its free-cube canonical free energy is finite at every positive inverse temperature and density.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The continuum existence problem","sourceOffset":565},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and the role of the fixed density","sourceOffset":3918},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the construction works","sourceOffset":7755},{"kind":"section","title":"A stable class with four-particle clusters","sourceOffset":10600},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The packing density and the core","sourceOffset":11031},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The canonical limit for every density","sourceOffset":18494},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The quadratic density transform","sourceOffset":28035},{"kind":"section","title":"Uniform estimates and the Kac approximation","sourceOffset":31354},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local moments and continuity in the attractive tail","sourceOffset":32098},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One long-range attraction","sourceOffset":37203},{"kind":"section","title":"An initial triple of supporting slopes","sourceOffset":43940},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The free-cluster limit","sourceOffset":44758}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist an admissible radial difference-only pair potential $\\Phi(x)=\\phi(|x|)$ on $\\R^3$, a nonempty open interval $I\\subset(0,\\infty)$, and one $\\beta_c\\in(1/2,3/2)$ with the following properties. The potential satisfies $\\phi(r)=o(r^{-3})$ as $r\\to\\infty$. The free-cube limit \\eqref{eq:free-energy-definition} exists and is finite for every $\\beta>0$ and every density $\\rho>0$. For every $\\rho\\in I$, the finite one-sided derivatives at the same critical point satisfy \\[ D_\\beta^- f_\\Phi(\\beta_c,\\rho) >D_\\beta^+ f_\\Phi(\\beta_c,\\rho). \\] Thus one potential and one critical inverse temperature give a strict canonical temperature singularity throughout $I$.","sourceOffset":2758,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Uniform stability","label":"prop:stability","text":"Put $A_0=E/8$ and $B_0=E/2$. Every potential in \\eqref{eq:physical-class} satisfies, for every finite configuration, \\begin{equation} \\sum_{\\ell<k}\\psi_t(x_\\ell-x_k) \\ge A_0\\sum_i n_i^2-B_0N, \\qquad U_N^\\varphi\\ge (A_0-M/2)\\sum_i n_i^2-B_0N. \\end{equation} These inequalities remain valid if positive core interactions between members of a partition into unions of whole cells are deleted, or if any negative interactions are multiplied by factors in $[0,1]$.","sourceOffset":16086,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/228.md","scopeText":"The formalization constructs one stable radial pair potential in three-dimensional continuum space with a divergent repulsive core and an integrable tail that is $o(r^{-3})$. Its canonical free energy exists for every positive inverse temperature and density. At one inverse temperature $\\beta_c\\in(1/2,3/2)$, the free energy has a strict finite jump between its one-sided derivatives with respect to inverse temperature, for every density in one nonempty open interval.\n\nThe formalization constructs a bounded continuous stable radial pair potential in $\\mathbb R^3$ satisfying $|\\phi(r)|\\le Cr^{-3-1/32}$ for $r\\ge1$. There is a nonempty open interval of positive densities around $5p/3$, where $p$ is the unit-separated packing-density limit, on which the canonical free energy exists at every pos","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/228.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":99438,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-continuum-temperature-singularity-for-a-radial-pair-potential-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"228-02","slug":"A-radial-continuum-phase-transition-with-algebraic-decay-September-24-2026","familyId":"228","title":"A radial continuum phase transition with algebraic decay","abstract":"We construct a bounded, continuous, stable radial pair potential in three dimensions with $|\\phi(r)|\\le Cr^{-3-1/32}$ for r ≥ 1. Throughout an open interval of positive densities, its canonical thermodynamic free energy is finite at every positive inverse temperature and has a strict downward derivative jump at one common finite positive inverse temperature.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs stable distance-dependent pair interactions for three-dimensional classical particles with a first-order phase transition: the canonical free energy has a derivative jump at one inverse temperature throughout an open density interval. One potential has a divergent repulsive core; another is bounded and continuous with an integrable power-law tail, realizing the type of transition sought in Simon's continuum problem.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-radial-continuum-phase-transition-with-algebraic-decay-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-radial-continuum-phase-transition-with-algebraic-decay-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-radial-continuum-phase-transition-with-algebraic-decay-September-24-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/A-radial-continuum-phase-transition-with-algebraic-decay-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-radial-continuum-phase-transition-with-algebraic-decay-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-radial-continuum-phase-transition-with-algebraic-decay-September-24-2026/build/sections/foundations.tex","preprints/A-radial-continuum-phase-transition-with-algebraic-decay-September-24-2026/build/sections/local_bounds.tex","preprints/A-radial-continuum-phase-transition-with-algebraic-decay-September-24-2026/build/sections/transform.tex","preprints/A-radial-continuum-phase-transition-with-algebraic-decay-September-24-2026/build/sections/range.tex","preprints/A-radial-continuum-phase-transition-with-algebraic-decay-September-24-2026/build/sections/initial.tex","preprints/A-radial-continuum-phase-transition-with-algebraic-decay-September-24-2026/build/figures/support_geometry.tex","preprints/A-radial-continuum-phase-transition-with-algebraic-decay-September-24-2026/build/sections/iteration.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-radial-continuum-phase-transition-with-algebraic-decay-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a bounded, continuous, stable radial pair potential in three dimensions with \\(|\\phi(r)|\\le Cr^{-3-1/32}\\) for \\(r\\ge1\\). Throughout an open interval of positive densities, its canonical thermodynamic free energy is finite at every positive inverse temperature and has a strict downward derivative jump at one common finite positive inverse temperature.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":412},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and the role of the tail","sourceOffset":2365},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":6371},{"kind":"section","title":"A stable family and its thermodynamic functions","sourceOffset":11591},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Packing and the microscopic core","sourceOffset":12043},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Quadratic control of cell counts","sourceOffset":16654},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Canonical limits and pressure duality","sourceOffset":20843},{"kind":"section","title":"Occupation bounds and perturbation estimates","sourceOffset":31808},{"kind":"section","title":"Ideal attraction and supporting slopes","sourceOffset":41617},{"kind":"section","title":"A uniform quantitative range estimate","sourceOffset":51809},{"kind":"section","title":"An initial triple of pressure slopes","sourceOffset":67541},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Clusters and the bare-core pressure","sourceOffset":68286}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a bounded continuous function \\(\\phi:[0,\\infty)\\to\\R\\) and finite constants \\(B,C>0\\) such that \\[ \\sum_{i<j}\\phi(\\abs{x_i-x_j})\\ge-BN, \\qquad \\abs{\\phi(r)}\\le C r^{-3-1/32}\\quad(r\\ge1). \\] There are an open interval \\(I\\subset(0,\\infty)\\) centered at \\(5p/3\\) and a common inverse temperature \\(\\beta_c\\in[7/8,9/8]\\) such that, for every \\(\\rho\\in I\\), the canonical free energy \\[ f(\\beta,\\rho)= -\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}\\frac1{\\beta L^3} \\log Z^\\phi_{L,\\lfloor\\rho L^3\\rfloor}(\\beta) \\] exists and is finite for every \\(\\beta>0\\). Its finite one-sided derivatives at \\(\\beta_c\\) satisfy \\[ f'_-(\\beta_c,\\rho)>f'_+(\\beta_c,\\rho). \\] In particular, \\(f(\\,\\cdot\\,,\\rho)\\) is not differentiable at \\(\\beta_c\\).","sourceOffset":1593,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Common stability","label":"f:stability","text":"Set \\begin{equation} D_0=t^2/8,\\qquad B_0=t^2/2. \\end{equation} For every configuration, including coincident particles, \\begin{equation} U^{\\psi_t}\\ge D_0\\sum_i n_i^2-B_0N, \\qquad U^v\\ge(D_0-M/2)\\sum_i n_i^2-B_0N. \\end{equation} Choose $t$ sufficiently large that \\begin{equation} D_0\\ge8M,\\qquad ct>4(A+1),\\qquad c=1/3456. \\end{equation} Then, in any axis-parallel cube of side $L\\ge1$, with $V=L^3$, \\begin{equation} U^v\\ge ct^2\\frac{N^2}{V}-\\frac{t^2}{2}N. \\end{equation} In particular all members of \\eqref{f:family} are stable with the same constant $B_0$.","sourceOffset":18619,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Ideal attraction","label":"k:transform","text":"For $a>0$, define \\begin{equation} T_aQ(\\beta,h) =\\sup_{u\\in\\R}\\left\\{ Q(\\beta,h+\\beta au)-\\frac{\\beta a}{2}u^2\\right\\}. \\end{equation} An active shift at $\\lambda=(\\beta,h)$ is a value of $u$ attaining this supremum. Its shifted parameter is $x_u=(\\beta,h+\\beta au)$.","sourceOffset":42041,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/228.md","scopeText":"The formalization constructs one stable radial pair potential in three-dimensional continuum space with a divergent repulsive core and an integrable tail that is $o(r^{-3})$. Its canonical free energy exists for every positive inverse temperature and density. At one inverse temperature $\\beta_c\\in(1/2,3/2)$, the free energy has a strict finite jump between its one-sided derivatives with respect to inverse temperature, for every density in one nonempty open interval.\n\nThe formalization constructs a bounded continuous stable radial pair potential in $\\mathbb R^3$ satisfying $|\\phi(r)|\\le Cr^{-3-1/32}$ for $r\\ge1$. There is a nonempty open interval of positive densities around $5p/3$, where $p$ is the unit-separated packing-density limit, on which the canonical free energy exists at every pos","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/228.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":105006,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-radial-continuum-phase-transition-with-algebraic-decay-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"229-01","slug":"The-Reconstruction-Threshold-for-the-Ferromagnetic-Four-State-Potts-Model-October-5-2026","familyId":"229","title":"The Reconstruction Threshold for the Ferromagnetic Four-State Potts Model","abstract":"We establish the exact Kesten–Stigum reconstruction threshold for the ferromagnetic four-state Potts broadcast model on every regular d-ary tree with d ≥ 2 and every Poisson Galton–Watson tree of mean d > 0. Reconstruction occurs exactly when $d\\lambda^2\\gt 1$; we prove nonreconstruction at and below the threshold, including equality. In the Poisson model the whole tree is observed and the reconstruction advantage is averaged without conditioning on survival. The proof uses reproducible exact-arithmetic verification of polynomial inequalities.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the exact reconstruction threshold $d\\lambda^2\\gt 1$, with nonreconstruction at equality, for three-state symmetric and four-state ferromagnetic broadcasting on regular trees (d ≥ 2) and observed Poisson trees (mean d > 1 and d > 0, respectively), with Poisson advantage averaged without conditioning on survival. The three-state theorem allows both signs of λ and gives the exact weak-recovery threshold for the symmetric three-community stochastic block model.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Reconstruction-Threshold-for-the-Ferromagnetic-Four-State-Potts-Model-October-5-2026/four-state-potts.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Reconstruction-Threshold-for-the-Ferromagnetic-Four-State-Potts-Model-October-5-2026/four-state-potts.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Reconstruction-Threshold-for-the-Ferromagnetic-Four-State-Potts-Model-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-Reconstruction-Threshold-for-the-Ferromagnetic-Four-State-Potts-Model-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Reconstruction-Threshold-for-the-Ferromagnetic-Four-State-Potts-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-Reconstruction-Threshold-for-the-Ferromagnetic-Four-State-Potts-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-experiments.tex","preprints/The-Reconstruction-Threshold-for-the-Ferromagnetic-Four-State-Potts-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-cone.tex","preprints/The-Reconstruction-Threshold-for-the-Ferromagnetic-Four-State-Potts-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-tree-decay.tex","preprints/The-Reconstruction-Threshold-for-the-Ferromagnetic-Four-State-Potts-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-polynomial-certificates.tex","preprints/The-Reconstruction-Threshold-for-the-Ferromagnetic-Four-State-Potts-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-finite-verification.tex","preprints/The-Reconstruction-Threshold-for-the-Ferromagnetic-Four-State-Potts-Model-October-5-2026/build/references.tex","preprints/The-Reconstruction-Threshold-for-the-Ferromagnetic-Four-State-Potts-Model-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-code-appendix.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Reconstruction-Threshold-for-the-Ferromagnetic-Four-State-Potts-Model-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We establish the exact Kesten--Stigum reconstruction threshold for the ferromagnetic four-state Potts broadcast model on every regular $d$-ary tree with $d\\ge2$ and every Poisson Galton--Watson tree of mean $d>0$. Reconstruction occurs exactly when $d\\lambda^2>1$; we prove nonreconstruction at and below the threshold, including equality. In the Poisson model the whole tree is observed and the reconstruction advantage is averaged without conditioning on survival. The proof uses reproducible exact-arithmetic verification of polynomial inequalities.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":598},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model and main result","sourceOffset":1141},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":3496},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof mechanism","sourceOffset":5451},{"kind":"section","title":"Symmetric posterior experiments","sourceOffset":8172},{"kind":"section","title":"A preserved constraint on posterior laws","sourceOffset":14559},{"kind":"section","title":"Decay on the tree","sourceOffset":20230},{"kind":"section","title":"Exact polynomial certificates","sourceOffset":26133},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ordered probabilities and homogeneous coefficients","sourceOffset":26592},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The single-posterior checks","sourceOffset":29639},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two rational inequalities","sourceOffset":32347},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A Taylor lower bound: Type T","sourceOffset":34854}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For the channel \\eqref{eq:broadcast-channel}, if $d\\lambda^2\\leq1$, then \\[ \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}a_n(d,\\lambda)=0 \\] in each of the following models: \\begin{enumerate} \\item the rooted $d$-ary tree, for every integer $d\\geq2$; \\item the Poisson$(d)$ Galton--Watson tree, for every real $d>0$. \\end{enumerate} In particular, the assertion includes $d\\lambda^2=1$.","sourceOffset":2432,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Polynomial inequalities","label":"prop:polynomial-inequalities","text":"For every $p,q\\in\\Deltafour$, the polynomials in \\eqref{eq:polynomial-table} satisfy $F(p)\\geq0$, $G(p)\\geq0$, and $H(p)\\geq-1/2$. For $x=Lp$ and $0\\leq t\\leq1$, \\begin{equation} H(tx)\\geq t^3H(x). \\end{equation} Moreover, \\begin{align} [zA(P)](p,q) &\\leq A(p)+A(q)-H(p)F(q)-F(p)H(q)-D_0(p,q), \\\\ [zH(P)](p,q) &\\geq H(p)G(q)+G(p)H(q). \\end{align}","sourceOffset":16362,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/229.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":58470,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Reconstruction-Threshold-for-the-Ferromagnetic-Four-State-Potts-Model-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"229-02","slug":"A-Capacity-Criterion-for-Four-State-Potts-Reconstruction-on-Trees-October-5-2026","familyId":"229","title":"A Capacity Criterion for Four-State Potts Reconstruction on Trees","abstract":"For the ferromagnetic four-state broadcast model with $0\\lt \\lambda\\lt 1$, we prove that reconstruction on a bounded-degree deterministic rooted tree occurs exactly when its L3 capacity with edge resistances $\\lambda^{-2|e|}$ is positive. This gives an exact criterion without regularity or growth-rate assumptions on the tree, including at the exponential critical boundary.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the exact reconstruction threshold $d\\lambda^2\\gt 1$, with nonreconstruction at equality, for three-state symmetric and four-state ferromagnetic broadcasting on regular trees (d ≥ 2) and observed Poisson trees (mean d > 1 and d > 0, respectively), with Poisson advantage averaged without conditioning on survival. The three-state theorem allows both signs of λ and gives the exact weak-recovery threshold for the symmetric three-community stochastic block model.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Capacity-Criterion-for-Four-State-Potts-Reconstruction-on-Trees-October-5-2026/four-state-capacity.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Capacity-Criterion-for-Four-State-Potts-Reconstruction-on-Trees-October-5-2026/four-state-capacity.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Capacity-Criterion-for-Four-State-Potts-Reconstruction-on-Trees-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/A-Capacity-Criterion-for-Four-State-Potts-Reconstruction-on-Trees-October-5-2026/build/main.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Capacity-Criterion-for-Four-State-Potts-Reconstruction-on-Trees-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For the ferromagnetic four-state broadcast model with $0<\\lambda<1$, we prove that reconstruction on a bounded-degree deterministic rooted tree occurs exactly when its $L^3$ capacity with edge resistances $\\lambda^{-2|e|}$ is positive. This gives an exact criterion without regularity or growth-rate assumptions on the tree, including at the exponential critical boundary.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":419},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and relation to earlier work","sourceOffset":2959},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":5257},{"kind":"section","title":"Posterior experiments and the companion input","sourceOffset":7150},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Channels and products","sourceOffset":8508},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The moment-saving input","sourceOffset":10651},{"kind":"section","title":"From reconstruction to capacity","sourceOffset":12001},{"kind":"section","title":"Local estimates for information loss","sourceOffset":16082},{"kind":"section","title":"From capacity to reconstruction","sourceOffset":20002},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A flow profile and terminal observations","sourceOffset":20580},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local and accumulated losses","sourceOffset":22597},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Averaging the losses along a flow path","sourceOffset":25210}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $T$ be an infinite rooted tree with at most $B<\\infty$ children per vertex, and let $0<\\lambda<1$. For the broadcast model \\eqref{eq:channel}, \\[ a_\\infty(T,\\lambda)>0 \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\Capacity_{3,\\lambda}(T)>0. \\]","sourceOffset":2322,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Companion input, {\\cite[Proposition 3.2 and Equations (3.8)--(3.9)","label":"prop:companion","text":"{OpenAI26Potts}}] There is a class $\\calC$ of symmetric posterior laws containing the revealing and uninformative laws and preserved by channel steps with any parameter in $[0,1]$ and by conditionally independent products. For $\\mu,\\nu\\in\\calC$, \\begin{equation} m(\\mu\\star\\nu)\\leq m(\\mu)+m(\\nu) -c\\left(\\E_\\mu[A^2]m(\\nu)+m(\\mu)\\E_\\nu[A^2]\\right), \\qquad c=\\frac1{1000}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":10850,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/229.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":28126,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Capacity-Criterion-for-Four-State-Potts-Reconstruction-on-Trees-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-Capacity-Criterion-for-Four-State-Potts-Reconstruction-on-Trees-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/A-Capacity-Criterion-for-Four-State-Potts-Reconstruction-on-Trees-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"229-03","slug":"The-exact-reconstruction-threshold-for-the-three-state-symmetric-channel-September-25-2026","familyId":"229","title":"The exact reconstruction threshold for the three-state symmetric channel","abstract":"We determine the exact reconstruction threshold for the symmetric three-state broadcast process on every regular b-ary tree, b ≥ 2, and every observed Poisson Galton–Watson tree of mean d > 1. Reconstruction occurs exactly when $d\\lambda^2\\gt 1$, with d = b in the regular model; there is non-reconstruction at equality for either sign of the channel parameter. The Poisson advantage is averaged over trees and spins without conditioning on survival. This resolves the all-degree three-state regular-tree prediction. Combining the Poisson theorem with known tree-to-graph and algorithmic results gives the exact weak-recovery threshold for the symmetric three-community sparse stochastic block model with independent uniform labels, fixed within- and between-community rates $a,b\\gt 0$, and mean degree $(a+2b)/3\\gt 1$: recovery is possible exactly when $(a-b)^2\\gt 3(a+2b)$. Above this threshold it is achievable in $O(n\\log n)$ time; at or below it, weak recovery is information-theoretically impossible.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the exact reconstruction threshold $d\\lambda^2\\gt 1$, with nonreconstruction at equality, for three-state symmetric and four-state ferromagnetic broadcasting on regular trees (d ≥ 2) and observed Poisson trees (mean d > 1 and d > 0, respectively), with Poisson advantage averaged without conditioning on survival. The three-state theorem allows both signs of λ and gives the exact weak-recovery threshold for the symmetric three-community stochastic block model.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-exact-reconstruction-threshold-for-the-three-state-symmetric-channel-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-exact-reconstruction-threshold-for-the-three-state-symmetric-channel-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-exact-reconstruction-threshold-for-the-three-state-symmetric-channel-September-25-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-exact-reconstruction-threshold-for-the-three-state-symmetric-channel-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-exact-reconstruction-threshold-for-the-three-state-symmetric-channel-September-25-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-exact-reconstruction-threshold-for-the-three-state-symmetric-channel-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-exact-reconstruction-threshold-for-the-three-state-symmetric-channel-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-foundations.tex","preprints/The-exact-reconstruction-threshold-for-the-three-state-symmetric-channel-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-positive.tex","preprints/The-exact-reconstruction-threshold-for-the-three-state-symmetric-channel-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-local.tex","preprints/The-exact-reconstruction-threshold-for-the-three-state-symmetric-channel-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-weighted.tex","preprints/The-exact-reconstruction-threshold-for-the-three-state-symmetric-channel-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-grid.tex","preprints/The-exact-reconstruction-threshold-for-the-three-state-symmetric-channel-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-conclusion.tex","preprints/The-exact-reconstruction-threshold-for-the-three-state-symmetric-channel-September-25-2026/build/sections/appendix-radial.tex","preprints/The-exact-reconstruction-threshold-for-the-three-state-symmetric-channel-September-25-2026/build/sections/appendix-interval.tex","preprints/The-exact-reconstruction-threshold-for-the-three-state-symmetric-channel-September-25-2026/build/sections/appendix-grid.tex","preprints/The-exact-reconstruction-threshold-for-the-three-state-symmetric-channel-September-25-2026/build/sections/appendix-certificates.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-exact-reconstruction-threshold-for-the-three-state-symmetric-channel-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We determine the exact reconstruction threshold for the symmetric three-state broadcast process on every regular $b$-ary tree, $b\\ge2$, and every observed Poisson Galton--Watson tree of mean $d>1$. Reconstruction occurs exactly when $d\\lambda^2>1$, with $d=b$ in the regular model; there is non-reconstruction at equality for either sign of the channel parameter. The Poisson advantage is averaged over trees and spins without conditioning on survival. This resolves the all-degree three-state regular-tree prediction. Combining the Poisson theorem with known tree-to-graph and algorithmic results gives the exact weak-recovery threshold for the symmetric three-community sparse stochastic block model with independent uniform labels, fixed within- and between-community rates $a,b>0$, and mean degree $(a+2b)/3>1$: recovery is possible exactly when $(a-b)^2>3(a+2b)$. Above this threshold it is achievable in $O(n\\log n)$ time; at or below it, weak recovery is information-theoretically impossible.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":1062},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model and result","sourceOffset":1914},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":3827},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and its precedents","sourceOffset":6948},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A consequence for community detection","sourceOffset":11122},{"kind":"section","title":"Posterior experiments and reductions","sourceOffset":13034},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Observations, likelihoods, and posterior coordinates","sourceOffset":13670},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exact posterior recursion","sourceOffset":20280},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Convergence, fixed points, and extinction","sourceOffset":24354},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reconstruction above the second-moment threshold","sourceOffset":30430},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Decreasing the channel strength or the offspring mean","sourceOffset":32400},{"kind":"section","title":"Positive channels at equality","sourceOffset":36004}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Exact threshold","label":"thm:threshold","text":"For every integer $b\\ge2$ in the regular model and every real $d>1$ in the Poisson model, reconstruction occurs if and only if \\[ d\\lambda^2>1, \\] with $d=b$ in the regular case. At every admissible equality $d\\lambda^2=1$, there is non-reconstruction.","sourceOffset":3233,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Exact weak-recovery threshold for three communities","label":"cor:sbm","text":"In the model above, the following statements hold. \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\roman*)] \\item If $(a-b)^2\\le 3(a+2b)$, then every sequence of estimators $\\widehat\\sigma_n$ using only $G_n$ and independent randomization satisfies \\[ \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\E\\operatorname{ov}(\\sigma,\\widehat\\sigma_n)=\\frac13. \\] \\item If $(a-b)^2>3(a+2b)$, there are $\\varepsilon>0$ and an algorithm, allowed to depend on the fixed parameters $a,b$, which runs in $O(n\\log n)$ time and satisfies \\[ \\PP\\!\\left(\\operatorname{ov}(\\sigma,\\widehat\\sigma_n) \\ge\\frac13+\\varepsilon\\right)\\longrightarrow1. \\] \\end{enumerate} Thus weak recovery is possible exactly under the strict inequality, and is information-theoretically impossible at equality.","sourceOffset":11998,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/229.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves the supercritical direction of reconstruction for the symmetric three-state broadcast channel, whose parameter satisfies $-1/2\\le\\lambda\\le1$. On a regular $b$-ary tree, reconstruction holds when $b\\lambda^2>1$; on an observed Poisson Galton–Watson tree of mean $d$, it holds when $d\\lambda^2>1$. In each case the root-estimation advantage converges to a positive limit, with the Poisson advantage averaged over trees and spins.\n\nThe selected statements cover reconstruction above the Kesten–Stigum threshold. Non-reconstruction at or below the threshold and the stochastic-block-model consequences in the paper are outside them.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/229.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":152133,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-exact-reconstruction-threshold-for-the-three-state-symmetric-channel-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"230-01","slug":"An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026","familyId":"230","title":"An exact Hausdorff gauge for SLE","abstract":"For each $0\\lt \\kappa\\lt 8$, we construct a deterministic Hausdorff gauge that almost surely assigns positive finite measure to every nontrivial compact positive-time segment of chordal Schramm–Loewner evolution. This answers Schramm's Hausdorff-measure existence problem in this parameter range. The entire trace has finite expected gauge measure in each bounded box.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves Schramm’s Hausdorff-measure question for chordal SLEκ, $0\\lt \\kappa\\lt 8$. The explicit gauge $r^d(\\log\\log(1/r))^{(2-d)/2}$, $d=1+\\kappa/8$, gives almost surely positive finite measure to every trace segment $\\gamma([s,t])$ with $0\\lt s\\lt t\\lt \\infty$, and finite expected measure to the trace in every bounded disk.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026/build/sections/onepoint.tex","preprints/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026/build/sections/multipoint.tex","preprints/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026/build/figures/individual-stops.tex","preprints/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026/build/sections/mass.tex","preprints/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026/build/sections/coefficients.tex","preprints/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026/build/sections/gauge.tex","preprints/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026/build/sections/hausdorff.tex","preprints/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026/build/sections/upper-cover.tex","preprints/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026/build/figures/batch-timeline.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For each \\(0<\\kappa<8\\), we construct a deterministic Hausdorff gauge that almost surely assigns positive finite measure to every nontrivial compact positive-time segment of chordal Schramm--Loewner evolution. This answers Schramm's Hausdorff-measure existence problem in this parameter range. The entire trace has finite expected gauge measure in each bounded box.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":411},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The result","sourceOffset":1677},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Multipoint estimates and one canonical mass","sourceOffset":3355},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why the moments determine the gauge","sourceOffset":6498},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work and proof organization","sourceOffset":10501},{"kind":"section","title":"Conformal radius and the law centered at one point","sourceOffset":14251},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The local martingale and finite stopped laws","sourceOffset":18120},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform mixing and the remaining potential","sourceOffset":23904},{"kind":"section","title":"A uniform estimate for several radius hits","sourceOffset":29720},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Controlling the interaction of two active points","sourceOffset":31792},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Regularized factors and the product supermartingale","sourceOffset":34231},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Integrating the collision singularities","sourceOffset":44073}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"An exact moment gauge","label":"thm:main","text":"Fix $0<\\kappa<8$, and let $\\gamma$ be chordal $\\SLE_\\kappa$ from $0$ to $\\infty$ in the upper half-plane $\\HH$, parametrized by half-plane capacity $2t$. There is a deterministic Hausdorff gauge $h_\\kappa$, depending only on $\\kappa$, such that almost surely, simultaneously for every real $0<s<t<\\infty$, \\[ 0<\\Haus^{h_\\kappa}(\\gamma([s,t]))<\\infty. \\]","sourceOffset":2101,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Finite lifetime and tested potential identity","label":"op:potential","text":"Under the consistent centered laws, $T^*(x)=\\lim_{s\\to\\infty}\\tau_s(x)$ is finite almost surely. For every finite deterministic $t$ and bounded raw $\\mathcal F_t$-measurable random variable $H$, \\begin{equation} \\EE[H G_t(x)] =G_0(x)\\EE_x^*[H\\ind_{\\{t<T^*(x)\\}}]. \\end{equation} On the right, $H$ is evaluated only on stopped data extending beyond $t$. Thus \\begin{equation} \\EE G_t(x)=G_0(x)\\PP_x^*(T^*(x)>t)\\longrightarrow0 \\quad(t\\to\\infty). \\end{equation} For every stopping time $S$, \\begin{equation} \\EE[G_S(x)\\ind_{\\{S<\\infty\\}}]\\le G_0(x). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":27000,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/230.md","scopeText":"For $0<\\kappa<8$, put $d=1+\\kappa/8$. The formalization proves that every continuous nondecreasing Hausdorff gauge agreeing with $h(r)=r^d(\\log\\log(1/r))^{(2-d)/2}$ at sufficiently small positive radii almost surely assigns positive measure to every nontrivial compact positive-time segment of ordinary chordal $\\mathrm{SLE}_\\kappa$.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/230.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":121547,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode.tex","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/An-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex.tex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"230-02","slug":"An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026","familyId":"230","title":"An explicit exact Hausdorff gauge for SLE","abstract":"For each fixed $0\\lt \\kappa\\lt 8$, let $d=1+\\kappa/8$. The gauge $h(r)=r^d(\\log\\log(1/r))^{(2-d)/2}$ at sufficiently small radii almost surely gives positive finite Hausdorff measure to every nontrivial positive-time compact segment of chordal SLEκ. This gives an explicit solution to Schramm's Hausdorff-measure problem in this parameter range. The entire trace has finite expected measure in every bounded disk. The exponent-one iterated-logarithm gauge suggested by Schramm is not sigma-finite on any such segment.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves Schramm’s Hausdorff-measure question for chordal SLEκ, $0\\lt \\kappa\\lt 8$. The explicit gauge $r^d(\\log\\log(1/r))^{(2-d)/2}$, $d=1+\\kappa/8$, gives almost surely positive finite measure to every trace segment $\\gamma([s,t])$ with $0\\lt s\\lt t\\lt \\infty$, and finite expected measure to the trace in every bounded disk.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026/build/figures/captions.tex","preprints/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026/build/sections/abstract.tex","preprints/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026/build/sections/local-laws.tex","preprints/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026/build/sections/mesh-tails.tex","preprints/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026/build/figures/brownian-circuit.tex","preprints/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026/build/sections/retained-mass.tex","preprints/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026/build/sections/ribbon.tex","preprints/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026/build/figures/ribbon-front.tex","preprints/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026/build/figures/serpentine-ribbon.tex","preprints/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026/build/sections/dense-cover.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For each fixed $0<\\kappa<8$, let $d=1+\\kappa/8$. The gauge $h(r)=r^d(\\log\\log(1/r))^{(2-d)/2}$ at sufficiently small radii almost surely gives positive finite Hausdorff measure to every nontrivial positive-time compact segment of chordal $\\mathrm{SLE}_\\kappa$. This gives an explicit solution to Schramm's Hausdorff-measure problem in this parameter range. The entire trace has finite expected measure in every bounded disk. The exponent-one iterated-logarithm gauge suggested by Schramm is not sigma-finite on any such segment.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":577},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The result","sourceOffset":3171},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two Hausdorff bounds","sourceOffset":6115},{"kind":"section","title":"Boundary geometry and pointwise slit support","sourceOffset":12920},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite one-point laws and physical localization","sourceOffset":32259},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Stopped Green tilts","sourceOffset":33596},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Avoidance near the real line","sourceOffset":47478},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A conformal localization estimate","sourceOffset":51732},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Swallowing and a rule in physical disks","sourceOffset":57495},{"kind":"section","title":"A uniform tail for spatially integrated stopped densities","sourceOffset":62936},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Brownian occupation of a boundary layer","sourceOffset":68500},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Asynchronously started brackets for \\texorpdfstring{$\\kappa\\le4$}{kappa at most four}","sourceOffset":76274}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $\\gamma$ be ordinary chordal $\\mathrm{SLE}_\\kappa$ from $0$ to $\\infty$ in $\\mathbb H$, parametrized by half-plane capacity $2t$, and set $\\Gamma=\\gamma([0,\\infty))$. For each fixed $0<\\kappa<8$, on one event of probability one, \\[ 0<\\mathcal H^h(\\gamma([s,t]))<\\infty \\qquad\\text{for every real }0<s<t<\\infty. \\] For every deterministic $0<R<\\infty$, \\[ \\mathbb E\\mathcal H^h(\\Gamma\\cap\\overline B(0,R)) \\le C_{\\kappa,R}<\\infty. \\] In particular, on the same probability-one event, \\[ \\mathcal H^h\\bigl(\\Gamma\\cap([-m,m]+i[0,m])\\bigr)<\\infty \\qquad(m=1,2,\\ldots). \\]","sourceOffset":3666,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:larger-gauge","text":"Let $g(r)=r^d\\log\\log(1/r)$ at sufficiently small radii, with any continuous nondecreasing completion. On the event in Theorem \\ref{thm:main}, the restriction of $\\mathcal H^g$ to every $\\gamma([s,t])$, $0<s<t<\\infty$, is not sigma-finite; in particular its total measure is infinite.","sourceOffset":4509,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/230.md","scopeText":"For $0<\\kappa<8$, put $d=1+\\kappa/8$. The formalization proves that every continuous nondecreasing Hausdorff gauge agreeing with $h(r)=r^d(\\log\\log(1/r))^{(2-d)/2}$ at sufficiently small positive radii almost surely assigns positive measure to every nontrivial compact positive-time segment of ordinary chordal $\\mathrm{SLE}_\\kappa$.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/230.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":171314,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-explicit-exact-Hausdorff-gauge-for-SLE-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"231-01","slug":"The-free-uniform-spanning-forest-is-a-factor-of-IID-September-25-2026","familyId":"231","title":"The free uniform spanning forest is a factor of IID","abstract":"We prove that the free uniform spanning forest is a factor of IID on every infinite connected locally finite simple unweighted graph. One Borel rule works for all such graphs and uses no root, answering affirmatively the general factor question for unimodular random graphs. We also show that every translation-invariant strongly Rayleigh process indexed by a countable group is a factor of IID, including invariant determinantal processes with Hermitian positive-contraction kernels. This group-action conclusion requires no amenability.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"On every infinite connected locally finite simple unweighted graph, the free uniform spanning forest is a factor of independent vertex labels, by one isomorphism-equivariant rule using no root. Translation-invariant strongly Rayleigh binary processes on every countable group, including invariant determinantal processes with Hermitian positive-contraction kernels, are also factors of IID.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-free-uniform-spanning-forest-is-a-factor-of-IID-September-25-2026/The-free-uniform-spanning-forest-is-a-factor-of-IID-September-25-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-free-uniform-spanning-forest-is-a-factor-of-IID-September-25-2026/The-free-uniform-spanning-forest-is-a-factor-of-IID-September-25-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-free-uniform-spanning-forest-is-a-factor-of-IID-September-25-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-free-uniform-spanning-forest-is-a-factor-of-IID-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-free-uniform-spanning-forest-is-a-factor-of-IID-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-free-uniform-spanning-forest-is-a-factor-of-IID-September-25-2026/build/sections/response.tex","preprints/The-free-uniform-spanning-forest-is-a-factor-of-IID-September-25-2026/build/sections/observation.tex","preprints/The-free-uniform-spanning-forest-is-a-factor-of-IID-September-25-2026/build/sections/decoder.tex","preprints/The-free-uniform-spanning-forest-is-a-factor-of-IID-September-25-2026/build/sections/implementation.tex","preprints/The-free-uniform-spanning-forest-is-a-factor-of-IID-September-25-2026/build/sections/strongly-rayleigh.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-free-uniform-spanning-forest-is-a-factor-of-IID-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the free uniform spanning forest is a factor of IID on every infinite connected locally finite simple unweighted graph. One Borel rule works for all such graphs and uses no root, answering affirmatively the general factor question for unimodular random graphs. We also show that every translation-invariant strongly Rayleigh process indexed by a countable group is a factor of IID, including invariant determinantal processes with Hermitian positive-contraction kernels. This group-action conclusion requires no amenability.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":584},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Statement","sourceOffset":3325},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Strongly Rayleigh processes","sourceOffset":4945},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction","sourceOffset":7575},{"kind":"section","title":"Weighted trees and a canonical response field","sourceOffset":11720},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The free limit from finite determinants","sourceOffset":15159},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite tilts and the canonical drift","sourceOffset":21022},{"kind":"section","title":"Brownian observations and their innovations","sourceOffset":26072},{"kind":"section","title":"A deterministic inverse and a Brownian sampler","sourceOffset":33376},{"kind":"section","title":"One Borel graph rule","sourceOffset":43357},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From vertex labels to independent edge paths","sourceOffset":43616},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Measurability on varying graphs","sourceOffset":47033}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is one Borel, root-independent, equivariant rule \\[ \\Phi(G,U,e)\\in\\{0,1\\} \\] such that, for every infinite connected locally finite simple undirected unweighted graph $G$ and independent $\\operatorname{Uniform}[0,1]$ vertex labels $U=(U_v)_{v\\in V(G)}$, the entire edge set \\[ \\{e\\in E(G):\\Phi(G,U,e)=1\\} \\] has law $\\FUSF_G$.","sourceOffset":3860,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:unimodular","text":"The free uniform spanning forest is a graph factor of IID under every unimodular law supported on the stated class of graphs. The same rule works for every such law.","sourceOffset":4242,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/231.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves that the free uniform spanning forest is a factor of independent identically distributed vertex labels on every infinite connected locally finite simple unweighted graph. One Borel equivariant rule works for all such graphs and uses no distinguished root.\n\nThe linked supplements also give equivariant IID sampling for invariant strongly Rayleigh laws on countable groups and existence, uniqueness, and IID-factor results for determinantal laws with Hermitian positive-contraction kernels on countable index sets. These group results require neither amenability nor finite generation, and the kernels need not be trace class.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/231.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":59593,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-free-uniform-spanning-forest-is-a-factor-of-IID-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"232-01","slug":"Gaussian-free-field-limits-of-weighted-integer-Lipschitz-heights-October-6-2026","familyId":"232","title":"Gaussian free-field limits of weighted integer Lipschitz heights","abstract":"We prove a Gaussian free field scaling limit for weighted integer Lipschitz heights on the triangular lattice. With zero boundary values and a factor x for each edge on which the height changes, the field converges after division by a positive constant depending only on x to the zero-Dirichlet Gaussian free field, for every fixed $x\\in[1/\\sqrt2,1]$. The convergence holds as a random distribution on every bounded C2 Jordan domain under inside lattice approximations with uniformly convergent boundary parametrizations. This includes the uniform height model and the predicted critical endpoint.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Gaussian free field limits on bounded smooth simply connected domains for triangular-lattice height models: uniform odd heights with increments $0,\\pm2$ and two-arc boundary values $\\pm1$, and zero-boundary integer Lipschitz heights weighted by fixed $x\\in[1/\\sqrt2,1]$. Uniform real Lipschitz heights also converge to a Gaussian field; at a tuned opposite-boundary amplitude, their interface converges to chordal SLE4, establishing Schramm’s real-field/interface predictions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Gaussian-free-field-limits-of-weighted-integer-Lipschitz-heights-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Gaussian-free-field-limits-of-weighted-integer-Lipschitz-heights-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Gaussian-free-field-limits-of-weighted-integer-Lipschitz-heights-October-6-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Gaussian-free-field-limits-of-weighted-integer-Lipschitz-heights-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Gaussian-free-field-limits-of-weighted-integer-Lipschitz-heights-October-6-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Gaussian-free-field-limits-of-weighted-integer-Lipschitz-heights-October-6-2026/build/sections/02-discrete.tex","preprints/Gaussian-free-field-limits-of-weighted-integer-Lipschitz-heights-October-6-2026/build/figures/cell-cut.tex","preprints/Gaussian-free-field-limits-of-weighted-integer-Lipschitz-heights-October-6-2026/build/sections/03-moments.tex","preprints/Gaussian-free-field-limits-of-weighted-integer-Lipschitz-heights-October-6-2026/build/sections/04-plane.tex","preprints/Gaussian-free-field-limits-of-weighted-integer-Lipschitz-heights-October-6-2026/build/sections/05-pins.tex","preprints/Gaussian-free-field-limits-of-weighted-integer-Lipschitz-heights-October-6-2026/build/figures/pins-five-moves.tex","preprints/Gaussian-free-field-limits-of-weighted-integer-Lipschitz-heights-October-6-2026/build/sections/06-boundary.tex","preprints/Gaussian-free-field-limits-of-weighted-integer-Lipschitz-heights-October-6-2026/build/figures/boundary-attachment.tex","preprints/Gaussian-free-field-limits-of-weighted-integer-Lipschitz-heights-October-6-2026/build/sections/07-limit.tex","preprints/Gaussian-free-field-limits-of-weighted-integer-Lipschitz-heights-October-6-2026/build/sections/08-scalar-spectra.tex","preprints/Gaussian-free-field-limits-of-weighted-integer-Lipschitz-heights-October-6-2026/build/sections/09-annulus.tex","preprints/Gaussian-free-field-limits-of-weighted-integer-Lipschitz-heights-October-6-2026/build/figures/annulus-stepped-domain.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Gaussian-free-field-limits-of-weighted-integer-Lipschitz-heights-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a Gaussian free field scaling limit for weighted integer Lipschitz heights on the triangular lattice. With zero boundary values and a factor $x$ for each edge on which the height changes, the field converges after division by a positive constant depending only on $x$ to the zero-Dirichlet Gaussian free field, for every fixed $x\\in[1/\\sqrt2,1]$. The convergence holds as a random distribution on every bounded $C^2$ Jordan domain under inside lattice approximations with uniformly convergent boundary parametrizations. This includes the uniform height model and the predicted critical endpoint.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":669},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model, approximation, and theorem","sourceOffset":1218},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and significance","sourceOffset":5646},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Structure of the proof","sourceOffset":11555},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":14705},{"kind":"section","title":"Exact cuts and conditional comparison","sourceOffset":15974},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Spins, cells, and exact cuts","sourceOffset":16951},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Restricted histories and the two-law inequality","sourceOffset":36932},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Online exploration and buffered circuits","sourceOffset":49952},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Multiplicative comparison for rare pins","sourceOffset":67007},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Protected switches and height order","sourceOffset":72275},{"kind":"section","title":"Shell counts and smearing moments","sourceOffset":81063}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed $x\\in[1/\\sqrt2,1]$ there exists $\\sigma(x)\\in(0,\\infty)$, independent of $D$ and of the inside approximation in Definition \\ref{def:approximation}, such that \\[ \\frac{h_\\delta}{\\sigma(x)}\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ \\Phi_D \\qquad\\text{in }\\mathcal D'(D). \\] In particular, the fields are tight as random distributions and, for every $k\\ge1$ and $f_1,\\ldots,f_k\\in C_c^\\infty(D)$, the vector $(h_\\delta(f_i)/\\sigma(x))_{i=1}^k$ converges to $(\\Phi_D(f_i))_{i=1}^k$. All mixed moments of these vectors converge.","sourceOffset":3982,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Annulus circuit estimate","label":"thm:circuit-input","text":"For $r\\ge0$, let $\\Lambda_r$ be the ball of radius $r$ in the triangular graph metric, with its bounding honeycomb contour; thus $\\Lambda_r=\\Lambda_{\\lfloor r\\rfloor}$. For $a>1$ and an integer $n\\in\\N$, consider on $\\Lambda_{an}$ the cell spin law whose interior cells have weight $(x^2)^{\\ind\\{B\\text{ or }W\\text{ nonconstant}\\}}$ and whose boundary $Y$ cells are excluded. Fix $B=-$ on the boundary face layer and leave $W$ free. There is $c=c(x,a)>0$ such that, for $n\\ge 3/(a-1)$, the probability of an occupied open-plus cell circuit surrounding $\\Lambda_n$ and contained in $\\Lambda_{an}$ is at least $c$. The same statement holds with the signs or the two spin colors interchanged.","sourceOffset":35259,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Inside approximation","label":"def:approximation","text":"Let $D\\subset\\R^2$ be a bounded simply connected domain with $C^2$ Jordan boundary. For each mesh $\\delta$ in a sequence tending to zero, let $\\gamma_\\delta$ be a simple cycle in $\\delta\\hexlat$, let $D_\\delta$ be its bounded interior, and include every lattice vertex and edge on or inside the cycle. We require $D_\\delta\\subset D$, that every compact subset of $D$ is eventually contained in $D_\\delta$, and that there are parametrizations \\[ \\gamma_\\delta:S^1\\longrightarrow\\partial D_\\delta, \\qquad \\gamma:S^1\\longrightarrow\\partial D, \\qquad \\|\\gamma_\\delta-\\gamma\\|_\\infty\\longrightarrow0. \\] H","sourceOffset":1552,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Filled brackets","label":"def:brackets","text":"In the plane spin law impose $B=+$ on all of $S_{0,\\delta}$ and $W=+$ on $A_{\\delta,\\epsilon}$. Anchor the height to zero at one doubly pinned vertex, and restrict it to the original interior. Its law is the upper filled bracket, denoted $H_\\delta^{+,\\epsilon}$. For the lower filled bracket $H_\\delta^{-,\\epsilon}$ interchange the colors: impose $W=+$ on the whole skeleton and $B=+$ on $A_{\\delta,\\epsilon}$, with the same zero anchor.","sourceOffset":151825,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":341776,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":23,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Gaussian-free-field-limits-of-weighted-integer-Lipschitz-heights-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"232-02","slug":"The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-integer-Lipschitz-heights-with-two-arc-boundary-data-October-6-2026","familyId":"232","title":"The Gaussian free field limit of integer Lipschitz heights with two-arc boundary data","abstract":"We prove that the centered uniform odd integer height function on triangular-lattice approximations of a smooth simply connected domain, with neighboring differences zero or two and boundary values +1 and −1 on two arcs, converges to a universal multiple of the Dirichlet Gaussian free field. This resolves the field part of Schramm's Problem 2.2. We give an absolutely convergent finite-volume formula for the normalization. The proof combines reflection positivity, a spectral sum rule, and boundary comparison with Gaussian moment identities.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Gaussian free field limits on bounded smooth simply connected domains for triangular-lattice height models: uniform odd heights with increments $0,\\pm2$ and two-arc boundary values $\\pm1$, and zero-boundary integer Lipschitz heights weighted by fixed $x\\in[1/\\sqrt2,1]$. Uniform real Lipschitz heights also converge to a Gaussian field; at a tuned opposite-boundary amplitude, their interface converges to chordal SLE4, establishing Schramm’s real-field/interface predictions.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-integer-Lipschitz-heights-with-two-arc-boundary-data-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-integer-Lipschitz-heights-with-two-arc-boundary-data-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-integer-Lipschitz-heights-with-two-arc-boundary-data-October-6-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-integer-Lipschitz-heights-with-two-arc-boundary-data-October-6-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-integer-Lipschitz-heights-with-two-arc-boundary-data-October-6-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-integer-Lipschitz-heights-with-two-arc-boundary-data-October-6-2026/build/sections/02-spin.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-integer-Lipschitz-heights-with-two-arc-boundary-data-October-6-2026/build/sections/03-cuts.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-integer-Lipschitz-heights-with-two-arc-boundary-data-October-6-2026/build/sections/04-moments.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-integer-Lipschitz-heights-with-two-arc-boundary-data-October-6-2026/build/sections/05-spectral.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-integer-Lipschitz-heights-with-two-arc-boundary-data-October-6-2026/build/sections/06-normalization.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-integer-Lipschitz-heights-with-two-arc-boundary-data-October-6-2026/build/sections/07-pins.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-integer-Lipschitz-heights-with-two-arc-boundary-data-October-6-2026/build/figures/pin-moving.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-integer-Lipschitz-heights-with-two-arc-boundary-data-October-6-2026/build/sections/08-boundary.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-integer-Lipschitz-heights-with-two-arc-boundary-data-October-6-2026/build/figures/boundary-arches.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-integer-Lipschitz-heights-with-two-arc-boundary-data-October-6-2026/build/sections/09-limits.tex","preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-integer-Lipschitz-heights-with-two-arc-boundary-data-October-6-2026/build/sections/10-gaussian.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-integer-Lipschitz-heights-with-two-arc-boundary-data-October-6-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the centered uniform odd integer height function on triangular-lattice approximations of a smooth simply connected domain, with neighboring differences zero or two and boundary values $+1$ and $-1$ on two arcs, converges to a universal multiple of the Dirichlet Gaussian free field. This resolves the field part of Schramm's Problem 2.2. We give an absolutely convergent finite-volume formula for the normalization. The proof combines reflection positivity, a spectral sum rule, and boundary comparison with Gaussian moment identities.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":612},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and the limit","sourceOffset":1069},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":4469},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":8747},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and limit order","sourceOffset":12849},{"kind":"section","title":"Heights, two spins, and exact cuts","sourceOffset":14194},{"kind":"section","title":"Buffered circuits, mixing, and rare pins","sourceOffset":26301},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An annulus input and local circuits","sourceOffset":26720},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Loop estimates inside simple contours","sourceOffset":37401},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Plane comparison","sourceOffset":46410},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A comparison for separated rare pin events","sourceOffset":50964},{"kind":"section","title":"Smearing estimates and conditional moments","sourceOffset":57467}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $D$ be a bounded smooth simply connected planar domain with two distinct marked boundary points. Let $D_\\delta$ be simply connected triangular-lattice polygonal approximations with uniformly converging marked boundary parametrizations and edge-midpoint marks, and let $h_\\delta$ have the uniform odd-height law \\eqref{eq:odd-model}. There is a constant $\\sigma>0$, depending only on the unit triangular-lattice model, such that \\[ h_\\delta\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ \\bar g+\\sigma\\Phi_D \\qquad\\text{in }\\mathcal D'(D). \\] For every $f\\in C_c^\\infty(D)$, $\\EE h_\\delta(f)\\to\\int_D\\bar g f$. Consequently \\begin{equation} \\frac{h_\\delta-\\EE h_\\delta}{\\sigma} \\ \\Longrightarrow\\ \\Phi_D \\qquad\\text{in }\\mathcal D'(D). \\end{equation} All finite vectors of test-function integrals converge jointly to the corresponding Gaussian vectors, and all their mixed moments converge. The constant is specified by the absolutely convergent finite-volume series in \\cref{prop:normalization-series}; in the spectral convention below, $\\sigma=4\\pi\\sqrt{c_*}$.","sourceOffset":2670,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Loop estimates","label":"prop:loop-estimates","text":"There are constants $c_0>0$ and $0<\\rho<1$ such that, for all sufficiently large integer $n$, the following statements hold. \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\roman*)] \\item If the interior of a simple contour $L$ contains $\\Lambda_{2n}(z)$, then $\\PP_L$ gives probability at least $c_0$ to a loop contained in $\\Lambda_{2n}(z)$ and surrounding $\\Lambda_n(z)$. \\item If $L$ has graph inradius $n$ about $z$, then \\begin{equation} \\PP_L\\bigl(\\text{at least two loops surround }\\Lambda_{\\rho n}(z)\\bigr) \\le 1-c_0. \\end{equation} \\end{enumerate} There is no aspect-ratio or boundary-regularity assumption on $L$.","sourceOffset":38767,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Double paths","label":null,"text":"A dual edge is double-$B$ of sign $t$ if the two primal endpoints of the crossed edge both have $B=t$. A double path or circuit uses only such edges, with one fixed sign throughout. The analogous terminology applies with the colors interchanged.","sourceOffset":23237,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":242964,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":25,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-integer-Lipschitz-heights-with-two-arc-boundary-data-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/The-Gaussian-free-field-limit-of-integer-Lipschitz-heights-with-two-arc-boundary-data-October-6-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"232-03","slug":"Uniform-real-Lipschitz-surfaces-on-the-triangular-lattice-October-6-2026","familyId":"232","title":"Uniform real Lipschitz surfaces on the triangular lattice","abstract":"We prove the Gaussian free field and SLE$_4$ scaling limits for uniformly sampled real nearest-neighbor Lipschitz heights on the triangular lattice, resolving Schramm's Problem 2.3. On approximations of smooth simply connected domains, the centered height field converges as a random distribution to a multiple of the Dirichlet Gaussian free field. At one tuned two-arc boundary amplitude, the zero-height interface converges in uniform curve distance to chordal SLE$_4$. We identify the relation between the field variance and the boundary height in terms of an implicit stationary tangent-flux coefficient.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Gaussian free field limits on bounded smooth simply connected domains for triangular-lattice height models: uniform odd heights with increments $0,\\pm2$ and two-arc boundary values $\\pm1$, and zero-boundary integer Lipschitz heights weighted by fixed $x\\in[1/\\sqrt2,1]$. Uniform real Lipschitz heights also converge to a Gaussian field; at a tuned opposite-boundary amplitude, their interface converges to chordal SLE4, establishing Schramm’s real-field/interface predictions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Uniform-real-Lipschitz-surfaces-on-the-triangular-lattice-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-real-Lipschitz-surfaces-on-the-triangular-lattice-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-real-Lipschitz-surfaces-on-the-triangular-lattice-October-6-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Uniform-real-Lipschitz-surfaces-on-the-triangular-lattice-October-6-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Uniform-real-Lipschitz-surfaces-on-the-triangular-lattice-October-6-2026/build/sections/foundations.tex","preprints/Uniform-real-Lipschitz-surfaces-on-the-triangular-lattice-October-6-2026/build/triangular-figure.tex","preprints/Uniform-real-Lipschitz-surfaces-on-the-triangular-lattice-October-6-2026/build/sections/coupling.tex","preprints/Uniform-real-Lipschitz-surfaces-on-the-triangular-lattice-October-6-2026/build/sections/cell.tex","preprints/Uniform-real-Lipschitz-surfaces-on-the-triangular-lattice-October-6-2026/build/sections/trace-analysis.tex","preprints/Uniform-real-Lipschitz-surfaces-on-the-triangular-lattice-October-6-2026/build/sections/trace.tex","preprints/Uniform-real-Lipschitz-surfaces-on-the-triangular-lattice-October-6-2026/build/sections/shift.tex","preprints/Uniform-real-Lipschitz-surfaces-on-the-triangular-lattice-October-6-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Uniform-real-Lipschitz-surfaces-on-the-triangular-lattice-October-6-2026/build/sections/interface.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Uniform-real-Lipschitz-surfaces-on-the-triangular-lattice-October-6-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Gaussian free field and SLE$_4$ scaling limits for uniformly sampled real nearest-neighbor Lipschitz heights on the triangular lattice, resolving Schramm's Problem 2.3. On approximations of smooth simply connected domains, the centered height field converges as a random distribution to a multiple of the Dirichlet Gaussian free field. At one tuned two-arc boundary amplitude, the zero-height interface converges in uniform curve distance to chordal SLE$_4$. We identify the relation between the field variance and the boundary height in terms of an implicit stationary tangent-flux coefficient.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The Model and the Theorem","sourceOffset":671},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and scope","sourceOffset":3957},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Plan of the proof","sourceOffset":7130},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions for the Estimates","sourceOffset":8539},{"kind":"section","title":"Local Slack and Macroscopic Averages","sourceOffset":10307},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite-volume laws and the moment statement","sourceOffset":10784},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A local filling-volume deficit","sourceOffset":13071},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sparse exceptions and short stationary steps","sourceOffset":22956},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Filling holes and testing macroscopic averages","sourceOffset":28440},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From deficits to an exponential moment","sourceOffset":36580},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bounded values, sign constraints, and small tilts","sourceOffset":40431},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Distributional tightness and conditioning scope","sourceOffset":49002}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are constants $A>0$, $\\sigma>0$, and $\\lambda\\in(0,1/2]$, independent of $D,a,b$, such that the model \\eqref{eq:model} has \\[ \\gamma_\\delta\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{SLE}_4(D;a,b),\\qquad \\frac{h_\\delta-\\E h_\\delta}{\\sigma} \\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{GFF}_D \\] in the stated curve metric and as random distributions, respectively. The constants and limiting mean are \\begin{align} v&=2/\\sqrt3,&\\sigma^2&=(vA)^{-1},& \\lambda^2&=\\frac{\\pi}{8vA},\\\\ m_D(z)&=\\lambda\\bigl[\\omega_D(z,A_+)-\\omega_D(z,A_-)\\bigr].&&& \\end{align} Here $\\omega_D$ is harmonic measure and $A$ is the tangent-flux coefficient defined in the cell construction. The stiffness is $vA$; no elementary closed form for it is proposed.","sourceOffset":2877,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Macroscopic exponential moments","label":"f:averages","text":"Fix $c,C>0$ and $B<\\infty$. Let $R\\ge1$ and let deterministic real weights $q_x$ obey \\begin{equation} \\sum_x|q_x|\\le C,\\qquad \\max_x|q_x|\\le CR^{-2},\\qquad \\operatorname{supp}q\\subset Q, \\quad \\operatorname{diam}Q\\le CR, \\end{equation} where $Q$ is an ambient coordinate box. Assume either $\\sum_xq_x=0$, or there is a connected set of pins $K$, of diameter at least $cR$, such that $K\\cup Q$ lies in a coordinate box of diameter at most $CR$. For a sample $h$ from $\\mu_b$, there are $a>0$ and $M<\\infty$, depending only on $c,C,B$, such that \\begin{equation} \\E\\exp\\left(a\\left|\\sum_xq_xh(x)\\right|\\right)\\le M. \\end{equation} The conclusion is uniform over mixtures of bounded pin values whenever conditioning on those values leaves exactly the specified uniform law.","sourceOffset":11604,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Controlled tangent solution","label":"b:controlled","text":"A controlled solution of slope $q\\in\\R^2$ is a space-time stationary joining of $\\mathcal E$ with real relative heights $(w_x(k))$, defined modulo a spatial additive constant at each $k\\in\\mathbb Z$, such that: \\begin{enumerate} \\item The relative heights form a spatial cocycle: after choosing $w_0=0$, they obey $w_{x+y}-w_x=w_y\\circ\\tau_x$, where $\\tau_x$ shifts the joined environment and relative field by $x$. They update by $w(k+1)=J_k w(k)$ modulo constants. \\item For every $\\delta>0$ there are spatial constants $c_R$ for which \\[ \\frac1{|\\Lambda\\cap B_R|} \\#\\{x\\in\\Lambda\\cap B_R: |w_x-q\\c","sourceOffset":117874,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Thickness of an observed set","label":"t:thickness","text":"A compact set $K\\subset\\overline Q$ containing $\\partial Q$ has continuum thickness below $r_0$ if, for each $x\\in K$ and $0<r<r_0$, there is a compact connected set \\[ x\\in L\\subset K\\cap\\overline B(x,2r),\\qquad \\operatorname{diam}L\\ge r. \\] For mesh approximants the same condition is required only for $c/n\\le r<r_0$, with fixed $c$. All uniform assertions about a family of observed sets use common constants $c,r_0$.","sourceOffset":145638,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":440783,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":25,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Uniform-real-Lipschitz-surfaces-on-the-triangular-lattice-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Uniform-real-Lipschitz-surfaces-on-the-triangular-lattice-October-6-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"233-01","slug":"The-joint-scaling-limit-of-critical-Ashkin-Teller-currents-October-6-2026","familyId":"233","title":"The joint scaling limit of critical Ashkin-Teller currents","abstract":"For each fixed point on the critical Ashkin–Teller line, including the four-state Potts endpoint, we prove the conjectured joint scaling limit of the height and both current-cluster collections in every bounded Jordan domain, for every admissible polygonal approximation. The height converges to a Gaussian free field with the predicted coupling constant, and the clusters converge to canonical recursive two-valued local sets of that same field. The joint limit includes all nesting depths and the distinguished wired boundary cluster.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Identifies the joint scaling limit of Ashkin–Teller heights and both complete current-cluster collections throughout the critical line, including the four-state Potts endpoint. In bounded Jordan domains with admissible lattice approximations and wired primal/free dual boundaries, the height converges to the predicted Gaussian free field, and the clusters to canonical recursive sets of that same field, retaining every nesting depth.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-joint-scaling-limit-of-critical-Ashkin-Teller-currents-October-6-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-joint-scaling-limit-of-critical-Ashkin-Teller-currents-October-6-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-joint-scaling-limit-of-critical-Ashkin-Teller-currents-October-6-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-joint-scaling-limit-of-critical-Ashkin-Teller-currents-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-joint-scaling-limit-of-critical-Ashkin-Teller-currents-October-6-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-joint-scaling-limit-of-critical-Ashkin-Teller-currents-October-6-2026/build/sections/02-model.tex","preprints/The-joint-scaling-limit-of-critical-Ashkin-Teller-currents-October-6-2026/build/sections/03-spatial.tex","preprints/The-joint-scaling-limit-of-critical-Ashkin-Teller-currents-October-6-2026/build/sections/04-spectral.tex","preprints/The-joint-scaling-limit-of-critical-Ashkin-Teller-currents-October-6-2026/build/sections/05-screening.tex","preprints/The-joint-scaling-limit-of-critical-Ashkin-Teller-currents-October-6-2026/build/sections/06-gaussian.tex","preprints/The-joint-scaling-limit-of-critical-Ashkin-Teller-currents-October-6-2026/build/sections/07-local-sets.tex","preprints/The-joint-scaling-limit-of-critical-Ashkin-Teller-currents-October-6-2026/build/sections/08-local-kernel.tex","preprints/The-joint-scaling-limit-of-critical-Ashkin-Teller-currents-October-6-2026/build/sections/09-identification.tex","preprints/The-joint-scaling-limit-of-critical-Ashkin-Teller-currents-October-6-2026/build/sections/10-input-conventions.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-joint-scaling-limit-of-critical-Ashkin-Teller-currents-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For each fixed point on the critical Ashkin--Teller line, including the four-state Potts endpoint, we prove the conjectured joint scaling limit of the height and both current-cluster collections in every bounded Jordan domain, for every admissible polygonal approximation. The height converges to a Gaussian free field with the predicted coupling constant, and the clusters converge to canonical recursive two-valued local sets of that same field. The joint limit includes all nesting depths and the distinguished wired boundary cluster.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":600},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work and the scope of the result","sourceOffset":2131},{"kind":"section","title":"The model and the limiting objects","sourceOffset":6724},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Parameters and lattice domains","sourceOffset":6787},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The coupled currents and their height","sourceOffset":8158},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An auxiliary representation","sourceOffset":13375},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Continuum normalization and two-valued sets","sourceOffset":15136},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Topology and the theorem","sourceOffset":19330},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Stopping and limiting conventions","sourceOffset":24259},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":25892},{"kind":"section","title":"Cuts, comparison, and spatial estimates","sourceOffset":30728},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact cuts and the comparison order","sourceOffset":33404}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Joint critical-line limit","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed pair $J>0$, $U\\le J$ with $\\sinh(2J)=e^{-2U}$, every bounded Jordan domain $D$, and every admissible polygonal approximation as defined in \\cref{sec:lattice-domains}, \\[ \\bigl(H_\\delta,\\cC_\\delta^{\\rm w},\\cC_\\delta^{\\rm f}, C_{\\partial,\\delta}\\bigr) \\ \\xrightarrow[\\delta\\downarrow0]{\\mathrm{law}}\\ \\left(\\frac{h}{\\pi\\sqrt g},\\cC^{\\rm w},\\cC^{\\rm f}, \\TVS{a}{a}(h)\\right). \\] The height converges in \\(H^s_{\\rm loc}(D)\\) for every \\(s<-1\\). Each cluster collection converges in the matching topology just defined, and the distinguished component converges in Hausdorff topology. The continuum collections are locally finite above every positive diameter. All convergences are joint, and the same GFF determines both continuum collections.","sourceOffset":22873,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Flat-domain limit","label":"thm:flat-gff","text":"Let \\(U_\\delta\\) be a single deterministic flat chamber, or a finite mesh-dependent union of independent chambers with pairwise disjoint interiors, as above. Suppose \\(F_\\delta\\) converges in Hausdorff distance to \\(F\\), and set \\(U=B_0\\setminus F\\). Then \\[ K_\\delta\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ h_U/a \\quad\\text{in }H^s_{\\mathrm{loc}}(U),\\qquad s<-1, \\] where the restrictions of \\(h_U\\) to the components of \\(U\\) are independent zero-Dirichlet GFFs with logarithmic singularity \\(-\\log|x-y|\\). All finite joint smooth-test moments with supports in \\(U\\) converge. Different limiting components are independent even if their discrete precursors were the same chamber. Either wall parity and either atomic or interpolated smearing gives the same limit. The fields extended by zero off \\(U_\\delta\\) are uniformly tight in \\(H^s_{\\mathrm{loc}}(B_0)\\) for every \\(s<-1\\).","sourceOffset":180849,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Free cluster procedure","label":"def:free-procedure","text":"For a zero-boundary GFF \\(f\\) in a Jordan domain \\(O\\), define \\(\\mathcal F_a(f,O)\\) recursively as follows. \\begin{enumerate} \\item Expose \\(\\TVS{2\\lambda}{2\\lambda}(f)\\). In every complementary component \\(V\\), write its label as \\(\\xi\\,2\\lambda\\), \\(\\xi\\in\\{-1,1\\}\\), and let \\(f_V\\) be the zero-boundary remainder. \\item If \\(\\xi=1\\), retain \\(\\TVS{2\\lambda}{2a-2\\lambda}(f_V)\\) as one cluster. If \\(\\xi=-1\\), retain \\(\\TVS{(2a-2\\lambda)}{2\\lambda}(f_V)\\). The retained set includes its outer boundary \\(\\partial V\\). \\item In each complementary component of the retained set, subtract its condit","sourceOffset":16355,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":403553,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":27,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-joint-scaling-limit-of-critical-Ashkin-Teller-currents-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"234-01","slug":"All-temperature-pressure-for-orthogonally-invariant-Ising-spin-glasses-September-25-2026","familyId":"234","title":"All-temperature pressure for orthogonally invariant Ising spin glasses","abstract":"We determine the limiting pressure of an Ising spin glass with Haar orthogonal eigenvectors, a compact limiting spectral law, and no asymptotic outliers, at every fixed temperature. The pressure converges in expectation and almost surely to a variational formula. We also give the limiting pressure with deterministic external fields whose empirical laws converge in first-moment transport distance, and derive a formula for the zero-field ground-state energy by taking temperature to zero.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives an exact variational formula for the limiting pressure of orthogonally invariant Ising spin glasses at every fixed temperature, both almost surely and in expectation. The coupling matrix is a Haar-random rotation of a deterministic spectrum converging to a compactly supported law, with extreme eigenvalues converging to its support edges. The zero-field ground-state energy follows as temperature tends to zero.","pdfPath":"preprints/All-temperature-pressure-for-orthogonally-invariant-Ising-spin-glasses-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/All-temperature-pressure-for-orthogonally-invariant-Ising-spin-glasses-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/All-temperature-pressure-for-orthogonally-invariant-Ising-spin-glasses-September-25-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/All-temperature-pressure-for-orthogonally-invariant-Ising-spin-glasses-September-25-2026/build/invariant-sk.tex","preprints/All-temperature-pressure-for-orthogonally-invariant-Ising-spin-glasses-September-25-2026/build/10-arrays.tex","preprints/All-temperature-pressure-for-orthogonally-invariant-Ising-spin-glasses-September-25-2026/build/20-field.tex","preprints/All-temperature-pressure-for-orthogonally-invariant-Ising-spin-glasses-September-25-2026/build/30-rotation.tex","preprints/All-temperature-pressure-for-orthogonally-invariant-Ising-spin-glasses-September-25-2026/build/40-upper.tex","preprints/All-temperature-pressure-for-orthogonally-invariant-Ising-spin-glasses-September-25-2026/build/50-cavity.tex","preprints/All-temperature-pressure-for-orthogonally-invariant-Ising-spin-glasses-September-25-2026/build/60-general.tex","preprints/All-temperature-pressure-for-orthogonally-invariant-Ising-spin-glasses-September-25-2026/build/70-magnetic.tex","preprints/All-temperature-pressure-for-orthogonally-invariant-Ising-spin-glasses-September-25-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/All-temperature-pressure-for-orthogonally-invariant-Ising-spin-glasses-September-25-2026/build/invariant-sk.tex","sourceAbstract":"We determine the limiting pressure of an Ising spin glass with Haar orthogonal eigenvectors, a compact limiting spectral law, and no asymptotic outliers, at every fixed temperature. The pressure converges in expectation and almost surely to a variational formula. We also give the limiting pressure with deterministic external fields whose empirical laws converge in first-moment transport distance, and derive a formula for the zero-field ground-state energy by taking temperature to zero.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":536},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and the spectral transform","sourceOffset":1338},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The variational functional","sourceOffset":2993},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context","sourceOffset":6769},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":8550},{"kind":"section","title":"Overlap arrays and cascade calculus","sourceOffset":13060},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Ghirlanda--Guerra identities","sourceOffset":13666},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite cascades and hierarchical marks","sourceOffset":23341},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Gaussian marks and passage through finite replicas","sourceOffset":32491},{"kind":"section","title":"Concavity of the Ising field functional","sourceOffset":38199},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conditional magnetizations and covariance derivatives","sourceOffset":38646},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two monotonicity properties","sourceOffset":42193}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Limiting pressure","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $\\Lambda_N$ be deterministic real diagonal matrices whose empirical spectral measures converge weakly to a compactly supported probability law $\\mu$. Assume that their smallest and largest eigenvalues converge to the respective edges of $\\supp\\mu$. Let $U_N$ be Haar distributed on $O(N)$, and define $P_N$ using the uniform spin probability in Equation \\eqref{eq:pressure}. With $S$ and $G_1$ as in Equations \\eqref{eq:S}--\\eqref{eq:G}, \\begin{equation} \\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\E P_N =\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}P_N =\\mathcal F(\\mu) :=\\inf_{p\\in\\cP}\\{S(p)+G_1(p)\\}, \\end{equation} where the second limit holds almost surely. No independence of the matrices $U_N$ across dimensions is required.","sourceOffset":4875,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Structure of joint overlap arrays","label":"arr:structure","text":"Let $\\mathbf R=(R^1,\\ldots,R^m)$ be bounded, jointly weakly exchangeable Gram arrays with deterministic diagonals, satisfying \\eqref{arr:gg}. Each scalar component has nonnegative off-diagonal entries and is ultrametric: \\[ R^a_{23}\\geq\\min(R^a_{12},R^a_{13})\\quad\\hbox{almost surely}. \\] The support of the law of $\\mathbf R_{12}$ is ordered coordinatewise. Hence this law has a representation by simultaneous nondecreasing quantiles $p_a:(0,1)\\to\\R_+$. If $Q=\\sum_a R^a$ and $p$ is its off-diagonal quantile, then $p=\\sum_a p_a$, and every $p_a$ is a measurable function of $p$. In particular, each $p_a$ is constant almost everywhere on every interval on which $p$ is constant.","sourceOffset":19224,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/234.md","scopeText":"The formalization gives variational limits for orthogonally invariant Ising spin glasses whose eigenvectors have Haar law and whose empirical eigenvalue distributions converge to a compactly supported law with the stated control of extreme eigenvalues. The pressure converges both in expectation and almost surely to an explicit functional of the limiting spectral law. A version with external fields assumes convergence of their empirical laws in Wasserstein distance and gives the corresponding magnetic-field functional.\n\nThe formalization also covers every positive temperature, a ground-state limit, random spectral data with the stated conditional Haar law, and Gaussian-pattern specializations. These hypotheses identify the invariant models to which the limits apply.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/234.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":209221,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":18,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/All-temperature-pressure-for-orthogonally-invariant-Ising-spin-glasses-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/All-temperature-pressure-for-orthogonally-invariant-Ising-spin-glasses-September-25-2026/build/invariant-sk.tex","include":"glyphtounicode.tex","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/All-temperature-pressure-for-orthogonally-invariant-Ising-spin-glasses-September-25-2026/build/invariant-sk.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex.tex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"235-01","slug":"A-Limiting-Satisfiability-Threshold-for-Every-Fixed-Clause-Size-September-25-2026","familyId":"235","title":"A Limiting Satisfiability Threshold for Every Fixed Clause Size","abstract":"For every fixed integer k ≥ 3, random k-SAT with independent uniformly signed clauses on distinct variables, sampled with replacement, has a finite positive limiting satisfiability threshold. We credit Gaia Carenini [[5]](https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2026/229/) with priority for resolving the satisfiability conjecture. This paper gives an alternative proof, using concentration of a capped last satisfiable index and a comparison between different system sizes.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For random k-SAT with independent uniformly signed proper clauses sampled with replacement, proves finite positive limiting thresholds and hitting-time variance $\\Theta_k(n)$ for every fixed k ≥ 3, and computability of the 3-SAT threshold. We credit Gaia Carenini with priority for resolving the threshold-existence conjecture in her concurrent [ECCC TR26-229](https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2026/229/), made public October 5, 2026; this family supplies another proof and the sharper variance and computability results.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Limiting-Satisfiability-Threshold-for-Every-Fixed-Clause-Size-September-25-2026/article.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Limiting-Satisfiability-Threshold-for-Every-Fixed-Clause-Size-September-25-2026/article.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Limiting-Satisfiability-Threshold-for-Every-Fixed-Clause-Size-September-25-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/A-Limiting-Satisfiability-Threshold-for-Every-Fixed-Clause-Size-September-25-2026/build/source/paper.tex","preprints/A-Limiting-Satisfiability-Threshold-for-Every-Fixed-Clause-Size-September-25-2026/build/source/preamble.tex","preprints/A-Limiting-Satisfiability-Threshold-for-Every-Fixed-Clause-Size-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-Limiting-Satisfiability-Threshold-for-Every-Fixed-Clause-Size-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/forcing.tex","preprints/A-Limiting-Satisfiability-Threshold-for-Every-Fixed-Clause-Size-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/concentration.tex","preprints/A-Limiting-Satisfiability-Threshold-for-Every-Fixed-Clause-Size-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/anchors.tex","preprints/A-Limiting-Satisfiability-Threshold-for-Every-Fixed-Clause-Size-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/convergence.tex","preprints/A-Limiting-Satisfiability-Threshold-for-Every-Fixed-Clause-Size-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/three-clause.tex","preprints/A-Limiting-Satisfiability-Threshold-for-Every-Fixed-Clause-Size-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/boundaries.tex","preprints/A-Limiting-Satisfiability-Threshold-for-Every-Fixed-Clause-Size-September-25-2026/build/source/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Limiting-Satisfiability-Threshold-for-Every-Fixed-Clause-Size-September-25-2026/build/source/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed integer \\(k\\ge3\\), random \\(k\\)-SAT with independent uniformly signed clauses on distinct variables, sampled with replacement, has a finite positive limiting satisfiability threshold. We credit Gaia Carenini \\cite{Carenini2026Polynomial} with priority for resolving the satisfiability conjecture. This paper gives an alternative proof, using concentration of a capped last satisfiable index and a comparison between different system sizes.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The limiting-threshold problem","sourceOffset":502},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work and the role of the present argument","sourceOffset":2748},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the proof reaches the threshold","sourceOffset":7560},{"kind":"section","title":"Replacement and integrated forcing","sourceOffset":10453},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Killing a solution set with one clause","sourceOffset":11516},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Replacing a shorter clause","sourceOffset":12720},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Erasing one variable and summing over time","sourceOffset":14918},{"kind":"section","title":"Clause omission and polynomial concentration","sourceOffset":18669},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Omitting a clause without changing time indices","sourceOffset":19164},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pairs of pivotal times","sourceOffset":20890},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The variance bound","sourceOffset":22839},{"kind":"section","title":"Positive and finite center bounds","sourceOffset":24653}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed integer \\(k\\ge3\\), there is a constant \\(\\alpha_k\\in(0,\\infty)\\) such that, for independent proper uniformly signed \\(k\\)-clauses sampled with replacement, \\[ \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}P_n(\\lfloor cn\\rfloor) = \\begin{cases} 1,&0\\le c<\\alpha_k,\\\\ 0,&c>\\alpha_k. \\end{cases} \\]","sourceOffset":2050,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Integrated forcing","label":"prop:forcing","text":"For every fixed integer \\(k\\ge3\\), \\begin{equation} \\sum_{j=0}^{M_n}\\EE\\frac{b(F_{n,j})}{n}=O_k(n^{1/k}), \\qquad M_n=2^{k+1}n. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":15164,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/235.md","scopeText":"The random $k$-SAT threshold problem asks whether satisfiability changes at one limiting clause density. The formalization proves that every fixed integer $k\\ge3$ has a finite positive threshold $\\alpha_k$: for every fixed density $c<\\alpha_k$, the satisfiability probability tends to one as the number of variables grows, while for every $c>\\alpha_k$ it tends to zero. Clauses use distinct variables with independent uniform signs and are sampled independently with replacement. The statement makes no assertion at the threshold itself.\n\nLet $H_n$ be the first unsatisfiable prefix length in random $k$-SAT with independent uniformly signed clauses on $k$ distinct variables, sampled with replacement. The formalization proves $\\mathrm{Var}(H_n)=\\Theta_k(n)$ for every fixed $k\\ge4$. For $k=3$, it p","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/235.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":62268,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Limiting-Satisfiability-Threshold-for-Every-Fixed-Clause-Size-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"235-02","slug":"Linear-Variance-of-the-Random-3-SAT-Hitting-Time-October-5-2026","familyId":"235","title":"Linear Variance of the Random 3-SAT Hitting Time","abstract":"For random 3-SAT on n Boolean variables, with independent uniformly signed clauses on three distinct variables sampled with replacement, we prove that the first unsatisfiable prefix has variance $\\Theta(n)$. The upper bound removes the logarithmic loss in the earlier variance estimate; the matching lower bound follows from Wilson's transition-width theorem.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For random k-SAT with independent uniformly signed proper clauses sampled with replacement, proves finite positive limiting thresholds and hitting-time variance $\\Theta_k(n)$ for every fixed k ≥ 3, and computability of the 3-SAT threshold. We credit Gaia Carenini with priority for resolving the threshold-existence conjecture in her concurrent [ECCC TR26-229](https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2026/229/), made public October 5, 2026; this family supplies another proof and the sharper variance and computability results.","pdfPath":"preprints/Linear-Variance-of-the-Random-3-SAT-Hitting-Time-October-5-2026/linear-variance-of-the-random-3-sat-hitting-time.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Linear-Variance-of-the-Random-3-SAT-Hitting-Time-October-5-2026/linear-variance-of-the-random-3-sat-hitting-time.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Linear-Variance-of-the-Random-3-SAT-Hitting-Time-October-5-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Linear-Variance-of-the-Random-3-SAT-Hitting-Time-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Linear-Variance-of-the-Random-3-SAT-Hitting-Time-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For random 3-SAT on $n$ Boolean variables, with independent uniformly signed clauses on three distinct variables sampled with replacement, we prove that the first unsatisfiable prefix has variance $\\Theta(n)$. The upper bound removes the logarithmic loss in the earlier variance estimate; the matching lower bound follows from Wilson's transition-width theorem.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":408},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and contribution","sourceOffset":1566},{"kind":"section","title":"A potential for killing assignment sets","sourceOffset":6187},{"kind":"section","title":"Clause deletion and independent root tests","sourceOffset":9823},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exposure and its two filtrations","sourceOffset":11314},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Necessary tests for root flips","sourceOffset":14600},{"kind":"section","title":"Occupation and squared deletion delays","sourceOffset":16412},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Averaging the collision costs","sourceOffset":20420},{"kind":"section","title":"The uncapped hitting time and the lower bound","sourceOffset":22561},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removing the cap","sourceOffset":22637},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Wilson's separation theorem","sourceOffset":23690}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is an absolute constant $C<\\infty$ such that \\[ \\Var(H_n)\\le Cn\\qquad(n\\ge3). \\] There are also absolute constants $c>0$ and $n_0$ such that $\\Var(H_n)\\ge cn$ for $n\\ge n_0$. In particular, $\\Var(H_n)=\\Theta(n)$ as $n\\to\\infty$.","sourceOffset":995,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Remaining duration","label":"cor:duration","text":"For $i\\le m<L$, \\begin{equation} \\E\\left[\\sum_{s=m}^{L-1}\\ind_{A_s}\\,\\middle|\\,\\cH_m\\right]\\le W_m, \\qquad W_m=\\begin{cases} \\min\\{L,Kd^\\beta p_m^{-\\beta}\\},&p_m>0,\\\\ L,&p_m=0. \\end{cases} \\end{equation} In particular, $W_m$ is measurable with respect to the smaller sigma-field $\\cG_m$.","sourceOffset":18040,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/235.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":26268,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Linear-Variance-of-the-Random-3-SAT-Hitting-Time-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Linear-Variance-of-the-Random-3-SAT-Hitting-Time-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Linear-Variance-of-the-Random-3-SAT-Hitting-Time-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"235-03","slug":"Variance-of-the-Random-k-SAT-Hitting-Time-September-27-2026","familyId":"235","title":"Variance of the Random k-SAT Hitting Time","abstract":"Let Hn be the index of the first unsatisfiable prefix in random k-SAT on n variables, with independent uniformly signed clauses using k distinct variables and sampled with replacement. For every fixed k ≥ 4, we prove $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Var}}\\nolimits (H_n)=\\Theta_k(n)$. For k = 3, the variance is bounded below by a positive multiple of n and above by a constant multiple of $n\\log n$; the companion paper on random 3-SAT sharpens this to $\\Theta(n)$. The same upper bounds proved here hold after clipping at any fixed positive multiple of n.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For random k-SAT with independent uniformly signed proper clauses sampled with replacement, proves finite positive limiting thresholds and hitting-time variance $\\Theta_k(n)$ for every fixed k ≥ 3, and computability of the 3-SAT threshold. We credit Gaia Carenini with priority for resolving the threshold-existence conjecture in her concurrent [ECCC TR26-229](https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2026/229/), made public October 5, 2026; this family supplies another proof and the sharper variance and computability results.","pdfPath":"preprints/Variance-of-the-Random-k-SAT-Hitting-Time-September-27-2026/article.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Variance-of-the-Random-k-SAT-Hitting-Time-September-27-2026/article.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Variance-of-the-Random-k-SAT-Hitting-Time-September-27-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Variance-of-the-Random-k-SAT-Hitting-Time-September-27-2026/build/source/paper.tex","preprints/Variance-of-the-Random-k-SAT-Hitting-Time-September-27-2026/build/source/preamble.tex","preprints/Variance-of-the-Random-k-SAT-Hitting-Time-September-27-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Variance-of-the-Random-k-SAT-Hitting-Time-September-27-2026/build/source/sections/replacement.tex","preprints/Variance-of-the-Random-k-SAT-Hitting-Time-September-27-2026/build/source/sections/deletion.tex","preprints/Variance-of-the-Random-k-SAT-Hitting-Time-September-27-2026/build/source/sections/occupation.tex","preprints/Variance-of-the-Random-k-SAT-Hitting-Time-September-27-2026/build/source/sections/conventions.tex","preprints/Variance-of-the-Random-k-SAT-Hitting-Time-September-27-2026/build/source/sections/lower-bounds.tex","preprints/Variance-of-the-Random-k-SAT-Hitting-Time-September-27-2026/build/source/sections/three-clause-refinements.tex","preprints/Variance-of-the-Random-k-SAT-Hitting-Time-September-27-2026/build/source/sections/replacement-sharpness.tex","preprints/Variance-of-the-Random-k-SAT-Hitting-Time-September-27-2026/build/source/sections/direct-comparison.tex","preprints/Variance-of-the-Random-k-SAT-Hitting-Time-September-27-2026/build/source/sections/appendix-c.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Variance-of-the-Random-k-SAT-Hitting-Time-September-27-2026/build/source/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let $H_n$ be the index of the first unsatisfiable prefix in random $k$-SAT on $n$ variables, with independent uniformly signed clauses using $k$ distinct variables and sampled with replacement. For every fixed $k\\ge4$, we prove $\\Var(H_n)=\\Theta_k(n)$. For $k=3$, the variance is bounded below by a positive multiple of $n$ and above by a constant multiple of $n\\log n$; the companion paper on random 3-SAT sharpens this to $\\Theta(n)$. The same upper bounds proved here hold after clipping at any fixed positive multiple of $n$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Fluctuations of the satisfiability transition","sourceOffset":575},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier work and the new estimate","sourceOffset":3638},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The route through the upper bound","sourceOffset":7150},{"kind":"section","title":"Survival of a set of assignments","sourceOffset":10533},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Replacing one shorter clause","sourceOffset":12296},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Several tests and the expected lifetime","sourceOffset":15217},{"kind":"section","title":"Clause omission and root tests","sourceOffset":19064},{"kind":"subsection","title":"What the two filtrations reveal","sourceOffset":21383},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Flipping a root gives an independent test","sourceOffset":25837},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From ordinary clauses to chronological duration","sourceOffset":28475},{"kind":"section","title":"Occupation and incidence moments","sourceOffset":31881},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Time spent above a level","sourceOffset":32878}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:variance","text":"Fix an integer $k\\ge3$, and write \\[ \\ell_k(n)=\\begin{cases}\\log(en),&k=3,\\\\1,&k\\ge4,\\end{cases} \\qquad U_k=\\frac{\\log2}{-\\log(1-2^{-k})}. \\] There are constants $C_k,c_k>0$ such that \\[ \\Var(H_n)\\le C_kn\\ell_k(n)\\quad(n\\ge k),\\qquad \\Var(H_n)\\ge c_kn\\quad\\text{for all sufficiently large }n. \\] For every fixed $B>0$ there is $C_{k,B}<\\infty$ such that \\[ \\Var(T_{n,B})\\le C_{k,B}n\\ell_k(n)\\quad(n\\ge k). \\] If $B>U_k$, then also $\\Var(T_{n,B})\\ge c_kn$ for all sufficiently large $n$, with the size cutoff allowed to depend on $k$ and $B$.","sourceOffset":1779,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Lifetime from shorter-clause killing","label":"prop:lifetime","text":"For an integer \\(k\\ge3\\), put \\[ \\beta=\\frac{k}{k-1},\\qquad K_k=\\bigl(2^k(k-1)\\bigr)^{k-1},\\qquad C_k=4(2K_k)^{1/(k-1)}. \\] For every integer \\(u\\ge k\\), every \\(S\\subseteq\\{0,1\\}^u\\), and every integer \\(d\\ge1\\), \\begin{equation} \\tau_k(S)q_d(S)^\\beta\\le C_k d^\\beta. \\end{equation} Consequently, for nonempty \\(S\\) and \\(0<a\\le q_d(S)\\), \\begin{equation} \\tau_k(S)\\le C_k d^\\beta a^{-\\beta}. \\end{equation} There is no upper restriction on \\(d\\) and no positive lower cutoff on \\(a\\).","sourceOffset":16993,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/235.md","scopeText":"The random $k$-SAT threshold problem asks whether satisfiability changes at one limiting clause density. The formalization proves that every fixed integer $k\\ge3$ has a finite positive threshold $\\alpha_k$: for every fixed density $c<\\alpha_k$, the satisfiability probability tends to one as the number of variables grows, while for every $c>\\alpha_k$ it tends to zero. Clauses use distinct variables with independent uniform signs and are sampled independently with replacement. The statement makes no assertion at the threshold itself.\n\nLet $H_n$ be the first unsatisfiable prefix length in random $k$-SAT with independent uniformly signed clauses on $k$ distinct variables, sampled with replacement. The formalization proves $\\mathrm{Var}(H_n)=\\Theta_k(n)$ for every fixed $k\\ge4$. For $k=3$, it p","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/235.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":110242,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Variance-of-the-Random-k-SAT-Hitting-Time-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Variance-of-the-Random-k-SAT-Hitting-Time-September-27-2026/build/source/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"235-04","slug":"Computing-the-Random-3-SAT-Threshold-September-27-2026","familyId":"235","title":"Computing the Random 3-SAT Threshold","abstract":"The limiting satisfiability threshold of uniform random 3-SAT is a computable real. We credit Gaia Carenini [[4]](https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2026/229/) with priority for resolving the satisfiability conjecture, which establishes the threshold's existence. We prove that one finite deterministic machine can approximate it to any prescribed accuracy. A deletion estimate gives explicit lower certificates, while a finite hierarchical approximation of the soft pressure gives upper certificates at every larger rational density. A fair search through these certificates halts without requiring a computable rate of finite-size convergence.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For random k-SAT with independent uniformly signed proper clauses sampled with replacement, proves finite positive limiting thresholds and hitting-time variance $\\Theta_k(n)$ for every fixed k ≥ 3, and computability of the 3-SAT threshold. We credit Gaia Carenini with priority for resolving the threshold-existence conjecture in her concurrent [ECCC TR26-229](https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2026/229/), made public October 5, 2026; this family supplies another proof and the sharper variance and computability results.","pdfPath":"preprints/Computing-the-Random-3-SAT-Threshold-September-27-2026/article.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Computing-the-Random-3-SAT-Threshold-September-27-2026/article.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Computing-the-Random-3-SAT-Threshold-September-27-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Computing-the-Random-3-SAT-Threshold-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Computing-the-Random-3-SAT-Threshold-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Computing-the-Random-3-SAT-Threshold-September-27-2026/build/sections/certificates.tex","preprints/Computing-the-Random-3-SAT-Threshold-September-27-2026/build/sections/proof-map.tex","preprints/Computing-the-Random-3-SAT-Threshold-September-27-2026/build/sections/probability.tex","preprints/Computing-the-Random-3-SAT-Threshold-September-27-2026/build/sections/trials.tex","preprints/Computing-the-Random-3-SAT-Threshold-September-27-2026/build/sections/sensitivity.tex","preprints/Computing-the-Random-3-SAT-Threshold-September-27-2026/build/sections/transition.tex","preprints/Computing-the-Random-3-SAT-Threshold-September-27-2026/build/figures/hidden-cuts.tex","preprints/Computing-the-Random-3-SAT-Threshold-September-27-2026/build/sections/closure.tex","preprints/Computing-the-Random-3-SAT-Threshold-September-27-2026/build/sections/assembly.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Computing-the-Random-3-SAT-Threshold-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"The limiting satisfiability threshold of uniform random 3-SAT is a computable real. We credit Gaia Carenini \\cite{Carenini2026Polynomial} with priority for resolving the satisfiability conjecture, which establishes the threshold's existence. We prove that one finite deterministic machine can approximate it to any prescribed accuracy. A deletion estimate gives explicit lower certificates, while a finite hierarchical approximation of the soft pressure gives upper certificates at every larger rational density. A fair search through these certificates halts without requiring a computable rate of finite-size convergence.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":669},{"kind":"section","title":"Two certificate searches give a computable real","sourceOffset":5672},{"kind":"section","title":"Where the certificates come from","sourceOffset":8984},{"kind":"section","title":"Probability estimates for the two certificate searches","sourceOffset":15277},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A uniform moment bound for one free deletion","sourceOffset":17803},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Unequal blocks and a summable merging error","sourceOffset":27973},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The threshold in the proper-clause model","sourceOffset":35510},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Computing the lower certificates","sourceOffset":39904},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Robust unsatisfiability and finite-temperature witnesses","sourceOffset":42711},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite trials for the pressure","sourceOffset":46993},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Trial descriptions and their evaluation","sourceOffset":48176},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A local interpolation bound","sourceOffset":55205}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a real number $\\alpha_3\\in(0,\\infty)$ such that, for every fixed real $a\\ge0$, \\[ \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}p(n,\\lfloor an\\rfloor)= \\begin{cases}1,&a<\\alpha_3,\\\\0,&a>\\alpha_3.\\end{cases} \\] There is one finite deterministic Turing machine which, on input $1^r$ for any integer $r\\ge1$, halts with a rational number $q_r$ satisfying $|q_r-\\alpha_3|\\le2^{-r}$. Its only input is the unary precision $1^r$; it uses no oracle, advice, or noncomputable real constant.","sourceOffset":1653,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"A limiting center and a one-sided error","label":"cp:lower","text":"The sequence $f_n/n$ converges to a real number $\\alpha$. For every integer $k\\ge1$, \\begin{equation} \\alpha\\ge\\frac{f_k}{k}-2^{67}k^{-1/6}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":32862,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/235.md","scopeText":"The random $k$-SAT threshold problem asks whether satisfiability changes at one limiting clause density. The formalization proves that every fixed integer $k\\ge3$ has a finite positive threshold $\\alpha_k$: for every fixed density $c<\\alpha_k$, the satisfiability probability tends to one as the number of variables grows, while for every $c>\\alpha_k$ it tends to zero. Clauses use distinct variables with independent uniform signs and are sampled independently with replacement. The statement makes no assertion at the threshold itself.\n\nLet $H_n$ be the first unsatisfiable prefix length in random $k$-SAT with independent uniformly signed clauses on $k$ distinct variables, sampled with replacement. The formalization proves $\\mathrm{Var}(H_n)=\\Theta_k(n)$ for every fixed $k\\ge4$. For $k=3$, it p","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/235.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":160949,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Computing-the-Random-3-SAT-Threshold-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Computing-the-Random-3-SAT-Threshold-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"236-01","slug":"The-sharp-factor-of-IID-threshold-for-the-free-Ising-model-on-regular-trees-September-26-2026","familyId":"236","title":"The sharp factor-of-IID threshold for the free Ising model on regular trees","abstract":"For every integer d ≥ 3 and inverse temperature β ≥ 0, the free zero-field ferromagnetic Ising measure on the infinite d-regular tree is a factor of IID exactly when $\\tanh\\beta\\le(d-1)^{-1/2}$. We prove the positive implication, including equality, resolving the conjecture of Nam, Sly and Zhang; the strict converse is known. The spin factor can be chosen to commute with every tree automorphism on every label input.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Determines when the free zero-field ferromagnetic Ising state on the infinite d-regular tree is a factor of independent vertex labels: exactly when $\\tanh\\beta\\le(d-1)^{-1/2}$, including equality, for d ≥ 3 and β ≥ 0. The construction uses no root and is almost surely equivariant for each fixed tree automorphism, resolving the ferromagnetic case of Lyons's question.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-sharp-factor-of-IID-threshold-for-the-free-Ising-model-on-regular-trees-September-26-2026/article.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-sharp-factor-of-IID-threshold-for-the-free-Ising-model-on-regular-trees-September-26-2026/article.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-sharp-factor-of-IID-threshold-for-the-free-Ising-model-on-regular-trees-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-sharp-factor-of-IID-threshold-for-the-free-Ising-model-on-regular-trees-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-sharp-factor-of-IID-threshold-for-the-free-Ising-model-on-regular-trees-September-26-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-sharp-factor-of-IID-threshold-for-the-free-Ising-model-on-regular-trees-September-26-2026/build/sections/02-posterior-fields.tex","preprints/The-sharp-factor-of-IID-threshold-for-the-free-Ising-model-on-regular-trees-September-26-2026/build/sections/03-critical-information.tex","preprints/The-sharp-factor-of-IID-threshold-for-the-free-Ising-model-on-regular-trees-September-26-2026/build/sections/04-innovations.tex","preprints/The-sharp-factor-of-IID-threshold-for-the-free-Ising-model-on-regular-trees-September-26-2026/build/sections/05-coupling.tex","preprints/The-sharp-factor-of-IID-threshold-for-the-free-Ising-model-on-regular-trees-September-26-2026/build/sections/06-spectrum.tex","preprints/The-sharp-factor-of-IID-threshold-for-the-free-Ising-model-on-regular-trees-September-26-2026/build/sections/07-path-averages.tex","preprints/The-sharp-factor-of-IID-threshold-for-the-free-Ising-model-on-regular-trees-September-26-2026/build/figures/half-path-averages.tex","preprints/The-sharp-factor-of-IID-threshold-for-the-free-Ising-model-on-regular-trees-September-26-2026/build/sections/08-critical-comparison.tex","preprints/The-sharp-factor-of-IID-threshold-for-the-free-Ising-model-on-regular-trees-September-26-2026/build/sections/08b-recovery.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-sharp-factor-of-IID-threshold-for-the-free-Ising-model-on-regular-trees-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every integer \\(d\\ge3\\) and inverse temperature \\(\\beta\\ge0\\), the free zero-field ferromagnetic Ising measure on the infinite \\(d\\)-regular tree is a factor of IID exactly when \\(\\tanh\\beta\\le(d-1)^{-1/2}\\). We prove the positive implication, including equality, resolving the conjecture of Nam, Sly and Zhang; the strict converse is known. The spin factor can be chosen to commute with every tree automorphism on every label input.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":483},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why independent innovations determine the spins","sourceOffset":6213},{"kind":"subsection","title":"What changes at the critical point","sourceOffset":9120},{"kind":"section","title":"Posterior fields along the tree","sourceOffset":12989},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite posterior and its branch messages","sourceOffset":14699},{"kind":"section","title":"Critical information controls the response row","sourceOffset":24912},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Combining symmetric binary signals","sourceOffset":26289},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The amount of information in one component","sourceOffset":29266},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Attenuation from an intact side branch","sourceOffset":35401},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Time-integrable response rows","sourceOffset":41143},{"kind":"section","title":"Constructing the infinite innovation equation","sourceOffset":43512},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Brownian innovations and finite posterior energy","sourceOffset":48600}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $d\\ge3$ and every $\\beta\\ge0$, the free zero-field Ising law $\\mu_{d,\\beta}$ is a factor of IID in the sense of \\eqref{eq:equivariance} if and only if \\[ \\tanh\\beta\\le(d-1)^{-1/2}. \\] In this range the factor can be chosen to commute with every automorphism on every label input.","sourceOffset":2345,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Time-integrable response rows","label":"prop:row-energy","text":"For every vertex \\(v\\), there is \\(C<\\infty\\), depending only on \\(d\\) at the critical parameter, such that \\begin{equation} \\begin{aligned} A_v(t)&\\le1+\\frac{d\\theta^2}{1-b\\theta^2} &&\\text{if }b\\theta^2<1,\\quad t\\ge0,\\\\ \\E A_v(t)&\\le\\frac{C}{\\omega(t)} &&\\text{if }b\\theta^2=1,\\quad t>0,\\\\ \\E\\int_0^T A_v(t)\\dd t&<\\infty &&\\text{for every }T<\\infty\\text{ in either case}. \\end{aligned} \\end{equation} The same fixed-time and integrated upper bounds hold with \\(\\sum_u C_{vu}(t)^2\\) in place of \\(A_v(t)\\).","sourceOffset":41567,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/236.md","scopeText":"The formalized result determines the factor-of-IID threshold for the free zero-field Ising law on the infinite $d$-regular tree. For every $d\\ge3$ and $\\beta\\ge0$, the law is a factor of IID exactly when $\\tanh\\beta\\le1/\\sqrt{d-1}$, including equality. The factor uses independent uniform vertex labels and is almost surely equivariant for each fixed tree automorphism. Finitary coding and coding-radius bounds are not included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/236.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":165746,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-sharp-factor-of-IID-threshold-for-the-free-Ising-model-on-regular-trees-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"237-01","slug":"Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026","familyId":"237","title":"Radial transfer estimates and polygon length laws for honeycomb walks","abstract":"We prove critical diameter-tail exponents −2 for unrooted honeycomb polygons and −2/3 for length-weighted polygons, together with a truncated second-length-moment bound of exponent 2/3. Consequently, polygons conditioned to have length at least n have diameter $n^{3/4+o(1)}$ in probability under either weight.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the diameter form of Nienhuis's predicted three-quarter exponent: a uniformly chosen n-step self-avoiding walk on the honeycomb lattice has diameter $n^{3/4+o(1)}$. Its local mass and covering numbers have exponent 4/3. These estimates hold at every sufficiently large fixed length, simultaneously across scales, with arbitrarily high polynomial probability.","pdfPath":"preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/intro.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/finite.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/figures/local-cells.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/pressures.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/angular.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/radial.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/determinant.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/plateau.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/tails.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/two-01-scalar.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/two-02-pfaffian.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/two-03-angular-algebra.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/two-04-confluence.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/two-05-radial-phases.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/two-06-coercivity.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/two-07-partial-trace.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/two-08-hierarchy.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/figures/radial-regions.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/two-09-geometry.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/two-10-cutoff-consequences.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/boundary.tex","preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove critical diameter-tail exponents $-2$ for unrooted honeycomb polygons and $-2/3$ for length-weighted polygons, together with a truncated second-length-moment bound of exponent $2/3$. Consequently, polygons conditioned to have length at least $n$ have diameter $n^{3/4+o(1)}$ in probability under either weight.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Polygon size and the role of a marked edge","sourceOffset":382},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the main difficulty","sourceOffset":5296},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From finite cylinders to positive polygons","sourceOffset":8310},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite transfer identities","sourceOffset":13049},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local weights and the identities they satisfy","sourceOffset":13105},{"kind":"subsubsection","title":"Crossing and inverse transport","sourceOffset":16358},{"kind":"subsubsection","title":"Exchanging three adjacent columns","sourceOffset":17837},{"kind":"subsubsection","title":"Fusion to a single spin","sourceOffset":19947},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cylinder states and the physical transfer limit","sourceOffset":22619},{"kind":"subsubsection","title":"Rows, twists, and fusion","sourceOffset":23053},{"kind":"subsubsection","title":"Noncrossing pairings and loop weights","sourceOffset":25364},{"kind":"subsubsection","title":"The honeycomb weights","sourceOffset":30914}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Diameter tails and the second length moment","label":"thm:polygon-tails","text":"For the polygon classes and weights just defined, put \\[ A(r)=\\sum_{D(\\gamma)\\ge r}w(\\gamma),\\qquad P(r)=\\sum_{D(\\gamma)\\ge r}L(\\gamma)w(\\gamma),\\qquad M_2(r)=\\sum_{D(\\gamma)\\le r}L(\\gamma)^2w(\\gamma). \\] Then, as $r\\to\\infty$, \\begin{equation} A(r)=r^{-2+o(1)},\\qquad P(r)=r^{-2/3+o(1)},\\qquad M_2(r)\\le r^{2/3+o(1)}. \\end{equation} Every sum in this statement is finite. Here $f(r)=r^{a+o(1)}$ means that for every $\\varepsilon>0$, $r^{a-\\varepsilon}\\le f(r)\\le r^{a+\\varepsilon}$ for all sufficiently large $r$; the one-sided notation has the analogous meaning.","sourceOffset":1533,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Length cutoffs","label":"cor:length-cutoffs","text":"The length tails satisfy \\[ \\sum_{L(\\gamma)\\ge n}w(\\gamma)=n^{-3/2+o(1)},\\qquad \\sum_{L(\\gamma)\\ge n}L(\\gamma)w(\\gamma)=n^{-1/2+o(1)}. \\] Under either normalized weight $w(\\gamma)$ or $L(\\gamma)w(\\gamma)$, conditioned on $L(\\gamma)\\ge n$, one has $D(\\gamma)=n^{3/4+o(1)}$ in probability. The same conclusion holds with the length restriction $n\\le L(\\gamma)<n^{1+\\delta}$ for any fixed $\\delta>0$. Under the length-weighted law conditioned on $D(\\gamma)\\ge r$, one has $L(\\gamma)=r^{4/3+o(1)}$ in probability. An assertion $X_t=t^{a+o(1)}$ in probability means that its value lies between $t^{a-\\varepsilon}$ and $t^{a+\\varepsilon}$ with probability tending to one for every $\\varepsilon>0$.","sourceOffset":2670,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/237.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":365131,"sourceFileCount":23,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Radial-transfer-estimates-and-polygon-length-laws-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"237-02","slug":"Critical-honeycomb-chords-with-prescribed-boundary-endpoints-September-26-2026","familyId":"237","title":"Critical honeycomb chords with prescribed boundary endpoints","abstract":"Critical self-avoiding walks between prescribed, macroscopically separated boundary ports of a regular honeycomb hexagon have length $R^{4/3+o(1)}$ in probability, where R is the scale of the hexagon. We prove the corresponding statements for half-plane arches, parallel cuts and nonparallel pure cuts. The half-plane law also has mean length $R^{4/3+o(1)}$. A separate strip argument gives endpoint mean laws on one density-one set of heights and in an aligned, critically weighted mixture of all even heights in a macroscopic interval.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the diameter form of Nienhuis's predicted three-quarter exponent: a uniformly chosen n-step self-avoiding walk on the honeycomb lattice has diameter $n^{3/4+o(1)}$. Its local mass and covering numbers have exponent 4/3. These estimates hold at every sufficiently large fixed length, simultaneously across scales, with arbitrarily high polynomial probability.","pdfPath":"preprints/Critical-honeycomb-chords-with-prescribed-boundary-endpoints-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Critical-honeycomb-chords-with-prescribed-boundary-endpoints-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Critical-honeycomb-chords-with-prescribed-boundary-endpoints-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Critical-honeycomb-chords-with-prescribed-boundary-endpoints-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Critical-honeycomb-chords-with-prescribed-boundary-endpoints-September-26-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Critical-honeycomb-chords-with-prescribed-boundary-endpoints-September-26-2026/build/sections/inputs.tex","preprints/Critical-honeycomb-chords-with-prescribed-boundary-endpoints-September-26-2026/build/sections/window.tex","preprints/Critical-honeycomb-chords-with-prescribed-boundary-endpoints-September-26-2026/build/sections/same-side.tex","preprints/Critical-honeycomb-chords-with-prescribed-boundary-endpoints-September-26-2026/build/sections/strip-smoothness.tex","preprints/Critical-honeycomb-chords-with-prescribed-boundary-endpoints-September-26-2026/build/sections/parallel-atoms.tex","preprints/Critical-honeycomb-chords-with-prescribed-boundary-endpoints-September-26-2026/build/sections/tilted.tex","preprints/Critical-honeycomb-chords-with-prescribed-boundary-endpoints-September-26-2026/build/sections/mean.tex","preprints/Critical-honeycomb-chords-with-prescribed-boundary-endpoints-September-26-2026/build/sections/pvmeans.tex","preprints/Critical-honeycomb-chords-with-prescribed-boundary-endpoints-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Critical-honeycomb-chords-with-prescribed-boundary-endpoints-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Critical self-avoiding walks between prescribed, macroscopically separated boundary ports of a regular honeycomb hexagon have length $R^{4/3+o(1)}$ in probability, where $R$ is the scale of the hexagon. We prove the corresponding statements for half-plane arches, parallel cuts and nonparallel pure cuts. The half-plane law also has mean length $R^{4/3+o(1)}$. A separate strip argument gives endpoint mean laws on one density-one set of heights and in an aligned, critically weighted mixture of all even heights in a macroscopic interval.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":601},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite estimates and the irreducible law","sourceOffset":8932},{"kind":"section","title":"A prescribed source and a terminal interval","sourceOffset":14804},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Interior connectors","sourceOffset":15254},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bridge length and displacement windows","sourceOffset":22109},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The cost of two translation choices","sourceOffset":27238},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Recoverable attachments","sourceOffset":29707},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof of the interval length law","sourceOffset":36541},{"kind":"section","title":"Two prescribed ports on one line","sourceOffset":38390},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Common blocks and lateral concentration","sourceOffset":39503},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Regularity of the joining kernel","sourceOffset":44790},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Concentration under the prescribed-gap law","sourceOffset":48791}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Prescribed boundary endpoints","label":"c:main","text":"Let $D_R$ be convex pure lattice hexagons which, after scaling by $R$, converge to a regular hexagon. Let $a_R,b_R$ be boundary ports whose scaled limits are distinct points in the relative interiors of sides. Then, for every $\\varepsilon>0$, the critical chord law satisfies \\[ \\mathbb P_{D_R;a_R,b_R} \\{R^{4/3-\\varepsilon}\\le L(\\gamma)\\le R^{4/3+\\varepsilon}\\} \\longrightarrow1. \\] The same conclusion holds in a full half-plane for prescribed boundary ports at distance $R$, in a strip of height comparable to $R$ for one prescribed port on each of the two opposite boundary cuts, at mutual distance $O(R)$, and in a wedge of angle $\\pi/3$ or $2\\pi/3$ formed by two nonparallel pure cuts, with one prescribed port on each ray. In the wedge, each endpoint has clearance comparable to $R$ from the other cut and distance $O(R)$ from the apex. Constants are uniform on fixed compact sets of these nondegenerate scaled geometries. In each case the normalizing mass is at least $cR^{-5/4}$.","sourceOffset":2125,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Finite boundary and length inputs","label":"c:finite","text":"The following estimates hold in the port convention above. \\begin{enumerate} \\item For every integer $h\\ge0$, $B_h\\asymp(1+h)^{-1/4}$. For all sufficiently large integers $h$, the length-weighted bridge mass is at most $Ch^{13/12}$, and $B_h\\{L\\ge ch^{4/3}\\}\\ge ch^{-1/4}$. For every integer $h\\ge1$ and every $r\\ge0$, the mass of bridges of diameter exceeding $r$ is at most $C(1+r)^{-1/4}$. \\item In a convex pure domain, the mass from one boundary source of completed paths of diameter at least $r$ is at most $C(1+r)^{-1/4}$ for $r\\ge0$. For the half-plane arch kernel and integer $l\\ge1$, $K_l\\asymp l^{-5/4}$ and $K_{l+1}\\le K_l$. The same monotonicity comparison is valid along one straight side of a convex pure truncation whenever both compared adjacent ports remain on that side. Arches of diameter at most $Al$ retain mass $cl^{-5/4}$ for a fixed $A$. \\item From a prescribed port $a$ with inward pure normal $n$, consider the terminal line $m\\cdot(z-a)=s$, where $m$ is its outward pure unit normal and the angle between $n$ and $m$ is at most $\\pi/3$. Here $s>0$ is the Euclidean distance from $a$ to the terminal line. Crossings that stay in the initial half-plane and before the terminal line until their final exit have mass comparable to $s^{-1/4}$. For any fixed positive relative tolerance, and all sufficiently large admissible $s$ with a threshold depending only on that tolerance, the lower bound can be confined within distance equal to that tolerance times $s$ of the segment ","sourceOffset":9463,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/237.md","scopeText":"The linked formalization establishes finiteness of the critical bridge measures used in the paper. For every strip height $h\\ge1$, the total weight and first length moment of self-avoiding honeycomb bridges from a fixed initial port to a free terminal port are finite. The corresponding sums are also finite when the bridge is confined to the corridor of horizontal width $h(\\log h)^2$, including the normalized first-moment sums.\n\nThese are supporting summability statements. The paper's $h^{4/3+o(1)}$ mean-length law and its central-visit and hexagonal-chord exponents are outside them.\n\nThe linked formalization proves existence of the infinite-length free energy for the critical honeycomb self-avoiding-walk partition function. For every starting vertex, force direction, and real force paramet","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/237.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":155407,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":18,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Critical-honeycomb-chords-with-prescribed-boundary-endpoints-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"237-03","slug":"Cylinder-loop-weights-and-planar-nesting-September-26-2026","familyId":"237","title":"Cylinder loop weights and planar nesting","abstract":"We determine the growth exponent, at every fixed positive loop fugacity, of the critical honeycomb partition function for disjoint polygons separating two prescribed markers on a balanced cylinder. At fugacity two the cylinder exponent is 1/6; the corresponding planar nesting exponent and middle-strip nesting exponent are 1/12.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the diameter form of Nienhuis's predicted three-quarter exponent: a uniformly chosen n-step self-avoiding walk on the honeycomb lattice has diameter $n^{3/4+o(1)}$. Its local mass and covering numbers have exponent 4/3. These estimates hold at every sufficiently large fixed length, simultaneously across scales, with arbitrarily high polynomial probability.","pdfPath":"preprints/Cylinder-loop-weights-and-planar-nesting-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Cylinder-loop-weights-and-planar-nesting-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Cylinder-loop-weights-and-planar-nesting-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Cylinder-loop-weights-and-planar-nesting-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Cylinder-loop-weights-and-planar-nesting-September-26-2026/build/sections/00_introduction.tex","preprints/Cylinder-loop-weights-and-planar-nesting-September-26-2026/build/sections/02_cylinder.tex","preprints/Cylinder-loop-weights-and-planar-nesting-September-26-2026/build/sections/03_gas_mean.tex","preprints/Cylinder-loop-weights-and-planar-nesting-September-26-2026/build/sections/03_reference.tex","preprints/Cylinder-loop-weights-and-planar-nesting-September-26-2026/build/sections/04_gas_fluctuations.tex","preprints/Cylinder-loop-weights-and-planar-nesting-September-26-2026/build/sections/04b_interpolation.tex","preprints/Cylinder-loop-weights-and-planar-nesting-September-26-2026/build/sections/04c_exponent.tex","preprints/Cylinder-loop-weights-and-planar-nesting-September-26-2026/build/sections/06_all_count.tex","preprints/Cylinder-loop-weights-and-planar-nesting-September-26-2026/build/sections/05_pressure.tex","preprints/Cylinder-loop-weights-and-planar-nesting-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Cylinder-loop-weights-and-planar-nesting-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We determine the growth exponent, at every fixed positive loop fugacity, of the critical honeycomb partition function for disjoint polygons separating two prescribed markers on a balanced cylinder. At fugacity two the cylinder exponent is $1/6$; the corresponding planar nesting exponent and middle-strip nesting exponent are $1/12$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":396},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and its exponents","sourceOffset":972},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform counts and path length","sourceOffset":4839},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":6684},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier work","sourceOffset":11013},{"kind":"section","title":"Cylinder partition functions and contour sectors","sourceOffset":14320},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Strip input and the critical walk bound","sourceOffset":16209},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The polynomial cylinder vacuum","sourceOffset":19774},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Convergence of the physical cylinder state","sourceOffset":35165},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite separating-loop formula","sourceOffset":42644},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Contour sectors and residue cancellation","sourceOffset":58352},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Pfaffian denominator at confluence","sourceOffset":71396}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Cylinder fugacity exponent","label":"thm:04-cylinder-exponent","text":"For every fixed $\\chi>0$, along even $N$, \\[ Z_N(\\chi)=N^{\\tau(\\chi)+o(1)},\\qquad \\tau(\\chi)= \\begin{cases} \\displaystyle\\frac16-\\frac{2}{3\\pi^2} \\bigl(\\arccos(\\chi/2)\\bigr)^2,&0<\\chi\\le2,\\\\[4pt] \\displaystyle\\frac16+\\frac{2}{3\\pi^2} \\bigl(\\operatorname{arcosh}(\\chi/2)\\bigr)^2,&\\chi\\ge2. \\end{cases} \\]","sourceOffset":3141,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Critical weight","label":"prop:02-critical","text":"If $c_n$ is the number of $n$-edge self-avoiding walks from a specified honeycomb vertex and $\\mu=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}c_n^{1/n}$, then $\\rho=\\mu^{-1}$. Moreover, $c_n\\rho^n=\\exp(o(n))$ as an upper bound.","sourceOffset":16524,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/237.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":332119,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":24,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Cylinder-loop-weights-and-planar-nesting-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Cylinder-loop-weights-and-planar-nesting-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode.tex","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Cylinder-loop-weights-and-planar-nesting-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex.tex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"237-04","slug":"Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026","familyId":"237","title":"Mass and covering exponents for fixed-length honeycomb walks","abstract":"For uniform self-avoiding walks of every sufficiently large integer length on the honeycomb lattice, we prove diameter exponent 3/4 and simultaneous local-mass and covering exponent 4/3, with arbitrary positive exponent slack and arbitrary polynomial failure probability.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the diameter form of Nienhuis's predicted three-quarter exponent: a uniformly chosen n-step self-avoiding walk on the honeycomb lattice has diameter $n^{3/4+o(1)}$. Its local mass and covering numbers have exponent 4/3. These estimates hold at every sufficiently large fixed length, simultaneously across scales, with arbitrarily high polynomial probability.","pdfPath":"preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/glyphtounicode-cmex-used.tex","preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/00_introduction.tex","preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/01_inputs.tex","preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/02_full_box.tex","preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/figures/cone-row.tex","preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/03_sewing.tex","preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/figures/exterior-closures.tex","preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/figures/corner.tex","preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/04_renewal.tex","preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/05_probes.tex","preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/figures/hairpin.tex","preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/figures/paired-order.tex","preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/06_fast.tex","preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/07_slow.tex","preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/09_completion.tex","preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/A_asynchronous.tex","preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For uniform self-avoiding walks of every sufficiently large integer length on the honeycomb lattice, we prove diameter exponent $3/4$ and simultaneous local-mass and covering exponent $4/3$, with arbitrary positive exponent slack and arbitrary polynomial failure probability.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":321},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and the meaning of the exponents","sourceOffset":900},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Critical weights and the geometric argument","sourceOffset":4585},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":8739},{"kind":"section","title":"Critical masses and the analytic inputs","sourceOffset":11476},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ports, strips and local endpoint changes","sourceOffset":11880},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Strip flux and nesting","sourceOffset":15586},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Marked polygons and arcs","sourceOffset":18694},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A tail for every polygon count","sourceOffset":20845},{"kind":"section","title":"The critical mass of paths in a box","sourceOffset":22115},{"kind":"section","title":"Localized bridges, recoverable cuts, and length moments","sourceOffset":35552},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Localization and recoverable seams","sourceOffset":37236}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Fixed-length mass and covering exponents","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $\\delta>0$ and $k>0$, there are constants $C_{\\delta,k}<\\infty$ and $n_0(\\delta,k)$ such that, for every integer $n\\ge n_0(\\delta,k)$, an event of $\\Prob_n$-probability at least $1-C_{\\delta,k}n^{-k}$ has all the following properties: \\begin{align} n^{3/4-\\delta}&\\le D(\\gamma)\\le n^{3/4+\\delta},\\\\ n^{-\\delta}\\min\\{n,s^{4/3}\\} &\\le M_\\gamma(z,s)\\le n^{\\delta}\\min\\{n,s^{4/3}\\},\\\\ n^{-\\delta}(1+ns^{-4/3}) &\\le \\mathcal N_\\gamma(s)\\le n^{\\delta}(1+ns^{-4/3}). \\end{align} The last two bounds hold simultaneously for every $z\\in V(\\gamma)$ and every real $s\\in[1,n]$ (with $z$ relevant only to \\eqref{eq:main-mass}).","sourceOffset":1710,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Span in every lattice normal","label":"cor:directional-span","text":"For every \\(\\delta,k>0\\), outside an event of \\(\\Prob_n\\)-probability \\(O_{\\delta,k}(n^{-k})\\), the projection of \\(V(\\gamma)\\) onto each unit normal to a direction of triangular-lattice edges has span at least \\(n^{3/4-\\delta}\\). This holds for every sufficiently large integer \\(n\\).","sourceOffset":2782,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/237.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":260860,"sourceFileCount":18,"statementCount":22,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Mass-and-covering-exponents-for-fixed-length-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"237-05","slug":"Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026","familyId":"237","title":"Signed cylinder propagation and marked polygons on the honeycomb lattice","abstract":"At the critical activity and loop fugacity two, we prove a 1/6 partition exponent for disjoint honeycomb polygons separating opposite marks on a balanced infinite cylinder, and a 1/12 planar nesting exponent. For ordered first-exit chords in a regular hexagon of side R, with both boundary ports summed and diameter at least $R/10$, the finite critical partition is $R^{3/4+o(1)}$ and the normalized mean length is $R^{4/3+o(1)}$. A separate two-bond estimate on tilted cylinders with controlled site proportions bounds the length-square mass of planar polygons of diameter at most H, modulo translations, by $H^{2/3+o(1)}$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the diameter form of Nienhuis's predicted three-quarter exponent: a uniformly chosen n-step self-avoiding walk on the honeycomb lattice has diameter $n^{3/4+o(1)}$. Its local mass and covering numbers have exponent 4/3. These estimates hold at every sufficiently large fixed length, simultaneously across scales, with arbitrarily high polynomial probability.","pdfPath":"preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/figures/cylinder.tex","preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/local.tex","preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/c7-02-finite.tex","preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/c7-03-contour.tex","preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/c7-04-boson.tex","preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/c7-05-stability.tex","preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/c7-06-compact.tex","preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/trace-lemma.tex","preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/c7-07-pfaffian.tex","preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/cylinder-exponent.tex","preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/c7-01-boundary.tex","preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/c7-08-localization.tex","preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/figures/localization.tex","preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/c7-09-marked.tex","preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/c7-10-markedgas.tex","preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/c7-11-shifted.tex","preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/c7-12-mixed.tex","preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"At the critical activity and loop fugacity two, we prove a $1/6$ partition exponent for disjoint honeycomb polygons separating opposite marks on a balanced infinite cylinder, and a $1/12$ planar nesting exponent. For ordered first-exit chords in a regular hexagon of side $R$, with both boundary ports summed and diameter at least $R/10$, the finite critical partition is $R^{3/4+o(1)}$ and the normalized mean length is $R^{4/3+o(1)}$. A separate two-bond estimate on tilted cylinders with controlled site proportions bounds the length-square mass of planar polygons of diameter at most $H$, modulo translations, by $H^{2/3+o(1)}$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":706},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The physical observables","sourceOffset":1287},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier work","sourceOffset":6890},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":10045},{"kind":"section","title":"Boundary balance and local transfer identities","sourceOffset":17353},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An auxiliary cylinder and spectral equation","sourceOffset":19842},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local exchange and fusion","sourceOffset":22913},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A Fourier identity for the scalar kernels","sourceOffset":30870},{"kind":"section","title":"The two-puncture observable and its finite spin formula","sourceOffset":32155},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The oriented observable and its vacuum limits","sourceOffset":33904},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fusion and the algebraic vacuum projector","sourceOffset":38175},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The scalar normalizers","sourceOffset":42104}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Cylinder pressure and planar nesting","label":"j:cylinder","text":"As $m\\to\\infty$ through even integers, \\[ G_m(2)=m^{1/6+o(1)},\\qquad -\\left.\\frac{d^2}{dg^2}\\log G_m(g)\\right|_{g=0}=O(\\log m). \\] The derivative is the total basic weight of ordered incompatible pairs of separating cycles, including a cycle paired with itself. Locally uniformly for complex $\\Theta$ with $|\\Re\\Theta|<\\pi$, \\[ |G_m(2\\cos\\Theta)| \\le m^{\\Re(1/6-2\\Theta^2/(3\\pi^2))+o(1)}. \\] The planar partition satisfies $\\mathcal Z(R)=R^{1/12+o(1)}$ as $R$ tends to infinity through integers.","sourceOffset":5218,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Finite chords and marked polygons","label":"j:hexagon","text":"For the chord law just defined, \\[ Z_R^{\\rm chord}=R^{3/4+o(1)},\\qquad \\mathbb E_R^{\\rm chord}N=R^{4/3+o(1)}. \\] Uniformly for honeycomb vertices $v$ at distance at most $R/10$ from the hexagon center, \\[ \\mathbb P_R^{\\rm chord}(v\\in\\gamma)=R^{-2/3+o(1)}. \\] Furthermore, for planar polygons modulo lattice translations, \\begin{equation} \\sum_{\\operatorname{diam}\\lambda\\le H} x^{|\\lambda|}|\\lambda|^2\\le H^{2/3+o(1)}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":5805,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/237.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":427029,"sourceFileCount":20,"statementCount":18,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Signed-cylinder-propagation-and-marked-polygons-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"237-06","slug":"Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026","familyId":"237","title":"Cylinder amplitudes and logarithmic bridge-length windows on the honeycomb lattice","abstract":"We prove a logarithmic window of critical honeycomb bridge lengths: summed through height $2h(\\log h)^{1/64}$, bridges of lengths between $h^{4/3}(\\log h)^{-1/8}$ and $2h^{4/3}(\\log h)^{1/2}$ have mass at least $h^{3/4}(\\log h)^{-C}$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the diameter form of Nienhuis's predicted three-quarter exponent: a uniformly chosen n-step self-avoiding walk on the honeycomb lattice has diameter $n^{3/4+o(1)}$. Its local mass and covering numbers have exponent 4/3. These estimates hold at every sufficiently large fixed length, simultaneously across scales, with arbitrarily high polynomial probability.","pdfPath":"preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/01.tex","preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/figures/local-states.tex","preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/02.tex","preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/03.tex","preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/04.tex","preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/05.tex","preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/06.tex","preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/07.tex","preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/08.tex","preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/09.tex","preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/figures/two-scale.tex","preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/10.tex","preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/figures/cap-comparison.tex","preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/11.tex","preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/figures/connector.tex","preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a logarithmic window of critical honeycomb bridge lengths: summed through height $2h(\\log h)^{1/64}$, bridges of lengths between $h^{4/3}(\\log h)^{-1/8}$ and $2h^{4/3}(\\log h)^{1/2}$ have mass at least $h^{3/4}(\\log h)^{-C}$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":280},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The analytic obstacle and the geometric transfer","sourceOffset":4438},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier work and the role of exact normalization","sourceOffset":7126},{"kind":"section","title":"A periodic loop calculation at finite circumference","sourceOffset":10853},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Diagram identities","sourceOffset":23910},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Polynomial denominators","sourceOffset":34154},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Gluing rules","sourceOffset":49512},{"kind":"section","title":"Change of expansion for the color sums","sourceOffset":59778},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Charge space and the formal trace","sourceOffset":60799},{"kind":"subsubsection","title":"Color shifts and the change of twist","sourceOffset":63720},{"kind":"subsubsection","title":"Finite support at the trace cut","sourceOffset":68414},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Gaussian change of channel","sourceOffset":69874}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"A logarithmic bridge-length window","label":"L:thm:window","text":"There are constants $c,C>0$ and $h_0$ such that, for every real $h\\ge h_0$, with \\[ R_h=h(\\log h)^{1/64},\\qquad \\ell_-(h)=h^{4/3}(\\log h)^{-1/8},\\qquad \\ell_+(h)=h^{4/3}(\\log h)^{1/2}, \\] we have \\[ \\sum_{1\\le H\\le 2R_h} \\sum_{\\substack{\\omega\\in\\mathcal B_H\\\\ \\ell_-(h)\\le|\\omega|\\le2\\ell_+(h)}}\\mu(\\omega) \\ge c h^{3/4}(\\log h)^{-C}. \\] The sum over $H$ is over integer lattice heights.","sourceOffset":1886,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Finite boundary and polygon estimates","label":"L:thm:finite","text":"There are positive constants, independent of the integer parameters, such that \\[ B_H\\asymp H^{-1/4},\\qquad A_g\\asymp g^{-5/4}\\quad(H,g\\ge1). \\] The lower bound for $A_g$ can be restricted to arches of diameter at most $C g$, for a fixed sufficiently large $C$. For every sufficiently large fixed $T$, bridges of height $H$ whose transverse displacement from the starting normal stays at most $TH$ have mass at least $c_T H^{-1/4}$ and length-weighted mass at most $C_T H^{13/12}$. Either weak sign of terminal transverse displacement may be specified in the mass lower bound. For arches and bridges from a fixed wall port in a strip of height $H$, the total mass of paths with diameter exceeding $r\\ge C H$ is at most \\[ C H^{-1/4}e^{-c r/H}. \\] Finally, for all sufficiently large $R$, \\[ \\sum_{R\\le\\diam P<2R}\\mu(P)|P|^2 \\le C R^{2/3}(1+\\log\\log R). \\]","sourceOffset":3117,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/237.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":364422,"sourceFileCount":18,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Cylinder-amplitudes-and-logarithmic-bridge-length-windows-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"237-07","slug":"Marked-polygon-correlations-and-one-arc-bounds-September-26-2026","familyId":"237","title":"Marked polygon correlations and one-arc bounds","abstract":"At the critical honeycomb vertex activity, the squared-length mass of simple polygons of diameter at most H, counted modulo translations, is at most $H^{2/3+o(1)}$. We also prove a quantitative two-mark cylinder estimate and a polynomial one-arc bound uniform even for arbitrarily unequal marked intervals.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the diameter form of Nienhuis's predicted three-quarter exponent: a uniformly chosen n-step self-avoiding walk on the honeycomb lattice has diameter $n^{3/4+o(1)}$. Its local mass and covering numbers have exponent 4/3. These estimates hold at every sufficiently large fixed length, simultaneously across scales, with arbitrarily high polynomial probability.","pdfPath":"preprints/Marked-polygon-correlations-and-one-arc-bounds-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Marked-polygon-correlations-and-one-arc-bounds-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Marked-polygon-correlations-and-one-arc-bounds-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Marked-polygon-correlations-and-one-arc-bounds-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Marked-polygon-correlations-and-one-arc-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/00_introduction.tex","preprints/Marked-polygon-correlations-and-one-arc-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/01_reference.tex","preprints/Marked-polygon-correlations-and-one-arc-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/02_finite.tex","preprints/Marked-polygon-correlations-and-one-arc-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/03_denominator.tex","preprints/Marked-polygon-correlations-and-one-arc-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/04_mean.tex","preprints/Marked-polygon-correlations-and-one-arc-bounds-September-26-2026/build/figures/magnetic-geometry.tex","preprints/Marked-polygon-correlations-and-one-arc-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/05_analysis.tex","preprints/Marked-polygon-correlations-and-one-arc-bounds-September-26-2026/build/figures/marked-row.tex","preprints/Marked-polygon-correlations-and-one-arc-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/06_one_arc.tex","preprints/Marked-polygon-correlations-and-one-arc-bounds-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Marked-polygon-correlations-and-one-arc-bounds-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"At the critical honeycomb vertex activity, the squared-length mass of simple polygons of diameter at most $H$, counted modulo translations, is at most $H^{2/3+o(1)}$. We also prove a quantitative two-mark cylinder estimate and a polynomial one-arc bound uniform even for arbitrarily unequal marked intervals.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":371},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the proof works","sourceOffset":5527},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite cylinder vacua","sourceOffset":8369},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite diagram model","sourceOffset":8763},{"kind":"section","title":"The finite marked-pair identity","sourceOffset":13372},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A bilinear spin contraction","sourceOffset":14588},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite algebra of the marked pair","sourceOffset":18390},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The magnetic contours","sourceOffset":33866},{"kind":"section","title":"A denominator bound without balanced multiplicities","sourceOffset":37103},{"kind":"section","title":"A positive magnetic mean and coercive energy","sourceOffset":46321},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The contour carrying a positive magnetic mean","sourceOffset":46685},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact centering of the ordinary and marked factors","sourceOffset":56987}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Squared-length polygon mass","label":"thm:polygon-moment","text":"For every $\\eta>0$ there is $C_\\eta<\\infty$ such that, for $H\\ge1$, \\[ \\sum_{\\substack{[P]\\ \\operatorname{diam}P\\le H}} \\rho_{\\rm v}^{|P|}|P|^2\\le C_\\eta H^{2/3+\\eta}. \\] The sum is over the simple unoriented polygon translation classes just defined.","sourceOffset":1308,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Marked-pair contour identity","label":"thm:06-marked-identity","text":"At the physical parameters, and by continuation at regular complex parameters on the contours of Lemma \\ref{lem:06-contours}, \\begin{equation} \\widetilde h_N^2\\mathcal C(p,p') =(2c)^2(2d)^n \\sum_{M=-\\min(s,b)}^{\\min(s,b)} (-1)^{sb+M} [p_+^{\\,s-M}p_-^{\\,b+M}]I(\\mathcal T). \\end{equation} Here \\(p_+,p_-\\) are formal activities, unrelated to the marked port \\(p\\).","sourceOffset":21942,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/237.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":221407,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Marked-polygon-correlations-and-one-arc-bounds-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"237-08","slug":"Disk-transfer-representations-and-confined-bridge-mass-September-26-2026","familyId":"237","title":"Disk transfer representations and confined bridge mass","abstract":"We prove that critical honeycomb bridges crossing a strip of width R have mean length $R^{4/3+o(1)}$, with the same exponent after confinement to a fixed multiple of the strip width. The initial boundary port is fixed, the terminal port is summed, and all finite lengths receive their critical weights. The bridge mass is comparable to R−1/4; the half-plane arch kernel at endpoint separation m is comparable to m−5/4.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the diameter form of Nienhuis's predicted three-quarter exponent: a uniformly chosen n-step self-avoiding walk on the honeycomb lattice has diameter $n^{3/4+o(1)}$. Its local mass and covering numbers have exponent 4/3. These estimates hold at every sufficiently large fixed length, simultaneously across scales, with arbitrarily high polynomial probability.","pdfPath":"preprints/Disk-transfer-representations-and-confined-bridge-mass-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Disk-transfer-representations-and-confined-bridge-mass-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Disk-transfer-representations-and-confined-bridge-mass-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Disk-transfer-representations-and-confined-bridge-mass-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Disk-transfer-representations-and-confined-bridge-mass-September-26-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Disk-transfer-representations-and-confined-bridge-mass-September-26-2026/build/sections/boundary.tex","preprints/Disk-transfer-representations-and-confined-bridge-mass-September-26-2026/build/figures/box-order.tex","preprints/Disk-transfer-representations-and-confined-bridge-mass-September-26-2026/build/sections/analytic.tex","preprints/Disk-transfer-representations-and-confined-bridge-mass-September-26-2026/build/figures/disk-gluing.tex","preprints/Disk-transfer-representations-and-confined-bridge-mass-September-26-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Disk-transfer-representations-and-confined-bridge-mass-September-26-2026/build/sections/visits.tex","preprints/Disk-transfer-representations-and-confined-bridge-mass-September-26-2026/build/figures/exterior-arms.tex","preprints/Disk-transfer-representations-and-confined-bridge-mass-September-26-2026/build/figures/recoverable-cuts.tex","preprints/Disk-transfer-representations-and-confined-bridge-mass-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Disk-transfer-representations-and-confined-bridge-mass-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that critical honeycomb bridges crossing a strip of width $R$ have mean length $R^{4/3+o(1)}$, with the same exponent after confinement to a fixed multiple of the strip width. The initial boundary port is fixed, the terminal port is summed, and all finite lengths receive their critical weights. The bridge mass is comparable to $R^{-1/4}$; the half-plane arch kernel at endpoint separation $m$ is comparable to $m^{-5/4}$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":495},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The lattice and the positive path measures","sourceOffset":1086},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Boundary and finite-domain consequences","sourceOffset":4001},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and related representations","sourceOffset":5878},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two exact calculations and the spatial conversion","sourceOffset":9652},{"kind":"section","title":"Local exploration and the transfer row","sourceOffset":13453},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite exploration and a positive arch bound","sourceOffset":14318},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The local diagram weights and braid identity","sourceOffset":17601},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The physical half-cylinder vacuum","sourceOffset":27423},{"kind":"section","title":"A rapidity orbit and an inverse-matrix formula","sourceOffset":33888},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Endpoint transport on a finite orbit","sourceOffset":35493},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The spectral reduction and scalar commutant","sourceOffset":38805}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Bridge mass retained in a fixed corridor","label":"thm:confined-bridge","text":"There is a constant $C_0<\\infty$ such that, for every fixed $C\\ge C_0$, \\[ B_C(R)\\asymp B(R)\\asymp R^{-1/4},\\qquad M_C(R)=R^{13/12+o(1)},\\qquad M(R)=R^{13/12+o(1)}. \\] Consequently the mean number of visited vertices is $R^{4/3+o(1)}$ both under the unrestricted critical bridge law and under its restriction to this fixed corridor.","sourceOffset":3176,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Sharp boundary mass and annealed chord laws","label":"thm:boundary","text":"The following conclusions hold as the indicated scale tends to infinity. \\begin{enumerate} \\item The arch kernel is nonincreasing and \\[ h(m)\\asymp m^{-5/4},\\qquad \\mu(\\operatorname{height}>R)\\asymp \\mu(\\operatorname{diameter}>R)\\asymp R^{-1/4}. \\] \\item For every sufficiently large fixed $C$, normalize $\\mu$ either on $R\\le\\operatorname{diameter}\\le CR$, or on $\\operatorname{height}\\ge R$ and $\\operatorname{diameter}\\le CR$. Under either law, $\\mathbb E\\ell=R^{4/3+o(1)}$. \\item Let \\[ D_R=\\{y:e_i\\cdot y<R,\\ i=0,1,2\\},\\qquad I_R=\\{v\\text{ a lattice vertex}:e_i\\cdot v<(1-\\varepsilon)R, \\ i=0,1,2\\}, \\] where $R\\in\\frac12+\\frac32\\mathbb Z$ and $\\varepsilon>0$ is sufficiently small and fixed. Sum critical chord weights over the ordered pair of boundary ports and normalize on chords visiting $I_R$. Uniformly for $v\\in I_R$, their visit probability is $R^{-2/3+o(1)}$. The expected total number of visited vertices, and the expected number in $I_R$, are both $R^{4/3+o(1)}$. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":4431,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/237.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":304009,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Disk-transfer-representations-and-confined-bridge-mass-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"237-09","slug":"Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026","familyId":"237","title":"Polynomial vacuum representations and bridge mass for honeycomb walks","abstract":"We prove that, under the all-length critical measure, a honeycomb bridge crossing a strip of h layers, with its initial port fixed and its terminal port free, has mean length $h^{4/3+o(1)}$. We also obtain central visit probability $R^{-2/3+o(1)}$ and mean length $R^{4/3+o(1)}$ for macroscopic free-boundary chords in a regular hexagon.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the diameter form of Nienhuis's predicted three-quarter exponent: a uniformly chosen n-step self-avoiding walk on the honeycomb lattice has diameter $n^{3/4+o(1)}$. Its local mass and covering numbers have exponent 4/3. These estimates hold at every sufficiently large fixed length, simultaneously across scales, with arbitrarily high polynomial probability.","pdfPath":"preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/model.tex","preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/rows.tex","preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/row-figure.tex","preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/vacuum.tex","preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/pairing.tex","preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/boundary.tex","preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/complex.tex","preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/localization.tex","preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/localization-figure.tex","preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/visit-recursion.tex","preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/lateral-tail.tex","preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/visits.tex","preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/bridge.tex","preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/tripod-figure.tex","preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that, under the all-length critical measure, a honeycomb bridge crossing a strip of $h$ layers, with its initial port fixed and its terminal port free, has mean length $h^{4/3+o(1)}$. We also obtain central visit probability $R^{-2/3+o(1)}$ and mean length $R^{4/3+o(1)}$ for macroscopic free-boundary chords in a regular hexagon.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Critical bridge length and a polynomial polygon observable","sourceOffset":402},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Critical crossing mass and related laws","sourceOffset":4478},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A polygon observable and its construction","sourceOffset":6157},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Route through the proof","sourceOffset":10206},{"kind":"section","title":"Planar and cylindrical setup","sourceOffset":12413},{"kind":"section","title":"Row matrices and polynomial vacua","sourceOffset":22895},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The checked tile and its rank reductions","sourceOffset":23667},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Twisted rows and the physical eigenline","sourceOffset":32458},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Polynomial vacuum","sourceOffset":38272},{"kind":"subsubsection","title":"The physical normalization and its empty component","sourceOffset":50384},{"kind":"section","title":"The balanced puncture observable","sourceOffset":56180},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Interpolation of the contraction","sourceOffset":62269}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Strip mass and bridge length","label":"thm:pv:bridge","text":"As the integer layer height $h$ tends to infinity, \\[ b_h=h^{-1/4+o(1)},\\qquad \\ell_h=h^{13/12+o(1)},\\qquad \\mathbb E_h|\\gamma|=h^{4/3+o(1)}. \\] All three sums are finite. These exponents are unchanged if the paths are also confined to horizontal distance $h\\log^2h$ from their initial port.","sourceOffset":1942,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Hexagon visits","label":"thm:pv:hexagon","text":"The mass normalizing $\\mathbb P_R$ is $R^{3/4+o(1)}$. Uniformly at vertices in the concentric hexagon of side $R/3$, \\[ \\mathbb P_R(v\\in\\gamma)=R^{-2/3+o(1)}. \\] The corresponding upper bound holds at every vertex, and $\\mathbb E_R|\\gamma|=R^{4/3+o(1)}$.","sourceOffset":3027,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/237.md","scopeText":"The linked formalization establishes finiteness of the critical bridge measures used in the paper. For every strip height $h\\ge1$, the total weight and first length moment of self-avoiding honeycomb bridges from a fixed initial port to a free terminal port are finite. The corresponding sums are also finite when the bridge is confined to the corridor of horizontal width $h(\\log h)^2$, including the normalized first-moment sums.\n\nThese are supporting summability statements. The paper's $h^{4/3+o(1)}$ mean-length law and its central-visit and hexagonal-chord exponents are outside them.\n\nThe linked formalization proves existence of the infinite-length free energy for the critical honeycomb self-avoiding-walk partition function. For every starting vertex, force direction, and real force paramet","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/237.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":247032,"sourceFileCount":17,"statementCount":15,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Polynomial-vacuum-representations-and-bridge-mass-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"237-10","slug":"Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026","familyId":"237","title":"Renewal and changes of law for critical honeycomb walks","abstract":"For critical honeycomb self-avoiding walk, we prove spatial exponent 3/4 for the infinite irreducible-bridge law and for bridges of every sufficiently large compatible even length. We establish the corresponding thermal laws at every large discount scale, including all positive length and spatial moments. For unrestricted uniform walks, the endpoint lower law holds on a common set of lengths of natural density one. We also determine the near-critical exponential correlation scale and small-force free-energy exponent, and prove spatial local lower bounds that permit conditioning on a prescribed terminal vertex.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the diameter form of Nienhuis's predicted three-quarter exponent: a uniformly chosen n-step self-avoiding walk on the honeycomb lattice has diameter $n^{3/4+o(1)}$. Its local mass and covering numbers have exponent 4/3. These estimates hold at every sufficiently large fixed length, simultaneously across scales, with arbitrarily high polynomial probability.","pdfPath":"preprints/Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/glyphtounicode-used.tex","preprints/Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/renewal.tex","preprints/Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/exact.tex","preprints/Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/calibrated.tex","preprints/Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/thermal.tex","preprints/Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/censored.tex","preprints/Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/uniform.tex","preprints/Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/responses.tex","preprints/Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/alternatives.tex","preprints/Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/signed.tex","preprints/Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/geometric-transfers.tex","preprints/Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For critical honeycomb self-avoiding walk, we prove spatial exponent $3/4$ for the infinite irreducible-bridge law and for bridges of every sufficiently large compatible even length. We establish the corresponding thermal laws at every large discount scale, including all positive length and spatial moments. For unrestricted uniform walks, the endpoint lower law holds on a common set of lengths of natural density one. We also determine the near-critical exponential correlation scale and small-force free-energy exponent, and prove spatial local lower bounds that permit conditioning on a prescribed terminal vertex.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":682},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bridge theory and the additional estimates","sourceOffset":5288},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the normalizations are controlled","sourceOffset":8119},{"kind":"section","title":"One probability space for the different laws","sourceOffset":13079},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ports, strips and the finite input","sourceOffset":13369},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Factorization and marked rewards","sourceOffset":15398},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Joint increment estimates","sourceOffset":17978},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Infinite concatenation and spatial growth","sourceOffset":21204},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform half-plane paths have the same prefix limit","sourceOffset":23564},{"kind":"section","title":"Conditioning on one exact length","sourceOffset":28848},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A local renewal lower bound with bounded-factor tails","sourceOffset":29601},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform bridges at every compatible length","sourceOffset":34941}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Finite bridge estimates","label":"R:renew:finite-input","text":"There are constants $c,C>0$ such that, for every sufficiently large integer $h$, \\[ c h^{-1/4}\\le B_h\\le C h^{-1/4},\\qquad M_h\\le C h^{13/12},\\qquad B_h\\{L\\ge c h^{4/3}\\}\\ge c h^{-1/4}. \\] For all $h\\ge1$ and $x\\ge1$, one also has \\[ B_h\\{D>x\\}\\le C x^{-1/4}. \\] The same estimates hold in the symmetry-related pure directions.","sourceOffset":14381,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Renewal factorization","label":"R:renew:factorization","text":"The masses $p(\\gamma)$ sum to one. If $H,L,X,D$ denote respectively the height, visited-vertex length, signed transverse displacement and Euclidean diameter of an irreducible, then, for $s>0$ and $u\\ge0$, \\[ b(s,u):=\\sum_{h\\ge0}\\sum_{\\gamma\\in\\mathcal B_h} \\rho^{L(\\gamma)}e^{-sh-uL(\\gamma)} =\\frac1{1-\\E_p e^{-sH-uL}}. \\] In particular $b(s,0)\\asymp s^{-3/4}$ as $s\\downarrow0$. Under independent successive samples from $p$, $B_h$ is the probability that some renewal occurs at height $h$.","sourceOffset":15873,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/237.md","scopeText":"The linked formalization establishes finiteness of the critical bridge measures used in the paper. For every strip height $h\\ge1$, the total weight and first length moment of self-avoiding honeycomb bridges from a fixed initial port to a free terminal port are finite. The corresponding sums are also finite when the bridge is confined to the corridor of horizontal width $h(\\log h)^2$, including the normalized first-moment sums.\n\nThese are supporting summability statements. The paper's $h^{4/3+o(1)}$ mean-length law and its central-visit and hexagonal-chord exponents are outside them.\n\nThe linked formalization proves existence of the infinite-length free energy for the critical honeycomb self-avoiding-walk partition function. For every starting vertex, force direction, and real force paramet","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/237.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":358323,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":36,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Renewal-and-changes-of-law-for-critical-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"237-11","slug":"Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026","familyId":"237","title":"Critical strip-crossing mass on the honeycomb lattice","abstract":"At the critical weight of the regular honeycomb lattice, the total weight of self-avoiding paths crossing a strip of height N is comparable to N−1/4. The first horizontal-displacement moment of return paths is comparable to N3/4.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the diameter form of Nienhuis's predicted three-quarter exponent: a uniformly chosen n-step self-avoiding walk on the honeycomb lattice has diameter $n^{3/4+o(1)}$. Its local mass and covering numbers have exponent 4/3. These estimates hold at every sufficiently large fixed length, simultaneously across scales, with arbitrarily high polynomial probability.","pdfPath":"preprints/Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/01_introduction.tex","preprints/Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/figures/model-conventions.tex","preprints/Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/02_flux_transfers.tex","preprints/Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/figures/local-operator.tex","preprints/Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/figures/rhombus-dictionary.tex","preprints/Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/figures/cut-states.tex","preprints/Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/03_vacuum.tex","preprints/Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/04_arch_formula.tex","preprints/Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/figures/stepped-strip.tex","preprints/Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/05_positive_integral.tex","preprints/Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/06_hard_edge.tex","preprints/Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/sections/appendix_local.tex","preprints/Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"At the critical weight of the regular honeycomb lattice, the total weight of self-avoiding paths crossing a strip of height $N$ is comparable to $N^{-1/4}$. The first horizontal-displacement moment of return paths is comparable to $N^{3/4}$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":287},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and the result","sourceOffset":819},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier work","sourceOffset":5771},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":7825},{"kind":"section","title":"Positive flux and finite strip transfers","sourceOffset":10764},{"kind":"section","title":"The polynomial vacuum","sourceOffset":32186},{"kind":"section","title":"An exact formula for the arch moment","sourceOffset":47041},{"kind":"section","title":"A positive integral for the homogeneous moment","sourceOffset":60986},{"kind":"section","title":"The smallest coordinate and the strip exponent","sourceOffset":74559},{"kind":"section","title":"Local coefficient identities","sourceOffset":89165}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Critical strip mass","label":"thm:strip","text":"For every integer $N\\ge1$, all sums in \\eqref{eq:01-strip-quantities} are finite. With constants independent of $N$, \\begin{equation} c\\mathcal A_N+\\mathcal B_N=1,\\qquad m_{N+1}-m_N\\asymp\\mathcal B_N,\\qquad m_N\\asymp N^{3/4},\\qquad \\mathcal B_N\\asymp N^{-1/4}. \\end{equation} Moreover, $\\mathcal B_N$ is nonincreasing in $N$.","sourceOffset":4948,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Arch formula","label":"prop:arch-formula","text":"Let $\\eta=2d-1$ and \\begin{equation} g_X(z)=\\frac{p_{N+1}(a_1,\\ldots,a_N,z)}{p_N(a_1,\\ldots,a_N)}. \\end{equation} Then $g_X$ is monic of degree $N$ for generic $X$, and \\begin{equation} m(X)=\\sum_{i=1}^Na_i-\\eta[z^{N-1}]g_X(z). \\end{equation} The right side is understood by continuation at removable singularities.","sourceOffset":49400,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/237.md","scopeText":"The linked formalization establishes finiteness of the critical bridge measures used in the paper. For every strip height $h\\ge1$, the total weight and first length moment of self-avoiding honeycomb bridges from a fixed initial port to a free terminal port are finite. The corresponding sums are also finite when the bridge is confined to the corridor of horizontal width $h(\\log h)^2$, including the normalized first-moment sums.\n\nThese are supporting summability statements. The paper's $h^{4/3+o(1)}$ mean-length law and its central-visit and hexagonal-chord exponents are outside them.\n\nThe linked formalization proves existence of the infinite-length free energy for the critical honeycomb self-avoiding-walk partition function. For every starting vertex, force direction, and real force paramet","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/237.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":101375,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Critical-strip-crossing-mass-on-the-honeycomb-lattice-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"237-12","slug":"Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026","familyId":"237","title":"Uniform marked-polygon estimates and sharp finite bridge moments","abstract":"We prove a uniform bound for critical honeycomb polygons through two axial marks on a periodic staircase. The bound retains an explicit power of the ratio between the period and the marked separation. We also prove sharp finite strip estimates: bridge mass of order h−1/4, first-length mass at most $Ch^{13/12}$, and mass at least $ch^{-1/4}$ on bridges with length at least $ch^{4/3}$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the diameter form of Nienhuis's predicted three-quarter exponent: a uniformly chosen n-step self-avoiding walk on the honeycomb lattice has diameter $n^{3/4+o(1)}$. Its local mass and covering numbers have exponent 4/3. These estimates hold at every sufficiently large fixed length, simultaneously across scales, with arbitrarily high polynomial probability.","pdfPath":"preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/build/sections/strip-transfer.tex","preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/build/figures/strip-transfer.tex","preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/build/sections/strip-asymptotics.tex","preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/build/sections/periodic-polynomial.tex","preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/build/sections/gas.tex","preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/build/sections/saturation.tex","preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/build/figures/geometry-three-exits.tex","preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/build/figures/geometry-collars.tex","preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/build/sections/uniform-nesting.tex","preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/build/figures/geometry-current.tex","preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/build/sections/magnetic.tex","preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/build/figures/staircase.tex","preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/build/sections/sewing.tex","preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/build/figures/sewing.tex","preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/build/figures/sewing-band.tex","preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a uniform bound for critical honeycomb polygons through two axial marks on a periodic staircase. The bound retains an explicit power of the ratio between the period and the marked separation. We also prove sharp finite strip estimates: bridge mass of order $h^{-1/4}$, first-length mass at most $Ch^{13/12}$, and mass at least $ch^{-1/4}$ on bridges with length at least $ch^{4/3}$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":454},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and method","sourceOffset":6492},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof route","sourceOffset":10088},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite formula for strip crossing mass","sourceOffset":11494},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local matrices and fusion","sourceOffset":14458},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Commuting and fusing double rows","sourceOffset":26099},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The scalar crossing function","sourceOffset":30083},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite functional","sourceOffset":40643},{"kind":"section","title":"Positive interpolation and the strip exponent","sourceOffset":42046},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A positive determinant formula","sourceOffset":42333},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Characteristic products","sourceOffset":46792},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Interpolation residuals","sourceOffset":58611}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Finite bridge estimates","label":"thm:finite-bridges","text":"There are constants $c,C>0$ such that, for every sufficiently large integer $h$, \\begin{align} c h^{-1/4}&\\le B_h\\le C h^{-1/4},\\\\ \\sum_{\\gamma\\in\\mathcal B_h}\\ell(\\gamma)w(\\gamma)&\\le C h^{13/12},\\\\ B_h\\{\\ell\\ge c h^{4/3}\\}&\\ge c h^{-1/4}. \\end{align} Moreover, for every $h\\ge1$ and $x\\ge0$, \\begin{equation} B_h\\{\\diam\\gamma>x\\}\\le C(1+x)^{-1/4}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":2808,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Two axial marks","label":"thm:two-marks","text":"If $d$ is even, the critical mass of simple polygons in the cylinder $\\mathbb R^2/(kP\\mathbb Z)$ through both marks satisfies \\begin{equation} \\sum_{\\gamma\\text{ through both marks}}\\kappa^{\\ell(\\gamma)} \\le C N^{-4/3}(N/d)^{15/8}, \\end{equation} with one constant $C$ independent of $P_1,P_2,k$ and the axial edge choice.","sourceOffset":4275,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/237.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":413245,"sourceFileCount":19,"statementCount":20,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Uniform-marked-polygon-estimates-and-sharp-finite-bridge-moments-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"237-13","slug":"Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026","familyId":"237","title":"Cap-selected amplitudes and triangle chords for honeycomb walks","abstract":"At the critical honeycomb fugacity, self-avoiding port-to-port chords in an equilateral lattice triangle of side R, summed over both boundary endpoints and restricted to diameter at least $R/100$, have partition sum comparable to R3/4 and mean length $R^{4/3+o(1)}$. We also determine the amplitude selected by two vacuum caps on a cylinder of circumference N and prove that it grows as $N^{1/6+o(1)}$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the diameter form of Nienhuis's predicted three-quarter exponent: a uniformly chosen n-step self-avoiding walk on the honeycomb lattice has diameter $n^{3/4+o(1)}$. Its local mass and covering numbers have exponent 4/3. These estimates hold at every sufficiently large fixed length, simultaneously across scales, with arbitrarily high polynomial probability.","pdfPath":"preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/figures/seam.tex","preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/local.tex","preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/cylinder.tex","preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/vacuum.tex","preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/residue.tex","preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/common-twist.tex","preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/site-moments.tex","preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/repeated-moments.tex","preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/counting.tex","preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/physical-amplitude.tex","preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/asymptotics.tex","preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/projection.tex","preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/higher-jets.tex","preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/sections/chords.tex","preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/figures/slit.tex","preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"At the critical honeycomb fugacity, self-avoiding port-to-port chords in an equilateral lattice triangle of side $R$, summed over both boundary endpoints and restricted to diameter at least $R/100$, have partition sum comparable to $R^{3/4}$ and mean length $R^{4/3+o(1)}$. We also determine the amplitude selected by two vacuum caps on a cylinder of circumference $N$ and prove that it grows as $N^{1/6+o(1)}$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":510},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Critical weights and the amplitude problem","sourceOffset":3843},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The positive observables behind the proof","sourceOffset":6166},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A complete route through the argument","sourceOffset":10400},{"kind":"section","title":"Local transfer matrices and fusion","sourceOffset":13251},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The local operator","sourceOffset":13940},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rank reductions and inversion","sourceOffset":14953},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Interchange and row identities","sourceOffset":17493},{"kind":"section","title":"Positive boundary mass and winding polygons","sourceOffset":20332},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite boundary flux and half-plane summability","sourceOffset":22598},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Annular diagram rows and the zero-fugacity cap","sourceOffset":24865},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The one-winding derivative, fusion, and its polynomial","sourceOffset":32996}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Macroscopic triangle chords","label":"thm:triangle","text":"For the critical chord law $\\mathbb P_R$, \\[ Z_R^{\\rm chord}\\asymp R^{3/4}, \\qquad \\mathbb E_R m=R^{4/3+o(1)}. \\] The same mean exponent holds for ordinary centre-to-centre self-avoiding walks contained in $\\Delta_R$, with both endpoints on its inner boundary layer (the centres adjacent to a boundary port), weighted by $x$ per edge and restricted to have diameter at least $R/100$.","sourceOffset":2339,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Cylinder amplitude and planar nesting","label":"thm:amplitude","text":"For each sufficiently large $N$, the preceding cylinder sums are finite and \\[ \\mathcal A_N:=\\lim_{H\\to\\infty}\\lambda_N^{-H}\\mathcal G_N(H) \\quad\\text{exists in }(0,\\infty). \\] Their asymptotics and the planar nesting asymptotic are \\[ \\mathcal A_N=N^{1/6+o(1)}, \\qquad \\mathcal Z_v(r)=r^{1/12+o(1)}. \\] The bounds are unchanged by a lattice translation of $v$.","sourceOffset":8026,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/237.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":312371,"sourceFileCount":18,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Cap-selected-amplitudes-and-triangle-chords-for-honeycomb-walks-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"238-01","slug":"Optimal-order-mixing-of-the-Thorp-shuffle-September-26-2026","familyId":"238","title":"Optimal-order mixing of the Thorp shuffle","abstract":"We prove that the Thorp shuffle on $2^d$ cards mixes in $\\Theta(d)$ complete shuffles. The full permutation law after $1600d$ shuffles converges to uniform in total variation as $d\\to\\infty$, uniformly over the initial deck, while a support count gives a lower bound of $2d-O(1)$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the Thorp shuffle randomizes $N=2^d$ labeled cards in $\\Theta(\\log N)$ physical shuffles, settling its optimal mixing order for power-of-two deck sizes. Convergence is in total variation from the worst initial ordering and concerns the entire permutation, not just individual card positions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Optimal-order-mixing-of-the-Thorp-shuffle-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Optimal-order-mixing-of-the-Thorp-shuffle-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Optimal-order-mixing-of-the-Thorp-shuffle-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Optimal-order-mixing-of-the-Thorp-shuffle-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Optimal-order-mixing-of-the-Thorp-shuffle-September-26-2026/build/conditional-flow.tex","preprints/Optimal-order-mixing-of-the-Thorp-shuffle-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Optimal-order-mixing-of-the-Thorp-shuffle-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the Thorp shuffle on $2^d$ cards mixes in $\\Theta(d)$ complete shuffles. The full permutation law after $1600d$ shuffles converges to uniform in total variation as $d\\to\\infty$, uniformly over the initial deck, while a support count gives a lower bound of $2d-O(1)$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":326},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and related work","sourceOffset":2590},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":6113},{"kind":"section","title":"The model and the support obstruction","sourceOffset":9554},{"kind":"section","title":"A conditional flow with one sweep of memory","sourceOffset":12085},{"kind":"section","title":"Why the recent noise contracts","sourceOffset":18487},{"kind":"section","title":"The representation degrees needed for the lift","sourceOffset":26513},{"kind":"section","title":"From eight large marginals to the whole group","sourceOffset":30715},{"kind":"section","title":"Eight convolution factors and the final comparison","sourceOffset":36179}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Optimal-order mixing","label":"thm:mixing","text":"As $d\\to\\infty$, \\[ \\|q_d^{*(1600d)}-U_n\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}}\\longrightarrow0. \\] For every integer $t\\ge0$, \\[ \\|q_d^{*t}-U_n\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}} \\ge 1-\\frac{2^{tn/2}}{n!}. \\] Consequently the mixing time at distance $1/4$ satisfies \\[ t_{\\mathrm{mix}}(d)\\ge \\left\\lceil\\frac2n\\log_2\\frac{3n!}{4}\\right\\rceil =2d-O(1), \\qquad t_{\\mathrm{mix}}(d)=\\Theta(d). \\]","sourceOffset":1345,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Averaged ordered marginals","label":"prop:averaged-marginal","text":"Let \\(n=2^d\\), \\(T=200d\\), and \\(1\\le k\\le7n/8\\). Choose an ordered list \\(C=(c_1,\\ldots,c_k)\\) uniformly among all lists of distinct labels, independently of the shuffle. Then \\begin{equation} \\begin{aligned} \\mathbb E_C \\left\\|\\mathcal L\\bigl((\\Pi_T(c_1),\\ldots,\\Pi_T(c_k))\\mid C\\bigr) -U_{\\mathrm{inj}}\\right\\|_{\\mathrm{TV}} &\\le\\varepsilon_n,\\\\ \\varepsilon_n:=\\frac{n^{3/2}}2 \\sqrt{(31/32)^{198d}+32e^{-n/256}} &=O(n^{-5/2}). \\end{aligned} \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":24104,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves optimal-order mixing of the Thorp shuffle on $2^d$ cards. After $1600d$ complete shuffles, the full permutation law converges in total variation to uniform as $d\\to\\infty$, uniformly over initial decks. Together with the support lower bound of $2d-O(1)$, this gives mixing time $\\Theta(d)=\\Theta(\\log(2^d))$.\n\nThe linked supporting results include frame and conditional-list estimates, regular trace and spectral bounds, signed moments, and full-density $L^2$ control. They also bound reciprocal Specht-dimension sums and give the eight-block Fourier estimate used in the mixing argument.\n\nThe formalized supporting result turns bounds on partial-permutation cosets into Fourier bounds. Partition a finite set into $b\\ge1$ nonempty blocks, and let $f\\ge0$ be a subprobability","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":45696,"sourceFileCount":3,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Optimal-order-mixing-of-the-Thorp-shuffle-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"238-02","slug":"Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026","familyId":"238","title":"Random coordinate frames and partial permutation laws","abstract":"For the Thorp shuffle on $2^d$ cards, we prove that the worst-start total-variation distance after $32800d$ physical shuffles tends to zero as $d\\to\\infty$. Combining our fixed-list estimate with the companion Fourier transfer improves this bound to $512d$ shuffles. These results follow from bounds on partially observed permutation laws in random coordinate frames.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the Thorp shuffle randomizes $N=2^d$ labeled cards in $\\Theta(\\log N)$ physical shuffles, settling its optimal mixing order for power-of-two deck sizes. Convergence is in total variation from the worst initial ordering and concerns the entire permutation, not just individual card positions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/build/companion-references.tex","preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/build/sections/01-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/build/sections/02-frames.tex","preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/build/sections/03-marginals.tex","preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/build/sections/05-lift.tex","preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/build/sections/04-degrees.tex","preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/build/sections/06-completion.tex","preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/build/sections/07-shear.tex","preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/build/sections/07b-delayed-symmetry.tex","preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/build/sections/07c-transfer-applications.tex","preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/build/sections/07a-disjoint-transfer-proof.tex","preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/build/sections/08-frame-variants.tex","preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/build/sections/09-equivariant.tex","preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/build/sections/10-character-explicit.tex","preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/build/sections/11-character-repair.tex","preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For the Thorp shuffle on $2^d$ cards, we prove that the worst-start total-variation distance after $32800d$ physical shuffles tends to zero as $d\\to\\infty$. Combining our fixed-list estimate with the companion Fourier transfer improves this bound to $512d$ shuffles. These results follow from bounds on partially observed permutation laws in random coordinate frames.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":414},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coordinate frames and the first proof","sourceOffset":3286},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Further partial laws and their uses","sourceOffset":7362},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and related work","sourceOffset":9469},{"kind":"section","title":"The chain and its elementary lower bound","sourceOffset":12797},{"kind":"section","title":"Changing the position frame","sourceOffset":18043},{"kind":"section","title":"Online observations and ordinary marginals","sourceOffset":26541},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A chronological law and a hidden card","sourceOffset":27229},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From hidden cards to an ordinary tuple law","sourceOffset":32990},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A sampled omitted block","sourceOffset":36411},{"kind":"section","title":"From large marginals to a finite degree sum","sourceOffset":41475},{"kind":"section","title":"An elementary bound on representation degrees","sourceOffset":51476}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For $n=2^d$, \\[ \\|q_d^{*(32800d)}-U_n\\|_{\\TV}\\longrightarrow0 \\qquad(d\\longrightarrow\\infty). \\] The convergence is uniform over every deterministic initial ordering of the deck.","sourceOffset":1707,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"frames:char:main","text":"Let $n=2^d$, and count one physical Thorp shuffle as one time step, using $n/2$ independent fair switches. For all sufficiently large integers $d$, the worst-initial-deck total-variation mixing time at threshold $1/4$ satisfies \\[ d\\le t_{\\mathrm{mix}}(d)\\le 6060000d. \\] In fact, the worst-initial-deck distance at time $60000(101d-1)$ tends to zero as $d\\to\\infty$.","sourceOffset":202718,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":242371,"sourceFileCount":18,"statementCount":31,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Random-coordinate-frames-and-partial-permutation-laws-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"238-03","slug":"From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026","familyId":"238","title":"From partial permutation information to Fourier bounds","abstract":"We show how information about partial permutations controls full permutation laws. Let n tend to infinity through multiples of eight. If the images of a uniformly chosen $7n/8$ labels approach the uniform injection law in average total variation, and the sign mean tends to zero, then the product of two independent permutations with the given law converges to uniform on Sn.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the Thorp shuffle randomizes $N=2^d$ labeled cards in $\\Theta(\\log N)$ physical shuffles, settling its optimal mixing order for power-of-two deck sizes. Convergence is in total variation from the worst initial ordering and concerns the entire permutation, not just individual card positions.","pdfPath":"preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/foundations.tex","preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/core.tex","preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/comparisons.tex","preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/random-partition.tex","preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/degrees.tex","preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/characters.tex","preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/two-sided.tex","preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/equivariant.tex","preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/domains.tex","preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/tilts.tex","preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/palettes.tex","preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/replica.tex","preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/forests.tex","preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/tabloid.tex","preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/degree-supplement.tex","preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/tabloid-supplement.tex","preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/sections/transfer-supplement.tex","preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We show how information about partial permutations controls full permutation laws. Let $n$ tend to infinity through multiples of eight. If the images of a uniformly chosen $7n/8$ labels approach the uniform injection law in average total variation, and the sign mean tends to zero, then the product of two independent permutations with the given law converges to uniform on $S_n$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"What partial observations can determine","sourceOffset":426},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The first proof and its extensions","sourceOffset":4503},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Relation to earlier methods","sourceOffset":8514},{"kind":"section","title":"Degree sums, common trimming, and normalization","sourceOffset":12186},{"kind":"section","title":"The complete transfer from disjoint coset systems","sourceOffset":20112},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Central projections retain the whole Fourier matrix","sourceOffset":25818},{"kind":"section","title":"Coefficient spaces and finer fixed-partition estimates","sourceOffset":30355},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison of the information and the conclusions","sourceOffset":31128},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Evaluation of coefficient functions","sourceOffset":33654},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two more isotypic estimates","sourceOffset":35755},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Counting only types allowed by branching","sourceOffset":39756},{"kind":"section","title":"Keeping the partition random","sourceOffset":41234}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Complementary cosets control Fourier matrices","label":"l:main","text":"Partition $X$ into $b$ nonempty sets $M_1,\\ldots,M_b$. Put $H_i=H_{M_i}$. Suppose $f$ is a nonnegative measure on $G$ of mass at most one and, for every $g\\in G$ and $i$, \\begin{equation} f(gH_i)\\le \\frac{B}{[G:H_i]}. \\end{equation} For every $u>0$ and every irreducible $\\rho$ of degree $D$, \\begin{equation} \\|\\widehat f(\\rho)\\|_{\\HS}^2 \\le bBC_uD^{-1+(u+2)/b}. \\end{equation} There is also an independent orbit-span proof of the bound \\begin{equation} \\|\\widehat f(\\rho)\\|_{\\op}^2 \\le bBC_uD^{-1+(u+2)/b}. \\end{equation} No restriction is imposed on block sizes or on the multiplicities in $\\rho|_{H_1\\times\\cdots\\times H_b}$.","sourceOffset":2310,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Random-domain transfer","label":"l:domain-transfer","text":"Let $n\\to\\infty$ through multiples of $256$, and let $\\mu_n$ be probabilities on $S_n$ satisfying \\[ \\delta_n=\\E_{|R|=n/256} \\|(\\mu_n)_{-R}-U_{-R}\\|_{\\TV}\\longrightarrow0. \\] Condition on the event that at least $7/8$ of the sets $R$ satisfy $\\mu_n(gH_R)\\le2/[S_n:H_R]$, and call the resulting law $\\nu_n$. For all sufficiently large $n$ it is defined, satisfies $\\|\\nu_n-\\mu_n\\|_{\\TV}\\le16\\delta_n$, and obeys \\[ \\|\\widehat\\nu_n(\\rho)\\|_{\\op}\\le CD^{-1/8} \\qquad(D=\\dim\\rho>1) \\] with an absolute $C$. If $\\E_{\\mu_n}\\sgn(g)=o(1)$, then $\\|\\mu_n^{*32}-U_n\\|_{\\TV}\\to0$. The averaged relative-entropy hypothesis \\[ \\E_{|R|=n/256}D((\\mu_n)_{-R}\\|U_{-R})=o(1) \\] is sufficient for these conclusions.","sourceOffset":112313,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves optimal-order mixing of the Thorp shuffle on $2^d$ cards. After $1600d$ complete shuffles, the full permutation law converges in total variation to uniform as $d\\to\\infty$, uniformly over initial decks. Together with the support lower bound of $2d-O(1)$, this gives mixing time $\\Theta(d)=\\Theta(\\log(2^d))$.\n\nThe linked supporting results include frame and conditional-list estimates, regular trace and spectral bounds, signed moments, and full-density $L^2$ control. They also bound reciprocal Specht-dimension sums and give the eight-block Fourier estimate used in the mixing argument.\n\nThe formalized supporting result turns bounds on partial-permutation cosets into Fourier bounds. Partition a finite set into $b\\ge1$ nonempty blocks, and let $f\\ge0$ be a subprobability","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":215424,"sourceFileCount":20,"statementCount":36,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/From-partial-permutation-information-to-Fourier-bounds-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"238-04","slug":"Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026","familyId":"238","title":"Conditional information under deterministic coordinate sweeps","abstract":"We bound the information remaining after paths of specified cards in the Thorp shuffle on $n=2^d$ cards have been observed. After a fixed number of deterministic coordinate sweeps, the joint endpoint law of further cards is close to uniform on the available positions, on average over the observed paths, provided a fixed positive fraction of labels lies outside both lists. Combined with a Fourier transfer, these bounds give full-deck mixing after $2048d$ physical shuffles.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the Thorp shuffle randomizes $N=2^d$ labeled cards in $\\Theta(\\log N)$ physical shuffles, settling its optimal mixing order for power-of-two deck sizes. Convergence is in total variation from the worst initial ordering and concerns the entire permutation, not just individual card positions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/companion-labels.tex","preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/01-setup.tex","preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/02-first-route.tex","preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/03-base-paths.tex","preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/04-noise-extensions.tex","preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/05-martingales.tex","preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/06-cycles.tex","preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/07-sweeps.tex","preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/08-prefix.tex","preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/09-cycle-entropy.tex","preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/11-domains.tex","preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/10-palettes.tex","preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/12-delayed-groups.tex","preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/13-forest.tex","preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/14-replica.tex","preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/15-probes.tex","preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We bound the information remaining after paths of specified cards in the Thorp shuffle on $n=2^d$ cards have been observed. After a fixed number of deterministic coordinate sweeps, the joint endpoint law of further cards is close to uniform on the available positions, on average over the observed paths, provided a fixed positive fraction of labels lies outside both lists. Combined with a Fourier transfer, these bounds give full-deck mixing after $2048d$ physical shuffles.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":568},{"kind":"section","title":"The coordinate process and conditional revelation","sourceOffset":12917},{"kind":"section","title":"A complete adapted-noise argument","sourceOffset":20131},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exact noise and covariance identities","sourceOffset":20625},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform marginals from the preceding coordinate split","sourceOffset":26108},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and the first full-deck application","sourceOffset":31634},{"kind":"section","title":"Joint information conditional on base paths","sourceOffset":38496},{"kind":"section","title":"Random domains and predictable endpoint bounds","sourceOffset":44646},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Random blockers and disjoint subcubes","sourceOffset":45023},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Predictable endpoint bounds","sourceOffset":50935},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The four corresponding full-permutation applications","sourceOffset":55156},{"kind":"section","title":"Fixed domains, random domains, and Walsh coordinates","sourceOffset":60047}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Fixed lists and a full-deck consequence","label":"thm:first-main","text":"Uniformly over deterministic starting decks and specified ordered lists of distinct labels, the following hold as $d\\to\\infty$: \\begin{enumerate} \\item At time $256d$, the images of any $k\\le7n/8$ labels have total-variation distance $o(1)$ from $\\Unif(\\operatorname{inj}([k],V))$. \\item At time $1024d$, the images of any $k\\le15n/16$ labels have total-variation distance at most $n^{-3/2}$, for all sufficiently large $d$. \\item The full permutation law satisfies $\\|q_d^{*(2048d)}-U_{S_n}\\|_{\\TV}\\to0$. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":5802,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Vector noise and random-domain balance","label":"prop:c-vector-domain-energy","text":"For $t\\ge d$, \\begin{equation} A_t=\\sum_{\\ell=1}^d2^{-\\ell}S_{t-\\ell}. \\end{equation} There are constants $c_\\alpha,C_\\alpha>0$, depending only on $\\alpha$, such that, with $\\gamma=\\alpha/2$ and $b_n=C_\\alpha n e^{-c_\\alpha n}$, \\begin{equation} S_s\\le(1-\\gamma)A_s+b_n\\qquad(s\\ge d-1). \\end{equation} In particular, for $\\theta=1-\\gamma/4$, \\begin{equation} A_t\\le n\\theta^{t-2d}+b_n/\\gamma\\qquad(t\\ge0). \\end{equation} Consequently there is a fixed integer $C>2$, depending only on $\\alpha$, for which $A_{Cd}=O(n^{-4})$, uniformly in $m\\in[\\alpha n,n]$.","sourceOffset":185136,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":291998,"sourceFileCount":19,"statementCount":35,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Conditional-information-under-deterministic-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"238-05","slug":"Conditional-permutations-in-a-revealed-switching-environment-September-26-2026","familyId":"238","title":"Conditional permutations in a revealed switching environment","abstract":"Fix half the labels in a Thorp shuffle on $2^d$ positions and reveal their complete trajectories. We prove that, after an explicit absolute number of coordinate sweeps, the conditional permutation of the remaining labels approaches uniform in expected total variation as $d\\to\\infty$, uniformly in the initial layout. The resulting constructions give full-deck mixing in a constant multiple of d physical shuffles.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the Thorp shuffle randomizes $N=2^d$ labeled cards in $\\Theta(\\log N)$ physical shuffles, settling its optimal mixing order for power-of-two deck sizes. Convergence is in total variation from the worst initial ordering and concerns the entire permutation, not just individual card positions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Conditional-permutations-in-a-revealed-switching-environment-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Conditional-permutations-in-a-revealed-switching-environment-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Conditional-permutations-in-a-revealed-switching-environment-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Conditional-permutations-in-a-revealed-switching-environment-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Conditional-permutations-in-a-revealed-switching-environment-September-26-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Conditional-permutations-in-a-revealed-switching-environment-September-26-2026/build/sections/01-conditional-bijections.tex","preprints/Conditional-permutations-in-a-revealed-switching-environment-September-26-2026/build/sections/02-preparation-active.tex","preprints/Conditional-permutations-in-a-revealed-switching-environment-September-26-2026/build/sections/03-reset.tex","preprints/Conditional-permutations-in-a-revealed-switching-environment-September-26-2026/build/sections/04-overlay.tex","preprints/Conditional-permutations-in-a-revealed-switching-environment-September-26-2026/build/sections/05-two-halves.tex","preprints/Conditional-permutations-in-a-revealed-switching-environment-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Conditional-permutations-in-a-revealed-switching-environment-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Fix half the labels in a Thorp shuffle on $2^d$ positions and reveal their complete trajectories. We prove that, after an explicit absolute number of coordinate sweeps, the conditional permutation of the remaining labels approaches uniform in expected total variation as $d\\to\\infty$, uniformly in the initial layout. The resulting constructions give full-deck mixing in a constant multiple of $d$ physical shuffles.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":462},{"kind":"section","title":"From revealed paths to a permutation kernel","sourceOffset":9071},{"kind":"section","title":"Preparing an ordered half-deck","sourceOffset":16208},{"kind":"section","title":"Preparation and active sweeps","sourceOffset":28072},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Truncating the preparation interval","sourceOffset":29417},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Active truncation","sourceOffset":30786},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Trace estimate for an active interval","sourceOffset":33404},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From traces to contraction over chunks","sourceOffset":41237},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Completing the first route","sourceOffset":45150},{"kind":"section","title":"Reset minorization and central relative increments","sourceOffset":46936},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Partial mixing and forward minorization","sourceOffset":48167},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison conditional on a switch environment","sourceOffset":57671}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Conditional permutation and full-deck mixing","label":"thm:methods","text":"Let $n=2^d$, and start the shuffle from any deterministic deck. After $J(s_0+1)$ sweeps, where $s_0=400$ and $J=40000$, the conditional free-slot law defined above satisfies \\[ \\sup_{\\text{initial layouts}}\\E_{\\mathcal E} \\|\\nu_{\\mathcal E}-U_{n/2}\\|_{\\TV}\\longrightarrow0 \\qquad(d\\longrightarrow\\infty). \\] Consequently the full-deck law approaches uniform, uniformly in the starting deck, after $16\\,040\\,400d$ physical shuffles. For every integer $t\\ge0$, after $t$ physical shuffles the full-deck distance from uniform is at least $1-2^{tn/2}/n!$. Thus the full-deck mixing time is of order $d$.","sourceOffset":2780,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Uniform half-deck preparation","label":"prop:half-preparation","text":"For all sufficiently large $d$, every ordered list of $k\\le n/2$ distinct labels, from every deterministic starting layout, has endpoint law within $n^{-3}$ in total variation of uniform ordered distinct positions after $400$ sweeps.","sourceOffset":25008,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves optimal-order mixing of the Thorp shuffle on $2^d$ cards. After $1600d$ complete shuffles, the full permutation law converges in total variation to uniform as $d\\to\\infty$, uniformly over initial decks. Together with the support lower bound of $2d-O(1)$, this gives mixing time $\\Theta(d)=\\Theta(\\log(2^d))$.\n\nThe linked supporting results include frame and conditional-list estimates, regular trace and spectral bounds, signed moments, and full-density $L^2$ control. They also bound reciprocal Specht-dimension sums and give the eight-block Fourier estimate used in the mixing argument.\n\nThe formalized supporting result turns bounds on partial-permutation cosets into Fourier bounds. Partition a finite set into $b\\ge1$ nonempty blocks, and let $f\\ge0$ be a subprobability","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":119475,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Conditional-permutations-in-a-revealed-switching-environment-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"238-06","slug":"Routing-densities-and-representation-contraction-for-Thorp-sweeps-September-26-2026","familyId":"238","title":"Routing densities and representation contraction for Thorp sweeps","abstract":"For $N=2^d$ cards, we prove that an absolute number of coordinate sweeps of the Thorp shuffle brings the full permutation law to total-variation distance tending to zero from uniform. The mixing time therefore has optimal order $\\Theta(\\log N)$ in physical shuffles.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the Thorp shuffle randomizes $N=2^d$ labeled cards in $\\Theta(\\log N)$ physical shuffles, settling its optimal mixing order for power-of-two deck sizes. Convergence is in total variation from the worst initial ordering and concerns the entire permutation, not just individual card positions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Routing-densities-and-representation-contraction-for-Thorp-sweeps-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Routing-densities-and-representation-contraction-for-Thorp-sweeps-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Routing-densities-and-representation-contraction-for-Thorp-sweeps-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Routing-densities-and-representation-contraction-for-Thorp-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Routing-densities-and-representation-contraction-for-Thorp-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Routing-densities-and-representation-contraction-for-Thorp-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/01-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Routing-densities-and-representation-contraction-for-Thorp-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/02-routing.tex","preprints/Routing-densities-and-representation-contraction-for-Thorp-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/figures/network-node.tex","preprints/Routing-densities-and-representation-contraction-for-Thorp-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/03-factorial.tex","preprints/Routing-densities-and-representation-contraction-for-Thorp-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/04-certificate.tex","preprints/Routing-densities-and-representation-contraction-for-Thorp-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/05-strong-providers.tex","preprints/Routing-densities-and-representation-contraction-for-Thorp-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/06-cycle-windows.tex","preprints/Routing-densities-and-representation-contraction-for-Thorp-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/06b-contact-cancellation.tex","preprints/Routing-densities-and-representation-contraction-for-Thorp-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/07-casimir.tex","preprints/Routing-densities-and-representation-contraction-for-Thorp-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/08-harmonic-cycle.tex","preprints/Routing-densities-and-representation-contraction-for-Thorp-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/09-adaptive-alphabets.tex","preprints/Routing-densities-and-representation-contraction-for-Thorp-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/10-core-cutoff.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Routing-densities-and-representation-contraction-for-Thorp-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For $N=2^d$ cards, we prove that an absolute number of coordinate sweeps of the Thorp shuffle brings the full permutation law to total-variation distance tending to zero from uniform. The mixing time therefore has optimal order $\\Theta(\\log N)$ in physical shuffles.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":312},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Network ancestry and previous mixing bounds","sourceOffset":4326},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The first proof and the further estimates","sourceOffset":8643},{"kind":"section","title":"The physical chain, Fourier norms and dimension estimates","sourceOffset":15449},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Binary positions and sweep operators","sourceOffset":15855},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Plancherel and powers of a nonnormal sweep","sourceOffset":18729},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Injection multiplicities and inverse-degree sums","sourceOffset":20190},{"kind":"section","title":"The routing density and repeated irreducible copies","sourceOffset":24026},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Alternating colors and multiplicity","sourceOffset":24746},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How often can selected paths meet?","sourceOffset":31796},{"kind":"section","title":"The factorial-moment proof","sourceOffset":33047},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Closing routes and factorial cycle moments","sourceOffset":33800}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Routing density and representation contraction","label":"fac:main","text":"There are absolute constants $\\eta,c,C>0$ such that, with $a=1/1000$, \\[ \\log\\E_{y\\sim u_{N,k}}R_x(y)^{1+a} \\le Ck(k/N)^\\eta \\qquad(1\\le k\\le N), \\] uniformly over every starting injection $x$. For every sufficiently large dyadic $N$ and every nontrivial irreducible representation, \\[ T_N(\\lambda)=0\\quad\\hbox{or}\\quad \\|T_N(\\lambda)\\|_{\\op}\\le D_\\lambda^{-c}. \\] Consequently an absolute number of forward sweeps has worst-start total-variation distance from uniform tending to zero as $d\\to\\infty$.","sourceOffset":1985,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Certificate-network contraction","label":"net:main","text":"There is an absolute $c>0$ such that for every sufficiently large dyadic $n$ and every nontrivial irreducible of dimension $D$ not annihilated by a pair layer, \\[ \\|T_n(\\lambda)\\|_{\\op}\\le D^{-c}. \\] Consequently a fixed absolute number of sweeps has total-variation distance tending to zero.","sourceOffset":75161,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves optimal-order mixing of the Thorp shuffle on $2^d$ cards. After $1600d$ complete shuffles, the full permutation law converges in total variation to uniform as $d\\to\\infty$, uniformly over initial decks. Together with the support lower bound of $2d-O(1)$, this gives mixing time $\\Theta(d)=\\Theta(\\log(2^d))$.\n\nThe linked supporting results include frame and conditional-list estimates, regular trace and spectral bounds, signed moments, and full-density $L^2$ control. They also bound reciprocal Specht-dimension sums and give the eight-block Fourier estimate used in the mixing argument.\n\nThe formalized supporting result turns bounds on partial-permutation cosets into Fourier bounds. Partition a finite set into $b\\ge1$ nonempty blocks, and let $f\\ge0$ be a subprobability","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":305208,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Routing-densities-and-representation-contraction-for-Thorp-sweeps-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"238-07","slug":"Row-column-symmetry-and-contraction-of-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026","familyId":"238","title":"Row–column symmetry and contraction of coordinate sweeps","abstract":"We prove that the Thorp shuffle on $N=2^d$ labeled cards has full-permutation total-variation mixing time $\\Theta(\\log N)$ in physical shuffles. An absolute number of coordinate sweeps suffices for the upper bound.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the Thorp shuffle randomizes $N=2^d$ labeled cards in $\\Theta(\\log N)$ physical shuffles, settling its optimal mixing order for power-of-two deck sizes. Convergence is in total variation from the worst initial ordering and concerns the entire permutation, not just individual card positions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Row-column-symmetry-and-contraction-of-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Row-column-symmetry-and-contraction-of-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Row-column-symmetry-and-contraction-of-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Row-column-symmetry-and-contraction-of-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Row-column-symmetry-and-contraction-of-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Row-column-symmetry-and-contraction-of-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/weighted.tex","preprints/Row-column-symmetry-and-contraction-of-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/frames.tex","preprints/Row-column-symmetry-and-contraction-of-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/coherent.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Row-column-symmetry-and-contraction-of-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the Thorp shuffle on $N=2^d$ labeled cards has full-permutation total-variation mixing time $\\Theta(\\log N)$ in physical shuffles. An absolute number of coordinate sweeps suffices for the upper bound.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":277},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The sweep and its moments","sourceOffset":2671},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From row--column overlap to a bounded exponent","sourceOffset":6340},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two further forms of the overlap estimate","sourceOffset":10236},{"kind":"section","title":"Representations and subgroup projections","sourceOffset":12603},{"kind":"part","title":"Weighted contraction","sourceOffset":16396},{"kind":"section","title":"Dimensions away from a long first row","sourceOffset":16446},{"kind":"section","title":"Weights from hook trimming","sourceOffset":18450},{"kind":"section","title":"Signed representations on occupied cells","sourceOffset":22035},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two bounds for transverse overlap","sourceOffset":27584},{"kind":"section","title":"The row-column Fourier estimate","sourceOffset":34261},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positive blocks and stabilizer coefficients","sourceOffset":37739}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"proposition","name":"Uniform Schatten estimate","label":"ten:frames:main","text":"There is an absolute real exponent $8\\le p<\\infty$ such that, for every $d\\ge1$ and every $\\lambda\\vdash n$ with $n=2^d$, \\begin{equation} f^\\lambda \\|(\\mu_d)_\\lambda\\|_p^p \\ \\le\\ 1\\qquad (\\lambda\\vdash n). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":61475,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"rec:coherent-main","text":"There is an absolute $p_*\\ge1$ such that, for every dyadic $n$ and every irreducible type $\\lambda\\vdash n$, \\begin{equation} D_\\lambda\\Tr\\!\\left(((B_n^*B_n)|_{V_\\lambda})^{p_*}\\right)\\le1. \\end{equation} Consequently $\\|B_n|_{V_\\lambda}\\|\\le D_\\lambda^{-1/(2p_*)}$. The moment in the display is the Schatten power $2p_*$, because $\\operatorname{Tr}(B_n^*B_n)^{p_*}=\\|B_n\\|_{2p_*}^{2p_*}$.","sourceOffset":101969,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves optimal-order mixing of the Thorp shuffle on $2^d$ cards. After $1600d$ complete shuffles, the full permutation law converges in total variation to uniform as $d\\to\\infty$, uniformly over initial decks. Together with the support lower bound of $2d-O(1)$, this gives mixing time $\\Theta(d)=\\Theta(\\log(2^d))$.\n\nThe linked supporting results include frame and conditional-list estimates, regular trace and spectral bounds, signed moments, and full-density $L^2$ control. They also bound reciprocal Specht-dimension sums and give the eight-block Fourier estimate used in the mixing argument.\n\nThe formalized supporting result turns bounds on partial-permutation cosets into Fourier bounds. Partition a finite set into $b\\ge1$ nonempty blocks, and let $f\\ge0$ be a subprobability","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":128235,"sourceFileCount":5,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Row-column-symmetry-and-contraction-of-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"238-08","slug":"Random-subspace-tests-and-trace-smoothing-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026","familyId":"238","title":"Random-subspace tests and trace smoothing for coordinate sweeps","abstract":"We prove that an absolute number of coordinate sweeps of the Thorp shuffle on $n=2^d$ positions brings the full permutation to total-variation distance at most $\\frac12n^{-5}$ from uniform, uniformly over the initial deck. Thus $O(d)$ physical shuffles suffice, which is optimal in order.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the Thorp shuffle randomizes $N=2^d$ labeled cards in $\\Theta(\\log N)$ physical shuffles, settling its optimal mixing order for power-of-two deck sizes. Convergence is in total variation from the worst initial ordering and concerns the entire permutation, not just individual card positions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Random-subspace-tests-and-trace-smoothing-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Random-subspace-tests-and-trace-smoothing-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Random-subspace-tests-and-trace-smoothing-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Random-subspace-tests-and-trace-smoothing-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Random-subspace-tests-and-trace-smoothing-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Random-subspace-tests-and-trace-smoothing-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/proof.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Random-subspace-tests-and-trace-smoothing-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that an absolute number of coordinate sweeps of the Thorp shuffle on $n=2^d$ positions brings the full permutation to total-variation distance at most $\\frac12n^{-5}$ from uniform, uniformly over the initial deck. Thus $O(d)$ physical shuffles suffice, which is optimal in order.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":351},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The regular-trace statement","sourceOffset":1891},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Three ranges of representations","sourceOffset":3918},{"kind":"section","title":"Coarse smoothing","sourceOffset":8231},{"kind":"section","title":"Rows close to the full size","sourceOffset":9688},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A deformation on partial decks","sourceOffset":10423},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Contact counts for the deformed kernel","sourceOffset":12331},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From placements to regular multiplicities","sourceOffset":15914},{"kind":"section","title":"Angles between grid subgroup projections","sourceOffset":18517},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Tagged realization and overlap normalization","sourceOffset":20817},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Retention under random-plane tests","sourceOffset":25732},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compression to orthogonal cell tags","sourceOffset":29113}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Regular-trace smoothing","label":"ten:lowplanes:main","text":"There is an absolute $P\\ge2$ such that for every dyadic $n\\ge2$, \\begin{equation} \\operatorname{Tr}_{\\rm reg}|Q_n|^P\\le1+n^{-10}. \\end{equation} If $v$ is an integer with $2v\\ge P$, then $v$ independent sweeps have total-variation distance at most $\\frac12n^{-5}$ from uniform, from every deterministic initial deck. Here $\\|\\mu-\\nu\\|_{\\rm TV}=\\tfrac12\\sum_g|\\mu(g)-\\nu(g)|$.","sourceOffset":2912,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Partial-deck smoothing","label":"prop:coarse-smoothing","text":"Let $n=2^d$, $d\\ge0$, and let $Q_n$ be a sweep in any fixed order of the distinct coordinates. For either $B_n=Q_n^*Q_n$ or $B_n=Q_nQ_n^*$, there are an absolute integer $u\\ge1$ and $C<\\infty$ such that \\begin{equation} B_n^u(x,y)\\le\\frac{e^{Cl}}{(n)_l},\\qquad 0\\le l\\le n, \\end{equation} for every pair of ordered injections $x,y$ of $l$ distinct cards. Here $(n)_l=n!/(n-l)!$, including $(n)_0=1$. In particular, \\begin{equation} \\operatorname{Tr}_{\\rm reg}|Q_n|^{2u}\\le e^{Cn}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":8452,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves optimal-order mixing of the Thorp shuffle on $2^d$ cards. After $1600d$ complete shuffles, the full permutation law converges in total variation to uniform as $d\\to\\infty$, uniformly over initial decks. Together with the support lower bound of $2d-O(1)$, this gives mixing time $\\Theta(d)=\\Theta(\\log(2^d))$.\n\nThe linked supporting results include frame and conditional-list estimates, regular trace and spectral bounds, signed moments, and full-density $L^2$ control. They also bound reciprocal Specht-dimension sums and give the eight-block Fourier estimate used in the mixing argument.\n\nThe formalized supporting result turns bounds on partial-permutation cosets into Fourier bounds. Partition a finite set into $b\\ge1$ nonempty blocks, and let $f\\ge0$ be a subprobability","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":41902,"sourceFileCount":3,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Random-subspace-tests-and-trace-smoothing-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"238-09","slug":"Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026","familyId":"238","title":"Compatibility entropy and the spectrum of a Thorp sweep","abstract":"We prove that the Thorp shuffle on $n=2^d$ cards mixes in $\\Theta(d)$ physical shuffles. After a sufficiently large fixed number of coordinate sweeps, the total-variation distance of the full permutation from uniform tends to zero as $d\\to\\infty$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the Thorp shuffle randomizes $N=2^d$ labeled cards in $\\Theta(\\log N)$ physical shuffles, settling its optimal mixing order for power-of-two deck sizes. Convergence is in total variation from the worst initial ordering and concerns the entire permutation, not just individual card positions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/build/figures/compatibility-square.tex","preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/build/sections/01-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/build/sections/02-entropy-route.tex","preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/build/sections/03-spectral-conversion.tex","preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/build/sections/04-conditional-ranks.tex","preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/build/sections/05-inverse-color.tex","preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/build/sections/06-tilted-arrays.tex","preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/build/sections/07-fractional-density.tex","preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/build/sections/08-asymmetric.tex","preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/build/sections/09-projection-deletion.tex","preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/build/sections/10-first-interactions.tex","preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/build/sections/11-harmonic-overlap.tex","preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/build/sections/12-regular-ranks.tex","preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/build/sections/13-collision-series.tex","preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/build/sections/14-grid-recursions.tex","preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/build/sections/15-singular-bands.tex","preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/build/sections/16-pointwise-smoothing.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the Thorp shuffle on $n=2^d$ cards mixes in $\\Theta(d)$ physical shuffles. After a sufficiently large fixed number of coordinate sweeps, the total-variation distance of the full permutation from uniform tends to zero as $d\\to\\infty$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":350},{"kind":"section","title":"The sweep, Fourier norms and common estimates","sourceOffset":16538},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Binary positions and sweep operators","sourceOffset":16749},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Plancherel and powers of a nonnormal sweep","sourceOffset":20008},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Injection multiplicities and inverse-degree sums","sourceOffset":21286},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positive powers and coefficient densities","sourceOffset":25347},{"kind":"section","title":"From compatibility entropy to a regular moment","sourceOffset":29939},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A sparse path second moment","sourceOffset":30777},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The compatibility entropy inequality","sourceOffset":37647},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positive coefficients and compatibility","sourceOffset":46321},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Trace recursion and completion","sourceOffset":49762},{"kind":"section","title":"Regular moments, singular ranks and recursive exponents","sourceOffset":56077}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"corollary","name":"Vanishing regular remainder","label":"tra:asymmetric:mixing","text":"Let $p^*=\\sup_n p_n<\\infty$ be the bound obtained above and let $\\Pi$ be uniform averaging. For every integer $j\\ge2p^*$, $\\|T_n^j-\\Pi\\|_{\\mathrm{HS}}^2\\longrightarrow0$ as $n\\to\\infty$.","sourceOffset":192854,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"tra:regular-ranks:main","text":"There is an absolute $c>0$ such that \\begin{equation} s_j(T_N)\\le j^{-c}\\qquad (1\\le j\\le N!) \\end{equation} for every power of two $N$.","sourceOffset":311476,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves optimal-order mixing of the Thorp shuffle on $2^d$ cards. After $1600d$ complete shuffles, the full permutation law converges in total variation to uniform as $d\\to\\infty$, uniformly over initial decks. Together with the support lower bound of $2d-O(1)$, this gives mixing time $\\Theta(d)=\\Theta(\\log(2^d))$.\n\nThe linked supporting results include frame and conditional-list estimates, regular trace and spectral bounds, signed moments, and full-density $L^2$ control. They also bound reciprocal Specht-dimension sums and give the eight-block Fourier estimate used in the mixing argument.\n\nThe formalized supporting result turns bounds on partial-permutation cosets into Fourier bounds. Partition a finite set into $b\\ge1$ nonempty blocks, and let $f\\ge0$ be a subprobability","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":468768,"sourceFileCount":19,"statementCount":43,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Compatibility-entropy-and-the-spectrum-of-a-Thorp-sweep-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"238-10","slug":"Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026","familyId":"238","title":"Signed tensor densities and diagram budgets for the Thorp shuffle","abstract":"We prove that a fixed number of coordinate sweeps of the Thorp shuffle on $2^d$ cards makes the squared L2 distance of the full permutation density from uniform tend to zero as $d\\to\\infty$. In particular, the total-variation mixing time is $\\Theta(d)$ physical shuffles.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the Thorp shuffle randomizes $N=2^d$ labeled cards in $\\Theta(\\log N)$ physical shuffles, settling its optimal mixing order for power-of-two deck sizes. Convergence is in total variation from the worst initial ordering and concerns the entire permutation, not just individual card positions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/figures/marked-grid.tex","preprints/Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/01-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/02-static.tex","preprints/Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/03-spin.tex","preprints/Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/03b-isotypic.tex","preprints/Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/04-named.tex","preprints/Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/05-lowering.tex","preprints/Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/06-compressed.tex","preprints/Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/07-simultaneous.tex","preprints/Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/08-inverse.tex","preprints/Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/09-hook-budget.tex","preprints/Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/sections/10-hybrid.tex","preprints/Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/figures/hybrid-chains.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that a fixed number of coordinate sweeps of the Thorp shuffle on $2^d$ cards makes the squared $L^2$ distance of the full permutation density from uniform tend to zero as $d\\to\\infty$. In particular, the total-variation mixing time is $\\Theta(d)$ physical shuffles.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":320},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prescribed signed occurrences","sourceOffset":1554},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The full permutation law","sourceOffset":3991},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof mechanism","sourceOffset":5524},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and mathematical background","sourceOffset":8449},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The later bounds","sourceOffset":11099},{"kind":"part","title":"The every-occurrence bound","sourceOffset":12364},{"kind":"section","title":"The physical chain, Fourier norms and dimension estimates","sourceOffset":12428},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Binary positions and sweep operators","sourceOffset":12646},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Plancherel and powers of a nonnormal sweep","sourceOffset":16135},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Injection multiplicities and inverse-degree sums","sourceOffset":17413},{"kind":"section","title":"Signed densities and pointwise stabilizers","sourceOffset":21315}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"A moment bound for every signed occurrence","label":"ten:spin:main","text":"For each sufficiently small fixed $\\bar\\eta>0$, choose a sufficiently small $\\bar\\kappa>0$ with $\\bar\\kappa<\\bar\\eta/256$. There is an integer $r\\ge1$, depending only on these fixed constants, such that for every $d\\ge1$, every $\\lambda\\vdash n=2^d$, and every occurrence \\eqref{ten:spin:eq:1}, \\begin{equation} \\log\\!\\left(D_\\lambda\\operatorname{Tr}_{V_\\lambda}(B_n^*B_n)^r\\right) \\le\\eta_d F(\\alpha,\\beta)+g_n(l), \\end{equation} where \\[ \\eta_d=\\bar\\eta\\left(1-\\frac1{2\\sqrt d}\\right),\\qquad \\kappa_d=\\bar\\kappa\\left(1-\\frac1{2\\sqrt d}\\right),\\qquad g_n(l)=\\max\\{0,\\kappa_d l\\log n-l\\log(n/l)\\}. \\] Set $g_n(0)=0$ and interpret the logarithm of a zero trace as $-\\infty$.","sourceOffset":2750,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Named-object moment bound","label":"ten:named:main","text":"For the constants fixed above, there are absolute choices of \\(C_0\\) and an integer \\(h\\ge1\\) such that, for every dyadic \\(n\\ge2\\), every \\(\\lambda\\vdash n\\), and every occurrence shown below with \\(m=|\\tau|\\), \\begin{equation} \\lambda\\ \\text{in}\\ \\mu*\\nu^{\\rm t}*\\tau \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad W_n(\\lambda) \\le \\exp\\{ \\beta_n \\mathcal S(\\mu,\\nu)+ R_n(m)\\}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":91918,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves optimal-order mixing of the Thorp shuffle on $2^d$ cards. After $1600d$ complete shuffles, the full permutation law converges in total variation to uniform as $d\\to\\infty$, uniformly over initial decks. Together with the support lower bound of $2d-O(1)$, this gives mixing time $\\Theta(d)=\\Theta(\\log(2^d))$.\n\nThe linked supporting results include frame and conditional-list estimates, regular trace and spectral bounds, signed moments, and full-density $L^2$ control. They also bound reciprocal Specht-dimension sums and give the eight-block Fourier estimate used in the mixing argument.\n\nThe formalized supporting result turns bounds on partial-permutation cosets into Fourier bounds. Partition a finite set into $b\\ge1$ nonempty blocks, and let $f\\ge0$ be a subprobability","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":377412,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":18,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Signed-tensor-densities-and-diagram-budgets-for-coordinate-sweeps-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"238-11","slug":"Conditional-coordinate-sweeps-and-analytic-transfer-September-26-2026","familyId":"238","title":"Conditional coordinate sweeps and analytic transfer","abstract":"For coordinate sweeps with near-uniform line laws and line sizes among powers of two in a suitable fixed interval, we prove a Schatten-moment bound after conditioning on any feasible collection of prescribed card trajectories. An analytic transfer to binary sweeps then shows that a fixed number of sweeps mixes the Thorp shuffle on $2^d$ cards in $O(d)$ physical steps, matching the order of the support lower bound.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the Thorp shuffle randomizes $N=2^d$ labeled cards in $\\Theta(\\log N)$ physical shuffles, settling its optimal mixing order for power-of-two deck sizes. Convergence is in total variation from the worst initial ordering and concerns the entire permutation, not just individual card positions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Conditional-coordinate-sweeps-and-analytic-transfer-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Conditional-coordinate-sweeps-and-analytic-transfer-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Conditional-coordinate-sweeps-and-analytic-transfer-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/Conditional-coordinate-sweeps-and-analytic-transfer-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Conditional-coordinate-sweeps-and-analytic-transfer-September-26-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Conditional-coordinate-sweeps-and-analytic-transfer-September-26-2026/build/sections/02-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Conditional-coordinate-sweeps-and-analytic-transfer-September-26-2026/build/sections/03-paths.tex","preprints/Conditional-coordinate-sweeps-and-analytic-transfer-September-26-2026/build/figures/trajectory.tex","preprints/Conditional-coordinate-sweeps-and-analytic-transfer-September-26-2026/build/sections/04-sparse.tex","preprints/Conditional-coordinate-sweeps-and-analytic-transfer-September-26-2026/build/sections/05-density.tex","preprints/Conditional-coordinate-sweeps-and-analytic-transfer-September-26-2026/build/sections/06-induction.tex","preprints/Conditional-coordinate-sweeps-and-analytic-transfer-September-26-2026/build/sections/07-analytic.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Conditional-coordinate-sweeps-and-analytic-transfer-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For coordinate sweeps with near-uniform line laws and line sizes among powers of two in a suitable fixed interval, we prove a Schatten-moment bound after conditioning on any feasible collection of prescribed card trajectories. An analytic transfer to binary sweeps then shows that a fixed number of sweeps mixes the Thorp shuffle on $2^d$ cards in $O(d)$ physical steps, matching the order of the support lower bound.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":463},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier work","sourceOffset":4619},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The conditional estimate and the proof","sourceOffset":7232},{"kind":"section","title":"Representation facts and normalization","sourceOffset":12882},{"kind":"section","title":"Prescribed trajectories and their cost","sourceOffset":17400},{"kind":"section","title":"Contraction at sparse representation levels","sourceOffset":26154},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A bounded number of coordinates","sourceOffset":32558},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Column types and the common perturbation interval","sourceOffset":40397},{"kind":"section","title":"Positive tensor densities for hook diagrams","sourceOffset":43552},{"kind":"section","title":"The conditional moment induction","sourceOffset":51937},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The hook estimate on the current grid","sourceOffset":54864},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Adding the cells outside the hook","sourceOffset":64214}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Binary sweep contraction","label":"thm:binary","text":"There are absolute constants \\(g>0\\) and \\(d_0\\) such that, for \\(d\\ge d_0\\) and every \\(\\lambda\\vdash 2^d\\), \\[ \\|K_\\lambda\\|_{\\op}\\le D_\\lambda^{-g}. \\] The sign representation is annihilated. There is an absolute positive integer \\(w\\) such that the law after \\(w\\) independent sweeps converges to uniform in total variation as \\(d\\to\\infty\\), uniformly over deterministic initial decks.","sourceOffset":2917,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Support obstruction","label":"prop:support","text":"For every integer \\(t\\ge0\\), \\[ \\|q_d^{*t}-U_N\\|_{\\TV}\\ge1-\\frac{2^{tN/2}}{N!}. \\] Consequently, \\[ t_{\\rm mix}(d)\\ge \\left\\lceil\\frac2N\\log_2\\!\\left(\\frac{3N!}{4}\\right)\\right\\rceil =2d-O(1). \\]","sourceOffset":3453,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves optimal-order mixing of the Thorp shuffle on $2^d$ cards. After $1600d$ complete shuffles, the full permutation law converges in total variation to uniform as $d\\to\\infty$, uniformly over initial decks. Together with the support lower bound of $2d-O(1)$, this gives mixing time $\\Theta(d)=\\Theta(\\log(2^d))$.\n\nThe linked supporting results include frame and conditional-list estimates, regular trace and spectral bounds, signed moments, and full-density $L^2$ control. They also bound reciprocal Specht-dimension sums and give the eight-block Fourier estimate used in the mixing argument.\n\nThe formalized supporting result turns bounds on partial-permutation cosets into Fourier bounds. Partition a finite set into $b\\ge1$ nonempty blocks, and let $f\\ge0$ be a subprobability","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":77616,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Conditional-coordinate-sweeps-and-analytic-transfer-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"238-12","slug":"A-strict-four-row-permanent-inequality-and-permutation-moments-September-26-2026","familyId":"238","title":"A strict four-row permanent inequality and permutation moments","abstract":"We prove a permanent inequality with exponent strictly below two for laws on S4 sufficiently close to uniform and with exactly uniform coordinate marginals. Applied to a four-row recursion, it shows that an absolute number of coordinate sweeps brings the full permutation of the Thorp shuffle on $N=2^d$ cards to total-variation distance tending to zero from uniform as $N\\to\\infty$. Thus the mixing time has the optimal order $O(\\log N)$ in physical shuffles.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the Thorp shuffle randomizes $N=2^d$ labeled cards in $\\Theta(\\log N)$ physical shuffles, settling its optimal mixing order for power-of-two deck sizes. Convergence is in total variation from the worst initial ordering and concerns the entire permutation, not just individual card positions.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-strict-four-row-permanent-inequality-and-permutation-moments-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-strict-four-row-permanent-inequality-and-permutation-moments-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-strict-four-row-permanent-inequality-and-permutation-moments-September-26-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/A-strict-four-row-permanent-inequality-and-permutation-moments-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-strict-four-row-permanent-inequality-and-permutation-moments-September-26-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-strict-four-row-permanent-inequality-and-permutation-moments-September-26-2026/build/sections/02-permanent.tex","preprints/A-strict-four-row-permanent-inequality-and-permutation-moments-September-26-2026/build/sections/03-recursion.tex","preprints/A-strict-four-row-permanent-inequality-and-permutation-moments-September-26-2026/build/sections/04-multiplicities.tex","preprints/A-strict-four-row-permanent-inequality-and-permutation-moments-September-26-2026/build/sections/05-sparse.tex","preprints/A-strict-four-row-permanent-inequality-and-permutation-moments-September-26-2026/build/sections/06-tree.tex","preprints/A-strict-four-row-permanent-inequality-and-permutation-moments-September-26-2026/build/sections/07-amplification.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-strict-four-row-permanent-inequality-and-permutation-moments-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a permanent inequality with exponent strictly below two for laws on $S_4$ sufficiently close to uniform and with exactly uniform coordinate marginals. Applied to a four-row recursion, it shows that an absolute number of coordinate sweeps brings the full permutation of the Thorp shuffle on $N=2^d$ cards to total-variation distance tending to zero from uniform as $N\\to\\infty$. Thus the mixing time has the optimal order $O(\\log N)$ in physical shuffles.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":509},{"kind":"section","title":"The strict permanent inequality","sourceOffset":8524},{"kind":"section","title":"From columns to a four-row moment recursion","sourceOffset":14287},{"kind":"section","title":"Squared multiplicities and hook deficits","sourceOffset":24974},{"kind":"section","title":"Sparse stopping by encounter forests","sourceOffset":34782},{"kind":"section","title":"Summing the costs and comparing dimensions","sourceOffset":45080},{"kind":"section","title":"Independent forward sweeps","sourceOffset":55384}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"A robust four-row inequality","label":"thm:permanent","text":"There are absolute constants $p_0\\in(4/3,2)$ and $\\varepsilon>0$ with the following property. Let $\\nu$ be a probability measure on $S_4$ such that \\[ \\|\\nu-u\\|_{\\TV}<\\varepsilon, \\qquad \\nu\\{\\pi:\\pi(i)=j\\}=\\frac14\\quad(1\\le i,j\\le4). \\] Then every four nonnegative functions on $\\{1,2,3,4\\}$ satisfy \\begin{equation} \\E_\\nu\\prod_{i=1}^4f_i(\\pi(i))\\le\\prod_{i=1}^4\\|f_i\\|_{p_0}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":2217,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"A fixed moment with a degree saving","label":"thm:moment","text":"There exist an even integer $r\\ge2$ and $\\eta>0$, independent of $N$, such that for every dyadic $N$ and every $\\lambda\\vdash N$, \\begin{equation} D_\\lambda\\Tr B_N(\\lambda)^r\\le D_\\lambda^{1-\\eta}. \\end{equation} Consequently there is an absolute integer $q$ for which the total-variation distance of $q$ independent forward sweeps from uniform tends to zero as $N\\to\\infty$, uniformly over the starting deck.","sourceOffset":4793,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves optimal-order mixing of the Thorp shuffle on $2^d$ cards. After $1600d$ complete shuffles, the full permutation law converges in total variation to uniform as $d\\to\\infty$, uniformly over initial decks. Together with the support lower bound of $2d-O(1)$, this gives mixing time $\\Theta(d)=\\Theta(\\log(2^d))$.\n\nThe linked supporting results include frame and conditional-list estimates, regular trace and spectral bounds, signed moments, and full-density $L^2$ control. They also bound reciprocal Specht-dimension sums and give the eight-block Fourier estimate used in the mixing argument.\n\nThe formalized supporting result turns bounds on partial-permutation cosets into Fourier bounds. Partition a finite set into $b\\ge1$ nonempty blocks, and let $f\\ge0$ be a subprobability","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/238.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":58339,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-strict-four-row-permanent-inequality-and-permutation-moments-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"239-01","slug":"The-sharp-exponential-rate-of-singularity-for-symmetric-Bernoulli-matrices-October-3-2026","familyId":"239","title":"The sharp exponential rate of singularity for symmetric Bernoulli matrices","abstract":"Let An be a symmetric $n\\times n$ matrix whose entries on and above the diagonal are independent uniform signs. We prove $\\displaystyle \\Pr(\\det A_n=0)=\\left(\\frac12+o(1)\\right)^n.$","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Determines the sharp exponential singularity rate of symmetric random sign matrices with independent entries on and above the diagonal. Uniform signs give $\\Pr(\\det A_n=0)=(1/2+o(1))^n$; for fixed bias $p\\in(0,1)\\setminus\\{1/2\\}$, the rate is $(p^2+(1-p)^2+o(1))^n$. In the biased case, agreeing rows attain this rate.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-sharp-exponential-rate-of-singularity-for-symmetric-Bernoulli-matrices-October-3-2026/symmetric-bernoulli-singularity.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-sharp-exponential-rate-of-singularity-for-symmetric-Bernoulli-matrices-October-3-2026/symmetric-bernoulli-singularity.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-sharp-exponential-rate-of-singularity-for-symmetric-Bernoulli-matrices-October-3-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-sharp-exponential-rate-of-singularity-for-symmetric-Bernoulli-matrices-October-3-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-sharp-exponential-rate-of-singularity-for-symmetric-Bernoulli-matrices-October-3-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-sharp-exponential-rate-of-singularity-for-symmetric-Bernoulli-matrices-October-3-2026/build/sections/02-framework.tex","preprints/The-sharp-exponential-rate-of-singularity-for-symmetric-Bernoulli-matrices-October-3-2026/build/sections/03-lattice-tools.tex","preprints/The-sharp-exponential-rate-of-singularity-for-symmetric-Bernoulli-matrices-October-3-2026/build/sections/04-low-height-cover.tex","preprints/The-sharp-exponential-rate-of-singularity-for-symmetric-Bernoulli-matrices-October-3-2026/build/sections/05-typical-matrices.tex","preprints/The-sharp-exponential-rate-of-singularity-for-symmetric-Bernoulli-matrices-October-3-2026/build/sections/06-discriminant.tex","preprints/The-sharp-exponential-rate-of-singularity-for-symmetric-Bernoulli-matrices-October-3-2026/build/sections/07-minor-moves.tex","preprints/The-sharp-exponential-rate-of-singularity-for-symmetric-Bernoulli-matrices-October-3-2026/build/figures/layers.tex","preprints/The-sharp-exponential-rate-of-singularity-for-symmetric-Bernoulli-matrices-October-3-2026/build/sections/08-cube-concentration.tex","preprints/The-sharp-exponential-rate-of-singularity-for-symmetric-Bernoulli-matrices-October-3-2026/build/sections/09-certificate-pricing.tex","preprints/The-sharp-exponential-rate-of-singularity-for-symmetric-Bernoulli-matrices-October-3-2026/build/sections/10-potential-step.tex","preprints/The-sharp-exponential-rate-of-singularity-for-symmetric-Bernoulli-matrices-October-3-2026/build/sections/11-minor-paths.tex","preprints/The-sharp-exponential-rate-of-singularity-for-symmetric-Bernoulli-matrices-October-3-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-sharp-exponential-rate-of-singularity-for-symmetric-Bernoulli-matrices-October-3-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let \\(A_n\\) be a symmetric \\(n\\times n\\) matrix whose entries on and above the diagonal are independent uniform signs. We prove \\[ \\Pr(\\det A_n=0)=\\left(\\frac12+o(1)\\right)^n. \\]","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":256},{"kind":"section","title":"The binary model and the proof framework","sourceOffset":6048},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From signs to bits","sourceOffset":6346},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Admissible columns","sourceOffset":11514},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A potential on the discriminant group","sourceOffset":13378},{"kind":"section","title":"Lattice tools and robust subsets","sourceOffset":17117},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Determinants, quotients, and rational height","sourceOffset":17474},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Gaussian counts and determinant minimization","sourceOffset":24560},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Robust subsets and small determinants","sourceOffset":29195},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite cover for subspaces of controlled height","sourceOffset":35297},{"kind":"section","title":"Typical rank and spectral properties","sourceOffset":51504},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform rank tests","sourceOffset":52326}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For the symmetric Bernoulli matrix \\(A_n\\), \\[ \\Pr(\\det A_n=0)=\\left(\\frac12+o(1)\\right)^n =2^{-n+o(n)} \\qquad\\text{as }n\\to\\infty. \\]","sourceOffset":738,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Admissible-column estimate","label":"prop:admissible-potential","text":"For every fixed \\(A>0\\), typically for nonsingular binary matrices \\(B\\) of size \\(m\\in\\mathcal I_N\\), \\[ |\\mathcal S(B)|\\le 2^{N(\\Psi(B)+\\ell^{-A})}. \\]","sourceOffset":15503,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":208230,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-sharp-exponential-rate-of-singularity-for-symmetric-Bernoulli-matrices-October-3-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"239-02","slug":"The-sharp-singularity-rate-for-biased-symmetric-sign-matrices-October-4-2026","familyId":"239","title":"The sharp singularity rate for biased symmetric sign matrices","abstract":"Let An be a symmetric random matrix whose entries on and above the diagonal are independent signs, equal to 1 with fixed probability $p\\in(0,1)\\setminus\\{1/2\\}$. We prove that $\\mathbb P(\\det A_n=0)=(p^2+(1-p)^2+o(1))^n$. The rate is attained by the event that two rows agree.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Determines the sharp exponential singularity rate of symmetric random sign matrices with independent entries on and above the diagonal. Uniform signs give $\\Pr(\\det A_n=0)=(1/2+o(1))^n$; for fixed bias $p\\in(0,1)\\setminus\\{1/2\\}$, the rate is $(p^2+(1-p)^2+o(1))^n$. In the biased case, agreeing rows attain this rate.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-sharp-singularity-rate-for-biased-symmetric-sign-matrices-October-4-2026/biased-symmetric-sign-singularity.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-sharp-singularity-rate-for-biased-symmetric-sign-matrices-October-4-2026/biased-symmetric-sign-singularity.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-sharp-singularity-rate-for-biased-symmetric-sign-matrices-October-4-2026","subjectId":"15","subject":"Probability and statistical mechanics","subjectZh":"機率與統計力學","texPaths":["preprints/The-sharp-singularity-rate-for-biased-symmetric-sign-matrices-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-sharp-singularity-rate-for-biased-symmetric-sign-matrices-October-4-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-sharp-singularity-rate-for-biased-symmetric-sign-matrices-October-4-2026/build/sections/02-reductions.tex","preprints/The-sharp-singularity-rate-for-biased-symmetric-sign-matrices-October-4-2026/build/sections/03-typical.tex","preprints/The-sharp-singularity-rate-for-biased-symmetric-sign-matrices-October-4-2026/build/sections/04-lattice.tex","preprints/The-sharp-singularity-rate-for-biased-symmetric-sign-matrices-October-4-2026/build/sections/05-pairings.tex","preprints/The-sharp-singularity-rate-for-biased-symmetric-sign-matrices-October-4-2026/build/figures/layers.tex","preprints/The-sharp-singularity-rate-for-biased-symmetric-sign-matrices-October-4-2026/build/sections/06-paths.tex","preprints/The-sharp-singularity-rate-for-biased-symmetric-sign-matrices-October-4-2026/build/figures/terminal-window.tex","preprints/The-sharp-singularity-rate-for-biased-symmetric-sign-matrices-October-4-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-sharp-singularity-rate-for-biased-symmetric-sign-matrices-October-4-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Let $A_n$ be a symmetric random matrix whose entries on and above the diagonal are independent signs, equal to $1$ with fixed probability $p\\in(0,1)\\setminus\\{1/2\\}$. We prove that $\\Prob(\\det A_n=0)=(p^2+(1-p)^2+o(1))^n$. The rate is attained by the event that two rows agree.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":324},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":1294},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its main ingredients","sourceOffset":2979},{"kind":"section","title":"From kernel vectors to possible columns","sourceOffset":7239},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Corank one and tails close to sparse vectors","sourceOffset":8998},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The conditioned bit model and the column estimate","sourceOffset":16894},{"kind":"section","title":"Lattice tools and a prime-corank bound","sourceOffset":26060},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Corank over all prime fields","sourceOffset":26561},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Lattice height and a cover of rational subspaces","sourceOffset":29063},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removing directions of small determinant","sourceOffset":30661},{"kind":"section","title":"A simultaneous bound for lattice intersections","sourceOffset":34147},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From a large intersection to separated quotient sums","sourceOffset":36012}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed $p\\in(0,1)\\setminus\\{1/2\\}$, \\[ \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\Prob\\bigl(\\det A_n^{(p)}=0\\bigr)^{1/n} =p^2+(1-p)^2. \\]","sourceOffset":721,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Column estimate","label":"prop:column-bound","text":"For every fixed \\(L_0\\ge1\\) and every sufficiently small fixed \\(e_0>0\\), a matrix \\(B\\) in the bit model of size \\(m=N-1\\) satisfies, for all sufficiently large \\(N\\), \\[ \\Prob\\left( B\\text{ is nonsingular and } |\\mathcal S_{L_0}(B)|>2^{e_0N} \\right) \\le 2^{-N(2a-10e_0)}. \\]","sourceOffset":20285,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":114670,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-sharp-singularity-rate-for-biased-symmetric-sign-matrices-October-4-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"240-01","slug":"A-CH-Obstruction-to-a-Prescribed-Categoricity-Threshold-September-24-2026","familyId":"240","title":"A CH obstruction to a prescribed categoricity threshold","abstract":"Assuming the continuum hypothesis, we construct an abstract elementary class with Löwenheim–Skolem number ℵ0 that is categorical in every sufficiently large cardinal but has at least two nonisomorphic models of cardinality $\\beth_{\\omega_2}$. Thus categoricity does not transfer down to the proposed bound $\\beth_{(2^{\\aleph_0})^+}$, which equals $\\beth_{\\omega_2}$ under CH. Consequently, if ZFC is consistent, the prescribed-threshold form of Shelah's categoricity conjecture is not provable in ZFC.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Shelah's eventual categoricity conjecture in ZFC: for each bound on the Löwenheim–Skolem number, a uniform threshold makes categoricity of an abstract elementary class in one cardinal above that threshold imply categoricity throughout the same tail. Categoricity means uniqueness up to isomorphism at a given cardinality. Under the continuum hypothesis, a proposed specific Hanf threshold need not suffice.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-CH-Obstruction-to-a-Prescribed-Categoricity-Threshold-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-CH-Obstruction-to-a-Prescribed-Categoricity-Threshold-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-CH-Obstruction-to-a-Prescribed-Categoricity-Threshold-September-24-2026","subjectId":"6","subject":"Mathematical logic","subjectZh":"數理邏輯","texPaths":["preprints/A-CH-Obstruction-to-a-Prescribed-Categoricity-Threshold-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-CH-Obstruction-to-a-Prescribed-Categoricity-Threshold-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/A-CH-Obstruction-to-a-Prescribed-Categoricity-Threshold-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/A-CH-Obstruction-to-a-Prescribed-Categoricity-Threshold-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-colors.tex","preprints/A-CH-Obstruction-to-a-Prescribed-Categoricity-Threshold-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-aec.tex","preprints/A-CH-Obstruction-to-a-Prescribed-Categoricity-Threshold-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-endpoint.tex","preprints/A-CH-Obstruction-to-a-Prescribed-Categoricity-Threshold-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-categoricity.tex","preprints/A-CH-Obstruction-to-a-Prescribed-Categoricity-Threshold-September-24-2026/build/figures/matrix-decomposition.tex","preprints/A-CH-Obstruction-to-a-Prescribed-Categoricity-Threshold-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-conclusion.tex","preprints/A-CH-Obstruction-to-a-Prescribed-Categoricity-Threshold-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-booleanization.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-CH-Obstruction-to-a-Prescribed-Categoricity-Threshold-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"Assuming the continuum hypothesis, we construct an abstract elementary class with L\\\"owenheim--Skolem number \\(\\aleph_0\\) that is categorical in every sufficiently large cardinal but has at least two nonisomorphic models of cardinality \\(\\beth_{\\omega_2}\\). Thus categoricity does not transfer down to the proposed bound \\(\\beth_{(2^{\\aleph_0})^+}\\), which equals \\(\\beth_{\\omega_2}\\) under CH. Consequently, if ZFC is consistent, the prescribed-threshold form of Shelah's categoricity conjecture is not provable in ZFC.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":567},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The prescribed threshold and the result","sourceOffset":1391},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The mechanism of the construction","sourceOffset":5730},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":10311},{"kind":"section","title":"Countable diagrams and their tests","sourceOffset":11101},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Diagrams, codes, and restrictions","sourceOffset":12228},{"kind":"section","title":"The abstract elementary class","sourceOffset":18498},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The axioms and the basic structures","sourceOffset":18897},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Colors, objects, and strong inclusions","sourceOffset":28349},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Isomorphisms, the partial order, and coherence","sourceOffset":33495},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Directed unions and smoothness","sourceOffset":36166},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The downward L\\\"owenheim--Skolem bound","sourceOffset":42081}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Assume the continuum hypothesis. There is an abstract elementary class \\(K\\) in a countable finitary relational language such that \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item \\(\\LS(K)=\\aleph_0\\); \\item \\(K\\) has at least two nonisomorphic models of cardinality \\(\\beth_{\\omega_2}=H(K)\\); \\item putting \\(\\Lambda=\\beth_{(2^{\\aleph_1})^+}\\), the class \\(K\\) is categorical in every cardinal \\(\\mu\\ge\\Lambda\\). \\end{enumerate} In particular, \\(\\lambda=\\Lambda^+>H(K)\\) is a categoricity cardinal from which downward transfer to \\(H(K)\\) fails.","sourceOffset":4564,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"aec:class","text":"The pair \\(K=(\\mathcal C,\\le_K)\\) of Definition \\ref{aec:objects} is an abstract elementary class in a fixed countable finitary relational language, with \\[ \\LS(K)=\\aleph_0. \\] It has the union and common-upper-bound properties for every nonempty set-indexed directed family of strong substructures.","sourceOffset":47137,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Valid diagrams","label":"col:diagram","text":"An arity-\\(k\\) diagram is a triple \\(\\mathcal D=(E,R,\\rho)\\), where \\(E,R\\) are binary relations on \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) and \\(\\rho:\\mathcal L_k\\to\\delta\\). It is valid if the following conditions hold. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item \\(E\\) is an equivalence relation. The relation \\(R\\) and the tags respect \\(E\\): whenever \\(aEa'\\) and \\(bEb'\\), \\[ aRb\\ \\Longleftrightarrow\\ a'Rb', \\qquad \\rho(a)=\\rho(a'). \\] \\item On the quotient \\(Q_{\\mathcal D}=\\mathcal L_k/E\\), use \\(R\\) and \\(\\rho\\) also for the induced relation and tag function. They satisfy \\[ yRx\\ \\Longrightarrow\\ \\rho(y)<","sourceOffset":12545,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"The full subsequence test","label":"col:test","text":"Given \\(k\\ge1\\) and colors \\((s_J:\\varnothing\\ne J\\subseteq[k])\\), let \\[ T_k\\bigl((s_J)_J\\bigr) \\] mean that both of the following conditions hold. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item For every nonempty \\(J\\subseteq[k]\\), the color \\(s_J\\) decodes under \\(\\iota_{|J|}\\) to a valid diagram \\(\\mathcal D_J\\). \\item For every nonempty \\(J\\subseteq[k]\\), \\[ \\mathcal D_J =\\mathcal D_{[k]}\\restriction J. \\] \\end{enumerate} For an ordered tuple \\(t=(x_1,\\ldots,x_k)\\) of distinct elements, write \\(t\\restriction J=(x_{j_1},\\ldots,x_{j_m})\\). Colors attached to \\(t\\) and its nonempty subseq","sourceOffset":16000,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/240.md","scopeText":"Under the continuum hypothesis, the formalized result refutes the proposed transfer of categoricity down to the Hanf threshold. It gives an abstract elementary class in a countable relational language, with Löwenheim–Skolem number $\\aleph_0$, that has two nonisomorphic models at $\\beth_{\\omega_2}=h(\\aleph_0)$ but is categorical in every cardinal at least $\\beth_{(2^{\\aleph_1})^+}$.\n\nNo amalgamation, joint embedding, tameness, or absence-of-maximal-models assumption is imposed. The later canonical-point obstruction and the separate eventual-categoricity theorem are not included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/240.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":104076,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":6,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-CH-Obstruction-to-a-Prescribed-Categoricity-Threshold-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"240-02","slug":"Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026","familyId":"240","title":"Eventual categoricity for abstract elementary classes","abstract":"We prove Shelah's eventual categoricity conjecture for abstract elementary classes in ZFC. For each infinite bound on the Löwenheim–Skolem number there is a uniform threshold such that categoricity in any one cardinal at or above that threshold implies categoricity in every cardinal at or above the same threshold.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Shelah's eventual categoricity conjecture in ZFC: for each bound on the Löwenheim–Skolem number, a uniform threshold makes categoricity of an abstract elementary class in one cardinal above that threshold imply categoricity throughout the same tail. Categoricity means uniqueness up to isomorphism at a given cardinality. Under the continuum hypothesis, a proposed specific Hanf threshold need not suffice.","pdfPath":"preprints/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026","subjectId":"6","subject":"Mathematical logic","subjectZh":"數理邏輯","texPaths":["preprints/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-route.tex","preprints/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-reduction-orders.tex","preprints/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-reversal.tex","preprints/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-diagrams.tex","preprints/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-working-schemes.tex","preprints/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-calculus.tex","preprints/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-isolation.tex","preprints/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-marked-increments.tex","preprints/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-marked-boundaries.tex","preprints/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-geometry.tex","preprints/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026/build/sections/11-geometric-systems.tex","preprints/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026/build/sections/12-geometric-boundaries.tex","preprints/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026/build/sections/13-uniform-closure.tex","preprints/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026/build/sections/14-cardinal-transfer.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Shelah's eventual categoricity conjecture for abstract elementary classes in ZFC. For each infinite bound on the L\\\"owenheim--Skolem number there is a uniform threshold such that categoricity in any one cardinal at or above that threshold implies categoricity in every cardinal at or above the same threshold.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":392},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The statement","sourceOffset":825},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and scope","sourceOffset":5492},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":11292},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":19307},{"kind":"section","title":"Set codes and models indexed by orders","sourceOffset":19953},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite-arity partition argument","sourceOffset":29545},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A fixed presentation on every order","sourceOffset":32630},{"kind":"section","title":"Reversing maps on bounded tuples","sourceOffset":39021},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite-support orders and their cuts","sourceOffset":39967},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The orbit record of a short sequence","sourceOffset":48559},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bounded names for model orbits","sourceOffset":54988}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform eventual categoricity","label":"main:uniform","text":"For every infinite cardinal $\\lambda$ there is a cardinal $\\mu(\\lambda)\\ge\\lambda$ such that the following holds for every AEC $K$ with $\\LS(K)\\le\\lambda$: \\[ \\bigl(\\exists\\kappa\\ge\\mu(\\lambda)\\quad K\\text{ is categorical in }\\kappa\\bigr) \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\bigl(\\forall\\kappa'\\ge\\mu(\\lambda)\\quad K\\text{ is categorical in }\\kappa'\\bigr). \\] The same threshold is used in the premise and the conclusion.","sourceOffset":2713,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"The qualitative form","label":"main:qualitative","text":"An AEC categorical in unboundedly many cardinals is categorical on a tail of the cardinals.","sourceOffset":4124,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Abstract elementary class","label":"main:aec","text":"An abstract elementary class (AEC) is a pair $K=(\\mathcal C,\\le_K)$, where $\\mathcal C$ is a class of structures in a fixed language $\\tau$ and $\\le_K$ is a partial order satisfying the following conditions. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\arabic*)}] \\item The class is closed under isomorphism. If $M\\le_K N$, then $M$ is a $\\tau$-substructure of $N$. If $f:N\\cong N'$ and $M\\le_K N$, then $f[M]\\le_K N'$. \\item Coherence: if $M_0$ is a $\\tau$-substructure of $M_1$, and $M_0\\le_K M_2$ and $M_1\\le_K M_2$, then $M_0\\le_K M_1$. \\item For every nonzero ordinal $\\delta$ and increasing chain $(M_i:i<","sourceOffset":1102,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"found:orders-definition","text":"For an infinite limit ordinal $\\delta$, set \\[ \\Gamma_\\delta=\\Z^{(\\delta)},\\qquad H(\\delta)=\\Q^{(\\Gamma_\\delta)}. \\] Parentheses denote finite support. Both groups are ordered by their greatest differing coordinate. Write $e_\\alpha$ for the integer basis vector at $\\alpha<\\delta$ and $u_g$ for the rational basis vector at $g\\in\\Gamma_\\delta$. The rank of a nonzero vector is its greatest support coordinate. Unless group operations are explicitly mentioned, $H(\\delta)$ is used as a pure linear order; in particular $\\Aut(H(\\delta))$ means its order automorphism group. A cut of an order $L$ is a p","sourceOffset":40018,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/240.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":464642,"sourceFileCount":16,"statementCount":50,"definitionCount":17,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Eventual-Categoricity-for-Abstract-Elementary-Classes-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"241-01","slug":"Rigidity-of-the-Turing-degrees-September-24-2026","familyId":"241","title":"Rigidity of the Turing degrees","abstract":"We prove that every order automorphism of the full partial order of Turing degrees is the identity, resolving the rigidity conjecture for the Turing degrees positively.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Every order automorphism of the Turing degrees is the identity, resolving their rigidity problem. Thus no nontrivial relabeling of degrees preserves the ordering by relative computability.","pdfPath":"preprints/Rigidity-of-the-Turing-degrees-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Rigidity-of-the-Turing-degrees-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Rigidity-of-the-Turing-degrees-September-24-2026","subjectId":"6","subject":"Mathematical logic","subjectZh":"數理邏輯","texPaths":["preprints/Rigidity-of-the-Turing-degrees-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-the-Turing-degrees-September-24-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-the-Turing-degrees-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-the-Turing-degrees-September-24-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-the-Turing-degrees-September-24-2026/build/sections/representation.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-the-Turing-degrees-September-24-2026/build/sections/recovery.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-the-Turing-degrees-September-24-2026/build/figures/bounded-recurrence.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-the-Turing-degrees-September-24-2026/build/sections/rigidity.tex","preprints/Rigidity-of-the-Turing-degrees-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Rigidity-of-the-Turing-degrees-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every order automorphism of the full partial order of Turing degrees is the identity, resolving the rigidity conjecture for the Turing degrees positively.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":214},{"kind":"section","title":"Computability and category","sourceOffset":9221},{"kind":"section","title":"Representing an arbitrary automorphism","sourceOffset":15461},{"kind":"section","title":"Recovery from four values","sourceOffset":17745},{"kind":"section","title":"Rigidity and biinterpretability","sourceOffset":26870}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:rigidity","text":"Every order automorphism of the full Turing-degree structure is the identity: \\[ \\forall\\pi\\in\\Aut(\\DT,\\leT)\\ \\forall\\mathbf a\\in\\DT \\qquad \\pi(\\mathbf a)=\\mathbf a. \\]","sourceOffset":1198,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Slaman--Woodin","label":"thm:representation","text":"For every $\\pi\\in\\Aut(\\DT,\\leT)$ there is an arithmetic function $\\widehat F:2^\\N\\to2^\\N$ such that \\[ \\degT{\\widehat F(A)}=\\pi(\\degT A) \\qquad\\text{for every }A\\subseteq\\N. \\]","sourceOffset":15716,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/241.md","scopeText":"The rigidity problem asks whether the ordering of Turing degrees by relative computability has any nontrivial automorphism. The formalized result gives a negative answer: every order automorphism of the full Turing degrees of subsets of $\\mathbb N$ fixes every degree, with no definability or genericity assumption. The additional representation corollaries are not included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/241.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":33185,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Rigidity-of-the-Turing-degrees-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Rigidity-of-the-Turing-degrees-September-24-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"242-01","slug":"Single-fold-Diophantine-representations-September-24-2026","familyId":"242","title":"Single-fold Diophantine representations","abstract":"Every recursively enumerable set of natural-number tuples has a polynomial Diophantine representation with exactly one complete auxiliary tuple for each member. This proves the single-fold conjecture and, consequently, the finite-fold conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Every recursively enumerable set of tuples of natural numbers has a Diophantine representation with exactly one auxiliary solution for each member and none for nonmembers. This proves the single-fold conjecture and hence the finite-fold conjecture. Diophantine solvability over the nonnegative integers remains undecidable even with an at-most-one-solution promise.","pdfPath":"preprints/Single-fold-Diophantine-representations-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Single-fold-Diophantine-representations-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Single-fold-Diophantine-representations-September-24-2026","subjectId":"6","subject":"Mathematical logic","subjectZh":"數理邏輯","texPaths":["preprints/Single-fold-Diophantine-representations-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Single-fold-Diophantine-representations-September-24-2026/build/elliptic.tex","preprints/Single-fold-Diophantine-representations-September-24-2026/build/height-window-figure.tex","preprints/Single-fold-Diophantine-representations-September-24-2026/build/power.tex","preprints/Single-fold-Diophantine-representations-September-24-2026/build/computation.tex","preprints/Single-fold-Diophantine-representations-September-24-2026/build/consequences.tex","preprints/Single-fold-Diophantine-representations-September-24-2026/build/rank-one-proof.tex","preprints/Single-fold-Diophantine-representations-September-24-2026/build/witness-ledger.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Single-fold-Diophantine-representations-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every recursively enumerable set of natural-number tuples has a polynomial Diophantine representation with exactly one complete auxiliary tuple for each member. This proves the single-fold conjecture and, consequently, the finite-fold conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":320},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":5559},{"kind":"section","title":"Polynomial constructions with unique witnesses","sourceOffset":8959},{"kind":"section","title":"A rank-one curve with unique arithmetic codes","sourceOffset":13476},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The curve and its rational points","sourceOffset":13916},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Unique codes for rational coordinates","sourceOffset":14686},{"kind":"section","title":"A uniquely represented function with intermediate growth","sourceOffset":19977},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform heights and representatives","sourceOffset":20740},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Selecting and squaring an index","sourceOffset":24799},{"kind":"section","title":"A power graph from a growth function","sourceOffset":31700},{"kind":"section","title":"Testing containment of binary digits","sourceOffset":41905},{"kind":"section","title":"One witness for a halting computation","sourceOffset":45146}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $n\\ge1$ and every recursively enumerable set $S\\subseteq\\NN^n$, there are an integer $m\\ge1$ and a polynomial $P\\in\\ZZ[A_1,\\ldots,A_n,W_1,\\ldots,W_m]$ such that, for every $a\\in\\NN^n$, \\[ \\#\\{w\\in\\NN^m:P(a,w)=0\\}= \\begin{cases}1,&a\\in S,\\\\0,&a\\notin S.\\end{cases} \\]","sourceOffset":996,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Undecidability with an at-most-one-solution promise","label":"cor:unique-h10","text":"There exist integers $m,D\\ge1$ for which no algorithm has the following property: on every input polynomial $F\\in\\ZZ[W_1,\\ldots,W_m]$ of total degree at most $D$ satisfying the promise \\[ \\#\\{w\\in\\NN^m:F(w)=0\\}\\le1, \\] the algorithm halts and correctly decides whether this set is nonempty.","sourceOffset":5045,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":null,"text":"A single-fold polynomial system for a relation $R\\subseteq\\NN^d$ is a finite list of integer polynomial equations $F_i(a,z)=0$ such that the set of complete tuples $z$ is a singleton for $a\\in R$ and empty otherwise. A single-fold graph of a function $f:D\\to\\NN^e$, where $D\\subseteq\\NN^d$, is such a system for $\\{(a,f(a)):a\\in D\\}$. Thus each input $a\\in D$ determines both the output and all further auxiliary variables uniquely. Inputs outside $D$ have no solutions.","sourceOffset":9233,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/242.md","scopeText":"The single-fold Diophantine conjecture asks whether every recursively enumerable set has a polynomial representation with a unique auxiliary witness for each member. The formalization establishes this for every recursively enumerable subset of $\\mathbb N^n$, $n\\ge1$: an integer polynomial has exactly one complete natural-number witness tuple for members and none for nonmembers.\n\nThe consequence that solvability remains undecidable under an at-most-one-solution promise is not separately formalized.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/242.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":80800,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Single-fold-Diophantine-representations-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"243-01","slug":"Choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-does-not-capture-polynomial-time-September-23-2026","familyId":"243","title":"Choiceless polynomial time with counting does not capture polynomial time","abstract":"We prove that choiceless polynomial time with counting does not capture polynomial time on unordered finite structures, confirming the noncapture conjecture of Blass, Gurevich and Shelah. A linear-consistency query over 𝔽3 in a fixed binary vocabulary is decidable in polynomial time but not in the full counting formalism.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Confirms the Blass–Gurevich–Shelah noncapture conjecture: consistency of a linear system over 𝔽3 defines a polynomial-time query on unordered finite structures that choiceless polynomial time with counting cannot express. A separate result shows that adding witnessed symmetric choice strictly increases expressive power. Both separations hold for the full counting formalism, allowing hereditarily finite sets of arbitrary finite rank.","pdfPath":"preprints/Choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-does-not-capture-polynomial-time-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-does-not-capture-polynomial-time-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-does-not-capture-polynomial-time-September-23-2026","subjectId":"6","subject":"Mathematical logic","subjectZh":"數理邏輯","texPaths":["preprints/Choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-does-not-capture-polynomial-time-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-does-not-capture-polynomial-time-September-23-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-does-not-capture-polynomial-time-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-history.tex","preprints/Choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-does-not-capture-polynomial-time-September-23-2026/build/figures/proof-map.tex","preprints/Choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-does-not-capture-polynomial-time-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-structures.tex","preprints/Choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-does-not-capture-polynomial-time-September-23-2026/build/figures/unit-face.tex","preprints/Choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-does-not-capture-polynomial-time-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-support.tex","preprints/Choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-does-not-capture-polynomial-time-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-base-logic.tex","preprints/Choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-does-not-capture-polynomial-time-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-hf-transfer.tex","preprints/Choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-does-not-capture-polynomial-time-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-coding.tex","preprints/Choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-does-not-capture-polynomial-time-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-separation.tex","preprints/Choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-does-not-capture-polynomial-time-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-linear-algebraic-logics.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-does-not-capture-polynomial-time-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that choiceless polynomial time with counting does not capture polynomial time on unordered finite structures, confirming the noncapture conjecture of Blass, Gurevich and Shelah. A linear-consistency query over $\\mathbb F_3$ in a fixed binary vocabulary is decidable in polynomial time but not in the full counting formalism.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":408},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The separating query","sourceOffset":2199},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Main result","sourceOffset":4122},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and the obstruction to rank-dependent arguments","sourceOffset":6783},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":13812},{"kind":"section","title":"The grid structures","sourceOffset":20531},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Grid orientations and edge groups","sourceOffset":20947},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Atoms and binary relations","sourceOffset":23153},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Opposite answers","sourceOffset":27234},{"kind":"section","title":"Small orbits have sparse supports","sourceOffset":29817},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A central extension acting on the structures","sourceOffset":30707},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A sparse representative for an edge functional","sourceOffset":33907}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"The query $Q$ on all finite structures in the fixed vocabulary $\\tau$ of eight binary relations is isomorphism-invariant and decidable in deterministic polynomial time, but is not definable in full choiceless polynomial time with counting.","sourceOffset":4148,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:query-ptime","text":"The query $Q$ is isomorphism-invariant and decidable in deterministic polynomial time from an ordered encoding of its input structure.","sourceOffset":4715,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/243.md","scopeText":"The formalized result separates polynomial time from choiceless polynomial time with counting. It gives an explicit query on finite structures with eight relations that is invariant under isomorphism and decidable in polynomial time, but is not definable in the stated hereditarily finite-set language with cardinality and polynomial bounds on stages and intermediate objects. The query applies to all finite inputs without a promise. The separate witnessed-symmetric-choice result is not included.\n\nThe formalization proves that witnessed symmetric choice strictly increases the expressive power of choiceless polynomial time with counting. It constructs one fixed sentence with exactly one witnessed-choice occurrence that gives a Boolean answer on every finite input, while no sentence of the orig","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/243.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":103475,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-does-not-capture-polynomial-time-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"243-02","slug":"Witnessed-symmetric-choice-is-strictly-stronger-than-choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-September-24-2026","familyId":"243","title":"Witnessed symmetric choice is strictly stronger than choiceless polynomial time with counting","abstract":"We prove that witnessed symmetric choice strictly increases the expressive power of choiceless polynomial time with counting. A fixed sentence with one witnessed-choice occurrence defines a Boolean query on every finite input that is not definable in the original counting formalism.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Confirms the Blass–Gurevich–Shelah noncapture conjecture: consistency of a linear system over 𝔽3 defines a polynomial-time query on unordered finite structures that choiceless polynomial time with counting cannot express. A separate result shows that adding witnessed symmetric choice strictly increases expressive power. Both separations hold for the full counting formalism, allowing hereditarily finite sets of arbitrary finite rank.","pdfPath":"preprints/Witnessed-symmetric-choice-is-strictly-stronger-than-choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Witnessed-symmetric-choice-is-strictly-stronger-than-choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Witnessed-symmetric-choice-is-strictly-stronger-than-choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-September-24-2026","subjectId":"6","subject":"Mathematical logic","subjectZh":"數理邏輯","texPaths":["preprints/Witnessed-symmetric-choice-is-strictly-stronger-than-choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Witnessed-symmetric-choice-is-strictly-stronger-than-choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Witnessed-symmetric-choice-is-strictly-stronger-than-choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-formalism.tex","preprints/Witnessed-symmetric-choice-is-strictly-stronger-than-choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-structures.tex","preprints/Witnessed-symmetric-choice-is-strictly-stronger-than-choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-sentence.tex","preprints/Witnessed-symmetric-choice-is-strictly-stronger-than-choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-grid-behavior.tex","preprints/Witnessed-symmetric-choice-is-strictly-stronger-than-choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-September-24-2026/build/figures/grid-cycle.tex","preprints/Witnessed-symmetric-choice-is-strictly-stronger-than-choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-support.tex","preprints/Witnessed-symmetric-choice-is-strictly-stronger-than-choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-base-counting.tex","preprints/Witnessed-symmetric-choice-is-strictly-stronger-than-choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-hf-transfer.tex","preprints/Witnessed-symmetric-choice-is-strictly-stronger-than-choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-cpt-lower-bound.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Witnessed-symmetric-choice-is-strictly-stronger-than-choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that witnessed symmetric choice strictly increases the expressive power of choiceless polynomial time with counting. A fixed sentence with one witnessed-choice occurrence defines a Boolean query on every finite input that is not definable in the original counting formalism.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":329},{"kind":"section","title":"The computation model","sourceOffset":12889},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Hereditarily finite sets and ordinary iteration","sourceOffset":13165},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Witnessed choice and true-model classes","sourceOffset":15721},{"kind":"section","title":"Grid structures and their automorphisms","sourceOffset":18862},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The box and its edge states","sourceOffset":19728},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Atoms, relations, and charges","sourceOffset":22350},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A central extension acting on the full structure","sourceOffset":26112},{"kind":"section","title":"A witnessed-choice sentence on all inputs","sourceOffset":31316},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bounded notation, blocks, and distances","sourceOffset":32610},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ordering the block graph and choosing a tree","sourceOffset":37140},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparisons on four-cycles and the next choice","sourceOffset":38356}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist a vocabulary \\(\\tau\\) of eight binary relations, a fixed polynomial \\(p\\), and a \\(\\CPT+\\WSC\\) sentence \\((\\Phi,p)\\) with one occurrence of a WSC operator such that: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item \\((\\Phi,p)\\) has a Boolean value on every finite \\(\\tau\\)-structure; \\item no \\(\\CPT\\) sentence over \\(\\tau\\) has the same true-model class. \\end{enumerate} The \\(\\CPT\\) sentences in \\textup{(ii)} may use counting and hereditarily finite sets of unrestricted finite rank.","sourceOffset":2471,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"str:action","text":"For every $L\\ge2$ and $b\\in\\F^{V_L}$, the maps $h_{a,k}$ define a faithful action of $H$ by automorphisms of the entire $\\tau$-structure $A_b$. Every $h_{a,k}$ preserves each edge and configuration block setwise. For $k\\in K$, the central action $h_{0,k}$ fixes all face vectors and adds $k_e$ to the scalar coordinate on edge $e$.","sourceOffset":28107,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"sup:definition","text":"For an atom tuple $\\alpha=(\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_r)$, let \\[ K_\\alpha=\\{k\\in K:k\\cdot\\alpha_j=\\alpha_j \\text{ for every }1\\le j\\le r\\}. \\] The tuple $\\alpha$ is a $K$-support of an atom or hereditarily finite set $x$ over $A_b$ if every element of $K_\\alpha$ fixes $x$. The empty tuple is permitted, and $K_{()}=K$.","sourceOffset":82501,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/243.md","scopeText":"The formalized result separates polynomial time from choiceless polynomial time with counting. It gives an explicit query on finite structures with eight relations that is invariant under isomorphism and decidable in polynomial time, but is not definable in the stated hereditarily finite-set language with cardinality and polynomial bounds on stages and intermediate objects. The query applies to all finite inputs without a promise. The separate witnessed-symmetric-choice result is not included.\n\nThe formalization proves that witnessed symmetric choice strictly increases the expressive power of choiceless polynomial time with counting. It constructs one fixed sentence with exactly one witnessed-choice occurrence that gives a Boolean answer on every finite input, while no sentence of the orig","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/243.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":155200,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Witnessed-symmetric-choice-is-strictly-stronger-than-choiceless-polynomial-time-with-counting-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"244-01","slug":"The-Partition-Principle-does-not-imply-Choice-September-24-2026","familyId":"244","title":"The Partition Principle does not imply Choice","abstract":"We prove that the Partition Principle does not imply the Axiom of Choice: if ZF is consistent, then so is ZF with the Partition Principle, Choice for ordinal-indexed families, and the negation of the Axiom of Choice. Separately, over every countable transitive model of ZFC, we construct a transitive symmetric model of this theory with the same ordinals and no new countable sequences of ground elements.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Assuming ZF is consistent, constructs a model in which every surjective image of a set injects into that set, yet the axiom of choice fails. Choice for ordinal-indexed families still holds. From any countable transitive model of ZFC, a separate construction gives a transitive symmetric extension with these properties and no new countable sequences of ground-model elements.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Partition-Principle-does-not-imply-Choice-September-24-2026/partition-principle-without-choice.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Partition-Principle-does-not-imply-Choice-September-24-2026/partition-principle-without-choice.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Partition-Principle-does-not-imply-Choice-September-24-2026","subjectId":"6","subject":"Mathematical logic","subjectZh":"數理邏輯","texPaths":["preprints/The-Partition-Principle-does-not-imply-Choice-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Partition-Principle-does-not-imply-Choice-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-Partition-Principle-does-not-imply-Choice-September-24-2026/build/sections/monoid.tex","preprints/The-Partition-Principle-does-not-imply-Choice-September-24-2026/build/figures/probe-divisibility.tex","preprints/The-Partition-Principle-does-not-imply-Choice-September-24-2026/build/sections/forcing.tex","preprints/The-Partition-Principle-does-not-imply-Choice-September-24-2026/build/figures/support-descent.tex","preprints/The-Partition-Principle-does-not-imply-Choice-September-24-2026/build/sections/quotients.tex","preprints/The-Partition-Principle-does-not-imply-Choice-September-24-2026/build/figures/moved-generic-transport.tex","preprints/The-Partition-Principle-does-not-imply-Choice-September-24-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex","preprints/The-Partition-Principle-does-not-imply-Choice-September-24-2026/build/sections/metatheory.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Partition-Principle-does-not-imply-Choice-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the Partition Principle does not imply the Axiom of Choice: if ZF is consistent, then so is ZF with the Partition Principle, Choice for ordinal-indexed families, and the negation of the Axiom of Choice. Separately, over every countable transitive model of ZFC, we construct a transitive symmetric model of this theory with the same ordinals and no new countable sequences of ground elements.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":479},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and related methods","sourceOffset":2845},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":6725},{"kind":"section","title":"A monoid of diagrams","sourceOffset":11780},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The required algebra and the construction theorem","sourceOffset":12454},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The extension that inserts a probe","sourceOffset":17985},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Extending the real heights","sourceOffset":28426},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The extension that merges finitely many copies","sourceOffset":32750},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conservation under simulation","sourceOffset":38246},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The schedule and the countable-base rearrangement","sourceOffset":42756},{"kind":"section","title":"Product forcing and support locality","sourceOffset":52240},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The choice ground and the two forcings","sourceOffset":52754}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main-consistency","text":"If $\\ZF$ is consistent, then so is $\\ZF+\\PP+\\ACwo+\\neg\\AC$.","sourceOffset":1501,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main-transitive","text":"Let $V$ be a countable transitive model of $\\ZFC$. There are a set forcing $\\mathbb Q\\in V$, a $V$-generic filter $G$, and a transitive symmetric submodel $W$ of $V[G]$ such that \\[ V\\subseteq W\\subseteq V[G],\\qquad \\Ord^W=\\Ord^V,\\qquad W\\models\\ZF+\\PP+\\ACwo+\\neg\\AC. \\] Every countable sequence of elements of $V$ that belongs to $W$ already belongs to $V$.","sourceOffset":1681,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/244.md","scopeText":"The Partition Principle says that every surjection admits an injection in the reverse direction. The formalization proves the relative-consistency implication: if ZF is consistent, then so is ZF with the Partition Principle, Choice for ordinal-indexed families, and failure of the Axiom of Choice.\n\nThe linked model-construction result also constructs a transitive model of the Partition Principle without Choice from an externally countable transitive ground model satisfying the stated axioms, including Choice, and containing an internal strongly inaccessible cardinal. The paper's stronger transitive-model preservation assertions are outside that selected construction.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/244.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":155542,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Partition-Principle-does-not-imply-Choice-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"245-01","slug":"Weak-and-strong-normalization-in-pure-type-systems-September-25-2026","familyId":"245","title":"Weak and strong normalization in pure type systems","abstract":"We prove that every weakly β-normalizing pure type system is strongly β-normalizing. Both properties quantify over all legal expressions in all valid contexts, and reduction acts inside type annotations. No functionality hypothesis is required. This resolves the β-Barendregt–Geuvers–Klop conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that weak normalization implies strong normalization for every pure type system: if every legal expression in every valid context has a β-normal form, every β-reduction sequence terminates. This resolves the β-Barendregt–Geuvers–Klop conjecture, including nonfunctional rules and open contexts.","pdfPath":"preprints/Weak-and-strong-normalization-in-pure-type-systems-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Weak-and-strong-normalization-in-pure-type-systems-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Weak-and-strong-normalization-in-pure-type-systems-September-25-2026","subjectId":"6","subject":"Mathematical logic","subjectZh":"數理邏輯","texPaths":["preprints/Weak-and-strong-normalization-in-pure-type-systems-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Weak-and-strong-normalization-in-pure-type-systems-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Weak-and-strong-normalization-in-pure-type-systems-September-25-2026/build/sections/metatheory.tex","preprints/Weak-and-strong-normalization-in-pure-type-systems-September-25-2026/build/sections/profiles.tex","preprints/Weak-and-strong-normalization-in-pure-type-systems-September-25-2026/build/sections/observations.tex","preprints/Weak-and-strong-normalization-in-pure-type-systems-September-25-2026/build/sections/observation-free-spaces.inc.tex","preprints/Weak-and-strong-normalization-in-pure-type-systems-September-25-2026/build/sections/observation-bounds.inc.tex","preprints/Weak-and-strong-normalization-in-pure-type-systems-September-25-2026/build/sections/wrappers.tex","preprints/Weak-and-strong-normalization-in-pure-type-systems-September-25-2026/build/sections/channels.tex","preprints/Weak-and-strong-normalization-in-pure-type-systems-September-25-2026/build/sections/relational.tex","preprints/Weak-and-strong-normalization-in-pure-type-systems-September-25-2026/build/sections/erasure.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Weak-and-strong-normalization-in-pure-type-systems-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every weakly $\\beta$-normalizing pure type system is strongly $\\beta$-normalizing. Both properties quantify over all legal expressions in all valid contexts, and reduction acts inside type annotations. No functionality hypothesis is required. This resolves the $\\beta$-Barendregt--Geuvers--Klop conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":419},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pure type systems and normalization","sourceOffset":1230},{"kind":"section","title":"Structural properties","sourceOffset":13721},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conversion and preservation of typing","sourceOffset":21819},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Neutral expressions and normal forms","sourceOffset":26686},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite specifications and a common context","sourceOffset":29915},{"kind":"section","title":"Sort profiles and normalization candidates","sourceOffset":38375},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Profiles and the dependency graph","sourceOffset":39355},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Application tests and candidates","sourceOffset":46082},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The interface for one component","sourceOffset":52105},{"kind":"section","title":"Two component constructions","sourceOffset":67380},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Components without an odd closed walk","sourceOffset":67739}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"The $\\beta$-Barendregt--Geuvers--Klop conjecture","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $\\mathcal P=(\\mathcal S,\\mathcal A,\\mathcal R)$ be any pure type system. If every legal expression of $\\mathcal P$, in every valid context, is weakly $\\beta$-normalizing, then every such expression is strongly $\\beta$-normalizing. No functionality assumption is imposed on $\\mathcal A$ or $\\mathcal R$.","sourceOffset":5350,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Advancing one component","label":"prop:advance-component","text":"Under the hypotheses of Lemma \\ref{lem:sn-fundamental}, \\eqref{eq:processed-invariant} also holds for every actual type with profile in $S$.","sourceOffset":61782,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Specification and expressions","label":"pts:specification","text":"A pure type specification is a triple $\\mathcal P=(\\mathcal S,\\mathcal A,\\mathcal R)$, where $\\mathcal S$ is a set of sorts, $\\mathcal A\\subseteq\\mathcal S^2$ is a relation of axioms, and $\\mathcal R\\subseteq\\mathcal S^3$ is a relation of product rules. Neither relation is assumed functional. Using a countably infinite set of variables disjoint from $\\mathcal S$, form the raw expressions \\[ M,N,A,B ::= x\\mid s\\mid MN\\mid\\lambda x:A.M\\mid\\Pi x:A.B, \\qquad s\\in\\mathcal S. \\] Expressions are identified up to renaming bound variables. In each binder the variable binds in the body or codomain, but ","sourceOffset":1472,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Full beta reduction","label":"pts:beta","text":"The relation $\\to_\\beta$ is the compatible closure of \\[ (\\lambda x:A.M)N\\to_\\beta M[x:=N]. \\] Compatibility permits a step in either child of an application, product, or lambda. In particular, both $\\lambda x:A.M\\to_\\beta\\lambda x:A'.M$ when $A\\to_\\beta A'$ and $\\Pi x:A.B\\to_\\beta\\Pi x:A'.B$ are permitted. Write $\\to_\\beta^*$ for its reflexive-transitive closure and $=_\\beta$ for the equivalence relation it generates. A normal expression has no beta redex at any position. An expression is weakly normalizing if it reduces to a normal expression, and strongly normalizing if it starts no infinit","sourceOffset":2615,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/245.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves the $\\beta$-Barendregt–Geuvers–Klop conjecture for pure type systems: if every legal expression in every valid context has some terminating $\\beta$-reduction sequence, then every $\\beta$-reduction sequence from every such expression terminates. Reduction is allowed inside type annotations, and the specification may have arbitrary sorts and nonfunctional axioms or rules.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/245.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":267935,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":11,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Weak-and-strong-normalization-in-pure-type-systems-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"246-01","slug":"A-Modulus-Proof-of-Cannons-Conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"246","title":"A Modulus Proof of Cannon’s Conjecture","abstract":"We prove that every hyperbolic group whose boundary is homeomorphic to the two-sphere admits a proper cocompact isometric action on hyperbolic three-space with finite kernel. This resolves Cannon's conjecture positively.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Every word-hyperbolic group with boundary homeomorphic to S2 admits a proper cocompact isometric action on hyperbolic three-space with finite kernel, proving Cannon's conjecture. Every torsion-free such group is therefore the fundamental group of a closed hyperbolic three-manifold.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Modulus-Proof-of-Cannons-Conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Modulus-Proof-of-Cannons-Conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Modulus-Proof-of-Cannons-Conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/A-Modulus-Proof-of-Cannons-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-Modulus-Proof-of-Cannons-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/01_geometry.tex","preprints/A-Modulus-Proof-of-Cannons-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/02_scalar.tex","preprints/A-Modulus-Proof-of-Cannons-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/level-continuum.tex","preprints/A-Modulus-Proof-of-Cannons-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/03_exponential.tex","preprints/A-Modulus-Proof-of-Cannons-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/04_flow.tex","preprints/A-Modulus-Proof-of-Cannons-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/05_pairs.tex","preprints/A-Modulus-Proof-of-Cannons-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/pair-cost.tex","preprints/A-Modulus-Proof-of-Cannons-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Modulus-Proof-of-Cannons-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every hyperbolic group whose boundary is homeomorphic to the two-sphere admits a proper cocompact isometric action on hyperbolic three-space with finite kernel. This resolves Cannon's conjecture positively.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":318},{"kind":"section","title":"Boundary geometry and the modulus criterion","sourceOffset":9726},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Expansions","sourceOffset":10872},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Packing and paths","sourceOffset":14028},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The classical reduction","sourceOffset":16122},{"kind":"section","title":"A scalar limit from unbounded modulus","sourceOffset":23497},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Record scales and crossing duality","sourceOffset":24270},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Continuous functions with separating levels","sourceOffset":29846},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Oscillation and annular estimates","sourceOffset":36482},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The limiting function and measure","sourceOffset":41497},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two comparison tools","sourceOffset":44564},{"kind":"section","title":"Exponential records: comparing two magnifications","sourceOffset":47979}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Cannon's conjecture","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $G$ be a hyperbolic group with $\\partial G$ homeomorphic to $\\Sphere$. There is a homomorphism $\\rho:G\\to\\Isom(\\HH^3)$ for which the action on $\\HH^3$ is proper and cocompact. Its kernel is finite.","sourceOffset":937,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Bonk--Kleiner; Bourdon--Kleiner","label":"thm:uniformization","text":"If $\\sup_n M(n)<\\infty$, then $(Z,d)$ is quasi-M\\\"obius homeomorphic to the round sphere.","sourceOffset":17220,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/246.md","scopeText":"Cannon's conjecture asks whether a hyperbolic group with boundary homeomorphic to the two-sphere acts geometrically on hyperbolic three-space. The formalization proves that such a group admits an isometric action on $\\mathbb H^3$ that is proper and cocompact and has finite kernel. Hyperbolicity is expressed through uniformly thin geodesic triangles in a Cayley graph, and the boundary hypothesis is a homeomorphism with $S^2$.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/246.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":106544,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Modulus-Proof-of-Cannons-Conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"247-01","slug":"An-infinite-finitely-presented-residually-finite-2-group-October-5-2026","familyId":"247","title":"An infinite finitely presented residually finite 2-group","abstract":"We prove that the infinite, ordinarily finitely presented periodic Steinberg group $\\Gamma=\\mathop{\\mathrm{St}}\\nolimits _{12}(R)$ of a companion paper is residually finite. Its finite-index subgroup $G=\\ker(\\Gamma\\to\\mathop{\\mathrm{St}}\\nolimits _{12}(\\mathbb F_2))$ is infinite, ordinarily finitely presented, and residually finite, and every element of G has finite 2-power order. The orders of its elements are unbounded. Phases of long words in the companion's graded algebra allow finite degree truncations to detect all elements of Γ, including the central kernel.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs an infinite finitely presented residually finite group whose elements all have finite 2-power order, answering the finitely presented Burnside problem negatively even in this class. The construction also yields an infinite-dimensional finitely presented nil associative 𝔽2-algebra and a finitely presented infinite-dimensional algebraic unitization, giving negative answers to the corresponding nilpotence and Kurosh finiteness questions.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-residually-finite-2-group-October-5-2026/residually-finite-torsion.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-residually-finite-2-group-October-5-2026/residually-finite-torsion.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-residually-finite-2-group-October-5-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-residually-finite-2-group-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-residually-finite-2-group-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-residually-finite-2-group-October-5-2026/build/sections/ring-phases.tex","preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-residually-finite-2-group-October-5-2026/build/sections/steinberg.tex","preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-residually-finite-2-group-October-5-2026/build/sections/shifts.tex","preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-residually-finite-2-group-October-5-2026/build/sections/long-entries.tex","preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-residually-finite-2-group-October-5-2026/build/sections/detection.tex","preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-residually-finite-2-group-October-5-2026/build/sections/stability.tex","preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-residually-finite-2-group-October-5-2026/build/sections/construction.tex","preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-residually-finite-2-group-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-residually-finite-2-group-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the infinite, ordinarily finitely presented periodic Steinberg group $\\Gamma=\\St_{12}(R)$ of a companion paper is residually finite. Its finite-index subgroup $G=\\ker(\\Gamma\\to\\St_{12}(\\F_2))$ is infinite, ordinarily finitely presented, and residually finite, and every element of $G$ has finite $2$-power order. The orders of its elements are unbounded. Phases of long words in the companion's graded algebra allow finite degree truncations to detect all elements of $\\Gamma$, including the central kernel.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":567},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and relation to earlier constructions","sourceOffset":1519},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its main ingredients","sourceOffset":3919},{"kind":"section","title":"The algebra and phases of long words","sourceOffset":8610},{"kind":"section","title":"Steinberg groups and faithful strips","sourceOffset":18138},{"kind":"section","title":"Cyclic shifts and the relative central kernel","sourceOffset":24293},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Block roots and triangular evaluation","sourceOffset":25309},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removing the wrap of a cyclic shift","sourceOffset":30908},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Graded evaluation and powers of central elements","sourceOffset":36071},{"kind":"section","title":"Commutators of long entries","sourceOffset":39343},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Opposite roots and movement of a cut","sourceOffset":40277},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A subgroup detected by its matrix entries","sourceOffset":45113}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exists an infinite residually finite group with an ordinary finite presentation in which every element has finite 2-power order.","sourceOffset":1123,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"The companion algebra","label":"prop:ring-input","text":"There is an infinite finitely presented unital associative algebra \\[ R=\\bigoplus_{d\\geq0}R_d=\\F_2\\oplus I, \\qquad R_0=\\F_2, \\qquad I=\\bigoplus_{d\\geq1}R_d, \\] such that every $R_d$ is finite-dimensional and $I$ is matrix-nil. This algebra has the word presentation and hierarchy specified in Proposition \\ref{prop:hierarchy-input}.","sourceOffset":9247,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/247.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":83814,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-infinite-finitely-presented-residually-finite-2-group-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"247-02","slug":"An-infinite-finitely-presented-periodic-group-September-23-2026","familyId":"247","title":"An infinite finitely presented periodic group","abstract":"We construct an infinite group with an ordinary finite presentation in which every element has finite order, answering the finitely presented Burnside question negatively. We also construct an infinite-dimensional finitely presented nonunital nil associative algebra over 𝔽2 that is Jacobson radical but not nilpotent. Its unitization is finitely presented, algebraic, and infinite-dimensional. These algebras answer the finitely presented nil- and radical-algebra nilpotence questions and the finite-presentation version of Kurosh's algebraic finiteness question negatively.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs an infinite finitely presented residually finite group whose elements all have finite 2-power order, answering the finitely presented Burnside problem negatively even in this class. The construction also yields an infinite-dimensional finitely presented nil associative 𝔽2-algebra and a finitely presented infinite-dimensional algebraic unitization, giving negative answers to the corresponding nilpotence and Kurosh finiteness questions.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-periodic-group-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-periodic-group-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-periodic-group-September-23-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-periodic-group-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-periodic-group-September-23-2026/build/sections/finite-rules.tex","preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-periodic-group-September-23-2026/build/figures/encoded-block.tex","preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-periodic-group-September-23-2026/build/sections/cancellation.tex","preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-periodic-group-September-23-2026/build/sections/finite-presentation.tex","preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-periodic-group-September-23-2026/build/sections/stability.tex","preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-periodic-group-September-23-2026/build/sections/kernel-torsion.tex","preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-periodic-group-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-infinite-finitely-presented-periodic-group-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct an infinite group with an ordinary finite presentation in which every element has finite order, answering the finitely presented Burnside question negatively. We also construct an infinite-dimensional finitely presented nonunital nil associative algebra over $\\F_2$ that is Jacobson radical but not nilpotent. Its unitization is finitely presented, algebraic, and infinite-dimensional. These algebras answer the finitely presented nil- and radical-algebra nilpotence questions and the finite-presentation version of Kurosh's algebraic finiteness question negatively.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":625},{"kind":"section","title":"A hierarchy defined by finite local rules","sourceOffset":14031},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The alphabet and its encoded blocks","sourceOffset":19599},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Control tracks and admissible neighboring pairs","sourceOffset":23625},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Palette operations","sourceOffset":28941},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Acquisition and the local verifier","sourceOffset":31961},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An addressable computation tableau and the fixed point","sourceOffset":46909},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Faithful simulation on clean words","sourceOffset":57114},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Arbitrary words, cuts, and isolated normalization","sourceOffset":60820},{"kind":"section","title":"Cancellation and the matrix-nil algebra","sourceOffset":68033},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weighted sums and matrix powers","sourceOffset":69573},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cancellation inside one palette run","sourceOffset":71905}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is an infinite periodic group with an ordinary finite presentation. More precisely, the construction below gives a unital associative $\\F_2$-algebra $R$ for which $\\St_{12}(R)$ is infinite, finitely presented, and periodic.","sourceOffset":1935,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"A periodic Kazhdan group","label":"cor:kazhdan","text":"The group \\(\\St_{12}(R)\\) of Theorem \\ref{thm:main} is infinite, finitely presented, and periodic, and has Kazhdan's property \\((T)\\). In particular, it is nonamenable.","sourceOffset":2620,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Weighted tests","label":"ca:tests","text":"A letter test of dimension $d\\geq1$ at level $j$ assigns a matrix $M(a)\\in M_d(K)$ to each $a\\in\\Sigma_j$. For $w=a_1\\cdots a_n$ put $M(w)=M(a_1)\\cdots M(a_n)$ and $M(\\varnothing)=1_d$. Its length-$n$ sum is \\begin{equation} S_{j,n}(M)=\\sum_{w\\in\\Sigma_j^n} M(w)\\otimes[w]_j. \\end{equation} A chunk test of dimension $d$ and label bound $b\\geq1$ consists of a finite set $\\mathcal C$ of chunk identifiers, a nonempty label $v_\\gamma\\in\\Sigma_j^*$ with $|v_\\gamma|\\leq b$ for each $\\gamma\\in\\mathcal C$, and a weight $W_\\gamma\\in M_d(K)$. Its length-$n$ sum is \\begin{equation} C_{j,n}(\\mathcal C,W)= ","sourceOffset":70186,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/247.md","scopeText":"The finitely presented Burnside question asks whether a finitely presented group in which every element has finite order must be finite. The formalization gives a negative answer by constructing an infinite finitely presented periodic group, including a witness realized as a Steinberg group over an algebra of characteristic two. Periodicity means that each element has some finite order; no common exponent is asserted. The paper's separate nil-algebra and radical-algebra conclusions are outside these selected statements.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/247.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":172877,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-infinite-finitely-presented-periodic-group-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"248-01","slug":"Thompsons-group-F-is-nonamenable-September-23-2026","familyId":"248","title":"Thompson's group F is nonamenable","abstract":"We prove that Thompson's group F is nonamenable. This confirms Geoghegan's conjecture and resolves the amenability problem for F.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that Thompson's group F, the group of dyadic piecewise linear homeomorphisms of the interval, is nonamenable, resolving its longstanding amenability problem.","pdfPath":"preprints/Thompsons-group-F-is-nonamenable-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Thompsons-group-F-is-nonamenable-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Thompsons-group-F-is-nonamenable-September-23-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/Thompsons-group-F-is-nonamenable-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Thompsons-group-F-is-nonamenable-September-23-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/Thompsons-group-F-is-nonamenable-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Thompsons-group-F-is-nonamenable-September-23-2026/build/sections/finite-proof.tex","preprints/Thompsons-group-F-is-nonamenable-September-23-2026/build/figures/mixed-pairs.tex","preprints/Thompsons-group-F-is-nonamenable-September-23-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex","preprints/Thompsons-group-F-is-nonamenable-September-23-2026/build/sections/auxiliary.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Thompsons-group-F-is-nonamenable-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that Thompson's group \\(F\\) is nonamenable. This confirms Geoghegan's conjecture and resolves the amenability problem for \\(F\\).","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":184},{"kind":"section","title":"Recursive colors and finite averages","sourceOffset":7743},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Dyadic partitions and affine transport","sourceOffset":8029},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The recursive coloring","sourceOffset":12471},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A uniform boundary bound","sourceOffset":14899},{"kind":"section","title":"Consequences","sourceOffset":21808},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniformly bounded representations","sourceOffset":22266},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Percolation on Cayley graphs","sourceOffset":22917},{"kind":"section","title":"An explicit map with positive displacement","sourceOffset":24356}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Thompson's group \\(F\\) is not amenable.","sourceOffset":1978,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"avg:boundary","text":"For every nonempty finite set $A\\subset F$, \\begin{equation} \\max_{h\\in S}\\frac{|hA\\mathbin{\\triangle}A|}{|A|} \\geq \\frac{\\delta^2/L^2-4/D}{4(1-1/D)}>0. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":15663,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/248.md","scopeText":"The amenability problem for Thompson's group $F$ asks whether it admits a positive normalized left-invariant mean on bounded real functions. The formalized result rules out such a mean for the standard group of dyadic piecewise-linear homeomorphisms of the interval, proving nonamenability. No explicit boundary constant or prescribed generating set is given.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/248.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":34744,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Thompsons-group-F-is-nonamenable-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"249-01","slug":"A-finitely-generated-counterexample-to-the-Eilenberg-Ganea-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"249","title":"A finitely generated counterexample to the Eilenberg–Ganea conjecture","abstract":"We construct a finitely generated residually finite group of integral cohomological dimension two and geometric dimension three, disproving the Eilenberg–Ganea conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a finitely generated residually finite group with integral cohomological dimension two and geometric dimension three, disproving the Eilenberg–Ganea conjecture. It has no two-dimensional classifying space, even with infinitely many cells.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-finitely-generated-counterexample-to-the-Eilenberg-Ganea-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-finitely-generated-counterexample-to-the-Eilenberg-Ganea-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-finitely-generated-counterexample-to-the-Eilenberg-Ganea-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/A-finitely-generated-counterexample-to-the-Eilenberg-Ganea-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-finitely-generated-counterexample-to-the-Eilenberg-Ganea-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","preprints/A-finitely-generated-counterexample-to-the-Eilenberg-Ganea-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-finitely-generated-counterexample-to-the-Eilenberg-Ganea-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/degree.tex","preprints/A-finitely-generated-counterexample-to-the-Eilenberg-Ganea-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/presentation.tex","preprints/A-finitely-generated-counterexample-to-the-Eilenberg-Ganea-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/A-finitely-generated-counterexample-to-the-Eilenberg-Ganea-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/transport.tex","preprints/A-finitely-generated-counterexample-to-the-Eilenberg-Ganea-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-finitely-generated-counterexample-to-the-Eilenberg-Ganea-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a finitely generated residually finite group of integral cohomological dimension two and geometric dimension three, disproving the Eilenberg--Ganea conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":218},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the obstruction","sourceOffset":3659},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The geometric model and the proof interfaces","sourceOffset":5774},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the comparison and transport work","sourceOffset":8773},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and organization","sourceOffset":11688},{"kind":"section","title":"Degree on a conjugation-invariant part of a word fiber","sourceOffset":12990},{"kind":"section","title":"Comparison with an arbitrary aspherical presentation","sourceOffset":20696},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A presentation with distinguished boundary chains","sourceOffset":22989},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact counts and a uniform quadratic estimate","sourceOffset":29155},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Solving finite systems by degree","sourceOffset":35534},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite-coordinate compactness and the conclusion","sourceOffset":40153},{"kind":"section","title":"An acyclic level model for a height kernel","sourceOffset":44347}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a finitely generated residually finite group $G$ such that \\[ \\cdim G=2,\\qquad \\gdim G=3. \\] One may take $G$ to be the height kernel in the right-angled Artin group associated to a finite acyclic flag triangulation of the presentation complex \\begin{equation} \\langle x,y\\mid x^2=y^5,\\ x^2=(xy^{-1})^3\\rangle. \\end{equation} No two-dimensional classifying space for this $G$ exists, regardless of the number of its cells.","sourceOffset":2179,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"The presentation comparison theorem","label":"thm:presentation","text":"Suppose that $\\Gamma$ is residually finite and that $\\mathcal D$ is a finite indexed list of words on $S$, trivial in $\\Gamma$, whose chains form a free $\\Lambda$-basis of $\\ker\\partial_1$. Thus the canonical map \\begin{equation} \\Lambda^{(\\mathcal D)}\\longrightarrow\\ker\\partial_1, \\qquad e_z\\longmapsto[z], \\end{equation} is an isomorphism. If $\\Gamma$ admits a $K(\\Gamma,1)$ of dimension at most two, there is an $\\eta>0$ with the following property. Assign a label in $H=\\SU(2)$ to every oriented generator edge, assign the inverse label to its reverse, and assume that every label has distance less than $\\eta$ from $1$. If the product along every translate of every $z\\in\\mathcal D$ is $1$, then the product along every closed edge path is $1$.","sourceOffset":21477,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/249.md","scopeText":"The Eilenberg–Ganea conjecture predicts that a group of integral cohomological dimension two has a two-dimensional classifying space. The formalization constructs a finitely generated residually finite group of cohomological dimension two that has a three-dimensional classifying space but no two-dimensional one. Thus its geometric dimension is three, contradicting the conjecture.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/249.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":84565,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-finitely-generated-counterexample-to-the-Eilenberg-Ganea-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-finitely-generated-counterexample-to-the-Eilenberg-Ganea-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"250-01","slug":"Finite-algebraic-envelopes-and-the-Boone-Higman-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"250","title":"Finite algebraic envelopes and the Boone–Higman conjecture","abstract":"We prove the Boone–Higman conjecture. A finitely generated group has decidable word problem if and only if it embeds in a finitely presented simple group.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"A finitely generated group has decidable word problem exactly when it embeds in a finitely presented simple group, proving the Boone–Higman conjecture. The target can have type F∞: a classifying space with finitely many cells in each dimension. A single group of type F∞ can also contain every finitely presented group.","pdfPath":"preprints/Finite-algebraic-envelopes-and-the-Boone-Higman-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-algebraic-envelopes-and-the-Boone-Higman-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-algebraic-envelopes-and-the-Boone-Higman-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/Finite-algebraic-envelopes-and-the-Boone-Higman-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Finite-algebraic-envelopes-and-the-Boone-Higman-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/Finite-algebraic-envelopes-and-the-Boone-Higman-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/reduction.tex","preprints/Finite-algebraic-envelopes-and-the-Boone-Higman-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/affine.tex","preprints/Finite-algebraic-envelopes-and-the-Boone-Higman-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/kernel.tex","preprints/Finite-algebraic-envelopes-and-the-Boone-Higman-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/action.tex","preprints/Finite-algebraic-envelopes-and-the-Boone-Higman-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/algebra.tex","preprints/Finite-algebraic-envelopes-and-the-Boone-Higman-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/bootstrap.tex","preprints/Finite-algebraic-envelopes-and-the-Boone-Higman-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/compiler.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Finite-algebraic-envelopes-and-the-Boone-Higman-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Boone--Higman conjecture. A finitely generated group has decidable word problem if and only if it embeds in a finitely presented simple group.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":218},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof","sourceOffset":4081},{"kind":"section","title":"The group input and the algebraic data","sourceOffset":8301},{"kind":"section","title":"A finitely presented affine group","sourceOffset":14850},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The coefficient ring and the translation module","sourceOffset":15254},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite relative presentation","sourceOffset":17989},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The transition endomorphism","sourceOffset":24125},{"kind":"section","title":"Annihilating the Steinberg kernel","sourceOffset":26461},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conjugation with an unused index","sourceOffset":29113},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Interchanging and splitting corners","sourceOffset":32952},{"kind":"section","title":"The two-transitive envelope","sourceOffset":39564},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The mapping torus and its linear part","sourceOffset":40125}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every finitely generated group \\(G\\), the following are equivalent: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item \\(G\\) has decidable word problem. \\item \\(G\\) embeds in a finitely presented simple group. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":627,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:envelope","text":"Every finitely generated group with decidable word problem embeds in a finitely presented group \\(\\Gamma\\) admitting a faithful two-transitive action on a countably infinite set. Every point stabilizer of this action is finitely generated.","sourceOffset":2987,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/250.md","scopeText":"The Boone–Higman conjecture characterizes finitely generated groups with decidable word problem by embeddings into finitely presented simple groups. The formalization proves the equivalence in full: a finitely generated group has a computable word problem for a finite generating set exactly when it embeds by an injective homomorphism into a finitely presented simple group.\n\nThe formalization proves the higher-finiteness strengthening of the Boone–Higman conjecture: every finitely generated group with decidable word problem embeds by an injective homomorphism into a nontrivial simple group of type $F_\\infty$. Here type $F_\\infty$ means that the group has a classifying CW complex with finitely many cells in each dimension. No additional finiteness property of the original group is assumed.\n\n","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/250.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":111107,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Finite-algebraic-envelopes-and-the-Boone-Higman-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"250-02","slug":"Simple-F-infinity-overgroups-of-groups-with-decidable-word-problem-September-23-2026","familyId":"250","title":"Simple F∞ overgroups of groups with decidable word problem","abstract":"Every finitely generated group with decidable word problem embeds in a simple group of type F∞. This resolves the higher-finiteness strengthening of the Boone–Higman conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"A finitely generated group has decidable word problem exactly when it embeds in a finitely presented simple group, proving the Boone–Higman conjecture. The target can have type F∞: a classifying space with finitely many cells in each dimension. A single group of type F∞ can also contain every finitely presented group.","pdfPath":"preprints/Simple-F-infinity-overgroups-of-groups-with-decidable-word-problem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Simple-F-infinity-overgroups-of-groups-with-decidable-word-problem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Simple-F-infinity-overgroups-of-groups-with-decidable-word-problem-September-23-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/Simple-F-infinity-overgroups-of-groups-with-decidable-word-problem-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Simple-F-infinity-overgroups-of-groups-with-decidable-word-problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Simple-F-infinity-overgroups-of-groups-with-decidable-word-problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/homological.tex","preprints/Simple-F-infinity-overgroups-of-groups-with-decidable-word-problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/encoding.tex","preprints/Simple-F-infinity-overgroups-of-groups-with-decidable-word-problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/stage.tex","preprints/Simple-F-infinity-overgroups-of-groups-with-decidable-word-problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Simple-F-infinity-overgroups-of-groups-with-decidable-word-problem-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every finitely generated group with decidable word problem embeds in a simple group of type \\(F_\\infty\\). This resolves the higher-finiteness strengthening of the Boone--Higman conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":291},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier embeddings and the higher-finiteness obstacle","sourceOffset":3456},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Constructing the action and proving finiteness","sourceOffset":6449},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and conventions","sourceOffset":9799},{"kind":"section","title":"A homological device for ascending tori","sourceOffset":10456},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two finite systems of idempotents","sourceOffset":11304},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The ascending-torus theorem","sourceOffset":15859},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A product-module criterion","sourceOffset":17914},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniformly bounded simplex types","sourceOffset":21651},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Products of regular modules and the HNN sequence","sourceOffset":31779},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite extensions","sourceOffset":36575},{"kind":"section","title":"Encoding the diagrams and their actions","sourceOffset":39085}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Every finitely generated group \\(G\\) with decidable word problem admits an injective homomorphism into a nontrivial simple group \\(H\\) of type \\(\\Finfty\\).","sourceOffset":2119,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:torus","text":"Let $f:U\\to U$ be an injective endomorphism. Assume the following. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item There are a finitely presented group $P$ and homomorphisms $a:U\\to P$, $b:P\\to U$ with $f=ba$. \\item For each $\\ell\\in\\{2,3\\}$ there are homomorphisms $h_E:U\\to U$, indexed by $E\\in\\mathcal P_\\ell$, such that $h_I=f$. Whenever $E,P',E+P'\\in\\mathcal P_\\ell$ and $EP'=P'E=0$, the subgroups $h_E(U)$ and $h_{P'}(U)$ commute elementwise, and \\[ h_{E+P'}(u)=h_E(u)h_{P'}(u)\\qquad(u\\in U). \\] \\end{enumerate} Then $T(U,f)$ is of type $\\Finfty$.","sourceOffset":16111,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/250.md","scopeText":"The Boone–Higman conjecture characterizes finitely generated groups with decidable word problem by embeddings into finitely presented simple groups. The formalization proves the equivalence in full: a finitely generated group has a computable word problem for a finite generating set exactly when it embeds by an injective homomorphism into a finitely presented simple group.\n\nThe formalization proves the higher-finiteness strengthening of the Boone–Higman conjecture: every finitely generated group with decidable word problem embeds by an injective homomorphism into a nontrivial simple group of type $F_\\infty$. Here type $F_\\infty$ means that the group has a classifying CW complex with finitely many cells in each dimension. No additional finiteness property of the original group is assumed.\n\n","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/250.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":96476,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Simple-F-infinity-overgroups-of-groups-with-decidable-word-problem-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"250-03","slug":"A-universal-group-of-type-F-infinity-September-23-2026","familyId":"250","title":"A universal group of type F∞","abstract":"We construct a single group of type F∞ containing every finitely presented group. Its finitely generated subgroups, up to isomorphism, are exactly the finitely generated recursively presented groups. This answers the F∞ form of the higher-dimensional Higman embedding question.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"A finitely generated group has decidable word problem exactly when it embeds in a finitely presented simple group, proving the Boone–Higman conjecture. The target can have type F∞: a classifying space with finitely many cells in each dimension. A single group of type F∞ can also contain every finitely presented group.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-universal-group-of-type-F-infinity-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-universal-group-of-type-F-infinity-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-universal-group-of-type-F-infinity-September-23-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/A-universal-group-of-type-F-infinity-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-universal-group-of-type-F-infinity-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-universal-group-of-type-F-infinity-September-23-2026/build/sections/universal.tex","preprints/A-universal-group-of-type-F-infinity-September-23-2026/build/sections/idempotents.tex","preprints/A-universal-group-of-type-F-infinity-September-23-2026/build/sections/envelopes.tex","preprints/A-universal-group-of-type-F-infinity-September-23-2026/build/sections/torus.tex","preprints/A-universal-group-of-type-F-infinity-September-23-2026/build/sections/fillings.tex","preprints/A-universal-group-of-type-F-infinity-September-23-2026/build/sections/products.tex","preprints/A-universal-group-of-type-F-infinity-September-23-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex","preprints/A-universal-group-of-type-F-infinity-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-universal-group-of-type-F-infinity-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a single group of type $\\Finfty$ containing every finitely presented group. Its finitely generated subgroups, up to isomorphism, are exactly the finitely generated recursively presented groups. This answers the $\\Finfty$ form of the higher-dimensional Higman embedding question.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":338},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and construction","sourceOffset":2208},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":7620},{"kind":"section","title":"A universal finitely presented group","sourceOffset":7865},{"kind":"section","title":"Two finite systems of idempotents","sourceOffset":12088},{"kind":"section","title":"Faithful finite diagram envelopes","sourceOffset":18671},{"kind":"section","title":"The ascending-torus theorem","sourceOffset":24477},{"kind":"section","title":"Uniform fillings with finitely many simplex types","sourceOffset":28095},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The homogeneous complex and its bounds","sourceOffset":28580},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A controlled additivity calculation","sourceOffset":30908},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One iterate fills every cycle of a fixed bound","sourceOffset":37885},{"kind":"section","title":"From controlled fillings to finite skeleta","sourceOffset":43070}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exists a group $H$ of type $\\Finfty$ such that every finitely presented group admits an injective homomorphism into $H$.","sourceOffset":786,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:recursive","text":"A finitely generated group embeds in $H$ if and only if it is recursively presented.","sourceOffset":1198,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/250.md","scopeText":"The Boone–Higman conjecture characterizes finitely generated groups with decidable word problem by embeddings into finitely presented simple groups. The formalization proves the equivalence in full: a finitely generated group has a computable word problem for a finite generating set exactly when it embeds by an injective homomorphism into a finitely presented simple group.\n\nThe formalization proves the higher-finiteness strengthening of the Boone–Higman conjecture: every finitely generated group with decidable word problem embeds by an injective homomorphism into a nontrivial simple group of type $F_\\infty$. Here type $F_\\infty$ means that the group has a classifying CW complex with finitely many cells in each dimension. No additional finiteness property of the original group is assumed.\n\n","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/250.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":60640,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-universal-group-of-type-F-infinity-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"251-01","slug":"Unitarizability-Implies-Amenability-for-Countable-Groups-September-23-2026","familyId":"251","title":"Unitarizability implies amenability for discrete groups","abstract":"We prove that a discrete group is amenable if and only if every uniformly bounded representation on a complex Hilbert space is similar to a unitary representation. This resolves Dixmier's unitarizability problem affirmatively for discrete groups.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves Dixmier's problem for all discrete groups: amenability is equivalent to every uniformly bounded Hilbert-space representation being similar to a unitary representation. For countable discrete groups, amenability is also equivalent to strong Ulam stability: sufficiently accurate unitary approximate representations are uniformly close in operator norm to genuine representations on the same, possibly infinite-dimensional, Hilbert space.","pdfPath":"preprints/Unitarizability-Implies-Amenability-for-Countable-Groups-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Unitarizability-Implies-Amenability-for-Countable-Groups-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Unitarizability-Implies-Amenability-for-Countable-Groups-September-23-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/Unitarizability-Implies-Amenability-for-Countable-Groups-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Unitarizability-Implies-Amenability-for-Countable-Groups-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Unitarizability-Implies-Amenability-for-Countable-Groups-September-23-2026/build/sections/obstruction.tex","preprints/Unitarizability-Implies-Amenability-for-Countable-Groups-September-23-2026/build/sections/sparse.tex","preprints/Unitarizability-Implies-Amenability-for-Countable-Groups-September-23-2026/build/sections/weights.tex","preprints/Unitarizability-Implies-Amenability-for-Countable-Groups-September-23-2026/build/figures/edge-factorization.tex","preprints/Unitarizability-Implies-Amenability-for-Countable-Groups-September-23-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Unitarizability-Implies-Amenability-for-Countable-Groups-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that a discrete group is amenable if and only if every uniformly bounded representation on a complex Hilbert space is similar to a unitary representation. This resolves Dixmier's unitarizability problem affirmatively for discrete groups.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":293},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work and the remaining obstruction","sourceOffset":2643},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The operator obstruction and the proof","sourceOffset":7394},{"kind":"section","title":"An invariant-kernel obstruction","sourceOffset":11267},{"kind":"section","title":"Sparse assignments from nonamenability","sourceOffset":21291},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite-dimensional weights and sparse block operators","sourceOffset":26745},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A factorization through the indexed edges","sourceOffset":32033},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Consequences of an assignment to endpoints","sourceOffset":36981},{"kind":"section","title":"Proof of the main theorem","sourceOffset":41835},{"kind":"section","title":"A consequence for group \\texorpdfstring{$C^*$}{C*}-algebras","sourceOffset":50032}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"A discrete group is amenable if and only if it is unitarizable. For every discrete nonamenable group \\(G\\) and every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), there are a complex Hilbert space \\(\\mathcal K\\) and a representation \\(\\pi:G\\to\\mathrm{GL}(\\mathcal K)\\) with \\(|\\pi|\\le1+\\varepsilon\\) that is not similar to a unitary representation. If \\(G\\) is countable, \\(\\mathcal K\\) may be chosen separable.","sourceOffset":1876,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Direct-sum obstruction","label":"prop:direct-sum-obstruction","text":"Let $G$ be a countable group. For each $j\\in\\N$, let $k_j$ be a positive integer, put \\[ \\mathcal H_j=\\ell^2(G;\\C^{k_j}), \\qquad (U_j(g)\\xi)(x)=\\xi(g^{-1}x), \\] and let $A_j,T_j\\in\\mathcal B(\\mathcal H_j)$ with $A_j\\in U_j(G)'$. Write \\[ A_j[x,y]=W_j(x^{-1}y),\\qquad E_j=\\sum_{s\\in G}\\norm{W_j(s)}_{\\HS}^2. \\] Suppose that one constant $K\\ge0$ satisfies \\begin{align*} \\norm{\\operatorname{row}_e(T_j)}&\\le K, & \\norm{\\operatorname{col}_e(A_j-T_j)}&\\le K &&(j\\in\\N),\\\\ \\norm{T_j-U_j(g)T_jU_j(g)^{-1}}&\\le K &&&&(j\\in\\N,\\ g\\in G). \\end{align*} Assume also that \\[ \\frac{E_j}{k_j}\\longrightarrow\\infty. \\] Then $G$ has a representation on a separable complex Hilbert space whose operators are bounded and invertible, with \\[ \\sup_{g\\in G}\\norm{\\pi(g)}\\le1+K, \\] and which is not similar to a unitary representation. No uniform bound on $\\norm{T_j}$ or $\\norm{A_j}$ is assumed.","sourceOffset":16007,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/251.md","scopeText":"Dixmier's unitarizability problem asks whether a discrete group is amenable exactly when every uniformly bounded Hilbert-space representation is similar to a unitary one. The formalization establishes this equivalence for every discrete group.\n\nFor every nonamenable group and every $\\varepsilon>0$, it also gives a nonunitarizable representation with uniform operator bound at most $1+\\varepsilon$. The Hilbert space can be chosen separable for countable groups; a separate linked statement records a separable witness with uniform bound $101$ in that case.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/251.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":51836,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Unitarizability-Implies-Amenability-for-Countable-Groups-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"251-02","slug":"Strong-Ulam-Stability-Characterizes-Amenability-October-5-2026","familyId":"251","title":"Strong Ulam Stability Characterizes Amenability","abstract":"A countable discrete group is amenable if and only if it is strongly Ulam stable: every sufficiently accurate unitary almost representation, on any complex Hilbert space, is uniformly close in operator norm to a genuine representation on the same space. We prove the converse to Kazhdan's amenable stability theorem, answering the question of Burger, Ozawa, and Thom. The inclusion of infinite-dimensional Hilbert spaces is essential.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves Dixmier's problem for all discrete groups: amenability is equivalent to every uniformly bounded Hilbert-space representation being similar to a unitary representation. For countable discrete groups, amenability is also equivalent to strong Ulam stability: sufficiently accurate unitary approximate representations are uniformly close in operator norm to genuine representations on the same, possibly infinite-dimensional, Hilbert space.","pdfPath":"preprints/Strong-Ulam-Stability-Characterizes-Amenability-October-5-2026/strong-ulam-stability.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Strong-Ulam-Stability-Characterizes-Amenability-October-5-2026/strong-ulam-stability.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Strong-Ulam-Stability-Characterizes-Amenability-October-5-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/Strong-Ulam-Stability-Characterizes-Amenability-October-5-2026/build/strong-ulam-stability.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Strong-Ulam-Stability-Characterizes-Amenability-October-5-2026/build/strong-ulam-stability.tex","sourceAbstract":"A countable discrete group is amenable if and only if it is strongly Ulam stable: every sufficiently accurate unitary almost representation, on any complex Hilbert space, is uniformly close in operator norm to a genuine representation on the same space. We prove the converse to Kazhdan's amenable stability theorem, answering the question of Burger, Ozawa, and Thom. The inclusion of infinite-dimensional Hilbert spaces is essential.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":479},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and context","sourceOffset":1944},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction","sourceOffset":3743},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":6728},{"kind":"section","title":"Flags from a boundary action","sourceOffset":7288},{"kind":"section","title":"Frames and metric transfers","sourceOffset":16009},{"kind":"section","title":"Hilbert spaces and unitary circuits","sourceOffset":26648},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Labels and inner products","sourceOffset":27048},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The subspaces exchanged by one layer","sourceOffset":30734},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Metric transfer with priority for the next layer","sourceOffset":33261},{"kind":"section","title":"Comparing two and three configurations","sourceOffset":36389},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Changing the arrival frames","sourceOffset":37463}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"A countable discrete group is strongly Ulam stable if and only if it is amenable.","sourceOffset":1289,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Compatible sparse flags","label":"prop:flags","text":"Let $G$ be a nonamenable discrete group. For every integer $N\\geq1$ and every $0<\\beta<1$, there are finite nonempty sets $\\mathcal X_1,\\ldots,\\mathcal X_N$ and $\\mathcal Y_0,\\ldots,\\mathcal Y_{N-1}$, maps \\[ s_k:\\mathcal X_k\\times\\mathcal Y_{k-1}\\longrightarrow G, \\qquad b_k:G\\times\\mathcal X_k\\times\\mathcal Y_{k-1} \\longrightarrow\\{\\E,\\F\\}, \\] with the following properties. \\begin{enumerate} \\item[\\textup{(F1)}] If $k<N$ and $b_k(w;x,y)=\\E$, then, with $v=w s_k(x,y)$, \\[ b_{k+1}(v;x',y')=\\E \\quad\\text{for every }(x',y')\\in \\mathcal X_{k+1}\\times\\mathcal Y_k. \\] \\item[\\textup{(F2)}] For every $k,w,x$, \\[ \\frac{\\#\\{y\\in\\mathcal Y_{k-1}:b_k(w;x,y)=\\F\\}} {|\\mathcal Y_{k-1}|}\\leq\\beta. \\] \\item[\\textup{(F3)}] For every $k,v,y$, \\[ \\frac{\\#\\{x\\in\\mathcal X_k: b_k(v s_k(x,y)^{-1};x,y)=\\E\\}} {|\\mathcal X_k|}\\leq\\beta. \\] \\end{enumerate} Each alphabet may subsequently be replaced by any positive integer number of copies, independently of the others, while preserving these properties. In particular, its cardinality can be made arbitrarily large and divisible by any prescribed positive integer.","sourceOffset":12060,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/251.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":56190,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Strong-Ulam-Stability-Characterizes-Amenability-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"252-01","slug":"a-torsion-free-hyperbolic-group-that-is-not-residually-finite-September-23-2026","familyId":"252","title":"A torsion-free hyperbolic group that is not residually finite","abstract":"We construct a torsion-free word-hyperbolic group that is not residually finite, answering the residual-finiteness question for hyperbolic groups negatively.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a torsion-free word-hyperbolic group that is not residually finite, answering the residual-finiteness question negatively. One fixed nonidentity element is killed by every finite-dimensional linear representation over every commutative field, so the group is not linear over any such field.","pdfPath":"preprints/a-torsion-free-hyperbolic-group-that-is-not-residually-finite-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/a-torsion-free-hyperbolic-group-that-is-not-residually-finite-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/a-torsion-free-hyperbolic-group-that-is-not-residually-finite-September-23-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/a-torsion-free-hyperbolic-group-that-is-not-residually-finite-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/a-torsion-free-hyperbolic-group-that-is-not-residually-finite-September-23-2026/build/sections/history.tex","preprints/a-torsion-free-hyperbolic-group-that-is-not-residually-finite-September-23-2026/build/sections/construction.tex","preprints/a-torsion-free-hyperbolic-group-that-is-not-residually-finite-September-23-2026/build/sections/geometry-foundations.tex","preprints/a-torsion-free-hyperbolic-group-that-is-not-residually-finite-September-23-2026/build/sections/geometry-thinness.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/a-torsion-free-hyperbolic-group-that-is-not-residually-finite-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a torsion-free word-hyperbolic group that is not residually finite, answering the residual-finiteness question for hyperbolic groups negatively.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":203},{"kind":"section","title":"The construction and the rank obstruction","sourceOffset":10616},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The geometric criterion","sourceOffset":10693},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Punctured lines with few short cycles","sourceOffset":12385},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Blocks and triangular relations","sourceOffset":16129},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rank over the reals and over a fixed prime field","sourceOffset":22162},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local factorizations and the global certificate","sourceOffset":24609},{"kind":"section","title":"Metrics, planar fillings, and torsion","sourceOffset":28291},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Subdivisions and shortest paths","sourceOffset":29247},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reduced planar fillings with a prescribed boundary","sourceOffset":34550},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Angular curvature and nontrivial closed paths","sourceOffset":44429},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Shortest free loops and torsion","sourceOffset":47108}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exists a torsion-free word-hyperbolic group that is not residually finite.","sourceOffset":1072,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Finite-dimensional linear obstruction","label":"cor:nonlinear","text":"Let $G$ be the finitely generated torsion-free word-hyperbolic group constructed in Theorem \\ref{thm:main}. For every commutative field $K$, every integer $n\\geq 1$, and every homomorphism $\\rho:G\\to\\mathrm{GL}_n(K)$, \\[ R_f(G)\\subseteq\\ker\\rho. \\] Consequently, one fixed nonidentity element of $G$ is killed by all these representations, simultaneously over all such fields and dimensions. In particular, $G$ is not linear over any commutative field.","sourceOffset":1824,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/252.md","scopeText":"The residual-finiteness question for hyperbolic groups asks whether every nonidentity element survives in some finite quotient. The formalized result gives a negative answer by constructing a torsion-free word-hyperbolic group that is not residually finite.\n\nNonlinearity over every field is not a separate conclusion of the statement linked below.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/252.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":69843,"sourceFileCount":5,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/a-torsion-free-hyperbolic-group-that-is-not-residually-finite-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"253-01","slug":"An-Infinite-Finitely-Presented-Simple-Amenable-Group-September-23-2026","familyId":"253","title":"An infinite finitely presented simple amenable group","abstract":"We construct an infinite finitely presented simple amenable group, giving a positive answer to the finite-presentation existence question for simple amenable groups.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs an infinite finitely presented simple amenable group, answering the longstanding question of whether these properties can occur simultaneously.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-Infinite-Finitely-Presented-Simple-Amenable-Group-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-Infinite-Finitely-Presented-Simple-Amenable-Group-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-Infinite-Finitely-Presented-Simple-Amenable-Group-September-23-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/An-Infinite-Finitely-Presented-Simple-Amenable-Group-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/An-Infinite-Finitely-Presented-Simple-Amenable-Group-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/An-Infinite-Finitely-Presented-Simple-Amenable-Group-September-23-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/An-Infinite-Finitely-Presented-Simple-Amenable-Group-September-23-2026/build/sections/simplicity.tex","preprints/An-Infinite-Finitely-Presented-Simple-Amenable-Group-September-23-2026/build/sections/amenability.tex","preprints/An-Infinite-Finitely-Presented-Simple-Amenable-Group-September-23-2026/build/sections/homology.tex","preprints/An-Infinite-Finitely-Presented-Simple-Amenable-Group-September-23-2026/build/sections/lifting.tex","preprints/An-Infinite-Finitely-Presented-Simple-Amenable-Group-September-23-2026/build/sections/propagation.tex","preprints/An-Infinite-Finitely-Presented-Simple-Amenable-Group-September-23-2026/build/sections/transport.tex","preprints/An-Infinite-Finitely-Presented-Simple-Amenable-Group-September-23-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-Infinite-Finitely-Presented-Simple-Amenable-Group-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct an infinite finitely presented simple amenable group, giving a positive answer to the finite-presentation existence question for simple amenable groups.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":211},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and the role of finite presentation","sourceOffset":1260},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and the proof","sourceOffset":8905},{"kind":"section","title":"The polygon algebra and its translation groups","sourceOffset":13828},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A quadratic ring and four directions","sourceOffset":14143},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Boolean polygons and the Stone space","sourceOffset":18852},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Tracks, translation tables, and slots","sourceOffset":21246},{"kind":"section","title":"Comparison, perfection, and simplicity","sourceOffset":23106},{"kind":"section","title":"Amenability from random polygonal partitions","sourceOffset":29560},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Lattice counts and sites with a specified side","sourceOffset":32415},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positive averaging matrices","sourceOffset":37148},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Total variation of the entire field","sourceOffset":48993}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exists an infinite finitely presented simple amenable group.","sourceOffset":866,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:simplicity","text":"For every positive integer $m$, the group $A_m$ is infinite, perfect, and simple, and \\[ [F_m,F_m]=A_m. \\]","sourceOffset":26000,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/253.md","scopeText":"The formalized result answers the existence question affirmatively: there is one group that is infinite, finitely presented, simple, and amenable. Amenability is expressed by the Følner condition.\n\nThe later general claims about central kernels and enlargements across families are not included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/253.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":226284,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-Infinite-Finitely-Presented-Simple-Amenable-Group-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"254-01","slug":"Harmonic-heights-and-the-Artin-K-pi-1-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"254","title":"Harmonic heights and the Artin K(pi,1) conjecture","abstract":"We prove that the standard Salvetti complex of every Artin group with finitely many standard generators is aspherical. This resolves the Artin $K(\\pi,1)$ conjecture in finite rank.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"The Salvetti complex of every finite-rank Artin group is aspherical, proving the Artin $K(\\pi,1)$ conjecture. Arbitrary intersections of its parabolic subgroups are parabolic, proving the Parabolic Intersection Conjecture. An explicit Artin group admits no proper cocompact isometric action on any nonempty proper CAT$(0)$ space.","pdfPath":"preprints/Harmonic-heights-and-the-Artin-K-pi-1-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Harmonic-heights-and-the-Artin-K-pi-1-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Harmonic-heights-and-the-Artin-K-pi-1-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/Harmonic-heights-and-the-Artin-K-pi-1-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Harmonic-heights-and-the-Artin-K-pi-1-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Harmonic-heights-and-the-Artin-K-pi-1-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/dependencies.tex","preprints/Harmonic-heights-and-the-Artin-K-pi-1-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/topology.tex","preprints/Harmonic-heights-and-the-Artin-K-pi-1-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/category.tex","preprints/Harmonic-heights-and-the-Artin-K-pi-1-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/layers.tex","preprints/Harmonic-heights-and-the-Artin-K-pi-1-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/height-data.tex","preprints/Harmonic-heights-and-the-Artin-K-pi-1-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/two-point.tex","preprints/Harmonic-heights-and-the-Artin-K-pi-1-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/filtrations.tex","preprints/Harmonic-heights-and-the-Artin-K-pi-1-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/rigidity.tex","preprints/Harmonic-heights-and-the-Artin-K-pi-1-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/hom-filtration.tex","preprints/Harmonic-heights-and-the-Artin-K-pi-1-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/residues.tex","preprints/Harmonic-heights-and-the-Artin-K-pi-1-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/global.tex","preprints/Harmonic-heights-and-the-Artin-K-pi-1-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Harmonic-heights-and-the-Artin-K-pi-1-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the standard Salvetti complex of every Artin group with finitely many standard generators is aspherical. This resolves the Artin \\(K(\\pi,1)\\) conjecture in finite rank.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":245},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context","sourceOffset":2954},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its local estimates","sourceOffset":7162},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and organization","sourceOffset":15437},{"kind":"section","title":"The Salvetti complex and spherical parabolic cosets","sourceOffset":16438},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Classical inputs","sourceOffset":17298},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Permutahedral cells and their face maps","sourceOffset":20917},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The regular universal cover","sourceOffset":24860},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The spherical-coset comparison","sourceOffset":26548},{"kind":"section","title":"A framed category and its Artin action","sourceOffset":30937},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The geometric form and a frame","sourceOffset":31663},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fusion labels","sourceOffset":35172}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every Coxeter matrix on a finite set \\(S\\), the universal cover of \\(X(W,S)\\) is contractible. Equivalently, \\[ \\pi_n\\bigl(X(W,S)\\bigr)=0\\qquad(n\\ge2). \\] The conclusion allows arbitrary finite labels, infinite labels, and connected or disconnected diagrams.","sourceOffset":1221,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Standard parabolic embedding","label":"thm:topology-parabolic-embedding","text":"For every $T\\subseteq S$, the homomorphism from the Artin group defined by the submatrix on $T$ to $A$, sending its standard generators to the corresponding $\\sigma_t$, is injective. Its image is $A_T$.","sourceOffset":18681,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Cap","label":"def:height-data-cap","text":"For $q\\in\\Zbb$ and $K\\subseteq\\widehat S$, the data at a representative $x$ are capped at $q$ on $K$ if \\[ m_i^d(x)=0\\qquad(i\\in K, d>q). \\] For a set of colors $J$, let $\\partial J$ denote its outside neighbors in the framed diagram; it includes the frame when the frame is a neighbor. Positivity of the family weights makes a cap equivalent to the absence of generators in all the corresponding unfolded vertices and levels in every member of the family.","sourceOffset":91995,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:two-point-candidate","text":"A candidate in \\(A_Tx_0\\) is a pair \\[ X=(J,A_Jz),\\qquad J\\subsetneq T,\\qquad z\\in A_Tx_0, \\] with the following properties. Put \\(V=T\\setminus J\\), and let \\(p\\) agree with \\(m^q(z)\\) outside \\(J\\) and be harmonic on \\(J\\). \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item The data are capped at \\(q\\) on \\(V\\). \\item The vector \\(a\\), defined on \\(T\\) by \\(a_i=-(Cp)_i\\), is strictly positive on \\(V\\) and zero on \\(J\\). \\item For every representative \\(y\\in A_Jz\\) whose data are capped at \\(q\\) on \\(T\\), one has \\[ m_j^q(y)\\ge p_j\\qquad(j\\in J). \\] \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":104946,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/254.md","scopeText":"The Artin $K(\\pi,1)$ conjecture asserts that the standard Salvetti complex of every finite-rank Artin group is aspherical. The formalized result proves the equivalent statement that its universal cover is contractible.\n\nIt applies to every Coxeter matrix on a finite generating set, including empty and disconnected diagrams and infinite edge labels. The associated Coxeter and Artin groups may be infinite. Later corollaries in the manuscript are not included.\n\nThe geometric-action question asks whether every Artin group acts properly and cocompactly by isometries on a proper CAT(0) space. The formalized result gives a counterexample: an Artin group defined by a symmetric matrix on $116$ generators, with diagonal entries $1$ and off-diagonal entries in $\\{2,3,\\infty\\}$.\n\nIt admits no such act","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/254.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":228580,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":4,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Harmonic-heights-and-the-Artin-K-pi-1-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"254-02","slug":"An-Artin-group-with-no-geometric-CAT-0-action-September-23-2026","familyId":"254","title":"An Artin group with no geometric CAT(0) action","abstract":"We construct an Artin group on 116 generators that admits no proper, cocompact isometric action on a nonempty proper CAT(0) space. This refutes the CAT(0) conjecture for Artin groups.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"The Salvetti complex of every finite-rank Artin group is aspherical, proving the Artin $K(\\pi,1)$ conjecture. Arbitrary intersections of its parabolic subgroups are parabolic, proving the Parabolic Intersection Conjecture. An explicit Artin group admits no proper cocompact isometric action on any nonempty proper CAT$(0)$ space.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-Artin-group-with-no-geometric-CAT-0-action-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-Artin-group-with-no-geometric-CAT-0-action-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-Artin-group-with-no-geometric-CAT-0-action-September-23-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/An-Artin-group-with-no-geometric-CAT-0-action-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/An-Artin-group-with-no-geometric-CAT-0-action-September-23-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/An-Artin-group-with-no-geometric-CAT-0-action-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/An-Artin-group-with-no-geometric-CAT-0-action-September-23-2026/build/sections/lengths.tex","preprints/An-Artin-group-with-no-geometric-CAT-0-action-September-23-2026/build/sections/braids.tex","preprints/An-Artin-group-with-no-geometric-CAT-0-action-September-23-2026/build/sections/sectors.tex","preprints/An-Artin-group-with-no-geometric-CAT-0-action-September-23-2026/build/sections/diagrams.tex","preprints/An-Artin-group-with-no-geometric-CAT-0-action-September-23-2026/build/sections/localization.tex","preprints/An-Artin-group-with-no-geometric-CAT-0-action-September-23-2026/build/sections/assembly.tex","preprints/An-Artin-group-with-no-geometric-CAT-0-action-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-Artin-group-with-no-geometric-CAT-0-action-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct an Artin group on 116 generators that admits no proper, cocompact isometric action on a nonempty proper CAT(0) space. This refutes the CAT(0) conjecture for Artin groups.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":246},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and the obstruction","sourceOffset":3676},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and notation","sourceOffset":7074},{"kind":"section","title":"Stable lengths and centralizer characters","sourceOffset":8008},{"kind":"section","title":"A quantitative constraint from braid groups","sourceOffset":18676},{"kind":"section","title":"Six asymptotic sectors","sourceOffset":26623},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Orbit control and mirror coordinates","sourceOffset":28218},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The parameter disk","sourceOffset":32347},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform asymptotic maps","sourceOffset":36242},{"kind":"section","title":"Labelled triangular diagrams","sourceOffset":41698},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The triangular presentation and its link","sourceOffset":42598},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Signed counts on spheres","sourceOffset":47361}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is an Artin matrix \\(M\\) on 116 generators, with every off-diagonal label in \\(\\{2,3,\\infty\\}\\), such that \\(A_M\\) admits no geometric action. One explicit matrix is given in Definition \\ref{def:matrix}.","sourceOffset":3181,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:braid-angle","text":"Suppose a group acting geometrically on a CAT(0) space contains elements $s_1,\\ldots,s_{39}$ satisfying the $B_{40}$ relations, with $\\ell(s_1^2)>0$. Then \\begin{equation} \\left(\\frac{\\ell((s_1s_2)^3)}{3\\ell(s_1^2)}\\right)^2 \\ge\\frac{143}{513}>\\frac14, \\end{equation} and in particular \\[ \\frac{\\ell((s_1s_2)^3)}{3\\ell(s_1^2)} =\\frac{\\ell((s_1s_2)^3)}{3\\ell(s_2^2)}>\\frac12. \\]","sourceOffset":19114,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:matrix","text":"Let $\\mathcal E=\\{(a,b),(b,c),(c,a)\\}$, as before, and set \\[ S=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}\\ \\sqcup \\{s_j^{uv}:(u,v)\\in\\mathcal E,\\ 3\\le j\\le39\\}. \\] All symbols in the second set are distinct. For each $(u,v)\\in\\mathcal E$, also write $s_1^{uv}=u$ and $s_2^{uv}=v$; these are aliases for existing elements of $S$. Define a symmetric matrix $M=(m_{st})_{s,t\\in S}$ by the following prescriptions: \\begin{enumerate} \\item $m_{ss}=1$ for every $s\\in S$, and $m_{ab}=m_{bc}=m_{ca}=3$. \\item In each block $\\{s_1^{uv},\\ldots,s_{39}^{uv}\\}$, set \\[ m_{s_i^{uv},s_j^{uv}}= \\begin{cases} 3,&|i-j|=1,\\\\ 2,&|i-j|\\ge2. \\end{","sourceOffset":73074,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/254.md","scopeText":"The Artin $K(\\pi,1)$ conjecture asserts that the standard Salvetti complex of every finite-rank Artin group is aspherical. The formalized result proves the equivalent statement that its universal cover is contractible.\n\nIt applies to every Coxeter matrix on a finite generating set, including empty and disconnected diagrams and infinite edge labels. The associated Coxeter and Artin groups may be infinite. Later corollaries in the manuscript are not included.\n\nThe geometric-action question asks whether every Artin group acts properly and cocompactly by isometries on a proper CAT(0) space. The formalized result gives a counterexample: an Artin group defined by a symmetric matrix on $116$ generators, with diagonal entries $1$ and off-diagonal entries in $\\{2,3,\\infty\\}$.\n\nIt admits no such act","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/254.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":95798,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-Artin-group-with-no-geometric-CAT-0-action-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/An-Artin-group-with-no-geometric-CAT-0-action-September-23-2026/build/macros.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"254-03","slug":"Parabolic-intersections-in-Artin-groups-September-23-2026","familyId":"254","title":"Parabolic intersections in Artin groups","abstract":"We prove that every intersection of parabolic subgroups of any finite-rank Artin group, with arbitrary finite or infinite Coxeter labels, is parabolic. This resolves the Parabolic Intersection Conjecture affirmatively.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"The Salvetti complex of every finite-rank Artin group is aspherical, proving the Artin $K(\\pi,1)$ conjecture. Arbitrary intersections of its parabolic subgroups are parabolic, proving the Parabolic Intersection Conjecture. An explicit Artin group admits no proper cocompact isometric action on any nonempty proper CAT$(0)$ space.","pdfPath":"preprints/Parabolic-intersections-in-Artin-groups-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Parabolic-intersections-in-Artin-groups-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Parabolic-intersections-in-Artin-groups-September-23-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/Parabolic-intersections-in-Artin-groups-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Parabolic-intersections-in-Artin-groups-September-23-2026/build/sections/01_intro.tex","preprints/Parabolic-intersections-in-Artin-groups-September-23-2026/build/sections/02_category.tex","preprints/Parabolic-intersections-in-Artin-groups-September-23-2026/build/sections/03_layers.tex","preprints/Parabolic-intersections-in-Artin-groups-September-23-2026/build/sections/04_caps_twopoint.tex","preprints/Parabolic-intersections-in-Artin-groups-September-23-2026/build/sections/05_null.tex","preprints/Parabolic-intersections-in-Artin-groups-September-23-2026/build/sections/06_heights.tex","preprints/Parabolic-intersections-in-Artin-groups-September-23-2026/build/figures/tied-path.tex","preprints/Parabolic-intersections-in-Artin-groups-September-23-2026/build/sections/07_spherical.tex","preprints/Parabolic-intersections-in-Artin-groups-September-23-2026/build/sections/08_stabilizers.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Parabolic-intersections-in-Artin-groups-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every intersection of parabolic subgroups of any finite-rank Artin group, with arbitrary finite or infinite Coxeter labels, is parabolic. This resolves the Parabolic Intersection Conjecture affirmatively.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":282},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and method","sourceOffset":4601},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From relative families to stabilizers","sourceOffset":9943},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and conventions","sourceOffset":13312},{"kind":"section","title":"The relative categorical family","sourceOffset":14487},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Classical inputs and the relative frame","sourceOffset":15180},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Labels and the finite algebra","sourceOffset":20359},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite unfoldings","sourceOffset":26858},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Twists as bimodule homotopies","sourceOffset":30107},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The transported family and folded counts","sourceOffset":38111},{"kind":"section","title":"Layers and positive divisors","sourceOffset":42624},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The linear heart","sourceOffset":43146}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Parabolic intersections","label":"thm:main","text":"For every Coxeter matrix $M$ on a finite set $S$, subsets $X,Y\\subseteq S$, and $g,h\\in A_M$, there are $Z\\subseteq S$ and $k\\in A_M$ such that \\[ gA_Xg^{-1}\\cap hA_Yh^{-1}=kA_Zk^{-1}. \\]","sourceOffset":1164,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Arbitrary intersections and parabolic closure","label":"cor:arbitrary-intersections","text":"For every family $\\mathcal F$ of parabolic subgroups of $A$, there is a subfamily $\\mathcal F_0\\subseteq\\mathcal F$ with $|\\mathcal F_0|\\le |S|$ such that \\[ \\bigcap_{P\\in\\mathcal F}P=\\bigcap_{P\\in\\mathcal F_0}P. \\] Here the intersection of an empty family is $A$. In particular, arbitrary intersections of parabolic subgroups are parabolic, and every subset $E\\subseteq A$ is contained in a unique smallest parabolic subgroup.","sourceOffset":1867,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Cap","label":"cap:definition","text":"For $q\\in\\mathbb Z$ and a color set $D$, the family at $x$ is capped at $q$ on $D$ if \\[ m_i^d(x)=0\\qquad(i\\in D,\\ d>q). \\] Equivalently, every family member has no generator in those colors above level $q$. We write $\\partial J$ for the exterior neighbors of $J$ in $\\widehat S$.","sourceOffset":69251,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Candidate","label":"two:definition","text":"A candidate is $(J,A_Jz)$ with $J\\subsetneq T$ and $z\\in A_Tx_0$. Put $V=T\\setminus J$, let $p$ harmonically interpolate $m^q(z)$ on $J$, and put $a=-(Cp)_T$. The requirements are: \\begin{enumerate} \\item the open colors $V$ are capped at $q$; \\item $a$ is strictly positive on $V$ and zero on $J$; \\item every representative $y\\in A_Jz$ capped at $q$ on $T$ satisfies $m_J^q(y)\\ge p_J$. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":73260,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/254.md","scopeText":"The Artin $K(\\pi,1)$ conjecture asserts that the standard Salvetti complex of every finite-rank Artin group is aspherical. The formalized result proves the equivalent statement that its universal cover is contractible.\n\nIt applies to every Coxeter matrix on a finite generating set, including empty and disconnected diagrams and infinite edge labels. The associated Coxeter and Artin groups may be infinite. Later corollaries in the manuscript are not included.\n\nThe geometric-action question asks whether every Artin group acts properly and cocompactly by isometries on a proper CAT(0) space. The formalized result gives a counterexample: an Artin group defined by a symmetric matrix on $116$ generators, with diagonal entries $1$ and off-diagonal entries in $\\{2,3,\\infty\\}$.\n\nIt admits no such act","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/254.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":191470,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":20,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Parabolic-intersections-in-Artin-groups-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"255-01","slug":"quasi-isometric-recognition-of-virtually-polycyclic-groups-September-24-2026","familyId":"255","title":"Quasi-isometric recognition of virtually polycyclic groups","abstract":"We prove that every finitely generated group quasi-isometric to a finitely generated virtually polycyclic group is virtually polycyclic. This resolves the lattice-recognition conjecture of Eskin, Fisher and Whyte, which allows the ambient solvable Lie group in the conclusion to differ from the one in the hypothesis.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every finitely generated group quasi-isometric to a finitely generated virtually polycyclic group is virtually polycyclic, resolving the Eskin–Fisher–Whyte lattice-recognition conjecture. Equivalently, a group quasi-isometric to a lattice in a connected simply connected solvable Lie group is virtually a uniform lattice in some such Lie group, possibly a different one.","pdfPath":"preprints/quasi-isometric-recognition-of-virtually-polycyclic-groups-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/quasi-isometric-recognition-of-virtually-polycyclic-groups-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/quasi-isometric-recognition-of-virtually-polycyclic-groups-September-24-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/quasi-isometric-recognition-of-virtually-polycyclic-groups-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/quasi-isometric-recognition-of-virtually-polycyclic-groups-September-24-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/quasi-isometric-recognition-of-virtually-polycyclic-groups-September-24-2026/build/models.tex","preprints/quasi-isometric-recognition-of-virtually-polycyclic-groups-September-24-2026/build/boxes.tex","preprints/quasi-isometric-recognition-of-virtually-polycyclic-groups-September-24-2026/build/polynomial.tex","preprints/quasi-isometric-recognition-of-virtually-polycyclic-groups-September-24-2026/build/stationary.tex","preprints/quasi-isometric-recognition-of-virtually-polycyclic-groups-September-24-2026/build/profiles.tex","preprints/quasi-isometric-recognition-of-virtually-polycyclic-groups-September-24-2026/build/labels.tex","preprints/quasi-isometric-recognition-of-virtually-polycyclic-groups-September-24-2026/build/reflection-figure.tex","preprints/quasi-isometric-recognition-of-virtually-polycyclic-groups-September-24-2026/build/offsets.tex","preprints/quasi-isometric-recognition-of-virtually-polycyclic-groups-September-24-2026/build/uniformity.tex","preprints/quasi-isometric-recognition-of-virtually-polycyclic-groups-September-24-2026/build/boundary.tex","preprints/quasi-isometric-recognition-of-virtually-polycyclic-groups-September-24-2026/build/recognition.tex","preprints/quasi-isometric-recognition-of-virtually-polycyclic-groups-September-24-2026/build/lipschitz-appendix.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/quasi-isometric-recognition-of-virtually-polycyclic-groups-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every finitely generated group quasi-isometric to a finitely generated virtually polycyclic group is virtually polycyclic. This resolves the lattice-recognition conjecture of Eskin, Fisher and Whyte, which allows the ambient solvable Lie group in the conclusion to differ from the one in the hypothesis.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":380},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The recognition problem","sourceOffset":1987},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform bounded height","sourceOffset":4942},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How height gives recognition","sourceOffset":7139},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The height argument","sourceOffset":8717},{"kind":"section","title":"Cartan models and the canonical height","sourceOffset":14081},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Cartan decomposition and its multiplication map","sourceOffset":15160},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Intrinsic coordinates and the graded Cartan limit","sourceOffset":22175},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Canonical height and the adjoint action","sourceOffset":32123},{"kind":"section","title":"Weighted boxes and path packing","sourceOffset":38389},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Support exponents and normalized boxes","sourceOffset":39946},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Product schemes that remain nondegenerate","sourceOffset":49649}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:recognition","text":"Let $P$ be a finitely generated virtually polycyclic group, and let $H$ be any finitely generated group. If $H$ and $P$ are quasi-isometric, then $H$ is virtually polycyclic.","sourceOffset":972,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:lattice-recognition","text":"If a finitely generated group $H$ is quasi-isometric to a lattice in a connected simply connected solvable Lie group, then a finite-index subgroup of $H$ is a uniform lattice in some connected simply connected solvable Lie group.","sourceOffset":1511,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:canonical-height","text":"Let $\\mathfrak g$ be the Lie algebra of a real-triangulable group $G$. Denote the diagonal characters of a simultaneous real triangular form of $\\ad$ by $\\chi_1,\\ldots,\\chi_s$, including repetitions and zero characters. Put \\[ \\mathfrak k=\\bigcap_{j=1}^s\\ker\\chi_j, \\qquad W=\\mathfrak g/\\mathfrak k. \\] The exponential height map is the homomorphism $\\pi:G\\to(W,+)$ whose differential is the quotient map.","sourceOffset":5344,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Chart profiles","label":"def:chart-profiles","text":"Let $r\\to\\infty$ along a sequence. For each $r$, choose a map pair with first map $F$, a basepoint $x\\in G$, and a deterministic suffix $k_0\\in N$ satisfying \\begin{equation} k_0\\in B_r((1+\\delta)\\mathcal C,e^{\\varepsilon_r r}), \\qquad \\varepsilon_r\\ge0,\\quad \\varepsilon_r\\longrightarrow0. \\end{equation} Choose $a$ uniformly with respect to Haar measure on $B_r(\\mathcal C)$, and set $n=ak_0$. The chart profile is the random continuous function \\begin{equation} f_{r,x,n}(z)=\\frac{\\pi F(xn\\Delta_r z)-\\pi F(x)}{r}, \\qquad z\\in D_\\infty. \\end{equation} The law of this function is taken in $C_{\\mat","sourceOffset":112019,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/255.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves quasi-isometric recognition of virtually polycyclic groups: every finitely generated group quasi-isometric to a finitely generated virtually polycyclic group is itself virtually polycyclic. It also realizes a finite-index subgroup of the recognized group as a uniform lattice in a simply connected solvable Lie group, which may differ from the original ambient group.\n\nThe linked structural estimate controls the height-coordinate behavior of self quasi-isometries of the stated unimodular solvable Lie models, up to bounded error and one of finitely many linear height symmetries.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/255.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":249875,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/quasi-isometric-recognition-of-virtually-polycyclic-groups-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"256-01","slug":"Nonsingular-systems-of-equations-over-arbitrary-groups-October-5-2026","familyId":"256","title":"Nonsingular systems of equations over arbitrary groups","abstract":"Every finite nonsingular system of equations over an arbitrary group has a simultaneous solution in an overgroup. This proves Howie's conjecture on nonsingular systems.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every finite system of equations over an arbitrary group whose exponent-sum matrix has full row rank over ℚ has a simultaneous solution in an overgroup, resolving Howie's conjecture. The coefficient group embeds in the presented quotient. A companion proves Kervaire's conjecture: adjoining one generator and one relation cannot trivialize a nontrivial group.","pdfPath":"preprints/Nonsingular-systems-of-equations-over-arbitrary-groups-October-5-2026/nonsingular-systems-over-arbitrary-groups.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Nonsingular-systems-of-equations-over-arbitrary-groups-October-5-2026/nonsingular-systems-over-arbitrary-groups.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Nonsingular-systems-of-equations-over-arbitrary-groups-October-5-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/Nonsingular-systems-of-equations-over-arbitrary-groups-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Nonsingular-systems-of-equations-over-arbitrary-groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Nonsingular-systems-of-equations-over-arbitrary-groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/history.tex","preprints/Nonsingular-systems-of-equations-over-arbitrary-groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/fixed-space.tex","preprints/Nonsingular-systems-of-equations-over-arbitrary-groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/phase.tex","preprints/Nonsingular-systems-of-equations-over-arbitrary-groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/planar.tex","preprints/Nonsingular-systems-of-equations-over-arbitrary-groups-October-5-2026/build/sections/kernel.tex","preprints/Nonsingular-systems-of-equations-over-arbitrary-groups-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Nonsingular-systems-of-equations-over-arbitrary-groups-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every finite nonsingular system of equations over an arbitrary group has a simultaneous solution in an overgroup. This proves Howie's conjecture on nonsingular systems.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":214},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":2520},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof and technical contribution","sourceOffset":7423},{"kind":"section","title":"Simultaneous fixed spaces","sourceOffset":10346},{"kind":"section","title":"Spectral phase","sourceOffset":23578},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The trace and the choice of phase","sourceOffset":24041},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Subadditivity","sourceOffset":26924},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The phase of a weighted cycle","sourceOffset":29985},{"kind":"section","title":"The planar boundary obstruction","sourceOffset":31594},{"kind":"section","title":"From a kernel relation to a solution","sourceOffset":44970},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A punctured disk for the relation","sourceOffset":45464},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Disjoint bands from circle levels","sourceOffset":47819}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Nonsingular systems","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $G$ be any group, let $1\\le m\\le n$ be integers, and let $w_1,\\ldots,w_m\\in G*F_n$. If their exponent-sum matrix has row rank $m$ over $\\mathbb Q$, then the canonical homomorphism \\[ G\\longrightarrow (G*F_n)/\\langle\\!\\langle w_1,\\ldots,w_m\\rangle\\!\\rangle \\] is injective. Equivalently, the system has a simultaneous solution in an overgroup of $G$.","sourceOffset":1403,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Simultaneous fixed spaces","label":"thm:fixed-space","text":"Let $r\\geq 0$ and $n\\geq 1$, and let $k_1,\\ldots,k_r$ be positive integers. For each $j\\in\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$, let $\\mathcal B_j$ be a finite ordered list of pairs $(i,s)$ with $1\\leq i\\leq r$ and $s\\in\\{1,-1\\}$; repetitions are allowed. Set \\[ d_j=\\sum_{(i,s)\\in\\mathcal B_j}k_i, \\qquad b_{ij}=\\sum_{\\substack{(i',s)\\in\\mathcal B_j\\\\i'=i}}s, \\qquad B=(b_{ij}). \\] Suppose that $B$ has row rank $r$ over $\\mathbb Q$. For arbitrary $P_j\\in\\mathrm U(d_j)$, define \\[ D=\\prod_{i=1}^r\\mathrm U(k_i), \\qquad F_j(X)=\\operatorname{diag}_{(i,s)\\in\\mathcal B_j}(X_i^s)P_j \\quad (X\\in D). \\] Then there is $X\\in D$ such that \\begin{equation} \\sum_{j=1}^n\\dim_{\\mathbb C}\\ker(F_j(X)-I_{d_j}) \\ \\geq\\ \\sum_{i=1}^r k_i. \\end{equation} Here $\\mathrm U(0)$ and an empty product of groups are points, and the fixed-space dimension in dimension zero is zero.","sourceOffset":10886,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/256.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":56778,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Nonsingular-systems-of-equations-over-arbitrary-groups-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"256-02","slug":"The-Kervaire-Theorem-for-Groups-September-24-2026","familyId":"256","title":"The Kervaire theorem for groups","abstract":"We prove that no free product of a nontrivial group with an infinite cyclic group is normally generated by one element. This resolves the Kervaire conjecture positively.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every finite system of equations over an arbitrary group whose exponent-sum matrix has full row rank over ℚ has a simultaneous solution in an overgroup, resolving Howie's conjecture. The coefficient group embeds in the presented quotient. A companion proves Kervaire's conjecture: adjoining one generator and one relation cannot trivialize a nontrivial group.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Kervaire-Theorem-for-Groups-September-24-2026/The-Kervaire-Theorem-for-Groups-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Kervaire-Theorem-for-Groups-September-24-2026/The-Kervaire-Theorem-for-Groups-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Kervaire-Theorem-for-Groups-September-24-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/The-Kervaire-Theorem-for-Groups-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Kervaire-Theorem-for-Groups-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-Kervaire-Theorem-for-Groups-September-24-2026/build/sections/phase.tex","preprints/The-Kervaire-Theorem-for-Groups-September-24-2026/build/sections/degree.tex","preprints/The-Kervaire-Theorem-for-Groups-September-24-2026/build/sections/diagram.tex","preprints/The-Kervaire-Theorem-for-Groups-September-24-2026/build/sections/topology.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Kervaire-Theorem-for-Groups-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that no free product of a nontrivial group with an infinite cyclic group is normally generated by one element. This resolves the Kervaire conjecture positively.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":232},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and methods","sourceOffset":2027},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":6266},{"kind":"section","title":"Spectral phase and its trace","sourceOffset":7855},{"kind":"section","title":"A unitary with a large fixed space","sourceOffset":14061},{"kind":"section","title":"A planar surface cannot have one nontrivial boundary","sourceOffset":19418},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Slots, corners, and boundary cycles","sourceOffset":21432},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An upper bound obtained by mixing the disks","sourceOffset":25118},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Computing the phase along boundary circles","sourceOffset":28468},{"kind":"section","title":"From a coefficient relation to a planar surface","sourceOffset":31112}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every group $A$ and every unimodular word $w\\in A*\\langle t\\rangle$, the canonical homomorphism \\[ A\\longrightarrow (A*\\langle t\\rangle)/\\langle\\!\\langle w\\rangle\\!\\rangle_H \\] is injective.","sourceOffset":1215,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"The Kervaire conjecture","label":"cor:kervaire","text":"If $A\\ne1$, then $(A*\\langle t\\rangle)/\\langle\\!\\langle w\\rangle\\!\\rangle_H\\ne1$ for every $w\\in A*\\langle t\\rangle$.","sourceOffset":1616,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/256.md","scopeText":"Kervaire's conjecture states that the free product of a nontrivial group with an infinite cyclic group cannot be normally generated by one element. The linked formalization proves the stronger coefficient-injectivity statement used in the paper: for any group $A$ and any relator in $A*\\mathbb Z$ whose exponent sum in the cyclic generator is $1$ or $-1$, the natural map from $A$ into the quotient by that relator is injective. This is the selected unimodular-relator result underlying the conjecture.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/256.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":39011,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Kervaire-Theorem-for-Groups-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/The-Kervaire-Theorem-for-Groups-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/The-Kervaire-Theorem-for-Groups-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"257-01","slug":"A-hyperbolic-group-with-no-geometric-CAT0-action-September-25-2026","familyId":"257","title":"A hyperbolic group with no geometric CAT(0) action","abstract":"We construct a hyperbolic group with a finite classifying space that admits no geometric action on a proper complete CAT(0) space. Consequently, a finite aspherical simplicial complex with a linear combinatorial disk-filling inequality need not have a finite locally CAT(0) homotopy model, and hence need not have a finite locally CAT(−1) model. Here metric models carry geodesic length metrics inducing the given complex topology.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Answers negatively whether every word-hyperbolic group is a CAT(0) group. Constructs one with a finite classifying space but no proper cocompact isometric action on any proper complete $\\mathop{\\mathrm{CAT}}\\nolimits (0)$ space, in any dimension.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-hyperbolic-group-with-no-geometric-CAT0-action-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-hyperbolic-group-with-no-geometric-CAT0-action-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-hyperbolic-group-with-no-geometric-CAT0-action-September-25-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/A-hyperbolic-group-with-no-geometric-CAT0-action-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-hyperbolic-group-with-no-geometric-CAT0-action-September-25-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/A-hyperbolic-group-with-no-geometric-CAT0-action-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-hyperbolic-group-with-no-geometric-CAT0-action-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-model.tex","preprints/A-hyperbolic-group-with-no-geometric-CAT0-action-September-25-2026/build/figures/fano-template.tex","preprints/A-hyperbolic-group-with-no-geometric-CAT0-action-September-25-2026/build/figures/fans.tex","preprints/A-hyperbolic-group-with-no-geometric-CAT0-action-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-diagrams.tex","preprints/A-hyperbolic-group-with-no-geometric-CAT0-action-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-sampling.tex","preprints/A-hyperbolic-group-with-no-geometric-CAT0-action-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-energy.tex","preprints/A-hyperbolic-group-with-no-geometric-CAT0-action-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-obstruction.tex","preprints/A-hyperbolic-group-with-no-geometric-CAT0-action-September-25-2026/build/figures/transverse-square.tex","preprints/A-hyperbolic-group-with-no-geometric-CAT0-action-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-completion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-hyperbolic-group-with-no-geometric-CAT0-action-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a hyperbolic group with a finite classifying space that admits no geometric action on a proper complete CAT(0) space. Consequently, a finite aspherical simplicial complex with a linear combinatorial disk-filling inequality need not have a finite locally CAT(0) homotopy model, and hence need not have a finite locally CAT($-1$) model. Here metric models carry geodesic length metrics inducing the given complex topology.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":532},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context","sourceOffset":2095},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Structure of the proof","sourceOffset":6563},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":11788},{"kind":"section","title":"The incidence template and the nested tables","sourceOffset":12920},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Points, lines, and fans","sourceOffset":13098},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The independent tables","sourceOffset":19831},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The group and its two-complex","sourceOffset":23267},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The master pattern and admissible tests","sourceOffset":27130},{"kind":"section","title":"Diagrams and linear filling","sourceOffset":29029},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coincidence ranks","sourceOffset":30963},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Spherical pictures and their reductions","sourceOffset":33523}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist a finite connected two-dimensional aspherical simplicial complex $K$ and a constant $C<\\infty$ with the following properties. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item Every combinatorial edge loop of length $\\ell$ in the universal cover of $K$ bounds a disk diagram with at most $C\\ell$ triangular faces. \\item The group $\\pi_1K$ admits no geometric action on a proper complete $\\CAT(0)$ space. \\end{enumerate} In particular, $\\pi_1K$ is word-hyperbolic, and no finite simplicial complex homotopy equivalent to $K$ admits a locally $\\CAT(0)$ geodesic length metric inducing its given topology. Consequently, no such model admits a compatible locally $\\CAT(-1)$ geodesic length metric.","sourceOffset":1192,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:diagram","text":"Fix sufficiently small positive $\\alpha,\\beta,\\delta$ with $\\beta>7\\alpha$, and put $f=2-\\alpha$ and $p=3-\\beta$. As $n\\longrightarrow\\infty$, the probability tends to one that $P$ is aspherical and its letter presentation satisfies a linear disk isoperimetric inequality.","sourceOffset":29452,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"The incidence template","label":"def:template","text":"Identify the points of the projective plane over $\\F_2$ with the seven nonzero vectors of $\\F_2^3$. A line consists of the three nonzero vectors in a two-dimensional subspace. Fix \\[ A=e_1,\\qquad B=e_2,\\qquad C=e_3,\\qquad D=e_1+e_2+e_3. \\] No three of these four points are collinear. For distinct points $i,j$, write $ij$ for their line. The bipartite graph $\\Gamma$ has a vertex $X_i$ for every point, an additional vertex $Y_i$ for $i\\in\\{A,B,C,D\\}$, and a vertex for every line. Both representatives over a point are joined to each incident line, with one exception: the edge $Y_D$--$CD$ is omitt","sourceOffset":13135,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"The tables","label":"def:tables","text":"Use the following mutually independent Bernoulli arrays. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item For each point $i$, an array indexed by $S_n^{E(F_i)}$ includes every tuple with probability $N^{f-k_i}$. An included tuple, together with its type $i$, is a fan occurrence $b$. \\item An array indexed by $S_n^{E(\\Gamma)}$ marks every full core tuple with probability $N^{p-gf-1}$. A top occurrence $a$ is a marked tuple whose restriction to every $F_i$ is an included fan occurrence. We write $a\\to b$ when $b$ is the fan in one of the seven slots of $a$. \\item For each unoriented nonbacktrac","sourceOffset":20305,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/257.md","scopeText":"The formalized result constructs a finite connected aspherical complex with a linear disk-filling bound whose fundamental group is word-hyperbolic but admits no geometric action on a nonempty proper complete $\\mathrm{CAT}(0)$ space. It also excludes locally $\\mathrm{CAT}(-1)$ geodesic metrics on every finite complex homotopy equivalent to the witness. The stronger claims that the witness is two-dimensional and that those models admit no locally $\\mathrm{CAT}(0)$ metric are not included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/257.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":141032,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":4,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-hyperbolic-group-with-no-geometric-CAT0-action-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"258-01","slug":"Baumslag-Solitar-free-one-relator-groups-are-hyperbolic-September-25-2026","familyId":"258","title":"Baumslag-Solitar-free one-relator groups are hyperbolic","abstract":"We prove Gersten's conjecture: every finitely generated one-relator group containing no subgroup isomorphic to a Baumslag–Solitar group $\\mathop{\\mathrm{BS}}\\nolimits (m,n)$, for nonzero integers m, n, is word-hyperbolic.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Gersten's conjecture: every finitely generated one-relator group containing no Baumslag–Solitar subgroup $\\mathrm{BS}(m,n)$, with $m,n\\ne0$, is word-hyperbolic. It also proves that every word-hyperbolic one-relator group is virtually compact special.","pdfPath":"preprints/Baumslag-Solitar-free-one-relator-groups-are-hyperbolic-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Baumslag-Solitar-free-one-relator-groups-are-hyperbolic-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Baumslag-Solitar-free-one-relator-groups-are-hyperbolic-September-25-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/Baumslag-Solitar-free-one-relator-groups-are-hyperbolic-September-25-2026/build/source/paper.tex","preprints/Baumslag-Solitar-free-one-relator-groups-are-hyperbolic-September-25-2026/build/source/introduction.tex","preprints/Baumslag-Solitar-free-one-relator-groups-are-hyperbolic-September-25-2026/build/source/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Baumslag-Solitar-free-one-relator-groups-are-hyperbolic-September-25-2026/build/source/annuli.tex","preprints/Baumslag-Solitar-free-one-relator-groups-are-hyperbolic-September-25-2026/build/source/stationarity.tex","preprints/Baumslag-Solitar-free-one-relator-groups-are-hyperbolic-September-25-2026/build/source/carriers.tex","preprints/Baumslag-Solitar-free-one-relator-groups-are-hyperbolic-September-25-2026/build/source/expansion.tex","preprints/Baumslag-Solitar-free-one-relator-groups-are-hyperbolic-September-25-2026/build/source/rays.tex","preprints/Baumslag-Solitar-free-one-relator-groups-are-hyperbolic-September-25-2026/build/source/magnus.tex","preprints/Baumslag-Solitar-free-one-relator-groups-are-hyperbolic-September-25-2026/build/source/free-by-cyclic.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Baumslag-Solitar-free-one-relator-groups-are-hyperbolic-September-25-2026/build/source/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Gersten's conjecture: every finitely generated one-relator group containing no subgroup isomorphic to a Baumslag--Solitar group $\\operatorname{BS}(m,n)$, for nonzero integers $m,n$, is word-hyperbolic.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":273},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier work and the remaining step","sourceOffset":2505},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The combination argument","sourceOffset":6752},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Magnus subgraphs","sourceOffset":10299},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Structure of the proof","sourceOffset":11765},{"kind":"section","title":"Intersections, cyclic commensurators, and rank bounds","sourceOffset":12610},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Quasiconvex intersections","sourceOffset":12947},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Commensuration in a tree action","sourceOffset":17545},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Strong inertia in free groups","sourceOffset":22924},{"kind":"section","title":"Annuli and currents for a restricted HNN extension","sourceOffset":24209},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Frames and stabilizers of paths","sourceOffset":26293},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The algebraic annular-flaring criterion","sourceOffset":32530}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $X$ be a finite set and let $r\\in F(X)$. If the one-relator group $G=\\langle X\\mid r\\rangle$ contains no subgroup isomorphic to $\\operatorname{BS}(m,n)$ for any nonzero integers $m,n$, then $G$ is word-hyperbolic.","sourceOffset":868,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:magnus","text":"Let $\\Gamma$ be a finite connected graph, and let $\\lambda:S^1\\to\\Gamma$ be a nontrivial immersed circuit representing a non-power in $\\pi_1\\Gamma$. Attach a $2$-cell along $\\lambda$, obtaining $Y$. If $\\pi_1Y$ is Baumslag--Solitar-free, then, for every connected subgraph $S\\subseteq\\Gamma$ with $\\lambda(S^1)\\nsubseteq S$, the map \\[ \\pi_1S\\longrightarrow\\pi_1Y \\] is injective and its image is quasiconvex.","sourceOffset":10654,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"pre:strong-inertia-definition","text":"A finitely generated subgroup \\(E\\) of a finitely generated free group \\(P\\) is strongly inert if, for every finitely generated subgroup \\(S\\leq P\\), \\begin{equation} \\sum_{SpE\\in S\\backslash P/E} \\bar r(E\\cap p^{-1}Sp)\\leq\\bar r(S). \\end{equation} The summands are independent of the chosen double-coset representatives. Only finitely many intersections are nontrivial, by Lemma \\ref{pre:double-coset-finiteness}.","sourceOffset":23259,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"car:small-graph","text":"Pass to a subsequence $n\\to\\infty$ on which the natural graph of $\\Gamma_n$ has a fixed abstract type, and identify those natural graphs with one fixed finite graph. Pass to a further subsequence on which each natural edge has either a fixed label or length tending to infinity. For every edge of the latter kind, arrange convergence of all finite prefixes of its label in both orientations. The small graph $\\Gamma^{\\mathrm{sm}}$ consists of the natural vertices and the natural edges with fixed labels, subdivided according to those labels.","sourceOffset":89685,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":182413,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":18,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Baumslag-Solitar-free-one-relator-groups-are-hyperbolic-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"258-02","slug":"Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026","familyId":"258","title":"Virtual compact specialness of hyperbolic one-relator groups","abstract":"We prove that every word-hyperbolic one-relator group is virtually compact special. Combined with the work of Kielak–Linton, this resolves Wise's virtual free-by-cyclic conjecture for hyperbolic one-relator groups: every such group is virtually free-by-cyclic, with the free kernel allowed to have infinite rank.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Gersten's conjecture: every finitely generated one-relator group containing no Baumslag–Solitar subgroup $\\mathrm{BS}(m,n)$, with $m,n\\ne0$, is word-hyperbolic. It also proves that every word-hyperbolic one-relator group is virtually compact special.","pdfPath":"preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/01-filling.tex","preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/figures/selective-chambers.tex","preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/02-ascending.tex","preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/03-rows.tex","preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/figures/row-refinement.tex","preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/04-coefficients.tex","preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/04b-analytic.tex","preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/05-gates.tex","preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/06-closure.tex","preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/figures/row-refinement-folding.tex","preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/07-transfer.tex","preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/08-kernels.tex","preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/09-fans.tex","preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/figures/fan-templates.tex","preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/10-boundary.tex","preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/figures/fans-and-panels.tex","preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/11-free-by-cyclic.tex","preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every word-hyperbolic one-relator group is virtually compact special. Combined with the work of Kielak--Linton, this resolves Wise's virtual free-by-cyclic conjecture for hyperbolic one-relator groups: every such group is virtually free-by-cyclic, with the free kernel allowed to have infinite rank.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":359},{"kind":"section","title":"Background, conventions, and the reduction","sourceOffset":12504},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cubulation and hierarchy results","sourceOffset":13080},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The primitive extension reduction","sourceOffset":16504},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coefficient and tree conventions","sourceOffset":20761},{"kind":"section","title":"A filling criterion and progressive characters","sourceOffset":22540},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cofinite fillings and selective quotients","sourceOffset":25941},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removing walls near the retained apices","sourceOffset":32734},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sheets and a cubical branched model","sourceOffset":39820},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Novikov realization along quotient chains","sourceOffset":56812},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From two-sided Novikov acyclicity to a free kernel","sourceOffset":78148},{"kind":"section","title":"Hyperbolic ascending tori","sourceOffset":83532}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $X$ be finite, let $r\\in F(X)$, and suppose that \\[ G=F(X)/\\langle\\!\\langle r\\rangle\\!\\rangle \\] is word-hyperbolic. Then $G$ is virtually compact special. More precisely, there are a finite-index subgroup $H\\leq G$, a finite connected nonpositively curved cube complex $C$, and a finite simplicial graph $\\Lambda$ such that \\[ \\pi_1(C)\\cong H \\quad\\text{and}\\quad C\\longrightarrow S_\\Lambda \\] is a cubical local isometry to the Salvetti complex of the right-angled Artin group defined by $\\Lambda$.","sourceOffset":706,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:ascending-intro","text":"Let $F$ be a finitely generated free group and let $\\phi:F\\to F$ be injective. If its ascending mapping torus \\[ F*_{\\phi}=\\langle F,t\\mid t^{-1}ft=\\phi(f),\\ f\\in F\\rangle \\] is word-hyperbolic, then it is virtually compact special.","sourceOffset":6867,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"fill:selective-input","text":"Let \\(K\\lhd J\\), with \\(J/K\\) virtually RFRS. We say that \\((J,K)\\) has the selective realization property if, for every finite-index preimage \\(J'\\le J\\) and every finite set \\(T\\subset K\\), there are a finite-index preimage \\(H\\le J'\\), normal in \\(J'\\), a subgroup \\(U\\le K\\) containing \\(T\\), and a homomorphism from \\(H\\) to a virtually RFRS group that is injective on \\(U\\).","sourceOffset":58365,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Primitive data","label":"row:primitive-data","text":"Fix \\(k\\geq1\\), set \\(a_i=t^{-i}at^i\\), and put \\[ B_0=\\{a_1,\\ldots,a_{k-1}\\},\\qquad P_-=F(a_0,\\ldots,a_{k-1}),\\qquad P_+=F(a_1,\\ldots,a_k). \\] An empty \\(B_0\\) is allowed. For relatively prime positive integers \\(p,q\\), write \\(\\operatorname{pr}_{p/q}(v,w)\\) for the positive primitive word of slope \\(p/q\\) in two slots. The two presentations are \\begin{equation} G=\\langle a,t\\mid R\\rangle,\\qquad R=\\operatorname{pr}_{p/q}(x,y) \\quad\\hbox{or}\\quad R=\\operatorname{pr}_{p/q}(xy,z), \\end{equation} where \\[ x\\in P_-\\setminus F(B_0),\\qquad y\\in P_+\\setminus F(B_0),\\qquad 1\\neq z\\in F(B_0) \\] (the la","sourceOffset":119795,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":573621,"sourceFileCount":20,"statementCount":48,"definitionCount":8,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Virtual-compact-specialness-of-hyperbolic-one-relator-groups-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"259-01","slug":"A-group-without-fixed-price-October-5-2026","familyId":"259","title":"A group without fixed price","abstract":"We construct a finitely generated group with two essentially free probability-measure-preserving actions of different costs. This gives a negative answer to Gaboriau's general fixed-price problem. The group is an amalgam of a free group of rank 100 with a direct product. Its Bernoulli action has cost bounded away from one, whereas finite height extensions have costs tending to one.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a finitely generated group with two essentially free probability-measure-preserving actions of different costs, answering the general fixed-price problem negatively. Its Bernoulli action has cost bounded away from one, while a sequence of finite height extensions has costs tending to one.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-group-without-fixed-price-October-5-2026/unequal-costs-rank-100-amalgam.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-group-without-fixed-price-October-5-2026/unequal-costs-rank-100-amalgam.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-group-without-fixed-price-October-5-2026","subjectId":"5","subject":"Group theory","subjectZh":"群論","texPaths":["preprints/A-group-without-fixed-price-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-group-without-fixed-price-October-5-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/A-group-without-fixed-price-October-5-2026/build/group-actions.tex","preprints/A-group-without-fixed-price-October-5-2026/build/compression.tex","preprints/A-group-without-fixed-price-October-5-2026/build/deployment.tex","preprints/A-group-without-fixed-price-October-5-2026/build/finite-models.tex","preprints/A-group-without-fixed-price-October-5-2026/build/planar.tex","preprints/A-group-without-fixed-price-October-5-2026/build/rank-surgery.tex","preprints/A-group-without-fixed-price-October-5-2026/build/surgery-figure.tex","preprints/A-group-without-fixed-price-October-5-2026/build/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-group-without-fixed-price-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a finitely generated group with two essentially free probability-measure-preserving actions of different costs. This gives a negative answer to Gaboriau's general fixed-price problem. The group is an amalgam of a free group of rank $100$ with a direct product. Its Bernoulli action has cost bounded away from one, whereas finite height extensions have costs tending to one.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":449},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The group and the two actions","sourceOffset":2134},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the proof works","sourceOffset":3761},{"kind":"section","title":"The group and an action of small cost","sourceOffset":7343},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The amalgam","sourceOffset":7678},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Probability spaces and the two actions","sourceOffset":11035},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A graphing on the height extension","sourceOffset":14014},{"kind":"section","title":"Compressing a graphing after enlarging a supplied relation","sourceOffset":17694},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Borel selections and small complete sets","sourceOffset":18968},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compression and its cost","sourceOffset":22647},{"kind":"section","title":"Deployment and a relative cost lower bound","sourceOffset":30670},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite graphing and its subdivision","sourceOffset":32440}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"intro:main","text":"Put $K=e^{96}96^{99}/95^{95}$, choose \\[ 0<\\alpha<1/200,\\qquad K\\alpha^3<1/2, \\qquad \\eta=\\alpha/100. \\] For the group and actions just defined, \\[ \\operatorname{Cost}(\\mathcal R_{\\Gamma\\curvearrowright X}) \\ge1+\\eta, \\qquad \\operatorname{Cost}(\\mathcal R_{\\Gamma\\curvearrowright Y_M}) \\le1+\\frac{99}{M}\\quad(M>100). \\] In particular, choosing an integer $M>\\max\\{100,99/\\eta\\}$ gives two essentially free p.m.p. actions of the same finitely generated group with strictly different costs. Thus the general fixed-price assertion is false.","sourceOffset":3157,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"deployment:main","text":"Let $\\Gamma=A*_J B$ be finitely generated, let $J$ be infinite, and let $\\Gamma$ act essentially freely and p.m.p. on a standard probability space $(X,\\mu)$. If $\\delta_X$ is the infimum cost of graphings that generate $\\mathcal R_A$ together with the supplied relation $\\mathcal R_J$, then \\[ \\operatorname{Cost}(\\mathcal R_\\Gamma)\\geq 1+\\delta_X. \\]","sourceOffset":31614,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":85711,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-group-without-fixed-price-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"260-01","slug":"The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-with-charge-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026","familyId":"260","title":"The spacetime Penrose inequality with charge and original-data rigidity","abstract":"Under the stated energy, decay, and trapping hypotheses, we prove the sharp charged spacetime Penrose upper-area inequality for one-ended three-dimensional initial data with source-free electric and magnetic fields. The theorem allows arbitrary second fundamental form, nonzero ADM momentum, and disconnected boundary. Writing m for invariant ADM mass, Q for total charge magnitude, and rA for the minimum-enclosing-area radius, the bound is m ≥ Q and $r_A\\le m+\\sqrt{m^2-Q^2}$. The polynomial mass bound $m\\ge(r_A+Q^2/r_A)/2$ is asserted only when $r_A\\gt Q$. When m > Q, equality under the stated connected, outermost, outer-area-minimizing future-horizon hypotheses identifies the original data as a smooth spacelike slice of dyonic Reissner–Nordström, including smooth attachment at the future horizon.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sharp enclosing-area spacetime Penrose inequality for smooth one-ended asymptotically flat initial data in every spatial dimension n ≥ 3, under dominant energy, weak future trapping, positive enclosing area, and the stated decay assumptions. It bounds invariant ADM mass below using minimum enclosing area, with equality rigidity under additional horizon hypotheses. Charged upper-area bounds treat dyonic three-dimensional data; the higher-dimensional purely electric extension uses the matched neutral theorem.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-with-charge-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-with-charge-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-with-charge-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-with-charge-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-with-charge-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-with-charge-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/glyphtounicode.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-with-charge-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-with-charge-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/statements.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-with-charge-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/cuts.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-with-charge-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/end_preparation.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-with-charge-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/filled_system.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-with-charge-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/a_priori.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-with-charge-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/elliptic_solver.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-with-charge-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/comparison.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-with-charge-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/variation.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-with-charge-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/stationary_region.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-with-charge-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/rigidity.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-with-charge-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"Under the stated energy, decay, and trapping hypotheses, we prove the sharp charged spacetime Penrose upper-area inequality for one-ended three-dimensional initial data with source-free electric and magnetic fields. The theorem allows arbitrary second fundamental form, nonzero ADM momentum, and disconnected boundary. Writing $m$ for invariant ADM mass, $Q$ for total charge magnitude, and $r_A$ for the minimum-enclosing-area radius, the bound is $m\\ge Q$ and $r_A\\le m+\\sqrt{m^2-Q^2}$. The polynomial mass bound $m\\ge(r_A+Q^2/r_A)/2$ is asserted only when $r_A>Q$. When $m>Q$, equality under the stated connected, outermost, outer-area-minimizing future-horizon hypotheses identifies the original data as a smooth spacelike slice of dyonic Reissner--Nordstr\\\"om, including smooth attachment at the future horizon.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":879},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Construction of the numerical comparison","sourceOffset":4077},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Recovery of equality data","sourceOffset":6232},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":7895},{"kind":"section","title":"Initial data and the main theorems","sourceOffset":8381},{"kind":"section","title":"Full enclosing cuts and their minimizing frontiers","sourceOffset":17561},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An auxiliary perimeter problem","sourceOffset":21693},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Strict compactness and the derivative of the minimum","sourceOffset":32123},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compatibility of minimizing sheets","sourceOffset":37830},{"kind":"section","title":"Charge-preserving preparation of the asymptotic end","sourceOffset":41906},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Flux forms and conformal changes","sourceOffset":42474},{"kind":"subsection","title":"ADM flux under a change of asymptotic plane","sourceOffset":45609}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Charged spacetime Penrose inequality","label":"thm:numerical","text":"Let the data satisfy Definition \\ref{def:data}, and suppose $\\theta_+\\le0$ on every component of $S$. Then $E>|P|$, the invariant ADM mass $m=\\sqrt{E^2-|P|^2}$ is positive, and \\begin{equation} E\\ge\\sqrt{|P|^2+Q^2},\\qquad m\\ge Q,\\qquad r_A\\le m+\\sqrt{m^2-Q^2}. \\end{equation} The same conclusion holds if, on each boundary component, one fixes either the condition $H+\\operatorname{tr}_S K\\le0$ or the condition $H-\\operatorname{tr}_S K\\le0$.","sourceOffset":12834,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Original-data equality","label":"thm:rigidity","text":"Assume the connected horizon class and $m>Q$. If \\begin{equation} \\sqrt{\\frac{A}{4\\pi}}=m+\\sqrt{m^2-Q^2}, \\end{equation} then the entire original quadruple $(g,K,\\EE,\\BB)$, including its boundary, is induced by a global smooth orientation-preserving spacelike embedding into the regular future-horizon extension of one exterior of dyonic Reissner--Nordstr\\\"om with parameters $(m,Q_E,Q_B)$. The image lies in the domain of outer communication together with its corresponding future horizon. The boundary is a full smooth horizon cross-section or the bifurcation sphere, and the chosen end approaches the corresponding spatial infinity. The embedding, its future unit normal and the induced electromagnetic fields extend smoothly to $S$. The non-electromagnetic matter densities vanish throughout the exterior.","sourceOffset":14514,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Charged exterior data","label":"def:data","text":"Let $\\Omega$ be a connected oriented smooth three-manifold with nonempty compact smooth boundary $S$, and let $(g,K,\\EE,\\BB)$ be smooth up to $S$. Here $g$ is Riemannian, $K$ is a symmetric covariant two-tensor, and $\\EE,\\BB$ are vector fields. We require completeness of the metric space $(\\Omega,g)$ with its boundary included. Outside a compact set there is exactly one coordinate end, diffeomorphic to $\\{x\\in\\R^3:|x|>R_0\\}$. Throughout the proof we use geometric constraint densities: \\begin{align} 2\\mu&=R_g+\\tau^2-|K|_g^2, & J_i&=\\nabla^j(K_{ij}-\\tau g_{ij}), &\\tau&=\\operatorname{tr}_gK, \\\\ \\","sourceOffset":8952,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Full enclosing cut","label":"def:full-cut","text":"A full enclosing cut is $\\Gamma=\\partial D$, the entire intrinsic manifold boundary of a connected smooth codimension-zero submanifold-with-boundary $D\\subset\\Omega$ that is closed in $\\Omega$, has manifold interior in $\\operatorname{int}\\Omega$, and contains the whole sufficiently distant part of the end. The boundary $\\Gamma$ is required to be compact, smooth, embedded and two-sided. It is oriented toward the end. All components, including any portions coincident with $S$, are counted. In particular $D=\\Omega$ contributes $\\Gamma=S$. Define, in the original metric, \\begin{equation} a_g(S)=\\i","sourceOffset":11675,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/260.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":352380,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":29,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-with-charge-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"260-02","slug":"A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026","familyId":"260","title":"A Charged Reduction of the Spacetime Penrose Inequality in Spatial Dimensions at Least Four","abstract":"Using the companion neutral spacetime Penrose theorem exactly at its stated strong-decay, future-trapped, positive-area, and future-timelike scope, we prove the sharp purely electric upper-area inequality in every spatial dimension n ≥ 4 for one-ended charged data satisfying the charged dominant energy condition and $\\mathop{\\mathrm{div}}\\nolimits _g E=0$, with the specified decay, integrability, and finite-flux assumptions. The data may have arbitrary interior topology and ADM momentum, with a future or past trapping sign chosen independently on each boundary component. Positive enclosing area and strict ADM timelikeness are conclusions. The polynomial mass rearrangement is asserted only when the $(n-2)$nd power of the enclosing-area radius exceeds $|Q|$. When $m\\gt |Q|$, equality under the stated connected, outermost, outer-area-minimizing future-horizon hypotheses recovers the original metric, second fundamental form, and electric field as a global spacelike slice of a Reissner–Nordström–Tangherlini exterior. The equality classification includes only slices whose induced magnetic two-form vanishes.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sharp enclosing-area spacetime Penrose inequality for smooth one-ended asymptotically flat initial data in every spatial dimension n ≥ 3, under dominant energy, weak future trapping, positive enclosing area, and the stated decay assumptions. It bounds invariant ADM mass below using minimum enclosing area, with equality rigidity under additional horizon hypotheses. Charged upper-area bounds treat dyonic three-dimensional data; the higher-dimensional purely electric extension uses the matched neutral theorem.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-statements.tex","preprints/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-preparation.tex","preprints/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-filled-system.tex","preprints/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-elliptic.tex","preprints/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-flux-conversion.tex","preprints/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026/build/figures/height-bands.tex","preprints/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-original-variation.tex","preprints/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-singular-multipliers.tex","preprints/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026/build/sections/08-stationary-rigidity.tex","preprints/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026/build/sections/09-vacuum-uniqueness.tex","preprints/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026/build/sections/10-recovery-sharpness.tex","preprints/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026/build/figures/boundary-recovery.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"Using the companion neutral spacetime Penrose theorem exactly at its stated strong-decay, future-trapped, positive-area, and future-timelike scope, we prove the sharp purely electric upper-area inequality in every spatial dimension $n\\ge4$ for one-ended charged data satisfying the charged dominant energy condition and $\\Div_g E=0$, with the specified decay, integrability, and finite-flux assumptions. The data may have arbitrary interior topology and ADM momentum, with a future or past trapping sign chosen independently on each boundary component. Positive enclosing area and strict ADM timelikeness are conclusions. The polynomial mass rearrangement is asserted only when the $(n-2)$nd power of the enclosing-area radius exceeds $|Q|$. When $m>|Q|$, equality under the stated connected, outermost, outer-area-minimizing future-horizon hypotheses recovers the original metric, second fundamental form, and electric field as a global spacelike slice of a Reissner--Nordstr\\\"om--Tangherlini exterior. The equality classification includes only slices whose induced magnetic two-form vanishes.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":1168},{"kind":"section","title":"Data, conventions, and the precise conclusions","sourceOffset":7394},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Initial data and electromagnetic normalization","sourceOffset":7875},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Full enclosing cuts","sourceOffset":11622},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The precise neutral input","sourceOffset":12900},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Numerical theorem and original-data equality","sourceOffset":15061},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and order of implication","sourceOffset":19829},{"kind":"section","title":"Area positivity and preparation of the asymptotic end","sourceOffset":21192},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The full-cut convention and positive area","sourceOffset":22200},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conformal changes and a conditional endpoint reduction","sourceOffset":24574},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compact inverses for the linear cutoff errors","sourceOffset":30569},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A strict conformal direction with weak asymptotic data","sourceOffset":35651}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Neutral numerical input","label":"thm:neutral-input","text":"Let $d\\ge3$ and let $(U^d,h,L)$ be a connected oriented smooth initial-data exterior with nonempty compact smooth boundary $T$, complete with $T$ included, with compact complement of exactly one Euclidean coordinate end. Write \\[ 2\\mu=R_h+(\\tr_hL)^2-|L|_h^2, \\qquad J=\\Div_h(L-(\\tr_hL)h). \\] Suppose $\\mu\\ge|J|_h$, $\\mu,|J|_h\\in L^1$, and for some \\[ \\frac{d-2}{2}<q_0<d-2 \\] assume \\[ h-\\delta=O_6(r^{-q_0}),\\qquad L=O_5(r^{-1-q_0}). \\] Assume that the ADM limits exist, with energy factor $[2(d-1)\\omega_{d-1}]^{-1}$ and momentum factor $[(d-1)\\omega_{d-1}]^{-1}$ applied to the Euclidean fluxes in \\eqref{eq:adm-energy}--\\eqref{eq:adm-momentum}, with $g,K,\\tau$ replaced by $h,L,\\tr_hL$. Assume $\\mathcal E_{\\mathrm{ADM}}>|\\mathbf P_{\\mathrm{ADM}}|$, and suppose every component of $T$ satisfies \\[ H_T+\\tr_TL\\le0 \\] with normal toward the end. Let $A_*>0$ be the infimum of the areas of full cuts in the sense of Definition \\ref{def:full-cut}, now in $(U,h)$, with all components and contact with $T$ counted. Then \\begin{equation} \\sqrt{\\mathcal E_{\\mathrm{ADM}}^2-|\\mathbf P_{\\mathrm{ADM}}|^2} \\ge\\frac12\\left(\\frac{A_*}{\\omega_{d-1}}\\right)^{(d-2)/(d-1)}. \\end{equation} No minimizing cut is required to be smooth or attained. No spin, symmetry, maximality, vacuum or stationary-development hypothesis is imposed.","sourceOffset":13103,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Purely electric spacetime Penrose inequality","label":"thm:numerical","text":"Assume Theorem \\ref{thm:neutral-input}. For every exterior in Definition \\ref{def:data}, with full enclosing area as in Definition \\ref{def:full-cut}, \\begin{equation} 0<a\\le\\Area_g(S),\\qquad \\mathcal E_{\\mathrm{ADM}}>|\\mathbf P_{\\mathrm{ADM}}|. \\end{equation} Consequently the invariant mass \\[ m=\\sqrt{\\mathcal E_{\\mathrm{ADM}}^2-|\\mathbf P_{\\mathrm{ADM}}|^2} \\] is well defined and positive, and \\begin{equation} m\\ge |Q|,\\qquad X_A\\le m+\\sqrt{m^2-Q^2}. \\end{equation} Equivalently, the mass-charge bound applies when $0<X_A\\le|Q|$, whereas \\begin{equation} m\\ge\\frac12\\left(X_A+\\frac{Q^2}{X_A}\\right) \\qquad\\text{when }X_A>|Q|. \\end{equation} For $Q=0$ this is $m\\ge X_A/2$.","sourceOffset":15120,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Admissible purely electric exterior","label":"def:data","text":"Let $\\Omega$ be a connected oriented smooth $n$-manifold with nonempty compact smooth boundary $S$. The metric $g$, symmetric covariant two-tensor $K$, and vector field $E$ are smooth up to $S$. The metric space $(\\Omega,g)$, including $S$, is complete. Outside a compact set there is exactly one coordinate end \\[ \\Omega\\setminus C\\simeq\\{x\\in\\R^n:|x|>R_0\\}. \\] In particular the remaining topology is compact; there are no additional ends. Put $\\tau=\\tr_gK$ and define \\begin{equation} \\begin{split} 2\\mu_m&=R_g+\\tau^2-|K|_g^2-k(k-1)|E|_g^2,\\\\ (J_m)_i&=\\nabla^j(K_{ij}-\\tau g_{ij}). \\end{split} \\en","sourceOffset":7936,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Spacetime conventions","label":"def:normalization","text":"A Lorentz metric $G$ has signature $(-,+,\\ldots,+)$. Its electromagnetic two-form $\\mathcal F$ is normalized by \\begin{equation} \\Ric_G-\\frac{R_G}2G =2\\left(\\mathcal F_{ac}\\mathcal F_b{}^c -\\frac14\\mathcal F_{cd}\\mathcal F^{cd}G_{ab}\\right)+T_m, \\qquad \\dd\\mathcal F=\\dd(*_G\\mathcal F)=0. \\end{equation} For a spacelike embedding $\\iota$ with future unit normal $N$, the induced conventions are \\begin{equation} K(U,V)=G(\\nabla_U N,V),\\qquad E^\\flat=c_n^{-1}\\iota^*(i_N\\mathcal F),\\qquad \\iota^*\\mathcal F=0. \\end{equation} The induced orientation is $\\iota^*(i_N\\dd V_G)$, and $T_m(N,N)=\\mu_m$, with","sourceOffset":10626,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/260.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":356346,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":27,"definitionCount":4,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-Charged-Reduction-of-the-Spacetime-Penrose-Inequality-in-Spatial-Dimensions-at-Least-Four-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"260-03","slug":"Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026","familyId":"260","title":"Spacetime Penrose inequalities: enclosing area, charge, and rigidity","abstract":"We prove sharp spacetime Penrose inequalities for invariant ADM mass and minimum enclosing area in three and four spatial dimensions. Under their respective designated-end conventions, the neutral numerical results allow arbitrary second fundamental form, nonzero momentum, disconnected weakly future trapped boundary, and finitely many ends. Under the stated horizon hypotheses, equality for the neutral inequalities reconstructs the original exterior data as spacelike slices of Schwarzschild spacetime in three dimensions and Schwarzschild–Tangherlini spacetime in four; the four-dimensional equality theorem concerns a connected, one-ended exterior. We also prove a three-dimensional electric–magnetic charged upper-area inequality for one-ended data with nonzero momentum, with separate purely electric rest-frame corollaries allowing finitely many ends. Finally, for each fixed transverse-traceless seed and prescribed decaying solution branch, we prove a local Schwarzschild–anti-de Sitter inequality.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sharp enclosing-area spacetime Penrose inequality for smooth one-ended asymptotically flat initial data in every spatial dimension n ≥ 3, under dominant energy, weak future trapping, positive enclosing area, and the stated decay assumptions. It bounds invariant ADM mass below using minimum enclosing area, with equality rigidity under additional horizon hypotheses. Charged upper-area bounds treat dyonic three-dimensional data; the higher-dimensional purely electric extension uses the matched neutral theorem.","pdfPath":"preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/figures/proof-routes.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/modules.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/neutral/setup.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/neutral/sections/02-exterior-reduction.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/neutral/sections/03-trapped-domains.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/neutral/sections/04-deformation.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/neutral/sections/05-apriori.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/neutral/sections/06-elliptic-existence.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/neutral/sections/07-mass-limit.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/neutral-bridge.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/rigidity/sections/00-rigidity-setup.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/rigidity/sections/08-variational-rigidity.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/rigidity/sections/09-static-rigidity.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/charged/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/figures/charged-region.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/charged/sections/02-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/charged/sections/03-rest.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/charged/sections/04-preparation.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/charged/sections/05-elliptic.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/charged/sections/06-comparison.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/charged/sections/07-mass.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/charged/sections/08-conclusion.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/charged-extensions.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/four/setup.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/four/rigidity/01-enclosures.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/four/sections/01-end.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/four/sections/02-geometry.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/four/figures/two-frontiers.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/four/sections/03-system.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/four/sections/04-bounds.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/four/sections/05-regularity.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/four/sections/06-existence.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/four/rigidity/03-variation.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/four/rigidity/04-adjoint.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/four/rigidity/05-static-base.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/four/rigidity/06-classification.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/four/rigidity/07-recovery.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/ads/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/ads/sections/01-setting.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/ads/sections/02-analysis.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/ads/sections/03-cmc.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/ads/sections/04-quadratic.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/ads/sections/05-quartic.tex","preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/ads/sections/06-conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove sharp spacetime Penrose inequalities for invariant ADM mass and minimum enclosing area in three and four spatial dimensions. Under their respective designated-end conventions, the neutral numerical results allow arbitrary second fundamental form, nonzero momentum, disconnected weakly future trapped boundary, and finitely many ends. Under the stated horizon hypotheses, equality for the neutral inequalities reconstructs the original exterior data as spacelike slices of Schwarzschild spacetime in three dimensions and Schwarzschild--Tangherlini spacetime in four; the four-dimensional equality theorem concerns a connected, one-ended exterior. We also prove a three-dimensional electric--magnetic charged upper-area inequality for one-ended data with nonzero momentum, with separate purely electric rest-frame corollaries allowing finitely many ends. Finally, for each fixed transverse-traceless seed and prescribed decaying solution branch, we prove a local Schwarzschild--anti-de Sitter inequality.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":1074},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The results and their relation","sourceOffset":2981},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the proofs fit together","sourceOffset":7201},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Relation to earlier work","sourceOffset":11695},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reading conventions","sourceOffset":14180},{"kind":"part","title":"Three-dimensional initial data","sourceOffset":15062},{"kind":"section","title":"Three-dimensional exterior data and the mass inequality","sourceOffset":15101},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Initial data, normals, and enclosing area","sourceOffset":15192},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The one-ended exterior inequality","sourceOffset":19158},{"kind":"section","title":"Exterior approximation and reduction to a rest end","sourceOffset":19927},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A strict conformal direction","sourceOffset":20895},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compactness of enclosing minimizers","sourceOffset":33248}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"MOTS barriers and the total trapped region","label":"n3:domains:region-theorem","text":"Let $(N,g,Q)$ be a smooth compact three-dimensional initial data set with boundary $\\Gamma_-\\sqcup\\Gamma_+$. Assume $\\Gamma_+$ is nonempty and has positive expansion with its normal out of $N$. The inner part $\\Gamma_-$ may be empty; when it is present, assume its expansion is negative with its normal into $N$. Consider all smooth domains whose inner boundary is $\\Gamma_-$ and whose remaining boundary $\\Sigma$ lies in the interior of $N$ and satisfies $\\Theta_Q(\\Sigma)\\leq0$. They avoid $\\Gamma_+$, and their normal on $\\Sigma$ points out of the domain. If their union is nonempty, its closure has a smooth compact embedded stable MOTS frontier in the interior, separating it from $\\Gamma_+$. This closure is itself the closure of such a domain and contains every domain in the defining class. If $\\Gamma_-$ is nonempty, the two strict barriers also give a nonempty smooth embedded enclosing MOTS.","sourceOffset":69591,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Prepared domain","label":"n3:domains:prepared-domain","text":"The thresholds and the domain above can be chosen with the following properties. The numbers $c_b,c_w<0$ are right-continuity points of their respective full maximal-region families. The boundary of $\\Omega$ consists of the faces in Equation \\eqref{n3:domains:face-expansions}, has disjoint smooth outward leaf collars as in Lemma \\ref{n3:domains:stable-collar}, and preserves the enclosing-area lower bound in Equation \\eqref{n3:domains:inherited-area}. There are a smooth compactly supported function $C$ on $\\Omega$, constants $C_b\\geq0$, $C_w\\leq0$, and positive constants $k_B$ on its face collars such that \\begin{equation} \\begin{aligned} C_w\\leq C\\leq C_b,&\\qquad C=C_b-k_Bs&&\\text{in a black collar near }B_b,\\\\ &\\qquad C=C_w+k_Bs&&\\text{in a white collar near }B_w. \\end{aligned} \\end{equation} For an absent type, its corresponding constant is simply a bound. Set \\begin{equation} b_-=c_b-C_b<0, \\qquad b_+=-c_w-C_w>0. \\end{equation} There is a smooth positive function $\\rho$, equal to a positive constant times $r^{-4}$ on the far end and satisfying symbol derivative bounds there, such that, for every $h\\in[b_-,b_+]$, \\begin{equation} |(h+C)\\tr K|+|\\nabla C|+\\rho \\leq\\tfrac12\\mathfrak m \\quad\\hbox{everywhere on }\\Omega. \\end{equation} The thresholds, collars, offset, and $\\rho$ are fixed before the deformation parameters are chosen. After fixing the thresholds and collars, the offset and its first derivatives can be made arbitrarily small.","sourceOffset":94027,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Exterior and enclosing area","label":"n3:intro:exterior-class","text":"An exterior is a connected orientable smooth three-manifold \\(\\Omega\\) with nonempty compact smooth boundary, complete as a metric space with its boundary included, and with exactly one end, which is asymptotically flat. An enclosing cut is a compact smooth embedded surface in \\(\\overline\\Omega\\) separating the entire inner boundary from the sufficiently distant part of that end. Equivalently, it bounds the side containing the distant end; the other side is filled up to the inner obstacle. A cut may have several components and may coincide with the inner boundary. Its normal points toward the ","sourceOffset":17475,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"n3:domains:support-definition","text":"Let $D$ be closed in a smooth three-manifold and let $x\\in\\partial D$. A smooth exterior support is a smooth function $\\phi$ near $x$ with $\\phi(x)=0$, $\\dd\\phi(x)\\ne0$, and $D\\subset\\{\\phi\\leq0\\}$ locally near $x$. Its normal is $n=\\nabla\\phi/|\\nabla\\phi|$. Define \\begin{equation} \\mathcal E_Q[\\phi](x) =\\frac{(g^{ij}-n^in^j)\\nabla_i\\nabla_j\\phi}{|\\nabla\\phi|} +\\tr Q-Q(n,n). \\end{equation} We say $D$ has exterior-support expansion at most $c$ if every such support at every point of its frontier has $\\mathcal E_Q[\\phi]\\leq c$.","sourceOffset":82174,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/260.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":988644,"sourceFileCount":47,"statementCount":64,"definitionCount":9,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Spacetime-Penrose-inequalities-enclosing-area-charge-and-rigidity-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"260-04","slug":"The-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-Inequality-for-Axisymmetric-Electrovacuum-Exteriors-October-5-2026","familyId":"260","title":"The Kerr–Newman Penrose Inequality for Axisymmetric Electrovacuum Exteriors","abstract":"We prove the sharp Kerr–Newman Penrose inequality $\\displaystyle m^2\\ge \\frac{A}{16\\pi}+\\frac{Q^2}{2} +\\frac{\\pi(Q^4+4J^2)}{A}.$ Here $Q^2=Q_e^2+Q_b^2$, and J is the conserved total angular momentum, including its electromagnetic contribution. The result applies to smooth axisymmetric electrovacuum exteriors in the stated one-ended topological and decay class, with a connected outermost, outer-area-minimizing future marginally outer trapped boundary, Coulomb electromagnetic asymptotics, zero ADM momentum, and the explicitly assumed physical-area condition $A\\ge4\\pi\\sqrt{Q^4+4J^2}$. No maximality assumption is made. On the strict area branch, equality within this class characterizes the original data as an admissible spacelike exterior slice of a subextremal dyonic Kerr–Newman spacetime, with the boundary mapped smoothly to a future-horizon cross-section or the bifurcation sphere.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sharp enclosing-area spacetime Penrose inequality for smooth one-ended asymptotically flat initial data in every spatial dimension n ≥ 3, under dominant energy, weak future trapping, positive enclosing area, and the stated decay assumptions. It bounds invariant ADM mass below using minimum enclosing area, with equality rigidity under additional horizon hypotheses. Charged upper-area bounds treat dyonic three-dimensional data; the higher-dimensional purely electric extension uses the matched neutral theorem.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-Inequality-for-Axisymmetric-Electrovacuum-Exteriors-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-Inequality-for-Axisymmetric-Electrovacuum-Exteriors-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-Inequality-for-Axisymmetric-Electrovacuum-Exteriors-October-5-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/The-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-Inequality-for-Axisymmetric-Electrovacuum-Exteriors-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-Inequality-for-Axisymmetric-Electrovacuum-Exteriors-October-5-2026/build/sections/history.tex","preprints/The-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-Inequality-for-Axisymmetric-Electrovacuum-Exteriors-October-5-2026/build/sections/axial.tex","preprints/The-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-Inequality-for-Axisymmetric-Electrovacuum-Exteriors-October-5-2026/build/sections/cut.tex","preprints/The-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-Inequality-for-Axisymmetric-Electrovacuum-Exteriors-October-5-2026/build/sections/comparison.tex","preprints/The-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-Inequality-for-Axisymmetric-Electrovacuum-Exteriors-October-5-2026/build/sections/reduction.tex","preprints/The-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-Inequality-for-Axisymmetric-Electrovacuum-Exteriors-October-5-2026/build/sections/deformation.tex","preprints/The-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-Inequality-for-Axisymmetric-Electrovacuum-Exteriors-October-5-2026/build/sections/ceiling.tex","preprints/The-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-Inequality-for-Axisymmetric-Electrovacuum-Exteriors-October-5-2026/build/sections/regularity.tex","preprints/The-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-Inequality-for-Axisymmetric-Electrovacuum-Exteriors-October-5-2026/build/sections/solve.tex","preprints/The-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-Inequality-for-Axisymmetric-Electrovacuum-Exteriors-October-5-2026/build/sections/adjoint.tex","preprints/The-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-Inequality-for-Axisymmetric-Electrovacuum-Exteriors-October-5-2026/build/sections/rigidity.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-Inequality-for-Axisymmetric-Electrovacuum-Exteriors-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the sharp Kerr--Newman Penrose inequality \\[ m^2\\ge \\frac{A}{16\\pi}+\\frac{Q^2}{2} +\\frac{\\pi(Q^4+4J^2)}{A}. \\] Here \\(Q^2=Q_e^2+Q_b^2\\), and \\(J\\) is the conserved total angular momentum, including its electromagnetic contribution. The result applies to smooth axisymmetric electrovacuum exteriors in the stated one-ended topological and decay class, with a connected outermost, outer-area-minimizing future marginally outer trapped boundary, Coulomb electromagnetic asymptotics, zero ADM momentum, and the explicitly assumed physical-area condition \\(A\\ge4\\pi\\sqrt{Q^4+4J^2}\\). No maximality assumption is made. On the strict area branch, equality within this class characterizes the original data as an admissible spacelike exterior slice of a subextremal dyonic Kerr--Newman spacetime, with the boundary mapped smoothly to a future-horizon cross-section or the bifurcation sphere.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":945},{"kind":"section","title":"Geometric setting and main theorem","sourceOffset":6011},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exterior data and constraints","sourceOffset":6224},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Asymptotics and conserved quantities","sourceOffset":8111},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The horizon and enclosing area","sourceOffset":11487},{"kind":"section","title":"Axial variables and the comparison family","sourceOffset":14855},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The orbit space and its smooth angular coordinate","sourceOffset":15204},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The conserved potentials and their axis behavior","sourceOffset":19460},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Polarization and its exact source terms","sourceOffset":28739},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The spacetime quotient and local reconstruction","sourceOffset":33967},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Target geometry","sourceOffset":40425},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Kerr--Newman comparison maps","sourceOffset":41751}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Penrose inequality and nondegenerate rigidity","label":"m:main","text":"Under the hypotheses of this section, \\begin{equation} \\boxed{\\displaystyle m^2\\ge\\frac{A}{16\\pi}+\\frac{Q^2}{2} +\\frac{\\pi(Q^4+4J^2)}A.} \\end{equation} If \\(A>4\\pi\\sqrt{Q^4+4J^2}\\) and equality holds, the original exterior \\((\\Omega,g,K,E_{\\mathrm f},B_{\\mathrm f})\\) admits a smooth spacelike embedding into the domain of outer communication together with the appropriate future horizon of a subextremal dyonic \\KN\\ spacetime with mass \\(m\\), angular momentum \\(J\\), and charges \\(Q_e,Q_b\\), in the conventions above. The induced metric, second fundamental form with a consistent future normal, and induced fields agree with the given data. The end approaches the corresponding spatial infinity. The embedding and normal extend smoothly to \\(S\\), which maps to a smooth future-horizon cross-section or to the bifurcation sphere. Conversely, every such \\KN\\ exterior slice satisfying all the hypotheses and meeting that horizon attains equality. Nonconstant time graphs and nonzero second fundamental forms are permitted.","sourceOffset":13090,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Polarization","label":"a:polarization","text":"The data $(g_0,k_0)$ extend smoothly through the axis, have the stated asymptotic decay, and have the same ADM energy as $(g,K)$. For every invariant surface, its area and future expansion are unchanged. Let $\\mu_0,J_0$ be the constraint densities of $(g_0,k_0)$, with $J_0$ a covector. Write $\\dd\\mathcal A=f_a\\dd A_h$ and set $s=(-B_e,E_e,-Xf_a/2)$. Then \\begin{equation} 16\\pi\\mu_0=2\\big(|\\mathcal D_X|_{g_0}^2+|s|^2\\big), \\qquad 8\\pi J_0=2s\\cdot\\mathcal D_X. \\end{equation} The source combinations on the right are smooth across the axis. On a signed axis tube $s_1,s_2$ are odd of order $y$, and $s_3$ is even of order $y^2$. In particular the polarized data satisfy the dominant energy condition.","sourceOffset":29053,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Enclosing cut","label":"m:cut","text":"An enclosing cut \\(\\Gamma\\) is the full compact intrinsic boundary of a connected smooth codimension-zero submanifold \\(D\\subset\\Omega\\), closed as a subset of \\(\\Omega\\), whose interior lies in \\(\\operatorname{int}\\Omega\\) and which contains the entire sufficiently distant end. The boundary is smooth, embedded and two-sided. All its components are counted, including any portion coinciding with \\(S\\). No symmetry or trapping condition is imposed on \\(\\Gamma\\).","sourceOffset":12057,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/260.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":307259,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":18,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-Inequality-for-Axisymmetric-Electrovacuum-Exteriors-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"260-05","slug":"Electromagnetic-tails-and-the-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-inequality-October-5-2026","familyId":"260","title":"Electromagnetic tails and the Kerr–Newman Penrose inequality","abstract":"We construct smooth axisymmetric electrovacuum exteriors that violate the Kerr–Newman Penrose inequality when J is the bare gravitational ADM angular momentum and the electromagnetic fields have only $O(r^{-2})$ decay, allowing angularly varying leading tails. The examples have zero total electric and magnetic charge even though both electromagnetic fields are nonzero. They have a connected outermost, outer-area-minimizing future marginally outer trapped boundary and lie strictly on the physical area branch. We also obtain equality examples whose original data admit no Kerr–Newman spacelike realization with the corresponding mass, angular momentum, and charges. These counterexamples do not refute formulations using conserved total angular momentum with its electromagnetic correction or stronger Coulomb asymptotics.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sharp enclosing-area spacetime Penrose inequality for smooth one-ended asymptotically flat initial data in every spatial dimension n ≥ 3, under dominant energy, weak future trapping, positive enclosing area, and the stated decay assumptions. It bounds invariant ADM mass below using minimum enclosing area, with equality rigidity under additional horizon hypotheses. Charged upper-area bounds treat dyonic three-dimensional data; the higher-dimensional purely electric extension uses the matched neutral theorem.","pdfPath":"preprints/Electromagnetic-tails-and-the-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-inequality-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Electromagnetic-tails-and-the-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-inequality-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Electromagnetic-tails-and-the-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-inequality-October-5-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Electromagnetic-tails-and-the-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-inequality-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Electromagnetic-tails-and-the-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-inequality-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Electromagnetic-tails-and-the-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-inequality-October-5-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Electromagnetic-tails-and-the-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-inequality-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-construction.tex","preprints/Electromagnetic-tails-and-the-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-inequality-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-elliptic.tex","preprints/Electromagnetic-tails-and-the-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-inequality-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-geometry.tex","preprints/Electromagnetic-tails-and-the-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-inequality-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-counterexamples.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Electromagnetic-tails-and-the-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-inequality-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct smooth axisymmetric electrovacuum exteriors that violate the Kerr--Newman Penrose inequality when \\(J\\) is the bare gravitational ADM angular momentum and the electromagnetic fields have only \\(O(r^{-2})\\) decay, allowing angularly varying leading tails. The examples have zero total electric and magnetic charge even though both electromagnetic fields are nonzero. They have a connected outermost, outer-area-minimizing future marginally outer trapped boundary and lie strictly on the physical area branch. We also obtain equality examples whose original data admit no Kerr--Newman spacelike realization with the corresponding mass, angular momentum, and charges. These counterexamples do not refute formulations using conserved total angular momentum with its electromagnetic correction or stronger Coulomb asymptotics.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":880},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and the class of exteriors","sourceOffset":3153},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Main result","sourceOffset":7677},{"kind":"section","title":"Explicit electrovacuum seeds","sourceOffset":10647},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reduction to a scalar equation","sourceOffset":16773},{"kind":"section","title":"The conformal factor","sourceOffset":20990},{"kind":"section","title":"The horizon and all enclosing surfaces","sourceOffset":33163},{"kind":"section","title":"Asymptotic quantities and the counterexamples","sourceOffset":42896},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Mass, momentum, and charges","sourceOffset":43312},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The conserved electromagnetic correction","sourceOffset":47037},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Strict violation and nondegenerate equality","sourceOffset":49874}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"In the class of smooth axisymmetric source-free electrovacuum exteriors specified in \\Cref{sec:conventions}, both of the following occur. \\begin{enumerate} \\item There are data with \\[ Q_e=Q_b=0,\\qquad A>8\\pi\\abs J, \\qquad m^2<\\frac{A}{16\\pi}+\\frac{4\\pi J^2}{A}. \\] \\item There are data with \\[ Q_e=Q_b=0,\\qquad A>8\\pi\\abs J, \\qquad m^2=\\frac{A}{16\\pi}+\\frac{4\\pi J^2}{A}, \\] for which no smooth spacelike embedding into the Kerr--Newman spacetime with parameters \\((m,J,Q_e,Q_b)\\) induces the given metric, second fundamental form, and electromagnetic fields. \\end{enumerate} All these examples are maximal, \\(\\tr_gK=0\\), on \\(\\Omega=\\{x\\in\\R^3:\\abs{x}\\ge1\\}\\), with connected boundary \\(S=\\{r=1\\}\\). Both electromagnetic fields are nonzero.","sourceOffset":7726,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Uniform conformal factor","label":"prop:conformal-factor","text":"There is $L_0>4$, depending only on the fixed cutoff, such that for every $L\\ge L_0$ and $s\\in[0,1]$, Equation \\eqref{eq:scalar} has a smooth axisymmetric solution $u=u_0+v$ on $\\Omega$, where $u_0=1+r^{-1}$, satisfying \\begin{equation} u_r+\\tfrac12u=0\\quad\\text{on }S, \\qquad u\\longrightarrow1\\quad\\text{as }r\\longrightarrow\\infty. \\end{equation} It is unique among smooth solutions with $v\\ge0$ and $v=O(r^{-1})$. One may take a constant $M=3$, independent of $s,L$, such that $1\\le u_0\\le u\\le M$. Uniformly in these parameters, \\begin{equation} \\begin{aligned} |v|+r|v_r|&\\le Cs^2L^{-2} &&(r\\ge1),\\\\ |v_{rr}|&\\le Cs^2L^{-2} &&(1\\le r\\le2). \\end{aligned} \\end{equation} For every integer $k\\ge0$, all Cartesian derivatives satisfy \\begin{equation} |D^kv|\\le C_k\\frac{s^2}{L}r^{-1-k}, \\qquad |D^k(u-1)|\\le C_k r^{-1-k}. \\end{equation} For fixed $L$, $s\\mapsto v_s$ is continuous in the sup norm, as are \\[ s\\longmapsto\\int_{\\R^3}H_s\\,\\dd x, \\qquad s\\longmapsto N_s(0)=\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_{\\R^3}\\frac{H_s(x)}{|x|}\\,\\dd x, \\] where $H_s=pu_s^{-7}+\\ell u_s^{-3}$ is extended by zero inside the unit ball and $N_s$ is its Newton potential. Moreover, \\begin{equation} 0\\le\\int_{\\R^3}H_s\\,\\dd x\\le C\\frac{s^2}{L}, \\qquad 0\\le N_s(0)\\le C\\frac{s^2}{L^2}. \\end{equation} At $s=0$ the solution is exactly $u_0$.","sourceOffset":21252,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/260.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":53330,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Electromagnetic-tails-and-the-Kerr-Newman-Penrose-inequality-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"260-06","slug":"The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026","familyId":"260","title":"The nonmaximal anti-de Sitter Penrose Inequality and original-data rigidity","abstract":"We prove the spacetime Penrose Inequality for smooth three-dimensional initial-data exteriors with one spherical asymptotically anti-de Sitter end, satisfying the stated decay and integrability assumptions, the dominant energy condition, and a compact weakly future outer trapped boundary. We assume that the hyperbolic metric four-flux is future timelike; its Lorentz norm is the mass, and the area is the infimum over full enclosing cuts. On the connected outermost, outer area-minimizing horizon subclass, equality characterizes the original data as a spacelike hypersurface in Schwarzschild–anti-de Sitter spacetime with matching metric mass. No maximality, evolution, or auxiliary solvability assumption is used.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sharp enclosing-area spacetime Penrose inequality for smooth one-ended asymptotically flat initial data in every spatial dimension n ≥ 3, under dominant energy, weak future trapping, positive enclosing area, and the stated decay assumptions. It bounds invariant ADM mass below using minimum enclosing area, with equality rigidity under additional horizon hypotheses. Charged upper-area bounds treat dyonic three-dimensional data; the higher-dimensional purely electric extension uses the matched neutral theorem.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-history.tex","preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/figures/proof-architecture.tex","preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-setup.tex","preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-stress-bending.tex","preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-flat-system-floor.tex","preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-flat-algebra-fragment.tex","preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-rank-one-regularity.tex","preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-flat-existence.tex","preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-ah-deformation.tex","preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/07a-ah-flux-algebra.tex","preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/08-equality-adjoint.tex","preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/09-static-reduction.tex","preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/10-static-compactification.tex","preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/11-static-rigidity.tex","preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/12-original-embedding.tex","preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/sections/13-linear-estimates.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the spacetime Penrose Inequality for smooth three-dimensional initial-data exteriors with one spherical asymptotically anti-de Sitter end, satisfying the stated decay and integrability assumptions, the dominant energy condition, and a compact weakly future outer trapped boundary. We assume that the hyperbolic metric four-flux is future timelike; its Lorentz norm is the mass, and the area is the infimum over full enclosing cuts. On the connected outermost, outer area-minimizing horizon subclass, equality characterizes the original data as a spacelike hypersurface in Schwarzschild--anti-de Sitter spacetime with matching metric mass. No maximality, evolution, or auxiliary solvability assumption is used.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":781},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and scope","sourceOffset":830},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Significance and technical contributions","sourceOffset":4120},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The results","sourceOffset":6813},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof architecture","sourceOffset":8962},{"kind":"section","title":"Data, enclosing cuts, and the metric mass","sourceOffset":16220},{"kind":"section","title":"A saturating conserved stress and reduction to a flat end","sourceOffset":27130},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conformal preparation with preservation of the component mass","sourceOffset":29385},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Full-cut comparison under addition of a short collar","sourceOffset":39051},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The nonlinear Dirichlet problem","sourceOffset":40863},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The conformal boundary and its expansion","sourceOffset":55247},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform estimates when the finite-boundary heights grow","sourceOffset":63176}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Nonmaximal anti-de Sitter Penrose Inequality","label":"thm:main-numerical","text":"Let $(\\Omega,g,K)$ satisfy Definition \\ref{def:setup-data}, the same future boundary condition $H+\\tr_{TS}K\\le0$ on every component, and the future-timelike metric mass condition in Definition \\ref{def:setup-mass}. Then \\begin{equation} m_{\\AH}(g)\\ge \\sqrt{\\frac{A_{\\min}(S)}{16\\pi}} \\left(1+\\frac{A_{\\min}(S)}{4\\pi}\\right) =\\frac{r_A+r_A^3}{2}. \\end{equation} The coefficient is sharp. No maximality assumption, restriction on the compact topology or boundary genera, or outermostness hypothesis is required.","sourceOffset":7053,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Original-data equality","label":"thm:main-rigidity","text":"Assume in addition the connected horizon hypotheses of Definition \\ref{def:setup-horizon}. Equality in Equation \\ref{eq:main-penrose} holds if and only if the original pair $(g,K)$ is realized by a smooth proper spacelike embedding of $\\Omega$, with boundary, into the smooth maximally extended Schwarzschild--anti-de Sitter spacetime of parameter exactly $M=m_{\\AH}(g)$. Its interior lies in one black-hole exterior; its boundary maps diffeomorphically onto a full smooth spacelike cross-section of that exterior's future event horizon, with the bifurcation sphere also allowed; and its unique end reaches that exterior's conformal infinity. The embedding induces both $g$ and $K$ with the future-normal convention of Equation \\ref{eq:setup-expansion}.","sourceOffset":7693,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Initial-data exterior","label":"def:setup-data","text":"An initial-data exterior is a connected orientable three-manifold $\\Omega$ with nonempty compact smooth boundary $S$, a Riemannian metric $g$, and a symmetric covariant tensor $K$. The metric space $(\\Omega,g)$, with its boundary included, is complete and has exactly one end. Outside a compact set there is a chart $(r_0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb S^2$ with \\begin{equation} b=\\frac{dr^2}{1+r^2}+r^2\\sigma, \\qquad \\eta=\\operatorname{arsinh}r, \\end{equation} where $\\sigma$ is the unit round metric. For some $0<\\alpha<1$ and $3/2<\\tau<3$, assume \\begin{equation} g-b\\in C^{2,\\alpha}_\\tau, \\qquad K\\in C^{1","sourceOffset":16505,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Full enclosing cuts","label":"def:setup-cut","text":"An admissible exterior region is a connected smooth codimension-zero submanifold with boundary $D\\subset\\Omega$, closed in $\\Omega$, whose interior lies in $\\operatorname{int}\\Omega$ and which contains the entire sufficiently distant end. An admissible cut is its full compact intrinsic boundary $\\Gamma=\\partial D$, assumed smoothly embedded and two-sided. Coincidence with $S$, or with some of its components, is allowed. Define \\begin{equation} A_{\\min}(S;g)=\\inf_{\\Gamma}\\Area_g(\\Gamma), \\qquad r_A=\\sqrt{\\frac{A_{\\min}(S;g)}{4\\pi}}. \\end{equation} We omit $g$ when it is fixed. The competitor $D","sourceOffset":18542,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/260.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":503986,"sourceFileCount":19,"statementCount":36,"definitionCount":5,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-nonmaximal-anti-de-Sitter-Penrose-Inequality-and-original-data-rigidity-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"260-07","slug":"The-Penrose-inequality-for-maximal-asymptotically-hyperbolic-initial-data-October-5-2026","familyId":"260","title":"The Penrose inequality for maximal asymptotically hyperbolic initial data","abstract":"We prove the sharp Penrose inequality for three-dimensional maximal asymptotically hyperbolic initial data with spherical conformal infinity. Under the dominant energy condition, the stated decay and integrability assumptions, and a future-timelike mass covector, the invariant mass is bounded below by the Schwarzschild–anti-de Sitter mass associated with the minimum enclosing area of a weakly future outer-trapped boundary. The boundary may be disconnected, and no restriction is imposed on the compact topology.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sharp enclosing-area spacetime Penrose inequality for smooth one-ended asymptotically flat initial data in every spatial dimension n ≥ 3, under dominant energy, weak future trapping, positive enclosing area, and the stated decay assumptions. It bounds invariant ADM mass below using minimum enclosing area, with equality rigidity under additional horizon hypotheses. Charged upper-area bounds treat dyonic three-dimensional data; the higher-dimensional purely electric extension uses the matched neutral theorem.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Penrose-inequality-for-maximal-asymptotically-hyperbolic-initial-data-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Penrose-inequality-for-maximal-asymptotically-hyperbolic-initial-data-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Penrose-inequality-for-maximal-asymptotically-hyperbolic-initial-data-October-5-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/The-Penrose-inequality-for-maximal-asymptotically-hyperbolic-initial-data-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Penrose-inequality-for-maximal-asymptotically-hyperbolic-initial-data-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-Penrose-inequality-for-maximal-asymptotically-hyperbolic-initial-data-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-Penrose-inequality-for-maximal-asymptotically-hyperbolic-initial-data-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-preliminaries.tex","preprints/The-Penrose-inequality-for-maximal-asymptotically-hyperbolic-initial-data-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-graph-calculus.tex","preprints/The-Penrose-inequality-for-maximal-asymptotically-hyperbolic-initial-data-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-area-transfer.tex","preprints/The-Penrose-inequality-for-maximal-asymptotically-hyperbolic-initial-data-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-maximal-reduction.tex","preprints/The-Penrose-inequality-for-maximal-asymptotically-hyperbolic-initial-data-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-stress-extension.tex","preprints/The-Penrose-inequality-for-maximal-asymptotically-hyperbolic-initial-data-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-compact-construction.tex","preprints/The-Penrose-inequality-for-maximal-asymptotically-hyperbolic-initial-data-October-5-2026/build/sections/08-transmission-analysis.tex","preprints/The-Penrose-inequality-for-maximal-asymptotically-hyperbolic-initial-data-October-5-2026/build/sections/09-scalar-error.tex","preprints/The-Penrose-inequality-for-maximal-asymptotically-hyperbolic-initial-data-October-5-2026/build/sections/10-completion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Penrose-inequality-for-maximal-asymptotically-hyperbolic-initial-data-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the sharp Penrose inequality for three-dimensional maximal asymptotically hyperbolic initial data with spherical conformal infinity. Under the dominant energy condition, the stated decay and integrability assumptions, and a future-timelike mass covector, the invariant mass is bounded below by the Schwarzschild--anti-de Sitter mass associated with the minimum enclosing area of a weakly future outer-trapped boundary. The boundary may be disconnected, and no restriction is imposed on the compact topology.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction and statement of the result","sourceOffset":579},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Geometry, decay, and mass","sourceOffset":2613},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Constraints, boundary, and enclosing area","sourceOffset":4987},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Structure and new ingredients","sourceOffset":8294},{"kind":"section","title":"Preliminaries","sourceOffset":11609},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compact cores and auxiliary caps","sourceOffset":11660},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Mass covariance and small metric changes","sourceOffset":15848},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The AF comparison theorem","sourceOffset":18411},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local scalar elliptic estimates","sourceOffset":19558},{"kind":"section","title":"Static graph identities and differential estimates","sourceOffset":24620},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Curvature and the conserved current","sourceOffset":27101},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Boundary identities","sourceOffset":35659}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Global maximal AH Penrose inequality","label":"thm:main","text":"Under \\eqref{eq:decay}--\\eqref{eq:future-trapping}, the completeness, one-end, and smoothness assumptions above, and the future-timelike condition \\eqref{eq:invariant-mass}, one has \\begin{equation} m_{\\AH}(g)\\ge\\frac12\\bigl(r_A+r_A^3\\bigr), \\qquad r_A=\\sqrt{\\frac{A_{\\min}(S;g)}{4\\pi}}. \\end{equation} The coefficient is sharp: equality holds for every positive-mass time-symmetric Schwarzschild--anti-de Sitter exterior bounded by its minimal horizon.","sourceOffset":7042,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Time-symmetric time-component inequality","label":"thm:ts","text":"Let $(\\Omega,g)$ have the smoothness, topology, completeness, and end structure above. Suppose $g-b\\in C^{2,\\alpha}_\\tau$, $3/2<\\tau<3$, the four fluxes \\eqref{eq:ah-mass} are finite, and \\[ R_g\\ge-6,\\qquad H_g(S)\\le0, \\qquad \\int_{\\mathrm{end}}V_0(R_g+6)\\dd V_g<\\infty. \\] In every such fixed end chart, \\begin{equation} p_0(g)\\ge\\frac12(a+a^3), \\qquad a=\\sqrt{A_{\\min}(S;g)/(4\\pi)}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":7779,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Minimum enclosing area","label":"def:minimum-area","text":"An admissible end-side domain is a connected smooth codimension-zero submanifold-with-boundary $D\\subset\\Omega$, closed as a subset of $\\Omega$, such that \\[ \\operatorname{int}D\\subset\\operatorname{int}\\Omega \\quad\\hbox{and}\\quad \\{r>R\\}\\subset D\\quad\\hbox{for some }R. \\] Its entire intrinsic manifold boundary $\\partial D$ must be compact, smooth, embedded, and two-sided, with any portions coinciding with $S$ included. Define \\begin{equation} A_{\\min}(S;g)=\\inf_D\\Area_g(\\partial D). \\end{equation} In particular $D=\\Omega$ is admissible and contributes $\\partial D=S$. An empty relative frontier","sourceOffset":6101,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/260.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":322152,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Penrose-inequality-for-maximal-asymptotically-hyperbolic-initial-data-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/The-Penrose-inequality-for-maximal-asymptotically-hyperbolic-initial-data-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/The-Penrose-inequality-for-maximal-asymptotically-hyperbolic-initial-data-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"260-08","slug":"A-local-Penrose-inequality-for-conformal-perturbations-of-Schwarzschild-anti-de-Sitter-data-October-5-2026","familyId":"260","title":"A local Penrose inequality for conformal perturbations of Schwarzschild–anti-de Sitter data","abstract":"We prove the asymptotically hyperbolic Penrose inequality for sufficiently small maximal vacuum conformal perturbations of a positive-mass Schwarzschild–anti-de Sitter exterior, for every fixed decaying transverse-traceless seed and every solution branch satisfying the stated decay and mass assumptions. The bound uses the area of the marginally outer trapped boundary itself; it requires neither outermostness nor outer area-minimization, and no bulk-versus-boundary domination condition. For sufficiently small positive parameters, equality holds for radial seeds and the inequality is strict otherwise.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sharp enclosing-area spacetime Penrose inequality for smooth one-ended asymptotically flat initial data in every spatial dimension n ≥ 3, under dominant energy, weak future trapping, positive enclosing area, and the stated decay assumptions. It bounds invariant ADM mass below using minimum enclosing area, with equality rigidity under additional horizon hypotheses. Charged upper-area bounds treat dyonic three-dimensional data; the higher-dimensional purely electric extension uses the matched neutral theorem.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-local-Penrose-inequality-for-conformal-perturbations-of-Schwarzschild-anti-de-Sitter-data-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-local-Penrose-inequality-for-conformal-perturbations-of-Schwarzschild-anti-de-Sitter-data-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-local-Penrose-inequality-for-conformal-perturbations-of-Schwarzschild-anti-de-Sitter-data-October-5-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/A-local-Penrose-inequality-for-conformal-perturbations-of-Schwarzschild-anti-de-Sitter-data-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-local-Penrose-inequality-for-conformal-perturbations-of-Schwarzschild-anti-de-Sitter-data-October-5-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/A-local-Penrose-inequality-for-conformal-perturbations-of-Schwarzschild-anti-de-Sitter-data-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-setting.tex","preprints/A-local-Penrose-inequality-for-conformal-perturbations-of-Schwarzschild-anti-de-Sitter-data-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-analysis.tex","preprints/A-local-Penrose-inequality-for-conformal-perturbations-of-Schwarzschild-anti-de-Sitter-data-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-cmc.tex","preprints/A-local-Penrose-inequality-for-conformal-perturbations-of-Schwarzschild-anti-de-Sitter-data-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-quadratic.tex","preprints/A-local-Penrose-inequality-for-conformal-perturbations-of-Schwarzschild-anti-de-Sitter-data-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-quartic.tex","preprints/A-local-Penrose-inequality-for-conformal-perturbations-of-Schwarzschild-anti-de-Sitter-data-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-local-Penrose-inequality-for-conformal-perturbations-of-Schwarzschild-anti-de-Sitter-data-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the asymptotically hyperbolic Penrose inequality for sufficiently small maximal vacuum conformal perturbations of a positive-mass Schwarzschild--anti-de Sitter exterior, for every fixed decaying transverse-traceless seed and every solution branch satisfying the stated decay and mass assumptions. The bound uses the area of the marginally outer trapped boundary itself; it requires neither outermostness nor outer area-minimization, and no bulk-versus-boundary domination condition. For sufficiently small positive parameters, equality holds for radial seeds and the inequality is strict otherwise.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":681},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The mechanism of the proof","sourceOffset":3902},{"kind":"section","title":"Geometric setting and the theorem","sourceOffset":6762},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Mass and boundary conventions","sourceOffset":9029},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Statement","sourceOffset":11104},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Static operators and Taylor notation","sourceOffset":13096},{"kind":"section","title":"Weighted coefficients and actual expansions","sourceOffset":16277},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A scalar inverse and its asymptotics","sourceOffset":16702},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coefficient equations and the required spatial derivatives","sourceOffset":21623},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An exact Green formula for the mass","sourceOffset":26517},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Approximation on the full space of seeds","sourceOffset":30083},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Actual expansions and mass coefficients","sourceOffset":33799}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every background, fixed TT seed, and solution branch satisfying \\eqref{eq:background}--\\eqref{eq:branch} and the mass assumptions above, there is $0<\\eps_0\\leq\\eps_*$ such that \\[ m_{\\AH}(\\eps)\\geq \\sqrt{\\frac{A_\\eps}{16\\pi}}\\left(1+\\frac{A_\\eps}{4\\pi}\\right) \\qquad(0\\leq\\eps<\\eps_0). \\] For all sufficiently small positive $\\eps$, equality holds if the seed is radial, and the inequality is strict otherwise.","sourceOffset":11395,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:penrose","text":"In addition to the hypotheses of Theorem \\ref{thm:main}, suppose $q_{ss}\\geq0$ on $S$. Assume, for every sufficiently small positive $\\eps$, that no compact smooth embedded two-sided surface in the interior enclosing $S$ has $\\theta_+\\leq0$ everywhere, and that every smooth enclosing surface has area at least $A_\\eps$. Disconnected enclosing competitors are allowed, and $S$ itself is allowed in the area infimum. Then the exact local Penrose inequality holds with $A_{\\min}(S)=A_\\eps$.","sourceOffset":12294,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/260.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":122768,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-local-Penrose-inequality-for-conformal-perturbations-of-Schwarzschild-anti-de-Sitter-data-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"260-09","slug":"The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-and-enclosing-area-September-27-2026","familyId":"260","title":"The spacetime Penrose inequality and enclosing area","abstract":"We prove the sharp spacetime Penrose inequality for smooth one-ended initial-data exteriors in every spatial dimension n ≥ 3, under the dominant energy condition, weak future trapping, the stated differentiated decay and positive enclosing area. These hypotheses force the ADM energy-momentum to be future timelike. Area is the infimum over full enclosing cuts in the original metric. In dimensions three and four, two metric derivatives and one tensor derivative suffice, and both positive enclosing area and future timelikeness follow from the hypotheses.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sharp enclosing-area spacetime Penrose inequality for smooth one-ended asymptotically flat initial data in every spatial dimension n ≥ 3, under dominant energy, weak future trapping, positive enclosing area, and the stated decay assumptions. It bounds invariant ADM mass below using minimum enclosing area, with equality rigidity under additional horizon hypotheses. Charged upper-area bounds treat dyonic three-dimensional data; the higher-dimensional purely electric extension uses the matched neutral theorem.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-and-enclosing-area-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-and-enclosing-area-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-and-enclosing-area-September-27-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-and-enclosing-area-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-and-enclosing-area-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-and-enclosing-area-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-and-enclosing-area-September-27-2026/build/sections/prepared-comparison.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-and-enclosing-area-September-27-2026/build/sections/end-preparation.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-and-enclosing-area-September-27-2026/build/sections/scalar-identity.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-and-enclosing-area-September-27-2026/build/sections/elliptic.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-and-enclosing-area-September-27-2026/build/sections/height-enclosure.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-and-enclosing-area-September-27-2026/build/figures/two-densities.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-and-enclosing-area-September-27-2026/build/sections/weak3.tex","preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-and-enclosing-area-September-27-2026/build/sections/weak4.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-and-enclosing-area-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the sharp spacetime Penrose inequality for smooth one-ended initial-data exteriors in every spatial dimension $n\\ge3$, under the dominant energy condition, weak future trapping, the stated differentiated decay and positive enclosing area. These hypotheses force the ADM energy-momentum to be future timelike. Area is the infimum over full enclosing cuts in the original metric. In dimensions three and four, two metric derivatives and one tensor derivative suffice, and both positive enclosing area and future timelikeness follow from the hypotheses.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The inequality and its geometric quantity","sourceOffset":622},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Data, area and statements","sourceOffset":2054},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From time symmetry to spacetime data","sourceOffset":7845},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the proof controls curvature, mass and area","sourceOffset":13841},{"kind":"section","title":"The prepared geometric comparison","sourceOffset":19413},{"kind":"section","title":"Preparing an end with zero momentum","sourceOffset":29498},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conformal changes and a vacuum model with matching charges","sourceOffset":32496},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Joining the original and model ends","sourceOffset":37682},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Changing the vacuum slice and repairing the constraints","sourceOffset":48948},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparing every full enclosing cut","sourceOffset":54900},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A strict scalar perturbation","sourceOffset":57214},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A positive shell with fixed momentum","sourceOffset":69818}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"The enclosing-area Penrose inequality","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $(\\Omega^n,g,K)$ be the smooth one-ended exterior specified above, with integrable constraint densities, finite ADM limits, dominant energy condition and weakly future trapped boundary. Suppose \\[ g-\\delta=O_6(r^{-q}),\\qquad K=O_5(r^{-1-q}),\\qquad \\frac{n-2}{2}<q<n-2, \\] and assume $E>|P|_\\delta$ and $A_{\\min,g}(S)>0$. Then \\begin{equation} m\\geq\\frac12\\left(\\frac{A_{\\min,g}(S)}{\\omega_{n-1}} \\right)^{\\frac{n-2}{n-1}}. \\end{equation} The coefficient and exponent are sharp in each dimension $n\\geq3$.","sourceOffset":4860,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Automatic future timelikeness in the strong-decay class","label":"cor:automatic-timelikeness","text":"Assume all hypotheses of Theorem \\ref{thm:main} except the inequality $E>|P|_\\delta$. In particular, retain the one-ended exterior, the $O_6/O_5$ decay with $(n-2)/2<q<n-2$, and $A_{\\min,g}(S)>0$. Then $E>|P|_\\delta$, and Equation \\eqref{eq:setting:main} holds.","sourceOffset":5902,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Initial-data exterior","label":"def:data","text":"Let $n\\geq3$ and let $\\Omega$ be a smooth connected orientable $n$-manifold with nonempty compact smooth boundary $S$. The metric $g$ and symmetric covariant two-tensor $K$ are smooth up to $S$, and $g$ is complete with $S$ included. Outside a compact set, $\\Omega$ is a single coordinate end $\\{x\\in\\R^n:|x|>R_0\\}$. Thus there are no additional ends hidden in the compact-complement condition. Write $R_g$ for scalar curvature and $\\nabla$ for the connection of $g$. Write $\\tau=\\tr_gK$. Our constraint normalization is \\begin{equation} 2\\mu=R_g+(\\tr_gK)^2-|K|_g^2,\\qquad J_i=\\nabla^j\\bigl(K_{ij}-(\\","sourceOffset":2093,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Full enclosing cuts","label":"def:fullcuts","text":"A full enclosing cut is the entire intrinsic manifold boundary $\\Gamma=\\partial D$ of a connected smooth codimension-zero submanifold-with-boundary $D\\subset\\Omega$. We require $D$ to be closed in $\\Omega$, its manifold interior to lie in $\\operatorname{int}\\Omega$, and $D$ to contain the whole sufficiently distant end. Its full boundary must be compact, smooth, embedded and two-sided. In particular, $D=\\Omega$ is admissible and has boundary $S$. Define \\begin{equation} A_{\\min,g}(S)=\\inf_D\\Area_g(\\partial D). \\end{equation} The use of intrinsic boundary ensures that coincident portions of $S$","sourceOffset":4051,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/260.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":332410,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":21,"definitionCount":6,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-and-enclosing-area-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/The-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-and-enclosing-area-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"260-10","slug":"Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026","familyId":"260","title":"Conformal flow and the Riemannian Penrose inequality with minimizing frontiers","abstract":"We give a detailed conformal-flow proof of the numerical Riemannian Penrose inequality in every dimension n ≥ 3 for complete asymptotically flat exteriors with nonnegative scalar curvature and full compact frontiers that are outer minimizing and locally perimeter minimizing. The metric extends smoothly through the possibly singular frontier; neither spin nor frontier connectedness is assumed. The proof follows the conformal-flow approach of Bray, Bray–Lee, and Bi–Zhu.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sharp enclosing-area spacetime Penrose inequality for smooth one-ended asymptotically flat initial data in every spatial dimension n ≥ 3, under dominant energy, weak future trapping, positive enclosing area, and the stated decay assumptions. It bounds invariant ADM mass below using minimum enclosing area, with equality rigidity under additional horizon hypotheses. Charged upper-area bounds treat dyonic three-dimensional data; the higher-dimensional purely electric extension uses the matched neutral theorem.","pdfPath":"preprints/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026/build/geometry/geometry.tex","preprints/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026/build/sections/flow.tex","preprints/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026/build/separation/regularity-separation.tex","preprints/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026/build/figures/median-normalization.tex","preprints/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026/build/mass-capacity/mass-capacity.tex","preprints/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026/build/sections/mass-evolution.tex","preprints/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026/build/sections/estimates.tex","preprints/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026/build/sections/completion.tex","preprints/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026/build/sections/limit.tex","preprints/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026/build/geometry/proofs.tex","preprints/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026/build/sections/approximation.tex","preprints/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026/build/sections/weighted-minimizers.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a detailed conformal-flow proof of the numerical Riemannian Penrose inequality in every dimension $n\\ge3$ for complete asymptotically flat exteriors with nonnegative scalar curvature and full compact frontiers that are outer minimizing and locally perimeter minimizing. The metric extends smoothly through the possibly singular frontier; neither spin nor frontier connectedness is assumed. The proof follows the conformal-flow approach of Bray, Bray--Lee, and Bi--Zhu.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The inequality and the proof","sourceOffset":539},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Manifolds, frontiers, and mass","sourceOffset":4968},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Four quantities that control the proof","sourceOffset":9737},{"kind":"section","title":"Full enclosures and smooth obstacles","sourceOffset":14362},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The full area and its minimizing enclosure","sourceOffset":15273},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Regularity at a smooth obstacle","sourceOffset":18827},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Detachment from a strict inner barrier","sourceOffset":20159},{"kind":"section","title":"The initial enclosure, buried boundary, and flow notation","sourceOffset":23297},{"kind":"section","title":"Construction and preserved area of the flow","sourceOffset":27589},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weighted enclosures and their escape potentials","sourceOffset":30089},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The discrete flow and its area loss","sourceOffset":45065},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The limiting minimum area, nesting, and integral evolution","sourceOffset":53797}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Riemannian Penrose inequality with minimizing frontiers","label":"thm:rpi","text":"Let $(E,h)$ be a connected complete $n$-dimensional exterior, including its compact frontier $\\Sigma$ in the enclosing-set sense above. Suppose that $h$ is smooth on $E$ and through $\\Sigma$, and that \\begin{enumerate} \\item $R_h\\ge0$, $R_h\\in L^1(E,dV_h)$, and $R_h=O(r^{-n-\\beta})$ for some $\\beta>0$ on the end; \\item outside a compact set there is exactly one Euclidean coordinate end with $h-\\delta=O_2(r^{-\\tau})$, where $\\tau>d/2$; \\item the full frontier is outer minimizing and locally perimeter minimizing as the boundary of the filled side. Its regular part is a smooth embedded minimal hypersurface, and its singular set has Hausdorff dimension at most $n-8$. \\end{enumerate} Then \\begin{equation} m_{\\rm ADM}(h)\\ge\\frac12 \\left(\\frac{\\mathcal H_h^k(\\Sigma)}{\\omega}\\right)^{d/k}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":7025,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"The inequality for a strict inner barrier","label":"cor:g-strict-barrier-penrose","text":"Let $(X,g)$ be a smooth connected $n$-dimensional exterior, $n\\ge3$, complete including its nonempty compact smooth boundary $B$, with one Euclidean coordinate end and compact end complement. Suppose that \\[ g-\\delta=O_2(r^{-\\tau}),\\qquad \\tau>\\frac{n-2}{2},\\qquad R_g\\ge0,\\quad R_g\\in L^1(X,dV_g),\\quad R_g=O(r^{-n-\\beta})\\quad(\\beta>0). \\] If $H_B<0$ for the normal pointing into $X$, then \\[ m_{\\rm ADM}(g)\\ge\\frac12 \\left(\\frac{a_g(B)}{\\omega}\\right)^{(n-2)/(n-1)}. \\]","sourceOffset":22155,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/260.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":234142,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Conformal-flow-and-the-Riemannian-Penrose-inequality-with-minimizing-frontiers-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"260-11","slug":"Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026","familyId":"260","title":"Equality and rigidity in the spacetime Penrose inequality","abstract":"Equality in the spacetime Penrose inequality identifies the original initial data as a spacelike slice of a Schwarzschild–Tangherlini exterior, smoothly attached to its horizon, under the stated outermostness and decay hypotheses. We prove this in every spatial dimension n ≥ 3 for a strong-decay exterior class and for weaker decay in dimensions three and four. In dimension three the finite-component theorem forces a single spherical horizon and gives qualitative strictness for disconnected boundaries.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sharp enclosing-area spacetime Penrose inequality for smooth one-ended asymptotically flat initial data in every spatial dimension n ≥ 3, under dominant energy, weak future trapping, positive enclosing area, and the stated decay assumptions. It bounds invariant ADM mass below using minimum enclosing area, with equality rigidity under additional horizon hypotheses. Charged upper-area bounds treat dyonic three-dimensional data; the higher-dimensional purely electric extension uses the matched neutral theorem.","pdfPath":"preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/sections/01-setting.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/shared/01-enclosing-geometry.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/sections/02-original-variation.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/shared/localization/smooth-localization.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/static_ad/static-main.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/static_ad/rough_mass/rough-mass.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/static_ad/boosted/boosted-converse.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/three/00-data.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/three/01-equality.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/three/02-strict-direction.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/three/03-original-variation.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/three/04-static-base.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/three/06-global-classification.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/three/05-local-spinor.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/four/setup.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/four/01-enclosures.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/four/03-variation.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/four/04-adjoint.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/four/05-static-base.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/four/06-classification.tex","preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/four/07-recovery.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"Equality in the spacetime Penrose inequality identifies the original initial data as a spacelike slice of a Schwarzschild--Tangherlini exterior, smoothly attached to its horizon, under the stated outermostness and decay hypotheses. We prove this in every spatial dimension $n\\ge3$ for a strong-decay exterior class and for weaker decay in dimensions three and four. In dimension three the finite-component theorem forces a single spherical horizon and gives qualitative strictness for disconnected boundaries.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":572},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The equality statements and their scope","sourceOffset":1715},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why equality must be varied on the original data","sourceOffset":6477},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Mathematical context and attribution","sourceOffset":11166},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reading the proof","sourceOffset":16758},{"kind":"section","title":"Original data and the equality theorems","sourceOffset":18046},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The strict direction used in the variation","sourceOffset":26059},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the dimension-dependent conclusions fit together","sourceOffset":27797},{"kind":"section","title":"Minimizing enclosures and their area envelope","sourceOffset":29285},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Existence, contact regularity, and strict barriers","sourceOffset":31484},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Continuity of planes before differentiation","sourceOffset":41330},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Tied sheets and uniform graphical separation","sourceOffset":48977}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Numerical input","label":"thm:numerical-input","text":"The numerical spacetime Penrose theorem \\cite[Theorem 1.3]{CompanionNumerical} states that every initial-data exterior in Definition \\ref{def:data}, with the positive full-cut infimum in Definition \\ref{def:fullcuts}, satisfies \\begin{equation} \\boxed{\\quad m\\ge\\frac12 \\left(\\frac{A_{\\min}(S)}{\\omega}\\right)^{\\frac{n-2}{n-1}} . \\quad} \\end{equation} This input is used for nearby weakly trapped data as well as for the original pair. It imposes none of the additional equality hypotheses introduced below.","sourceOffset":21984,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Original-data rigidity","label":"thm:rigidity","text":"Suppose an exterior in Definition \\ref{def:setting:rigidity} attains equality in \\eqref{eq:setting:main}. There is a global smooth spacelike embedding of the entire \\(\\Omega\\), including \\(S\\), into the regular extension of one exterior of \\(\\mathbf G_m\\), inducing exactly the original \\(g\\) and \\(K\\) with convention \\eqref{eq:setting:Ksign}. The image lies in the domain \\(r>r_m\\) together with its corresponding future horizon and bifurcation sphere. The boundary maps to a full smooth section of that future horizon or to its bifurcation sphere. The embedding and future unit normal extend smoothly to \\(S\\) in horizon-regular coordinates, and the chosen end approaches spatial infinity. There is no conclusion about a region behind \\(S\\).","sourceOffset":23854,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"One-ended initial-data exterior","label":"def:data","text":"A one-ended initial-data exterior is a smooth connected orientable \\(n\\)-manifold \\(\\Omega\\) with nonempty compact smooth boundary \\(S\\), a smooth Riemannian metric \\(g\\), and a smooth symmetric covariant two-tensor \\(K\\), both smooth up to \\(S\\), satisfying the following conditions. The metric space \\((\\Omega,g)\\), with \\(S\\) included, is complete. There is a compact subset \\(C\\subset\\Omega\\) such that \\(\\Omega\\setminus C\\) is a single coordinate end diffeomorphic to \\(\\{x\\in\\R^n:|x|>R_0\\}\\). In these coordinates, for some \\[ \\frac{n-2}{2}<q<n-2, \\] we have \\begin{equation} g_{ij}-\\delta_{ij}","sourceOffset":18373,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Full enclosing cuts","label":"def:fullcuts","text":"A full enclosing cut is \\(\\Gamma=\\partial D\\), the entire intrinsic manifold boundary of a connected smooth codimension-zero submanifold-with-boundary \\(D\\) of \\(\\Omega\\), such that \\(D\\) is closed in \\(\\Omega\\), its manifold interior lies in \\(\\operatorname{int}\\Omega\\), and it contains the whole sufficiently distant coordinate end. We require \\(\\Gamma\\) to be compact, smooth, embedded, and two-sided. All components of \\(\\partial D\\) are counted, including any portion coincident with \\(S\\). In particular, \\(D=\\Omega\\) is admissible and has full boundary \\(S\\). Define \\begin{equation} A_{\\min}","sourceOffset":20940,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/260.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":559853,"sourceFileCount":24,"statementCount":37,"definitionCount":8,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Equality-and-rigidity-in-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"260-12","slug":"Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026","familyId":"260","title":"Boundary graph deformations for the spacetime Penrose inequality","abstract":"We construct graph and conformal deformations of trapped initial-data exteriors in three and four spatial dimensions. The resulting metrics have nonnegative scalar curvature, strictly negative inner mean curvature, a lower bound protecting every enclosing cut, and an arbitrarily small upper error in ADM energy. We also give a direct four-dimensional maximal vacuum construction that retains a noncompact decaying second fundamental form. These constructions give boundary routes to the corresponding numerical Penrose inequalities.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sharp enclosing-area spacetime Penrose inequality for smooth one-ended asymptotically flat initial data in every spatial dimension n ≥ 3, under dominant energy, weak future trapping, positive enclosing area, and the stated decay assumptions. It bounds invariant ADM mass below using minimum enclosing area, with equality rigidity under additional horizon hypotheses. Charged upper-area bounds treat dyonic three-dimensional data; the higher-dimensional purely electric extension uses the matched neutral theorem.","pdfPath":"preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/neutral/neutral.tex","preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/neutral/00-setting.tex","preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/neutral/01-domains.tex","preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/neutral/02-system.tex","preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/neutral/03-estimates.tex","preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/neutral/degree/degree.tex","preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/neutral/05-end.tex","preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/four/four-chapter.tex","preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/four/four-opening.tex","preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/four/four-geometry.tex","preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/four/four-frontiers.tex","preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/four/four-system.tex","preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/four/four-bounds.tex","preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/four/four-regularity.tex","preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/four/four-existence.tex","preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/four-transfer.tex","preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/transition.tex","preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/maximal/maximal-method.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct graph and conformal deformations of trapped initial-data exteriors in three and four spatial dimensions. The resulting metrics have nonnegative scalar curvature, strictly negative inner mean curvature, a lower bound protecting every enclosing cut, and an arbitrarily small upper error in ADM energy. We also give a direct four-dimensional maximal vacuum construction that retains a noncompact decaying second fundamental form. These constructions give boundary routes to the corresponding numerical Penrose inequalities.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":596},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The constructions and their relationship","sourceOffset":4062},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why the inner boundary matters","sourceOffset":7405},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Relation to earlier methods","sourceOffset":9567},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reading the proof","sourceOffset":14759},{"kind":"section","title":"A boundary deformation in three dimensions","sourceOffset":16425},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Initial data, normals, and enclosing area","sourceOffset":16921},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prepared data and the geometric output","sourceOffset":21076},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Consequence for weakly decaying original data","sourceOffset":24637},{"kind":"section","title":"Trapped domains, collars, and weak supports","sourceOffset":26024},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The bounding-region convention","sourceOffset":27201},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Choosing the two maximal regions","sourceOffset":30624}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"MOTS barriers and the total trapped region","label":"n3:domains:region-theorem","text":"Let $(N,g,Q)$ be a smooth compact three-dimensional initial data set with boundary $\\Gamma_-\\sqcup\\Gamma_+$. Assume $\\Gamma_+$ is nonempty and has positive expansion with its normal out of $N$. The inner part $\\Gamma_-$ may be empty; when it is present, assume its expansion is negative with its normal into $N$. Consider all smooth domains whose inner boundary is $\\Gamma_-$ and whose remaining boundary $\\Sigma$ lies in the interior of $N$ and satisfies $\\Theta_Q(\\Sigma)\\leq0$. They avoid $\\Gamma_+$, and their normal on $\\Sigma$ points out of the domain. If their union is nonempty, its closure has a smooth compact embedded stable MOTS frontier in the interior, separating it from $\\Gamma_+$. This closure is itself the closure of such a domain and contains every domain in the defining class. If $\\Gamma_-$ is nonempty, the two strict barriers also give a nonempty smooth embedded enclosing MOTS.","sourceOffset":27904,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Prepared domain","label":"n3:domains:prepared-domain","text":"The thresholds and the domain above can be chosen with the following properties. The numbers $c_b,c_w<0$ are right-continuity points of their respective full maximal-region families. The boundary of $\\Omega$ consists of the faces in Equation \\eqref{n3:domains:face-expansions}, has disjoint smooth outward leaf collars as in Lemma \\ref{n3:domains:stable-collar}, and preserves the enclosing-area lower bound in Equation \\eqref{n3:domains:inherited-area}. There are a smooth compactly supported function $C$ on $\\Omega$, constants $C_b\\geq0$, $C_w\\leq0$, and positive constants $k_B$ on its face collars such that \\begin{equation} \\begin{aligned} C_w\\leq C\\leq C_b,&\\qquad C=C_b-k_Bs&&\\text{in a black collar near }B_b,\\\\ &\\qquad C=C_w+k_Bs&&\\text{in a white collar near }B_w. \\end{aligned} \\end{equation} For an absent type, its corresponding constant is simply a bound. Set \\begin{equation} b_-=c_b-C_b<0, \\qquad b_+=-c_w-C_w>0. \\end{equation} There is a smooth positive function $\\rho$, equal to a positive constant times $r^{-4}$ on the far end and satisfying symbol derivative bounds there, such that, for every $h\\in[b_-,b_+]$, \\begin{equation} |(h+C)\\tr K|+|\\nabla C|+\\rho \\leq\\tfrac12\\mathfrak m \\quad\\hbox{everywhere on }\\Omega. \\end{equation} The thresholds, collars, offset, and $\\rho$ are fixed before the deformation parameters are chosen. After fixing the thresholds and collars, the offset and its first derivatives can be made arbitrarily small.","sourceOffset":55555,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Exterior and enclosing area","label":"n3:intro:exterior-class","text":"An exterior is a connected orientable smooth three-manifold \\(\\Omega\\) with nonempty compact smooth boundary, complete as a metric space with its boundary included, and with one asymptotically flat coordinate end whose complement is compact. An enclosing cut is the entire compact smooth embedded two-sided intrinsic boundary of a connected smooth end-containing domain $D$ closed in $\\overline\\Omega$, whose intrinsic interior lies in $\\operatorname{int}\\Omega$. It separates the entire inner boundary from the distant end. The inner side includes the original obstacle and every bounded complementa","sourceOffset":19204,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Prepared three-dimensional data","label":"n3:prepared:data","text":"Let $(\\Omega_0,g,K)$ be an exterior in Definition \\ref{n3:intro:exterior-class}, with inner boundary $S_0$. Suppose $K$ is compactly supported and, on its single coordinate end, $g-\\delta=O_j(r^{-1})$ for every fixed $j\\ge0$. Assume the physical constraint densities in Equation \\eqref{n3:intro:constraints} are integrable, the ADM energy is $E>0$, and the ADM momentum is zero. There are $0<\\delta<1$, $c>0$, and a smooth radius extension $\\varrho\\ge1$, equal to $r$ sufficiently far out, such that \\[ R_g=O(r^{-3-\\delta}),\\qquad 8\\pi(\\mu-|J|_g)\\ge c\\varrho^{-3-\\delta}. \\] The boundary is strictly ","sourceOffset":21317,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/260.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":548148,"sourceFileCount":20,"statementCount":35,"definitionCount":4,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Boundary-graph-deformations-for-the-spacetime-Penrose-inequality-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"260-13","slug":"Area-controlled-end-replacement-and-the-Bondi-Penrose-inequality-in-the-CKS-class-September-27-2026","familyId":"260","title":"Area-controlled end replacement and the Bondi Penrose inequality in the CKS class","abstract":"We replace a three-dimensional Cha–Khuri–Sakovich hyperboloidal end by an asymptotically flat end while preserving the dominant energy condition and each fixed compact interior. For strictly future-timelike CKS initial-data charge, the replacements lose asymptotically no enclosing area and their ADM masses tend to the invariant Bondi mass. Combining this construction with the companion numerical spacetime Penrose theorem yields the sharp Bondi bound for possibly disconnected weakly future trapped boundaries in this class. Horizon-regular Schwarzschild exteriors attain equality at every positive mass.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sharp enclosing-area spacetime Penrose inequality for smooth one-ended asymptotically flat initial data in every spatial dimension n ≥ 3, under dominant energy, weak future trapping, positive enclosing area, and the stated decay assumptions. It bounds invariant ADM mass below using minimum enclosing area, with equality rigidity under additional horizon hypotheses. Charged upper-area bounds treat dyonic three-dimensional data; the higher-dimensional purely electric extension uses the matched neutral theorem.","pdfPath":"preprints/Area-controlled-end-replacement-and-the-Bondi-Penrose-inequality-in-the-CKS-class-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Area-controlled-end-replacement-and-the-Bondi-Penrose-inequality-in-the-CKS-class-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Area-controlled-end-replacement-and-the-Bondi-Penrose-inequality-in-the-CKS-class-September-27-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Area-controlled-end-replacement-and-the-Bondi-Penrose-inequality-in-the-CKS-class-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Area-controlled-end-replacement-and-the-Bondi-Penrose-inequality-in-the-CKS-class-September-27-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Area-controlled-end-replacement-and-the-Bondi-Penrose-inequality-in-the-CKS-class-September-27-2026/build/sections/00-model.tex","preprints/Area-controlled-end-replacement-and-the-Bondi-Penrose-inequality-in-the-CKS-class-September-27-2026/build/sections/01-geometry.tex","preprints/Area-controlled-end-replacement-and-the-Bondi-Penrose-inequality-in-the-CKS-class-September-27-2026/build/sections/02-collar.tex","preprints/Area-controlled-end-replacement-and-the-Bondi-Penrose-inequality-in-the-CKS-class-September-27-2026/build/sections/03-bending.tex","preprints/Area-controlled-end-replacement-and-the-Bondi-Penrose-inequality-in-the-CKS-class-September-27-2026/build/sections/04-transfer.tex","preprints/Area-controlled-end-replacement-and-the-Bondi-Penrose-inequality-in-the-CKS-class-September-27-2026/build/sections/05-sharpness.tex","preprints/Area-controlled-end-replacement-and-the-Bondi-Penrose-inequality-in-the-CKS-class-September-27-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Area-controlled-end-replacement-and-the-Bondi-Penrose-inequality-in-the-CKS-class-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We replace a three-dimensional Cha--Khuri--Sakovich hyperboloidal end by an asymptotically flat end while preserving the dominant energy condition and each fixed compact interior. For strictly future-timelike CKS initial-data charge, the replacements lose asymptotically no enclosing area and their ADM masses tend to the invariant Bondi mass. Combining this construction with the companion numerical spacetime Penrose theorem yields the sharp Bondi bound for possibly disconnected weakly future trapped boundaries in this class. Horizon-regular Schwarzschild exteriors attain equality at every positive mass.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Replacing the end and retaining the area","sourceOffset":672},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exterior, its charges and its enclosing cuts","sourceOffset":1720},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The geometric theorem and its numerical consequence","sourceOffset":6046},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Predecessors and the construction","sourceOffset":9049},{"kind":"section","title":"The radial model and the angular obstruction","sourceOffset":14019},{"kind":"section","title":"Balancing and the constraint identities","sourceOffset":18110},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A balanced end chart","sourceOffset":18894},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sphere geometry and the constraints","sourceOffset":24179},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The original data in foliation variables","sourceOffset":30619},{"kind":"section","title":"A collar with round leaves","sourceOffset":33520},{"kind":"section","title":"Bending the round end","sourceOffset":49360},{"kind":"section","title":"Charges, completeness and enclosing area","sourceOffset":63704}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Area-controlled end replacement","label":"prop:replacement","text":"Let $(\\Omega,g,K)$ be a smooth connected orientable three-dimensional exterior, complete including its nonempty compact smooth boundary, with exactly one end of class \\eqref{eq:cks}. Suppose DEC holds and $E_B>|P_B|$. There is a balanced chart such that, for every sufficiently large $R$, smooth data $(G_R,k_R)$ on the same exterior have the following properties: \\begin{enumerate} \\item They agree with $(g,K)$ on the compact side of $r=R$ and satisfy DEC. \\item For numbers $0\\leq\\varepsilon_R<1$ with $\\varepsilon_R\\to0$, $G_R\\geq(1-\\varepsilon_R)g$ everywhere on $\\Omega$. \\item The end is asymptotically flat, with $G_R-\\delta=O_j(r^{-1})$ for every fixed finite $j$, and $k_R=0$ sufficiently far out. The constraints are integrable and its charges satisfy $P_R=0$, $E_R=m_B+\\eta_R$, where $0\\leq\\eta_R\\leq2R^{-1/2}$. \\end{enumerate} The new exterior is complete and $A_{\\min,G_R}(S)\\geq(1-\\varepsilon_R)A_{\\min,g}(S)$. No condition on the boundary expansion is needed.","sourceOffset":6608,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Sharp Bondi inequality in the CKS class","label":"cor:cks-inequality","text":"Under the hypotheses of Theorem \\ref{prop:replacement}, assume $\\theta_+(S)\\leq0$, with normal into $\\Omega$, and $A_{\\min,g}(S)>0$. Then \\begin{equation} \\sqrt{E_B^2-|P_B|^2}\\geq \\sqrt{\\frac{A_{\\min,g}(S)}{16\\pi}}. \\end{equation} The boundary may be disconnected. For every $m>0$ there is a horizon-regular Schwarzschild hyperboloidal exterior attaining equality with $m_B=m$ and $A_{\\min,g}(S)=16\\pi m^2$.","sourceOffset":7965,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/260.md","scopeText":"The formalization replaces a three-dimensional Cha–Khuri–Sakovich hyperboloidal end by asymptotically flat ends while preserving each fixed compact interior, completeness, and the dominant energy condition. For strictly future-timelike initial-data charge, the ADM masses approach the invariant Bondi mass and the loss of enclosing area tends to zero.\n\nThe Comparator also includes canonical Schwarzschild equality examples at every positive mass, with $m_{\\mathrm{Bondi}}=m$ and enclosing area $16\\pi m^2$. The paper's general Bondi–Penrose inequality for possibly disconnected weakly trapped boundaries remains unformalized.\n\nThe implementation retains a conditional inequality for the connected marginal-boundary case, assuming the asymptotically flat exterior Penrose inequality. This supporting ","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/260.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":83579,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Area-controlled-end-replacement-and-the-Bondi-Penrose-inequality-in-the-CKS-class-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"261-01","slug":"Absolutely-Continuous-Spectrum-for-Weak-Disorder-Anderson-Models-in-Dimensions-at-Least-Three-September-23-2026","familyId":"261","title":"Absolutely Continuous Spectrum for Weak-Disorder Anderson Models in Dimensions at Least Three","abstract":"For each fixed dimension d ≥ 3 and each fixed sufficiently small positive disorder strength, we prove that the Anderson operator on ℤd with independent uniform site potentials almost surely has purely absolutely continuous spectrum with nonzero weight on an open energy interval. The interval may depend on d but is independent of the disorder strength. This settles the purely absolutely continuous energy-range question in Simon's Problem 1 for this model.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the predicted spectral contrast for the lattice Anderson model with independent uniform site potentials. In dimension two, every positive disorder strength gives almost surely pure-point spectrum. In every fixed dimension d ≥ 3, sufficiently weak positive disorder gives purely absolutely continuous spectrum on a fixed open interval with nonzero spectral weight.","pdfPath":"preprints/Absolutely-Continuous-Spectrum-for-Weak-Disorder-Anderson-Models-in-Dimensions-at-Least-Three-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Absolutely-Continuous-Spectrum-for-Weak-Disorder-Anderson-Models-in-Dimensions-at-Least-Three-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Absolutely-Continuous-Spectrum-for-Weak-Disorder-Anderson-Models-in-Dimensions-at-Least-Three-September-23-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Absolutely-Continuous-Spectrum-for-Weak-Disorder-Anderson-Models-in-Dimensions-at-Least-Three-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Absolutely-Continuous-Spectrum-for-Weak-Disorder-Anderson-Models-in-Dimensions-at-Least-Three-September-23-2026/build/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Absolutely-Continuous-Spectrum-for-Weak-Disorder-Anderson-Models-in-Dimensions-at-Least-Three-September-23-2026/build/02-state.tex","preprints/Absolutely-Continuous-Spectrum-for-Weak-Disorder-Anderson-Models-in-Dimensions-at-Least-Three-September-23-2026/build/figures/site-states.tex","preprints/Absolutely-Continuous-Spectrum-for-Weak-Disorder-Anderson-Models-in-Dimensions-at-Least-Three-September-23-2026/build/03-initialization.tex","preprints/Absolutely-Continuous-Spectrum-for-Weak-Disorder-Anderson-Models-in-Dimensions-at-Least-Three-September-23-2026/build/04-sweep.tex","preprints/Absolutely-Continuous-Spectrum-for-Weak-Disorder-Anderson-Models-in-Dimensions-at-Least-Three-September-23-2026/build/05-local-sweeps.tex","preprints/Absolutely-Continuous-Spectrum-for-Weak-Disorder-Anderson-Models-in-Dimensions-at-Least-Three-September-23-2026/build/06-clearing.tex","preprints/Absolutely-Continuous-Spectrum-for-Weak-Disorder-Anderson-Models-in-Dimensions-at-Least-Three-September-23-2026/build/07-assembly.tex","preprints/Absolutely-Continuous-Spectrum-for-Weak-Disorder-Anderson-Models-in-Dimensions-at-Least-Three-September-23-2026/build/08-history.tex","preprints/Absolutely-Continuous-Spectrum-for-Weak-Disorder-Anderson-Models-in-Dimensions-at-Least-Three-September-23-2026/build/09-spectral.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Absolutely-Continuous-Spectrum-for-Weak-Disorder-Anderson-Models-in-Dimensions-at-Least-Three-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For each fixed dimension $d\\ge3$ and each fixed sufficiently small positive disorder strength, we prove that the Anderson operator on $\\mathbb Z^d$ with independent uniform site potentials almost surely has purely absolutely continuous spectrum with nonzero weight on an open energy interval. The interval may depend on $d$ but is independent of the disorder strength. This settles the purely absolutely continuous energy-range question in Simon's Problem 1 for this model.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":536},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and the result","sourceOffset":986},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and scope","sourceOffset":3275},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof mechanism","sourceOffset":7016},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coexistence with edge localization","sourceOffset":13521},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite-volume certificate and the matrix state","sourceOffset":16157},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The certificate","sourceOffset":16864},{"kind":"subsection","title":"True digits, site states, and retained information","sourceOffset":19447},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Schur reduction and the Ward Laplacian","sourceOffset":28722},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The bounds carried between stages","sourceOffset":33366},{"kind":"section","title":"The initial state and its comparison forms","sourceOffset":37500},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Duality and lattice estimates","sourceOffset":38058}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a fixed sufficiently small number $e_2\\in(0,1/100)$ such that, for every fixed integer $d\\ge3$, there is a number $\\lambda_d>0$ with the following property. Set \\begin{equation} I_d= \\begin{cases} (-6+e_2/2,-6+e_2),&d=3,\\\\ -2d+(1/200,1/100),&d\\ge4. \\end{cases} \\end{equation} Then for every fixed $0<\\lambda<\\lambda_d$, almost surely \\[ I_d\\subset\\sigma(H_{\\lambda,\\omega}),\\qquad \\chi_{I_d}(H_{\\lambda,\\omega})\\ell^2(\\ZZ^d) \\subset\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{ac}}(H_{\\lambda,\\omega}). \\] In particular, the spectral restriction to $I_d$ is purely absolutely continuous and $\\chi_{I_d}(H_{\\lambda,\\omega})\\ne0$. For $d\\ge4$, one may take $\\lambda_d=\\sqrt3\\,2^{-n_*(d)}$, where the finite-matrix specification of $n_*(d)$ is given in Definition \\ref{def:threshold}. For $d=3$, the proof supplies an existential positive threshold.","sourceOffset":1962,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Spectral coexistence","label":"cor:coexistence","text":"For every fixed $d\\ge3$ and every fixed $0<\\lambda<\\lambda_d$, there is a deterministic number $\\delta_{d,\\lambda}\\in(0,\\lambda/2]$ such that the open interval \\[ J_{d,\\lambda} =(-2d-\\lambda,-2d-\\lambda+\\delta_{d,\\lambda}) \\] is disjoint from $I_d$ and, almost surely, both $\\chi_{I_d}(H_{\\lambda,\\omega})$ and $\\chi_{J_{d,\\lambda}}(H_{\\lambda,\\omega})$ are nonzero. The restriction to $I_d$ is purely absolutely continuous, whereas the restriction to $J_{d,\\lambda}$ is pure point with exponentially decaying eigenfunctions. The probability-one event may depend on $d$ and $\\lambda$.","sourceOffset":13846,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Finite-box threshold","label":"def:threshold","text":"For the fixed dimension $d\\ge4$, let $n_*(d)$ be the least integer $k\\ge10$ with the following property. For every pair of integers $n_0\\ge k$ and $m\\ge4n_0$, every $u\\in(1/4,1]$, and every $E\\in I_d=-2d+(1/200,1/100)$, set \\[ L=4^{20m},\\qquad N=L^d,\\qquad \\lambda(n_0,u)=\\sqrt{3u}\\,2^{-n_0},\\qquad \\eta=u4^{-m}. \\] Let $H_{m,n_0,u}$ be the true periodic negative-hopping Anderson matrix on $(\\ZZ/L\\ZZ)^d$ with independent uniform $[-1,1]$ variables and coupling $\\lambda(n_0,u)$. With probability at least $1/2$, there must exist a set $A$ and coefficients $r=(r_i)_{i\\in A}$ such that \\begin{equati","sourceOffset":284411,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/261.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":315555,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Absolutely-Continuous-Spectrum-for-Weak-Disorder-Anderson-Models-in-Dimensions-at-Least-Three-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"261-02","slug":"Pure-Point-Spectrum-for-the-Two-Dimensional-Anderson-Model-at-Every-Positive-Disorder-September-23-2026","familyId":"261","title":"Pure-Point Spectrum for the Two-Dimensional Anderson Model at Every Positive Disorder","abstract":"For each fixed positive disorder strength, we prove that the nearest-neighbor Anderson operator on the square lattice with independent uniform site potentials almost surely has pure-point spectral type throughout its spectrum. This resolves the pure-point assertion of the two-dimensional Anderson localization conjecture for the uniform single-site law.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the predicted spectral contrast for the lattice Anderson model with independent uniform site potentials. In dimension two, every positive disorder strength gives almost surely pure-point spectrum. In every fixed dimension d ≥ 3, sufficiently weak positive disorder gives purely absolutely continuous spectrum on a fixed open interval with nonzero spectral weight.","pdfPath":"preprints/Pure-Point-Spectrum-for-the-Two-Dimensional-Anderson-Model-at-Every-Positive-Disorder-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Pure-Point-Spectrum-for-the-Two-Dimensional-Anderson-Model-at-Every-Positive-Disorder-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Pure-Point-Spectrum-for-the-Two-Dimensional-Anderson-Model-at-Every-Positive-Disorder-September-23-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Pure-Point-Spectrum-for-the-Two-Dimensional-Anderson-Model-at-Every-Positive-Disorder-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Pure-Point-Spectrum-for-the-Two-Dimensional-Anderson-Model-at-Every-Positive-Disorder-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Pure-Point-Spectrum-for-the-Two-Dimensional-Anderson-Model-at-Every-Positive-Disorder-September-23-2026/build/sections/setup.tex","preprints/Pure-Point-Spectrum-for-the-Two-Dimensional-Anderson-Model-at-Every-Positive-Disorder-September-23-2026/build/sections/matrix.tex","preprints/Pure-Point-Spectrum-for-the-Two-Dimensional-Anderson-Model-at-Every-Positive-Disorder-September-23-2026/build/sections/resonant.tex","preprints/Pure-Point-Spectrum-for-the-Two-Dimensional-Anderson-Model-at-Every-Positive-Disorder-September-23-2026/build/sections/impedance.tex","preprints/Pure-Point-Spectrum-for-the-Two-Dimensional-Anderson-Model-at-Every-Positive-Disorder-September-23-2026/build/sections/transmission.tex","preprints/Pure-Point-Spectrum-for-the-Two-Dimensional-Anderson-Model-at-Every-Positive-Disorder-September-23-2026/build/sections/hierarchy.tex","preprints/Pure-Point-Spectrum-for-the-Two-Dimensional-Anderson-Model-at-Every-Positive-Disorder-September-23-2026/build/sections/screening.tex","preprints/Pure-Point-Spectrum-for-the-Two-Dimensional-Anderson-Model-at-Every-Positive-Disorder-September-23-2026/build/sections/vanishing.tex","preprints/Pure-Point-Spectrum-for-the-Two-Dimensional-Anderson-Model-at-Every-Positive-Disorder-September-23-2026/build/sections/bootstrap.tex","preprints/Pure-Point-Spectrum-for-the-Two-Dimensional-Anderson-Model-at-Every-Positive-Disorder-September-23-2026/build/sections/spectral.tex","preprints/Pure-Point-Spectrum-for-the-Two-Dimensional-Anderson-Model-at-Every-Positive-Disorder-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Pure-Point-Spectrum-for-the-Two-Dimensional-Anderson-Model-at-Every-Positive-Disorder-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For each fixed positive disorder strength, we prove that the nearest-neighbor Anderson operator on the square lattice with independent uniform site potentials almost surely has pure-point spectral type throughout its spectrum. This resolves the pure-point assertion of the two-dimensional Anderson localization conjecture for the uniform single-site law.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":400},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and scope","sourceOffset":2033},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof mechanism","sourceOffset":5968},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite volumes and conventions","sourceOffset":9620},{"kind":"section","title":"Scattering matrices and analytic estimates","sourceOffset":13125},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Invertibility, absorption, and Schur complements","sourceOffset":14289},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Closing ports and comparing energies","sourceOffset":17688},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Dimension-independent analytic bounds","sourceOffset":24941},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Determinant phases and artificial absorptions","sourceOffset":32167},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The artificial probe profile","sourceOffset":35392},{"kind":"section","title":"The resonant cell law","sourceOffset":37169},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Clipping the corners","sourceOffset":37623}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"main:theorem","text":"For each fixed $h>0$, almost surely $H_v$ has pure-point spectral type, admits a complete orthonormal eigenbasis, and has spectrum \\[ \\sigma(H_v)=[-4-h,4+h]. \\] Its eigenvalues are dense in this interval. The probability-one event in this statement is allowed to depend on $h$.","sourceOffset":1175,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Properties of the branch law","label":"cell:branch","text":"For sufficiently large $L$, the above choices have the following properties, uniformly over permitted corner boxes: \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\roman*)] \\item Every interior draw has \\begin{equation} \\norm{A^{-1}}\\le2L^{C_2},\\qquad \\norm{-A^{-1}p-\\psi|_J}\\le C L^{C_2+1}\\epsilon. \\end{equation} The exponents in these bounds are independent of $D_2$, provided $D_3$ is subsequently chosen sufficiently large. \\item All true potentials on $V$ lie in a fixed compact subinterval of $(-h,h)$. The root center satisfies \\begin{equation} |c(v)-v_0^0|\\le C L^{C_2+1}\\epsilon. \\end{equation} The plateau of $\\vartheta$ can therefore be fixed to contain every true value on $V$ and every single side value in $B$, for all the cells used. The artificial absorptions on these sites are constant as $r$, the jitters, the common side shift, and the hidden root draw vary. \\item Relative to the true product law on the tile conditioned on these same four corner boxes, the branch has a density $\\mathcal L$ satisfying \\begin{equation} 0\\le\\mathcal L\\le \\exp\\bigl(C L^3+C L^2\\log L\\bigr), \\qquad \\int\\mathcal L\\,\\dd\\PP_{\\rm true,boxes}=1. \\end{equation} The corner-box conditional laws and the laws outside $V\\cup B$ are unchanged. \\end{enumerate} Artificial probes may have any prescribed mask of absent, constant, or profile-dependent strengths, as in \\Cref{mat:probes}. For subsequent cell estimates their common scale is restricted to $0\\le\\tau\\le e^{-c_*L}$, with a fixed $c_*>2\\nu$.","sourceOffset":54524,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/261.md","scopeText":"The linked formalization proves a supporting spectral statement for the nearest-neighbor Anderson operator on $\\mathbb Z^2$. For every disorder strength $h>0$ with independent site potentials uniform on $[-h,h]$, it constructs the bounded self-adjoint operator almost surely and identifies its spectrum as the real interval $[-4-h,4+h]$.\n\nThis selected statement identifies the spectral set. It does not assert the pure-point spectral type claimed in the accompanying paper.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/261.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":265134,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":19,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Pure-Point-Spectrum-for-the-Two-Dimensional-Anderson-Model-at-Every-Positive-Disorder-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"262-01","slug":"Equality-cases-in-the-sharp-one-dimensional-matrix-Lieb-Thirring-inequality-October-5-2026","familyId":"262","title":"Equality cases in the sharp one-dimensional matrix Lieb–Thirring inequality","abstract":"We classify all equality cases in the sharp one-dimensional Lieb–Thirring inequality for every finite matrix size and $1/2\\lt \\gamma\\lt 3/2$. For measurable Hermitian positive semidefinite potentials W with $\\int_\\mathbb R\\mathop{\\mathrm{tr}}\\nolimits (W^{\\gamma+1/2})\\lt \\infty$, equality holds precisely for direct sums, in one constant unitary basis, of scalar one-bound-state solitons and zero channels. The nonzero solitons may have independent scales and centers.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sharp one-dimensional Lieb–Thirring inequality for $1/2\\lt \\gamma\\lt 3/2$ and arbitrary finite-matrix potentials W ≥ 0 with $\\int\\mathop{\\mathrm{tr}}\\nolimits (W^{\\gamma+1/2})\\lt \\infty$: the optimal constant is the scalar one-bound-state value, independent of matrix size. All equality cases are direct sums, in one constant unitary basis, of scalar sech2 solitons with independent scales and centers, and zero channels.","pdfPath":"preprints/Equality-cases-in-the-sharp-one-dimensional-matrix-Lieb-Thirring-inequality-October-5-2026/sharp-one-dimensional-lieb-thirring-inequalities-matrix-potentials.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Equality-cases-in-the-sharp-one-dimensional-matrix-Lieb-Thirring-inequality-October-5-2026/sharp-one-dimensional-lieb-thirring-inequalities-matrix-potentials.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Equality-cases-in-the-sharp-one-dimensional-matrix-Lieb-Thirring-inequality-October-5-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Equality-cases-in-the-sharp-one-dimensional-matrix-Lieb-Thirring-inequality-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Equality-cases-in-the-sharp-one-dimensional-matrix-Lieb-Thirring-inequality-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Equality-cases-in-the-sharp-one-dimensional-matrix-Lieb-Thirring-inequality-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Equality-cases-in-the-sharp-one-dimensional-matrix-Lieb-Thirring-inequality-October-5-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Equality-cases-in-the-sharp-one-dimensional-matrix-Lieb-Thirring-inequality-October-5-2026/build/sections/action.tex","preprints/Equality-cases-in-the-sharp-one-dimensional-matrix-Lieb-Thirring-inequality-October-5-2026/build/sections/spectral-bound.tex","preprints/Equality-cases-in-the-sharp-one-dimensional-matrix-Lieb-Thirring-inequality-October-5-2026/build/sections/compactness.tex","preprints/Equality-cases-in-the-sharp-one-dimensional-matrix-Lieb-Thirring-inequality-October-5-2026/build/sections/rigidity.tex","preprints/Equality-cases-in-the-sharp-one-dimensional-matrix-Lieb-Thirring-inequality-October-5-2026/build/sections/appendix-trace.tex","preprints/Equality-cases-in-the-sharp-one-dimensional-matrix-Lieb-Thirring-inequality-October-5-2026/build/sections/appendix-paths.tex","preprints/Equality-cases-in-the-sharp-one-dimensional-matrix-Lieb-Thirring-inequality-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Equality-cases-in-the-sharp-one-dimensional-matrix-Lieb-Thirring-inequality-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We classify all equality cases in the sharp one-dimensional Lieb--Thirring inequality for every finite matrix size and $1/2<\\gamma<3/2$. For measurable Hermitian positive semidefinite potentials $W$ with $\\int_\\R\\tr(W^{\\gamma+1/2})<\\infty$, equality holds precisely for direct sums, in one constant unitary basis, of scalar one-bound-state solitons and zero channels. The nonzero solitons may have independent scales and centers.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":528},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and related results","sourceOffset":3406},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Quantitative defects and fixed channels","sourceOffset":5472},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Notation","sourceOffset":7977},{"kind":"section","title":"Spectral and convexity preliminaries","sourceOffset":8782},{"kind":"section","title":"An action estimate that retains the equality defects","sourceOffset":12497},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coercivity at the matrix-field constraint","sourceOffset":14837},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The action identity and the penalty limit","sourceOffset":21292},{"kind":"section","title":"Recovering the sharp spectral bound","sourceOffset":24938},{"kind":"section","title":"From equality to pointwise matrix relations","sourceOffset":27620},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite spectral approximations and vanishing errors","sourceOffset":30173},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Convergence of each fixed row","sourceOffset":34119}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Equality classification","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $W$ satisfying \\eqref{intro:class}, \\begin{equation} \\Tr(H_W)_-^\\gamma\\le C_\\gamma\\int_\\R\\tr(W^p), \\qquad C_\\gamma=\\left(\\frac{\\gamma-1/2}{\\gamma+1/2}\\right)^{\\gamma-1/2} \\frac{\\Gamma(\\gamma+1)}{\\sqrt\\pi\\,\\Gamma(\\gamma+3/2)}. \\end{equation} Put $r=(\\gamma-1/2)^{-1}$. Equality holds if and only if there are $k\\in\\{0,\\ldots,m\\}$, a constant unitary matrix $U$, positive numbers $a_1,\\ldots,a_k$, and real numbers $x_1,\\ldots,x_k$ such that almost everywhere \\begin{equation} W(x)=U\\diag\\bigl(w_1(x),\\ldots,w_k(x),0,\\ldots,0\\bigr)U^*, \\quad w_j(x)=(r+1)a_j^2\\sech^2\\!\\bigl(ra_j(x-x_j)\\bigr). \\end{equation} The case $k=0$ means $W=0$. Each nonzero channel has exactly one negative eigenvalue, $-a_j^2$, and therefore every extremal potential has at most $m$ negative eigenvalues, counted with multiplicity.","sourceOffset":2016,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Quantitative finite-interval action estimate","label":"act:quantitative-action","text":"Let $0<\\sigma<1$ and $\\kappa>0$. There is a constant $c_*=c_*(\\sigma,\\kappa)>0$ with the following property. For $n,d,\\Omega,K$ as above, suppose $U\\in H^1_0(\\Omega;\\R^{n\\times d})$ satisfies \\[ \\int_\\Omega UU^{\\mathsf T}\\,dx=I_n. \\] For every $\\eta>0$ there exist a real lower triangular matrix $C\\in\\R^{n\\times n}$ and a path $B\\in C^1(\\overline\\Omega;\\Sym_n)$ such that \\[ B(x_-)=K,\\qquad B(x_+)=-K,\\qquad \\sup_{x\\in\\overline\\Omega}\\|B(x)\\|_{\\op}\\leq\\kappa, \\] and, for $A=CU$, \\begin{equation} \\mathcal E_K(A)\\geq b_\\sigma\\sum_{i=1}^n\\ell_i^{2\\gamma} +\\int_\\Omega\\left(\\|A'-BA\\|_{\\HS}^2 +c_*\\Phi_K(A,B)\\right)\\,dx-\\eta. \\end{equation} In particular, $c_*$ is independent of $n,d,\\Omega,U$, and the smallest diagonal entry of $K$.","sourceOffset":13928,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/262.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":88956,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Equality-cases-in-the-sharp-one-dimensional-matrix-Lieb-Thirring-inequality-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"262-02","slug":"Sharp-one-dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-inequalities-for-matrix-potentials-October-5-2026","familyId":"262","title":"Sharp one-dimensional Lieb–Thirring inequalities for matrix potentials","abstract":"We prove the sharp one-dimensional Lieb–Thirring inequality for every finite matrix size and every exponent $1/2\\lt \\gamma\\lt 3/2$. The optimal constant is the scalar one-bound-state constant, independently of the matrix size. The inequality bounds the full sum of negative eigenvalue moments for every measurable Hermitian positive semidefinite potential W satisfying $\\int_\\mathbb R\\mathop{\\mathrm{tr}}\\nolimits (W^{\\gamma+1/2})\\lt \\infty$. The matrices may have arbitrary rank and need not commute at different points.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sharp one-dimensional Lieb–Thirring inequality for $1/2\\lt \\gamma\\lt 3/2$ and arbitrary finite-matrix potentials W ≥ 0 with $\\int\\mathop{\\mathrm{tr}}\\nolimits (W^{\\gamma+1/2})\\lt \\infty$: the optimal constant is the scalar one-bound-state value, independent of matrix size. All equality cases are direct sums, in one constant unitary basis, of scalar sech2 solitons with independent scales and centers, and zero channels.","pdfPath":"preprints/Sharp-one-dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-inequalities-for-matrix-potentials-October-5-2026/sharp-matrix-lieb-thirring.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Sharp-one-dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-inequalities-for-matrix-potentials-October-5-2026/sharp-matrix-lieb-thirring.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Sharp-one-dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-inequalities-for-matrix-potentials-October-5-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Sharp-one-dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-inequalities-for-matrix-potentials-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Sharp-one-dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-inequalities-for-matrix-potentials-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Sharp-one-dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-inequalities-for-matrix-potentials-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Sharp-one-dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-inequalities-for-matrix-potentials-October-5-2026/build/notes/history.tex","preprints/Sharp-one-dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-inequalities-for-matrix-potentials-October-5-2026/build/sections/action-framework.tex","preprints/Sharp-one-dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-inequalities-for-matrix-potentials-October-5-2026/build/sections/trace.tex","preprints/Sharp-one-dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-inequalities-for-matrix-potentials-October-5-2026/build/sections/field.tex","preprints/Sharp-one-dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-inequalities-for-matrix-potentials-October-5-2026/build/sections/paths.tex","preprints/Sharp-one-dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-inequalities-for-matrix-potentials-October-5-2026/build/sections/action-limit.tex","preprints/Sharp-one-dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-inequalities-for-matrix-potentials-October-5-2026/build/sections/spectral.tex","preprints/Sharp-one-dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-inequalities-for-matrix-potentials-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Sharp-one-dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-inequalities-for-matrix-potentials-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the sharp one-dimensional Lieb--Thirring inequality for every finite matrix size and every exponent $1/2<\\gamma<3/2$. The optimal constant is the scalar one-bound-state constant, independently of the matrix size. The inequality bounds the full sum of negative eigenvalue moments for every measurable Hermitian positive semidefinite potential $W$ satisfying $\\int_\\R\\tr(W^{\\gamma+1/2})<\\infty$. The matrices may have arbitrary rank and need not commute at different points.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":544},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and relationship to prior work","sourceOffset":2866},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The matrix comparison and the proof strategy","sourceOffset":5583},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":8348},{"kind":"section","title":"The finite-interval action inequality","sourceOffset":8898},{"kind":"section","title":"An integrated matrix trace inequality","sourceOffset":11075},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Resolvent estimates and half-line solutions","sourceOffset":13142},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The logarithmic integral and its weighted identity","sourceOffset":19780},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Completion of the trace comparison","sourceOffset":24163},{"kind":"section","title":"A regularized matrix field","sourceOffset":26772},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Construction and regularity","sourceOffset":28007},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Scalar shifts, matrix directions, and endpoints","sourceOffset":32608}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Sharp matrix inequality","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $1/2<\\gamma<3/2$, every finite $m\\ge1$, and every $W$ satisfying \\eqref{eq:potential-class}, \\begin{equation} \\Tr(H_W)_-^\\gamma\\le C_\\gamma\\int_\\R\\tr(W(x)^{\\gamma+1/2})\\,dx, \\qquad C_\\gamma= \\left(\\frac{\\gamma-1/2}{\\gamma+1/2}\\right)^{\\gamma-1/2} \\frac{\\Gamma(\\gamma+1)}{\\sqrt\\pi\\,\\Gamma(\\gamma+3/2)}. \\end{equation} The constant is optimal in every matrix dimension. With $r=(\\gamma-1/2)^{-1}$, it is attained by \\begin{equation} W(x)=\\diag\\big((r+1)\\sech^2(rx),0,\\ldots,0\\big). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":2057,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Action inequality","label":"thm:action","text":"For every $0<\\sigma<1$ and all the data just specified, \\begin{equation} \\sup_{C\\in\\mathcal L}\\mathcal E(CU) \\ge\\sum_{i=1}^n\\int_{-k_i}^{k_i}(k_i^2-s^2)^\\sigma\\,ds. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":9819,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/262.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":72339,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Sharp-one-dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-inequalities-for-matrix-potentials-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"262-03","slug":"Sharp-One-Dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-Constants-September-23-2026","familyId":"262","title":"Sharp one-dimensional Lieb–Thirring constants","abstract":"We resolve affirmatively the remaining cases of the scalar one-dimensional Lieb–Thirring conjecture: for every $\\frac12\\lt \\gamma\\lt \\frac32$, the optimal constant is the one-bound-state constant. The estimate holds for every nonnegative potential in $L^{\\gamma+1/2}(\\mathbb R)$, with all negative eigenvalues included.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sharp one-dimensional Lieb–Thirring inequality for $1/2\\lt \\gamma\\lt 3/2$ and arbitrary finite-matrix potentials W ≥ 0 with $\\int\\mathop{\\mathrm{tr}}\\nolimits (W^{\\gamma+1/2})\\lt \\infty$: the optimal constant is the scalar one-bound-state value, independent of matrix size. All equality cases are direct sums, in one constant unitary basis, of scalar sech2 solitons with independent scales and centers, and zero channels.","pdfPath":"preprints/Sharp-One-Dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-Constants-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Sharp-One-Dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-Constants-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Sharp-One-Dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-Constants-September-23-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Sharp-One-Dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-Constants-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Sharp-One-Dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-Constants-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Sharp-One-Dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-Constants-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Sharp-One-Dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-Constants-September-23-2026/build/sections/action-framework.tex","preprints/Sharp-One-Dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-Constants-September-23-2026/build/sections/matrix-field.tex","preprints/Sharp-One-Dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-Constants-September-23-2026/build/sections/rank-one.tex","preprints/Sharp-One-Dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-Constants-September-23-2026/build/sections/connecting-path.tex","preprints/Sharp-One-Dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-Constants-September-23-2026/build/sections/action-limit.tex","preprints/Sharp-One-Dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-Constants-September-23-2026/build/sections/spectral.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Sharp-One-Dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-Constants-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We resolve affirmatively the remaining cases of the scalar one-dimensional Lieb--Thirring conjecture: for every $\\frac12<\\gamma<\\frac32$, the optimal constant is the one-bound-state constant. The estimate holds for every nonnegative potential in $L^{\\gamma+1/2}(\\mathbb R)$, with all negative eigenvalues included.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":388},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and relation to prior work","sourceOffset":2726},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":6591},{"kind":"section","title":"The finite-interval action inequality","sourceOffset":9032},{"kind":"section","title":"A regularized matrix field","sourceOffset":11859},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Convergence and a primitive","sourceOffset":14109},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Scalar comparison and boundary values","sourceOffset":17247},{"kind":"section","title":"The rank-one comparison","sourceOffset":20556},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A tangent inequality on positive matrices","sourceOffset":21281},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The integrated tangent map","sourceOffset":23215},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Trace monotonicity and contraction","sourceOffset":26973},{"kind":"section","title":"A matrix path with prescribed endpoints","sourceOffset":29219}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $1/2<\\gamma<3/2$ and every nonnegative $W\\in L^{\\gamma+1/2}(\\R)$, \\begin{equation} \\Tr(H_{-W})_-^\\gamma \\le 2\\left(\\frac{\\gamma-1/2}{\\gamma+1/2}\\right)^{\\gamma-1/2} L_{\\gamma,1}^{\\cl}\\int_\\R W^{\\gamma+1/2}. \\end{equation} The constant is optimal and equals $L_{\\gamma,1}^{(1)}$. More precisely, if $r=(\\gamma-1/2)^{-1}$, equality is attained by $W(x)=(r+1)\\sech^2(rx)$.","sourceOffset":1845,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Action inequality","label":"thm:action","text":"Let $u=(u_1,\\ldots,u_N)^{\\transpose}\\in H^1_0(I;\\R^N)$ satisfy $\\int_I uu^{\\transpose}\\,dx=I_N$, and let $k_i>0$. Then \\begin{equation} \\sup_{C\\text{ lower triangular}}\\mathcal E_K(Cu) \\ge \\sum_{i=1}^N\\int_{-k_i}^{k_i}(k_i^2-s^2)^\\sigma\\,ds. \\end{equation} The supremum is over all real lower triangular $N\\times N$ matrices.","sourceOffset":9823,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/262.md","scopeText":"The formalized result determines the sharp one-dimensional Lieb–Thirring constant for $1/2<\\gamma<3/2$. For every nonnegative $W\\in L^{\\gamma+1/2}(\\mathbb R)$, it bounds the full negative-eigenvalue moment of $-d^2/dx^2-W$ by the one-bound-state constant times the potential integral. This constant is optimal and is attained by $(r+1)\\mathrm{sech}^2(rx)$, where $r=(\\gamma-1/2)^{-1}$. No finite spectral cutoff is imposed.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/262.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":55708,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Sharp-One-Dimensional-Lieb-Thirring-Constants-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"263-01","slug":"Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026","familyId":"263","title":"Uniform excess charge for Coulomb molecules and the outer radius of neutral atoms","abstract":"We prove that a molecule with M fixed nuclei of real charges at least one and total nuclear charge Z strictly binds at most $Z+CM$ electrons, where C is universal. The result holds for the full nonrelativistic Coulomb Hamiltonian with two spin states and arbitrary distinct nuclear positions. For neutral atoms with integer nuclear charge Z ≥ 1, the first ionization energy and, for every ground state, the radius outside which one half of an electron remains are each bounded above and below by positive universal constants.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For the full nonrelativistic Coulomb model with two electron spin states, proves that a molecule with M fixed nuclei of charges at least one and total charge Z strictly binds at most $Z+CM$ electrons. Neutral-atom first ionization energies and radii containing all but an expected half-electron have universal positive upper and lower bounds. The energy cost of removing m electrons has Thomas–Fermi asymptotics as $m\\to\\infty$ and $Z/m\\to\\infty$; neutral-atom outer radii have the corresponding iterated-limit asymptotics, taking $Z\\to\\infty$ first.","pdfPath":"preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-foundations.tex","preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-localization.tex","preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-screening.tex","preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/figures/patch.tex","preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-observations.tex","preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/05b-maximum.tex","preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-molecular-voids.tex","preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-molecular-barriers.tex","preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-molecular-comparison.tex","preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-molecular-bound.tex","preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-atomic-comparison.tex","preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/11-atomic-propagation.tex","preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/12-neutral-sectors.tex","preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/13-ionization-gap.tex","preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/14-outer-radius.tex","preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/15-interfaces.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that a molecule with $M$ fixed nuclei of real charges at least one and total nuclear charge $Z$ strictly binds at most $Z+CM$ electrons, where $C$ is universal. The result holds for the full nonrelativistic Coulomb Hamiltonian with two spin states and arbitrary distinct nuclear positions. For neutral atoms with integer nuclear charge $Z\\ge1$, the first ionization energy and, for every ground state, the radius outside which one half of an electron remains are each bounded above and below by positive universal constants.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":596},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and the results","sourceOffset":1297},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the scales of the problem","sourceOffset":4627},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Obstacles and the common argument","sourceOffset":9693},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and normalization","sourceOffset":14386},{"kind":"section","title":"A ground state at the minimum over all sectors","sourceOffset":15885},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Coulomb form and strict binding","sourceOffset":16303},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A coarse number bound","sourceOffset":20946},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Atomic sectors and neutral ground states","sourceOffset":25925},{"kind":"section","title":"Common localization and quantum comparisons","sourceOffset":30534},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coulomb energy and a kinetic bound","sourceOffset":31247},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cuts, conditional states, and the exact energy identity","sourceOffset":38088}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform excess charge","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a universal finite constant $C$ such that every nuclear system specified above and every integer $n\\ge1$ satisfy \\[ E_n<E_{n-1}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n\\le Z+CM. \\] Moreover, $E_n=E_{n-1}$ whenever $n>Z+CM$. For one nucleus this gives $n\\le Z+C$ under strict binding, for every real $Z\\ge1$. The same constant is independent of all charges and nuclear positions.","sourceOffset":2225,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform outer half-electron radius","label":"thm:radius","text":"There are universal constants $0<c\\le C<\\infty$ such that, for every integer $Z\\ge1$ and every normalized ground state of $H_{Z,Z}$, \\[ c\\le R(\\Psi_Z)\\le C. \\]","sourceOffset":3631,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/263.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":268766,"sourceFileCount":18,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"263-02","slug":"Generalized-ionization-energies-for-full-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026","familyId":"263","title":"Generalized ionization energies for full Coulomb atoms","abstract":"We prove the energy part of the generalized ionization conjecture for full nonrelativistic Coulomb atoms with two electron spin states. The energy needed to remove m electrons is asymptotic to $a_{\\mathrm{TF}}m^{7/3}$ whenever $m\\to\\infty$ and $Z/m\\to\\infty$, with the Thomas–Fermi constant in atomic units. We also obtain both conjectured iterated limits.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For the full nonrelativistic Coulomb model with two electron spin states, proves that a molecule with M fixed nuclei of charges at least one and total charge Z strictly binds at most $Z+CM$ electrons. Neutral-atom first ionization energies and radii containing all but an expected half-electron have universal positive upper and lower bounds. The energy cost of removing m electrons has Thomas–Fermi asymptotics as $m\\to\\infty$ and $Z/m\\to\\infty$; neutral-atom outer radii have the corresponding iterated-limit asymptotics, taking $Z\\to\\infty$ first.","pdfPath":"preprints/Generalized-ionization-energies-for-full-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Generalized-ionization-energies-for-full-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Generalized-ionization-energies-for-full-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Generalized-ionization-energies-for-full-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Generalized-ionization-energies-for-full-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","preprints/Generalized-ionization-energies-for-full-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/foundation-locators.tex","preprints/Generalized-ionization-energies-for-full-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Generalized-ionization-energies-for-full-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-pricing.tex","preprints/Generalized-ionization-energies-for-full-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-comparison.tex","preprints/Generalized-ionization-energies-for-full-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-barriers.tex","preprints/Generalized-ionization-energies-for-full-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/figures/barrier-step.tex","preprints/Generalized-ionization-energies-for-full-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-limit.tex","preprints/Generalized-ionization-energies-for-full-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-fixed-sectors.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Generalized-ionization-energies-for-full-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the energy part of the generalized ionization conjecture for full nonrelativistic Coulomb atoms with two electron spin states. The energy needed to remove $m$ electrons is asymptotic to $a_{\\TF}m^{7/3}$ whenever $m\\to\\infty$ and $Z/m\\to\\infty$, with the Thomas--Fermi constant in atomic units. We also obtain both conjectured iterated limits.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":443},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The theorem and its normalization","sourceOffset":2993},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A price per electron","sourceOffset":5985},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Analytic precedents and proof structure","sourceOffset":7479},{"kind":"section","title":"Priced sectors and an exterior cutoff","sourceOffset":11819},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Imported estimates and their hypotheses","sourceOffset":13636},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The rough charge deficit","sourceOffset":19542},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A cutoff at one fixed exterior scale","sourceOffset":21350},{"kind":"section","title":"Conditional density and field comparison at positive price","sourceOffset":25472},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Nested observations and their energy cost","sourceOffset":26408},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Statement and preliminary bounds","sourceOffset":30345},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A fine patch comparison with the price included","sourceOffset":35635}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Generalized ionization energy","label":"thm:main","text":"For full nonrelativistic Coulomb atoms with two electron spin states, \\begin{equation} \\frac{I_m(Z)}{m^{7/3}}\\longrightarrow a_{\\TF}>0 \\quad\\text{whenever}\\quad m\\longrightarrow\\infty, \\qquad \\frac Zm\\longrightarrow\\infty, \\end{equation} where $Z>m\\ge1$ are integers. In particular, \\begin{align} \\lim_{m\\to\\infty} \\frac{\\limsup_{Z\\to\\infty} I_m(Z)}{m^{7/3}} &=a_{\\TF},\\\\ \\lim_{m\\to\\infty} \\frac{\\liminf_{Z\\to\\infty} I_m(Z)}{m^{7/3}} &=a_{\\TF}. \\end{align} The inner limits are taken over integer $Z$ with $m$ fixed. No convergence of $I_m(Z)$ at a fixed $m$ is asserted or assumed.","sourceOffset":4246,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Deficit at a prescribed price","label":"thm:priced-deficit","text":"There is a universal number $q>0$ such that, for every sequence satisfying \\[ \\lambda=s^{-4},\\qquad s\\longrightarrow0, \\qquad Zs^3\\longrightarrow\\infty, \\] and every choice of $n$ minimizing $E_n+\\lambda n$, one has \\[ s^3(Z-n)\\longrightarrow q. \\] The number $q$ is the exterior Coulomb charge of the unique classical solution, tending uniformly to zero at infinity, of \\[ \\Delta F=4\\pi k\\bigl((F-1)_+\\bigr)^{3/2} \\quad\\text{on }\\R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}, \\qquad k=(5T/3)^{-3/2}, \\] that is bounded above and below by positive multiples of $|x|^{-4}$ near zero. The density $k((F-1)_+)^{3/2}$ has bounded support, and $F(x)=q/|x|$ outside that support. Moreover, \\[ a_{\\TF}=\\frac37q^{-4/3}. \\]","sourceOffset":6696,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/263.md","scopeText":"The formalization gives the large-ionization asymptotics for the full nonrelativistic two-spin Coulomb atom. Let $I_m(Z)$ be the energy needed to remove $m$ electrons from a neutral atom of nuclear charge $Z$. There is a positive coefficient $a_{\\mathrm{TF}}$ with the Thomas–Fermi variational characterization such that $I_m(Z)/m^{7/3}\\to a_{\\mathrm{TF}}$ whenever $m\\to\\infty$ and $Z/m\\to\\infty$, with integers $Z>m\\ge1$. The corresponding iterated limsup and liminf limits hold with $Z\\to\\infty$ first. The energy uses the full antisymmetric Sobolev form domain; fixed-$m$ convergence and ground-state attainment are outside this statement.\n\nFor neutral Coulomb atoms with two electron spin states, the formalization proves the asymptotic law for generalized outer-electron radii defined by an exp","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/263.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":105070,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Generalized-ionization-energies-for-full-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Generalized-ionization-energies-for-full-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"263-03","slug":"Generalized-outer-electron-radii-of-neutral-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026","familyId":"263","title":"Generalized outer-electron radii of neutral Coulomb atoms","abstract":"We prove the radius part of the generalized ionization conjecture for neutral nonrelativistic Coulomb atoms with two spin states. For every choice of ground states, the upper and lower large-nuclear-charge limits of the radius defined by an expected exterior electron mass m are both asymptotic to $(81\\pi^2/2)^{1/3}m^{-1/3}$ as m tends to infinity.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For the full nonrelativistic Coulomb model with two electron spin states, proves that a molecule with M fixed nuclei of charges at least one and total charge Z strictly binds at most $Z+CM$ electrons. Neutral-atom first ionization energies and radii containing all but an expected half-electron have universal positive upper and lower bounds. The energy cost of removing m electrons has Thomas–Fermi asymptotics as $m\\to\\infty$ and $Z/m\\to\\infty$; neutral-atom outer radii have the corresponding iterated-limit asymptotics, taking $Z\\to\\infty$ first.","pdfPath":"preprints/Generalized-outer-electron-radii-of-neutral-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Generalized-outer-electron-radii-of-neutral-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Generalized-outer-electron-radii-of-neutral-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Generalized-outer-electron-radii-of-neutral-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Generalized-outer-electron-radii-of-neutral-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/companion-locators.tex","preprints/Generalized-outer-electron-radii-of-neutral-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Generalized-outer-electron-radii-of-neutral-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-inputs.tex","preprints/Generalized-outer-electron-radii-of-neutral-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-scales.tex","preprints/Generalized-outer-electron-radii-of-neutral-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/figures/scales.tex","preprints/Generalized-outer-electron-radii-of-neutral-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-profile.tex","preprints/Generalized-outer-electron-radii-of-neutral-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-tails.tex","preprints/Generalized-outer-electron-radii-of-neutral-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-interface.tex","preprints/Generalized-outer-electron-radii-of-neutral-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Generalized-outer-electron-radii-of-neutral-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the radius part of the generalized ionization conjecture for neutral nonrelativistic Coulomb atoms with two spin states. For every choice of ground states, the upper and lower large-nuclear-charge limits of the radius defined by an expected exterior electron mass $m$ are both asymptotic to $(81\\pi^2/2)^{1/3}m^{-1/3}$ as $m$ tends to infinity.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":441},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Statement of the result","sourceOffset":924},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and scope","sourceOffset":2961},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof and reusable ingredients","sourceOffset":6194},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Notation","sourceOffset":9068},{"kind":"section","title":"Conditional screening inputs","sourceOffset":9561},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Energy offset and count bounds","sourceOffset":9927},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One family of observations and packets","sourceOffset":11409},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The inverse comparison","sourceOffset":14680},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Intermediate subsolutions","sourceOffset":16050},{"kind":"section","title":"Retained data on expanding annuli","sourceOffset":18684},{"kind":"section","title":"The unique neutral limiting profile","sourceOffset":27958}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Generalized ionization conjecture: radii","label":"intro:main","text":"Set $b_{\\mathrm{TF}}=(81\\pi^2/2)^{1/3}$. For every choice of normalized neutral ground states $(\\Psi_Z)_{Z\\ge1}$ of \\eqref{intro:hamiltonian}, \\begin{align} \\lim_{m\\to\\infty}m^{1/3} \\left[\\limsup_{\\substack{Z\\to\\infty\\\\ Z>m}}R_m(\\Psi_Z)\\right] &=b_{\\mathrm{TF}},\\\\ \\lim_{m\\to\\infty}m^{1/3} \\left[\\liminf_{\\substack{Z\\to\\infty\\\\ Z>m}}R_m(\\Psi_Z)\\right] &=b_{\\mathrm{TF}}. \\end{align} All limits are over integers. The inner limits hold $m$ fixed; no convergence in $Z$ at a fixed $m$ is assumed.","sourceOffset":2002,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Uniform offset and raw counts","label":"inp:energy-counts","text":"There are constants $D_0,Z_0<\\infty$ such that every normalized neutral ground state with $Z\\ge Z_0$ has offset at most $D_0$. For every fixed $0<\\alpha<\\beta<\\infty$ and every $u>0$, its raw electron configuration satisfies \\begin{equation} \\begin{aligned} \\left\\|\\#\\{i:\\alpha u<|x_i|<\\beta u\\}\\right\\|_{L^2(\\Prob)} &\\le C_{\\alpha,\\beta} \\bigl(\\max\\{u^{-3},1\\}+\\sqrt{D_0u}\\bigr),\\\\ \\E\\#\\{i:|x_i|>1\\}&\\le C(1+\\sqrt{D_0}). \\end{aligned} \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":10246,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/263.md","scopeText":"The formalization gives the large-ionization asymptotics for the full nonrelativistic two-spin Coulomb atom. Let $I_m(Z)$ be the energy needed to remove $m$ electrons from a neutral atom of nuclear charge $Z$. There is a positive coefficient $a_{\\mathrm{TF}}$ with the Thomas–Fermi variational characterization such that $I_m(Z)/m^{7/3}\\to a_{\\mathrm{TF}}$ whenever $m\\to\\infty$ and $Z/m\\to\\infty$, with integers $Z>m\\ge1$. The corresponding iterated limsup and liminf limits hold with $Z\\to\\infty$ first. The energy uses the full antisymmetric Sobolev form domain; fixed-$m$ convergence and ground-state attainment are outside this statement.\n\nFor neutral Coulomb atoms with two electron spin states, the formalization proves the asymptotic law for generalized outer-electron radii defined by an exp","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/263.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":57073,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Generalized-outer-electron-radii-of-neutral-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Generalized-outer-electron-radii-of-neutral-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Generalized-outer-electron-radii-of-neutral-Coulomb-atoms-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"264-01","slug":"Generic-Future-Inextendibility-with-Square-Integrable-Connection-Near-a-Fixed-Kerr-Spacetime-September-23-2026","familyId":"264","title":"Generic Future Inextendibility with Square-Integrable Connection Near a Fixed Kerr Spacetime","abstract":"For each fixed rotating subextremal Kerr background with mass M > 0 and rotation $0\\lt |\\mathfrak a|\\lt M$, we prove that the smooth vacuum data near its complete two-ended bridge whose full maximal globally hyperbolic development admits a future $C^0\\cap W^{1,2}_{\\mathrm{loc}}$ extension form a meagre set in the weighted smooth topology. The extension metric is continuous and nondegenerate, and its weak connection is locally square-integrable. The neighborhood requires smallness of only the tenth weighted seminorm, while the topology tests every finite order.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves local strong cosmic censorship near each fixed rotating subextremal Kerr bridge. A dense Gδ subset of a weighted smooth neighborhood of smooth complete two-ended asymptotically flat vacuum data has full maximal globally hyperbolic developments with no future continuous nondegenerate extension whose weak connection is locally square-integrable. No symmetry is imposed; extensions need not satisfy the vacuum equations.","pdfPath":"preprints/Generic-Future-Inextendibility-with-Square-Integrable-Connection-Near-a-Fixed-Kerr-Spacetime-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Generic-Future-Inextendibility-with-Square-Integrable-Connection-Near-a-Fixed-Kerr-Spacetime-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Generic-Future-Inextendibility-with-Square-Integrable-Connection-Near-a-Fixed-Kerr-Spacetime-September-23-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Generic-Future-Inextendibility-with-Square-Integrable-Connection-Near-a-Fixed-Kerr-Spacetime-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Generic-Future-Inextendibility-with-Square-Integrable-Connection-Near-a-Fixed-Kerr-Spacetime-September-23-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/Generic-Future-Inextendibility-with-Square-Integrable-Connection-Near-a-Fixed-Kerr-Spacetime-September-23-2026/build/companion-references.tex","preprints/Generic-Future-Inextendibility-with-Square-Integrable-Connection-Near-a-Fixed-Kerr-Spacetime-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Generic-Future-Inextendibility-with-Square-Integrable-Connection-Near-a-Fixed-Kerr-Spacetime-September-23-2026/build/sections/background.tex","preprints/Generic-Future-Inextendibility-with-Square-Integrable-Connection-Near-a-Fixed-Kerr-Spacetime-September-23-2026/build/sections/weak_exits.tex","preprints/Generic-Future-Inextendibility-with-Square-Integrable-Connection-Near-a-Fixed-Kerr-Spacetime-September-23-2026/build/figures/weak_exit.tex","preprints/Generic-Future-Inextendibility-with-Square-Integrable-Connection-Near-a-Fixed-Kerr-Spacetime-September-23-2026/build/sections/packet_preparation.tex","preprints/Generic-Future-Inextendibility-with-Square-Integrable-Connection-Near-a-Fixed-Kerr-Spacetime-September-23-2026/build/sections/nonlinear_comparison.tex","preprints/Generic-Future-Inextendibility-with-Square-Integrable-Connection-Near-a-Fixed-Kerr-Spacetime-September-23-2026/build/sections/attachment_signal.tex","preprints/Generic-Future-Inextendibility-with-Square-Integrable-Connection-Near-a-Fixed-Kerr-Spacetime-September-23-2026/build/sections/holonomy_category.tex","preprints/Generic-Future-Inextendibility-with-Square-Integrable-Connection-Near-a-Fixed-Kerr-Spacetime-September-23-2026/build/figures/averaged_loops.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Generic-Future-Inextendibility-with-Square-Integrable-Connection-Near-a-Fixed-Kerr-Spacetime-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For each fixed rotating subextremal Kerr background with mass $M>0$ and rotation $0<|\\mathfrak a|<M$, we prove that the smooth vacuum data near its complete two-ended bridge whose full maximal globally hyperbolic development admits a future $C^0\\cap W^{1,2}_{\\mathrm{loc}}$ extension form a meagre set in the weighted smooth topology. The extension metric is continuous and nondegenerate, and its weak connection is locally square-integrable. The neighborhood requires smallness of only the tenth weighted seminorm, while the topology tests every finite order.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":623},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Data and future extensions","sourceOffset":1265},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and scope","sourceOffset":6784},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The new arguments","sourceOffset":12299},{"kind":"section","title":"Background geometry and finite branch tubes","sourceOffset":16124},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Normalization of a fixed Kerr center","sourceOffset":16431},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The comparison development","sourceOffset":19107},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Seeds, section measure, and first runout","sourceOffset":29347},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Open branch tubes and launch feet","sourceOffset":31631},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite smooth persistence","sourceOffset":40363},{"kind":"section","title":"From an arbitrary weak exit to countably many tests","sourceOffset":41406},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Intrinsic diamonds and a first-exit graph","sourceOffset":42413}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed $M>0$ and $0<|\\mathfrak a|<M$, there is $\\eps=\\eps(M,\\mathfrak a)>0$ such that the set of $d\\in\\Ueps$ for which the full development $(M_d,g_d)$ admits a future extension in the sense of Definition \\ref{intro:extension} is meagre in $\\Ueps$. Consequently, future $C^0\\cap W^{1,2}_{\\mathrm{loc}}$-inextendible data contain a residual subset of $\\Ueps$ in the weighted smooth topology \\eqref{intro:seminorms}.","sourceOffset":5405,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Background estimates","label":"prop:bg-package","text":"For every $d\\in\\Ueps$, the comparison geometry has the following properties. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item The metrics $\\gamma^{\\pm1}$ and $\\mathcal A^{\\pm1}$ are bounded in angular $H^6$, and $\\xi_i,a^{-1}\\partial_vb$ are bounded in angular $H^5$. There are nonnegative measurable functions $g_i:\\R\\to[0,\\infty)$ with \\begin{equation} \\sum_{n\\in\\Z}\\sup_{[n,n+1]}g_i<\\infty \\end{equation} such that \\begin{equation} \\norm{\\chi_i}_{H^4}+\\norm{\\ell_i+\\kappa}_{H^4} +\\norm{B_i}_{H^5} \\le C\\bigl(q+g_i(y_i)\\bigr). \\end{equation} The corresponding first pure longitudinal derivatives $\\mathfrak L_i\\chi_i,L_i\\ell_i$, and $\\partial_u b$ in direction $0$, have directional summable bounds through angular $H^2$, after enlarging the $g_i$. \\item The first ordinary longitudinal jets satisfy \\begin{equation} \\begin{gathered} \\norm b_{H^5}+\\sum_i\\norm{(\\chi_i,\\ell_i+\\kappa)}_{H^4}\\to0,\\\\ \\sum_{k=0}^1\\left(\\norm{\\partial_{y_k}b}_{H^2} +\\sum_i\\norm{\\partial_{y_k}(\\chi_i,\\ell_i)}_{H^2}\\right)\\to0,\\\\ \\sum_{k=0}^1\\left(\\norm{\\partial_{y_k}\\gamma}_{H^4} +\\norm{\\partial_{y_k}\\log a+\\kappa}_{H^4}\\right)\\to0 \\end{gathered} \\end{equation} as $t\\to\\infty$ and $|u|,|v|\\to\\infty$. The displayed norms are bounded everywhere in the wedge. \\item For fixed $U\\in\\R$, $C>0$, and each fixed ordinary coordinate derivative order, the fields \\begin{equation} \\gamma^{\\pm1},\\quad\\log\\mathcal A,\\quad b, \\quad a^{-1}\\partial_vb \\end{equation} have $e^{o(T)}$ bounds on \\begin{equation} u\\le U,\\qquad v\\le","sourceOffset":25060,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Future extension","label":"intro:extension","text":"A future $C^0\\cap W^{1,2}_{\\mathrm{loc}}$ extension of $(M_d,g_d)$ consists of a connected time-oriented smooth four-manifold $\\widetilde M$, a continuous nondegenerate Lorentzian metric $\\widetilde g$ of signature $(-,+,+,+)$ whose components belong to $W^{1,2}_{\\mathrm{loc}}$ in smooth charts, and a smooth time-orientation-preserving isometric embedding $\\iota:M_d\\hookrightarrow\\widetilde M$ with proper open image. There must also be a future-directed timelike $C^1$ curve $c:[0,1]\\to\\widetilde M$ such that \\[ c([0,1))\\subset\\iota(M_d), \\qquad c(1)\\in\\partial\\iota(M_d). \\] No field equation o","sourceOffset":3162,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Necessary existential extension tests","label":"def:exit-test","text":"For $O\\in\\mathcal B$ and $B\\in\\N$, $B\\ge1$, let $E(O,B)\\subset\\Ueps$ consist of the data satisfying both of the following requirements. \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\roman*)] \\item There is an extension of $M_d$ of the regularity and type specified in Definition \\ref{intro:extension}, and a measurable $S\\subset O$ with \\begin{equation} \\mu_d(S)>.99\\,\\mu_d(O), \\end{equation} such that every seed in $S$ has finite full future lifetime. There are at most $B$ precompact ambient charts $\\psi_j:U_j\\to V_j$ witnessing \\Cref{ex:chart-witness} along each entire such geodesic, from $\\Sigma$ to its full endp","sourceOffset":65505,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":218171,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Generic-Future-Inextendibility-with-Square-Integrable-Connection-Near-a-Fixed-Kerr-Spacetime-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"264-02","slug":"Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026","familyId":"264","title":"Generic C1 Future Inextendibility Near Rotating Subextremal Kerr Spacetimes","abstract":"For each fixed Kerr spacetime with mass M > 0 and rotation $0\\lt \\mathfrak a\\lt M$, we prove that a dense Gδ set of nearby smooth, complete two-ended vacuum data has a maximal globally hyperbolic development with no future C1 extension. The neighborhood and genericity are defined in a weighted smooth topology, and ambient extensions may be nonvacuum.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves local strong cosmic censorship near each fixed rotating subextremal Kerr bridge. A dense Gδ subset of a weighted smooth neighborhood of smooth complete two-ended asymptotically flat vacuum data has full maximal globally hyperbolic developments with no future continuous nondegenerate extension whose weak connection is locally square-integrable. No symmetry is imposed; extensions need not satisfy the vacuum equations.","pdfPath":"preprints/Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026/build/companion-references.tex","preprints/Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026/build/figures/proof-map.tex","preprints/Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-phase-space.tex","preprints/Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-tests.tex","preprints/Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-background.tex","preprints/Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-preparation.tex","preprints/Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-energy.tex","preprints/Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-attachment.tex","preprints/Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-holonomy.tex","preprints/Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026/build/figures/flow-loop.tex","preprints/Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026/build/sections/09-proof.tex","preprints/Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026/build/sections/10-supplement.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For each fixed Kerr spacetime with mass \\(M>0\\) and rotation \\(0<\\mathfrak a<M\\), we prove that a dense \\(G_\\delta\\) set of nearby smooth, complete two-ended vacuum data has a maximal globally hyperbolic development with no future \\(C^1\\) extension. The neighborhood and genericity are defined in a weighted smooth topology, and ambient extensions may be nonvacuum.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":428},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why extension regularity matters","sourceOffset":2167},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The foundation and the two-sign construction","sourceOffset":5671},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From one future exit to one finite contradiction","sourceOffset":8652},{"kind":"section","title":"The constraint space and the theorem","sourceOffset":14005},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Mass normalization","sourceOffset":21021},{"kind":"section","title":"Closed transport tests and selection of a branch","sourceOffset":21704},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Seeds, positive norms, and the passing sets","sourceOffset":22439},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Covering future exits","sourceOffset":28678},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison runout and the corner estimates","sourceOffset":41529},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Null sections and exclusion of corner seeds","sourceOffset":49364},{"kind":"section","title":"The all-spin background and the constrained linear packet","sourceOffset":57660}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"phase:main","text":"For each fixed \\(M>0\\) and \\(0<\\mathfrak a<M\\), there is \\(\\eps_0=\\eps_0(M,\\mathfrak a)>0\\) such that \\(\\mathcal U_{\\eps_0}\\) contains a dense \\(G_\\delta\\) subset \\(\\mathcal G\\) for which every \\(\\mathcal M_d\\), \\(d\\in\\mathcal G\\), has no future \\(C^1\\) extension in the sense of Definition \\ref{phase:extension}.","sourceOffset":18202,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Coverage of future $C^1$ exits","label":"test:exit-cover","text":"If $\\mathcal M_d$, $d\\in\\mathcal U$, admits a future $C^1$ extension in the stated sense, then $d\\in\\mathcal A(O,B)$ for some $O\\in\\mathcal O$ and some integer $B\\ge1$.","sourceOffset":34090,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Weighted constraint space","label":"phase:space","text":"For smooth symmetric two-tensors \\(f,j\\) set \\begin{equation} p_m(f,j)=\\sum_{i=0}^m \\left\\{\\sup_\\Sigma \\rho^{1+i}|\\nabla_*^if|_* +\\sup_\\Sigma \\rho^{2+i}|\\nabla_*^ij|_*\\right\\}, \\qquad m=0,1,\\ldots. \\end{equation} Let \\(\\mathcal D\\) consist of smooth pairs \\(d=(h,K)\\) such that \\(h\\) is positive definite and complete, every \\(p_m(h-h_*,K-K_*)\\) is finite, and \\begin{equation} R(h)+(\\tr_hK)^2-|K|_h^2=0,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}_hK-\\dd(\\tr_hK)=0. \\end{equation} The topology on \\(\\mathcal D\\) is the relative topology of the seminorms \\eqref{phase:seminorms}. For \\(\\eps>0\\) let \\[ \\mathcal U_\\eps=\\","sourceOffset":14853,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Future \\(C^1\\) extension","label":"phase:extension","text":"A future \\(C^1\\) extension of \\(\\mathcal M_d\\) is a smooth, time-orientation-preserving isometric embedding \\(\\iota:\\mathcal M_d\\longrightarrow\\widetilde{\\mathcal M}\\) onto a proper open subset of a connected, time-oriented smooth four-manifold with nondegenerate \\(C^1\\) Lorentzian metric \\(\\widetilde g\\), together with a future-directed timelike \\(C^1\\) curve \\(c:[0,1]\\to\\widetilde{\\mathcal M}\\) such that \\[ c([0,1))\\subset\\iota(\\mathcal M_d),\\qquad c(1)\\in\\partial\\iota(\\mathcal M_d). \\] Neither the vacuum equations nor global hyperbolicity are imposed on the ambient spacetime.","sourceOffset":17527,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":255332,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":4,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Generic-C1-Future-Inextendibility-Near-Rotating-Subextremal-Kerr-Spacetimes-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"264-03","slug":"Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026","familyId":"264","title":"Quantitative Near-Kerr Evolution and Generic C2 Future Inextendibility","abstract":"For every fixed rotating subextremal Kerr bridge, we prove that smooth vacuum data admitting a C2 future extension of their full maximal globally hyperbolic development form a meagre set in a neighborhood defined by one finite-order seminorm. The topology allows arbitrary symbol-bounded asymptotically flat tails and imposes no symmetry.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves local strong cosmic censorship near each fixed rotating subextremal Kerr bridge. A dense Gδ subset of a weighted smooth neighborhood of smooth complete two-ended asymptotically flat vacuum data has full maximal globally hyperbolic developments with no future continuous nondegenerate extension whose weak connection is locally square-integrable. No symmetry is imposed; extensions need not satisfy the vacuum equations.","pdfPath":"preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/build/sections/history-fragment.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-category.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-geometry.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-stationary.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-compact.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-far.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-interior.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-comparison.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/build/sections/09-packet.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/build/appendices/far-energy.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/build/appendices/far-sources.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/build/appendices/triangular.tex","preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/build/appendices/constraints.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every fixed rotating subextremal Kerr bridge, we prove that smooth vacuum data admitting a \\(C^2\\) future extension of their full maximal globally hyperbolic development form a meagre set in a neighborhood defined by one finite-order seminorm. The topology allows arbitrary symbol-bounded asymptotically flat tails and imposes no symmetry.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":406},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The precise statement","sourceOffset":1342},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and relation to earlier work","sourceOffset":4657},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From regular exits to finite observations","sourceOffset":11371},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Scaling and conventions","sourceOffset":20576},{"kind":"section","title":"Closed tests and the finite-observation reduction","sourceOffset":22146},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Seeds and curvature measurements","sourceOffset":23962},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From an arbitrary extension to regular exits","sourceOffset":26712},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The analytic task","sourceOffset":31769},{"kind":"section","title":"Kerr geometry and spatial microlocal separation","sourceOffset":33181},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The stationary slicing and its ends","sourceOffset":33902},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Radial dynamics and the horizon source","sourceOffset":38761}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Local \\(C^2\\) strong cosmic censorship","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed \\(M>0\\) and \\(0<\\mathfrak a<M\\), there exists \\(\\epsilon>0\\) such that the data in \\[ \\mathcal U_\\epsilon =\\{d\\in\\mathcal D:p_{10}(d-(h_*,K_*))<\\epsilon\\} \\] whose full maximal globally hyperbolic vacuum developments admit a \\(C^2\\) future extension form a meagre subset of \\(\\mathcal U_\\epsilon\\). Equivalently, the future-inextendible data contain a countable intersection of relatively open dense subsets of \\(\\mathcal U_\\epsilon\\).","sourceOffset":3643,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Actual-principal comparison","label":"co2:actual-comparison","text":"For each level $(U,s,A_i)$ in \\eqref{co2:levels}, let $d_M^{\\alpha\\beta}$ be the change from $\\bar g$ to $g$ of the differential order-two wave coefficient in stationary normalization. On fixed compact reaches the reduced equation has the form \\begin{equation} \\mathbf L_{p(t),c(t)}U+d_M^{\\alpha\\beta}\\partial_{\\alpha\\beta}U =S_i^{\\rm lin}+S_i^{\\rm err},\\qquad S_i^{\\rm lin}=\\pm Q(\\partial_t)b_i,\\qquad b_i=a,a,a', \\end{equation} up to common normalizations, where \\begin{equation} S_i^{\\rm err}\\in L^1_{A_i}H^{s-1}+L^2_{A_i}H^{s-1/2}, \\qquad \\|S_i^{\\rm err}\\|\\le C_{\\rm fix}\\mathsf N^2 +C_{\\rm fix}e_{\\rm in}. \\end{equation} On larger bounded differential annuli only $L^2_{A_i}H^{s-1}$ is required. There is an actual-principal correction of the frozen energy whose additional integrated derivative errors are bounded in squared estimates by $C_{\\rm fix}\\mathsf N^3$, up to input terms. The frozen half-loss bulk and directional improvement remain on the left side of that estimate.","sourceOffset":189191,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Future extension","label":"intro:extension","text":"A \\(C^2\\) future extension of \\((M_d,g_d)\\) is a connected time-oriented smooth four-manifold \\(\\widetilde M\\) with a nondegenerate \\(C^2\\) Lorentzian metric \\(\\widetilde g\\), and a smooth time-orientation-preserving isometric embedding \\(\\iota:M_d\\longrightarrow\\widetilde M\\) onto a proper open subset, such that a future-directed timelike \\(C^1\\) curve \\(c:[0,1]\\longrightarrow\\widetilde M\\) satisfies \\[ c([0,1))\\subset\\iota(M_d),\\qquad c(1)\\in\\partial\\iota(M_d). \\]","sourceOffset":2817,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":557569,"sourceFileCount":17,"statementCount":38,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Quantitative-Near-Kerr-Evolution-and-Generic-C2-Future-Inextendibility-September-23-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"265-01","slug":"A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026","familyId":"265","title":"A two-dimensional area law from a global spectral gap","abstract":"We prove an entropy area law for the unique ground state of a finite-range Hamiltonian on any finite induced subgraph of the square lattice. A lower bound on the spectral gap of the full Hamiltonian and fixed bounds on the local dimension, interaction range, and interaction strength suffice. For every set of sites, its entanglement entropy is bounded by a constant times the number of edges crossing its boundary, independently of the size and shape of the domain.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves an entropy area law for unique ground states of finite-range Hamiltonians on arbitrary finite induced square-lattice domains, using only a uniform full-system spectral gap and bounds on the local interactions. On open $L\\times L$ squares, uniformly gapped nearest-neighbor ground states also admit projected entangled-pair state approximations with polynomial bond dimension and global vector error at most L−1.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-history.tex","preprints/A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-preliminaries.tex","preprints/A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-initial.tex","preprints/A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-quasilocal.tex","preprints/A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-conditional.tex","preprints/A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-replicas.tex","preprints/A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-transport.tex","preprints/A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-comparators.tex","preprints/A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-scanner.tex","preprints/A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-amplification.tex","preprints/A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-geometry.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove an entropy area law for the unique ground state of a finite-range Hamiltonian on any finite induced subgraph of the square lattice. A lower bound on the spectral gap of the full Hamiltonian and fixed bounds on the local dimension, interaction range, and interaction strength suffice. For every set of sites, its entanglement entropy is bounded by a constant times the number of edges crossing its boundary, independently of the size and shape of the domain.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":540},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The setting and the theorem","sourceOffset":1000},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and related work","sourceOffset":4242},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A tensor-network consequence","sourceOffset":11594},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the proof is organized","sourceOffset":13327},{"kind":"section","title":"Entropy and induced-graph locality","sourceOffset":18791},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Entropy identities and continuity","sourceOffset":19162},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local counting and degenerate cases","sourceOffset":22309},{"kind":"section","title":"Marginal tails and an initial box estimate","sourceOffset":25242},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A marginal spectral estimate","sourceOffset":26012},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An entropy discount from a buffer","sourceOffset":34231},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The first subvolume exponent","sourceOffset":49270}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Area law","label":"thm:area","text":"For every $q,R,J$ as above and every $\\Delta>0$, there is a finite constant $C=C(q,R,J,\\Delta)$ with the following property. Suppose that $\\Lambda$ is a finite induced square-lattice domain and that the Hamiltonian \\eqref{eq:hamiltonian} has a unique normalized ground vector $\\Omega$, up to phase, with ground energy $E_0$, and satisfies \\[ H-E_0\\id\\ \\ge\\ \\Delta\\bigl(\\id-\\ket{\\Omega}\\bra{\\Omega}\\bigr). \\] Then for every $A\\subseteq\\Lambda$, \\begin{equation} S_\\Omega(A)\\le C\\,|\\partial_\\Lambda A|. \\end{equation} The same constant applies to all such domains, sets, and Hamiltonians.","sourceOffset":2269,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:rectangles","text":"The conclusion of Theorem \\ref{thm:area} holds for every subset of a finite rectangular square lattice with bounded on-site and nearest-neighbor interactions and a unique ground state separated by a fixed global gap. For every domain covered by that theorem it also implies \\[ S_\\Omega(A)\\le4C|\\partial_{\\rm in}A|, \\qquad S_\\Omega(A)\\le2C|Z_A|. \\]","sourceOffset":3464,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"A template","label":"scanner:template","text":"Let \\(s_0\\ge1\\) and \\(n\\) be positive integers. A template with parameters \\((n,s_0)\\) is a nonempty finite set \\(T\\subset\\Z^2\\) of the form \\[ T=\\bigcup_{a=1}^{N_0}(P_a\\cap\\Z^2), \\qquad n\\ge C_{\\rm tpl}N_0(s_0+1), \\qquad \\diam_\\infty T\\le n, \\] where \\(N_0\\ge1\\) is an integer and each \\(P_a\\) is a closed convex rectangle or triangle of sup-norm diameter at most \\(s_0\\), all of whose sides have slope \\(0,\\infty,1\\), or \\(-1\\). Empty sampled pieces can be omitted. For the fixed cut \\(A\\), the template is separated from the cut if \\[ \\dist_\\infty(T,Z)>4D_0s_0. \\] As above, \\(T_j\\) is the ambient","sourceOffset":229916,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":310628,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/A-two-dimensional-area-law-from-a-global-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"265-02","slug":"Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026","familyId":"265","title":"Polynomial PEPS approximation of gapped square-grid ground states","abstract":"We prove that the unique ground state of a uniformly gapped nearest-neighbor Hamiltonian on an $L\\times L$ square lattice admits a projected entangled-pair state approximation with bond dimension polynomial in L and global vector error at most L−1 after normalization. Only the gap of the full Hamiltonian is assumed. The result is an existence theorem, with constants uniform over Hamiltonians of fixed local dimension, interaction strength, and gap.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves an entropy area law for unique ground states of finite-range Hamiltonians on arbitrary finite induced square-lattice domains, using only a uniform full-system spectral gap and bounds on the local interactions. On open $L\\times L$ squares, uniformly gapped nearest-neighbor ground states also admit projected entangled-pair state approximations with polynomial bond dimension and global vector error at most L−1.","pdfPath":"preprints/Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-history.tex","preprints/Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-information.tex","preprints/Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-patches.tex","preprints/Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-compression.tex","preprints/Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-frames.tex","preprints/Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-geometry.tex","preprints/Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026/build/figures/geometry-band-words.tex","preprints/Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026/build/figures/geometry-point-treatment.tex","preprints/Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-assembly.tex","preprints/Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-companion-interface.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the unique ground state of a uniformly gapped nearest-neighbor Hamiltonian on an $L\\times L$ square lattice admits a projected entangled-pair state approximation with bond dimension polynomial in $L$ and global vector error at most $L^{-1}$ after normalization. Only the gap of the full Hamiltonian is assumed. The result is an existence theorem, with constants uniform over Hamiltonians of fixed local dimension, interaction strength, and gap.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":532},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Statement of the result","sourceOffset":1550},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":3687},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":8987},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and entropy inputs","sourceOffset":14708},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The imported area law","sourceOffset":16353},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite-dimensional information facts","sourceOffset":18005},{"kind":"section","title":"Information bounds for separated regions","sourceOffset":22208},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The coarse collar interface","sourceOffset":23733},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A conditional information bound for a cell","sourceOffset":28976},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reducing a collar information cost with the global gap","sourceOffset":37802},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Regions tapering toward finitely many points","sourceOffset":50687}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Fix an integer $q\\ge2$ and constants $J,\\Delta>0$. There are constants $C,c>0$, depending only on $q,J,\\Delta$, with the following property. For every $L\\ge2$, consider any Hermitian Hamiltonian \\begin{equation} H=\\sum_{v\\in\\Lambda_L}h_v+\\sum_{e\\in E(\\Lambda_L)}h_e, \\qquad \\norm{h_v},\\norm{h_e}\\le J, \\end{equation} where each summand is supported on its designated site or edge. Suppose that $H$ has a unique normalized ground vector $\\Omega$, up to phase, with ground energy $E_0$, and \\begin{equation} H-E_0I\\ge\\Delta\\bigl(I-|\\Omega\\rangle\\langle\\Omega|\\bigr). \\end{equation} Then there is a nonzero PEPS $\\Phi$ on the same grid with maximum bond dimension at most $CL^c$ such that \\begin{equation} \\min_{\\theta\\in\\mathbb R} \\left\\|\\frac{\\Phi}{\\norm{\\Phi}}-e^{i\\theta}\\Omega\\right\\| \\le L^{-1}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":2185,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Entropy area law, {\\cite[Theorem 1.1 and Corollary 1.2","label":"prop:area-law","text":"{RepoAreaLaw}}] Under \\eqref{eq:model}--\\eqref{eq:global-gap}, there is $C_A=C_A(q,J,\\Delta)$ such that every $A\\subseteq\\Lambda$ satisfies \\[ S_\\Omega(A)\\le C_A|\\partial_\\Lambda A|, \\qquad \\partial_\\Lambda A=\\{\\{x,y\\}\\in E(\\Lambda):x\\in A, \\ y\\notin A\\}. \\] The same constant works for every $L$ and every Hamiltonian in the class.","sourceOffset":16595,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Encoded frame","label":"def:frame","text":"An encoded frame $\\mathcal F$ consists of a raw ownership assignment on one sheet, a collection of holes $(c,h)$ with pairwise disjoint outer squares, and a specified party holding each hole's tag. Its encoding $K_{\\mathcal F}$ is the product of the maps \\eqref{eq:hole-encoder}, with a fixed ordering of the newly appended tags, and its reference vector is \\begin{equation} \\Omega_{\\mathcal F}=K_{\\mathcal F}\\Omega. \\end{equation} A frame without holes is a raw sheet. An ownership guide in the plane specifies such an assignment by sampling its labels at the physical sites.","sourceOffset":111113,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":193721,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Polynomial-PEPS-approximation-of-gapped-square-grid-ground-states-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"266-01","slug":"The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026","familyId":"266","title":"The maximum number of mutually unbiased bases in dimension six","abstract":"We prove that the maximum number of mutually unbiased orthonormal bases in ℂ6 is three, resolving Zauner's dimension-six MUB conjecture. The upper bound is computer-assisted: under the stated binary64 arithmetic and compiler conditions, a complete execution of the documented verification pipeline excludes four arbitrary complex bases.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves $N(6)=3$, resolving Zauner's dimension-six mutually unbiased bases conjecture: three such bases exist in ℂ6, but four cannot. The exclusion is a complete certified computation under the stated binary64 arithmetic and compiler conditions. An independent companion proves the Matolcsi–Ruzsa–Weiner Fourier-vanishing conjecture for order-six complex Hadamard matrices outside Tao's cubic equivalence class.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-flow.tex","preprints/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026/build/sections/seed.tex","preprints/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026/build/sections/completion.tex","preprints/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026/build/sections/phase-cover.tex","preprints/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026/build/sections/refinement.tex","preprints/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026/build/sections/exclusion.tex","preprints/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026/build/figures/two-cliques.tex","preprints/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026/build/sections/execution.tex","preprints/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex","preprints/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026/build/sections/rounding.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the maximum number of mutually unbiased orthonormal bases in $\\C^6$ is three, resolving Zauner's dimension-six MUB conjecture. The upper bound is computer-assisted: under the stated binary64 arithmetic and compiler conditions, a complete execution of the documented verification pipeline excludes four arbitrary complex bases.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":405},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and scope","sourceOffset":1667},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The covering and exclusion strategy","sourceOffset":5248},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The elementary lower bound","sourceOffset":10696},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A Lie-algebra consequence","sourceOffset":12493},{"kind":"section","title":"Equivalences and an exhaustive seed cover","sourceOffset":14057},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Equivalences and phase gauges","sourceOffset":15293},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A symmetry arrangement for nine phases","sourceOffset":18840},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Vanishing-sum filters and the shared displacement","sourceOffset":24844},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Certified reuse of seed centers","sourceOffset":28936},{"kind":"section","title":"Completion over a fixed seed ball","sourceOffset":35289},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Seed blocks and fixed complementary frames","sourceOffset":35862}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Computer-assisted","label":"thm:main","text":"Under the arithmetic and compiler conditions in Appendix \\ref{sec:rounding}, the fresh complete execution documented in Section \\ref{execute:observed} certifies that there are no four pairwise mutually unbiased orthonormal bases in $\\C^6$. Consequently, $N(6)=3$.","sourceOffset":818,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Adjoint-closed Cartan subalgebras","label":"cor:adjoint-cartan","text":"Under the arithmetic and compiler conditions of Appendix \\ref{sec:rounding}, using the complete execution documented in Section \\ref{execute:observed} as in Theorem \\ref{thm:main}, the maximum number of pairwise Killing-orthogonal Cartan subalgebras of $\\mathfrak{sl}_6(\\C)$ that are all closed under the same standard adjoint $X\\mapsto X^*=\\overline X^{\\,T}$ is three.","sourceOffset":13255,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"seed:extendibility","text":"A matrix $H\\in\\mathbb C^{6\\times6}$ is a complex Hadamard matrix here if every entry has modulus one and $HH^*=6I$. It is extendible if the standard basis and the columns of $H/\\sqrt6$ belong to a family of four mutually unbiased orthonormal bases.","sourceOffset":15337,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Root certificate","label":"phase:root-data","text":"A \\texttt{Root} consists of a stored real center \\(P\\), a stored real \\(36\\)-by-\\(k\\) matrix \\(T\\), and nonnegative constants \\[ \\mathtt{C},\\quad \\mathtt{n0},\\quad \\mathtt{id},\\quad \\mathtt{ort},\\quad \\mathtt{hor},\\quad \\mathtt{mnorm},\\quad \\mathtt{froot}. \\] The source stores column \\(a\\) of \\(T\\) as \\texttt{T[a]} and uses \\(0\\leq k\\leq10\\). Write \\begin{equation} n_P(s)=\\mathtt{n0}+\\mathtt{id}\\,s+\\mathtt{C}\\,s^2, \\qquad s\\geq0. \\end{equation} It is certified if the basic bounds \\begin{equation} \\|T^*T-I\\|\\leq\\mathtt{ort},\\qquad \\|(I-\\Pi)T\\|\\leq\\mathtt{hor},\\qquad \\|F(P)\\|\\leq\\mathtt{froot} \\","sourceOffset":65008,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/266.md","scopeText":"The paper claims that at most three mutually unbiased orthonormal bases exist in $\\mathbb C^6$. The linked formalization proves a weaker family bound: every family in its mutually unbiased bases model has at most five members. It also proves a Fourier character-sum vanishing statement for order-six complex Hadamard matrices not equivalent to the Tao matrix, uniformly over coordinate permutations.\n\nThe selected statement does not establish the paper's upper bound of three or its computer-assisted exclusion of four arbitrary bases.\n\nThe linked formalization proves a cancellation lemma used in the order-six complex Hadamard analysis. Let $H$ and its entrywise square both be complex Hadamard matrices, and fix two distinct rows. If the cubes of their six entrywise ratios take only two distinct ","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/266.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":259275,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"266-02","slug":"Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026","familyId":"266","title":"Exact Fourier certificates for complex Hadamard matrices of order six","abstract":"We prove the Fourier-vanishing conjecture of Matolcsi, Ruzsa, and Weiner: every complex Hadamard matrix of order six outside Tao's cubic equivalence class has a vanishing character sum at every permutation of $(1,1,1,-1,-1,-1)$. We also give an exact certificate excluding seven mutually unbiased bases in ℂ6; by Weiner's completion theorem, this yields an upper bound of five. Both certificates use only integer and rational arithmetic, and the complete verifier is included.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves $N(6)=3$, resolving Zauner's dimension-six mutually unbiased bases conjecture: three such bases exist in ℂ6, but four cannot. The exclusion is a complete certified computation under the stated binary64 arithmetic and compiler conditions. An independent companion proves the Matolcsi–Ruzsa–Weiner Fourier-vanishing conjecture for order-six complex Hadamard matrices outside Tao's cubic equivalence class.","pdfPath":"preprints/Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026/Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026/Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-pair-moments.tex","preprints/Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-cubic.tex","preprints/Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-complete-family.tex","preprints/Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-mixed.tex","preprints/Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-verification.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Fourier-vanishing conjecture of Matolcsi, Ruzsa, and Weiner: every complex Hadamard matrix of order six outside Tao's cubic equivalence class has a vanishing character sum at every permutation of $(1,1,1,-1,-1,-1)$. We also give an exact certificate excluding seven mutually unbiased bases in $\\C^6$; by Weiner's completion theorem, this yields an upper bound of five. Both certificates use only integer and rational arithmetic, and the complete verifier is included.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":527},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fourier methods and mutually unbiased bases","sourceOffset":3248},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":7912},{"kind":"section","title":"Characters and phase invariance","sourceOffset":10858},{"kind":"section","title":"Injective moments and a dichotomy for one Hadamard matrix","sourceOffset":12319},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Injective moments","sourceOffset":13275},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From products of characters to injective moments","sourceOffset":17724},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An exact identity and its consequence","sourceOffset":20793},{"kind":"section","title":"The cubic alternative","sourceOffset":25549},{"kind":"section","title":"Complete families and the cubic obstruction","sourceOffset":33379},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Character identities of a complete family","sourceOffset":33661},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The cubic-pair obstruction","sourceOffset":37484}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"No complete family","label":"thm:main","text":"There do not exist seven orthonormal bases $B_r=(b_{r,0},\\ldots,b_{r,5})$ of $\\C^6$, $1\\le r\\le7$, such that \\[ \\bigl|\\langle b_{r,i},b_{s,j}\\rangle\\bigr|^2=\\frac16 \\qquad(r\\ne s,\\ 0\\le i,j\\le5). \\]","sourceOffset":6719,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Fourier vanishing","label":"thm:fourier","text":"Let $H$ be a complex Hadamard matrix of order six. If $H$ is not equivalent to the cubic matrix $T$ in \\eqref{eq:cubic-matrix}, then \\[ g_H(\\pi\\alpha)=0\\qquad\\text{for every coordinate permutation }\\pi. \\]","sourceOffset":2526,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/266.md","scopeText":"The paper claims that at most three mutually unbiased orthonormal bases exist in $\\mathbb C^6$. The linked formalization proves a weaker family bound: every family in its mutually unbiased bases model has at most five members. It also proves a Fourier character-sum vanishing statement for order-six complex Hadamard matrices not equivalent to the Tao matrix, uniformly over coordinate permutations.\n\nThe selected statement does not establish the paper's upper bound of three or its computer-assisted exclusion of four arbitrary bases.\n\nThe linked formalization proves a cancellation lemma used in the order-six complex Hadamard analysis. Let $H$ and its entrywise square both be complex Hadamard matrices, and fix two distinct rows. If the cubes of their six entrywise ratios take only two distinct ","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/266.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":87474,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Exact-Fourier-certificates-for-complex-Hadamard-matrices-of-order-six-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"267-01","slug":"Bose-Einstein-condensation-at-positive-temperature-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-October-5-2026","familyId":"267","title":"Bose–Einstein condensation at positive temperature in the dilute hard-sphere gas","abstract":"We prove Bose–Einstein condensation at positive temperature in the three-dimensional dilute hard-sphere gas. For each fixed exclusion distance and every sufficiently small fixed density, there is a strictly positive temperature, independent of the volume, at which the exact canonical Gibbs state has a positive condensate fraction in the thermodynamic limit. The condensate occupies the constant orbital.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Bose–Einstein condensation for the exact canonical Gibbs state of the three-dimensional hard-sphere gas: each fixed exclusion distance and sufficiently small fixed density admit a strictly positive temperature, independent of volume, with positive condensate fraction in the thermodynamic limit. At zero temperature, proves the Bogoliubov leading quantum-depletion law for hard spheres and fixed bounded nonnegative radial finite-range potentials of positive scattering length, taking the thermodynamic limit before the dilute limit.","pdfPath":"preprints/Bose-Einstein-condensation-at-positive-temperature-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-October-5-2026/positive-temperature-hard-spheres.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Bose-Einstein-condensation-at-positive-temperature-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-October-5-2026/positive-temperature-hard-spheres.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Bose-Einstein-condensation-at-positive-temperature-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-October-5-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Bose-Einstein-condensation-at-positive-temperature-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Bose-Einstein-condensation-at-positive-temperature-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Bose-Einstein-condensation-at-positive-temperature-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-October-5-2026/build/history-draft.tex","preprints/Bose-Einstein-condensation-at-positive-temperature-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-October-5-2026/build/sections/setup.tex","preprints/Bose-Einstein-condensation-at-positive-temperature-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-October-5-2026/build/sections/energy.tex","preprints/Bose-Einstein-condensation-at-positive-temperature-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-October-5-2026/build/sections/spatial.tex","preprints/Bose-Einstein-condensation-at-positive-temperature-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-October-5-2026/build/sections/slab.tex","preprints/Bose-Einstein-condensation-at-positive-temperature-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-October-5-2026/build/sections/bridges.tex","preprints/Bose-Einstein-condensation-at-positive-temperature-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-October-5-2026/build/sections/groups.tex","preprints/Bose-Einstein-condensation-at-positive-temperature-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-October-5-2026/build/sections/lattice.tex","preprints/Bose-Einstein-condensation-at-positive-temperature-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-October-5-2026/build/sections/comparison.tex","preprints/Bose-Einstein-condensation-at-positive-temperature-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-October-5-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Bose-Einstein-condensation-at-positive-temperature-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Bose--Einstein condensation at positive temperature in the three-dimensional dilute hard-sphere gas. For each fixed exclusion distance and every sufficiently small fixed density, there is a strictly positive temperature, independent of the volume, at which the exact canonical Gibbs state has a positive condensate fraction in the thermodynamic limit. The condensate occupies the constant orbital.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":469},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and the result","sourceOffset":930},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and related methods","sourceOffset":2850},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The argument","sourceOffset":10070},{"kind":"section","title":"A scaled formulation","sourceOffset":14454},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positive kernels and retained particles","sourceOffset":17142},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The estimate to be proved","sourceOffset":19088},{"kind":"section","title":"Local kinetic energy and particle-number costs","sourceOffset":20192},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two local consequences of the hard core","sourceOffset":21047},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Entropy and admissible marginals","sourceOffset":24873},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Insertion without changing the old marginal","sourceOffset":27494},{"kind":"section","title":"Localization and joint spatial estimates","sourceOffset":35219}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $a>0$, there is $\\rho_*(a)>0$ such that for each fixed $0<\\rho<\\rho_*(a)$ there is a fixed temperature $T=T(a,\\rho)>0$ for which \\[ \\liminf_{\\substack{L\\to\\infty\\\\N/L^3\\to\\rho}} \\frac{\\langle u_{0,L},\\gamma^{(1)}_{N,L,T}u_{0,L}\\rangle}{N}>0. \\] The exclusion distance, density, and temperature are held fixed in this limit.","sourceOffset":2195,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Scaled occupation bound","label":"thm:scaled","text":"There are constants $\\alpha_0\\ge2$, $D_*<\\infty$, and $c_*>0$ such that $B_{\\rm occ}\\ge c_*$ whenever the parameters satisfy \\eqref{eq:scaled-parameters}, $D\\ge D_*$, and $K$ is sufficiently large. The lower threshold for $K$ can be chosen locally uniformly for $D$ in $[D_*,\\infty)$, uniformly in $\\alpha\\in[\\alpha_0/2,2\\alpha_0]$ and $0<T\\le D^{-1}$.","sourceOffset":19330,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Regular path","label":"def:regular","text":"Fix a sufficiently large numerical constant $C_{\\rm reg}$. A path on $[0,2t]$ is regular if \\begin{equation} \\max_j A_j\\le D^\\gamma, \\qquad \\frac1{2m_t}\\sum_{j=1}^{2m_t}(1+A_j)^3\\le C_{\\rm reg}, \\end{equation} and its lifted diameter in each endpoint window $[0,h_0]$ and $[2t-h_0,2t]$ is at most $s_0/8$.","sourceOffset":59686,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/267.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":185727,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Bose-Einstein-condensation-at-positive-temperature-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Bose-Einstein-condensation-at-positive-temperature-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Bose-Einstein-condensation-at-positive-temperature-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"267-02","slug":"Quantum-Depletion-and-Momentum-Distribution-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas-October-5-2026","familyId":"267","title":"Quantum Depletion and Momentum Distribution in the Dilute Hard-Sphere Bose Gas","abstract":"We prove the full scaled Bogoliubov momentum distribution for the depleted particles in the three-dimensional hard-sphere Bose gas at zero temperature. The thermodynamic limit is taken at each fixed density before the dilute limit, uniformly over all pure and mixed ground states. All thermodynamic accumulation values have the same dilute asymptotic: the limiting nonzero-momentum occupation measure has total mass $8/(3\\sqrt\\pi)$, giving the depletion fraction $\\frac{8}{3\\sqrt\\pi}\\sqrt{\\rho a^3}+o(\\sqrt{\\rho a^3})$ for density ρ and hard-sphere exclusion distance a.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Bose–Einstein condensation for the exact canonical Gibbs state of the three-dimensional hard-sphere gas: each fixed exclusion distance and sufficiently small fixed density admit a strictly positive temperature, independent of volume, with positive condensate fraction in the thermodynamic limit. At zero temperature, proves the Bogoliubov leading quantum-depletion law for hard spheres and fixed bounded nonnegative radial finite-range potentials of positive scattering length, taking the thermodynamic limit before the dilute limit.","pdfPath":"preprints/Quantum-Depletion-and-Momentum-Distribution-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas-October-5-2026/Quantum-Depletion-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quantum-Depletion-and-Momentum-Distribution-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas-October-5-2026/Quantum-Depletion-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quantum-Depletion-and-Momentum-Distribution-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas-October-5-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Quantum-Depletion-and-Momentum-Distribution-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-and-Momentum-Distribution-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-and-Momentum-Distribution-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas-October-5-2026/build/sections/setup.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-and-Momentum-Distribution-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas-October-5-2026/build/sections/energy.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-and-Momentum-Distribution-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas-October-5-2026/build/sections/energy-consequences.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-and-Momentum-Distribution-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas-October-5-2026/build/sections/local-paths.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-and-Momentum-Distribution-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas-October-5-2026/build/sections/local-palm.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-and-Momentum-Distribution-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas-October-5-2026/build/sections/routes.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-and-Momentum-Distribution-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas-October-5-2026/build/sections/long-palm.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-and-Momentum-Distribution-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas-October-5-2026/build/sections/assembly.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-and-Momentum-Distribution-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas-October-5-2026/build/sections/momentum.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-and-Momentum-Distribution-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas-October-5-2026/build/figures/shifted-grid.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-and-Momentum-Distribution-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Quantum-Depletion-and-Momentum-Distribution-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the full scaled Bogoliubov momentum distribution for the depleted particles in the three-dimensional hard-sphere Bose gas at zero temperature. The thermodynamic limit is taken at each fixed density before the dilute limit, uniformly over all pure and mixed ground states. All thermodynamic accumulation values have the same dilute asymptotic: the limiting nonzero-momentum occupation measure has total mass $8/(3\\sqrt\\pi)$, giving the depletion fraction $\\frac{8}{3\\sqrt\\pi}\\sqrt{\\rho a^3}+o(\\sqrt{\\rho a^3})$ for density $\\rho$ and hard-sphere exclusion distance $a$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":640},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and the result","sourceOffset":1229},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and the sources of the method","sourceOffset":4962},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From local excitations to the momentum profile","sourceOffset":11166},{"kind":"section","title":"Scaled variables and ground-state estimates","sourceOffset":16553},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The form domain and the constant orbital","sourceOffset":17137},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Partitions and the two limiting operations","sourceOffset":21541},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Spatial inputs from the condensation theorem","sourceOffset":25538},{"kind":"section","title":"Local energy bounds with retained excitations","sourceOffset":29761},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The local energy and the relevant scales","sourceOffset":30935},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A rough bound retaining kinetic energy and count fluctuations","sourceOffset":36009},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Localization in the number of low-frequency excitations","sourceOffset":48401}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $a>0$, every bounded continuous real function $f$ on $\\R^3$, and every $\\eps>0$, there is $\\rho_0(a,f,\\eps)>0$ with the following property. Fix $0<\\rho<\\rho_0$ and put $\\eta=\\rho a^3$. For every sequence $N_j,L_j\\longrightarrow\\infty$ with $N_j/L_j^3\\longrightarrow\\rho$, \\begin{equation} \\limsup_{j\\to\\infty}\\ \\sup_{\\Gamma\\in\\mathcal G_{N_j,L_j,a}} \\left| \\frac{1}{N_j\\sqrt\\eta} \\sum_{\\substack{k\\in(2\\pi/L_j)\\Z^3\\\\k\\ne0}} f\\!\\left(\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{8\\pi\\rho a}}\\right)n_{\\Gamma,L_j}(k) -\\int_{\\R^3}f\\,\\dd\\nu_{\\mathrm{Bog}} \\right|\\le\\eps. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":3538,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Remaining excitations","label":"asm:infrared","text":"Put $Q_R=P_R-P_0$. Uniformly over normalized, possibly complex, bosonic ground vectors, \\begin{equation} D\\|(Q_R)_1\\Phi\\|_2^2\\le16\\epsilon_j+o(1)+o_K(1), \\end{equation} where $\\epsilon_j\\to0$. The coefficient $16$ is independent of $j$; the dilute remainder may depend on $j$. The exponents $u,w$ and the scale $R$ may be kept fixed when $j$ is increased; all dilute and volume thresholds may depend on $j$.","sourceOffset":252859,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Good site","label":"lp:certificate","text":"A unit-cell site is good if all of the following conditions hold. \\begin{enumerate} \\item It has at least $c f_0D$ interior labels. In its $R_*$ neighborhood, each unit cell has at most $CD$ endpoints at either time and at most $Cf_0D$ flagged endpoints at either time. \\item At either endpoint time, every ball $B(z,h)$ in the neighborhood, with $r\\leq h\\leq1$, satisfies \\begin{equation} \\#\\{i:\\ i\\text{ flagged},\\ Y_i\\in B(z,h)\\} \\leq C(f_0Dh^3+D^{1/100}), \\end{equation} and the same assertion holds for $Z_i$. \\item Every frozen path visiting the relevant neighborhood satisfies \\eqref{lp:cutoff","sourceOffset":113813,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/267.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":279336,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":18,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Quantum-Depletion-and-Momentum-Distribution-in-the-Dilute-Hard-Sphere-Bose-Gas-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"267-03","slug":"Quantum-Depletion-for-Fixed-Bounded-Repulsive-Potentials-October-5-2026","familyId":"267","title":"Quantum Depletion for Fixed Bounded Repulsive Potentials","abstract":"We prove the Bogoliubov quantum-depletion asymptotic for the ground state of a three-dimensional Bose gas with a fixed bounded, nonnegative, radial interaction of finite range and positive scattering length a. The thermodynamic limit is taken at each fixed density ρ before the dilute limit. Every thermodynamic accumulation value of the fraction outside the constant mode is $\\frac{8}{3\\sqrt\\pi}\\sqrt{\\rho a^3}+o(\\sqrt{\\rho a^3})$ as $\\rho\\downarrow0$. The assertion is uniform over ground-state density matrices and does not require the occupation to have a unique thermodynamic limit.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Bose–Einstein condensation for the exact canonical Gibbs state of the three-dimensional hard-sphere gas: each fixed exclusion distance and sufficiently small fixed density admit a strictly positive temperature, independent of volume, with positive condensate fraction in the thermodynamic limit. At zero temperature, proves the Bogoliubov leading quantum-depletion law for hard spheres and fixed bounded nonnegative radial finite-range potentials of positive scattering length, taking the thermodynamic limit before the dilute limit.","pdfPath":"preprints/Quantum-Depletion-for-Fixed-Bounded-Repulsive-Potentials-October-5-2026/fixed-repulsion-quantum-depletion.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quantum-Depletion-for-Fixed-Bounded-Repulsive-Potentials-October-5-2026/fixed-repulsion-quantum-depletion.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quantum-Depletion-for-Fixed-Bounded-Repulsive-Potentials-October-5-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Quantum-Depletion-for-Fixed-Bounded-Repulsive-Potentials-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-for-Fixed-Bounded-Repulsive-Potentials-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-for-Fixed-Bounded-Repulsive-Potentials-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/setup.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-for-Fixed-Bounded-Repulsive-Potentials-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/energy.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-for-Fixed-Bounded-Repulsive-Potentials-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/paths.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-for-Fixed-Bounded-Repulsive-Potentials-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/local-palm.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-for-Fixed-Bounded-Repulsive-Potentials-October-5-2026/build/source/figures/local-transport.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-for-Fixed-Bounded-Repulsive-Potentials-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/long-palm.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-for-Fixed-Bounded-Repulsive-Potentials-October-5-2026/build/source/figures/silent-transport.tex","preprints/Quantum-Depletion-for-Fixed-Bounded-Repulsive-Potentials-October-5-2026/build/source/sections/assembly.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Quantum-Depletion-for-Fixed-Bounded-Repulsive-Potentials-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Bogoliubov quantum-depletion asymptotic for the ground state of a three-dimensional Bose gas with a fixed bounded, nonnegative, radial interaction of finite range and positive scattering length $a$. The thermodynamic limit is taken at each fixed density $\\rho$ before the dilute limit. Every thermodynamic accumulation value of the fraction outside the constant mode is $\\frac{8}{3\\sqrt\\pi}\\sqrt{\\rho a^3}+o(\\sqrt{\\rho a^3})$ as $\\rho\\downarrow0$. The assertion is uniform over ground-state density matrices and does not require the occupation to have a unique thermodynamic limit.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":640},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and the result","sourceOffset":1114},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and antecedents","sourceOffset":3146},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From local occupations to the constant mode","sourceOffset":6757},{"kind":"section","title":"Scales, density tails, and ground-state paths","sourceOffset":10667},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ground states and scaled variables","sourceOffset":11089},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local density tails","sourceOffset":15716},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ground-state paths and local pair bounds","sourceOffset":20550},{"kind":"section","title":"Energy and mesoscopic occupation","sourceOffset":23921},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The upper bound and Neumann boxes","sourceOffset":25678},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A rough bound retaining count and kinetic penalties","sourceOffset":29840},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Restricting the excitation number","sourceOffset":36320}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every potential $v$ satisfying the assumptions above and every $\\epsilon>0$, there is $\\rho_0(v,\\epsilon)>0$ such that, for every fixed $0<\\rho<\\rho_0(v,\\epsilon)$ and every sequence $N_k,L_k\\to\\infty$ with $N_k/L_k^3\\to\\rho$, \\[ \\limsup_{k\\to\\infty}\\ \\sup_{\\Gamma\\in\\mathcal G^v_{N_k,L_k}} \\left|\\frac{1-B_\\Gamma}{\\sqrt{\\rho a_v^3}} -\\frac8{3\\sqrt\\pi}\\right|\\le\\epsilon. \\]","sourceOffset":2147,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Density-tail input from \\cite{C}","label":"prop:density-input","text":"Let $v$ satisfy the hypotheses of Theorem \\ref{thm:main}. There is a sufficiently large fixed $B_0$ such that, for all sufficiently small $\\rho>0$, the following statements hold at physical base length $b=B_0\\rho^{-1/2}$ and count scale $n=\\rho b^3$. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item If the particle density is at most $2\\rho$, let $A=\\sum_i g(x_i)$, where $0\\le g\\le1$, the support of $g$ is covered by $J$ base cubes, and $|\\nabla g|^2\\le C_gb^{-2}g$ almost everywhere for a prescribed fixed bound on $C_g$. Then \\[ \\Pp(A>M)\\le C e^{-cM},\\qquad M\\ge CnJ. \\] Sharp counts have such majorants after a fixed enlargement and a fixed increase in $J$. \\item If the density lies in $[\\rho/2,2\\rho]$ and $Q_1,\\ldots,Q_J$ are cubes of side $b$ with centers separated by a sufficiently large fixed multiple of $b$, then \\[ \\Pp\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^J n_{Q_i}\\le\\epsilon_0 nJ\\right) \\le e^{-c_1 nJ} \\] for fixed $\\epsilon_0,c_1>0$. \\end{enumerate} Both assertions hold in all sufficiently large volumes, uniformly down to arbitrarily small positive temperatures. Their volume thresholds do not grow as temperature decreases. Fixed positive multiplicative changes in cube sides are allowed after changing constants, and $B_0$ can be increased to meet any finite list of lower bounds.","sourceOffset":16048,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/267.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":172240,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Quantum-Depletion-for-Fixed-Bounded-Repulsive-Potentials-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"267-04","slug":"A-density-uniform-condensate-bound-for-dilute-Bose-gases-September-27-2026","familyId":"267","title":"A density-uniform condensate bound for dilute Bose gases","abstract":"For each fixed bounded measurable, nonnegative, radial interaction of finite range in three dimensions that is not zero almost everywhere, we prove a positive lower bound on the constant-orbital condensate fraction that is uniform over all sufficiently small densities and all temperatures between zero and the square of the density. The interaction and density remain fixed in the thermodynamic limit.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Bose–Einstein condensation for the exact canonical Gibbs state of the three-dimensional hard-sphere gas: each fixed exclusion distance and sufficiently small fixed density admit a strictly positive temperature, independent of volume, with positive condensate fraction in the thermodynamic limit. At zero temperature, proves the Bogoliubov leading quantum-depletion law for hard spheres and fixed bounded nonnegative radial finite-range potentials of positive scattering length, taking the thermodynamic limit before the dilute limit.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-density-uniform-condensate-bound-for-dilute-Bose-gases-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-density-uniform-condensate-bound-for-dilute-Bose-gases-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-density-uniform-condensate-bound-for-dilute-Bose-gases-September-27-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/A-density-uniform-condensate-bound-for-dilute-Bose-gases-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-density-uniform-condensate-bound-for-dilute-Bose-gases-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-density-uniform-condensate-bound-for-dilute-Bose-gases-September-27-2026/build/sections/history.tex","preprints/A-density-uniform-condensate-bound-for-dilute-Bose-gases-September-27-2026/build/sections/setup.tex","preprints/A-density-uniform-condensate-bound-for-dilute-Bose-gases-September-27-2026/build/sections/energy.tex","preprints/A-density-uniform-condensate-bound-for-dilute-Bose-gases-September-27-2026/build/sections/localization.tex","preprints/A-density-uniform-condensate-bound-for-dilute-Bose-gases-September-27-2026/build/sections/slab.tex","preprints/A-density-uniform-condensate-bound-for-dilute-Bose-gases-September-27-2026/build/sections/atypical.tex","preprints/A-density-uniform-condensate-bound-for-dilute-Bose-gases-September-27-2026/build/sections/defects.tex","preprints/A-density-uniform-condensate-bound-for-dilute-Bose-gases-September-27-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/A-density-uniform-condensate-bound-for-dilute-Bose-gases-September-27-2026/build/sections/opening.tex","preprints/A-density-uniform-condensate-bound-for-dilute-Bose-gases-September-27-2026/build/sections/comparison.tex","preprints/A-density-uniform-condensate-bound-for-dilute-Bose-gases-September-27-2026/build/figures/endpoint-changes.tex","preprints/A-density-uniform-condensate-bound-for-dilute-Bose-gases-September-27-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-density-uniform-condensate-bound-for-dilute-Bose-gases-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For each fixed bounded measurable, nonnegative, radial interaction of finite range in three dimensions that is not zero almost everywhere, we prove a positive lower bound on the constant-orbital condensate fraction that is uniform over all sufficiently small densities and all temperatures between zero and the square of the density. The interaction and density remain fixed in the thermodynamic limit.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The model and the uniform bound","sourceOffset":465},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and antecedents","sourceOffset":2702},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the comparison works","sourceOffset":9389},{"kind":"section","title":"Scales and conventions","sourceOffset":13354},{"kind":"section","title":"Energy and free-energy estimates","sourceOffset":15577},{"kind":"section","title":"Localization, occupation, and isolation","sourceOffset":24070},{"kind":"section","title":"A short imaginary-time slab","sourceOffset":40147},{"kind":"section","title":"A joint bound for atypical trajectories","sourceOffset":47956},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A localized kernel for one marked bridge","sourceOffset":50353},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The colored operator and its large powers","sourceOffset":53961},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The partition-function estimate with confined colors","sourceOffset":62246},{"kind":"section","title":"Defective cells and an insertion comparison","sourceOffset":66245}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every potential $v$ as above there are constants $\\rho_*(v)>0$ and $c_*(v)>0$ with the following property. For every $0<\\rho<\\rho_*(v)$ there is $L_0(\\rho,v)<\\infty$ such that, for every $L\\ge L_0(\\rho,v)$, every integer $N\\ge1$ satisfying $\\rho/2\\le N/L^3\\le2\\rho$, and every $0\\le T\\le\\rho^2$, \\[ \\frac{\\langle u_0,\\gamma^{(1)}_{N,L,T}u_0\\rangle}{N} \\ge c_*(v). \\] Consequently, for each such fixed density $\\rho$ and fixed $0\\le T\\le\\rho^2$, every sequence with $L\\to\\infty$ and $N/L^3\\to\\rho$ has lower limiting condensate fraction at least $c_*(v)$.","sourceOffset":1598,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Particle-number free-energy bounds","label":"prop:increments","text":"Uniformly in the temperature range under consideration, \\begin{align} F_N&\\geq c_v\\rho N,\\tag{4}\\\\ F_{N+k}&\\geq F_N+c_v\\rho k\\qquad(k\\geq0).\\tag{5} \\end{align} For every bounded $W\\geq0$, with no upper bound on its size required, \\begin{equation} F_j^{(-W)}-F_{j-1}^{(-W)}\\leq C_v\\rho \\qquad(1\\leq j\\leq4\\rho V).\\tag{6} \\end{equation} The constant in (6) is independent of $W$.","sourceOffset":20305,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/267.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":143741,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-density-uniform-condensate-bound-for-dilute-Bose-gases-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"267-05","slug":"Ground-state-condensation-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-September-24-2026","familyId":"267","title":"Ground-state condensation in the dilute hard-sphere gas","abstract":"We prove Bose–Einstein condensation in every ground state of a dilute three-dimensional hard-sphere Bose gas. At every sufficiently small fixed gas parameter, the constant orbital contains a positive fraction of the particles in the thermodynamic limit. The fraction can be chosen independently of the gas parameter, and the conclusion holds for arbitrary complex ground states.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Bose–Einstein condensation for the exact canonical Gibbs state of the three-dimensional hard-sphere gas: each fixed exclusion distance and sufficiently small fixed density admit a strictly positive temperature, independent of volume, with positive condensate fraction in the thermodynamic limit. At zero temperature, proves the Bogoliubov leading quantum-depletion law for hard spheres and fixed bounded nonnegative radial finite-range potentials of positive scattering length, taking the thermodynamic limit before the dilute limit.","pdfPath":"preprints/Ground-state-condensation-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Ground-state-condensation-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Ground-state-condensation-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-September-24-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Ground-state-condensation-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Ground-state-condensation-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Ground-state-condensation-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-September-24-2026/build/sections/setup.tex","preprints/Ground-state-condensation-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-September-24-2026/build/sections/spatial.tex","preprints/Ground-state-condensation-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-September-24-2026/build/sections/paths.tex","preprints/Ground-state-condensation-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-September-24-2026/build/sections/bridges.tex","preprints/Ground-state-condensation-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-September-24-2026/build/sections/groups.tex","preprints/Ground-state-condensation-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-September-24-2026/build/sections/lattice.tex","preprints/Ground-state-condensation-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-September-24-2026/build/sections/comparison.tex","preprints/Ground-state-condensation-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-September-24-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Ground-state-condensation-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-September-24-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Ground-state-condensation-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Bose--Einstein condensation in every ground state of a dilute three-dimensional hard-sphere Bose gas. At every sufficiently small fixed gas parameter, the constant orbital contains a positive fraction of the particles in the thermodynamic limit. The fraction can be chosen independently of the gas parameter, and the conclusion holds for arbitrary complex ground states.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":442},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The result","sourceOffset":982},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context","sourceOffset":3925},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the proof works","sourceOffset":8475},{"kind":"section","title":"Ground states and phase transfer","sourceOffset":14698},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Length units and the occupation fraction","sourceOffset":15135},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The full ground equation and its equality case","sourceOffset":18615},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The price of removing a part of each slice","sourceOffset":24443},{"kind":"section","title":"Energy comparisons and joint spatial bounds","sourceOffset":26751},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two local kinetic inequalities","sourceOffset":28184},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Normalized insertion and energy differences","sourceOffset":32386},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A joint bound for deficient cells","sourceOffset":36869}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Condensation in every ground state","label":"main:theorem","text":"There are absolute constants $\\varepsilon_0,c_0>0$ with the following property. Fix $a,\\rho>0$ satisfying $\\rho a^3<\\varepsilon_0$. For every sequence of particle numbers $N_k$ and lengths $L_k$ such that \\[ N_k\\longrightarrow\\infty,\\qquad L_k\\longrightarrow\\infty, \\qquad \\frac{N_k}{L_k^3}\\longrightarrow\\rho, \\] and every choice of normalized, possibly complex, bosonic ground eigenvectors $\\Psi_k$ of the hard-sphere Hamiltonians, \\begin{equation} \\liminf_{k\\to\\infty} B(\\Psi_k)\\ge c_0. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":2350,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Ground-space states","label":"main:mixed","text":"Under the hypotheses of Theorem \\ref{main:theorem}, the same lower bound holds for every sequence of density operators supported on the bosonic ground eigenspaces. In particular, it holds for the normalized ground-space projections $\\Pi_k/\\Tr\\Pi_k$, and for every sequence of normalized nonnegative ground functions.","sourceOffset":3263,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"path:regular-path","text":"Fix a sufficiently large numerical constant $C_{\\rm reg}$. A path is regular if \\begin{equation} \\max_j A_j\\le D^\\gamma, \\qquad \\frac1{2m_t}\\sum_{j=1}^{2m_t}(1+A_j)^3\\le C_{\\rm reg}, \\end{equation} and its lifted diameter on each of $[0,h_0]$ and $[2t-h_0,2t]$ is at most $s_0/8$.","sourceOffset":57220,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/267.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves ground-state Bose–Einstein condensation in the dilute hard-sphere gas. There are absolute constants $\\varepsilon_0,c_0>0$ such that, whenever the density $\\rho$ and hard-sphere radius $a$ satisfy $\\rho a^3<\\varepsilon_0$, the condensate occupation fraction has limit inferior at least $c_0$ along every thermodynamic sequence with $N/L^3\\to\\rho$.\n\nThe bound covers every pure ground state, every mixed state supported on the ground space, and the normalized ground-space projection. It is uniform in the gas parameter within this range and concerns ground states, without a positive-temperature assertion.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/267.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":171556,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Ground-state-condensation-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Ground-state-condensation-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Ground-state-condensation-in-the-dilute-hard-sphere-gas-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"268-01","slug":"The-periodic-spin-one-Haldane-gap-September-24-2026","familyId":"268","title":"The periodic spin-one Haldane gap","abstract":"We prove a uniform positive spectral gap for the pure antiferromagnetic spin-one Heisenberg chain on even periodic rings, establishing the positive even-periodic formulation of the spin-one Haldane conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the spin-one Haldane gap conjecture for the pure antiferromagnetic Heisenberg chain on even periodic rings: the spectral gap stays uniformly positive as the chain grows. A companion establishes a gap for odd open chains with endpoint field $h=3/5$ and gives boundary-selected infinite-volume states with topological index −1.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-periodic-spin-one-Haldane-gap-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-periodic-spin-one-Haldane-gap-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-periodic-spin-one-Haldane-gap-September-24-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/The-periodic-spin-one-Haldane-gap-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-periodic-spin-one-Haldane-gap-September-24-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/The-periodic-spin-one-Haldane-gap-September-24-2026/build/model.tex","preprints/The-periodic-spin-one-Haldane-gap-September-24-2026/build/transfer.tex","preprints/The-periodic-spin-one-Haldane-gap-September-24-2026/build/bootstrap.tex","preprints/The-periodic-spin-one-Haldane-gap-September-24-2026/build/initialization.tex","preprints/The-periodic-spin-one-Haldane-gap-September-24-2026/build/assembly.tex","preprints/The-periodic-spin-one-Haldane-gap-September-24-2026/build/thermal120.tex","preprints/The-periodic-spin-one-Haldane-gap-September-24-2026/build/thermal72.tex","preprints/The-periodic-spin-one-Haldane-gap-September-24-2026/build/trials.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-periodic-spin-one-Haldane-gap-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a uniform positive spectral gap for the pure antiferromagnetic spin-one Heisenberg chain on even periodic rings, establishing the positive even-periodic formulation of the spin-one Haldane conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":283},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":3001},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the proof works","sourceOffset":7226},{"kind":"section","title":"The Hamiltonian and its auxiliary twists","sourceOffset":10076},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Spin coordinates","sourceOffset":10467},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Twists and partition functions","sourceOffset":13052},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A uniform energy bound","sourceOffset":15221},{"kind":"section","title":"Partition functions as spatial moments","sourceOffset":16965},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rotation sectors","sourceOffset":23705},{"kind":"section","title":"From two purities to a uniform gap","sourceOffset":26357},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The coupled update","sourceOffset":28768},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Quadratic decay and intermediate lengths","sourceOffset":30358}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For the Hamiltonians \\eqref{eq:main-H}, the ground state is unique for every even $L\\ge60$, and \\begin{align} \\gamma_L&>\\frac4{105}\\log\\frac{80}{79} &&\\text{for every even }L\\ge60,\\\\ \\gamma_L&>\\frac{\\log20}{784} &&\\text{for every even }L\\ge2304. \\end{align} Consequently \\[ \\Delta_1\\ge\\frac{\\log20}{784}>0. \\] The spectral conclusions also hold for any simultaneously similar spin-one realization satisfying the same commutator and Casimir normalizations.","sourceOffset":1837,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Spatial transfer","label":"prop:transfer","text":"For each $b>0$ there are a Hilbert space $\\mathcal K_b$, a compact self-adjoint operator $X_b$, and a unitary representation $g\\mapsto U_g$ of $\\mathcal G$ commuting with $X_b$, such that \\begin{equation} Z_n(b,g)=\\Tr(X_b^nU_g) \\qquad(n\\ge4,\\ g\\in\\mathcal G). \\end{equation} The operator $X_b$ and the representation commute with complex conjugation. Moreover $X_b$ is Hilbert--Schmidt, so $X_b^k$ is trace class for every integer $k\\ge2$. If $\\lambda_i(b)$ are its nonzero eigenvalues, counted with multiplicity, then \\begin{equation} \\sum_i|\\lambda_i(b)|^k<\\infty\\quad(k\\ge2),\\qquad Z_n(b)=\\sum_i\\lambda_i(b)^n\\quad(n\\ge4). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":17683,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":83722,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-periodic-spin-one-Haldane-gap-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/The-periodic-spin-one-Haldane-gap-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"268-02","slug":"A-boundary-field-gap-for-the-spin-one-Heisenberg-chain-September-24-2026","familyId":"268","title":"A boundary-field gap for the spin-one Heisenberg chain","abstract":"We prove a uniform spectral gap for odd open spin-one antiferromagnetic Heisenberg chains with the same fixed magnetic field at both endpoints. The field $h=3/5$ gives a gap greater than $\\log(10)/392$ on every chain of $2L+1$ sites with L ≥ 960. Tasaki's index theorem then gives index −1 for every subsequential local limit of these boundary-selected ground states.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the spin-one Haldane gap conjecture for the pure antiferromagnetic Heisenberg chain on even periodic rings: the spectral gap stays uniformly positive as the chain grows. A companion establishes a gap for odd open chains with endpoint field $h=3/5$ and gives boundary-selected infinite-volume states with topological index −1.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-boundary-field-gap-for-the-spin-one-Heisenberg-chain-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-boundary-field-gap-for-the-spin-one-Heisenberg-chain-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-boundary-field-gap-for-the-spin-one-Heisenberg-chain-September-24-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/A-boundary-field-gap-for-the-spin-one-Heisenberg-chain-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-boundary-field-gap-for-the-spin-one-Heisenberg-chain-September-24-2026/build/sections/history.tex","preprints/A-boundary-field-gap-for-the-spin-one-Heisenberg-chain-September-24-2026/build/sections/transfer.tex","preprints/A-boundary-field-gap-for-the-spin-one-Heisenberg-chain-September-24-2026/build/sections/periodic.tex","preprints/A-boundary-field-gap-for-the-spin-one-Heisenberg-chain-September-24-2026/build/sections/boundary.tex","preprints/A-boundary-field-gap-for-the-spin-one-Heisenberg-chain-September-24-2026/build/sections/initialization.tex","preprints/A-boundary-field-gap-for-the-spin-one-Heisenberg-chain-September-24-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex","preprints/A-boundary-field-gap-for-the-spin-one-Heisenberg-chain-September-24-2026/build/sections/topology.tex","preprints/A-boundary-field-gap-for-the-spin-one-Heisenberg-chain-September-24-2026/build/sections/thermal.tex","preprints/A-boundary-field-gap-for-the-spin-one-Heisenberg-chain-September-24-2026/build/sections/trials.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-boundary-field-gap-for-the-spin-one-Heisenberg-chain-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a uniform spectral gap for odd open spin-one antiferromagnetic Heisenberg chains with the same fixed magnetic field at both endpoints. The field $h=3/5$ gives a gap greater than $\\log(10)/392$ on every chain of $2L+1$ sites with $L\\ge960$. Tasaki's index theorem then gives index $-1$ for every subsequential local limit of these boundary-selected ground states.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":417},{"kind":"section","title":"Spatial transfer with endpoint fields","sourceOffset":8043},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Hamiltonians and shifted partition functions","sourceOffset":8534},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The spatial operator","sourceOffset":9710},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Boundary vectors","sourceOffset":12525},{"kind":"section","title":"Periodic concentration estimates","sourceOffset":16394},{"kind":"section","title":"Concentration of boundary-weighted moments","sourceOffset":30263},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite initialization and quadratic improvement","sourceOffset":35571},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Starting residual and physical-purity bounds","sourceOffset":36742},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Explicit finite comparisons","sourceOffset":39671},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Indefinite continuation","sourceOffset":41729},{"kind":"section","title":"Same fields, odd lengths, and the physical gap","sourceOffset":43453}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For the Hamiltonian \\eqref{eq:target}, with the spin normalization above, \\[ E_1(L,3/5)-E_0(L,3/5)>\\frac{\\log 10}{392} \\qquad\\text{for every integer }L\\ge960. \\]","sourceOffset":1663,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Open-chain transfer","label":"tr:boundary","text":"For $b>0$, $n\\ge2$, and $g\\in\\mathcal G$, the pairing $\\langle v_b,X_b^nU_gv_b\\rangle$ is the shifted partition function of the open chain with left field $-hS_0^z$ and right field $+hS_{n-1}^{ge_z}$. Consequently \\begin{equation} Y_n(b)=\\langle v_b,X_b^nv_b\\rangle,\\qquad Y_n^{\\rm s}(b)=\\langle v_b,X_b^nU_Pv_b\\rangle. \\end{equation} Here $S^u=\\sum_\\alpha u_\\alpha S^\\alpha$ for a real vector $u$.","sourceOffset":12929,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":79083,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-boundary-field-gap-for-the-spin-one-Heisenberg-chain-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"269-01","slug":"Uniform-Stability-of-the-Spherical-Laughlin-Gap-October-5-2026","familyId":"269","title":"Uniform Stability of the Spherical Laughlin Gap","abstract":"We prove that the fermionic Laughlin V1 Hamiltonian at filling 1/3 on expanding round spheres retains a unique ground state and a uniform spectral gap under sufficiently weak bounded real scalar one-body potentials projected to the lowest Landau level. With coefficient one for each pair projector and Laughlin flux $q=3(N-1)$, the gap and perturbation threshold are uniform for all sufficiently large particle numbers and all potential profiles of supremum norm at most one. The result uses the uniform unperturbed Fock-space gap and gives existential constants.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the fermionic Laughlin spectral-gap conjecture for the full V1 interaction at filling 1/3 on the round sphere. The unique ground state remains uniformly gapped under sufficiently weak bounded real scalar one-body potentials projected to the lowest Landau level. Both the gap and disorder threshold are uniform over all sufficiently large particle numbers and all normalized potential profiles.","pdfPath":"preprints/Uniform-Stability-of-the-Spherical-Laughlin-Gap-October-5-2026/uniform-stability-spherical-laughlin-gap.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-Stability-of-the-Spherical-Laughlin-Gap-October-5-2026/uniform-stability-spherical-laughlin-gap.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-Stability-of-the-Spherical-Laughlin-Gap-October-5-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Uniform-Stability-of-the-Spherical-Laughlin-Gap-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Uniform-Stability-of-the-Spherical-Laughlin-Gap-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Uniform-Stability-of-the-Spherical-Laughlin-Gap-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Uniform-Stability-of-the-Spherical-Laughlin-Gap-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-locality.tex","preprints/Uniform-Stability-of-the-Spherical-Laughlin-Gap-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-pinning.tex","preprints/Uniform-Stability-of-the-Spherical-Laughlin-Gap-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-flags.tex","preprints/Uniform-Stability-of-the-Spherical-Laughlin-Gap-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-charge.tex","preprints/Uniform-Stability-of-the-Spherical-Laughlin-Gap-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-energy.tex","preprints/Uniform-Stability-of-the-Spherical-Laughlin-Gap-October-5-2026/build/sections/08-stability.tex","preprints/Uniform-Stability-of-the-Spherical-Laughlin-Gap-October-5-2026/build/appendices/retained-blocks.tex","preprints/Uniform-Stability-of-the-Spherical-Laughlin-Gap-October-5-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Uniform-Stability-of-the-Spherical-Laughlin-Gap-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the fermionic Laughlin $V_1$ Hamiltonian at filling $1/3$ on expanding round spheres retains a unique ground state and a uniform spectral gap under sufficiently weak bounded real scalar one-body potentials projected to the lowest Landau level. With coefficient one for each pair projector and Laughlin flux $q=3(N-1)$, the gap and perturbation threshold are uniform for all sufficiently large particle numbers and all potential profiles of supremum norm at most one. The result uses the uniform unperturbed Fock-space gap and gives existential constants.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":631},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model and main result","sourceOffset":1709},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and the additional difficulty","sourceOffset":3981},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its reusable estimates","sourceOffset":7504},{"kind":"section","title":"Lowest Landau level and zero modes","sourceOffset":11805},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coherent orbitals and pair annihilators","sourceOffset":12289},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The zero spaces and the unperturbed input","sourceOffset":16454},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Geometric scales","sourceOffset":20087},{"kind":"section","title":"A local calculus in the lowest Landau level","sourceOffset":20646},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Localized frames and auxiliary fermions","sourceOffset":21412},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local norms and summation","sourceOffset":35774},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Propagation and spectral filters","sourceOffset":42991}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform scalar-disorder stability","label":"thm:main","text":"There are constants $\\lambda_*>0$, $\\Delta_*>0$, and $N_*<\\infty$ such that, for every $N\\ge N_*$, every real bounded measurable $\\varphi$ on $S_{3(N-1)}$ with $\\|\\varphi\\|_\\infty\\le1$, and every real $|\\lambda|\\le\\lambda_*$, \\begin{equation} E_1(N,\\lambda,\\varphi)-E_0(N,\\lambda,\\varphi)\\ge\\Delta_*. \\end{equation} In particular the perturbed ground state is unique.","sourceOffset":2924,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform unperturbed gap","label":"thm:unperturbed","text":"Let $\\gamma_*=4616733319001/10^{14}$. For every $0<\\gamma<\\gamma_*$ there is $q_\\gamma$ such that \\begin{equation} H_q^2\\ge\\gamma H_q\\qquad(q\\ge q_\\gamma) \\end{equation} on all of $\\mathcal F_q$. In particular, for all sufficiently large $N$ and $q=3(N-1)$, \\begin{equation} H_{N,q}\\ge\\frac1{25}(I-P_{N,q}). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":19301,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/269.md","scopeText":"The linked formalization proves the uniform unperturbed gap estimate used in the paper's stability argument for the fermionic Laughlin state at filling $1/3$ on the sphere. At flux $q=3(N-1)$ and all sufficiently large particle numbers $N$, every antisymmetric state has $V_1$ energy at least $1/25$ times its squared distance from the Laughlin ground-state line.\n\nThis selected statement is the unperturbed Fock-space inequality. Stability under projected one-body potentials and uniqueness of the perturbed ground state are outside it.\n\nThe Laughlin spectral-gap problem asks for a positive gap that remains uniform as the system grows. The formalization includes the finite spherical $V_1$ bound of $1/100$ above the Laughlin state at flux $3(N-1)$ for all sufficiently large particle numbers $N$.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/269.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":226255,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":19,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Uniform-Stability-of-the-Spherical-Laughlin-Gap-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"269-02","slug":"A-Fock-space-inequality-and-the-Laughlin-spectral-gap-September-24-2026","familyId":"269","title":"A Fock-space inequality and the Laughlin spectral gap","abstract":"We prove the spherical fermionic Laughlin spectral-gap conjecture for the full V1 interaction. With coefficient one for each pair projector, the gap above the Laughlin state at filling 1/3 on the round sphere is at least 1/25 for all sufficiently large systems. More generally, $H_Q^2\\ge\\gamma H_Q$ for some fixed $\\gamma\\gt 1/25$ on the entire lowest-Landau-level Fock space for all sufficiently large flux Q, independently of particle number.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the fermionic Laughlin spectral-gap conjecture for the full V1 interaction at filling 1/3 on the round sphere. The unique ground state remains uniformly gapped under sufficiently weak bounded real scalar one-body potentials projected to the lowest Landau level. Both the gap and disorder threshold are uniform over all sufficiently large particle numbers and all normalized potential profiles.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Fock-space-inequality-and-the-Laughlin-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/A-Fock-space-inequality-and-the-Laughlin-spectral-gap-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Fock-space-inequality-and-the-Laughlin-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/A-Fock-space-inequality-and-the-Laughlin-spectral-gap-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Fock-space-inequality-and-the-Laughlin-spectral-gap-September-24-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/A-Fock-space-inequality-and-the-Laughlin-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-Fock-space-inequality-and-the-Laughlin-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-Fock-space-inequality-and-the-Laughlin-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/figures/relative-transfer.tex","preprints/A-Fock-space-inequality-and-the-Laughlin-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-operators.tex","preprints/A-Fock-space-inequality-and-the-Laughlin-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-spin.tex","preprints/A-Fock-space-inequality-and-the-Laughlin-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-comparison.tex","preprints/A-Fock-space-inequality-and-the-Laughlin-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-transfer.tex","preprints/A-Fock-space-inequality-and-the-Laughlin-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-gap.tex","preprints/A-Fock-space-inequality-and-the-Laughlin-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-plane.tex","preprints/A-Fock-space-inequality-and-the-Laughlin-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-arithmetic.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Fock-space-inequality-and-the-Laughlin-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the spherical fermionic Laughlin spectral-gap conjecture for the full \\(V_1\\) interaction. With coefficient one for each pair projector, the gap above the Laughlin state at filling \\(1/3\\) on the round sphere is at least \\(1/25\\) for all sufficiently large systems. More generally, \\(H_Q^2\\ge\\gamma H_Q\\) for some fixed \\(\\gamma>1/25\\) on the entire lowest-Landau-level Fock space for all sufficiently large flux \\(Q\\), independently of particle number.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The gap and the model","sourceOffset":508},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Normalization and main results","sourceOffset":1714},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work","sourceOffset":4116},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":7531},{"kind":"section","title":"Exterior algebra and the square of the interaction","sourceOffset":11607},{"kind":"section","title":"Spin coefficients and their limits","sourceOffset":15246},{"kind":"section","title":"Rotational averages of explicit squares","sourceOffset":21401},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The three-body comparison","sourceOffset":25098},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The four-body comparison before taking limits","sourceOffset":26464},{"kind":"section","title":"A relative error bound for finite spheres","sourceOffset":30546},{"kind":"section","title":"The tail estimate and the gap","sourceOffset":40021},{"kind":"section","title":"A planar corollary","sourceOffset":47356}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is an integer \\(N_0\\ge2\\) such that, for every \\(N\\ge N_0\\) and \\(Q=3(N-1)\\), \\begin{equation} H_{N,Q}\\ge\\frac1{25}\\bigl(I-P_{\\mathrm L,N}\\bigr) \\qquad\\text{on }\\bigwedge^N U_Q, \\end{equation} where \\(P_{\\mathrm L,N}\\) projects onto the line spanned by \\[ \\Psi_{\\mathrm L,N}=\\prod_{i<j}(u_iv_j-u_jv_i)^3. \\]","sourceOffset":3406,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:fock","text":"For every \\(0<\\gamma<\\gamma_*\\), there is an integer \\(Q_\\gamma\\) such that for every integer \\(Q\\ge Q_\\gamma\\), \\begin{equation} H_Q^2\\ge\\gamma H_Q\\qquad\\text{on }\\FF_Q. \\end{equation} The threshold \\(Q_\\gamma\\) is independent of particle number.","sourceOffset":2764,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/269.md","scopeText":"The linked formalization proves the uniform unperturbed gap estimate used in the paper's stability argument for the fermionic Laughlin state at filling $1/3$ on the sphere. At flux $q=3(N-1)$ and all sufficiently large particle numbers $N$, every antisymmetric state has $V_1$ energy at least $1/25$ times its squared distance from the Laughlin ground-state line.\n\nThis selected statement is the unperturbed Fock-space inequality. Stability under projected one-body potentials and uniqueness of the perturbed ground state are outside it.\n\nThe Laughlin spectral-gap problem asks for a positive gap that remains uniform as the system grows. The formalization includes the finite spherical $V_1$ bound of $1/100$ above the Laughlin state at flux $3(N-1)$ for all sufficiently large particle numbers $N$.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/269.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":59349,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Fock-space-inequality-and-the-Laughlin-spectral-gap-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-Fock-space-inequality-and-the-Laughlin-spectral-gap-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"270-01","slug":"The-unique-threshold-bound-state-of-the-SU-N-BFSS-model-September-24-2026","familyId":"270","title":"The unique threshold bound state of the SU(N) BFSS model","abstract":"For every finite N ≥ 2, we prove that undeformed $\\mathop{\\mathrm{SU}}\\nolimits (N)$ BFSS matrix quantum mechanics has exactly one normalizable zero-energy state after removing the center of mass. This establishes the threshold-bound-state conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the undeformed relative $\\mathrm{SU}(N)$ BFSS model has exactly one normalizable zero-energy state for every finite N ≥ 2, resolving the threshold-bound-state conjecture. For $\\mathrm{SU}(2)$, a companion proves infinitely many normalizable positive-energy eigenstates with unbounded energies, contradicting the original BFSS paper's exclusion of additional bound states at N = 2.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-unique-threshold-bound-state-of-the-SU-N-BFSS-model-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-unique-threshold-bound-state-of-the-SU-N-BFSS-model-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-unique-threshold-bound-state-of-the-SU-N-BFSS-model-September-24-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/The-unique-threshold-bound-state-of-the-SU-N-BFSS-model-September-24-2026/build/manuscript/main.tex","preprints/The-unique-threshold-bound-state-of-the-SU-N-BFSS-model-September-24-2026/build/manuscript/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-unique-threshold-bound-state-of-the-SU-N-BFSS-model-September-24-2026/build/manuscript/figures/induction.tex","preprints/The-unique-threshold-bound-state-of-the-SU-N-BFSS-model-September-24-2026/build/manuscript/sections/02-algebra.tex","preprints/The-unique-threshold-bound-state-of-the-SU-N-BFSS-model-September-24-2026/build/manuscript/sections/03-massive-index.tex","preprints/The-unique-threshold-bound-state-of-the-SU-N-BFSS-model-September-24-2026/build/manuscript/sections/04-fast-analysis.tex","preprints/The-unique-threshold-bound-state-of-the-SU-N-BFSS-model-September-24-2026/build/manuscript/sections/05-lifts.tex","preprints/The-unique-threshold-bound-state-of-the-SU-N-BFSS-model-September-24-2026/build/manuscript/sections/06-profiles.tex","preprints/The-unique-threshold-bound-state-of-the-SU-N-BFSS-model-September-24-2026/build/manuscript/sections/07-counting.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-unique-threshold-bound-state-of-the-SU-N-BFSS-model-September-24-2026/build/manuscript/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every finite \\(N\\ge2\\), we prove that undeformed \\(\\SU(N)\\) BFSS matrix quantum mechanics has exactly one normalizable zero-energy state after removing the center of mass. This establishes the threshold-bound-state conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":306},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The operator and the result","sourceOffset":1433},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the analytic issue","sourceOffset":3749},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its compactness mechanism","sourceOffset":8938},{"kind":"section","title":"The charge algebra, domains, and rotational invariance","sourceOffset":17106},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The invariant charge and its coercive inequality","sourceOffset":25453},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Closed domains and the massless kernel","sourceOffset":28993},{"kind":"section","title":"The massive Fredholm index","sourceOffset":35841},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Confinement and continuity at positive coupling","sourceOffset":37215},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The minima and their normal oscillators","sourceOffset":43457},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Small-coupling localization with norm exhaustion","sourceOffset":52698},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From localization to the index","sourceOffset":63706}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer \\(N\\ge2\\), \\[ \\dim\\Ker H_N=1. \\] The kernel consists of \\(\\Spin(9)\\)-invariant states and is even in the fermion grading just specified.","sourceOffset":3316,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Square of a charge","label":"prop:square","text":"On the gauge-invariant core, \\begin{equation} \\begin{aligned} \\cH(h,m)&=\\frac12p^2+U_{h,m} +\\frac{\\ii}{2}\\theta^t\\left(\\frac32mT-hK_j\\gamma^j\\right)\\theta, \\qquad K_j=\\ad(x_j),\\\\ 2U_{h,m}&=\\sum_{\\mathrm{cyc}} \\abs{h[x_a,x_b]-2mx_c}^2 +h^2\\sum_{\\substack{i<j\\\\j>3}}\\abs{[x_i,x_j]}^2 +m^2\\sum_{d=4}^9\\abs{x_d}^2,\\\\ D_v(h,m)^2&=\\cH(h,m)+mJ(R(v)), \\qquad R(v)_{ij}=s_jv^t\\gamma^i\\gamma^jTv. \\end{aligned} \\end{equation} The cyclic sum uses \\((a,b,c)=(1,2,3),(2,3,1),(3,1,2)\\). The matrix \\(R(v)\\) is skew, preserves \\(\\R^3\\oplus\\R^6\\), and has zero spin-basis average. The same formulas hold for an orthogonal direct sum of compact color algebras and abelian color spaces.","sourceOffset":19697,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Profile space","label":"def:profile-space","text":"For an unordered partition $C=(n_1,\\ldots,n_k)$ of $N$, with $k\\geq2$, let its labeled center space be \\[ \\mathcal Z_C=\\left\\{(c_1,\\ldots,c_k)\\in(\\R^9)^k: \\sum_\\alpha n_\\alpha c_\\alpha=0\\right\\}, \\qquad |\\dd c|^2=\\sum_\\alpha n_\\alpha|\\dd c_\\alpha|^2. \\] Write $\\mathcal Z_C^\\circ$ for the complement of the coincidence sets $c_\\alpha=c_\\beta$. Its center Clifford module is denoted by $\\mathcal F_C$. Set $\\mathcal K_n=\\Ker H_n$ and $\\mathcal K_1=\\C$, and form the Hilbert tensor product \\[ \\mathcal V_C=\\mathcal F_C\\,\\widehat\\otimes\\, \\widehat\\bigotimes_{\\alpha=1}^k\\mathcal K_{n_\\alpha}. \\] The pro","sourceOffset":95080,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":163394,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-unique-threshold-bound-state-of-the-SU-N-BFSS-model-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/The-unique-threshold-bound-state-of-the-SU-N-BFSS-model-September-24-2026/build/manuscript/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/The-unique-threshold-bound-state-of-the-SU-N-BFSS-model-September-24-2026/build/manuscript/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"270-02","slug":"Positive-eigenvalues-of-the-relative-SU-2-BFSS-Hamiltonian-October-5-2026","familyId":"270","title":"Positive eigenvalues of the relative SU(2) BFSS Hamiltonian","abstract":"We prove that the relative $\\mathop{\\mathrm{SU}}\\nolimits (2)$ BFSS Hamiltonian has infinitely many positive eigenvalues tending to infinity, with square-integrable eigenvectors. The operator is defined by closing the gauge-invariant supercharge form. This refutes, at N = 2, the exclusion of normalizable positive-energy states stated in the original BFSS paper.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the undeformed relative $\\mathrm{SU}(N)$ BFSS model has exactly one normalizable zero-energy state for every finite N ≥ 2, resolving the threshold-bound-state conjecture. For $\\mathrm{SU}(2)$, a companion proves infinitely many normalizable positive-energy eigenstates with unbounded energies, contradicting the original BFSS paper's exclusion of additional bound states at N = 2.","pdfPath":"preprints/Positive-eigenvalues-of-the-relative-SU-2-BFSS-Hamiltonian-October-5-2026/positive-eigenvalues-relative-su2-bfss.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Positive-eigenvalues-of-the-relative-SU-2-BFSS-Hamiltonian-October-5-2026/positive-eigenvalues-relative-su2-bfss.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Positive-eigenvalues-of-the-relative-SU-2-BFSS-Hamiltonian-October-5-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Positive-eigenvalues-of-the-relative-SU-2-BFSS-Hamiltonian-October-5-2026/build/positive-eigenvalues-relative-su2-bfss.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Positive-eigenvalues-of-the-relative-SU-2-BFSS-Hamiltonian-October-5-2026/build/positive-eigenvalues-relative-su2-bfss.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the relative $\\SU(2)$ BFSS Hamiltonian has infinitely many positive eigenvalues tending to infinity, with square-integrable eigenvectors. The operator is defined by closing the gauge-invariant supercharge form. This refutes, at $N=2$, the exclusion of normalizable positive-energy states stated in the original BFSS paper.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":384},{"kind":"section","title":"The quadratic form and rotations","sourceOffset":8651},{"kind":"section","title":"Transverse confinement in large rotation types","sourceOffset":15527},{"kind":"section","title":"Physical rotation sectors and positive eigenvalues","sourceOffset":26490}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are an orthonormal sequence $\\Psi_j\\in\\Dom(H)$ and real numbers $E_j>0$ tending to infinity such that \\[ H\\Psi_j=E_j\\Psi_j. \\] In particular, $\\sigma_{\\mathrm p}(H)\\cap(0,\\infty)$ is infinite.","sourceOffset":3831,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Confinement in a rotation sector","label":"prop:confinement","text":"Let $C$ be as in \\eqref{eq:fermion-bound}, and choose an integer $m\\geq0$ such that $c_m=(m+9)/3-C>0$. If $\\lambda_2>m+M$, every $\\Psi\\in\\Dom(q)\\cap\\Hphys_\\lambda$ belongs to $H^1(\\R^{27};\\F)$, satisfies $|x|^{1/2}\\Psi\\in L^2(\\R^{27};\\F)$, and obeys \\begin{equation} q(\\Psi)\\ \\geq\\ \\tfrac14\\norm{\\nabla\\Psi}_2^2 +c_m\\int_{\\R^{27}}|x|\\norm{\\Psi(x)}^2\\,dx. \\end{equation} The operator $H_\\lambda$ has compact resolvent and $\\ker H_\\lambda=\\{0\\}$.","sourceOffset":23414,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":34409,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Positive-eigenvalues-of-the-relative-SU-2-BFSS-Hamiltonian-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"271-01","slug":"Blochs-Law-for-Finite-Range-Heisenberg-Ferromagnets-in-Three-Dimensions-October-5-2026","familyId":"271","title":"Bloch's Law for Finite-Range Heisenberg Ferromagnets in Three Dimensions","abstract":"We prove Bloch's T3/2 law for the spontaneous magnetization of three-dimensional quantum Heisenberg ferromagnets at every fixed positive quantum spin. The result holds for every nonnegative symmetric finite-range interaction whose support generates ℤ3, including spatially anisotropic couplings. The leading magnetization deficit has the exact coefficient determined by the determinant of the quadratic one-magnon dispersion. The thermodynamic limit is taken before the right field derivative at zero, and the low-temperature limit is taken last.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Bloch's T3/2 law with its exact coefficient for three-dimensional quantum Heisenberg ferromagnets at every positive quantum spin, allowing nonnegative symmetric finite-range couplings whose support generates ℤ3. The thermodynamic limit precedes the zero-field derivative and low-temperature limit. The family also proves spontaneous magnetization for nearest-neighbor models in every dimension d ≥ 3 and determines the first lattice correction for three-dimensional nearest-neighbor couplings.","pdfPath":"preprints/Blochs-Law-for-Finite-Range-Heisenberg-Ferromagnets-in-Three-Dimensions-October-5-2026/bloch-law-heisenberg.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Blochs-Law-for-Finite-Range-Heisenberg-Ferromagnets-in-Three-Dimensions-October-5-2026/bloch-law-heisenberg.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Blochs-Law-for-Finite-Range-Heisenberg-Ferromagnets-in-Three-Dimensions-October-5-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Blochs-Law-for-Finite-Range-Heisenberg-Ferromagnets-in-Three-Dimensions-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Blochs-Law-for-Finite-Range-Heisenberg-Ferromagnets-in-Three-Dimensions-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Blochs-Law-for-Finite-Range-Heisenberg-Ferromagnets-in-Three-Dimensions-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-pins.tex","preprints/Blochs-Law-for-Finite-Range-Heisenberg-Ferromagnets-in-Three-Dimensions-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-geometry.tex","preprints/Blochs-Law-for-Finite-Range-Heisenberg-Ferromagnets-in-Three-Dimensions-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-bootstrap.tex","preprints/Blochs-Law-for-Finite-Range-Heisenberg-Ferromagnets-in-Three-Dimensions-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-returns.tex","preprints/Blochs-Law-for-Finite-Range-Heisenberg-Ferromagnets-in-Three-Dimensions-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-ideal.tex","preprints/Blochs-Law-for-Finite-Range-Heisenberg-Ferromagnets-in-Three-Dimensions-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-pressure.tex","preprints/Blochs-Law-for-Finite-Range-Heisenberg-Ferromagnets-in-Three-Dimensions-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Blochs-Law-for-Finite-Range-Heisenberg-Ferromagnets-in-Three-Dimensions-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Bloch's $T^{3/2}$ law for the spontaneous magnetization of three-dimensional quantum Heisenberg ferromagnets at every fixed positive quantum spin. The result holds for every nonnegative symmetric finite-range interaction whose support generates $\\Z^3$, including spatially anisotropic couplings. The leading magnetization deficit has the exact coefficient determined by the determinant of the quadratic one-magnon dispersion. The thermodynamic limit is taken before the right field derivative at zero, and the low-temperature limit is taken last.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":618},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and the result","sourceOffset":1195},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and dependencies","sourceOffset":3896},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How returns determine the coefficient","sourceOffset":7110},{"kind":"section","title":"Exchange pictures and pinned returns","sourceOffset":11095},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Spin slots and the exchange law","sourceOffset":11729},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pins, boundary approximation, and mark counts","sourceOffset":16414},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Single and simultaneous pin tests","sourceOffset":21622},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exposing the cycles that miss the pins","sourceOffset":28545},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removing the cycle condition from a first moment","sourceOffset":34185},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The fresh walk","sourceOffset":39955},{"kind":"section","title":"Kernels and the geometry of available slots","sourceOffset":44306}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Bloch's law","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed $S\\in\\{\\tfrac12,1,\\tfrac32,\\ldots\\}$ and every interaction satisfying \\eqref{eq:interaction}, \\begin{equation} \\lim_{\\beta\\to\\infty}\\beta^{3/2}\\bigl(S-m_{S,J}(\\beta)\\bigr) =\\frac{\\zeta(3/2)}{8\\pi^{3/2}\\sqrt{\\det D_{S,J}}}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":3234,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Loop representation","label":"prop:loop-representation","text":"For every finite cube, every $\\beta>0$, and every real $h$, \\begin{equation} Z_\\Lambda(h):=\\Tr e^{-\\beta(H^J_{S,N}-hM_{S,N})} =e^{\\beta w_\\Lambda/4}\\, \\mathbb E_{\\rm b}\\sum_\\sigma e^{\\beta hM(\\sigma)}. \\end{equation} At zero field the Gibbs spectral law of total magnetization is obtained by weighting each picture by $2^{\\#\\{\\text{cycles}\\}}$, coloring its cycles independently and fairly, and evaluating $M(\\sigma)$. More precisely, for every function $\\Phi$ on the finite spectrum of $M_{S,N}$, \\begin{equation} \\frac{\\Tr\\bigl(e^{-\\beta H^J_{S,N}}\\Phi(M_{S,N})\\bigr)}{Z_\\Lambda(0)} =\\frac{\\mathbb E_{\\rm b}\\sum_\\sigma\\Phi(M(\\sigma))} {\\mathbb E_{\\rm b}2^{\\#\\{\\text{cycles}\\}}}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":13561,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Sparse collections","label":"def:sparse","text":"A finite collection of slot points with site coordinates \\((x_i)\\) is sparse if \\begin{equation} \\#\\{i:\\bar x_i\\in B_h(z)\\}\\le L(1+h)^\\alpha \\qquad(z\\in\\Z^3,\\ h\\ge0). \\end{equation} Repeated bar coordinates count with their multiplicities, including points from different cosets. The same definition applies to a list of centers in \\(\\Z^3\\). Every subcollection remains sparse.","sourceOffset":50564,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Regular slice","label":"def:regular","text":"A slice \\(t\\) is regular for the anchor \\(z\\) at radius \\(R\\) if the following hold in every coset \\(\\gamma\\): \\begin{enumerate} \\item for every \\(w\\in B_{11R}(z)\\), \\(\\#(\\mathcal O_t^\\gamma\\cap B_{M_*}(w))<6\\); \\item for every \\(w\\in\\mathcal O_t^\\gamma\\setminus B_{10R}(z)\\), \\(\\operatorname{rad}_w\\mathcal C_t^\\gamma(w) \\le(1+|w-z|_\\infty)^\\nu\\). \\end{enumerate} The anchor itself need not be an available site.","sourceOffset":52473,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/271.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":133413,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":17,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Blochs-Law-for-Finite-Range-Heisenberg-Ferromagnets-in-Three-Dimensions-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"271-02","slug":"The-first-lattice-correction-to-Blochs-law-October-5-2026","familyId":"271","title":"The first lattice correction to Bloch's law","abstract":"We prove the first lattice correction to Bloch's law for the three-dimensional nearest-neighbor quantum Heisenberg ferromagnet at every fixed spin $S=\\tfrac12,1,\\tfrac32,\\ldots$. The spontaneous-magnetization deficit agrees with the full ideal-magnon density up to $o(\\beta^{-5/2})$. In addition to the leading Bloch term, this gives the correction $3\\zeta(5/2)(\\beta S)^{-5/2}/(128\\pi^{3/2})$. The magnetization is the right derivative at zero field of the thermodynamic pressure; the volume limit precedes the field derivative, and the low-temperature limit is taken last.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Bloch's T3/2 law with its exact coefficient for three-dimensional quantum Heisenberg ferromagnets at every positive quantum spin, allowing nonnegative symmetric finite-range couplings whose support generates ℤ3. The thermodynamic limit precedes the zero-field derivative and low-temperature limit. The family also proves spontaneous magnetization for nearest-neighbor models in every dimension d ≥ 3 and determines the first lattice correction for three-dimensional nearest-neighbor couplings.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-first-lattice-correction-to-Blochs-law-October-5-2026/first-lattice-correction-bloch-law.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-first-lattice-correction-to-Blochs-law-October-5-2026/first-lattice-correction-bloch-law.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-first-lattice-correction-to-Blochs-law-October-5-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/The-first-lattice-correction-to-Blochs-law-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-first-lattice-correction-to-Blochs-law-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-first-lattice-correction-to-Blochs-law-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-loops.tex","preprints/The-first-lattice-correction-to-Blochs-law-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-capacity.tex","preprints/The-first-lattice-correction-to-Blochs-law-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-geometry.tex","preprints/The-first-lattice-correction-to-Blochs-law-October-5-2026/build/figures/isolated-surgery.tex","preprints/The-first-lattice-correction-to-Blochs-law-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-profiles.tex","preprints/The-first-lattice-correction-to-Blochs-law-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-sources.tex","preprints/The-first-lattice-correction-to-Blochs-law-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-scalar.tex","preprints/The-first-lattice-correction-to-Blochs-law-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-energy.tex","preprints/The-first-lattice-correction-to-Blochs-law-October-5-2026/build/figures/period-averaging.tex","preprints/The-first-lattice-correction-to-Blochs-law-October-5-2026/build/sections/08-pressure.tex","preprints/The-first-lattice-correction-to-Blochs-law-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-first-lattice-correction-to-Blochs-law-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the first lattice correction to Bloch's law for the three-dimensional nearest-neighbor quantum Heisenberg ferromagnet at every fixed spin $S=\\tfrac12,1,\\tfrac32,\\ldots$. The spontaneous-magnetization deficit agrees with the full ideal-magnon density up to $o(\\beta^{-5/2})$. In addition to the leading Bloch term, this gives the correction $3\\zeta(5/2)(\\beta S)^{-5/2}/(128\\pi^{3/2})$. The magnetization is the right derivative at zero field of the thermodynamic pressure; the volume limit precedes the field derivative, and the low-temperature limit is taken last.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":637},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and the result","sourceOffset":1064},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and inputs","sourceOffset":2917},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The comparison behind the proof","sourceOffset":5731},{"kind":"section","title":"Pinned cycles and the return walk","sourceOffset":8124},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The picture and its pin law","sourceOffset":8509},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exposure and a fresh walk","sourceOffset":10578},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Kernel conventions and energy","sourceOffset":14033},{"kind":"section","title":"A capacity comparison on one period","sourceOffset":17743},{"kind":"section","title":"Sparse obstacles and local propagation","sourceOffset":24017},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Geometric inputs and the snapshot estimate","sourceOffset":24773},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Short blocks and isolated strands","sourceOffset":32826}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every fixed $S\\in\\{\\tfrac12,1,\\tfrac32,\\ldots\\}$, \\[ \\lim_{\\beta\\to\\infty}(\\beta S)^{5/2} \\bigl[S-m_S(\\beta)-n_S^{\\mathrm{sw}}(\\beta)\\bigr]=0. \\]","sourceOffset":2342,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:expansion","text":"For every fixed positive half-integer $S$, \\[ S-m_S(\\beta) =\\frac{\\zeta(3/2)}{8\\pi^{3/2}}(\\beta S)^{-3/2} +\\frac{3\\zeta(5/2)}{128\\pi^{3/2}}(\\beta S)^{-5/2} +o\\bigl((\\beta S)^{-5/2}\\bigr). \\]","sourceOffset":2664,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/271.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":130377,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-first-lattice-correction-to-Blochs-law-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"271-03","slug":"The-spherical-magnetization-law-for-the-three-dimensional-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-October-5-2026","familyId":"271","title":"The spherical magnetization law for the three-dimensional quantum Heisenberg ferromagnet","abstract":"We prove the spherical magnetization law for the three-dimensional nearest-neighbor isotropic quantum Heisenberg ferromagnet at every fixed positive quantum spin and every sufficiently low fixed positive temperature. As the even periodic cubes grow, the symmetric zero-field magnetization converges in moments to a uniform direction with a deterministic positive magnitude. This magnitude equals the right derivative at zero field of the infinite-volume pressure. The law is expressed through self-adjoint linear combinations of the spin components and requires no joint measurement of noncommuting observables.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Bloch's T3/2 law with its exact coefficient for three-dimensional quantum Heisenberg ferromagnets at every positive quantum spin, allowing nonnegative symmetric finite-range couplings whose support generates ℤ3. The thermodynamic limit precedes the zero-field derivative and low-temperature limit. The family also proves spontaneous magnetization for nearest-neighbor models in every dimension d ≥ 3 and determines the first lattice correction for three-dimensional nearest-neighbor couplings.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-spherical-magnetization-law-for-the-three-dimensional-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-October-5-2026/spherical-magnetization.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-spherical-magnetization-law-for-the-three-dimensional-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-October-5-2026/spherical-magnetization.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-spherical-magnetization-law-for-the-three-dimensional-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-October-5-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/The-spherical-magnetization-law-for-the-three-dimensional-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-spherical-magnetization-law-for-the-three-dimensional-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-spherical-magnetization-law-for-the-three-dimensional-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-exchange.tex","preprints/The-spherical-magnetization-law-for-the-three-dimensional-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-sparse-pins.tex","preprints/The-spherical-magnetization-law-for-the-three-dimensional-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-October-5-2026/build/figures/sparse-ghost.tex","preprints/The-spherical-magnetization-law-for-the-three-dimensional-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-spatial-comparison.tex","preprints/The-spherical-magnetization-law-for-the-three-dimensional-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-October-5-2026/build/figures/holes-comparison.tex","preprints/The-spherical-magnetization-law-for-the-three-dimensional-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-zero-field.tex","preprints/The-spherical-magnetization-law-for-the-three-dimensional-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-equilibrium.tex","preprints/The-spherical-magnetization-law-for-the-three-dimensional-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-pressure.tex","preprints/The-spherical-magnetization-law-for-the-three-dimensional-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-October-5-2026/build/sections/08-global-spin.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-spherical-magnetization-law-for-the-three-dimensional-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the spherical magnetization law for the three-dimensional nearest-neighbor isotropic quantum Heisenberg ferromagnet at every fixed positive quantum spin and every sufficiently low fixed positive temperature. As the even periodic cubes grow, the symmetric zero-field magnetization converges in moments to a uniform direction with a deterministic positive magnitude. This magnitude equals the right derivative at zero field of the infinite-volume pressure. The law is expressed through self-adjoint linear combinations of the spin components and requires no joint measurement of noncommuting observables.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":674},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model and result","sourceOffset":1332},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and antecedents","sourceOffset":3561},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two parts of the proof","sourceOffset":6775},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":11505},{"kind":"section","title":"Exchange cycles, pins, and zero-free traces","sourceOffset":12509},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Symmetric slots and permutation layers","sourceOffset":13006},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pins and exposure","sourceOffset":17671},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The conditional next-pin first moment","sourceOffset":25578},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Disk contractions and trace zero-freeness","sourceOffset":30748},{"kind":"section","title":"Sparse pins on the torus","sourceOffset":35675},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Soft pins and the ghost representation","sourceOffset":36254}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Spherical magnetization law","label":"thm:main","text":"For each fixed $S$ there is a finite $\\beta_0(S)$ such that, for every fixed finite $\\beta\\ge\\beta_0(S)$, one has $m=m_{S,\\beta}>0$ and \\begin{equation} \\lim_{\\substack{L\\to\\infty\\\\L\\text{ even}}} \\left\\langle \\exp\\!\\left(\\frac1V\\sum_{a=1}^3t_aM_L^a\\right) \\right\\rangle_{\\beta,L} =\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb S^2}e^{m t\\cdot n}\\dd\\sigma(n) =\\frac{\\sinh(m|t|)}{m|t|} \\qquad(t\\in\\R^3). \\end{equation} Here $\\sigma$ is surface-area measure on $\\mathbb S^2$, and the quotient at $t=0$ is interpreted as $1$. In addition, \\begin{equation} \\lim_{\\substack{L\\to\\infty\\\\L\\text{ even}}} \\frac1{V^2}\\sum_{x,y\\in\\Lambda_L} \\langle S_x\\cdot S_y\\rangle_{\\beta,L}=m^2. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":2491,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Sparse-pin bound","label":"prop:sparse-pins","text":"Fix \\(S\\) and \\(C_s\\). There is \\(\\beta_0<\\infty\\) such that, for every fixed \\(\\beta\\ge\\beta_0\\), there is \\(L_*(\\beta)<\\infty\\) with the following property. For every even \\(L\\ge L_*(\\beta)\\), every \\(q\\) satisfying \\(L^{-5/2}\\le q<1\\), every pin set \\(E\\) as above, and every sparse set \\(T\\) of real slot points at one deterministic time, \\begin{equation} \\PP_E(T\\subseteq\\mathcal B_E)\\le p^{|T|}, \\qquad p=\\beta^{-9/10}. \\end{equation} The constants and thresholds are independent of the number, locations, and times of the extra pins.","sourceOffset":40072,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/271.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":159787,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-spherical-magnetization-law-for-the-three-dimensional-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"271-04","slug":"Spontaneous-magnetization-in-the-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-September-24-2026","familyId":"271","title":"Spontaneous magnetization in the quantum Heisenberg ferromagnet","abstract":"For every dimension d ≥ 3 and every spin $S\\in\\{\\frac12,1,\\frac32,\\ldots\\}$, we prove that the nearest-neighbor isotropic quantum Heisenberg ferromagnet has a translation-invariant, spontaneously magnetized equilibrium state at every sufficiently low positive temperature. The same state satisfies the KMS condition for the zero-field dynamics and has magnetization at least $S/4$. This resolves the low-temperature ordering problem in the spontaneous-magnetization formulation.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Bloch's T3/2 law with its exact coefficient for three-dimensional quantum Heisenberg ferromagnets at every positive quantum spin, allowing nonnegative symmetric finite-range couplings whose support generates ℤ3. The thermodynamic limit precedes the zero-field derivative and low-temperature limit. The family also proves spontaneous magnetization for nearest-neighbor models in every dimension d ≥ 3 and determines the first lattice correction for three-dimensional nearest-neighbor couplings.","pdfPath":"preprints/Spontaneous-magnetization-in-the-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Spontaneous-magnetization-in-the-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Spontaneous-magnetization-in-the-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-September-24-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Spontaneous-magnetization-in-the-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-September-24-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Spontaneous-magnetization-in-the-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Spontaneous-magnetization-in-the-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/01-loops.tex","preprints/Spontaneous-magnetization-in-the-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/02-pin-test.tex","preprints/Spontaneous-magnetization-in-the-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/03-exposure.tex","preprints/Spontaneous-magnetization-in-the-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-September-24-2026/build/source/figures/exposure-first-moment.tex","preprints/Spontaneous-magnetization-in-the-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/04-sparse-geometry.tex","preprints/Spontaneous-magnetization-in-the-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/05-flows.tex","preprints/Spontaneous-magnetization-in-the-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-September-24-2026/build/source/figures/hull-routing.tex","preprints/Spontaneous-magnetization-in-the-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/06-leakage.tex","preprints/Spontaneous-magnetization-in-the-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-September-24-2026/build/source/sections/07-kms.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Spontaneous-magnetization-in-the-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-September-24-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every dimension $d\\geq3$ and every spin $S\\in\\{\\frac12,1,\\frac32,\\ldots\\}$, we prove that the nearest-neighbor isotropic quantum Heisenberg ferromagnet has a translation-invariant, spontaneously magnetized equilibrium state at every sufficiently low positive temperature. The same state satisfies the KMS condition for the zero-field dynamics and has magnetization at least $S/4$. This resolves the low-temperature ordering problem in the spontaneous-magnetization formulation.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":544},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and the result","sourceOffset":1086},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context and significance","sourceOffset":4206},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and the main mechanisms","sourceOffset":8916},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A hard-core boson consequence","sourceOffset":13499},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":15404},{"kind":"section","title":"Spin slots, loops, and pins","sourceOffset":16080},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pin laws and the boundary limit","sourceOffset":25735},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Factorial domination for exchange marks","sourceOffset":29646},{"kind":"section","title":"A simultaneous pin test","sourceOffset":34123},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The stability operations","sourceOffset":37064},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Layering the exchange picture and removing unpinned points","sourceOffset":41727}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $d\\geq3$ and every $S\\in\\{\\frac12,1,\\frac32,\\ldots\\}$, there is a finite $\\beta_0(d,S)>0$ such that, for every finite $\\beta\\geq\\beta_0(d,S)$, the dynamics associated with \\eqref{eq:hamiltonian} has a translation-invariant $\\beta$-KMS state $\\omega$ satisfying \\[ \\omega(S_0^z)\\ge S/4. \\]","sourceOffset":3570,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Exact spin-to-loop representation","label":"prop:spin-loops","text":"For every finite cube $\\Lambda$, spin $S$, and $0<\\beta<\\infty$ as above, the partition function is \\begin{equation} Z_\\Lambda(\\beta):=\\Tr_{\\mathcal H_\\Lambda}e^{-\\beta H_\\Lambda} =e^{\\beta q_\\Lambda/4}\\, \\mathbb E_{\\mathrm b}2^{c(\\omega)}. \\end{equation} More generally, for every function $\\Phi$ on the finite spectrum of $M_\\Lambda$, \\begin{equation} \\frac{\\Tr_{\\mathcal H_\\Lambda} \\bigl(e^{-\\beta H_\\Lambda}\\Phi(M_\\Lambda)\\bigr)} {Z_\\Lambda(\\beta)} =\\frac{\\displaystyle\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm b} \\sum_{\\varepsilon\\in\\{-1,1\\}^{\\{\\text{cycles of }\\omega\\}}} \\Phi\\!\\left(\\frac12\\sum_C\\varepsilon_C a_C\\right)} {\\displaystyle\\mathbb E_{\\mathrm b}2^{c(\\omega)}}. \\end{equation} Thus the spectral law of $M_\\Lambda$ is obtained by weighting the base picture by $2^{c(\\omega)}$, then coloring each cycle independently up or down with equal probabilities, and summing the corresponding $+1/2$ or $-1/2$ over its slots at time $0$.","sourceOffset":22148,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Regular anchor","label":"def:regular-anchor","text":"For a spatial anchor \\(x\\in\\Z^d\\), call time \\(t\\) regular for \\(x\\) at scale \\(R\\) if both conditions hold: \\begin{enumerate} \\item For every \\(z\\in B_{11R}(x)\\), \\(\\#(\\mathcal O_t\\cap B_{M_*}(z))<2d\\). \\item For every \\(z\\in\\mathcal O_t\\setminus B_{10R}(x)\\), \\(\\operatorname{rad}_z\\mathcal C_t(z) \\le(1+|z-x|_\\infty)^\\nu\\). \\end{enumerate} For \\(i\\in T\\), “regular for \\(i\\)” means regular for its spatial coordinate \\(x_i\\).","sourceOffset":99767,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/271.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves spontaneous magnetization for the nearest-neighbor isotropic quantum Heisenberg ferromagnet on $\\mathbb Z^d$ for every $d\\ge3$ and every spin $S\\in\\{\\tfrac12,1,\\tfrac32,\\ldots\\}$. At every sufficiently low positive temperature, it constructs a translation-invariant equilibrium state satisfying the KMS condition for the zero-field dynamics and having magnetization at least $S/4$.\n\nIt also proves convergence of the finite-volume dynamics to the infinite-volume dynamics used in the KMS statement.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/271.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":181714,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Spontaneous-magnetization-in-the-quantum-Heisenberg-ferromagnet-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"272-01","slug":"Entanglement-with-zero-distillable-secret-key-in-local-dimension-ten-September-27-2026","familyId":"272","title":"Entanglement with zero distillable secret key in local dimension ten","abstract":"We construct an entangled state on $\\mathbb C^{10}\\otimes\\mathbb C^{10}$ with zero distillable secret key for the local-instrument protocols specified here. They allow joint processing of all copies and unlimited two-way public communication, with the input as the only shared private resource and an eavesdropper holding a purification and the complete public record. The construction also disproves the unrestricted two-map PPT-composition conjecture. A separate trace-preserving PPT channel on $M_{21}(\\mathbb C)$ has a square that is not entanglement breaking.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs an entangled state on $\\mathbb C^{10}\\otimes\\mathbb C^{10}$ with zero distillable secret key for the specified local-instrument protocols that complete almost surely. These allow joint local processing and authenticated two-way public communication, with no other shared private resource and an eavesdropper holding the input purification and public record. A trace-preserving PPT channel on $M_{21}(\\mathbb C)$ whose square is not entanglement breaking disproves Christandl's PPT-square conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/Entanglement-with-zero-distillable-secret-key-in-local-dimension-ten-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Entanglement-with-zero-distillable-secret-key-in-local-dimension-ten-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Entanglement-with-zero-distillable-secret-key-in-local-dimension-ten-September-27-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Entanglement-with-zero-distillable-secret-key-in-local-dimension-ten-September-27-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Entanglement-with-zero-distillable-secret-key-in-local-dimension-ten-September-27-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Entanglement-with-zero-distillable-secret-key-in-local-dimension-ten-September-27-2026/build/source/sections/foundations.tex","preprints/Entanglement-with-zero-distillable-secret-key-in-local-dimension-ten-September-27-2026/build/source/sections/coherence.tex","preprints/Entanglement-with-zero-distillable-secret-key-in-local-dimension-ten-September-27-2026/build/source/sections/transcripts.tex","preprints/Entanglement-with-zero-distillable-secret-key-in-local-dimension-ten-September-27-2026/build/source/sections/secret-key.tex","preprints/Entanglement-with-zero-distillable-secret-key-in-local-dimension-ten-September-27-2026/build/source/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Entanglement-with-zero-distillable-secret-key-in-local-dimension-ten-September-27-2026/build/source/sections/certificate.tex","preprints/Entanglement-with-zero-distillable-secret-key-in-local-dimension-ten-September-27-2026/build/source/sections/applications.tex","preprints/Entanglement-with-zero-distillable-secret-key-in-local-dimension-ten-September-27-2026/build/source/sections/projection.tex","preprints/Entanglement-with-zero-distillable-secret-key-in-local-dimension-ten-September-27-2026/build/source/sections/combinatorial.tex","preprints/Entanglement-with-zero-distillable-secret-key-in-local-dimension-ten-September-27-2026/build/source/appendices/arithmetic.tex","preprints/Entanglement-with-zero-distillable-secret-key-in-local-dimension-ten-September-27-2026/build/source/appendices/supplementary-checks.tex","preprints/Entanglement-with-zero-distillable-secret-key-in-local-dimension-ten-September-27-2026/build/source/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Entanglement-with-zero-distillable-secret-key-in-local-dimension-ten-September-27-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct an entangled state on $\\C^{10}\\otimes\\C^{10}$ with zero distillable secret key for the local-instrument protocols specified here. They allow joint processing of all copies and unlimited two-way public communication, with the input as the only shared private resource and an eavesdropper holding a purification and the complete public record. The construction also disproves the unrestricted two-map PPT-composition conjecture. A separate trace-preserving PPT channel on $\\Mat{21}$ has a square that is not entanglement breaking.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":589},{"kind":"section","title":"PPT maps and the represented class","sourceOffset":18257},{"kind":"section","title":"A four-effect coherence bound","sourceOffset":28230},{"kind":"section","title":"Complete public transcripts","sourceOffset":36194},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite-record mechanism","sourceOffset":36795},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The admitted protocol model","sourceOffset":39872},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Common effects at a complete transcript","sourceOffset":48358},{"kind":"section","title":"Separation from an ideal secret bit","sourceOffset":69287},{"kind":"section","title":"Tensor geometry from singular directions","sourceOffset":86372},{"kind":"section","title":"An integer pencil and its exact certificate","sourceOffset":95995},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The explicit matrix pencil","sourceOffset":96532},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The maximal minors and a nonzero quotient","sourceOffset":100656}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main-state","text":"There is an explicitly specified entangled density operator $\\rho$ on $\\C^{10}\\otimes\\C^{10}$ whose range contains no nonzero product vector and for which $K_D(\\rho)=0$. More precisely, let $n\\geq1$ and let $\\tau_{K_AK_BE'}$ be the normalized output of any admitted completed bit-output protocol of Definition \\ref{def:protocol} on $\\rho^{\\otimes n}$, where $E'$ retains an initial purification and the complete public transcript. For every state $\\sigma_{E'}$, \\begin{equation} \\left\\lVert \\tau_{K_AK_BE'}- \\frac12\\sum_{i=0}^1|i,i\\rangle\\langle i,i|\\otimes\\sigma_{E'} \\right\\rVert_1\\geq\\frac15. \\end{equation} Thus the corresponding trace distance, with its conventional factor $1/2$, is at least $1/10$.","sourceOffset":3013,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main-pair","text":"There are explicitly specified PPT maps $\\Phi_1,\\Phi_2:\\Mat{10}\\to\\Mat{10}$ for which \\[ Z=J(\\Phi_2\\circ\\Phi_1) \\] is nonzero and its range contains no nonzero product vector in $\\C^{10}\\otimes\\C^{10}$. In particular, the composition $\\Phi_2\\circ\\Phi_1$ is not entanglement breaking.","sourceOffset":4905,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:represented-class","text":"A density operator $\\omega$ on $\\C^a\\otimes\\C^b$ belongs to the represented class $\\mathscr C$ if there are positive integers $r,s$, a vector $\\chi\\in\\C^r\\otimes\\C^s$, and linear maps $\\mathcal L:\\Mat r\\to\\Mat a$ and $\\mathcal M:\\Mat s\\to\\Mat b$ such that \\begin{equation} \\omega=(\\mathcal L\\otimes\\mathcal M)(\\chi\\chi^*), \\qquad \\mathcal L,\\ \\mathcal L\\circ T_r,\\ \\mathcal M,\\ \\mathcal M\\circ T_s \\text{ are CP}. \\end{equation} Only $\\omega$ is required to have trace one. The vector $\\chi$ may be unnormalized, and the maps need not preserve trace.","sourceOffset":24040,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Admitted local protocols","label":"def:protocol","text":"Let $A$ and $B$ be finite-dimensional complex input spaces. An admitted protocol consists of local quantum instruments, local memories and randomness, and authenticated two-way public classical communication. Each party may process its whole input space jointly. Its data and their joint laws satisfy the following requirements. \\begin{enumerate} \\item Initial resources. Before interaction, there is a recorded standard Borel public variable $C_0$, possibly constant, whose law is fixed once the protocol is chosen. It is independent of the supplied input together with any purifying or untouched re","sourceOffset":40413,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/272.md","scopeText":"The paper's entanglement construction yields counterexamples to PPT-composition claims. The linked formalization covers these consequences: two explicitly specified PPT maps on $10\\times10$ complex matrices have a composition that is not entanglement breaking, and the nonzero Choi matrix of that composition has no nonzero product vector in its range. A separate trace-preserving PPT channel on $21\\times21$ matrices has a square that is not entanglement breaking.\n\nThe paper's zero-distillable-secret-key statement is outside these two selected Comparator statements.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/272.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":188422,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Entanglement-with-zero-distillable-secret-key-in-local-dimension-ten-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"273-01","slug":"The-entropy-photon-number-inequality-September-24-2026","familyId":"273","title":"The entropy photon-number inequality","abstract":"We prove the entropy photon-number inequality for two independent bosonic inputs with finite mean energy, for every finite number of modes. This resolves the entropy photon-number conjecture in this finite-energy setting while allowing arbitrary entanglement among the modes within either input. As a consequence, we determine the exact minimum output entropy at fixed input entropy for every finite tensor power of an identical thermal attenuator, over finite-energy inputs that may be entangled across modes. We also obtain the exact classical capacity region of the degraded two-receiver pure-loss bosonic broadcast channel under a mean photon constraint.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the entropy photon-number inequality for beam-splitter mixing of two independent finite-energy bosonic inputs in any finite number of modes: the output's entropy photon number is at least the transmissivity-weighted average of the inputs'. Arbitrary entanglement within each input is allowed, and product thermal inputs attain equality even when their entropies differ.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-entropy-photon-number-inequality-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-entropy-photon-number-inequality-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-entropy-photon-number-inequality-September-24-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/The-entropy-photon-number-inequality-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-entropy-photon-number-inequality-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/The-entropy-photon-number-inequality-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-preliminaries.tex","preprints/The-entropy-photon-number-inequality-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-interpolation.tex","preprints/The-entropy-photon-number-inequality-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-minimization.tex","preprints/The-entropy-photon-number-inequality-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-metric.tex","preprints/The-entropy-photon-number-inequality-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-stationarity.tex","preprints/The-entropy-photon-number-inequality-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-broadcast.tex","preprints/The-entropy-photon-number-inequality-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-entropy-photon-number-inequality-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the entropy photon-number inequality for two independent bosonic inputs with finite mean energy, for every finite number of modes. This resolves the entropy photon-number conjecture in this finite-energy setting while allowing arbitrary entanglement among the modes within either input. As a consequence, we determine the exact minimum output entropy at fixed input entropy for every finite tensor power of an identical thermal attenuator, over finite-energy inputs that may be entangled across modes. We also obtain the exact classical capacity region of the degraded two-receiver pure-loss bosonic broadcast channel under a mean photon constraint.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":740},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and earlier results","sourceOffset":6809},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":12109},{"kind":"section","title":"Bosonic states, energy, and entropy","sourceOffset":15423},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Entropy estimates and compactness","sourceOffset":16439},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Passive mixing","sourceOffset":21376},{"kind":"section","title":"Interpolation of diagonal form comparisons","sourceOffset":23498},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Operations that preserve comparison","sourceOffset":25810},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A Stieltjes representation","sourceOffset":28218},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The additional weight and the final minimization","sourceOffset":37956},{"kind":"section","title":"A regularized minimum and its Hessian","sourceOffset":41382},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The auxiliary ports and the functional","sourceOffset":41662}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Entropy photon-number inequality","label":"thm:epni","text":"Let \\(n\\ge1\\) be finite, and let \\(\\rho_A,\\rho_B\\) be states on \\(n\\) bosonic modes, with annihilation operators \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_n\\) and \\(b_1,\\ldots,b_n\\), respectively, satisfying \\[ \\Tr\\rho_A\\sum_{j=1}^n a_j^\\dagger a_j<\\infty, \\qquad \\Tr\\rho_B\\sum_{j=1}^n b_j^\\dagger b_j<\\infty. \\] The joint input is \\(\\rho_A\\otimes\\rho_B\\); no independence assumption is made among the modes within either state. For \\(0\\le\\eta\\le1\\), let \\(\\rho_C\\) be the reduced state of the modes \\[ c_j=\\sqrt\\eta\\,a_j+\\sqrt{1-\\eta}\\,b_j,\\qquad 1\\le j\\le n, \\] after passive beam-splitter mixing. Then \\begin{equation} g^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{S(\\rho_C)}n\\right) \\ge \\eta\\,g^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{S(\\rho_A)}n\\right) +(1-\\eta)\\,g^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{S(\\rho_B)}n\\right), \\end{equation} where \\(S(\\rho)=-\\Tr\\rho\\log\\rho\\).","sourceOffset":1663,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Constrained entropy minimum for identical thermal attenuators","label":"cor:thermal-attenuator","text":"Let \\(n\\ge1\\) be finite, let \\(s,N_B\\ge0\\) be finite, and let \\(0\\le\\eta\\le1\\). Every state \\(\\rho\\) on \\(n\\) modes with \\(\\Tr\\rho\\sum_{j=1}^n a_j^\\dagger a_j<\\infty\\) satisfies \\begin{equation} \\frac1n S\\!\\left(\\mathcal E_{\\eta,N_B}^{\\otimes n}(\\rho)\\right) \\ge g\\!\\left(\\eta g^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{S(\\rho)}n\\right) +(1-\\eta)N_B\\right). \\end{equation} In particular, the exact constrained minimum is \\begin{equation} \\min_{\\substack{\\rho\\text{ a state on }n\\text{ modes}\\\\ \\Tr\\rho\\sum_{j=1}^n a_j^\\dagger a_j<\\infty\\\\ S(\\rho)=ns}} \\frac1n S\\!\\left(\\mathcal E_{\\eta,N_B}^{\\otimes n}(\\rho)\\right) =g\\!\\left(\\eta g^{-1}(s)+(1-\\eta)N_B\\right). \\end{equation} It is attained by the product thermal input \\(\\tau_{g^{-1}(s)}\\). No independence assumption is imposed among the modes of \\(\\rho\\); only the environment is required to be independent of that input.","sourceOffset":3405,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/273.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves the entropy photon-number inequality for two independent bosonic inputs with finite mean energy and any finite positive number $n$ of modes. Write $N(\\rho)=g^{-1}(S(\\rho)/n)$, where $S$ is von Neumann entropy and $g(x)=(x+1)\\log(x+1)-x\\log x$ is the thermal entropy per mode. For a beam splitter of transmissivity $0\\le\\eta\\le1$, its output satisfies $N(\\rho_C)\\ge\\eta N(\\rho_A)+(1-\\eta)N(\\rho_B)$.\n\nEntanglement among modes within either input is allowed. The thermal-attenuator minimum-output-entropy and broadcast-capacity consequences in the paper are outside this selected inequality.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/273.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":106812,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-entropy-photon-number-inequality-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"274-01","slug":"Product-projection-localization-and-the-QAC0-parity-lower-bound-September-24-2026","familyId":"274","title":"Product-projection localization and the QAC0 parity lower bound","abstract":"We prove that constant-depth quantum circuits with arbitrary one-qubit and unbounded-arity Toffoli gates cannot compute parity with any fixed positive worst-case advantage using polynomially many qubits. Ancillas start in zero, only one output qubit is measured, and all final garbage is unrestricted. This resolves Moore's parity conjecture in the measured-output model.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves Moore's parity conjecture in the measured-output model: constant-depth quantum circuits with arbitrary one-qubit gates, unbounded-arity Toffoli gates and polynomially many total qubits cannot compute parity with any fixed positive worst-case advantage. Ancillas start in zero, one output qubit is measured, and all other registers may be discarded. Xu–Li's reductions give the same bounded-error obstruction for strict majority.","pdfPath":"preprints/Product-projection-localization-and-the-QAC0-parity-lower-bound-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Product-projection-localization-and-the-QAC0-parity-lower-bound-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Product-projection-localization-and-the-QAC0-parity-lower-bound-September-24-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Product-projection-localization-and-the-QAC0-parity-lower-bound-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Product-projection-localization-and-the-QAC0-parity-lower-bound-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Product-projection-localization-and-the-QAC0-parity-lower-bound-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Product-projection-localization-and-the-QAC0-parity-lower-bound-September-24-2026/build/sections/localization.tex","preprints/Product-projection-localization-and-the-QAC0-parity-lower-bound-September-24-2026/build/sections/polynomial.tex","preprints/Product-projection-localization-and-the-QAC0-parity-lower-bound-September-24-2026/build/sections/depth_reduction.tex","preprints/Product-projection-localization-and-the-QAC0-parity-lower-bound-September-24-2026/build/sections/bootstrap.tex","preprints/Product-projection-localization-and-the-QAC0-parity-lower-bound-September-24-2026/build/sections/parity.tex","preprints/Product-projection-localization-and-the-QAC0-parity-lower-bound-September-24-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex","preprints/Product-projection-localization-and-the-QAC0-parity-lower-bound-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Product-projection-localization-and-the-QAC0-parity-lower-bound-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that constant-depth quantum circuits with arbitrary one-qubit and unbounded-arity Toffoli gates cannot compute parity with any fixed positive worst-case advantage using polynomially many qubits. Ancillas start in zero, only one output qubit is measured, and all final garbage is unrestricted. This resolves Moore's parity conjecture in the measured-output model.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":417},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":2535},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The localization argument","sourceOffset":6963},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Consequences","sourceOffset":12073},{"kind":"section","title":"Product projections and localization","sourceOffset":14023},{"kind":"section","title":"A polynomial approximation with controlled coefficients","sourceOffset":18287},{"kind":"section","title":"Depth reduction for small-support projections","sourceOffset":23053},{"kind":"section","title":"The finite scale induction","sourceOffset":30141},{"kind":"section","title":"From localization to parity","sourceOffset":37265},{"kind":"section","title":"Consequences of localization and parity","sourceOffset":40457},{"kind":"subsection","title":"State preparation","sourceOffset":41029},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Scalar-output Fourier concentration","sourceOffset":46847}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Parity lower bound","label":"thm:parity","text":"Fix a nonnegative integer \\(d\\), a real number \\(c\\ge1\\), and \\(0<\\varepsilon\\le1/2\\). For all sufficiently large \\(n\\), no circuit of depth at most \\(d\\) on \\(n\\) input qubits and at most \\(n^c\\) total qubits, using arbitrary one-qubit unitaries and unbounded-arity Toffoli gates, computes \\(\\parity(x)\\) with probability at least \\(1/2+\\varepsilon\\) for every \\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\). Gates in each layer have disjoint supports, ancillas start in \\(\\ket0\\), and one output qubit is measured; the other qubits may be discarded.","sourceOffset":1705,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Localization of conjugated product projections","label":"thm:localization","text":"Fix a nonnegative integer \\(d\\) and real numbers \\(0\\le s<t\\). For all sufficiently large \\(N\\), every \\(U\\in\\cU_d(N)\\) and every pair of counts \\(M,D\\) on \\(\\mathcal H_N\\) satisfy \\begin{equation} \\norm{[M\\ge N^t]\\,U[D=0]U^\\dagger[M=0]} \\le \\exp(-N^s). \\end{equation} The sufficient lower bound on \\(N\\) depends only on \\(d,s,t\\), not on the circuit, the counted subsets, or their reference vectors.","sourceOffset":17187,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/274.md","scopeText":"The formalization rules out bounded-error parity computation by constant-depth quantum circuits with polynomially many zero-initialized ancillary qubits. For every fixed depth, polynomial bound on the total number of qubits, and $0<\\varepsilon\\le1/2$, every sufficiently large input length has an input on which the measured-output parity success probability is less than $1/2+\\varepsilon$. A companion specialization gives success strictly below $2/3$. The formalization also includes the product-projection localization estimate supporting this bound.\n\nThe formalized result supplies the polynomial-size parity consequence of the paper. For every fixed circuit depth and polynomial bound on the number of qubits, all sufficiently large input lengths have an input on which any such circuit computes","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/274.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":64737,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Product-projection-localization-and-the-QAC0-parity-lower-bound-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Product-projection-localization-and-the-QAC0-parity-lower-bound-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Product-projection-localization-and-the-QAC0-parity-lower-bound-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"274-02","slug":"Regular-trajectories-pruning-and-quantum-parity-September-24-2026","familyId":"274","title":"Regular trajectories, pruning and quantum parity","abstract":"We prove that constant-depth quantum circuits with arbitrary one-qubit gates and unbounded-arity Toffoli gates cannot compute parity with any fixed positive worst-case advantage using polynomially many total qubits. Ancillary qubits are initialized to $|0\\rangle$, one output qubit is measured, and all other final registers may be discarded without restriction. This resolves Moore's parity conjecture in the measured-output model.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves Moore's parity conjecture in the measured-output model: constant-depth quantum circuits with arbitrary one-qubit gates, unbounded-arity Toffoli gates and polynomially many total qubits cannot compute parity with any fixed positive worst-case advantage. Ancillas start in zero, one output qubit is measured, and all other registers may be discarded. Xu–Li's reductions give the same bounded-error obstruction for strict majority.","pdfPath":"preprints/Regular-trajectories-pruning-and-quantum-parity-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Regular-trajectories-pruning-and-quantum-parity-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Regular-trajectories-pruning-and-quantum-parity-September-24-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Regular-trajectories-pruning-and-quantum-parity-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Regular-trajectories-pruning-and-quantum-parity-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Regular-trajectories-pruning-and-quantum-parity-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-framework.tex","preprints/Regular-trajectories-pruning-and-quantum-parity-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-filters.tex","preprints/Regular-trajectories-pruning-and-quantum-parity-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-propagation.tex","preprints/Regular-trajectories-pruning-and-quantum-parity-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-transport.tex","preprints/Regular-trajectories-pruning-and-quantum-parity-September-24-2026/build/figures/transport.tex","preprints/Regular-trajectories-pruning-and-quantum-parity-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-pruning.tex","preprints/Regular-trajectories-pruning-and-quantum-parity-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-parity.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Regular-trajectories-pruning-and-quantum-parity-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that constant-depth quantum circuits with arbitrary one-qubit gates and unbounded-arity Toffoli gates cannot compute parity with any fixed positive worst-case advantage using polynomially many total qubits. Ancillary qubits are initialized to $|0\\rangle$, one output qubit is measured, and all other final registers may be discarded without restriction. This resolves Moore's parity conjecture in the measured-output model.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":520},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The circuit model and the result","sourceOffset":1538},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Previous work","sourceOffset":5602},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its main tools","sourceOffset":10072},{"kind":"section","title":"Product tests and regular trajectories","sourceOffset":14295},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The gate model and product tests","sourceOffset":14829},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Asymptotic sequences and uniformity","sourceOffset":18859},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Operator degree and excitation shifts","sourceOffset":22678},{"kind":"section","title":"Two polynomial filters","sourceOffset":25013},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An elementary Chebyshev construction","sourceOffset":25821},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Approximating the zero projection","sourceOffset":28135},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A nonnegative tail detector","sourceOffset":30650}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:parity","text":"Fix positive integers $d,k,C$ and a real $0<\\varepsilon\\le1/2$. There is an integer $n_0=n_0(d,k,C,\\varepsilon)$ such that, for every $n\\ge n_0$, every allowed circuit of depth at most $d$ on at most $C(n+1)^k$ total qubits has an input $x\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n}$ on which its answer equals $\\Parity_n(x)$ with probability strictly less than $1/2+\\varepsilon$.","sourceOffset":2450,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Low-degree propagation","label":"prop:propagation","text":"Let $V$ be a circuit with a bounded number of analytic layers, and suppose the trajectory from $\\zeta$, with $\\norm{\\zeta}\\le 1$, is regular for $V$. Here $k,K$ denote degree exponents: fix $0<k<K<1$. For every operator $E$ with \\[ \\deg E\\le m^k, \\qquad \\norm{E}\\le \\exp(m^k), \\] there is, eventually, an operator $E'$ such that \\begin{equation} \\begin{gathered} \\norm{VE\\zeta-E'V\\zeta}\\le \\exp(-m^k),\\\\ \\deg E'\\le m^K, \\qquad \\norm{E'}\\le \\exp(m^K). \\end{gathered} \\end{equation} The starting vector need not be a product vector.","sourceOffset":34842,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Product test","label":"def:test","text":"A test $A$ consists of a subset $J_A\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$ and a specified unit vector $v_{A,j}$ at each $j\\in J_A$. Its excitation count and zero projection are \\[ H_A=\\sum_{j\\in J_A} \\bigl(I-\\lvert v_{A,j}\\rangle\\langle v_{A,j}\\rvert\\bigr)_j, \\qquad P_A=\\one_{H_A=0} =\\bigotimes_{j\\in J_A}\\lvert v_{A,j}\\rangle\\langle v_{A,j}\\rvert \\otimes I_{J_A^c}. \\] Here a subscript $j$ extends a one-site operator by identity at all other sites, and $\\one_{H_A\\in E}$ denotes the spectral projection of $H_A$ onto the set $E\\subseteq\\mathbb R$. The empty test has $H_A=0$ and $P_A=I$.","sourceOffset":17217,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Regular trajectory","label":"def:regular","text":"Write $V=U_t\\cdots U_1$ for analytic layers and set $\\xi_j=U_j\\cdots U_1\\xi_0$, where $\\norm{\\xi_0}\\le1$. The sequence of these trajectories is regular if the following holds at every reflection layer $U_j$: for every fixed triple \\[ 0\\le b<h<1,\\qquad 0<p<h, \\] eventually, simultaneously for every set $S$ of at most $m^b$ gates of that layer, \\begin{equation} \\norm{\\one_{H_S\\ge m^h}\\xi_{j-1}} \\le \\exp(-m^p). \\end{equation} There is no regularity condition at one-qubit layers. Regularity is a property of an asymptotic sequence, not of a single finite circuit.","sourceOffset":21191,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/274.md","scopeText":"The formalization rules out bounded-error parity computation by constant-depth quantum circuits with polynomially many zero-initialized ancillary qubits. For every fixed depth, polynomial bound on the total number of qubits, and $0<\\varepsilon\\le1/2$, every sufficiently large input length has an input on which the measured-output parity success probability is less than $1/2+\\varepsilon$. A companion specialization gives success strictly below $2/3$. The formalization also includes the product-projection localization estimate supporting this bound.\n\nThe formalized result supplies the polynomial-size parity consequence of the paper. For every fixed circuit depth and polynomial bound on the number of qubits, all sufficiently large input lengths have an input on which any such circuit computes","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/274.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":78420,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Regular-trajectories-pruning-and-quantum-parity-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Regular-trajectories-pruning-and-quantum-parity-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Regular-trajectories-pruning-and-quantum-parity-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"275-01","slug":"Continuum-Coulomb-hardness-with-binary-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026","familyId":"275","title":"Continuum Coulomb hardness with binary nuclear charges","abstract":"We prove that approximating the electronic Coulomb spectral infimum in three-dimensional space is QMA-hard when positive integer nuclear charges are encoded in binary. The nuclei have distinct rational positions, the electron number is unary, and the energy is minimized over all antisymmetric continuum states and spin sectors. A deterministic classical polynomial-time reduction produces instances with threshold separation at least one. The nuclear charges may be exponentially large, but every output has polynomial bit length.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves QMA-hardness of approximating the electronic Coulomb energy infimum in three dimensions, minimizing over the full spinful fermionic continuum space. Deterministic polynomial-time reductions work even with only unit-charge nuclei at distinct rational positions, polynomially many electrons and an energy-threshold separation of at least one.","pdfPath":"preprints/Continuum-Coulomb-hardness-with-binary-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/Continuum-Coulomb-hardness-with-binary-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Continuum-Coulomb-hardness-with-binary-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/Continuum-Coulomb-hardness-with-binary-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Continuum-Coulomb-hardness-with-binary-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Continuum-Coulomb-hardness-with-binary-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Continuum-Coulomb-hardness-with-binary-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Continuum-Coulomb-hardness-with-binary-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Continuum-Coulomb-hardness-with-binary-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-finite-models.tex","preprints/Continuum-Coulomb-hardness-with-binary-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-wells.tex","preprints/Continuum-Coulomb-hardness-with-binary-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-hopping.tex","preprints/Continuum-Coulomb-hardness-with-binary-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-continuum.tex","preprints/Continuum-Coulomb-hardness-with-binary-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-completion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Continuum-Coulomb-hardness-with-binary-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that approximating the electronic Coulomb spectral infimum in three-dimensional space is QMA-hard when positive integer nuclear charges are encoded in binary. The nuclei have distinct rational positions, the electron number is unary, and the energy is minimized over all antisymmetric continuum states and spin sectors. A deterministic classical polynomial-time reduction produces instances with threshold separation at least one. The nuclear charges may be exponentially large, but every output has polynomial bit length.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The problem and the result","sourceOffset":577},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The precise promise problem","sourceOffset":1141},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and the continuum obstruction","sourceOffset":5323},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Mechanism of the reduction","sourceOffset":9375},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite models and a controllable contact geometry","sourceOffset":14462},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The source problem and a quantitative perturbation bound","sourceOffset":15092},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Replacing signed bonds by positive Heisenberg bonds","sourceOffset":20158},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Contact geometry and independent length control","sourceOffset":24229},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The half-filled Hubbard model","sourceOffset":37311},{"kind":"section","title":"Isolated wells and deterministic energy tuning","sourceOffset":44656},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform ground-state estimates","sourceOffset":47743},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Moving the secondary nucleus","sourceOffset":53611}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"The promise problem above is QMA-hard under deterministic classical polynomial-time many-one reductions for $c=1$. The reduction can produce instances with $b-a\\ge1$.","sourceOffset":4156,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Source problem","label":"thm:source","text":"There is a QMA-complete promise problem whose Hamiltonians are \\[ H_{\\rm in}=\\sum_{e=LR}J_e h^s_{LR} \\] on finite, nonwrapping subgraphs of the square lattice, with no one-qubit terms. The signed weights $J_e$ are rational. The number of vertices and edges, the lattice extent, the weight magnitudes, and the threshold magnitudes are bounded by fixed polynomials in the source size $n\\geq2$. The rational thresholds $a_s<b_s$ have separation $\\delta_s=b_s-a_s\\geq1/\\poly(n)$.","sourceOffset":15163,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/275.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves QMA-hardness of approximating the electronic Coulomb spectral infimum in three-dimensional continuum space when positive integer nuclear charges are encoded in binary. The instances have distinct rational nuclear positions and a unary electron count, and the energy ranges over antisymmetric continuum states and all spin sectors. A deterministic polynomial-time many-one reduction produces the promise gap with threshold separation at least one, while keeping the complete output length polynomial. The same Comparator file also contains the companion unit-charge result.\n\nThe formalization proves QMA-hardness of approximating the electronic ground-energy infimum for clamped unit-charge nuclei in the full spinful fermionic continuum. The reduction is deterministic and po","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/275.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":155302,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Continuum-Coulomb-hardness-with-binary-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"275-02","slug":"QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026","familyId":"275","title":"QMA-hardness of continuum Coulomb energy with unit nuclear charges","abstract":"We prove that approximating the electronic ground-energy infimum for clamped unit-charge nuclei is QMA-hard on the full spinful fermionic continuum space. A deterministic classical polynomial-time reduction produces polynomially many nuclei at distinct rational positions and polynomially many electrons, with polynomial rational bit lengths and threshold separation at least one. No orbital basis, magnetic field, or additional external potential is supplied, and no binding assumption is imposed.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves QMA-hardness of approximating the electronic Coulomb energy infimum in three dimensions, minimizing over the full spinful fermionic continuum space. Deterministic polynomial-time reductions work even with only unit-charge nuclei at distinct rational positions, polynomially many electrons and an energy-threshold separation of at least one.","pdfPath":"preprints/QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/sections/source.tex","preprints/QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/sections/spin.tex","preprints/QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/contact-figure.tex","preprints/QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/sections/wells.tex","preprints/QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/sections/density.tex","preprints/QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/sections/nuclei.tex","preprints/QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/sections/many-body.tex","preprints/QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/sections/implementation.tex","preprints/QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that approximating the electronic ground-energy infimum for clamped unit-charge nuclei is QMA-hard on the full spinful fermionic continuum space. A deterministic classical polynomial-time reduction produces polynomially many nuclei at distinct rational positions and polynomially many electrons, with polynomial rational bit lengths and threshold separation at least one. No orbital basis, magnetic field, or additional external potential is supplied, and no binding assumption is imposed.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":544},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The electronic promise problem","sourceOffset":2408},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":6734},{"kind":"section","title":"The lattice source and rational normalization","sourceOffset":9833},{"kind":"section","title":"Positive spin model","sourceOffset":12948},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Energy reduction through singlet pairs","sourceOffset":13557},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Independently adjustable contact lengths","sourceOffset":17773},{"kind":"section","title":"Localized modes and the finite fermion model","sourceOffset":25373},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local modes and Coulomb coefficients","sourceOffset":26197},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The occupancy penalty and spin exchange","sourceOffset":30625},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Calibrating the separations","sourceOffset":34263},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Polynomial scale convention","sourceOffset":36343}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Unit-charge Coulomb hardness","label":"thm:main","text":"The electronic promise problem for \\eqref{eq:electronic-H} on $\\mathcal H_N$, with distinct rational nuclear positions, all nuclear charges equal to one, unary electron number, and rational thresholds separated by at least $L^{-1}$, is QMA-hard under deterministic classical polynomial-time many-one reductions. The reduction produces polynomially many nuclei and electrons, polynomial rational bit lengths, and threshold separation at least one. The only output data are the nuclear positions, electron number, and thresholds.","sourceOffset":4884,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Continuous one-particle control","label":"prop:continuous-control","text":"For the density and separated centers constructed above, with the polynomial scale chosen sufficiently large, there are fixed constants \\(c>0,C<\\infty\\) such that \\[ \\begin{split} (I-P)L_c(I-P)&\\ge c(I-P)\\quad\\text{on the complement},\\\\ L_c&\\ge -\\Delta_y/2-C,\\qquad \\|L_cP\\|\\le p(n,D)\\lambda^{-0.9}. \\end{split} \\tag{6} \\] The inequalities are quadratic-form inequalities on the one-particle $H^1$ domain, and the last bound is an operator norm from the range of $P$ to $L^2$. All estimates hold with either spin included.","sourceOffset":42595,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/275.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves QMA-hardness of approximating the electronic Coulomb spectral infimum in three-dimensional continuum space when positive integer nuclear charges are encoded in binary. The instances have distinct rational nuclear positions and a unary electron count, and the energy ranges over antisymmetric continuum states and all spin sectors. A deterministic polynomial-time many-one reduction produces the promise gap with threshold separation at least one, while keeping the complete output length polynomial. The same Comparator file also contains the companion unit-charge result.\n\nThe formalization proves QMA-hardness of approximating the electronic ground-energy infimum for clamped unit-charge nuclei in the full spinful fermionic continuum. The reduction is deterministic and po","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/275.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":81232,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"276-01","slug":"Classical-capacity-and-entropy-inequalities-for-generalized-amplitude-damping-September-24-2026","familyId":"276","title":"Classical capacity and entropy inequalities for generalized amplitude damping","abstract":"We determine the unassisted classical capacity of every qubit generalized amplitude-damping channel, for all damping strengths and thermal occupations. It equals the one-shot Holevo capacity and is given by a one-variable maximum. A binary pure-state ensemble attains the one-use optimum, and its independent products attain the optimum at every block length. We also prove that one-shot Holevo capacity, minimum output entropy, and regularized unassisted classical capacity are additive under tensor product with every finite-dimensional completely positive trace-preserving partner channel.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Determines the unassisted classical capacity of every qubit generalized amplitude-damping channel, including all damping and thermal parameters. An explicit one-variable optimization gives the capacity, attained by independent two-state signal ensembles with collective decoding. Holevo capacity, minimum output entropy and regularized classical capacity are additive when tensoring with any finite-dimensional quantum channel.","pdfPath":"preprints/Classical-capacity-and-entropy-inequalities-for-generalized-amplitude-damping-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Classical-capacity-and-entropy-inequalities-for-generalized-amplitude-damping-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Classical-capacity-and-entropy-inequalities-for-generalized-amplitude-damping-September-24-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Classical-capacity-and-entropy-inequalities-for-generalized-amplitude-damping-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Classical-capacity-and-entropy-inequalities-for-generalized-amplitude-damping-September-24-2026/build/sections/block.tex","preprints/Classical-capacity-and-entropy-inequalities-for-generalized-amplitude-damping-September-24-2026/build/sections/thermal.tex","preprints/Classical-capacity-and-entropy-inequalities-for-generalized-amplitude-damping-September-24-2026/build/sections/output.tex","preprints/Classical-capacity-and-entropy-inequalities-for-generalized-amplitude-damping-September-24-2026/build/sections/holevo.tex","preprints/Classical-capacity-and-entropy-inequalities-for-generalized-amplitude-damping-September-24-2026/build/sections/partners.tex","preprints/Classical-capacity-and-entropy-inequalities-for-generalized-amplitude-damping-September-24-2026/build/sections/special.tex","preprints/Classical-capacity-and-entropy-inequalities-for-generalized-amplitude-damping-September-24-2026/build/sections/alternative.tex","preprints/Classical-capacity-and-entropy-inequalities-for-generalized-amplitude-damping-September-24-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Classical-capacity-and-entropy-inequalities-for-generalized-amplitude-damping-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We determine the unassisted classical capacity of every qubit generalized amplitude-damping channel, for all damping strengths and thermal occupations. It equals the one-shot Holevo capacity and is given by a one-variable maximum. A binary pure-state ensemble attains the one-use optimum, and its independent products attain the optimum at every block length. We also prove that one-shot Holevo capacity, minimum output entropy, and regularized unassisted classical capacity are additive under tensor product with every finite-dimensional completely positive trace-preserving partner channel.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":638},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Definitions and result","sourceOffset":4846},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":10653},{"kind":"section","title":"An entropy inequality with one zero block","sourceOffset":14625},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From entropy to log determinants","sourceOffset":16861},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Joint convexity by a contraction","sourceOffset":18176},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The scalar deficit and its homogeneity","sourceOffset":20475},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sign averaging and the block estimate","sourceOffset":23482},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A scalar convexity fact for the channel application","sourceOffset":26973},{"kind":"section","title":"A thermal block entropy inequality","sourceOffset":28581},{"kind":"section","title":"From the thermal inequality to channel outputs","sourceOffset":33787},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The one-qubit entropy and its convexity","sourceOffset":34257}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For all $\\gamma,\\nu\\in[0,1]$ and every integer $n\\ge1$, \\begin{equation} \\chi(\\A_{\\gamma,\\nu}^{\\otimes n}) =n\\chi(\\A_{\\gamma,\\nu}) =\\frac n{\\ln2}\\max_{0\\le p\\le1} \\{h((1-\\gamma)p+\\gamma\\nu)-g(v_{\\gamma,\\nu}(p))\\}, \\end{equation} where \\begin{equation} v_{\\gamma,\\nu}(p) =\\gamma\\nu(1-\\nu)+\\gamma(1-\\gamma)(p-\\nu)^2. \\end{equation} In particular, $C(\\A_{\\gamma,\\nu})=\\chi(\\A_{\\gamma,\\nu})$. If $p$ maximizes \\eqref{eq:main}, independent products of the two equiprobable signals \\begin{equation} \\phi_\\pm=\\sqrt{1-p}\\,\\ket0\\ \\pm\\sqrt p\\,\\ket1 \\end{equation} attain the Holevo information at every block length.","sourceOffset":8650,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Additivity with a finite-dimensional partner","label":"cor:partner-additivity","text":"Let $\\gamma,\\nu\\in[0,1]$, put $\\A=\\A_{\\gamma,\\nu}$, and let $\\mathcal N:\\mathcal B(\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{in}})\\to \\mathcal B(\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{out}})$ be completely positive and trace preserving, where $\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{in}}$ and $\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{out}}$ are nonzero finite-dimensional complex Hilbert spaces. Then \\begin{align} \\chi(\\A\\otimes\\mathcal N) &=\\chi(\\A)+\\chi(\\mathcal N),\\\\ S_{\\min}(\\A\\otimes\\mathcal N) &=S_{\\min}(\\A)+S_{\\min}(\\mathcal N),\\\\ C(\\A\\otimes\\mathcal N) &=C(\\A)+C(\\mathcal N). \\end{align} Here $\\chi$ is the unconstrained one-shot Holevo capacity in \\eqref{eq:holevo}, and $C$ is the regularized unassisted classical capacity in \\eqref{eq:regularization}.","sourceOffset":9677,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/276.md","scopeText":"The formalized result determines the unassisted classical capacity of every generalized amplitude-damping channel with parameters in $[0,1]^2$. Its unrestricted Holevo information is additive across every number of repeated uses, equals an attained scalar maximum, and agrees with the operational capacity per use. Every maximizing equiprobable phase-pair product ensemble attains the corresponding block value. Additivity with an arbitrary different partner channel and decoding by separate measurements are not included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/276.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":64816,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Classical-capacity-and-entropy-inequalities-for-generalized-amplitude-damping-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"277-01","slug":"Threshold-parallel-repetition-for-finite-dimensional-entangled-games-September-25-2026","familyId":"277","title":"Threshold parallel repetition for finite-dimensional entangled games","abstract":"For every finite two-player game with entangled value v < 1, we prove exponential decay for the probability of winning at least a $v+\\delta$ fraction of k independent repetitions, uniformly over all finite-dimensional joint strategies. The result allows arbitrary correlated question distributions and holds for every $0\\lt \\delta\\lt 1-v$ and k ≥ 1. Its universal rate is proportional to $\\delta^5/(1+\\log(|\\mathcal A||\\mathcal B|))$, where $\\mathcal A,\\mathcal B$ are the answer alphabets. For each fixed question distribution, we also obtain an explicit cubic rate in δ.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves exponential threshold repetition for every finite two-player one-round game: if its entangled value is v < 1, the probability of winning at least a fraction $v+\\delta$ of k independent repetitions decays exponentially in k, for $0\\lt \\delta\\lt 1-v$. Arbitrary joint finite-dimensional entangled strategies and correlated question distributions are allowed.","pdfPath":"preprints/Threshold-parallel-repetition-for-finite-dimensional-entangled-games-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Threshold-parallel-repetition-for-finite-dimensional-entangled-games-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Threshold-parallel-repetition-for-finite-dimensional-entangled-games-September-25-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Threshold-parallel-repetition-for-finite-dimensional-entangled-games-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Threshold-parallel-repetition-for-finite-dimensional-entangled-games-September-25-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Threshold-parallel-repetition-for-finite-dimensional-entangled-games-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-threshold.tex","preprints/Threshold-parallel-repetition-for-finite-dimensional-entangled-games-September-25-2026/build/sections/00b-marginals.tex","preprints/Threshold-parallel-repetition-for-finite-dimensional-entangled-games-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-operators.tex","preprints/Threshold-parallel-repetition-for-finite-dimensional-entangled-games-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-exposure.tex","preprints/Threshold-parallel-repetition-for-finite-dimensional-entangled-games-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-root-support.tex","preprints/Threshold-parallel-repetition-for-finite-dimensional-entangled-games-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Threshold-parallel-repetition-for-finite-dimensional-entangled-games-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every finite two-player game with entangled value $v<1$, we prove exponential decay for the probability of winning at least a $v+\\delta$ fraction of $k$ independent repetitions, uniformly over all finite-dimensional joint strategies. The result allows arbitrary correlated question distributions and holds for every $0<\\delta<1-v$ and $k\\ge1$. Its universal rate is proportional to $\\delta^5/(1+\\log(|\\mathcal A||\\mathcal B|))$, where $\\mathcal A,\\mathcal B$ are the answer alphabets. For each fixed question distribution, we also obtain an explicit cubic rate in $\\delta$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":651},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Games and the main theorem","sourceOffset":1184},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Related work","sourceOffset":5133},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof in outline","sourceOffset":9367},{"kind":"section","title":"From conditioning to a threshold bound","sourceOffset":12071},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Marginal success probabilities","sourceOffset":13780},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Selecting the tested coordinates","sourceOffset":14881},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An exponential rate for every repetition length","sourceOffset":16246},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof of the universal threshold bound","sourceOffset":20191},{"kind":"section","title":"A common purification of effects","sourceOffset":22092},{"kind":"section","title":"Conditioning on a tested set","sourceOffset":28231},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exposing one question at each coordinate","sourceOffset":29402}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Threshold parallel repetition","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a universal constant $\\kappa_0>0$ such that the following holds. Let $G$ be any finite two-player one-round game with Boolean acceptance predicate and entangled value $v=\\omega^*(G)<1$. For every $0<\\delta<1-v$, every integer $k\\ge1$, and every finite-dimensional joint strategy for $k$ repetitions, \\begin{equation} \\Pr\\!\\left[W_k\\ge\\lceil(v+\\delta)k\\rceil\\right] \\le \\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\kappa_0\\delta^5} {1+\\log d}\\,k\\right). \\end{equation} The same inequality holds for the supremum over those strategies.","sourceOffset":2823,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Conditioning-to-threshold transfer","label":"prop:conditioning-to-tail","text":"Let $G$ be a finite two-prover game with nonempty answer alphabets, $d=|\\mathcal A||\\mathcal B|$, and $v=\\omega^*(G)<1$. Fix $0<\\delta<1-v$ and $0<T\\le\\delta/4$. Suppose the following holds for every $k\\ge1$, every finite-dimensional strategy for $k$ repetitions, and every $C\\subsetneq[k]$ with $p_C>0$: \\begin{equation} \\frac{\\log(1/p_C)+|C|\\log d}{k-|C|}\\le T \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\frac{1}{k-|C|}\\sum_{i\\notin C}\\Pr(I_i=1\\mid E_C) \\le v+\\delta/4. \\end{equation} Then, for every $k\\ge1$ and every finite-dimensional strategy, \\begin{equation} \\Pr\\bigl(W\\ge\\lceil(v+\\delta)k\\rceil\\bigr) \\le \\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\delta T}{16(1+\\log d)}\\,k\\right). \\end{equation} The same bound holds for the supremum over those strategies.","sourceOffset":16413,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/277.md","scopeText":"The formalization gives exponential threshold parallel repetition for every finite two-player game with arbitrary correlated questions. If its finite-dimensional entangled value is $v<1$, its answer sets are $A,B$, and $0<\\delta<1-v$, then the probability of winning at least the fraction $v+\\delta$ of $k$ repetitions is at most $\\exp(-\\kappa\\delta^{13}k/(1+\\log(|A||B|)))$ for one universal $\\kappa>0$. The bound applies to every joint finite-dimensional strategy and to their supremum, without assuming attainment. The separate distribution-dependent cubic bound is outside this scope.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/277.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":57462,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Threshold-parallel-repetition-for-finite-dimensional-entangled-games-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"278-01","slug":"A-Coulomb-Ground-State-Density-without-Kohn-Sham-Ensemble-Representation-September-25-2026","familyId":"278","title":"A Coulomb ground-state density without Kohn-Sham ensemble representation","abstract":"We construct a finite three-electron Coulomb molecule whose spin-summed ground-state density cannot be reproduced by any ground-state ensemble of noninteracting electrons in a single real, spin-independent local potential in $L^{3/2}(\\mathbf R^3)+L^\\infty(\\mathbf R^3)$. The molecule has two equal positive integer nuclear charges, specified by an exact finite nonnumerical formula.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a three-electron Coulomb molecule with two equal positive-integer-charge nuclei whose absolute ground-state density has no noninteracting ground-state ensemble representation by a single real spin-independent local potential in $L^{3/2}(\\mathbb R^3)+L^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3)$. This disproves Kohn–Sham ensemble representability for that potential class; the required nuclear charge is specified nonnumerically.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Coulomb-Ground-State-Density-without-Kohn-Sham-Ensemble-Representation-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Coulomb-Ground-State-Density-without-Kohn-Sham-Ensemble-Representation-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Coulomb-Ground-State-Density-without-Kohn-Sham-Ensemble-Representation-September-25-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/A-Coulomb-Ground-State-Density-without-Kohn-Sham-Ensemble-Representation-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-Coulomb-Ground-State-Density-without-Kohn-Sham-Ensemble-Representation-September-25-2026/build/figures/median-plane.tex","preprints/A-Coulomb-Ground-State-Density-without-Kohn-Sham-Ensemble-Representation-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-coulomb-setup.tex","preprints/A-Coulomb-Ground-State-Density-without-Kohn-Sham-Ensemble-Representation-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-channels.tex","preprints/A-Coulomb-Ground-State-Density-without-Kohn-Sham-Ensemble-Representation-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-angle-spectrum.tex","preprints/A-Coulomb-Ground-State-Density-without-Kohn-Sham-Ensemble-Representation-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-ensemble.tex","preprints/A-Coulomb-Ground-State-Density-without-Kohn-Sham-Ensemble-Representation-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-plane-obstruction.tex","preprints/A-Coulomb-Ground-State-Density-without-Kohn-Sham-Ensemble-Representation-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-parameters.tex","preprints/A-Coulomb-Ground-State-Density-without-Kohn-Sham-Ensemble-Representation-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-functional-consequence.tex","preprints/A-Coulomb-Ground-State-Density-without-Kohn-Sham-Ensemble-Representation-September-25-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Coulomb-Ground-State-Density-without-Kohn-Sham-Ensemble-Representation-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a finite three-electron Coulomb molecule whose spin-summed ground-state density cannot be reproduced by any ground-state ensemble of noninteracting electrons in a single real, spin-independent local potential in \\(L^{3/2}(\\R^3)+L^\\infty(\\R^3)\\). The molecule has two equal positive integer nuclear charges, specified by an exact finite nonnumerical formula.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":417},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Relation to earlier work","sourceOffset":3966},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The mechanism and the proof","sourceOffset":6581},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and conventions","sourceOffset":11237},{"kind":"section","title":"The Coulomb system and its small-coupling limit","sourceOffset":12396},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two lowest spatial orbitals","sourceOffset":15697},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ground states and perturbation","sourceOffset":22460},{"kind":"section","title":"Exterior channels and the density near the median plane","sourceOffset":29838},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The fiber equation and an exterior cutoff","sourceOffset":33213},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Localized multipoles and the slow channel","sourceOffset":43651},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The first nonzero complementary term on the plane","sourceOffset":53017},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform comparison of the three density scales","sourceOffset":59891}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Set \\(D=10^{200}\\) and \\(e_z=(0,0,1)\\). There is a finite positive integer \\(Z\\), specified by \\Cref{eq:explicit-charge}, for which the Coulomb Hamiltonian \\[ H_C=\\sum_{i=1}^3 \\left(-\\frac12\\Delta_i -\\frac{Z}{|r_i-(D/Z)e_z|} -\\frac{Z}{|r_i+(D/Z)e_z|}\\right) +\\sum_{1\\le i<j\\le3}\\frac1{|r_i-r_j|} \\] has a normalizable absolute ground state \\(\\Psi\\) in the sector \\(S_z=1/2\\), normalized by \\(\\|\\Psi\\|_2=1\\), but there is no \\(v\\in\\mathcal V\\) and no ground-state ensemble \\(\\Gamma\\) of \\(H_s(v)\\) such that \\[ n_\\Gamma=n_\\Psi\\qquad\\text{almost everywhere on }\\R^3. \\]","sourceOffset":2173,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Absence of a full-domain local first variation","label":"cor:hxc-first-variation","text":"For the physical ground-state density $n=n_\\Psi$ of \\Cref{thm:main}, $\\partial_{\\mathcal V}T_{\\mathrm{ens}}[n]=\\varnothing$. Moreover, there is no $w\\in\\mathcal V$ such that, for every $m\\in\\mathcal D$, \\begin{equation} \\lim_{t\\downarrow0} \\frac{E_{\\mathrm{Hxc}}[n+t(m-n)]-E_{\\mathrm{Hxc}}[n]}{t} =\\int_{\\R^3}w(m-n). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":153614,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Regular auxiliary data","label":"def:regular-data","text":"An auxiliary coupling \\(t\\in[0,1]\\) is called regular if all the following conditions hold. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item The two-electron ground level is isolated and simple, and its ground state is an axisymmetric, reflection-even spin singlet. The three-electron ground level in \\(S_z=1/2\\) is isolated and simple, is an absolute ground level, and has an axisymmetric, reflection-odd ground state. \\item The spin-summed density \\(n_t\\) is everywhere positive, and \\begin{equation} 2\\epsilon_b-E_3(t)>0.49,\\qquad 0.99<k_t<\\gamma, \\qquad k_t=\\sqrt{2(E_2(t)-E_3(t))}. \\end{equatio","sourceOffset":137509,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"The exact integer charge","label":"def:explicit-charge","text":"Using only the preceding data for the fixed unit-charge problem, define \\begin{equation} Z=2+\\left\\lceil \\sup_{0<t\\le1}\\frac1t\\,\\mathbf1_{\\mathcal B}(t) \\right\\rceil . \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":145876,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":162190,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":17,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Coulomb-Ground-State-Density-without-Kohn-Sham-Ensemble-Representation-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-Coulomb-Ground-State-Density-without-Kohn-Sham-Ensemble-Representation-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/A-Coulomb-Ground-State-Density-without-Kohn-Sham-Ensemble-Representation-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"279-01","slug":"Exact-quantum-factoring-over-a-fixed-finite-gate-set-September-25-2026","familyId":"279","title":"Exact quantum factoring over a fixed finite gate set","abstract":"We give a polynomial-time uniform quantum circuit family that outputs the complete prime factorization of every integer N ≥ 2 with probability one. A fixed finite set of bounded-arity gates suffices, and both the gate count and the number of qubits have polynomial worst-case bounds in the input length.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives a polynomial-time uniform quantum circuit family that outputs the complete prime factorization of every integer with probability one. Both gate count and qubit count are polynomial in the input length, and one fixed finite gate set suffices.","pdfPath":"preprints/Exact-quantum-factoring-over-a-fixed-finite-gate-set-September-25-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Exact-quantum-factoring-over-a-fixed-finite-gate-set-September-25-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Exact-quantum-factoring-over-a-fixed-finite-gate-set-September-25-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Exact-quantum-factoring-over-a-fixed-finite-gate-set-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Exact-quantum-factoring-over-a-fixed-finite-gate-set-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Exact-quantum-factoring-over-a-fixed-finite-gate-set-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-model.tex","preprints/Exact-quantum-factoring-over-a-fixed-finite-gate-set-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-order-transform.tex","preprints/Exact-quantum-factoring-over-a-fixed-finite-gate-set-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-dyadic-order.tex","preprints/Exact-quantum-factoring-over-a-fixed-finite-gate-set-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-factor-process.tex","preprints/Exact-quantum-factoring-over-a-fixed-finite-gate-set-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-exactness.tex","preprints/Exact-quantum-factoring-over-a-fixed-finite-gate-set-September-25-2026/build/figures/same-history-events.tex","preprints/Exact-quantum-factoring-over-a-fixed-finite-gate-set-September-25-2026/build/sections/appendix-primality.tex","preprints/Exact-quantum-factoring-over-a-fixed-finite-gate-set-September-25-2026/build/sections/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Exact-quantum-factoring-over-a-fixed-finite-gate-set-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a polynomial-time uniform quantum circuit family that outputs the complete prime factorization of every integer $N\\ge2$ with probability one. A fixed finite set of bounded-arity gates suffices, and both the gate count and the number of qubits have polynomial worst-case bounds in the input length.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":351},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Factoring and exact quantum computation","sourceOffset":1260},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and the ancestry of its ingredients","sourceOffset":4314},{"kind":"section","title":"Exact circuits and classical records","sourceOffset":8408},{"kind":"section","title":"An order trial with prescribed success probability","sourceOffset":14396},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A phase function implemented by a circuit","sourceOffset":15771},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Recovering fraction labels and their probabilities","sourceOffset":19616},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A dyadic majorant with an exact sum","sourceOffset":23484},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Three modes with a prescribed mass","sourceOffset":31083},{"kind":"section","title":"Factoring with verifiable auxiliary data","sourceOffset":34554},{"kind":"section","title":"From verified runs to certain output","sourceOffset":49457},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Equalizing one transition","sourceOffset":50126}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a polynomial-time uniform family of polynomial-size quantum circuits that outputs the complete prime factorization of every input integer $N\\ge2$ with probability one. The number of gates and qubits is bounded by a fixed polynomial in the bit length of $N$. One fixed finite gate set suffices: NOT, CNOT, Toffoli, Hadamard, $\\operatorname{diag}(1,i)$, their inverses, and their singly controlled versions.","sourceOffset":798,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:order-trial","text":"Let $2\\le m<B$ and let $a$ be a unit modulo $m$, of order $r$. There is a uniform quantum trial of worst-case cost polynomial in $n$ that returns either no candidate or an integer $d$ satisfying $1\\le d<B$ and $r\\mid d$. Moreover, \\[ \\Pr(d=r)=\\varphi(r)\\lambda(r), \\qquad \\lambda(d)=2^{-\\lceil\\log_2 d\\rceil-10}. \\] The trial uses only the fixed finite gate set specified above and does not require $r$ or any factorization as input.","sourceOffset":14860,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/279.md","scopeText":"The formalization constructs a polynomial-time uniform quantum circuit family that outputs the complete prime factorization of every integer $N\\ge2$ with probability exactly one. The circuits use one fixed finite set of bounded-arity gates, and both the gate count and number of qubits have polynomial worst-case bounds in the bit length of $N$. The probability-one conclusion is exact, rather than an asymptotic or bounded-error guarantee.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/279.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":76381,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Exact-quantum-factoring-over-a-fixed-finite-gate-set-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Exact-quantum-factoring-over-a-fixed-finite-gate-set-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"280-01","slug":"Strongly-rational-unitary-vertex-operator-algebras-and-conformal-nets-September-25-2026","familyId":"280","title":"Strongly rational unitary vertex operator algebras and conformal nets","abstract":"Every simple unitary strongly rational vertex operator algebra generates a completely rational conformal net. All its simple grading-restricted modules are unitarizable, its canonical fusion forms are positive, and the Carpi–Weiner–Xu functor gives a braided unitary tensor equivalence from its grading-restricted finite-length module category onto the finite-index sectors of the net. Gui's extension theorems then identify normalized irreducible finite-index local extensions of these nets with simple CFT-type conformal extensions of the vertex operator algebras.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the strongly rational case of the strong-locality conjecture: every simple unitary strongly rational complex vertex operator algebra generates a completely rational conformal net. Its simple modules are unitarizable, and its representation category agrees with the net’s finite-index sectors as a braided unitary tensor category.","pdfPath":"preprints/Strongly-rational-unitary-vertex-operator-algebras-and-conformal-nets-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Strongly-rational-unitary-vertex-operator-algebras-and-conformal-nets-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Strongly-rational-unitary-vertex-operator-algebras-and-conformal-nets-September-25-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Strongly-rational-unitary-vertex-operator-algebras-and-conformal-nets-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Strongly-rational-unitary-vertex-operator-algebras-and-conformal-nets-September-25-2026/build/01-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Strongly-rational-unitary-vertex-operator-algebras-and-conformal-nets-September-25-2026/build/02-energy.tex","preprints/Strongly-rational-unitary-vertex-operator-algebras-and-conformal-nets-September-25-2026/build/03-annuli.tex","preprints/Strongly-rational-unitary-vertex-operator-algebras-and-conformal-nets-September-25-2026/build/04-integration.tex","preprints/Strongly-rational-unitary-vertex-operator-algebras-and-conformal-nets-September-25-2026/build/05-fusion.tex","preprints/Strongly-rational-unitary-vertex-operator-algebras-and-conformal-nets-September-25-2026/build/06-unitarity.tex","preprints/Strongly-rational-unitary-vertex-operator-algebras-and-conformal-nets-September-25-2026/build/07-comparison.tex","preprints/Strongly-rational-unitary-vertex-operator-algebras-and-conformal-nets-September-25-2026/build/08-exhaustion.tex","preprints/Strongly-rational-unitary-vertex-operator-algebras-and-conformal-nets-September-25-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Strongly-rational-unitary-vertex-operator-algebras-and-conformal-nets-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every simple unitary strongly rational vertex operator algebra generates a completely rational conformal net. All its simple grading-restricted modules are unitarizable, its canonical fusion forms are positive, and the Carpi--Weiner--Xu functor gives a braided unitary tensor equivalence from its grading-restricted finite-length module category onto the finite-index sectors of the net. Gui's extension theorems then identify normalized irreducible finite-index local extensions of these nets with simple CFT-type conformal extensions of the vertex operator algebras.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":631},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The statement","sourceOffset":1571},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The correspondence problem and earlier work","sourceOffset":6692},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":11548},{"kind":"section","title":"Algebraic and analytic conventions","sourceOffset":15267},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Modules, duality and fusion","sourceOffset":15536},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Modes, smooth vectors and closed operators","sourceOffset":18922},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Creation and the adjoint convention","sourceOffset":22251},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The scalar case","sourceOffset":23876},{"kind":"section","title":"Energy bounds and strong locality","sourceOffset":24773},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite generating filtration","sourceOffset":26906},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Polynomial energy bounds","sourceOffset":31265}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $V$ be a simple unitary strongly rational vertex operator algebra over $\\mathbb C$. Then: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\arabic*)}] \\item $V$ has polynomial energy bounds and is CKLW-strongly local. The algebras \\eqref{eq:intro-net} form an irreducible conformal net $A_V$. \\item $V$ is completely unitary: every simple grading-restricted module is unitarizable, and all canonical invariant Hermitian fusion forms are positive definite. \\item Every unitary grading-restricted finite-length module is strongly integrable, with the closed-field equality \\eqref{eq:intro-cwx}. \\item The net $A_V$ is completely rational. \\item After choosing invariant positive forms on the simple modules, the Carpi--Weiner--Xu functor gives a braided unitary tensor equivalence \\[ \\Rep(V)\\simeq\\Rep^u(V) \\xrightarrow{\\ F_{\\mathrm{CWX}}^V\\ }\\Rep^f(A_V). \\] The tensor structure and braiding on the right are Connes fusion and DHR braiding, with a common orientation convention for the circle. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":5118,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:energy","text":"Let $V$ be a simple unitary strongly rational vertex operator algebra, and let $W$ be a grading-restricted unitary $V$-module. Every vertex operator on $W$ satisfies polynomial energy bounds. More precisely, if $a\\in V_d$ is quasi-primary, $d\\geq2$, and $(-1)^d\\Theta a=a$, then there are constants $C_a>0$ and $p_a\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\geq0}$ such that \\begin{equation} \\|a[m]\\xi\\| \\leq C_a(1+|m|)^{p_a} \\|(1+L_0)^{d-1}\\xi\\|, \\qquad m\\in\\mathbb Z,\\quad \\xi\\in W. \\end{equation} Weight-one fields and the Virasoro field satisfy energy bounds of at most linear order. The constants may depend on $V$, $W$, and $a$.","sourceOffset":25256,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/280.md","scopeText":"The linked formalization proves the first construction step relating strongly rational unitary vertex operator algebras to conformal nets. For a simple unitary strongly rational vertex operator algebra, it establishes polynomial energy bounds and strong locality and constructs an irreducible conformal net with the stated covariance, vacuum, and positive-energy properties.\n\nThe selected statement does not include complete rationality of the net, unitarizability of all simple modules, the braided tensor equivalence, or the classification of local extensions described in the paper.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/280.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":166408,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Strongly-rational-unitary-vertex-operator-algebras-and-conformal-nets-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Strongly-rational-unitary-vertex-operator-algebras-and-conformal-nets-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Strongly-rational-unitary-vertex-operator-algebras-and-conformal-nets-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"281-01","slug":"QAOA-attains-the-SK-ground-state-energy-in-the-thermodynamic-first-limit-September-25-2026","familyId":"281","title":"QAOA attains the SK ground-state energy in the thermodynamic-first limit","abstract":"We prove that the Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm (QAOA) approaches the ground-state energy per spin of the Gaussian zero-field Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model when system size tends to infinity first and circuit depth then increases. For every accuracy, some finite depth and deterministic angles, independent of system size and disorder, achieve that accuracy in the limiting expected energy per spin using the standard cost Hamiltonian and transverse-field mixer. This proves the eventual Parisi-optimality conjecture of Basso, Farhi, Marwaha, Villalonga, and Zhou in its fixed-parameter thermodynamic formulation. We give no quantitative bound on the required depth or efficient angle-selection procedure.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that QAOA approaches the ground-state energy of the Gaussian zero-field Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model when system size tends to infinity before circuit depth. For every accuracy, finite depth and deterministic angles independent of size and disorder achieve the required limiting expected energy per spin. This also yields leading-order optimal expected MaxCut values on large-degree random regular graphs, with size tending to infinity before degree.","pdfPath":"preprints/QAOA-attains-the-SK-ground-state-energy-in-the-thermodynamic-first-limit-September-25-2026/QAOA-attains-the-SK-ground-state-energy-in-the-thermodynamic-first-limit-September-25-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/QAOA-attains-the-SK-ground-state-energy-in-the-thermodynamic-first-limit-September-25-2026/QAOA-attains-the-SK-ground-state-energy-in-the-thermodynamic-first-limit-September-25-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/QAOA-attains-the-SK-ground-state-energy-in-the-thermodynamic-first-limit-September-25-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/QAOA-attains-the-SK-ground-state-energy-in-the-thermodynamic-first-limit-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/QAOA-attains-the-SK-ground-state-energy-in-the-thermodynamic-first-limit-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/QAOA-attains-the-SK-ground-state-energy-in-the-thermodynamic-first-limit-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-foundations.tex","preprints/QAOA-attains-the-SK-ground-state-energy-in-the-thermodynamic-first-limit-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-classical-optimization.tex","preprints/QAOA-attains-the-SK-ground-state-energy-in-the-thermodynamic-first-limit-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-classical-synthesis.tex","preprints/QAOA-attains-the-SK-ground-state-energy-in-the-thermodynamic-first-limit-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-seed-simulation.tex","preprints/QAOA-attains-the-SK-ground-state-energy-in-the-thermodynamic-first-limit-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-assembly.tex","preprints/QAOA-attains-the-SK-ground-state-energy-in-the-thermodynamic-first-limit-September-25-2026/build/sections/08-regular-maxcut.tex","preprints/QAOA-attains-the-SK-ground-state-energy-in-the-thermodynamic-first-limit-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-positive-temperature.tex","preprints/QAOA-attains-the-SK-ground-state-energy-in-the-thermodynamic-first-limit-September-25-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/QAOA-attains-the-SK-ground-state-energy-in-the-thermodynamic-first-limit-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the Quantum Approximate Optimization Algorithm (QAOA) approaches the ground-state energy per spin of the Gaussian zero-field Sherrington--Kirkpatrick model when system size tends to infinity first and circuit depth then increases. For every accuracy, some finite depth and deterministic angles, independent of system size and disorder, achieve that accuracy in the limiting expected energy per spin using the standard cost Hamiltonian and transverse-field mixer. This proves the eventual Parisi-optimality conjecture of Basso, Farhi, Marwaha, Villalonga, and Zhou in its fixed-parameter thermodynamic formulation. We give no quantitative bound on the required depth or efficient angle-selection procedure.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction and statement of the result","sourceOffset":785},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model and quantifiers","sourceOffset":4857},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the construction works","sourceOffset":7562},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A regular MaxCut consequence","sourceOffset":11305},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reading paths and conventions","sourceOffset":13714},{"kind":"section","title":"The fixed-word tree calculus","sourceOffset":15406},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pointed spaces and occupation embeddings","sourceOffset":17936},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Root insertions and edge energy","sourceOffset":22270},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Classical Gaussian positions and descendant formulas","sourceOffset":27954},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weights and the topology of convergence","sourceOffset":32097},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Normalized sums and a unitary central limit","sourceOffset":38385},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Smooth limits for ordinary root maps","sourceOffset":47223}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For the zero-field SK model \\eqref{eq:sk-model}, \\begin{equation} \\lim_{p\\to\\infty}Q_p=\\Pstar. \\end{equation} Equivalently, for every $\\varepsilon>0$ there exist a finite integer $p$ and deterministic real vectors $\\gamma,\\beta\\in\\R^p$, independent of $n$ and $J$, such that $v_p(\\gamma,\\beta)\\ge\\Pstar-\\varepsilon$.","sourceOffset":6391,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Regular MaxCut in successive limits","label":"cor:regular-maxcut","text":"For every $\\eta>0$ there are an integer $p\\ge1$, deterministic normalized angles $\\gamma,\\beta\\in\\R^p$, and an integer $D_0\\ge2$ such that, for every integer $D\\ge D_0$ and every finite simple unweighted $(D+1)$-regular graph $G$ of girth greater than $2p+1$, \\begin{equation} q_{p,G}(2\\gamma/\\sqrt D,\\beta) \\ge \\frac12+\\frac{\\Pstar-\\eta}{\\sqrt D}. \\end{equation} The word $p,\\gamma,\\beta$ is independent of $D$, the graph size, and $G$; the displayed usual MaxCut cost angles, unlike the normalized angles $\\gamma$, are scaled with the degree. Let $G_{n,d}$ be uniform among simple labelled $d$-regular graphs on $n$ vertices, along admissible $n$ with $nd$ even. For the same word, put \\[ \\Delta_D(G)=\\operatorname{OPT}(G) -q_{p,G}(2\\gamma/\\sqrt D,\\beta). \\] The word may be chosen so that \\begin{equation} \\lim_{D\\to\\infty} \\limsup_{\\substack{n\\to\\infty\\\\ n(D+1)\\ \\mathrm{even}}} \\Pr_G\\!\\left[\\sqrt D\\,\\Delta_D(G_{n,D+1})>\\eta\\right]=0. \\end{equation} Here the inner limit keeps $D,p,\\gamma,\\beta$ fixed, and $\\Pr_G$ is only over the graph: $q_{p,G}$ already averages quantum measurement.","sourceOffset":12007,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Finite descendant formula","label":"def:finite-descendant-formula","text":"A finite descendant formula is a finite expression built recursively from a local seed and finitely many Gaussian positions of centered formulas one level below, using pointwise scalar functions. The functions are required to be measurable and to produce square-integrable vectors whenever the formula is used as a vector. Additional empty levels identify formulas of different radii. Its finite-degree version replaces every Gaussian position by the normalized sum of the corresponding independent child variables.","sourceOffset":30219,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Exclusion calculation and gate","label":"def:exclusion-gate","text":"A finite exclusion calculation uses local seeds, one prescribed commuting spin axis at each control site, smooth pointwise functions of finite lists of messages, and normalized sums \\eqref{eq:masked-adjacency}. An axis may depend on the site's mask. A formula is split into finitely many named roles when different occurrences need different masks. The following conditions are imposed. \\begin{enumerate} \\item Every transmitted field is odd under simultaneous seed reflection and spin flip. Every pointwise function is smooth in its aggregate inputs, with polynomial bounds for every derivative. Its","sourceOffset":59852,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/281.md","scopeText":"The linked formalization supplies variational identities for the Gaussian zero-field Sherrington–Kirkpatrick ground-state energy used in the paper's QAOA argument. Conditional on an admissible Parisi minimizer and its associated diffusion, it proves convergence of the finite-system ground-state energy, identifies its limit with the Parisi value, and gives equivalent terminal-martingale and integrated-curvature formulas.\n\nIt also proves convergence of finite Gaussian coefficient sums to the curvature integral, which approaches the ground-state value as the terminal time tends to one. The selected statement covers these value and approximation results; it does not itself assert convergence of QAOA circuit energies.\n\nThe formalization proves that every admissible integrable minimizer of the z","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/281.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":260471,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/QAOA-attains-the-SK-ground-state-energy-in-the-thermodynamic-first-limit-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"281-02","slug":"Full-support-of-the-zero-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-order-parameter-September-27-2026","familyId":"281","title":"Full support of the zero-temperature Sherrington-Kirkpatrick order parameter","abstract":"We prove that every admissible integrable minimizer of the zero-temperature Parisi functional for the pure, zero-field Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model has full relative Stieltjes support on $[0,1)$. Thus its support has no gaps at any overlap scale below one. We use the covariance normalization $\\xi(t)=t^2/2$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that QAOA approaches the ground-state energy of the Gaussian zero-field Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model when system size tends to infinity before circuit depth. For every accuracy, finite depth and deterministic angles independent of size and disorder achieve the required limiting expected energy per spin. This also yields leading-order optimal expected MaxCut values on large-degree random regular graphs, with size tending to infinity before degree.","pdfPath":"preprints/Full-support-of-the-zero-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-order-parameter-September-27-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Full-support-of-the-zero-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-order-parameter-September-27-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Full-support-of-the-zero-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-order-parameter-September-27-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Full-support-of-the-zero-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-order-parameter-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Full-support-of-the-zero-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-order-parameter-September-27-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","preprints/Full-support-of-the-zero-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-order-parameter-September-27-2026/build/gap-convexity.tex","preprints/Full-support-of-the-zero-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-order-parameter-September-27-2026/build/regularity.tex","preprints/Full-support-of-the-zero-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-order-parameter-September-27-2026/build/signs.tex","preprints/Full-support-of-the-zero-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-order-parameter-September-27-2026/build/certificate.tex","preprints/Full-support-of-the-zero-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-order-parameter-September-27-2026/build/curvature.tex","preprints/Full-support-of-the-zero-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-order-parameter-September-27-2026/build/variation.tex","preprints/Full-support-of-the-zero-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-order-parameter-September-27-2026/build/order-parameter-regularity.tex","preprints/Full-support-of-the-zero-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-order-parameter-September-27-2026/build/consequences.tex","preprints/Full-support-of-the-zero-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-order-parameter-September-27-2026/build/algorithm-application.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Full-support-of-the-zero-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-order-parameter-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every admissible integrable minimizer of the zero-temperature Parisi functional for the pure, zero-field Sherrington--Kirkpatrick model has full relative Stieltjes support on $[0,1)$. Thus its support has no gaps at any overlap scale below one. We use the covariance normalization $\\xi(t)=t^2/2$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":356},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work and the distinction between support assertions","sourceOffset":5807},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof structure","sourceOffset":10605},{"kind":"section","title":"Integrable coefficients and the controlled diffusion","sourceOffset":14884},{"kind":"section","title":"Shape inequalities for finite step functions","sourceOffset":28663},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Boundary estimates and comparison","sourceOffset":31431},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Backward shape inequalities","sourceOffset":37031},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Forward score inequalities","sourceOffset":40231},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Variables for the integral certificate","sourceOffset":44833},{"kind":"section","title":"An exact coercive identity","sourceOffset":47619},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Choosing the derivative correction","sourceOffset":48996},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The nonnegative remainder","sourceOffset":51103}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Full support at zero temperature","label":"thm:full-support","text":"If $\\gamma\\in\\mathcal U$ minimizes $\\mathcal P$, then \\[ \\supp\\mu_\\gamma=[0,1). \\] In particular $\\gamma(b)>\\gamma(a)$ whenever $0\\leq a<b<1$. For the solution of \\eqref{eq:model-diffusion}, \\begin{equation} \\E[u_\\gamma(t,X_t)^2]=t,\\qquad \\E[\\Phi_{\\gamma,xx}(t,X_t)^2]=1, \\qquad 0\\leq t<1. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":4122,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Integrated coercivity","label":"cert:coercivity","text":"Suppose $r,v,w,z,s$ and the variables $R,K,b,j,L,B,S$ are related as in Lemma \\ref{shape:ibp}, with the derivative rules \\eqref{shape:D-rules} and signs \\eqref{cert:signs}. Assume their quotients extend across zero. Let $f$ be a probability density satisfying $f_x/f=r-s$ such that every term of \\eqref{cert:identity} is integrable and $rfF_2$ tends to zero at both spatial infinities. Then \\begin{equation} \\E_f[z^2-12vw^2+6v^4] \\geq\\frac{1661507}{1521000000}\\E_fv^4 \\geq\\frac1{1000}\\E_fv^4. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":53746,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/281.md","scopeText":"The linked formalization supplies variational identities for the Gaussian zero-field Sherrington–Kirkpatrick ground-state energy used in the paper's QAOA argument. Conditional on an admissible Parisi minimizer and its associated diffusion, it proves convergence of the finite-system ground-state energy, identifies its limit with the Parisi value, and gives equivalent terminal-martingale and integrated-curvature formulas.\n\nIt also proves convergence of finite Gaussian coefficient sums to the curvature integral, which approaches the ground-state value as the terminal time tends to one. The selected statement covers these value and approximation results; it does not itself assert convergence of QAOA circuit energies.\n\nThe formalization proves that every admissible integrable minimizer of the z","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/281.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":86736,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Full-support-of-the-zero-temperature-Sherrington-Kirkpatrick-order-parameter-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"282-01","slug":"Scale-and-conformal-symmetry-in-four-dimensional-operational-quantum-field-theory-September-26-2026","familyId":"282","title":"Scale and conformal symmetry in four-dimensional operational quantum field theory","abstract":"We prove that scale symmetry implies local conformal symmetry for a class of four-dimensional unitary, positive-energy quantum field theories with a Poincaré- and scale-invariant vacuum. The class has a discrete, bounded-below spectrum of scaling dimensions with finite multiplicities, and each original field has finite scaling support. The original fields and their adjoints have compatible affiliated realizations in a causally commuting bounded local net, and a physical local dilatation current generates scale transformations through its local Ward identities. The operational framework admits sharply localized reconstructed fields after their joint products and compatible affiliated realizations in the same net have been established. Under these hypotheses, the stress tensor admits a symmetric, conserved, traceless improvement with unchanged translation and Lorentz charges. The corresponding conformal currents satisfy unbroken local Ward identities on the physical field algebra. The conclusion is local; integration to a global conformal action on the whole bounded net remains a separate question.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Under the stated bounded-local-net and field-reconstruction hypotheses, proves that scale symmetry implies local conformal symmetry for four-dimensional unitary positive-energy theories with a discrete bounded-below scaling spectrum of finite multiplicity, finite scaling support and a physical local scale current. The stress tensor has a traceless improvement with unchanged spacetime charges. The conclusion concerns local Ward identities, not a global conformal action on the whole net.","pdfPath":"preprints/Scale-and-conformal-symmetry-in-four-dimensional-operational-quantum-field-theory-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Scale-and-conformal-symmetry-in-four-dimensional-operational-quantum-field-theory-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Scale-and-conformal-symmetry-in-four-dimensional-operational-quantum-field-theory-September-26-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Scale-and-conformal-symmetry-in-four-dimensional-operational-quantum-field-theory-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Scale-and-conformal-symmetry-in-four-dimensional-operational-quantum-field-theory-September-26-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Scale-and-conformal-symmetry-in-four-dimensional-operational-quantum-field-theory-September-26-2026/build/sections/02-foundations.tex","preprints/Scale-and-conformal-symmetry-in-four-dimensional-operational-quantum-field-theory-September-26-2026/build/sections/03-null-current.tex","preprints/Scale-and-conformal-symmetry-in-four-dimensional-operational-quantum-field-theory-September-26-2026/build/sections/04-dispersion.tex","preprints/Scale-and-conformal-symmetry-in-four-dimensional-operational-quantum-field-theory-September-26-2026/build/sections/05-charges-cones.tex","preprints/Scale-and-conformal-symmetry-in-four-dimensional-operational-quantum-field-theory-September-26-2026/build/sections/06-extraction.tex","preprints/Scale-and-conformal-symmetry-in-four-dimensional-operational-quantum-field-theory-September-26-2026/build/sections/07-short-products.tex","preprints/Scale-and-conformal-symmetry-in-four-dimensional-operational-quantum-field-theory-September-26-2026/build/sections/08-reconstruction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Scale-and-conformal-symmetry-in-four-dimensional-operational-quantum-field-theory-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that scale symmetry implies local conformal symmetry for a class of four-dimensional unitary, positive-energy quantum field theories with a Poincar\\'e- and scale-invariant vacuum. The class has a discrete, bounded-below spectrum of scaling dimensions with finite multiplicities, and each original field has finite scaling support. The original fields and their adjoints have compatible affiliated realizations in a causally commuting bounded local net, and a physical local dilatation current generates scale transformations through its local Ward identities. The operational framework admits sharply localized reconstructed fields after their joint products and compatible affiliated realizations in the same net have been established. Under these hypotheses, the stress tensor admits a symmetric, conserved, traceless improvement with unchanged translation and Lorentz charges. The corresponding conformal currents satisfy unbroken local Ward identities on the physical field algebra. The conclusion is local; integration to a global conformal action on the whole bounded net remains a separate question.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":1178},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The result and its scope","sourceOffset":2463},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the local-field question","sourceOffset":8278},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Structure of the proof","sourceOffset":12434},{"kind":"section","title":"Operational framework, vacuum waves, and null cuts","sourceOffset":18015},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Original physical data and the vacuum-generated domain","sourceOffset":18485},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Example: the full free scalar theory","sourceOffset":35859},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Selecting the current weights","sourceOffset":37189},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Modular input and a spectral comparison lemma","sourceOffset":40930},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Timelike vacuum channels","sourceOffset":54086},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Scalar comparison copies","sourceOffset":59172},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The physical null-cut generators","sourceOffset":64117}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Suppose Assumption \\ref{ass:framework} holds, including the algebraic direct-sum decomposition with finite scaling support for every original field. Then the physical stress tensor admits a local symmetric conserved improvement with the same translation and Lorentz Ward charges and with zero trace. More precisely, let $T^{(4)}$ and $V^{(3)}$ be the Hermitian dimension-four and dimension-three components selected by the affine Ward identities. There is a Hermitian physical scalar field $L$ of dimension two such that \\begin{equation} \\partial^\\mu V^{(3)}_\\mu=\\Box L, \\qquad t_{\\mu\\nu}=T^{(4)}_{\\mu\\nu} +\\frac13(\\partial_\\mu\\partial_\\nu-\\eta_{\\mu\\nu}\\Box)L \\end{equation} is symmetric, conserved and traceless. The replacement $T\\mapsto T^{(4)}$ also preserves the affine charges. The currents $X^\\nu t_{\\mu\\nu}$ for conformal Killing vector fields $X$ have unbroken local Ward identities on the physical field algebra, extending its Poincar\\'e and dilation actions.","sourceOffset":4671,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Original joint-test domain","label":"F:old-domain","text":"For every fixed finite ordered list of original fields and adjoints, the vacuum distribution has a unique tempered extension and its vacuum map has a continuous Hilbert-valued extension to all joint Schwartz kernels. The space $\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{old}}$ is Hilbert-dense and invariant under old field insertions and continuous joint polynomial insertions, with their prescribed adjoints. Finite field-label identities, covariance, locality, and the compact-slab Ward identities pass to this domain with their stated support conditions. For a compactly smeared original field $B(f)$, each retained closed affiliated realization and its Hilbert adjoint agree on $\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{old}}$ with these insertion actions. This assertion also holds for an originally supplied Schwartz realization. It does not require $\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{old}}$ to be a graph core for that realization.","sourceOffset":25462,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"assumption","name":"Operational physical theory","label":"ass:framework","text":"The physical Hilbert space $\\cH$ is generated from a vacuum $\\Omega$ by the original physical fields and their adjoints. The fields, in finite-component Lorentz multiplets, and their ordered products are operator-valued Wightman distributions on a common invariant polynomial domain, which we call the initial realization domain $\\mathcal E$. They have positive translation spectrum, Poincar\\'e covariance, and locality, with the usual grading when fermions occur. The vacuum is invariant under Poincar\\'e transformations and continuous dilations; uniqueness of the invariant vector is not assumed. L","sourceOffset":18921,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":419221,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":31,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Scale-and-conformal-symmetry-in-four-dimensional-operational-quantum-field-theory-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"283-01","slug":"Polynomial-Time-Unitary-Synthesis-from-a-Boolean-Oracle-October-5-2026","familyId":"283","title":"Polynomial-Time Unitary Synthesis from a Boolean Oracle","abstract":"We give a positive answer to the constant-error formulation of the Aaronson–Kuperberg unitary synthesis problem. For every n, a quantum oracle circuit generated in polynomial time from n alone can approximate the channel of every n-qubit unitary to full diamond-norm error at most 1/2, after a suitable Boolean oracle is chosen. Using the fixed gates $H,T,T^\\dagger,\\mathrm{CNOT}$, the circuit has polynomially many qubits, elementary gates, and oracle calls, and its oracle queries have polynomial length. The oracle may depend on the target unitary; the theorem does not give an efficient classical procedure for constructing it.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Solves the constant-error Aaronson–Kuperberg unitary synthesis problem: a uniform polynomial-size quantum oracle circuit approximates every n-qubit unitary channel within diamond-norm error 1/2, after a suitable Boolean oracle is chosen. Gates, qubits, oracle calls and query length are polynomially bounded. The target-dependent oracle may have an unrestricted truth table; its efficient classical construction is not asserted.","pdfPath":"preprints/Polynomial-Time-Unitary-Synthesis-from-a-Boolean-Oracle-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Polynomial-Time-Unitary-Synthesis-from-a-Boolean-Oracle-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Polynomial-Time-Unitary-Synthesis-from-a-Boolean-Oracle-October-5-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/Polynomial-Time-Unitary-Synthesis-from-a-Boolean-Oracle-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Time-Unitary-Synthesis-from-a-Boolean-Oracle-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Time-Unitary-Synthesis-from-a-Boolean-Oracle-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-slots.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Time-Unitary-Synthesis-from-a-Boolean-Oracle-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-contraction.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Time-Unitary-Synthesis-from-a-Boolean-Oracle-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-fields.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Time-Unitary-Synthesis-from-a-Boolean-Oracle-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-encoders.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Time-Unitary-Synthesis-from-a-Boolean-Oracle-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-recursion.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Time-Unitary-Synthesis-from-a-Boolean-Oracle-October-5-2026/build/figures/recursion.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Time-Unitary-Synthesis-from-a-Boolean-Oracle-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-compilation.tex","preprints/Polynomial-Time-Unitary-Synthesis-from-a-Boolean-Oracle-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Polynomial-Time-Unitary-Synthesis-from-a-Boolean-Oracle-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a positive answer to the constant-error formulation of the Aaronson--Kuperberg unitary synthesis problem. For every $n$, a quantum oracle circuit generated in polynomial time from $n$ alone can approximate the channel of every $n$-qubit unitary to full diamond-norm error at most $1/2$, after a suitable Boolean oracle is chosen. Using the fixed gates $H,T,T^\\dagger,\\mathrm{CNOT}$, the circuit has polynomially many qubits, elementary gates, and oracle calls, and its oracle queries have polynomial length. The oracle may depend on the target unitary; the theorem does not give an efficient classical procedure for constructing it.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":686},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model and main result","sourceOffset":1752},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier work","sourceOffset":4043},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The reduction","sourceOffset":5968},{"kind":"section","title":"Controlled tasks and programmable slots","sourceOffset":9944},{"kind":"section","title":"Producing a contracting block","sourceOffset":16175},{"kind":"section","title":"Fourier fields for a smaller unitary","sourceOffset":24708},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Closing the internal coordinates","sourceOffset":25394},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Truncation and averaging","sourceOffset":27160},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Few possible input indices at each Fourier frequency","sourceOffset":31409},{"kind":"section","title":"Implementing the field encodings","sourceOffset":34591},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A construction for a nearly isometric matrix","sourceOffset":35371}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Polynomial-time unitary synthesis","label":"thm:main","text":"There are a polynomial $p$ and a deterministic classical algorithm which, on input $1^n$ for any integer $n\\ge1$, outputs in time at most $p(n)$ a quantum oracle circuit $A_n$ with the following properties. The circuit uses the gates $H,T,T^\\dagger,\\mathrm{CNOT}$ and a single Boolean oracle of input length $m(n)\\le p(n)$. Its total number of qubits, elementary gates, and oracle calls are each at most $p(n)$. For every unitary $U\\in U(2^n)$ there is a function $f:\\{0,1\\}^{m(n)}\\to\\{0,1\\}$ for which the output channel $\\Phi_{n,f}$ of $A_n^{O_f}$ satisfies \\[ \\norm{\\Phi_{n,f}-\\mathcal U}_\\diamond\\le\\frac12. \\]","sourceOffset":2902,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"A block of uniformly small norm","label":"contract:block","text":"For every unitary $U$ on $\\C^d$, there are unitaries $P_{\\mathrm L}$ and $P_{\\mathrm R}$ on $\\C^d$ such that \\begin{equation} V=P_{\\mathrm L}UP_{\\mathrm R} =\\begin{pmatrix}A&B\\\\C&D\\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad \\norm D\\leq r, \\end{equation} where the block sizes are $k,s$ from \\eqref{contract:orders}. The transformations $P_{\\mathrm L},P_{\\mathrm R}$ and their inverses have implementations using $O(n)$ programmable slots and $O(n)$ clean working qubits, with a structure determined by $n,d$. The same structure works simultaneously for any family $U_\\lambda$, using the label $\\lambda$ as an additional control.","sourceOffset":19770,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":70737,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Polynomial-Time-Unitary-Synthesis-from-a-Boolean-Oracle-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"284-01","slug":"A-Nearly-Quartic-Separation-Between-Randomized-and-Quantum-Query-Complexity-October-5-2026","familyId":"284","title":"A Nearly Quartic Separation Between Randomized and Quantum Query Complexity","abstract":"We construct total Boolean functions with a nearly quartic separation between bounded-error randomized and quantum query complexity. Writing these complexities as $\\mathrm R(f)$ and $\\mathrm Q(f)$, the examples rule out every universal bound $\\mathrm R(f)=O((1+\\mathrm Q(f))^\\alpha)$ with α < 4. Thus the known quartic upper bound has the optimal exponent, disproving the conjectured cubic bound. Both complexities count worst-case bit queries with error at most 1/3 on every input.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Shows that the universal bound $R(f)=O((1+Q(f))^4)$ for total Boolean functions is sharp in its exponent, ruling out every smaller power and disproving the conjectured cubic relation. Here R and Q are randomized and quantum worst-case bit-query complexities with error at most 1/3; computation between queries is unrestricted.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Nearly-Quartic-Separation-Between-Randomized-and-Quantum-Query-Complexity-October-5-2026/quartic-query-separation.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Nearly-Quartic-Separation-Between-Randomized-and-Quantum-Query-Complexity-October-5-2026/quartic-query-separation.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Nearly-Quartic-Separation-Between-Randomized-and-Quantum-Query-Complexity-October-5-2026","subjectId":"17","subject":"Mathematical physics","subjectZh":"數學物理","texPaths":["preprints/A-Nearly-Quartic-Separation-Between-Randomized-and-Quantum-Query-Complexity-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-Nearly-Quartic-Separation-Between-Randomized-and-Quantum-Query-Complexity-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/A-Nearly-Quartic-Separation-Between-Randomized-and-Quantum-Query-Complexity-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-Nearly-Quartic-Separation-Between-Randomized-and-Quantum-Query-Complexity-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-construction.tex","preprints/A-Nearly-Quartic-Separation-Between-Randomized-and-Quantum-Query-Complexity-October-5-2026/build/figures/outward-pointers.tex","preprints/A-Nearly-Quartic-Separation-Between-Randomized-and-Quantum-Query-Complexity-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-quantum.tex","preprints/A-Nearly-Quartic-Separation-Between-Randomized-and-Quantum-Query-Complexity-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-classical.tex","preprints/A-Nearly-Quartic-Separation-Between-Randomized-and-Quantum-Query-Complexity-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-exponent.tex","preprints/A-Nearly-Quartic-Separation-Between-Randomized-and-Quantum-Query-Complexity-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-upper-bound.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Nearly-Quartic-Separation-Between-Randomized-and-Quantum-Query-Complexity-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct total Boolean functions with a nearly quartic separation between bounded-error randomized and quantum query complexity. Writing these complexities as $\\R(f)$ and $\\Q(f)$, the examples rule out every universal bound $\\R(f)=O((1+\\Q(f))^\\alpha)$ with $\\alpha<4$. Thus the known quartic upper bound has the optimal exponent, disproving the conjectured cubic bound. Both complexities count worst-case bit queries with error at most $1/3$ on every input.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":507},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":2387},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and its two analyses","sourceOffset":4996},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":8251},{"kind":"section","title":"An addressed certificate with outward pointers","sourceOffset":8852},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The partial function and its exact circuit","sourceOffset":9508},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The total function","sourceOffset":14170},{"kind":"section","title":"Quantum search with probabilistic comparisons","sourceOffset":20269},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Amplification without changing the sampled distribution","sourceOffset":20996},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The tournament","sourceOffset":24059},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparing columns and verifying the list","sourceOffset":29410},{"kind":"section","title":"The randomized lower bound","sourceOffset":33825}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"The optimal power is $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{tot}}=4$. More precisely, $\\R(f)=O((1+\\Q(f))^4)$ for every total Boolean function. For each fixed integer $k\\ge2$, there are positive constants $c_k,C_k,b_k$ and total Boolean functions $F_{k,m}$, for all sufficiently large powers of four $m$, such that \\begin{equation} \\Q(F_{k,m})\\le C_k\\sqrt m\\,(\\log m)^{b_k}, \\qquad \\R(F_{k,m})\\ge c_k\\frac{m^{2-1/k}}{(\\log m)^2}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":1719,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"A tournament for probabilistic comparisons","label":"prop:tournament","text":"For $m\\ge2$, put \\[ T=\\lceil\\log(1000m)\\rceil, \\qquad \\epsilon=\\frac{1}{1000T}. \\] Suppose a coherent comparison circuit, controlled by two candidate indices and reversible with all workspace retained, uses at most $c\\ge1$ bit queries. Let $a_{ij}$ be the probability that it reports $i$ defeating $j$, and assume \\eqref{eq:complementarity}. There is a quantum algorithm making \\[ O\\bigl(c\\sqrt m\\,T^2\\log T\\bigr) \\] queries on every branch and returning a list of at most $T$ candidates with the following guarantee. If $i_\\ast\\in[m]$ satisfies \\begin{equation} a_{i_\\ast j}\\ge1-\\epsilon\\qquad(j\\ne i_\\ast), \\end{equation} then the list contains $i_\\ast$ with probability at least $1-2m/2^T-T\\epsilon$.","sourceOffset":24741,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":54506,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Nearly-Quartic-Separation-Between-Randomized-and-Quantum-Query-Complexity-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"285-01","slug":"A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-Injectivity-September-23-2026","familyId":"285","title":"A torsion-free counterexample to reduced Baum–Connes injectivity","abstract":"We disprove rational injectivity of the coefficient-free reduced Baum–Connes assembly map for torsion-free groups. Specifically, we construct a finitely generated torsion-free discrete group whose degree-zero assembly map has a kernel class of infinite order.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves the coefficient-free reduced Baum–Connes conjecture through a failure of rational injectivity and a separate failure of surjectivity of assembly. A finitely generated torsion-free example witnesses the injectivity failure. Separately, a torsion-free group has a nontrivial projection in its reduced group C∗-algebra, disproving the Kadison–Kaplansky conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-Injectivity-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-Injectivity-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-Injectivity-September-23-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-Injectivity-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-Injectivity-September-23-2026/build/sections/00_introduction.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-Injectivity-September-23-2026/build/sections/01_labeling.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-Injectivity-September-23-2026/build/sections/02_expansion.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-Injectivity-September-23-2026/build/sections/03_pruning.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-Injectivity-September-23-2026/build/sections/04_analysis.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-Injectivity-September-23-2026/build/sections/05_geometry.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-Injectivity-September-23-2026/build/sections/05b_domains.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-Injectivity-September-23-2026/build/sections/06_assembly.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-Injectivity-September-23-2026/build/sections/07_completion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-Injectivity-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove rational injectivity of the coefficient-free reduced Baum--Connes assembly map for torsion-free groups. Specifically, we construct a finitely generated torsion-free discrete group whose degree-zero assembly map has a kernel class of infinite order.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":306},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The distinction from earlier counterexamples","sourceOffset":2221},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Maximal assembly and the Novikov conjecture","sourceOffset":4852},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction","sourceOffset":7437},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reading the construction","sourceOffset":12381},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite alphabet with deterministic recovery","sourceOffset":13645},{"kind":"subsection","title":"States, families, and extra coordinates","sourceOffset":15319},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite coefficient pools","sourceOffset":20007},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Universal corrections and permutation rules","sourceOffset":23966},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Auxiliary incidences and uniform coordinates","sourceOffset":28087},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Deleting gate failures","sourceOffset":32558},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Recovery of arbitrary padded prefixes","sourceOffset":36159}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist a finitely generated torsion-free discrete group \\(\\Ginj\\), an injective homomorphism \\(i:\\Z^2\\to \\Ginj\\), and a class \\(c\\in H^2(B\\Ginj;\\Z)\\) such that \\[ \\ip{(Bi)^*c}{[T^2]}=1,\\qquad i_*(\\beta)=0\\quad\\text{in }K_0(\\Cr \\Ginj), \\] where \\(\\beta\\) is a rank-zero Bott generator of \\(K_0(\\Cr\\Z^2)\\). In particular, \\(h=(Bi)_*[D_{T^2}]\\) has infinite order in \\(K_0(B\\Ginj)\\), while its reduced analytic assembly image is zero. Here \\([D_{T^2}]\\) is the spin Dirac \\(K\\)-homology class of the oriented torus.","sourceOffset":1139,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Simultaneous disjoint domains","label":"prop:domains","text":"There are sets $R_Q\\subseteq V(Q)$, one for each sheet, such that each $R_Q$ is empty, an old-centered ball $B_Q(v)$, or a private singleton, and the sets $D_Q=V(Q)\\setminus R_Q$ have the following properties. \\begin{enumerate} \\item The physical vertex sets $D_Q$ are pairwise disjoint. \\item Put \\[ E_{\\mathrm{int}}= \\bigcup_{Q\\in\\mathcal Q} \\{e\\in E(Q):\\text{both endpoints of }e\\text{ lie in }D_Q\\}. \\] Every edge in this union has a unique such sheet, and $(V(\\mathcal C),E(\\mathcal C)\\setminus E_{\\mathrm{int}})$ is a forest. \\item $\\operatorname{Star}_Q(R_Q)$ is a forest. If $R_Q$ contains an old vertex of layer $i$, then $\\abs{R_Q}\\leq2^{10H_i}$. A private singleton removes no old coordinate. \\end{enumerate} No equivariance of the choices $R_Q$ is asserted or needed.","sourceOffset":216845,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Coherent return","label":"def:coherent-return","text":"In band $k$, a coherent return is a positive-length nonbacktracking path \\[ v_0,e_1,v_1,\\ldots,e_p,v_p \\] in $X^{\\mathrm{good}}$, entirely in that band, whose endpoints are on the same side and agree through an available aligned depth \\begin{equation} h\\geq ps/2+M_k, \\qquad h\\leq\\min\\{D(v_0),D(v_p)\\}. \\end{equation} It is minimal if no proper positive-length contiguous subpath is a coherent return for the same band and the same $M_k$. An exact closed nonbacktracking path is included whenever it satisfies the available-depth condition.","sourceOffset":79616,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":291775,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-Injectivity-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"285-02","slug":"A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-the-Kadison-Kaplansky-Projection-Conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"285","title":"A torsion-free counterexample to the Kadison–Kaplansky projection conjecture","abstract":"We construct a finitely generated torsion-free discrete group whose reduced group C∗-algebra contains a projection other than zero and the identity. This disproves the Kadison–Kaplansky projection conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves the coefficient-free reduced Baum–Connes conjecture through a failure of rational injectivity and a separate failure of surjectivity of assembly. A finitely generated torsion-free example witnesses the injectivity failure. Separately, a torsion-free group has a nontrivial projection in its reduced group C∗-algebra, disproving the Kadison–Kaplansky conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-the-Kadison-Kaplansky-Projection-Conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-the-Kadison-Kaplansky-Projection-Conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-the-Kadison-Kaplansky-Projection-Conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-the-Kadison-Kaplansky-Projection-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-the-Kadison-Kaplansky-Projection-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-the-Kadison-Kaplansky-Projection-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-the-Kadison-Kaplansky-Projection-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/scalar.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-the-Kadison-Kaplansky-Projection-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/setup.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-the-Kadison-Kaplansky-Projection-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/tower.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-the-Kadison-Kaplansky-Projection-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/expansion.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-the-Kadison-Kaplansky-Projection-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/pruning.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-the-Kadison-Kaplansky-Projection-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-the-Kadison-Kaplansky-Projection-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/nilpotent.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-the-Kadison-Kaplansky-Projection-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/sampling.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-the-Kadison-Kaplansky-Projection-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/moving-band.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-the-Kadison-Kaplansky-Projection-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/spectral.tex","preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-the-Kadison-Kaplansky-Projection-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-the-Kadison-Kaplansky-Projection-Conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a finitely generated torsion-free discrete group whose reduced group \\(C^*\\)-algebra contains a projection other than zero and the identity. This disproves the Kadison--Kaplansky projection conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":288},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context","sourceOffset":1517},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The obstruction and the proof strategy","sourceOffset":4626},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":8795},{"kind":"section","title":"From a small matrix projection to a scalar projection","sourceOffset":9841},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions, parameters, and the matrix target","sourceOffset":16927},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Graphs and labels","sourceOffset":18452},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The growing parameter","sourceOffset":19812},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The order of choices","sourceOffset":21540},{"kind":"section","title":"The finite-alphabet tower before pruning","sourceOffset":23365},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Stream lengths and slots","sourceOffset":24746},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The linear slot evaluations","sourceOffset":28632}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist a finitely generated torsion-free discrete group \\(\\Gproj\\) and a projection \\(e\\in C_r^*(\\Gproj)\\) such that \\[ 0<\\tau_{\\Gproj}(e)<\\frac12. \\] In particular, \\(e\\) is neither zero nor the identity.","sourceOffset":825,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Geometric conclusion","label":"geo:main","text":"There are absolute constants $C_*,C_\\bullet$ such that, for sufficiently large $\\ell$, the preceding hypotheses have the following consequences. Every component map $\\phi:C\\to X$ is an embedding. Index copies by pairs $(C,\\phi)$ modulo the equivalence \\[ (C,\\phi)\\sim (\\delta_a C,\\phi\\delta_a^{-1}),\\qquad a\\in F. \\] Thus an indexed copy $Q$ remembers its source component and source vertices up to this deck alignment, in addition to its embedded image. There is a well-order of these copies and an induced intrinsic ball $B(Q)$ in each $Q$ such that \\begin{enumerate} \\item every vertex of $Q$ shared with an earlier copy belongs to $B(Q)$; \\item the subgraph of $Q$ consisting of all edges with at least one endpoint in $B(Q)$ is a forest; \\item for every vertex of $B(Q)$ lying over layer $i$, \\begin{equation} |V(B(Q))|\\le\\mathfrak w_i, \\qquad \\mathfrak w_i=2^{C_\\bullet H_i}; \\end{equation} \\item if $D_Q$ is $V(Q)$ minus its vertices shared with earlier copies, the domains $D_Q$ are pairwise disjoint, and deleting every edge of $Q$ whose two endpoints lie in $D_Q$, for all $Q$, leaves a forest in $X$. \\end{enumerate} Furthermore $\\Gbase$ is torsion-free. None of the constants depends on $F$, the voltage values, the number of components of $P$, or $|S|$.","sourceOffset":91276,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":260440,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":21,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Torsion-Free-Counterexample-to-the-Kadison-Kaplansky-Projection-Conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"285-03","slug":"An-Irrational-Trace-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-September-23-2026","familyId":"285","title":"An irrational-trace counterexample to reduced Baum–Connes","abstract":"We construct a finitely generated discrete group whose reduced group C∗-algebra contains a projection of irrational canonical trace. Its K0-class lies outside the image of the coefficient-free reduced Baum–Connes assembly map. This disproves the coefficient-free reduced Baum–Connes conjecture for countable discrete groups.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves the coefficient-free reduced Baum–Connes conjecture through a failure of rational injectivity and a separate failure of surjectivity of assembly. A finitely generated torsion-free example witnesses the injectivity failure. Separately, a torsion-free group has a nontrivial projection in its reduced group C∗-algebra, disproving the Kadison–Kaplansky conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-Irrational-Trace-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-Irrational-Trace-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-Irrational-Trace-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-September-23-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/An-Irrational-Trace-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/An-Irrational-Trace-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/An-Irrational-Trace-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-September-23-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/An-Irrational-Trace-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-September-23-2026/build/geometry.tex","preprints/An-Irrational-Trace-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-September-23-2026/build/data.tex","preprints/An-Irrational-Trace-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-September-23-2026/build/module.tex","preprints/An-Irrational-Trace-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-September-23-2026/build/spectrum.tex","preprints/An-Irrational-Trace-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-September-23-2026/build/figures/cycle-charging.tex","preprints/An-Irrational-Trace-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-September-23-2026/build/trace.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-Irrational-Trace-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a finitely generated discrete group whose reduced group \\(C^*\\)-algebra contains a projection of irrational canonical trace. Its \\(K_0\\)-class lies outside the image of the coefficient-free reduced Baum--Connes assembly map. This disproves the coefficient-free reduced Baum--Connes conjecture for countable discrete groups.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":424},{"kind":"section","title":"Coned geometry of graphical presentations","sourceOffset":10677},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Diagrams with adjustable sides","sourceOffset":13439},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Avoidance and uniform thinness","sourceOffset":21387},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Simultaneous overlaps","sourceOffset":27686},{"kind":"section","title":"Labelled expanders and their finite cycle spaces","sourceOffset":29722},{"kind":"section","title":"Codes and the module extension","sourceOffset":37137},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Port patterns and codes","sourceOffset":37765},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The quotient module","sourceOffset":42304},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The compact dual and its finite marginals","sourceOffset":45577},{"kind":"section","title":"An operator with a uniform spectral gap","sourceOffset":48204},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The reduced algebra and its evaluation representations","sourceOffset":48606}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist a finitely generated discrete group \\(\\Gsur\\) and a projection \\(b_0\\in C_r^*(\\Gsur)\\) with irrational canonical trace. Moreover, \\[ [b_0]\\notin\\mu_0^{\\Gsur}\\bigl(K_0^{\\Gsur}(\\underline E\\Gsur)\\bigr). \\] In particular, the coefficient-free reduced Baum--Connes conjecture for countable discrete groups is false.","sourceOffset":3272,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"L\\\"uck","label":"thm:trace-image","text":"For every countable discrete group \\(G\\), \\[ \\tau_{G*}\\!\\left(\\mu_0^G(K_0^G(\\underline EG))\\right)\\subseteq\\Q. \\]","sourceOffset":3855,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":null,"text":"Define the discrete additive group and distinguished element \\[ V=\\F_p^{(\\Gamma)}/W_0,\\qquad v_*=[\\mathbf e_1]\\in V, \\] with the induced \\(\\Gamma\\)-action, and put \\[ \\Gsur=V\\rtimes\\Gamma. \\] We use the multiplication \\((v,g)(v',g')=(v+gv',gg')\\) in \\(\\Gsur\\).","sourceOffset":44611,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":null,"text":"For $\\omega\\in\\Omega$, its enabled graph $G_\\omega$ has vertex set $\\Gamma$. At $h\\in\\Gamma$ the allowed outgoing labels are $\\mathcal J(X_\\omega(h))$. The Cayley edge from $h$ to $hs$ is enabled precisely when $s$ is allowed at $h$ and $s^{-1}$ is allowed at $hs$. A copy $P$ is valid for $\\omega$ if $X_\\omega|_P=w_P$. Let $U_\\omega$ be the complement in $\\Gamma$ of the union of all valid copies.","sourceOffset":51395,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":72013,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-Irrational-Trace-Counterexample-to-Reduced-Baum-Connes-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"286-01","slug":"Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026","familyId":"286","title":"Arithmeticity of twisted finite correspondences for lattices over local fields","abstract":"We prove arithmetic exhaustion for bifinite correspondences between arbitrarily scalar-twisted group factors of ICC groups commensurable with property-$(T)$ lattices over characteristic-zero local fields and twisted group factors of arbitrary countable ICC groups. Every such correspondence is a closed summand of a finite direct sum of models obtained from actual finite-index subgroup isomorphisms and finite-dimensional projective representations of the matched cocycle ratio. The proof includes reducible products, mixed real and finite places, and quaternionic and Cayley rank-one factors.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Classifies finite-index bimodules between scalar-twisted group factors of ICC groups commensurable with property-$(T)$ lattices over characteristic-zero local fields and arbitrary ICC group factors. Every such bimodule is a summand of finite sums of models arising from finite-index subgroup isomorphisms and finite-dimensional projective representations. The classification also recovers the group, scalar cocycle and amplification scale up to the stated stable equivalence.","pdfPath":"preprints/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026/build/figures/proof-map.tex","preprints/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/00-models.tex","preprints/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-setup.tex","preprints/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-links.tex","preprints/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-transfer.tex","preprints/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-finite-panels.tex","preprints/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-ray-estimates.tex","preprints/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-isolated-scales.tex","preprints/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-real-panels.tex","preprints/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-heights.tex","preprints/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026/build/figures/local-branch-table.tex","preprints/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/09-locality.tex","preprints/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/10-gluing.tex","preprints/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026/build/sections/11-arithmetic-recovery.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove arithmetic exhaustion for bifinite correspondences between arbitrarily scalar-twisted group factors of ICC groups commensurable with property-$(T)$ lattices over characteristic-zero local fields and twisted group factors of arbitrary countable ICC groups. Every such correspondence is a closed summand of a finite direct sum of models obtained from actual finite-index subgroup isomorphisms and finite-dimensional projective representations of the matched cocycle ratio. The proof includes reducible products, mixed real and finite places, and quaternionic and Cayley rank-one factors.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":725},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The class, conventions, and main theorem","sourceOffset":1590},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rigidity and finite-correspondence precedents","sourceOffset":7337},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From boundary measurements to subgroup graphs","sourceOffset":12059},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":20387},{"kind":"section","title":"Elementary arithmetic correspondences","sourceOffset":21116},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Projective induction","sourceOffset":21935},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite-module realizations and local-field geometry","sourceOffset":29433},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compact reduction and finite-index return","sourceOffset":30844},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Tensor copies and complementary regular realizations","sourceOffset":41859},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The source groups and full versus partial mixing","sourceOffset":48497},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Panel groups, Cartan coordinates, and Haar volumes","sourceOffset":56248}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Arithmetic exhaustion","label":"thm:arithmetic-exhaustion","text":"Let $\\Gamma\\in\\mathscr K$, let $\\Lambda$ be any countably infinite ICC group, and let $\\mu\\in Z^2(\\Gamma,\\mathbb T)$ and $\\omega\\in Z^2(\\Lambda,\\mathbb T)$ be normalized scalar cocycles. Every bifinite $L_\\mu(\\Gamma)$--$L_\\omega(\\Lambda)$ correspondence is unitarily isomorphic to a closed bimodule summand of a finite direct sum of the models $E(A,B,\\delta,\\sigma)$ in Equation \\eqref{eq:intro-model}, where $A\\leq\\Gamma$ and $B\\leq\\Lambda$ have finite index, $\\delta:A\\to B$ is an actual group isomorphism, and $\\sigma$ is a finite-dimensional projective unitary representation satisfying Equation \\eqref{eq:intro-multiplier}. Conversely, every such model, finite direct sum, and closed bimodule summand is a normal bifinite correspondence. If $n=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}V_\\sigma$, the elementary model has dimensions \\[ \\dim_{L_\\mu(\\Gamma)}E(A,B,\\delta,\\sigma)=n[\\Lambda:B],\\qquad \\dim E(A,B,\\delta,\\sigma)_{L_\\omega(\\Lambda)}=n[\\Gamma:A]. \\]","sourceOffset":5246,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Elementary normality and dimensions","label":"cor:models-dimensions","text":"The correspondence $E(A,B,\\delta,\\sigma)$ is normal and bifinite. If $n=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}V_\\sigma$, then \\[ \\dim_M E(A,B,\\delta,\\sigma)=n[\\Lambda:B],\\qquad \\dim E(A,B,\\delta,\\sigma)_N=n[\\Gamma:A]. \\]","sourceOffset":28320,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:class","text":"Let $\\mathscr K$ be the class of countably infinite ICC groups abstractly commensurable with a lattice in a finite nonempty product \\[ G=\\prod_{i=1}^r G_i,\\qquad G_i=\\mathbf H_i(k_i)^+. \\] Here $k_i$ is $\\mathbb R$, $\\mathbb C$, or a finite extension of $\\mathbb Q_p$; the primes may differ. The algebraic group $\\mathbf H_i$ is connected, adjoint, and absolutely simple over $k_i$. The superscript $+$ denotes the subgroup generated by the $k_i$-points of the unipotent radicals of proper $k_i$-parabolic subgroups. Each $G_i$ is required to be noncompact and to have Kazhdan's property $(T)$. A lat","sourceOffset":1644,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:ray-admissible","text":"An admissible representation for \\(T\\) consists of a finite factor \\[ D=B\\bar\\otimes L_\\eta(\\Gamma) \\] and a normalized projective representation \\(\\Theta:\\Gamma\\to\\mathcal U(D)\\) such that \\[ D\\cap\\Theta(\\Gamma)'=\\mathbb C1 \\] and the representation \\begin{equation} \\gamma\\longmapsto\\Ad\\Delta_T(\\Theta(\\gamma)) \\quad\\hbox{on}\\quad L^2\\bigl(B(L^2(X_T))\\bar\\otimes D,\\Tr\\otimes\\tau\\bigr) \\end{equation} has no invariant vector. Here \\(\\Delta_T(u_h)=\\lambda_{X_T}(h_T)\\otimes u_h\\), with the identity action on \\(B\\). The scalar multiplier of \\(\\Theta\\) is not required to equal \\(\\eta\\).","sourceOffset":311907,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":676210,"sourceFileCount":16,"statementCount":43,"definitionCount":4,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Arithmeticity-of-twisted-finite-correspondences-for-lattices-over-local-fields-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"286-02","slug":"The-arithmetic-category-and-stable-recovery-of-lattice-factors-September-23-2026","familyId":"286","title":"The arithmetic category and stable recovery of lattice factors","abstract":"We prove stable canonical recovery for scalar-twisted group factors of ICC groups commensurable with property-$(T)$ lattices over characteristic-zero local fields. Every specified stable isomorphism to a scalar-twisted factor of any countably infinite ICC group recovers the group, the cocycle up to a normalized cochain, and equal amplification scales, with an implementing partial isometry in the given matrix corners. We compute the arithmetic correspondence subcategory for arbitrary countable ICC groups, including all bounded maps, summands, conjugates, and Connes fusion, and combine it with the companion's arithmetic exhaustion theorem. Multiplication also recovers arbitrary finite-index factor neighbors, with the exact projective condition on the matched cocycle ratio.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Classifies finite-index bimodules between scalar-twisted group factors of ICC groups commensurable with property-$(T)$ lattices over characteristic-zero local fields and arbitrary ICC group factors. Every such bimodule is a summand of finite sums of models arising from finite-index subgroup isomorphisms and finite-dimensional projective representations. The classification also recovers the group, scalar cocycle and amplification scale up to the stated stable equivalence.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-arithmetic-category-and-stable-recovery-of-lattice-factors-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-arithmetic-category-and-stable-recovery-of-lattice-factors-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-arithmetic-category-and-stable-recovery-of-lattice-factors-September-23-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/The-arithmetic-category-and-stable-recovery-of-lattice-factors-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-arithmetic-category-and-stable-recovery-of-lattice-factors-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-arithmetic-category-and-stable-recovery-of-lattice-factors-September-23-2026/build/figures/proof-map.tex","preprints/The-arithmetic-category-and-stable-recovery-of-lattice-factors-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-models.tex","preprints/The-arithmetic-category-and-stable-recovery-of-lattice-factors-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-calculus.tex","preprints/The-arithmetic-category-and-stable-recovery-of-lattice-factors-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-endpoints.tex","preprints/The-arithmetic-category-and-stable-recovery-of-lattice-factors-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-examples.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-arithmetic-category-and-stable-recovery-of-lattice-factors-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove stable canonical recovery for scalar-twisted group factors of ICC groups commensurable with property-$(T)$ lattices over characteristic-zero local fields. Every specified stable isomorphism to a scalar-twisted factor of any countably infinite ICC group recovers the group, the cocycle up to a normalized cochain, and equal amplification scales, with an implementing partial isometry in the given matrix corners. We compute the arithmetic correspondence subcategory for arbitrary countable ICC groups, including all bounded maps, summands, conjugates, and Connes fusion, and combine it with the companion's arithmetic exhaustion theorem. Multiplication also recovers arbitrary finite-index factor neighbors, with the exact projective condition on the matched cocycle ratio.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":869},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The common conventions and lattice class","sourceOffset":2759},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Stable canonical recovery","sourceOffset":5278},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Arbitrary finite-index factor neighbors","sourceOffset":7311},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The arithmetic correspondence category","sourceOffset":9303},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the role of the arithmetic category","sourceOffset":14833},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and navigation","sourceOffset":19145},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite modules and the exhaustion interface","sourceOffset":22611},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Relative tensor products and elementary dimensions","sourceOffset":23186},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The geometric input","sourceOffset":24870},{"kind":"section","title":"Full-germ correspondence calculus","sourceOffset":26614},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Germs and their full stabilizers","sourceOffset":28079}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Presentation equivalence and stable canonical recovery","label":"thm:stable","text":"Let $\\Lambda\\in\\mathscr K$ and let $\\Pi$ be any countably infinite ICC group. Let $\\omega,\\nu$ be arbitrary normalized scalar cocycles on them, and let $t,s>0$. Then \\[ L_\\omega(\\Lambda)^t\\cong L_\\nu(\\Pi)^s \\] if and only if $t=s$ and there are an isomorphism $\\delta:\\Lambda\\to\\Pi$ and a normalized cochain $b:\\Lambda\\to\\mathbb T$ such that \\begin{equation} \\omega(g,h)=b(g)b(h)b(gh)^{-1}\\nu(\\delta(g),\\delta(h)). \\end{equation} Every specified isomorphism at unit scale has the form \\[ \\theta(u_g)=w\\,b(g)v_{\\delta(g)}w^* \\] for a unitary $w$ in the target. More explicitly, put $M=L_\\omega(\\Lambda)$ and $N=L_\\nu(\\Pi)$, and let \\[ \\theta:pM_n(M)p\\longrightarrow qM_m(N)q \\] be a specified isomorphism, where the unnormalized traces of $p$ and $q$ are $t$ and $s$. Then $t=s$, and one may choose $\\delta,b$ as above and $z\\in M_{m,n}(N)$ so that, for the canonical map $\\varphi(u_g)=b(g)v_{\\delta(g)}$, \\[ z^*z=\\varphi^{(n)}(p),\\qquad zz^*=q, \\qquad \\theta(x)=z\\varphi^{(n)}(x)z^*. \\] Whenever such an isomorphism exists, the recovered comparison group $\\Pi$ belongs to $\\mathscr K$. In particular every factor $L_\\omega(\\Lambda)$ in this class has trivial fundamental group.","sourceOffset":5526,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"All factor endpoints","label":"thm:endpoints","text":"Let $\\Gamma\\in\\mathscr K$, let $\\mu$ be any normalized scalar cocycle on $\\Gamma$, and let $P$ be a $\\mathrm{II}_1$ factor with separable predual. There is a nonzero bifinite $L_\\mu(\\Gamma)$--$P$ correspondence if and only if \\[ P\\cong L_\\omega(\\Lambda)^t \\] for a countably infinite ICC group $\\Lambda$, a normalized scalar cocycle $\\omega$ on $\\Lambda$, and $t>0$, for which there are finite-index subgroups $A\\leq\\Gamma$ and $B\\leq\\Lambda$, an actual isomorphism $\\delta:A\\to B$, and a nonzero finite-dimensional projective unitary representation of $A$ with multiplier \\[ \\frac{\\mu|_{A\\times A}}{\\delta^*(\\omega|_{B\\times B})}. \\] In particular, the reconstructed group $\\Lambda$ belongs to $\\mathscr K$.","sourceOffset":8139,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:class","text":"Let $\\mathscr K$ be the class of countably infinite ICC groups abstractly commensurable with a lattice in a finite nonempty product \\[ G=\\prod_{i=1}^r G_i,\\qquad G_i=\\mathbf H_i(k_i)^+. \\] Here $k_i$ is $\\mathbb R$, $\\mathbb C$, or a finite extension of $\\mathbb Q_p$; the primes may differ. The algebraic group $\\mathbf H_i$ is connected, adjoint, and absolutely simple over $k_i$. The superscript $+$ denotes the subgroup generated by the $k_i$-points of the unipotent radicals of proper $k_i$-parabolic subgroups. Each $G_i$ is required to be noncompact and to have Kazhdan's property $(T)$. A lat","sourceOffset":2813,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":94491,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-arithmetic-category-and-stable-recovery-of-lattice-factors-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/The-arithmetic-category-and-stable-recovery-of-lattice-factors-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"287-01","slug":"An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026","familyId":"287","title":"An isomorphism of the free group factors","abstract":"We solve the free group factor isomorphism problem affirmatively by proving that $L(\\mathbb F_2)$ and $L(\\mathbb F_3)$ are isomorphic as tracial von Neumann algebras. The classical free group factor alternative then implies that all interpolated free group factors, including $L(\\mathbb F_\\infty)$, are isomorphic and have fundamental group $\\mathbb R_{\\gt 0}$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the free group factor isomorphism problem: $L(\\mathbb F_2)\\cong L(\\mathbb F_3)$, and hence all interpolated free group factors, including $L(\\mathbb F_\\infty)$, are isomorphic. Their common factor has fundamental group $\\mathbb R_{\\gt 0}$.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026/An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026/An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026/build/sections/flow.tex","preprints/An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026/build/sections/cocycle.tex","preprints/An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026/build/sections/generation.tex","preprints/An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026/build/figures/iteration.tex","preprints/An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026/build/sections/amplification.tex","preprints/An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026/build/sections/entropy-dimensions.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We solve the free group factor isomorphism problem affirmatively by proving that $L(\\F_2)$ and $L(\\F_3)$ are isomorphic as tracial von Neumann algebras. The classical free group factor alternative then implies that all interpolated free group factors, including $L(\\F_\\infty)$, are isomorphic and have fundamental group $\\R_{>0}$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":376},{"kind":"section","title":"Tracial preliminaries and a free-sum estimate","sourceOffset":9342},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Haar tuples and normal isomorphisms","sourceOffset":11206},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conjugates of a bounded logarithm","sourceOffset":13956},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A bound independent of support size","sourceOffset":16652},{"kind":"section","title":"Trace-preserving polynomial flows","sourceOffset":19487},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The formal derivation and its trace identity","sourceOffset":22416},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Global solutions and actual moment invariance","sourceOffset":27244},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Surjectivity, normal extension, and estimates","sourceOffset":32259},{"kind":"section","title":"A small cocycle with a prescribed word value","sourceOffset":36584},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Free prefix words","sourceOffset":38231},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The spectral measures of the prefix sums","sourceOffset":41064}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exists a unital normal trace-preserving $*$-isomorphism \\[ \\Phi:L(\\F_2)\\longrightarrow L(\\F_3). \\]","sourceOffset":2983,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Isomorphism of consecutive ranks","label":"thm:rank-step","text":"For every integer $n\\geq3$ there is a unital trace-preserving normal $*$-isomorphism \\[ L(\\F_n)\\cong L(\\F_{n+1}). \\]","sourceOffset":2576,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":null,"text":"A freely generating Haar $d$-tuple in $M$ is a tuple of unitaries $U=(U_1,\\ldots,U_d)$ such that $W^*(U)=M$ and \\[ \\tau(U_g)=0\\qquad(g\\in\\F_d\\setminus\\{e\\}). \\] Here $U_g$ is the evaluation of the reduced group word $g$ in $U$, with inverse letters evaluated as adjoints.","sourceOffset":11255,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/287.md","scopeText":"The free group factor problem asks whether the von Neumann algebras of free groups of different ranks are isomorphic. The formalization proves that interpolated free group factors with any parameters $r,s>1$, including the infinite parameter, are normally trace-preservingly isomorphic. The paper's fundamental-group conclusion is a further consequence rather than a separate selected statement here.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/287.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":71973,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-isomorphism-of-the-free-group-factors-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"288-01","slug":"Kadisons-similarity-theorem-through-uniform-derivation-estimates-September-23-2026","familyId":"288","title":"Kadison's similarity theorem through uniform derivation estimates","abstract":"We prove that every bounded complex-linear unital algebra homomorphism from a unital complex C∗-algebra into the bounded operators on an arbitrary Hilbert space is similar to a $*$-homomorphism. This resolves Kadison's similarity conjecture positively. We also obtain one universal hyperreflexivity constant for all unital von Neumann algebras on arbitrary complex Hilbert spaces.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Kadison's similarity conjecture: every bounded complex-linear unital algebra homomorphism from a unital complex C∗-algebra to operators on a Hilbert space becomes a $*$-homomorphism after conjugation by a bounded invertible operator.","pdfPath":"preprints/Kadisons-similarity-theorem-through-uniform-derivation-estimates-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Kadisons-similarity-theorem-through-uniform-derivation-estimates-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Kadisons-similarity-theorem-through-uniform-derivation-estimates-September-23-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/Kadisons-similarity-theorem-through-uniform-derivation-estimates-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Kadisons-similarity-theorem-through-uniform-derivation-estimates-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Kadisons-similarity-theorem-through-uniform-derivation-estimates-September-23-2026/build/unicode-map.tex","preprints/Kadisons-similarity-theorem-through-uniform-derivation-estimates-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Kadisons-similarity-theorem-through-uniform-derivation-estimates-September-23-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Kadisons-similarity-theorem-through-uniform-derivation-estimates-September-23-2026/build/sections/tensors.tex","preprints/Kadisons-similarity-theorem-through-uniform-derivation-estimates-September-23-2026/build/sections/propagation.tex","preprints/Kadisons-similarity-theorem-through-uniform-derivation-estimates-September-23-2026/build/sections/hankel.tex","preprints/Kadisons-similarity-theorem-through-uniform-derivation-estimates-September-23-2026/build/sections/finite-factors.tex","preprints/Kadisons-similarity-theorem-through-uniform-derivation-estimates-September-23-2026/build/sections/global.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Kadisons-similarity-theorem-through-uniform-derivation-estimates-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every bounded complex-linear unital algebra homomorphism from a unital complex $C^*$-algebra into the bounded operators on an arbitrary Hilbert space is similar to a $*$-homomorphism. This resolves Kadison's similarity conjecture positively. We also obtain one universal hyperreflexivity constant for all unital von Neumann algebras on arbitrary complex Hilbert spaces.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":429},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":1930},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The uniform estimate and proof strategy","sourceOffset":5481},{"kind":"section","title":"Rows, cyclic modules, and derivations","sourceOffset":11135},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform row and column estimates","sourceOffset":13724},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Completely bounded implementation","sourceOffset":16377},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cyclic domains","sourceOffset":18940},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The derivation criterion for similarity","sourceOffset":24267},{"kind":"section","title":"Free-product geometry and invariant tensors","sourceOffset":25946},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Factoriality and symmetrization","sourceOffset":28033},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The word decomposition","sourceOffset":32222},{"kind":"section","title":"Propagation from small corners","sourceOffset":44726}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Similarity","label":"thm:similarity","text":"Let \\(A\\) be any unital complex \\(C^*\\)-algebra, let \\(H\\) be any complex Hilbert space, and let \\(\\pi:A\\to\\mathcal B(H)\\) be a bounded, complex-linear, unital algebra homomorphism. There exists an operator \\(S\\in\\mathcal B(H)\\), invertible with bounded inverse, such that \\[ \\rho(a)=S\\pi(a)S^{-1}\\qquad(a\\in A) \\] is a \\(*\\)-homomorphism. In particular, \\(\\rho(a^*)=\\rho(a)^*\\) for every \\(a\\in A\\).","sourceOffset":1155,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform commutator estimate","label":"thm:universal","text":"There is an absolute constant \\(C<\\infty\\) such that, for every complex Hilbert space \\(K\\), every unital von Neumann algebra \\(P\\subset\\mathcal B(K)\\), every \\(Y\\in\\mathcal B(K)\\), every integer \\(h\\ge1\\), and every \\(X\\in M_h(P)\\), \\begin{equation} \\|[Y^{(h)},X]\\|\\le C\\,g_P(Y)\\,\\|X\\|. \\end{equation} Here \\(M_h(P)\\) acts naturally on \\(K^h\\), and \\([T,U]=TU-UT\\).","sourceOffset":6008,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/288.md","scopeText":"Kadison's similarity problem asks whether every bounded unital homomorphism from a unital complex $C^*$-algebra into operators on a Hilbert space is similar to a $*$-homomorphism. The formalization proves this for arbitrary Hilbert spaces. It also gives one universal hyperreflexivity constant for all unital von Neumann algebras.\n\nThe linked commutator estimate is uniform over all finite matrix amplifications: its constant is independent of the algebra, Hilbert space, and amplification size. This is the quantitative derivation estimate supporting the similarity result.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/288.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":96639,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Kadisons-similarity-theorem-through-uniform-derivation-estimates-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Kadisons-similarity-theorem-through-uniform-derivation-estimates-September-23-2026/build/unicode-map.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"289-01","slug":"Universal-strong-Kadison-Kastler-stability-September-23-2026","familyId":"289","title":"Universal strong Kadison–Kastler stability","abstract":"We prove that sufficiently close unital von Neumann algebras are conjugate by a unitary arbitrarily close to the identity. The tolerance depends only on the prescribed distance of that unitary from the identity, uniformly over all algebras, representations, and Hilbert spaces. This resolves the strong Kadison–Kastler conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that sufficiently close unital von Neumann algebras on the same Hilbert space are conjugate by a unitary arbitrarily close to the identity, with a universal tolerance in the operator-norm distance between unit balls. Counterexamples show that near-identity conjugacy fails for one-sided near inclusions, and that arbitrarily close norm-separable C∗-algebras need not be ambiently unitarily conjugate.","pdfPath":"preprints/Universal-strong-Kadison-Kastler-stability-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Universal-strong-Kadison-Kastler-stability-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Universal-strong-Kadison-Kastler-stability-September-23-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/Universal-strong-Kadison-Kastler-stability-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Universal-strong-Kadison-Kastler-stability-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Universal-strong-Kadison-Kastler-stability-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Universal-strong-Kadison-Kastler-stability-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-foundations.tex","preprints/Universal-strong-Kadison-Kastler-stability-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-amplification.tex","preprints/Universal-strong-Kadison-Kastler-stability-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-boundary.tex","preprints/Universal-strong-Kadison-Kastler-stability-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-finite.tex","preprints/Universal-strong-Kadison-Kastler-stability-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-modular-preparation.tex","preprints/Universal-strong-Kadison-Kastler-stability-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-modular-alignment.tex","preprints/Universal-strong-Kadison-Kastler-stability-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-cores.tex","preprints/Universal-strong-Kadison-Kastler-stability-September-23-2026/build/sections/09-reductions.tex","preprints/Universal-strong-Kadison-Kastler-stability-September-23-2026/build/sections/10-comparison.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Universal-strong-Kadison-Kastler-stability-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that sufficiently close unital von Neumann algebras are conjugate by a unitary arbitrarily close to the identity. The tolerance depends only on the prescribed distance of that unitary from the identity, uniformly over all algebras, representations, and Hilbert spaces. This resolves the strong Kadison--Kastler conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":394},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":1718},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":5703},{"kind":"section","title":"Perturbation tools and a completely positive criterion","sourceOffset":13580},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Metrics, amplification, and limits","sourceOffset":13930},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The amenable embedding theorem","sourceOffset":16754},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From commutators to intertwiners","sourceOffset":17673},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The completely positive criterion","sourceOffset":20561},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Tensor products and averaging","sourceOffset":24266},{"kind":"section","title":"Uniform amplification of commutators","sourceOffset":28544},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Columns and trace limits","sourceOffset":29688},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Free projection words","sourceOffset":33044}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Strong Kadison--Kastler stability","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $\\eps>0$ there is $\\delta=\\delta(\\eps)>0$ such that the following holds. If $H$ is any complex Hilbert space and $M,N\\subseteq\\B(H)$ are unital von Neumann algebras with identity $I_H$ and $d(M,N)<\\delta$, there is a unitary $u\\in\\B(H)$ satisfying \\[ uMu^*=N,\\qquad \\norm{u-I_H}<\\eps. \\]","sourceOffset":1042,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Amenable near inclusions","label":"thm:amenable","text":"Let $R,Q\\subseteq\\B(H)$ be von Neumann algebras with identity $I$, with $R$ amenable. If $R\\subset_\\gamma Q$ and $\\gamma<1/100$, there is a unitary $v\\in(R\\cup Q)”$ such that \\[ vRv^*\\subseteq Q,\\qquad \\norm{v-I}\\le150\\gamma. \\] If also $Q\\subset_\\gamma R$ and $\\gamma<1/101$, the same conclusion holds with $vRv^*=Q$.","sourceOffset":16925,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Prepared pair","label":"def:prepared-pair","text":"A prepared pair consists of type $\\III_1$ factors $M,N\\subseteq\\B(H)$ on a separable Hilbert space, a common cyclic separating unit vector $\\Omega$, and a hyperfinite $\\II_1$ factor $R\\subseteq M\\cap N$ such that \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item $R'\\cap M=R'\\cap N=\\C1$; \\item the vector state $\\varphi(x)=\\ip{x\\Omega}{\\Omega}$ centralizes $R$ when restricted to either algebra; \\item $J_MrJ_M=J_NrJ_N$ for every $r\\in R$, where $J_M,J_N$ are the modular conjugations associated with $\\Omega$. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":127666,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/289.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves the strong Kadison–Kastler conjecture uniformly. For every $\\varepsilon>0$, there is a $\\delta>0$ such that any two unital von Neumann algebras on the same complex Hilbert space at Kadison–Kastler distance below $\\delta$ are conjugate by a unitary $u$ with $\\lVert u-1\\rVert<\\varepsilon$. The tolerance depends only on $\\varepsilon$, uniformly over the algebras, their representations, and the Hilbert space.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/289.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":214964,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Universal-strong-Kadison-Kastler-stability-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"289-02","slug":"Near-Inclusions-of-von-Neumann-Algebras-Without-Small-Spatial-Embeddings-October-5-2026","familyId":"289","title":"Near Inclusions of von Neumann Algebras Without Small Spatial Embeddings","abstract":"We give a negative answer to the unrestricted small-spatial-embedding problem for one-sided near inclusions of von Neumann algebras. On separable complex Hilbert spaces, we construct pairs of unital von Neumann algebras with common identity whose one-sided gaps tend to zero. Spatial embeddings of the source into the target exist, but every implementing unitary stays a fixed positive distance from the identity. The obstruction therefore concerns small implementing unitaries, rather than the existence of spatial embeddings.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that sufficiently close unital von Neumann algebras on the same Hilbert space are conjugate by a unitary arbitrarily close to the identity, with a universal tolerance in the operator-norm distance between unit balls. Counterexamples show that near-identity conjugacy fails for one-sided near inclusions, and that arbitrarily close norm-separable C∗-algebras need not be ambiently unitarily conjugate.","pdfPath":"preprints/Near-Inclusions-of-von-Neumann-Algebras-Without-Small-Spatial-Embeddings-October-5-2026/near-inclusions.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Near-Inclusions-of-von-Neumann-Algebras-Without-Small-Spatial-Embeddings-October-5-2026/near-inclusions.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Near-Inclusions-of-von-Neumann-Algebras-Without-Small-Spatial-Embeddings-October-5-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/Near-Inclusions-of-von-Neumann-Algebras-Without-Small-Spatial-Embeddings-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Near-Inclusions-of-von-Neumann-Algebras-Without-Small-Spatial-Embeddings-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Near-Inclusions-of-von-Neumann-Algebras-Without-Small-Spatial-Embeddings-October-5-2026/build/sections/circle.tex","preprints/Near-Inclusions-of-von-Neumann-Algebras-Without-Small-Spatial-Embeddings-October-5-2026/build/sections/near.tex","preprints/Near-Inclusions-of-von-Neumann-Algebras-Without-Small-Spatial-Embeddings-October-5-2026/build/sections/obstruction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Near-Inclusions-of-von-Neumann-Algebras-Without-Small-Spatial-Embeddings-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a negative answer to the unrestricted small-spatial-embedding problem for one-sided near inclusions of von Neumann algebras. On separable complex Hilbert spaces, we construct pairs of unital von Neumann algebras with common identity whose one-sided gaps tend to zero. Spatial embeddings of the source into the target exist, but every implementing unitary stays a fixed positive distance from the identity. The obstruction therefore concerns small implementing unitaries, rather than the existence of spatial embeddings.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":573},{"kind":"section","title":"A circle family with uniform Fourier bounds","sourceOffset":6576},{"kind":"section","title":"From a unitary pair to a near inclusion","sourceOffset":11938},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Matrix substitution and the algebras","sourceOffset":12228},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A norm estimate on the whole algebra","sourceOffset":14458},{"kind":"section","title":"Recovering the matrix obstruction","sourceOffset":17837},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The winding obstruction","sourceOffset":18176},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite-dimensional corner","sourceOffset":20379},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof of the main theorem","sourceOffset":24140}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist $\\varepsilon_0>0$, separable complex Hilbert spaces $H_n$, and von Neumann algebras $M_n,N_n\\subseteq\\mathcal B(H_n)$ with common identity $I_{H_n}$ such that \\[ \\gamma(M_n,N_n)\\longrightarrow 0, \\] but, for all sufficiently large $n$, every unitary $u\\in\\mathcal B(H_n)$ satisfying $uM_nu^*\\subseteq N_n$ obeys \\[ \\|u-I_{H_n}\\|\\geq\\varepsilon_0. \\]","sourceOffset":2889,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:circle","text":"There are unitary representations $\\pi_z:F\\to\\B(K)$, indexed by $z\\in\\mathbb T$, and operators $A_k(g)\\in\\B(K)$, indexed by $k\\in\\mathbb Z$ and $g\\in F$, such that \\begin{align} \\pi_z(g)&=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}z^kA_k(g), &\\sup_{g\\in F}\\|A_k(g)\\|&\\leq c_k, &c_k&=\\mathbf 1_{\\{k=0\\}}+\\frac{2t^{|k|}}{1-t}, \\\\ \\pi_z(s_j)&\\xrightarrow[j\\to\\infty]{\\WOT}tzp_e. \\end{align} The series in \\eqref{eq:fourier} converges absolutely in operator norm, uniformly in $g$ and $z$, and \\begin{equation} S_0:=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}c_k<\\infty, \\qquad S_1:=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}|k|c_k<\\infty. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":7319,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/289.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":28341,"sourceFileCount":5,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Near-Inclusions-of-von-Neumann-Algebras-Without-Small-Spatial-Embeddings-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Near-Inclusions-of-von-Neumann-Algebras-Without-Small-Spatial-Embeddings-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Near-Inclusions-of-von-Neumann-Algebras-Without-Small-Spatial-Embeddings-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"289-03","slug":"Close-Separable-Cstar-Algebras-Without-Spatial-Conjugacy-October-5-2026","familyId":"289","title":"Close Separable C*-Algebras Without Spatial Conjugacy","abstract":"We disprove the separable C∗-algebraic spatial form of the Kadison–Kastler conjecture. For every ε > 0, we construct unital, norm-separable C∗-algebras on a common separable complex Hilbert space, with the same identity and Kadison–Kastler distance less than ε, that are not conjugate by any unitary. The two algebras have the same von Neumann closure.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that sufficiently close unital von Neumann algebras on the same Hilbert space are conjugate by a unitary arbitrarily close to the identity, with a universal tolerance in the operator-norm distance between unit balls. Counterexamples show that near-identity conjugacy fails for one-sided near inclusions, and that arbitrarily close norm-separable C∗-algebras need not be ambiently unitarily conjugate.","pdfPath":"preprints/Close-Separable-Cstar-Algebras-Without-Spatial-Conjugacy-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Close-Separable-Cstar-Algebras-Without-Spatial-Conjugacy-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Close-Separable-Cstar-Algebras-Without-Spatial-Conjugacy-October-5-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/Close-Separable-Cstar-Algebras-Without-Spatial-Conjugacy-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Close-Separable-Cstar-Algebras-Without-Spatial-Conjugacy-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Close-Separable-Cstar-Algebras-Without-Spatial-Conjugacy-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove the separable $C^*$-algebraic spatial form of the Kadison--Kastler conjecture. For every $\\varepsilon>0$, we construct unital, norm-separable $C^*$-algebras on a common separable complex Hilbert space, with the same identity and Kadison--Kastler distance less than $\\varepsilon$, that are not conjugate by any unitary. The two algebras have the same von Neumann closure.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":429},{"kind":"section","title":"A convergent-sequence obstruction","sourceOffset":5316},{"kind":"section","title":"A uniformly small free-group deformation","sourceOffset":12319},{"kind":"section","title":"Close inclusions distinguished by a tensor norm","sourceOffset":17913},{"kind":"section","title":"The counterexamples","sourceOffset":24647}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $\\varepsilon>0$ there are a separable complex Hilbert space $H$ and unital, norm-separable $C^*$-algebras $A,B\\subseteq\\B(H)$ with common identity $\\id_H$ such that \\[ \\KK(A,B)<\\varepsilon,\\qquad A”=B”, \\] but no unitary $u\\in\\B(H)$ satisfies $uAu^*=B$.","sourceOffset":1316,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:sequences","text":"Let $L$ be a nonzero separable Hilbert space, let $D\\subseteq\\B(L)$ be a unital norm-separable $C^*$-algebra with $Z(D)=\\C\\id_L$, and let $P,Q\\subseteq D$ be unital $C^*$-subalgebras containing $\\id_L$. On $H=\\bigoplus_{n\\ge1}L$, define \\begin{equation} \\E_D(P)=\\{(x_n)_{n\\ge1}:x_n\\in D,\\ x_n\\to p\\in P\\text{ in norm}\\}, \\end{equation} and define $\\E_D(Q)$ similarly. Then: \\begin{enumerate} \\item These are unital norm-separable $C^*$-algebras with common identity, and \\begin{equation} \\KK(\\E_D(P),\\E_D(Q))=\\KK(P,Q). \\end{equation} \\item If a unitary conjugates $\\E_D(P)$ onto $\\E_D(Q)$, there are unitaries $U_n\\in\\B(L)$ with $U_nDU_n^*=D$ such that, for every $b\\in Q$, some $p_b\\in P$ satisfies \\begin{equation} \\norm{b-U_np_bU_n^*}\\longrightarrow0. \\end{equation} The same sequence $(U_n)$ works for every $b$. \\item The two algebras have the same commutant, and hence the same von Neumann closure. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":5601,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:tensor","text":"For a unital inclusion $P\\subseteq D\\subseteq\\B(L)$, say that $P$ has the spatial tensor-norm identity relative to $D$ if \\begin{equation} \\norm{\\sum_{i=1}^m p_i z_i}_{\\B(L)} =\\norm{\\sum_{i=1}^m p_i\\otimes z_i}_{\\B(L\\otimes L)} \\end{equation} for every positive integer $m$, all $p_1,\\ldots,p_m\\in P$, and all $z_1,\\ldots,z_m\\in D'$.","sourceOffset":9863,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/289.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":27568,"sourceFileCount":2,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Close-Separable-Cstar-Algebras-Without-Spatial-Conjugacy-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"290-01","slug":"Expected-amenable-subalgebras-preserving-core-commutants-September-23-2026","familyId":"290","title":"Expected amenable subalgebras preserving core commutants","abstract":"We prove that every inclusion $N\\subset M$ of von Neumann algebras with separable preduals and a faithful normal conditional expectation contains an expected amenable subalgebra $P\\subset N$ such that $P'\\cap c(M)=N'\\cap c(M)$, where $c(M)$ denotes the continuous core of M. This resolves the relative bicentralizer conjecture in this setting.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Connes' bicentralizer conjecture for every type III1 factor with separable predual and every faithful normal state. More generally, for every inclusion $N\\subset M$ of von Neumann algebras with separable preduals admitting a faithful normal conditional expectation, constructs an amenable expected subalgebra $P\\subset N$ with $P'\\cap c(M)=N'\\cap c(M)$, resolving the relative bicentralizer conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/Expected-amenable-subalgebras-preserving-core-commutants-September-23-2026/Expected-amenable-subalgebras-preserving-core-commutants-September-23-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Expected-amenable-subalgebras-preserving-core-commutants-September-23-2026/Expected-amenable-subalgebras-preserving-core-commutants-September-23-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Expected-amenable-subalgebras-preserving-core-commutants-September-23-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/Expected-amenable-subalgebras-preserving-core-commutants-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Expected-amenable-subalgebras-preserving-core-commutants-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every inclusion $N\\subset M$ of von Neumann algebras with separable preduals and a faithful normal conditional expectation contains an expected amenable subalgebra $P\\subset N$ such that $P'\\cap c(M)=N'\\cap c(M)$, where $c(M)$ denotes the continuous core of $M$. This resolves the relative bicentralizer conjecture in this setting.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":391},{"kind":"section","title":"Modular notation and bicentralizer inputs","sourceOffset":10596},{"kind":"section","title":"The absolute bicentralizer","sourceOffset":16491},{"kind":"section","title":"The two relative products","sourceOffset":25534},{"kind":"section","title":"An estimate uniform in total modular energy","sourceOffset":27586},{"kind":"section","title":"Repeated tensor tests","sourceOffset":39630},{"kind":"section","title":"The continuous core and the general inclusion","sourceOffset":48874}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $N\\subset M$ be a unital inclusion of von Neumann algebras with separable preduals, and suppose that there is a faithful normal conditional expectation $E_N:M\\to N$. There exist a unital amenable von Neumann subalgebra $P\\subset N$ and a faithful normal conditional expectation $F:N\\to P$ such that \\[ P'\\cap c(M)=N'\\cap c(M). \\] Here amenable means injective.","sourceOffset":1168,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:masa","text":"Under the hypotheses of Theorem \\ref{thm:main}, there is a maximal abelian subalgebra $A\\subset N$ with a faithful normal conditional expectation $F:N\\to A$ such that \\[ A'\\cap c(M)=c(A)\\vee\\bigl(N'\\cap c(M)\\bigr). \\]","sourceOffset":6180,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/290.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":61636,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Expected-amenable-subalgebras-preserving-core-commutants-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"290-02","slug":"Bounded-recovery-for-modular-spectral-averages-September-23-2026","familyId":"290","title":"Bounded recovery for modular spectral averages","abstract":"We prove a bounded spectral recovery theorem for a von Neumann algebra with a faithful normal state whose centralizer consists only of scalars. A positive averaged squared norm for an arbitrary bounded Hilbert-space operator on shrinking modular spectral bands can be recovered on uniformly bounded algebra elements with shrinking spectral support. We apply this theorem to spectral intertwining rigidity and obtain an alternative proof of bicentralizer triviality for type III1 factors with separable predual.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Connes' bicentralizer conjecture for every type III1 factor with separable predual and every faithful normal state. More generally, for every inclusion $N\\subset M$ of von Neumann algebras with separable preduals admitting a faithful normal conditional expectation, constructs an amenable expected subalgebra $P\\subset N$ with $P'\\cap c(M)=N'\\cap c(M)$, resolving the relative bicentralizer conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/Bounded-recovery-for-modular-spectral-averages-September-23-2026/Bounded-recovery-for-modular-spectral-averages-September-23-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Bounded-recovery-for-modular-spectral-averages-September-23-2026/Bounded-recovery-for-modular-spectral-averages-September-23-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Bounded-recovery-for-modular-spectral-averages-September-23-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/Bounded-recovery-for-modular-spectral-averages-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Bounded-recovery-for-modular-spectral-averages-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Bounded-recovery-for-modular-spectral-averages-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Bounded-recovery-for-modular-spectral-averages-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-bicentralizers.tex","preprints/Bounded-recovery-for-modular-spectral-averages-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-modular-averaging.tex","preprints/Bounded-recovery-for-modular-spectral-averages-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-bounded-recovery.tex","preprints/Bounded-recovery-for-modular-spectral-averages-September-23-2026/build/figures/bounded-recovery.tex","preprints/Bounded-recovery-for-modular-spectral-averages-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-spectral-rigidity.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Bounded-recovery-for-modular-spectral-averages-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a bounded spectral recovery theorem for a von Neumann algebra with a faithful normal state whose centralizer consists only of scalars. A positive averaged squared norm for an arbitrary bounded Hilbert-space operator on shrinking modular spectral bands can be recovered on uniformly bounded algebra elements with shrinking spectral support. We apply this theorem to spectral intertwining rigidity and obtain an alternative proof of bicentralizer triviality for type $\\mathrm{III}_1$ factors with separable predual.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":568},{"kind":"section","title":"Bicentralizers and the reduction","sourceOffset":10440},{"kind":"section","title":"Modular localization and averaging","sourceOffset":14211},{"kind":"section","title":"Recovering bounded operators from spectral vectors","sourceOffset":21567},{"kind":"section","title":"Spectral rigidity","sourceOffset":34303},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From bounded recovery to an interval estimate","sourceOffset":34584},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The joint spectral pairing","sourceOffset":37878},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conclusion of the proof","sourceOffset":42381}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $M$ be a type $\\mathrm{III}_1$ factor with separable predual and let $\\phi$ be any faithful normal state on $M$. Then \\[ \\BC(M,\\phi)=\\C1. \\]","sourceOffset":4810,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Spectral intertwining rigidity","label":"thm:rigidity","text":"Let $M$ be a von Neumann algebra with a faithful normal state $\\phi$ such that $M_\\phi=\\C1$. Let $b:\\R\\curvearrowright M$ be a continuous $\\phi$-preserving action. Suppose that, for every $s\\in\\R$, every uniformly bounded sequence $(w_n)$ in $M$, and every $a\\in M$, \\begin{equation} \\norm{\\phi w_n-e^s w_n\\phi}_{M_*}\\longrightarrow0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad w_na-b_s(a)w_n\\longrightarrow0 \\quad\\text{strongly}. \\end{equation} Then $b_s=\\id_M$ for every $s\\in\\R$.","sourceOffset":6534,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/290.md","scopeText":"For a faithful normal state with scalar centralizer in the stated standard-space representation, the formalized result converts positive modular spectral averages into uniformly bounded algebra elements. Given unit vectors in shrinking spectral bands around a real number $s$ and a positive limiting symmetric average for a bounded operator $T$, it finds a subsequence of bounded algebra elements whose images under $T$ stay uniformly nonzero and whose spectral bands have four times the original widths. The absolute bicentralizer conjecture is not included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/290.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":45882,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Bounded-recovery-for-modular-spectral-averages-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"291-01","slug":"Equivariant-Jiang-Su-Stability-for-Amenable-Actions-in-the-Unital-Stably-Finite-Case-October-5-2026","familyId":"291","title":"Equivariant Jiang–Su Stability for Amenable Actions in the Unital Stably Finite Case","abstract":"Every action of a countable discrete amenable group on a simple, separable, unital, infinite-dimensional, nuclear, stably finite complex C∗-algebra that is already Jiang–Su stable absorbs the trivial action on the Jiang–Su algebra up to cocycle conjugacy. No restriction is imposed on the action on the tracial-state simplex. This proves the unital, stably finite case of Szabó's Conjecture A on automatic equivariant Jiang–Su stability.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves equivariant Jiang–Su stability for every countable discrete amenable group action on a simple separable unital infinite-dimensional nuclear stably finite Jiang–Su-stable C∗-algebra, resolving this case of Szabó's conjecture without restrictions on trace dynamics. The family also proves the unital Toms–Winter conjecture, equating strict comparison, finite nuclear dimension and Jiang–Su stability in the simple separable unital infinite-dimensional nuclear setting.","pdfPath":"preprints/Equivariant-Jiang-Su-Stability-for-Amenable-Actions-in-the-Unital-Stably-Finite-Case-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Equivariant-Jiang-Su-Stability-for-Amenable-Actions-in-the-Unital-Stably-Finite-Case-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Equivariant-Jiang-Su-Stability-for-Amenable-Actions-in-the-Unital-Stably-Finite-Case-October-5-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/Equivariant-Jiang-Su-Stability-for-Amenable-Actions-in-the-Unital-Stably-Finite-Case-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Equivariant-Jiang-Su-Stability-for-Amenable-Actions-in-the-Unital-Stably-Finite-Case-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Equivariant-Jiang-Su-Stability-for-Amenable-Actions-in-the-Unital-Stably-Finite-Case-October-5-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Equivariant-Jiang-Su-Stability-for-Amenable-Actions-in-the-Unital-Stably-Finite-Case-October-5-2026/build/sections/rows.tex","preprints/Equivariant-Jiang-Su-Stability-for-Amenable-Actions-in-the-Unital-Stably-Finite-Case-October-5-2026/build/sections/gaussian.tex","preprints/Equivariant-Jiang-Su-Stability-for-Amenable-Actions-in-the-Unital-Stably-Finite-Case-October-5-2026/build/sections/splittings.tex","preprints/Equivariant-Jiang-Su-Stability-for-Amenable-Actions-in-the-Unital-Stably-Finite-Case-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Equivariant-Jiang-Su-Stability-for-Amenable-Actions-in-the-Unital-Stably-Finite-Case-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every action of a countable discrete amenable group on a simple, separable, unital, infinite-dimensional, nuclear, stably finite complex \\(\\mathrm C^*\\)-algebra that is already Jiang--Su stable absorbs the trivial action on the Jiang--Su algebra up to cocycle conjugacy. No restriction is imposed on the action on the tracial-state simplex. This proves the unital, stably finite case of Szab\\'o's Conjecture A on automatic equivariant Jiang--Su stability.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":501},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and scope","sourceOffset":2108},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Construction and proof structure","sourceOffset":3064},{"kind":"section","title":"Traces and the absorption criterion","sourceOffset":5287},{"kind":"section","title":"Commuting orbit rows and joint moments","sourceOffset":8522},{"kind":"section","title":"Gaussian quadratics over F{\\o}lner sets","sourceOffset":23085},{"kind":"section","title":"Invariant trace splittings and absorption","sourceOffset":39885},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A weighted Haar unitary","sourceOffset":41334},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Equal trace projections","sourceOffset":44785},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The cocycle equations","sourceOffset":48539}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(A\\) be a simple, separable, unital, infinite-dimensional, nuclear, stably finite complex \\(\\mathrm C^*\\)-algebra satisfying \\(A\\cong A\\otimes\\Zalg\\). Let \\(G\\) be a countable discrete amenable group and let \\(\\alpha:G\\to\\Aut(A)\\) be any action. Put \\[ B=A\\otimes\\Zalg,\\qquad \\beta_g=\\alpha_g\\otimes\\operatorname{id}_{\\Zalg}. \\] There are a unital \\(*\\)-isomorphism \\(\\Phi:A\\to B\\) and unitaries \\(u_g\\in B\\), \\(g\\in G\\), such that \\begin{align} u_e&=1,& u_{gh}&=u_g\\beta_g(u_h),\\\\ \\Phi(\\alpha_g(a))&=u_g\\beta_g(\\Phi(a))u_g^* &&(g\\in G,\\ a\\in A). \\end{align}","sourceOffset":937,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Szab\\'o--Wouters","label":"thm:absorption","text":"Let a countable discrete amenable group act on a separable, unital, simple, nuclear, \\(\\Zalg\\)-stable \\(\\mathrm C^*\\)-algebra with nonempty tracial-state space. If the action has equivariant uniform property Gamma, it is cocycle conjugate to its tensor product with the trivial action on \\(\\Zalg\\).","sourceOffset":7744,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:gamma","text":"The action \\(\\alpha\\) has equivariant uniform property Gamma if, for every integer \\(k\\geq2\\), there are pairwise orthogonal projections \\(p_1,\\ldots,p_k\\in(A^\\omega\\cap A')^{\\bar\\alpha}\\) with \\begin{equation} \\tau(ap_i)=\\frac{\\tau(a)}{k} \\qquad(a\\in A,\\ \\tau\\in\\T_\\omega(A),\\ 1\\leq i\\leq k). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":7187,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/291.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":51060,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Equivariant-Jiang-Su-Stability-for-Amenable-Actions-in-the-Unital-Stably-Finite-Case-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"291-02","slug":"Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026","familyId":"291","title":"Cuntz comparison and Jiang–Su absorption","abstract":"We prove that strict comparison in the extended-functional sense implies Jiang–Su absorption for separable simple nuclear non-elementary C∗-algebras. This resolves the corresponding implication of the Toms–Winter regularity problem, including nonunital algebras and allowing unbounded traces. More generally, every separable nuclear C∗-algebra whose Cuntz semigroup is almost unperforated and fully almost divisible absorbs the Jiang–Su algebra.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves equivariant Jiang–Su stability for every countable discrete amenable group action on a simple separable unital infinite-dimensional nuclear stably finite Jiang–Su-stable C∗-algebra, resolving this case of Szabó's conjecture without restrictions on trace dynamics. The family also proves the unital Toms–Winter conjecture, equating strict comparison, finite nuclear dimension and Jiang–Su stability in the simple separable unital infinite-dimensional nuclear setting.","pdfPath":"preprints/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026/build/sections/extended-traces.tex","preprints/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026/build/sections/rounding.tex","preprints/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026/build/sections/divisibility.tex","preprints/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026/build/sections/support-tools.tex","preprints/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026/build/sections/shrinking.tex","preprints/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026/build/sections/norm.tex","preprints/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026/build/sections/tracial.tex","preprints/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026/build/sections/localization.tex","preprints/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026/build/sections/comparison.tex","preprints/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026/build/sections/central.tex","preprints/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026/build/sections/absorption.tex","preprints/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026/build/sections/assembly.tex","preprints/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that strict comparison in the extended-functional sense implies Jiang--Su absorption for separable simple nuclear non-elementary $C^*$-algebras. This resolves the corresponding implication of the Toms--Winter regularity problem, including nonunital algebras and allowing unbounded traces. More generally, every separable nuclear $C^*$-algebra whose Cuntz semigroup is almost unperforated and fully almost divisible absorbs the Jiang--Su algebra.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":501},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison and the simple theorem","sourceOffset":1268},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The nonsimple absorption mechanism","sourceOffset":3946},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the main obstacles","sourceOffset":7499},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and organization","sourceOffset":15427},{"kind":"section","title":"Cuntz comparison and support calculus","sourceOffset":20023},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Order zero and the Cuntz semigroup","sourceOffset":21088},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Extended traces and rank","sourceOffset":26873},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The comparison convention","sourceOffset":29089},{"kind":"section","title":"Simple algebras and extended traces","sourceOffset":30776},{"kind":"section","title":"Trace transfer and spectral rounding","sourceOffset":34428},{"kind":"section","title":"Almost divisibility with a relative error","sourceOffset":38108}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Simple comparison and absorption","label":"ext:thm:main","text":"Let $A$ be a separable simple nuclear non-elementary $C^*$-algebra. If $A$ has strict comparison in the sense of Equation \\eqref{ext:eq:comparison}, then $A\\cong A\\otimes_{\\min}\\Z$.","sourceOffset":2626,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Absorption from condition \\textup{(C)}","label":"thm:condition-C","text":"Let $A$ be a nonzero separable nuclear $C^*$-algebra satisfying \\textup{(C)}. For every fixed free ultrafilter $\\omega$ on $\\mathbb N$, there is a unital $*$-homomorphism $\\Z\\to F_\\omega(A)$. Consequently $A\\cong A\\otimes_{\\min}\\Z$.","sourceOffset":5459,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"loc:relative-ideal","text":"For $b\\in A_+$ define $J_b\\subseteq A_\\omega$ as follows. An element $z=(z_n)$ belongs to $J_b$ if, for every $s>0$ and $\\eta>0$, there is $E\\in\\omega$ such that \\begin{equation} d_\\tau((|z_n|-s)_+)\\le\\eta d_\\tau(b) \\quad\\text{for every }n\\in E\\text{ and every }\\tau \\text{ with }d_\\tau(b)<\\infty. \\end{equation} For $d_\\tau(b)=0$ this means exact zero on the left. The set $E$ is common to the entire indicated trace family. It is enough to test positive rational $s,\\eta$.","sourceOffset":133076,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/291.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":220108,"sourceFileCount":16,"statementCount":17,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Cuntz-comparison-and-Jiang-Su-absorption-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"291-03","slug":"Nuclear-dimension-and-Jiang-Su-stability-without-elementary-subquotients-September-23-2026","familyId":"291","title":"Nuclear dimension and Jiang–Su stability without elementary subquotients","abstract":"For separable nuclear C∗-algebras with no nonzero elementary ideal subquotients, finite nuclear dimension is equivalent to Jiang–Su stability. More generally, $\\dim_{\\mathrm{nuc}}(A_0\\otimes\\mathcal Z)\\le1$ for every separable nuclear A0. This proves Robert and Tikuisis's Conjecture (C1) and the nuclear-dimension equivalence in the nonsimple Toms–Winter regularity question.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves equivariant Jiang–Su stability for every countable discrete amenable group action on a simple separable unital infinite-dimensional nuclear stably finite Jiang–Su-stable C∗-algebra, resolving this case of Szabó's conjecture without restrictions on trace dynamics. The family also proves the unital Toms–Winter conjecture, equating strict comparison, finite nuclear dimension and Jiang–Su stability in the simple separable unital infinite-dimensional nuclear setting.","pdfPath":"preprints/Nuclear-dimension-and-Jiang-Su-stability-without-elementary-subquotients-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Nuclear-dimension-and-Jiang-Su-stability-without-elementary-subquotients-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Nuclear-dimension-and-Jiang-Su-stability-without-elementary-subquotients-September-23-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/Nuclear-dimension-and-Jiang-Su-stability-without-elementary-subquotients-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Nuclear-dimension-and-Jiang-Su-stability-without-elementary-subquotients-September-23-2026/build/sections/01_introduction.tex","preprints/Nuclear-dimension-and-Jiang-Su-stability-without-elementary-subquotients-September-23-2026/build/figures/proof-overview.tex","preprints/Nuclear-dimension-and-Jiang-Su-stability-without-elementary-subquotients-September-23-2026/build/sections/02_preliminaries.tex","preprints/Nuclear-dimension-and-Jiang-Su-stability-without-elementary-subquotients-September-23-2026/build/sections/03_model.tex","preprints/Nuclear-dimension-and-Jiang-Su-stability-without-elementary-subquotients-September-23-2026/build/sections/04_traces.tex","preprints/Nuclear-dimension-and-Jiang-Su-stability-without-elementary-subquotients-September-23-2026/build/sections/05_commutant.tex","preprints/Nuclear-dimension-and-Jiang-Su-stability-without-elementary-subquotients-September-23-2026/build/sections/06_endgame.tex","preprints/Nuclear-dimension-and-Jiang-Su-stability-without-elementary-subquotients-September-23-2026/build/sections/07_gaussian.tex","preprints/Nuclear-dimension-and-Jiang-Su-stability-without-elementary-subquotients-September-23-2026/build/sections/08_tensor.tex","preprints/Nuclear-dimension-and-Jiang-Su-stability-without-elementary-subquotients-September-23-2026/build/sections/09_central.tex","preprints/Nuclear-dimension-and-Jiang-Su-stability-without-elementary-subquotients-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Nuclear-dimension-and-Jiang-Su-stability-without-elementary-subquotients-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For separable nuclear $C^*$-algebras with no nonzero elementary ideal subquotients, finite nuclear dimension is equivalent to Jiang--Su stability. More generally, $\\dim_{\\mathrm{nuc}}(A_0\\otimes\\mathcal Z)\\le1$ for every separable nuclear $A_0$. This proves Robert and Tikuisis's Conjecture (C1) and the nuclear-dimension equivalence in the nonsimple Toms--Winter regularity question.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":430},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and the two obstacles","sourceOffset":5380},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":11649},{"kind":"section","title":"Preliminaries","sourceOffset":16889},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Order zero and finite-dimensional approximation","sourceOffset":18132},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison and extended traces","sourceOffset":21315},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ultraproducts and bounded witnesses","sourceOffset":28451},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Domination and unitary equivalence","sourceOffset":31606},{"kind":"section","title":"Matrix models with local trace control","sourceOffset":33767},{"kind":"section","title":"The common part and localized trace comparison","sourceOffset":57234},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Order-zero maps into the Jiang--Su algebra","sourceOffset":57695},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model and its common part","sourceOffset":60832}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Nonsimple nuclear regularity","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $A$ be a separable nuclear complex $C^*$-algebra with no nonzero elementary ideal subquotients. The following conditions are equivalent: \\begin{enumerate} \\item $\\nucdim(A)<\\infty$; \\item $A\\cong A\\otimes\\Z$; \\item $\\nucdim(A)\\le1$. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":1703,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Sharp bound after Jiang--Su absorption","label":"intro:main","text":"For every separable nuclear complex $C^*$-algebra $A_0$, \\[ \\nucdim(A_0\\otimes\\Z)\\le1. \\]","sourceOffset":3532,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Winter--Zacharias {\\cite[Definition 2.1","label":"prelim:nuclear-dimension","text":"{WZNuclear}}] For an integer $d\\ge0$, a $C^*$-algebra $D$ has nuclear dimension at most $d$, written $\\nucdim(D)\\le d$, if for every finite $\\mathcal F\\subset D$ and $\\varepsilon>0$ there are finite-dimensional algebras $E_0,\\ldots,E_d$, a c.p.c. map $\\psi:D\\to\\bigoplus_{j=0}^dE_j$, and c.p.c. order-zero maps $\\varphi_j:E_j\\to D$ such that \\[ \\left\\|\\sum_{j=0}^d\\varphi_j\\psi_j(a)-a\\right\\|<\\varepsilon \\qquad(a\\in\\mathcal F). \\] The $d+1$ summands are called colours. The sum of the return maps need not be contractive; each coloured return map must be contractive.","sourceOffset":19352,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/291.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":139703,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":17,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Nuclear-dimension-and-Jiang-Su-stability-without-elementary-subquotients-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"291-04","slug":"Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026","familyId":"291","title":"Tracial projection methods and uniform property Gamma","abstract":"For a simple, separable, unital, infinite-dimensional, nuclear, stably finite C∗-algebra with traces, real rank zero of the uniform tracial ultrapower of its uniform tracial completion implies uniform property Γ. This answers Problem XXI of Schafhauser, Tikuisis and White affirmatively. Independently, strict comparison implies Jiang–Su absorption for simple, separable, unital, infinite-dimensional nuclear algebras, resolving the unital Toms–Winter conjecture. Under comparison tested on traces of finite target rank, we also obtain uniform property Γ and absorption for simple, separable, nuclear, stably projectionless algebras whose densely finite traces are all bounded and have a nonempty compact normalized base.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves equivariant Jiang–Su stability for every countable discrete amenable group action on a simple separable unital infinite-dimensional nuclear stably finite Jiang–Su-stable C∗-algebra, resolving this case of Szabó's conjecture without restrictions on trace dynamics. The family also proves the unital Toms–Winter conjecture, equating strict comparison, finite nuclear dimension and Jiang–Su stability in the simple separable unital infinite-dimensional nuclear setting.","pdfPath":"preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/build/sections/01_introduction.tex","preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/build/figures/routes.tex","preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/build/sections/02_rr_preliminaries.tex","preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/build/sections/03_rr_projections.tex","preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/build/sections/04_rr_weighted.tex","preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/build/sections/05_rr_packing.tex","preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/build/sections/06_rr_completion.tex","preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/build/sections/07_unital_preliminaries.tex","preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/build/sections/08_unital_ranks.tex","preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/build/sections/09_unital_centering.tex","preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/build/sections/10_unital_averaging.tex","preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/build/sections/11_unital_gamma.tex","preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/build/sections/12_nonunital_framework.tex","preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/build/sections/13_nonunital_selection.tex","preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/build/sections/14_nonunital_models.tex","preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/build/sections/16_nonunital_amplification.tex","preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/build/sections/17_nonunital_absorption.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For a simple, separable, unital, infinite-dimensional, nuclear, stably finite $C^*$-algebra with traces, real rank zero of the uniform tracial ultrapower of its uniform tracial completion implies uniform property $\\Gamma$. This answers Problem XXI of Schafhauser, Tikuisis and White affirmatively. Independently, strict comparison implies Jiang--Su absorption for simple, separable, unital, infinite-dimensional nuclear algebras, resolving the unital Toms--Winter conjecture. Under comparison tested on traces of finite target rank, we also obtain uniform property $\\Gamma$ and absorption for simple, separable, nuclear, stably projectionless algebras whose densely finite traces are all bounded and have a nonempty compact normalized base.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":803},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Strict comparison and Jiang--Su absorption","sourceOffset":9362},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The three constructions and their interfaces","sourceOffset":14335},{"kind":"section","title":"Notation and the tracial completion","sourceOffset":19144},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The specified completion and limit traces","sourceOffset":21164},{"kind":"section","title":"Projection tools","sourceOffset":24077},{"kind":"section","title":"Nuclear averaging and weighted matrix blocks","sourceOffset":39536},{"kind":"section","title":"Sampling and orthogonal packing","sourceOffset":50521},{"kind":"section","title":"The projection in the prescribed ultrapower","sourceOffset":63233},{"kind":"section","title":"Comparison and the tracial ultrapower","sourceOffset":69333},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Traces and comparison conventions","sourceOffset":70252},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The uniform tracial ultrapower","sourceOffset":75258}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Real rank zero and uniform property $\\Gamma$","label":"r:thm:main","text":"Let $A$ be a unital, separable, simple, infinite-dimensional, nuclear, stably finite $C^*$-algebra with $T(A)\\ne\\varnothing$, and let $B=\\overline A^{T(A)}$. Fix any free ultrafilter $\\omega$ on $\\N$. If $B^\\omega$ has real rank zero in its quotient $C^*$-norm, then there is a projection $p\\in B^\\omega\\cap B'$ such that \\[ \\lambda(px)=\\tfrac12\\lambda(x) \\qquad(x\\in B,\\ \\lambda\\in\\Lambda_\\omega). \\] In particular, $A$ has uniform property $\\Gamma$.","sourceOffset":4001,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"The direct unital comparison route","label":"u:thm:main","text":"Let $A$ be a simple, separable, unital, infinite-dimensional nuclear $C^*$-algebra with strict comparison in the preceding sense. Then $A\\cong A\\otimes\\Z$. When $T(A)\\ne\\varnothing$, the proof constructs uniform property $\\Gamma$ directly.","sourceOffset":11377,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Strict comparison","label":"u:def:strict-comparison","text":"The algebra $A$ has strict comparison if, whenever $a,b\\in(A\\otimes_{\\min}\\K)_+$ and $b\\ne0$, the inequalities \\[ d_\\tau(a)<d_\\tau(b)\\qquad(\\tau\\in T(A)) \\] imply $a\\precsim b$. When $T(A)=\\varnothing$, this requires $a\\precsim b$ for every positive $a$ and every nonzero positive $b$ in the stabilization.","sourceOffset":71126,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Uniform property $\\Gamma$","label":"u:def:uniform-gamma","text":"For a simple separable unital $A$ with $T(A)\\ne\\varnothing$, uniform property $\\Gamma$ means that for every $m\\ge1$ there are projections $p_1,\\ldots,p_m\\in D\\cap A'$ satisfying \\[ \\sum_{i=1}^m p_i=1, \\qquad \\tau(ap_i)=\\frac{1}{m}\\tau(a) \\quad(a\\in A,\\ \\tau\\in\\Lambda,\\ 1\\le i\\le m). \\] This is \\cite[Definition 2.1 and Equation (2.1)]{CETW}, in the uniform tracial ultrapower notation used here.","sourceOffset":82030,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/291.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves that real rank zero of the uniform tracial ultrapower of the uniform tracial completion implies uniform property $\\Gamma$ for a simple separable unital infinite-dimensional nuclear stably finite $C^*$-algebra with traces. The conclusion holds at every specified free ultrafilter under the corresponding real-rank-zero hypothesis.\n\nThe linked comparison results also prove Jiang–Su absorption and uniform property $\\Gamma$ for simple separable unital infinite-dimensional nuclear algebras with strict comparison, and for the stated stably projectionless nuclear algebras with bounded densely finite traces, a nonempty compact normalized trace base, and the prescribed finite-target-rank comparison condition.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/291.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":177990,"sourceFileCount":18,"statementCount":25,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Tracial-projection-methods-and-uniform-property-Gamma-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"292-01","slug":"An-explicit-obstruction-to-nuclear-norm-ultrapower-embeddings-September-23-2026","familyId":"292","title":"An explicit obstruction to nuclear norm-ultrapower embeddings","abstract":"We give a negative answer to Kirchberg's norm-ultrapower embedding problem. We construct an explicit separable unital full group C∗-algebra that admits no unital embedding into Bω, for any nonzero unital nuclear C∗-algebra B and any free ultrafilter ω on ℕ. In particular, it does not embed unitally into $\\mathcal O_2^\\omega$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs an explicit separable unital full group C∗-algebra that cannot embed unitally into the norm ultrapower of any fixed nonzero unital nuclear C∗-algebra, for any free ultrafilter on the natural numbers. Taking the target to be $\\mathcal O_2$ answers Kirchberg's norm-ultrapower embedding problem negatively.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-explicit-obstruction-to-nuclear-norm-ultrapower-embeddings-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-explicit-obstruction-to-nuclear-norm-ultrapower-embeddings-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-explicit-obstruction-to-nuclear-norm-ultrapower-embeddings-September-23-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/An-explicit-obstruction-to-nuclear-norm-ultrapower-embeddings-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/An-explicit-obstruction-to-nuclear-norm-ultrapower-embeddings-September-23-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/An-explicit-obstruction-to-nuclear-norm-ultrapower-embeddings-September-23-2026/build/group-example.tex","preprints/An-explicit-obstruction-to-nuclear-norm-ultrapower-embeddings-September-23-2026/build/spectral-preliminaries.tex","preprints/An-explicit-obstruction-to-nuclear-norm-ultrapower-embeddings-September-23-2026/build/nuclear-finiteness.tex","preprints/An-explicit-obstruction-to-nuclear-norm-ultrapower-embeddings-September-23-2026/build/ultrapower-contradiction.tex","preprints/An-explicit-obstruction-to-nuclear-norm-ultrapower-embeddings-September-23-2026/build/figures/finite-chain.tex","preprints/An-explicit-obstruction-to-nuclear-norm-ultrapower-embeddings-September-23-2026/build/consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-explicit-obstruction-to-nuclear-norm-ultrapower-embeddings-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a negative answer to Kirchberg's norm-ultrapower embedding problem. We construct an explicit separable unital full group $C^*$-algebra that admits no unital embedding into $B^\\omega$, for any nonzero unital nuclear $C^*$-algebra $B$ and any free ultrafilter $\\omega$ on $\\mathbb N$. In particular, it does not embed unitally into $\\mathcal O_2^\\omega$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The embedding problem","sourceOffset":423},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Previous work and the new obstruction","sourceOffset":3217},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof outline","sourceOffset":5985},{"kind":"section","title":"The group and a finite-dimensional representation","sourceOffset":7824},{"kind":"section","title":"A spectral test for representation matching","sourceOffset":11037},{"kind":"section","title":"Finiteness of approximate representation matches","sourceOffset":14198},{"kind":"section","title":"From an ultrapower embedding to a finite contradiction","sourceOffset":23747},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Unitary lifts and a gap for varying matrix dimensions","sourceOffset":24465},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The initial representation occurs","sourceOffset":27708},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A predecessor for every representation at one coordinate","sourceOffset":29747},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite chains force periodicity","sourceOffset":35864},{"kind":"section","title":"Consequences for existential closure and nuclear approximation","sourceOffset":40204}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\[ G=\\Z[1/2]^3\\rtimes\\bigl(\\SL_3(\\Z)\\times\\Z\\bigr), \\qquad (M,k)\\cdot v=2^kMv, \\] and let $A=C^*(G)$ be its full group $C^*$-algebra. Then $A$ is separable and unital. For every nonzero unital nuclear $C^*$-algebra $B$ and every free ultrafilter $\\omega$ on $\\N$, there is no unital embedding \\[ A\\longrightarrow B^\\omega. \\] In particular, $A$ does not embed unitally into $\\Otwo^\\omega$.","sourceOffset":2219,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Consequences of negative KEP","label":"cor:kep-consequences","text":"Neither $\\Otwo$ nor any exact or nuclear unital $C^*$-algebra is existentially closed among unital $C^*$-algebra extensions. For every free ultrafilter $\\omega$ on $\\N$, there are a separable unital $C^*$-algebra $D$ and $\\alpha\\in\\operatorname{Aut}(D)$ such that $D$ embeds unitally into $\\Otwo^\\omega$ but $D\\rtimes_\\alpha\\Z$ does not embed unitally there. Some satisfiable finite condition $p(x)=\\{\\varphi_j(x)<r_j:1\\le j\\le s\\}$ in the unital $C^*$-algebra language, with $x=(x_1,\\ldots,x_m)$, quantifier-free $\\varphi_j$ and $r_j>0$, lacks good nuclear witnesses: for some $\\varepsilon_0>0$, every realization $a=(a_i)_{i=1}^m$ of $p$ in every unital $C^*$-algebra $E$ satisfies $\\max_{1\\le i\\le m}\\|\\psi\\phi(a_i)-a_i\\|\\ge\\varepsilon_0$ for every finite-dimensional $C^*$-algebra $F$ and all completely positive contractive maps $\\phi:E\\to F$, $\\psi:F\\to E$, not necessarily unital.","sourceOffset":41665,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/292.md","scopeText":"Kirchberg's norm-ultrapower embedding problem asks whether separable $C^*$-algebras embed into norm ultrapowers of nuclear algebras. The formalization constructs an explicit separable unital full group $C^*$-algebra that has no unital embedding into $B^\\omega$ for any nonzero unital nuclear $C^*$-algebra $B$ and any free ultrafilter $\\omega$ on $\\mathbb N$. In particular, it does not embed unitally into an ultrapower of the Cuntz algebra $\\mathcal O_2$.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/292.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":43220,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-explicit-obstruction-to-nuclear-norm-ultrapower-embeddings-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"293-01","slug":"Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026","familyId":"293","title":"Invariant-projection counterexamples for every irrational rotation","abstract":"For every irrational angle, we construct a continuous nonnegative circle weight with exactly one zero and logarithmic integral $-\\infty$ whose weighted rotation has no nontrivial invariant projection in the associated hyperfinite type II1 factor. This answers negatively the question of Zhu, Fang, and Shi, with the angle prescribed in advance. The resulting operator is nonzero and norm-quasinilpotent.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a nonzero norm-quasinilpotent operator on every infinite-dimensional separable complex Hilbert space with no nonzero proper closed subspace invariant under every commuting operator. The construction also gives operators with no nontrivial invariant projection in the hyperfinite type II1 factor.","pdfPath":"preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/build/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/build/01-parameters.tex","preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/build/02-fields.tex","preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/build/03-couplings.tex","preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/build/04-obstruction.tex","preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/build/figures/product-endpoint-conditioning.tex","preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/build/05-continuity.tex","preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/build/appendix-product.tex","preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/build/product-scales.tex","preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/build/product-sampling.tex","preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/build/product-operator.tex","preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/build/product-couplings.tex","preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/build/product-obstruction.tex","preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/build/product-structure.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every irrational angle, we construct a continuous nonnegative circle weight with exactly one zero and logarithmic integral $-\\infty$ whose weighted rotation has no nontrivial invariant projection in the associated hyperfinite type $\\mathrm{II}_1$ factor. This answers negatively the question of Zhu, Fang, and Shi, with the angle prescribed in advance. The resulting operator is nonzero and norm-quasinilpotent.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":461},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Hyperinvariant subspaces and commutants","sourceOffset":2517},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work","sourceOffset":6207},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":8513},{"kind":"section","title":"Sampling at a prescribed irrational angle","sourceOffset":12267},{"kind":"section","title":"Projection fields and transfer between endpoints","sourceOffset":31680},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fields determined by scalar multipliers","sourceOffset":32178},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The tracial representation","sourceOffset":34607},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A compact-set transfer lemma","sourceOffset":38726},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The measurable weight and its cocycles","sourceOffset":41805},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From invariant projections to endpoint identities","sourceOffset":44153},{"kind":"section","title":"Ordered pairs from independent digits","sourceOffset":48253}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every irrational $\\theta\\in(0,1)$ there exists a continuous function $f:\\T\\to[0,1]$ such that \\[ f^{-1}(\\{0\\})=\\{1\\},\\qquad \\int_\\T\\log f\\,dm=-\\infty, \\] and every projection $p\\in R_\\theta$ satisfying $(1-p)Uf(V)p=0$ is either $0$ or $1$.","sourceOffset":1824,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:transitive-commutant","text":"Every separable infinite-dimensional complex Hilbert space carries a nonzero norm-quasinilpotent operator with no nontrivial hyperinvariant subspace. Its commutant is a proper strongly closed transitive unital algebra.","sourceOffset":4579,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/293.md","scopeText":"For every irrational rotation angle $\\theta\\in(0,1)$, the formalization constructs a continuous nonnegative circle weight with a single zero and logarithmic integral $-\\infty$ whose weighted rotation is nonzero and quasinilpotent. In its associated hyperfinite type $\\mathrm{II}_1$ factor, the operator has no nontrivial invariant projection: $(1-p)Tp=0$ forces $p=0$ or $p=1$. The linked statements also include an earlier existence construction, a product realization, and the selected product model's Brown measure $\\delta_0$.\n\nThe formalization for the companion paper [Backward intertwiners and a transitive commutant](../../preprints/Backward-intertwiners-and-a-transitive-commutant-September-27-2026/paper.pdf) gives, on every separable infinite-dimensional complex Hilbert space, a nonzero qu","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/293.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":152095,"sourceFileCount":16,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Invariant-projection-counterexamples-for-every-irrational-rotation-September-27-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"293-02","slug":"Backward-intertwiners-and-a-transitive-commutant-September-27-2026","familyId":"293","title":"Backward intertwiners and a transitive commutant","abstract":"We give a negative answer to the hyperinvariant-subspace problem by constructing, on every infinite-dimensional separable complex Hilbert space, a nonzero bounded norm-quasinilpotent operator with no nonzero proper closed hyperinvariant subspace. Its commutant is a proper strongly closed unital transitive complex operator algebra.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a nonzero norm-quasinilpotent operator on every infinite-dimensional separable complex Hilbert space with no nonzero proper closed subspace invariant under every commuting operator. The construction also gives operators with no nontrivial invariant projection in the hyperfinite type II1 factor.","pdfPath":"preprints/Backward-intertwiners-and-a-transitive-commutant-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Backward-intertwiners-and-a-transitive-commutant-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Backward-intertwiners-and-a-transitive-commutant-September-27-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/Backward-intertwiners-and-a-transitive-commutant-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Backward-intertwiners-and-a-transitive-commutant-September-27-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","preprints/Backward-intertwiners-and-a-transitive-commutant-September-27-2026/build/companion-refs.tex","preprints/Backward-intertwiners-and-a-transitive-commutant-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Backward-intertwiners-and-a-transitive-commutant-September-27-2026/build/sections/criterion.tex","preprints/Backward-intertwiners-and-a-transitive-commutant-September-27-2026/build/sections/domar-proof.tex","preprints/Backward-intertwiners-and-a-transitive-commutant-September-27-2026/build/sections/weights.tex","preprints/Backward-intertwiners-and-a-transitive-commutant-September-27-2026/build/sections/intertwiner.tex","preprints/Backward-intertwiners-and-a-transitive-commutant-September-27-2026/build/figures/commuting-square.tex","preprints/Backward-intertwiners-and-a-transitive-commutant-September-27-2026/build/sections/subspaces.tex","preprints/Backward-intertwiners-and-a-transitive-commutant-September-27-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Backward-intertwiners-and-a-transitive-commutant-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a negative answer to the hyperinvariant-subspace problem by constructing, on every infinite-dimensional separable complex Hilbert space, a nonzero bounded norm-quasinilpotent operator with no nonzero proper closed hyperinvariant subspace. Its commutant is a proper strongly closed unital transitive complex operator algebra.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":395},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction theorem","sourceOffset":1447},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and relation to the companion","sourceOffset":3846},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview and elementary inputs","sourceOffset":6716},{"kind":"section","title":"The quadratic weighted-translation criterion","sourceOffset":9743},{"kind":"section","title":"The 2-adic weights","sourceOffset":21071},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The base transformation","sourceOffset":21335},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The weights","sourceOffset":22373},{"kind":"section","title":"A bounded backward intertwiner","sourceOffset":26930},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two-ended drift","sourceOffset":27306},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Solving and normalizing the recurrence","sourceOffset":28721},{"kind":"section","title":"All common invariant subspaces","sourceOffset":33812}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:direct-model","text":"There are measurable positive weights $\\beta_j:X\\to(0,\\infty)$, $j\\in\\Z$, and a contraction $V\\in\\B(H_0)$ with the following properties. The shifts \\[ S_xe_j=\\beta_j(x)e_{j+1},\\qquad (Sf)(x)=S_xf(x), \\] define a bounded operator $S$ on $H_0$, and: \\begin{enumerate} \\item For every $x\\in X$, the closed invariant subspaces of $S_x$ are exactly $\\Ktail{h}$, $h\\in\\Z\\cup\\{-\\infty,+\\infty\\}$. \\item The operator $S$ is nonzero, $\\|S\\|=e^{-10}$, and for every integer $n\\ge1$, \\begin{equation} \\|S^n\\|\\le \\exp\\left(-10\\left\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)^2}{4}\\right\\rfloor\\right). \\end{equation} \\item The operator $V$ commutes with $S$ and has the form \\[ (Vf)(\\theta x)=V_xf(x),\\qquad V_xe_j=b_j(x)e_{j-1}, \\] where $0\\le b_j(x)\\le1$ are measurable and are positive on a common conull set. On that set, \\[ \\overline{V_x\\Ktail{h}}=\\Ktail{h-1}\\quad(h\\in\\Z), \\qquad \\overline{V_xK}=K. \\] \\item The unital complex algebra generated by $S$, $V$, and all scalar multipliers $M_g$ is transitive. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":1967,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:direct-algebra","text":"Every infinite-dimensional separable complex Hilbert space admits a nonzero bounded norm-quasinilpotent operator with no nonzero proper closed hyperinvariant subspace. Its commutant is a proper strongly closed unital transitive complex operator algebra.","sourceOffset":3290,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/293.md","scopeText":"For every irrational rotation angle $\\theta\\in(0,1)$, the formalization constructs a continuous nonnegative circle weight with a single zero and logarithmic integral $-\\infty$ whose weighted rotation is nonzero and quasinilpotent. In its associated hyperfinite type $\\mathrm{II}_1$ factor, the operator has no nontrivial invariant projection: $(1-p)Tp=0$ forces $p=0$ or $p=1$. The linked statements also include an earlier existence construction, a product realization, and the selected product model's Brown measure $\\delta_0$.\n\nThe formalization for the companion paper [Backward intertwiners and a transitive commutant](../../preprints/Backward-intertwiners-and-a-transitive-commutant-September-27-2026/paper.pdf) gives, on every separable infinite-dimensional complex Hilbert space, a nonzero qu","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/293.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":44706,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Backward-intertwiners-and-a-transitive-commutant-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Backward-intertwiners-and-a-transitive-commutant-September-27-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"294-01","slug":"A-counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-quasitrace-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"294","title":"A counterexample to Kaplansky's quasitrace conjecture and failure of tensor-product stable finiteness","abstract":"We refute Kaplansky's quasitrace conjecture by constructing a separable unital complex C∗-algebra that admits normalized 2-quasitraces but has no tracial state. As a consequence, we show that the minimal tensor product of two unital simple stably finite complex C∗-algebras can be properly infinite, even when one factor is the reduced free-group algebra $C_r^*(\\mathbb F_2)$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves Kaplansky's quasitrace conjecture by constructing a separable unital complex C∗-algebra admitting normalized 2-quasitraces, all of which are nonadditive. As a consequence, two unital simple stably finite C∗-algebras can have a properly infinite minimal tensor product, with one factor $C_r^*(\\mathbb F_2)$.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-quasitrace-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-quasitrace-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-quasitrace-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/A-counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-quasitrace-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-quasitrace-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-quasitrace-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/02-preliminaries.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-quasitrace-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/03-scaling.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-quasitrace-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/04-bundles.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-quasitrace-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/05-algebra.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-quasitrace-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/finite-test.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-quasitrace-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/06-conclusion.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-quasitrace-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/07-consequences.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-quasitrace-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-quasitrace-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We refute Kaplansky's quasitrace conjecture by constructing a separable unital complex \\(C^*\\)-algebra that admits normalized \\(2\\)-quasitraces but has no tracial state. As a consequence, we show that the minimal tensor product of two unital simple stably finite complex \\(C^*\\)-algebras can be properly infinite, even when one factor is the reduced free-group algebra \\(C_r^*(\\mathbb F_2)\\).","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":439},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction","sourceOffset":6953},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":10914},{"kind":"section","title":"Quasitraces and proper infiniteness","sourceOffset":11446},{"kind":"section","title":"Two bundle-map lemmas","sourceOffset":14130},{"kind":"section","title":"Bundle tests of bounded degree","sourceOffset":23683},{"kind":"section","title":"The algebra and a uniform additivity defect","sourceOffset":33615},{"kind":"section","title":"Tensor products and stable finiteness","sourceOffset":44176}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist a separable unital complex \\(C^*\\)-algebra \\(A\\), admitting a normalized \\(2\\)-quasitrace, and positive contractions \\(a,b\\in A\\) such that every normalized \\(2\\)-quasitrace \\(\\tau\\) on \\(A\\) satisfies \\[ \\tau(a+b)-\\tau(a)-\\tau(b)\\ge\\frac1{144}. \\]","sourceOffset":4306,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Tensor products and stable finiteness","label":"cor:stable-finiteness","text":"\\leavevmode \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item There are two unital simple stably finite complex \\(C^*\\)-algebras whose minimal tensor product is properly infinite. One factor can be taken to be \\(C_r^*(\\mathbb F_2)\\). \\item There is a separable unital complex \\(C^*\\)-algebra \\(B\\) admitting a normalized \\(2\\)-quasitrace such that \\(B\\otimes_{\\min}C_r^*(\\mathbb F_2)\\) is properly infinite and admits no normalized \\(2\\)-quasitrace. \\item There is a separable unital stably finite complex \\(C^*\\)-algebra with no tracial state. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":5621,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/294.md","scopeText":"Kaplansky's quasitrace conjecture predicts that every $2$-quasitrace on a $C^*$-algebra is a trace. The formalization constructs a separable $C^*$-algebra with normalized $2$-quasitraces and fixed positive contractions $a,b$ for which every such quasitrace satisfies $\\mathrm{Re}(\\tau(a+b)-\\tau(a)-\\tau(b))\\ge1/144$. Thus none is additive on this pair.\n\nThe formalization also gives simple stably finite $C^*$-algebras whose spatial tensor product is properly infinite, a separable stably finite algebra with no tracial state, and a tensor-product example in which normalized $2$-quasitraces are lost. These are the stable-finiteness consequences named in the paper's title.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/294.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":51379,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-quasitrace-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"295-01","slug":"Vanishing-of-higher-bounded-Hochschild-cohomology-September-23-2026","familyId":"295","title":"Vanishing of higher bounded Hochschild cohomology","abstract":"We prove that every bounded Hochschild cocycle of degree at least two on a complex von Neumann algebra, with values in the algebra itself, has a bounded primitive. Together with the established degree-one inner-derivation theorem, this resolves the Kadison–Ringrose cohomology conjecture positively.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every bounded Hochschild cocycle of degree at least two on a complex von Neumann algebra, with coefficients in the algebra itself, has a bounded primitive. Equivalently, all higher bounded Hochschild cohomology groups vanish, resolving the Kadison–Ringrose conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/Vanishing-of-higher-bounded-Hochschild-cohomology-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Vanishing-of-higher-bounded-Hochschild-cohomology-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Vanishing-of-higher-bounded-Hochschild-cohomology-September-23-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/Vanishing-of-higher-bounded-Hochschild-cohomology-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Vanishing-of-higher-bounded-Hochschild-cohomology-September-23-2026/build/supporting/typography-mappings.tex","preprints/Vanishing-of-higher-bounded-Hochschild-cohomology-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Vanishing-of-higher-bounded-Hochschild-cohomology-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-walk.tex","preprints/Vanishing-of-higher-bounded-Hochschild-cohomology-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-liouville.tex","preprints/Vanishing-of-higher-bounded-Hochschild-cohomology-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-rigidity.tex","preprints/Vanishing-of-higher-bounded-Hochschild-cohomology-September-23-2026/build/figures/proof-map.tex","preprints/Vanishing-of-higher-bounded-Hochschild-cohomology-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-cohomology.tex","preprints/Vanishing-of-higher-bounded-Hochschild-cohomology-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Vanishing-of-higher-bounded-Hochschild-cohomology-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every bounded Hochschild cocycle of degree at least two on a complex von Neumann algebra, with values in the algebra itself, has a bounded primitive. Together with the established degree-one inner-derivation theorem, this resolves the Kadison--Ringrose cohomology conjecture positively.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":347},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work","sourceOffset":2580},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The new ingredients","sourceOffset":5107},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and classical reductions","sourceOffset":8825},{"kind":"section","title":"A random walk with asymptotically free products","sourceOffset":10801},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite requirements from ultrapower freeness","sourceOffset":13447},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One measure satisfying all moment tests","sourceOffset":22768},{"kind":"section","title":"A Liouville theorem for the walk","sourceOffset":28443},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Continuity and tracial ultrapowers","sourceOffset":29135},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The harmonic map and its martingale","sourceOffset":32332},{"kind":"section","title":"Rigidity of the first-letter identities","sourceOffset":42676},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Norm control for free words","sourceOffset":44600}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(M\\) be a complex von Neumann algebra and let \\(k\\geq2\\). Every \\(f\\in C_b^k(M,M)\\) satisfying \\(df=0\\) has the form \\(f=dg\\) for some \\(g\\in C_b^{k-1}(M,M)\\). Consequently \\(H_b^k(M,M)=0\\).","sourceOffset":2204,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Classical reductions","label":"prop:classical","text":"Let \\(M\\) be a von Neumann algebra and \\(k\\geq2\\). \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item If \\(f\\in C_b^k(M,M)\\) is a cocycle, there are a separately normal bounded cocycle \\(f_0\\) and a bounded \\((k-1)\\)-cochain \\(b\\) such that \\(f=f_0+db\\). \\item If \\(M\\) has no type \\(\\mathrm{II}_1\\) central summand, then \\(H_b^k(M,M)=0\\). \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":9525,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:ball-continuity","text":"Let $(N,\\rho)$ be a finite von Neumann algebra with a faithful normal tracial state. A linear map $R:N\\to N$ is bounded-ball $2$-continuous if \\begin{equation} \\sup_j\\norm{x_j}<\\infty,\\qquad \\norm{x_j}_2\\longrightarrow0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\norm{R(x_j)}_2\\longrightarrow0. \\end{equation} Here and below $\\norm{x}_2=\\rho(x^*x)^{1/2}$ when the ambient algebra is $(N,\\rho)$.","sourceOffset":29184,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/295.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves vanishing of bounded Hochschild cohomology in every degree at least two for a complex von Neumann algebra with coefficients in itself. Every bounded multilinear cocycle of such a degree is the Hochschild differential of a bounded multilinear cochain one degree lower. No separability or type restriction is imposed. The degree-one inner-derivation theorem is outside this selected statement.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/295.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":93970,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Vanishing-of-higher-bounded-Hochschild-cohomology-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Vanishing-of-higher-bounded-Hochschild-cohomology-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"296-01","slug":"Relative-generation-and-the-generator-problem-for-finite-factors-September-23-2026","familyId":"296","title":"Relative generation and the generator problem for finite factors","abstract":"We prove that every type II1 factor with separable predual is generated by one operator, equivalently by two self-adjoint operators. Combined with the established direct-integral reduction to this case, this gives an affirmative solution of the generator problem for von Neumann algebras with separable predual.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every type II1 factor with separable predual is generated by a single operator, equivalently by two self-adjoint operators, resolving the generator problem. More strongly, for every irreducible inclusion $P\\subset M$ of such factors, the unitaries u with $M=W^*(P,u)$ form a dense Gδ subset in the trace 2-norm topology.","pdfPath":"preprints/Relative-generation-and-the-generator-problem-for-finite-factors-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Relative-generation-and-the-generator-problem-for-finite-factors-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Relative-generation-and-the-generator-problem-for-finite-factors-September-23-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/Relative-generation-and-the-generator-problem-for-finite-factors-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Relative-generation-and-the-generator-problem-for-finite-factors-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Relative-generation-and-the-generator-problem-for-finite-factors-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Relative-generation-and-the-generator-problem-for-finite-factors-September-23-2026/build/sections/independence.tex","preprints/Relative-generation-and-the-generator-problem-for-finite-factors-September-23-2026/build/sections/perturbation.tex","preprints/Relative-generation-and-the-generator-problem-for-finite-factors-September-23-2026/build/sections/generation.tex","preprints/Relative-generation-and-the-generator-problem-for-finite-factors-September-23-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Relative-generation-and-the-generator-problem-for-finite-factors-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every type $\\mathrm{II}_1$ factor with separable predual is generated by one operator, equivalently by two self-adjoint operators. Combined with the established direct-integral reduction to this case, this gives an affirmative solution of the generator problem for von Neumann algebras with separable predual.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":369},{"kind":"section","title":"Free Haar unitaries in an ultrapower","sourceOffset":8932},{"kind":"section","title":"A perturbation with uniform correlation","sourceOffset":19329},{"kind":"section","title":"Relative generation by continuity","sourceOffset":32848},{"kind":"section","title":"Two self-adjoint generators","sourceOffset":40535},{"kind":"section","title":"Consequences for generation and free entropy","sourceOffset":46541},{"kind":"subsection","title":"General von Neumann algebras and generator invariants","sourceOffset":46826},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Dependence of free entropy dimensions on generators","sourceOffset":48360}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Every type $\\mathrm{II}_1$ factor $M$ with separable predual is generated by two self-adjoint elements. Equivalently, there is an $x\\in M$ such that $M=W^*(x)$.","sourceOffset":1530,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:relative-generation","text":"Let $P\\subset M$ be an irreducible inclusion of type $\\mathrm{II}_1$ factors, with $M$ of separable predual. The set \\[ \\{u\\in\\mathcal U(M):W^*(P,u)=M\\} \\] is a dense $G_\\delta$ subset of the unitary group $\\mathcal U(M)$ for the trace $2$-norm topology.","sourceOffset":1212,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/296.md","scopeText":"The formalization answers the generator problem affirmatively for type $\\mathrm{II}_1$ factors with separable predual: one bounded operator generates the factor under adjoints and weak-operator closure. It imposes no separability assumption on the representing Hilbert space or on the operator-norm topology.\n\nIt also proves the paper's relative-generation result. For an irreducible inclusion of type $\\mathrm{II}_1$ factors with separable predual for the larger factor, the unitaries that generate the larger factor together with the smaller one form a dense $G_\\delta$ set in the normalized trace two-norm topology. The normalized faithful normal trace is included in the assertion.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/296.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":50528,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Relative-generation-and-the-generator-problem-for-finite-factors-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"297-01","slug":"A-counterexample-to-Naimarks-problem-in-ZFC-September-24-2026","familyId":"297","title":"A counterexample to Naimark's problem in ZFC","abstract":"We construct in ZFC a unital infinite-dimensional simple complex C∗-algebra with a faithful tracial state whose nonzero irreducible representations are all unitarily equivalent. This gives a negative answer to Naimark's problem without additional set-theoretic assumptions.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Gives an alternative to [Tanaka's ZFC construction](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.26930v1) of a unital infinite-dimensional simple complex C∗-algebra with a faithful tracial state and exactly one nonzero irreducible representation up to unitary equivalence. Thus the unrestricted compact-operator characterization fails without additional set-theoretic assumptions; the counterexample is nonseparable.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-counterexample-to-Naimarks-problem-in-ZFC-September-24-2026/naimark-counterexample-zfc.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-counterexample-to-Naimarks-problem-in-ZFC-September-24-2026/naimark-counterexample-zfc.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-counterexample-to-Naimarks-problem-in-ZFC-September-24-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/A-counterexample-to-Naimarks-problem-in-ZFC-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Naimarks-problem-in-ZFC-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Naimarks-problem-in-ZFC-September-24-2026/build/sections/states.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Naimarks-problem-in-ZFC-September-24-2026/build/sections/hnn.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Naimarks-problem-in-ZFC-September-24-2026/build/sections/bridge.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Naimarks-problem-in-ZFC-September-24-2026/build/sections/iteration.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-Naimarks-problem-in-ZFC-September-24-2026/build/sections/construction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-counterexample-to-Naimarks-problem-in-ZFC-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct in ZFC a unital infinite-dimensional simple complex $C^*$-algebra with a faithful tracial state whose nonzero irreducible representations are all unitarily equivalent. This gives a negative answer to Naimark's problem without additional set-theoretic assumptions.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":350},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and scope","sourceOffset":1427},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof in outline","sourceOffset":3656},{"kind":"section","title":"Pure states, excision, and peaking projections","sourceOffset":7960},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Unique extensions and peaking","sourceOffset":9734},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Excision and inequivalent states","sourceOffset":11967},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Expectations from peaking projections","sourceOffset":18128},{"kind":"section","title":"Reduced HNN adjunctions","sourceOffset":21625},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The reduced adjunction","sourceOffset":22539},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Construction from an amalgamated free product","sourceOffset":24343},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Interchanging adjunctions and taking limits","sourceOffset":33500},{"kind":"section","title":"Joining two classes while preserving unique extensions","sourceOffset":38380}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"na:main","text":"There exists a unital infinite-dimensional simple complex $C^*$-algebra $A$ with a faithful tracial state such that all its nonzero irreducible representations are unitarily equivalent. In particular, $A$ is not isomorphic to $K(H)$ for any complex Hilbert space $H$.","sourceOffset":719,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"na:hnn","text":"Let $(A,\\tau)$ be a unital $C^*$-algebra with faithful tracial state, let $D_1,D_2\\subset A$ be unital subalgebras with trace-preserving conditional expectations $E_i:A\\to D_i$, and let $\\theta:D_1\\to D_2$ be a trace-preserving isomorphism. There is a unital extension with a faithful tracial state extending $\\tau$, \\[ H(A)=C^*(A,v),\\qquad vdv^*=\\theta(d)\\quad(d\\in D_1), \\] where $v$ is called the stable unitary, and a trace-preserving conditional expectation $E_A:H(A)\\to A$ annihilating every nonempty reduced word \\eqref{na:hnn-word}. Among faithfully tracial extensions generated by $A$ and such a unitary, the relation and vanishing of the trace on nonempty reduced words determine $H(A)$ uniquely, by an isomorphism fixing $A$ and $v$.","sourceOffset":23303,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/297.md","scopeText":"Naimark's problem asks whether a $C^*$-algebra whose nonzero irreducible representations are all unitarily equivalent must be an algebra of compact operators. The formalization gives a counterexample in ZFC: a unital infinite-dimensional simple complex $C^*$-algebra with a faithful tracial state has that uniqueness property for irreducible representations but is not isomorphic to the compact operators on any Hilbert space. No additional set-theoretic assumption is used.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/297.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":75042,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-counterexample-to-Naimarks-problem-in-ZFC-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"298-01","slug":"A-finite-entropy-separation-of-microstates-and-nonmicrostates-free-entropy-September-25-2026","familyId":"298","title":"A finite-entropy separation of microstates and nonmicrostates free entropy","abstract":"We answer the finite-entropy equality question for microstates and nonmicrostates free entropy negatively. We construct a bounded self-adjoint tuple X in a von Neumann algebra with faithful normal tracial state such that $-\\infty\\lt \\chi(X)\\leq\\chi^*(X)-\\tfrac12\\lt \\infty$. Here χ is the original microstates entropy with an operator-norm cutoff and a limsup over matrix sizes. The counterexample uses a large but fixed number of variables.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a bounded self-adjoint tuple in a tracial von Neumann algebra whose microstates and nonmicrostates free entropies satisfy $-\\infty\\lt \\chi\\lt \\chi^*\\lt \\infty$. This answers Voiculescu’s finite-entropy equality question negatively: the matrix-approximation and free-Fisher-information definitions differ even when both are finite.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-finite-entropy-separation-of-microstates-and-nonmicrostates-free-entropy-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-finite-entropy-separation-of-microstates-and-nonmicrostates-free-entropy-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-finite-entropy-separation-of-microstates-and-nonmicrostates-free-entropy-September-25-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/A-finite-entropy-separation-of-microstates-and-nonmicrostates-free-entropy-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-finite-entropy-separation-of-microstates-and-nonmicrostates-free-entropy-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-finite-entropy-separation-of-microstates-and-nonmicrostates-free-entropy-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-conventions.tex","preprints/A-finite-entropy-separation-of-microstates-and-nonmicrostates-free-entropy-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-fisher.tex","preprints/A-finite-entropy-separation-of-microstates-and-nonmicrostates-free-entropy-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-signal.tex","preprints/A-finite-entropy-separation-of-microstates-and-nonmicrostates-free-entropy-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-flow.tex","preprints/A-finite-entropy-separation-of-microstates-and-nonmicrostates-free-entropy-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-blocks.tex","preprints/A-finite-entropy-separation-of-microstates-and-nonmicrostates-free-entropy-September-25-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-finite-entropy-separation-of-microstates-and-nonmicrostates-free-entropy-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We answer the finite-entropy equality question for microstates and nonmicrostates free entropy negatively. We construct a bounded self-adjoint tuple $X$ in a von Neumann algebra with faithful normal tracial state such that $-\\infty<\\chi(X)\\leq\\chi^*(X)-\\tfrac12<\\infty$. Here $\\chi$ is the original microstates entropy with an operator-norm cutoff and a limsup over matrix sizes. The counterexample uses a large but fixed number of variables.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":489},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and preliminary facts","sourceOffset":7085},{"kind":"section","title":"A cumulant bound for the Fisher deficit","sourceOffset":15340},{"kind":"section","title":"The signal and its two noise scales","sourceOffset":23052},{"kind":"section","title":"Entropy production for specified matrix ensembles","sourceOffset":30692},{"kind":"section","title":"A persistent block-structure entropy deficit","sourceOffset":36774}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist an integer $n\\geq2$ and a bounded self-adjoint tuple $X=(X_1,\\ldots,X_n)$ in a von Neumann algebra with faithful normal tracial state such that \\[ -\\infty<\\chi(X)\\leq\\chi^*(X)-\\tfrac12<\\infty. \\] Here $\\chi$ is the original microstates entropy using an operator-norm cutoff and a limsup over matrix sizes.","sourceOffset":2720,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Squared-cumulant Fisher bound","label":"prop:fisher","text":"Let $C$ be a centered bounded self-adjoint $n$-tuple with diagonal covariance $\\tau(C_iC_j)=\\delta_{ij}c_i$, where $c_i\\geq0$, and let $S$ be a standard semicircular family free from $C$. Put $V_u=C+\\sqrt u S$ and $v_i(u)=u+c_i$ for $u>0$. For every $u>0$, \\[ \\sum_i\\frac1{v_i(u)}\\leq\\Phi^*(V_u)\\leq\\frac n u. \\] Whenever $\\sum_{r\\geq2}u^{-r}\\|\\kappa_{r+1}(C)\\|_2^2<\\infty$, \\begin{equation} 0\\leq\\Phi^*(V_u)-\\sum_i\\frac1{v_i(u)} \\leq\\frac1{u^2}\\sum_{r\\geq2}u^{-r}\\|\\kappa_{r+1}(C)\\|_2^2. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":20130,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/298.md","scopeText":"The formalization disproves equality of microstates and nonmicrostates free entropy even when both quantities are finite. It constructs a bounded self-adjoint tuple $X$ in a von Neumann algebra with a faithful normal tracial state such that $-\\infty<\\chi(X)\\le\\chi^*(X)-1/2<\\infty$. Here $\\chi$ is the microstates entropy with an operator-norm cutoff and a limsup over matrix sizes, and $\\chi^*$ is the nonmicrostates entropy defined using free semicircular noise. The example has a fixed finite number of variables.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/298.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":49238,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-finite-entropy-separation-of-microstates-and-nonmicrostates-free-entropy-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"299-01","slug":"The-Kirchberg-Rordam-character-criterion-September-25-2026","familyId":"299","title":"The Kirchberg–Rørdam character criterion","abstract":"We prove the Kirchberg–Rørdam character criterion: for every free ultrafilter on ℕ, a nonzero unital separable complex C∗-algebra absorbs the Jiang–Su algebra if and only if its norm central-sequence algebra has no characters. We also prove that the infinite minimal tensor power of every nonzero unital separable complex C∗-algebra without characters is $\\mathcal Z$-stable and contains a unital copy of $\\mathcal Z$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"A nonzero unital separable complex C∗-algebra is Jiang–Su stable exactly when its norm central-sequence algebra has no characters, for every free ultrafilter. This answers the Kirchberg–Rørdam character question. Also, the infinite minimal tensor power of every such algebra without characters is Jiang–Su stable, answering the Dadarlat–Toms question.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Kirchberg-Rordam-character-criterion-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Kirchberg-Rordam-character-criterion-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Kirchberg-Rordam-character-criterion-September-25-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/The-Kirchberg-Rordam-character-criterion-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Kirchberg-Rordam-character-criterion-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-Kirchberg-Rordam-character-criterion-September-25-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/The-Kirchberg-Rordam-character-criterion-September-25-2026/build/sections/rw-criterion.tex","preprints/The-Kirchberg-Rordam-character-criterion-September-25-2026/build/sections/tensor.tex","preprints/The-Kirchberg-Rordam-character-criterion-September-25-2026/build/sections/dimension-drop.tex","preprints/The-Kirchberg-Rordam-character-criterion-September-25-2026/build/figures/cone-support.tex","preprints/The-Kirchberg-Rordam-character-criterion-September-25-2026/build/sections/application.tex","preprints/The-Kirchberg-Rordam-character-criterion-September-25-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Kirchberg-Rordam-character-criterion-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Kirchberg--R\\o rdam character criterion: for every free ultrafilter on $\\N$, a nonzero unital separable complex $C^*$-algebra absorbs the Jiang--Su algebra if and only if its norm central-sequence algebra has no characters. We also prove that the infinite minimal tensor power of every nonzero unital separable complex $C^*$-algebra without characters is $\\Z$-stable and contains a unital copy of $\\Z$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":461},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier results and the remaining construction","sourceOffset":4041},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":7149},{"kind":"section","title":"Preliminaries","sourceOffset":8839},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Tensor products and positive elements","sourceOffset":8889},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The dimension-drop criterion","sourceOffset":11039},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Central-sequence inputs","sourceOffset":12096},{"kind":"section","title":"The dimension-drop criterion without stable rank one","sourceOffset":15465},{"kind":"section","title":"A matrix-cone homomorphism with a full corner","sourceOffset":27837},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Algebraic independence of commuting factors","sourceOffset":28409},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Labels that remain independent in representations","sourceOffset":31891},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A full square-zero element","sourceOffset":36565}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $A$ be a nonzero unital separable complex $C^*$-algebra, and let $\\omega$ be any free ultrafilter on $\\N$. Then \\[ F_\\omega(A)\\text{ has no characters} \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad A\\cong A\\otmin\\Z. \\]","sourceOffset":2280,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:finite","text":"Let $D$ be a nonzero unital complex $C^*$-algebra without characters. There is an integer $m\\ge1$ and a unital $*$-homomorphism \\[ I(2,3)\\longrightarrow D^{\\otimes_{\\max}m}, \\] where \\[ I(2,3)=\\{f\\in C([0,1],M_2\\otimes M_3): f(0)\\in M_2\\otimes1_3,\\ f(1)\\in1_2\\otimes M_3\\}. \\]","sourceOffset":2801,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/299.md","scopeText":"The Kirchberg–Rørdam criterion relates Jiang–Su absorption to characters of the central-sequence algebra. The formalized result proves that, for every nonzero unital separable complex $C^*$-algebra $A$ and every free ultrafilter on $\\mathbb N$, the norm central-sequence algebra has no nonzero character exactly when $A\\cong A\\otimes_{\\min}\\mathcal Z$. No nuclearity, simplicity, trace, or comparison hypothesis is imposed.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/299.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":65336,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Kirchberg-Rordam-character-criterion-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/The-Kirchberg-Rordam-character-criterion-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"300-01","slug":"Approximation-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026","familyId":"300","title":"Approximation Paving over Arbitrary Maximal Abelian Subalgebras","abstract":"We prove the approximation-paving conjecture of Popa and Vaes for every maximal abelian subalgebra of a complex von Neumann algebra. For each $0\\lt \\varepsilon\\lt 1$, every self-adjoint operator is a strong limit of self-adjoint operators of norm at most three times its norm, each admitting a norm paving with error at most ε times the approximant's own norm. The number of projections is at most $C\\varepsilon^{-6}$ for a universal constant C. No separability or conditional-expectation hypothesis is required.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every self-adjoint element of a complex von Neumann algebra admits strong-operator paving relative to any maximal abelian subalgebra with $O(\\varepsilon^{-2})$ blocks. The norm bound holds after compression by a projection arbitrarily close to the identity in the strong topology, resolving the Popa–Vaes quadratic paving conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/Approximation-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/Approximation-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Approximation-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/Approximation-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Approximation-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/Approximation-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Approximation-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Approximation-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Approximation-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/polynomials.tex","preprints/Approximation-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/slabs.tex","preprints/Approximation-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/figures/maharam.tex","preprints/Approximation-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/pinning.tex","preprints/Approximation-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/expected.tex","preprints/Approximation-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/expected-atomless.tex","preprints/Approximation-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/general.tex","preprints/Approximation-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/sources/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Approximation-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the approximation-paving conjecture of Popa and Vaes for every maximal abelian subalgebra of a complex von Neumann algebra. For each \\(0<\\varepsilon<1\\), every self-adjoint operator is a strong limit of self-adjoint operators of norm at most three times its norm, each admitting a norm paving with error at most \\(\\varepsilon\\) times the approximant's own norm. The number of projections is at most \\(C\\varepsilon^{-6}\\) for a universal constant \\(C\\). No separability or conditional-expectation hypothesis is required.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":574},{"kind":"section","title":"State topology and bounded sequence quotients","sourceOffset":8442},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite mixed polynomials and buffered pinning","sourceOffset":14224},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Stable polynomials and local spectral data","sourceOffset":14715},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The initial largest-root bound","sourceOffset":26509},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Residue tails and exceedance lengths","sourceOffset":31698},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The buffered choice","sourceOffset":36687},{"kind":"section","title":"Measured matrices and coherent choices on Maharam slabs","sourceOffset":44783},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Invariant measure and weighted polynomial systems","sourceOffset":47575},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Locality and infinite bounded slabs","sourceOffset":52206},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform comparison under far deletions","sourceOffset":57805},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A color choice independent of height","sourceOffset":73437}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a universal constant \\(C_{\\mathrm{ap}}>0\\) such that for every \\(0<\\varepsilon<1\\) there is an integer \\(R(\\varepsilon)\\leq C_{\\mathrm{ap}}\\varepsilon^{-6}\\) with the following property. Let \\(M\\subseteq\\mathcal B(H)\\) be a complex von Neumann algebra, let \\(A\\subseteq M\\) be a MASA, and let \\(x=x^*\\in M\\). For every finite \\(F\\subset H\\) and every \\(\\delta>0\\), there exist \\(y=y^*\\in M\\), a partition \\(\\mathcal P=(p_i)_{i=1}^{R(\\varepsilon)}\\) of the unit in \\(A\\), and \\(a=a^*\\in A\\), such that \\[ \\norm{y}\\leq3\\norm{x},\\qquad \\norm{(y-x)\\xi}<\\delta\\quad(\\xi\\in F), \\] and \\[ \\norm{a}\\leq\\norm{y},\\qquad \\norm{C_{\\mathcal P}(y)-a}\\leq\\varepsilon\\norm{y}. \\] Zero projections may be included in the partition.","sourceOffset":2519,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:so-paving","text":"Suppose that a MASA \\(A\\subseteq M\\) is the range of a normal conditional expectation. For each \\(0<\\varepsilon<1\\) there is an integer \\(R_s(\\varepsilon)\\), independent of the inclusion, such that for every \\(x=x^*\\in M\\) and every strong-operator neighborhood \\(\\mathcal V\\) of zero, there exist an \\(R_s(\\varepsilon)\\)-partition \\(\\mathcal P\\) in \\(A\\), an \\(a=a^*\\in A\\) with \\(\\norm{a}\\leq\\norm{x}\\), and a projection \\(q\\in M\\) with \\(\\one-q\\in\\mathcal V\\), satisfying \\[ \\norm{q(C_{\\mathcal P}(x)-a)q}\\leq\\varepsilon\\norm{x}. \\]","sourceOffset":3909,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":162219,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Approximation-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"300-02","slug":"Quadratic-Strong-Operator-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026","familyId":"300","title":"Quadratic Strong-Operator Paving over Arbitrary Maximal Abelian Subalgebras","abstract":"We prove the quadratic strong-operator paving conjecture of Popa and Vaes. For every $0\\lt \\varepsilon \\lt 1$, every self-adjoint element of a von Neumann algebra admits strong-operator paving over each maximal abelian subalgebra with at most $5\\times10^8\\varepsilon ^{-2}$ projections. The bound is uniform over representations and requires no separability or conditional-expectation assumption.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every self-adjoint element of a complex von Neumann algebra admits strong-operator paving relative to any maximal abelian subalgebra with $O(\\varepsilon^{-2})$ blocks. The norm bound holds after compression by a projection arbitrarily close to the identity in the strong topology, resolving the Popa–Vaes quadratic paving conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/Quadratic-Strong-Operator-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/Quadratic-Strong-Operator-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quadratic-Strong-Operator-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/Quadratic-Strong-Operator-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Quadratic-Strong-Operator-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/Quadratic-Strong-Operator-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Quadratic-Strong-Operator-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Quadratic-Strong-Operator-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/reductions.tex","preprints/Quadratic-Strong-Operator-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/kernels.tex","preprints/Quadratic-Strong-Operator-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/polynomials.tex","preprints/Quadratic-Strong-Operator-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/invariant.tex","preprints/Quadratic-Strong-Operator-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/coloring.tex","preprints/Quadratic-Strong-Operator-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/transfer.tex","preprints/Quadratic-Strong-Operator-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/figures/shortest-pair.tex","preprints/Quadratic-Strong-Operator-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/completion.tex","preprints/Quadratic-Strong-Operator-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Quadratic-Strong-Operator-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the quadratic strong-operator paving conjecture of Popa and Vaes. For every $0<\\eps<1$, every self-adjoint element of a von Neumann algebra admits strong-operator paving over each maximal abelian subalgebra with at most $5\\times10^8\\eps^{-2}$ projections. The bound is uniform over representations and requires no separability or conditional-expectation assumption.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":420},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":3866},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Outline of the proof","sourceOffset":7970},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":12136},{"kind":"section","title":"Reduction to a faithful central state","sourceOffset":13076},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The part without central normal functionals","sourceOffset":14056},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Assembly of corners","sourceOffset":20877},{"kind":"subsection","title":"State norm and separable reduction","sourceOffset":23924},{"kind":"section","title":"The normalizing algebra and atomless kernels","sourceOffset":28964},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Normalizers and the measured relation","sourceOffset":29615},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A type I observation","sourceOffset":33679},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Atomless kernels off the normalizing algebra","sourceOffset":37887}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $0<\\eps<1$, set \\[ r_\\eps=\\left\\lceil400000000\\eps^{-2}\\right\\rceil +\\left\\lceil4/\\eps\\right\\rceil+1 \\le500000000\\eps^{-2}. \\] For every masa $A\\subseteq M\\subseteq\\mathcal B(H)$ and every $x=x^*\\in M$, the element $x$ is $(\\eps,r_\\eps)$ so-pavable over $A$. In particular, the number of projections is fixed before the inclusion, operator, and strong neighborhood. No separability or conditional-expectation assumption is imposed on the inclusion.","sourceOffset":1695,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Quadratic norm paving in Ocneanu ultrapowers","label":"cor:ultrapower-paving","text":"Let $A\\subseteq M$ be a masa. Suppose that $A$ is countably decomposable and is the range of a normal conditional expectation from $M$. Let $\\omega$ be a free ultrafilter on $\\mathbb N$, and let $A^\\omega\\subseteq M^\\omega$ be the associated inclusion in the Ocneanu von Neumann ultrapower. For every $0<\\eps<1$ and every $x=x^*\\in M^\\omega$, there are a partition $p_1,\\ldots,p_{r_\\eps}\\in A^\\omega$ and an element $a=a^*\\in A^\\omega$ such that \\[ \\|a\\|\\le\\|x\\|, \\qquad \\left\\|\\sum_{i=1}^{r_\\eps}p_i x p_i-a\\right\\|\\le\\eps\\|x\\|. \\] Here $r_\\eps$ is the same integer as in Theorem \\ref{thm:main}.","sourceOffset":2825,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Strong-operator paving","label":"def:so-paving","text":"Let $x=x^*\\in M$, $\\eps>0$, and $r\\ge1$ be an integer. We say that $x$ is $(\\eps,r)$ so-pavable over $A$ if for every finite $F\\subset H$ and every $\\delta>0$ there are a partition $P=(p_1,\\ldots,p_r)$ in $A$, an element $a=a^*\\in A$ with $\\|a\\|\\le\\|x\\|$, and a projection $q\\in M$ such that \\[ \\|(1-q)\\xi\\|<\\delta\\quad(\\xi\\in F), \\qquad \\|q(S_P(x)-a)q\\|\\le\\eps\\|x\\|. \\] The integer $r$ is fixed before $F$ and $\\delta$ are chosen.","sourceOffset":1172,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":195247,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Quadratic-Strong-Operator-Paving-over-Arbitrary-Maximal-Abelian-Subalgebras-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"301-01","slug":"The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026","familyId":"301","title":"The trace cone classifies Razak–Jacelon stabilizations","abstract":"We prove that the canonical topological cone of all extended lower-semicontinuous tracial weights determines a separable nuclear C∗-algebra after tensoring with the Razak–Jacelon algebra and the compact operators, answering Robert's trace-cone classification question positively. The isomorphism realizes the prescribed cone map, with arbitrary ideal structure and without a density assumption on the finite domains of the weights.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Classifies separable nuclear complex C∗-algebras after tensoring with the Razak–Jacelon algebra and the compact operators, using their full topological cones of extended lower-semicontinuous tracial weights. This answers Robert’s trace-cone question, including algebras with arbitrary ideal structure and both finite and infinite subquotients.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026/The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026/The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026/build/figures/proof-roadmap.tex","preprints/The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-structure.tex","preprints/The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-sequence-models.tex","preprints/The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-small-stabilizers.tex","preprints/The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-uniqueness.tex","preprints/The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-tracial-cone.tex","preprints/The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-cone-correction.tex","preprints/The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026/build/sections/08-point-intertwining.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the canonical topological cone of all extended lower-semicontinuous tracial weights determines a separable nuclear \\(C^*\\)-algebra after tensoring with the Razak--Jacelon algebra and the compact operators, answering Robert's trace-cone classification question positively. The isomorphism realizes the prescribed cone map, with arbitrary ideal structure and without a density assumption on the finite domains of the weights.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":502},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The invariant and the main theorem","sourceOffset":964},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":3389},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction","sourceOffset":8822},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and conventions","sourceOffset":18489},{"kind":"section","title":"The invariant, its ideals, and matrix conventions","sourceOffset":19429},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ranks and realification","sourceOffset":19987},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite ideals, zero ideals, and open supports","sourceOffset":25151},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Folding and rational averaging","sourceOffset":31925},{"kind":"section","title":"Sequence models and local error ideals","sourceOffset":35899},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Regularized limits and their model data","sourceOffset":36332},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform comparison and cut sandwiches","sourceOffset":44806}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) be separable nuclear complex \\(C^*\\)-algebras. If \\(T(A)\\) and \\(T(B)\\) are isomorphic as topological cones, then \\[ A\\otimes\\W\\otimes\\K\\ \\cong\\ B\\otimes\\W\\otimes\\K, \\] where \\(\\K=\\K(\\ell^2(\\N))\\) and all tensor products are spatial.","sourceOffset":2048,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Trace realification","label":"st:realification","text":"For every \\(C^*\\)-algebra \\(C\\), the trace cone with topology \\eqref{intro:trace-topology} is compact Hausdorff, and it is second countable when \\(C\\) is separable. If \\(C\\) is exact, the assignment \\(\\tau\\mapsto d_\\tau\\) identifies this cone with the topological cone of functionals on \\(\\Cu(C)\\). For every \\(C^*\\)-algebra \\(C\\), \\[ \\Cu(C\\otimes\\W)\\cong\\Cu(C)_{\\R}. \\] Realification is determined functorially by the topological functional cone. Its realization as functions on that cone has pointwise order, pointwise addition, and multiplication by positive real scalars. It has the same functional cone as \\(\\Cu(C)\\). Consequently the given trace-cone isomorphism induces isomorphisms \\begin{equation} F:T(Q)\\longrightarrow T(P),\\qquad G:\\Cu(P)\\longrightarrow\\Cu(Q),\\qquad d_\\tau(Gx)=d_{F\\tau}(x). \\end{equation} In particular, for \\(x,y\\in\\Cu(Q)\\), \\begin{equation} x\\leq y \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad d_\\tau(x)\\leq d_\\tau(y)\\quad\\hbox{for every }\\tau\\in T(Q). \\end{equation} Multiplication by every positive real scalar is an order automorphism preserving increasing suprema and compact containment.","sourceOffset":21038,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Model","label":"seq:model","text":"A model for \\((Y,m,F)\\) is a homomorphism \\(p:S_Y\\to D\\) such that \\begin{equation} \\rho\\circ p=m\\otimes F(\\sigma),\\qquad \\sigma=\\rho|_Q, \\end{equation} for every regularized limit \\(\\rho\\).","sourceOffset":39606,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Local error ideal","label":"seq:smallideal","text":"For an open \\(U\\subseteq X\\), an element \\(x=[(x_n)]\\in D\\) belongs to \\(J(U)\\) if, for every \\(\\eps>0\\), there are a positive element \\(c\\) compactly Cuntz supported in \\(Q(U)\\), a sequence \\(r_n>0\\) tending to zero, and an index \\(N\\), such that \\begin{equation} d_\\tau((|x_n|-\\eps)_+) \\leq r_n d_\\tau(c) \\qquad(n\\geq N,\\ \\tau\\in T(Q)). \\end{equation} The controller \\(c\\), the decay sequence, and \\(N\\) may depend on \\(x,U,\\eps\\). Once these have been chosen, the same bound holds for all traces and all coordinates in the tail.","sourceOffset":52710,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/301.md","scopeText":"The paper classifies separable nuclear complex $C^*$-algebras after tensoring with the Razak–Jacelon algebra and the compact operators, using their cones of extended lower-semicontinuous traces. The linked formalization proves the ideal-transport part: a trace-cone equivalence induces an order isomorphism of closed two-sided ideals, carrying the ideals determined by finiteness and vanishing of each trace to those of its image.\n\nFor each closed ideal $I$, it also constructs the trace that is zero on positive elements of $I$ and infinite elsewhere. These traces are exactly the additive idempotents, meaning $\\tau+\\tau=\\tau$, and their addition recovers ideal inclusion. Spatial classification, existence of the stabilization models, norm uniqueness, and final intertwining are outside this suppo","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/301.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":211360,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":21,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/The-trace-cone-classifies-Razak-Jacelon-stabilizations-September-25-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"302-01","slug":"Filtered-products-and-boundary-preserving-compression-in-complex-cobordism-September-25-2026","familyId":"302","title":"Filtered products and boundary-preserving compression in complex cobordism","abstract":"We prove that, in sufficiently large smash powers, an $MU$-null restriction to a finite pointed subcomplex becomes stably null on the entire union of products with a fixed positive proportion of restricted factors. This coherent vanishing theorem yields boundary-preserving compression of cube-valued maps on every compact metrizable input space. When the torus slot bundle embeds continuously into a trivial bundle of rank less than twice the source rank, the compression places a positive proportion of slots on their boundaries in arbitrarily large powers and fixes every original boundary slot exactly. An example at equality shows that the strict rank inequality cannot be removed.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every minimal homeomorphism h of an infinite compact metrizable space X, the radius of comparison of $C(X)\\rtimes_h\\mathbb Z$ equals $\\tfrac12\\mathrm{mdim}(X,h)$, including infinite values. Zero mean dimension is equivalent to the small boundary property, Jiang–Su stability and finite nuclear dimension; in this case nuclear dimension is at most one.","pdfPath":"preprints/Filtered-products-and-boundary-preserving-compression-in-complex-cobordism-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Filtered-products-and-boundary-preserving-compression-in-complex-cobordism-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Filtered-products-and-boundary-preserving-compression-in-complex-cobordism-September-25-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/Filtered-products-and-boundary-preserving-compression-in-complex-cobordism-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Filtered-products-and-boundary-preserving-compression-in-complex-cobordism-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Filtered-products-and-boundary-preserving-compression-in-complex-cobordism-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-filtered-products.tex","preprints/Filtered-products-and-boundary-preserving-compression-in-complex-cobordism-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-stable-avoidance.tex","preprints/Filtered-products-and-boundary-preserving-compression-in-complex-cobordism-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-boundary-compression.tex","preprints/Filtered-products-and-boundary-preserving-compression-in-complex-cobordism-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-torus-criterion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Filtered-products-and-boundary-preserving-compression-in-complex-cobordism-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that, in sufficiently large smash powers, an $MU$-null restriction to a finite pointed subcomplex becomes stably null on the entire union of products with a fixed positive proportion of restricted factors. This coherent vanishing theorem yields boundary-preserving compression of cube-valued maps on every compact metrizable input space. When the torus slot bundle embeds continuously into a trivial bundle of rank less than twice the source rank, the compression places a positive proportion of slots on their boundaries in arbitrarily large powers and fixes every original boundary slot exactly. An example at equality shows that the strict rank inequality cannot be removed.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":748},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coherent products","sourceOffset":1720},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cube slots and the torus criterion","sourceOffset":3464},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and the proof mechanism","sourceOffset":7039},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":12195},{"kind":"section","title":"Compatible smash products and quantitative nilpotence","sourceOffset":12693},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A compatible lift on the union","sourceOffset":14472},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The quantitative nilpotence input","sourceOffset":26336},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From local vanishing to integral vanishing","sourceOffset":30289},{"kind":"section","title":"Point tests and controlled avoidance","sourceOffset":36182},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Point tests and their products","sourceOffset":36531},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coordinate complements in boxes","sourceOffset":40124}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Filtered products","label":"thm:filtered-product","text":"Let $G\\subset E$ be a finite pointed CW pair, let $d\\geq0$ be an integer, and let $v:E\\to S^d$ be a based map. Suppose that the composite \\[ \\Sigma^\\infty G\\longrightarrow\\Sigma^\\infty E \\xrightarrow{\\Sigma^\\infty v}\\Sigma^d\\mathbb S \\longrightarrow\\Sigma^d MU \\] is nullhomotopic. For $\\ell\\geq1$, let $U_\\ell\\subset E^{\\wedge\\ell}$ be the union of the subcomplexes in which at least $\\lceil\\ell/2\\rceil$ factors are required to belong to $G$. Then, for every sufficiently large $\\ell$, the restriction \\[ v^{\\wedge\\ell}|_{U_\\ell}:U_\\ell\\longrightarrow S^{d\\ell} \\] is stably nullhomotopic.","sourceOffset":2135,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Torus compression","label":"thm:torus-compression","text":"Let $p,n\\ge1$ be integers, put $t=2p+1$, and choose $L,H$ as above. Let $0<a<1/16$, let $Y$ be any compact metrizable space, and let $f:Y\\to C(t,n)$ be continuous. Suppose there is one continuous complex bundle monomorphism \\[ E_f\\longrightarrow\\mathcal T(f,Z_{an}(t,n))\\times\\mathbb C^K \\] over the entire locus $\\mathcal T(f,Z_{an}(t,n))$, for a nonnegative integer $K<2pn$. Then for every integer $U\\ge1$ there are an integer $u\\ge U$ and a continuous map \\[ G:Y^u\\longrightarrow C(t,un) \\] such that at least $(a/8)un$ slots of $G(\\mathbf y)$ lie in $\\partial[-1,1]^t$ for every $\\mathbf y=(y_0,\\ldots,y_{u-1})$. Moreover, for $0\\le b<u$ and $1\\le j\\le n$, \\[ f_j(y_b)\\in\\partial[-1,1]^t \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad G_{b,j}(\\mathbf y)=f_j(y_b). \\]","sourceOffset":5421,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"notation","name":"Torus locus and slot bundle","label":"not:torus-bundle","text":"On $L^n$ put \\[ H_n=\\bigoplus_{j=1}^n\\operatorname{pr}_j^*H \\subset L^n\\times\\mathbb C^{2pn}. \\] For $f:Y\\to C(2p+1,n)$ and $Z\\subset T(2p+1,n)$, let \\[ \\mathcal T(f,Z)=\\{(y,z)\\in Y\\times Z:f(y)-z\\in L^n\\}, \\qquad d_f(y,z)=f(y)-z. \\] The slot bundle on this locus is $E_f=d_f^*H_n$, of complex rank $pn$, with its fixed embedding in the trivial bundle of rank $2pn$.","sourceOffset":4922,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Point test","label":"def:point-test","text":"Recall $c=1/32$. Let $P$ be a finite polyhedron, let $f:P\\to C(t,n)$ be PL, and let $Z\\subseteq T(t,n)$ be a subpolyhedron. Define \\begin{align*} A_c(f,Z)&=\\{(x,z)\\in P\\times Z:\\|f(x)-z\\|_\\infty\\ge c\\},\\\\ Q_c(f,Z)&=(P\\times Z)/A_c(f,Z). \\end{align*} For $0<\\rho<c$, take the oriented collapse $\\kappa_\\rho:\\R^M\\to S^M$ of the complement of $(-\\rho,\\rho)^M$. The point test is the based map \\begin{equation} \\varphi_\\rho(f,Z):Q_c(f,Z)\\longrightarrow S^M, \\qquad [x,z]\\longmapsto\\kappa_\\rho(f(x)-z). \\end{equation} Its stable class is denoted by \\[ \\omega_{\\mathbb S}(f,Z)\\in [\\Sigma^\\infty Q_c(f,Z),\\S","sourceOffset":37018,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":84104,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Filtered-products-and-boundary-preserving-compression-in-complex-cobordism-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"302-02","slug":"Radius-of-comparison-equals-half-the-mean-dimension-September-25-2026","familyId":"302","title":"Radius of comparison equals half the mean dimension","abstract":"We prove the integer-action case of the Phillips–Toms conjecture: for every minimal homeomorphism of an infinite compact metrizable space, the radius of comparison of its crossed product equals one half of its mean dimension, including equality at infinity. For these systems, zero mean dimension and the small boundary property are equivalent to Jiang–Su stability and to finite nuclear dimension of the crossed product; in this case its nuclear dimension is at most one.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every minimal homeomorphism h of an infinite compact metrizable space X, the radius of comparison of $C(X)\\rtimes_h\\mathbb Z$ equals $\\tfrac12\\mathrm{mdim}(X,h)$, including infinite values. Zero mean dimension is equivalent to the small boundary property, Jiang–Su stability and finite nuclear dimension; in this case nuclear dimension is at most one.","pdfPath":"preprints/Radius-of-comparison-equals-half-the-mean-dimension-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Radius-of-comparison-equals-half-the-mean-dimension-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Radius-of-comparison-equals-half-the-mean-dimension-September-25-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/Radius-of-comparison-equals-half-the-mean-dimension-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Radius-of-comparison-equals-half-the-mean-dimension-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Radius-of-comparison-equals-half-the-mean-dimension-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-compression-interface.tex","preprints/Radius-of-comparison-equals-half-the-mean-dimension-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-dynamical-comparison.tex","preprints/Radius-of-comparison-equals-half-the-mean-dimension-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-cover-reconstruction.tex","preprints/Radius-of-comparison-equals-half-the-mean-dimension-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-lower-bound.tex","preprints/Radius-of-comparison-equals-half-the-mean-dimension-September-25-2026/build/figures/block-allocation.tex","preprints/Radius-of-comparison-equals-half-the-mean-dimension-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-regularity.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Radius-of-comparison-equals-half-the-mean-dimension-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the integer-action case of the Phillips--Toms conjecture: for every minimal homeomorphism of an infinite compact metrizable space, the radius of comparison of its crossed product equals one half of its mean dimension, including equality at infinity. For these systems, zero mean dimension and the small boundary property are equivalent to Jiang--Su stability and to finite nuclear dimension of the crossed product; in this case its nuclear dimension is at most one.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":521},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The statement and conventions","sourceOffset":1231},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior results and the remaining lower bound","sourceOffset":3692},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Zero mean dimension and regularity","sourceOffset":6818},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof: comparison, compression, and colors","sourceOffset":9410},{"kind":"section","title":"The boundary-preserving compression input","sourceOffset":13364},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Slots, sparse parameters, and the fixed torus bundle","sourceOffset":13858},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The imported theorem and its role","sourceOffset":15985},{"kind":"section","title":"Comparison along uniformly allocated orbit blocks","sourceOffset":18409},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Open towers and uniformly good starting points","sourceOffset":19306},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A fixed linear map for changing coordinate supports","sourceOffset":26418},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The parametrized bundle embedding","sourceOffset":27626}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(h\\) be a minimal homeomorphism of an infinite compact metrizable space \\(X\\). Then \\begin{equation} \\rc\\bigl(C(X)\\rtimes_h\\Z\\bigr)=\\frac12\\mdim(X,h) \\qquad\\text{in }[0,\\infty]. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":2904,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Zero mean dimension and crossed-product regularity","label":"cor:regularity","text":"Let \\(h\\) be a minimal homeomorphism of an infinite compact metrizable space \\(X\\), and put \\(A=C(X)\\rtimes_h\\Z\\). The following conditions are equivalent: \\begin{enumerate} \\item \\(\\mdim(X,h)=0\\); \\item \\((X,h)\\) has the small boundary property; \\item \\(A\\cong A\\otimes\\mathcal Z\\); \\item \\(\\dim_{\\mathrm{nuc}}(A)<\\infty\\); \\item \\(\\dim_{\\mathrm{nuc}}(A)\\le1\\). \\end{enumerate} In particular, if \\(\\mdim(X,h)\\in(0,\\infty]\\), then \\(A\\not\\cong A\\otimes\\mathcal Z\\) and \\(\\dim_{\\mathrm{nuc}}(A)=\\infty\\).","sourceOffset":7985,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":74602,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Radius-of-comparison-equals-half-the-mean-dimension-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"303-01","slug":"Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026","familyId":"303","title":"Weak pure infiniteness and O-infinity absorption","abstract":"We prove that every complex C∗-algebra in which each positive element is properly infinite is strongly purely infinite. This answers the ordinary-to-strong part of Kirchberg–Rørdam's comparison question. For exact algebras, we also prove that proper infiniteness of one fixed finite amplification of every positive element implies proper infiniteness of each positive element. Consequently, every separable nuclear algebra with this fixed-amplification property absorbs $\\mathcal O_\\infty$, without assumptions of unitality or simplicity.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the ordinary-to-strong pure-infiniteness question of Kirchberg and Rørdam for complex C∗-algebras. For exact algebras, proper infiniteness of one fixed finite amplification of every positive element also suffices. Consequently, every separable nuclear algebra with this property absorbs $\\mathcal O_\\infty$, without unitality or simplicity assumptions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026","subjectId":"16","subject":"Operator algebras","subjectZh":"算子代數","texPaths":["preprints/Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026/build/manuscript.tex","preprints/Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026/build/proof-routes.tex","preprints/Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026/build/foundations.tex","preprints/Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026/build/graph.tex","preprints/Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026/build/four-variables.tex","preprints/Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026/build/separation.tex","preprints/Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026/build/nuclear.tex","preprints/Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026/build/exactness.tex","preprints/Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026/build/perturbation.tex","preprints/Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026/build/localization.tex","preprints/Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026/build/quantitative.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026/build/manuscript.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every complex \\(C^*\\)-algebra in which each positive element is properly infinite is strongly purely infinite. This answers the ordinary-to-strong part of Kirchberg--R{\\o}rdam's comparison question. For exact algebras, we also prove that proper infiniteness of one fixed finite amplification of every positive element implies proper infiniteness of each positive element. Consequently, every separable nuclear algebra with this fixed-amplification property absorbs \\(\\Oinf\\), without assumptions of unitality or simplicity.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":585},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Infiniteness notions and main results","sourceOffset":1273},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier results and the role of ideal structure","sourceOffset":8035},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and the independent routes","sourceOffset":10887},{"kind":"section","title":"Comparison and transport","sourceOffset":19553},{"kind":"section","title":"Graph measurements and conditional shrinking","sourceOffset":33905},{"kind":"section","title":"Four entries under individual proper infiniteness","sourceOffset":45452},{"kind":"section","title":"Uniform separation and general diagonalization","sourceOffset":60961},{"kind":"section","title":"Removing amplification under nuclearity","sourceOffset":71437},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite tuple that exact algebras cannot realize","sourceOffset":72768},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Choosing graph entries under nuclearity","sourceOffset":80127},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local rich elements and their merger","sourceOffset":87350}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:individual-to-strong","text":"Let \\(C\\) be a complex \\(C^*\\)-algebra satisfying \\(\\propP\\). For all \\(a,b\\in C_+\\), \\(c\\in C\\), and \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), there are \\(s,t\\in C\\) such that \\begin{equation} \\|s^*as-a\\|<\\varepsilon,\\qquad \\|t^*bt-b\\|<\\varepsilon,\\qquad \\|s^*ct\\|<\\varepsilon. \\end{equation} Consequently \\(C\\) is strongly purely infinite.","sourceOffset":4130,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:exact-infiniteness","text":"Let \\(C\\) be an exact complex \\(C^*\\)-algebra. Suppose that there is one integer \\(n\\ge1\\) such that \\(h^{\\oplus n}\\) is properly infinite for every \\(h\\in C_+\\). Then every positive element of \\(C\\) is properly infinite. Consequently \\(C\\) is strongly purely infinite.","sourceOffset":5231,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:rich","text":"A positive contraction \\(a\\) is rich if \\((a-\\varepsilon)_+\\) is properly infinite for every \\(\\varepsilon\\ge0\\).","sourceOffset":33126,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/303.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves that a complex $C^*$-algebra in which every positive element is properly infinite is strongly purely infinite, with the full positive-element diagonalization property. No exactness, nuclearity, unitality, or simplicity assumption is needed for this implication.\n\nFor exact algebras, proper infiniteness of one fixed finite amplification of every positive element already implies individual proper infiniteness and strong pure infiniteness.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/303.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":174508,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026/build/manuscript.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Weak-pure-infiniteness-and-O-infinity-absorption-September-25-2026/build/manuscript.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"304-01","slug":"The-Hilbert-Smith-conjecture-in-every-finite-dimension-September-23-2026","familyId":"304","title":"The Hilbert–Smith conjecture in every finite dimension","abstract":"We prove the Hilbert–Smith conjecture in every finite dimension: every locally compact second-countable Hausdorff group acting faithfully and jointly continuously on a connected Hausdorff second-countable finite-dimensional topological manifold without boundary is a Lie group.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Every locally compact second-countable Hausdorff group acting faithfully and jointly continuously on a connected finite-dimensional topological manifold is a Lie group. This proves the Hilbert–Smith conjecture in all finite dimensions, for Hausdorff second-countable manifolds without boundary.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Hilbert-Smith-conjecture-in-every-finite-dimension-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Hilbert-Smith-conjecture-in-every-finite-dimension-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Hilbert-Smith-conjecture-in-every-finite-dimension-September-23-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/The-Hilbert-Smith-conjecture-in-every-finite-dimension-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Hilbert-Smith-conjecture-in-every-finite-dimension-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-Hilbert-Smith-conjecture-in-every-finite-dimension-September-23-2026/build/figures/local-test.tex","preprints/The-Hilbert-Smith-conjecture-in-every-finite-dimension-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-categories.tex","preprints/The-Hilbert-Smith-conjecture-in-every-finite-dimension-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-localization.tex","preprints/The-Hilbert-Smith-conjecture-in-every-finite-dimension-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-lattice.tex","preprints/The-Hilbert-Smith-conjecture-in-every-finite-dimension-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-local-tests.tex","preprints/The-Hilbert-Smith-conjecture-in-every-finite-dimension-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-character-classes.tex","preprints/The-Hilbert-Smith-conjecture-in-every-finite-dimension-September-23-2026/build/appendices/duality-signs.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Hilbert-Smith-conjecture-in-every-finite-dimension-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Hilbert--Smith conjecture in every finite dimension: every locally compact second-countable Hausdorff group acting faithfully and jointly continuously on a connected Hausdorff second-countable finite-dimensional topological manifold without boundary is a Lie group.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":426},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The problem and its predecessors","sourceOffset":3188},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Duality and signatures","sourceOffset":7045},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The obstruction: equal parts in a fixed lattice","sourceOffset":8817},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the action supplies the equal parts","sourceOffset":12509},{"kind":"section","title":"Compact-source sheaf categories and homotopy invariance","sourceOffset":17769},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Spaces, coefficients, and generators","sourceOffset":18213},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Duality on the generated categories","sourceOffset":24806},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Shifted forms and the cone relation","sourceOffset":34648},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Natural external products","sourceOffset":39226},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The interval boundary and homotopies","sourceOffset":45030},{"kind":"section","title":"Localization, germs, and dense subcategories","sourceOffset":50823}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"$p$-adic exclusion","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(n\\geq1\\) be an integer and \\(p\\) a prime. Every jointly continuous action of \\(\\Zp\\) on a connected Hausdorff second-countable topological \\(n\\)-manifold without boundary has nonzero kernel. Consequently the action factors through a finite quotient of \\(\\Zp\\).","sourceOffset":1723,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"The Hilbert--Smith conjecture","label":"cor:lie","text":"Let \\(n\\geq1\\). A locally compact second-countable Hausdorff group acting faithfully and jointly continuously on a connected Hausdorff second-countable topological \\(n\\)-manifold without boundary is a Lie group.","sourceOffset":830,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"The test categories","label":"def:test-categories","text":"Let $\\Tzero(X)$ be the smallest full replete triangulated subcategory of $\\mathsf D(X)$ containing \\begin{equation} Rf_*\\const{\\R}{B}, \\qquad f:B\\longrightarrow X, \\qquad B\\text{ a compact finite polyhedron}, \\end{equation} where $f$ is any continuous map. Let $\\T(X)$ be its closure under direct summands in $\\mathsf D(X)$. When $X$ has a specified semialgebraic model, define $\\Pzero(X)$ and $\\Pcat(X)$ in the same manner, requiring $B$ to be a compact polyhedron in a Euclidean space and $f$ to be continuous semialgebraic in the specified models. For an arbitrary open subset $U$ of a semialgebra","sourceOffset":19125,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Character classes","label":"def:character-classes","text":"Let \\[ \\ell_U:E_d(X)\\xrightarrow{\\sim}W_d(\\mathcal Q_U(X)) \\] be the integral isomorphism of \\Cref{prop:contractible-quotient}. For $0\\leq i<m$ define $z_i(t)\\in E_d(X)$ uniquely by \\begin{equation} \\ell_U(z_i(t))= [A_t,\\alpha_t]+[A_t,\\alpha_t\\circ(2e_i-\\id)]. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":126495,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":148917,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":24,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Hilbert-Smith-conjecture-in-every-finite-dimension-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"305-01","slug":"A-boundary-only-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026","familyId":"305","title":"A boundary-only obstruction to four-dimensional disk embedding","abstract":"We disprove the four-dimensional disc embedding conjecture without a fundamental-group hypothesis, even when no homotopy classes or output framings are prescribed. We construct a compact oriented smooth four-manifold containing finitely many disc maps with framed algebraic dual spheres whose boundary circles bound no disjoint locally flat discs. Consequently, the free group on two generators is not good in the sense of Freedman–Quinn, and neither is any group containing it as a subgroup.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"The unrestricted four-dimensional disk-embedding conjecture fails: framed algebraic dual spheres do not suffice to obtain disjoint locally flat spanning disks. In particular, the free group F2 is not good in the sense of Freedman–Quinn. Also constructs a finitely presented integral Poincaré duality group of dimension four with a finite classifying space but no realization as the fundamental group of a closed aspherical topological four-manifold, disproving Wall's conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-boundary-only-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-boundary-only-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-boundary-only-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/A-boundary-only-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-boundary-only-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-boundary-only-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/A-boundary-only-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/figures/geometry-graph.tex","preprints/A-boundary-only-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/figures/geometry-ribbon.tex","preprints/A-boundary-only-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/figures/geometry-stages.tex","preprints/A-boundary-only-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/sections/deformation.tex","preprints/A-boundary-only-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/sections/connections.tex","preprints/A-boundary-only-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/sections/algebra.tex","preprints/A-boundary-only-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/sections/frobenius.tex","preprints/A-boundary-only-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-boundary-only-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove the four-dimensional disc embedding conjecture without a fundamental-group hypothesis, even when no homotopy classes or output framings are prescribed. We construct a compact oriented smooth four-manifold containing finitely many disc maps with framed algebraic dual spheres whose boundary circles bound no disjoint locally flat discs. Consequently, the free group on two generators is not good in the sense of Freedman--Quinn, and neither is any group containing it as a subgroup.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":578},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Good groups and the free-group problem","sourceOffset":3854},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Surgery and previous obstruction approaches","sourceOffset":7814},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The obstruction","sourceOffset":11477},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and organization","sourceOffset":17267},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite geometric test for disc embedding","sourceOffset":18573},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The ambient manifold and its boundary","sourceOffset":19226},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The word list and capped stages","sourceOffset":25404},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exterior, the dual spheres, and their labels","sourceOffset":39722},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Topological disc images give smooth cuts","sourceOffset":51621},{"kind":"section","title":"Deformations of the cut data","sourceOffset":60636},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The paired restriction maps","sourceOffset":61696}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist a compact connected oriented smooth four-manifold $M$ with nonempty boundary, an integer $k\\geq1$, proper generically immersed discs \\[ f_i\\colon(D^2,S^1)\\longrightarrow(M,\\partial M),\\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq k, \\] whose boundary restrictions form an embedding of $\\coprod_iS^1$, and framed sphere maps $g_i\\colon S^2\\to M$ such that \\begin{equation} \\lambda(f_i,g_j)=\\delta_{ij}\\,1,\\qquad \\lambda(g_i,g_j)=0,\\qquad \\widetilde\\mu(g_i)=0 \\quad(1\\leq i,j\\leq k), \\end{equation} but the given boundary circles do not bound pairwise disjoint proper locally flat embedded discs in $M$. Each $g_i$ can be chosen to be a smooth embedded sphere; different $g_i$ are allowed to intersect.","sourceOffset":2742,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Nongoodness of the free group","label":"cor:nongood","text":"The free group $F_2$ on two generators is not good in the sense of Freedman--Quinn. Every group containing a subgroup isomorphic to $F_2$ is likewise not good.","sourceOffset":6440,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Scalar rings and formal coefficients","label":"def:coefficient-convention","text":"An algebraic parameter chart will mean a local \\(\\mathbb C\\)-algebra essentially of finite type and etale over an affine coordinate space at the designated origin. Its completion is \\(B=\\mathbb C[[v_1,\\ldots,v_N]]\\), with maximal ideal \\(\\mathfrak m\\). Finite collections of algebraic germs obtained by an implicit equation with invertible Jacobian can always be placed in one such chart, after replacing it by a common etale neighborhood. For a smooth manifold \\(Z\\), smooth forms with formal coefficients mean \\[ \\Omega^j(Z)\\widehat\\otimes B =\\varprojlim_k\\bigl(\\Omega^j(Z)\\otimes_{\\mathbb C} B/\\ma","sourceOffset":67956,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"The polarized wall function","label":"conn:potential-definition","text":"Use the global frame in Lemma \\ref{conn:wall-collar}, and denote the fiber connection form by \\(A_i\\). With \\(\\epsilon_i=\\int_{\\partial J_i}d\\theta_i\\wedge d\\eta_i\\), put \\begin{align} \\operatorname{CS}_{J_i}(A_i) &=\\int_{J_i}\\operatorname{tr} \\left(A_i\\wedge dA_i+\\frac23 A_i\\wedge A_i\\wedge A_i\\right), \\notag\\\\ P_i(a_i,y_i) &=\\operatorname{CS}_{J_i}(A_i) +\\epsilon_i\\operatorname{tr}(U_iV_i). \\end{align} The differential in this integral is fiberwise. Integrals over all closed components are included with their chosen orientations.","sourceOffset":92473,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":186460,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":15,"definitionCount":3,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-boundary-only-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"305-02","slug":"A-marked-tensor-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026","familyId":"305","title":"A marked tensor obstruction to four-dimensional disk embedding","abstract":"We disprove the unrestricted four-dimensional disk-embedding conjecture. We construct immersed disks in a compact oriented smooth four-manifold with framed algebraic dual spheres satisfying the usual equivariant intersection and reduced self-intersection conditions, but with no pairwise disjoint locally flat replacements that preserve the boundary maps and induced normal framings. The obstruction holds even when the replacement disks' relative homotopy classes are not prescribed.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"The unrestricted four-dimensional disk-embedding conjecture fails: framed algebraic dual spheres do not suffice to obtain disjoint locally flat spanning disks. In particular, the free group F2 is not good in the sense of Freedman–Quinn. Also constructs a finitely presented integral Poincaré duality group of dimension four with a finite classifying space but no realization as the fundamental group of a closed aspherical topological four-manifold, disproving Wall's conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-marked-tensor-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-marked-tensor-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-marked-tensor-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/A-marked-tensor-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-marked-tensor-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/A-marked-tensor-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-geometry.tex","preprints/A-marked-tensor-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/figures/geometry-rim-collar.tex","preprints/A-marked-tensor-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/figures/geometry-sheets.tex","preprints/A-marked-tensor-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-tensor.tex","preprints/A-marked-tensor-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-edge-germs.tex","preprints/A-marked-tensor-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-algebraicity.tex","preprints/A-marked-tensor-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/sections/04a-formal-comparison.tex","preprints/A-marked-tensor-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-vertices.tex","preprints/A-marked-tensor-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-conclusion.tex","preprints/A-marked-tensor-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/appendices/formal-trace-comparison.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-marked-tensor-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove the unrestricted four-dimensional disk-embedding conjecture. We construct immersed disks in a compact oriented smooth four-manifold with framed algebraic dual spheres satisfying the usual equivariant intersection and reduced self-intersection conditions, but with no pairwise disjoint locally flat replacements that preserve the boundary maps and induced normal framings. The obstruction holds even when the replacement disks' relative homotopy classes are not prescribed.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":531},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Idea of the proof","sourceOffset":5191},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The geometric and algebraic antecedents","sourceOffset":9355},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and organization","sourceOffset":13532},{"kind":"section","title":"The geometric reduction","sourceOffset":15492},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite list of relations","sourceOffset":17671},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The plumbing blocks","sourceOffset":23557},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Marked grope bodies and their framings","sourceOffset":26890},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The disk problem in the exterior","sourceOffset":35729},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Equivariant cuts with connected pieces","sourceOffset":48038},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The parity quotient and its peripheral homology","sourceOffset":53195},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite tensor obstruction","sourceOffset":58076}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist a compact connected oriented smooth four-manifold \\(M\\) with boundary, an integer \\(k\\geq1\\), generically immersed proper disks \\[ f_1,\\ldots,f_k:(D^2,S^1)\\longrightarrow(M,\\partial M) \\] with disjoint embedded boundary circles, and generically immersed framed spheres \\(g_1,\\ldots,g_k:S^2\\longrightarrow M\\), such that \\[ \\lambda(f_i,g_j)=\\delta_{ij},\\qquad \\lambda(g_i,g_j)=0\\quad\\text{for all }i,j,\\qquad \\widetilde\\mu(g_i)=0\\quad\\text{for all }i, \\] but there are no pairwise disjoint locally flat embedded disks in \\(M\\) with the same boundary maps and the boundary normal framings induced by the \\(f_i\\).","sourceOffset":4011,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Geometric reduction","label":"prop:geometric-reduction","text":"For the integers \\(m,p\\) and the finite list just specified, there is a compact connected oriented smooth four-manifold \\(X\\), constructed without Assumption \\ref{ass:disk}, with the following properties. It has a regular cover with deck group \\(F_m\\), whose block graph is the Cayley tree of \\(F_m\\). Each block is a four-dimensional one-handlebody on the \\(p\\) extra letters. Its exposed boundary piece is \\[ K_v\\cong \\bigl(\\#^p(S^1\\times S^2)\\bigr) \\setminus\\operatorname{int}\\nu(L_{2m}), \\] where \\(L_{2m}\\) is a \\(2m\\)-component unlink in a ball. The port meridians and the extra letters freely generate \\(\\pi_1K_v\\). At every join, the meridian on one side is the longitude on the other. There is a finite family of framed grope bodies with boundary in these pieces, equivariant in the cover, realizing the list \\eqref{eq:three-slot-inputs}. If Assumption \\ref{ass:disk} holds, there are equivariant cuts, with compact quotient, having connected vertex pieces \\(V_v\\) and connected edge three-manifolds \\(Y_e\\). Each \\(Y_e\\) has as its entire boundary the prescribed join torus. Every corresponding three-slot grope word is trivial in \\(\\pi_1V_v\\). In the index-two parity quotient, these cuts give two connected vertex pieces \\(A,B\\) and \\(2m\\) connected oriented edge pieces \\(Y_1,\\ldots,Y_{2m}\\). Their ambient manifold \\(X_2\\) is homotopy equivalent to the graph with two vertices and \\(2m\\) edges, with \\(p\\) additional circles at each vertex. For any characteristic-zero field \\(k\\), diff","sourceOffset":20442,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[{"kind":"assumption","name":"Universal framed disk replacement","label":"ass:disk","text":"Let \\(M\\) be a compact connected oriented smooth four-manifold with boundary. Suppose that a finite collection of proper generic immersions \\[ f_i\\colon(D^2,S^1)\\longrightarrow(M,\\partial M),\\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq k, \\] has pairwise disjoint embedded boundary circles, and admits generically immersed framed spheres \\(g_1,\\ldots,g_k\\) such that \\[ \\lambda(f_i,g_j)=\\delta_{ij},\\qquad \\lambda(g_i,g_j)=0,\\qquad \\widetilde\\mu(g_i)=0 \\quad\\text{for all }i,j. \\] Here intersections are counted in \\(\\mathbb Z[\\pi_1M]\\), with fixed whiskers, the diagonal equations are included, and \\[ \\widetilde\\mu(g_i)\\in \\","sourceOffset":16089,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":193098,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-marked-tensor-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-marked-tensor-obstruction-to-four-dimensional-disk-embedding-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"305-03","slug":"A-PD4-group-without-an-aspherical-manifold-model-September-24-2026","familyId":"305","title":"A PD4 group without an aspherical manifold model","abstract":"We construct a finitely presented integral Poincaré duality group of dimension four that has a finite classifying space but is not the fundamental group of any closed aspherical topological four-manifold. This gives a negative answer to Wall's manifold-realization question in dimension four.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"The unrestricted four-dimensional disk-embedding conjecture fails: framed algebraic dual spheres do not suffice to obtain disjoint locally flat spanning disks. In particular, the free group F2 is not good in the sense of Freedman–Quinn. Also constructs a finitely presented integral Poincaré duality group of dimension four with a finite classifying space but no realization as the fundamental group of a closed aspherical topological four-manifold, disproving Wall's conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-PD4-group-without-an-aspherical-manifold-model-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-PD4-group-without-an-aspherical-manifold-model-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-PD4-group-without-an-aspherical-manifold-model-September-24-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/A-PD4-group-without-an-aspherical-manifold-model-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-PD4-group-without-an-aspherical-manifold-model-September-24-2026/build/sections/chamber.tex","preprints/A-PD4-group-without-an-aspherical-manifold-model-September-24-2026/build/sections/reflection.tex","preprints/A-PD4-group-without-an-aspherical-manifold-model-September-24-2026/build/sections/stable.tex","preprints/A-PD4-group-without-an-aspherical-manifold-model-September-24-2026/build/sections/control.tex","preprints/A-PD4-group-without-an-aspherical-manifold-model-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-PD4-group-without-an-aspherical-manifold-model-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a finitely presented integral Poincar\\'e duality group of dimension four that has a finite classifying space but is not the fundamental group of any closed aspherical topological four-manifold. This gives a negative answer to Wall's manifold-realization question in dimension four.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":340},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The source of the obstruction","sourceOffset":3690},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof architecture","sourceOffset":6586},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":9910},{"kind":"section","title":"A chamber with no marked graph filling","sourceOffset":10794},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The geometric and tensor inputs","sourceOffset":12339},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exterior with its side marking","sourceOffset":18139},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The middle form and the Poincar\\'e pair","sourceOffset":23138},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Topological fillings and the cut obstruction","sourceOffset":26850},{"kind":"section","title":"Reflection and the duality group","sourceOffset":36091},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Panels and finite developments","sourceOffset":37442},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The universal development","sourceOffset":42460}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a finitely presented group $G$ with a finite, oriented, four-dimensional aspherical Poincar\\'e complex $Q$ such that \\[ H^i(G;\\Z G)=0\\quad(i\\ne4),\\qquad H^4(G;\\Z G)\\cong\\Z \\] with trivial right $G$-action on the last group, but no closed aspherical topological four-manifold has fundamental group $G$. In particular the trivial left $\\Z G$-module $\\Z$ has a finite resolution by finitely generated free modules.","sourceOffset":2844,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Removal of the remaining planes","label":"ct:removal","text":"The compact manifold $Z$ admits interior surgeries producing a compact oriented topological four-manifold $V$ with boundary $S$, fundamental group $H$, and contractible universal cover. The boundary map to $BH$ is the original central marking.","sourceOffset":108731,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":117392,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-PD4-group-without-an-aspherical-manifold-model-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"306-01","slug":"Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026","familyId":"306","title":"Purely cosmetic surgery on knots in the three-sphere","abstract":"We prove the purely cosmetic surgery conjecture for knots in the three-sphere. Distinct Dehn surgery slopes on a nontrivial smooth knot never give orientation-preservingly homeomorphic manifolds.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Distinct Dehn surgery slopes on a nontrivial smooth knot in S3 never produce orientation-preservingly homeomorphic manifolds, proving the purely cosmetic surgery conjecture. The statement includes the meridional slope.","pdfPath":"preprints/Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-ordinary.tex","preprints/Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-negative.tex","preprints/Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026/build/figures/negative-contraction.tex","preprints/Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-caps.tex","preprints/Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-stack.tex","preprints/Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026/build/figures/stack-pattern.tex","preprints/Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-geometry.tex","preprints/Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-estimates.tex","preprints/Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-indices.tex","preprints/Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026/build/sections/09-analysis.tex","preprints/Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026/build/sections/10-gluing.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the purely cosmetic surgery conjecture for knots in the three-sphere. Distinct Dehn surgery slopes on a nontrivial smooth knot never give orientation-preservingly homeomorphic manifolds.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":258},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The remaining case","sourceOffset":1462},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The argument","sourceOffset":5563},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local constructions and organization","sourceOffset":11457},{"kind":"section","title":"Ordinary instanton theory and the handle units","sourceOffset":14034},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Complexes and index conventions","sourceOffset":16807},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The polygon model and its metric families","sourceOffset":24630},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compatible perturbations","sourceOffset":33830},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ordinary gluing and harmonic necks","sourceOffset":45118},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cap shifts and primary-face exclusions","sourceOffset":60517},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The hard cap and its restriction degree","sourceOffset":71609},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Boundary identities and the bridge","sourceOffset":90082}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $K\\subset S^3$ be a smooth nontrivial knot. For any $r,s\\in\\Q\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$, an orientation-preserving homeomorphism \\[ S^3_r(K)\\cong S^3_s(K) \\] implies $r=s$.","sourceOffset":861,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Cosmetic surgery reduction","label":"prop:introduction:reduction","text":"If a smooth nontrivial knot $K\\subset S^3$ has distinct purely cosmetic slopes, then $g(K)=2$ and the two slopes are $-2$ and $2$.","sourceOffset":4500,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:indices:types","text":"The phase circle $\\mathbb T$ acts by multiplying $\\Phi$. On the quotient by determinant-one gauge, its kernel at a free orbit is $\\{1,-1\\}$. A main component has type $A$ when $\\Phi=0$ and the connection is irreducible, type $R$ when $\\Phi=0$ and the connection has continuous stabilizer, type $S$ when $\\Phi\\ne0$ lies in one summand of a parallel splitting, and type $P$ otherwise. A fixed-type tuple has no component of type $P$. The letters here denote field types; in particular $A$ is not the capped Seifert surface, and $P$ is not the positive bridge cell. For a split connection write $E=L_1\\o","sourceOffset":361299,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:indices:analytic-hypotheses","text":"Call a choice of equations and representatives admissible for the exclusion calculation if it has the following properties, for all ideal main-part tuples in the fixed total charge budget, including the enlarged candidate class described in Section \\ref{subsec:analysis:candidates}. In this class the completed main fields satisfy their equations and retained nonphase cuts, together with the phase cuts when imposed. Matching across true cuts and the actual lens-cap and connector fields are omitted. The assigned lens-cap and connector charges obey their permitted nonnegative ranges and the total ","sourceOffset":380958,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":548030,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":37,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"307-01","slug":"Failure-of-rational-injectivity-for-maximal-coarse-assembly-October-5-2026","familyId":"307","title":"Failure of rational injectivity for maximal coarse assembly","abstract":"We construct a uniformly discrete bounded-geometry space whose maximal coarse assembly map has an infinite-order element in its kernel. The space is a coarse disjoint union of finite connected graphs of uniformly bounded degree, so maximal coarse assembly need not be rationally injective even for such graph unions.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a uniformly discrete bounded-geometry space whose maximal coarse assembly map is not rationally injective. The example is a coarse disjoint union of finite connected graphs of uniformly bounded degree, with an infinite-order kernel class. A companion gives the analogous failure for reduced coarse assembly, disproving the rational coarse Novikov conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/Failure-of-rational-injectivity-for-maximal-coarse-assembly-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Failure-of-rational-injectivity-for-maximal-coarse-assembly-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Failure-of-rational-injectivity-for-maximal-coarse-assembly-October-5-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/Failure-of-rational-injectivity-for-maximal-coarse-assembly-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Failure-of-rational-injectivity-for-maximal-coarse-assembly-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Failure-of-rational-injectivity-for-maximal-coarse-assembly-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Failure-of-rational-injectivity-for-maximal-coarse-assembly-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-geometry.tex","preprints/Failure-of-rational-injectivity-for-maximal-coarse-assembly-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-persistence.tex","preprints/Failure-of-rational-injectivity-for-maximal-coarse-assembly-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-maximal-index.tex","preprints/Failure-of-rational-injectivity-for-maximal-coarse-assembly-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-transfer.tex","preprints/Failure-of-rational-injectivity-for-maximal-coarse-assembly-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-sliding.tex","preprints/Failure-of-rational-injectivity-for-maximal-coarse-assembly-October-5-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Failure-of-rational-injectivity-for-maximal-coarse-assembly-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a uniformly discrete bounded-geometry space whose maximal coarse assembly map has an infinite-order element in its kernel. The space is a coarse disjoint union of finite connected graphs of uniformly bounded degree, so maximal coarse assembly need not be rationally injective even for such graph unions.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":362},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and prior work","sourceOffset":3005},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and the proof","sourceOffset":7133},{"kind":"section","title":"Congruence fibers over discrete tori","sourceOffset":10341},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite quotients and their twists","sourceOffset":10724},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two ends and the central collar","sourceOffset":13868},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coarse disjoint unions","sourceOffset":17097},{"kind":"section","title":"An integral class that persists at every scale","sourceOffset":18959},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The classes on the two ends","sourceOffset":19995},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Retractions to the tori","sourceOffset":22367},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The congruence calculation","sourceOffset":24106},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From line-bundle roots to divisibility in \\texorpdfstring{$K^1$}{K1}","sourceOffset":29641}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist finite connected graphs $(X_j)_{j\\geq1}$ with a common finite bound on vertex degree, a coarse disjoint union $X=\\bigsqcup_{j\\geq1}X_j$, and an infinite-order class $\\alpha\\in\\KX_1(X)$ such that \\[ \\mu_X^{\\max}(\\alpha)=0. \\] Consequently, $\\mu_X^{\\max}\\otimes\\Q$ is not injective.","sourceOffset":2171,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"top:infinite-order","text":"The class $\\alpha\\in\\KX_1(X)$ defined in \\eqref{top:alpha} has infinite order. In particular, $\\alpha\\otimes1\\neq0$ in $\\KX_1(X)\\otimes\\mathbb Q$.","sourceOffset":34119,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/307.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":75778,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Failure-of-rational-injectivity-for-maximal-coarse-assembly-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"307-02","slug":"A-counterexample-to-the-coarse-Novikov-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"307","title":"A counterexample to the coarse Novikov conjecture","abstract":"We disprove the coarse Novikov conjecture: ordinary coarse assembly need not be rationally injective for uniformly discrete spaces of bounded geometry. We construct a coarse disjoint union of finite graphs of uniformly bounded degree and an infinite-order class in its degree-one coarse K-homology whose image under ordinary coarse assembly in the K-theory of the reduced, locally compact Roe algebra vanishes.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a uniformly discrete bounded-geometry space whose maximal coarse assembly map is not rationally injective. The example is a coarse disjoint union of finite connected graphs of uniformly bounded degree, with an infinite-order kernel class. A companion gives the analogous failure for reduced coarse assembly, disproving the rational coarse Novikov conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-coarse-Novikov-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-coarse-Novikov-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-coarse-Novikov-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-coarse-Novikov-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-coarse-Novikov-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-coarse-Novikov-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-conventions.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-coarse-Novikov-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-geometry.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-coarse-Novikov-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/disc.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-coarse-Novikov-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-persistence.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-coarse-Novikov-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-transfer.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-coarse-Novikov-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-vanishing.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-coarse-Novikov-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove the coarse Novikov conjecture: ordinary coarse assembly need not be rationally injective for uniformly discrete spaces of bounded geometry. We construct a coarse disjoint union of finite graphs of uniformly bounded degree and an infinite-order class in its degree-one coarse K-homology whose image under ordinary coarse assembly in the K-theory of the reduced, locally compact Roe algebra vanishes.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":484},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Predecessors and the scope of the obstruction","sourceOffset":3581},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the construction works","sourceOffset":6192},{"kind":"section","title":"Coarse assembly and locally finite classes","sourceOffset":8907},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rips complexes and their coarse structure","sourceOffset":9410},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Roe algebras and naturality","sourceOffset":12975},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Families and winding pairings","sourceOffset":14657},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite discs with congruence fibers","sourceOffset":16330},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Congruence quotients and a separated holonomy","sourceOffset":16780},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The base discs and the lifted edges","sourceOffset":19743},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Separated unions and their boundary maps","sourceOffset":24259},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Short loops have zero charge far out","sourceOffset":26084}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a uniformly discrete bounded-geometry metric space $X$, which is a coarse disjoint union of finite connected graphs of uniformly bounded degree, and an infinite-order element $\\alpha\\in KX_1(X)$ such that \\[ \\mu_X(\\alpha)=0\\quad\\text{in }K_1(C^*(X)). \\] Consequently, ordinary coarse assembly for $X$ fails to be injective both integrally and after tensoring its domain and target with $\\Q$.","sourceOffset":3132,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:persistence","text":"For every integer $r\\ge1$ there is a homomorphism \\[ \\lambda_r:K_1^{\\lf}(P_r(X))\\longrightarrow\\Z \\quad\\text{such that}\\quad \\lambda_r(\\alpha_r)=1. \\] Consequently, every nonzero integer multiple of $\\alpha$ is nonzero in $KX_1(X)$.","sourceOffset":29668,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/307.md","scopeText":"The coarse Novikov conjecture predicts rational injectivity of the ordinary coarse assembly map for uniformly discrete spaces of bounded geometry. The formalization constructs a counterexample from a coarse disjoint union of finite connected graphs with uniformly bounded degree. Its degree-one coarse $K$-homology contains an infinite-order class whose image under ordinary coarse assembly into the reduced locally compact Roe algebra vanishes. The class remains nonzero after tensoring with $\\mathbb Q$, so rational injectivity fails.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/307.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":44084,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-counterexample-to-the-coarse-Novikov-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"308-01","slug":"Finite-Smith-Toda-Complexes-at-Varying-Primes-September-23-2026","familyId":"308","title":"Finite Smith–Toda Complexes at Varying Primes","abstract":"For every nonnegative integer n, we construct a Smith–Toda complex $V(n)$ at some prime p depending on n. It is a finite p-local spectrum whose Brown–Peterson homology is $\\mathrm{BP}_*/(p,v_1,\\ldots,v_n)$ with its canonical comodule structure and generator in degree zero. Each listed generator is killed to its first power.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every n ≥ 0, constructs a finite Smith–Toda spectrum at a prime p depending on n, with Brown–Peterson homology $BP_*/(p,v_1,\\ldots,v_n)$ and the canonical comodule structure. Thus Smith–Toda complexes exist at every height when the prime may vary; an explicit example realizes $V(4)$ at p = 1009.","pdfPath":"preprints/Finite-Smith-Toda-Complexes-at-Varying-Primes-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-Smith-Toda-Complexes-at-Varying-Primes-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-Smith-Toda-Complexes-at-Varying-Primes-September-23-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/Finite-Smith-Toda-Complexes-at-Varying-Primes-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Finite-Smith-Toda-Complexes-at-Varying-Primes-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Finite-Smith-Toda-Complexes-at-Varying-Primes-September-23-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Finite-Smith-Toda-Complexes-at-Varying-Primes-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-foundations.tex","preprints/Finite-Smith-Toda-Complexes-at-Varying-Primes-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-uniform-power.tex","preprints/Finite-Smith-Toda-Complexes-at-Varying-Primes-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-layer.tex","preprints/Finite-Smith-Toda-Complexes-at-Varying-Primes-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-universal.tex","preprints/Finite-Smith-Toda-Complexes-at-Varying-Primes-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-connectivity.tex","preprints/Finite-Smith-Toda-Complexes-at-Varying-Primes-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-normalization.tex","preprints/Finite-Smith-Toda-Complexes-at-Varying-Primes-September-23-2026/build/figures/towers.tex","preprints/Finite-Smith-Toda-Complexes-at-Varying-Primes-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-cobordism.tex","preprints/Finite-Smith-Toda-Complexes-at-Varying-Primes-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-realization.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Finite-Smith-Toda-Complexes-at-Varying-Primes-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every nonnegative integer $n$, we construct a Smith--Toda complex $V(n)$ at some prime $p$ depending on $n$. It is a finite $p$-local spectrum whose Brown--Peterson homology is $\\BP_*/(p,v_1,\\ldots,v_n)$ with its canonical comodule structure and generator in degree zero. Each listed generator is killed to its first power.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":390},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and earlier methods","sourceOffset":2231},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Construction and Detection","sourceOffset":6212},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":10718},{"kind":"section","title":"Ultraproducts, gradings, and finite cells","sourceOffset":11349},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The ordinary categorical ultraproduct","sourceOffset":12526},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Rational algebra models and specified nullhomotopies","sourceOffset":16810},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coherent sphere gradings","sourceOffset":21953},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Normalized mapping objects","sourceOffset":25539},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite-cell comparison and the objects \\texorpdfstring{$Y_j$}{Yj}","sourceOffset":28664},{"kind":"section","title":"Uniform localization of permutation powers","sourceOffset":34445},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Euler-graded mapping algebra","sourceOffset":42924}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $n\\geq0$, there exist a prime $p$ and a finite $p$-local spectrum $X$ such that \\[ \\BP_*X\\cong\\BP_*/(p,v_1,\\ldots,v_n) \\] as graded $\\BP_*\\BP$-comodules, with the canonical quotient coaction and generator in degree zero.","sourceOffset":1587,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"found:ultraproduct","text":"The category $\\cC$ is stable and symmetric monoidal, with finite stable constructions and tensor products computed primewise. If $X=[(X_p)]$ and $Y=[(Y_p)]$, then \\begin{equation} \\map_{\\cC}(X,Y) \\simeq\\colim_{U\\in\\U}\\prod_{p\\in U} \\map_{\\Sp_{(p)}}(X_p,Y_p). \\end{equation} Every mapping spectrum is rational, and the endomorphism algebra of the tensor unit is $HD$. The ring $D$ contains $\\Q$, and $\\varpi$ is a nonzerodivisor. Objects, arrows, and homotopies forming a diagram indexed by a finite simplicial set can be represented simultaneously on a large set of primes. The same holds for specified finite cofiber diagrams. Sequence objects become compact in $\\Ind(\\cC)$.","sourceOffset":13315,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"universal:coefficients","text":"The graded algebra underlying $R^a$ is the free graded-commutative $D$-algebra on variables \\[ z_{j,S},\\qquad 0\\leq j\\leq a,\\quad S\\subseteq[j), \\qquad |z_{j,S}|_{\\mathrm{chain}}=-|S|, \\qquad \\wt(z_{j,S})=\\gamma_{j,S} :=w_j-\\sum_{h\\in S}w_h. \\] Set \\[ b_j^u=\\sum_{S\\subseteq[j)}z_{j,S}e_S, \\qquad \\partial e_j=b_j^u. \\] The differential on $R^a$ is determined by $\\partial b_j^u=0$, with coefficients compared in the exterior basis. Define \\[ K_-^a=R^a[e_j:j<a],\\qquad K^a=R^a[e_j:j\\leq a]. \\] The superscript $u$ indicates these universal cycles.","sourceOffset":70891,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":182023,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Finite-Smith-Toda-Complexes-at-Varying-Primes-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"308-02","slug":"A-finite-Smith-Toda-complex-V4-at-the-prime-1009-September-23-2026","familyId":"308","title":"A finite Smith–Toda complex V(4) at the prime 1009","abstract":"We construct a Smith–Toda complex $V(4)$ at the prime 1009. It is an ordinary finite 1009-local spectrum whose Brown–Peterson homology is $BP_*/(1009,v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4)$, with its canonical comodule structure and generator in degree zero.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For every n ≥ 0, constructs a finite Smith–Toda spectrum at a prime p depending on n, with Brown–Peterson homology $BP_*/(p,v_1,\\ldots,v_n)$ and the canonical comodule structure. Thus Smith–Toda complexes exist at every height when the prime may vary; an explicit example realizes $V(4)$ at p = 1009.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-finite-Smith-Toda-complex-V4-at-the-prime-1009-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-finite-Smith-Toda-complex-V4-at-the-prime-1009-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-finite-Smith-Toda-complex-V4-at-the-prime-1009-September-23-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/A-finite-Smith-Toda-complex-V4-at-the-prime-1009-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-finite-Smith-Toda-complex-V4-at-the-prime-1009-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/A-finite-Smith-Toda-complex-V4-at-the-prime-1009-September-23-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/A-finite-Smith-Toda-complex-V4-at-the-prime-1009-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-framework.tex","preprints/A-finite-Smith-Toda-complex-V4-at-the-prime-1009-September-23-2026/build/sections/02a-cobar-preliminaries.tex","preprints/A-finite-Smith-Toda-complex-V4-at-the-prime-1009-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-lower-complexes.tex","preprints/A-finite-Smith-Toda-complex-V4-at-the-prime-1009-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-may-and-first-obstruction.tex","preprints/A-finite-Smith-Toda-complex-V4-at-the-prime-1009-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-frobenius-elimination.tex","preprints/A-finite-Smith-Toda-complex-V4-at-the-prime-1009-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-final-obstruction.tex","preprints/A-finite-Smith-Toda-complex-V4-at-the-prime-1009-September-23-2026/build/sections/appendix-resolution.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-finite-Smith-Toda-complex-V4-at-the-prime-1009-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a Smith--Toda complex $V(4)$ at the prime $1009$. It is an ordinary finite $1009$-local spectrum whose Brown--Peterson homology is $BP_*/(1009,v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4)$, with its canonical comodule structure and generator in degree zero.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":316},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Methods and proof strategy","sourceOffset":3608},{"kind":"section","title":"Brown--Peterson coordinates and the Adams--Novikov resolution","sourceOffset":8811},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coordinates and additive ranges","sourceOffset":10478},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Convergence, pairings, and the edge","sourceOffset":16976},{"kind":"section","title":"Filtered cobar preliminaries","sourceOffset":21243},{"kind":"section","title":"The unital complexes through height three","sourceOffset":26636},{"kind":"section","title":"The height-four obstruction calculation","sourceOffset":38977},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Possible differential targets","sourceOffset":39157},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The first obstruction","sourceOffset":41441},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The remaining targets","sourceOffset":52877},{"kind":"section","title":"Eliminating two obstruction degrees by Frobenius","sourceOffset":59089}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"intro:main","text":"At $p=1009$ there is a finite $p$-local spectrum $X$ and an isomorphism \\[ BP_*X\\ \\cong\\ BP_*/(p,v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4) \\] of graded $BP_*BP$-comodules, with the canonical quotient comodule on the right and its generator in degree zero.","sourceOffset":2589,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"All later obstruction candidates","label":"may:remaining-list","text":"For $k\\geq2$ the complete initial May page in \\eqref{may:obstruction-degrees} is Table \\ref{may:remaining-table}; all other values of $k$ give an empty page. Put $N_0=(p-1)^2$ and $N=p(p-1)-2$. \\begin{table}[htbp] \\centering \\begin{tabular}{c l} \\toprule $k$ & Initial May monomials\\\\ \\midrule $2$ & $xac B A^{p-2}C^{p-2}$, $\\quad xacyz A^{p-2}C^{p-2}$\\\\[2pt] $p-1$ & $y B^3 A^{N_0-3}$, $\\quad b B^2 A^{N_0-2}$, $\\quad ybz B A^{N_0-2}$\\\\[2pt] $p$ & $cyb A^{N+1}$, $\\quad ayb B A^N$\\\\ \\bottomrule \\end{tabular} \\caption{Every initial May monomial for the later $v_4$ obstruction bidegrees.} \\end{table}","sourceOffset":52913,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":135706,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-finite-Smith-Toda-complex-V4-at-the-prime-1009-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-finite-Smith-Toda-complex-V4-at-the-prime-1009-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"309-01","slug":"The-Kervaire-Invariant-Problem-at-the-Prime-Three-September-24-2026","familyId":"309","title":"The Kervaire invariant problem at the prime three","abstract":"We solve the Kervaire invariant problem at the prime three for the standard Kervaire classes in the mod-three Adams spectral sequence. These classes survive precisely at indices 0, 2, and 3, and each surviving detection coset contains an element of additive order three. In particular, there is an order-three Kervaire element in stem 322.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the odd-primary Kervaire invariant problem at the prime three: exactly the standard classes with indices 0, 2, and 3 survive in the mod-three Adams spectral sequence, in stems 10, 106, and 322. Each surviving detection coset contains an element of exact additive order three.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Kervaire-Invariant-Problem-at-the-Prime-Three-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Kervaire-Invariant-Problem-at-the-Prime-Three-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Kervaire-Invariant-Problem-at-the-Prime-Three-September-24-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/The-Kervaire-Invariant-Problem-at-the-Prime-Three-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Kervaire-Invariant-Problem-at-the-Prime-Three-September-24-2026/build/pdf-glyphs.tex","preprints/The-Kervaire-Invariant-Problem-at-the-Prime-Three-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-Kervaire-Invariant-Problem-at-the-Prime-Three-September-24-2026/build/sections/detector.tex","preprints/The-Kervaire-Invariant-Problem-at-the-Prime-Three-September-24-2026/build/sections/coefficients.tex","preprints/The-Kervaire-Invariant-Problem-at-the-Prime-Three-September-24-2026/build/sections/subgroup.tex","preprints/The-Kervaire-Invariant-Problem-at-the-Prime-Three-September-24-2026/build/sections/ascent.tex","preprints/The-Kervaire-Invariant-Problem-at-the-Prime-Three-September-24-2026/build/figures/ascent-forcing.tex","preprints/The-Kervaire-Invariant-Problem-at-the-Prime-Three-September-24-2026/build/sections/cube.tex","preprints/The-Kervaire-Invariant-Problem-at-the-Prime-Three-September-24-2026/build/sections/finite.tex","preprints/The-Kervaire-Invariant-Problem-at-the-Prime-Three-September-24-2026/build/sections/recurrence.tex","preprints/The-Kervaire-Invariant-Problem-at-the-Prime-Three-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Kervaire-Invariant-Problem-at-the-Prime-Three-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We solve the Kervaire invariant problem at the prime three for the standard Kervaire classes in the mod-three Adams spectral sequence. These classes survive precisely at indices $0$, $2$, and $3$, and each surviving detection coset contains an element of additive order three. In particular, there is an order-three Kervaire element in stem $322$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":394},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Unstable consequences","sourceOffset":2347},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work and the prime-three obstruction","sourceOffset":6166},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ideas and proof structure","sourceOffset":11530},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reduction to the main arguments","sourceOffset":15515},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and organization","sourceOffset":17241},{"kind":"section","title":"The detector and a finite Euler bound","sourceOffset":18134},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The action and grading conventions","sourceOffset":18579},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison of filtrations","sourceOffset":21688},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Adams--Novikov two-line in the detector degree","sourceOffset":25964},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Integral CM detection and the actual image","sourceOffset":31980},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The full-gap assertion and its torsion-free repair","sourceOffset":38209}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:classification","text":"At the prime three, \\[ K=K_3=\\{0,2,3\\}. \\] Thus the surviving classes occur in stems $10$, $106$, and $322$, and each admits an additive-order-three representative. The class $b_1$ and every $b_j$ with $j\\geq4$ fail to survive.","sourceOffset":1584,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"intro:unstable-consequences","text":"\\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item There are homotopy equivalences of H-spaces \\[ \\Omega S^{163}\\{3\\}\\simeq T^{163}(3)\\times\\Omega T^{487}(3), \\qquad BW_{81}\\simeq\\Omega T^{487}(3). \\] Consequently, $W_{81}\\simeq\\Omega^2T^{487}(3)$. \\item For every integer $n>1$, the underlying space $\\Omega S^{2n+1}\\{3\\}$ has a nontrivial product decomposition if and only if $n\\in\\{3,27,81\\}$. For every other $n>1$ it is mod-three atomic, hence indecomposable. Here a product is nontrivial when both factors are noncontractible. \\item For every integer $n>1$, the homotopy type $T^{2n+1}(3)$ admits a homotopy-associative H-space multiplication if and only if $n\\in\\{3,27,81\\}$. The multiplication on $T^{163}(3)$ supplied by the displayed product is homotopy associative and homotopy commutative. For this multiplication, the third-power map on $T^{163}(3)$ is null-homotopic, as is the third-power map on the loop H-space $\\Omega T^{487}(3)$. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":3112,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":312785,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":24,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Kervaire-Invariant-Problem-at-the-Prime-Three-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"310-01","slug":"Rational-homology-and-Quillens-conjecture-September-24-2026","familyId":"310","title":"Rational homology and Quillen's conjecture","abstract":"We prove Quillen's conjecture for all finite groups and all primes. More precisely, if a finite group G has trivial largest normal p-subgroup $O_p(G)$, then the poset of nontrivial elementary abelian p-subgroups of G has nonzero augmented reduced rational homology. This establishes the stronger rational-homology form of the conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the rational-homology form of Quillen's conjecture for every finite group and every prime. If the largest normal p-subgroup of G is trivial, the poset of nontrivial elementary abelian p-subgroups has nonzero augmented reduced rational homology and is therefore not contractible.","pdfPath":"preprints/Rational-homology-and-Quillens-conjecture-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Rational-homology-and-Quillens-conjecture-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Rational-homology-and-Quillens-conjecture-September-24-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/Rational-homology-and-Quillens-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Rational-homology-and-Quillens-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","preprints/Rational-homology-and-Quillens-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Rational-homology-and-Quillens-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-reductions.tex","preprints/Rational-homology-and-Quillens-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-propagation.tex","preprints/Rational-homology-and-Quillens-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-frames.tex","preprints/Rational-homology-and-Quillens-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-chain-procedures.tex","preprints/Rational-homology-and-Quillens-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/figures/fixed-complement.tex","preprints/Rational-homology-and-Quillens-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-linear.tex","preprints/Rational-homology-and-Quillens-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-symplectic.tex","preprints/Rational-homology-and-Quillens-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-orthogonal.tex","preprints/Rational-homology-and-Quillens-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-exceptional.tex","preprints/Rational-homology-and-Quillens-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Rational-homology-and-Quillens-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Quillen's conjecture for all finite groups and all primes. More precisely, if a finite group $G$ has trivial largest normal $p$-subgroup $O_p(G)$, then the poset of nontrivial elementary abelian $p$-subgroups of $G$ has nonzero augmented reduced rational homology. This establishes the stronger rational-homology form of the conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":408},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and the reduction theorems","sourceOffset":10583},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Posets, chains, and elementary rank","sourceOffset":10878},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Components and automorphisms","sourceOffset":12971},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The established reductions","sourceOffset":14623},{"kind":"section","title":"Propagation from a faithful component extension","sourceOffset":19057},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Centralizers and faithful configurations","sourceOffset":20173},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Deletion and augmented chains","sourceOffset":23922},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Products of cycles","sourceOffset":27647},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The propagation lemma","sourceOffset":31369},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Choosing a configuration of maximal rank","sourceOffset":37190},{"kind":"section","title":"Cycles from frames and a dimension estimate","sourceOffset":39683}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every finite group $G$ and every prime $p$, if $\\Op(G)=1$, then \\[ \\widetilde H_*(\\Ap(G);\\Q)\\ne0. \\] Here reduced homology is augmented, so that the empty poset has $\\widetilde H_{-1}(\\varnothing;\\Q)=\\Q$.","sourceOffset":1075,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Reduction at two","label":"thm:reduction-two","text":"Let $G$ be a minimal-order counterexample to \\eqref{eq:HQC} for $p=2$. Then $G$ has a simple component $L$ in the following list, where the field parameter $q$ has characteristic at least five: \\[ \\begin{array}{ll} \\PSL_n(q),\\ \\PSU_n(q)& n\\geq4,\\\\ \\PSp_{2n}(q)& n\\geq3,\\\\ \\Omega_{2n+1}(q)& n\\geq2,\\\\ \\mathrm P\\Omega_{2n}^{\\pm}(q)& n\\geq4. \\end{array} \\]","sourceOffset":14664,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:faithful-configuration","text":"Fix a finite group $G$, a prime $p$, and a nonabelian simple component $L$ of $G$. A faithful configuration for $L$ in $G$ is a subgroup $A\\in\\Ap(G)$ such that \\[ A\\le N_G(L),\\qquad A\\cap L\\ne1,\\qquad C_{LA}(L)=1,\\qquad \\Op(C_G(LA))=1. \\] We write $H=LA$. The condition $C_H(L)=1$ says that the conjugation homomorphism $H\\longrightarrow\\Aut(L)$ is injective. We may therefore identify $L\\le H\\le\\Aut(L)$ using this homomorphism.","sourceOffset":21621,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":215961,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Rational-homology-and-Quillens-conjecture-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Rational-homology-and-Quillens-conjecture-September-24-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"311-01","slug":"Stabilizer-Orbits-and-Thick-Tensor-Ideals-of-Dualizable-Kn-Local-Spectra-September-24-2026","familyId":"311","title":"Stabilizer orbits and thick tensor ideals of dualizable K(n)-local spectra","abstract":"We classify the prime and radical ideals of the Lubin–Tate deformation ring invariant under an open Morava stabilizer subgroup, at every prime and positive height. This proves Chai's invariant-ideal conjecture in the standard finite-residue-field formulation. It also proves the Hovey–Strickland conjecture: the dualizable $K(n)$-local category has exactly $n+2$ thick tensor ideals, and its Balmer spectrum is a chain of $n+1$ points.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Chai's invariant-ideal conjecture for Lubin–Tate deformation rings over finite residue fields, at every prime and positive height n. Through the implication of Barthel–Heard–Naumann, this proves the Hovey–Strickland conjecture: dualizable $K(n)$-local spectra have exactly $n+2$ thick tensor ideals, and their Balmer spectrum is a chain of $n+1$ points.","pdfPath":"preprints/Stabilizer-Orbits-and-Thick-Tensor-Ideals-of-Dualizable-Kn-Local-Spectra-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Stabilizer-Orbits-and-Thick-Tensor-Ideals-of-Dualizable-Kn-Local-Spectra-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Stabilizer-Orbits-and-Thick-Tensor-Ideals-of-Dualizable-Kn-Local-Spectra-September-24-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/Stabilizer-Orbits-and-Thick-Tensor-Ideals-of-Dualizable-Kn-Local-Spectra-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Stabilizer-Orbits-and-Thick-Tensor-Ideals-of-Dualizable-Kn-Local-Spectra-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Stabilizer-Orbits-and-Thick-Tensor-Ideals-of-Dualizable-Kn-Local-Spectra-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-flow.tex","preprints/Stabilizer-Orbits-and-Thick-Tensor-Ideals-of-Dualizable-Kn-Local-Spectra-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-formal-closure.tex","preprints/Stabilizer-Orbits-and-Thick-Tensor-Ideals-of-Dualizable-Kn-Local-Spectra-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-bundles.tex","preprints/Stabilizer-Orbits-and-Thick-Tensor-Ideals-of-Dualizable-Kn-Local-Spectra-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-deformations.tex","preprints/Stabilizer-Orbits-and-Thick-Tensor-Ideals-of-Dualizable-Kn-Local-Spectra-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-orbits.tex","preprints/Stabilizer-Orbits-and-Thick-Tensor-Ideals-of-Dualizable-Kn-Local-Spectra-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-classification.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Stabilizer-Orbits-and-Thick-Tensor-Ideals-of-Dualizable-Kn-Local-Spectra-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We classify the prime and radical ideals of the Lubin--Tate deformation ring invariant under an open Morava stabilizer subgroup, at every prime and positive height. This proves Chai's invariant-ideal conjecture in the standard finite-residue-field formulation. It also proves the Hovey--Strickland conjecture: the dualizable \\(K(n)\\)-local category has exactly \\(n+2\\) thick tensor ideals, and its Balmer spectrum is a chain of \\(n+1\\) points.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":508},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the two classification problems","sourceOffset":4475},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The argument","sourceOffset":9253},{"kind":"section","title":"Frobenius separation and formal closure","sourceOffset":13780},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fields and Honda formal groups","sourceOffset":14136},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Separating coefficients","sourceOffset":16066},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reduced formal subgroups","sourceOffset":19583},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From an equation to an annihilator","sourceOffset":27255},{"kind":"section","title":"A nondegeneracy lemma from the Fargues--Fontaine curve","sourceOffset":30256},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Degree-one bundles and independent sections","sourceOffset":30828},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Honda groups as relative section sheaves","sourceOffset":33262},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Endomorphism orbits","sourceOffset":41880}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Hovey--Strickland conjecture","label":"thm:main","text":"For every prime \\(p\\) and every \\(n\\geq 1\\), the thick tensor ideals of \\(\\mathcal D_{(n,p)}\\) are exactly \\[ \\mathcal D_{(n,p)}=\\mathcal D_0 \\supsetneq\\mathcal D_1\\supsetneq\\cdots \\supsetneq\\mathcal D_n\\supsetneq\\mathcal D_{n+1}=0. \\] In particular, there are \\(n+2\\) such ideals, and each \\(\\mathcal D_k\\) is independent of the chosen finite type-\\(k\\) spectrum.","sourceOffset":3708,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Stabilizer-invariant ideals","label":"thm:invariant-radicals","text":"Let \\(U\\subseteq A_n^\\times\\) be any open subgroup. The proper prime ideals of \\(E_0\\) stabilized by \\(U\\) are precisely \\[ 0,\\ I_1,\\ldots,I_n. \\] The \\(U\\)-invariant radical ideals are precisely \\[ 0,\\ I_1,\\ldots,I_n,\\ E_0. \\] The latter list also gives the radical ideals invariant under the full canonical \\(G_n\\)-action.","sourceOffset":1709,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":112368,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Stabilizer-Orbits-and-Thick-Tensor-Ideals-of-Dualizable-Kn-Local-Spectra-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"312-01","slug":"The-Grothendieck-homotopy-hypothesis-via-elementary-expansions-September-24-2026","familyId":"312","title":"The Grothendieck homotopy hypothesis via elementary expansions","abstract":"We prove the Grothendieck homotopy hypothesis for every Grothendieck coherator in the Ara–Henry convention: its weak globular infinity-groupoids recover the homotopy theory of spaces. We also resolve Henry's pushout conjecture, showing that elementary expansions preserve components and all homotopy groups of cellular infinity-groupoids.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the Grothendieck homotopy hypothesis for ∞-groupoids associated with every Grothendieck coherator in the Ara–Henry convention: these algebraic objects recover the homotopy theory of spaces.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Grothendieck-homotopy-hypothesis-via-elementary-expansions-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Grothendieck-homotopy-hypothesis-via-elementary-expansions-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Grothendieck-homotopy-hypothesis-via-elementary-expansions-September-24-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/The-Grothendieck-homotopy-hypothesis-via-elementary-expansions-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Grothendieck-homotopy-hypothesis-via-elementary-expansions-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-Grothendieck-homotopy-hypothesis-via-elementary-expansions-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/The-Grothendieck-homotopy-hypothesis-via-elementary-expansions-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-preliminaries.tex","preprints/The-Grothendieck-homotopy-hypothesis-via-elementary-expansions-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-cutoffs-trees.tex","preprints/The-Grothendieck-homotopy-hypothesis-via-elementary-expansions-September-24-2026/build/figures/tree-string.tex","preprints/The-Grothendieck-homotopy-hypothesis-via-elementary-expansions-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-partial-cylinders.tex","preprints/The-Grothendieck-homotopy-hypothesis-via-elementary-expansions-September-24-2026/build/figures/joint-endpoints.tex","preprints/The-Grothendieck-homotopy-hypothesis-via-elementary-expansions-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Grothendieck-homotopy-hypothesis-via-elementary-expansions-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Grothendieck homotopy hypothesis for every Grothendieck coherator in the Ara--Henry convention: its weak globular infinity-groupoids recover the homotopy theory of spaces. We also resolve Henry's pushout conjecture, showing that elementary expansions preserve components and all homotopy groups of cellular infinity-groupoids.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":385},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof","sourceOffset":7015},{"kind":"section","title":"Globular models and the weak-equivalence test","sourceOffset":10075},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The fixed coherator and its free models","sourceOffset":10300},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Homotopies, division, and change of boundary","sourceOffset":17630},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exact-boundary criterion","sourceOffset":25215},{"kind":"section","title":"Coskeletal cutoffs and contractible trees","sourceOffset":31422},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The cutoff reflector","sourceOffset":31905},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Spines of globular sums","sourceOffset":37188},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The descending induction and its upper-cell consequence","sourceOffset":41783},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Trees in a fixed globular dimension","sourceOffset":46199},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Whiskers on globular sums","sourceOffset":53179}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Fix any Grothendieck coherator \\(\\mathcal C\\). Let \\(X\\) be a cellular \\(\\mathcal C\\)-infinity-groupoid, let \\(n\\ge0\\), and let \\(a:D_n\\to X\\) be any cell. In the pushout square \\[ \\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline=(current bounding box.center), node distance=1.25cm and 2.2cm,>=Stealth] \\node (a) {\\(D_n\\)}; \\node (b) [right=of a] {\\(D_{n+1}\\)}; \\node (c) [below=of a] {\\(X\\)}; \\node (d) at (b |- c) {\\(X^+\\)}; \\draw[->] (a) -- node[above] {\\(s_n\\)} (b); \\draw[->] (a) -- node[left] {\\(a\\)} (c); \\draw[->] (b) -- (d); \\draw[->] (c) -- node[below] {\\(i\\)} (d); \\end{tikzpicture} \\] where \\(s_n\\) is the source inclusion, the map \\(i:X\\to X^+\\) is a weak equivalence.","sourceOffset":3120,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Weak-equivalence test","label":"prop:weak-test","text":"For a model map $f:X\\to Y$, the following are equivalent. \\begin{enumerate} \\item $f\\in\\mathcal W$. \\item It induces a bijection on $\\pi_0$ and a bijection \\[ \\pi_r(X;u,v)\\longrightarrow\\pi_r(Y;fu,fv) \\] for every $r\\ge1$ and every parallel pair $u,v$ in $X$. \\item For every $r\\ge0$, every specified boundary $b:\\partial D_r\\to X$, and every $r$-cell $y$ of $Y$ with boundary $fb$, there are an $r$-cell $x$ with boundary exactly $b$ and an $(r+1)$-cell $fx\\to y$ in $Y$. \\end{enumerate} In particular, a weak equivalence reflects homotopy between parallel cells, including equality of components for vertices.","sourceOffset":25540,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"The coherator convention","label":"def:coherator","text":"A globular theory is a category under $\\Theta_0$, with the same objects and preserving the designated globular sums. A Grothendieck coherator $\\mathcal C$ is such a theory with the following two properties. \\begin{enumerate} \\item It admits a defining tower $\\mathcal C_0=\\Theta_0\\to\\mathcal C_1\\to\\cdots$ with $\\mathcal C\\cong\\colim_{r\\ge0}\\mathcal C_r$. At stage $r+1$, one freely adjoins a filler for each pair in a chosen set of parallel pairs $d_{j-1}\\rightrightarrows S$ in $\\mathcal C_r$ satisfying $j>0$ and $\\dim(S)\\le j$. The new arrow $d_j\\to S$ has that pair as its source and target. \\it","sourceOffset":11692,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:prelim-contractible","text":"A model $X$ is contractible if $X\\to1$ belongs to $\\mathcal W$. Equivalently, $X$ is nonempty and every parallel pair of cells has a joining cell.","sourceOffset":29062,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/312.md","scopeText":"The Grothendieck homotopy hypothesis asks whether algebraic $\\infty$-groupoids recover the homotopy theory of spaces. The formalized result is the elementary-expansion theorem used in this approach: for every Grothendieck coherator in the Ara–Henry convention and every boundary-cellular model, attaching an $(n+1)$-disk along its source $n$-face induces a weak equivalence.\n\nThe later semi-model structure and full comparison with the homotopy theory of spaces are not included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/312.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":91899,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Grothendieck-homotopy-hypothesis-via-elementary-expansions-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"313-01","slug":"Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026","familyId":"313","title":"Finite generation for the K(n)-local sphere","abstract":"We prove that the homotopy groups of the $K(n)$-local sphere are finitely generated ℤp-modules in every integer degree, for every prime p and positive height n. Equivalently, the same conclusion holds after $K(n)$-localizing any finite p-local spectrum. This answers the degreewise finiteness question of Hovey and Hovey–Strickland.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Answers the degreewise finiteness question of Hovey and Hovey–Strickland: every homotopy group of the $K(n)$-local sphere is a finitely generated ℤp-module, for every prime, positive height and integer degree. The same conclusion holds after $K(n)$-localizing any finite p-local spectrum.","pdfPath":"preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-history.tex","preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-continuous-cohomology.tex","preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-finite-markings.tex","preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-completed-tower.tex","preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-kummer-supports.tex","preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-independent-tuples.tex","preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026/build/figures/support-directions.tex","preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-natural-transfers.tex","preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-laurent-model.tex","preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-residue-cutoff.tex","preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-pro-transfer.tex","preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/11-coefficient-finiteness.tex","preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/12-sphere.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the homotopy groups of the $K(n)$-local sphere are finitely generated $\\mathbb Z_p$-modules in every integer degree, for every prime $p$ and positive height $n$. Equivalently, the same conclusion holds after $K(n)$-localizing any finite $p$-local spectrum. This answers the degreewise finiteness question of Hovey and Hovey--Strickland.","sections":[{"kind":"part","title":"The finiteness problem and continuous coefficients","sourceOffset":423},{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":507},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and relation to earlier work","sourceOffset":1883},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The coefficient theorem","sourceOffset":8567},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The open-stratum argument","sourceOffset":10608},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":16470},{"kind":"section","title":"Continuous coefficients and full-group duality","sourceOffset":17686},{"kind":"part","title":"Finite markings and the completed tower","sourceOffset":40592},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite markings and integral models","sourceOffset":40658},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The coefficient class","sourceOffset":42959},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Connected jets and whole finite models","sourceOffset":46167},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Generator lifts and roots","sourceOffset":55215}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every prime $p$, every integer $n\\geq 1$, and every integer $t$, the $\\Zp$-module \\[ \\pi_t L_{K(n)}S_p^\\wedge \\] is finitely generated. Equivalently, for every finite $p$-local spectrum $F$, the module $\\pi_t L_{K(n)}F$ is finitely generated over $\\Zp$ in every integer degree $t$.","sourceOffset":1112,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"A uniform finite-isotropy bound","label":null,"text":"\\label[proposition]{prop:cts-isotropy-bound} Fix a normal uniform open subgroup $K\\subset G$, and put $d_G=\\dim G$ and $a_G=[G:K]$. If $M$ is trace-admissible, then \\[ H^j(G,M)=0\\qquad(j>a_Gd_G). \\] The same bound holds for $M\\otimes_\\kk V$ for every finite-dimensional continuous $G$-representation $V$. Neither the bound nor the choice of $K$ depends on $M$ or $V$.","sourceOffset":30289,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Complete steps","label":null,"text":"\\label[definition]{def:cts-steps} A coefficient module with complete steps is a vector space $M=\\bigcup_{a\\geq 0}M_a$, where the $M_a$ are complete Hausdorff linearly topologized $\\kk$-spaces and the inclusions are continuous injections. The inclusions need not induce the topology on the smaller space. An action of $G$ is required to have the following property: for every $a$ there is a $b$ for which $G M_a\\subset M_b$ and $G\\times M_a\\longrightarrow M_b$ is continuous. Morphisms satisfy the analogous condition with no group variable. We use the bounded-step continuous cochains \\[ C^j_{\\cts}(G","sourceOffset":18609,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Trace elements","label":null,"text":"\\label[definition]{def:cts-trace} Let $A$ be a commutative $\\kk$-algebra with a $G$-action. A semilinear $A$-module $M$ with complete steps is trace-admissible if, for every finite subgroup $F\\subset G$, there is $t_F\\in A$ satisfying \\[ \\sum_{f\\in F}f(t_F)=1, \\] and multiplication by $t_F$ carries each complete step continuously into some complete step. The action is semilinear in the sense that $g(am)=g(a)g(m)$.","sourceOffset":26919,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":345685,"sourceFileCount":16,"statementCount":31,"definitionCount":4,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Finite-generation-for-the-Kn-local-sphere-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"314-01","slug":"Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026","familyId":"314","title":"Cyclic length and chromatic fixed-point loss","abstract":"For a finite p-group G and a subgroup H, we prove that the optimal chromatic fixed-point loss equals the shortest length of a subnormal chain from H to G with cyclic quotients. The equality holds at every prime and every nonnegative height, resolving positively the equality proposed by Kuhn and Lloyd.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Determines the optimal chromatic loss from geometric H-fixed points to geometric G-fixed points for every subgroup H of a finite p-group G. At every nonnegative height, the loss equals the shortest subnormal-chain length from H to G with cyclic quotients. Each quotient counts once regardless of order, and finite spectra witness sharpness.","pdfPath":"preprints/Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026/Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026/Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026/build/tools/unicode-map.tex","preprints/Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-background.tex","preprints/Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-characters.tex","preprints/Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-congruences.tex","preprints/Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-profinite.tex","preprints/Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-gluing.tex","preprints/Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-support.tex","preprints/Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For a finite \\(p\\)-group \\(G\\) and a subgroup \\(H\\), we prove that the optimal chromatic fixed-point loss equals the shortest length of a subnormal chain from \\(H\\) to \\(G\\) with cyclic quotients. The equality holds at every prime and every nonnegative height, resolving positively the equality proposed by Kuhn and Lloyd.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":368},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":2453},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof","sourceOffset":6108},{"kind":"section","title":"Chromatic and equivariant preliminaries","sourceOffset":9603},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Chromatic tests and finite localization","sourceOffset":10043},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Genuine fixed points and cofree spectra","sourceOffset":11473},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ambidexterity and Burnside marks","sourceOffset":13685},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prime points and finite truncation","sourceOffset":15626},{"kind":"section","title":"Rational characters from finite groups","sourceOffset":18374},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The rational tensor category and occurrence","sourceOffset":19080},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A maximizing tower","sourceOffset":26053},{"kind":"section","title":"Integration and subgroup congruences","sourceOffset":34046}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every prime \\(p\\), finite \\(p\\)-group \\(G\\), subgroup \\(H\\le G\\), and integer \\(n\\ge0\\), \\[ \\boxed{r_n(G,H)=\\ell(G,H).} \\]","sourceOffset":1455,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Cyclic upper bound","label":"prop:cyclic-upper","text":"For every finite \\(p\\)-group \\(G\\), subgroup \\(H\\), and \\(n\\ge0\\), \\(r_n(G,H)\\le\\ell(G,H)\\).","sourceOffset":12900,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":102684,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Cyclic-Length-and-Chromatic-Fixed-Point-Loss-September-24-2026/build/tools/unicode-map.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"315-01","slug":"The-Singer-conjecture-in-dimension-four-September-25-2026","familyId":"315","title":"The Singer conjecture in dimension four","abstract":"We prove the four-dimensional Singer conjecture: the L2-Betti numbers of the universal cover of a closed connected aspherical topological four-manifold vanish outside degree two. More generally, the same vanishing holds for finite connected aspherical integral Poincaré complexes of formal dimension four, including nonorientable ones.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that the L2-Betti numbers of the universal cover of every closed connected aspherical topological four-manifold vanish outside degree two. More generally, the same conclusion holds for every finite connected aspherical integral Poincaré complex of formal dimension four, proving the four-dimensional Singer conjecture in this wider class.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Singer-conjecture-in-dimension-four-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Singer-conjecture-in-dimension-four-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Singer-conjecture-in-dimension-four-September-25-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/The-Singer-conjecture-in-dimension-four-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Singer-conjecture-in-dimension-four-September-25-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/The-Singer-conjecture-in-dimension-four-September-25-2026/build/foundations.tex","preprints/The-Singer-conjecture-in-dimension-four-September-25-2026/build/compactification.tex","preprints/The-Singer-conjecture-in-dimension-four-September-25-2026/build/dynamics.tex","preprints/The-Singer-conjecture-in-dimension-four-September-25-2026/build/boundary-topology.tex","preprints/The-Singer-conjecture-in-dimension-four-September-25-2026/build/pretree.tex","preprints/The-Singer-conjecture-in-dimension-four-September-25-2026/build/blocks.tex","preprints/The-Singer-conjecture-in-dimension-four-September-25-2026/build/annuli.tex","preprints/The-Singer-conjecture-in-dimension-four-September-25-2026/build/path-pushing.tex","preprints/The-Singer-conjecture-in-dimension-four-September-25-2026/build/annular-chains.tex","preprints/The-Singer-conjecture-in-dimension-four-September-25-2026/build/conclusion.tex","preprints/The-Singer-conjecture-in-dimension-four-September-25-2026/build/consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Singer-conjecture-in-dimension-four-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the four-dimensional Singer conjecture: the $L^2$-Betti numbers of the universal cover of a closed connected aspherical topological four-manifold vanish outside degree two. More generally, the same vanishing holds for finite connected aspherical integral Poincar\\'e complexes of formal dimension four, including nonorientable ones.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":403},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":4484},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof in outline","sourceOffset":10440},{"kind":"section","title":"Topological and algebraic reductions","sourceOffset":14817},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The singular-chain definition and duality","sourceOffset":18349},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cohomology, ends, and splittings","sourceOffset":23882},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Hilbert space of finite-energy gradients","sourceOffset":29553},{"kind":"section","title":"A boundary from finite-energy functions","sourceOffset":32964},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A function with connected level sides","sourceOffset":33842},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Boxes with finitely many components","sourceOffset":38544},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coordinates which distinguish box components","sourceOffset":42709},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The compact space and its boundary","sourceOffset":44977}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $Q$ be a finite connected aspherical integral Poincar\\'e complex of formal dimension four, with arbitrary orientation character. Then \\[ b_p^{(2)}(\\widetilde Q)=0\\qquad(p\\ge0,\\ p\\ne2). \\]","sourceOffset":2221,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:singer-manifolds","text":"Let $M$ be a closed connected aspherical topological four-manifold. Then \\[ b_p^{(2)}(\\widetilde M)=0\\qquad(p\\ge0,\\ p\\ne2). \\]","sourceOffset":2461,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":154223,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Singer-conjecture-in-dimension-four-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"316-01","slug":"The-Stable-Hurewicz-Image-of-the-Sphere-at-Two-September-25-2026","familyId":"316","title":"The Stable Hurewicz Image of the Sphere at Two","abstract":"We prove Curtis's conjecture: in every positive degree, the mod-two stable Hurewicz image of the sphere is spanned by the images of η, ν, σ and the Kervaire-invariant-one classes that exist. Consequently, Eccles's conjecture holds for every sphere Sn with n > 0.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Curtis’s conjecture: the positive-degree mod-two stable Hurewicz image of the sphere is spanned by the images of the Hopf-invariant-one classes η, ν, σ and the Kervaire-invariant-one classes that exist.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Stable-Hurewicz-Image-of-the-Sphere-at-Two-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Stable-Hurewicz-Image-of-the-Sphere-at-Two-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Stable-Hurewicz-Image-of-the-Sphere-at-Two-September-25-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/The-Stable-Hurewicz-Image-of-the-Sphere-at-Two-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Stable-Hurewicz-Image-of-the-Sphere-at-Two-September-25-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-Stable-Hurewicz-Image-of-the-Sphere-at-Two-September-25-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/The-Stable-Hurewicz-Image-of-the-Sphere-at-Two-September-25-2026/build/figures/proof-route.tex","preprints/The-Stable-Hurewicz-Image-of-the-Sphere-at-Two-September-25-2026/build/weights.tex","preprints/The-Stable-Hurewicz-Image-of-the-Sphere-at-Two-September-25-2026/build/cones.tex","preprints/The-Stable-Hurewicz-Image-of-the-Sphere-at-Two-September-25-2026/build/powers.tex","preprints/The-Stable-Hurewicz-Image-of-the-Sphere-at-Two-September-25-2026/build/algebra.tex","preprints/The-Stable-Hurewicz-Image-of-the-Sphere-at-Two-September-25-2026/build/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Stable-Hurewicz-Image-of-the-Sphere-at-Two-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove Curtis's conjecture: in every positive degree, the mod-two stable Hurewicz image of the sphere is spanned by the images of \\(\\eta,\\nu,\\sigma\\) and the Kervaire-invariant-one classes that exist. Consequently, Eccles's conjecture holds for every sphere \\(S^n\\) with \\(n>0\\).","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":369},{"kind":"section","title":"Weights, primitives, and homology suspension","sourceOffset":12579},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A structured free-algebra model","sourceOffset":13672},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Suspension and the linear coproduct","sourceOffset":18520},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Steenrod modules in a primitive weight","sourceOffset":23114},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The weight filtration at level zero","sourceOffset":32416},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The suspension kernel and square roots of primitives","sourceOffset":39405},{"kind":"section","title":"Mapping-cone squares and a module extension","sourceOffset":44378},{"kind":"section","title":"Excluding fourth and higher powers","sourceOffset":55845},{"kind":"section","title":"An obstruction in Steenrod modules","sourceOffset":74768},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Milnor primitives and coinvariants","sourceOffset":77837},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cartier halving, with its exponent threshold","sourceOffset":82826}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:curtis","text":"The image of \\(\\bigoplus_{d>0}h_d\\) is spanned over \\(\\F_2\\) by the Hurewicz images of the Hopf-invariant-one classes \\(\\eta,\\nu,\\sigma\\), in degrees \\(1,3,7\\), and the Kervaire-invariant-one classes \\(\\theta_j\\), in degrees \\(2^{j+1}-2\\), \\(j\\ge1\\), whenever they exist. In particular, Curtis's conjecture is true in all positive degrees.","sourceOffset":1382,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Eccles's conjecture for spheres","label":"cor:eccles-spheres","text":"For every integer \\(n>0\\), each nonzero mod-2 spherical class in \\(H_*(QS^n;\\F_2)\\) is either \\(e_n\\), or a nonzero class of the form \\[ \\sigma_*^n h_d(\\alpha),\\qquad (\\alpha,d)\\in\\{(\\eta,1),(\\nu,3),(\\sigma,7)\\}. \\] Consequently Eccles's conjecture holds for every sphere \\(S^n\\), \\(n>0\\): the alternatives are the bottom stably spherical class and Hopf-invariant-one classes.","sourceOffset":2666,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":117270,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Stable-Hurewicz-Image-of-the-Sphere-at-Two-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"317-01","slug":"Thomason-Model-Structures-in-Every-Strict-Higher-Dimension-September-25-2026","familyId":"317","title":"Thomason Model Structures in Every Strict Higher Dimension","abstract":"We prove the higher-dimensional Thomason model-structure conjecture of Ara and Maltsiniotis. For every $1\\le n\\le\\infty$, the category of small strict globular n-categories admits a proper combinatorial model structure that is Quillen equivalent to simplicial sets. Its weak equivalences and fibrations are detected by the twice-extended Street nerve $\\mathrm{Ex}^2N_n$, and the Quillen equivalence is given by $c_n\\mathrm{Sd}^2\\dashv\\mathrm{Ex}^2N_n$. Thus strict higher categories model the homotopy theory of spaces in every positive finite dimension and in dimension ω.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the Ara–Maltsiniotis conjecture: for every n ≥ 1 and n = ω, small strict globular n-categories admit proper combinatorial Thomason model structures Quillen equivalent to simplicial sets. Thus strict higher categories model the homotopy theory of spaces in every stated dimension.","pdfPath":"preprints/Thomason-Model-Structures-in-Every-Strict-Higher-Dimension-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Thomason-Model-Structures-in-Every-Strict-Higher-Dimension-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Thomason-Model-Structures-in-Every-Strict-Higher-Dimension-September-25-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/Thomason-Model-Structures-in-Every-Strict-Higher-Dimension-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Thomason-Model-Structures-in-Every-Strict-Higher-Dimension-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Thomason-Model-Structures-in-Every-Strict-Higher-Dimension-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-foundations.tex","preprints/Thomason-Model-Structures-in-Every-Strict-Higher-Dimension-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-relative-prisms.tex","preprints/Thomason-Model-Structures-in-Every-Strict-Higher-Dimension-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-labelled-pieces.tex","preprints/Thomason-Model-Structures-in-Every-Strict-Higher-Dimension-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-couniversality.tex","preprints/Thomason-Model-Structures-in-Every-Strict-Higher-Dimension-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-subdivided-attachments.tex","preprints/Thomason-Model-Structures-in-Every-Strict-Higher-Dimension-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-model-structure.tex","preprints/Thomason-Model-Structures-in-Every-Strict-Higher-Dimension-September-25-2026/build/sections/09-presentability.tex","preprints/Thomason-Model-Structures-in-Every-Strict-Higher-Dimension-September-25-2026/build/sections/08-method-context.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Thomason-Model-Structures-in-Every-Strict-Higher-Dimension-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the higher-dimensional Thomason model-structure conjecture of Ara and Maltsiniotis. For every $1\\le n\\le\\infty$, the category of small strict globular $n$-categories admits a proper combinatorial model structure that is Quillen equivalent to simplicial sets. Its weak equivalences and fibrations are detected by the twice-extended Street nerve $\\Ex^2N_n$, and the Quillen equivalence is given by $c_n\\Sd^2\\dashv\\Ex^2N_n$. Thus strict higher categories model the homotopy theory of spaces in every positive finite dimension and in dimension $\\omega$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":621},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":2849},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Stationary cylinders and the pushout argument","sourceOffset":6431},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":10151},{"kind":"section","title":"Strict categories, directed chains, and simplicial gluing","sourceOffset":11042},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The directed tensor convention","sourceOffset":13945},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Right cylinders and truncation","sourceOffset":19645},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Alexander--Whitney map","sourceOffset":23487},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finiteness and gluing in simplicial sets","sourceOffset":27008},{"kind":"section","title":"Positive relative prisms","sourceOffset":34916},{"kind":"section","title":"Labelled pieces of algebraic pushouts","sourceOffset":47211},{"kind":"section","title":"Couniversality and the unit on posets","sourceOffset":62257}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $n\\in\\{1,2,3,\\ldots\\}\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$, the classes $(C_n,W_n,F_n)$ in \\eqref{eq:prescribed-classes} form a proper combinatorial model structure on $\\nCat$. It is cofibrantly generated by $(L_nI,L_nJ)$, where \\[ I=\\{\\partial\\Delta[m]\\hookrightarrow\\Delta[m]:m\\ge0\\},\\qquad J=\\{\\Lambda^k[m]\\hookrightarrow\\Delta[m]:m\\ge1,\\ 0\\le k\\le m\\}. \\] Moreover, \\[ L_n:\\sSet_{\\KQ}\\rightleftarrows(\\nCat,C_n,W_n,F_n):R_n \\] is a Quillen equivalence.","sourceOffset":2354,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Labelled attachments","label":"prop:labelled-pushout","text":"Let $A\\subset P$ be a full subposet, let $a:\\U{A}\\to X$ be any strict functor, and form \\[ Y=X\\amalg_{\\U{A}}\\U{P}. \\] Suppose a monotone map $\\lambda:P\\to[1]$ and a strict functor $\\xi:X\\to\\Disk{1}$ satisfy \\[ \\xi a=U(\\lambda|_A):\\U{A}\\longrightarrow\\Disk{1}, \\qquad \\U{[1]}=\\Disk{1}. \\] Write $P_\\epsilon=\\lambda^{-1}(\\epsilon)$, $A_\\epsilon=A\\cap P_\\epsilon$, and $X_\\epsilon=\\xi^{-1}(\\epsilon)$. Then the induced label $Y\\to\\Disk{1}$ has actual full fibers \\begin{equation} Y_\\epsilon\\cong X_\\epsilon\\amalg_{\\U{A_\\epsilon}}\\U{P_\\epsilon}. \\end{equation} Moreover, independently of these labels, \\begin{equation} \\operatorname{Ob}Y\\cong \\operatorname{Ob}X\\amalg_A P \\cong\\operatorname{Ob}X\\amalg(P\\setminus A). \\end{equation} For two such pairs of compatible labels, write $\\bar\\lambda_1,\\bar\\lambda_2:Y\\to\\Disk{1}$ for the induced labels. Their $0$-fibers intersect in the $0$-fiber of $\\max\\circ(\\bar\\lambda_1,\\bar\\lambda_2)$; their $1$-fibers intersect in the $1$-fiber of $\\min\\circ(\\bar\\lambda_1,\\bar\\lambda_2)$. Formula \\eqref{eq:pushout-fiber}, using the corresponding maximum or minimum on both original legs, computes these intersections as well.","sourceOffset":56092,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/317.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves the higher-dimensional Thomason model-structure theorem for small strict globular $n$-categories in every positive finite dimension and in dimension $\\omega$. Each category has the stated proper combinatorial model structure, Quillen equivalent to simplicial sets through the twice-subdivided categorification and twice-extended Street nerve adjunction.\n\nThe selected statements also identify weak equivalences by the Street nerve in all these dimensions. Thus the constructions model the homotopy theory of spaces throughout the stated range.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/317.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":121110,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":13,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Thomason-Model-Structures-in-Every-Strict-Higher-Dimension-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"318-01","slug":"Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026","familyId":"318","title":"Filtered chromatic splitting at generic primes","abstract":"For every n ≥ 1 and prime $p\\gt n+1$, we construct a $2^n$-stage ordered filtration of $L_{n-1}L_{K(n)}S_p^\\wedge$ with the classical chromatic-splitting cofibers. The map from the first stage to the target is the canonical localization unit.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves strong chromatic splitting at height three for primes p ≥ 5, and weak splitting for the derived p-completed sphere at heights p (p ≥ 5) and $p+1$ (p ≥ 7). Nevertheless, for n ≥ 1 and $p\\gt n+1$, the overlap $L_{n-1}L_{K(n)}S_p^\\wedge$ admits a $2^n$-stage filtration by the predicted localized-sphere pieces. At height three and prime three, even finite assembly from such pieces fails in the category of $E(2)$-local modules over the derived completed sphere.","pdfPath":"preprints/Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-assembly.tex","preprints/Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-rings.tex","preprints/Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-coefficient-scope.tex","preprints/Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-support.tex","preprints/Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026/build/sections/08-compact.tex","preprints/Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026/build/sections/09-boundary.tex","preprints/Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026/build/sections/10-full-frame.tex","preprints/Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-constants.tex","preprints/Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-generators.tex","preprints/Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026/build/sections/10-constant-module.tex","preprints/Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026/build/sections/11-spectral.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every \\(n\\geq1\\) and prime \\(p>n+1\\), we construct a \\(2^n\\)-stage ordered filtration of \\(L_{n-1}L_{K(n)}S_p^\\wedge\\) with the classical chromatic-splitting cofibers. The map from the first stage to the target is the canonical localization unit.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":324},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The theorem","sourceOffset":1169},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":4014},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and the ingredients of the proof","sourceOffset":10110},{"kind":"section","title":"A criterion for the filtration","sourceOffset":15704},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Localizations and scalar conventions","sourceOffset":16341},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The simultaneous basis criterion","sourceOffset":19246},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite frame rings and Cartier transfers","sourceOffset":25605},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Labeled rings before localization","sourceOffset":27877},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Connected markings and compatible lifts","sourceOffset":38990},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The dual distribution ring","sourceOffset":44784},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Powering and natural finite diagrams","sourceOffset":49889}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(n\\geq1\\) and let \\(p>n+1\\) be prime. There exist an enumeration \\(I_1,\\ldots,I_{2^n}\\) of the subsets of \\(\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\), a filtration \\(F_\\bullet\\), and a family of classes \\(y_i\\in\\pi_{1-2i}D\\), \\(1\\leq i\\leq n\\), with the following properties. \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\alph*)] \\item Filtration and unit. The enumeration has \\(I_1=\\varnothing\\) and \\(\\max I_j\\) nondecreasing for \\(j\\geq2\\). The filtration is \\[ 0=F_0\\longrightarrow F_1\\longrightarrow\\cdots \\longrightarrow F_{2^n}\\xrightarrow{\\simeq}X_{n,p} \\] with \\(\\operatorname{cofib}(F_{j-1}\\to F_j)\\simeq C_{I_j}\\), where \\begin{equation} \\begin{aligned} C_\\varnothing&=L_{n-1}S,\\\\ C_I&=\\Sigma^{-d(I)}L_{n-\\max I}S \\quad(I\\ne\\varnothing). \\end{aligned} \\end{equation} Under \\(F_1=C_\\varnothing\\), the composite \\(F_1\\to X_{n,p}\\) is the canonical localization unit. \\item Simultaneous product bases. For this one family, set \\(y_\\varnothing=1\\) and \\(y_I=y_{i_1}\\cdots y_{i_r}\\) for \\(I=\\{i_1<\\cdots<i_r\\}\\). For every \\(1\\leq t<n\\), the specified map \\begin{equation} L_{K(t)} \\left(\\bigvee_{I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n-t\\}} \\Sigma^{-d(I)}S\\xrightarrow{(y_I)}D\\right) \\quad\\text{is an equivalence}. \\end{equation} \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":1494,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Filtration criterion","label":"prop:assembly","text":"Let \\(n\\geq1\\), let \\(p\\) be odd, and let \\(D\\) be an \\(S\\)-module with a specified map \\(u:S\\to D\\). Suppose that maps \\[ z_I:\\Sigma^{-d(I)}S\\longrightarrow D, \\qquad I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n-1\\}, \\qquad z_\\varnothing=u, \\] have the following properties. \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\alph*)] \\item For each \\(1\\leq t<n\\), the map with the specified components \\begin{equation} \\bigvee_{I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n-t\\}}\\Sigma^{-d(I)}S \\xrightarrow{(z_I)} D \\end{equation} is a \\(K(t)\\)-equivalence. \\item For every integer \\(b\\), \\begin{equation} \\dim_{\\Qp}\\pi_{-b}L_0D =\\#\\{I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}:d(I)=b\\}. \\end{equation} If \\(n=1\\), assume in addition that \\(\\pi_0L_0u\\) is nonzero. \\end{enumerate} Then \\(X=L_{n-1}D\\) has the filtration and unit compatibility of Theorem \\ref{thm:main}(a), with \\(u\\) in place of its unit.","sourceOffset":19530,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Coefficient and cochain convention","label":"rings:bounded-cochains","text":"For a finite projective module over a finite frame algebra we use continuous cochains with bounded poles. On a compact profinite source this means that one power of $x$ places the entire image in a finite power-series lattice, where the cochain is continuous for the parameter-adic topology. For a union of frame, root, or associated-bundle coefficients, one common finite stage is required on each compact source. Equivalently the cochain complex of such a union is the filtered colimit of its finite-stage bounded-pole cochain complexes. These conventions also apply with an additional compact prof","sourceOffset":43699,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:compact:admissible","text":"An admissible compact coefficient on $A$ or $C_1$ is a finite free module with continuous $P_n$-action satisfying the following condition on every successive labeled height stratum. After restriction to its exact-height open, and after a finite joint frame change and a fixed finite Frobenius root change, the module has a finite $P_n$-stable filtration whose graded pieces admit $P_n$-equivariant identifications with finite direct sums of the coefficient ring, with $P_n$ acting only on that ring. We call these identifications constant frames; any commuting auxiliary frame actions are retained. T","sourceOffset":63175,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":397464,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":26,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Filtered-chromatic-splitting-at-generic-primes-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"318-02","slug":"The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026","familyId":"318","title":"The height-three chromatic overlap: an explicit filtration and its attachments","abstract":"For every prime p ≥ 5, we construct an explicit eight-stage filtration of $L_2L_{K(3)}\\mathbb S_p^\\wedge$ by the local-sphere layers in the height-three chromatic-splitting pattern. The map from its first stage to the overlap is the canonical unit, and two signed fracture formulas identify all attachments for the chosen local maps and compatibility homotopy. A companion canonical-map theorem further implies that the first height-one attachment is nonzero.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves strong chromatic splitting at height three for primes p ≥ 5, and weak splitting for the derived p-completed sphere at heights p (p ≥ 5) and $p+1$ (p ≥ 7). Nevertheless, for n ≥ 1 and $p\\gt n+1$, the overlap $L_{n-1}L_{K(n)}S_p^\\wedge$ admits a $2^n$-stage filtration by the predicted localized-sphere pieces. At height three and prime three, even finite assembly from such pieces fails in the category of $E(2)$-local modules over the derived completed sphere.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026/build/sections/02-fracture.tex","preprints/The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026/build/sections/03-framework.tex","preprints/The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026/build/sections/04-towers.tex","preprints/The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026/build/sections/05-analytic.tex","preprints/The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026/build/sections/06-coheight-one.tex","preprints/The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026/build/sections/07-descent.tex","preprints/The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026/build/sections/08-ordinary.tex","preprints/The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026/build/sections/09-integral.tex","preprints/The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026/build/sections/11-rational.tex","preprints/The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026/build/sections/12-application.tex","preprints/The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026/build/sections/12-attachment.tex","preprints/The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026/build/sections/10-rational-boundary.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every prime \\(p\\geq5\\), we construct an explicit eight-stage filtration of \\(L_2L_{K(3)}\\mathbb S_p^\\wedge\\) by the local-sphere layers in the height-three chromatic-splitting pattern. The map from its first stage to the overlap is the canonical unit, and two signed fracture formulas identify all attachments for the chosen local maps and compatibility homotopy. A companion canonical-map theorem further implies that the first height-one attachment is nonzero.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":529},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The explicit filtration","sourceOffset":3392},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Three blocks and two gluing problems","sourceOffset":5706},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why the localized bases require geometry","sourceOffset":7905},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reading the proof","sourceOffset":11422},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite fracture construction","sourceOffset":13285},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Module localizations and rotation signs","sourceOffset":13690},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The stages and their boundaries","sourceOffset":17892},{"kind":"section","title":"Coefficients, stabilizers, and comparison inputs","sourceOffset":29473},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Localizations and coefficient fields","sourceOffset":29986},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Stabilizers and reduced norm","sourceOffset":31758},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Continuous cochains and filtered coefficients","sourceOffset":36560}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Explicit height-three filtration","label":"thm:main","text":"For every prime \\(p\\geq5\\), there are \\(E(2)\\)-local \\(S\\)-modules and maps \\[ 0=F_0\\longrightarrow F_1\\longrightarrow\\cdots\\longrightarrow F_8, \\qquad e:F_8\\xrightarrow{\\simeq}X, \\] whose successive cofibers \\(C_i=\\cofib(F_{i-1}\\to F_i)\\), in order, are \\[ \\begin{array}{c|l@{\\qquad\\qquad}c|l} i&C_i&i&C_i\\\\ \\hline 1&L_2S&5&\\Sigma^{-5}H\\Qp\\\\ 2&\\Sigma^{-1}L_2S&6&\\Sigma^{-6}H\\Qp\\\\ 3&\\Sigma^{-3}L_1S&7&\\Sigma^{-8}H\\Qp\\\\ 4&\\Sigma^{-4}L_1S&8&\\Sigma^{-9}H\\Qp. \\end{array} \\] Under \\(F_1\\simeq L_2S\\), the composite \\(F_1\\to F_8\\xrightarrow e X\\) is the canonical map \\(L_2(S\\to L_{K(3)}S)\\).","sourceOffset":3661,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Ordered fracture filtration","label":"prop:fracture","text":"Let \\(X\\) be an \\(E(2)\\)-local \\(S\\)-module. Suppose the following module maps and homotopies are given. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item A map \\(w:W\\to X\\) for which \\(L_{K(2)}w\\) is an equivalence. Put \\(Y=\\cofib(w)\\), with quotient \\(q:X\\to Y\\). \\item An equivalence \\(\\kappa:L_{K(1)}U_{\\mathrm{mid}}\\xrightarrow{\\simeq}L_{K(1)}Y\\), a rational map \\(r:L_0U_{\\mathrm{mid}}\\to L_0Y\\), and a specified homotopy of \\(Q\\)-module maps between \\begin{equation} \\begin{gathered} L_0U_{\\mathrm{mid}}\\longrightarrow L_0L_{K(1)}U_{\\mathrm{mid}} \\xrightarrow{L_0\\kappa}L_0L_{K(1)}Y,\\\\ L_0U_{\\mathrm{mid}}\\xrightarrow rL_0Y\\longrightarrow L_0L_{K(1)}Y. \\end{gathered} \\end{equation} \\item A map \\(b:V\\to L_0X\\) for which \\begin{equation} t_0:V\\xrightarrow bL_0X\\xrightarrow{L_0q}L_0Y \\longrightarrow\\cofib(r) \\end{equation} is an equivalence. \\end{enumerate} Then there is a filtration in \\(E(2)\\)-local \\(S\\)-modules \\[ 0=F_0\\longrightarrow F_1\\longrightarrow\\cdots\\longrightarrow F_8 \\xrightarrow[\\simeq]{e}X \\] with ordered cofibers \\(W_1,W_2,U_3,U_4,V_5,V_6,V_7,V_8\\). The composite \\(F_2=W\\to F_8\\xrightarrow eX\\) is \\(w\\).","sourceOffset":18229,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":419945,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":26,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-height-three-chromatic-overlap-an-explicit-filtration-and-its-attachments-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"318-03","slug":"A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026","familyId":"318","title":"A rational obstruction to strong chromatic splitting at height three","abstract":"For every prime p ≥ 5, the canonical map $L_0L_{K(3)}S\\to L_0L_{K(2)}L_{K(3)}S$ for the sphere spectrum S is nonzero on π−3. Consequently, the height-three strong chromatic splitting formula is false in this range, even as an equivalence of underlying $E(2)$-local spectra without specified summand maps.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves strong chromatic splitting at height three for primes p ≥ 5, and weak splitting for the derived p-completed sphere at heights p (p ≥ 5) and $p+1$ (p ≥ 7). Nevertheless, for n ≥ 1 and $p\\gt n+1$, the overlap $L_{n-1}L_{K(n)}S_p^\\wedge$ admits a $2^n$-stage filtration by the predicted localized-sphere pieces. At height three and prime three, even finite assembly from such pieces fails in the category of $E(2)$-local modules over the derived completed sphere.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026/build/unicode-mappings.tex","preprints/A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-cohomology.tex","preprints/A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-completed-tower.tex","preprints/A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-modifications.tex","preprints/A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-degree-three.tex","preprints/A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-witt-transfer.tex","preprints/A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-detection.tex","preprints/A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/wedge-obstruction.tex","preprints/A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/08-elementary-background.tex","preprints/A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/09-frobenius.tex","preprints/A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/10-oriented-projection.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every prime \\(p\\ge5\\), the canonical map \\(L_0L_{K(3)}S\\to L_0L_{K(2)}L_{K(3)}S\\) for the sphere spectrum \\(S\\) is nonzero on \\(\\pi_{-3}\\). Consequently, the height-three strong chromatic splitting formula is false in this range, even as an equivalence of underlying \\(E(2)\\)-local spectra without specified summand maps.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":388},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why the canonical map matters","sourceOffset":2556},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A detecting spectrum","sourceOffset":5044},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Transporting the primitive","sourceOffset":8222},{"kind":"section","title":"Integral cohomology and degree-three group classes","sourceOffset":12923},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coefficients and continuous descent","sourceOffset":13360},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compact groups and the affine building","sourceOffset":30171},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The parabolic restriction","sourceOffset":37804},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The integral Drinfeld theorem","sourceOffset":42003},{"kind":"section","title":"The completed coheight-one tower","sourceOffset":44218},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The characteristic-\\(p\\) tower","sourceOffset":45184},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Acyclicity of constants","sourceOffset":50901}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every prime \\(p\\geq5\\), the canonical map \\begin{equation} \\tau_{L_{K(3)}S}:L_0L_{K(3)}S\\longrightarrow L_0L_{K(2)}L_{K(3)}S \\end{equation} is nonzero on \\(\\pi_{-3}\\).","sourceOffset":1907,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:strong","text":"For every prime \\(p\\geq5\\), there is no equivalence of \\(E(2)\\)-local spectra \\(L_2L_{K(3)}S\\simeq\\mathcal W_3\\), with \\(\\mathcal W_3\\) as in \\eqref{eq:proposed-wedge}. Thus the height-three strong chromatic splitting formula is false in this range, whether or not an equivalence is required to preserve its specified unit summand.","sourceOffset":2171,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":175036,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-rational-obstruction-to-strong-chromatic-splitting-at-height-three-September-25-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"318-04","slug":"Failure-of-finite-assembly-for-a-chromatic-overlap-at-the-prime-three-September-25-2026","familyId":"318","title":"Failure of finite assembly for a chromatic overlap at the prime three","abstract":"At the prime three and height three, the chromatic overlap cannot be constructed from the rational, height-one, and height-two local spheres by finitely many sums, shifts, cofibers, and retracts in the category of $E(2)$-local modules over the derived 3-complete sphere. This gives a negative answer to the ordinary finite-assembly question for chromatic overlaps.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves strong chromatic splitting at height three for primes p ≥ 5, and weak splitting for the derived p-completed sphere at heights p (p ≥ 5) and $p+1$ (p ≥ 7). Nevertheless, for n ≥ 1 and $p\\gt n+1$, the overlap $L_{n-1}L_{K(n)}S_p^\\wedge$ admits a $2^n$-stage filtration by the predicted localized-sphere pieces. At height three and prime three, even finite assembly from such pieces fails in the category of $E(2)$-local modules over the derived completed sphere.","pdfPath":"preprints/Failure-of-finite-assembly-for-a-chromatic-overlap-at-the-prime-three-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Failure-of-finite-assembly-for-a-chromatic-overlap-at-the-prime-three-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Failure-of-finite-assembly-for-a-chromatic-overlap-at-the-prime-three-September-25-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/Failure-of-finite-assembly-for-a-chromatic-overlap-at-the-prime-three-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Failure-of-finite-assembly-for-a-chromatic-overlap-at-the-prime-three-September-25-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/Failure-of-finite-assembly-for-a-chromatic-overlap-at-the-prime-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Failure-of-finite-assembly-for-a-chromatic-overlap-at-the-prime-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-detector.tex","preprints/Failure-of-finite-assembly-for-a-chromatic-overlap-at-the-prime-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-setup.tex","preprints/Failure-of-finite-assembly-for-a-chromatic-overlap-at-the-prime-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-towers.tex","preprints/Failure-of-finite-assembly-for-a-chromatic-overlap-at-the-prime-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-finite-resolutions.tex","preprints/Failure-of-finite-assembly-for-a-chromatic-overlap-at-the-prime-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-analytic.tex","preprints/Failure-of-finite-assembly-for-a-chromatic-overlap-at-the-prime-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-decompletion.tex","preprints/Failure-of-finite-assembly-for-a-chromatic-overlap-at-the-prime-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/08-homotopy.tex","preprints/Failure-of-finite-assembly-for-a-chromatic-overlap-at-the-prime-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/09-jets.tex","preprints/Failure-of-finite-assembly-for-a-chromatic-overlap-at-the-prime-three-September-25-2026/build/sections/10-obstruction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Failure-of-finite-assembly-for-a-chromatic-overlap-at-the-prime-three-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"At the prime three and height three, the chromatic overlap cannot be constructed from the rational, height-one, and height-two local spheres by finitely many sums, shifts, cofibers, and retracts in the category of \\(E(2)\\)-local modules over the derived 3-complete sphere. This gives a negative answer to the ordinary finite-assembly question for chromatic overlaps.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":484},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Splittings, finite constructions, and the prime three","sourceOffset":2711},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A detector that is stable under finite construction","sourceOffset":7504},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From finite markings to actual homotopy","sourceOffset":9977},{"kind":"section","title":"The finite-assembly detector","sourceOffset":17937},{"kind":"section","title":"Markings, coefficients, and conventions","sourceOffset":24181},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The one-parameter stratum","sourceOffset":26108},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The connected marking tower","sourceOffset":33294},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Continuous cochains at finite stages","sourceOffset":35777},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Geometric notation","sourceOffset":38132},{"kind":"section","title":"The marking towers and their determinant normalization","sourceOffset":39075},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite torsion algebras","sourceOffset":40409}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"At \\(p=n=3\\), with \\(S=S_3^\\wedge\\), \\[ L_2L_{K(3)}S \\notin\\Thick\\{L_0S,L_1S,L_2S\\} \\] in the category of \\(E(2)\\)-local \\(S\\)-modules.","sourceOffset":2247,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Constituents forced by finite assembly","label":"prop:constituent-test","text":"Suppose that \\(L_{K(2)}X\\) belongs to the ordinary thick subcategory generated by \\(\\mathbf 1_2\\) in \\(K(2)\\)-local spectra. Then, for every \\(d\\in\\mathbb Z\\), the representation \\(\\mathcal V_d(X)\\) is finite-dimensional over \\(Q_x\\), and each simple constituent is isomorphic to \\(\\overline\\omega^{\\otimes b}\\) for some \\(b\\in\\mathbb Z\\).","sourceOffset":21630,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Stage cochains","label":"def:stage-cochains","text":"For \\(Q=P,H,H'\\), or a closed subgroup of one of these groups, cochains with the incomplete coefficients \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) mean \\begin{align} C^s_{\\mathrm{cts}}(Q,L;T) &:=\\colim_U \\operatorname{Map}_{\\mathrm{cts}}(T\\times Q^s,L^U), \\nonumber\\\\ C^s_{\\mathrm{cts}}(Q,R;T) &:=\\colim_{U,e} \\operatorname{Map}_{\\mathrm{cts}} (T\\times Q^s,(L^U)^{1/p^e}). \\end{align} We also use the completed-stage complex \\begin{equation} \\colim_U C^\\bullet_{\\mathrm{cts}}(Q,(L^U)^\\flat;T). \\end{equation} All filtered colimits in these definitions are taken degreewise. Omitting \\(T\\) means that \\(T\\) is a point.","sourceOffset":36296,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:locally-finite-distributions","text":"For a complete characteristic-\\(p\\) coefficient group \\(R_0\\), define the locally finite distribution module on \\(F\\) by \\begin{equation} \\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{lf}}(F,R_0) = \\varprojlim_n \\prod_{\\alpha\\in F/p^n\\mathcal O_F} R_0, \\end{equation} where the transition sums over the finitely many children of each coset. Equivalently, take the product over \\(F/\\mathcal O_F\\) of the distribution modules on those compact open cosets. Every finite-partition module and every product in \\eqref{eq:analytic-distributions} carries its product topology.","sourceOffset":106734,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":255248,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":15,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Failure-of-finite-assembly-for-a-chromatic-overlap-at-the-prime-three-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Failure-of-finite-assembly-for-a-chromatic-overlap-at-the-prime-three-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Failure-of-finite-assembly-for-a-chromatic-overlap-at-the-prime-three-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"318-05","slug":"Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026","familyId":"318","title":"Counterexamples to weak chromatic splitting: sphere kernels and descent exponents","abstract":"For the derived p-completion of the sphere, the canonical weak chromatic splitting map has no homotopy retraction at height p for every prime p ≥ 5, and at height $p+1$ for every prime p ≥ 7. The classical sphere product $\\beta_1^{(p-1)^2}$ gives a nonzero kernel class in both ranges.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves strong chromatic splitting at height three for primes p ≥ 5, and weak splitting for the derived p-completed sphere at heights p (p ≥ 5) and $p+1$ (p ≥ 7). Nevertheless, for n ≥ 1 and $p\\gt n+1$, the overlap $L_{n-1}L_{K(n)}S_p^\\wedge$ admits a $2^n$-stage filtration by the predicted localized-sphere pieces. At height three and prime three, even finite assembly from such pieces fails in the category of $E(2)$-local modules over the derived completed sphere.","pdfPath":"preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026/build/sections/02-completion.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026/build/sections/07-lower-product.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026/build/sections/04-adic.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026/build/sections/08-upper-kernels.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026/build/sections/06-towers.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026/build/sections/09-exponent-lower.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026/build/sections/10-cooperations.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026/build/sections/11-upper-nullity.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026/build/sections/12-coherent-trace.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026/build/sections/13-exponent-contradiction.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026/build/sections/03-ordinary-products.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For the derived \\(p\\)-completion of the sphere, the canonical weak chromatic splitting map has no homotopy retraction at height \\(p\\) for every prime \\(p\\geq5\\), and at height \\(p+1\\) for every prime \\(p\\geq7\\). The classical sphere product \\(\\beta_1^{(p-1)^2}\\) gives a nonzero kernel class in both ranges.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The canonical weak splitting map","sourceOffset":370},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An independent descent-exponent obstruction","sourceOffset":3406},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Relation to earlier splitting results","sourceOffset":5124},{"kind":"section","title":"Completion and the canonical map","sourceOffset":7177},{"kind":"section","title":"The named sphere product at the lower height","sourceOffset":9727},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The actual unit in the ordinary detector","sourceOffset":10569},{"kind":"section","title":"Semilinear descent and an adic bound","sourceOffset":14967},{"kind":"section","title":"Upper annihilation and the canonical kernels","sourceOffset":23761},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Relative Adams stages","sourceOffset":24223},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The separated upper filtration","sourceOffset":26443},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Annihilating the detected sphere product","sourceOffset":30233},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite descent and exponent bounds","sourceOffset":32650}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"A named kernel at height $p$","label":"thm:height-p-kernel","text":"For every prime $p\\geq5$, the image of $z_p$ in $\\pi_{d_pk_p}L_{p-1}X$ is nonzero and is killed by $\\pi_{d_pk_p}(i_{p,X})$. Consequently $i_{p,X}$ has no homotopy retraction.","sourceOffset":1466,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"The same kernel at height $p+1$","label":"thm:height-plus-one-kernel","text":"For every prime $p\\geq7$, the image of the same $z_p$ in $\\pi_{d_pk_p}L_pX$ is nonzero and is killed by $\\pi_{d_pk_p}(i_{p+1,X})$. Consequently $i_{p+1,X}$ has no homotopy retraction.","sourceOffset":1730,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":88928,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":18,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Counterexamples-to-weak-chromatic-splitting-sphere-kernels-and-descent-exponents-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"319-01","slug":"Counterexamples-to-the-Hahn-Wilson-conjecture-at-height-two-September-26-2026","familyId":"319","title":"Counterexamples to the Hahn-Wilson conjecture at height two","abstract":"We disprove the Hahn–Wilson conjecture at height two. For every sufficiently large prime p, we construct a connective p-complete spectrum X of exact fp-type two outside the ordinary thick subcategory generated by the specified standard form of $\\mathrm{BP}\\langle2\\rangle_p^\\wedge$. The same spectrum satisfies both localization comparisons $L_2^fX\\simeq L_2X$ and $L_{T(2)}X\\simeq L_{K(2)}X$.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Refutes the Hahn–Wilson conjecture at chromatic height two. For every sufficiently large prime p, constructs a connective p-complete spectrum of exact fp-type two that cannot be built from completed $\\mathrm{BP}\\langle2\\rangle$ by finitely many sums, shifts, cones and retracts. The examples nevertheless satisfy the finite and telescopic localization comparisons.","pdfPath":"preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-Hahn-Wilson-conjecture-at-height-two-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-Hahn-Wilson-conjecture-at-height-two-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-Hahn-Wilson-conjecture-at-height-two-September-26-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-Hahn-Wilson-conjecture-at-height-two-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-Hahn-Wilson-conjecture-at-height-two-September-26-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-Hahn-Wilson-conjecture-at-height-two-September-26-2026/build/sections/completion.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-Hahn-Wilson-conjecture-at-height-two-September-26-2026/build/sections/operator.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-Hahn-Wilson-conjecture-at-height-two-September-26-2026/build/sections/gluing.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-Hahn-Wilson-conjecture-at-height-two-September-26-2026/build/sections/obstruction.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-Hahn-Wilson-conjecture-at-height-two-September-26-2026/build/sections/comparison.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-Hahn-Wilson-conjecture-at-height-two-September-26-2026/build/sections/transfer.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-Hahn-Wilson-conjecture-at-height-two-September-26-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-Hahn-Wilson-conjecture-at-height-two-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-Hahn-Wilson-conjecture-at-height-two-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove the Hahn--Wilson conjecture at height two. For every sufficiently large prime $p$, we construct a connective $p$-complete spectrum $X$ of exact fp-type two outside the ordinary thick subcategory generated by the specified standard form of $\\BP\\langle2\\rangle_p^\\wedge$. The same spectrum satisfies both localization comparisons $L_2^fX\\simeq L_2X$ and $L_{T(2)}X\\simeq L_{K(2)}X$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":438},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The question and the theorem","sourceOffset":988},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Global finiteness and the local classification","sourceOffset":3727},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction problem","sourceOffset":6394},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reading the proof","sourceOffset":11263},{"kind":"section","title":"Law charts and the height-two quotient","sourceOffset":12781},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The integral law groupoid","sourceOffset":13691},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removing the central procyclic group","sourceOffset":20635},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Periodic complexes and sections","sourceOffset":28601},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The height-one open and height-two thickenings","sourceOffset":30473},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The reduced stabilizer and its operation ring","sourceOffset":38810},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Derived sections and cohomological bounds","sourceOffset":45778}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every sufficiently large prime $p$, there is a connective $p$-complete spectrum $X$ of exact fp-type two such that, for a standard form of $\\BP\\langle2\\rangle_p^\\wedge$, \\[ X\\notin\\Thick(\\BP\\langle2\\rangle_p^\\wedge). \\] The same spectrum satisfies \\[ L_2^fX\\simeq L_2X, \\qquad L_{T(2)}X\\simeq L_{K(2)}X. \\]","sourceOffset":2885,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"geom:law-charts","text":"The map $f$ is flat and affine. The maps $\\Spec A_2\\to\\mathcal M$ and $\\Spec A_{\\mathrm{Haz}}\\to\\mathcal M$ are flat affine covers. In graded coordinates, the cooperation Hopf algebroid of the latter chart has arrow ring \\[ A_{\\mathrm{Haz}}\\otimes_{P,\\eta_L}\\Gamma_P \\otimes_{P,\\eta_R}A_{\\mathrm{Haz}}. \\]","sourceOffset":16254,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":286030,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":29,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Counterexamples-to-the-Hahn-Wilson-conjecture-at-height-two-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"320-01","slug":"Nonhomeomorphic-closed-aspherical-four-manifolds-with-the-same-homotopy-type-October-4-2026","familyId":"320","title":"Nonhomeomorphic closed aspherical four-manifolds with the same homotopy type","abstract":"We construct closed connected aspherical topological four-manifolds that are homotopy equivalent but not homeomorphic, disproving the homeomorphism-existence formulation of the Borel conjecture in dimension four. Their common fundamental group is word-hyperbolic. One of the manifolds also has a self-homotopy equivalence not homotopic to a homeomorphism.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs closed connected aspherical topological four-manifolds that are homotopy equivalent but not homeomorphic, with a common word-hyperbolic fundamental group. This disproves even the homeomorphism-existence formulation of the Borel conjecture in dimension four.","pdfPath":"preprints/Nonhomeomorphic-closed-aspherical-four-manifolds-with-the-same-homotopy-type-October-4-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Nonhomeomorphic-closed-aspherical-four-manifolds-with-the-same-homotopy-type-October-4-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Nonhomeomorphic-closed-aspherical-four-manifolds-with-the-same-homotopy-type-October-4-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/Nonhomeomorphic-closed-aspherical-four-manifolds-with-the-same-homotopy-type-October-4-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Nonhomeomorphic-closed-aspherical-four-manifolds-with-the-same-homotopy-type-October-4-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Nonhomeomorphic-closed-aspherical-four-manifolds-with-the-same-homotopy-type-October-4-2026/build/sections/cyclic.tex","preprints/Nonhomeomorphic-closed-aspherical-four-manifolds-with-the-same-homotopy-type-October-4-2026/build/sections/reflection.tex","preprints/Nonhomeomorphic-closed-aspherical-four-manifolds-with-the-same-homotopy-type-October-4-2026/build/sections/models.tex","preprints/Nonhomeomorphic-closed-aspherical-four-manifolds-with-the-same-homotopy-type-October-4-2026/build/sections/extension.tex","preprints/Nonhomeomorphic-closed-aspherical-four-manifolds-with-the-same-homotopy-type-October-4-2026/build/sections/hyperbolicity.tex","preprints/Nonhomeomorphic-closed-aspherical-four-manifolds-with-the-same-homotopy-type-October-4-2026/build/sections/normalizer.tex","preprints/Nonhomeomorphic-closed-aspherical-four-manifolds-with-the-same-homotopy-type-October-4-2026/build/sections/chambers.tex","preprints/Nonhomeomorphic-closed-aspherical-four-manifolds-with-the-same-homotopy-type-October-4-2026/build/sections/tensors.tex","preprints/Nonhomeomorphic-closed-aspherical-four-manifolds-with-the-same-homotopy-type-October-4-2026/build/sections/formal.tex","preprints/Nonhomeomorphic-closed-aspherical-four-manifolds-with-the-same-homotopy-type-October-4-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Nonhomeomorphic-closed-aspherical-four-manifolds-with-the-same-homotopy-type-October-4-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct closed connected aspherical topological four-manifolds that are homotopy equivalent but not homeomorphic, disproving the homeomorphism-existence formulation of the Borel conjecture in dimension four. Their common fundamental group is word-hyperbolic. One of the manifolds also has a self-homotopy equivalence not homotopic to a homeomorphism.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":403},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction","sourceOffset":4721},{"kind":"section","title":"Cyclic covers and realized symmetries","sourceOffset":9750},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A criterion from cyclic covers","sourceOffset":10146},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A criterion for distinct manifold models","sourceOffset":16307},{"kind":"section","title":"Reflected cyclic covers","sourceOffset":18804},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The reflection input","sourceOffset":19261},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The parity chamber","sourceOffset":23267},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparing reflected cyclic covers","sourceOffset":25467},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The triangulation used in the construction","sourceOffset":29440},{"kind":"section","title":"Two manifold models of the same double cover","sourceOffset":30936},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A rigid space of label relations","sourceOffset":32360}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:pair","text":"There exist closed connected aspherical topological four-manifolds $M$ and $N$ that are homotopy equivalent but not homeomorphic. Their common fundamental group is word-hyperbolic.","sourceOffset":978,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:self-map","text":"There exist a closed connected aspherical topological four-manifold $M$ and a homotopy equivalence $f\\colon M\\to M$ such that no homeomorphism $M\\to M$ is homotopic to $f$.","sourceOffset":1401,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Marked filling","label":"ch:marked-filling","text":"A marked filling of $S_d$ is a compact oriented topological four-manifold $D'_d$, an orientation-preserving identification $\\partial D'_d=S_d$, and a homotopy equivalence $D'_d\\to BH_d$ whose boundary restriction is homotopic to the marking $S_d\\to P_d\\simeq BH_d$.","sourceOffset":150815,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":232041,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":28,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Nonhomeomorphic-closed-aspherical-four-manifolds-with-the-same-homotopy-type-October-4-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"321-01","slug":"A-Counterexample-to-Walls-D2-Problem-October-6-2026","familyId":"321","title":"A Counterexample to Wall's D(2) Problem","abstract":"We give a negative answer to Wall's finite $D(2)$ problem. We construct a finite connected three-dimensional CW complex satisfying the $D(2)$ finiteness condition but not homotopy equivalent to any finite CW complex of dimension at most two. The example has infinite fundamental group.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a finite connected three-dimensional CW complex whose universal cover has no integral homology above degree two and whose third cohomology vanishes for every local coefficient module, but which has no finite two-dimensional homotopy model. This disproves Wall's finite D(2) conjecture; the example has infinite fundamental group.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Walls-D2-Problem-October-6-2026/wall-d2-counterexample.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Walls-D2-Problem-October-6-2026/wall-d2-counterexample.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Walls-D2-Problem-October-6-2026","subjectId":"4","subject":"Topology","subjectZh":"拓撲學","texPaths":["preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Walls-D2-Problem-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Walls-D2-Problem-October-6-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Walls-D2-Problem-October-6-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Walls-D2-Problem-October-6-2026/build/sections/02-tower.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Walls-D2-Problem-October-6-2026/build/figures/tower.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Walls-D2-Problem-October-6-2026/build/sections/03-plus.tex","preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Walls-D2-Problem-October-6-2026/build/sections/04-construction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Walls-D2-Problem-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a negative answer to Wall's finite $D(2)$ problem. We construct a finite connected three-dimensional CW complex satisfying the $D(2)$ finiteness condition but not homotopy equivalent to any finite CW complex of dimension at most two. The example has infinite fundamental group.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":331},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and approach","sourceOffset":1656},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof structure","sourceOffset":5597},{"kind":"section","title":"A tower obstruction in dimension two","sourceOffset":7102},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite plus construction","sourceOffset":18905},{"kind":"section","title":"The counterexample","sourceOffset":24251}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a finite connected three-dimensional CW complex $X$ such that \\[ H_i(\\widetilde X;\\Z)=0\\quad(i>2),\\qquad H^3(X;M)=0\\quad\\text{for every }\\Z[\\pi_1(X)]\\text{-module }M, \\] but $X$ is not homotopy equivalent to any finite CW complex of dimension at most two.","sourceOffset":1243,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Tower obstruction","label":"thm:tower","text":"Let $Y$ be a finite connected CW complex of dimension at most two, and let $L$ be a rank-one complex local system on $Y$ with $H_2(Y;L)=0$. There is no map $f:F\\to Y$ from a finite connected simplicial complex satisfying $b_1(F;\\Q)=0$ for which $f^*L$ has nontrivial monodromy.","sourceOffset":11376,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":31211,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Counterexample-to-Walls-D2-Problem-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"322-01","slug":"A-positive-solution-to-Tingleys-problem-September-23-2026","familyId":"322","title":"A positive solution to Tingley’s problem","abstract":"Every surjective isometry between the unit spheres of real Banach spaces extends uniquely to a surjective real-linear isometry, giving an affirmative solution to Tingley's problem. If distances between different radii are not preserved, we realize the positive maximal defect in a possibly enlarged pair of Banach spaces. We then align extremal chords using common supports and Darbo's fixed-point theorem and obtain a contradiction from support and convexity estimates.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves Tingley's problem: every surjective isometry between the unit spheres of nonzero real Banach spaces extends uniquely to a surjective real-linear isometry of the whole spaces. No dimension restriction is imposed, so the metric geometry of the unit sphere determines the Banach space up to linear isometry.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-positive-solution-to-Tingleys-problem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-positive-solution-to-Tingleys-problem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-positive-solution-to-Tingleys-problem-September-23-2026","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","texPaths":["preprints/A-positive-solution-to-Tingleys-problem-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-positive-solution-to-Tingleys-problem-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-positive-solution-to-Tingleys-problem-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every surjective isometry between the unit spheres of real Banach spaces extends uniquely to a surjective real-linear isometry, giving an affirmative solution to Tingley's problem. If distances between different radii are not preserved, we realize the positive maximal defect in a possibly enlarged pair of Banach spaces. We then align extremal chords using common supports and Darbo's fixed-point theorem and obtain a contradiction from support and convexity estimates.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":516},{"kind":"section","title":"Supporting functionals and radial contact","sourceOffset":5843},{"kind":"section","title":"Attaining the maximal defect","sourceOffset":8202},{"kind":"section","title":"Aligning extremal chords","sourceOffset":12261},{"kind":"section","title":"The chord contradiction","sourceOffset":19334},{"kind":"section","title":"The linear extension","sourceOffset":25804},{"kind":"section","title":"The fixed-point ingredients","sourceOffset":27669}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $X,Y$ be nonzero real Banach spaces and let $f:S_X\\to S_Y$ be a surjective isometry. Then \\[ T(0)=0,\\qquad T(x)=\\norm{x}f\\left(\\frac{x}{\\norm{x}}\\right)\\quad(x\\ne0) \\] is a surjective real-linear isometry. It is the unique linear extension of $f$.","sourceOffset":1054,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:condensing","text":"Let $C$ be a nonempty closed bounded convex subset of a Banach space. If $g:C\\to C$ is continuous and, for some $0\\le\\gamma<1$, \\[ \\chi(g(H))\\le\\gamma\\chi(H)\\qquad(H\\subset C), \\] then $g$ has a fixed point.","sourceOffset":12661,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/322.md","scopeText":"Tingley's problem asks whether a surjective isometry between the unit spheres of Banach spaces extends to a linear isometry of the spaces. The formalized result gives a unique surjective real-linear isometric extension for arbitrary nonzero real Banach spaces, with no finite-dimensionality, separability, reflexivity, or convexity assumptions. For complex spaces viewed as real spaces, the conclusion is real linearity rather than complex linearity.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/322.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":38952,"sourceFileCount":2,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-positive-solution-to-Tingleys-problem-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"323-01","slug":"Relative-independence-of-the-separable-quotient-problem-September-23-2026","familyId":"323","title":"Relative independence of the separable quotient problem","abstract":"The separable quotient problem asks whether every infinite-dimensional Banach space has a separable infinite-dimensional quotient. We prove that this statement is independent of ZFC, relative to the consistency of ZFC with a measurable cardinal. This holds over both the real and complex fields. For spaces of norm density ℵ1, we obtain independence relative to the consistency of ZFC alone.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Establishes, relative to the consistency of a measurable cardinal, that the separable quotient problem is independent of ZFC. The assertion that every infinite-dimensional Banach space has a separable infinite-dimensional quotient can hold for all real and complex Banach spaces, whereas the continuum hypothesis yields counterexamples over both fields.","pdfPath":"preprints/Relative-independence-of-the-separable-quotient-problem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Relative-independence-of-the-separable-quotient-problem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Relative-independence-of-the-separable-quotient-problem-September-23-2026","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","texPaths":["preprints/Relative-independence-of-the-separable-quotient-problem-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Relative-independence-of-the-separable-quotient-problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Relative-independence-of-the-separable-quotient-problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/evaluation.tex","preprints/Relative-independence-of-the-separable-quotient-problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/positive.tex","preprints/Relative-independence-of-the-separable-quotient-problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/scalar-fields.tex","preprints/Relative-independence-of-the-separable-quotient-problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/norming.tex","preprints/Relative-independence-of-the-separable-quotient-problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/paths.tex","preprints/Relative-independence-of-the-separable-quotient-problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/exact-pairs.tex","preprints/Relative-independence-of-the-separable-quotient-problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/branches.tex","preprints/Relative-independence-of-the-separable-quotient-problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/predual.tex","preprints/Relative-independence-of-the-separable-quotient-problem-September-23-2026/build/sections/models.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Relative-independence-of-the-separable-quotient-problem-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"The separable quotient problem asks whether every infinite-dimensional Banach space has a separable infinite-dimensional quotient. We prove that this statement is independent of ZFC, relative to the consistency of ZFC with a measurable cardinal. This holds over both the real and complex fields. For spaces of norm density $\\aleph_1$, we obtain independence relative to the consistency of ZFC alone.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":498},{"kind":"section","title":"Separable evaluation images","sourceOffset":9747},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Small restrictions of strictly singular operators","sourceOffset":14854},{"kind":"section","title":"A measure on the continuum gives separable quotients","sourceOffset":16623},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The measure hypothesis","sourceOffset":17173},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The density forced by failure of a quotient","sourceOffset":19668},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Borel coding and ordered sampling","sourceOffset":24573},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The positive conclusion","sourceOffset":31494},{"kind":"section","title":"Passing between real and complex spaces","sourceOffset":32889},{"kind":"section","title":"A dual space with coded norming functionals","sourceOffset":39833},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Parameters and finite coordinate closures","sourceOffset":41401},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Operations, paths and the norm","sourceOffset":48863}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"intro:main","text":"For each $\\mathbb F\\in\\{\\R,\\C\\}$, ZFC proves the following implications. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item If $\\mathfrak c$ is real-valued measurable, then $\\SQ_{\\mathbb F}$ holds. \\item If the continuum hypothesis holds, then $\\SQ_{\\mathbb F}$ fails. \\end{enumerate} Consequently, if ZFC with a measurable cardinal is consistent, then $\\SQ_{\\mathbb F}$ is independent of ZFC. Consistency of its failure requires only consistency of ZFC.","sourceOffset":2159,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"intro:small-density","text":"If ZFC is consistent, then $\\SQ^{\\aleph_1}_{\\mathbb F}$ is independent of ZFC for each $\\mathbb F\\in\\{\\R,\\C\\}$.","sourceOffset":3070,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/323.md","scopeText":"The separable quotient problem asks whether every infinite-dimensional Banach space has an infinite-dimensional separable quotient. The linked formalization proves the negative direction under the continuum hypothesis: over both the real and complex fields, there is an infinite-dimensional Banach space admitting no bounded linear surjection onto an infinite-dimensional separable Banach space.\n\nThis is the conditional counterexample direction. The positive consistency direction and the paper's full relative-independence conclusions are outside the selected statement.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/323.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":126571,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Relative-independence-of-the-separable-quotient-problem-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Relative-independence-of-the-separable-quotient-problem-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"324-01","slug":"Lipschitz-Equivalent-Separable-Banach-Spaces-Need-Not-Be-Linearly-Isomorphic-September-24-2026","familyId":"324","title":"Lipschitz Equivalent Separable Banach Spaces Need Not Be Linearly Isomorphic","abstract":"There are separable real Banach spaces that are globally bi-Lipschitz equivalent but not linearly isomorphic. This gives a negative answer to the separable Banach-space Lipschitz-isomorphism problem.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs separable real Banach spaces that are globally bi-Lipschitz equivalent but not linearly isomorphic, resolving the separable Lipschitz-isomorphism problem negatively. Thus even the complete metric structure up to bi-Lipschitz equivalence does not determine a separable Banach space's linear isomorphism class.","pdfPath":"preprints/Lipschitz-Equivalent-Separable-Banach-Spaces-Need-Not-Be-Linearly-Isomorphic-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Lipschitz-Equivalent-Separable-Banach-Spaces-Need-Not-Be-Linearly-Isomorphic-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Lipschitz-Equivalent-Separable-Banach-Spaces-Need-Not-Be-Linearly-Isomorphic-September-24-2026","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","texPaths":["preprints/Lipschitz-Equivalent-Separable-Banach-Spaces-Need-Not-Be-Linearly-Isomorphic-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Lipschitz-Equivalent-Separable-Banach-Spaces-Need-Not-Be-Linearly-Isomorphic-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Lipschitz-Equivalent-Separable-Banach-Spaces-Need-Not-Be-Linearly-Isomorphic-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-free-graph.tex","preprints/Lipschitz-Equivalent-Separable-Banach-Spaces-Need-Not-Be-Linearly-Isomorphic-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-triangular.tex","preprints/Lipschitz-Equivalent-Separable-Banach-Spaces-Need-Not-Be-Linearly-Isomorphic-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-localization.tex","preprints/Lipschitz-Equivalent-Separable-Banach-Spaces-Need-Not-Be-Linearly-Isomorphic-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-hilbert.tex","preprints/Lipschitz-Equivalent-Separable-Banach-Spaces-Need-Not-Be-Linearly-Isomorphic-September-24-2026/build/figures/slot-rearrangement.tex","preprints/Lipschitz-Equivalent-Separable-Banach-Spaces-Need-Not-Be-Linearly-Isomorphic-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-assembly.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Lipschitz-Equivalent-Separable-Banach-Spaces-Need-Not-Be-Linearly-Isomorphic-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"There are separable real Banach spaces that are globally bi-Lipschitz equivalent but not linearly isomorphic. This gives a negative answer to the separable Banach-space Lipschitz-isomorphism problem.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":246},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical setting","sourceOffset":1904},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":4775},{"kind":"section","title":"Free spaces and the graph-space obstruction","sourceOffset":9244},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Free-space facts and the two external inputs","sourceOffset":9721},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weighted graph spaces","sourceOffset":14984},{"kind":"section","title":"A triangular change of variables","sourceOffset":23329},{"kind":"section","title":"Local orthogonality forces complete continuity","sourceOffset":29686},{"kind":"section","title":"A localized change of variables in Hilbert space","sourceOffset":44349},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The frozen rearrangement","sourceOffset":45958},{"kind":"subsection","title":"What the moving slots must satisfy","sourceOffset":49390},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite stages and separating coordinates","sourceOffset":51067}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are separable real Banach spaces \\(X,Y\\) and a bijection \\(\\Psi:X\\to Y\\) such that, for every \\(s,t\\in X\\), \\begin{equation} \\frac4{21}\\|s-t\\|_X \\le \\|\\Psi(s)-\\Psi(t)\\|_Y \\le \\frac{76}{25}\\|s-t\\|_X. \\end{equation} The space \\(X\\) contains a linearly isometric copy of \\(c_0(\\ell_2)\\), while \\(Y\\) contains no closed subspace linearly isomorphic to \\(c_0(\\ell_2)\\). In particular, \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) are not linearly isomorphic.","sourceOffset":1133,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Compact reduction for a weakly null sequence","label":"thm:compact-reduction","text":"Let \\((M,d,0)\\) be a complete pointed metric space, and let \\(\\mu_i\\to0\\) weakly in \\(\\F(M)\\). For every \\(\\tau>0\\), there is a compact \\(K\\subset M\\), with \\(0\\in K\\), such that \\[ \\dist(\\mu_i,\\F(K))<\\tau\\qquad\\hbox{for every }i. \\]","sourceOffset":13094,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/324.md","scopeText":"The formalized counterexample gives separable real Banach spaces $X,Y$ that are bi-Lipschitz equivalent but not linearly isomorphic. The bijection has lower Lipschitz bound $4/21$ and upper bound $76/25$. The linear obstruction is explicit: $X$ contains a linear isometric copy of $c_0(\\ell_2)$, whereas $Y$ contains no bounded linear copy of that space.\n\nThe formalized result constructs one separable real Banach space $X$ that is bi-Lipschitz equivalent to $X\\times c_0$ but contains no closed linear subspace isomorphic to $c_0$. The same space contains a bi-Lipschitz copy of $c_0$, is bi-Lipschitz universal for separable metric spaces, and is not linearly isomorphic to $X\\times c_0$. Thus nonlinear absorption of $c_0$ does not force a linear copy of it.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/324.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":69277,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Lipschitz-Equivalent-Separable-Banach-Spaces-Need-Not-Be-Linearly-Isomorphic-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"324-02","slug":"Bi-Lipschitz-Absorption-of-c0-Without-a-Linear-Copy-of-c0-September-26-2026","familyId":"324","title":"Bi-Lipschitz Absorption of c0 Without a Linear Copy of c0","abstract":"We construct a separable real Banach space Z that contains no linear copy of c0, yet is bi-Lipschitz equivalent to $Z\\oplus_\\infty c_0$. The same space contains a bi-Lipschitz image of every separable metric space.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs separable real Banach spaces that are globally bi-Lipschitz equivalent but not linearly isomorphic, resolving the separable Lipschitz-isomorphism problem negatively. Thus even the complete metric structure up to bi-Lipschitz equivalence does not determine a separable Banach space's linear isomorphism class.","pdfPath":"preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Absorption-of-c0-Without-a-Linear-Copy-of-c0-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Absorption-of-c0-Without-a-Linear-Copy-of-c0-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Absorption-of-c0-Without-a-Linear-Copy-of-c0-September-26-2026","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","texPaths":["preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Absorption-of-c0-Without-a-Linear-Copy-of-c0-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Absorption-of-c0-Without-a-Linear-Copy-of-c0-September-26-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Absorption-of-c0-Without-a-Linear-Copy-of-c0-September-26-2026/build/sections/02-absorption.tex","preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Absorption-of-c0-Without-a-Linear-Copy-of-c0-September-26-2026/build/sections/03-weights.tex","preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Absorption-of-c0-Without-a-Linear-Copy-of-c0-September-26-2026/build/sections/04-shift.tex","preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Absorption-of-c0-Without-a-Linear-Copy-of-c0-September-26-2026/build/figures/terminal-scalar.tex","preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Absorption-of-c0-Without-a-Linear-Copy-of-c0-September-26-2026/build/sections/05-tests.tex","preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Absorption-of-c0-Without-a-Linear-Copy-of-c0-September-26-2026/build/sections/06-molecules.tex","preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Absorption-of-c0-Without-a-Linear-Copy-of-c0-September-26-2026/build/sections/07-no-c0.tex","preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Absorption-of-c0-Without-a-Linear-Copy-of-c0-September-26-2026/build/sections/08-assembly.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Absorption-of-c0-Without-a-Linear-Copy-of-c0-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a separable real Banach space \\(Z\\) that contains no linear copy of \\(c_0\\), yet is bi-Lipschitz equivalent to \\(Z\\oplus_\\infty c_0\\). The same space contains a bi-Lipschitz image of every separable metric space.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":271},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof overview","sourceOffset":5069},{"kind":"section","title":"The absorption criterion","sourceOffset":9286},{"kind":"section","title":"Coordinate weights and localized corrections","sourceOffset":12187},{"kind":"section","title":"The state-dependent shift is onto","sourceOffset":24989},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Frozen and state-dependent shifts","sourceOffset":25307},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Terminal scalars and Brouwer surjectivity","sourceOffset":30257},{"kind":"section","title":"Selected tests and their supports","sourceOffset":36865},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two local operations","sourceOffset":37371},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Scale budgets and tail factors","sourceOffset":41320},{"kind":"section","title":"Molecules and the lifted correction","sourceOffset":47345},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The quotient and its completion","sourceOffset":47695}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a separable real Banach space \\(Z\\) containing no closed linear subspace isomorphic to \\(c_0\\), and an onto bi-Lipschitz map \\[ F:Z\\oplus_\\infty c_0\\longrightarrow Z. \\]","sourceOffset":2977,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Global distance bounds","label":"prop:global","text":"For every \\(x,x'\\in D\\), \\begin{equation} \\left(\\frac15-24\\eta\\right)\\|x-x'\\|_D \\leq\\|g(x)-g(x')\\|_U \\leq(5+24\\eta)\\|x-x'\\|_D. \\end{equation} In particular, \\(g\\) is injective and continuous.","sourceOffset":27614,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/324.md","scopeText":"The formalized counterexample gives separable real Banach spaces $X,Y$ that are bi-Lipschitz equivalent but not linearly isomorphic. The bijection has lower Lipschitz bound $4/21$ and upper bound $76/25$. The linear obstruction is explicit: $X$ contains a linear isometric copy of $c_0(\\ell_2)$, whereas $Y$ contains no bounded linear copy of that space.\n\nThe formalized result constructs one separable real Banach space $X$ that is bi-Lipschitz equivalent to $X\\times c_0$ but contains no closed linear subspace isomorphic to $c_0$. The same space contains a bi-Lipschitz copy of $c_0$, is bi-Lipschitz universal for separable metric spaces, and is not linearly isomorphic to $X\\times c_0$. Thus nonlinear absorption of $c_0$ does not force a linear copy of it.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/324.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":88100,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Bi-Lipschitz-Absorption-of-c0-Without-a-Linear-Copy-of-c0-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"325-01","slug":"A-direct-proof-of-the-complete-Crouzeix-inequality-September-26-2026","familyId":"325","title":"A direct proof of the complete Crouzeix inequality","abstract":"We give a direct proof of the sharp constant-two numerical-range inequality for matrix-valued polynomials in all finite base and coefficient dimensions. This resolves the complete Crouzeix conjecture in its matrix formulation, including matrices whose numerical ranges are points or line segments.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the complete Crouzeix conjecture: for every bounded operator A on a complex Hilbert space and every finite matrix-valued polynomial P, one has $\\lVert P[A]\\rVert\\le2\\sup_{z\\in W(A)}\\lVert P(z)\\rVert$, where $W(A)$ is the numerical range. The constant 2 is sharp, independent of the matrix size, and valid in infinite dimensions.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-direct-proof-of-the-complete-Crouzeix-inequality-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-direct-proof-of-the-complete-Crouzeix-inequality-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-direct-proof-of-the-complete-Crouzeix-inequality-September-26-2026","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","texPaths":["preprints/A-direct-proof-of-the-complete-Crouzeix-inequality-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-direct-proof-of-the-complete-Crouzeix-inequality-September-26-2026/build/figures/normal.tex","preprints/A-direct-proof-of-the-complete-Crouzeix-inequality-September-26-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-direct-proof-of-the-complete-Crouzeix-inequality-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We give a direct proof of the sharp constant-two numerical-range inequality for matrix-valued polynomials in all finite base and coefficient dimensions. This resolves the complete Crouzeix conjecture in its matrix formulation, including matrices whose numerical ranges are points or line segments.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":342},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier bounds and related arguments","sourceOffset":1930},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the coefficients give the sharp bound","sourceOffset":5333},{"kind":"section","title":"Exterior coordinates and coefficient estimates","sourceOffset":7428},{"kind":"section","title":"Singular vectors and the amplified estimate","sourceOffset":16334},{"kind":"section","title":"Enclosing domains and arbitrary numerical ranges","sourceOffset":25063},{"kind":"section","title":"The exterior conformal collar","sourceOffset":30236}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every pair of positive integers $n,m$, every $A\\in M_n(\\C)$, every nonnegative integer $d$, and every polynomial $P(z)=\\sum_{k=0}^d B_kz^k$ with $B_k\\in M_m(\\C)$, \\begin{equation} \\|P[A]\\|\\le2\\max_{z\\in W(A)}\\|P(z)\\|. \\end{equation} The constant two cannot be decreased uniformly over $n,m$, and $d$.","sourceOffset":1251,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"The bound on an analytic convex domain","label":"thm:domain","text":"For positive integers $n,m$, let $\\Omega\\subset\\C$ be bounded, open, and convex with regular real-analytic Jordan boundary, and let $A\\in M_n(\\C)$ satisfy $W(A)\\subset\\Omega$. For every $M_m(\\C)$-valued polynomial $F$ with $\\|F(z)\\|\\le1$ on $\\partial\\Omega$, one has $\\|F[A]\\|\\le2$.","sourceOffset":18701,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/325.md","scopeText":"The complete Crouzeix inequality bounds a matrix-valued polynomial evaluated at a matrix by its maximum norm on the numerical range. The formalized result proves the bound with constant $2$ for every positive matrix and coefficient size, every polynomial degree, and arbitrary complex coefficients. It also proves that no smaller universal constant works. Normality of the matrix and nonempty interior of its numerical range are not assumed.\n\nThe formalization proves the complete Crouzeix inequality with sharp constant two for every bounded operator on an arbitrary complex Hilbert space. For every matrix-valued polynomial, its operator evaluation has norm at most twice its supremum norm on the numerical range. The same bound holds for finite matrix-valued functions holomorphic near the closure","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/325.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":39356,"sourceFileCount":3,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-direct-proof-of-the-complete-Crouzeix-inequality-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"325-02","slug":"The-complete-Crouzeix-theorem-September-23-2026","familyId":"325","title":"The complete Crouzeix theorem: optimal similarity and a common positive boundary representation","abstract":"We resolve the complete Crouzeix conjecture by proving the sharp constant-two numerical-range inequality for every bounded operator on a complex Hilbert space and every matrix-valued polynomial. No separability assumption is needed. The closure of the numerical range is a complete 2-spectral set, and the bound extends to finite matrix-valued functions holomorphic near that closure. For a finite matrix and a bounded convex domain containing its numerical range with regular real-analytic Jordan boundary, the optimal similarity making its conformal disk image contractive is attained with condition number at most two. For the similar matrix, one continuous positive boundary density of mass the identity represents the evaluation of every matrix-valued function holomorphic near the closed domain.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the complete Crouzeix conjecture: for every bounded operator A on a complex Hilbert space and every finite matrix-valued polynomial P, one has $\\lVert P[A]\\rVert\\le2\\sup_{z\\in W(A)}\\lVert P(z)\\rVert$, where $W(A)$ is the numerical range. The constant 2 is sharp, independent of the matrix size, and valid in infinite dimensions.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-complete-Crouzeix-theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-complete-Crouzeix-theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-complete-Crouzeix-theorem-September-23-2026","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","texPaths":["preprints/The-complete-Crouzeix-theorem-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-complete-Crouzeix-theorem-September-23-2026/build/figures/coordinates.tex","preprints/The-complete-Crouzeix-theorem-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-complete-Crouzeix-theorem-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We resolve the complete Crouzeix conjecture by proving the sharp constant-two numerical-range inequality for every bounded operator on a complex Hilbert space and every matrix-valued polynomial. No separability assumption is needed. The closure of the numerical range is a complete $2$-spectral set, and the bound extends to finite matrix-valued functions holomorphic near that closure. For a finite matrix and a bounded convex domain containing its numerical range with regular real-analytic Jordan boundary, the optimal similarity making its conformal disk image contractive is attained with condition number at most two. For the similar matrix, one continuous positive boundary density of mass the identity represents the evaluation of every matrix-valued function holomorphic near the closed domain.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The structural question","sourceOffset":871},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coordinates and evaluation","sourceOffset":1471},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The complete inequality and previous work","sourceOffset":5027},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the two conclusions are proved","sourceOffset":8539},{"kind":"section","title":"The metric, its representation, and its extremal data","sourceOffset":13383},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A metric for the disk coordinate","sourceOffset":13465},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One density for every coefficient size","sourceOffset":16043},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Endpoint separation and an exact extremal","sourceOffset":18474},{"kind":"section","title":"Local boundary tools","sourceOffset":25257},{"kind":"section","title":"From extremal vectors to two positive density systems","sourceOffset":38291},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Equality in the common positive representation","sourceOffset":39009},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The original-matrix positive block","sourceOffset":41653}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"The optimal metric","label":"thm:structural","text":"For every admissible $\\Omega$, every finite $n\\ge1$, every $A\\in M_n(\\C)$ with $W(A)\\subset\\Omega$, and the interior conformal coordinate $f$ above, the problem \\eqref{eq:minimum} is strictly feasible and has an attained minimum $1\\le\\kappa^2\\le4$. If $H$ is a minimizing metric, then \\[ S=H^{1/2},\\qquad A'=SAS^{-1},\\qquad D=Sf(A)S^{-1}=f(A') \\] satisfy \\[ D^*D\\preceq I,\\qquad \\rho(D)<1, \\qquad \\|S\\|\\|S^{-1}\\|=\\kappa\\le2. \\]","sourceOffset":3831,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"A common positive representation","label":"thm:representation","text":"With the same $\\Omega,A,f,G$ and any chosen minimizing metric in Theorem \\ref{thm:structural}, there is one continuous function $\\Lambda:\\T\\to M_n(\\C)$ satisfying \\[ \\Lambda(t)\\succeq0,\\qquad \\int_\\T\\Lambda(t)\\,d\\sigma(t)=I_n, \\] such that, for every positive integer $m$ and every $M_m(\\C)$-valued function $v$ holomorphic on a neighborhood of $\\overline\\Omega$, \\begin{equation} v[A']=\\int_\\T\\Lambda(t)\\otimes v(G(t))\\,d\\sigma(t), \\qquad \\|v[A]\\|\\le\\kappa\\max_{\\overline\\Omega}\\|v\\|. \\end{equation} The same $S$, $\\Lambda$, and $\\kappa$ work for all $m$ and $v$.","sourceOffset":4335,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/325.md","scopeText":"The complete Crouzeix inequality bounds a matrix-valued polynomial evaluated at a matrix by its maximum norm on the numerical range. The formalized result proves the bound with constant $2$ for every positive matrix and coefficient size, every polynomial degree, and arbitrary complex coefficients. It also proves that no smaller universal constant works. Normality of the matrix and nonempty interior of its numerical range are not assumed.\n\nThe formalization proves the complete Crouzeix inequality with sharp constant two for every bounded operator on an arbitrary complex Hilbert space. For every matrix-valued polynomial, its operator evaluation has norm at most twice its supremum norm on the numerical range. The same bound holds for finite matrix-valued functions holomorphic near the closure","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/325.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":74193,"sourceFileCount":3,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-complete-Crouzeix-theorem-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"326-01","slug":"The-cotype-cotype-conjecture-under-the-approximation-property-September-23-2026","familyId":"326","title":"The cotype–cotype conjecture under the approximation property","abstract":"We prove the cotype–cotype conjecture under the ordinary approximation property. A nonzero real Banach space with this property is K-convex if and only if both the space and its dual have finite Rademacher cotype, possibly with different exponents.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the cotype–cotype conjecture for real Banach spaces with the approximation property. Such a nonzero space is K-convex if and only if both it and its dual have finite Rademacher cotype, with possibly different exponents. Equivalently, these cotype assumptions force nontrivial Rademacher type.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-cotype-cotype-conjecture-under-the-approximation-property-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-cotype-cotype-conjecture-under-the-approximation-property-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-cotype-cotype-conjecture-under-the-approximation-property-September-23-2026","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","texPaths":["preprints/The-cotype-cotype-conjecture-under-the-approximation-property-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-cotype-cotype-conjecture-under-the-approximation-property-September-23-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/The-cotype-cotype-conjecture-under-the-approximation-property-September-23-2026/build/preliminaries.tex","preprints/The-cotype-cotype-conjecture-under-the-approximation-property-September-23-2026/build/coefficient.tex","preprints/The-cotype-cotype-conjecture-under-the-approximation-property-September-23-2026/build/walsh.tex","preprints/The-cotype-cotype-conjecture-under-the-approximation-property-September-23-2026/build/approximation.tex","preprints/The-cotype-cotype-conjecture-under-the-approximation-property-September-23-2026/build/consequences.tex","preprints/The-cotype-cotype-conjecture-under-the-approximation-property-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-cotype-cotype-conjecture-under-the-approximation-property-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the cotype--cotype conjecture under the ordinary approximation property. A nonzero real Banach space with this property is \\(K\\)-convex if and only if both the space and its dual have finite Rademacher cotype, possibly with different exponents.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The characterization","sourceOffset":300},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and scope","sourceOffset":5021},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The mechanism of the proof","sourceOffset":9177},{"kind":"section","title":"Walsh transforms and moment lifts","sourceOffset":11754},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Walsh norms","sourceOffset":12588},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fixed-length cotype estimates","sourceOffset":14703},{"kind":"section","title":"A scalar Fourier-coefficient lemma","sourceOffset":19173},{"kind":"section","title":"Uniform Walsh decay for finite-rank operators","sourceOffset":26512},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Dimension growth","sourceOffset":26586},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform decay","sourceOffset":38668},{"kind":"section","title":"Compact factorization and the approximation property","sourceOffset":41973},{"kind":"section","title":"The conjecture on Grothendieck pairs","sourceOffset":53124}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(X\\) be a nonzero real Banach space with the ordinary approximation property. Then \\(X\\) is \\(K\\)-convex if and only if \\[ C_q(X)<\\infty \\quad\\hbox{and}\\quad C_r(X^*)<\\infty \\qquad\\text{for some }q,r\\in[2,\\infty). \\] The exponents \\(q\\) and \\(r\\) may differ. The forward implication holds for every real Banach space, without an approximation hypothesis.","sourceOffset":1927,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Uniform cotype bounds and entropy duality","label":"cor:uniform-cotype-entropy","text":"Fix \\(q,r\\in[2,\\infty)\\) and \\(C,D\\in[1,\\infty)\\). There is a finite constant \\(\\kappa=\\kappa(q,r,C,D)\\) such that every nonzero real Banach space \\(X\\) with AP and \\[ C_q(X)\\le C,\\qquad C_r(X^*)\\le D \\] satisfies \\(K(X)\\le\\kappa\\). Consequently there are \\(a,b\\ge1\\), depending only on \\(q,r,C,D\\), such that for every \\(n\\ge1\\), all origin-symmetric convex bodies \\(K,L\\subset\\R^n\\), and every \\(t>0\\), \\begin{equation} \\frac1b\\log N(L^\\circ,atK^\\circ) \\le \\log N(K,tL) \\le b\\log N(L^\\circ,a^{-1}tK^\\circ), \\end{equation} provided either \\(X_K\\) or \\(X_L\\) satisfies the displayed primal and dual cotype bounds.","sourceOffset":3898,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/326.md","scopeText":"The cotype–cotype problem asks whether finite cotype of a Banach space and its dual characterizes $K$-convexity. The formalized result establishes this equivalence for every nonzero real Banach space with the approximation property: $X$ is $K$-convex exactly when both $X$ and $X^*$ have finite cotype.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/326.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":60970,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-cotype-cotype-conjecture-under-the-approximation-property-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"327-01","slug":"Nontrivial-Markov-Type-Forces-Superreflexivity-September-23-2026","familyId":"327","title":"Nontrivial Markov Type Forces Superreflexivity","abstract":"We prove that every real Banach space with Markov type p > 1 is superreflexive. Together with the known converse, this characterizes superreflexivity by nontrivial Markov type. This answers Naor's question: every real Banach space with nontrivial Markov type admits an equivalent uniformly smooth norm.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every real Banach space with Markov type p for some p > 1 admits an equivalent uniformly convex norm, answering Naor's renorming question. Together with the known converse, this characterizes superreflexivity by nontrivial Markov type.","pdfPath":"preprints/Nontrivial-Markov-Type-Forces-Superreflexivity-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Nontrivial-Markov-Type-Forces-Superreflexivity-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Nontrivial-Markov-Type-Forces-Superreflexivity-September-23-2026","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","texPaths":["preprints/Nontrivial-Markov-Type-Forces-Superreflexivity-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Nontrivial-Markov-Type-Forces-Superreflexivity-September-23-2026/build/figures/chart-transport.tex","preprints/Nontrivial-Markov-Type-Forces-Superreflexivity-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Nontrivial-Markov-Type-Forces-Superreflexivity-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every real Banach space with Markov type $p>1$ is superreflexive. Together with the known converse, this characterizes superreflexivity by nontrivial Markov type. This answers Naor's question: every real Banach space with nontrivial Markov type admits an equivalent uniformly smooth norm.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":484},{"kind":"section","title":"Reduction to a finite walk","sourceOffset":6633},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The ordered norm","sourceOffset":6959},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite-walk target","sourceOffset":8378},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Applying Markov type to edge labels","sourceOffset":9870},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof of the main theorem","sourceOffset":12938},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite charts for ordered motion","sourceOffset":14042},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Averaging on a finite order","sourceOffset":14496},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reversible chart transport","sourceOffset":17068},{"kind":"section","title":"Two levels from reduced words","sourceOffset":20606},{"kind":"section","title":"Construction of the finite walks","sourceOffset":25274},{"kind":"section","title":"Construction of the spreading norm","sourceOffset":32757}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Every real Banach space with Markov type $p$ for at least one $p>1$ is superreflexive.","sourceOffset":1362,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"A finite walk with linear displacement","label":"prop:walks","text":"Let $N$ be a spreading invariant norm on $c_{00}$ for which merging adjacent entries is contractive. For every integer $n\\geq1$, there are a finite stationary reversible marked chain and an odd labeling $g$ in a finite coordinate span such that \\[ N(g(e))=1\\qquad\\text{for every edge }e, \\] and, for the first $n$ edges $E_1,\\ldots,E_n$ of the stationary chain, \\begin{equation} \\Prob\\left\\{N\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^n g(E_i)\\right) \\geq\\frac n{24}\\right\\}\\geq\\frac3{20}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":9091,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/327.md","scopeText":"The formalized result proves that a real Banach space has nontrivial Markov type exactly when it admits an equivalent uniformly convex norm, hence exactly when it is superreflexive. Nontrivial Markov type means a uniform Markov-type bound for some exponent $p>1$ over all finite stationary reversible chains and all positive times. The exponent and equivalent norm may depend on the space, and the zero space is included. Additional formalized consequences concern uniformly smooth renormings and reflexivity of finitely representable spaces.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/327.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":45359,"sourceFileCount":3,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Nontrivial-Markov-Type-Forces-Superreflexivity-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"328-01","slug":"Fixed-Points-of-Nonexpansive-Maps-in-Reflexive-Banach-Spaces-September-24-2026","familyId":"328","title":"Fixed Points of Nonexpansive Maps in Reflexive Banach Spaces","abstract":"Every nonexpansive selfmap of a nonempty closed bounded convex subset of a real reflexive Banach space has a fixed point. This resolves the reflexive-space fixed point problem for the given norm.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves Kirk's reflexive-space fixed-point problem: every nonexpansive selfmap of a nonempty closed bounded convex subset of a real reflexive Banach space has a fixed point. The result uses the original norm, without assuming uniform convexity.","pdfPath":"preprints/Fixed-Points-of-Nonexpansive-Maps-in-Reflexive-Banach-Spaces-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Fixed-Points-of-Nonexpansive-Maps-in-Reflexive-Banach-Spaces-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Fixed-Points-of-Nonexpansive-Maps-in-Reflexive-Banach-Spaces-September-24-2026","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","texPaths":["preprints/Fixed-Points-of-Nonexpansive-Maps-in-Reflexive-Banach-Spaces-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Fixed-Points-of-Nonexpansive-Maps-in-Reflexive-Banach-Spaces-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Fixed-Points-of-Nonexpansive-Maps-in-Reflexive-Banach-Spaces-September-24-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/Fixed-Points-of-Nonexpansive-Maps-in-Reflexive-Banach-Spaces-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Fixed-Points-of-Nonexpansive-Maps-in-Reflexive-Banach-Spaces-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-anchor.tex","preprints/Fixed-Points-of-Nonexpansive-Maps-in-Reflexive-Banach-Spaces-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-tree.tex","preprints/Fixed-Points-of-Nonexpansive-Maps-in-Reflexive-Banach-Spaces-September-24-2026/build/figures/ideal-cutoff.tex","preprints/Fixed-Points-of-Nonexpansive-Maps-in-Reflexive-Banach-Spaces-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-switching.tex","preprints/Fixed-Points-of-Nonexpansive-Maps-in-Reflexive-Banach-Spaces-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-foundations.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Fixed-Points-of-Nonexpansive-Maps-in-Reflexive-Banach-Spaces-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every nonexpansive selfmap of a nonempty closed bounded convex subset of a real reflexive Banach space has a fixed point. This resolves the reflexive-space fixed point problem for the given norm.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":241},{"kind":"section","title":"Weak compactness and a minimal invariant set","sourceOffset":7524},{"kind":"section","title":"An anchor for successive choices","sourceOffset":15496},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Resolvents and displacement estimates","sourceOffset":16040},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A compact semigroup of maps","sourceOffset":19114},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Successive choices with prescribed thresholds","sourceOffset":25835},{"kind":"section","title":"A tree of maps and separating functionals","sourceOffset":29253},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Evaluation on an ordered tree","sourceOffset":29891},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Predictions using earlier candidates","sourceOffset":31848},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The inductive construction","sourceOffset":35409},{"kind":"section","title":"Switching leaves and completing the proof","sourceOffset":42792},{"kind":"section","title":"Separation and compactness","sourceOffset":52733}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $X$ be a real reflexive Banach space, and let $C\\subseteq X$ be nonempty, norm closed, bounded, and convex. If $F:C\\to C$ satisfies \\[ \\norm{F(a)-F(b)}\\le \\norm{a-b}\\qquad(a,b\\in C), \\] then $F$ has a fixed point.","sourceOffset":544,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Commuting families","label":"cor:commuting","text":"Let $X$ be a real reflexive Banach space, and let $C\\subseteq X$ be nonempty, norm closed, bounded, and convex. For any pairwise commuting family $\\mathcal S$ of nonexpansive selfmaps of $C$, the common fixed set \\[ \\operatorname{Fix}(\\mathcal S) =\\{x\\in C:T(x)=x\\text{ for every }T\\in\\mathcal S\\} \\] is nonempty and is a nonexpansive retract of $C$. That is, there is a nonexpansive map $R:C\\to\\operatorname{Fix}(\\mathcal S)$ that is the identity on $\\operatorname{Fix}(\\mathcal S)$.","sourceOffset":1002,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/328.md","scopeText":"The fixed-point question asks whether reflexivity suffices for nonexpansive maps on bounded convex sets. The formalized result gives an affirmative answer: every nonexpansive self-map of a nonempty closed bounded convex subset of a real reflexive Banach space has a fixed point in the original norm. Zero and nonseparable spaces are included, with no uniform convexity, normal structure, or weak-continuity assumption.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/328.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":57607,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Fixed-Points-of-Nonexpansive-Maps-in-Reflexive-Banach-Spaces-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Fixed-Points-of-Nonexpansive-Maps-in-Reflexive-Banach-Spaces-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"329-01","slug":"Counterexamples-to-the-duality-conjecture-for-metric-entropy-September-24-2026","familyId":"329","title":"Counterexamples to the duality conjecture for metric entropy","abstract":"We disprove Pietsch's dimension-free duality conjecture for metric entropy. For every proposed pair of universal constants, we construct origin-symmetric convex bodies that violate the corresponding covering-entropy inequality, already when the covering body is a cube.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves Pietsch's dimension-free duality conjecture for metric entropy. Origin-symmetric convex bodies violate every proposed choice of universal constants in the conjectured comparison between covering numbers and those of the polar bodies, even when the covering body is a cube.","pdfPath":"preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-duality-conjecture-for-metric-entropy-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-duality-conjecture-for-metric-entropy-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-duality-conjecture-for-metric-entropy-September-24-2026","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","texPaths":["preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-duality-conjecture-for-metric-entropy-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-duality-conjecture-for-metric-entropy-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-duality-conjecture-for-metric-entropy-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-duality-conjecture-for-metric-entropy-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-matrix-contract.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-duality-conjecture-for-metric-entropy-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-compression.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-duality-conjecture-for-metric-entropy-September-24-2026/build/figures/pivot-comparison.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-duality-conjecture-for-metric-entropy-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-partitions.tex","preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-duality-conjecture-for-metric-entropy-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-counterexample.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Counterexamples-to-the-duality-conjecture-for-metric-entropy-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove Pietsch's dimension-free duality conjecture for metric entropy. For every proposed pair of universal constants, we construct origin-symmetric convex bodies that violate the corresponding covering-entropy inequality, already when the covering body is a cube.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":315},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Related work","sourceOffset":2037},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and its main mechanism","sourceOffset":5397},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Notation","sourceOffset":9462},{"kind":"section","title":"The matrix construction target","sourceOffset":10035},{"kind":"section","title":"Uniform compression from partitions","sourceOffset":14439},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite-field partitions and separated rows","sourceOffset":28007},{"kind":"section","title":"Choice of parameters and failure of duality","sourceOffset":37475},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A consequence for convexified packing","sourceOffset":41591}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $a,b\\ge1$, there are a positive integer $n$ and an origin-symmetric convex body $K\\subset\\R^n$ such that, with $L=[-1,1]^n$, \\[ \\log N(K,L)>b\\log N(L^\\circ,a^{-1}K^\\circ). \\]","sourceOffset":1441,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Uniform compression","label":"prop:compression","text":"Under the hypotheses above, there is a finite list $\\mathcal A$ of real-valued functions on $X$, of cardinality $Q$, such that every $f\\in\\mathcal F_+$ has an approximant $A\\in\\mathcal A$ satisfying \\begin{equation} f(x)-3\\theta\\le A(x)\\le f(x)+\\theta\\qquad(x\\in X). \\end{equation} In particular, $\\|f-A\\|_{\\infty,X}\\le3\\theta$. The list can be chosen with \\begin{equation} \\log Q\\le hs\\log q+C_{h,\\theta,u}, \\qquad C_{h,\\theta,u} =\\log u+hs2^u\\log\\left(1+\\frac{s2^u}{\\theta}\\right). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":17828,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/329.md","scopeText":"Metric-entropy duality predicts a universal comparison between covering numbers of convex bodies and their polars. The formalized result disproves such a comparison: for every $a,b\\ge1$, there is an origin-symmetric convex body $K$ in a positive finite dimension with $\\log N(K,B_\\infty)>b\\log N(B_\\infty^\\circ,a^{-1}K^\\circ)$, where $N$ is the least number of translates in a finite cover. A further construction makes the dual-to-primal logarithmic entropy ratio tend to zero as the dimension grows.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/329.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":43225,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Counterexamples-to-the-duality-conjecture-for-metric-entropy-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"330-01","slug":"Failure-of-Bounded-Approximation-in-a-Lipschitz-Free-Space-over-a-Uniformly-Discrete-Metric-Space-September-26-2026","familyId":"330","title":"A uniformly discrete counterexample to bounded approximation in Lipschitz-free spaces","abstract":"We construct a countable uniformly discrete metric space whose real Lipschitz-free space has the approximation property but fails the bounded approximation property, answering Kalton's question negatively. The identity can be approximated on every compact set by finite-rank operators, but no uniform bound on their norms is possible.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a countable uniformly discrete metric space whose real Lipschitz-free Banach space has the approximation property but not the bounded approximation property, answering Kalton's question negatively. Finite-rank operators approximate the identity on every compact set, but their norms cannot share a finite bound.","pdfPath":"preprints/Failure-of-Bounded-Approximation-in-a-Lipschitz-Free-Space-over-a-Uniformly-Discrete-Metric-Space-September-26-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Failure-of-Bounded-Approximation-in-a-Lipschitz-Free-Space-over-a-Uniformly-Discrete-Metric-Space-September-26-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Failure-of-Bounded-Approximation-in-a-Lipschitz-Free-Space-over-a-Uniformly-Discrete-Metric-Space-September-26-2026","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","texPaths":["preprints/Failure-of-Bounded-Approximation-in-a-Lipschitz-Free-Space-over-a-Uniformly-Discrete-Metric-Space-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Failure-of-Bounded-Approximation-in-a-Lipschitz-Free-Space-over-a-Uniformly-Discrete-Metric-Space-September-26-2026/build/common.tex","preprints/Failure-of-Bounded-Approximation-in-a-Lipschitz-Free-Space-over-a-Uniformly-Discrete-Metric-Space-September-26-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Failure-of-Bounded-Approximation-in-a-Lipschitz-Free-Space-over-a-Uniformly-Discrete-Metric-Space-September-26-2026/build/sections/reduction.tex","preprints/Failure-of-Bounded-Approximation-in-a-Lipschitz-Free-Space-over-a-Uniformly-Discrete-Metric-Space-September-26-2026/build/sections/blocks.tex","preprints/Failure-of-Bounded-Approximation-in-a-Lipschitz-Free-Space-over-a-Uniformly-Discrete-Metric-Space-September-26-2026/build/sections/operators.tex","preprints/Failure-of-Bounded-Approximation-in-a-Lipschitz-Free-Space-over-a-Uniformly-Discrete-Metric-Space-September-26-2026/build/sections/walks.tex","preprints/Failure-of-Bounded-Approximation-in-a-Lipschitz-Free-Space-over-a-Uniformly-Discrete-Metric-Space-September-26-2026/build/sections/tests.tex","preprints/Failure-of-Bounded-Approximation-in-a-Lipschitz-Free-Space-over-a-Uniformly-Discrete-Metric-Space-September-26-2026/build/sections/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Failure-of-Bounded-Approximation-in-a-Lipschitz-Free-Space-over-a-Uniformly-Discrete-Metric-Space-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a countable uniformly discrete metric space whose real Lipschitz-free space has the approximation property but fails the bounded approximation property, answering Kalton's question negatively. The identity can be approximated on every compact set by finite-rank operators, but no uniform bound on their norms is possible.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":380},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite obstruction in each bounded block","sourceOffset":4272},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The obstruction: signed measures and opposing tests","sourceOffset":8001},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Methods and organization","sourceOffset":13407},{"kind":"section","title":"From bounded blocks to AP without BAP","sourceOffset":14673},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The coefficient estimate","sourceOffset":15232},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The metric wedge and its retractions","sourceOffset":19145},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The approximation properties of the wedge","sourceOffset":22661},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Failure of nonlinear approximability","sourceOffset":25623},{"kind":"section","title":"Colored graphs and the bounded block","sourceOffset":27308},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A deterministic sequence with a colored tree limit","sourceOffset":28710},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Labels, anchors, and the metric","sourceOffset":33199}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a countable, unbounded, nonproper pointed metric space $(M,d,o)$ such that $d(x,y)\\ge1$ for distinct points and $\\F(M)$ has AP but fails $\\lambda$-BAP for every finite $\\lambda\\ge1$.","sourceOffset":2465,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:blocks","text":"For each integer $p\\ge1$, there are a bounded countably infinite pointed metric space $(M_p,d_p,o)$ and a finite set $A_p\\subseteq M_p$ containing $o$ such that \\[ d_p(x,y)\\ge1\\quad(x\\ne y),\\qquad \\operatorname{diam}M_p\\le B_p:=300p^2+150p+5, \\] and every bounded finite-rank linear operator $T_0:\\F(M_p)\\to\\F(M_p)$ with $\\norm{T_0}\\le p$ satisfies \\[ \\max_{a\\in A_p}\\norm{T_0\\delta_a-\\delta_a}\\ge\\frac12. \\]","sourceOffset":5656,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/330.md","scopeText":"Kalton's question asks whether uniform discreteness of a metric space forces its Lipschitz-free space to have the bounded approximation property. The formalization constructs a countable metric space with all distinct points at distance at least one whose real Lipschitz-free space has the approximation property but fails every bounded approximation bound. The metric space is unbounded and not proper.\n\nThe earlier quantitative renorming result for real $\\ell_1$ is retained: for each integer $p\\ge1$, an equivalent complete norm has the $2B_p$-bounded approximation property but fails the $p$-bounded approximation property, where $B_p=300p^2+150p+5$.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/330.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":69662,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Failure-of-Bounded-Approximation-in-a-Lipschitz-Free-Space-over-a-Uniformly-Discrete-Metric-Space-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"331-01","slug":"Asymptotic-midpoint-uniform-convexity-and-unbounded-diamond-distortion-in-a-reflexive-tree-space-September-27-2026","familyId":"331","title":"Asymptotic midpoint uniform convexity and unbounded diamond distortion in a reflexive tree space","abstract":"We construct a separable reflexive real Banach space whose given norm is asymptotically midpoint uniformly convex but which admits no asymptotically uniformly convex equivalent norm. Its averaged midpoint modulus is at least $\\sqrt{1+t^2/12}-1$, and the countably branching diamond of depth k has distortion at least $\\sqrt{1+k/12}$ in this space. This gives a negative answer to the reflexive diamond converse for asymptotic uniform convexifiability.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a real reflexive Banach space with an asymptotically midpoint uniformly convex norm but no equivalent asymptotically uniformly convex norm, extending Baudier's separation to reflexive spaces. In the same space, depth-k countably branching diamonds require distortion at least $\\sqrt{1+k/12}$, so midpoint uniform convexity does not force uniformly bounded diamond distortion even under reflexivity.","pdfPath":"preprints/Asymptotic-midpoint-uniform-convexity-and-unbounded-diamond-distortion-in-a-reflexive-tree-space-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Asymptotic-midpoint-uniform-convexity-and-unbounded-diamond-distortion-in-a-reflexive-tree-space-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Asymptotic-midpoint-uniform-convexity-and-unbounded-diamond-distortion-in-a-reflexive-tree-space-September-27-2026","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","texPaths":["preprints/Asymptotic-midpoint-uniform-convexity-and-unbounded-diamond-distortion-in-a-reflexive-tree-space-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Asymptotic-midpoint-uniform-convexity-and-unbounded-diamond-distortion-in-a-reflexive-tree-space-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Asymptotic-midpoint-uniform-convexity-and-unbounded-diamond-distortion-in-a-reflexive-tree-space-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Asymptotic-midpoint-uniform-convexity-and-unbounded-diamond-distortion-in-a-reflexive-tree-space-September-27-2026/build/sections/forest-definition.tex","preprints/Asymptotic-midpoint-uniform-convexity-and-unbounded-diamond-distortion-in-a-reflexive-tree-space-September-27-2026/build/sections/forest.tex","preprints/Asymptotic-midpoint-uniform-convexity-and-unbounded-diamond-distortion-in-a-reflexive-tree-space-September-27-2026/build/figures/forest-gates.tex","preprints/Asymptotic-midpoint-uniform-convexity-and-unbounded-diamond-distortion-in-a-reflexive-tree-space-September-27-2026/build/sections/obstruction.tex","preprints/Asymptotic-midpoint-uniform-convexity-and-unbounded-diamond-distortion-in-a-reflexive-tree-space-September-27-2026/build/sections/paired.tex","preprints/Asymptotic-midpoint-uniform-convexity-and-unbounded-diamond-distortion-in-a-reflexive-tree-space-September-27-2026/build/figures/paired-cut.tex","preprints/Asymptotic-midpoint-uniform-convexity-and-unbounded-diamond-distortion-in-a-reflexive-tree-space-September-27-2026/build/sections/general-forests.tex","preprints/Asymptotic-midpoint-uniform-convexity-and-unbounded-diamond-distortion-in-a-reflexive-tree-space-September-27-2026/build/sections/diamonds.tex","preprints/Asymptotic-midpoint-uniform-convexity-and-unbounded-diamond-distortion-in-a-reflexive-tree-space-September-27-2026/build/figures/diamond.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Asymptotic-midpoint-uniform-convexity-and-unbounded-diamond-distortion-in-a-reflexive-tree-space-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a separable reflexive real Banach space whose given norm is asymptotically midpoint uniformly convex but which admits no asymptotically uniformly convex equivalent norm. Its averaged midpoint modulus is at least $\\sqrt{1+t^2/12}-1$, and the countably branching diamond of depth $k$ has distortion at least $\\sqrt{1+k/12}$ in this space. This gives a negative answer to the reflexive diamond converse for asymptotic uniform convexifiability.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":499},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The example and result","sourceOffset":5275},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the argument fits together","sourceOffset":7500},{"kind":"section","title":"The coordinate structure of the forest","sourceOffset":9148},{"kind":"section","title":"Why no equivalent norm is AUC","sourceOffset":16134},{"kind":"section","title":"A paired extension of segment tests","sourceOffset":20603},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The coordinate completion on a general forest","sourceOffset":26604},{"kind":"section","title":"From approximate midpoints to diamonds","sourceOffset":29459},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An infinite family of approximate midpoints","sourceOffset":29529},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Adding the loss over the diamond levels","sourceOffset":31108}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"The space $X=J^*$ defined by \\eqref{eq:segment-norm} is infinite dimensional, separable, and reflexive, and has no equivalent AUC norm. Its given norm satisfies \\[ \\widehat\\delta_X(t)\\ge\\sqrt{1+t^2/12}-1\\qquad(t>0). \\] For every integer $k\\ge0$, every embedding of $D_k$ into $X$ has distortion at least $\\sqrt{1+k/12}$.","sourceOffset":6860,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/331.md","scopeText":"The paper constructs a Banach space whose midpoint geometry is asymptotically uniformly convex even though no equivalent norm is asymptotically uniformly convex in the usual one-sided sense. The formalization proves that the full dual of the specified segment-norm completion is infinite-dimensional, separable, and reflexive; its averaged midpoint modulus is at least $\\sqrt{1+t^2/12}-1$ for every $t>0$, while every equivalent norm fails asymptotic uniform convexity. It also proves that an embedding of a depth-$k$ countably branching diamond has distortion at least $\\sqrt{1+k/12}$.\n\nThe general-forest and word-forest results use the closed span of the coordinate functionals. Their conclusions retain the finite-ancestor and equivalent-norm hypotheses, including the scale $\\alpha/(2\\beta)$ whe","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/331.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":34308,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":1,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Asymptotic-midpoint-uniform-convexity-and-unbounded-diamond-distortion-in-a-reflexive-tree-space-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"331-02","slug":"Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026","familyId":"331","title":"Midpoint lenses in segment spaces","abstract":"For a real segment-forest dual with unbounded finite component heights and the real infinite-height coordinate predual, we bound the tail of an arbitrary displacement in a symmetric lens by $2\\sqrt{R^2-\\|x\\|^2}$, where R is the lens radius and x is its finitely supported center. Both given norms are asymptotically midpoint uniformly convex, although neither space admits an equivalent asymptotically uniformly convex norm. The finite-height forest dual is reflexive.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a real reflexive Banach space with an asymptotically midpoint uniformly convex norm but no equivalent asymptotically uniformly convex norm, extending Baudier's separation to reflexive spaces. In the same space, depth-k countably branching diamonds require distortion at least $\\sqrt{1+k/12}$, so midpoint uniform convexity does not force uniformly bounded diamond distortion even under reflexivity.","pdfPath":"preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","texPaths":["preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/forest-definition.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/forest-foundations.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/infinite-foundations.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/atomic.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/figures/opposite-crossing.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/joint-budget.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/midpoint-growth.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/foundation-variants.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/infinite-obstruction.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/clipping.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/primal-additions.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/radial-endpoints.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/joint-consequences.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/presentations.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/toolkit.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/stability.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/endpoint-laws.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/coefficients.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/original-coefficients.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/cancellation-comparisons.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/target-tail-signs.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/sign-trimming.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/sequential-cancellation.tex","preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/sections/qualitative-midpoint.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For a real segment-forest dual with unbounded finite component heights and the real infinite-height coordinate predual, we bound the tail of an arbitrary displacement in a symmetric lens by $2\\sqrt{R^2-\\|x\\|^2}$, where $R$ is the lens radius and $x$ is its finitely supported center. Both given norms are asymptotically midpoint uniformly convex, although neither space admits an equivalent asymptotically uniformly convex norm. The finite-height forest dual is reflexive.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":518},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The space and the tail estimate","sourceOffset":1614},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The mechanism of the proof","sourceOffset":5145},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The other quantities measured by the methods","sourceOffset":7034},{"kind":"section","title":"The coordinate structure of the forest","sourceOffset":10892},{"kind":"section","title":"The infinite-height coordinate predual","sourceOffset":15219},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite convex representations","sourceOffset":22789},{"kind":"section","title":"Cancellation at the boundary","sourceOffset":26513},{"kind":"section","title":"Keeping the coefficient and slack budgets together","sourceOffset":31011},{"kind":"section","title":"Asymptotic midpoint growth","sourceOffset":35481},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Averaged midpoint growth at every radius","sourceOffset":37561},{"kind":"section","title":"Coordinate variants and bounded paths","sourceOffset":38866}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main-tail","text":"Let $Y$ be either the finite-height forest dual $X=J^*$ or the infinite-height coordinate predual $B_\\infty$. For every finite ancestral $H$, every $x,y\\in Y$ with $x=P_Hx$, and every $R\\ge0$ with $\\|x+y\\|,\\|x-y\\|\\le R$, \\begin{equation} \\|Q_Hy\\|\\le2\\sqrt{R^2-\\|x\\|^2}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":4038,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"The completed atomic ball","label":"cor:closed-atomic-ball","text":"Let $\\mathcal A$ be the set of all represented atoms in $X$. Then \\[ B_X=\\overline{\\operatorname{conv}\\mathcal A}^{\\,\\|\\cdot\\|}. \\] Every segment functional $d_S$ has norm one.","sourceOffset":25256,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/331.md","scopeText":"The paper constructs a Banach space whose midpoint geometry is asymptotically uniformly convex even though no equivalent norm is asymptotically uniformly convex in the usual one-sided sense. The formalization proves that the full dual of the specified segment-norm completion is infinite-dimensional, separable, and reflexive; its averaged midpoint modulus is at least $\\sqrt{1+t^2/12}-1$ for every $t>0$, while every equivalent norm fails asymptotic uniform convexity. It also proves that an embedding of a depth-$k$ countably branching diamond has distortion at least $\\sqrt{1+k/12}$.\n\nThe general-forest and word-forest results use the closed span of the coordinate functionals. Their conclusions retain the finite-ancestor and equivalent-norm hypotheses, including the scale $\\alpha/(2\\beta)$ whe","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/331.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":245883,"sourceFileCount":27,"statementCount":53,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Midpoint-lenses-in-segment-spaces-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"331-03","slug":"Diamond-distortion-from-midpoint-and-tree-energies-September-27-2026","familyId":"331","title":"Distortion of countably branching diamonds from midpoint and tree energies","abstract":"We derive quantitative distortion bounds for countably branching diamond graphs from midpoint estimates and direct tree energies. In the dual of a finite-height segment forest, every distortion-D embedding of the depth-k diamond satisfies $D^2\\ge1+k/4$. The same bound holds in the infinite-height coordinate predual. We also obtain power-type distortion bounds for path-cost and recursive tree norms.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a real reflexive Banach space with an asymptotically midpoint uniformly convex norm but no equivalent asymptotically uniformly convex norm, extending Baudier's separation to reflexive spaces. In the same space, depth-k countably branching diamonds require distortion at least $\\sqrt{1+k/12}$, so midpoint uniform convexity does not force uniformly bounded diamond distortion even under reflexivity.","pdfPath":"preprints/Diamond-distortion-from-midpoint-and-tree-energies-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Diamond-distortion-from-midpoint-and-tree-energies-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Diamond-distortion-from-midpoint-and-tree-energies-September-27-2026","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","texPaths":["preprints/Diamond-distortion-from-midpoint-and-tree-energies-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Diamond-distortion-from-midpoint-and-tree-energies-September-27-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Diamond-distortion-from-midpoint-and-tree-energies-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Diamond-distortion-from-midpoint-and-tree-energies-September-27-2026/build/sections/metrics.tex","preprints/Diamond-distortion-from-midpoint-and-tree-energies-September-27-2026/build/figures/diamond-replacement.tex","preprints/Diamond-distortion-from-midpoint-and-tree-energies-September-27-2026/build/sections/direct.tex","preprints/Diamond-distortion-from-midpoint-and-tree-energies-September-27-2026/build/sections/transfer-extensions.tex","preprints/Diamond-distortion-from-midpoint-and-tree-energies-September-27-2026/build/sections/d-applications.tex","preprints/Diamond-distortion-from-midpoint-and-tree-energies-September-27-2026/build/sections/f-applications.tex","preprints/Diamond-distortion-from-midpoint-and-tree-energies-September-27-2026/build/sections/segment-transfer-variants.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Diamond-distortion-from-midpoint-and-tree-energies-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We derive quantitative distortion bounds for countably branching diamond graphs from midpoint estimates and direct tree energies. In the dual of a finite-height segment forest, every distortion-$D$ embedding of the depth-$k$ diamond satisfies $D^2\\ge1+k/4$. The same bound holds in the infinite-height coordinate predual. We also obtain power-type distortion bounds for path-cost and recursive tree norms.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":451},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Midpoint methods and earlier work","sourceOffset":3950},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Direct proofs and the organization of the paper","sourceOffset":7923},{"kind":"section","title":"From infinitely many midpoints to diamond distortion","sourceOffset":10908},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The graph and its slopes","sourceOffset":11856},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite-dimensional pair selection","sourceOffset":15686},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The quadratic loss from the pooled atomic estimate","sourceOffset":17501},{"kind":"section","title":"The segment forest and its atoms","sourceOffset":20451},{"kind":"section","title":"Direct descent with primal tests","sourceOffset":24941},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The graph slopes","sourceOffset":25593},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A correction at the first fresh roots","sourceOffset":26695},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Balancing dyadic sizes along a random descent","sourceOffset":30506}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"met:intro-main","text":"For every integer $k\\ge0$, every distortion-$D$ embedding of $\\mathcal D_k$ into either $X$ or $B_\\infty$ satisfies \\[ D^2\\ge1+k/4. \\]","sourceOffset":2976,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"dir:deterministic-descent","text":"Every embedding of $\\mathcal D_k$ into $X$ with distortion at most $D$ satisfies $k\\le256D^2$.","sourceOffset":29225,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/331.md","scopeText":"The paper constructs a Banach space whose midpoint geometry is asymptotically uniformly convex even though no equivalent norm is asymptotically uniformly convex in the usual one-sided sense. The formalization proves that the full dual of the specified segment-norm completion is infinite-dimensional, separable, and reflexive; its averaged midpoint modulus is at least $\\sqrt{1+t^2/12}-1$ for every $t>0$, while every equivalent norm fails asymptotic uniform convexity. It also proves that an embedding of a depth-$k$ countably branching diamond has distortion at least $\\sqrt{1+k/12}$.\n\nThe general-forest and word-forest results use the closed span of the coordinate functionals. Their conclusions retain the finite-ancestor and equivalent-norm hypotheses, including the scale $\\alpha/(2\\beta)$ whe","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/331.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":123077,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":20,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Diamond-distortion-from-midpoint-and-tree-energies-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"331-04","slug":"Exact-asymptotic-moduli-in-a-Daugavet-subspace-of-L1-September-27-2026","familyId":"331","title":"Exact asymptotic moduli in a Daugavet subspace of L1","abstract":"For an infinite-dimensional real subspace of L1 whose unit ball is totally bounded in measure and whose norm has the Daugavet property, we compute the averaged midpoint and one-sided asymptotic moduli at every unit center. They are $\\max\\{t/2,t-1\\}$ and $\\max\\{0,t-2\\}$, respectively. The same weak-neighborhood geometry excludes every equivalent asymptotically uniformly convex norm. A quantitative realization of the Kadets–Werner construction supplies a space with both hypotheses.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a real reflexive Banach space with an asymptotically midpoint uniformly convex norm but no equivalent asymptotically uniformly convex norm, extending Baudier's separation to reflexive spaces. In the same space, depth-k countably branching diamonds require distortion at least $\\sqrt{1+k/12}$, so midpoint uniform convexity does not force uniformly bounded diamond distortion even under reflexivity.","pdfPath":"preprints/Exact-asymptotic-moduli-in-a-Daugavet-subspace-of-L1-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Exact-asymptotic-moduli-in-a-Daugavet-subspace-of-L1-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Exact-asymptotic-moduli-in-a-Daugavet-subspace-of-L1-September-27-2026","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","texPaths":["preprints/Exact-asymptotic-moduli-in-a-Daugavet-subspace-of-L1-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Exact-asymptotic-moduli-in-a-Daugavet-subspace-of-L1-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Exact-asymptotic-moduli-in-a-Daugavet-subspace-of-L1-September-27-2026/build/figures/moduli.tex","preprints/Exact-asymptotic-moduli-in-a-Daugavet-subspace-of-L1-September-27-2026/build/sections/moduli.tex","preprints/Exact-asymptotic-moduli-in-a-Daugavet-subspace-of-L1-September-27-2026/build/sections/construction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Exact-asymptotic-moduli-in-a-Daugavet-subspace-of-L1-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For an infinite-dimensional real subspace of $L^1$ whose unit ball is totally bounded in measure and whose norm has the Daugavet property, we compute the averaged midpoint and one-sided asymptotic moduli at every unit center. They are $\\max\\{t/2,t-1\\}$ and $\\max\\{0,t-2\\}$, respectively. The same weak-neighborhood geometry excludes every equivalent asymptotically uniformly convex norm. A quantitative realization of the Kadets--Werner construction supplies a space with both hypotheses.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":534},{"kind":"section","title":"Compact clips leave half the mass outside","sourceOffset":8228},{"kind":"section","title":"Almost diametral points in weak neighborhoods","sourceOffset":12202},{"kind":"section","title":"One family of directions gives both curves","sourceOffset":17867},{"kind":"section","title":"Why a different norm cannot be AUC","sourceOffset":20057},{"kind":"section","title":"Constructing the measure-precompact Daugavet space","sourceOffset":21970},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite-dimensional enlargement","sourceOffset":24687},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A heavy-tail estimate","sourceOffset":25933},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof of the enlargement","sourceOffset":28558},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Passage to the closed space","sourceOffset":33467}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:exact","text":"Let $E$ be an infinite-dimensional closed real subspace of $L^1(\\Omega,\\mathbb P)$, where $\\mathbb P$ is a probability measure. Suppose its closed unit ball is totally bounded for convergence in measure and its inherited norm has the Daugavet property. Then every $x\\in S_E$ and $t>0$ satisfy \\[ H_E(x,t)=\\max\\{t/2,t-1\\},\\qquad D_E(x,t)=\\max\\{0,t-2\\}. \\] Consequently these are also the formulas for $\\widehat\\delta_E(t)$ and $\\overline\\delta_E(t)$. The space $E$ admits no equivalent AUC norm.","sourceOffset":3916,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Kadets--Werner construction","label":"kw:existence","text":"There is an infinite-dimensional closed real subspace $E\\subseteq L^1$ such that $B(E)$ is totally bounded for $d$ and has the following slice property: for every $w\\in S(E)$, every nonempty slice \\[ H=\\{y\\in B(E):\\ell(y)>\\alpha\\},\\qquad \\ell\\in E^*,\\quad\\alpha\\in\\R, \\] and every $\\rho>0$, there is $y\\in H\\cap S(E)$ such that $\\norm{w+y}\\ge2-\\rho$. In particular, $E$ has the Daugavet property.","sourceOffset":24208,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/331.md","scopeText":"The paper constructs a Banach space whose midpoint geometry is asymptotically uniformly convex even though no equivalent norm is asymptotically uniformly convex in the usual one-sided sense. The formalization proves that the full dual of the specified segment-norm completion is infinite-dimensional, separable, and reflexive; its averaged midpoint modulus is at least $\\sqrt{1+t^2/12}-1$ for every $t>0$, while every equivalent norm fails asymptotic uniform convexity. It also proves that an embedding of a depth-$k$ countably branching diamond has distortion at least $\\sqrt{1+k/12}$.\n\nThe general-forest and word-forest results use the closed span of the coordinate functionals. Their conclusions retain the finite-ancestor and equivalent-norm hypotheses, including the scale $\\alpha/(2\\beta)$ whe","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/331.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":36434,"sourceFileCount":5,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Exact-asymptotic-moduli-in-a-Daugavet-subspace-of-L1-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"331-05","slug":"Midpoint-convexity-from-bounded-tree-potentials-and-path-costs-September-27-2026","familyId":"331","title":"Midpoint convexity from bounded tree potentials and path costs","abstract":"Four real Banach spaces defined by bounded tree potentials satisfy the averaged asymptotic midpoint bound $\\widehat\\delta(t)\\ge\\sqrt{1+t^2/4}-1$ for $0\\lt t\\lt 1$, while none admits an asymptotically uniformly convex (AUC) renorming. On finite-height trees, the globally constrained norm equals the least additive cost of a Hilbert vector and root paths. The quadratic path and segment-start outer Hilbert sums are reflexive and asymptotically midpoint uniformly convex, and admit no equivalent AUC norm.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a real reflexive Banach space with an asymptotically midpoint uniformly convex norm but no equivalent asymptotically uniformly convex norm, extending Baudier's separation to reflexive spaces. In the same space, depth-k countably branching diamonds require distortion at least $\\sqrt{1+k/12}$, so midpoint uniform convexity does not force uniformly bounded diamond distortion even under reflexivity.","pdfPath":"preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-bounded-tree-potentials-and-path-costs-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-bounded-tree-potentials-and-path-costs-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-bounded-tree-potentials-and-path-costs-September-27-2026","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","texPaths":["preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-bounded-tree-potentials-and-path-costs-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-bounded-tree-potentials-and-path-costs-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-bounded-tree-potentials-and-path-costs-September-27-2026/build/sections/constrained.tex","preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-bounded-tree-potentials-and-path-costs-September-27-2026/build/sections/selections.tex","preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-bounded-tree-potentials-and-path-costs-September-27-2026/build/sections/freeroot.tex","preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-bounded-tree-potentials-and-path-costs-September-27-2026/build/sections/linear.tex","preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-bounded-tree-potentials-and-path-costs-September-27-2026/build/sections/quadratic.tex","preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-bounded-tree-potentials-and-path-costs-September-27-2026/build/sections/segments.tex","preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-bounded-tree-potentials-and-path-costs-September-27-2026/build/figures/segment-matching.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-bounded-tree-potentials-and-path-costs-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Four real Banach spaces defined by bounded tree potentials satisfy the averaged asymptotic midpoint bound $\\widehat\\delta(t)\\ge\\sqrt{1+t^2/4}-1$ for $0<t<1$, while none admits an asymptotically uniformly convex (AUC) renorming. On finite-height trees, the globally constrained norm equals the least additive cost of a Hilbert vector and root paths. The quadratic path and segment-start outer Hilbert sums are reflexive and asymptotically midpoint uniformly convex, and admit no equivalent AUC norm.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":544},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bounded potentials on the infinite tree","sourceOffset":5314},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Path and segment costs on finite-height trees","sourceOffset":7577},{"kind":"section","title":"Four potential norms, one pair of inward tests","sourceOffset":10498},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why every equivalent norm still has bounded branches","sourceOffset":19504},{"kind":"section","title":"Selecting tail tests","sourceOffset":22821},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One of two inward parts","sourceOffset":24191},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Four pieces classified by attachment sign","sourceOffset":26336},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Independent component selection","sourceOffset":29630},{"kind":"section","title":"A free root and a planar perturbation","sourceOffset":33559},{"kind":"section","title":"Linear costs of root paths","sourceOffset":43750},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The norm and its finite heads","sourceOffset":44192}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:four","text":"Each of the four completed spaces $X_K$ is a separable infinite-dimensional real Banach space. For $K\\in\\{K_{\\rm s},K_{\\rm p},K_{\\rm a},K_{\\rm g}\\}$ and $0<t<1$, \\begin{equation} \\widehat\\delta_{X_K}(t)\\ge\\sqrt{1+t^2/4}-1. \\end{equation} None of these spaces admits an equivalent AUC norm.","sourceOffset":13532,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:free-root-modulus","text":"The real Banach space $X_{\\mathrm e}$ defined by \\eqref{fr:tests} and \\eqref{fr:norm} satisfies \\begin{equation} \\widehat\\delta_{\\|\\cdot\\|_{\\mathrm e}}(t) \\ge\\frac{\\sqrt{1+t^2/576}-1}{4}\\qquad(0<t<1). \\end{equation} It admits no equivalent AUC norm. More precisely, if $\\alpha\\|\\cdot\\|_{\\mathrm e}\\le N\\le\\beta\\|\\cdot\\|_{\\mathrm e}$, where $0<\\alpha\\le\\beta<\\infty$, then $\\overline\\delta_N(\\alpha/(4\\beta))=0$.","sourceOffset":36462,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/331.md","scopeText":"The paper constructs a Banach space whose midpoint geometry is asymptotically uniformly convex even though no equivalent norm is asymptotically uniformly convex in the usual one-sided sense. The formalization proves that the full dual of the specified segment-norm completion is infinite-dimensional, separable, and reflexive; its averaged midpoint modulus is at least $\\sqrt{1+t^2/12}-1$ for every $t>0$, while every equivalent norm fails asymptotic uniform convexity. It also proves that an embedding of a depth-$k$ countably branching diamond has distortion at least $\\sqrt{1+k/12}$.\n\nThe general-forest and word-forest results use the closed span of the coordinate functionals. Their conclusions retain the finite-ancestor and equivalent-norm hypotheses, including the scale $\\alpha/(2\\beta)$ whe","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/331.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":174159,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":34,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Midpoint-convexity-from-bounded-tree-potentials-and-path-costs-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"331-06","slug":"Independent-products-in-real-L1-asymptotic-midpoint-convexity-without-AUC-renormings-September-27-2026","familyId":"331","title":"Independent products in real L1: asymptotic midpoint convexity without AUC renormings","abstract":"For a countably branching tree, the closed real L1 spans of products of independent exponential or Gaussian-square multipliers along its paths have positive averaged asymptotic midpoint moduli and admit no equivalent asymptotically uniformly convex norm. The renorming obstruction holds for every positive nonconstant mean-one multiplier with finite second moment.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a real reflexive Banach space with an asymptotically midpoint uniformly convex norm but no equivalent asymptotically uniformly convex norm, extending Baudier's separation to reflexive spaces. In the same space, depth-k countably branching diamonds require distortion at least $\\sqrt{1+k/12}$, so midpoint uniform convexity does not force uniformly bounded diamond distortion even under reflexivity.","pdfPath":"preprints/Independent-products-in-real-L1-asymptotic-midpoint-convexity-without-AUC-renormings-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Independent-products-in-real-L1-asymptotic-midpoint-convexity-without-AUC-renormings-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Independent-products-in-real-L1-asymptotic-midpoint-convexity-without-AUC-renormings-September-27-2026","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","texPaths":["preprints/Independent-products-in-real-L1-asymptotic-midpoint-convexity-without-AUC-renormings-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Independent-products-in-real-L1-asymptotic-midpoint-convexity-without-AUC-renormings-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Independent-products-in-real-L1-asymptotic-midpoint-convexity-without-AUC-renormings-September-27-2026/build/sections/conditional.tex","preprints/Independent-products-in-real-L1-asymptotic-midpoint-convexity-without-AUC-renormings-September-27-2026/build/figures/first-exit.tex","preprints/Independent-products-in-real-L1-asymptotic-midpoint-convexity-without-AUC-renormings-September-27-2026/build/sections/exponential.tex","preprints/Independent-products-in-real-L1-asymptotic-midpoint-convexity-without-AUC-renormings-September-27-2026/build/sections/gaussian.tex","preprints/Independent-products-in-real-L1-asymptotic-midpoint-convexity-without-AUC-renormings-September-27-2026/build/sections/obstruction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Independent-products-in-real-L1-asymptotic-midpoint-convexity-without-AUC-renormings-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For a countably branching tree, the closed real $L^1$ spans of products of independent exponential or Gaussian-square multipliers along its paths have positive averaged asymptotic midpoint moduli and admit no equivalent asymptotically uniformly convex norm. The renorming obstruction holds for every positive nonconstant mean-one multiplier with finite second moment.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":413},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and method","sourceOffset":3806},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite conditional kernels and fresh multipliers","sourceOffset":7406},{"kind":"section","title":"Exponential products: a uniform excess estimate","sourceOffset":17681},{"kind":"section","title":"Gaussian squares: two scalar mechanisms","sourceOffset":21938},{"kind":"section","title":"Bounded paths exclude AUC renormings","sourceOffset":27460}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every multiplier $W$ as above, the Banach space $X_W$ is infinite dimensional and has no equivalent AUC norm. For the exponential and Gaussian-square laws, the inherited norms satisfy \\begin{align} \\whdelta_{X_{\\mathrm{exp}}}(t) &\\ge \\frac{7t}{30}c(10/t)>0 &&(t>0),\\\\ \\whdelta_{X_{\\mathrm{sq}}}(t) &\\ge\\frac{\\PP\\bigl(|G|\\ge4\\sqrt2(2+\\sqrt2)/t\\bigr)}{8}>0 &&(t>0),\\\\ \\whdelta_{X_{\\mathrm{sq}}}(t) &\\ge\\frac{\\PP\\bigl(|G|\\ge8\\sqrt6/t\\bigr)}{4}>0 &&(t>0). \\end{align}","sourceOffset":2912,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:exponential","text":"For every $t,K>0$, the exponential product space satisfies \\begin{equation} \\whdelta_{X_{\\mathrm{exp}}}(t) \\ge c(K)\\left(\\frac t3-\\frac1K\\right)_+. \\end{equation} In particular, the exponential estimate in Theorem \\ref{thm:main} holds.","sourceOffset":19891,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/331.md","scopeText":"The paper constructs a Banach space whose midpoint geometry is asymptotically uniformly convex even though no equivalent norm is asymptotically uniformly convex in the usual one-sided sense. The formalization proves that the full dual of the specified segment-norm completion is infinite-dimensional, separable, and reflexive; its averaged midpoint modulus is at least $\\sqrt{1+t^2/12}-1$ for every $t>0$, while every equivalent norm fails asymptotic uniform convexity. It also proves that an embedding of a depth-$k$ countably branching diamond has distortion at least $\\sqrt{1+k/12}$.\n\nThe general-forest and word-forest results use the closed span of the coordinate functionals. Their conclusions retain the finite-ancestor and equivalent-norm hypotheses, including the scale $\\alpha/(2\\beta)$ whe","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/331.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":30965,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Independent-products-in-real-L1-asymptotic-midpoint-convexity-without-AUC-renormings-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"331-07","slug":"Midpoint-convexity-from-two-recursive-potentials-September-27-2026","familyId":"331","title":"Midpoint convexity from two recursive potentials","abstract":"We study tree norms computed by two least nonnegative fields whose difference is the vector. For Euclidean child aggregation, two root-sum spaces have an averaged asymptotic midpoint modulus of at least $t^3/128$ for $0\\lt t\\lt 1$, including a reflexive joining-root space. A reflexive construction with a fixed zero root also satisfies a homogeneous cubic estimate. These spaces admit no equivalent asymptotically uniformly convex norm. We also obtain sixth-power and cubic estimates when the aggregation exponent depends on the height of a finite component.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a real reflexive Banach space with an asymptotically midpoint uniformly convex norm but no equivalent asymptotically uniformly convex norm, extending Baudier's separation to reflexive spaces. In the same space, depth-k countably branching diamonds require distortion at least $\\sqrt{1+k/12}$, so midpoint uniform convexity does not force uniformly bounded diamond distortion even under reflexivity.","pdfPath":"preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-two-recursive-potentials-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-two-recursive-potentials-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-two-recursive-potentials-September-27-2026","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","texPaths":["preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-two-recursive-potentials-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-two-recursive-potentials-September-27-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-two-recursive-potentials-September-27-2026/build/sections/foundations.tex","preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-two-recursive-potentials-September-27-2026/build/sections/energy.tex","preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-two-recursive-potentials-September-27-2026/build/figures/first-exit.tex","preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-two-recursive-potentials-September-27-2026/build/sections/obstruction.tex","preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-two-recursive-potentials-September-27-2026/build/sections/stopping.tex","preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-two-recursive-potentials-September-27-2026/build/sections/variable.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Midpoint-convexity-from-two-recursive-potentials-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We study tree norms computed by two least nonnegative fields whose difference is the vector. For Euclidean child aggregation, two root-sum spaces have an averaged asymptotic midpoint modulus of at least $t^3/128$ for $0<t<1$, including a reflexive joining-root space. A reflexive construction with a fixed zero root also satisfies a homogeneous cubic estimate. These spaces admit no equivalent asymptotically uniformly convex norm. We also obtain sixth-power and cubic estimates when the aggregation exponent depends on the height of a finite component.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":599},{"kind":"section","title":"The fields, the roots, and the completed spaces","sourceOffset":7690},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Three Euclidean constructions","sourceOffset":9135},{"kind":"section","title":"From root defect to boundary energy","sourceOffset":17000},{"kind":"section","title":"Bounded paths exclude AUC renormings","sourceOffset":24840},{"kind":"section","title":"Stopping the propagation of normalized defects","sourceOffset":29246},{"kind":"section","title":"Logarithmic propagation on the joined tree","sourceOffset":41162},{"kind":"section","title":"Changing the child exponent with the height","sourceOffset":50766},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The norm and its completion","sourceOffset":51740},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A boundary quantity and a sixth-power estimate","sourceOffset":57227},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Accumulating the exit energy","sourceOffset":63898}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"rec:main","text":"For $N=N_\\Sigma$ on $X_\\Sigma$, or $N=N_J$ on $X_J$, one has \\begin{equation} \\widehat\\delta_N(t)\\ge t^3/128\\qquad(0<t<1). \\end{equation} For $X_0$, a finite initial set $H$, a vector $x$ supported in $H$, and $z\\in F_H\\setminus\\{0\\}$, one has \\begin{equation} \\frac{N_0(x+z)+N_0(x-z)}2\\ge N_0(x) +\\frac{N_0(z)^3}{8(2N_0(x)+N_0(z))^2}. \\end{equation} Each of $X_\\Sigma,X_0,X_J$ admits no equivalent AUC norm.","sourceOffset":10570,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"rec:completion","text":"These formulas define infinite-dimensional Banach spaces after completion. Coordinate evaluations and the fields extend continuously. If $H$ is a finite set containing the root and all predecessors of its nodes, then coordinate restriction $\\Pi_H$ is a contractive projection. Its kernel $F_H$ has finite codimension, and finitely supported elements of $F_H$ are dense in $F_H$. Both $X_0$ and $X_J$ are reflexive.","sourceOffset":11472,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/331.md","scopeText":"The paper constructs a Banach space whose midpoint geometry is asymptotically uniformly convex even though no equivalent norm is asymptotically uniformly convex in the usual one-sided sense. The formalization proves that the full dual of the specified segment-norm completion is infinite-dimensional, separable, and reflexive; its averaged midpoint modulus is at least $\\sqrt{1+t^2/12}-1$ for every $t>0$, while every equivalent norm fails asymptotic uniform convexity. It also proves that an embedding of a depth-$k$ countably branching diamond has distortion at least $\\sqrt{1+k/12}$.\n\nThe general-forest and word-forest results use the closed span of the coordinate functionals. Their conclusions retain the finite-ancestor and equivalent-norm hypotheses, including the scale $\\alpha/(2\\beta)$ whe","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/331.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":72591,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Midpoint-convexity-from-two-recursive-potentials-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"332-01","slug":"Metric-Markov-Cotype-Two-of-l1-October-5-2026","familyId":"332","title":"Metric Markov Cotype Two of ℓ1","abstract":"We prove that the real Banach space ℓ1 has metric Markov cotype two, answering a question of Mendel and Naor. As a consequence, every Lipschitz map from an arbitrary subset of a real Hilbert space into ℓ1 extends to the whole Hilbert space with a universal multiplicative loss in its Lipschitz constant, resolving Ball's extension problem for this target.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that real ℓ1 has metric Markov cotype two, answering Mendel and Naor's question. Consequently, every Lipschitz map from an arbitrary subset of a real Hilbert space into ℓ1 extends to the whole space with a universal multiplicative loss in its Lipschitz constant, resolving Ball's extension problem for this target.","pdfPath":"preprints/Metric-Markov-Cotype-Two-of-l1-October-5-2026/l1-markov-cotype.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Metric-Markov-Cotype-Two-of-l1-October-5-2026/l1-markov-cotype.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Metric-Markov-Cotype-Two-of-l1-October-5-2026","subjectId":"11","subject":"Functional analysis","subjectZh":"泛函分析","texPaths":["preprints/Metric-Markov-Cotype-Two-of-l1-October-5-2026/build/main.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Metric-Markov-Cotype-Two-of-l1-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the real Banach space $\\ell_1$ has metric Markov cotype two, answering a question of Mendel and Naor. As a consequence, every Lipschitz map from an arbitrary subset of a real Hilbert space into $\\ell_1$ extends to the whole Hilbert space with a universal multiplicative loss in its Lipschitz constant, resolving Ball's extension problem for this target.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":414},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":2353},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The nonlinear construction","sourceOffset":4445},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite cuts and reconstruction","sourceOffset":6861},{"kind":"section","title":"A flat map and a quartic potential","sourceOffset":9645},{"kind":"section","title":"Geometric and Ces\\`aro endpoint costs","sourceOffset":13299},{"kind":"section","title":"The stopped walk and the main theorem","sourceOffset":15492},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A martingale that realizes the two costs","sourceOffset":16419},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Completion of the proof","sourceOffset":18503},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An expected-median interpretation","sourceOffset":19626},{"kind":"section","title":"Complex scalars and Lipschitz extension","sourceOffset":20686}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"The real space $\\ell_1$ satisfies \\[ N_2(\\ell_1)\\leq12\\sqrt{21}. \\]","sourceOffset":2010,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:martingale","text":"Let $(M_m)_{m\\geq0}$ be an $H$-valued martingale with respect to a filtration $(\\calF_m)$, taking values in $Q$ and converging almost surely to $M_\\infty$. If $e$ is an $\\calF_0$-measurable binary vector, then \\begin{align} \\E\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty \\|\\Phi(M_{m+1})-\\Phi(M_m)\\|_{1,w}^2 &\\leq108\\bigl(\\E\\|M_\\infty-e\\|_H^4 -\\E\\|M_0-e\\|_H^4\\bigr)\\notag\\\\ &\\leq108\\E\\|M_\\infty-e\\|_H^4. \\end{align}","sourceOffset":12189,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Mendel--Naor","label":"def:cotype","text":"A metric space $(X,d)$ has metric Markov cotype two with constant $C>0$ if, for every $n,t\\in\\N$, every probability vector $\\pi$, every stochastic matrix $A$ reversible with respect to $\\pi$, and every $x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\in X$, there exist $y_1,\\ldots,y_n\\in X$ such that \\begin{equation} \\sum_i\\pi_i d(x_i,y_i)^2 +t\\sum_{i,j}\\pi_i a_{ij}d(y_i,y_j)^2 \\leq C^2\\sum_{i,j}\\pi_i \\left(\\frac1t\\sum_{s=1}^t A^s\\right)_{ij}d(x_i,x_j)^2. \\end{equation} The infimum of all such constants is denoted $N_2(X)$.","sourceOffset":1343,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":22674,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Metric-Markov-Cotype-Two-of-l1-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Metric-Markov-Cotype-Two-of-l1-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Metric-Markov-Cotype-Two-of-l1-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"333-01","slug":"Smooth-isometric-immersions-of-closed-surfaces-into-Euclidean-four-space-September-23-2026","familyId":"333","title":"Smooth isometric immersions of closed surfaces into Euclidean four-space","abstract":"Every closed smooth Riemannian surface admits a smooth isometric immersion into Euclidean four-space, without an orientability assumption. This resolves the closed-surface form of the classical four-dimensional isometric-immersion problem.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Every closed smooth Riemannian surface admits a smooth isometric immersion into ℝ4, resolving the closed-surface form of the four-dimensional isometric-immersion problem. This includes nonorientable surfaces and metrics of arbitrary Gaussian curvature.","pdfPath":"preprints/Smooth-isometric-immersions-of-closed-surfaces-into-Euclidean-four-space-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Smooth-isometric-immersions-of-closed-surfaces-into-Euclidean-four-space-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Smooth-isometric-immersions-of-closed-surfaces-into-Euclidean-four-space-September-23-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Smooth-isometric-immersions-of-closed-surfaces-into-Euclidean-four-space-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Smooth-isometric-immersions-of-closed-surfaces-into-Euclidean-four-space-September-23-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/Smooth-isometric-immersions-of-closed-surfaces-into-Euclidean-four-space-September-23-2026/build/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Smooth-isometric-immersions-of-closed-surfaces-into-Euclidean-four-space-September-23-2026/build/primitive-statement.tex","preprints/Smooth-isometric-immersions-of-closed-surfaces-into-Euclidean-four-space-September-23-2026/build/small-modes.tex","preprints/Smooth-isometric-immersions-of-closed-surfaces-into-Euclidean-four-space-September-23-2026/build/exact-correction.tex","preprints/Smooth-isometric-immersions-of-closed-surfaces-into-Euclidean-four-space-September-23-2026/build/velocity-loop.tex","preprints/Smooth-isometric-immersions-of-closed-surfaces-into-Euclidean-four-space-September-23-2026/build/figure-geometry.tex","preprints/Smooth-isometric-immersions-of-closed-surfaces-into-Euclidean-four-space-September-23-2026/build/primitive-realization.tex","preprints/Smooth-isometric-immersions-of-closed-surfaces-into-Euclidean-four-space-September-23-2026/build/geometry-preservation.tex","preprints/Smooth-isometric-immersions-of-closed-surfaces-into-Euclidean-four-space-September-23-2026/build/global-construction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Smooth-isometric-immersions-of-closed-surfaces-into-Euclidean-four-space-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every closed smooth Riemannian surface admits a smooth isometric immersion into Euclidean four-space, without an orientability assumption. This resolves the closed-surface form of the classical four-dimensional isometric-immersion problem.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":302},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context","sourceOffset":1285},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction","sourceOffset":5071},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":8218},{"kind":"section","title":"Normal geometry and admissible phases","sourceOffset":8878},{"kind":"section","title":"The primitive metric addition","sourceOffset":20084},{"kind":"section","title":"Small oscillatory metric increments","sourceOffset":26787},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weighted bounds and the increment statement","sourceOffset":27969},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The conjugated linearized equation","sourceOffset":31752},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A fixed exponent for polynomial perturbations","sourceOffset":34493},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The quadratic mean and its finite adjustment","sourceOffset":38001},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cancellation and the cubic remainder","sourceOffset":42425}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Every closed smooth Riemannian surface $(M,g)$ admits a $C^\\infty$ isometric immersion into $(\\R^4,\\delta_4)$, where $\\delta_4=\\sum_{i=1}^4 dx_i^2$.","sourceOffset":861,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Primitive addition with preserved boundary conditions","label":"prop:primitive-addition","text":"Let $M$ be a closed smooth surface and $F:M\\to\\R^4$ a smooth immersion whose second fundamental form is nonzero as a tensor at every point. Assume $F$ has a preferred normal $n$. Let $D$ be a disk with smooth boundary $C_0$ and coordinates $(x,y)$ on a neighborhood of $\\overline D$. Let $a\\in C^\\infty(M)$ be nonnegative, positive exactly on $D$, and put $Y_0=\\partial_y$. Suppose \\begin{equation} \\Hess_{\\gamma(F)}x(\\partial_y,\\partial_y)>0 \\quad\\text{on }\\overline D. \\end{equation} Let $C_1,\\ldots,C_m$ be finitely many later curves with nonzero smooth vector fields $Y_i$ on their neighborhoods. Assume the following: \\begin{enumerate} \\item All curves $C_0,\\ldots,C_m$ are pairwise transverse and have no triple intersection. \\item On the portion of each later curve $C_i$, $1\\le i\\le m$, in $\\overline D$, the vector $\\partial_y$ is tangent to $C_i$ at only finitely many points. \\item At every $C_i\\cap C_l\\cap D$ with distinct $i,l>0$, $dx(Y_i)\\ne0$ and $dx(Y_l)\\ne0$. \\item Conditions \\eqref{eq:boundary-invariants} hold for all indices $0,\\ldots,m$. \\end{enumerate} Then, for every $\\varepsilon>0$, there are a smooth immersion $F_+:M\\to\\R^4$ and a preferred normal $n_+$ such that \\[ \\gamma(F_+)=\\gamma_+:=\\gamma(F)+a^2\\,dx^2, \\qquad B_{F_+}\\ne0\\ \\text{at every point}, \\] conditions \\eqref{eq:boundary-invariants} hold for $(F_+,n_+)$ and all later indices $1,\\ldots,m$, and \\[ \\|F_+-F\\|_{C^2(M\\setminus D)}<\\varepsilon. \\] The tensor $a^2dx^2$ is extended by zero outside the coordinate","sourceOffset":22383,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":null,"text":"A nonzero covector $\\xi\\in T_p^*M$ is good for $F$ if $B_F(Y,Y)\\ne0$ for a nonzero vector $Y\\in\\ker\\xi$. This condition is independent of the choice of $Y$ in that line.","sourceOffset":10833,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/333.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves that every closed smooth Riemannian surface admits a smooth isometric immersion into $\\mathbb R^4$. The differential preserves the Riemannian inner product on every tangent space. No orientability assumption is imposed, and the statement asks for an immersion rather than an embedding.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/333.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":130244,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Smooth-isometric-immersions-of-closed-surfaces-into-Euclidean-four-space-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"334-01","slug":"A-Smooth-Metric-with-No-Local-Isometric-Immersion-into-Three-Space-September-24-2026","familyId":"334","title":"A Smooth Metric with No Local Isometric Immersion into Three-Space","abstract":"We construct a smooth positive-definite Riemannian metric on $(-1,1)^2$ that agrees with the Euclidean metric to every order at the origin, yet no neighborhood of the origin admits a smooth isometric immersion into Euclidean three-space. This gives a negative answer to the unrestricted smooth local isometric realization problem for surfaces.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a smooth positive-definite metric on $(-1,1)^2$ for which no neighborhood of the origin admits a smooth isometric immersion into ℝ3. This answers the unrestricted smooth local isometric realization problem for surfaces negatively, even after shrinking the neighborhood.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Smooth-Metric-with-No-Local-Isometric-Immersion-into-Three-Space-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Smooth-Metric-with-No-Local-Isometric-Immersion-into-Three-Space-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Smooth-Metric-with-No-Local-Isometric-Immersion-into-Three-Space-September-24-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-Smooth-Metric-with-No-Local-Isometric-Immersion-into-Three-Space-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-Smooth-Metric-with-No-Local-Isometric-Immersion-into-Three-Space-September-24-2026/build/sections/model.tex","preprints/A-Smooth-Metric-with-No-Local-Isometric-Immersion-into-Three-Space-September-24-2026/build/figures/geometry.tex","preprints/A-Smooth-Metric-with-No-Local-Isometric-Immersion-into-Three-Space-September-24-2026/build/sections/caps.tex","preprints/A-Smooth-Metric-with-No-Local-Isometric-Immersion-into-Three-Space-September-24-2026/build/figures/cap.tex","preprints/A-Smooth-Metric-with-No-Local-Isometric-Immersion-into-Three-Space-September-24-2026/build/sections/pulse.tex","preprints/A-Smooth-Metric-with-No-Local-Isometric-Immersion-into-Three-Space-September-24-2026/build/figures/pulse.tex","preprints/A-Smooth-Metric-with-No-Local-Isometric-Immersion-into-Three-Space-September-24-2026/build/sections/assembly.tex","preprints/A-Smooth-Metric-with-No-Local-Isometric-Immersion-into-Three-Space-September-24-2026/build/figures/accumulation.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Smooth-Metric-with-No-Local-Isometric-Immersion-into-Three-Space-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a smooth positive-definite Riemannian metric on \\((-1,1)^2\\) that agrees with the Euclidean metric to every order at the origin, yet no neighborhood of the origin admits a smooth isometric immersion into Euclidean three-space. This gives a negative answer to the unrestricted smooth local isometric realization problem for surfaces.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":391},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Curvature and regularity","sourceOffset":1824},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ideas and proof structure","sourceOffset":4329},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":8827},{"kind":"section","title":"The model patch and the height equation","sourceOffset":9366},{"kind":"section","title":"Uniform regularity on caps","sourceOffset":15292},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The differentiated equation","sourceOffset":17879},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A weighted gradient estimate","sourceOffset":22902},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Completion of the cap induction","sourceOffset":33662},{"kind":"section","title":"The oscillatory pulse and the patch obstruction","sourceOffset":35184},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A bounded solution class","sourceOffset":35750},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The test metrics and smooth data at the pulse edges","sourceOffset":39259}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"maintheorem","name":"","label":null,"text":"There is a \\(C^\\infty\\) positive definite Riemannian metric \\(g\\) on \\((-1,1)^2\\) such that no open neighborhood \\(U\\) of the origin admits a \\(C^\\infty\\) isometric immersion \\[ F:(U,g)\\longrightarrow\\R^3. \\] In particular, no such neighborhood admits a smooth isometric embedding.","sourceOffset":1061,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Patch obstruction","label":"prop:patch","text":"The metrics in \\(\\mathcal U\\) admitting no admissible height form a residual subset of \\(\\mathcal U\\). In particular, for every \\(C^\\infty\\) neighborhood of \\(g_0\\) in \\(\\mathcal U\\), there is a metric in that neighborhood with no admissible height.","sourceOffset":12762,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/334.md","scopeText":"The local isometric-immersion problem asks whether every smooth surface metric can be realized locally in Euclidean three-space. The formalized counterexample is a smooth positive-definite metric on $(-1,1)^2$ for which no neighborhood of the origin admits a smooth isometric immersion into $\\mathbb R^3$. The obstruction applies to every smooth candidate map on every such neighborhood.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/334.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":67070,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Smooth-Metric-with-No-Local-Isometric-Immersion-into-Three-Space-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/A-Smooth-Metric-with-No-Local-Isometric-Immersion-into-Three-Space-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"335-01","slug":"An-integral-scalar-curvature-bound-for-real-simplicial-volume-October-5-2026","familyId":"335","title":"An integral scalar curvature bound for real simplicial volume","abstract":"We prove the integral scalar-curvature inequality proposed by Gromov. For every dimension n ≥ 3, there is a constant $a_n\\gt 0$, depending only on n, such that $\\displaystyle \\int_M(\\mathop{\\mathrm{Scal}}\\nolimits _g^-)^{n/2}\\,dV_g\\ge a_n\\|M\\|$ for every closed connected oriented smooth n-manifold M and every smooth Riemannian metric g. Here $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Scal}}\\nolimits _g^-:=\\max\\{0,-\\mathop{\\mathrm{Scal}}\\nolimits _g\\}$, and $\\|M\\|$ is real simplicial volume. The proof uses the nonnegative-scalar-curvature vanishing theorem of the companion paper on rational inessentiality.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves $\\int_M(\\mathrm{Scal}_g^-)^{n/2}\\,dV_g\\ge a_n\\lVert M\\rVert$ for every closed connected oriented smooth n-manifold, n ≥ 3, and every smooth metric, with $a_n\\gt 0$ depending only on dimension. Here $\\mathrm{Scal}_g^-=\\max\\{0,-\\mathrm{Scal}_g\\}$ and $\\lVert M\\rVert$ is real simplicial volume. Also proves rational inessentiality under positive scalar curvature, resolving the Gromov–Lawson conjecture; every nonnegative-scalar-curvature metric on a closed aspherical manifold is flat.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-integral-scalar-curvature-bound-for-real-simplicial-volume-October-5-2026/v126-proof.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-integral-scalar-curvature-bound-for-real-simplicial-volume-October-5-2026/v126-proof.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-integral-scalar-curvature-bound-for-real-simplicial-volume-October-5-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/An-integral-scalar-curvature-bound-for-real-simplicial-volume-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/An-integral-scalar-curvature-bound-for-real-simplicial-volume-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/An-integral-scalar-curvature-bound-for-real-simplicial-volume-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-reduction-detector.tex","preprints/An-integral-scalar-curvature-bound-for-real-simplicial-volume-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-graphs.tex","preprints/An-integral-scalar-curvature-bound-for-real-simplicial-volume-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-entropy.tex","preprints/An-integral-scalar-curvature-bound-for-real-simplicial-volume-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-degree.tex","preprints/An-integral-scalar-curvature-bound-for-real-simplicial-volume-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-transfer.tex","preprints/An-integral-scalar-curvature-bound-for-real-simplicial-volume-October-5-2026/build/figures/resolution.tex","preprints/An-integral-scalar-curvature-bound-for-real-simplicial-volume-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-integral-scalar-curvature-bound-for-real-simplicial-volume-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the integral scalar-curvature inequality proposed by Gromov. For every dimension \\(n\\ge3\\), there is a constant \\(a_n>0\\), depending only on \\(n\\), such that \\[ \\int_M(\\Scal_g^-)^{n/2}\\,dV_g\\ge a_n\\|M\\| \\] for every closed connected oriented smooth \\(n\\)-manifold \\(M\\) and every smooth Riemannian metric \\(g\\). Here \\(\\Scal_g^-:=\\max\\{0,-\\Scal_g\\}\\), and \\(\\|M\\|\\) is real simplicial volume. The proof uses the nonnegative-scalar-curvature vanishing theorem of the companion paper on rational inessentiality.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":585},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The route to the estimate","sourceOffset":4140},{"kind":"section","title":"Reduction and the bounded cohomology detector","sourceOffset":10620},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The conformal reduction","sourceOffset":10981},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A bounded cocycle and its probability form","sourceOffset":13969},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Measured equivariant families","sourceOffset":19928},{"kind":"section","title":"Weighted graph deformations and a sweep","sourceOffset":22999},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The graph identity","sourceOffset":24184},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prescribed graphs on complete leaves","sourceOffset":30954},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Localization of the positive prescription","sourceOffset":44927},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A sweep of the root probabilities","sourceOffset":63207},{"kind":"section","title":"Entropy metrics with controlled labels","sourceOffset":84931}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\), there is a constant \\(a_n>0\\), depending only on \\(n\\), such that every closed connected oriented smooth \\(n\\)-manifold \\(M\\) and every smooth Riemannian metric \\(g\\) on \\(M\\) satisfy \\begin{equation} \\int_M(\\Scal_g^-)^{n/2}\\,dV_g\\ge a_n\\|M\\|. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":3492,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"OpenAI, Corollary 1.5 of \\cite{OpenAIScalarInessentiality2026}","label":"thm:vanishing-input","text":"Let \\(M\\) be a closed connected oriented smooth manifold of positive dimension. If \\(M\\) admits a smooth Riemannian metric with nonnegative scalar curvature, then its real simplicial volume is zero.","sourceOffset":11089,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Controlled measured band","label":"def:degree-band","text":"Let $T>0$. A controlled measured $d$-band over $Q_T^d$ consists of a measured space of oriented $d$-manifolds as above, a leafwise metric $h$, a function $\\phi$, and a map $G=(G_1,\\ldots,G_d):X\\to Q_T^d$. For every $0<t<T$ the part $\\{|G|_\\infty\\le t\\}$ has a countable flow-box cover with the following properties. \\begin{enumerate} \\item The boxes have one common positive size and fixed positive buffers: their smaller concentric boxes cover the indicated part. They can be chosen inside $\\{|G|_\\infty<t'\\}$ for some $t<t'<T$. After rescaling coordinates we may, and do, call them unit boxes with ","sourceOffset":148243,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":316311,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-integral-scalar-curvature-bound-for-real-simplicial-volume-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"335-02","slug":"Positive-scalar-curvature-forces-rational-inessentiality-September-23-2026","familyId":"335","title":"Positive scalar curvature forces rational inessentiality","abstract":"We prove that every closed connected oriented smooth manifold admitting strictly positive scalar curvature is rationally inessential: its rational fundamental class maps to zero under the classifying map. No spin or fundamental-group hypothesis is needed, and there is no upper dimension bound. This proves the Gromov–Lawson aspherical conjecture; moreover, every nonnegative-scalar-curvature metric on a closed aspherical manifold is flat. We also show that every closed oriented smooth manifold of positive dimension with nonnegative scalar curvature has zero real simplicial volume, proving the qualitative vanishing consequence of Gromov's conjectural comparison.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves $\\int_M(\\mathrm{Scal}_g^-)^{n/2}\\,dV_g\\ge a_n\\lVert M\\rVert$ for every closed connected oriented smooth n-manifold, n ≥ 3, and every smooth metric, with $a_n\\gt 0$ depending only on dimension. Here $\\mathrm{Scal}_g^-=\\max\\{0,-\\mathrm{Scal}_g\\}$ and $\\lVert M\\rVert$ is real simplicial volume. Also proves rational inessentiality under positive scalar curvature, resolving the Gromov–Lawson conjecture; every nonnegative-scalar-curvature metric on a closed aspherical manifold is flat.","pdfPath":"preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-forces-rational-inessentiality-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-forces-rational-inessentiality-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-forces-rational-inessentiality-September-23-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-forces-rational-inessentiality-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-forces-rational-inessentiality-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-forces-rational-inessentiality-September-23-2026/build/figures/direction-splitting.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-forces-rational-inessentiality-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-reductions.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-forces-rational-inessentiality-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-comparison.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-forces-rational-inessentiality-September-23-2026/build/figures/degree-triad.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-forces-rational-inessentiality-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-graph-calculus.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-forces-rational-inessentiality-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-graph-existence.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-forces-rational-inessentiality-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-shortening.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-forces-rational-inessentiality-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-obstruction.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-forces-rational-inessentiality-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-proof.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-forces-rational-inessentiality-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every closed connected oriented smooth manifold admitting strictly positive scalar curvature is rationally inessential: its rational fundamental class maps to zero under the classifying map. No spin or fundamental-group hypothesis is needed, and there is no upper dimension bound. This proves the Gromov--Lawson aspherical conjecture; moreover, every nonnegative-scalar-curvature metric on a closed aspherical manifold is flat. We also show that every closed oriented smooth manifold of positive dimension with nonnegative scalar curvature has zero real simplicial volume, proving the qualitative vanishing consequence of Gromov's conjectural comparison.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":760},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From a detected class to contracting maps","sourceOffset":2201},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Consequences","sourceOffset":11216},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The obstruction problem and earlier work","sourceOffset":14959},{"kind":"section","title":"Conventions and the nonnegative scalar-curvature reduction","sourceOffset":19514},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fundamental classes and degree","sourceOffset":20495},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The scalar-flat alternative","sourceOffset":23062},{"kind":"section","title":"Compact pairings and comparison coordinates","sourceOffset":26807},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Probability maps and a finite parameter manifold","sourceOffset":30015},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A compactly supported pairing","sourceOffset":40379},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Distance profiles","sourceOffset":48516},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From a relative pairing to local degree","sourceOffset":53559}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Rational inessentiality","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $M^n$ be a closed connected oriented smooth manifold of dimension $n\\ge2$. If $M$ admits a Riemannian metric of strictly positive scalar curvature, then \\[ (c_M)_*[M]=0\\qquad\\text{in }H_n(B\\pi_1(M);\\Q). \\]","sourceOffset":1623,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"The Gromov--Lawson aspherical conjecture","label":"cor:aspherical","text":"No closed smooth aspherical manifold, whether orientable or not, admits a Riemannian metric of strictly positive scalar curvature.","sourceOffset":11532,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:torical-metric","text":"A torus-stabilized metric on a smooth manifold $Y$ is a metric \\begin{equation} \\mathcal G=g+\\sum_{\\nu=1}^{b}m_\\nu^2\\,d\\theta_\\nu^2 \\qquad\\text{on }Y\\times\\T^b, \\end{equation} where $b\\geq0$ is finite, $g$ is a smooth metric on $Y$, and the functions $m_\\nu:Y\\to(0,\\infty)$ are smooth. Each circle is $\\R/(2\\pi\\Z)$. We call $g$ the base metric. Scalar curvature always refers to the full metric $\\mathcal G$. Differentials of base maps have the norm determined by $g$.","sourceOffset":111471,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":124347,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Positive-scalar-curvature-forces-rational-inessentiality-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-forces-rational-inessentiality-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"336-01","slug":"Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-Urysohn-width-October-5-2026","familyId":"336","title":"Spectral scalar curvature and uniform Urysohn width","abstract":"For every n ≥ 4, a complete connected smooth Riemannian n-manifold without boundary satisfying $-4\\Delta+\\mathop{\\mathrm{Scal}}\\nolimits \\ge1$ as a quadratic-form inequality admits a continuous map to a simplicial complex of dimension at most $n-2$ whose entire fibers have diameter bounded only in terms of n. The bound is measured in the original metric. This extends the uniform Urysohn width theorem from a pointwise scalar-curvature lower bound to a spectral lower bound.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Every complete connected smooth boundaryless n-manifold, n ≥ 3, satisfying $-4\\Delta+\\mathrm{Scal}\\ge1$ as a quadratic-form inequality admits a continuous map to a simplicial complex of dimension at most $n-2$ whose entire fibers have diameter bounded only by n in the original metric. This strengthens Gromov's width conclusion to spectral scalar curvature. Universal covers of closed positive-scalar-curvature manifolds also have continuous macroscopic dimension at most $n-2$ for every n ≥ 2.","pdfPath":"preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-Urysohn-width-October-5-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-Urysohn-width-October-5-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-Urysohn-width-October-5-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-Urysohn-width-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-Urysohn-width-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-Urysohn-width-October-5-2026/build/sections/interface.tex","preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-Urysohn-width-October-5-2026/build/sections/preprocessing.tex","preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-Urysohn-width-October-5-2026/build/sections/pressure.tex","preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-Urysohn-width-October-5-2026/build/sections/stretching.tex","preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-Urysohn-width-October-5-2026/build/sections/assembly.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-Urysohn-width-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every $n\\ge4$, a complete connected smooth Riemannian $n$-manifold without boundary satisfying $-4\\Delta+\\Scal\\ge1$ as a quadratic-form inequality admits a continuous map to a simplicial complex of dimension at most $n-2$ whose entire fibers have diameter bounded only in terms of $n$. The bound is measured in the original metric. This extends the uniform Urysohn width theorem from a pointwise scalar-curvature lower bound to a spectral lower bound.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":529},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and relation to earlier work","sourceOffset":2803},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The new constructions and the proof","sourceOffset":6099},{"kind":"section","title":"A partition theorem for stretched data","sourceOffset":9304},{"kind":"section","title":"From spectral positivity to a quadratic reserve","sourceOffset":14948},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The angle equation and elliptic estimates","sourceOffset":19292},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A global graph with linear discrepancy pressure","sourceOffset":24959},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Scalar comparison for a graph enlargement","sourceOffset":30232},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Completion of the reserve estimate","sourceOffset":34469},{"kind":"section","title":"A pressure law for a signed drift","sourceOffset":37967},{"kind":"section","title":"Local stretching with a signed drift","sourceOffset":47996},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Slope modification and spatial cutoffs","sourceOffset":50104}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $n\\ge4$ there is a finite constant $C_n$ with the following property. Every connected complete smooth Riemannian $n$-manifold $(M,g)$ without boundary satisfying \\eqref{eq:spectral-bound} admits a continuous map $f:M\\to K$ to a simplicial complex of dimension at most $n-2$ such that \\[ \\diam_g f^{-1}(y)\\le C_n\\qquad(y\\in K). \\] In particular, $\\UW_{n-2}(M,g)\\le C_n$.","sourceOffset":2050,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Partition theorem for stretched data {\\cite{OpenAIWidth}}","label":"interface:width","text":"For each integer $n\\ge4$, there is a finite number $D_{\\mathrm{nat}}(n)>0$ with the following property. Let $(M^n,g)$ be a connected complete smooth Riemannian manifold without boundary, and let $D\\ge D_{\\mathrm{nat}}(n)$. Choose a maximal $D$-separated set of centers $(x_i)_{i\\in I}$, where $I=\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ or $I=\\mathbb N$, and put \\[ A_i=\\overline B_g(x_i,D),\\qquad B_i=M\\setminus B_g(x_i,2D). \\] Suppose every $B_i$ is nonempty. Choose positive numbers $p_i^*$ for $i\\in\\{0\\}\\cup I$ such that \\[ \\sum_{i\\in\\{0\\}\\cup I}p_i^*=\\frac1{20}. \\] Assume there are a complete smooth metric $h\\ge g$ and a finite or countable family of smooth real functions $(p_\\alpha)$ with locally finite compact supports such that \\begin{equation} \\dist_h(A_i,B_i)>10+2\\pi\\sqrt{2/p_i^*}\\quad(i\\in I), \\qquad R(h,(p_\\alpha))\\ge\\frac25\\quad\\hbox{on }M. \\end{equation} Then there is a continuous map $f:M\\to K$ to a simplicial complex of dimension at most $n-2$, with its usual polyhedral topology, such that \\[ \\sup_{y\\in K}\\diam_g f^{-1}(y)\\le4D+2. \\] The diameter bounds the entire fiber, including all its components.","sourceOffset":10805,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":82781,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-Urysohn-width-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"336-02","slug":"Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-Urysohn-width-in-dimension-three-October-5-2026","familyId":"336","title":"Spectral scalar curvature and Urysohn width in dimension three","abstract":"Every connected complete smooth Riemannian three-manifold without boundary satisfying $-4\\Delta+\\mathop{\\mathrm{Scal}}\\nolimits \\ge\\lambda\\gt 0$ as a quadratic-form inequality admits a continuous map to a graph whose entire fibers have diameter at most $500/\\sqrt\\lambda$ in the original metric. No orientability, spin, compactness, or bounded-geometry assumption is required.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Every complete connected smooth boundaryless n-manifold, n ≥ 3, satisfying $-4\\Delta+\\mathrm{Scal}\\ge1$ as a quadratic-form inequality admits a continuous map to a simplicial complex of dimension at most $n-2$ whose entire fibers have diameter bounded only by n in the original metric. This strengthens Gromov's width conclusion to spectral scalar curvature. Universal covers of closed positive-scalar-curvature manifolds also have continuous macroscopic dimension at most $n-2$ for every n ≥ 2.","pdfPath":"preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-Urysohn-width-in-dimension-three-October-5-2026/spectral-urysohn-three-manifolds.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-Urysohn-width-in-dimension-three-October-5-2026/spectral-urysohn-three-manifolds.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-Urysohn-width-in-dimension-three-October-5-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-Urysohn-width-in-dimension-three-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-Urysohn-width-in-dimension-three-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-Urysohn-width-in-dimension-three-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-weighted-surfaces.tex","preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-Urysohn-width-in-dimension-three-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-cutting.tex","preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-Urysohn-width-in-dimension-three-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-doubling-bubbles.tex","preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-Urysohn-width-in-dimension-three-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-slabs.tex","preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-Urysohn-width-in-dimension-three-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-graph-gluing.tex","preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-Urysohn-width-in-dimension-three-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-Urysohn-width-in-dimension-three-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every connected complete smooth Riemannian three-manifold without boundary satisfying $-4\\Delta+\\Scal\\ge\\lambda>0$ as a quadratic-form inequality admits a continuous map to a graph whose entire fibers have diameter at most $500/\\sqrt\\lambda$ in the original metric. No orientability, spin, compactness, or bounded-geometry assumption is required.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":393},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and scope","sourceOffset":2457},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its useful components","sourceOffset":6215},{"kind":"section","title":"Weighted curvature and stable surfaces","sourceOffset":10380},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From the quadratic form to a positive density","sourceOffset":11330},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The stability inequality","sourceOffset":13592},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The conformal length estimate","sourceOffset":18044},{"kind":"section","title":"Cutting to obtain separation","sourceOffset":23315},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A maximal locally finite family","sourceOffset":24961},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Producing an additional stable surface","sourceOffset":27465},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From closed to neat hypersurfaces","sourceOffset":39115},{"kind":"section","title":"Doubling and separating interfaces","sourceOffset":41154}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $(M^3,g)$ be a connected complete smooth Riemannian three-manifold without boundary. Suppose that $\\lambda>0$ and \\begin{equation} \\int_M\\bigl(4|\\nabla_g\\phi|_g^2+\\Scal_g\\phi^2\\bigr)\\,d\\vol_g \\ge \\lambda\\int_M\\phi^2\\,d\\vol_g \\qquad\\bigl(\\phi\\in C_c^\\infty(M;\\R)\\bigr). \\end{equation} Then there exist a simplicial complex $K$ of dimension at most one and a continuous map $f:M\\to K$ such that \\[ \\diam_g f^{-1}(y)\\le \\frac{500}{\\sqrt\\lambda} \\qquad(y\\in K). \\] The complex has its usual polyhedral topology. No orientability, spin, compactness, or bounded-geometry assumption is imposed on $M$.","sourceOffset":1372,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Closed stable surfaces","label":"prop:stable-surface","text":"Let $\\Sigma$ be a smooth closed connected two-sided surface in a Riemannian three-manifold $(X,h)$. Let $T,\\mu$ be smooth ambient functions. Suppose that $\\Sigma$ satisfies $H-\\nu T=\\mu$, is stable for weighted area with pressure $\\mu$, and satisfies \\eqref{eq:pressure-curvature-condition} for a constant $c>0$. Then, in the induced metric $k=h|_\\Sigma$, \\[ \\diam_k\\Sigma\\le\\frac{2\\pi}{\\sqrt c}. \\] Moreover, $\\Sigma$ is diffeomorphic to $S^2$ or $\\R P^2$.","sourceOffset":20253,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":74675,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Spectral-scalar-curvature-and-Urysohn-width-in-dimension-three-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"336-03","slug":"Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026","familyId":"336","title":"Positive scalar curvature and uniform codimension-two width","abstract":"We prove the quantitative continuous form of Gromov's scalar-curvature conjecture in every dimension n ≥ 4. Every complete connected smooth boundaryless n-manifold with scalar curvature at least one admits a continuous map to a simplicial complex of dimension at most $n-2$ whose entire fibers have diameter bounded only in terms of n. We also obtain the continuous macroscopic-dimension conclusion for universal covers of closed manifolds with positive scalar curvature in every dimension n ≥ 2.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Every complete connected smooth boundaryless n-manifold, n ≥ 3, satisfying $-4\\Delta+\\mathrm{Scal}\\ge1$ as a quadratic-form inequality admits a continuous map to a simplicial complex of dimension at most $n-2$ whose entire fibers have diameter bounded only by n in the original metric. This strengthens Gromov's width conclusion to spectral scalar curvature. Universal covers of closed positive-scalar-curvature manifolds also have continuous macroscopic dimension at most $n-2$ for every n ≥ 2.","pdfPath":"preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026/build/sections/pressure.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026/build/sections/local-step.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026/build/sections/stretching-rounds.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026/build/sections/finite-cuts.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026/build/figures/packet-split.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026/build/sections/countable-partitions.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026/build/sections/reconstruction.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026/build/sections/closed-covers.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026/build/appendices/packets.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026/build/appendices/minimizers.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026/build/appendices/shield-local.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026/build/appendices/shield-global.tex","preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026/build/appendices/regularization.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the quantitative continuous form of Gromov's scalar-curvature conjecture in every dimension $n\\ge4$. Every complete connected smooth boundaryless $n$-manifold with scalar curvature at least one admits a continuous map to a simplicial complex of dimension at most $n-2$ whose entire fibers have diameter bounded only in terms of $n$. We also obtain the continuous macroscopic-dimension conclusion for universal covers of closed manifolds with positive scalar curvature in every dimension $n\\ge2$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":604},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":5602},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its main constructions","sourceOffset":10639},{"kind":"section","title":"A pressure law with a quadratic budget","sourceOffset":16656},{"kind":"section","title":"A compactly supported stretching step","sourceOffset":25674},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The slope switch and the order of constants","sourceOffset":27516},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cutoffs and a closed auxiliary manifold","sourceOffset":31068},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The modified graph equation","sourceOffset":34396},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Decay on the region of constant floor","sourceOffset":44996},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The scalar identity and its two slope regimes","sourceOffset":48507},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cutting off the deformation and obtaining distance","sourceOffset":54760},{"kind":"section","title":"Wide separations at a fixed original scale","sourceOffset":58565}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer \\(n\\ge4\\), there is a finite constant \\(C_n\\) such that every connected complete smooth Riemannian \\(n\\)-manifold \\((M,g)\\), without boundary and with \\(\\Scal_g\\ge1\\), admits a continuous map \\(f:M\\to K\\) to a simplicial complex of dimension at most \\(n-2\\) satisfying \\[ \\diam_g f^{-1}(y)\\le C_n\\qquad(y\\in K). \\] In particular, \\(\\UW_{n-2}(M,g)\\le C_n\\).","sourceOffset":1592,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Continuous macroscopic dimension of universal covers","label":"cover:main","text":"Let $(M,h)$ be a closed connected smooth Riemannian manifold of dimension $n\\ge2$ with positive scalar curvature. Its universal cover, with the lifted original metric $\\widetilde h$, satisfies \\[ \\dim_{\\mathrm{mc}}(\\widetilde M,\\widetilde h)\\le n-2. \\]","sourceOffset":4065,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Packet diagram","label":"packet:category","text":"At stage $r$, a status is $\\alpha\\in\\{+,-,*\\}^{r}$. Its entries record the first $r$ processed cuts. A sign $+$ or $-$ selects the corresponding retained side of a cut, and a star $*$ selects the wall, called a hit. The space $E_\\alpha$ is a smooth second countable manifold without boundary, possibly empty or disconnected; when nonempty its base dimension is $m(\\alpha)=n-\\#\\{i:\\alpha_i=*\\}$. Its connected components are called packets. Replacing signs by stars gives the face order. If $B$ is a packet with $d$ additional stars relative to a coarser status, then for that coarser status it has ex","sourceOffset":71217,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Analytic invariant","label":"packet:invariant","text":"At stage $r$, write $J_r$ for the unprocessed labels. On a packet $E$ of base dimension $m$, retain its base metric and all circle warps. Write $R_E$ for the scalar curvature of that full warped metric and $\\omega_E$ for the product of its circle lengths, suppressing fixed circle-volume constants. Gradients of invariant functions are base gradients; their integrals use $\\omega_E\\,\\dd\\vol_E$, and their Laplacian is the corresponding weighted base Laplacian. For every $j\\in J_r$ the data comprise a compatible continuous profile $\\mu_j:E\\to[-\\infty,\\infty]$ with locally Lipschitz finite truncatio","sourceOffset":77563,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":355050,"sourceFileCount":15,"statementCount":19,"definitionCount":6,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Positive-scalar-curvature-and-uniform-codimension-two-width-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"337-01","slug":"Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026","familyId":"337","title":"Generalized Cartan–Hadamard isoperimetry and Euclidean equality rigidity","abstract":"We resolve the generalized Cartan–Hadamard isoperimetric conjecture in every dimension. In a complete simply connected smooth manifold with sectional curvature at most κ ≤ 0, every finite-volume set of finite ambient perimeter has perimeter at least that of the equal-volume ball in curvature κ. For bounded positive-volume sets, equality in the Euclidean comparison holds precisely when the set agrees up to null sets with an open region isometric, with its induced metric, to a round Euclidean ball.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves generalized Cartan–Hadamard isoperimetry in every dimension: in a complete simply connected manifold with sectional curvature at most κ ≤ 0, every finite-volume finite-perimeter set satisfies the sharp comparison with the equal-volume model ball. Bounded positive-volume equality regions for κ = 0 are Euclidean balls. Also proves sharp Euclidean filling bounds for compactly supported integral n-cycles, n ≥ 2, in arbitrary proper CAT$(0)$ spaces.","pdfPath":"preprints/Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","preprints/Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-maps.tex","preprints/Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-sobolev.tex","preprints/Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-profile.tex","preprints/Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026/build/sections/04a-bv-calculus.tex","preprints/Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026/build/figures/confined-boundary.tex","preprints/Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-comparison.tex","preprints/Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-dimensions.tex","preprints/Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-equality-regularity.tex","preprints/Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-rigidity.tex","preprints/Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026/build/figures/offset-sphere.tex","preprints/Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026/build/sections/09-consequences.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We resolve the generalized Cartan--Hadamard isoperimetric conjecture in every dimension. In a complete simply connected smooth manifold with sectional curvature at most $\\kappa\\le0$, every finite-volume set of finite ambient perimeter has perimeter at least that of the equal-volume ball in curvature $\\kappa$. For bounded positive-volume sets, equality in the Euclidean comparison holds precisely when the set agrees up to null sets with an open region isometric, with its induced metric, to a round Euclidean ball.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":590},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The model comparison","sourceOffset":1257},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Model-ball spectral and torsional consequences","sourceOffset":4130},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and related work","sourceOffset":5857},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Main ideas and proof organization","sourceOffset":9110},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":13685},{"kind":"section","title":"Comparison maps into a sphere","sourceOffset":14578},{"kind":"section","title":"The sharp hypersurface Sobolev inequality","sourceOffset":25442},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A geometric square","sourceOffset":27931},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compactness below the Euclidean constant","sourceOffset":30223},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Balanced variations","sourceOffset":35356},{"kind":"section","title":"The confined isoperimetric profile","sourceOffset":40800}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $n\\ge2$, let $\\kappa\\le0$, and let $(M^n,g)$ be a complete simply connected smooth Riemannian manifold with $\\Sec_g\\le\\kappa$. Every measurable set $E\\subset M$ with $\\vol_g(E)<\\infty$ and $P_g(E)<\\infty$ satisfies \\[ P_g(E)\\ge \\mathcal I_{\\sqrt{-\\kappa}}\\bigl(\\vol_g(E)\\bigr). \\]","sourceOffset":2568,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Euclidean equality rigidity","label":"thm:equality","text":"Let $(M^n,g)$ be complete, simply connected and smooth, with $n\\ge2$ and $\\Sec_g\\le0$. Let $E\\subset M$ be bounded and measurable, with $0<\\vol_g(E)<\\infty$ and $P_g(E)<\\infty$. Then \\[ P_g(E)=n\\omega_n^{1/n}\\vol_g(E)^{(n-1)/n} \\] if and only if $E$ agrees up to ambient volume zero with an open region whose induced Riemannian metric is isometric to a round Euclidean ball.","sourceOffset":3399,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/337.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":125380,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Generalized-Cartan-Hadamard-isoperimetry-and-Euclidean-equality-rigidity-September-23-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"337-02","slug":"Sharp-integral-fillings-in-CAT(0)-spaces-September-23-2026","familyId":"337","title":"Sharp integral fillings in CAT(0) spaces","abstract":"Every compactly supported integral n-cycle, n ≥ 2, in a proper CAT(0) space bounds a compactly supported integral current with the sharp Euclidean mass bound. The theorem allows arbitrary integer multiplicities and unrestricted ambient dimension. In particular, we prove the Euclidean Cartan–Hadamard isoperimetric conjecture in dimensions at least three.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves generalized Cartan–Hadamard isoperimetry in every dimension: in a complete simply connected manifold with sectional curvature at most κ ≤ 0, every finite-volume finite-perimeter set satisfies the sharp comparison with the equal-volume model ball. Bounded positive-volume equality regions for κ = 0 are Euclidean balls. Also proves sharp Euclidean filling bounds for compactly supported integral n-cycles, n ≥ 2, in arbitrary proper CAT$(0)$ spaces.","pdfPath":"preprints/Sharp-integral-fillings-in-CAT(0)-spaces-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Sharp-integral-fillings-in-CAT(0)-spaces-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Sharp-integral-fillings-in-CAT(0)-spaces-September-23-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Sharp-integral-fillings-in-CAT(0)-spaces-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Sharp-integral-fillings-in-CAT(0)-spaces-September-23-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/Sharp-integral-fillings-in-CAT(0)-spaces-September-23-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","preprints/Sharp-integral-fillings-in-CAT(0)-spaces-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Sharp-integral-fillings-in-CAT(0)-spaces-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-currents.tex","preprints/Sharp-integral-fillings-in-CAT(0)-spaces-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-radial.tex","preprints/Sharp-integral-fillings-in-CAT(0)-spaces-September-23-2026/build/figures/radial-variation.tex","preprints/Sharp-integral-fillings-in-CAT(0)-spaces-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-sobolev.tex","preprints/Sharp-integral-fillings-in-CAT(0)-spaces-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-minimizer.tex","preprints/Sharp-integral-fillings-in-CAT(0)-spaces-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-chords.tex","preprints/Sharp-integral-fillings-in-CAT(0)-spaces-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-conclusion.tex","preprints/Sharp-integral-fillings-in-CAT(0)-spaces-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-classical.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Sharp-integral-fillings-in-CAT(0)-spaces-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every compactly supported integral $n$-cycle, $n\\ge2$, in a proper CAT(0) space bounds a compactly supported integral current with the sharp Euclidean mass bound. The theorem allows arbitrary integer multiplicities and unrestricted ambient dimension. In particular, we prove the Euclidean Cartan--Hadamard isoperimetric conjecture in dimensions at least three.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":406},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Cartan--Hadamard consequence","sourceOffset":2399},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The filling problem and its predecessors","sourceOffset":4947},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Main ideas and proof route","sourceOffset":7975},{"kind":"section","title":"Compact fillings and Euclidean current charts","sourceOffset":11667},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reduction to a compact convex ball","sourceOffset":12224},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The current-theoretic inputs","sourceOffset":15224},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An extremizing cycle and its normalization","sourceOffset":22057},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Metric differentiation and Euclidean chart norms","sourceOffset":24739},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Quadratic mass bounds and exact chart mass","sourceOffset":30414},{"kind":"section","title":"Radial variation and the two-dimensional base case","sourceOffset":39240},{"kind":"section","title":"An almost Euclidean critical Sobolev inequality","sourceOffset":56032}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Sharp integral filling","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $X$ be a proper CAT(0) space and $n\\ge2$ an integer. Every compactly supported $T\\in\\I_n(X)$ with $\\partial T=0$ admits a compactly supported $S\\in\\I_{n+1}(X)$ such that \\begin{equation} \\partial S=T,\\qquad \\M(S)\\le c_n\\M(T)^{(n+1)/n} =\\frac{\\M(T)^{(n+1)/n}} {(n+1)^{(n+1)/n}\\omega_{n+1}^{1/n}}. \\end{equation} There is no restriction on the dimension of $X$ or on the integer multiplicities of the currents. The coefficient is optimal.","sourceOffset":1659,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:cartan-hadamard","text":"Let $M$ be a Cartan--Hadamard manifold of dimension $d\\ge3$. For every bounded domain $\\Omega\\subset M$ with smooth boundary, \\begin{equation} \\operatorname{area}(\\partial\\Omega) \\ge d\\omega_d^{1/d}\\vol(\\Omega)^{(d-1)/d}. \\end{equation} The same inequality holds for relatively compact sets of finite perimeter, with ambient perimeter in place of boundary area.","sourceOffset":2835,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/337.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":140579,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Sharp-integral-fillings-in-CAT(0)-spaces-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Sharp-integral-fillings-in-CAT(0)-spaces-September-23-2026/build/glyph-mappings.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"338-01","slug":"Uniformization-of-complete-Kahler-manifolds-with-positive-bisectional-curvature-September-23-2026","familyId":"338","title":"Uniformization of complete Kähler manifolds with positive bisectional curvature","abstract":"We prove that every complete connected noncompact Kähler manifold with strictly positive holomorphic bisectional curvature is biholomorphic to complex Euclidean space. This resolves Yau's uniformization conjecture positively in every complex dimension.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Yau's uniformization conjecture: every complete connected noncompact Kähler manifold with strictly positive holomorphic bisectional curvature is biholomorphic to ℂn.","pdfPath":"preprints/Uniformization-of-complete-Kahler-manifolds-with-positive-bisectional-curvature-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniformization-of-complete-Kahler-manifolds-with-positive-bisectional-curvature-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniformization-of-complete-Kahler-manifolds-with-positive-bisectional-curvature-September-23-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Uniformization-of-complete-Kahler-manifolds-with-positive-bisectional-curvature-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Uniformization-of-complete-Kahler-manifolds-with-positive-bisectional-curvature-September-23-2026/build/unicode-map.tex","preprints/Uniformization-of-complete-Kahler-manifolds-with-positive-bisectional-curvature-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Uniformization-of-complete-Kahler-manifolds-with-positive-bisectional-curvature-September-23-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Uniformization-of-complete-Kahler-manifolds-with-positive-bisectional-curvature-September-23-2026/build/sections/flow.tex","preprints/Uniformization-of-complete-Kahler-manifolds-with-positive-bisectional-curvature-September-23-2026/build/sections/discs.tex","preprints/Uniformization-of-complete-Kahler-manifolds-with-positive-bisectional-curvature-September-23-2026/build/sections/variation.tex","preprints/Uniformization-of-complete-Kahler-manifolds-with-positive-bisectional-curvature-September-23-2026/build/sections/ellipsoids.tex","preprints/Uniformization-of-complete-Kahler-manifolds-with-positive-bisectional-curvature-September-23-2026/build/sections/charts.tex","preprints/Uniformization-of-complete-Kahler-manifolds-with-positive-bisectional-curvature-September-23-2026/build/sections/dynamics.tex","preprints/Uniformization-of-complete-Kahler-manifolds-with-positive-bisectional-curvature-September-23-2026/build/sections/uniformization.tex","preprints/Uniformization-of-complete-Kahler-manifolds-with-positive-bisectional-curvature-September-23-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Uniformization-of-complete-Kahler-manifolds-with-positive-bisectional-curvature-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every complete connected noncompact K\\\"ahler manifold with strictly positive holomorphic bisectional curvature is biholomorphic to complex Euclidean space. This resolves Yau's uniformization conjecture positively in every complex dimension.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":317},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Antecedents and the remaining analytic difficulties","sourceOffset":1502},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Structure of the proof","sourceOffset":8451},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":12626},{"kind":"section","title":"Functions, sections, and smoothing on the initial manifold","sourceOffset":14039},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The weight and holomorphic interpolation","sourceOffset":16400},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Hodge heat without a curvature upper bound","sourceOffset":24517},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Dyadic smoothing and averaged curvature","sourceOffset":33018},{"kind":"section","title":"A global flow and scalar comparison by localization","sourceOffset":36728},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Complete Hermitian approximations","sourceOffset":38020},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The largest metric eigenvalue","sourceOffset":42022},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The local limit and its potential estimates","sourceOffset":47747}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:uniformization","text":"Let $M$ be a connected noncompact complex manifold of complex dimension $n\\geq1$. If $M$ admits a smooth complete K\\\"ahler metric with strictly positive holomorphic bisectional curvature, then $M$ is biholomorphic to $\\C^n$.","sourceOffset":651,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Initial analytic data","label":"prop:initial-data","text":"The following objects and estimates are available. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item There is a smooth strictly plurisubharmonic function $\\psi$ with $\\psi\\le C(1+r)$. \\item Holomorphic functions and holomorphic tangent fields interpolate arbitrary finite jets at a finite set of points. There are finitely many holomorphic functions defining an injective immersion $F:M\\longrightarrow\\C^N$, and finitely many global holomorphic tangent fields spanning $T^{1,0}M$ at every point. \\item At every point $x$ there is a global holomorphic canonical section $S$ with $S(x)\\ne0$ and $\\log|S|_g^2\\le C_S(1+r)$. There is also a smooth function $\\Xi$ such that \\begin{equation} \\dd\\Xi\\ge\\Ric(g),\\qquad \\Xi\\le C(1+r^{3/2}). \\end{equation} \\item There is a smooth exhaustion $u\\ge1$ such that \\begin{equation} C^{-1}(1+r)\\le u\\le C(1+r),\\qquad |\\partial u|_g\\le C,\\qquad |\\dd u|_g\\le C/u. \\end{equation} \\item If $S(o)\\ne0$ is chosen as in \\textup{(iii)}, then, for $L\\ge1$, \\begin{equation} \\frac{1}{\\Vol B(o,L)}\\int_{B(o,L)} \\bigl|\\log|S|_g^2\\bigr|\\,dV_g\\le CL, \\qquad \\frac{1}{\\Vol B(o,L)}\\int_{B(o,L)}R_g\\,dV_g\\le C/L. \\end{equation} \\end{enumerate} Neither $F$ nor $\\psi$ is asserted to be proper. The exhaustion in \\textup{(iv)} has a two-sided complex Hessian bound.","sourceOffset":14835,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":276217,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Uniformization-of-complete-Kahler-manifolds-with-positive-bisectional-curvature-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Uniformization-of-complete-Kahler-manifolds-with-positive-bisectional-curvature-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"339-01","slug":"Entropy-equality-and-local-symmetry-in-negative-curvature-September-23-2026","familyId":"339","title":"Entropy equality and local symmetry in negative curvature","abstract":"For every closed connected smooth Riemannian manifold of dimension at least three with strictly negative sectional curvature, we prove that normalized Liouville measure has maximal entropy for the unit-speed geodesic flow if and only if the metric is locally symmetric. This resolves Katok's entropy rigidity conjecture positively in these dimensions, including all rank-one symmetric types at arbitrary scale.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Katok's entropy rigidity conjecture for closed connected Riemannian manifolds of dimension at least three with strictly negative sectional curvature: normalized Liouville measure maximizes entropy for the geodesic flow if and only if the metric is locally symmetric.","pdfPath":"preprints/Entropy-equality-and-local-symmetry-in-negative-curvature-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Entropy-equality-and-local-symmetry-in-negative-curvature-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Entropy-equality-and-local-symmetry-in-negative-curvature-September-23-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Entropy-equality-and-local-symmetry-in-negative-curvature-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Entropy-equality-and-local-symmetry-in-negative-curvature-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Entropy-equality-and-local-symmetry-in-negative-curvature-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-normal-forms.tex","preprints/Entropy-equality-and-local-symmetry-in-negative-curvature-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-hulls.tex","preprints/Entropy-equality-and-local-symmetry-in-negative-curvature-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-symmetries.tex","preprints/Entropy-equality-and-local-symmetry-in-negative-curvature-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-algebra.tex","preprints/Entropy-equality-and-local-symmetry-in-negative-curvature-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-realization.tex","preprints/Entropy-equality-and-local-symmetry-in-negative-curvature-September-23-2026/build/figures/mixed-rectangle.tex","preprints/Entropy-equality-and-local-symmetry-in-negative-curvature-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-smoothness.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Entropy-equality-and-local-symmetry-in-negative-curvature-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every closed connected smooth Riemannian manifold of dimension at least three with strictly negative sectional curvature, we prove that normalized Liouville measure has maximal entropy for the unit-speed geodesic flow if and only if the metric is locally symmetric. This resolves Katok's entropy rigidity conjecture positively in these dimensions, including all rank-one symmetric types at arbitrary scale.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":456},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and context","sourceOffset":2366},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":5066},{"kind":"section","title":"Dynamical preliminaries","sourceOffset":9743},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Leaf estimates and regular sets","sourceOffset":11411},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Entropy and the conormal forms","sourceOffset":17287},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The classical final step","sourceOffset":22336},{"kind":"section","title":"Contracting plaque jets and exact normal coordinates","sourceOffset":22749},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The raw graph and log-density towers","sourceOffset":23655},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Estimates and measurable choices","sourceOffset":31606},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact resonance normalization","sourceOffset":38270},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fiber polynomiality and finite weight blocks","sourceOffset":47765}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Suppose $n\\ge3$ and every sectional curvature of $g$ is strictly negative. Then \\[ h_{m_L}(\\phi_1)=\\htop(\\phi_1) \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\nabla^gR_g=0. \\] Equivalently, equality holds precisely when the universal Riemannian cover is a rank-one symmetric space of noncompact type, at an arbitrary overall scale.","sourceOffset":1326,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"The two formal affine fields","label":"hull:main","text":"At every point of a conull invariant set there are formal affine fields $\\mathcal A^u,\\mathcal A^s$ of horizontal $m$-covectors with the following properties. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item Their values contain $u$ and $w$, respectively. At the center, the exterior product of any element of $\\mathcal A^u$ with any element of $\\mathcal A^s$ equals the fixed nonzero exterior product $u\\wedge w$. \\item The fields are equivariant under $f$, changes of section, and flip, with the same constant and height multipliers as $u,w$. In a flow box their height dependences are $e^{-h\\tau}$ and $e^{h\\tau}$. \\item On each entire typical strong stable or strong unstable leaf, every ambient Taylor coefficient has a smooth representative agreeing almost everywhere with the regular construction. These representatives are holonomic: differentiating along the leaf gives the corresponding higher ambient coefficients. The analogous assertion holds on typical weak leaves, using flow transport. \\item At every finite order the local representatives, their bounds, and positive radii can be chosen measurably. On compact plaque subboxes one may restrict to positive-measure families with uniform finite-order bounds. No common convergence radius for all orders is asserted. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":54347,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"nf:tempered","text":"A measurable function $C\\geq1$ on the regular set is tempered if \\[ \\lim_{|n|\\to\\infty}\\frac{\\log C(f^n v)}{|n|}=0. \\] A positive radius is tempered when its reciprocal, increased to at least one, is tempered. An estimate with arbitrarily small exponential slack means that for every $\\epsilon>0$ its prefactor can be chosen tempered, while its exponential rate increases by at most $\\epsilon$.","sourceOffset":31981,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":256432,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":22,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Entropy-equality-and-local-symmetry-in-negative-curvature-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"340-01","slug":"A-counterexample-to-the-nearby-Lagrangian-conjecture-September-23-2026","familyId":"340","title":"A counterexample to the nearby Lagrangian conjecture","abstract":"We disprove the unrestricted nearby Lagrangian conjecture. For some sufficiently large even integer N, we construct a closed exact smoothly embedded Lagrangian in $T^*(S^9\\times S^{N-1})$ that is diffeomorphic to the base but is not Hamiltonian isotopic to the zero section.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves the unrestricted nearby Lagrangian conjecture. For some sufficiently large even N, constructs a closed exact embedded Lagrangian in $T^*(S^9\\times S^{N-1})$ that is diffeomorphic to the base but not Hamiltonian isotopic to its zero section.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-nearby-Lagrangian-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-nearby-Lagrangian-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-nearby-Lagrangian-conjecture-September-23-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-nearby-Lagrangian-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-nearby-Lagrangian-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-nearby-Lagrangian-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-nearby-Lagrangian-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/tubes.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-nearby-Lagrangian-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/morse.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-nearby-Lagrangian-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/obstruction.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-nearby-Lagrangian-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/regular-band.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-nearby-Lagrangian-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/embedding.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-nearby-Lagrangian-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/figures/momentum-separation.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-nearby-Lagrangian-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/sections/transport.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-nearby-Lagrangian-conjecture-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove the unrestricted nearby Lagrangian conjecture. For some sufficiently large even integer $N$, we construct a closed exact smoothly embedded Lagrangian in $T^*(S^9\\times S^{N-1})$ that is diffeomorphic to the base but is not Hamiltonian isotopic to the zero section.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":322},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and predecessors","sourceOffset":2094},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and its obstruction","sourceOffset":6436},{"kind":"section","title":"Stable tubes and a kernel class","sourceOffset":9942},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Functions and domains","sourceOffset":10263},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The stable kernel","sourceOffset":14529},{"kind":"section","title":"Families with one critical point","sourceOffset":22142},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Normalization and the negative sphere bundle","sourceOffset":23584},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A smoothing lemma for stabilization","sourceOffset":31342},{"kind":"section","title":"The zero-level cobordism","sourceOffset":35272},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A regular band and its two complementary regions","sourceOffset":36161},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Stabilizing the cobordism inside a fixed cylinder","sourceOffset":41764}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For some sufficiently large even integer $N$, the manifold $Q=S^9\\times S^{N-1}$ admits a closed exact smoothly embedded Lagrangian $L\\subset T^*Q$ such that $L$ is diffeomorphic to $Q$ and no compactly supported Hamiltonian isotopy carries the zero section to $L$.","sourceOffset":1490,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Waldhausen's tube fibration","label":"thm:tube-fibration","text":"For the stable smooth tube space \\eqref{eq:stable-tubes} there is a homotopy fiber sequence \\begin{equation} \\Hs(*)\\longrightarrow\\T\\xrightarrow{\\gamma}BG. \\end{equation} The rigid tube map $\\iota:BO\\to\\T$, defined by nondegenerate quadratic forms, satisfies $\\gamma\\circ\\iota\\simeq BJ$, where $BJ:BO\\to BG$ is the stable spherical fibration map.","sourceOffset":15825,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":92919,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-counterexample-to-the-nearby-Lagrangian-conjecture-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"341-01","slug":"Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026","familyId":"341","title":"Deforming hypersymplectic four-manifolds to hyperkähler triples","abstract":"Every smooth normalized positive triple of closed two-forms on a closed connected oriented four-manifold admits a smooth deformation, with each cohomology class fixed, to a hyperkähler triple. This resolves Donaldson's hypersymplectic deformation conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Donaldson's hypersymplectic deformation conjecture in a cohomology-preserving form. Every positive triple of smooth closed two-forms on a closed connected oriented four-manifold, normalized by $\\int\\omega_i\\wedge\\omega_j=\\delta_{ij}$, deforms through positive closed triples to a hyperkähler triple while preserving all three cohomology classes. Any positive triple can first be normalized by a constant linear change.","pdfPath":"preprints/Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026/build/sections/02-definite-pairs.tex","preprints/Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026/build/sections/03-current-energy.tex","preprints/Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026/build/sections/04-density-splitting.tex","preprints/Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026/build/sections/05-families.tex","preprints/Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026/build/sections/06-lattice-chamber.tex","preprints/Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026/build/sections/07-pencil.tex","preprints/Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026/build/sections/08-local-models.tex","preprints/Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026/build/sections/09-regular-preparation.tex","preprints/Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026/build/sections/10-auxiliary-volume.tex","preprints/Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026/build/sections/11-endpoint.tex","preprints/Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026/build/figures/endpoint-path.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Every smooth normalized positive triple of closed two-forms on a closed connected oriented four-manifold admits a smooth deformation, with each cohomology class fixed, to a hyperk\\\"ahler triple. This resolves Donaldson's hypersymplectic deformation conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":306},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":4970},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The main ideas","sourceOffset":9879},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and conventions","sourceOffset":16814},{"kind":"section","title":"Definite pairs and the coefficient sphere","sourceOffset":17960},{"kind":"section","title":"Separation, quadratic mass, and angular energy","sourceOffset":27312},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and separation","sourceOffset":28336},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A closed lift of anti-invariant forms","sourceOffset":33173},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The quadratic mass estimate","sourceOffset":38344},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The resolvent kernel and positive pairings","sourceOffset":45200},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Projection leakage and the topological energy","sourceOffset":51967},{"kind":"section","title":"Density splitting and the taming cone","sourceOffset":57252}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $X$ be a closed connected oriented smooth four-manifold, and let $\\omega=(\\omega_1,\\omega_2,\\omega_3)$ be a smooth hypersymplectic structure normalized by \\begin{equation} \\int_X\\omega_i\\wedge\\omega_j=\\delta_{ij}. \\end{equation} There is a smooth family of hypersymplectic structures $\\omega(t)$, $0\\le t\\le1$, such that \\[ \\omega(0)=\\omega,\\qquad [\\omega_i(t)]=[\\omega_i] \\quad (i=1,2,3), \\] and a positive volume form $\\mu_1$ with \\begin{equation} \\omega_i(1)\\wedge\\omega_j(1)=2\\delta_{ij}\\mu_1. \\end{equation} The endpoint forms are parallel and self-dual for a hyperk\\\"ahler metric.","sourceOffset":811,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Individual K\\\"ahler realizations","label":"cor:individual-kahler","text":"Let $X$ be a closed connected oriented smooth four-manifold with a smooth positive triple $\\omega$ of closed real two-forms. For every fixed $c\\in\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$, the form $\\alpha_c=\\sum_i c_i\\omega_i$ is a K\\\"ahler form for a parallel integrable complex structure $J_c$ of some hyperk\\\"ahler metric $g_c$ on $X$: \\[ \\alpha_c(v,w)=g_c(J_cv,w). \\] The compatible structure is chosen separately for each $c$; no simultaneous transport of the triple is asserted.","sourceOffset":2460,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":null,"text":"A pair $(A,C)$ of real two-forms is definite if its wedge-product matrix is positive definite at every point. A two-form $B$ tames an almost-complex structure $J$ if $B(v,Jv)>0$ for every nonzero tangent vector $v$. It is compatible if, in addition, $B(Ju,Jv)=B(u,v)$. When these forms are called symplectic, closedness is included.","sourceOffset":18416,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":284086,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":21,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Deforming-hypersymplectic-four-manifolds-to-hyperkahler-triples-October-7-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex.tex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"342-01","slug":"Taming-implies-compatibility-on-four-manifolds-October-6-2026","familyId":"342","title":"Taming implies compatibility on four-manifolds","abstract":"We prove that every smooth almost complex structure on a closed four-manifold which is tamed by a symplectic form is compatible with a symplectic form. This gives a positive solution to Donaldson's tamed-to-compatible conjecture. We also characterize the compatible cone by strict intersection positivity on the closure of the invariant part of the taming cone.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Donaldson's tamed-to-compatible conjecture: every smooth almost complex structure on a closed four-manifold that is tamed by a symplectic form admits a compatible symplectic form. The almost complex structure stays fixed; the form's cohomology class may change.","pdfPath":"preprints/Taming-implies-compatibility-on-four-manifolds-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Taming-implies-compatibility-on-four-manifolds-October-6-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Taming-implies-compatibility-on-four-manifolds-October-6-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Taming-implies-compatibility-on-four-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Taming-implies-compatibility-on-four-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Taming-implies-compatibility-on-four-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Taming-implies-compatibility-on-four-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/02-currents.tex","preprints/Taming-implies-compatibility-on-four-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/03-mass.tex","preprints/Taming-implies-compatibility-on-four-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/04-energy.tex","preprints/Taming-implies-compatibility-on-four-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/05-density.tex","preprints/Taming-implies-compatibility-on-four-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/06-splitting.tex","preprints/Taming-implies-compatibility-on-four-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/07-conclusion.tex","preprints/Taming-implies-compatibility-on-four-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/sections/08-cones.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Taming-implies-compatibility-on-four-manifolds-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that every smooth almost complex structure on a closed four-manifold which is tamed by a symplectic form is compatible with a symplectic form. This gives a positive solution to Donaldson's tamed-to-compatible conjecture. We also characterize the compatible cone by strict intersection positivity on the closure of the invariant part of the taming cone.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":407},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The argument and its main estimate","sourceOffset":6560},{"kind":"section","title":"A current obtained by separation","sourceOffset":10717},{"kind":"section","title":"Quadratic mass growth","sourceOffset":17129},{"kind":"section","title":"Regularization and angular control","sourceOffset":22942},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The kernel and its positive leading part","sourceOffset":24365},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Mixing of invariant and anti-invariant components","sourceOffset":29463},{"kind":"section","title":"Densities and planar tangent measures","sourceOffset":32489},{"kind":"section","title":"Closedness of the density restriction","sourceOffset":43025},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The error from the \\(L^2\\) correction","sourceOffset":44986},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Tangential derivatives of the density cutoff","sourceOffset":45782},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Passing to the positive-density set","sourceOffset":51971}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Suppose that a smooth almost complex structure \\(J\\) on a closed connected smooth real four-manifold \\(X\\) is tamed by a symplectic form. Then there is a smooth symplectic form compatible with \\(J\\).","sourceOffset":1451,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Taming and compatible cones","label":"thm:taming-cones","text":"Let \\(X,J\\) satisfy the hypotheses of Theorem \\ref{thm:main}, and give \\(X\\) the orientation induced by \\(J\\). Let \\(\\mathcal K_J^t,\\mathcal K_J^c\\subset H^2(X;\\R)\\) be the classes represented by taming and compatible symplectic forms, respectively. Let \\(H_J^-\\) be the subspace represented by smooth closed real anti-invariant two-forms. Set \\[ \\mathcal V=(H_J^-)^\\perp,\\qquad \\mathcal C=\\mathcal K_J^t\\cap\\mathcal V, \\] where orthogonality is for the intersection pairing. Then \\begin{align} \\mathcal K_J^t&=\\mathcal C+H_J^-,\\\\ \\mathcal K_J^c&= \\{a\\in\\mathcal C:\\ a\\cdot y>0 \\text{ for every }0\\ne y\\in\\overline{\\mathcal C}\\}. \\end{align} The closure is taken in \\(\\mathcal V\\), and the sum in \\eqref{eq:tame-decomposition} is a Minkowski sum.","sourceOffset":2080,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/342.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":72866,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Taming-implies-compatibility-on-four-manifolds-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"343-01","slug":"Symplectic-Ball-Packings-in-Higher-Dimensions-September-23-2026","familyId":"343","title":"Symplectic Ball Packings in Higher Dimensions","abstract":"We prove that, for all integers n ≥ 3 and k ≥ 1 and all positive real capacities $R_1,\\ldots,R_k$, the closed standard symplectic $2n$-balls of these capacities embed disjointly into the interior of a ball of capacity R > 0 if and only if $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^k R_i^n\\lt R^n, \\qquad R_i+R_j\\lt R\\quad(i\\ne j).$ Capacity is π times the squared Euclidean radius, and each embedding is defined on a neighborhood of its closed source ball. This proves Siegel and Yao's Conjecture A.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the Siegel–Yao conjecture for arbitrary capacities in every dimension $2n\\ge6$. Finitely many closed symplectic balls of capacities $R_1,\\ldots,R_k$ embed disjointly into an open ball of capacity R exactly when $\\sum_iR_i^n\\lt R^n$ and $R_i+R_j\\lt R$ for every distinct pair i, j.","pdfPath":"preprints/Symplectic-Ball-Packings-in-Higher-Dimensions-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Symplectic-Ball-Packings-in-Higher-Dimensions-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Symplectic-Ball-Packings-in-Higher-Dimensions-September-23-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Symplectic-Ball-Packings-in-Higher-Dimensions-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Symplectic-Ball-Packings-in-Higher-Dimensions-September-23-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/Symplectic-Ball-Packings-in-Higher-Dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Symplectic-Ball-Packings-in-Higher-Dimensions-September-23-2026/build/figures/proof-flow.tex","preprints/Symplectic-Ball-Packings-in-Higher-Dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/comparison.tex","preprints/Symplectic-Ball-Packings-in-Higher-Dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/surface.tex","preprints/Symplectic-Ball-Packings-in-Higher-Dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Symplectic-Ball-Packings-in-Higher-Dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/applications.tex","preprints/Symplectic-Ball-Packings-in-Higher-Dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/sixdim.tex","preprints/Symplectic-Ball-Packings-in-Higher-Dimensions-September-23-2026/build/figures/sixdim-moment.tex","preprints/Symplectic-Ball-Packings-in-Higher-Dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex","preprints/Symplectic-Ball-Packings-in-Higher-Dimensions-September-23-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Symplectic-Ball-Packings-in-Higher-Dimensions-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that, for all integers \\(n\\ge3\\) and \\(k\\ge1\\) and all positive real capacities \\(R_1,\\ldots,R_k\\), the closed standard symplectic \\(2n\\)-balls of these capacities embed disjointly into the interior of a ball of capacity \\(R>0\\) if and only if \\[ \\sum_{i=1}^k R_i^n<R^n, \\qquad R_i+R_j<R\\quad(i\\ne j). \\] Capacity is \\(\\pi\\) times the squared Euclidean radius, and each embedding is defined on a neighborhood of its closed source ball. This proves Siegel and Yao's Conjecture A.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":581},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Packing rigidity and stability","sourceOffset":2151},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From a mean area inequality to ball embeddings","sourceOffset":4012},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The geometric models and the three cases","sourceOffset":6798},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and the easy direction","sourceOffset":11499},{"kind":"section","title":"Hamiltonian comparison on a toric domain","sourceOffset":13044},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coordinate rearrangements","sourceOffset":15124},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A fixed point with a uniform gap","sourceOffset":18198},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Realization by Hamiltonian maps","sourceOffset":21596},{"kind":"section","title":"Packing over a surface with a handle","sourceOffset":33178},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Horizontal forms and relative primitives","sourceOffset":33593},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The surface packing lemma","sourceOffset":38714}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"intro:main","text":"Let \\(n\\ge3\\) and \\(k\\ge1\\) be integers. For positive real numbers \\(R,R_1,\\ldots,R_k\\), there exists a symplectic embedding \\[ \\bigsqcup_{i=1}^k B^{2n}(R_i) \\hookrightarrow \\Int B^{2n}(R) \\] if and only if \\begin{equation} \\sum_{i=1}^k R_i^n<R^n, \\qquad R_i+R_j<R\\quad (1\\le i<j\\le k). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":1300,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Normal-cone transfer","label":"geo:transfer","text":"Let \\(T\\) be a smooth projective manifold, \\(Z\\subset T\\) a smooth closed submanifold of positive codimension, possibly disconnected, and \\(L\\) an ample rational polarization. Let \\[ T'=\\Bl_ZT,\\qquad Y=\\PP_Z(\\mathbf1\\oplus N_{Z/T}),\\qquad D_\\infty=\\PP_Z(N_{Z/T})\\subset Y. \\] Write \\(E_Z\\) for the exceptional divisor on \\(T'\\); when \\(Z\\) is a divisor, \\(T'\\simeq T\\) and \\(E_Z\\) means \\(Z\\). Suppose \\(d>0\\) is rational and \\[ \\pi^*L-dE_Z\\quad\\hbox{on }T', \\qquad L|_Z+dU\\quad\\hbox{on }Y \\] are ample. Let \\(F\\subset T\\) be a finite union of pairwise disjoint smooth closed complex submanifolds. Assume the inverse image of \\(F\\cap Z\\) under \\(Y\\to Z\\) is also a finite union of pairwise disjoint smooth complex submanifolds; the empty union is allowed. Let \\(\\omega_T\\) be any K\\\"ahler form in class \\(L\\), and let \\(\\omega_Y\\) be a K\\\"ahler form in class \\(L|_Z+dU\\), invariant under scalar rotation of the normal summand. Then every compact subset of \\[ Y\\setminus\\bigl(D_\\infty\\cup\\pi_Y^{-1}(F\\cap Z)\\bigr) \\] has a neighborhood admitting a symplectic embedding into \\((T\\setminus F,\\omega_T)\\), with its given form \\(\\omega_Y\\). In particular any compact packing there transfers with neighborhood embeddings.","sourceOffset":64345,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/343.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves Siegel and Yao's ball-packing criterion in every symplectic dimension $2n$ with $n\\ge3$. For $k\\ge1$ closed standard balls of positive capacities $R_1,\\ldots,R_k$, disjoint symplectic embeddings into the interior of a ball of capacity $R$ exist exactly when\n$\\sum_i R_i^n<R^n$ and $R_i+R_j<R$ for all $i\\ne j$.\nCapacity is $\\pi$ times squared Euclidean radius, and each embedding is defined on a neighborhood of its closed source ball. The linked statements include both the full equivalence and the necessity direction.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/343.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":142311,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Symplectic-Ball-Packings-in-Higher-Dimensions-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Symplectic-Ball-Packings-in-Higher-Dimensions-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"344-01","slug":"The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026","familyId":"344","title":"The metric Blaschke theorem","abstract":"We prove the metric Blaschke conjecture: every connected closed smooth Riemannian manifold of positive dimension whose global injectivity radius equals its diameter is, up to scale, a standard compact rank-one symmetric space.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the metric Blaschke conjecture: every closed connected Riemannian manifold of positive dimension whose injectivity radius equals its diameter is, up to scaling, a standard compact rank-one symmetric space.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-geometric-inputs.tex","preprints/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-lagrangian.tex","preprints/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026/build/figures/schur-paths.tikz.tex","preprints/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-weighted-comparison.tex","preprints/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-global-comparison.tex","preprints/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-incidence.tex","preprints/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-signature.tex","preprints/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-geodesic-space.tex","preprints/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026/build/figures/attachment-square.tikz.tex","preprints/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-volume-algebra.tex","preprints/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/09-classification.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the metric Blaschke conjecture: every connected closed smooth Riemannian manifold of positive dimension whose global injectivity radius equals its diameter is, up to scale, a standard compact rank-one symmetric space.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":273},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The problem and its predecessors","sourceOffset":2089},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two volume bounds and their common equality case","sourceOffset":4928},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Recent related rigidity results","sourceOffset":9643},{"kind":"section","title":"Blaschke geometry and model normalization","sourceOffset":10401},{"kind":"section","title":"A Lagrangian comparison along a closed geodesic","sourceOffset":15538},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The symplectic curve of vanishing Jacobi fields","sourceOffset":16536},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The determinant and a fixed graph chart","sourceOffset":20039},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The chart poles and determinant-root profiles","sourceOffset":24124},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Schur complements for arbitrary endpoint order","sourceOffset":30279},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Scalar lower bounds for the two factors","sourceOffset":37628},{"kind":"section","title":"Weighted comparison along a closed geodesic","sourceOffset":41744}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"The metric Blaschke theorem","label":"thm:blaschke","text":"Let $(M,g)$ be a connected closed smooth Riemannian manifold of positive dimension. If $\\inj(M,g)=\\diam(M,g)$, then $(M,g)$ is isometric, up to multiplication of the metric by a positive constant, to a round sphere, a standard real, complex or quaternionic projective space, or the Cayley plane.","sourceOffset":1227,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Sharp volume comparison","label":"thm:comparison","text":"Every Blaschke manifold and its model at the same diameter satisfy \\[ \\Vol(M,g)\\ge\\Vol(M_0,g_0). \\] Equality holds if and only if $(M,g)$ is isometric to $(M_0,g_0)$.","sourceOffset":5073,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":130007,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":19,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"345-01","slug":"Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026","familyId":"345","title":"Infinitely many closed geodesic images on every Riemannian sphere","abstract":"We resolve the sphere case of the closed-geodesic infinitude problem: every smooth Riemannian metric on the standard sphere Sn, n ≥ 2, has infinitely many prime closed geodesics with pairwise distinct images. The two-sphere case is classical; the result in higher dimensions includes degenerate metrics. Using finite covers and theorems of Perelman and Rademacher–Taimanov, we also obtain the same conclusion for every smooth Riemannian metric on a nonempty closed smooth three-manifold.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every smooth Riemannian metric on Sn, n ≥ 2, has infinitely many prime closed geodesics with pairwise distinct images. The same conclusion holds on every closed manifold admitting a finite smooth spherical cover and on every closed three-manifold, without orientability or nondegeneracy restrictions.","pdfPath":"preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/setup.tex","preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/local.tex","preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/shortening.tex","preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/cutjoin.tex","preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/build/figures/native-cut.tex","preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/ordinary.tex","preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/equivariant.tex","preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/budget.tex","preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/transients.tex","preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/build/figures/degree-gap.tex","preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/activation.tex","preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/build/sections/cover-proof.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We resolve the sphere case of the closed-geodesic infinitude problem: every smooth Riemannian metric on the standard sphere $S^n$, $n\\ge2$, has infinitely many prime closed geodesics with pairwise distinct images. The two-sphere case is classical; the result in higher dimensions includes degenerate metrics. Using finite covers and theorems of Perelman and Rademacher--Taimanov, we also obtain the same conclusion for every smooth Riemannian metric on a nonempty closed smooth three-manifold.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":556},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Idea of the proof","sourceOffset":6676},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and reusable ingredients","sourceOffset":11244},{"kind":"section","title":"Loop spaces, actions and sublevels","sourceOffset":12351},{"kind":"section","title":"Local control without nondegeneracy","sourceOffset":15122},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Energy sublevels and finite-dimensional models","sourceOffset":15625},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prime images and iteration","sourceOffset":23899},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A common flow for local restriction maps","sourceOffset":29022},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Computing ranks while retaining the original maps","sourceOffset":37035},{"kind":"section","title":"Shortening families in a fixed degree","sourceOffset":48772},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Arclength parametrization in \\texorpdfstring{$H^1$}{H1}","sourceOffset":49590},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A bounded local path replacement","sourceOffset":54917}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:spheres","text":"For every integer $n\\ge2$ and every smooth Riemannian metric on the standard smooth sphere $S^n$, there are infinitely many prime closed geodesics with pairwise different images.","sourceOffset":1103,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:finite-cover","text":"Let $N$ be a closed manifold admitting a finite smooth cover diffeomorphic to a standard sphere $S^n$, where $n\\ge2$. Every smooth Riemannian metric on $N$ has infinitely many geometrically distinct prime closed geodesics.","sourceOffset":4296,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":172235,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Infinitely-many-closed-geodesic-images-on-every-Riemannian-sphere-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"346-01","slug":"A-Codimension-One-Bound-for-the-Singular-Set-of-a-Stationary-Integral-Varifold-October-5-2026","familyId":"346","title":"A Codimension-One Bound for the Singular Set of a Stationary Integral Varifold","abstract":"The singular set of every stationary integral m-varifold in an open Euclidean set has Hausdorff dimension at most $m-1$, in every positive dimension and codimension. This sharp bound proves the Euclidean singular-set conjecture recorded by Brena, Decio, and De Lellis.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every stationary integral m-varifold in a Euclidean open set has singular set of Hausdorff dimension at most $m-1$, a sharp bound in every positive dimension and codimension. On round spheres, the family also proves almost-everywhere regularity: the singular set has zero m-dimensional Hausdorff measure.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Codimension-One-Bound-for-the-Singular-Set-of-a-Stationary-Integral-Varifold-October-5-2026/varifold-singular-dimension.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Codimension-One-Bound-for-the-Singular-Set-of-a-Stationary-Integral-Varifold-October-5-2026/varifold-singular-dimension.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Codimension-One-Bound-for-the-Singular-Set-of-a-Stationary-Integral-Varifold-October-5-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-Codimension-One-Bound-for-the-Singular-Set-of-a-Stationary-Integral-Varifold-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-Codimension-One-Bound-for-the-Singular-Set-of-a-Stationary-Integral-Varifold-October-5-2026/build/macros.tex","preprints/A-Codimension-One-Bound-for-the-Singular-Set-of-a-Stationary-Integral-Varifold-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-Codimension-One-Bound-for-the-Singular-Set-of-a-Stationary-Integral-Varifold-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-flat-scales.tex","preprints/A-Codimension-One-Bound-for-the-Singular-Set-of-a-Stationary-Integral-Varifold-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-normal-inputs.tex","preprints/A-Codimension-One-Bound-for-the-Singular-Set-of-a-Stationary-Integral-Varifold-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-fitting-frequency.tex","preprints/A-Codimension-One-Bound-for-the-Singular-Set-of-a-Stationary-Integral-Varifold-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-centered-blowup.tex","preprints/A-Codimension-One-Bound-for-the-Singular-Set-of-a-Stationary-Integral-Varifold-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-linearized-measures.tex","preprints/A-Codimension-One-Bound-for-the-Singular-Set-of-a-Stationary-Integral-Varifold-October-5-2026/build/sections/06b-density-centers.tex","preprints/A-Codimension-One-Bound-for-the-Singular-Set-of-a-Stationary-Integral-Varifold-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-dimension-reduction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Codimension-One-Bound-for-the-Singular-Set-of-a-Stationary-Integral-Varifold-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"The singular set of every stationary integral $m$-varifold in an open Euclidean set has Hausdorff dimension at most $m-1$, in every positive dimension and codimension. This sharp bound proves the Euclidean singular-set conjecture recorded by Brena, Decio, and De Lellis.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":316},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and antecedents","sourceOffset":2484},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The proof and its new components","sourceOffset":6701},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":10453},{"kind":"section","title":"Flat scales carrying many singular centers","sourceOffset":11173},{"kind":"section","title":"Normal cylinders and signed excess","sourceOffset":19094},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Geometry and units","sourceOffset":19501},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two imported measure estimates","sourceOffset":22536},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fixed parameters","sourceOffset":27024},{"kind":"section","title":"Fitting intervals and frequency control","sourceOffset":27896},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local fits and their gluing","sourceOffset":29312},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weighted height on a fitting interval","sourceOffset":36955}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every stationary integral $m$-varifold $V$ in an open set $U\\subset\\R^{m+n}$, where $m,n\\ge1$, \\[ \\dim_{\\Hh}\\Sing V\\le m-1. \\]","sourceOffset":1905,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Flat scales and a measure of centers","label":"prop:flat-scales","text":"Suppose that $V$ is a stationary integral $m$-varifold in an open subset of $\\R^{m+n}$ and that \\[ m-1<s<\\dim_{\\Hh}\\Sing(V). \\] After a translation there exist a fixed point $0\\in\\Sing(V)$, an integer $Q\\ge1$, an $m$-plane $\\Pi$ through zero, radii $L_i\\downarrow0$, and positive Radon measures $\\sigma_i$ such that \\begin{equation} \\Theta_V(0)=Q,\\qquad V_{L_i}\\rightharpoonup Q|\\Pi| \\quad\\hbox{locally in }\\R^{m+n}. \\end{equation} Each $\\sigma_i$ is supported in $\\overline{\\mathbf B}_1$ on singular points of $V_{L_i}$ having density exactly $Q$. There are constants $c,C>0$, independent of $i$, for which \\begin{equation} \\sigma_i(\\mathbf B_u(X))\\le C u^s \\quad(X\\in\\R^{m+n},\\ u>0),\\qquad c\\le\\sigma_i(\\overline{\\mathbf B}_1)\\le C. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":13544,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":119532,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Codimension-One-Bound-for-the-Singular-Set-of-a-Stationary-Integral-Varifold-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"346-02","slug":"Almost-everywhere-regularity-of-stationary-integral-varifolds-September-23-2026","familyId":"346","title":"Almost-everywhere regularity of stationary integral varifolds","abstract":"We resolve the almost-everywhere regularity conjecture of Brena, Decio, and De Lellis for stationary integral varifolds of arbitrary positive dimension and codimension in Euclidean open sets. The singular set has zero measure in the dimension of the varifold: near almost every support point, the varifold is a constant positive integer multiple of a smooth embedded minimal submanifold. The corresponding statement also holds on round spheres.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every stationary integral m-varifold in a Euclidean open set has singular set of Hausdorff dimension at most $m-1$, a sharp bound in every positive dimension and codimension. On round spheres, the family also proves almost-everywhere regularity: the singular set has zero m-dimensional Hausdorff measure.","pdfPath":"preprints/Almost-everywhere-regularity-of-stationary-integral-varifolds-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Almost-everywhere-regularity-of-stationary-integral-varifolds-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Almost-everywhere-regularity-of-stationary-integral-varifolds-September-23-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Almost-everywhere-regularity-of-stationary-integral-varifolds-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-regularity-of-stationary-integral-varifolds-September-23-2026/build/unicode-map.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-regularity-of-stationary-integral-varifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-regularity-of-stationary-integral-varifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/local.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-regularity-of-stationary-integral-varifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/signed-statement.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-regularity-of-stationary-integral-varifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/contact.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-regularity-of-stationary-integral-varifolds-September-23-2026/build/figures/contact.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-regularity-of-stationary-integral-varifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/excess.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-regularity-of-stationary-integral-varifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/packing.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-regularity-of-stationary-integral-varifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/centering.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-regularity-of-stationary-integral-varifolds-September-23-2026/build/figures/collar.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-regularity-of-stationary-integral-varifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/inward.tex","preprints/Almost-everywhere-regularity-of-stationary-integral-varifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/spheres.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Almost-everywhere-regularity-of-stationary-integral-varifolds-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We resolve the almost-everywhere regularity conjecture of Brena, Decio, and De Lellis for stationary integral varifolds of arbitrary positive dimension and codimension in Euclidean open sets. The singular set has zero measure in the dimension of the varifold: near almost every support point, the varifold is a constant positive integer multiple of a smooth embedded minimal submanifold. The corresponding statement also holds on round spheres.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":547},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and significance","sourceOffset":2988},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The argument","sourceOffset":6569},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":13141},{"kind":"section","title":"Local first variation and height estimates","sourceOffset":13864},{"kind":"subsection","title":"First variation and integer counts","sourceOffset":14346},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Configurations and height bounds","sourceOffset":32685},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Gaussian and height identities","sourceOffset":46340},{"kind":"section","title":"The signed-excess estimate","sourceOffset":59156},{"kind":"section","title":"Heat-potential contacts and separated configurations","sourceOffset":65261},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The entrance boundary and its contact measure","sourceOffset":66086},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Localization of the signed source","sourceOffset":73502}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Every stationary integral $m$-varifold in an open subset of $\\R^{m+n}$ satisfies \\[ \\HH^m(\\operatorname{Sing}V)=0. \\] Here $m,n\\geq1$ are arbitrary. No stability, minimizing, orientability, density upper bound, or codimension-one hypothesis is required.","sourceOffset":1732,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Round spheres","label":"cor:round-spheres","text":"Let $m,n\\geq1$ and let $V$ be an integral $m$-varifold on the full unit round sphere $S^{m+n}$, stationary for variations tangent to the sphere. A support point is regular if on a spherical neighborhood $V=q|\\Sigma|$ for an integer $q\\geq1$ and a smooth embedded minimal $m$-submanifold $\\Sigma$ of the sphere. If $\\operatorname{Sing}_S V$ denotes the remaining support points, then \\[ \\HH^m_{g_{\\mathrm{round}}}(\\operatorname{Sing}_S V)=0. \\] No fixed upper bound on mass, density, or multiplicity is required, nor are stability, orientability, or mod-two cyclicity. The same conclusion holds on every round sphere of positive radius.","sourceOffset":2034,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":285115,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Almost-everywhere-regularity-of-stationary-integral-varifolds-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Almost-everywhere-regularity-of-stationary-integral-varifolds-September-23-2026/build/unicode-map.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"347-01","slug":"Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-in-Dimension-Twenty-Two-October-5-2026","familyId":"347","title":"Hamiltonian Fixed Points Below the Stable Morse Number in Dimension Twenty-Two","abstract":"We disprove the stable Morse lower bound for nondegenerate Hamiltonian fixed points with examples in fixed real dimension twenty-two. For every integer m ≥ 1, we construct a simply connected closed Kähler manifold with stable Morse number $80+1968m$ and a Hamiltonian diffeomorphism with exactly $80+1952m$ fixed points, all nondegenerate and with contractible orbit loops. The deficit is therefore unbounded, and the fixed-point count is at most 127/128 of the stable Morse number.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves stable-Morse lower bounds for nondegenerate Hamiltonian fixed points: on simply connected closed Kähler manifolds of real dimension 22, the deficit below the stable Morse number is unbounded. A separate Hamiltonian diffeomorphism of the complex quadric threefold has exactly three fixed points, fewer than the four critical points required of every smooth function.","pdfPath":"preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-in-Dimension-Twenty-Two-October-5-2026/hamiltonian-fixed-points-below-stable-morse-number.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-in-Dimension-Twenty-Two-October-5-2026/hamiltonian-fixed-points-below-stable-morse-number.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-in-Dimension-Twenty-Two-October-5-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-in-Dimension-Twenty-Two-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-in-Dimension-Twenty-Two-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-in-Dimension-Twenty-Two-October-5-2026/build/sections/morse.tex","preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-in-Dimension-Twenty-Two-October-5-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-in-Dimension-Twenty-Two-October-5-2026/build/sections/dynamics.tex","preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-in-Dimension-Twenty-Two-October-5-2026/build/sections/counts.tex","preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-in-Dimension-Twenty-Two-October-5-2026/build/appendices/handles.tex","preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-in-Dimension-Twenty-Two-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-in-Dimension-Twenty-Two-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove the stable Morse lower bound for nondegenerate Hamiltonian fixed points with examples in fixed real dimension twenty-two. For every integer $m\\geq1$, we construct a simply connected closed Kähler manifold with stable Morse number $80+1968m$ and a Hamiltonian diffeomorphism with exactly $80+1952m$ fixed points, all nondegenerate and with contractible orbit loops. The deficit is therefore unbounded, and the fixed-point count is at most $127/128$ of the stable Morse number.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":533},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and earlier work","sourceOffset":2741},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Mechanism and proof structure","sourceOffset":5358},{"kind":"section","title":"Integral homology and Morse numbers","sourceOffset":8353},{"kind":"section","title":"Torsion in a fixed complex dimension","sourceOffset":16539},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two projective quotient surfaces","sourceOffset":17191},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Simultaneous projection of the centers","sourceOffset":22403},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The integral blow-up formula","sourceOffset":25651},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The family of ninefolds","sourceOffset":31339},{"kind":"section","title":"From Morse functions to Hamiltonian fixed points","sourceOffset":32680},{"kind":"section","title":"The integral counts and the relative deficit","sourceOffset":44745},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two torsion profiles","sourceOffset":45295}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $m\\geq1$, there exist a simply connected closed Kähler manifold $(M_m,\\omega_m)$ of real dimension $22$ and a smooth one-periodic Hamiltonian $H_m$ such that all fixed points of $\\phi_{H_m}^1$ are nondegenerate and \\begin{align*} \\SM(M_m)&=80+1968m,\\\\ \\#\\Fix(\\phi_{H_m}^1) &=\\#\\Fix_0(\\phi_{H_m}^1;H_m)=80+1952m. \\end{align*} Consequently, with $\\delta=1/128$, for every $R>0$ some member of this family satisfies \\[ \\SM(M_m)\\geq R,\\qquad \\#\\Fix(\\phi_{H_m}^1)\\leq(1-\\delta)\\SM(M_m). \\]","sourceOffset":2186,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"morse:stable-lower","text":"For every connected closed smooth manifold $W$, \\[ \\SM(W)\\geq\\lambda(W). \\]","sourceOffset":10230,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/347.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":68347,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-in-Dimension-Twenty-Two-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"347-02","slug":"Sharpness-of-the-Cyclic-Integral-Floer-Bound-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026","familyId":"347","title":"Sharpness of the Cyclic Integral Floer Bound Below the Stable Morse Number","abstract":"We construct a simply connected closed Kähler manifold of real dimension 3332 and minimal Chern number one whose Hamiltonian fixed-point count attains the cyclic integral Floer bound while falling below the stable Morse number. The Hamiltonian diffeomorphism has exactly $1\\,872\\,232$ fixed points, all nondegenerate and with contractible orbit loops, whereas the stable Morse number is $1\\,872\\,264$. Thus the stronger stable Morse bound fails by exactly 32, even when the cyclic integral bound is sharp.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves stable-Morse lower bounds for nondegenerate Hamiltonian fixed points: on simply connected closed Kähler manifolds of real dimension 22, the deficit below the stable Morse number is unbounded. A separate Hamiltonian diffeomorphism of the complex quadric threefold has exactly three fixed points, fewer than the four critical points required of every smooth function.","pdfPath":"preprints/Sharpness-of-the-Cyclic-Integral-Floer-Bound-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/sharp-cyclic-integral-floer-bound-below-stable-morse-number.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Sharpness-of-the-Cyclic-Integral-Floer-Bound-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/sharp-cyclic-integral-floer-bound-below-stable-morse-number.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Sharpness-of-the-Cyclic-Integral-Floer-Bound-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Sharpness-of-the-Cyclic-Integral-Floer-Bound-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Sharpness-of-the-Cyclic-Integral-Floer-Bound-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Sharpness-of-the-Cyclic-Integral-Floer-Bound-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/build/sections/morse.tex","preprints/Sharpness-of-the-Cyclic-Integral-Floer-Bound-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/build/sections/involution.tex","preprints/Sharpness-of-the-Cyclic-Integral-Floer-Bound-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Sharpness-of-the-Cyclic-Integral-Floer-Bound-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/build/sections/construction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Sharpness-of-the-Cyclic-Integral-Floer-Bound-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a simply connected closed K\\\"ahler manifold of real dimension $3332$ and minimal Chern number one whose Hamiltonian fixed-point count attains the cyclic integral Floer bound while falling below the stable Morse number. The Hamiltonian diffeomorphism has exactly $1\\,872\\,232$ fixed points, all nondegenerate and with contractible orbit loops, whereas the stable Morse number is $1\\,872\\,264$. Thus the stronger stable Morse bound fails by exactly $32$, even when the cyclic integral bound is sharp.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":557},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and proof strategy","sourceOffset":3421},{"kind":"section","title":"Integral homology and geometric Morse counts","sourceOffset":7269},{"kind":"section","title":"An involution with a prescribed perturbation","sourceOffset":15672},{"kind":"section","title":"Projective surfaces and integral blow-ups","sourceOffset":26109},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two surfaces","sourceOffset":26491},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The integral blow-up formula","sourceOffset":30971},{"kind":"section","title":"Balancing the torsion profiles","sourceOffset":35994},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Seven disjoint centers","sourceOffset":36303},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two prime multiplicities","sourceOffset":38325},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Products and the exact gap","sourceOffset":39801},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Completion of the construction","sourceOffset":44179}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist a simply connected closed K\\\"ahler manifold $(M,\\omega)$ of complex dimension $1666$ and a smooth one-periodic Hamiltonian $H$ such that \\[ c_1(TM)(\\pi_2(M))=\\Z, \\] every fixed point of $\\phi_H^1$ is nondegenerate, and \\begin{align*} \\#\\Fix(\\phi_H^1) &=\\#\\Fix_0(\\phi_H^1;H) =\\beta_{\\Z}^{\\mathrm{cyc}}(M)=1\\,872\\,232,\\\\ \\SM(M)&=\\beta_{\\Z}^{\\mathrm{cyc}}(M)+32=1\\,872\\,264. \\end{align*}","sourceOffset":2793,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"morse:stable","text":"For every closed smooth manifold $W$, with the exact quadratic condition at infinity and all stabilizations $k\\geq0$ allowed, \\[ \\SM(W)\\geq\\lambda(W). \\]","sourceOffset":9925,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/347.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":45265,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Sharpness-of-the-Cyclic-Integral-Floer-Bound-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"347-03","slug":"Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026","familyId":"347","title":"Hamiltonian Fixed Points Below the Stable Morse Number","abstract":"We disprove the stable Morse lower bound for nondegenerate Hamiltonian fixed points by constructing a simply connected closed Kähler manifold with sixteen fewer fixed points than its stable Morse number. All the corresponding periodic orbits are contractible. The construction combines a Hamiltonian involution with integral homology torsion at two different primes; its proof uses finite-dimensional Morse theory and complex blowups.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves stable-Morse lower bounds for nondegenerate Hamiltonian fixed points: on simply connected closed Kähler manifolds of real dimension 22, the deficit below the stable Morse number is unbounded. A separate Hamiltonian diffeomorphism of the complex quadric threefold has exactly three fixed points, fewer than the four critical points required of every smooth function.","pdfPath":"preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/build/sections/history.tex","preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/build/sections/morse.tex","preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/build/sections/involution.tex","preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/build/sections/counts.tex","preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/build/sections/assembly.tex","preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/build/appendices/handles.tex","preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove the stable Morse lower bound for nondegenerate Hamiltonian fixed points by constructing a simply connected closed Kähler manifold with sixteen fewer fixed points than its stable Morse number. All the corresponding periodic orbits are contractible. The construction combines a Hamiltonian involution with integral homology torsion at two different primes; its proof uses finite-dimensional Morse theory and complex blowups.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":480},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and prior work","sourceOffset":2643},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and its mechanism","sourceOffset":6107},{"kind":"section","title":"Integral homology and geometric Morse counts","sourceOffset":8731},{"kind":"section","title":"A simply connected K\\\"ahler manifold with two torsion primes","sourceOffset":17055},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two quotient surfaces","sourceOffset":17625},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Integral homology under blow-up","sourceOffset":22669},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The manifold \\texorpdfstring{$X$}{X}","sourceOffset":28126},{"kind":"section","title":"Perturbing a Hamiltonian involution","sourceOffset":31669},{"kind":"section","title":"The difference in Morse counts","sourceOffset":43111},{"kind":"section","title":"Proof of the counterexample","sourceOffset":47155},{"kind":"section","title":"Morse attainment by handle cancellation","sourceOffset":49898}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist a simply connected closed Kähler manifold $(M,\\omega)$ and a smooth one-periodic Hamiltonian $H$ such that every fixed point of $\\phi_H^1$ is nondegenerate and \\[ \\#\\Fix_0(\\phi_H^1;H)=\\#\\Fix(\\phi_H^1)=\\SM(M)-16. \\] One may take $\\dim_{\\R}M=1412$, with $\\SM(M)=318968$ and $\\#\\Fix(\\phi_H^1)=318952$.","sourceOffset":2283,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:stable-lower","text":"For every connected closed smooth manifold $W$, \\[ \\SM(W)\\geq \\lambda(W). \\]","sourceOffset":11507,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":null,"text":"Let $W$ be a connected closed smooth manifold. Its stable Morse number $\\SM(W)$ is the minimum of $\\#\\Crit(F)$ over all integers $k\\geq0$ and smooth Morse functions \\[ F:W\\times\\R^k\\longrightarrow\\R \\] such that, when $k>0$, there are a nondegenerate quadratic form $Q$ on $\\R^k$ and $R>0$ with $F(x,z)=Q(z)$ for every $x\\in W$ and $|z|\\geq R$. The signature of $Q$ is unrestricted. For $k=0$, there is no condition at infinity. The ordinary Morse number $\\Morse(W)$ is the same minimum restricted to $k=0$.","sourceOffset":767,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/347.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":67868,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Hamiltonian-Fixed-Points-Below-the-Stable-Morse-Number-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"347-04","slug":"A-nondegenerate-counterexample-to-the-Morse-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026","familyId":"347","title":"A nondegenerate counterexample to the Morse-number Arnold bound","abstract":"We construct a smooth one-periodic Hamiltonian on a closed symplectic twelve-manifold whose time-one map has fewer fixed points than the manifold's ordinary Morse number. Every fixed point is nondegenerate and has a contractible Hamiltonian trajectory. This disproves the Morse-number form of the Arnold conjecture. The deficit can be arbitrarily large among twelve-dimensional examples.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves stable-Morse lower bounds for nondegenerate Hamiltonian fixed points: on simply connected closed Kähler manifolds of real dimension 22, the deficit below the stable Morse number is unbounded. A separate Hamiltonian diffeomorphism of the complex quadric threefold has exactly three fixed points, fewer than the four critical points required of every smooth function.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-nondegenerate-counterexample-to-the-Morse-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-nondegenerate-counterexample-to-the-Morse-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-nondegenerate-counterexample-to-the-Morse-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-nondegenerate-counterexample-to-the-Morse-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-nondegenerate-counterexample-to-the-Morse-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-nondegenerate-counterexample-to-the-Morse-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-algebra.tex","preprints/A-nondegenerate-counterexample-to-the-Morse-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-relative-model.tex","preprints/A-nondegenerate-counterexample-to-the-Morse-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-cancellation.tex","preprints/A-nondegenerate-counterexample-to-the-Morse-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-hamiltonian.tex","preprints/A-nondegenerate-counterexample-to-the-Morse-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-counting.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-nondegenerate-counterexample-to-the-Morse-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a smooth one-periodic Hamiltonian on a closed symplectic twelve-manifold whose time-one map has fewer fixed points than the manifold's ordinary Morse number. Every fixed point is nondegenerate and has a contractible Hamiltonian trajectory. This disproves the Morse-number form of the Arnold conjecture. The deficit can be arbitrarily large among twelve-dimensional examples.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The Morse-number question","sourceOffset":450},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From homological bounds to ordinary Morse theory","sourceOffset":2524},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and its two counts","sourceOffset":6551},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof organization","sourceOffset":9764},{"kind":"section","title":"Group generators and augmentation ideals","sourceOffset":10287},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An elementary row reduction","sourceOffset":17826},{"kind":"section","title":"A relative Morse model in twelve dimensions","sourceOffset":21707},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The domain and its collars","sourceOffset":23696},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An initial height function","sourceOffset":25861},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Levels and the relative handle complex","sourceOffset":27561},{"kind":"section","title":"Geometric reduction of the handles","sourceOffset":32844},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Introducing the augmentation generators","sourceOffset":33310}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist a closed connected symplectic manifold $(M,\\omega)$ of real dimension twelve and a smooth one-periodic Hamiltonian $H$ such that every fixed point of $\\phi_H^1$ is nondegenerate and \\[ \\#\\Fix_0(\\phi_H^1;H)=\\#\\Fix(\\phi_H^1) =\\chi(M)+128<\\Morse(M). \\]","sourceOffset":1900,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:deficit","text":"For every real number $D>0$, there is an example as in Theorem \\ref{thm:main} satisfying \\[ \\Morse(M)-\\#\\Fix(\\phi_H^1)>D. \\] The manifold is allowed to depend on $D$.","sourceOffset":2302,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/347.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":64444,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-nondegenerate-counterexample-to-the-Morse-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"347-05","slug":"A-degenerate-counterexample-to-the-critical-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026","familyId":"347","title":"Three fixed points on the symplectic quadric threefold","abstract":"We construct a smooth Hamiltonian diffeomorphism of the complex quadric threefold with exactly three fixed points, at least one of which is degenerate. The critical number and the unit-inclusive rational cup length of this manifold are both four. Thus the example disproves the unrestricted critical-number and rational cup-length forms of the Arnold conjecture.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves stable-Morse lower bounds for nondegenerate Hamiltonian fixed points: on simply connected closed Kähler manifolds of real dimension 22, the deficit below the stable Morse number is unbounded. A separate Hamiltonian diffeomorphism of the complex quadric threefold has exactly three fixed points, fewer than the four critical points required of every smooth function.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-degenerate-counterexample-to-the-critical-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-degenerate-counterexample-to-the-critical-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-degenerate-counterexample-to-the-critical-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-degenerate-counterexample-to-the-critical-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-degenerate-counterexample-to-the-critical-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/A-degenerate-counterexample-to-the-critical-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-reduction.tex","preprints/A-degenerate-counterexample-to-the-critical-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-quadric.tex","preprints/A-degenerate-counterexample-to-the-critical-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-three-points.tex","preprints/A-degenerate-counterexample-to-the-critical-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-critical-bound.tex","preprints/A-degenerate-counterexample-to-the-critical-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-conclusion.tex","preprints/A-degenerate-counterexample-to-the-critical-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-degenerate-counterexample-to-the-critical-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a smooth Hamiltonian diffeomorphism of the complex quadric threefold with exactly three fixed points, at least one of which is degenerate. The critical number and the unit-inclusive rational cup length of this manifold are both four. Thus the example disproves the unrestricted critical-number and rational cup-length forms of the Arnold conjecture.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":408},{"kind":"section","title":"Perturbing a finite-order Hamiltonian map","sourceOffset":8386},{"kind":"section","title":"A Hamiltonian involution of a quadric","sourceOffset":14287},{"kind":"section","title":"Three critical points on the fixed submanifold","sourceOffset":19557},{"kind":"section","title":"The critical number and cup length of the quadric","sourceOffset":23028},{"kind":"section","title":"The Hamiltonian counterexample","sourceOffset":29486}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:counterexample","text":"There is a smooth Hamiltonian diffeomorphism $\\phi$ of the closed connected symplectic six-manifold $Q^3$ such that \\begin{equation} \\#\\Fix(\\phi)=3<4=\\Crit(Q^3) =\\operatorname{cuplength}(Q^3;\\mathbb Q). \\end{equation} At least one fixed point of $\\phi$ is degenerate.","sourceOffset":2858,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:reduction","text":"Let \\((M,\\omega)\\) be a closed symplectic manifold, and let \\(A\\in\\Ham(M,\\omega)\\) satisfy \\(A^m=\\id\\) for some positive integer \\(m\\). Suppose that \\(F=\\Fix(A)\\) is a closed embedded submanifold and that \\[ T_qF=\\ker(dA_q-\\id) \\qquad\\text{for every }q\\in F. \\] For every smooth function \\(f:F\\to\\R\\), there is a smooth \\(A\\)-invariant extension \\(K:M\\to\\R\\) such that, if \\(B_s\\) is its Hamiltonian flow, then for every sufficiently small \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) the map \\[ \\phi_\\varepsilon=A\\circ B_\\varepsilon \\] is Hamiltonian and satisfies \\[ \\Fix(\\phi_\\varepsilon)=\\CritSet(f), \\] where the critical set on the right is viewed as a subset of \\(M\\).","sourceOffset":8744,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/347.md","scopeText":"The critical-number form of Arnold's fixed-point conjecture predicts at least as many fixed points as the minimum number of critical points of a smooth function. The formalized counterexample is a Hamiltonian diffeomorphism of the complex quadric threefold with exactly three fixed points, while every smooth function on that manifold has at least four critical points. At least one fixed point is degenerate. The separate nondegenerate Morse-number construction is not included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/347.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":36467,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-degenerate-counterexample-to-the-critical-number-Arnold-bound-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"348-01","slug":"Zero-Plane-Rigidity-for-Einstein-Four-Manifolds-October-4-2026","familyId":"348","title":"Zero-Plane Rigidity for Einstein Four-Manifolds","abstract":"We prove that a closed Einstein four-manifold with positive Einstein constant, nonnegative sectional curvature, and a zero-curvature plane has universal Riemannian cover isometric to a product of two round two-spheres. Under the normalization $\\mathop{\\mathrm{Ric}}\\nolimits =3g$, both spheres have radius $1/\\sqrt3$. The proof extends coupled estimates for the two Weyl curvature blocks to the boundary of the sectional-curvature cone and determines their equality case.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Classifies closed connected Einstein four-manifolds with positive Einstein constant and nonnegative sectional curvature: up to scaling, their universal Riemannian covers are the round S4, Fubini–Study $\\mathbb{CP}^2$, or a product of equal round two-spheres. A closed simply connected nonnegatively curved four-manifold is diffeomorphic to one of these whenever the scale-invariant L2 norm of its trace-free Ricci curvature lies below a universal positive constant.","pdfPath":"preprints/Zero-Plane-Rigidity-for-Einstein-Four-Manifolds-October-4-2026/einstein-boundary.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Zero-Plane-Rigidity-for-Einstein-Four-Manifolds-October-4-2026/einstein-boundary.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Zero-Plane-Rigidity-for-Einstein-Four-Manifolds-October-4-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Zero-Plane-Rigidity-for-Einstein-Four-Manifolds-October-4-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Zero-Plane-Rigidity-for-Einstein-Four-Manifolds-October-4-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Zero-Plane-Rigidity-for-Einstein-Four-Manifolds-October-4-2026/build/sections/02-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Zero-Plane-Rigidity-for-Einstein-Four-Manifolds-October-4-2026/build/sections/03-analytic.tex","preprints/Zero-Plane-Rigidity-for-Einstein-Four-Manifolds-October-4-2026/build/sections/04-volume.tex","preprints/Zero-Plane-Rigidity-for-Einstein-Four-Manifolds-October-4-2026/build/sections/05-hessian.tex","preprints/Zero-Plane-Rigidity-for-Einstein-Four-Manifolds-October-4-2026/build/sections/06-coupling.tex","preprints/Zero-Plane-Rigidity-for-Einstein-Four-Manifolds-October-4-2026/build/sections/08-classification.tex","preprints/Zero-Plane-Rigidity-for-Einstein-Four-Manifolds-October-4-2026/build/sections/07-certificates.tex","preprints/Zero-Plane-Rigidity-for-Einstein-Four-Manifolds-October-4-2026/build/figures/spectral-domains.tex","preprints/Zero-Plane-Rigidity-for-Einstein-Four-Manifolds-October-4-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Zero-Plane-Rigidity-for-Einstein-Four-Manifolds-October-4-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that a closed Einstein four-manifold with positive Einstein constant, nonnegative sectional curvature, and a zero-curvature plane has universal Riemannian cover isometric to a product of two round two-spheres. Under the normalization $\\operatorname{Ric}=3g$, both spheres have radius $1/\\sqrt3$. The proof extends coupled estimates for the two Weyl curvature blocks to the boundary of the sectional-curvature cone and determines their equality case.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":504},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and the boundary problem","sourceOffset":2079},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reduction and proof strategy","sourceOffset":5083},{"kind":"section","title":"Curvature blocks and their covariant derivatives","sourceOffset":8779},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two Weyl blocks","sourceOffset":10019},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Algebra of the derivative representations","sourceOffset":13392},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The gradient parametrization and the norm equation","sourceOffset":21581},{"kind":"section","title":"Scalar estimates and the characteristic identities","sourceOffset":28407},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A strengthened scalar variance estimate","sourceOffset":29189},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The sharp scalar inequality and the Weyl gap","sourceOffset":32278},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Euler and signature formulas","sourceOffset":35571},{"kind":"section","title":"Upper volume deficit and balanced Weyl moments","sourceOffset":36927}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Zero-plane rigidity","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $(M^4,g)$ be a connected smooth closed Riemannian manifold satisfying \\[ \\operatorname{Ric}_g=3g,\\qquad K_g(\\sigma)\\ge0 \\] for every tangent two-plane $\\sigma$. If $K_g(\\sigma_0)=0$ for at least one tangent two-plane, then the universal Riemannian cover is isometric to \\[ S^2(1/\\sqrt3)\\times S^2(1/\\sqrt3) \\] with its product metric. In particular, the tangent bundle of the universal cover is the orthogonal sum of two parallel rank-two subbundles.","sourceOffset":1248,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:nonnegative-classification","text":"Let $(M^4,g)$ be connected, smooth, and closed, with $\\operatorname{Ric}=3g$ and nonnegative sectional curvature. Its universal Riemannian cover is one of the round $S^4$ of curvature $1$, the complex projective plane with its Fubini--Study metric normalized by $\\operatorname{Ric}=3g$, or $S^2(1/\\sqrt3)\\times S^2(1/\\sqrt3)$.","sourceOffset":4616,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/348.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":137537,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":15,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Zero-Plane-Rigidity-for-Einstein-Four-Manifolds-October-4-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"348-02","slug":"An-L2-Einstein-Gap-for-Nonnegatively-Curved-Four-Manifolds-October-5-2026","familyId":"348","title":"An L² Einstein Gap for Nonnegatively Curved Four-Manifolds","abstract":"We prove a universal, scale-invariant L2 gap for the trace-free Ricci tensor on simply connected closed four-manifolds with nonnegative sectional curvature. If the trace-free Ricci energy is sufficiently small, the manifold is diffeomorphic to S4, $\\mathbb{CP}^2$, or $S^2\\times S^2$. The proof uses the classification of positive-Einstein, nonnegatively curved four-manifolds supplied by the companion zero-plane rigidity theorem, stated explicitly as the classification premise of our result. No auxiliary curvature, volume, diameter, injectivity-radius, or Sobolev bound is required. The conclusion concerns the smooth manifold, not an isometry of the original metric.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Classifies closed connected Einstein four-manifolds with positive Einstein constant and nonnegative sectional curvature: up to scaling, their universal Riemannian covers are the round S4, Fubini–Study $\\mathbb{CP}^2$, or a product of equal round two-spheres. A closed simply connected nonnegatively curved four-manifold is diffeomorphic to one of these whenever the scale-invariant L2 norm of its trace-free Ricci curvature lies below a universal positive constant.","pdfPath":"preprints/An-L2-Einstein-Gap-for-Nonnegatively-Curved-Four-Manifolds-October-5-2026/einstein-gap.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-L2-Einstein-Gap-for-Nonnegatively-Curved-Four-Manifolds-October-5-2026/einstein-gap.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/An-L2-Einstein-Gap-for-Nonnegatively-Curved-Four-Manifolds-October-5-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/An-L2-Einstein-Gap-for-Nonnegatively-Curved-Four-Manifolds-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/An-L2-Einstein-Gap-for-Nonnegatively-Curved-Four-Manifolds-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a universal, scale-invariant $L^2$ gap for the trace-free Ricci tensor on simply connected closed four-manifolds with nonnegative sectional curvature. If the trace-free Ricci energy is sufficiently small, the manifold is diffeomorphic to $S^4$, $\\CP^2$, or $S^2\\times S^2$. The proof uses the classification of positive-Einstein, nonnegatively curved four-manifolds supplied by the companion zero-plane rigidity theorem, stated explicitly as the classification premise of our result. No auxiliary curvature, volume, diameter, injectivity-radius, or Sobolev bound is required. The conclusion concerns the smooth manifold, not an isometry of the original metric.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":715},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and methods","sourceOffset":2669},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof organization","sourceOffset":6314},{"kind":"section","title":"Normalization and initial geometry","sourceOffset":7323},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A support estimate and the Sobolev inequality","sourceOffset":11693},{"kind":"section","title":"A uniform smoothing interval","sourceOffset":15137},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Entropy supplies a weighted Sobolev inequality","sourceOffset":16011},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The high-curvature part stays small","sourceOffset":18171},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Noncollapse and the common lifespan","sourceOffset":21433},{"kind":"section","title":"The Einstein and sectional-curvature defects","sourceOffset":24335},{"kind":"section","title":"The compact Einstein limit and the gap","sourceOffset":28014}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:gap","text":"Assume Classification Assumption \\ref{ass:classification}. There is a universal constant $\\varepsilon_0>0$ with the following property. If $(M,g)$ is a connected, simply connected, smooth closed Riemannian four-manifold with $\\sec_g\\ge0$ and \\[ \\int_M\\left|\\Ric_g-\\frac{\\Scal_g}{4}g\\right|_g^2\\,d\\mu_g <\\varepsilon_0, \\] then $M$ is diffeomorphic, without a prescribed orientation, to the standard smooth $S^4$, $\\CP^2$, or $S^2\\times S^2$.","sourceOffset":1862,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:initial","text":"After constant rescaling, the metrics in \\eqref{eq:sequence} satisfy \\begin{equation} \\frac1V\\int_M R\\dd=12,\\qquad \\norm{R-12}_2\\le4\\norm E_2,\\qquad \\norm{\\Ric-3g}_2\\longrightarrow0, \\end{equation} where $V=\\Vol(M,g)$. There are uniform positive constants $v_0,V_0,D_0$ such that \\begin{equation} v_0\\le V\\le V_0,\\qquad \\diam(M,g)\\le D_0. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":7761,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"assumption","name":"","label":"ass:classification","text":"Every connected smooth closed Riemannian four-manifold $(N,h)$ satisfying \\[ \\Ric_h=3h,\\qquad \\sec_h\\ge0 \\] has universal Riemannian cover isometric to one of the following: the round $S^4$ of sectional curvature $1$; Fubini--Study $\\CP^2$ normalized by $\\Ric=3g$; or $S^2(1/\\sqrt3)\\times S^2(1/\\sqrt3)$ with its product metric.","sourceOffset":1375,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/348.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":33027,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/An-L2-Einstein-Gap-for-Nonnegatively-Curved-Four-Manifolds-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/An-L2-Einstein-Gap-for-Nonnegatively-Curved-Four-Manifolds-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/An-L2-Einstein-Gap-for-Nonnegatively-Curved-Four-Manifolds-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"348-03","slug":"Positively-curved-Einstein-four-manifolds-September-23-2026","familyId":"348","title":"Positively curved Einstein four-manifolds","abstract":"We prove the classification conjecture for connected smooth closed Einstein four-manifolds with strictly positive sectional curvature. Up to positive scaling and isometry, every such manifold is the round four-sphere, the complex projective plane with its Fubini–Study metric, or real projective four-space with its round metric. No orientability assumption is needed.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Classifies closed connected Einstein four-manifolds with positive Einstein constant and nonnegative sectional curvature: up to scaling, their universal Riemannian covers are the round S4, Fubini–Study $\\mathbb{CP}^2$, or a product of equal round two-spheres. A closed simply connected nonnegatively curved four-manifold is diffeomorphic to one of these whenever the scale-invariant L2 norm of its trace-free Ricci curvature lies below a universal positive constant.","pdfPath":"preprints/Positively-curved-Einstein-four-manifolds-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Positively-curved-Einstein-four-manifolds-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Positively-curved-Einstein-four-manifolds-September-23-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Positively-curved-Einstein-four-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Positively-curved-Einstein-four-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Positively-curved-Einstein-four-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/figures/proof-map.tex","preprints/Positively-curved-Einstein-four-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Positively-curved-Einstein-four-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-analytic.tex","preprints/Positively-curved-Einstein-four-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-volume.tex","preprints/Positively-curved-Einstein-four-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-hessian.tex","preprints/Positively-curved-Einstein-four-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-coupling.tex","preprints/Positively-curved-Einstein-four-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-certificates.tex","preprints/Positively-curved-Einstein-four-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/figures/spectral-domains.tex","preprints/Positively-curved-Einstein-four-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-classification.tex","preprints/Positively-curved-Einstein-four-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/appendix-a-analytic.tex","preprints/Positively-curved-Einstein-four-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/sections/appendix-b-topology.tex","preprints/Positively-curved-Einstein-four-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Positively-curved-Einstein-four-manifolds-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the classification conjecture for connected smooth closed Einstein four-manifolds with strictly positive sectional curvature. Up to positive scaling and isometry, every such manifold is the round four-sphere, the complex projective plane with its Fubini--Study metric, or real projective four-space with its round metric. No orientability assumption is needed.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":415},{"kind":"section","title":"Curvature blocks and their covariant derivatives","sourceOffset":12528},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two Weyl blocks","sourceOffset":13483},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Algebra of the derivative representations","sourceOffset":16841},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The gradient parametrization and the norm equation","sourceOffset":24728},{"kind":"section","title":"Scalar estimates and the characteristic identities","sourceOffset":31313},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A strengthened scalar variance estimate","sourceOffset":32193},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The sharp scalar inequality and the Weyl gap","sourceOffset":35453},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Euler and signature formulas","sourceOffset":38747},{"kind":"section","title":"Upper volume deficit and balanced Weyl moments","sourceOffset":39958},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Minimizing geodesics and upper polar integration","sourceOffset":43708},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Discrete determinant comparisons","sourceOffset":46200}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"proposition","name":"The remaining parallel tensors","label":"prop:parallel-models","text":"Up to reversing orientation, either $T=U=0$, or $U=0$ and $T$ is parallel with eigenvalues $2,-1,-1$.","sourceOffset":135501,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:classification","text":"Let $(M,g)$ be a connected smooth closed Einstein four-manifold with strictly positive sectional curvature, without an orientability assumption. There is a constant $a>0$ such that $(M,ag)$ is isometric to the standard round $S^4$, to $\\mathbb{CP}^2$ with its Fubini--Study metric, or to $\\mathbb{RP}^4$ with its standard round metric.","sourceOffset":1050,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/348.md","scopeText":"The formalization classifies connected smooth closed Einstein four-manifolds with strictly positive sectional curvature. Up to positive scaling and isometry, every such manifold is the round four-sphere, the complex projective plane with its Fubini–Study metric, or round real projective four-space. No orientability hypothesis is required. In the oriented case, only the sphere and complex projective plane occur.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/348.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":175958,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":16,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Positively-curved-Einstein-four-manifolds-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"349-01","slug":"The-Solomon-Yau-least-volume-theorem-September-23-2026","familyId":"349","title":"The Solomon–Yau least-volume theorem","abstract":"We prove the Solomon–Yau least-volume conjecture for minimal hypersurfaces of unit round spheres: in every dimension m ≥ 2, a closed connected minimal immersion with non-totally-geodesic image has volume at least the smallest m-dimensional minimal Clifford product. Volume is measured on the domain, so covering multiplicities are included.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the Solomon–Yau least-volume conjecture for minimal hypersurfaces of round spheres. For every m ≥ 2, a closed connected minimal immersion into the unit sphere $S^{m+1}$ with non-totally-geodesic image has volume at least that of the smallest minimal Clifford product, counting covering multiplicity.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Solomon-Yau-least-volume-theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Solomon-Yau-least-volume-theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Solomon-Yau-least-volume-theorem-September-23-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/The-Solomon-Yau-least-volume-theorem-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Solomon-Yau-least-volume-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/The-Solomon-Yau-least-volume-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/The-Solomon-Yau-least-volume-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/index.tex","preprints/The-Solomon-Yau-least-volume-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/minmax.tex","preprints/The-Solomon-Yau-least-volume-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/family.tex","preprints/The-Solomon-Yau-least-volume-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/cones.tex","preprints/The-Solomon-Yau-least-volume-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Solomon-Yau-least-volume-theorem-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Solomon--Yau least-volume conjecture for minimal hypersurfaces of unit round spheres: in every dimension \\(m\\ge2\\), a closed connected minimal immersion with non-totally-geodesic image has volume at least the smallest \\(m\\)-dimensional minimal Clifford product. Volume is measured on the domain, so covering multiplicities are included.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":396},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier results and the geometric obstacle","sourceOffset":1918},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Curvature, index, and the variational family","sourceOffset":4898},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compactness and the final induction","sourceOffset":7843},{"kind":"section","title":"Normalization and the immersion reduction","sourceOffset":9771},{"kind":"section","title":"Average curvature and index rigidity","sourceOffset":16404},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A two-sided curvature maximum","sourceOffset":17761},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Normal coordinates and conformal tests","sourceOffset":28920},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The least possible index","sourceOffset":34086},{"kind":"section","title":"Cohomological detection and the min--max input","sourceOffset":40379},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The filling cover and projective detection","sourceOffset":43274},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A strict perimeter gap","sourceOffset":48743}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:volume","text":"Let \\(m\\ge2\\), let \\(M^m\\) be a closed connected smooth manifold, and let \\(F:M\\to S^{m+1}\\) be a smooth minimal immersion into the unit round sphere. If \\(F(M)\\) is not totally geodesic, then \\[ \\Vol\\bigl(M,F^*g_{\\mathrm{round}}\\bigr)\\ge a_m. \\]","sourceOffset":1416,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Immersion reduction","label":"prop:immersion-reduction","text":"Let \\(F:M^m\\to S^{m+1}\\) be a smooth minimal immersion of a closed manifold, with \\(m\\ge2\\). If some image point has \\(q\\) preimages, then \\[ \\Vol\\bigl(M,F^*g_{\\mathrm{round}}\\bigr)\\ge q\\sigma_m. \\] In particular, every immersion violating the bound in Theorem \\ref{thm:volume} is an embedding.","sourceOffset":12452,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Detection","label":"def:detecting","text":"A flat-continuous map $\\Phi:X\\to\\mathcal Z_m^0(S^{m+1};\\mathbb Z_2)$ from a finite CW complex is detecting if \\[ (\\Phi^*\\lambda)^p\\ne0\\quad\\hbox{in }H^p(X;\\mathbb Z_2). \\] Detection is preserved by flat homotopies. We say that $\\Phi$ has no concentration of mass if \\begin{equation} \\lim_{\\rho\\downarrow0}\\ \\sup_{x\\in X,\\,z\\in S^{m+1}} \\|\\Phi(x)\\|(B_\\rho(z))=0. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":46630,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":108837,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Solomon-Yau-least-volume-theorem-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"350-01","slug":"Sharp-nodal-length-on-smooth-surfaces-September-23-2026","familyId":"350","title":"Sharp nodal length on smooth surfaces","abstract":"We prove that a nonzero real Laplace eigenfunction with eigenvalue λ > 0 on a fixed smooth closed connected Riemannian surface has nodal length at most $C\\sqrt\\lambda$. Together with the known lower bound, this proves Yau's conjecture in this setting.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sharp $C\\sqrt\\lambda$ upper bound for nodal length on every fixed smooth closed surface, completing Yau's conjecture there. The upper bound fails for fixed smooth metrics in dimensions three and four, including metrics on S3 arbitrarily close to round. In dimension five, nodal measure can grow faster than $\\lambda^{1/2+\\varepsilon_0}$ for some fixed $\\varepsilon_0\\gt 0$, ruling out even arbitrarily small power losses.","pdfPath":"preprints/Sharp-nodal-length-on-smooth-surfaces-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Sharp-nodal-length-on-smooth-surfaces-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Sharp-nodal-length-on-smooth-surfaces-September-23-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Sharp-nodal-length-on-smooth-surfaces-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Sharp-nodal-length-on-smooth-surfaces-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Sharp-nodal-length-on-smooth-surfaces-September-23-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/Sharp-nodal-length-on-smooth-surfaces-September-23-2026/build/profiles.tex","preprints/Sharp-nodal-length-on-smooth-surfaces-September-23-2026/build/subdivision.tex","preprints/Sharp-nodal-length-on-smooth-surfaces-September-23-2026/build/descent.tex","preprints/Sharp-nodal-length-on-smooth-surfaces-September-23-2026/build/persistence.tex","preprints/Sharp-nodal-length-on-smooth-surfaces-September-23-2026/build/length.tex","preprints/Sharp-nodal-length-on-smooth-surfaces-September-23-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Sharp-nodal-length-on-smooth-surfaces-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that a nonzero real Laplace eigenfunction with eigenvalue $\\lambda>0$ on a fixed smooth closed connected Riemannian surface has nodal length at most $C\\sqrt\\lambda$. Together with the known lower bound, this proves Yau's conjecture in this setting.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction and main result","sourceOffset":303},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Mean growth as the target","sourceOffset":2004},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Affine correction and persistent growth","sourceOffset":3848},{"kind":"section","title":"Subharmonic limits of tilted norms","sourceOffset":5963},{"kind":"section","title":"Affine improvement under subdivision","sourceOffset":24248},{"kind":"section","title":"Descent and stability","sourceOffset":38121},{"kind":"section","title":"Persistence and packing","sourceOffset":49603},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Entries and persistent nearby regions","sourceOffset":50742},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Packing with witnesses outside the squares","sourceOffset":60938},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The terminal mean growth","sourceOffset":67832},{"kind":"section","title":"Nodal length at the wavelength scale","sourceOffset":70590}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $(M,g)$ be a closed connected $C^\\infty$ Riemannian surface. There is a finite constant $C=C(M,g)$ such that every nonzero real Laplace eigenfunction satisfying \\[ -\\Delta_g u=\\lambda u,\\qquad \\lambda>0, \\] obeys \\[ \\mathcal H_g^1(Z_u)\\le C\\sqrt\\lambda. \\] Here $\\mathcal H_g^1$ is Hausdorff measure for the Riemannian distance. The same constant applies to every eigenfunction in every positive eigenspace.","sourceOffset":582,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Subharmonicity of logarithmic profiles","label":"thm:subharmonic-profile","text":"On a fixed planar disk $\\Omega$, let real smooth functions $U_j,p_j$ satisfy \\[ \\Delta U_j+K_j^2p_jU_j=0, \\] where $K_j\\ge0$. Let $S_j>0$, $B_j\\in\\R^2$, and $D_j=S_j+|B_j|$. Assume \\begin{equation} \\begin{gathered} S_j\\longrightarrow\\infty,\\qquad \\frac{K_j}{D_j}\\longrightarrow0,\\\\ \\sup_j\\|p_j\\|_\\infty<\\infty,\\qquad \\frac{K_j^2\\|\\nabla p_j\\|_\\infty}{S_jD_j}\\longrightarrow0. \\end{gathered} \\end{equation} Local versions of the coefficient assumptions on relatively compact subdisks suffice. For positive constants $c_j$, suppose the measures \\[ d\\mu_j=c_j e^{-2B_j\\cdot x}|U_j(x)|^2\\,dx \\] have mass at most one on $\\Omega$. Every upper semicontinuous logarithmic profile with the compact, open, and weighted bounds of Lemma \\ref{lem:profile-extraction} is subharmonic on $\\Omega$, with the identically $-\\infty$ function allowed.","sourceOffset":9726,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Growth and affine excess","label":"def:growth","text":"Fix an isothermal coordinate region in which \\(\\Delta u+k^2p u=0\\), and work in a relatively compact subset on which \\(p\\) and \\(\\nabla p\\) have fixed uniform bounds. Set \\(R=1000\\). Choose a root square \\(Q_0\\), with side \\(s_0\\le1\\) and center \\(x_{Q_0}\\), such that \\(\\overline{\\D_{2Rs_0}(x_{Q_0})}\\) lies in this subset. For an integer \\(A\\ge2\\), subdivide each square into \\(A^2\\) congruent squares and write \\(q\\prec Q\\) when \\(q\\) is an immediate child of \\(Q\\). For a square \\(Q\\) of side \\(s=s(Q)\\) and center \\(x_Q\\), put \\(K_Q=ks\\). For a real vector \\(b\\in\\R^2\\), define \\begin{equation} ","sourceOffset":24623,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Excursions","label":"def:excursions","text":"Choose a threshold $H>\\max\\{L,C_{\\mathrm{init}}\\}$. Along each descending grid path, an excursion starts the first time $N(Q)>H$ and ends the first subsequent time $N(Q)\\le L$. After an excursion has ended, apply the same rule again. A square at which an excursion starts is called an entry. An excursion is active at a square if it has started and has not yet ended there.","sourceOffset":50794,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/350.md","scopeText":"Yau's nodal-set conjecture predicts nodal size of order $\\sqrt\\lambda$ for Laplace eigenfunctions of eigenvalue $\\lambda$. The formalization proves the upper bound on every fixed smooth closed connected Riemannian surface: there is a surface-dependent constant $C$ such that the one-dimensional Hausdorff measure of the zero set of every nonzero real eigenfunction with $\\lambda>0$ is at most $C\\sqrt\\lambda$. The known lower bound is not part of this selected statement.\n\nThe formalized results contradict Yau's proposed $O(\\sqrt\\lambda)$ upper bound for nodal measure in dimensions three and four. On $S^3$, every prescribed smooth neighborhood of the round metric contains one fixed smooth metric with an eigenfunction sequence of unbounded nodal-measure ratio. A second fixed metric on $S^2\\times","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/350.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":79236,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Sharp-nodal-length-on-smooth-surfaces-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Sharp-nodal-length-on-smooth-surfaces-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"350-02","slug":"Smooth-counterexamples-to-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-in-dimensions-three-and-four-September-23-2026","familyId":"350","title":"Smooth counterexamples to Yau's nodal upper bound in dimensions three and four","abstract":"We construct a smooth metric on the three-sphere, arbitrarily close to the round metric in the smooth topology, and a smooth metric on $S^2\\times\\mathbb T^2$ for which sequences of exact real Laplace eigenfunctions have unbounded nodal measure divided by the square root of the eigenvalue. Each sequence belongs to one fixed metric. Thus the upper-bound part of Yau's nodal conjecture fails for smooth metrics in dimensions three and four.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sharp $C\\sqrt\\lambda$ upper bound for nodal length on every fixed smooth closed surface, completing Yau's conjecture there. The upper bound fails for fixed smooth metrics in dimensions three and four, including metrics on S3 arbitrarily close to round. In dimension five, nodal measure can grow faster than $\\lambda^{1/2+\\varepsilon_0}$ for some fixed $\\varepsilon_0\\gt 0$, ruling out even arbitrarily small power losses.","pdfPath":"preprints/Smooth-counterexamples-to-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-in-dimensions-three-and-four-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Smooth-counterexamples-to-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-in-dimensions-three-and-four-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Smooth-counterexamples-to-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-in-dimensions-three-and-four-September-23-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Smooth-counterexamples-to-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-in-dimensions-three-and-four-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Smooth-counterexamples-to-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-in-dimensions-three-and-four-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Smooth-counterexamples-to-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-in-dimensions-three-and-four-September-23-2026/build/sections/s3-profiles.tex","preprints/Smooth-counterexamples-to-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-in-dimensions-three-and-four-September-23-2026/build/sections/s3-waves.tex","preprints/Smooth-counterexamples-to-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-in-dimensions-three-and-four-September-23-2026/build/sections/s3-exactification.tex","preprints/Smooth-counterexamples-to-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-in-dimensions-three-and-four-September-23-2026/build/sections/s3-persistence.tex","preprints/Smooth-counterexamples-to-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-in-dimensions-three-and-four-September-23-2026/build/sections/s4-envelopes.tex","preprints/Smooth-counterexamples-to-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-in-dimensions-three-and-four-September-23-2026/build/sections/s4-waves.tex","preprints/Smooth-counterexamples-to-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-in-dimensions-three-and-four-September-23-2026/build/sections/s4-signs.tex","preprints/Smooth-counterexamples-to-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-in-dimensions-three-and-four-September-23-2026/build/sections/s4-correction.tex","preprints/Smooth-counterexamples-to-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-in-dimensions-three-and-four-September-23-2026/build/sections/s4-spectral.tex","preprints/Smooth-counterexamples-to-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-in-dimensions-three-and-four-September-23-2026/build/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Smooth-counterexamples-to-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-in-dimensions-three-and-four-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a smooth metric on the three-sphere, arbitrarily close to the round metric in the smooth topology, and a smooth metric on $S^2\\times\\T^2$ for which sequences of exact real Laplace eigenfunctions have unbounded nodal measure divided by the square root of the eigenvalue. Each sequence belongs to one fixed metric. Thus the upper-bound part of Yau's nodal conjecture fails for smooth metrics in dimensions three and four.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":478},{"kind":"part","title":"The three-sphere construction","sourceOffset":10809},{"kind":"section","title":"Profiles with large mean gradient","sourceOffset":10848},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Localization and periodic averaging","sourceOffset":14031},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Preparing a full region","sourceOffset":16682},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Radial corrugation in two transverse variables","sourceOffset":21065},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A fixed gain and finite iteration","sourceOffset":28952},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Profiles on the round sphere","sourceOffset":31890},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite complex waves and robust nodal-area witnesses","sourceOffset":36697},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Setting and the critical-point hypothesis","sourceOffset":37223},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Complex Hessians below the profile","sourceOffset":39547},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite Taylor construction","sourceOffset":44195}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $g_*$ be the round metric on $\\Sph^3$. Every neighborhood of $g_*$ in the $C^\\infty$ topology contains a smooth Riemannian metric $g_\\infty$ for which there are nonzero smooth real functions $u_j$ and positive numbers $\\lambda_j$ satisfying \\[ -\\Delta_{g_\\infty}u_j=\\lambda_j u_j,\\qquad \\lambda_j\\longrightarrow\\infty,\\qquad \\frac{\\HH^2_{g_\\infty}(Z_{u_j})}{\\sqrt{\\lambda_j}} \\longrightarrow\\infty. \\]","sourceOffset":1264,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:four","text":"There are a smooth Riemannian metric $g_\\infty$ on $S^2\\times\\T^2$, nonzero smooth real functions $u_j$, and positive numbers $\\lambda_j\\to\\infty$ such that \\[ -\\Delta_{g_\\infty}u_j=\\lambda_j u_j,\\qquad \\frac{\\cH^3_{g_\\infty}(Z_{u_j})}{\\sqrt{\\lambda_j}} \\longrightarrow\\infty. \\]","sourceOffset":1727,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Strict and full profiles","label":"def:strict-profile","text":"A smooth function $\\phi$ is strict at a point if there exists a real vector $b$ at that point such that \\begin{equation} b\\perp a_\\phi,\\qquad |b|=L_\\phi,\\qquad H_\\phi(a_\\phi,a_\\phi)+H_\\phi(b,b)>0. \\end{equation} It is full at that point if the last inequality holds for every $b$ satisfying the first two conditions.","sourceOffset":11768,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Sign score","label":"score:definition","text":"Subdivide $D$, up to its grid boundaries, into finitely many disjoint open coordinate cubes $Q$, fine enough that numbers $B_Q>0$ satisfy \\[ B_Q\\le B(x)\\le 2B_Q\\qquad (x\\in Q). \\] Choose $c_0>0$ as in Lemma \\ref{score:expectation} below and put \\[ \\ell_Q=\\frac{c_0}{kB_Q},\\qquad Q^- =\\{x\\in Q:\\operatorname{dist}_{\\infty}(x,\\partial Q)>\\ell_Q\\}, \\qquad T_k=\\sum_Q\\ell_Q^{-1}|Q|. \\] For a real continuous function $\\psi$ on $M$, define \\begin{equation} \\mathcal S(\\psi)=\\sum_Q\\ell_Q^{-1} \\int_{Q^-}\\sum_{i=1}^4 \\mathbf 1_{\\{\\psi(x)\\psi(x+\\ell_Q d_i)<0\\}}\\,dx, \\end{equation} where $d_i$ are the four c","sourceOffset":145090,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/350.md","scopeText":"Yau's nodal-set conjecture predicts nodal size of order $\\sqrt\\lambda$ for Laplace eigenfunctions of eigenvalue $\\lambda$. The formalization proves the upper bound on every fixed smooth closed connected Riemannian surface: there is a surface-dependent constant $C$ such that the one-dimensional Hausdorff measure of the zero set of every nonzero real eigenfunction with $\\lambda>0$ is at most $C\\sqrt\\lambda$. The known lower bound is not part of this selected statement.\n\nThe formalized results contradict Yau's proposed $O(\\sqrt\\lambda)$ upper bound for nodal measure in dimensions three and four. On $S^3$, every prescribed smooth neighborhood of the round metric contains one fixed smooth metric with an eigenfunction sequence of unbounded nodal-measure ratio. A second fixed metric on $S^2\\times","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/350.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":196567,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Smooth-counterexamples-to-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-in-dimensions-three-and-four-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Smooth-counterexamples-to-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-in-dimensions-three-and-four-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"350-03","slug":"Power-law-violations-of-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-September-23-2026","familyId":"350","title":"Power-law violations of Yau's nodal upper bound","abstract":"We construct a smooth Riemannian metric on $S^4\\times S^1$ and a sequence of real Laplace eigenfunctions whose nodal four-volume grows faster than $\\lambda^{1/2+\\epsilon_0}$ for one fixed $\\epsilon_0\\gt 0$. This disproves the smooth upper-bound assertion in Yau's nodal-set conjecture and the proposed bound with an arbitrarily small positive power loss.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the sharp $C\\sqrt\\lambda$ upper bound for nodal length on every fixed smooth closed surface, completing Yau's conjecture there. The upper bound fails for fixed smooth metrics in dimensions three and four, including metrics on S3 arbitrarily close to round. In dimension five, nodal measure can grow faster than $\\lambda^{1/2+\\varepsilon_0}$ for some fixed $\\varepsilon_0\\gt 0$, ruling out even arbitrarily small power losses.","pdfPath":"preprints/Power-law-violations-of-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Power-law-violations-of-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Power-law-violations-of-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-September-23-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Power-law-violations-of-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Power-law-violations-of-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Power-law-violations-of-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-September-23-2026/build/sections/framework.tex","preprints/Power-law-violations-of-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-September-23-2026/build/sections/envelopes.tex","preprints/Power-law-violations-of-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-September-23-2026/build/sections/waves.tex","preprints/Power-law-violations-of-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-September-23-2026/build/sections/global.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Power-law-violations-of-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a smooth Riemannian metric on $S^4\\times S^1$ and a sequence of real Laplace eigenfunctions whose nodal four-volume grows faster than $\\lambda^{1/2+\\epsilon_0}$ for one fixed $\\epsilon_0>0$. This disproves the smooth upper-bound assertion in Yau's nodal-set conjecture and the proposed bound with an arbitrarily small positive power loss.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":397},{"kind":"section","title":"Framework and the quantitative step","sourceOffset":7383},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The weighted base operator","sourceOffset":7451},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A round region and sign certificates","sourceOffset":10199},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The one-step construction","sourceOffset":12537},{"kind":"section","title":"Envelopes with a fixed power gain","sourceOffset":14351},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A seed and the preparation condition","sourceOffset":14960},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A repeatable local corrugation","sourceOffset":20390},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Iteration and a common exponent for every finite order","sourceOffset":36873},{"kind":"section","title":"Local packets below an envelope","sourceOffset":43099},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The phase Hessian","sourceOffset":44245},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Construction and estimates for a packet family","sourceOffset":49514}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist a smooth Riemannian metric $g$ on $S^4\\times S^1$, a number $\\epsilon_0>0$, and nonzero real smooth functions $u_j$ with positive eigenvalues $\\lambda_j$ such that \\[ -\\Delta_g u_j=\\lambda_j u_j,\\qquad \\lambda_j\\longrightarrow\\infty,\\qquad \\frac{\\Hh_g^4(\\Z(u_j))} {\\lambda_j^{1/2+\\epsilon_0}}\\longrightarrow\\infty. \\] The metric and $\\epsilon_0$ are fixed throughout the sequence.","sourceOffset":1452,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:step","text":"There exist a $C^1$ neighborhood $\\mathcal U$ of the round pair $(g_0^{-1},1)$ among smooth positive pairs, a chart $\\Omega$ as above, and a number $\\gamma>0$ with the following property. Fix a smooth pair $(A,\\rho)\\in\\mathcal U$, numbers \\[ \\frac14\\le l_-<l_+\\le\\frac12, \\] and suppose that $(A,\\rho)=(g_0^{-1},1)$ on $\\{t<l_+\\}$. For every integer $r\\ge0$ and every $\\eta>0$, there exist $c>0$ and $N_0$ such that for every integer $N\\ge N_0$, with $k$ as in \\eqref{eq:round-beam}, there are a smooth positive pair $(\\widehat A,\\widehat\\rho)\\in\\mathcal U$ and a nonzero real smooth function $u$ satisfying: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item $\\|(\\widehat A-A,\\widehat\\rho-\\rho)\\|_{C^r}<\\eta$, and $(\\widehat A,\\widehat\\rho)=(g_0^{-1},1)$ on $\\{t<l_-\\}$; \\item for $\\widehat L$ defined by \\eqref{eq:weighted-operator}, $-\\widehat Lu=k^2u$, and $k^2$ is a simple eigenvalue of $-\\widehat L$ on the base $B$; \\item $u$ has a sign certificate in $\\Omega$ of size at least $c k^{1+\\gamma}$. \\end{enumerate} The neighborhood $\\mathcal U$, chart $\\Omega$, and exponent $\\gamma$ are common to all stages. The constant $c$ and threshold $N_0$ may depend on the fixed input pair, $l_-,l_+,r,\\eta$.","sourceOffset":12577,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:certificate","text":"A sign certificate for a continuous real function $u$ in $\\Omega$ is a finite family of pairs $(D_i^-,D_i^+)$ of congruent closed parallel three-dimensional Euclidean disks such that: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item each pair is the two ends of a straight closed cylinder compactly contained in a Euclidean coordinate ball; \\item these coordinate balls are pairwise disjoint; \\item $u$ has strictly opposite signs on $D_i^-$ and $D_i^+$. \\end{enumerate} The size of the certificate is $\\sum_i\\Hh_{\\mathrm{Euc}}^3(D_i^+)$.","sourceOffset":11462,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/350.md","scopeText":"Yau's nodal-set conjecture predicts nodal size of order $\\sqrt\\lambda$ for Laplace eigenfunctions of eigenvalue $\\lambda$. The formalization proves the upper bound on every fixed smooth closed connected Riemannian surface: there is a surface-dependent constant $C$ such that the one-dimensional Hausdorff measure of the zero set of every nonzero real eigenfunction with $\\lambda>0$ is at most $C\\sqrt\\lambda$. The known lower bound is not part of this selected statement.\n\nThe formalized results contradict Yau's proposed $O(\\sqrt\\lambda)$ upper bound for nodal measure in dimensions three and four. On $S^3$, every prescribed smooth neighborhood of the round metric contains one fixed smooth metric with an eigenfunction sequence of unbounded nodal-measure ratio. A second fixed metric on $S^2\\times","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/350.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":108685,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Power-law-violations-of-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"351-01","slug":"A-closed-Ricci-flow-with-bounded-scalar-curvature-and-finite-time-curvature-blowup-September-24-2026","familyId":"351","title":"A closed Ricci flow with bounded scalar curvature and finite-time curvature blowup","abstract":"We disprove the scalar-curvature extension conjecture in its unrestricted all-dimensions form. In sufficiently high dimension, we construct a Ricci flow on a closed manifold whose scalar curvature remains uniformly bounded while full curvature diverges at a finite maximal time. In one fixed sufficiently high dimension, the examples have two-sided power-law curvature blowup with arbitrarily large exponents.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that a smooth Ricci flow on a closed four-manifold extends past any finite time at which scalar curvature remains uniformly bounded. A higher-dimensional counterexample has bounded scalar curvature but unbounded full curvature at its finite maximal time, disproving the unrestricted scalar-curvature extension conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-closed-Ricci-flow-with-bounded-scalar-curvature-and-finite-time-curvature-blowup-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-closed-Ricci-flow-with-bounded-scalar-curvature-and-finite-time-curvature-blowup-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-closed-Ricci-flow-with-bounded-scalar-curvature-and-finite-time-curvature-blowup-September-24-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-closed-Ricci-flow-with-bounded-scalar-curvature-and-finite-time-curvature-blowup-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-closed-Ricci-flow-with-bounded-scalar-curvature-and-finite-time-curvature-blowup-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-closed-Ricci-flow-with-bounded-scalar-curvature-and-finite-time-curvature-blowup-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-construction.tex","preprints/A-closed-Ricci-flow-with-bounded-scalar-curvature-and-finite-time-curvature-blowup-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-geometry.tex","preprints/A-closed-Ricci-flow-with-bounded-scalar-curvature-and-finite-time-curvature-blowup-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-cap.tex","preprints/A-closed-Ricci-flow-with-bounded-scalar-curvature-and-finite-time-curvature-blowup-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-reaction.tex","preprints/A-closed-Ricci-flow-with-bounded-scalar-curvature-and-finite-time-curvature-blowup-September-24-2026/build/figures/drift-cylinder.tex","preprints/A-closed-Ricci-flow-with-bounded-scalar-curvature-and-finite-time-curvature-blowup-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-barriers.tex","preprints/A-closed-Ricci-flow-with-bounded-scalar-curvature-and-finite-time-curvature-blowup-September-24-2026/build/figures/scales.tex","preprints/A-closed-Ricci-flow-with-bounded-scalar-curvature-and-finite-time-curvature-blowup-September-24-2026/build/references/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-closed-Ricci-flow-with-bounded-scalar-curvature-and-finite-time-curvature-blowup-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We disprove the scalar-curvature extension conjecture in its unrestricted all-dimensions form. In sufficiently high dimension, we construct a Ricci flow on a closed manifold whose scalar curvature remains uniformly bounded while full curvature diverges at a finite maximal time. In one fixed sufficiently high dimension, the examples have two-sided power-law curvature blowup with arbitrarily large exponents.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":456},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two scales and the proof","sourceOffset":4711},{"kind":"section","title":"The construction used as input","sourceOffset":7864},{"kind":"section","title":"Geometric identities and profile consequences","sourceOffset":14907},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Warping equations","sourceOffset":15324},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Consequences of the construction","sourceOffset":19104},{"kind":"section","title":"Curvature at the radius and cap scales","sourceOffset":24297},{"kind":"section","title":"A dimension-explicit Ricci reaction bound","sourceOffset":35273},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A one-dimensional interior estimate","sourceOffset":35713},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cylinders moving with the radial drift","sourceOffset":38765},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Ricci norm and its reaction matrix","sourceOffset":43421},{"kind":"section","title":"Comparison and the scalar-curvature bound","sourceOffset":46508}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are an integer \\(q\\ge10\\), a time \\(T>0\\), and a smooth Ricci flow \\(g(t)\\), \\(0\\le t<T\\), on the closed connected manifold \\(M=\\Sp^2\\times\\Sp^{q+1}\\), with smooth initial metric, such that \\[ \\sup_{M\\times[0,T)}|R_{g(t)}|<\\infty, \\qquad \\lim_{t\\uparrow T}\\max_M|\\Rm_{g(t)}|=\\infty. \\] In particular, \\(T\\) is the finite maximal existence time of this flow.","sourceOffset":3623,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Construction input","label":"prop:input","text":"For the parameters above, Stolarski's construction supplies a smooth Ricci flow up to a finite time \\(T\\) on \\(\\Sp^p\\times\\Sp^{q+1}\\) with the following properties, after translating its starting time to zero. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item Off the two pole orbits it has the form \\[ g=ds^2+\\phi^2g_{\\Sp^p}+r^2g_{\\Sp^q}. \\] Here \\(s\\) is radial arclength at each time. The metric is invariant under reflection exchanging the poles. On each open hemisphere, oriented from its pole toward the equator, \\(r_s>0\\). At a pole, \\(r=0\\), \\(r_s=1\\), and \\(\\phi_s=0\\); the usual odd and even polar expansions hold for \\(r\\) and \\(\\phi\\), respectively, and \\(\\phi>0\\) for every \\(t<T\\). \\item Write \\(u=\\log\\phi\\), \\(z=r_s^2\\), and \\[ \\Ut=\\log\\frac{\\phi}{(A/B)r},\\qquad \\Zt=z-B^2. \\] There are positive tolerances \\(\\eta_j^U,\\eta_j^Z\\), large fixed numbers \\(1<Y_1\\le Y_2\\), and constants \\(M_0>0\\), \\(0<\\beta<1/2\\), such that for \\(j=0,1,2\\), \\begin{equation} \\begin{aligned} \\left|\\partial_\\gamma^j(\\Ut-\\delta^\\nu U_k)\\right| &\\le\\eta_j^U\\delta^\\nu \\bigl(\\gamma^{-d-j}+\\gamma^{2k-d}\\bigr),\\\\ \\left|\\partial_\\gamma^j(\\Zt-\\delta^\\nu Z_k)\\right| &\\le\\eta_j^Z\\delta^\\nu \\bigl(\\gamma^{-d-j}+\\gamma^{2k-d}\\bigr). \\end{aligned} \\end{equation} The first estimate holds from \\(Y_1\\delta^{\\nu/d}\\) to \\(M_0e^{\\beta\\tau}\\), and the second from \\(Y_2\\delta^{\\nu/d}\\) to that upper endpoint, within the hemisphere. The profiles are smooth on \\(\\gamma>0\\). The function \\(U_k\\) is \\(\\gamma^{-d}\\) times ","sourceOffset":8893,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":64316,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-closed-Ricci-flow-with-bounded-scalar-curvature-and-finite-time-curvature-blowup-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"351-02","slug":"Bounded-scalar-curvature-and-smooth-extension-of-four-dimensional-Ricci-flow-September-24-2026","familyId":"351","title":"Bounded scalar curvature and smooth extension of four-dimensional Ricci flow","abstract":"We prove that a smooth Ricci flow on a closed real four-manifold extends on the same manifold through every finite time at which its scalar curvature remains uniformly bounded. This resolves the scalar-curvature extension problem in dimension four.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that a smooth Ricci flow on a closed four-manifold extends past any finite time at which scalar curvature remains uniformly bounded. A higher-dimensional counterexample has bounded scalar curvature but unbounded full curvature at its finite maximal time, disproving the unrestricted scalar-curvature extension conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/Bounded-scalar-curvature-and-smooth-extension-of-four-dimensional-Ricci-flow-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Bounded-scalar-curvature-and-smooth-extension-of-four-dimensional-Ricci-flow-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Bounded-scalar-curvature-and-smooth-extension-of-four-dimensional-Ricci-flow-September-24-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Bounded-scalar-curvature-and-smooth-extension-of-four-dimensional-Ricci-flow-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Bounded-scalar-curvature-and-smooth-extension-of-four-dimensional-Ricci-flow-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Bounded-scalar-curvature-and-smooth-extension-of-four-dimensional-Ricci-flow-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Bounded-scalar-curvature-and-smooth-extension-of-four-dimensional-Ricci-flow-September-24-2026/build/figures/overview.tex","preprints/Bounded-scalar-curvature-and-smooth-extension-of-four-dimensional-Ricci-flow-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-static-input.tex","preprints/Bounded-scalar-curvature-and-smooth-extension-of-four-dimensional-Ricci-flow-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-geometry.tex","preprints/Bounded-scalar-curvature-and-smooth-extension-of-four-dimensional-Ricci-flow-September-24-2026/build/figures/buffered-intervals.tex","preprints/Bounded-scalar-curvature-and-smooth-extension-of-four-dimensional-Ricci-flow-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-parabolic.tex","preprints/Bounded-scalar-curvature-and-smooth-extension-of-four-dimensional-Ricci-flow-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-collar.tex","preprints/Bounded-scalar-curvature-and-smooth-extension-of-four-dimensional-Ricci-flow-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-contradiction.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Bounded-scalar-curvature-and-smooth-extension-of-four-dimensional-Ricci-flow-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that a smooth Ricci flow on a closed real four-manifold extends on the same manifold through every finite time at which its scalar curvature remains uniformly bounded. This resolves the scalar-curvature extension problem in dimension four.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":312},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The extension problem and its geometric obstruction","sourceOffset":2265},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The static functional and the flow estimates","sourceOffset":5734},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof structure","sourceOffset":9109},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":10495},{"kind":"section","title":"The static input and its normalization","sourceOffset":11188},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The geometric data and the functional","sourceOffset":11719},{"kind":"section","title":"Small-scale geometry and the outermost scale","sourceOffset":18168},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Heat localization and two-sided persistence of the spatial radius","sourceOffset":21618},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Energy, small bad sets, and volume comparison","sourceOffset":37228},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Slice limits, quotient cones, and quantitative necks","sourceOffset":42304},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removal of punctures and intrinsic singular points","sourceOffset":56170}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Smooth extension","label":"thm:extension","text":"Let \\(M\\) be a closed connected smooth four-manifold, let \\(0<T<\\infty\\), and let \\(g(t)\\), \\(0\\le t<T\\), be a smooth Ricci flow with smooth initial metric. If \\[ \\sup_{M\\times[0,T)}|R(g(t))|<\\infty, \\] then there are \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) and a smooth Ricci flow \\(\\widetilde g(t)\\) on \\(M\\), \\(0\\le t<T+\\varepsilon\\), such that \\(\\widetilde g(t)=g(t)\\) for every \\(t<T\\).","sourceOffset":780,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:scalar-blowup","text":"If a Ricci flow on a closed smooth four-manifold has finite maximal smooth existence time \\(T\\), then \\[ \\limsup_{t\\uparrow T}\\max_{x\\in M}R(g(t))(x)=+\\infty. \\]","sourceOffset":1651,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Spatial curvature radius","label":"geom:radius","text":"Fix a small cap $l_0>0$. At a material point $x\\in M$ set \\[ l(x,t)=\\sup\\{0<r\\leq l_0: \\sup_{B_t(x,r)}|\\Rm|\\leq r^{-2}\\}. \\] The definition uses the whole spatial ball, not just curvature at its center. The function $l$ is positive and continuous before $T$, and $|l(x,t)-l(y,t)|\\leq d_t(x,y)$. Indeed a ball of radius $r-d_t(x,y)$ at $y$ is contained in the radius-$r$ ball at $x$, and its allowed curvature bound is larger. Continuity in time follows by compactness and smooth dependence of the metric, using radii slightly smaller or larger than the supremum.","sourceOffset":18932,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":204403,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":15,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Bounded-scalar-curvature-and-smooth-extension-of-four-dimensional-Ricci-flow-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"351-03","slug":"Path-selection-and-an-elliptic-inequality-on-degenerating-Ricci-flat-trees-September-24-2026","familyId":"351","title":"Path selection and an elliptic inequality on degenerating Ricci-flat trees","abstract":"We prove a sequential elliptic inequality for the renormalized Einstein–Hilbert functional on a fixed finite tree of four-dimensional Ricci-flat spaces. As the joining lengths diverge and the scale-neutral weighted Ricci error M tends to zero, the functional satisfies $E=o(M)$. The root end has decay exponent greater than one, and every joined quotient group is nontrivial. We also prove the multivariable path-selection theorem for asymptotic expansions used to obtain this estimate.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that a smooth Ricci flow on a closed four-manifold extends past any finite time at which scalar curvature remains uniformly bounded. A higher-dimensional counterexample has bounded scalar curvature but unbounded full curvature at its finite maximal time, disproving the unrestricted scalar-curvature extension conjecture.","pdfPath":"preprints/Path-selection-and-an-elliptic-inequality-on-degenerating-Ricci-flat-trees-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Path-selection-and-an-elliptic-inequality-on-degenerating-Ricci-flat-trees-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Path-selection-and-an-elliptic-inequality-on-degenerating-Ricci-flat-trees-September-24-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Path-selection-and-an-elliptic-inequality-on-degenerating-Ricci-flat-trees-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Path-selection-and-an-elliptic-inequality-on-degenerating-Ricci-flat-trees-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Path-selection-and-an-elliptic-inequality-on-degenerating-Ricci-flat-trees-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Path-selection-and-an-elliptic-inequality-on-degenerating-Ricci-flat-trees-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-tree.tex","preprints/Path-selection-and-an-elliptic-inequality-on-degenerating-Ricci-flat-trees-September-24-2026/build/figures/tree-scales.tex","preprints/Path-selection-and-an-elliptic-inequality-on-degenerating-Ricci-flat-trees-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-path.tex","preprints/Path-selection-and-an-elliptic-inequality-on-degenerating-Ricci-flat-trees-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-family.tex","preprints/Path-selection-and-an-elliptic-inequality-on-degenerating-Ricci-flat-trees-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-tails.tex","preprints/Path-selection-and-an-elliptic-inequality-on-degenerating-Ricci-flat-trees-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-length.tex","preprints/Path-selection-and-an-elliptic-inequality-on-degenerating-Ricci-flat-trees-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-observables.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Path-selection-and-an-elliptic-inequality-on-degenerating-Ricci-flat-trees-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a sequential elliptic inequality for the renormalized Einstein--Hilbert functional on a fixed finite tree of four-dimensional Ricci-flat spaces. As the joining lengths diverge and the scale-neutral weighted Ricci error $M$ tends to zero, the functional satisfies $E=o(M)$. The root end has decay exponent greater than one, and every joined quotient group is nontrivial. We also prove the multivariable path-selection theorem for asymptotic expansions used to obtain this estimate.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":581},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why an analytic path enters the proof","sourceOffset":4089},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The elliptic reduction and its quantitative output","sourceOffset":6255},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and conventions","sourceOffset":9828},{"kind":"section","title":"Tree geometry and the renormalized functional","sourceOffset":10881},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The geometric data and the functional","sourceOffset":11172},{"kind":"section","title":"Selection of paths at several exponential ends","sourceOffset":19178},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The class of functions and the selection statement","sourceOffset":19755},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sectors, uniqueness, and clocks","sourceOffset":24793},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Regular systems and finite analytic operations","sourceOffset":44099},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Adjoining a reciprocal fastest length","sourceOffset":53666},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Construction for several lengths","sourceOffset":65758}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Tree inequality","label":"thm:tree-inequality","text":"Fix a finite rooted tree with the end identifications and order-$20$ metric bounds of Section \\ref{ell:setup}. Let $(g_j,L^{(j)},M_j)$ satisfy the compact vertex convergence, the same uniform half-end and root-end bounds, and Equation \\eqref{eq:tree-ricci-error} through order $10$, with every $L_e^{(j)}\\to\\infty$ and $M_j\\to0$. Then, in physical root units, \\begin{equation} E(g_j)=o(M_j). \\end{equation} For an index with $M_j=0$, the assertion means $E(g_j)=0$. The little-oh is taken for this fixed tree; it does not assert a power exponent uniform over different trees.","sourceOffset":16752,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Path selection","label":"thm:path-selection","text":"Let \\(P_1,\\ldots,P_s\\) be an admissible finite family, and let a finite Boolean combination of conditions \\(P_j>0\\), \\(P_j=0\\), and \\(P_j<0\\) hold along a sequence \\[ L_i^{(n)}\\longrightarrow+\\infty,\\qquad z^{(n)}\\longrightarrow z_0. \\] There is a real analytic map \\(u\\mapsto(L(u),z(u))\\), defined for all sufficiently large real \\(u\\), with the same limits and satisfying those conditions on its tail. Each actual coordinate \\(L_i(u)\\) and \\(z_a(u)\\) is eventually monotone or constant. Given any additional finite admissible list, its values on the path can also be required to be eventually monotone or constant. Finite prescribed limits of functions can be imposed at the same time.","sourceOffset":22377,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"path:def-admissible","text":"An admissible family in \\(m\\) lengths consists of real holomorphic functions \\(P(L_1,\\ldots,L_m,z)\\), with the following properties. These properties are required for every recursively extracted coefficient as well as for the initial functions. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item The functions are bounded on a product of regions \\(Q(A,c)\\), locally uniformly in the ordinary parameters. \\item For each length there is an expansion \\begin{equation} P(L,z)\\sim\\sum_{d\\in D_i}e^{-dL_i} \\sum_{k=0}^{k_d}L_i^k P_{dk}(L_{\\widehat i},z). \\end{equation} Here \\(D_i\\subset[0,\\infty)\\) is local","sourceOffset":20069,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"path:def-sector","text":"An admissible angular loss is a positive continuous function \\(\\omega(r)\\), for large \\(r\\), such that \\[ \\omega(r)\\ge(\\log r)^{-2},\\qquad \\sum_{n\\ge n_0}\\sup_{2^n<r<2^{n+2}}\\omega(r)<\\infty. \\] Its sector is the open set \\(S_\\omega=\\{z:|z|>R,\\ |\\arg z|<\\pi/2-\\omega(|z|)\\}\\). Every positive loss satisfying the displayed bounds has a continuous admissible enlargement: at dyadic radii choose a summable majorant of the neighboring shell suprema and interpolate linearly in \\(\\log r\\). Thus enlarging a loss to make its sector open only shrinks that sector. Increasing a loss by a fixed factor or tak","sourceOffset":24839,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":148423,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":4,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Path-selection-and-an-elliptic-inequality-on-degenerating-Ricci-flat-trees-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"352-01","slug":"A-finite-time-singularity-of-Calabi-flow-on-projective-space-September-24-2026","familyId":"352","title":"A finite-time singularity of Calabi flow on projective space","abstract":"We construct a smooth Kähler metric in the Fubini–Study class of $\\mathbb{CP}^{10}$ whose Calabi flow develops unbounded scalar curvature in finite time. This disproves Chen's smooth long-time existence conjecture, even in a class containing a constant-scalar-curvature metric.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves Chen's smooth long-time existence conjecture for Calabi flow by constructing a smooth $U(10)$-invariant Kähler metric on $\\mathbb{CP}^{10}$ whose flow develops a finite-time singularity. The metric lies in the Fubini–Study class, so the failure occurs even in a class containing a constant-scalar-curvature metric.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-finite-time-singularity-of-Calabi-flow-on-projective-space-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-finite-time-singularity-of-Calabi-flow-on-projective-space-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-finite-time-singularity-of-Calabi-flow-on-projective-space-September-24-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-finite-time-singularity-of-Calabi-flow-on-projective-space-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-finite-time-singularity-of-Calabi-flow-on-projective-space-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/A-finite-time-singularity-of-Calabi-flow-on-projective-space-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-finite-time-singularity-of-Calabi-flow-on-projective-space-September-24-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/A-finite-time-singularity-of-Calabi-flow-on-projective-space-September-24-2026/build/sections/profile.tex","preprints/A-finite-time-singularity-of-Calabi-flow-on-projective-space-September-24-2026/build/sections/parabolic.tex","preprints/A-finite-time-singularity-of-Calabi-flow-on-projective-space-September-24-2026/build/sections/flat.tex","preprints/A-finite-time-singularity-of-Calabi-flow-on-projective-space-September-24-2026/build/sections/compact.tex","preprints/A-finite-time-singularity-of-Calabi-flow-on-projective-space-September-24-2026/build/figures/cutoff-support.tex","preprints/A-finite-time-singularity-of-Calabi-flow-on-projective-space-September-24-2026/build/sections/certificate.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-finite-time-singularity-of-Calabi-flow-on-projective-space-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a smooth K\\\"ahler metric in the Fubini--Study class of \\(\\CP^{10}\\) whose Calabi flow develops unbounded scalar curvature in finite time. This disproves Chen's smooth long-time existence conjecture, even in a class containing a constant-scalar-curvature metric.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":320},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and significance","sourceOffset":3110},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Structure of the construction","sourceOffset":5684},{"kind":"section","title":"The geometric reduction and the finite-time obstruction","sourceOffset":10018},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A regular auxiliary sphere and its flat limit","sourceOffset":18248},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The self-similar core","sourceOffset":23539},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Curvature at the concentrating point","sourceOffset":26104},{"kind":"section","title":"A positive shrinking profile","sourceOffset":30514},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reduction and the analytic family at the origin","sourceOffset":32299},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The matching coordinates","sourceOffset":37533},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Construction on the half-line","sourceOffset":43550},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Matching the two solutions","sourceOffset":50940}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a smooth \\(U(10)\\)-invariant K\\\"ahler form \\(\\omega_0\\) on \\(\\CP^{10}\\), in the class \\([\\omega_{\\mathrm{FS}}]\\), whose maximal smooth Calabi flow has existence interval \\([0,T_*)\\) with \\(0<T_*<\\infty\\). There are a point \\(p\\in\\CP^{10}\\) and a constant \\(a>0\\) such that \\[ S(\\omega(t))(p)\\sim a(T_*-t)^{-1/2}\\qquad\\text{as }t\\uparrow T_*. \\]","sourceOffset":1273,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Products with stationary factors","label":"cor:product-singularities","text":"Let \\(\\omega(t)\\), \\(T_*\\), \\(p\\), and \\(a\\) be as in Theorem \\ref{thm:main}, and let \\((Y,\\eta)\\) be a compact connected K\\\"ahler manifold with \\(\\eta\\) of constant scalar curvature. With \\(\\pi_X\\) and \\(\\pi_Y\\) the projections from \\(\\CP^{10}\\times Y\\), the maximal smooth Calabi flow from \\(\\Omega_0=\\pi_X^*\\omega_0+\\pi_Y^*\\eta\\) is \\[ \\Omega(t)=\\pi_X^*\\omega(t)+\\pi_Y^*\\eta, \\qquad 0\\leq t<T_*. \\] For every \\(y\\in Y\\), \\(S(\\Omega(t))(p,y)\\sim a(T_*-t)^{-1/2}\\). In particular, finite-time smooth Calabi-flow singularities occur in every complex dimension \\(n\\geq10\\), on \\(\\CP^{10}\\times\\CP^{n-10}\\), in a class containing a K\\\"ahler--Einstein metric.","sourceOffset":2265,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Weighted local spaces","label":"ana:spaces","text":"Let $M$ be either $\\R^m$ or the fixed sphere $\\Sigma$, let $I$ be a time interval, and let $s(P)>0$ be a scale on $M\\times I$. Fix a sufficiently small number $a_0>0$. In Euclidean coordinates, or normal coordinates on $\\Sigma$, centered at $P=(z_0,t_0)$, write $s_0=s(P)$ and set \\[ \\Psi_P(y,\\theta)=(\\exp_{z_0}(s_0y),t_0+s_0^4\\theta),\\qquad D_P=B_{a_0}\\times \\bigl((-a_0^4,a_0^4)\\cap s_0^{-4}(I-t_0)\\bigr). \\] In the Euclidean case the exponential map means translation. The one-sided endpoints belonging to $I$ are included. For a fixed weight $p$, define \\begin{align} \\|u\\|_{X_{\\alpha,p}(s)} &=\\","sourceOffset":60528,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":166274,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-finite-time-singularity-of-Calabi-flow-on-projective-space-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"353-01","slug":"Smooth-Nonquadratic-Affine-Maximal-Graph-in-Dimension-Ten-October-5-2026","familyId":"353","title":"A Smooth Nonquadratic Entire Affine Maximal Graph in Dimension Ten","abstract":"We construct a smooth nonquadratic entire graph in dimension ten that solves the classical affine maximal equation and has positive-definite Hessian everywhere. This gives a smooth counterexample to the entire-graph affine Bernstein assertion in dimension ten. Hessian positivity is pointwise; no global uniform lower bound or completeness of the Berwald–Blaschke metric is asserted.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every smooth locally uniformly convex affine-maximal graph of dimension three through nine, complete for its induced Euclidean metric, is an elliptic paraboloid. A smooth entire nonquadratic example in dimension ten makes this range sharp. In dimensions three through nine, the paraboloid classification also holds for connected open locally uniformly convex affine-maximal hypersurfaces complete for the affine Berwald–Blaschke metric.","pdfPath":"preprints/Smooth-Nonquadratic-Affine-Maximal-Graph-in-Dimension-Ten-October-5-2026/affine-maximal-dimension-ten.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Smooth-Nonquadratic-Affine-Maximal-Graph-in-Dimension-Ten-October-5-2026/affine-maximal-dimension-ten.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Smooth-Nonquadratic-Affine-Maximal-Graph-in-Dimension-Ten-October-5-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Smooth-Nonquadratic-Affine-Maximal-Graph-in-Dimension-Ten-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Smooth-Nonquadratic-Affine-Maximal-Graph-in-Dimension-Ten-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a smooth nonquadratic entire graph in dimension ten that solves the classical affine maximal equation and has positive-definite Hessian everywhere. This gives a smooth counterexample to the entire-graph affine Bernstein assertion in dimension ten. Hessian positivity is pointwise; no global uniform lower bound or completeness of the Berwald--Blaschke metric is asserted.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":431},{"kind":"section","title":"Reduction to a radial system","sourceOffset":4028},{"kind":"section","title":"A smooth radial solution at the origin","sourceOffset":7383},{"kind":"section","title":"An invariant box and global continuation","sourceOffset":10686}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exists a nonquadratic function $u\\in C^\\infty(\\R^{10})$ with $D^2u$ positive definite at every point such that \\[ \\sum_{i,j=1}^{10}U^{ij}\\partial_{ij} (\\det D^2u)^{-11/12}=0 \\quad\\hbox{on }\\R^{10}, \\qquad U=\\det(D^2u)(D^2u)^{-1}. \\]","sourceOffset":1154,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:reduction","text":"Let $F,P$ be positive smooth functions on an open set $\\Omega\\subset\\R^m$, and suppose $H=D_x^2F$ is positive definite. If \\begin{equation} \\det H=FP^{-\\gamma},\\qquad \\tr(H^{-1}D_x^2P)+kP/F=0, \\end{equation} then \\[ u(x,t)=\\sqrt{F(x)^2+t^2} \\] has positive definite Hessian on $\\Omega\\times\\R$ and satisfies \\eqref{eq:affine} in dimension $n=m+1$.","sourceOffset":4500,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/353.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":17177,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Smooth-Nonquadratic-Affine-Maximal-Graph-in-Dimension-Ten-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Smooth-Nonquadratic-Affine-Maximal-Graph-in-Dimension-Ten-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Smooth-Nonquadratic-Affine-Maximal-Graph-in-Dimension-Ten-October-5-2026/build/source/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"353-02","slug":"The-affine-Bernstein-theorem-in-dimensions-three-through-nine-September-24-2026","familyId":"353","title":"The affine Bernstein theorem in dimensions three through nine","abstract":"We prove the Euclidean-complete affine Bernstein conjecture in dimensions three through nine: a smooth locally uniformly convex affine maximal graph is an elliptic paraboloid whenever its induced Euclidean metric is complete. The same conclusion holds, without an initial graph assumption, for connected smooth open (noncompact and without boundary) affine-complete locally uniformly convex immersed hypersurfaces that are classically affine maximal in these dimensions.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every smooth locally uniformly convex affine-maximal graph of dimension three through nine, complete for its induced Euclidean metric, is an elliptic paraboloid. A smooth entire nonquadratic example in dimension ten makes this range sharp. In dimensions three through nine, the paraboloid classification also holds for connected open locally uniformly convex affine-maximal hypersurfaces complete for the affine Berwald–Blaschke metric.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-affine-Bernstein-theorem-in-dimensions-three-through-nine-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-affine-Bernstein-theorem-in-dimensions-three-through-nine-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-affine-Bernstein-theorem-in-dimensions-three-through-nine-September-24-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/The-affine-Bernstein-theorem-in-dimensions-three-through-nine-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-affine-Bernstein-theorem-in-dimensions-three-through-nine-September-24-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/The-affine-Bernstein-theorem-in-dimensions-three-through-nine-September-24-2026/build/figures/thin-slice.tex","preprints/The-affine-Bernstein-theorem-in-dimensions-three-through-nine-September-24-2026/build/sections/area.tex","preprints/The-affine-Bernstein-theorem-in-dimensions-three-through-nine-September-24-2026/build/sections/tubes.tex","preprints/The-affine-Bernstein-theorem-in-dimensions-three-through-nine-September-24-2026/build/sections/bounds.tex","preprints/The-affine-Bernstein-theorem-in-dimensions-three-through-nine-September-24-2026/build/sections/rigidity.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-affine-Bernstein-theorem-in-dimensions-three-through-nine-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the Euclidean-complete affine Bernstein conjecture in dimensions three through nine: a smooth locally uniformly convex affine maximal graph is an elliptic paraboloid whenever its induced Euclidean metric is complete. The same conclusion holds, without an initial graph assumption, for connected smooth open (noncompact and without boundary) affine-complete locally uniformly convex immersed hypersurfaces that are classically affine maximal in these dimensions.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":518},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The main ideas","sourceOffset":5300},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Notation and organization","sourceOffset":7906},{"kind":"section","title":"Convex limits and centered fibers","sourceOffset":8680},{"kind":"section","title":"Affine area and a cap identity","sourceOffset":29062},{"kind":"section","title":"An integral inequality for convex tubes","sourceOffset":35475},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Support coordinates and invariant measures","sourceOffset":36224},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The stationarity equation in support coordinates","sourceOffset":40907},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weighted integration and coercivity","sourceOffset":45306},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The logarithmic inequality","sourceOffset":49629},{"kind":"section","title":"Excluding models with several directions","sourceOffset":51856},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positive mass away from the coordinate faces","sourceOffset":56749}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(3\\le n\\le9\\). Suppose \\(u\\in C^\\infty(\\Omega)\\) has positive-definite Hessian and satisfies \\eqref{eq:affine-maximal}. If its graph is complete for the induced Euclidean metric \\[ g_{ij}=\\delta_{ij}+u_i u_j, \\] then \\(\\Omega=\\R^n\\) and \\[ u(x)=\\tfrac12 x^{\\mathsf T}Ax+b\\cdot x+c \\] for a positive-definite symmetric matrix \\(A\\), a vector \\(b\\), and a constant \\(c\\). In particular, the graph is an invertible affine image of the standard elliptic paraboloid.","sourceOffset":1325,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Uniform centering at maximal direction number","label":"prop:centering","text":"Suppose $m=n+1-k>0$. There is a constant $C_*\\ge1$, depending on the family $\\mathcal F(D)$, such that \\begin{equation} -K_s\\subset C_*K_s \\qquad(s_i>0) \\end{equation} for every $k$-model and every admissible choice of its coordinates. In particular, zero is an interior point of every such fiber.","sourceOffset":17964,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"","label":"def:model","text":"A model with $k$ directions, or a $k$-model, is a member $C$ of $\\mathcal F(D)$ equipped with affine coordinates \\[ (s,y)\\in\\R^k\\times\\R^m,\\qquad m=n+1-k, \\qquad 1\\le k\\le n+1, \\] such that \\[ \\{(s,0):s_i\\ge0\\}\\subset C \\subset\\{(s,y):s_i\\ge0\\}, \\] and every cap $C\\cap\\{\\sum_i s_i\\le T\\}$, $T\\ge0$, is compact. Its transverse fibers are \\[ K_s=\\{y:(s,y)\\in C\\}. \\] The coordinates are part of the model data.","sourceOffset":14438,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/353.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves the Euclidean-complete affine Bernstein theorem for graph dimensions $3\\le n\\le9$. If a smooth function on a nonempty open convex domain has positive-definite Hessian, satisfies the affine maximal equation, and its graph is complete in the induced Euclidean metric, then the domain is all of $\\mathbb R^n$ and the function is a positive-definite quadratic polynomial plus an affine term. Its graph is therefore an elliptic paraboloid.\n\nThe paper's extension to immersed hypersurfaces without an initial graph assumption is outside this selected statement.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/353.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":80507,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-affine-Bernstein-theorem-in-dimensions-three-through-nine-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"354-01","slug":"The-Isoperimetric-Conjecture-for-the-Cubic-Flat-Three-Torus-September-24-2026","familyId":"354","title":"The Isoperimetric Conjecture for the Cubic Flat Three-Torus","abstract":"We prove the isoperimetric conjecture for the cubic flat three-torus and classify all minimizers, including the equality cases at the transition volumes $4\\pi/81$ and $1/\\pi$. The minimizing regions are balls, circular tubes about shortest closed geodesics, coordinate slabs, and their complements.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Determines the isoperimetric profile of the unit cubic flat three-torus and classifies every finite-perimeter minimizer: balls, circular tubes around shortest closed geodesics, coordinate slabs, and their complements. The transition volumes are $4\\pi/81$ and $1/\\pi$, with exactly the adjacent two types minimizing at each transition.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Isoperimetric-Conjecture-for-the-Cubic-Flat-Three-Torus-September-24-2026/article.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Isoperimetric-Conjecture-for-the-Cubic-Flat-Three-Torus-September-24-2026/article.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Isoperimetric-Conjecture-for-the-Cubic-Flat-Three-Torus-September-24-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/The-Isoperimetric-Conjecture-for-the-Cubic-Flat-Three-Torus-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Isoperimetric-Conjecture-for-the-Cubic-Flat-Three-Torus-September-24-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/The-Isoperimetric-Conjecture-for-the-Cubic-Flat-Three-Torus-September-24-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/The-Isoperimetric-Conjecture-for-the-Cubic-Flat-Three-Torus-September-24-2026/build/figures/vertex-patterns.tex","preprints/The-Isoperimetric-Conjecture-for-the-Cubic-Flat-Three-Torus-September-24-2026/build/sections/sections.tex","preprints/The-Isoperimetric-Conjecture-for-the-Cubic-Flat-Three-Torus-September-24-2026/build/sections/early-exclusions.tex","preprints/The-Isoperimetric-Conjecture-for-the-Cubic-Flat-Three-Torus-September-24-2026/build/sections/nesting.tex","preprints/The-Isoperimetric-Conjecture-for-the-Cubic-Flat-Three-Torus-September-24-2026/build/figures/nesting-sections.tex","preprints/The-Isoperimetric-Conjecture-for-the-Cubic-Flat-Three-Torus-September-24-2026/build/sections/elementary-estimates.tex","preprints/The-Isoperimetric-Conjecture-for-the-Cubic-Flat-Three-Torus-September-24-2026/build/sections/numerics.tex","preprints/The-Isoperimetric-Conjecture-for-the-Cubic-Flat-Three-Torus-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Isoperimetric-Conjecture-for-the-Cubic-Flat-Three-Torus-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the isoperimetric conjecture for the cubic flat three-torus and classify all minimizers, including the equality cases at the transition volumes $4\\pi/81$ and $1/\\pi$. The minimizing regions are balls, circular tubes about shortest closed geodesics, coordinate slabs, and their complements.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":358},{"kind":"section","title":"Classical inputs and the two-volume reduction","sourceOffset":6356},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The standard competitors","sourceOffset":8850},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Upper supports for the profile","sourceOffset":11725},{"kind":"section","title":"Reflection symmetry and the two vertex patterns","sourceOffset":16168},{"kind":"section","title":"Identities for planar sections","sourceOffset":26718},{"kind":"section","title":"The first endpoint exclusions","sourceOffset":32417},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The four-vertex pattern","sourceOffset":32820},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The three-vertex pattern","sourceOffset":35842},{"kind":"section","title":"Slice nesting at the cylinder--slab transition","sourceOffset":40001},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An integrated flux inequality","sourceOffset":41049},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The bottom slice controls both strip orientations","sourceOffset":46667}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"intro:main","text":"For \\(V\\in(0,1)\\), put \\(v=\\min(V,1-V)\\). Among finite-perimeter subsets of \\(\\mathbb R^3/\\mathbb Z^3\\) of volume \\(V\\), the minimum perimeter is \\begin{equation} I_{\\rm unit}(V)= \\min\\left\\{(36\\pi)^{1/3}v^{2/3},\\ 2\\sqrt{\\pi v},\\ 2\\right\\}. \\end{equation} For \\(0<V\\le1/2\\), every minimizing region is, up to an ambient isometry and a null set, one of the following: \\[ \\begin{array}{c|c} \\text{volume range}&\\text{region}\\\\ \\hline 0<V\\le 4\\pi/81 & \\text{a ball of radius }(3V/(4\\pi))^{1/3}\\\\[2pt] 4\\pi/81\\le V\\le1/\\pi &\\text{a solid circular tube of radius }\\sqrt{V/\\pi}\\\\ &\\text{about a shortest closed geodesic}\\\\[2pt] 1/\\pi\\le V\\le1/2 &\\text{a slab of width }V\\text{ between coordinate tori}. \\end{array} \\] Both listed types occur at each common endpoint of adjacent ranges, and there are no further equality cases. For \\(V>1/2\\), the minimizing regions are the complements of those at volume \\(1-V\\). At zero or full volume the conclusion is trivial.","sourceOffset":934,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Classical flat-manifold structure","label":"pre:structure","text":"Let \\(E\\) be an isoperimetric region in a complete orientable flat three-manifold, with closed boundary \\(\\Sigma\\). Either \\(\\Sigma\\) is a union of parallel planar surfaces, or \\(\\Sigma\\) is connected. In the connected case: \\begin{enumerate} \\renewcommand{\\labelenumi}{\\textup{(\\roman{enumi})}} \\item genus zero gives a round sphere; \\item genus one gives a flat quotient of a plane or a circular cylinder; \\item the genus is at most four; \\item in genus two or three, \\(H^2|\\Sigma|\\le 2\\pi\\), where \\(H=h/2\\); in genus four, \\(H=0\\). \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":7459,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/354.md","scopeText":"The formalization determines the isoperimetric profile and all minimizers in the unit cubic flat three-torus. At every volume $0<V<1$, a minimizer exists, and the minimizers, up to null-set changes, are exactly balls, circular tubes about shortest closed geodesics, coordinate slabs, and their complements in the appropriate volume ranges.\n\nThe classification includes all equality cases at the transition volumes $4\\pi/81$ and $1/\\pi$, and every minimizer has the stated candidate-profile perimeter.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/354.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":91191,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Isoperimetric-Conjecture-for-the-Cubic-Flat-Three-Torus-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"355-01","slug":"Tangent-flow-uniqueness-2026-09-24","familyId":"355","title":"Uniqueness of tangent flows at the first singular time of embedded surface mean-curvature flow","abstract":"We prove that, at each singular point of the first singular time of the mean-curvature flow of a smooth compact connected embedded surface without boundary in ℝ3, all fixed-center rescalings converge locally smoothly on compact negative-time intervals to one multiplicity-one homothetic self-shrinker flow. The limit is unique in the original ambient coordinates, including its position and axes. No mean-convexity assumption or prescribed tangent model is required.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves the first-singular-time case of tangent-flow uniqueness for smooth compact connected embedded surfaces without boundary in ℝ3. At every singular point, all fixed-center backward tangent flows agree as area measures at every negative time in the original ambient coordinates, without mean-convexity or a prescribed tangent model.","pdfPath":"preprints/Tangent-flow-uniqueness-2026-09-24/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Tangent-flow-uniqueness-2026-09-24/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Tangent-flow-uniqueness-2026-09-24","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Tangent-flow-uniqueness-2026-09-24/build/paper.tex","preprints/Tangent-flow-uniqueness-2026-09-24/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Tangent-flow-uniqueness-2026-09-24/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Tangent-flow-uniqueness-2026-09-24/build/sections/linear.tex","preprints/Tangent-flow-uniqueness-2026-09-24/build/sections/real-comparison.tex","preprints/Tangent-flow-uniqueness-2026-09-24/build/sections/contours.tex","preprints/Tangent-flow-uniqueness-2026-09-24/build/sections/gauges.tex","preprints/Tangent-flow-uniqueness-2026-09-24/build/sections/finite-linear.tex","preprints/Tangent-flow-uniqueness-2026-09-24/build/sections/arrays.tex","preprints/Tangent-flow-uniqueness-2026-09-24/build/sections/jump.tex","preprints/Tangent-flow-uniqueness-2026-09-24/build/sections/phase.tex","preprints/Tangent-flow-uniqueness-2026-09-24/build/sections/finite-jet.tex","preprints/Tangent-flow-uniqueness-2026-09-24/build/sections/localized.tex","preprints/Tangent-flow-uniqueness-2026-09-24/build/sections/flow.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Tangent-flow-uniqueness-2026-09-24/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that, at each singular point of the first singular time of the mean-curvature flow of a smooth compact connected embedded surface without boundary in $\\R^3$, all fixed-center rescalings converge locally smoothly on compact negative-time intervals to one multiplicity-one homothetic self-shrinker flow. The limit is unique in the original ambient coordinates, including its position and axes. No mean-convexity assumption or prescribed tangent model is required.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":533},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Geometric background and preceding uniqueness results","sourceOffset":4209},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The stationary obstruction","sourceOffset":7332},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite jets, localization, and convergence","sourceOffset":12153},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":13626},{"kind":"section","title":"Geometric input and normalization","sourceOffset":14417},{"kind":"section","title":"A normalized family and its formal Gaussian action","sourceOffset":25035},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Gaussian domain and compact observations","sourceOffset":26933},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Cylindrical strip estimates, traces, and separated rates","sourceOffset":32974},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Infinite conical ends","sourceOffset":56874},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Compact norms and coefficient maps","sourceOffset":67110},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The analytic family preserving end types and its opening jets","sourceOffset":72916}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $M_0\\subset\\R^3$ be a smooth compact connected embedded surface without boundary, and let $(M_t)_{0\\le t<T}$ be its maximal smooth mean curvature flow. At every singular point $(x_0,T)$ at its first singular time there is a smooth properly embedded self-shrinker $S$ such that, as $\\lambda\\to\\infty$, the full family \\eqref{intro:rescalings} converges locally smoothly in space and with multiplicity one to $\\sqrt{-s}\\,S$, uniformly for $s$ in compact subintervals of $(-\\infty,0)$. The convergence is in the original ambient coordinates, including the axes and position of the limit. Every backward integral Brakke tangent in the stated convention therefore equals the area-measure flow of $\\sqrt{-s}\\,S$ at every negative time.","sourceOffset":3077,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Tangent section and ends","label":"geo:tangent","text":"A backward tangent flow of the flow in \\Cref{thm:main} has multiplicity one and is the homothetic motion $\\sqrt{-s}\\,S$, $s<0$, of a smooth properly embedded self-shrinking surface. Its time slices have uniform quadratic local area bounds. The surface $S$ has bounded geometry and finite genus. Outside a compact set it is the disjoint union of finitely many ends, each either smoothly asymptotic to a regular cone under dilations or a cylindrical tail whose translates centered at points receding outward along a ray from the origin converge smoothly on compact sets to the full round cylinder of radius $\\sqrt2$. The corresponding links and cylindrical directions are separated on the unit sphere.","sourceOffset":18512,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":410758,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Tangent-flow-uniqueness-2026-09-24.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"356-01","slug":"Giglis-distributional-curvature-characterization-of-Alexandrov-spaces-September-24-2026","familyId":"356","title":"Gigli’s distributional curvature characterization of Alexandrov spaces","abstract":"For every integer n ≥ 2 and κ ∈ ℝ, we prove that a complete separable metric space is an n-dimensional Alexandrov space of curvature at least κ if and only if, with reference measure $\\mathcal H^n$, it is a full-support $\\mathrm{RCD}((n-1)\\kappa,n)$ space whose distributional sectional curvature is at least κ in Gigli's original global test classes. This resolves Gigli's characterization conjecture in dimensions at least two.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Gigli's conjecture: in every integer dimension n ≥ 2, Alexandrov curvature at least κ is characterized by the full-support $\\mathop{\\mathrm{RCD}}\\nolimits ((n-1)\\kappa,n)$ condition with reference measure $\\mathcal H^n$ and distributional sectional curvature at least κ in the original global test classes. The RCD condition is unreduced.","pdfPath":"preprints/Giglis-distributional-curvature-characterization-of-Alexandrov-spaces-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Giglis-distributional-curvature-characterization-of-Alexandrov-spaces-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Giglis-distributional-curvature-characterization-of-Alexandrov-spaces-September-24-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Giglis-distributional-curvature-characterization-of-Alexandrov-spaces-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Giglis-distributional-curvature-characterization-of-Alexandrov-spaces-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Giglis-distributional-curvature-characterization-of-Alexandrov-spaces-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Giglis-distributional-curvature-characterization-of-Alexandrov-spaces-September-24-2026/build/figures/proof-route.tex","preprints/Giglis-distributional-curvature-characterization-of-Alexandrov-spaces-September-24-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Giglis-distributional-curvature-characterization-of-Alexandrov-spaces-September-24-2026/build/sections/forward.tex","preprints/Giglis-distributional-curvature-characterization-of-Alexandrov-spaces-September-24-2026/build/sections/heat-index.tex","preprints/Giglis-distributional-curvature-characterization-of-Alexandrov-spaces-September-24-2026/build/sections/frames.tex","preprints/Giglis-distributional-curvature-characterization-of-Alexandrov-spaces-September-24-2026/build/sections/comparison.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Giglis-distributional-curvature-characterization-of-Alexandrov-spaces-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every integer \\(n\\ge2\\) and \\(\\kappa\\in\\mathbb R\\), we prove that a complete separable metric space is an \\(n\\)-dimensional Alexandrov space of curvature at least \\(\\kappa\\) if and only if, with reference measure \\(\\mathcal H^n\\), it is a full-support \\(\\RCD((n-1)\\kappa,n)\\) space whose distributional sectional curvature is at least \\(\\kappa\\) in Gigli's original global test classes. This resolves Gigli's characterization conjecture in dimensions at least two.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":550},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":3843},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The new arguments","sourceOffset":7003},{"kind":"section","title":"Calculus and background results","sourceOffset":12855},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Test calculus and localization","sourceOffset":13302},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weak Hessians and Bochner measures","sourceOffset":20578},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Geometry, transport and along-curve calculus","sourceOffset":23663},{"kind":"section","title":"From Alexandrov comparison to the tensor bound","sourceOffset":27707},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and a short-flow expansion","sourceOffset":29933},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The configuration comparison","sourceOffset":36641},{"kind":"subsection","title":"First variation of the optimal interpolation densities","sourceOffset":41763},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The fourth-order transport estimate","sourceOffset":48700}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Gigli's characterization","label":"main:equivalence","text":"Let \\(n\\ge2\\) be an integer, let \\(\\kappa\\in\\R\\), and let \\((M,d)\\) be a complete separable metric space. The following conditions are equivalent. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\Alph*)}] \\item \\((M,d)\\) is an \\(n\\)-dimensional Alexandrov space with curvature bounded below by \\(\\kappa\\). \\item With \\(m=\\mathcal H^n_d\\), the space \\((M,d,m)\\) has full support, is \\(\\RCD((n-1)\\kappa,n)\\), and satisfies \\begin{equation} R(X,Y,Y,X)(f)\\ge \\kappa\\int_M f\\bigl(|X|^2|Y|^2-\\langle X,Y\\rangle^2\\bigr)\\dd m \\end{equation} for every \\(X,Y\\in\\TestV(M)\\) and every nonnegative \\(f\\in\\Test(M)\\). \\end{enumerate} The \\(\\RCD\\) condition is unreduced, and the test classes and curvature sign are exactly those defined above.","sourceOffset":3021,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"fwd:curvature","text":"Let \\((M,d)\\) be a complete \\(n\\)-dimensional Alexandrov space with curvature bounded below by \\(\\kappa\\), where \\(n\\geq2\\), and put \\(m=\\mathcal H^n_d\\). Then \\((M,d,m)\\) is \\(\\RCD((n-1)\\kappa,n)\\), its measure has full support, and \\begin{equation} R(X,Y,Y,X)(f) \\geq \\kappa\\int f\\bigl(|X|^2|Y|^2-\\langle X,Y\\rangle^2\\bigr)\\dd m \\end{equation} for every nonnegative \\(f\\in\\Test(M)\\) and every \\(X,Y\\in\\TestV(M)\\), with the global test classes and curvature convention of Theorem \\ref{main:equivalence}.","sourceOffset":27785,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/356.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":179687,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Giglis-distributional-curvature-characterization-of-Alexandrov-spaces-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"356-02","slug":"Weak-Hessian-bounds-along-every-geodesic-in-RCD-spaces-September-24-2026","familyId":"356","title":"Weak Hessian bounds along every geodesic in RCD spaces","abstract":"On a full-support $\\mathrm{RCD}(K,N)$ space with $1\\lt N\\lt \\infty$, we prove that a bounded globally Lipschitz function whose distributional Hessian is bounded above by a bounded continuous function satisfies the corresponding second-derivative inequality along every minimizing geodesic.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves Gigli's conjecture: in every integer dimension n ≥ 2, Alexandrov curvature at least κ is characterized by the full-support $\\mathop{\\mathrm{RCD}}\\nolimits ((n-1)\\kappa,n)$ condition with reference measure $\\mathcal H^n$ and distributional sectional curvature at least κ in the original global test classes. The RCD condition is unreduced.","pdfPath":"preprints/Weak-Hessian-bounds-along-every-geodesic-in-RCD-spaces-September-24-2026/weak-hessian-geodesics.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Weak-Hessian-bounds-along-every-geodesic-in-RCD-spaces-September-24-2026/weak-hessian-geodesics.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Weak-Hessian-bounds-along-every-geodesic-in-RCD-spaces-September-24-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Weak-Hessian-bounds-along-every-geodesic-in-RCD-spaces-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Weak-Hessian-bounds-along-every-geodesic-in-RCD-spaces-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Weak-Hessian-bounds-along-every-geodesic-in-RCD-spaces-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Weak-Hessian-bounds-along-every-geodesic-in-RCD-spaces-September-24-2026/build/sections/calculus.tex","preprints/Weak-Hessian-bounds-along-every-geodesic-in-RCD-spaces-September-24-2026/build/sections/drift.tex","preprints/Weak-Hessian-bounds-along-every-geodesic-in-RCD-spaces-September-24-2026/build/sections/curvature-change.tex","preprints/Weak-Hessian-bounds-along-every-geodesic-in-RCD-spaces-September-24-2026/build/sections/every-geodesic.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Weak-Hessian-bounds-along-every-geodesic-in-RCD-spaces-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"On a full-support \\(\\RCD(K,N)\\) space with \\(1<N<\\infty\\), we prove that a bounded globally Lipschitz function whose distributional Hessian is bounded above by a bounded continuous function satisfies the corresponding second-derivative inequality along every minimizing geodesic.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":342},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and the analytic obstacle","sourceOffset":3664},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof structure","sourceOffset":6941},{"kind":"section","title":"Test functions, approximation and Bochner measures","sourceOffset":8414},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A compact core and local approximation","sourceOffset":8831},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weak Hessians and admissible Bochner multipliers","sourceOffset":13119},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Metric and heat-flow facts","sourceOffset":15272},{"kind":"section","title":"Bounded changes of measure and the generator domain","sourceOffset":16394},{"kind":"section","title":"From a weak Hessian bound to variable curvature","sourceOffset":20220},{"kind":"section","title":"Localizing the entropy inequality to a prescribed geodesic","sourceOffset":28571}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Weak Hessian bounds along every geodesic","label":"weight:every-geodesic","text":"Let \\((M,d,m)\\) be a full-support \\(\\RCD(K,N)\\) space, where \\(K\\in\\mathbb R\\) and \\(1<N<\\infty\\). Let \\(F\\colon M\\to\\mathbb R\\) be bounded and globally Lipschitz, and let \\(G\\colon M\\to\\mathbb R\\) be bounded and continuous. Suppose that for every compactly supported \\(g\\in\\Test(M)\\) and every nonnegative \\(h\\in\\Lip_c(M)\\), \\begin{equation} H_F(\\nabla g,\\nabla g)(h) \\le\\int_M hG\\Gamma(g)\\dd m. \\end{equation} Then every constant-speed minimizing geodesic \\(\\sigma\\colon[0,1]\\to M\\), of length \\(\\ell\\), satisfies \\begin{equation} (F\\circ\\sigma)”\\le\\ell^2G\\circ\\sigma \\qquad\\text{in distributions on }(0,1). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":2455,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"A distributional Hessian bound changes curvature","label":"weight:variable-cd","text":"Let \\((M,d,m)\\) be a full-support \\(\\RCD(K,N)\\) space, where \\(K\\in\\mathbb R\\) and \\(1<N<\\infty\\). Let \\(F\\colon M\\to\\mathbb R\\) be bounded and globally Lipschitz, and let \\(G\\colon M\\to\\mathbb R\\) be bounded and continuous. Suppose that for every compactly supported \\(g\\in\\Test\\) and every nonnegative \\(h\\in\\Lip_c(M)\\), \\[ H_F(\\nabla g,\\nabla g)(h) \\le\\int hG\\Gamma(g)\\dd m. \\] For every \\(\\lambda>0\\), the measure \\(m_\\lambda=e^{\\lambda F}m\\) satisfies \\(\\CD(K-\\lambda G,\\infty)\\). The metric is unchanged.","sourceOffset":21958,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/356.md","scopeText":"The formalization transfers a weak Hessian upper bound to every prescribed minimizing geodesic in an $\\mathrm{RCD}(K,N)$ space with finite $N>1$. If a bounded globally Lipschitz function $F$ has weak Hessian bounded above by $G$ times the metric, where $G$ is bounded and continuous, then every constant-speed geodesic $\\gamma:[0,1]\\to X$ satisfies $(F\\circ\\gamma)''\\le G(\\gamma)\\,d(\\gamma(0),\\gamma(1))^2$ in the distributional sense. Constant geodesics are included. The space is complete and separable with full support and measure finite on bounded sets; compactness and metric nonbranching are not assumed.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/356.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":32779,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Weak-Hessian-bounds-along-every-geodesic-in-RCD-spaces-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"357-01","slug":"Bi-Lipschitz-Coordinates-at-Regular-Points-of-Noncollapsed-RCD-Spaces-September-25-2026","familyId":"357","title":"Bi-Lipschitz Coordinates at Regular Points of Noncollapsed RCD Spaces","abstract":"We resolve the regular-point bi-Lipschitz conjecture in the noncollapsed setting. For every integer n ≥ 2 and every real K, every regular point of a noncollapsed $\\mathrm{RCD}(K,n)$ space has an open neighborhood bi-Lipschitz homeomorphic to an open subset of ℝn. The bi-Lipschitz constant depends only on n, and the neighborhood uses the restricted ambient distance.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that every regular point of a noncollapsed $\\mathop{\\mathrm{RCD}}\\nolimits (K,n)$ space, for K ∈ ℝ and integer n ≥ 2, has an open neighborhood bi-Lipschitz to an open subset of ℝn. Regularity requires all pointed tangents to be Euclidean, the reference measure is exactly $\\mathcal H^n$, and the chart compares ambient distances with a point-dependent finite constant.","pdfPath":"preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Coordinates-at-Regular-Points-of-Noncollapsed-RCD-Spaces-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Coordinates-at-Regular-Points-of-Noncollapsed-RCD-Spaces-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Coordinates-at-Regular-Points-of-Noncollapsed-RCD-Spaces-September-25-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Coordinates-at-Regular-Points-of-Noncollapsed-RCD-Spaces-September-25-2026/build/source/paper.tex","preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Coordinates-at-Regular-Points-of-Noncollapsed-RCD-Spaces-September-25-2026/build/source/introduction.tex","preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Coordinates-at-Regular-Points-of-Noncollapsed-RCD-Spaces-September-25-2026/build/source/coordinates.tex","preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Coordinates-at-Regular-Points-of-Noncollapsed-RCD-Spaces-September-25-2026/build/source/pinching.tex","preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Coordinates-at-Regular-Points-of-Noncollapsed-RCD-Spaces-September-25-2026/build/source/renormalization.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Bi-Lipschitz-Coordinates-at-Regular-Points-of-Noncollapsed-RCD-Spaces-September-25-2026/build/source/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We resolve the regular-point bi-Lipschitz conjecture in the noncollapsed setting. For every integer \\(n\\ge2\\) and every real \\(K\\), every regular point of a noncollapsed \\(\\RCD(K,n)\\) space has an open neighborhood bi-Lipschitz homeomorphic to an open subset of \\(\\R^n\\). The bi-Lipschitz constant depends only on \\(n\\), and the neighborhood uses the restricted ambient distance.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":425},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":3187},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two main estimates","sourceOffset":6492},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":12351},{"kind":"section","title":"Uniform harmonic coordinates","sourceOffset":13420},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local calculus and rescaling","sourceOffset":13714},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Transformation at every center and scale","sourceOffset":23633},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Open coordinate images","sourceOffset":31523},{"kind":"section","title":"Heat averages and cumulative matrix control","sourceOffset":34510},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Continuous kernels and compactly supported sources","sourceOffset":35209},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The dyadic heat comparison","sourceOffset":44495},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Accumulation without commuting matrices","sourceOffset":53433}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer \\(n\\ge2\\) there is a constant \\(L_n\\ge1\\) with the following property. Let \\(K\\in\\R\\), and let \\((X,d,\\mathcal H_d^n)\\) be a noncollapsed \\(\\RCD(K,n)\\) space. Let \\(p\\in X\\). If every pointed Gromov--Hausdorff tangent of \\(X\\) at \\(p\\) is pointed-isometric to \\((\\R^n,|\\cdot|,0)\\), then there exist an open neighborhood \\(U\\) of \\(p\\), an open set \\(V\\subset\\R^n\\), and a homeomorphism \\(F:U\\to V\\) such that \\[ L_n^{-1}d(x,z)\\le |F(x)-F(z)|\\le L_nd(x,z) \\qquad (x,z\\in U). \\] The distance on the left and right is the original distance \\(d\\) restricted to \\(U\\).","sourceOffset":1216,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"cor:full-measure-atlas","text":"Under the hypotheses of Theorem \\ref{thm:main}, there is an open set \\(\\mathcal U\\subset X\\) containing every \\(n\\)-regular point, with \\(\\mathcal H_d^n(X\\setminus\\mathcal U)=0\\), and a countable atlas of \\(L_n\\)-bi-Lipschitz charts from ambient-open subsets of \\(\\mathcal U\\) onto open subsets of \\(\\R^n\\). Each chart uses the restricted ambient distance, and its transition maps are \\(L_n^2\\)-bi-Lipschitz.","sourceOffset":2104,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":138085,"sourceFileCount":5,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Bi-Lipschitz-Coordinates-at-Regular-Points-of-Noncollapsed-RCD-Spaces-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"358-01","slug":"A-Three-Manifold-Without-Conjugate-Points-and-Without-a-Nonpositively-Curved-Metric-September-24-2026","familyId":"358","title":"A Three-Manifold Without Conjugate Points and Without a Nonpositively Curved Metric","abstract":"We construct a closed connected orientable smooth three-manifold that admits a smooth Riemannian metric without conjugate points but admits no smooth Riemannian metric of nonpositive sectional curvature.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a closed connected orientable smooth three-manifold that admits a metric without conjugate points but no metric of nonpositive sectional curvature. This answers negatively, already in dimension three, whether the first metric-existence property implies the second.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Three-Manifold-Without-Conjugate-Points-and-Without-a-Nonpositively-Curved-Metric-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Three-Manifold-Without-Conjugate-Points-and-Without-a-Nonpositively-Curved-Metric-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Three-Manifold-Without-Conjugate-Points-and-Without-a-Nonpositively-Curved-Metric-September-24-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-Three-Manifold-Without-Conjugate-Points-and-Without-a-Nonpositively-Curved-Metric-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-Three-Manifold-Without-Conjugate-Points-and-Without-a-Nonpositively-Curved-Metric-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-Three-Manifold-Without-Conjugate-Points-and-Without-a-Nonpositively-Curved-Metric-September-24-2026/build/sections/topology.tex","preprints/A-Three-Manifold-Without-Conjugate-Points-and-Without-a-Nonpositively-Curved-Metric-September-24-2026/build/sections/cometric.tex","preprints/A-Three-Manifold-Without-Conjugate-Points-and-Without-a-Nonpositively-Curved-Metric-September-24-2026/build/figures/neck-gluing.tex","preprints/A-Three-Manifold-Without-Conjugate-Points-and-Without-a-Nonpositively-Curved-Metric-September-24-2026/build/sections/variations.tex","preprints/A-Three-Manifold-Without-Conjugate-Points-and-Without-a-Nonpositively-Curved-Metric-September-24-2026/build/sections/turning.tex","preprints/A-Three-Manifold-Without-Conjugate-Points-and-Without-a-Nonpositively-Curved-Metric-September-24-2026/build/sections/global.tex","preprints/A-Three-Manifold-Without-Conjugate-Points-and-Without-a-Nonpositively-Curved-Metric-September-24-2026/build/sections/punctured-torus.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Three-Manifold-Without-Conjugate-Points-and-Without-a-Nonpositively-Curved-Metric-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a closed connected orientable smooth three-manifold that admits a smooth Riemannian metric without conjugate points but admits no smooth Riemannian metric of nonpositive sectional curvature.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":266},{"kind":"section","title":"The manifold and the obstruction to nonpositive curvature","sourceOffset":6223},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two product pieces and one shear","sourceOffset":6893},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The two incompatible orthogonality conditions","sourceOffset":10303},{"kind":"section","title":"A concave cometric and smooth product cores","sourceOffset":16036},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The matrix path and its endpoint coordinates","sourceOffset":16467},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Smooth convex collars","sourceOffset":23116},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Punctured-torus cores and the final gluing","sourceOffset":24820},{"kind":"section","title":"Geodesic passages and the index form","sourceOffset":29406},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Normal index forms and coordinate second variation","sourceOffset":30845},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The possible passages","sourceOffset":35904},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Passages requiring no turning estimate","sourceOffset":40366}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exists a closed connected orientable smooth three-manifold $M$ with a $C^\\infty$ Riemannian metric without conjugate points, such that $M$ admits no $C^\\infty$ Riemannian metric whose sectional curvature is everywhere at most zero.","sourceOffset":1786,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:no-cat0-action","text":"The fundamental group $\\Gamma=\\pi_1(M)$ of the closed three-manifold defined by \\eqref{eq:torus-gluing} admits no proper cocompact isometric action on a nonempty proper $\\operatorname{CAT}(0)$ space.","sourceOffset":10363,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/358.md","scopeText":"The formalized result separates absence of conjugate points from nonpositive sectional curvature. It constructs a closed connected orientable smooth three-manifold with a smooth Riemannian metric having no conjugate points, while the same manifold admits no smooth metric of everywhere nonpositive sectional curvature. The no-conjugate-points assertion ranges over all geodesics and tangent-bundle Jacobi fields, including constant geodesics and unbounded time intervals. The separate no-focal-points and CAT(0) consequences are not included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/358.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":66685,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Three-Manifold-Without-Conjugate-Points-and-Without-a-Nonpositively-Curved-Metric-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"359-01","slug":"A-negatively-pinched-Kahler-threefold-without-bounded-holomorphic-coordinates-September-25-2026","familyId":"359","title":"A negatively pinched Kähler threefold without bounded holomorphic coordinates","abstract":"We construct a contractible domain in complex dimension three with a complete Kähler metric whose real sectional curvatures lie between two finite negative constants. It admits no bounded holomorphic map to ℂ3 with nowhere-vanishing Jacobian and is therefore not biholomorphic to a bounded domain. This gives a negative answer to the negatively pinched Kähler uniformization question.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a contractible domain in ℂ3 with a complete negatively pinched Kähler metric but no bounded holomorphic coordinates, disproving bounded-domain uniformization in this setting. A higher-dimensional example has sectional curvature at most −1 and only constant bounded holomorphic functions; its curvature is not bounded below.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-negatively-pinched-Kahler-threefold-without-bounded-holomorphic-coordinates-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-negatively-pinched-Kahler-threefold-without-bounded-holomorphic-coordinates-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-negatively-pinched-Kahler-threefold-without-bounded-holomorphic-coordinates-September-25-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-negatively-pinched-Kahler-threefold-without-bounded-holomorphic-coordinates-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-negatively-pinched-Kahler-threefold-without-bounded-holomorphic-coordinates-September-25-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/A-negatively-pinched-Kahler-threefold-without-bounded-holomorphic-coordinates-September-25-2026/build/sections/02-reduction.tex","preprints/A-negatively-pinched-Kahler-threefold-without-bounded-holomorphic-coordinates-September-25-2026/build/sections/03-peaks.tex","preprints/A-negatively-pinched-Kahler-threefold-without-bounded-holomorphic-coordinates-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-densities-construction.tex","preprints/A-negatively-pinched-Kahler-threefold-without-bounded-holomorphic-coordinates-September-25-2026/build/sections/04-densities-estimates.tex","preprints/A-negatively-pinched-Kahler-threefold-without-bounded-holomorphic-coordinates-September-25-2026/build/sections/05-logarithmic-tests.tex","preprints/A-negatively-pinched-Kahler-threefold-without-bounded-holomorphic-coordinates-September-25-2026/build/sections/06-base-potential.tex","preprints/A-negatively-pinched-Kahler-threefold-without-bounded-holomorphic-coordinates-September-25-2026/build/sections/07-hartogs-metric.tex","preprints/A-negatively-pinched-Kahler-threefold-without-bounded-holomorphic-coordinates-September-25-2026/build/sections/08-curvature.tex","preprints/A-negatively-pinched-Kahler-threefold-without-bounded-holomorphic-coordinates-September-25-2026/build/sections/09-pinching.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-negatively-pinched-Kahler-threefold-without-bounded-holomorphic-coordinates-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a contractible domain in complex dimension three with a complete K\\\"ahler metric whose real sectional curvatures lie between two finite negative constants. It admits no bounded holomorphic map to $\\mathbb C^3$ with nowhere-vanishing Jacobian and is therefore not biholomorphic to a bounded domain. This gives a negative answer to the negatively pinched K\\\"ahler uniformization question.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":498},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Intrinsic metrics and bounded functions","sourceOffset":3647},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Construction and proof strategy","sourceOffset":5164},{"kind":"section","title":"Two inputs and the bounded-map obstruction","sourceOffset":9966},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Centered charts and the two propositions","sourceOffset":10497},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The Jacobian on the zero section","sourceOffset":14320},{"kind":"section","title":"Separated homogeneous peaks","sourceOffset":17324},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Projective packing and polynomial bounds","sourceOffset":18915},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Patches and a radial profile","sourceOffset":23097},{"kind":"section","title":"Positive representing densities","sourceOffset":27903},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact moments and phase frequencies","sourceOffset":33055},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniform positivity and derivative estimates","sourceOffset":36474}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"main:theorem","text":"There exist a contractible domain $M\\subset\\C^3$, a smooth complete K\\\"ahler metric $g$ on $M$, and finite constants $0<A\\le B$ such that \\[ -B\\le K_g(\\sigma)\\le -A<0 \\] for every real two-plane $\\sigma\\subset T_pM$ and every $p\\in M$. No bounded holomorphic map $F:M\\to\\C^3$ has a nowhere-vanishing Jacobian determinant. In particular, $M$ is not biholomorphic to a bounded domain in $\\C^3$.","sourceOffset":2487,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform representing densities","label":"density:main","text":"There is a constant $K_*>0$, depending only on the fixed profiles $f,b$ and $R_0$, with the following property. For every fixed $D_0>\\max\\{1,2\\sqrt{2R_0}\\}$ there is an integer threshold $Q_0$ such that, for every integer $Q\\ge Q_0$ and every choice of the separated sets above, the recursion \\eqref{density:recursion} has the following properties for all $j\\ge1$: \\begin{enumerate} \\item $W_j$ is smooth and strictly positive, and $W_j\\,d\\sigma$ represents holomorphic evaluation at zero. In particular it is a probability measure. \\item Under global phase rotation, $W_j$ has frequencies only between $-\\ell_j$ and $\\ell_j$, and its mean on every phase orbit is exactly one. \\item At every point of $S$, \\begin{equation} \\begin{gathered} c_0W_{j-1}\\le W_j\\le2W_{j-1},\\\\ |ZW_j|\\le K_*\\sqrt{k_j}\\,W_j, \\qquad |TW_j|\\le K_*k_jW_j \\end{gathered} \\end{equation} for every unit horizontal vector $Z$. \\end{enumerate} The threshold $Q_0$ may also be enlarged to impose any fixed lower threshold on the degrees $k_j$.","sourceOffset":31710,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/359.md","scopeText":"The formalized result constructs a nonempty contractible complex threefold with a complete Kähler metric whose real sectional curvatures lie between two fixed negative constants. Nevertheless, it has no system of bounded holomorphic coordinates and is not biholomorphic to a bounded domain. The metric is obtained from an explicitly controlled potential. This is the negatively pinched construction; the different companion with only one-sided curvature control is separate.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/359.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":106665,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-negatively-pinched-Kahler-threefold-without-bounded-holomorphic-coordinates-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"359-02","slug":"One-sided-negative-sectional-curvature-and-the-holomorphic-Liouville-property-September-25-2026","familyId":"359","title":"One-sided negative sectional curvature and the holomorphic Liouville property","abstract":"We construct, in some sufficiently large fixed finite complex dimension m, a domain in ℂm diffeomorphic to $\\mathbb R^{2m}$ that admits a complete Kähler metric with real sectional curvature at most −1 and has only constant bounded holomorphic functions. Its sectional curvatures are unbounded below. The construction gives a negative answer to the one-sided bounded-holomorphic-function question.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs a contractible domain in ℂ3 with a complete negatively pinched Kähler metric but no bounded holomorphic coordinates, disproving bounded-domain uniformization in this setting. A higher-dimensional example has sectional curvature at most −1 and only constant bounded holomorphic functions; its curvature is not bounded below.","pdfPath":"preprints/One-sided-negative-sectional-curvature-and-the-holomorphic-Liouville-property-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/One-sided-negative-sectional-curvature-and-the-holomorphic-Liouville-property-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/One-sided-negative-sectional-curvature-and-the-holomorphic-Liouville-property-September-25-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/One-sided-negative-sectional-curvature-and-the-holomorphic-Liouville-property-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/One-sided-negative-sectional-curvature-and-the-holomorphic-Liouville-property-September-25-2026/build/01-introduction.tex","preprints/One-sided-negative-sectional-curvature-and-the-holomorphic-Liouville-property-September-25-2026/build/02-tube.tex","preprints/One-sided-negative-sectional-curvature-and-the-holomorphic-Liouville-property-September-25-2026/build/figures/tube.tex","preprints/One-sided-negative-sectional-curvature-and-the-holomorphic-Liouville-property-September-25-2026/build/03-analytic.tex","preprints/One-sided-negative-sectional-curvature-and-the-holomorphic-Liouville-property-September-25-2026/build/04-fiber.tex","preprints/One-sided-negative-sectional-curvature-and-the-holomorphic-Liouville-property-September-25-2026/build/05-quiet.tex","preprints/One-sided-negative-sectional-curvature-and-the-holomorphic-Liouville-property-September-25-2026/build/06-radial.tex","preprints/One-sided-negative-sectional-curvature-and-the-holomorphic-Liouville-property-September-25-2026/build/07-centers.tex","preprints/One-sided-negative-sectional-curvature-and-the-holomorphic-Liouville-property-September-25-2026/build/08-assembly.tex","preprints/One-sided-negative-sectional-curvature-and-the-holomorphic-Liouville-property-September-25-2026/build/figures/stage-clock.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/One-sided-negative-sectional-curvature-and-the-holomorphic-Liouville-property-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct, in some sufficiently large fixed finite complex dimension $m$, a domain in $\\mathbb C^m$ diffeomorphic to $\\mathbb R^{2m}$ that admits a complete K\\\"ahler metric with real sectional curvature at most $-1$ and has only constant bounded holomorphic functions. Its sectional curvatures are unbounded below. The construction gives a negative answer to the one-sided bounded-holomorphic-function question.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":528},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bounded functions, bounded coordinates, and invariant metrics","sourceOffset":2692},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and its main obstacles","sourceOffset":6369},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions and uniformity","sourceOffset":11585},{"kind":"section","title":"The tube transfer","sourceOffset":13103},{"kind":"section","title":"Polynomial tests for bounded holomorphic functions","sourceOffset":24151},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Polynomial grids and their affine lifts","sourceOffset":25861},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Choosing phases uniformly for a fixed Taylor order","sourceOffset":38761},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From disk estimates to the holomorphic Liouville property","sourceOffset":44736},{"kind":"section","title":"The fiber Legendre transform and its curvature","sourceOffset":55683},{"kind":"section","title":"A spatially varying quiet fiber profile","sourceOffset":69489},{"kind":"section","title":"Radial spatial geometry and aligned profiles","sourceOffset":96053}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For some finite integer $m\\ge2$ there is a domain $\\mathcal T\\subset\\mathbb C^m$, diffeomorphic to $\\mathbb R^{2m}$, with a complete K\\\"ahler metric $g$ such that \\[ \\operatorname{Sec}_g\\le-1, \\qquad H^\\infty(\\mathcal T)=\\mathbb C. \\] The sectional curvatures of $g$ are unbounded below.","sourceOffset":1524,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"tube:negative-plane","text":"Under the hypotheses of Lemma \\ref{tube:transfer}, suppose that $g_\\ell$ has a real two-plane of strictly negative sectional curvature at some point. Then \\[ \\inf\\operatorname{Sec}_{g_{\\mathcal T}}=-\\infty. \\]","sourceOffset":23204,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/359.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":192629,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/One-sided-negative-sectional-curvature-and-the-holomorphic-Liouville-property-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"360-01","slug":"Global-Support-and-Convex-Injectivity-Domains-under-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026","familyId":"360","title":"Global Support and Convex Injectivity Domains under Weak MTW","abstract":"We prove that weak Ma–Trudinger–Wang curvature on a smooth, connected, compact Riemannian manifold of dimension at least two without boundary implies convexity of every tangent injectivity domain, resolving Villani's conjecture in this setting. Conjugate cut points are allowed. More generally, every ordinary subgradient of a squared-distance cost potential is a minimizing velocity with a global supporting mountain. No density hypothesis is used.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"On every closed connected Riemannian manifold of dimension at least two satisfying weak Ma–Trudinger–Wang curvature, all tangent injectivity domains are convex, resolving Villani’s conjecture in this setting. For squared-distance transport between measurable probability densities bounded above and away from zero, the optimal map and its inverse are Hölder continuous.","pdfPath":"preprints/Global-Support-and-Convex-Injectivity-Domains-under-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Global-Support-and-Convex-Injectivity-Domains-under-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Global-Support-and-Convex-Injectivity-Domains-under-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Global-Support-and-Convex-Injectivity-Domains-under-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Global-Support-and-Convex-Injectivity-Domains-under-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Global-Support-and-Convex-Injectivity-Domains-under-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026/build/sections/preliminaries.tex","preprints/Global-Support-and-Convex-Injectivity-Domains-under-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026/build/sections/conjugacy.tex","preprints/Global-Support-and-Convex-Injectivity-Domains-under-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Global-Support-and-Convex-Injectivity-Domains-under-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026/build/appendices/degree.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Global-Support-and-Convex-Injectivity-Domains-under-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that weak Ma--Trudinger--Wang curvature on a smooth, connected, compact Riemannian manifold of dimension at least two without boundary implies convexity of every tangent injectivity domain, resolving Villani's conjecture in this setting. Conjugate cut points are allowed. More generally, every ordinary subgradient of a squared-distance cost potential is a minimizing velocity with a global supporting mountain. No density hypothesis is used.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":514},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weak MTW and convexity","sourceOffset":992},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Global support and intermediate geometry","sourceOffset":4009},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Supporting geometry and earlier methods","sourceOffset":6235},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The continuation argument","sourceOffset":8468},{"kind":"section","title":"Minimizing velocities and the branch Hessian","sourceOffset":10839},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Smooth geodesic branches","sourceOffset":16362},{"kind":"section","title":"Excluding conjugacy in minimizing convex hulls","sourceOffset":21507},{"kind":"section","title":"Global supports and intermediate transport","sourceOffset":30045},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Supports, divided action, and subgradients","sourceOffset":31931},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The subgradient graph and its projections","sourceOffset":38837},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local injectivity and continuation","sourceOffset":42672}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Convexity of injectivity domains","label":"thm:main","text":"If $(M,g)$ satisfies \\eqref{eq:weak-mtw}, then $I(x)$ is convex for every $x\\in M$. More precisely, for every $v_0,v_1\\in I(x)$ and $\\theta\\in[0,1]$, \\[ (1-\\theta)v_0+\\theta v_1\\in I(x). \\]","sourceOffset":2340,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Convex injectivity domains","label":"geo:convexity","text":"For every $x$, both $G_x$ and $I(x)$ are convex.","sourceOffset":53324,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/360.md","scopeText":"On a compact connected smooth Riemannian manifold of dimension at least two, the formalized result proves that weak MTW curvature implies convexity of every open tangent injectivity domain. Prior convexity or nonfocality is not assumed. Additional formalized results give global support at ordinary subgradients, compact convex lifted gap sections with the stated diameter bound, and scale and local-injectivity estimates for finite target families. The latter finite-family result does not cover the paper's arbitrary-potential local-injectivity assertion.\n\nThe formalized result gives uniform bi-Hölder optimal transport for squared-distance cost on each fixed compact connected smooth Riemannian manifold of dimension at least two satisfying weak MTW. For probability densities between fixed posit","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/360.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":71263,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Global-Support-and-Convex-Injectivity-Domains-under-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"360-02","slug":"Uniform-Bi-Holder-Transport-from-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026","familyId":"360","title":"Uniform Bi-Holder Transport from Weak MTW","abstract":"We prove that the weak Ma–Trudinger–Wang condition on a fixed smooth connected compact boundaryless Riemannian manifold of dimension at least two implies a common Hölder estimate for optimal transport maps and their inverses over the entire class of probability densities with fixed positive upper and lower bounds. The maps have homeomorphic representatives, conjugate cut points are allowed, and no density regularity is assumed.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"On every closed connected Riemannian manifold of dimension at least two satisfying weak Ma–Trudinger–Wang curvature, all tangent injectivity domains are convex, resolving Villani’s conjecture in this setting. For squared-distance transport between measurable probability densities bounded above and away from zero, the optimal map and its inverse are Hölder continuous.","pdfPath":"preprints/Uniform-Bi-Holder-Transport-from-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-Bi-Holder-Transport-from-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-Bi-Holder-Transport-from-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/Uniform-Bi-Holder-Transport-from-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Uniform-Bi-Holder-Transport-from-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Uniform-Bi-Holder-Transport-from-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Uniform-Bi-Holder-Transport-from-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026/build/sections/spectral.tex","preprints/Uniform-Bi-Holder-Transport-from-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026/build/sections/scales.tex","preprints/Uniform-Bi-Holder-Transport-from-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026/build/sections/jets.tex","preprints/Uniform-Bi-Holder-Transport-from-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Uniform-Bi-Holder-Transport-from-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the weak Ma--Trudinger--Wang condition on a fixed smooth connected compact boundaryless Riemannian manifold of dimension at least two implies a common H\\\"older estimate for optimal transport maps and their inverses over the entire class of probability densities with fixed positive upper and lower bounds. The maps have homeomorphic representatives, conjugate cut points are allowed, and no density regularity is assumed.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":498},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and geometric input","sourceOffset":3066},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":6511},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof structure","sourceOffset":7618},{"kind":"section","title":"The density-free foundation","sourceOffset":10534},{"kind":"section","title":"Jacobi fields and uniform comparison of exponential Jacobians","sourceOffset":16983},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The divided-action identities","sourceOffset":18634},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Radial shortening at a conjugate endpoint","sourceOffset":22464},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A uniform gain from an extra minimizing extension","sourceOffset":30991},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From transverse MTW to comparison along an affine segment","sourceOffset":34380},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Jacobian comparison for a barycentric configuration","sourceOffset":44409},{"kind":"section","title":"Sections, scales, and a quantitative separation","sourceOffset":45896}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform bi-H\\\"older transport","label":"thm:main","text":"Fix $(M,g)$ as above and $0<\\lambda\\leq\\Lambda<\\infty$ for which the following density class is nonempty. There are $\\alpha\\in(0,1]$ and $C<\\infty$, depending only on $(M,g),\\lambda,\\Lambda$, such that the following holds for every pair of measurable densities satisfying \\[ \\int_M\\rho_i\\,d\\vol=1,\\qquad \\lambda\\leq\\rho_i\\leq\\Lambda\\quad\\vol\\text{-almost everywhere}, \\qquad i=0,1. \\] The almost-everywhere unique optimal map from $\\rho_0\\vol$ to $\\rho_1\\vol$ for the cost $c$ has a homeomorphic representative $\\widetilde T:M\\to M$, and \\[ d(\\widetilde T(x),\\widetilde T(x'))\\leq C d(x,x')^\\alpha, \\qquad d(\\widetilde T^{-1}(y),\\widetilde T^{-1}(y')) \\leq C d(y,y')^\\alpha \\] for all $x,x',y,y'\\in M$.","sourceOffset":1909,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Convex injectivity domains","label":"geo:convexity","text":"Both $G_x$ and $I(x)$ are convex for every $x\\in M$.","sourceOffset":14890,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/360.md","scopeText":"On a compact connected smooth Riemannian manifold of dimension at least two, the formalized result proves that weak MTW curvature implies convexity of every open tangent injectivity domain. Prior convexity or nonfocality is not assumed. Additional formalized results give global support at ordinary subgradients, compact convex lifted gap sections with the stated diameter bound, and scale and local-injectivity estimates for finite target families. The latter finite-family result does not cover the paper's arbitrary-potential local-injectivity assertion.\n\nThe formalized result gives uniform bi-Hölder optimal transport for squared-distance cost on each fixed compact connected smooth Riemannian manifold of dimension at least two satisfying weak MTW. For probability densities between fixed posit","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/360.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":125881,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Uniform-Bi-Holder-Transport-from-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"361-01","slug":"A-counterexample-to-integer-degree-harmonic-dimension-comparison-September-25-2026","familyId":"361","title":"A counterexample to integer-degree harmonic dimension comparison","abstract":"For some even n ≥ 8 and integer k ≥ 2, we construct a complete smooth metric on ℝn with nonnegative Ricci curvature whose space of real harmonic functions of pointwise polynomial growth at most k has dimension larger than the Euclidean harmonic-polynomial dimension. The metric is Euclidean near the origin, has asymptotic volume ratio strictly between zero and one, and has nonunique tangent cones at infinity. This answers the integer-degree form of Yau's dimension comparison question in the negative.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves Yau's proposed Euclidean dimension bound for harmonic functions of integer growth on manifolds with nonnegative Ricci curvature. For every sufficiently large integer k, a complete smooth metric on ℝ3 has at least $(k+2)^2$ independent harmonic functions of growth at most k, exceeding the Euclidean count $(k+1)^2$. The metric may depend on k.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-counterexample-to-integer-degree-harmonic-dimension-comparison-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-counterexample-to-integer-degree-harmonic-dimension-comparison-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-counterexample-to-integer-degree-harmonic-dimension-comparison-September-25-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-counterexample-to-integer-degree-harmonic-dimension-comparison-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-integer-degree-harmonic-dimension-comparison-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-integer-degree-harmonic-dimension-comparison-September-25-2026/build/sections/links.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-integer-degree-harmonic-dimension-comparison-September-25-2026/build/sections/counting.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-integer-degree-harmonic-dimension-comparison-September-25-2026/build/sections/algebra.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-integer-degree-harmonic-dimension-comparison-September-25-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-integer-degree-harmonic-dimension-comparison-September-25-2026/build/figures/period.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-integer-degree-harmonic-dimension-comparison-September-25-2026/build/sections/transmission.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-integer-degree-harmonic-dimension-comparison-September-25-2026/build/sections/conclusion.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-integer-degree-harmonic-dimension-comparison-September-25-2026/build/sections/finite-selection.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-integer-degree-harmonic-dimension-comparison-September-25-2026/build/sections/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-counterexample-to-integer-degree-harmonic-dimension-comparison-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For some even $n\\ge8$ and integer $k\\ge2$, we construct a complete smooth metric on $\\mathbb R^n$ with nonnegative Ricci curvature whose space of real harmonic functions of pointwise polynomial growth at most $k$ has dimension larger than the Euclidean harmonic-polynomial dimension. The metric is Euclidean near the origin, has asymptotic volume ratio strictly between zero and one, and has nonunique tangent cones at infinity. This answers the integer-degree form of Yau's dimension comparison question in the negative.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":568},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why a moving link can change the count","sourceOffset":4427},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From angular control to entire harmonic functions","sourceOffset":6901},{"kind":"section","title":"Volume-normalized Berger links","sourceOffset":11044},{"kind":"section","title":"A strict spectral surplus","sourceOffset":17356},{"kind":"section","title":"Control of the angular modes","sourceOffset":31596},{"kind":"section","title":"A complete metric with nonnegative Ricci curvature","sourceOffset":52638},{"kind":"section","title":"Exact transmission on the regular harmonic subspaces","sourceOffset":66179},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The ideal pulse and its geometric realization","sourceOffset":67186},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The center-regular value equation","sourceOffset":69941},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The response of one pulse","sourceOffset":77370},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Removing the full diagonal leg","sourceOffset":85495}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There are an even integer $n\\geq8$, an integer $k\\geq2$, and a complete smooth metric $g$ on $\\mathbb R^n$ such that $\\operatorname{Ric}_g\\geq0$ and \\[ h_k(\\mathbb R^n,g)>h_k(\\mathbb R^n,g_{\\mathrm E}). \\] The metric is Euclidean near the origin, and its asymptotic volume ratio satisfies \\[ 0<\\lim_{R\\to\\infty} \\frac{\\operatorname{vol}_g B_g(0,R)}{\\omega_nR^n}<1, \\] where $\\omega_n$ is the volume of the Euclidean unit ball.","sourceOffset":1579,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Simultaneous control","label":"prop:control","text":"Fix positive numbers \\(\\kappa_l\\), \\(2\\leq l\\leq L\\). As \\(J\\) ranges over all orthogonal complex structures on \\(\\mathbb R^{2s}\\), the tuples \\begin{equation} X_J=\\bigl(\\kappa_l(D_J^2|_{V_l})_{\\mathrm{tf}}\\bigr)_{l=2}^L \\end{equation} generate, under real linear combinations and brackets, the full Lie algebra \\[ \\bigoplus_{l=2}^L\\mathfrak{sl}(V_l). \\]","sourceOffset":32433,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/361.md","scopeText":"The formalized result disproves the proposed Euclidean upper comparison for dimensions of harmonic functions with integer polynomial growth. It constructs one complete smooth metric with nonnegative Ricci curvature on an even-dimensional Euclidean space, Euclidean near the origin and with asymptotic volume ratio strictly between zero and one, admitting more independent harmonic functions of the prescribed growth degree than the Euclidean count. The construction uses dimension $16$ and degree $50000$. Separate tangent-cone and cone-spectrum conclusions are not included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/361.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":113149,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-counterexample-to-integer-degree-harmonic-dimension-comparison-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"361-02","slug":"A-Three-Dimensional-Counterexample-to-Integer-Degree-Harmonic-Dimension-Comparison-September-26-2026","familyId":"361","title":"A Three-Dimensional Counterexample to Integer-Degree Harmonic Dimension Comparison","abstract":"For every $4/9\\lt v\\lt 1$ and $1\\lt c\\lt 9v/4$, all sufficiently large integers k admit a complete smooth metric on ℝ3 with nonnegative Ricci curvature, asymptotic volume ratio v, and at least $c(k+1)^2$ linearly independent real harmonic functions of pointwise growth at most k. This answers Yau's integer-degree dimension comparison question negatively in dimension three, with a fixed-factor excess over the Euclidean count. For each $1\\lt c\\lt 9/4$, these metrics can be chosen arbitrarily close to the Euclidean metric in global bi-Lipschitz distance. The metric may depend on k.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Disproves Yau's proposed Euclidean dimension bound for harmonic functions of integer growth on manifolds with nonnegative Ricci curvature. For every sufficiently large integer k, a complete smooth metric on ℝ3 has at least $(k+2)^2$ independent harmonic functions of growth at most k, exceeding the Euclidean count $(k+1)^2$. The metric may depend on k.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Three-Dimensional-Counterexample-to-Integer-Degree-Harmonic-Dimension-Comparison-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Three-Dimensional-Counterexample-to-Integer-Degree-Harmonic-Dimension-Comparison-September-26-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Three-Dimensional-Counterexample-to-Integer-Degree-Harmonic-Dimension-Comparison-September-26-2026","subjectId":"7","subject":"Differential geometry","subjectZh":"微分幾何","texPaths":["preprints/A-Three-Dimensional-Counterexample-to-Integer-Degree-Harmonic-Dimension-Comparison-September-26-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-Three-Dimensional-Counterexample-to-Integer-Degree-Harmonic-Dimension-Comparison-September-26-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-Three-Dimensional-Counterexample-to-Integer-Degree-Harmonic-Dimension-Comparison-September-26-2026/build/sections/crossings.tex","preprints/A-Three-Dimensional-Counterexample-to-Integer-Degree-Harmonic-Dimension-Comparison-September-26-2026/build/sections/angular-program.tex","preprints/A-Three-Dimensional-Counterexample-to-Integer-Degree-Harmonic-Dimension-Comparison-September-26-2026/build/figures/crossing-cycle.tex","preprints/A-Three-Dimensional-Counterexample-to-Integer-Degree-Harmonic-Dimension-Comparison-September-26-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/A-Three-Dimensional-Counterexample-to-Integer-Degree-Harmonic-Dimension-Comparison-September-26-2026/build/sections/transfer.tex","preprints/A-Three-Dimensional-Counterexample-to-Integer-Degree-Harmonic-Dimension-Comparison-September-26-2026/build/sections/matching.tex","preprints/A-Three-Dimensional-Counterexample-to-Integer-Degree-Harmonic-Dimension-Comparison-September-26-2026/build/sections/growth.tex","preprints/A-Three-Dimensional-Counterexample-to-Integer-Degree-Harmonic-Dimension-Comparison-September-26-2026/build/sections/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Three-Dimensional-Counterexample-to-Integer-Degree-Harmonic-Dimension-Comparison-September-26-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"For every \\(4/9<v<1\\) and \\(1<c<9v/4\\), all sufficiently large integers \\(k\\) admit a complete smooth metric on \\(\\mathbb R^3\\) with nonnegative Ricci curvature, asymptotic volume ratio \\(v\\), and at least \\(c(k+1)^2\\) linearly independent real harmonic functions of pointwise growth at most \\(k\\). This answers Yau's integer-degree dimension comparison question negatively in dimension three, with a fixed-factor excess over the Euclidean count. For each \\(1<c<9/4\\), these metrics can be chosen arbitrarily close to the Euclidean metric in global bi-Lipschitz distance. The metric may depend on \\(k\\).","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":649},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Averaging rates by crossing eigenlines","sourceOffset":5866},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact harmonic continuation along the program","sourceOffset":9148},{"kind":"section","title":"Isolating and prescribing eigenvalue crossings","sourceOffset":13235},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A fixed angular measure and the splitting formula","sourceOffset":16478},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Avoiding unwanted multiplicities","sourceOffset":23609},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Keeping one double while separating the rest","sourceOffset":32212},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Pole-regular modes of a rotational sphere","sourceOffset":38725},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An isolated double at every adjacent band position","sourceOffset":41754},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite cycle with mean frequency below the degree","sourceOffset":54325},{"kind":"section","title":"Radial realization and its geometric limits","sourceOffset":65856},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Comparison with the Euclidean metric","sourceOffset":77524}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"n:main","text":"For every \\(v\\in(4/9,1)\\) and \\(c\\in(1,9v/4)\\), there is an integer \\(k_0(v,c)\\) such that for every integer \\(k\\geq k_0(v,c)\\) there is a complete smooth metric \\(g_k\\) on \\(\\mathbb R^3\\), Euclidean near the origin, with \\(\\operatorname{Ric}_{g_k}\\geq0\\), \\(\\operatorname{AVR}(g_k)=v\\), and \\[ h_k(\\mathbb R^3,g_k)\\geq c(k+1)^2>(k+1)^2 =h_k(\\mathbb R^3,g_{\\mathrm E}). \\] The Ricci curvature is positive outside a compact set. The metric has uncountably many pairwise nonisometric pointed tangent cones at infinity. There are points tending to infinity and planes containing the radial direction at those points whose sectional curvature is negative. The metric is chosen after \\(k\\) and may depend on it.","sourceOffset":1886,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":"n:near-euclidean","text":"For every \\(\\epsilon>0\\) and \\(1<c<9/4\\), all sufficiently large integers \\(k\\) admit metrics with the conclusions of Theorem \\ref{n:main} and \\[ (1+\\epsilon)^{-2}g_{\\mathrm E}\\leq g_k \\leq(1+\\epsilon)^2g_{\\mathrm E} \\qquad\\text{on }\\mathbb R^3. \\] Their asymptotic volume ratio can be prescribed arbitrarily close to \\(1\\). In particular, the identity map is globally \\((1+\\epsilon)\\)-bi-Lipschitz.","sourceOffset":2755,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/361.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":139688,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Three-Dimensional-Counterexample-to-Integer-Degree-Harmonic-Dimension-Comparison-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"362-01","slug":"Global-classical-solutions-of-the-three-dimensional-relativistic-Vlasov-Maxwell-system-September-23-2026","familyId":"362","title":"Global classical solutions of the three-dimensional relativistic Vlasov–Maxwell system","abstract":"We prove global existence and uniqueness for arbitrary smooth admissible initial data in the three-dimensional, one-species relativistic Vlasov–Maxwell system. The particle density is initially compactly supported, and the electromagnetic fields have finite energy and bounded derivatives of all orders. The solution remains smooth on every finite time interval. This resolves the large-data global classical regularity problem for this model, without size or symmetry restrictions on the data.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves large-data global existence and uniqueness for the three-dimensional, one-species relativistic Vlasov–Maxwell system. Smooth admissible initial data may be arbitrary provided the particle density is compactly supported and the electromagnetic fields have finite energy and bounded derivatives of every order; the solution remains smooth on every finite time interval.","pdfPath":"preprints/Global-classical-solutions-of-the-three-dimensional-relativistic-Vlasov-Maxwell-system-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Global-classical-solutions-of-the-three-dimensional-relativistic-Vlasov-Maxwell-system-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Global-classical-solutions-of-the-three-dimensional-relativistic-Vlasov-Maxwell-system-September-23-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Global-classical-solutions-of-the-three-dimensional-relativistic-Vlasov-Maxwell-system-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Global-classical-solutions-of-the-three-dimensional-relativistic-Vlasov-Maxwell-system-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Global-classical-solutions-of-the-three-dimensional-relativistic-Vlasov-Maxwell-system-September-23-2026/build/sections/setup.tex","preprints/Global-classical-solutions-of-the-three-dimensional-relativistic-Vlasov-Maxwell-system-September-23-2026/build/figures/figure-dependencies.tex","preprints/Global-classical-solutions-of-the-three-dimensional-relativistic-Vlasov-Maxwell-system-September-23-2026/build/figures/figure-dependencies-caption.tex","preprints/Global-classical-solutions-of-the-three-dimensional-relativistic-Vlasov-Maxwell-system-September-23-2026/build/sections/retarded.tex","preprints/Global-classical-solutions-of-the-three-dimensional-relativistic-Vlasov-Maxwell-system-September-23-2026/build/figures/figure-retarded.tex","preprints/Global-classical-solutions-of-the-three-dimensional-relativistic-Vlasov-Maxwell-system-September-23-2026/build/sections/occupation.tex","preprints/Global-classical-solutions-of-the-three-dimensional-relativistic-Vlasov-Maxwell-system-September-23-2026/build/sections/direct.tex","preprints/Global-classical-solutions-of-the-three-dimensional-relativistic-Vlasov-Maxwell-system-September-23-2026/build/sections/cancellation.tex","preprints/Global-classical-solutions-of-the-three-dimensional-relativistic-Vlasov-Maxwell-system-September-23-2026/build/sections/direction.tex","preprints/Global-classical-solutions-of-the-three-dimensional-relativistic-Vlasov-Maxwell-system-September-23-2026/build/sections/selection.tex","preprints/Global-classical-solutions-of-the-three-dimensional-relativistic-Vlasov-Maxwell-system-September-23-2026/build/sections/closure.tex","preprints/Global-classical-solutions-of-the-three-dimensional-relativistic-Vlasov-Maxwell-system-September-23-2026/build/sections/continuation.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Global-classical-solutions-of-the-three-dimensional-relativistic-Vlasov-Maxwell-system-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove global existence and uniqueness for arbitrary smooth admissible initial data in the three-dimensional, one-species relativistic Vlasov--Maxwell system. The particle density is initially compactly supported, and the electromagnetic fields have finite energy and bounded derivatives of all orders. The solution remains smooth on every finite time interval. This resolves the large-data global classical regularity problem for this model, without size or symmetry restrictions on the data.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":558},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":2876},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":8109},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":11640},{"kind":"section","title":"The signed momentum estimate and its consequence","sourceOffset":12350},{"kind":"section","title":"Energy and the retarded force","sourceOffset":20305},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The retarded source labels","sourceOffset":23786},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exact electric and magnetic kernels","sourceOffset":25954},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Receiver geometry and pointwise force bounds","sourceOffset":31688},{"kind":"section","title":"Occupation of retarded bins","sourceOffset":40009},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The momentum and angular ranges","sourceOffset":41521},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Two changes of variables","sourceOffset":45114}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:global","text":"Every smooth admissible datum \\eqref{eq:data} generates a unique global classical solution of \\eqref{eq:rvm}. For every finite $T$, this solution satisfies \\[ f\\in C^\\infty([0,T]\\times\\R^3_x\\times\\R^3_v),\\qquad E,B\\in C^\\infty([0,T]\\times\\R^3_x;\\R^3), \\] and $f$ has compact phase-space support on $[0,T]$. The two divergence constraints hold for all time.","sourceOffset":2028,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Signed momentum increments","label":"prop:increment","text":"For each smooth admissible datum and finite $T_0$, there are constants $M,A\\ge1$ and $P_0$ with the following property. On every compact classical solution interval $[0,t_*]$ as above, for every dyadic $P\\ge P_0$ satisfying \\eqref{eq:P-range}, every supported receiver, every $w=2^k$, $k\\ge0$, and every interval $[t_1,t_2]\\subset[0,t_*]$ on which $w/8\\le q_X(t)\\le8w$ throughout, one has \\begin{equation} |V_X(t_2)-V_X(t_1)| \\le MP\\sqrt{t_2-t_1}+A(t_2-t_1)S(w). \\end{equation} The constants are independent of $t_*$ and $P$.","sourceOffset":13701,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/362.md","scopeText":"The formalized result gives global existence and uniqueness for the three-dimensional one-species relativistic Vlasov–Maxwell system. The initial particle density is nonnegative, smooth, and compactly supported; the initial fields have bounded derivatives of all orders, finite energy, and satisfy both Gauss constraints. The solution is smooth on every finite time interval, has compact particle phase support there, and is unique among classical solutions. No smallness, symmetry, or neutrality assumption is imposed.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/362.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":152463,"sourceFileCount":14,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Global-classical-solutions-of-the-three-dimensional-relativistic-Vlasov-Maxwell-system-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"363-01","slug":"Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026","familyId":"363","title":"Nonuniqueness with local conservation for the hard-sphere Boltzmann equation","abstract":"We prove nonuniqueness for the three-dimensional periodic hard-sphere Boltzmann equation among global entropy solutions satisfying exact local conservation of mass, momentum, and kinetic energy. We construct one nonnegative initial density with bounded velocity support and finite mass, energy, and absolute entropy that gives rise to two distinct such solutions. Both are strongly continuous in L1, and their collision gain and loss terms are integrable with every polynomial velocity weight on every bounded time interval.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs two distinct global entropy solutions of the three-dimensional periodic hard-sphere Boltzmann equation from the same nonnegative initial density, with bounded velocity support and finite mass, energy and absolute entropy. Both are strongly continuous in L1 and satisfy exact local conservation of mass, momentum and kinetic energy.","pdfPath":"preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026/build/paper/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026/build/paper/02-framework.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026/build/paper/03-kernel.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026/build/paper/04-construction.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026/build/paper/figures/jet-geometry.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026/build/paper/05-cold.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026/build/paper/06-early.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026/build/paper/07-global.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026/build/paper/08-compactness.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026/build/paper/09-dilation.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026/build/paper/figures/scales.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026/build/paper/10-nonconcentration.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026/build/paper/11-branching.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026/build/paper/12-separation.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove nonuniqueness for the three-dimensional periodic hard-sphere Boltzmann equation among global entropy solutions satisfying exact local conservation of mass, momentum, and kinetic energy. We construct one nonnegative initial density with bounded velocity support and finite mass, energy, and absolute entropy that gives rise to two distinct such solutions. Both are strongly continuous in \\(L^1\\), and their collision gain and loss terms are integrable with every polynomial velocity weight on every bounded time interval.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":592},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The equation and the solution class","sourceOffset":4726},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The construction and its new global estimate","sourceOffset":8656},{"kind":"section","title":"Coordinates and collision estimates","sourceOffset":12692},{"kind":"section","title":"Collision kernels and a velocity majorant","sourceOffset":16177},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exact Carleman formula","sourceOffset":18146},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A geometric slice estimate","sourceOffset":20853},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sheets and normalized kernel measures","sourceOffset":24885},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Gaussian parameter laws and compressed axial maps","sourceOffset":31048},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The hot--hot gain","sourceOffset":35193},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A small-mass gain estimate","sourceOffset":37666},{"kind":"section","title":"The datum and the finite colored evolutions","sourceOffset":42515}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist \\(R<\\infty\\) and a nonnegative datum \\(F_0\\) with \\[ \\supp_vF_0\\subset B_R(0),\\qquad \\int_\\Omega F_0(1+|v|^2+|\\log F_0|)\\dd x\\dd v<\\infty, \\] for which \\eqref{eq:collision} has two global entropy solutions with local conservation in the sense of Definition \\ref{def:admissible}, differing on a set of positive \\((t,x,v)\\)-measure. Both solutions belong to \\(C([0,\\infty);L^1(\\Omega))\\). Moreover, for every finite \\(T\\) and every \\(k\\ge0\\), they satisfy \\begin{equation} \\int_0^T\\!\\!\\int_\\Omega \\langle v\\rangle^k\\bigl[Q^+(F,F)+Q^-(F,F)\\bigr] \\dd x\\dd v\\dd t<\\infty, \\qquad \\langle v\\rangle=(1+|v|^2)^{1/2}. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":7905,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Finite evolution and its identities","label":"con:finite-evolution","text":"For fixed \\(N,b,T,\\lambda\\), the colored equations have a unique nonnegative local mild solution with bounded total \\(H\\)-norm. It continues while that norm remains bounded, with the prescribed transfers at retirement times, and depends continuously in this norm on its initial data on every common bounded interval of existence. The norm of each component is continuous between transfers. On every such interval, total mass, momentum, and energy are conserved. The exact local collision-invariant balances hold, with all polynomial velocity fluxes integrable. For \\(\\rho=M\\) or \\(\\rho=\\mu\\), one has at every time of existence \\begin{equation} \\mathcal H_\\rho(F(t))+ \\int_0^t\\mathcal D^b(F(s))\\dd s \\le\\mathcal H_\\rho(F(0)), \\end{equation} where \\(\\mathcal D^b\\) is the entropy dissipation with the same cutoff.","sourceOffset":57975,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Entropy solution with local conservation","label":"def:admissible","text":"Let \\(F_0\\ge0\\) have finite mass, energy, and absolute entropy. A global entropy solution with local conservation is a nonnegative function \\(F\\) satisfying the following conditions. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item \\(F\\in C_w([0,\\infty);L^1(\\Omega))\\), \\(F(0)=F_0\\), with continuity against every bounded measurable test. For every finite \\(T\\), \\[ \\sup_{0\\le t\\le T}\\int_\\Omega F(t)(1+|v|^2+|\\log F(t)|)\\dd x\\dd v<\\infty. \\] \\item The two functions \\(Q^\\pm(F,F)/(1+G)\\) belong to \\(L^1([0,T]\\times\\Omega)\\) for every finite \\(T\\). For every \\(\\beta\\in C^1([0,\\infty))\\) with \\(\\bet","sourceOffset":5996,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Uniform sheet class","label":"ker:sheet-class","text":"Fix $L\\geq1$. An elementary sheet in $\\mathcal S_L$ is a product probability measure \\begin{equation} c(\\dd p)=\\mathcal G_d(\\dd\\bar p)\\rho(\\dd p_3),\\qquad 0\\leq d\\leq\\tfrac18,\\qquad \\int_{\\R}e^{8a^2}\\rho(\\dd a)\\leq L. \\end{equation} We also allow probability mixtures of elementary sheets. An upper bound by a sheet with amplitude $A_0$ means domination as measures by $A_0$ times such a mixture.","sourceOffset":24937,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/363.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":254727,"sourceFileCount":16,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Nonuniqueness-with-local-conservation-for-the-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"363-02","slug":"Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026","familyId":"363","title":"Nonuniqueness for the periodic hard-sphere Boltzmann equation","abstract":"We prove nonuniqueness for the periodic hard-sphere Boltzmann equation by constructing two distinct global renormalized solutions with the same nonnegative initial density on $\\mathbb T^3\\times\\mathbb R^3$. This density has bounded velocity support and finite mass, energy, and absolute entropy. Both solutions conserve local mass and total momentum and satisfy the global energy and entropy-dissipation inequalities. On a common initial interval, they are strongly continuous in L1, and their collision gains and losses are integrable.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs two distinct global entropy solutions of the three-dimensional periodic hard-sphere Boltzmann equation from the same nonnegative initial density, with bounded velocity support and finite mass, energy and absolute entropy. Both are strongly continuous in L1 and satisfy exact local conservation of mass, momentum and kinetic energy.","pdfPath":"preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026/build/figures/proof-route.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-framework.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-kernel.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-jets.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-cap.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-admissibility.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-dilation.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-nonconcentration.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026/build/sections/09-branching.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026/build/sections/10-separation.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove nonuniqueness for the periodic hard-sphere Boltzmann equation by constructing two distinct global renormalized solutions with the same nonnegative initial density on \\(\\T^3\\times\\R^3\\). This density has bounded velocity support and finite mass, energy, and absolute entropy. Both solutions conserve local mass and total momentum and satisfy the global energy and entropy-dissipation inequalities. On a common initial interval, they are strongly continuous in \\(L^1\\), and their collision gains and losses are integrable.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":603},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniqueness and related constructions","sourceOffset":4045},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One datum and two approximation families","sourceOffset":6442},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From a cap hit to separation","sourceOffset":8833},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization","sourceOffset":13969},{"kind":"section","title":"Equation, admissibility, and notation","sourceOffset":14699},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A velocity weight and colored components","sourceOffset":18319},{"kind":"section","title":"Collision kernels and a velocity majorant","sourceOffset":19909},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The exact Carleman formula","sourceOffset":20895},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A geometric slice estimate","sourceOffset":23467},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sheets and normalized kernel measures","sourceOffset":27499},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Gaussian parameter laws and compressed axial maps","sourceOffset":37082}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a nonnegative density \\(F_0\\) on \\(\\T^3\\times\\R^3\\), with bounded velocity support and \\[ \\int_{\\T^3\\times\\R^3} F_0(1+|v|^2+|\\log F_0|)\\dd x\\dd v<\\infty, \\] for which the hard-sphere Boltzmann equation has two distinct global admissible solutions, in the sense of Definition \\ref{def:admissible}, with initial datum \\(F_0\\). On some common initial interval both solutions are strongly continuous in \\(L^1\\), and their collision gains and losses are integrable in space-time-velocity.","sourceOffset":3190,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Uniform cold control","label":"prop:cold-control","text":"There is a constant \\(L\\), depending on the fixed parameters, such that after imposing the additional condition \\begin{equation} Ld_0\\overline s^9\\le1, \\end{equation} the following statements hold at every time of existence of the finite approximation in \\(0\\le t\\le\\overline T\\). \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item For every retained label \\(i=(j,\\sigma,n)\\), \\begin{equation} 0\\le c_i\\le f_i+\\varepsilon_j m_i,\\qquad \\varepsilon_j=Ld_{s_j}/s_j . \\end{equation} \\item At each spatial point call the unique label with \\(x\\in\\mathcal O_i(t)\\), if one exists and is retained, the main label. Let \\(c_{\\mathrm{main}}\\) be that color, or zero, and set \\(e=c-c_{\\mathrm{main}}\\). For every \\(A\\ge0\\), \\begin{equation} 0\\le e(t,x,v)\\le C_A t^A e^{-4|v|^2}. \\end{equation} The upper bound remains valid after restricting the escaped sum to any subset of its labels. \\item If the main label has scale \\(s\\), its velocity measure is bounded by a fixed multiple of \\((t+s)^{-1}\\) times a sum of admissible probability sheets from Lemma \\ref{lem:sheet-bound}. Its free-core contribution has the exact parameterization \\eqref{eq:core-jacobian} below. The remainder has the smaller velocity-mass coefficient \\(Cd_s/s^2\\). In particular, for every fixed \\(k\\ge0\\), \\begin{equation} \\int_{\\R^3}\\langle v\\rangle^k c_{\\mathrm{main}}\\,\\dd v \\le\\frac{C_k}{t+s},\\qquad \\int_{|v|>R}\\langle v\\rangle^k c_{\\mathrm{main}}\\,\\dd v \\le\\frac{C_k e^{-c_kR^2}}{t+s}\\quad(R\\ge3). \\end{equation} \\item For the mixed-c","sourceOffset":58637,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Entropy-class solution","label":"def:admissible","text":"For a nonnegative datum \\(F_0\\) satisfying \\[ \\int_\\Omega F_0(1+|v|^2+|\\log F_0|)\\dd x\\dd v<\\infty, \\] an admissible global solution is a nonnegative \\(F\\) with the following properties. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item \\(F\\in C_w([0,\\infty);L^1(\\Omega))\\), with \\(F(0)=F_0\\), where weak continuity means continuity against every bounded measurable test. On each bounded time interval, \\(\\int_\\Omega F(1+|v|^2+|\\log F|)\\) is uniformly bounded. \\item \\(Q^\\pm(F,F)/(1+G)\\) belong to \\(L^1([0,T]\\times\\Omega)\\) for every finite \\(T\\). For every \\(\\beta\\in C^1([0,\\infty))\\) with \\(\\beta","sourceOffset":16519,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Uniform sheet class","label":"ker:sheet-class","text":"Fix $L\\geq1$. An elementary sheet in $\\mathcal S_L$ is a product probability measure \\begin{equation} c(\\dd p)=\\mathcal G_d(\\dd\\bar p)\\rho(\\dd p_3),\\qquad 0\\leq d\\leq\\tfrac18,\\qquad \\int_{\\R}e^{8a^2}\\rho(\\dd a)\\leq L. \\end{equation} We also allow probability mixtures of elementary sheets. An upper bound by a sheet with amplitude $A_0$ means domination as measures by $A_0$ times such a mixture.","sourceOffset":27551,"truncated":false}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/363.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves nonuniqueness for the periodic hard-sphere Boltzmann equation by constructing two distinct global renormalized solutions on $\\mathbb T^3\\times\\mathbb R^3$ with the same nonnegative initial density. The data have bounded velocity support and finite mass, energy, and absolute entropy. Both solutions conserve local mass and total momentum and satisfy the global energy and entropy-dissipation inequalities.\n\nOn a common initial interval they are strongly continuous in $L^1$, with integrable collision gains and losses. These are the conditions of the selected periodic result; later local-conservation refinements are outside it.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/363.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":195180,"sourceFileCount":13,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Nonuniqueness-for-the-periodic-hard-sphere-Boltzmann-equation-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"364-01","slug":"The-Boltzmann-Grad-limit-for-stable-radial-potentials-on-regular-kinetic-intervals-September-23-2026","familyId":"364","title":"The Boltzmann–Grad limit for stable radial potentials on regular kinetic intervals","abstract":"We derive the nonlinear Boltzmann equation from a grand-canonical Newtonian gas with initial pair exclusion. We treat stable, finite-range radial potentials that are C2 except for an allowed repulsive singularity at the origin, and C1 initial probability densities with spatially summable Gaussian bounds on the density and its spatial gradient. All fixed-order rescaled factorial marginals converge in L1, uniformly throughout every finite interval on which the classical kinetic solution has a uniform Gaussian bound. The result allows attractive wells and dynamically formed clusters, without restrictions on the differential scattering cross-section.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Derives the nonlinear Boltzmann equation from three-dimensional grand-canonical Newtonian gases throughout every regular kinetic interval with uniform Gaussian decay. Stable finite-range radial potentials may have attractive wells and a singular repulsive core; initial pair exclusion and spatially summable Gaussian density and gradient bounds are assumed. A companion gives finite-dimensional hard-sphere Gaussian fluctuations, centered at the exact microscopic expectation and governed by the linear fluctuating Boltzmann equation.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Boltzmann-Grad-limit-for-stable-radial-potentials-on-regular-kinetic-intervals-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Boltzmann-Grad-limit-for-stable-radial-potentials-on-regular-kinetic-intervals-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Boltzmann-Grad-limit-for-stable-radial-potentials-on-regular-kinetic-intervals-September-23-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/The-Boltzmann-Grad-limit-for-stable-radial-potentials-on-regular-kinetic-intervals-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-Boltzmann-Grad-limit-for-stable-radial-potentials-on-regular-kinetic-intervals-September-23-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/The-Boltzmann-Grad-limit-for-stable-radial-potentials-on-regular-kinetic-intervals-September-23-2026/build/figures/exposure.tex","preprints/The-Boltzmann-Grad-limit-for-stable-radial-potentials-on-regular-kinetic-intervals-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-preliminaries.tex","preprints/The-Boltzmann-Grad-limit-for-stable-radial-potentials-on-regular-kinetic-intervals-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-cell-expansion.tex","preprints/The-Boltzmann-Grad-limit-for-stable-radial-potentials-on-regular-kinetic-intervals-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-centering.tex","preprints/The-Boltzmann-Grad-limit-for-stable-radial-potentials-on-regular-kinetic-intervals-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-history-estimates.tex","preprints/The-Boltzmann-Grad-limit-for-stable-radial-potentials-on-regular-kinetic-intervals-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-exposure.tex","preprints/The-Boltzmann-Grad-limit-for-stable-radial-potentials-on-regular-kinetic-intervals-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-closure.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Boltzmann-Grad-limit-for-stable-radial-potentials-on-regular-kinetic-intervals-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We derive the nonlinear Boltzmann equation from a grand-canonical Newtonian gas with initial pair exclusion. We treat stable, finite-range radial potentials that are $C^2$ except for an allowed repulsive singularity at the origin, and $C^1$ initial probability densities with spatially summable Gaussian bounds on the density and its spatial gradient. All fixed-order rescaled factorial marginals converge in $L^1$, uniformly throughout every finite interval on which the classical kinetic solution has a uniform Gaussian bound. The result allows attractive wells and dynamically formed clusters, without restrictions on the differential scattering cross-section.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":745},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and scope","sourceOffset":5004},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Structure of the proof","sourceOffset":8129},{"kind":"section","title":"Microscopic flow, scattering, and kinetic envelopes","sourceOffset":14245},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Potential assumptions and finite-particle flow","sourceOffset":14737},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The ensemble and external isolation","sourceOffset":17699},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Scattering flux and the collision operator","sourceOffset":23705},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exceptional deflections and rotational parameters","sourceOffset":28728},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A fixed spatial Gaussian envelope on the kinetic interval","sourceOffset":33594},{"kind":"section","title":"An exact finite expansion on one cell","sourceOffset":40544},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Contact components and kernels","sourceOffset":41164},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Boolean inversion and the generation identity","sourceOffset":46809}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"maintheorem","name":"Regular-lifespan kinetic limit","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $\\Phi$ satisfy Assumption \\ref{pre:potential}, and let $\\beta>0$. Let $f_0\\ge0$ be a $C^1$ probability density on $\\R^3\\times\\R^3$ such that \\begin{equation} \\|f_0\\|_{\\mathrm{Bol},2\\beta} +\\|\\nabla_xf_0\\|_{\\mathrm{Bol},2\\beta}<\\infty. \\end{equation} Fix $0<T<\\infty$ for which the equation \\begin{equation} (\\partial_t+v\\cdot\\nabla_x)f=Q_\\Phi(f,f), \\qquad f|_{t=0}=f_0, \\end{equation} has a nonnegative classical probability-density solution satisfying \\begin{equation} \\sup_{0\\le t\\le T}\\sup_{x,v} e^{2\\beta|v|^2}f(t,x,v)<\\infty. \\end{equation} Start the exact Newtonian process from the grand-canonical law \\eqref{pre:model}. For its scaled factorial densities $F_s^\\varepsilon$, defined in \\eqref{pre:factorial}, every fixed integer $s\\ge1$ satisfies \\begin{equation} \\lim_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}\\; \\sup_{0\\le t\\le T} \\bigl\\|F_s^\\varepsilon(t)-f(t)^{\\otimes s}\\bigr\\|_ {L^1((\\R^3\\times\\R^3)^s)}=0. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":2828,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Actual witnesses and layer remainders","label":"close:tails","text":"With the preceding schedule, for sufficiently small $\\varepsilon$ and every $1\\leq j\\leq L$, \\begin{equation} \\mathbb P\\bigl(\\mathcal B_j(D_j)\\bigr)\\leq e^{-8D_j}, \\qquad R_j\\leq e^{-8K_j}. \\end{equation} The estimates use the original Newtonian process, without conditioning it to stay in a good set.","sourceOffset":241163,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"assumption","name":"Potential class","label":"pre:potential","text":"Write $\\Phi(q)=\\phi(|q|)$, with $\\Phi(q)=0$ for $|q|\\geq1$. Assume either $\\Phi\\in C^2(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb R)$, or $\\Phi\\in C^2(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\})$ with $\\Phi(q)\\to+\\infty$ as $q\\to0$. In the latter case set $\\Phi(0)=+\\infty$. Assume thermodynamic stability: for some $B\\geq0$, \\begin{equation} \\sum_{\\substack{i,j\\in R\\\\i<j}}\\Phi(q_i-q_j)\\geq-B|R| \\end{equation} for every finite configuration of distinct points. The potential is fixed independently of $\\varepsilon$.","sourceOffset":14798,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Exact initial ensemble","label":"eq:pre-partition","text":"Let $\\mu=\\varepsilon^{-2}$ and \\[ {\\cal D}_R^\\varepsilon =\\{Z_R:|x_i-x_j|>\\varepsilon\\text{ for all }i\\ne j\\in R\\}. \\] For the prescribed $C^1$ nonnegative probability density $f_0$, set \\begin{align} {\\cal Z}_\\varepsilon &=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty\\frac{\\mu^n}{n!} \\int f_0^{\\otimes n}(Z_n) \\mathbf1_{{\\cal D}_{[n]}^\\varepsilon}(Z_n)\\,dZ_n, \\\\ W_{0,n}(Z_n) &={\\cal Z}_\\varepsilon^{-1}\\mu^n f_0^{\\otimes n}(Z_n) \\mathbf1_{{\\cal D}_{[n]}^\\varepsilon}(Z_n). \\end{align} Here $[n]=\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$ and empty products equal one. The probability law on ordered coordinates is \\begin{equation} \\mathbb P(N=n,Z_n\\in ","sourceOffset":17749,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":252619,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":24,"definitionCount":8,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Boltzmann-Grad-limit-for-stable-radial-potentials-on-regular-kinetic-intervals-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"364-02","slug":"Hard-sphere-fluctuations-on-the-regular-Boltzmann-lifespan-September-23-2026","familyId":"364","title":"Hard-sphere fluctuations on the regular Boltzmann lifespan","abstract":"We prove a finite-dimensional central limit theorem away from equilibrium for a deterministic grand-canonical hard-sphere gas in three dimensions. The initial probability density is smooth, with spatially summable Gaussian velocity bounds on the density and its spatial gradient. The limit holds on every finite interval on which the classical Boltzmann solution has uniform Gaussian velocity decay. The empirical measure is centered by its exact microscopic expectation. Its fluctuations converge to the Gaussian solution of the linear fluctuating Boltzmann equation.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Derives the nonlinear Boltzmann equation from three-dimensional grand-canonical Newtonian gases throughout every regular kinetic interval with uniform Gaussian decay. Stable finite-range radial potentials may have attractive wells and a singular repulsive core; initial pair exclusion and spatially summable Gaussian density and gradient bounds are assumed. A companion gives finite-dimensional hard-sphere Gaussian fluctuations, centered at the exact microscopic expectation and governed by the linear fluctuating Boltzmann equation.","pdfPath":"preprints/Hard-sphere-fluctuations-on-the-regular-Boltzmann-lifespan-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Hard-sphere-fluctuations-on-the-regular-Boltzmann-lifespan-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Hard-sphere-fluctuations-on-the-regular-Boltzmann-lifespan-September-23-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Hard-sphere-fluctuations-on-the-regular-Boltzmann-lifespan-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Hard-sphere-fluctuations-on-the-regular-Boltzmann-lifespan-September-23-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Hard-sphere-fluctuations-on-the-regular-Boltzmann-lifespan-September-23-2026/build/sections/02-initial-target.tex","preprints/Hard-sphere-fluctuations-on-the-regular-Boltzmann-lifespan-September-23-2026/build/sections/03-dhm-inputs.tex","preprints/Hard-sphere-fluctuations-on-the-regular-Boltzmann-lifespan-September-23-2026/build/sections/04-cluster-expansion.tex","preprints/Hard-sphere-fluctuations-on-the-regular-Boltzmann-lifespan-September-23-2026/build/sections/06-sharp-asymptotics.tex","preprints/Hard-sphere-fluctuations-on-the-regular-Boltzmann-lifespan-September-23-2026/build/figures/chronological-mergers.tex","preprints/Hard-sphere-fluctuations-on-the-regular-Boltzmann-lifespan-September-23-2026/build/sections/07-gaussian-limit.tex","preprints/Hard-sphere-fluctuations-on-the-regular-Boltzmann-lifespan-September-23-2026/build/sections/05-cutoff.tex","preprints/Hard-sphere-fluctuations-on-the-regular-Boltzmann-lifespan-September-23-2026/build/sections/08-transfer.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Hard-sphere-fluctuations-on-the-regular-Boltzmann-lifespan-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a finite-dimensional central limit theorem away from equilibrium for a deterministic grand-canonical hard-sphere gas in three dimensions. The initial probability density is smooth, with spatially summable Gaussian velocity bounds on the density and its spatial gradient. The limit holds on every finite interval on which the classical Boltzmann solution has uniform Gaussian velocity decay. The empirical measure is centered by its exact microscopic expectation. Its fluctuations converge to the Gaussian solution of the linear fluctuating Boltzmann equation.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction and statement of the theorem","sourceOffset":642},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Kinetic equation and normalization","sourceOffset":3478},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Microscopic law and fluctuation field","sourceOffset":4922},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof structure and new estimates","sourceOffset":8820},{"kind":"section","title":"The initial ensemble and the Gaussian target","sourceOffset":12013},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Initial factorial measures and connected graphs","sourceOffset":12341},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Velocity moments and the backward propagator","sourceOffset":24462},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Construction and uniqueness of the Gaussian field","sourceOffset":30009},{"kind":"section","title":"Layered histories and the analytic inputs","sourceOffset":35081},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Parameters and the restarted dynamics","sourceOffset":35453},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Histories, roots, and normalization","sourceOffset":38545},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The imported analytic package","sourceOffset":44215}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Finite-dimensional fluctuation limit","label":"thm:main","text":"Under \\Cref{ass:kinetic}, the centered Gaussian generalized field \\(\\zeta\\) with weak equation \\begin{equation} \\dd\\zeta_t(\\phi)=\\zeta_t(A_t\\phi)\\,\\dd t+\\dd W_t(\\phi) \\end{equation} is well defined by its backward mild form on a scale of tests with small Gaussian growth. Its initial field is independent of \\(W\\), and \\begin{align} \\E[\\zeta_0(\\phi)\\zeta_0(\\psi)] &=\\int_{\\R^6}f_0\\phi\\psi\\,\\dd z,\\\\ \\E[W_t(\\phi)W_s(\\psi)] &=\\int_0^{t\\wedge s}C_r(\\phi,\\psi)\\,\\dd r. \\end{align} The Gaussian noise \\(W\\) has independent increments. For every integer \\(m\\ge1\\), fixed times \\(t_1,\\ldots,t_m\\in[0,T]\\), and real tests \\(\\phi_1,\\ldots,\\phi_m\\in C_c^\\infty(\\R^6)\\), \\begin{equation} \\bigl(\\zeta_{t_1}^\\eps(\\phi_1),\\ldots, \\zeta_{t_m}^\\eps(\\phi_m)\\bigr) \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\bigl(\\zeta_{t_1}(\\phi_1),\\ldots, \\zeta_{t_m}(\\phi_m)\\bigr) \\qquad(\\eps\\downarrow0). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":6823,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Initial ensemble","label":"prop:initial","text":"For all sufficiently small $\\eps$, the following assertions hold. \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)}] \\item The factorial densities satisfy \\begin{equation} 0\\le F_{h,0}^{\\eps}\\le\\prod_{j=1}^h f_0(z_j), \\qquad (1-C\\eps)f_0\\le g_0\\le f_0. \\end{equation} Consequently $\\|g_0-f_0\\|_{\\Bol,2\\beta}=O(\\eps)$ and, for every fixed integer $p\\ge1$, \\begin{equation} \\E(N)_p\\le\\mu^p, \\qquad \\sup_{0<\\eps\\le\\eps_0}\\E(N/\\mu)^p<\\infty. \\end{equation} \\item With $\\upsilon=1/81$, for every $h\\ge2$, \\begin{equation} |c_{h,0}^{\\eps}(Z_h)| \\le (Ch)^{Ch}\\prod_{j=1}^h f_0(z_j) \\sum_{\\mathcal T\\text{ tree on }[h]} \\prod_{\\{i,j\\}\\in\\mathcal T} \\bigl(\\ind_{|x_i-x_j|\\le\\eps}+\\eps^\\upsilon\\bigr). \\end{equation} Here $C$ and the upper bound on $\\eps$ are independent of $h$. For fixed $h\\ge2$ and $D>0$ there is $C_{D,h}<\\infty$ such that \\begin{equation} |c_{h,0}^{\\eps}(Z_h)| \\le C_{D,h}\\prod_{j=1}^h f_0(z_j) \\sum_{\\mathcal T\\text{ tree on }[h]} \\prod_{\\{i,j\\}\\in\\mathcal T} \\bigl(\\ind_{|x_i-x_j|\\le C_{D,h}\\eps}+\\eps^D\\bigr). \\end{equation} Moreover, \\begin{equation} \\|c_{h,0}^{\\eps}\\|_{L^1}\\le C_h\\eps^{3(h-1)} \\qquad(h\\ge2). \\end{equation} \\item For every finite list of bounded real tests, the exact-centered initial fluctuations converge jointly to a centered Gaussian vector with covariance $\\int f_0\\phi\\psi$. For a smooth compactly supported real test $\\phi$, one also has \\begin{equation} \\lim_{\\eps\\to0}\\sqrt\\mu \\left(\\E\\pi_0^\\eps(\\phi)-\\int f_0\\phi\\right) =-\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\int_{\\R^6} f_0(x,v)\\phi(","sourceOffset":13522,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[{"kind":"assumption","name":"","label":"ass:kinetic","text":"The function \\(f_0\\ge0\\) is a smooth probability density and, for some \\(\\beta>0\\), \\begin{equation} \\|f_0\\|_{\\Bol,2\\beta} +\\|\\nabla_xf_0\\|_{\\Bol,2\\beta}<\\infty. \\end{equation} There is a nonnegative classical solution \\(f\\) of \\eqref{eq:boltzmann} with initial datum \\(f_0\\) on a prescribed finite interval \\([0,T]\\), \\(T>0\\), such that \\begin{equation} M:=\\sup_{0\\le t\\le T}\\sup_{x,v} e^{2\\beta|v|^2}f(t,x,v)<\\infty. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":4366,"truncated":false},{"kind":"definition","name":"Layered C/O history","label":"def:dhm-history","text":"A history consists of finitely many oriented particle lines and binary atoms, with an order on the atoms along every line. Each atom has two incoming and two outgoing half-edges, paired into two serial particle lines. A C-atom applies elastic scattering, and an O-atom records an incoming encounter with straight continuation. A line segment between atoms is a bond. Segments with only one incident atom are ends. A line with no atom is a single empty end; it is integrated unless its state is fixed, just like any other end. Atoms belong to specified slabs; the order along every line must be consis","sourceOffset":38595,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":221621,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Hard-sphere-fluctuations-on-the-regular-Boltzmann-lifespan-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"365-01","slug":"Determination-of-a-metric-and-a-unitary-connection-from-one-boundary-patch-October-5-2026","familyId":"365","title":"Determination of a metric and a unitary connection from one boundary patch","abstract":"We prove that zero-frequency boundary measurements on any nonempty open boundary patch determine both a smooth Riemannian metric and a smooth unitary connection on the trivial Hermitian rank-two bundle over a compact connected smooth manifold of dimension at least three with smooth boundary. Both inputs and observations are restricted to the same patch. The metric and connection are determined up to a diffeomorphism and a unitary gauge that restrict to the identity on the measured patch.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Zero-frequency measurements on any nonempty open boundary patch determine a smooth metric and smooth unitary connection on a trivial Hermitian rank-two bundle over a compact connected manifold of dimension at least three, up to diffeomorphism and gauge fixed on that patch. Inputs and observations use the same patch. In contrast, distinct uniformly positive bounded measurable scalar conductivities on a three-dimensional ball can have identical full-boundary data.","pdfPath":"preprints/Determination-of-a-metric-and-a-unitary-connection-from-one-boundary-patch-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Determination-of-a-metric-and-a-unitary-connection-from-one-boundary-patch-October-5-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Determination-of-a-metric-and-a-unitary-connection-from-one-boundary-patch-October-5-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Determination-of-a-metric-and-a-unitary-connection-from-one-boundary-patch-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Determination-of-a-metric-and-a-unitary-connection-from-one-boundary-patch-October-5-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Determination-of-a-metric-and-a-unitary-connection-from-one-boundary-patch-October-5-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Determination-of-a-metric-and-a-unitary-connection-from-one-boundary-patch-October-5-2026/build/sections/02-boundary.tex","preprints/Determination-of-a-metric-and-a-unitary-connection-from-one-boundary-patch-October-5-2026/build/sections/03-product.tex","preprints/Determination-of-a-metric-and-a-unitary-connection-from-one-boundary-patch-October-5-2026/build/sections/04-spheres.tex","preprints/Determination-of-a-metric-and-a-unitary-connection-from-one-boundary-patch-October-5-2026/build/sections/05-angular.tex","preprints/Determination-of-a-metric-and-a-unitary-connection-from-one-boundary-patch-October-5-2026/build/sections/06-matrix.tex","preprints/Determination-of-a-metric-and-a-unitary-connection-from-one-boundary-patch-October-5-2026/build/sections/07-global.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Determination-of-a-metric-and-a-unitary-connection-from-one-boundary-patch-October-5-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that zero-frequency boundary measurements on any nonempty open boundary patch determine both a smooth Riemannian metric and a smooth unitary connection on the trivial Hermitian rank-two bundle over a compact connected smooth manifold of dimension at least three with smooth boundary. Both inputs and observations are restricted to the same patch. The metric and connection are determined up to a diffeomorphism and a unitary gauge that restrict to the identity on the measured patch.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":563},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and contribution","sourceOffset":3315},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the proof is organized","sourceOffset":7045},{"kind":"section","title":"Boundary data, exterior sources, and contact coordinates","sourceOffset":8848},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Dirichlet solutions and boundary jets","sourceOffset":9626},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A common cap and its Green matrices","sourceOffset":16646},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Evaluations identify points and fibers","sourceOffset":20450},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Coordinates at a possible interior frontier","sourceOffset":25890},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Dilation of the contact data","sourceOffset":32236},{"kind":"section","title":"Continuation of solutions in two variables","sourceOffset":35450},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Separate equations and extension from a cross","sourceOffset":36143},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A geometric criterion for local extension","sourceOffset":42637}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $M$ and $\\Gamma$ be as above. For $j=1,2$, let $g_j$ be a smooth Riemannian metric on $M$ and let $A_j\\in C^\\infty(M;T^*M\\otimes\\mathfrak u(2))$. If \\[ \\langle\\Lambda_{g_1,A_1,\\Gamma}f,h\\rangle =\\langle\\Lambda_{g_2,A_2,\\Gamma}f,h\\rangle \\quad\\text{for all }f,h\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Gamma;\\C^2), \\] then there are a smooth diffeomorphism $\\Phi:M\\longrightarrow M$ and a smooth map $U:M\\longrightarrow U(2)$ such that \\begin{equation} \\Phi|_\\Gamma=\\Id,\\qquad U|_\\Gamma=I,\\qquad g_2=\\Phi^*g_1,\\qquad A_2=U^{-1}(\\Phi^*A_1)U+U^{-1}dU. \\end{equation} Here $I$ denotes the $2\\times2$ identity matrix.","sourceOffset":2322,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Strict improvement of the principal tensors","label":"mat:improvement","text":"With the fixed choices in Lemma \\ref{mat:interpolation}, there are constants $C$ and $\\rho_0>0$, uniform for sufficiently small $\\lambda$, such that \\eqref{mat:provisional} implies \\begin{equation} H_8(y)\\le C r_\\delta(y)^{3/2} \\qquad\\text{if }y\\in K\\text{ and }r_\\delta(y)<\\rho_0. \\end{equation} On the same set, the derivatives of $D$ through order nine are bounded by $Cr_\\delta^{3/2}$.","sourceOffset":155755,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/365.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":175149,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Determination-of-a-metric-and-a-unitary-connection-from-one-boundary-patch-October-5-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"365-02","slug":"Smooth-Anisotropic-Uniqueness-in-the-Calderon-Problem-from-One-Boundary-Patch-September-24-2026","familyId":"365","title":"Smooth anisotropic uniqueness in the Calderón problem from one boundary patch","abstract":"We resolve the smooth anisotropic Calderón uniqueness problem with arbitrary same-patch measurements. A smooth Riemannian metric on a compact connected manifold of dimension at least three is determined, up to a diffeomorphism fixing the measured patch, by its zero-frequency Dirichlet-to-Neumann energy form with both input and observation on any nonempty open boundary patch.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Zero-frequency measurements on any nonempty open boundary patch determine a smooth metric and smooth unitary connection on a trivial Hermitian rank-two bundle over a compact connected manifold of dimension at least three, up to diffeomorphism and gauge fixed on that patch. Inputs and observations use the same patch. In contrast, distinct uniformly positive bounded measurable scalar conductivities on a three-dimensional ball can have identical full-boundary data.","pdfPath":"preprints/Smooth-Anisotropic-Uniqueness-in-the-Calderon-Problem-from-One-Boundary-Patch-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Smooth-Anisotropic-Uniqueness-in-the-Calderon-Problem-from-One-Boundary-Patch-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Smooth-Anisotropic-Uniqueness-in-the-Calderon-Problem-from-One-Boundary-Patch-September-24-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Smooth-Anisotropic-Uniqueness-in-the-Calderon-Problem-from-One-Boundary-Patch-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Smooth-Anisotropic-Uniqueness-in-the-Calderon-Problem-from-One-Boundary-Patch-September-24-2026/build/preamble.tex","preprints/Smooth-Anisotropic-Uniqueness-in-the-Calderon-Problem-from-One-Boundary-Patch-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Smooth-Anisotropic-Uniqueness-in-the-Calderon-Problem-from-One-Boundary-Patch-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-boundary-data.tex","preprints/Smooth-Anisotropic-Uniqueness-in-the-Calderon-Problem-from-One-Boundary-Patch-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-product-continuation.tex","preprints/Smooth-Anisotropic-Uniqueness-in-the-Calderon-Problem-from-One-Boundary-Patch-September-24-2026/build/figures/product-cross.tex","preprints/Smooth-Anisotropic-Uniqueness-in-the-Calderon-Problem-from-One-Boundary-Patch-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-shrinking-balls.tex","preprints/Smooth-Anisotropic-Uniqueness-in-the-Calderon-Problem-from-One-Boundary-Patch-September-24-2026/build/figures/shrinking-functional.tex","preprints/Smooth-Anisotropic-Uniqueness-in-the-Calderon-Problem-from-One-Boundary-Patch-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-angular-estimate.tex","preprints/Smooth-Anisotropic-Uniqueness-in-the-Calderon-Problem-from-One-Boundary-Patch-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-bootstrap.tex","preprints/Smooth-Anisotropic-Uniqueness-in-the-Calderon-Problem-from-One-Boundary-Patch-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-globalization.tex","preprints/Smooth-Anisotropic-Uniqueness-in-the-Calderon-Problem-from-One-Boundary-Patch-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-scalar-consequence.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Smooth-Anisotropic-Uniqueness-in-the-Calderon-Problem-from-One-Boundary-Patch-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We resolve the smooth anisotropic Calder\\'on uniqueness problem with arbitrary same-patch measurements. A smooth Riemannian metric on a compact connected manifold of dimension at least three is determined, up to a diffeomorphism fixing the measured patch, by its zero-frequency Dirichlet-to-Neumann energy form with both input and observation on any nonempty open boundary patch.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":468},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The measurement and the theorem","sourceOffset":1069},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Historical context","sourceOffset":5993},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":11457},{"kind":"section","title":"Boundary data and a common interior region","sourceOffset":16427},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The density and the boundary jets","sourceOffset":18356},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Attaching a common cap","sourceOffset":23426},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Harmonic tests from sources in the cap","sourceOffset":28591},{"kind":"section","title":"Local continuation and product solutions","sourceOffset":33863},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The local metric extension theorem","sourceOffset":33937},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Product solutions and extension from a cross","sourceOffset":36876},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A geometric criterion for local extension","sourceOffset":43757}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Smooth anisotropic uniqueness from one patch","label":"main:patch","text":"Let $n\\ge3$. Let $M$ be a compact connected smooth $n$-manifold with nonempty smooth boundary, and let $\\Gamma\\subset\\partial M$ be nonempty, proper, and relatively open. If two smooth Riemannian metrics $g_1,g_2$ on $M$ satisfy \\[ \\Lambda_{g_1,\\Gamma}=\\Lambda_{g_2,\\Gamma}, \\] then there is a smooth diffeomorphism $\\Phi:M\\to M$, smooth through the boundary, such that \\[ \\Phi|_\\Gamma=\\Id,\\qquad g_2=\\Phi^*g_1. \\]","sourceOffset":2702,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Full-boundary uniqueness","label":"main:full","text":"Let $M,g_1,g_2$ satisfy the geometric hypotheses of Theorem \\ref{main:patch}. If their Dirichlet energies agree for every pair of smooth boundary values, there is a smooth diffeomorphism $F:M\\to M$ fixing $\\partial M$ pointwise and satisfying $g_1=F^*g_2$.","sourceOffset":4017,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/365.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":151369,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":12,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Smooth-Anisotropic-Uniqueness-in-the-Calderon-Problem-from-One-Boundary-Patch-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"365-03","slug":"Nonuniqueness-for-Bounded-Measurable-Scalar-Conductivities-in-Three-Dimensions-September-23-2026","familyId":"365","title":"Nonuniqueness for bounded measurable scalar conductivities in three dimensions","abstract":"We construct two distinct uniformly positive bounded measurable scalar conductivities on a ball in ℝ3 with the same full Dirichlet-to-Neumann operator. Both conductivities equal one near the boundary. This gives nonuniqueness in the scalar Calderón problem at bounded measurable regularity.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Zero-frequency measurements on any nonempty open boundary patch determine a smooth metric and smooth unitary connection on a trivial Hermitian rank-two bundle over a compact connected manifold of dimension at least three, up to diffeomorphism and gauge fixed on that patch. Inputs and observations use the same patch. In contrast, distinct uniformly positive bounded measurable scalar conductivities on a three-dimensional ball can have identical full-boundary data.","pdfPath":"preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-Bounded-Measurable-Scalar-Conductivities-in-Three-Dimensions-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-Bounded-Measurable-Scalar-Conductivities-in-Three-Dimensions-September-23-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-Bounded-Measurable-Scalar-Conductivities-in-Three-Dimensions-September-23-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-Bounded-Measurable-Scalar-Conductivities-in-Three-Dimensions-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-Bounded-Measurable-Scalar-Conductivities-in-Three-Dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/scalarization.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-Bounded-Measurable-Scalar-Conductivities-in-Three-Dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/block.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-Bounded-Measurable-Scalar-Conductivities-in-Three-Dimensions-September-23-2026/build/sections/nonuniqueness.tex","preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-Bounded-Measurable-Scalar-Conductivities-in-Three-Dimensions-September-23-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Nonuniqueness-for-Bounded-Measurable-Scalar-Conductivities-in-Three-Dimensions-September-23-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct two distinct uniformly positive bounded measurable scalar conductivities on a ball in $\\R^3$ with the same full Dirichlet-to-Neumann operator. Both conductivities equal one near the boundary. This gives nonuniqueness in the scalar Calder\\'on problem at bounded measurable regularity.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":342},{"kind":"section","title":"Weak equations and changes of variables","sourceOffset":10726},{"kind":"section","title":"Exact scalarization of two Cauchy pairs","sourceOffset":13729},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An open class of finite laminates","sourceOffset":16456},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A split in preliminary coordinates","sourceOffset":21635},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact localized flux waves","sourceOffset":23942},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Realizing one coordinate join","sourceOffset":28588},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite-depth exhaustion","sourceOffset":44190},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The strong limit selected by Baire","sourceOffset":50685},{"kind":"section","title":"Constructing the branching block","sourceOffset":55945},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Attaching flat ends","sourceOffset":57668},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Matching one flat end exactly","sourceOffset":62094}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There exist real scalar functions $\\gamma_0,\\gamma_1\\in L^\\infty(B(0,3);\\R)$ and constants $0<c<C<\\infty$ such that \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)},itemsep=2pt] \\item $c\\leq\\gamma_j\\leq C$ almost everywhere, for $j=0,1$; \\item both conductivities equal $1$ in a neighborhood of the boundary; \\item $\\abs{\\{x:\\gamma_0(x)\\ne\\gamma_1(x)\\}}>0$; \\item $\\Lambda_{\\gamma_0}=\\Lambda_{\\gamma_1}$ as bounded operators from $H^{1/2}(\\partial B(0,3))$ to $H^{-1/2}(\\partial B(0,3))$. \\end{enumerate}","sourceOffset":1309,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Localized nonuniqueness","label":"cor:localized","text":"Let $c,C$ be the constants in Theorem \\ref{thm:main}. For every bounded connected Lipschitz domain $\\Omega\\subset\\R^3$ and every finite family of pairwise disjoint balls $B_1,\\ldots,B_m\\Subset\\Omega$, $m\\geq1$, there are $2^m$ pairwise distinct real scalar conductivities $\\gamma_\\varepsilon\\in L^\\infty(\\Omega)$, indexed by $\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}^m$, such that \\[ c\\leq\\gamma_\\varepsilon\\leq C\\quad\\text{almost everywhere}, \\qquad \\gamma_\\varepsilon=1\\quad\\text{on } \\Omega\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^m B_i, \\] and all their full weak Dirichlet-to-Neumann operators from $H^{1/2}(\\partial\\Omega)$ to $H^{-1/2}(\\partial\\Omega)$ agree. Distinct conductivities differ on a set of positive measure.","sourceOffset":2223,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Subsolution","label":"sc:subsolution","text":"A subsolution consists of a measurable symmetric matrix $A$, two potentials $u_1,u_2\\in H^1(U)$, and the column matrices \\[ E=(\\nabla u_1,\\nabla u_2),\\qquad F=AE, \\] such that $A\\in\\mathcal G$ almost everywhere and, for $j=1,2$, \\begin{align*} u_j-u_j^{\\rm in}&\\in H^1_0(U),& \\Div F_j&=0,\\\\ \\int_U F_j\\cdot\\nabla\\psi\\,\\dd x &=\\int_U F_j^{\\rm in}\\cdot\\nabla\\psi\\,\\dd x &&\\text{for every }\\psi\\in H^1(U). \\end{align*} It must also have an open cover of a full-measure subset of $U$ on which $A,u_1,u_2$ are smooth and have the local rank property in Theorem \\ref{thm:scalarization}: on each member one ","sourceOffset":20601,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/365.md","scopeText":"The Calderón inverse problem asks whether boundary measurements determine an interior conductivity. The formalized result gives nonuniqueness for bounded measurable scalar conductivities on the ball $B(0,3)\\subset\\mathbb R^3$: two uniformly positive conductivities differ on a set of positive volume, equal $1$ near the boundary, and have the same full weak Dirichlet-to-Neumann operator. Weak solutions exist uniquely for every boundary trace.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/365.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":119853,"sourceFileCount":5,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":1,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Nonuniqueness-for-Bounded-Measurable-Scalar-Conductivities-in-Three-Dimensions-September-23-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"366-01","slug":"Interior-regularity-of-planar-Mumford-Shah-minimizers-September-24-2026","familyId":"366","title":"Interior regularity of planar Mumford–Shah minimizers","abstract":"We prove the interior regularity assertion of the planar Mumford–Shah conjecture for reduced absolute minimizers with bounded fidelity data. Every interior point of the closed discontinuity set has a neighborhood consisting of a $C^{1,\\alpha}$ arc, an arc ending at that point, or three such arcs meeting at 120 degrees. Only finitely many global connected components meet any relatively compact open set.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the interior regularity conjecture for reduced absolute planar Mumford–Shah minimizers with bounded fidelity data. Locally, the closed discontinuity set is a $C^{1,\\alpha}$ arc, a regular crack tip, or three arcs meeting at $120^\\circ$; only finitely many global connected components meet any compact interior region.","pdfPath":"preprints/Interior-regularity-of-planar-Mumford-Shah-minimizers-September-24-2026/Interior-regularity-of-planar-Mumford-Shah-minimizers-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Interior-regularity-of-planar-Mumford-Shah-minimizers-September-24-2026/Interior-regularity-of-planar-Mumford-Shah-minimizers-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Interior-regularity-of-planar-Mumford-Shah-minimizers-September-24-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Interior-regularity-of-planar-Mumford-Shah-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Interior-regularity-of-planar-Mumford-Shah-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Interior-regularity-of-planar-Mumford-Shah-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/sections/reductions.tex","preprints/Interior-regularity-of-planar-Mumford-Shah-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/sections/blowup.tex","preprints/Interior-regularity-of-planar-Mumford-Shah-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/Interior-regularity-of-planar-Mumford-Shah-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/sections/interaction.tex","preprints/Interior-regularity-of-planar-Mumford-Shah-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/figures/compact-translation.tex","preprints/Interior-regularity-of-planar-Mumford-Shah-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/sections/completion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Interior-regularity-of-planar-Mumford-Shah-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the interior regularity assertion of the planar Mumford--Shah conjecture for reduced absolute minimizers with bounded fidelity data. Every interior point of the closed discontinuity set has a neighborhood consisting of a $C^{1,\\alpha}$ arc, an arc ending at that point, or three such arcs meeting at $120$ degrees. Only finitely many global connected components meet any relatively compact open set.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":471},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The variational problem","sourceOffset":935},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Main theorem","sourceOffset":2451},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and related work","sourceOffset":4153},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":8083},{"kind":"section","title":"Reduction to rectifiable minimizers","sourceOffset":10416},{"kind":"section","title":"Planar compactness and regularity facts","sourceOffset":25551},{"kind":"section","title":"Isolating a compact piece","sourceOffset":33229},{"kind":"section","title":"Excluding an isolated compact piece","sourceOffset":36057},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite-energy field carrying the compact jump","sourceOffset":40213},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The energy comparison and its harmonic interaction","sourceOffset":43244},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Jump variations force the whole field to vanish","sourceOffset":49335}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $(u,K)$ be a reduced absolute minimizer of \\eqref{eq:original-energy} in a bounded Lipschitz domain $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^2$, with $g\\in L^\\infty(\\Omega)$. Every $x\\in K$ has an interior neighborhood in which $K$ has one of the following forms, with $C^{1,\\alpha}$ arcs for some $\\alpha>0$: \\begin{enumerate}[label=\\textup{(\\roman*)},nosep] \\item a single embedded arc with $x$ in its interior; \\item a single embedded arc ending at $x$; \\item three embedded arcs meeting only at $x$, with pairwise angles $120$ degrees. \\end{enumerate} Moreover, for every open $U\\Subset\\Omega$, \\begin{equation} \\#\\{C\\in\\pi_0(K):C\\cap U\\ne\\varnothing\\}<\\infty, \\end{equation} where $\\pi_0(K)$ denotes the set of global connected components of $K$.","sourceOffset":2477,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Reduction to a rectifiable pair","label":"prop:initial-reduction","text":"Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^2$ be bounded and open, let $g\\in L^\\infty(\\Omega)$, and let $(u,K)$ be an admissible absolute minimizer for $\\mathcal F_g$ as defined in Section \\ref{sec:problem}. In particular, $K$ is relatively closed, $\\mathcal H^1(K)<\\infty$, and $u\\in L^2(\\Omega)\\cap W^{1,2}(\\Omega\\setminus K)$; no rectifiability of $K$ is assumed. Then \\[ u\\in L^\\infty_{\\rm loc}(\\Omega)\\cap SBV_{\\rm loc}(\\Omega), \\qquad \\mathcal H^1(K\\setminus J_u)=0. \\] The function satisfies $\\Delta u=u-g$ on $\\Omega\\setminus K$ and has there a $C^{1,\\alpha}_{\\rm loc}$ representative for every $\\alpha\\in(0,1)$. If the pair is reduced, then \\begin{equation} K=\\operatorname{spt}_{\\Omega}(\\mathcal H^1\\!\\llcorner J_u) =\\overline{J_u}^{\\,\\Omega}. \\end{equation} Consequently, in the reduced case, on every smooth open set $W\\Subset\\Omega$, the restricted pair is a reduced rectifiable absolute minimizer with bounded function.","sourceOffset":11804,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":65860,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Interior-regularity-of-planar-Mumford-Shah-minimizers-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"367-01","slug":"The-critical-dimension-for-one-phase-Bernoulli-minimizers-September-24-2026","familyId":"367","title":"The critical dimension for one-phase Bernoulli minimizers","abstract":"We prove that seven is the critical dimension for the one-phase Bernoulli problem: every nonzero one-homogeneous global minimizer in dimensions at most six is flat, while a nonflat one-homogeneous global minimizer exists in dimension seven. It follows that the interior free boundary of a local minimizer is smooth in dimensions at most six. In dimension n ≥ 7, its singular set has Hausdorff dimension at most $n-7$, and this bound is sharp. In dimension seven, the singular set is locally finite.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Establishes seven as the first dimension admitting a nonflat, one-homogeneous global minimizer of the one-phase Bernoulli energy. Consequently, minimizing free boundaries are smooth through dimension six, and their singular sets have dimension at most $n-7$ in higher dimensions.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-critical-dimension-for-one-phase-Bernoulli-minimizers-September-24-2026/The-critical-dimension-for-one-phase-Bernoulli-minimizers-September-24-2026.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-critical-dimension-for-one-phase-Bernoulli-minimizers-September-24-2026/The-critical-dimension-for-one-phase-Bernoulli-minimizers-September-24-2026.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-critical-dimension-for-one-phase-Bernoulli-minimizers-September-24-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/The-critical-dimension-for-one-phase-Bernoulli-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/The-critical-dimension-for-one-phase-Bernoulli-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/sections/history.tex","preprints/The-critical-dimension-for-one-phase-Bernoulli-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/sections/reductions.tex","preprints/The-critical-dimension-for-one-phase-Bernoulli-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/sections/geometry.tex","preprints/The-critical-dimension-for-one-phase-Bernoulli-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/sections/certificate.tex","preprints/The-critical-dimension-for-one-phase-Bernoulli-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/sections/flux.tex","preprints/The-critical-dimension-for-one-phase-Bernoulli-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/sections/certificate-proof.tex","preprints/The-critical-dimension-for-one-phase-Bernoulli-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/sections/cutoffs.tex","preprints/The-critical-dimension-for-one-phase-Bernoulli-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/figures/suspension-cutoff.tex","preprints/The-critical-dimension-for-one-phase-Bernoulli-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/sections/consequences.tex","preprints/The-critical-dimension-for-one-phase-Bernoulli-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/appendices/tables.tex","preprints/The-critical-dimension-for-one-phase-Bernoulli-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-critical-dimension-for-one-phase-Bernoulli-minimizers-September-24-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that seven is the critical dimension for the one-phase Bernoulli problem: every nonzero one-homogeneous global minimizer in dimensions at most six is flat, while a nonflat one-homogeneous global minimizer exists in dimension seven. It follows that the interior free boundary of a local minimizer is smooth in dimensions at most six. In dimension $n\\ge7$, its singular set has Hausdorff dimension at most $n-7$, and this bound is sharp. In dimension seven, the singular set is locally finite.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":546},{"kind":"section","title":"Reduction to a smooth link and its suspension","sourceOffset":11190},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The regularity inputs","sourceOffset":11547},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Dimension reduction at a nonzero point","sourceOffset":18563},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Suspension and the working domain","sourceOffset":27586},{"kind":"section","title":"Spherical geometry and the stability inequality","sourceOffset":32279},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The spherical Hessian and its boundary values","sourceOffset":33287},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Transport to a suspension","sourceOffset":41573},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Second variation under compact perturbations","sourceOffset":45398},{"kind":"section","title":"A universal algebraic certificate","sourceOffset":54549},{"kind":"section","title":"From the algebraic certificate to a geometric flux","sourceOffset":57729},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Normalized differentiation and the fourth derivatives","sourceOffset":58758}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Every nonzero one-homogeneous global minimizer of \\eqref{eq:energy} in \\(\\R^d\\), \\(1\\leq d\\leq6\\), is flat. There is a nonflat one-homogeneous global minimizer in \\(\\R^7\\). Consequently, \\[ d_*=7. \\]","sourceOffset":1378,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Sharp singular-set bound","label":"cor:singular-set","text":"Let $n\\ge2$, let $D\\subset\\R^n$ be open, and let $u\\in H^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(D)$ be a nonnegative local minimizer of the one-phase Bernoulli energy in $D$. Its interior free boundary is smooth outside $\\operatorname{Sing}(u)$. This set is empty when $n\\le6$, is locally finite when $n=7$, and satisfies \\[ \\dim_{\\mathrm H}\\operatorname{Sing}(u)\\le n-7\\qquad(n\\ge7). \\] For every $n\\ge7$, a global minimizer attains this dimension bound.","sourceOffset":2082,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/367.md","scopeText":"The paper identifies seven as the critical dimension for nonflat one-homogeneous global minimizers of the one-phase Bernoulli problem. The linked formalization proves the existence side: in dimension seven there is a nonzero one-homogeneous global minimizer that is not a half-space solution.\n\nThe flatness classification in dimensions at most six and the resulting regularity and singular-set bounds are outside this selected statement.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/367.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":155970,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-critical-dimension-for-one-phase-Bernoulli-minimizers-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"368-01","slug":"Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-1-le-p-le-2-September-24-2026","familyId":"368","title":"Strong diffeomorphic approximation in three dimensions for 1≤p≤2","abstract":"We resolve the three-dimensional Ball–Evans approximation problem for $1\\le p\\le2$. Every $W^{1,p}$ homeomorphism between arbitrary bounded domains in ℝ3 is a strong $W^{1,p}$ limit of smooth diffeomorphisms onto the same target.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the three-dimensional Ball–Evans approximation problem: every $W^{1,p}$ homeomorphism between arbitrary bounded domains in ℝ3, for $1\\le p\\lt \\infty$, is a strong $W^{1,p}$ limit of smooth diffeomorphisms onto the same target.","pdfPath":"preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-1-le-p-le-2-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-1-le-p-le-2-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-1-le-p-le-2-September-24-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-1-le-p-le-2-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-1-le-p-le-2-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-1-le-p-le-2-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-smoothing.tex","preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-1-le-p-le-2-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-low-tools.tex","preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-1-le-p-le-2-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-low-assembly.tex","preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-1-le-p-le-2-September-24-2026/build/sections/10-conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-1-le-p-le-2-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We resolve the three-dimensional Ball--Evans approximation problem for $1\\le p\\le2$. Every $W^{1,p}$ homeomorphism between arbitrary bounded domains in $\\R^3$ is a strong $W^{1,p}$ limit of smooth diffeomorphisms onto the same target.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction and conventions","sourceOffset":280},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work and the three-dimensional difficulty","sourceOffset":2486},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Measurements, local models, and the proof strategy","sourceOffset":7479},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":12305},{"kind":"section","title":"Qualitative topology and strong smoothing","sourceOffset":16636},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Relative qualitative tools","sourceOffset":18584},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Smoothing a locally finite PL homeomorphism","sourceOffset":25122},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Fine meshes and controlled patch insertion","sourceOffset":35060},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite PL libraries before resolution selection","sourceOffset":44141},{"kind":"section","title":"Parametrization, topology, and trace budgets for the low exponents","sourceOffset":58430},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Boundary parametrization of a disk","sourceOffset":59337},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Subcritical collapse onto boundary data","sourceOffset":76122}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"main:theorem","text":"Let $\\Omega,\\Lambda\\subset\\R^3$ be nonempty bounded connected open sets, let $1\\le p\\le2$, and let $f:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda$ be a homeomorphism in $W^{1,p}(\\Omega;\\R^3)$. There exist $C^\\infty$ diffeomorphisms $f_j:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda$, each belonging to $W^{1,p}(\\Omega;\\R^3)$, such that \\begin{equation} \\int_\\Omega\\bigl( |f_j-f|^p+|Df_j-Df|^p\\bigr)\\,dx\\longrightarrow0. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":1712,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Strong smoothing of locally bi-Lipschitz maps","label":"sm:smoothing","text":"Let $\\Omega,\\Omega'\\subset\\R^3$ be bounded domains, let $1\\le p<\\infty$, and let $H:\\Omega\\to\\Omega'$ be a locally bi-Lipschitz homeomorphism in $W^{1,p}(\\Omega;\\R^3)$. For every $\\eps>0$ and every continuous function $\\xi:\\Omega\\to(0,\\infty)$, there is a $C^\\infty$ diffeomorphism $g:\\Omega\\to\\Omega'$ such that \\begin{equation} \\norm{g-H}_{W^{1,p}(\\Omega)}<\\eps, \\qquad |g(x)-H(x)|<\\xi(x)\\quad(x\\in\\Omega). \\end{equation} In particular, $g$ belongs to $W^{1,p}(\\Omega;\\R^3)$, and the approximations have exactly the target $\\Omega'$.","sourceOffset":17523,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Essential tangential compatibility","label":"lt:compatibility","text":"Let $D$ be a compact polygonal disk or a convex polyhedral cell and let $v:D\\to\\R^d$ be Lipschitz. We say that $v$ has essential tangential compatibility if the following properties hold. \\begin{enumerate} \\item At almost every interior point $x$, the a.e. derivative of $v$ has essential limit $Dv(x)$ as its argument tends to $x$. \\item In radial coordinates based at an interior point, at almost every boundary parameter the derivatives in boundary directions have essential limits, from the interior as well, equal to the derivatives of the boundary restriction. \\end{enumerate} The exceptional s","sourceOffset":59692,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Tie budget","label":"lt:tie-budget","text":"In a tetrahedron $\\sigma$ with vertices $v_0,\\ldots,v_3$, write its barycentric coordinates as $\\lambda_i$. For a set $I$ of $m+1$ vertices, the associated dual stratum is \\[ Z_{\\sigma,I}= \\{\\lambda_i=\\max_j\\lambda_j\\ (i\\in I),\\quad \\lambda_j<\\max_i\\lambda_i\\ (j\\notin I)\\} \\cap\\relint\\sigma. \\] Write a source parametrization as $P:K\\to\\Omega$ and its measured image as $\\Gamma=f\\circ P$; lengths and areas of $\\Gamma$ mean integrals on $K$. For such a generic measured $m$-trace, its budget $\\mathcal B_m(\\Gamma)$ is the sum, over its visits to these strata, of \\[ \\cH^m(\\conv\\{v_i:i\\in I\\}), \\] wi","sourceOffset":122755,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":223766,"sourceFileCount":6,"statementCount":8,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-1-le-p-le-2-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"368-02","slug":"Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-p-gt-2-September-24-2026","familyId":"368","title":"Strong diffeomorphic approximation in three dimensions for p>2","abstract":"We resolve the three-dimensional Ball–Evans approximation problem for every finite p > 2. Every $W^{1,p}$ homeomorphism between arbitrary bounded domains in ℝ3 can be approximated strongly in $W^{1,p}$ by smooth diffeomorphisms onto the same target.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the three-dimensional Ball–Evans approximation problem: every $W^{1,p}$ homeomorphism between arbitrary bounded domains in ℝ3, for $1\\le p\\lt \\infty$, is a strong $W^{1,p}$ limit of smooth diffeomorphisms onto the same target.","pdfPath":"preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-p-gt-2-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-p-gt-2-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-p-gt-2-September-24-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-p-gt-2-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-p-gt-2-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-p-gt-2-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-high-preparation.tex","preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-p-gt-2-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-quantizers.tex","preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-p-gt-2-September-24-2026/build/sections/07-walls.tex","preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-p-gt-2-September-24-2026/build/sections/08-mesh.tex","preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-p-gt-2-September-24-2026/build/sections/09-high-assembly.tex","preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-p-gt-2-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-smoothing.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-p-gt-2-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We resolve the three-dimensional Ball--Evans approximation problem for every finite $p>2$. Every $W^{1,p}$ homeomorphism between arbitrary bounded domains in $\\R^3$ can be approximated strongly in $W^{1,p}$ by smooth diffeomorphisms onto the same target.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction and conventions","sourceOffset":300},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Earlier results and the dimensional obstacle","sourceOffset":2842},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and organization","sourceOffset":6224},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":12076},{"kind":"section","title":"Retained rank data and an exact carrier","sourceOffset":16407},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Regular points and fine tolerances","sourceOffset":17647},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Flattening the deficient positive ranks in the target","sourceOffset":24750},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Protected endpoint pairs","sourceOffset":49746},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Implanting exact cores by cubical collapse","sourceOffset":59105},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Opening isolated ball fibres","sourceOffset":83084},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exceptional boxes and regular affine cores","sourceOffset":87053},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An exact carrier with prescribed residual volume","sourceOffset":93778}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"main:theorem","text":"Let $\\Omega,\\Lambda\\subset\\R^3$ be bounded domains, let $2<p<\\infty$, and let $f:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda$ be a homeomorphism in $W^{1,p}(\\Omega;\\R^3)$. There exist $C^\\infty$ diffeomorphisms $f_j:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda$, each belonging to $W^{1,p}(\\Omega;\\R^3)$, such that \\begin{equation} \\int_\\Omega\\bigl( |f_j-f|^p+|Df_j-Df|^p\\bigr)\\,dx\\longrightarrow0. \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":1611,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Target flattening","label":"hp:flattening","text":"Given $\\eps>0$, $0<\\lambda<1/100$, and a positive continuous fine tolerance $a$ on $\\Lambda$, one can retain a compact set $K\\subset D$ with $\\mu(D\\setminus K)<\\eps$ and construct a Lipschitz map $S_0:\\Lambda\\to\\Lambda$ with the following properties. \\begin{enumerate} \\item The map is the identity outside finitely many disjoint rectangular boxes compactly contained in $\\Lambda$, $\\Lip(S_0)\\le C$, and $|S_0(y)-y|<a(y)$. The constant $C$ is absolute. For $0<\\kappa\\le1$, \\[ S_\\kappa=(1-\\kappa)S_0+\\kappa\\Id \\] is a bi-Lipschitz homeomorphism of $\\Lambda$ onto itself. \\item With $F_\\kappa=S_\\kappa\\circ f$ for $0\\le\\kappa\\le1$, \\begin{equation} |DF_\\kappa|\\le C|Df|\\quad\\hbox{a.e. on }\\Omega, \\qquad |DF_\\kappa-Df|\\le C\\lambda|Df| \\quad\\hbox{a.e. on }K. \\end{equation} Moreover, $F_\\kappa\\to F_0$ strongly in $W^{1,p}(\\Omega;\\R^3)$, and $\\rank DF_0=\\rank Df$ almost everywhere on $K$. \\item The compact set $F_0(K)$ is contained in finitely many compact flat plate patches with pairwise disjoint ambient neighborhoods. Each patch lies in a plane of slope at most $C\\lambda$ in one of the chosen coordinates. Each patch has an assigned rank, either one or two; the rank-one patches can in addition retain any prescribed small directional grouping of $\\operatorname{im}Df$. \\item There is a compact zero-volume set $Z\\Subset\\Lambda$, contained in finitely many piecewise affine graph sheets, such that \\begin{equation} S_0:\\Lambda\\setminus S_0^{-1}(Z)\\longrightarrow\\Lambda\\setminus Z \\end{equation} ","sourceOffset":36731,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[{"kind":"definition","name":"Pre-stack contract","label":"wl:prestack","text":"Let $M_x,M_y$ be the source and target exteriors and let $h_*:M_x\\to M_y$ be the fixed reference homeomorphism. A pre-stack system consists of a locally finite, countable, two-colour family of compact collars \\[ \\mathcal E_j:\\Sigma_j\\times[0,w_j]\\longrightarrow C_j\\subset M_x, \\qquad u_j(\\mathcal E_j(z,\\lambda))=\\lambda,\\qquad w_j>0, \\] whose central sampled wall is the level $u_j=w_j/2$, subject to the following conditions. \\begin{enumerate} \\item Each $\\mathcal E_j$ is a bi-Lipschitz homeomorphism onto its image. For colour $A$, $\\Sigma_j$ is an annulus whose core is essential in $M_x$; for ","sourceOffset":208164,"truncated":true},{"kind":"definition","name":"Guards and prior capacities","label":"wl:guards","text":"Choose a locally finite compact cover of $M_x$ by smaller buffered coordinate regions $K_a$, with larger regions $U_a$ carrying fixed bi-Lipschitz ball or half-ball charts. All smaller regions have positive margins in the larger charts. Fix finite numbers $C_a$ as upper capacities for \\begin{equation} \\int_{U_a}G^p\\leq C_a. \\end{equation} These numbers, the coordinate bounds and the prescribed continuous label accuracies are fixed before guards, sample weights and the final resolution are chosen. A guard system is obtained from a sufficiently fine homeomorphic approximation $h_g$ to $F_*$, agr","sourceOffset":230821,"truncated":true}],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":500021,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":19,"definitionCount":2,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-p-gt-2-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"369-01","slug":"Strict-hot-spots-and-absence-of-interior-critical-points-on-smooth-simply-connected-planar-domains-September-24-2026","familyId":"369","title":"Strict hot spots and absence of interior critical points on smooth simply connected planar domains","abstract":"We prove the strict hot spots conjecture for smooth bounded simply connected planar domains. More precisely, every nonzero eigenfunction for the first positive Neumann eigenvalue has nonvanishing gradient in the interior, so all its global maxima and minima lie on the boundary. This holds even when the eigenvalue is multiple.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves a strict form of Burdzy's simply connected hot spots conjecture. On every smooth bounded simply connected planar domain, each nonzero eigenfunction for the first positive Neumann eigenvalue has no interior critical point, so all global extrema lie on the boundary. Eigenvalue multiplicity is allowed.","pdfPath":"preprints/Strict-hot-spots-and-absence-of-interior-critical-points-on-smooth-simply-connected-planar-domains-September-24-2026/main.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Strict-hot-spots-and-absence-of-interior-critical-points-on-smooth-simply-connected-planar-domains-September-24-2026/main.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Strict-hot-spots-and-absence-of-interior-critical-points-on-smooth-simply-connected-planar-domains-September-24-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Strict-hot-spots-and-absence-of-interior-critical-points-on-smooth-simply-connected-planar-domains-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Strict-hot-spots-and-absence-of-interior-critical-points-on-smooth-simply-connected-planar-domains-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Strict-hot-spots-and-absence-of-interior-critical-points-on-smooth-simply-connected-planar-domains-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-preliminaries.tex","preprints/Strict-hot-spots-and-absence-of-interior-critical-points-on-smooth-simply-connected-planar-domains-September-24-2026/build/figures/boundary-signs.tex","preprints/Strict-hot-spots-and-absence-of-interior-critical-points-on-smooth-simply-connected-planar-domains-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-vector-form.tex","preprints/Strict-hot-spots-and-absence-of-interior-critical-points-on-smooth-simply-connected-planar-domains-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-boundary-kernels.tex","preprints/Strict-hot-spots-and-absence-of-interior-critical-points-on-smooth-simply-connected-planar-domains-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-reciprocal-kernels.tex","preprints/Strict-hot-spots-and-absence-of-interior-critical-points-on-smooth-simply-connected-planar-domains-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Strict-hot-spots-and-absence-of-interior-critical-points-on-smooth-simply-connected-planar-domains-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the strict hot spots conjecture for smooth bounded simply connected planar domains. More precisely, every nonzero eigenfunction for the first positive Neumann eigenvalue has nonvanishing gradient in the interior, so all its global maxima and minima lie on the boundary. This holds even when the eigenvalue is multiple.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":373},{"kind":"section","title":"Spectral and planar preliminaries","sourceOffset":9977},{"kind":"section","title":"A variational principle for tangent vector fields","sourceOffset":19245},{"kind":"section","title":"Boundary kernels and the multiplier identity","sourceOffset":23174},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A subcritical disk potential","sourceOffset":23605},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Positive kernels and their estimates","sourceOffset":26654},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The boundary energy","sourceOffset":29261},{"kind":"section","title":"Reciprocal Green functions and a squared-distance kernel","sourceOffset":32565},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A bounded regularization of the Green kernel","sourceOffset":36146},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite resolvent update","sourceOffset":39854},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The interior resolvent kernel","sourceOffset":41537},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The boundary limit","sourceOffset":46060}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For every $0\\ne u\\in\\V$, \\[ \\grad u(x)\\ne0\\qquad(x\\in\\Omega). \\] In particular, \\begin{equation} \\min_{y\\in\\partial\\Omega}u(y)<u(x)<\\max_{y\\in\\partial\\Omega}u(y) \\qquad(x\\in\\Omega). \\end{equation}","sourceOffset":1416,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:multiplicity","text":"The real eigenspace $\\V$ has dimension at most two.","sourceOffset":18094,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/369.md","scopeText":"The strict hot-spots conjecture asks whether extrema of a first nonconstant Neumann eigenfunction occur only on the boundary. The formalization proves the stronger interior statement on every nonempty smooth bounded simply connected planar domain: every nonzero eigenfunction in the first positive Neumann eigenspace has nonvanishing gradient throughout the interior. Consequently all global maxima and minima lie on the boundary. The conclusion applies to every eigenfunction even when the eigenvalue is multiple.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/369.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":55092,"sourceFileCount":8,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Strict-hot-spots-and-absence-of-interior-critical-points-on-smooth-simply-connected-planar-domains-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Strict-hot-spots-and-absence-of-interior-critical-points-on-smooth-simply-connected-planar-domains-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"},{"source":"preprints/Strict-hot-spots-and-absence-of-interior-critical-points-on-smooth-simply-connected-planar-domains-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"370-01","slug":"The-Subcritical-Henon-Lane-Emden-Conjecture-September-24-2026","familyId":"370","title":"The Subcritical Hénon–Lane–Emden Conjecture","abstract":"We prove the subcritical Hénon–Lane–Emden conjecture: for every dimension n ≥ 2, positive powers p, q, and real weights A, B, the system has no strictly positive entire solution when $(n+A)/(p+1)+(n+B)/(q+1)\\gt n-2$. Solutions need only be continuous at the origin and classical elsewhere, without a condition at infinity. For n ≥ 3 and $A,B\\gt -2$, combining this result with the radial existence theorem of Bidaut-Véron and Giacomini proves Phan's Conjecture C in full: a positive radial entire solution exists whenever the strict inequality fails.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Resolves the subcritical Lane–Emden conjecture and its weighted Hénon extension. For n ≥ 2, $p,q\\gt 0$ and real A, B, the system $-\\Delta u=|x|^A v^p$, $-\\Delta v=|x|^B u^q$ has no positive entire solution when $(n+A)/(p+1)+(n+B)/(q+1)\\gt n-2$, with solutions continuous at the origin and classical elsewhere. No symmetry or growth assumption is needed. Known radial existence gives the exact existence criterion for n ≥ 3 and $A,B\\gt -2$.","pdfPath":"preprints/The-Subcritical-Henon-Lane-Emden-Conjecture-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Subcritical-Henon-Lane-Emden-Conjecture-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-Subcritical-Henon-Lane-Emden-Conjecture-September-24-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/The-Subcritical-Henon-Lane-Emden-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/The-Subcritical-Henon-Lane-Emden-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/The-Subcritical-Henon-Lane-Emden-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/02-preliminaries.tex","preprints/The-Subcritical-Henon-Lane-Emden-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/03-localization.tex","preprints/The-Subcritical-Henon-Lane-Emden-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/04-intervals.tex","preprints/The-Subcritical-Henon-Lane-Emden-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/05-pressure.tex","preprints/The-Subcritical-Henon-Lane-Emden-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/sections/06-conclusion.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/The-Subcritical-Henon-Lane-Emden-Conjecture-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove the subcritical H\\'enon--Lane--Emden conjecture: for every dimension $n\\ge2$, positive powers $p,q$, and real weights $A,B$, the system has no strictly positive entire solution when $(n+A)/(p+1)+(n+B)/(q+1)>n-2$. Solutions need only be continuous at the origin and classical elsewhere, without a condition at infinity. For \\(n\\ge3\\) and \\(A,B>-2\\), combining this result with the radial existence theorem of Bidaut-V\\'eron and Giacomini proves Phan's Conjecture C in full: a positive radial entire solution exists whenever the strict inequality fails.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":623},{"kind":"section","title":"Reductions and Newton potentials","sourceOffset":7568},{"kind":"section","title":"A localized virial identity","sourceOffset":20671},{"kind":"section","title":"An interval-pair inequality","sourceOffset":28878},{"kind":"section","title":"Pressure from weighted source layers","sourceOffset":34612},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Angular localization","sourceOffset":35782},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Measurable intervals and their loss","sourceOffset":38731},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Weighted endpoints and nonnegative integration","sourceOffset":43018},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Recovery of the cutoff pressure","sourceOffset":47322},{"kind":"section","title":"The energy bound and the scaling contradiction","sourceOffset":49507}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let \\(n\\ge2\\) be an integer, let \\(p,q>0\\), and let \\(A,B\\in\\R\\). Suppose that \\begin{equation} \\frac{n+A}{p+1}+\\frac{n+B}{q+1}>n-2. \\end{equation} There are no functions \\[ u,v\\in C^2(\\R^n\\setminus\\{0\\})\\cap C(\\R^n), \\qquad u(x)>0,\\quad v(x)>0\\quad(x\\in\\R^n), \\] satisfying Equation \\eqref{eq:system} at every \\(x\\ne0\\).","sourceOffset":2957,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Unweighted Lane--Emden conjecture","label":"cor:lane-emden","text":"For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and \\(p,q>0\\) satisfying \\[ \\frac{1}{p+1}+\\frac{1}{q+1}>\\frac{n-2}{n}, \\] there is no strictly positive \\(C^2(\\R^n)\\) solution of \\[ -\\Delta u=v^p,\\qquad -\\Delta v=u^q\\qquad\\text{in }\\R^n. \\]","sourceOffset":3351,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":true,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/370.md","scopeText":"The formalized result proves nonexistence of positive entire solutions to the subcritical Hénon–Lane–Emden system. For $n\\ge2$, $p,q>0$, and real $A,B$ with $(n+A)/(p+1)+(n+B)/(q+1)>n-2$, there are no positive functions, continuous everywhere and $C^2$ off the origin, satisfying $-\\Delta u=|x|^A v^p$ and $-\\Delta v=|x|^B u^q$ away from the origin. It includes the globally $C^2$ unweighted case for $n\\ge3$. The critical equality case is not included.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/370.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":55050,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":6,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/The-Subcritical-Henon-Lane-Emden-Conjecture-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"371-01","slug":"Stable-Self-Similar-Blowup-for-a-Supercritical-Defocusing-Schrodinger-Equation-on-the-Torus-September-24-2026","familyId":"371","title":"Stable self-similar blowup for a supercritical defocusing Schrödinger equation on the torus","abstract":"We prove stable self-similar finite-time blowup for a supercritical defocusing nonlinear Schrödinger equation on the twelve-dimensional torus. For a sufficiently large odd power, the blowup initial data contain a nonempty open set in a high Sobolev space. As a consequence, Gaussian Fourier initial data of arbitrarily high Sobolev regularity can have positive probability of finite-time blowup.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For a sufficiently large odd nonlinearity power, constructs a nonempty open set of initial data in $H^k(\\mathbb T^{12})$, with k > 8, whose solutions of the scalar defocusing nonlinear Schrödinger equation blow up in finite time. Thus finite-time blowup is stable under Sobolev perturbations in this supercritical regime.","pdfPath":"preprints/Stable-Self-Similar-Blowup-for-a-Supercritical-Defocusing-Schrodinger-Equation-on-the-Torus-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Stable-Self-Similar-Blowup-for-a-Supercritical-Defocusing-Schrodinger-Equation-on-the-Torus-September-24-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Stable-Self-Similar-Blowup-for-a-Supercritical-Defocusing-Schrodinger-Equation-on-the-Torus-September-24-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Stable-Self-Similar-Blowup-for-a-Supercritical-Defocusing-Schrodinger-Equation-on-the-Torus-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Stable-Self-Similar-Blowup-for-a-Supercritical-Defocusing-Schrodinger-Equation-on-the-Torus-September-24-2026/build/sections/free.tex","preprints/Stable-Self-Similar-Blowup-for-a-Supercritical-Defocusing-Schrodinger-Equation-on-the-Torus-September-24-2026/build/sections/profiles.tex","preprints/Stable-Self-Similar-Blowup-for-a-Supercritical-Defocusing-Schrodinger-Equation-on-the-Torus-September-24-2026/build/figures/core-limit.tex","preprints/Stable-Self-Similar-Blowup-for-a-Supercritical-Defocusing-Schrodinger-Equation-on-the-Torus-September-24-2026/build/sections/spectrum.tex","preprints/Stable-Self-Similar-Blowup-for-a-Supercritical-Defocusing-Schrodinger-Equation-on-the-Torus-September-24-2026/build/figures/turning-point.tex","preprints/Stable-Self-Similar-Blowup-for-a-Supercritical-Defocusing-Schrodinger-Equation-on-the-Torus-September-24-2026/build/sections/linear.tex","preprints/Stable-Self-Similar-Blowup-for-a-Supercritical-Defocusing-Schrodinger-Equation-on-the-Torus-September-24-2026/build/sections/nonlinear.tex","preprints/Stable-Self-Similar-Blowup-for-a-Supercritical-Defocusing-Schrodinger-Equation-on-the-Torus-September-24-2026/build/sections/certificates.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Stable-Self-Similar-Blowup-for-a-Supercritical-Defocusing-Schrodinger-Equation-on-the-Torus-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove stable self-similar finite-time blowup for a supercritical defocusing nonlinear Schr\\\"odinger equation on the twelve-dimensional torus. For a sufficiently large odd power, the blowup initial data contain a nonempty open set in a high Sobolev space. As a consequence, Gaussian Fourier initial data of arbitrarily high Sobolev regularity can have positive probability of finite-time blowup.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":444},{"kind":"section","title":"Free exterior matching","sourceOffset":10077},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The slow solution and the Laguerre cone","sourceOffset":14034},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exclusion of high angular degrees","sourceOffset":23541},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Boundary homotopy and the low angular counts","sourceOffset":27705},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Profile degree and nonvanishing of the exterior","sourceOffset":35272},{"kind":"section","title":"Smooth profiles with a pressurized core","sourceOffset":40068},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Similarity variables and the amplitude equations","sourceOffset":40893},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The inner boundary value problem","sourceOffset":46289},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A uniform slow solution on the exterior","sourceOffset":59152},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Matching, pressure transport, and deformation","sourceOffset":67490},{"kind":"section","title":"The outgoing spectrum","sourceOffset":76114}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"proposition","name":"Free matching","label":"free:main","text":"For every $(b,Z)\\in\\mathcal D$, the following conclusions hold. \\begin{enumerate} \\item The quotient \\[ j(b,Z)=-\\frac{H'(-ib,6,-iZ)}{H(-ib,6,-iZ)} \\] is well defined and satisfies $|j(b,Z)|<3\\cdot10^{-9}$. It is nonzero on $\\partial\\mathcal D$, and, identifying $\\mathbb C$ with $\\mathbb R^2$ in the order real part, imaginary part, \\[ \\deg(j,\\mathcal D,0)=-1. \\] \\item For every real $z\\ge Z$, $H(-ib,6,-iz)\\ne0$ and \\begin{equation} \\operatorname{Re}j(b,z)<.043. \\end{equation} For $z\\ge3$ one also has $\\operatorname{Im}j(b,z)>0$. \\item For $\\ell\\in\\{0,1,2,\\ldots\\}$ and $h\\in\\{1,-1\\}$ define \\begin{equation} q_h=\\lambda+\\ell/2-hi b,\\qquad J_h(\\lambda)= \\begin{pmatrix}H(q_h,6+\\ell,-hiZ)\\\\-H'(q_h,6+\\ell,-hiZ)\\end{pmatrix}. \\end{equation} The columns $J_h$ are holomorphic and nonzero on a neighborhood of $\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda\\ge-1/32$. The determinant $\\Delta_\\ell(\\lambda;b,Z)=\\det(J_+(\\lambda),J_-(\\lambda))$ has no zero on $\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda=-1/32$. In the closed half-plane $\\operatorname{Re}\\lambda\\ge-1/32$ its numbers of zeros, counted with holomorphic multiplicity, are \\[ \\begin{array}{c|ccc} \\ell&0&1&\\ell\\ge2\\\\ \\hline \\#\\{\\Delta_\\ell=0\\}&2&1&0. \\end{array} \\] \\end{enumerate} The locations of the symmetry zeros are identified later at parameters where $j(b,Z)=0$; the count in this proposition holds on the entire disk.","sourceOffset":12557,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"An open set of blowup data","label":"thm:blowup","text":"There exist an odd integer $p\\geq3$, a finite integer $k>8$, and a nonempty open set $\\mathcal U\\subset H^k(\\mathbb T^{12};\\mathbb C)$ with the following property. For every $u_0\\in\\mathcal U$, the unique local solution of \\eqref{eq:nls} extends to \\[ u\\in C([0,T);H^k)\\cap C^1([0,T);H^{k-2}) \\] for some finite $T>0$, but has no continuous $H^k$ extension through $T$. More precisely, there are $x_*\\in\\mathbb T^{12}$ and $c_0>0$ such that \\[ \\lim_{t\\uparrow T}(T-t)^{1/(p-1)}|u(t,x_*)|=c_0. \\] The power can be chosen sufficiently large, and $k$ is then fixed after that choice.","sourceOffset":1626,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/371.md","scopeText":"The formalization constructs stable self-similar finite-time blowup for defocusing nonlinear Schrödinger equations on the twelve-dimensional torus. For every prescribed lower bound on the power, it selects an odd power at least that large and a Sobolev index $k>8$ for which a nonempty open set of $H^k$ initial data has the stated classical blowup behavior. The selected quantifier gives arbitrarily large odd powers, rather than every sufficiently large odd power.\n\nFor each such construction, Gaussian Fourier data with decay exponent $\\alpha>k+6$ assign positive probability to the blowup set, and almost-sure global existence fails. The exponent $\\alpha$ may be arbitrarily large.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/371.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":188020,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":9,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Stable-Self-Similar-Blowup-for-a-Supercritical-Defocusing-Schrodinger-Equation-on-the-Torus-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"372-01","slug":"Global-Uniqueness-for-the-Smooth-Isotropic-Elasticity-Inverse-Problem-September-24-2026","familyId":"372","title":"Global Uniqueness for the Smooth Isotropic Elasticity Inverse Problem","abstract":"We prove that the full static displacement-to-traction map uniquely determines both real smooth Lamé moduli on every bounded connected smooth domain in ℝ3, provided μ > 0 and $3\\lambda+2\\mu\\gt 0$ on the closure. This resolves the smooth three-dimensional isotropic elastic Calderón uniqueness problem under these positivity assumptions.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves that full static boundary displacement-to-traction data determine both smooth real Lamé moduli on every bounded connected smooth domain in ℝ3, provided μ > 0 and $3\\lambda+2\\mu\\gt 0$ on the closure. Neither analyticity, proximity to constant coefficients nor prior knowledge near the boundary is required.","pdfPath":"preprints/Global-Uniqueness-for-the-Smooth-Isotropic-Elasticity-Inverse-Problem-September-24-2026/article.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Global-Uniqueness-for-the-Smooth-Isotropic-Elasticity-Inverse-Problem-September-24-2026/article.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Global-Uniqueness-for-the-Smooth-Isotropic-Elasticity-Inverse-Problem-September-24-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Global-Uniqueness-for-the-Smooth-Isotropic-Elasticity-Inverse-Problem-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Global-Uniqueness-for-the-Smooth-Isotropic-Elasticity-Inverse-Problem-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Global-Uniqueness-for-the-Smooth-Isotropic-Elasticity-Inverse-Problem-September-24-2026/build/sections/boundary-reduction.tex","preprints/Global-Uniqueness-for-the-Smooth-Isotropic-Elasticity-Inverse-Problem-September-24-2026/build/sections/analytic-estimates.tex","preprints/Global-Uniqueness-for-the-Smooth-Isotropic-Elasticity-Inverse-Problem-September-24-2026/build/sections/physical-amplitudes.tex","preprints/Global-Uniqueness-for-the-Smooth-Isotropic-Elasticity-Inverse-Problem-September-24-2026/build/sections/transport-matching.tex","preprints/Global-Uniqueness-for-the-Smooth-Isotropic-Elasticity-Inverse-Problem-September-24-2026/build/sections/coefficient-recovery.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Global-Uniqueness-for-the-Smooth-Isotropic-Elasticity-Inverse-Problem-September-24-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove that the full static displacement-to-traction map uniquely determines both real smooth Lam\\'e moduli on every bounded connected smooth domain in $\\R^3$, provided $\\mu>0$ and $3\\lambda+2\\mu>0$ on the closure. This resolves the smooth three-dimensional isotropic elastic Calder\\'on uniqueness problem under these positivity assumptions.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":416},{"kind":"section","title":"Boundary reduction and physical transfer","sourceOffset":11301},{"kind":"section","title":"An augmented system and analytic estimates","sourceOffset":15051},{"kind":"section","title":"Physical solutions realizing local transport frames","sourceOffset":26363},{"kind":"section","title":"Matching the leading transports","sourceOffset":42563},{"kind":"section","title":"Recovery of the coefficients","sourceOffset":66935},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The bundle on the null conic","sourceOffset":67257},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A finite-type uniqueness lemma","sourceOffset":72700},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Completion of the coefficient recovery","sourceOffset":75412}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"Let $\\Omega\\subset\\R^3$ be any bounded connected domain with $C^\\infty$ boundary. For $j=1,2$, let $\\lambda_j,\\mu_j\\in C^\\infty(\\overline\\Omega;\\R)$ satisfy $\\mu_j>0$ and $3\\lambda_j+2\\mu_j>0$ on $\\overline\\Omega$. If \\[ \\Lambda_{\\lambda_1,\\mu_1} =\\Lambda_{\\lambda_2,\\mu_2} \\colon H^{1/2}(\\partial\\Omega;\\R^3) \\longrightarrow H^{-1/2}(\\partial\\Omega;\\R^3), \\] then $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2$ and $\\mu_1=\\mu_2$ throughout $\\Omega$.","sourceOffset":1790,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Transfer of physical solutions","label":"prop:physical-transfer","text":"Let $B$ be a ball containing $\\overline B_r$. Every smooth solution $u_1$ of $L_1u_1=0$ on $B$ has a smooth counterpart $u_2$ satisfying $L_2u_2=0$ on $B$ and \\[ u_2=u_1\\quad\\text{on }B\\setminus\\overline\\Omega. \\] In particular, $u_2-u_1$ and $\\diverg u_2-\\diverg u_1$ are smooth and supported in $\\overline\\Omega$.","sourceOffset":13625,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/372.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves global uniqueness in the three-dimensional static isotropic elasticity inverse problem. On a bounded connected smooth domain, let two pairs of smooth real Lamé moduli satisfy $\\mu>0$ and $3\\lambda+2\\mu>0$ on the closure. If their full displacement-to-traction maps agree, then both Lamé moduli agree throughout the domain. The selected statement is uniqueness; it does not supply a reconstruction algorithm.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/372.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":79021,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Global-Uniqueness-for-the-Smooth-Isotropic-Elasticity-Inverse-Problem-September-24-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"373-01","slug":"A-counterexample-to-the-Monge-ansatz-for-the-three-marginal-Coulomb-cost-September-25-2026","familyId":"373","title":"A counterexample to the Monge ansatz for the three-marginal Coulomb cost","abstract":"We construct a smooth compactly supported probability density on ℝ3, with a smooth compactly supported square root, for which the three-marginal Coulomb transport minimum is not attained by any pair of measure-preserving Borel maps. Nevertheless, the Monge and Kantorovich infima agree: we construct preserving maps whose costs approach the Kantorovich minimum. For every d ≥ 2 and s > 0, we also construct a smooth identical marginal with the same nonattainment and equality-of-infima properties for the inverse-power interaction $\\sum_{i\\lt j}|x_i-x_j|^{-s}$ on ℝd.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"An optimal three-particle Coulomb configuration need not be a deterministic function of the first particle, even for smooth identical spatial densities. The Monge and Kantorovich infima agree, but the Monge infimum is not attained. The same phenomenon occurs for every inverse-power Riesz exponent in each dimension at least two, with a suitable density in each case.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Monge-ansatz-for-the-three-marginal-Coulomb-cost-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Monge-ansatz-for-the-three-marginal-Coulomb-cost-September-25-2026/paper.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Monge-ansatz-for-the-three-marginal-Coulomb-cost-September-25-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Monge-ansatz-for-the-three-marginal-Coulomb-cost-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Monge-ansatz-for-the-three-marginal-Coulomb-cost-September-25-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Monge-ansatz-for-the-three-marginal-Coulomb-cost-September-25-2026/build/figures/components.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Monge-ansatz-for-the-three-marginal-Coulomb-cost-September-25-2026/build/sections/splitting.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Monge-ansatz-for-the-three-marginal-Coulomb-cost-September-25-2026/build/sections/local.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Monge-ansatz-for-the-three-marginal-Coulomb-cost-September-25-2026/build/sections/certificate.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Monge-ansatz-for-the-three-marginal-Coulomb-cost-September-25-2026/build/sections/proof.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Monge-ansatz-for-the-three-marginal-Coulomb-cost-September-25-2026/build/sections/infima.tex","preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Monge-ansatz-for-the-three-marginal-Coulomb-cost-September-25-2026/build/sections/riesz.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-counterexample-to-the-Monge-ansatz-for-the-three-marginal-Coulomb-cost-September-25-2026/build/paper.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct a smooth compactly supported probability density on \\(\\R^3\\), with a smooth compactly supported square root, for which the three-marginal Coulomb transport minimum is not attained by any pair of measure-preserving Borel maps. Nevertheless, the Monge and Kantorovich infima agree: we construct preserving maps whose costs approach the Kantorovich minimum. For every \\(d\\ge2\\) and \\(s>0\\), we also construct a smooth identical marginal with the same nonattainment and equality-of-infima properties for the inverse-power interaction \\(\\sum_{i<j}|x_i-x_j|^{-s}\\) on \\(\\R^d\\).","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The problem and the result","sourceOffset":631},{"kind":"section","title":"An obstruction to deterministic selection","sourceOffset":9495},{"kind":"section","title":"Two local Coulomb branches","sourceOffset":11109},{"kind":"section","title":"A supporting inequality on five components","sourceOffset":15557},{"kind":"section","title":"The marginal and exclusion of every Monge optimizer","sourceOffset":20382},{"kind":"section","title":"Equality of the two infima","sourceOffset":23994},{"kind":"section","title":"Inverse-power Riesz interactions","sourceOffset":33272}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is a nonnegative density \\(\\rho\\) on \\(\\R^3\\) such that \\[ \\int_{\\R^3}\\rho(x)\\dd x=1,\\qquad \\rho\\in C_c^\\infty(\\R^3),\\qquad \\sqrt{\\rho}\\in C_c^\\infty(\\R^3), \\] with the following properties. For \\(\\mu=\\rho\\dd x\\), the value \\(\\E(\\mu)\\) is finite and is attained by a coupling in \\(\\Pi(\\mu,\\mu,\\mu)\\), but every pair of Borel maps \\(T_2,T_3:\\R^3\\to\\R^3\\) satisfying \\((T_2)_\\#\\mu=(T_3)_\\#\\mu=\\mu\\) obeys \\[ \\int_{\\R^3}c\\bigl(x,T_2(x),T_3(x)\\bigr)\\dd\\mu(x)>\\E(\\mu). \\] The inequality permits the left-hand side to be infinite.","sourceOffset":1762,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"","label":"prop:certificate","text":"There are pairwise disjoint open balls \\(U_0,U_k^+,U_k^-\\) centered at \\(0,a_k,-a_k\\), respectively, and a bounded continuous function \\(u:U\\to\\R\\), where \\[ U=U_0\\cup U_1^+\\cup U_1^-\\cup U_2^+\\cup U_2^-, \\] such that \\begin{equation} c(x_1,x_2,x_3)\\ge\\sum_{i=1}^3u(x_i) \\quad\\text{on }U^3. \\end{equation} Equality holds precisely at the triples in \\(U^3\\) which, after a permutation, have the form \\begin{equation} (X_k(y),y,Z_k(y))\\in U_0\\times U_k^+\\times U_k^-, \\qquad y\\in U_k^+,\\quad k\\in\\{1,2\\}. \\end{equation} The maps \\(X_k,Z_k\\) are diffeomorphisms on \\(U_k^+\\), with \\[ X_k(U_k^+)\\subset U_0,\\qquad Z_k(U_k^+)\\subset U_k^-. \\]","sourceOffset":15959,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/373.md","scopeText":"The Monge ansatz asks whether an optimal multi-marginal transport plan can be induced by maps from one marginal. For the three-marginal Coulomb cost in $\\mathbb R^3$, the formalization constructs a smooth compactly supported probability density, with smooth compactly supported square root, for which no pair of measure-preserving Borel maps attains the Kantorovich minimum.\n\nNevertheless, the Monge and Kantorovich infima are equal: preserving maps with finite costs approach the minimum. This is the three-dimensional Coulomb result; the paper's inverse-power extensions in every dimension are outside this statement.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/373.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":40344,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-counterexample-to-the-Monge-ansatz-for-the-three-marginal-Coulomb-cost-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"374-01","slug":"Sharp-One-Third-Stability-of-Brenier-Maps-September-25-2026","familyId":"374","title":"Sharp One-Third Stability of Brenier Maps","abstract":"For the uniform probability measure ρ on a compact convex body in ℝd, d ≥ 2, quadratic optimal transport maps are one-third Hölder continuous in $L^2(\\rho)$ with respect to the target's 2-Wasserstein distance. The constant is uniform over all targets supported in a fixed compact set, and the exponent is optimal. A three-atom family on a fixed cube disproves Letrouit's conjectured uniform square-root estimate.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"For uniform source measure ρ on a compact convex body with interior in dimension at least two, quadratic optimal transport maps satisfy $\\|T_\\mu-T_\\nu\\|_{L^2(\\rho)}\\le C W_2(\\mu,\\nu)^{1/3}$ uniformly over targets in a fixed compact set. The exponent is sharp, even for three-atom targets, disproving Letrouit's conjectured square-root bound.","pdfPath":"preprints/Sharp-One-Third-Stability-of-Brenier-Maps-September-25-2026/article.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Sharp-One-Third-Stability-of-Brenier-Maps-September-25-2026/article.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Sharp-One-Third-Stability-of-Brenier-Maps-September-25-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Sharp-One-Third-Stability-of-Brenier-Maps-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","preprints/Sharp-One-Third-Stability-of-Brenier-Maps-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/introduction.tex","preprints/Sharp-One-Third-Stability-of-Brenier-Maps-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/sharpness.tex","preprints/Sharp-One-Third-Stability-of-Brenier-Maps-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/cells.tex","preprints/Sharp-One-Third-Stability-of-Brenier-Maps-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/moments.tex","preprints/Sharp-One-Third-Stability-of-Brenier-Maps-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/finite_stability.tex","preprints/Sharp-One-Third-Stability-of-Brenier-Maps-September-25-2026/build/source/sections/gradient.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Sharp-One-Third-Stability-of-Brenier-Maps-September-25-2026/build/source/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"For the uniform probability measure \\(\\rho\\) on a compact convex body in \\(\\R^d\\), \\(d\\ge2\\), quadratic optimal transport maps are one-third H\\\"older continuous in \\(L^2(\\rho)\\) with respect to the target's \\(2\\)-Wasserstein distance. The constant is uniform over all targets supported in a fixed compact set, and the exponent is optimal. A three-atom family on a fixed cube disproves Letrouit's conjectured uniform square-root estimate.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":501},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History and significance","sourceOffset":2276},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Sharpness and the finite-cell argument","sourceOffset":5877},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Conventions","sourceOffset":8810},{"kind":"section","title":"Sharpness on a fixed cube","sourceOffset":9279},{"kind":"section","title":"Finite affine potentials and prescribed cell masses","sourceOffset":16944},{"kind":"section","title":"A uniform estimate for cell moments","sourceOffset":27066},{"kind":"section","title":"Moving sites with fixed masses","sourceOffset":36686},{"kind":"section","title":"From potentials to maps","sourceOffset":43572}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"main:stability","text":"For every \\(K,Y\\) as above there is a finite constant \\(C(K,Y)\\) such that \\[ \\|T_\\mu-T_\\nu\\|_{L^2(\\rho)} \\le C(K,Y)\\,W_2(\\mu,\\nu)^{1/3} \\qquad\\bigl(\\mu,\\nu\\in\\Prob(Y)\\bigr). \\] The targets may be atomic, singular, or absolutely continuous. The exponent \\(1/3\\) cannot be replaced by any larger exponent in general, even for \\(K=Y=[-1,1]^d\\) and targets with exactly three atoms.","sourceOffset":1574,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Optimality of the exponent","label":"sharpness:exponent","text":"For every fixed $d\\ge2$, let $K=Y=[-1,1]^d$ and $\\rho=2^{-d}1_K\\,dx$. For every $\\alpha>1/3$, \\[ \\sup_{\\substack{\\mu,\\nu\\in\\Prob(Y)\\\\ \\mu\\ne\\nu}} \\frac{\\|T_\\mu-T_\\nu\\|_{L^2(\\rho)}}{W_2(\\mu,\\nu)^\\alpha} =+\\infty. \\] The supremum remains infinite when both targets are required to have exactly three atoms.","sourceOffset":11454,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/374.md","scopeText":"The formalization proves one-third Hölder stability of Brenier maps from the uniform measure on a compact convex body with nonempty interior in $\\mathbb R^d$, for every $d\\ge2$. For targets supported in one fixed nonempty compact set, the $L^2$ distance between the unique quadratic optimal maps is at most a constant times the one-third power of the targets' $2$-Wasserstein distance. The constant is uniform over those targets.\n\nIt also proves optimality of the exponent: on a fixed cube, pairs of three-atom target measures violate every analogous bound with exponent greater than $1/3$. In particular, the conjectured uniform square-root estimate fails.","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/374.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":53898,"sourceFileCount":7,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Sharp-One-Third-Stability-of-Brenier-Maps-September-25-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"375-01","slug":"De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026","familyId":"375","title":"A positive resolution of De Giorgi's conjecture in dimension eight","abstract":"We resolve De Giorgi's conjecture positively in dimension eight: every entire C2 solution $u:\\mathbb R^8\\to(-1,1)$ of $\\Delta u=u^3-u$ with an everywhere positive directional derivative is a planar heteroclinic. We prove that every stable solution $v:\\mathbb R^7\\to[-1,1]$ of this equation is a constant well or a planar heteroclinic, without an energy-growth assumption.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves De Giorgi's conjecture at its sharp dimension-eight endpoint: every entire C2 solution $u:\\mathbb R^8\\to(-1,1)$ of $\\Delta u=u^3-u$ that is strictly increasing in one direction depends on only one linear coordinate. A stronger theorem classifies all stable entire solutions $v:\\mathbb R^7\\to[-1,1]$ as constant wells or planar transitions, without an energy-growth assumption.","pdfPath":"preprints/De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026/article.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026/article.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","preprints/De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026/build/sections/00-classical.tex","preprints/De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026/build/sections/02-monotone.tex","preprints/De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026/build/sections/01-strategy.tex","preprints/De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026/build/sections/01-diffuse.tex","preprints/De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026/build/sections/02-finite-density.tex","preprints/De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026/build/sections/03-reach.tex","preprints/De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026/build/sections/04-interface.tex","preprints/De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026/build/sections/05-growth.tex","preprints/De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026/build/sections/06-radial.tex","preprints/De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026/build/sections/09-dimension-nine.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We resolve De Giorgi's conjecture positively in dimension eight: every entire $C^2$ solution $u:\\R^8\\to(-1,1)$ of $\\Delta u=u^3-u$ with an everywhere positive directional derivative is a planar heteroclinic. We prove that every stable solution $v:\\R^7\\to[-1,1]$ of this equation is a constant well or a planar heteroclinic, without an energy-growth assumption.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":406},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The stable equation","sourceOffset":3378},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From stable end profiles to the monotone theorem","sourceOffset":5359},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The finite-density step","sourceOffset":8120},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Organization and conventions","sourceOffset":9688},{"kind":"section","title":"Two classical estimates for the quartic equation","sourceOffset":11227},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Modica's gradient estimate","sourceOffset":11838},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Normalized energy and monotonicity","sourceOffset":12858},{"kind":"part","title":"The geometric deduction","sourceOffset":14069},{"kind":"section","title":"From stable end profiles to the monotone theorem","sourceOffset":14101},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Stable end profiles and a variational comparison","sourceOffset":15509},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Exact convergence to a minimizing boundary","sourceOffset":23121}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Monotone rigidity in dimension eight","label":"thm:monotone","text":"Let $u\\in C^2(\\R^8,(-1,1))$ satisfy \\[ \\Delta u=u^3-u,\\qquad \\partial_8u>0\\quad\\text{in }\\R^8. \\] Then there are $e\\in\\mathbb S^7$ with $e_8>0$ and $c\\in\\R$ such that \\[ u(x)=\\tanh\\!\\left(\\frac{e\\cdot x-c}{\\sqrt2}\\right) \\qquad\\text{for every }x\\in\\R^8. \\]","sourceOffset":2719,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Stable rigidity in dimension seven","label":"thm:stable","text":"Let $v\\in C^2(\\R^7,[-1,1])$ be a stable solution of $\\Delta v=v^3-v$. Then either $v\\equiv1$, $v\\equiv-1$, or \\[ v(x)=\\tanh\\!\\left(\\frac{a\\cdot x-b}{\\sqrt2}\\right) \\qquad\\text{for some }a\\in\\mathbb S^6,\\ b\\in\\R. \\] No energy-growth assumption is required.","sourceOffset":4287,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":333615,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":11,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/De-Giorgis-conjecture-in-dimension-eight-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"376-01","slug":"Finite-Instructions-and-Solenoidal-Shear-Flows-September-27-2026","familyId":"376","title":"Finite Instructions and Solenoidal Shear Flows","abstract":"We realize finite reciprocal affine instruction maps by smooth incompressible shear flows on a flat three-torus. Applied to a reversible one-head recorder with a finite transition table, this gives a complete machine-to-fluid construction: a fixed particle enters a fixed open strip exactly when a given machine halts, with zero initial velocity, zero pressure, and a solenoidal mean-zero force that is periodic after initialization. The construction acts on full closed rectangles and controls every intermediate trajectory. Separate heights resolve overlap between sources and targets, and an endpoint-size estimate controls all excursions independently of the reciprocal scaling factor.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs viscous incompressible flows starting from rest on a fixed flat three-dimensional domain that perform universal computation under smooth external forcing. A terminating compiler turns a Turing machine and input into a finite program for the force, so a designated particle reaches a fixed region exactly when the machine halts. The viscosity is fixed, positive and computable.","pdfPath":"preprints/Finite-Instructions-and-Solenoidal-Shear-Flows-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-Instructions-and-Solenoidal-Shear-Flows-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Finite-Instructions-and-Solenoidal-Shear-Flows-September-27-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Finite-Instructions-and-Solenoidal-Shear-Flows-September-27-2026/build/main.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Finite-Instructions-and-Solenoidal-Shear-Flows-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We realize finite reciprocal affine instruction maps by smooth incompressible shear flows on a flat three-torus. Applied to a reversible one-head recorder with a finite transition table, this gives a complete machine-to-fluid construction: a fixed particle enters a fixed open strip exactly when a given machine halts, with zero initial velocity, zero pressure, and a solenoidal mean-zero force that is periodic after initialization. The construction acts on full closed rectangles and controls every intermediate trajectory. Separate heights resolve overlap between sources and targets, and an endpoint-size estimate controls all excursions independently of the reciprocal scaling factor.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"From finite instructions to a fluid experiment","sourceOffset":735},{"kind":"section","title":"A recorder with an inverse on its full domain","sourceOffset":6833},{"kind":"section","title":"A smooth realization by seven shear stages","sourceOffset":14372},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The excursion estimate","sourceOffset":14743},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The realization theorem","sourceOffset":16241},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Observation bounds","sourceOffset":30120},{"kind":"section","title":"Closed rectangles, initialization, and the all-time event","sourceOffset":31282},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An affine map for every auxiliary instruction","sourceOffset":31546},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Loading the fixed label","sourceOffset":37360},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Completion of the fluid and observation statements","sourceOffset":39413},{"kind":"section","title":"Optional recorder and schedule changes","sourceOffset":43616},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Keeping the return mark at checkpoints","sourceOffset":43883}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Initialized computation by a solenoidal force","label":"thm:initialized","text":"Fix a positive computable viscosity $\\nu$ on the unit flat torus $\\T^3$. There is an algorithm taking a deterministic one-tape machine $M$ with head moves in $\\{-1,0,1\\}$ and a finite input word $w$ to an effective smooth force $f_{M,w}$ with these properties. \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\roman*),itemsep=2pt] \\item The force is divergence free and has zero spatial mean. Equation \\eqref{eq:NS} has a global smooth solution $(u,p)=(U,0)$, unique in the classical class just specified on every finite time interval. \\item All mixed derivatives of $U$ and $f_{M,w}$ are globally bounded, and the kinetic energy is uniformly bounded. Both fields have period one for $t\\ge1$ and vanish on a neighborhood of every integer time. The period-one mechanism after initialization depends on $M$ alone. \\item With fixed label $a_*=(1/8,3/8,1/2)$ and fixed open strip $O=\\{(x,y,z):1/2<x<1\\}$, the trajectory enters $O$ at a finite time if and only if $M$ halts on $w$, including an initially halted machine. \\end{enumerate} For arbitrary fixed real $\\nu>0$, the same formulas and conclusions hold; the evaluation algorithm is then relative to that parameter.","sourceOffset":5020,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Reciprocal affine maps by shears","label":"thm:shears","text":"For $1\\le i\\le N$, let $P_i,Q_i$ be closed axis-parallel rectangles with rational endpoints and positive side lengths in the planar chart of $\\T_L^3$. Let their centers be $p_i,q_i$, and suppose \\[ F_i(X)=q_i+\\diag(\\lambda_i,\\lambda_i^{-1})(X-p_i), \\quad F_i(P_i)=Q_i, \\quad\\lambda_i\\in\\Q_{>0}. \\] Assume the following: \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\roman*),nosep] \\item The $P_i$ are pairwise positively separated, and the $Q_i$ are pairwise positively separated. \\item Every coordinate half-width of every $P_i$ and $Q_i$ is at most a given rational $h>0$. \\item All $p_i,q_i$ belong to a closed rectangle $K$ with rational endpoints, and $K+[-2h,2h]^2$ is compactly contained in the planar chart. \\item The coding height $z_0$ is rational and lies inside the vertical chart. \\end{enumerate} There is an effectively constructed smooth velocity $V$ on $\\R\\times\\T_L^3$ with the following properties. \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\alph*),itemsep=2pt] \\item It is one-periodic in time and vanishes near every integer time. Every mixed space and time derivative has an effective uniform bound. \\item $\\divg V=0$, $\\int_{\\T_L^3}V=0$, and $(V\\cdot\\nabla)V=0$. \\item Its period map is $(X,z)\\mapsto(F_i(X),z)$ on an effectively specified positive neighborhood of each $P_i\\times\\{z_0\\}$. \\item Starting from $(X,z_0)$ with $X\\in P_i$, the four scaling stages stay within horizontal sup distance $2h$ of $p_i$. The next stage follows $((1-\\theta)p_i+\\theta q_i)+d$, where $0\\le\\theta\\le1$ and $|d|_\\infty\\le h$. Th","sourceOffset":16555,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/376.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":49159,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":4,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Finite-Instructions-and-Solenoidal-Shear-Flows-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"376-02","slug":"A-Fixed-Particle-Test-for-Computation-in-a-Forced-Viscous-Flow-September-27-2026","familyId":"376","title":"A Fixed Particle Test for Computation in a Forced Viscous Flow","abstract":"We construct smooth external forces for incompressible Navier–Stokes flow on a fixed flat three-torus at any fixed positive computable viscosity, from zero initial velocity, such that a fixed particle enters a fixed open set exactly when a prescribed Turing machine halts. Every mixed derivative of the force and velocity is bounded and square-integrable in time in spatial supremum norm. The construction uses the machine's ordinary instructions, records their history in a third coordinate, and compensates for finer spatial gates by longer time steps.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs viscous incompressible flows starting from rest on a fixed flat three-dimensional domain that perform universal computation under smooth external forcing. A terminating compiler turns a Turing machine and input into a finite program for the force, so a designated particle reaches a fixed region exactly when the machine halts. The viscosity is fixed, positive and computable.","pdfPath":"preprints/A-Fixed-Particle-Test-for-Computation-in-a-Forced-Viscous-Flow-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Fixed-Particle-Test-for-Computation-in-a-Forced-Viscous-Flow-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/A-Fixed-Particle-Test-for-Computation-in-a-Forced-Viscous-Flow-September-27-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/A-Fixed-Particle-Test-for-Computation-in-a-Forced-Viscous-Flow-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/A-Fixed-Particle-Test-for-Computation-in-a-Forced-Viscous-Flow-September-27-2026/build/analytic-tools.tex","preprints/A-Fixed-Particle-Test-for-Computation-in-a-Forced-Viscous-Flow-September-27-2026/build/analytic-appendix.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/A-Fixed-Particle-Test-for-Computation-in-a-Forced-Viscous-Flow-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct smooth external forces for incompressible Navier--Stokes flow on a fixed flat three-torus at any fixed positive computable viscosity, from zero initial velocity, such that a fixed particle enters a fixed open set exactly when a prescribed Turing machine halts. Every mixed derivative of the force and velocity is bounded and square-integrable in time in spatial supremum norm. The construction uses the machine's ordinary instructions, records their history in a third coordinate, and compensates for finer spatial gates by longer time steps.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":601},{"kind":"section","title":"The statement and the history invariant","sourceOffset":5290},{"kind":"section","title":"A machine instruction as a planar motion","sourceOffset":10662},{"kind":"section","title":"Recording and selecting the instruction","sourceOffset":15981},{"kind":"section","title":"Slowing the gates and obtaining the force","sourceOffset":21924},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Bounds for the prescribed velocity","sourceOffset":22219},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Uniqueness for the constructed torus flow","sourceOffset":23971},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Solenoidal forcing with the same velocity","sourceOffset":25280},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Effective evaluation without a simulated execution","sourceOffset":29391},{"kind":"section","title":"Comparison on noncompact domains and Fourier identification","sourceOffset":34245},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The precise comparison class","sourceOffset":34588},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Identifying continuous periodic functions","sourceOffset":39538}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"There is an algorithm taking a deterministic single-tape machine $M$ and a finite word $w$ to an effective smooth mean-zero force $f_{M,w}$ for \\eqref{eq:NS}, with the following properties. \\begin{enumerate} \\item The equation has a global smooth solution $u_{M,w}$, unique among classical solutions on every finite time interval. \\item For every time order $h\\geq0$ and spatial multi-index $\\mu$, both \\[ t\\longmapsto\\|\\partial_t^h\\partial_X^\\mu u_{M,w}(t)\\|_\\infty, \\qquad t\\longmapsto\\|\\partial_t^h\\partial_X^\\mu f_{M,w}(t)\\|_\\infty \\] belong to $L^\\infty(0,\\infty)\\cap L^2(0,\\infty)$. \\item The particle initially at $a_*=(1/8,3/8,0)$ enters \\[ O=\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\T^3:1/2<x<1\\} \\] at a finite time if and only if $M$ halts on $w$. \\end{enumerate} The force may additionally be chosen divergence free, with unchanged velocity and observation. Constants in the derivative estimates may depend on $M,w,\\nu$ and the derivative orders. After a loading interval, the prescribed velocity mechanism depends on $M$ but not on $w$.","sourceOffset":6920,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"","label":null,"text":"No algorithm decides the fixed particle event for every force description produced by this construction.","sourceOffset":33434,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/376.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":43098,"sourceFileCount":3,"statementCount":2,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/A-Fixed-Particle-Test-for-Computation-in-a-Forced-Viscous-Flow-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"376-03","slug":"Universal-Computation-with-Eventually-Stationary-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026","familyId":"376","title":"Universal Computation with Eventually Stationary Navier–Stokes Forcing","abstract":"At every fixed positive computable viscosity, we construct smooth mean-zero forces on the flat three-torus that become stationary after time one and make a fixed particle, initially in a fluid at rest, enter a fixed open set exactly when a prescribed Turing machine halts. The force has bounded derivatives of every order and a finite effective description. A reversible recording table is realized on whole planar rectangles by Hamiltonian motions, then driven by a mean-zero spatial clock. Separate choices give periodic forcing from time zero or a force whose derivatives are square-integrable in time.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs viscous incompressible flows starting from rest on a fixed flat three-dimensional domain that perform universal computation under smooth external forcing. A terminating compiler turns a Turing machine and input into a finite program for the force, so a designated particle reaches a fixed region exactly when the machine halts. The viscosity is fixed, positive and computable.","pdfPath":"preprints/Universal-Computation-with-Eventually-Stationary-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Universal-Computation-with-Eventually-Stationary-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Universal-Computation-with-Eventually-Stationary-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Universal-Computation-with-Eventually-Stationary-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026/build/main.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Universal-Computation-with-Eventually-Stationary-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"At every fixed positive computable viscosity, we construct smooth mean-zero forces on the flat three-torus that become stationary after time one and make a fixed particle, initially in a fluid at rest, enter a fixed open set exactly when a prescribed Turing machine halts. The force has bounded derivatives of every order and a finite effective description. A reversible recording table is realized on whole planar rectangles by Hamiltonian motions, then driven by a mean-zero spatial clock. Separate choices give periodic forcing from time zero or a force whose derivatives are square-integrable in time.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction and result","sourceOffset":650},{"kind":"section","title":"Effective profiles and analytic realization","sourceOffset":7770},{"kind":"section","title":"A finite reversible rule table","sourceOffset":13075},{"kind":"section","title":"An area-preserving planar processor","sourceOffset":19263},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Separated radix coordinates","sourceOffset":21871},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Localized Hamiltonian motions","sourceOffset":25947},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Routing without collisions","sourceOffset":28209},{"kind":"section","title":"An autonomous spatial clock and a steady force","sourceOffset":37709},{"kind":"section","title":"A slow clock with infinite accumulated time","sourceOffset":43208}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Eventually steady forcing","label":"thm:main","text":"Fix a positive computable viscosity $\\nu$. There is an effective procedure taking a deterministic Turing machine and finite input to a body force $f$ on the flat torus $\\mathbb T^3$, such that: \\begin{enumerate} \\item $f$ is smooth on $\\mathbb T^3\\times[0,\\infty)$, has zero spatial mean, and is independent of time for $t\\ge1$. Every spatial, temporal, and mixed derivative of $f$ is bounded on this entire domain. \\item With initial velocity zero, the Navier--Stokes equation has a global smooth solution, unique in the classical class on every finite time interval. Its kinetic energy is bounded uniformly in time. \\item For the fixed particle label and fixed open set \\[ P=(1/4,1/4,0),\\qquad \\mathcal O=\\{(X,Y,Z)\\in\\mathbb T^3:1/32<Y<1/8\\}, \\] the material trajectory from $P$ enters $\\mathcal O$ at a finite time if and only if the machine halts on the input. \\end{enumerate} The force is specified by a finite formula which does not simulate the chosen execution. The construction is also uniform as a formula in an arbitrary positive parameter $\\nu$.","sourceOffset":5302,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"A smooth planar processor","label":"thm:planar-processor","text":"From the machine and input one can effectively construct a smooth field $V(s,X,Y)$ on $\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb T^2$ with the following properties. \\begin{enumerate} \\item $V$ is $1$-periodic in $s$, vanishes on neighborhoods of integer $s$, is divergence free in $(X,Y)$, and has zero spatial mean. Its planar support is a compact subset of the open unit-square chart. It has a smooth periodic Hamiltonian potential supported in that chart. The same formula, extended by zero, defines a compactly supported field on $\\R^2$ with the same conclusions below. The construction also supplies a finite list of closed rational source rectangles $D_r$, target rectangles $H_r$ and affine maps $F_r:D_r\\to H_r$, each with positive reciprocal diagonal linear part. The two rectangle families are separately disjoint. These maps implement the symbolic branches under an explicit tape encoding. The period map agrees with $F_r$ on a positive neighborhood of $D_r$, with an effective lower bound for the neighborhood radius. All its derivatives have effective finite uniform bounds. \\item Starting from $p=(1/4,1/4)$ at $s=0$, the trajectory at successive integer times encodes every transition of the compiled machine, until its terminal state is reached. All these trajectory segments lie in \\[ J_*=[1/8,7/8]\\times[1/32,7/8]. \\] The trajectory reaches the fixed strip \\[ H=\\{(X,Y)\\in\\mathbb T^2:1/32<Y<1/8\\} \\] at a finite time if and only if the original machine halts. If the machine does not halt, $3/16\\le Y\\","sourceOffset":19764,"truncated":true}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/376.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":46814,"sourceFileCount":1,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Universal-Computation-with-Eventually-Stationary-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"376-04","slug":"Geometric-Programs-for-Solenoidal-Forcing-September-27-2026","familyId":"376","title":"Geometric Programs for Solenoidal Forcing","abstract":"We construct smooth solenoidal mean-zero forces, periodic from time zero, for which a fixed particle on a flat three-torus reaches a fixed open strip exactly when a given machine halts. The initial fluid velocity and the pressure are zero. We also realize positive diagonal maps on coding sheets by incompressible shears, with explicit normal compensation for changes of planar area, and reciprocal maps on whole boxes of positive thickness. Complete local inverses, initialization rules, and intermediate trajectory bounds connect these geometric constructions to finite computations.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs viscous incompressible flows starting from rest on a fixed flat three-dimensional domain that perform universal computation under smooth external forcing. A terminating compiler turns a Turing machine and input into a finite program for the force, so a designated particle reaches a fixed region exactly when the machine halts. The viscosity is fixed, positive and computable.","pdfPath":"preprints/Geometric-Programs-for-Solenoidal-Forcing-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Geometric-Programs-for-Solenoidal-Forcing-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Geometric-Programs-for-Solenoidal-Forcing-September-27-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Geometric-Programs-for-Solenoidal-Forcing-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Geometric-Programs-for-Solenoidal-Forcing-September-27-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/Geometric-Programs-for-Solenoidal-Forcing-September-27-2026/build/analytic-imports.tex","preprints/Geometric-Programs-for-Solenoidal-Forcing-September-27-2026/build/body.tex","preprints/Geometric-Programs-for-Solenoidal-Forcing-September-27-2026/build/reciprocal-template.tex","preprints/Geometric-Programs-for-Solenoidal-Forcing-September-27-2026/build/clocks.tex","preprints/Geometric-Programs-for-Solenoidal-Forcing-September-27-2026/build/recorder-import.tex","preprints/Geometric-Programs-for-Solenoidal-Forcing-September-27-2026/build/normal-figure.tex","preprints/Geometric-Programs-for-Solenoidal-Forcing-September-27-2026/build/interpreter.tex","preprints/Geometric-Programs-for-Solenoidal-Forcing-September-27-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Geometric-Programs-for-Solenoidal-Forcing-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct smooth solenoidal mean-zero forces, periodic from time zero, for which a fixed particle on a flat three-torus reaches a fixed open strip exactly when a given machine halts. The initial fluid velocity and the pressure are zero. We also realize positive diagonal maps on coding sheets by incompressible shears, with explicit normal compensation for changes of planar area, and reciprocal maps on whole boxes of positive thickness. Complete local inverses, initialization rules, and intermediate trajectory bounds connect these geometric constructions to finite computations.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The geometry of a finite fluid instruction","sourceOffset":631},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Main results","sourceOffset":1985},{"kind":"subsection","title":"How the constructions fit together","sourceOffset":6232},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Ideas and relation to earlier work","sourceOffset":7922},{"kind":"section","title":"From transverse pulses to fluid motion","sourceOffset":12506},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Transverse pulses and classical uniqueness","sourceOffset":12575},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The reciprocal geometric input","sourceOffset":15397},{"kind":"section","title":"A common geometric and symbolic toolkit","sourceOffset":17035},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Profiles that preserve the exact shear paths","sourceOffset":17470},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The seven-stage construction and its pulse convention","sourceOffset":21110},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A digit interface for full rule domains","sourceOffset":26176},{"kind":"section","title":"A compiler periodic from time zero","sourceOffset":30607}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Periodic initialization","label":"sv:periodic-theorem","text":"Fix a computable viscosity $\\nu>0$. From a deterministic one-tape machine, with head moves in $\\{-1,0,1\\}$, and a finite input, one can effectively construct a smooth force on $\\mathbb T_1^3$ such that: \\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\roman*),itemsep=2pt] \\item The force is divergence free, has zero spatial mean, and is one-periodic from time zero. Equation \\eqref{sv:NS} has a global smooth solution with pressure zero, unique in the stated classical class. \\item The velocity is one-periodic, vanishes near every integer time, has zero instantaneous self-advection, and has uniformly bounded kinetic energy. Every mixed derivative of force and velocity is globally bounded. \\item For the fixed label $a_*=(1/4,1/2,1/4)$ and fixed open strip $O=\\{1/2<x_1<7/8\\}$, the material trajectory enters $O$ at a finite real time exactly when the machine halts, including an initially halted machine. \\end{enumerate} The construction may use one Cartesian velocity component at a time. For arbitrary fixed real $\\nu>0$ the same formulas hold and are effective relative to that viscosity.","sourceOffset":2898,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"General diagonal maps on a sheet","label":"sv:sheet-theorem","text":"Let $P_i,Q_i\\subset[2,3]^2$, $1\\le i\\le N$, be closed rectangles with rational vertices and positive side lengths. Suppose the sources are pairwise positively separated and the targets are pairwise positively separated. Let $F_i:P_i\\to Q_i$ be the center-to-center affine map with any positive rational diagonal linear part, with $F_i(P_i)=Q_i$. For each computable $\\nu>0$ there is an effective smooth mean-zero solenoidal force on $\\mathbb T_{10}^3$, one-periodic from time zero, whose unique solution in the classical class has pressure zero and period map \\[ (y,2)\\longmapsto(F_i(y),2)\\qquad(y\\in P_i). \\] Its velocity is zero near integer times and has zero self-advection; all mixed derivatives of force and velocity are bounded. No condition is imposed on source--target intersections. For $N=0$ use zero.","sourceOffset":4643,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/376.md","scopeText":"The formalization realizes finite families of prescribed positive diagonal maps on coding sheets by smooth incompressible flows on a flat three-torus. At every positive computable viscosity, it supplies a solution starting from rest with zero pressure and a force that is divergence free, has spatial mean zero, and is one-periodic from time zero. Each map acts on every point of its source sheet, and the construction provides effective bounds for all mixed derivatives.\n\nThis selected statement covers the sheet programs. The paper's fixed-particle halting detector and its reciprocal maps on solid boxes are outside its scope.\n\nThe formalization proves that a fluid particle can detect arbitrary finite-machine halting using alternating-coordinate memory in smooth incompressible Navier–Stokes flo","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/376.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":109518,"sourceFileCount":10,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Geometric-Programs-for-Solenoidal-Forcing-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"376-05","slug":"Prefix-Instructions-and-Incompressible-Flows-September-27-2026","familyId":"376","title":"Prefix Instructions and Incompressible Flows","abstract":"Finite prefix instructions may change area and erase information. We give explicit history processors and smooth incompressible motions that retain that information and realize every instruction on its full domain. A first application assigns each machine and finite input a smooth mean-zero force on the flat unit three-torus, at any fixed positive computable viscosity. The solution starts from rest; a fixed particle enters a fixed open strip exactly when the machine halts. The force repeats with period one after an initial loading interval. We then prove alternative realizations using full boxes, normal compensation, invariant planes, and a spatial clock. The constructions specify their initialization, comparison class, derivative bounds, and continuous-time observation. Exact formulas and effective cutoffs provide finite descriptions of the fields and all their derivatives.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs viscous incompressible flows starting from rest on a fixed flat three-dimensional domain that perform universal computation under smooth external forcing. A terminating compiler turns a Turing machine and input into a finite program for the force, so a designated particle reaches a fixed region exactly when the machine halts. The viscosity is fixed, positive and computable.","pdfPath":"preprints/Prefix-Instructions-and-Incompressible-Flows-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Prefix-Instructions-and-Incompressible-Flows-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Prefix-Instructions-and-Incompressible-Flows-September-27-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Prefix-Instructions-and-Incompressible-Flows-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Prefix-Instructions-and-Incompressible-Flows-September-27-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/Prefix-Instructions-and-Incompressible-Flows-September-27-2026/build/principal-theorem.tex","preprints/Prefix-Instructions-and-Incompressible-Flows-September-27-2026/build/analysis.tex","preprints/Prefix-Instructions-and-Incompressible-Flows-September-27-2026/build/sheet-geometry.tex","preprints/Prefix-Instructions-and-Incompressible-Flows-September-27-2026/build/first-simulation.tex","preprints/Prefix-Instructions-and-Incompressible-Flows-September-27-2026/build/full-boxes.tex","preprints/Prefix-Instructions-and-Incompressible-Flows-September-27-2026/build/planes-and-histories.tex","preprints/Prefix-Instructions-and-Incompressible-Flows-September-27-2026/build/prefix-realizations.tex","preprints/Prefix-Instructions-and-Incompressible-Flows-September-27-2026/build/compact-reciprocal.tex","preprints/Prefix-Instructions-and-Incompressible-Flows-September-27-2026/build/autonomous.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Prefix-Instructions-and-Incompressible-Flows-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"Finite prefix instructions may change area and erase information. We give explicit history processors and smooth incompressible motions that retain that information and realize every instruction on its full domain. A first application assigns each machine and finite input a smooth mean-zero force on the flat unit three-torus, at any fixed positive computable viscosity. The solution starts from rest; a fixed particle enters a fixed open strip exactly when the machine halts. The force repeats with period one after an initial loading interval. We then prove alternative realizations using full boxes, normal compensation, invariant planes, and a spatial clock. The constructions specify their initialization, comparison class, derivative bounds, and continuous-time observation. Exact formulas and effective cutoffs provide finite descriptions of the fields and all their derivatives.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":933},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The fluid problem and a first result","sourceOffset":2777},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Antecedents and the role of the present constructions","sourceOffset":5513},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy and reading order","sourceOffset":9123},{"kind":"section","title":"Analytic classes and changes of physical coordinates","sourceOffset":10657},{"kind":"section","title":"Prefix maps with changing area","sourceOffset":21182},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From prefix cylinders to closed rectangles","sourceOffset":22174},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Local motion and normal compensation","sourceOffset":25446},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Closing the fluid argument","sourceOffset":30056},{"kind":"section","title":"The first initialized simulation","sourceOffset":32897},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History interspersed among work symbols","sourceOffset":33367},{"kind":"section","title":"Prescribing a positive thickness","sourceOffset":38024}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Interspersed history on moving sheets","label":"thm:sa-interleaved","text":"At fixed positive computable viscosity, every machine and finite input effectively determine a smooth mean-zero general force on the unit $\\mathbb T^3$, with all mixed derivatives bounded and period one for $t\\ge1$. Its zero-data solution is globally smooth and unique in the classical periodic comparison class, with uniformly bounded kinetic energy. Its material trajectory from $(1/8,1/4,1/4)$ enters $\\{x:1/2<x_1<1\\}$ at some finite time exactly when the machine halts. The repeated template depends only on the table, and the input enters only the loading pulse.","sourceOffset":4360,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Projection on a flat torus","label":"prop:projection-l2","text":"An effective smooth mean-zero force $g$ on $\\T_L^3$ has an effective mean-zero potential $\\phi$ satisfying $\\Delta\\phi=\\operatorname{div}g$. Replacing $(g,p)$ by $(g-\\nabla\\phi,p-\\phi)$ preserves velocity and makes the force solenoidal. Uniform mixed-derivative bounds, temporal $L^2$ bounds of their spatial suprema, and separately imposed time periodicity, eventual stationarity, or orderwise decay bounds are preserved.","sourceOffset":18720,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/376.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":194995,"sourceFileCount":11,"statementCount":14,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Prefix-Instructions-and-Incompressible-Flows-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[{"source":"preprints/Prefix-Instructions-and-Incompressible-Flows-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","include":"glyphtounicode-cmex","category":"tex-system-font-map"}],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"376-06","slug":"Computation-under-Rapidly-Vanishing-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026","familyId":"376","title":"Computation under Rapidly Vanishing Navier–Stokes Forcing","abstract":"At any fixed positive computable viscosity, smooth forces can make a fixed fluid particle detect the halting of an arbitrary machine, starting from rest, while every mixed derivative of the force and velocity decreases faster than every inverse power of time. We give three complete memory constructions: compact moving curls, alternating fractional coordinates, and a periodic lattice on the flat three-torus. Each machine step takes one unit of physical time. The constructions retain earlier records at separated spatial scales and use an exact open detector with a shrinking signal.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs viscous incompressible flows starting from rest on a fixed flat three-dimensional domain that perform universal computation under smooth external forcing. A terminating compiler turns a Turing machine and input into a finite program for the force, so a designated particle reaches a fixed region exactly when the machine halts. The viscosity is fixed, positive and computable.","pdfPath":"preprints/Computation-under-Rapidly-Vanishing-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Computation-under-Rapidly-Vanishing-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Computation-under-Rapidly-Vanishing-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Computation-under-Rapidly-Vanishing-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Computation-under-Rapidly-Vanishing-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026/build/addressed.tex","preprints/Computation-under-Rapidly-Vanishing-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026/build/alternating.tex","preprints/Computation-under-Rapidly-Vanishing-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026/build/lattice.tex","preprints/Computation-under-Rapidly-Vanishing-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026/build/references.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Computation-under-Rapidly-Vanishing-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"At any fixed positive computable viscosity, smooth forces can make a fixed fluid particle detect the halting of an arbitrary machine, starting from rest, while every mixed derivative of the force and velocity decreases faster than every inverse power of time. We give three complete memory constructions: compact moving curls, alternating fractional coordinates, and a periodic lattice on the flat three-torus. Each machine step takes one unit of physical time. The constructions retain earlier records at separated spatial scales and use an exact open detector with a shrinking signal.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"The question and the three memories","sourceOffset":632},{"kind":"section","title":"Forces, solution classes, and effective profiles","sourceOffset":6502},{"kind":"subsection","title":"What the program can evaluate","sourceOffset":10877},{"kind":"section","title":"Addressed compact curls","sourceOffset":12653},{"kind":"subsection","title":"One transition on a complete finite configuration","sourceOffset":14292},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Reading the newest record while retaining the earlier ones","sourceOffset":16844},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Moving each address by a localized curl","sourceOffset":18861},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Why every fixed derivative decreases rapidly","sourceOffset":21318},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The particle reads its own current configuration","sourceOffset":24067},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite evaluation without executing the input","sourceOffset":25193},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A solenoidal force on the unit torus","sourceOffset":27022},{"kind":"section","title":"Alternating fractional memories","sourceOffset":30173}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Compact support and rapid decay","label":"thm:rapid","text":"Fix a positive computable viscosity $\\nu$. From a deterministic Turing machine $M$ and a finite input $w$ one can effectively construct a smooth force on $[0,\\infty)\\times\\R^3$, supported for all times in the fixed compact set \\[ K=[-2,1]\\times[-1,2]\\times[-1,1], \\] such that, for every pair of nonnegative integers $J,l$ and spatial multi-index $\\alpha$, \\begin{equation} \\sup_{t\\geq0,\\,x\\in\\R^3} (1+t)^J\\bigl|\\partial_t^l\\partial_x^\\alpha f(t,x)\\bigr|<\\infty. \\end{equation} The Navier--Stokes equation with this force and zero initial velocity has an explicit global smooth solution, unique in the finite-interval class \\eqref{eq:energy-class}. Its material trajectory from the origin satisfies \\begin{equation} M\\text{ halts on }w \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad X_1(t,0)<-1\\text{ for some }t\\geq0. \\end{equation} The velocity has the same fixed support and rapid mixed-derivative bounds. The pressure is zero. The force is a general body force. Because $K$ is fixed, the same bounds hold with $(1+t+|x|)^J$ in place of $(1+t)^J$.","sourceOffset":13115,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Solenoidal rapid forcing on the torus","label":"cor:rapid-torus","text":"Fix a positive computable viscosity. From any deterministic Turing machine $M$ and finite input $w$, one can effectively construct on the unit flat torus $\\T^3$ a smooth, solenoidal, spatially mean-zero force satisfying all the bounds \\eqref{eq:rapid-force-bound} with $\\T^3$ in place of $\\R^3$, with zero initial velocity, such that the unique smooth solution satisfies \\[ M\\text{ halts on }w \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\frac14<X_1(t,a_*)<\\frac38\\text{ for some }t\\geq0, \\qquad a_*=(1/2,1/4,1/2). \\] Here the inequality denotes the indicated open coordinate slab of the torus. Pressure is normalized to mean zero, and uniqueness holds in the classical periodic class of Section \\ref{sec:analytic}. The velocity and all its mixed derivatives also decrease faster than every inverse power of time.","sourceOffset":27248,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/376.md","scopeText":"The formalization realizes finite families of prescribed positive diagonal maps on coding sheets by smooth incompressible flows on a flat three-torus. At every positive computable viscosity, it supplies a solution starting from rest with zero pressure and a force that is divergence free, has spatial mean zero, and is one-periodic from time zero. Each map acts on every point of its source sheet, and the construction provides effective bounds for all mixed derivatives.\n\nThis selected statement covers the sheet programs. The paper's fixed-particle halting detector and its reciprocal maps on solid boxes are outside its scope.\n\nThe formalization proves that a fluid particle can detect arbitrary finite-machine halting using alternating-coordinate memory in smooth incompressible Navier–Stokes flo","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/376.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":65478,"sourceFileCount":5,"statementCount":5,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Computation-under-Rapidly-Vanishing-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"376-07","slug":"Velocity-Field-Detection-of-Computation-in-Forced-Navier-Stokes-Flows-September-27-2026","familyId":"376","title":"Velocity-Field Detection of Computation in Forced Navier–Stokes Flows","abstract":"We construct smooth forces for three-dimensional incompressible Navier–Stokes flow, from rest at any fixed positive computable viscosity, whose velocity field detects whether a prescribed machine halts. On the unit flat torus, a pointwise test of the third velocity component uses successively shorter stirring intervals in a fixed spatial region. On $\\mathbb R^2\\times\\mathbb T$, an integral test uses an expanding array of translation regions and a force with globally bounded mixed derivatives. The vertical velocity solves an advection–diffusion equation: short bursts control its pointwise error in the first construction, and a moving cutoff controls the total escaped mass in the second.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs viscous incompressible flows starting from rest on a fixed flat three-dimensional domain that perform universal computation under smooth external forcing. A terminating compiler turns a Turing machine and input into a finite program for the force, so a designated particle reaches a fixed region exactly when the machine halts. The viscosity is fixed, positive and computable.","pdfPath":"preprints/Velocity-Field-Detection-of-Computation-in-Forced-Navier-Stokes-Flows-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Velocity-Field-Detection-of-Computation-in-Forced-Navier-Stokes-Flows-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Velocity-Field-Detection-of-Computation-in-Forced-Navier-Stokes-Flows-September-27-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Velocity-Field-Detection-of-Computation-in-Forced-Navier-Stokes-Flows-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Velocity-Field-Detection-of-Computation-in-Forced-Navier-Stokes-Flows-September-27-2026/build/compact.tex","preprints/Velocity-Field-Detection-of-Computation-in-Forced-Navier-Stokes-Flows-September-27-2026/build/bounded.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Velocity-Field-Detection-of-Computation-in-Forced-Navier-Stokes-Flows-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct smooth forces for three-dimensional incompressible Navier--Stokes flow, from rest at any fixed positive computable viscosity, whose velocity field detects whether a prescribed machine halts. On the unit flat torus, a pointwise test of the third velocity component uses successively shorter stirring intervals in a fixed spatial region. On $\\R^2\\times\\T$, an integral test uses an expanding array of translation regions and a force with globally bounded mixed derivatives. The vertical velocity solves an advection--diffusion equation: short bursts control its pointwise error in the first construction, and a moving cutoff controls the total escaped mass in the second.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Observing the velocity rather than a particle","sourceOffset":728},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Background and the two mechanisms","sourceOffset":5239},{"kind":"section","title":"A scalar component of a viscous velocity","sourceOffset":9299},{"kind":"section","title":"Short stirring bursts on the torus","sourceOffset":11372},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An injection and a finite computation in each block","sourceOffset":14417},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Constructing the scalar and controlling old mass","sourceOffset":18200},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A controlled diffusion error during the burst","sourceOffset":23190},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Effective prescription and physical viscosity","sourceOffset":26727},{"kind":"section","title":"The fixed torus detector","sourceOffset":28715},{"kind":"section","title":"Bounded forcing on an expanding array","sourceOffset":31213},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Finite addresses and their transitions","sourceOffset":32213},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Disjoint translation gates at increasing scales","sourceOffset":35642}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"thm:main","text":"For each positive computable $\\nu$, a machine and a finite input effectively determine each of the following two forces. \\begin{enumerate} \\item On $\\T^3$, a smooth force has a unique global smooth solution in the torus class above, with pressure zero, for which \\eqref{eq:torus-event} holds exactly when the machine halts. The force is supported in $K\\times\\T$ for a compact $K\\subset(0,1)^2$ independent of time. Its derivatives are bounded on every finite time interval; their bounds may grow with that interval. \\item On $\\R^2\\times\\T$, a smooth force and all its mixed derivatives are globally bounded. On each finite time interval its horizontal support is compact. It has a unique global smooth solution in \\eqref{eq:comparison-class}, with pressure zero and integrable nonnegative third component. Event \\eqref{eq:plane-event} holds exactly when the machine halts. \\end{enumerate} The sets and thresholds in both observations are independent of the machine and input. The compact set $K$ in the first construction may depend on that instance, but is independent of time. Both force prescriptions install all the required local instructions, without executing the selected computation.","sourceOffset":3153,"truncated":false},{"kind":"theorem","name":"Injection, stirring and waiting","label":"thm:compact-detector","text":"For $V,p,E$ as above, choose either of the two pairs \\begin{equation} (d,H)=(1/32,10^9)\\quad\\hbox{or}\\quad(1/16,10^8). \\end{equation} There is a smooth force on $\\T^3$, effectively determined by $V,p$, for which zero initial velocity has a global smooth solution, unique in the classical torus class of Section \\ref{sec:introduction}, with pressure zero and the following properties. \\begin{enumerate} \\item If $\\Psi_k(p)\\in E$ for some integer $k\\ge0$, then $u_3(t,Y,z)>1/2$ for some finite $t$ and some $Y\\in E$. \\item If $\\dist_{\\T^2}(\\Psi_s(p),E)\\ge2d$ for every $s\\ge0$, then $u_3(t,Y,z)<1/2$ for every $t\\ge0$ and $Y\\in E$. \\end{enumerate} The third component is nonnegative. At viscosity one, its total horizontal mass is less than $10^{-11}$. The same conclusions and threshold hold for every positive computable viscosity, with the time and horizontal velocity rescaled as in \\eqref{eq:compact-viscosity}. The force is supported in the union of the horizontal support of $V$ and one fixed small neighborhood of $p$, times $\\T$. All smoothness and uniqueness assertions concern every finite time interval.","sourceOffset":12448,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":true,"scopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/376.md","scopeText":"The formalization realizes finite families of prescribed positive diagonal maps on coding sheets by smooth incompressible flows on a flat three-torus. At every positive computable viscosity, it supplies a solution starting from rest with zero pressure and a force that is divergence free, has spatial mean zero, and is one-periodic from time zero. Each map acts on every point of its source sheet, and the construction provides effective bounds for all mixed derivatives.\n\nThis selected statement covers the sheet programs. The paper's fixed-particle halting detector and its reciprocal maps on solid boxes are outside its scope.\n\nThe formalization proves that a fluid particle can detect arbitrary finite-machine halting using alternating-coordinate memory in smooth incompressible Navier–Stokes flo","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/376.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"official-formalization-listed-not-rebuilt","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":59819,"sourceFileCount":3,"statementCount":3,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Velocity-Field-Detection-of-Computation-in-Forced-Navier-Stokes-Flows-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"376-08","slug":"Scalar-Potentials-and-Slow-Clocks-for-Forced-Fluid-Computation-September-27-2026","familyId":"376","title":"Scalar Potentials and Slow Clocks for Forced Fluid Computation","abstract":"We construct smooth forces of fixed compact spatial support for three-dimensional incompressible Navier–Stokes flow from rest whose particle at the origin detects halting by entering a fixed half-space. Every mixed derivative of the force and velocity decays at rate $O((1+t)^{-1-j})$ for time order j. Three scalar potentials lift a coded rectangle, perform its instruction, and lower its image; an unbounded logarithmic clock supplies the decay. We also realize area-changing prefix instructions on an invariant torus plane, and construct a planar Hamiltonian processor admitting periodic forcing, stationary forcing after startup, and a velocity-field detector through a companion diffusion theorem.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs viscous incompressible flows starting from rest on a fixed flat three-dimensional domain that perform universal computation under smooth external forcing. A terminating compiler turns a Turing machine and input into a finite program for the force, so a designated particle reaches a fixed region exactly when the machine halts. The viscosity is fixed, positive and computable.","pdfPath":"preprints/Scalar-Potentials-and-Slow-Clocks-for-Forced-Fluid-Computation-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Scalar-Potentials-and-Slow-Clocks-for-Forced-Fluid-Computation-September-27-2026/manuscript.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Scalar-Potentials-and-Slow-Clocks-for-Forced-Fluid-Computation-September-27-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Scalar-Potentials-and-Slow-Clocks-for-Forced-Fluid-Computation-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Scalar-Potentials-and-Slow-Clocks-for-Forced-Fluid-Computation-September-27-2026/build/main-result.tex","preprints/Scalar-Potentials-and-Slow-Clocks-for-Forced-Fluid-Computation-September-27-2026/build/analytic.tex","preprints/Scalar-Potentials-and-Slow-Clocks-for-Forced-Fluid-Computation-September-27-2026/build/bcs-clocks.tex","preprints/Scalar-Potentials-and-Slow-Clocks-for-Forced-Fluid-Computation-September-27-2026/build/bcs-scalar.tex","preprints/Scalar-Potentials-and-Slow-Clocks-for-Forced-Fluid-Computation-September-27-2026/build/scalar-stages.tex","preprints/Scalar-Potentials-and-Slow-Clocks-for-Forced-Fluid-Computation-September-27-2026/build/bcs-clock-options.tex","preprints/Scalar-Potentials-and-Slow-Clocks-for-Forced-Fluid-Computation-September-27-2026/build/bcs-prefix.tex","preprints/Scalar-Potentials-and-Slow-Clocks-for-Forced-Fluid-Computation-September-27-2026/build/bcs-expanded.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Scalar-Potentials-and-Slow-Clocks-for-Forced-Fluid-Computation-September-27-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We construct smooth forces of fixed compact spatial support for three-dimensional incompressible Navier--Stokes flow from rest whose particle at the origin detects halting by entering a fixed half-space. Every mixed derivative of the force and velocity decays at rate $O((1+t)^{-1-j})$ for time order $j$. Three scalar potentials lift a coded rectangle, perform its instruction, and lower its image; an unbounded logarithmic clock supplies the decay. We also realize area-changing prefix instructions on an invariant torus plane, and construct a planar Hamiltonian processor admitting periodic forcing, stationary forcing after startup, and a velocity-field detector through a companion diffusion theorem.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Finite instructions in a viscous flow","sourceOffset":751},{"kind":"subsection","title":"History of the ideas and the role of the forcing","sourceOffset":5332},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof organization","sourceOffset":8837},{"kind":"section","title":"Prescribed solutions and their uniqueness","sourceOffset":9706},{"kind":"section","title":"Changing the speed without losing computation","sourceOffset":13481},{"kind":"section","title":"A compact realization by three scalar potentials","sourceOffset":17531},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Recording an instruction before moving the head","sourceOffset":18298},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From the local table to full rectangles","sourceOffset":22704},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Lifting, interpolation, and lowering","sourceOffset":25223},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Loading and an unbounded slow clock","sourceOffset":29897},{"kind":"section","title":"Other clocks and spatial startup profiles","sourceOffset":31796},{"kind":"section","title":"Prefix histories and a plane that compensates area change","sourceOffset":35675}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"","label":"bcs:scalar-theorem","text":"Fix a positive computable viscosity $\\nu$. A deterministic one-tape machine and finite input effectively determine a smooth force on $[0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R^3$, supported in one compact spatial set for all time, whose solution from zero velocity has pressure zero and satisfies \\[ \\|\\partial_t^jD_x^\\alpha u(t)\\|_\\infty+ \\|\\partial_t^jD_x^\\alpha f(t)\\|_\\infty \\le C_{j,\\alpha}(1+t)^{-1-j} \\] for every $j\\ge0$ and spatial multi-index $\\alpha$. The particle initially at the origin enters $\\{x_1<-1\\}$ exactly when the machine halts. Every displayed derivative belongs to space-time $L^2$, and the kinetic energy is uniformly bounded. Uniqueness holds among smooth classical solutions with, for every finite $T$, \\[ u\\in C([0,T];H^2)\\cap C^1([0,T];L^2),\\quad p\\in C([0,T];H^1),\\quad \\sup_{[0,T]\\times\\mathbb R^3}(|u|+|\\nabla u|)<\\infty. \\] The formulas remain valid for any fixed positive real viscosity, with evaluation relative to that parameter.","sourceOffset":1615,"truncated":false},{"kind":"proposition","name":"The root clocks","label":"bcs:power-clock","text":"Under the hypotheses of Lemma \\ref{bcs:clock}, set $g(t)=(1+t)^q-1$, where $q=1/3$ or $q=1/4$, and put $\\beta=1-q$. All mixed derivatives of the velocity and force in \\eqref{bcs:clock-force} are bounded, and for every fixed $j,\\alpha$, \\[ \\|\\partial_t^jD_x^\\alpha u(t)\\|_\\infty+ \\|\\partial_t^jD_x^\\alpha f(t)\\|_\\infty \\le C_{j,\\alpha}(1+t)^{-\\beta}. \\] In particular each is square-integrable in time in spatial supremum norm. The cube-root exponent is $2/3$ and the fourth-root exponent is $3/4$.","sourceOffset":32462,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/376.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":79093,"sourceFileCount":9,"statementCount":7,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Scalar-Potentials-and-Slow-Clocks-for-Forced-Fluid-Computation-September-27-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"376-09","slug":"Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026","familyId":"376","title":"Incompressible Box Transport and Finite Computation","abstract":"We realize finite positive diagonal affine maps of determinant one by effective smooth incompressible flows on neighborhoods of entire closed rational solid boxes. The source and target families are each disjoint, but may overlap each other. The construction uses localized curls, evacuation to storage and obstacle detours. A balanced three-stack recorder then assigns every machine and finite input a smooth Navier–Stokes force with one compact spatial support, periodic after a loading interval, at any fixed positive computable viscosity. The fluid starts at rest, and one fixed particle enters one fixed open cube exactly when the machine halts. Further constructions give fixed torus charts, periodicity from time zero, alternative history guards and bounded or slab observers. Onto slow clocks yield separate decaying forces. Each construction includes an all-time observation proof, effective derivative bounds and a stated pressure comparison class.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Constructs viscous incompressible flows starting from rest on a fixed flat three-dimensional domain that perform universal computation under smooth external forcing. A terminating compiler turns a Turing machine and input into a finite program for the force, so a designated particle reaches a fixed region exactly when the machine halts. The viscosity is fixed, positive and computable.","pdfPath":"preprints/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026/manuscript.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026/manuscript.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","preprints/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026/build/introduction.tex","preprints/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026/build/analysis.tex","preprints/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026/build/geometry.tex","preprints/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026/build/balanced.tex","preprints/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026/build/compilers.tex","preprints/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026/build/coding-conventions.tex","preprints/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026/build/routing-conventions.tex","preprints/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026/build/initialization.tex","preprints/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026/build/compact-processors.tex","preprints/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026/build/persistent-history.tex","preprints/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026/build/guarded-history.tex","preprints/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026/build/observer-routing.tex","preprints/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026/build/solid-routing.tex","preprints/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026/build/torus-routing.tex","preprints/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026/build/clocks.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026/build/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We realize finite positive diagonal affine maps of determinant one by effective smooth incompressible flows on neighborhoods of entire closed rational solid boxes. The source and target families are each disjoint, but may overlap each other. The construction uses localized curls, evacuation to storage and obstacle detours. A balanced three-stack recorder then assigns every machine and finite input a smooth Navier--Stokes force with one compact spatial support, periodic after a loading interval, at any fixed positive computable viscosity. The fluid starts at rest, and one fixed particle enters one fixed open cube exactly when the machine halts. Further constructions give fixed torus charts, periodicity from time zero, alternative history guards and bounded or slab observers. Onto slow clocks yield separate decaying forces. Each construction includes an all-time observation proof, effective derivative bounds and a stated pressure comparison class.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":1004},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Prior work and the role of forcing","sourceOffset":4463},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Equation, effectiveness and reading path","sourceOffset":7858},{"kind":"section","title":"Analytic classes and changes of physical coordinates","sourceOffset":11079},{"kind":"section","title":"Transporting complete boxes","sourceOffset":25022},{"kind":"subsection","title":"An affine motion and its curl extension","sourceOffset":25944},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Three geometric schedules","sourceOffset":31132},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Solid boxes with overlapping projections","sourceOffset":37732},{"kind":"section","title":"A complete balanced three-stack realization","sourceOffset":41637},{"kind":"section","title":"History records and complete symbolic domains","sourceOffset":50195},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A one-cell code and its exact inverse test","sourceOffset":51519},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Moving the work head before recording","sourceOffset":54319}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"proposition","name":"Projection on a flat torus","label":"prop:projection-l2","text":"Let $g$ be a smooth force on $[0,\\infty)\\times\\T_L^3$ with zero spatial mean at each time. There is a unique smooth mean-zero potential $\\phi$ satisfying $\\Delta\\phi=\\operatorname{div}g$. Put $Qg=\\nabla\\phi$ and $Pg=g-Qg$. Then $Pg$ is solenoidal and mean zero, and replacing $(g,p)$ by $(Pg,p-\\phi)$ preserves the velocity and the pressure normalization. Use normalized spatial measure, frequencies $\\xi_k=2\\pi k/L$, and norms \\[ \\|G\\|_{H^s}^2=\\sum_{k\\in\\Z^3}(1+|\\xi_k|^2)^s|\\widehat G(k)|^2, \\qquad \\|G\\|_{C^r}=\\max_{|\\beta|\\le r}\\|\\partial_x^\\beta G\\|_\\infty. \\] For all integers $h,r\\ge0$, at each time, \\begin{equation} \\begin{aligned} \\max\\{\\|\\partial_t^h Pg\\|_{C^r},\\|\\partial_t^h Qg\\|_{C^r}\\} &\\le S_L\\|\\partial_t^h g\\|_{H^{r+2}},\\\\ \\|\\partial_t^h\\phi\\|_{C^{r+1}} &\\le S_Ld_L\\|\\partial_t^h g\\|_{H^{r+2}}, \\end{aligned} \\end{equation} where $S_L=(1+52(L/(2\\pi))^4)^{1/2}$ and $d_L=(1+(L/(2\\pi))^2)^{1/2}$. Uniform bounds, temporal $L^2$ bounds on spatial suprema, and qualitative uniform decay of all mixed derivatives pass from $g$ to $Pg,Qg,\\phi$. A quantitative envelope for time order $h$ and output spatial order $r$ passes to $Pg,Qg$ through order $r$ and to $\\phi$ through order $r+1$ when the input derivatives through spatial order $r+2$ satisfy that envelope. The constants may depend on the derivative order. Periodicity and stationarity, including either property on a time tail, are preserved on their respective intervals. For computable $L>0$, effective mixed-derivative evaluat","sourceOffset":19426,"truncated":true},{"kind":"proposition","name":"Fluid realization and a pressure refinement","label":"prop:bb-realization","text":"Fix $\\nu>0$. A finite collection of the preceding motions, repeated with period one, or preceded by one finite loading interval, yields a smooth prescribed velocity $U$ with $U(0)=0$. Suppose its spatial support is contained in one compact set in $\\R^3$, or its potentials are contained in interior torus charts. Then \\begin{equation} f=U_t+(U\\cdot\\nabla)U-\\nu\\Delta U \\end{equation} has the same support and time periodicity and bounded mixed derivatives of every order. The solution is $(U,0)$, with the uniqueness and material-flow conclusions of Lemma \\ref{lem:p2-realization}. In $\\R^3$ its uniqueness statement also holds when the competing pressure has an $H^1$ representative at each time, without requiring continuity into $H^1$, while the other velocity hypotheses of Section \\ref{sec:model} are retained. On the torus $U$ and $f$ have zero mean. Under the effective-data assumptions of Lemma \\ref{lem:bb-curl}, the formula is $f_0+\\nu f_1$ with effective coefficients; evaluation is effective for computable $\\nu$, and relative to $\\nu$ otherwise.","sourceOffset":28578,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/docs/376.md","scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":298348,"sourceFileCount":16,"statementCount":46,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Incompressible-Box-Transport-and-Finite-Computation-October-6-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true},{"id":"377-01","slug":"Uniform-Interior-C1alpha-Estimates-for-Infinity-Harmonic-Functions-October-4-2026","familyId":"377","title":"Uniform Interior $C^{1,\\alpha}$ Estimates for Infinity-Harmonic Functions","abstract":"We prove a uniform interior $C^{1,\\alpha_d}$ estimate for bounded infinity-harmonic functions in every dimension d ≥ 3. For some $\\alpha_d\\in(0,1/3]$ depending only on the dimension, the gradient's supremum norm and αd-Hölder seminorm on B1/2 are bounded by a dimension-dependent constant times the oscillation on B1. The exponent is not explicit, and no endpoint or boundary regularity claim is made. In particular, infinity-harmonic functions are locally C1 on every open subset of ℝd, for d ≥ 3.","abstractSource":"CONTENTS.md","familySummary":"Proves uniform interior $C^{1,\\alpha_d}$ regularity for bounded infinity-harmonic functions in every dimension d ≥ 3, for a positive exponent depending only on dimension. The gradient's supremum norm and Hölder seminorm on the half unit ball are bounded by a dimension-dependent constant times the oscillation on the unit ball. The exponent is not explicit.","pdfPath":"preprints/Uniform-Interior-C1alpha-Estimates-for-Infinity-Harmonic-Functions-October-4-2026/interior-c1-infinity-harmonic.pdf","pdfUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-Interior-C1alpha-Estimates-for-Infinity-Harmonic-Functions-October-4-2026/interior-c1-infinity-harmonic.pdf","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/tree/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Uniform-Interior-C1alpha-Estimates-for-Infinity-Harmonic-Functions-October-4-2026","subjectId":"9","subject":"Partial differential equations","subjectZh":"偏微分方程","texPaths":["preprints/Uniform-Interior-C1alpha-Estimates-for-Infinity-Harmonic-Functions-October-4-2026/build/manuscript/main.tex","preprints/Uniform-Interior-C1alpha-Estimates-for-Infinity-Harmonic-Functions-October-4-2026/build/manuscript/sections/01-introduction.tex","preprints/Uniform-Interior-C1alpha-Estimates-for-Infinity-Harmonic-Functions-October-4-2026/build/manuscript/sections/02-flatness.tex","preprints/Uniform-Interior-C1alpha-Estimates-for-Infinity-Harmonic-Functions-October-4-2026/build/manuscript/sections/03-reduction.tex","preprints/Uniform-Interior-C1alpha-Estimates-for-Infinity-Harmonic-Functions-October-4-2026/build/manuscript/figures/anisotropic-fit.tex","preprints/Uniform-Interior-C1alpha-Estimates-for-Infinity-Harmonic-Functions-October-4-2026/build/manuscript/sections/04-limiting-tools.tex","preprints/Uniform-Interior-C1alpha-Estimates-for-Infinity-Harmonic-Functions-October-4-2026/build/manuscript/sections/05-envelope-contact.tex","preprints/Uniform-Interior-C1alpha-Estimates-for-Infinity-Harmonic-Functions-October-4-2026/build/manuscript/sections/06-discrete-propagation.tex","preprints/Uniform-Interior-C1alpha-Estimates-for-Infinity-Harmonic-Functions-October-4-2026/build/manuscript/sections/07-contact-localization.tex","preprints/Uniform-Interior-C1alpha-Estimates-for-Infinity-Harmonic-Functions-October-4-2026/build/manuscript/figures/contact-localization.tex","preprints/Uniform-Interior-C1alpha-Estimates-for-Infinity-Harmonic-Functions-October-4-2026/build/manuscript/sections/08-rigidity.tex","preprints/Uniform-Interior-C1alpha-Estimates-for-Infinity-Harmonic-Functions-October-4-2026/build/references/bibliography.tex"],"texRoot":"preprints/Uniform-Interior-C1alpha-Estimates-for-Infinity-Harmonic-Functions-October-4-2026/build/manuscript/main.tex","sourceAbstract":"We prove a uniform interior $C^{1,\\alpha_d}$ estimate for bounded infinity-harmonic functions in every dimension $d\\ge3$. For some $\\alpha_d\\in(0,1/3]$ depending only on the dimension, the gradient's supremum norm and $\\alpha_d$-H\\\"older seminorm on $B_{1/2}$ are bounded by a dimension-dependent constant times the oscillation on $B_1$. The exponent is not explicit, and no endpoint or boundary regularity claim is made. In particular, infinity-harmonic functions are locally $C^1$ on every open subset of $\\mathbb{R}^d$, for $d\\ge3$.","sections":[{"kind":"section","title":"Introduction","sourceOffset":601},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The result","sourceOffset":1217},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Context and methods","sourceOffset":3089},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Proof strategy","sourceOffset":6034},{"kind":"section","title":"Uniform local flatness","sourceOffset":12533},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A linear lift and affine blow-ups","sourceOffset":12887},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The three-point estimate","sourceOffset":18307},{"kind":"section","title":"Uniform power decay and the gradient estimate","sourceOffset":24728},{"kind":"subsection","title":"A flatness modulus for the normalized family","sourceOffset":26396},{"kind":"subsection","title":"Selection of scales","sourceOffset":32602},{"kind":"subsection","title":"The normalized limit","sourceOffset":38400},{"kind":"subsection","title":"From cylinder fits to a H\\\"older gradient","sourceOffset":51458}],"primaryStatements":[{"kind":"theorem","name":"Uniform interior estimate","label":"thm:main","text":"For every integer $d\\ge3$ there are constants $\\alpha_d\\in(0,1/3]$ and $C_d<\\infty$ such that every bounded infinity-harmonic function $u\\in C(B_1)$, where $B_1\\subset\\R^d$, belongs to $C^{1,\\alpha_d}(B_{1/2})$ and satisfies \\[ \\|\\nabla u\\|_{L^\\infty(B_{1/2})} +[\\nabla u]_{C^{0,\\alpha_d}(B_{1/2})} \\le C_d\\osc_{B_1}u. \\]","sourceOffset":1789,"truncated":false},{"kind":"corollary","name":"Interior $C^1$ regularity","label":"cor:interior-c1","text":"Let $d\\ge3$, let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\R^d$ be a nonempty connected open set, and let $u\\in C(\\Omega)$ be infinity-harmonic. Then $u\\in C^1_{\\mathrm{loc}}(\\Omega)$.","sourceOffset":2344,"truncated":false}],"definitions":[],"lean":{"listed":false,"scopeMapped":false,"scopeUrl":null,"scopeText":"","familyScopeUrl":null,"scopeNote":"A family-level Lean link does not establish that this individual paper is formalized. Scope documents and YAML membership are tracked separately.","independentlyBuilt":false},"verification":{"level":"manuscript-claim-not-independently-verified","independentlyVerified":false,"sourceRead":"full source include tree parsed"},"extraction":{"status":"extracted","method":"tex-include-tree","chars":157746,"sourceFileCount":12,"statementCount":10,"definitionCount":0,"extractUrl":"data/papers/Uniform-Interior-C1alpha-Estimates-for-Infinity-Harmonic-Functions-October-4-2026.json","textUrl":null,"missingIncludes":[],"issues":[]},"compactPreview":true}],"withdrawals":[{"title":"Algebraicity of Kuga-Satake Correspondences for K3 Surfaces","slug":"Algebraicity-of-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-for-K3-Surfaces-October-3-2026","status":"withdrawn","date":"October 6, 2026","notice":"# [Withdrawal notice: Algebraicity of Kuga-Satake Correspondences for K3 Surfaces](manuscript.pdf)\n\nOpenAI  \n**Withdrawn on October 6, 2026.**\n\nThe proof relies on an adaptation of the flawed stabilization-trace construction in [Algebraicity of Weil classes on split abelian eightfolds](https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Algebraicity-of-Weil-classes-on-split-abelian-eightfolds-September-18-2026/paper.pdf). The sign error in that construction prevents the required signed-double-point cancellation. The resulting gap leaves this paper's claimed algebraicity theorem unproved.\n\nThis withdrawal concerns the proof; it does not assert that the mathematical statement is false.\n\n","sourcePath":"preprints/Algebraicity-of-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-for-K3-Surfaces-October-3-2026/README.md","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Algebraicity-of-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-for-K3-Surfaces-October-3-2026/README.md","archiveUrls":["https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Algebraicity-of-Weil-classes-on-split-abelian-eightfolds-September-18-2026/paper.pdf","https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Algebraicity-of-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-for-K3-Surfaces-October-3-2026/manuscript.pdf"],"established":false},{"title":"Algebraicity of Weil classes on split abelian eightfolds","slug":"Algebraicity-of-Weil-classes-on-split-abelian-eightfolds-September-18-2026","status":"withdrawn","date":"October 6, 2026","notice":"# [Withdrawal notice: Algebraicity of Weil classes on split abelian eightfolds](paper.pdf)\n\nOpenAI  \n**Withdrawn on October 6, 2026.**\n\nThe proof contains a sign error in the stabilization-trace argument used to obtain negative double points. In the manuscript's signed double-point convention, write $I(f_1)=-m$ with $m>0$. The proof assigns each reverse stabilization trace sign $+1$ and therefore claims that inserting $m$ such traces makes the signed count zero.\n\nAccounting for the opposite source orientations of the two branches of the standard cusp gives sign $-1$ for each reverse trace in this convention. The resulting count is therefore\n\n$$I_{\\mathrm{new}}=I(f_1)-m=-2m\\ne0.$$\n\nThe Eliashberg-Murphy cancellation theorem invoked at this step requires zero signed double-point count, so its hypothesis is not met. The subsequent oriented-surgery and embedded-brane construction is therefore unsupported, and the paper does not establish its claimed algebraicity theorem.\n\nThis withdrawal concerns the proof; it does not assert that the mathematical statement is false.\n\n","sourcePath":"preprints/Algebraicity-of-Weil-classes-on-split-abelian-eightfolds-September-18-2026/README.md","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Algebraicity-of-Weil-classes-on-split-abelian-eightfolds-September-18-2026/README.md","archiveUrls":["https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Algebraicity-of-Weil-classes-on-split-abelian-eightfolds-September-18-2026/paper.pdf"],"established":false},{"title":"The rational Hodge conjecture for products of K3 surfaces","slug":"The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-products-of-K3-surfaces-October-4-2026","status":"withdrawn","date":"October 6, 2026","notice":"# [Withdrawal notice: The rational Hodge conjecture for products of K3 surfaces](hodge-conjecture-products-k3.pdf)\n\nOpenAI  \n**Withdrawn on October 6, 2026.**\n\nThe proof relies on an adaptation of the flawed stabilization-trace construction in [Algebraicity of Weil classes on split abelian eightfolds](https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Algebraicity-of-Weil-classes-on-split-abelian-eightfolds-September-18-2026/paper.pdf). The sign error in that construction prevents the required signed-double-point cancellation. The resulting gap leaves this paper's claimed rational Hodge theorem for products of projective K3 surfaces unproved.\n\nThis withdrawal concerns the proof; it does not assert that the mathematical statement is false.\n\n","sourcePath":"preprints/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-products-of-K3-surfaces-October-4-2026/README.md","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-products-of-K3-surfaces-October-4-2026/README.md","archiveUrls":["https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/preprints/Algebraicity-of-Weil-classes-on-split-abelian-eightfolds-September-18-2026/paper.pdf","https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/The-rational-Hodge-conjecture-for-products-of-K3-surfaces-October-4-2026/hodge-conjecture-products-k3.pdf"],"established":false}],"history":"# History\n\nFor any withdrawn papers, their README files explain the gap and link to the retracted manuscript. For any updated papers, previously published editions remain accessible through the version notes in their README.\n\n## October 7, 2026\n\n**Withdrawals**\n\nIn “Algebraicity of Weil classes on split abelian eightfolds” a sign error invalidates a stabilization-trace cancellation argument and the construction used by two dependent papers. As a result, we have withdrawn the following three manuscripts:\n\n- Algebraicity of Weil classes on split abelian eightfolds\n- Algebraicity of Kuga–Satake Correspondences for K3 Surfaces\n- The rational Hodge conjecture for products of K3 surfaces\n\nThe withdrawn papers now carry notices explaining the gap and linking to the archived manuscripts.\n\n**Fixes**\n\nWe have revised 14 other manuscripts with proof repairs, corrected statements, clearer hypotheses and dependencies, and one correction to an obsolete citation. The changes include:\n\n- Lipschitz heights and Ashkin–Teller currents (4 manuscripts): repaired crossing, boundary-attachment, conditioning and convergence arguments, including additional work on the real-Lipschitz interface proof.\n- Kähler minimal model programs and abundance (6 manuscripts): expanded positivity and contraction arguments and clarified which results are used as inputs, with their required hypotheses.\n- Taming and hypersymplectic deformation (2 manuscripts): corrected the cone-equality claim in Taming implies compatibility, added a strict-inclusion example, and removed an unnecessary cone-comparison dependency from the hypersymplectic paper.\n- Incompressible Box Transport and Finite Computation: revised the torus-projection and common-clock estimates.\n- Exact Birch–Swinnerton-Dyer Formula from Low Selmer Corank: removed an obsolete introductory citation to a removed supporting manuscript.\n\nAlso, as a consequence of these fixes we updated 13 additional manuscripts to cite the revised editions of companion papers. These changes update references and version dates.\n\n**Additional Formalizations**\n\nWe have added an additional 6 formalizations and 5 other additions covering supporting results. This brings the total percentage of top-line results formalized to 300 / 719 = ~42%.\n"};
